"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[709],{7709:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('[{"id":"gcp-machine-learning-jan-2026","title":"GCP Machine Learning — Jan 2026","questions":[{"id":"36","question":"A gaming company wants to build embeddings from user text interactions using BigQuery ML. They need high-quality, semantically rich embeddings. What function should they use?","options":{"A":"Use ML.TEXT_TOKENIZE to extract embedding vectors from tokens","B":"Use ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS for semantic vector generation","C":"Use BigQuery PCA to reduce dimensionality of token buckets","D":"Use hashing functions to approximate semantic text embeddings"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDINGS provides semantic embeddings from foundation models integrated into BigQuery ML, providing high-quality semantic vectors suitable for clustering and downstream ML tasks."},{"id":"37","question":"A logistics optimization model uses BigQuery ML boosted trees. The team wants to ensure the model generalizes well and stops training when improvement stagnates. Which option should they enable?","options":{"A":"Disable regularization to allow the model to converge faster","B":"Increase tree depth so the model learns more nuanced partitions","C":"Enable early stopping to halt training when validation loss plateaus","D":"Reduce the dataset size so the model focuses on key features only"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enabling early stopping prevents overfitting by monitoring validation loss and halting the training process as soon as model improvements plateau."},{"id":"38","question":"You want to continuously monitor production data distributions against training baselines to catch degradation. How should you implement this in BigQuery ML?","options":{"A":"Run manual SQL queries comparing weekly averages","B":"Enable drift detection using the ML.DETECT_DRIFT query","C":"Export predictions to Cloud Storage and run custom Python scripts","D":"Rebuild the training tables daily to prevent data staleness"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. With BigQuery ML monitoring functions: ML.VALIDATE_DATA_SKEW compares serving data against the training statistics stored in the model; ML.VALIDATE_DATA_DRIFT compares two serving-data windows to each other.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• They return a per-feature distance (L-infinity for categoricals, Jensen-Shannon for numerics) and an is_anomaly flag based on configurable thresholds.\\n• ML.DESCRIBE_DATA computes descriptive statistics.\\n• Automate with scheduled queries and alerts.\\n\\nExam tip: \'continuously monitor\', \'production vs training baseline\', \'native in BigQuery ML\'.\\nDo not confuse with: manual average queries, external scripts, or retraining every day without measuring anything.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"39","question":"A cybersecurity company needs to identify rare attack patterns. Their dataset is extremely imbalanced. What is the best technique within BigQuery ML?","options":{"A":"Train a logistic regression model with equal class weighting","B":"Use CLASS_WEIGHTS in boosted-tree classifiers to balance gradients","C":"Remove majority-class samples to artificially increase minority ratios","D":"Train an autoencoder and convert outputs to balanced synthetic labels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Weight the classes: CLASS_WEIGHTS assigns a higher weight to minority-class errors in the loss, or AUTO_CLASS_WEIGHTS=TRUE computes weights inversely proportional to frequency.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Available on LOGISTIC_REG, BOOSTED_TREE_CLASSIFIER, RANDOM_FOREST_CLASSIFIER, DNN_CLASSIFIER, and others.\\n• Example: CLASS_WEIGHTS=[(\'attack\', 50), (\'normal\', 1)].\\n• You do not drop information, unlike undersampling.\\n\\nExam tip: \'extremely imbalanced\', \'rare events\', \'built-in\'.\\nDo not confuse with: equal weights (ignores the problem), deleting majority samples (loses data), or synthetic labels from an autoencoder.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"40","question":"A company wants to automatically tune hyperparameters and select the best model during a BigQuery ML training routine. Which approach should they take?","options":{"A":"Use ML.EVALUATE to compare manual test runs","B":"Use ML.TUNE to perform automated model selection during training","C":"Use ML.TRAIN with randomized grid parameters","D":"Export the dataset to Vertex AI Workbench for manual sweeps"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. With the hyperparameter tuning built into CREATE MODEL: you set NUM_TRIALS (and optionally MAX_PARALLEL_TRIALS, HPARAM_TUNING_ALGORITHM, HPARAM_TUNING_OBJECTIVES) and ranges with HPARAM_RANGE / HPARAM_CANDIDATES.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• BigQuery ML uses Vertex AI Vizier underneath (Bayesian search by default).\\n• ML.TRIAL_INFO lists each trial, its hyperparameters, and its metric; the model uses the optimal trial by default.\\n• ML.EVALUATE and ML.PREDICT can target a specific trial.\\n\\nExam tip: \'automatically tune hyperparameters\', \'select best model\'.\\nDo not confuse with: comparing manual runs with ML.EVALUATE or exporting to Workbench for manual sweeps.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"41","question":"A data science team wants to inspect the architecture and configuration details of a trained BigQuery ML model. Which query should they run?","options":{"A":"Use ML.GLOBAL_EXPLAIN to extract model-level architectural details","B":"Use ML.EVALUATE to inspect training logs and system operations","C":"Use ML.FEATURE_INFO to display feature processing and schema details","D":"Use ML.PREDICT to retrieve configuration metadata embedded in results"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ML.FEATURE_INFO exposes the underlying input schema, feature transformations, and structural processing details applied during training."},{"id":"42","question":"You are forecasting regional retail sales using an ARIMA_PLUS model and need to account for localized calendar events. Which parameter should you include?","options":{"A":"Set TIME_SERIES_LENGTH to automatically pad missing days","B":"Include HOLIDAY_REGION so seasonality reflects local events","C":"Use TRANSFORM to manually hardcode holiday boolean flags","D":"Set CLEAN_ANOMALIES to false to preserve holiday spikes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. HOLIDAY_REGION: it adds holiday effects for a region (e.g. \'MX\', \'US\', \'GLOBAL\') so the model separates them from trend and seasonality.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Set it in the CREATE MODEL OPTIONS with MODEL_TYPE=\'ARIMA_PLUS\'.\\n• The model decomposes the series into trend, seasonality, holiday effects, spikes/dips, and level shifts.\\n• You can see the decomposition with ML.EXPLAIN_FORECAST.\\n• You can also define custom holidays.\\n\\nExam tip: \'localized calendar events\', \'holidays\', \'ARIMA_PLUS\'.\\nDo not confuse with: CLEAN_SPIKES_AND_DIPS (cleans anomalies, does not model holidays) or hard-coding boolean flags in TRANSFORM.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"43","question":"A company builds a BigQuery ML classifier. They want to validate whether the learned thresholds produce optimal recall. What should they evaluate?","options":{"A":"Use ROC curves to inspect threshold-independent model quality","B":"Use calibration curves to measure prediction probability correctness","C":"Use ML.THRESHOLD_EVALUATE to test recall at different cutoff thresholds","D":"Use confusion matrices exported from ML.WEIGHTS output metadata"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ML.THRESHOLD_EVALUATE evaluates model performance metrics like precision and recall across custom classification probability thresholds."},{"id":"44","question":"A data team wants to verify that incoming serving data matches the training schema before generating batch predictions. What BigQuery ML tool should they use?","options":{"A":"Use ML.EVALUATE on the serving table","B":"Use ML.VALIDATE_FEATURES to check feature compatibility","C":"Use ML.FEATURE_INFO to compare metadata schemas","D":"Use standard SQL WHERE clauses to filter out nulls"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Validate the serving data against the training statistics stored in the model with ML.VALIDATE_DATA_SKEW.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• It compares each feature in the serving table with the training distribution and flags anomalies per feature.\\n• Missing columns or incompatible types make ML.PREDICT fail, so validate first.\\n• You can schedule it as a prior step in a scheduled query or pipeline.\\n\\nExam tip: \'verify serving data matches training schema before batch predictions\'.\\nDo not confuse with: ML.EVALUATE (needs labels and measures quality), ML.FEATURE_INFO (training statistics only), or filtering nulls with WHERE.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"45","question":"A company wants to reduce BigQuery ML training cost. They currently use unpartitioned tables with billions of rows. What should they do?","options":{"A":"Use table clustering on key columns to reduce training cost","B":"Partition tables and train models on relevant date partitions","C":"Compress all data before training using column truncation","D":"Convert tables to CSV and reload them with compute-friendly options"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Partitioning tables allows BigQuery ML to scan only the relevant date slices needed for training, dramatically reducing processed bytes and overall compute costs."},{"id":"46","question":"A streaming data pipeline requires low-latency inference directly from a BigQuery ML model. What serving pattern should be implemented?","options":{"A":"Schedule hourly batch prediction queries via Cloud Scheduler","B":"Export the model to Cloud Storage and run inference in Cloud Functions","C":"Use BigQuery ML online prediction endpoints","D":"Use materialized views to precalculate predictions for all possible inputs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Serve the model on a Vertex AI endpoint: register the BigQuery ML model in Vertex AI Model Registry and deploy it to an online-prediction endpoint.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• In CREATE MODEL: MODEL_REGISTRY=\'VERTEX_AI\' (or EXPORT MODEL to Cloud Storage and import it).\\n• Deploy to an endpoint with autoscaling; the pipeline calls the endpoint per row/event.\\n• ML.PREDICT in BigQuery is batch: each query has seconds of latency.\\n\\nExam tip: \'low-latency\', \'streaming\', \'online\'.\\nDo not confuse with: hourly scheduled batch predictions or materialized views of every possible input.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"47","question":"A forecasting team wants to detect anomalies in a time series without external regressors. They want statistical anomaly detection based on ARIMA residuals. What should they use?","options":{"A":"Use ML.ANOMALY_DETECTION to detect pattern deviations across models","B":"Use manual residual analysis outside BigQuery with exported predictions","C":"Use ML.PREDICT anomaly detection output columns","D":"Replace ARIMA with logistic regression for anomaly detection"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. ML.DETECT_ANOMALIES on the ARIMA_PLUS model: it flags as anomalies the points that fall outside the model\'s prediction interval.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• ML.DETECT_ANOMALIES(MODEL m, STRUCT(0.95 AS anomaly_probability_threshold)) evaluates the historical training data; it also accepts a table of new data.\\n• It returns is_anomaly, lower_bound, upper_bound, and anomaly_probability.\\n• ARIMA_PLUS does not use ML.PREDICT; for forecasting you use ML.FORECAST.\\n\\nExam tip: \'anomalies in time series\', \'ARIMA residuals\', \'without external regressors\'.\\nDo not confuse with: manual analysis outside BigQuery or logistic regression (needs labels).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"48","question":"You want to create custom numerical bucketization during feature preprocessing without leaving BigQuery ML. How should this be implemented?","options":{"A":"Precompute buckets using a separate ETL Dataflow job","B":"Define bucket expressions inside the TRANSFORM clause","C":"Use ML.FEATURE_INFO to apply bucket thresholds post-training","D":"Enable AUTO_BUCKET in the model training options"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Custom bucketing expressions and feature preprocessing steps are fully supported via the TRANSFORM clause within the CREATE MODEL statement."},{"id":"49","question":"A startup wants to explore hidden patterns in customer surveys using BigQuery ML. They do not have labels. Which model suits them?","options":{"A":"Logistic regression because it can cluster unlabeled responses","B":"Boosted trees because they optimize nonlinear classification","C":"PCA because it identifies principal components of variance","D":"ARIMA because it identifies latent factors in text surveys"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is an unsupervised learning method that reduces dimensionality and isolates principal variance patterns without requiring labeled target data."},{"id":"50","question":"An anomaly detection model is experiencing high false-positive rates due to shifting seasonal baseline data. How can you dynamically adapt anomaly boundaries?","options":{"A":"Hardcode static standard deviation thresholds in SQL","B":"Use dynamic quantile thresholds generated from reconstruction losses","C":"Train a supervised logistic regression model to replace the anomaly detector","D":"Disable anomaly smoothing parameters during inference"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Use dynamic thresholds based on quantiles of the reconstruction error computed on recent data, instead of a fixed threshold.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The autoencoder reconstructs each row; reconstruction error measures how \'rare\' it is.\\n• The threshold is set as a percentile (e.g. 99) of those errors; in BigQuery ML the contamination parameter of ML.DETECT_ANOMALIES controls this.\\n• Retraining or recomputing periodically on the recent window lets the threshold follow the seasonal change.\\n\\nExam tip: \'shifting seasonal baseline\', \'dynamically adapt boundaries\', \'reconstruction loss\'.\\nDo not confuse with: fixed standard-deviation thresholds (they do not adapt) or replacing it with a supervised model (needs labels).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"51","question":"A retail company wants to automate model retraining in BigQuery ML whenever new daily data arrives. They want minimal manual intervention and reproducible scheduling. What is the recommended design?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Scheduler to export data and retrain models in external systems","B":"Use Vertex Pipelines to orchestrate BigQuery training with custom containers","C":"Use BigQuery scheduled queries to trigger retraining using SQL-based pipelines","D":"Use BigQuery row-level triggers to automatically rerun training jobs each day"},"correct":"C","explanation":"BigQuery scheduled queries natively automate SQL-based workflows like CREATE OR REPLACE MODEL on a defined cadence without external orchestration tools."},{"id":"52","question":"A team needs to understand why an individual customer was flagged as high-risk by a boosted tree classifier. Which BigQuery ML function should they use?","options":{"A":"Use ML.GLOBAL_EXPLAIN to view overall feature importance","B":"Use ML.WEIGHTS to inspect tree split thresholds","C":"Use ML.LOCAL_EXPLAIN to obtain row-level feature attribution scores","D":"Use ML.FEATURE_INFO to check the input data types"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ML.LOCAL_EXPLAIN returns per-prediction feature attributions similar to SHAP values, allowing you to interpret individual predictions."},{"id":"53","question":"A logistics company needs a model that predicts delivery delays and automatically tunes the best regression algorithm. They want the simplest approach. What should they use?","options":{"A":"Use BigQuery ML AUTO_ML_REGRESSOR to select algorithms and tune parameters","B":"Use manual boosted-tree tuning scripts with parameter sweeps","C":"Use logistic regression with polynomial terms to approximate target value","D":"Use PCA reduction followed by manual regression modeling steps"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AUTO_ML_REGRESSOR automates the entire architecture search, feature preprocessing, and hyperparameter tuning workflow directly inside BigQuery ML."},{"id":"54","question":"You need to automate continuous monitoring for prediction drift and data drift on a scheduled basis. What is the best native approach?","options":{"A":"Export logs to Cloud Logging and create metric alerts","B":"Use ML.DETECT_DRIFT with alerting thresholds and scheduled queries","C":"Re-evaluate the model daily using ML.EVALUATE and compare loss values","D":"Use Dataflow to stream predictions into a drift analysis dashboard"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. A scheduled query that runs ML.VALIDATE_DATA_DRIFT (current window vs previous) and/or ML.VALIDATE_DATA_SKEW (vs training) with per-feature thresholds, stores the result, and fires alerts.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The functions return is_anomaly per column based on the configured threshold.\\n• For prediction drift, include the prediction column in the compared tables.\\n• Alerts can go out via the scheduled-query email or Cloud Monitoring.\\n\\nExam tip: \'scheduled basis\', \'native\', \'data drift and prediction drift\'.\\nDo not confuse with: comparing loss with ML.EVALUATE (needs labels and does not measure distributions) or building your own Dataflow pipeline.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"55","question":"Your organization is building a time-series forecasting system using BigQuery ML. They want to automatically detect structural breaks and anomalies during long forecast windows. Which functionality addresses this?","options":{"A":"Use manual SQL queries to detect prediction gaps across time","B":"Use logistic regression to approximate seasonality disruptions","C":"Use ARIMA_PLUS’s built-in anomaly detection returned by ML.PREDICT","D":"Use cluster distances from a KMEANS model to detect anomalies"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The ARIMA_PLUS algorithm natively tracks prediction intervals and surfaces structural breaks or spikes directly as anomaly flags during ML.PREDICT execution."},{"id":"56","question":"How does BigQuery ML automatically handle missing values in numerical columns during model training unless overridden?","options":{"A":"It drops any row containing a NULL value across any feature column","B":"Impute missing values automatically using mean or mode strategies","C":"It replaces missing numerical values with zeros","D":"It halts training and throws a schema validation error"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. It imputes them automatically: numeric NULLs are replaced by the column mean computed on the training data, both at train time and at predict time.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Numerics: the training mean.\\n• Categoricals (one-hot/multi-hot): NULL is treated as an extra category, not as the mode.\\n• You can take control with ML.IMPUTER in TRANSFORM (mean, median, most_frequent).\\n\\nExam tip: \'automatic\', \'unless overridden\', \'missing values\'.\\nDo not confuse with: dropping rows with NULL, replacing with zero, or failing training; BigQuery ML does none of that by default.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"57","question":"A company wants to limit BigQuery ML training costs. They note that training queries scan huge tables unnecessarily. What is a best practice?","options":{"A":"Restrict training queries to filtered and partitioned subsets of data","B":"Increase cluster count to reduce the amount of scanned data","C":"Train models using SELECT * because BigQuery ML optimizes scans","D":"Use unclustered tables to encourage BigQuery to optimize I/O"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Best practice for optimizing BigQuery costs is to filter out unnecessary columns and leverage table partitioning/clustering to scan only the data required for training."},{"id":"58","question":"What is the most direct way to extract forecast intervals and confidence bounds from an ARIMA_PLUS model in BigQuery ML?","options":{"A":"Calculate standard error manually using historical variance in SQL","B":"Use prediction lower and upper bounds from ARIMA ML.PREDICT","C":"Run ML.EVALUATE to extract confidence boundaries","D":"Use ML.EXPLAIN_PREDICT to generate upper and lower limits"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Use ML.FORECAST: it returns forecast_value and prediction_interval_lower_bound / prediction_interval_upper_bound for the stated confidence level.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• ML.FORECAST(MODEL m, STRUCT(30 AS horizon, 0.9 AS confidence_level)).\\n• ML.EXPLAIN_FORECAST returns the same plus the decomposition (trend, seasonality, holidays).\\n• For ARIMA_PLUS you do not use ML.PREDICT.\\n\\nExam tip: \'forecast intervals\', \'confidence bounds\', \'most direct\'.\\nDo not confuse with: computing the historical standard error by hand, ML.EVALUATE (metrics, not intervals), or ML.EXPLAIN_PREDICT (explanations of supervised models).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"59","question":"A mobile app team wants extremely fast predictions from a BigQuery ML model and requires less than 100ms latency. Which approach fits?","options":{"A":"Use BigQuery scheduled batch predictions to simulate real-time scoring","B":"Use partitioned tables with materialized predictions refreshed hourly","C":"Use BigQuery ML online prediction endpoints for sub-100ms inference","D":"Export the model and host it inside a custom container at low cost"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Serve the model online on a Vertex AI endpoint (registered from BigQuery ML into Vertex AI Model Registry, or exported), not by querying BigQuery on every request.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A BigQuery query takes seconds; an endpoint answers in milliseconds.\\n• Keep min_replica_count ≥ 1 to avoid cold starts and size the machine type.\\n• Exported BigQuery ML models (TensorFlow SavedModel, XGBoost) can use Vertex AI prebuilt containers.\\n\\nExam tip: \'sub-100ms\', \'mobile app\', \'real-time\'.\\nDo not confuse with: scheduled batch predictions or tables materialized every hour (stale data, not online).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"60","question":"You are managing multiple online prediction endpoints and need to prevent unexpected billing spikes from excessive client requests. What should you configure?","options":{"A":"Enable cost-based inference quotas on online endpoints","B":"Scale down the endpoint node count to zero during peak hours","C":"Put a Cloud CDN caching layer in front of the BigQuery ML model","D":"Switch the model serving mode from online to batch prediction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Usage limits that cap cost: an endpoint capacity ceiling and per-client request limits.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• In Vertex AI: the autoscaling max_replica_count sets the maximum node cost.\\n• Quotas: you can lower the project\'s online-prediction request limit with a quota override; with API Gateway/Apigee you apply per-consumer quotas and rate limiting.\\n• Cloud Billing budgets and alerts warn (and can fire automations via Pub/Sub), but they do not brake by themselves.\\n\\nExam tip: \'prevent unexpected billing spikes\', \'excessive requests\'.\\nDo not confuse with: scaling to zero at peak hours (cuts the service), Cloud CDN in front of a model (it does not cache inference that way), or switching to batch (changes the requirement).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"61","question":"A data team wants to simplify feature engineering by having BigQuery ML automatically transform features. They want interactions and bucketization without manual SQL. What should they use?","options":{"A":"Use MODEL_OPTIONS to enable auto-transformation of all inputs","B":"Use manually written SQL scripts that precompute all feature transformations","C":"Use the TRANSFORM clause to declaratively specify feature transformations","D":"Use feature engineering notebooks outside BigQuery to manage pipelines"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The TRANSFORM clause embeds feature engineering logic directly inside the model artifact, ensuring consistent transformations across both training and serving."},{"id":"62","question":"You need to obtain per-instance feature attribution explanations for predictions generated by a BigQuery ML model. Which function should you use?","options":{"A":"Use ML.GLOBAL_EXPLAIN","B":"Use ML.FEATURE_IMPORTANCE","C":"Use ML.LOCAL_EXPLAIN to retrieve attribution scores for each row","D":"Use ML.WEIGHTS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. The per-row local explanation, which in BigQuery ML is ML.EXPLAIN_PREDICT.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• It returns the prediction and the top_k_features with their attribution for each row.\\n• That is what you need to justify individual decisions (credit, fraud).\\n\\nExam tip: \'per-instance\', \'each row\'.\\nDo not confuse with: ML.GLOBAL_EXPLAIN and ML.FEATURE_IMPORTANCE (aggregates at model level) or ML.WEIGHTS (model coefficients, the same for every row).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"63","question":"A company wants to consolidate multiple BigQuery ML models and evaluate them simultaneously. They need a unified performance comparison. Which feature should they use?","options":{"A":"Use confusion matrices stored in monitoring tables to compare runs","B":"Use ML.COMPRESS_MODELS to aggregate multiple candidates for comparison","C":"Use ML.COMPARE to analyze metrics across multiple models","D":"Use SQL windows to compute metric differences across predictions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ML.COMPARE evaluates and contrasts performance metrics (like accuracy, RMSE, or AUC) across multiple model artifacts simultaneously in a single tabular view."},{"id":"64","question":"A team needs to identify feature incompatibility between incoming serving data and the training schema. Which tool should they use?","options":{"A":"Use ML.DETECT_DRIFT","B":"Use ML.VALIDATE_FEATURES to check feature compatibility","C":"Use ML.FEATURE_INFO","D":"Use ML.EVALUATE"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. ML.VALIDATE_DATA_SKEW: compares the serving table with the training statistics stored in the model and flags anomalous features.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Distance per feature (L-infinity for categoricals, Jensen-Shannon for numerics) plus is_anomaly by thresholds.\\n• Missing or wrong-type columns also break ML.PREDICT, so validate first.\\n\\nExam tip: \'incompatibility between serving data and training schema\'.\\nDo not confuse with: drift between production windows (ML.VALIDATE_DATA_DRIFT), ML.FEATURE_INFO (training stats only), or ML.EVALUATE (quality with labels).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"65","question":"A manufacturing firm uses BigQuery ML for anomaly detection. They want to evaluate anomalies over many variables at once. What approach works best?","options":{"A":"Use logistic regression to model rare occurrences across variables","B":"Use autoencoder models to detect anomalies using reconstruction losses","C":"Use boosted-tree classification without including negative examples","D":"Use PCA-based dimension reduction for predicting anomalies directly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Autoencoders compress and reconstruct multi-variable input data; observations with high reconstruction error signify multivariate anomalies."},{"id":"66","question":"Which BigQuery ML serving capability provides stable, low-latency, query-based model serving for interactive applications?","options":{"A":"Cloud Functions triggering ML.PREDICT","B":"BigQuery scheduled batch inference queries","C":"Use BigQuery ML online prediction endpoints","D":"Exporting models to local edge devices"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Deploy the model to a Vertex AI endpoint (register it with MODEL_REGISTRY=\'VERTEX_AI\' or export it) and call it via API.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The endpoint keeps replicas loaded in memory with autoscaling → millisecond latencies.\\n• It supports traffic split between versions and Vertex AI Model Monitoring.\\n\\nExam tip: \'interactive applications\', \'stable, low-latency\'.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Run functions that run ML.PREDICT (each call is a BigQuery query, seconds of latency), scheduled batch queries, or export to edge devices.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"67","question":"A sales analytics team wants to generate feature-level insights from a linear regression model. They want to understand how features influence predictions globally. Which tool provides this?","options":{"A":"Use ML.LOCAL_EXPLAIN for global coefficient attribution patterns","B":"Use ML.COMPRESS_MODEL to summarize model-level transformations","C":"Use ML.WEIGHTS for global coefficients and feature importance","D":"Use ML.FEATURE_INFO to understand feature data types only"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ML.WEIGHTS extracts the underlying linear regression weights (coefficients), revealing exactly how each feature influences the global output predictions."},{"id":"68","question":"When deploying online prediction endpoints in BigQuery ML, how can you ensure predictability in cloud infrastructure expenditures?","options":{"A":"Enable cost-based inference quotas on online endpoints","B":"Restrict queries to run only during off-peak billing hours","C":"Use unpartitioned tables for all batch prediction storage","D":"Compress the model weights using ML.COMPRESS_MODEL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cost-based inference quotas place hard operational limits on endpoint usage, preventing unexpected billing overages from high traffic volumes."},{"id":"69","question":"A data science team wants to evaluate the impact of new neural embeddings introduced into their BigQuery ML classifier. They want feature contribution summaries aggregated across all predictions. What function should they call?","options":{"A":"Use ML.LOCAL_EXPLAIN to retrieve feature explanations for each prediction","B":"Use ML.PREDICT to extract embedding weight impacts row by row","C":"Use ML.WEIGHTS to retrieve global linear coefficients for embeddings","D":"Use ML.GLOBAL_EXPLAIN to obtain global feature attribution summaries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ML.GLOBAL_EXPLAIN aggregates SHAP-based attributions across the entire evaluation dataset to quantify overall global feature importance for complex models."},{"id":"71","question":"A company uses BigQuery ML for daily churn prediction. They want to know whether the model\'s input distribution is shifting significantly. What solution fits?","options":{"A":"Use ML.THRESHOLD EVALUATE to compare classification thresholds","B":"Use ML.DETECT DRIFT to detect input feature and prediction drift","C":"Use ML-WEIGHTS to track changes in model parameters","D":"Use ROC curves from ML.EVALUATE to detect data drift patterns RI"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Drift monitoring: compare the distribution of features (and of predictions) across time windows with ML.VALIDATE_DATA_DRIFT, or against training with ML.VALIDATE_DATA_SKEW.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Schedule the daily comparison with a scheduled query.\\n• Review which features exceed the threshold (is_anomaly) to decide whether to retrain.\\n\\nExam tip: \'input distribution shifting\', \'daily\'.\\nDo not confuse with: evaluating classification thresholds, tracking changes in ML.WEIGHTS (parameters, not data), or ROC curves (quality with labels, not distribution).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"72","question":"A research team wants to build a BigQuery ML forecasting model but also incorporate external regressors for greater accuracy. What should they choose?","options":{"A":"Use ARIMA PLUS to model regressors implicitly through timestamps","B":"Use LOGISTIC_REG to approximate forecasting across regressors","C":"Use AUTO ML FORECASTER to automatically select model types","D":"Use ARIMA _PLUS XREG to include exogenous variables"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. ARIMA_PLUS_XREG: extends ARIMA_PLUS with exogenous variables (covariates) in addition to the series itself.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• CREATE MODEL ... OPTIONS(MODEL_TYPE=\'ARIMA_PLUS_XREG\', TIME_SERIES_TIMESTAMP_COL=..., TIME_SERIES_DATA_COL=...); the remaining columns are regressors.\\n• To forecast with ML.FORECAST you must pass a table with future values of the regressors.\\n• It keeps ARIMA_PLUS capabilities (holidays, seasonality, step changes).\\n\\nExam tip: \'external regressors\', \'exogenous variables\'.\\nDo not confuse with: ARIMA_PLUS (series only; no regressors), LOGISTIC_REG (classification), or \'AUTO ML FORECASTER\' (not a BigQuery ML model type; AutoML forecasting lives in Vertex AI).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"74","question":"A team needs to determine whether their BigQuery ML model is still accurate after deployment. They collect actual labels periodically and want automated evaluation. What should they implement?","options":{"A":"Use monitoring dashboards that track prediction histograms daily","B":"Use ML.RETRAIN MODEL to automatically measure accuracy drift","C":"Use ML.MONITORING tables with accuracy-based triggers","D":"Use manual SQL joins to compare predictions with labels monthly"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Automated ground-truth evaluation: a scheduled query joins saved predictions with real labels, runs ML.EVALUATE on those new data, writes metrics to a monitoring table, and fires alerts/retraining if they drop below a threshold.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• ML.EVALUATE(MODEL m, TABLE newly_labeled) computes metrics on recent data.\\n• A historical metrics table → dashboard and alerts (Cloud Monitoring, email).\\n• The accuracy/AUC threshold acts as the trigger.\\n\\nExam tip: \'collect actual labels periodically\', \'automated evaluation\'.\\nDo not confuse with: prediction histograms (they measure drift, not accuracy), \'ML.RETRAIN MODEL\' (does not exist), or monthly manual joins.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"76","question":"A pipeline loads CSV files into BigQuery nightly. Sometimes corrupted rows appear. ou need a scalable method to detect malformed records before loading. What should you implement?","options":{"A":"Implement a Dataflow batch pipeline with schema validation before BigQuery load","B":"Use Cloud Functions to upload only valid rows to BigQuery","C":"Write a BigQuery scheduled query to detect invalid rows","D":"Use Pub/Sub filtering rules to drop malformed entries"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. A Dataflow batch pipeline that validates each row\'s schema before writing to BigQuery, sending invalids to a dead-letter (error table or bucket).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Parse/validate ParDo with multiple outputs: valids → BigQuery; invalids → dead-letter with the error and the original row.\\n• Autoscales with volume.\\n• Lets you review and reprocess errors later.\\n\\nExam tip: \'scalable\', \'detect malformed records before loading\'.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Run functions (time/memory limits on large files), a scheduled query (detects after load), or Pub/Sub filters (filter attributes, not CSV content).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"78","question":"Your ML feature store requires both historical offline features and low-latency online serving features. Access patterns differ drastically. Which architecture best satisfies both needs?","options":{"A":"Use Dataproc for offline features and Firestore for online lookup","B":"Store both offline and online features in the same BigQuery table","C":"Store offline features entirely in Cloud Storage for batch reads","D":"Use BigQuery for offline features and Bigtable for online serving"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. BigQuery as the offline store and Bigtable as the online store.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• BigQuery: massive analytical scans, point-in-time joins to build training datasets.\\n• Bigtable: key lookups in milliseconds at large scale for serving.\\n• This is the Vertex AI Feature Store architecture: the data source lives in BigQuery and online serving syncs to Bigtable (or optimized online serving).\\n\\nExam tip: \'historical offline + low-latency online\', \'access patterns differ\'.\\nDo not confuse with: Dataproc + Firestore (Dataproc is compute, not a store), one BigQuery table for everything (not ms latency), or Cloud Storage alone.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"80","question":"An ML workflow requires storing large tabular features for repeated model retraining and efficient querying. Which approach is optimal?","options":{"A":"Store the features in BigQuery partitioned tables for fast analytical access","B":"Store them in Cloud Storage text files and query using gsutil each time","C":"Use local SSDs on Compute Engine instances to store training data","D":"Store them in Firestore collections for high read throughput"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Store them in partitioned BigQuery tables (and cluster them if it helps).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Scalable analytical SQL to build and filter training datasets.\\n• Partition by date → retrain only on the needed window at lower cost.\\n• Vertex AI, TensorFlow (tf.data/BigQuery connector), and Dataflow read fast with the BigQuery Storage Read API.\\n\\nExam tip: \'large tabular features\', \'repeated retraining\', \'efficient querying\'.\\nDo not confuse with: text files in Cloud Storage queried with gsutil (not a query engine), local SSDs (ephemeral, not shared), or Firestore (not for massive analysis).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"82","question":"Your Dataflow job joins streaming events with a frequently updated lookup file stored in Cloud Storage. Loading the entire file each refresh cycle wastes resources. You need granular, low-latency lookups. hat should you do?","options":{"A":"Embed all lookup data in worker static memory","B":"Rewrite the lookup file into thousands of small segments","C":"Move lookup data into Bigtable and perform per-event lookups","D":"Use BigQuery views for each streaming event request"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Bigtable is built for single-row reads with millisecond latency at any scale, so the Dataflow workers can look up each event\'s key on demand and always see the latest value, without reloading a whole file every refresh cycle.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) static worker memory goes stale and is limited by worker RAM. B) thousands of small files still have to be listed and reloaded, and Cloud Storage is not a key-value store. D) running a BigQuery query per streaming event is slow, costly and quota-bound; BigQuery is for analytics, not point lookups.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"84","question":"A dataset contains multiple versions of the same entity across days. You need to build ML features using only the latest available record for each entity. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Sort entities manually in Cloud Storage before processing","B":"Store all entity versions as raw JSON and extract latest on demand","C":"Use BigQuery window functions to pick latest per entity","D":"Use Cloud SQL stored procedures to precompute latest values"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. A window function such as ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY entity_id ORDER BY updated_at DESC), then keeping row 1 (or QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() ... = 1), picks the latest record per entity in one scalable, declarative query. This is the standard dedup pattern for feature tables.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) manual sorting in Cloud Storage does not scale and is error-prone. B) raw JSON with on-demand extraction moves the work to every consumer. D) Cloud SQL stored procedures do not scale to analytical volumes.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"86","question":"A production ML pipeline writes inference results into BigQuery. Some rows are corrupt because they were written during an upstream outage. You need a mechanism to isolate unhealthy data segments for reprocessing. What is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Store data in partitioned BigQuery tables and mark unhealthy partitions","B":"Store all inference results in a single unpartitioned BigQuery table","C":"Use Dataflow to automatically delete unhealthy rows upon detection","D":"Merge corrupted rows into Cloud Storage files without partition tags"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Store results in partitioned tables (by date/time) and mark the affected partitions to reprocess and replace them.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The partition bounds the outage window; you can overwrite only that partition (WRITE_TRUNCATE with the table$YYYYMMDD decorator, or DELETE + INSERT filtered by partition).\\n• A control table records which partitions are \'unhealthy\'.\\n• Time travel lets you query the previous state if needed.\\n\\nExam tip: \'isolate unhealthy data segments\', \'reprocessing\'.\\nDo not confuse with: an unpartitioned table (you cannot isolate the segment cheaply), automatically deleting rows with Dataflow (you lose data and traceability), or moving to Cloud Storage without tags.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"88","question":"Your team needs to generate daily aggregates for ML features stored in BigQuery. Aggregations must run faster than current SQL queries. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Use BigQuery BI Engine to accelerate analytical workloads","B":"Use BigQuery materialized views for efficient incremental aggregation","C":"Export the data to Cloud Storage and aggregate using local scripts","D":"Use scheduled queries that always recompute all records"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. BigQuery materialized views: they precompute and maintain the aggregation with incremental refresh, processing only new data.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• BigQuery refreshes the view automatically and can rewrite queries on the base table to use it.\\n• Reading the already-computed aggregate is much faster and cheaper than recomputing everything.\\n• They support common aggregations (SUM, COUNT, AVG, etc.) with some SQL limits.\\n\\nExam tip: \'daily aggregates\', \'faster\', \'incremental\'.\\nDo not confuse with: BI Engine (in-memory cache for interactive dashboard queries; it does not avoid recompute), export to local scripts, or scheduled queries that recompute everything.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"90","question":"You ingest millions of structured logs daily and need a storage format optimized for ML feature extraction. You must support predicate pushdown and columnar reads. hat format is best?","options":{"A":"Store logs in Cloud Storage as JSON files","B":"Store logs in Cloud Storage as Avro files","C":"Store logs in Cloud Storage as Parquet files","D":"Store logs in Cloud Storage as plain text records"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Parquet: a compressed columnar format that lets you read only the needed columns and skip blocks using statistics (predicate pushdown).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Data organized by columns and row groups with min/max per block.\\n• Very good compression and vectorized reads in Spark/Dataproc, BigQuery (external/BigLake tables), and Dataflow.\\n• Schema embedded in the file.\\n\\nExam tip: \'predicate pushdown\', \'columnar reads\'.\\nDo not confuse with: JSON and plain text (row-wise, no schema, slow), Avro (row-wise: great for write/streaming and schema evolution, but not columnar).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"92","question":"You maintain a pipeline that extracts features for training and validation. Recently, feature distributions shifted subtly, causing reduced model accuracy. You need automated drift detection across features. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Implement manual SQL queries to compare distributions each week","B":"Use TensorFlow Data Validation to compute drift and anomaly scores","C":"Use Cloud Logging metrics to alert on distribution changes","D":"Use Pub/Sub filtering rules to drop unexpected outlier values"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. TensorFlow Data Validation (TFDV): it computes statistics for each feature, infers a schema, and compares datasets to detect anomalies, skew, and drift.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• generate_statistics_from_* → statistics; infer_schema → expected schema.\\n• validate_statistics with drift_comparator / skew_comparator (L-infinity for categoricals, Jensen-Shannon for numerics) and per-feature thresholds.\\n• Scales with Apache Beam/Dataflow and integrates into TFX and Vertex AI Pipelines.\\n\\nExam tip: \'automated drift detection across features\', \'pipeline\'.\\nDo not confuse with: weekly manual SQL, Cloud Logging metrics (they do not compare distributions), or Pub/Sub filters (they drop messages, they do not detect drift).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"94","question":"You have a Dataflow streaming pipeline consuming Pub/Sub events. You want to ensure that message duplication never affects downstream analytics. What should ou implement?","options":{"A":"Use exactly-once Pub/Sub delivery for all consumers","B":"Use Dataflow’s built-in deduplication with stable unique IDs","C":"Use Cloud Functions to drop any duplicate messages manually","D":"Use BigQuery merge statements on all consumed events"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Deduplication in Dataflow based on a stable unique ID per event.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Pub/Sub delivers at least once; the publisher can also resend the same event.\\n• Dataflow already deduplicates Pub/Sub redeliveries by message ID; for publisher duplicates, use an event ID as an attribute (id_label / withIdAttribute) or a Deduplicate transform by key.\\n• With Dataflow streaming exactly-once mode, processing and writes to compatible sinks are consistent.\\n\\nExam tip: \'duplication never affects downstream\', \'stable unique IDs\'.\\nDo not confuse with: Pub/Sub exactly-once delivery (applies to pull subscriptions and does not remove events published twice), manual Cloud Run functions, or MERGE in BigQuery for everything (expensive and late).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"96","question":"A model training pipeline depends on several upstream preprocessing tasks. Some tasks occasionally fail, leaving partial outputs in Cloud Storage. You need to ensure downstream components only consume fully generated datasets. What is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Scheduler to verify data exists before training begins","B":"Use Cloud Logging alerts to notify engineers about missing files","C":"Use naming conventions in Cloud Storage to track processing status","D":"Use Dataflow to produce atomic output folders with completion markers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Produce atomic outputs: Dataflow writes to a temporary/versioned folder and, on success, publishes the final folder and a completion marker (e.g. _SUCCESS or a manifest).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Consumers only read folders that have the marker (or the manifest with the exact file list).\\n• If the job fails, there is no marker and the partial output is ignored or cleaned up.\\n• In Vertex AI Pipelines, the training step depends on the previous step\'s output artifact, which exists only if it finished well.\\n\\nExam tip: \'partial outputs\', \'only consume fully generated datasets\'.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Scheduler checking that \'data exist\' (partial files also exist), logging alerts, or naming conventions with no guarantee.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"98","question":"An ML feature store needs sub-second lookup times for online predictions while also supporting large offline batch reads for retraining. What architecture satisfies both requirements?","options":{"A":"Use Firestore for online lookup and train directly from Firestore exports","B":"Use Memorystore for online lookup and store offline features in Cloud SQL","C":"Use Cloud SQL for online lookup and use BigQuery for offline features","D":"Use Bigtable for online lookup and store offline features in BigQuery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Bigtable for the online lookup and BigQuery for offline features.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Bigtable: millisecond latency by row key, horizontal scale.\\n• BigQuery: massive scans and SQL to build training datasets.\\n• Same pattern as Vertex AI Feature Store (offline in BigQuery, online serving in Bigtable).\\n\\nExam tip: \'sub-second online lookups\' + \'large offline batch reads\'.\\nDo not confuse with: training from Firestore exports (slow and awkward), Memorystore + Cloud SQL (Cloud SQL is not for massive analytical batch), or Cloud SQL for online at large scale.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"100","question":"A customer-behavior model consumes daily snapshots stored in Cloud Storage. You notice that some snapshots are incomplete due to partially uploaded files. ou must prevent the pipeline from ingesting incomplete snapshots. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Storage lifecycle rules to automatically delete incomplete snapshots","B":"Use Pub/Sub notifications to process files as soon as uploads begin","C":"Use BigQuery error logs to detect incomplete ingestion events","D":"Use Cloud Storage compose or object finalize events to process only completed files"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Trigger processing with the object-finalization event (OBJECT_FINALIZE, via Pub/Sub notifications or Eventarc), which fires only when the object has been fully written.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• In Cloud Storage an object is not visible until the upload finishes (writes are atomic).\\n• If the snapshot is many files, upload a marker/manifest last (or use compose) and trigger on its finalize.\\n• Optional: validate checksums (CRC32C/MD5) before processing.\\n\\nExam tip: \'partially uploaded\', \'only completed files\'.\\nDo not confuse with: lifecycle rules (they delete by age, not completeness), processing when the upload \'starts\', or reviewing BigQuery error logs after the damage.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"102","question":"Your Dataflow pipeline currently consumes Pub/Sub events and writes results to BigQuery. You notice growing lag during sudden traffic spikes. Which improvement increases elasticity without manual provisioning?","options":{"A":"Increase Pub/Sub publisher batching delay","B":"Reduce Dataflow autoscaling limits to stabilize throughput","C":"Switch to Dataproc streaming with manual worker scripts","D":"Enable Dataflow Streaming Engine for enhanced autoscaling"},"correct":"D","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Streaming Engine moves window state and shuffle out of the worker VMs into the Dataflow service backend. Workers become lighter, and horizontal autoscaling reacts faster and more smoothly to traffic spikes, with no manual provisioning.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) publisher batching delay adds latency; it does not add processing capacity. B) lowering the autoscaling limits makes the lag worse. C) Dataproc with manual worker scripts is the opposite of elastic and serverless.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"104","question":"A dataset used for model training mixes timestamps from multiple time zones. Some events appear temporally inconsistent, affecting feature calculations. What should ou implement?","options":{"A":"Leave timestamps unchanged and rely on the model to learn offsets","B":"Convert all timestamps to local device time for consistency","C":"Tag each timestamp with time-zone metadata but store raw times only","D":"Normalize all timestamps to UTC before upstream processing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Normalize all timestamps to UTC as early as possible in the pipeline, keeping the original time zone as a separate column if it is useful for features (local hour, local day of week).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A single time frame guarantees correct order, windows, and consistent joins.\\n• BigQuery TIMESTAMP already represents an absolute instant (UTC); use DATETIME only for local \'wall-clock\' time.\\n• Local features are derived explicitly from UTC + zone.\\n\\nExam tip: \'multiple time zones\', \'temporally inconsistent\'.\\nDo not confuse with: letting the model learn the offsets, converting to the device\'s local time (inconsistent, daylight saving), or storing only raw times with metadata.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"106","question":"A Dataflow streaming pipeline processes telemetry events and writes the results to Cloud Storage. You observe that output folder structures grow endlessly and make downstream discovery difficult. You need a scalable and organized layout. What is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Generate output directories based on event type and day of processing","B":"Store all objects in a single folder but include type prefixes in filenames","C":"Write files into partitioned subfolders using event timestamps","D":"Randomize folder names to reduce contention during writes S att! u ty"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Finalize events ensure that files are fully written, and verifying checksums prevents ingestion of truncated or corrupt files."},{"id":"107","question":"You operate a training pipeline that joins transactional data stored in BigQuery with large reference tables stored in Cloud Storage. Query performance is slow due to frequent full scans. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Convert the Cloud Storage reference files from CSV to JSON for faster loading","B":"Load the Cloud Storage reference data into Firestore for low-latency lookups","C":"Load the reference objects into BigQuery and apply clustering to speed joins","D":"Preload the reference objects into Compute Engine VMs for local joins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Load the reference data into native BigQuery tables and apply clustering on the join columns.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• External tables over Cloud Storage (especially CSV/JSON) are read fully on each query, without storage optimizations.\\n• In native storage, BigQuery uses a columnar format, statistics, and clustering to prune blocks on joins and filters.\\n• If they must stay in Cloud Storage, BigLake tables on Parquet with metadata cache improve performance some.\\n\\nExam tip: \'slow joins\', \'reference tables in Cloud Storage\', \'full scans\'.\\nDo not confuse with: converting CSV to JSON (just as slow or worse), Firestore for lookups (not for analytical joins), or VMs with local joins.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"109","question":"Your ML pipeline must handle late- arriving data for up to 72 hours. However, storing huge amounts of state for that duration increases memory pressure. You need a scalable approach. What do you do?","options":{"A":"Reduce allowed lateness and discard late events after 24 hours","B":"Use Dataflow state expiration policies to prune old keys","C":"Store all historical state in Cloud SQL for long-term retention","D":"Enforce synchronous clock correction on all upstream devices"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Use Dataflow state expiration/garbage collection to delete keys and windows that can no longer receive events.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Beam keeps state per key and window while the window is open.\\n• When the watermark passes window end + allowed lateness (72 h), the runner drops that state.\\n• In GlobalWindow there is no automatic GC: use timers to clear inactive keys.\\n\\nExam tip: \'late-arriving up to 72 hours\' + \'memory pressure\' + \'scalable\'.\\nDo not confuse with: cutting lateness to 24 h (breaks the 72 h requirement), Cloud SQL (does not scale in streaming), or fixing clocks (does not reduce state).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"111","question":"A fraud-detection model requires computing rolling 5-minute sums. Because of bursts in network delays, events often arrive after their window closes. The model must still receive corrected aggregates. What should you implement?","options":{"A":"Watermarks with zero allowed lateness to enforce strict timing","B":"Sliding windows without late firings to simplify computation","C":"Fixed windows with late triggers to recompute outputs when needed","D":"Session windows that merge all events within a period of inactivity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Fixed 5 min windows with allowed lateness and late triggers: when a late event arrives, Dataflow re-emits the corrected aggregate.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Event time assigns each event to its 5 min bucket.\\n• The on-time trigger fires when the watermark passes; withLateFirings fires again if late data enters.\\n• accumulatingFiredPanes (or retractions) updates the consumer.\\n\\nExam tip: \'rolling 5-minute sums\' + \'arrive after window closes\' + \'corrected aggregates\'.\\nDo not confuse with: zero lateness (drops lates), sliding without late firings (does not recompute), or session windows (group by inactivity, not fixed 5 min).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"113","question":"A Dataflow pipeline writes tens of thousands of small files into Cloud Storage each hour. Downstream systems struggle with performance due to object listing overhead. How can you reduce this bottleneck?","options":{"A":"Increase downstream CPU resources to handle listing operations","B":"Use Cloud Functions to batch files into groups after writing","C":"Use Dataflow to combine small outputs into fewer large files","D":"Store all results in a single large file and append continuously"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Combine outputs in Dataflow (fewer, larger shards) before writing to Cloud Storage.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Reduce the number of windows/shards or use a Write with fewer numShards / file compaction.\\n• Fewer objects → cheaper LIST and opens; better throughput in Spark, BigQuery load, and Dataflow.\\n• Columnar format (Parquet/Avro) in files of hundreds of MB is the usual target.\\n\\nExam tip: \'tens of thousands of small files\' + \'object listing overhead\'.\\nDo not confuse with: more CPU downstream (listing is metadata, not compute), Cloud Functions to re-batch (extra hop), or a single file with append (GCS is not an append-friendly log and creates a serial bottleneck).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"115","question":"Your Dataflow job enriches streaming events using a lookup table stored in BigQuery. Occasionally, the lookup table is updated, but the Dataflow job does not immediately pick up changes, causing stale enrichments. You need more timely lookups without restarting the pipeline. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Use Dataflow side inputs that reload data only when the pipeline restarts","B":"Use Dataflow BigQuery Storage API reads with periodic refresh intervals","C":"Use Cloud Functions to push updated reference rows directly into worker memory","D":"Use Cloud Scheduler to pause the pipeline while reference tables update"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. BigQuery side inputs with periodic refresh (PeriodicImpulse + ReadAllFromBigQuery / Storage Read API), not a lookup loaded only at startup.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Each interval the table is re-read and published as a new side-input version.\\n• The ParDo uses AsDict/AsList of the time-aligned version.\\n• You do not need to stop the pipeline.\\n\\nExam tip: \'lookup table updated\' + \'without restarting\'.\\nDo not confuse with: a side input that only loads at restart (that IS the problem), Cloud Functions stuffing rows into worker RAM (not a Dataflow API), or pausing the job with Scheduler.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"117","question":"Your BigQuery ingestion workflow receives files from external partners. Some partners occasionally send files with incorrect column orders. You want to validate file structure before ingestion and reject incorrectly ordered data automatically. What is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Rely on BigQuery to auto-detect incorrect ordering and realign columns","B":"Use Cloud Logging to manually inspect schema mismatches each day","C":"Allow ingestion and use post-processing queries to fix order issues","D":"Use Dataflow to parse files and enforce strict column order validation"},"correct":"D","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: D. A Dataflow (Apache Beam) step can parse each file, compare its header and column order against the expected schema, and route bad files or rows to a dead-letter location before anything reaches BigQuery. Rejection happens automatically and is auditable.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) BigQuery schema auto-detect infers types; it does not detect or fix a wrong column order, so values load into the wrong columns. B) daily manual inspection is not automatic. C) fixing data after ingestion lets bad data reach consumers.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"119","question":"You run a pipeline that joins streaming events with a 20-GB static mapping file stored in Cloud Storage. Loading this mapping file as a Dataflow side input leads to repeated reloads and worker memory pressure. You need a more scalable approach. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Split the mapping file into thousands of small shards across many buckets","B":"Load the mapping file into Bigtable and perform lookups directly from Dataflow","C":"Convert the mapping file into Avro and rely on local worker caching","D":"Store the mapping file inside Pub/Sub as a retained configuration stream"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. A 20 GB side input has to be broadcast to and held by every worker, which causes memory pressure and reloads. Putting the mapping into Bigtable and doing keyed lookups from a DoFn (ideally batched or cached per bundle) scales independently of worker memory.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) sharding the file across buckets is still a file, with no point lookups. C) Avro changes the format, not the broadcast problem. D) Pub/Sub is a messaging service, not a lookup store.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"121","question":"Your Dataflow pipeline reads from Pub/Sub, but message throughput varies widely throughout the week. At peak times, Dataflow autoscaling cannot keep up, causing lag. You want to improve performance without provisioning static compute. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Tune Pub/Sub publisher batching parameters to slow ingestion","B":"Use Cloud Functions to preprocess messages before Dataflow consumes them","C":"Introduce Cloud Storage buffering before messages enter Dataflow","D":"Enable Dataflow Streaming Engine to separate compute from state scaling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Dataflow Streaming Engine: it moves shuffle and window state off the VMs into the service backend so compute can scale independently of state.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Without SE, state lives on the worker\'s Persistent Disk: scale-up/down is slow (state must migrate).\\n• With SE, smaller workers, more reactive autoscaling, and faster handover.\\n• It stays serverless: you do not pin a static size.\\n\\nExam tip: \'autoscaling cannot keep up\' + \'without static compute\'.\\nDo not confuse with: publisher batching to slow ingestion (worsens the SLA), Cloud Functions preprocess, or a GCS buffer (adds batch-like latency).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"123","question":"A pipeline writes transformed training data into BigQuery using the Storage Write API. You observe occasional errors due to schema evolution upstream. You need a safe and automated method to prevent writes when incompatible changes occur. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Disable schema enforcement and allow BigQuery to append unknown columns","B":"Enable BigQuery schema compatibility checks before each batch write","C":"Convert all rows into JSON and store them without column constraints","D":"Rely on downstream feature stores to correct incompatible schemas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Check schema compatibility before each batch/stream write and reject the batch if it does not match the table.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The Storage Write API requires the descriptor/Arrow to match the table; extra fields → SCHEMA_MISMATCH_EXTRA_FIELD.\\n• Validate proto/JSON against TableSchema (e.g. SchemaCompact in the Java client) before AppendRows.\\n• Dead-letter incompatible rows; do not turn enforcement off.\\n\\nExam tip: \'schema evolution\' + \'prevent writes when incompatible\'.\\nDo not confuse with: JSON with no columns (destroys the contract), disabling enforcement, or \'fixing it later\' in a feature store.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"125","question":"Your streaming pipeline enriches clickstream events with session metadata. During traffic spikes, the enrichment step becomes a bottleneck due to high-latency lookups in Cloud SQL. You need lower- latency and more scalable enrichment without redesigning the data model. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Enable SQL read replicas to distribute lookup traffic automatically","B":"Cache session metadata inside Cloud Functions for faster retrieval","C":"Preload session metadata into Compute Engine VMs using cron jobs","D":"Move session metadata to Bigtable and perform lookups from Dataflow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Move session metadata to Bigtable and look it up by key from Dataflow.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Same key (session_id / user_id) → value; you do not change the logical model.\\n• Bigtable: millisecond reads at millions of QPS; fits a Dataflow enrichment ParDo.\\n• Cloud SQL, even with replicas, has a connection pool, SQL latency, and a vertical ceiling.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"streaming enrichment\\" + \\"high-latency Cloud SQL lookups\\" + \\"scalable\\".\\nDo not confuse with: read replicas (they help a little, they do not give KV-style latency), a cache in Cloud Functions, or a cron job onto GCE (fragile operations).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"127","question":"You receive daily Parquet files from an external partner. Some files contain outdated schemas that do not match your current feature definitions. You need to enforce schema compatibility before loading them into BigQuery. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Assume schema changes are backward compatible and load files directly","B":"Use Dataflow to inspect the Parquet schema and reject incompatible files","C":"Allow BigQuery to auto-evolve the schema whenever new fields appear","D":"Store outdated files in a separate bucket without validation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Use Dataflow to read the embedded schema of each Parquet file and reject (dead-letter) files that are incompatible with your current features.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Parquet stores the schema in the footer; you compare it to the contract (types, required fields).\\n• Compatible → write to BigQuery; incompatible → reject bucket/table + alert.\\n• Do not load \\"and see what happens\\".\\n\\nExam tip: \\"enforce schema compatibility before loading\\".\\nDo not confuse with: autodetect/auto-evolve (accepts new fields and does not reject old schemas), loading directly, or storing without validation.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"129","question":"Your team must perform anomaly detection on incoming events in a Dataflow streaming pipeline. Anomalies are rare, and the pipeline should flag them while continuing normal processing with minimal overhead. What is the best design?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Functions to evaluate each event and report anomalies","B":"Use scheduled BigQuery queries to scan for anomalies periodically","C":"Store anomalies in memory within worker instances for fast retrieval","D":"Apply Dataflow branching to route anomalies to a dedicated sink"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Branching in Dataflow: one output continues normal processing and another sends anomalies to a dedicated sink.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A ParDo/DoFn with multiple outputs (TupleTag / with_outputs) classifies each event.\\n• The 99% stay on the main path; the rare ones go to Pub/Sub, BigQuery, or a GCS alerts bucket.\\n• One job, no extra hop per event.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"anomalies are rare\\" + \\"continuing normal processing\\" + \\"minimal overhead\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Functions per event (cold start, cost, another system), periodic BigQuery queries (not streaming), or storing anomalies only in worker RAM (lost on restart).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"131","question":"Your BigQuery tables are partitioned by event date, but clustering is not used. Analysts complain that queries scanning multiple user IDs still read entire partitions. You need a cost-effective improvement. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Split partitions into independent tables per user ID","B":"Add clustering by user ID to reduce scanned blocks","C":"Add additional date fields to reduce dependence on partitions","D":"Export all partitions into Cloud Storage as optimized files"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Add clustering by user_id (or the column you filter on). BigQuery sorts blocks inside each partition and can skip blocks that do not contain those IDs.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• PARTITION BY event_date CLUSTER BY user_id.\\n• The date filter prunes partitions; the user_id filter prunes clustered blocks.\\n• Without clustering, a WHERE user_id IN (...) inside the day scans the whole day.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"partitioned by event date\\" + \\"queries scanning multiple user IDs\\" + \\"entire partitions\\".\\nDo not confuse with: more date fields (they do not help a user filter), one table per user_id (inoperable), or exporting to GCS (does not speed SQL in BigQuery).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"133","question":"You store intermediate ML datasets as Avro in Cloud Storage. While these files are read efficiently by Dataflow, analysts struggle to work with them interactively. You need a format that supports both efficient pipeline consumption and analytical SQL queries. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Convert all files into compressed JSON for analyst readability","B":"Convert Avro files into CSV for simple SQL joins in BigQuery","C":"Convert them into nested Protocol Buffers for consistent structure","D":"Load the data into BigQuery and maintain tables for interactive queries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Load (or replicate) the data into BigQuery tables for interactive SQL; Dataflow can keep reading Avro or read from BigQuery.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• BigQuery loads Avro natively (schema included).\\n• Analysts: SQL, partitioning/clustering, BI Engine.\\n• Pipelines: BigQuery Storage Read API, or the original Avro if the batch job prefers it.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"efficient pipeline consumption\\" + \\"analytical SQL queries\\".\\nDo not confuse with: switching to CSV (worse for Dataflow and for BigQuery), compressed JSON, or nested protobufs (awkward in SQL).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"135","question":"Your ML team uses Dataflow to generate normalized user-level features. Some users generate extremely large olumes of events causing worker memory pressure during grouping. You need a scalable way to handle these skewed keys without redesigning the data schema. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Enforce strict per-key memory limits to skip oversized user groups","B":"Use Dataflow hot-key mitigation by sharding large keys during grouping","C":"Route heavy-volume users to a separate pipeline with dedicated workers","D":"Store all heavy users’ events temporarily in Cloud SQL before grouping"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Mitigate hot keys by sharding those keys during GroupByKey/Combine and recombining afterwards.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A hot key forces a single worker to materialize the whole group.\\n• withHotKeyFanout / with_hot_key_fanout (or \\"salting\\": user_id#shard) spreads the partial aggregation.\\n• A second Combine by user_id merges the partials. The output schema is still per user.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"skewed keys\\" + \\"memory pressure\\" + \\"without redesigning the schema\\".\\nDo not confuse with: skipping large groups (data loss), a separate pipeline for heavy users (extra operations), or parking events in Cloud SQL.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"137","question":"A Dataflow streaming job uses fixed windows for aggregating metrics. However, when late-arriving data arrives after the window closes, recomputed outputs overwrite prior results, creating inconsistencies. You need an approach that preserves output history. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Use session windows to avoid recomputation entirely","B":"Use dual pipelines to store early and late results separately","C":"Disable accumulation mode to prevent window recomputation","D":"Use Dataflow accumulation with timestamped versions of each update"},"correct":"D","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: D. With triggers and accumulating panes, Beam/Dataflow re-emits an updated result whenever late data arrives within the allowed lateness. If each pane is written with its timing (PaneInfo, e.g. EARLY/ON_TIME/LATE, plus a processing timestamp) and appended instead of overwritten, you keep the full history of the window\'s results.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) session windows change how events are grouped; late data still updates them. B) two separate pipelines duplicate logic and cost. C) discarding mode or turning off accumulation does not stop late firings; it just loses context from earlier panes.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"139","question":"A pipeline periodically refreshes a Cloud Storage dataset containing millions of small metadata files. Listing these files increasingly slows down downstream tasks. How can you improve efficiency?","options":{"A":"Merge the metadata files manually using Cloud Console file operations","B":"Use Dataflow to compact small files into larger columnar bundles","C":"Use Pub/Sub notifications to speed up Cloud Storage directory listing","D":"Use BigQuery to query metadata directly from Cloud Storage paths"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Use Dataflow to compact those objects into a few large columnar bundles (Parquet/Avro).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You read the prefix, group, and write files of hundreds of MB.\\n• Later jobs list and open orders of magnitude fewer objects.\\n• Same content, different physical layout.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"millions of small metadata files\\" + \\"listing slows downstream\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Pub/Sub notifications (they announce NEW objects; they do not speed a LIST of millions already there), merging by hand in the Console, or \\"query paths in BigQuery\\" without compacting.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"211","question":"Your TensorFlow CNN underfits even with long training. Both training and validation accuracy remain low. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Increase model capacity by adding deeper layers or more filters to capture richer patterns","B":"Increase dropout which reduces model complexity and deepens underfitting","C":"Apply stronger augmentation which typically makes learning harder for an already weak model","D":"Decrease learning rate though this rarely fixes insufficient representational power"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Increase model capacity (more layers, more filters/channels, wider kernels) so it can represent richer patterns.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Underfitting = high bias: the model is too simple for the task.\\n• More capacity lowers train error and, if you do not overshoot, validation error too.\\n• Also review features, epochs, and whether regularization is choking learning.\\n\\nExam tip: train and val both low at the same time.\\nDo not confuse with: more dropout or more augmentation (they make learning harder and worsen underfitting) or lowering the LR as the first move (it does not add representational power).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"212","question":"Your XGBoost model shows good training accuracy but poor validation accuracy. Trees appear too deep. What should you change?","options":{"A":"Increase number of estimators though deeper ensembles worsen overfitting","B":"Reduce max depth and increase regularization to constrain model complexity","C":"Increase learning rate which speeds training but worsens overfitting","D":"Disable sampling which usually increases variance and harms generalization"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When an XGBoost model exhibits high training accuracy but poor validation accuracy, it is overfitting. Reducing max depth and increasing regularization constrains tree complexity and improves generalization on unseen data."},{"id":"214","question":"Distributed TensorFlow shows poor scaling because GPUs communicate too often. What helps reduce communication costs?","options":{"A":"Increase hidden size which increases compute but not reduce communication frequency","B":"Train for fewer epochs though this does not address scaling efficiency","C":"Reduce dropout which affects regularization but not communication frequency","D":"Increase batch size or apply gradient accumulation to reduce synchronization events"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Increasing the batch size or applying gradient accumulation reduces the number of gradient synchronization events across GPU workers, significantly lowering communication overhead during distributed training."},{"id":"216","question":"Your TensorFlow model experiences unstable loss when trained on TPU. Gradients fluctuate widely. What is the best fix?","options":{"A":"Remove batch normalization though instability often arises from large effective batch sizes","B":"Reduce global batch size or apply gradient clipping to stabilize gradients","C":"Use fewer TPU cores though the instability is not related to core count","D":"Increase learning rate though this typically amplifies instability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Training on TPUs often involves large effective batch sizes that can cause gradient spikes. Reducing global batch size or applying gradient clipping prevents gradient explosion and stabilizes training loss."},{"id":"218","question":"Your XGBoost binary classifier produces extremely imbalanced predictions. What tuning change addresses the issue?","options":{"A":"Reduce gamma to allow trees to grow more shallow structures.","B":"Increase colsample bytree to include more features per split.","C":"Adjust scale_pos weight to reflect class distribution balance.","D":"Increase subsample values to reduce the effect of minority samples."},"correct":"C","explanation":"For imbalanced binary classification tasks in XGBoost, adjusting scale_pos weight scales the gradient steps for the positive minority class, helping the algorithm learn balanced decision boundaries."},{"id":"220","question":"A TensorFlow RNN model is too slow during training. What is the best performance optimization?","options":{"A":"Replace LSTM layers with deeper LSTM stacks for more GPU usage.","B":"Use CuDNN-optimized LSTM layers to accelerate RNN computation.","C":"Increase dropout within LSTM layers to reduce computation.","D":"Decrease sequence length without altering model design."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Standard recurrent layers require multiple individual GPU kernel launches. Using CuDNN-optimized LSTM layers consolidates these operations into high-performance NVIDIA routines, significantly accelerating RNN training."},{"id":"222","question":"Your TensorFlow model trains slowly due to Python loops inside the model graph. What improves performance?","options":{"A":"Increase optimizer learning rate though this does not improve execution speed","B":"Add GPUs which does not solve Python execution bottlenecks","C":"Increase dropout which has no effect on model graph execution","D":"Convert Python logic into TensorFlow ops to eliminate Python bottlenecks"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Python loops inside a model graph create execution bottlenecks because they cannot be optimized by the TensorFlow runtime. Converting Python logic into native TensorFlow tensor operations eliminates these bottlenecks and enables full graph optimization."},{"id":"224","question":"You are deploying a new model version to Vertex AI and want zero downtime while gradually shifting traffic for testing. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Configure a Vertex AI endpoint with traffic split between the old and new model versions.","B":"Deploy the new model to a separate endpoint and manually route traffic through Cloud Load Balancing.","C":"Deploy the new model to the same endpoint by overriding the previous version entirely.","D":"Use Cloud Functions to proxy requests between endpoints for gradual migration."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vertex AI endpoints natively support multi-model deployment with percentage-based traffic splitting. This enables zero-downtime canary deployments and gradual traffic shifts without requiring external infrastructure changes."},{"id":"226","question":"You want to gradually change the model threshold used to convert probabilities to binary decisions without redeploying the model itself. Downstream systems want stability. What is the best design?","options":{"A":"Hardcode the new threshold value inside the model container and rebuild images frequently.","B":"Make the threshold configurable via an external config service and update it under change control.","C":"Ask downstream systems to adjust the threshold locally on whatever probabilities they receive.","D":"Retrain the model with a different loss function so the probabilities map better to decisions."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Decoupling post-processing logic from the container artifact allows operational flexibility. Making the classification threshold configurable via an external config service enables safe, controlled updates without rebuilding or redeploying model images."},{"id":"228","question":"You suspect that a newly deployed model performs well overall but clusters of low-performing segments exist for specific user groups. You want to detect this automatically. What is the best strategy?","options":{"A":"Compute global accuracy metrics daily and accept small local variations in performance.","B":"Use sliced evaluation to compute metrics per segment and store them for analysis and alerts.","C":"Enable more verbose logging for predictions and review random samples manually.","D":"Retrain the model with more data and assume performance improves across all user groups."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Global aggregate metrics can obscure localized performance degradation in specific user subsets. Sliced evaluation systematically computes and tracks performance metrics across key categorical segments to automatically detect hidden bias or cohort failures."},{"id":"230","question":"A model deployed to Vertex AI is receiving invalid inputs that cause failures. You want to enforce input validation before inference. What should you implement?","options":{"A":"Add input validation logic directly inside the model serving container before prediction.","B":"Reject any invalid requests at the network load balancer level using basic HTTP rules.","C":"Disable online prediction and require all clients to send prevalidated requests.","D":"Use BigQuery to verify inputs asynchronously prior to sending predictions."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Embedding input schema validation and error-handling logic directly inside the model serving container guarantees that malformed payloads are safely intercepted and rejected before prediction execution."},{"id":"232","question":"Your ML pipeline produces several artifacts, including preprocessing transformations and model binaries. You need centralized versioning for these. Which Vertex AI feature supports this?","options":{"A":"Vertex AI Artifact Registry for tracking and versioning ML artifacts across workflow stages.","B":"Cloud Storage lifecycle rules configured to retain historical file versions indefinitely.","C":"Cloud Build cache storage to store intermediate binary versions.","D":"BigQuery table partitions to maintain historical configurations."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vertex AI Artifact Registry provides a centralized, secure repository designed to track, version, and manage ML artifacts, containers, and dependency packages across MLOps workflow stages."},{"id":"234","question":"A new version of your Vertex AI online prediction model causes unpredictable latency spikes. Resource metrics show identical utilization patterns as the previous version. You must identify the root cause efficiently. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Enable per-request prediction logging to compare model execution timelines between versions.","B":"Enable Cloud Profiler on the model containers to generate tightly sampled performance traces.","C":"Revert autoscaling settings to force identical replica counts across both versions.","D":"Disable model monitoring to remove external overhead from the inference request path."},"correct":"A","explanation":"When hardware utilization metrics are identical but latency spikes occur, enabling per-request prediction logging allows you to trace request timelines and isolate software-level or payload-handling bottlenecks between model versions."},{"id":"236","question":"Your model requires frequent redeployment due to rapid data changes. You want to minimize container build times, speed up CI/CD, and avoid rebuilding the entire image for small code changes. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Use a multi-stage container design that separates static dependencies from frequently changing code.","B":"Build a single-layer image containing all dependencies and code for simplified deployment.","C":"Store all code and dependencies in Cloud Storage and mount them dynamically at runtime.","D":"Rebuild containers using Cloud Build triggers that always recompile all layers for consistency."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Structuring container images to separate heavy, static dependencies into early layers and frequently changing application code into final layers maximizes Docker layer caching, drastically reducing CI/CD build times."},{"id":"238","question":"Your team deploys a Vertex AI model that consumes embeddings generated from a separate feature pipeline. Due to version inconsistencies, the deployed model sometimes receives embeddings from incompatible transformation logic. You must ensure consistent feature-generation versions across training and serving. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Embed transformation metadata directly inside prediction requests supplied by client systems.","B":"Use the Vertex AI Feature Store to manage transformation versions consistently across pipelines.","C":"Create manual version files in Cloud Storage that map each model to specific embeddings.","D":"Require downstream services to track transformation logic independently of training workflows."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vertex AI Feature Store serves as a centralized repository for curated features and embeddings. It eliminates training-serving skew by guaranteeing consistent feature versions across offline training and online serving pipelines."},{"id":"240","question":"A custom model deployed on Vertex AI shows random container restarts. Logs indicate memory spikes caused by large input payloads. You need to prevent these restarts with minimal code changes. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Compress prediction payloads and decompress them inside the container during request handling.","B":"Adjust request size limits and implement defensive batching to reduce memory consumption.","C":"Reduce max concurrent requests and set strict request timeouts for better error isolation.","D":"Increase machine type RAM to allow larger payloads without modifying inference behavior."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Large payload spikes can exceed container memory limits, causing out-of-memory restarts. Enforcing request size limits and implementing defensive batching controls RAM usage and ensures container stability with minimal code changes."},{"id":"242","question":"Your model inference service connects to external APIs for enrichment, which occasionally become slow. Latency SLAs are violated when external services degrade. You must protect the model endpoint. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Call external APIs directly inside the model for every request regardless of performance.","B":"Add circuit breakers and timeouts, and use cached results when external services degrade.","C":"Increase endpoint machine size so the model can perform more calls in parallel to external APIs.","D":"Disable enrichment when APIs slow down without any logging or fallback mechanism."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Synchronous calls to external APIs create vulnerability to third-party degradation. Implementing circuit breakers, timeouts, and fallback caching prevents latency SLAs from being violated when external enrichment services slow down."},{"id":"244","question":"You maintain a shared Vertex AI Feature Store for multiple teams. One team plans to deprecate a feature that another model still uses in production. You must handle deprecation safely. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Remove the feature from Feature Store immediately and inform all teams after deletion.","B":"Clone the feature into a separate project and ask dependent teams to migrate quickly.","C":"Disable writes to the feature while leaving old values available indefinitely for consumers.","D":"Mark the feature as deprecated, communicate timelines, and monitor usage before removal."},"correct":"D","explanation":"In a multi-tenant feature store, abruptly deleting shared features can break production models. Safely handling deprecation requires marking the feature as deprecated, communicating clear timelines, and monitoring consumer usage to ensure zero dependencies before physical removal."}]},{"id":"gcp-machine-learning-march-2025","title":"GCP Machine Learning — March 2025","questions":[{"id":"2","question":"What is the purpose of Vertex AI Model Registry?","options":{"A":"Train models using different frameworks","B":"Create custom containers for model serving","C":"Store and version registered models for deployment","D":"Visualize training metrics"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Vertex AI Model Registry helps manage model versions and supports downstream deployment workflows to prediction endpoints."},{"id":"3","question":"Which service allows you to create an end-to-end pipeline for model deployment and continuous training in Vertex AI?","options":{"A":"Cloud Scheduler","B":"Cloud Composer","C":"Vertex AI Pipelines","D":"Dataflow"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vertex AI Pipelines enable automated, repeatable, and serverless orchestration of ML workflows using MLOps best practices."},{"id":"4","question":"When deploying a model using a custom container in Vertex AI, what is a required file to expose the model\'s prediction logic?","options":{"A":"requirements.txt","B":"Dockerfile","C":"model_config.yaml","D":"predictor.py"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Dockerfile is required to build a custom container image, which packages the HTTP serving application and dependencies needed to expose the model logic."},{"id":"5","question":"How does Vertex AI support zero-downtime model updates in production?","options":{"A":"By automatically deleting the old model","B":"Using multi-region model caching","C":"Supporting versioned deployments and traffic splitting","D":"Automatically retraining the model before deployment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vertex AI allows you to deploy multiple model versions to a single endpoint and split traffic between them to ensure safe, zero-downtime rollouts."},{"id":"6","question":"Which monitoring feature in Vertex AI allows tracking of model drift?","options":{"A":"BigQuery ML","B":"Explainable AI","C":"Model Monitoring","D":"Cloud Logging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vertex AI Model Monitoring helps detect data drift, prediction skew, and training-serving skew by evaluating incoming production features against a baseline."},{"id":"7","question":"What kind of metrics can Vertex AI Model Monitoring detect for deployed models?","options":{"A":"CPU and memory usage only","B":"Data drift, prediction drift, and skew","C":"Cost of training jobs","D":"Training job duration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vertex AI Model Monitoring tracks statistical distance deviations in incoming request features to flag data drift, prediction drift, and training-serving skew."},{"id":"8","question":"Which MLOps practice involves automatically testing model training code when pushed to a repository?","options":{"A":"Continuous Deployment","B":"Continuous Monitoring","C":"Continuous Integration","D":"Model Validation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CI in MLOps ensures that code changes, data validation tests, and training scripts are automatically tested and verified when committed to a repository."},{"id":"9","question":"What is the benefit of using Vertex AI with CI/CD tools like Cloud Build and Cloud Source Repositories?","options":{"A":"Free model hosting","B":"Manual model versioning","C":"Automating retraining and redeployment workflows","D":"Removing the need for pipelines"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Integrating CI/CD tools like Cloud Build with Vertex AI supports automated, hands-free training, verification, and deployment of models based on triggers."},{"id":"10","question":"You are working on a binary classification problem with an imbalanced dataset. Which evaluation metric is most appropriate for selecting a model?","options":{"A":"Accuracy","B":"Mean Absolute Error (MAE)","C":"F1 Score","D":"R-squared"},"correct":"C","explanation":"F1 Score balances precision and recall, making it highly effective for evaluation on imbalanced classification datasets where vanilla accuracy can be highly misleading."},{"id":"11","question":"How do you specify the time column in a time-series model?","options":{"A":"OPTIONS(TIMESTAMP_COL=\'timestamp’)","B":"OPTIONS(TIME_COLUMN=\'timestamp\')","C":"OPTIONS(DATE_FIELD=\'timestamp’)","D":"OPTIONS(DATETIME_COL=\'timestamp’)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The TIMESTAMP_COL option is required for time-series models (such as ARIMA_PLUS) in BigQuery ML to define the chronological/temporal column."},{"id":"12","question":"Which TensorFlow method is best used for early stopping during model training?","options":{"A":"tf.keras.callbacks.ReduceLROnPlateau","B":"tf.keras.callbacks.ModelCheckpoint","C":"tf.keras.callbacks.EarlyStopping","D":"tf.keras.metrics.AUC"},"correct":"C","explanation":"EarlyStopping halts training when a monitored metric (like validation loss) stops improving for a consecutive number of epochs, preventing overfitting."},{"id":"13","question":"Which of the following best explains why you would choose XGBoost over a neural network for tabular data?","options":{"A":"Neural networks train faster than XGBoost","B":"XGBoost provides interpretability and often outperforms NN on structured data","C":"Neural networks cannot handle categorical variables","D":"XGBoost requires GPU acceleration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"XGBoost is highly effective on tabular/structured datasets, trains efficiently without massive architectures, and provides clean feature importance metrics."},{"id":"14","question":"In TensorFlow, which API should you use for building custom training loops?","options":{"A":"tf.estimator","B":"tf.keras.Sequential","C":"tf.function","D":"tf.GradientTape"},"correct":"D","explanation":"tf.GradientTape is used for recording forward-pass operations for automatic differentiation, making it the foundational component for building custom Keras training loops."},{"id":"15","question":"You are using XGBoost with the learning rate set too high. What is the likely outcome?","options":{"A":"Model will converge too slowly","B":"Model might not converge and could overshoot the optimal solution","C":"Model will underfit","D":"Feature importance will be skewed"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A learning rate that is set too high can cause gradient descent steps to overshoot the global minima or diverge entirely, resulting in non-convergence."},{"id":"16","question":"Which hyperparameter in XGBoost controls model complexity by limiting tree depth?","options":{"A":"learning_rate","B":"gamma","C":"max_depth","D":"n_estimators"},"correct":"C","explanation":"max_depth limits how deep individual decision trees can grow. Lowering it directly restricts model complexity and mitigates overfitting."},{"id":"17","question":"What is the purpose of the batch_size hyperparameter in TensorFlow?","options":{"A":"Number of training steps per epoch","B":"Number of layers in the model","C":"Number of samples processed before model weights are updated","D":"Total number of data points"},"correct":"C","explanation":"batch_size defines the specific window size of data samples propagated through the network before backpropagation calculates the gradients and updates weights."},{"id":"18","question":"Which optimizer generally performs better for deep learning models in TensorFlow?","options":{"A":"SGD","B":"RMSProp","C":"Adam","D":"Momentum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adam combines the core concepts of both RMSProp and Momentum, using adaptive learning rates that make it the gold standard baseline for modern deep learning architectures."},{"id":"19","question":"A TensorFlow model is overfitting the training data. Which strategy would help mitigate this?","options":{"A":"Increase learning rate","B":"Add more dense layers","C":"Reduce dropout","D":"Use L2 regularization"},"correct":"D","explanation":"L2 regularization penalizes large weights by adding their squared value to the loss function, forcing the model to learn smoother transformations and reducing overfitting."},{"id":"20","question":"You need to process a large stream of real-time logs and write the results to BigQuery. Which GCP service is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"Cloud Pub/Sub","B":"Cloud Dataflow","C":"Cloud Composer","D":"BigQuery ML"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Dataflow is built on Apache Beam for managed batch and stream processing. It scales horizontally to ingest from Pub/Sub streams and output directly into BigQuery."},{"id":"21","question":"Which of the following ensures schema consistency in data stored in BigQuery?","options":{"A":"Schema auto-detection","B":"Schema enforcement via defined schema","C":"Appending JSON directly","D":"Using federated queries"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Explicitly defining a schema on a destination BigQuery table ensures incoming records adhere to strict structural constraints, maintaining clean data integrity."},{"id":"22","question":"You\'re building a pipeline to detect anomalies in streaming sensor data. What Dataflow feature helps maintain data order in a stream?","options":{"A":"Watermarks","B":"Job templates","C":"Shuffle","D":"Windowing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Windowing cuts unbounded streaming elements into finite chronological sets based on event time, which lets you analyze records in proper sequence for tasks like anomaly detection."},{"id":"23","question":"To efficiently query structured historical data and integrate with ML pipelines, which storage solution should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud Storage","B":"Cloud Spanner","C":"BigQuery","D":"Firestore"},"correct":"C","explanation":"BigQuery is optimized for analytical workloads, scales across large datasets efficiently, and bridges natively into downstream ML training infrastructure."},{"id":"24","question":"You need to validate and clean inconsistent input data before training a model. What tool should you use?","options":{"A":"TensorFlow Data Validation","B":"AI Platform Notebooks","C":"BigQuery ML","D":"Vertex AI Pipelines"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. TensorFlow Data Validation (TFDV) computes statistics, infers a schema and detects anomalies, missing values, skew and drift. That makes it the tool for checking input data quality before training, and it runs inside TFX or Vertex AI Pipelines.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) Notebooks (formerly AI Platform Notebooks, now Vertex AI Workbench) is a development environment, not a validation tool. C) BigQuery ML trains models with SQL. D) Pipelines orchestrates steps; TFDV is the step that validates.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"25","question":"Which BigQuery feature allows you to reduce query costs for repeated queries?","options":{"A":"On-demand pricing","B":"Slot reservations","C":"Materialized views","D":"Streaming insert"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Materialized views systematically cache and precompute common query expressions, saving execution time and reducing computational processing costs."},{"id":"26","question":"What method should you use in Cloud Dataflow to handle late-arriving data?","options":{"A":"Windowing with triggers","B":"Custom DoFn","C":"Aggregation","D":"Streaming insert"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dataflow leverages window triggers alongside allowed lateness windows to correctly recalculate and handle streaming data arriving late relative to the watermark."},{"id":"27","question":"Which BigQuery feature supports federated queries on external data sources like Cloud Storage?","options":{"A":"Scheduled Queries","B":"External Tables","C":"Datasets","D":"Partitions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"External tables expose structured source data living outside BigQuery (like flat files in Cloud Storage) as a reference table without needing to pay ingestion costs."},{"id":"28","question":"You need to build a scalable ETL pipeline that ingests data, processes it, and stores it in BigQuery daily. What service combination should you use?","options":{"A":"Dataflow + BigQuery","B":"Data Studio + BigQuery","C":"Cloud Functions + Firestore","D":"Pub/Sub + Cloud Storage"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Dataflow provides the required scalable computational core to process logic transformations, and BigQuery serves as the analytics data warehouse target."},{"id":"29","question":"You want to build a binary classification model in BigQuery ML to predict whether a customer will churn. Which model type should you use?","options":{"A":"linear_reg","B":"logistic_reg","C":"kmeans","D":"arima_plus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The logistic_reg configuration inside BigQuery ML is optimized for discrete binary classification problems like modeling customer churn predictions."},{"id":"30","question":"Which BigQuery ML function allows you to create a model using a SQL statement?","options":{"A":"CREATE PREDICTION MODEL","B":"CREATE MODEL","C":"TRAIN MODEL","D":"DEFINE MODEL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The CREATE MODEL SQL statement is the unified native declaration syntax used to define parameters and kick off training routines inside BigQuery ML."},{"id":"31","question":"What SQL clause is used in CREATE MODEL to specify the type of model?","options":{"A":"OPTIONS(MODEL_TYPE=...)","B":"WITH MODEL TYPE","C":"MODEL AS","D":"SET MODEL TYPE"},"correct":"A","explanation":"BigQuery ML captures critical model hyperparameters—such as model types, regularizations, and columns—directly inside the model parameter OPTIONS wrapper."},{"id":"32","question":"You want to evaluate the accuracy of your classification model. Which BigQuery ML function should you use?","options":{"A":"ML.EVALUATE","B":"ML.TRAIN","C":"ML.TEST","D":"ML.VALIDATE"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Calling ML.EVALUATE prints a clean table containing key standard scoring indicators including precision, precision-recall curve details, accuracy, and absolute recall values."},{"id":"33","question":"Which statement is TRUE about BigQuery ML model training?","options":{"A":"Models are trained outside of BigQuery using AutoML.","B":"BigQuery ML models must use external CSV data.","C":"Training is done using SQL inside BigQuery.","D":"You must install TensorFlow to use BigQuery ML."},"correct":"C","explanation":"BigQuery ML completely executes statistical models directly within the processing infrastructure utilizing standard SQL syntax, removing the need for external tools."},{"id":"34","question":"Which model type should you use to predict future sales using BigQuery ML?","options":{"A":"kmeans","B":"logistic_reg","C":"arima_plus","D":"matrix factorization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"arima_plus handles the seasonal trends, forecasting periods, anomalies, and adjustments native to time-series sequences like forward-looking retail projections."},{"id":"35","question":"You want to cluster customer data based on purchasing behavior. Which model type is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"logistic_reg","B":"kmeans","C":"linear_reg","D":"arima_plus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The kmeans configuration leverages unsupervised algorithms to isolate statistical cohorts and categorize vectors dynamically by feature similarities."},{"id":"36","question":"You want to recommend products based on user-item interactions. Which BigQuery ML model type should you use?","options":{"A":"logistic_reg","B":"kmeans","C":"matrix factorization","D":"arima_plus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. MATRIX_FACTORIZATION, which implements collaborative filtering.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• It decomposes the user \xd7 item matrix into latent vectors for users and for items.\\n• It predicts a user\'s affinity for items they have not interacted with.\\n• Options: USER_COL, ITEM_COL, RATING_COL, NUM_FACTORS, FEEDBACK_TYPE.\\n• Recommendations with ML.RECOMMEND.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"recommendations\\", \\"users who bought X\\", \\"collaborative filtering\\".\\nDo not confuse with: logistic_reg (classification), kmeans (segmentation), or arima_plus (forecasting).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"37","question":"In BigQuery ML, how can you use a trained model to generate predictions?","options":{"A":"ML.PREDICT(MODEL ...)","B":"ML.APPLY(MODEL ...)","C":"PREDICT FROM MODEL","D":"EXECUTE MODEL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. With the ML.PREDICT function on the model and a query that supplies the input data.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• SELECT * FROM ML.PREDICT(MODEL `ds.model`, (SELECT * FROM `ds.new_customers`));\\n• It returns the input columns plus predicted_<label> (and probabilities in classification).\\n• To save results: CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT ... FROM ML.PREDICT(...).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"generate predictions in BigQuery ML\\".\\nDo not confuse with: ML.APPLY, PREDICT FROM MODEL, or EXECUTE MODEL, which do not exist.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"38","question":"You notice model performance degradation over time. What should you do first in Vertex AI?","options":{"A":"Increase instance size","B":"Enable automatic hyperparameter tuning","C":"Review Vertex AI Model Monitoring alerts","D":"Rebuild the training container"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Review Vertex AI Model Monitoring alerts and statistics to understand the cause (drift or skew on specific features).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Model Monitoring shows which features changed in distribution and by how much.\\n• With that diagnosis you decide: retrain on recent data, fix preprocessing, or review the data source.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"diagnose first\\", \\"degradation over time\\".\\nDo not confuse with: increasing instance size (latency, not quality), hyperparameter tuning (does not address data change), or rebuilding the container (does not change the model).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"39","question":"Which of the following is required to enable Model Monitoring in Vertex AI?","options":{"A":"Enable billing export","B":"Define objective functions","C":"Set up a sampling strategy and alerting thresholds","D":"Provide a Dockerfile for serving"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. A sampling strategy (sampling rate) and per-feature alerting thresholds, plus the monitoring interval and who to notify.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Sampling rate: what % of requests are logged and analyzed (controls cost).\\n• Monitoring interval: how often it is computed (e.g. every hour).\\n• Thresholds: the distance at which an alert fires.\\n• For skew, also the training dataset as a baseline.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"sampling\\", \\"thresholds\\", \\"alerts\\".\\nDo not confuse with: billing export (cost), objective functions (training/tuning), or Dockerfile (serving).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"40","question":"Which CI/CD tool in Google Cloud helps create automated workflows for building, testing, and deploying ML models?","options":{"A":"Cloud Functions","B":"Cloud Build","C":"BigQuery","D":"Cloud Logging"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud Build: a serverless CI/CD service that runs steps defined in a cloudbuild.yaml file.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Each step runs in a container (tests, docker build, gcloud, Python scripts).\\n• Triggers: push or pull request on GitHub/GitLab/Bitbucket, Pub/Sub, or manual.\\n• It stores images in Artifact Registry and can launch Vertex AI Pipelines or deploy to endpoints.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"CI/CD\\", \\"automated build, test, deploy\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Functions/Cloud Run functions (code on events), BigQuery (warehouse), or Cloud Logging (logs).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"41","question":"What happens if a deployed Vertex AI model fails due to a change in input schema?","options":{"A":"The model self-heals","B":"Prediction calls return errors","C":"Vertex AI updates the model automatically","D":"The model continues predicting without issue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Prediction calls return errors (or incorrect results), because the model expects features with the names, types, and order it was trained on.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The endpoint does not adapt the model; it only runs it on what it receives.\\n• Fix: update the client or preprocessing, or retrain and redeploy with the new schema.\\n• Prevention: data and schema validation in the pipeline and in the client.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"schema change\\" → prediction errors.\\nDo not confuse with: \\"the model self-heals\\", \\"Vertex AI updates it automatically\\", or \\"it keeps working\\": Vertex AI does none of that.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"42","question":"In Vertex AI, what is the primary way to trigger a model retraining pipeline on a regular basis?","options":{"A":"Cloud SQL","B":"Cloud Scheduler","C":"BigQuery Scheduled Queries","D":"Cloud Spanner"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud Scheduler, the managed cron that can fire the pipeline with a cron expression.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Cloud Scheduler calls the Vertex AI API (or a Cloud Run function or a Pub/Sub topic) that creates the PipelineJob.\\n• Today Vertex AI Pipelines also has native scheduling: PipelineJob.create_schedule(cron=...) (Vertex AI Scheduler API).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"periodically\\", \\"every night/week\\", \\"cron\\".\\nDo not confuse with: BigQuery Scheduled Queries (schedules SQL; useful for retraining BigQuery ML models, not Vertex AI pipelines), Cloud SQL and Spanner (databases).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"43","question":"Which of the following is a benefit of using managed endpoints in Vertex AI?","options":{"A":"Manual scaling is required","B":"Enables local model testing","C":"Simplifies deployment with automatic scaling and monitoring","D":"Requires setting up VMs manually"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. They simplify deployment: Vertex AI manages the infrastructure, autoscaling, and monitoring integration.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You define machine type, accelerators, and min/max replicas.\\n• Autoscaling adds or removes nodes based on load (CPU/GPU, default target 60%).\\n• Latency, error, and traffic metrics in Cloud Monitoring; optional Model Monitoring.\\n• Traffic split across versions.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"managed\\", \\"automatic scaling\\", \\"without administering VMs\\".\\nDo not confuse with: manual scaling or manual VMs (exactly what this avoids), or local tests (done outside the endpoint).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"44","question":"You want to deploy a model and monitor input feature distribution. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud Pub/Sub","B":"Vertex AI Workbench","C":"Vertex AI Model Monitoring","D":"Cloud SQL"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Vertex AI Model Monitoring on the endpoint, configured to detect drift (and skew if you provide training data).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• It logs a sample of requests and computes per-feature distributions.\\n• Each interval is compared to the baseline and alerts if the threshold is crossed.\\n• It shows per-feature distribution charts in the console.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"monitor feature distribution\\", \\"detect drift after deploying\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Pub/Sub (messaging), Vertex AI Workbench (notebooks for development), or Cloud SQL (database).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"45","question":"Which metric is useful for measuring real-time model performance degradation in production?","options":{"A":"Training loss","B":"CPU utilization","C":"Prediction accuracy on recent labeled data","D":"Number of input records"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Accuracy (or another quality metric) computed on recent labeled data, comparing predictions with ground truth.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You store predictions (e.g. endpoint request-response logging to BigQuery).\\n• When the real label arrives (confirmed fraud, actual churn), you join them and compute the metric.\\n• If it drops versus the baseline, you trigger retraining.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"real model performance\\", \\"ground truth\\".\\nDo not confuse with: training loss (that is the past), CPU (infrastructure), or number of records (volume, not quality).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"46","question":"Which file is typically used to define a custom pipeline in Vertex AI using Kubeflow Pipelines?","options":{"A":"config.yaml","B":"pipeline.py","C":"requirements.txt","D":"dataset.csv"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. In a Python script (e.g. pipeline.py) that uses the Kubeflow Pipelines (KFP) SDK.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You define components with @dsl.component and the pipeline with @dsl.pipeline.\\n• You compile with compiler.Compiler().compile(..., \'pipeline.yaml\').\\n• You submit the YAML as a PipelineJob to Vertex AI (or upload it to a template repo in Artifact Registry).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"define a pipeline\\" → Python code with the KFP SDK.\\nDo not confuse with: config.yaml (not the definition format; YAML is the compiled output), requirements.txt (dependencies), or dataset.csv (data).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"47","question":"Which loss function is most appropriate for a multi-class classification task in TensorFlow?","options":{"A":"Binary Crossentropy","B":"Mean Squared Error","C":"Sparse Categorical Crossentropy","D":"Hinge Loss"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Sparse Categorical Crossentropy, the categorical cross-entropy that accepts labels as integers.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Output layer with N neurons and softmax (or logits with from_logits=True).\\n• model.compile(loss=\'sparse_categorical_crossentropy\', ...).\\n• If labels are one-hot, you use categorical_crossentropy.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"multiclass\\" + \\"integer labels\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Binary Crossentropy (2 classes or multilabel), Mean Squared Error (regression), or Hinge Loss (SVM-style classifiers).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"48","question":"When using XGBoost, what effect does increasing the subsample parameter have?","options":{"A":"Increases the number of trees","B":"Decreases overfitting by using a subset of data","C":"Reduces tree depth","D":"Improves accuracy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. It controls the fraction of training rows randomly sampled to build each tree (default 1.0 = all). Using less than 1 adds randomness and reduces overfitting.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Typical values 0.5–0.9.\\n• Lower subsample: more randomness and less overfitting, with possible underfitting if it is too low.\\n• Raising it toward 1.0 uses more data per tree and removes that regularization.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"subset of data per tree\\" → subsample.\\nDo not confuse with: n_estimators (number of trees), max_depth (depth), or colsample_bytree (fraction of columns).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"49","question":"Which technique is used in TensorFlow to adjust the learning rate during training?","options":{"A":"EarlyStopping","B":"ReduceLROnPlateau","C":"Dropout","D":"GradientTape"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. The callback tf.keras.callbacks.ReduceLROnPlateau.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• monitor=\'val_loss\', factor=0.1 (multiplies the lr), patience=N epochs without improvement, min_lr as a floor.\\n• Smaller steps help fine-tune near the minimum when training stalls.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"reduce learning rate when the metric stops improving\\".\\nDo not confuse with: EarlyStopping (stops training), Dropout (regularization), or GradientTape (computes gradients in custom loops).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"50","question":"What is one advantage of using XGBoost’s gpu_hist tree method?","options":{"A":"Better interpretability","B":"Lower model accuracy","C":"Faster training with GPU","D":"Avoids overfitting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Much faster training on large datasets, because histogram construction and split search run on the GPU.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• In XGBoost 2.0 and later: tree_method=\'hist\' + device=\'cuda\' (gpu_hist is deprecated).\\n• Same histogram algorithm as on CPU, with equivalent results.\\n• On Vertex AI: a custom training job on a machine with a GPU (e.g. NVIDIA T4, L4).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"GPU\\" + \\"XGBoost\\" → faster training.\\nDo not confuse with: more interpretability, worse accuracy, or avoiding overfitting: the GPU only changes speed.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"51","question":"In TensorFlow, what does the model.fit() method do?","options":{"A":"Compiles the model","B":"Evaluates the model","C":"Trains the model","D":"Saves the model"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. It trains the model: it iterates over the data in batches and epochs, computes the loss, and updates the weights with the optimizer.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• model.fit(x, y, epochs=10, batch_size=32, validation_data=(xv, yv), callbacks=[...]).\\n• It returns a History object with loss and metrics per epoch.\\n• You must have called model.compile() first.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"train\\" → fit.\\nDo not confuse with: compile (configures optimizer, loss, and metrics), evaluate (measures on test data), or save (writes to disk).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"52","question":"Which TensorFlow function is used to compile a model before training?","options":{"A":"model.summary()","B":"model.evaluate()","C":"model.compile()","D":"model.predict()"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. model.compile().\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• model.compile(optimizer=\'adam\', loss=\'sparse_categorical_crossentropy\', metrics=[\'accuracy\']).\\n• It does not train or touch the data; it only prepares the model for fit.\\n• You can call it again to change optimizer or loss (e.g. in fine-tuning).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"configure optimizer, loss, and metrics\\".\\nDo not confuse with: summary (shows the architecture), evaluate (measures performance), or predict (generates predictions).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"53","question":"Why is hyperparameter tuning critical in ML model development?","options":{"A":"It eliminates the need for validation data","B":"It improves code readability","C":"It optimizes model performance","D":"It helps collect more data"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Because choosing hyperparameters well (learning rate, depth, regularization, batch size…) can improve model performance a lot.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You try combinations and compare a metric on the validation set.\\n• Methods: grid search, random search, Bayesian optimization.\\n• Always with validation data separate from test so you do not overfit the selection.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"optimize model performance\\".\\nDo not confuse with: eliminating the need for validation (the opposite; it needs it), improving code readability, or collecting more data.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"54","question":"What is the primary role of cross-validation in ML?","options":{"A":"Increases training speed","B":"Prevents data leakage","C":"Provides better evaluation by using multiple data splits","D":"Enhances feature extraction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. To give a more reliable evaluation of the model by training and testing it on several different partitions of the data.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• k-fold: you split the data into k parts; you train k times leaving a different part for validation.\\n• You average the metric; its deviation indicates model stability.\\n• Very useful on small datasets, where a single split is not representative.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"robust evaluation\\", \\"multiple splits\\", \\"model stability\\".\\nDo not confuse with: speeding up training (it makes it k times slower), preventing data leakage (it does not by itself), or extracting features.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"55","question":"Which of the following is NOT a hyperparameter in XGBoost?","options":{"A":"max_depth","B":"learning_rate","C":"subsample","D":"weight_decay"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. weight_decay, which is a neural-network optimizer concept (e.g. AdamW), not an XGBoost parameter.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The equivalent regularization in XGBoost is lambda (L2) and alpha (L1) on the leaf weights.\\n• max_depth, learning_rate (eta), and subsample are native hyperparameters.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"NOT from XGBoost\\" → the neural-network term.\\nDo not confuse with: lambda/reg_lambda, which does exist in XGBoost and plays a similar role.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"56","question":"Which practice ensures the integrity of data during transmission to BigQuery?","options":{"A":"Disabling encryption","B":"Base64 encoding","C":"TLS encryption","D":"Delayed replication"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. TLS encryption in transit, which prevents data from being intercepted or altered during transmission.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Google Cloud APIs (including BigQuery) only accept HTTPS/TLS connections.\\n• TLS encrypts and also verifies integrity (detects tampering).\\n• Inside Google\'s network there is also encryption in transit by default.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"in transit\\", \\"interception\\", \\"tampering\\".\\nDo not confuse with: disabling encryption (the opposite), Base64 (encoding, not encryption; anyone can reverse it), or delayed replication (protects nothing).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"57","question":"To automate a data pipeline that runs every 24 hours, which tool is most suitable?","options":{"A":"Cloud Scheduler","B":"Cloud Functions","C":"BigQuery ML","D":"Cloud Spanner"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud Scheduler: managed cron that fires a job at a fixed time.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You define a cron expression (e.g. \'0 2 * * *\' = every day at 2:00) and a time zone.\\n• Destination: HTTP endpoint (e.g. launch a Dataflow Flex Template or a PipelineJob), Pub/Sub topic, or App Engine.\\n• Configurable retries.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"every day\\", \\"at a fixed time\\", \\"time-based\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Functions (now Cloud Run functions; they run code but do not schedule by themselves), BigQuery ML (models), or Spanner (database).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"58","question":"You are validating a dataset for training. Which metric is least useful for checking class imbalance?","options":{"A":"Mean","B":"Distribution plot","C":"Frequency counts","D":"Standard deviation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. The mean of the features: it does not say how many examples there are of each class. To see imbalance you use per-class frequency counts or a distribution plot.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Frequencies: SELECT label, COUNT(*) ... GROUP BY label.\\n• Bar chart or histogram of the label.\\n• If one class is less than 10% of the total, consider imbalance techniques.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"least useful for imbalance\\" → a central-tendency statistic.\\nDo not confuse with: distribution plot and frequency counts, which show imbalance directly.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"59","question":"In Dataflow, what is a side input?","options":{"A":"An error message","B":"An auxiliary input to a DoFn","C":"The pipeline output","D":"A BigQuery result"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. An extra auxiliary input passed to a DoFn (inside a ParDo), besides the main PCollection, typically for lookups or configuration.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• It is created from another PCollection (View.asMap, asList, asSingleton) and broadcast to all workers.\\n• Each element of the main stream can query it (e.g. code table → names).\\n• It should be relatively small; for data that changes it is refreshed periodically.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"auxiliary data\\", \\"lookup\\", \\"enrich with a small table\\".\\nDo not confuse with: an error message, the pipeline output, or a BigQuery result.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"60","question":"Which service allows you to create reusable templates of your Dataflow jobs?","options":{"A":"Cloud Functions","B":"Cloud Composer","C":"Dataflow Flex Templates","D":"Vertex AI Pipelines"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Dataflow Flex Templates: they package the pipeline as a container image plus a spec file, and are launched with parameters.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The image goes to Artifact Registry and the template spec (JSON) to Cloud Storage.\\n• They are launched from the console, gcloud, the API, Cloud Scheduler, or Composer without a development environment.\\n• The pipeline graph is built at launch time, so parameters can change its structure.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"reusable\\", \\"parameterizable\\", \\"launch without code\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Functions (code on events), Cloud Composer (orchestrates, does not package Dataflow jobs), or Vertex AI Pipelines (ML workflows).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"61","question":"Which method is best for partitioning time-series data in BigQuery?","options":{"A":"Sharding","B":"Clustering on NULL","C":"Time-based partitioning","D":"Using random suffixes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Partition the table by time (a DATE/TIMESTAMP column or ingestion time), so each query reads only the partitions in the filtered range.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• PARTITION BY DATE(event_ts) in the DDL.\\n• A WHERE filter on the partition column does partition pruning: fewer bytes, lower cost.\\n• You can add partition expiration to delete old data automatically.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"filter by date\\", \\"time series\\", \\"reduce bytes scanned\\".\\nDo not confuse with: sharding (per-date tables like events_20250101, discouraged), clustering on NULL (makes no sense), or random suffixes (no pruning).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"62","question":"How can you ensure that sensitive information is not exposed in BigQuery datasets?","options":{"A":"Assign IAM roles to “allUsers”","B":"Disable logging","C":"Use column-level security and data masking","D":"Store data in external tables only"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. With column-level security (policy tags) and dynamic data masking.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You create a policy-tag taxonomy (e.g. \\"PII\\") and assign it to the sensitive columns.\\n• Only someone with Fine-Grained Reader on the tag sees the real value.\\n• With masking rules, other users see the masked value (hash, NULL, default) instead of an error.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"protect sensitive columns\\", \\"PII\\", \\"mask\\".\\nDo not confuse with: granting roles to allUsers (makes the data public), disabling logging (removes audit), or using only external tables (protects nothing).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"63","question":"What format is recommended for storing data in Cloud Storage to optimize performance in BigQuery?","options":{"A":"CSV","B":"JSON","C":"Avro","D":"Plain text"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Avro: a compressed binary format with the schema embedded, which BigQuery can read in parallel even when it is compressed.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The schema travels in the file, so there is no type inference.\\n• Compressed blocks are read in parallel; a gzip-compressed CSV or JSON is not.\\n• Parquet and ORC (columnar) are also good options.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"fastest format to load\\", \\"self-describing schema\\".\\nDo not confuse with: CSV and JSON (text, no types, slower and more fragile) or plain text.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"64","question":"Which of the following is NOT a responsibility of Cloud Dataflow?","options":{"A":"Job orchestration","B":"Stream and batch processing","C":"Automatic scaling","D":"Windowed aggregation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Job orchestration. Dataflow runs the processing; coordinating several jobs and their dependencies is done by an orchestrator such as Cloud Composer.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Dataflow: batch and streaming processing, worker autoscaling, and windows.\\n• Cloud Composer (Airflow): DAGs that launch Dataflow, BigQuery, Dataproc, etc. in order, with retries.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"NOT a Dataflow responsibility\\" → orchestration.\\nDo not confuse with: stream/batch processing, autoscaling, and windowed aggregation, which are core Dataflow functions.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"65","question":"Which of the following is a required component in a CREATE MODEL statement?","options":{"A":"MODEL_TARGET","B":"MODEL_INPUT","C":"MODEL_TYPE","D":"TARGET_FEATURE"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The MODEL_TYPE must be specified in the OPTIONS clause of a CREATE MODEL statement to determine the kind of model being trained."},{"id":"66","question":"Which clause is used to specify the column that the model should learn to predict?","options":{"A":"TARGET_COLUMN","B":"LABEL_COLUMN","C":"PREDICT_COLUMN","D":"OUTPUT_COLUMN"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. With the option INPUT_LABEL_COLS=[\'column\'] in OPTIONS, or by naming the column \'label\' in the SELECT (the quiz calls this LABEL_COLUMN).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• OPTIONS(MODEL_TYPE=\'LINEAR_REG\', INPUT_LABEL_COLS=[\'price\']).\\n• If omitted, BigQuery ML looks for a column named label.\\n• The other SELECT columns are used as features.\\n\\nExam tip: the option that names the label column.\\nDo not confuse with: TARGET_COLUMN, PREDICT_COLUMN, or OUTPUT_COLUMN, which do not exist.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"67","question":"What is the default data split used by BigQuery ML if no split is defined?","options":{"A":"50% training / 50% evaluation","B":"80% training / 20% evaluation","C":"90% training / 10% evaluation","D":"70% training / 30% evaluation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. It uses DATA_SPLIT_METHOD=\'AUTO_SPLIT\', which depends on the number of rows. For medium sizes that is about 80% training / 20% evaluation.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Fewer than 500 rows: no split; everything is used to train.\\n• Between 500 and 50,000 rows: 20% random for evaluation.\\n• More than 50,000 rows: it holds out about 10,000 rows for evaluation.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"default split\\" → 80/20 among the options.\\nDo not confuse with: 50/50, 90/10, or 70/30, which are not the default behavior.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"68","question":"You trained a model and want to understand which features contributed most to the predictions. Which function do you use?","options":{"A":"ML.PREDICT_EXPLAIN","B":"ML.FEATURE_IMPORTANCE","C":"ML.EXPLAIN_PREDICT","D":"ML.WEIGHTS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. ML.EXPLAIN_PREDICT, which returns the prediction together with each feature\'s attributions (Shapley-style values) for each row.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• SELECT * FROM ML.EXPLAIN_PREDICT(MODEL m, (SELECT ...), STRUCT(3 AS top_k_features)).\\n• It returns top_feature_attributions, baseline_prediction_value, and prediction_value.\\n• Available for linear, tree, DNN, tabular AutoML, ARIMA_PLUS (with its forecast variant) models, etc.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"why this prediction\\", \\"per-row feature contribution\\".\\nDo not confuse with: ML.PREDICT_EXPLAIN (does not exist), ML.WEIGHTS (linear-model coefficients), or ML.FEATURE_IMPORTANCE (global importance on tree models).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"69","question":"Which model supports time-series forecasting with external regressors in BigQuery ML?","options":{"A":"linear_reg","B":"arima_plus","C":"logistic_reg","D":"boosted_tree_regressor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. ARIMA_PLUS_XREG, the ARIMA_PLUS variant with external regressors (the quiz simply calls it arima_plus).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• MODEL_TYPE=\'ARIMA_PLUS_XREG\' with TIME_SERIES_TIMESTAMP_COL and TIME_SERIES_DATA_COL; the other columns are regressors.\\n• To forecast, ML.FORECAST needs the future values of those regressors.\\n• It keeps the trend, seasonality, and holiday decomposition.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"time series\\" + \\"external features / covariates\\".\\nDo not confuse with: linear_reg and boosted_tree_regressor (they do not model temporal seasonality by themselves), logistic_reg (classification).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"70","question":"What evaluation metric is typically used for regression models in BigQuery ML?","options":{"A":"Precision","B":"RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error)","C":"AUC","D":"F1 Score"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error), along with MAE and R\xb2; ML.EVALUATE returns these metrics for regression.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• RMSE = square root of the mean of squared errors; same unit as the label.\\n• ML.EVALUATE for regression returns mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error, mean_squared_log_error, median_absolute_error, r2_score, and explained_variance (RMSE = sqrt of mean_squared_error).\\n\\nExam tip: \'regression\', \'continuous numeric value\'.\\nDo not confuse with: precision, AUC, and F1 Score, which are classification metrics.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"71","question":"How can you schedule model retraining in BigQuery ML?","options":{"A":"By using RETRAIN MODEL","B":"With a BigQuery scheduled query","C":"Using AutoML pipelines","D":"By enabling real-time mode"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. With a BigQuery scheduled query that runs CREATE OR REPLACE MODEL on the updated data at the frequency you choose.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The scheduled query (BigQuery Data Transfer Service) is set daily, weekly, etc.\\n• CREATE OR REPLACE MODEL retrains and replaces the model with the new data.\\n• Alternatives for more complex flows: Vertex AI Pipelines (BigQuery ML components), Dataform, or Cloud Composer.\\n\\nExam tip: \'retrain in BigQuery ML periodically with SQL\'.\\nDo not confuse with: RETRAIN MODEL (does not exist), AutoML pipelines (not the BigQuery ML mechanism), or a \'real-time mode\' (does not exist).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"72","question":"What is a limitation of BigQuery ML’s kmeans clustering?","options":{"A":"Cannot process more than 10 rows","B":"Only works with categorical features","C":"Requires numeric input features","D":"Only works for time-series data"},"correct":"C","explanation":"No option is strictly correct. BigQuery ML k-means acepta features categ\xf3ricas (one-hot autom\xe1tico), as\xed que C es falsa y las otras opciones tambi\xe9n; la explicaci\xf3n original no corresponde a la pregunta. The marked option (C) is a documented misconception, so this question is excluded from graded practice.","disputed":true},{"id":"73","question":"You want to ensure that your model generalizes well. What should you apply?","options":{"A":"Oversampling","B":"Normalization","C":"Cross-validation","D":"Batch prediction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cross-validation: you evaluate the model on several validation partitions, which shows whether performance holds on data it did not see while training.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• k-fold: k trainings, each validated on a different slice.\\n• Stable performance across folds indicates good generalization; large swing indicates overfitting or too little data.\\n• It also lets you pick hyperparameters without touching the test set.\\n\\nExam tip: \'generalize\', \'unseen data\'.\\nDo not confuse with: oversampling (treats imbalance; done badly it can worsen generalization), normalization (scales features so training works better), or batch prediction (serving).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"74","question":"In the CI/CD context, what is \\"model validation\\"?","options":{"A":"Checking if a model fits in memory","B":"Ensuring the model training code has no syntax errors","C":"Testing model performance before deployment","D":"Verifying data schema"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. In ML CI/CD, model validation is the gate that evaluates a newly trained model on held-out data and checks it against thresholds or the current production model (accuracy, fairness, latency) before deployment. Only models that pass are promoted.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) memory fit is an infrastructure concern. B) syntax checks are code CI and unit tests. D) schema checks are data validation, a different step earlier in the pipeline.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"75","question":"You deployed a model to an endpoint and want to split traffic 80/20 between a new ersion and the old version. What should you configure?","options":{"A":"AutoML settings","B":"Endpoint traffic split","C":"Batch prediction pipeline","D":"Hyperparameter tuning job"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. The endpoint traffic split, with both models deployed on the same endpoint.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• endpoint.deploy(model=new, traffic_percentage=20) or traffic_split={\'old_id\': 80, \'new_id\': 20}.\\n• You can change the percentages later with no downtime.\\n• Compare metrics of both versions before moving to 100%.\\n\\nExam tip: \'split traffic 80/20\', \'A/B\', \'canary\'.\\nDo not confuse with: AutoML settings (training), a batch prediction pipeline (does not use endpoints), or a hyperparameter tuning job (training).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"76","question":"Which tool helps store metadata and lineage of your ML artifacts in Vertex AI?","options":{"A":"Cloud Storage","B":"TensorBoard","C":"ML Metadata Store","D":"Model Monitoring"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Vertex ML Metadata (the metadata store), based on ML Metadata (MLMD).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• It records artifacts (datasets, models, metrics), executions (steps that ran), and contexts (groupings such as an experiment or pipeline run).\\n• Vertex AI Pipelines feeds it automatically.\\n• It lets you answer: which data and code trained this model? (audit and reproducibility).\\n\\nExam tip: \'lineage\', \'metadata\', \'traceability\', \'audit\'.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Storage (stores files without lineage), TensorBoard (plots training metrics), or Model Monitoring (production).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"77","question":"What is the advantage of using Vertex AI Pipelines with caching enabled?","options":{"A":"Reduces disk space","B":"Avoids data validation","C":"Skips unchanged pipeline steps for efficiency","D":"Enables parallel processing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. It skips steps that did not change by reusing their outputs from previous runs, which saves time and cost.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The cache key combines the component definition and its inputs.\\n• If they match a previous successful execution, its outputs are reused without running it.\\n• Turn it off with enable_caching=False on the PipelineJob or task.set_caching_options(False) on a step.\\n\\nExam tip: \'do not recompute unchanged steps\', \'iterate faster\'.\\nDo not confuse with: reducing disk space, skipping data validation, or parallelizing (independent steps already run in parallel without cache).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"78","question":"What is the purpose of containerizing our model before deployment?","options":{"A":"It makes the model faster","B":"Allows you to use AutoML","C":"Ensures consistent environments across development and production","D":"Enables online learning as"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. A container packages the model with its exact runtime, libraries and serving code. The artifact you tested is identical to the one that runs in production (Vertex AI custom containers, Cloud Run, GKE), so you avoid \'works on my machine\' problems.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) a container does not make inference faster by itself. B) AutoML does not require you to containerize your model. D) online learning is a training design choice, unrelated to packaging.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"79","question":"Which GCP service allows centralized monitoring of logs, alerts, and metrics for ML workloads?","options":{"A":"Cloud Build","B":"Cloud Logging","C":"BigQuery","D":"Cloud Run"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud Logging, part of Google Cloud Observability together with Cloud Monitoring.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• It collects logs from Vertex AI (training jobs, endpoints), GKE, Cloud Run, Dataflow, etc.\\n• Log-based metrics turn log patterns into metrics you can alert on.\\n• Sinks export logs to BigQuery, Cloud Storage, or Pub/Sub; Log Analytics lets you query them with SQL.\\n\\nExam tip: \'centralize logs\', \'monitor ML workloads\'.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Build (CI/CD), BigQuery (warehouse), or Cloud Run (run containers).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"80","question":"You want to enable rollback in case a newly deployed model version fails. What feature can help with this?","options":{"A":"AutoML","B":"Traffic splitting with versioning","C":"Real-time explainability","D":"Data Labeling Service"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Traffic splitting with versioning: you keep the previous version deployed on the endpoint and, if the new one fails, you send 100% of traffic back to the old one.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Versions live in Model Registry; both are deployed on the same endpoint.\\n• Rollback is a traffic-split change, with no redeploy and no downtime.\\n• When the new version is validated, you retire the old one (undeploy).\\n\\nExam tip: \'rollback\', \'return to the previous version quickly\'.\\nDo not confuse with: AutoML (training), real-time explainability (explains predictions), or Data Labeling Service (labeling; also discontinued).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"81","question":"What does continuous delivery in MLOps mean?","options":{"A":"Building a container once a week","B":"Monitoring model predictions","C":"Automatically deploying validated models into production","D":"Creating notebooks for training"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Continuous delivery in MLOps means models that pass automated validation are automatically packaged and deployed (or made ready to deploy) to production, often through a model registry and gradual traffic split, with no manual hand-offs.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) a weekly container build is just scheduling. B) monitoring is a separate practice (continuous monitoring). D) notebooks are for experimentation, not delivery.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"82","question":"Which of the following ensures reproducibility in ML pipelines?","options":{"A":"Logging loss values","B":"Using the same hardware","C":"Versioning datasets, code, and models","D":"Saving training logs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Version datasets, code, and models (and the environment), so any result can be reconstructed.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Code in Git with commits referenced on each run.\\n• Data: snapshots or versions (dated tables, Vertex AI datasets, immutable Cloud Storage paths).\\n• Environment: container images with a fixed tag in Artifact Registry.\\n• Models: versions in Model Registry; lineage in ML Metadata.\\n\\nExam tip: \'reproducibility\', \'audit\', \'repeat an experiment\'.\\nDo not confuse with: logging the loss or saving logs (useful but not enough) or using the same hardware (guarantees nothing without versioned data and code).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"83","question":"A model trained using tf.keras.Sequential() is showing high training accuracy but poor validation accuracy. What is the issue?","options":{"A":"Underfitting","B":"Overfitting","C":"Data Leakage","D":"Class Imbalance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Overfitting: the model memorized the training set and does not generalize to new data.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• At training the error falls; on validation it stalls or rises.\\n• The net is too flexible (too many parameters) relative to the size or noise of the data.\\n• Mitigation: more data, dropout, L1/L2, early stopping, data augmentation, or a simpler architecture.\\n\\nExam tip: \'high train accuracy, low val accuracy\' → overfitting.\\nDo not confuse with: underfitting (bad on train and val), data leakage (val too good), or class imbalance (misleading accuracy on both).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"84","question":"What is the impact of using a very small batch size during training?","options":{"A":"Training will converge faster","B":"May lead to noisy gradients and slow convergence","C":"Uses more GPU memory","D":"Prevents overfitting"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Gradients are estimated from few samples: they are noisy and convergence is usually slower and less stable.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Each update uses an average of few examples; the descent direction varies a lot between steps.\\n• You need more steps to cover the dataset, and the learning rate often has to be lowered.\\n• It uses less GPU memory, not more.\\n\\nExam tip: \'very small batch\' → noisy gradients, slow convergence.\\nDo not confuse with: converges faster, uses more GPU, or \'prevents overfitting\' as the main effect.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"85","question":"Which of the following algorithms is best suited for time series forecasting?","options":{"A":"Logistic Regression","B":"XGBoost","C":"LSTM","D":"Naive Bayes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory): a recurrent net that keeps memory across the sequence and captures temporal dependencies.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• It processes the input step by step (t, t+1, …) and updates a cell state.\\n• The forget/input/output gates decide what to remember and what to forget.\\n• It works for series, text, and any sequential data.\\n\\nExam tip: \'time series forecasting\' + classic options → LSTM.\\nDo not confuse with: logistic regression and Naive Bayes (i.i.d. classification) or XGBoost (tabular; you can force it with lags, it is not natively sequential).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"86","question":"In TensorFlow, which method is used to save the model\'s architecture, weights, and optimizer state?","options":{"A":"model.predict()","B":"model.save()","C":"model.fit()","D":"model.compile()"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. model.save(): it serializes the full model so you can resume training or serve predictions.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Modern format: a path with the .keras extension (Keras v3).\\n• SavedModel (directory, often with saved_model.pb): the serving format on Vertex AI / TF Serving.\\n• Legacy .h5: architecture + weights + optimizer config.\\n\\nExam tip: \'save the whole model, weights and optimizer\' → model.save().\\nDo not confuse with: predict() (infers), fit() (trains), or compile() (loss/optimizer/metrics).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"87","question":"What effect does increasing the number of trees (n_estimators) in XGBoost typically have?","options":{"A":"Reduces model complexity","B":"Improves performance but increases risk of overfitting","C":"Decreases runtime","D":"Reduces variance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. It usually improves the fit and the training metric, but it raises the overfitting risk if you do not regularize or use early stopping.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Each new tree corrects residuals of the previous ones (boosting).\\n• More trees = more capacity; past a point it memorizes noise.\\n• Combine it with a low learning_rate and early_stopping_rounds.\\n\\nExam tip: \'more trees / n_estimators\' → better performance + overfitting risk.\\nDo not confuse with: reduces complexity, lowers runtime, or reduces variance (more trees take longer).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"88","question":"Which of the following techniques helps in automatically adjusting the learning rate in TensorFlow based on epoch count?","options":{"A":"EarlyStopping","B":"LearningRateScheduler","C":"ModelCheckpoint","D":"OptimizerDecay"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. LearningRateScheduler: it receives a schedule(epoch, lr) function and changes the learning rate at the start of each epoch.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• E.g. schedule = lambda epoch, lr: lr * 0.1 if epoch == 10 else lr.\\n• Useful for step decay, exponential decay, or a warmup defined up front.\\n• Passed in model.fit(..., callbacks=[tf.keras.callbacks.LearningRateScheduler(schedule)]).\\n\\nExam tip: \'adjust lr based on epoch count\', \'predefined schedule\'.\\nDo not confuse with: EarlyStopping (stops), ModelCheckpoint (saves), or OptimizerDecay (does not exist in Keras).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"89","question":"You are training a TensorFlow model and want to ensure it is compatible with mobile deployment. Which model format should you use?","options":{"A":".h5","B":".pb","C":".tflite","D":".cSV"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. .tflite (TensorFlow Lite): a compact FlatBuffer, optimized for inference on mobile, IoT, and edge.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• TFLiteConverter converts a SavedModel or Keras model to .tflite.\\n• Optional: float16 or int8 quantization for less latency and size.\\n• The TFLite interpreter runs on Android, iOS, microcontrollers, and also on a server.\\n\\nExam tip: \'mobile deployment\', \'embedded\' → .tflite.\\nDo not confuse with: .h5 (Keras), .pb (SavedModel/frozen graph for servers), or .csv (data).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"90","question":"Which optimizer in TensorFlow adapts the learning rate for each parameter individually?","options":{"A":"SGD","B":"Momentum","C":"Adam","D":"AdaGrad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Adam: it combines momentum (1st moment of the gradient) with a per-weight adaptive learning rate (2nd moment, like RMSProp).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Each parameter has its own effective step based on how \'noisy\' or large its gradient is.\\n• Default lr 0.001; it usually converges well with little tuning.\\n• AdaGrad is also per-parameter, but its lr only shrinks and falls short on long trainings.\\n\\nExam tip: \'adapts each parameter\'s lr\' + options SGD/Momentum/Adam/AdaGrad → Adam (the marked answer).\\nDo not confuse with: SGD and Momentum (a single global lr).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"91","question":"What is the purpose of dropout in training a neural network?","options":{"A":"Increase model complexity","B":"Prevent underfitting","C":"Reduce the size of input features","D":"Reduce overfitting by randomly disabling neurons"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. It reduces overfitting by randomly turning off a fraction of neurons at each training step, so they do not become codependent.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• In Keras: Dropout(0.3) between layers; the rate is the probability of turning off.\\n• At inference dropout is disabled and activations are scaled (or inverted dropout is used).\\n• It is equivalent to averaging many subnetworks (an implicit ensemble).\\n\\nExam tip: \'randomly disabling neurons\', \'reduce overfitting\'.\\nDo not confuse with: increasing complexity, avoiding underfitting, or reducing the number of input features.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"92","question":"To detect data drift in production ML systems, what tool should be used?","options":{"A":"Vertex AI Model Monitoring","B":"BigQuery","C":"Dataflow","D":"Cloud Scheduler"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Vertex AI Model Monitoring compares production prediction inputs (and feature attributions) with the training baseline or a previous window. It raises alerts on training-serving skew and on drift.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) BigQuery can store prediction logs, but it does not detect drift by itself. C) Dataflow is a processing engine. D) Cloud Scheduler only triggers jobs.\\n\\n2026 note: in current docs Vertex AI appears as Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform; the monitoring concept is the same.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"93","question":"How can you efficiently load large, historical datasets into BigQuery?","options":{"A":"Streaming insert","B":"Web UI upload","C":"Batch load using GCS files","D":"Appending row-by-row via API"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. With a batch load job from files in Cloud Storage (bq load or LOAD DATA), not with streaming or single-row inserts.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You upload Avro/Parquet/ORC/CSV/JSON to a bucket and launch the job; BigQuery reads in parallel.\\n• Batch load is not billed (you pay only the resulting storage).\\n• For terabyte-scale history this is the recommended path.\\n\\nExam tip: \'large historical datasets\' → batch load from GCS.\\nDo not confuse with: streaming insert (expensive, real time), UI upload (size limits), or row-by-row API (slow).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"94","question":"You\'re processing streaming user interaction data and need to group it into 5- minute intervals. What Dataflow feature helps?","options":{"A":"Global Window","B":"Fixed Window","C":"Side Input","D":"Sliding Window"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fixed Windows divide the stream into non- overlapping, equal-sized intervals (e.g., every 5 minutes), which is ideal for grouped analysis."},{"id":"95","question":"You want to optimize query performance on a non-partitioned BigQuery table with repetitive filtering on region and product. hat should you do?","options":{"A":"Convert to JSON format","B":"Add sharding","C":"Enable clustering on region and product","D":"Increase table size"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Add clustering on those columns: CLUSTER BY region, product. BigQuery groups similar rows into the same blocks and can skip blocks (block pruning).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Up to 4 clustering columns, from most to least frequent filter.\\n• Works on partitioned and unpartitioned tables (especially if >64 MB).\\n• Reduces bytes scanned and cost on repeated queries with those filters.\\n\\nExam tip: \'non-partitioned\' + \'repeated filter on columns\' → clustering.\\nDo not confuse with: convert to JSON, sharding, or \'increase the size\' (that worsens the scan).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"96","question":"Your Dataflow job reads from Pub/Sub, processes the data, and writes to Cloud Storage. What is the correct output mode for streaming?","options":{"A":"Append mode","B":"Batch mode","C":"Streaming mode with windowed writes","D":"Replace mode"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When writing streaming data to Cloud Storage, using windowed writes allows output files to be generated for each time window."},{"id":"97","question":"To enforce consistent feature distribution between training and serving, what should ou use?","options":{"A":"Batch predictions","B":"Feature Store","C":"Hyperparameter tuning","D":"Data Labeling Service"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Feature Store ensures consistency by serving the same preprocessed features to both training and prediction pipelines."},{"id":"98","question":"Which Cloud Dataflow concept controls when the results of a windowed computation are emitted?","options":{"A":"Aggregators","B":"Checkpoints","C":"Triggers","D":"Pipelines"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Triggers: they define at which moment (watermark, processing time, count, or a composition) a pane fires and the window aggregate is emitted.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Default: AfterWatermark (when the watermark passes the end of the window).\\n• Early firings: speculative results before completion.\\n• Late firings: recompute if late data arrives (allowed lateness).\\n\\nExam tip: \'when windowed results are emitted\' → Triggers.\\nDo not confuse with: Aggregators (accumulators), Checkpoints (state/recovery), or Pipelines (the whole job).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"99","question":"Your data pipeline must join a large streaming dataset with a small static reference dataset. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Side input","B":"GroupByKey","C":"Global window","D":"Streaming SQL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. A side input: the small table is materialized as a View (asMap/asList/asSingleton) and broadcast to every worker to enrich each stream element.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The main PCollection is the stream; the side input is looked up in the DoFn (e.g. code → country).\\n• It must fit in memory reasonably; if it grows, you shard it or refresh it by windows.\\n• Avoids an expensive shuffle join against the stream.\\n\\nExam tip: \'large streaming + small static reference\' → side input.\\nDo not confuse with: GroupByKey (groups by key, not broadcast), Global window, or Streaming SQL.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"100","question":"To ensure that BigQuery table rows are unique during data load, what strategy should you use?","options":{"A":"Use CSV format","B":"Partition by hash","C":"Deduplicate upstream","D":"Set max_results in queries"},"correct":"C","explanation":"BigQuery doesn’t enforce uniqueness on its own, so you must deduplicate data during ingestion or use MERGE to remove duplicates."},{"id":"101","question":"Which SQL function in BigQuery ML helps you assess precision, recall, and F1 score?","options":{"A":"ML.EVALUATE","B":"MLVALIDATE","C":"ML.SCORE","D":"ML.CLASSIFY"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. ML.EVALUATE: it returns the model\'s evaluation metrics (accuracy, precision, recall, f1_score, log_loss, roc_auc in classification).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• SELECT * FROM ML.EVALUATE(MODEL `ds.model`); uses the training holdout.\\n• Or ML.EVALUATE(MODEL m, (SELECT ...), STRUCT(0.4 AS threshold)) for another set or threshold.\\n• MLVALIDATE, ML.SCORE, and ML.CLASSIFY do not exist.\\n\\nExam tip: \'precision, recall, F1\' + BigQuery ML → ML.EVALUATE.\\nDo not confuse with: ML.PREDICT (predictions) or ML.CONFUSION_MATRIX (counts per class).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"102","question":"You want to predict numeric housing prices. Which model is appropriate?","options":{"A":"logistic_reg","B":"kmeans","C":"linear_reg","D":"arima_plus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. LINEAR_REG: supervised regression for a continuous target (price, demand, temperature).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• OPTIONS(MODEL_TYPE=\'LINEAR_REG\', INPUT_LABEL_COLS=[\'price\']).\\n• The label must be numeric (no infinities or NaN).\\n• Stronger alternatives: BOOSTED_TREE_REGRESSOR, DNN_REGRESSOR, AUTOML_REGRESSOR.\\n\\nExam tip: \'numeric housing prices\', \'continuous target\' → linear_reg.\\nDo not confuse with: logistic_reg (classes), kmeans (no label), or arima_plus (time series).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"103","question":"You are using matrix_factorization in BigQuery ML. What is the required format?","options":{"A":"Wide table with separate columns per item","B":"Time-series data","C":"Three-column table (user, item, rating)","D":"A cluster with all features"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. A tall table of three columns: user, item, and rating (or interaction intensity), not a wide user\xd7item matrix.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• OPTIONS(MODEL_TYPE=\'MATRIX_FACTORIZATION\', USER_COL=\'user_id\', ITEM_COL=\'item_id\', RATING_COL=\'rating\').\\n• Each row is an observed interaction.\\n• FEEDBACK_TYPE=\'EXPLICIT\' (stars) or \'IMPLICIT\' (clicks/purchases).\\n\\nExam tip: \'three-column table (user, item, rating)\', \'tall format\'.\\nDo not confuse with: a wide table (one column per item), time-series, or a feature cluster.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"104","question":"Which clause defines the maximum number of clusters in kmeans?","options":{"A":"OPTIONS(MAX_ITERATIONS-...)","B":"OPTIONS(MAX CLUSTERS.-...","C":"OPTIONS(NUM _CLUSTERS.-...","D":"OPTIONS(CENTER COUNT-..."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. NUM_CLUSTERS: OPTIONS(MODEL_TYPE=\'KMEANS\', NUM_CLUSTERS=8). It is an INT64 between 2 and 100 (or a range if you do hyperparameter tuning).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• If you omit it, the default is about log10(n) based on the number of rows.\\n• MAX_ITERATIONS limits algorithm passes, not k.\\n• With NUM_TRIALS you can tune NUM_CLUSTERS = HPARAM_RANGE(2, 25).\\n\\nExam tip: \'number of clusters\' → NUM_CLUSTERS, not MAX_ITERATIONS or invented names.\\nDo not confuse with: MAX_CLUSTERS or CENTER_COUNT, which do not exist.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"105","question":"What is the maximum dataset size BigQuery ML can handle?","options":{"A":"10 GB","B":"No practical limit — it uses BigQuery’s scaling","C":"50 million rows","D":"500,000 rows only"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. There is no 50-million-row cap. BigQuery ML scales with BigQuery\'s infrastructure; the real limit is usually model size (~90 MB) and quotas, not a row cap.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• CREATE MODEL reads the table in the warehouse; you do not export to a VM.\\n• The automatic split changes at 500 and 50,000 rows (holdout), not at 50 million.\\n• Cardinality of categorical features: on the order of 5–10 million distinct values.\\n\\nExam tip: \'maximum dataset size\' → no practical limit / BigQuery scaling.\\nDo not confuse with: 10 GB, 50 million rows, or 500,000 rows, which are not documented limits.","explanationSource":"card-en"}]},{"id":"gcp-profesional-cloud-architect-april-2025","title":"GCP Profesional Cloud Architect — April 2025","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"You are designing a cloud-native application that must ingest real-time sensor data from thousands of IoT devices. What is the best solution for handling the ingestion?","options":{"A":"Use Pub/Sub for ingestion and App Engine for processing the data stream","B":"Use Cloud Pub/Sub for ingestion and Dataflow to process the stream in real time","C":"Use Cloud SQL for ingestion and a batch job on Compute Engine","D":"Use Cloud Storage to store data and schedule batch processing using Cloud Scheduler"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Pub/Sub absorbs bursts from thousands of devices with durable, at-least-once delivery, and Dataflow processes the stream in real time with autoscaling, windowing and exactly-once processing. This is Google\'s reference streaming architecture.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) App Engine is not a stream-processing engine (no windowing or watermarks). C) Cloud SQL is not built to ingest high-throughput telemetry, and batch jobs are not real time. D) Cloud Storage plus a scheduled batch adds minutes of latency.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"2","question":"You need to design a secure and scalable networking model for a multi-tier application across projects. What is the most appropriate choice?","options":{"A":"Use a shared VPC with custom routes across all projects","B":"Use VPC peering between projects with centralized firewall rules","C":"Use a shared VPC with centralized control in a host project","D":"Use a VPN connection between projects and configure static routes"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Shared VPC keeps one network in a host project, where the network and security team centrally owns subnets, firewall rules and routes. Service projects for each tier attach to it, and app teams deploy without managing the network.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Shared VPC is right, but the key point is centralized control; custom routes everywhere add complexity. B) VPC peering is not transitive and each side keeps its own firewall policy, so control is decentralized. D) VPN between projects in the same cloud is unnecessary overhead.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"3","question":"Your application requires a high- throughput, low-latency database. It needs to serve user profile data globally. What should ou use?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud SQL in multi-region configuration with read replicas","B":"Use Firestore in Datastore mode with caching for faster reads","C":"Use Memorystore for Redis with regional failover Re i]","D":"Use Cloud Spanner with multi-region configuration for global availability"},"correct":"D","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Multi-region Spanner gives globally distributed, strongly consistent reads and writes, horizontal scaling and up to 99.999% availability. It fits user profiles served worldwide with high throughput and low latency.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Cloud SQL is regional; cross-region read replicas are asynchronous and writes stay in one region. B) Firestore can be multi-region, but it is a document store and not the classic answer for global high-throughput relational data. C) Memorystore is a regional cache, not a durable system of record.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"4","question":"Acompany wants to automate infrastructure provisioning using infrastructure as code. Which tool is best for managing resources across multiple GCP projects?","options":{"A":"Use Terraform with GCP provider and a modular project structure","B":"Use Deployment Manager with Bash scripts to configure VM instances","C":"Use gcloud CLI with shell scripts and version control integration","D":"Use Cloud Shell to write and apply YAML templates for qull 10 A) provisioning"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Terraform with the Google provider and reusable modules (or Infrastructure Manager, Google\'s managed Terraform) manages resources declaratively across many projects, with state, plan/apply review and version control.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) Deployment Manager is deprecated (end of support March 31, 2026); use Terraform or Infrastructure Manager. C) gcloud shell scripts are imperative and do not track state. D) Cloud Shell is a terminal, not an IaC tool.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"5","question":"You need to ensure minimal downtime during a database migration from a legacy on-premises system to GCP. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Use Dataflow with batch jobs to sync data at intervals","B":"Use Cloud Data Fusion to extract, transform, and load data","C":"Use Database Migration Service with continuous replication","D":"Use BigQuery Data Transfer Service with scheduled syncs “% 2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Database Migration Service (DMS) supports minimal downtime migrations with continuous replication. It provides a reliable, serverless solution for migrating MySQL, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server databases to Cloud SQL."},{"id":"6","question":"You are designing a cloud solution to meet strict data residency requirements for a European client. What is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Use multi-region buckets for redundancy and compliance","B":"Use region-specific resources located in europe-west or europe- north","C":"Use global resources with identity restrictions based on location suit","D":"Use US-central region with customer-managed encryption keys"},"correct":"B","explanation":"To meet strict data residency requirements, resources should be deployed specifically within EU regions like europe-west4 or europe-north1. This ensures compliance with GDPR and local regulations."},{"id":"7","question":"You are designing a system where user- facing services should scale quickly under unpredictable load. What is the best compute choice?","options":{"A":"Use Compute Engine with instance groups and autoscaling","B":"Use Cloud Run with autoscaling based on incoming requests","C":"Use App Engine standard environment with manual scaling","D":"Use Kubernetes Engine with node autoscaler turned off"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Run is ideal for event-driven, serverless applications. It automatically scales containers up and down based on demand, including scaling to zero, which suits unpredictable traffic patterns."},{"id":"8","question":"Your customer wants to reduce operational overhead while building a pipeline that transforms JSON data into BigQuery. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Use Dataflow templates with user-managed VMs for processing","B":"Use Cloud Functions to parse JSON and upload it to Cloud SQL","C":"Use Cloud Dataprep for real-time transformation of streaming data","D":"Use Dataflow templates for serverless transformation into BigQuery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dataflow templates offer a serverless way to ingest, transform, and load data into BigQuery with minimal operational overhead, supporting JSON and other structured/unstructured formats."},{"id":"9","question":"You need to securely connect on-premises workloads to GCP with low latency and high bandwidth. What is the most appropriate solution?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud VPN with high-availability and BGP routing","B":"Use Dedicated Interconnect for private and high-speed connectivity","C":"Use VPC peering between on-prem and GCP networks","D":"Use Cloud NAT for secure access from on-prem to GCP % _ io \\\\) &*. = =, = % y"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dedicated Interconnect offers private, high- throughput connectivity with low latency, ideal for enterprise-grade hybrid cloud scenarios. It is preferred over VPN for bandwidth-sensitive needs."},{"id":"10","question":"A retail company needs to serve static content globally with low latency. Which solution should they implement?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud CDN with backend buckets in a single region","B":"Use Cloud Storage near customers with signed URLs","C":"Use Cloud CDN with Cloud Storage and global edge caching","D":"Use Compute Engine VMs with nginx and load balancing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud CDN combined with Cloud Storage allows caching of static content at Google’s globally distributed edge locations, significantly improving latency and scalability for static content delivery."},{"id":"11","question":"Which solution offers least effort to run containerized applications with automatic scaling and no server management?","options":{"A":"Use GKE with custom scaling and node pools","B":"Use Cloud Run for fully managed serverless containers","C":"Use Compute Engine with Docker and startup scripts","D":"Use App Engine flexible environment with container support Me"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Run abstracts server management completely, allowing you to run containers in a serverless fashion with autoscaling and per-request billing. It requires minimal configuration and management."},{"id":"12","question":"You need to schedule jobs to run every 30 minutes to update analytics data in BigQuery. hat is the best service to use?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Functions triggered by Cloud Pub/Sub every 30 mins","B":"Use Cloud Scheduler to trigger Dataflow or a Cloud Function","C":"Use a cron job on a Compute Engine instance 1,","D":"Use BigQuery Data Transfer Service for external data tis"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud Scheduler is the managed cron service. Every 30 minutes it can call an HTTP endpoint (a Cloud Run function or Cloud Function) or publish to Pub/Sub to start a Dataflow job, with retries and no servers to manage. For SQL-only refreshes, BigQuery scheduled queries are also a valid choice.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Pub/Sub does not publish on a timer by itself; something still has to schedule it. C) cron on a VM means patching, a single point of failure and paying for an idle VM. D) BigQuery Data Transfer Service imports data from external sources; it is not a general scheduler.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"13","question":"Asolution must ingest events from multiple systems and process them in order. hich combination best satisfies this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use Pub/Sub with unordered delivery and Dataflow","B":"Use Cloud Storage for ingestion and batch processing","C":"Use Pub/Sub with ordering keys and Dataflow with windowing","D":"Use App Engine for ingesting messages and reordering in code \\\\)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pub/Sub supports ordering via ordering keys, and Dataflow can use windowing and session logic to maintain processing order, making it a reliable solution for ordered event processing."},{"id":"14","question":"Your organization wants to enforce uniform security policies across all projects. What is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Use Organization Policies to enforce constraints globally","B":"Use IAM custom roles at the project level","C":"Use firewall rules on each VPC individually","D":"Use gcloud to deploy identical service accounts in all projects Oe % .% = := is Si Ce =: = ie %, “iw"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Organization Policies allow enforcement of security and resource constraints across the entire organization or folders, ensuring consistent application of policies across projects."},{"id":"15","question":"You need to track API calls and usage for billing and auditing across services. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Audit Logs only for critical services","B":"Use Stackdriver Debugger to capture API usage","C":"Use billing export to BigQuery for event tracking","D":"Use Cloud Audit Logs and Cloud Monitoring for observability"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cloud Audit Logs provide a full audit trail of API usage, and Cloud Monitoring enables metric-based observability. Combined, they offer a complete picture of API usage and system behavior."},{"id":"16","question":"A client wants to host a low-traffic website with minimal cost and high durability. Which solution is best?","options":{"A":"Host on Compute Engine with minimal instances","B":"Host on Cloud Run and auto-scale to zero","C":"Host on Cloud Storage with Cloud CDN and HTTPS","D":"Host on App Engine with manual scaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud Storage is ideal for static content, offering high durability and very low cost. When paired with Cloud CDN and HTTPS load balancing, it provides a performant, secure solution for low-traffic websites."},{"id":"17","question":"Which GCP service allows you to monitor VM-level metrics and receive alerts?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Monitoring to track VM metrics and trigger alerts","B":"Use Cloud Logging with alerts on log entries","C":"Use Cloud Debugger to inspect real-time metrics","D":"Use Compute Engine metadata server for stats"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Monitoring collects metrics from Compute Engine and other services and allows setting up alerting policies based on those metrics. It\'s essential for infrastructure health monitoring."},{"id":"18","question":"You are setting up automation to deploy the same infrastructure to dev, test, and prod. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Use Deployment Manager with environment flags","B":"Use Bash scripts with gcloud and separate configs","C":"Use Cloud Shell with templated commands","D":"Use Terraform with separate workspaces per environment s"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A Shared VPC allows you to centrally manage network resources in a host project while enabling service projects to consume those resources. It’s the recommended approach for large-scale, multi-project environments."},{"id":"19","question":"What is the main benefit of using VPC Service Controls?","options":{"A":"Controls access between on-premises and cloud services","B":"Reduces latency by isolating services in private networks","C":"Manages global VPC peering across organizations","D":"Mitigates data exfiltration risks in high-trust services"},"correct":"D","explanation":"VPC Service Controls enhance security by creating a service perimeter that limits data exfiltration from GCP services like BigQuery or Cloud Storage. It is especially useful in regulated environments."},{"id":"20","question":"You are designing a microservices architecture with service discovery. What is the best GCP-native solution?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Run with Cloud Load Balancing and internal DNS","B":"Use App Engine with static IPs for all services","C":"Use Compute Engine with external HTTP Load Balancer","D":"Use Cloud Functions and manually configure IP-based routing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Run supports automatic service discovery via internal DNS when used within the same VPC. Coupled with Cloud Load Balancing, it allows seamless communication between microservices."},{"id":"21","question":"You must enforce data encryption at rest across all storage services in your project. hich solution meets the requirement with the least effort?","options":{"A":"Create signed URLs to enforce encrypted access to data","B":"Require Cloud KMS keys in service accounts for decryption","C":"Use organization policies to enforce CMEK across storage %, 1,","D":"Enable Cloud HSM to provide symmetric encryption support"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Using organization policies allows enforcement of Customer-Managed Encryption Keys (CMEK) at the project or folder level, ensuring consistent encryption behavior without per-resource manual setup."},{"id":"23","question":"You want to reduce public IP exposure for internal services. What architecture should you use?","options":{"A":"Use external HT TP(S) Load Balancer for internal backends","B":"Use internal TCP/UDP Load Balancer with private IPs only","C":"Use Identity-Aware Proxy with external static addresses only","D":"Use firewall rules to block all internet access to services % en “se Ti s"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The internal TCP/UDP Load Balancer allows private IP-based access within a VPC, preventing public exposure and meeting security and compliance requirements for internal applications."},{"id":"24","question":"You are designing a multi-cloud strategy with a centralized monitoring solution. What should you implement?","options":{"A":"Use third-party APM services deployed on each provider","B":"Deploy Logging agents on each VM to ship logs via FTP","C":"Use Cloud Monitoring and Logging with custom metrics","D":"Store logs on local disks and upload periodically via CLI"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cloud Monitoring and Cloud Logging (Google Cloud Observability) can ingest metrics and logs from other clouds and on-premises through the Ops Agent, Managed Service for Prometheus or OpenTelemetry. Custom metrics cover app KPIs, so you get one central pane.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) a separate APM per provider fragments visibility. B) shipping logs over FTP is insecure and not real time. D) local disks with periodic uploads lose data and delay detection.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"25","question":"Aworkload needs high memory and CPU and must scale automatically. What compute option meets this need?","options":{"A":"Use App Engine with instance class F4 configured","B":"Use Cloud Functions with high timeout and memory","C":"Use GKE with node auto-provisioning for large workloads","D":"Use Cloud Run with 1 vCPU and scale manually as % aS “iw 13 eL// ‘Ss Ti \\\\ s needed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) with node auto-provisioning allows clusters to automatically scale node pools based on workload demands, including CPU/memory- intensive workloads."},{"id":"26","question":"Your security team mandates identity- based access to all cloud resources. What is the best practice?","options":{"A":"Use static access keys for all developers to access APIs","B":"Use shared service accounts for automation and scripting","C":"Use custom roles with IAM for team-level resource access","D":"Use IAM with least privilege and user-level credentials a"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Applying the principle of least privilege using IAM with user-specific roles ensures traceability and security. Avoid using shared accounts or static keys when possible."},{"id":"27","question":"You must allow two VPC networks to communicate with low latency and high bandwidth. Which GCP feature should you use?","options":{"A":"Use VPC Network Peering for internal GCP communication","B":"Use Cloud VPN with multiple tunnels between the networks","C":"Use a NAT gateway to translate routes between networks","D":"Use Cloud Interconnect for traffic between VPC networks"},"correct":"A","explanation":"VPC Network Peering allows direct and private communication between VPCs within GCP with high performance and low latency, avoiding public internet exposure."},{"id":"28","question":"Your analytics team needs access to real- time data updates for reporting. Which architecture supports this best?","options":{"A":"Use BigQuery scheduled queries with CSV imports every hour","B":"Use Data Fusion batch jobs to extract updates hourly","C":"Use Cloud Storage uploads followed by batch transformation","D":"Use Pub/Sub to stream data into BigQuery via Dataflow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Pub/Sub to ingest events in streaming and Dataflow to transform them and write them continuously to BigQuery.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Producers publish events to a Pub/Sub topic.\\n• A Dataflow streaming job (e.g. Pub/Sub to BigQuery template) cleans and enriches the data.\\n• Dataflow writes to BigQuery with the Storage Write API; data can be queried in seconds.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabreal-time\xbb, \xabstreaming\xbb, \xabreporting/analytics\xbb.\\nDo not confuse with: scheduled queries with CSV every hour, hourly Data Fusion batch, or uploads to Cloud Storage + batch (all are batch with hours of latency, not real time).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"30","question":"You need to ensure consistent tagging and labeling of resources across multiple teams. What is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Use a Cloud Function to add labels post-deployment","B":"Use Resource Manager policies to enforce label constraints","C":"Use shell scripts to verify labels during deployment % ee, oa","D":"Use Billing export filters to detect unlabeled resources TT A) ‘Ss Ts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Resource Manager supports organization policies that enforce labels and naming standards across the organization, ensuring consistent tagging at deployment time."},{"id":"31","question":"Which GCP service allows cross-region load balancing for web applications with minimal latency?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Load Balancing with HTTP(S) global frontend","B":"Use regional TCP Load Balancer with multiple health checks","C":"Use Compute Engine instance groups with static routes set","D":"Use App Engine with zone-level traffic routing manually sy, % WL cma “Ss “1iWw* Ss set"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Load Balancing supports global HT TP(S) traffic distribution, automatic multi-region failover, and integrated health checks, providing a robust and low-latency solution."},{"id":"32","question":"Acompany needs to manage configuration for thousands of devices across different regions. Which GCP service should be used?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Functions and Firestore to write device configs","B":"Use Cloud IoT Core with MQTT and device configuration","C":"Use GKE and expose REST API for managing configuration","D":"Use BigQuery for configuration storage and access control “aw"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud IoT Core supports secure device connection via MQTT and device configuration management, making it ideal for managing large-scale, distributed device fleets."},{"id":"33","question":"You are planning a backup strategy for critical databases. What is the recommended approach on GCP?","options":{"A":"Use gcloud CLI to take snapshots and store on local disks","B":"Use Persistent Disk snapshots manually each week","C":"Use scheduled backups in Cloud SQL with automated restore","D":"Use Cloud Functions to write backup files to Cloud Storage"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud SQL provides built-in automated and scheduled backup functionality, including point-in-time recovery, which is the preferred method for backing up managed databases."},{"id":"34","question":"Your team needs to automate deployment of network policies in GKE clusters. What tool best supports this?","options":{"A":"Use gcloud commands in Cloud Shell to apply YAML files","B":"Use Deployment Manager to configure firewall rules manually","C":"Use Cloud Functions to run kubectl with bash scripts","D":"Use Config Connector with GitOps for Kubernetes policies MZ)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Config Connector integrates Kubernetes-native APIs with GCP resources, enabling GitOps workflows for infrastructure as code, including automated policy management in GKE."},{"id":"36","question":"What is a benefit of using Managed Instance Groups in Compute Engine?","options":{"A":"Provides fixed capacity VM pools with manual failover only","B":"Supports autoscaling and rolling updates across zones","C":"Enables GPU workloads to run faster across all zones","D":"Assigns static IPs across all regional workloads for la, % eL// A) “1Ww* failover"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Managed Instance Groups build identical VMs from an instance template. They provide autoscaling, autohealing with health checks, regional (multi-zone) distribution, and rolling or canary updates of the template.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) MIGs give elastic capacity and automatic recreation, not fixed pools with manual failover. C) GPU speed depends on the machine type, not on the group. D) MIG VMs normally use ephemeral IPs behind a load balancer; static IPs are not the point.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"37","question":"You want to automate changes to IAM policies across multiple projects. What solution is best?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Shell scripts to assign policies to resources","B":"Use Terraform with modules and variable IAM bindings","C":"Use Deployment Manager with YAML templates per project","D":"Use gcloud CLI to loop through project IAM roles manually 5 - 9 al as S Gy"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Terraform modules with variables for IAM bindings (for example google_project_iam_member) apply the same reviewed policy to many projects in a repeatable, version-controlled and auditable way. Drift shows up in the plan.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) and D) ad-hoc scripts and manual loops are imperative, error-prone and hard to audit. C) Deployment Manager is deprecated (end of support March 31, 2026).","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"39","question":"You must implement budget controls across projects in a GCP organization. What is the best method?","options":{"A":"Set billing alerts at the billing account level only","B":"Use quota limits to enforce strict usage policies","C":"Use budgets and alerts in Cloud Billing per project","D":"Use IAM deny policies to block new resource creation"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cloud Billing budgets can be scoped to projects (or labels and services) with threshold alerts on actual or forecasted spend, so each project owner is notified. Budgets can also publish to Pub/Sub for automated responses, such as capping spend.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) an account-level alert alone does not show which project overspends. B) quotas cap resource usage, not money. D) IAM deny policies are about permissions, not cost control.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"40","question":"What is the role of Cloud NAT in VPC design?","options":{"A":"Enables inbound access to VMs from the internet directly","B":"Enables outbound internet access for private VMs securely","C":"Enables firewall-based rules for routing internal traffic","D":"Enables logging of packet-level network traffic events % i ani 13"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud NAT provides outbound internet access for instances without external IP addresses, maintaining privacy and security while enabling updates or API calls."},{"id":"41","question":"Your client must comply with internal policies requiring all data access to be audited. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Enable Cloud Audit Logs for all admin and data access events","B":"Use custom logging in applications for user activity capture","C":"Set IAM roles to log access with Stackdriver log entries","D":"Create BigQuery datasets to store all system-level events"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Audit Logs automatically capture admin, data, and system access events across GCP services, making it the most reliable and scalable audit mechanism."},{"id":"42","question":"You need to prevent accidental deletion of production resources in GCP. What should you implement?","options":{"A":"Use custom scripts to monitor for resource deletions weekly","B":"Apply resource locks and enforce IAM delete permissions","C":"Enable Cloud Logging to detect delete operations after the fact","D":"Create daily snapshots of data and restore if data is lost MIN Yy Creeres “af “11Ww* \xab"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Applying deletion protection and configuring IAM permissions to restrict deletes help prevent unintentional removal of production-critical resources."},{"id":"43","question":"You are migrating from a monolithic app to microservices. What is a recommended GCP approach?","options":{"A":"Package the entire monolith into one App Engine service","B":"Move backend only and leave frontend hosted on- premises","C":"Refactor into services and deploy to GKE with autoscaling","D":"Lift and shift entire app into Compute Engine unmanaged VMs G % Me a 7 ni A)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Refactor the monolith into independent services (usually incrementally) and deploy them on GKE (or Cloud Run) with autoscaling.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Identify functional domains and extract services one by one (strangler fig pattern).\\n• Each service is containerized, scaled, and deployed separately with CI/CD.\\n• GKE provides autoscaling (HPA, cluster autoscaler), service discovery, and rolling updates.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabmonolith to microservices\xbb → refactor + containers on GKE/Cloud Run.\\nDo not confuse with: packing the monolith into a single App Engine service or lift-and-shift to VMs (still a monolith); moving only the backend (not a migration to microservices).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"45","question":"Your system must scale based on workload but keep costs low during inactivity. Which GCP service is best?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Run to auto-scale to zero during idle periods","B":"Use Compute Engine with manual scaling configurations only","C":"Use GKE with fixed node pool and resource allocation tags % %, re","D":"Use App Engine flexible with fixed concurrency assignment sey TT"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Run scales applications down to zero when idle and up during traffic spikes, providing cost efficiency without sacrificing scalability."},{"id":"47","question":"Which solution offers serverless integration between services with minimal setup?","options":{"A":"Use Eventarc to trigger Cloud Functions on GCP events","B":"Use Compute Engine to manage services and logging manually","C":"Use App Engine cron services with external monitoring jobs","D":"Use custom webhook URLs to poll each GCP service directly % = seilly A) S Ts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Eventarc is the native serverless event routing service on GCP that connects events from various services to Cloud Functions, enabling minimal-code integrations."},{"id":"48","question":"You need to route traffic between services securely within the same VPC. What is the correct method?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Armor rules with public IPs for each service","B":"Use internal Load Balancers with private IP communication","C":"Use Identity-Aware Proxy to intercept service requests internally","D":"Use NAT Gateway to translate service requests between zones"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Internal Load Balancers route traffic over private IPs within a VPC, ensuring fast, secure communication without traversing the public internet."},{"id":"49","question":"How should you manage secrets used in serverless GCP services?","options":{"A":"Embed secrets in the environment variable and rotate weekly","B":"Store secrets in Secret Manager and use IAM-controlled access","C":"Write secrets to disk and delete after service startup completes % = aun ee “Ss Ts setly","D":"Store all secrets in source code and restrict code repository s s"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Secret Manager provides secure, auditable, and manageable storage of secrets, and integrates with IAM to control access securely and at scale."},{"id":"50","question":"What is the benefit of Cloud Functions over Compute Engine in an event-driven architecture?","options":{"A":"Supports persistent disk and static IP configuration","B":"Offers longer processing time and full instance control","C":"Auto-scales per event and abstracts infrastructure management 4%, St c","D":"Allows real-time container updates using SSH terminal 3 sey ani\xbb Ss Ts session \\\\ s"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud Functions are serverless, scale instantly based on event triggers, and require no server provisioning or management, making them ideal for event-driven systems."},{"id":"51","question":"You want to isolate workloads in the same GKE cluster. Which method provides logical separation?","options":{"A":"Use Kubernetes namespaces to isolate resource access scope","B":"Create dedicated clusters for each team in separate regions","C":"Assign each workload to a specific Compute Engine backend","D":"Allocate fixed memory quotas to each container manually"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Namespaces in Kubernetes allow you to logically separate workloads, apply access control, and manage resources within the same cluster environment."},{"id":"53","question":"Your application must remain online during OS patches and zone failures. What is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Use single-zone App Engine standard environment for apps","B":"Use regional Compute Engine instances with manual backups","C":"Use Managed Instance Groups with multi-zone distribution","D":"Use Cloud Run to control zone availability and VM restart la, 14: pene “1Ww* silly, Sly TTI Ss order"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Terraform’s modular and declarative structure makes it easy to apply IAM changes consistently across Many projects with minimal duplication and improved control."},{"id":"54","question":"You need to analyze logs and extract custom metrics to trigger alerts. What combination should you use?","options":{"A":"Export logs to Cloud Storage and process in Data Fusion","B":"Use Cloud Logging with custom metrics and alert policies","C":"Use App Engine logs directly and set IAM alerts manually","D":"Use Logging API to read logs and raise Slack notifications"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Logging supports custom metrics from log entries and integrates with alerting in Cloud Monitoring, making it ideal for real-time observability and response."},{"id":"55","question":"What feature enables cost control and planning for multiple projects?","options":{"A":"Set budgets and alerts at the billing account or project level","B":"Use Cloud Monitoring metrics to charge each VM resource hourly","C":"Assign static IP addresses to services to reduce external cost","D":"Enable detailed billing CSV exports to local spreadsheets lla, ee “1Ww* 42) sely weekly A)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud Billing budgets and alerts, at the billing-account level or filtered by project, service, or label.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You set an amount (fixed or last period\'s spend) and thresholds (e.g. 50%, 90%, 100%, also on forecasted spend).\\n• It notifies billing admins by email or Monitoring channels.\\n• It can publish to Pub/Sub to automate responses.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"cost control\\", \\"planning\\", \\"multiple projects\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Monitoring metrics to charge per VM (not a billing tool); static IPs (they do not cut cost; unused reserved IPs cost money); weekly CSV to spreadsheets (manual; prefer billing export to BigQuery).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"57","question":"You must ensure only trusted code runs in your Cloud Build pipeline. Which feature helps?","options":{"A":"Use Binary Authorization to enforce image verification policies","B":"Use firewall rules to block unauthorized image repositories","C":"Require Cloud Run to verify code integrity during deployment G OJ SG selly","D":"Configure OS Login to restrict SSH access to build agents"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Binary Authorization enforces at deploy time (GKE, Cloud Run) that only container images signed or attested by trusted parties, such as your Cloud Build pipeline after tests and vulnerability scanning, can run. It is the supply-chain control for \'only trusted code runs\'.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) firewall rules filter network traffic, not image provenance. C) Cloud Run does not verify integrity by itself; it does so through a Binary Authorization policy. D) OS Login controls SSH access to VMs.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"58","question":"What is the recommended way to expose an internal service to a few external partners?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud NAT and provide external IPs for direct access","B":"Use API Gateway with IAM or service account access control","C":"Use GKE ingress and configure firewall rules for IP whitelisting","D":"Use shared VPCs and grant access to all partner subnets globally"},"correct":"B","explanation":"API Gateway provides secure and controlled exposure of services with IAM integration, ideal for limited external access to internal APIs."},{"id":"59","question":"Which GCP service is designed for schema management and integration pipelines?","options":{"A":"Use App Engine with cloud database for all schema tracking","B":"Use Data Catalog for metadata, lineage, and schema discovery","C":"Use GKE and write custom ETL pipelines in backend containers","D":"Use BigQuery export with CSV schema definitions daily"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Data Catalog allows you to manage metadata, tag data assets, and support schema discovery and data lineage across GCP services."},{"id":"60","question":"Your application uses APIs that must authenticate requests securely. What is a recommended practice?","options":{"A":"Use OAuth 2.0 access tokens with Identity Platform or IAM","B":"Use static API keys stored in user browsers for convenience","C":"Allow anonymous access and log user activity via logging","D":"Require HTTPS with self-signed certificates for all requests \\"4 Se ‘S “iw eee Ts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"OAuth 2.0 enables secure, standards-compliant authentication and authorization for APIs, integrated with Identity Platform or IAM for policy enforcement."},{"id":"61","question":"You want to implement autoscaling based on application performance. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Enable Cloud Functions and let it scale by default timeout","B":"Use App Engine with manual scaling and high max instance count","C":"Use GKE HPA with custom metrics and CPU utilization target i -_ -","D":"Use Compute Engine and increase instance size monthly manually on,"},"correct":"C","explanation":"GKE Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) can scale based on CPU, memory, or custom metrics, making it the right tool for performance-based scaling."},{"id":"62","question":"How do you reduce latency for a global website hosted on GCP?","options":{"A":"Use Compute Engine and serve from us-central1 only","B":"Use Cloud CDN with global Load Balancer and signed URLs","C":"Use GKE and manually route traffic using static IPs % es 11.5 *s eons “ef “11Ww* WL","D":"Use App Engine standard with fixed zone selection"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. A global external Application Load Balancer gives one anycast IP and routes users to the nearest healthy backend. Cloud CDN caches content at Google\'s edge, which cuts latency worldwide. Signed URLs add controlled access to cached private content.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) one region (us-central1) is far from many users. C) manual routing with static IPs gives no global anycast or caching. D) a fixed zone is the opposite of global distribution.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"63","question":"Which resource policy allows automated VM restart after host maintenance?","options":{"A":"Use static scheduling with resource reservation templates","B":"Use automatic restart with availability policies on Compute VMs","C":"Use managed instance groups with maintenance mode off","D":"Use user-defined maintenance intervals with Cloud Scheduler \'2, \xa9 2 — wd _\xbb MNT, Cs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Availability policies in Compute Engine enable automatic restart of instances on host maintenance, enhancing uptime without user intervention."},{"id":"65","question":"A developer needs to manage infrastructure as code and track changes. What solution should they use?","options":{"A":"Use Terraform with version control for all deployments","B":"Use Cloud Shell to deploy and manually track infrastructure","C":"Use Deployment Manager without source control integration","D":"Use gcloud CLI for each update and log changes in sheets 4%, St iS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Terraform enables infrastructure-as-code with modular, version-controlled templates, supporting collaboration and tracking infrastructure changes."},{"id":"67","question":"You must allow a serverless function to access BigQuery securely. What should you configure?","options":{"A":"Use OAuth 2.0 tokens generated on external service gateway","B":"Use signed URLs with access duration set to one week max","C":"Use IAM role binding for service account permissions","D":"Use VPN with access whitelist to all BigQuery endpoints % T= silly A) ‘Ss Ti"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Granting the Cloud Function\'s service account proper IAM roles for BigQuery access is the most secure and scalable access control method."},{"id":"68","question":"You need to integrate an external on- prem system with a GCP-hosted API. What is the best method?","options":{"A":"Expose internal IPs with firewall rules for safe access","B":"Use Cloud Endpoints with authentication and quotas set","C":"Use SSH tunneling for secure API calls from on-prem only 4","D":"Copy the code to on-prem and call API through Cloud NAT villa, A) Gre SZ TTS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Endpoints provides a secure and managed gateway for API hosting with authentication, quota enforcement, and logging, ideal for external integration."},{"id":"70","question":"You must secure VM-to-VM communication within a VPC. What is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Use public IPs and configure firewall deny-all policies","B":"Use external Load Balancer and internal TLS-only ports","C":"Use VPC firewall rules with IAM and network tags :","D":"Use NAT and route all traffic through external gateway i “iw"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Using VPC firewall rules along with IAM and network tags enables granular and secure control over internal VM communications."},{"id":"71","question":"What tool enables centralized control and enforcement of policy across projects?","options":{"A":"Use gcloud scripts and apply them to every new folder","B":"Use Deployment Manager for all resource access control","C":"Use Organization Policies and Resource Hierarchy","D":"Use billing export reports to enforce access after audit ta, % seilly Creer oo Ti \\\\ S"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Organization Policy constraints set at the organization or folder level are inherited down the resource hierarchy (org > folders > projects). That gives central, preventive enforcement, such as allowed regions, no external IPs, or no service account keys.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) scripts per folder are manual and easy to bypass. B) Deployment Manager is deprecated and is not a policy engine. D) billing exports are after-the-fact reporting, not enforcement.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"72","question":"Which design principle supports high availability in GCP architecture?","options":{"A":"Use static IPs and assign them manually to all instances","B":"Use single-zone deployment with automatic scaling off","C":"Use multi-zone deployments and health-checked backends % Cs","D":"Use Cloud Scheduler to restart failed services manually sty"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. High availability on Google Cloud comes from removing single points of failure: spread instances across zones (regional MIGs, regional GKE) behind a load balancer whose health checks route traffic away from unhealthy backends.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) manually assigned static IPs do not fail over. B) a single zone with scaling off fails with the zone. D) restarting services by hand on a schedule is slow and not automatic.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"73","question":"You want to automate data movement between services when files are uploaded. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Use gcloud CLI to schedule daily transfer jobs via cron","B":"Use App Engine polling loop and sleep for interval gaps","C":"Use Eventarc to trigger Dataflow pipeline automatically","D":"Use Compute Engine startup scripts for data ingestion % fouate “se Ts wee"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Eventarc routes events, such as a Cloud Storage object being finalized, to targets like Cloud Run or Workflows, which can launch a Dataflow job immediately. It is event-driven and serverless, with no polling.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) daily cron jobs add latency. B) polling loops waste resources and react slowly. D) startup scripts only run at VM boot.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"74","question":"A client wants to optimize network usage across hybrid cloud. What is the best GCP solution?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud NAT to route all on-prem traffic to external IPs","B":"Use VPC peering to connect on-prem with GCP networks","C":"Use Cloud VPN tunnels with multiple load balancer tiers","D":"Use Dedicated Interconnect with routing and SLA support 47) ely"},"correct":"D","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Dedicated Interconnect provides private, high-bandwidth (10/100 Gbps) physical links to Google\'s network, with Cloud Router BGP routing and an SLA of up to 99.99% in the recommended topology. It also lowers egress pricing compared with internet traffic, which optimizes hybrid network usage.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Cloud NAT gives private VMs outbound internet access; it does not connect on-premises. B) VPC peering connects VPC networks, not on-premises sites. C) Cloud VPN runs over the internet with lower bandwidth per tunnel; load balancer tiers do not apply here.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"76","question":"Your application processes payment data subject to PCI-DSS. Which GCP tool can help ensure that access policies are defined and enforced appropriately?","options":{"A":"Identity-Aware Proxy for user credential security","B":"VPC Service Controls to define network segmentation","C":"Cloud IAM to implement fine-grained permissions","D":"Cloud Audit Logs to detect unauthorized access attempts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud IAM is the primary tool in GCP for defining and enforcing who has access to which resources. It supports fine-grained role-based access controls that align with PCI-DSS requirements."},{"id":"77","question":"Which GCP service helps restrict data access to within defined security perimeters, reducing the risk of data exfiltration?","options":{"A":"Cloud Key Management Service for encryption control","B":"Cloud Armor for network-level DDoS protection","C":"IAM Conditions for context-aware access policies % avila, —— a= 7 Canna? * naw”","D":"VPC Service Controls for setting security perimeters Sy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"VPC Service Controls create a virtual security perimeter around GCP services, mitigating data exfiltration by controlling data access across project boundaries."},{"id":"78","question":"A financial services customer requires auditing of all IAM policy changes. Which service should be used to meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Cloud Monitoring for resource health checks","B":"Cloud Audit Logs for tracking admin activities","C":"Cloud Logging for storing structured application logs","D":"Security Command Center for vulnerability scanning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Organization Policies enforce constraints and rules across the GCP resource hierarchy, ensuring uniform control across all projects and folders."},{"id":"79","question":"Which approach provides the strongest identity-based access control to an application hosted on GKE?","options":{"A":"Using service accounts with wide project-level permissions","B":"Creating static API keys embedded in application config","C":"Configuring Workload Identity to map GKE pods to IAM roles","D":"Running the application with default Compute Engine Gi Of J te ve 11: reer oan Ts credentials"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Workload Identity allows Kubernetes service accounts to act as IAM service accounts securely, offering fine-grained, identity-based access without managing static credentials."},{"id":"80","question":"Your architecture must prevent privilege escalation through IAM misconfigurations. What is a best practice?","options":{"A":"Apply the principle of least privilege to all IAM roles","B":"Use Owner roles temporarily to test service integration","C":"Assign Editor roles to all project-level users by default","D":"Configure logging filters to ignore access-related logs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Assigning only the minimal permissions required (least privilege) reduces the attack surface and helps prevent privilege escalation due to over-permissive roles."},{"id":"81","question":"To meet HIPAA compliance, you must ensure the confidentiality and integrity of ePHI data in GCP. Which combination meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Cloud SQL with default settings and VPC firewall rules","B":"CMEK for encryption and IAM for access control enforcement","C":"Shared VPC and Cloud CDN for accelerated access","D":"Cloud Run with unauthenticated access for external users wins, %, ML/ =A ol Ww = Se a g ) “aw"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Using Customer-Managed Encryption Keys (CMEK) ensures control over data encryption, and Cloud IAM enforces secure and compliant access controls—both critical for HIPAA compliance."},{"id":"82","question":"What is the purpose of using IAM Conditions in an enterprise GCP environment?","options":{"A":"To define firewall rules based on identity groups","B":"To allow context-aware access based on resource attributes","C":"To configure routing paths across hybrid networks","D":"To trigger alerts based on usage thresholds Ree Shao oS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IAM Conditions allow you to set policies that adapt based on attributes such as request time, IP address, or resource tags, enabling advanced, context-aware access control."},{"id":"83","question":"A security team wants to enforce organization-wide security posture. What feature should they use?","options":{"A":"Granting inherited roles at folder level","B":"Using Organization Policy to enforce constraints","C":"Adding firewall rules to every project VPC","D":"Configuring manual key rotation schedules oe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Organization Policy lets administrators define constraints at the org/folder/project level. This allows security teams to enforce security best practices consistently across all resources."},{"id":"84","question":"Which GCP service provides centralized security insights including misconfigurations, vulnerabilities, and threats?","options":{"A":"Security Command Center for security posture monitoring","B":"Cloud Scheduler for automating log analysis","C":"Secret Manager for storing private keys %,","D":"Cloud Run for container-based service deployment at/75 \\\\) “iw"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Security Command Center offers centralized visibility into your GCP environment’s security and compliance risks. It identifies vulnerabilities and enables threat detection."},{"id":"85","question":"You are designing a secure architecture for a retail customer handling credit card data. What GCP feature ensures compliance with PCI-DSS for access control?","options":{"A":"Cloud Armor enables traffic restriction using IP addresses only","B":"Cloud IAM enables granular access control and role assignments","C":"Cloud CDN encrypts cached data at edge locations for fast delivery a0","D":"Secret Manager provides automated alerts when data is accessed Z"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud IAM is fundamental to meeting PCI-DSS by allowing organizations to control access based on roles and responsibilities, enforcing least privilege for sensitive operations."},{"id":"86","question":"A customer wants to enforce encryption of all data stored in Cloud Storage using their own keys. What should they implement?","options":{"A":"Enable automatic key rotation for Google-managed keys in project","B":"Use IAM Conditions to enforce secure access from trusted devices","C":"Use Customer-Managed Encryption Keys (CMEK) in Cloud KMS","D":"Enable firewall rules to control storage access across regions \xab\' 4% Nn By % = .< i= $ CLL “iw"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CMEK provides control over key usage, allowing compliance with requirements where customers manage encryption keys instead of relying solely on Google- managed encryption."},{"id":"87","question":"Which GCP security service would best help you detect unusual access behavior across multiple projects?","options":{"A":"Enable VPC flow logs and route events to Cloud Functions pipeline","B":"Configure IAM conditions with time-based constraints for all roles","C":"Deploy firewall rules with logging enabled on ingress and egress","D":"Use Cloud Audit Logs and integrate with Security Command Center eeeoneet “aw i 4"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Combining Cloud Audit Logs with Security Command Center allows continuous monitoring and detection of anomalous access patterns across the organization."},{"id":"88","question":"What is a recommended best practice for managing access to secrets in a GCP environment?","options":{"A":"Use Secret Manager with IAM roles to control access securely","B":"Use Startup Scripts to inject sensitive data at boot into metadata","C":"Store sensitive strings directly inside the application container","D":"Embed API credentials in environment variables for fast access S147"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Deploying across multiple zones with health- checked backends ensures high availability and enables automatic failover in case of zone outages."},{"id":"89","question":"Your company requires logs that show who accessed sensitive data. Which GCP feature fulfills this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use Dataflow to export access metrics to a log analytics engine","B":"Enable Data Access logs within Cloud Audit Logs in each project","C":"Set up Identity-Aware Proxy to capture traffic via HTTPS endpoints","D":"Enable log-based metrics with custom dashboards in Cloud Monitoring yur Nn marie -= = = Z S %, “S Wii, iw"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Data Access logs capture detailed information about who accessed which resources and when, helping fulfill audit requirements for sensitive data."},{"id":"90","question":"A client must ensure all VMs in their project do not have public IPs. How can they enforce this policy?","options":{"A":"Assign static IPs to only the VMs running internal batch jobs","B":"Use firewall rules to deny outbound traffic to all external IPs","C":"Apply an Org Policy constraint to block external IP assignment","D":"Disable identity and access logs for better network performance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Organization Policies allow enforcement of constraints, such as preventing the use of external IPs, which enhances security by limiting public exposure."},{"id":"91","question":"You need to restrict access to BigQuery datasets based on department tags. What should you configure?","options":{"A":"Use IAM Conditions with resource tags to enforce role bindings","B":"Enable automatic encryption and user-based VPN per department","C":"Use GKE clusters to isolate data access for each department group","D":"Create distinct service accounts and disable cross-project access atl/75 %, 4 ‘ S"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IAM Conditions allow the use of resource tags to manage access policies, enabling fine-grained access controls aligned with organizational attributes."},{"id":"92","question":"What is the advantage of using VPC Service Controls with Cloud Storage?","options":{"A":"It allows data replication to multi-regions without user approval","B":"It permits setting IAM permissions based on API key location only","C":"It prevents external API calls from regions outside the VPC range","D":"It restricts data movement across trusted network perimeters “o maw’"},"correct":"D","explanation":"VPC Service Controls help mitigate data exfiltration by enforcing perimeters that control service access based on network context, not just IAM."},{"id":"93","question":"Which mechanism is recommended to allow short-term, auditable access to production systems?","options":{"A":"Assign Editor role and log removal event after 1 hour using script","B":"Use IAM Conditions with time-bound access via Access Context Manager","C":"Add users to static security groups with expiration policy set manually aT","D":"Include admin credentials in sealed envelope stored in Cloud Storage B f 10 t,\\"* - Gy me THN"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Time-based IAM Conditions ensure short-lived access that is both auditable and revocable, ideal for break- glass or operational tasks in production environments."},{"id":"94","question":"A customer is required to protect data in transit within GCP. What should they implement?","options":{"A":"Enable default TLS on public APIs used by all backend services","B":"Use firewall rules to block internal port access between subnets","C":"Rely on encryption in transit provided by Google’s secure network","D":"Deploy reverse proxies to add custom SSL certificates on all services"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Google Cloud encrypts data in transit by default between services using HTTPS and other secure protocols, complying with industry best practices."},{"id":"96","question":"Which feature helps enforce compliance requirements by controlling resource configuration?","options":{"A":"Use custom monitoring scripts to detect changes in audit logs","B":"Use GKE Workload Identity to bind containers to IAM policies","C":"Enable auto-scaling with encrypted boot disks foreachVM J","D":"Use Organization Policy Service to restrict resource behavior"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Organization Policy Service allows administrators to enforce compliance requirements by restricting what types of resources can be created and configured."},{"id":"98","question":"How does Binary Authorization enhance security for container-based workloads?","options":{"A":"It provides automatic encryption of container logs at runtime","B":"It limits container deployment to only regions with logging enabled","C":"It ensures only trusted images can be deployed in GKE clusters","D":"It allows public registries to push images to private repositories HTK ee"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Eventarc listens to events such as Cloud Storage uploads and triggers downstream services like Dataflow for seamless, serverless data processing."},{"id":"99","question":"You want to audit access to sensitive datasets. Which service should you integrate with BigQuery?","options":{"A":"Enable Cloud Audit Logs and configure sinks for export and analysis","B":"Use VPC Flow Logs to monitor ingress traffic to BigQuery tables","C":"Deploy Access Transparency reports to all users via email","D":"Assign Editor roles to track usage manually in IAM console 7 ie A,\\" “fe ?, ~ “11Ww*"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Audit Logs record BigQuery access events, and exporting these logs to destinations like BigQuery or Cloud Storage allows compliance teams to audit access effectively."},{"id":"100","question":"What is the recommended way to securely manage service account keys in GCP?","options":{"A":"Store keys in Cloud Storage and rotate them once a year automatically","B":"Download keys and distribute them via email to approved team members","C":"Avoid downloading keys and use Workload Identity for secure access","D":"Keep keys in application code and restrict repository access to qull “1nWw* MINI Yy, M 7 TIN admins"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. The best key is no key. Use attached service accounts, Workload Identity Federation (for GKE, other clouds and CI/CD) or service account impersonation, so credentials are short-lived and never downloaded. You can also enforce the iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation org policy.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) a key stored in Cloud Storage and rotated yearly is still a long-lived secret. B) emailing keys is a leak waiting to happen. D) keys in source code end up in repositories and logs.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"102","question":"Which strategy helps meet regulatory requirements for key separation in multi- tenant environments?","options":{"A":"Encrypt data using the same key across all tenants for uniformity","B":"Use firewall rules to isolate tenants at the network layer only","C":"Use Secret Manager with identical access policies for all tenants","D":"Use CMEK to assign different encryption keys for each tenant\'s data %, THN"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Customer-Managed Encryption Keys (CMEK) can be uniquely assigned per tenant, supporting regulatory requirements for data separation and cryptographic isolation."},{"id":"103","question":"To ensure auditability of configuration changes, which GCP capability should be enabled?","options":{"A":"Identity-Aware Proxy and Security Bulletins for user protection alerts","B":"Cloud Audit Logs with Admin Activity tracking for configuration actions","C":"Shared VPC configurations and packet mirroring for log storage analysis","D":"Firewall rules with tags for dynamic filtering based on device groups \\"o Ts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Admin Activity logs in Cloud Audit Logs record configuration changes across GCP, supporting security, auditing, and compliance goals."},{"id":"105","question":"Which practice minimizes security risks when managing user access to GCP resources?","options":{"A":"Use Owner role for service accounts and default to full permissions","B":"Store access credentials in shared folders with encrypted ZIP files","C":"Assign predefined roles with limited permissions to specific users OS “nia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Predefined roles grant only necessary permissions and should be scoped to users based on their responsibilities, following the principle of least privilege."},{"id":"106","question":"What is the purpose of enabling Access Transparency in Google Cloud?","options":{"A":"It grants access to GCP resources without any access restrictions","B":"It allows customers to edit Google’s internal monitoring configurations","C":"It provides logs when Google personnel access customer resources","D":"It prevents any Google support engineer from accessing your 15 :: ie ALL A) services"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Access Transparency writes near real-time logs whenever Google personnel access your content, including who, when, why and the justification. It supports audits and compliance. Access Approval goes further and requires your explicit approval first.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) it grants no access. B) customers cannot edit Google\'s internal monitoring. D) it records access rather than preventing it; preventing or approving access is Access Approval or Assured Workloads controls.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"107","question":"A healthcare company wants to protect PHI data stored in Cloud SQL. What should they configure?","options":{"A":"Enable CMEK and restrict IAM roles to essential database access","B":"Deploy Public IP to allow faster connections from medical providers","C":"Add cloud functions to retrieve passwords and distribute to all apps","D":"Disable encryption settings to improve backup and restore time"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. CMEK to encrypt the instance with its own keys, and IAM roles restricted to essential database access only.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• CMEK is chosen when you create the Cloud SQL instance (you cannot add it later); it encrypts data, backups, and replicas.\\n• IAM: roles/cloudsql.client only for apps that must connect; admin for a few people.\\n• Complements: private IP, Cloud SQL Auth Proxy/connectors, IAM database authentication, and Data Access audit logs.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabPHI\xbb, \xabCloud SQL\xbb, \xabprotect\xbb.\\nDo not confuse with: public IP for clinicians (increases exposure); functions that hand passwords to every app (spreads secrets); turning encryption off (not possible on Cloud SQL and contradicts HIPAA).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"108","question":"What is the recommended way to ensure key rotation for security compliance?","options":{"A":"Manually generate a new key and upload to each instance every year","B":"Use startup scripts to rotate service account keys on boot manually","C":"Use Cloud KMS automatic rotation feature with defined intervals 4","D":"Create custom keys locally and upload using gsutil via terminal villa, 10 command A)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IAM Recommender analyzes usage data to recommend removing unused permissions, helping reduce risk and enforce the principle of least privilege."},{"id":"111","question":"How can you reduce the impact of compromised credentials in a cloud application?","options":{"A":"Increase session duration to avoid repeated login for critical users","B":"Use shared credentials among team members and rotate weekly manually","C":"Enable two-factor authentication and limit IAM permissions 47)","D":"Assign the Owner role to reduce login errors during critical events willy 10... panies? mm)"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Multi-factor authentication makes a stolen password insufficient, and least-privilege IAM limits what an attacker can do with a compromised identity (blast radius). Short sessions and context-aware access help further.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) longer sessions extend the window for a stolen token. B) shared credentials remove accountability and widen exposure. D) the Owner role maximizes the damage.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"112","question":"What is a benefit of using security health analytics in Security Command Center?","options":{"A":"It detects misconfigurations and vulnerabilities automatically","B":"It replicates VM instances to zones with minimal latency only","C":"It prevents IAM policy changes from external identity providers","D":"It scans source code in real-time to identify business logic G SE ie i 11; eres est we “1Ww* L// Ss issues"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Security Health Analytics scans your environment for known security misconfigurations and vulnerabilities, helping maintain compliance and secure posture."},{"id":"113","question":"A customer wants to prevent external entities from using their internal APIs. What should be configured?","options":{"A":"Cloud Functions triggers to log all API requests from public IPs","B":"Use VPC Service Controls to isolate internal API endpoints","C":"Grant public access but log unauthorized attempts in BigQuery tables","D":"Allow access to all APIs and encrypt all responses via KMS manually % eL/ A)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Access Transparency provides visibility into Google’s administrative access to customer resources, enhancing trust and enabling compliance with regulatory audits."},{"id":"115","question":"A customer wants to ensure that Google Support personnel can access their environment only during active support cases. What should they enable?","options":{"A":"Enable VPC Firewall Rules to allow only internal access during alerts","B":"Use Private Google Access to restrict support access to private IPs","C":"Enable IAM role logging for audit visibility across all users and roles","D":"Use Access Approval to require explicit permission for each access 2.112: = - am = 3 S % Y Pres “iw"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Access Approval lets you approve or deny Google personnel access to your resources, ensuring administrative access aligns with support case timelines."},{"id":"116","question":"To meet compliance regulations, an organization needs to prove that encryption keys are not managed by Google. What should they use?","options":{"A":"Use Google-managed keys and store all audit logs in Cloud Logging","B":"Use external key manager with Cloud EKM for key control assurance","C":"Enable default encryption settings for all services using Cloud IAM","D":"Create symmetric keys in Cloud KMS and assign Viewer role to all staff slay, %, \', w =A ol = =. oe % AY “an “iw"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud External Key Manager (Cloud EKM) uses keys held in a supported external key manager outside Google\'s infrastructure. Google never possesses the key material, and you can deny access at any time, which proves keys are not managed by Google.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) and C) Google-managed or default encryption is exactly what the regulator wants to avoid. D) Cloud KMS keys are customer-managed but still live in Google\'s infrastructure, and giving everyone Viewer adds nothing to control.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"117","question":"How can an organization ensure that all data created by users is encrypted using their own encryption keys?","options":{"A":"Enforce CMEK usage with Org Policy constraint on all services","B":"Require default encryption only at the folder or billing account level","C":"Set audit log filters to detect any encrypted resource access attempt","D":"Manually assign service accounts with the Owner role to each key"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Organization Policy constraints can enforce the use of CMEK across services, ensuring encryption uses customer-controlled keys for compliance."},{"id":"118","question":"What is the role of Cloud DLP in securing sensitive information across GCP?","options":{"A":"It replaces encryption and IAM by creating real-time access filters","B":"It decrypts all sensitive content before writing it into BigQuery logs","C":"It discovers, classifies, and redacts sensitive data automatically qull","D":"It transfers secret information to third-party platforms for validation &” . willy,"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud Data Loss Prevention (DLP) automatically inspects and redacts sensitive data, like PII, helping meet privacy and compliance requirements."},{"id":"119","question":"What GCP feature allows service identity separation between development and production?","options":{"A":"Use separate service accounts scoped to each environment only","B":"Assign network tags to enforce traffic rules between all workloads","C":"Add firewall rules with labels for application-level zone restriction","D":"Set up one global key for all services and rotate the key annually MNT"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Using different service accounts per environment enforces separation of duties and limits the blast radius of compromised credentials."},{"id":"120","question":"A company needs to ensure that only trusted identities can access sensitive APIs. What should they implement?","options":{"A":"Use default service accounts with custom-defined permissions per user","B":"Apply Cloud Logging filters to detect unsafe access to all endpoints","C":"Implement IAM Conditions with device and IP-based restrictions","D":"Assign Owner role to IAM groups and audit them weekly via logs 5 “iw"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. IAM Conditions plus context-aware access (Access Context Manager access levels based on device posture, IP range or region) allow a call only when both the identity and its context are trusted. This is the zero-trust model behind BeyondCorp and IAP.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) default service accounts are over-privileged. B) logging filters detect after the fact; they do not prevent access. D) the Owner role with weekly audits is the opposite of least privilege.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"121","question":"To protect against zero-day attacks, which approach is best when securing cloud infrastructure?","options":{"A":"Use firewall rules with all ports opened internally for flexibility","B":"Apply defense-in-depth using multiple layers of security controls","C":"Disable audit logging and enable faster recovery from future attacks","D":"Use minimal service accounts with overly permissive IAM policies “1iWw* avy 7 TT"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Defense-in-depth combines layered security mechanisms such as IAM, network controls, and monitoring to reduce exposure to new threats."},{"id":"122","question":"Which GCP service helps protect cloud applications from DDoS attacks?","options":{"A":"Cloud VPN to reroute public traffic across global internal networks","B":"Cloud Logging to detect spikes in traffic and user-agent anomalies","C":"Cloud Armor to filter and block malicious incoming traffic “1Ww* Ney, TTT","D":"Secret Manager to prevent unauthorized access to applications A)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud Armor protects against distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks by applying security policies at the edge of the Google network."},{"id":"123","question":"How should you implement secure password storage in a cloud-native application?","options":{"A":"Store encrypted passwords in runtime memory without backups","B":"Save passwords inside container images with strong permissions","C":"Use Secret Manager with access control policies and audit logs","D":"Store passwords in the database alongside public user attributes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For compliance with healthcare standards (e.g., HIPAA), encryption with CMEK combined with limited IAM roles ensures only authorized access to sensitive health data."},{"id":"124","question":"An organization wants to be alerted when IAM policy changes happen. What should they configure?","options":{"A":"Create log-based alerting on Admin Activity in Audit Logs","B":"Disable IAM policy inheritance to prevent any permission override","C":"Enable password logging in Stackdriver for policy verification Wet","D":"Create manual reports from IAM console every six months"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Audit Logs track IAM policy changes. Log- based alerts on these changes help detect unauthorized or risky modifications in real-time."},{"id":"126","question":"A customer wants to prevent accidental public exposure of Cloud Storage buckets. hat should be configured?","options":{"A":"Set lifecycle rules to delete objects with public permissions daily","B":"Encrypt bucket contents using an external KMS key every hour","C":"Enforce ‘public access prevention’ policy at the org or folder level","D":"Configure static credentials in all apps using those storage buckets"},"correct":"C","explanation":"GCP supports a policy that fully blocks public access to Cloud Storage resources, which is ideal for compliance and data protection."},{"id":"127","question":"Which mechanism ensures a secure way to connect a private VPC network to GCP services?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud NAT and allow only outbound access with custom routes","B":"Use Private Google Access to route requests securely to GCP APIs","C":"Assign public IPs to VM instances and block all outbound access","D":"Deploy Cloud CDN and set up access rules at the backend service ; as THA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Private Google Access enables instances without public IPs to securely access Google APIs over Google’s private backbone, maintaining network isolation."},{"id":"129","question":"A customer wants to be notified when someone accesses a storage bucket. What should they do?","options":{"A":"Create an audit log sink and trigger alert from the exported logs","B":"Create a file watcher that polls bucket access every five minutes","C":"Run a cron job that checks metadata of objects and user details","D":"Enable auto-encryption for every object uploaded to the bucket R"},"correct":"A","explanation":"By exporting Audit Logs and setting alert conditions, organizations can monitor access to sensitive data and respond to unauthorized activity."},{"id":"131","question":"Which control helps enforce role-based access to APIs and services?","options":{"A":"Apply firewall rules based on the subnet tag in each zone","B":"Use IAM roles to assign specific permissions to service identities","C":"Enable internal HTTPS load balancing across all available projects","D":"Use DNS peering and cloud routers to isolate region-wide access"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IAM roles define what actions a user or service can take on a resource, enabling fine-grained access control for APIs and services."},{"id":"133","question":"Which service allows you to store and retrieve secrets in a secure, auditable way?","options":{"A":"Cloud Storage with bucket-level IAM and object versioning enabled","B":"Memorystore with access controls applied to Redis data endpoints","C":"Secret Manager with audit logging and fine-grained access control","D":"Cloud Bigtable with row-level encryption on sensitive credentials"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Secret Manager, with audit logging and fine-grained access control per secret.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Versioned secrets with states (enabled, disabled, destroyed) and aliases.\\n• IAM per secret; every access is in Cloud Audit Logs (Data Access).\\n• Scheduled rotation with Pub/Sub notifications; automatic or regional replication; optional CMEK.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabstore and retrieve secrets\xbb, \xabsecure\xbb, \xabauditable\xbb.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Storage with IAM and versioning (possible but not designed for secrets: no built-in rotation or per-version access); Memorystore (in-memory Redis cache); Bigtable (NoSQL database, not a secret store).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"135","question":"How can you prevent escalation of privileges via custom roles?","options":{"A":"Grant Owner access only on weekends to minimize exposure","B":"Review role permissions to ensure they do not include admin rights","C":"Assign roles at the project level with full resource inheritance","D":"Allow users to duplicate roles for testing in multiple projects %, of J iS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. By reviewing each custom role’s permissions so it does not include administrative or IAM-management permissions (least privilege).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A custom role is a permission list; if it includes setIamPolicy, the user can grant themselves more roles.\\n• Review roles periodically and use IAM Recommender / role recommendations to drop unused permissions.\\n• Restrict who can create or edit roles (iam.roles.create / iam.roles.update).\\n\\nExam tip: \'custom roles\', \'escalation of privileges\' → review permissions, least privilege.\\nDo not confuse with: assigning at project level with full inheritance (widens scope, worsens risk); granting Owner \'weekends only\' or duplicating roles does not control permissions.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"137","question":"A customer needs to restrict access toa set of VMs to only internal apps. What should be done?","options":{"A":"Remove external IP addresses from VM templates and recreate them","B":"Use firewall rules to allow access from trusted internal subnets","C":"Assign static external IPs and block unused protocols at the host % ee villa, “1iWw* \\\\\\\\ TTA","D":"Schedule periodic reboots to flush inbound connection buffers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Firewall rules provide the most granular method to restrict VM access, ensuring only specified internal services can connect."},{"id":"138","question":"Which of the following supports compliance with GDPR and similar laws?","options":{"A":"Use only predefined roles with project-level Viewer permissions","B":"Enable Data Regions and specify multi-region storage in the EU","C":"Enable billing exports to track resource creation timestamps vies -_ =","D":"Assign Editor roles to users for resource deployment flexibility a”. willy, oe"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. GDPR-style data residency is met by keeping data in EU locations: EU multi-region or EU regional buckets and datasets, enforced with the gcp.resourceLocations org policy (and optionally Assured Workloads for EU data boundary controls).\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Viewer roles do not address where data lives. C) billing exports track cost, not compliance. D) broad Editor roles weaken security, which hurts compliance.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"139","question":"How can you monitor attempts to use service accounts from unauthorized sources?","options":{"A":"Enable cloud billing to track service account network usage","B":"Use Audit Logs to trace service account usage patterns","C":"Set password rotation policies for all service accounts","D":"Use third-party monitoring tools to restrict GCP API usage By “iw"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Audit Logs record which identities use service accounts and from where, enabling visibility into potential abuse or misconfiguration."},{"id":"140","question":"Which design ensures that GKE workloads access only the APIs they require?","options":{"A":"Create a single IAM role with all permissions needed by developers","B":"Assign Owner role to the default service account for all namespaces","C":"Use Workload Identity to bind workloads to scoped IAM roles % = ani\xbb ae “Ss Ti silly","D":"Use environment variables to share credentials across all pods \\\\ SS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Workload Identity securely maps Kubernetes service accounts to IAM roles, giving workloads minimal access to only required APIs."},{"id":"141","question":"How do VPC Service Controls enhance data security in GCP?","options":{"A":"They prevent data exfiltration by restricting service boundaries","B":"They provide high-speed routing between hybrid and cloud networks","C":"They block port-level traffic to all resources without exception","D":"They monitor IAM activity logs and disable sensitive APIs 77 des \\"ny PaaS S “1Ww* \\"2, S Ty, A) manually"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. VPC Service Controls draw a service perimeter around projects and Google APIs (Cloud Storage, BigQuery and others). Even with valid credentials, data cannot be copied to resources outside the perimeter, which mitigates exfiltration and stolen-credential use.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) hybrid routing is Interconnect or VPN. C) it does not block ports; firewall rules do. D) it does not monitor IAM activity; Cloud Audit Logs and Security Command Center cover that.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"142","question":"What helps prevent unauthorized access when using service account credentials?","options":{"A":"Embed keys directly in the source code and obfuscate them fully","B":"Rotate service account roles instead of rotating actual keys","C":"Avoid key creation and use IAM for fine-grained permissions % =","D":"Schedule key sharing with DevOps for each deployment “11Ww* selly manually"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Avoiding key creation altogether (attached service accounts, Workload Identity Federation, impersonation), combined with narrowly scoped IAM roles, removes long-lived secrets that can be stolen and limits what any identity can do.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) obfuscated keys in code are still extractable. B) changing roles does not invalidate a leaked key. D) sharing keys with people multiplies exposure.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"143","question":"Which IAM feature helps enforce short- lived access to critical resources?","options":{"A":"Assign predefined roles and set expiration on the resource itself","B":"Rotate credentials every day using a custom startup script","C":"Use IAM Conditions with expiration timestamps for access policies","D":"Set object-level access expiration via gsutil after each upload :"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. An IAM Condition with a time expression, such as request.time < timestamp(\'2026-11-01T00:00:00Z\'), creates a binding that expires automatically. This is the native way to grant temporary or break-glass access. Privileged Access Manager (PAM) builds on this for just-in-time requests.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) resources do not carry role expiry. B) rotating credentials by script does not limit role duration. D) object-level expiry handles data lifecycle, not access.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"144","question":"Which is a key benefit of enabling Cloud Armor for your application?","options":{"A":"It encrypts all backend services using mutual TLS exclusively","B":"It protects applications from web-based and DDoS attacks","C":"It enables usage-based billing for Layer 4 load balancing","D":"It replaces traditional CDN routing with custom firewall logic"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Protecting the application from web attacks (OWASP Top 10: SQLi, XSS) and DDoS attacks at Google’s edge.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• It attaches as a security policy to backend services of an external load balancer (for example the global external Application Load Balancer).\\n• IP/geo rules, preconfigured WAF rules, rate limiting, and bot protection.\\n• Adaptive Protection uses ML to detect layer-7 DDoS and suggest rules.\\n\\nExam tip: \'DDoS\', \'WAF\', \'OWASP\', \'block IPs/countries\' → Cloud Armor.\\nDo not confuse with: mTLS (not its job), L4 load-balancing billing, or a Cloud CDN replacement (they can be used together).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"146","question":"How should a customer enforce organization-wide logging retention?","options":{"A":"Enable Stackdriver Profiler to archive logs in cold storage always","B":"Assign Viewer roles to all users with access to shared dashboards","C":"Set retention policies on centralized log sinks in Cloud Logging","D":"Route all logs to Google Workspace using custom API connectors qull 10... “1 MN iey, A)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. You centralize logs with an organization-level aggregated sink to a central log bucket and set retention on that bucket.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Sink on the organization (or folder) with includeChildren=true: collects logs from every project.\\n• The destination is a log bucket (or BigQuery/Cloud Storage) with its own retention period (1 to 3650 days on log buckets).\\n• You can lock the bucket retention so nobody can shorten it (compliance).\\n\\nExam tip: \'organization-wide\', \'log retention\' → aggregated sink + bucket with retention.\\nDo not confuse with: Stackdriver Profiler (now Cloud Profiler) analyzes CPU/memory, it does not archive logs; Viewer roles or sending logs to Google Workspace do not set retention.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"148","question":"What is the role of Security Command Center in a GCP environment?","options":{"A":"It provisions user accounts and configures encryption keys","B":"It centralizes security findings and monitors cloud threats","C":"It replaces IAM policies with context-based access filtering","D":"It automates firewall changes across multi-region deployments Se"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Security Command Center provides visibility into security risks across GCP resources, helping teams detect and respond to threats."},{"id":"149","question":"You are reviewing the performance of a GKE cluster used in a customer-facing application. The cluster experiences latency spikes during autoscaling events. What should you recommend to improve performance consistency?","options":{"A":"Enable binary authorization to secure container images","B":"Configure a warm pool of pre-provisioned nodes to reduce cold start times","C":"Replace GKE with Cloud Functions to reduce operational complexity 7","D":"Reduce the number of pods to lower load on the cluster AL scheduler “nie tS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Warm pools pre-provision nodes so that autoscaling adds capacity with minimal delay. This helps eliminate performance issues due to cold start node provisioning."},{"id":"151","question":"You manage a critical application hosted on Compute Engine that must meet a 99.99% uptime SLA. What should you configure to ensure SLA compliance?","options":{"A":"Enable serial console logging for crash diagnostics","B":"Schedule maintenance events during off-peak hours","C":"Deploy instances across multiple zones with health checks and failover 4","D":"Use sustained use discounts to minimize compute cost Milyy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Deploying across multiple zones with health checks ensures high availability and meets SLA requirements by reducing single points of failure."},{"id":"153","question":"Your team needs to optimize storage costs for large volumes of infrequently accessed data. What is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Storage Nearline or Coldline for cost-effective archival","B":"Enable high-performance SSDs for faster throughput","C":"Compress files and store them in Firestore for searchability","D":"Use persistent disks instead of object storage for all file types"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Storage Nearline and Coldline are designed for data accessed less frequently, offering significant cost savings over standard storage."},{"id":"154","question":"You are asked to evaluate application latency during peak traffic. Which approach best identifies the cause of performance degradation?","options":{"A":"Review billing reports for unexpected spikes in spend","B":"Increase VM instance types to handle more load","C":"Use Cloud Trace to analyze request latency and service bottlenecks 4,","D":"Enable autohealing on Compute Engine managed instance groups \\\\at/75 \\\\) TN"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cloud Trace: distributed tracing that shows end-to-end latency of each request and of each span.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Apps are instrumented (OpenTelemetry or automatic integration in App Engine, Cloud Run, etc.).\\n• Each request produces a trace with spans per service/call (DB, external API, microservice).\\n• You see latency distributions and which component is the bottleneck.\\n\\nExam tip: \'latency\', \'microservices\', \'which component is slow\' → Cloud Trace.\\nDo not confuse with: a larger VM type (hides the symptom without diagnosing); autohealing (recreates failed VMs); billing reports.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"155","question":"A development team wants to deploy a new microservice architecture but needs to ensure cost visibility for each team. What should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Assign IAM roles based on service access levels","B":"Use labels and Cloud Billing reports to allocate costs per service","C":"Group microservices into a single VPC for management simplicity","D":"Schedule instance shutdowns during weekends to reduce usage - A S \'\xa2, ed te: oat TN"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Using labels lets you attribute costs to specific services or teams via Cloud Billing export, enabling fine- grained cost reporting."},{"id":"156","question":"An enterprise customer is trying to meet a 200ms API response time SLA but fails during batch load jobs. What should be done?","options":{"A":"Increase logging verbosity to capture API traces","B":"Reduce request timeout values in the application","C":"Add retries with exponential backoff in client libraries","D":"Separate batch processing and interactive traffic with different backends"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Separating batch and real-time traffic ensures that large jobs don’t degrade the responsiveness of customer-facing APIs."},{"id":"157","question":"Your company wants to assess business value gained from a recent GCP migration. Which metric best represents this?","options":{"A":"Percentage reduction in operational costs post-migration","B":"Number of cloud-native APIs integrated into production","C":"Growth of virtual machine usage across departments","D":"Quantity of tickets opened in Cloud Support post- migration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Business value is often tied to cost reduction or revenue increase. A drop in operational costs directly reflects value gained."},{"id":"158","question":"The CTO asks for a dashboard to track uptime and service health across all GCP workloads. What should be implemented?","options":{"A":"Enable debug logging in all application environments","B":"Use Cloud Asset Inventory to monitor resource compliance","C":"Configure Cloud Monitoring dashboards with uptime checks and alerts","D":"Export logs to BigQuery and build manual reports using SQL 44 :"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cloud Monitoring dashboards with uptime checks and alert policies.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Uptime checks probe endpoints from several locations and measure availability and latency.\\n• Dashboards aggregate metrics from many projects using metrics scopes (a scoping project that can see the others).\\n• Alert policies notify when a check fails or a metric degrades.\\n\\nExam tip: \'dashboard\', \'uptime\', \'service health\' → Cloud Monitoring + uptime checks + alerts.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Asset Inventory (resource inventory/compliance, not health); debug logging everywhere (noise and cost); BigQuery export with manual reports (not real time).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"160","question":"A mobile gaming app faces high egress charges from Cloud Storage. How can you reduce these costs effectively?","options":{"A":"Enable CMEK on all Cloud Storage buckets for billing insights","B":"Enable VPC Flow Logs to audit traffic across the network","C":"Use Cloud CDN to cache static assets close to end users","D":"Use object lifecycle rules to automatically delete old assets a ny aly OF awe Rn K"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud CDN caches content at edge locations, reducing the volume of data transferred from origin buckets and lowering egress costs."},{"id":"161","question":"A healthcare application hosted on GKE fails to meet performance KPIs under load. hat optimization should be made?","options":{"A":"Enable horizontal pod autoscaling to adjust capacity as needed","B":"Enable binary authorization to verify container authenticity","C":"Disable preemptible VMs and switch to committed-use pricing","D":"Replace persistent disks with SSDs to reduce boot latency 73 %, m1 — ol TTY"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Horizontal Pod Autoscaling automatically adjusts workload replicas based on CPU or custom metrics to maintain performance under load."},{"id":"162","question":"You need to ensure cost transparency across multiple GCP projects managed by different teams. What should be done?","options":{"A":"Create custom roles that restrict billing account access per team","B":"Apply consistent labels and export billing data to BigQuery","C":"Schedule monthly email reports via Cloud Monitoring alerts % _ = ill Uy, %","D":"Enable data residency controls to organize billing accounts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Labels enable cost segmentation by team, project, or service. Exporting to BigQuery allows flexible reporting for cost transparency."},{"id":"163","question":"Which metric best reflects the reliability of a critical e-commerce service?","options":{"A":"Number of registered users per week on the platform","B":"Volume of storage used across Google Cloud resources","C":"Percentage of uptime over a rolling 30-day window","D":"Amount of cache hits recorded across the application"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Percentage of uptime (availability) over a rolling 30-day window.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Measures the fraction of time (or of requests) the service responded correctly.\\n• A rolling window avoids a calendar month \'resetting\' the counter and reflects recent experience.\\n• It is the typical SLI on which availability SLOs and SLAs are defined.\\n\\nExam tip: \'reliability\', \'availability\', \'SLA\' → % uptime over a 30-day window.\\nDo not confuse with: registered users (business), storage used (capacity), cache hits (efficiency): they do not measure whether the service works.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"164","question":"A company wants to improve latency for global users of a web app. Which change will provide the best performance boost?","options":{"A":"Upgrade to larger Compute Engine VM machine types","B":"Add more VPC firewall rules for east-west traffic control","C":"Use Cloud Load Balancing with global external frontends","D":"Replace HTTPS with gRPC to improve payload handling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud Load Balancing offers a globally distributed frontend that automatically routes users to the nearest healthy backend."},{"id":"165","question":"How can you best monitor whether a GCP service meets its defined SLA?","options":{"A":"Use uptime checks and SLO dashboards in Cloud Monitoring","B":"Review error logs and audit reports manually each day","C":"Enable all debug log levels across production systems","D":"Export application metrics to CSV and calculate uptime % 7 iamiee “oe Ts a SY"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Uptime checks and SLO dashboards in Cloud Monitoring (Service Monitoring).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You define a service and its SLIs (availability, latency) and an SLO (e.g. 99.9% over 30 days).\\n• Cloud Monitoring computes compliance and remaining error budget in real time.\\n• Uptime checks add an external view of availability; alerts fire if you are close to missing the target.\\n\\nExam tip: \'meets the SLA\', \'real-time visibility\' → SLOs + uptime checks in Cloud Monitoring.\\nDo not confuse with: reviewing logs by hand every day, turning on debug in production, or exporting to CSV: manual, slow, and error-prone.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"166","question":"You are asked to reduce the cost of a data processing pipeline running nightly on Dataflow. What should be done?","options":{"A":"Move from streaming to batch mode to improve precision","B":"Use flex templates and enable autoscaling of worker nodes","C":"Increase number of workers and disable autoscaling options","D":"Increase persistent disk size to handle large file writes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Enable worker autoscaling (and package the job as a Flex Template to standardize how it is launched).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• With autoscaling, Dataflow adjusts worker count to pending work and scales down when workers are surplus.\\n• You can cap the maximum with max_num_workers to bound cost.\\n• Flex Templates let you launch a parameterized pipeline from Cloud Scheduler or Composer; by themselves they do not save money.\\n\\nExam tip: \'reduce Dataflow cost\' → autoscaling (and FlexRS for flexible batch).\\nDo not confuse with: more workers without autoscaling (more expensive); switching streaming to batch \'for accuracy\' (does not make sense); larger disks (more cost).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"168","question":"Which KPI is most relevant when evaluating user satisfaction with an online transaction system?","options":{"A":"Number of backups performed during business hours","B":"Average memory use of backend server instances","C":"Average end-to-end response time for transaction workflows","D":"Monthly costs for API Gateway usage across projects"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Response time directly affects user experience and satisfaction, especially in systems handling financial or transactional processes."},{"id":"169","question":"An analytics platform must meet its SLA of 99.9% uptime. Which GCP design ensures the most availability?","options":{"A":"Use regional managed instance groups with load balancing","B":"Use zonal deployments with health checks and failovers","C":"Use multi-zone deployments with autoscaling and health checks","D":"Use a single region with persistent disks and GPU % ci 11 3 Sueno? mf 411\xbb selly acceleration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Deploy across multiple zones with autoscaling and health checks (in practice: a regional MIG behind a load balancer).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• If one zone fails, instances in the others keep serving.\\n• Health checks detect unhealthy instances; autohealing recreates them and the LB stops sending them traffic.\\n• Autoscaling replaces the lost capacity.\\n\\nExam tip: \'99.9% or more\', \'availability\' → multi-zone + health checks + autoscaling.\\nDo not confuse with: zonal deployments (one zone = single point of failure); a region with GPUs (performance, not availability).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"171","question":"Which KPI is most effective for monitoring long-term infrastructure cost optimization?","options":{"A":"Volume of backup snapshots across storage regions","B":"Total monthly spend normalized by user or team usage","C":"Count of active virtual machines provisioned per hour","D":"Number of projects created within each organization la, % eL/ Ss Creer oan “we"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Total monthly spend normalized by usage (per user, team, or business unit): a \'unit cost\'.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Absolute spend rises if the business grows; dividing by a unit of value (user, transaction) shows whether you are more efficient.\\n• Lets you compare months and teams fairly.\\n• Calculated with billing export to BigQuery + usage metrics.\\n\\nExam tip: \'long-term\', \'cost optimization\', \'efficiency\' → cost normalized per unit.\\nDo not confuse with: snapshot count, active VMs, or projects created: they count resources, not efficiency.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"172","question":"Your client is overspending on App Engine services. What is the most efficient fix?","options":{"A":"Move traffic to Compute Engine using standard pricing","B":"Migrate services to Cloud Functions using static instances","C":"Enable autoscaling and optimize memory and CPU quotas","D":"Add more memory to reduce cold-start latency in Europe Kes willy = * le) pent OP: ans"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Configure autoscaling correctly and rightsize instance memory/CPU to what the app actually needs.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• In app.yaml you choose instance_class (F1, F2…) and automatic_scaling parameters: max_instances, min_instances, target_cpu_utilization, max_concurrent_requests.\\n• A high min_instances or oversized instances pay for idle capacity.\\n• App Engine Standard can scale to zero when there is no traffic.\\n\\nExam tip: \'overspending on App Engine\' → rightsizing + autoscaling.\\nDo not confuse with: migrating to Compute Engine (more operations, not necessarily cheaper); more memory (more cost).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"175","question":"What is the most cost-efficient storage choice for quarterly backup archives?","options":{"A":"Cloud SQL backups using SSD and high-availability mode","B":"Cloud Filestore standard tier with regional replication","C":"Cloud Storage Coldline tier with lifecycle rules","D":"Compute Engine persistent disks with RAID 10 setup est hilly a % A \\\\oetno"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cloud Storage Coldline with lifecycle rules.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Coldline is designed for data read about once a quarter (90-day minimum duration).\\n• Lifecycle rules move backups to Archive or delete them when retention expires.\\n• Much cheaper per GB than disks or databases.\\n\\nExam tip: \'quarterly\', \'backup archives\', \'cost-efficient\' → Coldline (+ lifecycle).\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud SQL backups with SSD/HA (expensive and not an archive); Filestore (NFS for active workloads); persistent disks with RAID 10 (expensive and not for archival).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"176","question":"Which strategy helps track whether service reliability meets user expectations?","options":{"A":"Measure SLO compliance and track error budget burn rate","B":"Monitor CPU and memory usage of the hosting containers","C":"Measure API response volume sorted by query parameter","D":"Report total number of logged events per application hour sly, yy Sanna? oan Ti s SS"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. SLOs express user expectations (for example, 99.9% of requests succeed in under 300 ms). Tracking SLO compliance and the error budget burn rate shows whether reliability meets them and when to slow releases. Cloud Monitoring has native SLO and burn-rate alerting.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) CPU and memory are resource signals, not user experience. C) request volume per parameter is traffic, not reliability. D) log event counts do not reflect user-perceived quality.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"177","question":"An internal app’s cost is growing unexpectedly. What is the best method to locate the cause?","options":{"A":"Export logs from Cloud Functions and review latency","B":"Analyze GCP billing reports with label-based attribution","C":"Create new IAM roles with audit logging enabled on storage % os","D":"Rebuild deployment using a single zone for low latency sty “1Ww* Ss"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Billing reports, or the Cloud Billing export to BigQuery, broken down by project, service, SKU and labels (team, app, environment) show exactly which resource or component drives the growth.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) function latency logs do not reveal cost. C) new IAM roles are unrelated. D) redeploying to a single zone is a change, not a diagnosis, and it hurts availability.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"178","question":"To meet user performance expectations, which metric should you optimize?","options":{"A":"API request volume tracked per minute per region","B":"End-to-end latency between request and response cycles","C":"Total number of databases active in production cone “s maw’ a Y","D":"Hourly VM instance count in use by project group"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. End-to-end latency between request and response, which is what the user perceives.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Includes network, load balancer, backend services, and database.\\n• Tracked by percentiles (p95/p99) with Cloud Monitoring and diagnosed with Cloud Trace.\\n• It is the classic performance SLI on which SLOs are set.\\n\\nExam tip: \'user performance expectations\' → end-to-end latency.\\nDo not confuse with: request volume (load, not experience); number of databases or VMs per hour (capacity/cost).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"179","question":"A customer wants to ensure their Cloud Run service scales predictably with traffic. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Configure VPC egress with minimum instance limits","B":"Add a load balancer with regional instance backends","C":"Set concurrency limits and enable autoscaling policies","D":"Use gRPC endpoints with HTTP 2 multiplexing by default Nn oc L/ A)"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cloud Run scales the number of instances from in-flight requests divided by the per-instance concurrency setting. Tuning concurrency, plus min and max instances (min instances to avoid cold starts), makes scaling predictable.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) VPC egress settings control outbound networking. B) Cloud Run already includes request routing; a load balancer with instance backends does not make it scale. D) HTTP/2 or gRPC changes the protocol, not the scaling behavior.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"182","question":"How can you reduce BigQuery costs ina project with high daily scan volume?","options":{"A":"Increase reservations to handle batch workloads faster","B":"Use materialized views for repetitive aggregate queries","C":"Use Flat-rate pricing and rerun queries at peak hours","D":"Convert tables to JSON and move to external federation ny The: “@ Sawn et wert “1inw* a4, Re"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Create materialized views for those repetitive aggregate queries.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A materialized view precomputes and stores the result; BigQuery keeps it incrementally up to date.\\n• Queries read the view (few bytes) instead of the full base table.\\n• Smart tuning: BigQuery can rewrite queries on the base table to use the view automatically.\\n\\nExam tip: \'repetitive queries\', \'aggregations\', \'lots of scanning\' → materialized views.\\nDo not confuse with: more reservations (you pay more capacity); \'flat-rate\' (old model; today BigQuery editions); converting to JSON and using external tables (usually slower and does not reduce scan).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"183","question":"Which KPI helps validate performance goals for a cloud-hosted search engine?","options":{"A":"Memory utilization rate for full index storage","B":"Average response time for user search queries","C":"Number of background index refreshes daily","D":"Number of users performing keyword searches"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Average response time of user searches (better still, their p95/p99 percentiles).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Directly measures what the user experiences when searching.\\n• Used as a latency SLI with an SLO (e.g. 95% of searches < 300 ms).\\n• Monitored in Cloud Monitoring and diagnosed with Cloud Trace.\\n\\nExam tip: \'performance goals\', \'search engine\' → query latency.\\nDo not confuse with: index memory use, number of index refreshes, or number of users: they do not say whether search is fast.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"184","question":"A financial services company wants to track cost trends per team. What\'s the solution?","options":{"A":"Create separate VPCs for all environment zones","B":"Export logs from billing account as JSON metrics","C":"Use labels on resources and analyze billing data","D":"Create new service accounts for each VM instance % = 11 3 Saeaet + & Te selly \\\\ S"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Put labels on resources (e.g. team=risk) and analyze billing data grouped by those labels.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Cloud Billing reports let you filter and group by label with trend charts.\\n• For detailed analysis, billing export to BigQuery and queries by label, service, and date.\\n• Mandatory labels via IaC or organization policies/review processes.\\n\\nExam tip: \'cost trends per team\' → labels + billing data.\\nDo not confuse with: separate VPCs (network, not cost); exporting logs as JSON (logs are not billing data); a service account per VM (identity).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"185","question":"Your GCP project frequently exceeds egress quotas. What change reduces egress costs?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud CDN to serve content closer to end users","B":"Use global load balancing with local response caching","C":"Set up multi-region GKE clusters for consistent routing % o","D":"Enable VM snapshots and regional replication policies 42)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Use Cloud CDN to serve content from caches close to users.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Cacheable responses are served from Google\'s edge, not from your backends.\\n• Less egress from the origin (VMs or buckets) and less load on the servers.\\n• CDN cache egress has its own rates, usually lower than internet egress from the origin.\\n\\nExam tip: \'egress\', \'repeated content\', \'end users\' → Cloud CDN.\\nDo not confuse with: global load balancing without CDN (routes, but does not cache); multi-region GKE (more resources, not less egress); snapshots and replication (add traffic).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"187","question":"Which approach is best for ensuring compliance with a 99.95% SLA?","options":{"A":"Deploy in one region and use zone-local disks always","B":"Use spot VMs and retry failed job steps post-crash","C":"Distribute across zones with health checks and failover","D":"Enable auto snapshots and disable network throttling 5"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Spread the load across several zones with health checks and automatic failover.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• 99.95% allows about 22 minutes of downtime per month: a zone failure without redundancy breaks it.\\n• Regional MIG or regional GKE cluster + load balancer with health checks.\\n• Also use regional data services (Cloud SQL HA, regional disks, regional Spanner).\\n\\nExam tip: \'99.95%\', \'SLA\' → multi-zone + health checks + failover.\\nDo not confuse with: one region with zonal disks (single point of failure); Spot VMs (Google can stop them at any time); snapshots (backup, not availability).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"188","question":"Which metric helps determine if autoscaling policies are tuned effectively?","options":{"A":"Monthly costs from VM uptime across all regions","B":"Number of requests hitting global HTTP endpoints","C":"CPU utilization before and after scale events occur ts","D":"Number of zones supporting backend VM replication a 4t*"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. CPU utilization (or the autoscaler\'s target metric) before and after each scale event.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• If CPU saturates before scaling, the autoscaler reacts late (target too high or initialization period too long).\\n• If CPU is very low after scaling, you overscale (overpay) or flap.\\n• Compare it with latency to see whether the user notices the scale event.\\n\\nExam tip: \'autoscaling tuned effectively\' → target metric around scale events.\\nDo not confuse with: monthly cost, request count, or number of zones: they do not show how the autoscaler responds.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"189","question":"Which method best ensures long-term cost visibility in multi-team GCP use?","options":{"A":"Use IAM roles to assign project spending privileges","B":"Use BigQuery billing export with custom labels for teams","C":"Use Pub/Sub to capture daily cost summaries by script % qtilly","D":"Use autoscaling instances and compare CPU utilization Bak) CS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Billing export to BigQuery combined with labels per team.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The export stores the full cost history in BigQuery, with no practical retention limit.\\n• Queries by label (team), project, service, and SKU; dashboards in Looker Studio.\\n• It is the basis of showback/chargeback across teams.\\n\\nExam tip: \'long-term\', \'multi-team\', \'cost visibility\' → BigQuery billing export + labels.\\nDo not confuse with: IAM roles (access, not visibility); scripts with Pub/Sub (reinventing the export); comparing CPU (that is not cost).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"190","question":"To reduce database response time under load, what is the best action?","options":{"A":"Add read replicas and disable autocommit for writes","B":"Use horizontal scaling and push logging to Cloud Run","C":"Enable query caching and add read replicas where needed","D":"Move writes to GCS and analyze metrics in BigQuery views oe Aorta"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Add query caching and read replicas where needed, to take reads off the primary instance.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Read replicas (Cloud SQL, AlloyDB read pools) serve reads; the primary focuses on writes.\\n• A cache (Memorystore for Redis/Valkey) serves frequent data in memory with sub-millisecond latency.\\n• The app must send reads to replicas and tolerate some replication lag.\\n\\nExam tip: \'read-heavy\', \'response time under load\' → cache + read replicas.\\nDo not confuse with: turning off autocommit (does not scale reads); sending logs to Cloud Run; moving writes to GCS (not a transactional database).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"191","question":"What is the most relevant KPI for user- facing latency monitoring?","options":{"A":"Time from client request to final content delivery","B":"Storage growth rate over monthly usage tracking","C":"Number of database inserts per background worker","D":"Weekly traffic logs with IP and regional grouping"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Time from the client request to final content delivery (end-to-end latency).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Includes network, CDN/load balancer, backend, and database: everything the user waits for.\\n• You can measure it on the client (RUM) or at the load balancer (total_latencies metric).\\n• Track it by percentiles (p95/p99) and set an SLO on it.\\n\\nExam tip: \'user-facing latency\' → end-to-end time from request to response.\\nDo not confuse with: storage growth, background worker inserts, or weekly logs by IP: they do not reflect user wait.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"192","question":"A product owner asks how to track cloud cost spikes. What is best?","options":{"A":"Enable real-time Stackdriver logs with project budgets","B":"Create scheduled reports using IAM role changes audit","C":"Set budget alerts and daily summaries in Cloud Billing","D":"Export all logs from GKE and monitor hourly error counts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Configure Cloud Billing budgets with threshold alerts (and review daily cost reports).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A budget is defined for a billing account, projects, services, or labels, with a fixed amount or based on last month\'s spend.\\n• Thresholds (e.g. 50%, 90%, 100%) on actual or forecasted spend send emails.\\n• They can also publish to a Pub/Sub topic to automate actions.\\n\\nExam tip: \'cost spikes\', \'notify\' → budget alerts in Cloud Billing.\\nDo not confuse with: Stackdriver logs (not cost); IAM change audit; counting GKE errors.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"193","question":"How do you ensure performance KPIs are tracked after deployments?","options":{"A":"Export metrics to Data Studio with Cloud Scheduler jobs","B":"Enable Firebase Crashlytics and view logs via Stackdriver","C":"Tag deployments and review monitoring export metrics","D":"Set SLOs in Cloud Monitoring and configure alerting rules la, ae 4 . “Ss 7TTIV\\\\ wily, ices (75 TTI A)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Define SLOs in Cloud Monitoring and configure alert policies on them.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• SLOs continuously evaluate SLIs (latency, availability) after each release, without a manual review.\\n• Alerts (ideally burn rate) fire if a deploy consumes the error budget faster than normal.\\n• Combine with canary deployments to roll back before everyone is affected.\\n\\nExam tip: \'tracked after deployments\', \'performance KPIs\' → SLOs + alerting.\\nDo not confuse with: exporting to Looker Studio with Scheduler (reports, not alerts); Crashlytics (mobile crashes); tagging deploys and reviewing exports (manual).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"194","question":"Which GCP tool best helps identify a specific billing anomaly?","options":{"A":"Stackdriver Logging with labels for filtered services","B":"BigQuery billing export and analysis by label or service","C":"Identity-Aware Proxy with sign-in logs per instance zone","D":"Dataflow with billing mode set to batch hourly mode WL A)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Billing export to BigQuery, analyzed by label, service, SKU, project, and date.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• With SQL you filter the spike day and group by service.description, sku.description, project.id, and labels.\\n• The detailed export goes down to the individual resource (e.g. which VM or bucket).\\n• Cloud Billing reports are a first view; BigQuery is for fine analysis.\\n\\nExam tip: \'billing anomaly\', \'identify the origin\', \'by SKU/service\' → BigQuery billing export.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Logging with labels (logs, not cost); IAP (access control); Dataflow \'billing mode\' (does not exist).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"195","question":"You want to improve performance and reduce cost for Pub/Sub jobs. What helps?","options":{"A":"Use push delivery and batch acknowledgments with retry","B":"Use Cloud Logging to capture endpoint latency from scripts","C":"Add retries in client apps and write payloads to GCS logs lla, Maly Gy ranee? “~ ITVS MIN, A)","D":"Move topic messages to Firebase or use global functions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Batch messages and choose the delivery mode (push or pull) well, with retries.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Pub/Sub bills by data volume with a 1,000-byte minimum per publish/pull/push request; sending many tiny messages separately is expensive.\\n• Client-library batching groups messages, reduces calls, and raises throughput.\\n• Push delivers to an HTTPS endpoint (Cloud Run) without keeping subscribers polling; pull gives more flow control.\\n\\nExam tip: \'performance and cost in Pub/Sub\' → batching.\\nDo not confuse with: writing payloads to GCS logs or moving messages to Firebase (they do not replace a messaging system).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"196","question":"A service must serve 99.999% uptime. What\'s the key deployment pattern?","options":{"A":"Use zonal autoscaling and load balancer failover groups","B":"Use preemptible VMs and start spares when traffic rises","C":"Use multi-region load balancing with health-checked backends","D":"Use cold standby across two Cloud Functions deployments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Multi-region load balancing with backends that pass health checks in several regions (active-active).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• 99.999% ≈ 26 seconds of downtime per month: you must survive even an entire region failing.\\n• A global external Application Load Balancer sends traffic to the nearest healthy region and automatically removes ones that fail.\\n• Data must also be multi-region (e.g. multi-region Spanner, with a 99.999% SLA).\\n\\nExam tip: \'five nines\' → active-active multi-region.\\nDo not confuse with: zonal autoscaling (one zone/region = point of failure); preemptible VMs (can disappear); cold standby (failover takes minutes).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"199","question":"Which change improves backend performance while keeping costs stable?","options":{"A":"Add GPUs to existing VMs and use regional placement groups","B":"Use Cloud Functions to reduce latency and cold start errors","C":"Use autoscaled instances and remove idle reserved capacity","D":"Add larger disks to each VM and monitor file system latency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Use autoscaled instances and remove idle reserved capacity.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Autoscaling adds capacity only at peaks, so performance holds when it is needed.\\n• Removing unused VMs or reservations frees budget that pays for the peaks.\\n• Rightsize machines with Recommender recommendations.\\n\\nExam tip: \'better performance without raising cost\' → autoscaling + remove idle resources.\\nDo not confuse with: adding GPUs (expensive and only useful for accelerated workloads); Cloud Functions \'to reduce cold starts\' (serverless has cold starts); larger disks (more cost).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"200","question":"What is the best way to track SLA violations in a global API service?","options":{"A":"Monitor the total bytes sent between zones and projects","B":"Count backend 500 errors logged in Stackdriver per day","C":"Use uptime checks and track request latency in dashboards","D":"Use GKE node logs and identify peak-hour memory spikes Re"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Uptime checks from several regions and tracking request latency on Cloud Monitoring dashboards.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Uptime checks measure external availability as a client in different parts of the world sees it.\\n• Latency and error rate (load balancer metrics) are the SLA\'s SLIs.\\n• SLOs and alerts warn before you miss the target.\\n\\nExam tip: \'global API\', \'SLA violations\' → uptime checks + latency in Cloud Monitoring.\\nDo not confuse with: bytes between zones (network cost); counting 500 errors per day (partial and late); GKE node memory spikes (internal cause, not an SLI).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"202","question":"You want to enforce a performance- based KPI on an internal tool. What\'s ideal?","options":{"A":"Track average latency per operation and trigger alerts","B":"Measure uptime per zone and store results in text files","C":"Count the number of rows inserted during job execution","D":"Export billing reports and mark high traffic project 1c *. = reer of “11Ww* aL IDs Ss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Measure average (or percentile) latency per operation and fire alerts when it exceeds the target.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Record each operation\'s latency as a metric (custom metric, log-based metric, or OpenTelemetry).\\n• Define a target (e.g. p95 < 500 ms) and an alert policy in Cloud Monitoring.\\n• That way the KPI is \'enforced\' automatically, not only observed.\\n\\nExam tip: \'enforce a performance-based KPI\' → measure latency per operation + alerts.\\nDo not confuse with: uptime per zone stored in text files (availability, no alerts); counting inserted rows (volume); billing reports (cost).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"203","question":"Asystem must alert stakeholders when latency exceeds 250ms. What works?","options":{"A":"Store latency in logs and process it in Dataflow pipelines","B":"Set SLO alerts in Monitoring and notify via Pub/Sub alerts","C":"Use Logging sinks to send metrics to a third-party vendor","D":"Configure quotas and raise thresholds during high traffic A> %, TTT my"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. A Cloud Monitoring alert (on a latency SLO or a metric threshold) with notifications, e.g. via Pub/Sub.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You define a latency SLI (requests < 250 ms) and an SLO, or a threshold alert on the latency metric.\\n• The alert policy evaluates in near real time and opens an incident.\\n• Notification channels: email, SMS, Slack, PagerDuty, webhooks, or Pub/Sub (to automate).\\n\\nExam tip: \'alert when latency > X\' → alert policy in Cloud Monitoring.\\nDo not confuse with: logs processed in Dataflow (complex and slow); sending metrics to third parties via logging sinks; raising quotas (notifies nobody).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"204","question":"A CIO wants to view monthly efficiency KPIs. Which metric helps most?","options":{"A":"Number of deployed services across internal project groups","B":"Total backend requests split by compute region and zone","C":"Cost per user session compared against traffic baseline","D":"VM reservation counts exported weekly to GCS as CSV (J CA 2 Sanaet & “11> seilly Ss"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cost per user session compared against a traffic baseline (unit cost).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Ties spend to value delivered: if traffic grows and cost per session falls, the platform is more efficient.\\n• Computed by combining billing export (BigQuery) with session metrics (analytics or logs).\\n• Lets you compare months even when volume changes.\\n\\nExam tip: \'efficiency KPI\', \'CIO\', \'monthly\' → cost per unit (session, transaction, user).\\nDo not confuse with: number of services deployed, requests per region, or reservation counts — activity with no link to cost.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"207","question":"Which option best supports cost predictability in Dataflow pipelines?","options":{"A":"Use flex templates with regional autoscaling and quotas","B":"Use static pricing with dedicated workers and high memory","C":"Use Cloud Functions as controllers and log CPU events % =","D":"Use Cloud Composer and set maximum retry attempts low L// A) s Ti"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Use standardized Flex Templates with capped autoscaling (max workers) and quotas.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• max_num_workers sets an autoscaling ceiling: the maximum cost per job is bounded.\\n• Project quotas (CPUs, IPs) cap total consumption in the region.\\n• Templates make every run use the same proven configuration.\\n\\nExam tip: \'cost predictability\' in Dataflow → autoscaling with limits + quotas.\\nDo not confuse with: dedicated high-memory workers without autoscaling (expensive and fixed); Cloud Functions as controllers; Composer with few retries (does not cap job resources).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"208","question":"To meet a 1-Second response KPI, what metric should be tracked?","options":{"A":"Number of GKE pods restarted during each deployment wave","B":"P95 or P99 latency for each API call over peak traffic","C":"Number of logged events on GCS buckets during request","D":"API availability in Cloud DNS zones filtered by response \\"ty 7 ob _ |) TT \\\\\\\\a"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. P95 or P99 latency of each API call during peak traffic.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• P95 = 95% of requests are faster than that value; it shows the slow tail real users feel.\\n• The average hides outliers: a 300 ms mean can coexist with 5% of requests at 3 s.\\n• Measure at peaks, which is when the KPI usually breaks.\\n\\nExam tip: \'response KPI\', \'strict latency\' → high percentiles (p95/p99).\\nDo not confuse with: pod restarts (stability), bucket events, or Cloud DNS availability — they do not measure API response time.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"211","question":"How can you identify noisy neighbors affecting VM latency?","options":{"A":"Use Ops Agent to capture metrics and set alert baselines","B":"Add IAM bindings that restrict CPU spike capabilities","C":"Export logs to BigQuery and manually inspect intervals","D":"Use shielded VMs with sandboxing and disable cloud sly, 47) sey A) Sanaet aS APIs"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. The Ops Agent collects detailed VM metrics, including CPU steal and wait, disk and network latency, and process metrics. With baselines and alert policies you can correlate latency spikes with contention from co-tenant or noisy workloads.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) IAM does not control CPU usage. C) manual log inspection is slow and logs lack the right metrics. D) Shielded VM protects boot integrity, not performance.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"212","question":"What can help reduce Cloud Storage access latency worldwide?","options":{"A":"Use signed URLs and fetch buckets from source regions","B":"Move assets to Firestore for document-style retrieval","C":"Use persistent disk cache with proxy-controlled writes","D":"Use Cloud CDN to cache content at global edge locations % = ee “1 )"},"correct":"D","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Cloud CDN, behind a global external Application Load Balancer with a backend bucket, caches Cloud Storage objects at Google edge locations. Users worldwide get them from a nearby cache instead of the bucket\'s region.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) signed URLs control access, not latency. B) Firestore is a document database, not a static asset store. C) persistent disk caches are zonal and local to a VM.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"213","question":"Which KPI is most suitable to evaluate support system efficiency?","options":{"A":"Total storage used by logs across support VPC subnets","B":"Cost per incident handled across all global user tiers","C":"Time to resolution for user-submitted support tickets","D":"API latency measured from hourly infrastructure scans avila, oS"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Time to resolution (MTTR for tickets) directly measures how efficiently support resolves users\' problems, which is an outcome KPI.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) log storage is an infrastructure metric. B) cost per incident is useful but measures cost, not efficiency of service. D) API latency from infrastructure scans is unrelated to support performance.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"214","question":"A cost anomaly is found in a staging environment. How to resolve?","options":{"A":"Use billing export and filter by label and SKU metrics","B":"Rebuild instance groups and enable secure boot features","C":"Deploy new network firewall rules and monitor ingress 4","D":"Modify the IAM policy bindings and rerun access tests"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Export billing data to BigQuery and filter by labels (env=staging) and SKU to find which resource and usage type caused the anomaly. Then fix it with rightsizing, deletion or schedules. Cost anomaly detection in Cloud Billing can raise the alert.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) secure boot is a security feature. C) firewall rules do not explain spend. D) IAM changes do not identify the cost driver.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"216","question":"Which metric best measures Dataflow pipeline health?","options":{"A":"Number of pipeline retries and logged job executions","B":"System lag between ingestion and final output write","C":"Total bytes transferred between zones in pipeline tasks","D":"Memory usage per worker VM over runtime duration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. System lag (and data freshness): how far processing trails data arrival.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• System lag: the longest time an element has been waiting to be processed.\\n• Data freshness: age of the watermark; how far results trail real time.\\n• If they grow steadily there is a backlog or bottleneck (too few workers, hot keys, slow sink).\\n\\nExam tip: \'pipeline health\', \'backlog\', \'real-time\' → system lag / data freshness.\\nDo not confuse with: retries or bytes between zones (partial symptoms); memory per worker (a resource, not flow health).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"217","question":"What is a good strategy to prevent unexpected budget overruns?","options":{"A":"Disable billing exports and enable spend trend analysis","B":"Set budget thresholds and tie alerts to billing Pub/Sub","C":"Use the Pricing Calculator weekly and download as PDF","D":"Set IAM deny rules based on project service account name aati! 11.5 “@ Senne"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Define budgets with alert thresholds and connect their notifications to Pub/Sub so you can automate responses.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Alerts at 50/90/100% of actual or forecast spend arrive before month-end close.\\n• The Pub/Sub message can trigger a function that shuts down non-critical resources or pages the team in Slack.\\n• Remember: a budget by itself does not cut spend.\\n\\nExam tip: \'prevent budget overruns\', \'proactive\' → budgets + alerts + Pub/Sub.\\nDo not confuse with: turning off billing export (you lose visibility); weekly Pricing Calculator (estimate, not control); IAM deny rules by service-account name (does not control spend).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"218","question":"What KPI best shows efficiency in serverless workloads?","options":{"A":"Cost per execution normalized by average latency time","B":"Storage cost as a percentage of memory used per session","C":"Function cold-start rate compared to retry invocation rate Hla, 14: ie “1Ww* silly, ~* estat Op TTI","D":"Peak invocation count per region in trailing thirty days"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cost per execution (invocation) related to average latency.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Serverless bills on usage (requests, CPU and memory during the run), so unit cost per invocation is the natural efficiency measure.\\n• Relating it to latency shows whether you save money by hurting performance.\\n• You optimize by tuning memory/CPU, concurrency, and min instances.\\n\\nExam tip: \'efficiency\', \'serverless\' → cost per invocation vs latency.\\nDo not confuse with: storage vs memory per session (meaningless); cold-start vs retry ratio (partial technical signal); peak invocations (volume).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"220","question":"Your app crashes under heavy load. What is a fast mitigation?","options":{"A":"Enable SLO alerts for future workloads and increase quota","B":"Split traffic with traffic director and move to east region","C":"Add autoscaling and pre-warmed instances in backend pool %, %, =","D":"Use Identity-Aware Proxy and limit user requests per second avy TT NK A) s Ts"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Under heavy load, the immediate fix is capacity: enable autoscaling (MIG autoscaler, Cloud Run max instances) and keep warm minimum capacity so new requests are not dropped while instances start.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) alerts for future workloads do not mitigate anything now. B) moving regions is slow and risky during an incident. D) IAP authenticates users; it is not a rate limiter (Cloud Armor rate limiting would be the security-side tool).","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"221","question":"A business dashboard takes too long to load. How to fix?","options":{"A":"Materialize dashboard queries and pre-aggregate tables","B":"Move SQL processing to Cloud Functions and use triggers","C":"Store dashboard data as JSON and query with Cloud Run","D":"Delay queries to off-peak hours and reduce chart usage la seily Saeaet Oa Te \\\\ >"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Materialize the dashboard queries and pre-aggregate the tables (materialized views or summary tables).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The dashboard reads already-computed results instead of aggregating millions of rows on every load.\\n• Materialized views refresh incrementally; summary tables refresh with scheduled queries.\\n• Combine with BI Engine to speed it up further.\\n\\nExam tip: \'slow dashboard\', \'repeated queries\' → pre-aggregation / materialized views.\\nDo not confuse with: moving SQL to Cloud Functions or storing JSON and querying it with Cloud Run (slower and more complex); delaying queries or removing charts (does not solve it).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"223","question":"Which strategy improves workload resilience across multiple Google Cloud regions?","options":{"A":"Replicate services to separate zones within a single region only","B":"Distribute services across multiple geographically distant regions","C":"Host all critical services in a single multizone region only","D":"Use a single regional GKE cluster to avoid data latency issues 1 e"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Distributing services across multiple regions provides geo-redundancy, enhancing availability during regional outages. It protects against localized failures, ensuring a globally resilient architecture."},{"id":"224","question":"What does an effective SLO (Service Level Objective) typically include?","options":{"A":"The total system budget for running operations annually","B":"A vague guideline of what the system should try to achieve","C":"A measurable target for availability, latency, or throughput","D":"Internal support team contact information for stakeholders % % a= “ani B Ts silly \\\\ SS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An SLO defines a quantitative goal for a system’s behavior (like 99.9% availability). It’s part of SRE principles to manage system reliability through measurable objectives."},{"id":"225","question":"Which monitoring strategy provides the most accurate insights into real-time service health?","options":{"A":"Use scheduled batch logs exported weekly to BigQuery","B":"Use Cloud Billing reports to infer system performance","C":"Use deployment logs to infer uptime retrospectively","D":"Use metrics, logs, and traces with real-time dashboards 7 e"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Combining metrics, logs, and traces in near- real-time dashboards (via tools like Cloud Monitoring and Cloud Trace) gives proactive visibility and facilitates fast incident detection."},{"id":"226","question":"You are designing a backup strategy for critical data in GCP. What ensures the highest reliability?","options":{"A":"Automate scheduled snapshots across regions for redundancy","B":"Store backups only in the same region to reduce cost overhead % 2","C":"Use manual snapshots stored in project-specific buckets . I, yy 22ers Ti TT","D":"Disable object versioning to avoid conflicts during restores s Sy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Automated cross-regional snapshots provide high availability and durability of backup data, ensuring recovery even if an entire region fails."},{"id":"227","question":"Which component in GCP enables root cause analysis during postmortems?","options":{"A":"Cloud Scheduler for scheduling batch jobs","B":"Cloud Load Balancer for routing incoming traffic","C":"Cloud Logging with correlation IDs and error reports","D":"Cloud Pub/Sub for buffering and processing data"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud Logging, especially when using correlation IDs, helps trace issues end-to-end, aiding in diagnosing faults and supporting postmortem analysis."},{"id":"228","question":"What is the key advantage of using Error Reporting in Cloud Operations Suite?","options":{"A":"It allows automatic retry of failed workloads","B":"It limits error logs based on IAM roles","C":"It aggregates and surfaces real-time application errors","D":"It prevents error messages from reaching developers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Error Reporting detects, aggregates, and alerts developers about uncaught exceptions and app crashes, aiding in quick resolution."},{"id":"229","question":"What approach ensures minimal downtime during VM instance updates?","options":{"A":"Use rolling updates with Managed Instance Groups","B":"Shut down all instances and relaunch with new images","C":"Manually restart all instances during low traffic","D":"Upgrade VM templates and redeploy all workloads together ally"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Rolling updates let instances update gradually, maintaining availability and reducing downtime during infrastructure changes."},{"id":"230","question":"How should a Cloud Architect define incident response in production systems?","options":{"A":"Manually search for issues in logs and respond accordingly","B":"Automate alerting and playbook-driven responses to issues","C":"Disable logs during production for performance reasons","D":"Alert the entire development team regardless of severity Villy, tte yy Ti % 77 \\\\ A)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. With automated alerts and playbook-driven (runbook) responses, escalated by severity.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Alert policies detect the problem from symptoms (SLIs) and notify the right on-call.\\n• Each alert links to a playbook with diagnosis and mitigation steps.\\n• Repetitive responses are automated (e.g. Pub/Sub → a function that restarts or scales).\\n\\nExam tip: \'incident response\', \'production\' → automated alerts + playbooks.\\nDo not confuse with: hunting problems by hand in logs (slow, reactive); turning logs off; paging the whole team regardless of severity (alert fatigue).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"232","question":"What Google Cloud feature supports managing the full lifecycle of containerized apps?","options":{"A":"Cloud Source Repositories for storing container manifests","B":"Cloud Scheduler for triggering scheduled container jobs","C":"Cloud Run Jobs for long-running background tasks","D":"Artifact Registry, Cloud Build, and GKE for full lifecycle Bh hawcat “ef “11Ww* Cres"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lifecycle management includes image storage (Artifact Registry), CI/CD (Cloud Build), and orchestration (GKE) — a robust stack for managing containerized apps end-to-end."},{"id":"233","question":"Which reliability metric helps quantify user Satisfaction over time?","options":{"A":"System usage reports","B":"Error budgets consumed vs remaining within SLOs","C":"Cost-to-performance ratio from billing exports","D":"Total resource consumption across the fleet"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An error budget reflects how much deviation from the SLO is acceptable. It helps balance innovation (deployments) with reliability."},{"id":"236","question":"Which best practice improves incident detection for customer-facing applications?","options":{"A":"Rely on customer complaints to raise alerts","B":"Monitor only infrastructure health and not app metrics","C":"Monitor SLIs such as latency, error rate, and availability","D":"Delay monitoring until application reaches full traffic"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SLIs (Service Level Indicators) measure aspects critical to user experience. Monitoring them helps detect incidents quickly and accurately."},{"id":"237","question":"What is the primary goal of running chaos engineering experiments?","options":{"A":"To test UI responsiveness across devices","B":"To verify how systems behave during failure conditions","C":"To simulate traffic growth and marketing impact 4%, SF 11.5 *s “@ Raaae\xae aS “1nWw* sly","D":"To assess code syntax correctness and debugging"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chaos engineering intentionally breaks parts of the system to observe behavior, identify weaknesses, and improve resilience."},{"id":"238","question":"Which is a key use case of Google Cloud\'s Uptime Checks?","options":{"A":"Monitor external availability of endpoints and services","B":"Estimate monthly billing and cost trends","C":"Schedule automatic scaling across services","D":"Audit resource compliance across organizations la, eld ally Sere TTI A)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Monitor external availability of endpoints and services (websites, APIs, TCP ports) from several locations worldwide.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Google probes the endpoint periodically and records whether it responds, the status code, and latency.\\n• They attach to alert policies to notify on outages.\\n• For private endpoints you use private uptime checks with Service Directory.\\n\\nExam tip: \'external availability\', \'endpoint down\' → uptime checks.\\nDo not confuse with: estimating cost (Cloud Billing), scaling services (autoscalers), or auditing compliance (Security Command Center / Cloud Asset Inventory).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"239","question":"Which GCP feature allows automated alerting based on custom metrics?","options":{"A":"IAM Conditions","B":"Cloud Monitoring Alert Policies","C":"Service Directory","D":"Audit Logs Configuration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alert Policies let you define threshold-based rules on metrics. They notify stakeholders when a system deviates from expected behavior."},{"id":"240","question":"What provides the best resilience for globally distributed static content?","options":{"A":"Store static files in regional buckets only","B":"Use a zonal bucket and configure retries","C":"Upload files via FTP to all regions","D":"Use Cloud CDN with Cloud Storage backend"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cloud CDN caches static content close to users globally. Backed by Cloud Storage, it boosts resilience and latency performance."},{"id":"241","question":"What ensures compliance during system decommissioning?","options":{"A":"Ignore logs if services are terminated","B":"Delete VMs and disks manually only","C":"Use data retention policies and audit logging My, \\"y sly","D":"Turn off billing exports for decommissioned services Gannet oan Ts \\\\ SS"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Compliant decommissioning keeps required records: apply retention policies (Cloud Storage bucket lock, log bucket retention) for what must be kept, and keep Cloud Audit Logs showing who deleted what and when.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) ignoring logs destroys the evidence trail. B) manual deletion with no records cannot be audited. D) disabling billing exports removes history.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"242","question":"Which strategy allows rollback of infrastructure during failed deployments?","options":{"A":"Use hardcoded deployment scripts","B":"Remove deployed resources manually","C":"Use Terraform with version control and state rollback","D":"Deploy changes with minimal documentation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Using Infrastructure as Code tools like Terraform with state files allows safe rollbacks, improving resilience after failed changes."},{"id":"243","question":"Which method offers automated issue resolution for known infrastructure faults in production?","options":{"A":"Schedule nightly reboots of all virtual machine resources","B":"Implement Cloud Functions triggered by monitoring alerts","C":"Redirect user traffic manually when issues are detected","D":"Disable alerting and rely solely on daily manual reviews"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Using Cloud Functions with alert policies enables automated remediation, ensuring faster, consistent responses to known problems."},{"id":"244","question":"What is the most reliable strategy for preserving logs in case of an incident investigation?","options":{"A":"Store logs in local SSDs and export periodically to CSV files","B":"Keep logs in memory to speed up data retrieval operations","C":"Route logs to BigQuery with retention and IAM controls","D":"Archive logs only after systems have been decommissioned WL \\\\ Ss: %"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Storing logs in BigQuery enables long-term retention, queryability, and access control, ideal for forensic and audit scenarios."},{"id":"246","question":"What\'s the best way to ensure version control and repeatability in infrastructure provisioning?","options":{"A":"Use shared spreadsheets to track manual setup steps","B":"Manage infrastructure using Terraform with modules","C":"Use one-time Bash scripts stored on local machines only","D":"Rely on team knowledge and document changes later tla, 47) sey Sanne? Oa Ts s S"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Terraform with reusable modules, stored in Git and applied through CI/CD (or Infrastructure Manager), gives versioned, reviewable and repeatable environments.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A), C) and D) spreadsheets, local scripts and tribal knowledge are neither versioned nor reproducible.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"247","question":"What monitoring strategy improves fault diagnosis in multi-tenant GKE clusters?","options":{"A":"Monitor node health alone and not container-level metrics","B":"Enable Google Cloud Managed Prometheus for workloads","C":"Collect logs only during peak usage times and discard the rest % Cs","D":"Disable metrics to prevent overload during large deployments willy A) “11Ww*"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus collects workload and container-level metrics (by namespace or tenant) with managed, globally queryable storage and PromQL. It is the recommended way to diagnose faults per workload in shared GKE clusters.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) node-only health hides container issues. C) logging only at peak loses the evidence. D) disabling metrics leaves you blind.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"250","question":"What approach best aligns with Google’s recommended Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) practices?","options":{"A":"Define SLOs and monitor SLIs to manage reliability tradeoffs","B":"Allocate resources based on the loudest stakeholder demands","C":"Avoid tracking downtime to prevent pressure on engineers MP 4% aoe “aw 13 selly","D":"Accept failures without formal error budgets or objectives S Ti \\\\ SS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Define SLOs and monitor SLIs to manage the tradeoffs between reliability and innovation, using error budgets.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• SLIs measure user experience; SLOs set how much reliability is enough.\\n• The error budget decides whether you speed up or slow down the change rate.\\n• It avoids over-engineering: you do not chase 100%.\\n\\nExam tip: \'SRE practices\' → SLOs + SLIs + error budgets.\\nDo not confuse with: allocating resources to whoever shouts loudest, not measuring outages so you \'do not pressure people\', or accepting failures with no formal targets.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"253","question":"What Google Cloud tool lets you define SLIs based on your service\'s specific metrics?","options":{"A":"Cloud Build pipelines with logs exported to BigQuery","B":"IAM conditions applied to service-specific log groups","C":"Cloud Monitoring custom dashboards and alert policies","D":"Cloud Run revisions used to manually track performance ol heen aS “1w*"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cloud Monitoring (Service Monitoring), with custom dashboards and alert policies.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You define a service (auto-detected on GKE, Cloud Run, App Engine, Istio, or a custom one).\\n• You create request-based SLIs (good/total) or windows-based SLIs from any metric, including custom and log-based metrics.\\n• On the SLI you define the SLO, see error budget on the dashboard, and create burn-rate alerts.\\n\\nExam tip: \'define SLIs with your own metrics\' → Cloud Monitoring.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Build with logs in BigQuery (CI/CD); IAM conditions (access control, not metrics); Cloud Run revisions followed by hand.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"257","question":"What method ensures rollback of Cloud Function deployments if an error occurs?","options":{"A":"Use source versioning with staged traffic rollout","B":"Overwrite functions directly without traffic controls","C":"Deploy manually and edit the live code in the console","D":"Disable traffic splitting to ensure faster updates eld"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Version the source and deploy with a staged traffic rollout (traffic splitting between revisions).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Each deploy creates an immutable revision; you can deploy the new one with no traffic.\\n• You send a small percentage (e.g. 5%) to the new revision and watch errors and latency.\\n• If it fails, you move 100% of traffic to the previous revision instantly (rollback).\\n\\nExam tip: \'rollback\', \'staged rollout\' → revisions + traffic splitting.\\nDo not confuse with: overwriting the function with no traffic control, editing code live in the console, or turning traffic splitting off.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"258","question":"Which design enables fast service restoration following configuration drift in production systems?","options":{"A":"Store current configurations in encrypted email backups","B":"Use manually written notes to recreate configurations quickly","C":"Apply infrastructure as code to revert to known state"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Infrastructure as code (IaC) ensures reliable re- application of desired system state, preventing issues from configuration drift."},{"id":"259","question":"What should you do when alert noise increases and impacts team response efficiency?","options":{"A":"Disable most alerts to prevent team stress overload","B":"Tune thresholds and remove non-actionable conditions","C":"Route all alerts to the highest-priority escalation team \\"y","D":"Wait for recurring alerts to stop before tuning policies eS homres 1 “1Ww*"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Excessive alerts reduce efficiency. Tuning policies improves signal-to-noise ratio, ensuring meaningful incident responses."},{"id":"261","question":"Which technique ensures traceability when executing deployments across environments?","options":{"A":"Use version-controlled CI/CD pipelines with audit logs","B":"Perform ad-hoc deployments using remote shell commands","C":"Apply configuration changes without change tracking tools","D":"Schedule deployments based on team members’ availability tes -_ - 1% eS yy Tae? ~ Ts Vly, TT NK s S"},"correct":"A","explanation":"CI/CD pipelines with version control and audit trails support traceable, compliant, and repeatable deployments across systems."},{"id":"262","question":"What is the primary benefit of using managed services in reliability-focused designs?","options":{"A":"Prevents developers from accessing cloud infrastructure","B":"Reduces IAM permissions required to manage resources","C":"Offloads operational complexity to Google Cloud teams","D":"Eliminates all failure domains by using custom machines"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Managed services reduce the operational burden, enabling teams to focus on code and architecture rather than infrastructure upkeep."},{"id":"263","question":"What provides proactive insight into degrading performance in a regional database?","options":{"A":"Enable firewall logging to track incoming database packets","B":"Set up custom metrics and latency-based alerting rules","C":"Remove monitoring to avoid excess usage fees over time","D":"Use billing reports to infer read/write activity patterns la, 14. 7TTIV\\\\ silly, sesh / 7s TTI Ss"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Managed Prometheus offers granular container-level monitoring in GKE, helping isolate and diagnose multi-tenant issues effectively."},{"id":"264","question":"What approach allows safe rollback of misconfigured service policies in production?","options":{"A":"Apply new policies manually via console and hope for success","B":"Store and version policies in source-controlled templates","C":"Disable policies temporarily without any proper testing","D":"Modify policies live while user traffic is fully active ally A)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Version-controlled policy templates allow easy reversion when misconfigurations occur, improving system reliability and governance."},{"id":"266","question":"Which mechanism helps detect and respond to security events in real time?","options":{"A":"Manual user reviews of login history every week","B":"Disable monitoring on low-usage days to cut costs","C":"Integrate SIEM alerts with automated Cloud Functions My, 47) WU] Ss CN TTT","D":"Archive logs to cold storage for compliance reviews"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pairing SIEM tools with Cloud Functions allows for real-time response to anomalies such as unauthorized access or threats."},{"id":"267","question":"What tool enables versioned control of policies and audit-enforced deployment?","options":{"A":"Enable Dataflow to monitor configuration throughput","B":"Use Config Controller with GitOps-based enforcement","C":"Disable CI/CD to allow faster manual changes","D":"Apply IAM bindings using random service accounts lay ceiad ORS Ti a SY"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Config Controller with GitOps ensures policy versioning, auditability, and automated policy deployment across resources."},{"id":"270","question":"What increases resilience when serving static websites from Google Cloud?","options":{"A":"Serve HTML from Compute Engine with internal IPs only","B":"Disable caching to ensure fresh content every second","C":"Use Cloud Storage with Cloud CDN for global delivery","D":"Redirect requests to a single regional bucket without failover % ee “iw 13 cae ‘Ss Tie"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Host static assets in Cloud Storage (multi-region or dual-region for durability) and serve them through Cloud CDN behind a global load balancer. You get global edge caching and resilience with no servers.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) a VM on internal IPs is a single point of failure and not public. B) disabling caching increases latency and load. D) a single regional bucket with no failover is fragile.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"271","question":"What feature helps prioritize incident response based on business impact?","options":{"A":"Review monthly costs to determine resource allocations","B":"Assign severity levels and match alerting by priority","C":"Group alerts randomly across all system services","D":"Trigger alerts only when multiple services are offline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"By using severity levels, incident response becomes focused and efficient, aligning response urgency with business impact."},{"id":"273","question":"What is a benefit of scheduled health checks for backend services?","options":{"A":"Avoids the need for autoscaling policies on all services","B":"Guarantees services never fail under heavy workloads","C":"Ensures early detection of service degradation or failure","D":"Eliminates the need for SLIs and SLOs for those services la, 4 es euett’ Ts 75 TTL \\\\ SY"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Billing Alerts provide real-time notifications when spending exceeds defined budget thresholds, preventing surprise overages."},{"id":"274","question":"What action best supports faster remediation during incident escalation?","options":{"A":"Notify all users without identifying the source of failure","B":"Restart all services across the environment in parallel","C":"Use playbooks with predefined mitigation steps and roles","D":"Delay response until logs have been manually reviewed % St nie Sanne? “oe Ts silly s SS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Use playbooks with predefined mitigation steps and assigned roles.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The playbook (runbook) says for each alert type how to diagnose, what to mitigate first (rollback, failover, scale), and whom to escalate to.\\n• Defined roles avoid confusion about who decides and who communicates.\\n• They are linked from the alert-policy documentation and improved after each postmortem.\\n\\nExam tip: \'faster remediation\', \'escalation\' → playbooks + roles.\\nDo not confuse with: notifying everyone without knowing the cause, restarting everything in parallel (risk of making it worse), or waiting to review logs by hand.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"275","question":"Which method reduces the risk of accidental resource deletion in GCP?","options":{"A":"Remove all labels to avoid attention during reviews","B":"Grant all users delete rights to speed up cleanups","C":"Apply resource locks and IAM least privilege policies","D":"Disable billing on unused resources to hide them tea tOy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Resource locks and least privilege IAM policies protect critical resources by limiting unauthorized or accidental deletion."},{"id":"277","question":"What improves observability when a system interacts with external APIs?","options":{"A":"Disable external logging to reduce cost of storage","B":"Use a proxy that blocks all requests to unknown endpoints","C":"Implement structured logging and API response tracing","D":"Rely only on external vendor status pages and emails wana lly"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Structured logs and tracing allow you to monitor latency, errors, and response trends, improving visibility into external dependencies."},{"id":"278","question":"Which approach improves security and reliability in production service deployments?","options":{"A":"Use separate CI/CD pipelines for staging and production","B":"Perform all tests and deployments using the same script","C":"Allow manual uploads for configuration files in production","D":"Deploy updates directly from local laptops via shell % oo THIS trae oS i Oe S access"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Isolating CI/CD pipelines ensures that staging and production have separate validation paths, reducing risk of accidental errors in production."},{"id":"279","question":"What helps ensure reliability during updates to critical configuration files?","options":{"A":"Disable configuration change logs for performance reasons","B":"Use source control and CI checks to validate updates","C":"Update configs using ad hoc edits in the management console","D":"Apply YAML changes only after users report system failure % ie ani 13 aL : s Ts \\\\ SS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tracking configuration in source control and validating with CI checks reduces misconfiguration and ensures Safer rollouts."},{"id":"280","question":"What best ensures service continuity when network partitioning occurs?","options":{"A":"Design services to retry and gracefully degrade output","B":"Restart all network-dependent applications during outages","C":"Rely on user-reported issues to detect failures in regions","D":"Disable alerting when packet loss exceeds 20% in one % v= ani ee Te selly \\\\ s zone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Graceful degradation and automatic retry logic help maintain partial functionality and user experience during network failures."}]},{"id":"gcp-profesional-cloud-architect-feb-2025","title":"GCP Profesional Cloud Architect — Feb 2025","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"Your company runs a web application on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) in a single region. The application experiences downtime during GKE control plane upgrades. What is the most effective way to improve reliability?","options":{"A":"Use multi-zonal node pools.","B":"Enable regional clusters.","C":"Use a single-zone cluster with auto-repair enabled.","D":"Configure a Custom GKE Upgrade Policy."},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. A regional GKE cluster replicates the control plane across three zones, so control plane upgrades and zonal outages do not make the API unavailable. Nodes are also spread across zones.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) multi-zonal node pools spread nodes, but a zonal cluster still has a single control plane. C) auto-repair fixes nodes, not control plane downtime. D) maintenance windows and exclusions control when upgrades happen, not whether the API stays up.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"2","question":"Your application logs are crucial for debugging and compliance. How can you ensure logs are reliably stored and retrievable even if a GCP region goes down?","options":{"A":"Store logs in a single-region Cloud Storage bucket.","B":"Use Cloud Logging with a log sink to a multi-region Cloud Storage bucket.","C":"Store logs in a Compute Engine instance.","D":"Export logs to BigQuery in the same region as the application."},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. A Cloud Logging sink that routes logs to a multi-region (or dual-region) Cloud Storage bucket stores them redundantly across regions. They survive a regional outage and can use retention locks for compliance.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) and D) single-region storage fails with that region. C) a VM is neither durable nor managed log storage.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"3","question":"A critical workload is running on Compute Engine instances. How can you ensure reliability in case of a zone failure?","options":{"A":"Use instance groups with regional autoscaling.","B":"Deploy instances in a single zone with persistent disks.","C":"Use snapshots to back up data regularly.","D":"Deploy a single high-memory VM instance."},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. A regional managed instance group spreads VMs across zones in a region, with autohealing and autoscaling. If one zone fails, the remaining zones keep serving and the MIG can recreate capacity.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) a single zone fails with the zone. C) snapshots enable recovery, not availability. D) one VM is a single point of failure.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"4","question":"Your company runs an API on Cloud Run that is experiencing intermittent failures. What is the best way to improve availability?","options":{"A":"Increase CPU and memory allocation.","B":"Deploy the Cloud Run service in multiple regions and use Cloud Load Balancing.","C":"Migrate to Compute Engine for more control.","D":"Use Cloud Pub/Sub instead of HTTP API calls."},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Deploy the Cloud Run service in several regions behind a global external Application Load Balancer with serverless NEGs. Traffic goes to the nearest healthy region, which removes regional single points of failure.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) more CPU or memory will not fix regional or intermittent infrastructure faults. C) Compute Engine adds operational burden and is not more available. D) Pub/Sub changes the interaction pattern, not the API\'s availability.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"5","question":"How can you reduce the risk of downtime during Google Cloud SQL automatic updates?","options":{"A":"Use a single-instance Cloud SQL setup with automatic updates disabled.","B":"Create read replicas and promote one to primary when needed.","C":"Manually apply updates outside business hours.","D":"Use Memorystore instead of Cloud SQL."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. By controlling WHEN maintenance happens: set a maintenance window outside business hours and, if you want, apply the pending update yourself (self-service maintenance) at a time you choose.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Maintenance window: preferred day and time for Google to apply maintenance.\\n• Deny maintenance period: blocks maintenance on critical dates (e.g. Black Friday).\\n• Self-service maintenance: you apply the pending version before the scheduled date.\\n• Cloud SQL Enterprise Plus cuts planned-maintenance downtime to about < 1 s.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"automatic updates\\", \\"avoid business hours\\".\\nDo not confuse with: turning updates off (not possible); promoting read replicas (promotion is for DR and is irreversible); Memorystore (it is a cache, not a database replacement).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"6","question":"Your company stores critical customer data in Cloud Storage. What is the most reliable way to protect against accidental deletion?","options":{"A":"Use Object Versioning and Retention Policies.","B":"Copy data to a different Cloud Storage bucket daily.","C":"Store data in a single-region bucket with automatic backups.","D":"Enable Cloud IAM to restrict deletion."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Enable Object Versioning together with Retention Policies (and take advantage of soft delete).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Object Versioning: on delete or overwrite, the previous version stays as noncurrent and can be restored.\\n• Retention Policy: blocks delete/overwrite before a minimum period; with Bucket Lock it becomes immutable (WORM).\\n• Soft delete (on by default, 7 days): lets you recover deleted objects during that window.\\n• Lifecycle rules limit how many old versions you keep so costs stay under control.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"accidental deletion\\", \\"critical data\\" → versioning + retention.\\nDo not confuse with: copying to another bucket daily (you lose same-day changes and can replicate a deletion); IAM limits who can delete, but does not protect against a mistake by someone who is authorized.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"7","question":"A global e-commerce application is deployed in multiple GCP regions. How can ou ensure users are routed to the nearest healthy backend?","options":{"A":"Use Google Cloud Armor.","B":"Use an Internal Load Balancer.","C":"Use a Global HTTP(S) Load Balancer with geo- based routing.","D":"Deploy Cloud Functions instead of virtual machines."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. With a Global External Application Load Balancer (formerly \\"Global HTTP(S) Load Balancer\\").\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A single global anycast IP; traffic enters Google\'s network at the point of presence closest to the user.\\n• It sends the request to the nearest backend that has capacity and passes health checks.\\n• If a region fails or saturates, traffic automatically overflows to the next region.\\n• It integrates with Cloud CDN and Cloud Armor.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"multi-region\\", \\"nearest user\\", \\"healthy backend\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Armor (WAF/DDoS, does not route); Internal Load Balancer (VPC-internal traffic only); Cloud Functions (does not solve global routing).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"8","question":"You are designing a disaster recovery plan for a Compute Engine-based application. hich approach provides the highest reliability?","options":{"A":"Schedule daily snapshots and restore manually when needed.","B":"Deploy Compute Engine instances in multiple regions and use a Global Load Balancer.","C":"Use a single large instance with high CPU and memory allocation.","D":"Rely on Google Cloud\'s default reliability measures."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Deploy instances (managed instance groups) in multiple regions behind a Global Load Balancer.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Each region has its MIG with health checks and autohealing.\\n• The Global LB sends traffic only to healthy backends; if a region fails, it redirects automatically to another.\\n• This is active-active (or hot standby): RTO and RPO near zero for the compute layer.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"highest reliability\\", \\"regional failure\\", \\"DR\\" → multi-region + Global LB.\\nDo not confuse with: daily snapshots and manual restore (hours of RTO, RPO up to 24 h); one large instance (single point of failure); relying on Google\'s default measures (live migration does not protect against zone/region failures).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"10","question":"A company is migrating a legacy monolithic application to Google Cloud. They want to optimize for scalability and performance. What is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Lift-and-shift the application to Compute Engine","B":"Deploy the monolithic application on Cloud Run","C":"Refactor the application into microservices and use Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)","D":"Migrate the application to App Engine flexible environment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Refactoring the application into microservices and deploying on GKE improves scalability, performance, and maintainability, which aligns with Google Cloud best practices."},{"id":"11","question":"Your company is experiencing high operational costs due to unused resources in GCP. What is the best way to optimize costs?","options":{"A":"Move all workloads to on-premises","B":"Use Committed Use Discounts (CUDs) and enable autoscaling for Compute Engine","C":"Manually scale down VMs during non-peak hours","D":"Disable billing alerts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Committed Use Discounts reduce costs for predictable workloads, while autoscaling ensures that only necessary resources are used during peak demand."},{"id":"12","question":"Acompany wants to improve business agility by adopting a hybrid cloud strategy. hat is the best Google Cloud service to facilitate this?","options":{"A":"Cloud VPN","B":"Anthos","C":"Cloud Functions","D":"Cloud Spanner"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anthos provides a consistent platform for managing hybrid and multi-cloud environments, enabling businesses to modernize applications efficiently."},{"id":"13","question":"You need to ensure high availability for a mission-critical application deployed on GKE. What is the best practice?","options":{"A":"Deploy all workloads in a single availability zone","B":"Use multi-regional clusters with autoscaling and pod disruption budgets","C":"Use only a single-node cluster to reduce costs","D":"Run the workload on a preemptible instance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Multi-regional clusters improve resilience, while autoscaling and PodDisruptionBudgets ensure workloads can handle failures without downtime."},{"id":"14","question":"A retail company wants to analyze customer purchasing behavior. Which GCP service should they use?","options":{"A":"Cloud Bigtable","B":"Cloud Spanner","C":"BigQuery","D":"Cloud SQL"},"correct":"C","explanation":"BigQuery is optimized for analytical workloads and can quickly process large datasets, making it ideal for customer behavior analysis."},{"id":"15","question":"Acompany wants to automate infrastructure deployment in Google Cloud. What tool should they use?","options":{"A":"Terraform","B":"Cloud Shell","C":"gcloud commands","D":"Cloud IAM"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Terraform allows infrastructure as code (IaC), enabling automation and version-controlled deployment of cloud resources."},{"id":"16","question":"You need to secure API access to a Cloud Function. What is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Use API Gateway with IAM authentication","B":"Expose the function publicly","C":"Use Cloud Pub/Sub to secure API requests","D":"Use a VPN for API access"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Put API Gateway in front and leave the function private, invocable only by the gateway\'s service account (IAM roles/run.invoker).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The function does not allow unauthenticated invocations (no allUsers).\\n• API Gateway validates clients (API keys, Firebase/Auth0/Google ID-token JWTs).\\n• The gateway calls the backend with its service account, authorized by IAM.\\n• It adds quotas, rate limiting, and an OpenAPI contract.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"secure API access\\" for a serverless function.\\nDo not confuse with: exposing it publicly; Pub/Sub (messaging, not API authentication); VPN (network connectivity, not per-client access control).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"17","question":"Which Google Cloud security service provides centralized visibility into security findings and compliance violations?","options":{"A":"Cloud Armor","B":"Security Command Center","C":"Identity- Aware Proxy (IAP)","D":"Cloud Identity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Security Command Center provides centralized security visibility, detecting threats and compliance violations in Google Cloud."},{"id":"18","question":"You want to enforce the principle of least privilege for IAM roles. Which IAM feature should you use?","options":{"A":"Custom Roles","B":"Predefined Roles","C":"Basic Roles","D":"Service Accounts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Custom Roles: roles that contain exactly the permissions you need.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You create them at organization or project level with a concrete permission list.\\n• You maintain them: if Google adds new permissions, they are not added automatically.\\n• Recommended order: predefined if they fit; custom if predefined is too broad; never basic roles in production.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"only the necessary permissions\\", \\"least privilege\\" → custom role.\\nDo not confuse with: Basic roles (Owner/Editor/Viewer, far too broad); predefined roles (granular but can include extra permissions); service accounts (identities, not roles).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"21","question":"How can you restrict external internet access for your Compute Engine instances while still allowing software updates?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud NAT","B":"Remove external IP addresses and enable Private Google Access","C":"Set up an IAP tunnel","D":"Use a firewall rule to block all outbound traffic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Remove external IPs and use Cloud NAT: the VMs go out to the internet (outbound traffic they initiate only) and nobody can start inbound connections.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Cloud NAT translates private IPs to managed public IPs for egress only.\\n• Private Google Access lets you reach Google APIs (Cloud Storage, Artifact Registry) without a public IP, but NOT external internet repositories.\\n• The usual pattern: VMs with no external IP + Cloud NAT + Private Google Access.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"no public IP\\" + \\"updates / download from the internet\\" → Cloud NAT. \\"Google APIs only\\" → Private Google Access.\\nDo not confuse with: IAP TCP forwarding (inbound admin SSH/RDP); blocking all egress (that blocks updates).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"22","question":"Which Google Cloud service helps detect and protect against DDoS attacks?","options":{"A":"Cloud Armor","B":"Cloud IDS","C":"Security Command Center","D":"VPC Firewall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Google Cloud Armor.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Applied as a security policy on the backends of an external Application Load Balancer (and other external LBs).\\n• DDoS protection at L3/L4 and L7; Adaptive Protection detects L7 attacks with ML and suggests rules.\\n• WAF with preconfigured OWASP Top 10 rules (SQLi, XSS), IP/geo lists, and rate limiting.\\n• Cloud Armor Enterprise adds DDoS response support and advanced protection.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"DDoS\\", \\"WAF\\", \\"block IPs/countries\\", \\"OWASP\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud IDS (intrusion detection on internal traffic, does not mitigate DDoS); Security Command Center (posture and findings, does not block traffic); VPC firewall (filters by IP/port, not L7 DDoS).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"24","question":"How can you ensure a Compute Engine VM uses a company-approved OS image?","options":{"A":"Use OS Inventory Management","B":"Implement Binary Authorization","C":"Enforce an Organization Policy on VM image constraints","D":"Enable Shielded VMs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. With an Organization Policy that restricts images: the constraint constraints/compute.trustedImageProjects.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You define which projects are allowed image sources (e.g. your \\"golden images\\" project).\\n• It is applied at organization/folder/project and is inherited.\\n• Any attempt to create a disk/VM from another image fails.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"approved image\\", \\"enforce across the organization\\" → Organization Policy.\\nDo not confuse with: OS Inventory Management (VM Manager, only reports what is installed); Binary Authorization (container images on GKE/Cloud Run, not VMs); Shielded VMs (verified boot, does not control which image is used).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"25","question":"You need to provision a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster with high availability across multiple zones. Which type of cluster should you create?","options":{"A":"Zonal cluster","B":"Regional cluster","C":"Single-node cluster","D":"Private cluster"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A regional cluster in GKE distributes nodes across multiple zones in a region, ensuring higher availability and resilience to zone failures."},{"id":"27","question":"You need to deploy an application that requires Redis for caching. What is the most managed way to deploy Redis on GCP?","options":{"A":"Deploy Redis on a Compute Engine VM","B":"Use Memorystore for Redis","C":"Deploy Redis on a Kubernetes cluster","D":"Use Cloud Bigtable instead"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Memorystore is a fully managed in-memory data store service that supports Redis, ensuring scalability, availability, and security."},{"id":"28","question":"You need to provision a high-performance shared storage system for a GKE cluster. hich storage solution should you choose?","options":{"A":"Cloud SOL","B":"Cloud Spanner","C":"Filestore","D":"Local SSD"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Filestore: fully managed NFS file storage.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Mounted as an NFS volume; supports ReadWriteMany access from many pods/nodes.\\n• On GKE you use the Filestore CSI driver (dynamic PersistentVolume).\\n• Tiers (Basic, Zonal, Regional, Enterprise) by performance and availability.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"shared storage\\", \\"NFS\\", \\"ReadWriteMany\\", \\"file system\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud SQL and Spanner (databases, not file systems); Local SSD (fast but local to one node and ephemeral); Persistent Disk (ReadWriteOnce for writes).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"29","question":"What is the recommended way to automate infrastructure deployment in GCP?","options":{"A":"Use Terraform","B":"Use the gcloud CLI","C":"Manually create resources in the console","D":"Use Kubernetes manifests"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Terraform is an Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool that allows automated, repeatable, and version-controlled deployments."},{"id":"30","question":"Which load balancing solution in GCP should ou use to distribute HTTP/HTTPS traffic across multiple backend services?","options":{"A":"Internal TCP/UDP Load Balancer","B":"Global External HTTP(S) Load Balancer","C":"TCP Proxy Load Balancer","D":"Network Load Balancer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. The Global External Application Load Balancer (formerly \\"Global External HTTP(S) Load Balancer\\").\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• L7 proxy: terminates TLS (Google-managed certificates) and inspects the HTTP request.\\n• URL maps: route by host or path to different backend services (MIGs, NEGs, Cloud Run, buckets).\\n• Global anycast IP, Cloud CDN and Cloud Armor integrated.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"HTTP/HTTPS\\", \\"several backend services\\", \\"Internet\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Internal TCP/UDP LB (internal L4 traffic); TCP Proxy LB (L4 TCP with no URL routing); Network LB (passthrough L4, regional).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"31","question":"Which Google Cloud storage service supports POSIX-compliant file storage for high- performance computing workloads?","options":{"A":"Cloud Storage","B":"Cloud Spanner","C":"Filestore","D":"BigQuery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Filestore (managed NFS). For extreme HPC there is also Google Cloud Managed Lustre / Parallelstore.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• An NFS file system mountable by many VMs at once, with POSIX semantics (permissions, locks, paths).\\n• High-performance tiers (Zonal/Regional) with large throughput and IOPS.\\n• Managed Lustre and Parallelstore: parallel file systems for large-scale HPC/AI.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"POSIX\\", \\"shared file system\\", \\"HPC\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Storage (object storage, not full POSIX); Spanner (database); BigQuery (data warehouse).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"32","question":"You need to deploy an application in multiple regions while maintaining a single database instance. What is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud SQL with read replicas","B":"Use Firestore in Datastore mode","C":"Use Cloud Spanner","D":"Use Compute Engine with persistent disks"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Spanner (Cloud Spanner), a globally distributed relational database.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Multi-region configurations: read/write replicas in several regions with external (strong) consistency.\\n• Automatic horizontal scale by splitting data into splits.\\n• Up to 99.999% SLA in multi-region; SQL and ACID transactions.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"several regions\\", \\"a single database\\", \\"strong consistency\\", \\"global\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud SQL with read replicas (one primary for writes; async read-only replicas); Firestore in Datastore mode (document NoSQL); persistent disks (zonal/regional, not global).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"33","question":"Your company needs to migrate a large on- premises application to Google Cloud. The application consists of a database and a stateless web tier. Which migration strategy should you use to minimize downtime?","options":{"A":"Lift and shift the entire application at once","B":"Migrate the database first, then migrate the web tier","C":"Migrate the web tier first, then migrate the database","D":"Rewrite the application using cloud-native technologies before migration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Migrating the database first ensures that data consistency is maintained and avoids data loss during migration. The stateless web tier can then be migrated without affecting database integrity."},{"id":"34","question":"Your client wants to deploy a multi-region application in Google Cloud with high availability. Which load balancing option should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Internal HTTP(S) Load Balancer","B":"Cloud DNS with round-robin records","C":"Global HTTP(S) Load Balancer","D":"TCP Proxy Load Balancer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Global HTTP(S) Load Balancer is designed for multi-region deployments and directs traffic to the nearest healthy backend."},{"id":"35","question":"Your company requires a hybrid cloud architecture with minimal latency between on- premises data centers and Google Cloud. Which networking solution should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Cloud VPN","B":"Cloud Interconnect","C":"NAT Gateway","D":"Shared VPC"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Interconnect provides a dedicated private connection between on-premises and Google Cloud, reducing latency compared to VPN."},{"id":"36","question":"You need to design a data warehouse solution in Google Cloud. The data should be easily queried and support analytics. Which service should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud SQL","B":"BigQuery","C":"Cloud Spanner","D":"Cloud Datastore"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. BigQuery.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Serverless data warehouse; separates storage and compute.\\n• Standard SQL (GoogleSQL); partitioning and clustering to cut cost and latency.\\n• Integrates with Looker, Dataflow, BigQuery ML, and external tables (BigLake) on Cloud Storage.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"data warehouse\\", \\"analytics\\", \\"ad-hoc queries\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud SQL and Spanner (transactional OLTP); Datastore/Firestore (NoSQL for applications).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"37","question":"Your application requires storing sensitive customer data while ensuring compliance with regulations like GDPR. Which storage solution should you choose?","options":{"A":"Cloud Storage with client-side encryption","B":"Cloud Bigtable with default encryption","C":"Cloud SQL with automatic backups","D":"Cloud Storage with CMEK (Customer- Managed Encryption Keys)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Cloud Storage with Customer-Managed Encryption Keys (CMEK) in Cloud KMS.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Data is encrypted with keys you create and control in Cloud KMS (rotation, permissions, disable, destroy).\\n• Disabling or destroying the key makes the data unreadable (crypto-shredding).\\n• Key use is auditable in Cloud Audit Logs.\\n• Complement it by choosing the bucket location (data residency).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"compliance\\", \\"control the keys\\", \\"sensitive data\\".\\nDo not confuse with: default encryption (Google manages the key, no customer control); client-side encryption (valid, but more operational load); automatic backups (they are not encryption control).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"38","question":"Acompany wants to modernize a legacy monolithic application by breaking it into microservices. Which Google Cloud product should you use for inter-service communication with security and observability?","options":{"A":"Cloud Run","B":"Cloud Functions","C":"Apigee","D":"Anthos Service Mesh"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anthos Service Mesh provides service-to- service security, monitoring, and observability, ideal for microservices architectures."},{"id":"39","question":"Your company wants to use machine learning models for real-time predictions. hich service should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Cloud Functions","B":"AI Platform Prediction","C":"Cloud Spanner","D":"Cloud Pub/Sub"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Vertex AI online prediction (deploy the model to a Vertex AI Endpoint). It is the successor of AI Platform Prediction.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Upload the model to the Vertex AI Model Registry and deploy it to an endpoint with autoscaling.\\n• The endpoint answers REST/gRPC requests with low latency.\\n• Supports traffic split across versions and Model Monitoring (drift/skew).\\n• For large, non-urgent jobs: batch prediction.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"real-time predictions\\", \\"serve models\\" → Vertex AI endpoint.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Run functions (could host a tiny model, but not the managed serving platform); Spanner (database); Pub/Sub (messaging).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"40","question":"A web application experiences a sudden traffic spike. How can you ensure the backend scales automatically?","options":{"A":"Use Managed Instance Groups with autoscaling","B":"Deploy on a single Compute Engine instance","C":"Use a Static instance group","D":"Manually scale the instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. With Managed Instance Groups (MIGs) and autoscaling.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The MIG creates identical VMs from an instance template.\\n• The autoscaler adds/removes VMs based on CPU, load-balancer serving capacity, Cloud Monitoring metrics, or schedules.\\n• Autohealing with health checks recreates failed VMs.\\n• Regional MIGs spread VMs across zones.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"traffic spikes\\", \\"automatic scaling\\" on VMs → MIG + autoscaler.\\nDo not confuse with: a single instance (does not scale); unmanaged/static instance group (no autoscaling or autohealing); manual scaling (slow).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"42","question":"A microservices application running on GKE is experiencing cascading failures. What can you do to improve reliability?","options":{"A":"Increase the replica count of all services.","B":"Use circuit breakers and retries in service communication.","C":"Deploy a global load balancer.","D":"Use a single large node pool with more resources."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Apply circuit breakers and retries (with backoff and limits) in service-to-service communication.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Circuit breaker: after X errors or timeouts, stop calling the damaged service for a while and fail fast (or return a fallback).\\n• Retries with exponential backoff and jitter, capped, for transient errors.\\n• Short timeouts so threads/connections do not pile up waiting.\\n• Configure this in code or in the mesh (Cloud Service Mesh / Istio: outlier detection, connection pools).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"cascading failures\\" → circuit breaker.\\nDo not confuse with: more replicas (do not stop saturation from spreading); global LB (spreads external traffic, does not isolate internal dependencies); a large node pool (does not isolate failures).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"43","question":"You need to ensure high availability for an application running on Compute Engine instances behind an HTTP(S) Load Balancer. hat should you configure?","options":{"A":"A health check for the backend service","B":"A static external IP for each instance","C":"A managed instance group in a single zone","D":"A firewall rule allowing all inbound traffic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. A health check on the backend service.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The load balancer periodically probes each backend (HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, gRPC).\\n• Instances that fail stop receiving traffic until they pass again.\\n• You must allow Google\'s health-check ranges in the firewall (35.191.0.0/16 and 130.211.0.0/22).\\n• With a MIG, a separate health check can trigger autohealing (recreate the VM).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"traffic only to healthy instances\\" → health check.\\nDo not confuse with: a static external IP per VM (unnecessary behind the LB); a MIG in a single zone (no zonal HA); a firewall that allows everything (insecure).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"44","question":"You are designing a CI/CD pipeline for a Kubernetes-based application on GKE. How can you minimize deployment failures?","options":{"A":"Deploy changes directly to production without testing","B":"Use blue/green or canary deployments with GKE","C":"Always deploy using a single-node cluster","D":"Rely on manual approvals before every deployment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. With progressive deployment strategies: blue/green or canary.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Blue/green: deploy the new version (green) in parallel; shift all traffic once it is validated; instant rollback by returning to blue.\\n• Canary: send a small % of traffic to the new version, watch metrics, then increase.\\n• Tools: Cloud Deploy (native canary on GKE/Cloud Run), Gateway API or a service mesh to split traffic.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"minimize deployment failures\\", \\"controlled risk\\", \\"fast rollback\\".\\nDo not confuse with: deploying without testing; single-node clusters; manual approvals on every deploy (they slow you down and do not reduce the technical blast radius of a failure).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"45","question":"How can you ensure that a Cloud Spanner database remains highly available in case of a regional failure?","options":{"A":"Deploy the database in a multi-region configuration","B":"Take daily backups and store them in Cloud Storage","C":"Use a single-instance setup with high CPU allocation","D":"Use Memorystore as a cache layer"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Deploy it with a multi-region instance configuration (e.g. nam-eur-asia1, nam6).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Read/write replicas in two regions plus a witness replica in a third for Paxos quorum.\\n• If one region fails, Spanner keeps serving with automatic failover and no loss of committed data.\\n• 99.999% SLA (versus 99.99% for regional).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"regional failure\\" on Spanner → multi-region configuration.\\nDo not confuse with: daily backups (they help recover, but imply downtime and loss of recent data); a single instance with more CPU (no geographic redundancy); Memorystore as a cache (does not keep the DB available).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"46","question":"Your application uses Pub/Sub for event-driven messaging. How can you ensure that messages are not lost if subscribers go down?","options":{"A":"Use a single-zone topic and subscriber model","B":"Enable message retention in Pub/Sub","C":"Set a short message retention period","D":"Process messages synchronously in subscribers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Pub/Sub retains unacked messages until the subscriber processes them and acks, for the configured retention period.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Subscription retention: default 7 days (adjustable from 10 min to 7 days) for unacked messages.\\n• Topic message retention: up to 31 days; lets you seek/replay even already-acked messages.\\n• Dead-letter topics for messages that fail repeatedly.\\n• The subscriber should ack only after successful processing.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"do not lose messages if the subscriber fails\\" → retention + ack after processing.\\nDo not confuse with: short retention (raises loss risk); synchronous processing (unrelated to durability); Pub/Sub topics are global, not single-zone.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"47","question":"You want to reduce downtime for a Cloud SQL instance during maintenance events. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Enable automatic failover with a high availability (HA) configuration","B":"Manually restart the instance every night","C":"Use a single Cloud SQL instance with automated backups","D":"Store database files in Cloud Storage"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. High availability (HA, regional) with automatic failover.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Primary instance + standby in another zone; the disk is replicated synchronously.\\n• If the zone or primary fails, automatic failover in ~1 minute keeps the same IP.\\n• On Cloud SQL Enterprise Plus, planned maintenance causes < 1 s of interruption.\\n• Combine it with maintenance windows.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"minimize downtime\\" on Cloud SQL → HA (regional).\\nDo not confuse with: nightly restarts (cause downtime); a single instance with backups (recovers data but does not avoid the outage); files in Cloud Storage (not a Cloud SQL deployment mode).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"48","question":"A company is seeing poor database performance in Cloud SQL. What should they do first?","options":{"A":"Scale up the database instance","B":"Enable query caching and analyze slow queries","C":"Restart the database","D":"Increase the disk size"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Query optimization, caching, and indexing should be analyzed before scaling, as they often resolve performance issues at a lower cost."},{"id":"49","question":"Acompany is running an application on Compute Engine with inconsistent performance. What is the most cost-effective way to improve performance?","options":{"A":"Move to an always-on, high-performance VM","B":"Use preemptible instances","C":"Implement autoscaling with Load Balancer","D":"Manually adjust VM sizes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Implement autoscaling (Managed Instance Group) behind a load balancer.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The LB spreads load across several VMs and avoids saturating one.\\n• The autoscaler adds VMs on peaks and removes them in valleys → you pay only for needed capacity.\\n• Signals: CPU, LB utilization (RPS), or custom metrics.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"inconsistent performance\\" + \\"cost-effective\\" → scale out on demand.\\nDo not confuse with: a large VM always on (you pay the peak 24/7); Spot/preemptible VMs (cheap but can be interrupted, worse consistency); sizing by hand (slow and error-prone).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"50","question":"Your company wants to improve security by limiting employee access to Google Cloud services based on their roles. Which service should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud IAM (Identity and Access Management)","B":"Cloud VPN","C":"Cloud Armor","D":"Cloud SOL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud IAM allows fine-grained access control based on roles, ensuring employees only have permissions necessary for their tasks."},{"id":"51","question":"A company has multiple teams working on different Google Cloud projects. They want centralized cost tracking and billing alerts. What should they implement?","options":{"A":"Use separate billing accounts for each project","B":"Use a single billing account with labels and budgets","C":"Disable billing alerts","D":"Use a third-party cost management tool"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. One billing account linked to all projects gives one invoice and central reporting. Labels (team, env) attribute cost, and budgets with alerts per project or label notify each team.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) separate billing accounts fragment visibility. C) disabling alerts removes control. D) third-party tools are not needed for this native capability.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"52","question":"A company wants to modernize its batch processing workload, which currently runs ona self-managed Hadoop cluster. What is the best alternative in Google Cloud?","options":{"A":"Migrate to Compute Engine and manually install Hadoop","B":"Use Cloud Dataproc for managed Hadoop and Spark","C":"Replace Hadoop with Cloud Functions","D":"Use Cloud Spanner for batch processing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Dataproc provides a managed Hadoop and Spark environment, reducing operational overhead while maintaining compatibility."},{"id":"53","question":"You are designing a CI/CD pipeline for a microservices architecture on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). Which tools should ou use?","options":{"A":"Jenkins, Cloud Build, and GKE","B":"Cloud Build, Cloud Functions, and Cloud SOL","C":"Cloud Run and Cloud Pub/Sub","D":"Cloud Storage and Compute Engine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Jenkins is a widely used CI/CD tool, while Cloud Build provides integration with Google Cloud, and GKE is the deployment target."},{"id":"54","question":"A company wants to ensure disaster recovery for their Cloud SQL instance. What should they configure?","options":{"A":"Enable read replicas and use Cloud Storage backups","B":"Rely on Cloud CDN","C":"Use a single-zone deployment","D":"Manually copy data to a Compute Engine instance Bag"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Read replicas in another region (cross-region) that can be promoted, plus automated backups and exports.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Cross-region read replica: asynchronous copy in another region; in a regional disaster you promote it to primary.\\n• Automated backups and point-in-time recovery (binlogs/WAL) to recover from corruption or deletion.\\n• Exports to Cloud Storage for long retention.\\n• Regional HA protects against zone failure, not region failure.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"disaster recovery\\" on Cloud SQL → cross-region replica + backups.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud CDN (web content cache); single-zone deployment; manual copies to a VM.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"55","question":"What is the recommended way to securely access a private Cloud SQL instance?","options":{"A":"Assign a public IP and use SSL/TLS","B":"Use Cloud VPN","C":"Use a private IP with IAM authentication","D":"Configure firewall rules to allow external access"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Use a private IP (no public IP) and IAM database authentication, usually through the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy or the connectors.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Private IP: the instance is reachable only from your VPC (Private Service Access or Private Service Connect).\\n• IAM database authentication: users/service accounts sign in with IAM tokens instead of passwords.\\n• Cloud SQL Auth Proxy / Language Connectors: automatic TLS encryption and IAM authorization (roles/cloudsql.client).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"private\\", \\"secure\\", \\"do not expose\\".\\nDo not confuse with: public IP with SSL (exposed to the Internet); Cloud VPN (only useful from on-prem); opening the firewall to external access.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"56","question":"Which Google Cloud feature provides end- to-end encryption for Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)?","options":{"A":"Cloud KMS","B":"GKE Shielded Nodes","C":"Node-to-Node TLS","D":"Binary Authorization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Enable GKE Dataplane V2 inter-node transparent encryption, or use mTLS between services with Cloud Service Mesh.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Inter-node transparent encryption: encrypts pod-to-pod traffic between nodes with WireGuard (requires Dataplane V2).\\n• Cloud Service Mesh: end-to-end mTLS between services with identity.\\n• In addition, Google encrypts traffic between its data centers at the physical layer.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"encryption in transit between nodes/pods\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud KMS (keys for encryption at rest, e.g. application-layer secrets encryption); Shielded Nodes (node boot integrity); Binary Authorization (trusted images only).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"57","question":"You want to ensure that logs stored in Cloud Logging cannot be deleted for a specific retention period. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Use Log Exclusions","B":"Apply a Retention Policy","C":"Enable Bucket Lock","D":"Use a Custom Role"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Configure the log bucket\'s retention and lock it (bucket lock).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Logs are stored in log buckets (_Default, _Required, or custom) with configurable retention (1 day to 10 years).\\n• Locking the bucket means nobody can reduce retention or delete the bucket or the logs early.\\n• For long-term WORM archive: sink to Cloud Storage with Retention Policy + Bucket Lock.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"cannot be deleted\\", \\"compliance\\", \\"immutable\\".\\nDo not confuse with: log exclusions (stop ingesting logs); retention policy without lock (can still be changed); a custom role (controls who, but an admin could still change it).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"58","question":"What is the primary purpose of IAM Conditions in Google Cloud?","options":{"A":"To allow access only under specific circumstances","B":"To restrict network traffic","C":"To enforce encryption policies","D":"To manage service accounts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. To grant a role only under certain circumstances (conditional access) using CEL expressions on the binding.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Date/time attributes: temporary access (expires Friday) or only during business hours.\\n• Resource attributes: name, type, or tags (e.g. only buckets with prefix \\"dev-\\").\\n• Request attributes: access level (Access Context Manager), IP/device on some services.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"temporary access\\", \\"only if…\\", \\"based on attributes\\".\\nDo not confuse with: restricting network traffic (firewall); encryption policies (Organization Policy / KMS); managing service accounts.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"59","question":"Which method provides centralized identity management across Google Cloud and on- premises environments?","options":{"A":"Cloud IAM","B":"Cloud Identity","C":"Google Groups","D":"Cloud VPN"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud Identity (Google\'s IDaaS), synchronized or federated with your on-prem directory.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Manages users, groups, MFA, and devices for Google Cloud.\\n• Google Cloud Directory Sync (GCDS) syncs users/groups from Active Directory/LDAP.\\n• SSO with SAML or OIDC: the corporate IdP (AD FS, Entra ID, Okta) authenticates.\\n• Workforce Identity Federation: access to Google Cloud without syncing accounts.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"centralized identity\\", \\"corporate users\\", \\"SSO with AD\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud IAM (authorization: what each identity can do, not identity management); Google Groups (only groups); Cloud VPN (network).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"60","question":"You need to scan your Google Cloud environment for misconfigurations and compliance violations. What tool should you use?","options":{"A":"Security Command Center","B":"Cloud Asset Inventory","C":"Forseti Security","D":"Cloud Security Scanner"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Security Command Center (Security Health Analytics).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Automatically detects insecure configs: public buckets, 0.0.0.0/0 SSH firewall, old service-account keys, logging disabled…\\n• Maps findings to standards (CIS, PCI DSS, NIST) in the compliance panel.\\n• Offers remediation recommendations.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"misconfigurations\\", \\"compliance\\", \\"scan the environment\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Asset Inventory (resource inventory and history, does not score risk); Forseti Security (archived open-source project); Web Security Scanner, formerly Cloud Security Scanner (scans web apps for XSS, etc., not GCP configuration).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"63","question":"Your application runs on Compute Engine and requires automatic failover to another region in case of a regional outage. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Enable live migration","B":"Use managed instance groups with cross-region autoscaling","C":"Configure instance templates with local SSDs","D":"Set up a single VM with an internal load balancer Ba"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Deploy regional managed instance groups in at least two regions behind a Global External Load Balancer.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Each MIG scales and self-heals in its region.\\n• The global LB, with health checks, stops sending traffic to the failed region and overflows to the other.\\n• The surviving region\'s autoscaler absorbs the extra load.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"automatic failover between regions\\" → multi-region MIGs + global LB.\\nDo not confuse with: live migration (moves VMs between hosts in the same zone during maintenance, not between regions); Local SSD (ephemeral, not HA); a single VM with an internal LB (single point of failure and regional LB).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"65","question":"What GCP service enables automated configuration and policy enforcement for cloud resources?","options":{"A":"Cloud Functions","B":"Config Connector","C":"Cloud Scheduler","D":"Cloud Composer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Config Connector: a Kubernetes add-on that represents GCP resources (buckets, Cloud SQL, Pub/Sub, IAM…) as custom resources.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You apply YAML (kubectl apply) and the controller creates/updates the resource in GCP.\\n• Continuous reconciliation: if someone changes the resource by hand, it returns to the declared state.\\n• Combined with Config Sync (GitOps) and Policy Controller (OPA Gatekeeper policies) you get automated configuration and policies.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"Kubernetes-native\\", \\"declarative\\", \\"manage GCP resources from the cluster\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Run functions (code on events); Cloud Scheduler (cron); Cloud Composer (Airflow to orchestrate data flows).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"66","question":"You need to secure an internal GKE service and restrict access only to specific internal clients. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Use an Internal Load Balancer with Identity- Aware Proxy","B":"Use a Global External Load Balancer","C":"Enable Cloud CDN","D":"Use Cloud Armor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Expose it with an Internal Application Load Balancer and protect access with Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The internal LB has only a private IP: the service is not reachable from the Internet.\\n• IAP verifies identity (user or service account) and applies IAM (IAP-secured Web App User role) before letting traffic through.\\n• On GKE you configure it with an internal Gateway/Ingress and BackendConfig/GCPBackendPolicy with IAP.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"internal\\" + \\"only specific clients\\" → ILB + IAP.\\nDo not confuse with: Global External LB (exposes to the Internet); Cloud CDN (cache); Cloud Armor (filters by IP/WAF rules, not by user identity).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"67","question":"What is the best approach to managing encryption keys in GCP?","options":{"A":"Use customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK)","B":"Store keys in a Compute Engine instance","C":"Use default Google-managed keys","D":"Store keys in Cloud Storage"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Use customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) in Cloud KMS.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You create key rings and keys in Cloud KMS (software, Cloud HSM, or external with Cloud EKM).\\n• You configure automatic rotation and control who uses the key with IAM (roles/cloudkms.cryptoKeyEncrypterDecrypter).\\n• Every use is in Cloud Audit Logs; disabling the key revokes access to the data.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"control of the keys\\", \\"rotation\\", \\"compliance\\".\\nDo not confuse with: storing keys on a VM or in Cloud Storage (insecure); Google default keys (valid and secure, but no customer control).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"69","question":"Which Google Cloud storage option is best for high-throughput, low-latency access to time-series data?","options":{"A":"Cloud SQL","B":"Cloud Bigtable","C":"Cloud Datastore","D":"Cloud Storage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Bigtable (Cloud Bigtable): a wide-column NoSQL database.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Single-digit millisecond latency and millions of operations per second; you scale by adding nodes.\\n• Row-key design is critical: e.g. device_id#reversed timestamp, avoiding sequential keys that create hotspots.\\n• Compatible with the HBase API; multi-cluster replication.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"time series\\", \\"IoT\\", \\"high throughput\\", \\"low latency\\", \\"petabytes\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud SQL (relational, vertical scale); Datastore/Firestore (app documents, lower throughput); Cloud Storage (objects, not low-latency key lookups).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"70","question":"You need to enforce IAM policies at the organization level in Google Cloud. What should you use?","options":{"A":"IAM Conditions","B":"Service Accounts","C":"Organization Policies","D":"Cloud KMS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Organization Policies (Organization Policy Service).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Applied on the organization node and inherited by folders and projects.\\n• IAM example: constraints/iam.allowedPolicyMemberDomains (Domain Restricted Sharing) blocks granting roles to accounts outside your domain.\\n• Others: iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation, gcp.resourceLocations.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"at organization level\\", \\"across all projects\\", \\"guardrails\\".\\nDo not confuse with: IAM Conditions (conditions on one specific binding); service accounts (identities); Cloud KMS (keys).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"71","question":"Your application requires a database that supports global consistency and automatic horizontal scaling. Which option should you choose?","options":{"A":"Cloud SQL","B":"Cloud Bigtable","C":"Cloud Spanner","D":"Firestore"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Spanner is the relational database with external (strong) consistency across regions and automatic horizontal scaling (split-based sharding), with up to 99.999% availability in multi-region configurations.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Cloud SQL scales vertically and is regional. B) Bigtable scales horizontally but is NoSQL wide-column without multi-row transactions or global strong consistency for multi-cluster writes. D) Firestore is a document database, not a horizontally scaled relational system.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"72","question":"How can you ensure that only traffic from an internal network reaches your Compute Engine instances?","options":{"A":"Use a NAT Gateway","B":"Configure firewall rules allowing only private IP ranges","C":"Enable public IPs and block all incoming traffic","D":"Use a global external load balancer —,"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. VPC firewall rules (or hierarchical firewall policies) that allow ingress only from internal ranges (RFC 1918 or your on-premises CIDRs) block everything else by default. Removing external IPs strengthens this further.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Cloud NAT is outbound only. C) a public IP that blocks all traffic also blocks your internal traffic. D) an external load balancer exposes the service publicly.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"73","question":"Your company requires a managed Kubernetes environment that integrates with on-premises infrastructure. Which product should you choose?","options":{"A":"Cloud Run","B":"Kubernetes Engine (GKE)","C":"Anthos","D":"Compute Engine"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Anthos (rebranded as GKE Enterprise, now folded into GKE) manages Kubernetes clusters both in Google Cloud and on premises (Google Distributed Cloud), with fleets, Config Sync and Policy Controller for consistent management.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Cloud Run is serverless in Google Cloud. B) GKE alone runs in Google Cloud. D) Compute Engine is IaaS.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"76","question":"How can you improve the reliability of a Cloud Functions-based application that processes API requests?","options":{"A":"Use a single-region deployment with maximum memory allocation","B":"Deploy Cloud Functions in multiple regions and use Cloud Load Balancing","C":"Reduce timeout limits to force faster responses a","D":"Replace Cloud Functions with Compute Engine instances"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Deploy the function in several regions and put it behind a Global External Application Load Balancer with serverless NEGs.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• One serverless NEG per region points at that region\'s function/service.\\n• The global LB sends the user to the nearest region and fails over if a region does not respond.\\n• Adds Cloud Armor, Cloud CDN, and a custom domain with a managed certificate.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"reliability\\" + serverless → multi-region + Cloud Load Balancing.\\nDo not confuse with: one region with more memory (still a regional single point of failure); shortening timeouts (causes more errors); moving to Compute Engine (more ops without reliability by itself).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"77","question":"What is the best way to monitor application reliability in Google Cloud?","options":{"A":"Only check system logs when users report issues","B":"Store logs in Cloud Storage and analyze them monthly","C":"Use Cloud Monitoring and Cloud Logging with custom alerts","D":"Manually review logs in Compute Engine instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Use Cloud Monitoring and Cloud Logging with custom alerts (ideally SLO-based).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Cloud Monitoring: infrastructure and application metrics, dashboards, uptime checks, and alerting policies (email, SMS, PagerDuty, Pub/Sub).\\n• Cloud Logging: centralized logs; log-based metrics to alert on errors.\\n• SLOs and error budgets in Cloud Monitoring to measure reliability from the user\'s point of view.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"monitor reliability\\", \\"proactive\\", \\"alerts\\".\\nDo not confuse with: reviewing logs only when users complain (reactive); analyzing logs monthly in Cloud Storage; reading logs by hand on the VMs.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"78","question":"Your application runs on App Engine. How can you minimize downtime when rolling out updates?","options":{"A":"Use traffic splitting for gradual rollout","B":"Deploy all updates at once without testing","C":"Manually restart all instances after every deployment","D":"Use a single-instance App Engine service"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Use traffic splitting: deploy the new version and give it only a percentage of traffic, increasing it when it is stable.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Each deploy creates a version; the previous one stays alive and can still receive traffic.\\n• You split traffic by cookie, IP, or at random (e.g. 5% → 25% → 100%).\\n• If errors appear, you send 100% back to the previous version instantly (rollback with no redeploy).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"App Engine\\" + \\"minimize downtime\\" / \\"gradual rollout\\" → traffic splitting.\\nDo not confuse with: deploying everything at once; restarting instances by hand; a single instance (single point of failure).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"79","question":"What is the best way to ensure uptime for a multi-region Cloud Storage bucket?","options":{"A":"Use a dual-region or multi-region bucket","B":"Manually copy files between regions","C":"Store data in a single-zone bucket","D":"Enable object lifecycle policies"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. A dual-region or multi-region bucket: Google replicates objects across regions and keeps serving if one region fails.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Multi-region (US, EU, ASIA): data across a wide geography; better availability for dispersed users.\\n• Dual-region: two specific regions you choose; with turbo replication the target RPO is 15 minutes.\\n• Durability (11 nines) is high for every type; what changes is availability if a region fails.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"uptime\\" / \\"multi-region\\" + Cloud Storage → dual- or multi-region bucket.\\nDo not confuse with: copying by hand across regions; a regional bucket (fails with the region); lifecycle (saves cost, does not give HA).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"80","question":"Your application running on Kubernetes frequently runs out of memory. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Configure resource requests and limits for containers","B":"Use only one large node instead of multiple small nodes","C":"Disable auto-scaling to avoid unnecessary complexity","D":"Run all workloads in a single pod"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Memory (and CPU) resource requests and limits so the scheduler reserves capacity and the kubelet kills or avoids pods that exceed it.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• request: what Kubernetes reserves when placing the pod (scheduling).\\n• limit: the cap; if the container exceeds memory, it is killed (OOMKilled).\\n• With no request, the node is overcommitted and pods get evicted under pressure.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"GKE\\" / \\"Kubernetes\\" + \\"out of memory\\" → requests and limits.\\nDo not confuse with: one huge node; turning autoscaling off; one pod for everything (you still get OOM and a SPOF).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"81","question":"A GKE-based application is experiencing frequent pod crashes due to unhandled exceptions. What should you do","options":{"A":"Implement retries and exception handling in the application code","B":"Increase the node pool size","C":"Deploy additional GKE clusters","D":"Reduce CPU limits for containers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Fix the code: catch exceptions and retry only transient errors. Kubernetes restarts the pod; it does not fix the bug.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• An uncaught exception kills the process → the container exits ≠ 0 → kubelet restarts it.\\n• If it fails at startup, it enters CrashLoopBackOff.\\n• More nodes or more clusters do not prevent an application crash.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"pod crashes\\" + \\"unhandled exceptions\\" → code (retries + handling), not infra.\\nDo not confuse with: enlarging the node pool; more clusters; lowering CPU limits (worse under contention).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"82","question":"What is the recommended way to automate disaster recovery for a Cloud SQL instance?","options":{"A":"Enable cross-region replication","B":"Take manual snapshots every week","C":"Store database dumps in Cloud Storage","D":"Increase CPU and memory allocation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Enable a cross-region replica (and, on Enterprise Plus, Advanced DR) that you can promote if the primary region fails.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The cross-region replica receives changes asynchronously in another region.\\n• In a disaster you promote it (or do replica failover to the DR replica on Enterprise Plus).\\n• Complement it with automated backups + PITR. Regional HA only covers a zone failure.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"DR\\" / \\"another region\\" + Cloud SQL → cross-region replication.\\nDo not confuse with: weekly manual snapshots; dumps to GCS (high RTO); raising CPU (not DR).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"83","question":"A financial institution needs to comply with data residency regulations requiring customer data to be stored within a specific country. What should they do?","options":{"A":"Use a multi-regional storage option","B":"Store data in a Cloud Storage bucket within the required region","C":"Move data to on-premises","D":"Use preemptible VMs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Regional Cloud Storage ensures data remains within a specific geographic location, complying with residency regulations."},{"id":"84","question":"A company wants to process video analytics using AI/ML with minimal infrastructure management. What service should they use?","options":{"A":"AI Platform (Vertex AI)","B":"Compute Engine","C":"Cloud Run","D":"BigQuery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AI Platform (Vertex AI) provides a managed AI/ML environment, reducing infrastructure management while enabling scalable analytics."},{"id":"85","question":"You need to enforce organization-wide security policies for Google Cloud resources. hat should you use?","options":{"A":"IAM Roles","B":"Organization Policies","C":"Cloud CDN","D":"Cloud VPN"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Organization Policies (Organization Policy Service): configuration guardrails that are inherited.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• They are set on the Organization (or folder) and flow down to projects and resources.\\n• They do not grant permissions: they restrict which configurations are legal (locations, SA keys, public IPs…).\\n• Even a project Owner cannot bypass an inherited constraint.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"organization-wide\\", \\"enforce policies\\", \\"all projects\\" → Organization Policies.\\nDo not confuse with: IAM Roles (who can act); Cloud CDN (cache); Cloud VPN (network).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"86","question":"A company wants to reduce storage costs for infrequently accessed data. What is the best option?","options":{"A":"Use Coldline storage in Cloud Storage","B":"Move all data to BigQuery","C":"Store data in Cloud SQL","D":"Keep all data in Standard storage"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Coldline (or Archive if it is < once/year). Coldline is designed for infrequent access (about quarterly).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Cheaper at rest than Standard/Nearline; you pay retrieval and operations.\\n• 90-day minimum duration (early delete is billed as if it stayed).\\n• Same API and millisecond access latency; it is not offline tape.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"infrequently accessed\\" + lower cost → Coldline (Archive if it is yearly archive).\\nDo not confuse with: moving it to BigQuery or Cloud SQL (another product and usually more expensive); leaving it in Standard.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"87","question":"A company is experiencing high latency in their globally distributed application. How can they improve performance?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud CDN","B":"Increase VM sizes","C":"Use a single region for all deployments","D":"Rely on preemptible instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud CDN: caches responses at Google\'s points of presence (edge), close to the user.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• It is enabled on an External Application Load Balancer.\\n• The first hit goes to the origin (GCS, GCE, GKE, Cloud Run…); later hits come from the edge.\\n• It reduces RTT and origin load. Ideal for statics and cacheable responses.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"globally distributed\\" + \\"latency\\" → Cloud CDN.\\nDo not confuse with: larger VMs (they do not shorten distance); a single region; Spot VMs (price, not latency).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"88","question":"A company wants to run serverless containers while reducing operational overhead. What is the best choice?","options":{"A":"Compute Engine","B":"Cloud Run","C":"GKE with manual scaling","D":"Cloud SQL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud Run: you upload an image and Google scales the instances (including to zero).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Any language/binary in a container that listens on PORT.\\n• Scales by requests (or instances); you pay CPU/RAM/time + requests.\\n• You do not manage nodes, clusters, or OS patches.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"serverless containers\\" / \\"reduce operations\\" → Cloud Run.\\nDo not confuse with: Compute Engine (VMs); GKE with manual scaling (you operate the cluster); Cloud SQL (database).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"89","question":"A company is planning a data lake on Google Cloud. What is the best storage service?","options":{"A":"Cloud Storage","B":"Cloud SOL","C":"Cloud Spanner","D":"Cloud Pub/Sub"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud Storage: cheap, practically unlimited, and open formats (Parquet, Avro, ORC, Iceberg).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The lake is a bucket (or several) organized in layers (raw / curated / consumer).\\n• BigQuery (BigLake / external tables), Dataproc, and Dataflow read gs:// without copying to HDFS.\\n• Do not use an OLTP database as a lake: it does not scale to objects/petabytes or flexible schemas.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"data lake\\" → Cloud Storage.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud SQL/Spanner (OLTP); Pub/Sub (ingest, not a store).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"90","question":"How does Cloud KMS help with compliance and security?","options":{"A":"It provides centralized key management and encryption","B":"It restricts IAM permissions","C":"It manages service accounts","D":"It provides IAM audit logs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. It centralizes the lifecycle of encryption keys (create, rotate, disable, audit) used by your services.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Key rings + keys (software, Cloud HSM, or external Cloud EKM).\\n• Services use CMEK (envelope encryption): KMS wraps the DEK; you control the KEK.\\n• Every use is recorded in Cloud Audit Logs; disabling the key leaves the data unreadable.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"keys\\", \\"encryption\\", \\"compliance\\" → Cloud KMS.\\nDo not confuse with: restricting IAM (IAM/Org Policy); managing service accounts; IAM audit logs (Cloud Audit Logs).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"91","question":"What security feature prevents unauthorized modification of Google Cloud services?","options":{"A":"IAM Roles","B":"Binary Authorization","C":"Organization Policies","D":"VPC Firewall"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Organization Policies: constraints that block configurations (APIs, locations, keys, IPs) even if the user has broad IAM.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The org admin defines allow/deny constraints; they are inherited.\\n• Examples: ban SA keys, limit IAM members to your domain, restrict regions.\\n• IAM decides who calls the API; Org Policy decides which request is legal.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"prevent unauthorized modification of services\\" at platform level → Organization Policies.\\nDo not confuse with: Binary Authorization (container images only); IAM Roles (who); VPC Firewall (packets).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"92","question":"What should you use to manage and rotate service account keys securely?","options":{"A":"Store keys in Cloud Storage","B":"Use Workload Identity Federation","C":"Manually rotate keys","D":"Enable SSH access"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Workload Identity Federation: external identities (CI, AWS, Azure, IdP) get short-lived tokens. There are no keys to rotate.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The external IdP issues an OIDC/SAML token.\\n• A workload identity pool + provider exchanges it for a Google federated token.\\n• You impersonate an SA (or use a federated principal) with no JSON key.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"rotate/manage SA keys securely\\" in 2025 → better to have no keys: WIF.\\nDo not confuse with: storing keys in Cloud Storage (leak); rotating by hand (keys still exist); SSH.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"93","question":"You want to restrict access to your Cloud Storage bucket so that only specific VPC networks can access it. What should you use?","options":{"A":"IAM Conditions","B":"VPC Service Controls","C":"Cloud Armor","D":"Cloud Audit Logs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"VPC Service Controls allow you to define security perimeters to prevent unauthorized access from outside a defined set of VPC networks."},{"id":"94","question":"How can you ensure that Cloud SQL databases are only accessible from authorized private IP addresses?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Armor to filter incoming SQL requests","B":"Configure Cloud SQL with a private IP and use VPC Firewall rules","C":"Assign a public IP and use SSL/TLS for encryption","D":"Configure IAM roles to restrict SQL queries"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud SQL instances should use private IPs with firewall rules to ensure access control and prevent unauthorized connections."},{"id":"95","question":"Which Google Cloud security feature helps detect and mitigate security misconfigurations?","options":{"A":"Cloud Armor","B":"Security Command Center","C":"Cloud Load Balancing","D":"VPC Flow Logs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Security Command Center (Security Health Analytics): scans the organization and generates findings.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Detects public buckets, SSH 0.0.0.0/0, old SA keys, logging disabled, missing CMEK, and more.\\n• Maps to CIS, PCI DSS, NIST, ISO on the compliance panel.\\n• Premium/Enterprise adds Event Threat Detection, Container Threat Detection, and correlation.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"detect misconfigurations\\" / \\"recommendations\\" → Security Command Center.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Armor (WAF); Cloud Load Balancing (traffic); VPC Flow Logs (VPC pcap, they do not evaluate posture).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"96","question":"Your organization must comply with PCI DSS for storing credit card data. Which Google Cloud storage service should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud Storage","B":"Firestore","C":"Cloud Spanner","D":"BigQuery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Storage is PCI DSS compliant and supports encryption at rest, making it suitable for storing sensitive financial data."},{"id":"97","question":"You need to ensure that only approved container images can be deployed to your GKE cluster. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud Armor","B":"Binary Authorization","C":"Cloud IAM","D":"Forseti Security"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Binary Authorization: admission control that rejects pods whose image lacks the required attestations.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The pipeline (Cloud Build) signs the image (attestor + note in Artifact Analysis).\\n• On the cluster, BinAuthz verifies signatures against the policy (e.g. require attestation in prod).\\n• Enforcement: enforced (blocks) or dry-run (log only). Breakglass for emergencies (stays audited).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"only approved container images\\" → Binary Authorization.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Armor; Cloud IAM (who calls the API, not the image contents); Forseti (archived).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"98","question":"Your organization wants to automate the provisioning of GCP resources using a Kubernetes- native approach. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud Deployment Manager","B":"Config Connector","C":"Terraform","D":"Cloud Composer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Config Connector (KCC): an add-on that exposes buckets, SQL, Pub/Sub, IAM… as CRDs.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• kubectl apply of a YAML (e.g. SQLInstance); the controller calls GCP APIs.\\n• Continuous reconciliation: if someone changes the resource by hand, it returns to the declared state.\\n• Fits Config Sync (GitOps). Terraform is not Kubernetes-native.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"Kubernetes-native\\" + GCP resources → Config Connector.\\nDo not confuse with: Terraform (multi-cloud IaC, not CRDs); Deployment Manager (classic GCP IaC); Cloud Composer (Airflow).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"99","question":"Your company is implementing a multi- cloud strategy. What GCP service can be used to deploy and manage Kubernetes clusters across multiple cloud providers?","options":{"A":"Kubernetes Engine (GKE)","B":"Anthos","C":"Cloud Run","D":"Compute Engine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anthos is a multi-cloud and hybrid-cloud platform that allows the management of Kubernetes clusters across GCP, AWS, and on-premises environments."},{"id":"100","question":"You are designing an autoscaling strategy for Compute Engine instances running a web application. Which metric should you scale on for handling traffic spikes?","options":{"A":"CPU utilization","B":"Disk usage","C":"Internal IP address count","D":"Number of firewall rules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. CPU utilization (or load-balancer utilization). CPU is the default signal and the most used for websites.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The autoscaler keeps the group\'s average CPU near the target (e.g. 60%).\\n• If there is an HTTP(S) LB, scaling on load balancing utilization (RPS/capacity) reacts better to real traffic.\\n• Also: Cloud Monitoring metrics, schedules, scale-in controls.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"traffic spikes\\" + Compute Engine → CPU (or LB utilization).\\nDo not confuse with: disk usage (does not follow traffic); count of internal IPs; number of firewall rules.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"101","question":"You need to deploy an application that requires high IOPS and low-latency storage. hich storage option should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud Storage Standard","B":"Persistent Disk (SSD)","C":"Filestore","D":"Cloud SQL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Persistent Disk SSD (pd-ssd) or, if you need more, Hyperdisk Extreme / PD Extreme. Zonal PD SSD is the classic exam answer.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• PD SSD: a block disk replicated in the zone, low latency, high IOPS vs pd-standard (HDD).\\n• Performance scales with size (and with provisioned IOPS on Extreme/Hyperdisk).\\n• It still exists after you stop the VM; it can be snapshotted.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"high IOPS\\" + \\"low-latency\\" + VM disk → Persistent Disk SSD.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Storage Standard (objects, not block); Filestore (shared NFS); Cloud SQL (managed database, not your disk).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"102","question":"Your application requires a database that scales horizontally and supports ACID transactions. Which GCP service should you choose?","options":{"A":"Cloud Spanner","B":"Cloud SQL","C":"BigQuery","D":"Firestore"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud Spanner: relational, global, Paxos replicas, and ACID transactions with TrueTime.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You scale by adding processing units / nodes, not a vertically bigger box.\\n• SQL (GoogleSQL or PostgreSQL dialect) + strong consistency.\\n• Multi-region: nearby reads and quorum writes; 99.999% SLA on regional/multi configs.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"horizontal\\" + \\"ACID\\" → Spanner (almost unique).\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud SQL (ACID but vertical scale + read replicas); BigQuery (analytics, not OLTP); Firestore (docs, limited transactions).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"103","question":"Your organization wants to provision Compute Engine instances automatically based ona predefined template. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Instance Templates and Managed Instance Groups","B":"Cloud Functions","C":"Terraform only","D":"Cloud Scheduler"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Instance Templates + Managed Instance Groups (MIG).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Instance template: an immutable recipe (image, type, disk, network, startup script, SA).\\n• MIG: keeps N replicas of that template, recreates them if they fail (autohealing), and can autoscaling and rolling updates.\\n• You change the template and do a rolling replace; you do not edit VMs by hand.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"predefined template\\" + provision VMs → templates + MIGs.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Functions (events, not a VM pool); Terraform only (it can create them, but the runtime scaler is the MIG); Cloud Scheduler (cron).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"104","question":"Your company needs to enforce organizational compliance for resource management in Google Cloud. What should ou use?","options":{"A":"IAM policies","B":"Cloud Identity- Aware Proxy","C":"Organization Policies","D":"Cloud Storage Lifecycle Rules"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Organization Policies: configuration rules (constraints) that nobody in a project can bypass.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Defined on org/folder; inheritance downward.\\n• Compliance examples: resourceLocations, disable SA keys, Domain Restricted Sharing, restrict external IPs.\\n• IAM says who administers; Org Policy says what is allowed. Complementary.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"organizational compliance\\" + \\"resource management\\" → Organization Policies.\\nDo not confuse with: IAM policies (access); IAP (who reaches an app/VM); Cloud Storage lifecycle (object classes).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"105","question":"Acompany\'s application requires low- latency access to logs and structured data. hich database should they use?","options":{"A":"Cloud Firestore","B":"Cloud Bigtable","C":"Cloud SQL","D":"BigQuery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud Bigtable: low-latency (ms) wide-column NoSQL with high throughput; the typical use case for logs, time-series, and IoT.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You scale nodes; the row key (e.g. service#logid#timestamp) determines performance.\\n• Built for billions of rows, not ad hoc SQL (that is BigQuery).\\n• Firestore is for mobile/app documents, not a high-volume log lake.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"logs\\" + \\"low-latency\\" + structured → Bigtable.\\nDo not confuse with: Firestore (realtime apps); Cloud SQL (small relational); BigQuery (analytics, seconds).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"106","question":"Your company needs to deploy an application that must handle high traffic loads while minimizing operational overhead. hich Google Cloud service should you use?","options":{"A":"Compute Engine with auto-scaling","B":"Cloud Functions","C":"App Engine","D":"Cloud Run"},"correct":"C","explanation":"App Engine is a fully managed serverless platform that automatically scales based on demand, reducing operational overhead."},{"id":"107","question":"You are designing a data lake solution in Google Cloud. Which service should you use for cost-effective, scalable storage?","options":{"A":"Cloud Bigtable","B":"Cloud Storage","C":"Cloud SQL","D":"Cloud Spanner"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud Storage: durable object storage, low cost, and practically unlimited capacity; it is the usual foundation of a data lake.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You store raw data (CSV, Parquet, Avro, JSON, logs, images) in gs:// buckets.\\n• Compute (BigQuery, Dataflow, Dataproc, Vertex AI) reads the lake without duplicating the data.\\n• Standard, Nearline, Coldline, and Archive classes, plus lifecycle rules, lower cost by access.\\n• 11 nines of durability; dual- or multi-region for geographic resilience.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"data lake\\" + \\"cost-effective, scalable storage\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Bigtable (low-latency NoSQL); Cloud SQL and Spanner (OLTP relational, expensive at lake scale).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"108","question":"Your company needs to ensure secure access to Google Cloud resources from on- premises employees. What is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud VPN with firewall rules","B":"Require SSH keys for all users","C":"Use Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP)","D":"Use public IPs with strict IAM policies 0%"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP): zero-trust access by identity, without exposing public IPs or depending on being on the VPN.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The user authenticates (Google Identity or corporate IdP) and IAP checks IAM (e.g. roles/iap.httpsResourceAccessor) and, if applicable, the device access level.\\n• It protects HTTP(S) apps behind an Application Load Balancer, App Engine, or Cloud Run, and SSH/RDP to VMs (IAP TCP).\\n• The perimeter is no longer the network: every request is verified.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"secure access\\" of employees to GCP, \\"by identity\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud VPN + firewall (network, not identity); SSH keys for everyone; public IPs + IAM (the VM is still on the Internet).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"109","question":"You need to deploy an application that requires a Redis-compatible in-memory data store for caching. Which Google Cloud service should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud Bigtable","B":"Memorystore","C":"Cloud SOL","D":"Firestore"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Memorystore: a managed service compatible with Redis (there is also Memcached and Valkey) for low-latency cache.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Redis: strings, hashes, lists, sets, TTL, pub/sub; typical for sessions or expensive-to-compute results.\\n• Google operates patches, HA (Standard replica), and backups; Redis Cluster scales horizontally.\\n• The app uses a private endpoint (Private Services Access or Private Service Connect).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"Redis-compatible\\", \\"in-memory\\", \\"caching\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Bigtable (disk, does not speak Redis); Cloud SQL (persistent relational); Firestore (documents).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"110","question":"A company wants to host a relational database on Google Cloud with minimal operational effort. Which service should they choose?","options":{"A":"Cloud SQL","B":"Cloud Spanner","C":"BigQuery","D":"Cloud Datastore"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud SQL: fully managed MySQL, PostgreSQL, or SQL Server; it is the lowest-ops option for a classic relational database.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Google applies patches, automatic backups, and PITR; with HA, automatic zonal failover.\\n• You scale vertically and with read replicas; it is not global and does not offer unlimited writes.\\n• Ideal if the app already uses standard SQL and does not need multi-region transactions.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"relational\\" + \\"minimal operational effort\\".\\nDo not confuse with: Spanner (global, more expensive); BigQuery (analytics, not OLTP); Datastore (NoSQL; today Firestore in Datastore mode).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"111","question":"How can you ensure API reliability for a microservices-based application?","options":{"A":"Use retries with exponential backoff","B":"Increase API timeouts indefinitely","C":"Disable auto-scaling to keep traffic stable","D":"Rely on a single API endpoint in one region"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Retries with exponential backoff: after a transient failure you wait 1 s, 2 s, 4 s… so you do not take the service down.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Retry only transient errors (429, 503, timeouts), never 400/403/404.\\n• Wait grows exponentially; add random jitter to avoid a thundering herd.\\n• Google client libraries already include this.\\n• Complement it with timeouts, a circuit breaker, and idempotency.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"API reliability\\" + microservices → retries with backoff.\\nDo not confuse with: infinite timeouts; disabling autoscaling; a single endpoint in one region.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"112","question":"Your company\'s web application must be available during maintenance windows. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Shut down the application during updates","B":"Deploy a rolling update strategy","C":"Use a static single-instance setup","D":"Disable auto-scaling during maintenance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. A rolling update: you replace instances or pods a few at a time; those not yet updated keep serving traffic.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• In a MIG: maxSurge (extra capacity) and maxUnavailable (how many can go down).\\n• In GKE: Deployment rollingUpdate + readinessProbe so you do not send traffic to unready pods.\\n• The LB health check sends only to healthy instances.\\n• If it fails, you abort and roll back to the previous template.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"available during maintenance\\" → rolling (or blue/green/canary).\\nDo not confuse with: shutting the app down; a single VM; disabling autoscaling.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"113","question":"What is the best way to prevent cascading failures in a distributed system?","options":{"A":"Use circuit breakers and bulkheads","B":"Increase the number of active connections","C":"Disable load balancing","D":"Reduce the number of retries"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Circuit breakers and bulkheads: they isolate the failed component so it does not take the rest down.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Circuit breaker: if the error rate crosses a threshold, it opens and fails fast instead of keep calling; after a cooldown it probes again (half-open).\\n• Bulkhead: partitions thread pools, queues, or instances so service A cannot consume all of B\'s resources.\\n• On GCP: GKE limits, Cloud Service Mesh (outlier detection), Pub/Sub as a buffer.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"cascading failures\\", \\"circuit breakers\\", \\"bulkheads\\".\\nDo not confuse with: more connections (increases pressure); removing the LB; only reducing retries.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"114","question":"How can you make a global API endpoint more reliable?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Load Balancing with failover policies","B":"Deploy a single Compute Engine instance","C":"Use an internal load balancer","D":"Limit the number of instances to reduce complexity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. A global Cloud Load Balancing service (Application or proxy) with backends in several regions and failover policies: traffic goes only to healthy backends.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Anycast: one worldwide IP; the user enters at the nearest edge.\\n• Health checks: if a region fails, traffic jumps to another.\\n• failoverPolicy / capacity scaler avoid saturating the backup region.\\n• Clients do not change DNS or IP.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"global API endpoint\\" + \\"reliable\\" → global LB + failover.\\nDo not confuse with: Internal LB (VPC only); a single VM; cutting instances \\"to simplify\\".","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"115","question":"Your company wants to detect and respond to incidents faster. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Manually check logs every few days","B":"Implement Cloud Monitoring with automated alerts","C":"Use Compute Engine instances instead of managed services","D":"Disable logging to improve performance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud Monitoring with automated alerting policies: it notifies you as soon as a metric or log crosses a threshold.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• GCP metrics, custom metrics, and log-based metrics.\\n• Condition (threshold, metric absence, SLO burn rate) + notification channels (email, Slack, PagerDuty, Pub/Sub, SMS).\\n• Uptime checks probe URLs from several continents.\\n• Incidents open and close on their own; they correlate with logs and Cloud Trace.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"detect and respond faster\\", \\"automated alerts\\".\\nDo not confuse with: reviewing logs by hand every few days; disabling logging \\"for performance\\".","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"116","question":"How can you ensure that a GKE cluster remains operational during a control plane upgrade?","options":{"A":"Use a regional cluster","B":"Use a single-zone cluster","C":"Disable control plane upgrades","D":"Increase node pool size"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Use a regional cluster: the control plane is replicated across three zones and the API server upgrade is rolling.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Zonal: a single master; during the upgrade kubectl/deploys can fail (pods keep running).\\n• Regional: 3 masters; they update one at a time and the API stays available.\\n• Nodes upgrade separately (surge or blue-green) with PodDisruptionBudgets.\\n• Autopilot can also be regional.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"control plane upgrade\\" + \\"operational\\" → regional cluster.\\nDo not confuse with: a single-zone cluster; \\"disable upgrades\\"; enlarging the node pool (does not protect the master).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"117","question":"What is the best way to prevent accidental deletion of critical resources?","options":{"A":"Enable resource locks and IAM policies","B":"Use manual approval before all deletions","C":"Avoid using Cloud IAM","D":"Store resources in a single-zone bucket"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Combine restrictive IAM (nobody with delete permission) and native delete protections. GCP does not have Azure-identical \\"resource locks\\".\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• IAM / deny policies: remove storage.buckets.delete, compute.instances.delete, etc.\\n• Deletion protection on Cloud SQL, GKE, VMs, and others.\\n• Project lien: prevents deleting the project.\\n• Cloud Storage: Retention Policy + Bucket Lock (WORM).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"prevent accidental deletion\\".\\nDo not confuse with: manual approval of everything (does not scale); not using IAM; a single-zone bucket.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"118","question":"What is the most effective way to ensure global high availability for a Cloud SQL database?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Spanner instead","B":"Enable cross-region replication","C":"Use a single-zone Cloud SQL instance","D":"Store backups in Cloud Storage S"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Enable cross-region replicas (and advanced DR on Enterprise Plus) so you can fail over to another region.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The replica is asynchronous → RPO > 0 (you can lose the last seconds or minutes).\\n• Classic promotion or replica failover to the DR replica; the write endpoint can re-point.\\n• Regional HA (synchronous standby) protects zones, not regions.\\n• Backups in Cloud Storage are not HA: only slow recovery.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"global\\" + Cloud SQL → cross-region replica; true global HA → Spanner.\\nDo not confuse with: a single-zone instance; backups only.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"119","question":"A healthcare company needs to process sensitive patient data securely in Google Cloud. Which service should they use for encryption?","options":{"A":"Cloud Key Management Service (KMS)","B":"Cloud CDN","C":"Cloud Scheduler","D":"Cloud Build"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS): it holds the keys (CMEK, Cloud HSM, or Cloud EKM) used to encrypt the data.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Google already encrypts at rest by default; KMS comes in when you need to control, rotate, and audit the key.\\n• CMEK: your key rings/keys; GCS, BigQuery, GCE, Cloud SQL, etc. use them (envelope encryption).\\n• Every use is in Cloud Audit Logs; disabling the key blocks access to the data.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"encryption\\" + sensitive/compliance data → Cloud KMS/CMEK.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Scheduler (cron); Cloud CDN (cache); Cloud Build (CI/CD).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"121","question":"A company wants to analyze real-time clickstream data. What Google Cloud service should they use?","options":{"A":"BigQuery with streaming inserts","B":"Cloud Storage","C":"Cloud SQL","D":"Cloud Datastore"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. BigQuery streaming ingestion (the Storage Write API, or legacy streaming inserts) makes clickstream events queryable within seconds, so you can run real-time analytics with SQL. Typically Pub/Sub plus Dataflow, or a Pub/Sub BigQuery subscription, feeds it.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) Cloud Storage is object storage with no query engine. C) Cloud SQL is OLTP and not suited to clickstream volumes. D) Datastore (Firestore) is a document database for apps, not analytics.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"122","question":"A company wants to ensure data consistency for transactions across multiple regions. What database should they use?","options":{"A":"Cloud SQL","B":"Cloud Spanner","C":"BigQuery","D":"Cloud Storage"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Spanner provides ACID transactions with external consistency across regions, which is exactly \'consistency for transactions across multiple regions\'.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Cloud SQL replicas across regions are asynchronous, and writes happen in one region. C) BigQuery is an analytics warehouse. D) Cloud Storage is object storage without multi-object transactions.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"123","question":"A company is running an API service on Compute Engine but wants to simplify API management. What should they use?","options":{"A":"Apigee API Gateway","B":"Cloud SQL","C":"Cloud Functions","D":"Cloud Pub/Sub"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Apigee (Apigee API Management): an API lifecycle platform with portal, security, quotas, transformation, and analytics.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• An Apigee proxy sits in front of the backend (GCE, GKE, Cloud Run…).\\n• Policies: API key/OAuth/JWT, rate limit, spike arrest, XML/JSON transformation, cache.\\n• Analytics and monetization; developer portal.\\n• Apigee Hybrid: runtime on your GKE, control plane on Google.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"simplify API management\\" → Apigee.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud SQL (data); Cloud Run functions (compute); Pub/Sub (messaging).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"124","question":"A company wants to monitor the uptime and performance of their services. What should they use?","options":{"A":"Cloud Monitoring and Cloud Logging","B":"Cloud Spanner","C":"Cloud Build","D":"Cloud Run"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud Monitoring (metrics, uptime, alerts) and Cloud Logging (logs): the native observability stack.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Monitoring: dashboards, SLOs, uptime checks, alerting policies.\\n• Logging: Log Router, buckets, sinks to BigQuery/Pub/Sub/GCS; Error Reporting for exceptions.\\n• Cloud Trace and Cloud Profiler complete latency and CPU.\\n• Ops Agent on VMs; GKE and Cloud Run already export.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"uptime and performance\\" → Monitoring + Logging.\\nDo not confuse with: Spanner (database); Cloud Build (CI); Cloud Run (runtime, not the monitor).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"125","question":"A company wants to ensure that employees cannot deploy resources outside approved regions. What should they configure?","options":{"A":"IAM Roles","B":"Organization Policies","C":"Compute Engine instance groups","D":"Cloud CDN"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. The gcp.resourceLocations Organization Policy constraint restricts where resources can be created. It is enforced for everyone in the org, folder or project, whatever their IAM role.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) IAM controls who can act, not where resources go. C) instance groups are a compute feature. D) Cloud CDN is content caching.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"126","question":"A company is deploying a high- availability web application. What is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Use Global Load Balancer with multi-regional backends","B":"Deploy all instances in a single region","C":"Use only preemptible instances","D":"Rely on Cloud Functions alone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. A Global External Load Balancer with backends in several regions (MIGs or NEGs). Traffic goes to the nearest healthy region.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Anycast: one worldwide IP.\\n• Health checks take the downed region out; the survivor\'s autoscaler absorbs the load.\\n• Data: Spanner, Cloud SQL with a cross-region replica, or multi/dual-region Cloud Storage.\\n• A single region is a regional single point of failure (earthquake, network cut).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"high-availability web application\\" → global LB + multi-region.\\nDo not confuse with: all instances in one region; Spot/preemptible only; Cloud Run functions only.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"128","question":"You need to enforce a policy that all Google Cloud storage objects must be encrypted using Customer-Managed Encryption Keys (CMEK). What should you use?","options":{"A":"IAM Conditions","B":"Organization Policy","C":"Cloud KMS","D":"VPC Firewall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. An Organization Policy, not Cloud KMS by itself. KMS creates the keys; the policy forces their use.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• constraints/gcp.restrictNonCmekServices in Deny with storage.googleapis.com: new/rewritten buckets and objects require CMEK.\\n• constraints/gcp.restrictCmekCryptoKeyProjects limits which projects keys can come from.\\n• Inherited organization → folder → project.\\n• It does not apply retroactively to objects already created with GMEK.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"enforce\\" + \\"all\\" + CMEK → Organization Policy.\\nDo not confuse with: IAM Conditions (one binding); Cloud KMS (does not enforce); VPC Firewall (network).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"129","question":"How does BeyondCorp Enterprise improve security for accessing Google Cloud resources?","options":{"A":"It allows access only through Google-managed VPN","B":"It enforces zero-trust security by verifying user and device attributes","C":"It restricts API calls based on IP addresses","D":"It scans VMs for vulnerabilities"},"correct":"B","explanation":"BeyondCorp Enterprise implements a zero- trust model, requiring users and devices to be verified before accessing resources."},{"id":"130","question":"You are designing a security architecture for a multi-region application on Google Cloud. How can you ensure that each region has its own security policies?","options":{"A":"Use separate Google Cloud projects for each region","B":"Configure IAM conditions for each region","C":"Set up Organization Policies for each region","D":"Use multiple VPC networks with per-region firewall rules"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Several VPCs (or one VPC with tightly scoped regional firewalls) and per-region rules, so you do not share the same network perimeter.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A VPC is global, subnets are regional: you can isolate further with one VPC per region/environment.\\n• Firewall rules or hierarchical firewall policies targeted at those subnets/VMs.\\n• Routes and peering/VPN only where needed; smaller blast radius.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"each region has its own security policies\\" + network → VPCs + firewalls.\\nDo not confuse with: Org Policy \\"per region\\" (not the firewall mechanism); IAM Conditions (API authorization, not L3/L4).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"131","question":"You need to detect and prevent suspicious network activity in Google Cloud. hich service should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud IDS","B":"Cloud KMS","C":"Cloud CDN","D":"Cloud Scheduler"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud IDS is a managed network intrusion detection service, built on Palo Alto Networks technology, that inspects mirrored traffic for threats and malware and sends findings to Security Command Center. To block threats inline, pair it with Cloud NGFW Enterprise or Cloud Armor.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) Cloud KMS manages keys. C) Cloud CDN caches content. D) Cloud Scheduler runs cron jobs.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"132","question":"Which feature allows Google Cloud customers to control and manage encryption keys for compliance purposes?","options":{"A":"VPC Peering","B":"Binary Authorization","C":"Customer- Managed Encryption Keys (CMEK)","D":"Identity-Aware Proxy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Customer-Managed Encryption Keys (CMEK) in Cloud KMS: you create, rotate, disable, and audit the key.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Envelope encryption: KMS holds the KEK; the service encrypts data with a DEK wrapped by that KEK.\\n• IAM on the key (roles/cloudkms.cryptoKeyEncrypterDecrypter); every use in Audit Logs.\\n• Automatic rotation; disabling the key makes data at rest unreadable.\\n• Software, Cloud HSM, or Cloud EKM (external key).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"control and manage encryption keys\\", \\"compliance\\".\\nDo not confuse with: VPC Peering (network); Binary Authorization (images); IAP (access).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"133","question":"What should you configure to ensure that only specific users or groups can access certain Cloud Storage objects?","options":{"A":"IAM Policies","B":"Firewall Rules","C":"Cloud Armor Policies","D":"VPC Flow Logs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. With IAM Policies on the bucket (or, if there is no uniform access, per-object ACLs).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Uniform bucket-level access (recommended): IAM only; grant roles to a group on the bucket or on a managed folder / prefix (IAM Conditions).\\n• Fine-grained: per-object ACLs plus IAM; harder to audit.\\n• roles/storage.objectViewer, objectAdmin, etc.; prefer a group over a lone user.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"specific users or groups\\" + objects → IAM.\\nDo not confuse with: Firewall (network); Cloud Armor (WAF on the LB); VPC Flow Logs (telemetry, they do not authorize).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"134","question":"You need to ensure that all Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) workloads are signed and verified before deployment. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud KMS","B":"Binary Authorization","C":"Cloud Armor","D":"Forseti Security"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Binary Authorization: an admission controller that rejects pods whose image lacks the required attestations.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• CI (Cloud Build) signs the image (attestor + key in KMS or note in Container Analysis).\\n• The cluster policy requires those attestors (e.g. \\"built by Cloud Build\\" + \\"passed vuln scan\\").\\n• Enable BinAuthz on GKE; Dry-run only audits.\\n• Blocks kubectl run of unsigned Docker Hub images.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"signed and verified before deployment\\" on GKE → Binary Authorization.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud KMS (signs the attestation, does not admit pods); Cloud Armor (WAF); Forseti (archived).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"135","question":"Your team wants to monitor cloud resource changes over time. Which GCP service should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud Audit Logs","B":"Cloud Scheduler","C":"Cloud Profiler","D":"Cloud Build"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud Audit Logs: they record who did what, when, and on which resource (Admin Activity, Data Access, System Event).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Admin Activity (always on, free): SetIamPolicy, create/update/delete of resources.\\n• Data Access (opt-in, paid): data reads/writes.\\n• Queried in Logging; sinks to BigQuery for long history.\\n• Cloud Asset Inventory complements with configuration state and history (up to 35 days).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"monitor resource changes over time\\" → Audit Logs.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Scheduler (cron); Cloud Profiler (CPU/heap); Cloud Build (CI).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"136","question":"Your company wants to implementa disaster recovery plan that automatically fails over a database to another region. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud SQL with cross-region replication","B":"Cloud Bigtable","C":"Cloud Memorystore","D":"Filestore"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud SQL with a cross-region replica (advanced DR on Enterprise Plus): you promote or launch replica failover in the healthy region.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Asynchronous replica in another region; RPO > 0.\\n• Regional HA (another zone) does NOT cover a region outage.\\n• Cross-region failover is invoked (runbook/API); it is not as autonomous as zonal HA.\\n• Write endpoint on Enterprise Plus avoids changing the app\'s IP.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"failover to another region\\" + managed SQL database → Cloud SQL cross-region.\\nDo not confuse with: Bigtable (NoSQL); Memorystore (cache); Filestore (NFS).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"137","question":"Your organization is using Terraform to manage cloud infrastructure. Where should Terraform’s state file be stored securely?","options":{"A":"Locally on a developer’s laptop","B":"Cloud Storage with versioning enabled","C":"A shared Google Drive folder","D":"Cloud Pub/Sub"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. In a Cloud Storage bucket with versioning (gcs backend), not on a laptop or Drive.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• State has IDs, IPs, and sometimes secrets: it is an operational secret.\\n• Versioning lets you recover a corrupt state or a bad apply.\\n• IAM on the bucket (least privilege); optional CMEK; uniform access.\\n• The gcs backend locks with a lease object so two applies do not run at once.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"Terraform state\\" + \\"securely\\" → Cloud Storage + versioning.\\nDo not confuse with: laptop (no backup or lock); Google Drive; Pub/Sub (messaging).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"138","question":"You want to automate the enforcement of network security policies across all projects. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Organization Policy","B":"Cloud Armor","C":"Firewall Rules","D":"VPC Peering"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Organization Policy: central guardrails inherited by folders and projects (e.g. restrict 0.0.0.0/0 firewalls or external IPs).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Constraints such as compute.vmExternalIpAccess, compute.restrictFirewallRules, compute.skipDefaultNetworkCreation.\\n• Evaluated at the API: a non-compliant create/update is rejected.\\n• Operational complement: hierarchical firewall policies (filter packets across the org).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"across all projects\\" + \\"enforce\\" → Organization Policy.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Armor (WAF on one LB); Firewall Rules of one VPC; VPC Peering (connectivity).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"139","question":"Your organization needs to automate daily snapshots of Compute Engine instances for backup. What is the best way to achieve this?","options":{"A":"Use gcloud commands in a cron job","B":"Create a Cloud Scheduler job that triggers a Cloud Function","C":"Use Compute Engine Snapshot Schedules","D":"Manually create snapshots in the Cloud Console"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Compute Engine snapshot schedules (resource policies): Google creates the snapshots on the schedule you define, without a homemade cron.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You attach a resource policy (daily/weekly, retention, location) to the disk.\\n• Incremental snapshots, regional or multi-region; can be encrypted with CMEK.\\n• Application-consistent: guest-flush / VSS on Windows or fsfreeze + script.\\n• A cron + gcloud or Cloud Scheduler + function works, but is more fragile and is not what the exam asks.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"daily snapshots\\" + Compute Engine → Snapshot Schedules.\\nDo not confuse with: cron on a VM; Cloud Scheduler+Function; manual clicks.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"140","question":"You need to configure IAM policies so that a specific user can manage Cloud Storage buckets but not delete them. What is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Assign the Storage Admin role","B":"Assign the Storage Object Creator role","C":"Assign the Storage Object Admin role","D":"Create a custom IAM role"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Create a custom IAM role with management permissions (create, get, list, update, IAM) and without storage.buckets.delete.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Predefined roles do not fit: Storage Admin includes delete; Object Admin/Creator do not cover the bucket.\\n• Custom role at project or org level; grant it to a group, not to the lone user.\\n• Extra defense: deny policy on storage.buckets.delete and retention/lock on critical buckets.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"manage but not delete\\" → custom role (least privilege).\\nDo not confuse with: Storage Admin (deletes); Object Creator/Admin (do not manage the bucket).","explanationSource":"card-en"}]},{"id":"gcp-profesional-cloud-architect-march-2025","title":"GCP Profesional Cloud Architect — March 2025","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"You are designing a solution to collect IoT sensor data from thousands of devices. What is the most scalable and cost-effective approach for ingesting this data into Google Cloud?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Pub/Sub to receive sensor data and write directly to Cloud SQL","B":"Send the data via HTTP and store it temporarily in Cloud Filestore","C":"Use Cloud Pub/Sub to ingest streaming data and route it to Cloud Dataflow : Ws Wy","D":"Use Compute Engine instances to listen to device traffic and write to eC BigQuery a 15. aii"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Pub/Sub scales automatically to millions of messages from thousands of devices, and Dataflow processes them serverlessly before writing to BigQuery, Bigtable or Cloud Storage. It scales and you pay per use.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Cloud SQL cannot absorb high-velocity telemetry writes. B) Filestore is NFS file storage, not an ingestion layer. D) self-managed VMs listening to devices do not scale or self-heal and cost more to run.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"2","question":"A retail company wants to migrate their analytics workload to the cloud and process both batch and real-time data. Which design approach should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Storage for batch files and Cloud Pub/Sub for streaming, then process both with Cloud Dataflow","B":"Use Cloud SQL for batch analytics and Firebase for real-time data","C":"Stream data into Cloud Spanner and analyze using Cloud Functions","D":"Store data in local memory cache and batch sync to Cloud Bigtable 9 nightly"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud Storage holds batch files and Pub/Sub handles real-time events. Dataflow (Apache Beam) runs the same programming model for both batch and streaming, usually loading into BigQuery for analytics.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) Cloud SQL and Firebase are app databases, not analytics. C) Cloud Functions are not an analytics engine. D) in-memory caches with nightly syncs lose data and are not real time.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"3","question":"Which of the following design choices best follows Google Cloud\'s principles for high availability?","options":{"A":"Use a single Compute Engine instance with persistent disk for simplicity","B":"Deploy services across multiple zones using a regional managed instance group","C":"Use Cloud Functions with a regional trigger only qull 10 “1 willy A)","D":"Use static IPs across all services to avoid failover complexities"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. A regional managed instance group spreads identical instances across zones with autohealing. Behind a load balancer, it survives a zone failure.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) one instance is a single point of failure. C) a regional trigger alone does not address resilience of the whole service. D) static IPs do not provide failover.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"4","question":"When architecting a solution that integrates third-party APIs and internal microservices, what is the best way to manage data flow between systems?","options":{"A":"Use Memorystore to cache API results for reuse","B":"Deploy Apigee in front of the microservices for latency control","C":"Use Cloud Pub/Sub for decoupling services and enabling asynchronous communication","D":"Establish direct TCP sockets between each microservice for fast integration"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Pub/Sub decouples producers from consumers. Third-party callbacks and internal services publish and subscribe asynchronously, absorb bursts, retry, and can evolve independently.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) caching does not manage data flow. B) Apigee manages APIs (security, quotas, analytics); it does not handle asynchronous decoupling between services. D) direct sockets tightly couple every pair of services.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"5","question":"A customer wants to automate the creation of infrastructure for each new environment (dev, test, prod). What should ou recommend?","options":{"A":"Create scripts that call gcloud commands manually","B":"Use Cloud Console templates for provisioning new projects","C":"Define infrastructure as code using Terraform and store in Cloud Source Repositories","D":"Use Deployment Manager scripts triggered from Cloud Scheduler oc es"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Terraform provides infrastructure as code and supports version control, collaboration, and automation, which is ideal for managing environments consistently."},{"id":"6","question":"Your team is tasked with designing a multi- region disaster recovery plan. What\'s the best practice for data storage replication?","options":{"A":"Use zonal Cloud SQL and export data daily","B":"Rely on Cloud Filestore with a manual backup schedule","C":"Use multi-region buckets in Cloud Storage for critical data","D":"Configure Persistent Disks with snapshot replication hetween zones"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Multi-region Cloud Storage provides automatic data replication across multiple regions, ensuring availability in case of regional outages."},{"id":"7","question":"A solution must ensure secure and auditable communication between microservices. What should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Deploy services in a private VPC subnet with Cloud NAT","B":"Use firewall rules with service tags to limit access","C":"Use service-to-service authentication with Identity-Aware Proxy or mTLS % aL Se","D":"Enable public access with API keys to simplify W777 *s “@ anaet “a “1 RA) communication"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Strong service identity, such as mTLS with Cloud Service Mesh (formerly Anthos Service Mesh) or IAP/IAM-authenticated calls, gives encrypted, mutually authenticated service-to-service traffic that can be audited.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) a private subnet with NAT is network placement, not authentication. B) firewall tags cannot identify the calling workload. D) public API keys are weak, shareable secrets.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"8","question":"When designing a system that must scale based on user demand, which architectural component is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"Use autoscaling Managed Instance Groups with load balancing","B":"Use a fixed number of Compute Engine VMs with persistent storage","C":"Use zonal Kubernetes nodes with horizontal scaling turned off","D":"Create cron jobs to monitor and resize infrastructure manually"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Pub/Sub provides global messaging ingestion at scale, ideal for loT scenarios. Cloud Dataflow can process and route the data efficiently to downstream services like BigQuery."},{"id":"9","question":"A client is storing sensitive health data and must comply with HIPAA. What is a compliant design for storing and processing this data?","options":{"A":"Use Datastore with public access disabled","B":"Store data in Cloud Spanner with default encryption","C":"Use Bigtable and enable Stackdriver logging","D":"Use Cloud Storage with CMEK and enforce VPC Service Controls “aw MINTy, TT % 4,"},"correct":"D","explanation":"CMEK ensures the customer controls encryption keys. VPC Service Controls prevent data exfiltration and help meet compliance like HIPAA."},{"id":"10","question":"An application receives financial transactions at high speed. What is a reliable way to design ingestion and processing for minimal loss?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud SQL to directly ingest and process data","B":"Use Pub/Sub with a Dataflow pipeline for reliable, scalable ingestion","C":"Use BigQuery with streaming insert mode and monitor queues manually","D":"Store all data on persistent disk and batch import it every 5 minutes"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Pub/Sub durably stores messages until they are acknowledged, and Dataflow processes them with checkpointing and exactly-once semantics. Together they give reliable, scalable ingestion with minimal loss even at high speed.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Cloud SQL as a direct ingestion buffer becomes a bottleneck. C) streaming straight into BigQuery with manually monitored queues lacks buffering and replay. D) local disks with 5-minute batches risk loss and add latency.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"11","question":"What is a recommended approach for migrating a large on-premise relational database to Google Cloud with minimal downtime?","options":{"A":"Take a snapshot, import into BigQuery, and reconfigure app access","B":"Use Storage Transfer Service to copy the database into Cloud Storage","C":"Use Database Migration Service to migrate data while syncing changes","D":"Export CSV files and import using gcloud commands into Cloud"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Database Migration Service (DMS) with a continuous job: initial load + change replication (CDC) until cutover.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Initial snapshot/dump, then only inserts/updates/deletes.\\n• Downtime ≈ stop writes, lag ≈ 0, promote the destination (Cloud SQL/AlloyDB) and point the app.\\n• Serverless: you do not stand up a replica server.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabminimal downtime\xbb + relational DB → DMS, not CSV.\\nDo not confuse with: importing to BigQuery (analytics, not OLTP), Storage Transfer Service (objects, not a DB), CSV export + gcloud into Cloud SQL (long downtime and no sync).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"13","question":"Which strategy ensures the best security when designing APIs that handle sensitive data across services?","options":{"A":"Authenticate via username and password sent with every request","B":"Use Google authentication with OAuth2 and API keys together","C":"Create a shared secret hardcoded in both client and server","D":"Allow any traffic inside the VPC as it is trusted by default"},"correct":"B","explanation":"OAuth2 with API keys and IAM integration ensures secure and controlled access, avoiding hardcoded secrets and insecure transmission."},{"id":"14","question":"What is the most appropriate way to decouple services in a scalable cloud-native architecture?","options":{"A":"Use HTTPS calls between services directly for fast communication","B":"Integrate services using Cloud Pub/Sub as a messaging backbone","C":"Allow services to connect through shared files using Filestore qull “1Ww* MY, \\\\ “aw","D":"Send database triggers to all microservices on data update"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Pub/Sub offers reliable, scalable message delivery and is ideal for decoupling producers and consumers in distributed systems."},{"id":"15","question":"Aclient’s solution must meet data sovereignty requirements by staying within specific regions. What should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud SQL with no location specified to allow global flexibility","B":"Use Firebase and Cloud Functions for a scalable regional solution","C":"Use Cloud Spanner with global configuration and regional failover","D":"Configure regional storage and compute in the designated locations qull “1nWw* NN tyy, \\\\\\\\ “iw"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Specifying regional resources ensures data stays within the chosen locations, helping meet sovereignty and compliance needs."},{"id":"16","question":"An application writes large logs to disk. hat’s the best practice to make logs queryable and useful for future analytics?","options":{"A":"Stream logs to Cloud Logging and export to BigQuery via sink","B":"Store logs in Cloud Storage and read via gsutil when needed","C":"Write logs to Cloud SQL and perform queries with indexes","D":"Save logs to Persistent Disk and use cron jobs to parse logs ‘2 “Se"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Send them to Cloud Logging and export them to BigQuery with a sink.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The Ops Agent or logging system writes to Cloud Logging.\\n• A sink with a filter copies (or routes) to a BigQuery dataset.\\n• You analyze with SQL, dashboards, and the dataset’s own retention.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabqueryable\xbb + \xabanalytics\xbb → Logging + sink to BigQuery.\\nDo not confuse with: GCS + gsutil (not analytical), Cloud SQL as a log store (expensive and poorly scalable), a cron over Persistent Disk (fragile).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"17","question":"Asystem must process data from multiple sensors every second and support schema evolution. What is a scalable design?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud SQL with JSON fields to store flexible schema","B":"Write sensor data to Pub/Sub and Cloud Storage directly","C":"Stream sensor output to Bigtable using batch sync nightly","D":"Use Pub/Sub to Dataflow and then into BigQuery for flexibility"},"correct":"D","explanation":"BigQuery supports schema evolution and combined with Pub/Sub and Dataflow, it allows scalable, real-time ingest and transformation."},{"id":"19","question":"You need to store financial records with strict write- once-read-many (WORM) compliance. What is the best GCP solution?","options":{"A":"Use Firestore in strong consistency mode for record integrity","B":"Save records in Cloud SQL with triggers to avoid overwrite","C":"Use Cloud Storage with bucket retention policy and object lock S14","D":"Store all data in Pub/Sub and archive with BigQuery ai Ti"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Object versioning and retention policies in Cloud Storage support regulatory WORM requirements and prevent deletion or modification."},{"id":"20","question":"Which service allows the integration of legacy data pipelines into modern cloud- native analytics platforms?","options":{"A":"Use Memorystore to bridge new and old systems","B":"Use Apigee to wrap legacy systems as modern APIs","C":"Use Dataflow to ingest and transform data from legacy sources S73 \\",","D":"Use Cloud Run to wrap legacy software in containers for reusability Cees TIN “iw"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dataflow supports both batch and stream ingestion, allowing transformation of legacy data formats into structures ready for modern analytics."},{"id":"21","question":"Which solution provides automated scaling of stateless web applications based on load?","options":{"A":"Use persistent disks on Compute Engine with manual VM scaling","B":"Use App Engine Standard to scale applications automatically","C":"Use unmanaged instance groups to scale VM instances","D":"Create a Cloud Function that launches new VMs on high load Big] -_* = oxy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. App Engine Standard: a PaaS that creates or deletes instances based on requests and can scale to zero.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You upload code (or a container in 2nd gen); Google manages the runtime.\\n• Automatic scaling by QPS, latency, or utilization.\\n• Stateless: session elsewhere (Memorystore, Firestore, cookies).\\n\\nExam tip: \xabautomated scaling\xbb + \xabstateless web\xbb → App Engine Standard (or Cloud Run, not listed here as \xabbest\xbb).\\nDo not confuse with: PD + manual scaling, unmanaged instance groups (no native autoscaler), a Cloud Function that launches VMs (anti-pattern).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"22","question":"You want to connect two VPCs in different projects. What is the best way to allow internal communication between them?","options":{"A":"Enable public IP access and set up external firewall rules","B":"Create SSH tunnels between VMs in both projects","C":"Configure VPC peering and update internal DNS and routes","D":"Use Cloud CDN to route traffic between private VPCs 77 a"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. VPC Network Peering (plus any routes/DNS you need).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Private peering over Google’s network: RFC1918 between both VPCs, low latency, no internet.\\n• Not transitive: A–B and B–C do not give A–C.\\n• Ranges cannot overlap; you must import/export custom routes if you use them.\\n• DNS: peering does not share Cloud DNS by itself; use DNS peering or private records.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabtwo VPCs\xbb + \xabdifferent projects\xbb + internal → VPC peering.\\nDo not confuse with: public IPs + firewall, SSH tunnels, Cloud CDN (cache, not a private network).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"23","question":"A customer needs a managed Kubernetes service that integrates with GCP services. Which solution is best?","options":{"A":"Deploy Kubernetes manually on Compute Engine instances","B":"Use Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) for native integration","C":"Use Cloud Functions with Firebase to mimic Kubernetes workloads","D":"Use Cloud Run on top of unmanaged Kubernetes clusters Cae “eS Ts a SY LL) S"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE): a managed control plane, nodes on GCP, and native IAM, VPC, Artifact Registry, Observability, and Load Balancing.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Google operates etcd/API server (regional on Autopilot/regional clusters).\\n• Add-ons: Workload Identity, GKE Sandbox, Binary Authorization, Gateway.\\n• Autopilot removes node management; Standard leaves it with you.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabmanaged Kubernetes\xbb + \xabintegrates with GCP\xbb → GKE.\\nDo not confuse with: kubeadm on GCE (you operate everything), Cloud Functions+Firebase (not K8s), Cloud Run on unmanaged clusters (Run does not replace a Kubernetes API).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"24","question":"How can you ensure zero-downtime deployment during software rollout in GKE?","options":{"A":"Create multiple clusters and switch DNS routing between them","B":"Use rolling updates with readiness and liveness probes","C":"Restart all pods with the updated image at the same time","D":"Pause workloads and update all deployments manually"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. A Deployment rolling update with readiness and liveness probes.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Kubernetes creates new pods; the Service only sends traffic to Ready pods.\\n• maxUnavailable / maxSurge control how many change at once.\\n• Liveness restarts zombie pods; readiness takes them out of the LB if they are not serving yet.\\n• If it fails: rollback to the previous ReplicaSet.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabzero-downtime\xbb + GKE → rolling + probes, not a mass restart.\\nDo not confuse with: changing DNS between clusters (heavy blue/green), killing every pod at once, pausing and updating by hand.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"25","question":"Which of the following is the best method for automating infrastructure deployment in a CI/CD pipeline?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Shell to deploy changes from developer laptops","B":"Use gcloud commands stored in a spreadsheet and run manually","C":"Use Cloud Monitoring alerts to trigger resource updates","D":"Integrate Terraform scripts with Cloud Build triggers pias BIS Ti a SY"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Terraform triggered by Cloud Build (triggers on the repo).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A push or PR runs a trigger: terraform plan and, with approval, apply.\\n• State lives in a remote backend (GCS) with locking.\\n• The same recipe in every environment; no laptops or spreadsheets.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabCI/CD\xbb + infrastructure → Terraform + Cloud Build.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud Shell from a laptop, gcloud in a spreadsheet, Monitoring alerts that \xabupdate\xbb resources.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"26","question":"A team needs a cost-effective solution to run containerized workloads without managing infrastructure. What is the best option?","options":{"A":"Use Compute Engine and configure container runtimes manually","B":"Use Cloud Run for fully managed container deployment","C":"Use GKE with node pools and custom autoscaling rules","D":"Use App Engine with flexible environment and Dockerfiles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Run abstracts infrastructure entirely and charges based on request usage, ideal for event-driven container workloads."},{"id":"27","question":"Asecurity team needs to ensure all VMs use only approved OS images. What should you implement?","options":{"A":"Lock VM configurations using startup scripts","B":"Use Organization Policy with allowed image projects","C":"Use custom SSH configurations for secure image enforcement","D":"Enforce rules via VPC Service Controls and subnet masking a 12 (Ptr"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. An Organization Policy with the trusted image projects constraint (compute.trustedImageProjects).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You define which projects images can be launched from (e.g. your golden-image project + some Google ones).\\n• It inherits to folders/projects; a user with VM-create permission cannot bypass it.\\n• Combine with hardened images (CIS) and Binary Authorization on GKE.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabapproved OS images\xbb → Org Policy, not scripts.\\nDo not confuse with: startup scripts (they do not block the image), custom SSH, VPC-SC (API exfiltration, not an image catalog).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"28","question":"What\'s the most efficient way to deploy resources across multiple GCP regions with consistent configuration?","options":{"A":"Copy and paste YAML files into each region manually","B":"Create Cloud Scheduler jobs to replicate resources hourly","C":"Use Deployment Manager templates with region variables","D":"Use console UI and clone resources into different A)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Regional managed instance groups (MIGs) allow deployment across zones, improving fault tolerance and meeting high availability requirements."},{"id":"29","question":"What is the primary advantage of using Cloud Load Balancer in a global application?","options":{"A":"It supports only Layer 4 traffic routing within a region","B":"It provides simple local failover with no DNS changes","C":"It encrypts all internal traffic between services automatically","D":"It distributes traffic across regions and supports autoscaling Pe"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cloud Load Balancing provides global routing, autoscaling, and health checks, which are ideal for highly available applications."},{"id":"30","question":"A customer is worried about excessive costs from misconfigured services. How can you prevent this?","options":{"A":"Set up automatic daily deletion of unused resources","B":"Configure all services to stop after business hours","C":"Use Budgets and Alerts with quotas and IAM limits % % i","D":"Manually track costs in a shared Google Sheets file avy TTA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cloud Billing budgets and alerts, plus quotas and least-privilege IAM.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A budget with thresholds (50/90/100%) emails and can publish to Pub/Sub.\\n• Quotas (including custom) cap API/resource use.\\n• IAM keeps anyone from creating expensive GPUs or IPs.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabexcessive costs\xbb / \xabmisconfigured\xbb → budgets + quotas + IAM.\\nDo not confuse with: deleting resources every night (dangerous), shutting everything off after hours (breaks prod), manual Sheets.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"31","question":"Which method allows the most granular control of GCP resource permissions?","options":{"A":"Assign Editor role at the project level for all users","B":"Apply custom IAM roles at the resource level","C":"Enable default permissions and remove billing access","D":"Use Shared VPC with DNS access only between users sly, 47) WL Sanaet Oa Ts \\\\ >"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Custom IAM roles applied at the resource (or the lowest possible ancestor).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A custom role is an exact list of permissions (e.g. storage.objects.get).\\n• The binding can sit on org, folder, project, or resource (bucket, instance, dataset).\\n• Least privilege: not project Editor.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabmost granular\xbb → custom roles + resource level.\\nDo not confuse with: Editor for everyone, \xabremove billing and leave defaults\xbb, Shared VPC (network, not object ACLs).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"32","question":"A business requires a hybrid cloud setup for burst compute workloads. What is a recommended pattern?","options":{"A":"Use only on-premise servers and sync data nightly to GCP","B":"Send full traffic to GCP during high business hours only","C":"Build a VM image and email it to the cloud team % =","D":"Use VPN or Interconnect and configure workload bursting ely A) ‘Ss Ti"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Connect on-prem with HA VPN or Cloud Interconnect and launch the extra load on GCP.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The hybrid network (BGP via Cloud Router) makes GCP VMs/GKE look like an extension of the datacenter.\\n• Burst: queues or the orchestrator send extra jobs to a MIG/GKE/Dataproc in the cloud.\\n• Hot data is replicated or read over the interconnect.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabhybrid\xbb + \xabburst compute\xbb → VPN/Interconnect + elastic capacity.\\nDo not confuse with: nightly sync (not burst), \xabeverything to GCP at peak hours\xbb with no network, emailing an image.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"33","question":"What is the primary purpose of using Shared VPC in a large enterprise?","options":{"A":"To expose services to public IPs in development environments","B":"To centralize network administration across multiple projects","C":"To allow external partners access to internal billing reports","D":"To support multi-cloud routing between private VPCs GO ed J"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Centralize network administration: a host project owns the VPC and lends subnets to service projects.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Network admins live in the host; app teams only use the assigned subnets.\\n• Firewall, routes, NAT, and Interconnect are defined once.\\n• IAM: Shared VPC Admin vs Network User.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabcentralize network administration across projects\xbb → Shared VPC.\\nDo not confuse with: exposing public IPs, giving billing to partners, \xabmulti-cloud routing\xbb (that is Interconnect/VPN, not Shared VPC).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"35","question":"Which method is best for implementing infrastructure updates with minimal risk?","options":{"A":"Manually SSH into each machine and apply updates","B":"Deploy entire environments at once in production","C":"Use canary deployments with monitoring and rollback","D":"Build custom scripts for every project deployment a"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Canary: you release the change to a subset, watch metrics, and if it goes well you continue; if not, rollback.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Infra or app: 5–10% of MIGs/pods/traffic on the new version.\\n• SLIs (errors, latency) in Cloud Monitoring; threshold → halt.\\n• Automatic or one-click rollback to the previous artifact.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabminimal risk\xbb + updates → canary + monitor + rollback.\\nDo not confuse with: mass SSH, big-bang to prod, ad-hoc scripts per project.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"36","question":"What is the best solution for autoscaling VMs based on CPU and memory usage?","options":{"A":"Create a Cloud Function that runs when usage spikes","B":"Use a startup script to clone VMs manually","C":"Monitor usage in Cloud Monitoring and generate reports","D":"Use Managed Instance Groups with autoscaling policies {"},"correct":"D","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: D. A managed instance group autoscaler scales on CPU utilization, load balancing serving capacity, or Cloud Monitoring metrics such as memory reported by the Ops Agent. It adds and removes VMs automatically.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) a function on spikes is a home-made autoscaler. B) cloning VMs manually is not automatic. C) reports do not take action.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"37","question":"Ateam needs to deploy a service that scales to zero when idle. Which service should they use?","options":{"A":"Cloud Run with containerized app and HTTP trigger","B":"Cloud Functions with persistent disk attached","C":"App Engine Flexible with custom autoscaler % 2 Le","D":"GKE Standard with minimum node count set to zero % aL “1Ww* Ss"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Cloud Run scales to zero instances when there are no requests (you pay nothing while idle) and scales out automatically on HTTP traffic.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) Cloud Functions do not attach persistent disks. C) App Engine flexible keeps at least one instance running. D) GKE Standard node pools can scale to zero, but the cluster still runs and costs money; it is not the simplest scale-to-zero service.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"38","question":"You want to enable automatic failover between zones for a web application. Which approach is most suitable?","options":{"A":"Deploy all instances in a single zone for simplicity","B":"Use startup scripts to detect failures and recreate instances","C":"Use preemptible VMs with manual failover configurations","D":"Use regional Managed Instance Groups with health checks"},"correct":"D","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Regional managed instance groups place VMs across zones, and health-check-based autohealing recreates failed VMs. Behind a load balancer, traffic fails over to healthy zones automatically.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) one zone means no failover. B) startup scripts that detect failures reinvent autohealing. C) preemptible (Spot) VMs can be reclaimed at any time, and manual failover is not automatic.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"39","question":"What is a cost-effective strategy to reduce egress charges for content delivered globally?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud NAT with public IPs for better routing","B":"Use Cloud CDN to cache and deliver content closer to users","C":"Route traffic through a VPN and compress data streams","D":"Use multiple zones and serve content from Compute Engine % oe villa, RY"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud CDN serves cached content from the edge, so fewer requests reach the origin. Cache egress is priced lower than origin internet egress, which reduces both latency and egress cost.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Cloud NAT is outbound access for private VMs. C) VPN adds overhead, and compression alone does not remove egress. D) serving from VMs in more zones does not reduce internet egress.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"40","question":"Ateam must deploy infrastructure as code using GCP-native tooling. What should they use?","options":{"A":"Use Deployment Manager with YAML configuration files","B":"Write shell scripts and store them in Git repositories","C":"Use gcloud interactive sessions from Cloud Shell only","D":"Create HT TP functions that provision services on demand"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Deployment Manager with YAML configurations (and Jinja/Python templates).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• A config declares GCP resources; DM creates them and keeps them as a deployment.\\n• It is the classic \xabGCP-native\xbb answer on older exams.\\n• Today: Infrastructure Manager (managed Terraform) is the recommended native; DM is deprecated.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabGCP-native tooling\xbb → Deployment Manager (in this set).\\nDo not confuse with: a shell in Git (not a native provisioner), Cloud Shell only, Cloud Functions that create resources on demand (not IaC).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"41","question":"Your organization wants to manage all billing and resource access in one place. What architecture pattern supports this?","options":{"A":"Use a separate billing account for each project independently","B":"Assign Project Owner IAM roles to all team leads per project","C":"Set up an Organization Node with folders and centralized billing","D":"Configure budgets per region without a billing administrator"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. An Organization node (Resource Manager) with folders and a centralized billing account.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Org → folders (by department or environment) → projects.\\n• IAM and Org Policies inherit; the Billing Account is linked to projects (or inherited).\\n• A Billing Admin sees aggregated spend; Project Owners do not have to be billing admins.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabmanage all billing and resource access in one place\xbb → Organization + folders + centralized billing.\\nDo not confuse with: one billing account per project, Owner for every lead, budgets per region with no billing admin.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"43","question":"Which GCP service supports private container registry with version control?","options":{"A":"Cloud Build with internal registry support for containers","B":"Artifact Hub integrated into Kubernetes environments","C":"Artifact Registry with Docker-compatible repositories","D":"Memorystore configured with image layer caching logic"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Artifact Registry securely stores container images and supports Docker-compatible registries, ideal for versioned deployments."},{"id":"44","question":"A developer needs to automate VM creation with custom startup actions. What\'s the best method?","options":{"A":"Use instance metadata and startup scripts for customization","B":"Use Google Forms to input configuration during boot","C":"Build the image manually and deploy via the Cloud Console","D":"Schedule a cron job that runs remote commands nightly % S re 11 3 anaet oan Ts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Startup scripts in instance metadata (or in the MIG instance template).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• metadata startup-script or startup-script-url (GCS).\\n• cloud-init / the Compute Engine agent runs it on first boot (or every boot, depending on config).\\n• Automatable with gcloud, Terraform, or the API; no human UI.\\n\\nExam tip: “automate VM creation” + “custom startup” → metadata + startup script.\\nDo not confuse with: Google Forms (no boot integration), building an image by hand in the console, a remote nightly cron.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"46","question":"Ateam must enforce encryption at rest with their own key rotation. What is the right choice?","options":{"A":"Use Google-managed encryption for simplicity and ease","B":"Use public Cloud KMS keys that rotate automatically","C":"Use Customer-Managed Encryption Keys with rotation policies","D":"Store secrets in Cloud Logging and encrypt with IAM roles Sy % aL aeaet Oa Te \\\\ >"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. CMEK (Customer-Managed Encryption Keys) in Cloud KMS, with a rotation schedule.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You create a key ring + crypto key; the service (GCS, BQ, PD, SQL…) wraps DEKs with your KEK.\\n• Automatic rotation (e.g. 90 days) creates a new version; old data is rewrapped when used.\\n• You can disable/destroy the key and cut access.\\n\\nExam tip: “own key rotation” → CMEK, not GMEK.\\nDo not confuse with: “public” KMS keys, secrets in Cloud Logging, IAM that “encrypts”.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"47","question":"A customer wants to allow external partners to access APIs securely. What is a scalable solution?","options":{"A":"Use Apigee to manage API keys and enforce quotas","B":"Share gcloud credentials with time-limited sessions","C":"Deploy APIs on GKE and whitelist partner IPs manually","D":"Store partner credentials in Cloud Functions environment seul dy. ces “ey Ts Oe Y avila, a * B93 TTA"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Apigee (API Management): keys/OAuth, quotas, plans, analytics, and a proxy in front of your backends.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The partner calls the proxy; Apigee authenticates, applies quota/spike arrest, and routes to Cloud Run/GKE/on-prem.\\n• Developer portal and API products for onboarding.\\n• You do not share gcloud or project credentials.\\n\\nExam tip: “external partners” + APIs → Apigee.\\nDo not confuse with: sharing gcloud credentials, hand-maintained IP allowlists on GKE, secrets in Cloud Functions env vars.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"48","question":"What is a secure and scalable way to manage secrets like database credentials?","options":{"A":"Use environment variables hardcoded into deployment files","B":"Store secrets in Google Sheets and restrict access via IAM","C":"Use Secret Manager with IAM-based access control","D":"Send secrets over Pub/Sub topics with encryption enabled % Se cS Rad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Secret Manager with IAM-based access (and rotation/versioning).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You store the secret; each change is a version.\\n• The app or SA calls the API at runtime (or it is mounted on Cloud Run/GKE).\\n• IAM: secretmanager.secretAccessor only to those who need it; access audit logs.\\n\\nExam tip: “manage secrets” → Secret Manager, not Sheets or env vars.\\nDo not confuse with: variables in the YAML, Google Sheets + IAM, sending secrets over Pub/Sub.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"50","question":"A project requires asynchronous task execution for user uploads. What is the best architecture?","options":{"A":"Use Compute Engine with polling scripts for new uploads","B":"Use Cloud Pub/Sub with Cloud Functions to trigger workflows","C":"Schedule Cloud Tasks to run every hour and check for updates % Se","D":"Store metadata in Cloud SQL and check periodically via cron \\\\ s"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pub/Sub and Cloud Functions support event- driven, serverless execution ideal for asynchronous tasks like file processing."},{"id":"51","question":"How can you prevent data exfiltration from sensitive storage buckets?","options":{"A":"Block all VPC traffic to the internet globally","B":"Apply VPC Service Controls to isolate and protect resources","C":"Encrypt bucket names using custom IAM permissions","D":"Use Cloud CDN to limit exposure of object URLs % a"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. VPC Service Controls: a service perimeter around the APIs (Cloud Storage, BigQuery, etc.).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Even if IAM allows storage.objects.get, copying to a project/bucket outside the perimeter is denied.\\n• Access Context Manager (IP, device, user) defines who enters.\\n• Combine with uniform bucket-level access, Public Access Prevention, and CMEK.\\n\\nExam tip: “prevent data exfiltration” → VPC Service Controls.\\nDo not confuse with: cutting the whole VPC off the internet (breaks legitimate egress), “encrypting the bucket name”, Cloud CDN (distributes, does not isolate).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"52","question":"Which design ensures compliance with disaster recovery objectives for stateful services?","options":{"A":"Use local SSDs for fast access and high capacity","B":"Replicate VM snapshots between two zones manually","C":"Use regional persistent disks with automatic failover","D":"Schedule cron jobs to backup Cloud SQL twice a day “4, vill, ee “eS “1iWw* Ss"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Regional persistent disks replicate across zones automatically and ensure continuity of stateful services during outages."},{"id":"54","question":"What is the benefit of using Cloud Interconnect over VPN for enterprise workloads?","options":{"A":"VPN provides higher availability with lower latency","B":"Interconnect offers consistent performance and lower latency","C":"VPN is better suited for large bulk data transfers","D":"Interconnect requires no setup and is enabled by default Bat} e Ny"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. More predictable performance and lower latency: a private high-bandwidth link, not IPsec over the internet.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Dedicated: 10 or 100 Gbps in a colo; Partner: from 50 Mbps via a provider.\\n• VLAN attachment + Cloud Router (BGP) advertises RFC1918.\\n• Less jitter than VPN; egress to on-prem is usually cheaper.\\n\\nExam tip: “consistent performance” / “lower latency” → Interconnect.\\nDo not confuse with: “VPN is more available and faster” (false), “VPN for bulk transfer” (backwards), “Interconnect comes enabled by default”.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"55","question":"Which solution provides automated, versioned backups of structured data?","options":{"A":"Manually export databases from Cloud Shell each day","B":"Use Cloud SQL automated backups with point-in-time recovery","C":"Use BigQuery exports to Cloud Storage and delete daily","D":"Write scripts to save CSVs into Filestore hourly Sy, aie % oe % % aS * a ae Peas nf! “a naw SAL AY S"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud SQL: automated backups + point-in-time recovery (PITR) with transaction logs.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Daily backup window; configurable retention.\\n• PITR: you restore to a specific second (on-demand or to a new instance).\\n• Backups are managed; you do not depend on a manual export from Cloud Shell.\\n\\nExam tip: “automated, versioned backups” + structured → Cloud SQL backups/PITR.\\nDo not confuse with: a daily BQ export you then delete, CSV to Filestore, a hand dump.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"57","question":"Aweb app must support TLS termination with low latency. What is the best tool?","options":{"A":"Use Compute Engine with self-signed certificates","B":"Use HT TPS Load Balancer with SSL certificates","C":"Use Pub/Sub and attach TLS headers manually","D":"Configure TLS on Cloud SQL and route via proxy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. The External Application Load Balancer (HTTPS) with certificates (ideally Google-managed).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• TLS terminates at the Google Front End, near the user.\\n• Managed certificates renew themselves (Google’s Let’s Encrypt-like flow).\\n• Optional: HTTP/3, Cloud CDN, Cloud Armor.\\n\\nExam tip: “TLS termination” + “low latency” → HTTPS Load Balancer.\\nDo not confuse with: self-signed certs on every VM, Pub/Sub (does not terminate web TLS), Cloud SQL TLS (that is the DB, not the web).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"58","question":"Which is the most scalable way to process 100,000 image files uploaded every hour?","options":{"A":"Use Compute Engine and poll for new uploads via cron","B":"Store files in Filestore and run batch jobs every night","C":"Use Cloud Functions triggered by Cloud Storage events","D":"Use Datastore and check for new entries via queries"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cloud Storage + a trigger (Cloud Run functions / Cloud Functions) for each created object.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Each OBJECT_FINALIZE launches an invocation; the service scales in parallel.\\n• 100k/h ≈ 28/s: comfortable for functions/Run; use a Pub/Sub queue if you want smoothing.\\n• Idempotency: the same object must not be processed badly twice.\\n\\nExam tip: many files + “uploaded” → GCS event, not cron.\\nDo not confuse with: GCE + poll, Filestore + nightly batch, Datastore queries.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"59","question":"How can you enforce a naming convention for GCP resources across a large organization?","options":{"A":"Manually review names and reject non-compliant ones","B":"Use policy enforcement via Cloud Asset Inventory and Config Validator","C":"Rename all resources using scripts during off-peak hours","D":"Use deployment manager with placeholder variables"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Config Validator can enforce policies using templates and integrate with the Asset Inventory to audit naming conventions."},{"id":"60","question":"A project requires strict control over internet access. What\'s the recommended approach?","options":{"A":"Assign public IPs to all VMs and disable firewall","B":"Use App Engine and configure DNS zones manually","C":"Use Private Google Access and configure Cloud NAT","D":"Allow all traffic by default and restrict only outbound ports"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud NAT allows instances without public IPs to access the internet securely, and Private Google Access enables access to GCP APIs."},{"id":"62","question":"What strategy ensures consistent tagging of resources for cost tracking?","options":{"A":"Use project labels manually in the Cloud Console","B":"Require developers to include cost labels in commit messages","C":"Use Organization Policy to enforce required labels","D":"Monitor tags in billing reports and notify team monthly 4% 2 selly *s, “@ Saeaet “a “1nWw* A)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Organization Policy can enforce label requirements, ensuring every resource is consistently tagged for cost attribution."},{"id":"63","question":"How should you isolate environments (dev/test/prod) in GCP for governance?","options":{"A":"Use one project and prefix resources with environment name","B":"Separate environments by folders and merge roles","C":"Create a separate GCP project for each environment","D":"Use resource names only to distinguish environments ee) Gb prneeert eaeet Op TTI A)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Regional MIGs automatically distribute instances across zones and perform health checks, ensuring availability during zone failures."},{"id":"64","question":"What is the recommended GCP service for real-time metrics and alerting?","options":{"A":"Cloud Tasks with built-in alert webhook support","B":"Memorystore with manual scripts to scan logs","C":"Cloud Functions for custom metric collection","D":"Cloud Monitoring with Alerting Policies and \\"y Se oo 11 3 “11Ww* silly A) Dashboards"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cloud Monitoring offers native integration with GCP services, real-time metrics collection, dashboards, and customizable alerting."},{"id":"65","question":"Which architecture ensures reliability when processing 24/7 global event streams?","options":{"A":"Stream events into Cloud SQL via API Gateway","B":"Write directly to Cloud Datastore from remote devices","C":"Use Pub/Sub, Dataflow, and BigQuery for global streams %% a ve","D":"Upload files every hour from IoT devices into Filestore aL TT"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pub/Sub, Dataflow, and BigQuery together form a scalable, reliable architecture for ingesting and processing streaming data globally."},{"id":"66","question":"You must provide least-privilege access to an intern analyzing logs. What do you assign?","options":{"A":"Project Owner role for unrestricted visibility","B":"IAM Viewer with additional Compute Admin","C":"Logs Viewer role only for needed project","D":"Cloud SQL Admin to grant restricted log reading"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. A minimal logs role on the needed project (e.g. roles/logging.viewer), never Owner or Compute Admin.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Logging Viewer: list and read logs; they do not change sinks or VMs.\\n• If it is only one log bucket: roles/logging.viewAccessor on the view.\\n• Never pile Viewer + Compute Admin “just in case”.\\n\\nExam tip: “least-privilege” + “analyzing logs” → Logs Viewer only.\\nDo not confuse with: Project Owner, Viewer+Compute Admin, Cloud SQL Admin.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"68","question":"A customer needs to control data flow between two GCP regions. What should they use?","options":{"A":"Shared VPC to route traffic directly via private subnet","B":"VPC firewall rules and custom route configurations","C":"IAM roles assigned to inter-region service accounts","D":"Assign public IPs and use NAT between regions % eS ae senly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. VPC firewall rules and custom routes (the VPC is global; subnets are regional).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• By default, subnets in different regions in the same VPC reach each other over Google’s network.\\n• Firewall (priority, tags, service accounts) can deny or limit that east-west traffic.\\n• Custom routes / network tags steer or drop (e.g. force an appliance).\\n\\nExam tip: “control data flow between two GCP regions” → firewall + routes.\\nDo not confuse with: Shared VPC (shares subnets, not an inter-region filter), IAM on SAs (does not route packets), public IPs + NAT between regions.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"70","question":"Which service enables central management of users and devices in GCP?","options":{"A":"Identity Platform with custom SAML federation","B":"Cloud Identity with device and account management","C":"IAM groups created for each service in each region % = TT 13 aL S Ts","D":"Firebase Auth for managing enterprise access s"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Identity is a centralized identity-as-a- service that supports single sign-on, device policies, and directory sync..."},{"id":"71","question":"What strategy prevents developers from deploying unapproved services?","options":{"A":"Use Deployment Manager with templates only","B":"Require all users to submit manual approvals via email","C":"Apply Organization Policy constraints on allowed services","D":"Limit VPC network access to internal DNS zones only Sy, % silly Ss Sannet Oa “11Ww*"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. The Organization Policy gcp.restrictServiceUsage (or restricting which APIs can be enabled) blocks unapproved services across the whole org or folder, whatever the developer\'s role.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Deployment Manager templates are deprecated and easy to bypass. B) email approvals are not enforcement. D) DNS zones do not stop service deployment.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"72","question":"You must ensure secure storage for encryption keys and secrets. What service should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud SQL with strong password protection","B":"Cloud Storage with KMS-managed object labels","C":"Cloud Key Management Service with IAM permissions % — willy","D":"BigQuery tables encrypted using Dataflow masking RA)"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cloud KMS stores and manages encryption keys (software, HSM or external through EKM), with IAM-controlled use and audit logs. Application secrets themselves belong in Secret Manager, which is encrypted with KMS.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) a database password is not a key management system. B) object labels are metadata, not protection. D) Dataflow masking is a data transformation, not key storage.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"73","question":"What is the main benefit of Resource Hierarchy (Organization > Folder > Project) in GCP?","options":{"A":"It provides a billing dashboard across all zones","B":"It allows centralized IAM and policy enforcement","C":"It enables each developer to access all projects","D":"It supports assigning different quotas to every region"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. IAM policies and Organization Policies set on the organization or folder are inherited by every project below. The hierarchy therefore gives central governance with exceptions where needed.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) billing dashboards are not the purpose. C) inheritance does not grant everyone access. D) quotas are per project and region, not driven by the hierarchy.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"74","question":"A solution must process and transform petabytes of structured data. Which tool should you use?","options":{"A":"Cloud SQL with sharded data pipelines","B":"Datastore with batch ingestion jobs every week","C":"BigQuery for serverless petabyte-scale analytics","D":"Cloud Functions with Memcached storage for compute"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. BigQuery: a serverless analytic warehouse that scales to petabytes without provisioning clusters.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• SQL over columns; the engine splits the scan across thousands of slots.\\n• You transform with SQL, scheduled queries, BigQuery Data Transfer, or Dataform.\\n• You pay storage + bytes scanned (on-demand) or slots (editions/reservations).\\n\\nExam tip: “petabytes” + “structured” + analytics/transform → BigQuery.\\nDo not confuse with: Datastore/Firestore (document NoSQL, not analytic PB), Cloud SQL (relational at TB scale, not PB), Cloud Functions + Memcached (request/cache, not massive ETL).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"75","question":"You need to ensure that only a specific group of users can manage Compute Engine instances in a project. Which approach aligns best with the principle of least privilege?","options":{"A":"Assign all users the Owner role at the project level","B":"Assign the Compute Admin role only to the group at the project level","C":"Assign the Editor role to each user individually in the group","D":"Assign the Compute Admin role to everyone in the organization s 1S"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Compute Admin role grants the exact permissions needed to manage instances without providing broader access like Editor or Owner roles. Assigning it to the group ensures manageability and enforces least privilege."},{"id":"77","question":"A client must meet GDPR requirements for data deletion. What Google Cloud feature Supports secure and permanent deletion of data?","options":{"A":"Using Data Deletion Requests via Cloud DLP","B":"Disabling access logging for reduced exposure","C":"Retaining logs in Stackdriver indefinitely %, BS","D":"Backing up data to a regional bucket 11. - Mila"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud DLP (Data Loss Prevention) supports mechanisms to identify and redact sensitive data. It also enables organizations to programmatically request data deletions to comply with privacy regulations like GDPR."},{"id":"79","question":"Your organization wants to prevent confidential data from leaving its boundary ia emails or logs. What GCP service can be used?","options":{"A":"Cloud Scheduler to delete logs regularly","B":"Stackdriver to generate warnings","C":"Dataflow to cleanse logs of data Qs","D":"Cloud DLP to inspect and redact sensitive content S “ani"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cloud DLP allows scanning, identifying, and redacting sensitive data (like PII, credit card numbers) before it’s logged or sent externally, helping meet compliance goals."},{"id":"80","question":"You must meet HIPAA compliance and log access to healthcare data in GCP. What feature should be enabled?","options":{"A":"Disable all logging for privacy","B":"Enable Cloud Audit Logs for data access events","C":"Share data with external reviewers for transparency","D":"Use standard Cloud Logging with minimal metadata \\"y"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Audit Logs track who accessed what data and when, which is essential for HIPAA compliance and forensic investigations."},{"id":"81","question":"A security audit revealed that multiple users had broad permissions on GCP projects. How can this be corrected?","options":{"A":"Remove all users and assign only Owner roles","B":"Use IAM Recommender to reduce roles to least privilege","C":"Grant all users the Security Reviewer role","D":"Add a VPC firewall rule to restrict user traffic Senay, \\"4"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IAM Recommender analyzes actual usage and suggests reduced permissions, helping enforce least privilege while maintaining functionality."},{"id":"82","question":"Which GCP tool helps detect misconfigured resources that might violate security policies?","options":{"A":"Cloud Trace to visualize network performance","B":"Cloud DNS to filter invalid domains","C":"Security Command Center to detect misconfigurations seal ites “ef Ts a s","D":"Cloud Tasks to audit user access"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Security Command Center scans for vulnerabilities, policy violations, and misconfigurations across GCP resources, offering actionable security insights."},{"id":"83","question":"What is the most secure way to store and manage API keys and credentials in GCP?","options":{"A":"Store keys in a Cloud Storage bucket","B":"Use metadata server to inject secrets into VMs","C":"Use Secret Manager with IAM policies","D":"Hardcode secrets in the application codebase qull 4 WL] | “ai"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Secret Manager is designed for securely storing secrets like API keys. It integrates with IAM to enforce access control and supports audit logging."},{"id":"84","question":"Which mechanism ensures secure communication between services in GCP?","options":{"A":"Enabling mutual TLS and Identity-Aware Proxy","B":"Disabling firewall rules for internal IP ranges","C":"Relying on public IPs for service-to-service communication","D":"Using legacy network configurations for flexibility heaaet an naw” a SY"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mutual TLS (mTLS) ensures that both parties are authenticated, while Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP) enables access control at the application layer, together enforcing secure service communication."},{"id":"85","question":"You need to implement fine-grained access control on BigQuery datasets. What is the best option?","options":{"A":"Assign BigQuery Admin at project level","B":"Use authorized views or column-level access policies","C":"Enable dataset sharing with everyone in the domain","D":"Remove all access controls for performance spe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Authorized views and column-level security allow precise control over what data is exposed, aligning with the principle of least privilege."},{"id":"86","question":"Which method helps ensure compliance with local data residency regulations?","options":{"A":"Store all backups in multi-regional storage","B":"Use region-specific storage for sensitive data","C":"Configure cross-region replication","D":"Export data to external cold storage les avila, _— * ew TT NK Gate y* poe oS Ts Oe SY"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Storing data in specific GCP regions ensures that it remains within jurisdictional boundaries, aiding compliance with residency laws like GDPR or CCPA."},{"id":"87","question":"What is the recommended way to prevent privilege escalation via IAM?","options":{"A":"Grant Service Account Token Creator to all users","B":"Allow users to create custom roles freely","C":"Restrict role binding using organization policy constraints","D":"Assign inherited roles from parent folders"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Organization policy constraints can restrict which roles are assignable, preventing users from escalating privileges through role misconfiguration."},{"id":"89","question":"To verify that only the required ports are exposed to the internet on VM instances, what should be implemented?","options":{"A":"Assign Editor role for better control","B":"Use Cloud Run for all workloads","C":"Configure Firewall Rules with least privilege","D":"Enable public IPs for external access"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Firewall Rules can be configured to only allow access to specific ports and IP ranges, reducing the attack surface for VMs."},{"id":"90","question":"How can you prevent service accounts from being used outside their designated VPC service perimeter?","options":{"A":"Add logging to all service accounts manually","B":"Enable Cloud Logging and alert on usage spikes","C":"Use VPC Service Controls with access levels","D":"Grant project-wide Owner permissions to users"},"correct":"C","explanation":"VPC Service Controls support defining access levels and perimeters, which can prevent unauthorized data movement across boundaries."},{"id":"91","question":"Which IAM practice reduces the risk of unauthorized privilege escalation?","options":{"A":"Assign Owner to all service accounts by default","B":"Use inherited roles across entire organization","C":"Allow developers to modify their own IAM roles","D":"Use predefined roles instead of custom roles 7 TTA"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Predefined roles are managed by Google, regularly updated for least privilege. Custom roles can be misconfigured to grant excess permissions."},{"id":"92","question":"A customer wants to encrypt data before storing it in Cloud Storage. What is the best approach?","options":{"A":"Use client-side encryption before data upload","B":"Use Dataflow to delete data after upload","C":"Rely only on Google-managed encryption keys","D":"Use Data Studio to store encrypted versions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Client-side encryption ensures data is encrypted before it enters the cloud, giving customers full control over key management and access."},{"id":"93","question":"Which service would you use to manage and rotate encryption keys manually?","options":{"A":"Secret Manager with static expiration dates","B":"Cloud KMS with scheduled key rotation","C":"Cloud SQL with user-managed SSL certs","D":"BigQuery with auto key deletion enabled"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud KMS supports automatic rotation on a schedule (a rotation period) and manual rotation on demand. New key versions encrypt new data, and old versions stay available to decrypt.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Secret Manager stores secrets; it does not rotate encryption keys. C) SSL certificates are not encryption keys for data at rest. D) BigQuery has no \'auto key deletion\' feature.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"94","question":"What feature should be enabled to track who is accessing specific Google Cloud resources?","options":{"A":"Disable logs for security reasons only","B":"Enable log deletion to reduce storage costs","C":"Enable Cloud Audit Logs for resource access","D":"Store logs in a public-access Cloud Storage bucket Bi)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud Audit Logs capture all access and changes to resources, helping maintain an audit trail for compliance and investigations."},{"id":"95","question":"What Google Cloud feature helps detect policy violations in real time?","options":{"A":"Use Data Fusion to automate workflows","B":"Use Security Command Center for violations","C":"Use Memorystore to log all activities","D":"Use App Engine with default permissions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Security Command Center provides actionable insights, detects threats, and flags policy misconfigurations as they occur."},{"id":"96","question":"Which method can prevent unauthorized users from binding powerful roles?","options":{"A":"Assign Admin rights to all developers","B":"Allow users to edit IAM policies freely","C":"Set firewall rules to deny IAM binding","D":"Apply IAM policy constraints at org level"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Organization policies / custom constraints on IAM at the organization level (and deny policies).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Custom org policy on `AllowPolicy`: DENY if the binding is `roles/owner` or `roles/editor`.\\n• Managed constraints: do not give basic roles to default SAs; limit member domains.\\n• Separate `resourcemanager.projects.setIamPolicy` (Security Admin only).\\n\\nExam tip: \\"prevent binding powerful roles\\" → IAM policy constraints at org.\\nDo not confuse with: firewall (does not understand IAM), \\"let everyone edit IAM\\", Admin to all devs.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"97","question":"What strategy helps protect data-in- transit in GCP services?","options":{"A":"Only use static IPs for all communication","B":"Send data unencrypted for faster delivery","C":"Enable TLS encryption for all traffic","D":"Compress all data to reduce packet size qull “1Ww* % villa, TT] A) %"},"correct":"C","explanation":"TLS ensures data is encrypted while being transmitted across services, reducing the risk of data leakage or interception."},{"id":"98","question":"Which solution prevents accidental public sharing of storage buckets?","options":{"A":"Use organization policy to block public access","B":"Add all users as bucket editors for control","C":"Set lifecycle policies to auto-delete objects","D":"Disable object versioning to simplify usage"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Resource hierarchy enables policy and access inheritance, letting organizations centralize control over security and billing."},{"id":"101","question":"A client wants a detailed report on network access. Which GCP service is best?","options":{"A":"Use Data Catalog to manage permissions","B":"Use App Engine logging for this purpose","C":"Use VPC Flow Logs with Log Analytics","D":"Use Pub/Sub to send data summaries 1533 TrKK\\\\ Gy ee “1Ww*"},"correct":"C","explanation":"VPC Flow Logs collect information about traffic to and from VM instances, which can be analyzed using Log Analytics for deeper insights."},{"id":"104","question":"What is the benefit of using Identity- Aware Proxy (IAP)?","options":{"A":"Allows unauthenticated access to apps","B":"Enables secure access to web applications","C":"Makes services publicly available by default","D":"Requires public IP for all applications"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Identity-Aware Proxy authenticates and authorizes each request with Google identity and context-aware rules before it reaches the app. Web apps (and TCP/SSH via IAP TCP forwarding) are accessible securely without a VPN or public exposure.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) and C) it does the opposite of unauthenticated or public access. D) IAP removes the need for public IPs on backends.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"105","question":"Which setting enforces HTTPS traffic for all users accessing App Engine?","options":{"A":"Add custom domains without SSL enabled","B":"Use firewall rules to filter HTTP requests","C":"Force HTTPS redirect in App Engine settings","D":"Allow both HTTP and FTP protocols by default 1 : ii"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Force an HTTP→HTTPS redirect on the app: handlers with `secure: always` in `app.yaml` (standard) or the \\"Strict-Transport-Security\\" / HTTPS-only option in settings.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• App Engine terminates TLS with managed certificates (custom domain too).\\n• A request to `http://` gets a 301/302 redirect to `https://`.\\n• The App Engine firewall does not replace TLS: it filters IPs, not the scheme.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"enforce HTTPS\\" + App Engine → force HTTPS redirect.\\nDo not confuse with: custom domain without SSL, allowing HTTP+FTP.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"106","question":"A company wants to minimize impact if credentials are compromised. What is recommended?","options":{"A":"Create one key for multiple services always","B":"Disable key expiration to avoid outages","C":"Use short-lived service account tokens","D":"Store static keys in GitHub repositories"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Short-lived credentials (OAuth access tokens valid about an hour from impersonation, metadata server tokens or Workload Identity Federation) expire quickly, so a leaked token gives an attacker only a brief window.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) one key for many services widens the blast radius. B) non-expiring keys stay valuable forever. D) keys in GitHub are publicly leaked.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"107","question":"Which is the best way to prevent lateral movement across GCP services?","options":{"A":"Grant all permissions at the organization level","B":"Use Cloud Functions to auto-create roles","C":"Share service accounts across environments MEDy, co","D":"Define VPC Service Control perimeters Sy Saeae\xae “Ss Ts"},"correct":"D","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: D. VPC Service Controls perimeters restrict which projects and networks can reach protected Google APIs and data. Compromised credentials or workloads cannot pivot to resources in other perimeters.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) organization-wide permissions maximize lateral movement. B) auto-created roles add risk. C) shared service accounts across environments link them together.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"114","question":"To prevent data exfiltration, what should be implemented?","options":{"A":"Grant cross-project access using wildcard roles","B":"Remove all access logging for storage buckets","C":"Set VPC Service Controls for sensitive APIs -","D":"Disable firewall rules to restrict all traffic Mes %, WL] A) “11Ww*"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. VPC Service Controls around sensitive APIs (Cloud Storage, BigQuery and others) block copying data to projects outside the perimeter. They are the primary control against data exfiltration on Google Cloud.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) wildcard roles widen access. B) removing logs hides exfiltration. D) disabling firewall rules does not restrict API-level data movement.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"116","question":"Which GCP feature helps ensure compliance by managing resource configurations?","options":{"A":"Use Config Validator to enforce policies","B":"Use Cloud Functions to delete changes daily","C":"Rely on GCS for manual compliance reporting","D":"Disable Admin API to block unauthorized users Baby"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Config Validator enables enforcement of constraints on GCP resources, elping ensure they comply with organizational policies."},{"id":"117","question":"A developer needs access to deploy but not delete GKE clusters. What role helps?","options":{"A":"GKE Admin with full permissions included","B":"Project Owner for deployment capabilities","C":"Compute Admin role with override policy","D":"Custom role with deploy-only permissions \xa7"},"correct":"D","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: D. A custom IAM role with only the needed permissions (for example container.clusters.get, update and create, but not container.clusters.delete) follows least privilege when no predefined role matches.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) and C) admin roles include delete. B) Project Owner can do everything.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"118","question":"What helps enforce encryption requirements across GCP storage?","options":{"A":"Publish IAM policies to user-facing portals","B":"Disable KMS to reduce storage cost impact","C":"Apply org policy requiring CMEK usage","D":"Configure firewall to allow encrypted traffic"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Organization policies can require the use of CMEK for storage services, ensuring encryption is enforced across the environment."},{"id":"119","question":"You want to ensure only designated users can create service accounts. What helps?","options":{"A":"Set allowAllUsers on service account objects","B":"Assign Editor role across all active projects","C":"Restrict iam.serviceAccounts.create via IAM","D":"Enable auto-create service accounts for users ?, ~ “1nWw*"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. You restrict the `iam.serviceAccounts.create` permission (Service Account Admin / Creator role) to that group. Nobody else has it — in particular, do not give Project Editor to everyone.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Editor includes creating SAs and often grants them wide roles: that is the opposite.\\n• Org policy `iam.disableServiceAccountCreation` (or managed equivalent) as a guardrail, with an exception on the platform project.\\n• Whoever creates the SA does not have to be Token Creator / Key Admin.\\n\\nExam tip: least privilege on `iam.serviceAccounts.create`.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"121","question":"Which method helps prevent excessive permission granting in IAM?","options":{"A":"Use project-wide inherited roles for everyone","B":"Implement least privilege via IAM Recommender","C":"Give users both Owner and Editor roles always","D":"Disable logs that show permission violations il anak es S Ti a SY"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Continuous least privilege with IAM Recommender: it reviews usage and lowers roles (or generates a narrower custom role).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Insights on unused permissions (~90 days).\\n• Apply the recommendation or a smaller equivalent role.\\n• Combine with groups, predefined roles, and not inheriting Editor on the org.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"excessive permission granting\\" → IAM Recommender / least privilege.\\nDo not confuse with: Owner+Editor to everyone, disabling violation logs, inherited roles for everybody.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"122","question":"To comply with data retention regulations, what should you enable?","options":{"A":"Delete all data after 24 hours regardless","B":"Enable Object Versioning and Retention Policy","C":"Use Public ACLs with monthly cleanup script","D":"Set IAM to allow deletion by all authenticated"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retention policies enforce data preservation for a specified period, and versioning prevents permanent deletion before expiry."},{"id":"125","question":"To prevent hardcoded secrets in code, what is the best GCP feature?","options":{"A":"Use environment variables with GitHub repos","B":"Store secrets in public buckets using naming","C":"Use Secret Manager with IAM-controlled access","D":"Define secrets in shared shell script templates"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Secret Manager with IAM: the app fetches the secret at runtime; the repo does not contain it.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• The Cloud Run/GKE/VM SA has `secretmanager.secretAccessor`.\\n• Injection: Secret Manager volume on GKE, referenced env var on Cloud Run, or API client.\\n• Rotation = new version; the code asks for `latest` or an alias.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"prevent hardcoded secrets\\" → Secret Manager + IAM.\\nDo not confuse with: env vars in a GitHub repo, public buckets, shared scripts.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"126","question":"How do you enforce secure image usage in GKE workloads?","options":{"A":"Enable unrestricted image pulls always","B":"Use Binary Authorization with signed images","C":"Allow Docker Hub for all cluster workloads","D":"Disable container scanning for flexibility sly, OTK"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Binary Authorization requires images to be signed or attested (for example after Artifact Analysis vulnerability scanning in your CI) before GKE admits them. Unsigned or untrusted images are blocked at deploy time.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) and C) unrestricted pulls, including from Docker Hub, allow arbitrary images. D) disabling scanning removes a security signal.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"127","question":"You want to restrict GCS access based on network identity. What do you use?","options":{"A":"Use signed URLs shared over public sites","B":"Use lifecycle policies to delete objects","C":"Use IAM Conditions with request attributes","D":"Grant storage access to anonymous users"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IAM Conditions allow access control based on attributes like IP address or request time, aligning with network-based policies."},{"id":"129","question":"To support internal audit, what logging feature should be enabled?","options":{"A":"Disable logging to reduce log size","B":"Enable Data Access audit logging","C":"Allow logs to be stored on user devices","D":"Configure firewall to collect log data"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Data Access logs capture reads and writes to data resources, which is crucial for internal audits and compliance validation."},{"id":"131","question":"A customer needs to meet FedRAMP High compliance. What helps achieve this?","options":{"A":"Use unmanaged public repositories for source","B":"Deploy only to Google Cloud Assured Workloads","C":"Rely on standard multi-tenant services","D":"Create personal accounts for audit tracking"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Assured Workloads enforces compliance with FedRAMP, CJIS, and other frameworks by restricting resources to compliant regions."},{"id":"137","question":"What setting helps enforce separation of duties in a GCP environment?","options":{"A":"Assign Owner role to all engineers equally","B":"Use IAM role boundaries with least privilege","C":"Disable audit logs for performance tracking","D":"Allow anonymous access for development"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IAM role boundaries and least privilege access ensure that users only perform tasks aligned with their job responsibilities."},{"id":"138","question":"A customer wants to ensure secrets are not logged in GCP. What is the solution?","options":{"A":"Allow unrestricted write access to Cloud Logs","B":"Use Cloud DLP to scan and redact secrets","C":"Export logs to unsecured local machines daily","D":"Store plain text passwords in audit trails"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Sensitive Data Protection (Cloud DLP) to inspect and redact secrets/PII in the log pipeline, plus not logging them at the source.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• InfoTypes for credentials, keys, passwords; de-identify before the sink.\\n• Log Router exclusion filters for known patterns.\\n• Secret Manager: the app does not print the value.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"secrets are not logged\\" → DLP redact.\\nDo not confuse with: Policy Controller/OPA (that was the original explanation, a different topic), logs to laptops, passwords in audit trails.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"141","question":"Which helps verify infrastructure complies with security standards?","options":{"A":"Use NAT gateways to scan traffic content","B":"Enable Security Health Analytics scans","C":"Assign Editor role to cloud users always","D":"Run unmanaged code on production clusters"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Security Health Analytics, a built-in Security Command Center service, continuously scans for misconfigurations (public buckets, open firewalls, missing MFA and similar) and maps findings to standards such as CIS, PCI DSS and NIST.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) NAT gateways do not inspect content. C) broad Editor roles weaken compliance. D) unmanaged code in production is a risk, not a check.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"142","question":"What prevents external IP usage ina highly secure network?","options":{"A":"Create default routes to all public IPs","B":"Assign global access to VPC subnets","C":"Use IAP to tunnel public services through","D":"Apply org policy denying external IPs qull “1nw* WALL A)"},"correct":"D","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: D. The Organization Policy constraint compute.vmExternalIpAccess (set to deny all, or an allowlist) prevents VMs from getting external IPs. Outbound traffic can go through Cloud NAT, and admin access through IAP.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) and B) default routes to public IPs and global subnet access increase exposure. C) IAP TCP forwarding is how you reach VMs without external IPs, but it does not prevent external IPs from being assigned.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"143","question":"How can you reduce the impact of leaked service account keys?","options":{"A":"Never rotate service account keys after use","B":"Enable full API access for all accounts","C":"Use key expiration with limited duration","D":"Store key backups in public Cloud Storage"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. If keys are unavoidable, the iam.serviceAccountKeyExpiryHours org policy (or regular rotation) limits key lifetime, so a leaked key soon stops working. Even better is to avoid keys entirely with Workload Identity Federation or impersonation.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) never rotating keys leaves them valid indefinitely. B) full API access maximizes the blast radius. D) public buckets leak keys.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"144","question":"Which IAM practice is ideal for temporary project access?","options":{"A":"Assign permanent roles without review cycles","B":"Grant Editor at the organization level","C":"Use IAM Conditions for time-bound grants","D":"Add credentials to shared Google Sheets"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. An IAM Condition with an expiry time on the role binding grants temporary access that revokes itself. Privileged Access Manager adds just-in-time request and approval workflows on top.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) permanent roles with no review drift into over-privilege. B) organization-level Editor is far too broad. D) credentials in shared sheets are a leak.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"145","question":"You want to monitor for unusual IAM activity. What helps?","options":{"A":"Remove all logging to increase system speed","B":"Disable role assignments for service accounts","C":"Use Cloud Logging with alerts on anomalies","D":"Use custom DNS to detect network attacks po siset’ “iw"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cloud Logging (audit logs) + alerts: log-based metrics or alerting policies on `SetIamPolicy`, privileged roles, or anomalous spikes.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Filter: `protoPayload.methodName=\\"SetIamPolicy\\"` or `roles/owner`.\\n• Metric + threshold, or log sink to Pub/Sub / Chronicle / SCC ETD.\\n• Notification channels (PagerDuty, Slack) with high severity.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"unusual IAM activity\\" → Logging + alerts.\\nDo not confuse with: removing logs, custom DNS as IDS, blindly disabling SA roles.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"146","question":"To ensure sensitive logs are not publicly accessible, what is best?","options":{"A":"Store logs in a bucket with public read set","B":"Apply bucket-level IAM to restrict access","C":"Use signed URLs shared via public domains","D":"Allow Viewer access for all Google users"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. IAM at the bucket (or log bucket) level: only security/ops identities read; never `allUsers` or \\"all Google users\\".\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Sink to a bucket or log bucket in the security project.\\n• Uniform bucket-level access + Public Access Prevention.\\n• Roles: `roles/storage.objectViewer` or `roles/logging.viewAccessor` to a group, not `roles/viewer` of the whole Google org.\\n\\nExam tip: restrict, do not widen to allGoogleUsers.\\nDo not confuse with: signed URLs on public domains, Viewer to all Google accounts.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"149","question":"Your organization is running a batch data processing job that consistently uses only 50% of the allocated vCPUs. What is the best way to optimize cost without impacting performance?","options":{"A":"Migrate to a region with lower network egress costs","B":"Replace VM instances with larger persistent disks","C":"Resize the VM instances to smaller machine types","D":"Add autoscaling to increase instance capacity during Ny is fe WL] 11; “Wwe A; aa execution"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Resizing VM instances to smaller machine types helps reduce underutilized CPU resources, which directly reduces cost. Since only 50% of vCPUs are used, a smaller machine will meet processing needs efficiently without overprovisioning."},{"id":"150","question":"A service-level agreement (SLA) requires 99.99% uptime for an e-commerce platform. hich architecture design principle best supports this SLA?","options":{"A":"Minimize logging for improved throughput","B":"Use multiple zones with health checks for failover","C":"Schedule periodic restarts of all VMs to refresh instances * 1","D":"Centralize all components in a single availability zone & =. A,\\" 4, Cs ae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Designing for high availability by using multiple zones ensures that if one zone fails, the system can continue operating from another. Combined with health checks, this strategy supports achieving the 99.99% SLA target."},{"id":"151","question":"Your finance department needs monthly reports on actual versus budgeted cloud spend. Which solution should you implement in GCP to provide this?","options":{"A":"Enable Compute Engine resource quotas","B":"Export Stackdriver logs to BigQuery","C":"Use Pub/Sub to monitor budget usage","D":"Set up Budget alerts and export billing data to BigQuery wey"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Exporting billing data to BigQuery and setting up Budget alerts allows detailed cost analysis and forecasting. Finance teams can compare actual usage with budgeted values through scheduled queries and dashboards."},{"id":"152","question":"Acompany’s Cloud Storage usage is increasing rapidly due to frequent backups. hat can be done to reduce storage costs without losing data?","options":{"A":"Use Nearline Storage for active transactional data","B":"Enable object versioning on all buckets","C":"Move older backups to Coldline or Archive Storage","D":"Increase bucket size and enable faster replication Me"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Coldline and Archive storage classes are ideal for infrequent access, such as backups. Migrating older data to these tiers significantly lowers storage costs while maintaining data availability when needed."},{"id":"153","question":"A performance monitoring tool shows high latency in one of your services. What is the most effective first step in identifying the bottleneck?","options":{"A":"Analyze Cloud Trace to find slow requests","B":"Restart the instances serving that service","C":"Increase CPU quota for the project","D":"Resize all VM instances to high-memory machines"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Trace provides distributed tracing of requests, allowing you to pinpoint the exact function, microservice, or dependency that is causing latency. This diagnostic step should precede any resource changes."},{"id":"154","question":"Your organization wants to track KPIs related to customer logins per second and error rates. What is the best approach in GCP?","options":{"A":"Use GCS logs and export to Pub/Sub","B":"Set up Cloud Functions with Bigtable triggers","C":"Use AI Platform to detect anomalies","D":"Create custom metrics and dashboards in Cloud Monitoring x % - = = S “aw"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cloud Monitoring supports creating custom metrics, which are ideal for tracking business KPIs like logins and error rates. Dashboards help visualize trends, and alerting can be configured to detect issues early."},{"id":"155","question":"Your web app on GKE is showing periodic performance degradation during peak traffic. What should you implement to address this?","options":{"A":"Enable horizontal pod autoscaling on the deployment","B":"Increase node disk size for all worker nodes","C":"Migrate the cluster to a different GCP region","D":"Disable liveness and readiness probes temporarily"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Horizontal Pod Autoscaling dynamically adjusts the number of pods based on CPU or custom metrics. This ensures sufficient capacity during traffic spikes, improving app responsiveness without overprovisioning."},{"id":"156","question":"Your team receives alerts daily for minor issues that don\'t require action. How can you reduce alert fatigue without ignoring important problems?","options":{"A":"Disable all non-critical alerting policies","B":"Set alert thresholds to trigger only on errors","C":"Adjust severity levels and use notification filters","D":"Remove logs from alert-generating services “aw “aw"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Using severity levels and filters allows engineers to receive high-severity alerts while suppressing noise from non-actionable warnings. This optimizes operational efficiency and avoids alert desensitization."},{"id":"157","question":"Your client wants to lower costs associated with Compute Engine instances running during non-business hours. Which approach is best?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Scheduler with Cloud Functions to stop instances nightly","B":"Apply autoscaling policies based on HTTP requests","C":"Convert instances into preemptible VMs","D":"Move workloads to a different region with lower costs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Using Cloud Scheduler and Functions enables automation to stop and restart instances based on defined schedules. This effectively reduces cost for non-critical resources outside business hours."},{"id":"158","question":"You\'ve noticed inconsistent latency in our microservices architecture. What\'s the most effective way to gather detailed performance insights?","options":{"A":"Enable GCS versioning for audit trail","B":"Use Cloud Trace for distributed tracing","C":"Scale up GKE cluster size","D":"Move microservices to separate regions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud Trace (distributed tracing): a waterfall per request across the services.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Shared trace ID; each hop adds spans.\\n• You see p95, outliers, and which dependency (DB, API, another pod) is taking time.\\n• Do not scale the cluster \\"just in case\\" or version a bucket: that does not diagnose.\\n\\nExam tip: \\"inconsistent latency\\" + microservices → Cloud Trace.\\nDo not confuse with: org policy on external IPs (that was the original explanation, from another question).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"159","question":"You need to optimize the cost of a BigQuery workload that queries a large table multiple times per day. What is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Partition the table by a non-queried column","B":"Export the table to CSV for offline queries","C":"Use table partitioning by frequently filtered field","D":"Replicate the table in each region for faster access By"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Partitioning BigQuery tables by frequently queried fields (like timestamp) helps scan less data, reducing cost and improving performance. Other options increase complexity or storage cost without performance gain."},{"id":"160","question":"A client wants near real-time insights from data ingestion without incurring high storage or compute costs. What is the most cost-effective design?","options":{"A":"Load data into BigQuery every 30 minutes via Dataflow","B":"Stream data into BigQuery and use scheduled queries","C":"Use Cloud SQL to buffer incoming data","D":"Store in Cloud Storage and process weekly using DataProc Say, %, LL _ ofa “aw Lao} z. % ) “ai"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Streaming into BigQuery combined with scheduled queries allows real-time processing with cost- effective compute. Other options involve higher latency or complexity for near real-time use cases."},{"id":"161","question":"Your solution must ensure that the application does not exceed its budget and reacts to cost surges. Which approach meets the requirement?","options":{"A":"Assign IAM policies to billing admin roles","B":"Configure budget alerts and apply programmatic enforcement","C":"Enable autoscaling for all services regardless of load Sees","D":"Use service accounts to monitor resource creation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Setting up budget alerts and enforcing automated actions (via scripts or policy enforcement tools) allows for proactive budget management. IAM roles or autoscaling alone don’t address cost control."},{"id":"162","question":"A legacy app is deployed on Compute Engine with constant but low usage. How can ou reduce cost without modifying the app?","options":{"A":"Replace instances with smaller machine types","B":"Convert the app to use App Engine flexible environment","C":"Use GPUs to offload processing","D":"Move it to a separate VPC with reduced egress charges 12: “iw"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Downsizing machine types maintains the same architecture while reducing compute cost. Other options require re-architecting or won\'t yield meaningful savings."},{"id":"163","question":"To meet a 99.9% uptime SLA, which design should you use for a web app?","options":{"A":"Deploy in a single zone with startup probes","B":"Use local SSDs to improve disk IOPS","C":"Host static content on Cloud Storage only al as s","D":"Use regional GKE clusters with multi-zone nodes Ef} Cy ret"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Multi-zone regional deployments ensure high availability during zone failures. Startup probes, SSDs, or static hosting alone cannot guarantee SLA compliance across failures."},{"id":"164","question":"You need to measure business impact by tracking user conversion events. What GCP service best supports this need?","options":{"A":"Pub/Sub for stream processing of requests","B":"Cloud Monitoring with custom business metrics","C":"Dataflow with sliding window transforms","D":"VPC flow logs exported to BigQuery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Custom metrics in Cloud Monitoring allow you to track business KPIs like user conversion rates, login success, or purchase flows in near real-time with alerting and dashboards."},{"id":"165","question":"Your organization wants to reduce VM cost without sacrificing availability. Which recommendation helps achieve this?","options":{"A":"Use preemptible VMs in a managed instance group","B":"Disable autoscaling to reduce overuse","C":"Consolidate to a single large VM instance","D":"Schedule manual VM shutdowns daily \\"a"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Preemptible VMs are significantly cheaper and can be used in managed instance groups that automatically replace them upon eviction, maintaining availability with lower cost."},{"id":"166","question":"A system is experiencing increased latency due to request queuing. What is the most effective approach to improve performance?","options":{"A":"Schedule requests to run outside peak hours","B":"Use smaller instance types for better cost ratio","C":"Deploy services in more regions","D":"Increase instance capacity with autoscaling enabled Oy \xa310. 2 4 Fe"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoscaling adds instances when demand increases, reducing queuing and improving latency. Scheduling or smaller instances don’t address the capacity issue."},{"id":"167","question":"A GCP workload intermittently exceeds expected CPU usage, affecting other services. How should you monitor this proactively?","options":{"A":"Enable billing export to BigQuery","B":"Create Cloud Monitoring alerting policies for CPU thresholds","C":"Use Cloud Armor to rate-limit the API %, Be","D":"Schedule daily reports using BigQuery A) %"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Monitoring alerting policies detect and notify admins when CPU thresholds are exceeded, enabling proactive responses. Billing or rate-limiting do not address resource contention."},{"id":"168","question":"To reduce storage costs, a company wants to retain only the last 30 days of logs. hat is the best method to achieve this?","options":{"A":"Set up a retention policy on the log bucket","B":"Export logs to BigQuery with expiration set","C":"Use Stackdriver logging without filters BAS","D":"Archive logs to Coldline storage without retention ‘ 7"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Configure the log bucket retention period (30 days); Cloud Logging automatically deletes older logs.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Logs are stored in log buckets: _Required, _Default, and custom buckets.\\n• _Default retains 30 days by default; retention on _Default and custom buckets is configurable from 1 to 3650 days (beyond 30 days has a cost).\\n• _Required (Admin Activity audit logs, etc.) is fixed: 400 days, at no charge.\\n• To reduce ingest, also use exclusion filters.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabretain only last N days\xbb → log bucket retention.\\nDo not confuse with: export to BigQuery with expiration (you duplicate storage), \xabStackdriver with no filters\xbb (limits nothing and raises cost), Coldline without retention (does not delete).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"169","question":"Which strategy ensures cloud costs are predictable while avoiding service disruption?","options":{"A":"Enable sustained use discounts and avoid autoscaling","B":"Use committed use contracts and monitor utilization","C":"Use ephemeral disks and restrict instance access AM se","D":"Apply resource quotas and deny usage to developers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Use committed use discounts (CUDs) for the baseline load and monitor their utilization.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You commit for 1 or 3 years (resource-based: vCPU/memory; or spend-based/flexible: hourly spend) in exchange for significant discounts.\\n• You pay the commitment even if you do not use it → monitor utilization in Billing CUD reports.\\n• Usage above the commitment is billed on-demand, so the service is not interrupted.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabpredictable costs\xbb + \xabavoid disruption\xbb → CUDs + monitoring.\\nDo not confuse with: SUDs without autoscaling (do not make cost predictable), quotas that deny use to developers (cause interruptions), ephemeral disks (irrelevant).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"171","question":"A service requires fast recovery from outages. What architecture improves recovery time objective (RTO) effectively?","options":{"A":"Deploy active-active services across multiple zones","B":"Schedule snapshot backups weekly at midnight","C":"Store VM images in different cloud regions","D":"Use a larger instance type in the same zone Slay, \\"\\", = 2 % TTY = “nw"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Active-active architecture ensures zero downtime during failures, minimizing RTO. Snapshots and larger instances don’t offer immediate recovery capabilities."},{"id":"172","question":"You want to track the cost of each GKE namespace separately. What is the recommended GCP solution?","options":{"A":"Create a separate project for every namespace","B":"Use labels for resources and analyze billing data","C":"Run separate Kubernetes clusters per namespace","D":"Configure network tags and export to Pub/Sub Pues oe THA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Applying labels allows resource-level cost tracking using BigQuery export or Billing Reports. Separate clusters or projects create complexity and are not scalable."},{"id":"173","question":"An application runs intensive machine learning workloads. How can you reduce cost without changing its structure?","options":{"A":"Convert the app to App Engine standard environment","B":"Schedule jobs only on weekends for lower pricing","C":"Migrate to larger general-purpose instances","D":"Use preemptible VMs for non-critical training jobs 77 od"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Preemptible VMs cost much less and are ideal for fault-tolerant, batch ML workloads. They don’t require code changes and reduce compute expenses significantly."},{"id":"174","question":"You want to ensure consistent performance for a database under variable load. What should you implement?","options":{"A":"Reserve IOPS using object storage metrics","B":"Choose zonal persistent disks with automatic resizing","C":"Use Cloud Storage with signed URL authentication & 7 avila, —7 _ (ot) CTT \\"y aes aS “1Ww*","D":"Archive old data using Dataflow and scheduler"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Zonal persistent disks with autosizing ensure consistent performance by adapting capacity based on workload. Other solutions are storage-tier dependent or archival focused."},{"id":"175","question":"Acloud team wants to manage long- term costs for unused resources. Which approach is best?","options":{"A":"Use Recommender API to find idle or underused assets","B":"Auto-increase quotas for projects with low usage","C":"Store all VM snapshots indefinitely for safety","D":"Replace all instances with higher-cost premium tiers Raf 75 selly Bee ‘ S Te s >"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Recommender API provides actionable insights on cost optimization, such as idle VMs or oversized resources. It enables proactive cleanup and budget management."},{"id":"176","question":"Your SLA requires 99.95% uptime. What must be part of the design to meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use one large VM with attached SSD disks","B":"Select preemptible instances to improve cost","C":"Deploy across multiple zones with load balancing","D":"Place database and app inthe same region only"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. A 99.95% target means no single zone can be a point of failure. Run instances in several zones (a regional MIG, regional GKE or regional Cloud SQL HA) behind a load balancer. Single-zone instances carry lower SLAs.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) one VM cannot meet 99.95%. B) preemptible (Spot) VMs can be reclaimed at any time. D) co-locating in one region is fine, but staying in one region does not by itself give multi-zone redundancy.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"177","question":"Asystem\'s average memory usage is below 30% across multiple zones. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Downsize VM instances and monitor impact","B":"Add more memory to all instances for reliability","C":"Increase instance count for redundancy 4, a","D":"Replace memory with GPUs for faster execution villa, RA) “11Ww*"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Consistently low memory use means the VMs are over-provisioned. Rightsize them to smaller or custom machine types (the Recommender suggests sizes) and monitor latency and errors after the change.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) adding memory increases waste. C) more instances increase cost without need. D) GPUs do not replace memory and are expensive.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"178","question":"You need to track time spent in each microservice for a user request. What is the most appropriate GCP tool?","options":{"A":"Cloud Functions with logs export","B":"Cloud Trace with custom annotations","C":"BigQuery scheduled query metrics","D":"Data Catalog with lineage tracking"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud Trace collects distributed traces (via OpenTelemetry), showing spans and latency for each service a request passes through. Custom spans and annotations add business context.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) function logs do not give per-request timing across services. C) scheduled BigQuery queries are batch analytics. D) Data Catalog (now Dataplex Universal Catalog) lineage tracks data, not requests.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"179","question":"You want to compare actual versus forecasted GCP spending by department. What is the best way to do this?","options":{"A":"Enable audit logs and summarize data quarterly","B":"Use labels and export billing data to BigQuery","C":"Allocate static budgets and avoid dynamic changes","D":"Use IAM policies to assign billing administrators"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Partitioning and clustering reduce scanned data per query. This significantly lowers BigQuery cost without affecting output."},{"id":"180","question":"Your SLA specifies a 1-hour response time for incidents. How can you ensure accountability across the team?","options":{"A":"Define escalation policies in Cloud Monitoring alerts","B":"Manually check logs when a service fails","C":"Schedule weekly team check-ins to discuss metrics % = = silly, _ * ew “aw DP icarst oa Ti Oe Sy","D":"Use Pub/Sub to queue incidents for later review"},"correct":"A","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Alert policies with notification channels and severity, routed to on-call rotations and escalation (for example through PagerDuty integration), ensure someone owns every incident within the 1-hour SLA.\\n\\nWhy not the others: B) manual log checks are reactive and unowned. C) weekly check-ins are too slow. D) queuing incidents for later review breaks the response-time commitment.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"181","question":"A developer notices fluctuating API performance. What\'s the first step to identifying root cause?","options":{"A":"Increase API replica count across the board","B":"Use Cloud Trace to identify latency hotspots","C":"Enable budget alerts to detect surges","D":"Switch API backend to Cloud Functions"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Diagnose before acting. Cloud Trace shows which span (database call, downstream API, cold start) adds latency, so you fix the real hotspot.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) adding replicas blindly may not help and costs money. C) budget alerts track spend. D) migrating the backend is a large change with no diagnosis behind it.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"182","question":"An analytics team wants historical insights on query cost. Which service should they use?","options":{"A":"Cloud SQL with read replicas","B":"BigQuery export with billing data in tables","C":"GCS versioning and archiving","D":"VPC Flow Logs for traffic analytics"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Export Cloud Billing data to BigQuery (standard or detailed usage cost tables). Combined with INFORMATION_SCHEMA.JOBS (bytes billed per query and user), it gives historical, queryable insight into query cost.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Cloud SQL replicas are for OLTP reads. C) GCS versioning is object history. D) VPC Flow Logs analyze network traffic, not query cost.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"183","question":"What is a key benefit of using custom metrics for KPIs in Cloud Monitoring?","options":{"A":"You can define specific business logic and thresholds","B":"You can avoid alerting and focus on storage only","C":"It avoids the need for IAM roles or service accounts","D":"Custom metrics cost less than standard system logs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Multi-zone deployment with load balancing provides fault tolerance and availability required to meet 99.95% SLA. Single-zone setups are vulnerable to regional outages."},{"id":"184","question":"A batch process is idle for 6 hours a day. What is a cost-efficient way to run this on Compute Engine?","options":{"A":"Increase vCPUs to complete faster","B":"Use Cloud Scheduler to shut down VMs off-hours","C":"Migrate to larger persistent disks","D":"Create multiple VPCs for traffic isolation ann lly avila, ll, TT pause"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Shutting down VMs when not in use prevents unnecessary billing. Scheduling via Cloud Scheduler ensures automation and consistency."},{"id":"185","question":"A team wants to avoid vendor lock-in while ensuring scalability. What is the best design principle?","options":{"A":"Use open-source frameworks and managed services","B":"Use proprietary APIs for cloud-native scaling","C":"Optimize for single-region deployment only","D":"Use monolithic apps hosted in Compute Engine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Open-source and managed services reduce lock-in while retaining portability and scalability. Avoiding proprietary APIs and tightly coupled systems supports this goal."},{"id":"186","question":"An e-commerce app must scale during sales events. What GCP strategy supports this without constant overprovisioning?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud Storage and migrate app to Python","B":"Add more load balancers to improve performance","C":"Schedule batch jobs in off-peak hours","D":"Implement autoscaling based on CPU or request load %, slay, \', = 2 % TIN “iw"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correct answer: D. Autoscaling based on CPU or request load (MIG, GKE, or serverless).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• It scales out when traffic rises and reduces instances when it falls → you pay only for what you need.\\n• For known peaks (e.g. Black Friday), combine with scaling schedules or a higher minimum instance count, because creating VMs takes time.\\n• Review regional quotas before the event.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabsales events\xbb + \xabwithout overprovisioning\xbb → autoscaling.\\nDo not confuse with: migrating to Python/Cloud Storage (nonsensical), more load balancers (one global LB already scales), batch off-peak (does not apply to user traffic).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"188","question":"You need to monitor how long an order processing system takes per transaction. hich approach helps most?","options":{"A":"Enable API gateway request validation","B":"Store data in cold storage for faster reads","C":"Use Cloud Trace to measure end-to-end latency","D":"Migrate from BigQuery to Cloud SQL for speed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud Trace helps measure and visualize transaction latency from initiation to completion. It\'s ideal for monitoring business-critical time-based KPIs."},{"id":"189","question":"What is the best way to reduce long- term Cloud Storage costs for archived audit logs?","options":{"A":"Use standard class and apply lifecycle retention","B":"Transition older logs to Archive class with rules","C":"Enable versioning and replicate across regions","D":"Store logs in persistent disks for compliance “iw"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Archive storage class is optimized for long- term, rarely accessed data like audit logs. Lifecycle rules automate transitions to minimize cost."},{"id":"190","question":"You want to measure how effectively our team resolves incidents. Which metric should you track?","options":{"A":"CPU utilization percentage","B":"Mean time to resolution (MTTR) for alerts","C":"Number of load-balanced backends 7s","D":"Number of users accessing logs per week"},"correct":"B","explanation":"MTTR is a key performance indicator for incident response efficiency. It helps evaluate the effectiveness of monitoring and team response strategies."},{"id":"191","question":"A finance team wants alerts when spending exceeds a threshold. What is the GCP-native solution?","options":{"A":"IAM budget viewer roles","B":"Budget alerts with email and Pub/Sub triggers","C":"Cloud DNS billing rules and log sinks","D":"Cloud Run triggers and billing history download"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GCP Budget alerts can be configured to trigger notifications or Pub/Sub messages when spending approaches or exceeds set thresholds."},{"id":"192","question":"What Cloud Monitoring metric indicates whether autoscaling is working as expected?","options":{"A":"Number of active instances during peak traffic","B":"Uptime checks passing without delay","C":"Load balancer IP reuse rate","D":"Static quota allocations for projects"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Monitoring active instances allows you to verify scaling activity during load changes. It ensures the autoscaler behaves as configured."},{"id":"193","question":"What GCP solution allows you to trace memory leaks in a Python web app?","options":{"A":"Stackdriver Logging with BigQuery export","B":"App Engine flexible environment autoscaler","C":"Cloud Profiler with memory heap snapshots","D":"Cloud Run concurrency monitoring"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Downsizing reduces cost without sacrificing performance. Monitoring ensures the app remains within acceptable memory thresholds post-change."},{"id":"194","question":"A GKE workload is overprovisioned. What change optimizes cost?","options":{"A":"Lower pod resource requests and set autoscaling thresholds","B":"Increase nodes to spread traffic","C":"Add more pods for higher latency","D":"Switch to on-demand GPUs for compute speed"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lowering unnecessary resource requests helps fit more pods per node and reduces cluster cost. Autoscaling ensures scaling when needed."},{"id":"195","question":"A service is failing intermittently, and logs are too noisy. What should you do first?","options":{"A":"Restart all services in the environment","B":"Disable non-critical alerting rules","C":"Filter logs and create severity-based alerts","D":"Schedule regular service restarts overnight"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Filtering logs and applying severity-based alerts helps focus on meaningful incidents and avoid alert fatigue while still maintaining visibility."},{"id":"196","question":"What KPI helps assess how often your system fails?","options":{"A":"User login rate","B":"Mean time between failures (MTBF)","C":"Log export volume sy,","D":"Percentage of preemptible VMs used 2.19.5"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. MTBF (mean time between failures): average operating time between one failure and the next. Higher MTBF = less frequent failures.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• MTBF = total operating time / number of failures.\\n• Together with MTTR it determines availability: ≈ MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR).\\n\\nExam tip: \xabhow often system fails\xbb → MTBF.\\nDo not confuse with: login rate (usage/business metric), log-export volume, % of preemptible VMs (cost).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"198","question":"What helps measure the impact of a Cloud Function on overall latency?","options":{"A":"Export logs to BigQuery","B":"Enable Cloud Trace to view request duration","C":"Attach debugger for live execution trace","D":"Upgrade to GKE for more observability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Trace can capture performance metrics like latency, helping pinpoint how a Cloud Function contributes to overall system delay."},{"id":"199","question":"You\'re evaluating cloud cost savings after applying autoscaling. What metric should you review?","options":{"A":"Total disk IOPS across projects","B":"Total user logins per instance","C":"Average VM utilization across autoscaled groups","D":"Number of deleted snapshots by policy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Average VM utilization shows how efficiently resources are used. Higher utilization after autoscaling means improved cost efficiency."},{"id":"200","question":"A dashboard shows a spike in memory usage for a backend service. What action helps most?","options":{"A":"Move the service to another availability zone","B":"Restart all services nightly","C":"Review code with Cloud Profiler and adjust memory usage","D":"Increase CPU quota and expand disk size % o"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud Profiler reveals code-level memory usage and leaks. Optimizing memory at the code level reduces overprovisioning and prevents instability."},{"id":"201","question":"A team needs to monitor SLA violations for an external dependency. What is the right method?","options":{"A":"Create uptime checks and alert on response failures","B":"Enable Cloud DNS logs and summarize usage","C":"Export IAM policy logs to Pub/Sub","D":"Schedule weekly log archive analysis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Uptime checks actively monitor endpoints and generate alerts on failures, helping track SLA adherence of external services."},{"id":"202","question":"A data pipeline fails every 4 hours due to timeouts. What helps improve its stability?","options":{"A":"Switch to preemptible instances","B":"Add retries and increase timeout thresholds","C":"Reduce pipeline steps to improve cost","D":"Move the pipeline to a new VPC aa Mes “ain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retry logic and optimized timeout settings increase fault tolerance for intermittent failures, improving pipeline reliability."},{"id":"203","question":"A service sends too many alerts at low severity. What strategy improves alert quality?","options":{"A":"Group alerts and apply severity thresholds","B":"Migrate services to Cloud Functions","C":"Turn off all monitoring during business hours","D":"Disable logging to reduce alert load 214\xb0 Fy rane? ~ “nw"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Group related alerts and apply severity thresholds so only what matters notifies urgently.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Assign severity (critical, error, warning) to each alerting policy and route to different channels (PagerDuty vs email/chat).\\n• Use duration windows and realistic thresholds so brief spikes do not alert.\\n• Prefer alerting on user-visible symptoms (SLOs, burn rate) instead of every cause.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabtoo many low-severity alerts\xbb / alert fatigue → group + severity.\\nDo not confuse with: migrating to Cloud Functions (does not change alerts), turning off monitoring or logging (you lose visibility).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"204","question":"A cost analysis shows high network egress from Cloud Storage. What can reduce charges?","options":{"A":"Increase Cloud NAT throughput","B":"Add signed URLs to public buckets","C":"Use Cloud CDN for frequently accessed content","D":"Move storage to persistent disks instead % Pea aS Ti s \'e"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Put Cloud CDN (on an external Application Load Balancer with a backend bucket) in front of frequently accessed content.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Content is cached at Google\'s edge, near users; cache hits do not leave the bucket.\\n• Cloud CDN cache egress is usually cheaper than Cloud Storage egress straight to the internet, and it also lowers latency.\\n• Configure Cache-Control/TTL to maximize the cache hit ratio.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabhigh egress\xbb + \xabfrequently accessed content\xbb → Cloud CDN.\\nDo not confuse with: Cloud NAT (outbound internet from private VMs), signed URLs (access control, do not reduce egress), persistent disks (do not serve public content and are more expensive).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"205","question":"What metric is most useful for understanding how quickly your team addresses incidents?","options":{"A":"Cloud Logging ingestion rate","B":"Mean time to acknowledge (MTTA)","C":"Stackdriver uptime check frequency","D":"Number of alerts disabled"},"correct":"B","explanation":"MTTA measures how fast alerts are acknowledged, which is a leading indicator of incident responsiveness and team efficiency."},{"id":"206","question":"Your app performance degrades under load. What should you do first?","options":{"A":"Add static IPs to all services","B":"Use Cloud Trace to identify slow dependencies SMI,","C":"Archive logs to save memory","D":"Deploy app in a single-zone VPC TTT Gy ee “1nWw*"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Use Cloud Trace to identify which dependencies (services, databases, external APIs) are slow.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Diagnose first: the trace shows which span grows when load increases.\\n• Then act on the real bottleneck (indexes, cache, scale that component).\\n\\nExam tip: \xabwhat should you do first\xbb + degradation → diagnose with Trace before changing infrastructure.\\nDo not confuse with: static IPs (do not affect performance), archiving logs (does not free app memory), single-zone (reduces availability).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"207","question":"A company needs to track spend by business unit. What’s the scalable GCP-native way?","options":{"A":"Use service accounts for each team","B":"Label resources and analyze billing via BigQuery","C":"Restrict IAM permissions for cost visibility 42)","D":"Export billing to Google Sheets daily sly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Labels allow logical grouping of resources. Combined with billing export to BigQuery, this enables cost attribution by team or function."},{"id":"209","question":"What is the best metric to track user- facing downtime of your public application?","options":{"A":"Number of log entries with warnings","B":"Uptime check failure rate over time","C":"Disk utilization percentage","D":"Average bytes sent per second “iw"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Uptime checks simulate user interactions and detect public downtime. This makes them the best indicator of real availability issues."},{"id":"210","question":"Your compute environment is underutilized 70% of the time. What is the best action?","options":{"A":"Use autoscaling and downsize instance types","B":"Disable monitoring agents for performance","C":"Increase network bandwidth for balance s 1","D":"Add disk snapshots to reduce latency 13: eS aii"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autoscaling and right-sizing instance types align resources with demand, improving utilization and reducing unnecessary cost."},{"id":"211","question":"A business KPI measures checkout success rate. What GCP solution can track this?","options":{"A":"VPC flow logs and export to BigQuery","B":"Cloud Armor for HTTP rule enforcement","C":"Custom metrics in Cloud Monitoring with alerting","D":"App Engine debug logs and daily review"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Emit the checkout success rate as a custom or log-based metric in Cloud Monitoring, chart it, define an SLO and alert on drops. That turns a business KPI into a monitored signal.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) VPC flow logs record network flows, not business outcomes. B) Cloud Armor is a WAF. D) reviewing debug logs daily is too slow and manual.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"212","question":"Which strategy reduces inter-zone latency in a multi-zone deployment?","options":{"A":"Move all services to one single zone","B":"Use zonal affinity and optimized load balancing","C":"Export traffic logs to BigQuery","D":"Schedule resource creation outside peak hours LL) RQ s hentes “Ss Ts"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Keeping traffic in the same zone when possible (zonal affinity, locality-aware load balancing such as the Service Mesh or GKE topology-aware routing) avoids unnecessary cross-zone hops, which lowers both latency and inter-zone egress cost.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) a single zone removes high availability. C) exporting logs does not change routing. D) scheduling resource creation is unrelated to latency.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"213","question":"Your SLAs include latency and availability metrics. What tools help validate these?","options":{"A":"Use Pub/Sub to track dropped messages","B":"Enable audit logs across all APIs","C":"Combine uptime checks with Cloud Trace","D":"Store logs in Archive and review quarterly"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Uptime checks probe availability from multiple global locations, and Cloud Trace measures request latency and its distribution. Together, plus SLO monitoring, they validate both SLA dimensions.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) dropped messages are a narrow metric. B) audit logs record who did what, not SLA performance. D) quarterly reviews of archived logs are far too late.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"214","question":"An engineer requests access to view cloud costs. What is the appropriate action?","options":{"A":"Make them a project owner temporarily","B":"Assign Billing Viewer IAM role on the billing account","C":"Share monthly reports manually as PDFs","D":"Create a duplicate billing account for read-only view"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Grant Billing Account Viewer (roles/billing.viewer) on the billing account. The engineer can see costs and reports and can change nothing, which is least privilege.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Project Owner is excessive. C) PDFs are manual and stale. D) a duplicate billing account does not show the real costs.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"215","question":"You want to forecast cloud spend growth based on trends. Which solution supports this?","options":{"A":"Use BigQuery billing export and Data Studio dashboards","B":"Enable firewall rules to isolate usage","C":"Configure multi-region deployments with no quotas","D":"Run manual queries monthly on Stackdriver logs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"BigQuery billing data supports time-series analysis, and Data Studio enables visual trend forecasting based on usage patterns."},{"id":"217","question":"What KPI best measures operational efficiency of your cloud environment?","options":{"A":"Number of support tickets created","B":"Total billing exports downloaded","C":"Cost per transaction or cost per user session","D":"Number of IAM policy bindings created qull “1nWw* Sy, “ay"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cost per transaction directly relates to efficiency by showing how much is spent to deliver value. It is a meaningful KPI in cloud ops."},{"id":"218","question":"What is a valid method to reduce GKE cost during low usage?","options":{"A":"Enable node auto-provisioning with autoscaler","B":"Add fixed memory reservations for all pods","C":"Move nodes to always-on standard class","D":"Allocate separate clusters for dev and prod"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Custom metrics let you define and track business-level outcomes, like conversion or checkout success, with dashboards and alerts."},{"id":"219","question":"What best describes a good practice for tracking service-level indicators (SLIs)?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud NAT for traffic shaping","B":"Create custom dashboards with availability metrics","C":"Increase disk quota to avoid alerts","D":"Disable alerts for minor latency variations end"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. SLIs (for example availability = good requests / total, or latency percentiles) should be computed from metrics and shown on Cloud Monitoring dashboards and SLOs, so trends and error budgets are visible.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) Cloud NAT is networking. C) raising disk quota to avoid alerts hides problems. D) suppressing latency alerts reduces visibility.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"220","question":"A team deploys services without cleanup. What policy helps control cost?","options":{"A":"Apply labels and use Recommender for cleanup","B":"Schedule snapshot duplication to all regions","C":"Enable persistent disks on all instances","D":"Restrict team access to billing console only"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Labels make cost tracking easy, and Recommender surfaces underused resources. This combination reduces waste and controls spending."},{"id":"221","question":"What metric best reflects app usability from a user\'s perspective?","options":{"A":"Percentage of disk usage by logging agents","B":"Average response time for successful requests","C":"Number of container restarts in Kubernetes","D":"Count of firewall rules across all projects 12):"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Average response time directly affects user experience. It reflects the system’s ability to serve requests quickly and efficiently."},{"id":"222","question":"A critical service must not drop below 99.95% uptime. What should be included in the design?","options":{"A":"Deploy on one large instance with persistent disk","B":"Use regional GKE clusters with autoscaling enabled","C":"Convert the app to preemptible Compute Engine VMs","D":"Limit service to a single private subnet for security"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Regional GKE with autoscaling supports multi- zone redundancy and demand-based resource management, crucial for SLA adherence."},{"id":"223","question":"What is the most appropriate strategy to ensure reliability when deploying a new microservice to production?","options":{"A":"Deploy the service to all instances simultaneously for consistency","B":"Use a rolling update strategy across instances to minimize downtime","C":"Force a blue/green switch to test service failover mechanisms","D":"Use random canary deployments without rollback mechanisms ."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A rolling update allows you to update instances gradually, reducing risk of downtime or system-wide failure. Canary deployments are often used, but a rolling update is broader and safer for most production releases."},{"id":"224","question":"Which of the following is a Google Cloud native service that enables monitoring, logging, and alerting?","options":{"A":"App Engine Task Queues","B":"GKE Autoscaler","C":"Cloud Operations suite","D":"Cloud Endpoints Gateway"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Cloud Operations suite (formerly Stackdriver) provides tools for observability, including logging, monitoring, tracing, error reporting, and alerting, and is essential for solution reliability."},{"id":"225","question":"A company uses multiple Compute Engine instances and wants to be notified when CPU usage exceeds 80% for more than 5 minutes. What should you configure?","options":{"A":"Use Identity-Aware Proxy with runtime alerting","B":"Write a custom Python script and run it via cron jobs","C":"Create a metric-based alerting policy in Cloud Monitoring yy TIT","D":"Enable autoscaling with predictive metrics in BigQuery\xe9 3: Cus _ \xa2,** ra ( Pte “aw"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud Monitoring allows you to define alert policies on metrics like CPU utilization with thresholds and notification channels."},{"id":"226","question":"Which strategy best ensures zero downtime deployment in a highly available GKE application?","options":{"A":"Use blue/green deployments with managed load balancing","B":"Manually copy container images to all regions","C":"Terminate old pods before scheduling new ones","D":"Create multiple clusters without synchronization & 1 snes > co “ef Ti a SY"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Blue/green deployments allow traffic to switch seamlessly between old and new versions with minimal disruption, especially when backed by a load balancer."},{"id":"227","question":"Which of the following methods provides proactive reliability for data- intensive applications in GCP?","options":{"A":"Schedule frequent manual backups to local disks","B":"Use snapshot schedules and replication with regional storage","C":"Increase cache size without redundancy","D":"Reduce disk size to limit latency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Snapshot schedules (automatic backups) and replication with regional storage (e.g. Regional Persistent Disk).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Snapshot schedule: a resource policy with frequency and retention; incremental snapshots stored in a regional or multi-regional location.\\n• Regional PD replicates synchronously across 2 zones → failover on zonal failure with no data loss.\\n• For regional DR: Persistent Disk Asynchronous Replication to another region.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabproactive\xbb + \xabdata-intensive\xbb → scheduled snapshots + replication.\\nDo not confuse with: manual backups to local disks (lost with the VM), cache without redundancy, smaller disks (less performance).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"228","question":"What is the purpose of Cloud Logging in operational reliability?","options":{"A":"Executes SQL queries across regions","B":"Scans GCP resources for vulnerabilities","C":"Encrypts data during transfer","D":"Aggregates logs for monitoring, alerting, and auditing la, ened. RL —s TT K\\\\ = on oan “1Ww*"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Zonal affinity and intelligent load balancing reduce inter-zone communication. This minimizes latency and improves service response times."},{"id":"229","question":"What feature in GCP ensures that Kubernetes workloads are automatically restarted if a node fails?","options":{"A":"IAM role-based scheduling","B":"GKE node auto-repair and pod rescheduling","C":"Resource reservations in Dataflow pipelines","D":"Persistent disk caching in Cloud Spanner ey"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GKE has node auto-repair and Kubernetes automatically reschedules pods to healthy nodes, enhancing workload reliability."},{"id":"230","question":"Which Google Cloud service allows tracking of API request latency for microservices?","options":{"A":"Cloud Source Repositories","B":"Memorystore for Redis","C":"Cloud Identity","D":"Cloud Trace"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cloud Trace is used for performance tracing and latency analysis across distributed applications, helping to optimize performance."},{"id":"231","question":"What should you use to notify operations teams about incidents in real- time?","options":{"A":"Cloud DNS zones","B":"Cloud Monitoring alerting policies with notification channels","C":"Billing export to BigQuery","D":"IAM condition expressions aM, \\", = 2 4 TFN “ai"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Monitoring supports alerting policies that send notifications to email, SMS, PagerDuty, or other integrated services for real-time incident response."},{"id":"232","question":"How can you reduce Mean Time to Recovery (MTTR) in a distributed system?","options":{"A":"Restrict change deployment to once monthly","B":"Avoid synthetic testing of application uptime","C":"Implement structured logging and runbook automation","D":"Use encrypted snapshots only for audit logs 47) WL Os"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Structured logging enables faster issue diagnosis, while runbooks provide automated, repeatable recovery procedures to reduce MT TR."},{"id":"233","question":"How does implementing service-level objectives (SLOs) support reliability?","options":{"A":"Enables budgeting of network costs across zones","B":"Sets measurable goals to track availability and latency targets","C":"Replaces the need for alerts and dashboards entirely","D":"Prevents need for infrastructure autoscaling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SLOs define reliability targets (e.g., uptime, response time), which help monitor performance against agreed standards and drive reliability practices."},{"id":"234","question":"What role does Cloud Scheduler play in ensuring operational reliability?","options":{"A":"Offers serverless RESTful APIs","B":"Automates periodic tasks like backups and health checks","C":"Monitors DNS propagation time","D":"Encrypts logs at rest"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Scheduler automates job execution on a recurring schedule, ensuring critical tasks such as backup and cleanup are consistently performed."},{"id":"235","question":"What can you use to perform health checks on backend services behind a load balancer?","options":{"A":"Use TCP or HTTP health checks in load balancing configuration","B":"Poll IAM audit logs for traffic errors","C":"Check for failed login attempts in BigQuery","D":"Use Pub/Sub retry messages with no expiration \\"4 Se"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Load balancers in GCP support health checks (HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, SSL) to ensure only healthy instances serve traffic, which improves reliability."},{"id":"236","question":"Why is regional redundancy important in reliability design?","options":{"A":"It increases billing complexity","B":"It ensures availability if one region becomes unavailable","C":"It eliminates the need for backups","D":"It allows auto-scaling based on CPU only"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Because it keeps the service available if an entire region goes down.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Deploy in 2+ regions behind a global external Application Load Balancer (anycast IP) that routes to the nearest healthy region.\\n• Data replicated across regions: multi-region Spanner, dual/multi-region buckets, Cloud SQL cross-region replicas, Bigtable replication.\\n• It costs more: use it according to the required RTO/RPO.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabregion becomes unavailable\xbb → multi-region.\\nDo not confuse with: \xabit increases billing complexity\xbb (not a benefit), \xabit eliminates backups\xbb (false), CPU-only autoscaling.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"237","question":"Which service provides long-term reliability by retaining historical logs?","options":{"A":"Identity-Aware Proxy","B":"Cloud Composer","C":"Cloud Shell","D":"Cloud Logging with log retention policies AL Cie"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cloud Logging allows configuration of log retention policies to meet compliance and operational needs, supporting forensics and diagnostics."},{"id":"238","question":"Which GCP feature best reduces the impact of configuration drift in infrastructure?","options":{"A":"Use Infrastructure as Code with Cloud Deployment Manager or Terraform","B":"Rely on manually updated documentation","C":"Set up remote SSH for all changes","D":"Clone VMs and apply custom scripts manually Ryan"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Infrastructure as Code (IaC) with Terraform (or Infrastructure Manager, which runs Terraform as a managed service).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Desired state lives in versioned files; terraform plan detects differences (drift) against what exists.\\n• Changes go through review and a pipeline, not the console or SSH.\\n• Re-applying restores the expected configuration.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabconfiguration drift\xbb → IaC.\\nDo not confuse with: manual documentation (goes stale), remote SSH for every change, or cloning VMs with hand scripts (they create drift).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"240","question":"What is the best GCP solution for isualizing service health and metrics across projects?","options":{"A":"Use GKE logs only","B":"Monitor logs in Cloud Shell","C":"Use Cloud Monitoring Dashboards with custom metrics Ou","D":"Export data to Sheets using App Script Posen “iw"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dashboards in Cloud Monitoring help visualize key performance indicators and track reliability metrics across services and projects."},{"id":"241","question":"How do GCP Uptime Checks support solution reliability?","options":{"A":"Offer multi-region backups","B":"Regularly verify service availability from global locations","C":"Increase network throughput si,","D":"Provide IAM policy management Set TIT"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Uptime checks simulate external traffic to your services from multiple locations to detect outages and initiate alerting quickly."},{"id":"242","question":"How can you maintain configuration integrity in production?","options":{"A":"Run manual CLI updates as needed","B":"Use version-controlled IaC pipelines with review and testing","C":"Disable audit logging to prevent overhead really","D":"Schedule frequent SSH changes to configs riers BRS Ts a Y"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Configuration stored in version-controlled IaC allows peer review, testing, rollback, and consistency across environments."},{"id":"243","question":"What strategy helps ensure consistent rollback in CI/CD pipelines?","options":{"A":"Maintain versioned artifacts and define rollback steps in pipelines","B":"Use load balancer logs to determine rollback","C":"Write firewall rules to block changes","D":"Use a different pipeline per developer % WUT 77 =a io i) CTT \\\\a opr TTT Node"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A robust CI/CD pipeline includes tested rollback procedures with versioned releases, minimizing recovery time and ensuring reliability."},{"id":"244","question":"Which GCP product integrates deeply with Cloud Monitoring for alert visualization?","options":{"A":"Cloud Armor","B":"Incident Response Management","C":"BigQuery ML","D":"App Engine Tasks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IRM provides a view of alerts, timelines, and resolution tracking, helping teams understand and act on system issues quickly."},{"id":"245","question":"How does autoscaling contribute to operational reliability?","options":{"A":"It reduces billing by limiting usage","B":"Automatically adjusts resources to match demand and maintain performance","C":"It blocks traffic during high usage","D":"It logs firewall policy changes ?, ~ “11Ww*"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Autoscaling prevents overloading services by dynamically adjusting capacity based on actual usage, keeping performance stable."},{"id":"247","question":"Which approach best supports reliability for batch jobs that must complete within defined time windows?","options":{"A":"Launch jobs manually from Cloud Console each time","B":"Use Cloud Scheduler to trigger Cloud Functions with retries","C":"Use Cloud Build to trigger jobs based on Git pushes","D":"Embed the logic into App Engine standard environment ‘"},"correct":"B","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Cloud Scheduler starts the job on time, and its retry configuration (plus idempotent jobs) recovers from transient failures. The batch then completes within its window without anyone launching it. For multi-step jobs, Workflows or Cloud Run jobs add orchestration.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) manual launches are unreliable. C) Git pushes are not a time-based trigger. D) App Engine standard request deadlines are poorly suited to long batch jobs.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"248","question":"You want to monitor availability and performance of your APIs running in App Engine. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Create custom shell scripts and run via Compute Engine","B":"Enable billing logs and filter for error traffic","C":"Use Cloud Monitoring uptime checks and alert policies","D":"Enable SSH access and check log files manually"},"correct":"C","answerSource":"derived-by-audit","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Cloud Monitoring uptime checks probe the API endpoints from global locations (availability and latency), and alert policies notify you on failures or SLO burn. App Engine also exports request latency and error metrics natively.\\n\\nWhy not the others: A) custom scripts on VMs are reinventing uptime checks. B) billing logs do not show errors. D) SSH into App Engine to read log files is manual and not how the platform works.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"249","question":"What is the most appropriate strategy for managing stateful workloads to improve resilience?","options":{"A":"Use persistent disks and regional snapshots for failover","B":"Store all application state in in-memory Redis cache","C":"Store local backups only on instance boot disks","D":"Write state data to standard output logs for parsing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Use persistent disks (ideally Regional PD) with snapshots that allow failover or restore in another zone or region.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• State lives on durable disks separate from the VM lifecycle.\\n• Regional PD replicates across 2 zones; snapshots let you recreate the disk in another location.\\n• On GKE: StatefulSets with PersistentVolumes (e.g. regional PD).\\n\\nExam tip: \xabstateful\xbb + \xabresilience\xbb → replicated persistent disk + snapshots.\\nDo not confuse with: all state only in in-memory Redis (can be lost), local backups on the same instance\'s boot disk (lost with it), writing state to stdout (not storage).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"250","question":"Which strategy helps prevent a monitoring system from becoming a single point of failure?","options":{"A":"Use a centralized dashboard with custom queries only","B":"Rely solely on Cloud Logging without alert configuration","C":"Send all logs to the same cloud storage bucket only","D":"Use distributed monitoring agents and multi-region r logging ans “iw"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Distributed agents and replicated logs reduce the risk of observability loss and improve fault tolerance in the monitoring infrastructure itself."},{"id":"251","question":"A service has intermittent spikes in traffic. What reliability technique should be used?","options":{"A":"Implement autoscaling with load-based policies in GKE","B":"Fix resource allocation manually to avoid excess billing","C":"Use standard instances with fixed-size load balancers","D":"Disable vertical scaling to keep latency predictable"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Correct answer: A. Autoscaling with load-based policies: Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (CPU, memory, or custom/external metrics such as RPS) + cluster autoscaler for nodes.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• HPA adjusts replicas to the target metric; the cluster autoscaler adds nodes if pods are pending.\\n• For very fast spikes leave headroom (lower target, minReplicas), because creating nodes takes minutes.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabintermittent spikes\xbb → load-based autoscaling.\\nDo not confuse with: fixed manual allocation (does not react), standard instances with a fixed-size LB, disabling vertical scaling.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"254","question":"Which strategy enhances observability and root cause analysis in production systems?","options":{"A":"Centralize logs and correlate them with trace and metrics","B":"Rely only on status page updates and incident tickets","C":"Use logs from staging environments only to reduce noise % =","D":"Suppress low-severity logs to maintain cleaner dashboards 11 3: Sanaet “oe Ts eL/ \\\\ >"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Centralizing logs and correlating them with tracing and monitoring metrics enables faster and more accurate root cause analysis."},{"id":"255","question":"Which of the following supports high availability during zonal failure in GKE?","options":{"A":"Deploy workloads in a single zone to reduce network hops","B":"Use preemptible nodes in the same availability zone","C":"Use regional clusters spanning multiple zones","D":"Configure node pools manually in a single availability zone \\"1 Ge"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. Regional clusters that span several zones.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Replicated control plane and nodes spread across zones; pods are redistributed if a zone fails.\\n• Use topologySpreadConstraints per zone so replicas are not all in the same zone.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabzonal failure\xbb + GKE → regional cluster.\\nDo not confuse with: workloads in a single zone, preemptible nodes in the same zone, node pools hand-configured in one zone.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"256","question":"What method ensures minimum disruption when patching running virtual machines?","options":{"A":"Use live migration features of Compute Engine","B":"Reboot all instances during low-traffic periods","C":"Use cron jobs to pull security updates daily","D":"Schedule manual reboots across all instances Sy, % silly Creer “we Ts \\\\ s weekly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Live migration allows Compute Engine to patch underlying infrastructure without stopping the VM, maintaining availability."},{"id":"258","question":"What’s a recommended action to reduce downtime from configuration drift?","options":{"A":"Allow teams to manually adjust resources as needed","B":"Revert to snapshots when changes go unnoticed","C":"Use CI/CD pipelines integrated with infrastructure as code","D":"Disable rollback to ensure full manual control over changes & = = RL ae"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Correct answer: C. CI/CD integrated with infrastructure as code: every infrastructure change goes through the pipeline, is tested, and can be reverted.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Drift detection with a periodic terraform plan.\\n• Fast rollback by redeploying the previous version of the infrastructure code.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabconfiguration drift\xbb + downtime → CI/CD + IaC.\\nDo not confuse with: each team hand-tuning, reverting snapshots after problems already happened (reactive and can lose data), disabling rollback.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"259","question":"Ateam notices latency in a global application. What tool can analyze network paths?","options":{"A":"Network Intelligence Center’s Performance Dashboard","B":"VPC Flow Logs with log router custom metrics","C":"GKE resource manager and node-level profiler","D":"Pub/Sub metrics dashboard in billing export reports % tilly ll * (st) TIT Senne\xae ~ “1Ww* etre"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Network Intelligence Center provides detailed diagnostics and visualizations for network performance, aiding latency investigation."},{"id":"260","question":"What strategy reduces risk when deploying new code in a production service?","options":{"A":"Push changes directly and revert only if users complain","B":"Disable monitoring during deployments to avoid noise","C":"Use canary deployments with rollback on failure triggers","D":"Replace entire node groups with new versions at once"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Canary deployments gradually introduce new versions to a small subset of users, minimizing risk and supporting safe rollback."},{"id":"261","question":"What is the benefit of using managed instance groups (MIGs) with autoscaling?","options":{"A":"They can be manually resized based on static CPU metrics","B":"They require external load balancers to scale horizontally","C":"They automatically adjust based on real-time workload metrics % we, ca","D":"They distribute traffic only within a single GCP zone aL"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MIGs with autoscaling respond to metrics like CPU or request load, maintaining performance and reliability under variable traffic."},{"id":"262","question":"What should you do to ensure deleted resources don’t affect existing monitoring?","options":{"A":"Archive old logs into a cold storage GCS bucket","B":"Decrease logging retention time to 7 days","C":"Create custom dashboards with filters for active resources","D":"Turn off metrics exports when resources are removed 2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Filtering dashboards for active resources helps avoid confusion and ensures monitoring remains accurate after deletions."},{"id":"263","question":"What action can help reduce Mean Time to Detect (MTTD) in incident response?","options":{"A":"Use batch alerts aggregated every 6 hours","B":"Implement threshold-based alerting with real-time notifications","C":"Rely on periodic service reviews and feedback surveys","D":"Manually inspect logs after user complaints arise % la, ye wR “11Ww* sly TTI A)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Scheduler combined with Cloud Functions enables timed execution with retry logic, ensuring reliability for time-sensitive batch operations."},{"id":"264","question":"How can you help reduce Mean Time to Recovery (MTTR) after a service crash?","options":{"A":"Wait for full root cause analysis before restoring service","B":"Implement runbooks and automated rollback procedures","C":"Increase alert severity thresholds to reduce noise","D":"Replace affected services with new builds manually"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. With documented runbooks and automated rollback procedures: restore the service first, investigate later.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Rollback to the last stable version (Cloud Run revisions, Cloud Deploy, kubectl rollout undo).\\n• Root-cause analysis (blameless postmortem) happens after you mitigate.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabafter a crash\xbb → mitigate fast (rollback) before RCA.\\nDo not confuse with: waiting for a full RCA before restoring, raising alert thresholds (you detect later), replacing services with hand-built builds (slow and error-prone).","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"265","question":"What is a best practice for managing system health during application updates?","options":{"A":"Use readiness and liveness probes in Kubernetes deployments","B":"Push code updates during peak usage to test real load","C":"Set compute quotas very high to prevent throttling","D":"Allow unauthenticated access during updates for testing lla, * or % ie oe : Ve tas att a mw al \\"Ny, AY S"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Readiness and liveness probes help determine when pods are ready to serve traffic or need to be restarted, enabling smoother deployments."},{"id":"266","question":"Which logging strategy supports long- term audit and compliance?","options":{"A":"Store all logs in local VM disks and rotate every week","B":"Export audit logs to a versioned Cloud Storage bucket","C":"Filter out INFO-level logs to reduce billing charges","D":"Delete logs older than 30 days for simplicity “iw"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Exporting logs to Cloud Storage with versioning provides durability and traceability, fulfilling compliance and audit requirements."},{"id":"267","question":"What helps ensure configuration changes do not negatively impact production systems?","options":{"A":"Disable alerting rules before deploying configuration files","B":"Test all changes in staging environments before deploying","C":"Apply changes directly in the production environment daily","D":"Use unmanaged instances to avoid configuration conflicts % oe aS “11Ww* eet tilly — * BR TTI"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Testing configuration updates in staging environments ensures stability before deployment, reducing the chance of failures in production."},{"id":"268","question":"What is the benefit of using Cloud Load Balancer health checks?","options":{"A":"It removes failed backends from the traffic distribution list","B":"It increases the number of virtual machine instances globally","C":"It adds unused resources to minimize application traffic","D":"It triggers auto-deployment based on custom DNS settings \\"4 core"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Health checks ensure only healthy backends serve traffic, improving availability and avoiding routing requests to unresponsive services."},{"id":"269","question":"How does using Terraform with GCP support lifecycle reliability?","options":{"A":"It runs workloads faster than traditional GCP SDK commands","B":"It allows multiple users to apply untracked configuration files ee -_ -","C":"It maintains infrastructure as code with version control 47) sly oh","D":"It requires fewer IAM roles and permissions per user A)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Terraform enables consistent, repeatable deployments by managing infrastructure as code, supporting change tracking and rollback."},{"id":"270","question":"Ateam needs to automate response actions after error thresholds are exceeded. What should they use?","options":{"A":"Use Cloud NAT to reroute packets during incidents","B":"Launch GCE instances from the monitoring dashboard","C":"Use alerting policies with Cloud Functions for remediation “iw","D":"Scale down workloads manually via Console after issues ‘"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud Monitoring alerts can trigger Cloud Functions that automatically execute remediation actions, reducing downtime and MTTR."},{"id":"271","question":"Acompany wants to recover critical app data after a disaster. What ensures this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use zonal standard disks without replication settings enabled","B":"Retain logs for 14 days and generate weekly summary reports","C":"Create instance snapshots stored only in the current region","D":"Use cross-region backups and test restore procedures"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cross-region backups provide durability during regional failures. Testing recovery procedures ensures data can be restored when needed."},{"id":"272","question":"What is the role of Cloud Profiler in supporting reliability?","options":{"A":"It identifies inefficient code that impacts system latency","B":"It predicts infrastructure billing cost for future quarters","C":"It synchronizes logs across all availability zones globally","D":"It validates IAM policy changes during CI/CD workflows G iG “Se 11.5 *s “@ rer “fk “11Ww* selly )"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Profiler helps developers understand where latency or resource consumption occurs, improving performance and operational health."},{"id":"273","question":"How can Cloud Run services be made more resilient during sudden traffic increases?","options":{"A":"Use manual instance provisioning to handle sudden changes","B":"Enable concurrency and autoscaling in service settings","C":"Configure GCS triggers to reroute request paths to buckets","D":"Attach startup scripts that poll external monitoring tools"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cloud Monitoring’s uptime checks and alerting policies offer integrated and automated monitoring of App Engine services, with minimal manual effort."},{"id":"274","question":"How can teams identify early signs of service degradation?","options":{"A":"Rely on bug reports submitted by end-users post-failure","B":"Use SLO-based alerting with burn rate-based conditions","C":"Wait until scheduled health reviews detect chronic failures","D":"Disable detailed logging to prevent alerting on minor zz Sanne? oan “1iWw* sey Ss issues"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. With SLO-based alerts and burn-rate conditions (how fast the error budget is consumed).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• Burn rate 1 = you exhaust the error budget exactly at the end of the SLO window; burn rate 10 = you would exhaust it 10\xd7 faster.\\n• Common practice: fast alert (high burn rate in a short window, e.g. 1 h) + slow alert (low burn rate in a long window, e.g. 24 h).\\n• Cloud Monitoring supports burn-rate alerting policies on service SLOs.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabearly signs\xbb + SLO → burn rate alerts.\\nDo not confuse with: waiting for user bug reports, scheduled health reviews, disabling detailed logging.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"275","question":"What helps detect anomalous behavior in application metrics?","options":{"A":"Use regional static thresholds with default alert rules only","B":"Collect logs without enabling metric analysis or filtering","C":"Use Cloud Monitoring’s anomaly detection feature","D":"Set warning notifications based on fixed calendar schedules % <S se silly TT A) RS Ti"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud Monitoring supports anomaly detection to identify unexpected deviations in metric trends, enabling proactive responses."},{"id":"276","question":"What method helps optimize resources and prevent over-provisioning?","options":{"A":"Set instance types with the highest CPU cores by default","B":"Analyze metrics using Recommender for right-sizing VMs","C":"Remove alert policies to avoid unnecessary scaling triggers ta, 42)","D":"Disable autoscaling to maintain consistent performance eL// Saaet aS Te \\\\ SS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Use the Recommender\'s right-sizing recommendations (machine type recommendations) based on historical usage metrics.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• It analyzes the last ~8 days of CPU and memory (memory is more accurate with the Ops Agent) and suggests a smaller or larger machine type.\\n• Apply with a click or via API; there are also recommendations for MIGs.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabright-sizing\xbb / \xabover-provisioning\xbb → Recommender.\\nDo not confuse with: defaulting to the machine type with the most cores, deleting alert policies, disabling autoscaling.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"279","question":"What tool should you use to simulate failure in a production system for resilience testing?","options":{"A":"Deploy legacy apps and monitor manual behavior","B":"Use Chaos Engineering tools like Chaos Monkey or Litmus","C":"Disable autoscaling and observe user complaints “nw","D":"Create service accounts with reduced IAM roles only ‘Ss THA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. Chaos Engineering tools such as Chaos Monkey or LitmusChaos, which inject controlled failures (kill instances or pods, add latency, cut the network).\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• You define a steady-state hypothesis (SLIs), inject the failure with limited blast radius, and observe.\\n• Start in staging and with a small blast radius; have the abort mechanism ready.\\n• Reveals fragile dependencies, badly configured timeouts, and missing redundancy.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabsimulate failure\xbb / \xabresilience testing\xbb → chaos engineering.\\nDo not confuse with: deploying legacy apps and watching by hand, disabling autoscaling and waiting for complaints, creating service accounts with fewer roles.","explanationSource":"card-en"},{"id":"280","question":"What is the purpose of GKE node auto- repair in supporting reliability?","options":{"A":"It adds additional features to control network traffic logs","B":"It automatically replaces unhealthy nodes in the cluster","C":"It triggers new pod deployments every 24 hours by default om","D":"It synchronizes data to all clusters across zones instantly & 13: uit nite Miia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Correct answer: B. To automatically replace unhealthy nodes in the cluster.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n• GKE watches each node\'s state (e.g. NotReady or not reporting for a sustained time, or boot disk full) and repairs it: drains and recreates it.\\n• Enabled by default on Autopilot and on new Standard node pools.\\n• Pods managed by controllers are rescheduled onto other nodes.\\n\\nExam tip: \xabauto-repair\xbb → automatic replacement of unhealthy nodes.\\nDo not confuse with: network traffic logs, automatic redeploys every 24 hours, instant data sync between clusters.","explanationSource":"card-en"}]},{"id":"ml-fundamentals","title":"Machine Learning Fundamentals","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"A bank has 5 years of loan applications, each labeled as \'repaid\' or \'defaulted\'. It wants a model that predicts the outcome for new applications. Which type of machine learning fits this problem?","options":{"A":"Unsupervised learning, because the model has to discover patterns on its own","B":"Supervised learning, because each historical example has a known label","C":"Reinforcement learning, because the model receives a reward for each correct loan","D":"Clustering, because applications must be grouped into repaid and defaulted"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Supervised learning learns a mapping from inputs to a known target using labeled examples. Here every historical application has a label (repaid or defaulted), so the model can learn from those answers and predict the label for new applications."},{"id":"2","question":"A real-estate company wants to predict the sale price, in dollars, of houses from their size, location and age. What kind of supervised task is this?","options":{"A":"Binary classification","B":"Multi-class classification","C":"Regression","D":"Clustering"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Regression predicts a continuous numeric value, such as a price in dollars. Classification predicts a discrete category, such as \'cheap\' or \'expensive\'. Since the target is a number that can take any value in a range, this is a regression task."},{"id":"3","question":"A team is building an agent that learns to play a video game. The agent tries actions, gets points when it advances and loses points when it fails, and gradually improves its strategy. Which learning paradigm is this?","options":{"A":"Supervised learning","B":"Unsupervised learning","C":"Semi-supervised learning","D":"Reinforcement learning"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In reinforcement learning an agent interacts with an environment, takes actions and receives rewards or penalties. It learns a policy that maximizes the total reward over time. There is no labeled dataset of correct actions; the feedback comes from the environment."},{"id":"4","question":"A data scientist splits a dataset into training, validation and test sets. What is the main purpose of the validation set?","options":{"A":"To compare models and tune hyperparameters without touching the test set","B":"To add more data to the training process and improve accuracy","C":"To report the final performance of the chosen model to stakeholders","D":"To store examples that have missing labels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The validation set is used during development to compare models and choose hyperparameters. The test set is kept aside and used only once at the end to estimate how the chosen model will perform on unseen data. If you tune on the test set, its score becomes optimistic and no longer reflects real-world performance."},{"id":"5","question":"A model that predicts hospital readmission reaches 99% accuracy in validation but performs poorly in production. The team discovers that one feature, \'discharge_followup_scheduled\', is only filled in after a patient has already been readmitted. What problem is this?","options":{"A":"Underfitting","B":"Class imbalance","C":"Data leakage","D":"Vanishing gradients"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Data leakage happens when training data contains information that would not be available at prediction time, often information derived from the target itself. The model learns to rely on that shortcut, scores unrealistically well in validation, and fails in production where the leaked feature is missing or different."},{"id":"6","question":"A model has 98% accuracy on the training set and 71% on the validation set. Adding more training epochs increases training accuracy but lowers validation accuracy. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"Underfitting: the model is too simple","B":"Overfitting: the model memorizes the training data instead of generalizing","C":"The learning rate is too low","D":"The validation set is too large"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A large gap between training and validation performance, which grows as training continues, is the classic sign of overfitting. The model fits noise and details specific to the training data. Typical remedies are more data, regularization, a simpler model, dropout or early stopping."},{"id":"7","question":"A linear model trained to predict energy consumption has high error on both the training set and the validation set, and the two errors are very similar. What is the most likely problem and fix?","options":{"A":"Underfitting; use a more expressive model or add more informative features","B":"Overfitting; add stronger regularization","C":"Data leakage; remove features that use future information","D":"The test set is too small; collect more test data"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When training and validation errors are both high and close to each other, the model is too simple to capture the underlying pattern. This is underfitting, or high bias. Adding regularization would make it worse; instead, use a more flexible model, engineer better features, or train longer."},{"id":"8","question":"Which statement best describes the bias–variance tradeoff?","options":{"A":"Increasing model complexity always reduces both bias and variance","B":"Bias measures how much predictions change between training sets, and variance measures systematic error","C":"Bias and variance can be eliminated completely with enough epochs","D":"Simpler models tend to have high bias and low variance, while more complex models tend to have low bias and high variance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bias is error from overly simple assumptions: the model systematically misses the true pattern. Variance is error from sensitivity to the specific training data: the model changes a lot if trained on a different sample. Increasing complexity usually lowers bias but raises variance, so the goal is a balance that minimizes total error on new data."},{"id":"9","question":"A linear model is trained on a dataset with 500 features, many of which are probably irrelevant. The team wants the model to automatically drive the weights of useless features to exactly zero. Which regularization should they use?","options":{"A":"L2 regularization (Ridge)","B":"L1 regularization (Lasso)","C":"Increasing the learning rate","D":"Removing the bias term"},"correct":"B","explanation":"L1 regularization adds the sum of absolute weight values to the loss. Its geometry pushes many weights to exactly zero, producing a sparse model that effectively performs feature selection. L2 regularization adds the sum of squared weights and shrinks weights toward zero, but rarely makes them exactly zero."},{"id":"10","question":"During training of a neural network, a technique randomly sets the output of a fraction of neurons to zero at each training step, and uses all neurons at inference time. What is this technique and what is its purpose?","options":{"A":"Batch normalization, to speed up convergence","B":"Pruning, to make the model smaller for deployment","C":"Dropout, to reduce overfitting by preventing neurons from co-adapting","D":"Gradient clipping, to prevent exploding gradients"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dropout randomly deactivates a fraction of neurons during each training step. The network cannot rely on any single neuron, so it learns more robust, redundant representations, which reduces overfitting. At inference time all neurons are active and the outputs are scaled so the expected values match training."},{"id":"11","question":"A team has only 1,200 labeled examples. A single 80/20 train/validation split gives very different scores depending on the random seed. Which technique gives a more reliable estimate of model performance?","options":{"A":"k-fold cross-validation","B":"Training for more epochs","C":"Using the test set for validation as well","D":"Increasing the batch size"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In k-fold cross-validation the data is divided into k folds; the model is trained k times, each time validating on a different fold and training on the rest. The k scores are averaged, so every example is used for validation exactly once and the estimate is much less dependent on one lucky or unlucky split. It is especially valuable for small datasets."},{"id":"12","question":"A dataset for detecting a rare disease has 2% positive cases. After a random train/validation split, one validation fold contains almost no positive examples. What should the team use?","options":{"A":"A larger learning rate","B":"A time-based split","C":"Removing the positive examples from validation","D":"A stratified split, so each subset keeps the 2% class proportion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stratified splitting preserves the class proportions in every subset. With rare classes, a purely random split can leave a fold with very few or no positive cases, making metrics like recall unstable or undefined. Stratified k-fold cross-validation applies the same idea to each fold."},{"id":"13","question":"In a fraud dataset, 99.5% of transactions are legitimate. A model that predicts \'legitimate\' for every transaction reaches 99.5% accuracy. What does this show?","options":{"A":"The model is excellent and ready for production","B":"Accuracy is misleading on imbalanced data; metrics such as precision, recall or PR AUC are needed","C":"The dataset needs more legitimate transactions","D":"Accuracy should be replaced by mean squared error"},"correct":"B","explanation":"On highly imbalanced data, a trivial model that always predicts the majority class gets very high accuracy while detecting zero fraud. This is known as the accuracy paradox. Metrics that focus on the minority class, such as precision, recall, F1 or the area under the precision–recall curve, show the real usefulness of the model."},{"id":"14","question":"A spam filter moves emails to the spam folder. Users complain that important emails are being lost in spam. Which metric should the team prioritize improving for the \'spam\' class?","options":{"A":"Recall","B":"Accuracy on the training set","C":"Precision","D":"Mean absolute error"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Precision measures, of everything the model labeled as spam, how much really was spam: TP / (TP + FP). Important emails sent to spam are false positives, so improving precision directly reduces that problem. Recall measures how much of the actual spam was caught, which is not the complaint here."},{"id":"15","question":"A model screens medical scans for a serious, treatable disease. Missing a sick patient is far more costly than sending a healthy patient for an extra test. Which metric should be prioritized?","options":{"A":"Recall (sensitivity)","B":"Precision","C":"Specificity","D":"Accuracy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Recall measures, of all actually sick patients, how many the model detected: TP / (TP + FN). When false negatives are the most expensive error, as with missing a serious disease, recall should be prioritized, usually accepting more false positives that a follow-up test can rule out."},{"id":"16","question":"A team needs a single number that balances precision and recall for an imbalanced classification problem, and it should be low if either of the two is low. Which metric fits?","options":{"A":"Accuracy","B":"F1 score","C":"Arithmetic mean of precision and recall","D":"R-squared"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall: 2\xb7P\xb7R / (P + R). The harmonic mean is dominated by the smaller value, so a model with precision 1.0 and recall 0.1 gets an F1 of about 0.18, while a simple average would give 0.55. This makes F1 a good single metric when both kinds of error matter."},{"id":"17","question":"Two binary classifiers output probabilities. The team wants to compare how well each one ranks positive examples above negative ones across all possible thresholds, before choosing a threshold. Which metric is designed for this?","options":{"A":"Accuracy at a 0.5 threshold","B":"Mean squared error of the probabilities","C":"The number of true positives","D":"The area under the ROC curve (ROC AUC)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ROC curve plots the true positive rate against the false positive rate for every threshold. The area under it, ROC AUC, summarizes performance across all thresholds and equals the probability that the model scores a random positive example higher than a random negative one. An AUC of 0.5 means random guessing and 1.0 means perfect ranking."},{"id":"18","question":"A fraud model is evaluated on data where only 0.1% of transactions are fraud. Its ROC AUC is 0.97, but in practice most of its alerts are false alarms. Which metric would better reflect performance on the rare class?","options":{"A":"Accuracy","B":"Specificity","C":"The area under the precision–recall curve (PR AUC)","D":"The number of true negatives"},"correct":"C","explanation":"With extreme imbalance, the false positive rate stays tiny even when false positives outnumber true positives, because the number of negatives is huge. ROC AUC can therefore look excellent while precision is poor. The precision–recall curve focuses on the positive class, so PR AUC (also called average precision) exposes the problem."},{"id":"19","question":"A binary classifier\'s confusion matrix shows: 80 true positives, 20 false negatives, 30 false positives and 870 true negatives. What are its precision and recall?","options":{"A":"Precision 0.80, recall 0.73","B":"Precision 0.73, recall 0.80","C":"Precision 0.95, recall 0.80","D":"Precision 0.80, recall 0.97"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Precision = TP / (TP + FP) = 80 / (80 + 30) ≈ 0.73. Recall = TP / (TP + FN) = 80 / (80 + 20) = 0.80. A common mistake is to swap them: precision is about the predicted positives, while recall is about the actual positives."},{"id":"20","question":"A delivery-time model is evaluated with two metrics. The team notices that RMSE is much larger than MAE. What does this most likely indicate?","options":{"A":"Some predictions have very large errors, which RMSE penalizes more than MAE","B":"The model is perfectly calibrated","C":"The model is underfitting all examples equally","D":"MAE is always larger than RMSE, so the metrics were swapped"},"correct":"A","explanation":"RMSE squares the errors before averaging, so large errors weigh much more than small ones. MAE averages absolute errors, treating every unit of error equally. When RMSE is much larger than MAE, a few predictions have big errors, such as outliers or rare cases the model handles badly. RMSE is always greater than or equal to MAE."},{"id":"21","question":"Which loss function is the standard choice for training a binary classifier that outputs a probability, such as logistic regression?","options":{"A":"Mean squared error","B":"Mean absolute error","C":"Binary cross-entropy (log loss)","D":"Hinge loss computed on the predicted labels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Binary cross-entropy, also called log loss, measures how far the predicted probability is from the true label: −[y\xb7log(p) + (1−y)\xb7log(1−p)]. It heavily penalizes confident wrong predictions, such as predicting 0.99 when the true label is 0, and it works well with gradient-based training of probabilistic classifiers."},{"id":"22","question":"While training a model with gradient descent, the loss jumps up and down wildly and sometimes grows to infinity instead of decreasing. What is the most likely cause?","options":{"A":"The training set is too large","B":"The model has too few parameters","C":"The learning rate is too small","D":"The learning rate is too large"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The learning rate controls the size of each step in the direction that reduces the loss. If it is too large, the steps overshoot the minimum, bounce from side to side, and can diverge, making the loss grow. If it is too small, training is stable but very slow. Lowering the learning rate, or using a schedule that reduces it over time, usually fixes divergence."},{"id":"23","question":"A dataset has 50 million rows. Computing the gradient on the full dataset for every update is too slow. Which approach is standard for training on large datasets?","options":{"A":"Mini-batch gradient descent, computing each update on a small random subset of examples","B":"Full-batch gradient descent with a larger learning rate","C":"Training only on the first 1,000 rows","D":"Replacing gradient descent with k-means clustering"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mini-batch gradient descent estimates the gradient on a small random batch (for example 32 to 512 examples) and updates the parameters after each batch. Updates are much cheaper and more frequent, the noise in the estimate can help escape poor regions, and batches map well to GPU parallelism. One pass over all the batches is called an epoch."},{"id":"24","question":"A k-nearest neighbors model uses two features: annual income (values around 20,000–200,000) and number of children (values 0–5). The model\'s predictions are driven almost entirely by income. What preprocessing step would fix this?","options":{"A":"One-hot encoding both features","B":"Scaling the features, for example with standardization or min-max normalization","C":"Removing the number-of-children feature","D":"Adding more neighbors (increasing k)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Distance-based models such as k-NN, and models trained with gradient descent, are sensitive to feature scale. A difference of 10,000 in income dwarfs a difference of 3 children, so income dominates the distance. Standardization (subtracting the mean and dividing by the standard deviation) or min-max scaling puts the features on comparable ranges. Tree-based models are largely insensitive to scaling."},{"id":"25","question":"A feature \'color\' has the values red, green and blue. A team encodes it as red = 1, green = 2, blue = 3 and feeds it to a linear model. Why is this problematic, and what is the usual alternative?","options":{"A":"It is not problematic; numeric codes are always preferred","B":"Linear models cannot use numeric features; the column should be dropped","C":"The codes imply an order and distance that don\'t exist; one-hot encoding is the usual alternative","D":"The values should be sorted alphabetically before encoding"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Integer codes imply that blue is \'greater\' than red and that green is exactly halfway between them, an ordering the model will try to use even though it is meaningless. One-hot encoding creates one binary column per category (is_red, is_green, is_blue), so each color gets its own independent weight. Integer encoding is fine for truly ordinal categories such as small, medium and large."},{"id":"26","question":"A recommendation model uses a \'product_id\' feature with 2 million distinct values. One-hot encoding creates an enormous, sparse input and the model cannot learn that some products are similar. Which representation is the usual solution?","options":{"A":"Learned embeddings: map each product_id to a dense, low-dimensional vector","B":"Integer encoding sorted by product_id","C":"Dropping the feature","D":"Min-max scaling of product_id"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An embedding layer maps each category to a dense vector of, for example, 32 or 64 numbers that are learned during training. Products that behave similarly end up with similar vectors, which lets the model share what it learns across related items. The feature hashing trick is another option to bound the input size when similarity is not needed."},{"id":"27","question":"A dataset of customer records has 15% missing values in the \'age\' column. Dropping those rows would remove too much data. Which approach is a reasonable first step?","options":{"A":"Replace missing ages with 0","B":"Fill missing ages using the mean age computed on the full dataset, including the test set","C":"Delete the \'age\' column from every model","D":"Impute missing ages with the median from the training set, and optionally add a binary \'age_missing\' indicator feature"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Imputing with a robust statistic such as the median, computed on the training set only, keeps the rows without inventing extreme values. Adding a missing-indicator feature lets the model learn whether the fact that the value is missing is itself informative. Filling with 0 creates fake, impossible ages, and computing statistics on the test set leaks information."},{"id":"28","question":"A churn dataset has 95% customers who stay and 5% who leave. The model almost never predicts churn. Which change directly makes the model pay more attention to the minority class during training?","options":{"A":"Removing all churned customers from the training data","B":"Using class weights so errors on the minority class cost more in the loss","C":"Evaluating with accuracy instead of recall","D":"Applying oversampling to the validation and test sets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Class weights multiply the loss of each example by a weight that depends on its class, so mistakes on rare churners cost more and the model is pushed to detect them. Resampling the training set (oversampling the minority class or undersampling the majority) has a similar effect. Resampling must be applied only to the training data, never to validation or test, which must reflect the real distribution."},{"id":"29","question":"A loan-default classifier outputs probabilities, and by default a loan is flagged as risky if the probability is above 0.5. The business says it wants to catch more risky loans, even if it means reviewing more safe ones. What is the simplest change, without retraining?","options":{"A":"Raise the decision threshold to 0.8","B":"Switch the loss function to mean squared error","C":"Lower the decision threshold, for example to 0.3","D":"Remove the probability output and use only the predicted labels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The decision threshold converts probabilities into labels. Lowering it flags more loans as risky, which increases recall at the cost of precision. The threshold is a business decision and should be chosen on the validation set, for example using the precision–recall curve or the expected cost of each type of error. The 0.5 default has no special meaning."},{"id":"30","question":"Which of the following is a hyperparameter rather than a model parameter?","options":{"A":"The maximum depth of a decision tree","B":"The weights of a linear regression","C":"The split thresholds a decision tree learns at each node","D":"The biases of a neural network\'s neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Parameters are learned from data during training, such as weights, biases and a tree\'s split thresholds. Hyperparameters are set before training and control how learning happens or how complex the model can be, such as maximum tree depth, learning rate, number of layers or regularization strength. Hyperparameters are chosen using a validation set or cross-validation."},{"id":"31","question":"When a decision tree for classification chooses which feature and threshold to split a node on, what does it typically try to do?","options":{"A":"Pick the feature with the largest numeric range","B":"Pick the split that makes the resulting child nodes as pure as possible, for example by minimizing Gini impurity or entropy","C":"Pick a random feature at every node","D":"Pick the split that puts the same number of examples in each child"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A classification tree evaluates candidate splits and chooses the one that most reduces impurity, measured with Gini impurity or entropy (information gain). A pure node contains examples of a single class. Regression trees use the same idea but minimize the variance or squared error of the target in each child."},{"id":"32","question":"A random forest trains many decision trees and averages their predictions. Which two sources of randomness make the trees different from each other?","options":{"A":"Random learning rates and random loss functions","B":"Random labels and random test sets","C":"Random depths and random activation functions","D":"Each tree is trained on a bootstrap sample of the rows, and each split considers only a random subset of features"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A random forest uses bagging (bootstrap aggregating): each tree is trained on a random sample of rows drawn with replacement. In addition, each split only considers a random subset of features, which decorrelates the trees. Averaging many diverse, high-variance trees greatly reduces variance and therefore overfitting, while keeping the low bias of deep trees."},{"id":"33","question":"How does gradient boosting (as in XGBoost or LightGBM) build its ensemble of trees?","options":{"A":"Sequentially: each new shallow tree is trained to correct the errors (residuals) of the trees built so far","B":"In parallel: every tree is trained independently on a bootstrap sample and the results are averaged","C":"By training one very deep tree and pruning it","D":"By clustering the data and training one tree per cluster"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gradient boosting adds trees one at a time. Each new tree, usually shallow, is fitted to the remaining errors of the current ensemble (more precisely, to the negative gradient of the loss), and its contribution is scaled by a learning rate. This mainly reduces bias. Because it keeps fitting the errors, it can overfit if too many trees are added, so early stopping on a validation set is common."},{"id":"34","question":"A k-nearest neighbors classifier with k = 1 reaches 100% accuracy on the training set but performs poorly on new data. What is the most appropriate change?","options":{"A":"Set k = 0","B":"Remove feature scaling","C":"Increase k and choose its value with cross-validation","D":"Train the model for more epochs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"With k = 1, each training point is its own nearest neighbor, so the model memorizes the training set and its decision boundary follows every noisy point: high variance. Larger values of k average over more neighbors and give smoother boundaries, which usually generalize better, although too large a k causes underfitting. k-NN has no training epochs; it simply stores the data."},{"id":"35","question":"A marketing team wants to segment customers into groups based on purchasing behavior, with no predefined labels. They use k-means but don\'t know how many clusters to choose. Which approach helps pick k?","options":{"A":"Use accuracy on a labeled test set","B":"Plot the within-cluster sum of squares for several values of k and look for the \'elbow\', or compare silhouette scores","C":"Always choose k equal to the number of features","D":"Choose the k that gives the lowest within-cluster sum of squares, even if k equals the number of customers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The within-cluster sum of squares (inertia) always decreases as k grows, reaching zero when every point is its own cluster, so its minimum is useless. The elbow method looks for the k after which adding clusters brings only small improvements. The silhouette score measures how well each point fits its own cluster compared with the nearest other cluster. Business interpretability also matters."},{"id":"36","question":"A dataset has 300 highly correlated numeric features. The team wants to reduce it to a few new features that keep most of the variation in the data, for visualization and to speed up training. Which technique fits?","options":{"A":"One-hot encoding","B":"k-means clustering","C":"L2 regularization","D":"Principal component analysis (PCA)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PCA finds new orthogonal axes, the principal components, ordered by how much variance in the data they capture. Keeping the first few components compresses many correlated features into a small number of uncorrelated ones while preserving most of the information. The components are linear combinations of the original features, so they are harder to interpret, and features should be standardized first."},{"id":"37","question":"Despite its name, logistic regression is used for classification. How does it turn a linear combination of features into a prediction?","options":{"A":"It passes the linear score through a sigmoid function, producing a probability between 0 and 1, which is then compared with a threshold","B":"It rounds the linear score to the nearest integer class","C":"It fits a separate straight line for each class and picks the longest one","D":"It clusters the data and assigns the label of the largest cluster"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Logistic regression computes z = w\xb7x + b and applies the sigmoid function σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(−z)), which maps any real number to a value between 0 and 1 that is interpreted as the probability of the positive class. It is trained with log loss, and a threshold turns the probability into a class. Its weights are interpretable: each one changes the log-odds of the positive class."},{"id":"38","question":"A support vector machine (SVM) is trained on a binary classification problem. Which statement describes the core idea of a linear SVM?","options":{"A":"It builds many decision trees and averages them","B":"It assigns each example the label of its nearest neighbor","C":"It finds the separating boundary with the largest margin to the closest points of each class","D":"It groups examples into k clusters before classifying"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A linear SVM looks for the hyperplane that separates the classes with the widest possible margin, where the margin is the distance to the nearest training points, called support vectors. A wide margin tends to generalize better. A soft-margin parameter C allows some points to violate the margin, and the kernel trick lets SVMs learn non-linear boundaries."},{"id":"39","question":"A team wants to know which features a trained gradient boosting model relies on most, measured by how much validation performance drops when a feature\'s values are shuffled. What is this technique called?","options":{"A":"One-hot encoding","B":"Permutation feature importance","C":"Principal component analysis","D":"Early stopping"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Permutation importance randomly shuffles one feature\'s values in the validation set, breaking its relationship with the target, and measures how much the model\'s score drops. A large drop means the model depends heavily on that feature. It works with any model, but correlated features can share importance, and a high importance shows what the model uses, not that the feature causes the outcome."},{"id":"40","question":"A team needs a quick, simple baseline to classify short text messages as spam or not spam, using word counts as features and assuming the words are independent of each other given the class. Which algorithm matches this description?","options":{"A":"k-means","B":"Principal component analysis","C":"Gradient boosting","D":"Naive Bayes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Naive Bayes applies Bayes\' theorem with the \'naive\' assumption that features are conditionally independent given the class. With word counts it estimates how likely each word is in spam and in non-spam messages and combines those probabilities. The independence assumption is rarely true, but the model is very fast, needs little data and works surprisingly well as a text-classification baseline."},{"id":"41","question":"A neural network is built by stacking several dense layers but with no activation functions between them. Why can\'t it learn complex non-linear patterns, no matter how many layers it has?","options":{"A":"Because dense layers cannot have more than 100 neurons","B":"Because the network would need a larger batch size","C":"Because a composition of linear layers is still a single linear function","D":"Because without activations the network cannot be trained with gradient descent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Each dense layer computes a linear transformation, and stacking linear transformations produces another linear transformation, so the whole network collapses into the equivalent of one linear layer. Non-linear activation functions such as ReLU, sigmoid or tanh between layers are what let deep networks represent curved decision boundaries and complex functions. ReLU, max(0, x), is the most common default in hidden layers."},{"id":"42","question":"A neural network must classify images into one of 10 mutually exclusive categories. Which output layer and loss are the standard choice?","options":{"A":"10 output units with softmax activation, trained with categorical cross-entropy","B":"1 output unit with ReLU activation, trained with mean squared error","C":"10 output units with no activation, trained with mean absolute error","D":"1 output unit with sigmoid activation, trained with binary cross-entropy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Softmax turns the 10 raw scores (logits) into probabilities that are all positive and sum to 1, one per class, and the predicted class is the one with the highest probability. Categorical cross-entropy then penalizes a low probability on the correct class. For multi-label problems, where several classes can be true at once, independent sigmoid outputs with binary cross-entropy are used instead."},{"id":"43","question":"What does the backpropagation algorithm compute during neural network training?","options":{"A":"The best network architecture for the dataset","B":"The gradient of the loss with respect to every weight, by applying the chain rule from the output layer back to the input","C":"The optimal learning rate for each epoch","D":"The predictions of the network on the test set"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Backpropagation runs a forward pass to compute the predictions and the loss, then applies the chain rule of calculus backwards, layer by layer, to compute how much each weight contributed to the error. Those gradients are then used by an optimizer such as SGD or Adam to update the weights. Backpropagation computes the gradients; the optimizer decides how to use them."},{"id":"44","question":"In a very deep network that uses sigmoid activations, the weights of the first layers barely change during training while the last layers learn normally. What is the most likely cause?","options":{"A":"Exploding gradients caused by a learning rate that is too small","B":"Data leakage in the training set","C":"The batch size is too small","D":"Vanishing gradients: multiplying many small derivatives during backpropagation makes the gradients of early layers nearly zero"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The sigmoid\'s derivative is at most 0.25, and backpropagation multiplies one such factor per layer, so the gradient reaching the first layers shrinks exponentially with depth. Those layers then learn extremely slowly. Common fixes are ReLU-family activations, careful weight initialization, batch normalization and residual (skip) connections, which give gradients a shortcut path."},{"id":"45","question":"Why are convolutional neural networks (CNNs) better suited to images than fully connected networks?","options":{"A":"They do not need any training data","B":"They convert images to text before classifying them","C":"Small filters slide over the image and share their weights, detecting local patterns anywhere with far fewer parameters","D":"They process each pixel completely independently of its neighbors"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A convolutional layer applies small learnable filters (for example 3\xd73) across the whole image. Each filter detects a local pattern, such as an edge or texture, wherever it appears, because the same weights are reused at every position. This weight sharing needs far fewer parameters than connecting every pixel to every neuron, and stacking layers builds up from edges to shapes to whole objects."},{"id":"46","question":"Transformer models replaced recurrent neural networks (RNNs) as the standard architecture for language. Which mechanism lets a transformer relate each word directly to every other word in the sequence, and process the sequence in parallel?","options":{"A":"Self-attention","B":"Max pooling","C":"Dropout","D":"Bagging"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Self-attention computes, for each token, a weighted combination of all the tokens in the sequence, with weights that reflect how relevant each one is. Distant words can therefore influence each other directly, without passing information step by step as an RNN does. Because there is no sequential dependency between positions, the whole sequence can be processed in parallel. Positional encodings are added so the model still knows word order."},{"id":"47","question":"A startup needs an image classifier for 5 types of manufacturing defects but has only 800 labeled images. Training a large CNN from scratch overfits badly. What is the most effective approach?","options":{"A":"Train an even larger network from scratch for more epochs","B":"Transfer learning: start from a model pre-trained on a large image dataset and fine-tune it on the defect images","C":"Convert the images to text and use Naive Bayes","D":"Use k-means to cluster the 800 images into 5 groups and treat them as labels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Transfer learning reuses a model pre-trained on a large dataset such as ImageNet, whose early layers already detect general visual features like edges, textures and shapes. You replace the final layer with one for your 5 classes and train it, often first with the pre-trained layers frozen and then fine-tuning some of them with a small learning rate. This needs far less data and compute than training from scratch."},{"id":"48","question":"A demand-forecasting model performed well for a year, but its accuracy dropped after the company opened stores in a new region with very different customers. The relationship between features and demand is unchanged, but the input data now looks different from the training data. What is this called, and what is the usual response?","options":{"A":"Overfitting; add dropout","B":"Data leakage; remove the region feature","C":"Data drift; monitor input distributions and retrain the model with recent, representative data","D":"Vanishing gradients; switch to ReLU activations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Data drift (covariate shift) occurs when the distribution of the inputs in production changes compared with the training data. Concept drift is related but different: the relationship between inputs and target itself changes. Both degrade models over time, so production models need monitoring of input distributions and performance, and periodic retraining on recent data."},{"id":"49","question":"A model performs well offline, but right after deployment its predictions are much worse. The team finds that during training a \'days_since_signup\' feature was computed in a batch SQL job, while the online service computes it in application code that rounds differently and uses another time zone. What is this problem called?","options":{"A":"Concept drift","B":"Class imbalance","C":"Underfitting","D":"Training–serving skew"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Training–serving skew is a difference between how data is processed during training and how it is processed when serving predictions. Even small inconsistencies in feature computation give the model inputs it never saw during training. Common remedies are to share the same transformation code or pipeline between training and serving, use a feature store, and compare the features logged at serving time with the training features."},{"id":"50","question":"A hiring-screening model was trained on 10 years of past hiring decisions. An audit shows it rates equally qualified candidates from one group lower than others, reproducing patterns from historical decisions. What is the root cause, and what is an appropriate first step?","options":{"A":"The model is underfitting; add more layers","B":"The training labels reflect historical bias; audit the data and evaluate metrics per group before and after deployment","C":"The learning rate is too high; lower it","D":"The test set is too large; reduce it"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A model learns whatever patterns are in its labels, including human biases in past decisions. Simply removing the sensitive attribute is often not enough, because other features such as zip code or school can act as proxies for it. Appropriate steps include auditing the data and labels, measuring performance and error rates per group, choosing fairness criteria with stakeholders, and keeping human oversight for high-impact decisions."}]},{"id":"ml-fundamentals-es","title":"Fundamentos de Machine Learning","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"Un banco tiene 5 a\xf1os de solicitudes de pr\xe9stamo, cada una etiquetada como \'pagado\' o \'impago\'. Quiere un modelo que prediga el resultado de solicitudes nuevas. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico encaja con este problema?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje no supervisado, porque el modelo tiene que descubrir patrones por s\xed solo","B":"Aprendizaje supervisado, porque cada ejemplo hist\xf3rico tiene una etiqueta conocida","C":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo, porque el modelo recibe una recompensa por cada pr\xe9stamo acertado","D":"Clustering, porque las solicitudes deben agruparse en pagadas e impagas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El aprendizaje supervisado aprende a relacionar entradas con un objetivo conocido usando ejemplos etiquetados. Aqu\xed cada solicitud hist\xf3rica tiene su etiqueta (pagado o impago), as\xed que el modelo aprende de esas respuestas y predice la etiqueta de solicitudes nuevas."},{"id":"2","question":"Una inmobiliaria quiere predecir el precio de venta, en d\xf3lares, de casas a partir de su tama\xf1o, ubicaci\xf3n y antig\xfcedad. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de tarea supervisada es?","options":{"A":"Clasificaci\xf3n binaria","B":"Clasificaci\xf3n multiclase","C":"Regresi\xf3n","D":"Clustering"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La regresi\xf3n predice un valor num\xe9rico continuo, como un precio en d\xf3lares. La clasificaci\xf3n predice una categor\xeda discreta, como \'barata\' o \'cara\'. Como el objetivo es un n\xfamero que puede tomar cualquier valor en un rango, es una tarea de regresi\xf3n."},{"id":"3","question":"Un equipo construye un agente que aprende a jugar un videojuego. El agente prueba acciones, gana puntos cuando avanza y pierde puntos cuando falla, y poco a poco mejora su estrategia. \xbfQu\xe9 paradigma de aprendizaje es?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje supervisado","B":"Aprendizaje no supervisado","C":"Aprendizaje semisupervisado","D":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo"},"correct":"D","explanation":"En el aprendizaje por refuerzo un agente interact\xfaa con un entorno, toma acciones y recibe recompensas o penalizaciones. Aprende una pol\xedtica que maximiza la recompensa total a lo largo del tiempo. No hay un dataset etiquetado con las acciones correctas; la retroalimentaci\xf3n viene del entorno."},{"id":"4","question":"Un cient\xedfico de datos divide un dataset en conjuntos de entrenamiento, validaci\xf3n y prueba (test). \xbfCu\xe1l es el prop\xf3sito principal del conjunto de validaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Comparar modelos y ajustar hiperpar\xe1metros sin tocar el conjunto de test","B":"A\xf1adir m\xe1s datos al entrenamiento para mejorar la precisi\xf3n","C":"Reportar a los interesados el rendimiento final del modelo elegido","D":"Guardar los ejemplos a los que les falta la etiqueta"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El conjunto de validaci\xf3n se usa durante el desarrollo para comparar modelos y elegir hiperpar\xe1metros. El conjunto de test se aparta y se usa una sola vez al final para estimar c\xf3mo rendir\xe1 el modelo elegido con datos nuevos. Si ajustas usando el test, su puntuaci\xf3n se vuelve optimista y deja de reflejar el rendimiento real."},{"id":"5","question":"Un modelo que predice reingresos hospitalarios alcanza un 99% de exactitud en validaci\xf3n, pero rinde mal en producci\xf3n. El equipo descubre que una feature, \'seguimiento_programado_al_alta\', solo se rellena despu\xe9s de que el paciente ya reingres\xf3. \xbfQu\xe9 problema es este?","options":{"A":"Underfitting (subajuste)","B":"Desbalance de clases","C":"Fuga de datos (data leakage)","D":"Desvanecimiento del gradiente"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La fuga de datos ocurre cuando los datos de entrenamiento contienen informaci\xf3n que no estar\xe1 disponible en el momento de predecir, a menudo derivada del propio objetivo. El modelo aprende a apoyarse en ese atajo, obtiene puntuaciones irreales en validaci\xf3n y falla en producci\xf3n, donde esa feature falta o es distinta."},{"id":"6","question":"Un modelo tiene 98% de exactitud en entrenamiento y 71% en validaci\xf3n. Al a\xf1adir m\xe1s \xe9pocas de entrenamiento, la exactitud de entrenamiento sube pero la de validaci\xf3n baja. \xbfCu\xe1l es el diagn\xf3stico m\xe1s probable?","options":{"A":"Underfitting: el modelo es demasiado simple","B":"Overfitting: el modelo memoriza los datos de entrenamiento en vez de generalizar","C":"La tasa de aprendizaje es demasiado baja","D":"El conjunto de validaci\xf3n es demasiado grande"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Una brecha grande entre el rendimiento en entrenamiento y en validaci\xf3n, que crece al seguir entrenando, es la se\xf1al cl\xe1sica de overfitting. El modelo se ajusta al ruido y a detalles propios de los datos de entrenamiento. Remedios t\xedpicos: m\xe1s datos, regularizaci\xf3n, un modelo m\xe1s simple, dropout o early stopping."},{"id":"7","question":"Un modelo lineal entrenado para predecir el consumo de energ\xeda tiene un error alto tanto en entrenamiento como en validaci\xf3n, y ambos errores son muy parecidos. \xbfCu\xe1l es el problema m\xe1s probable y su soluci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Underfitting; usar un modelo m\xe1s expresivo o a\xf1adir features m\xe1s informativas","B":"Overfitting; a\xf1adir una regularizaci\xf3n m\xe1s fuerte","C":"Fuga de datos; quitar las features que usan informaci\xf3n futura","D":"El conjunto de test es muy peque\xf1o; recolectar m\xe1s datos de test"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cuando el error de entrenamiento y el de validaci\xf3n son ambos altos y parecidos, el modelo es demasiado simple para capturar el patr\xf3n subyacente. Es underfitting, o sesgo alto. A\xf1adir regularizaci\xf3n lo empeorar\xeda; en su lugar, usa un modelo m\xe1s flexible, crea mejores features o entrena m\xe1s tiempo."},{"id":"8","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n describe mejor el equilibrio sesgo–varianza (bias–variance tradeoff)?","options":{"A":"Aumentar la complejidad del modelo siempre reduce tanto el sesgo como la varianza","B":"El sesgo mide cu\xe1nto cambian las predicciones entre conjuntos de entrenamiento, y la varianza mide el error sistem\xe1tico","C":"El sesgo y la varianza se eliminan por completo con suficientes \xe9pocas","D":"Los modelos simples tienden a tener sesgo alto y varianza baja, y los complejos sesgo bajo y varianza alta"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El sesgo es el error por suposiciones demasiado simples: el modelo falla sistem\xe1ticamente el patr\xf3n real. La varianza es el error por sensibilidad a los datos de entrenamiento concretos: el modelo cambia mucho si se entrena con otra muestra. Aumentar la complejidad suele bajar el sesgo pero subir la varianza, as\xed que el objetivo es un equilibrio que minimice el error total con datos nuevos."},{"id":"9","question":"Un modelo lineal se entrena con un dataset de 500 features, muchas de ellas probablemente irrelevantes. El equipo quiere que el modelo lleve autom\xe1ticamente a exactamente cero los pesos de las features in\xfatiles. \xbfQu\xe9 regularizaci\xf3n debe usar?","options":{"A":"Regularizaci\xf3n L2 (Ridge)","B":"Regularizaci\xf3n L1 (Lasso)","C":"Aumentar la tasa de aprendizaje","D":"Eliminar el t\xe9rmino de sesgo (bias)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La regularizaci\xf3n L1 suma a la p\xe9rdida el valor absoluto de los pesos. Su geometr\xeda empuja muchos pesos a exactamente cero, lo que produce un modelo disperso (sparse) que en la pr\xe1ctica selecciona features. La regularizaci\xf3n L2 suma los pesos al cuadrado y los encoge hacia cero, pero rara vez los deja exactamente en cero."},{"id":"10","question":"Durante el entrenamiento de una red neuronal, una t\xe9cnica pone a cero, al azar, la salida de una fracci\xf3n de neuronas en cada paso, y usa todas las neuronas en inferencia. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica es y para qu\xe9 sirve?","options":{"A":"Batch normalization, para acelerar la convergencia","B":"Pruning (poda), para reducir el modelo antes de desplegarlo","C":"Dropout, para reducir el overfitting evitando que las neuronas se coadapten","D":"Gradient clipping, para evitar la explosi\xf3n de gradientes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dropout desactiva al azar una fracci\xf3n de neuronas en cada paso de entrenamiento. La red no puede depender de ninguna neurona en particular, as\xed que aprende representaciones m\xe1s robustas y redundantes, lo que reduce el overfitting. En inferencia todas las neuronas est\xe1n activas y las salidas se escalan para que los valores esperados coincidan con el entrenamiento."},{"id":"11","question":"Un equipo solo tiene 1.200 ejemplos etiquetados. Una \xfanica divisi\xf3n 80/20 de entrenamiento/validaci\xf3n da puntuaciones muy distintas seg\xfan la semilla aleatoria. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica da una estimaci\xf3n m\xe1s fiable del rendimiento del modelo?","options":{"A":"Validaci\xf3n cruzada k-fold","B":"Entrenar durante m\xe1s \xe9pocas","C":"Usar tambi\xe9n el conjunto de test como validaci\xf3n","D":"Aumentar el tama\xf1o del batch"},"correct":"A","explanation":"En la validaci\xf3n cruzada k-fold los datos se dividen en k partes (folds); el modelo se entrena k veces, cada vez validando con un fold distinto y entrenando con el resto. Se promedian las k puntuaciones, as\xed que cada ejemplo se usa para validar exactamente una vez y la estimaci\xf3n depende mucho menos de una divisi\xf3n con suerte o sin ella. Es especialmente valiosa con datasets peque\xf1os."},{"id":"12","question":"Un dataset para detectar una enfermedad rara tiene un 2% de casos positivos. Tras una divisi\xf3n aleatoria en entrenamiento/validaci\xf3n, un fold de validaci\xf3n casi no tiene ejemplos positivos. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xeda usar el equipo?","options":{"A":"Una tasa de aprendizaje mayor","B":"Una divisi\xf3n temporal","C":"Quitar los ejemplos positivos de la validaci\xf3n","D":"Una divisi\xf3n estratificada, para que cada subconjunto mantenga la proporci\xf3n del 2%"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La divisi\xf3n estratificada conserva la proporci\xf3n de clases en cada subconjunto. Con clases raras, una divisi\xf3n puramente aleatoria puede dejar un fold con muy pocos positivos o ninguno, lo que vuelve inestables o indefinidas m\xe9tricas como el recall. La validaci\xf3n cruzada k-fold estratificada aplica la misma idea a cada fold."},{"id":"13","question":"En un dataset de fraude, el 99,5% de las transacciones son leg\xedtimas. Un modelo que predice \'leg\xedtima\' para todas las transacciones alcanza un 99,5% de exactitud. \xbfQu\xe9 demuestra esto?","options":{"A":"Que el modelo es excelente y est\xe1 listo para producci\xf3n","B":"Que la exactitud enga\xf1a con datos desbalanceados; se necesitan m\xe9tricas como precision, recall o PR AUC","C":"Que el dataset necesita m\xe1s transacciones leg\xedtimas","D":"Que la exactitud debe sustituirse por el error cuadr\xe1tico medio"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Con datos muy desbalanceados, un modelo trivial que siempre predice la clase mayoritaria obtiene una exactitud alt\xedsima sin detectar ning\xfan fraude. Es la llamada paradoja de la exactitud. Las m\xe9tricas centradas en la clase minoritaria, como precision, recall, F1 o el \xe1rea bajo la curva precision–recall, muestran la utilidad real del modelo."},{"id":"14","question":"Un filtro de spam mueve correos a la carpeta de spam. Los usuarios se quejan de que se pierden correos importantes en spam. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica debe priorizar mejorar el equipo para la clase \'spam\'?","options":{"A":"Recall (exhaustividad)","B":"La exactitud en el conjunto de entrenamiento","C":"Precision (precisi\xf3n)","D":"El error absoluto medio"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La precision mide, de todo lo que el modelo marc\xf3 como spam, cu\xe1nto era realmente spam: TP / (TP + FP). Los correos importantes enviados a spam son falsos positivos, as\xed que mejorar la precision reduce directamente ese problema. El recall mide cu\xe1nto del spam real se detect\xf3, que no es la queja en este caso."},{"id":"15","question":"Un modelo revisa im\xe1genes m\xe9dicas para detectar una enfermedad grave pero tratable. No detectar a un paciente enfermo es mucho m\xe1s costoso que mandar a un paciente sano a una prueba adicional. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica se debe priorizar?","options":{"A":"Recall (sensibilidad)","B":"Precision","C":"Especificidad","D":"Exactitud"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El recall mide, de todos los pacientes realmente enfermos, cu\xe1ntos detect\xf3 el modelo: TP / (TP + FN). Cuando los falsos negativos son el error m\xe1s caro, como no detectar una enfermedad grave, se prioriza el recall, aceptando normalmente m\xe1s falsos positivos que una prueba posterior puede descartar."},{"id":"16","question":"Un equipo necesita un \xfanico n\xfamero que equilibre precision y recall en un problema de clasificaci\xf3n desbalanceado, y que sea bajo si cualquiera de los dos es bajo. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica encaja?","options":{"A":"Exactitud","B":"F1 score","C":"La media aritm\xe9tica de precision y recall","D":"R cuadrado"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El F1 score es la media arm\xf3nica de precision y recall: 2\xb7P\xb7R / (P + R). La media arm\xf3nica est\xe1 dominada por el valor m\xe1s peque\xf1o, as\xed que un modelo con precision 1,0 y recall 0,1 obtiene un F1 de unos 0,18, mientras que una media simple dar\xeda 0,55. Por eso F1 es una buena m\xe9trica \xfanica cuando importan los dos tipos de error."},{"id":"17","question":"Dos clasificadores binarios devuelven probabilidades. El equipo quiere comparar qu\xe9 tan bien ordena cada uno los ejemplos positivos por encima de los negativos en todos los umbrales posibles, antes de elegir un umbral. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica est\xe1 pensada para esto?","options":{"A":"La exactitud con umbral 0,5","B":"El error cuadr\xe1tico medio de las probabilidades","C":"El n\xfamero de verdaderos positivos","D":"El \xe1rea bajo la curva ROC (ROC AUC)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La curva ROC representa la tasa de verdaderos positivos frente a la tasa de falsos positivos para cada umbral. El \xe1rea bajo ella, ROC AUC, resume el rendimiento en todos los umbrales y equivale a la probabilidad de que el modelo punt\xfae un positivo al azar por encima de un negativo al azar. Un AUC de 0,5 equivale a adivinar al azar y 1,0 a un orden perfecto."},{"id":"18","question":"Un modelo de fraude se eval\xfaa con datos en los que solo el 0,1% de las transacciones son fraude. Su ROC AUC es 0,97, pero en la pr\xe1ctica la mayor\xeda de sus alertas son falsas alarmas. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica reflejar\xeda mejor el rendimiento en la clase rara?","options":{"A":"Exactitud","B":"Especificidad","C":"El \xe1rea bajo la curva precision–recall (PR AUC)","D":"El n\xfamero de verdaderos negativos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Con un desbalance extremo, la tasa de falsos positivos se mantiene muy baja aunque los falsos positivos superen a los verdaderos positivos, porque el n\xfamero de negativos es enorme. Por eso el ROC AUC puede verse excelente mientras la precision es mala. La curva precision–recall se centra en la clase positiva, as\xed que el PR AUC (tambi\xe9n llamado average precision) deja ver el problema."},{"id":"19","question":"La matriz de confusi\xf3n de un clasificador binario muestra: 80 verdaderos positivos, 20 falsos negativos, 30 falsos positivos y 870 verdaderos negativos. \xbfCu\xe1les son su precision y su recall?","options":{"A":"Precision 0,80, recall 0,73","B":"Precision 0,73, recall 0,80","C":"Precision 0,95, recall 0,80","D":"Precision 0,80, recall 0,97"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Precision = TP / (TP + FP) = 80 / (80 + 30) ≈ 0,73. Recall = TP / (TP + FN) = 80 / (80 + 20) = 0,80. Un error com\xfan es intercambiarlas: la precision se refiere a los positivos predichos, y el recall a los positivos reales."},{"id":"20","question":"Un modelo de tiempo de entrega se eval\xfaa con dos m\xe9tricas. El equipo ve que el RMSE es mucho mayor que el MAE. \xbfQu\xe9 indica esto lo m\xe1s probablemente?","options":{"A":"Que algunas predicciones tienen errores muy grandes, que el RMSE penaliza","B":"Que el modelo est\xe1 perfectamente calibrado","C":"Que el modelo tiene underfitting en todos los ejemplos por igual","D":"Que el MAE siempre es mayor que el RMSE, as\xed que las m\xe9tricas est\xe1n intercambiadas"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El RMSE eleva los errores al cuadrado antes de promediar, as\xed que los errores grandes pesan mucho m\xe1s que los peque\xf1os. El MAE promedia los errores absolutos y trata igual cada unidad de error. Cuando el RMSE es mucho mayor que el MAE, unas pocas predicciones tienen errores grandes, como outliers o casos raros que el modelo maneja mal. El RMSE siempre es mayor o igual que el MAE."},{"id":"21","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 funci\xf3n de p\xe9rdida es la elecci\xf3n est\xe1ndar para entrenar un clasificador binario que devuelve una probabilidad, como la regresi\xf3n log\xedstica?","options":{"A":"Error cuadr\xe1tico medio","B":"Error absoluto medio","C":"Entrop\xeda cruzada binaria (log loss)","D":"Hinge loss calculada sobre las etiquetas predichas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La entrop\xeda cruzada binaria, tambi\xe9n llamada log loss, mide cu\xe1n lejos est\xe1 la probabilidad predicha de la etiqueta real: −[y\xb7log(p) + (1−y)\xb7log(1−p)]. Penaliza mucho las predicciones equivocadas hechas con mucha confianza, como predecir 0,99 cuando la etiqueta real es 0, y funciona bien con el entrenamiento por gradiente de clasificadores probabil\xedsticos."},{"id":"22","question":"Al entrenar un modelo con descenso por gradiente, la p\xe9rdida sube y baja de forma descontrolada y a veces crece hasta infinito en lugar de bajar. \xbfCu\xe1l es la causa m\xe1s probable?","options":{"A":"El conjunto de entrenamiento es demasiado grande","B":"El modelo tiene muy pocos par\xe1metros","C":"La tasa de aprendizaje es demasiado peque\xf1a","D":"La tasa de aprendizaje es demasiado grande"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La tasa de aprendizaje controla el tama\xf1o de cada paso en la direcci\xf3n que reduce la p\xe9rdida. Si es demasiado grande, los pasos se pasan del m\xednimo, rebotan de un lado a otro y pueden divergir, haciendo crecer la p\xe9rdida. Si es demasiado peque\xf1a, el entrenamiento es estable pero muy lento. Bajar la tasa de aprendizaje, o usar un calendario que la reduzca con el tiempo, suele resolver la divergencia."},{"id":"23","question":"Un dataset tiene 50 millones de filas. Calcular el gradiente sobre todo el dataset en cada actualizaci\xf3n es demasiado lento. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque es el est\xe1ndar para entrenar con datasets grandes?","options":{"A":"Descenso por gradiente con mini-batches, calculando cada actualizaci\xf3n sobre un peque\xf1o subconjunto aleatorio de ejemplos","B":"Descenso por gradiente con el lote completo y una tasa de aprendizaje mayor","C":"Entrenar solo con las primeras 1.000 filas","D":"Sustituir el descenso por gradiente por clustering k-means"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El descenso por gradiente con mini-batches estima el gradiente con un peque\xf1o lote aleatorio (por ejemplo, de 32 a 512 ejemplos) y actualiza los par\xe1metros tras cada lote. Las actualizaciones son mucho m\xe1s baratas y frecuentes, el ruido de la estimaci\xf3n puede ayudar a salir de regiones malas, y los lotes aprovechan bien el paralelismo de las GPU. Una pasada por todos los lotes se llama \xe9poca."},{"id":"24","question":"Un modelo de k vecinos m\xe1s cercanos (k-NN) usa dos features: ingreso anual (valores de unos 20.000 a 200.000) y n\xfamero de hijos (valores de 0 a 5). Las predicciones dependen casi por completo del ingreso. \xbfQu\xe9 paso de preprocesamiento lo arreglar\xeda?","options":{"A":"Aplicar one-hot encoding a las dos features","B":"Escalar las features, por ejemplo con estandarizaci\xf3n o normalizaci\xf3n min-max","C":"Eliminar la feature de n\xfamero de hijos","D":"Usar m\xe1s vecinos (aumentar k)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los modelos basados en distancias, como k-NN, y los entrenados con descenso por gradiente son sensibles a la escala de las features. Una diferencia de 10.000 en el ingreso eclipsa una diferencia de 3 hijos, as\xed que el ingreso domina la distancia. La estandarizaci\xf3n (restar la media y dividir entre la desviaci\xf3n est\xe1ndar) o el escalado min-max ponen las features en rangos comparables. Los modelos basados en \xe1rboles casi no se ven afectados por la escala."},{"id":"25","question":"Una feature \'color\' tiene los valores rojo, verde y azul. Un equipo la codifica como rojo = 1, verde = 2, azul = 3 y se la da a un modelo lineal. \xbfPor qu\xe9 es problem\xe1tico y cu\xe1l es la alternativa habitual?","options":{"A":"No es problem\xe1tico; los c\xf3digos num\xe9ricos siempre son preferibles","B":"Los modelos lineales no admiten features num\xe9ricas; hay que eliminar la columna","C":"Los c\xf3digos implican un orden y una distancia que no existen; la alternativa habitual es one-hot encoding","D":"Hay que ordenar los valores alfab\xe9ticamente antes de codificarlos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los c\xf3digos enteros implican que azul es \'mayor\' que rojo y que verde est\xe1 justo a medio camino, un orden que el modelo intentar\xe1 usar aunque no tenga sentido. El one-hot encoding crea una columna binaria por categor\xeda (es_rojo, es_verde, es_azul), as\xed que cada color recibe su propio peso independiente. La codificaci\xf3n con enteros s\xed es adecuada para categor\xedas realmente ordinales, como peque\xf1o, mediano y grande."},{"id":"26","question":"Un modelo de recomendaci\xf3n usa una feature \'id_producto\' con 2 millones de valores distintos. El one-hot encoding crea una entrada enorme y dispersa, y el modelo no puede aprender que algunos productos se parecen. \xbfQu\xe9 representaci\xf3n es la soluci\xf3n habitual?","options":{"A":"Embeddings aprendidos: asignar a cada id_producto un vector denso de pocas dimensiones","B":"Codificaci\xf3n con enteros ordenada por id_producto","C":"Eliminar la feature","D":"Escalado min-max de id_producto"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Una capa de embeddings asigna a cada categor\xeda un vector denso de, por ejemplo, 32 o 64 n\xfameros que se aprenden durante el entrenamiento. Los productos que se comportan de forma parecida acaban con vectores parecidos, lo que permite al modelo compartir lo aprendido entre art\xedculos relacionados. El truco de hashing de features es otra opci\xf3n para limitar el tama\xf1o de la entrada cuando no se necesita similitud."},{"id":"27","question":"Un dataset de clientes tiene un 15% de valores faltantes en la columna \'edad\'. Eliminar esas filas quitar\xeda demasiados datos. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque es un primer paso razonable?","options":{"A":"Sustituir las edades faltantes por 0","B":"Rellenar las edades faltantes con la media calculada sobre todo el dataset, incluido el test","C":"Eliminar la columna \'edad\' de todos los modelos","D":"Imputar las edades faltantes con la mediana del conjunto de entrenamiento y, opcionalmente, a\xf1adir una feature binaria \'edad_faltante\'"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Imputar con un estad\xedstico robusto como la mediana, calculado solo con el conjunto de entrenamiento, conserva las filas sin inventar valores extremos. A\xf1adir una feature indicadora de valor faltante permite al modelo aprender si el hecho de que falte el dato es informativo en s\xed mismo. Rellenar con 0 crea edades falsas e imposibles, y calcular estad\xedsticos con el test provoca fuga de datos."},{"id":"28","question":"Un dataset de abandono (churn) tiene un 95% de clientes que se quedan y un 5% que se van. El modelo casi nunca predice abandono. \xbfQu\xe9 cambio hace directamente que el modelo preste m\xe1s atenci\xf3n a la clase minoritaria durante el entrenamiento?","options":{"A":"Quitar todos los clientes que abandonaron de los datos de entrenamiento","B":"Usar pesos por clase para que los errores en la clase minoritaria cuesten m\xe1s en la p\xe9rdida","C":"Evaluar con exactitud en lugar de recall","D":"Aplicar sobremuestreo a los conjuntos de validaci\xf3n y test"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los pesos por clase multiplican la p\xe9rdida de cada ejemplo por un peso que depende de su clase, as\xed que los errores en los pocos clientes que abandonan cuestan m\xe1s y el modelo se ve empujado a detectarlos. Remuestrear el conjunto de entrenamiento (sobremuestrear la clase minoritaria o submuestrear la mayoritaria) tiene un efecto parecido. El remuestreo solo se aplica a los datos de entrenamiento, nunca a validaci\xf3n ni a test, que deben reflejar la distribuci\xf3n real."},{"id":"29","question":"Un clasificador de impago devuelve probabilidades y, por defecto, un pr\xe9stamo se marca como riesgoso si la probabilidad supera 0,5. El negocio dice que quiere detectar m\xe1s pr\xe9stamos riesgosos, aunque implique revisar m\xe1s pr\xe9stamos seguros. \xbfCu\xe1l es el cambio m\xe1s simple, sin reentrenar?","options":{"A":"Subir el umbral de decisi\xf3n a 0,8","B":"Cambiar la funci\xf3n de p\xe9rdida a error cuadr\xe1tico medio","C":"Bajar el umbral de decisi\xf3n, por ejemplo a 0,3","D":"Quitar la salida de probabilidad y usar solo las etiquetas predichas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El umbral de decisi\xf3n convierte las probabilidades en etiquetas. Bajarlo marca m\xe1s pr\xe9stamos como riesgosos, lo que aumenta el recall a costa de la precision. El umbral es una decisi\xf3n de negocio y debe elegirse con el conjunto de validaci\xf3n, por ejemplo con la curva precision–recall o el coste esperado de cada tipo de error. El valor por defecto de 0,5 no tiene nada de especial."},{"id":"30","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un hiperpar\xe1metro y no un par\xe1metro del modelo?","options":{"A":"La profundidad m\xe1xima de un \xe1rbol de decisi\xf3n","B":"Los pesos de una regresi\xf3n lineal","C":"Los umbrales de corte que un \xe1rbol de decisi\xf3n aprende en cada nodo","D":"Los sesgos (biases) de las neuronas de una red neuronal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Los par\xe1metros se aprenden de los datos durante el entrenamiento, como los pesos, los sesgos y los umbrales de corte de un \xe1rbol. Los hiperpar\xe1metros se fijan antes de entrenar y controlan c\xf3mo se aprende o cu\xe1n complejo puede ser el modelo, como la profundidad m\xe1xima del \xe1rbol, la tasa de aprendizaje, el n\xfamero de capas o la fuerza de la regularizaci\xf3n. Los hiperpar\xe1metros se eligen con un conjunto de validaci\xf3n o validaci\xf3n cruzada."},{"id":"31","question":"Cuando un \xe1rbol de decisi\xf3n de clasificaci\xf3n elige con qu\xe9 feature y umbral dividir un nodo, \xbfqu\xe9 intenta hacer normalmente?","options":{"A":"Elegir la feature con el mayor rango num\xe9rico","B":"Elegir la divisi\xf3n que deje los nodos hijos lo m\xe1s puros posible, por ejemplo minimizando la impureza de Gini o la entrop\xeda","C":"Elegir una feature al azar en cada nodo","D":"Elegir la divisi\xf3n que ponga el mismo n\xfamero de ejemplos en cada hijo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un \xe1rbol de clasificaci\xf3n eval\xfaa las divisiones candidatas y elige la que m\xe1s reduce la impureza, medida con la impureza de Gini o la entrop\xeda (ganancia de informaci\xf3n). Un nodo puro contiene ejemplos de una sola clase. Los \xe1rboles de regresi\xf3n usan la misma idea, pero minimizan la varianza o el error cuadr\xe1tico del objetivo en cada hijo."},{"id":"32","question":"Un random forest entrena muchos \xe1rboles de decisi\xf3n y promedia sus predicciones. \xbfQu\xe9 dos fuentes de aleatoriedad hacen que los \xe1rboles sean distintos entre s\xed?","options":{"A":"Tasas de aprendizaje aleatorias y funciones de p\xe9rdida aleatorias","B":"Etiquetas aleatorias y conjuntos de test aleatorios","C":"Profundidades aleatorias y funciones de activaci\xf3n aleatorias","D":"Cada \xe1rbol se entrena con una muestra bootstrap de las filas, y cada divisi\xf3n considera solo un subconjunto aleatorio de features"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Un random forest usa bagging (bootstrap aggregating): cada \xe1rbol se entrena con una muestra aleatoria de filas tomada con reemplazo. Adem\xe1s, cada divisi\xf3n solo considera un subconjunto aleatorio de features, lo que reduce la correlaci\xf3n entre \xe1rboles. Promediar muchos \xe1rboles diversos y de alta varianza reduce mucho la varianza y, por tanto, el overfitting, manteniendo el sesgo bajo de los \xe1rboles profundos."},{"id":"33","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo construye el gradient boosting (como en XGBoost o LightGBM) su conjunto de \xe1rboles?","options":{"A":"En secuencia: cada nuevo \xe1rbol poco profundo se entrena para corregir los errores (residuos) de los \xe1rboles construidos hasta el momento","B":"En paralelo: cada \xe1rbol se entrena de forma independiente con una muestra bootstrap y se promedian los resultados","C":"Entrenando un \xfanico \xe1rbol muy profundo y pod\xe1ndolo","D":"Agrupando los datos en clusters y entrenando un \xe1rbol por cluster"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El gradient boosting a\xf1ade \xe1rboles de uno en uno. Cada \xe1rbol nuevo, normalmente poco profundo, se ajusta a los errores que a\xfan comete el conjunto actual (m\xe1s exactamente, al gradiente negativo de la p\xe9rdida), y su aporte se escala con una tasa de aprendizaje. Esto reduce sobre todo el sesgo. Como sigue ajustando los errores, puede sobreajustar si se a\xf1aden demasiados \xe1rboles, por eso es habitual usar early stopping con un conjunto de validaci\xf3n."},{"id":"34","question":"Un clasificador de k vecinos m\xe1s cercanos con k = 1 alcanza un 100% de exactitud en el conjunto de entrenamiento, pero rinde mal con datos nuevos. \xbfCu\xe1l es el cambio m\xe1s adecuado?","options":{"A":"Fijar k = 0","B":"Quitar el escalado de features","C":"Aumentar k y elegir su valor con validaci\xf3n cruzada","D":"Entrenar el modelo durante m\xe1s \xe9pocas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Con k = 1, cada punto de entrenamiento es su propio vecino m\xe1s cercano, as\xed que el modelo memoriza el conjunto de entrenamiento y su frontera de decisi\xf3n sigue cada punto ruidoso: varianza alta. Valores de k mayores promedian m\xe1s vecinos y dan fronteras m\xe1s suaves, que suelen generalizar mejor, aunque un k demasiado grande provoca underfitting. k-NN no tiene \xe9pocas de entrenamiento; simplemente guarda los datos."},{"id":"35","question":"Un equipo de marketing quiere segmentar clientes en grupos seg\xfan su comportamiento de compra, sin etiquetas predefinidas. Usan k-means pero no saben cu\xe1ntos clusters elegir. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque ayuda a elegir k?","options":{"A":"Usar la exactitud sobre un conjunto de test etiquetado","B":"Graficar la suma de cuadrados dentro de los clusters para varios valores de k y buscar el \'codo\', o comparar el coeficiente de silueta","C":"Elegir siempre k igual al n\xfamero de features","D":"Elegir el k con la menor suma de cuadrados dentro de los clusters, aunque k sea igual al n\xfamero de clientes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La suma de cuadrados dentro de los clusters (inercia) siempre baja al aumentar k y llega a cero cuando cada punto es su propio cluster, as\xed que su m\xednimo no sirve. El m\xe9todo del codo busca el k a partir del cual a\xf1adir clusters aporta mejoras peque\xf1as. El coeficiente de silueta mide cu\xe1n bien encaja cada punto en su cluster comparado con el cluster vecino m\xe1s cercano. La interpretabilidad para el negocio tambi\xe9n importa."},{"id":"36","question":"Un dataset tiene 300 features num\xe9ricas muy correlacionadas. El equipo quiere reducirlo a unas pocas features nuevas que conserven la mayor parte de la variaci\xf3n de los datos, para visualizar y acelerar el entrenamiento. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica encaja?","options":{"A":"One-hot encoding","B":"Clustering k-means","C":"Regularizaci\xf3n L2","D":"An\xe1lisis de componentes principales (PCA)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El PCA encuentra nuevos ejes ortogonales, los componentes principales, ordenados seg\xfan cu\xe1nta varianza de los datos capturan. Quedarse con los primeros componentes comprime muchas features correlacionadas en unas pocas no correlacionadas, conservando la mayor parte de la informaci\xf3n. Los componentes son combinaciones lineales de las features originales, as\xed que son m\xe1s dif\xedciles de interpretar, y conviene estandarizar las features antes."},{"id":"37","question":"Pese a su nombre, la regresi\xf3n log\xedstica se usa para clasificaci\xf3n. \xbfC\xf3mo convierte una combinaci\xf3n lineal de features en una predicci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Pasa la puntuaci\xf3n lineal por una funci\xf3n sigmoide, que produce una probabilidad entre 0 y 1, y luego la compara con un umbral","B":"Redondea la puntuaci\xf3n lineal a la clase entera m\xe1s cercana","C":"Ajusta una recta distinta para cada clase y elige la m\xe1s larga","D":"Agrupa los datos en clusters y asigna la etiqueta del cluster m\xe1s grande"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La regresi\xf3n log\xedstica calcula z = w\xb7x + b y aplica la funci\xf3n sigmoide σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e^(−z)), que transforma cualquier n\xfamero real en un valor entre 0 y 1 que se interpreta como la probabilidad de la clase positiva. Se entrena con log loss, y un umbral convierte la probabilidad en una clase. Sus pesos son interpretables: cada uno cambia el logaritmo de las probabilidades relativas (log-odds) de la clase positiva."},{"id":"38","question":"Se entrena una m\xe1quina de vectores de soporte (SVM) para un problema de clasificaci\xf3n binaria. \xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n describe la idea central de una SVM lineal?","options":{"A":"Construye muchos \xe1rboles de decisi\xf3n y los promedia","B":"Asigna a cada ejemplo la etiqueta de su vecino m\xe1s cercano","C":"Encuentra la frontera de separaci\xf3n con el mayor margen hasta los puntos m\xe1s cercanos de cada clase","D":"Agrupa los ejemplos en k clusters antes de clasificar"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Una SVM lineal busca el hiperplano que separa las clases con el margen m\xe1s amplio posible, donde el margen es la distancia a los puntos de entrenamiento m\xe1s cercanos, llamados vectores de soporte. Un margen amplio suele generalizar mejor. Un par\xe1metro de margen suave C permite que algunos puntos violen el margen, y el truco del kernel permite a las SVM aprender fronteras no lineales."},{"id":"39","question":"Un equipo quiere saber de qu\xe9 features depende m\xe1s un modelo de gradient boosting ya entrenado, midiendo cu\xe1nto cae el rendimiento en validaci\xf3n cuando se barajan los valores de una feature. \xbfC\xf3mo se llama esta t\xe9cnica?","options":{"A":"One-hot encoding","B":"Importancia de features por permutaci\xf3n","C":"An\xe1lisis de componentes principales","D":"Early stopping"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La importancia por permutaci\xf3n baraja al azar los valores de una feature en el conjunto de validaci\xf3n, rompiendo su relaci\xf3n con el objetivo, y mide cu\xe1nto cae la puntuaci\xf3n del modelo. Una ca\xedda grande significa que el modelo depende mucho de esa feature. Funciona con cualquier modelo, pero las features correlacionadas pueden repartirse la importancia, y una importancia alta muestra lo que el modelo usa, no que la feature cause el resultado."},{"id":"40","question":"Un equipo necesita una l\xednea base r\xe1pida y simple para clasificar mensajes de texto cortos como spam o no spam, usando conteos de palabras como features y suponiendo que las palabras son independientes entre s\xed dada la clase. \xbfQu\xe9 algoritmo encaja con esta descripci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"k-means","B":"An\xe1lisis de componentes principales","C":"Gradient boosting","D":"Naive Bayes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Naive Bayes aplica el teorema de Bayes con la suposici\xf3n \'ingenua\' de que las features son condicionalmente independientes dada la clase. Con conteos de palabras estima cu\xe1n probable es cada palabra en los mensajes de spam y en los que no lo son, y combina esas probabilidades. La suposici\xf3n de independencia rara vez es cierta, pero el modelo es muy r\xe1pido, necesita pocos datos y funciona sorprendentemente bien como l\xednea base de clasificaci\xf3n de texto."},{"id":"41","question":"Se construye una red neuronal apilando varias capas densas, pero sin funciones de activaci\xf3n entre ellas. \xbfPor qu\xe9 no puede aprender patrones no lineales complejos, por muchas capas que tenga?","options":{"A":"Porque las capas densas no pueden tener m\xe1s de 100 neuronas","B":"Porque la red necesitar\xeda un tama\xf1o de batch mayor","C":"Porque una composici\xf3n de capas lineales sigue siendo una \xfanica funci\xf3n lineal","D":"Porque sin activaciones la red no se puede entrenar con descenso por gradiente"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cada capa densa calcula una transformaci\xf3n lineal, y apilar transformaciones lineales produce otra transformaci\xf3n lineal, as\xed que toda la red se reduce al equivalente de una sola capa lineal. Las funciones de activaci\xf3n no lineales entre capas, como ReLU, sigmoide o tanh, son las que permiten a las redes profundas representar fronteras de decisi\xf3n curvas y funciones complejas. ReLU, max(0, x), es la opci\xf3n por defecto m\xe1s com\xfan en las capas ocultas."},{"id":"42","question":"Una red neuronal debe clasificar im\xe1genes en una de 10 categor\xedas mutuamente excluyentes. \xbfQu\xe9 capa de salida y qu\xe9 p\xe9rdida son la elecci\xf3n est\xe1ndar?","options":{"A":"10 unidades de salida con activaci\xf3n softmax, entrenadas con entrop\xeda cruzada categ\xf3rica","B":"1 unidad de salida con activaci\xf3n ReLU, entrenada con error cuadr\xe1tico medio","C":"10 unidades de salida sin activaci\xf3n, entrenadas con error absoluto medio","D":"1 unidad de salida con activaci\xf3n sigmoide, entrenada con entrop\xeda cruzada binaria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La softmax convierte las 10 puntuaciones brutas (logits) en probabilidades positivas que suman 1, una por clase, y la clase predicha es la de mayor probabilidad. La entrop\xeda cruzada categ\xf3rica penaliza entonces una probabilidad baja en la clase correcta. En problemas multietiqueta, donde varias clases pueden ser ciertas a la vez, se usan en cambio salidas sigmoide independientes con entrop\xeda cruzada binaria."},{"id":"43","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 calcula el algoritmo de retropropagaci\xf3n (backpropagation) durante el entrenamiento de una red neuronal?","options":{"A":"La mejor arquitectura de red para el dataset","B":"El gradiente de la p\xe9rdida respecto de cada peso, aplicando la regla de la cadena desde la capa de salida hacia la entrada","C":"La tasa de aprendizaje \xf3ptima para cada \xe9poca","D":"Las predicciones de la red sobre el conjunto de test"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La retropropagaci\xf3n hace una pasada hacia delante para calcular las predicciones y la p\xe9rdida, y luego aplica la regla de la cadena del c\xe1lculo hacia atr\xe1s, capa por capa, para calcular cu\xe1nto contribuy\xf3 cada peso al error. Esos gradientes los usa despu\xe9s un optimizador como SGD o Adam para actualizar los pesos. La retropropagaci\xf3n calcula los gradientes; el optimizador decide c\xf3mo usarlos."},{"id":"44","question":"En una red muy profunda con activaciones sigmoide, los pesos de las primeras capas apenas cambian durante el entrenamiento, mientras las \xfaltimas capas aprenden con normalidad. \xbfCu\xe1l es la causa m\xe1s probable?","options":{"A":"Explosi\xf3n de gradientes por una tasa de aprendizaje demasiado peque\xf1a","B":"Fuga de datos en el conjunto de entrenamiento","C":"El tama\xf1o del batch es demasiado peque\xf1o","D":"Desvanecimiento del gradiente: multiplicar muchas derivadas peque\xf1as durante la retropropagaci\xf3n deja los gradientes de las primeras capas casi en cero"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La derivada de la sigmoide es como m\xe1ximo 0,25, y la retropropagaci\xf3n multiplica un factor as\xed por cada capa, de modo que el gradiente que llega a las primeras capas se encoge exponencialmente con la profundidad. Esas capas aprenden entonces muy lentamente. Las soluciones habituales son activaciones de la familia ReLU, una inicializaci\xf3n cuidadosa de los pesos, batch normalization y conexiones residuales (skip connections), que dan a los gradientes un camino directo."},{"id":"45","question":"\xbfPor qu\xe9 las redes neuronales convolucionales (CNN) se adaptan mejor a las im\xe1genes que las redes totalmente conectadas?","options":{"A":"Porque no necesitan datos de entrenamiento","B":"Porque convierten las im\xe1genes en texto antes de clasificarlas","C":"Porque peque\xf1os filtros recorren la imagen y comparten sus pesos, detectando patrones locales en cualquier posici\xf3n con muchos menos par\xe1metros","D":"Porque procesan cada p\xedxel de forma totalmente independiente de sus vecinos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Una capa convolucional aplica peque\xf1os filtros aprendibles (por ejemplo, de 3\xd73) sobre toda la imagen. Cada filtro detecta un patr\xf3n local, como un borde o una textura, est\xe9 donde est\xe9, porque los mismos pesos se reutilizan en cada posici\xf3n. Este reparto de pesos necesita muchos menos par\xe1metros que conectar cada p\xedxel con cada neurona, y al apilar capas se pasa de bordes a formas y a objetos completos."},{"id":"46","question":"Los modelos transformer sustituyeron a las redes neuronales recurrentes (RNN) como arquitectura est\xe1ndar para el lenguaje. \xbfQu\xe9 mecanismo permite a un transformer relacionar cada palabra directamente con todas las dem\xe1s de la secuencia y procesar la secuencia en paralelo?","options":{"A":"La autoatenci\xf3n (self-attention)","B":"El max pooling","C":"El dropout","D":"El bagging"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La autoatenci\xf3n calcula, para cada token, una combinaci\xf3n ponderada de todos los tokens de la secuencia, con pesos que reflejan cu\xe1n relevante es cada uno. As\xed, palabras lejanas pueden influirse directamente, sin pasar la informaci\xf3n paso a paso como hace una RNN. Como no hay dependencia secuencial entre posiciones, toda la secuencia se puede procesar en paralelo. Se a\xf1aden codificaciones posicionales para que el modelo siga conociendo el orden de las palabras."},{"id":"47","question":"Una startup necesita un clasificador de im\xe1genes para 5 tipos de defectos de fabricaci\xf3n, pero solo tiene 800 im\xe1genes etiquetadas. Entrenar una CNN grande desde cero provoca un overfitting fuerte. \xbfCu\xe1l es el enfoque m\xe1s eficaz?","options":{"A":"Entrenar desde cero una red todav\xeda m\xe1s grande durante m\xe1s \xe9pocas","B":"Transfer learning: partir de un modelo preentrenado con un gran dataset de im\xe1genes y ajustarlo (fine-tuning) con las im\xe1genes de defectos","C":"Convertir las im\xe1genes en texto y usar Naive Bayes","D":"Usar k-means para agrupar las 800 im\xe1genes en 5 grupos y tratarlos como etiquetas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El transfer learning reutiliza un modelo preentrenado con un gran dataset como ImageNet, cuyas primeras capas ya detectan rasgos visuales generales como bordes, texturas y formas. Se sustituye la \xfaltima capa por una con tus 5 clases y se entrena, a menudo primero con las capas preentrenadas congeladas y despu\xe9s ajustando algunas de ellas con una tasa de aprendizaje peque\xf1a. Esto requiere muchos menos datos y c\xf3mputo que entrenar desde cero."},{"id":"48","question":"Un modelo de previsi\xf3n de demanda funcion\xf3 bien durante un a\xf1o, pero su precisi\xf3n cay\xf3 despu\xe9s de que la empresa abriera tiendas en una regi\xf3n nueva con clientes muy distintos. La relaci\xf3n entre las features y la demanda no ha cambiado, pero los datos de entrada ahora se ven distintos de los de entrenamiento. \xbfC\xf3mo se llama esto y cu\xe1l es la respuesta habitual?","options":{"A":"Overfitting; a\xf1adir dropout","B":"Fuga de datos; quitar la feature de regi\xf3n","C":"Deriva de datos (data drift); monitorizar las distribuciones de entrada y reentrenar el modelo con datos recientes y representativos","D":"Desvanecimiento del gradiente; cambiar a activaciones ReLU"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La deriva de datos (data drift, o covariate shift) ocurre cuando la distribuci\xf3n de las entradas en producci\xf3n cambia respecto a la de entrenamiento. La deriva de concepto (concept drift) es parecida pero distinta: cambia la propia relaci\xf3n entre las entradas y el objetivo. Ambas degradan los modelos con el tiempo, as\xed que los modelos en producci\xf3n necesitan monitorizar las distribuciones de entrada y el rendimiento, y reentrenarse peri\xf3dicamente con datos recientes."},{"id":"49","question":"Un modelo rinde bien offline, pero justo despu\xe9s de desplegarse sus predicciones son mucho peores. El equipo descubre que en entrenamiento la feature \'dias_desde_registro\' se calculaba en un proceso SQL por lotes, mientras que el servicio online la calcula en el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n, que redondea distinto y usa otra zona horaria. \xbfC\xf3mo se llama este problema?","options":{"A":"Deriva de concepto","B":"Desbalance de clases","C":"Underfitting","D":"Desfase entre entrenamiento y servicio (training–serving skew)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El training–serving skew es una diferencia entre c\xf3mo se procesan los datos durante el entrenamiento y c\xf3mo se procesan al servir predicciones. Incluso peque\xf1as incoherencias en el c\xe1lculo de las features dan al modelo entradas que nunca vio al entrenar. Las soluciones habituales son compartir el mismo c\xf3digo o pipeline de transformaci\xf3n entre entrenamiento y servicio, usar un feature store y comparar las features registradas al servir con las de entrenamiento."},{"id":"50","question":"Un modelo de preselecci\xf3n de candidatos se entren\xf3 con 10 a\xf1os de decisiones de contrataci\xf3n pasadas. Una auditor\xeda muestra que punt\xfaa m\xe1s bajo a candidatos igual de cualificados de un grupo concreto, reproduciendo patrones de las decisiones hist\xf3ricas. \xbfCu\xe1l es la causa de fondo y cu\xe1l es un primer paso adecuado?","options":{"A":"El modelo tiene underfitting; a\xf1adir m\xe1s capas","B":"Las etiquetas de entrenamiento reflejan un sesgo hist\xf3rico; auditar los datos y evaluar las m\xe9tricas por grupo antes y despu\xe9s del despliegue","C":"La tasa de aprendizaje es demasiado alta; bajarla","D":"El conjunto de test es demasiado grande; reducirlo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un modelo aprende los patrones que hay en sus etiquetas, incluidos los sesgos humanos de decisiones pasadas. Quitar el atributo sensible no suele bastar, porque otras features, como el c\xf3digo postal o la universidad, pueden actuar como sustitutos (proxies). Los pasos adecuados incluyen auditar los datos y las etiquetas, medir el rendimiento y las tasas de error por grupo, acordar criterios de equidad con los interesados y mantener supervisi\xf3n humana en decisiones de alto impacto."}]},{"id":"databricks-associate-developer-for-apache-spark","title":"Databricks Certified Developer for Apache Spark Associate","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"A PySpark application calls three actions in sequence: `df1.count()`, `df2.write.parquet(path)`, and `df3.collect()`. On the Spark UI\'s Jobs tab, how many jobs and what triggers each one?","options":{"A":"Three separate jobs, because Spark launches one job per action, and each job is then broken into stages and tasks that run on the executors.","B":"One job, because Spark batches every action in a script into a single job that only starts once the driver reaches the end of the file.","C":"Three jobs, but only the two write actions actually launch tasks on executors; `count()` is resolved entirely inside the driver\'s JVM.","D":"Two jobs, because `collect()` and `count()` are lazy aggregation calls that Spark merges into the job triggered by the `write.parquet` action.","E":"One job per unique DataFrame lineage, so `df1`, `df2`, and `df3` each contribute to the same job since they share the same SparkSession."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct. Each action triggers Spark\'s lazy-evaluated transformations to actually execute, and the driver submits a distinct job per action, which is then split into stages and tasks.B. Incorrect. Spark does not defer job submission to the end of the script; each action independently triggers its own job as soon as it is called.C. Incorrect. `count()` still requires reading and aggregating the underlying partitions across executors; it is not resolved purely inside the driver without executor work.D. Incorrect. Each action produces its own job in the Spark UI; `collect()` and `count()` are not merged into a job triggered by an unrelated `write.parquet` call on a different DataFrame.E. Incorrect. Job counting is tied to actions, not to shared lineage or a shared SparkSession; three actions on three DataFrames still produce three jobs even under one session."},{"id":"2","question":"A platform team submits a long-running Spark application to a YARN cluster using `spark-submit`. They want the driver process itself to run on a node inside the cluster so that the application keeps running even if the machine that issued the submit command disconnects. Which deploy mode should they specify?","options":{"A":"Standalone mode","B":"Interactive mode","C":"Client mode","D":"Cluster mode","E":"Local mode"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because this term refers to Spark\'s built-in cluster manager, not to a deploy mode that controls where the driver process runs.B. This is incorrect because this is not a recognized Spark deploy mode; the deploy mode options control driver placement, not an interactive session type.C. This is incorrect because in this mode the driver runs on the machine that issued the submit command, so the application would stop if that machine disconnected.D. This is correct because this deploy mode launches the driver process on a node inside the cluster itself, so the application continues running independently of the machine that originally issued the submit command.E. This is incorrect because this mode runs the driver and executors within a single JVM on one machine and is not used for submitting a job to a YARN cluster."},{"id":"3","question":"A developer is reviewing this notebook cell to understand which lines actually trigger a Spark job:\\n```python\\ndf1 = spark.read.parquet(\\"/data/raw\\")\\ndf2 = df1.withColumn(\\"year\\", F.year(\\"event_time\\"))\\ndf3 = df2.filter(df2.year == 2026)\\ntotal = df3.count()\\ndf3.write.mode(\\"overwrite\\").parquet(\\"/data/2026\\")\\n```\\nChoose 2 answers: which two lines trigger the execution of a Spark job?(Select 2)","options":{"A":"`df2 = df1.withColumn(\\"year\\", F.year(\\"event_time\\"))`, because adding a derived column requires Spark to immediately materialize the new column\'s values.","B":"`df3 = df2.filter(df2.year == 2026)`, because filtering rows is treated as an action in Spark and therefore executes a job as soon as the line runs.","C":"`total = df3.count()`, because `count()` is an action that forces Spark to execute the accumulated transformations and return a value to the driver.","D":"`df3.write.mode(\\"overwrite\\").parquet(\\"/data/2026\\")`, because writing output is an action that forces Spark to execute the transformation chain and materialize files.","E":"`df1 = spark.read.parquet(\\"/data/raw\\")`, because reading a file eagerly loads all of its records straight into the driver\'s memory before any transformation can even be defined."},"explanation":"A. `withColumn()` is a transformation, not an action; it lazily extends the logical plan with a new column expression without materializing any values at this point.B. `filter()` is a transformation, not an action; row filtering is recorded in the plan and only executes once a downstream action like `count()` or `write()` runs.C. This is correct: `count()` is a classic action that returns a value to the driver, forcing Spark to execute the read, column addition, and filter that precede it in the lazy plan.D. This is also correct: writing to storage is an action, so this line triggers its own job that executes the full transformation chain again to produce and persist the output files.E. `spark.read.parquet()` only builds a logical plan describing the source; it does not eagerly load records into driver memory, so this line alone does not trigger a job.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"4","question":"Which two statements about `coalesce()` are correct? (Choose 2 answers)(Select 2)","options":{"A":"`coalesce()` can only decrease a DataFrame\'s partition count and can never raise it beyond however many partitions already exist.","B":"`coalesce()` avoids a full shuffle by combining existing partitions on the same executor, moving less data than `repartition()`.","C":"`coalesce()` always redistributes rows evenly across the resulting partitions, matching the guarantees that `repartition()` provides.","D":"`coalesce()` triggers a full shuffle across the cluster identical to calling `repartition()` with the same target partition count.","E":"`coalesce()` recalculates the DataFrame\'s schema, dropping any columns that a subsequent action does not reference anywhere downstream."},"explanation":"A. Coalesce is designed only to merge existing partitions together, so it cannot raise the partition count above what the DataFrame already has; requesting more partitions than currently exist has no effect.B. By combining nearby partitions on the same executor instead of redistributing every row across the network, coalesce moves far less data than a full shuffle, which is exactly why it is the cheaper way to reduce partition count.C. Because coalesce merges whatever partitions already exist rather than hashing rows into new ones, the resulting partitions can be uneven in size, unlike the more even distribution a full shuffle in `repartition` typically produces.D. A full shuffle moving every row across the cluster is the behavior of `repartition`, not `coalesce`; coalesce specifically avoids that cost when it is only reducing the partition count.E. Coalesce only changes how a DataFrame\'s existing partitions are grouped together; it has no effect on the DataFrame\'s schema and does not drop or recompute any columns.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"5","question":"A retail company runs separate Hadoop MapReduce jobs for nightly ETL, a dedicated SQL engine for analyst reporting, and a standalone cluster for demand-forecasting models, and wants to consolidate onto one platform without losing performance. Which advantage of Apache Spark most directly supports this consolidation?","options":{"A":"Spark rewrites every submitted job into a MapReduce-compatible execution plan, so the existing Hadoop tooling keeps running unmodified while it still gains in-memory execution speed.","B":"Spark enforces schema-on-write for every file format it reads, which removes the analyst reporting layer because raw files become query-ready without any engine.","C":"Spark bundles a built-in charting dashboard that replaces the reporting tool entirely, so analysts no longer need a separate SQL engine to produce reports.","D":"Spark\'s cluster manager provisions a dedicated GPU pool automatically whenever a machine learning job is submitted, replacing the standalone forecasting cluster.","E":"Spark exposes a unified engine where Spark SQL, DataFrames, Structured Streaming, and MLlib share the same execution model, so ETL, reporting, and forecasting run on one cluster."},"correct":"E","explanation":"A. Spark does not translate jobs into MapReduce plans; it compiles work into its own DAG of stages and tasks executed by its own scheduler. Framing Spark as a MapReduce-compatibility layer misstates how its execution engine works.B. Spark does not enforce schema-on-write, and reading raw files does not remove the need for a query engine to serve reporting workloads. Schema inference and enforcement are configurable, not automatic replacements for a SQL layer.C. Spark itself has no built-in charting dashboard; visualization is handled by separate tools such as notebooks or BI products that connect to Spark. This option describes a capability Spark does not provide.D. Spark does not auto-provision GPU pools when ML code runs; hardware allocation depends on the cluster manager and the cluster configuration chosen by an administrator. This overstates what happens automatically.E. This is correct: Spark\'s unified engine lets the same cluster and APIs handle batch ETL, SQL reporting, and MLlib model training, which is exactly the consolidation the company wants. This eliminates the need to operate three separate specialized systems."},{"id":"6","question":"A data engineer runs the following code in a Databricks notebook cell:\\n```python\\ndf = spark.read.parquet(\\"/mnt/sales/transactions\\")\\nfiltered = df.filter(df.amount > 1000)\\ngrouped = filtered.groupBy(\\"region\\").sum(\\"amount\\")\\n```\\nNo further code is executed in this cell. What happens when this cell finishes running?","options":{"A":"Spark raises a runtime error when the cell finishes executing because `grouped` was never assigned to an action, leaving the driver unable to resolve an incomplete DataFrame plan.","B":"Spark reads the Parquet file\'s footer statistics to execute the filter eagerly, but defers only the final `groupBy` aggregation until an action is later called.","C":"No Spark job is submitted to the cluster; Spark only records `filtered` and `grouped` as logical plans built on top of `df`, since every call in the cell is a transformation.","D":"Spark submits one job that reads the Parquet files and applies the filter, then submits a second job later to compute the grouped sum once that first job finishes.","E":"Spark submits a single job immediately because `groupBy` is a wide transformation that always forces materialization of its parent DataFrame to build shuffle partitions."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because leaving a DataFrame unused by an action is not an error condition in Spark; the cell simply finishes with `grouped` holding an unexecuted plan, and no exception is raised.B. This is incorrect because Spark does not selectively execute some transformations eagerly based on file metadata. Every transformation in the chain, including the filter, remains unexecuted until an action is called.C. This is correct: `read`, `filter`, and `groupBy`/`sum` are all transformations, so the cell only builds up a logical plan referencing `df`. No job is submitted to the cluster because no action was called.D. This is incorrect because no action appears anywhere in the cell, so no job is submitted at all, let alone two separate ones. Filtering and grouping stay purely as plan-building steps until something forces execution.E. This is incorrect because being a wide transformation does not make `groupBy` eager. Wide transformations still only add to the logical plan and only cause a shuffle when a later action forces the plan to run."},{"id":"7","question":"A developer wants to chain a `StringIndexer` that encodes a categorical `category` column and a `LogisticRegression` estimator into a single reusable workflow that can be fit on training data and later applied consistently to new data:\\n```python\\nindexer = StringIndexer(inputCol=\\"category\\", outputCol=\\"categoryIndex\\")\\nlr = LogisticRegression(featuresCol=\\"features\\", labelCol=\\"label\\")\\n```\\nWhich snippet correctly builds and fits that workflow?","options":{"A":"`model = Pipeline(indexer, lr).fit(train_df)`, which passes the stages as separate positional arguments instead of the required `stages` keyword list, so this raises a `TypeError`.","B":"`pipeline = Pipeline(stages=[indexer, lr])`\\n`model = pipeline.fit(train_df)`, which chains both stages in order and trains them together with one `fit()` call.","C":"`model = lr.fit(indexer.fit(train_df))`, which passes a fitted `StringIndexerModel` object into `lr.fit()` instead of the transformed DataFrame of features that estimator expects.","D":"`model = [indexer, lr].fit(train_df)`, which calls `.fit()` on a plain Python list, and a list has no such method, so this raises an `AttributeError`.","E":"`model = lr.fit(train_df, transformers=[indexer])`, which passes a `transformers` keyword that `LogisticRegression.fit()` does not accept, so this raises a `TypeError`."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. `Pipeline` expects its stages to be passed as a `stages` keyword argument holding a list, not as separate positional arguments, so constructing it this way raises a `TypeError` before fitting begins.B. `Pipeline` accepts an ordered list of stages, and a single `fit()` call runs each stage\'s transformation or training in sequence, which is exactly the reusable, chained workflow the developer wants.C. `indexer.fit(train_df)` produces a fitted `StringIndexerModel`, not a transformed DataFrame; `lr.fit()` needs an actual DataFrame of features and labels, so feeding it a model object is a type mismatch.D. A Python list is not a Spark ML construct and has no `.fit()` method, so this line fails immediately rather than chaining the indexer and the classifier.E. `LogisticRegression.fit()` only accepts a DataFrame (and optional params), not a `transformers` argument, so this call does not exist as written and raises a `TypeError`."},{"id":"8","question":"A streaming ingestion job writes a fresh batch of transactions every hour to a Delta table and must add each batch alongside all previously written data, never replacing it. Which write call implements this correctly?","options":{"A":"`batch_df.write.format(\\"delta\\").mode(\\"overwrite\\").save(path)`, which commits each hour\'s rows as the sole contents of the table, replacing everything written before it","B":"`batch_df.write.format(\\"delta\\").mode(\\"errorifexists\\").save(path)`, which raises an exception on every run after the first because the target path already contains data","C":"`batch_df.write.format(\\"delta\\").save(path)` with no mode specified, which defaults to merging incoming rows with existing rows by matching primary key values","D":"`batch_df.write.format(\\"delta\\").mode(\\"ignore\\").save(path)`, which silently discards every hourly batch after the first because the target path already has data in it","E":"`batch_df.write.format(\\"delta\\").mode(\\"append\\").save(path)`, which commits the new rows as an additional atomic transaction on top of the existing table history"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A. Overwrite mode replaces the entire table contents with the new batch, so each hourly run would erase every transaction written in earlier runs rather than accumulating them.B. Errorifexists mode is the default guard against accidentally clobbering an existing table and raises an exception once the path already holds data, so it cannot support repeated hourly writes at all.C. Spark\'s DataFrameWriter has no automatic key-based merge behavior triggered by omitting the mode; without an explicit mode it falls back to `errorifexists`, which would fail after the first successful write.D. Ignore mode is designed to silently skip a write only when the target already contains data, so it would drop every batch after the first hour instead of accumulating the streaming transactions.E. Append mode commits each new batch as an additional atomic transaction without touching existing rows, so every hour\'s transactions accumulate on top of the prior history exactly as the job requires."},{"id":"9","question":"What is the key difference between `DataFrame.createTempView(name)` and `DataFrame.createOrReplaceTempView(name)` in Spark SQL?","options":{"A":"`createTempView` persists the view to the metastore across cluster restarts, while `createOrReplaceTempView` keeps the view in memory only.","B":"`createTempView` requires the DataFrame to be cached first, while `createOrReplaceTempView` can register an uncached DataFrame directly.","C":"`createTempView` accepts a SQL query string as its argument, while `createOrReplaceTempView` accepts only a list of column names.","D":"`createTempView` raises an exception if a temporary view with that name already exists, while `createOrReplaceTempView` silently overwrites it.","E":"`createTempView` registers the view in the `global_temp` database, while `createOrReplaceTempView` registers it in the session\'s default database."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Neither method writes anything to the metastore; both create purely in-memory, session-scoped catalog entries that are lost when the session ends.B. Neither method requires the DataFrame to be cached beforehand; registering a view only binds a name to the DataFrame\'s logical plan, independent of caching.C. Both methods take only the view name string as their argument; neither accepts a SQL query string or a column list.D. This is the documented behavioral distinction: the non-replacing method fails with an already-exists error on a duplicate name, while the replacing method overwrites the prior registration without error.E. Both methods register a session-scoped temporary view in the session\'s default namespace; neither one uses the `global_temp` database, which is reserved for the separate global temp view methods."},{"id":"10","question":"Two notebooks are attached to the same cluster and run in separate Spark sessions. A developer in the first notebook wants a DataFrame to be queryable by name from the second notebook without writing it to storage. Which two statements are true about achieving this? Choose 2 answers.(Select 2)","options":{"A":"Global temp views persist to disk under a `global_temp` directory, so the second notebook can still read them even after the cluster restarts and the original session ends.","B":"Calling `df.createOrReplaceTempView(\\"orders\\")` in the first notebook will not make `orders` visible to a query in the second notebook, because a plain temp view is scoped to the session that created it.","C":"Calling `df.createOrReplaceGlobalTempView(\\"orders\\")` in the first notebook makes the view visible from the second one too, because global temp views are shared across sessions in the same Spark application.","D":"The second notebook must query the view as `SELECT * FROM orders`, because the `global_temp` prefix is only required when calling `spark.sql` from Python, not from a SQL cell.","E":"Calling `df.createOrReplaceTempView(\\"orders\\")` in the first notebook automatically registers `orders` as a permanent, shared entry in the metastore, so the second notebook can query it like any managed table."},"explanation":"A. Incorrect — global temp views live only in memory for the lifetime of the Spark application; they are not persisted to disk and disappear once the application (and its cluster) stops.B. Correct — a session-scoped temp view created with `createOrReplaceTempView` only exists for the Spark session that created it, so a different session\'s queries cannot see it by name.C. Correct — a global temp view is tied to the `global_temp` system database, which is shared by every session running in the same Spark application, so other sessions can query it.D. Incorrect — the `global_temp.` prefix is required for every session accessing a global temp view, regardless of whether the query runs from a SQL cell, `spark.sql`, or another language.E. Incorrect — a plain temp view is an in-memory, session-local logical plan; it is never written to the metastore, so no other session or later job can discover it as a table.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"11","question":"A data engineer needs to persist a large `transactions_df` DataFrame as a managed table so that later queries filtering on `transaction_date` can skip irrelevant files entirely, with no bucketing or sorting required. Which code correctly persists the table with this optimization?","options":{"A":"transactions_df.write.mode(\\"overwrite\\").orderBy(\\"transaction_date\\").saveAsTable(\\"transactions\\")","B":"transactions_df.repartition(\\"transaction_date\\").write.mode(\\"overwrite\\").saveAsTable(\\"transactions\\")","C":"transactions_df.write.mode(\\"overwrite\\").partitionBy(\\"transaction_date\\").saveAsTable(\\"transactions\\")","D":"transactions_df.write.mode(\\"overwrite\\").bucketBy(50, \\"transaction_date\\").saveAsTable(\\"transactions\\")","E":"transactions_df.write.mode(\\"overwrite\\").sortBy(\\"transaction_date\\").saveAsTable(\\"transactions\\")"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. `DataFrameWriter` has no `orderBy` method; ordering a DataFrame before a write is done with `DataFrame.orderBy`, not on the writer object. This call fails with an attribute error at runtime.B. `repartition` changes how many in-memory Spark partitions are used during the write, which can affect the number of output files, but it does not create the on-disk directory structure that lets a reader prune by `transaction_date` value.C. `partitionBy` writes the table as Hive-style directories keyed by `transaction_date`, so a later query filtering on that column only reads the matching directory. This is the standard way to get file-skipping without introducing bucketing or sort metadata.D. `bucketBy` hashes rows into a fixed number of bucket files rather than creating per-value directories, so a reader cannot tell from the file layout which bucket holds a given `transaction_date`. It optimizes joins and aggregations on the bucketing column, not date-based file skipping.E. `sortBy` can only be used together with a preceding `bucketBy` call on the same writer. Calling it alone raises an error before any data is written, so this call never reaches the metastore."},{"id":"12","question":"An analyst has a DataFrame `orders` with columns `order_id`, `amount`, and `region`. They need to add a new column `amount_tier` that labels rows as `\\"high\\"` when `amount` is greater than 1000 and `\\"standard\\"` otherwise, without mutating the original DataFrame:\\n\\n```python\\norders_tiered = orders.____(\\n\\"amount_tier\\",\\nwhen(col(\\"amount\\") > 1000, \\"high\\").otherwise(\\"standard\\")\\n)\\n```\\n\\nWhich method correctly completes this task?","options":{"A":"filter","B":"withColumnsRenamed","C":"select","D":"withColumnRenamed","E":"withColumn"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A. filter restricts which rows are kept based on a boolean condition; it does not add or compute a new column, so it does not fit this signature.B. withColumnsRenamed takes a dictionary mapping existing names to new names for bulk renaming, not a name plus a computed expression, so it does not match this call.C. select projects a set of column expressions but does not take a target column name and a value expression as two positional arguments the way this call is written, so this signature does not fit.D. withColumnRenamed only accepts an existing column name and a new name string; it cannot evaluate a when/otherwise expression, so it cannot produce a computed column.E. withColumn takes a target column name and a Column expression, adding the result as a new column while returning a new DataFrame, which matches this call exactly."},{"id":"13","question":"A `sensor_df` DataFrame has a `reading` column of type double. Some rows contain Python `None` (missing sensor data) and others contain the result of `0.0/0.0` (an invalid calculation), which Spark stores as `NaN`. You need to identify only the rows with the invalid `NaN` calculation, not the rows with missing sensor data. Which filter should you use?","options":{"A":"sensor_df.filter(F.isnan(\\"reading\\"))","B":"sensor_df.filter(F.col(\\"reading\\").isNull())","C":"sensor_df.filter(F.col(\\"reading\\").isNotNull())","D":"sensor_df.na.drop(subset=[\\"reading\\"])","E":"sensor_df.filter(F.isnull(\\"reading\\"))"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. isnan() specifically flags Spark\'s floating-point NaN marker used for invalid numeric results, distinguishing it from a genuinely missing None value.B. isNull() matches rows where the value is Python None/SQL NULL, which is exactly the missing-data case this scenario wants to exclude, not the invalid calculation.C. Filtering with isNotNull() returns both valid numeric readings and NaN rows together, since NaN is a defined floating-point value rather than a null, so it fails to isolate the invalid rows.D. na.drop(subset=[\\"reading\\"]) removes rows with null values and returns everything else, which keeps the NaN rows mixed in with valid readings rather than isolating them.E. isnull() is functionally the same null check as isNull(), so it identifies missing-data rows rather than the NaN rows produced by the invalid calculation."},{"id":"14","question":"A data engineer wants a single-row report showing the total row count and the average `order_total` for the entire `orders` DataFrame, with no grouping. Which line of code produces this correctly?","options":{"A":"`orders.agg(F.count(\\"*\\").alias(\\"total_orders\\"), F.avg(\\"order_total\\").alias(\\"avg_total\\"))`, which applies both aggregate expressions across the whole DataFrame and returns one row labeled by the given aliases.","B":"`orders.count(), orders.select(F.avg(\\"order_total\\"))`, which evaluates two independent expressions rather than a single line of code, so the result is a Python tuple, not a DataFrame.","C":"`orders.avg(\\"order_total\\").withColumn(\\"total_orders\\", F.count(\\"*\\"))`, which fails because `DataFrame` has no `.avg()` method and `F.count()` cannot be added as a column outside an aggregate context.","D":"`orders.select(F.count(\\"*\\"), F.avg(\\"order_total\\")).groupBy(\\"order_total\\")`, which regroups the already-aggregated single row by `order_total`, producing one row per distinct total rather than a single summary row.","E":"`orders.groupBy().count().alias(\\"avg_total\\")`, which returns the row count as a DataFrame and then renames the entire DataFrame object rather than adding an average column."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. This is correct: calling `.agg()` directly on the DataFrame without a preceding `.groupBy()` key aggregates across every row, and the two aliased expressions produce exactly the two labeled columns the report needs in one row.B. Writing two comma-separated expressions on one line creates a tuple of two separate results in Python rather than combining them into a single DataFrame row, so it does not produce the requested report.C. `DataFrame` objects do not expose a bare `.avg()` method, and adding an aggregate function as a new column outside of `.agg()` or `.select()` is not valid, so this raises an error before producing any result.D. Once `.select()` has collapsed the data to a single aggregated row, grouping by `order_total` afterward just partitions that one row by its own value, which does not restore a meaningful per-order breakdown or match the requested single summary row.E. `.alias()` on a DataFrame does not rename a column or introduce an average; it is a no-op renaming of the DataFrame reference itself, so no average column is produced."},{"id":"15","question":"```python\\norders = spark.createDataFrame(\\n[(101, \\"widget\\", 3), (102, \\"gadget\\", 1), (103, \\"gizmo\\", 5)],\\n[\\"order_id\\", \\"item\\", \\"qty\\"]\\n)\\npromotions = spark.createDataFrame(\\n[(101, \\"SAVE10\\"), (103, \\"SAVE20\\")],\\n[\\"order_id\\", \\"code\\"]\\n)\\nresult = orders.join(promotions, on=\\"order_id\\", how=\\"left\\")\\n```\\nWhat value appears in the `code` column for order `102` after this left join?","options":{"A":"The row for order `102` is removed entirely, because left joins drop rows that lack a match on either side.","B":"`null`, because `promotions` has no row with `order_id` = 102, so the left join keeps the order row with a null for the unmatched column.","C":"`\\"SAVE10\\"`, because Spark falls back to the first available promotion code when no exact match is found.","D":"The literal value `\\"gadget\\"`, because Spark reuses the closest matching column value when no promotion code exists.","E":"An empty string `\\"\\"`, because Spark fills missing right-side string columns with empty strings rather than nulls."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. A left join keeps every row from the left DataFrame no matter what, filling right-side columns with null when there is no match, rather than dropping the row.B. This is correct. A left join preserves all rows from `orders`, and since `promotions` has no row for `order_id` 102, the `code` column for that row is populated with `null`.C. Spark does not borrow a value from an unrelated matched row; there is no fallback to reuse `\\"SAVE10\\"` for an order that has no corresponding promotion row.D. Spark never copies a value from a different column, such as `item`, into an unrelated output column; a missing match produces a null, not a reused string from elsewhere in the row.E. Spark does not substitute empty strings for missing values produced by a join; unmatched right-side columns are populated with `null`, not `\\"\\"`."},{"id":"16","question":"A dashboard computes \\"days since signup\\" for each user from a `DateType` column `signup_date`. The developer writes:\\n```python\\ndf.withColumn(\\"days_since_signup\\", datediff(col(\\"signup_date\\"), current_date()))\\n```\\nand the resulting values are negative for every existing user. What should change, and why?","options":{"A":"Cast `signup_date` to `TimestampType` before calling `datediff()`, because `datediff()` returns negative numbers whenever it receives a `DateType` argument instead of a `TimestampType`.","B":"Wrap the result in `abs()`, because `datediff()` always returns a negative value whenever its first argument is chronologically earlier than the true current date being compared.","C":"Use `months_between(col(\\"signup_date\\"), current_date())` instead, because `months_between()` returns whole day counts while `datediff()` is limited to whole calendar months.","D":"Swap the arguments to `datediff(current_date(), col(\\"signup_date\\"))`; `datediff(end, start)` returns `end` minus `start` in days, so the earlier date first yields a negative count.","E":"Replace `datediff()` with `date_add(col(\\"signup_date\\"), -1)`, because `date_add()` measures elapsed days between two columns while `datediff()` only shifts a single date forward or backward."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. `datediff()` works correctly with `DateType` arguments and the column\'s data type is not the source of the negative sign; the cause is the order in which the two dates are passed.B. Wrapping the result in `abs()` hides the sign without fixing the underlying argument order, and the claim that `datediff()` always returns a negative value for an earlier first argument describes exactly the bug rather than a real universal rule to rely on.C. `months_between()` returns a number of months, not days, and `datediff()` returns days, not months, so this option reverses what each function actually measures.D. `datediff()` computes its first argument minus its second argument in days, so passing the earlier `signup_date` as the first argument and today as the second produces a negative number; swapping the order fixes the sign.E. `date_add()` shifts a single date by a fixed number of days and does not compute the elapsed days between two columns at all, so it cannot replace `datediff()` for this calculation."},{"id":"17","question":"A data engineer reads a daily CSV extract with no header row. The `order_id` column must be read as a long (not the int Spark would infer) to avoid downstream overflow, and schema inference is too slow on the multi-gigabyte file. Which line fills the blank so the file is read with the required types without triggering an inference scan?\\n\\n```python\\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, LongType, StringType\\n\\norder_schema = StructType([\\nStructField(\\"order_id\\", LongType(), True),\\nStructField(\\"customer\\", StringType(), True)\\n])\\n\\ndf = spark.read.____.csv(\\"/mnt/raw/orders/\\")\\n```","options":{"A":"format(\\"csv\\").load()","B":"option(\\"inferSchema\\", \\"true\\")","C":"schema(order_schema)","D":"option(\\"header\\", \\"true\\")"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Chaining `format` and `load` is just an alternate way to specify the CSV source path and does not attach a schema, so the file would still fall back to default typing behavior.B. Turning on `inferSchema` makes Spark scan the file to guess column types, which is exactly the slow, unreliable path the engineer needs to avoid, and it would not guarantee `order_id` comes back as a long.C. Passing the `StructType` directly forces every row to be parsed according to the declared field names and types, so `order_id` is read as a `LongType` with no inference scan required.D. Declaring a header only tells Spark to treat the first line as column names; it says nothing about column types and does nothing to prevent inference from running."},{"id":"18","question":"Given:\\n```python\\ndf = spark.createDataFrame([(1, \\"a\\"), (3, \\"c\\"), (2, \\"b\\")], [\\"id\\", \\"val\\"])\\nresult = df.orderBy(\\"id\\", ascending=False).select(\\"id\\").collect()\\n```\\nWhat are the `id` values in `result`, in order?","options":{"A":"1, 2, 3","B":"1, 3, 2 (the original insertion order is preserved)","C":"A `TypeError` is raised because `ascending` must be a list matching the number of sort columns","D":"3, 1, 2","E":"3, 2, 1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A. This would be the result of an ascending sort, but `ascending=False` explicitly requests descending order, so the lowest-to-highest sequence 1, 2, 3 is not what this call produces.B. `orderBy()` always reshuffles rows into the requested sort order; a `DataFrame` has no guaranteed row ordering to preserve in the first place, so the output reflects the sort, not the construction order.C. `ascending` accepts a single boolean when sorting by one column; a list is only required when sorting by multiple columns and specifying a direction per column, so a bare `False` here is valid and does not raise an error.D. This sequence matches neither a full ascending nor a full descending sort on `id`, so it does not correspond to any ordering that `orderBy(\\"id\\", ascending=False)` would produce.E. `ascending=False` reverses the sort direction for the single `id` column, so rows are ordered from the highest value to the lowest, producing 3, then 2, then 1."},{"id":"19","question":"A Spark job automatically uses a broadcast hash join for a small dimension table with no explicit hint applied anywhere in the code. Which configuration property controls this automatic behavior, and what does its default value represent?","options":{"A":"`spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` sets the largest estimated table size Spark broadcasts automatically, defaulting to 10 MB unless the session overrides it.","B":"Setting `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` controls how many post-shuffle partitions Spark creates, and it defaults to 200 regardless of any table\'s estimated size.","C":"The property `spark.sql.broadcastTimeout` controls how many seconds Spark waits for a broadcast to complete, defaulting to 300 seconds rather than a size limit.","D":"Increasing `spark.driver.maxResultSize` raises the largest table Spark will broadcast automatically, since broadcast tables are collected through the driver\'s result buffer.","E":"Only `spark.sql.adaptive.enabled` determines whether Spark can choose a broadcast join at runtime, with automatic size-based broadcasting disabled until AQE is turned on."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. This is correct: `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` is the size cutoff the planner compares a table\'s estimated size against, and its default of 10 MB is why small dimension tables get broadcast without any explicit hint. Setting it to -1 disables this automatic behavior entirely.B. `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` only controls the number of partitions used after a shuffle occurs and has nothing to do with the size threshold that triggers automatic broadcasting. Its default of 200 is unrelated to table size estimation.C. `spark.sql.broadcastTimeout` governs how long a broadcast exchange is allowed to run before timing out, not what size table qualifies for broadcasting in the first place. Its 300-second default is a wait limit, not a size limit.D. `spark.driver.maxResultSize` limits how much data a driver can collect from actions like `collect()`, and it is not the property the planner consults when deciding whether a table is small enough to broadcast. Raising it does not change broadcast eligibility.E. Automatic size-based broadcasting is independent of `spark.sql.adaptive.enabled` and works whether or not adaptive query execution is turned on. AQE can additionally convert a join to broadcast at runtime, but it is not a prerequisite for the size-based default behavior."},{"id":"20","question":"A data engineer has a small Python dictionary `country_codes` (well under 1 MB) that maps ISO codes to country names, and needs to look it up inside a PySpark UDF applied to a 500 million row DataFrame. To avoid Spark serializing and shipping a fresh copy of `country_codes` with every task, which change should the engineer make to the following code?\\n\\n```python\\ndef lookup_country(code):\\nreturn country_codes.get(code, \\"Unknown\\")\\n\\nlookup_udf = udf(lookup_country, StringType())\\ndf = df.withColumn(\\"country_name\\", lookup_udf(col(\\"iso_code\\")))\\n```","options":{"A":"Register `country_codes` as a Spark accumulator with `sc.accumulator(country_codes)` so tasks can read and update the shared mapping while the batch job keeps running.","B":"Raise `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` so Spark automatically distributes the dictionary as part of query planning for this specific UDF call in the job.","C":"Wrap `country_codes` with `spark.sparkContext.broadcast(country_codes)` and reference `bc.value.get(code, \\"Unknown\\")`, so every executor keeps one cached copy.","D":"Move the dictionary definition inside `lookup_country` itself, rebuilding `country_codes` on every call so each task always works with its own freshly built local copy of the mapping.","E":"Convert `country_codes` into a Spark DataFrame and perform a `join` against the 500 million row DataFrame on the ISO code column before applying any further transformations."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Accumulators are write-only aggregators whose running total only the driver can read; they are not a mechanism for distributing an arbitrary read-only mapping for executors to look values up in.B. `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` only influences whether Catalyst chooses a broadcast join strategy for DataFrame joins, and has no effect on a plain Python closure referenced inside a UDF.C. Broadcasting caches a single read-only copy of the dictionary on each executor, and referencing `.value` inside the UDF avoids re-serializing and re-sending the dictionary with every task.D. Rebuilding the dictionary inside the function makes the problem worse: Spark still ships whatever data built it, and the work of constructing the mapping now repeats on every single row.E. A join is a valid pattern for larger reference tables, but for a small in-memory dictionary it adds an unnecessary shuffle and does not fix the serialization behavior of the existing UDF code."},{"id":"21","question":"Which `timeoutConf` value should be passed to `applyInPandasWithState` so a group\'s state expires after a fixed amount of wall-clock time has elapsed since it last received data, independent of any event-time watermark in the incoming records?","options":{"A":"GroupStateTimeout.WatermarkTimeout()","B":"GroupStateTimeout.NoTimeout()","C":"GroupStateTimeout.EventTimeTimeout()","D":"GroupStateTimeout.ProcessingTimeTimeout()"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. `GroupStateTimeout` has no member with this name; the only valid timeout configurations are `NoTimeout()`, `ProcessingTimeTimeout()`, and `EventTimeTimeout()`.B. This disables automatic expiration entirely, so state for a group is retained indefinitely until the per-group function itself calls `state.remove()`, which does not satisfy a fixed wall-clock expiration requirement.C. This ties expiration to the watermark computed from an event-time column in the data, so how quickly state expires depends on data arrival patterns rather than a fixed span of processing time.D. This measures elapsed wall-clock time against the duration set with `state.setTimeoutDuration()`, independent of any event-time column or watermark, which is exactly the behavior described."},{"id":"22","question":"A team reviews an `applyInPandasWithState` implementation that declares `stateStructType = StructType([StructField(\\"count\\", LongType()), StructField(\\"last_seen\\", TimestampType())])` and passes `GroupStateTimeout.NoTimeout()` as the `timeoutConf`. Which two statements about this setup are correct? (Choose 2 answers)(Select 2)","options":{"A":"`state.setTimeoutDuration()` can still be called safely to expire idle keys even though `timeoutConf` is `GroupStateTimeout.NoTimeout()`, since the timeout configuration only affects `EventTimeTimeout()`.","B":"State declared with `stateStructType` is scoped to the entire streaming query rather than per grouping key, so two different `device_id` values processed by the same function share one combined state object.","C":"Every call to `state.update()` inside the per-group function must pass a two-element tuple whose values line up with `count` and `last_seen`, since the value is validated against `stateStructType`.","D":"Because `timeoutConf` is `GroupStateTimeout.NoTimeout()`, `state.hasTimedOut` will always evaluate to `False` inside the function, so no branch of the function will ever run purely due to a timeout."},"explanation":"A. Calling `state.setTimeoutDuration()` while `timeoutConf` is `NoTimeout()` raises an error at runtime, since duration-based timeouts are only valid when `ProcessingTimeTimeout()` is the configured timeout type.B. State declared with `stateStructType` is maintained independently per grouping key produced by `groupBy()`; each distinct key gets its own isolated state entry, not one shared object across keys.C. `state.update()` validates its argument against the declared `stateStructType`, so passing a tuple of the wrong arity or incompatible types raises an error; here that means a two-element tuple matching `count` and `last_seen`.D. Timeout callbacks only fire when `timeoutConf` enables them; with `NoTimeout()` selected, Spark never invokes the function purely because time has elapsed, so this property stays `False` on every call.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"23","question":"A DataFrame with 1000 partitions is filtered down to a small result set. An engineer wants the final write to produce exactly 8 output files while spending as little execution time as possible on the operation itself:\\n\\n```python\\nresult = spark.read.table(\\"sales.transactions\\").filter(col(\\"region\\") == \\"EMEA\\")\\nresult.write.mode(\\"overwrite\\").parquet(\\"/mnt/output/emea_sales\\")\\n```\\n\\nWhich change achieves this most efficiently?","options":{"A":"Insert `result = result.repartition(8)` before the write, since renumbering partition metadata this way never triggers a network shuffle of the underlying rows.","B":"Set `spark.conf.set(\\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\\", 8)` right before the write call, since that setting fixes the output partition count for any write operation.","C":"Insert `result = result.repartition(8, \\"region\\")` before the write, since hashing on the filter column guarantees exactly 8 evenly sized output files here.","D":"Call `result.persist()` immediately before the write, since caching a DataFrame in memory automatically collapses its partition count to match the write target.","E":"Insert `result = result.coalesce(8)` before the write, since it merges existing partitions on the same executors and avoids a full shuffle across the cluster."},"correct":"E","explanation":"A. `repartition()` always performs a full shuffle to redistribute rows across the new partition count, regardless of direction, so it is more expensive than needed when only reducing the number of partitions.B. `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` only controls the number of partitions produced by shuffle operations such as joins and aggregations; it has no effect on the partition count of a plain filter-then-write DataFrame operation.C. Hash partitioning on a single low-cardinality column like `region` does not guarantee balanced output; rows with the same region hash to the same partition, so file sizes can still be very uneven, and this still forces a full shuffle.D. Caching a DataFrame stores its existing partitions in memory or on disk; it does not change the partition count at all, so the write would still emit the original number of files.E. `coalesce()` reduces the number of partitions by combining existing ones on the same executors, so it can shrink partition count without a full shuffle when the target is fewer partitions than the current count. This makes it the cheapest way to hit exactly 8 output files here."},{"id":"24","question":"A data engineer wants Spark to pick the number of shuffle partitions automatically for each job rather than always using a fixed count, letting AQE\'s coalescing feature size the partitions itself. Which setting achieves this on a Databricks cluster with AQE enabled?","options":{"A":"`spark.conf.set(\\"spark.sql.files.maxPartitionBytes\\", \\"auto\\")` lets Spark size the input read partitions dynamically, which also determines the partition count for every downstream shuffle.","B":"`spark.conf.set(\\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\\", \\"auto\\")` starts each shuffle with a large partition count and lets AQE coalesce it down toward the advisory partition size.","C":"`spark.conf.set(\\"spark.sql.adaptive.coalescePartitions.enabled\\", \\"auto\\")` switches partition coalescing between on and off automatically depending on the size of the input DataFrame.","D":"`spark.conf.set(\\"spark.sql.shuffle.partitions\\", \\"0\\")` tells Spark to compute the shuffle partition count from the number of executor cores available at job submission time.","E":"`spark.conf.set(\\"spark.databricks.adaptive.autoOptimizeShuffle.enabled\\", \\"false\\")` forces Spark back onto a manually specified partition count for every shuffle stage in the job."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because this setting controls input file read partitioning, not the number of partitions used for shuffles triggered later by joins or aggregations in the query.B. This is correct because setting the shuffle partition count to `\\"auto\\"` on Databricks starts with a large initial partition count and delegates the final sizing to AQE\'s coalescing logic based on actual shuffled data volume.C. This is incorrect because the coalescing enabled flag is a plain boolean; it does not accept an `\\"auto\\"` value that conditionally toggles behavior by input size.D. This is incorrect because setting the partition count to zero is not a supported way to derive partitions from executor core count; it does not enable automatic AQE-driven sizing.E. This is incorrect because disabling this Databricks-specific auto-optimization flag does the opposite of what is requested, forcing a fixed manual partition count instead of automatic sizing."},{"id":"25","question":"A join between a 2 TB fact table and what the engineer believed was a small dimension table causes repeated executor `OutOfMemoryError` failures:\\n```python\\nfact = spark.read.format(\\"delta\\").load(\\"/mnt/gold/fact_sales\\")\\ndim = spark.read.format(\\"delta\\").load(\\"/mnt/gold/dim_store\\")\\nresult = fact.join(broadcast(dim), \\"store_id\\")\\n```\\nInvestigation shows `dim_store` has grown to 12 GB over time and `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` is set to `-1` in the cluster\'s Spark config. Which two actions would most directly resolve the OutOfMemoryError? (Choose 2 answers.)(Select 2)","options":{"A":"Remove the explicit `broadcast(dim)` hint so the join falls back to a standard sort-merge join, since forcing a 12 GB table to be broadcast to every executor is what is overwhelming executor memory regardless of the threshold setting.","B":"Reduce `dim_store` to only the columns and rows the join actually needs before the join executes, and confirm the filtered DataFrame is genuinely small so the existing `broadcast(dim)` hint can safely execute without overwhelming any executor.","C":"Increase `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` to a much higher value so the sort-merge join that `broadcast(dim)` supposedly triggers under the hood can process the 12 GB dimension table across more, smaller shuffle partitions instead.","D":"Persist `dim_store` with `StorageLevel.DISK_ONLY` before the join, since caching a DataFrame to disk exempts it from the broadcast join threshold check and allows the explicit `broadcast()` hint to succeed without exceeding executor memory.","E":"Rewrite the join as `fact.crossJoin(dim).filter(fact.store_id == dim.store_id)`, since a cross join followed by a filter processes the two tables independently on each executor and avoids ever broadcasting `dim_store` in full."},"explanation":"A. An explicit `broadcast()` hint forces Spark to ship the entire hinted DataFrame to every executor regardless of the `autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` setting, so removing the hint lets the optimizer fall back to a standard sort-merge join that processes `dim_store` in shuffled partitions instead of copying all 12 GB into every executor\'s memory.B. The underlying problem is that `dim_store` grew past the point where broadcasting it is safe, so trimming it down to only the join-relevant columns and rows — and verifying the result is actually small — restores the assumption the original code was written under and lets the broadcast hint execute without exhausting executor memory.C. `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` controls partitioning for shuffle-based joins such as sort-merge joins, but an explicit `broadcast()` hint bypasses the shuffle path entirely and copies the whole DataFrame to every executor, so raising this setting has no effect on the broadcast operation that is actually failing.D. Setting a storage level on `dim_store` controls how that DataFrame\'s data is cached for reuse, but it has no relationship to the broadcast join threshold or to how the explicit `broadcast()` hint decides to ship data to executors, so this change would not prevent the same broadcast operation from failing.E. A cross join followed by a filter still requires materializing the full pairwise combination of both tables\' partitions on the executors that process them, which is typically far more expensive than either a sort-merge join or a safely sized broadcast, and does not avoid memory pressure — it usually makes it worse.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"26","question":"A data engineer starts the following streaming query without specifying a `.trigger(...)` clause:\\n```python\\nquery = (streamingDF.writeStream\\n.format(\\"console\\")\\n.outputMode(\\"append\\")\\n.start())\\n```\\nWhich best describes how Spark schedules micro-batches for this query?","options":{"A":"Spark waits indefinitely for an explicit trigger interval to be configured, and the query remains idle until one is set","B":"Spark processes exactly one micro-batch containing all currently available data, then stops the query automatically after that batch","C":"Spark uses the default micro-batch trigger, immediately starting the next micro-batch as soon as the previous one finishes processing","D":"Spark falls back to continuous processing mode automatically, executing records with millisecond-level latency instead of batching them"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because the absence of a trigger clause does not pause the query; Spark falls back to its default trigger behavior rather than waiting for configuration.B. This is incorrect because processing a single batch and stopping is the behavior of a one-time trigger such as `Trigger.Once()`, not the default trigger that is used when no trigger is specified.C. This is correct: when no trigger is specified, Spark uses the default micro-batch trigger, which starts a new batch as soon as the prior one finishes, giving the lowest latency the micro-batch engine can offer without explicit tuning.D. This is incorrect because continuous processing is an experimental, opt-in execution mode requiring `.trigger(continuous=...)`; omitting a trigger does not switch the engine into that mode."},{"id":"27","question":"Which statement accurately distinguishes Spark\'s default micro-batch processing model from its continuous processing mode?","options":{"A":"Micro-batch mode guarantees at-most-once delivery semantics, while continuous processing is the only Spark mode capable of exactly-once guarantees","B":"Micro-batch mode polls the source and batches accumulated data, while continuous processing runs long tasks that handle records as they arrive for lower latency","C":"Micro-batch mode requires a fixed cluster size that cannot ever autoscale, while continuous processing dynamically adds new executors for every single incoming record","D":"Micro-batch mode only supports file-based sinks, while continuous processing is required whenever the sink is a Delta table or a JDBC connection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because micro-batch mode is the execution model that provides Structured Streaming\'s well-supported exactly-once guarantees, while continuous processing offers weaker, at-least-once guarantees for its supported operations.B. This is correct: micro-batch execution repeatedly polls the source and processes whatever has accumulated in discrete batches, while continuous processing keeps long-running tasks reading and processing records individually to achieve much lower latency.C. This is incorrect because cluster autoscaling behavior is independent of which streaming execution mode is chosen; neither mode inherently fixes or dynamically resizes the cluster per record.D. This is incorrect because micro-batch mode supports a wide range of sinks including Delta tables and JDBC, not only files; sink support is not the distinguishing factor between the two modes."},{"id":"28","question":"A team processes streaming user activity events and wants to group consecutive events from the same `userId` into a single session that closes automatically whenever there is at least a 5-minute gap of inactivity, rather than using a fixed calendar window. Which construct should they use?","options":{"A":"`window()` with a 5-minute duration and no slide duration approximates session grouping, since a short tumbling window mirrors typical gaps between a user\'s events.","B":"`groupBy(\\"userId\\").agg(max(\\"event_time\\"))` combined with a watermark tracks each user\'s latest event, which is treated as equivalent to a session boundary in practice.","C":"`window()` with a 5-minute slide and a 10-minute duration merges adjacent events through overlapping windows, approximating the session boundaries the team wants.","D":"`dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark(\\"userId\\")` applied before the aggregation removes repeat events per user, which approximates grouping a user\'s activity into one session.","E":"`session_window()` builds one window per group that keeps extending while new events arrive within the gap duration, and finalizes once that inactivity gap has elapsed."},"correct":"E","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because a tumbling window has a fixed boundary aligned to the clock regardless of activity, so it would split or merge events based on wall-clock time rather than actual inactivity gaps.B. This is incorrect because tracking only the maximum event time per user produces a single running aggregate value, not distinct session windows that open and close based on gaps between events.C. This is incorrect because sliding windows still have fixed durations and slide intervals tied to the clock, so they do not dynamically expand or contract based on the actual gap between a user\'s events.D. This is incorrect because `dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark` removes duplicate rows within a watermark bound; it does not group events into variable-length sessions based on inactivity gaps.E. This is correct because `session_window` is purpose-built for dynamic-length windows keyed on a gap duration: a session stays open while new events keep arriving within that gap and is finalized once the gap is exceeded, matching the requirement exactly."},{"id":"29","question":"A developer joins two streaming DataFrames and writes the result:\\n```python\\njoined = orders_stream.join(shipments_stream, \\"order_id\\")\\n\\nquery = joined.writeStream.outputMode(______).format(\\"console\\").start()\\n```\\nWhich two output modes are **not** valid for this streaming-to-streaming join and will raise an `AnalysisException` when passed to `.outputMode()`? (Choose 2 answers)(Select 2)","options":{"A":"`once`","B":"`default`","C":"`append`","D":"`update`","E":"`complete`"},"explanation":"A. `once` is a trigger setting, not an output mode string, so it is not a candidate answer to this question about `.outputMode()` values.B. `default` is not a recognized output mode identifier in Structured Streaming; the valid mode names are `append`, `update`, and `complete`.C. Stream-to-stream joins are only supported with the `append` output mode, so this mode is valid and does not raise an exception.D. `update` mode is not yet supported for stream-to-stream joins; passing it to `.outputMode()` for a join query raises an `AnalysisException`.E. `complete` mode is not supported for stream-to-stream joins either, since the join does not produce a bounded result table that can be rewritten in full each trigger.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"30","question":"A sensor telemetry stream has columns `device_id`, `reading_time`, and `temperature`. The team writes:\\n```python\\ncleaned = sensor_df \\\\\\n.withWatermark(\\"reading_time\\", \\"15 minutes\\") \\\\\\n.dropDuplicates([\\"device_id\\", \\"reading_time\\"])\\n```\\nA record for `device_id=\\"A1\\"` with `reading_time=\\"10:00:00\\"` arrives, is processed, and the watermark subsequently advances to `10:20:00`. A duplicate record for the same `device_id` and `reading_time=\\"10:00:00\\"` then arrives. What happens to this late duplicate?","options":{"A":"The record is buffered by the engine until the next checkpoint interval, at which point it is compared against a stored snapshot of every key deleted so far before being emitted.","B":"The state for that key was already evicted once the watermark passed 10:00:00 by more than the 15-minute delay, so the engine can no longer recognize the duplicate and the record passes through as new.","C":"The engine always retains a permanent record of every key it has ever emitted, kept in a separate index outside the bounded state store, so the duplicate is correctly filtered out no matter how late it arrives.","D":"The query throws a runtime exception because the incoming event time is now older than the current watermark, which Structured Streaming treats as an unrecoverable error in the input data.","E":"The engine automatically extends the watermark delay backward to accommodate the late arrival, silently widening the effective lateness bound to keep the original key\'s state alive longer."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. There is no buffering-until-checkpoint mechanism that reconciles late records against deleted keys; the state for expired keys is gone, and buffering indefinitely would reintroduce the unbounded growth the watermark is meant to prevent.B. This is correct: once the watermark advances past a key\'s event time by more than the declared delay, the engine treats that key\'s state as safe to remove, so a genuine duplicate arriving after that point is no longer matched and is emitted as a new row. This is the expected tradeoff of bounding state with a watermark.C. There is no secondary unbounded index kept outside the state store; the watermark\'s entire purpose is to let the engine physically discard state, and once discarded there is no other record of the key to check against.D. Structured Streaming does not throw an exception for late data relative to the watermark; late records that fall outside the bound are simply treated as new input rather than causing the query to fail.E. The watermark delay is a fixed configuration set when the query is defined; the engine does not dynamically extend it backward in response to individual late arrivals, since that would defeat the purpose of bounding state at all."},{"id":"31","question":"Which statement about setting the watermark delay threshold for streaming deduplication is accurate?","options":{"A":"The threshold is measured in a count of records rather than a time duration, so it should be set to the expected number of duplicate retries observed per key.","B":"The threshold only affects the aggregation operators present in a query, and has no bearing on how streaming deduplication state is retained or expired over time.","C":"The threshold should exceed the maximum expected gap between an event and its latest duplicate, so genuine duplicates are not mistaken for new records once the watermark passes.","D":"The threshold has no effect on correctness at all, and only changes how frequently the Spark UI refreshes the numbers shown on its streaming query statistics panel.","E":"The threshold must always be set to exactly equal the micro-batch trigger interval, since Spark derives all watermark expiry decisions directly from the configured trigger duration."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. The watermark threshold is expressed as a time duration relative to event time, such as minutes or hours, not as a count of expected retries per key.B. The watermark threshold governs state retention for any stateful operator that uses it, including deduplication, not only aggregation operators; deduplication state expiry depends directly on this same threshold.C. This is correct: the watermark delay directly determines how long state is retained for a given key, so it must cover the realistic lateness of duplicate arrivals or the engine will expire state too early and fail to catch genuine duplicates.D. The watermark threshold directly controls state retention and correctness of duplicate detection, not merely how often UI statistics are refreshed, which is an unrelated concern from query configuration.E. The watermark delay is an independently configured duration passed to `withWatermark` and is not derived from or required to match the micro-batch trigger interval, which controls how often batches are processed."},{"id":"32","question":"A platform team upgrades the Spark version running on their Spark Connect server to pick up performance improvements, while client applications keep running unmodified PySpark scripts such as:\\n```python\\ndf = spark.read.table(\\"events\\").groupBy(\\"user_id\\").count()\\n```\\nWhich two statements correctly explain why this server upgrade does not require the client applications to be rebuilt or redeployed? (Choose 2 answers)(Select 2)","options":{"A":"The client and server exchange a shared JVM classpath at connection time, so any server-side JAR upgrade is automatically mirrored onto the client machine.","B":"The server converts the client\'s compiled Java bytecode into an updated intermediate representation, which removes any dependency on the original Spark version.","C":"Client applications never actually run against the upgraded server, because Spark Connect automatically pins each client to the exact server version it was originally built against.","D":"The thin client library only encodes DataFrame operations into unresolved logical plans; it does not depend on the internal execution engine version running on the server.","E":"Spark Connect\'s gRPC protocol maintains compatibility across versions, so an older client library can keep issuing requests to a newer server without a matching client-side upgrade."},"explanation":"A. Incorrect — there is no shared JVM classpath exchanged between client and server; Spark Connect deliberately avoids that kind of tight coupling, which is why client and server can be upgraded independently.B. Incorrect — clients submit DataFrame operations as plans, not compiled Java bytecode, so there is no bytecode-to-intermediate-representation conversion happening as part of a server upgrade.C. Incorrect — Spark Connect does not pin clients to a fixed server version; the whole point of protocol compatibility is that a client can keep talking to a server that has since been upgraded.D. Correct — because the client only builds and transmits an unresolved logical plan, everything version-specific about execution stays on the server side, so a server upgrade does not change what the client needs to send.E. Correct — Spark Connect is designed so the gRPC-based protocol between client and server stays compatible across versions, letting an existing client keep working against a newly upgraded server without needing its own rebuild.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"33","question":"A data scientist installs Databricks Connect in a local IDE and writes PySpark DataFrame code that references a table in Unity Catalog, then runs the script. Which description matches how Databricks Connect executes this code?","options":{"A":"Databricks Connect copies the entire Databricks Runtime onto the local IDE\'s machine, so all DataFrame operations execute locally before results sync back to Unity Catalog.","B":"Databricks Connect requires the script to be uploaded as a notebook first, since local IDEs cannot submit Spark Connect requests directly to a remote cluster.","C":"Databricks Connect runs the driver locally and the executors locally as well, only contacting Unity Catalog at the very end to write the final output table to storage.","D":"Databricks Connect converts every DataFrame call into a SQL warehouse query behind the scenes, bypassing the Spark execution engine on the remote cluster entirely.","E":"Databricks Connect acts as a Spark Connect client: DataFrame operations build a logical plan locally and send it over gRPC to the remote cluster for execution."},"correct":"E","explanation":"A. Databricks Connect does not install or copy the Databricks Runtime onto the local machine; the whole point of the thin client design is that execution happens remotely, not that the runtime is duplicated locally.B. One of the main benefits of Databricks Connect is that it lets a local IDE submit Spark Connect requests directly to a remote cluster without ever converting the script into a notebook first.C. Running both the driver and executors locally would make Databricks Connect indistinguishable from local mode, but its purpose is remote execution against a real Databricks cluster and its Unity Catalog access, not local computation.D. Databricks Connect submits work through the Spark execution engine via Spark Connect, not by rewriting DataFrame operations into SQL warehouse queries; the underlying engine and plan structure remain Spark\'s own.E. Databricks Connect is built as a Spark Connect client, so DataFrame code written locally builds a logical plan that is sent over gRPC to the remote Databricks cluster, which executes it with access to Unity Catalog. This matches the intended workflow."},{"id":"34","question":"Which statement accurately describes the relationship between `pyspark.pandas` and the native `pyspark.sql.DataFrame` API?","options":{"A":"`pyspark.pandas` is a pandas-compatible layer built on top of Spark DataFrames, so operations written with its API are translated into the same distributed Spark execution plan that native PySpark DataFrames use.","B":"`pyspark.pandas` is a completely separate execution engine from Spark SQL, so DataFrames created with it run on a dedicated single-node process that exists outside of the Spark cluster entirely.","C":"`pyspark.pandas` replaces `pyspark.sql.DataFrame` starting from Spark 3.2, meaning the native DataFrame API is deprecated and all new PySpark code should use pandas-on-Spark DataFrames exclusively from that version onward.","D":"`pyspark.pandas` only works when Spark is running in local mode, so any pandas-on-Spark code submitted to a multi-node cluster automatically falls back to a native PySpark DataFrame instead.","E":"`pyspark.pandas` is a client-side charting library that renders pandas-style plots from query results, and it does not provide any DataFrame transformation or aggregation methods of its own."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. This is correct: `pyspark.pandas` provides pandas-compatible syntax that is executed through the same underlying Spark DataFrame execution engine, so it is a layer on top of, not a replacement for, native PySpark DataFrames.B. This is incorrect because `pyspark.pandas` runs within the Spark cluster and relies on the same distributed engine as native DataFrames; it is not a separate single-node execution engine outside the cluster.C. This is incorrect because the native `pyspark.sql.DataFrame` API remains fully supported and is not deprecated; `pyspark.pandas` is an additional, complementary interface rather than a replacement.D. This is incorrect because the pandas API on Spark is designed to run on distributed multi-node clusters just like native PySpark, and there is no local-mode-only restriction or automatic fallback behavior.E. This is incorrect because `pyspark.pandas` provides a full set of DataFrame transformation, aggregation, and I/O methods mirroring pandas; plotting is only one small part of its functionality, not its entire purpose."},{"id":"35","question":"A pipeline needs to filter out rows where `amount` is negative and add a `processed_at` timestamp column, operating on arbitrary batches of the whole DataFrame rather than per group. Which call correctly uses `mapInPandas` to do this?","options":{"A":"```python\\ndef clean_batch(iterator):\\nfor pdf in iterator:\\npdf = pdf[pdf[\\"amount\\"] >= 0].copy()\\npdf[\\"processed_at\\"] = pd.Timestamp.now()\\nyield pdf\\n\\ndf.mapInPandas(clean_batch)\\n```, omitting the `schema` argument since mapInPandas can infer it from the yielded DataFrame.","B":"```python\\ndef clean_batch(iterator):\\nfor pdf in iterator:\\npdf = pdf[pdf[\\"amount\\"] >= 0].copy()\\npdf[\\"processed_at\\"] = pd.Timestamp.now()\\nyield pdf\\n\\ndf.mapInPandas(clean_batch, schema=new_schema)\\n```, applied directly on the ungrouped DataFrame.","C":"The same `clean_batch` function, but called as `df.groupby(\\"amount\\").mapInPandas(clean_batch, schema=new_schema)` so each batch is scoped to one distinct `amount` value.","D":"```python\\n@pandas_udf(new_schema)\\ndef clean_batch(pdf: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:\\npdf = pdf[pdf[\\"amount\\"] >= 0].copy()\\npdf[\\"processed_at\\"] = pd.Timestamp.now()\\nreturn pdf\\n\\ndf.mapInPandas(clean_batch)\\n```, decorating the function with `pandas_udf` before passing it to `mapInPandas`.","E":"```python\\ndef clean_batch(pdf):\\npdf = pdf[pdf[\\"amount\\"] >= 0].copy()\\npdf[\\"processed_at\\"] = pd.Timestamp.now()\\nreturn pdf\\n\\ndf.mapInPandas(clean_batch, schema=new_schema)\\n```, where the function takes and returns a single pandas DataFrame instead of an iterator."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. mapInPandas requires the schema to be passed explicitly as an argument because Spark cannot infer the output schema from Python code before execution; omitting it raises an error rather than being inferred automatically.B. mapInPandas takes a function that accepts and returns an iterator of pandas DataFrames representing arbitrary batches, and requires an explicit schema argument describing the output; calling it directly on the ungrouped DataFrame matches exactly how this batch-level filtering and column addition is meant to be expressed.C. mapInPandas processes arbitrary batches of the whole DataFrame and is not a GroupedData method, so chaining it after groupby is not a supported call pattern for this operation.D. mapInPandas expects a plain Python function with an iterator-to-iterator signature, not a function decorated with pandas_udf; wrapping the function this way and calling mapInPandas without a schema argument does not match its required calling convention.E. mapInPandas requires the function to accept and yield an iterator of pandas DataFrames, not a single DataFrame passed and returned directly, so this signature does not match what mapInPandas expects to call."}]},{"id":"databricks-context-engineer-associate","title":"Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"A retrieval step returns three chunks: one relevant passage about a product\'s warranty policy and two unrelated chunks about a different product line that happened to match on shared keywords. The agent\'s final answer paraphrases details from one of the unrelated chunks instead of the relevant warranty passage. Which failure mode best explains this behavior?","options":{"A":"Context clash","B":"Context confusion","C":"Context poisoning","D":"Context distraction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Context clash requires two contradictory statements both present in context; the unrelated chunks do not contradict the warranty passage, they are simply off-topic.B. Correct. Irrelevant content (the unrelated product chunks) was present in the context and ended up being used to generate the response instead of the relevant passage — this is context confusion, where superfluous content degrades output quality.C. Incorrect. Context poisoning requires a hallucinated or erroneous fact to be introduced and then repeatedly relied on; here the unrelated chunks are real content, just irrelevant to the question.D. Incorrect. Context distraction is about over-weighting a large volume of prior actions/history, not about a small number of irrelevant retrieved documents being misused."},{"id":"2","question":"A team migrating from a legacy Agent Evaluation workflow wants to consolidate tracing, evaluation, and monitoring for their GenAI agent under a single, currently supported Databricks framework. Which tool should they migrate to?","options":{"A":"MLflow 3 for GenAI","B":"Lakebase","C":"Unity Catalog","D":"MCP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct. MLflow 3 for GenAI is the current, unified framework for tracing, evaluation, and monitoring of GenAI applications, and Databricks documents migration paths from legacy Agent Evaluation to it.B. Incorrect. Lakebase is a transactional Postgres database for application and agent state, not an evaluation or tracing framework.C. Incorrect. Unity Catalog is a governance and data catalog layer; it does not provide tracing, evaluation, or monitoring for agent behavior.D. Incorrect. MCP connects agents to tools and resources; it has no built-in evaluation or tracing capability of its own."},{"id":"3","question":"In a long-running coding agent session, a large raw file listing (thousands of lines) returned by a tool call several turns ago is still sitting in the message history, even though the agent has since moved on to unrelated edits. What is the most effective way to reduce the attention this stale content consumes?","options":{"A":"Raise the reasoning effort so the model can better ignore the irrelevant listing on its own","B":"Re-run the same tool call again so the agent has a fresher copy of the listing","C":"Append a new system prompt instruction telling the model to never re-read old tool outputs","D":"Apply tool result clearing to discard the superseded raw output while keeping the turn\'s outcome in history"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Incorrect. A higher reasoning effort spends more compute per response but does not remove the stale tokens still occupying context, so the underlying dilution remains.B. Incorrect. Re-running the tool call adds another large, mostly redundant listing to history, increasing token consumption rather than reducing it.C. Incorrect. An added instruction competes for attention just like any other token and does not actually remove the large stale listing from context, so the disproportionate consumption remains.D. Correct. Once a tool result deep in history is no longer needed verbatim, clearing that raw output is one of the lightest-touch and safest forms of compaction, freeing attention budget while preserving the decision or outcome that mattered."},{"id":"4","question":"During a long customer-support session, an agent begins repeatedly re-calling the same order-lookup tool it already called 30 turns earlier, and its final answers start to contradict facts it stated correctly at the start of the session. What is the best first intervention to restore reliable behavior for this and future long sessions?","options":{"A":"Switch the agent to a higher-latency reasoning endpoint, using additional processing time to prevent contradictory answers and repeated tool calls.","B":"Add more few-shot examples to the system prompt that illustrate how to retain earlier tool results and avoid contradicting previous statements.","C":"Introduce context compaction that summarizes older turns and superseded tool results before the context window becomes full.","D":"Increase the maximum token limit for model responses to carry more conversation state forward, reducing the chance of redundant tool lookups."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Switching to a higher-latency reasoning endpoint provides more compute time per turn but does not mitigate the real issue: the agent\'s context has become overloaded with accumulated history. The repeated tool calls and contradictions stem from a diluted context, not insufficient processing time, so this intervention would not restore reliable behavior.B. Incorrect. Adding more few-shot examples to the system prompt directly increases the token count in an already strained context, which is likely to exacerbate the degradation. This intervention does not remove accumulated noise and may even intensify contradictions and redundant calls by further diluting the model\'s attention.C. Correct. Repeated redundant tool calls and contradictory answers indicate that the model\'s reasoning is being degraded by an overlong context. Context compaction summarizes older turns and superseded tool results, preserving essential facts while removing noise, which restores reliable behavior for long sessions.D. Incorrect. Increasing the maximum token limit for model responses only makes each response longer; it does not resolve the underlying problem of an overly long context collapsing reasoning. In fact, longer responses add more tokens to the conversation history, which can further dilute the model\'s ability to recall earlier facts and increase the chance of contradictions."},{"id":"5","question":"A Genie space owner wants a colleague to update the space\'s instructions and example queries but must prevent that colleague from sharing the space with additional users or changing its permissions. Which permission level should the owner grant?","options":{"A":"CAN USE","B":"CAN VIEW","C":"CAN RUN","D":"CAN EDIT","E":"CAN MANAGE"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The right level is CAN EDIT. In a Genie space it lets the colleague change instructions, example SQL queries and other space configuration, but it does not allow sharing the space or changing its permissions; that requires CAN MANAGE.\\n\\nCAN MANAGE would also let them share and change permissions, which the owner wants to prevent. CAN RUN and CAN VIEW, or CAN USE, only let them ask questions or view the space, not edit its instructions.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"6","question":"A context engineer is curating few-shot examples for a Genie space under a strict token budget. The candidate pool already has three examples that each demonstrate a straightforward single-table filter query returning the same output shape. A fourth candidate demonstrates a multi-step aggregation with a re-used calculation the finance team asks about weekly. Which action best reflects marginal-contribution reasoning under the budget?","options":{"A":"Rewrite the fourth example as a single-table filter so it matches the existing examples","B":"Include the fourth example and drop one of the redundant single-table filter examples","C":"Exclude the fourth example because multi-step aggregations consume more tokens than filters","D":"Include all four examples so the agent sees the widest possible variety of query shapes","E":"Drop all three filter examples and keep only the aggregation example"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Under a fixed token budget, every few-shot example should add new information. The three single-table filters teach the same pattern, so their marginal contribution is low. The multi-step aggregation covers a frequent, harder question that none of the others demonstrate. Swap one redundant filter for it.\\n\\nRewriting the aggregation as a filter removes exactly what it was adding. Excluding it purely for its token cost ignores its value. Keeping all four may break the budget. Dropping every filter example loses coverage of the most common pattern.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"7","question":"When a user asks about sales in \\"Australia,\\" a Genie agent\'s query returns zero rows because the region column stores three-letter codes like \\"AUS\\" rather than full country names. Which revision most directly and cheaply fixes this failure?","options":{"A":"Enable example values or a value dictionary so the agent maps spoken country names to stored codes","B":"Add a general instruction that spells out every country name-to-code mapping the agent might need","C":"Instruct users to always type the three-letter country code instead of the full country name","D":"Rewrite the underlying Delta table so country values are stored as full names instead of codes","E":"Add several example SQL queries that filter on each possible country code one by one"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct. This is exactly the value-matching failure mode that example values and value dictionaries are designed to solve: the agent learns the mapping once, with no added per-query tokens and no ongoing instruction upkeep.B. Incorrect. Spelling out every mapping in a general instruction bloats the prompt with data that belongs in metadata, adds tokens to every request, and must be manually updated whenever new countries appear.C. Incorrect. Pushing the mapping burden onto users breaks the natural-language interface and does not fix the agent\'s behavior for anyone who phrases the question naturally.D. Incorrect. Changing the underlying table schema is a data engineering change outside prompt revision scope and may break other systems that depend on the coded values.E. Incorrect. Enumerating every code in separate example queries is far higher maintenance than a single value dictionary and does not scale as new codes are added."},{"id":"8","question":"A context engineer runs two system prompt configurations for a medical-device troubleshooting agent through MLflow experiment tracking. Config A (800 tokens, plain instructions) scores 82% on the LLM-judge accuracy scorer with 1.2s average latency. Config B (2,200 tokens, adds six few-shot examples of correctly diagnosing edge-case failures) scores 94% accuracy with 2.1s average latency. Wrong diagnoses in this domain can lead to safety incidents. Which conclusion best fits the tracked results?","options":{"A":"Config A is justified: the 800-token plain instructions already cover the edge cases that the six few-shot examples illustrate, avoiding extra latency.","B":"Neither config is usable: MLflow experiment tracking cannot compare prompts of different token lengths, as the size difference skews the metrics.","C":"Config B is justified: the few-shot examples drive a large accuracy gain in a safety-critical domain where the added latency is quite acceptable.","D":"Config A is justified: the 82% accuracy with 1.2s latency meets safety thresholds, and any latency increase above 1s is unacceptable regardless of the few-shot accuracy gain.","E":"Config B is justified: the higher token count of the few-shot prompt directly improves output quality, so the longer configuration should be selected."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Config A’s 82% accuracy suggests it does not adequately cover the edge cases captured by the few-shot examples in Config B. The lower accuracy implies that the plain instructions miss important failure patterns that the examples help diagnose correctly.B. Incorrect. MLflow experiment tracking can compare prompts of different token lengths without bias, as metrics like accuracy and latency are measured based on model outputs, not prompt sizes. The token count difference does not invalidate the comparison; it is a legitimate variable in the experiment.C. Correct. The few-shot examples in Config B lead to a substantial 12-point accuracy increase, which is critical for safety in medical-device troubleshooting. The added latency (from 1.2s to 2.1s) is modest and acceptable given the high stakes, so the tradeoff strongly favors Config B.D. Incorrect. The scenario does not state any pre-defined safety threshold for accuracy, and a hard 1-second latency limit is an arbitrary constraint not supported by the domain. In safety-critical contexts, the priority is typically on reducing wrong diagnoses, not an absolute latency ceiling.E. Incorrect. A higher token count alone does not inherently improve output quality; it is the specific few-shot examples that likely drive the accuracy gain. Selecting Config B simply because it is longer ignores the fact that prompt content, not length, determines effectiveness."},{"id":"9","question":"A Unity Catalog table has columns `created_at` and `updated_at` with no descriptions. When users ask \\"how many customers signed up last month,\\" Genie sometimes filters on `updated_at` instead of `created_at`, producing incorrect counts. What is the highest-impact fix?","options":{"A":"Define a One-to-One relationship between the `created_at` and `updated_at` columns.","B":"Add column descriptions noting `created_at` is signup time, `updated_at` is last-modified time.","C":"Add an example-value dictionary listing sample dates for the `created_at` column.","D":"Add a general instruction that lists every possible date-related question a user might ask.","E":"Rename the `updated_at` column so it is less likely to be confused with `created_at`."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Incorrect. `created_at` and `updated_at` are two attributes of the same row, not two separate tables needing a join relationship; cardinality configuration is not applicable here.B. Correct. The ambiguity is purely semantic: two similarly named timestamp columns with no documentation of what each represents. Column descriptions give Genie the business meaning it needs to pick the right column for \\"signed up\\".C. Incorrect. Sample date values do not clarify which column represents signup versus modification; the values in both columns look the same (dates), so a value dictionary does not resolve the ambiguity.D. Incorrect. Enumerating every possible phrasing does not scale and does not address the root cause, which is that the columns themselves are undocumented.E. Incorrect. Renaming columns is a schema change outside typical metadata configuration and is unnecessary when a column description can resolve the ambiguity without touching the underlying table."},{"id":"10","question":"When establishing a starting point for chunk overlap while tuning a chunking strategy, what overlap percentage is commonly used as an initial baseline?","options":{"A":"Approximately 50%","B":"Approximately 90%","C":"No overlap (0%), since overlap is not recommended","D":"Approximately 10%"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Incorrect. 50% overlap is far higher than the typical starting baseline and would substantially increase index size and redundancy for most corpora.B. Incorrect. 90% overlap is an extreme value that would make most adjacent chunks nearly identical, wasting index space without a corresponding baseline recommendation.C. Incorrect. Some overlap is generally recommended as a starting point to avoid losing context at chunk boundaries; a 0% baseline is not the typical recommendation.D. Correct. A common starting point for chunk overlap when tuning a chunking strategy is around 10%, which is then adjusted iteratively based on document structure and evaluation results."},{"id":"11","question":"A team is building a support assistant over a 5,000-page product manual corpus that is revised only once per quarter. Users ask free-form natural-language questions, and the team wants sub-second answers with minimal per-query compute overhead. Which retrieval strategy best fits this use case?","options":{"A":"Pre-inference retrieval using an AI Search embedding index built from the Delta table holding the manual content","B":"Just-in-time agentic retrieval that issues a fresh SQL query against the manual\'s Delta table on every request","C":"A Unity Catalog function tool call that recomputes embeddings for the entire manual at each inference","D":"A Genie space that runs an ad hoc aggregation query against the manual table for every question"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct. The corpus is large, unstructured, and changes infrequently, so pre-computing embeddings once and querying the resulting AI Search index at inference time gives fast semantic matches without repeating expensive indexing work per query.B. Incorrect. Issuing a fresh SQL query per request suits structured, frequently changing data, not semantic search over free-form text; it also cannot perform similarity matching the way an embedding index can.C. Incorrect. Recomputing embeddings for the entire corpus on every inference call wastes compute and adds latency that a static quarterly-updated index avoids entirely.D. Incorrect. Genie spaces are designed for structured tabular question answering via generated SQL, not semantic retrieval over long-form unstructured manual text."},{"id":"12","question":"An AI Search deployment for a RAG agent must apply user-department-level security so that Finance users cannot retrieve HR documents, but Databricks AI Search does not support native row- or column-level permissions on its indexes. Which design correctly enforces this constraint before deployment?","options":{"A":"Configure the embedding model to encode department names so semantically similar rows are excluded automatically","B":"Grant every user CAN MANAGE on the AI Search endpoint so departmental filtering happens client-side","C":"Add a department metadata column to the source Delta table and apply it as a filter in the AI Search query API for every retrieval call","D":"Rely on Unity Catalog table ACLs on the AI Search index object itself to filter individual rows by department","E":"Split the vector index into per-department Delta Sync Indexes and let the agent randomly choose one at query time"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Embeddings capture semantic similarity, not access policy; they cannot reliably or deterministically exclude a department\'s records.B. Incorrect. Broad CAN MANAGE grants increase exposure rather than restrict it, and endpoint-level permissions do not implement row-level filtering.C. Correct. Since AI Search lacks native row/column-level security, the documented application-level pattern is to tag rows with a security metadata column and enforce it via the query-time filter API on every call.D. Incorrect. Unity Catalog ACLs on the index govern who can manage or query the index object as a whole; they do not filter individual rows by a department value.E. Incorrect. Random selection provides no reliable access boundary and could just as easily return the wrong department\'s data."},{"id":"13","question":"In the context of diagnosing retrieval failures with Unity Catalog governance, what is the primary difference between a Unity Catalog grant (such as SELECT) and a Unity Catalog row filter when used to fix a retrieval access problem?","options":{"A":"A grant restricts which columns are visible by filtering columns in query results, while a row filter restricts which rows are cached in the AI Search index by evaluating the filter at indexing time.","B":"A grant is enforced at query time by the SQL warehouse when a user runs a SELECT, while a row filter is enforced only when the Delta table is first created and filters are baked into the data.","C":"A grant and a row filter are interchangeable mechanisms that both operate at the column level by specifying column masks that apply during query execution, so they address retrieval failures the same.","D":"A grant controls whether a principal can access a table at all, while a row filter restricts which specific rows a principal sees within a table they can already access.","E":"A grant applies only to Genie spaces and controls dataset access for those spaces, while a row filter applies only to AI Search indexes and determines which indexed rows are returned in search results."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Grants do not filter columns—that is the role of column masks. Row filters are enforced at query time on the active data, not merely at indexing time; they dynamically restrict rows for each query, rather than being baked into indexes.B. Incorrect. While grants are enforced at query time, row filters are also enforced dynamically at query time and are not limited to table creation. Row filters apply to the live Delta table content and are not permanently baked into the data, so this option misrepresents their enforcement.C. Incorrect. Grants and row filters are distinct mechanisms: grants control object-level access, while row filters restrict row-level visibility. They do not both target columns, and they address retrieval failures differently—row filters do not act as column masks.D. Correct. A Unity Catalog grant (e.g., SELECT) decides whether a principal can access a table at all, acting as a coarse permission. A row filter applies after table access is granted, narrowing the result set to only those rows the principal is allowed to see, thus providing fine-grained control.E. Incorrect. Unity Catalog grants are not scoped only to Genie spaces; they control access to any securable object (e.g., tables, views) across Databricks. Row filters operate at query time on tables, not exclusively during AI Search indexing, and they limit visible rows regardless of the access interface."},{"id":"14","question":"A team\'s evaluation harness shows that their RAG agent\'s top-10 retrieved chunks contain the correct passage about 80% of the time, but the agent\'s final answers are still wrong roughly 30% of the time because the correct chunk is often ranked 6th-10th, surrounded by superficially similar but less relevant chunks. Latency budget allows an extra ~250ms-1s. Which configuration change most directly addresses this specific problem?","options":{"A":"Switch from Delta Sync to Direct Vector Access so the index stays current, helping rank the correct chunk higher.","B":"Increase chunk overlap to 20% to retain surrounding context, improving the ranking of the correct chunk among top results.","C":"Fine-tune a custom embedding model on domain data to produce more accurate vectors, helping rank correct chunk higher.","D":"Add Databricks reranker to reorder retrieved chunks, moving the correct one up, before prompting LLM."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Switching to Direct Vector Access ensures the index reflects the latest data but does not alter the ranking of already retrieved chunks. The problem is not stale data but low relevance ranking among top results, so this change would not directly fix the issue.B. Incorrect. Increasing chunk overlap may preserve more context but can also introduce noise and does not reorder the retrieved chunks to prioritize the most relevant one. It is an indirect and less targeted approach compared to reranking.C. Incorrect. Fine-tuning an embedding model is a lengthy process that might improve future retrieval quality, but it does not immediately re-rank the current top-10 chunks to surface the correct one. It also cannot guarantee that the correct chunk will move above similar but less relevant neighbors within the allowed latency.D. Correct. The Databricks reranker re-scores the top retrieved chunks based on relevance, specifically moving the correct passage from a lower rank (6th-10th) to the top, directly addressing the ranking issue. This operation adds only a few hundred milliseconds of latency, fitting well within the 250ms-1s budget."},{"id":"15","question":"In an agent built with the Databricks agent framework, a retriever tool queries an AI Search index and returns the top-k matching chunks for a user\'s question. How are these chunks typically incorporated so the agent can generate a grounded response?","options":{"A":"The retriever tool\'s output chunks are appended to the conversation as a message, allowing the LLM to ground its next response on them.","B":"The chunks replace the system prompt entirely, so the LLM generates its answer using only the retrieved content from the AI Search index.","C":"The chunks are written back into the source Delta table, updating the data so that when the LLM is invoked it queries the refreshed table for context.","D":"The chunks bypass the agent\'s context window and are injected directly into the Unity Catalog function definition, so the LLM reads them from the definition."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct. When the retriever tool executes, its output (the retrieved chunks) is appended to the conversation as a message, effectively adding the retrieved information to the agent\'s context. The LLM can then ground its next response on this newly available context along with the existing conversation history.B. Incorrect. The system prompt defines the agent\'s overall instructions and behavior and is not replaced by retrieved chunks. Instead, the chunks are added as supplementary context, augmenting rather than substituting the prompt.C. Incorrect. Writing chunks back to the source Delta table does not make them available to the LLM for the current request and would only duplicate data already indexed. The LLM obtains context directly from the retriever\'s output, not by re-querying the table.D. Incorrect. Unity Catalog function definitions describe a tool\'s interface and implementation, not a mechanism for passing per-query retrieval results. Retrieved content flows through the agent\'s context window, not by being injected into function definitions."},{"id":"16","question":"An agent runs a multi-step data migration that can take up to three days to complete, with the underlying compute occasionally restarting between steps. The team needs the agent to resume exactly where it left off after any restart. Which memory design best fits this requirement?","options":{"A":"Keep the step-by-step progress only in the model\'s context window scratchpad","B":"Persist step progress to a Lakebase-backed state table and reload it on restart","C":"Re-summarize the entire conversation history at the start of every new step","D":"Store progress in a temporary variable inside the agent\'s Python process"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Incorrect: an in-context scratchpad is lost whenever the process restarts or the session ends, so it cannot survive a multi-day job with compute restarts.B. Correct: a Delta-backed state store like Lakebase persists across restarts and sessions, letting the agent read back exactly where it stopped and resume reliably.C. Incorrect: resummarizing conversation history does not recover durable checkpoint data and still depends on context that may already be gone after a restart.D. Incorrect: an in-process variable is held in memory and is wiped out the moment the process restarts, offering no durability."},{"id":"17","question":"A support agent stores a running log of past customer interactions as memory entries persisted in Lakebase. When a new ticket arrives, the agent wants to surface prior interactions that are conceptually related to the new issue, even if they use different wording and share no exact keywords with the new ticket. Which mechanism should retrieve these memories?","options":{"A":"A structured query sorting memory rows by creation timestamp","B":"A structured query joining memory rows to a fixed lookup table","C":"Databricks AI Search vector similarity search over embedded memory entries","D":"A structured query filtering memory rows on an exact ticket-category column"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Sorting by recency retrieves memories based on time, not on conceptual relevance to the new ticket\'s content.B. Incorrect. A join against a fixed lookup table resolves known relationships between records; it does not measure semantic similarity between free-text memory content and a new query.C. Correct. Finding conceptually related content that shares no exact keywords requires comparing embeddings for semantic closeness, which is exactly what Databricks AI Search\'s vector similarity search provides.D. Incorrect. An exact-match category filter only returns rows that share the same discrete label; it cannot surface conceptually related memories that fall outside that category or use different wording."},{"id":"18","question":"Two context configurations are evaluated with both Correctness() and Safety() scorers. Configuration X scores highest on Correctness but noticeably lower on Safety than configuration Y, which scores slightly lower on Correctness but highest on Safety. A reviewer wants to pick the configuration that produced the most reliable outcomes overall for the task. What approach should they take?","options":{"A":"Discard both runs and rerun evaluation until one configuration wins on every scorer","B":"Select configuration X, since Correctness is always the primary signal for reliability","C":"Select configuration Y, since Safety scores should always outrank Correctness scores","D":"Weigh both scorer results together against the task\'s requirements rather than deciding from either scorer alone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Different scorers measure different dimensions. Correctness checks whether answers match the expected facts, while Safety checks for harmful or policy-violating content. Reliability for a task means weighing them against that task\'s requirements; a regulated or customer-facing task may make safety a hard constraint.\\n\\nAlways treating one scorer as primary, in either direction, ignores the context. Rerunning until one configuration wins on every scorer wastes effort and may never converge.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"19","question":"In the context of an agent memory system, what does the term \'context pollution\' refer to?","options":{"A":"Degraded output quality caused by irrelevant retrieved memories crowding out the relevant ones in context","B":"A security vulnerability where memory records are tampered with by unauthorized users","C":"A network failure that prevents the memory store from returning any results","D":"A schema mismatch that causes memory writes to be silently dropped"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct: context pollution describes the over-retrieval symptom where too much irrelevant or low-value memory content is included, diluting the relevant signal and degrading response quality.B. Incorrect: this describes a security/tampering issue, not the quality-dilution effect that context pollution refers to.C. Incorrect: this describes an availability failure, not the retrieval-quality issue captured by \'context pollution\'.D. Incorrect: this describes a data-integrity or write-path issue, unrelated to the concept of irrelevant content diluting retrieved context."},{"id":"20","question":"A team is auditing an agent that keeps producing accurate but contextually mismatched answers. Which of the following observations suggest that the pipeline is retrieving before user intent has been resolved, rather than having a retrieval-quality problem? (Select 3.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"The underlying Delta table backing the index was refreshed one day later than scheduled, so the retriever uses a snapshot captured prior to user intent processing.","B":"Retrieved passages are on-target but answer a different sub-question than intended, implying retrieval occurred before intent resolution.","C":"Query latency grows as the AI Search index grows larger, and it often returns results based on a partial query before the user finishes typing.","D":"The agent frequently needs a second retrieval pass after user rephrasing, because the first pass targeted the wrong meaning of the query.","E":"The same literal query text should map to different answers depending on the conversation context the agent had ignored before retrieving."},"explanation":"A. Incorrect. A day-late Delta table refresh results in a stale index snapshot, which is a data-freshness issue in the retrieval layer. This timing mismatch does not indicate that user intent was unresolved before retrieval; it simply means the retriever is working with outdated data.F. Incorrect. Upgrading the embedding model changes the vector space, causing a misalignment between retrieval and query rewriting. This is a retrieval-quality or model-drift issue, not evidence that the pipeline is retrieving before resolving user intent.B. Correct. When retrieved passages are on-target but answer a different sub-question than intended, it indicates the system fetched documents without first disambiguating the query\'s intended meaning. This is a clear sign that retrieval happened before intent resolution, rather than a retrieval-quality problem.C. Incorrect. Growing query latency as the index grows larger is a performance and scalability issue, not evidence that intent resolution was skipped. Returning results for partial queries may reflect an as-you-type search pattern, which is a user interface design and does not necessarily mean the pipeline failed to resolve user intent before retrieval.D. Correct. Requiring a second retrieval pass after user rephrasing suggests the initial query targeted the wrong meaning, implying that intent was not properly resolved before the first retrieval. This rephrase-and-retry pattern is characteristic of unresolved intent, not a deficiency in retrieval quality.E. Correct. When conversation context that should disambiguate a literal query is ignored before retrieval, the agent fails to resolve user intent. This directly leads to different answers for the same query text, which is a telltale sign that retrieval was performed without first interpreting what the user meant.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"21","question":"A customer support agent resets its conversation buffer at the start of every new chat session and has no other memory mechanism. A customer who opened a ticket last month and is returning today has to re-explain their entire issue history, and the agent cannot reference the prior ticket\'s resolution steps. Which memory strategy addresses this mismatch?","options":{"A":"Extend the conversation buffer to retain the previous month\'s chat history across sessions, so the agent automatically has context when a returning customer starts a new chat.","B":"Configure the agent to keep a single persistent buffer for all customer chats, and when a customer returns, have the agent reference that buffer to recall prior conversations.","C":"Have the agent ask a series of questions at the start of each new chat to collect the previous ticket\'s details from the customer, proceeding without re-explanation.","D":"Persist past ticket history to Lakebase keyed by customer account ID, and retrieve the relevant history when a new session for that customer begins."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Incorrect. The conversation buffer is reset at the start of every new session regardless of its length, so extending it does not allow information to survive across separate sessions.B. Incorrect. Retaining one shared buffer across all customers indefinitely conflates unrelated customers\' histories and fails to scope persistence to the correct customer identity, creating a new mismatch rather than resolving the original one.C. Incorrect. This approach works around the missing memory rather than fixing it, and it increases friction for the customer instead of matching the memory type to the actual cross-session need.D. Correct. The information need spans separate sessions weeks apart, which is a cross-session persistence need. Storing ticket history durably in Lakebase, keyed by customer account ID, and retrieving the relevant history at the start of a new session is the correct memory strategy."},{"id":"22","question":"A team is deciding where to persist an agent\'s long-term memory records so that multiple agent instances running on different compute nodes can read and write the same memory consistently. Which characteristic of Lakebase makes it well suited to this durable, shared state role?","options":{"A":"It requires each compute node to maintain its own local copy of the memory table for fast reads and writes that do not need network access.","B":"It only supports a single connection at a time and serializes all memory operations to prevent write conflicts across nodes.","C":"It is a fully managed Postgres database. Any authorized compute node can connect to it for consistent reads and writes.","D":"It stores memory records locally on the creating node and allows other nodes to read and write those records directly over the network."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Lakebase provides a single shared database, not per-node local copies. Maintaining separate local copies would cause data divergence and consistency issues, undermining the goal of durable, shared state across distributed agent instances.B. Incorrect. As a fully managed Postgres database, Lakebase supports multiple concurrent connections and uses standard concurrency control mechanisms. Serializing all operations through a single connection would create a severe bottleneck and limit scalability for distributed agent workflows.C. Correct. Lakebase is a fully managed Postgres database, providing a centralized, consistent data store. Multiple agent instances on different compute nodes can connect to it with proper authorization, enabling reliable concurrent reads and writes with ACID guarantees.D. Incorrect. Lakebase does not store memory records locally on the creating node; it is a managed remote database service. Allowing other nodes to directly read and write local storage over the network would bypass centralized consistency controls and lead to conflicts."},{"id":"23","question":"A retail support agent has two MCP tools:\\n- search_products: \\"Finds matching entries for a query.\\"\\n- search_faqs: \\"Finds matching entries for a query.\\"\\nWhen a customer asks a product-availability question, the agent sometimes calls search_faqs instead of search_products. What in the tool descriptions is driving this ambiguous selection?","options":{"A":"Both tools are hosted on the same external MCP server, so Unity Catalog collapses their permission scopes into a single entry.","B":"Both descriptions say the tool \\"finds matching entries for a query\\" without naming the data source, so intent can\'t map to catalog vs. FAQ content.","C":"Both tools accept an identical \\"query\\" parameter name, which causes the agent\'s schema validator to treat the two functions as one.","D":"Both descriptions use present-tense verbs, which the agent\'s planner interprets as a signal that only one tool should ever be selected."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Being hosted on the same MCP server does not merge Unity Catalog permission scopes or tool identities; each registered tool retains its own description and access control entry.B. Correct. Neither description states what is being searched (the product catalog vs. the FAQ knowledge base), so the two tools read as functionally identical to the model, which then has to guess.C. Incorrect. A shared parameter name affects how arguments are supplied, not whether the model can distinguish which tool\'s purpose matches the user\'s intent — the failure here is in the description text, not the schema.D. Incorrect. Verb tense in a description has no bearing on how many tools an LLM is willing to select from; this isn\'t a documented mechanism of tool-selection ambiguity."},{"id":"24","question":"An engineer worries that adopting progressive disclosure for their agent\'s 25 tools will \\"hide\\" tools from the model and make some tools permanently unreachable, so functionality will be lost compared to loading all 25 full schemas upfront. Which statement correctly addresses this concern?","options":{"A":"Progressive disclosure changes only when a schema enters context, not which tools exist; every tool stays discoverable and callable","B":"The concern is valid: progressive disclosure permanently removes any tool whose schema is not loaded in the first two turns","C":"The concern is valid: progressive disclosure limits the agent to calling at most one tool per session, regardless of connections","D":"Progressive disclosure resolves this only by disabling tool calling entirely and having a human run every tool manually"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct. Progressive disclosure is a staging strategy for token cost, not a restriction on capability: the always-available summary keeps every one of the 25 tools discoverable, and any tool\'s full schema can still be loaded into context whenever the agent decides to call it, so no functionality is lost relative to loading everything upfront.B. Incorrect. Nothing about progressive disclosure imposes a hard cutoff after a fixed number of turns; a tool\'s full schema can be loaded on whichever turn the agent actually selects it, even late in a long conversation, as long as its summary remained discoverable.C. Incorrect. There is no inherent one-tool-per-session limit in progressive disclosure; the agent can call as many different tools as the task requires, it just loads each one\'s full schema only at the point it is needed rather than all at once.D. Incorrect. Progressive disclosure operates entirely within the agent\'s own tool-calling flow -- it changes what enters context and when, not whether the agent can call tools at all -- so it does not require removing automated tool calling or substituting human execution."},{"id":"25","question":"When choosing among multiple tools registered in Unity Catalog for a given agent task, which three factors should primarily guide the selection?","options":{"A":"Execution cost, hosting cloud region, and catalog owner","B":"Function name length, docstring length, and author name","C":"Databricks Runtime version, cluster size, and SQL warehouse type","D":"Functional fit, input/output compatibility, and task requirements"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Cost, region, and ownership are operational or governance details, not the criteria used to determine whether a tool functionally matches a given agent task.B. Incorrect. Superficial text properties like name or docstring length say nothing about whether a tool\'s behavior and schema satisfy the task.C. Incorrect. Runtime, cluster, and warehouse configuration affect where a Unity Catalog function executes, not whether it is the semantically correct tool to select for a task.D. Correct. Tool selection from a registered set should be justified by how well a tool\'s purpose (functional fit), its parameter and return types (input/output compatibility), and its match to what the task actually needs (task requirements) align with the request."},{"id":"26","question":"While configuring a Genie space, an agent is explicitly told by the user to exclude the deprecated table \\"sales_2019_legacy\\" from the space. Several turns later, a compaction step summarizes the session as \\"configured Genie space with the relevant sales tables.\\" On the next turn, the agent adds \\"sales_2019_legacy\\" back into the space\'s table list. Which category of information was incorrectly discarded during compaction?","options":{"A":"The list of table row counts was discarded, rather than being carried forward as reference statistics","B":"The Genie space\'s display name was discarded, rather than being carried forward as a naming detail","C":"The explicit exclusion decision the user gave was discarded, rather than being carried forward as a standing constraint","D":"The SQL warehouse type backing the Genie space was discarded, rather than being carried forward as configuration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Row counts are reference statistics unrelated to which table was excluded; losing them would not explain the agent re-adding a specifically excluded table.B. Incorrect. The display name has no bearing on which tables are included in the space, so its loss would not produce this symptom.C. Correct. A specific user decision (\\"exclude sales_2019_legacy\\") was reduced to a vague narrative phrase (\\"relevant sales tables\\") that no longer encodes the exclusion, so the agent re-added the table. Decisions and their rationale need to survive compaction as explicit, standing constraints, not paraphrased narrative.D. Incorrect. Nothing in the scenario suggests the warehouse type was lost or caused the observed behavior; the failure is specifically about a table being re-added, tied to a decision, not a configuration detail like warehouse type."},{"id":"27","question":"After several rounds of precision tuning, an engineer wants to confirm that the compaction prompt\'s newest, more aggressive version has not regressed the recall achieved in earlier iterations. What is the most appropriate validation step?","options":{"A":"Ask the agent whether it feels confident in its own summarized context after each compaction run","B":"Re-run the summarizer on traces with known important facts and confirm those facts still appear in the summaries","C":"Assume recall is fine, since precision changes only affect formatting and never touch which facts are kept","D":"Measure only the token count reduction the new prompt version achieves compared to the previous version"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A regression test for recall needs ground truth. Re-run the new compaction prompt on traces where you already know which facts must survive, then check (manually or with an LLM judge) that they still appear in the summaries.\\n\\nThe agent\'s self-reported confidence is not evidence. Precision tuning can drop facts, so recall cannot be assumed. Token reduction measures cost, not whether important information was kept.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"28","question":"A long-running Databricks agent performs a multi-step data migration. In an early turn, the user specifies a target schema that every later transformation step must conform to. By turn 150, context pressure triggers a hard-coded trimming heuristic that drops the oldest 50 turns, including the one containing the schema definition. The agent then produces a transformation that violates the schema. What does this failure indicate about the task\'s information relevance pattern?","options":{"A":"The pattern is non-monotonic: a fact stated early stays critical throughout, so recency-only trimming misses it and compaction is needed here.","B":"The pattern is purely recency-based, so the trimming heuristic was correctly configured, and this failure was an unrelated pipeline bug instead.","C":"The pattern shows old turns are always safe to discard, so the schema definition should have been dropped even sooner than at turn 150.","D":"The pattern shows context window pressure had not truly been reached yet, so trimming should never have run at all at turn 150."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct. The schema fact remains critical long after it ages out of recency-based trimming, showing the relevance pattern is not purely recency-driven and requires compaction to preserve durable facts.B. Incorrect. If the pattern were purely recency-based, dropping the schema turn would not have caused a downstream failure; the observed failure demonstrates the opposite.C. Incorrect. This assumes all older turns are equally safe to discard, which is exactly the assumption the failure disproves for this task.D. Incorrect. The scenario states context pressure did trigger trimming; the failure is about what was dropped, not whether pressure existed."},{"id":"29","question":"An agent trace shows a retrieval step that returned five document chunks from Databricks AI Search for a policy question. The agent\'s answer cites and quotes only chunk 2; chunks 1, 3, 4, and 5 are never referenced again anywhere in the trace. During compaction, which content is safe to remove without affecting downstream task execution?","options":{"A":"The one chunk the agent cited and quoted in its answer","B":"The user\'s original policy question that started retrieval","C":"The agent\'s final answer that quotes the cited chunk","D":"The four unused chunks not referenced again in the trace"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Incorrect. This is the chunk the answer actually quotes; removing it would strip the source evidence the final answer relies on.B. Incorrect. The original question is needed to verify the answer addressed the right policy topic and to support any follow-up questions in the same session.C. Incorrect. This is the agent\'s live deliverable built from the cited chunk, not superseded retrieval noise, so it should be preserved.D. Correct. Chunks 1, 3, 4, and 5 were retrieved but never used in reasoning or in the final answer, so they are redundant retrieval results that can be discarded without any loss of used information."},{"id":"30","question":"A team builds an agent that drafts legal memoranda over dozens of turns, where subtle qualifications negotiated earlier in the conversation must not be lost. Token cost is a secondary concern compared to accuracy. Which compaction approach should the team adopt, and why?","options":{"A":"Aggressive compaction, because it reduces token cost and the agent can always re-derive lost qualifications from memory","B":"Conservative compaction, because it eliminates the need to ever compact the conversation history","C":"Aggressive compaction, because legal drafting tasks rarely depend on details established earlier in a conversation","D":"A hybrid where the conversation is trimmed but never summarized, since trimming has no fidelity cost","E":"Conservative compaction, because it preserves higher fidelity of prior context even though it costs more tokens"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When fidelity matters more than cost, as in multi-turn legal drafting where earlier qualifications must survive, use conservative compaction. It summarizes less aggressively and keeps more of the prior context, at a higher token cost.\\n\\nAggressive compaction trades fidelity for cost, the opposite priority. Conservative compaction still compacts; it does not eliminate compaction. Trimming without summarizing drops earlier turns outright, which is the biggest fidelity loss. Legal tasks depend heavily on earlier details.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"31","question":"The support-ticket triage system is being redesigned so each dispatched sub-agent gets a self-contained task briefing instead of just a ticket ID. Which elements should the coordinator include in that briefing to prevent dispatch failures without growing each sub-agent\'s context window? (Select all that apply)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"The specific objective the sub-agent is responsible for completing","B":"The exact output format expected for the sub-agent\'s response","C":"A clear boundary describing what is out of scope for this sub-agent","D":"The full transcript of every ticket the coordinator has ever routed","E":"A larger context window sized to hold the coordinator\'s full history"},"multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B","C"],"multiSelect":true,"explanation":"A self-contained briefing gives the sub-agent exactly what it needs to act alone: the specific objective, the expected output format so the coordinator can merge results, and explicit scope boundaries so it does not duplicate or overreach. Each is short, so the sub-agent\'s context stays small.\\n\\nThe full routing transcript and a context window sized to the coordinator\'s history both bloat the sub-agent\'s context, which defeats the purpose of focused delegation.","explanationSource":"written"},{"id":"32","question":"A long-horizon data-migration workflow uses periodic checkpoints to track which tables have been migrated. A re-planning sub-agent starts a new step using a checkpoint taken before another sub-agent finished migrating three additional tables, so the re-planning sub-agent schedules those same tables for migration again, conflicting with the sub-agent that already completed them. Which context propagation change would most likely prevent this conflict?","options":{"A":"Give the re-planning sub-agent direct write access to the source tables being migrated","B":"Refresh the re-planning sub-agent\'s context with the latest checkpoint before it plans next","C":"Have the re-planning sub-agent skip checkpoints and always start from the original plan","D":"Run the re-planning sub-agent and the migration sub-agent fully in parallel with no coordination","E":"Lengthen the interval between checkpoints so fewer checkpoints need to be tracked"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Write access changes what the sub-agent is permitted to do, but does not ensure it is given the current migration progress before it plans.B. Correct. Propagating the most recent checkpoint state into the re-planning sub-agent\'s context before it plans ensures it knows which tables are already migrated, directly preventing the duplicate scheduling.C. Incorrect. Ignoring checkpoints and restarting from the original plan discards progress information entirely, which would cause even more redundant re-migration rather than preventing it.D. Incorrect. Running the sub-agents in parallel without coordination removes propagation altogether, making it more likely the re-planning sub-agent acts on stale information.E. Incorrect. Fewer checkpoints reduces tracking overhead but widens the window during which the re-planning sub-agent\'s view of progress is stale."},{"id":"33","question":"A sub-agent performs a multi-step data-cleaning task that generates extensive intermediate reasoning and scratch notes across many tool calls. The orchestrator only needs the final outcome to continue planning, but currently receives every intermediate step. Which sub-agent output design change would most reduce orchestrator context load while keeping the workflow coherent?","options":{"A":"Store the intermediate reasoning in the orchestrator\'s own context for later review","B":"Compress the intermediate reasoning into one long paragraph without the final result","C":"Persist intermediate reasoning locally and return only a compact final-result summary","D":"Delete the intermediate reasoning and scratch notes entirely once the task completes","E":"Return the intermediate reasoning and scratch notes but omit the final result"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Storing the intermediate reasoning in the orchestrator\'s own context is the opposite of load reduction; it moves the bulky content into the very context that is saturating.B. Incorrect. A single long paragraph of compressed reasoning without the final result still adds unnecessary bulk while withholding the outcome the orchestrator actually requires.C. Correct. Keeping the detailed intermediate reasoning local to the sub-agent and passing only a compact summary of the final outcome gives the orchestrator exactly what it needs to continue planning while removing the bulk of the token volume from its context.D. Incorrect. Deleting the intermediate notes entirely removes any ability to audit or debug the sub-agent\'s process later, which is unnecessary risk when the notes could simply be kept local rather than discarded.E. Incorrect. Omitting the final result while keeping the verbose intermediate reasoning defeats the purpose — the orchestrator still receives the bulky content and loses the one piece of information it actually needs."},{"id":"34","question":"A team nests four layers of \\"supervisor of supervisors,\\" where a top-level supervisor delegates to mid-level supervisors, which delegate to yet more supervisors, before finally reaching an agent that performs the actual work. Each layer summarizes the user\'s request and the sub-supervisor\'s response before passing it along. The team finds that by the time a request reaches the working agent, key constraints from the original user request have been dropped, and response time is dominated by the delegation chain rather than actual task execution. What should the team do to fix the underlying boundary placement issue?","options":{"A":"Move all Genie spaces into the top-level supervisor to reduce the need for delegation","B":"Reduce the number of supervisor layers and delegation hops between the user and the working agent","C":"Add additional Unity Catalog functions to the working agent to expand its capabilities","D":"Increase the size of each sub-agent\'s context window to prevent summarization loss"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Consolidating Genie spaces into the top-level supervisor does not by itself reduce the number of delegation layers between the user and the working agent where the constraint loss is occurring.B. Correct. Four layers of nested supervisors mean the original request is summarized and re-summarized repeatedly before reaching the working agent — excessive handoff compression overhead. Flattening the hierarchy to fewer delegation hops reduces the number of lossy summarization points.C. Incorrect. Expanding the working agent\'s tool set does nothing to address constraints being dropped during the multi-layer delegation chain; the loss happens in the handoffs, not in the working agent\'s capabilities.D. Incorrect. The problem described is detail lost during repeated summarization across supervisor layers, not any single agent running out of context space, so enlarging context windows would not fix the root cause."},{"id":"35","question":"In a multi-agent system, a Genie agent answers using table metadata curated six months ago, while a newer function-calling agent uses a vector index updated last week. A user asks whether a recently changed policy applies, and the two agents give different answers, with neither agent aware that the other is drawing on knowledge of a different age. What does this scenario primarily illustrate?","options":{"A":"An incorrectly configured SQL warehouse for the Genie agent causing it to query outdated metadata, producing an answer that contradicts the other agent.","B":"A chunking strategy that was too aggressive for the vector index, causing retrieval of irrelevant fragments and producing an outdated answer.","C":"A shared-context gap between agents about the currency of their knowledge sources produces inconsistent answers to the same question.","D":"A Unity Catalog permission conflict causes each agent to retrieve the policy from a different data snapshot, producing inconsistent answers."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect. An incorrectly configured SQL warehouse would typically cause query errors or wrong results, but the described disagreement stems from differing knowledge currency between agents, not a configuration error in one agent\'s execution environment.B. Incorrect. Aggressive chunking affects retrieval quality within a single vector index, not the cross-agent unawareness of different knowledge ages. The problem here is that neither agent knows the other is using differently aged sources.C. Correct. Neither agent is aware that the other is using knowledge of a different age, so their outputs diverge; this mismatch in shared understanding of context freshness is a core shared-context failure.D. Incorrect. The scenario does not describe any permission conflict; both agents can access their sources, but the disagreement arises because they are unaware of each other\'s knowledge recency, not because Unity Catalog permissions gave them different snapshots."}]},{"id":"databricks-data-analyst-associate","title":"Databricks Certified Data Analyst Associate","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"A team building an AI agent wants the agent to call out to external tools and APIs through a standardized interface, and finds a relevant offering while browsing Databricks Marketplace. Which listing category would expose this kind of offering?","options":{"A":"Notebooks","B":"MCP Servers","C":"Datasets","D":"AI Models"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Notebooks listings expose reference code for a use case, not a running interface an agent can call at runtime.B. MCP Servers listings expose Model Context Protocol servers, which provide the tools and APIs an agent calls to interact with external systems, matching the team\'s need.C. Datasets listings expose tabular or volume-based data for querying, not a standardized interface for an agent to call external tools.D. AI Models listings expose trained models for inference, not the tool-calling interface an agent uses to reach external systems."},{"id":"2","question":"A workspace admin needs a table\'s data files to live in the standard managed storage path that Unity Catalog controls for its schema, rather than at a location the team chooses. Which table-creation approach achieves this?","options":{"A":"Run `CREATE TABLE catalog.schema.table (...)` without a `LOCATION` clause, letting Unity Catalog place the files under the schema\'s own managed storage path.","B":"Run `CREATE TABLE catalog.schema.table (...) LOCATION \'s3://team-bucket/table/\'`, pointing Unity Catalog at a bucket the team itself provisions and controls.","C":"Create the table first as external with an explicit `LOCATION`, then run `ALTER TABLE ... SET MANAGED` to move it under Unity Catalog\'s control.","D":"Upload the files directly into any cloud storage bucket and register them afterward with `MSCK REPAIR TABLE` so Unity Catalog adopts them as managed."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct. Omitting `LOCATION` produces a managed table, and Unity Catalog automatically stores its data under the managed storage path configured for the containing schema, catalog, or metastore.B. Incorrect. Supplying an explicit `LOCATION` at creation makes the table external, with Unity Catalog governing only metadata over a team-controlled bucket rather than its own managed path.C. Incorrect. There is no `ALTER TABLE ... SET MANAGED` conversion path; a table\'s managed-versus-external type is fixed at creation and is not changed by altering it afterward.D. Incorrect. `MSCK REPAIR TABLE` is a Hive-style partition-discovery command for existing metastore tables, not a mechanism for adopting arbitrary uploaded files as a Unity Catalog managed table."},{"id":"3","question":"A team has built an ingestion step that lands raw files, a declarative pipeline that cleans and aggregates the data, and a notebook that retrains a forecasting model, and they need all three steps to run in the correct order every night with alerting on failure. Which component should coordinate this end-to-end workflow?","options":{"A":"Unity Catalog, which stores governance metadata for each asset the workflow touches but has no mechanism to schedule tasks or track execution order between them.","B":"Databricks SQL, which lets analysts query the outputs of a finished workflow from a warehouse but does not schedule or sequence the upstream tasks themselves.","C":"Delta Lake, which records every table write in a transaction log for auditability but has no concept of a task, a schedule, or a dependency graph at all.","D":"Lakeflow Jobs, which orchestrates dependent tasks across ingestion, pipelines, notebooks, and ML steps on a schedule, with retries and failure alerts built in."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Governance metadata and lineage tracking describe a cataloging function, not a scheduler, so this component cannot sequence the ingestion, pipeline, and notebook steps the scenario describes.B. A SQL analytics surface for querying finished results is a downstream consumer of the workflow\'s output, not the mechanism that schedules or sequences the upstream tasks that produce it.C. A storage format\'s transaction log records what was written and when, but it has no scheduler or dependency graph, so it cannot coordinate the ordering of separate ingestion, pipeline, and notebook tasks.D. Coordinating multiple heterogeneous steps in a fixed order, with retries and alerting, is precisely the orchestration role that this component fills across ingestion, pipelines, notebooks, and model-training tasks."},{"id":"4","question":"A data analyst at a retail company wants to search Catalog Explorer for only the tables that have been certified as trustworthy for analysis, without manually checking each table\'s status. Which search approach lets them filter the results to just certified assets?","options":{"A":"Sort the schema\'s table list by the \\"Created\\" column and manually review the top entries, since certified tables are always created first.","B":"Open each table\'s Permissions tab and look for a `CERTIFIED` grant, since certification is stored as an access-control privilege.","C":"Enter `certificationStatus:certified` in the Catalog Explorer search box, which filters results to assets tagged with the certified system tag.","D":"Enter `tag:certified` in the Catalog Explorer search box, which filters results to any table carrying a user-defined tag with that exact value."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Creation date has no relationship to certification status, since tables can be certified at any point after creation, including years later. Sorting by creation date would not surface certified assets reliably.B. Certification status is not stored as a permissions grant, so there is no `CERTIFIED` entry to find on a Permissions tab. Access control and certification are separate, independently managed concepts in Unity Catalog.C. The `certificationStatus:certified` search keyword filters Catalog Explorer results to assets carrying the system certification tag with a certified value. This is the built-in mechanism for discovering only trustworthy, vetted datasets without opening each asset individually.D. There is no `tag:certified` search syntax in Catalog Explorer, and certification is not implemented as an arbitrary user-defined tag value. Using this keyword would not reliably match certified assets."},{"id":"5","question":"A data engineer runs a query that reads a table by referencing its underlying cloud storage path, such as `s3://bucket/table-path`, rather than its `catalog.schema.table` name, then writes the transformed columns into a new Unity Catalog table. When they open the Lineage tab for the new table, what do they observe about column-level lineage?","options":{"A":"Column-level lineage appears, but only for numeric columns, since Unity Catalog can trace storage-path-based reads solely for columns that hold a primitive numeric data type.","B":"Column-level lineage for the new table\'s columns is missing, because Unity Catalog cannot capture column lineage when a source is referenced by path rather than by table name.","C":"Column-level lineage appears normally, because Unity Catalog resolves storage paths back to their registered table names automatically before recording any lineage relationships.","D":"Table-level lineage is also missing in this case, since referencing data by storage path prevents Unity Catalog from recording any lineage graph at all."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because the path-based limitation is not restricted by data type; no column, numeric or otherwise, gets column-level lineage when the source is a storage path.B. This is correct because column-level lineage cannot be captured when a source is referenced by file path rather than by table name, which is a documented limitation distinct from table-level tracking.C. This is incorrect because Unity Catalog does not silently resolve arbitrary storage paths back to registered tables for lineage purposes; the path reference itself breaks column tracing.D. This is incorrect because the limitation applies specifically to column-level detail; table-level lineage can still be recorded even when a source is referenced by storage path."},{"id":"6","question":"In Databricks SQL, what happens when `DELETE FROM catalog.schema.events` is executed with no `WHERE` clause?","options":{"A":"Every row in the table is deleted, since an omitted predicate is treated as matching all rows","B":"The statement fails validation because Delta Lake requires an explicit predicate on every DELETE","C":"Only rows containing at least one NULL column value get deleted, since that is the default target","D":"The statement runs but silently deletes zero rows until an explicit WHERE clause is added later"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Databricks documentation states that when no predicate is provided, DELETE FROM deletes all rows in the table, so omitting WHERE clears the entire table rather than doing nothing or requiring extra syntax.B. Delta Lake does not enforce a mandatory WHERE predicate on DELETE statements; the syntax explicitly allows an optional predicate, so no validation error is raised.C. DELETE FROM has no special default behavior that targets only NULL-containing rows; without a predicate it applies to the entire table regardless of which columns hold NULLs.D. The statement executes immediately rather than staying inert, and its effect is to remove all rows, not to silently do nothing until a condition is supplied."},{"id":"7","question":"While working inside a Databricks notebook, an analyst has a small reference CSV on their laptop that they want to add to an existing Unity Catalog volume without leaving the notebook to open Catalog Explorer. Which built-in option lets them do this directly from the notebook interface?","options":{"A":"The notebook\'s `Run` menu exposes a `Sync local file` command that mounts the laptop\'s file system to DBFS and copies files whenever the notebook cell executes.","B":"Typing `%upload` as a magic command in a notebook cell opens a native file picker that pushes the selected file straight into the attached cluster\'s driver storage.","C":"The `Data` tab inside the notebook results pane lets analysts paste a local file path, which Databricks then fetches automatically from the analyst\'s machine over SSH.","D":"The notebook\'s `File` menu offers `Upload files to volume`, which opens the same upload dialog used elsewhere and writes the selected file into the chosen volume path."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. There is no `Run` menu command that mounts a laptop\'s local file system to DBFS; DBFS mounts are configured against cloud object storage, not a local machine, and files uploaded through the UI land in Unity Catalog volumes rather than DBFS.B. Databricks notebooks do not support a `%upload` magic command, and uploaded files are staged into Unity Catalog volumes rather than pushed into a cluster\'s ephemeral driver storage.C. The notebook results pane has no file-path field that fetches files from a local machine over SSH; Databricks has no mechanism to reach into an analyst\'s laptop this way.D. The notebook\'s `File` menu includes an `Upload files to volume` action that opens the standard upload dialog, letting the analyst stage the local CSV into a Unity Catalog volume without switching to Catalog Explorer."},{"id":"8","question":"During a company-wide reporting event, dozens of analysts simultaneously open the same dashboard connected to a single SQL warehouse, and queries begin queuing noticeably. The warehouse is already set to the largest available cluster size. What change would most directly relieve this queuing?","options":{"A":"Switch the warehouse to the Classic type, since Classic warehouses are marketed as being optimized specifically for high concurrent user counts","B":"Disable Photon on the warehouse so that more compute resources are freed up for handling the additional incoming concurrent connections","C":"Increase the maximum cluster count so the warehouse can spin up additional clusters and distribute the concurrent queries across them","D":"Increase the cluster size further, since a bigger single cluster processes a larger volume of simultaneous queries in parallel more efficiently"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Classic warehouses are not specifically optimized for concurrency; they offer entry-level performance without the intelligent workload management that helps distribute concurrent load, so switching to Classic would not resolve this queuing.B. Photon is a vectorized query engine that speeds up query execution rather than a resource competing with concurrency, so disabling it would not free capacity for more simultaneous queries and could slow individual queries down.C. Raising the maximum cluster count is correct because SQL warehouses handle high concurrency by adding clusters that share the incoming query load, rather than by making one cluster larger; more clusters means more queries can run in parallel instead of queuing.D. Cluster size (t-shirt size) mainly affects how much compute a single query gets to run faster, not how many separate queries can run at once, so growing it further does little to relieve queuing from many simultaneous users."},{"id":"9","question":"Which statement correctly differentiates a materialized view from a plain (dynamic) view in Databricks SQL?","options":{"A":"A materialized view can only be queried from within its originating pipeline, while a plain view is queryable from anywhere in Unity Catalog.","B":"A materialized view persists and caches its query results for reuse, while a plain view is evaluated on demand and recomputed on every query.","C":"A materialized view is evaluated on demand for every query, while a plain view persists and caches its results between refresh cycles.","D":"A materialized view and a plain view both persist their results to storage, differing only in which compute engine executes the query."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Standalone materialized views can be queried broadly through Unity Catalog like other tables, and pipeline scoping applies to plain views defined inside a Lakeflow pipeline, not to materialized views generally.B. A materialized view caches its computed output so downstream reads reuse precomputed data, whereas a plain view has no persisted state and recomputes its query logic every time it is read.C. This reverses the actual behavior: a materialized view is the one that caches and persists results, while a plain view is the one recomputed on demand for each query.D. A plain view does not persist any results to storage at all, so claiming both object types persist their output misstates how plain views work."},{"id":"10","question":"A workspace admin is evaluating whether to enable Genie Code for analysts who only have `SELECT` privileges on a subset of Unity Catalog tables. They are concerned the assistant might surface data from tables or columns those analysts are not authorized to query. What should the admin expect regarding Genie Code\'s access to workspace data?","options":{"A":"Genie Code runs under a shared service identity with elevated privileges, so it can read content from any table regardless of the requesting user\'s grants.","B":"Genie Code caches a full copy of the metastore\'s schema and sample rows once, then answers every later request from that cache instead of live permissions.","C":"Genie Code requires the workspace admin to manually allowlist each table before any analyst can reference it in a Genie Code conversation with it.","D":"Genie Code is governed by the requesting user\'s Unity Catalog permissions, so it can only access data and operations that user already has grants for."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Genie Code does not bypass the requesting analyst\'s grants through a privileged shared identity; its access is scoped to what that specific user can already see.B. Genie Code does not operate from a static cached snapshot of the metastore that ignores live permission checks; access is scoped per request to what the user can see.C. There is no manual per-table allowlisting step required from the admin; access is governed automatically by the existing Unity Catalog grants for the requesting user.D. Genie Code operates within the requesting user\'s own Unity Catalog permissions, so it can only access and surface data and operations that user is already entitled to, which directly addresses the admin\'s governance concern."},{"id":"11","question":"A table `shipments` records `order_id` and `warehouse_region`, and a table `pricing` records `order_id`, `warehouse_region`, and `unit_cost`. Some order_id values repeat across regions with different costs, so joining on `order_id` alone produces incorrect cross-region matches. Which join clause correctly returns only the pricing row that matches both the order and its specific region?","options":{"A":"`ON shipments.order_id = pricing.order_id AND shipments.warehouse_region = pricing.warehouse_region`, matching on both columns together","B":"`ON shipments.order_id = pricing.order_id`, followed by a separate WHERE clause comparing warehouse_region after the join runs","C":"`USING (order_id)`, since USING automatically expands to include every other column shared by name between the two tables","D":"`ON shipments.order_id = pricing.order_id OR shipments.warehouse_region = pricing.warehouse_region`, matching on either column being equal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Combining both columns with AND in the ON clause requires each joined row to match on order_id and warehouse_region simultaneously, which is exactly what is needed to avoid pairing an order with the wrong region\'s cost.B. Filtering with a separate WHERE clause after an order_id-only join can work logically similarly to an AND condition for an inner join, but it still first materializes the incorrect cross-region matches and is not what the ON clause itself was asked to express; the join predicate should carry the multi-key logic directly.C. USING(order_id) only deduplicates and matches the single named column order_id; it does not automatically pull in warehouse_region as part of the match condition, so cross-region mismatches would still occur.D. An OR condition matches rows where either column is equal, which is looser than required and would still produce incorrect matches whenever order_id matches but the region differs."},{"id":"12","question":"An analyst runs a query with `SELECT product_id, category, price FROM catalog ORDER BY 2, 3 DESC;`. What result ordering does this produce?","options":{"A":"Rows sort ascending by product_id only, because positional references are ignored and ORDER BY silently falls back to using the first selected column","B":"Rows sort ascending by category first, then within each category tie sort descending by price, since ORDER BY accepts SELECT-list positions per key","C":"The query fails to parse, because ORDER BY only accepts column names or aliases and rejects positional integers that reference the SELECT list","D":"Rows sort descending by both category and price together, because the trailing DESC keyword applies retroactively to every positional reference before it"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Incorrect: positional references are fully honored by ORDER BY rather than ignored, and product_id is never used as a sort key in this query since it was not referenced by position or name.B. Correct: ORDER BY supports referencing SELECT-list columns by their integer position, so 2 refers to category and 3 refers to price; each key sorts independently unless a shared direction is stated, so category sorts ascending by default while price sorts descending as explicitly marked.C. Incorrect: Databricks SQL explicitly supports ordering by column position as an alternative to naming the column or alias directly, so this query parses and runs successfully.D. Incorrect: a DESC keyword only applies to the sort key it immediately follows; it does not retroactively apply to earlier keys in the same ORDER BY list, so category still sorts ascending here."},{"id":"13","question":"A data analyst needs to build a report showing exactly what changed in the `pricing` table over the past week: which rows were updated, when each change happened, and which user or job made each write. Which command should they run first to identify the relevant commits before writing any time travel queries?","options":{"A":"`SELECT * FROM pricing VERSION AS OF 1;` to read the earliest version and manually compare it row-by-row against the current table","B":"`EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM pricing;` to view the query plan, which lists every prior write operation applied to the table\'s files","C":"`DESCRIBE HISTORY pricing;` to list each commit\'s version, timestamp, operation type, and the user or job that performed it","D":"`SHOW TBLPROPERTIES pricing;` to list the table\'s configured retention settings and infer how many changes occurred from them"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect: reading only the earliest version and manually diffing every row against the current table is a slow, error-prone substitute for the operation log and would not surface the acting user or job for each change.B. Incorrect: `EXPLAIN` shows the physical or logical execution plan for the current query, not a historical record of past write operations performed on the table.C. Correct: `DESCRIBE HISTORY` returns the audit trail of a Delta table\'s operations, including version, timestamp, operation type, and the user or job identity, making it the direct way to identify which commits to inspect further.D. Incorrect: `SHOW TBLPROPERTIES` returns configuration settings such as retention durations, not a log of individual write events, so it cannot reveal who changed what or when."},{"id":"14","question":"While building a unified sales table that joins sources from two business units, an analyst must reference an existing Delta table by its fully qualified name inside the `CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT` query. Which reference correctly follows Unity Catalog\'s namespace convention?","options":{"A":"`sales_schema.orders_delta`, because Unity Catalog only requires the schema and table name once a default catalog has been set for the current session.","B":"`finance_catalog.orders_delta`, because Unity Catalog flattens the schema and table names into one combined identifier whenever a table was originally created without a schema name.","C":"`orders_delta@finance_catalog`, because Unity Catalog uses an at-sign separator to bind a table name to its owning catalog for cross-catalog joins.","D":"`finance_catalog.sales_schema.orders_delta`, because Unity Catalog resolves objects through a three-level catalog, schema, and table hierarchy rather than a single database name."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Omitting the catalog only works when a default catalog has already been set for the session, so it is not the reliable, fully qualified reference the scenario calls for when joining across business units.B. Every table in Unity Catalog belongs to a schema, so there is no mode where the schema and table collapse into a single flattened identifier under the catalog.C. Unity Catalog identifiers use dot separators between catalog, schema, and table, not an at-sign, so this syntax does not match how object names are resolved.D. Unity Catalog always resolves objects through the three-level `catalog.schema.table` hierarchy, so naming all three levels explicitly is the fully qualified reference the join requires."},{"id":"15","question":"A team has already created a connection named `pg_inventory` for a PostgreSQL server. Which statement correctly registers a queryable Unity Catalog catalog on top of that connection, scoped to the `retail_db` database?","options":{"A":"`CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG pg_catalog USING CONNECTION pg_inventory OPTIONS (database \'retail_db\')`, which mirrors the remote database\'s schemas and tables.","B":"`CREATE CATALOG pg_catalog USING CONNECTION pg_inventory OPTIONS (database \'retail_db\')`, which provisions a managed catalog backed by cloud storage that syncs from the connection.","C":"`CREATE FOREIGN SCHEMA pg_catalog FROM CONNECTION pg_inventory OPTIONS (database \'retail_db\')`, which creates a single foreign schema rather than a full catalog.","D":"`ALTER CONNECTION pg_inventory SET CATALOG pg_catalog OPTIONS (database \'retail_db\')`, which attaches a catalog reference directly onto the existing connection object."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. `CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG` is the correct statement for federation; it takes an existing connection and a remote database name, then mirrors that database\'s schemas and tables as a foreign catalog in Unity Catalog.B. Plain `CREATE CATALOG` provisions a managed catalog backed by Databricks-managed storage, not a mirror of a remote federated database, so it is the wrong statement for this scenario.C. There is no `CREATE FOREIGN SCHEMA ... FROM CONNECTION` statement in Lakehouse Federation; foreign catalogs are the unit created on top of a connection, and schemas underneath sync automatically.D. `ALTER CONNECTION` modifies properties of an existing connection object; it cannot be used to create or attach a new foreign catalog, which requires its own `CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG` statement."},{"id":"16","question":"A pipeline writes newly ingested batch data into a Delta Lake table every hour, and the team wants to reduce the time spent producing the output files. Which Photon capability directly speeds up this part of the workload?","options":{"A":"Photon speeds up writes by automatically converting the target table to Z-ordered layout on every write, regardless of whether Z-ordering columns were configured.","B":"Photon accelerates the Parquet file writes underlying Delta Lake table writes, reducing the time spent encoding and persisting the ingested batch data to storage.","C":"Photon compresses the ingested data using a proprietary binary format instead of Parquet, which is why Delta Lake tables written under Photon cannot be read by non-Photon engines.","D":"Photon reduces write time by skipping Delta Lake\'s transaction log commit for batch writes, deferring log updates until the next scheduled OPTIMIZE job runs."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because Photon does not automatically apply Z-ordering on writes; Z-ordering is a separate, explicitly configured optimization technique, not a side effect of Photon\'s write acceleration.B. This is correct: Photon accelerates the underlying Parquet write path used by Delta Lake (and Iceberg), so encoding and persisting the batch output to storage completes faster.C. This is incorrect because Photon still writes standard Parquet files under Delta Lake\'s open format; it does not introduce a proprietary binary layout, and tables remain readable by any Delta-compatible engine.D. This is incorrect because Photon does not alter Delta Lake\'s transactional guarantees; every write still commits to the transaction log as normal, and log updates are not deferred to a later OPTIMIZE run."},{"id":"17","question":"A support analyst is validating a nightly job by checking whether the previous night\'s run of `DESCRIBE HISTORY billing` recorded a `DELETE` operation that should not have happened. The table has thousands of commits. Which addition to the query most directly limits the output to just the latest commit for a quick check?","options":{"A":"Append `ORDER BY version ASC LIMIT 1` so the very first commit ever made to the table is returned for comparison","B":"Append `GROUP BY operation` to collapse the results into one row per operation type before inspecting them","C":"Append `LIMIT 1` to the statement so only the single most recent commit, which is listed first, is returned","D":"Append `WHERE operation = \'DELETE\'` and no limit, returning every delete ever recorded across the table\'s full history"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect: sorting ascending by version and limiting to one row returns the oldest commit in the table\'s history, the opposite of the most recent commit the analyst needs to inspect.B. Incorrect: grouping by operation type collapses rows across the entire history into aggregated buckets, losing the per-commit timestamp and version detail needed to confirm what happened in last night\'s specific run.C. Correct: `DESCRIBE HISTORY` returns commits in reverse chronological order, so appending `LIMIT 1` isolates just the most recent commit, which is the fastest way to check what last night\'s run did.D. Incorrect: filtering on `operation = \'DELETE\'` with no limit returns every delete across the table\'s entire lifetime, which is far more output than needed for a quick check of just the latest run."},{"id":"18","question":"A team maintains a Delta table that was set up years ago with Hive-style partitioning by `order_date` and periodic `OPTIMIZE ... ZORDER BY (customer_id)` jobs. They want to migrate it to liquid clustering to simplify maintenance and allow the clustering key to evolve later. Which statement about this migration is correct?","options":{"A":"Liquid clustering can be layered on top of the existing partitioning so both the partition columns and clustering keys apply simultaneously","B":"The existing `OPTIMIZE ... ZORDER BY` jobs can keep running unchanged alongside a new `CLUSTER BY` declaration on the same columns","C":"Migrating to liquid clustering requires first removing all historical data files so the table starts from an empty state","D":"The existing partitioning must be removed as part of the migration, since liquid clustering cannot be combined with partitioning or Z-order on the same table"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Delta Lake does not support running partitioning and liquid clustering on the same table at once, so the two layout strategies cannot be layered together rather than one replacing the other.B. Once a table is declared with `CLUSTER BY`, Z-order compaction is no longer the applicable maintenance operation, so continuing to run `ZORDER BY` jobs would conflict with rather than complement the new clustering configuration.C. Migrating to liquid clustering does not require deleting historical files; existing data can remain in place and be reorganized later through an `OPTIMIZE FULL` operation rather than starting from an empty table.D. Liquid clustering is documented as incompatible with both Hive-style partitioning and Z-ordering, so migrating means the team drops the partitioning scheme and stops the Z-order jobs in favor of a `CLUSTER BY` declaration on the columns that matter for their queries."},{"id":"19","question":"A team runs the same reporting query each morning against a serverless SQL warehouse that Databricks automatically stops overnight due to inactivity. The underlying table has not changed since the previous run. When the team runs the identical query again after the warehouse restarts, the result appears almost instantly with no new compute activity. What explains this behavior?","options":{"A":"The remote result cache is stored outside the cluster and persists across a warehouse restart, so the identical query reuses the earlier computed result.","B":"The disk cache retained the raw data files from the prior day\'s run in the cluster\'s local memory, letting the warehouse skip reading from cloud storage entirely after the restart.","C":"Databricks automatically materializes any query run in the SQL editor as a view, so subsequent identical queries read from that view instead of the original source table.","D":"The SQL warehouse never actually stopped overnight; Databricks only pauses billing for idle serverless warehouses while quietly keeping the underlying compute cluster running."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. The remote result cache is a workspace-level store separate from any single cluster, has a lifecycle of up to 24 hours, and survives a warehouse being stopped and restarted, so an unchanged identical query returns the cached result immediately.B. The disk cache lives on local SSD attached to compute nodes and is cleared when a cluster or warehouse restarts, so it could not have survived the overnight stop to serve this result.C. Running a query in the SQL editor does not create a materialized view of the result; no persistent view object is created just by executing a SELECT statement.D. A stopped serverless warehouse is not still running underneath the billing layer; the near-instant response comes from the persistent remote result cache, not from hidden active compute."},{"id":"20","question":"A report combining two monthly sales extracts uses `UNION ALL` to stack the results, and the analyst notices the row count exactly matches the sum of both extracts\' row counts, including a handful of exact duplicate rows that exist in both extracts. Which statement correctly explains this behavior and how to change it if duplicates should be removed?","options":{"A":"`UNION ALL` keeps every row from both inputs without checking for duplicates, so the matching row count is expected; switching to plain `UNION` removes exact duplicate rows across the combined result.","B":"`UNION ALL` always removes duplicate rows automatically, so any duplicates surviving in the output indicate a bug in Databricks SQL that must be worked around with a separate `DISTINCT` clause.","C":"The row count only matches by coincidence, since `UNION ALL` internally deduplicates based on the primary key column before it re-adds a synthetic row for every duplicate it originally removed.","D":"`UNION ALL` compares every column across both inputs and drops rows only when all columns tie exactly, so a full duplicate removal step happens automatically without needing plain `UNION`."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. This is correct: `UNION ALL` concatenates all rows from both queries with no deduplication step at all, which is exactly why the combined row count equals the sum of the two extracts; switching to `UNION` performs a distinct-style removal of exact duplicate rows.B. This reverses the actual behavior: `UNION ALL` is specifically the variant that skips deduplication, while plain `UNION` is the one that removes duplicate rows, so no bug or workaround is involved here.C. `UNION ALL` performs no internal deduplication logic based on keys or any other columns, so describing the matching row count as coincidental misrepresents how the operator is defined to behave.D. This describes the behavior of plain `UNION`, not `UNION ALL`; the whole reason the combined count matches the sum of the two extracts is that no such column-wise duplicate comparison occurs during the union."},{"id":"21","question":"An analyst wants to show what percentage of this month\'s total sales came from each of four product categories, as a single snapshot rather than a trend over time. Which visualization type is the most appropriate choice?","options":{"A":"A line chart, since it presents the change in one or more metrics as they move steadily across a sequence of time periods.","B":"An area chart, since it combines line and bar visualizations to show how groups\' values change over time.","C":"A cohort chart, since it groups users by shared characteristics and tracks their behavior across time periods.","D":"A pie chart, since it is designed to show proportionality between categories and is not intended for time-series data."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. A line chart is built to present how metrics change across time periods, which does not match a single-point snapshot of category proportions for one month.B. An area chart also emphasizes change over time by combining line and bar elements, so it does not suit a one-time proportional breakdown of categories.C. A cohort chart tracks retention or behavior for grouped users across time periods, which is unrelated to a single snapshot of sales share by product category.D. A pie chart shows proportionality between metrics as slices of a whole and is documented as not intended for time-series data, matching this single-snapshot category breakdown."},{"id":"22","question":"Which pair of visualization types is documented as limited to 64K rows or 10MB of underlying query data in Databricks?","options":{"A":"Bar and line visualizations","B":"Scatter and bubble visualizations","C":"Heatmap and table visualizations","D":"Counter and pivot visualizations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Bar and line visualizations are not the types documented with this particular row and size limit.B. Scatter and bubble visualizations are not the types documented with this particular row and size limit.C. Heatmap and table visualizations are the two types documented with the 64K row or 10MB underlying-data limit, since both render every individual data point or cell directly.D. Counter and pivot visualizations are not the types called out with this specific row and size limit in the documentation."},{"id":"23","question":"A dashboard owner copies a shareable link from the Share dialog and emails it to a recipient who has no workspace access and has never opened this dashboard before. What happens when that recipient clicks the link?","options":{"A":"The recipient\'s browser silently provisions a brand-new workspace account in the background, since opening any shareable link always creates full workspace membership automatically.","B":"The recipient is prompted to authenticate through the identity provider or an email one-time passcode, then sees a dashboard-only, view-focused experience.","C":"The recipient sees the full dashboard immediately with no authentication step at all, since shareable links are designed to work for completely anonymous visitors.","D":"The recipient is shown a permanent access-denied page, because shareable links only ever work for users who already belong to the dashboard\'s home workspace."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Opening a shareable link authenticates the recipient as a registered account member for a view-only copy of the dashboard; it does not create or grant any workspace membership.B. Recipients without workspace access still need to authenticate, either through the account\'s identity provider or a one-time passcode sent by email, before landing in a simplified, dashboard-only view.C. Shareable links are not anonymous; a login step through the identity provider or a one-time passcode is required before the recipient can see the dashboard\'s contents.D. Shareable links are specifically designed to extend access to registered account members outside the workspace, so lacking workspace membership does not by itself result in denied access."},{"id":"24","question":"An alert\'s underlying query returns a result set sorted so that the most recent hourly `error_count` value appears in the first row, followed by older hourly values in subsequent rows. The analyst wants the alert to evaluate only that most recent hour\'s count, ignoring the rest of the rows. Which condition setting achieves this?","options":{"A":"Set the alert\'s Data Value option to evaluate the first value of the `error_count` column, so only the top row of the sorted result set is compared against the threshold.","B":"Leave the Data Value option on its aggregation default and add `LIMIT 1` to the notification template body, since template limits control which row the condition threshold reads.","C":"Set the Data Value option to Sum across all rows of `error_count`, since summing a sorted result set automatically weights the most recent row highest in the comparison.","D":"Set the Data Value option to Average across all rows of `error_count`, because averaging a time-ordered result naturally isolates the newest row\'s value from the older rows."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. The first-value setting evaluates only the top row of the query result, which is the most recent hour given the sort order, matching exactly what the team wants.B. The notification template only formats the text of an already-fired alert message; a `LIMIT 1` placed there has no effect on which row the condition itself compares.C. Summing across all rows combines every hour\'s error count into one total, which still mixes in older rows instead of isolating just the most recent hour\'s value.D. Averaging across all rows blends the newest count together with every older row\'s count, so it does not isolate the most recent hour on its own."},{"id":"25","question":"When configuring a dataset parameter in an AI/BI dashboard, which field in the parameter editor is the one that can only be changed by editing the actual `:name` marker text inside the SQL query itself?","options":{"A":"Display name","B":"Type","C":"Allow multiple selections","D":"Keyword"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. The display name is a label shown in filter editors and widgets and can be freely edited from the parameter configuration panel without touching the underlying query text.B. The parameter\'s type, such as String, Date, or Numeric, is a dropdown choice set in the configuration panel and does not require editing the SQL query.C. The multi-select checkbox toggling array-based filtering is set directly in the configuration panel and does not require any change to the query text.D. The keyword is the literal identifier that appears after the colon in the SQL query, so it can only be renamed by editing that query text; the parameter editor\'s gear-icon panel does not offer a separate control to rename it."},{"id":"26","question":"Which statement accurately describes a limitation of the pie visualization type in an AI/BI Dashboard widget?","options":{"A":"Pie visualizations require a numeric x-axis and y-axis pair, since slice angles are computed directly from two independent continuous variables.","B":"Pie visualizations can only be built from a Genie space dataset, since standard SQL editor query results are not supported as a data source.","C":"Pie visualizations show proportionality between metrics but are not meant for conveying time series data, since slices do not communicate change over time.","D":"Pie visualizations cannot display more than two categories at once, since each widget instance is limited to exactly two slices per rendered chart."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Pie visualizations encode proportion through slice size based on category values, not through a numeric x-axis and y-axis pair, so there is no such two-continuous-variable requirement.B. Pie visualizations, like other dashboard widgets, can be built from datasets defined in the SQL editor or notebooks, not only from Genie space datasets, so this restriction does not exist.C. Pie visualizations are documented as showing proportionality between metrics but not being meant for conveying time series data, because static slices cannot represent how a value changes across a continuous time axis.D. Pie visualizations are not restricted to two slices; they can represent several categories at once, though usability degrades as the number of slices grows, so this stated hard limit is incorrect."},{"id":"27","question":"Business users in a Genie space keep asking for the same customer churn calculation, and each time Genie generates a slightly different SQL formula, producing inconsistent numbers. Which Genie feature should the analyst configure to make the space consistently reuse one vetted, correct query for this calculation?","options":{"A":"A governed tag applied to the churn column, so Unity Catalog labels the column as sensitive and restricts who can query it directly.","B":"A trusted asset defined as an example SQL query for the churn calculation, so Genie reuses vetted logic instead of generating a new formula each time.","C":"A scheduled alert on the churn metric with an email destination, so a notification fires automatically whenever the calculated value crosses a defined threshold.","D":"A dashboard parameter bound to the churn metric, so users select a predefined value from a dropdown instead of typing a natural-language question."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. A governed tag classifies and can restrict access to a column, but it has no effect on which formula Genie chooses when generating SQL for a churn question.B. This is correct because a trusted asset, a vetted example SQL query, gives Genie one approved calculation to reference instead of re-deriving the formula for each question.C. A scheduled alert notifies users when a value crosses a threshold using whatever formula last computed it; it does not standardize or correct the SQL logic Genie uses to compute that value.D. Dashboard parameters belong to AI/BI dashboards, not Genie spaces, and they control filter values in a fixed visualization rather than standardizing a conversational calculation."},{"id":"28","question":"The BI team wants to embed a Genie space directly inside an internal customer-support console hosted at https://support.acme.internal, so agents can ask questions without leaving that tool. Before the Genie space author can generate a working embed, what must a workspace admin configure first?","options":{"A":"In Settings > Security > External access, set the embed dashboards policy to Allow approved domains and add the support console\'s domain to the approved list.","B":"In the Genie space\'s Share dialog, grant the support console\'s service account CAN MANAGE so the console can silently create new Genie spaces of its own on demand.","C":"In the Unity Catalog metastore, create a new catalog scoped to the support console and migrate the curated tables into that catalog before embedding.","D":"In the workspace admin console, enable Photon on the SQL warehouse backing the space so the embedded iframe can render query results faster."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct. Workspace admins control embedding policy under Settings > Security > External access, and an author cannot successfully embed a space in an external domain until that domain is allowed or explicitly approved there.B. CAN MANAGE on the space controls who can administer or share that specific Genie space, but it does not control which external domains are permitted to host an embedded iframe.C. Creating a new catalog and migrating tables changes data organization, not embedding permissions, and is unrelated to whether a domain is allowed to embed the space.D. Photon is a query execution engine setting that affects performance, not whether an external domain is authorized to embed a Genie space."},{"id":"29","question":"A user in a Genie space gets an answer to a sensitive budget question and is not confident the generated SQL is correct, but does not want to open a separate support ticket outside the tool. What should the user do?","options":{"A":"Submit a ticket through the organization\'s general IT help desk describing the question and the answer that seemed wrong.","B":"Give the response a thumbs down and wait for the next scheduled digest email that summarizes all negative feedback received.","C":"Edit the generated SQL directly inside the Genie space\'s chat window and rerun it manually to get a corrected result.","D":"Use the request-review option on that response so an administrator can inspect the prompt, the SQL, and an optional comment."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. A general IT ticket routes to a help desk that has no direct visibility into that Genie space\'s SQL or curated instructions, adding an unnecessary detour.B. A thumbs down records general sentiment but does not guarantee an administrator inspects this particular response, and there is no defined digest workflow tied to it.C. Editing SQL directly requires the user to already know the correct query, which defeats the purpose of asking Genie and does not flag anything for an administrator to fix.D. Requesting review on that exact response routes the specific prompt, SQL, and any comment straight to a space administrator for verification, which matches wanting an in-tool check without leaving the space."},{"id":"30","question":"An analyst tries to select a SQL warehouse while creating a Genie space, but the warehouse they need never appears in the picker. A workspace admin checks and finds the analyst has broad catalog access but nothing configured on the warehouse itself. What must be granted on the SQL warehouse for it to show up as selectable?","options":{"A":"CAN VIEW permission on the SQL warehouse, since read-only visibility into its configuration is enough for Genie to route queries through it","B":"CAN USE permission on the SQL warehouse, which is the minimum access level required before it can be attached to a Genie space","C":"CAN MANAGE permission on the SQL warehouse, since Genie spaces require full administrative control before a warehouse can be attached","D":"CAN RESTART permission on the SQL warehouse, since Genie spaces need to restart compute directly rather than just submit queries"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because read-only visibility does not let the warehouse actually execute queries submitted by the space; CAN USE, not CAN VIEW, is the required level.B. This is correct because CAN USE is documented as the minimum permission level a user needs on a SQL warehouse before that warehouse becomes available to attach to a Genie space.C. This is incorrect because Genie spaces do not require administrative control of the warehouse; the far lower CAN USE level is sufficient for attaching it.D. This is incorrect because attaching a warehouse to a Genie space only requires the ability to submit queries against it, not a separate restart-level permission."},{"id":"31","question":"A marketing team wants to associate promotions with the products they apply to, where a single promotion can cover many products and a single product can be featured in many promotions at once. They are building this relationship inside a data vault model that already has separate `product` and `promotion` hubs. What should they add to represent this association?","options":{"A":"A foreign key column added directly inside the promotion hub that points to the product hub\'s business key for every associated product.","B":"A star schema fact table joining the two hubs, since only fact tables are capable of representing many-to-many relationships between entities.","C":"A link table connecting the product and promotion hubs, capturing the many-to-many association between the two business concepts.","D":"A second satellite on the product hub, since satellites can store references to any number of related promotions for each product."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Hub tables are meant to hold only a business key and minimal metadata, and a single foreign key column could not represent a many-to-many relationship where either side can have multiple matches.B. Fact tables belong to star schema Gold-layer modeling, and many-to-many relationships are commonly represented there through bridge tables; a data vault model uses link tables instead, not fact tables, for this purpose.C. Link tables exist precisely to represent relationships, including many-to-many associations, between two or more hub entities, which matches the product-to-promotion relationship described here.D. Satellites hold descriptive attribute history for a hub or link, not references to other hubs; representing a many-to-many relationship through a satellite would not match its intended role."},{"id":"32","question":"A data engineer is loading raw clickstream JSON files from cloud object storage into a Databricks table with almost no transformation, keeping the original field names and adding only an ingestion timestamp column for auditability. No fact or dimension modeling has been applied yet. Which Medallion layer does this table represent, and why does industry-standard dimensional modeling not apply here yet?","options":{"A":"This is the Silver layer, because adding an ingestion timestamp already counts as the conformance step that normalizes raw data ahead of dimensional modeling in Gold.","B":"This is the Gold layer, because the ingestion timestamp column alone makes the raw table business-ready for dashboards, even though no fact or dimension structure has been applied to it yet.","C":"This is a data vault hub table, because the ingestion timestamp functions as the business key that other satellite tables will later reference for historical tracking.","D":"This is the Bronze layer, because its purpose is capturing source data as-is with minimal transformation, before any conforming or dimensional modeling happens."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Adding a single timestamp column is not the same as the deduplication and enterprise-wide conformance work that defines the Silver layer; the data described here is still raw and unconformed, which keeps it at Bronze.B. A raw, minimally transformed table with original field names is not business-ready and has no aggregate or dimensional structure, so it does not meet the definition of the Gold layer regardless of having a timestamp column.C. A hub table\'s business key identifies a specific business entity for satellites to attach history to; an ingestion timestamp added for auditing raw file loads is not a business key and does not make this a data vault hub.D. Keeping original field names with minimal transformation and only an audit timestamp is exactly the Bronze layer\'s role: capture data as-is for auditability and historical replay before any cleansing or dimensional modeling is applied downstream."},{"id":"33","question":"An analyst wants a list of every table defined in the `finance` catalog, across all of its schemas, without opening Catalog Explorer. Which query correctly returns that list using Unity Catalog\'s namespace structure?","options":{"A":"`SELECT table_schema, table_name FROM finance.information_schema.tables;`, since every catalog exposes its own `information_schema` schema listing the objects it contains.","B":"`SELECT table_schema, table_name FROM information_schema.tables;`, since `information_schema` is a single schema shared globally across every catalog in the metastore.","C":"`SHOW TABLES IN CATALOG finance;`, since the `SHOW TABLES` command accepts a catalog name directly and lists every table without needing to reference any schema.","D":"`DESCRIBE CATALOG finance;`, since the `DESCRIBE CATALOG` command returns the full contents of a catalog, including every schema, table, and volume it holds."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Each catalog in Unity Catalog has its own `information_schema` schema that surfaces metadata for the objects inside that specific catalog, so qualifying it as `finance.information_schema.tables` correctly scopes the metadata query to the `finance` catalog.B. This is incorrect because `information_schema` is not a single global schema shared across catalogs; without qualifying it with a catalog name, the reference resolves to whichever catalog is currently set as the session default, not necessarily `finance`.C. This is incorrect because `SHOW TABLES` requires a schema to be specified (or a current schema to be set); it does not accept a bare catalog name and list every table across all of that catalog\'s schemas in one call.D. This is incorrect because `DESCRIBE CATALOG` returns catalog-level metadata such as its owner and comment, not a full listing of every schema, table, and volume it contains."},{"id":"34","question":"A catalog owner grants `SELECT` on the entire `analytics` catalog to the `bi-team` group. Three weeks later, an engineer creates a brand-new schema and table inside that catalog without issuing any additional grants. Can the `bi-team` group query the new table, and why?","options":{"A":"Yes, but only after the catalog owner re-runs the same `GRANT SELECT` statement again, since inheritance refreshes just once per day on a scheduled background job.","B":"Yes, because a privilege granted on a container object like a catalog automatically applies to all current and future child schemas and tables created inside it.","C":"No, because `SELECT` granted at the catalog level only inherits down to schemas, not further down to the individual tables nested inside those schemas.","D":"No, because privilege grants in Unity Catalog only apply to objects that already existed at the moment the grant was issued, so the new table needs its own grant."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. There is no scheduled or periodic refresh required for privilege inheritance to take effect; Unity Catalog evaluates inherited privileges at query time, so re-running the grant statement is unnecessary.B. Unity Catalog privilege inheritance flows down from container objects to both current and future child objects, so a `SELECT` grant on the catalog automatically covers the newly created schema and table without any further action from the owner.C. Inheritance in Unity Catalog does not stop at the schema level; a privilege granted on a catalog continues cascading down through schemas to the tables they contain, so this describes an inheritance limit that does not exist.D. This describes the opposite of how Unity Catalog inheritance actually behaves; a grant on a catalog is not a one-time snapshot of existing objects and does apply to objects created afterward, so the new table would not need a separate grant."},{"id":"35","question":"By default in Unity Catalog, who owns a securable object such as a table immediately after it is created, and who owns the workspace catalog when Unity Catalog is automatically enabled for a workspace?","options":{"A":"Ownership defaults to whoever already owns the parent schema rather than the object\'s creator, and the workspace catalog has no owner until an admin manually assigns one later.","B":"New objects are owned collectively by the `account_admins` group by default, and the workspace catalog inherits its ownership from the metastore\'s root owner instead.","C":"The user who runs the `CREATE TABLE` statement owns the new object by default, and workspace administrators automatically own the workspace catalog created when Unity Catalog is auto-enabled.","D":"The metastore admin always owns every new object by default, and individual users must request an explicit ownership grant before they can modify anything they personally create in any schema."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. New objects are not owned by the parent schema\'s owner by default, and the workspace catalog is not left ownerless; every securable object, including the workspace catalog, must have an owner from creation.B. Ownership is not assigned to a group by default when an object is created, and the workspace catalog\'s owner comes from workspace administrator status, not from inheriting a metastore root owner.C. The creator of an object is its default owner in Unity Catalog, and when Unity Catalog is automatically enabled for a workspace, workspace administrators are set as owners of the resulting workspace catalog.D. Metastore admins do not automatically own every new object; ownership defaults to the creating user, and requiring a separate grant before that user can modify their own object is not how Unity Catalog behaves."}]},{"id":"databricks-data-engineer-associate","title":"Databricks Certified Data Engineer Associate","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"Several data scientists want to share one running cluster throughout the day so they can attach notebooks, run ad hoc exploratory queries, and install shared libraries without waiting for a new cluster to start each time. Which compute type fits this pattern?","options":{"A":"A serverless SQL warehouse, since it is designed for teams to attach notebooks directly and install custom Python libraries for exploration.","B":"A Classic SQL warehouse, since Classic warehouses allow notebook attachment and multi-user library installation during interactive sessions.","C":"An all-purpose cluster, since it stays available for multiple users to attach notebooks and share libraries across an interactive session.","D":"A job cluster, since Databricks provisions it fresh for each notebook attachment and automatically shares libraries across every user session."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. SQL warehouses are built to run SQL queries against Databricks SQL and do not support attaching general-purpose notebooks or installing arbitrary Python libraries for exploratory work. This makes them unsuitable for the described shared notebook workflow.B. Like other SQL warehouse tiers, Classic warehouses serve SQL queries rather than hosting attached notebooks or arbitrary library installs, so they do not match the described interactive multi-user notebook workflow. Notebook attachment is a cluster capability, not a SQL warehouse one.C. An all-purpose cluster is designed to remain running so multiple users can attach notebooks, share installed libraries, and run interactive exploratory queries without waiting for a fresh cluster to start. This directly matches the described collaborative workflow.D. A job cluster is created for a single scheduled job run and terminates when that run finishes, so it does not stay available for multiple data scientists to attach notebooks throughout the day. It is not intended for shared, ongoing interactive use."},{"id":"2","question":"A company runs three Databricks workspaces in the same AWS region for different business units and wants every workspace to enforce the same table permissions, lineage tracking, and audit logs without maintaining separate governance configuration in each workspace. What should they do?","options":{"A":"Create an identical set of catalogs and manually replicate every grant statement in each workspace\'s own separate metastore","B":"Configure a job cluster per workspace with matching cluster policies so that compute-level settings mirror the intended governance settings","C":"Enable a classic SQL warehouse in each workspace, since SQL warehouses independently enforce table-level access control","D":"Attach a single Unity Catalog metastore to all three workspaces so grants, lineage, and audit logs apply consistently across them"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Manually duplicating catalogs and grants across separate metastores per workspace creates ongoing maintenance overhead and drift risk, which is exactly what a shared metastore is meant to avoid.B. Cluster policies control compute configuration such as instance types and autoscaling limits; they do not enforce data governance features like table grants, lineage, or audit logging.C. SQL warehouses execute queries under whatever governance layer is attached to the workspace; they do not independently define or enforce catalog-level access control across workspaces.D. A single Unity Catalog metastore can be attached to multiple workspaces in the same region, so permissions, lineage, and audit logs defined once apply consistently across all attached workspaces."},{"id":"3","question":"A data engineer wants Auto Loader to process all currently available files in a source directory and then stop on its own, rather than running continuously, so the workload can be scheduled as a Lakeflow Job that runs once per hour. Which configuration achieves this batch-style behavior?","options":{"A":"Configure the `writeStream` with `Trigger.AvailableNow`, which processes all files available at the start of the run and then stops the stream automatically.","B":"Configure the `writeStream` with no trigger specified at all, which causes Auto Loader to process one file per run and then exit without a checkpoint.","C":"Configure the `readStream` to omit `cloudFiles.schemaLocation`, which forces Auto Loader to treat the workload as a one-time batch load by default.","D":"Configure the `writeStream` with `Trigger.Continuous`, which processes files as they arrive and stops automatically once the source directory is empty."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. `Trigger.AvailableNow` processes all files that are available when the job starts and then stops the stream once they are handled, which matches the hourly scheduled batch-style pattern the engineer wants.B. Leaving the trigger unspecified does not produce a one-file-and-exit batch behavior; the default streaming trigger processes micro-batches continuously rather than stopping after one file.C. Omitting the schema location does not turn a streaming job into a batch job; it only removes the location Auto Loader uses to persist and evolve the inferred schema, and is required for most formats to avoid errors.D. `Trigger.Continuous` is designed for low-latency continuous processing and does not stop automatically when a directory becomes empty, which is the opposite of the stop-on-its-own behavior needed here."},{"id":"4","question":"A source S3 bucket receives files from multiple upstream systems, and a data engineer wants a single `COPY INTO` run to ingest only the files under a `landing/2026/09/` prefix whose names end in `.csv`, ignoring `.json` and `.txt` files that land in the same prefix. Which clause should the engineer add to the statement?","options":{"A":"`FILEFORMAT = CSV` alone, since specifying the file format automatically restricts the command to loading only files with a matching extension.","B":"`PATTERN = \'landing/2026/09/*.csv\'` so the command matches only files under that prefix whose names end with the `.csv` extension using glob-style matching.","C":"`FILES = (\'landing/2026/09/*.csv\')` so the command treats the entire glob expression as a single explicit filename and attempts to load only that one literal path.","D":"`FORMAT_OPTIONS (\'extension\' = \'csv\')` so the command filters the format reader to only parse files that Spark identifies as CSV-formatted content."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. This is incorrect. `FILEFORMAT` tells `COPY INTO` which parser to use for the selected files; it does not filter the source listing by file extension, so non-CSV files would still be attempted.B. This is correct. `PATTERN` accepts glob-style expressions, so restricting it to `*.csv` under the target prefix filters the source listing down to only the matching CSV files.C. This is incorrect. `FILES` expects a list of explicit file names rather than a glob expression, and it cannot be combined with wildcard matching to select a subset of files by extension.D. This is incorrect. There is no `extension` key recognized under `FORMAT_OPTIONS`; format options configure how a given file format is parsed, not which files are selected for the load."},{"id":"5","question":"A team needs to ingest data from a niche internal REST API that is not among the enterprise applications supported by Lakeflow Connect\'s managed connectors, and they need control over pagination, authentication headers, and retry logic in the extraction code. Which approach is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"`COPY INTO`, which is designed to bulk-load files that are already staged in cloud object storage rather than call a live REST API endpoint directly.","B":"The Zerobus direct-write API, which is intended for individual applications writing records straight into Delta tables rather than polling an external REST API.","C":"A standard connector built as a custom Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipeline, trading some automation for the flexibility to code custom extraction logic.","D":"A managed connector, which offers a simple UI and prebuilt API integration for any REST source regardless of whether it is officially supported by Lakeflow Connect."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. This SQL command loads files that are already staged in cloud object storage and has no ability to call a live REST API directly to retrieve data from it.B. This direct-write API is designed for applications to push individual records into Delta tables themselves, not for Databricks to poll and paginate through an external REST API on a schedule.C. Building a standard connector as a custom pipeline gives the team direct control over pagination, authentication headers, and retry logic in the extraction code, which is the flexibility a niche unsupported source requires, at the cost of some of the automation a managed connector would otherwise provide.D. A managed connector only supports a specific, prebuilt catalog of enterprise sources, so an unsupported niche REST API would not have a managed connector available for it regardless of the UI\'s simplicity."},{"id":"6","question":"While planning a managed connector rollout for a Google Analytics source, an engineer discovers that the connector is listed as not yet available in their workspace\'s cloud region. What should the engineer do based on how Databricks documents managed connector availability?","options":{"A":"Assume this is a temporary outage affecting all regions equally and simply retry the connector setup again in a few minutes without further checks.","B":"Conclude that Google Analytics can never be ingested into Databricks and switch the entire ingestion strategy to a different analytics platform.","C":"Deploy the workspace\'s Unity Catalog metastore to a different region automatically, since metastore location is what determines connector availability.","D":"Check Databricks\' regional feature support documentation, since managed connectors are not universally available across every region and workspace."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Regional unavailability is a documented, standing limitation for some connectors, not a transient outage, so retrying without checking regional support docs is unlikely to resolve it.B. A regional limitation does not mean the source can never be ingested; the connector may be available in a supported region, or a standard connector approach may still work.C. Metastore location is a Unity Catalog governance concern and is not what Databricks documents as controlling managed connector regional availability.D. Databricks documents that managed connectors are not available in every region, so the correct next step is checking regional feature support documentation rather than assuming the feature is universally present."},{"id":"7","question":"A pipeline owner wants Auto Loader to never fail the stream when unexpected columns appear in incoming files, but also wants those unexpected values preserved rather than silently discarded. Which `cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode` setting satisfies both requirements?","options":{"A":"`rescue`, which prevents schema evolution and captures unexpected columns in the rescued data column instead of failing the stream.","B":"`none`, which ignores unexpected columns entirely and continues the stream without capturing their values anywhere.","C":"`addNewColumns`, which evolves the schema automatically but stops the stream once with an exception before resuming.","D":"`failOnNewColumns`, which halts the stream permanently until an updated schema is explicitly provided by the user."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. The `rescue` mode is built exactly for this case: it blocks automatic schema evolution and instead captures unexpected column values in the rescued data column without ever failing the stream.B. The `none` mode avoids failure but simply ignores unexpected columns, meaning their values are not preserved anywhere, which fails the second requirement.C. The `addNewColumns` mode does preserve new columns by evolving the schema, but it still interrupts the stream with an exception, which fails the no-failure requirement.D. The `failOnNewColumns` mode does the opposite of what is needed, halting the stream indefinitely until a person manually supplies an updated schema."},{"id":"8","question":"A data engineering team is designing an ingestion strategy for a new source system and is unsure whether to build a custom connector immediately. Following Databricks\' recommended approach to selecting an ingestion method, what should they do first?","options":{"A":"Start with Auto Loader for every source type, since it is described as the single default entry point for all ingestion needs.","B":"Start with the most managed layer available, such as a managed connector, and drop to less-managed layers only if requirements aren\'t met.","C":"Start by building a fully custom connector first, then evaluate whether a managed connector could have replaced it later.","D":"Start with a community connector regardless of source support, since community connectors are the most cost-effective default choice."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Auto Loader is a strong fit for cloud object storage ingestion specifically, not a universal default for every source type such as SaaS applications or databases.B. The recommended strategy is a layered approach: begin with the most managed option, such as a managed connector, and only move to standard, community, or custom options if that layer cannot meet the requirements.C. Building a custom connector before checking whether a managed or standard connector already covers the source inverts the recommended order and adds unnecessary engineering effort.D. Defaulting to a community connector regardless of whether a managed connector already supports the source skips the more automated, better-supported layer that should be tried first."},{"id":"9","question":"A JDBC read of a narrow, 10-column table with 5 million rows from a notebook is unexpectedly slow even though the source database responds quickly to test queries. Reviewing the Spark job, the JDBC data source is issuing tens of thousands of small round trips, each returning only a handful of rows. Which option should the engineer adjust first?","options":{"A":"Set `partitionColumn` to a column that is not indexed on the source database, which forces the database to return larger row batches per query instead of many small ones.","B":"Lower the cluster\'s `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` value, since shuffle partition count directly controls how many rows a JDBC connector requests from the source in each round trip.","C":"Increase the `fetchSize` option so each round trip to the source database returns a larger batch of rows, reducing the number of network round trips needed to pull the full result set.","D":"Increase `numPartitions` so more parallel JDBC connections open against the source, since adding connections reduces the number of rows returned per individual round trip automatically."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Choosing an unindexed `partitionColumn` affects how partition boundaries are computed and can hurt performance, but it has no direct effect on how many rows are returned per network round trip.B. `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` governs shuffle behavior for wide Spark operations like joins and aggregations after data is loaded; it has no effect on JDBC round-trip batching during the read itself.C. `fetchSize` directly controls how many rows the JDBC driver buffers per round trip, so raising it from a small default reduces the number of round trips needed and directly addresses the symptom described.D. Adding partitions increases the number of parallel connections used to split a scan by row range; it does not change the row batch size returned per round trip within any one connection."},{"id":"10","question":"A data engineer is building an Auto Loader stream that reads deeply nested JSON files. Downstream consumers require that a nested `user_info.dob` field always be materialized as a `DATE` column rather than whatever type Auto Loader would otherwise infer for it. Which mechanism lets the engineer force that specific nested field to a chosen type while still letting Auto Loader infer the rest of the schema automatically?","options":{"A":"A Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines expectation that rejects rows where the field is not a valid date","B":"A `COPY INTO` `FORCE` option applied directly to the destination Delta table definition","C":"A schema hint such as `\\"user_info.dob DATE\\"` passed as a reader option to the stream","D":"Casting the entire DataFrame after the stream is read, using a single `withColumn` call"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. A Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines expectation validates or drops rows based on a condition at write time; it enforces a rule about the data rather than overriding the inferred column type during Auto Loader\'s schema inference step.B. `COPY INTO` is a separate batch-loading SQL command with its own `FORCE` option for reloading files, and it does not apply to an Auto Loader streaming read or provide per-field type overrides.C. Schema hints let you override the inferred type of a specific column path, including nested fields addressed with dot notation, while Auto Loader continues to infer the remaining schema automatically.D. Post-read casting on the full DataFrame changes the value\'s type only after inference has already happened for every field, so it does not target a single nested field during schema inference itself."},{"id":"11","question":"A data engineer has an `active_customers` DataFrame with 6 columns and an `archived_customers` DataFrame with 5 columns, and attempts `active_customers.union(archived_customers)`. What is the result?","options":{"A":"Spark combines the rows successfully but leaves the sixth column entirely absent from the resulting DataFrame\'s schema","B":"Spark raises an `AnalysisException` because `union` requires both DataFrames to have the same number of columns","C":"Spark combines the rows successfully and fills the missing sixth column in `archived_customers` with null values","D":"Spark drops the extra column from `active_customers` automatically and unions the remaining five matching columns"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. The operation does not complete successfully with a mismatched column count, so there is no resulting DataFrame with a column silently removed from its schema.B. `union` requires both DataFrames to have the same number of columns with compatible types by position, so a 6-column DataFrame combined with a 5-column DataFrame raises an analysis error before any rows are combined.C. `union` does not perform schema reconciliation or fill in missing columns with null; a column count mismatch causes an error rather than an automatic null-filled merge.D. `union` does not silently drop columns to make schemas match; it strictly requires the same column count up front and fails when that is not the case."},{"id":"12","question":"After cleaning a bronze `events` table with PySpark, an engineer needs to persist the cleaned DataFrame as a new managed Unity Catalog silver table named `main.sales.events_silver`, replacing any existing data on each run. Which call accomplishes this?","options":{"A":"Write with `mode(\\"overwrite\\")` and `saveAsTable` to fully replace the managed silver table on every single run","B":"Write with `mode(\\"append\\")` and `saveAsTable` to add new rows onto whatever data already exists in that same table","C":"Call `createOrReplaceTempView` to register the cleaned data, which only lasts for the current Spark session","D":"Write the DataFrame out as CSV files to a path instead of registering it as a queryable table in the catalog"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. `saveAsTable` with `mode(\\"overwrite\\")` writes the DataFrame as a managed table under the given three-level name and replaces all existing rows on each run, matching the requirement to fully refresh the silver table.B. `append` mode adds the cleaned rows on top of whatever already exists in the table, which would duplicate previously written data instead of replacing it as the scenario requires.C. A temporary view only exists for the current Spark session and is never persisted as a table in Unity Catalog, so the cleaned data would disappear once the session ends.D. Writing CSV files to a local or object-store path creates plain files rather than a queryable Unity Catalog table, so it does not register `events_silver` as a table at all."},{"id":"13","question":"A data engineer builds `df.groupBy(\\"region\\", \\"product_category\\").agg(sum(\\"units_sold\\").alias(\\"total_units\\"))` on a sales DataFrame. What does the resulting DataFrame contain?","options":{"A":"One row per original input row, each annotated with the running total of `units_sold` accumulated up to that row","B":"One row per distinct `region` value only, with `product_category` and `units_sold` dropped from the output entirely","C":"A single row containing the grand total of `units_sold` across the whole DataFrame, ignoring both grouping columns","D":"One row per distinct combination of `region` and `product_category`, each with the summed `units_sold` for that combination"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. `groupBy().agg()` collapses rows within each group into a single summary row per group; it does not preserve one row per original input row or compute a running total across rows.B. Both `region` and `product_category` were passed to `groupBy`, so the result is grouped by their combination, not by `region` alone, and the aggregated `units_sold` column is retained in the output.C. Because two grouping columns were specified, the aggregation is computed separately for each combination of `region` and `product_category` rather than collapsing to one overall grand total.D. Correct — grouping by both `region` and `product_category` produces one output row per unique combination of those two columns, each carrying the sum of `units_sold` for that specific group."},{"id":"14","question":"A job aggregates a 2 TB dataset while `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` is left at its default of 200. The Spark UI shows each shuffle task processing several gigabytes of data, heavy spill to disk, and stage durations far longer than comparable jobs on smaller data. Which change should be applied and re-measured first?","options":{"A":"Increase `spark.default.parallelism`, since this setting overrides `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` for DataFrame and SQL aggregations.","B":"Increase `spark.driver.memory` so the driver process can hold the intermediate shuffle output in memory instead of letting it spill.","C":"Increase `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` so each shuffle partition holds a smaller amount of data and disk spill is reduced.","D":"Increase `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` so the 2 TB dataset is broadcast to every executor instead of being shuffled."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. `spark.default.parallelism` applies to RDD-based operations and does not override the SQL-specific shuffle partition setting for DataFrame or SQL aggregations. Raising it would not change the aggregation\'s shuffle partition count.B. Shuffle spill happens on the executors that hold the shuffle data, not on the driver, so increasing driver memory has no effect on executor-side spill during this aggregation.C. Raising the shuffle partition count spreads a fixed volume of data across more, smaller partitions, so each task handles less data and spills less to local disk. This directly targets the observed multi-gigabyte-per-task spill pattern.D. A 2 TB dataset is far larger than any practical broadcast threshold, and forcing a broadcast of that size would exhaust executor memory rather than fix the spill. The broadcast threshold governs join planning, not aggregation shuffle sizing."},{"id":"15","question":"A junior data engineer proposes defining the gold-layer `customer_lifetime_value` object as a streaming table, reasoning that customers table updates frequently and a streaming table processes new records efficiently. The transformation logic recalculates lifetime value by aggregating a customer\'s full historical order history and applying corrections whenever late-arriving refund records are back-dated into earlier months. Why is a streaming table a poor fit for this specific gold-layer object?","options":{"A":"A streaming table can only be defined using Python decorators and does not support SQL syntax at all, so the team would be forced to rewrite the entire lifetime-value aggregation logic in PySpark instead of SQL.","B":"A streaming table applies exactly-once, append-only processing to each record, so it cannot handle logic that must reprocess aggregated historical values or update earlier results because of back-dated refund corrections.","C":"A streaming table cannot be created inside a Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipeline at all, so any attempt to define `customer_lifetime_value` as a streaming table would immediately fail to compile or deploy within that pipeline.","D":"A streaming table always stores its data in the internal `__databricks_internal` catalog rather than a user-facing schema, making it impossible for BI tools to connect to or query the resulting lifetime value numbers directly."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. This is incorrect. Streaming tables can be defined with either SQL (`CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE`) or Python; SQL support is available and does not require rewriting the logic in PySpark.B. This is correct. Streaming tables assume an append-only source and process each record exactly once, which does not accommodate aggregation logic that must revisit and correct historical results when back-dated refunds arrive.C. This is incorrect. Streaming tables are a core dataset type supported natively within Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines; the mismatch here is about processing semantics, not about whether the object type is permitted.D. This is incorrect. Streaming tables are Unity Catalog managed tables registered in a normal catalog and schema, and BI tools can query them directly like any other table; internal backing storage details do not block that access."},{"id":"16","question":"An engineer writes `SELECT order_id, explode(items) AS item_id, explode(tags) AS tag FROM orders` to expand two different array columns from the same table in a single query. What is the most likely result of running this statement?","options":{"A":"The query fails, because Spark SQL only allows one `explode` (or other generator function) to appear in a single `SELECT` clause; the two arrays need to be exploded in separate steps.","B":"The query succeeds and produces the full cross product of `items` and `tags` for every single order row, similar to running a `CROSS JOIN LATERAL` between the two exploded array columns.","C":"The query succeeds and silently ignores the second `explode` call, returning only the rows produced by expanding the `items` array and leaving `tags` untouched.","D":"The query succeeds and interleaves the two arrays element by element, pairing the first item with the first tag, the second item with the second tag, and so on."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Spark SQL restricts a `SELECT` clause to a single generator function such as `explode`; including two `explode` calls in the same clause raises an analysis error, and the two array expansions must instead be done through separate, chained `SELECT` statements or lateral views.B. A single `explode` call does not perform a cross product between two different array columns; more importantly, the statement does not even run successfully because two generator functions cannot coexist in one `SELECT` clause.C. Spark SQL does not silently drop one of two `explode` calls in the same clause; the presence of two generator functions in a single `SELECT` causes the query to fail validation rather than quietly ignoring one of them.D. There is no positional zip behavior built into having two `explode` calls in one `SELECT` clause; the query does not reach execution at all because Spark SQL rejects multiple generator functions in the same clause."},{"id":"17","question":"A team writes Lakeflow pipeline code in Python using `import pyspark.pipelines as dp`. They need to silently discard any order record whose `order_total` is negative before it reaches the silver table, without failing the update. Which decorator call on the table function meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"`@dp.expect(\\"valid_total\\", \\"order_total >= 0\\")` records violations as a quality metric but still writes the negative-total rows into the target table.","B":"`@dp.expect_or_fail(\\"valid_total\\", \\"order_total >= 0\\")` stops the pipeline update entirely the first time a negative `order_total` is encountered.","C":"`@dp.expect_or_drop(\\"valid_total\\", \\"order_total < 0\\")` removes rows that fail the condition before the write, so only the negative-total rows would be kept in the target table.","D":"`@dp.expect_or_drop(\\"valid_total\\", \\"order_total >= 0\\")` removes rows that fail the condition before the write and lets the update finish normally."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. The plain `expect` decorator only tracks violations for reporting; rows that fail the check are still written to the target table, so negative totals would remain in the silver table, which does not meet the requirement.B. The `expect_or_fail` decorator makes invalid records prevent the update from succeeding, which is the opposite of the \'without failing the update\' requirement in this scenario. It suits critical checks where any bad row should stop the run.C. The right decorator, but the constraint is inverted: an expectation constraint states what a valid record looks like, so `order_total < 0` treats negative totals as valid and drops every non-negative order. The silver table would keep exactly the rows the team wants to discard.D. The `expect_or_drop` decorator drops records that fail its constraint before the data is written to the target, and the constraint `order_total >= 0` describes a valid record. Negative totals are therefore discarded while the update still completes."},{"id":"18","question":"Which set of comparison operators can an If/else condition task in a Lakeflow Job evaluate when comparing a task value or job parameter against a literal?","options":{"A":"==, CONTAINS, STARTSWITH, ENDSWITH","B":"==, !=, AND, OR","C":"==, !=, >, >=, <, <=","D":"==, !=, LIKE, IN"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. String-matching helpers such as `CONTAINS`, `STARTSWITH`, and `ENDSWITH` are not operators the If/else task evaluates. The task is limited to simple equality and relational comparisons, not substring matching.B. `AND` and `OR` are logical operators for combining multiple conditions, not comparison operators for a single value check, and the If/else task does not expose them as selectable operators. Each If/else task evaluates one comparison at a time.C. The If/else task type supports equality, inequality, and the four relational operators for comparing a dynamic value or job parameter against a literal. This is the complete operator set documented for condition tasks.D. Pattern-matching operators like `LIKE` and set-membership operators like `IN` are not part of the If/else task\'s supported comparison operators. These are SQL-style operators rather than condition-task operators."},{"id":"19","question":"A table update trigger is starting job runs more frequently than the team\'s downstream systems can absorb, because the monitored table receives many small writes throughout the day. The team wants to cap the trigger so a new run cannot start until a fixed cooldown has elapsed after the previous run finished. Which setting should they configure?","options":{"A":"Change the `condition` to `ALL_UPDATED` so a run only fires once every monitored table has changed at least once.","B":"Set `min_time_between_triggers_seconds` to enforce a minimum cooldown before the next run can start after a run completes.","C":"Remove the table update trigger and replace it with a file arrival trigger monitoring the table\'s underlying storage path.","D":"Set `wait_after_last_change_seconds` to delay each run until table writes stop for that duration, resetting on each write."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. The `ALL_UPDATED` condition changes which tables must change to fire a trigger with multiple monitored tables, but it does not throttle how often runs occur for a single table.B. The `min_time_between_triggers_seconds` setting caps run frequency by enforcing a minimum wait after the previous run completes before the next one can start, matching the cooldown requirement.C. Monitoring the underlying storage path with a file arrival trigger would react to file-level writes instead of table state, and it does not offer the run-cooldown behavior being requested.D. The `wait_after_last_change_seconds` setting debounces individual writes rather than enforcing a hard cooldown between completed runs, so it solves a different problem than limiting run frequency."},{"id":"20","question":"An upstream process periodically overwrites the same file name in a Unity Catalog volume with refreshed content instead of writing a new file. A data engineer configured a file arrival trigger on that volume and is confused that the downstream job never starts after these overwrites. What is the most likely explanation?","options":{"A":"File arrival triggers require the storage location to be an external location rather than a Unity Catalog volume, so the trigger never fires.","B":"File arrival triggers only fire when genuinely new files appear in the location, so overwriting an existing file name does not start a run.","C":"File arrival triggers only support paths containing wildcard patterns, so a fixed file name is silently ignored by the trigger.","D":"File arrival triggers check for changes only once every twenty-four hours, so the overwrite has simply not yet been noticed."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. File arrival triggers support both Unity Catalog volumes and external locations, so using a volume is not the reason the trigger fails to fire here.B. File arrival triggers detect new files landing in the monitored location, and overwriting an existing file does not count as a new arrival, so no run is started.C. File arrival triggers explicitly do not allow wildcard patterns in the monitored path, so this claim describes the opposite of how the feature actually works.D. File arrival triggers make a best-effort check for new files roughly every minute rather than once a day, so check frequency is not the cause of the missing run."},{"id":"21","question":"A finance team runs a nightly reconciliation job that must always execute at 2 AM in the America/New_York time zone regardless of whether any new data has arrived, because downstream analysts expect the report to be ready every morning at a fixed time. Which trigger type is the appropriate choice here, and why?","options":{"A":"A continuous trigger, because it keeps a run active at all times and can therefore be expected to produce fresh output well before analysts arrive each morning.","B":"A file arrival trigger, because the reconciliation job still depends on transaction data files that were written earlier in the day and must react to their arrival.","C":"A scheduled trigger using a Quartz cron expression with the time zone set to America/New_York, since the requirement is a fixed wall-clock time rather than a data event.","D":"A table update trigger on the transaction table, because that underlying table is technically updated at some point before 2 AM on most business nights."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. A continuous trigger reruns the job back-to-back with no fixed schedule at all, so it cannot guarantee output is ready at any specific wall-clock time like 2 AM.B. Even though the job reads transaction files, the actual requirement is a guaranteed fixed start time regardless of data timing, and a file arrival trigger would instead run whenever files land, which could be well before or after 2 AM.C. A cron-based scheduled trigger with the correct time zone directly guarantees the job starts at 2 AM local time every night, matching a requirement that is explicitly about a fixed wall-clock time rather than about reacting to any particular data event.D. Relying on the transaction table\'s update timing is an approximation at best, since the table could update earlier or later than expected on a given night, which does not guarantee the fixed 2 AM start the analysts require."},{"id":"22","question":"A `disable_switch` task is marked disabled so it is skipped at runtime, but a downstream task depends on it with the `All succeeded` run-if condition. Based on how Lakeflow Jobs evaluates disabled upstream tasks, what happens to the downstream task?","options":{"A":"The downstream task runs anyway, because Lakeflow Jobs treats a disabled upstream task as successful when it evaluates the `All succeeded` run-if condition.","B":"The downstream task stays pending indefinitely, because Lakeflow Jobs cannot evaluate any run-if condition against a disabled upstream task at all, ever.","C":"The downstream task runs only after an administrator manually approves the disabled task from inside the job run\'s UI page, using the approvals panel.","D":"The downstream task does not run; it is marked disabled for that run, because a disabled parent does not satisfy `All succeeded`."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. This confuses two distinct, separately documented result states. Databricks documents that upstream tasks in the `Excluded` result state are treated as successful for run-if evaluation, but a task that has been explicitly disabled is documented separately, and a disabled parent does not satisfy the succeeded requirement — so the downstream task does not run.B. Lakeflow Jobs does evaluate run-if conditions against disabled upstream tasks automatically during the same run; it does not leave a downstream task pending indefinitely, so this option misstates how the disablement rule is applied.C. There is no manual approval workflow for disabled tasks in Lakeflow Jobs. Run-if evaluation, and any resulting disablement of the downstream task, happens automatically without any operator action.D. This matches Databricks\' documentation: a disabled parent task does not satisfy the `All succeeded` requirement, so the downstream task does not run, and Lakeflow Jobs also marks that downstream task disabled for the run rather than executing or failing it."},{"id":"23","question":"A developer in a Databricks Git folder has staged several changed files and clicks Commit & Push, but leaves the commit message field empty. What happens?","options":{"A":"Databricks blocks the commit because a commit message is required before changes can be pushed to the remote branch.","B":"Databricks pushes the changes using an auto-generated message that lists every modified file path in the folder.","C":"Databricks queues the commit locally and prompts for a message only the next time the folder is opened.","D":"Databricks pushes the changes with no commit message, leaving the message field blank in the Git provider\'s history."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. The Git dialog requires a non-empty commit message before Commit & Push will proceed, so an empty message stops the operation.B. There is no feature that auto-generates a commit message from the list of changed file paths; the developer must supply one.C. Databricks does not defer message entry to a later session; the requirement is enforced at the moment Commit & Push is attempted.D. A push with a blank message is not permitted, so the change is not sent to the remote repository until a message is entered."},{"id":"24","question":"A data engineering team keeps a Lakeflow Job and a Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipeline defined as source in a repository, and wants to promote the exact same resource definitions from a dev workspace to a prod workspace without hand-editing settings in the UI at each stage. Which capability of Declarative Automation Bundles addresses this directly?","options":{"A":"A cluster policy attached to the job restricts which workspace the job can run in, which indirectly forces the same compute configuration to apply consistently across dev and prod.","B":"A `databricks.yml` file declares the job and pipeline as code, and named targets override settings like the workspace host so the same definitions deploy consistently across environments.","C":"The Databricks workspace UI automatically detects every resource change made in dev and replicates it into prod once an administrator approves the diff through the audit log console.","D":"Lakeflow Jobs and Spark Declarative Pipelines synchronize their configuration across workspaces natively whenever the resources share the same catalog and schema names in both environments."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. A cluster policy constrains compute settings for a single job run and has no mechanism for propagating or synchronizing configuration between separate workspaces.B. This describes the core mechanism of a bundle: resources are declared once in `databricks.yml`, and targets override host, variables, and other settings per environment so the same source deploys consistently across dev, test, and prod.C. There is no UI feature that auto-detects and replicates resource changes between workspaces through an audit-log approval flow; promotion across environments is driven by deploying bundle configuration, not by UI diffing.D. Jobs and pipelines do not natively synchronize configuration across workspaces based on shared catalog or schema names; each workspace\'s resources are independent unless a bundle deploys the same definitions to both."},{"id":"25","question":"A bundle variable has a target-level override defined inside `databricks.yml`, and a `--var` flag for the same variable is also passed on the `bundle deploy` command line. Which value does the Databricks CLI use?","options":{"A":"The CLI raises a validation error and refuses to deploy, because a variable cannot be set from two different sources at once.","B":"Both values are merged into a single ordered list, and the job receives the target override first followed by the command-line value as a fallback.","C":"The command-line `--var` value wins, because CLI flags take precedence over target overrides, environment variables, and top-level defaults.","D":"The target-level override wins, because values already defined inside `databricks.yml` always take precedence over any command-line input."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Having multiple potential sources for the same variable is expected and handled through a defined precedence order rather than treated as a conflict, so the CLI does not raise a validation error in this situation.B. Bundle variables resolve to a single scalar or complex value per deployment; the CLI does not merge values from multiple sources into a list or apply one as a runtime fallback for the other.C. The Databricks CLI resolves variable values in a fixed priority order, and a command-line `--var` flag sits at the top of that order, overriding target-level overrides, `BUNDLE_VAR_` environment variables, and top-level defaults.D. Target-level overrides are lower priority than a command-line `--var` flag; they only take effect when no higher-priority source, such as a CLI flag or environment variable, supplies a value for that variable."},{"id":"26","question":"A platform team wants pull-request pipelines in GitHub Actions to fail fast if a contributor\'s databricks.yml has a typo or an invalid resource reference, before any workspace credentials are even needed. Which CLI step best fits early in that pipeline?","options":{"A":"Add a step that runs `databricks bundle deploy -t dev --auto-approve`, since deploying to a scratch dev target is the only way the CLI can confirm the configuration parses correctly","B":"Add a step that runs `databricks bundle run --validate-only`, since this flag exists specifically to check every resource type in the bundle for configuration errors before any deploy happens at all","C":"Add a step that runs `databricks bundle destroy -t dev --auto-approve`, since tearing down and recreating the dev target on every pull request is described as the standard way to catch config drift","D":"Add a step that runs `databricks bundle validate`, since it only parses and checks the local configuration and does not require deploying or authenticating against a live workspace target"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Deploying to a dev target requires valid workspace credentials and actually creates or updates resources, which is unnecessary overhead and risk just to catch a configuration typo early in the pipeline.B. `--validate-only` is a flag scoped to `databricks bundle run` for pipeline resources specifically, not a general bundle-wide configuration check, and `bundle run` still requires the bundle to already be deployed.C. `databricks bundle destroy` deletes deployed resources and is unrelated to catching configuration typos; using it as a pull-request check would also be destructive and require existing deployed state.D. This is correct: `databricks bundle validate` checks configuration files for errors and prints a resource summary using only local parsing, so it can catch typos and bad references in CI before any deployment or workspace call is needed."},{"id":"27","question":"A pipeline job that normally completes in about 12 minutes has been taking over 40 minutes for the last three runs, though it still finishes with a Succeeded status each time. Using only the Lakeflow Jobs UI, how can the engineer confirm this is an unusual trend rather than normal variance?","options":{"A":"Open the job\'s run history and compare the total duration bars across recent runs against the historical baseline shown in the Matrix or Runs list view.","B":"Check the Spark UI stage detail page for the most recent run, since only stage-level shuffle metrics reveal whether a run duration is abnormal.","C":"Query the job\'s cluster event log directly, because the Jobs UI only reports whether a run succeeded or failed and never reports duration.","D":"Rerun the job manually from the Repair run option, since a successful repair run duration is the only reliable duration baseline available."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. The run history views display each run\'s total duration alongside prior runs, so lining up recent bars against the historical pattern is the direct way to confirm a 12-minute job is now consistently taking 40 minutes. This does not require leaving the Jobs UI at all.B. Stage-level shuffle metrics can help explain why a run is slow, but they are not needed just to confirm that a duration trend exists, and reading only the most recent run would miss the comparison against the historical baseline entirely.C. The Jobs UI does report run duration for every run, so this understates what the UI provides and sends the engineer to a source that does not track run-over-run trends the way the run history view does.D. Repair run reruns only the tasks that failed in a specific past run, and since every recent run already succeeded there is nothing to repair; it also would not establish a historical duration baseline."},{"id":"28","question":"A `groupBy` aggregation is heavily skewed because a single customer ID accounts for a large share of all rows, causing one task to process far more data than the others in the Spark UI\'s stage metrics. AQE\'s skew join optimization does not apply here because the bottleneck occurs in an aggregation rather than a join. Which technique would most directly reduce the skew for this aggregation?","options":{"A":"Repartitioning the DataFrame by a monotonically increasing surrogate column, ignoring the original grouping key altogether.","B":"Salting the skewed key with a random suffix before grouping, then re-aggregating to spread its rows across partitions.","C":"Reducing `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions` to a smaller value, consolidating the aggregation into fewer, larger partitions per task.","D":"Converting the aggregation to a broadcast join against a lookup table, eliminating the shuffle step regardless of key distribution."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Repartitioning by an unrelated surrogate column changes how rows are physically distributed for storage but does not affect how Spark shuffles data by the grouping key during the aggregation itself, so the skew on that key remains.B. This is correct. Salting appends a random suffix to the overrepresented key, splitting its rows across several synthetic sub-keys during the first aggregation pass, then a second pass aggregates the partial results back together, distributing the heavy key\'s workload across many tasks.C. Reducing the shuffle partition count consolidates data into fewer partitions, which would make the imbalance worse, not better, since the already-heavy key\'s partition would absorb even more of the remaining data.D. An aggregation is not a join between two tables, so there is no join side to broadcast; this option does not apply to reducing skew within a single `groupBy` on one DataFrame."},{"id":"29","question":"Which three maintenance operations does predictive optimization automatically manage for eligible Unity Catalog managed tables?","options":{"A":"`OPTIMIZE`, `VACUUM`, and `REORG TABLE`, combining compaction, cleanup, and type-widening rewrites automatically","B":"`OPTIMIZE`, `ZORDER`, and `VACUUM`, since ZORDER is required alongside file compaction for predictive optimization to run","C":"`OPTIMIZE`, `VACUUM`, and `ANALYZE`, covering file compaction, stale file cleanup, and statistics collection respectively","D":"`ANALYZE`, `REORG TABLE`, and `REPAIR TABLE`, since these rebuild metadata and table structure for query planning"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. `REORG TABLE` is a manually run command used for tasks like type widening, not one of the operations predictive optimization schedules automatically.B. `ZORDER` is a manual clustering technique that is actually incompatible with liquid clustering; it is not one of the three operations predictive optimization automates.C. Predictive optimization automatically runs `OPTIMIZE` for file compaction and incremental clustering, `VACUUM` for removing unreferenced files, and `ANALYZE` for refreshing table statistics.D. `REORG TABLE` and `REPAIR TABLE` are manually invoked commands for specific maintenance tasks; neither is part of the automated set predictive optimization manages."},{"id":"30","question":"A serverless Lakeflow task issues 150 SQL queries during a single run, and an engineer notices that several later queries show no performance insight badge even though they appear slow in the query history. What explains this?","options":{"A":"Databricks aggregates query-level performance metrics for only the first 100 queries in a run, so later queries are excluded.","B":"Performance insight badges only ever appear for queries that read from Unity Catalog managed tables rather than external tables.","C":"The job\'s expected completion time threshold was already exceeded, which suppresses insight badges for remaining queries.","D":"The task was executed on a job cluster instead of serverless compute, which disables performance insight badges entirely."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. This is correct because Databricks aggregates query-level performance metrics across only the first 100 queries within a run, so a task issuing 150 queries will have its later queries fall outside that aggregation window and therefore lack insight badges.B. This is incorrect because performance insight badges are not gated on whether a table is Unity Catalog managed versus external; the limiting factor described for this feature is the count of queries per run, not the storage type queried.C. This is incorrect because the expected completion time is a separate duration-threshold warning on the overall run and has no effect on whether individual queries receive performance insight badges.D. This is incorrect because performance insight badges, the Timeline view, and per-query aggregation are serverless-specific capabilities in the first place; the scenario already states the task ran on serverless compute, so cluster type is not the explanation."},{"id":"31","question":"When a Spark job on a Databricks cluster fails with an out-of-memory error, which combination of resources gives the most direct evidence of whether the failure originated on the driver or on an executor?","options":{"A":"The Databricks billing usage dashboard, which reports which specific node incurred the highest compute cost during the run and is therefore the likely failure point.","B":"The workspace audit log, which records every notebook command but does not capture memory allocation on either the driver or the executors.","C":"The cluster\'s autoscaling history graph, which shows only how many executors were attached over time and not their individual memory usage.","D":"The driver logs for stack traces mentioning the failing process, combined with the Spark UI Executors tab for per-node memory and garbage-collection metrics."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Compute cost reflects how much a node ran and what instance type it used, not how much memory it consumed or whether it failed, so a billing dashboard cannot distinguish a driver failure from an executor failure. Cost data is unrelated to memory diagnostics.B. The audit log is built for tracking user actions and commands for governance purposes and does not record memory allocation or process-level errors, so it cannot help localize an out-of-memory failure to the driver or an executor.C. An autoscaling history graph shows how many executors were attached at a given time, which is useful for capacity planning but does not report memory usage on any individual node, so it cannot show which process exhausted its memory.D. Driver logs surface the actual stack trace and error message that identify which process ran out of memory, while the Spark UI\'s Executors tab shows per-node memory usage and garbage-collection time that reveal whether individual executors were under memory pressure. Together these two sources give the most direct, process-level evidence needed to localize an out-of-memory failure."},{"id":"32","question":"A data engineer runs `CREATE TABLE analytics.sales.orders (...)` and omits any `LOCATION` clause. Where does Unity Catalog store the resulting data files?","options":{"A":"In the workspace\'s default external location, which every Unity Catalog metastore provisions automatically for tables that omit an explicit `LOCATION` clause at creation time.","B":"In the same storage path as the most recently created external table in that schema, since Unity Catalog reuses the last referenced path by default.","C":"Under the managed storage location resolved from the containing schema, catalog, or metastore, whichever level has a managed location configured, since no path was given.","D":"In a temporary DBFS root path that must be manually migrated to permanent storage before the table can be queried by any other user or job in the workspace."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Omitting `LOCATION` creates a managed table, not one placed in a default external location, and there is no such automatic external-location provisioning behavior.B. Incorrect. Unity Catalog does not reuse the storage path of another table; each managed table resolves its own path from the schema, catalog, or metastore managed location.C. Correct. Omitting `LOCATION` creates a managed table, and Unity Catalog resolves its storage path from the nearest configured managed location on the schema, catalog, or metastore.D. Incorrect. Managed tables are not placed in a temporary DBFS root requiring manual migration; Unity Catalog resolves a permanent managed location automatically."},{"id":"33","question":"A column mask policy redacts customer email addresses for most groups. The compliance team must still see the full, unmasked email address for investigations. How should this exception be expressed in the `CREATE POLICY` statement?","options":{"A":"Add a `WHEN has_tag_value(\'sensitivity\',\'high\')` clause naming the compliance team, since `WHEN` clauses in `CREATE POLICY` select principals rather than tables.","B":"Add an `EXCEPT compliance_team` clause to the policy\'s principal list, so the masking function is skipped whenever a member of that group runs the query.","C":"Create a second column mask policy that masks the column for every group, then rely on the newer policy to silently override the first one.","D":"Grant the compliance team the `MODIFY` privilege on the table, since only write privileges determine whether column mask policies apply to a group\'s queries."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. This is incorrect. A `WHEN` clause in `CREATE POLICY` scopes which tagged tables the policy applies to, not which principals are exempt; principal exceptions belong in the `TO`/`EXCEPT` clause instead.B. This is correct: the `EXCEPT` clause in a policy\'s principal list exempts named principals from the masking or filtering behavior, so the compliance team sees the unmodified column value while everyone else still sees the masked one.C. This is incorrect. Stacking a second masking policy for every group does not create a targeted exception for one team, and relying on implicit override behavior between overlapping policies is not how exceptions are expressed.D. This is incorrect. Write privileges like `MODIFY` govern the ability to change data, not whether a read-time column mask policy is applied; masking exceptions are controlled through the policy\'s principal clause, not table privileges."},{"id":"34","question":"A compliance team asks whether a data engineer can run `SELECT * FROM finance.transactions VERSION AS OF 5` on a table that has an active row filter and column mask to inspect a prior data state before the policies existed. What is the expected outcome?","options":{"A":"Time travel is unsupported on tables with row filters or column masks attached, so this `VERSION AS OF` query is expected to fail outright","B":"The query succeeds and returns the version-5 data exactly as it looked before any row filter or column mask was ever attached to it","C":"The query succeeds, but Unity Catalog silently ignores the `VERSION AS OF` clause and returns the table\'s current state instead of it","D":"The query succeeds only for members of the account\'s top-level admin group, regardless of what the row filter or mask logic actually specifies"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Unity Catalog documents time travel as one of the operations unsupported on tables with row filters or column masks attached, so a `VERSION AS OF` query against such a table is expected to be rejected rather than succeed.B. Because time travel itself is unsupported once these policies are present, the engineer cannot retrieve the historical version at all, regardless of whether the older data predates the policy\'s creation.C. The restriction causes the query to fail rather than quietly substitute the current table state for the requested historical version, so no result set is silently returned.D. The unsupported-operation restriction is not bypassed by admin group membership; row filter and column mask logic controls row and column visibility, but the time-travel limitation applies at the table level regardless of who is querying."},{"id":"35","question":"An engineer runs `GRANT USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA, SELECT ON CATALOG analytics TO bi_team` before any schemas exist in the catalog. Months later, several new schemas and tables are created inside `analytics`. Does `bi_team` need any additional grants to query the new tables?","options":{"A":"Yes, the group needs a fresh grant on each new schema, because catalog-level grants only apply to objects that already existed when the statement ran.","B":"Yes, but only `USE SCHEMA` needs to be re-granted on each new schema, since `SELECT` inherited from the catalog covers tables while `USE` privileges never inherit downward.","C":"No, but only because Unity Catalog automatically re-runs every catalog-level grant statement nightly against newly created schemas and tables.","D":"No, because privileges granted on a catalog also apply to schemas and tables created in it later, not only to objects that existed at grant time."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Catalog-level grants are not limited to objects that existed at grant time; they apply to all current and future schemas and tables in the catalog, so a per-schema grant is redundant.B. Incorrect. USE SCHEMA was granted at the catalog level in the same statement, and a USE SCHEMA grant on a catalog automatically applies to all current and future schemas in it. Container privileges do inherit downward, so nothing needs to be re-granted.C. Incorrect. There is no nightly re-application job; inheritance is a property of how the original grant is evaluated against child objects, not a scheduled re-grant process.D. Correct. Unity Catalog documents that when you grant a privilege on a parent object, it applies to all current and future child objects. The statement already covers the three privileges needed to read a table (USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA and SELECT), so the new schemas and tables are readable without any further grant."}]},{"id":"databricks-data-engineer-professional","title":"Databricks Certified Data Engineer Professional","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"A platform team is rolling out governed Python and SQL functions in Unity Catalog. A new analyst reports `PERMISSION_DENIED` errors both when trying to register a new function in the `analytics` schema and when trying to call an existing function owned by someone else. Which two grants resolve these two specific failures?(Select 2)","options":{"A":"`GRANT OWNERSHIP ON SCHEMA main.analytics` to the analyst, enabling new function registration without any catalog-level grant.","B":"`GRANT USAGE, CREATE ON SCHEMA main.analytics` plus `GRANT USAGE ON CATALOG main` to the analyst, enabling new function registration.","C":"`GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION main.analytics.existing_func` to the analyst, enabling the analyst to call the function someone else owns.","D":"`GRANT MODIFY ON FUNCTION main.analytics.existing_func` to the analyst, enabling the analyst to call a function owned by another principal.","E":"`GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON METASTORE main` to the analyst, enabling both registering and calling any function in the catalog at once."},"explanation":"A. Transferring ownership of the whole schema is unnecessary and overly broad just to let one analyst create functions there, and it still would not resolve the separate failure to call another owner\'s existing function.F. `SELECT` is a table- and view-level privilege; it has no effect on the ability to invoke a function, so granting it on the schema would not resolve either reported failure.B. Registering a new function requires `USAGE` and `CREATE` on the target schema together with `USAGE` on the containing catalog; without both, the registration attempt fails with a permission error.C. Calling a function owned by another principal requires an explicit `EXECUTE` grant on that function; ownership of the schema or catalog alone does not confer the right to run someone else\'s function.D. `MODIFY` is not the privilege that governs invoking a function; running a function is controlled by the `EXECUTE` privilege, and `MODIFY`-style grants do not substitute for it.E. Granting metastore-wide `ALL PRIVILEGES` is a far broader change than needed for either failure and is not the targeted grant Unity Catalog uses to resolve schema-level or function-level permission errors.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"2","question":"A Declarative Automation Bundle currently uploads a local wheel to the DBFS root and configures the job\'s `whl` library entry with a `dbfs:/` path. On a workspace running Databricks Runtime 15.1 or later, bundle deploys succeed but job runs fail to install the wheel. What should the team change to fix this on current runtimes?","options":{"A":"Downgrade the job\'s Databricks Runtime version pinned in the bundle to a release earlier than 14.0, since only runtimes before 14.0 support installing wheel libraries from any storage location at all.","B":"Repackage the wheel as a Maven coordinate in the `libraries` block, since Maven-based resolution bypasses the DBFS root restriction that applies only to `whl` and `pypi` library entry types.","C":"Point the bundle\'s `whl` library entry at a workspace file or Unity Catalog volume path, since storing library files in the DBFS root is deprecated and disabled by default on Databricks Runtime 15.1 and above.","D":"Add an explicit `dbfs_root_enabled: true` setting to the job\'s cluster spec, since Databricks Runtime 15.1 requires that flag before it will read wheel files from the DBFS root path on any cluster type."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Downgrading the runtime does not address the underlying storage-location problem and abandons current runtime features solely to work around a deprecation; it also misstates which runtimes support wheel installs from arbitrary locations.B. Maven coordinates resolve Java or Scala artifacts from a Maven repository and are not a valid packaging format for a Python wheel, so repackaging the wheel this way does not bypass the DBFS root restriction or make the library installable.C. Storing library files in the DBFS root is deprecated and disabled by default starting with Databricks Runtime 15.1, so a `whl` entry pointing at a `dbfs:/` path stops resolving on those runtimes. Moving the wheel to a workspace file or Unity Catalog volume path is the supported replacement location.D. There is no `dbfs_root_enabled` cluster spec setting; Databricks Runtime 15.1 disables DBFS root library storage by default rather than gating it behind a flag that jobs can re-enable, so adding this setting would not fix the failure."},{"id":"3","question":"A bundle defines a `variables` block with a `warehouse_size` variable used by a job cluster\'s node type. Developers should get a small single-node cluster when deploying to `dev`, while the CI/CD pipeline should deploy a larger multi-node cluster to `prod`, without maintaining two separate copies of the job definition. Which structure achieves this?","options":{"A":"Set `warehouse_size` inside the `artifacts` block for each target, since artifact-level values are the only place bundle variables can differ between deployment targets.","B":"Declare `warehouse_size` once under the top-level `variables` block, then override it under `targets.dev.variables` and `targets.prod.variables` with the size for each.","C":"Declare a separate `warehouse_size_dev` and `warehouse_size_prod` variable at the top level, then reference whichever one matches the current git branch inside the job\'s `node_type_id` field.","D":"Move the job definition into two files, `resources/job_dev.yml` and `resources/job_prod.yml`, and use `include:` to load only the file matching the active target during deploy."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. The `artifacts` block configures how build artifacts like wheels are produced; it does not host bundle variables, and variable overrides belong under each target\'s `variables` key instead.B. Declaring the variable once and overriding it per target is the intended pattern for values that should differ by environment; the job definition stays single-sourced while `dev` and `prod` each substitute their own size at deploy time.C. Splitting into two differently named variables and picking between them based on the git branch adds indirection the bundle format does not need, since target-scoped variable overrides already solve this without branch-sensitive logic in the job definition.D. Maintaining two near-duplicate job files defeats the goal of a single definition; `include:` also loads all matched files unconditionally rather than selecting one based on the active target."},{"id":"4","question":"Which statement correctly describes the current state of the Python API for Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines (formerly Delta Live Tables)?","options":{"A":"The `dlt` module is replaced by `pyspark.pipelines`, commonly imported as `dp`; existing `import dlt` pipeline code keeps running unchanged, but Databricks recommends migrating to `dp` decorators such as `@dp.table`.","B":"The `dlt` module was removed entirely in the Lakeflow rebrand, so every existing pipeline written with `import dlt` must be rewritten with `@dp.table` before it will run again.","C":"The `dlt` module now only supports SQL pipeline definitions, while the new `pyspark.pipelines` module exclusively handles Python pipeline definitions, so mixing the two in one pipeline is not possible.","D":"The `@dlt.table` and `@dp.table` decorators define different physical dataset types, so a pipeline mixing both decorators materializes two independent copies of the underlying table."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. This correctly describes the rebrand: `pyspark.pipelines`, imported as `dp`, is the current recommended API, replacing the `@table`/`@view` decorators formerly imported from `dlt`, while existing `dlt`-based pipeline code continues to run without any required migration.B. The `dlt` module was not removed and existing pipelines built with `import dlt` continue to run without modification; Databricks recommends adopting the new names going forward, but no rewrite is required for old code to keep working.C. The distinction between `dlt` and `pyspark.pipelines` is not a SQL-versus-Python split; both relate to the Python API naming, and SQL pipeline definitions are a separate, unaffected syntax, so this description misstates the change entirely.D. The decorator rename did not introduce a new physical dataset type; `@dlt.table` and `@dp.table` are the old and new names for the same underlying streaming-table concept, so mixing them does not produce two independent copies of a table."},{"id":"5","question":"A Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines pipeline uses Auto Loader (`cloudFiles`) to land append-only clickstream JSON files. Downstream consumers need every event processed exactly once, with the lowest possible latency and compute cost per micro-batch. Which table type should the engineer define for this dataset?\\n```python\\nfrom pyspark import pipelines as dp\\n\\n@dp.table\\ndef clicks_raw():\\nreturn (spark.readStream\\n.format(\\"cloudFiles\\")\\n.option(\\"cloudFiles.format\\", \\"json\\")\\n.load(\\"/Volumes/raw/clickstream/\\"))\\n```","options":{"A":"A streaming table with checkpointing disabled, so Lakeflow can safely recompute the entire clickstream history from scratch on every scheduled trigger instead of tracking progress.","B":"A materialized view refreshed continuously, because continuous mode forces a full join across every historical file to preserve strict event ordering guarantees for the clickstream.","C":"A materialized view, because it recomputes the full result from the entire file history on every run, which is required to guarantee freshness for a purely append-only clickstream source.","D":"A streaming table, because it processes each newly arrived record exactly once through incremental append-mode computation, matching the append-only source at low latency and cost."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Checkpoints are what let a streaming table track which records it already processed exactly once; disabling them would break incremental processing and force reprocessing, which is the opposite of the low-cost goal.B. Materialized views do not use continuous refresh to force full historical joins for ordering; continuous mode only controls how often the pipeline checks for new data, and this description misrepresents how materialized views compute results.C. Materialized views recompute results as needed against the current state of the source, which is useful for changing or joined data but wastes compute reprocessing an append-only stream that never needs a full recalculation.D. A streaming table is the correct choice here because it maintains a streaming flow over an append-only source, so Lakeflow processes only the newly arrived clickstream files each micro-batch instead of recomputing history, which minimizes both latency and compute cost."},{"id":"6","question":"An analyst opens an interactive serverless notebook to explore a sales dataset and needs the compute to start within seconds so they aren\'t kept waiting between commands. A teammate suggests switching the notebook\'s compute to Standard performance mode to cut cost. Which statement correctly evaluates that suggestion?","options":{"A":"The suggestion works, but only after the analyst enables the High memory tier, since high memory must be turned on before Standard mode can be selected for any workload.","B":"The suggestion works as described, because Standard performance mode is available for serverless notebooks and simply trades a slower startup for lower DBU cost.","C":"The suggestion cannot be applied, because serverless notebooks only support Performance Optimized mode, built for low-latency, always-warm interactive sessions.","D":"The suggestion cannot be applied, because serverless compute does not support notebooks at all, so the analyst must attach the notebook to a classic interactive cluster instead for this exploration."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. The memory tier and the performance mode are independent settings in the notebook\'s Environment panel, so enabling High memory has no bearing on which performance mode is selectable.B. Standard performance mode exists for Lakeflow Jobs and Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines, but it is not offered as a selectable option for serverless notebooks, so this evaluation misstates what\'s configurable.C. Serverless notebooks are restricted to Performance Optimized mode precisely because interactive sessions need warm, low-latency compute, which correctly explains why the teammate\'s suggestion isn\'t something the analyst can turn on.D. Serverless compute does support notebooks as one of its core workload types, so claiming notebooks require classic compute misrepresents the platform\'s current capabilities."},{"id":"7","question":"Which Structured Streaming output mode rewrites the entire result table to the sink on every trigger, making it unsuitable for a large, unbounded aggregation whose full state must be retained indefinitely?","options":{"A":"Update","B":"Complete","C":"Continuous","D":"Append"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Update mode writes only the rows whose aggregate value changed since the last trigger, which is more efficient than a full rewrite but is not what this description asks for.B. Complete mode rewrites the entire result table to the sink after every trigger, which requires retaining full aggregation state indefinitely and matches the behavior described.C. Continuous is a low-latency processing mode, not an output mode, so it does not fit alongside append, update, and complete as an answer to this question.D. Append mode only writes new rows that will never change again, such as finalized windows once the watermark has passed, and it does not rewrite the full result table on each trigger."},{"id":"8","question":"Which statements correctly distinguish `AUTO CDC INTO` from `AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT` in Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines? (Select all that apply)(Select 2)","options":{"A":"`AUTO CDC INTO` consumes a continuous stream of row-level events, such as insert, update, and delete records emitted by a CDC log or message bus.","B":"`AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT` is designed for sources that periodically expose a full table snapshot rather than a stream of discrete change events.","C":"`AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT` is available through both the SQL and Python interfaces, while `AUTO CDC INTO` is restricted to the Python interface only.","D":"Both APIs require the source to carry a boolean or string column identifying the operation type for every single incoming row, with no exceptions.","E":"Neither API can write to a Unity Catalog–governed streaming table; both are restricted to writing external Iceberg tables outside of Unity Catalog."},"explanation":"A. `AUTO CDC INTO` is the API built around an incoming stream of discrete change events, matching sources like a CDC log or a message bus that emit one record per insert, update, or delete.B. `AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT` targets sources that only ever expose a full point-in-time snapshot rather than a change feed, and it works by diffing successive snapshots to derive the effective inserts, updates, and deletes.C. The availability is reversed from this claim: `AUTO CDC INTO` is supported in both SQL and Python, while `AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT` is the one restricted to the Python interface only.D. An explicit operation-type column is only needed to identify which rows represent deletes via `APPLY AS DELETE WHEN`; ordinary insert and update rows do not require such a column, so this is not a universal requirement of every incoming row.E. Tables produced by Lakeflow pipelines, including those written by either CDC API, are Delta tables managed as Unity Catalog streaming tables; external Iceberg access is an optional add-on via table properties, not a restriction that excludes Unity Catalog governance.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"9","question":"A metastore admin creates a connection to a Snowflake warehouse and wants three business analyst groups to be able to query different foreign catalogs built from that one connection, without any of the groups being able to see the underlying host, port, or credential values, and without any group being able to mint additional foreign catalogs beyond the ones the admin creates for them. Which grant strategy achieves this?","options":{"A":"Grant `CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG` on the connection to each group so every group can build and query its own catalog directly from the shared connection object.","B":"Grant `ALL PRIVILEGES` on the connection to each group so credential visibility and query access both come from the broad grant on the connection itself.","C":"Grant `OWNER` on the connection to one member per group so that member can redistribute connection-level access across their own group later on.","D":"Grant `USE CONNECTION` on the connection to each group, then grant `USE CATALOG` plus `SELECT` on just the one foreign catalog assigned to that group."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Granting `CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG` would let every group independently create catalogs from the shared connection, which violates the requirement that only the admin controls how many foreign catalogs exist.B. `ALL PRIVILEGES` on the connection includes catalog-creation and ownership-transfer rights well beyond read access, and connection-level options such as the stored credential remain outside what any grant exposes to a non-owner querying through it, but the privilege scope here is far broader than required and risky to hand to three separate groups.C. Transferring `OWNER` hands full control of the connection, including the ability to alter its credentials and drop it, to a group member — a much larger blast radius than the read-only, catalog-scoped access the scenario calls for.D. `USE CONNECTION` lets a principal query through catalogs that reference the connection without exposing the stored host, port, or secret values, and scoping `USE CATALOG`/`SELECT` to just one foreign catalog per group keeps each group limited to its assigned data without letting any group create new catalogs."},{"id":"10","question":"A data engineer wants to enable Lakeflow Connect CDC ingestion from a SQL Server availability group by pointing the connector at one of the readable secondary replicas, since it has more spare capacity than the primary. Why will this configuration fail?","options":{"A":"The ingestion gateway cannot reach a secondary replica over the network even when VPN or Direct Connect connectivity has been configured correctly.","B":"Change tracking and CDC are not supported on SQL Server read replicas, so the connector must point at the primary instance instead.","C":"Lakeflow Connect only supports SQL Server instances hosted on Azure VMs, so a secondary replica elsewhere is automatically rejected.","D":"Secondary replicas do not allow Unity Catalog connections to authenticate, regardless of which CDC mechanism the source database uses."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Network reachability is a separate concern from CDC support; even with working connectivity, change tracking and CDC simply are not exposed on secondary replicas.B. SQL Server\'s change tracking and CDC features are only available against the primary instance; secondary replicas cannot serve as a CDC source, so the connector must be pointed at the primary regardless of its available capacity.C. Lakeflow Connect supports SQL Server across on-premises deployments, EC2, Azure VMs, Azure SQL, and Amazon RDS, so the connector is not restricted to Azure-hosted deployments.D. Authentication is handled through the Unity Catalog connection\'s stored credentials and is independent of whether the target instance is primary or secondary; the failure here is about CDC support, not authentication."},{"id":"11","question":"Given a `VARIANT` column `raw` containing `{\\"store\\": {\\"bicycle\\": [{\\"price\\": 19.95}]}}`, an engineer writes this Spark SQL query:\\n```sql\\nSELECT raw:store.bicycle[0].price FROM inventory\\n```\\nWhich `variant_get` call is functionally equivalent to this shorthand path expression?","options":{"A":"`variant_get(raw, \'$store.bicycle[0].price\')`","B":"`variant_get(raw, \'$.store[bicycle][0].price\')`","C":"`variant_get(raw, \'$.store.bicycle[0].price\')`","D":"`variant_get(raw, \'$.store.bicycle.price[0]\')`"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. This path string omits the required `.` immediately after the leading `$` root marker, which makes it a malformed JSON path rather than an equivalent way of expressing the same navigation.B. This path wraps the `bicycle` key in unquoted brackets as if it were a variable or index rather than a field name, which does not correctly express navigating into the `bicycle` object key.C. This path string mirrors the shorthand exactly: it navigates into the `store` object, then `bicycle`, indexes the first array element, and finally reads `price`, matching the intended nested lookup field for field.D. This path swaps the array index and the field name, indexing into `price` rather than into `bicycle`, so it does not describe the same nested location as the original shorthand expression."},{"id":"12","question":"A dataset function is defined as follows:\\n\\n```python\\nrules = {\\n\\"valid_price\\": \\"price > 0\\",\\n\\"valid_qty\\": \\"quantity > 0\\",\\n}\\n\\n@dp.table\\n@dp.expect_all_or_drop(rules)\\ndef silver_line_items():\\nreturn spark.readStream.table(\\"bronze_line_items\\")\\n```\\n\\nWhich statements correctly describe the behavior of this pipeline definition? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"The pipeline update fails and rolls back entirely the first time any row violates one of the two rules.","B":"Rows that violate exactly one of the two rules are still written to the table, since `expect_all_or_drop` only removes rows that fail both rules at once.","C":"Both `valid_price` and `valid_qty` are tracked as separate named expectations in the pipeline\'s data quality metrics.","D":"Replacing `expect_all_or_drop` with `expect_all` would cause violating rows to be written to the table while still recording violation counts for both rules.","E":"A row is excluded from `silver_line_items` if it violates either the `valid_price` rule or the `valid_qty` rule."},"explanation":"A. Rolling back the entire update on the first violation describes the fail variant of the group decorator, not the drop variant, which instead removes offending rows and keeps the update running.B. The or-drop decorator drops a row as soon as it fails any single rule in the set, so a row violating only one of the two rules is still excluded rather than being written through.C. Each key in the rules dictionary becomes its own named expectation, so the pipeline\'s data quality metrics report violation counts for `valid_price` and `valid_qty` independently.D. The plain warn variant of the group decorator keeps every row in the output regardless of rule failures while still tallying how many rows violated each named rule, unlike the drop variant used in the snippet.F. The dictionary keys are just user-chosen expectation names for tracking purposes and have no requirement to match any column name in the source table.E. The or-drop group decorator removes a row if it fails any one of the rules in the dictionary, so violating either the price rule or the quantity rule is enough to exclude that row from the target table.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"13","question":"A data engineer needs to enrich a Delta table of customer support tickets with a sentiment label generated by a Databricks-hosted foundation model, calling the model directly from a Spark SQL query inside a nightly batch job. The `ticket_text` column holds free-form text. Which SQL expression correctly returns a sentiment label using `ai_query` against the pay-per-token `system.ai/llama-4-70b` endpoint?","options":{"A":"`ai_query(\'system.ai/llama-4-70b\', \'Classify sentiment as positive, negative, or neutral: \' || ticket_text) AS sentiment`","B":"`ai_query(\'system.ai/llama-4-70b\', request => named_struct(\'ticket_text\', ticket_text)) AS sentiment`","C":"`ai_query(\'system.ai/llama-4-70b\', ticket_text, returnType => \'STRING\', failOnError => false) AS sentiment`","D":"`ai_query(endpoint => \'system.ai/llama-4-70b\', model_input => ticket_text, output_type => \'STRING\') AS sentiment_result_label`"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Foundation model endpoints take a single STRING request; concatenating an explicit instruction with the column value produces a prompt the model can act on to return a sentiment label.B. A named_struct request is the pattern for custom model-serving endpoints that expose named input features; pay-per-token foundation model endpoints expect a plain STRING prompt, so this call would not classify correctly.C. Passing the raw column value with no instruction text gives the model nothing to classify against, so even though the returnType and failOnError syntax is valid, the model has no directive to produce a sentiment label.D. `model_input` is not a recognized ai_query parameter name; ai_query\'s parameters are `endpoint` and `request`, so this call raises an error rather than running."},{"id":"14","question":"A report needs subtotals of `revenue` broken out by `region`, by `region, product_category` together, and a single grand total across all rows, all returned in one result set alongside the detail rows. Which Spark SQL construct produces exactly this multi-level aggregation in a single query?","options":{"A":"`GROUP BY region, product_category WITH TOTALS`, which appends a grand total row to the standard two-column grouped result without producing the intermediate `region`-only subtotal.","B":"`GROUP BY ROLLUP (region, product_category)`, which produces the detail-level groups plus subtotals for `region` and a grand total, following the hierarchical order of the listed columns.","C":"`GROUP BY CUBE (region, product_category)`, which produces every combination of the two columns being grouped or not, including a subtotal for `product_category` alone that rollup does not produce.","D":"`GROUP BY region, product_category` combined with a `UNION ALL` against a separately grouped `region`-only query and a third ungrouped query for the grand total, unioned together."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Spark SQL\'s `GROUP BY` clause has no `WITH TOTALS` syntax; that construct does not exist in Spark SQL and the query would fail to parse.B. Correct. `ROLLUP (region, product_category)` produces the full detail grouping, then a subtotal per `region` (dropping `product_category`), then a single grand total, matching the hierarchical requirement described without needing a `product_category`-only subtotal.C. Incorrect for this requirement. `CUBE` also produces a `product_category`-only subtotal (with region dropped) that was not requested, so it returns a superset of the needed groupings rather than the specific hierarchical set the report calls for.D. Workable but not the right construct here. Manually unioning three separately grouped queries can reproduce the same rows, but it requires three separate scans and aggregations and more code than the purpose-built rollup syntax designed for exactly this hierarchical subtotal pattern."},{"id":"15","question":"Monthly compute spend has spiked. An engineer joins `system.compute.clusters` to `system.billing.usage` and finds several all-purpose clusters with `autotermination_minutes = 0` running continuously under a shared `owned_by` service principal that no longer maps to an active team. What is the most effective first remediation based on this system table data?","options":{"A":"Increase each cluster\'s `min_workers` setting so the autoscaler can shut down worker nodes more aggressively once the workload becomes idle for an extended period.","B":"Reprice the clusters through `system.billing.list_prices` so the SKU rate charged for their compute automatically drops once utilization is shown to be low.","C":"Set an autotermination window on the clusters and update `owned_by` to the team that uses them, so idle time stops accruing and future usage attributes correctly.","D":"Delete the corresponding rows from `system.compute.clusters` for the idle clusters so the billing report no longer includes their historical usage in any future cost query."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Raising `min_workers` forces the autoscaler to keep more workers running at all times, which increases baseline cost rather than reducing it, and does nothing to address a cluster that never terminates.B. `system.billing.list_prices` is a reference table of historical SKU rates published by Databricks; it cannot be written to in order to negotiate a lower price, and utilization data has no mechanism to trigger a repricing through this table.C. Enforcing autotermination directly removes the root cause of idle spend, and correcting `owned_by` restores accountability so the team responsible can monitor and control the cluster\'s cost going forward.D. System tables are read-only records generated by the platform from actual account activity; deleting rows from them is not a supported operation and would not stop the underlying cluster from continuing to accrue real charges."},{"id":"16","question":"A data engineer wants to inspect the event log of a running Lakeflow Declarative Pipeline directly from a notebook, without changing any pipeline settings or waiting for an admin to configure anything. The pipeline ID is known. Which approach reads the event log data with no configuration change required?","options":{"A":"Turn on event log publishing in the pipeline\'s advanced settings so a visible catalog table is created, then query that new published table with a standard SQL `SELECT` statement.","B":"Query `system.lakeflow.pipelines` for the pipeline ID, since this system table stores per-update flow metrics and expectation results alongside pipeline metadata for every run.","C":"Call the Jobs API `getrun` endpoint using the pipeline\'s underlying job ID to pull the flow-level `data_quality` metrics that the event log normally exposes to a notebook.","D":"Run `SELECT * FROM event_log(\'<pipeline_id>\')` in a notebook attached to a cluster or SQL warehouse, reading the pipeline\'s hidden Delta event log table directly by ID."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Publishing the event log to a visible catalog table is an optional convenience for governed access, not a prerequisite for querying it, so this adds an unnecessary configuration step.B. `system.lakeflow.pipelines` holds pipeline-level metadata for billing and auditing rather than per-flow progress or expectation metrics, so it cannot answer this event-level question.C. The Jobs API reports task and run status for jobs, not the flow-level `data_quality` metrics that only appear inside the pipeline event log\'s `flow_progress` events.D. The `event_log()` table-valued function reads the pipeline\'s default hidden event log table by pipeline ID with no advance setup, which matches the no-configuration-change requirement."},{"id":"17","question":"A platform team wants a 90-day trend report of job run durations and outcomes across every workspace in the same cloud region, computed with a single SQL query and joinable against `system.billing.usage` for cost attribution. They are deciding between querying `system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline` and polling the Jobs REST API `listruns` endpoint on a schedule to build this report. Which statements correctly describe the tradeoff? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Polling `listruns` on a schedule guarantees data availability within seconds of a run finishing, while `system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline` rows for that run may take up to an hour to appear.","B":"The `listruns` endpoint retains a full 365 days of run history by default with automatic regional replication, while `system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline` only retains the most recent 30 days before rows are purged.","C":"Repeatedly polling `listruns` for a 90-day window requires paging through and re-fetching runs already retrieved on a prior poll, since the endpoint has no native cursor for returning only rows changed since the last call.","D":"`system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline` aggregates run history across every workspace in the same region into one queryable table, which the `listruns` endpoint cannot do since it is scoped to a single workspace per call.","E":"`system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline` can be joined directly with `system.billing.usage` in ordinary SQL, whereas correlating REST API run objects with billing data requires the caller to write custom stitching logic."},"explanation":"A. Polling the endpoint on demand reflects a run\'s state as soon as it changes, while rows for a given run in the system table are documented to typically appear within about an hour rather than immediately.B. The retention relationship is reversed: the system table is documented to retain roughly 365 days of history, while the REST API is a live, point-in-time view of runs rather than a long-term, retention-guaranteed archive.C. The endpoint has no changed-since cursor, so a scheduled poll covering a rolling window has to re-request and re-filter runs it has already seen on earlier polls to detect what changed.F. The system table is documented to lag behind real-time state changes by up to about an hour, so it is not lower latency than a REST poll for any sub-minute polling schedule; the two approaches trade latency for aggregation and joinability in opposite directions.D. The system table is designed to aggregate data across workspaces sharing the same metastore region, giving one queryable surface for the whole region, while the REST endpoint only ever returns runs for the single workspace it is called against.E. Because both are SQL tables in the same catalog, they can be joined directly with a standard SQL join, whereas combining REST API JSON responses with billing usage rows requires the caller to extract, normalize, and stitch the data together outside of SQL.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"18","question":"A multi-task Lakeflow job runs five tasks on shared job clusters. An engineer needs a report showing the average `execution_duration_seconds` and `queue_duration_seconds` for each individual task over the last 30 days, not a single aggregate figure for the whole job run. Which system table should the query target?","options":{"A":"`system.lakeflow.jobs`, since it stores the trigger type and schedule configuration that determine how often each task executes.","B":"`system.lakeflow.job_task_run_timeline`, since it records per-task duration columns for both single-task and multi-task jobs.","C":"`system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline`, since its duration columns are populated for every job run regardless of how many tasks it contains.","D":"`system.lakeflow.job_tasks`, since it is a slowly changing dimension table that stores each task\'s currently configured timeout."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. This table holds job-level metadata like trigger type and schedule, not per-task or per-run duration figures, so it does not answer the reporting question.B. This table records per-task duration metrics for every job, and is the recommended source for multi-task jobs since it captures each task\'s own setup, queue, and execution duration.C. Its duration columns are documented as populated only for single-task jobs; for a multi-task job it does not give reliable per-task figures, so it does not satisfy the requirement.D. This table tracks task configuration such as dependencies and timeouts, not run-time duration metrics, so it cannot produce the requested average execution and queue times."},{"id":"19","question":"Your team migrated a set of legacy Databricks SQL alerts to the current alert editor to consolidate monitoring for governed business-metric dashboards. When reviewing alert history after the migration, which statement about the alert status model is accurate?","options":{"A":"Alerts report status as either Healthy or Unhealthy only, the terminology used for table health badges in Catalog Explorer.","B":"Alerts report status as Pending until a workspace admin manually acknowledges the notification, then the status moves to a final Resolved state.","C":"Alerts now report status as one of OK, Triggered, or Error, and the previously used Unknown status has been removed from the evaluation model.","D":"Alerts still report status as Unknown whenever the query returns no rows, exactly as it did before the alert editor was updated."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Healthy and Unhealthy are the status labels used by Lakehouse Monitoring anomaly detection for table health badges, not the status vocabulary used by SQL alerts themselves.B. SQL alerts do not have a manual-acknowledgement Pending/Resolved workflow; their status is derived automatically from evaluating the query condition on each scheduled run.C. The current alert editor evaluates each alert to OK, Triggered, or Error, and it explicitly no longer supports the Unknown status that legacy alerts used. This is the correct, up-to-date status model for monitoring governed metrics.D. Unknown was part of the legacy alert status model, not the current one. Carrying that assumption forward after a migration to the new editor leads teams to misinterpret alert history."},{"id":"20","question":"A `sales_events` table uses liquid clustering with clustering keys `region` and `customer_id`. A batch job writes a transaction that inserts roughly 40 MB of new rows into the table. No manual `OPTIMIZE` command runs afterward. What should the team expect about the physical layout of that newly written data?","options":{"A":"Liquid clustering only takes effect the first time `OPTIMIZE FULL` runs on the table, so this incremental write is stored exactly as raw, unclustered files just as it would have been before the table ever adopted clustering.","B":"Because two clustering columns are configured on this table, the write triggers an immediate rewrite of the entire existing dataset to keep the new rows co-located with matching historical data.","C":"The 40 MB write likely falls below the size threshold for clustering-on-write with two clustering columns on a Unity Catalog managed table, so the new files may not be clustered until a subsequent `OPTIMIZE` runs.","D":"Every write to a liquid-clustered table is clustered on write regardless of transaction size, so the 40 MB of new rows is always guaranteed to be laid out by `region` and `customer_id` immediately once the transaction commits."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. `OPTIMIZE FULL` is used to recluster all existing data the first time clustering keys are set or changed, but liquid clustering itself, including clustering-on-write behavior for sufficiently large transactions, is active from table creation and does not wait for a manual full optimize.B. Incremental clustering only rewrites the files necessary to accommodate new data relative to the current clustering keys; it does not force a rewrite of the entire historical table on every write, which is precisely what makes liquid clustering more efficient than repartitioning.C. Clustering-on-write only engages once accumulated new data crosses a size threshold that scales with the number of clustering columns; for two clustering columns on a Unity Catalog managed table that threshold is 256 MB, so a 40 MB transaction is likely to land unclustered until a later `OPTIMIZE` incrementally reorganizes it.D. Clustering on write is threshold-based rather than unconditional, so small writes below the applicable size threshold are not guaranteed to be clustered immediately; only writes at or above the threshold trigger clustering at write time."},{"id":"21","question":"A 400 GB `orders` table is filtered heavily on `customer_id`, a high-cardinality column with a skewed distribution: a small number of customers account for a large share of the rows. The nightly `OPTIMIZE ... ZORDER BY (customer_id)` job is taking longer every week as the table grows. Which change should the team make?","options":{"A":"Repartition the table with `PARTITIONED BY (customer_id)` so each customer\'s rows live in a dedicated set of partition files.","B":"Recreate the table with `CLUSTER BY (customer_id)` so the file layout is maintained incrementally as the skewed data grows.","C":"Switch to `CLUSTER BY AUTO` with no explicit key so Databricks avoids clustering on the skewed `customer_id` column entirely.","D":"Add `order_date` alongside `customer_id` in the `OPTIMIZE ... ZORDER BY` clause to spread the rewrite work across two columns."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Partitioning by a high-cardinality, skewed column like `customer_id` creates an excessive number of small partitions, which drives up file-listing overhead and small-file problems instead of fixing the rewrite cost.B. Liquid clustering with `customer_id` declared as a key incrementally maintains the layout as new data arrives, avoiding the full-table rewrite cost that a growing skewed table incurs under Z-ORDER.C. Given how heavily queries filter on `customer_id`, automatic clustering would likely select that same column as a key rather than avoid it, so this does not describe expected behavior.D. Adding a second Z-ORDER column does not solve the underlying cost problem, since a Z-ORDER `OPTIMIZE` still rewrites the full set of touched files on every run regardless of how many columns are listed."},{"id":"22","question":"A pipeline uses Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines\' AUTO CDC functionality to apply row-level changes read via Change Data Feed from `crm.customers` into a downstream SCD Type 2 dimension table, where late-arriving events must be applied in correct logical order and deletes must be reflected in the dimension\'s history. Which configuration choices are correct?(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Omit `apply_as_deletes` entirely, since AUTO CDC automatically infers a delete from any row where `_change_type` equals `update_preimage` without further configuration.","B":"Set `apply_as_deletes` to the condition that identifies delete events from the feed, so that deleted source rows are tombstoned and excluded from the current view of the dimension.","C":"Set `stored_as_scd_type` to `1` to preserve full history automatically, since Type 1 tracking creates a new versioned row in the target table for every update it receives.","D":"Set `sequence_by` to the `_commit_version` column, or a business timestamp, so out-of-order change events apply in true logical order rather than arrival order.","E":"Set `stored_as_scd_type` to `2` so the target table retains a full, queryable history of prior values for each key instead of overwriting the row in place on every update."},"explanation":"A. AUTO CDC does not infer deletes automatically from any `_change_type` value, including `update_preimage`, which simply represents the pre-change state of an ordinary update. Deletes must be identified explicitly through the `apply_as_deletes` parameter or they will not be applied.B. Explicitly configuring `apply_as_deletes` with the condition that identifies delete events is the documented way to make AUTO CDC tombstone those rows in the target table. Without this configuration, delete events from the source would not be reflected downstream at all.C. SCD Type 1 is the in-place update mode that overwrites the existing row with no retained history, which is the opposite of what preserving full history requires. The dimension table in this scenario needs Type 2, not Type 1, to meet its stated history requirement.F. `keys` identifies the column or columns that uniquely match an incoming change event to an existing target row, not a partitioning hint. Using a non-unique column like `region` would cause unrelated customer records to be incorrectly matched and overwritten.D. `sequence_by` tells AUTO CDC how to determine the true logical order of events, which is essential when events can arrive out of order. Using the commit version or a business timestamp ensures a late-arriving older change does not incorrectly overwrite a newer one.E. SCD Type 2 is the configuration that tells AUTO CDC to retain historical versions of each row rather than updating in place, which is exactly the historical tracking the dimension table requires. This is the correct setting for the stated requirement.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"23","question":"A streaming table ingests high-volume clickstream events and is queried concurrently by many downstream jobs filtering on `user_id` (extremely high cardinality) and `event_type` (low cardinality, about 20 values). Query patterns shift week to week as new dashboards are added. Which characteristics make liquid clustering, configured with `CLUSTER BY AUTO`, a better fit than a legacy layout that partitions by `event_type` and Z-Orders by `user_id`? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Liquid clustering guarantees that every query filtering on `user_id` reads exactly one data file, eliminating file listing overhead completely and permanently.","B":"`CLUSTER BY AUTO` removes the need for Databricks to ever examine historical query patterns, since clustering keys are fixed once at table creation time.","C":"Databricks can automatically change the clustering keys as the observed query workload shifts, without requiring the table to be dropped and recreated.","D":"Databricks can automatically change the clustering keys as the query workload shifts over time, without ever dropping and recreating the underlying table.","E":"`OPTIMIZE` reclusters only the data that needs it incrementally, instead of requiring a full table rewrite every time new clickstream events are appended."},"explanation":"A. This overstates the guarantee; liquid clustering improves data skipping through better file organization, but it does not guarantee single-file reads for every filtered query.B. This is backwards; `CLUSTER BY AUTO` relies on Databricks continuously analyzing query history to decide whether and how to adjust clustering keys over time.F. Z-Order statistics are only maintained within each partition, so the optimizer cannot use them to skip data across `event_type` partition boundaries, limiting global data skipping.C. This is accurate: `CLUSTER BY AUTO` lets Databricks pick and revise clustering keys based on observed query history as access patterns evolve, without a table rebuild.D. This is accurate: without fixed partition directories, concurrent writers are not funneled into contention over a small set of `event_type` folders the way rigid partitioning would force.E. This is accurate: standard `OPTIMIZE` on a liquid-clustered table only rewrites data that needs reclustering, rather than rewriting the whole table on every run like a full Z-Order pass would.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"24","question":"A metastore admin runs `GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON SCHEMA finance.reports TO data_engineers;` to simplify onboarding a team. Which privilege is NOT included in this grant, and must be granted separately if the team needs it?","options":{"A":"`CREATE TABLE`, which controls the ability to create new managed or external tables inside the schema, and is intentionally excluded from `ALL PRIVILEGES` grants.","B":"`USE SCHEMA`, which controls the ability to reference any object inside the schema, and is intentionally excluded from `ALL PRIVILEGES` grants.","C":"`MANAGE`, which controls the ability to change permissions, transfer ownership, or delete the schema, and is intentionally excluded from `ALL PRIVILEGES` grants.","D":"`SELECT`, which controls the ability to query rows from tables and views inside the schema, and is intentionally excluded from `ALL PRIVILEGES` grants."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. `CREATE TABLE` is included when `ALL PRIVILEGES` is granted on a schema, since it is one of the ordinary creation privileges the grant is meant to bundle together.B. `USE SCHEMA` is included when `ALL PRIVILEGES` is granted, since usage on the schema itself is one of the baseline privileges the bundled grant covers.C. `ALL PRIVILEGES` grants every applicable privilege on the schema except a small set of administrative privileges, and `MANAGE` (along with `READ METADATA`, `EXTERNAL USE SCHEMA`, and `EXTERNAL USE LOCATION`) is deliberately excluded so that broad data grants do not automatically hand out administrative control.D. `SELECT` is a standard data-access privilege and is included when `ALL PRIVILEGES` is granted on a schema, so it does not need to be granted separately."},{"id":"25","question":"An engineering team is evaluating how attribute-based access control (ABAC) governed tags and policies behave across a Unity Catalog metastore before rolling them out broadly. Which of the following statements are accurate? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"A DENY policy always takes precedence over every conflicting GRANT statement, regardless of which specific privilege the DENY or GRANT statement covers or references.","B":"A single ABAC policy attached at the catalog level automatically extends to new tables created later in that catalog, as long as those tables carry the tag the policy\'s `WHEN` clause matches.","C":"Governed tags applied at the catalog level propagate down to schemas and tables beneath it, so a lower-level object inherits the tag unless a more specific tag overrides it closer to the object.","D":"A tag applied to a table does not automatically apply to that table\'s columns, so column-level ABAC policies require the columns themselves to carry a matching tag.","E":"A GRANT policy defined through ABAC can revoke access that a principal was already given through a separate, direct `GRANT` statement issued outside of that policy."},"explanation":"A. This statement is inaccurate: DENY policies are currently limited in scope to the `MANAGE ACCESS CONTROL` privilege, so they do not universally override every GRANT regardless of which privilege is involved.F. This statement is inaccurate: row filter and column mask policies apply to table-level securables such as tables, materialized views, and streaming tables, not to volumes or registered models.B. This statement is accurate: attaching a policy at the catalog level lets it apply automatically to any table that later acquires the matching tag, which is the core scaling benefit of ABAC over manually attaching filters table by table.C. This statement is accurate: tags follow the catalog-to-schema-to-table hierarchy by default, so an object inherits a tag from its parent unless a more specific tag is set closer to that object.D. This statement is accurate: tags do not flow from a table down to its columns, so a column-level policy condition only matches columns that have been tagged directly, even if the parent table carries a matching tag.E. This statement is inaccurate: ABAC GRANT policies only add privileges when their tag condition is met, and documentation is explicit that they cannot revoke access a principal already holds through a direct grant made outside the policy.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C","D"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"26","question":"In Unity Catalog, what is the fundamental difference between how a row filter and a column mask affect the result of a query against a table containing PII?","options":{"A":"A row filter is a function evaluated per row that excludes the row from the result set when it returns `false`, while a column mask is a function that transforms the value of one column, leaving the row itself present.","B":"A row filter transforms the values returned for a specific column, while a column mask instead excludes entire rows from the result set outright, which is essentially the reverse of what their actual names describe here.","C":"A row filter can only be enforced on streaming tables and materialized views, while a column mask can only be enforced on tables that are queried in batch through a SQL warehouse connection.","D":"A row filter is enforced once, at the moment data is first written into the table, while a column mask is enforced only later, when the table happens to be accessed through Delta Sharing."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. This matches how the two mechanisms are defined: a row filter is a function returning a boolean that hides whole rows failing the condition, while a column mask is a function that substitutes or transforms the value shown for one column, leaving the row present.B. This swaps the two mechanisms\' actual behavior — row filters act on whole rows and column masks act on individual column values, not the other way around as this option describes.C. Row filters and column masks are not restricted to only streaming tables versus only batch tables; both mechanisms can be applied across the table types Unity Catalog supports for this feature, so this distinction does not hold.D. Both row filters and column masks are query-time controls evaluated when a table is read, not one-time write-time transformations or controls limited to Delta Sharing access paths."},{"id":"27","question":"An analytics team is only permitted to study workforce age distribution in bands (e.g. \\"born in the 1990s\\") and must never see an employee\'s exact `date_of_birth`, while HR staff still need the full date for benefits administration. The engineer must implement this with a single Unity Catalog column mask on `date_of_birth`. Which masking approach satisfies both requirements?","options":{"A":"Have the mask return `sha2(CAST(date_of_birth AS STRING), 256)` for everyone outside HR so the exact date is pseudonymized into a fixed-length hash.","B":"Have the mask look up `date_of_birth` in a separate token vault table and return the matching token string for everyone outside HR.","C":"Have the mask return `NULL` for everyone outside HR so `date_of_birth` is fully suppressed and no birth-year information reaches the analytics team at all.","D":"Have the mask return `YEAR(date_of_birth)` cast back to the column\'s date type for everyone outside HR, generalizing the value to birth year only."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. A cryptographic hash of the date produces an opaque fixed-length string that cannot be grouped into meaningful birth-year bands or cast back to a date, so it does not give the analytics team the age information they are supposed to have.B. Token-vault lookups are built for reversible pseudonymization of identifiers like account or card numbers, not for producing a coarser, still-analyzable version of a date; the analytics team would receive an opaque token instead of a usable birth-year signal.C. Returning `NULL` suppresses the value entirely, which satisfies the privacy requirement but destroys the birth-year information the analytics team was explicitly granted access to. Suppression is too aggressive for this requirement.D. Reducing the date to its year component is generalization: it keeps exactly the granularity the analytics team is authorized to see for age-band studies while hiding the exact day and month, and HR still receives the untouched value through the mask\'s conditional logic."},{"id":"28","question":"A search team is evaluating how tags and comments surface in Catalog Explorer search results. Which statements correctly describe this discoverability behavior in Unity Catalog?(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Tag-based search in Unity Catalog requires an exact match on the tag key and value, rather than performing partial or fuzzy matching","B":"Tags applied at the catalog level are queryable through `INFORMATION_SCHEMA` views such as `CATALOG_TAGS`, supporting programmatic discovery","C":"Comments are visible only to users holding the `MODIFY` privilege on a table, so read-only analysts never see them in search results","D":"Catalog Explorer performs fuzzy, typo-tolerant matching on tag values by default, so a search for `financ` also surfaces every object tagged `finance`","E":"Any user holding the `BROWSE` privilege on an object can view its comments, even without broader read access to the underlying data"},"explanation":"A. Tag search matches the key and value exactly rather than doing partial or fuzzy text matching, so a search term must correspond precisely to the stored tag.B. Catalog-level tags are exposed through `INFORMATION_SCHEMA` views like `CATALOG_TAGS`, which lets teams query tag assignments programmatically instead of browsing the UI object by object.C. Viewing a comment only requires `BROWSE`, a much lower bar than the `MODIFY` privilege, so read-only analysts with browse access can still see comments in search results.D. Tag matching in Unity Catalog is exact, not fuzzy or typo-tolerant, so a partial term like `financ` would not surface objects tagged `finance`.F. Tags are indexed and surfaced by Catalog Explorer\'s search, aiding discoverability directly, rather than existing solely as an access-control mechanism.E. `BROWSE` is deliberately a lighter-weight privilege than full read access, and it is sufficient on its own to let a user view an object\'s comment, supporting discovery without granting data access.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"29","question":"A Structured Streaming job\'s micro-batches have started taking roughly eight times longer than usual. In the Spark UI, the engineer opens the Stages tab for the slow batch and finds a stage with a long tail: 199 of 200 tasks finish in under 10 seconds, but one task runs for over 20 minutes and shows a much larger `Shuffle Read Size` than the others. Which of the following are appropriate next steps to confirm and address this using the Spark UI? (Select all that apply)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Enable adaptive query execution\'s skew join optimization, or increase `spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`, so the oversized partition splits across more tasks.","B":"Restart the cluster with a larger driver node type, since driver memory pressure is the only possible cause of a single slow shuffle task.","C":"Switch the job from Structured Streaming to a batch job, since only batch jobs expose shuffle read metrics on the Stages tab.","D":"Open the Executors tab to check whether the executor running the slow task is under GC pauses or memory pressure that would compound the skew.","E":"Open the associated SQL/DataFrame tab to inspect the physical plan and confirm which operator produced the oversized shuffle partition."},"explanation":"A. Adaptive query execution\'s skew join handling, or a higher shuffle partition count, directly targets an oversized shuffle partition by splitting it into smaller pieces so no single task carries a disproportionate share of the data.B. Driver memory pressure affects planning and coordination, not the size of an individual executor task\'s shuffle partition, so a larger driver node would not address a single skewed reducer task.C. Structured Streaming micro-batches run as ordinary Spark jobs and expose the same Stages tab with per-task shuffle metrics as batch jobs, so this claim about batch-only visibility is incorrect.F. Speculative execution reruns slow tasks on other executors as a mitigation, but disabling it removes that safety net without doing anything to rebalance the skewed partition itself, so the underlying skew remains.D. Checking the executor running the outlier task for GC pauses or memory pressure is a standard Spark UI workflow: a struggling executor can turn ordinary skew into an extreme long tail, and this rules that factor in or out before changing the job.E. The physical plan in the SQL/DataFrame tab identifies exactly which shuffle-producing operator (such as a join or aggregation) created the oversized partition, which is necessary before deciding how to rewrite or reconfigure the query.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"30","question":"A data engineer is repairing a failed job run through the Jobs API `repairrun` endpoint and wants to correctly reason about the request before sending it. Which statements about `repairrun` behavior are accurate? (Select 3.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Values supplied in `job_parameters` for the repair request take precedence over the parameter values that were used in the original failed run.","B":"Setting `rerun_all_failed_tasks` to true tells the service to re-execute every task that did not complete successfully in the prior attempt.","C":"The `latest_repair_id` field must reference the most recent repair attempt on that run when the request is itself repairing an already-repaired run.","D":"Task parameters such as `notebook_params` can only be overridden the first time a run is repaired and are locked for every subsequent repair attempt.","E":"Every call to `repairrun` permanently overwrites the job\'s default parameter values, so later unrelated runs also start using the repaired values."},"explanation":"A. Correct: parameters passed in the repair request override the values from the original attempt for the tasks being re-executed, which is exactly how the Repair job run dialog\'s override behavior works when driven through the API.B. Correct: this boolean flag reruns every task that did not succeed in the prior attempt, which is the API equivalent of letting the service auto-detect failed and canceled tasks rather than listing them manually.C. Incorrect: `latest_repair_id` is only required as a safeguard when repairing a run that has already been repaired at least once, so it does not need to reference anything on a run\'s very first repair attempt.D. Incorrect: task parameters can be overridden on any repair attempt, and clearing an overridden field on a later repair returns that parameter to its original value rather than the setting being locked after one use.E. Incorrect: a repair request only changes the parameter values used for that specific repaired run; it does not mutate the job definition\'s stored default parameters used by future runs.F. Correct: `rerun_tasks` accepts specific task keys so the caller can target exactly which tasks to re-execute, as an alternative to the automatic failed-task or dependent-task detection flags.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"31","question":"An engineer suspects that one particular executor is hanging during a long-running stage, since every task assigned to it never completes while tasks on other executors finish normally. Which Spark UI actions let the engineer inspect the live JVM thread state of that specific executor to identify exactly where it is stuck?","options":{"A":"Open the Executors tab, locate the row for that executor\'s ID, and click the link in its Thread Dump column to capture a snapshot of every thread\'s current state.","B":"Open the Stages tab, expand the stuck stage\'s task table, and click the Logs link on the hanging task\'s row to stream its executor\'s live application log output.","C":"Open the SQL tab, select the physical plan node currently executing on that executor, and click Explain to render the Catalyst plan for the running operator.","D":"Open the Jobs tab, click into the DAG visualization for the running job, and hover over the stuck stage\'s node to display that executor\'s current CPU and memory gauges."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. The Executors tab exposes a Thread Dump link per executor that captures a live snapshot of every thread\'s stack trace and state on that JVM, which is exactly the mechanism designed for identifying where a specific executor is stuck.B. A task-level Logs link streams stdout/stderr application log lines for that task, but log output only shows what the code explicitly printed; it does not capture the live thread stack state needed to see exactly where execution is currently blocked.C. The SQL tab\'s Explain option renders the logical or physical query plan for an operator; it describes what the query intends to compute, not the live runtime thread state of a particular executor process.D. The DAG visualization on the Jobs tab shows stage dependencies and progress, and hovering over a node does not surface per-executor CPU/memory gauges or thread stack traces for diagnosing a hang."},{"id":"32","question":"A team\'s Databricks workspace is on Azure, and their code repository lives in Azure Repos (Azure DevOps). They configure a CI/CD pipeline that uses a Microsoft Entra ID access token to authenticate the Git integration used by their production Git folder, expecting the same OAuth-based flow that works for GitHub. The Git folder update fails to authenticate. What should the team do instead?","options":{"A":"Reconfigure the production Git folder to use the Jobs API instead of the Repos API, since only the Jobs API supports Entra ID token authentication.","B":"Generate an Azure DevOps personal access token, since Databricks Git folders do not accept Microsoft Entra ID tokens for this integration.","C":"Switch the workspace\'s cloud provider to AWS, since Azure-hosted workspaces cannot integrate with any external Git provider through Git folders.","D":"Disable Unity Catalog on the workspace, since enabled catalogs block Git folder authentication for any non-GitHub Git provider."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. The Jobs API manages job definitions and runs; it is not the interface used to authenticate or synchronize a Git folder with a remote repository, so switching to it would not resolve an authentication failure.B. Azure DevOps is a documented exception: Databricks Git folders require an Azure DevOps personal access token for this integration and do not accept Microsoft Entra ID tokens, unlike the OAuth flow available for providers such as GitHub.C. The cloud the workspace runs on does not determine which Git providers it can integrate with; Azure-hosted workspaces can link to GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, and other supported providers just as workspaces on other clouds can.D. Unity Catalog governs data and object access within the workspace and has no bearing on which token type a Git folder accepts when authenticating to an external Git provider."},{"id":"33","question":"A bundle defines:\\n```\\nvariables:\\nwarehouse_id:\\ndescription: SQL warehouse used by the nightly job\\ndefault: abc123\\n```\\nand a job task references `${var.warehouse_id}`. A CI pipeline needs to deploy to a staging target using a different warehouse ID on every run, without editing `databricks.yml` or committing a target-specific override file. Which approach satisfies this requirement?","options":{"A":"Pass the value with `databricks bundle deploy --profile warehouse_id=<id>`, since the `--profile` flag accepts arbitrary key-value pairs that override any variable declared in the bundle configuration.","B":"Edit the `variable-overrides.json` file checked into the target\'s `.databricks/bundle` directory on every run, since that file always takes precedence over environment variables and CLI flags.","C":"Export an environment variable named `BUNDLE_VAR_warehouse_id` set to the desired ID before invoking `databricks bundle deploy`, since CLI variable resolution checks that environment prefix ahead of the declared default.","D":"Add a `lookup` block under the `warehouse_id` variable definition so the CLI resolves the correct SQL warehouse object by name from the workspace at deploy time instead of using a static ID."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. The `--profile` flag selects a named Databricks CLI authentication profile; it does not accept key-value pairs for overriding bundle variables, so this would not affect `warehouse_id` at all.B. A committed overrides file is lower precedence than an environment variable in the CLI\'s resolution order, and it would also require a file edit and commit on every run, which the requirement explicitly rules out.C. The `BUNDLE_VAR_<name>` environment variable convention is read by the CLI ahead of a variable\'s declared default, so exporting it per CI run supplies a fresh warehouse ID each time without touching any committed file.D. A `lookup` block resolves an object\'s ID by looking it up by name in the workspace; it solves a different problem than supplying a caller-chosen literal value per run and would not let CI inject an arbitrary ID."},{"id":"34","question":"A team builds a standalone `daily_revenue_by_region` materialized view on top of an hourly-updating `fact_sales` table. Stakeholders want the view to reflect new source data within a few minutes of it landing, but they do not want a pipeline running on a fixed timer when no new data has arrived, since that would waste serverless compute. Which materialized view refresh configuration best meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Create the materialized view as ad-hoc with no schedule or trigger, and rely on analysts to run `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW` manually whenever they notice the dashboard numbers look stale.","B":"Create the materialized view with `SCHEDULE CRON` set to run every minute, guaranteeing the shortest possible lag between any source change and a refreshed view, regardless of whether new data actually arrived.","C":"Create the materialized view without any refresh clause and query it directly, since materialized views recompute their results live at query time like a standard view does.","D":"Create the materialized view with `TRIGGER ON UPDATE` so a refresh is automatically scheduled only when the underlying source tables actually change, avoiding unnecessary compute when `fact_sales` is idle."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. An ad-hoc materialized view only refreshes when someone explicitly runs the refresh command, so freshness depends entirely on an analyst remembering to trigger it. That does not reliably deliver updates within a few minutes of new data landing.B. A per-minute cron schedule does refresh quickly, but it runs on a fixed timer even when `fact_sales` has not changed, which is exactly the wasted-compute pattern the team wants to avoid. Trigger-based refresh reacts to actual changes instead.C. A materialized view always physically stores its results and only updates them on refresh; it does not recompute live at query time the way a standard view does. Querying it without ever refreshing would just return stale, previously materialized data.D. The `TRIGGER ON UPDATE` refresh mode watches the source tables and schedules a refresh only when they actually change, which delivers near-real-time freshness without running compute on a fixed cadence while the source is idle. This matches both the freshness and the cost-avoidance requirement."},{"id":"35","question":"A platform team manages hundreds of Unity Catalog managed Delta tables and does not have the capacity to manually choose and periodically revisit clustering keys as BI dashboards evolve. They want Databricks to observe query history and select clustering keys automatically, changing them only when the switch is expected to pay for itself in reduced maintenance cost. Which configuration satisfies this requirement?","options":{"A":"Create or alter the tables with `CLUSTER BY AUTO` so Databricks Runtime 15.4 LTS or later analyzes each table\'s query history and updates clustering keys only when the savings outweigh the reclustering cost.","B":"Set the table property `delta.autoOptimize.autoCompact = true` on each table so Delta Lake automatically infers and rewrites clustering keys during every small write to keep files evenly sized.","C":"Configure a nightly job that runs `ANALYZE TABLE COMPUTE STATISTICS` on each table and manually swaps the `CLUSTER BY` columns whenever the column statistics distribution changes significantly.","D":"Enable predictive optimization at the catalog level so Databricks schedules `OPTIMIZE FULL` on a fixed interval, which re-evaluates and reselects the most selective columns for `ZORDER BY` on each scheduled run."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. This is exactly the automatic clustering-key selection feature: Databricks analyzes the workload against the table and only changes keys when doing so is expected to be cost-effective, requiring no manual key management.B. Auto compaction controls small-file bin-packing behavior on write, but it does not select or change clustering keys, so it does not meet the requirement to automate key selection.C. This describes a manually built and maintained job that inspects statistics and swaps keys by hand, which is the opposite of the hands-off, workload-aware automation the team wants.D. Predictive optimization schedules maintenance jobs like `OPTIMIZE` and `VACUUM`, and `ZORDER BY` is a separate, non-adaptive indexing technique used with partitioned tables, not an automatic clustering-key selector."}]},{"id":"databricks-generative-ai-engineer-associate","title":"Databricks Certified Generative AI Engineer Associate","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"A legal operations team wants to pull specific fields (contract start date, renewal term, counterparty name) out of thousands of scanned PDF contracts and load the results into a Delta table with a fixed schema. Which model task should the application be built around?","options":{"A":"Multi-class classification that assigns each contract to a single category label","B":"Structured information extraction against a defined schema","C":"Open-ended text generation to draft a plain-language summary of each contract","D":"Multi-turn chat that lets a user ask follow-up questions about one contract at a time"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Classification assigns each document to one of a set of predefined labels, which does not recover the specific field values the team needs to populate table columns.B. Pulling named fields such as dates, terms, and party names into a fixed schema is exactly what structured information extraction (e.g. `ai_extract`) is designed for, and it maps directly onto a Delta table with fixed columns.C. Generation would produce free-form prose summaries, which do not give the fixed, queryable columns (start date, term, counterparty) the team needs for the Delta table.D. A chat interface answers ad hoc questions interactively but does not batch-produce structured field values across thousands of documents for a table load."},{"id":"2","question":"A generative AI engineer is building a tool-calling agent for a claims-processing use case. The agent has three tools: `lookup_claim_details`, `check_policy_coverage`, and `issue_payment`. Payments must never be issued for claims that fail coverage verification. How should the engineer define and order these tools for the agent\'s multi-stage reasoning loop?","options":{"A":"Constrain `issue_payment` so it only runs after `check_policy_coverage` approves the claim, and write tool descriptions that lead the agent to call lookup, then coverage check, then payment in that order.","B":"Let the agent call `issue_payment` first as the main objective, then invoke `lookup_claim_details` and `check_policy_coverage` afterward purely to produce an audit log of the completed transaction.","C":"Give all three tools identical generic descriptions so the LLM treats them as interchangeable, and let it invoke `issue_payment` and `check_policy_coverage` in parallel to cut latency.","D":"Drop `check_policy_coverage` from the tool set entirely and depend on a human reviewer to catch any improperly paid claims after the payment has already settled."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Constraining the payment tool to run only after coverage approval, and describing the tools so the agent naturally sequences lookup, then verification, then action, matches Databricks guidance that consequential actions in a multi-stage reasoning loop should be gated behind the verification step they depend on. This prevents the model from reaching an irreversible action tool before the required check has succeeded.B. Calling the payment tool before verifying coverage defeats the purpose of the coverage check, since the payment would already be issued by the time the audit-log calls run. This ordering allows an unapproved claim to be paid, which is exactly the failure mode the design must prevent.C. Identical generic tool descriptions remove the signal the agent needs to reason about which tool serves which purpose, and running the payment and coverage-check tools in parallel means the payment can complete before the coverage result is even known. This creates a race condition that can pay out non-covered claims.D. Removing the coverage-check tool eliminates the automated gate entirely, so every claim would be paid regardless of eligibility, with correctness pushed onto a reviewer who only sees the claim after money has already moved. This does not satisfy the requirement that payments never occur for claims failing verification."},{"id":"3","question":"An engineer builds a RAG pipeline over long onboarding guides. During testing, they notice that a key instruction spanning the end of one chunk and the beginning of the next is never retrieved in full: the retriever returns one chunk or the other, but neither chunk alone contains the complete instruction. Which adjustment to the chunking configuration most directly addresses this failure?","options":{"A":"Increase the total number of chunks retrieved at query time so the retriever returns more candidate chunks per query.","B":"Switch the embedding model to one trained specifically on the onboarding guide\'s domain vocabulary.","C":"Sort the chunks by document order before indexing so neighboring chunks sit next to each other in the vector index.","D":"Configure adjacent chunks to overlap by a set number of tokens so boundary content appears in more than one chunk."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Returning more candidate chunks increases the chance both neighboring chunks are retrieved together, but neither chunk individually contains the complete instruction, so the response context is still fragmented across two partial chunks.B. A domain-tuned embedding model can improve semantic matching for retrieval, but it does not change where chunk boundaries fall, so the instruction would still be split across two chunks.C. Physical storage order in the vector index does not affect which chunks are returned for a similarity search, so this does not help the retriever surface the complete instruction.D. Adding overlap between adjacent chunks means content near a boundary is duplicated into both chunks, so an instruction that straddles the boundary will appear in full inside at least one chunk and can be retrieved intact."},{"id":"4","question":"A team writes new batches of chunked text into their Unity Catalog Delta table once per night via a scheduled job, and they want the AI Search index to refresh once after each nightly batch completes rather than continuously watching the table for changes. Which index configuration achieves this?","options":{"A":"Create a Direct Vector Access Index and call its upsert API from the nightly job so the index updates in lockstep with the batch write, bypassing the Delta table sync mechanism entirely.","B":"Create the Delta Sync Index without specifying `pipeline_type` and rely on the default hourly cron schedule, since Delta Sync indexes always poll the source table once per hour regardless of configuration.","C":"Create the Delta Sync Index with `pipeline_type=\\"TRIGGERED\\"` so the index only syncs when explicitly triggered or scheduled after each nightly write completes.","D":"Create the Delta Sync Index with `pipeline_type=\\"CONTINUOUS\\"` so the index runs on a dedicated always-on cluster that watches the Delta table and syncs immediately as each night\'s rows are committed."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. A direct access index requires the application to manage embeddings and upserts itself via API calls rather than syncing from a Delta table, which abandons the Delta table-driven sync workflow the team already has in place.B. A Delta Sync Index does not fall back to a fixed hourly schedule when `pipeline_type` is unspecified; the pipeline type must be explicitly chosen to control whether syncing is triggered or continuous.C. A triggered pipeline type syncs the index on demand or on a schedule the team controls, which matches a workflow where new data lands once nightly and the index only needs to refresh after that batch is written.D. A continuous pipeline type keeps compute running at all times to sync near-instantly as changes occur, which is unnecessary and more costly for a workload that only produces new data once per night."},{"id":"5","question":"A team is preparing a source corpus by scraping publicly available community forum posts to supplement an internal knowledge base for a RAG assistant. Some forum posts contain personal contact details and occasional offensive language. Before these documents are added as knowledge sources, what should the team do to protect application quality and safety?","options":{"A":"Store the forum posts in a separate Delta table so they are queried less frequently than the internal knowledge base","B":"Run the scraped documents through classifiers to detect and filter out personal information and harmful language before ingestion","C":"Ingest the forum posts as-is and rely on the LLM\'s guardrails at inference time to avoid repeating any sensitive content","D":"Shorten the chunk size used for the forum posts so any sensitive text is split across multiple smaller chunks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Placing the forum posts in a separate table that is queried less often reduces but does not eliminate the chance that sensitive or harmful content is retrieved and surfaced to users. The unfiltered content remains available as a knowledge source.B. Applying classifiers to detect personal information and harmful language during source preparation directly prevents sensitive or offensive content from entering the corpus at all. This matches the guidance that sensitive information and harmful language in source documents should be filtered using classifiers before ingestion.C. Relying on inference-time guardrails does not stop the retriever from surfacing the sensitive content as retrieved context, and the guardrail could still fail to catch every instance. The underlying risk originates in the source data, so filtering needs to happen during preparation rather than after retrieval.D. Splitting sensitive text across smaller chunks does not remove personal information or offensive language from the corpus; the content is still present and retrievable, just fragmented. Chunk size affects retrieval granularity, not content safety."},{"id":"6","question":"A technical support search tool must return the single correct troubleshooting article at the very top of the results, since agents only read the first couple of results before acting. Which metric best evaluates this requirement?","options":{"A":"Precision@k evaluated at a small k, such as precision@3, to confirm that nearly every top result is relevant","B":"Recall@k evaluated at a large k, such as recall@50, to confirm that all relevant articles exist somewhere in the results","C":"Average relevance score computed across the entire retrieved candidate set, not just the top results","D":"Relevance distribution across all graded buckets, to check the overall spread of result quality"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Precision at a small k directly measures whether the handful of results an agent actually reads are relevant, which matches a scenario where exact top-of-list accuracy matters more than exhaustive coverage.B. Recall at a large k answers whether relevant articles exist anywhere in a broad candidate pool, which does not address whether the top one or two results the agent will actually read are correct.C. An average relevance score across the whole candidate set dilutes the signal from the top positions with lower-ranked results the agent will never see, so it does not isolate top-of-list accuracy.D. A relevance distribution describes how graded scores are spread across all retrieved results, which is a diagnostic overview rather than a targeted measure of whether the very top result is correct."},{"id":"7","question":"During evaluation, a RAG engineer notices that answers referencing information near a chunk boundary are frequently incomplete, because the fact needed to answer the question is split across two adjacent chunks. Which change to the chunking configuration best addresses this specific problem?","options":{"A":"Switch from a paragraph-based splitter to a fixed-character splitter to produce more uniform boundaries.","B":"Reduce the chunk size so each chunk covers a narrower span of the source document than before.","C":"Increase the number of chunks retrieved per query so more candidate chunks reach the language model.","D":"Configure the chunker to carry a window of trailing sentences into the start of the next chunk."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Switching to a fixed-character splitter changes where boundaries fall but does not add continuity across them, so facts can still be split between chunks just as before, only at different points in the text.B. Shrinking the chunk size increases the number of boundaries in the document, which makes it more likely, not less, that a given fact will end up split across two separate chunks.C. Retrieving more chunks increases the chance that a second chunk with the missing fact is also returned, but it does not guarantee it, and it adds noise and cost to the prompt without directly fixing the boundary-splitting issue.D. Carrying a window of trailing sentences from one chunk into the next, known as chunk overlap, ensures continuity across the boundary so a fact split between two chunks is likely to appear intact in at least one of them."},{"id":"8","question":"During retrieval quality evaluation, an engineer runs the same set of test queries through the pipeline twice — once with the reranker disabled and once with it enabled — and compares DCG@10 scores for each query type. For one query type, enabling the reranker raises DCG@10 significantly with only a modest latency increase; for another query type, it makes almost no difference. What is the most appropriate way to use this evaluation result?","options":{"A":"Disable the reranker everywhere, since a query type with no DCG@10 improvement proves the reranker doesn\'t help this retrieval pipeline overall","B":"Discard the DCG@10 results entirely, since re-ranking quality can only be judged using recall@k rather than any ranking-order-sensitive metric","C":"Enable the reranker for the query types where it measurably improves DCG@10 within the latency budget, and skip it where the gain doesn\'t justify the cost","D":"Enable the reranker everywhere, since any nonzero DCG@10 improvement on any query type justifies applying it uniformly across all traffic"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. One query type showing little improvement doesn\'t invalidate the significant DCG@10 gain observed for the other query type. Disabling the reranker everywhere would throw away a measured quality improvement that the evaluation clearly demonstrated for at least part of the traffic.B. DCG@10 is a ranking-order-sensitive metric that is well suited to measuring exactly what reranking changes: the order of results within the top-k. Recall@k measures whether relevant items appear at all in the candidate set, which doesn\'t capture the reordering benefit reranking provides.C. Comparing DCG@10 with and without the reranker per query type is exactly how retrieval quality evaluation is meant to guide a rollout decision: enable reranking where it delivers a real, latency-affordable quality gain, and leave it off where the benefit doesn\'t clear that bar. This targeted approach makes the best use of the evaluation data collected.D. Applying the reranker uniformly ignores the finding that one query type saw almost no benefit; enabling it there still adds latency cost without a corresponding quality payoff. The evaluation data specifically shows the impact varies by query type, so a blanket decision discards that signal."},{"id":"9","question":"A knowledge base built from scraped HTML product pages returns chunks full of navigation menu links, cookie-consent text, and sidebar ads whenever a RAG chatbot answers customer questions. Which data preparation approach best reduces this extraneous content before the pages are chunked for embedding?","options":{"A":"Re-rank retrieved chunks with a cross-encoder so that navigation and ad chunks are pushed further down the results list before the chatbot composes an answer.","B":"Parse the HTML with a library that isolates the main article content and strips navigation, ad, and boilerplate elements before extracting text.","C":"Keep the raw HTML text as-is and rely on the LLM\'s instruction-following ability to ignore irrelevant navigation-menu, cookie-consent, and sidebar-ad sections at answer time.","D":"Split each page into very small fixed-size chunks so that ad and navigation text ends up isolated from the article body, then remove chunks containing only boilerplate."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Re-ranking retrieved chunks with a cross-encoder only changes the order of chunks already retrieved; it does not remove navigation and ad chunks from the index. Noisy chunks can still surface for some queries and continue to degrade RAG output.B. Correct. Parsing the HTML with a library that isolates the main article content and strips navigation, ad, and boilerplate elements before extracting text removes the extraneous content before chunking and embedding. This prevents noisy text from ever entering the vector index and degrading RAG retrieval.C. Incorrect. Relying on the LLM to ignore irrelevant navigation-menu, cookie-consent, and sidebar-ad sections at answer time does not fix the underlying data quality problem and still consumes context window and retrieval capacity with noisy content. The extraneous text remains embedded and can continue to reduce retrieval precision.D. Incorrect. Splitting into very small fixed-size chunks based on token count ignores HTML structure, so navigation and ad text can still land inside the same chunks as article body text. Only removing chunks that contain solely boilerplate does not address mixed chunks that still contain ads or menu links."},{"id":"10","question":"Which built-in MLflow GenAI judge requires a labeled ground-truth expected response in order to compute its score?","options":{"A":"Safety, which is reference-free and flags harmful or toxic content without needing an expected answer","B":"RelevanceToQuery, which is reference-free and scores whether the response addresses the query\'s intent","C":"RetrievalGroundedness, which is reference-free and checks the response only against the retrieved context","D":"Correctness, which compares the generated response against a labeled expected answer to score factual accuracy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Safety is a reference-free judge that assesses whether content is harmful, offensive, or toxic based on the response alone, so it does not require any labeled expected answer.B. RelevanceToQuery is reference-free and only needs the question and the response to judge whether the response addresses the query\'s intent.C. RetrievalGroundedness is reference-free and checks whether the response is supported by the retrieved context, not by a labeled ground-truth answer.D. Correctness explicitly needs a labeled expected answer supplied as ground truth, against which it compares the generated response to score factual accuracy; without that label, the judge cannot run."},{"id":"11","question":"Which Python package provides purpose-built LangChain tool wrappers for Unity Catalog functions, letting a governed UC function be surfaced directly as a LangChain-compatible tool for an agent?","options":{"A":"databricks-langchain","B":"databricks-connect","C":"databricks-sdk","D":"databricks-feature-engineering"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. This package provides the LangChain integrations, including a toolkit that wraps Unity Catalog functions so they can be passed directly into a LangChain agent\'s tool list.B. This package is a client for running Spark workloads against a remote Databricks cluster from a local environment; it has nothing to do with wrapping UC functions as LangChain tools.C. This package is a general-purpose REST API client for managing Databricks workspace resources such as clusters and jobs, not a LangChain tool integration library.D. This package is used for managing feature tables and feature lookups in the Feature Store, which is unrelated to exposing UC functions as LangChain tools."},{"id":"12","question":"A legal team wants to summarize full contracts, some running 50 to 100 pages, in a single prompt without splitting the document into chunks. Which model attribute should be prioritized when selecting an LLM for this application?","options":{"A":"Fine-tuning for real-time voice transcription, since transcription-focused training improves comprehension of long written contracts","B":"A large context window that can accept the entire contract in a single prompt, avoiding the need to split the document into chunks","C":"The highest reported benchmark score on coding tasks, since strong coding performance typically transfers to accurate long-document summarization","D":"The lowest cost per token, since summarization quality depends primarily on minimizing inference spend rather than input capacity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A large context window that can accept the entire contract in a single prompt, avoiding the need to split the document into chunks. Prioritizing a large context window is correct because the application\'s defining constraint is fitting long documents into one prompt, and an insufficient context window would force chunking and risk losing cross-section context in the contract.The highest reported benchmark score on coding tasks, since strong coding performance typically transfers to accurate long-document summarization. This is incorrect because coding benchmarks measure a different capability and do not indicate whether a model can accept or reason over very long input documents.The lowest cost per token, since summarization quality depends primarily on minimizing inference spend rather than input capacity. This is incorrect because a model with an inadequate context window is unusable for this task regardless of price, so input capacity is the binding constraint rather than cost.Fine-tuning for real-time voice transcription, since transcription-focused training improves comprehension of long written contracts. This is incorrect because voice transcription tuning targets audio-to-text conversion and has no bearing on a model\'s ability to process long written legal text."},{"id":"13","question":"A team is choosing between two candidate LLMs listed on Databricks Marketplace for a latency-sensitive, high-volume customer support chatbot. Model X has a higher MMLU benchmark score but a much larger parameter count and higher measured serving latency; Model Y has a slightly lower benchmark score, a smaller parameter count, and meets the application\'s response-time requirement. Based on the model cards, which model should the team select?","options":{"A":"Model Y, but only after fine-tuning it further to exactly match Model X\'s total parameter count and architecture","B":"Model X, because a higher MMLU benchmark score always outweighs serving latency in production systems","C":"Model Y, because it satisfies the latency requirement while offering benchmark quality close to Model X\'s score","D":"Model X, because larger parameter counts guarantee better real-world accuracy regardless of the benchmark used"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Fine-tuning a smaller model specifically to match a larger model\'s parameter count and architecture is not a real or necessary step, since parameter count is an inherent architectural property rather than something fine-tuning changes, and doing so would defeat the latency advantage.B. Treating benchmark score as the only decision factor ignores the application\'s stated latency requirement; a chatbot that cannot respond within the required time degrades the user experience regardless of its raw accuracy score.C. Selecting the model that meets the latency requirement while remaining close in benchmark quality is correct because model selection should weigh application attributes like response-time constraints alongside evaluation metrics, not optimize for a single metric in isolation.D. A larger parameter count does not guarantee better real-world accuracy; benchmark scores already capture measured quality, and here the smaller model\'s score is only slightly lower while its latency profile fits the use case."},{"id":"14","question":"While selecting a foundation model for a support-ticket classification task, an engineer notices that Model X has higher published scores on general-purpose leaderboards than Model Y. However, an MLflow evaluation run on the company\'s own labeled ticket dataset shows Model Y outperforming Model X for this specific task. Which model should be selected, and why?","options":{"A":"Select Model X, because model card metadata takes precedence over MLflow experiment metrics whenever the two sources disagree on model quality","B":"Select whichever model has the lower listed parameter count, because smaller models are assumed to generalize better to task-specific applications than the leaderboard results suggest","C":"Select Model X, because published leaderboard benchmarks are always more statistically reliable than results from a single internal evaluation dataset","D":"Select Model Y, because task-specific evaluation results from the company\'s own labeled dataset are more representative of production performance than general-purpose benchmark scores"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Model card metadata provides general reference information, but it doesn\'t override task-specific experiment results when the two disagree; the internal evaluation is more directly relevant to this application.B. Parameter count alone doesn\'t determine how well a model generalizes to a specific classification task, and this option ignores the actual evaluation metrics that were generated for the two candidates.C. General-purpose leaderboard scores measure performance on broad, generic tasks and don\'t account for the specific distribution of the company\'s support tickets, so they aren\'t inherently more reliable than a task-specific evaluation.D. Correct. An evaluation run on data that reflects the actual application, such as the company\'s own labeled ticket dataset, more accurately predicts production performance for that task than a general-purpose leaderboard score."},{"id":"15","question":"A developer is prototyping a retrieval tool locally in a notebook before deploying an agent that queries a Databricks-managed vector search index built with Databricks-managed embeddings. They want a LangChain-compatible tool object they can pass directly to their agent with minimal setup. Which option fits this stage of development?","options":{"A":"Install `databricks-langchain` and instantiate `VectorSearchRetrieverTool` with the `index_name` and `tool_description` parameters.","B":"Install `databricks-openai` and instantiate `DatabricksEmbeddings` directly as the callable tool passed to the agent\'s tool list.","C":"Deploy the index behind the managed AI Search MCP server first and connect only through `DatabricksMCPClient` for all local prototyping.","D":"Create a Unity Catalog connection object pointing at the vector index and define a retriever function that calls the connection directly."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. This is correct because this class from the Databricks AI Bridge package is purpose-built to wrap a Databricks-managed vector index as a ready-to-use LangChain tool with minimal setup for local prototyping.B. This is incorrect because an embeddings class produces vector representations of text; it is not itself a retriever tool and cannot be passed to an agent as a callable tool.C. This is incorrect because standing up a managed MCP server is the recommended path for production-grade, governed access, but it adds infrastructure overhead unnecessary for quick local prototyping against an already Databricks-managed index.D. This is incorrect because the Unity Catalog connection pattern is intended for indexes or vector stores hosted outside Databricks, not for an index that already uses Databricks-managed embeddings and hosting."},{"id":"16","question":"An agent has already sent a message to a Genie space and received a `message_id`. It now needs to obtain the tabular result of the query Genie generated so it can pass the rows back to the orchestrating agent. What is the correct sequence to follow?","options":{"A":"Poll `POST /api/2.0/genie/spaces/{space_id}/start-conversation` repeatedly with the same message text until the response includes a populated result set.","B":"Poll `GET .../messages/{message_id}` until the status is conclusive (such as completed), then call `GET .../messages/{message_id}/query-result/{attachment_id}` to fetch the rows.","C":"Call `GET .../messages/{message_id}/query-result/{attachment_id}` immediately, since the query result is always available synchronously as soon as the message is created.","D":"Call `DELETE .../conversations/{conversation_id}` to force Genie to finalize the query, then re-issue `start-conversation` to retrieve the finished rows."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because repeatedly calling start-conversation creates a new conversation thread each time rather than checking the status of the message that was already submitted.B. This is correct because message processing is asynchronous: the agent must poll the message endpoint until a conclusive status is reached before the generated SQL\'s results become retrievable via the query-result endpoint keyed by attachment ID.C. This is incorrect because SQL generation and execution take time and are not guaranteed to finish synchronously; fetching the query result before processing completes will not reliably return the finished rows.D. This is incorrect because deleting a conversation removes it rather than finalizing it, and starting a brand-new conversation discards the in-progress question instead of retrieving its results."},{"id":"17","question":"A team is deploying a customer-facing chat agent and has three candidate LLMs, each logged as an MLflow run with a quality judge score, average token latency, and per-1M-token cost recorded as run metrics. The product requirement is sub-second responses at the lowest cost while still clearing a minimum quality bar. Which approach best uses the logged experiment metrics to select the deployment model?","options":{"A":"Select the model with the lowest per-1M-token cost first, then verify its quality judge score after deployment using production inference tables.","B":"Select the model with the lowest average token latency, since sub-second response time is stated as a requirement and cost can be optimized with provisioned throughput later.","C":"Filter the runs to those meeting the minimum quality score, then among the remaining runs choose the one with the lowest combined latency and cost.","D":"Select the model with the highest quality judge score regardless of its logged latency or cost metrics, since customer satisfaction outweighs the other run values."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Choosing purely on cost and deferring quality verification to after deployment abandons the experiment metrics that were already collected and risks shipping a model that fails the minimum quality bar.B. Optimizing only for latency ignores the logged quality and cost metrics, so the selected model could fail the minimum quality bar or exceed the target cost despite meeting the latency goal.C. Filtering out runs that fail the quality threshold before comparing latency and cost across the remaining candidates directly uses all three logged metrics to satisfy the quality bar, latency target, and cost goal together.D. Ignoring the logged latency and cost metrics risks selecting a model that violates the sub-second response requirement or exceeds the cost target, even though it scored well on quality."},{"id":"18","question":"A developer needs an LLM step in a pipeline to return machine-readable output so a downstream Python function can parse it without errors. With a plain instruction such as `Extract the product name and price from this review`, the model sometimes returns a sentence like \'The product is a blender priced at $39.99\' instead of structured data. Which prompt design change best guarantees the desired structured output?","options":{"A":"Ask the model to \'try to format the answer nicely\' by outputting the product name on one line and the price on the next line, such as `blender` and `$39.99`, so the downstream parser can split the response on line breaks.","B":"Instruct the model to underline the product name and price in its response using visible delimiters, such as `__blender__` and `__$39.99__`, so the downstream parser can extract the fields by scanning for those markers.","C":"Tell the model to answer in as few words as possible by replacing the prose answer with a compact token string, such as `blender 39.99`, so the downstream parser can split the short response without extra formatting.","D":"Explicitly instruct the model to respond only with a JSON object matching a given schema, such as `{\\"product_name\\": string, \\"price\\": number}`, and include an example of correctly formatted output."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Asking the model to \'try to format the answer nicely\' is vague and does not impose a strict, predictable schema or syntax. Even if the model places `blender` and `$39.99` on separate lines, minor variations in wording, labels, or spacing can still break a downstream parser that expects consistent machine-readable output.B. Incorrect. Using visible delimiters such as underscores is not a standard machine-readable format and does not guarantee consistent key-value structure. A parser that scans for `__blender__` and `__$39.99__` may still fail if the model adds extra text, changes the markers, or varies the field order.C. Incorrect. Shortness alone does not create structured output. A compact token string like `blender 39.99` is still unstructured prose that lacks labeled fields or a reliable syntax, making it difficult for a downstream function to extract the product name and price without error.D. Correct. Explicitly requiring only a JSON object that matches a defined schema — including field names like `product_name` and `price` with their types — removes ambiguity about the expected structure. Including an example of correctly formatted output further steers the model toward machine-readable JSON that downstream parsers can consume reliably."},{"id":"19","question":"A retail chatbot receives: \\"Can you check if the blue XL hoodie is in stock at my usual store?\\" The application already has the user\'s stored preferences, including their default store ID and past purchase history. Which strategy best augments the prompt sent to the LLM so it can answer accurately?","options":{"A":"Detect the entities \\"blue XL hoodie\\" and \\"usual store\\" in the message, resolve \\"usual store\\" against the user\'s stored default store ID, and add the resolved store ID and current inventory data for the SKU into the prompt context.","B":"Forward the message unchanged and instruct the LLM to guess a plausible store location based on common naming patterns in its training data, then add the guessed store location and current inventory data for the hoodie into the prompt context.","C":"Ask the LLM to first generate a list of all stores in the country, then have the user manually pick one from that generated list, and add the selected store ID plus current inventory data for the blue XL hoodie into the prompt context before answering.","D":"Summarize the user\'s entire multi-year purchase history into the prompt without identifying which specific entities in the current message need resolution, then rely on the LLM to infer the store and product from that history and return an availability answer."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct. Identifying the key entities in the current message and resolving ambiguous references like \\"usual store\\" against the user\'s stored default store ID gives the model concrete, user-specific context. Adding the resolved store ID and the relevant current SKU inventory data lets the LLM answer accurately instead of reasoning from vague or inferred facts.B. Incorrect. Forwarding the message unchanged and instructing the LLM to guess a store from generic training-data naming patterns ignores the exact default store ID the application already has. This produces an unreliable location and can make the inventory lookup irrelevant or incorrect.C. Incorrect. Generating a nationwide list of stores and asking the user to manually pick one defeats the purpose of automatic prompt augmentation, since the user\'s usual store is already stored in the profile. It adds unnecessary friction and may rely on a model-generated store list rather than the known default store ID.D. Incorrect. Dumping the entire multi-year purchase history without identifying which specific terms in the current message need resolution adds noisy, unfocused context. The LLM may infer an incorrect store or product when the application could instead resolve the \\"usual store\\" and \\"blue XL hoodie\\" directly against known user data."},{"id":"20","question":"A generative AI engineer is assembling a simple chain in LangChain on Databricks that takes a user question, applies a `ChatPromptTemplate`, sends it to a `ChatDatabricks` LLM, and parses the output with a `StrOutputParser`. Which code correctly composes these three components into a single runnable chain using LangChain Expression Language?","options":{"A":"Chain the components with the pipe operator: `prompt | llm | output_parser`, composing them into one Runnable in sequence.","B":"Wrap the components in `LLMChain(prompt, llm, output_parser)`, passing all three arguments positionally to build the chain.","C":"Wrap the components in `SequentialChain(chains=[prompt, llm, output_parser])`, listing each step in the chains parameter.","D":"Call `RunnableSequence(steps=[prompt, llm, output_parser])`, passing each component as an ordered list of steps."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct: the pipe operator is LangChain Expression Language\'s standard syntax for composing Runnables, so linking the prompt template, chat model, and output parser this way builds a single sequential chain.B. Incorrect: `LLMChain` is the legacy chain class and is not constructed by passing a prompt, chat model, and parser as three positional arguments in this way.C. Incorrect: `SequentialChain` composes multiple named sub-chains with declared input/output keys for multi-step workflows, it is not how a single prompt-LLM-parser pipeline built from these three objects is expressed.D. Incorrect: `RunnableSequence` is not instantiated with a `steps` keyword this way; the pipe operator is the documented syntax that builds a `RunnableSequence` under the hood."},{"id":"21","question":"A team is deploying an agent whose Unity Catalog table queries must respect each individual end user\'s own row- and column-level permissions rather than a single shared identity\'s permissions. Which configuration achieves this on a Model Serving endpoint?","options":{"A":"Declare the tables as resource dependencies with mlflow.models.resources so the system-generated service principal gets read-only access shared by every caller.","B":"Store one service principal\'s personal access token as a Databricks secret and reference it via `{{secrets/scope/key}}` so all requests authenticate identically.","C":"Grant the workspace\'s default Unity Catalog metastore admin role to the endpoint so it bypasses table-level access checks for every incoming request.","D":"Enable on-behalf-of-user authentication, build the client with `ModelServingUserCredentials` inside `predict`, and declare `api_scopes` in an `AuthPolicy` at logging time."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Automatic authentication passthrough with a declared resource grants the same service-principal-level access to every caller of the endpoint, so it cannot differentiate row- or column-level permissions between individual end users.B. Referencing a single service principal\'s token means every request authenticates as that one identity, which enforces uniform access rather than each end user\'s individual row- and column-level entitlements.C. Granting a broad metastore admin role bypasses access checks entirely rather than applying each user\'s own permissions, which is the opposite of enforcing per-user row- and column-level governance.D. On-behalf-of-user authentication is the feature designed for this scenario: initializing `ModelServingUserCredentials` inside the prediction function (since user identity is only known at request time) and declaring the needed API scopes lets each request enforce that specific user\'s own Unity Catalog permissions."},{"id":"22","question":"An agent\'s retrieval tool queries a vector search index and needs to combine semantic similarity with exact keyword matching to improve recall for exact product names, while also restricting results to documents where `category` equals `electronics`. Which combination of query call parameters achieves this?","options":{"A":"Set `query_type=\\"HYBRID\\"` and pass `columns=[\\"category\\"]` to restrict the search to documents in that category.","B":"Set `query_type=\\"FULL_TEXT\\"` and pass `filters={\\"category\\": \\"electronics\\"}`, since full-text indexes always blend semantic scoring.","C":"Set `query_type=\\"HYBRID\\"` and pass `filters={\\"category\\": \\"electronics\\"}` in the `similarity_search` call.","D":"Set `query_type=\\"ANN\\"` and pass `filters={\\"category\\": \\"electronics\\"}`, since ANN already blends keyword and vector scoring."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. The `columns` parameter only controls which fields are returned in the results; it does not restrict which documents are searched, so category filtering would not actually apply.B. A full-text query type performs keyword-based search and does not automatically blend in semantic vector scoring, so it would not provide the combined similarity behavior needed.C. HYBRID query type combines vector similarity with keyword matching, and the `filters` argument restricts results to matching metadata, together satisfying both requirements.D. ANN performs pure approximate nearest-neighbor vector search only; it does not blend in keyword scoring, so it would not improve recall for exact product-name matches."},{"id":"23","question":"A batch summarization job calls `ai_query()` against a foundation model endpoint for every article in a table, but the generated summaries are consistently too long and vary unpredictably in style between runs. The team wants shorter, more consistent output without changing the prompt text. Which addition to the `ai_query()` call addresses this?","options":{"A":"Set `files => content` on the call so the request is processed as multimodal input with a smaller default response budget.","B":"Pass `modelParameters => named_struct(\'max_tokens\', 100, \'temperature\', 0.2)` to cap the generation length and reduce output variability.","C":"Pass `returnType => \'STRING\'` explicitly so the engine truncates any response longer than the inferred custom endpoint schema allows.","D":"Wrap the endpoint name in a `named_struct()` so the request is treated as a structured custom-model call with a fixed output length."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. `files` is used to attach image content for multimodal requests; it is unrelated to text summarization and does not influence response length or determinism for a text-only prompt.B. Correct: `modelParameters` carries chat/completion controls like `max_tokens` and `temperature` straight through to the foundation model endpoint, so capping `max_tokens` bounds summary length and lowering `temperature` reduces run-to-run variability.C. `returnType` describes the expected schema of a custom endpoint\'s response for parsing purposes; it is not a truncation mechanism and has no effect on how much text a foundation chat model generates.D. The endpoint argument is a plain string identifying which serving endpoint to call; wrapping it in a struct does not change generation length and would break the call rather than configure output size."},{"id":"24","question":"When evaluating AI Search retrieval quality, which metric does Databricks recommend as the primary metric because it weighs highly relevant results more heavily and accounts for their rank position in the result list?","options":{"A":"DCG@10","B":"MRR","C":"Recall@10","D":"Precision@10"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Discounted Cumulative Gain at 10 sums graded relevance scores while discounting results that appear lower in the ranking, so highly relevant documents near the top contribute more than marginally relevant ones further down. This combination of graded relevance and rank-position weighting is why Databricks recommends it as the primary retrieval quality metric.B. Mean Reciprocal Rank only considers the position of the first relevant result and ignores the graded relevance or ranking of any other results in the list. It does not account for multiple relevant documents at varying relevance levels the way the recommended metric does.C. Recall@10 measures the fraction of all relevant documents that were retrieved in the top 10 results, but it does not weight results by degree of relevance or by their position within that top 10. It is not the metric Databricks highlights as primary for this reason.D. Precision@10 measures the fraction of the top 10 retrieved results that are relevant, treating all relevance levels and positions within the top 10 equally. It does not capture the graded, position-weighted signal that the recommended metric provides."},{"id":"25","question":"What is Databricks Lakebase, as used for self-managed agent memory?","options":{"A":"A managed Unity Catalog securable object that stores memory entries under a specific scope and path.","B":"A vector index service that stores and retrieves embeddings for semantic similarity search.","C":"A batch scheduling service that triggers periodic Delta Lake table maintenance jobs.","D":"A fully managed, serverless Postgres OLTP database that agents use as a durable memory store."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. A Unity Catalog securable memory store with scoped, path-organized entries describes managed agent memory, which is a separate, Databricks-governed approach rather than what Lakebase itself is.B. Storing and retrieving embeddings for similarity search describes a vector search index, not Lakebase, which is a relational Postgres database rather than an embedding index.C. Lakebase is an operational Postgres database for transactional workloads, not a scheduling service, and it has no role in triggering Delta Lake table maintenance jobs.D. Lakebase is Databricks\' fully managed, serverless Postgres OLTP database, and self-managed agent memory uses it as a durable store for short-term and long-term agent state such as checkpoints and conversation history."},{"id":"26","question":"A team is building an agent that must run governed Unity Catalog SQL functions as predefined tools, without standing up any new infrastructure. The workspace already has the functions registered in a catalog and schema, and the team wants Unity Catalog permissions to govern which users can invoke each function. Which approach best satisfies this requirement?","options":{"A":"Register the Unity Catalog schema as an external MCP server through a Unity Catalog connection with managed OAuth so the agent reaches it as a third-party service.","B":"Have the agent call the functions directly through the SQL warehouse REST API and enforce access with a workspace-level access token instead of Unity Catalog grants.","C":"Connect the agent to the managed Unity Catalog Functions MCP server at `/api/2.0/mcp/functions/{catalog}/{schema}`, letting Unity Catalog permissions govern access.","D":"Deploy a custom MCP server as a Databricks App that wraps each Unity Catalog function in a `@mcp.tool()` decorator and exposes it over the app\'s `/mcp` path."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. External MCP registration through Unity Catalog connections is meant for servers hosted outside Databricks; functions that already live in a Unity Catalog schema inside the workspace are served by the managed server, not treated as an external third-party connection.B. Calling the SQL warehouse REST API directly bypasses the MCP tool-calling contract the agent framework expects and substitutes a workspace token for Unity Catalog\'s fine-grained function-level grants, weakening governance rather than preserving it.C. This is correct: Databricks hosts a managed Unity Catalog Functions MCP server that exposes registered SQL/Python functions as predefined tools at a workspace endpoint, with Unity Catalog enforcing which principals can call which function, so no additional hosting is required.D. Building a custom MCP server on Databricks Apps is unnecessary extra work here since the functions are already governed Unity Catalog objects that the managed Unity Catalog Functions server can expose directly without any app code or deployment."},{"id":"27","question":"A team logs a RAG agent that expects OpenAI-style chat messages as input (a `messages` list with `role`/`content` fields) and needs the resulting endpoint to work correctly with the AI Playground and the review app. They do not want to hand-write the MLflow model signature. What should they do at logging time?","options":{"A":"Enable autologging for the LangChain retriever when logging the RAG agent, since autologging captures an OpenAI-style chat signature from the retriever\'s `messages` input and applies it to the endpoint.","B":"Pass a representative `input_example` such as a `messages` list with a sample user turn to `log_model`, letting MLflow infer the signature from that example and the chain\'s output.","C":"Leave both `input_example` and the signature unset when logging to Unity Catalog, since Unity Catalog assigns a default chat signature to registered models and the endpoint can infer the `messages` schema.","D":"Write a `predict` method on the RAG agent that returns a hard-coded JSON schema string describing the expected `messages` list with `role` and `content` fields, and log that string as a model tag for AI Playground."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Incorrect. MLflow autologging does not cover LangChain retrievers, so enabling autologging for the retriever will not capture an OpenAI-style chat signature from the retriever\'s `messages` input. Even retriever execution were logged, that does not apply a chat signature to the RAG agent endpoint.B. Correct. Providing a representative `input_example` such as a `messages` list with a sample user turn lets MLflow execute the chain and infer the signature from the observed input and output shapes. This is the recommended way to obtain a valid OpenAI-style chat signature without hand-writing the model signature, satisfying AI Playground and review app requirements.C. Incorrect. Unity Catalog does not automatically assign a default chat signature when a model is logged without `input_example` and signature, and the endpoint cannot reliably infer the `messages` schema on its own. The model version remains effectively unsigned, so AI Playground and the review app lack the schema they require.D. Incorrect. Having `predict` return a hard-coded JSON schema string and logging that string as a model tag only stores metadata; it does not create an MLflow model signature. Because it is not validated against the model\'s actual inputs and outputs, it does not satisfy the signature requirement for AI Playground or the review app."},{"id":"28","question":"When a built-in Unity AI Gateway guardrail detects a policy violation, which two actions can it be configured to take on the flagged content?","options":{"A":"Block the request or response outright, or sanitize it by rewriting the flagged content in place.","B":"Retry the inference call with a truncated context window, or escalate the request to a human reviewer queue.","C":"Log the violation to an external SIEM tool, or automatically downgrade the serving endpoint to a smaller model.","D":"Cache the flagged content for later audit, or throttle the requesting user\'s rate limit for the session."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Built-in guardrails support two enforcement actions: blocking, which rejects the request or response outright with an error, and sanitizing, which dynamically rewrites the flagged text in place without terminating the request.B. Retrying with a truncated context and escalating to a human queue are not actions that a Unity AI Gateway guardrail applies to flagged content; guardrails enforce policy directly rather than routing to manual review.C. Forwarding violations to an external SIEM tool and automatically downgrading the model are not guardrail enforcement actions; guardrails act on the content itself through blocking or sanitizing, not infrastructure changes.D. Caching flagged content and throttling rate limits are unrelated to how a guardrail responds to a detected violation; enforcement is limited to blocking the content or sanitizing it, not session-level rate control."},{"id":"29","question":"A RAG application\'s retrieval layer queries a claims table where analysts in the `us-team` group should see only rows for the `US` region, while the claim amount values themselves must remain fully visible to every analyst. Which governance control satisfies this requirement?","options":{"A":"Attach a column mask to the `region` column that returns a placeholder string for users outside the `us-team` group, leaving all rows visible.","B":"Attach a column mask to the `claim_amount` column that zeroes out the value for users outside the `us-team` group, leaving all rows visible.","C":"Attach a row filter function that excludes rows where the claim amount exceeds a threshold for users outside the `us-team` group.","D":"Attach a row filter function that excludes rows where the region does not match the querying user\'s team, leaving every column value unmasked."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Masking the region column would hide which region a visible row belongs to rather than restrict which rows are returned, so analysts would still see every row\'s claim amount regardless of region, failing the row-level requirement.B. Masking the claim_amount column directly contradicts the requirement that claim amounts remain fully visible to every analyst, and it does nothing to restrict rows by region.C. Filtering rows by claim amount threshold restricts access based on the wrong attribute, since the requirement is to restrict rows by region, not by the size of the claim amount.D. A row filter is designed to exclude entire rows based on a condition like matching region to team, which restricts which claims an analyst can see while leaving the claim amount column itself fully visible in the rows that remain."},{"id":"30","question":"A product team is building a RAG assistant using articles scraped from third-party blogs with unclear reuse terms. Legal flags that the scraped corpus creates copyright and licensing risk for the company. What should the team recommend to mitigate this risk in the data source?","options":{"A":"Keep the scraped corpus and add a disclaimer in the system prompt stating that content is used under fair use","B":"Replace the scraped blog corpus with first-party content or a dataset under a license that explicitly permits this use","C":"Keep the scraped corpus but append a citation with the original blog URL to every generated response","D":"Keep the scraped corpus but reduce the chunk size used during embedding to shorten each retrieved passage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. A fair-use disclaimer in the prompt is not a legal determination and does not change the actual licensing status of the scraped content, so the underlying risk to the company persists.B. Swapping in first-party content or a dataset with a license that clearly permits the intended use removes the legal risk at its source, since the corpus no longer relies on ambiguous reuse terms.C. Citing the source URL may be good practice for attribution, but it does not resolve the underlying licensing uncertainty about whether the company is permitted to reuse and redistribute the scraped content.D. Shortening the chunk size changes how much text is retrieved per query, but the content still originates from a corpus with unresolved reuse rights, so the licensing risk remains unchanged."},{"id":"31","question":"Legal counsel flags that a search feature\'s embedding model was trained partly on web-scraped user-generated content with unclear rights, and the feature is about to ship to external customers. What is the appropriate remediation before launch?","options":{"A":"Apply differential privacy noise to the embeddings before deployment so individual web-scraped contributions cannot be attributed, and document it as satisfying the legal review before launch.","B":"Encrypt the embedding vectors at rest with the production key-management service, because encryption satisfies the licensing requirements for the web-scraped data before launch.","C":"Remove the web-scraped content with unclear licensing from the training set and replace it with data under a confirmed commercially permissive license before shipping.","D":"Ship the feature as planned but add a disclaimer in the product documentation noting that some training data has unverified origins, and list the web-scraped sources in the launch notes."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect. Differential privacy noise may limit attribution of individual web-scraped contributions, but it does not establish copyright or licensing permissions for the underlying content. Documenting it as satisfying legal review therefore mischaracterizes the legal risk.B. Incorrect. Encrypting embedding vectors at rest with a production key-management service protects confidentiality, but it has no effect on whether the organization has the legal right to use the web-scraped data. Encryption does not satisfy licensing requirements.C. Correct. Removing the web-scraped content with unclear licensing from the training set and replacing it with data under a confirmed commercially permissive license directly eliminates the legal exposure before the feature ships to external customers. This addresses the root cause instead of working around it.D. Incorrect. Adding a disclaimer and listing the web-scraped sources in launch notes only discloses the risk; it does not remove the underlying legal exposure from using data with unverified rights in a launched product. Documentation alone cannot cure a licensing gap."},{"id":"32","question":"A retrieval-augmented question-answering system is being evaluated with two candidate LLMs on the same held-out test set of question-answer pairs. Model A scores 88% on an answer-correctness metric with an average response time of 1.2 seconds. Model B scores 89% on the same metric with an average response time of 4.5 seconds. The application has a strict service-level requirement that responses must return in under 2 seconds. Based on this evaluation data, what is the correct model selection decision?","options":{"A":"Model B should be selected, because a higher score on the correctness metric should always outweigh latency considerations in evaluation-based model selection","B":"Model A should be selected, because any accuracy difference smaller than five percentage points should be treated as statistically meaningless and ignored entirely","C":"Model B should be selected, because latency measurements taken during evaluation do not reflect the latency the model will exhibit in production","D":"Model A should be selected, because Model B\'s 4.5-second response time violates the 2-second latency requirement despite its marginally higher correctness score"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because model selection must weigh all relevant quantitative requirements together; a correctness edge does not override a hard latency service-level requirement that the application has defined.B. This is incorrect because there is no such blanket statistical rule; the scenario asks for a decision based on the stated latency requirement, not on discounting the accuracy gap outright.C. This is incorrect because evaluation-time latency measurements are a standard and relevant proxy for expected production latency, and dismissing them contradicts the purpose of measuring latency during evaluation.D. This is correct: the application defines a hard latency requirement under 2 seconds, and Model B\'s measured 4.5-second average response time fails that requirement, so Model A is the only candidate that satisfies both the accuracy bar and the latency constraint."},{"id":"33","question":"A platform team supports five separate agent applications, each tagged with its own cost-center identifier, all routed through a shared Unity Catalog AI Gateway endpoint. Finance wants an email alert the moment any one cost center\'s monthly spend on that endpoint crosses $2,000, and they want the option to automatically block further requests from that cost center once the threshold is hit. Which Databricks feature should the team configure?","options":{"A":"A budget scoped to the cost-center tags with a defined threshold and usage blocking enabled for the AI Gateway endpoint.","B":"A rate limit on the endpoint expressed in tokens per minute, applied uniformly across all five cost-center tags.","C":"An inference table capturing every request and response payload for the endpoint, reviewed weekly by finance.","D":"A Unity Catalog access control list restricting which cost centers may query the shared endpoint at all."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Databricks budgets can be scoped to custom tags such as cost-center identifiers, tracking spend against a defined monthly threshold and sending near real-time alerts for Unity AI Gateway usage, with an optional usage-blocking setting that halts further requests once the threshold is reached, matching both the alerting and blocking requirements.B. A tokens-per-minute rate limit caps request throughput rather than tracking dollar spend against a threshold, and applying one limit uniformly across all cost centers would not distinguish which specific cost center crossed $2,000, so it does not satisfy the tag-based spend alerting requirement.C. An inference table logs request and response payloads for auditing, evaluation, and monitoring quality, but it has no built-in mechanism to compute dollar spend per tag, trigger a threshold alert, or block further calls.D. An access control list can permit or deny which principals may query an endpoint, but it does not track cumulative spend or generate a threshold-crossing alert; it would only enforce an all-or-nothing access decision, not a cost-based one."},{"id":"34","question":"A team writes a custom scorer for a support agent and wants stakeholders reviewing evaluation runs to see not just a numeric score but also a short written reason for that score, attached to each trace. What should the `@scorer`-decorated function return to achieve this?","options":{"A":"Return a `str` containing both the score and reasoning combined into one sentence, since strings support free text","B":"Return a `Feedback` object carrying a numeric `value` plus a `rationale` string explaining why that score was assigned","C":"Return a plain `float` between 0 and 1, since numeric scores are the only values MLflow can attach to a trace","D":"Return a `list` of raw floats, one per stakeholder, since only lists let a scorer report more than one value"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. A `str` can hold text, but jamming a score and reasoning into one unstructured sentence loses the distinct, queryable `value` and `rationale` fields that a `Feedback` object provides.B. A `Feedback` object is built for exactly this case: it pairs a `value` (the score) with a `rationale` (the explanation), giving stakeholders both the number and the reasoning behind it in a structured way attached to the trace.C. A plain `float` is a valid scorer return type, but it carries only the number with no place to attach an explanation, so it cannot satisfy the requirement for a visible written rationale.D. A list return is meant for reporting several distinct named metrics from one scorer, not for pairing a single score with an explanation; it does not provide a structured rationale field either."},{"id":"35","question":"A platform team wants to attribute LLM spend on a shared AI Gateway endpoint across several business units, without changing which model or endpoint each unit calls. Which approach lets them break down usage by business unit afterward?","options":{"A":"Create a separate rate limit group per business unit on the endpoint and read off each group\'s configured QPM value as its usage total for the billing period in the billing dashboard.","B":"Enable Unity Catalog lineage tracking on the serving endpoint and read the upstream table and notebook lineage graph in Catalog Explorer to determine which business unit issued each call.","C":"Attach custom key-value pairs to each request through the Databricks-Ai-Gateway-Request-Tags header, then group results by the request_tags column in the usage tracking system table.","D":"Filter the inference table\'s status_code column for successful requests, then group the resulting rows by the department field recorded in each request payload and echoed in the response JSON body."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Incorrect. A rate limit\'s configured QPM value is a throughput ceiling, not a measurement of what a group actually consumed. Reading it off a billing dashboard would show the configured limit rather than real usage or spend, so it cannot attribute cost to each business unit.B. Incorrect. Unity Catalog lineage shows how tables, notebooks, and models relate to one another, not which caller or business unit sent a given inference request. It therefore cannot provide per-business-unit cost attribution for requests on a shared endpoint.C. Correct. Request tags sent in the `Databricks-Ai-Gateway-Request-Tags` header label each individual request with custom key-value pairs, such as a business unit name. Those tags are recorded in the `request_tags` column of the usage tracking system table, so the team can group usage and cost by business unit without changing which model or endpoint each unit calls.D. Incorrect. The `status_code` column only records the HTTP outcome of a request, so filtering on it selects successful calls but does not attribute them to anyone. A department value is not something the gateway records or returns on its own, so grouping by it would not give a reliable per-business-unit breakdown."}]},{"id":"databricks-machine-learning-associate","title":"Databricks Certified Machine Learning Associate","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"A team maintains a model training pipeline that must produce identical results when re-run six months later, including the exact versions of scikit-learn, XGBoost, and their transitive dependencies used at training time. Which characteristic of Databricks Runtime for Machine Learning most directly supports this requirement?","options":{"A":"Databricks Runtime for Machine Learning removes the need to track library versions at all, because MLflow autologging reconstructs the original environment from the model artifact alone.","B":"Databricks Runtime for Machine Learning automatically upgrades scikit-learn and XGBoost to their latest release on every cluster restart, keeping the environment current with upstream fixes.","C":"Each Databricks Runtime for Machine Learning release pins a tested, documented set of library versions, so re-running a job on the same runtime version reproduces the same dependency set.","D":"Databricks Runtime for Machine Learning stores every notebook\'s output as a Delta table, which independently guarantees identical library versions across separate training runs."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. MLflow autologging can record library versions as part of a model\'s metadata, but it does not eliminate the need to track versions during training or reconstruct an environment on its own without a pinned runtime to install against.B. Databricks Runtime for Machine Learning does not silently auto-upgrade libraries on restart; a given runtime release keeps a fixed library set, which is what makes it reproducible rather than a moving target.C. Each Databricks Runtime for Machine Learning release ships a specific, tested, and documented set of library versions, so pinning a job to that runtime version reproduces the same scikit-learn, XGBoost, and dependency versions on a later run.D. Saving notebook output to Delta tables preserves data, not the software environment; it does not record or guarantee which library versions produced that output on a separate run."},{"id":"2","question":"A data science manager wants to compare the parameters, metrics, and artifacts of every model AutoML tried for a pricing-prediction problem, including runs that were not selected as the best model. Which Databricks AutoML behavior makes this possible?","options":{"A":"AutoML keeps detailed records only for the single best-performing trial and permanently discards the parameters and metrics from every other trial once training finishes.","B":"AutoML stores trial results in a local CSV file on the cluster\'s driver node, which is deleted automatically when the cluster terminates after the run.","C":"AutoML emails a static summary report of the best trial to the workspace administrator, without persisting any run data that could be reopened or compared.","D":"AutoML logs every trial as an MLflow run, capturing parameters, metrics, and artifacts so the manager can compare all trials, not only the best one, in the MLflow UI."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. AutoML does not discard the non-winning trials; each one remains logged as an MLflow run with its own parameters and metrics available for later comparison.B. Trial results are not stashed in an ephemeral local CSV on the driver — they are written to MLflow, which persists independently of the cluster\'s lifecycle.C. AutoML does not communicate results only through a one-time emailed summary; the run data for every trial stays queryable and reopenable in MLflow after the job finishes.D. Every AutoML trial is tracked as its own MLflow run with parameters, metrics, and artifacts, so the manager can open the MLflow experiment and compare all trials side by side, not just the one AutoML selected as best."},{"id":"3","question":"Which open-source libraries does Databricks AutoML draw its trial algorithms from when running a classification experiment?","options":{"A":"scikit-learn, XGBoost, and LightGBM implementations of classification algorithms tried during trials.","B":"Prophet and ARIMA implementations, the same forecasting libraries AutoML uses for time series experiments.","C":"TensorFlow and PyTorch implementations of deep learning classifiers tuned automatically by AutoML runs.","D":"Spark MLlib\'s distributed classification implementations, run natively across the cluster\'s worker nodes."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Databricks AutoML evaluates classification and regression trials using open source algorithms from scikit-learn, XGBoost, and LightGBM, training and comparing multiple model types from these libraries before ranking the trials.B. Prophet and ARIMA are the libraries AutoML uses specifically for forecasting experiments, not classification; classification trials draw from a different set of algorithm libraries.C. AutoML\'s classification trials are not built on TensorFlow or PyTorch deep learning models; its algorithm pool for classification and regression comes from classical machine learning libraries instead.D. AutoML does not train its trials using Spark MLlib\'s distributed algorithm implementations; it distributes trials of single-node scikit-learn, XGBoost, and LightGBM models across the cluster instead."},{"id":"4","question":"A team runs identical training pipeline code in dev, staging, and production Databricks workspaces, and retraining is cheap because the dataset is small. Following Databricks\' recommended MLOps strategy, how should a validated model move into production?","options":{"A":"Point the production serving endpoint at the staging workspace\'s tracking server so production always serves whatever model staging last trained.","B":"Promote the pipeline code into the production workspace so the production job retrains on production data and registers its own fresh model version.","C":"Copy the serialized model file produced in staging directly into the production catalog, skipping retraining so the production version matches staging exactly.","D":"Export the staging MLflow run\'s artifact and re-upload it as a new run in production, keeping the original training code only in the staging workspace."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Having production depend on staging\'s tracking server couples environments together and removes the isolation MLOps practices rely on, since a change in staging would silently affect production.B. This matches Databricks\' code-first promotion guidance: promoting the pipeline through environments and retraining in production keeps the deployed model consistent with production data and infrastructure rather than a possibly stale artifact.C. Copying a serialized artifact between environments bypasses retraining on production data and skips the governance and reproducibility benefits of running the pipeline where it will serve, so it is not the recommended approach when retraining is cheap.D. Re-uploading an artifact as a new run without rerunning the training code in production leaves the production environment without a reproducible pipeline, undermining lineage and future retraining."},{"id":"5","question":"A data science team in Workspace A builds a demand-forecasting feature table for a retail company. The marketing analytics team, working in Workspace B, is attached to the same Unity Catalog metastore and wants to reuse those exact features for a promotion-targeting model instead of recomputing them. The features were originally created as an account-level table in Unity Catalog rather than in a workspace-level feature store. What is the primary benefit this gives the marketing team?","options":{"A":"Because the feature table was registered with the legacy `FeatureStoreClient`, MLflow automatically forwards its schema to every workspace\'s local model registry so downstream teams inherit access.","B":"Because the feature table was written with Auto Loader, Databricks automatically replicates the underlying files into every workspace\'s own DBFS mount, letting each team keep an independent physical copy of the data rather than one shared source.","C":"Because the feature table lives in Unity Catalog, it is accessible to every workspace attached to the same metastore, so the marketing team can read and reuse it through a normal `catalog.schema.table` reference without copying data.","D":"Because the feature table is stored in a workspace-level Hive metastore, Databricks grants read access to any user across the entire account regardless of which metastore their workspace is attached to."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. The legacy `FeatureStoreClient` is workspace-scoped and does not propagate schemas into other workspaces\' model registries; registry entries are not how table read access is granted. This mixes up model registry replication with the actual mechanism, metastore-level table access.B. Auto Loader is an ingestion tool for incrementally loading files into a table; it has no role in replicating a Unity Catalog table\'s files into other workspaces\' DBFS mounts. This describes a mechanism that does not exist and misattributes cross-workspace access to the wrong feature.C. Unity Catalog objects are scoped to the metastore, not to a single workspace, so any workspace attached to that metastore can query the table directly by its three-level name. This is exactly why account-level feature tables enable reuse across teams without duplicating pipelines.D. A workspace-level Hive metastore is scoped to a single workspace by definition, so it cannot grant account-wide access — this is the opposite of how workspace-level feature stores behave. It also inverts the premise of the question, which already states the table is account-level."},{"id":"6","question":"A data scientist runs `fe.write_table(name=\\"loan_features\\", df=updates_df, mode=\\"merge\\")` against a feature table whose primary key is the composite `(loan_id, as_of_date)`, and the call fails during validation. `updates_df` contains `loan_id`, `credit_score`, and `utilization_ratio`, but no `as_of_date` column. What is the most likely cause of the failure?","options":{"A":"Merge mode is only supported for tables with a single-column primary key, so a composite-key table like `loan_features` must always use `mode=\\"overwrite\\"` instead.","B":"The DataFrame\'s column order does not match the feature table\'s stored column order, and `write_table` requires primary key columns to appear first in the schema.","C":"The feature table has `publish_table` online replication enabled, and any offline write is blocked until the online store finishes syncing the previous batch.","D":"The DataFrame is missing one of the feature table\'s primary key columns, and `write_table` requires every primary key column to be present so it can determine which rows to update or insert."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. This claim about merge mode is invented: merge mode works the same way regardless of whether the primary key is a single column or a composite key, so restricting composite-key tables to overwrite mode is not an actual constraint of the API.B. Column order in the incoming DataFrame does not matter to `write_table`; it matches columns by name against the feature table\'s schema, so this is not the reason the call fails.C. Publishing to an online store does not place a lock on offline writes to the feature table, so a prior online sync would not block this `write_table` call.D. Because the feature table\'s primary key is `(loan_id, as_of_date)`, every write must supply both columns so the client can identify which existing rows to update and which new rows to insert; omitting `as_of_date` leaves the operation unable to resolve the primary key, which is why validation fails."},{"id":"7","question":"A team is building a feature table for fraud detection where feature values change throughout the day and the training set must only use values that were known at each transaction\'s timestamp, to avoid leaking future information. Which approach correctly creates this table with `FeatureEngineeringClient.create_table`?","options":{"A":"Include the timestamp column in `primary_keys` with the entity key, and pass that column to `timeseries_columns` so lookups respect point-in-time correctness.","B":"Pass the timestamp column only to the `schema` argument as a plain feature column, letting `create_training_set` infer time ordering from row insertion order automatically.","C":"Create the table without a timestamp column, and rely on `fe.write_table` in `merge` mode to always overwrite older feature values before each training run.","D":"Add the timestamp column as a `tags` entry on the table so downstream jobs know which column represents event time during scoring and inference."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. Correct. Time series feature tables require the time column to be part of the primary key and declared through `timeseries_columns`, which is what enables point-in-time lookups during training-set creation.B. Incorrect. Treating the timestamp as a plain schema column gives it no special meaning; `create_training_set` has no mechanism to infer time ordering from insertion order alone.C. Incorrect. Omitting the timestamp key removes any way to look up feature values as of a specific point in time, and `merge` mode write behavior does not substitute for that.D. Incorrect. `tags` store descriptive key-value metadata for humans and catalog search; they are not read by the training-set or lookup logic that enforces point-in-time correctness."},{"id":"8","question":"What is the primary purpose of using the MLflow Client API\'s `search_runs` method with `order_by` and `max_results` parameters, compared to browsing the MLflow Tracking UI\'s runs table?","options":{"A":"It uploads the ranked run results into Unity Catalog as a new registered model version, replacing the separate manual model registration step needed otherwise.","B":"It lets code retrieve a ranked, limited set of runs by metric value, enabling automated best-run selection without manual inspection in the UI.","C":"It permanently deletes every run that does not rank within the requested `max_results` window, keeping only the best-performing runs in the experiment.","D":"It replaces the MLflow Tracking UI entirely, since the UI cannot display metrics, parameters, or artifacts once the client API has been used on an experiment."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Retrieving the best run through `search_runs` does not register anything; registering a model into Unity Catalog is a separate, explicit call to `mlflow.register_model`.B. `search_runs` with `order_by` and `max_results` is built to filter and rank runs programmatically, which is exactly what automated pipelines need to select a best run without a human browsing the UI.C. `search_runs` only queries and returns run metadata; it never deletes runs that happen to fall outside the requested result window.D. The Tracking UI keeps displaying metrics, parameters, and artifacts for an experiment regardless of whether the client API has also been used to query the same runs."},{"id":"9","question":"A workspace administrator with full workspace admin rights repeatedly gets a permission-denied error when running `mlflow.register_model(model_uri, \\"ops.ml.models.fraud_detector\\")` against the `ops.ml.models` schema. What privilege must be granted for the registration to succeed?","options":{"A":"Grant `CAN_MANAGE` on the cluster running the notebook, since compute-level permissions control whether registry write calls are allowed to execute.","B":"Grant `MODIFY` on the `ops` catalog only, since catalog-level `MODIFY` privilege is sufficient to create objects in every schema it contains.","C":"Grant `CREATE MODEL` on the `ops.ml.models` schema in addition to `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA`, since workspace admin rights do not carry Unity Catalog object privileges.","D":"Add the user as a metastore admin in the Unity Catalog metastore, since only metastore-level roles can create new registered models in any schema."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Cluster compute permissions govern who can attach to or manage a cluster; they do not grant or restrict access to Unity Catalog securable objects like schemas or registered models.B. Catalog-level `MODIFY` alone does not substitute for the schema-level `CREATE MODEL` privilege needed to create a new registered model object inside a specific schema; the schema-level grant is still required.C. Unity Catalog privileges are separate from workspace admin rights, so registering a model requires `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` to traverse the namespace plus `CREATE MODEL` on the schema to create the object itself. Missing any of these three produces a permission-denied error regardless of workspace-level roles.D. Metastore admin is a broad administrative role for managing the metastore itself; it is not the minimal privilege intended for day-to-day model registration and is not required just to create a registered model in a schema the user should already have scoped access to."},{"id":"10","question":"A data scientist is training a scikit-learn classifier over 20 epochs inside a `with mlflow.start_run():` block on Databricks and wants the MLflow UI to plot how validation accuracy changes across epochs, not just show a single final number. Which manual logging call, placed inside the training loop, achieves this?","options":{"A":"Call `mlflow.log_param(\\"val_accuracy\\", value)` at the end of every epoch, since parameters and metrics are both rendered as line charts in the run\'s UI page.","B":"Call `mlflow.log_artifact(\\"accuracy_history.json\\")` once after training completes, uploading a JSON file of every epoch\'s value so the UI renders it as a metrics chart automatically.","C":"Call `mlflow.log_metric(\\"val_accuracy\\", value, step=epoch)` once per epoch, passing the epoch number as `step` so the metric history builds one point per epoch.","D":"Call `mlflow.log_metrics({\\"val_accuracy\\": value})` once per epoch without a `step` argument, since batching a dictionary automatically tracks call order as the chart\'s x-axis."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because parameters are meant for static configuration values, are not designed to accept repeated updates to the same key across a run, and are not charted as a time series in the UI.B. This is incorrect because uploaded files are stored as opaque artifacts; MLflow does not parse artifact contents and turn them into interactive metric charts.C. This is correct because passing an explicit `step` value on each call is exactly how MLflow builds a per-step metric history for a key, which the UI then renders as a trend line across epochs.D. This is incorrect because omitting `step` logs every call at the default step of 0, so repeated calls create multiple points stacked at the same step rather than a clean per-epoch trend."},{"id":"11","question":"An experiment has 200 runs logged over several weeks of model iteration. A data scientist wants to view, directly in the MLflow UI run table, only the runs where `params.model_type` is `\\"random_forest\\"` and the logged `metrics.f1_score` is above 0.85. What should they do?","options":{"A":"Open the Compare Runs view and drag a Parallel Coordinates Plot threshold line at `f1_score = 0.85`, which highlights matching lines but does not filter by the `model_type` parameter.","B":"Type a filter expression such as `params.model_type = \\"random_forest\\" and metrics.f1_score > 0.85` into the run table\'s search box, which MLflow parses and applies directly against the logged parameters and metrics.","C":"Click each column header to sort the run table alphabetically by parameter value, then manually scroll through all 200 rows to identify which runs meet both the model type and score conditions.","D":"Export the entire experiment to a CSV file from the Artifacts tab and open it in a spreadsheet application, since the run table\'s own search box is believed to have no way to combine a parameter and a metric condition."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. The Parallel Coordinates Plot can visually emphasize a metric range along one axis, but it does not apply a `model_type` equality condition, so it would not isolate runs matching both criteria.B. The MLflow UI\'s search box accepts a SQL-like filter syntax referencing `params.<name>` and `metrics.<name>` combined with comparison operators and `and`, so this exact expression narrows the run table to runs matching both conditions at once.C. Column-header sorting only reorders visible rows by one field at a time; it provides no way to jointly satisfy a parameter equality condition and a metric threshold without manually scanning every row.D. The run table supports combined parameter and metric filtering natively through its search box, so exporting to a spreadsheet to combine two conditions is unnecessary and the Artifacts tab does not offer a full experiment CSV export in this way."},{"id":"12","question":"A registered model named `pricing_model` carries a `status=deprecated` tag from an earlier retirement decision that has since been reversed. The team wants that tag gone entirely, not just cleared to an empty value, and the tag lives at the registered-model level, not on any single version. Which call removes it correctly?","options":{"A":"`client.delete_registered_model_tag(name=\\"pricing_model\\", key=\\"status\\")` to remove the key from the registered model.","B":"`client.set_registered_model_tag(name=\\"pricing_model\\", key=\\"status\\", value=\\"\\")` to overwrite the value with an empty string.","C":"`client.delete_model_version_tag(name=\\"pricing_model\\", version=1, key=\\"status\\")` to remove the key from the first model version.","D":"`client.transition_model_version_stage(name=\\"pricing_model\\", version=1, stage=\\"Production\\")` to move the version out of the deprecated stage."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. `delete_registered_model_tag` removes the key entirely from the registered model\'s tag set, which matches the requirement to drop the `status` key rather than merely blank out its value.B. Setting the value to an empty string still leaves the `status` key present with an empty value, so it would still show up in tag listings and tag-based searches instead of disappearing.C. This targets a tag on a specific model version rather than the registered model, so it would not touch the model-level `status` tag the team wants removed.D. Unity Catalog\'s Model Registry does not use the legacy workspace-registry stage concept, and even if a stage transition existed here, changing a version\'s stage would not delete a tag on the registered model."},{"id":"13","question":"A data scientist samples a Spark DataFrame down to a small pandas DataFrame inside a notebook and wants a rich, browsable report with per-column data types, missing-value counts, and a small histogram for each numeric column, rendered inline. Which call is designed for this?","options":{"A":"`pandas_df.describe()`, because pandas\' built-in summary method renders the same interactive report, including histograms and missing-value counts, directly inside the notebook cell.","B":"`spark.createDataFrame(pandas_df).summary()`, because converting back to a Spark DataFrame is required to get histogram visualizations that pandas objects cannot render on their own.","C":"`dbutils.data.summarize(pandas_df, precise=True)`, because setting `precise` to `True` is what enables the histogram and missing-value visualizations, which are otherwise left out of the report entirely.","D":"`dbutils.data.summarize(pandas_df)`, because the function accepts pandas DataFrames as well as Spark DataFrames and renders an interactive report with types, null counts, and histograms per column."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. pandas\' `.describe()` returns a plain numeric table of count, mean, stddev, and quartiles with no histograms, type breakdown, or interactive rendering, so it does not meet the visualization requirement.B. `summary()` on a Spark DataFrame returns a plain text-based statistics table, not an interactive report with histograms, so converting the pandas object back to Spark first does not add the requested visualizations.C. The `precise` flag controls whether statistics are computed exactly or approximately; it does not gate whether the histogram and visualization elements of the report are shown, which appear regardless of this setting.D. `dbutils.data.summarize()` works on both pandas and Spark DataFrames and produces an interactive, browsable notebook report with per-column type, null-count, and histogram information, matching exactly what was asked for."},{"id":"14","question":"In the standard IQR-based outlier rule, bounds are set as `Q1 - k*IQR` and `Q3 + k*IQR`. What is the effect of raising `k` from `1.5` to `3` on which values get flagged as outliers?","options":{"A":"Raising `k` to `3` shifts the fences from being based on the interquartile range to being based on the full range of the data, so the dataset\'s minimum and maximum values always become the new fence boundary.","B":"Raising `k` to `3` has no effect on which values get flagged, since the multiplier only changes the reported IQR statistic itself and does not feed into the upper or lower fence calculation at all.","C":"Raising `k` to `3` widens the fences away from the quartiles, so fewer, more extreme values beyond that boundary get flagged, matching \'extreme\' outliers versus the broader set at `1.5`.","D":"Raising `k` to `3` narrows the fences closer to the median, so more values near the center of the distribution get flagged, which increases the total count of rows removed as outliers on the same data."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. The fence formula continues to use `Q1`, `Q3`, and the IQR regardless of the value of `k`; changing `k` never switches the calculation to use the dataset\'s minimum or maximum instead.B. The multiplier `k` is multiplied directly against the IQR inside the fence formula, so changing it from `1.5` to `3` directly changes where the upper and lower bounds fall.C. A larger multiplier pushes both fences further away from the quartiles, so fewer, more extreme points fall outside them; `k=1.5` is the common threshold for moderate outliers and `k=3` for extreme ones.D. Increasing the multiplier widens the fences rather than narrowing them, since `k*IQR` grows as `k` grows, so more values fall inside the boundary and fewer get flagged, not more."},{"id":"15","question":"Which method is the standard approach for measuring the strength and direction of the linear relationship between two continuous features?","options":{"A":"One-hot encoding","B":"Pearson correlation coefficient","C":"Chi-square test of independence","D":"One-way ANOVA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. This technique converts categorical labels into binary indicator columns and is unrelated to measuring the relationship between two continuous features.B. The Pearson correlation coefficient is the standard statistic for measuring the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two continuous features.C. This test compares observed versus expected frequency counts between two categorical features and does not apply to continuous numeric features.D. ANOVA compares the mean of a continuous variable across groups defined by a categorical variable; it does not measure the relationship between two continuous features."},{"id":"16","question":"An analyst suspects the continuous `delivery_time_hours` column has a handful of unusually large values that could be outliers, and wants a single plot that shows the median, the interquartile range, and any points that fall far outside that range. Which visualization satisfies this?","options":{"A":"A box plot of `delivery_time_hours`, drawing the median line, the interquartile box, and points beyond the whiskers as outliers.","B":"A histogram of `delivery_time_hours`, binning the values and counting rows per bin without marking a median or flagging outliers.","C":"A bar chart of `delivery_time_hours`, showing one bar per unique delivery time and computing no quartiles or median at all.","D":"A pie chart of `delivery_time_hours`, dividing the total delivery hours across every row into proportional wedges only."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. A box plot is built specifically from the median and the interquartile range, and it plots any value beyond 1.5 times the IQR from the box as an individual outlier point, matching every requirement in the scenario.B. A histogram summarizes the count of values per bin, which shows overall shape but does not compute or draw a median line or explicitly flag individual outlier points.C. A bar chart of a continuous column like delivery time treats each raw value as a separate category and provides no summary statistic such as a median or interquartile range.D. A pie chart shows proportional shares of a total and has no mechanism for representing quartiles, a median, or outlier points along a numeric range."},{"id":"17","question":"A data scientist is deciding between mean and median imputation for a household `annual_income` feature that is right-skewed by a small number of very high earners. A colleague argues mean imputation is fine because it will not change the overall column average. Which response correctly evaluates that argument?","options":{"A":"The argument is correct, because preserving the exact column mean after imputation is the primary goal of any imputation strategy, regardless of skew.","B":"The argument is irrelevant, because annual income should always be imputed with the mode instead of either the mean or the median for any dataset.","C":"The argument is correct, because a right-skewed distribution has no meaningful median at all, so the mean is presented as the only statistic that can be computed reliably here.","D":"The argument is flawed, because keeping the column average unchanged is not the same as filling in a typical, realistic income for most missing households."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Preserving the exact mean is a side effect of mean imputation, not the actual goal; the relevant question is whether the imputed value is representative, and skew undermines that for the mean.B. Annual income is a continuous numeric feature, so the mode is not the natural choice; this claim also does not engage with the colleague\'s argument about the mean at all.C. A median is well defined for any ordered numeric distribution including a right-skewed one, so this claim about the median being unavailable is factually incorrect.D. Mean imputation trivially preserves the column average by construction, but the goal of imputation is a plausible value per household, and the mean is skewed high by the top earners, misrepresenting most missing rows."},{"id":"18","question":"A data engineer writes the following code to fill missing values in a heavily right-skewed `transaction_amount` column on a Spark DataFrame, expecting the median to be used:\\n```\\nfrom pyspark.ml.feature import Imputer\\nimputer = Imputer(inputCols=[\\"transaction_amount\\"], outputCols=[\\"transaction_amount_filled\\"])\\nmodel = imputer.fit(df)\\n```\\nWhat is the actual result of running this code, and what change is needed to get the intended behavior?","options":{"A":"The code raises an error at `fit` time because `outputCols` must exactly match the names given in `inputCols`, so the two column-name lists need to be made identical before any strategy can be applied.","B":"`Imputer` defaults to `strategy=\'mode\'`, so the most frequent transaction amount is used instead of the median; the code must set `strategy=\'median\'` explicitly to fix this.","C":"`Imputer` defaults to `strategy=\'mean\'`, so the outlier-sensitive mean is used instead of the median; the code must set `strategy=\'median\'` explicitly to match the intended behavior.","D":"`Imputer` defaults to `strategy=\'median\'`, so the code already fills the column with the median as intended, and no change to the constructor arguments is required."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. `inputCols` and `outputCols` are allowed to differ, which is how a new filled column is created alongside the original; this code\'s mismatched names are valid and do not cause a `fit`-time error.B. Mode is not the default strategy for `pyspark.ml.feature.Imputer`; the default is mean, so describing this failure as a mode-based fill misidentifies both the actual behavior and the fix needed.C. `pyspark.ml.feature.Imputer` defaults to the mean strategy when `strategy` is not passed, so this code silently fills the skewed column with an outlier-sensitive mean rather than the intended median, and `strategy=\'median\'` must be added explicitly.D. The default strategy for `pyspark.ml.feature.Imputer` is mean, not median, so this code does not already produce the intended behavior and would fill the skewed column with a value pulled toward its outliers."},{"id":"19","question":"A model trained with `OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\'ignore\')` on a `subscription_tier` feature is deployed for real-time scoring in Databricks Model Serving. In production, a request arrives containing a new tier value, `enterprise-plus`, that did not exist in the training data. What happens when this request is scored?","options":{"A":"The encoder maps `enterprise-plus` to the most frequent training category, silently substituting it in place of the unseen value before the row reaches the model.","B":"The encoder outputs a row of all zeros for `subscription_tier`, so the model scores the request using no information from that feature rather than raising an error.","C":"The encoder raises a `ValueError` and the scoring request fails, because `handle_unknown=\'ignore\'` only suppresses warnings during `.fit()` and has no effect during `.transform()`.","D":"The encoder automatically creates a new binary column for `enterprise-plus` on the fly, expanding the model\'s input dimensionality to match the newly observed category."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Incorrect — `OneHotEncoder` has no built-in logic for substituting the most frequent category for unseen values; that behavior would require a separate imputation step, and `handle_unknown=\'ignore\'` produces all zeros, not a substituted category.B. Correct — with `handle_unknown=\'ignore\'` set at fit time, any category not seen during training is encoded as all zeros across that feature\'s one-hot columns at transform time, letting the pipeline continue scoring instead of raising an error, though the model then has no signal from that feature for this row.C. Incorrect — `handle_unknown=\'ignore\'` specifically changes behavior at `.transform()` time; the default `handle_unknown=\'error\'` is what raises a `ValueError` on unseen categories, so describing the parameter as fit-time-only reverses its actual purpose.D. Incorrect — a fitted `OneHotEncoder` has a fixed set of output columns determined during `.fit()`; it cannot expand the number of columns at transform time, since that would change the input shape the downstream model was trained to expect."},{"id":"20","question":"A data scientist is building a gradient-boosted tree ensemble to classify support tickets using a `category` column with 40 distinct values. A colleague suggests one-hot encoding it the same way they would for the logistic regression baseline. What should the data scientist consider before doing so?","options":{"A":"One-hot encoding a 40-level column produces exactly the same split quality as leaving it as ordinal integers, so the choice between the two encodings has no effect on this model.","B":"Tree-based models split on individual feature values, so encoding `category` with `OrdinalEncoder` instead often keeps the feature compact while the trees still learn useful splits.","C":"One-hot encoding is required before fitting any scikit-learn estimator, tree-based or otherwise, so the suggestion to encode `category` the same way is the only technically valid option here.","D":"Gradient-boosted trees cannot process categorical data in any encoded form, so the `category` column must be dropped from the feature set entirely before training."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. The two encodings are not interchangeable in effect: one-hot encoding gives the tree a separate binary split per category while ordinal encoding forces splits along a single learned numeric ordering, which can change which splits the model finds and how many are needed.B. Because tree ensembles choose split thresholds on individual columns rather than forming a linear combination of features, an ordinal-encoded categorical column can still be split on effectively, avoiding the wide, sparse feature set that one-hot encoding a 40-level column would create.C. Some scikit-learn estimators and gradient-boosting implementations can accept categorical or ordinal-encoded inputs directly, so one-hot encoding every categorical column before every estimator is not a universal requirement.D. Gradient-boosted trees can consume encoded categorical features just fine, whether ordinal integers or one-hot columns, so dropping the column outright discards a feature the model could otherwise use."},{"id":"21","question":"A data scientist builds a scikit-learn `Pipeline` with steps `[(\'scaler\', StandardScaler()), (\'pca\', PCA(n_components=10)), (\'clf\', LogisticRegression())]`. A teammate asks why `StandardScaler` and `PCA` can occupy the middle positions while `LogisticRegression` can only occupy the last position. What is the correct explanation?","options":{"A":"Only the first step in a `Pipeline` needs a `fit` method; each later step, including `LogisticRegression`, must implement `transform` to reshape features before the classifier scores them.","B":"`Pipeline` runs all steps independently and in parallel on the raw input, so `StandardScaler` and `PCA` never actually see the output of each other before `LogisticRegression` is trained.","C":"Every step before the last one must implement `fit` and `transform` so its output can feed the next step, while only the final step is allowed to be a plain estimator like a classifier.","D":"`Pipeline` requires every step, including the last one, to implement `transform`, so `pipe.transform()` always returns a fitted feature matrix no matter which estimator sits at the end."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. This misstates the rule: it is every step except the last that needs `transform`, not just the first, and `LogisticRegression` is deliberately exempt from implementing `transform` precisely because it is the final estimator that produces predictions.B. `Pipeline` executes its steps sequentially, not in parallel; each step\'s `fit_transform` output is passed directly as the input to the next step, which is exactly why `PCA` operates on the scaled output of `StandardScaler` rather than on raw features.C. This is correct: scikit-learn\'s `Pipeline` contract requires every step except the last to implement both `fit` and `transform`, because each step\'s transformed output becomes the next step\'s input, and only the final step is allowed to be a non-transformer such as a classifier or regressor.D. This is backwards; `Pipeline` does not require the last step to implement `transform` at all. When the last step is a classifier like `LogisticRegression`, the pipeline exposes `predict` instead, and calling `.transform()` on it raises an `AttributeError`."},{"id":"22","question":"A data scientist on Databricks is predicting a continuous target, total monthly revenue, from five numeric features. Diagnostic plots show a roughly straight-line relationship between each feature and revenue, and the business asks for a model whose coefficients can be quoted directly to stakeholders as \\"revenue changes by X per unit of feature Y.\\" Which model best fits this scenario?","options":{"A":"Logistic regression, because it estimates class probabilities from a linear combination of features and outputs coefficients that translate into odds ratios for reporting.","B":"A random forest regressor, because averaging many decision trees reduces variance and produces a feature importance ranking stakeholders can read as each feature\'s relative contribution to revenue.","C":"A k-means clustering model, because grouping similar revenue records together and presenting the resulting cluster centers as typical revenue profiles per feature satisfies the reporting request.","D":"Linear regression, because it fits a continuous target with a linear relationship to the predictors and produces coefficients that directly quantify each feature\'s effect on the outcome."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. This is incorrect because logistic regression models the probability of a categorical (typically binary) outcome, not a continuous target like monthly revenue, so its coefficients describe log-odds rather than a direct revenue effect.B. This is incorrect because a random forest is an ensemble of decision trees without a single coefficient per feature; feature importance ranks relative influence but cannot be quoted as a per-unit revenue change the way a linear coefficient can.C. This is incorrect because k-means is an unsupervised clustering technique that groups observations by similarity and does not model or predict a target variable like revenue at all.D. This is correct because linear regression is designed for continuous targets with linear predictor relationships, and its fitted coefficients have a direct, interpretable meaning: the expected change in the target per unit change in a feature."},{"id":"23","question":"A team building a churn model on Databricks decides to apply SMOTE to the minority (churned) class before training. A reviewer asks how SMOTE actually generates its additional training rows. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"It creates new synthetic rows by interpolating between a minority-class sample and one of its nearest minority neighbors.","B":"It copies existing minority-class rows verbatim and appends the copies to the training set until the classes are balanced.","C":"It removes majority-class rows nearest to the decision boundary so the remaining classes end up closer in size.","D":"It reweights the loss function so minority-class errors count more, without adding or removing any rows from the dataset."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. SMOTE generates a new point along the line segment between a minority-class sample and one of its k-nearest minority-class neighbors, producing genuinely new feature values rather than exact copies. This interpolation is the defining mechanism of the technique.B. Verbatim duplication describes random oversampling, not SMOTE; SMOTE is specifically designed to avoid exact duplicates by synthesizing new feature vectors instead of repeating rows.C. Removing majority-class rows near the boundary describes an undersampling strategy such as Tomek links or edited nearest neighbors, not SMOTE, which only adds minority-class rows.D. Adjusting the loss function without changing the dataset describes class weighting, a separate mitigation technique that leaves the row count untouched, unlike SMOTE."},{"id":"24","question":"A data scientist is configuring Hyperopt\'s `fmin` and wants the search to use a Bayesian-style approach that adapts its choice of the next hyperparameter set based on the outcomes of earlier trials, rather than sampling each trial independently of the others. Which `algo` argument accomplishes this?","options":{"A":"`algo=Trials()`, which stores the history of completed evaluations for later inspection but does not itself influence how the next candidate gets selected.","B":"`algo=tpe.suggest`, which implements the Tree-structured Parzen Estimator and models prior trial outcomes to propose the next, more promising candidate.","C":"`algo=rand.suggest`, which draws each candidate independently from the defined search space and therefore reaches a comparable result using less per-trial computation overall.","D":"`algo=hp.choice`, which defines a categorical hyperparameter dimension within the search space and does not control how candidates are proposed across successive trials."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. `Trials()` is the object passed to `fmin` to record evaluation history for later inspection; it is not a valid value for `algo` and does not itself decide how the next candidate is chosen.B. This is correct: `tpe.suggest` is Hyperopt\'s Bayesian-style algorithm, and it explicitly conditions each new candidate on the distribution of hyperparameter values that scored well versus poorly in earlier trials.C. `rand.suggest` implements plain random search, drawing each trial\'s candidate independently of every prior trial, which is the opposite of the adaptive, history-informed behavior being asked for.D. `hp.choice` is used inside the search-space definition to declare a categorical parameter; it plays no role in the `algo` argument and does not determine how successive candidates are proposed."},{"id":"25","question":"In scikit-learn\'s `GridSearchCV`, what determines the total number of model fits performed during the search phase, before any optional final refit on the full training data?","options":{"A":"The number of cross-validation folds alone, since every hyperparameter combination shares the same set of fold splits","B":"The sum of the number of hyperparameter combinations and the number of cross-validation folds specified by `cv`","C":"The product of the number of hyperparameter combinations in the grid and the number of cross-validation folds specified by `cv`","D":"The number of hyperparameter combinations in the grid alone, since each combination is scored once regardless of the fold count"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. This ignores that a distinct model is trained for every hyperparameter combination within each fold, not just once per fold. Sharing the same fold splits across combinations does not mean the combinations share trained models.B. Adding the combination count and fold count instead of multiplying them misrepresents the nested structure of the search, where every combination is independently retrained across all folds rather than folds and combinations being counted separately.C. `GridSearchCV` trains a distinct model for every hyperparameter combination on every fold\'s training split, so the total search-phase fit count is the product of the combination count and the fold count, matching scikit-learn\'s documented behavior.D. This ignores that each combination must still be trained separately on each fold to produce a cross-validated score. Fold count is not incidental; it directly multiplies the number of fits performed for every combination in the grid."},{"id":"26","question":"A data scientist notices that a single train-validation split on a customer dataset happened to place nearly all of one important customer segment into the training partition and almost none into the validation partition, making the validation score misleading. Which statement correctly describes how k-fold cross-validation relates to this specific problem?","options":{"A":"Cross-validation completely eliminates this segment-imbalance problem by removing all randomness from fold assignment, guaranteeing every fold contains an identical distribution of every customer segment.","B":"Cross-validation does not affect this problem at all, because folds are drawn from the dataset using the exact same random assignment process a single train-validation split already uses.","C":"Cross-validation only fixes this problem once the fold count is raised to match the number of customer segments present, so fold count must track segment count rather than dataset size.","D":"Cross-validation rotates which rows serve as the test fold, so no single arbitrary partition dominates the result, and a stratified k-fold variant additionally preserves segment proportions in every fold."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Standard k-fold cross-validation still assigns rows to folds using randomized partitioning by default and does not guarantee identical segment distributions across folds unless a stratified variant is explicitly used.B. Cross-validation differs meaningfully from a single split because it averages results across multiple rotating partitions rather than relying on one fixed assignment, so it is not equivalent to repeating the same random process once.C. The number of folds is a general hyperparameter typically chosen based on dataset size and compute budget (commonly 5 or 10), not a value that must be matched to the number of customer segments present in the data.D. This is correct: because each row eventually serves as test data across the rotation of folds, no single arbitrary partition dominates the reported metric, and `StratifiedKFold` goes further by explicitly preserving the proportion of each class or segment within every fold, directly addressing the described imbalance."},{"id":"27","question":"A binary classification dataset is severely imbalanced, with 95% negative and 5% positive labels. An engineer wants to run k-fold cross-validation while keeping the same 95/5 class ratio inside every fold. Which cross-validator should they use?","options":{"A":"StratifiedKFold","B":"KFold","C":"ShuffleSplit","D":"LeaveOneOut"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. `StratifiedKFold` builds each fold so the proportion of each class matches the proportion in the full dataset, which keeps the rare positive class represented in every fold\'s train and test split.B. Plain `KFold` splits rows into folds without regard to class labels, so with a 95/5 imbalance it can easily produce folds where the minority class is underrepresented or missing entirely.C. `ShuffleSplit` generates random train/test partitions with possible overlap between iterations and does not account for class proportions, so it offers no built-in guarantee of preserving the 95/5 ratio.D. `LeaveOneOut` holds out a single row per iteration and trains on the rest, which is computationally expensive on larger data and does nothing to balance class proportions within folds."},{"id":"28","question":"After training a regression model to predict house prices, a machine learning engineer evaluates it on a holdout set and finds an R-squared value of -0.15. What does this result indicate about the model?","options":{"A":"Root mean squared error and mean absolute error must both equal zero for this model, because a negative coefficient of determination only occurs when squared error terms fully cancel out","B":"The model fits the holdout data worse than a naive baseline that always predicts the average house price, so the learned relationship is actively misleading rather than simply weak","C":"The model explains about fifteen percent less variance than a perfect predictor, which is a mediocre but still broadly acceptable result for an early modeling iteration","D":"The negative score signals a data leakage bug in the training pipeline, since the coefficient of determination is mathematically bounded between zero and one"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Root mean squared error and mean absolute error are unrelated in magnitude to whether R-squared is negative, and neither metric is forced to zero by a negative coefficient of determination.B. R-squared compares the model\'s squared error against the squared error of always predicting the mean; a negative value means the fitted model performs worse than that trivial baseline, which is a genuine warning sign rather than a rounding artifact.C. R-squared is not a percentage scaled from zero to one hundred in this way, and a negative value does not mean the model is merely fifteen percent worse than perfect; it means the model underperforms the mean-prediction baseline.D. The coefficient of determination is only bounded above by one; it is unbounded below and can legitimately go negative whenever a model fits worse than the mean, so a negative value alone does not indicate a pipeline bug."},{"id":"29","question":"A data scientist sets `n_neighbors=1` for a k-nearest neighbors classifier and observes that predictions change drastically when a single training point near the decision boundary is removed. Which statement best explains this behavior and how to address it?","options":{"A":"The instability comes from an unscaled feature space rather than model complexity; standardizing the features while keeping `n_neighbors=1` would make predictions stable regardless of neighbor count.","B":"k-nearest neighbors is a parametric model, so its complexity is fixed by the feature count rather than `n_neighbors`; the instability must come from a bug in the training pipeline.","C":"With `n_neighbors=1`, model complexity is effectively maximized because each prediction depends on one nearby point, producing high variance; increasing `n_neighbors` trades some variance for bias.","D":"With `n_neighbors=1`, the model averages over too many neighbors, producing high bias; decreasing `n_neighbors` further would smooth the boundary and reduce sensitivity to individual points."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Feature scaling affects which points are considered nearest, but it does not remove the fundamental instability caused by basing every prediction on a single neighbor. Even on perfectly scaled features, a one-neighbor model remains maximally sensitive to individual training points.B. k-nearest neighbors is a classic example of a non-parametric method, and `n_neighbors` is precisely the hyperparameter that controls its effective complexity. The observed instability is expected behavior for this setting, not evidence of a pipeline defect.C. A single-neighbor classifier draws a highly irregular decision boundary that hugs each individual training point, so removing or adding one point can flip nearby predictions — the hallmark of high variance. Increasing `n_neighbors` averages over more points, smoothing the boundary and accepting some bias in exchange for lower variance.D. With only one neighbor there is no averaging happening at all, so this describes the opposite regime from what `n_neighbors=1` actually produces. Decreasing `n_neighbors` below 1 is not possible and would not smooth anything even if it were."},{"id":"30","question":"A data scientist computes MAE directly on a model\'s `np.log1p`-transformed predictions and the corresponding `np.log1p`-transformed actual values, then tells the business team \\"our model is off by an average of 0.18.\\" What is wrong with this statement?","options":{"A":"An MAE of 0.18 is measured in log-transformed units, not the target\'s original units, so it does not describe how far off predictions are in real terms.","B":"MAE cannot be computed on log-transformed data at all, so the reported value of 0.18 is not a valid metric and the whole calculation must be redone.","C":"The statement is correct as written, because MAE computed on log-transformed values is always numerically identical to MAE on the original, untransformed target.","D":"The value 0.18 is implausibly small for any regression metric, which suggests the model was almost certainly trained against the wrong target column."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. This is correct: 0.18 is an average absolute difference in log-space, which does not translate linearly into a dollar or unit difference; exponentiating predictions and actuals first is needed before the error can be described in the target\'s real-world units.B. MAE is a perfectly valid calculation on log-transformed arrays; the numeric computation itself succeeds, the problem is only that the resulting figure is in the wrong units for the business audience.C. MAE computed in log space and MAE computed in original units are generally different numbers, especially for right-skewed targets, because the log transform compresses large values disproportionately.D. A small MAE value like 0.18 is exactly what is expected when errors are measured on a compressed log scale, so its size alone does not indicate a wrong target column was used."},{"id":"31","question":"A team has trained a custom scikit-learn fraud-detection model and registered it in Unity Catalog. The application must return a prediction within 50 milliseconds of each transaction as it happens, and transactions arrive continuously throughout the day. Which approach should the team use to serve predictions?","options":{"A":"Schedule a nightly Lakeflow job that loads the model with `mlflow.pyfunc.load_model` and scores the day\'s transactions in bulk through a vectorized pandas UDF on a Spark DataFrame.","B":"Register the model inside a Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipeline flow so it scores transaction records as they land in a bronze streaming table before downstream aggregation runs on them.","C":"Load the model inside a notebook and rerun the scoring cell interactively whenever an analyst opens a flagged transaction, recording the resulting score by hand in a shared tracking table.","D":"Deploy the registered model to a Model Serving endpoint as a served entity, then have the transaction service call the endpoint\'s REST API synchronously for each incoming transaction."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. A nightly batch job scores transactions long after they occur, so it cannot meet a requirement that each transaction be scored the moment it arrives.B. A streaming pipeline flow scores records as micro-batches land in a table, which adds pipeline trigger and table-commit latency that is far larger than the required per-transaction response time.C. Manually rerunning a notebook cell depends on an analyst being available and cannot deliver a consistent, automated response within a fixed millisecond budget for every transaction.D. A Model Serving endpoint keeps the model loaded and ready to respond to individual REST calls with single-digit-to-double-digit millisecond overhead, which matches a per-transaction, sub-50ms latency requirement."},{"id":"32","question":"A data engineer has a Delta table with 50 million transaction rows and a model registered at `models:/fraud_detector/Production`. They need to score every row and write predictions to a new Delta table while distributing the scoring work across the cluster. Which approach should they use?","options":{"A":"Skip Spark and call `ai_query()` from a SQL notebook cell against a deployed serving endpoint, since it removes the need to load the model or write any DataFrame or pandas UDF code here.","B":"Call `mlflow.pyfunc.load_model(\\"models:/fraud_detector/Production\\")` on the driver, convert the table to pandas with `.toPandas()`, then run a single `.predict()` call over the whole DataFrame at once.","C":"Load the model with `mlflow.pyfunc.spark_udf(spark, \\"models:/fraud_detector/Production\\")` and add predictions to the Spark DataFrame via `withColumn`, letting executors score partitions in parallel.","D":"Register the model on a real-time Model Serving endpoint and loop over the 50 million rows in the driver notebook, sending one REST request per transaction to the endpoint."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. This SQL function can call a served model per row, but it requires a live serving endpoint to already be deployed and bypasses pandas or Spark DataFrame scoring altogether, so it does not match a pandas-based batch scoring workflow over an existing Delta table.B. Collecting 50 million rows into a single pandas DataFrame with `.toPandas()` pulls the entire dataset into driver memory, which risks an out-of-memory failure and abandons the cluster\'s distributed executors entirely. It also removes any parallelism, since one `.predict()` call runs sequentially on the driver.C. Wrapping the registered model as a pandas UDF with `mlflow.pyfunc.spark_udf` and applying it through `withColumn` lets each executor load the model once and score its own partition in parallel, which is the standard pattern for distributed batch inference over a large Delta table.D. Issuing 50 million individual REST calls from a single driver notebook is extremely slow, adds network latency per row, and does not use the cluster\'s compute for scoring at all, making it impractical at this scale."},{"id":"33","question":"A team is deploying a large image-classification deep learning model to a Model Serving endpoint and finds that CPU-based serving does not meet the required response latency under production load. Which change to the served entity\'s configuration is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"Add a second served entity with the identical CPU configuration and split traffic evenly, since doubling CPU replicas replaces the need for GPU acceleration.","B":"Change the served entity\'s `workload_type` to a GPU option such as `GPU_SMALL` or `GPU_MEDIUM` so inference runs on accelerator hardware sized for the model.","C":"Increase the `entity_version` number on the served entity, since a higher version number automatically provisions faster underlying serving hardware.","D":"Disable `scale_to_zero_enabled` and leave `workload_type` on `CPU`, since keeping compute always warm resolves latency caused by GPU-only architectures."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Adding more CPU replicas increases throughput for concurrent requests but does not reduce the per-request inference time the way GPU acceleration does for a compute-heavy model.B. Setting `workload_type` to a GPU option moves inference onto accelerator hardware, which is the configuration change that addresses latency for a compute-heavy deep learning model that CPU serving cannot meet.C. `entity_version` identifies which trained model artifact is served; incrementing it does not change or upgrade the underlying compute hardware assigned to the served entity.D. Keeping CPU compute warm avoids cold-start delay but does not add the parallel compute throughput a GPU provides, so it does not resolve latency caused by an under-powered CPU workload type."},{"id":"34","question":"A single Lakeflow pipeline defines two flows: one applies AUTO CDC logic to merge inserts, updates, and deletes into a `customers` dimension table, and the other continuously appends model predictions onto incoming clickstream events using `mlflow.pyfunc.spark_udf`. Which statement best describes how these two flows relate to each other?","options":{"A":"Each flow independently reads its own source, applies its own transformation, and writes to its own target dataset, so the CDC merge flow and the model-scoring flow can both run in the same pipeline.","B":"Both flows must write into the same target table, because a single pipeline is limited to exactly one streaming table target, so the CDC output and the scored predictions have to be unioned before being written.","C":"The AUTO CDC flow always executes only after every other flow in the pipeline has completed, because change-data-capture flows are scheduled last regardless of the dependency graph between datasets.","D":"Applying a model UDF inside a flow automatically converts that flow\'s target into a materialized view, because Lakeflow treats any UDF-based transformation as forcing a full, batch-style recompute."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. This is correct: a flow reads from a source, applies its own transformation, and writes to its own target, so an AUTO CDC merge flow and a model-scoring flow can coexist as independent flows within one pipeline.B. A pipeline is not limited to a single streaming table target; each flow can write to its own distinct target dataset, so unioning the CDC output with scored predictions is not required.C. Flow execution order is derived from the dependency graph between datasets, not a fixed rule that change-data-capture flows always run last regardless of what they depend on.D. Using a UDF, including a model-scoring UDF, inside a flow does not change the target\'s dataset type; a streaming table continues to process data incrementally regardless of the transformation applied."},{"id":"35","question":"A team is rolling out a new pricing model as a canary. They start it at 5% of endpoint traffic, and over the following days plan to raise its share to 25%, then 50%, then 100% as confidence grows, while the old version keeps serving the remainder at each stage. What is the main operational advantage of doing this rollout through the endpoint\'s traffic split instead of switching client applications to a new endpoint URL at each stage?","options":{"A":"The endpoint automatically reverts the traffic_config to its previous percentages whenever the new version average response time increases by any measurable amount during the rollout.","B":"Traffic-split rollouts remove the need for the new pricing model to be registered in Unity Catalog before it can receive any share of the endpoint\'s live traffic.","C":"Splitting traffic through one endpoint guarantees the new and old pricing models will return numerically identical predictions for any request routed during the rollout.","D":"Client applications keep calling the same endpoint URL throughout the rollout, so each traffic_config update changes routing behind the scenes without any client redeployment."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Databricks does not automatically roll back traffic percentages based on observed latency changes; adjusting or reverting the split in response to performance is a manual decision the team makes using metrics from tools like inference tables.B. A served entity must still reference a model registered in Unity Catalog before it can be added to the endpoint and receive any traffic share, so registration remains a prerequisite regardless of the rollout strategy.C. Splitting traffic routes each request to one model or the other; it does not make the two different model versions produce identical outputs, since they are distinct trained artifacts making independent predictions.D. Because the served entities and their traffic split live inside one endpoint\'s configuration, raising the canary\'s share from 5% to 25% to 50% only requires updating that endpoint\'s traffic_config, so calling applications never need to change the URL they invoke at any stage."}]},{"id":"databricks-machine-learning-professional","title":"Databricks Certified Machine Learning Professional","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"An engineer configures `TorchDistributor.init(num_processes=4, local_mode=True, use_gpu=True)` and calls `.run(train_fn)` on a Databricks cluster with a driver that has 4 GPUs and several worker nodes attached. Where does the training actually execute?","options":{"A":"The 4 processes are spread one per worker node across the cluster, since `local_mode=True` always maps each process to a distinct physical node.","B":"TorchDistributor ignores `local_mode` when `use_gpu=True` and always distributes processes across every available GPU in the entire cluster.","C":"The call raises an error because `num_processes` must equal the total worker node count whenever `local_mode` is explicitly set to `True`.","D":"All 4 processes run on the single driver machine, since `local_mode=True` confines TorchDistributor to launching multiple processes on one node."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Spreading one process per worker node is the behavior of multi-node mode (`local_mode=False`), not local mode, which intentionally keeps every process on one machine.B. `use_gpu` only controls whether processes use GPU devices; it does not override `local_mode`, which independently governs whether execution stays on one node or spans the cluster.C. `num_processes` in local mode specifies how many processes run on the single local machine and has no required relationship to the number of worker nodes in the cluster.D. Correct: `local_mode=True` tells TorchDistributor to launch the specified number of processes on a single machine, so all 4 GPU processes run on the driver rather than being spread across workers."},{"id":"2","question":"A hyperparameter search currently uses Hyperopt\'s `fmin` with `SparkTrials`. Which of the following are accurate reasons, per current Databricks guidance, to migrate this workload away from Hyperopt? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Migrating away from Hyperopt is only necessary for teams using GPU clusters, since CPU-only clusters continue to include Hyperopt indefinitely.","B":"Hyperopt has been removed from Databricks Runtime ML versions released after 16.4 LTS, so it is no longer available by default on current runtimes.","C":"Optuna with `MlflowSparkStudy` provides a currently supported path for Spark-distributed hyperparameter trials with MLflow-backed shared state.","D":"Ray Tune is named alongside Optuna as a currently recommended option for distributed hyperparameter tuning on Databricks.","E":"Hyperopt was deprecated because it never supported any form of parallel trial execution, unlike every currently recommended tuning library."},"explanation":"A. Incorrect: the removal of Hyperopt from Databricks Runtime ML after 16.4 LTS applies generally, not only to GPU clusters, so CPU-only clusters on current runtimes are affected too.F. Incorrect: Optuna integrates directly with MLflow through `MlflowStorage` and `MlflowSparkStudy`, so migrating away from Hyperopt does not require giving up MLflow tracking.B. Correct: Hyperopt\'s removal from Databricks Runtime ML after version 16.4 LTS is exactly the documented reason current-runtime users lose access to it and must migrate.C. Correct: `MlflowSparkStudy` combined with `MlflowStorage` is the documented current replacement path for Spark-distributed trials with shared, MLflow-backed state.D. Correct: current guidance names Ray Tune, alongside Optuna, as a supported option for distributed hyperparameter tuning going forward.E. Incorrect: Hyperopt did support parallel trial execution through `SparkTrials`; the deprecation is about runtime removal, not an inherent inability to run trials in parallel.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C","D"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"3","question":"A recommendation service needs millisecond-latency reads against a feature table for online inference. The team already has the feature table in Unity Catalog and wants to provision the low-latency copy using the Databricks SDK. What is the correct approach?","options":{"A":"Use `dbutils.fs.cp` to copy the feature table\'s underlying Parquet files onto the serving cluster\'s local disk before the endpoint starts.","B":"Build an `OnlineTableSpec` naming the table and keys, wrap it in an `OnlineTable`, and call `online_tables.create_and_wait(table=t)`.","C":"Call `spark.sql(\\"CACHE TABLE feature_table\\")` in the notebook, which SDK docs describe as the supported way to provision online tables.","D":"Set the feature table\'s `delta.enableChangeDataFeed` property to `true` and query it directly from the endpoint, skipping any online table object."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Copying raw Parquet files onto local disk bypasses the managed online table infrastructure entirely and does not produce anything a serving endpoint or `FeatureLookup` can query.B. This is the documented SDK pattern: an `OnlineTableSpec` describes the source table and keys, an `OnlineTable` object wraps that spec, and `create_and_wait` provisions the low-latency online copy.C. `CACHE TABLE` caches a DataFrame in cluster memory for the life of that Spark session; it does not create a durable, low-latency online table and is not part of the online table provisioning API.D. Enabling Change Data Feed is a prerequisite for some publishing modes, but it does not by itself create an online table; a serving endpoint cannot query an unprovisioned online table just because CDF is on."},{"id":"4","question":"A team is designing a Structured Streaming job that reads raw events and writes aggregated features into a feature table via `fe.write_table(mode=\\"merge\\")`. Which of the following are genuine design considerations for this streaming feature pipeline?(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Applying a watermark on the event-time column to bound how long the job retains state for late-arriving events.","B":"Picking a trigger interval that matches how fresh the feature values need to be, such as a fixed processing time or `availableNow`.","C":"Setting the write mode to `\\"overwrite\\"` instead of `\\"merge\\"`, since only `\\"overwrite\\"` is supported once a streaming source is involved.","D":"Disabling Change Data Feed on the feature table, since streaming writes are documented as incompatible with Change Data Feed.","E":"Choosing a checkpoint location so the streaming query can recover its progress and avoid reprocessing or dropping events after a restart."},"explanation":"A. A watermark on event time bounds how long windowed aggregation state is retained, which is necessary to keep the job\'s memory and state store from growing without limit as late events trickle in.B. The trigger interval controls how often micro-batches run and therefore how fresh the written feature values are, so it should be chosen to match the freshness the downstream use case needs.C. `\\"merge\\"` is the only mode `write_table` supports for both batch and streaming input; there is no requirement or need to switch to `\\"overwrite\\"` for streaming sources.D. Change Data Feed is unrelated to whether the write side is streaming; it is a source-table property used for downstream incremental consumption and is not something streaming writes require disabling.F. Merge writes do not require single-partition output; forcing one partition would only hurt throughput and is not a documented requirement of `write_table`.E. A checkpoint location lets Structured Streaming persist offsets and state, so the job can resume correctly after a restart instead of reprocessing or losing events.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"5","question":"A training script computes accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 score at the end of each epoch and currently makes four separate `mlflow.log_metric()` calls per epoch inside a tight loop over 200 epochs, resulting in 800 individual tracking-server calls. An engineer wants to reduce the number of network round trips while logging the exact same information. Which change accomplishes this?","options":{"A":"Replace the four calls with `mlflow.log_param({\\"accuracy\\": acc, \\"precision\\": prec, \\"recall\\": rec, \\"f1\\": f1})`, since parameters accept dictionaries natively.","B":"Wrap the existing four calls inside `mlflow.start_run(nested=True)` for each epoch, which batches all metric writes from that nested run into one request.","C":"Call `mlflow.log_artifact()` once per epoch with a JSON file containing all four values, since artifacts always require fewer network calls than metrics.","D":"Replace the four `log_metric` calls with a single `mlflow.log_metrics({\\"accuracy\\": acc, \\"precision\\": prec, \\"recall\\": rec, \\"f1\\": f1}, step=epoch)` call."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. `log_param` stores fixed configuration values and is not intended for per-epoch numeric series; using it here also does not solve the batching goal, since it is still a per-call operation.B. Opening a nested run per epoch adds run-management overhead and does not batch metric writes into fewer calls; it would in fact multiply the number of tracking-server interactions.C. Uploading a JSON artifact each epoch still requires one call per epoch and adds file-storage overhead, and it loses the native metric charting that `log_metrics` provides in the UI.D. `log_metrics` accepts a dictionary and sends all the named values for a given step in a single tracking-server call, which cuts four calls down to one per epoch while logging identical data."},{"id":"6","question":"After training, an engineer has both a single serialized preprocessing object, `scaler.pkl`, and an entire local folder, `evaluation_report/`, containing several plots and a metrics CSV. They want to log the single file and the whole folder to the current MLflow run as artifacts. Which pair of calls is correct?","options":{"A":"`mlflow.log_artifact(\\"scaler.pkl\\")` and `mlflow.log_artifact(\\"evaluation_report\\")`, since the singular form automatically detects whether the path is a file or a directory.","B":"`mlflow.log_model(\\"scaler.pkl\\")` for the single file, and `mlflow.log_artifacts(\\"evaluation_report\\")` for the folder, since only `log_model` supports serialized Python objects.","C":"`mlflow.log_artifact(\\"scaler.pkl\\")` for the single file, and `mlflow.log_artifacts(\\"evaluation_report\\")` for the folder, since the plural form uploads a directory\'s contents.","D":"`mlflow.log_artifacts(\\"scaler.pkl\\")` for the single file, and `mlflow.log_artifact(\\"evaluation_report\\")` for the folder, since the plural form is required for any file with an extension."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. `log_artifact` does not accept a directory path; passing a folder to it raises an error, since only `log_artifacts` is designed to recurse through a directory\'s contents.B. `log_model` is for logging a trained model in a specific ML flavor format with a signature, not for arbitrary serialized files, and it does not accept a directory of mixed report files.C. `log_artifact` uploads one local file, while `log_artifacts` walks a local directory and uploads its full contents, so pairing them this way matches each call to its intended input type.D. This reverses the actual behavior: `log_artifacts` expects a directory path and `log_artifact` expects a single file path, regardless of whether that file has a recognizable extension."},{"id":"7","question":"A team enables `mlflow.autolog()` at the top of their training script for a scikit-learn `RandomForestClassifier`, which automatically logs the model\'s hyperparameters, training metrics, and the fitted model artifact. They also want to additionally log a custom business metric, projected cost savings, that autologging has no way of knowing about. Which statements about combining autologging with custom logging are correct?(Select 2)","options":{"A":"Enabling autologging replaces the need to call `mlflow.start_run()` at all, since autologging manages its own separate run outside of the script\'s control.","B":"A custom call like `mlflow.log_metric(\\"projected_cost_savings\\", value)` can be added inside the same active run that `autolog()` is populating, without disabling autologging.","C":"Autologging and manual `log_metric` calls write to the same active run object, so both the automatic and the custom metrics appear together on one run\'s page.","D":"Calling `mlflow.autolog()` locks the active run against any further logging calls, so custom metrics must be logged in a completely separate follow-up run.","E":"The custom `projected_cost_savings` metric must be named with an `autolog_` prefix, or the autologging integration will silently discard it during the run."},"explanation":"A. Autologging still requires an active run, either one explicitly started with `start_run()` or one it creates automatically if none is active; it does not remove the need for run management.F. If a custom metric name collides with one autologging already logs, the later call simply overwrites the earlier value for that metric name, which is not guaranteed to favor either source and can be surprising rather than a documented precedence rule.B. Autologging populates whatever run is active without preventing additional manual logging calls, so a custom metric like cost savings can be logged into that same run alongside the autologged values.C. Both autologging and manual calls target the currently active MLflow run, so a reviewer opening that run\'s page sees the automatically captured metrics together with any manually logged custom ones.D. Autologging does not lock the run against further calls; it simply adds its own logging calls under the hood while leaving the run fully open to any additional manual logging the script performs.E. There is no naming convention required for custom metrics to coexist with autologged ones; any metric name not already used by the autologging integration logs normally.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"8","question":"A machine learning engineer is assembling a Spark ML `Pipeline` with a `StringIndexer`, a `OneHotEncoder`, a `VectorAssembler`, and a `LogisticRegression` estimator as its stages, in that order. What happens when `pipeline.fit(trainingData)` is called?","options":{"A":"Each stage runs in order: `Transformer` stages transform immediately, `Estimator` stages fit and are replaced by fitted transformers, giving a `PipelineModel`.","B":"Only the final `LogisticRegression` stage executes; the earlier `StringIndexer`, `OneHotEncoder`, and `VectorAssembler` stages act as inert configuration only.","C":"All four stages are fit and executed in parallel across the cluster, and their outputs are merged into a single feature vector before logistic regression begins.","D":"The call raises an exception because a `Pipeline` cannot mix indexing, encoding, and assembling stages together with a classification estimator in one object."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. `Pipeline.fit()` runs each stage in sequence: transformer stages like `OneHotEncoder` and `VectorAssembler` apply their transform immediately, while estimator stages like `StringIndexer` and `LogisticRegression` are fit on the data produced by prior stages and become fitted transformers inside the returned `PipelineModel`.B. Every stage in the list actually executes; the earlier stages are not skipped or treated as inert configuration, since each one produces the transformed columns the next stage depends on.C. Pipeline stages run sequentially because each stage typically consumes the output column of the previous one, so they cannot be executed in parallel as independent, unrelated steps.D. Mixing indexing, encoding, assembling, and a classifier in one `Pipeline` is the standard pattern the API is designed for, so no exception is raised for combining these stage types."},{"id":"9","question":"A team previously used Hyperopt\'s `SparkTrials` to distribute hyperparameter search for a scikit-learn model across a Databricks cluster. They are now setting up a new project on a current Databricks Runtime ML version. What should they know about this approach?","options":{"A":"Hyperopt remains the only Databricks-recommended distributed tuning library, and `SparkTrials` is required for any hyperparameter search run on a Databricks cluster today.","B":"Hyperopt\'s `SparkTrials` was renamed to `MlflowSparkStudy` in current runtimes, but its API and distributed execution model are otherwise unchanged.","C":"Hyperopt is only deprecated for Spark ML estimators; it remains fully supported and recommended for tuning single-node scikit-learn models on current runtimes.","D":"Hyperopt is removed from Databricks Runtime ML after version 16.4 LTS, and current Databricks guidance recommends Optuna, with MLflow 3 integration, or Ray Tune instead."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. This is the opposite of current guidance: Hyperopt is deprecated on current runtimes, and teams starting new projects are directed toward Optuna or Ray Tune rather than continuing to rely on `SparkTrials`.B. `MlflowSparkStudy` is part of the newer Optuna-with-MLflow-3 integration, not a renamed version of Hyperopt\'s `SparkTrials`; the two come from different libraries with different APIs.C. Hyperopt\'s removal from Databricks Runtime ML after 16.4 LTS applies regardless of whether it is tuning a Spark ML estimator or a single-node scikit-learn model, so it is not selectively deprecated by model type.D. Databricks removed Hyperopt from Databricks Runtime ML after the 16.4 LTS release, and current documentation points teams toward Optuna, which integrates with MLflow 3 through `MlflowStorage` and `MlflowSparkStudy`, or toward Ray Tune for distributed search."},{"id":"10","question":"A multiclass product-category classifier has ten roughly balanced classes. The team wants to report a single overall correctness metric as well as a metric that accounts for per-class support when averaging precision across the ten classes. Select the valid `metricName` values for `MulticlassClassificationEvaluator` that provide these two readings.(Select 2)","options":{"A":"`\\"accuracy\\"`, which reports the overall fraction of rows correctly classified across all ten classes.","B":"`\\"weightedPrecision\\"`, which averages per-class precision weighted by each class\'s support in the dataset.","C":"`\\"f1\\"`, which reports the harmonic mean of precision and recall, averaged across the ten classes.","D":"`\\"areaUnderROC\\"`, which reports the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve for the classifier.","E":"`\\"rmse\\"`, which reports the root mean squared error between the predicted and true class labels."},"explanation":"A. `\\"accuracy\\"` is a valid `MulticlassClassificationEvaluator` metric name and directly reports the overall fraction of correctly classified rows across all classes, matching the request for a single correctness metric.B. `\\"weightedPrecision\\"` is a valid metric name that averages per-class precision weighted by class support, matching the request for a support-weighted precision average across the ten classes.C. `\\"f1\\"` is a valid `MulticlassClassificationEvaluator` metric, but it reports a precision/recall harmonic mean rather than a support-weighted precision average, so it does not satisfy the second requirement specifically.D. `\\"areaUnderROC\\"` belongs to `BinaryClassificationEvaluator` for two-class problems; it is not a valid `metricName` on `MulticlassClassificationEvaluator` for a ten-class problem.E. `\\"rmse\\"` is a `RegressionEvaluator` metric for continuous numeric targets; it is not a valid `metricName` on `MulticlassClassificationEvaluator` for categorical class labels.F. `\\"r2\\"` is a `RegressionEvaluator` metric describing explained variance for continuous targets; it is not defined for a multiclass categorical classification problem.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"11","question":"A single-node model currently takes 6 hours to train on a Databricks cluster driver, and the team is deciding whether to move to a bigger single-node instance type or to a horizontally scaled multi-node cluster. Which consideration most strongly favors staying with vertical scaling for this workload?","options":{"A":"The training job already scales near-linearly with additional workers on the current distributed framework, so the team should add more worker nodes to the multi-node cluster and expect training time to drop in proportion to the extra compute.","B":"The organization wants training throughput to keep growing indefinitely as data volume increases over the next several years, so the team should build out a horizontally scaled cluster now that can keep adding worker nodes without re-architecting the pipeline later.","C":"The training algorithm is inherently sequential and cannot be partitioned across workers, so a larger single machine avoids the coordination overhead of a distributed job that would not actually parallelize the computation.","D":"The dataset no longer fits in the memory or disk of any available single-node instance type, so the team should scale out to a cluster whose combined worker memory and distributed storage can hold the full training set."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Near-linear scaling with added workers is precisely the property that makes horizontal scaling attractive, so this observation favors adding nodes rather than favoring a bigger single machine.B. Indefinite future growth is an argument for an elastic, horizontally scalable architecture rather than for committing to ever-larger single instances, which eventually hit a hardware ceiling.C. Correct: when an algorithm cannot be meaningfully partitioned, distributing it across nodes adds network and coordination overhead without unlocking real parallel speedup, so a bigger single machine is the more effective lever.D. A dataset too large for any single instance is a textbook argument for horizontal scaling, not vertical scaling, since no single machine can hold the data regardless of size."},{"id":"12","question":"A data scientist calls `MlflowSparkStudy(study_name=\\"tuning\\", storage=mlflow_storage)` and then `mlflow_study.optimize(objective, n_trials=40, n_jobs=8)` without specifying a sampler. Which statement correctly describes what happens by default?","options":{"A":"Optuna defaults to pure random search across every hyperparameter dimension, so the 40 trials sample independently and adaptive samplers like TPE only take effect once passed explicitly to `optimize`.","B":"Optuna requires an explicit sampler argument in the `optimize` call, so this configuration raises a `ValueError` immediately and none of the 40 requested trials begin executing across the 8 workers.","C":"Optuna defaults to exhaustive grid search over the parameter ranges implied by the `suggest_float` and `suggest_categorical` calls in the objective, enumerating every combination across the 8 workers.","D":"Optuna defaults to the tree-structured Parzen estimator sampler, so the 40 trials are proposed adaptively based on prior results while 8 run concurrently across executors."},"correct":"D","explanation":"A. Plain random search is available as an explicit sampler choice, but it is not Optuna\'s default; the default sampler already performs adaptive, history-informed suggestion.B. No sampler argument is required to start a study; omitting it simply causes Optuna to fall back to its default TPESampler rather than raising an error.C. Grid search is a separate, explicitly selectable Optuna sampler; the default sampler explores the continuous and categorical space adaptively rather than exhaustively enumerating a grid.D. Correct: Optuna studies default to the TPESampler, an adaptive Bayesian-style sampler, so trials are proposed based on the outcomes of earlier trials rather than sampled independently at random."},{"id":"13","question":"In a `FeatureLookup` used for a point-in-time join, what does the value passed to `timestamp_lookup_key` represent?","options":{"A":"The name of the column in the label DataFrame containing the timestamp against which the feature table\'s time series values are matched.","B":"The name of the column in the feature table that stores the Delta Lake commit timestamp marking when each row was physically written to the table.","C":"A fixed cutoff date applied uniformly to every row in the training set, using the maximum label timestamp instead of each row\'s own event time.","D":"The refresh interval that the Databricks Feature Engineering client uses when re-publishing the feature table to the online store for serving."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. `timestamp_lookup_key` names the column on the label/spine DataFrame holding each row\'s own timestamp, which the as-of join compares against the feature table\'s timestamp key to find the correct historical value per row.B. Write time to the Delta table is unrelated to `timestamp_lookup_key`; the parameter refers to a column on the label DataFrame, not metadata about when feature rows were physically ingested.C. The parameter is per-row, not a single fixed cutoff — every label row can carry its own distinct timestamp, and the join is evaluated independently for each one.D. Publishing cadence for online tables is controlled by scheduling policies like `TRIGGERED` or `CONTINUOUS`, which are unrelated to `timestamp_lookup_key` and to training-set construction entirely."},{"id":"14","question":"After a model pipeline is deployed, ML engineers manage the running jobs and serving endpoints in the production workspace, while data scientists who built the model can view logs and metrics for debugging but cannot modify production assets. Which environment does this describe?","options":{"A":"Development, where data scientists hold full read-write control over every asset they create.","B":"Production, where ML engineers own pipelines and data scientists keep read-only visibility.","C":"A disaster-recovery workspace that only activates when the primary production workspace fails.","D":"Staging, where automated integration tests run before any human reviews the pipeline."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Development grants data scientists broad read-write control, which is the opposite of the read-only visibility described for the environment in this scenario.B. This matches the production environment\'s access pattern: ML engineers own and operate the deployed pipeline, while data scientists keep read-only visibility for monitoring and debugging rather than write access.C. The scenario describes normal day-to-day operation of the live pipeline, not a failover workspace that only activates during an outage.D. Staging is for automated pipeline testing before promotion, not for ML engineers managing live jobs while data scientists hold read-only debugging access."},{"id":"15","question":"A platform team needs a way to orchestrate the sequence of steps in a CI/CD deployment pipeline, running validation, comparison, and promotion steps automatically once code merges to the release branch. Which Databricks feature is responsible for this lifecycle activity?","options":{"A":"Lakehouse federation, which lets queries reach external data sources without moving data into Databricks.","B":"MLflow Model Registry, which tracks model version lineage and alias assignments after training completes.","C":"Lakeflow Jobs (formerly Databricks Workflows), which orchestrates the execution of pipeline steps in sequence.","D":"Cluster policies, which restrict the compute configurations a workspace user is allowed to launch."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Lakehouse federation is about querying external data sources; it has no role in orchestrating the steps of a CI/CD deployment pipeline.B. The Model Registry records lineage and alias state after each step completes, but it does not itself sequence or trigger the validation, comparison, and promotion steps.C. Lakeflow Jobs is the orchestration engine that runs validation, comparison, and promotion steps in sequence once code merges, which is exactly the coordination activity described.D. Cluster policies constrain compute configuration choices; they do not orchestrate the order of pipeline steps in a CI/CD deployment."},{"id":"16","question":"Which of the following feature-to-activity pairings are correct within the Databricks model lifecycle management process? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Lakeflow Jobs are used to orchestrate the steps of a CI/CD promotion pipeline across environments.","B":"Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines are used to serve real-time model predictions to applications.","C":"Databricks Git folders (formerly Repos) are used to assign the Challenger alias to a newly validated model version.","D":"MLflow Tracking is used to log and compare parameters and metrics during model development.","E":"Unity Catalog model aliases are used to mark which model version is currently serving as Champion."},"explanation":"A. Lakeflow Jobs is the orchestration tool used to run and sequence the steps of a CI/CD promotion pipeline as code moves across environments.B. Serving real-time predictions is the role of a Model Serving endpoint, not Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines, which builds data and feature pipelines instead.C. Alias assignment happens through Unity Catalog\'s model registry mechanisms, not through Git folders (formerly Repos), which only manages source code synchronization with a Git remote.D. MLflow Tracking is the tool used to log and compare parameters, metrics, and artifacts across experiment runs while a model is being developed.E. Unity Catalog model aliases, such as Champion, are exactly the mechanism used to mark which model version is currently designated for production serving.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"17","question":"A team is setting up Lakehouse Monitoring on an inference table for a new fraud model and must choose what data to load into the baseline table so future drift comparisons are meaningful. What should the baseline table contain?","options":{"A":"A representative sample of the training or validation data whose distribution reflects what the model was built to expect.","B":"The very first day of raw, unprocessed production traffic captured before any data cleaning or filtering was applied.","C":"A synthetic dataset generated to cover every possible feature value, regardless of how likely those values are in production.","D":"The current day\'s live inference table itself, refreshed continuously so the baseline always matches the newest traffic."},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. A baseline built from the training or validation distribution gives drift metrics a stable, meaningful reference point representing what the model expects, which is what current production data is compared against.B. Unprocessed raw traffic from before any cleaning does not represent the distribution the model was trained and validated on, so comparisons against it would flag differences that have nothing to do with real drift.C. A synthetic dataset covering unlikely feature combinations skews the baseline away from realistic production behavior, making drift statistics measure divergence from an artificial reference rather than the model\'s actual training assumptions.D. Continuously refreshing the baseline from the live table eliminates any fixed reference point, so drift can never be detected because the baseline would always move in lockstep with the data being compared."},{"id":"18","question":"An automated retraining pipeline promotes a new Champion by reassigning the alias, and the inference job runs nightly, always loading whatever model version currently holds the \\"Champion\\" alias. Why does this pattern let retraining automation update production behavior without any change to the inference job\'s code or configuration?","options":{"A":"Because aliases are tied to the job\'s cluster configuration and node pool rather than to the registered model itself.","B":"Because the inference job resolves the alias to a model version at run time, picking up a new assignment on its next run.","C":"Because Unity Catalog automatically restarts and reruns every job that references a model whenever its Champion alias changes.","D":"Because the inference job caches the model version permanently, so alias changes only take effect after a manual cache clear."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Aliases are a property of the registered model and its versions in Unity Catalog, not of the job\'s cluster configuration, so cluster settings have nothing to do with how the alias is resolved.B. The alias is resolved to whichever version currently holds it each time the inference job runs, so reassigning the Champion alias is enough for the next scheduled run to load the new version with no code or config edits.C. Unity Catalog does not automatically restart or re-trigger jobs when an alias changes; the inference job simply resolves the alias the next time it happens to run on its own schedule or trigger.D. There is no permanent caching of a specific model version tied to the alias that requires a manual clear; each run resolves the alias fresh, which is exactly what lets the new version take effect automatically."},{"id":"19","question":"A team wants their automated retraining pipeline to react specifically to changes in the data itself, rather than to a fixed calendar or to unrelated model registry events. Select the three trigger mechanisms that react to a data-related change.(Select 3)","options":{"A":"A SQL alert on the drift metrics table that calls the Jobs API once a drift threshold is breached.","B":"A Model update trigger, which fires on Unity Catalog registry events such as a new model version.","C":"A Continuous trigger, which fires as soon as the job\'s own previous run completes or fails.","D":"A Table update trigger, which fires when the referenced Delta table receives a new commit.","E":"A File arrival trigger, which fires when new objects land in a monitored storage location."},"explanation":"A. This is correct: a SQL alert tied to the drift metrics table reacts to a measured change in the data\'s distribution, making it a data-driven trigger even though it is implemented through an alert-to-API call rather than a native Jobs trigger type.B. A Model update trigger reacts to registry events like a new model version or alias assignment, which are events about the model artifact, not about the underlying training or production data changing.C. A Continuous trigger reacts only to the prior run of the same job finishing, which has nothing to do with whether the underlying data has actually changed.F. A Scheduled trigger fires purely based on calendar time, so it runs on its fixed cadence whether or not any data has changed at all.D. This is correct: a Table update trigger fires specifically because the underlying data table changed, which is a direct reaction to a data-related event.E. This is correct: a File arrival trigger reacts to new data objects showing up in storage, which is also a direct, data-driven event rather than a calendar or registry-based one.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"20","question":"A Databricks ML team is asked to justify, in an architecture review, why they organize their Python project with functions in importable modules, unit tests in a corresponding test suite, and a staging integration test suite that runs the assembled pipeline. Which justification is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Modules and unit tests are optional conveniences with no real effect on defects, and only the staging integration suite meaningfully catches bugs.","B":"Modules exist only to satisfy Scala packaging requirements, unit tests exist only for coverage metrics, and integration tests exist only for compliance audits.","C":"Modules make functions independently testable, unit tests catch function defects early, and staging integration tests confirm the pipeline works as a whole.","D":"Modules, unit tests, and integration tests are three names for identical testing work performed on different days of the calendar year."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Unit tests catch function-level defects early and cheaply, which is a meaningful and distinct benefit from integration testing; treating them as having no effect ignores the documented value of catching issues before the more expensive integration stage.B. Modules are a general Python organization pattern, not a Scala-specific requirement; coverage metrics and compliance audits are not the stated reasons for unit or integration testing, which instead exist to catch actual functional defects.C. This matches the documented reasoning: modules let Python functions be imported and tested outside notebooks, unit tests are added continuously during development to catch function-level issues early, and staging integration tests run the whole pipeline together to catch cross-stage defects before production.D. Modules, unit tests, and integration tests are distinct concepts serving different purposes, module organization enables testability, unit tests check individual functions, and integration tests check the assembled pipeline, not different names for one activity."},{"id":"21","question":"During a retrospective, a team lists several claims about their organization of functions, unit tests, and integration tests in staging. Which of these claims are consistent with documented Databricks testing guidance? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Unit tests are meant to replace integration tests entirely, since a sufficiently thorough set of unit tests always implies pipeline-level correctness.","B":"Staging is designed to closely mirror production so that the full test suite can run under representative conditions before promotion.","C":"For SQL logic, functions are best organized by embedding the SQL directly inside Python notebook cells rather than as SQL user-defined functions.","D":"Integration testing in staging is meant to confirm that feature engineering, training, and inference work correctly together, not just individually.","E":"For Python code, storing functions outside notebooks in modules improves both reusability and compatibility with standard test frameworks."},"explanation":"A. This is not accurate; passing unit tests for every individual function does not guarantee that the functions work correctly together as a connected pipeline, which is exactly why integration testing exists as a distinct, necessary stage.B. This matches documented guidance: staging is built to mirror production as closely as feasible and is where the complete testing suite runs before code or artifacts are promoted further.C. This contradicts documented guidance for SQL, which recommends organizing SQL logic as SQL user-defined functions stored within a schema, not by embedding SQL inside Python notebook cells.D. This matches documented guidance on integration testing: it exists to validate that pipelines including feature engineering, training, and inference function correctly together, beyond what testing each in isolation would show.E. This matches documented guidance for Python: moving functions into modules outside notebooks makes them usable both inside and outside notebooks and lets standard test frameworks run against them more directly than against notebook cells.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"22","question":"An MLOps engineer is authoring a Declarative Automation Bundle (DAB, formerly Databricks Asset Bundle) and needs the bundle deploy to create and manage a real-time serving endpoint for a Unity Catalog model, fully described in version-controlled YAML alongside the training job. Under which top-level `resources` key should this endpoint be declared?","options":{"A":"model_serving_endpoints","B":"serving_endpoints","C":"model_endpoints","D":"endpoints"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A. model_serving_endpoints is the resource key Declarative Automation Bundles (formerly Databricks Asset Bundles) use for declaring a Model Serving endpoint, including its served_entities and traffic_config, in the resources section.B. serving_endpoints is not a recognized Declarative Automation Bundle resource key; the bundle schema uses the fuller model_serving_endpoints name for this resource type.C. model_endpoints is not a valid Declarative Automation Bundle resource key; no such shortened name exists in the resources schema.D. endpoints alone is too generic and is not the resource key the bundle schema expects for Model Serving endpoints."},{"id":"23","question":"A platform team is designing environment isolation for a highly regulated ML project and wants layered controls beyond Unity Catalog namespace separation alone. Which of the following genuinely add a distinct layer of isolation or governance beyond catalog-level separation? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Deploy production compute to its own Databricks workspace with its own network configuration, separate from the workspace used for dev and staging.","B":"Use a distinct service principal per environment for CI/CD deployments, so a compromised or misconfigured dev credential cannot deploy or modify prod resources.","C":"Scope `grants` on production `registered_models` resources to only the specific principals that need access, rather than granting broadly to every workspace user.","D":"Reuse one shared service principal with broad admin rights across dev, staging, and prod, since a single credential is simpler for the CI/CD pipeline to manage.","E":"Rely solely on catalog-level separation, skipping workspace-level or identity-level controls, since Unity Catalog namespaces alone are sufficient for regulated workloads."},"explanation":"A. A separate workspace with its own network configuration adds an isolation layer at the compute and network level, which catalog separation inside a shared workspace does not provide on its own.B. Distinct per-environment service principals limit the blast radius of a compromised or misconfigured dev credential, adding an identity-level control on top of namespace separation.C. Scoping grants tightly to the specific principals that need access on production model resources adds a fine-grained authorization layer beyond simply placing the model in a separate catalog.D. A single shared service principal with broad rights across every environment removes the identity-level boundary between environments, so a dev-side compromise could reach production resources.E. Catalog separation alone governs data and metadata namespace boundaries; it does not address compute network isolation or scoped CI/CD identities, so relying on it exclusively leaves those layers uncovered for a regulated workload.F. Giving every data scientist identical permissions regardless of environment ignores that production sensitivity typically calls for tighter access than a dev sandbox, undermining layered governance rather than adding to it.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B","C"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"24","question":"A reviewer is doing a final check on a bundle\'s ML-specific resources — `model_serving_endpoints`, `experiments`, and `registered_models` — before its first production deployment. Which of the following are legitimate things to verify at this stage? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Confirm `grants` on the production `registered_models` resource are scoped to the specific principals that need access, not granted broadly by default.","B":"Confirm the `experiments` resource\'s `comment` field is left blank, since Databricks documentation states a populated comment field is known to break MLflow run logging.","C":"Confirm the `model_serving_endpoints` resource has `traffic_config` omitted entirely, since Model Serving endpoints route all traffic automatically without any configuration.","D":"Confirm the served entity references the model by alias (such as `@Champion`) rather than a version number hardcoded to whatever was true when the YAML was written.","E":"Confirm `catalog_name` and `schema_name` on the `registered_models` resource resolve to the intended production namespace once the prod target\'s variables are applied."},"explanation":"A. Verifying grants are scoped to only the principals that actually need access is exactly the least-privilege check a reviewer should make before a production model resource goes live.B. A populated comment field has no effect on MLflow run logging; comment is purely descriptive metadata, so this is not a real risk worth checking for.C. Traffic does not route itself automatically without configuration; traffic_config\'s routes are what define how requests are split across served entities, so omitting it entirely is not a valid or harmless state to leave unchecked.F. Hardcoding entity_version to the current highest version freezes the endpoint on that version and requires a manual redeploy for every future promotion, which is the opposite of what a reviewer should confirm is in place.D. Checking that the served entity tracks an alias rather than a version frozen at authoring time confirms the endpoint will automatically follow future promotions instead of silently going stale.E. Verifying that catalog_name and schema_name resolve correctly once target variables are applied catches a misconfigured or unresolved variable before it registers the model into the wrong namespace in production.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"25","question":"A monitor\'s label column points at `repaid_on_time`, populated only for loans over 60 days old. This week\'s performance metrics show a sharp accuracy drop for the newest fully-labeled cohort, while input drift metrics stay stable. What should the team check first?","options":{"A":"Whether to delete the baseline table, since a performance drop without drift proves it is invalid.","B":"Whether the label\'s definition or collection process changed, since stable inputs point to the label side.","C":"Whether the Kolmogorov-Smirnov threshold was too strict, since a quiet drift table always needs loosening.","D":"Whether to switch from a labeled cohort window to snapshot profiling, required whenever metrics disagree."},"correct":"B","explanation":"A. Deleting the baseline is not a documented response to this mismatch and would remove the drift reference point without addressing the accuracy drop\'s cause.B. With input features stable but real accuracy down, a change in how the label is defined, recorded, or delayed is a plausible cause worth checking first, since it need not show up as input drift.C. A stable drift table does not mean the threshold is miscalibrated; loosening it until drift appears would manufacture a false signal rather than diagnose the real cause.D. There is no rule forcing a switch to snapshot profiling when metrics disagree; inference profiling is exactly the type built to support labeled-cohort performance metrics."},{"id":"26","question":"A team monitoring a forecasting model wants to compare accuracy between two customer segments receiving different pricing treatments, using the monitor\'s existing output. Which steps achieve this? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Configure a slicing expression on the segment column so profile and drift tables report per-segment rows.","B":"Ensure the label column is configured so joined ground truth produces segment-level metrics.","C":"Compare the resulting segment-level performance rows to spot a meaningful accuracy difference.","D":"Configure two separate baseline tables per segment, since slicing cannot apply to inference profiles.","E":"Disable the label column, since segment-level accuracy only needs raw predictions, never ground truth."},"explanation":"A. Slicing by the segment column is the mechanism that produces separate profile and drift rows per segment for comparison.B. The label column configuration is what lets ground truth be joined in, which is required to compute performance metrics at all, including per segment.C. Comparing the segment-level performance rows against each other is exactly how the team surfaces whether accuracy differs meaningfully between the two treatment groups.D. Slicing expressions apply to inference profile types just as they do to time series or snapshot profiles; separate baselines per segment are not required.E. Performance metrics fundamentally require ground-truth labels; disabling the label column would prevent segment-level accuracy from being computed at all.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B","C"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"27","question":"A team is reviewing how the built-in drift tests compute statistical significance across two numerical columns and one categorical column, all compared against the baseline table. Which statements are correct? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"The categorical column is evaluated with the chi-squared test comparing category counts to baseline.","B":"A lower p-value on either test indicates a more statistically significant difference from the baseline.","C":"A high p-value on any test guarantees the column has zero practical difference from the baseline.","D":"The two tests can be swapped freely between numerical and categorical columns without changing results.","E":"Both numerical columns are evaluated with the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test against the baseline distribution."},"explanation":"A. Chi-squared is the built-in test applied to categorical columns, comparing observed category counts to the baseline distribution.B. For both tests, a lower p-value indicates the observed difference from baseline is less likely to be due to chance, meaning stronger statistical significance.C. A high p-value only means the test failed to find statistically significant evidence of a difference; it does not guarantee there is zero practical difference, especially with a small sample.D. Kolmogorov-Smirnov operates on continuous distributions and chi-squared operates on category counts, so they are not interchangeable between column types.E. Kolmogorov-Smirnov is the built-in test applied to numerical columns, so both numerical columns are evaluated with it against the baseline.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"28","question":"A platform team is setting up dev, staging, and production workspaces for an ML project and deciding what kind of testing belongs in each. Which statement correctly matches a testing activity to the environment where it is described as primarily occurring?","options":{"A":"Staging is reserved exclusively for manual exploratory testing, while automated unit and integration tests run solely in development.","B":"Development and production share one testing suite, while staging is used exclusively for manual business sign-off.","C":"Development supports exploratory work without strict access controls, while staging hosts the full testing suite before production.","D":"Production hosts the full testing suite so that any defects are caught using real production data before rollback is needed."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. Staging is where the automated unit and integration test suite is described as running, not development; development is oriented toward exploratory work, not the formal test suite.B. Development and production are described as distinct environments with different access controls and purposes, and staging\'s defining role is to run the full automated test suite, not just manual sign-off.C. The development environment is where data scientists experiment with models and pipelines without strict access controls, while staging is designed to mirror production as closely as feasible and is where the complete testing suite runs before promotion.D. Running the full testing suite in production would mean validating code against live production traffic without the staging gate that exists specifically to catch defects before they reach production."},{"id":"29","question":"A team configuring a Model Serving endpoint for a new high-traffic use case is reviewing available compute and scaling settings. Which of the following are valid, documented configuration levers for the endpoint? (Select all that apply.)(Select 4)","options":{"A":"Selecting a workload type such as CPU, CPU_MEDIUM, CPU_LARGE, or a GPU tier based on the model\'s compute needs.","B":"Choosing a concurrency tier such as Small, Medium, or Large that bounds how many simultaneous requests the endpoint handles.","C":"Enabling or disabling scale-to-zero, trading idle-cost savings against cold-start latency on the first request after idling.","D":"Setting `min_provisioned_concurrency` and `max_provisioned_concurrency` in multiples of four to bound autoscaling range.","E":"Manually assigning each incoming request to a specific virtual machine by IP address to guarantee consistent routing."},"explanation":"A. Workload type selection, including CPU and GPU tiers, is a documented compute configuration option for a Model Serving endpoint.B. Concurrency tiers are a documented setting that bounds how much simultaneous request traffic the endpoint can handle at once.C. Scale-to-zero is a documented toggle that saves idle cost at the expense of cold-start latency on the next incoming request.D. Provisioned concurrency bounds set in multiples of four are documented settings for controlling the endpoint\'s autoscaling range.E. Model Serving does not expose per-request manual VM or IP assignment; scaling and routing are managed by the platform, not by hand-assigning machines.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B","C","D"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"30","question":"A platform team is defining guardrails for how deployment strategies should be chosen across Model Serving endpoints. Which of the following reflect sound practice based on the tradeoffs of canary and blue-green rollouts? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Every endpoint, regardless of traffic volume or business criticality, should always use the exact same fixed 50/50 traffic split for every rollout.","B":"Rollout decisions should account for how quickly a regression can be detected and how much traffic is exposed during that detection window.","C":"Because Model Serving is documented as highly available, rollout strategy is irrelevant and any version can be swapped in at 100% traffic with no monitoring.","D":"High-traffic, revenue-critical endpoints should default to a canary rollout so a regression is caught while affecting only a small fraction of requests.","E":"A low-traffic internal tool with lenient latency needs may reasonably use a simpler blue-green cutover, since the blast radius of a mistake is smaller."},"explanation":"A. A single fixed split ignores that different endpoints carry different risk profiles; sound guardrails size the rollout to the endpoint\'s criticality and traffic instead.B. Detection speed and exposure during the detection window are exactly the factors that should drive whether a slower canary ramp or a faster cutover is appropriate.C. High availability describes serving infrastructure uptime, not whether an untested model version is safe to expose to all traffic at once; staged rollout remains necessary.D. Defaulting critical, high-traffic endpoints to a gradual canary rollout limits how much traffic is exposed to a bad new version before it is caught.E. A lower-stakes internal tool can reasonably accept the simplicity of a full blue-green swap because the cost of a mistake affecting all its traffic is lower.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"31","question":"Which of the following are true, documented characteristics of Model Serving that a team should factor into a deployment-strategy decision for a high-traffic production model? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Model Serving can deploy custom Python models, Databricks-hosted foundation models, and certain external models through one unified interface.","B":"Model Serving requires every served model to be scored exclusively through a Spark cluster that stays attached to the calling notebook session.","C":"Model Serving exposes each deployed model as a documented REST API that any application running outside Databricks can integrate against.","D":"Model Serving guarantees that scale-to-zero endpoints never incur any added cold-start latency whatsoever on the very first request after idling.","E":"Model Serving is designed for high-availability, low-latency production use and can support a very large volume of queries per second with low overhead."},"explanation":"A. Supporting custom models, foundation models, and external models behind one interface is a documented, accurate capability of Model Serving.B. Model Serving does not require a caller-attached Spark cluster to score a model; it exposes a serverless REST endpoint instead of that.C. Exposing each model as a REST API that external applications can call is a core, documented feature of Model Serving worth relying on.D. Scale-to-zero endpoints do incur cold-start latency on the first request after idling, so a claim of a zero-latency guarantee is inaccurate.E. High availability, low latency, and a documented high-throughput ceiling are core, accurate characteristics of the platform relevant to a high-traffic decision.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"32","question":"Reviewing sample interview notes from an ML platform candidate, an interviewer flags a few statements as technically wrong about deploying custom models on Model Serving. Which of the following statements are INCORRECT? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"\\"Loading a large reference file once at container startup belongs in `load_context`, not inside `predict` on every call.\\"","B":"\\"Registering a model to Unity Catalog automatically creates and starts a serving endpoint for that registered model.\\"","C":"\\"You must use `mlflow.sklearn.log_model` for every model type; `pyfunc` models cannot be deployed to Model Serving at all.\\"","D":"\\"A `pyfunc` model\'s `predict` method can call arbitrary Python logic, including custom preprocessing, before returning a prediction.\\"","E":"\\"Unity Catalog will register any model regardless of whether it has a signature, so signatures are optional for serving deployment.\\""},"explanation":"A. This statement is accurate: `load_context` is the documented place for one-time startup work like loading a large reference file once.B. This is incorrect: registering a model to Unity Catalog does not itself create a serving endpoint; endpoint creation remains a separate, explicit step.C. This is incorrect: `pyfunc` models built by subclassing `PythonModel` are a documented, standard way to deploy custom logic to Model Serving, not something excluded.D. This statement is accurate: a `pyfunc` model\'s `predict` method is exactly where arbitrary custom preprocessing and inference logic can live and run.E. This is incorrect: Unity Catalog requires a valid signature before accepting a model registration, so signatures are not optional there at all.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"33","question":"A data scientist wants `mlflow.register_model()` to place a newly logged custom model into a Unity Catalog three-level namespace instead of the legacy workspace Model Registry. What must they configure before calling `register_model`?","options":{"A":"Set the `MLFLOW_REGISTRY_MODE` environment variable to `unity_catalog` and pass the model\'s run ID as the model name.","B":"Call `mlflow.set_experiment(\\"unity_catalog\\")` before logging the model so the run is associated with Unity Catalog by default.","C":"Call `mlflow.set_registry_uri(\\"databricks-uc\\")` and pass a model name formatted as `catalog.schema.model_name`.","D":"Call `mlflow.set_tracking_uri(\\"databricks-uc\\")` and pass a model name formatted as `schema.model_name.version`."},"correct":"C","explanation":"A. There is no `MLFLOW_REGISTRY_MODE` environment variable in MLflow, and a run ID is not a valid model name for registration in either registry.B. `set_experiment` only selects which experiment new runs are logged under; it has no effect on which model registry a subsequent `register_model` call targets.C. Setting the registry URI to `databricks-uc` points `register_model` at Unity Catalog, and Unity Catalog model names must follow the three-level `catalog.schema.model_name` convention rather than the flat names used by the workspace registry.D. `set_tracking_uri` controls where experiment runs and metrics are logged, not where models are registered, and it does not accept `databricks-uc` as a valid tracking destination."},{"id":"34","question":"Before a custom pyfunc model can be registered under a Unity Catalog three-level name such as `ml_prod.fraud.scoring_model`, which of the following conditions must already be true? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"The caller has `CREATE MODEL` or equivalent privilege on the target schema so the registration operation is authorized.","B":"The MLflow client\'s registry URI is set to `databricks-uc` so `register_model` targets Unity Catalog rather than the workspace registry.","C":"The model was trained using a Unity Catalog-managed Feature Store table, since Unity Catalog rejects models trained on any other data source.","D":"The model\'s Python class was written using only Databricks Runtime ML\'s built-in algorithms, since Unity Catalog rejects custom pyfunc classes.","E":"The `ml_prod` catalog and `fraud` schema already exist in Unity Catalog, or the caller has permission to create them."},"explanation":"A. Unity Catalog enforces standard privilege checks, so the caller needs the appropriate model-creation privilege on the target schema for the registration call to succeed.B. Pointing the MLflow registry URI at `databricks-uc` is required so that `register_model` resolves the three-level name against Unity Catalog instead of the legacy workspace registry.C. Unity Catalog model registration has no requirement that training data originated from a Feature Store table; models trained on any data source can be registered as long as the model itself is properly logged.D. Unity Catalog fully supports custom pyfunc models built with arbitrary Python logic; it does not restrict registration to built-in Databricks Runtime ML algorithms.E. The catalog and schema in the three-level name must exist, or the registering identity needs permission to create them, before a model can be registered under that namespace.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B","E"],"multiSelect":true},{"id":"35","question":"A team wants to update the served model version on a live custom model endpoint without causing a visible outage for callers currently sending requests. Which of the following are valid techniques for achieving this? (Select all that apply.)(Select 3)","options":{"A":"Add the new model version as a second served entity on the same endpoint and initially route a small percentage of traffic to it.","B":"Update the endpoint configuration via the REST API or the SDK to adjust `traffic_percentage` between the served entities over time.","C":"Monitor request latency and error rate for the new served entity closely before increasing its share of the traffic further.","D":"Delete the endpoint entirely and recreate it again from scratch every time a new model version needs to be served.","E":"Ask every calling application to pause its own retries manually while an engineer swaps the model file on disk."},"explanation":"A. Adding the new version as an additional served entity with a small initial traffic share is the documented mechanism for introducing a new model version without an abrupt cutover.B. Adjusting `traffic_percentage` across served entities through the REST API or SDK is how the gradual shift from an old to a new version is actually performed.C. Watching latency and error metrics for the new entity before increasing its share lets the team detect a regression before most traffic depends on the new version.D. Deleting and recreating the entire endpoint causes exactly the outage the scenario is trying to avoid, and discards the multi-entity, gradual-rollout capability the endpoint already provides.E. Coordinating manual pauses across every calling application is not a Model Serving mechanism at all, and served model files are not swapped on disk outside of MLflow\'s deployment workflow.","multi":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B","C"],"multiSelect":true}]},{"id":"databricks-dea","title":"Databricks Data Engineer Associate — Practice Exam 1","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"A data engineering team must guarantee that raw customer records never leave their own cloud account, but they still want Databricks to manage the notebooks, job scheduling and cluster configuration. Which statement describes how the Databricks architecture supports this?","options":{"A":"All data is copied into the Databricks control plane so that clusters can be created faster","B":"The control plane manages workspace metadata and orchestration, while data stays in the customer\'s cloud account in the data plane","C":"The data plane stores notebooks and cluster definitions, and the control plane stores the Delta tables","D":"Data locality cannot be guaranteed, so a private link is the only way to keep data in the account"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Databricks splits responsibilities: the control plane (managed by Databricks) holds workspace metadata — notebooks, job definitions, cluster configuration, Unity Catalog metadata — and the data plane runs in the customer\'s cloud account, where clusters read and write the actual data in cloud storage. That is why raw data never has to leave the account.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) is the classic misconception — copying data into the control plane would break the whole security model and is not how it works. (C) inverts the two planes. (D) private link improves network isolation but is not what guarantees data residency; the split architecture does.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"data must stay in our account\\" + \\"Databricks still manages orchestration\\" → control plane / data plane split."},{"id":"2","question":"A pipeline ingests thousands of JSON files per day from cloud storage into a Delta table. New files sometimes add new fields, and the pipeline must not fail when that happens. Which ingestion approach handles schema evolution with the least operational effort?","options":{"A":"COPY INTO with a fixed schema and a manual ALTER TABLE for every new field","B":"A scheduled notebook that reads each file with pandas and appends rows","C":"Auto Loader (cloudFiles) with schema evolution enabled, checkpointing each run","D":"A streaming query with a hard-coded schema and DROP of unknown columns"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Auto Loader (format cloudFiles) is the managed incremental ingestion path: it discovers new files without reprocessing old ones, and with schema evolution enabled it adds new columns to the target automatically instead of failing the batch. The checkpoint records which files were processed, so restarts are incremental by design.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) COPY INTO can ingest incrementally, but a fixed schema plus manual ALTER TABLE per field is exactly the operational burden the question rules out. (B) pandas in a notebook is neither incremental nor scalable and loses the Delta guarantees. (D) hard-coding the schema and dropping columns silently discards data.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"new fields\\" + \\"must not fail\\" + \\"least operational effort\\" → Auto Loader with schema evolution."},{"id":"3","question":"A team ingests around 50 new files per hour into a Delta table and wants the lowest-latency, most scalable file discovery available in Auto Loader. Their cloud storage can publish event notifications. What should they configure?","options":{"A":"Directory listing with a 5-minute trigger interval","B":"A full table refresh on every run","C":"COPY INTO inside a for-each loop over the bucket prefix","D":"File notification mode, with the required queue and IAM permissions created for them"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Auto Loader has two discovery modes. File notification mode subscribes to the storage events (S3 → SQS/SNS, ADLS → Event Grid, GCS → Pub/Sub) and reacts to new files as they arrive; it scales better and costs less per file because it does not repeatedly list the directory. Databricks can create the queue and the IAM role for you when you enable it.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) directory listing works and needs no extra permissions, but it lists the prefix every interval — slower to detect files and more expensive as the file count grows. (B) a full refresh reprocesses everything and defeats incremental ingestion. (C) driving COPY INTO per prefix is manual orchestration and still depends on listing.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"lowest latency\\" + \\"storage can publish events\\" → notification mode; \\"no extra permissions / simplest setup\\" → directory listing."},{"id":"4","question":"A team must load 200 CSV files once into a Delta table, then keep appending a few files a day. They want the simplest option that is still incremental after the initial load. What should they use?","options":{"A":"COPY INTO for the initial load, which can also be re-run incrementally to pick up new files","B":"Auto Loader with schema evolution and a notification queue","C":"A one-off CREATE TABLE AS SELECT over the whole folder","D":"An external table that reads the CSVs in place forever"},"correct":"A","explanation":"COPY INTO is the SQL way to load files idempotently into a Delta table: it tracks which files were already loaded, so re-running it only ingests new ones. For a one-off bulk load plus a few daily files it is the least machinery — no checkpoint management, no streaming query to keep alive.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) Auto Loader is the right answer when files arrive continuously, in large numbers, or need schema evolution and low latency; here it adds a queue and streaming state for little benefit. (C) CTAS is a single snapshot: the new files would never be picked up. (D) an external table does not ingest — it reads the files on every query and loses Delta performance and features.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"load once, then a few files a day\\" + \\"simplest\\" → COPY INTO. \\"Thousands of files / continuous / schema evolution\\" → Auto Loader."},{"id":"5","question":"While ingesting JSON files with Auto Loader, some records contain a field whose type does not match the inferred schema. The team does not want the load to fail but cannot lose those records. What does Auto Loader provide for this case?","options":{"A":"It silently drops the offending records and reports the count in the checkpoint","B":"It aborts the stream and requires a manual ALTER TABLE before restarting","C":"It keeps the record and puts the unparsed or mismatched fields in the `_rescued_data` column","D":"It casts every mismatched value to STRING and overwrites the original column"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Auto Loader never throws away a record because of a type mismatch. When a field cannot be parsed into the expected type (or is not in the schema), it stores the original payload of the rescued fields in the `_rescued_data` column so you can inspect, quarantine or reprocess it later. That is the built-in rescue mechanism.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) Auto Loader does not drop records — losing data silently is precisely what `_rescued_data` prevents. (B) a type mismatch does not abort the stream; only an unrecoverable error would. (D) it does not overwrite the column with a cast: the parsed column keeps its value and the raw value is preserved alongside.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"must not fail\\" + \\"cannot lose records\\" + Auto Loader → `_rescued_data`."},{"id":"6","question":"A streaming ingestion job must be run every 10 minutes by a scheduled job, process only files that arrived since the previous run, and then stop — instead of running continuously. Which trigger setting should the job use?","options":{"A":"Trigger.AvailableNow, in a job that runs continuously","B":"Trigger.AvailableNow (or Once) with the scheduled job stopping when the batch completes","C":"A continuous cluster with auto-scaling and no trigger","D":"Trigger.ProcessingTime(\\"10 minutes\\") on a continuous cluster"},"correct":"B","explanation":"`Trigger.AvailableNow` processes all data available at the moment the query starts — like a bounded batch — and then stops. Combined with a scheduled job (every 10 minutes), it gives you incremental processing without paying for a cluster that runs 24/7. `Trigger.Once` is the older equivalent for this pattern.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) AvailableNow plus a continuous job defeats the purpose — the whole point is that the run ends. (C) a continuous cluster with no trigger is the opposite: always on and always paying. (D) ProcessingTime is for continuous streaming with micro-batches; it keeps the query alive.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"process only new files, then stop\\" + \\"scheduled job\\" → AvailableNow/Once, not a continuous stream."},{"id":"7","question":"Each night a batch provides the full current state of products, including updates and brand-new products, but no deletions. The target Delta table already has yesterday\'s rows. Which statement loads today\'s file correctly without duplicating existing products?","options":{"A":"INSERT INTO target SELECT * FROM staging","B":"INSERT OVERWRITE target SELECT * FROM staging","C":"MERGE INTO target USING staging ON target.id = staging.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *","D":"DELETE FROM target, then INSERT INTO target SELECT * FROM staging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MERGE INTO is the upsert primitive in Delta Lake: the ON clause decides which staging rows match existing target rows, WHEN MATCHED updates them and WHEN NOT MATCHED inserts the new ones. One pass, ACID, and no duplicates for products that already exist.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) plain INSERT appends everything, so every product appears twice. (B) INSERT OVERWRITE replaces the whole table with today\'s file — correct only if the file truly is the complete state and you accept losing history/partitions; it is not an upsert and it is not safe for partial extracts. (D) delete-then-insert leaves the table empty for readers during the operation and breaks concurrency.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"full state with updates and new rows\\" + \\"no duplicates\\" → MERGE INTO. \\"Only new rows, never seen before\\" → append/INSERT."},{"id":"8","question":"A 500 TB Delta table is filtered most often by customer_id and by event_date. The team is tired of choosing between partitioning and Z-ORDER and wants Databricks to pick and maintain the layout automatically, without rewriting the table. What should they enable?","options":{"A":"Partition by event_date and Z-ORDER BY customer_id on every write","B":"Liquid clustering on customer_id and event_date","C":"Optimize with Z-ORDER BY customer_id once a week and drop the partitions","D":"Bucketing by customer_id with 200 buckets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Liquid clustering replaces the partitioning/Z-ORDER decision: you declare clustering keys on the table and Databricks lays out the data incrementally, without rewriting existing files when the keys change. On a huge table with two independent filter columns it avoids the over-partitioning problem (millions of tiny files) that date partitioning creates.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) Z-ORDER on every write is expensive and partitions by date can explode the file count. (C) weekly Z-ORDER is a manual maintenance job and dropping partitions loses the pruning benefit for date filters. (D) bucketing is a Hive-era technique that Delta does not need and that users must manage.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"wants Databricks to choose and maintain the layout\\" + \\"without rewriting\\" → liquid clustering. \\"Legacy table, manual tuning\\" → Z-ORDER."},{"id":"9","question":"An auditor needs to query the Delta table as it looked 30 days ago. The team runs VACUUM on the table daily with default settings. What happens to that requirement?","options":{"A":"It fails: the default retention is 7 days, so versions older than that are physically removed even though time travel still works within the window","B":"It works, because VACUUM only removes files that no query is reading at that moment","C":"It works, because Delta keeps a full copy of every version for a year by default","D":"It fails only if the table was written with INSERT OVERWRITE"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Time travel is limited by retention. VACUUM physically deletes data files that are no longer referenced, and the default retention threshold is 7 days. After a daily VACUUM with defaults, any version older than seven days is gone: `VERSION AS OF` / `TIMESTAMP AS OF` will error because the underlying files no longer exist. To keep 30 days you must set `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration` (or the `RETAIN N HOURS` clause) well beyond the audit window — and accept the storage cost.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) VACUUM removes unreferenced files regardless of who is reading now; its safety is about the retention window, not live queries. (C) there is no year-long copy by default — that is exactly the misconception the question tests. (D) the mechanism is the same for any write that leaves old versions behind.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"time travel older than X days\\" + \\"VACUUM\\" → check retention (7 days by default)."},{"id":"10","question":"In a Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipeline, one dataset must incrementally process only new rows arriving from a streaming source, and another must recompute a full aggregation over all history each time it runs. Which combination is correct?","options":{"A":"Both as materialized views, because they always recompute from the source","B":"Both as streaming tables, because streaming is always more efficient","C":"The first as a materialized view and the second as a streaming table","D":"The first as a streaming table and the second as a materialized view"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A streaming table is append/incremental: it consumes each row once and only processes new data, which is what an ingestion layer needs. A materialized view re-evaluates its query over the full underlying data on each refresh, which is what a full aggregation over history needs (and what keeps it correct when old rows change).\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) materialized views for both would reprocess all history every time — expensive and unnecessary for the incremental feed. (B) streaming tables cannot recompute a full aggregate correctly when source rows are updated or deleted; they are append-oriented. (C) is exactly inverted.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"only new rows, incrementally\\" → streaming table; \\"full aggregation over all data each run\\" → materialized view."},{"id":"11","question":"A declarative pipeline must guarantee that no order with a negative total reaches the gold table. Invalid rows should be dropped and counted, but the pipeline must keep running and the metrics must be visible. What should the team define?","options":{"A":"A WHERE filter in the gold query, with no metric","B":"An expectation on the dataset that drops violating rows and records the violation metrics","C":"A try/except in a notebook that writes errors to a log table","D":"A job-level retry with a notification on failure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Expectations are the declarative data-quality mechanism: you attach a rule to a dataset (`EXPECT ... `) and choose the violation behaviour. Expecting with a DROP action keeps the pipeline running, discards the offending rows and publishes per-rule metrics (passed/failed/dropped) that you can monitor. Failing the update instead stops the pipeline, which is the wrong choice here because the requirement is to keep running.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a WHERE filter drops rows but gives you no visibility into how many or why. (C) manual try/except is imperative code the pipeline cannot govern as a quality rule and it does not produce the standard metrics. (D) a retry re-runs the same bad data; it never fixes the rule violation.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"must keep running\\" + \\"metrics visible\\" → expectation with a DROP (or warn) action, not a fail action."},{"id":"12","question":"A downstream team needs to read only the rows that changed in a Delta table (inserts, updates and deletes) instead of rescanning the full table every night. What must be enabled on the source table?","options":{"A":"Liquid clustering on the primary key","B":"A materialized view that refreshes nightly","C":"Change Data Feed, then read with table_changes() / readChangeFeed","D":"Time travel with VERSION AS OF between the two nights"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Change Data Feed (CDF) records row-level changes with a `_change_type` (insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete) and the commit version, so consumers can read only the delta between two versions instead of the whole table. It is enabled with `delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true` on the table.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) clustering is a query-performance layout, it does not expose changes. (B) a materialized view recomputes and rewrites the result; it does not give you a change stream. (D) time travel can diff two snapshots but forces you to scan both full versions and compute the difference yourself — CDF is the purpose-built mechanism.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"only changed rows\\" + \\"inserts, updates and deletes\\" → Change Data Feed."},{"id":"13","question":"A streaming query reads from a source that can deliver the same event twice, and the target must contain exactly one row per event_id. Which approach handles this correctly over time?","options":{"A":"Deduplicate with a watermark on the event timestamp and dropDuplicates on event_id","B":"A SELECT DISTINCT over the whole table at the end of the pipeline","C":"MERGE INTO on every micro-batch without a key condition","D":"OPTIMIZE with Z-ORDER on event_id after each batch"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Duplicate events arrive within a bounded window of their event time, so the correct streaming pattern is a watermark on the event timestamp plus `dropDuplicates(\\"event_id\\")`: Spark keeps state only for the watermark window (bounded memory) and drops repeats inside it. That is the standard exactly-once-ish pattern for streams with at-least-once delivery.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) DISTINCT hides duplicates in that query but does nothing for the table and cannot be maintained incrementally. (C) a MERGE without an ON key condition is invalid; with a key it would work but is far more expensive per micro-batch than stateful dedup. (D) Z-ORDER changes file layout, never row uniqueness.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"duplicates\\" + \\"streaming\\" + \\"one row per key\\" → watermark + dropDuplicates on the key."},{"id":"14","question":"A Lakeflow job has 12 tasks: ingest, then three parallel cleaning tasks, then a join, then publish. Halfway through a run, only the publish task fails on a transient permission error that has now been fixed. What is the cheapest correct way to finish the run?","options":{"A":"Re-run the whole job so every task starts from scratch","B":"Repair the run, which re-executes only the failed task and its downstream dependencies","C":"Create a new one-off job containing just the publish task","D":"Delete the run and schedule it again for the next day"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lakeflow Jobs tracks task state per run. \\"Repair run\\" re-executes the failed task and only what depends on it, reusing the successful outputs of the other tasks — you pick the tasks to repair. That is the designed recovery path for a partially failed DAG and the cheapest in compute.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a full re-run recomputes every task, including the expensive ingestion, for no benefit. (C) a one-off job loses the lineage and parameters of the original run and duplicates orchestration logic. (D) deleting the run destroys the history and delays the business result by a day.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"only one task failed\\" + \\"cheapest\\" → repair run, not re-run."},{"id":"15","question":"A nightly job and an analyst working interactively both need Spark compute. The team wants to minimise cost and avoid one workload starving the other. What is the recommended compute choice?","options":{"A":"One large all-purpose cluster shared by the job and the analyst","B":"A single all-purpose cluster with autoscaling and no isolation","C":"A job cluster for the nightly job and a separate all-purpose cluster for the analyst","D":"A SQL warehouse for both, because it is always cheaper"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Job clusters are created for the job run and terminated when it finishes, so you pay only for the job\'s duration and the workload is fully isolated. All-purpose clusters are for interactive work and stay alive (until idle timeout), which is the right home for an analyst but wasteful for a batch job. Separating them gives cost control and isolation.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) and (B) share one cluster, so the analyst competes for resources with the batch and the cluster has to stay up all day for a job that runs 40 minutes. (D) a SQL warehouse executes SQL, not arbitrary Spark/Python notebooks, and it is not universally cheaper.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"minimise cost\\" + \\"avoid starving\\" + scheduled job → job cluster; interactive work → all-purpose cluster."},{"id":"16","question":"A team keeps notebook paths, cluster IDs and catalog names hard-coded per environment, and every promotion to production is a manual edit. They want the same artifacts deployed identically to dev, staging and prod. What should they adopt?","options":{"A":"Export notebooks as .ipynb and copy them into each workspace by hand","B":"A separate Git branch per environment and manual job re-creation","C":"A shell script that calls the REST API with hard-coded IDs","D":"Declarative Automation Bundles (formerly Databricks Asset Bundles), with per-target variables and databricks bundle deploy -t <target>"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Declarative Automation Bundles (DAB, formerly Databricks Asset Bundles) describe jobs, pipelines, notebooks and their configuration in versioned YAML, with targets (dev/staging/prod) that supply environment-specific variables such as workspace host, catalog or cluster size. `databricks bundle deploy -t prod` validates and deploys the same bundle definition, so nothing is edited by hand between environments.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) manual copying is exactly the drift problem being solved. (B) branches alone do not parameterise IDs or catalogs and still require manual job creation. (C) a REST script with hard-coded IDs is the same fragility in code form — no validation, no diff, no idempotency guarantees.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"same artifacts to every environment\\" + \\"no manual edits\\" → Declarative Automation Bundles with targets."},{"id":"17","question":"A new analyst must be able to read one schema in Unity Catalog but nothing else in the metastore. What is the correct grant?","options":{"A":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG main TO `analysts`; GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA main.sales TO `analysts`; GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA main.sales TO `analysts`","B":"GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON METASTORE TO `analysts`","C":"GRANT SELECT ON TABLE main.sales.orders TO `analysts` only","D":"Add the analyst to the workspace admins group so they can see everything"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Unity Catalog privileges are hierarchical and every level must be granted: USE CATALOG to see the catalog, USE SCHEMA to see the schema, and then the data privilege (SELECT) on the schema or table. Granting USE + SELECT on the schema gives read access to all current and future tables in it without exposing the rest of the metastore.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) metastore-wide privileges violate least privilege and expose every catalog. (C) granting on a single table works for that table only — new tables in the schema would stay unreadable, which is not what \\"read the schema\\" means. (D) workspace admin is an operational role that bypasses data governance entirely; it is the opposite of least privilege.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"read one schema, nothing else\\" → USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA + SELECT on that schema."},{"id":"18","question":"The security team requires that support agents can query a customers table but only ever see rows for their own region, and never see the email column in clear text. What should be implemented in Unity Catalog?","options":{"A":"Two separate tables, one per region, and a view without the email column","B":"A row filter function using the current user\'s region, plus a column mask on the email column","C":"A dynamic view that all agents query instead of the table, with no table grants","D":"SELECT privileges only on a subset of columns and a WHERE clause the agents must remember"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Unity Catalog supports row filters and column masks natively, applied at the table level so every query path is covered: the row filter restricts visible rows per principal, and the column mask returns a masked value (for example a hash or `***`) for principals without the unmask privilege. Because the rules live on the table, they also apply to dashboards, notebooks and BI tools — not just to hand-written SQL.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) duplicating tables per region creates drift and does not mask the column. (C) a view helps but depends on everyone using it, and without grants on the table it is an all-or-nothing design rather than row-level security. (D) column-level SELECT does not filter rows, and a WHERE clause each user must remember is not enforcement.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"only their own region\\" + \\"never see the email\\" → row filter + column mask."},{"id":"19","question":"A Spark job that joins a 2 TB fact table with a 40 GB dimension runs for hours, most tasks finish fast and a handful take forever, and the Spark UI shows a huge shuffle read on those tasks. What is the most likely cause and first fix?","options":{"A":"Too few executors: raise the cluster size and add more cores","B":"The Delta table needs VACUUM before the join","C":"Data skew on the join key: inspect the key distribution and salt or broadcast as appropriate","D":"Photon is disabled: enable it and re-run"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The signature is textbook skew: a small number of tasks doing enormous shuffle reads while the rest finish immediately. A few values of the join key hold most of the rows, so those partitions cannot be split across executors. The first step is to measure the key distribution (`GROUP BY key ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC`); then broadcast the small side if it fits, or salt the hot keys so the work spreads across partitions.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) adding executors does not split a single hot partition — the long tasks stay long. (B) VACUUM is housekeeping for old files and has no effect on shuffle behaviour. (D) Photon speeds up execution generally but does not redistribute skewed partitions.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"most tasks fast, a few take forever\\" + \\"huge shuffle read\\" → skew."},{"id":"20","question":"An operations team needs a daily report of which jobs failed, how long queries took and who accessed which table, all queryable with SQL and retained beyond the workspace UI. What is the right source?","options":{"A":"The Spark UI, opened per cluster and screenshotted into a ticket","B":"Cluster logs delivered to cloud storage and parsed with a custom script","C":"The job run history page in the workspace","D":"System tables (query history, job runs, audit logs) queried with SQL"},"correct":"D","explanation":"System tables expose operational metadata as Delta tables you can join and query with SQL, with retention beyond what the UI keeps: query history, job run history and audit logs (who accessed which object) among others. That makes dashboards, alerts and cross-workspace reporting possible without exporting anything.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) the Spark UI is per-cluster, ephemeral and not queryable — useless for a daily report. (B) log delivery is for diagnostics (driver/executor logs), not for access auditing, and requires you to build the parser. (C) the run history page is a UI view with limited retention and no SQL access.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"queryable with SQL\\" + \\"retained beyond the UI\\" + \\"who accessed what\\" → system tables."}]},{"id":"databricks-dea-es","title":"Databricks Data Engineer Associate — Examen de pr\xe1ctica 1 (ES)","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"Un equipo de ingenier\xeda de datos necesita garantizar que los datos crudos de clientes nunca salgan de su propia cuenta cloud, pero quiere que Databricks siga gestionando los notebooks, la planificaci\xf3n de jobs y la configuraci\xf3n de cl\xfasteres. \xbfQu\xe9 describe c\xf3mo la arquitectura de Databricks lo permite?","options":{"A":"Todos los datos se copian al control plane de Databricks para que los cl\xfasteres arranquen m\xe1s r\xe1pido","B":"El control plane gestiona metadatos del workspace y la orquestaci\xf3n, mientras los datos se quedan en la cuenta cloud del cliente, en el data plane","C":"El data plane guarda los notebooks y la definici\xf3n de cl\xfasteres, y el control plane guarda las tablas Delta","D":"La localizaci\xf3n del dato no se puede garantizar, as\xed que un private link es la \xfanica forma de mantenerlo en la cuenta"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Databricks separa responsabilidades: el control plane (gestionado por Databricks) guarda los metadatos del workspace — notebooks, definiciones de jobs, configuraci\xf3n de cl\xfasteres, metadatos de Unity Catalog — y el data plane corre en la cuenta cloud del cliente, donde los cl\xfasteres leen y escriben los datos reales en el almacenamiento de esa nube. Por eso los datos crudos no tienen que salir de la cuenta.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) es el error cl\xe1sico — copiar los datos al control plane romper\xeda todo el modelo de seguridad y no funciona as\xed. (C) invierte los dos planos. (D) el private link mejora el aislamiento de red, pero no es lo que garantiza la residencia del dato; lo hace la separaci\xf3n de planos.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xablos datos deben quedarse en nuestra cuenta\xbb + \xabDatabricks sigue orquestando\xbb → separaci\xf3n control plane / data plane."},{"id":"2","question":"Un pipeline ingiere miles de ficheros JSON al d\xeda desde cloud storage a una tabla Delta. A veces los ficheros nuevos a\xf1aden campos y el pipeline no debe fallar cuando ocurre. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque de ingesta gestiona la evoluci\xf3n del esquema con el menor esfuerzo operativo?","options":{"A":"COPY INTO con esquema fijo y un ALTER TABLE manual por cada campo nuevo","B":"Un notebook programado que lee cada fichero con pandas y a\xf1ade filas","C":"Auto Loader (cloudFiles) con evoluci\xf3n de esquema activada y checkpoint en cada ejecuci\xf3n","D":"Una query en streaming con esquema fijo que descarta las columnas desconocidas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Auto Loader (formato cloudFiles) es el camino gestionado de ingesta incremental: descubre ficheros nuevos sin reprocesar los antiguos y, con la evoluci\xf3n de esquema activada, a\xf1ade las columnas nuevas a la tabla destino en vez de fallar el lote. El checkpoint registra qu\xe9 ficheros ya se procesaron, as\xed que un reinicio contin\xfaa de forma incremental.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) COPY INTO s\xed puede ingerir de forma incremental, pero esquema fijo m\xe1s ALTER TABLE manual por campo es justo la carga operativa que el enunciado descarta. (B) pandas en un notebook no es incremental ni escalable y pierde las garant\xedas de Delta. (D) fijar el esquema y descartar columnas tira datos en silencio.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabcampos nuevos\xbb + \xabno debe fallar\xbb + \xabm\xednimo esfuerzo operativo\xbb → Auto Loader con evoluci\xf3n de esquema."},{"id":"3","question":"Un equipo ingiere unas 50 ficheros nuevos por hora en una tabla Delta y quiere el descubrimiento de ficheros de menor latencia y m\xe1s escalable que ofrece Auto Loader. Su almacenamiento cloud puede publicar notificaciones de eventos. \xbfQu\xe9 debe configurar?","options":{"A":"Directory listing con un trigger cada 5 minutos","B":"Un refresh completo de la tabla en cada ejecuci\xf3n","C":"COPY INTO dentro de un bucle for-each sobre el prefijo del bucket","D":"Modo de notificaci\xf3n de ficheros, con la cola y los permisos IAM necesarios creados por Databricks"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Auto Loader tiene dos modos de descubrimiento. El modo de notificaci\xf3n se suscribe a los eventos del almacenamiento (S3 → SQS/SNS, ADLS → Event Grid, GCS → Pub/Sub) y reacciona a los ficheros nuevos seg\xfan llegan; escala mejor y cuesta menos por fichero porque no lista el directorio una y otra vez. Databricks puede crear la cola y el rol IAM por ti al activarlo.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) el listado de directorio funciona y no necesita permisos extra, pero lista el prefijo en cada intervalo: detecta m\xe1s tarde y es m\xe1s caro cuanto m\xe1s crece el n\xfamero de ficheros. (B) un refresh completo reprocesa todo y anula la ingesta incremental. (C) lanzar COPY INTO por prefijo es orquestaci\xf3n manual y sigue dependiendo del listado.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabmenor latencia\xbb + \xabel storage puede publicar eventos\xbb → modo notificaci\xf3n; \xabsin permisos extra / lo m\xe1s simple\xbb → listado de directorio."},{"id":"4","question":"Un equipo debe cargar una vez 200 ficheros CSV en una tabla Delta y despu\xe9s ir a\xf1adiendo unos pocos ficheros al d\xeda. Quieren la opci\xf3n m\xe1s simple que siga siendo incremental tras la carga inicial. \xbfQu\xe9 usan?","options":{"A":"COPY INTO para la carga inicial, que adem\xe1s puede re-ejecutarse de forma incremental para recoger los ficheros nuevos","B":"Auto Loader con evoluci\xf3n de esquema y una cola de notificaciones","C":"Un CREATE TABLE AS SELECT puntual sobre toda la carpeta","D":"Una tabla externa que lee los CSV en su sitio para siempre"},"correct":"A","explanation":"COPY INTO es la v\xeda SQL para cargar ficheros de forma idempotente en una tabla Delta: recuerda qu\xe9 ficheros ya carg\xf3, as\xed que re-ejecutarlo solo ingiere los nuevos. Para una carga masiva puntual m\xe1s unos pocos ficheros diarios es la menor maquinaria posible: sin gestionar checkpoints ni mantener viva una query en streaming.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (B) Auto Loader es la respuesta cuando los ficheros llegan de forma continua, en gran n\xfamero, o necesitan evoluci\xf3n de esquema y baja latencia; aqu\xed a\xf1ade una cola y estado de streaming sin apenas beneficio. (C) CTAS es una foto fija: los ficheros nuevos no se recoger\xedan nunca. (D) una tabla externa no ingiere: lee los ficheros en cada consulta y pierde rendimiento y features de Delta.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabcargar una vez y luego pocos ficheros al d\xeda\xbb + \xablo m\xe1s simple\xbb → COPY INTO. \xabMiles de ficheros / continuo / evoluci\xf3n de esquema\xbb → Auto Loader."},{"id":"5","question":"Al ingerir ficheros JSON con Auto Loader, algunos registros traen un campo cuyo tipo no coincide con el esquema inferido. El equipo no quiere que la carga falle, pero tampoco puede perder esos registros. \xbfQu\xe9 ofrece Auto Loader para este caso?","options":{"A":"Descarta los registros problem\xe1ticos en silencio y reporta el conteo en el checkpoint","B":"Aborta el stream y exige un ALTER TABLE manual antes de reiniciar","C":"Conserva el registro y guarda los campos no parseados o con tipo distinto en la columna `_rescued_data`","D":"Convierte a STRING todos los valores que no encajan y sobrescribe la columna original"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Auto Loader nunca tira un registro por un problema de tipo. Cuando un campo no se puede parsear al tipo esperado (o no est\xe1 en el esquema), guarda el payload original de los campos rescatados en la columna `_rescued_data`, de modo que puedas inspeccionarlo, ponerlo en cuarentena o reprocesarlo despu\xe9s. Es el mecanismo de rescate integrado.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) Auto Loader no descarta registros — perder datos en silencio es justo lo que `_rescued_data` evita. (B) un desajuste de tipo no aborta el stream; solo lo har\xeda un error irrecuperable. (D) no sobrescribe la columna con un cast: la columna parseada conserva su valor y el valor crudo queda al lado.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabno debe fallar\xbb + \xabno podemos perder registros\xbb + Auto Loader → `_rescued_data`."},{"id":"6","question":"Un job de ingesta en streaming debe ejecutarse cada 10 minutos desde un job programado, procesar solo los ficheros que llegaron desde la ejecuci\xf3n anterior y luego parar, en vez de correr de forma continua. \xbfQu\xe9 configuraci\xf3n de trigger usas?","options":{"A":"Trigger.AvailableNow dentro de un job que corre de forma continua","B":"Trigger.AvailableNow (o Once) con el job programado terminando al acabar el lote","C":"Un cl\xfaster continuo con auto-scaling y sin trigger","D":"Trigger.ProcessingTime(\\"10 minutes\\") sobre un cl\xfaster continuo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"`Trigger.AvailableNow` procesa todos los datos disponibles en el momento en que arranca la query —como un batch acotado— y luego termina. Combinado con un job programado (cada 10 minutos) da procesamiento incremental sin pagar un cl\xfaster encendido 24/7. `Trigger.Once` es el equivalente antiguo para este patr\xf3n.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) AvailableNow dentro de un job continuo anula el prop\xf3sito: la gracia es que la ejecuci\xf3n acabe. (C) un cl\xfaster continuo sin trigger es lo contrario: siempre encendido y siempre pagando. (D) ProcessingTime es para streaming continuo con micro-lotes; mantiene la query viva.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabprocesa solo los ficheros nuevos y luego para\xbb + \xabjob programado\xbb → AvailableNow/Once, no un stream continuo."},{"id":"7","question":"Cada noche llega un lote con el estado completo y actual de los productos, incluyendo modificaciones y productos nuevos, pero sin borrados. La tabla Delta destino ya tiene las filas de ayer. \xbfQu\xe9 sentencia carga el fichero de hoy correctamente sin duplicar los productos que ya existen?","options":{"A":"INSERT INTO target SELECT * FROM staging","B":"INSERT OVERWRITE target SELECT * FROM staging","C":"MERGE INTO target USING staging ON target.id = staging.id WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *","D":"DELETE FROM target y despu\xe9s INSERT INTO target SELECT * FROM staging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MERGE INTO es la primitiva de upsert en Delta Lake: la cl\xe1usula ON decide qu\xe9 filas del staging coinciden con filas existentes, WHEN MATCHED las actualiza y WHEN NOT MATCHED inserta las nuevas. Una sola pasada, ACID, y sin duplicados para los productos que ya estaban.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) INSERT a secas a\xf1ade todo, as\xed que cada producto aparece dos veces. (B) INSERT OVERWRITE reemplaza la tabla entera por el fichero de hoy: solo ser\xeda correcto si el fichero es de verdad el estado completo y aceptas perder historial y particiones; no es un upsert y no es seguro con extracciones parciales. (D) borrar y luego insertar deja la tabla vac\xeda para los lectores durante la operaci\xf3n y rompe la concurrencia.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabestado completo con modificaciones y filas nuevas\xbb + \xabsin duplicados\xbb → MERGE INTO. \xabSolo filas nuevas, nunca vistas\xbb → append/INSERT."},{"id":"8","question":"Una tabla Delta de 500 TB se filtra casi siempre por customer_id y por event_date. El equipo est\xe1 cansado de elegir entre particionar y usar Z-ORDER y quiere que Databricks elija y mantenga el layout autom\xe1ticamente, sin reescribir la tabla. \xbfQu\xe9 activan?","options":{"A":"Particionar por event_date y hacer Z-ORDER BY customer_id en cada escritura","B":"Liquid clustering por customer_id y event_date","C":"OPTIMIZE con Z-ORDER BY customer_id una vez a la semana y eliminar las particiones","D":"Bucketing por customer_id con 200 buckets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Liquid clustering sustituye la decisi\xf3n partici\xf3n/Z-ORDER: declaras las claves de clustering en la tabla y Databricks organiza los datos de forma incremental, sin reescribir los ficheros existentes cuando cambian las claves. En una tabla enorme con dos columnas de filtro independientes evita el problema de sobre-particionar (millones de ficheros diminutos) que crea particionar por fecha.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) hacer Z-ORDER en cada escritura es caro y particionar por fecha puede multiplicar el n\xfamero de ficheros. (C) el Z-ORDER semanal es un mantenimiento manual y quitar las particiones pierde el pruning por fecha. (D) el bucketing es una t\xe9cnica de la \xe9poca Hive que Delta no necesita y que hay que gestionar a mano.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabquiere que Databricks elija y mantenga el layout\xbb + \xabsin reescribir\xbb → liquid clustering. \xabTabla heredada, ajuste manual\xbb → Z-ORDER."},{"id":"9","question":"Un auditor necesita consultar la tabla Delta tal como estaba hace 30 d\xedas. El equipo ejecuta VACUUM a diario con la configuraci\xf3n por defecto. \xbfQu\xe9 pasa con ese requisito?","options":{"A":"Falla: la retenci\xf3n por defecto es de 7 d\xedas, as\xed que las versiones m\xe1s antiguas se borran f\xedsicamente aunque el time travel siga funcionando dentro de esa ventana","B":"Funciona, porque VACUUM solo elimina ficheros que ninguna query est\xe1 leyendo en ese momento","C":"Funciona, porque Delta guarda una copia completa de cada versi\xf3n durante un a\xf1o por defecto","D":"Falla solo si la tabla se escribi\xf3 con INSERT OVERWRITE"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El time travel est\xe1 limitado por la retenci\xf3n. VACUUM borra f\xedsicamente los ficheros que ya no referencia nadie, y el umbral de retenci\xf3n por defecto es de 7 d\xedas. Con un VACUUM diario y valores por defecto, cualquier versi\xf3n de hace m\xe1s de siete d\xedas ya no existe: `VERSION AS OF` / `TIMESTAMP AS OF` dar\xe1 error porque los ficheros subyacentes no est\xe1n. Para conservar 30 d\xedas hay que subir `delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration` (o la cl\xe1usula `RETAIN N HOURS`) por encima de la ventana de auditor\xeda, y aceptar el coste de almacenamiento.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (B) VACUUM elimina ficheros no referenciados sin importar qui\xe9n est\xe9 leyendo; su seguridad depende de la ventana de retenci\xf3n, no de queries vivas. (C) no existe una copia de un a\xf1o por defecto — ese es justo el error que la pregunta quiere detectar. (D) el mecanismo es el mismo para cualquier escritura que deje versiones antiguas.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabtime travel de hace m\xe1s de X d\xedas\xbb + \xabVACUUM\xbb → revisa la retenci\xf3n (7 d\xedas por defecto)."},{"id":"10","question":"En un pipeline declarativo de Lakeflow, un dataset debe procesar de forma incremental solo las filas nuevas que llegan de una fuente en streaming, y otro debe recalcular una agregaci\xf3n completa sobre todo el historial en cada ejecuci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 combinaci\xf3n es correcta?","options":{"A":"Los dos como vistas materializadas, porque siempre recalculan desde el origen","B":"Los dos como tablas en streaming, porque el streaming siempre es m\xe1s eficiente","C":"El primero como vista materializada y el segundo como tabla en streaming","D":"El primero como tabla en streaming y el segundo como vista materializada"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Una tabla en streaming es incremental: consume cada fila una vez y solo procesa datos nuevos, que es lo que necesita una capa de ingesta. Una vista materializada reeval\xfaa su consulta sobre todos los datos subyacentes en cada refresco, que es lo que necesita una agregaci\xf3n completa sobre el historial (y lo que la mantiene correcta cuando cambian filas antiguas).\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) dos vistas materializadas reprocesar\xedan todo el historial cada vez: caro e innecesario para el feed incremental. (B) las tablas en streaming no pueden recalcular una agregaci\xf3n completa de forma correcta cuando las filas de origen se actualizan o borran; est\xe1n orientadas a append. (C) es exactamente lo contrario.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabsolo filas nuevas, incremental\xbb → tabla en streaming; \xabagregaci\xf3n completa sobre todos los datos en cada ejecuci\xf3n\xbb → vista materializada."},{"id":"11","question":"Un pipeline declarativo debe garantizar que ning\xfan pedido con total negativo llega a la tabla gold. Las filas inv\xe1lidas deben descartarse y contarse, pero el pipeline no debe detenerse y las m\xe9tricas tienen que ser visibles. \xbfQu\xe9 define el equipo?","options":{"A":"Un filtro WHERE en la consulta gold, sin m\xe9trica alguna","B":"Una expectation sobre el dataset que descarta las filas que la incumplen y registra las m\xe9tricas de violaci\xf3n","C":"Un try/except en un notebook que escribe los errores en una tabla de log","D":"Un reintento a nivel de job con notificaci\xf3n en caso de fallo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las expectations son el mecanismo declarativo de calidad de datos: asocias una regla a un dataset (`EXPECT ...`) y eliges el comportamiento ante la violaci\xf3n. Con acci\xf3n DROP el pipeline sigue corriendo, descarta las filas infractoras y publica m\xe9tricas por regla (pasadas/falladas/descartadas) que puedes monitorizar. Poner la acci\xf3n en fail detendr\xeda el pipeline, que es lo contrario de lo que pide el enunciado.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) un filtro WHERE descarta filas pero no te da visibilidad de cu\xe1ntas ni por qu\xe9. (C) el try/except manual es c\xf3digo imperativo que el pipeline no puede gobernar como regla de calidad y no produce las m\xe9tricas est\xe1ndar. (D) un reintento vuelve a procesar los mismos datos malos; nunca corrige la violaci\xf3n.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabdebe seguir corriendo\xbb + \xabm\xe9tricas visibles\xbb → expectation con acci\xf3n DROP (o warn), no fail."},{"id":"12","question":"Un equipo aguas abajo necesita leer solo las filas que cambiaron en una tabla Delta (inserts, updates y deletes) en vez de reescanear la tabla completa cada noche. \xbfQu\xe9 hay que activar en la tabla origen?","options":{"A":"Liquid clustering sobre la clave primaria","B":"Una vista materializada que se refresca cada noche","C":"Change Data Feed y leer con table_changes() / readChangeFeed","D":"Time travel con VERSION AS OF entre las dos noches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Change Data Feed (CDF) registra los cambios a nivel de fila con un `_change_type` (insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete) y la versi\xf3n del commit, as\xed que los consumidores leen solo el delta entre dos versiones en lugar de la tabla entera. Se activa con `delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true` en la tabla.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) el clustering es un layout para rendimiento de consulta, no expone cambios. (B) una vista materializada recalcula y reescribe el resultado; no da un flujo de cambios. (D) el time travel puede comparar dos snapshots, pero te obliga a escanear las dos versiones completas y calcular la diferencia t\xfa mismo; CDF es el mecanismo pensado para esto.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabsolo las filas que cambiaron\xbb + \xabinserts, updates y deletes\xbb → Change Data Feed."},{"id":"13","question":"Una query en streaming lee de una fuente que puede entregar el mismo evento dos veces y la tabla destino debe tener exactamente una fila por event_id. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque lo resuelve correctamente a lo largo del tiempo?","options":{"A":"Deduplicar con un watermark sobre el timestamp del evento y dropDuplicates por event_id","B":"Un SELECT DISTINCT sobre toda la tabla al final del pipeline","C":"Un MERGE INTO en cada micro-lote sin condici\xf3n de clave","D":"OPTIMIZE con Z-ORDER por event_id despu\xe9s de cada lote"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Los eventos duplicados llegan dentro de una ventana acotada de su event time, as\xed que el patr\xf3n correcto en streaming es un watermark sobre el timestamp del evento m\xe1s `dropDuplicates(\\"event_id\\")`: Spark mantiene estado solo para la ventana del watermark (memoria acotada) y descarta las repeticiones dentro de ella. Es el patr\xf3n est\xe1ndar para fuentes con entrega at-least-once.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (B) DISTINCT esconde duplicados en esa consulta pero no hace nada por la tabla y no se puede mantener de forma incremental. (C) un MERGE sin condici\xf3n de clave en el ON no es v\xe1lido; con clave funcionar\xeda, pero es mucho m\xe1s caro por micro-lote que la deduplicaci\xf3n con estado. (D) Z-ORDER cambia el layout de ficheros, nunca la unicidad de filas.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabduplicados\xbb + \xabstreaming\xbb + \xabuna fila por clave\xbb → watermark + dropDuplicates por la clave."},{"id":"14","question":"Un job de Lakeflow tiene 12 tareas: ingesta, luego tres tareas de limpieza en paralelo, luego un join y por \xfaltimo publish. A mitad de ejecuci\xf3n solo falla publish por un error transitorio de permisos que ya est\xe1 corregido. \xbfCu\xe1l es la forma m\xe1s barata y correcta de terminar la ejecuci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Re-ejecutar el job completo para que todas las tareas empiecen de cero","B":"Reparar la ejecuci\xf3n, que re-ejecuta solo la tarea fallida y sus dependencias aguas abajo","C":"Crear un job puntual que contenga solo la tarea publish","D":"Borrar la ejecuci\xf3n y volver a programarla para el d\xeda siguiente"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Lakeflow Jobs guarda el estado de cada tarea por ejecuci\xf3n. \xabRepair run\xbb re-ejecuta la tarea fallida y solo lo que depende de ella, reutilizando las salidas correctas del resto — t\xfa eliges qu\xe9 tareas reparar. Es el camino de recuperaci\xf3n dise\xf1ado para un DAG parcialmente fallido y el m\xe1s barato en c\xf3mputo.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) una re-ejecuci\xf3n completa recalcula todas las tareas, incluida la ingesta cara, sin ning\xfan beneficio. (C) un job puntual pierde el linaje y los par\xe1metros de la ejecuci\xf3n original y duplica la l\xf3gica de orquestaci\xf3n. (D) borrar la ejecuci\xf3n destruye el historial y retrasa el resultado de negocio un d\xeda.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabsolo fall\xf3 una tarea\xbb + \xablo m\xe1s barato\xbb → repair run, no re-run."},{"id":"15","question":"Un job nocturno y un analista que trabaja de forma interactiva necesitan c\xf3mputo Spark. El equipo quiere minimizar el coste y evitar que una carga deje sin recursos a la otra. \xbfQu\xe9 elecci\xf3n de c\xf3mputo es la recomendada?","options":{"A":"Un cl\xfaster all-purpose grande compartido por el job y el analista","B":"Un \xfanico cl\xfaster all-purpose con autoscaling y sin aislamiento","C":"Un job cluster para el job nocturno y otro all-purpose separado para el analista","D":"Un SQL warehouse para ambos, porque siempre es m\xe1s barato"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los job clusters se crean para la ejecuci\xf3n del job y se apagan al terminar, as\xed que pagas solo la duraci\xf3n del job y la carga queda totalmente aislada. Los all-purpose clusters son para trabajo interactivo y siguen vivos (hasta el timeout de inactividad), que es el sitio correcto para un analista pero un desperdicio para un batch. Separarlos da control de coste y aislamiento.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) y (B) comparten cl\xfaster, as\xed que el analista compite por recursos con el batch y el cl\xfaster tiene que estar encendido todo el d\xeda para un job de 40 minutos. (D) un SQL warehouse ejecuta SQL, no notebooks arbitrarios de Spark/Python, y no es siempre m\xe1s barato.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabminimizar coste\xbb + \xabevitar que se estorben\xbb + job programado → job cluster; trabajo interactivo → all-purpose."},{"id":"16","question":"Un equipo mantiene rutas de notebooks, IDs de cl\xfaster y nombres de cat\xe1logo hard-codeados por entorno, y cada promoci\xf3n a producci\xf3n es una edici\xf3n manual. Quieren desplegar los mismos artefactos de forma id\xe9ntica en dev, staging y prod. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedan adoptar?","options":{"A":"Exportar los notebooks como .ipynb y copiarlos a cada workspace a mano","B":"Una rama de Git por entorno y recrear los jobs manualmente","C":"Un script de shell que llama a la API REST con IDs hard-codeados","D":"Declarative Automation Bundles (antes Databricks Asset Bundles), con variables por target y databricks bundle deploy -t <target>"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Declarative Automation Bundles (DAB, antes Databricks Asset Bundles) describen jobs, pipelines, notebooks y su configuraci\xf3n en YAML versionado, con targets (dev/staging/prod) que aportan las variables espec\xedficas de entorno: host del workspace, cat\xe1logo o tama\xf1o de cl\xfaster. `databricks bundle deploy -t prod` valida y despliega la misma definici\xf3n, as\xed que no se edita nada a mano entre entornos.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) copiar a mano es justo el problema de deriva que se quiere resolver. (B) las ramas por s\xed solas no parametrizan IDs ni cat\xe1logos y siguen exigiendo crear jobs a mano. (C) un script REST con IDs hard-codeados es la misma fragilidad en forma de c\xf3digo — sin validaci\xf3n, sin diff y sin idempotencia.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xablos mismos artefactos en todos los entornos\xbb + \xabsin ediciones manuales\xbb → Declarative Automation Bundles con targets."},{"id":"17","question":"Un analista nuevo debe poder leer un esquema de Unity Catalog y nada m\xe1s del metastore. \xbfCu\xe1l es el grant correcto?","options":{"A":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG main TO `analysts`; GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA main.sales TO `analysts`; GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA main.sales TO `analysts`","B":"GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON METASTORE TO `analysts`","C":"Solo GRANT SELECT ON TABLE main.sales.orders TO `analysts`","D":"A\xf1adir al analista al grupo de workspace admins para que lo vea todo"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Los privilegios de Unity Catalog son jer\xe1rquicos y hay que conceder cada nivel: USE CATALOG para ver el cat\xe1logo, USE SCHEMA para ver el esquema y despu\xe9s el privilegio de datos (SELECT) sobre el esquema o la tabla. Conceder USE + SELECT sobre el esquema da lectura a todas las tablas actuales y futuras de ese esquema sin exponer el resto del metastore.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (B) privilegios a nivel de metastore violan el m\xednimo privilegio y exponen todos los cat\xe1logos. (C) conceder sobre una sola tabla sirve solo para esa tabla — las tablas nuevas del esquema quedar\xedan ilegibles, que no es lo que significa \xableer el esquema\xbb. (D) workspace admin es un rol operativo que se salta el gobierno del dato; es lo contrario del m\xednimo privilegio.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xableer un esquema y nada m\xe1s\xbb → USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA + SELECT sobre ese esquema."},{"id":"18","question":"El equipo de seguridad exige que los agentes de soporte puedan consultar una tabla de clientes pero solo vean filas de su propia regi\xf3n, y que nunca vean la columna email en claro. \xbfQu\xe9 hay que implementar en Unity Catalog?","options":{"A":"Dos tablas separadas, una por regi\xf3n, y una vista sin la columna email","B":"Una funci\xf3n de row filter que use la regi\xf3n del usuario actual, m\xe1s un column mask sobre la columna email","C":"Una vista din\xe1mica que todos los agentes consulten en lugar de la tabla, sin grants sobre la tabla","D":"Privilegios SELECT solo sobre un subconjunto de columnas y un WHERE que los agentes deben recordar"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Unity Catalog soporta row filters y column masks de forma nativa, y se aplican a nivel de tabla, as\xed que cubren todas las rutas de consulta: el row filter limita las filas visibles por principal y el column mask devuelve un valor enmascarado (un hash o `***`) a quien no tiene el privilegio de desenmascarar. Como las reglas viven en la tabla, tambi\xe9n aplican a dashboards, notebooks y herramientas de BI, no solo al SQL escrito a mano.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) duplicar tablas por regi\xf3n genera deriva y no enmascara la columna. (C) una vista ayuda, pero depende de que todos la usen, y sin grants sobre la tabla es un dise\xf1o todo-o-nada en vez de seguridad a nivel de fila. (D) el SELECT por columna no filtra filas, y un WHERE que cada usuario debe recordar no es una imposici\xf3n.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabsolo su propia regi\xf3n\xbb + \xabnunca ver el email\xbb → row filter + column mask."},{"id":"19","question":"Un job de Spark que hace join de una tabla de hechos de 2 TB con una dimensi\xf3n de 40 GB tarda horas: la mayor\xeda de las tareas acaban r\xe1pido y unas pocas no terminan nunca, y el Spark UI muestra una lectura de shuffle enorme en esas tareas. \xbfCu\xe1l es la causa m\xe1s probable y el primer arreglo?","options":{"A":"Faltan executors: subir el tama\xf1o del cl\xfaster y a\xf1adir m\xe1s cores","B":"Hay que hacer VACUUM en la tabla Delta antes del join","C":"Skew en la clave del join: inspeccionar la distribuci\xf3n de la clave y aplicar salting o broadcast seg\xfan el caso","D":"Photon est\xe1 desactivado: activarlo y re-ejecutar"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La firma es de manual: unas pocas tareas hacen lecturas de shuffle enormes mientras el resto acaba al instante. Unos pocos valores de la clave del join concentran casi todas las filas, as\xed que esas particiones no se pueden repartir entre executors. El primer paso es medir la distribuci\xf3n de la clave (`GROUP BY clave ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC`); despu\xe9s, hacer broadcast del lado peque\xf1o si cabe, o aplicar salting a las claves calientes para repartir el trabajo.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) a\xf1adir executors no divide una partici\xf3n caliente — las tareas largas siguen largas. (B) VACUUM es mantenimiento de ficheros antiguos y no afecta al shuffle. (D) Photon acelera la ejecuci\xf3n en general pero no redistribuye particiones sesgadas.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabla mayor\xeda acaba r\xe1pido, unas pocas tardan much\xedsimo\xbb + \xabshuffle enorme\xbb → skew."},{"id":"20","question":"Un equipo de operaciones necesita un informe diario de qu\xe9 jobs fallaron, cu\xe1nto tardaron las queries y qui\xe9n accedi\xf3 a cada tabla, todo consultable con SQL y con retenci\xf3n m\xe1s all\xe1 de la UI del workspace. \xbfCu\xe1l es la fuente correcta?","options":{"A":"El Spark UI, abierto por cl\xfaster y capturado en un ticket","B":"Los logs de cl\xfaster enviados a cloud storage y parseados con un script propio","C":"La p\xe1gina de historial de ejecuciones del job en el workspace","D":"Las system tables (query history, job runs, audit logs) consultadas con SQL"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las system tables exponen los metadatos operativos como tablas Delta que puedes unir y consultar con SQL, con retenci\xf3n m\xe1s all\xe1 de la que guarda la UI: historial de queries, historial de ejecuciones de jobs y audit logs (qui\xe9n accedi\xf3 a qu\xe9 objeto), entre otros. Eso permite dashboards, alertas y reporting entre workspaces sin exportar nada.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las otras: (A) el Spark UI es por cl\xfaster, ef\xedmero y no consultable — in\xfatil para un informe diario. (B) el env\xedo de logs sirve para diagn\xf3stico (logs de driver/executor), no para auditar accesos, y te obliga a construir el parser. (C) la p\xe1gina de historial es una vista de UI con retenci\xf3n limitada y sin acceso SQL.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabconsultable con SQL\xbb + \xabretenci\xf3n m\xe1s all\xe1 de la UI\xbb + \xabqui\xe9n accedi\xf3 a qu\xe9\xbb → system tables."}]},{"id":"gcp-pca-case-studies","title":"GCP Professional Cloud Architect — Case Studies","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"Altostrat Media case study. Altostrat runs Kubernetes both in GKE and in its on-premises data centers (content ingestion and archival). Their technical requirements ask for modernized CI/CD for containerized deployments with a centralized management platform, and scalable Kubernetes environments on-premises and in the cloud. What should the architect recommend?","options":{"A":"Register the GKE and on-premises clusters in a GKE Enterprise fleet, manage configuration and policy centrally with Config Sync and Policy Controller, and release with Cloud Build + Cloud Deploy","B":"Keep separate Jenkins servers per environment and replicate the YAML manifests between them with a nightly rsync job","C":"Migrate every on-premises workload to Cloud Run functions so that no Kubernetes is left to manage","D":"Create one GKE Autopilot cluster per team in the cloud and leave the on-premises clusters managed by hand"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The key words are \\"centralized management platform\\" and \\"Kubernetes both on-premises and in the cloud\\". A GKE Enterprise fleet lets you register GKE clusters and on-premises clusters (GKE on bare metal / VMware or attached clusters) under one control surface: Config Sync applies the same Git-defined configuration to all of them and Policy Controller enforces guardrails. Cloud Build builds and tests the images and Cloud Deploy promotes releases through environments.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) is exactly the fragmented, manual setup the case wants to leave behind. (C) ingestion and archival are long-running, stateful workflows on-premises; the case asks for Kubernetes on-premises, not for a rewrite to functions. (D) solves only the cloud half and keeps two operating models.\\n\\nExam cue: hybrid + \\"centralized management\\" + containers → GKE Enterprise fleet (formerly Anthos)."},{"id":"2","question":"Altostrat Media case study. The media library in Cloud Storage keeps growing. A small set of new releases is accessed heavily for a few weeks, after which most objects are rarely or never read, but some older documentaries unexpectedly become popular again. Altostrat wants to optimize storage cost while keeping high availability, without having to predict access patterns. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Move every object older than 30 days to Archive storage with a lifecycle rule","B":"Copy the library to Persistent Disk snapshots, which are cheaper than object storage","C":"Enable Autoclass on the buckets so objects move between storage classes based on actual access, and use a dual-region or multi-region bucket for availability","D":"Keep everything in Standard storage and delete objects that have not been read in 90 days"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Autoclass moves each object to a colder class when it is not accessed and back to Standard when it is read again, with no retrieval fees or early-deletion charges for those transitions. That fits an access pattern you cannot predict (old documentaries that suddenly trend). A dual-region or multi-region location gives the required availability.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a fixed age rule sends trending old content to Archive, where reads cost retrieval fees and objects have a 365-day minimum storage duration. (B) snapshots are for disks, not a media library served to users. (D) deleting content destroys the catalogue, which is the business asset.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"optimize storage cost\\" + \\"unpredictable / changing access patterns\\" → Autoclass; predictable age-based patterns → lifecycle rules."},{"id":"3","question":"Altostrat Media case study. Altostrat wants to automatically generate concise summaries and extract rich metadata (topics, people, scenes, spoken content) from thousands of podcasts and videos already stored in Cloud Storage, with as little custom ML work as possible. Which approach fits best?","options":{"A":"Train a custom video-captioning model from scratch on GPUs in GKE","B":"Trigger a Cloud Run function (or a batch job) on new objects that sends the media to a multimodal Gemini model with a prompt that returns a summary and structured JSON metadata, and store the results in BigQuery","C":"Use Cloud Translation to translate the audio files into text summaries","D":"Export the videos to BigQuery as BYTES and summarize them with a SQL GROUP BY"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gemini models are multimodal: they accept audio and video directly from Cloud Storage and can return both a summary and structured output (JSON schema) in one call. Altostrat already uses Cloud Run functions for event-driven tasks such as metadata extraction, so wiring a function or a batch job to new objects reuses what they have. Landing the metadata in BigQuery feeds the \\"analyze trends\\" and \\"inform content strategy\\" requirements.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) training from scratch is the opposite of \\"minimal custom ML\\" and is slow and expensive. (C) Translation translates text between languages; it does not summarize audio or video. (D) BigQuery is not a media analysis engine; SQL cannot summarize raw video bytes.\\n\\nExam cue: summarize / extract metadata from audio-video with little ML effort → multimodal foundation model (Gemini) via the API, not custom training."},{"id":"4","question":"Altostrat Media case study. Altostrat is launching a 24/7 conversational assistant built on an LLM. Requirements include detecting and filtering inappropriate content and protecting the assistant from users who try prompt injection or try to make it leak sensitive data. Which control is designed for exactly this?","options":{"A":"A Cloud Armor security policy with OWASP rules in front of the load balancer","B":"VPC Service Controls around the project that hosts the model endpoint","C":"Binary Authorization on the GKE cluster that runs the chatbot frontend","D":"Model Armor templates that screen prompts and responses for prompt injection/jailbreak, sensitive data and harmful content, combined with the model\'s safety filters"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Model Armor sits between the application and the LLM and screens both prompts and responses: prompt-injection and jailbreak detection, sensitive data (via Sensitive Data Protection), malicious URLs and responsible-AI categories (hate, harassment, sexually explicit, dangerous content). Combined with Gemini\'s built-in safety filters it covers \\"detect and filter inappropriate content\\" for a conversational AI.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) Cloud Armor protects HTTP traffic (WAF, DDoS) but does not understand the semantics of a prompt. (B) VPC Service Controls prevent data exfiltration through Google APIs at the perimeter level, not malicious prompts. (C) Binary Authorization verifies container images at deploy time.\\n\\nExam cue: prompt injection, jailbreak, LLM leaking data, harmful generated content → Model Armor."},{"id":"5","question":"Altostrat Media case study. Altostrat currently monitors with a mix of Cloud Monitoring and self-managed Prometheus, and alerts arrive by email, where they are easy to miss. They want more reliable operations across Google Cloud and on-premises without rewriting their existing PromQL dashboards and rules. What should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Replace Prometheus with a custom logging agent that writes metrics to BigQuery and query them daily","B":"Adopt Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus for the GKE and on-premises clusters, keep PromQL queries in Cloud Monitoring, and route alerting policies to on-call channels such as PagerDuty or Pub/Sub instead of email","C":"Keep the current setup and increase the number of email recipients for each alert","D":"Move all metrics to Cloud Logging as log entries and create log-based alerts for every metric"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Managed Service for Prometheus collects Prometheus metrics from GKE and from clusters outside Google Cloud into Monarch, the same backend as Cloud Monitoring, and supports PromQL, so existing dashboards and alert rules keep working without operating Prometheus storage. Alerting policies can notify PagerDuty, Slack, webhooks or Pub/Sub, which solves the \\"ignored email\\" problem.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) batch queries in BigQuery are not real-time alerting. (C) more email recipients make alert fatigue worse. (D) turning every metric into log entries is expensive and loses metric semantics.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"we already use Prometheus\\" + \\"managed\\" / \\"hybrid\\" → Managed Service for Prometheus; \\"alerts by email are ignored\\" → proper notification channels and on-call routing."},{"id":"6","question":"Altostrat Media case study. Content ingestion still runs on-premises and pushes large video files to Google Cloud every day. The technical requirements ask for secure, high-performance hybrid connectivity for data ingestion. Altostrat\'s data center is in a colocation facility where Google has a point of presence. What should you choose?","options":{"A":"A single Classic VPN tunnel over the public internet","B":"Upload files through the Cloud Console in the browser","C":"Dedicated Interconnect with redundant connections (in two edge availability domains) for bandwidth and an SLA, with MACsec or HA VPN over Interconnect if encryption in transit is required","D":"Direct Peering, because it gives private RFC 1918 access to the VPC"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Being in a colocation facility with a Google point of presence makes Dedicated Interconnect possible: 10 or 100 Gbps physical links, traffic that does not traverse the public internet, and an SLA when you deploy redundant connections across edge availability domains. Interconnect is not encrypted by default; MACsec (link layer) or HA VPN over Cloud Interconnect add encryption when the security requirement demands it.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a single Classic VPN tunnel has limited throughput and no high availability. (B) manual browser uploads are neither automated nor high-performance. (D) Direct Peering reaches Google\'s public services (Workspace, public APIs); it does not give private access to VPC resources.\\n\\nExam cue: high bandwidth + colocated with Google → Dedicated Interconnect; not colocated → Partner Interconnect; low bandwidth / quick start → HA VPN."},{"id":"7","question":"Cymbal Retail case study. Cymbal\'s web application lets customers browse the catalog by querying relational databases for product names and categories. The business wants customers to describe what they need in natural language (\\"waterproof hiking boots for wide feet under $120\\") and get highly relevant, personalized results, without Cymbal building its own ranking models. What should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Add SQL LIKE \'%term%\' filters on every text column of the MySQL catalog tables","B":"Deploy a self-managed Elasticsearch cluster on Compute Engine and tune synonyms by hand","C":"Train a custom recommendation model in BigQuery ML with matrix factorization and expose it as the search box","D":"Use Vertex AI Search for commerce (Discovery AI, now Agent Platform Search for commerce): ingest the product catalog and user events, and use its semantic search, browse and recommendations APIs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The case explicitly names Google Cloud\'s Discovery AI. Vertex AI Search for commerce (formerly Retail API / Vertex AI Search for retail, shown in the 2026 docs as Agent Platform Search for commerce) is a managed product: you import the catalog and stream user events (views, add-to-cart, purchases) and it provides semantic, personalized search, browse and recommendations ranked by Google\'s models. That covers \\"improve product discoverability\\" and \\"automate product discovery\\" without building ranking models.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) LIKE filters are keyword matching; they do not understand intent such as \\"under $120\\" or \\"wide feet\\". (B) self-managed search means more operations and manual relevance tuning — the opposite of the cost-reduction goal. (C) matrix factorization recommends items from interaction history; it is not a natural-language search engine.\\n\\nExam cue: retail + \\"natural language product search\\" + \\"Discovery AI\\" → Vertex AI Search for commerce."},{"id":"8","question":"Cymbal Retail case study. Gemini will generate product attributes and descriptions from supplier data. The technical requirements demand a human-in-the-loop (HITL) step where associates can approve, reject or modify each suggestion before it reaches the product catalog. How should you design the flow?","options":{"A":"Write the generated suggestions to a staging store with a status field (pending/approved/rejected), expose them in a review UI for associates, and only publish approved or edited records to the live catalog","B":"Write Gemini\'s output directly to the live catalog and let customers report errors through a feedback button","C":"Lower the model temperature to 0 so the output is always correct and no review is needed","D":"Ask Gemini to review its own output a second time and publish whatever it approves"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HITL means a person decides before the content goes live. The standard pattern is a staging area (for example a Firestore or Cloud SQL table, or a BigQuery table) where each generated record has a review status, a UI for associates (a small Cloud Run app) and a publish step that only promotes approved or edited records into the catalog. Keeping the original supplier data, the generated suggestion and the human decision side by side also gives an audit trail and data to improve the prompts later.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) publishing first and correcting later is exactly what \\"before updating the product catalog\\" forbids. (C) temperature 0 makes the output more deterministic, not correct; hallucinated attributes are still possible. (D) a model grading itself is a useful extra check (LLM-as-a-judge) but it is not a human in the loop.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"approve, reject or modify before …\\" → staging + review UI + publish only approved."},{"id":"9","question":"Cymbal Retail case study. Cymbal needs to generate product image variations from a base photo: the same item in different colors, a different background, and text overlays for promotions. Which Google Cloud capability matches this requirement most directly?","options":{"A":"Cloud Vision API label detection","B":"Document AI form parser","C":"Imagen image editing on Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (mask-based inpainting/outpainting, background replacement and product image editing), with the results reviewed through the HITL flow","D":"AutoML image classification trained on the product photos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Imagen is the image-generation model family on Google\'s AI platform, and its editing capabilities work from a base image: mask-based editing (inpainting to change a region such as the product color, outpainting to extend the canvas), background replacement for product shots and adding visual elements. Generated images still go through the human-in-the-loop review the case requires.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) Vision API analyzes images (labels, objects, OCR); it does not create new ones. (B) Document AI extracts data from documents. (D) image classification assigns a label to an image; it generates nothing.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"generate / edit images\\", \\"background changes\\", \\"variations\\" → Imagen; \\"detect / classify / read text in images\\" → Vision API or AutoML."},{"id":"10","question":"Cymbal Retail case study. To reduce call-center staffing costs, Cymbal wants its IVR and website to handle routine requests (order status, returns, product questions grounded in the catalog) with an AI virtual agent, and to hand off to a human agent with full context when needed. Which approach fits best?","options":{"A":"Record all calls to Cloud Storage and have agents listen to them at the end of the day","B":"Build the virtual agent with Conversational Agents (Dialogflow CX) / Customer Engagement Suite, using generative playbooks grounded on the catalog data stores and tools that call the order APIs, and configure live-agent handoff with conversation context","C":"Replace the IVR with a static FAQ web page","D":"Fine-tune a Gemini model on past call transcripts and expose the raw model endpoint directly to callers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Conversational Agents (the evolution of Dialogflow CX, part of Google\'s Customer Engagement Suite / Contact Center AI) are built for this: voice and chat channels, generative playbooks grounded on data stores (the product catalog, return policies), tools/webhooks that call backend APIs such as order status, and a handoff to a live agent that passes the conversation context so the customer does not repeat themselves. That directly reduces calls that reach human agents.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) recording calls does not automate anything. (C) a static FAQ does not handle transactions or voice. (D) a raw fine-tuned model has no conversation management, no grounding on live order data, no telephony integration and no handoff; fine-tuning is also unnecessary when grounding solves the knowledge problem.\\n\\nExam cue: IVR / contact center + virtual agent + handoff to humans → Conversational Agents (Dialogflow CX) / Customer Engagement Suite."},{"id":"11","question":"Cymbal Retail case study. Cymbal wants to reduce data-center hosting costs by moving its MySQL and Microsoft SQL Server catalog and customer databases to Google Cloud with minimal downtime and as little operational effort as possible after the move. What should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Install MySQL and SQL Server on Compute Engine VMs and copy the data with a nightly mysqldump/backup restore","B":"Export all tables to CSV and load them into Bigtable","C":"Rewrite the applications to use Firestore before migrating any data","D":"Use Database Migration Service to replicate continuously from the source databases into Cloud SQL for MySQL and Cloud SQL for SQL Server, then cut over with a short downtime window"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Database Migration Service supports homogeneous migrations to Cloud SQL for MySQL and Cloud SQL for SQL Server (and to AlloyDB/Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL). It performs an initial load and then continuous replication (CDC), so the cutover only needs a short window. Cloud SQL is managed — backups, patches, high availability and replicas — which delivers the \\"less operational effort\\" and \\"reduce hosting costs\\" goals. Redis maps naturally to Memorystore for Redis.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) VMs keep all the administration (OS, patches, backups) and nightly dumps mean long downtime or data loss at cutover. (B) Bigtable is a wide-column NoSQL store; it does not serve relational queries for these applications. (C) rewriting every application before moving is the slowest and riskiest path.\\n\\nExam cue: lift-and-shift of MySQL/SQL Server/PostgreSQL with minimal downtime → Database Migration Service + Cloud SQL (or AlloyDB for PostgreSQL)."},{"id":"12","question":"Cymbal Retail case study. Customer interactions with the virtual agents may contain personal data (names, addresses, card numbers). The technical requirements say all customer data must be handled securely and comply with industry regulations, and the conversation logs will be analyzed in BigQuery. What should you do first to protect the logs used for analytics?","options":{"A":"Run Sensitive Data Protection (formerly Cloud DLP) to inspect and de-identify (mask or tokenize) PII in transcripts before they land in BigQuery, and restrict access to the raw data with IAM","B":"Make the BigQuery dataset public so the analytics team can access it from any tool","C":"Rely on the fact that Google encrypts data at rest by default, so no further action is needed","D":"Ask the virtual agent to tell customers not to share personal data"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sensitive Data Protection (Cloud DLP) detects more than 150 infoTypes (credit card numbers, names, addresses…) and de-identifies them through masking, redaction, or tokenization (format-preserving or deterministic encryption) before the data reaches the analytics store, so analysts work with useful but non-identifying data. IAM (and, if needed, column-level security or policy tags in BigQuery) limits who can read the raw transcripts. For card data, PCI DSS requires minimizing where cardholder data is stored.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) a public dataset is the opposite of compliance. (C) encryption at rest protects the disks, not the content from people who have read access. (D) you cannot rely on customers\' behavior to protect data.\\n\\nExam cue: PII in text/logs + analytics → Sensitive Data Protection de-identification before storage; access control on top."},{"id":"13","question":"EHR Healthcare case study. EHR manages users in Microsoft Active Directory and wants employees to sign in to Google Cloud with their existing corporate accounts, without maintaining a second set of passwords, while AD remains the source of truth. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Create every user manually in the Google Cloud console and email them a temporary password","B":"Use Google Cloud Directory Sync to provision users and groups from AD into Cloud Identity, and configure SAML single sign-on against AD FS (or Entra ID) so authentication stays with the corporate IdP","C":"Share one Google account per team and store its password in a password manager","D":"Grant roles directly to the AD domain name in IAM, which Google Cloud reads automatically"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The reference pattern for AD is two pieces: Google Cloud Directory Sync (GCDS) runs on-premises and one-way synchronizes users and groups from AD/LDAP into Cloud Identity, so AD stays the source of truth; SAML SSO federates authentication to AD FS or Microsoft Entra ID, so passwords never live in Google. IAM roles are then granted to synchronized groups. (Workforce Identity Federation is an alternative when you do not want to sync identities at all.)\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) manual accounts and second passwords are exactly what the requirement avoids and do not scale. (C) shared accounts break auditing and least privilege. (D) IAM has no way to read an AD domain directly; identities must exist in Cloud Identity or be federated.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"users are managed in Active Directory\\" → GCDS + SAML SSO (or Workforce Identity Federation)."},{"id":"14","question":"EHR Healthcare case study. Customer-facing containerized applications must provide at least 99.9% availability and lower latency for multinational medical offices and hospitals, and must absorb traffic spikes that previously caused outages. Which design best meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"One zonal GKE cluster in a single region with a fixed number of nodes sized for peak traffic","B":"Compute Engine VMs in one zone behind a regional internal passthrough Network Load Balancer","C":"A single Cloud SQL instance shared by all regions and accessed over the public internet with no load balancer","D":"Regional GKE clusters in several regions close to customers, with the cluster autoscaler and Horizontal Pod Autoscaler, exposed through a global external Application Load Balancer (multi-cluster Gateway) with Cloud CDN for static content"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Regional GKE clusters spread control plane and nodes across zones, so a zone failure does not take the application down. Deploying them in several regions and placing a global external Application Load Balancer in front (for example with multi-cluster Gateway) sends each user to the nearest healthy backend over a single anycast IP, which reduces latency and fails over between regions. The cluster autoscaler and HPA absorb spikes — the case says outages came from \\"inadequate capacity to manage spikes in traffic\\".\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a zonal cluster is a single point of failure and a fixed size cannot absorb spikes economically. (B) a single zone fails the availability target, and an internal load balancer is not reachable by external customers. (C) a single database endpoint with no load balancing addresses neither availability nor latency.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"99.9%+\\", \\"global customers\\", \\"reduce latency\\" → multi-region (or at least regional) backends behind a global external Application Load Balancer, plus autoscaling."},{"id":"15","question":"EHR Healthcare case study. The technical requirements ask for consistent logging, log retention, monitoring and alerting across all environments, and the business must maintain regulatory compliance. Today each team uses different open-source tools and alerts go to email. What should you implement?","options":{"A":"An aggregated log sink at the organization (or folder) level that routes logs from every project to a central log bucket with a retention policy (locked if regulations require it), plus Cloud Monitoring dashboards and alerting policies with on-call notification channels","B":"Let each project keep its default _Default bucket with 30-day retention and let teams check logs when something breaks","C":"Disable Data Access audit logs to reduce volume and cost","D":"Copy logs weekly to each developer\'s laptop for long-term retention"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An aggregated sink at the organization or folder level captures logs from all current and future child projects and routes them to a central destination — a log bucket with custom retention (and a bucket lock when retention must be immutable), and optionally BigQuery for analysis or Cloud Storage for cheap archival. That gives consistency by design rather than per-team configuration. Cloud Monitoring alerting policies with PagerDuty, Slack or Pub/Sub channels replace ignored email alerts and provide the \\"centralized visibility and proactive action\\" requirement.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) the _Default bucket keeps logs for 30 days and leaves each project inconsistent. (C) Data Access audit logs are precisely what healthcare auditors ask for (who read which record). (D) laptops are neither secure nor compliant storage.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"consistent logging across projects\\" + \\"retention\\" → organization-level aggregated sink to a log bucket with retention (locked if required)."},{"id":"16","question":"EHR Healthcare case study. EHR wants to onboard new insurance providers as quickly as possible and create interfaces to ingest and process data from them, while the legacy on-premises integrations must keep working until they are retired. Which approach best supports this?","options":{"A":"Give every insurance provider a VPN connection directly into the production VPC and let them write to the databases","B":"Build a new point-to-point integration with custom code for each provider and host it on unmanaged VMs","C":"Publish standardized provider APIs through Apigee (API keys/OAuth, quotas, analytics, a developer portal for self-service onboarding), backed by services on Google Cloud, and keep reaching the legacy on-premises integrations over the Interconnect/VPN hybrid link","D":"Ask providers to email CSV files to a shared mailbox that an operator uploads daily"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Apigee is Google Cloud\'s API management platform: you expose one well-defined set of APIs with security (API keys, OAuth), quotas and rate limits, analytics and a developer portal where new partners can register and get credentials themselves, which shortens onboarding. Backends can live in Google Cloud while still calling the on-premises legacy systems over the private hybrid connection, satisfying \\"maintain legacy interfaces\\". File-based providers can drop files into Cloud Storage, with Pub/Sub notifications triggering processing.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) giving partners network access into production is a serious security and compliance risk. (B) one custom integration per provider is the slow, costly pattern that limits onboarding speed. (D) email and manual uploads are slow, error-prone and not auditable for health data.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"onboard partners quickly\\", \\"expose interfaces to third parties\\" → Apigee (API management + developer portal)."},{"id":"17","question":"EHR Healthcare case study. EHR stores protected health information (PHI) and must maintain regulatory compliance (HIPAA in the US) after moving to Google Cloud. Which combination is the most appropriate starting point?","options":{"A":"Sign a Business Associate Agreement (BAA) with Google, keep PHI only in services covered by the BAA, and add controls such as VPC Service Controls perimeters, CMEK where required, least-privilege IAM and Data Access audit logs","B":"Nothing is needed: every Google Cloud service is automatically HIPAA-compliant for any configuration","C":"Store PHI in a public Cloud Storage bucket protected by an obscure bucket name","D":"Encrypt PHI with a key hard-coded in the application source so only developers can read it"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HIPAA follows a shared-responsibility model. Google signs a BAA and lists which products are covered; the customer must keep PHI in those covered products and configure them correctly. Typical controls: VPC Service Controls to reduce exfiltration risk around services such as BigQuery, Cloud Storage and the Cloud Healthcare API; customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) in Cloud KMS when policy requires key control; least-privilege IAM; and Data Access audit logs to know who read which record. Assured Workloads can add data-residency and personnel controls for other regulatory regimes.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) a BAA does not make any configuration compliant; the customer still owns its part. (C) public buckets expose PHI; obscurity is not security. (D) hard-coded keys are a well-known anti-pattern; keys belong in Cloud KMS or Secret Manager.\\n\\nExam cue: healthcare / PHI / HIPAA → BAA + covered services + VPC SC + CMEK + audit logs; the Cloud Healthcare API (FHIR, HL7v2, DICOM) for clinical data."},{"id":"18","question":"EHR Healthcare case study. The business wants to increase its ability to provide insights into healthcare trends and to make predictions and generate reports on industry trends based on provider data. The data team knows SQL well but has little ML experience. What should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Run the analytics directly against the production MySQL databases during business hours","B":"Centralize de-identified provider data in BigQuery, build forecasting and classification models with BigQuery ML (for example ARIMA_PLUS or AI.FORECAST for trends), and publish reports with Looker","C":"Hire a team to write custom TensorFlow models before any data is centralized","D":"Export all tables to spreadsheets and email them to analysts every month"},"correct":"B","explanation":"BigQuery is the analytics warehouse that scales to all provider data without affecting the transactional systems, and BigQuery ML lets a SQL-skilled team train and use models with CREATE MODEL / ML.FORECAST / ML.PREDICT — ARIMA_PLUS for time-series trends, or the AI.FORECAST function (TimesFM) without training at all. Looker (or Looker Studio) turns the results into governed reports. Data should be de-identified (Sensitive Data Protection) before broad analytical use because it is health data.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) heavy analytics on production OLTP databases hurts customer-facing availability. (C) custom TensorFlow is slow to deliver and ignores the team\'s SQL skills. (D) spreadsheets by email are neither scalable nor compliant.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"insights / predictions / reports\\" + \\"SQL skills\\" → BigQuery + BigQuery ML + Looker."},{"id":"19","question":"KnightMotives Automotive case study. Adherence to EU data protection regulations is critical, especially for the emerging autonomous platforms, and security is a paramount concern after past breaches. The company wants to guarantee that data for EU drivers is stored and processed only in EU regions and that this cannot be bypassed by an individual project. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Write a wiki page asking developers to always choose europe-west regions","B":"Use a multi-region US bucket and encrypt it with Google-managed keys","C":"Place the EU workloads in a folder governed by Assured Workloads (EU data boundary controls) and enforce the resource locations organization policy (gcp.resourceLocations) restricted to EU locations, combined with CMEK for key control","D":"Rely on IAM roles alone, since only EU employees will have access"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Data residency must be enforced by the platform, not by convention. The organization policy constraint gcp.resourceLocations prevents creating resources outside allowed locations for everything under the folder or organization. Assured Workloads packages those controls for regulatory regimes (for example the EU data boundary / sovereign controls): it configures location restrictions, restricts services to those that support the regime and can add key-management and personnel controls. CMEK in Cloud KMS (possibly with an external key manager) gives the company control of the keys.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a wiki page is not an enforceable control. (B) a US multi-region violates the residency requirement regardless of encryption. (D) IAM controls who accesses data, not where it is stored or processed.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"data must stay in the EU\\", \\"sovereignty\\", \\"cannot be bypassed\\" → organization policy resource locations + Assured Workloads."},{"id":"20","question":"KnightMotives Automotive case study. Connected vehicles will send telemetry for real-time AI features and analytics, but vehicle connectivity in rural areas is unreliable. The ingestion layer must absorb millions of devices, tolerate vehicles that reconnect after hours offline and feed both streaming analytics and the data warehouse. Which design is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"Each vehicle opens a direct connection to a Cloud SQL instance and inserts rows synchronously","B":"Buffer events on the vehicle and publish them to Pub/Sub when connectivity is available; process the stream with Dataflow using event-time windows and allowed lateness, and write to BigQuery (and Bigtable for low-latency per-vehicle lookups)","C":"Upload one CSV file per vehicle per month to Cloud Storage over a USB key at the dealer","D":"Send telemetry by email to a monitored mailbox and parse it with a cron job"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pub/Sub is the global, autoscaling ingestion buffer for millions of publishers; the vehicle keeps events locally while offline and publishes when it reconnects. Dataflow (Apache Beam) handles exactly this kind of late, out-of-order data with event-time windows, watermarks and allowed lateness, so analytics stay correct when a car uploads several hours later. BigQuery serves analytics and the data-monetization platform; Bigtable serves low-latency per-vehicle reads (latest state, time series by vehicle key).\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) millions of synchronous connections to a relational database do not scale and fail with intermittent links. (C) monthly batches cannot support real-time AI features. (D) email is not a telemetry transport.\\n\\nExam cue: IoT / telemetry + \\"intermittent connectivity\\" + \\"real time\\" → Pub/Sub + Dataflow (event time, late data) + BigQuery / Bigtable."},{"id":"21","question":"KnightMotives Automotive case study. KnightMotives wants to monetize corporate data (for example aggregated driving and road-condition data) by sharing it with partners such as insurers and cities, under strict security and privacy measures. Today the data is siloed. Which approach best meets these goals?","options":{"A":"Export full tables as CSV files and send them to each partner over SFTP every week","B":"Give partners IAM viewer roles on the production project that holds raw vehicle data","C":"Copy the raw data into a separate BigQuery project per partner and let each partner query everything","D":"Consolidate curated, de-identified and aggregated datasets in BigQuery, and publish them as listings through BigQuery sharing (formerly Analytics Hub) — with data clean rooms where partners must join data without seeing raw rows — governed with Dataplex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"BigQuery sharing (formerly Analytics Hub) lets a publisher expose datasets as listings in exchanges; subscribers get a linked dataset that reads the publisher\'s data in place, without copies, while the publisher keeps control, can revoke access and sees usage. Data clean rooms add privacy controls (for example aggregation thresholds) so partners can run analysis joined with their own data without seeing row-level records. Dataplex (Knowledge Catalog) provides governance, lineage and data quality across the previously siloed data. De-identification and aggregation come before publishing.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) file copies lose control, go stale and multiply breach risk. (B) access to raw production data violates least privilege and privacy. (C) per-partner copies of raw data are expensive to keep in sync and expose too much.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"share / monetize data with external organizations\\" without copying → BigQuery sharing (Analytics Hub) and data clean rooms."},{"id":"22","question":"KnightMotives Automotive case study. Network connectivity between manufacturing plants and headquarters is a challenge, yet plants need low-latency AI (for example visual quality inspection on the assembly line) that keeps working if the WAN link drops. The company is adopting a hybrid cloud strategy. What should you recommend for the plants?","options":{"A":"Run the inference workloads on Google Distributed Cloud (connected) hardware at each plant, managed from Google Cloud, so models run locally and results sync to the cloud when the link is available","B":"Send every camera frame to a model endpoint in a single cloud region and wait for the response before moving the line","C":"Stop using AI on the assembly line until the network is upgraded","D":"Store all frames on a local NAS and analyze them manually at the end of each shift"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Google Distributed Cloud (connected) brings Google-managed infrastructure (Kubernetes clusters on hardware in the customer\'s site) to the edge, so AI inference runs next to the cameras with very low latency and keeps working during WAN outages, while management, updates and fleet configuration come from Google Cloud. Results and samples sync to the cloud for retraining when connectivity allows. This fits the hybrid strategy and the \\"network upgrades\\" constraint.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) a round trip to a remote region adds latency and stops the line whenever the link fails. (C) giving up the AI requirement is not an architecture. (D) manual end-of-shift review is not real-time inspection.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"low latency at a factory/store/edge\\" + \\"poor or intermittent connectivity\\" → Google Distributed Cloud (edge) with cloud-managed operations."},{"id":"23","question":"KnightMotives Automotive case study. After past data breaches, the technical requirements call for a comprehensive security framework against cyber threats and an incident response plan. The security team wants one place to see misconfigurations, vulnerabilities and active threats across all Google Cloud projects, and to prevent risky configurations from being created at all. What should you implement?","options":{"A":"A spreadsheet where each team lists its firewall rules once a quarter","B":"Cloud Armor rules only, since all threats arrive over HTTP","C":"Security Command Center (Premium/Enterprise) at the organization level for posture findings, vulnerabilities and threat detection, organization policy constraints as preventive guardrails (for example no external IPs, no service account key creation, uniform bucket-level access), and alert routing into the incident response process","D":"Turn off audit logs to reduce the attack surface"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Security Command Center activated at the organization level gives the central view: misconfiguration findings (Security Health Analytics), vulnerability findings, threat detection (Event Threat Detection, Container Threat Detection) and attack-path / risk analysis in higher tiers. Organization policy constraints are preventive: they stop risky resources from being created in the first place (for example constraints/compute.vmExternalIpAccess, iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation, storage.uniformBucketLevelAccess). Findings can be exported through Pub/Sub to the SIEM/SOAR (for example Google Security Operations) that executes the incident response plan.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a quarterly spreadsheet is neither continuous nor enforceable. (B) Cloud Armor covers web-facing attacks only; it does not see IAM misconfigurations, leaked keys or vulnerable images. (D) audit logs are essential for detection and forensics; disabling them hurts incident response.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"centralized security posture\\", \\"detect threats across the organization\\" → Security Command Center; \\"prevent\\" misconfigurations → organization policies."},{"id":"24","question":"KnightMotives Automotive case study. Autonomous-vehicle development requires training perception models on petabytes of camera and lidar data and running large simulation workloads. The current AI infrastructure is obsolete. The team needs to train much faster than on a single machine without managing its own GPU fleet long-term. What should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Train on a single large CPU-only VM, adding more RAM as the dataset grows","B":"Buy GPUs for each engineer\'s workstation and copy the dataset to every desk","C":"Use BigQuery ML linear regression on the raw lidar point clouds","D":"Keep the sensor data in Cloud Storage and run distributed training on GPUs or TPUs (data parallelism across many accelerators, model parallelism if the model does not fit in one device) with Agent Platform custom training or GKE, using Spot capacity for fault-tolerant simulation jobs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Large perception models need accelerators and distributed training. With data parallelism every accelerator holds a copy of the model and processes a different shard of each batch, scaling throughput across many GPUs/TPUs; model (or pipeline/tensor) parallelism splits a model that does not fit in a single device\'s memory. Managed custom training on Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI) or GKE with GPU/TPU node pools provides that capacity on demand, reading training data from Cloud Storage. Simulation jobs that tolerate interruption are good candidates for Spot VMs to cut cost.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a single CPU machine cannot train deep vision models on petabytes in useful time. (B) per-desk GPUs and dataset copies are the obsolete, unmanageable setup the case wants to leave, and a security risk. (C) BigQuery ML linear regression is not a model for raw point-cloud perception.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"train faster\\" + \\"large deep learning model\\" → GPUs/TPUs with distributed training; \\"model doesn\'t fit in memory\\" → model parallelism; \\"interruptible batch\\" → Spot VMs."}]},{"id":"gcp-pca-case-studies-es","title":"GCP Professional Cloud Architect — Casos de estudio (ES)","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"Caso de estudio Altostrat Media. Altostrat ejecuta Kubernetes en GKE y en sus centros de datos on-premises (ingesta y archivo de contenido). Sus requisitos t\xe9cnicos piden modernizar el CI/CD de despliegues en contenedores con una plataforma de gesti\xf3n centralizada, y entornos Kubernetes escalables on-premises y en la nube. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xeda recomendar el arquitecto?","options":{"A":"Registrar los cl\xfasteres de GKE y los on-premises en una flota de GKE Enterprise, gestionar configuraci\xf3n y pol\xedticas de forma central con Config Sync y Policy Controller, y publicar con Cloud Build + Cloud Deploy","B":"Mantener servidores Jenkins separados por entorno y replicar los manifiestos YAML entre ellos con un rsync nocturno","C":"Migrar todas las cargas on-premises a Cloud Run functions para no tener que gestionar Kubernetes","D":"Crear un cl\xfaster GKE Autopilot por equipo en la nube y dejar los cl\xfasteres on-premises gestionados a mano"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Las palabras clave son \xabplataforma de gesti\xf3n centralizada\xbb y \xabKubernetes on-premises y en la nube\xbb. Una flota (fleet) de GKE Enterprise registra cl\xfasteres de GKE y on-premises (GKE en bare metal / VMware o cl\xfasteres adjuntos) bajo una misma superficie de control: Config Sync aplica la misma configuraci\xf3n definida en Git a todos y Policy Controller impone las reglas. Cloud Build construye y prueba las im\xe1genes y Cloud Deploy promociona las releases entre entornos.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) es justo el modelo fragmentado y manual que el caso quiere abandonar. (C) la ingesta y el archivo son flujos largos y con estado on-premises; el caso pide Kubernetes on-premises, no reescribirlo todo a funciones. (D) resuelve solo la mitad cloud y mantiene dos modelos de operaci\xf3n.\\n\\nPista de examen: h\xedbrido + \xabgesti\xf3n centralizada\xbb + contenedores → flota de GKE Enterprise (antes Anthos)."},{"id":"2","question":"Caso de estudio Altostrat Media. La biblioteca de medios en Cloud Storage no para de crecer. Un peque\xf1o grupo de estrenos se consulta mucho durante unas semanas y despu\xe9s la mayor\xeda de objetos casi no se leen, pero algunos documentales antiguos vuelven a ponerse de moda sin previo aviso. Altostrat quiere optimizar el coste de almacenamiento manteniendo alta disponibilidad y sin tener que predecir los patrones de acceso. \xbfQu\xe9 haces?","options":{"A":"Mover a Archive todo objeto con m\xe1s de 30 d\xedas mediante una regla de ciclo de vida","B":"Copiar la biblioteca a snapshots de Persistent Disk, que son m\xe1s baratos que el almacenamiento de objetos","C":"Activar Autoclass en los buckets para que los objetos cambien de clase seg\xfan el acceso real, y usar un bucket dual-region o multi-region para la disponibilidad","D":"Dejar todo en Standard y borrar los objetos que no se hayan le\xeddo en 90 d\xedas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Autoclass baja cada objeto a una clase m\xe1s fr\xeda cuando no se accede y lo devuelve a Standard cuando vuelve a leerse, sin cargos de recuperaci\xf3n ni de borrado anticipado por esas transiciones. Encaja con un patr\xf3n de acceso imposible de predecir (documentales antiguos que de repente se vuelven tendencia). Una ubicaci\xf3n dual-region o multi-region da la disponibilidad pedida.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) una regla fija por antig\xfcedad manda a Archive contenido que vuelve a leerse, con cargos de recuperaci\xf3n y 365 d\xedas de almacenamiento m\xednimo. (B) los snapshots son para discos, no para una biblioteca que se sirve a usuarios. (D) borrar contenido destruye el cat\xe1logo, que es el activo del negocio.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xaboptimizar coste de almacenamiento\xbb + \xabpatrones de acceso impredecibles\xbb → Autoclass; patrones predecibles por antig\xfcedad → reglas de ciclo de vida."},{"id":"3","question":"Caso de estudio Altostrat Media. Altostrat quiere generar autom\xe1ticamente res\xfamenes breves y extraer metadatos ricos (temas, personas, escenas, contenido hablado) de miles de podcasts y v\xeddeos que ya est\xe1n en Cloud Storage, con el m\xednimo trabajo de ML a medida. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque encaja mejor?","options":{"A":"Entrenar desde cero un modelo propio de subtitulado de v\xeddeo con GPUs en GKE","B":"Disparar una Cloud Run function (o un job por lotes) con cada objeto nuevo que env\xede el medio a un modelo Gemini multimodal con un prompt que devuelva el resumen y los metadatos en JSON estructurado, y guardar el resultado en BigQuery","C":"Usar Cloud Translation para traducir los audios a res\xfamenes de texto","D":"Exportar los v\xeddeos a BigQuery como BYTES y resumirlos con un GROUP BY en SQL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los modelos Gemini son multimodales: aceptan audio y v\xeddeo directamente desde Cloud Storage y pueden devolver en una sola llamada el resumen y una salida estructurada (esquema JSON). Altostrat ya usa Cloud Run functions para tareas por eventos como la extracci\xf3n de metadatos, as\xed que conectar una funci\xf3n o un job por lotes a los objetos nuevos reaprovecha lo que tienen. Dejar los metadatos en BigQuery alimenta los requisitos de \xabanalizar tendencias\xbb y \xaborientar la estrategia de contenidos\xbb.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) entrenar desde cero es lo contrario de \xabm\xednimo ML a medida\xbb, adem\xe1s de lento y caro. (C) Translation traduce texto entre idiomas; no resume audio ni v\xeddeo. (D) BigQuery no es un motor de an\xe1lisis de medios; SQL no resume bytes de v\xeddeo.\\n\\nPista de examen: resumir / extraer metadatos de audio-v\xeddeo con poco esfuerzo de ML → modelo fundacional multimodal (Gemini) v\xeda API, no entrenamiento propio."},{"id":"4","question":"Caso de estudio Altostrat Media. Altostrat lanza un asistente conversacional 24/7 basado en un LLM. Los requisitos incluyen detectar y filtrar contenido inapropiado y proteger al asistente de usuarios que intenten prompt injection o hacerle filtrar datos sensibles. \xbfQu\xe9 control est\xe1 pensado exactamente para esto?","options":{"A":"Una pol\xedtica de Cloud Armor con reglas OWASP delante del balanceador","B":"VPC Service Controls alrededor del proyecto que aloja el endpoint del modelo","C":"Binary Authorization en el cl\xfaster GKE que ejecuta el frontend del chatbot","D":"Plantillas de Model Armor que analizan prompts y respuestas en busca de prompt injection/jailbreak, datos sensibles y contenido da\xf1ino, junto con los filtros de seguridad del modelo"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Model Armor se coloca entre la aplicaci\xf3n y el LLM y analiza tanto los prompts como las respuestas: detecci\xf3n de prompt injection y jailbreak, datos sensibles (v\xeda Sensitive Data Protection), URLs maliciosas y categor\xedas de IA responsable (odio, acoso, contenido sexual expl\xedcito, contenido peligroso). Junto con los filtros de seguridad propios de Gemini cubre \xabdetectar y filtrar contenido inapropiado\xbb en una IA conversacional.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) Cloud Armor protege tr\xe1fico HTTP (WAF, DDoS) pero no entiende la sem\xe1ntica de un prompt. (B) VPC Service Controls impide la exfiltraci\xf3n de datos por las APIs de Google a nivel de per\xedmetro, no prompts maliciosos. (C) Binary Authorization verifica im\xe1genes de contenedor al desplegar.\\n\\nPista de examen: prompt injection, jailbreak, LLM que filtra datos, contenido generado da\xf1ino → Model Armor."},{"id":"5","question":"Caso de estudio Altostrat Media. Altostrat monitoriza hoy con una mezcla de Cloud Monitoring y Prometheus autogestionado, y las alertas llegan por correo, donde se pierden f\xe1cilmente. Quieren operaciones m\xe1s fiables en Google Cloud y on-premises sin reescribir sus dashboards y reglas en PromQL. \xbfQu\xe9 recomiendas?","options":{"A":"Sustituir Prometheus por un agente de logging propio que escriba las m\xe9tricas en BigQuery y consultarlas una vez al d\xeda","B":"Adoptar Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus en los cl\xfasteres GKE y on-premises, mantener las consultas PromQL en Cloud Monitoring y enviar las pol\xedticas de alertas a canales de guardia como PagerDuty o Pub/Sub en lugar de correo","C":"Mantener lo actual y aumentar el n\xfamero de destinatarios de cada alerta por correo","D":"Mover todas las m\xe9tricas a Cloud Logging como entradas de log y crear alertas basadas en logs para cada m\xe9trica"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Managed Service for Prometheus recoge m\xe9tricas Prometheus de GKE y de cl\xfasteres fuera de Google Cloud en Monarch, el mismo backend de Cloud Monitoring, y admite PromQL, as\xed que los dashboards y reglas actuales siguen funcionando sin operar el almacenamiento de Prometheus. Las pol\xedticas de alertas pueden notificar a PagerDuty, Slack, webhooks o Pub/Sub, lo que resuelve el problema del \xabcorreo ignorado\xbb.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) consultas por lotes en BigQuery no son alertas en tiempo real. (C) m\xe1s destinatarios de correo empeoran la fatiga de alertas. (D) convertir cada m\xe9trica en logs es caro y pierde la sem\xe1ntica de m\xe9trica.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabya usamos Prometheus\xbb + \xabgestionado\xbb / \xabh\xedbrido\xbb → Managed Service for Prometheus; \xablas alertas por correo se ignoran\xbb → canales de notificaci\xf3n y guardias de verdad."},{"id":"6","question":"Caso de estudio Altostrat Media. La ingesta de contenidos sigue on-premises y env\xeda cada d\xeda ficheros de v\xeddeo grandes a Google Cloud. Los requisitos t\xe9cnicos piden conectividad h\xedbrida segura y de alto rendimiento para la ingesta. El centro de datos de Altostrat est\xe1 en una colocation donde Google tiene punto de presencia. \xbfQu\xe9 eliges?","options":{"A":"Un \xfanico t\xfanel Classic VPN por internet p\xfablico","B":"Subir los ficheros por la consola de Cloud desde el navegador","C":"Dedicated Interconnect con conexiones redundantes (en dos edge availability domains) para ancho de banda y SLA, a\xf1adiendo MACsec o HA VPN sobre Interconnect si se exige cifrado en tr\xe1nsito","D":"Direct Peering, porque da acceso privado RFC 1918 a la VPC"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Estar en una colocation con punto de presencia de Google permite Dedicated Interconnect: enlaces f\xedsicos de 10 o 100 Gbps, tr\xe1fico que no pasa por internet p\xfablico y SLA cuando se despliegan conexiones redundantes en edge availability domains distintos. Interconnect no va cifrado por defecto; MACsec (capa de enlace) o HA VPN sobre Cloud Interconnect a\xf1aden cifrado si la seguridad lo exige.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) un solo t\xfanel Classic VPN tiene rendimiento limitado y ninguna alta disponibilidad. (B) subir a mano desde el navegador no es automatizado ni de alto rendimiento. (D) Direct Peering llega a los servicios p\xfablicos de Google (Workspace, APIs p\xfablicas); no da acceso privado a recursos de la VPC.\\n\\nPista de examen: mucho ancho de banda + colocalizado con Google → Dedicated Interconnect; sin colocation → Partner Interconnect; poco ancho de banda / arranque r\xe1pido → HA VPN."},{"id":"7","question":"Caso de estudio Cymbal Retail. La web de Cymbal permite navegar el cat\xe1logo consultando bases de datos relacionales por nombre y categor\xeda de producto. El negocio quiere que los clientes describan lo que necesitan en lenguaje natural (\xabbotas de monta\xf1a impermeables para pie ancho por menos de 120 $\xbb) y reciban resultados muy relevantes y personalizados, sin que Cymbal construya sus propios modelos de ranking. \xbfQu\xe9 recomiendas?","options":{"A":"A\xf1adir filtros SQL LIKE \'%t\xe9rmino%\' en todas las columnas de texto de las tablas MySQL del cat\xe1logo","B":"Desplegar un cl\xfaster Elasticsearch autogestionado en Compute Engine y ajustar los sin\xf3nimos a mano","C":"Entrenar un modelo de recomendaci\xf3n propio en BigQuery ML con matrix factorization y exponerlo como buscador","D":"Usar Vertex AI Search for commerce (Discovery AI, ahora Agent Platform Search for commerce): ingerir el cat\xe1logo y los eventos de usuario y usar sus APIs de b\xfasqueda sem\xe1ntica, navegaci\xf3n y recomendaciones"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El caso nombra expl\xedcitamente Discovery AI de Google Cloud. Vertex AI Search for commerce (antes Retail API / Vertex AI Search for retail; en la documentaci\xf3n de 2026 aparece como Agent Platform Search for commerce) es un producto gestionado: importas el cat\xe1logo y env\xedas eventos de usuario (vistas, a\xf1adir al carrito, compras) y ofrece b\xfasqueda sem\xe1ntica y personalizada, navegaci\xf3n y recomendaciones ordenadas por los modelos de Google. Cubre \xabmejorar la descubribilidad\xbb y \xabautomatizar el descubrimiento de producto\xbb sin construir modelos de ranking.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) LIKE es coincidencia de palabras; no entiende intenciones como \xabmenos de 120 $\xbb o \xabpie ancho\xbb. (B) un buscador autogestionado implica m\xe1s operaci\xf3n y ajuste manual de relevancia, lo contrario del objetivo de reducir costes. (C) matrix factorization recomienda a partir del historial de interacciones; no es un buscador en lenguaje natural.\\n\\nPista de examen: retail + \xabb\xfasqueda de productos en lenguaje natural\xbb + \xabDiscovery AI\xbb → Vertex AI Search for commerce."},{"id":"8","question":"Caso de estudio Cymbal Retail. Gemini generar\xe1 atributos y descripciones de producto a partir de los datos del proveedor. Los requisitos t\xe9cnicos exigen un paso human-in-the-loop (HITL) en el que los empleados puedan aprobar, rechazar o modificar cada sugerencia antes de que llegue al cat\xe1logo. \xbfC\xf3mo dise\xf1as el flujo?","options":{"A":"Escribir las sugerencias generadas en un almac\xe9n intermedio con un campo de estado (pendiente/aprobada/rechazada), mostrarlas en una UI de revisi\xf3n para los empleados y publicar en el cat\xe1logo solo los registros aprobados o editados","B":"Escribir la salida de Gemini directamente en el cat\xe1logo y que los clientes reporten errores con un bot\xf3n de feedback","C":"Bajar la temperatura del modelo a 0 para que la salida sea siempre correcta y no haga falta revisi\xf3n","D":"Pedir a Gemini que revise su propia salida una segunda vez y publicar lo que apruebe"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HITL significa que una persona decide antes de que el contenido se publique. El patr\xf3n habitual es un \xe1rea intermedia (por ejemplo, una tabla en Firestore, Cloud SQL o BigQuery) donde cada registro generado tiene un estado de revisi\xf3n, una UI para los empleados (una peque\xf1a app en Cloud Run) y un paso de publicaci\xf3n que solo promueve al cat\xe1logo lo aprobado o editado. Guardar juntos el dato original del proveedor, la sugerencia y la decisi\xf3n humana da adem\xe1s trazabilidad y datos para mejorar los prompts.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) publicar primero y corregir despu\xe9s es justo lo que proh\xedbe \xabantes de actualizar el cat\xe1logo\xbb. (C) temperatura 0 hace la salida m\xe1s determinista, no correcta; puede seguir inventando atributos. (D) que el modelo se eval\xfae a s\xed mismo es un control extra \xfatil (LLM-as-a-judge) pero no es un humano en el bucle.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabaprobar, rechazar o modificar antes de…\xbb → staging + UI de revisi\xf3n + publicar solo lo aprobado."},{"id":"9","question":"Caso de estudio Cymbal Retail. Cymbal necesita generar variantes de imagen de producto a partir de una foto base: el mismo art\xedculo en distintos colores, otro fondo y textos superpuestos para promociones. \xbfQu\xe9 capacidad de Google Cloud encaja de forma m\xe1s directa con este requisito?","options":{"A":"Detecci\xf3n de etiquetas de Cloud Vision API","B":"El form parser de Document AI","C":"La edici\xf3n de im\xe1genes de Imagen en Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (edici\xf3n con m\xe1scara inpainting/outpainting, cambio de fondo y edici\xf3n de imagen de producto), revisando el resultado con el flujo HITL","D":"Clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes con AutoML entrenada con las fotos de producto"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Imagen es la familia de modelos de generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes de la plataforma de IA de Google, y sus capacidades de edici\xf3n parten de una imagen base: edici\xf3n con m\xe1scara (inpainting para cambiar una zona, como el color del producto; outpainting para ampliar el lienzo), sustituci\xf3n de fondo para fotos de producto y a\xf1adir elementos visuales. Las im\xe1genes generadas pasan igualmente por la revisi\xf3n human-in-the-loop que exige el caso.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) Vision API analiza im\xe1genes (etiquetas, objetos, OCR); no crea nuevas. (B) Document AI extrae datos de documentos. (D) la clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes asigna una etiqueta; no genera nada.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabgenerar / editar im\xe1genes\xbb, \xabcambiar fondo\xbb, \xabvariaciones\xbb → Imagen; \xabdetectar / clasificar / leer texto en im\xe1genes\xbb → Vision API o AutoML."},{"id":"10","question":"Caso de estudio Cymbal Retail. Para reducir costes de personal en el call center, Cymbal quiere que su IVR y su web resuelvan peticiones rutinarias (estado del pedido, devoluciones, dudas de producto basadas en el cat\xe1logo) con un agente virtual de IA, y que se pase a un agente humano con todo el contexto cuando haga falta. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque encaja mejor?","options":{"A":"Grabar todas las llamadas en Cloud Storage y que los agentes las escuchen al final del d\xeda","B":"Construir el agente virtual con Conversational Agents (Dialogflow CX) / Customer Engagement Suite, con playbooks generativos anclados en data stores del cat\xe1logo y herramientas que llamen a las APIs de pedidos, y configurar el traspaso a agente humano con el contexto de la conversaci\xf3n","C":"Sustituir el IVR por una p\xe1gina web est\xe1tica de preguntas frecuentes","D":"Hacer fine-tuning de un modelo Gemini con transcripciones de llamadas antiguas y exponer el endpoint del modelo directamente a quien llama"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Conversational Agents (la evoluci\xf3n de Dialogflow CX, dentro de Customer Engagement Suite / Contact Center AI) est\xe1 hecho para esto: canales de voz y chat, playbooks generativos anclados en data stores (cat\xe1logo, pol\xedticas de devoluci\xf3n), herramientas/webhooks que llaman a APIs del backend como el estado del pedido, y traspaso a un agente humano que pasa el contexto para que el cliente no tenga que repetirse. Eso reduce directamente las llamadas que llegan a personas.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) grabar llamadas no automatiza nada. (C) unas FAQ est\xe1ticas no gestionan transacciones ni voz. (D) un modelo con fine-tuning a pelo no tiene gesti\xf3n de la conversaci\xf3n, ni grounding sobre datos de pedidos en vivo, ni integraci\xf3n telef\xf3nica, ni traspaso; adem\xe1s el fine-tuning sobra cuando el grounding resuelve el conocimiento.\\n\\nPista de examen: IVR / contact center + agente virtual + traspaso a humano → Conversational Agents (Dialogflow CX) / Customer Engagement Suite."},{"id":"11","question":"Caso de estudio Cymbal Retail. Cymbal quiere reducir costes de hosting de centro de datos llevando a Google Cloud sus bases de datos MySQL y Microsoft SQL Server de cat\xe1logo y clientes, con el m\xednimo tiempo de inactividad y el m\xednimo esfuerzo operativo despu\xe9s de la migraci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 recomiendas?","options":{"A":"Instalar MySQL y SQL Server en VMs de Compute Engine y copiar los datos con un mysqldump/restauraci\xf3n de backup cada noche","B":"Exportar todas las tablas a CSV y cargarlas en Bigtable","C":"Reescribir las aplicaciones para usar Firestore antes de migrar ning\xfan dato","D":"Usar Database Migration Service para replicar de forma continua desde las bases de origen a Cloud SQL for MySQL y Cloud SQL for SQL Server, y hacer el corte con una ventana de inactividad corta"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Database Migration Service admite migraciones homog\xe9neas a Cloud SQL for MySQL y Cloud SQL for SQL Server (y a AlloyDB/Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL). Hace una carga inicial y luego replicaci\xf3n continua (CDC), as\xed que el corte solo necesita una ventana corta. Cloud SQL es gestionado —backups, parches, alta disponibilidad y r\xe9plicas—, lo que cumple \xabmenos esfuerzo operativo\xbb y \xabreducir costes de hosting\xbb. Redis encaja de forma natural en Memorystore for Redis.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) en VMs sigue toda la administraci\xf3n (SO, parches, backups) y los volcados nocturnos suponen mucha inactividad o p\xe9rdida de datos en el corte. (B) Bigtable es NoSQL de columna ancha; no sirve consultas relacionales a estas aplicaciones. (C) reescribir todas las aplicaciones antes de mover nada es el camino m\xe1s lento y arriesgado.\\n\\nPista de examen: lift-and-shift de MySQL/SQL Server/PostgreSQL con m\xednima inactividad → Database Migration Service + Cloud SQL (o AlloyDB para PostgreSQL)."},{"id":"12","question":"Caso de estudio Cymbal Retail. Las conversaciones de los clientes con los agentes virtuales pueden contener datos personales (nombres, direcciones, n\xfameros de tarjeta). Los requisitos t\xe9cnicos dicen que todos los datos de clientes deben tratarse de forma segura y cumplir la normativa del sector, y los logs de conversaci\xf3n se analizar\xe1n en BigQuery. \xbfQu\xe9 haces primero para proteger los logs que se usan en anal\xedtica?","options":{"A":"Pasar Sensitive Data Protection (antes Cloud DLP) para inspeccionar y desidentificar (enmascarar o tokenizar) la PII de las transcripciones antes de que lleguen a BigQuery, y restringir con IAM el acceso a los datos en bruto","B":"Hacer p\xfablico el dataset de BigQuery para que el equipo de anal\xedtica acceda desde cualquier herramienta","C":"Confiar en que Google cifra los datos en reposo por defecto, as\xed que no hace falta nada m\xe1s","D":"Pedir al agente virtual que avise a los clientes de que no compartan datos personales"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sensitive Data Protection (Cloud DLP) detecta m\xe1s de 150 infoTypes (n\xfameros de tarjeta, nombres, direcciones…) y los desidentifica con enmascarado, redacci\xf3n o tokenizaci\xf3n (cifrado que preserva el formato o determinista) antes de que el dato llegue al almac\xe9n anal\xedtico, de modo que los analistas trabajan con datos \xfatiles pero no identificativos. IAM (y si hace falta seguridad a nivel de columna o policy tags en BigQuery) limita qui\xe9n lee las transcripciones en bruto. Para datos de tarjeta, PCI DSS exige minimizar d\xf3nde se guardan.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) un dataset p\xfablico es lo contrario del cumplimiento. (C) el cifrado en reposo protege los discos, no el contenido frente a quien tiene permiso de lectura. (D) no se puede delegar la protecci\xf3n del dato en el comportamiento del cliente.\\n\\nPista de examen: PII en texto/logs + anal\xedtica → desidentificaci\xf3n con Sensitive Data Protection antes de almacenar; control de acceso encima."},{"id":"13","question":"Caso de estudio EHR Healthcare. EHR gestiona los usuarios en Microsoft Active Directory y quiere que los empleados entren en Google Cloud con su cuenta corporativa, sin mantener un segundo juego de contrase\xf1as, y que AD siga siendo la fuente de verdad. \xbfQu\xe9 haces?","options":{"A":"Crear a mano cada usuario en la consola de Google Cloud y enviarle por correo una contrase\xf1a temporal","B":"Usar Google Cloud Directory Sync para aprovisionar usuarios y grupos de AD en Cloud Identity, y configurar single sign-on SAML contra AD FS (o Entra ID) para que la autenticaci\xf3n siga en el IdP corporativo","C":"Compartir una cuenta de Google por equipo y guardar su contrase\xf1a en un gestor de contrase\xf1as","D":"Dar roles en IAM directamente al nombre de dominio de AD, que Google Cloud lee autom\xe1ticamente"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El patr\xf3n de referencia con AD tiene dos piezas: Google Cloud Directory Sync (GCDS) se ejecuta on-premises y sincroniza en un sentido usuarios y grupos de AD/LDAP a Cloud Identity, as\xed AD sigue siendo la fuente de verdad; el SSO SAML federa la autenticaci\xf3n con AD FS o Microsoft Entra ID, as\xed las contrase\xf1as nunca viven en Google. Despu\xe9s los roles de IAM se asignan a los grupos sincronizados. (Workforce Identity Federation es la alternativa cuando no quieres sincronizar identidades.)\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) cuentas manuales y segundas contrase\xf1as son justo lo que el requisito evita, y no escalan. (C) las cuentas compartidas rompen la auditor\xeda y el m\xednimo privilegio. (D) IAM no puede leer un dominio de AD; las identidades tienen que existir en Cloud Identity o estar federadas.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xablos usuarios se gestionan en Active Directory\xbb → GCDS + SSO SAML (o Workforce Identity Federation)."},{"id":"14","question":"Caso de estudio EHR Healthcare. Las aplicaciones en contenedores orientadas al cliente deben ofrecer al menos un 99,9 % de disponibilidad y menor latencia para consultas m\xe9dicas y hospitales de varios pa\xedses, y absorber los picos de tr\xe1fico que antes causaban ca\xeddas. \xbfQu\xe9 dise\xf1o cumple mejor estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Un cl\xfaster GKE zonal en una sola regi\xf3n con un n\xfamero fijo de nodos dimensionado para el pico","B":"VMs de Compute Engine en una zona detr\xe1s de un Network Load Balancer interno regional de paso directo","C":"Una \xfanica instancia de Cloud SQL compartida por todas las regiones y accedida por internet p\xfablico, sin balanceador","D":"Cl\xfasteres GKE regionales en varias regiones cercanas a los clientes, con cluster autoscaler y Horizontal Pod Autoscaler, expuestos con un Application Load Balancer externo global (multi-cluster Gateway) y Cloud CDN para el contenido est\xe1tico"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Los cl\xfasteres GKE regionales reparten plano de control y nodos entre zonas, as\xed que la ca\xedda de una zona no tumba la aplicaci\xf3n. Desplegarlos en varias regiones con un Application Load Balancer externo global delante (por ejemplo con multi-cluster Gateway) lleva a cada usuario al backend sano m\xe1s cercano con una \xfanica IP anycast, lo que reduce latencia y hace failover entre regiones. El cluster autoscaler y el HPA absorben los picos —el caso dice que las ca\xeddas ven\xedan de \xabcapacidad insuficiente para picos de tr\xe1fico\xbb.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) un cl\xfaster zonal es un punto \xfanico de fallo y un tama\xf1o fijo no absorbe picos de forma econ\xf3mica. (B) una sola zona no cumple la disponibilidad y un balanceador interno no es alcanzable por clientes externos. (C) un \xfanico endpoint de base de datos sin balanceo no resuelve ni disponibilidad ni latencia.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xab99,9 %+\xbb, \xabclientes globales\xbb, \xabreducir latencia\xbb → backends multirregi\xf3n (o al menos regionales) detr\xe1s de un Application Load Balancer externo global, m\xe1s autoescalado."},{"id":"15","question":"Caso de estudio EHR Healthcare. Los requisitos t\xe9cnicos piden logging, retenci\xf3n de logs, monitorizaci\xf3n y alertas consistentes en todos los entornos, y el negocio debe mantener el cumplimiento normativo. Hoy cada equipo usa herramientas open source distintas y las alertas van por correo. \xbfQu\xe9 implementas?","options":{"A":"Un sink agregado a nivel de organizaci\xf3n (o carpeta) que env\xede los logs de todos los proyectos a un log bucket central con pol\xedtica de retenci\xf3n (bloqueada si la normativa lo exige), m\xe1s dashboards y pol\xedticas de alertas de Cloud Monitoring con canales de guardia","B":"Dejar que cada proyecto use su bucket _Default con 30 d\xedas de retenci\xf3n y que los equipos miren los logs cuando algo falle","C":"Desactivar los Data Access audit logs para reducir volumen y coste","D":"Copiar los logs cada semana al port\xe1til de cada desarrollador para la retenci\xf3n a largo plazo"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Un sink agregado en la organizaci\xf3n o una carpeta captura los logs de todos los proyectos hijos, presentes y futuros, y los env\xeda a un destino central: un log bucket con retenci\xf3n personalizada (y bucket lock cuando la retenci\xf3n debe ser inmutable), y opcionalmente BigQuery para an\xe1lisis o Cloud Storage para archivo barato. As\xed la consistencia viene del dise\xf1o, no de la configuraci\xf3n de cada equipo. Las pol\xedticas de alertas de Cloud Monitoring con canales PagerDuty, Slack o Pub/Sub sustituyen al correo ignorado y cubren \xabvisibilidad centralizada y acci\xf3n proactiva\xbb.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) el bucket _Default guarda 30 d\xedas y deja cada proyecto distinto. (C) los Data Access audit logs son justo lo que piden los auditores en sanidad (qui\xe9n ley\xf3 qu\xe9 historial). (D) un port\xe1til no es almacenamiento seguro ni conforme.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xablogging consistente entre proyectos\xbb + \xabretenci\xf3n\xbb → sink agregado de organizaci\xf3n a un log bucket con retenci\xf3n (bloqueada si hace falta)."},{"id":"16","question":"Caso de estudio EHR Healthcare. EHR quiere dar de alta nuevas aseguradoras lo m\xe1s r\xe1pido posible y crear interfaces para ingerir y procesar sus datos, mientras las integraciones legacy on-premises deben seguir funcionando hasta que se retiren. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque lo soporta mejor?","options":{"A":"Dar a cada aseguradora una VPN directa a la VPC de producci\xf3n y dejar que escriban en las bases de datos","B":"Construir una integraci\xf3n punto a punto con c\xf3digo propio para cada aseguradora y alojarla en VMs no gestionadas","C":"Publicar APIs estandarizadas para aseguradoras con Apigee (API keys/OAuth, cuotas, anal\xedtica, portal de desarrolladores para el alta autoservicio), con servicios en Google Cloud detr\xe1s, y seguir llegando a las integraciones legacy on-premises por el enlace h\xedbrido Interconnect/VPN","D":"Pedir a las aseguradoras que env\xeden CSV por correo a un buz\xf3n compartido y que un operador los suba cada d\xeda"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Apigee es la plataforma de gesti\xf3n de APIs de Google Cloud: expones un conjunto \xfanico y bien definido de APIs con seguridad (API keys, OAuth), cuotas y l\xedmites, anal\xedtica y un portal de desarrolladores donde los nuevos socios se registran y obtienen credenciales solos, lo que acorta el alta. Los backends pueden vivir en Google Cloud y seguir llamando a los sistemas legacy on-premises por la conexi\xf3n h\xedbrida privada, cumpliendo \xabmantener las interfaces legacy\xbb. Las aseguradoras que trabajan con ficheros pueden dejarlos en Cloud Storage, con notificaciones de Pub/Sub que disparan el procesamiento.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) dar a terceros acceso de red a producci\xf3n es un riesgo grave de seguridad y cumplimiento. (B) una integraci\xf3n a medida por aseguradora es el patr\xf3n lento y caro que frena las altas. (D) correo y subidas manuales son lentos, propensos a errores y no auditables para datos de salud.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabdar de alta socios r\xe1pido\xbb, \xabexponer interfaces a terceros\xbb → Apigee (gesti\xf3n de APIs + portal de desarrolladores)."},{"id":"17","question":"Caso de estudio EHR Healthcare. EHR guarda informaci\xf3n sanitaria protegida (PHI) y debe mantener el cumplimiento normativo (HIPAA en EE. UU.) tras pasar a Google Cloud. \xbfQu\xe9 combinaci\xf3n es el punto de partida m\xe1s adecuado?","options":{"A":"Firmar un Business Associate Agreement (BAA) con Google, guardar PHI solo en servicios cubiertos por el BAA y a\xf1adir controles como per\xedmetros de VPC Service Controls, CMEK donde se exija, IAM de m\xednimo privilegio y Data Access audit logs","B":"No hace falta nada: todos los servicios de Google Cloud cumplen HIPAA autom\xe1ticamente con cualquier configuraci\xf3n","C":"Guardar PHI en un bucket p\xfablico de Cloud Storage protegido con un nombre de bucket dif\xedcil de adivinar","D":"Cifrar la PHI con una clave escrita en el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n para que solo los desarrolladores puedan leerla"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HIPAA sigue un modelo de responsabilidad compartida. Google firma un BAA y publica qu\xe9 productos cubre; el cliente debe mantener la PHI en esos productos y configurarlos bien. Controles t\xedpicos: VPC Service Controls para reducir el riesgo de exfiltraci\xf3n alrededor de servicios como BigQuery, Cloud Storage y Cloud Healthcare API; claves gestionadas por el cliente (CMEK) en Cloud KMS cuando la pol\xedtica exige controlar la clave; IAM de m\xednimo privilegio; y Data Access audit logs para saber qui\xe9n ley\xf3 qu\xe9 historial. Assured Workloads a\xf1ade controles de residencia de datos y de personal para otros marcos regulatorios.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) un BAA no vuelve conforme cualquier configuraci\xf3n; el cliente sigue siendo responsable de su parte. (C) un bucket p\xfablico expone la PHI; la oscuridad no es seguridad. (D) las claves en el c\xf3digo son un antipatr\xf3n conocido; las claves van en Cloud KMS o Secret Manager.\\n\\nPista de examen: sanidad / PHI / HIPAA → BAA + servicios cubiertos + VPC SC + CMEK + audit logs; Cloud Healthcare API (FHIR, HL7v2, DICOM) para datos cl\xednicos."},{"id":"18","question":"Caso de estudio EHR Healthcare. El negocio quiere aumentar su capacidad de ofrecer informaci\xf3n sobre tendencias sanitarias y hacer predicciones e informes sobre tendencias del sector a partir de los datos de los proveedores. El equipo de datos domina SQL pero tiene poca experiencia en ML. \xbfQu\xe9 recomiendas?","options":{"A":"Lanzar la anal\xedtica directamente contra las bases MySQL de producci\xf3n en horario laboral","B":"Centralizar en BigQuery los datos de proveedores desidentificados, crear modelos de previsi\xf3n y clasificaci\xf3n con BigQuery ML (por ejemplo ARIMA_PLUS o AI.FORECAST para tendencias) y publicar los informes con Looker","C":"Contratar un equipo para escribir modelos TensorFlow a medida antes de centralizar ning\xfan dato","D":"Exportar todas las tablas a hojas de c\xe1lculo y enviarlas por correo a los analistas cada mes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"BigQuery es el almac\xe9n anal\xedtico que escala a todos los datos de proveedores sin afectar a los sistemas transaccionales, y BigQuery ML permite a un equipo que sabe SQL entrenar y usar modelos con CREATE MODEL / ML.FORECAST / ML.PREDICT: ARIMA_PLUS para tendencias de series temporales, o la funci\xf3n AI.FORECAST (TimesFM) sin entrenar nada. Looker (o Looker Studio) convierte los resultados en informes gobernados. Al ser datos de salud, conviene desidentificarlos (Sensitive Data Protection) antes de un uso anal\xedtico amplio.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) la anal\xedtica pesada sobre las bases OLTP de producci\xf3n perjudica la disponibilidad de cara al cliente. (C) TensorFlow a medida tarda en entregar e ignora que el equipo sabe SQL. (D) hojas de c\xe1lculo por correo no escalan ni cumplen la normativa.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabinsights / predicciones / informes\xbb + \xabsabe SQL\xbb → BigQuery + BigQuery ML + Looker."},{"id":"19","question":"Caso de estudio KnightMotives Automotive. Cumplir la normativa de protecci\xf3n de datos de la UE es cr\xedtico, sobre todo para las plataformas aut\xf3nomas emergentes, y la seguridad es prioritaria tras brechas pasadas. La empresa quiere garantizar que los datos de conductores de la UE solo se almacenen y procesen en regiones de la UE y que ning\xfan proyecto pueda salt\xe1rselo. \xbfQu\xe9 haces?","options":{"A":"Escribir una p\xe1gina en la wiki pidiendo a los desarrolladores que elijan siempre regiones europe-west","B":"Usar un bucket multi-regi\xf3n de EE. UU. y cifrarlo con claves gestionadas por Google","C":"Colocar las cargas de la UE en una carpeta gobernada por Assured Workloads (controles de frontera de datos de la UE) y aplicar la pol\xedtica de organizaci\xf3n de ubicaciones de recursos (gcp.resourceLocations) limitada a ubicaciones de la UE, junto con CMEK para controlar las claves","D":"Confiar solo en los roles de IAM, ya que solo empleados de la UE tendr\xe1n acceso"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La residencia de datos tiene que imponerla la plataforma, no la buena voluntad. La restricci\xf3n de pol\xedtica de organizaci\xf3n gcp.resourceLocations impide crear recursos fuera de las ubicaciones permitidas en todo lo que cuelga de la carpeta u organizaci\xf3n. Assured Workloads empaqueta esos controles para marcos regulatorios (por ejemplo la frontera de datos de la UE / controles soberanos): configura las restricciones de ubicaci\xf3n, limita los servicios a los que soportan ese r\xe9gimen y puede a\xf1adir controles de gesti\xf3n de claves y de personal. CMEK en Cloud KMS (incluso con un gestor de claves externo) da a la empresa el control de las claves.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) una p\xe1gina de wiki no es un control que se pueda imponer. (B) una multi-regi\xf3n de EE. UU. incumple la residencia aunque est\xe9 cifrada. (D) IAM controla qui\xe9n accede, no d\xf3nde se guarda ni se procesa el dato.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xablos datos deben quedarse en la UE\xbb, \xabsoberan\xeda\xbb, \xabno se puede saltar\xbb → pol\xedtica de organizaci\xf3n de ubicaciones + Assured Workloads."},{"id":"20","question":"Caso de estudio KnightMotives Automotive. Los veh\xedculos conectados enviar\xe1n telemetr\xeda para funciones de IA en tiempo real y anal\xedtica, pero la conectividad en zonas rurales es poco fiable. La capa de ingesta debe absorber millones de dispositivos, tolerar veh\xedculos que se reconectan tras horas sin red y alimentar tanto la anal\xedtica en streaming como el data warehouse. \xbfQu\xe9 dise\xf1o es el m\xe1s adecuado?","options":{"A":"Que cada veh\xedculo abra una conexi\xf3n directa a una instancia de Cloud SQL e inserte filas de forma s\xedncrona","B":"Guardar los eventos en el veh\xedculo y publicarlos en Pub/Sub cuando haya conexi\xf3n; procesar el flujo con Dataflow usando ventanas por tiempo de evento y allowed lateness, y escribir en BigQuery (y en Bigtable para lecturas por veh\xedculo de baja latencia)","C":"Subir un CSV por veh\xedculo y mes a Cloud Storage con una memoria USB en el concesionario","D":"Enviar la telemetr\xeda por correo a un buz\xf3n vigilado y procesarla con un cron"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pub/Sub es el buffer de ingesta global y autoescalable para millones de publicadores; el veh\xedculo guarda los eventos mientras no tiene red y los publica al reconectar. Dataflow (Apache Beam) est\xe1 hecho para datos tard\xedos y desordenados: ventanas por tiempo de evento, watermarks y allowed lateness, as\xed la anal\xedtica sigue siendo correcta aunque un coche suba datos horas despu\xe9s. BigQuery sirve la anal\xedtica y la plataforma de monetizaci\xf3n de datos; Bigtable sirve lecturas de baja latencia por veh\xedculo (\xfaltimo estado, series temporales por clave de veh\xedculo).\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) millones de conexiones s\xedncronas a una base relacional no escalan y fallan con enlaces intermitentes. (C) lotes mensuales no permiten funciones de IA en tiempo real. (D) el correo no es un transporte de telemetr\xeda.\\n\\nPista de examen: IoT / telemetr\xeda + \xabconectividad intermitente\xbb + \xabtiempo real\xbb → Pub/Sub + Dataflow (tiempo de evento, datos tard\xedos) + BigQuery / Bigtable."},{"id":"21","question":"Caso de estudio KnightMotives Automotive. KnightMotives quiere monetizar datos corporativos (por ejemplo, datos agregados de conducci\xf3n y estado de carreteras) comparti\xe9ndolos con socios como aseguradoras y ayuntamientos, con medidas estrictas de seguridad y privacidad. Hoy los datos est\xe1n en silos. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque cumple mejor estos objetivos?","options":{"A":"Exportar tablas completas a CSV y enviarlas a cada socio por SFTP cada semana","B":"Dar a los socios roles de lector en IAM sobre el proyecto de producci\xf3n que tiene los datos en bruto de los veh\xedculos","C":"Copiar los datos en bruto a un proyecto de BigQuery por socio y dejar que cada socio lo consulte todo","D":"Consolidar en BigQuery datasets curados, desidentificados y agregados, y publicarlos como listings con BigQuery sharing (antes Analytics Hub) —con data clean rooms cuando el socio deba cruzar datos sin ver filas en bruto—, gobernados con Dataplex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"BigQuery sharing (antes Analytics Hub) permite a un publicador exponer datasets como listings dentro de exchanges; el suscriptor obtiene un dataset enlazado que lee los datos del publicador en origen, sin copias, mientras el publicador mantiene el control, puede revocar el acceso y ve el uso. Las data clean rooms a\xf1aden controles de privacidad (por ejemplo umbrales de agregaci\xf3n) para que el socio analice cruzando con sus propios datos sin ver registros individuales. Dataplex (Knowledge Catalog) aporta gobierno, linaje y calidad de datos sobre lo que antes estaba en silos. La desidentificaci\xf3n y agregaci\xf3n van antes de publicar.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) las copias en ficheros pierden el control, se quedan obsoletas y multiplican el riesgo de fuga. (B) dar acceso a datos en bruto de producci\xf3n viola el m\xednimo privilegio y la privacidad. (C) copias en bruto por socio son caras de mantener sincronizadas y exponen demasiado.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabcompartir / monetizar datos con organizaciones externas\xbb sin copiar → BigQuery sharing (Analytics Hub) y data clean rooms."},{"id":"22","question":"Caso de estudio KnightMotives Automotive. La conectividad entre las plantas de fabricaci\xf3n y la sede es un problema, pero las plantas necesitan IA de baja latencia (por ejemplo, inspecci\xf3n visual de calidad en la l\xednea de montaje) que siga funcionando si se cae el enlace WAN. La empresa est\xe1 adoptando una estrategia de nube h\xedbrida. \xbfQu\xe9 recomiendas para las plantas?","options":{"A":"Ejecutar la inferencia en hardware de Google Distributed Cloud (connected) en cada planta, gestionado desde Google Cloud, para que los modelos corran en local y los resultados se sincronicen con la nube cuando haya enlace","B":"Enviar cada imagen de c\xe1mara a un endpoint de modelo en una \xfanica regi\xf3n cloud y esperar la respuesta antes de mover la l\xednea","C":"Dejar de usar IA en la l\xednea de montaje hasta que se mejore la red","D":"Guardar todas las im\xe1genes en un NAS local y analizarlas a mano al final de cada turno"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Google Distributed Cloud (connected) lleva infraestructura gestionada por Google (cl\xfasteres Kubernetes sobre hardware en las instalaciones del cliente) al edge, as\xed la inferencia corre junto a las c\xe1maras con latencia muy baja y sigue funcionando aunque caiga la WAN, mientras la gesti\xf3n, las actualizaciones y la configuraci\xf3n de la flota llegan desde Google Cloud. Resultados y muestras se sincronizan con la nube para reentrenar cuando hay conexi\xf3n. Encaja con la estrategia h\xedbrida y con la restricci\xf3n de red.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) un viaje de ida y vuelta a una regi\xf3n remota a\xf1ade latencia y para la l\xednea cada vez que falla el enlace. (C) renunciar al requisito de IA no es una arquitectura. (D) revisar a mano al final del turno no es inspecci\xf3n en tiempo real.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabbaja latencia en f\xe1brica/tienda/edge\xbb + \xabconectividad pobre o intermitente\xbb → Google Distributed Cloud (edge) con operaci\xf3n gestionada desde la nube."},{"id":"23","question":"Caso de estudio KnightMotives Automotive. Tras brechas de datos anteriores, los requisitos t\xe9cnicos piden un marco de seguridad completo frente a ciberamenazas y un plan de respuesta a incidentes. El equipo de seguridad quiere un \xfanico sitio donde ver configuraciones err\xf3neas, vulnerabilidades y amenazas activas en todos los proyectos de Google Cloud, y adem\xe1s impedir que se creen configuraciones peligrosas. \xbfQu\xe9 implementas?","options":{"A":"Una hoja de c\xe1lculo donde cada equipo apunte sus reglas de firewall una vez por trimestre","B":"Solo reglas de Cloud Armor, porque todas las amenazas llegan por HTTP","C":"Security Command Center (Premium/Enterprise) a nivel de organizaci\xf3n para hallazgos de postura, vulnerabilidades y detecci\xf3n de amenazas, restricciones de pol\xedtica de organizaci\xf3n como barreras preventivas (por ejemplo sin IPs externas, sin creaci\xf3n de claves de cuentas de servicio, acceso uniforme a buckets) y env\xedo de alertas al proceso de respuesta a incidentes","D":"Desactivar los audit logs para reducir la superficie de ataque"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Security Command Center activado en la organizaci\xf3n da la visi\xf3n central: hallazgos de configuraci\xf3n (Security Health Analytics), de vulnerabilidades, detecci\xf3n de amenazas (Event Threat Detection, Container Threat Detection) y, en los niveles superiores, an\xe1lisis de rutas de ataque y riesgo. Las restricciones de pol\xedtica de organizaci\xf3n son preventivas: impiden crear recursos peligrosos desde el principio (por ejemplo constraints/compute.vmExternalIpAccess, iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation, storage.uniformBucketLevelAccess). Los hallazgos pueden exportarse por Pub/Sub al SIEM/SOAR (por ejemplo Google Security Operations) que ejecuta el plan de respuesta a incidentes.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) una hoja trimestral ni es continua ni se puede imponer. (B) Cloud Armor solo cubre ataques web; no ve IAM mal configurado, claves filtradas ni im\xe1genes vulnerables. (D) los audit logs son esenciales para detectar e investigar; desactivarlos perjudica la respuesta a incidentes.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabpostura de seguridad centralizada\xbb, \xabdetectar amenazas en toda la organizaci\xf3n\xbb → Security Command Center; \xabimpedir\xbb malas configuraciones → pol\xedticas de organizaci\xf3n."},{"id":"24","question":"Caso de estudio KnightMotives Automotive. El desarrollo del veh\xedculo aut\xf3nomo exige entrenar modelos de percepci\xf3n con petabytes de datos de c\xe1mara y lidar y ejecutar grandes cargas de simulaci\xf3n. La infraestructura de IA actual est\xe1 obsoleta. El equipo necesita entrenar mucho m\xe1s r\xe1pido que en una sola m\xe1quina sin gestionar a largo plazo su propia flota de GPUs. \xbfQu\xe9 recomiendas?","options":{"A":"Entrenar en una sola VM grande solo con CPU, a\xf1adiendo RAM a medida que crece el dataset","B":"Comprar GPUs para la estaci\xf3n de trabajo de cada ingeniero y copiar el dataset a cada puesto","C":"Usar regresi\xf3n lineal de BigQuery ML sobre las nubes de puntos lidar en bruto","D":"Mantener los datos de sensores en Cloud Storage y hacer entrenamiento distribuido en GPUs o TPUs (paralelismo de datos entre muchos aceleradores, de modelo si el modelo no cabe en un dispositivo) con custom training de Agent Platform o GKE, usando capacidad Spot para los trabajos de simulaci\xf3n tolerantes a interrupciones"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Los modelos de percepci\xf3n grandes necesitan aceleradores y entrenamiento distribuido. Con paralelismo de datos cada acelerador tiene una copia del modelo y procesa un trozo distinto de cada batch, escalando el rendimiento entre muchas GPUs/TPUs; el paralelismo de modelo (o de pipeline/tensores) reparte un modelo que no cabe en la memoria de un solo dispositivo. El custom training gestionado de Agent Platform (antes Vertex AI) o GKE con node pools de GPU/TPU da esa capacidad bajo demanda, leyendo los datos de Cloud Storage. Las simulaciones que toleran interrupciones son buenas candidatas para Spot VMs y abaratar el coste.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) una sola m\xe1quina con CPU no entrena modelos de visi\xf3n profundos sobre petabytes en un tiempo \xfatil. (B) GPUs por puesto y copias del dataset son justo el modelo obsoleto e inmanejable que el caso quiere dejar, y un riesgo de seguridad. (C) la regresi\xf3n lineal de BigQuery ML no es un modelo de percepci\xf3n sobre nubes de puntos.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabentrenar m\xe1s r\xe1pido\xbb + \xabmodelo de deep learning grande\xbb → GPUs/TPUs con entrenamiento distribuido; \xabel modelo no cabe en memoria\xbb → paralelismo de modelo; \xabbatch interrumpible\xbb → Spot VMs."}]},{"id":"databricks-2026","title":"Databricks — 2026 exam-guide objectives (all certifications)","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"A 40 TB Delta table of events is queried by analysts who filter on customer_id (high cardinality) and event_date, and the filter columns used by the business keep changing every quarter. The table is currently partitioned by event_date and ZORDERed weekly, which is slow to maintain and produces many small partitions. Which approach reduces maintenance and adapts best to the access pattern?","options":{"A":"Repartition the table by customer_id so each customer gets its own directory","B":"Convert to a Unity Catalog managed table with Liquid Clustering (CLUSTER BY AUTO or explicit clustering keys) and let Predictive Optimization run OPTIMIZE automatically","C":"Keep the partitioning and run ZORDER BY on every column after each load","D":"Disable data skipping statistics to speed up writes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Liquid Clustering replaces Hive-style partitioning and ZORDER: clustering keys can be changed without rewriting the table, it handles high-cardinality columns without creating tiny partitions, and clustering is incremental. With CLUSTER BY AUTO on a Unity Catalog managed table, Databricks chooses and evolves the keys from the observed query workload, and Predictive Optimization runs OPTIMIZE/VACUUM for you.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) partitioning by a high-cardinality column creates millions of tiny directories — the classic small-file problem. (C) ZORDER on many columns dilutes its benefit and must be rerun as a full maintenance job. (D) statistics are what enable data skipping; removing them makes reads slower.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"high cardinality\\", \\"changing query patterns\\", \\"reduce maintenance\\" → Liquid Clustering (+ CLUSTER BY AUTO / Predictive Optimization)."},{"id":"2","question":"A silver Delta table receives MERGE statements with inserts, updates and deletes. A downstream gold pipeline currently re-reads the whole silver table every hour to recompute aggregates, which is expensive. How should the engineer expose only the row-level changes so the downstream job can process them incrementally?","options":{"A":"Run DESCRIBE HISTORY on the silver table and parse the operation metrics","B":"Compare the full table with a copy taken one hour earlier using EXCEPT","C":"Use VACUUM to delete old files so only new rows remain","D":"Enable Change Data Feed on the silver table (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true) and read changes with readChangeFeed / table_changes(), using _change_type to apply inserts, updates and deletes downstream"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Change Data Feed records row-level changes made after it is enabled. Readers get each changed row with metadata columns _change_type (insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete), _commit_version and _commit_timestamp, either in batch (table_changes(\'tbl\', startVersion)) or as a stream (option readChangeFeed = true). The gold job then processes only what changed, including deletes, which a plain append stream cannot see.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) DESCRIBE HISTORY gives commit-level metrics, not the changed rows. (B) a full EXCEPT comparison is as expensive as the full re-read it is trying to avoid. (C) VACUUM removes unreferenced old files; it does not filter rows and breaks time travel.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"propagate updates and deletes incrementally\\" → Change Data Feed."},{"id":"3","question":"A company has hundreds of tables across several catalogs that contain email and phone columns. Security wants those columns masked for everyone except the privacy team, applied automatically to any new table that contains such data, without attaching a mask function to each column one by one. What should the data engineer use?","options":{"A":"Unity Catalog attribute-based access control (ABAC): tag the columns with a governed tag (for example pii=email) and define a policy at catalog or schema level that applies a column mask function to every column carrying that tag","B":"Create a separate copy of every table with the columns removed and grant access to the copies","C":"Add a WHERE clause to each dashboard query that hides the columns","D":"Set a DENY on each column for all users"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ABAC policies in Unity Catalog combine governed tags with row filter and column mask functions. A policy defined at catalog or schema level says, for example, \\"for columns tagged pii=email, apply mask_email() unless the user is in privacy_team\\"; it applies to existing and new tables as soon as the columns are tagged (tagging can even be assisted by automatic data classification). That scales far better than ALTER TABLE … SET MASK on each column.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) copies multiply storage, drift out of sync and are hard to govern. (C) query-level hiding is bypassed by anyone querying the table directly. (D) Unity Catalog privileges are granted on securables such as tables, not individual columns, and a DENY would hide the column instead of showing a masked value.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"at scale\\", \\"automatically for new tables\\", \\"based on tags\\" → ABAC policies with governed tags; one-off → per-column mask."},{"id":"4","question":"Under a GDPR right-to-erasure request, a customer\'s rows must be removed from a Delta table so that they cannot be recovered. The table has deletion vectors enabled and a 7-day retention period. Which sequence physically removes the data?","options":{"A":"Run DELETE only; the rows disappear from storage immediately","B":"Run RESTORE TABLE to a version before the customer was inserted","C":"Run DELETE, then REORG TABLE … APPLY (PURGE) to rewrite the files that still hold the soft-deleted rows, and then VACUUM once the retention period has passed so old files (and time travel to them) are removed","D":"Drop the customer\'s column from the table schema"},"correct":"C","explanation":"With deletion vectors, DELETE only marks rows as deleted; the data still sits in the Parquet files. REORG TABLE … APPLY (PURGE) rewrites those files without the deleted rows. Even then, earlier table versions still reference the old files, so VACUUM must run after the retention period to delete them — after that, time travel to versions containing the customer is no longer possible. Change Data Feed files, if enabled, are also cleaned up by VACUUM.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) DELETE alone is a logical delete; the bytes remain recoverable through time travel. (B) RESTORE would also discard every legitimate change made since that version. (D) dropping a column does not remove a customer\'s rows and, with column mapping, does not even rewrite the data.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"right to be forgotten\\" in Delta → DELETE + (REORG PURGE with deletion vectors) + VACUUM after retention."},{"id":"5","question":"The analytics group must be able to read every current and future table in the schema sales.reporting, and nothing else in the sales catalog. What is the minimal set of Unity Catalog grants?","options":{"A":"GRANT SELECT on each existing table in sales.reporting, and repeat whenever a table is created","B":"GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON CATALOG sales","C":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG sales, GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA sales.reporting and GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA sales.reporting","D":"GRANT SELECT ON CATALOG sales"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unity Catalog privileges are inherited downward: SELECT granted on a schema applies to all current and future tables and views in it. To reach any object, a principal also needs USE CATALOG on the parent catalog and USE SCHEMA on the parent schema — these grant no data access by themselves, only the ability to traverse. Together they give exactly read access to sales.reporting.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) per-table grants work but miss future tables and must be maintained by hand. (B) ALL PRIVILEGES on the catalog grants far more than reading. (D) SELECT on the catalog exposes every schema in sales, not just reporting (and still needs USE CATALOG/USE SCHEMA).\\n\\nExam cue: \\"current and future tables\\" → grant at the schema level; always remember USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA."},{"id":"6","question":"A classification model served on Databricks Model Serving logs its requests and responses to an inference table. Ground-truth labels arrive two days later and are joined into that table. The team wants dashboards of model accuracy and data drift over time, sliced by region. What should they configure?","options":{"A":"A Lakehouse Monitoring (data profiling) monitor with the InferenceLog profile on the inference table, specifying the prediction, label, model id and timestamp columns, slicing expressions for region, and a time granularity","B":"A Snapshot profile on the training table only","C":"A SQL alert that counts rows in the inference table every hour","D":"MLflow autologging on the serving endpoint"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lakehouse Monitoring has three profile types: Snapshot (whole table each refresh), TimeSeries (windows by a timestamp column) and InferenceLog (a time series plus model columns: prediction, label, model id). With the InferenceLog profile on the inference table, the monitor computes model quality metrics once labels are present (accuracy, precision, recall, F1 for classification) and drift metrics per time window, and slicing expressions break both down by region. Results land in the profile and drift metrics tables plus an auto-generated dashboard.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) profiling only the training table says nothing about production behaviour. (C) a row count is a health signal, not accuracy or drift. (D) autologging captures training runs, not production performance.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"model performance over time from served predictions\\" → InferenceLog monitor on the inference table."},{"id":"7","question":"In the drift metrics table produced by Lakehouse Monitoring, a data scientist wants to test whether the distribution of a continuous numerical feature (transaction_amount) in the current window differs significantly from the baseline. Which statistic is designed for that comparison?","options":{"A":"The chi-squared test, because it compares two continuous distributions directly","B":"The count of null values in the current window","C":"The mode of transaction_amount in each window","D":"The Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) test, which compares two numerical distributions and returns a statistic and p-value"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lakehouse Monitoring\'s drift metrics include statistical tests chosen by data type: the two-sample Kolmogorov-Smirnov test for numerical columns and the chi-squared test for categorical columns, each reported with a statistic and p-value, plus distance measures such as Jensen-Shannon, Wasserstein (numeric), total variation and population stability index. A small KS p-value indicates the current distribution differs significantly from the baseline (or previous window).\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) chi-squared compares frequencies of categories; continuous values would have to be binned first. (B) null counts measure data quality, not distribution shift. (C) a single mode ignores the shape of the distribution.\\n\\nExam cue: numerical drift → KS test (or Wasserstein); categorical drift → chi-squared (or JS distance)."},{"id":"8","question":"A regulated team cannot let production data be read from the development workspace, but production models must be trained on production data. Which MLOps deployment pattern recommended by Databricks fits this constraint?","options":{"A":"Deploy models: train in dev on a copy of production data and promote the model artifact to prod","B":"Deploy code: promote the training code through dev → staging → prod with CI/CD, and run the training pipeline in the production environment against production data","C":"Train in a personal notebook and upload the pickle file to the prod endpoint","D":"Skip staging and deploy directly from a feature branch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Databricks\' MLOps guidance distinguishes \\"deploy models\\" (the trained artifact is promoted across environments) from \\"deploy code\\" (the code is promoted and the model is retrained in each environment). With deploy code, the training pipeline is tested in dev and staging, and the production model is trained inside the prod environment, so production data never needs to be accessible from dev. It is the recommended default and fits regulated settings; deploy models is simpler when training is very expensive or must happen once.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) copying production data into dev breaks the access constraint. (C) manual uploads bypass testing, lineage and the registry. (D) skipping staging removes the integration-test gate.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"production data cannot be accessed from dev\\" → deploy code; \\"training is too expensive to repeat\\" → deploy models."},{"id":"9","question":"A high-traffic recommendation endpoint on Databricks Model Serving serves model version 3. The team wants to expose version 4 to 10% of real traffic, compare the results, and roll back instantly if errors rise. How should they implement this?","options":{"A":"Add version 4 as a second served entity on the same endpoint and set the traffic configuration to 90% (v3) / 10% (v4), monitor via the inference table and endpoint metrics, and shift traffic (or set it back to 0%) by updating the config","B":"Delete the endpoint and recreate it with version 4","C":"Create a second endpoint with version 4 and ask 10% of client teams to change their URL","D":"Overwrite version 3\'s artifacts in Unity Catalog with version 4"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Model Serving endpoint can host several served entities (model versions) and split traffic between them by percentage. That is a canary rollout: a small share of real requests goes to the new version, its behaviour is compared through the inference table and endpoint metrics (latency, error rate), and traffic is moved gradually — or back to 0% for an immediate rollback — by updating the endpoint config, with no downtime and no client changes. Blue-green, by contrast, switches 100% at once between two full environments.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) recreating the endpoint causes downtime and has no gradual exposure. (C) making clients choose the URL is not a controlled traffic split. (D) Unity Catalog model versions are immutable; you never overwrite one.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"send X% of traffic to the new model\\" → traffic split between served entities on one endpoint (canary)."},{"id":"10","question":"A team wants retraining to start automatically when Lakehouse Monitoring detects significant drift, and the new model should only replace production if it beats the current one. Which design implements this on Databricks?","options":{"A":"Retrain every hour regardless of drift and always promote the newest model","B":"Ask the on-call engineer to read the drift dashboard daily and retrain manually","C":"Create a SQL alert on the drift metrics table with a threshold that triggers a Lakeflow Job; the job retrains a challenger, evaluates it against the current @champion on the same holdout data, and moves the champion alias in Unity Catalog only if it wins","D":"Delete the drift metrics table so alerts stop firing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The monitor writes drift statistics to a Delta table, so a Databricks SQL alert can watch a threshold (for example a KS p-value below 0.05 or a JS distance above a limit) and trigger a Lakeflow Job. The job retrains a challenger, compares it with the current champion on the same evaluation data and promotion criteria, and, if it is better, reassigns the @champion alias in the Unity Catalog model registry — serving that loads models by alias picks it up without code changes.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) blind periodic retraining wastes compute and can promote a worse model. (B) a manual daily check is slow and unreliable. (D) removing the signal hides the problem.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"retrain on drift\\" + \\"only if better\\" → alert-triggered job + champion/challenger with Unity Catalog aliases."},{"id":"11","question":"A team wants a lightweight Python service, running outside the cluster, to submit DataFrame queries to a remote Spark cluster without running a Spark driver locally, and to upgrade the server without breaking the client. Which Spark feature is designed for this, and what is one of its limitations?","options":{"A":"Spark Streaming DStreams; they only support Scala","B":"Spark Connect: a thin client sends unresolved logical plans over gRPC to the remote driver; the client uses the DataFrame API but cannot use the SparkContext or RDD APIs","C":"The Pandas API on Spark; it requires all data to fit on the client","D":"spark-submit in client mode; it must run on a cluster node"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Spark Connect decouples the client from the driver: the client library builds DataFrame operations as unresolved logical plans and sends them over gRPC to a Spark Connect server, which runs them on the cluster and streams Arrow-encoded results back. The client is small, can live in any application or IDE, and client and server can be upgraded more independently. The trade-off is that driver-side APIs such as SparkContext and RDDs are not available from the client.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) DStreams are the legacy streaming API and are unrelated. (C) the Pandas API on Spark distributes pandas-style code on the cluster; it does not require data on the client. (D) client-mode spark-submit runs a full driver on the submitting machine.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"remote/thin client\\", \\"gRPC\\", \\"no local driver\\" → Spark Connect (no RDD/SparkContext)."},{"id":"12","question":"A data scientist has a working pandas script that now fails with out-of-memory errors because the input grew to 300 GB. They want to scale it on a Spark cluster with the fewest code changes. What should they do?","options":{"A":"Call df.toPandas() on a Spark DataFrame so pandas runs on the driver","B":"Rewrite the whole script in RDD transformations","C":"Increase the driver memory to 300 GB and keep plain pandas","D":"Use the Pandas API on Spark (import pyspark.pandas as ps), which exposes a pandas-like API whose operations execute distributed on the cluster, converting with pandas_api() / to_spark() where Spark-specific steps are needed"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Pandas API on Spark (formerly Koalas) implements most of the pandas API on top of Spark DataFrames, so existing pandas code often runs distributed after changing the import to pyspark.pandas. It is possible to move between representations: spark_df.pandas_api() and psdf.to_spark(). Points to watch: the default index type adds cost (a distributed sequence index), and operations that need global order can be expensive.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) toPandas() collects all the data to the driver — the same out-of-memory problem. (B) RDDs are low-level and lose Catalyst optimizations; a full rewrite is the opposite of minimal changes. (C) a single-machine design does not scale and very large drivers are costly and fragile.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"existing pandas code\\" + \\"scale with minimal changes\\" → pyspark.pandas; \\"toPandas\\" on big data → OOM on the driver."},{"id":"13","question":"Every night, 2 million new support tickets in a Unity Catalog table must be classified by topic and summarized with an LLM, and the results written back to a table for BI. The team works mostly in SQL and wants no serving infrastructure of its own. What should they use?","options":{"A":"A Python loop in a notebook that calls a chat endpoint one ticket at a time from the driver","B":"A real-time agent deployed to Databricks Apps that users must open for each ticket","C":"Export the tickets to CSV and paste them into a chat UI","D":"The ai_query() SQL function (or task-specific AI Functions such as ai_classify and ai_summarize) in a scheduled Lakeflow Job, calling a Foundation Model API or serving endpoint in batch over the table"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ai_query() invokes a model serving endpoint (including Foundation Model APIs) directly from SQL or a DataFrame, row by row over a table, and Databricks parallelizes the calls for batch inference. Task-specific AI Functions (ai_classify, ai_summarize, ai_extract…) wrap common tasks. Scheduled in a Lakeflow Job, it writes results straight into a governed table — no serving stack to operate, and it fits a SQL-first team.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a driver-side loop is sequential, slow and fragile at 2 million rows. (B) an interactive app is for user-facing conversations, not nightly batch processing. (C) manual copy-paste neither scales nor is governed.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"batch LLM inference over a table\\" + SQL → ai_query() / AI Functions."},{"id":"14","question":"A RAG assistant searches 5 million document chunks stored in a Delta table. The documents are updated once a day, the budget is tight, and the team does not want to manage embedding computation. How should the Vector Search index be configured?","options":{"A":"A Delta Sync index with Databricks-managed embeddings (pointing at an embedding model endpoint) using the Triggered sync mode, refreshed once a day after the update job","B":"A Direct Vector Access index where the application computes embeddings and writes vectors through the API on every request","C":"A Delta Sync index in Continuous mode so it syncs within seconds","D":"No index: compute cosine similarity against all 5 million chunks in Python for each query"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A Delta Sync index keeps itself in sync with a source Delta table; with managed embeddings, Databricks computes the vectors using the embedding model endpoint you specify, so the team manages no embedding code. The sync mode decides cost and freshness: Triggered updates only when you start a sync (cheap, perfect for a daily update — just trigger it at the end of the update job), while Continuous keeps a pipeline running for seconds-level freshness at a higher cost.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) Direct Vector Access is for when you manage vectors yourself and write them via the API — the opposite of the requirement. (C) Continuous pays for freshness the use case does not need. (D) brute-force similarity over millions of chunks per query is slow and costly.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"source is a Delta table\\" + \\"don\'t manage embeddings\\" → Delta Sync + managed embeddings; \\"infrequent updates, low cost\\" → Triggered."},{"id":"15","question":"A customer-facing GenAI chatbot on a Databricks serving endpoint must block unsafe requests and responses and keep users\' personal data (emails, card numbers) from being sent to or returned by the model, with usage tracked centrally. What is the most direct way to add these guardrails?","options":{"A":"Fine-tune the model on polite conversations so it refuses everything harmful","B":"Ask users in the UI not to type personal data","C":"Configure AI Gateway on the endpoint with input/output guardrails (safety filtering and PII detection/masking), plus usage tracking, rate limits and payload logging to inference tables","D":"Increase the model\'s max_tokens so it can explain why a request is unsafe"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mosaic AI Gateway adds governance to serving endpoints: guardrails on requests and responses (safety filtering for unsafe content and PII detection that blocks or masks personal data), rate limits, usage tracking in system tables and payload logging to inference tables for auditing. It is applied by configuring the endpoint, without changing the model. It can be complemented with a dedicated guardrail model or prompt rules for domain-specific checks.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) fine-tuning is expensive and gives no guarantee; it is not a guardrail. (B) relying on user behaviour is not a control. (D) max_tokens only controls response length.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"guardrails\\", \\"PII\\", \\"unsafe content\\", \\"track usage\\" on an endpoint → AI Gateway."},{"id":"16","question":"An engineer logs a RAG chain with MLflow and tries to register it in Unity Catalog, but registration fails. The chain works when called in the notebook. What is the most likely missing piece?","options":{"A":"The chain must be converted to a Spark UDF before registration","B":"A model signature: Unity Catalog requires models to be logged with a signature (inferred from an input example or set explicitly), with the registry URI set to Unity Catalog","C":"The chain must first be deployed to a serving endpoint","D":"Unity Catalog only accepts scikit-learn models"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Registering to the Unity Catalog model registry (mlflow.set_registry_uri(\\"databricks-uc\\"), model name catalog.schema.model) requires the model to have a signature describing its inputs and outputs. The usual fix is to pass an input_example when logging so MLflow infers the signature, or to supply it explicitly. The signature is also what Model Serving uses to validate requests.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a Spark UDF is a way to use a model, not a registration requirement. (C) serving comes after registration, not before. (D) Unity Catalog accepts any MLflow flavor, including pyfunc, LangChain and custom chains.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"registration to Unity Catalog fails\\" → missing model signature / input example."},{"id":"17","question":"An agent must query an existing Genie space, call several Unity Catalog SQL functions and search a Vector Search index, all governed by Unity Catalog permissions, and the team does not want to host or maintain any MCP server code. Which option fits?","options":{"A":"Write a custom MCP server from scratch and host it on a VM outside Databricks","B":"Use Databricks managed MCP servers, which expose Genie spaces, Unity Catalog functions and Vector Search indexes as MCP tools with Unity Catalog permissions enforced","C":"Paste the table contents into the system prompt so no tools are needed","D":"Give the agent a personal access token with workspace admin rights"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Databricks offers three MCP options: managed MCP servers (ready-made servers for Genie spaces, Unity Catalog functions and Vector Search, with Unity Catalog permissions applied on behalf of the caller), external MCP servers (third-party servers reached through Unity Catalog connections) and custom MCP servers (your own code, typically hosted on Databricks Apps). When the tools already exist in Databricks and nobody wants to maintain server code, managed MCP servers are the direct fit.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a custom server is the most maintenance and moves governance outside Unity Catalog. (C) stuffing tables into the prompt wastes context, goes stale and bypasses permissions. (D) an admin token breaks least privilege.\\n\\nExam cue: Genie / UC functions / Vector Search as tools + \\"no server to maintain\\" → managed MCP; third-party service → external MCP via UC connection; bespoke logic → custom MCP on Databricks Apps."},{"id":"18","question":"A logistics agent answers questions such as \\"Where is shipment 8812 right now?\\". Shipment status in a Delta table changes every few minutes, and only a tiny fraction of shipments is asked about each day. Today the team embeds a nightly snapshot of all shipments into a vector index and retrieves from it up front, and answers are often stale. What should the context engineer change?","options":{"A":"Embed the snapshot every hour instead of nightly and keep pre-inference retrieval","B":"Put the full shipments table into the system prompt","C":"Increase the number of retrieved chunks from 5 to 50","D":"Switch to just-in-time agentic retrieval: give the agent a tool (for example a Unity Catalog SQL function) that queries the shipment\'s current status from the Delta table when the question needs it"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pre-inference retrieval (embedding-based, loaded up front) suits relatively stable knowledge such as manuals and policies. When the data is volatile, structured and only needed for some requests, just-in-time retrieval through a tool call is better: the agent decides it needs the status, calls a governed function that queries the live table, and gets an exact, current value with minimal context. It also avoids loading irrelevant data into the window.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) hourly embedding is still stale and re-embeds data almost nobody asks about. (B) a whole table in the prompt overflows and pollutes the context. (C) more chunks from a stale index adds noise, not freshness.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"changes frequently\\", \\"exact lookup by id\\", \\"needed only sometimes\\" → just-in-time tool retrieval; \\"stable documents, semantic questions\\" → pre-inference RAG."},{"id":"19","question":"A corpus of technical manuals has a clear structure (chapters, sections, numbered procedures and tables). The embedding model accepts at most 512 tokens per input, and users ask procedure-level questions (\\"How do I reset the controller?\\"). Retrieval currently uses fixed 2,000-character chunks and often returns half a procedure. Which chunking strategy should be chosen?","options":{"A":"Structure-aware chunking that splits on headings and procedure boundaries, keeps each chunk under the 512-token limit with a small overlap, and attaches the section title path as metadata","B":"One chunk per entire manual so nothing is ever split","C":"One chunk per sentence with no overlap","D":"Random fixed-size chunks of 4,000 tokens"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chunking should follow the document structure and the query granularity. Splitting on headings and procedure boundaries keeps a complete procedure together; respecting the embedding model\'s limit (512 tokens) avoids silent truncation of the chunk\'s tail; a small overlap preserves context across boundaries; and the section path as metadata improves both ranking and filtering, and gives the LLM provenance.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) whole manuals exceed the embedding limit (the rest is truncated) and dilute relevance. (C) single sentences lose the steps that belong together. (D) 4,000-token chunks are truncated to 512 by the embedding model, so most of each chunk is never represented.\\n\\nExam cue: structured documents + embedding token limit → structure-aware chunking within the limit, with overlap and metadata."},{"id":"20","question":"An agent has access to 120 MCP tools. Loading every tool\'s full JSON schema into the context at the start of each conversation consumes most of the token budget and the agent often picks the wrong tool. What approach reduces token usage while keeping task success?","options":{"A":"Load all 120 full schemas but write them in a smaller font","B":"Remove 100 tools permanently, keeping only the 20 most used","C":"Progressive disclosure: start with tool names and one-line descriptions (or a small discovery/search tool), and load a tool\'s full schema only after the agent selects it","D":"Merge all tools into one tool with 120 optional parameters"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Progressive disclosure stages how much tool information enters the context window: a compact index (names and short, distinct descriptions, or a search tool that finds relevant tools) is loaded first, and full parameter schemas are pulled in only for the tools actually chosen. Baseline context cost drops dramatically and tool selection becomes easier because the agent compares short, non-overlapping descriptions instead of wading through long schemas.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) presentation does not change token count. (B) permanently removing tools breaks the tasks that need them. (D) one mega-tool with 120 optional parameters makes selection and argument filling harder.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"too many tools / schemas fill the context\\" → progressive disclosure (staged tool discovery); \\"agent confuses two tools\\" → fix overlapping descriptions."},{"id":"21","question":"A shopping assistant should remember each user\'s stated preferences (sizes, favourite brands, dietary restrictions) across conversations held weeks apart, while the details of the current conversation only matter until it ends. Which memory design is appropriate on Databricks?","options":{"A":"Keep everything in the current conversation history and replay all past conversations into each new prompt","B":"Store preferences in a Python variable on the serving container","C":"Re-ask the user for their preferences at the start of every conversation","D":"Use short-term memory (the session\'s message history / checkpointed state) for the current conversation, and a durable long-term store backed by Lakebase for per-user preferences, retrieved by user id at the start of a session"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Memory should match the information need. Within a session, the message history (or checkpointed agent state) is the right source. Facts that must persist across sessions belong in a durable store: Lakebase (Databricks\' managed Postgres) can back long-term agent memory, keyed by user id, and be read at session start with a structured query — or searched semantically when memories are free text. Mixing the two causes either forgetting (session-only) or context pollution (replaying everything).\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) replaying every past conversation bloats the window and buries the relevant facts. (B) container memory disappears on restart or scale-out and is not shared between replicas. (C) asking again defeats personalization.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"across sessions\\" → durable store (Lakebase); \\"within the conversation\\" → session history/state."},{"id":"22","question":"Business users ask an AI/BI Genie space \\"What was net revenue last quarter?\\" and get numbers that differ from the finance report, because Genie sums the gross amount column and ignores refunds. The tables are correct. What is the most effective fix?","options":{"A":"Tell users to rephrase questions until the number looks right","B":"Curate the space: add column descriptions/synonyms in Unity Catalog, general instructions defining net revenue, and a trusted asset (an example SQL query or a SQL function) that computes net revenue the way finance does","C":"Add every table in the workspace to the Genie space so it has more data","D":"Replace Genie with a static dashboard and remove natural-language questions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Genie answers from the tables and context you curate. Accuracy depends on metadata and guidance: clear table and column comments (in Unity Catalog), instructions that define business terms, and example SQL queries or SQL functions marked as trusted assets for important metrics. When a question matches a trusted asset, Genie uses that vetted logic and labels the answer as trusted. Keeping the space focused on the right tables also reduces ambiguity.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) pushing the burden onto users does not fix the definition. (C) adding unrelated tables increases ambiguity and wrong joins. (D) abandons the self-service requirement instead of fixing it.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"Genie gives wrong or inconsistent metrics\\" → better metadata + instructions + trusted assets (example SQL / functions), and a focused set of tables."},{"id":"23","question":"An executive dashboard runs an expensive aggregation over 3 billion rows every time it is opened, and dozens of people open it each morning. The underlying data is loaded once per night. How should the analyst reduce cost and latency while keeping the numbers correct?","options":{"A":"Convert the query to a standard view so it is computed at read time","B":"Ask executives to open the dashboard less often","C":"Create a materialized view with the aggregation in Databricks SQL, refreshed on a schedule (or when the source table updates) after the nightly load, and point the dashboard datasets at it","D":"Export the result to a spreadsheet each morning and email it"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A materialized view stores the precomputed result and is refreshed by a managed pipeline — incrementally when possible — on a schedule or triggered after the source updates. Dashboard queries then read a small precomputed table instead of scanning billions of rows, which cuts warehouse cost and latency, and the numbers stay consistent with the nightly load. Databricks SQL\'s query result cache helps repeated identical queries too, but it does not survive data changes or eliminate the first expensive run.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a standard view is just a saved query; it recomputes on every read. (B) not an engineering solution. (D) spreadsheets lose governance, freshness and interactivity.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"expensive aggregation read many times\\" + \\"data changes on a known schedule\\" → materialized view; \\"always current, cheap query\\" → view."},{"id":"24","question":"A churn model uses customer features from a feature table in Unity Catalog that is updated daily. Training labels are events with timestamps from the past two years. Validation accuracy looks excellent but production accuracy is much lower; the team suspects the training set used feature values from after each label\'s event date. How should the training set be built?","options":{"A":"Declare the feature table as a time series feature table (primary keys plus a timestamp key) and create the training set with a point-in-time lookup, passing the label\'s timestamp, so each row only gets feature values known at that moment","B":"Join the labels with the latest snapshot of the feature table","C":"Shuffle the rows before training to remove the time effect","D":"Drop the timestamp column from the labels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is label leakage through features from the future. With Databricks Feature Engineering in Unity Catalog, a feature table with a timestamp key is a time series feature table; FeatureLookup with timestamp_lookup_key performs an as-of (point-in-time) join, returning for each label the most recent feature values at or before the label\'s timestamp. The same lookup logic is packaged with the model, keeping training and serving consistent.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) the latest snapshot is exactly what leaks future information into old labels. (C) shuffling rows does not change which feature values were joined. (D) removing the timestamp makes a correct as-of join impossible.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"great validation, poor production\\" + time-varying features → point-in-time lookup on a time series feature table."}]},{"id":"databricks-2026-es","title":"Databricks — objetivos de las gu\xedas 2026 (ES)","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"Una tabla Delta de eventos de 40 TB se consulta filtrando por customer_id (alta cardinalidad) y event_date, y las columnas de filtro que usa el negocio cambian cada trimestre. Hoy est\xe1 particionada por event_date y se le hace ZORDER semanal, lo que es lento de mantener y genera muchas particiones peque\xf1as. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque reduce el mantenimiento y se adapta mejor al patr\xf3n de acceso?","options":{"A":"Reparticionar la tabla por customer_id para que cada cliente tenga su propio directorio","B":"Convertirla en tabla gestionada de Unity Catalog con Liquid Clustering (CLUSTER BY AUTO o claves expl\xedcitas) y dejar que Predictive Optimization ejecute OPTIMIZE autom\xe1ticamente","C":"Mantener las particiones y ejecutar ZORDER BY sobre todas las columnas despu\xe9s de cada carga","D":"Desactivar las estad\xedsticas de data skipping para acelerar las escrituras"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Liquid Clustering sustituye al particionado estilo Hive y a ZORDER: las claves de clustering se pueden cambiar sin reescribir la tabla, admite columnas de alta cardinalidad sin crear particiones diminutas y el clustering es incremental. Con CLUSTER BY AUTO en una tabla gestionada de Unity Catalog, Databricks elige y ajusta las claves seg\xfan las consultas reales, y Predictive Optimization ejecuta OPTIMIZE/VACUUM por ti.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) particionar por una columna de alta cardinalidad crea millones de directorios diminutos (el cl\xe1sico problema de ficheros peque\xf1os). (C) ZORDER sobre muchas columnas diluye su efecto y hay que relanzarlo como mantenimiento completo. (D) las estad\xedsticas son las que permiten el data skipping; quitarlas hace las lecturas m\xe1s lentas.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabalta cardinalidad\xbb, \xabpatrones de consulta cambiantes\xbb, \xabmenos mantenimiento\xbb → Liquid Clustering (+ CLUSTER BY AUTO / Predictive Optimization)."},{"id":"2","question":"Una tabla Delta silver recibe MERGE con inserciones, actualizaciones y borrados. Un pipeline gold vuelve a leer cada hora toda la tabla silver para recalcular agregados, lo que es caro. \xbfC\xf3mo debe exponer el ingeniero solo los cambios a nivel de fila para que el job posterior los procese de forma incremental?","options":{"A":"Ejecutar DESCRIBE HISTORY sobre la tabla silver y parsear las m\xe9tricas de cada operaci\xf3n","B":"Comparar la tabla completa con una copia tomada una hora antes usando EXCEPT","C":"Usar VACUUM para borrar los ficheros antiguos y que solo queden las filas nuevas","D":"Activar Change Data Feed en la tabla silver (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true) y leer los cambios con readChangeFeed / table_changes(), usando _change_type para aplicar inserciones, actualizaciones y borrados"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Change Data Feed registra los cambios a nivel de fila hechos despu\xe9s de activarlo. El lector recibe cada fila cambiada con las columnas _change_type (insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete), _commit_version y _commit_timestamp, en batch (table_changes(\'tbl\', versionInicial)) o en streaming (opci\xf3n readChangeFeed = true). El job gold procesa solo lo que cambi\xf3, incluidos los borrados, que un stream de solo append no ve.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) DESCRIBE HISTORY da m\xe9tricas por commit, no las filas cambiadas. (B) un EXCEPT completo es tan caro como la relectura que se quiere evitar. (C) VACUUM borra ficheros antiguos no referenciados; no filtra filas y rompe el time travel.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabpropagar updates y deletes de forma incremental\xbb → Change Data Feed."},{"id":"3","question":"Una empresa tiene cientos de tablas en varios cat\xe1logos con columnas de email y tel\xe9fono. Seguridad quiere enmascararlas para todos salvo el equipo de privacidad, aplic\xe1ndolo autom\xe1ticamente a cualquier tabla nueva con esos datos y sin asociar una funci\xf3n de m\xe1scara a cada columna una por una. \xbfQu\xe9 debe usar el ingeniero?","options":{"A":"Control de acceso basado en atributos (ABAC) de Unity Catalog: etiquetar las columnas con un governed tag (por ejemplo pii=email) y definir una pol\xedtica a nivel de cat\xe1logo o esquema que aplique una funci\xf3n de m\xe1scara a toda columna con esa etiqueta","B":"Crear una copia de cada tabla sin esas columnas y dar acceso a las copias","C":"A\xf1adir un WHERE a cada consulta de los dashboards para ocultar las columnas","D":"Poner un DENY en cada columna para todos los usuarios"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Las pol\xedticas ABAC de Unity Catalog combinan governed tags con funciones de filtro de filas y m\xe1scara de columnas. Una pol\xedtica a nivel de cat\xe1logo o esquema dice, por ejemplo, \xaba las columnas con pii=email aplica mask_email() salvo que el usuario est\xe9 en privacy_team\xbb; se aplica a tablas existentes y nuevas en cuanto las columnas se etiquetan (el etiquetado puede apoyarse en la clasificaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de datos). Escala mucho mejor que ALTER TABLE … SET MASK columna a columna.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) las copias multiplican almacenamiento, se desincronizan y son dif\xedciles de gobernar. (C) ocultar en la consulta se lo salta cualquiera que consulte la tabla directamente. (D) en Unity Catalog los privilegios se dan sobre securables como tablas, no columnas sueltas, y un DENY ocultar\xeda la columna en vez de mostrar el valor enmascarado.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xaba escala\xbb, \xabautom\xe1tico en tablas nuevas\xbb, \xabseg\xfan etiquetas\xbb → pol\xedticas ABAC con governed tags; caso puntual → m\xe1scara por columna."},{"id":"4","question":"Ante una solicitud de derecho al olvido del RGPD, hay que eliminar las filas de un cliente de una tabla Delta de forma que no se puedan recuperar. La tabla tiene deletion vectors activados y una retenci\xf3n de 7 d\xedas. \xbfQu\xe9 secuencia elimina f\xedsicamente los datos?","options":{"A":"Ejecutar solo DELETE; las filas desaparecen del almacenamiento al momento","B":"Ejecutar RESTORE TABLE a una versi\xf3n anterior a la inserci\xf3n del cliente","C":"Ejecutar DELETE, luego REORG TABLE … APPLY (PURGE) para reescribir los ficheros que a\xfan tienen las filas borradas l\xf3gicamente, y despu\xe9s VACUUM una vez pasado el periodo de retenci\xf3n para eliminar los ficheros antiguos (y el time travel a ellos)","D":"Eliminar la columna del cliente del esquema de la tabla"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Con deletion vectors, DELETE solo marca las filas como borradas; los datos siguen en los ficheros Parquet. REORG TABLE … APPLY (PURGE) reescribe esos ficheros sin las filas borradas. Aun as\xed, las versiones anteriores siguen apuntando a los ficheros viejos, as\xed que hay que ejecutar VACUUM pasado el periodo de retenci\xf3n para borrarlos; a partir de ah\xed ya no se puede hacer time travel a versiones con el cliente. Los ficheros de Change Data Feed, si est\xe1 activo, tambi\xe9n los limpia VACUUM.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) DELETE solo es un borrado l\xf3gico; los bytes siguen recuperables con time travel. (B) RESTORE descartar\xeda adem\xe1s todos los cambios leg\xedtimos posteriores. (D) quitar una columna no elimina las filas de un cliente y, con column mapping, ni siquiera reescribe los datos.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabderecho al olvido\xbb en Delta → DELETE + (REORG PURGE si hay deletion vectors) + VACUUM tras la retenci\xf3n."},{"id":"5","question":"El grupo de anal\xedtica debe poder leer todas las tablas actuales y futuras del esquema sales.reporting, y nada m\xe1s del cat\xe1logo sales. \xbfCu\xe1l es el conjunto m\xednimo de permisos de Unity Catalog?","options":{"A":"GRANT SELECT en cada tabla existente de sales.reporting, repiti\xe9ndolo cada vez que se cree una tabla","B":"GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON CATALOG sales","C":"GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG sales, GRANT USE SCHEMA ON SCHEMA sales.reporting y GRANT SELECT ON SCHEMA sales.reporting","D":"GRANT SELECT ON CATALOG sales"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los privilegios de Unity Catalog se heredan hacia abajo: un SELECT sobre un esquema se aplica a todas sus tablas y vistas actuales y futuras. Para llegar a cualquier objeto, el principal necesita adem\xe1s USE CATALOG en el cat\xe1logo padre y USE SCHEMA en el esquema padre; por s\xed solos no dan acceso a datos, solo permiten atravesar la jerarqu\xeda. Juntos dan exactamente lectura sobre sales.reporting.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) los permisos por tabla funcionan pero no cubren tablas futuras y hay que mantenerlos a mano. (B) ALL PRIVILEGES en el cat\xe1logo da mucho m\xe1s que lectura. (D) SELECT en el cat\xe1logo expone todos los esquemas de sales, no solo reporting (y sigue haciendo falta USE CATALOG/USE SCHEMA).\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabtablas actuales y futuras\xbb → permiso a nivel de esquema; acu\xe9rdate siempre de USE CATALOG + USE SCHEMA."},{"id":"6","question":"Un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n servido con Databricks Model Serving registra peticiones y respuestas en una inference table. Las etiquetas reales llegan dos d\xedas despu\xe9s y se unen a esa tabla. El equipo quiere dashboards de accuracy y de drift a lo largo del tiempo, por regi\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 debe configurar?","options":{"A":"Un monitor de Lakehouse Monitoring (data profiling) con el perfil InferenceLog sobre la inference table, indicando las columnas de predicci\xf3n, etiqueta, id de modelo y timestamp, expresiones de slicing por regi\xf3n y una granularidad temporal","B":"Un perfil Snapshot solo sobre la tabla de entrenamiento","C":"Una alerta SQL que cuente filas de la inference table cada hora","D":"MLflow autologging en el endpoint de serving"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lakehouse Monitoring tiene tres perfiles: Snapshot (la tabla entera en cada refresco), TimeSeries (ventanas por una columna de tiempo) e InferenceLog (serie temporal m\xe1s columnas de modelo: predicci\xf3n, etiqueta, id de modelo). Con InferenceLog sobre la inference table, el monitor calcula m\xe9tricas de calidad del modelo cuando hay etiquetas (accuracy, precision, recall, F1 en clasificaci\xf3n) y m\xe9tricas de drift por ventana, y las expresiones de slicing lo desglosan por regi\xf3n. El resultado va a las tablas de m\xe9tricas de perfil y de drift y a un dashboard generado.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) perfilar solo la tabla de entrenamiento no dice nada de producci\xf3n. (C) contar filas es una se\xf1al de salud, no accuracy ni drift. (D) autologging captura ejecuciones de entrenamiento, no el rendimiento en producci\xf3n.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabrendimiento del modelo en el tiempo a partir de predicciones servidas\xbb → monitor InferenceLog sobre la inference table."},{"id":"7","question":"En la tabla de m\xe9tricas de drift de Lakehouse Monitoring, un cient\xedfico de datos quiere comprobar si la distribuci\xf3n de una variable num\xe9rica continua (transaction_amount) en la ventana actual difiere significativamente de la l\xednea base. \xbfQu\xe9 estad\xedstico est\xe1 pensado para esa comparaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"El test chi-cuadrado, porque compara directamente dos distribuciones continuas","B":"El n\xfamero de nulos en la ventana actual","C":"La moda de transaction_amount en cada ventana","D":"El test de Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS), que compara dos distribuciones num\xe9ricas y devuelve un estad\xedstico y un p-valor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las m\xe9tricas de drift de Lakehouse Monitoring incluyen tests estad\xedsticos seg\xfan el tipo de dato: Kolmogorov-Smirnov de dos muestras para columnas num\xe9ricas y chi-cuadrado para columnas categ\xf3ricas, cada uno con estad\xedstico y p-valor, adem\xe1s de distancias como Jensen-Shannon, Wasserstein (num\xe9rica), variaci\xf3n total y population stability index. Un p-valor KS peque\xf1o indica que la distribuci\xf3n actual difiere significativamente de la base (o de la ventana anterior).\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) chi-cuadrado compara frecuencias de categor\xedas; los valores continuos habr\xeda que agruparlos antes. (B) contar nulos mide calidad de datos, no cambio de distribuci\xf3n. (C) una moda ignora la forma de la distribuci\xf3n.\\n\\nPista de examen: drift num\xe9rico → test KS (o Wasserstein); drift categ\xf3rico → chi-cuadrado (o distancia JS)."},{"id":"8","question":"Un equipo regulado no puede permitir que los datos de producci\xf3n se lean desde el workspace de desarrollo, pero los modelos de producci\xf3n deben entrenarse con datos de producci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 patr\xf3n de despliegue MLOps recomendado por Databricks encaja?","options":{"A":"Deploy models: entrenar en dev con una copia de datos de producci\xf3n y promover el artefacto del modelo a prod","B":"Deploy code: promover el c\xf3digo de entrenamiento dev → staging → prod con CI/CD y ejecutar el pipeline de entrenamiento en el entorno de producci\xf3n con datos de producci\xf3n","C":"Entrenar en un notebook personal y subir el fichero pickle al endpoint de prod","D":"Saltarse staging y desplegar directamente desde una rama de feature"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La gu\xeda MLOps de Databricks distingue \xabdeploy models\xbb (se promueve el artefacto entrenado entre entornos) de \xabdeploy code\xbb (se promueve el c\xf3digo y el modelo se reentrena en cada entorno). Con deploy code, el pipeline se prueba en dev y staging y el modelo de producci\xf3n se entrena dentro de prod, as\xed que los datos de producci\xf3n nunca tienen que ser accesibles desde dev. Es la opci\xf3n recomendada por defecto y encaja en entornos regulados; deploy models es m\xe1s simple cuando entrenar es muy caro o solo puede hacerse una vez.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) copiar datos de producci\xf3n a dev rompe la restricci\xf3n de acceso. (C) subir a mano se salta las pruebas, el linaje y el registro. (D) saltarse staging elimina la puerta de pruebas de integraci\xf3n.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xablos datos de prod no se pueden leer desde dev\xbb → deploy code; \xabentrenar es demasiado caro para repetirlo\xbb → deploy models."},{"id":"9","question":"Un endpoint de recomendaciones con mucho tr\xe1fico en Databricks Model Serving sirve la versi\xf3n 3 del modelo. El equipo quiere exponer la versi\xf3n 4 al 10 % del tr\xe1fico real, comparar resultados y volver atr\xe1s al instante si suben los errores. \xbfC\xf3mo lo implementa?","options":{"A":"A\xf1adir la versi\xf3n 4 como segunda served entity en el mismo endpoint y fijar la configuraci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico en 90 % (v3) / 10 % (v4), vigilar con la inference table y las m\xe9tricas del endpoint, y mover el tr\xe1fico (o devolverlo a 0 %) actualizando la configuraci\xf3n","B":"Borrar el endpoint y crearlo de nuevo con la versi\xf3n 4","C":"Crear un segundo endpoint con la versi\xf3n 4 y pedir al 10 % de los equipos cliente que cambien su URL","D":"Sobrescribir los artefactos de la versi\xf3n 3 en Unity Catalog con los de la versi\xf3n 4"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Un endpoint de Model Serving puede alojar varias served entities (versiones de modelo) y repartir el tr\xe1fico entre ellas por porcentaje. Es un despliegue canary: una parte peque\xf1a de las peticiones reales va a la versi\xf3n nueva, se compara su comportamiento con la inference table y las m\xe9tricas del endpoint (latencia, tasa de error) y el tr\xe1fico se mueve poco a poco —o vuelve a 0 % para un rollback inmediato— actualizando la configuraci\xf3n, sin ca\xedda y sin cambios en los clientes. Blue-green, en cambio, cambia el 100 % de golpe entre dos entornos completos.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) recrear el endpoint provoca ca\xedda y no hay exposici\xf3n gradual. (C) que el cliente elija URL no es un reparto de tr\xe1fico controlado. (D) las versiones de modelo en Unity Catalog son inmutables; no se sobrescriben.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabenviar el X % del tr\xe1fico al modelo nuevo\xbb → reparto de tr\xe1fico entre served entities de un endpoint (canary)."},{"id":"10","question":"Un equipo quiere que el reentrenamiento arranque solo cuando Lakehouse Monitoring detecte un drift significativo, y que el modelo nuevo solo sustituya al de producci\xf3n si lo supera. \xbfQu\xe9 dise\xf1o lo implementa en Databricks?","options":{"A":"Reentrenar cada hora haya drift o no y promover siempre el modelo m\xe1s reciente","B":"Pedir al ingeniero de guardia que mire el dashboard de drift cada d\xeda y reentrene a mano","C":"Crear una alerta SQL sobre la tabla de m\xe9tricas de drift con un umbral que dispare un Lakeflow Job; el job reentrena un challenger, lo eval\xfaa contra el @champion actual con los mismos datos de validaci\xf3n y mueve el alias champion en Unity Catalog solo si gana","D":"Borrar la tabla de m\xe9tricas de drift para que dejen de saltar las alertas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El monitor escribe las m\xe9tricas de drift en una tabla Delta, as\xed que una alerta de Databricks SQL puede vigilar un umbral (por ejemplo p-valor KS menor que 0,05 o distancia JS por encima de un l\xedmite) y disparar un Lakeflow Job. El job reentrena un challenger, lo compara con el champion actual sobre los mismos datos y criterios, y, si es mejor, reasigna el alias @champion en el registro de modelos de Unity Catalog; el serving que carga modelos por alias lo recoge sin cambiar c\xf3digo.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) reentrenar a ciegas gasta c\xf3mputo y puede promover un modelo peor. (B) una revisi\xf3n manual diaria es lenta y poco fiable. (D) quitar la se\xf1al esconde el problema.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabreentrenar por drift\xbb + \xabsolo si es mejor\xbb → job disparado por alerta + champion/challenger con alias de Unity Catalog."},{"id":"11","question":"Un equipo quiere que un servicio Python ligero, fuera del cl\xfaster, env\xede consultas DataFrame a un cl\xfaster Spark remoto sin ejecutar un driver local, y poder actualizar el servidor sin romper el cliente. \xbfQu\xe9 funcionalidad de Spark est\xe1 pensada para esto y cu\xe1l es una de sus limitaciones?","options":{"A":"Los DStreams de Spark Streaming; solo admiten Scala","B":"Spark Connect: un cliente ligero env\xeda planes l\xf3gicos sin resolver por gRPC al driver remoto; el cliente usa la API DataFrame pero no puede usar SparkContext ni la API de RDD","C":"La Pandas API on Spark; exige que todos los datos quepan en el cliente","D":"spark-submit en modo client; tiene que ejecutarse en un nodo del cl\xfaster"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Spark Connect desacopla el cliente del driver: la librer\xeda cliente construye las operaciones DataFrame como planes l\xf3gicos sin resolver y los env\xeda por gRPC a un servidor Spark Connect, que los ejecuta en el cl\xfaster y devuelve los resultados en Arrow. El cliente es peque\xf1o, puede vivir en cualquier aplicaci\xf3n o IDE, y cliente y servidor se actualizan con m\xe1s independencia. A cambio, las APIs del lado del driver como SparkContext y los RDD no est\xe1n disponibles desde el cliente.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) los DStreams son la API antigua de streaming y no tienen relaci\xf3n. (C) la Pandas API on Spark distribuye c\xf3digo estilo pandas en el cl\xfaster; no exige los datos en el cliente. (D) spark-submit en modo client ejecuta un driver completo en la m\xe1quina que lanza.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabcliente remoto/ligero\xbb, \xabgRPC\xbb, \xabsin driver local\xbb → Spark Connect (sin RDD/SparkContext)."},{"id":"12","question":"Un cient\xedfico de datos tiene un script de pandas que ahora falla por falta de memoria porque la entrada creci\xf3 a 300 GB. Quiere escalarlo en un cl\xfaster Spark con los m\xednimos cambios de c\xf3digo. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer?","options":{"A":"Llamar a df.toPandas() sobre un DataFrame de Spark para que pandas se ejecute en el driver","B":"Reescribir todo el script con transformaciones de RDD","C":"Subir la memoria del driver a 300 GB y seguir con pandas normal","D":"Usar la Pandas API on Spark (import pyspark.pandas as ps), que ofrece una API tipo pandas cuyas operaciones se ejecutan distribuidas en el cl\xfaster, convirtiendo con pandas_api() / to_spark() donde haga falta un paso espec\xedfico de Spark"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La Pandas API on Spark (antes Koalas) implementa la mayor parte de la API de pandas sobre DataFrames de Spark, as\xed que el c\xf3digo pandas existente suele ejecutarse distribuido cambiando el import a pyspark.pandas. Se puede pasar de una representaci\xf3n a otra: spark_df.pandas_api() y psdf.to_spark(). A vigilar: el tipo de \xedndice por defecto a\xf1ade coste (un \xedndice secuencial distribuido) y las operaciones que necesitan un orden global pueden ser caras.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) toPandas() trae todos los datos al driver: el mismo problema de memoria. (B) los RDD son de bajo nivel y pierden las optimizaciones de Catalyst; reescribirlo todo es lo contrario de cambios m\xednimos. (C) un dise\xf1o de una sola m\xe1quina no escala y los drivers enormes son caros y fr\xe1giles.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabc\xf3digo pandas existente\xbb + \xabescalar con cambios m\xednimos\xbb → pyspark.pandas; \xabtoPandas\xbb con muchos datos → OOM en el driver."},{"id":"13","question":"Cada noche hay que clasificar por tema y resumir con un LLM 2 millones de tickets de soporte nuevos de una tabla de Unity Catalog, y escribir el resultado en una tabla para BI. El equipo trabaja sobre todo en SQL y no quiere infraestructura de serving propia. \xbfQu\xe9 debe usar?","options":{"A":"Un bucle de Python en un notebook que llame a un endpoint de chat ticket a ticket desde el driver","B":"Un agente en tiempo real desplegado en Databricks Apps que los usuarios abran para cada ticket","C":"Exportar los tickets a CSV y pegarlos en una interfaz de chat","D":"La funci\xf3n SQL ai_query() (o AI Functions espec\xedficas como ai_classify y ai_summarize) en un Lakeflow Job programado, llamando en batch sobre la tabla a una Foundation Model API o a un endpoint de serving"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ai_query() invoca un endpoint de model serving (incluidas las Foundation Model APIs) directamente desde SQL o desde un DataFrame, fila a fila sobre una tabla, y Databricks paraleliza las llamadas para inferencia por lotes. Las AI Functions espec\xedficas (ai_classify, ai_summarize, ai_extract…) envuelven tareas comunes. Programado en un Lakeflow Job, escribe el resultado directamente en una tabla gobernada, sin stack de serving que operar, y encaja con un equipo que trabaja en SQL.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) un bucle en el driver es secuencial, lento y fr\xe1gil con 2 millones de filas. (B) una app interactiva es para conversaciones con usuarios, no para procesos nocturnos por lotes. (C) copiar y pegar a mano ni escala ni est\xe1 gobernado.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabinferencia LLM por lotes sobre una tabla\xbb + SQL → ai_query() / AI Functions."},{"id":"14","question":"Un asistente RAG busca en 5 millones de fragmentos de documentos guardados en una tabla Delta. Los documentos se actualizan una vez al d\xeda, el presupuesto es ajustado y el equipo no quiere gestionar el c\xe1lculo de embeddings. \xbfC\xf3mo debe configurarse el \xedndice de Vector Search?","options":{"A":"Un \xedndice Delta Sync con embeddings gestionados por Databricks (apuntando a un endpoint de modelo de embeddings) en modo de sincronizaci\xf3n Triggered, refrescado una vez al d\xeda tras el job de actualizaci\xf3n","B":"Un \xedndice Direct Vector Access en el que la aplicaci\xf3n calcula los embeddings y escribe los vectores por la API en cada petici\xf3n","C":"Un \xedndice Delta Sync en modo Continuous para que sincronice en segundos","D":"Sin \xedndice: calcular la similitud coseno contra los 5 millones de fragmentos en Python en cada consulta"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Un \xedndice Delta Sync se mantiene sincronizado con una tabla Delta de origen; con embeddings gestionados, Databricks calcula los vectores con el endpoint de embeddings que indiques, as\xed que el equipo no gestiona c\xf3digo de embeddings. El modo de sincronizaci\xf3n decide coste y frescura: Triggered actualiza solo cuando lanzas la sincronizaci\xf3n (barato, ideal para una actualizaci\xf3n diaria: basta con dispararla al final del job), mientras que Continuous mantiene un pipeline en marcha para frescura de segundos a mayor coste.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) Direct Vector Access es para cuando gestionas t\xfa los vectores y los escribes por la API: lo contrario del requisito. (C) Continuous paga una frescura que el caso no necesita. (D) la similitud por fuerza bruta sobre millones de fragmentos en cada consulta es lenta y cara.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabel origen es una tabla Delta\xbb + \xabno gestionar embeddings\xbb → Delta Sync + embeddings gestionados; \xabpocas actualizaciones, bajo coste\xbb → Triggered."},{"id":"15","question":"Un chatbot de IA generativa para clientes, sobre un endpoint de serving de Databricks, debe bloquear peticiones y respuestas inseguras y evitar que los datos personales de los usuarios (emails, n\xfameros de tarjeta) se env\xeden al modelo o salgan de \xe9l, con el uso controlado de forma central. \xbfCu\xe1l es la forma m\xe1s directa de a\xf1adir estas barreras?","options":{"A":"Hacer fine-tuning del modelo con conversaciones educadas para que rechace todo lo da\xf1ino","B":"Pedir a los usuarios en la interfaz que no escriban datos personales","C":"Configurar AI Gateway en el endpoint con guardrails de entrada/salida (filtro de seguridad y detecci\xf3n/enmascarado de PII), m\xe1s seguimiento de uso, l\xedmites de peticiones y registro de payloads en inference tables","D":"Subir el max_tokens del modelo para que pueda explicar por qu\xe9 una petici\xf3n es insegura"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mosaic AI Gateway a\xf1ade gobierno a los endpoints de serving: guardrails sobre peticiones y respuestas (filtro de contenido inseguro y detecci\xf3n de PII que bloquea o enmascara datos personales), l\xedmites de peticiones, seguimiento del uso en system tables y registro de payloads en inference tables para auditor\xeda. Se aplica configurando el endpoint, sin tocar el modelo. Se puede complementar con un modelo guardi\xe1n propio o reglas en el prompt para comprobaciones del dominio.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) el fine-tuning es caro y no garantiza nada; no es un guardrail. (B) confiar en el usuario no es un control. (D) max_tokens solo limita la longitud de la respuesta.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabguardrails\xbb, \xabPII\xbb, \xabcontenido inseguro\xbb, \xabcontrolar uso\xbb en un endpoint → AI Gateway."},{"id":"16","question":"Un ingeniero registra con MLflow una cadena RAG e intenta darla de alta en Unity Catalog, pero el registro falla. La cadena funciona al llamarla en el notebook. \xbfQu\xe9 es lo m\xe1s probable que falte?","options":{"A":"Hay que convertir la cadena en una Spark UDF antes de registrarla","B":"Una firma (signature) del modelo: Unity Catalog exige que el modelo se registre con firma (inferida de un input example o definida expl\xedcitamente), con el registry URI apuntando a Unity Catalog","C":"Hay que desplegar primero la cadena en un endpoint de serving","D":"Unity Catalog solo acepta modelos de scikit-learn"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Registrar en el registro de modelos de Unity Catalog (mlflow.set_registry_uri(\\"databricks-uc\\"), nombre catalogo.esquema.modelo) exige que el modelo tenga una firma que describa entradas y salidas. Lo habitual es pasar un input_example al hacer el log para que MLflow infiera la firma, o definirla expl\xedcitamente. La firma es tambi\xe9n lo que usa Model Serving para validar peticiones.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) una Spark UDF es una forma de usar el modelo, no un requisito de registro. (C) el serving va despu\xe9s del registro, no antes. (D) Unity Catalog acepta cualquier flavor de MLflow, incluidos pyfunc, LangChain y cadenas propias.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabfalla el registro en Unity Catalog\xbb → falta la firma / input example."},{"id":"17","question":"Un agente debe consultar un Genie space existente, llamar a varias funciones SQL de Unity Catalog y buscar en un \xedndice de Vector Search, todo gobernado por los permisos de Unity Catalog, y el equipo no quiere alojar ni mantener c\xf3digo de servidores MCP. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n encaja?","options":{"A":"Escribir un servidor MCP propio desde cero y alojarlo en una VM fuera de Databricks","B":"Usar los servidores MCP gestionados de Databricks, que exponen Genie spaces, funciones de Unity Catalog e \xedndices de Vector Search como herramientas MCP aplicando los permisos de Unity Catalog","C":"Pegar el contenido de las tablas en el system prompt para no necesitar herramientas","D":"Dar al agente un token personal con permisos de administrador del workspace"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Databricks ofrece tres opciones MCP: servidores MCP gestionados (listos para Genie spaces, funciones de Unity Catalog y Vector Search, aplicando los permisos de Unity Catalog de quien llama), servidores MCP externos (de terceros, accesibles mediante conexiones de Unity Catalog) y servidores MCP propios (tu c\xf3digo, normalmente alojado en Databricks Apps). Cuando las herramientas ya existen en Databricks y nadie quiere mantener c\xf3digo de servidor, los gestionados son el encaje directo.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) un servidor propio es lo que m\xe1s mantenimiento exige y saca el gobierno de Unity Catalog. (C) meter tablas en el prompt gasta contexto, se queda obsoleto y se salta los permisos. (D) un token de administrador rompe el m\xednimo privilegio.\\n\\nPista de examen: Genie / funciones UC / Vector Search como herramientas + \xabsin servidor que mantener\xbb → MCP gestionado; servicio de terceros → MCP externo v\xeda conexi\xf3n UC; l\xf3gica a medida → MCP propio en Databricks Apps."},{"id":"18","question":"Un agente de log\xedstica responde preguntas como \xab\xbfD\xf3nde est\xe1 ahora el env\xedo 8812?\xbb. El estado de los env\xedos, en una tabla Delta, cambia cada pocos minutos, y cada d\xeda solo se pregunta por una parte m\xednima de los env\xedos. Hoy el equipo embebe cada noche una foto de todos los env\xedos en un \xedndice vectorial y recupera de \xe9l por adelantado, y las respuestas suelen estar desfasadas. \xbfQu\xe9 debe cambiar el context engineer?","options":{"A":"Embeber la foto cada hora en lugar de cada noche y mantener la recuperaci\xf3n previa a la inferencia","B":"Meter la tabla completa de env\xedos en el system prompt","C":"Subir el n\xfamero de fragmentos recuperados de 5 a 50","D":"Pasar a recuperaci\xf3n ag\xe9ntica just-in-time: dar al agente una herramienta (por ejemplo, una funci\xf3n SQL de Unity Catalog) que consulte el estado actual del env\xedo en la tabla Delta cuando la pregunta lo necesite"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La recuperaci\xf3n previa a la inferencia (por embeddings, cargada por adelantado) sirve para conocimiento relativamente estable, como manuales y pol\xedticas. Cuando el dato es vol\xe1til, estructurado y solo hace falta en algunas peticiones, es mejor la recuperaci\xf3n just-in-time con una llamada a herramienta: el agente decide que necesita el estado, llama a una funci\xf3n gobernada que consulta la tabla viva y obtiene un valor exacto y actual con el m\xednimo contexto. Adem\xe1s evita cargar en la ventana datos irrelevantes.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) embeber cada hora sigue desfasado y re-embebe datos por los que casi nadie pregunta. (B) una tabla entera en el prompt desborda y contamina el contexto. (C) m\xe1s fragmentos de un \xedndice desfasado a\xf1aden ruido, no frescura.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabcambia a menudo\xbb, \xabb\xfasqueda exacta por id\xbb, \xabsolo se necesita a veces\xbb → herramienta just-in-time; \xabdocumentos estables, preguntas sem\xe1nticas\xbb → RAG previo a la inferencia."},{"id":"19","question":"Un corpus de manuales t\xe9cnicos tiene una estructura clara (cap\xedtulos, secciones, procedimientos numerados y tablas). El modelo de embeddings admite como m\xe1ximo 512 tokens por entrada y los usuarios preguntan a nivel de procedimiento (\xab\xbfC\xf3mo reinicio el controlador?\xbb). La recuperaci\xf3n usa hoy fragmentos fijos de 2.000 caracteres y a menudo devuelve medio procedimiento. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de chunking debe elegirse?","options":{"A":"Chunking consciente de la estructura que corte por t\xedtulos y l\xedmites de procedimiento, mantenga cada fragmento bajo el l\xedmite de 512 tokens con un peque\xf1o solapamiento y a\xf1ada como metadato la ruta de t\xedtulos de la secci\xf3n","B":"Un fragmento por manual completo para no cortar nunca nada","C":"Un fragmento por frase y sin solapamiento","D":"Fragmentos aleatorios de tama\xf1o fijo de 4.000 tokens"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El chunking debe seguir la estructura del documento y la granularidad de las preguntas. Cortar por t\xedtulos y l\xedmites de procedimiento mantiene juntos los procedimientos completos; respetar el l\xedmite del modelo de embeddings (512 tokens) evita que se trunque en silencio el final del fragmento; un peque\xf1o solapamiento conserva el contexto entre fragmentos; y la ruta de la secci\xf3n como metadato mejora el ranking y el filtrado y da trazabilidad al LLM.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) un manual entero supera el l\xedmite de embeddings (el resto se trunca) y diluye la relevancia. (C) frases sueltas pierden los pasos que van juntos. (D) fragmentos de 4.000 tokens se truncan a 512 en el modelo de embeddings, as\xed que casi todo el fragmento no queda representado.\\n\\nPista de examen: documentos estructurados + l\xedmite de tokens del embedding → chunking por estructura dentro del l\xedmite, con solapamiento y metadatos."},{"id":"20","question":"Un agente tiene acceso a 120 herramientas MCP. Cargar el esquema JSON completo de todas al inicio de cada conversaci\xf3n consume casi todo el presupuesto de tokens y el agente elige a menudo la herramienta equivocada. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque reduce los tokens sin perder \xe9xito en las tareas?","options":{"A":"Cargar los 120 esquemas completos pero con una letra m\xe1s peque\xf1a","B":"Eliminar para siempre 100 herramientas y quedarse con las 20 m\xe1s usadas","C":"Divulgaci\xf3n progresiva (progressive disclosure): empezar con nombres y descripciones de una l\xednea (o una peque\xf1a herramienta de b\xfasqueda) y cargar el esquema completo de una herramienta solo cuando el agente la elige","D":"Fusionar todas las herramientas en una sola con 120 par\xe1metros opcionales"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La divulgaci\xf3n progresiva escalona cu\xe1nta informaci\xf3n de herramientas entra en la ventana de contexto: primero un \xedndice compacto (nombres y descripciones cortas y diferenciadas, o una herramienta de b\xfasqueda que encuentra las relevantes) y los esquemas completos de par\xe1metros solo para las herramientas que de verdad se eligen. El coste base de contexto baja much\xedsimo y la selecci\xf3n mejora, porque el agente compara descripciones breves que no se solapan en lugar de esquemas largos.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) la presentaci\xf3n no cambia el n\xfamero de tokens. (B) quitar herramientas para siempre rompe las tareas que las necesitan. (D) una megaherramienta con 120 par\xe1metros opcionales complica la selecci\xf3n y el relleno de argumentos.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabdemasiadas herramientas / los esquemas llenan el contexto\xbb → progressive disclosure; \xabel agente confunde dos herramientas\xbb → arreglar las descripciones que se solapan."},{"id":"21","question":"Un asistente de compras debe recordar las preferencias que cada usuario ha dicho (tallas, marcas favoritas, restricciones alimentarias) entre conversaciones separadas por semanas, mientras que los detalles de la conversaci\xf3n actual solo importan hasta que termina. \xbfQu\xe9 dise\xf1o de memoria es el adecuado en Databricks?","options":{"A":"Guardar todo en el historial de la conversaci\xf3n actual y reinyectar todas las conversaciones pasadas en cada prompt nuevo","B":"Guardar las preferencias en una variable de Python del contenedor de serving","C":"Volver a preguntar las preferencias al inicio de cada conversaci\xf3n","D":"Usar memoria a corto plazo (el historial de mensajes / estado con checkpoints de la sesi\xf3n) para la conversaci\xf3n actual, y un almac\xe9n duradero a largo plazo respaldado por Lakebase para las preferencias por usuario, recuperadas por id de usuario al iniciar la sesi\xf3n"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La memoria debe corresponder a la necesidad de informaci\xf3n. Dentro de una sesi\xf3n, la fuente correcta es el historial de mensajes (o el estado del agente con checkpoints). Los hechos que deben persistir entre sesiones van en un almac\xe9n duradero: Lakebase (el Postgres gestionado de Databricks) puede respaldar la memoria a largo plazo del agente, indexada por id de usuario, y leerse al inicio de la sesi\xf3n con una consulta estructurada, o buscarse sem\xe1nticamente cuando los recuerdos son texto libre. Mezclar las dos provoca olvido (solo sesi\xf3n) o contaminaci\xf3n del contexto (reinyectarlo todo).\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) reinyectar todas las conversaciones pasadas infla la ventana y entierra lo relevante. (B) la memoria del contenedor desaparece al reiniciar o escalar y no se comparte entre r\xe9plicas. (C) volver a preguntar anula la personalizaci\xf3n.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabentre sesiones\xbb → almac\xe9n duradero (Lakebase); \xabdentro de la conversaci\xf3n\xbb → historial/estado de la sesi\xf3n."},{"id":"22","question":"Los usuarios de negocio preguntan a un Genie space de AI/BI \xab\xbfCu\xe1les fueron los ingresos netos del \xfaltimo trimestre?\xbb y reciben cifras distintas del informe de finanzas, porque Genie suma la columna de importe bruto e ignora las devoluciones. Las tablas son correctas. \xbfCu\xe1l es la correcci\xf3n m\xe1s eficaz?","options":{"A":"Pedir a los usuarios que reformulen hasta que la cifra parezca correcta","B":"Curar el espacio: a\xf1adir descripciones/sin\xf3nimos de columnas en Unity Catalog, instrucciones generales que definan los ingresos netos y un trusted asset (una consulta SQL de ejemplo o una funci\xf3n SQL) que calcule los ingresos netos como lo hace finanzas","C":"A\xf1adir al Genie space todas las tablas del workspace para que tenga m\xe1s datos","D":"Sustituir Genie por un dashboard est\xe1tico y quitar las preguntas en lenguaje natural"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Genie responde a partir de las tablas y el contexto que curas. La precisi\xf3n depende de los metadatos y de la gu\xeda: comentarios claros de tablas y columnas (en Unity Catalog), instrucciones que definan los t\xe9rminos del negocio y consultas SQL de ejemplo o funciones SQL marcadas como trusted assets para las m\xe9tricas importantes. Cuando una pregunta coincide con un trusted asset, Genie usa esa l\xf3gica validada y marca la respuesta como fiable. Mantener el espacio centrado en las tablas correctas tambi\xe9n reduce la ambig\xfcedad.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) cargar el problema al usuario no arregla la definici\xf3n. (C) a\xf1adir tablas ajenas aumenta la ambig\xfcedad y los joins err\xf3neos. (D) renuncia al autoservicio en lugar de arreglarlo.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabGenie da m\xe9tricas err\xf3neas o inconsistentes\xbb → mejores metadatos + instrucciones + trusted assets (SQL de ejemplo / funciones) y un conjunto de tablas acotado."},{"id":"23","question":"Un dashboard de direcci\xf3n ejecuta una agregaci\xf3n cara sobre 3.000 millones de filas cada vez que se abre, y decenas de personas lo abren cada ma\xf1ana. Los datos se cargan una vez por noche. \xbfC\xf3mo reduce el analista coste y latencia manteniendo las cifras correctas?","options":{"A":"Convertir la consulta en una vista normal para que se calcule al leer","B":"Pedir a los directivos que abran menos el dashboard","C":"Crear en Databricks SQL una vista materializada con la agregaci\xf3n, refrescada de forma programada (o al actualizarse la tabla origen) tras la carga nocturna, y apuntar los datasets del dashboard a ella","D":"Exportar cada ma\xf1ana el resultado a una hoja de c\xe1lculo y enviarla por correo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Una vista materializada guarda el resultado precalculado y se refresca con un pipeline gestionado —de forma incremental cuando es posible— seg\xfan una programaci\xf3n o al actualizarse el origen. Las consultas del dashboard leen entonces una tabla peque\xf1a precalculada en lugar de escanear miles de millones de filas, lo que reduce coste del warehouse y latencia, y las cifras son coherentes con la carga nocturna. La cach\xe9 de resultados de Databricks SQL tambi\xe9n ayuda con consultas id\xe9nticas repetidas, pero no sobrevive a cambios en los datos ni evita la primera ejecuci\xf3n cara.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) una vista normal es una consulta guardada; se recalcula en cada lectura. (B) no es una soluci\xf3n t\xe9cnica. (D) las hojas de c\xe1lculo pierden gobierno, frescura e interactividad.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabagregaci\xf3n cara le\xedda muchas veces\xbb + \xabdatos que cambian con una frecuencia conocida\xbb → vista materializada; \xabsiempre al d\xeda, consulta barata\xbb → vista."},{"id":"24","question":"Un modelo de abandono usa variables de clientes de una feature table de Unity Catalog que se actualiza a diario. Las etiquetas de entrenamiento son eventos con fecha de los dos \xfaltimos a\xf1os. La accuracy de validaci\xf3n es excelente pero en producci\xf3n es mucho m\xe1s baja; el equipo sospecha que el entrenamiento us\xf3 valores de variables posteriores a la fecha de cada evento. \xbfC\xf3mo debe construirse el conjunto de entrenamiento?","options":{"A":"Declarar la feature table como serie temporal (claves primarias m\xe1s una clave de timestamp) y crear el training set con un lookup point-in-time, pasando el timestamp de la etiqueta, para que cada fila solo reciba valores conocidos en ese momento","B":"Unir las etiquetas con la \xfaltima foto de la feature table","C":"Barajar las filas antes de entrenar para quitar el efecto del tiempo","D":"Quitar la columna de fecha de las etiquetas"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Es una fuga de informaci\xf3n (leakage) por variables del futuro. Con Databricks Feature Engineering en Unity Catalog, una feature table con clave de timestamp es una feature table de serie temporal; FeatureLookup con timestamp_lookup_key hace un join as-of (point-in-time) y devuelve, para cada etiqueta, los valores m\xe1s recientes en o antes de su fecha. La misma l\xf3gica de lookup se empaqueta con el modelo, lo que mantiene coherentes entrenamiento y serving.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) la \xfaltima foto es justo lo que filtra informaci\xf3n del futuro a etiquetas antiguas. (C) barajar filas no cambia qu\xe9 valores se unieron. (D) sin la fecha es imposible hacer un join as-of correcto.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabvalidaci\xf3n excelente, producci\xf3n mala\xbb + variables que cambian en el tiempo → lookup point-in-time sobre una feature table de serie temporal."}]},{"id":"gcp-pmle-2026","title":"GCP Professional ML Engineer — 2026 exam guide","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"A support team wants a Gemini-based assistant that answers thousands of questions per hour about the same 300-page product manual. Latency and cost are too high because the full manual is sent with every request. Which change most directly reduces cost and latency without hurting answer quality?","options":{"A":"Fine-tune Gemini on the manual so it no longer needs it in the prompt","B":"Switch to the largest available Pro model so it reads the manual faster","C":"Use context caching for the manual (a reused prefix billed at a reduced rate), and evaluate whether a Flash model meets the quality bar","D":"Split each request across ten parallel calls with one chapter each and concatenate the answers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When many requests share a large, identical prefix (the manual), context caching stores those tokens once and later requests reference the cache, which lowers per-request input cost and time to first token. Choosing a smaller model tier (Flash) when evaluation shows it meets the bar is the other standard lever for cost and latency. For non-interactive workloads, batch prediction is cheaper still; for guaranteed capacity, Provisioned Throughput.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) fine-tuning teaches style and task behaviour, not reliable recall of a 300-page document, and it is costly; grounding/caching is the right tool for knowledge. (B) larger models are slower and more expensive. (D) splitting the manual loses cross-chapter context and multiplies calls.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"same large context in every request\\" → context caching; \\"reduce cost/latency\\" → smaller model tier, batch prediction for offline work."},{"id":"2","question":"A retail analytics team has labeled examples of product reviews and their correct category in a BigQuery table. Prompting Gemini from BigQuery gives inconsistent categories. The team only knows SQL and wants to adapt the model to its labeling style without leaving BigQuery. What should they do?","options":{"A":"Create a remote model over a Gemini endpoint with CREATE MODEL and supervised tuning options (training data with a prompt column and a label column), then call it with AI.GENERATE / ML.GENERATE_TEXT","B":"Export the table to CSV and train a custom TensorFlow classifier on GPUs","C":"Use ML.TRANSLATE to normalize the reviews into one language","D":"Train a K-means model and use the cluster ids as categories"},"correct":"A","explanation":"BigQuery ML can tune a Gemini remote model with supervised tuning: CREATE MODEL … REMOTE WITH CONNECTION … OPTIONS (endpoint = \'<gemini model>\', max_iterations = …, …) AS SELECT prompt, label FROM training_table. The tuned model is then used from SQL with the generation functions and evaluated with ML.EVALUATE. This fits a SQL-only team and keeps the data in BigQuery.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) exporting and writing TensorFlow ignores the team\'s skills and the low-code requirement. (C) translation does not teach the labeling scheme. (D) K-means is unsupervised; its clusters do not match the business\'s categories.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"tune Gemini\\" + \\"SQL team\\" / \\"data in BigQuery\\" → supervised tuning of a remote model in BigQuery ML."},{"id":"3","question":"An insurer receives thousands of scanned claim forms and invoices per day and must extract fields such as policy number, dates, amounts and line items into BigQuery, with minimal ML development. Which solution fits best?","options":{"A":"Cloud Vision API label detection on each page","B":"Train an image classification model with AutoML to recognize each field","C":"Cloud Translation API to convert the scans to text","D":"Document AI processors (pre-trained invoice/form parsers, or a Custom Extractor for the claim form) called in batch from a pipeline, writing the extracted entities to BigQuery, with human review for low-confidence fields"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Document AI is the industry-specific API for documents: OCR plus layout understanding plus entity extraction. Pre-trained processors (Invoice Parser, Form Parser, Expense Parser…) work out of the box, and the Custom Extractor (generative-AI based) handles a proprietary claim form with few or no examples. Batch processing from Cloud Storage scales to thousands of documents, outputs structured entities with confidence scores, and low-confidence results can be routed to human review.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) label detection describes what an image shows; it does not extract fields. (B) image classification assigns one label per image, not structured fields. (C) Translation translates text; it is not an OCR/extraction service.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"extract fields from forms/invoices/receipts\\" → Document AI."},{"id":"4","question":"A team needs a model for a narrow task: classifying short support messages into 12 intents with low latency and low cost, at very high volume. They are choosing a model in Model Garden. Which selection approach is most appropriate?","options":{"A":"Always choose the largest proprietary model because it is the most accurate on every task","B":"Shortlist candidates (for example a Gemini Flash model and smaller open models), evaluate them on a representative labeled set for quality, latency and cost, and pick the smallest model that meets the requirements","C":"Pick the model with the most downloads in the catalog","D":"Choose an image-generation model because it supports more modalities"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Model Garden gives access to Google first-party models (Gemini, Imagen, Veo), partner models as a service and open models that can be deployed or tuned. Model selection should be evidence-based: define the task\'s quality metric and constraints (latency, cost, context length, data residency, licensing), evaluate a few candidates on your own data, and choose the smallest model that meets the bar — a simple, high-volume classification task rarely needs the largest model.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) the biggest model is slower and more expensive and not necessarily better on a narrow task. (C) popularity says nothing about fit to your data. (D) modality must match the task; image generation does not classify text.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"choose a model from Model Garden for task X with cost/latency constraints\\" → evaluate candidates on your data, pick the smallest that meets requirements."},{"id":"5","question":"A healthcare team wants to train a model on clinical notes stored in BigQuery. Data scientists must not see patient names, phone numbers or medical record numbers, but the de-identified text must keep its meaning for training. What should be done before the data reaches the training environment?","options":{"A":"Run Sensitive Data Protection de-identification on the notes (replace names and identifiers with infoType tokens or consistent pseudonyms), write the result to a separate dataset, and grant data scientists access only to that dataset","B":"Drop the notes column entirely","C":"Rely on BigQuery\'s default encryption at rest","D":"Give data scientists access to the raw table and ask them not to look at identifiers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sensitive Data Protection (Cloud DLP) inspects text for infoTypes (person names, phone numbers, medical record numbers, custom identifiers) and de-identifies them: replacement with the infoType name, masking, or deterministic tokenization/pseudonymization so the same patient maps to the same token. The meaning of the note is kept for modeling while the identifiers are gone. Writing to a separate dataset and granting IAM only there enforces the separation; the raw data stays restricted.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) dropping the notes removes the training signal. (C) encryption at rest does not hide content from authorized readers. (D) a request is not a control and would fail any audit.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"PII in training data\\" + \\"keep utility\\" → Sensitive Data Protection de-identification before training, with access separation."},{"id":"6","question":"A team built a RAG assistant and must compare three prompt and retrieval configurations before release. There is no single correct answer per question, and human review of every answer is too slow. How should they evaluate the configurations?","options":{"A":"Measure only the latency of each configuration and choose the fastest","B":"Use exact-match accuracy against one reference answer per question","C":"Use the Gen AI evaluation service on Agent Platform with a representative evaluation dataset: model-based metrics (LLM-as-a-judge with rubrics such as groundedness, relevance, instruction following) plus computation metrics where references exist, log runs in Experiments, and spot-check judge results with humans","D":"Ask the model to rate its own answers in the same request"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For open-ended generation, exact match fails because many answers are valid. The Gen AI evaluation service runs an evaluation dataset through each candidate and scores it with model-based metrics — an LLM-as-a-judge applying rubrics (groundedness/faithfulness to retrieved context, relevance, helpfulness, safety, instruction following), pointwise or pairwise — and with computation metrics (ROUGE, BLEU, exact match, tool-call metrics) where references exist. Results are tracked in Experiments for comparison, and a human sample calibrates the judge.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) latency matters but says nothing about answer quality. (B) a single reference punishes correct paraphrases. (D) self-rating in the same call is biased and is not a controlled evaluation.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"evaluate generative output at scale\\" → Gen AI evaluation service with LLM-as-a-judge rubrics + human calibration."},{"id":"7","question":"A data science team of 15 needs managed notebooks for prototyping with PyTorch on sensitive data. Security requires no public IPs, data access only through each user\'s own identity, and notebooks that cannot exfiltrate data to the internet. Which setup best follows collaboration and security best practices?","options":{"A":"Personal laptops with service account keys downloaded as JSON files","B":"Colab Enterprise or Agent Platform Workbench instances using runtime templates with no public IP in a VPC inside a VPC Service Controls perimeter, end-user credentials (single-user access), CMEK if required, and notebooks versioned in Git","C":"One shared Workbench instance where everyone uses the same default service account with Editor role","D":"Public notebooks shared by link so reviewers can see results"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Managed notebooks on Google Cloud can be hardened: runtime templates (Colab Enterprise) or instance settings (Workbench) without external IPs on a private subnet; VPC Service Controls around the project so data in BigQuery and Cloud Storage cannot be copied out; end-user credentials / single-user access so every query is attributed to the person; CMEK where policy requires; and code in Git for review and reproducibility. Colab Enterprise also offers sharing via IAM for collaboration.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) downloaded service account keys are a top exfiltration risk and data on laptops escapes control. (C) a shared Editor service account breaks least privilege and auditability. (D) public links expose sensitive results.\\n\\nExam cue: secure notebooks → no public IP + VPC SC + per-user identity + no SA keys; Colab Enterprise for collaboration, Workbench for heavier customization."},{"id":"8","question":"An auditor asks which dataset version, preprocessing component and hyperparameters produced the model currently deployed, and the team must also compare dozens of past training runs side by side. Which Google Cloud capabilities answer both needs?","options":{"A":"Cloud Logging text searches for the word \\"train\\"","B":"A spreadsheet where engineers paste metrics after each run","C":"Cloud Storage object versioning on the model file only","D":"Experiments on Agent Platform to log parameters and metrics of each run for comparison, and ML Metadata (populated automatically by Agent Platform Pipelines) to trace the lineage from dataset and components to the registered model"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Experiments (Agent Platform Experiments, formerly Vertex AI Experiments) records parameters, metrics and artifacts for each run and lets you compare runs in tables and charts, with TensorBoard for detailed curves. ML Metadata stores lineage — which artifacts (datasets, transformed data, models) were produced and consumed by which executions — and Agent Platform Pipelines writes it automatically for every pipeline run, so you can walk back from the model in Model Registry to the exact data and code that created it.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) free-text log searches are not structured lineage. (B) manual spreadsheets are error-prone and unauditable. (C) versioning the model file says nothing about how it was produced.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"compare runs\\" → Experiments; \\"lineage / which data produced this model\\" → ML Metadata (via Pipelines)."},{"id":"9","question":"A team trains a large transformer whose matrix-heavy workload is written in JAX, runs for days and is sensitive to cost per training step. They have no custom CUDA kernels. Which accelerator choice is the most appropriate starting point on Google Cloud?","options":{"A":"CPU-only machines with many cores, because JAX is CPU-optimized","B":"Cloud TPUs (for example a TPU slice through Agent Platform custom training or GKE), since large dense matrix workloads in JAX/XLA map well to TPUs and scale across chips with data and model parallelism","C":"A single small GPU so the job uses less power","D":"Edge TPUs on a developer laptop"},"correct":"B","explanation":"TPUs are designed for large dense matrix multiplications compiled through XLA, which JAX (and TensorFlow, and PyTorch via PyTorch/XLA) target natively. For long training of large models without custom CUDA operations, TPU slices typically give strong price-performance and scale across many chips with data, model and pipeline parallelism. GPUs remain the better choice when the code depends on custom CUDA kernels, specific GPU libraries, or ops TPUs do not support well; CPUs suit small or non-matrix workloads.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) CPUs are far slower for large matrix workloads. (C) a single small GPU cannot train a large transformer in reasonable time. (D) Edge TPUs are for on-device inference, not training.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"large model + JAX/TensorFlow + matrix-heavy + long training\\" → TPU; \\"custom CUDA ops / GPU-only libraries\\" → GPU; \\"small/simple\\" → CPU."},{"id":"10","question":"A bank serves a fraud model on Agent Platform. Requests come only from applications inside its VPC, regulators require that prediction traffic never traverse the public internet, and latency must be as low as possible. How should the model be deployed?","options":{"A":"A public endpoint protected by an API key embedded in the mobile app","B":"Batch prediction jobs every hour","C":"A public endpoint behind Cloud CDN","D":"A private endpoint (Private Service Connect or VPC peering) so the VPC\'s applications reach the model over private IPs, combined with VPC Service Controls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI) supports private endpoints for online prediction: with Private Service Connect (recommended) or VPC Network Peering, applications in your VPC send requests to private IP addresses, traffic stays on Google\'s network and the endpoint is not exposed publicly, which also tends to reduce latency. VPC Service Controls adds a perimeter around the API.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a public endpoint violates the requirement, and keys embedded in apps leak. (B) hourly batch jobs cannot score transactions in real time. (C) CDN caches static content; it does not make predictions private.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"traffic must not traverse the internet\\" / \\"only from our VPC\\" → private endpoint (PSC)."},{"id":"11","question":"A PyTorch model needs custom preprocessing (tokenization and feature lookups) before inference and custom postprocessing (thresholds and business rules) after it, exactly as done in training. The team wants to serve it on Agent Platform with these steps packaged together. What should they do?","options":{"A":"Build a custom container (for example with a Custom Prediction Routine or their own HTTP server) that implements preprocess → predict → postprocess, push it to Artifact Registry, upload it to Model Registry and deploy it to an endpoint","B":"Use the prebuilt scikit-learn container and do the preprocessing in each client application","C":"Deploy only the raw weights and ask clients to send already-tokenized tensors","D":"Convert the model to a BigQuery ML linear model"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Prebuilt containers serve a model in a standard framework format, but when inference needs extra logic you package it in a custom container. Custom Prediction Routines (CPR) generate such a container from a Python predictor class with preprocess, predict and postprocess methods; alternatively you write your own server that meets the container requirements (health and predict routes). Putting the same preprocessing code in the container keeps training and serving consistent and avoids training-serving skew.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) and (C) push preprocessing into every client, which duplicates logic and invites skew. (D) changes the model entirely and loses the PyTorch architecture.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"custom pre/postprocessing at serving time\\" → custom container / Custom Prediction Routine; standard model → prebuilt container."},{"id":"12","question":"A team must run large-scale hyperparameter sweeps and distributed reinforcement-learning simulations written with the Ray framework. They want a managed Ray cluster on Google Cloud that integrates with their other ML services, without rewriting the code. What should they use?","options":{"A":"Rewrite the code as BigQuery ML SQL","B":"Run Ray on a single Workbench notebook VM","C":"Ray on Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Ray on Vertex AI), which provisions managed Ray clusters that can be used from notebooks or jobs and integrate with Agent Platform services","D":"Cloud Functions with one function per Ray task"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ray on Agent Platform creates and manages Ray clusters (head and worker nodes, with GPUs when needed) so existing Ray code — Ray Tune for hyperparameter search, Ray Train, RLlib, Ray Data — runs at scale without managing the cluster yourself. It connects with notebooks (Colab Enterprise, Workbench), Model Registry and other platform services. The exam guide lists it next to Agent Platform Pipelines and Managed Service for Apache Airflow as orchestration options.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) rewriting in SQL is not feasible for RL simulations and discards the existing code. (B) a single VM limits scale and makes the notebook a fragile cluster. (D) short-lived functions cannot coordinate a distributed Ray job.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"existing Ray code\\" / \\"Ray Tune, RLlib at scale\\" → Ray on Agent Platform (or Ray on GKE when Kubernetes control is required)."},{"id":"13","question":"A model performs well offline but poorly in production. Investigation shows the training pipeline normalized a feature with statistics computed in a Dataflow job, while the online service reimplemented the normalization in Java with slightly different rules. What is the best long-term fix?","options":{"A":"Retrain more often so the model adapts to the Java normalization","B":"Add more layers to the model to make it robust to scaling differences","C":"Disable normalization in training","D":"Define the transformation once and reuse it in both paths — for example compute and store features in Feature Store, or export the transformation graph (TensorFlow Transform / a preprocessing layer inside the model) so serving applies exactly the same logic — and monitor training-serving skew"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is training-serving skew caused by two implementations of the same preprocessing. The fix is a single source of truth: serve features from Agent Platform Feature Store (computed once, read online and offline), or ship the transformation with the model (TensorFlow Transform\'s transform graph, Keras preprocessing layers, or the same Python code in a custom serving container). Model Monitoring can then watch training-serving skew to catch regressions.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) retraining does not remove the mismatch between pipelines. (B) model capacity does not fix inconsistent inputs. (C) removing normalization may hurt the model and does not address the root cause.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"works offline, fails online\\" + \\"preprocessing implemented twice\\" → reuse one transformation (Feature Store / TFT / preprocessing in the model)."},{"id":"14","question":"A team wants every merge to main to rebuild and test its training pipeline, and wants production models retrained when new labeled data arrives or monitoring detects degradation — but promoted only if they pass evaluation. Which design implements CI/CD/CT on Google Cloud?","options":{"A":"Engineers retrain from their notebooks whenever they remember and copy the model file to production","B":"Cloud Build triggers on merge to build the components\' containers, run unit and integration tests and compile/deploy the Agent Platform pipeline (CI/CD); the pipeline is run on a schedule, on new data (event-driven via Pub/Sub/Eventarc) or on a monitoring alert (CT), and includes an evaluation step that registers and deploys the model only if it beats the current one","C":"Retrain every hour and always deploy the newest model","D":"Only run CI on the notebook files and deploy models manually"},"correct":"B","explanation":"MLOps on Google Cloud separates CI (testing code and pipeline components), CD (deploying the pipeline and, through it, models) and CT (continuous training). Cloud Build handles CI/CD on each commit; the training pipeline in Agent Platform Pipelines is triggered by a schedule, by new-data events or by Model Monitoring alerts; and a conditional evaluation step (for example comparing metrics to the production model or a threshold) gates registration in Model Registry and deployment to the endpoint.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) manual retraining is unreliable and unauditable. (C) retraining blindly and always deploying risks promoting worse models. (D) without automated CT and deployment, models go stale and releases are error-prone.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"CI/CD/CT\\" → Cloud Build for code, pipeline runs triggered by schedule/data/monitoring, evaluation gate before deployment."},{"id":"15","question":"A credit model\'s input feature distributions look stable in production, but Model Monitoring reports that the model now relies much more on \\"days_since_last_payment\\" and much less on \\"income\\" than at training time. What kind of issue is this and what does it suggest?","options":{"A":"Training-serving skew caused by a schema change","B":"Concept drift proven by falling accuracy","C":"Feature attribution drift: the features\' contributions to predictions have shifted, which can reveal changing relationships or data problems even when input distributions look stable — investigate and consider retraining","D":"An endpoint latency issue"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Model Monitoring can track two families of signals: feature drift/skew (changes in input distributions versus training data or over time) and feature attribution drift/skew (changes in how much each feature contributes to predictions, using Explainable AI attributions). Attribution drift can surface problems that input statistics miss — for example interactions between features changing, or a feature\'s pipeline silently degrading — and is a cue to investigate and possibly retrain.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) skew compares serving inputs to training data, and the case says inputs look stable. (B) concept drift is a change in the relationship between features and target, confirmed with labels and performance metrics; the case gives no accuracy data. (D) nothing in the case concerns latency.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"feature importance/attributions changed\\" → feature attribution drift; \\"input distribution changed\\" → data drift; \\"serving inputs differ from training\\" → skew."},{"id":"16","question":"A loan-approval model must be checked for unfair outcomes across demographic groups before and after deployment, and regulators ask the bank to explain individual decisions. Which approach aligns with responsible AI practices on Google Cloud?","options":{"A":"Evaluate model performance and fairness metrics on slices by sensitive attribute (for example with model evaluation slices and fairness metrics), monitor them in production, and provide per-prediction feature attributions with Explainable AI on Agent Platform","B":"Remove the sensitive attributes from the data and assume the model is then fair","C":"Publish the model\'s overall accuracy as proof of fairness","D":"Use a deeper neural network because larger models are less biased"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Responsible AI requires measuring, not assuming. Evaluate metrics such as recall, false-positive rate or approval rate on slices for each group and compare them (fairness metrics, model evaluation slices), keep monitoring them in production because data shifts can reintroduce bias, and explain individual predictions with Explainable AI feature attributions (sampled Shapley, integrated gradients, XRAI for images) so decisions can be justified to regulators and customers.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) dropping sensitive attributes does not remove proxies (postcode, income) that correlate with them, and makes bias harder to measure. (C) good overall accuracy can hide large errors for a subgroup. (D) model size does not imply fairness and usually reduces interpretability.\\n\\nExam cue: \\"monitor for bias\\" → sliced fairness metrics in evaluation and production; \\"explain individual decisions\\" → Explainable AI feature attributions."}]},{"id":"gcp-pmle-2026-es","title":"GCP Professional ML Engineer — gu\xeda de examen 2026 (ES)","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"Un equipo de soporte quiere un asistente basado en Gemini que responda miles de preguntas por hora sobre el mismo manual de producto de 300 p\xe1ginas. La latencia y el coste son demasiado altos porque cada petici\xf3n env\xeda el manual completo. \xbfQu\xe9 cambio reduce m\xe1s directamente coste y latencia sin empeorar las respuestas?","options":{"A":"Hacer fine-tuning de Gemini con el manual para no tener que enviarlo en el prompt","B":"Pasar al modelo Pro m\xe1s grande disponible para que lea el manual m\xe1s r\xe1pido","C":"Usar context caching para el manual (un prefijo reutilizado que se factura a tarifa reducida) y evaluar si un modelo Flash cumple el nivel de calidad","D":"Dividir cada petici\xf3n en diez llamadas en paralelo, una por cap\xedtulo, y concatenar las respuestas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cuando muchas peticiones comparten un prefijo grande e id\xe9ntico (el manual), el context caching guarda esos tokens una vez y las peticiones siguientes referencian la cach\xe9, lo que baja el coste de entrada por petici\xf3n y el tiempo hasta el primer token. Elegir un modelo m\xe1s peque\xf1o (Flash) cuando la evaluaci\xf3n demuestra que cumple es la otra palanca habitual de coste y latencia. Para cargas no interactivas, la predicci\xf3n por lotes es a\xfan m\xe1s barata; para capacidad garantizada, Provisioned Throughput.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) el fine-tuning ense\xf1a estilo y comportamiento de tarea, no a recordar con fiabilidad un documento de 300 p\xe1ginas, y es caro; para conocimiento, grounding o cach\xe9. (B) los modelos m\xe1s grandes son m\xe1s lentos y caros. (D) partir el manual pierde el contexto entre cap\xedtulos y multiplica las llamadas.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabel mismo contexto grande en cada petici\xf3n\xbb → context caching; \xabreducir coste/latencia\xbb → modelo m\xe1s peque\xf1o, predicci\xf3n por lotes para trabajo offline."},{"id":"2","question":"Un equipo de anal\xedtica de retail tiene en una tabla de BigQuery ejemplos etiquetados de rese\xf1as con su categor\xeda correcta. Al hacer prompting a Gemini desde BigQuery obtiene categor\xedas inconsistentes. El equipo solo sabe SQL y quiere adaptar el modelo a su criterio de etiquetado sin salir de BigQuery. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer?","options":{"A":"Crear un modelo remoto sobre un endpoint de Gemini con CREATE MODEL y opciones de supervised tuning (datos de entrenamiento con una columna de prompt y otra de etiqueta), y llamarlo con AI.GENERATE / ML.GENERATE_TEXT","B":"Exportar la tabla a CSV y entrenar un clasificador TensorFlow propio con GPUs","C":"Usar ML.TRANSLATE para normalizar las rese\xf1as a un idioma","D":"Entrenar un modelo K-means y usar los ids de cluster como categor\xedas"},"correct":"A","explanation":"BigQuery ML puede ajustar un modelo remoto de Gemini con supervised tuning: CREATE MODEL … REMOTE WITH CONNECTION … OPTIONS (endpoint = \'<modelo gemini>\', max_iterations = …, …) AS SELECT prompt, label FROM tabla_entrenamiento. El modelo ajustado se usa despu\xe9s desde SQL con las funciones de generaci\xf3n y se eval\xfaa con ML.EVALUATE. Encaja con un equipo que solo sabe SQL y mantiene los datos en BigQuery.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) exportar y escribir TensorFlow ignora las habilidades del equipo y el requisito low-code. (C) traducir no ense\xf1a el criterio de etiquetado. (D) K-means es no supervisado; sus clusters no coinciden con las categor\xedas del negocio.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabajustar Gemini\xbb + \xabequipo SQL\xbb / \xabdatos en BigQuery\xbb → supervised tuning de un modelo remoto en BigQuery ML."},{"id":"3","question":"Una aseguradora recibe a diario miles de formularios de siniestro y facturas escaneados y debe extraer campos como n\xfamero de p\xf3liza, fechas, importes y l\xedneas de detalle a BigQuery, con el m\xednimo desarrollo de ML. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n encaja mejor?","options":{"A":"Detecci\xf3n de etiquetas de Cloud Vision API en cada p\xe1gina","B":"Entrenar con AutoML un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes que reconozca cada campo","C":"Cloud Translation API para convertir los escaneos en texto","D":"Procesadores de Document AI (parsers preentrenados de facturas/formularios, o un Custom Extractor para el formulario de siniestro) llamados por lotes desde un pipeline, escribiendo las entidades extra\xeddas en BigQuery, con revisi\xf3n humana de los campos de baja confianza"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Document AI es la API espec\xedfica para documentos: OCR, comprensi\xf3n de la maquetaci\xf3n y extracci\xf3n de entidades. Los procesadores preentrenados (Invoice Parser, Form Parser, Expense Parser…) funcionan sin entrenar, y el Custom Extractor (basado en IA generativa) resuelve un formulario propio con pocos o ning\xfan ejemplo. El procesamiento por lotes desde Cloud Storage escala a miles de documentos, devuelve entidades estructuradas con su confianza, y los resultados dudosos se pueden enviar a revisi\xf3n humana.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) la detecci\xf3n de etiquetas describe qu\xe9 muestra una imagen; no extrae campos. (B) la clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes da una etiqueta por imagen, no campos estructurados. (C) Translation traduce texto; no es un servicio de OCR ni de extracci\xf3n.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabextraer campos de formularios/facturas/recibos\xbb → Document AI."},{"id":"4","question":"Un equipo necesita un modelo para una tarea concreta: clasificar mensajes cortos de soporte en 12 intenciones con baja latencia y bajo coste, a muy alto volumen. Est\xe1 eligiendo modelo en Model Garden. \xbfQu\xe9 forma de elegir es la m\xe1s adecuada?","options":{"A":"Elegir siempre el modelo propietario m\xe1s grande porque es el m\xe1s preciso en cualquier tarea","B":"Preseleccionar candidatos (por ejemplo un Gemini Flash y modelos abiertos m\xe1s peque\xf1os), evaluarlos sobre un conjunto etiquetado representativo en calidad, latencia y coste, y quedarse con el modelo m\xe1s peque\xf1o que cumpla los requisitos","C":"Elegir el modelo con m\xe1s descargas del cat\xe1logo","D":"Elegir un modelo de generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes porque admite m\xe1s modalidades"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Model Garden da acceso a modelos de Google (Gemini, Imagen, Veo), modelos de partners como servicio y modelos abiertos que se pueden desplegar o ajustar. La elecci\xf3n debe basarse en evidencia: definir la m\xe9trica de calidad de la tarea y las restricciones (latencia, coste, longitud de contexto, residencia de datos, licencia), evaluar unos pocos candidatos con tus datos y elegir el modelo m\xe1s peque\xf1o que cumpla; una clasificaci\xf3n sencilla de alto volumen rara vez necesita el modelo m\xe1s grande.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) el modelo m\xe1s grande es m\xe1s lento y caro y no necesariamente mejor en una tarea concreta. (C) la popularidad no dice nada de c\xf3mo encaja con tus datos. (D) la modalidad debe corresponder a la tarea; la generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes no clasifica texto.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabelegir modelo de Model Garden para la tarea X con restricciones de coste/latencia\xbb → evaluar candidatos con tus datos y elegir el m\xe1s peque\xf1o que cumpla."},{"id":"5","question":"Un equipo sanitario quiere entrenar un modelo con notas cl\xednicas guardadas en BigQuery. Los cient\xedficos de datos no deben ver nombres de pacientes, tel\xe9fonos ni n\xfameros de historia cl\xednica, pero el texto desidentificado debe conservar su sentido para entrenar. \xbfQu\xe9 hay que hacer antes de que los datos lleguen al entorno de entrenamiento?","options":{"A":"Pasar la desidentificaci\xf3n de Sensitive Data Protection sobre las notas (sustituir nombres e identificadores por tokens de infoType o seud\xf3nimos consistentes), escribir el resultado en un dataset separado y dar a los cient\xedficos de datos acceso solo a ese dataset","B":"Eliminar por completo la columna de notas","C":"Confiar en el cifrado en reposo por defecto de BigQuery","D":"Dar a los cient\xedficos acceso a la tabla en bruto y pedirles que no miren los identificadores"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sensitive Data Protection (Cloud DLP) inspecciona el texto en busca de infoTypes (nombres de persona, tel\xe9fonos, n\xfameros de historia cl\xednica, identificadores propios) y los desidentifica: sustituci\xf3n por el nombre del infoType, enmascarado o tokenizaci\xf3n/seudonimizaci\xf3n determinista para que el mismo paciente tenga siempre el mismo token. El sentido de la nota se conserva para el modelo y los identificadores desaparecen. Escribir en un dataset separado y dar IAM solo all\xed impone la separaci\xf3n; el dato en bruto sigue restringido.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) quitar las notas elimina la se\xf1al de entrenamiento. (C) el cifrado en reposo no oculta el contenido a lectores autorizados. (D) una petici\xf3n no es un control y no pasar\xeda ninguna auditor\xeda.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabPII en datos de entrenamiento\xbb + \xabconservar utilidad\xbb → desidentificaci\xf3n con Sensitive Data Protection antes de entrenar, con separaci\xf3n de accesos."},{"id":"6","question":"Un equipo construy\xf3 un asistente RAG y debe comparar tres configuraciones de prompt y recuperaci\xf3n antes de publicarlo. No hay una \xfanica respuesta correcta por pregunta y revisar a mano cada respuesta es demasiado lento. \xbfC\xf3mo debe evaluar las configuraciones?","options":{"A":"Medir solo la latencia de cada configuraci\xf3n y elegir la m\xe1s r\xe1pida","B":"Usar accuracy por coincidencia exacta contra una \xfanica respuesta de referencia por pregunta","C":"Usar el Gen AI evaluation service de Agent Platform con un dataset de evaluaci\xf3n representativo: m\xe9tricas basadas en modelo (LLM-as-a-judge con r\xfabricas como groundedness, relevancia o seguimiento de instrucciones) m\xe1s m\xe9tricas calculadas donde haya referencias, registrar las ejecuciones en Experiments y revisar a mano una muestra del juez","D":"Pedir al modelo que punt\xfae sus propias respuestas en la misma petici\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"En generaci\xf3n abierta, la coincidencia exacta falla porque muchas respuestas son v\xe1lidas. El Gen AI evaluation service pasa un dataset de evaluaci\xf3n por cada candidato y lo punt\xfaa con m\xe9tricas basadas en modelo —un LLM-as-a-judge que aplica r\xfabricas (groundedness/fidelidad al contexto recuperado, relevancia, utilidad, seguridad, seguimiento de instrucciones), en modo pointwise o pairwise— y con m\xe9tricas calculadas (ROUGE, BLEU, coincidencia exacta, m\xe9tricas de llamadas a herramientas) donde hay referencias. Los resultados se registran en Experiments para comparar, y una muestra humana calibra al juez.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) la latencia importa pero no dice nada de la calidad. (B) una \xfanica referencia castiga par\xe1frasis correctas. (D) autoevaluarse en la misma llamada est\xe1 sesgado y no es una evaluaci\xf3n controlada.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabevaluar salida generativa a escala\xbb → Gen AI evaluation service con r\xfabricas LLM-as-a-judge + calibraci\xf3n humana."},{"id":"7","question":"Un equipo de 15 cient\xedficos de datos necesita notebooks gestionados para prototipar con PyTorch sobre datos sensibles. Seguridad exige sin IPs p\xfablicas, acceso a datos solo con la identidad de cada usuario y notebooks que no puedan exfiltrar datos a internet. \xbfQu\xe9 configuraci\xf3n sigue mejor las buenas pr\xe1cticas de colaboraci\xf3n y seguridad?","options":{"A":"Port\xe1tiles personales con claves de cuenta de servicio descargadas como ficheros JSON","B":"Colab Enterprise o instancias de Agent Platform Workbench con runtime templates sin IP p\xfablica en una VPC dentro de un per\xedmetro de VPC Service Controls, credenciales del propio usuario (acceso single-user), CMEK si se exige y notebooks versionados en Git","C":"Una \xfanica instancia de Workbench compartida en la que todos usan la misma cuenta de servicio por defecto con rol Editor","D":"Notebooks p\xfablicos compartidos por enlace para que los revisores vean los resultados"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los notebooks gestionados de Google Cloud se pueden blindar: runtime templates (Colab Enterprise) o ajustes de instancia (Workbench) sin IP externa en una subred privada; VPC Service Controls alrededor del proyecto para que los datos de BigQuery y Cloud Storage no puedan copiarse fuera; credenciales del usuario / acceso single-user para que cada consulta se atribuya a la persona; CMEK cuando la pol\xedtica lo pide; y c\xf3digo en Git para revisi\xf3n y reproducibilidad. Colab Enterprise permite adem\xe1s compartir mediante IAM para colaborar.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) las claves de cuenta de servicio descargadas son uno de los mayores riesgos de exfiltraci\xf3n, y los datos en port\xe1tiles escapan al control. (C) una cuenta de servicio Editor compartida rompe el m\xednimo privilegio y la auditor\xeda. (D) los enlaces p\xfablicos exponen resultados sensibles.\\n\\nPista de examen: notebooks seguros → sin IP p\xfablica + VPC SC + identidad por usuario + sin claves de SA; Colab Enterprise para colaborar, Workbench para m\xe1s personalizaci\xf3n."},{"id":"8","question":"Un auditor pregunta qu\xe9 versi\xf3n del dataset, qu\xe9 componente de preprocesado y qu\xe9 hiperpar\xe1metros produjeron el modelo desplegado, y adem\xe1s el equipo debe comparar en paralelo decenas de entrenamientos pasados. \xbfQu\xe9 capacidades de Google Cloud responden a ambas necesidades?","options":{"A":"B\xfasquedas de texto en Cloud Logging con la palabra \xabtrain\xbb","B":"Una hoja de c\xe1lculo donde los ingenieros pegan las m\xe9tricas tras cada ejecuci\xf3n","C":"Versionado de objetos de Cloud Storage solo sobre el fichero del modelo","D":"Experiments en Agent Platform para registrar par\xe1metros y m\xe9tricas de cada ejecuci\xf3n y compararlas, y ML Metadata (que Agent Platform Pipelines rellena autom\xe1ticamente) para trazar el linaje desde el dataset y los componentes hasta el modelo registrado"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Experiments (Agent Platform Experiments, antes Vertex AI Experiments) registra par\xe1metros, m\xe9tricas y artefactos de cada ejecuci\xf3n y permite compararlas en tablas y gr\xe1ficos, con TensorBoard para las curvas detalladas. ML Metadata guarda el linaje —qu\xe9 artefactos (datasets, datos transformados, modelos) produjo y consumi\xf3 cada ejecuci\xf3n— y Agent Platform Pipelines lo escribe autom\xe1ticamente en cada ejecuci\xf3n, as\xed que se puede ir desde el modelo en Model Registry hasta los datos y el c\xf3digo exactos que lo crearon.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) buscar texto en logs no es linaje estructurado. (B) las hojas manuales son propensas a errores y no auditables. (C) versionar el fichero del modelo no dice c\xf3mo se produjo.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabcomparar ejecuciones\xbb → Experiments; \xablinaje / qu\xe9 datos produjeron este modelo\xbb → ML Metadata (v\xeda Pipelines)."},{"id":"9","question":"Un equipo entrena un transformer grande cuya carga, muy intensiva en matrices, est\xe1 escrita en JAX, dura d\xedas y es sensible al coste por paso de entrenamiento. No usa kernels CUDA propios. \xbfQu\xe9 acelerador es el punto de partida m\xe1s adecuado en Google Cloud?","options":{"A":"M\xe1quinas solo con CPU y muchos n\xfacleos, porque JAX est\xe1 optimizado para CPU","B":"Cloud TPUs (por ejemplo un slice de TPU v\xeda custom training de Agent Platform o GKE), porque las cargas de matrices densas en JAX/XLA encajan bien en TPU y escalan entre chips con paralelismo de datos y de modelo","C":"Una sola GPU peque\xf1a para que el trabajo consuma menos","D":"Edge TPUs en un port\xe1til de desarrollo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las TPU est\xe1n dise\xf1adas para grandes multiplicaciones de matrices densas compiladas con XLA, que es el objetivo nativo de JAX (y de TensorFlow, y de PyTorch mediante PyTorch/XLA). Para entrenamientos largos de modelos grandes sin operaciones CUDA propias, los slices de TPU suelen dar muy buena relaci\xf3n precio-rendimiento y escalan entre muchos chips con paralelismo de datos, de modelo y de pipeline. Las GPU siguen siendo mejor opci\xf3n cuando el c\xf3digo depende de kernels CUDA propios, librer\xedas espec\xedficas de GPU u operaciones que la TPU no soporta bien; las CPU sirven para cargas peque\xf1as o sin matrices.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) las CPU son mucho m\xe1s lentas con matrices grandes. (C) una sola GPU peque\xf1a no entrena un transformer grande en un tiempo razonable. (D) las Edge TPU son para inferencia en el dispositivo, no para entrenar.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabmodelo grande + JAX/TensorFlow + matrices + entrenamiento largo\xbb → TPU; \xabops CUDA propias / librer\xedas solo GPU\xbb → GPU; \xabpeque\xf1o/simple\xbb → CPU."},{"id":"10","question":"Un banco sirve un modelo de fraude en Agent Platform. Las peticiones solo llegan desde aplicaciones dentro de su VPC, el regulador exige que el tr\xe1fico de predicci\xf3n nunca pase por internet p\xfablico y la latencia debe ser la m\xednima posible. \xbfC\xf3mo debe desplegarse el modelo?","options":{"A":"Un endpoint p\xfablico protegido con una API key incrustada en la app m\xf3vil","B":"Jobs de predicci\xf3n por lotes cada hora","C":"Un endpoint p\xfablico detr\xe1s de Cloud CDN","D":"Un endpoint privado (Private Service Connect o VPC peering) para que las aplicaciones de la VPC lleguen al modelo por IPs privadas, junto con VPC Service Controls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Agent Platform (antes Vertex AI) admite endpoints privados para predicci\xf3n online: con Private Service Connect (recomendado) o VPC Network Peering, las aplicaciones de tu VPC env\xedan peticiones a IPs privadas, el tr\xe1fico se queda en la red de Google y el endpoint no se expone p\xfablicamente, lo que adem\xe1s suele reducir la latencia. VPC Service Controls a\xf1ade un per\xedmetro alrededor de la API.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) un endpoint p\xfablico incumple el requisito y las claves incrustadas en apps se filtran. (B) lotes cada hora no punt\xfaan transacciones en tiempo real. (C) una CDN cachea contenido est\xe1tico; no hace privadas las predicciones.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabel tr\xe1fico no debe pasar por internet\xbb / \xabsolo desde nuestra VPC\xbb → endpoint privado (PSC)."},{"id":"11","question":"Un modelo PyTorch necesita preprocesado propio (tokenizaci\xf3n y b\xfasqueda de variables) antes de la inferencia y postprocesado propio (umbrales y reglas de negocio) despu\xe9s, exactamente como en el entrenamiento. El equipo quiere servirlo en Agent Platform con estos pasos empaquetados juntos. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer?","options":{"A":"Construir un contenedor propio (por ejemplo con una Custom Prediction Routine o su propio servidor HTTP) que implemente preprocess → predict → postprocess, subirlo a Artifact Registry, registrarlo en Model Registry y desplegarlo en un endpoint","B":"Usar el contenedor precompilado de scikit-learn y hacer el preprocesado en cada aplicaci\xf3n cliente","C":"Desplegar solo los pesos y pedir a los clientes que env\xeden tensores ya tokenizados","D":"Convertir el modelo en un modelo lineal de BigQuery ML"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Los contenedores precompilados sirven un modelo en un formato est\xe1ndar del framework, pero cuando la inferencia necesita l\xf3gica adicional se empaqueta en un contenedor propio. Las Custom Prediction Routines (CPR) generan ese contenedor a partir de una clase Python con m\xe9todos preprocess, predict y postprocess; como alternativa, escribes tu propio servidor que cumpla los requisitos del contenedor (rutas de health y predict). Poner el mismo c\xf3digo de preprocesado en el contenedor mantiene coherentes entrenamiento y serving y evita el training-serving skew.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) y (C) llevan el preprocesado a cada cliente, lo que duplica la l\xf3gica y provoca skew. (D) cambia el modelo por completo y pierde la arquitectura PyTorch.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabpre/postprocesado propio al servir\xbb → contenedor propio / Custom Prediction Routine; modelo est\xe1ndar → contenedor precompilado."},{"id":"12","question":"Un equipo tiene que ejecutar b\xfasquedas de hiperpar\xe1metros a gran escala y simulaciones distribuidas de aprendizaje por refuerzo escritas con el framework Ray. Quiere un cl\xfaster Ray gestionado en Google Cloud que se integre con sus otros servicios de ML, sin reescribir el c\xf3digo. \xbfQu\xe9 debe usar?","options":{"A":"Reescribir el c\xf3digo como SQL de BigQuery ML","B":"Ejecutar Ray en una sola VM de notebook de Workbench","C":"Ray on Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (antes Ray on Vertex AI), que aprovisiona cl\xfasteres Ray gestionados utilizables desde notebooks o jobs e integrados con los servicios de Agent Platform","D":"Cloud Functions con una funci\xf3n por cada tarea de Ray"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ray on Agent Platform crea y gestiona cl\xfasteres Ray (nodo head y workers, con GPUs si hace falta), as\xed que el c\xf3digo Ray existente —Ray Tune para hiperpar\xe1metros, Ray Train, RLlib, Ray Data— se ejecuta a escala sin gestionar el cl\xfaster. Se conecta con notebooks (Colab Enterprise, Workbench), Model Registry y otros servicios de la plataforma. La gu\xeda del examen lo cita junto a Agent Platform Pipelines y Managed Service for Apache Airflow como opciones de orquestaci\xf3n.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) reescribir en SQL no es viable para simulaciones de RL y tira el c\xf3digo existente. (B) una sola VM limita la escala y convierte el notebook en un cl\xfaster fr\xe1gil. (D) funciones ef\xedmeras no pueden coordinar un job Ray distribuido.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabc\xf3digo Ray existente\xbb / \xabRay Tune, RLlib a escala\xbb → Ray on Agent Platform (o Ray en GKE si se necesita control de Kubernetes)."},{"id":"13","question":"Un modelo funciona bien offline pero mal en producci\xf3n. La investigaci\xf3n muestra que el pipeline de entrenamiento normalizaba una variable con estad\xedsticas calculadas en un job de Dataflow, mientras que el servicio online reimplement\xf3 la normalizaci\xf3n en Java con reglas ligeramente distintas. \xbfCu\xe1l es la mejor soluci\xf3n a largo plazo?","options":{"A":"Reentrenar m\xe1s a menudo para que el modelo se adapte a la normalizaci\xf3n en Java","B":"A\xf1adir m\xe1s capas al modelo para que sea robusto a diferencias de escala","C":"Desactivar la normalizaci\xf3n en el entrenamiento","D":"Definir la transformaci\xf3n una sola vez y reutilizarla en ambos caminos —por ejemplo calcular y guardar las variables en Feature Store, o exportar el grafo de transformaci\xf3n (TensorFlow Transform / una capa de preprocesado dentro del modelo) para que el serving aplique exactamente la misma l\xf3gica— y monitorizar el training-serving skew"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Es training-serving skew causado por dos implementaciones del mismo preprocesado. La soluci\xf3n es una \xfanica fuente de verdad: servir las variables desde Feature Store de Agent Platform (calculadas una vez, le\xeddas online y offline) o enviar la transformaci\xf3n con el modelo (el grafo de TensorFlow Transform, capas de preprocesado de Keras o el mismo c\xf3digo Python en un contenedor de serving propio). Despu\xe9s, Model Monitoring vigila el training-serving skew para detectar regresiones.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) reentrenar no elimina la discrepancia entre pipelines. (B) m\xe1s capacidad del modelo no arregla entradas inconsistentes. (C) quitar la normalizaci\xf3n puede empeorar el modelo y no ataca la causa.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabfunciona offline, falla online\xbb + \xabpreprocesado implementado dos veces\xbb → reutilizar una transformaci\xf3n (Feature Store / TFT / preprocesado dentro del modelo)."},{"id":"14","question":"Un equipo quiere que cada merge a main reconstruya y pruebe su pipeline de entrenamiento, y que los modelos de producci\xf3n se reentrenen al llegar datos etiquetados nuevos o cuando la monitorizaci\xf3n detecte degradaci\xf3n, pero que solo se promuevan si pasan la evaluaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 dise\xf1o implementa CI/CD/CT en Google Cloud?","options":{"A":"Que los ingenieros reentrenen desde sus notebooks cuando se acuerden y copien el fichero del modelo a producci\xf3n","B":"Triggers de Cloud Build en cada merge que construyen los contenedores de los componentes, ejecutan pruebas unitarias y de integraci\xf3n y compilan/despliegan el pipeline de Agent Platform (CI/CD); el pipeline se ejecuta programado, al llegar datos nuevos (por eventos con Pub/Sub/Eventarc) o ante una alerta de monitorizaci\xf3n (CT), e incluye un paso de evaluaci\xf3n que registra y despliega el modelo solo si supera al actual","C":"Reentrenar cada hora y desplegar siempre el modelo m\xe1s reciente","D":"Hacer CI solo de los notebooks y desplegar los modelos a mano"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El MLOps en Google Cloud separa CI (probar el c\xf3digo y los componentes del pipeline), CD (desplegar el pipeline y, a trav\xe9s de \xe9l, los modelos) y CT (entrenamiento continuo). Cloud Build se encarga de CI/CD en cada commit; el pipeline de entrenamiento en Agent Platform Pipelines se dispara por calendario, por eventos de datos nuevos o por alertas de Model Monitoring; y un paso condicional de evaluaci\xf3n (por ejemplo, comparar m\xe9tricas con el modelo de producci\xf3n o con un umbral) decide si se registra en Model Registry y se despliega en el endpoint.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) reentrenar a mano no es fiable ni auditable. (C) reentrenar a ciegas y desplegar siempre puede promover modelos peores. (D) sin CT ni despliegue automatizados los modelos se quedan obsoletos y las publicaciones fallan.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabCI/CD/CT\xbb → Cloud Build para el c\xf3digo, ejecuciones del pipeline por calendario/datos/monitorizaci\xf3n, puerta de evaluaci\xf3n antes de desplegar."},{"id":"15","question":"Las distribuciones de las variables de entrada de un modelo de cr\xe9dito parecen estables en producci\xf3n, pero Model Monitoring indica que el modelo ahora depende mucho m\xe1s de \xabdays_since_last_payment\xbb y mucho menos de \xabincome\xbb que al entrenarlo. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de problema es y qu\xe9 sugiere?","options":{"A":"Training-serving skew causado por un cambio de esquema","B":"Concept drift demostrado por una ca\xedda de accuracy","C":"Feature attribution drift: la contribuci\xf3n de las variables a las predicciones ha cambiado, lo que puede revelar relaciones cambiantes o problemas en los datos aunque las distribuciones de entrada parezcan estables; hay que investigar y valorar reentrenar","D":"Un problema de latencia del endpoint"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Model Monitoring puede vigilar dos familias de se\xf1ales: drift/skew de variables (cambios en la distribuci\xf3n de las entradas frente al entrenamiento o a lo largo del tiempo) y drift/skew de atribuciones (cambios en cu\xe1nto contribuye cada variable a las predicciones, usando las atribuciones de Explainable AI). El drift de atribuciones puede destapar problemas que las estad\xedsticas de entrada no ven —por ejemplo interacciones entre variables que cambian o el pipeline de una variable que se degrada en silencio— y es una se\xf1al para investigar y quiz\xe1 reentrenar.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) el skew compara las entradas de serving con los datos de entrenamiento, y el caso dice que las entradas parecen estables. (B) el concept drift es un cambio en la relaci\xf3n entre variables y objetivo, que se confirma con etiquetas y m\xe9tricas; el caso no da datos de accuracy. (D) nada del caso habla de latencia.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabcambi\xf3 la importancia/atribuci\xf3n de las variables\xbb → feature attribution drift; \xabcambi\xf3 la distribuci\xf3n de entrada\xbb → data drift; \xablas entradas de serving difieren del entrenamiento\xbb → skew."},{"id":"16","question":"Un modelo de aprobaci\xf3n de pr\xe9stamos debe revisarse para detectar resultados injustos entre grupos demogr\xe1ficos antes y despu\xe9s del despliegue, y el regulador pide al banco que explique decisiones individuales. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque sigue las pr\xe1cticas de IA responsable en Google Cloud?","options":{"A":"Evaluar m\xe9tricas de rendimiento y de equidad por segmentos del atributo sensible (por ejemplo con slices de evaluaci\xf3n y m\xe9tricas de fairness), monitorizarlas en producci\xf3n y dar atribuciones de variables por predicci\xf3n con Explainable AI en Agent Platform","B":"Quitar los atributos sensibles de los datos y suponer que as\xed el modelo es justo","C":"Publicar la accuracy global del modelo como prueba de equidad","D":"Usar una red neuronal m\xe1s profunda porque los modelos grandes tienen menos sesgo"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La IA responsable exige medir, no suponer. Se eval\xfaan m\xe9tricas como recall, tasa de falsos positivos o tasa de aprobaci\xf3n por segmento de cada grupo y se comparan (m\xe9tricas de fairness, slices de evaluaci\xf3n), se siguen monitorizando en producci\xf3n porque los cambios en los datos pueden reintroducir sesgo, y se explican las predicciones individuales con atribuciones de Explainable AI (sampled Shapley, integrated gradients, XRAI en im\xe1genes) para justificar las decisiones ante reguladores y clientes.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) quitar los atributos sensibles no elimina variables proxy (c\xf3digo postal, ingresos) que se correlacionan con ellos y hace m\xe1s dif\xedcil medir el sesgo. (C) una buena accuracy global puede esconder errores grandes en un subgrupo. (D) el tama\xf1o del modelo no implica equidad y suele reducir la interpretabilidad.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabmonitorizar sesgo\xbb → m\xe9tricas de fairness por segmento en evaluaci\xf3n y producci\xf3n; \xabexplicar decisiones individuales\xbb → atribuciones de Explainable AI."}]},{"id":"gcp-genai-leader-2026","title":"GCP Generative AI Leader — 2026 exam guide","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"A consulting firm wants to automatically summarize two-hour client workshops. Each recording includes audio, shared slides and whiteboard photos. Which kind of foundation model best fits this use case?","options":{"A":"A small text-only model, after staff manually transcribe each workshop","B":"A multimodal model with a long context window, such as Gemini, that can take the audio, slides and images in one request","C":"An image diffusion model such as Imagen, because most of the content is visual","D":"A new large language model trained from scratch on the firm\'s past workshops"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Choosing a foundation model starts with modality and context window. The input mixes audio, slides and images and is two hours long, so the model must be multimodal and accept a long context. Gemini models do both natively.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) manual transcription removes the automation benefit and loses the visual content. (C) diffusion models generate images; they do not summarize. (D) training an LLM from scratch is far too expensive and slow compared with using an existing foundation model.\\n\\nExam cue: match the model to modality, context window, cost and performance before considering any customization."},{"id":"2","question":"A retailer has two years of purchase history but no predefined customer categories. Marketing wants the data itself to reveal groups of customers with similar behaviour. Which machine learning approach fits?","options":{"A":"Supervised learning, because the purchase history is the label","B":"Reinforcement learning, because customers react to rewards such as discounts","C":"Prompt tuning, because a language model can describe customers","D":"Unsupervised learning, such as clustering, because there are no labels and the goal is to discover structure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Unsupervised learning finds patterns in unlabeled data. Clustering customers into segments is the textbook example: nobody tells the model what the groups are.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) supervised learning needs a known target to predict (for example \'will churn: yes/no\'); purchase history is input, not a label. (B) reinforcement learning trains an agent by trial, error and reward in an environment. (C) prompt tuning adapts a generative model\'s behaviour; it is not a segmentation method.\\n\\nExam cue: labels → supervised; no labels, find groups → unsupervised; learn by reward → reinforcement."},{"id":"3","question":"A gen AI shopping assistant gives contradictory answers about product sizes. Investigation shows the catalog stores the same measurement in centimetres in some records and inches in others, and some products appear twice with different values. Which data quality characteristic is the main problem?","options":{"A":"Consistency","B":"Availability","C":"Relevance","D":"Format"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Consistency means the same fact is represented the same way everywhere. Mixed units and duplicate records with conflicting values are consistency failures, and a model grounded on that data will repeat the contradictions.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) availability is about whether the data can be accessed when needed; here it can. (C) relevance is whether the data matters for the task; product sizes clearly do. (D) format is the file or data type (CSV, JSON, images); both units are stored in the same format, just inconsistently.\\n\\nExam cue: data quality dimensions are completeness, consistency, relevance, availability, cost and format. Gen AI output is only as good as the data behind it."},{"id":"4","question":"An insurer lists its data sources for a gen AI project. Which one is unstructured data?","options":{"A":"A BigQuery table of policies with columns for policy ID, premium and start date","B":"A spreadsheet of claims with one row per claim and fixed columns","C":"Recorded phone calls between customers and claims agents","D":"A relational table that links each policy to its customer ID"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unstructured data has no predefined schema of rows and columns: audio, video, images, free text, PDFs. Recorded calls are audio, so they are unstructured. Gen AI is especially useful here because multimodal models can transcribe, summarize and extract insights from it.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A), (B) and (D) are all tabular, with a fixed schema of typed columns, which is structured data.\\n\\nRelated: labeled data carries a known answer for each example (for example \'claim was fraudulent\'); unlabeled data does not."},{"id":"5","question":"The gen AI landscape is often described in layers: infrastructure, models, platforms, agents and applications. Where does Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI), which provides tools to access, tune, evaluate, deploy and govern models and agents, sit?","options":{"A":"Infrastructure, because it runs on TPUs and GPUs","B":"Models, because it includes Gemini","C":"Platforms, because it is the layer that lets teams build, deploy and manage models and agents","D":"Applications, because end users chat with it directly"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The platform layer sits between models and the things built with them. It gives developers managed tooling to discover, customize, evaluate, deploy and monitor models and agents, with enterprise security and governance. That is the role of Agent Platform.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) infrastructure is the compute, storage and networking (TPUs, GPUs, AI Hypercomputer) that everything runs on. (B) models are Gemini, Gemma, Imagen and others; the platform gives access to them but is not the model. (D) applications are the end-user products, such as the Gemini app or Gemini for Google Workspace.\\n\\nExam cue: infrastructure → models → platforms → agents → applications; know one Google example for each layer."},{"id":"6","question":"A manufacturer wants a lightweight model it can download, fine-tune and run on its own servers and on edge devices on the factory floor, with full control over the weights. Which Google model family fits best?","options":{"A":"Gemma, Google\'s family of open models","B":"Gemini Pro, served only through the managed API","C":"Imagen, Google\'s image generation model","D":"Veo, Google\'s video generation model"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gemma is Google\'s family of lightweight open models, built from the same research as Gemini. You can download the weights, fine-tune them and run them wherever you want, including on-premises and on edge devices.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) Gemini is consumed as a managed service; you do not get the weights to run on your own hardware. (C) Imagen generates and edits images. (D) Veo generates video.\\n\\nExam cue: open weights, self-hosting, edge or full control → Gemma; most capable multimodal reasoning as a managed service → Gemini; images → Imagen; video → Veo."},{"id":"7","question":"A marketing team wants to produce short, high-quality product video clips from text descriptions for social media campaigns. Which Google model is designed for this?","options":{"A":"Gemma","B":"Imagen","C":"Chirp","D":"Veo"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Veo is Google\'s video generation model: it creates video clips from text or image prompts, which is exactly the campaign need.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) Gemma is a family of open text models. (B) Imagen generates still images, not video. (C) Chirp is Google\'s speech model, used for speech recognition.\\n\\nBusiness note: generated media should still go through brand and responsible AI review before publishing; Google adds SynthID watermarks to content generated by these models."},{"id":"8","question":"A board member asks what Google Cloud\'s AI Hypercomputer is and why it matters for the company\'s gen AI plans. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"A single giant server that the company can rent to train one model at a time","B":"An integrated supercomputing architecture that combines AI-optimized hardware (TPUs and GPUs), storage and networking with open software and flexible consumption models","C":"A consumer app that lets employees chat with Gemini","D":"A pricing plan that makes all AI services free for the first year"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AI Hypercomputer is Google Cloud\'s system-level approach to AI infrastructure. It co-designs performance-optimized hardware (Google\'s custom TPUs and NVIDIA GPUs), storage and networking with open software and frameworks (JAX, PyTorch, Kubernetes) and flexible consumption options. For the business this means efficient, scalable training and serving at lower cost per unit of work.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) it is an architecture spanning many systems, not one rentable server. (C) that is the Gemini app, in the applications layer. (D) it is not a pricing plan.\\n\\nExam cue: Google\'s infrastructure strengths are custom TPUs, GPUs, data centers and AI Hypercomputer."},{"id":"9","question":"A sales organization already uses Gmail, Docs, Sheets and Meet. Leadership wants every employee to get AI help drafting emails, summarizing long threads and creating meeting notes, with no custom development. What should they adopt?","options":{"A":"Gemini for Google Workspace","B":"A custom agent built from scratch on Agent Platform","C":"Imagen through the API","D":"Google Cloud Contact Center as a Service"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gemini for Google Workspace brings Gemini into the apps employees already use: it drafts and refines emails in Gmail, summarizes threads and documents, helps build Sheets and takes notes in Meet. It is a prebuilt offering, so value comes quickly without development, under Workspace\'s enterprise data protections.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) a custom agent is the right choice for bespoke processes, not for generic productivity already built in. (C) Imagen generates images. (D) Contact Center as a Service is for customer service operations.\\n\\nExam cue: productivity inside Gmail, Docs, Sheets and Meet → Gemini for Google Workspace."},{"id":"10","question":"Employees at a global company waste hours looking for information spread across Google Drive, Confluence, Jira and SharePoint. The CIO wants one place where staff can search all of it with natural language, respecting each user\'s existing permissions, and use or create agents for common tasks. Which offering fits?","options":{"A":"The Gemini app with a personal Gem","B":"Google Search with a custom search engine","C":"Gemini Enterprise","D":"Imagen with an internal style guide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gemini Enterprise is Google Cloud\'s prebuilt platform for AI-powered work: it connects to enterprise data sources (Google and third-party, such as Confluence, Jira and SharePoint), offers multimodal search that respects source permissions, and lets employees use prebuilt agents or create their own without code.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a personal Gem does not connect to and index company-wide systems with access controls. (B) public Google Search does not see private internal content. (D) Imagen generates images.\\n\\nExam cue: \'search across company data sources plus agents for employees\' → Gemini Enterprise."},{"id":"11","question":"A telecom company\'s human support agents handle complex calls. Managers want the agents to see real-time suggested answers, relevant knowledge articles and an automatic summary at the end of each call. Which Customer Engagement Suite capability provides this?","options":{"A":"Conversational Agents, which replace the human agent with a virtual agent","B":"Agent Assist","C":"Conversational Insights","D":"Grounding with Google Search"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Agent Assist supports human agents during live conversations: it suggests responses and knowledge articles in real time and generates call summaries, which shortens handling time and improves consistency.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) Conversational Agents are virtual agents that talk to customers directly (self-service), not helpers for human agents. (C) Conversational Insights analyzes conversations after the fact (topics, sentiment, trends) for managers. (D) grounding with Google Search connects a model to public web information; it is not a contact-center tool.\\n\\nExam cue: helping the human agent live → Agent Assist; self-service bot → Conversational Agents; analytics on calls → Conversational Insights; full contact-center platform → Contact Center as a Service."},{"id":"12","question":"A product team wants to compare Gemini, open models such as Gemma and Llama, and partner models side by side, then deploy the one that best fits cost and quality, all inside Google Cloud with enterprise controls. Which Agent Platform capability is built for this?","options":{"A":"Google AI Studio","B":"AutoML","C":"Agent Search","D":"Model Garden"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Model Garden is Agent Platform\'s catalog of first-party (Gemini, Imagen, Veo), open (Gemma, Llama and others) and partner models. Teams can discover, test, tune and deploy them with Google Cloud\'s security, governance and billing.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) Google AI Studio is a quick prototyping tool for Gemini models, mainly for individual developers, not the enterprise model catalog. (B) AutoML trains custom models on your own data with little code; it does not compare foundation models. (C) Agent Search (formerly Vertex AI Search) builds search and RAG over your content.\\n\\nExam cue: \'choose among many foundation models\' → Model Garden."},{"id":"13","question":"A customer-service agent built on Gemini must tell customers the real-time status of their orders, which lives in the company\'s order database. What lets the agent do this?","options":{"A":"Increasing the model\'s temperature so it guesses more confidently","B":"Fine-tuning the model every night on the latest order data","C":"Writing the order list into the system prompt once a week","D":"Giving the agent a tool, such as a function or extension that calls an order-status API (for example a Cloud Run service), which the model can invoke when needed"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Agents act on the outside world through tools: functions, extensions, data stores and plugins. The model decides when to call the order-status function, the application executes it against the live system and the result goes back to the model to answer. That keeps answers current and accurate.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) temperature controls randomness; it cannot create data the model does not have. (B) fine-tuning changes behaviour and style, is costly and is stale minutes after it runs. (C) a weekly prompt snapshot is outdated and does not scale.\\n\\nExam cue: live or private data and actions → tools; Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions, databases and pre-built AI APIs are typical tool backends."},{"id":"14","question":"A developer prototyped a Gemini prompt in Google AI Studio using an API key. The bank now wants to put it in production with IAM, VPC Service Controls, data residency, audit logs and its existing Google Cloud billing. Where should the team build it?","options":{"A":"Keep it in Google AI Studio and share the API key with the production team","B":"Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, using Agent Studio and the platform\'s enterprise controls","C":"The Gemini consumer app, with a shared account for the team","D":"A spreadsheet that stores the prompts and responses"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Google AI Studio is ideal for fast experimentation by individual developers. When a solution needs enterprise controls (IAM, VPC Service Controls, CMEK, data residency, audit logging, SLAs and Google Cloud billing), it belongs on Agent Platform, where Agent Studio offers the same prompt design and agent building with governance.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a shared API key provides none of the required controls and is a security risk. (C) the consumer app is not a development platform. (D) a spreadsheet is not a runtime.\\n\\nExam cue: prototype quickly → Google AI Studio; enterprise production with governance → Agent Platform (Agent Studio)."},{"id":"15","question":"An airline\'s gen AI chatbot sometimes invents baggage fees and refund rules that do not exist. What is the most effective Google-recommended way to reduce these hallucinations?","options":{"A":"Ask users to rephrase their question when the answer looks wrong","B":"Increase the maximum output length so the model can explain more","C":"Ground the model on the airline\'s official policy documents, for example with retrieval-augmented generation through Agent Search","D":"Retrain a foundation model from scratch on public travel websites"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hallucinations happen when a model fills gaps with plausible but unsupported text. Grounding connects the model to authoritative sources: with RAG, the system retrieves the relevant policy passages and the model answers from them, ideally with citations. Agent Search provides prebuilt RAG over your documents.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) pushes the problem onto users. (B) longer outputs do not make them more factual and can add more invented detail. (D) training from scratch is extremely costly and public websites are not the airline\'s policy.\\n\\nExam cue: limitations such as knowledge cutoff, hallucinations and bias are addressed with grounding, RAG, prompt engineering, fine-tuning and human in the loop; for factual company answers, grounding comes first."},{"id":"16","question":"A team needs Gemini to classify incoming emails into exactly one of five categories and answer in a strict JSON format. Plain instructions alone give inconsistent formats. Which prompting technique most directly fixes this?","options":{"A":"Few-shot prompting: include several example emails with their correct JSON output in the prompt","B":"Zero-shot prompting with a shorter instruction","C":"Raising the temperature to explore more formats","D":"Prompt chaining that first translates each email into another language"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Few-shot prompting shows the model a handful of input-output examples. The model then imitates both the reasoning and the exact output format, which is the most direct fix for inconsistent formatting. One-shot uses a single example; zero-shot uses none.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) zero-shot is what is already failing. (C) higher temperature increases randomness, the opposite of what you want for a strict format. (D) translation adds a step without addressing the format.\\n\\nExam cue: zero-, one- and few-shot differ in the number of examples; role prompting sets a persona; prompt chaining splits a task into sequential prompts."},{"id":"17","question":"A travel agent built with Gemini must answer questions like \'Can I get from Madrid to Lisbon before 3 p.m. tomorrow and still make my connection?\'. It has to reason step by step, look up live schedules, check the result and decide its next step. Which prompting approach is designed for this pattern?","options":{"A":"Zero-shot prompting","B":"Role prompting only","C":"ReAct prompting, which interleaves reasoning with actions such as tool calls and observes their results","D":"Lowering top-p so the answer is more focused"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ReAct (Reason + Act) prompts the model to alternate thinking (\'I need tomorrow\'s trains\'), acting (calling a schedule tool) and observing the result before reasoning again. It is the basis of many agent loops.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) zero-shot gives no structure for multi-step tool use. (B) a role sets tone and expertise but not a reason-act loop. (D) top-p is a sampling setting; it does not give the model access to live data.\\n\\nRelated: chain-of-thought asks the model to reason step by step without external actions. It helps with logic and math; ReAct adds tool use."},{"id":"18","question":"A compliance team uses Gemini to extract key clauses from contracts. They want answers that are as consistent and deterministic as possible between runs. Which setting change helps most?","options":{"A":"Raise the temperature to 1.5","B":"Raise top-p to 1.0 so all tokens are considered","C":"Disable safety settings","D":"Lower the temperature, close to 0"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Temperature controls randomness in token sampling. A low temperature makes the model consistently pick the most likely tokens, giving focused, repeatable output, which suits extraction and factual tasks. High temperatures suit brainstorming and creative writing.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) higher temperature increases variability. (B) top-p (nucleus sampling) at 1.0 widens the candidate pool, also adding variability. (C) safety settings filter harmful content; disabling them does not improve consistency and adds risk.\\n\\nOther controls: maximum output tokens caps response length and cost; top-p restricts sampling to the most probable tokens whose cumulative probability reaches p."},{"id":"19","question":"The CISO asks which Google framework gives a structured approach to securing AI systems across their lifecycle, covering risks such as prompt injection, data poisoning and model theft. What is it?","options":{"A":"Google\'s Secure AI Framework (SAIF)","B":"The Google Cloud Architecture Framework\'s cost optimization pillar","C":"The ML lifecycle\'s data ingestion stage","D":"Model Garden\'s model cards"},"correct":"A","explanation":"SAIF (Secure AI Framework) is Google\'s conceptual framework for securing AI systems. It extends strong security foundations to AI, extends detection and response, automates defenses, harmonizes platform controls, adapts controls with feedback loops and puts AI risks in business context. It addresses AI-specific threats such as prompt injection, training data poisoning and model exfiltration.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) cost optimization is not about AI security. (C) data ingestion is one lifecycle stage, not a security framework. (D) model cards document a model\'s capabilities and limits; they are transparency artifacts, not a security framework.\\n\\nRelated Google Cloud tools: secure-by-design infrastructure, IAM, Security Command Center (including AI protection) and workload monitoring."},{"id":"20","question":"A healthcare provider wants to use patient feedback to fine-tune a model that drafts replies. Legal requires that analysts and the model never see who wrote each message, but records from the same patient must still be linkable for analysis. Which technique fits this privacy requirement?","options":{"A":"Publish the raw messages internally so everyone can check the model\'s work","B":"Pseudonymize the data: replace direct identifiers with consistent tokens before training, for example with Sensitive Data Protection","C":"Delete every message that mentions a medical condition","D":"Increase the model\'s temperature so outputs do not resemble training data"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pseudonymization replaces identifiers (names, IDs, emails) with consistent surrogate tokens. Records stay linkable for analysis, but people are not directly identifiable. Anonymization goes further and removes linkability entirely. Sensitive Data Protection (formerly Cloud DLP) can find and de-identify such fields automatically.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) exposing raw patient data violates privacy. (C) deleting all medical messages destroys the useful data instead of protecting it. (D) temperature does not protect training data privacy.\\n\\nResponsible AI also covers transparency, fairness and bias, accountability and explainability: keep humans accountable and be able to explain how outputs are produced."}]},{"id":"gcp-genai-leader-2026-es","title":"GCP Generative AI Leader — gu\xeda de examen 2026 (ES)","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"Una consultora quiere resumir autom\xe1ticamente talleres de dos horas con sus clientes. Cada grabaci\xf3n incluye audio, diapositivas compartidas y fotos de la pizarra. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de modelo fundacional encaja mejor?","options":{"A":"Un modelo peque\xf1o solo de texto, despu\xe9s de que el personal transcriba a mano cada taller","B":"Un modelo multimodal con ventana de contexto larga, como Gemini, que acepte audio, diapositivas e im\xe1genes en una sola petici\xf3n","C":"Un modelo de difusi\xf3n de im\xe1genes como Imagen, porque casi todo el contenido es visual","D":"Un LLM nuevo entrenado desde cero con los talleres anteriores de la consultora"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Elegir un modelo fundacional empieza por la modalidad y la ventana de contexto. La entrada mezcla audio, diapositivas e im\xe1genes y dura dos horas, as\xed que el modelo debe ser multimodal y aceptar un contexto largo. Los modelos Gemini hacen ambas cosas de forma nativa.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) transcribir a mano elimina la automatizaci\xf3n y pierde el contenido visual. (C) los modelos de difusi\xf3n generan im\xe1genes; no resumen. (D) entrenar un LLM desde cero es much\xedsimo m\xe1s caro y lento que usar un modelo fundacional existente.\\n\\nPista de examen: ajusta el modelo a modalidad, ventana de contexto, coste y rendimiento antes de pensar en personalizarlo."},{"id":"2","question":"Un retailer tiene dos a\xf1os de historial de compras pero ninguna categor\xeda de clientes predefinida. Marketing quiere que los propios datos revelen grupos de clientes con comportamiento parecido. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque de machine learning encaja?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje supervisado, porque el historial de compras es la etiqueta","B":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo, porque los clientes reaccionan a recompensas como descuentos","C":"Prompt tuning, porque un modelo de lenguaje puede describir a los clientes","D":"Aprendizaje no supervisado, como clustering, porque no hay etiquetas y el objetivo es descubrir estructura"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El aprendizaje no supervisado encuentra patrones en datos sin etiquetar. Agrupar clientes en segmentos es el ejemplo de manual: nadie le dice al modelo cu\xe1les son los grupos.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) el supervisado necesita un objetivo conocido que predecir (por ejemplo \xabse dar\xe1 de baja: s\xed/no\xbb); el historial es la entrada, no una etiqueta. (B) el refuerzo entrena a un agente por prueba, error y recompensa en un entorno. (C) el prompt tuning adapta el comportamiento de un modelo generativo; no es un m\xe9todo de segmentaci\xf3n.\\n\\nPista de examen: con etiquetas → supervisado; sin etiquetas, encontrar grupos → no supervisado; aprender por recompensa → refuerzo."},{"id":"3","question":"Un asistente de compras con IA generativa da respuestas contradictorias sobre tallas. Al investigar, el cat\xe1logo guarda la misma medida en cent\xedmetros en unos registros y en pulgadas en otros, y algunos productos aparecen dos veces con valores distintos. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de calidad de datos es el problema principal?","options":{"A":"Consistencia","B":"Disponibilidad","C":"Relevancia","D":"Formato"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La consistencia significa que un mismo dato se representa igual en todas partes. Unidades mezcladas y registros duplicados con valores en conflicto son fallos de consistencia, y un modelo que se apoye en esos datos repetir\xe1 las contradicciones.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) la disponibilidad trata de si se puede acceder a los datos cuando hace falta; aqu\xed s\xed se puede. (C) la relevancia es si el dato importa para la tarea; las tallas claramente importan. (D) el formato es el tipo de archivo o de dato (CSV, JSON, im\xe1genes); ambas unidades est\xe1n en el mismo formato, solo que de forma incoherente.\\n\\nPista de examen: las dimensiones de calidad son completitud, consistencia, relevancia, disponibilidad, coste y formato. La salida de la IA generativa es tan buena como sus datos."},{"id":"4","question":"Una aseguradora enumera sus fuentes de datos para un proyecto de IA generativa. \xbfCu\xe1l es un dato no estructurado?","options":{"A":"Una tabla de BigQuery de p\xf3lizas con columnas de ID de p\xf3liza, prima y fecha de inicio","B":"Una hoja de c\xe1lculo de siniestros con una fila por siniestro y columnas fijas","C":"Las llamadas grabadas entre clientes y agentes de siniestros","D":"Una tabla relacional que vincula cada p\xf3liza con el ID de su cliente"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los datos no estructurados no tienen un esquema predefinido de filas y columnas: audio, v\xeddeo, im\xe1genes, texto libre, PDF. Las llamadas grabadas son audio, as\xed que son no estructuradas. Aqu\xed la IA generativa resulta especialmente \xfatil porque los modelos multimodales pueden transcribirlas, resumirlas y extraer informaci\xf3n.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A), (B) y (D) son tabulares, con un esquema fijo de columnas tipadas: datos estructurados.\\n\\nRelacionado: los datos etiquetados llevan la respuesta conocida de cada ejemplo (por ejemplo \xabel siniestro fue fraudulento\xbb); los no etiquetados, no."},{"id":"5","question":"El panorama de la IA generativa se suele describir en capas: infraestructura, modelos, plataformas, agentes y aplicaciones. \xbfD\xf3nde se sit\xfaa Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (antes Vertex AI), que ofrece herramientas para acceder, ajustar, evaluar, desplegar y gobernar modelos y agentes?","options":{"A":"Infraestructura, porque se ejecuta sobre TPU y GPU","B":"Modelos, porque incluye Gemini","C":"Plataformas, porque es la capa que permite construir, desplegar y gestionar modelos y agentes","D":"Aplicaciones, porque los usuarios finales chatean con ella directamente"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La capa de plataforma est\xe1 entre los modelos y lo que se construye con ellos. Da a los desarrolladores herramientas gestionadas para descubrir, personalizar, evaluar, desplegar y monitorizar modelos y agentes, con seguridad y gobierno empresarial. Ese es el papel de Agent Platform.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) la infraestructura es el c\xf3mputo, almacenamiento y red (TPU, GPU, AI Hypercomputer) sobre el que corre todo. (B) los modelos son Gemini, Gemma, Imagen y otros; la plataforma da acceso a ellos pero no es el modelo. (D) las aplicaciones son los productos de usuario final, como la app de Gemini o Gemini para Google Workspace.\\n\\nPista de examen: infraestructura → modelos → plataformas → agentes → aplicaciones; conoce un ejemplo de Google en cada capa."},{"id":"6","question":"Un fabricante quiere un modelo ligero que pueda descargar, ajustar y ejecutar en sus propios servidores y en dispositivos edge de la planta, con control total de los pesos. \xbfQu\xe9 familia de modelos de Google encaja mejor?","options":{"A":"Gemma, la familia de modelos abiertos de Google","B":"Gemini Pro, servido solo mediante la API gestionada","C":"Imagen, el modelo de generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes de Google","D":"Veo, el modelo de generaci\xf3n de v\xeddeo de Google"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gemma es la familia de modelos abiertos y ligeros de Google, creada a partir de la misma investigaci\xf3n que Gemini. Puedes descargar los pesos, ajustarlos y ejecutarlos donde quieras, incluido on-premises y en dispositivos edge.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) Gemini se consume como servicio gestionado; no recibes los pesos para ejecutarlo en tu hardware. (C) Imagen genera y edita im\xe1genes. (D) Veo genera v\xeddeo.\\n\\nPista de examen: pesos abiertos, autoalojamiento, edge o control total → Gemma; razonamiento multimodal m\xe1s capaz como servicio gestionado → Gemini; im\xe1genes → Imagen; v\xeddeo → Veo."},{"id":"7","question":"Un equipo de marketing quiere producir clips de v\xeddeo cortos y de alta calidad a partir de descripciones de texto para campa\xf1as en redes sociales. \xbfQu\xe9 modelo de Google est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para esto?","options":{"A":"Gemma","B":"Imagen","C":"Chirp","D":"Veo"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Veo es el modelo de generaci\xf3n de v\xeddeo de Google: crea clips a partir de prompts de texto o imagen, que es justo lo que pide la campa\xf1a.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) Gemma es una familia de modelos de texto abiertos. (B) Imagen genera im\xe1genes fijas, no v\xeddeo. (C) Chirp es el modelo de voz de Google, para reconocimiento del habla.\\n\\nNota de negocio: el contenido generado debe pasar igualmente revisi\xf3n de marca y de IA responsable antes de publicarse; Google a\xf1ade marcas de agua SynthID al contenido generado con estos modelos."},{"id":"8","question":"Un miembro del consejo pregunta qu\xe9 es AI Hypercomputer de Google Cloud y por qu\xe9 importa para los planes de IA generativa de la empresa. \xbfQu\xe9 descripci\xf3n es correcta?","options":{"A":"Un \xfanico servidor gigante que la empresa puede alquilar para entrenar un modelo a la vez","B":"Una arquitectura de supercomputaci\xf3n integrada que combina hardware optimizado para IA (TPU y GPU), almacenamiento y red con software abierto y modelos de consumo flexibles","C":"Una app de consumo para que los empleados chateen con Gemini","D":"Un plan de precios que hace gratuitos todos los servicios de IA el primer a\xf1o"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AI Hypercomputer es el enfoque de sistema completo de Google Cloud para la infraestructura de IA. Dise\xf1a conjuntamente hardware optimizado (las TPU propias de Google y GPU de NVIDIA), almacenamiento y red con software y frameworks abiertos (JAX, PyTorch, Kubernetes) y opciones de consumo flexibles. Para el negocio significa entrenar y servir de forma eficiente y escalable, con menor coste por unidad de trabajo.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) es una arquitectura que abarca muchos sistemas, no un servidor alquilable. (C) eso es la app de Gemini, en la capa de aplicaciones. (D) no es un plan de precios.\\n\\nPista de examen: las fortalezas de infraestructura de Google son las TPU propias, las GPU, los centros de datos y AI Hypercomputer."},{"id":"9","question":"Una organizaci\xf3n de ventas ya usa Gmail, Docs, Sheets y Meet. La direcci\xf3n quiere que todos los empleados tengan ayuda de IA para redactar correos, resumir hilos largos y crear notas de reuniones, sin desarrollo a medida. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedan adoptar?","options":{"A":"Gemini para Google Workspace","B":"Un agente personalizado creado desde cero en Agent Platform","C":"Imagen a trav\xe9s de la API","D":"Google Cloud Contact Center as a Service"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gemini para Google Workspace integra Gemini en las apps que los empleados ya usan: redacta y mejora correos en Gmail, resume hilos y documentos, ayuda a construir hojas en Sheets y toma notas en Meet. Es una oferta predise\xf1ada, as\xed que aporta valor r\xe1pido sin desarrollo y con las protecciones de datos empresariales de Workspace.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) un agente a medida es para procesos propios, no para productividad gen\xe9rica que ya viene integrada. (C) Imagen genera im\xe1genes. (D) Contact Center as a Service es para operaciones de atenci\xf3n al cliente.\\n\\nPista de examen: productividad dentro de Gmail, Docs, Sheets y Meet → Gemini para Google Workspace."},{"id":"10","question":"Los empleados de una empresa global pierden horas buscando informaci\xf3n repartida entre Google Drive, Confluence, Jira y SharePoint. El CIO quiere un \xfanico lugar donde buscar en todo ello con lenguaje natural, respetando los permisos existentes de cada usuario, y usar o crear agentes para tareas habituales. \xbfQu\xe9 oferta encaja?","options":{"A":"La app de Gemini con un Gem personal","B":"Google Search con un buscador personalizado","C":"Gemini Enterprise","D":"Imagen con una gu\xeda de estilo interna"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gemini Enterprise es la plataforma predise\xf1ada de Google Cloud para el trabajo con IA: se conecta a fuentes de datos empresariales (de Google y de terceros, como Confluence, Jira y SharePoint), ofrece b\xfasqueda multimodal que respeta los permisos de origen y permite usar agentes predise\xf1ados o crear los propios sin c\xf3digo.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) un Gem personal no conecta ni indexa los sistemas de toda la empresa con control de acceso. (B) la b\xfasqueda p\xfablica de Google no ve contenido interno privado. (D) Imagen genera im\xe1genes.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabbuscar en las fuentes de datos de la empresa y agentes para empleados\xbb → Gemini Enterprise."},{"id":"11","question":"Los agentes humanos de soporte de una teleco atienden llamadas complejas. Los responsables quieren que vean en tiempo real respuestas sugeridas, art\xedculos de conocimiento relevantes y un resumen autom\xe1tico al final de cada llamada. \xbfQu\xe9 capacidad de Customer Engagement Suite lo proporciona?","options":{"A":"Conversational Agents, que sustituyen al agente humano por un agente virtual","B":"Agent Assist","C":"Conversational Insights","D":"Grounding con Google Search"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Agent Assist ayuda a los agentes humanos durante la conversaci\xf3n: sugiere respuestas y art\xedculos de conocimiento en tiempo real y genera res\xfamenes de llamada, lo que reduce el tiempo de gesti\xf3n y mejora la coherencia.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) Conversational Agents son agentes virtuales que hablan directamente con el cliente (autoservicio), no ayudantes del agente humano. (C) Conversational Insights analiza las conversaciones a posteriori (temas, sentimiento, tendencias) para los responsables. (D) el grounding con Google Search conecta un modelo con informaci\xf3n p\xfablica de la web; no es una herramienta de contact center.\\n\\nPista de examen: ayudar al agente humano en directo → Agent Assist; bot de autoservicio → Conversational Agents; anal\xedtica de llamadas → Conversational Insights; plataforma completa → Contact Center as a Service."},{"id":"12","question":"Un equipo de producto quiere comparar Gemini, modelos abiertos como Gemma y Llama y modelos de socios, y desplegar el que mejor equilibre coste y calidad, todo dentro de Google Cloud con controles empresariales. \xbfQu\xe9 capacidad de Agent Platform est\xe1 pensada para esto?","options":{"A":"Google AI Studio","B":"AutoML","C":"Agent Search","D":"Model Garden"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Model Garden es el cat\xe1logo de Agent Platform con modelos propios (Gemini, Imagen, Veo), abiertos (Gemma, Llama y otros) y de socios. Los equipos pueden descubrirlos, probarlos, ajustarlos y desplegarlos con la seguridad, el gobierno y la facturaci\xf3n de Google Cloud.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) Google AI Studio es una herramienta de prototipado r\xe1pido con Gemini, sobre todo para desarrolladores individuales, no el cat\xe1logo empresarial de modelos. (B) AutoML entrena modelos propios con tus datos y poco c\xf3digo; no compara modelos fundacionales. (C) Agent Search (antes Vertex AI Search) crea b\xfasqueda y RAG sobre tu contenido.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabelegir entre muchos modelos fundacionales\xbb → Model Garden."},{"id":"13","question":"Un agente de atenci\xf3n al cliente basado en Gemini debe decir a los clientes el estado en tiempo real de sus pedidos, que est\xe1 en la base de datos de pedidos de la empresa. \xbfQu\xe9 permite al agente hacerlo?","options":{"A":"Subir la temperatura del modelo para que adivine con m\xe1s seguridad","B":"Hacer fine-tuning del modelo cada noche con los \xfaltimos pedidos","C":"Escribir la lista de pedidos en el prompt de sistema una vez por semana","D":"Darle al agente una herramienta, como una funci\xf3n o extensi\xf3n que llame a una API de estado de pedidos (por ejemplo un servicio de Cloud Run), que el modelo invoca cuando la necesita"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Los agentes act\xfaan sobre el mundo exterior mediante herramientas: funciones, extensiones, data stores y plugins. El modelo decide cu\xe1ndo llamar a la funci\xf3n de estado del pedido, la aplicaci\xf3n la ejecuta contra el sistema real y el resultado vuelve al modelo para responder. As\xed las respuestas son actuales y exactas.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) la temperatura controla la aleatoriedad; no crea datos que el modelo no tiene. (B) el fine-tuning cambia comportamiento y estilo, es caro y queda desfasado minutos despu\xe9s. (C) una foto semanal en el prompt est\xe1 desactualizada y no escala.\\n\\nPista de examen: datos vivos o privados y acciones → herramientas; Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions, bases de datos y las APIs de IA predise\xf1adas son backends t\xedpicos."},{"id":"14","question":"Un desarrollador prototip\xf3 un prompt de Gemini en Google AI Studio con una API key. El banco quiere ahora llevarlo a producci\xf3n con IAM, VPC Service Controls, residencia de datos, logs de auditor\xeda y su facturaci\xf3n existente de Google Cloud. \xbfD\xf3nde deber\xeda construirlo el equipo?","options":{"A":"Mantenerlo en Google AI Studio y compartir la API key con el equipo de producci\xf3n","B":"En Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, usando Agent Studio y los controles empresariales de la plataforma","C":"En la app de consumo de Gemini, con una cuenta compartida para el equipo","D":"En una hoja de c\xe1lculo que guarde los prompts y las respuestas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Google AI Studio es ideal para experimentar r\xe1pido como desarrollador individual. Cuando la soluci\xf3n necesita controles empresariales (IAM, VPC Service Controls, CMEK, residencia de datos, auditor\xeda, SLA y facturaci\xf3n de Google Cloud), su sitio es Agent Platform, donde Agent Studio ofrece el mismo dise\xf1o de prompts y creaci\xf3n de agentes con gobierno.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) una API key compartida no da ninguno de esos controles y es un riesgo de seguridad. (C) la app de consumo no es una plataforma de desarrollo. (D) una hoja de c\xe1lculo no es un entorno de ejecuci\xf3n.\\n\\nPista de examen: prototipar r\xe1pido → Google AI Studio; producci\xf3n empresarial con gobierno → Agent Platform (Agent Studio)."},{"id":"15","question":"El chatbot de IA generativa de una aerol\xednea a veces se inventa tasas de equipaje y normas de reembolso que no existen. \xbfCu\xe1l es la forma m\xe1s eficaz, recomendada por Google, de reducir estas alucinaciones?","options":{"A":"Pedir a los usuarios que reformulen la pregunta cuando la respuesta parezca err\xf3nea","B":"Aumentar la longitud m\xe1xima de salida para que el modelo explique m\xe1s","C":"Hacer grounding del modelo en los documentos oficiales de pol\xedticas de la aerol\xednea, por ejemplo con RAG mediante Agent Search","D":"Reentrenar un modelo fundacional desde cero con webs de viajes p\xfablicas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las alucinaciones ocurren cuando un modelo rellena huecos con texto plausible pero sin respaldo. El grounding conecta el modelo con fuentes autorizadas: con RAG, el sistema recupera los fragmentos relevantes de la pol\xedtica y el modelo responde a partir de ellos, idealmente con citas. Agent Search ofrece RAG predise\xf1ado sobre tus documentos.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) traslada el problema a los usuarios. (B) salidas m\xe1s largas no son m\xe1s fieles y pueden a\xf1adir m\xe1s detalles inventados. (D) entrenar desde cero es car\xedsimo y las webs p\xfablicas no son la pol\xedtica de la aerol\xednea.\\n\\nPista de examen: las limitaciones (fecha de corte del conocimiento, alucinaciones, sesgo) se abordan con grounding, RAG, ingenier\xeda de prompts, fine-tuning y human in the loop; para respuestas fieles sobre la empresa, primero grounding."},{"id":"16","question":"Un equipo necesita que Gemini clasifique los correos entrantes en exactamente una de cinco categor\xedas y responda en un formato JSON estricto. Las instrucciones solas dan formatos inconsistentes. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica de prompting lo arregla m\xe1s directamente?","options":{"A":"Few-shot prompting: incluir en el prompt varios correos de ejemplo con su salida JSON correcta","B":"Zero-shot prompting con una instrucci\xf3n m\xe1s corta","C":"Subir la temperatura para explorar m\xe1s formatos","D":"Prompt chaining que primero traduzca cada correo a otro idioma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El few-shot prompting muestra al modelo unos cuantos ejemplos de entrada y salida. El modelo imita tanto el razonamiento como el formato exacto de salida, que es la soluci\xf3n m\xe1s directa a un formato inconsistente. One-shot usa un ejemplo; zero-shot, ninguno.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) zero-shot es lo que ya est\xe1 fallando. (C) m\xe1s temperatura aumenta la aleatoriedad, lo contrario de lo que pide un formato estricto. (D) traducir a\xf1ade un paso sin resolver el formato.\\n\\nPista de examen: zero-, one- y few-shot se diferencian en el n\xfamero de ejemplos; el role prompting fija un personaje; el prompt chaining divide una tarea en prompts secuenciales."},{"id":"17","question":"Un agente de viajes creado con Gemini debe responder preguntas como \xab\xbfPuedo ir de Madrid a Lisboa antes de las 15:00 ma\xf1ana y llegar a mi conexi\xf3n?\xbb. Tiene que razonar paso a paso, consultar horarios en vivo, comprobar el resultado y decidir el siguiente paso. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque de prompting est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para este patr\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Zero-shot prompting","B":"Solo role prompting","C":"ReAct prompting, que alterna razonamiento con acciones como llamadas a herramientas y observa sus resultados","D":"Bajar top-p para que la respuesta sea m\xe1s enfocada"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ReAct (Reason + Act) hace que el modelo alterne pensar (\xabnecesito los trenes de ma\xf1ana\xbb), actuar (llamar a una herramienta de horarios) y observar el resultado antes de volver a razonar. Es la base de muchos bucles de agente.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) zero-shot no da estructura para usar herramientas en varios pasos. (B) un rol fija el tono y la experiencia, no un bucle razonar-actuar. (D) top-p es un par\xe1metro de muestreo; no da acceso a datos en vivo.\\n\\nRelacionado: chain-of-thought pide razonar paso a paso sin acciones externas. Ayuda en l\xf3gica y c\xe1lculo; ReAct a\xf1ade el uso de herramientas."},{"id":"18","question":"Un equipo de cumplimiento usa Gemini para extraer cl\xe1usulas clave de contratos. Quiere respuestas lo m\xe1s consistentes y deterministas posible entre ejecuciones. \xbfQu\xe9 cambio de configuraci\xf3n ayuda m\xe1s?","options":{"A":"Subir la temperatura a 1,5","B":"Subir top-p a 1,0 para considerar todos los tokens","C":"Desactivar los ajustes de seguridad","D":"Bajar la temperatura, cerca de 0"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La temperatura controla la aleatoriedad al muestrear tokens. Con temperatura baja el modelo elige casi siempre los tokens m\xe1s probables y da salidas enfocadas y repetibles, ideales para extracci\xf3n y tareas factuales. Las temperaturas altas sirven para lluvia de ideas y escritura creativa.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) m\xe1s temperatura aumenta la variabilidad. (B) top-p (nucleus sampling) en 1,0 ampl\xeda el conjunto de candidatos y tambi\xe9n a\xf1ade variabilidad. (C) los ajustes de seguridad filtran contenido da\xf1ino; desactivarlos no mejora la consistencia y a\xf1ade riesgo.\\n\\nOtros controles: el m\xe1ximo de tokens de salida limita la longitud y el coste; top-p restringe el muestreo a los tokens m\xe1s probables cuya probabilidad acumulada llega a p."},{"id":"19","question":"El CISO pregunta qu\xe9 marco de Google da un enfoque estructurado para proteger los sistemas de IA a lo largo de su ciclo de vida, cubriendo riesgos como prompt injection, envenenamiento de datos y robo de modelos. \xbfCu\xe1l es?","options":{"A":"El Secure AI Framework (SAIF) de Google","B":"El pilar de optimizaci\xf3n de costes del Google Cloud Architecture Framework","C":"La etapa de ingesta de datos del ciclo de vida de ML","D":"Las model cards de Model Garden"},"correct":"A","explanation":"SAIF (Secure AI Framework) es el marco conceptual de Google para proteger sistemas de IA. Extiende unas bases de seguridad s\xf3lidas a la IA, ampl\xeda la detecci\xf3n y respuesta, automatiza las defensas, armoniza los controles de plataforma, adapta los controles con bucles de retroalimentaci\xf3n y sit\xfaa los riesgos de IA en el contexto del negocio. Aborda amenazas propias de la IA como prompt injection, envenenamiento de datos de entrenamiento y exfiltraci\xf3n de modelos.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) la optimizaci\xf3n de costes no trata de seguridad de IA. (C) la ingesta es una etapa del ciclo de vida, no un marco de seguridad. (D) las model cards documentan capacidades y l\xedmites de un modelo; son transparencia, no un marco de seguridad.\\n\\nHerramientas de Google Cloud relacionadas: infraestructura segura por dise\xf1o, IAM, Security Command Center (incluida la protecci\xf3n de IA) y la monitorizaci\xf3n de cargas."},{"id":"20","question":"Un proveedor sanitario quiere usar las opiniones de pacientes para hacer fine-tuning de un modelo que redacta respuestas. Legal exige que ni los analistas ni el modelo vean qui\xe9n escribi\xf3 cada mensaje, pero los registros de un mismo paciente deben poder relacionarse para el an\xe1lisis. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica cumple este requisito de privacidad?","options":{"A":"Publicar los mensajes en bruto internamente para que todos revisen el trabajo del modelo","B":"Seudonimizar los datos: sustituir los identificadores directos por tokens consistentes antes de entrenar, por ejemplo con Sensitive Data Protection","C":"Borrar cada mensaje que mencione una condici\xf3n m\xe9dica","D":"Subir la temperatura del modelo para que las salidas no se parezcan a los datos de entrenamiento"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La seudonimizaci\xf3n sustituye los identificadores (nombres, IDs, correos) por tokens sustitutos consistentes. Los registros siguen pudiendo relacionarse para el an\xe1lisis, pero las personas no son identificables directamente. La anonimizaci\xf3n va m\xe1s all\xe1 y elimina tambi\xe9n la posibilidad de relacionarlos. Sensitive Data Protection (antes Cloud DLP) puede detectar y desidentificar estos campos autom\xe1ticamente.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) exponer datos de pacientes en bruto viola la privacidad. (C) borrar todos los mensajes m\xe9dicos destruye los datos \xfatiles en vez de protegerlos. (D) la temperatura no protege la privacidad de los datos de entrenamiento.\\n\\nLa IA responsable tambi\xe9n abarca transparencia, equidad y sesgo, responsabilidad y explicabilidad: que haya personas responsables y que se pueda explicar c\xf3mo se generan las salidas."}]},{"id":"gcp-ace-2026","title":"GCP Associate Cloud Engineer — 2026 exam guide","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"Your company must keep all new resources for its European business unit inside EU regions. The unit\'s projects live under a folder named `eu`. Developers have Editor on their projects. What is the most effective control?","options":{"A":"Remove the Editor role and grant developers Viewer only","B":"Set the `gcp.resourceLocations` organization policy on the `eu` folder to allow only EU locations","C":"Create a Cloud Monitoring alert that fires when a resource is created outside the EU","D":"Ask developers to always pass `--region=europe-west1` in their gcloud commands"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Organization policies are preventive and inherited down the resource hierarchy. Setting the resource locations constraint (for example `in:eu-locations`) on the folder blocks creation of location-bound resources outside the EU for every project below it, whatever IAM role the developer has.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) IAM controls who can act, not where; Viewer would also stop developers from working. (C) an alert detects violations after they happen. (D) a convention is not enforcement.\\n\\nExam cue: \'must never\', \'across all projects in a folder\' → organization policy at the folder or organization node."},{"id":"2","question":"Your company\'s employees sign in with Microsoft Entra ID. Security does not want to synchronize user accounts into Cloud Identity, but employees need console and gcloud access to Google Cloud with their existing corporate sign-in. What should you configure?","options":{"A":"Create a service account key for each employee","B":"Create a consumer Gmail account for each employee and grant it IAM roles","C":"Set up Google Cloud Directory Sync to copy every user into Cloud Identity","D":"Workforce Identity Federation with a workforce pool and provider for Entra ID, then grant IAM roles to the pool\'s principals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Workforce Identity Federation lets users from an external identity provider (Entra ID, Okta and other OIDC or SAML providers) access Google Cloud directly with their own credentials. No user accounts are synchronized; you grant IAM roles to `principal://` or `principalSet://` identifiers from the workforce pool.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) service account keys are for workloads, are long-lived secrets and remove individual accountability. (B) personal Gmail accounts are outside corporate control. (C) Directory Sync is the synchronization approach security wants to avoid.\\n\\nRelated: Workload Identity Federation is the equivalent for workloads (CI/CD, other clouds) instead of people."},{"id":"3","question":"Finance wants to analyze Google Cloud costs by project, service, SKU and custom labels with SQL, and build dashboards that go back more than a year. What should you set up?","options":{"A":"Cloud Billing export to BigQuery (standard or detailed usage cost data)","B":"A budget with an email alert at 90% of the monthly amount","C":"Downloading the monthly invoice PDF and copying totals into a spreadsheet","D":"A Cloud Monitoring dashboard of CPU utilization for all VMs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Billing export to BigQuery continuously writes detailed cost and usage records (project, service, SKU, labels, credits) to a dataset you own. Finance can query it with SQL and connect Looker Studio or other BI tools. Data is only exported from the moment you enable it, so enable it early.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) budgets alert on thresholds but do not provide analyzable detail. (C) invoices are aggregated and manual. (D) CPU utilization is not cost.\\n\\nExam cue: \'analyze costs with SQL\', \'by label\' → billing export to BigQuery; \'notify when spend reaches X\' → budgets and alerts."},{"id":"4","question":"A deployment fails with an error saying the regional CPUS quota in `us-central1` is exceeded. The workload legitimately needs more capacity in that region. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Delete the project and create a new one, which resets quotas","B":"Switch every VM to a larger machine type so fewer VMs are needed","C":"Review the quota on the Quotas page (Cloud Quotas) and request a quota increase for that region","D":"Open a firewall rule for the deployment\'s IP range"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Quotas protect both you and the platform. When a legitimate workload needs more, you view current usage and limits in the console\'s Quotas page (or with the Cloud Quotas API or gcloud) and submit an increase request for the specific quota and region. You can also set up quota alerts to know before hitting limits.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) new projects get default quotas, often lower, and you lose everything. (B) larger machine types use the same vCPU quota. (D) firewall rules are unrelated to quotas.\\n\\nExam cue: \'quota exceeded\' → check usage, request increase; plan quotas before large launches."},{"id":"5","question":"A nightly batch job renders thousands of independent images. It checkpoints its progress and can be restarted at any time. You want the lowest possible Compute Engine cost. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Sole-tenant nodes with committed use discounts","B":"Standard on-demand VMs in a single large instance","C":"Spot VMs in a managed instance group","D":"Regional persistent disks attached to every VM"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Spot VMs offer deep discounts (often 60–91% off) in exchange for possible preemption at any time. A fault-tolerant, checkpointing batch job is the ideal workload, and a managed instance group recreates preempted VMs when capacity returns.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) sole-tenant nodes are for isolation or licensing and cost more. (B) on-demand is the most expensive option and one VM does not parallelize the work. (D) regional disks add cost for high availability the job does not need.\\n\\nRelated: custom machine types let you pick the exact vCPU and memory ratio, avoiding paying for unused resources."},{"id":"6","question":"A small team wants to run containerized microservices on Kubernetes but does not want to manage nodes, node pools, upgrades or capacity planning, and prefers to pay for the resources their Pods request. Which option fits?","options":{"A":"A GKE Autopilot cluster","B":"A GKE Standard zonal cluster with manually sized node pools","C":"Compute Engine VMs with Docker installed","D":"A self-managed Kubernetes cluster built with kubeadm on Compute Engine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"GKE Autopilot is the mode where Google manages the nodes, scaling, security hardening and upgrades. You deploy workloads and are billed mainly for the CPU, memory and storage your Pods request. It keeps the full Kubernetes API.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) Standard mode makes you size and manage node pools. (C) plain VMs with Docker give up Kubernetes orchestration. (D) a self-managed cluster is the most operational work of all.\\n\\nExam cue: \'Kubernetes without managing nodes\', \'pay per Pod\' → Autopilot; \'need custom node configuration or specific hardware control\' → Standard."},{"id":"7","question":"Each time a PDF is uploaded to a Cloud Storage bucket, you need to extract its text and store the result, with no servers to manage and no polling. What should you deploy?","options":{"A":"A Compute Engine VM that lists the bucket every minute","B":"A Cloud Scheduler job that runs a script every hour","C":"A GKE Deployment that keeps a persistent connection to the bucket","D":"A Cloud Run function triggered through Eventarc by the Cloud Storage object finalized event"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eventarc delivers Cloud Storage events (such as `google.cloud.storage.object.v1.finalized`) to Cloud Run services or Cloud Run functions. The function runs only when a file arrives, scales automatically, including to zero, and needs no servers.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a polling VM costs money while idle and reacts late. (B) an hourly schedule adds latency and processes files in batches. (C) a GKE Deployment adds cluster management for an event-driven task.\\n\\nExam cue: \'when a file is uploaded\', \'Pub/Sub message\', \'audit log event\' → Eventarc or a direct trigger on Cloud Run functions."},{"id":"8","question":"You must store quarterly compliance reports. Each is read about once every three months for an audit and must remain available within milliseconds when requested. You want the lowest storage cost for this pattern. Which Cloud Storage class should you choose?","options":{"A":"Standard","B":"Coldline","C":"Archive","D":"Nearline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Coldline is designed for data accessed less than once a quarter, with a 90-day minimum storage duration. Its storage price is lower than Nearline\'s, and all Cloud Storage classes return data with millisecond latency.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) Standard is for frequently accessed data and costs the most to store. (C) Archive is cheaper to store but targets data accessed less than once a year, with a 365-day minimum and higher retrieval costs. (D) Nearline (30-day minimum) suits roughly monthly access; for quarterly access Coldline is cheaper.\\n\\nRule of thumb: Standard (hot) → Nearline (~monthly) → Coldline (~quarterly) → Archive (~yearly)."},{"id":"9","question":"Security wants firewall rules that target VMs by an attribute that only an authorized IAM role can attach, so that a project Editor cannot simply add a label to a VM to bypass a rule. What should you use in the Cloud NGFW policy rules?","options":{"A":"Secure Tags (IAM-governed Resource Manager tags) as source and target","B":"Network tags, which any user who can edit the VM can set","C":"VM instance names in the rule\'s target field","D":"The VM\'s external IP addresses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Secure Tags are Resource Manager tags whose attachment is controlled by IAM (for example the Tag User role). Used in Cloud NGFW network firewall policies, they let security define who can bind a VM to a rule, so a project Editor cannot change which rules apply to their VM.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) network tags are free-form strings that anyone with permission to edit the instance can change. (C) firewall rules do not target by instance name. (D) IP addresses change and do not express identity.\\n\\nRelated: rules can also target by service account; firewall rule attributes include action, direction (ingress or egress), source, destination, target, protocols, ports and priority."},{"id":"10","question":"A public HTTPS web application runs on managed instance groups in `us-east1` and `europe-west1`. Users worldwide should reach the closest healthy backend through a single IP address, with TLS terminated at the edge. Which load balancer should you choose?","options":{"A":"A regional external passthrough Network Load Balancer","B":"An internal Application Load Balancer","C":"A global external Application Load Balancer","D":"A regional internal passthrough Network Load Balancer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The global external Application Load Balancer (HTTP/HTTPS, layer 7) uses a single anycast IP, terminates TLS at Google\'s edge and routes each user to the nearest backend with capacity and passing health checks, across regions. It also supports Cloud CDN and Cloud Armor.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a passthrough network load balancer is regional and layer 4, without TLS termination at the edge or cross-region routing. (B) and (D) internal load balancers are for traffic inside the VPC, not public users.\\n\\nExam cue: global HTTP(S) users → global external Application LB; TCP/UDP preserving client IP → passthrough Network LB; private traffic → internal LBs. Premium Network Service Tier is required for global load balancing."},{"id":"11","question":"Pods in a GKE Standard cluster fail with `ImagePullBackOff` when pulling an image from Artifact Registry in another project. The nodes use a custom service account. What is the most likely fix?","options":{"A":"Make the Artifact Registry repository public on the internet","B":"Grant the node service account the Artifact Registry Reader role (`roles/artifactregistry.reader`) on the repository or its project","C":"Give the node service account the Owner role on both projects","D":"Recreate the cluster as a zonal cluster"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GKE nodes pull images using the node pool\'s service account. With a custom service account, or a repository in another project, that account needs `roles/artifactregistry.reader` on the repository (or project). Granting it fixes the pull with least privilege.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) making images public exposes them to everyone. (C) Owner is grossly over-privileged. (D) the cluster\'s location type is unrelated to image authorization.\\n\\nTroubleshooting: `kubectl describe pod` shows the pull error; check the image path (`REGION-docker.pkg.dev/PROJECT/REPO/IMAGE:TAG`) and the node service account\'s IAM."},{"id":"12","question":"You need to release a new version of a Cloud Run service with minimal risk: deploy it without sending it any users, test it, then move 10% of traffic and increase gradually. How do you do this?","options":{"A":"Delete the service and redeploy it with the new image","B":"Deploy the new image to a second, separate Cloud Run service and change DNS manually","C":"Scale the current revision to zero instances while the new one starts","D":"Deploy a new revision with `--no-traffic` (optionally with a tag for a test URL), then use `gcloud run services update-traffic` to shift 10% and increase step by step"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Each Cloud Run deployment creates an immutable revision. With `--no-traffic` the new revision receives no users; a revision tag gives it its own URL for testing. Then `update-traffic --to-revisions=NEW=10` performs a canary split, and you raise the percentage or roll back instantly to the previous revision.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) deleting causes downtime and loses rollback. (B) a separate service with DNS changes is slower and has no percentage split. (C) Cloud Run does not let you scale a serving revision to zero manually while it receives traffic, and this would still cause an abrupt switch.\\n\\nExam cue: gradual rollout or canary on Cloud Run → revisions plus traffic splitting."},{"id":"13","question":"Application logs are written to a Cloud Storage bucket. They are read often for 30 days, rarely after that, and must be deleted after one year. What is the simplest way to minimize cost automatically?","options":{"A":"Run a weekly script that copies old objects to a new bucket and deletes the originals","B":"Enable Object Versioning so old logs are kept as noncurrent versions","C":"Use Archive class for all logs from the start","D":"Configure Object Lifecycle Management rules: `SetStorageClass` to Coldline when age reaches 30 days and `Delete` when age reaches 365 days"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Object Lifecycle Management applies rules to objects automatically based on conditions such as age, storage class or number of newer versions. Actions include `SetStorageClass` (move to a colder class) and `Delete`. This keeps hot data in Standard, moves it to a cheaper class when access drops and deletes it on schedule, with no code.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) custom scripts are operational work and copying adds cost. (B) versioning keeps more data and increases cost. (C) Archive for data read often in the first 30 days incurs retrieval charges and early-deletion fees.\\n\\nRelated: Autoclass can move objects between classes automatically based on actual access, when patterns are unpredictable."},{"id":"14","question":"A subnet with the primary range `10.0.1.0/24` has run out of IP addresses for new VMs. The VPC has no overlapping ranges nearby. What is the least disruptive fix?","options":{"A":"Delete the subnet and recreate it as `10.0.0.0/22`","B":"Expand the subnet\'s primary range with `gcloud compute networks subnets expand-ip-range --prefix-length=23`","C":"Shrink the subnet to `/25` to free addresses","D":"Assign external IPs to the new VMs instead of internal ones"},"correct":"B","explanation":"You can expand a subnet\'s primary IPv4 range in place (for example from /24 to /23) as long as the new range does not overlap other ranges in the VPC or peered networks. Existing VMs keep their addresses and nothing is interrupted.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) deleting a subnet requires removing all its resources first, which is disruptive. (C) subnets cannot be shrunk, and a smaller range has fewer addresses. (D) every VM interface still needs an internal IP from the subnet.\\n\\nRemember: expansion is one-way; plan ranges with growth in mind."},{"id":"15","question":"The security team wants all Admin Activity audit logs from every project in the organization stored in one place for two years and queryable with SQL. What should you configure?","options":{"A":"Increase the retention of the `_Default` log bucket in each project to two years and query each one","B":"Ask each project owner to download logs monthly as CSV","C":"An aggregated log sink at the organization level that includes child projects and routes audit logs to a BigQuery dataset (or to a log bucket with Log Analytics)","D":"Create a Cloud Monitoring uptime check for each project"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Log sinks route log entries matching a filter to a destination: a Cloud Storage bucket, a BigQuery dataset, Pub/Sub, or a Cloud Logging log bucket. An aggregated sink at the organization or folder level with `--include-children` captures logs from every project centrally. BigQuery, or a log bucket upgraded to Log Analytics, lets you query them with SQL, and you control retention.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) per-project retention does not centralize anything. (B) manual exports are unreliable. (D) uptime checks probe availability; they do not collect logs.\\n\\nRelated: send to Pub/Sub to export logs to external systems such as a SIEM."},{"id":"16","question":"An application writes a log line containing `payment_failed` each time a payment fails. The on-call team wants a page when there are more than 20 such failures in 5 minutes. The application exports no metrics. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Create a log-based counter metric that matches `payment_failed`, then a Cloud Monitoring alerting policy on that metric with a threshold of 20 over 5 minutes","B":"Ask the on-call engineer to search the logs every 5 minutes","C":"Create an uptime check against the payment API\'s home page","D":"Export all logs to Cloud Storage and review them daily"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Log-based metrics turn matching log entries into Cloud Monitoring time series (a counter here) without changing application code. An alerting policy on that metric with a threshold and a 5-minute window notifies the on-call channel.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) manual searching is unreliable. (C) an uptime check tests availability of an endpoint, not payment failures. (D) daily review is far too slow for paging.\\n\\nRelated: applications can also write custom metrics directly (OpenTelemetry or the Monitoring API), and the Ops Agent collects VM system and application metrics."},{"id":"17","question":"Support engineers need to start, stop and reset Compute Engine VMs, but must not create, delete or change their configuration. No predefined role matches exactly. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Grant Compute Admin, because it includes start and stop","B":"Grant the basic Editor role on the project","C":"Create a custom role with only `compute.instances.start`, `compute.instances.stop`, `compute.instances.reset` and the needed `get`/`list` permissions, and grant it to a group","D":"Share the project owner\'s credentials with the support team"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Custom roles let you bundle exactly the permissions a job needs when predefined roles are too broad. Granting the role to a Google group rather than individuals keeps management simple. Custom roles can be defined at the organization or project level.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) Compute Admin includes creating and deleting instances. (B) basic roles such as Editor are very broad and discouraged in production. (D) sharing credentials destroys accountability.\\n\\nRole types: basic (Owner, Editor, Viewer; avoid), predefined (managed by Google, per service), custom (yours to maintain). Policies are inherited down the hierarchy, so grant at the lowest level that works."},{"id":"18","question":"A developer needs to test locally what a deployment pipeline\'s service account can access, without creating or downloading a service account key. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Create a JSON key for the service account and email it to the developer","B":"Grant the developer the same roles as the service account directly","C":"Add the developer\'s password to Secret Manager","D":"Grant the developer the Service Account Token Creator role on that service account, so they can run gcloud with `--impersonate-service-account` and receive short-lived credentials"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Service account impersonation lets an authorized user obtain short-lived OAuth tokens for a service account. With `roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator` on that service account, the developer runs commands as it (`--impersonate-service-account` or `gcloud config set auth/impersonate_service_account`). No key is created, tokens expire quickly and audit logs record both identities.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) downloaded keys are long-lived secrets that leak easily. (B) the developer would test their own access, not the service account\'s, and accumulate privileges. (C) Secret Manager stores secrets; it does not give service account access.\\n\\nRelated: Service Account User (`roles/iam.serviceAccountUser`) lets you attach a service account to resources such as VMs."},{"id":"19","question":"An application running in GKE Pods must read objects from a Cloud Storage bucket. You want each workload to have its own identity, with no service account keys stored as Kubernetes Secrets. What is the recommended approach?","options":{"A":"Enable Workload Identity Federation for GKE and grant the IAM role to the workload\'s Kubernetes ServiceAccount (directly as a principal or by linking it to an IAM service account)","B":"Mount a service account JSON key into each Pod as a Kubernetes Secret","C":"Give the node pool\'s service account Storage Admin so every Pod inherits it","D":"Make the bucket publicly readable"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Workload Identity Federation for GKE lets Kubernetes ServiceAccounts act as IAM principals. You grant roles such as `roles/storage.objectViewer` to the specific workload, and Pods get short-lived credentials from the GKE metadata server automatically. No keys exist to leak, and each workload has least privilege.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) mounted keys are long-lived secrets that are hard to rotate. (C) a node-level identity is shared by every Pod on the node, and Storage Admin is too broad. (D) public buckets expose the data.\\n\\nExam cue: \'GKE Pods access Google Cloud APIs securely\' → Workload Identity Federation for GKE."},{"id":"20","question":"A platform team already manages all its applications with Kubernetes manifests and GitOps. They want to create and manage Google Cloud resources such as Cloud SQL instances, Pub/Sub topics and IAM bindings declaratively, with the same Kubernetes tooling. Which tool fits?","options":{"A":"Helm charts that run gcloud commands in init containers","B":"Config Connector, which represents Google Cloud resources as Kubernetes custom resources","C":"Manual changes in the Google Cloud console, documented in a wiki","D":"Cloud Shell scripts saved in a shared folder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Config Connector is a Kubernetes add-on that adds custom resources for Google Cloud services. You apply YAML (for example `SQLInstance` or `PubSubTopic`) and its controllers create and continuously reconcile the real resources, so it fits naturally into GitOps.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) Helm packages Kubernetes manifests; running gcloud from containers is imperative and does not reconcile drift. (C) and (D) manual changes and ad hoc scripts are not declarative or repeatable.\\n\\nIaC options in the guide: Terraform (and Fabric FAST, Google\'s opinionated Terraform landing-zone blueprints), Config Connector and Helm. AI-assisted options include Gemini Cloud Assist, Gemini CLI and Application Design Center."}]},{"id":"gcp-ace-2026-es","title":"GCP Associate Cloud Engineer — gu\xeda de examen 2026 (ES)","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"Tu empresa debe mantener todos los recursos nuevos de su unidad de negocio europea en regiones de la UE. Los proyectos de la unidad est\xe1n bajo una carpeta llamada `eu`. Los desarrolladores tienen Editor en sus proyectos. \xbfCu\xe1l es el control m\xe1s eficaz?","options":{"A":"Quitar el rol Editor y dar a los desarrolladores solo Viewer","B":"Establecer la pol\xedtica de organizaci\xf3n `gcp.resourceLocations` en la carpeta `eu` para permitir solo ubicaciones de la UE","C":"Crear una alerta de Cloud Monitoring que salte cuando se cree un recurso fuera de la UE","D":"Pedir a los desarrolladores que siempre pasen `--region=europe-west1` en sus comandos gcloud"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las pol\xedticas de organizaci\xf3n son preventivas y se heredan hacia abajo en la jerarqu\xeda de recursos. Fijar la restricci\xf3n de ubicaciones (por ejemplo `in:eu-locations`) en la carpeta bloquea la creaci\xf3n de recursos con ubicaci\xf3n fuera de la UE en todos los proyectos que cuelgan de ella, tenga el rol IAM que tenga el desarrollador.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) IAM controla qui\xe9n act\xfaa, no d\xf3nde; con Viewer adem\xe1s no podr\xedan trabajar. (C) una alerta detecta la infracci\xf3n cuando ya ha ocurrido. (D) una convenci\xf3n no es un control.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabnunca\xbb, \xaben todos los proyectos de una carpeta\xbb → pol\xedtica de organizaci\xf3n en la carpeta o en la organizaci\xf3n."},{"id":"2","question":"Los empleados de tu empresa inician sesi\xf3n con Microsoft Entra ID. Seguridad no quiere sincronizar las cuentas de usuario en Cloud Identity, pero los empleados necesitan acceso a la consola y a gcloud con su inicio de sesi\xf3n corporativo. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas configurar?","options":{"A":"Crear una clave de cuenta de servicio para cada empleado","B":"Crear una cuenta de Gmail personal para cada empleado y darle roles IAM","C":"Configurar Google Cloud Directory Sync para copiar todos los usuarios a Cloud Identity","D":"Workforce Identity Federation con un pool de workforce y un proveedor para Entra ID, y dar roles IAM a los principales del pool"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Workforce Identity Federation permite que usuarios de un proveedor de identidad externo (Entra ID, Okta y otros OIDC o SAML) accedan a Google Cloud directamente con sus credenciales. No se sincronizan cuentas; das roles IAM a identificadores `principal://` o `principalSet://` del pool de workforce.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) las claves de cuenta de servicio son para cargas de trabajo, son secretos de larga duraci\xf3n y eliminan la responsabilidad individual. (B) las cuentas personales de Gmail quedan fuera del control corporativo. (C) Directory Sync es precisamente la sincronizaci\xf3n que seguridad quiere evitar.\\n\\nRelacionado: Workload Identity Federation es lo equivalente para cargas de trabajo (CI/CD, otras nubes) en lugar de personas."},{"id":"3","question":"Finanzas quiere analizar los costes de Google Cloud por proyecto, servicio, SKU y etiquetas personalizadas con SQL, y crear paneles con m\xe1s de un a\xf1o de historia. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas configurar?","options":{"A":"La exportaci\xf3n de Cloud Billing a BigQuery (datos de coste de uso est\xe1ndar o detallados)","B":"Un presupuesto con alerta por correo al 90% del importe mensual","C":"Descargar la factura mensual en PDF y copiar los totales en una hoja de c\xe1lculo","D":"Un panel de Cloud Monitoring con el uso de CPU de todas las VM"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La exportaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n a BigQuery escribe de forma continua registros detallados de coste y uso (proyecto, servicio, SKU, etiquetas, cr\xe9ditos) en un dataset tuyo. Finanzas puede consultarlo con SQL y conectar Looker Studio u otras herramientas de BI. Solo se exportan datos desde el momento en que la activas, as\xed que conviene activarla pronto.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) los presupuestos avisan al cruzar umbrales pero no dan detalle analizable. (C) las facturas est\xe1n agregadas y es un proceso manual. (D) el uso de CPU no es coste.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabanalizar costes con SQL\xbb, \xabpor etiqueta\xbb → exportaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n a BigQuery; \xabavisar cuando el gasto llegue a X\xbb → presupuestos y alertas."},{"id":"4","question":"Un despliegue falla con un error que dice que se ha superado la cuota regional CPUS en `us-central1`. La carga necesita leg\xedtimamente m\xe1s capacidad en esa regi\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas hacer?","options":{"A":"Borrar el proyecto y crear uno nuevo, lo que reinicia las cuotas","B":"Cambiar todas las VM a un tipo de m\xe1quina mayor para necesitar menos VM","C":"Revisar la cuota en la p\xe1gina Quotas (Cloud Quotas) y solicitar un aumento para esa regi\xf3n","D":"Abrir una regla de firewall para el rango de IP del despliegue"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las cuotas te protegen a ti y a la plataforma. Cuando una carga leg\xedtima necesita m\xe1s, consultas el uso y los l\xedmites en la p\xe1gina Quotas de la consola (o con la API de Cloud Quotas o gcloud) y pides un aumento para esa cuota y regi\xf3n concretas. Tambi\xe9n puedes configurar alertas de cuota para enterarte antes de llegar al l\xedmite.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) un proyecto nuevo tiene cuotas por defecto, a menudo m\xe1s bajas, y pierdes todo. (B) las m\xe1quinas m\xe1s grandes consumen la misma cuota de vCPU. (D) las reglas de firewall no tienen relaci\xf3n con las cuotas.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabquota exceeded\xbb → revisar uso y pedir aumento; planifica las cuotas antes de lanzamientos grandes."},{"id":"5","question":"Un trabajo batch nocturno renderiza miles de im\xe1genes independientes. Guarda puntos de control y puede reiniciarse en cualquier momento. Quieres el menor coste posible en Compute Engine. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas usar?","options":{"A":"Nodos sole-tenant con descuentos por compromiso de uso","B":"VM est\xe1ndar bajo demanda en una \xfanica instancia grande","C":"Spot VMs en un grupo de instancias gestionado","D":"Discos persistentes regionales conectados a cada VM"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las Spot VMs tienen grandes descuentos (a menudo entre el 60 y el 91%) a cambio de poder ser interrumpidas en cualquier momento. Un trabajo batch tolerante a fallos con puntos de control es la carga ideal, y un grupo de instancias gestionado recrea las VM interrumpidas cuando vuelve la capacidad.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) los nodos sole-tenant son para aislamiento o licencias y cuestan m\xe1s. (B) bajo demanda es lo m\xe1s caro y una sola VM no paraleliza el trabajo. (D) los discos regionales a\xf1aden coste por una alta disponibilidad que el trabajo no necesita.\\n\\nRelacionado: los tipos de m\xe1quina personalizados permiten elegir la proporci\xf3n exacta de vCPU y memoria y no pagar por lo que no usas."},{"id":"6","question":"Un equipo peque\xf1o quiere ejecutar microservicios en contenedores sobre Kubernetes, pero no quiere gestionar nodos, node pools, actualizaciones ni planificaci\xf3n de capacidad, y prefiere pagar por los recursos que piden sus Pods. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n encaja?","options":{"A":"Un cl\xfaster GKE Autopilot","B":"Un cl\xfaster GKE Standard zonal con node pools dimensionados a mano","C":"VM de Compute Engine con Docker instalado","D":"Un cl\xfaster de Kubernetes autogestionado con kubeadm sobre Compute Engine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"GKE Autopilot es el modo en que Google gestiona los nodos, el escalado, el endurecimiento de seguridad y las actualizaciones. T\xfa despliegas cargas y pagas sobre todo por la CPU, memoria y almacenamiento que piden tus Pods. Mantiene la API completa de Kubernetes.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) el modo Standard te obliga a dimensionar y gestionar node pools. (C) VM con Docker renuncian a la orquestaci\xf3n de Kubernetes. (D) un cl\xfaster autogestionado es lo que m\xe1s trabajo operativo da.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabKubernetes sin gestionar nodos\xbb, \xabpagar por Pod\xbb → Autopilot; \xabconfiguraci\xf3n de nodo a medida o control de hardware\xbb → Standard."},{"id":"7","question":"Cada vez que se sube un PDF a un bucket de Cloud Storage necesitas extraer su texto y guardar el resultado, sin servidores que gestionar y sin sondeos. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas desplegar?","options":{"A":"Una VM de Compute Engine que liste el bucket cada minuto","B":"Un job de Cloud Scheduler que ejecute un script cada hora","C":"Un Deployment de GKE que mantenga una conexi\xf3n persistente con el bucket","D":"Una Cloud Run function activada mediante Eventarc por el evento de objeto finalizado de Cloud Storage"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eventarc entrega eventos de Cloud Storage (como `google.cloud.storage.object.v1.finalized`) a servicios de Cloud Run o Cloud Run functions. La funci\xf3n solo se ejecuta cuando llega un archivo, escala autom\xe1ticamente, incluso a cero, y no necesita servidores.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) una VM que sondea cuesta dinero aunque est\xe9 ociosa y reacciona tarde. (B) una programaci\xf3n horaria a\xf1ade latencia y procesa por lotes. (C) un Deployment de GKE a\xf1ade gesti\xf3n de cl\xfaster a una tarea dirigida por eventos.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabcuando se sube un archivo\xbb, \xabmensaje de Pub/Sub\xbb, \xabevento de audit log\xbb → Eventarc o un trigger directo en Cloud Run functions."},{"id":"8","question":"Debes guardar informes trimestrales de cumplimiento. Cada uno se lee aproximadamente una vez cada tres meses para una auditor\xeda y debe estar disponible en milisegundos cuando se pida. Quieres el menor coste de almacenamiento para este patr\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 clase de Cloud Storage eliges?","options":{"A":"Standard","B":"Coldline","C":"Archive","D":"Nearline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Coldline est\xe1 pensada para datos que se leen menos de una vez por trimestre, con una duraci\xf3n m\xednima de almacenamiento de 90 d\xedas. Su precio de almacenamiento es menor que el de Nearline, y todas las clases de Cloud Storage devuelven los datos con latencia de milisegundos.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) Standard es para datos de acceso frecuente y es la m\xe1s cara de almacenar. (C) Archive es m\xe1s barata de almacenar pero est\xe1 pensada para datos que se leen menos de una vez al a\xf1o, con m\xednimo de 365 d\xedas y recuperaci\xf3n m\xe1s cara. (D) Nearline (m\xednimo 30 d\xedas) encaja con accesos aproximadamente mensuales; para accesos trimestrales Coldline sale m\xe1s barata.\\n\\nRegla: Standard (caliente) → Nearline (~mensual) → Coldline (~trimestral) → Archive (~anual)."},{"id":"9","question":"Seguridad quiere reglas de firewall que apunten a VM por un atributo que solo un rol IAM autorizado pueda asignar, para que un Editor del proyecto no pueda saltarse una regla simplemente a\xf1adiendo una etiqueta a una VM. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas usar en las reglas de la pol\xedtica de Cloud NGFW?","options":{"A":"Secure Tags (tags de Resource Manager gobernados por IAM) como origen y destino","B":"Network tags, que cualquiera que pueda editar la VM puede asignar","C":"Los nombres de las instancias en el campo de destino de la regla","D":"Las IP externas de las VM"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Los Secure Tags son tags de Resource Manager cuya asignaci\xf3n se controla con IAM (por ejemplo, el rol Tag User). Usados en pol\xedticas de firewall de red de Cloud NGFW, permiten a seguridad decidir qui\xe9n puede vincular una VM a una regla, as\xed que un Editor del proyecto no puede cambiar qu\xe9 reglas se aplican a su VM.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) las network tags son cadenas libres que cualquiera con permiso para editar la instancia puede cambiar. (C) las reglas no apuntan por nombre de instancia. (D) las IP cambian y no expresan identidad.\\n\\nRelacionado: las reglas tambi\xe9n pueden apuntar por cuenta de servicio; los atributos de una regla son acci\xf3n, direcci\xf3n (ingress o egress), origen, destino, objetivo, protocolos, puertos y prioridad."},{"id":"10","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n web HTTPS p\xfablica corre en grupos de instancias gestionados en `us-east1` y `europe-west1`. Los usuarios de todo el mundo deben llegar al backend sano m\xe1s cercano mediante una \xfanica IP, con TLS terminado en el edge. \xbfQu\xe9 balanceador de carga eliges?","options":{"A":"Un Network Load Balancer passthrough externo regional","B":"Un Application Load Balancer interno","C":"Un Application Load Balancer externo global","D":"Un Network Load Balancer passthrough interno regional"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El Application Load Balancer externo global (HTTP/HTTPS, capa 7) usa una \xfanica IP anycast, termina TLS en el edge de Google y env\xeda a cada usuario al backend m\xe1s cercano con capacidad y health checks correctos, entre regiones. Adem\xe1s admite Cloud CDN y Cloud Armor.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) un Network Load Balancer passthrough es regional y de capa 4, sin terminaci\xf3n TLS en el edge ni enrutamiento entre regiones. (B) y (D) los balanceadores internos son para tr\xe1fico dentro de la VPC, no para usuarios p\xfablicos.\\n\\nPista de examen: usuarios HTTP(S) globales → Application LB externo global; TCP/UDP conservando la IP del cliente → Network LB passthrough; tr\xe1fico privado → balanceadores internos. El balanceo global requiere el Network Service Tier Premium."},{"id":"11","question":"Los Pods de un cl\xfaster GKE Standard fallan con `ImagePullBackOff` al descargar una imagen de Artifact Registry de otro proyecto. Los nodos usan una cuenta de servicio personalizada. \xbfCu\xe1l es la soluci\xf3n m\xe1s probable?","options":{"A":"Hacer p\xfablico en internet el repositorio de Artifact Registry","B":"Dar a la cuenta de servicio de los nodos el rol Artifact Registry Reader (`roles/artifactregistry.reader`) en el repositorio o su proyecto","C":"Dar a la cuenta de servicio de los nodos el rol Owner en ambos proyectos","D":"Volver a crear el cl\xfaster como zonal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los nodos de GKE descargan im\xe1genes con la cuenta de servicio del node pool. Con una cuenta personalizada, o con un repositorio en otro proyecto, esa cuenta necesita `roles/artifactregistry.reader` en el repositorio (o el proyecto). Concederlo resuelve la descarga con m\xednimo privilegio.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) hacer las im\xe1genes p\xfablicas las expone a todo el mundo. (C) Owner es un privilegio desmesurado. (D) el tipo de ubicaci\xf3n del cl\xfaster no tiene que ver con la autorizaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes.\\n\\nDiagn\xf3stico: `kubectl describe pod` muestra el error de descarga; revisa la ruta de la imagen (`REGION-docker.pkg.dev/PROYECTO/REPO/IMAGEN:TAG`) y el IAM de la cuenta de servicio de los nodos."},{"id":"12","question":"Necesitas publicar una nueva versi\xf3n de un servicio de Cloud Run con el m\xednimo riesgo: desplegarla sin enviarle usuarios, probarla, luego mover un 10% del tr\xe1fico y aumentarlo poco a poco. \xbfC\xf3mo lo haces?","options":{"A":"Borrar el servicio y volver a desplegarlo con la nueva imagen","B":"Desplegar la nueva imagen en un segundo servicio de Cloud Run aparte y cambiar el DNS a mano","C":"Escalar a cero instancias la revisi\xf3n actual mientras arranca la nueva","D":"Desplegar una nueva revisi\xf3n con `--no-traffic` (opcionalmente con un tag para tener URL de prueba) y usar `gcloud run services update-traffic` para pasar un 10% e ir aumentando"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cada despliegue de Cloud Run crea una revisi\xf3n inmutable. Con `--no-traffic` la nueva revisi\xf3n no recibe usuarios; un tag de revisi\xf3n le da su propia URL para probarla. Despu\xe9s `update-traffic --to-revisions=NUEVA=10` hace un reparto canary, y puedes subir el porcentaje o volver al instante a la revisi\xf3n anterior.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) borrar provoca ca\xedda del servicio y pierdes la vuelta atr\xe1s. (B) un servicio aparte con cambios de DNS es m\xe1s lento y no reparte por porcentaje. (C) Cloud Run no te deja escalar a cero a mano una revisi\xf3n que recibe tr\xe1fico, y seguir\xeda siendo un cambio brusco.\\n\\nPista de examen: despliegue gradual o canary en Cloud Run → revisiones + reparto de tr\xe1fico."},{"id":"13","question":"Los logs de una aplicaci\xf3n se escriben en un bucket de Cloud Storage. Se leen a menudo durante 30 d\xedas, rara vez despu\xe9s, y deben borrarse al cabo de un a\xf1o. \xbfCu\xe1l es la forma m\xe1s sencilla de minimizar el coste autom\xe1ticamente?","options":{"A":"Ejecutar un script semanal que copie los objetos antiguos a otro bucket y borre los originales","B":"Activar Object Versioning para conservar los logs viejos como versiones no actuales","C":"Usar la clase Archive para todos los logs desde el principio","D":"Configurar reglas de Object Lifecycle Management: `SetStorageClass` a Coldline a los 30 d\xedas de antig\xfcedad y `Delete` a los 365 d\xedas"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Object Lifecycle Management aplica reglas autom\xe1ticamente a los objetos seg\xfan condiciones como la antig\xfcedad, la clase de almacenamiento o el n\xfamero de versiones m\xe1s nuevas. Las acciones incluyen `SetStorageClass` (pasar a una clase m\xe1s fr\xeda) y `Delete`. As\xed los datos calientes siguen en Standard, pasan a una clase m\xe1s barata cuando bajan los accesos y se borran seg\xfan lo previsto, sin c\xf3digo.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) los scripts propios son trabajo operativo y copiar a\xf1ade coste. (B) el versionado guarda m\xe1s datos y sube el coste. (C) Archive para datos que se leen a menudo en los primeros 30 d\xedas genera cargos de recuperaci\xf3n y de borrado anticipado.\\n\\nRelacionado: Autoclass mueve objetos entre clases autom\xe1ticamente seg\xfan el acceso real, cuando los patrones son impredecibles."},{"id":"14","question":"Una subred con rango primario `10.0.1.0/24` se ha quedado sin IP para nuevas VM. La VPC no tiene rangos solapados cerca. \xbfCu\xe1l es la soluci\xf3n menos disruptiva?","options":{"A":"Borrar la subred y recrearla como `10.0.0.0/22`","B":"Ampliar el rango primario de la subred con `gcloud compute networks subnets expand-ip-range --prefix-length=23`","C":"Reducir la subred a `/25` para liberar direcciones","D":"Asignar IP externas a las nuevas VM en lugar de internas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Puedes ampliar el rango IPv4 primario de una subred sin recrearla (por ejemplo de /24 a /23) siempre que el nuevo rango no se solape con otros rangos de la VPC o de redes con peering. Las VM existentes conservan sus direcciones y no se interrumpe nada.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) para borrar una subred hay que eliminar antes todos sus recursos, lo que es disruptivo. (C) las subredes no se pueden reducir, y un rango menor tiene menos direcciones. (D) cada interfaz de VM sigue necesitando una IP interna de la subred.\\n\\nRecuerda: la ampliaci\xf3n es en un solo sentido; planifica los rangos pensando en crecer."},{"id":"15","question":"El equipo de seguridad quiere guardar en un solo sitio, durante dos a\xf1os y consultables con SQL, todos los audit logs de Admin Activity de todos los proyectos de la organizaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas configurar?","options":{"A":"Aumentar a dos a\xf1os la retenci\xf3n del log bucket `_Default` de cada proyecto y consultarlos uno a uno","B":"Pedir a cada responsable de proyecto que descargue los logs en CSV cada mes","C":"Un sink de logs agregado a nivel de organizaci\xf3n que incluya los proyectos hijos y env\xede los audit logs a un dataset de BigQuery (o a un log bucket con Log Analytics)","D":"Crear un uptime check de Cloud Monitoring en cada proyecto"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los sinks de logs env\xedan las entradas que cumplen un filtro a un destino: un bucket de Cloud Storage, un dataset de BigQuery, Pub/Sub o un log bucket de Cloud Logging. Un sink agregado a nivel de organizaci\xf3n o carpeta con `--include-children` recoge los logs de todos los proyectos de forma centralizada. BigQuery, o un log bucket con Log Analytics, permite consultarlos con SQL, y t\xfa controlas la retenci\xf3n.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) la retenci\xf3n por proyecto no centraliza nada. (B) las exportaciones manuales no son fiables. (D) los uptime checks comprueban disponibilidad; no recogen logs.\\n\\nRelacionado: env\xeda a Pub/Sub para exportar logs a sistemas externos como un SIEM."},{"id":"16","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n escribe una l\xednea de log con `payment_failed` cada vez que falla un pago. La guardia quiere un aviso cuando haya m\xe1s de 20 fallos en 5 minutos. La aplicaci\xf3n no exporta m\xe9tricas. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas hacer?","options":{"A":"Crear una m\xe9trica basada en logs de tipo contador que coincida con `payment_failed` y una pol\xedtica de alertas de Cloud Monitoring sobre ella con umbral de 20 en 5 minutos","B":"Pedir a la persona de guardia que busque en los logs cada 5 minutos","C":"Crear un uptime check contra la p\xe1gina principal de la API de pagos","D":"Exportar todos los logs a Cloud Storage y revisarlos a diario"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Las m\xe9tricas basadas en logs convierten las entradas que coinciden en series temporales de Cloud Monitoring (aqu\xed un contador) sin cambiar el c\xf3digo. Una pol\xedtica de alertas sobre esa m\xe9trica con umbral y ventana de 5 minutos avisa al canal de guardia.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) buscar a mano no es fiable. (C) un uptime check prueba la disponibilidad de un endpoint, no los fallos de pago. (D) revisar a diario es demasiado lento para avisos.\\n\\nRelacionado: las aplicaciones tambi\xe9n pueden escribir m\xe9tricas personalizadas directamente (OpenTelemetry o la API de Monitoring), y el Ops Agent recoge m\xe9tricas de sistema y de aplicaci\xf3n en las VM."},{"id":"17","question":"Los ingenieros de soporte necesitan iniciar, detener y reiniciar VM de Compute Engine, pero no deben crearlas, borrarlas ni cambiar su configuraci\xf3n. Ning\xfan rol predefinido encaja exactamente. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas hacer?","options":{"A":"Dar Compute Admin, porque incluye iniciar y detener","B":"Dar el rol b\xe1sico Editor en el proyecto","C":"Crear un rol personalizado con solo `compute.instances.start`, `compute.instances.stop`, `compute.instances.reset` y los permisos `get`/`list` necesarios, y asignarlo a un grupo","D":"Compartir las credenciales del propietario del proyecto con el equipo de soporte"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los roles personalizados agrupan exactamente los permisos que necesita un puesto cuando los predefinidos son demasiado amplios. Asignar el rol a un grupo de Google en vez de a personas concretas simplifica la gesti\xf3n. Se pueden definir a nivel de organizaci\xf3n o de proyecto.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) Compute Admin incluye crear y borrar instancias. (B) los roles b\xe1sicos como Editor son muy amplios y se desaconsejan en producci\xf3n. (D) compartir credenciales elimina la trazabilidad.\\n\\nTipos de rol: b\xe1sicos (Owner, Editor, Viewer; evitarlos), predefinidos (gestionados por Google, por servicio) y personalizados (los mantienes t\xfa). Las pol\xedticas se heredan por la jerarqu\xeda, as\xed que concede al nivel m\xe1s bajo que funcione."},{"id":"18","question":"Un desarrollador necesita probar en local a qu\xe9 puede acceder la cuenta de servicio de un pipeline de despliegue, sin crear ni descargar una clave de cuenta de servicio. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas hacer?","options":{"A":"Crear una clave JSON de la cuenta de servicio y envi\xe1rsela por correo","B":"Dar al desarrollador directamente los mismos roles que tiene la cuenta de servicio","C":"A\xf1adir la contrase\xf1a del desarrollador a Secret Manager","D":"Dar al desarrollador el rol Service Account Token Creator sobre esa cuenta de servicio, para que ejecute gcloud con `--impersonate-service-account` y reciba credenciales de corta duraci\xf3n"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La suplantaci\xf3n (impersonation) de cuentas de servicio permite a un usuario autorizado obtener tokens OAuth de corta duraci\xf3n de una cuenta de servicio. Con `roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator` sobre ella, el desarrollador ejecuta comandos como esa cuenta (`--impersonate-service-account` o `gcloud config set auth/impersonate_service_account`). No se crea ninguna clave, los tokens caducan pronto y los audit logs registran ambas identidades.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) las claves descargadas son secretos de larga duraci\xf3n que se filtran con facilidad. (B) el desarrollador probar\xeda su propio acceso, no el de la cuenta, y acumular\xeda privilegios. (C) Secret Manager guarda secretos; no da acceso como cuenta de servicio.\\n\\nRelacionado: Service Account User (`roles/iam.serviceAccountUser`) permite asociar una cuenta de servicio a recursos como VM."},{"id":"19","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n que corre en Pods de GKE debe leer objetos de un bucket de Cloud Storage. Quieres que cada carga tenga su propia identidad, sin claves de cuenta de servicio guardadas como Secrets de Kubernetes. \xbfCu\xe1l es el enfoque recomendado?","options":{"A":"Activar Workload Identity Federation for GKE y dar el rol IAM al ServiceAccount de Kubernetes de la carga (directamente como principal o vincul\xe1ndolo a una cuenta de servicio IAM)","B":"Montar en cada Pod una clave JSON de cuenta de servicio como Secret de Kubernetes","C":"Dar Storage Admin a la cuenta de servicio del node pool para que todos los Pods lo hereden","D":"Hacer el bucket de lectura p\xfablica"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Workload Identity Federation for GKE permite que los ServiceAccounts de Kubernetes act\xfaen como principales de IAM. Das roles como `roles/storage.objectViewer` a la carga concreta, y los Pods obtienen credenciales de corta duraci\xf3n del servidor de metadatos de GKE autom\xe1ticamente. No hay claves que filtrar y cada carga tiene el m\xednimo privilegio.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) las claves montadas son secretos de larga duraci\xf3n dif\xedciles de rotar. (C) una identidad a nivel de nodo la comparten todos los Pods del nodo, y Storage Admin es demasiado amplio. (D) un bucket p\xfablico expone los datos.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabPods de GKE acceden a APIs de Google Cloud de forma segura\xbb → Workload Identity Federation for GKE."},{"id":"20","question":"Un equipo de plataforma ya gestiona todas sus aplicaciones con manifiestos de Kubernetes y GitOps. Quiere crear y gestionar de forma declarativa recursos de Google Cloud como instancias de Cloud SQL, temas de Pub/Sub y bindings de IAM, con las mismas herramientas de Kubernetes. \xbfQu\xe9 herramienta encaja?","options":{"A":"Charts de Helm que ejecuten comandos gcloud en init containers","B":"Config Connector, que representa los recursos de Google Cloud como recursos personalizados de Kubernetes","C":"Cambios manuales en la consola de Google Cloud documentados en una wiki","D":"Scripts de Cloud Shell guardados en una carpeta compartida"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Config Connector es un complemento de Kubernetes que a\xf1ade recursos personalizados para los servicios de Google Cloud. Aplicas YAML (por ejemplo `SQLInstance` o `PubSubTopic`) y sus controladores crean y reconcilian continuamente los recursos reales, as\xed que encaja de forma natural con GitOps.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) Helm empaqueta manifiestos de Kubernetes; ejecutar gcloud desde contenedores es imperativo y no corrige las desviaciones. (C) y (D) los cambios manuales y los scripts sueltos no son declarativos ni repetibles.\\n\\nOpciones de IaC en la gu\xeda: Terraform (y Fabric FAST, los blueprints de landing zone de Google en Terraform), Config Connector y Helm. Entre las opciones asistidas por IA: Gemini Cloud Assist, Gemini CLI y Application Design Center."}]},{"id":"gcp-pde-2026","title":"GCP Professional Data Engineer — 2026 exam guide","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"A retailer loads customer support transcripts into BigQuery for analytics. The transcripts contain names, emails and credit card numbers. Analysts need to count interactions per customer but must never see the raw personal data. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Load the raw data and ask analysts not to query the sensitive columns","B":"Inspect and de-identify the data with Sensitive Data Protection (formerly Cloud DLP) in the ingestion pipeline, using deterministic tokenization for customer identifiers and masking or redaction for card numbers","C":"Encrypt the whole table with a customer-managed key and give analysts access to the key","D":"Store the transcripts in a separate project with no IAM changes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sensitive Data Protection detects PII inside free text and structured fields and applies de-identification transforms. Deterministic tokenization (for example deterministic encryption or format-preserving encryption) replaces each identifier with the same token every time, so analysts can still count and join per customer without seeing who they are. Card numbers can be masked or redacted entirely.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a policy without technical control is not protection. (C) CMEK protects data at rest from Google-side access; anyone with access to the table and key still sees plaintext. (D) moving data without changing access controls changes nothing.\\n\\nExam cue: \'PII in free text\', \'analyze without exposing identities\' → Sensitive Data Protection de-identification; column-level controls → policy tags and dynamic data masking."},{"id":"2","question":"A European bank\'s regulator requires that customer data used for analytics is stored and processed only in the EU. The data team creates BigQuery datasets, Cloud Storage buckets and Dataflow jobs in several projects. How do you guarantee compliance with the least ongoing effort?","options":{"A":"Write the requirement in the team wiki and review new resources each quarter","B":"Use only the US multi-region and encrypt the data with CMEK","C":"Grant BigQuery Data Viewer only to EU-based employees","D":"Create datasets and buckets in EU locations, run Dataflow in EU regions, and enforce the `gcp.resourceLocations` organization policy (for example `in:eu-locations`) on the folder that holds the projects"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Data residency has two parts: choose EU locations for storage (BigQuery `EU` multi-region or EU regions, EU buckets) and processing (Dataflow regional endpoints in the EU), then enforce it. The resource locations organization policy blocks creating location-bound resources elsewhere, across all projects in the folder. For stricter regimes, Assured Workloads adds EU data boundary controls.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) periodic reviews detect violations after the fact. (B) encryption does not change where data is stored. (C) restricting who reads data does not control where it lives.\\n\\nExam cue: \'data sovereignty\', \'must stay in region X\' → location choice + organization policy, optionally Assured Workloads."},{"id":"3","question":"A payments company needs an operational database for account balances. Transfers between accounts must be ACID transactions, users are in North America, Europe and Asia, and the system must stay available even if a whole region fails, with no data loss. Which service fits?","options":{"A":"Spanner with a multi-region configuration","B":"Bigtable with multi-cluster replication","C":"BigQuery with streaming inserts","D":"Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL with a cross-region read replica"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Spanner provides relational ACID transactions with external consistency, horizontal scaling and synchronous replication across regions. A multi-region configuration survives a regional outage with zero data loss (RPO 0) and offers up to 99.999% availability.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) Bigtable replication is eventually consistent and has no multi-row transactions. (C) BigQuery is an analytics warehouse, not an OLTP system for balances. (D) Cloud SQL replicas are asynchronous; failing over to a cross-region replica can lose recent writes, and writes are limited to one primary region.\\n\\nExam cue: \'global\', \'strong consistency\', \'ACID\', \'no data loss on regional failure\' → Spanner multi-region."},{"id":"4","question":"You are migrating analytics off an on-premises Oracle database. The source must keep running during the migration, and BigQuery should receive inserts, updates and deletes with only seconds to minutes of delay, without building custom change-capture code. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Nightly CSV exports uploaded with `gcloud storage cp` and loaded into BigQuery","B":"Transfer Appliance shipped every week","C":"Datastream for change data capture (CDC) from Oracle, replicating directly into BigQuery","D":"BigQuery Data Transfer Service scheduled once a day from Oracle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Datastream is a serverless CDC and replication service. It reads the source database\'s change logs (Oracle, MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server) and streams changes into BigQuery (or Cloud Storage), applying inserts, updates and deletes with low latency and no custom code. The source keeps running.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) nightly CSVs mean up to a day of delay and no deletes. (B) Transfer Appliance is for moving large offline volumes once, not continuous replication. (D) daily transfers do not meet the latency requirement.\\n\\nMigration toolbox: Database Migration Service (database to Cloud SQL or AlloyDB), Datastream (CDC to BigQuery), BigQuery Data Transfer Service (SaaS and warehouse sources, scheduled), Storage Transfer Service and Transfer Appliance (files and bulk)."},{"id":"5","question":"A Dataflow streaming pipeline computes revenue per 1-minute event-time window. Some mobile events arrive up to 10 minutes late because devices go offline. Those late events are currently dropped. The business wants them included, with results updated when they arrive. What should you change?","options":{"A":"Switch to processing-time windows so every event lands in the current window","B":"Increase the number of Dataflow workers","C":"Set allowed lateness of at least 10 minutes on the window and use a trigger that fires again for late data (for example an after-watermark trigger with late firings), with accumulating panes if consumers want updated totals","D":"Reject events whose timestamp is older than 1 minute at the Pub/Sub subscription"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In Apache Beam, the watermark estimates when all data for a window has arrived. Data behind the watermark is late, and it is dropped unless the window has allowed lateness. Allowed lateness keeps window state open, and a trigger with late firings emits updated results. Accumulating mode re-emits the full updated total; discarding mode emits only the delta.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) processing-time windows assign events by arrival time, which gives wrong per-minute revenue. (B) more workers do not change lateness semantics. (D) rejecting data is the opposite of the requirement.\\n\\nExam cue: \'late-arriving data must be included\' → allowed lateness + triggers; know event time vs processing time and the watermark."},{"id":"6","question":"A company is moving hundreds of existing Apache Spark jobs from an on-premises Hadoop cluster. Leadership wants them running on Google Cloud quickly, with minimal code changes and no cluster to manage where possible. What should you recommend?","options":{"A":"Run the jobs on Dataproc, using Google Cloud Serverless for Apache Spark (Dataproc Serverless) where possible and job-scoped clusters otherwise, with data in Cloud Storage","B":"Rewrite every job in Apache Beam and run it on Dataflow before migrating","C":"Rewrite every job as BigQuery stored procedures before migrating","D":"Install Hadoop manually on Compute Engine VMs to replicate the old cluster"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dataproc runs open-source Spark and Hadoop with little or no code change, which makes it the lift-and-shift path. Serverless for Apache Spark removes cluster management entirely: you submit batches and pay for what runs. Keeping data in Cloud Storage (the Cloud Storage connector replaces HDFS) separates storage from compute.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) and (C) rewriting hundreds of jobs first is slow and risky; that can be a later modernization step. (D) self-managed Hadoop on VMs carries all the operational burden.\\n\\nExam cue: \'existing Spark/Hadoop\', \'minimal changes\' → Dataproc; \'new pipeline, unified batch and streaming, serverless\' → Dataflow."},{"id":"7","question":"Dozens of applications publish and consume events with the Apache Kafka client API on a self-managed on-premises Kafka cluster. The team wants to move to Google Cloud, stop operating brokers and ZooKeeper or KRaft, and avoid rewriting producers and consumers. What should they use?","options":{"A":"Pub/Sub, after rewriting every producer and consumer to the Pub/Sub client libraries","B":"Kafka installed manually on GKE StatefulSets","C":"Cloud Tasks queues","D":"Google Cloud Managed Service for Apache Kafka"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Managed Service for Apache Kafka runs open-source Apache Kafka clusters for you: Google handles broker provisioning, scaling, patching and replication across zones. Applications keep using the standard Kafka APIs and clients, so no rewrite is needed.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) Pub/Sub is a great serverless messaging service, but moving to it means changing every client. (B) Kafka on GKE is still self-managed. (C) Cloud Tasks is for dispatching individual asynchronous tasks to handlers, not a log-based event streaming platform.\\n\\nExam cue: \'keep Kafka APIs\', \'stop managing brokers\' → Managed Service for Apache Kafka; \'new, serverless, global messaging\' → Pub/Sub."},{"id":"8","question":"Product reviews in many languages are already loaded into a BigQuery table. Analysts want a new column with each review\'s sentiment and main complaint category, refreshed daily, written in SQL and without moving the data out of BigQuery. What is the simplest approach?","options":{"A":"Export the table to CSV, label it by hand in a spreadsheet and reload it","B":"Create a remote model in BigQuery over a Gemini model and call it from SQL (for example with `AI.GENERATE` or `ML.GENERATE_TEXT`), then schedule the query","C":"Train a logistic regression with `CREATE MODEL` on the unlabeled reviews","D":"Copy the reviews to Cloud SQL and write a stored procedure that classifies them"},"correct":"B","explanation":"BigQuery\'s generative AI functions call Gemini models through a remote model (backed by a Cloud resource connection), so you can enrich rows with sentiment, classification, extraction or translation using SQL. A scheduled query keeps the column fresh, and the data never leaves BigQuery. This is the \'AI data enrichment\' item in the guide.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) manual labeling does not scale. (C) logistic regression needs labeled training data, and there are no labels. (D) Cloud SQL has no language understanding and moving the data adds work.\\n\\nRelated: `ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING` creates embeddings for semantic search and RAG; Gemini in BigQuery can also help write and translate SQL."},{"id":"9","question":"Each night a workflow must: wait for a partner file in Cloud Storage, run a Dataproc Spark job, then run three BigQuery transformations in order, retry failed steps, alert on failure and allow backfilling past dates. What is the most appropriate orchestration?","options":{"A":"An Apache Airflow DAG on Cloud Composer (Managed Service for Apache Airflow) using Google Cloud operators and sensors, with retries, alerting and backfill","B":"Four independent cron jobs on a Compute Engine VM spaced one hour apart","C":"A single BigQuery scheduled query that tries to do everything","D":"A Cloud Run function that sleeps until the file arrives"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Composer, the managed Apache Airflow service, models the workflow as a DAG. A sensor waits for the file, Dataproc and BigQuery operators run the steps with explicit dependencies, and per-task retries, failure callbacks or alerts, and backfill by logical date come built in.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) time-spaced cron jobs ignore dependencies and fail silently. (C) a scheduled query cannot wait for files or run Spark. (D) functions have execution time limits and sleeping wastes resources.\\n\\nRelated: Workflows suits lightweight serverless orchestration of API calls; Composer suits complex data DAGs with many operators, backfills and scheduling. Dataform manages SQL transformations inside BigQuery."},{"id":"10","question":"A Bigtable table stores readings from 500,000 sensors, each sending a value every second. Dashboards read the latest hour for one sensor. With the row key `timestamp#sensor_id`, writes all hit the same node and performance is poor. Which row key design fixes this?","options":{"A":"Keep `timestamp#sensor_id` and add more nodes","B":"Use a random UUID as the row key","C":"Use `sensor_id#reversed_timestamp` (or `sensor_id#timestamp`) so writes spread across sensors and each sensor\'s recent rows are contiguous","D":"Use only `timestamp` as the row key and store the sensor ID in a column"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bigtable sorts rows by key and splits the key space into tablets served by nodes. A key that starts with a timestamp sends every new write to the end of the key space, creating a hotspot. Leading with the sensor ID distributes writes, and adding a (reversed) timestamp keeps one sensor\'s readings together, ordered so the latest come first, which makes \'latest hour for a sensor\' a fast range scan.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) more nodes do not help when one tablet receives all writes. (B) random keys spread writes but make range scans by sensor and time impossible. (D) a timestamp-only key is the worst hotspot.\\n\\nExam cue: Bigtable row keys should match the main read pattern and avoid monotonically increasing prefixes; consider field promotion and salting."},{"id":"11","question":"A 40 TB BigQuery table of orders grows daily. Almost every query filters on `order_date` for a few days and often on `customer_id`. Costs are high because queries scan the whole table. How should you redesign it?","options":{"A":"Split the data into one table per customer","B":"Partition the table by `order_date` and cluster it by `customer_id`, and optionally require a partition filter","C":"Partition the table by `customer_id` using integer-range partitioning only","D":"Create a view with `SELECT *` over the table"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Partitioning by date lets BigQuery prune whole partitions that do not match the date filter, so only those days are scanned and billed. Clustering by `customer_id` sorts data inside each partition, so filters on customer read fewer blocks. Setting `require_partition_filter` prevents accidental full scans.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) thousands of per-customer tables (sharding) are hard to manage and slower than one partitioned table. (C) integer-range partitioning on a high-cardinality ID is limited in partition count and does not help the date filter. (D) a view does not change how much data is scanned.\\n\\nRelated: denormalize with nested and repeated fields (STRUCT and ARRAY) to avoid expensive joins in a warehouse design."},{"id":"12","question":"A data lake in Cloud Storage holds Parquet and Apache Iceberg tables used by both Spark and BigQuery. Security wants row- and column-level access control applied the same way in every engine, without copying the data into BigQuery storage and without giving users direct access to the bucket. What should you use?","options":{"A":"Signed URLs for every object","B":"Bucket-level IAM roles for each analyst","C":"Load all data into native BigQuery tables every night","D":"BigLake tables over the Cloud Storage data, with access delegated through a connection and fine-grained security defined on the tables"},"correct":"D","explanation":"BigLake tables let BigQuery and open-source engines (Spark through the BigQuery connector or BigLake metastore) query open formats in Cloud Storage. Access is delegated through a service connection, so users never need bucket permissions, and row-level security, column-level security (policy tags) and data masking apply consistently. The data stays in the lake.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) signed URLs grant object-level access with no row or column control. (B) bucket IAM is all-or-nothing at object level. (C) copying data duplicates storage and creates a second source of truth.\\n\\nRelated: BigLake Iceberg tables in BigQuery support writes and an open format; Dataplex adds discovery, profiling and data quality across the lake."},{"id":"13","question":"A large company has data domains (sales, logistics, finance), each owning its own projects with BigQuery datasets and Cloud Storage buckets. Leadership wants each domain to keep ownership while a central team provides a shared catalog, consistent policies, data profiling and data quality checks. Which approach fits?","options":{"A":"Copy every domain\'s data into one central BigQuery project managed by the central team","B":"Let each domain choose its own tools with no shared catalog","C":"Store a list of all datasets in a shared spreadsheet","D":"A federated governance model with Dataplex: lakes and zones per domain, the Dataplex Universal Catalog for search and metadata, central policies, and data profiling and quality scans"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dataplex supports data mesh-style federated governance. Domains keep their data and projects, organized logically into lakes and zones, while the platform provides a unified catalog (Dataplex Universal Catalog, formerly Data Catalog), centrally managed policies and access, automatic discovery, data profiling, data quality scans and lineage.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) centralizing all data removes domain ownership and duplicates data. (B) no shared catalog or policies means no governance. (C) a spreadsheet goes stale immediately and enforces nothing.\\n\\nExam cue: \'data mesh\', \'federated governance\', \'discover and govern across projects\' → Dataplex."},{"id":"14","question":"A Looker Studio executive dashboard runs the same aggregation (daily revenue by region over a 5 TB table) hundreds of times a day. It is slow and each refresh scans the base table. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Export the table to Google Sheets and connect the dashboard to the sheet","B":"Create a BigQuery materialized view with the aggregation, and enable BI Engine to accelerate the dashboard\'s queries","C":"Increase the dashboard\'s refresh rate so the cache stays warm","D":"Duplicate the base table into several datasets so queries spread out"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A materialized view precomputes and incrementally maintains the aggregation, so queries read a small result instead of scanning 5 TB, and BigQuery can rewrite matching queries to use it automatically. BI Engine is an in-memory analysis service that accelerates BI queries with sub-second response times. Together they make the dashboard fast and cheap.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) Sheets cannot hold or process data at this scale and goes stale. (C) more refreshes mean more full scans and more cost. (D) duplicating data multiplies storage and does not reduce the scan per query.\\n\\nRelated: precalculated fields and summary tables (for example built with Dataform or scheduled queries) are another way to prepare data for visualization."},{"id":"15","question":"A company wants a RAG assistant over 2 million internal PDF manuals stored in Cloud Storage, with the retrieval index managed inside BigQuery. How should the data engineer prepare the data?","options":{"A":"Load each whole PDF as one row and send entire files to the model at query time","B":"Train a classification model on the PDF titles with `CREATE MODEL`","C":"Parse the PDFs (for example with Document AI or an object table plus a parsing function), split the text into overlapping chunks with metadata, generate embeddings with `ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING`, store them in a table with a vector index, and retrieve with `VECTOR_SEARCH`","D":"Convert the PDFs to images and store them in Bigtable"},"correct":"C","explanation":"RAG retrieves the most relevant passages and gives them to the model as context. Preparing unstructured data means: extract the text (Document AI layout parsing or BigQuery object tables with a parsing function), chunk it into pieces sized for retrieval (with some overlap and metadata such as source and page), create embeddings, and index them. In BigQuery, `ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING` with a remote embedding model, `CREATE VECTOR INDEX` and `VECTOR_SEARCH` cover this end to end.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) whole files exceed useful context, increase cost and dilute relevance. (B) a title classifier does not retrieve passages. (D) images in Bigtable cannot be searched semantically.\\n\\nAlternative: Agent Search on Agent Platform provides prebuilt RAG if you do not need to manage the pipeline yourself."},{"id":"16","question":"A weather data company wants to share curated, frequently updated BigQuery datasets with dozens of partner organizations. Partners must always see the latest data, the company must not maintain copies per partner and it wants to control and audit who subscribes. What should it use?","options":{"A":"BigQuery sharing (formerly Analytics Hub): publish the datasets as listings in a data exchange that partners subscribe to as linked datasets","B":"Export the tables to CSV daily and email them to each partner","C":"Copy the datasets into each partner\'s project every night","D":"Make the datasets public to `allUsers`"},"correct":"A","explanation":"BigQuery sharing (Analytics Hub) lets a publisher create exchanges and listings. Subscribers get a linked dataset in their own project that points to the shared data, so there are no copies, they always see the current data and pay for their own queries. The publisher controls who can subscribe and can see usage. Data clean rooms extend this for privacy-safe joint analysis.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) emailed CSVs are stale, insecure and do not scale. (C) nightly copies multiply storage and become stale between runs. (D) public access loses all control over who uses the data.\\n\\nExam cue: \'share data with other organizations without copying\' → BigQuery sharing / Analytics Hub."},{"id":"17","question":"A team keeps a large Dataproc cluster running 24/7, but its Spark jobs run only about four hours each night. Input and output data are in Cloud Storage and BigQuery. How can they cut costs the most without slowing the jobs?","options":{"A":"Keep the persistent cluster and switch it to a larger machine type","B":"Move the input data into the cluster\'s HDFS","C":"Use job-scoped (ephemeral) clusters created for each run and deleted afterwards, for example through workflow templates or Composer, or run the jobs on Serverless for Apache Spark, with secondary workers on Spot VMs if clusters are kept","D":"Turn off autoscaling so the cluster size is predictable"},"correct":"C","explanation":"With data in Cloud Storage and BigQuery, compute is stateless, so clusters can exist only while jobs run. Ephemeral clusters (created per job or workflow, then deleted, or with scheduled deletion after idle time) or serverless Spark batches eliminate 20 idle hours of cost per day. Spot VMs for secondary workers lower the cost further for fault-tolerant work.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a larger always-on cluster costs more. (B) HDFS ties data to the cluster, which prevents deleting it. (D) disabling autoscaling removes another cost lever.\\n\\nExam cue: \'persistent vs job-based clusters\' → job-based (ephemeral) unless you need an always-on interactive cluster."},{"id":"18","question":"On BigQuery Enterprise edition, nightly ETL jobs that feed the finance close sometimes miss their deadline because analysts run large ad hoc queries at the same time and consume the shared slots. How should you guarantee capacity for the ETL?","options":{"A":"Ask analysts not to query at night","B":"Switch all workloads to on-demand pricing","C":"Rewrite the ETL as smaller queries that run one at a time","D":"Create separate reservations (for example `etl` and `adhoc`), assign the ETL project to the `etl` reservation with a baseline of slots plus autoscaling, and assign the analysts\' project to `adhoc` with its own limits"},"correct":"D","explanation":"BigQuery editions use reservations: pools of slot capacity with a baseline and optional autoscaling. Assignments map projects, folders or the organization to a reservation for a job type (QUERY, PIPELINE and others). Separate reservations isolate workloads, so analysts cannot starve the business-critical ETL. Idle slots can still be shared between reservations when unused.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) a request is not a guarantee. (B) on-demand capacity is shared and does not guarantee slots for critical work. (C) serializing the ETL makes it slower and still competes for the same slots.\\n\\nRelated: interactive vs batch query priority. Batch queries queue until slots are free, which suits non-urgent work."},{"id":"19","question":"A BigQuery query that returns all 2 billion rows of a table sorted with `ORDER BY event_time` fails with \'Resources exceeded during query execution\'. The analyst only needs the most recent 1,000 events. What is the best fix?","options":{"A":"Add `LIMIT 1000` (and filter on the partition column if possible) so BigQuery does not have to sort the full result on a single worker","B":"Buy more slots and rerun the same query","C":"Request a quota increase for query bytes","D":"Export the table to Cloud Storage and sort the files manually"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A global `ORDER BY` over a huge result must be finalized in a single place, which exceeds memory and causes the error. Adding `LIMIT` lets BigQuery compute a top-N in a distributed way, and filtering on the partition column reduces the data read. The query execution graph and `INFORMATION_SCHEMA.JOBS` show which stage is expensive.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (B) more slots do not remove the single-worker final sort. (C) this is not a quota error. (D) moving the data out does not answer the question efficiently.\\n\\nOther common causes: skewed joins on hot keys, `COUNT(DISTINCT)` on huge sets (consider `APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT`), and `SELECT *` on wide tables. Use the BigQuery admin resource charts and Cloud Monitoring to watch slot usage and errors."},{"id":"20","question":"A Cloud SQL for PostgreSQL instance in `europe-west1` backs a data ingestion API. It already has high availability within the region. The business now requires recovery within an hour if the whole region becomes unavailable. What should you add?","options":{"A":"A second zone in the same region","B":"A cross-region read replica in another region (for example `europe-west4`), promoted or switched over in a regional outage, with the application able to point to the new primary","C":"Daily exports to a Cloud Storage bucket in the same region","D":"A larger machine type for the primary instance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Regional high availability (a standby in another zone) protects against zone failures only. For regional disasters, a cross-region read replica keeps an asynchronously replicated copy in another region. If the primary region fails, you promote the replica, or use Cloud SQL Enterprise Plus switchover and DR replica features, and repoint the application. That meets an RTO of about an hour with a small RPO.\\n\\nWhy the others fail: (A) the instance is already multi-zone. (C) backups in the same region are lost with the region and restoring takes longer. (D) a bigger machine does nothing for regional failure.\\n\\nRelated: for Memorystore for Redis, use cross-region replication; for BigQuery, cross-region dataset replication. Spanner multi-region has RPO 0 by design."}]},{"id":"gcp-pde-2026-es","title":"GCP Professional Data Engineer — gu\xeda de examen 2026 (ES)","questions":[{"id":"1","question":"Un retailer carga transcripciones de soporte en BigQuery para anal\xedtica. Contienen nombres, correos y n\xfameros de tarjeta. Los analistas necesitan contar interacciones por cliente pero nunca deben ver los datos personales en bruto. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas hacer?","options":{"A":"Cargar los datos en bruto y pedir a los analistas que no consulten las columnas sensibles","B":"Inspeccionar y desidentificar los datos con Sensitive Data Protection (antes Cloud DLP) en el pipeline de ingesta, con tokenizaci\xf3n determinista para los identificadores de cliente y enmascarado o eliminaci\xf3n para los n\xfameros de tarjeta","C":"Cifrar toda la tabla con una clave gestionada por el cliente y dar a los analistas acceso a la clave","D":"Guardar las transcripciones en otro proyecto sin cambiar el IAM"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sensitive Data Protection detecta PII dentro de texto libre y de campos estructurados y aplica transformaciones de desidentificaci\xf3n. La tokenizaci\xf3n determinista (por ejemplo cifrado determinista o con preservaci\xf3n de formato) sustituye cada identificador siempre por el mismo token, as\xed que los analistas pueden contar y unir por cliente sin saber qui\xe9n es. Los n\xfameros de tarjeta se pueden enmascarar o eliminar por completo.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) una norma sin control t\xe9cnico no es protecci\xf3n. (C) CMEK protege los datos en reposo frente al acceso del lado de Google; quien tenga acceso a la tabla y a la clave sigue viendo el texto en claro. (D) mover los datos sin cambiar los controles de acceso no cambia nada.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabPII en texto libre\xbb, \xabanalizar sin exponer identidades\xbb → desidentificaci\xf3n con Sensitive Data Protection; controles por columna → policy tags y enmascaramiento din\xe1mico de datos."},{"id":"2","question":"El regulador de un banco europeo exige que los datos de clientes usados en anal\xedtica se almacenen y procesen solo en la UE. El equipo crea datasets de BigQuery, buckets de Cloud Storage y jobs de Dataflow en varios proyectos. \xbfC\xf3mo garantizas el cumplimiento con el menor esfuerzo continuado?","options":{"A":"Escribir el requisito en la wiki del equipo y revisar los recursos nuevos cada trimestre","B":"Usar solo la multirregi\xf3n US y cifrar los datos con CMEK","C":"Dar BigQuery Data Viewer solo a empleados ubicados en la UE","D":"Crear datasets y buckets en ubicaciones de la UE, ejecutar Dataflow en regiones de la UE y aplicar la pol\xedtica de organizaci\xf3n `gcp.resourceLocations` (por ejemplo `in:eu-locations`) en la carpeta que contiene los proyectos"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La residencia de datos tiene dos partes: elegir ubicaciones de la UE para el almacenamiento (multirregi\xf3n `EU` de BigQuery o regiones de la UE, buckets en la UE) y para el procesamiento (endpoints regionales de Dataflow en la UE), y despu\xe9s imponerlo. La pol\xedtica de organizaci\xf3n de ubicaciones bloquea la creaci\xf3n de recursos con ubicaci\xf3n en otro sitio en todos los proyectos de la carpeta. Para reg\xedmenes m\xe1s estrictos, Assured Workloads a\xf1ade controles de l\xedmite de datos de la UE.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) las revisiones peri\xf3dicas detectan la infracci\xf3n cuando ya ha ocurrido. (B) cifrar no cambia d\xf3nde se almacenan los datos. (C) limitar qui\xe9n lee los datos no controla d\xf3nde est\xe1n.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabsoberan\xeda de datos\xbb, \xabdeben quedarse en la regi\xf3n X\xbb → elecci\xf3n de ubicaci\xf3n + pol\xedtica de organizaci\xf3n, opcionalmente Assured Workloads."},{"id":"3","question":"Una empresa de pagos necesita una base de datos operativa para los saldos de las cuentas. Las transferencias entre cuentas deben ser transacciones ACID, los usuarios est\xe1n en Norteam\xe9rica, Europa y Asia, y el sistema debe seguir disponible aunque falle una regi\xf3n entera, sin p\xe9rdida de datos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio encaja?","options":{"A":"Spanner con configuraci\xf3n multirregional","B":"Bigtable con replicaci\xf3n multicl\xfaster","C":"BigQuery con inserciones en streaming","D":"Cloud SQL para PostgreSQL con una r\xe9plica de lectura en otra regi\xf3n"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Spanner ofrece transacciones relacionales ACID con consistencia externa, escalado horizontal y replicaci\xf3n s\xedncrona entre regiones. Una configuraci\xf3n multirregional sobrevive a la ca\xedda de una regi\xf3n sin perder datos (RPO 0) y ofrece hasta un 99,999% de disponibilidad.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) la replicaci\xf3n de Bigtable es eventualmente consistente y no tiene transacciones de varias filas. (C) BigQuery es un almac\xe9n anal\xedtico, no un sistema OLTP para saldos. (D) las r\xe9plicas de Cloud SQL son as\xedncronas; promover una r\xe9plica de otra regi\xf3n puede perder escrituras recientes, y las escrituras se limitan a una regi\xf3n primaria.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabglobal\xbb, \xabconsistencia fuerte\xbb, \xabACID\xbb, \xabsin p\xe9rdida de datos si cae una regi\xf3n\xbb → Spanner multirregional."},{"id":"4","question":"Est\xe1s migrando la anal\xedtica desde una base de datos Oracle on-premises. El origen debe seguir funcionando durante la migraci\xf3n y BigQuery debe recibir inserciones, actualizaciones y borrados con solo segundos o minutos de retraso, sin programar captura de cambios propia. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas usar?","options":{"A":"Exportaciones CSV nocturnas subidas con `gcloud storage cp` y cargadas en BigQuery","B":"Transfer Appliance enviado cada semana","C":"Datastream para captura de cambios (CDC) desde Oracle, replicando directamente en BigQuery","D":"BigQuery Data Transfer Service programado una vez al d\xeda desde Oracle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Datastream es un servicio serverless de CDC y replicaci\xf3n. Lee los logs de cambios de la base de datos de origen (Oracle, MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server) y env\xeda los cambios a BigQuery (o Cloud Storage), aplicando inserciones, actualizaciones y borrados con poca latencia y sin c\xf3digo propio. El origen sigue funcionando.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) los CSV nocturnos suponen hasta un d\xeda de retraso y no reflejan los borrados. (B) Transfer Appliance sirve para mover grandes vol\xfamenes offline una vez, no para replicar de forma continua. (D) una transferencia diaria no cumple la latencia exigida.\\n\\nHerramientas de migraci\xf3n: Database Migration Service (base de datos a Cloud SQL o AlloyDB), Datastream (CDC a BigQuery), BigQuery Data Transfer Service (fuentes SaaS y almacenes, programado), Storage Transfer Service y Transfer Appliance (archivos y volumen)."},{"id":"5","question":"Un pipeline de streaming en Dataflow calcula los ingresos por ventana de 1 minuto en tiempo de evento. Algunos eventos m\xf3viles llegan hasta 10 minutos tarde porque los dispositivos se quedan sin conexi\xf3n, y ahora se descartan. Negocio quiere incluirlos y que los resultados se actualicen cuando lleguen. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas cambiar?","options":{"A":"Pasar a ventanas de tiempo de procesamiento para que todo evento caiga en la ventana actual","B":"Aumentar el n\xfamero de workers de Dataflow","C":"Configurar en la ventana un allowed lateness de al menos 10 minutos y un trigger que vuelva a disparar con los datos tard\xedos (por ejemplo un trigger tras la watermark con disparos tard\xedos), con paneles acumulativos si los consumidores quieren totales actualizados","D":"Rechazar en la suscripci\xf3n de Pub/Sub los eventos con m\xe1s de 1 minuto de antig\xfcedad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"En Apache Beam, la watermark estima cu\xe1ndo han llegado todos los datos de una ventana. Los datos por detr\xe1s de la watermark son tard\xedos y se descartan salvo que la ventana tenga allowed lateness. El allowed lateness mantiene abierto el estado de la ventana y un trigger con disparos tard\xedos emite resultados actualizados. El modo acumulativo vuelve a emitir el total actualizado; el modo de descarte emite solo la diferencia.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) las ventanas de tiempo de procesamiento asignan los eventos por hora de llegada, lo que da ingresos por minuto err\xf3neos. (B) m\xe1s workers no cambian la sem\xe1ntica de la tardanza. (D) rechazar datos es lo contrario de lo que se pide.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabhay que incluir los datos tard\xedos\xbb → allowed lateness + triggers; distingue tiempo de evento, tiempo de procesamiento y watermark."},{"id":"6","question":"Una empresa traslada cientos de jobs de Apache Spark existentes desde un cl\xfaster Hadoop on-premises. La direcci\xf3n los quiere funcionando pronto en Google Cloud, con cambios m\xednimos de c\xf3digo y, donde se pueda, sin cl\xfaster que gestionar. \xbfQu\xe9 recomiendas?","options":{"A":"Ejecutar los jobs en Dataproc, con Google Cloud Serverless for Apache Spark (Dataproc Serverless) donde sea posible y cl\xfasteres por job en el resto, con los datos en Cloud Storage","B":"Reescribir cada job en Apache Beam y ejecutarlo en Dataflow antes de migrar","C":"Reescribir cada job como procedimientos almacenados de BigQuery antes de migrar","D":"Instalar Hadoop a mano en VM de Compute Engine para replicar el cl\xfaster antiguo"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dataproc ejecuta Spark y Hadoop de c\xf3digo abierto con pocos o ning\xfan cambio, as\xed que es la v\xeda de lift-and-shift. Serverless for Apache Spark elimina por completo la gesti\xf3n del cl\xfaster: env\xedas lotes y pagas por lo que se ejecuta. Mantener los datos en Cloud Storage (el conector de Cloud Storage sustituye a HDFS) separa almacenamiento y c\xf3mputo.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) y (C) reescribir primero cientos de jobs es lento y arriesgado; puede ser una modernizaci\xf3n posterior. (D) Hadoop autogestionado en VM conlleva toda la carga operativa.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabSpark/Hadoop existente\xbb, \xabcambios m\xednimos\xbb → Dataproc; \xabpipeline nuevo, batch y streaming unificados, serverless\xbb → Dataflow."},{"id":"7","question":"Decenas de aplicaciones publican y consumen eventos con la API de cliente de Apache Kafka sobre un cl\xfaster Kafka autogestionado on-premises. El equipo quiere pasar a Google Cloud, dejar de operar brokers y ZooKeeper o KRaft, y no reescribir productores ni consumidores. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"Pub/Sub, tras reescribir cada productor y consumidor con las bibliotecas cliente de Pub/Sub","B":"Kafka instalado a mano en StatefulSets de GKE","C":"Colas de Cloud Tasks","D":"Google Cloud Managed Service for Apache Kafka"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Managed Service for Apache Kafka ejecuta por ti cl\xfasteres de Apache Kafka de c\xf3digo abierto: Google se ocupa del aprovisionamiento de brokers, el escalado, los parches y la replicaci\xf3n entre zonas. Las aplicaciones siguen usando las APIs y clientes est\xe1ndar de Kafka, as\xed que no hay que reescribir nada.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) Pub/Sub es un gran servicio de mensajer\xeda serverless, pero pasarse a \xe9l supone cambiar todos los clientes. (B) Kafka en GKE sigue siendo autogestionado. (C) Cloud Tasks sirve para despachar tareas as\xedncronas individuales a manejadores, no es una plataforma de streaming basada en log.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabmantener las APIs de Kafka\xbb, \xabdejar de gestionar brokers\xbb → Managed Service for Apache Kafka; \xabmensajer\xeda nueva, serverless y global\xbb → Pub/Sub."},{"id":"8","question":"Las rese\xf1as de producto en muchos idiomas ya est\xe1n en una tabla de BigQuery. Los analistas quieren una columna nueva con el sentimiento y la categor\xeda de queja principal de cada rese\xf1a, actualizada a diario, escrita en SQL y sin sacar los datos de BigQuery. \xbfCu\xe1l es el enfoque m\xe1s sencillo?","options":{"A":"Exportar la tabla a CSV, etiquetarla a mano en una hoja de c\xe1lculo y volver a cargarla","B":"Crear en BigQuery un modelo remoto sobre un modelo Gemini y llamarlo desde SQL (por ejemplo con `AI.GENERATE` o `ML.GENERATE_TEXT`), y programar la consulta","C":"Entrenar una regresi\xf3n log\xedstica con `CREATE MODEL` sobre las rese\xf1as sin etiquetar","D":"Copiar las rese\xf1as a Cloud SQL y escribir un procedimiento almacenado que las clasifique"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las funciones de IA generativa de BigQuery llaman a modelos Gemini a trav\xe9s de un modelo remoto (con una conexi\xf3n de recurso de Cloud), as\xed que puedes enriquecer filas con sentimiento, clasificaci\xf3n, extracci\xf3n o traducci\xf3n usando SQL. Una consulta programada mantiene la columna al d\xeda, y los datos nunca salen de BigQuery. Es el punto \xabAI data enrichment\xbb de la gu\xeda.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) etiquetar a mano no escala. (C) la regresi\xf3n log\xedstica necesita datos de entrenamiento etiquetados, y no los hay. (D) Cloud SQL no entiende lenguaje y mover los datos a\xf1ade trabajo.\\n\\nRelacionado: `ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING` crea embeddings para b\xfasqueda sem\xe1ntica y RAG; Gemini en BigQuery tambi\xe9n ayuda a escribir y traducir SQL."},{"id":"9","question":"Cada noche un flujo debe: esperar a un archivo de un socio en Cloud Storage, ejecutar un job de Spark en Dataproc y despu\xe9s tres transformaciones de BigQuery en orden, reintentar los pasos fallidos, alertar si hay fallos y permitir reprocesar fechas pasadas. \xbfCu\xe1l es la orquestaci\xf3n m\xe1s adecuada?","options":{"A":"Un DAG de Apache Airflow en Cloud Composer (Managed Service for Apache Airflow) con operadores y sensores de Google Cloud, reintentos, alertas y backfill","B":"Cuatro cron jobs independientes en una VM de Compute Engine separados una hora","C":"Una \xfanica consulta programada de BigQuery que intente hacerlo todo","D":"Una Cloud Run function que espere dormida a que llegue el archivo"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cloud Composer, el servicio gestionado de Apache Airflow, modela el flujo como un DAG. Un sensor espera el archivo, los operadores de Dataproc y BigQuery ejecutan los pasos con dependencias expl\xedcitas, y vienen incluidos los reintentos por tarea, callbacks o alertas de fallo y el backfill por fecha l\xf3gica.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) los cron espaciados por tiempo ignoran las dependencias y fallan en silencio. (C) una consulta programada no puede esperar archivos ni ejecutar Spark. (D) las funciones tienen l\xedmite de tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n y dormir desperdicia recursos.\\n\\nRelacionado: Workflows sirve para orquestaci\xf3n serverless ligera de llamadas a APIs; Composer, para DAGs de datos complejos con muchos operadores, backfill y programaci\xf3n. Dataform gestiona transformaciones SQL dentro de BigQuery."},{"id":"10","question":"Una tabla de Bigtable guarda lecturas de 500.000 sensores que env\xedan un valor por segundo. Los paneles leen la \xfaltima hora de un sensor. Con la clave de fila `timestamp#sensor_id`, todas las escrituras van al mismo nodo y el rendimiento es malo. \xbfQu\xe9 dise\xf1o de clave lo arregla?","options":{"A":"Mantener `timestamp#sensor_id` y a\xf1adir m\xe1s nodos","B":"Usar un UUID aleatorio como clave de fila","C":"Usar `sensor_id#timestamp_invertido` (o `sensor_id#timestamp`) para repartir las escrituras entre sensores y tener contiguas las filas recientes de cada sensor","D":"Usar solo `timestamp` como clave y guardar el ID del sensor en una columna"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bigtable ordena las filas por clave y divide el espacio de claves en tablets que sirven los nodos. Una clave que empieza por timestamp env\xeda cada escritura nueva al final del espacio de claves y crea un punto caliente. Empezar por el ID del sensor reparte las escrituras, y a\xf1adir un timestamp (invertido) mantiene juntas las lecturas de un sensor, con las m\xe1s recientes primero, as\xed que \xabla \xfaltima hora de un sensor\xbb es un escaneo de rango r\xe1pido.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) m\xe1s nodos no ayudan si un solo tablet recibe todas las escrituras. (B) las claves aleatorias reparten escrituras pero impiden escanear por sensor y tiempo. (D) una clave solo con timestamp es el peor punto caliente.\\n\\nPista de examen: la clave de fila de Bigtable debe seguir el patr\xf3n de lectura principal y evitar prefijos crecientes; considera la promoci\xf3n de campos y el salting."},{"id":"11","question":"Una tabla de pedidos de 40 TB en BigQuery crece a diario. Casi todas las consultas filtran por `order_date` para unos pocos d\xedas y a menudo por `customer_id`. El coste es alto porque las consultas recorren toda la tabla. \xbfC\xf3mo la redise\xf1as?","options":{"A":"Dividir los datos en una tabla por cliente","B":"Particionar la tabla por `order_date` y agruparla (clustering) por `customer_id`, y opcionalmente exigir filtro de partici\xf3n","C":"Particionar la tabla por `customer_id` usando solo particionado por rango de enteros","D":"Crear una vista con `SELECT *` sobre la tabla"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Particionar por fecha permite a BigQuery descartar las particiones que no cumplen el filtro de fecha, as\xed que solo se leen y facturan esos d\xedas. El clustering por `customer_id` ordena los datos dentro de cada partici\xf3n, de modo que los filtros por cliente leen menos bloques. `require_partition_filter` evita escaneos completos accidentales.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) miles de tablas por cliente (sharding) son dif\xedciles de gestionar y m\xe1s lentas que una tabla particionada. (C) el particionado por rango de enteros sobre un ID de alta cardinalidad tiene un n\xfamero de particiones limitado y no ayuda al filtro de fecha. (D) una vista no cambia cu\xe1ntos datos se leen.\\n\\nRelacionado: desnormaliza con campos anidados y repetidos (STRUCT y ARRAY) para evitar joins caros en un almac\xe9n de datos."},{"id":"12","question":"Un data lake en Cloud Storage contiene tablas Parquet y Apache Iceberg que usan tanto Spark como BigQuery. Seguridad quiere control de acceso por fila y por columna aplicado igual en todos los motores, sin copiar los datos al almacenamiento de BigQuery y sin dar a los usuarios acceso directo al bucket. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas usar?","options":{"A":"URL firmadas para cada objeto","B":"Roles IAM a nivel de bucket para cada analista","C":"Cargar todos los datos en tablas nativas de BigQuery cada noche","D":"Tablas BigLake sobre los datos de Cloud Storage, con acceso delegado mediante una conexi\xf3n y seguridad de grano fino definida en las tablas"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las tablas BigLake permiten que BigQuery y los motores de c\xf3digo abierto (Spark mediante el conector de BigQuery o el metastore de BigLake) consulten formatos abiertos en Cloud Storage. El acceso se delega mediante una conexi\xf3n de servicio, as\xed que los usuarios nunca necesitan permisos sobre el bucket, y la seguridad por fila, la seguridad por columna (policy tags) y el enmascaramiento se aplican de forma coherente. Los datos se quedan en el lake.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) las URL firmadas dan acceso por objeto sin control de filas ni columnas. (B) el IAM de bucket es todo o nada a nivel de objeto. (C) copiar los datos duplica el almacenamiento y crea una segunda fuente de verdad.\\n\\nRelacionado: las tablas BigLake Iceberg en BigQuery admiten escritura y formato abierto; Dataplex a\xf1ade descubrimiento, perfilado y calidad de datos en todo el lake."},{"id":"13","question":"Una gran empresa tiene dominios de datos (ventas, log\xedstica, finanzas), cada uno due\xf1o de sus proyectos con datasets de BigQuery y buckets de Cloud Storage. La direcci\xf3n quiere que cada dominio conserve la propiedad mientras un equipo central ofrece un cat\xe1logo compartido, pol\xedticas coherentes, perfilado y controles de calidad de datos. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque encaja?","options":{"A":"Copiar los datos de todos los dominios a un \xfanico proyecto central de BigQuery gestionado por el equipo central","B":"Dejar que cada dominio elija sus herramientas sin cat\xe1logo compartido","C":"Guardar una lista de todos los datasets en una hoja de c\xe1lculo compartida","D":"Un modelo de gobierno federado con Dataplex: lakes y zonas por dominio, Dataplex Universal Catalog para b\xfasqueda y metadatos, pol\xedticas centrales y escaneos de perfilado y calidad de datos"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dataplex permite un gobierno federado de tipo data mesh. Los dominios conservan sus datos y proyectos, organizados l\xf3gicamente en lakes y zonas, mientras la plataforma ofrece un cat\xe1logo unificado (Dataplex Universal Catalog, antes Data Catalog), pol\xedticas y accesos gestionados de forma central, descubrimiento autom\xe1tico, perfilado, escaneos de calidad de datos y linaje.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) centralizar todos los datos elimina la propiedad de los dominios y duplica datos. (B) sin cat\xe1logo ni pol\xedticas compartidas no hay gobierno. (C) una hoja de c\xe1lculo se queda obsoleta al momento y no impone nada.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabdata mesh\xbb, \xabgobierno federado\xbb, \xabdescubrir y gobernar entre proyectos\xbb → Dataplex."},{"id":"14","question":"Un panel ejecutivo de Looker Studio ejecuta cientos de veces al d\xeda la misma agregaci\xf3n (ingresos diarios por regi\xf3n sobre una tabla de 5 TB). Va lento y cada refresco recorre la tabla base. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas hacer?","options":{"A":"Exportar la tabla a Google Sheets y conectar el panel a la hoja","B":"Crear una vista materializada de BigQuery con la agregaci\xf3n y activar BI Engine para acelerar las consultas del panel","C":"Aumentar la frecuencia de refresco del panel para mantener la cach\xe9 caliente","D":"Duplicar la tabla base en varios datasets para repartir las consultas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Una vista materializada precalcula y mantiene de forma incremental la agregaci\xf3n, as\xed que las consultas leen un resultado peque\xf1o en vez de recorrer 5 TB, y BigQuery puede reescribir autom\xe1ticamente las consultas compatibles para usarla. BI Engine es un servicio de an\xe1lisis en memoria que acelera las consultas de BI con respuestas por debajo del segundo. Juntos hacen el panel r\xe1pido y barato.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) Sheets no puede guardar ni procesar datos a esta escala y se queda desactualizado. (C) m\xe1s refrescos suponen m\xe1s escaneos completos y m\xe1s coste. (D) duplicar datos multiplica el almacenamiento y no reduce lo que lee cada consulta.\\n\\nRelacionado: los campos precalculados y las tablas resumen (por ejemplo con Dataform o consultas programadas) son otra forma de preparar datos para visualizaci\xf3n."},{"id":"15","question":"Una empresa quiere un asistente RAG sobre 2 millones de manuales internos en PDF guardados en Cloud Storage, con el \xedndice de recuperaci\xf3n gestionado en BigQuery. \xbfC\xf3mo debe preparar los datos el ingeniero de datos?","options":{"A":"Cargar cada PDF entero como una fila y enviar los archivos completos al modelo al consultar","B":"Entrenar un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n sobre los t\xedtulos de los PDF con `CREATE MODEL`","C":"Parsear los PDF (por ejemplo con Document AI o una object table con una funci\xf3n de parseo), dividir el texto en fragmentos solapados con metadatos, generar embeddings con `ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING`, guardarlos en una tabla con \xedndice vectorial y recuperar con `VECTOR_SEARCH`","D":"Convertir los PDF en im\xe1genes y guardarlos en Bigtable"},"correct":"C","explanation":"RAG recupera los fragmentos m\xe1s relevantes y se los da al modelo como contexto. Preparar datos no estructurados significa: extraer el texto (parseo de dise\xf1o con Document AI u object tables de BigQuery con una funci\xf3n de parseo), dividirlo en fragmentos del tama\xf1o adecuado para la recuperaci\xf3n (con algo de solapamiento y metadatos como origen y p\xe1gina), crear embeddings e indexarlos. En BigQuery, `ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING` con un modelo remoto de embeddings, `CREATE VECTOR INDEX` y `VECTOR_SEARCH` lo cubren de principio a fin.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) los archivos completos superan el contexto \xfatil, suben el coste y diluyen la relevancia. (B) un clasificador de t\xedtulos no recupera fragmentos. (D) las im\xe1genes en Bigtable no se pueden buscar sem\xe1nticamente.\\n\\nAlternativa: Agent Search en Agent Platform ofrece RAG predise\xf1ado si no necesitas gestionar t\xfa el pipeline."},{"id":"16","question":"Una empresa de datos meteorol\xf3gicos quiere compartir datasets de BigQuery curados y actualizados con frecuencia con decenas de organizaciones socias. Los socios deben ver siempre los \xfaltimos datos, la empresa no quiere mantener copias por socio y quiere controlar y auditar qui\xe9n se suscribe. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"BigQuery sharing (antes Analytics Hub): publicar los datasets como listings en un data exchange al que los socios se suscriben como datasets vinculados","B":"Exportar las tablas a CSV a diario y enviarlas por correo a cada socio","C":"Copiar los datasets al proyecto de cada socio cada noche","D":"Hacer los datasets p\xfablicos para `allUsers`"},"correct":"A","explanation":"BigQuery sharing (Analytics Hub) permite al publicador crear exchanges y listings. Los suscriptores obtienen en su proyecto un dataset vinculado que apunta a los datos compartidos, as\xed que no hay copias, siempre ven los datos actuales y pagan sus propias consultas. El publicador controla qui\xe9n puede suscribirse y ve el uso. Las data clean rooms lo ampl\xedan para an\xe1lisis conjunto que preserva la privacidad.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) los CSV por correo est\xe1n desactualizados, son inseguros y no escalan. (C) las copias nocturnas multiplican el almacenamiento y quedan desactualizadas entre ejecuciones. (D) el acceso p\xfablico pierde todo control sobre qui\xe9n usa los datos.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabcompartir datos con otras organizaciones sin copiarlos\xbb → BigQuery sharing / Analytics Hub."},{"id":"17","question":"Un equipo mantiene encendido 24/7 un gran cl\xfaster de Dataproc, pero sus jobs de Spark solo se ejecutan unas cuatro horas cada noche. Los datos de entrada y salida est\xe1n en Cloud Storage y BigQuery. \xbfC\xf3mo reducen m\xe1s el coste sin ralentizar los jobs?","options":{"A":"Mantener el cl\xfaster persistente y cambiarlo a un tipo de m\xe1quina mayor","B":"Mover los datos de entrada al HDFS del cl\xfaster","C":"Usar cl\xfasteres por job (ef\xedmeros) creados en cada ejecuci\xf3n y borrados despu\xe9s, por ejemplo con plantillas de workflow o Composer, o ejecutar los jobs en Serverless for Apache Spark, con workers secundarios en Spot VMs si se mantienen cl\xfasteres","D":"Desactivar el autoescalado para que el tama\xf1o del cl\xfaster sea predecible"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Con los datos en Cloud Storage y BigQuery el c\xf3mputo no guarda estado, as\xed que los cl\xfasteres pueden existir solo mientras corren los jobs. Los cl\xfasteres ef\xedmeros (creados por job o workflow y borrados despu\xe9s, o con borrado programado tras un tiempo inactivo) o los lotes de Spark serverless eliminan 20 horas diarias de coste ocioso. Las Spot VMs para workers secundarios bajan a\xfan m\xe1s el coste en trabajos tolerantes a fallos.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) un cl\xfaster siempre encendido y m\xe1s grande cuesta m\xe1s. (B) HDFS ata los datos al cl\xfaster e impide borrarlo. (D) desactivar el autoescalado quita otra palanca de coste.\\n\\nPista de examen: \xabcl\xfasteres persistentes o por job\xbb → por job (ef\xedmeros), salvo que necesites un cl\xfaster interactivo siempre encendido."},{"id":"18","question":"En BigQuery edici\xf3n Enterprise, los jobs ETL nocturnos que alimentan el cierre financiero a veces no llegan a tiempo porque los analistas lanzan grandes consultas ad hoc a la misma hora y consumen los slots compartidos. \xbfC\xf3mo garantizas capacidad para el ETL?","options":{"A":"Pedir a los analistas que no consulten de noche","B":"Pasar todas las cargas a precios bajo demanda","C":"Reescribir el ETL como consultas m\xe1s peque\xf1as que se ejecuten de una en una","D":"Crear reservas separadas (por ejemplo `etl` y `adhoc`), asignar el proyecto de ETL a la reserva `etl` con una base de slots m\xe1s autoescalado, y el proyecto de los analistas a `adhoc` con sus propios l\xedmites"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las ediciones de BigQuery usan reservas: grupos de capacidad en slots con una base y autoescalado opcional. Las asignaciones vinculan proyectos, carpetas o la organizaci\xf3n a una reserva para un tipo de job (QUERY, PIPELINE y otros). Las reservas separadas a\xedslan las cargas, de modo que los analistas no pueden dejar sin capacidad al ETL cr\xedtico. Los slots ociosos se pueden seguir compartiendo entre reservas cuando no se usan.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) una petici\xf3n no es una garant\xeda. (B) la capacidad bajo demanda es compartida y no garantiza slots para el trabajo cr\xedtico. (C) serializar el ETL lo hace m\xe1s lento y sigue compitiendo por los mismos slots.\\n\\nRelacionado: prioridad interactiva o batch. Las consultas batch esperan en cola hasta que hay slots libres, lo que encaja con trabajo no urgente."},{"id":"19","question":"Una consulta de BigQuery que devuelve las 2.000 millones de filas de una tabla ordenadas con `ORDER BY event_time` falla con \xabResources exceeded during query execution\xbb. El analista solo necesita los 1.000 eventos m\xe1s recientes. \xbfCu\xe1l es la mejor soluci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"A\xf1adir `LIMIT 1000` (y filtrar por la columna de partici\xf3n si es posible) para que BigQuery no tenga que ordenar todo el resultado en un \xfanico worker","B":"Comprar m\xe1s slots y volver a ejecutar la misma consulta","C":"Pedir un aumento de cuota de bytes de consulta","D":"Exportar la tabla a Cloud Storage y ordenar los archivos a mano"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Un `ORDER BY` global sobre un resultado enorme debe terminarse en un \xfanico sitio, lo que supera la memoria y provoca el error. A\xf1adir `LIMIT` permite a BigQuery calcular un top-N de forma distribuida, y filtrar por la columna de partici\xf3n reduce los datos le\xeddos. El grafo de ejecuci\xf3n de la consulta e `INFORMATION_SCHEMA.JOBS` muestran qu\xe9 etapa es cara.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (B) m\xe1s slots no eliminan la ordenaci\xf3n final en un solo worker. (C) no es un error de cuota. (D) sacar los datos no responde a la pregunta de forma eficiente.\\n\\nOtras causas habituales: joins con claves calientes (sesgo), `COUNT(DISTINCT)` sobre conjuntos enormes (considera `APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT`) y `SELECT *` sobre tablas anchas. Usa los gr\xe1ficos de recursos del panel de administraci\xf3n de BigQuery y Cloud Monitoring para vigilar el uso de slots y los errores."},{"id":"20","question":"Una instancia de Cloud SQL para PostgreSQL en `europe-west1` respalda una API de ingesta de datos. Ya tiene alta disponibilidad dentro de la regi\xf3n. Ahora negocio exige recuperarse en menos de una hora si toda la regi\xf3n deja de estar disponible. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas a\xf1adir?","options":{"A":"Una segunda zona en la misma regi\xf3n","B":"Una r\xe9plica de lectura en otra regi\xf3n (por ejemplo `europe-west4`), que se promociona o a la que se conmuta ante una ca\xedda regional, con la aplicaci\xf3n preparada para apuntar al nuevo primario","C":"Exportaciones diarias a un bucket de Cloud Storage en la misma regi\xf3n","D":"Un tipo de m\xe1quina mayor para la instancia primaria"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La alta disponibilidad regional (un standby en otra zona) solo protege frente a ca\xeddas de zona. Para desastres regionales, una r\xe9plica de lectura en otra regi\xf3n mantiene una copia replicada de forma as\xedncrona. Si cae la regi\xf3n primaria, promocionas la r\xe9plica, o usas las funciones de switchover y r\xe9plica DR de Cloud SQL Enterprise Plus, y rediriges la aplicaci\xf3n. Eso cumple un RTO de aproximadamente una hora con un RPO peque\xf1o.\\n\\nPor qu\xe9 fallan las dem\xe1s: (A) la instancia ya es multizona. (C) las copias en la misma regi\xf3n se pierden con ella y restaurar tarda m\xe1s. (D) una m\xe1quina mayor no hace nada ante una ca\xedda regional.\\n\\nRelacionado: en Memorystore for Redis, replicaci\xf3n entre regiones; en BigQuery, replicaci\xf3n de datasets entre regiones. Spanner multirregional tiene RPO 0 por dise\xf1o."}]},{"id":"aws-aif-c01-open","title":"AWS AI Practitioner (AIF-C01) — banco abierto (EN)","questions":[{"id":"aif-q004","question":"A manufacturer built a model that classifies photos of parts as defective or not. The team wants to know what proportion of the part images the model classified correctly. Which evaluation metric should they use?","options":{"A":"Mean absolute error (MAE)","B":"Number of training epochs","C":"Root mean squared error (RMSE)","D":"Accuracy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Accuracy is the ratio of correct predictions to the total number of predictions, so it directly measures how many part images the model classified correctly, which is exactly what the team wants for this classification task.\\nRoot mean squared error (RMSE) and mean absolute error (MAE) are regression metrics that quantify the difference between predicted and actual continuous values, so neither is suited to measuring classification correctness. Number of training epochs is a training configuration setting that controls how many passes the model makes over the data; it is not an evaluation metric at all."},{"id":"aif-q006","question":"A company runs an ML pipeline on Amazon SageMaker AI in production. Its requests carry large payloads of up to 1 GB and can take up to an hour to process, and the company wants near real-time latency. Which SageMaker AI inference option meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Real-time inference","B":"Batch transform","C":"Serverless inference","D":"Asynchronous inference"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Asynchronous inference is built for large payloads of up to 1 GB and long processing times of up to an hour; it queues incoming requests and processes them in the background, returning results when ready without requiring the caller to wait synchronously, which suits workloads that need near real-time handling of demanding requests.\\nReal-time inference targets small payloads and fast synchronous responses, and its payload and timeout limits would be exceeded by 1 GB inputs and hour-long processing. Serverless inference suits intermittent traffic with smaller payloads and likewise cannot handle such large inputs or long durations. Batch transform processes large datasets offline in bulk and is not designed to deliver near real-time latency."},{"id":"aif-q007","question":"A company needs to build models for several new but related tasks. Rather than training models from scratch, it wants to adapt existing pre-trained models. Which ML strategy meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Increase the number of epochs","B":"Use transfer learning","C":"Decrease the number of epochs","D":"Use unsupervised learning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Transfer learning lets a company take a model already trained on one task and adapt it to new, related tasks, which reuses the knowledge captured in the pre-trained model and saves significant time and data compared to training from scratch.\\nIncreasing the number of epochs simply trains a model for more passes over the data and does not provide a way to reuse an existing pre-trained model. Decreasing the number of epochs reduces training passes and likewise has nothing to do with adapting pre-trained models for new tasks. Unsupervised learning finds patterns in unlabeled data but is a learning approach rather than a strategy for reusing and adapting an already trained model."},{"id":"aif-q014","question":"A startup is building an educational quiz app that asks players questions such as: \\"A bag holds six red, four green, and three yellow marbles. What is the probability of drawing a green marble?\\" Which approach satisfies this requirement with the LEAST operational overhead?","options":{"A":"Train a supervised learning regression model to predict the probability","B":"Build an unsupervised learning model to estimate the probability density","C":"Apply reinforcement learning to train a model that returns the probability","D":"Write simple code that calculates the probability using basic arithmetic rules"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The probability here is a deterministic calculation of favorable outcomes divided by total outcomes, so a few lines of code applying basic arithmetic produce an exact answer with no data to gather, no model to train, and no endpoint to host, giving the least operational overhead.\\nThe three machine learning options all stand up an unnecessary pipeline of data collection, training, tuning, and hosting for a problem that already has a known closed-form answer. Beyond that shared waste, supervised regression would only approximate a value that can be computed exactly; unsupervised learning is meant to uncover structure in unlabeled data, which is irrelevant when the formula is known; and reinforcement learning is designed for sequential decision-making guided by rewards in an environment, not for answering a static arithmetic question."},{"id":"aif-q015","question":"A company has deployed a model to production and wants a metric that reflects how efficiently the model operates at runtime. Which metric should it use?","options":{"A":"Average inference latency","B":"Customer satisfaction score (CSAT)","C":"Number of training examples","D":"Training time per epoch"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Average inference latency captures how long the model takes to return a prediction once it is running, so it directly reflects the runtime efficiency of operating the model in production, where lower latency means faster, more efficient responses.\\nCustomer satisfaction score (CSAT) measures how happy users are with a product or service, which is a business outcome rather than a measure of operational efficiency. Training time per epoch and number of training examples both relate to the training phase, namely how long training runs and how much data it uses, and say nothing about how efficiently the deployed model operates at inference time."},{"id":"aif-q017","question":"A retailer has accumulated petabytes of customer records that have no labels or predefined categories. The business wants to group these customers into distinct segments to target them with tailored promotions. Which machine learning approach best fits this requirement?","options":{"A":"Supervised learning","B":"Unsupervised learning","C":"Reinforcement learning","D":"Semi-supervised learning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Unsupervised learning discovers patterns and natural groupings within unlabeled data, making it the right approach for segmenting customers when no predefined labels or categories exist, as is the case with the retailer\'s unlabeled records.\\nSupervised learning requires labeled training data where each record has a known target output, which the retailer does not have. Reinforcement learning trains an agent through rewards and penalties as it interacts with an environment, which does not apply to segmenting a static dataset of customers. Semi-supervised learning relies on a small amount of labeled data combined with a larger pool of unlabeled data, but here there are no labels at all, so a purely unsupervised approach is the correct fit."},{"id":"aif-q020","question":"A company wants to use AI to protect its application from threats by checking whether an incoming IP address comes from a suspicious source. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Build a speech recognition system","B":"Develop an anomaly detection system","C":"Create a fraud forecasting system","D":"Set up a named entity recognition system using Natural Language Processing (NLP)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An anomaly detection system learns what normal traffic and behavior look like and flags activity that deviates from those patterns, so it can pick out IP addresses that behave suspiciously or differ from legitimate sources, directly meeting the goal of protecting the application from threats.\\nA speech recognition system converts spoken audio into text and has nothing to do with analyzing IP addresses. A named entity recognition system based on NLP extracts entities such as names and places from text, not network threat signals. A fraud forecasting system predicts future fraud volumes or trends rather than flagging an individual suspicious IP as it arrives."},{"id":"aif-q027","question":"A company has built an image classification model and wants a web application to call it for real-time predictions. The company needs a fully managed way to host the model and serve those predictions that scales automatically with demand. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Use Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference to deploy the model","B":"Use Amazon CloudFront to deploy the model","C":"Use Amazon API Gateway to host the model and serve predictions","D":"Use AWS Batch to host the model and serve predictions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference is a fully managed option that hosts a model and serves predictions on demand while automatically provisioning and scaling the underlying compute, so a web application can call the model for real-time predictions without the company managing capacity.\\nAmazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and distributes content to reduce latency, and it cannot host an ML model or generate predictions. Amazon API Gateway creates and manages the API layer that fronts a backend, but it does not itself host a model or run inference and would still need a compute service behind it. AWS Batch runs batch computing jobs on managed compute and is suited to offline bulk processing rather than serving low-latency, on-demand predictions to a web application."},{"id":"aif-q031","question":"A wildlife researcher has a large collection of animal photos and wants to automatically identify and categorize the animals in each image without manual effort. Which strategy meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Named entity recognition","B":"Inpainting","C":"Object detection","D":"Anomaly detection"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Object detection is a Computer Vision technique that automatically finds and classifies the objects within an image, so it can locate each animal in a photo and assign it to a category without manual effort, which is exactly what the researcher needs.\\nAnomaly detection flags data points that deviate from normal patterns, not the contents of an image. Named entity recognition is a Natural Language Processing (NLP) technique that extracts entities such as names and places from text rather than from photos. Inpainting is a generative Computer Vision technique that fills in missing or removed regions of an image, rather than identifying and categorizing what is in it."},{"id":"aif-q045","question":"A hotel chain wants to read sentiment from written guest reviews. Which two AWS services can meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Polly","C":"Amazon Bedrock","D":"Amazon Lex","E":"Amazon Rekognition"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Amazon Comprehend is a Natural Language Processing (NLP) service with built-in sentiment analysis that can determine whether text is positive, negative, neutral, or mixed, making it well suited to analyze written reviews. Amazon Bedrock provides access to foundation models that can also classify the sentiment of text through prompting.\\nAmazon Polly is a text-to-speech service that converts written text into lifelike spoken audio, which is the opposite of analyzing text content. Amazon Lex is a service for building conversational chatbots and voice interfaces, not for analyzing the sentiment of existing text. Amazon Rekognition is a Computer Vision service that analyzes images and video for objects, faces, and scenes, and cannot process written text for sentiment."},{"id":"aif-q056","question":"An electronics company wants to forecast customer demand for its memory products. Its team has no coding skills and no knowledge of ML algorithms, yet needs to build a data-driven predictive model that draws on both internal and external data. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Import the data into Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler and build the forecasting models using Amazon SageMaker AI built-in algorithms","B":"Import the data into Amazon SageMaker Canvas and build the forecasting models in Canvas","C":"Store the data in Amazon S3 and build the forecasting models using Amazon SageMaker AI built-in algorithms","D":"Import the data into Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler and build the forecasting models by writing custom training code"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas is a no-code service that lets business users import data and build ML models and generate predictions through a visual point-and-click interface, without writing code or understanding ML algorithms, which is exactly what a team with no coding or ML experience needs to forecast demand.\\nThe other options all require technical skills the team does not have. Using Amazon SageMaker AI built-in algorithms, whether the data is stored in Amazon S3 or prepared in Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler, still involves coding to build and train the models, and writing custom training code clearly does too. Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler is also focused on data preparation rather than no-code model building, so none of these meets the no-code requirement."},{"id":"aif-q058","question":"A company is building a customer support chatbot that should keep getting better at responding by learning from its previous conversations. Which AI learning strategy provides this self-improvement capability?","options":{"A":"Supervised learning using a hand-labeled set of good and bad example responses","B":"Unsupervised learning to group similar customer questions into clusters","C":"Reinforcement learning that rewards responses which gain positive customer feedback","D":"Supervised learning driven by a regularly updated FAQ knowledge base"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Reinforcement Learning trains a model by rewarding desirable outcomes and penalising undesirable ones, enabling the chatbot to continuously improve its responses based on real signals such as positive customer feedback without requiring a manually prepared labeled dataset for each update.\\nSupervised Learning with a manually curated dataset of good and bad responses requires ongoing human effort to label training examples and does not enable autonomous self-improvement from live interactions. Unsupervised Learning identifies patterns and clusters in unlabeled data, which could help group similar customer questions but provides no mechanism for improving response quality based on feedback signals. Supervised Learning with a continuously updated FAQ database also relies on manual maintenance of the underlying dataset rather than autonomous learning from customer interactions."},{"id":"aif-q059","question":"An AI practitioner has trained a deep learning model that classifies images into different dog breeds. The practitioner now wants to measure how well the model performs. Which metric will help evaluate the model\'s performance?","options":{"A":"Confusion matrix","B":"Correlation matrix","C":"R2 score","D":"Mean squared error (MSE)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A confusion matrix is the appropriate metric for evaluating a classification model because it breaks predictions down into true positives, false positives, true negatives, and false negatives, giving a detailed view of where the model is correct and where it misclassifies each breed.\\nA correlation matrix measures the statistical relationships between variables in a dataset and is a data exploration tool used during EDA, not a model performance metric. R2 score, or the coefficient of determination, measures how well a regression model explains variance in continuous output values and is not applicable to classification tasks. Mean squared error measures the average squared difference between predicted and actual continuous values, making it a regression metric that is also unsuitable for evaluating a classification model."},{"id":"aif-q062","question":"An insurance company needs to run its ML model over several years of archived claims records to generate predictions, with each dataset running to several gigabytes. The results are not required immediately. Which Amazon SageMaker AI inference option best fits this need?","options":{"A":"Serverless inference","B":"Batch transform","C":"Real-time inference","D":"Asynchronous inference"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Batch transform runs inference as an offline job over an entire dataset without a persistent endpoint, so it can process the multiple-gigabyte archived data efficiently when the predictions are not needed immediately.\\nReal-time inference and serverless inference both return predictions synchronously from a live endpoint for low-latency, on-demand requests, so neither suits offline bulk processing of large datasets. Asynchronous inference queues incoming requests and is intended for large individual payloads that need near-real-time handling, rather than running a single job across an entire archived dataset."},{"id":"aif-q068","question":"A recruitment agency receives a growing volume of resumes as PDF files that it can no longer review manually. It needs an automated way to convert these PDF resumes into plain text for further processing. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Lex","B":"Amazon Textract","C":"Amazon Transcribe","D":"Amazon Personalize"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Textract automatically extracts text and data from scanned documents and PDFs, so it can turn the resumes into plain text for downstream processing.\\nAmazon Lex builds conversational chatbots and voice interfaces. Amazon Transcribe converts speech audio into text rather than reading documents. Amazon Personalize generates real-time recommendations. None of these extract text from PDF files."},{"id":"aif-q072","question":"A data science team has just gathered a new dataset and is examining it by calculating summary statistics, plotting charts, and building a correlation matrix. Which stage of the ML pipeline does this describe?","options":{"A":"Model training","B":"Exploratory data analysis","C":"Data collection","D":"Feature engineering"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Exploratory data analysis is the stage where a team summarizes a dataset through statistics, visualisations, and correlation matrices to understand its structure, distribution, and relationships before any modeling begins, which is exactly what the team is doing.\\nModel training is a later stage where an algorithm learns patterns from prepared data, not where the raw dataset is examined. Data collection is the earlier stage of gathering the raw data; although the data was just gathered, the activities described are about analyzing it rather than collecting it. Feature engineering creates or selects input variables to improve model performance, which happens after the data is understood."},{"id":"aif-q073","question":"A company recovered a set of text documents after a database error, but some words are missing. It wants an ML model that can predict the most likely word for each gap. Which type of model is suitable?","options":{"A":"Topic modeling","B":"Clustering models","C":"BERT-based models","D":"Prescriptive analytics models"},"correct":"C","explanation":"BERT-based models are transformer language models trained with masked language modeling, so they predict a missing token by drawing on the context that surrounds it on both sides, which is exactly what suggesting words to fill the gaps requires.\\nTopic modeling discovers the themes present across a collection of documents but does not predict individual missing words. Clustering models group similar items together in an unsupervised way and produce no word-level predictions. Prescriptive analytics models recommend which action to take given a predicted outcome and have nothing to do with text completion."},{"id":"aif-q092","question":"A software company wants to use AI to boost its developers\' productivity when writing software. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use a binary classification model to generate code reviews","B":"Use a clustering algorithm to group similar code snippets together","C":"Use a Natural Language Processing (NLP) tool to generate code","D":"Use a forecasting model to predict future numbers of code defects"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Natural Language Processing (NLP) tools, which underpin generative AI coding assistants, can turn plain-language descriptions into working code, autocomplete snippets, and write whole blocks of code, automating repetitive work and speeding up development, so they directly increase developer productivity.\\nA binary classification model only assigns inputs to one of two categories, so it cannot generate code or reviews. A clustering algorithm groups similar items together but produces no new code. A forecasting model predicts future numeric values, such as defect counts over time, and likewise does nothing to help developers write code faster."},{"id":"aif-q093","question":"A retail store plans to forecast demand for a product over the coming weeks using the Amazon SageMaker AI DeepAR algorithm. Which type of data is required to meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Text data","B":"Time series data","C":"Audio data","D":"Image data"},"correct":"B","explanation":"DeepAR is a forecasting algorithm that predicts future values from historical observations recorded in sequence over time, so it needs time series data such as past demand indexed by date in order to learn temporal patterns and project them forward.\\nText data suits Natural Language Processing tasks, image data suits Computer Vision tasks, and audio data suits speech or sound processing; none of these capture the chronological, time-indexed measurements that a demand-forecasting model relies on."},{"id":"aif-q103","question":"An AI practitioner needs to predict which species each flower belongs to, using its petal length, petal width, sepal length, and sepal width. Which algorithm meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Linear regression","B":"K-nearest neighbors (k-NN)","C":"K-means","D":"Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"K-nearest neighbors (k-NN) is a supervised classification algorithm that labels a new data point by the majority class of its closest neighbors in feature space, so it fits assigning a flower to a species from its petal and sepal measurements.\\nK-means is an unsupervised clustering algorithm that groups unlabeled data rather than assigning known categories. Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA) is a time-series forecasting method for sequential data ordered over time. Linear regression predicts a continuous numeric value, not a category."},{"id":"aif-q115","question":"A wildlife research team wants to train a model that identifies the animal species shown in camera-trap photos. It has a large set of images that are already tagged with the correct species and will not be labeling any more. Which type of learning should it use?","options":{"A":"Reinforcement learning","B":"Supervised learning","C":"Active learning","D":"Unsupervised learning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Supervised learning is the right choice because the team already has a large dataset in which every image is paired with a known label identifying the species. The model learns to map inputs to the correct output labels from this labeled training data, which is the defining characteristic of supervised learning.\\nUnsupervised learning is used when no labeled data is available and the model must discover patterns or groupings on its own, which does not apply here because labeled data already exists. Reinforcement learning trains an agent to take actions in an environment to maximize a cumulative reward through trial and error, and is not suited to classifying images from a static labeled dataset. Active learning is a strategy that iteratively selects the most uncertain examples and requests human labelers to annotate them, reducing the total labeling effort needed; however, the team has explicitly stated they will not be labeling any more data, so active learning\'s core mechanism cannot be used and it offers no benefit in this scenario."},{"id":"aif-q117","question":"A restaurant chain wants to build an ML model to cut daily food waste and increase revenue, and it needs the model\'s accuracy to keep improving over time. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Use Amazon Personalize and refine it with historical data only","B":"Use Amazon Rekognition to improve the model","C":"Use Amazon SageMaker AI and retrain it on newer data","D":"Use Amazon CloudWatch to examine customer orders"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker AI is a fully managed ML platform that supports the complete model development lifecycle, including building, training, and deploying models. Retraining on newer data lets the model continuously learn from recent patterns in food demand and waste, directly improving accuracy over time as conditions change.\\nAmazon Personalize delivers personalized product and content recommendations and is not a general-purpose platform for training custom regression or forecasting models; relying on historical data only would also stop the model adapting to recent changes. Amazon CloudWatch is a monitoring and observability service for metrics, logs, and alarms on AWS resources, so it cannot examine customer orders or train ML models. Amazon Rekognition is a Computer Vision service for image and video analysis such as object detection and facial recognition, which does not apply to a demand-planning model for reducing food waste."},{"id":"aif-q118","question":"A property company has built an ML model that predicts home sale prices and wants to host it for predictions without having to manage any servers or infrastructure. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Deploy the model on an Amazon EC2 instance","B":"Deploy the model using Amazon CloudFront with an Amazon S3 integration","C":"Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker AI endpoint","D":"Deploy the model on an Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) cluster"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An Amazon SageMaker AI endpoint is a fully managed deployment option that handles the underlying servers, scaling, and maintenance automatically, so the company can serve predictions from its model without provisioning or managing any infrastructure of its own.\\nDeploying the model on an Amazon EC2 instance or an Amazon EKS cluster would require the company to provision, scale, patch, and manage the servers or containers itself, which is the opposite of what it wants. Amazon CloudFront with Amazon S3 is a content delivery and static-storage combination for distributing files and web content, not a service for hosting and running ML model inference."},{"id":"aif-q120","question":"A manufacturing company uses AI to inspect products and find any damages or defects. Which type of AI application is the company using?","options":{"A":"Recommendation system","B":"Natural language processing (NLP)","C":"Computer vision","D":"Image processing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Computer Vision is the field of AI that enables machines to interpret and analyze visual information from images and videos, making it the technology behind automated inspection systems that detect product damage or defects on a production line.\\nA recommendation system analyzes user behavior and preferences to suggest relevant products or content, which is unrelated to visual defect detection. Natural Language Processing (NLP) focuses on understanding and generating human language in text or speech, not on analyzing images. Image processing refers to low-level manipulation of images such as resizing, filtering, or enhancing them; while it can be part of a vision pipeline, it does not involve the AI-driven interpretation and classification required for defect detection."},{"id":"aif-q121","question":"A company wants to build an ML model to predict customer satisfaction and needs the model\'s tuning to be fully automated. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Athena","B":"Amazon Personalize","C":"Amazon Comprehend","D":"Amazon SageMaker AI"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker AI is a fully managed ML platform whose Automatic Model Tuning (hyperparameter optimization) feature automatically searches for the best hyperparameters and tunes the model with no manual effort, which is exactly the fully automated tuning the company needs for its customer-satisfaction model.\\nAmazon Personalize is a managed service for generating personalized recommendations, not for training and tuning a custom prediction model. Amazon Athena is an interactive query service for analyzing data in Amazon S3 with SQL and has no model-tuning capability. Amazon Comprehend is a Natural Language Processing (NLP) service for extracting insights from text and does not train or tune custom ML models."},{"id":"aif-q127","question":"A company needs a highly scalable AWS service to monitor the performance of its production ML systems. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch is a highly scalable monitoring and observability service that collects real-time metrics such as resource usage, inference latency, request counts, and errors, and it supports alarms, dashboards, and automated actions, making it well suited to tracking ML system performance.\\nAWS CloudTrail records API activity for auditing and governance rather than performance metrics. AWS Config tracks resource configuration changes and compliance. AWS Trusted Advisor offers best-practice recommendations on cost, security, and fault tolerance. None of these is built for real-time performance monitoring of ML systems."},{"id":"aif-q130","question":"A nonprofit is building a phone app for people with low vision. The app must understand spoken questions and reply with spoken output. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Build ML models to find patterns in numeric data","B":"Use generative AI summarization to produce human-like text","C":"Build custom models for image classification and recognition","D":"Use a deep learning neural network to perform speech recognition"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A deep learning neural network for speech recognition lets the app convert users\' spoken words into text it can act on, and paired with text-to-speech it can reply with spoken output, which fits an app for users with low vision.\\nML models for numeric pattern-finding address tabular data, not speech. Generative AI summarization condenses text but does not capture or speak audio. Image classification models analyze pictures, which is unrelated to hearing and speaking."},{"id":"aif-q135","question":"A property company wants to estimate the sale price of homes from features such as floor area, location, and number of bedrooms. Which algorithm should it use to meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"K-means","B":"Logistic regression","C":"Linear regression","D":"K-nearest neighbours (k-NN)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Linear regression is a supervised algorithm that models the relationship between input features and a continuous numeric target, making it the standard choice for estimating a home\'s sale price from attributes such as floor area, location, and number of bedrooms.\\nK-means is an unsupervised clustering algorithm that groups similar records and does not predict a numeric value. Logistic regression predicts the probability of a categorical outcome rather than a continuous price, despite its name. K-nearest neighbours (k-NN) can perform regression, but linear regression is the canonical and most interpretable algorithm for predicting continuous values like house prices from numeric features."},{"id":"aif-q142","question":"When evaluating a classification model, what does an F1 score measure?","options":{"A":"The model\'s speed in generating responses","B":"The financial cost of operating the model","C":"The model\'s precision and recall","D":"The energy efficiency of the model\'s computations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, so it measures the balance between them and is especially useful when the classes are imbalanced.\\nResponse speed is a latency measure. Operating cost is a financial measure. Energy efficiency is a sustainability measure. None of these is what an F1 score captures."},{"id":"aif-q170","question":"An online retailer has built a system that flags orders as potentially fraudulent, and a team manually reviews each flagged order. The company wants to reduce the time the team wastes reviewing flagged orders that turn out to be legitimate. Which evaluation metric should it optimize for?","options":{"A":"Recall","B":"Precision","C":"F1 score","D":"Accuracy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Precision is the proportion of flagged cases that are genuinely fraudulent, calculated as TP / (TP + FP). Optimizing for precision minimizes false positives, which are exactly the legitimate orders wrongly flagged that waste the team\'s review time, so it directly targets the company\'s goal.\\nRecall measures how much of the actual fraud the system catches, TP / (TP + FN); maximizing it reduces missed fraud but does nothing to limit false positives and can even increase them. Accuracy is the share of all predictions that are correct, which is misleading on imbalanced fraud data where most transactions are legitimate. F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall and balances both, rather than specifically reducing the false positives that drive the review burden."},{"id":"aif-q177","question":"Which option is a benefit of using Infrastructure as Code (IaC) in Machine Learning Operations (MLOps)?","options":{"A":"IaC eliminates the need for hyperparameter tuning","B":"IaC always provisions powerful compute instances, contributing to the training of more accurate models","C":"IaC streamlines the deployment of scalable and consistent ML workloads in cloud environments","D":"IaC minimizes overall expenses by deploying only low-cost instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IaC allows infrastructure to be defined, versioned, and automated in code, enabling repeatable and consistent provisioning of ML environments across development, testing, and production stages, which directly supports the MLOps principles of scalable systems and repeatable processes.\\nHyperparameter tuning is a model optimization activity that adjusts parameters such as learning rate and batch size during training to improve model performance; it is entirely separate from infrastructure provisioning, so IaC cannot eliminate it. IaC does not inherently provision powerful or low-cost instances, as it simply provisions whatever resources are defined in the configuration code; the choice of instance type and cost profile is determined by the engineer writing the template, not by IaC itself."},{"id":"aif-q181","question":"A bank wants to label every credit card transaction as either fraudulent or legitimate based on its transaction data. Which type of ML model meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Regression","B":"Binary classification","C":"Multi-class classification","D":"Diffusion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Binary classification predicts which of exactly two categories an input belongs to, so it is the right model type for labeling each transaction as one of two outcomes, fraudulent or legitimate, based on its features.\\nMulti-class classification assigns inputs to one of three or more categories, which is unnecessary when there are only two possible outcomes. Regression predicts continuous numeric values rather than discrete categories. Diffusion models are generative models that create new content such as images, not models that classify transactions."},{"id":"aif-q182","question":"A medical clinic wants doctors to dictate their clinical notes and have the audio automatically converted into text. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Transcribe","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Transcribe is the managed AI service that converts speech into text, so it can turn doctors\' dictated clinical notes into written text, which meets the requirement.\\nAmazon Polly does the reverse, converting text into speech. Amazon Rekognition analyzes images and video. Amazon Comprehend extracts insights such as entities and sentiment from text that already exists, but it does not convert speech to text."},{"id":"aif-q183","question":"Which type of AI model is built to predict continuous numeric values?","options":{"A":"Transformer","B":"Multi-modal","C":"Diffusion","D":"Regression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Regression is a supervised learning technique designed to predict continuous numerical values by learning the relationship between input features and a numeric output, making it the appropriate model type for tasks such as forecasting sales figures, estimating house prices, or predicting temperatures.\\nDiffusion models are generative models that learn to create new content such as images or audio by gradually denoising random noise, and are not used for numeric prediction tasks. Transformers are a neural network architecture that processes sequential data using attention mechanisms and form the basis of large language models, but the architecture itself is not a model type associated with numeric prediction. Multi-modal models are designed to process and generate multiple types of data such as text, images, and audio together, which describes the input and output modalities of a model rather than its predictive function."},{"id":"aif-q204","question":"A subscription streaming service is building an ML model to predict which customers are likely to cancel their plan. Which metric is appropriate for evaluating this binary classification model?","options":{"A":"R-squared","B":"Mean squared error (MSE)","C":"F1 score","D":"Model training time"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The F1 score combines precision and recall into a single value, which makes it well suited to a binary classification task such as churn prediction, where correctly identifying the customers who will cancel matters and a balance between false positives and false negatives is important.\\nR-squared and mean squared error (MSE) are regression metrics that measure error on continuous-value predictions, so they do not apply to a classification task. Model training time only reflects how long the model takes to train, an operational figure that says nothing about how accurately the model predicts churn."},{"id":"aif-q205","question":"While evaluating a classification model in Amazon SageMaker AI, an AI practitioner needs a metric that gives the ratio of correctly classified items to the total number of items classified, whether correctly or incorrectly. Which metric meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Recall","B":"Precision","C":"Accuracy","D":"F1 score"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Accuracy is the number of correct predictions (true positives plus true negatives) divided by the total number of predictions (all true and false positives and negatives), which is exactly the ratio of correctly classified items to every item classified that the requirement describes.\\nPrecision measures the proportion of positive predictions that are actually correct (true positives over true positives plus false positives), not all classifications. Recall measures the proportion of actual positives the model correctly identifies (true positives over true positives plus false negatives). F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, balancing those two values rather than reporting overall correct classifications."},{"id":"aif-q206","question":"A logistics company ingests several gigabytes of shipment records each day and uses them to train an ML model that predicts next-day delivery volumes. The company only needs predictions generated once per day on the full set of accumulated data. Which type of inference best meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Batch inference","B":"Asynchronous inference","C":"Real-time inference","D":"Serverless inference"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Batch inference is designed for high-volume predictions on large datasets processed together on a schedule, so the company can run a single daily job over all accumulated shipment records to generate its forecasts without maintaining a persistent endpoint.\\nAsynchronous inference is suited to handling individual requests with large payloads or long processing times by queuing them, rather than scoring an entire daily dataset in one scheduled run. Real-time inference provides immediate predictions through a continuously running endpoint for low-latency use cases, which is unnecessary and more costly when results are only needed once per day. Serverless inference automatically provisions capacity for intermittent, on-demand requests, which suits unpredictable traffic rather than a predictable bulk job processed on a fixed daily schedule."},{"id":"aif-q209","question":"Which ML technique uses training data that is labeled with the correct output values?","options":{"A":"Supervised learning","B":"Unsupervised learning","C":"Reinforcement learning","D":"Transfer learning"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Supervised learning trains models on datasets where each input example is paired with a known correct output label, allowing the model to learn the mapping between inputs and targets and make predictions on new unseen data.\\nUnsupervised learning works with entirely unlabeled data and discovers hidden patterns or groupings without any known output values to learn from. Reinforcement learning does not use labeled training data at all, instead training an agent to take actions in an environment by rewarding desirable outcomes and penalising undesirable ones. Transfer learning is a technique where a model pre-trained on one task is adapted for a different task, which is about reusing existing model knowledge rather than describing how training data is structured."},{"id":"aif-q216","question":"A studio that produces animated films wants to automatically generate subtitles for the dialogue in its videos. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Transcribe","B":"Amazon Translate","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Transcribe uses automatic speech recognition to convert spoken audio into time-stamped text and can output caption files, so it can generate the subtitles for the dialogue in the studio\'s animated videos.\\nAmazon Translate converts text from one language to another but does not turn audio into text, so it cannot produce subtitles on its own. Amazon Polly performs the opposite task, synthesising speech from written text rather than transcribing speech. Amazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service that extracts insights such as sentiment and entities from existing text and does not create transcripts from audio."},{"id":"aif-q217","question":"An online retailer wants to divide its customers into segments based on their buying behavior and preferences so it can tailor the app experience to each segment. Which ML technique should it use?","options":{"A":"Classification","B":"Regression","C":"Clustering","D":"Content Generation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Clustering is an unsupervised technique that automatically groups customers with similar purchase histories and preferences into segments without any predefined labels, which is exactly what the retailer needs in order to personalize the experience for each segment.\\nClassification is a supervised technique that assigns items to predefined categories, which would require the segments to be labeled in advance, but they are not. Regression is a supervised technique that predicts a continuous numeric value such as expected spend, not groups of similar customers. Content generation produces new content such as text or images and does not segment customers at all."},{"id":"aif-q228","question":"A business wants to automatically read incoming support emails and route each one into a topic such as billing, technical issues, or account changes. Which AI concept does this scenario represent?","options":{"A":"Speech Recognition","B":"Natural Language Processing (NLP)","C":"Computer Vision","D":"Forecasting"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Reading support emails and routing each one into a topic is a Natural Language Processing task, specifically text classification, because it requires interpreting human language in text and assigning it to a predefined category.\\nSpeech Recognition converts spoken audio into text and deals with the spoken word rather than analyzing and categorizing existing written text. Computer Vision interprets images and video rather than language, so it does not apply to email content. Forecasting predicts future numerical values from historical data, which is unrelated to sorting written messages by topic."},{"id":"aif-q232","question":"A company is building an application that must automatically organize customers and products into groups of similar items based on their attributes. Which ML strategy should the company use to meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Supervised learning","B":"Reinforcement learning","C":"Unsupervised learning","D":"Semi-supervised learning"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unsupervised learning automatically discovers patterns and natural groupings within data, which makes it the right strategy for clustering similar customers and products by their attributes when no predefined groups are provided.\\nSupervised learning requires labeled training data with known target outputs, so it cannot form groups when no labels or categories are supplied. Reinforcement learning trains an agent through rewards and penalties as it interacts with an environment, which does not apply to grouping items in a static dataset. Semi-supervised learning combines a small amount of labeled data with a larger pool of unlabeled data, but here there are no labels at all, so a fully unsupervised approach is the correct fit."},{"id":"aif-q233","question":"An online learning platform publishes its course materials in English and wants to make the same content available to learners in several other languages. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Add Amazon Transcribe to the platform","B":"Use the real-time translation feature of Amazon Translate","C":"Add Amazon Personalize to the platform","D":"Use the real-time document processing feature of Amazon Textract"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Translate provides fast, high-quality language translation of text, making it the right choice for automatically converting course materials from English into multiple other languages.\\nAmazon Transcribe is an automatic speech recognition service that converts spoken audio into text, which does not translate content between languages. Amazon Personalize generates personalized recommendations based on user behavior, which is unrelated to translating content. Amazon Textract extracts text and structured data from scanned documents and images, which addresses document data extraction rather than language translation."},{"id":"aif-q236","question":"A documentary filmmaker wants to reach a wider audience by automatically adding subtitles and voice-overs in multiple languages to their films. Which two steps together meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use Amazon Polly to generate voice-overs in multiple languages","B":"Use Amazon Transcribe and Amazon Translate to produce subtitles in multiple languages","C":"Use Amazon Translate to generate voice-overs in multiple languages","D":"Use Amazon Textract and Amazon Translate to produce subtitles in multiple languages"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Amazon Transcribe converts the film\'s spoken dialogue into text using automatic speech recognition, and Amazon Translate then renders that text into other languages to create multilingual subtitles. Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service that turns the translated text into lifelike spoken audio, enabling the multilingual voice-overs the filmmaker needs.\\nThe option pairing Amazon Textract with Amazon Translate is incorrect because Textract extracts text from scanned documents and images using optical character recognition, not from spoken audio in video files. The option using only Amazon Translate for voice-overs is incorrect because Translate converts text between languages but outputs text, not audio; generating spoken voice-overs requires Amazon Polly to synthesize speech from the translated text."},{"id":"aif-q240","question":"A healthcare company has trained a model on historical patient data, including medical history, demographics, and treatments, to predict in real time whether a patient will be readmitted within 30 days of discharge. Which task in this scenario represents AI model inference?","options":{"A":"Apply the trained model to predict whether a patient will be readmitted","B":"Collect historical patient readmission records","C":"Measure the model\'s performance using suitable evaluation metrics","D":"Analyze the data to uncover patterns and correlations among patients"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Model inference is the stage of applying an already-trained model to new data to generate predictions, so using the trained model to predict whether a patient will be readmitted is the task that represents inference in this scenario.\\nCollecting historical readmission records is the data collection stage that happens before training. Measuring the model\'s performance with evaluation metrics is the evaluation stage, which assesses a model rather than serving predictions. Analyzing the data to uncover patterns and correlations is exploratory analysis carried out before or during model building, not the act of generating predictions from a finished model."},{"id":"aif-q241","question":"A bank wants to set up workflows in which staff review the predictions produced by its ML models. It needs to define confidence thresholds that determine when a prediction is sent for human review and to adjust those thresholds as its needs change. Which AWS service meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","B":"Amazon Inspector","C":"Amazon Augmented AI (Amazon A2I)","D":"AWS Audit Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Augmented AI (Amazon A2I) is purpose-built for adding human review of ML predictions, letting the bank set confidence thresholds that decide when a prediction is sent to a human reviewer and tune those thresholds over time.\\nAmazon SageMaker Ground Truth also relies on human workers, but it is for labeling training datasets rather than reviewing live model predictions against confidence thresholds. Amazon Inspector is an automated security service that scans workloads for vulnerabilities, and AWS Audit Manager automates the collection of evidence for compliance audits; neither has anything to do with human review of ML predictions."},{"id":"aif-q243","question":"A retail company wants to build and deploy ML models on AWS without writing any code. Which AWS service or feature meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Rekognition","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas","D":"AWS DeepRacer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas offers a no-code visual interface that lets users build, train, and deploy custom ML models without writing any code, which is exactly what the company needs.\\nAmazon Rekognition (Computer Vision) and Amazon Comprehend (Natural Language Processing) are pre-built AI services that return predictions from their own models rather than letting you build and deploy your own. AWS DeepRacer is an educational tool for learning Reinforcement Learning through a simulated race car, not a service for building business ML models."},{"id":"aif-q251","question":"A company needs to collect a large dataset to train an AI assistant in a specific content area. Which dataset will meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Diverse conversations that use relevant terminology","B":"Time series data of general purpose historical sales","C":"Sentiment analysis of news articles","D":"Unique product IDs and corresponding user IDs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diverse conversations that use relevant terminology provide the contextual, domain-specific language an AI assistant needs to learn how to understand and respond accurately within the targeted content area, making this the appropriate training dataset.\\nTime series data of general purpose historical sales is structured numerical data suited to forecasting trends over time, not to training a conversational assistant in a specialized subject. Sentiment analysis of news articles produces classifications of positive or negative tone rather than the conversational examples needed to teach an assistant how to interact. Unique product IDs and corresponding user IDs are identifiers used for tasks such as recommendation systems, and contain no linguistic or contextual content from which an assistant could learn to converse."},{"id":"aif-q256","question":"An online retailer wants to divide its customers into groups based on their demographics and purchasing behavior. Which algorithm should it use to meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Decision tree","B":"Support vector machine","C":"K-nearest neighbours (k-NN)","D":"K-means"},"correct":"D","explanation":"K-means is an unsupervised clustering algorithm that partitions data points into a chosen number of groups based on similarity, so it naturally segments customers by shared demographics and buying patterns without needing labeled examples.\\nK-nearest neighbours (k-NN), Decision tree, and Support vector machine are all supervised algorithms that require labeled training data to predict a known target or class, so they suit classification or regression rather than discovering natural groupings in unlabeled customer data."},{"id":"aif-q264","question":"Which of the following scenarios is an example of unsupervised learning?","options":{"A":"A model that organizes thousands of news articles into related topics without predefined categories","B":"A model that flags a loan application as high risk or low risk","C":"A model that forecasts a city\'s electricity demand for the next day","D":"A model that teaches a robot to walk by rewarding successful movements"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Organizing news articles into related topics without predefined categories is an example of unsupervised learning, where the model finds natural patterns and clusters in the data without relying on any labeled outputs.\\nFlagging a loan application as high risk or low risk is supervised learning, because the model is trained on labeled examples where the correct outcome is already known. Forecasting a city\'s electricity demand for the next day is also supervised learning, specifically a regression task that learns from labeled historical values. Teaching a robot to walk by rewarding successful movements is reinforcement learning, where an agent improves by receiving rewards and penalties from its actions rather than discovering clusters in unlabeled data."},{"id":"aif-q268","question":"A support team uses sentiment analysis to determine the mood expressed in customer feedback. Sentiment analysis is a subset of which broader field of AI?","options":{"A":"Natural Language Processing (NLP)","B":"Computer Vision","C":"Time Series Forecasting","D":"Speech Recognition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sentiment analysis is a Natural Language Processing (NLP) task. NLP is the field of AI focused on enabling machines to understand, interpret, and derive meaning from human language, and detecting the emotional tone of text is one of its core applications.\\nComputer Vision deals with interpreting images and video rather than language. Speech Recognition converts spoken audio into text and does not itself analyze opinion or sentiment. Time Series Forecasting predicts future numeric values from sequential historical data and is unrelated to understanding language."},{"id":"aif-q270","question":"A SaaS company collects large volumes of free-form customer feedback and wants to analyze its sentiment. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use a regression algorithm to sort the feedback into predefined categories","B":"Use a recommendation engine to detect user sentiment","C":"Use a large language model (LLM) to perform Natural Language Processing (NLP) for sentiment analysis","D":"Use a time-series algorithm to predict user sentiment from past feedback"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A large language model can apply Natural Language Processing (NLP) to read unstructured text and determine whether the sentiment is positive, negative, or neutral, which suits large volumes of free-form feedback.\\nA regression algorithm predicts numeric values, not sentiment from text. A recommendation engine suggests items based on behavior. A time-series algorithm forecasts values over time. None of these analyzes the sentiment of unstructured text."},{"id":"aif-q273","question":"Which of the following is an example of Unsupervised Learning?","options":{"A":"Training a model to identify animals in photographs","B":"Producing human-like text in response to a written prompt","C":"Grouping data points into clusters based on how similar they are","D":"Predicting a house\'s selling price from features such as its size and location"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Grouping data points into clusters by similarity is a classic example of Unsupervised Learning, where the algorithm finds structure and natural groupings in the data on its own, without any labeled examples to guide it.\\nTraining a model to identify animals in photographs is Supervised Learning, since it learns from images already labeled with the correct animal. Predicting a house\'s selling price from its features is also Supervised Learning, specifically regression, because it is trained on examples with known prices. Producing human-like text from a prompt is Generative AI, which creates new content rather than discovering groupings in unlabeled data."},{"id":"aif-q276","question":"A company is training ML models on datasets. The datasets contain some classes that have more examples than other classes. The company wants to measure how well the model balances detecting and labeling the classes. Which metric should the company use?","options":{"A":"Accuracy","B":"Recall","C":"Precision","D":"F1 score"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, making it the most appropriate metric for imbalanced datasets because it penalises models that perform well on one measure but poorly on the other, ensuring the model genuinely balances both detecting all instances of a class and labeling them correctly across all classes.\\nAccuracy measures the overall percentage of correct predictions but is misleading on imbalanced datasets because a model that always predicts the majority class can achieve high accuracy while completely failing to detect minority classes. Recall measures how well the model detects all actual instances of each class, capturing the ability to find true positives, but it ignores the rate of false positives and so does not reflect how well the model balances detection and correct labeling simultaneously. Precision measures how often a positive prediction is actually correct but ignores false negatives, meaning a model that makes very few predictions can achieve high precision while missing most of the actual instances in underrepresented classes."},{"id":"aif-q277","question":"A bank reviews financial transaction records and assigns each one to either a personal or a business category, recording that category on the record. Which data preparation step does this describe?","options":{"A":"Data normalisation","B":"Data encoding","C":"Data balancing","D":"Data labeling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Data labeling is the step of attaching a meaningful category or tag to each record so it can serve as the target for Supervised Learning, which is exactly what assigning every transaction a personal or business category and storing it on the record achieves.\\nData encoding converts categorical values into a numeric form that algorithms can process, rather than creating the categories in the first place. Data normalisation rescales numeric features onto a common range so that no single feature dominates. Data balancing adjusts the proportion of classes in a dataset, for example through over- or under-sampling, so they are more evenly represented; none of these produces the category labels themselves."},{"id":"aif-q280","question":"An energy provider wants to monitor voltage levels across a power grid in rural regions. It plans to record voltage readings continuously and store them in Amazon RDS, then analyze how the readings fluctuate throughout each day to build an AI model that predicts potential power outages. Which type of data should the provider collect for this task?","options":{"A":"Tabular data","B":"Text data","C":"Time series data","D":"Audio data"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Time series data consists of values recorded sequentially over time, such as voltage readings taken at regular intervals, which is exactly what is needed to analyze fluctuations throughout the day and build a model that predicts power outages.\\nTabular data organizes information into rows and columns of features but does not inherently capture the time-ordered sequence required to model changes throughout the day. Text data represents unstructured written language such as documents or messages, which is not relevant to numerical voltage measurements. Audio data captures sound signals and has no application to monitoring electrical readings over time."},{"id":"aif-q281","question":"A manufacturer wants to train an ML model that flags unusual readings in its equipment sensor data, but it has no labeled examples to learn from. Which ML method meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Decision tree","B":"Autoencoders","C":"Linear regression","D":"Logistic regression"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Autoencoders are an unsupervised method that learn to reconstruct normal patterns in the data; readings that produce a large reconstruction error stand out as anomalies, so the technique can detect abnormal sensor behavior without any labeled training data.\\nDecision tree, linear regression, and logistic regression all fail for the same reason: they are supervised methods that require labeled examples to learn from, which the company does not have. Linear regression predicts continuous values and the other two perform classification, but none can be trained for anomaly detection on unlabeled data."},{"id":"aif-q286","question":"A music streaming app wants to suggest songs to each listener based on their listening history. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Personalize","B":"Amazon Transcribe","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Personalize is a fully managed ML service that delivers real-time personalized recommendations by analyzing each user\'s history, interactions, and behavior, making it purpose-built for use cases such as suggesting songs on a music streaming app.\\nAmazon Transcribe is an automatic speech recognition service that converts audio and speech into text transcripts and plays no role in analyzing listening history or producing recommendations. Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service that synthesises written text into lifelike speech and cannot generate recommendations. Amazon Comprehend is a Natural Language Processing (NLP) service that extracts insights such as sentiment, entities, and key phrases from text and is not designed to produce personalized recommendations."},{"id":"aif-q290","question":"A team is adopting MLOps to manage its machine learning workflows more reliably. Which two practices should the team prioritize?","options":{"A":"Model versioning for reproducibility","B":"Automated model testing and validation","C":"Manual-only deployment processes","D":"Minimal documentation","E":"No production monitoring"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Model versioning for reproducibility allows teams to track changes, reproduce results, and roll back to previous versions when needed, directly supporting the MLOps objective of repeatable processes. Automated model testing and validation verifies that a model performs correctly before deployment, supporting the MLOps objective of achieving production readiness. Together these practices help teams build scalable, reliable ML systems as defined in objective 1.3.5.\\nManual-only deployment processes contradict the MLOps goal of automating repeatable workflows to reduce errors and increase consistency. Minimal documentation undermines collaboration, auditability, and maintainability, all of which are important to sustainable ML operations and managing technical debt. No production monitoring is the opposite of good practice, as continuous monitoring is essential for detecting performance degradation and data drift over time, which is explicitly called out in the MLOps objectives."},{"id":"aif-q300","question":"In the context of AI, what does the term inference refer to?","options":{"A":"Gathering the training data used to build AI systems","B":"Designing brand-new AI algorithms from scratch","C":"Merging several AI models together into a single model","D":"Applying an already-trained model to new, unseen data to produce predictions or decisions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Inference is the stage where an already-trained model is applied to new, unseen data to generate predictions or decisions, drawing on the patterns it learned during training. It is distinct from training, when the model is still learning from data.\\nDesigning new AI algorithms is research and development work, not the act of running a finished model. Merging several models into one describes model ensembling or consolidation rather than producing predictions. Gathering training data is the data collection step that happens before training, well ahead of inference."},{"id":"aif-q304","question":"A public health agency has collected a year of patient records and wants to produce a monthly trend report for officials. Each month it needs to run a model across a large set of historical records at one scheduled time, with no requirement for immediate results. Which inference method meets this need most cost-effectively?","options":{"A":"Real-time inference","B":"Asynchronous inference","C":"Batch transform","D":"Serverless inference"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Batch transform is designed to run inference over a large dataset all at once as a scheduled, offline job, so the agency can process a whole month of records to generate the report without paying for a continuously running endpoint, which makes it the most cost-effective choice for this periodic bulk workload.\\nReal-time inference keeps an endpoint permanently available for low-latency individual predictions, which is unnecessary and more expensive for a scheduled bulk job. Serverless inference suits intermittent, unpredictable request traffic that still needs a quick response per request, not large scheduled batch processing. Asynchronous inference queues individual requests with large payloads or long processing times for near-real-time handling, but it still runs a hosted endpoint and is not intended for processing an entire dataset in one pass."},{"id":"aif-q306","question":"A bank is building an ML model to predict the likelihood that a loan applicant will default. The model uses applicant data such as income, credit score, employment length, and existing debt. The historical dataset includes a label indicating whether each past applicant ultimately defaulted. Which ML technique meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Unsupervised learning","B":"Supervised learning","C":"Reinforcement learning","D":"Semi-supervised learning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Supervised learning is the right technique when the dataset contains both input features, such as income, credit score, and existing debt, and a known label indicating the outcome, because the model learns to predict that labeled target from the example data.\\nUnsupervised learning works with unlabeled data to discover patterns or clusters, which does not fit a problem where a known default outcome is already provided for each record. Reinforcement learning trains an agent through rewards and penalties as it interacts with an environment, which does not apply to predicting an outcome from a fixed labeled dataset. Semi-supervised learning is used when only a small portion of the data is labeled and the rest is not, whereas here every historical record already has a known outcome, making fully supervised learning the appropriate choice."},{"id":"aif-q308","question":"A photo-sharing service needs to transpose and rotate large batches of images using simple numerical transformations. Which solution is MOST operationally efficient?","options":{"A":"Build a deep neural network that takes the images as input","B":"Use an Amazon Bedrock large language model (LLM) with a high temperature","C":"Create an AWS Lambda function to perform the transformations","D":"Use AWS Glue Data Quality to correct each image"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An AWS Lambda function can run straightforward numerical image operations such as transposing and rotating at scale without training or hosting any model, which is the most operationally efficient approach.\\nBuilding a deep neural network is unnecessary effort for deterministic transformations. An Amazon Bedrock LLM generates text and cannot transform images. AWS Glue Data Quality validates and cleans data, not rotate images."},{"id":"aif-q312","question":"Which statement best describes how Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) relate to each other?","options":{"A":"AI is a branch of ML","B":"AI and ML mean the same thing","C":"ML is a branch of AI","D":"AI and ML are unrelated fields"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Machine Learning is a branch of Artificial Intelligence. AI is the broad field of building systems that mimic human intelligence, and ML is one approach within it that lets systems learn patterns from data and improve without being explicitly programmed for every rule.\\nStating that AI is a branch of ML reverses the relationship and is incorrect. Saying the two are unrelated ignores that ML sits inside the wider field of AI. Claiming they mean the same thing wrongly equates the broad field with just one of its techniques, overlooking other AI approaches such as rule-based or expert systems."},{"id":"aif-q313","question":"Which of the following is NOT a type of machine learning?","options":{"A":"Supervised learning","B":"Unsupervised learning","C":"Reinforcement learning","D":"Diagnostic learning"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diagnostic learning is not a recognized type of machine learning and does not appear in the AI/ML literature or the AWS exam guide, making it the option that does not belong among the established ML learning paradigms.\\nSupervised learning, unsupervised learning, and reinforcement learning are the three primary types of machine learning. Supervised learning trains models on labeled datasets where each input is paired with a known correct output. Unsupervised learning discovers hidden patterns in unlabeled data without any predefined output values to learn from. Reinforcement learning trains an agent to make sequential decisions by rewarding desirable behaviors and penalising undesirable ones."},{"id":"aif-q314","question":"A team is building a Computer Vision model. Which type of data is the best fit for training it?","options":{"A":"Text data","B":"Image data","C":"Tabular data","D":"Time-series data"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Image data is the best fit because Computer Vision models learn from the visual features and pixel patterns in images (and video frames) to perform tasks such as object detection and image classification.\\nTabular data holds structured rows and columns and suits classic ML problems like regression. Time-series data captures values ordered over time, such as sensor or sales readings. Text data is the input for Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks. None of these carry the visual information a Computer Vision model needs."},{"id":"aif-q315","question":"A company wants to automatically analyze customer feedback written as text. Which AWS service is best suited for this Natural Language Processing (NLP) task?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker AI","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Comprehend is a managed Natural Language Processing service that analyzes text to extract sentiment, key phrases, entities, and language, making it the purpose-built choice for analyzing customer feedback.\\nAmazon SageMaker AI is a platform for building, training, and deploying custom ML models, which could perform NLP but requires significant development effort rather than providing ready-made NLP capabilities. Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service that converts written text into lifelike speech, which is the opposite of analyzing text. Amazon Transcribe is an automatic speech recognition service that converts spoken audio into text, and does not analyze the meaning or sentiment of that text."},{"id":"aif-q316","question":"During the ML development lifecycle, why does a data scientist perform exploratory data analysis (EDA) before building a model?","options":{"A":"To train the model","B":"To deploy the model","C":"To uncover the structure, quality, and relationships within the data","D":"To monitor the model in production"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exploratory data analysis is carried out early in the ML development lifecycle to uncover the structure, distribution, quality, and relationships within a dataset, which informs later decisions about data cleaning, feature engineering, and algorithm selection before any training begins.\\nTraining the model is a later stage that only happens after EDA and data preparation are complete. Deploying the model occurs after training and evaluation and is concerned with serving predictions, not with understanding the data. Monitoring the model in production is part of the final lifecycle stage, which focuses on detecting performance degradation and data drift once the model is already deployed."},{"id":"aif-q317","question":"Which of these would NOT normally be a stage in an ML pipeline?","options":{"A":"Model training","B":"Brand advertising","C":"Data collection","D":"Feature engineering"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Brand advertising is a marketing activity aimed at promoting a product and raising awareness, so it is not part of an ML pipeline, which is made up of technical stages for building, validating, and deploying models.\\nData collection, feature engineering, and model training are all genuine stages of a typical ML pipeline. Data collection gathers the relevant raw data from available sources. Feature engineering transforms that raw data into meaningful input variables to improve model performance. Model training fits a model to the prepared data by adjusting its parameters to minimize prediction error."},{"id":"aif-q318","question":"In the context of model performance metrics, what does the acronym AUC stand for?","options":{"A":"Area Under the Curve","B":"Average Usage Cost","C":"Augmented Use Case","D":"Automated Utility Calculation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AUC stands for Area Under the Curve, specifically the area beneath the ROC (Receiver Operating Characteristic) curve. It measures how well a classification model separates the classes and ranges from 0 to 1, where 1.0 indicates perfect separation and 0.5 means the model is no better than random guessing.\\nThe other choices are invented expansions that string together an A, U, and C word, but none of them is the recognized meaning of AUC as a model performance metric."},{"id":"aif-q319","question":"You have a large dataset in which every example is already labeled. Which type of machine learning is the best fit?","options":{"A":"Unsupervised learning","B":"Supervised learning","C":"Semi-supervised learning","D":"Reinforcement learning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Supervised learning trains on labeled examples, where each input is paired with its correct output, so the model learns the mapping from inputs to known targets. That makes it the right choice when you have a large, fully labeled dataset.\\nUnsupervised learning works with unlabeled data to uncover hidden structure or groupings, so it does not make use of the labels available here. Semi-supervised learning is intended for cases with a small amount of labeled data alongside a large amount of unlabeled data, not a fully labeled set. Reinforcement learning trains an agent through trial-and-error rewards from an environment and does not rely on a labeled dataset at all."},{"id":"aif-q320","question":"What is a key advantage of using pre-trained models?","options":{"A":"They always outperform custom-built models","B":"They are guaranteed to be more accurate for every task","C":"They can be used straight away without providing your own training data","D":"They remove the need to fine-tune for specialized tasks"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A pre-trained model has already been trained on a large dataset, so it can be applied straight away for inference without the user supplying their own training data, which saves the time, data, and effort of building a model from scratch and is its main advantage.\\nClaiming a pre-trained model always outperforms custom-built models or is guaranteed to be more accurate for every task are both overclaims, because a model built or fine-tuned for a specific task can easily beat a general pre-trained model. Saying it removes the need to fine-tune for specialized tasks is also false, since pre-trained models frequently must be fine-tuned to perform well on domain-specific work."},{"id":"aif-q321","question":"A data scientist wants to automatically find the optimal hyperparameters for a model instead of tuning them manually. Which AWS service is best suited for this task?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Autopilot","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Autopilot automatically builds, trains, and tunes machine learning models, including exploring and selecting hyperparameter configurations on the data scientist\'s behalf, which removes the need for manual hyperparameter experimentation.\\nAmazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service that extracts insights such as sentiment and entities from text, and has no role in model training or hyperparameter tuning. Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service that converts written text into lifelike speech, which is unrelated to building or tuning ML models. Amazon Transcribe is an automatic speech recognition service that converts audio into text, and likewise plays no part in hyperparameter optimization."},{"id":"aif-q322","question":"In a machine learning context, what is the term MLOps an abbreviation of?","options":{"A":"Machine Learning Optimization","B":"Managed Learning Outputs","C":"Machine Learning Operations","D":"Model Lifecycle Operations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MLOps stands for Machine Learning Operations, the set of practices that bring machine learning together with DevOps principles to automate and manage the deployment, monitoring, and ongoing maintenance of models in production.\\nThe remaining choices are invented expansions that string together plausible machine-learning-sounding words, but none of them is the recognized meaning of the acronym, so they are all incorrect."},{"id":"aif-q323","question":"Which of the following is NOT a typical business metric used to evaluate Machine Learning models?","options":{"A":"Return on investment (ROI)","B":"F1 score","C":"Customer satisfaction","D":"Time to market"},"correct":"B","explanation":"F1 score is a model performance metric, the harmonic mean of precision and recall, so it measures prediction quality rather than business value, which makes it the option that is not a business metric.\\nReturn on investment (ROI), customer satisfaction, and time to market are all business metrics that capture the real-world impact, value, and operational outcomes of deploying a model rather than its statistical accuracy."},{"id":"aif-q324","question":"Which of the following are considered business metrics for AI model evaluation?","options":{"A":"Precision score","B":"Customer satisfaction score","C":"Mean Average Precision","D":"Time-to-value","E":"Root Mean Square Error"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Customer satisfaction score measures how well an AI solution meets end user needs and directly reflects the value delivered to the business, while time-to-value measures how quickly the AI solution begins delivering measurable business outcomes, making both examples of business metrics used to assess whether an AI model is meeting organizational objectives.\\nPrecision score is a technical model performance metric that measures the proportion of positive predictions that are actually correct (true positives divided by all positive predictions), not a business outcome measure. Mean Average Precision is a technical evaluation metric used in information retrieval and object detection tasks to quantify how well a model ranks relevant results, measuring accuracy across ranked outputs rather than business value. Root Mean Square Error is a regression metric that measures the average magnitude of prediction errors by taking the square root of the mean of squared differences between predicted and actual values, again a technical performance measure rather than an indicator of business impact."},{"id":"aif-q325","question":"A company has rolled out an AI solution and wants to evaluate how successful it has been. Which two types of metrics should be included in the assessment?","options":{"A":"Improvements to business operations","B":"Model prediction accuracy","C":"Return on investment (ROI)","D":"Technical performance metrics only","E":"User satisfaction only"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Improvements to business operations and return on investment (ROI) both measure the real-world value an AI solution delivers, and together they complement technical evaluation to give a full picture of whether the solution has succeeded.\\nTechnical performance metrics only and user satisfaction only fail for the same reason: judging success on a single dimension ignores the wider business value the solution is meant to create. Model prediction accuracy is just one technical measure that is useful for monitoring model quality but says nothing on its own about whether the solution meets its business goals."},{"id":"aif-q326","question":"Which type of machine learning is most appropriate for training an agent to make decisions by interacting with its environment?","options":{"A":"Supervised learning","B":"Unsupervised learning","C":"Reinforcement learning","D":"Transfer learning"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Reinforcement learning trains an agent to make decisions by interacting with an environment, where it receives rewards for desirable actions and penalties for undesirable ones and gradually learns a strategy that maximizes cumulative reward.\\nSupervised learning trains a model on labeled examples with known correct outputs, rather than having an agent learn from the consequences of its own actions. Unsupervised learning finds patterns or clusters in unlabeled data and does not involve an agent, an environment, or a reward signal. Transfer learning reuses knowledge from a model trained on one task to improve performance on a related task, which describes a way of reusing model knowledge rather than learning through environmental interaction."},{"id":"aif-q327","question":"A company wants to generate spoken audio narration from its written articles. Which AWS service is the best fit?","options":{"A":"Amazon Transcribe","B":"Amazon Polly","C":"Amazon Translate","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service that turns written text into lifelike spoken audio, which is exactly what generating narration from articles requires.\\nAmazon Transcribe does the reverse, converting speech audio into text. Amazon Translate converts text from one language into another. Amazon Comprehend is a Natural Language Processing (NLP) service that extracts insights such as sentiment and entities from text, but it does not produce audio."},{"id":"aif-q328","question":"Within the ML development lifecycle, what is the main goal of feature engineering?","options":{"A":"To create new input variables or transform existing ones so the model performs better","B":"To gather additional raw data for the project","C":"To measure how well the trained model performs","D":"To release the model into a production environment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Feature engineering is the stage where raw data is turned into useful model inputs by creating new features or transforming existing ones, which improves the model\'s accuracy and overall performance.\\nGathering additional raw data describes the data collection stage, measuring how well the model performs describes model evaluation, and releasing the model into production describes deployment; each of these is a separate stage of the ML lifecycle rather than the purpose of feature engineering."},{"id":"aif-q330","question":"What is the main difference between batch inferencing and real-time inferencing?","options":{"A":"Batch inferencing is always more accurate","B":"Real-time inferencing can only be done on small datasets","C":"Batch inferencing processes multiple inputs at once, while real-time inferencing processes individual inputs as they arrive","D":"Real-time inferencing is always faster than batch inferencing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Batch inferencing collects multiple inputs and processes them together in a single job, making it well suited for non-time-sensitive workloads where throughput and efficiency are the priority. Real-time inferencing processes each input individually as it arrives and returns a prediction immediately, making it suitable for applications that require instant responses such as fraud detection or content moderation.\\nBatch inferencing is not always more accurate than real-time inferencing because accuracy is a property of the model and its training data, not of the inferencing method used to serve predictions. Real-time inferencing is not limited to small datasets as it processes individual inputs on demand regardless of the overall size of the data being served. Real-time inferencing is not always faster than batch inferencing because while it offers lower latency per individual request, batch inferencing can achieve higher overall throughput by processing many inputs simultaneously."},{"id":"aif-q331","question":"A team wants to use a single AWS service to build, train, deploy, and monitor machine learning models end to end. Which service is best suited for this?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon SageMaker AI","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Translate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker AI is a fully managed platform that supports the entire machine learning lifecycle, including data preparation, model training, hyperparameter tuning, deployment, and ongoing monitoring, making it the right choice for managing ML models end to end.\\nAmazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service for extracting insights such as sentiment and entities from text, not a platform for managing the full ML lifecycle. Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service that converts text into lifelike speech, which addresses a single AI capability rather than end-to-end model development. Amazon Translate provides language translation between languages and likewise serves one specific task rather than supporting the complete ML lifecycle."},{"id":"aif-q333","question":"Which of these is NOT a typical use case for AI or ML?","options":{"A":"Fraud detection","B":"Manual record keeping","C":"Speech recognition","D":"Recommendation systems"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Manual record keeping is not a typical AI/ML use case because it is a routine, deterministic clerical task that does not involve learning from data or making predictions.\\nFraud detection, recommendation systems, and speech recognition are all established AI/ML applications that rely on machine learning models to identify patterns, make predictions, or interpret unstructured data such as transactions, user behavior, or audio."},{"id":"aif-q345","question":"Which AWS services can be used for document analysis and information extraction?","options":{"A":"Amazon Textract","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Translate","E":"Amazon Transcribe"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Amazon Textract extracts text and structured data such as forms and tables directly from documents and images using optical character recognition, making it the primary service for the document analysis layer. Amazon Comprehend then applies natural language processing to extracted text to identify entities, key phrases, sentiment, and other insights, enabling information extraction from the content of documents.\\nAmazon Polly converts text to synthesised speech, Amazon Translate performs language translation between languages, and Amazon Transcribe converts spoken audio into text transcripts. None of these services are designed for document analysis or information extraction."},{"id":"aif-q016","question":"A telecom support team wants to study what customers talk about during recorded phone calls and pull out the important points. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Create classification labels with Amazon Comprehend","B":"Build a conversational chatbot with Amazon Lex","C":"Convert the call recordings to text with Amazon Transcribe","D":"Extract information from the recordings with Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Transcribe converts the speech in the call recordings into text, which the team can then analyze to pull out the key information discussed.\\nAmazon Comprehend analyzes text for insights such as classification, but it needs text rather than raw audio as input. Amazon Lex builds conversational chatbots and does not transcribe existing recordings. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor watches deployed models for drift and has nothing to do with audio transcription."},{"id":"aif-q116","question":"At which stage of the ML lifecycle are regulatory and compliance obligations primarily established?","options":{"A":"Model training","B":"Data collection","C":"Business goal identification","D":"Feature engineering"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Regulatory and compliance obligations are primarily established during business goal identification, because defining what you want to build determines which laws and regulations apply to the project before any data is collected or processed.\\nData collection is where those obligations are applied in practice, such as obtaining consent and verifying data sources, but the obligations themselves must be understood before collection begins. Model training and feature engineering are later technical stages focused on building and optimizing the model, not on identifying the legal and regulatory framework that governs the project."},{"id":"aif-q148","question":"A streaming service studies subscriber habits and demographics to suggest tailored shows. After putting its custom model into production, the team needs to watch whether the model\'s quality declines over time. Which AWS service or feature meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","D":"Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor continuously tracks a deployed model in production and detects drift and quality degradation over time, which is what the team needs to watch as subscriber behavior changes.\\nAmazon SageMaker Clarify focuses on bias and explainability, while Amazon Comprehend and Amazon Rekognition are managed services for text and image analysis, none of which monitor a deployed model for quality drift."},{"id":"aif-q166","question":"An organization runs many ML models in production and needs to know when any of them starts behaving differently from its baseline so the team can step in. Which AWS service or feature meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","B":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","C":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","D":"Amazon SageMaker HyperPod"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor continuously measures data and prediction quality for deployed models and alerts when they deviate from a baseline, letting the organization detect and resolve quality changes quickly.\\nAmazon SageMaker Data Wrangler prepares data, Amazon SageMaker JumpStart provides pre-built models, and Amazon SageMaker HyperPod provides distributed training infrastructure; none of these monitors deployed models for quality changes."},{"id":"aif-q230","question":"After producing a number of ML models, a team needs one place to keep them, manage them, and track each version. Which AWS service or feature meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","B":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Registry","D":"AWS Audit Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Registry is a central repository for storing, managing, and versioning ML models and tracking their deployment status across the lifecycle, which meets the team\'s needs.\\nAmazon SageMaker Model Monitor detects drift in deployed models, Amazon SageMaker Canvas builds models with a no-code interface, and AWS Audit Manager collects audit evidence; none of these is the service for model versioning and management."},{"id":"aif-q261","question":"While watching a predictive model with Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor, a team sees data drift cross the threshold it had set. The team wants to limit any harm this could cause the model. Which solution will meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Adjust the monitoring sensitivity","B":"Restart the SageMaker AI endpoint","C":"Re-train the model with fresh data","D":"Set up experiment tracking"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When data drift passes the defined threshold, the data no longer matches what the model was trained on, so re-training the model with fresh data restores its accuracy and limits the negative impact.\\nAdjusting the monitoring sensitivity only changes when alerts fire, restarting the endpoint does not address the changed data, and setting up experiment tracking records runs without correcting the drift."},{"id":"aif-q332","question":"Why do teams monitor models once they are running in production?","options":{"A":"To collect more data","B":"To detect issues such as model drift or data drift","C":"To perform feature engineering","D":"To train new models"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The primary purpose of model monitoring in production is to detect issues such as model drift or data drift so teams can act before performance degrades.\\nCollecting more data, performing feature engineering, and training new models are separate lifecycle activities, not the purpose of monitoring a deployed model."},{"id":"aif-q346","question":"Which task is Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor designed to perform?","options":{"A":"To serve models for inference","B":"To detect data drift and model performance degradation","C":"To create training datasets","D":"To train models faster"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor continuously watches deployed models and detects data drift and model performance degradation, alerting teams when investigation or retraining is needed.\\nServing models for inference, creating training datasets, and training models faster are handled by other capabilities and are not the purpose of Model Monitor."},{"id":"aif-q125","question":"An online store wants to tailor its recommendations to each individual shopper. Which AWS service fits?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Personalize","C":"Amazon Kendra","D":"Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Personalize builds machine learning models from user behavior to deliver individualized recommendations, which is exactly what tailoring results per shopper requires.\\nAmazon Comprehend analyzes text, Amazon Kendra provides enterprise search, and Amazon Rekognition analyzes images and video; none personalizes recommendations per user."},{"id":"aif-q150","question":"A company needs to produce its product descriptions in several languages automatically. Which AWS service does this?","options":{"A":"Amazon Polly","B":"Amazon Translate","C":"Amazon Comprehend","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Translate uses neural machine translation to convert text between languages automatically, which is what generating product descriptions in multiple languages requires.\\nAmazon Polly converts text to speech, Amazon Comprehend extracts insights from text, and Amazon Transcribe converts speech to text; none translates text."},{"id":"aif-q153","question":"A hospital wants an AI application that reads structured patient records, extracts the relevant clinical details, and produces concise summaries. Which solution fits?","options":{"A":"Use Amazon Personalize to model patient engagement and pass the output to a general model","B":"Use Amazon Comprehend Medical to extract medical entities and relationships, then apply rule-based logic to format the summaries","C":"Use Amazon Textract to digitize scanned documents, then run keyword extraction","D":"Use Amazon Kendra to index the records for search, then apply a template"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Comprehend Medical is purpose-built to extract medical entities and relationships from clinical text, and applying rule-based logic afterward formats the information into concise summaries, meeting both needs.\\nAmazon Personalize models engagement for recommendations, Amazon Textract digitizes documents without interpreting clinical meaning, and Amazon Kendra indexes content for search rather than extracting structured clinical entities."},{"id":"aif-q221","question":"A company has built custom Computer Vision models and needs an easy-to-use interface for labeling data to reduce errors on new, real-world images. Which AWS tool fits?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas","B":"Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","C":"Amazon Rekognition Custom Labels","D":"Amazon SageMaker Autopilot"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth provides a built-in labeling interface with workflows and quality controls for tasks such as Computer Vision, producing high-quality labeled data that reduces model errors.\\nSageMaker Canvas is a no-code interface for building models, Rekognition Custom Labels trains a model rather than offering a general labeling interface, and SageMaker Autopilot auto-builds models from tabular data."},{"id":"aif-q223","question":"A bank deployed an ML model to predict customer churn and wants to measure how accurately it predicts churn compared with actual behavior. Which metric fits?","options":{"A":"Root mean squared error (RMSE)","B":"F1 score","C":"Return on investment (ROI)","D":"Perplexity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The F1 score balances precision and recall, which makes it well suited to measuring accuracy on a binary classification task such as predicting whether a customer will churn.\\nRMSE measures error for regression predictions, ROI is a business-value metric, and perplexity measures language-modeling fit; none fits binary churn classification."},{"id":"aif-q245","question":"Which two capabilities make Amazon Kendra valuable for enterprise search applications?","options":{"A":"Managed relational database hosting","B":"Natural language understanding","C":"Video transcoding","D":"Semantic search","E":"Infrastructure monitoring"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Amazon Kendra provides natural language understanding that interprets user intent and semantic search that returns results by meaning and context, which together make it effective for enterprise search.\\nManaged relational database hosting, video transcoding, and infrastructure monitoring are unrelated tasks that Kendra does not perform."},{"id":"aif-q263","question":"A model\'s inference quality dropped after four months in production. The team wants alerts if quality degrades again and to prevent recurrence. Which solution fits?","options":{"A":"Build a new model and monitor drift with Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","B":"Retrain the model and monitor drift with Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","C":"Retrain the model and monitor drift with Amazon SageMaker Clarify","D":"Build a new model and watch it with Amazon CloudWatch alarms only"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor continuously detects data and model drift in production and can send alerts, so the team is notified and can retrain before quality degrades again.\\nSageMaker Clarify focuses on bias and explainability, SageMaker Feature Store stores features, and CloudWatch alarms track infrastructure metrics but do not detect model drift on their own."},{"id":"aif-q274","question":"An online learning company has large volumes of educational material and wants enterprise search across it. Which AWS service fits?","options":{"A":"Amazon Polly","B":"Amazon Kendra","C":"Amazon Textract","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Kendra is an intelligent enterprise search service that searches large volumes of unstructured content, such as educational materials, which fits this need.\\nAmazon Polly converts text to speech, Amazon Textract extracts text from documents, and Amazon Comprehend analyzes text for insights; none provides enterprise search."},{"id":"aif-q011","question":"A company is building an ML model with Amazon SageMaker and needs to share and manage variables used for model development across several teams. Which SageMaker feature meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","B":"Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","C":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","D":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Feature Store is a centralized repository for storing, sharing, and reusing features, which lets multiple teams manage the same variables for model development.\\nAmazon SageMaker Data Wrangler prepares and transforms data, Amazon SageMaker Clarify detects bias and supports explainability, and Amazon SageMaker Model Cards document model details. None of these is designed to share and manage features across teams."},{"id":"aif-q036","question":"A company deployed a deep learning model for object detection to production. When the model examines a new image to identify the objects in it, which AI process is taking place?","options":{"A":"Training","B":"Inference","C":"Bias correction","D":"Model deployment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inference is the process in which a deployed model analyzes new data, such as a new image, to produce a prediction, so identifying objects in an incoming image is inference.\\nTraining is the earlier process of fitting the model to data, model deployment is making the trained model available to serve requests, and bias correction adjusts for unfair outcomes. None of these describes analyzing a new image to make a prediction."},{"id":"aif-q147","question":"A company wants to find harmful language in the comments on social media posts and does not plan to use labeled data to train a model. Which approach should the company use?","options":{"A":"Use Amazon Rekognition moderation","B":"Use Amazon Comprehend toxicity detection","C":"Use Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithms to train a model","D":"Use Amazon Polly to monitor the comments"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Comprehend provides pre-trained natural language processing, including toxicity detection, so it can identify harmful language in comments without the company having to label data or train a model.\\nAmazon Rekognition moderates images and video, not text. Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithms would require labeled data to train a model. Amazon Polly converts text to speech and does not analyze text for harmful content."},{"id":"aif-q253","question":"A practitioner with limited ML experience wants to predict employee attrition without writing any code. Which Amazon SageMaker feature meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"SageMaker Canvas","B":"SageMaker Clarify","C":"SageMaker Model Monitor","D":"SageMaker Data Wrangler"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas offers a no-code visual interface that lets a user with limited ML experience build, train, and generate predictions, such as employee attrition, without writing code.\\nSageMaker Clarify detects bias and explains predictions, SageMaker Model Monitor tracks deployed models for drift, and SageMaker Data Wrangler prepares data. None of these provides a no-code way to build a predictive model."},{"id":"aif-q347","question":"A bank must provide regulators with a clear explanation of every factor that influenced each loan decision. The team is choosing between a gradient boosting model and a large language model. Which factor most strongly favors using the traditional ML model?","options":{"A":"Explainability requirements","B":"Multilingual support","C":"Content generation capability","D":"Conversational interface needs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Traditional ML models such as gradient boosting produce outputs that can be traced back to specific input features and their weights, making them well suited to regulated environments where every decision must be fully explainable to auditors and regulators.\\nMultilingual support and conversational interface needs are strengths of foundation models, not reasons to choose traditional ML. Content generation capability is a defining feature of generative AI and is irrelevant to a loan-decision model that classifies applications rather than generating text."},{"id":"aif-q348","question":"A healthcare startup wants to deploy a diagnostic support tool that must run on devices with limited memory and no internet connection. Which constraint most strongly favors a traditional ML model over a foundation model?","options":{"A":"The need for real-time image generation","B":"Operational constraints on memory and connectivity","C":"The requirement for multilingual chat support","D":"The desire for open-ended text summarization"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Traditional ML models are typically much smaller and can run efficiently on resource-constrained edge devices without requiring cloud connectivity, making them the appropriate choice when operational constraints such as limited memory and offline operation apply.\\nReal-time image generation, multilingual chat support, and open-ended text summarization are capabilities that favor foundation models, not constraints that would lead a team to choose traditional ML."},{"id":"aif-q349","question":"A logistics company processes thousands of delivery route requests each day. Which benefit does AI/ML provide that a purely rule-based system cannot easily achieve?","options":{"A":"Deterministic outputs for every input","B":"Ability to scale and adapt to changing patterns in the data","C":"Guaranteed correctness without validation","D":"Elimination of all computational costs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AI/ML systems learn patterns from data and can adapt as those patterns change over time, and they scale to handle large volumes of requests by generalizing from examples rather than requiring manually coded rules for every scenario, which is a key advantage over rule-based systems.\\nDeterministic outputs are actually a characteristic of rule-based systems, not a unique benefit of AI/ML. No system can guarantee correctness without validation. AI/ML does not eliminate computational costs; in fact, training and inference often require significant compute resources."},{"id":"aif-q350","question":"A retailer wants to personalize product recommendations for millions of customers. Why is AI/ML more suitable than a manual approach for this task?","options":{"A":"AI/ML removes the need for any data","B":"AI/ML can process large volumes of data and identify patterns humans cannot easily detect","C":"AI/ML guarantees 100% accuracy on every prediction","D":"AI/ML eliminates the need for computing infrastructure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AI/ML excels at processing large volumes of customer data, identifying subtle patterns and preferences, and generating personalized recommendations at a scale that would be impractical for humans to achieve manually, which is why it adds value for this use case.\\nAI/ML requires data to learn from, so it does not remove the need for any data. No system guarantees 100% accuracy. AI/ML requires computing infrastructure for training and inference, so it does not eliminate infrastructure needs."},{"id":"aif-q351","question":"A startup wants to use a foundation model for its application but lacks the data and resources to train one from scratch. Which approach allows the team to obtain a foundation model without pre-training it themselves?","options":{"A":"Use an open-source pre-trained model from a model hub","B":"Build a rule-based expert system instead","C":"Collect more labeled data and train a decision tree","D":"Write custom inference code without any model"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Open-source pre-trained models are available from model hubs and can be used directly or fine-tuned for specific tasks, allowing teams to leverage foundation models without the massive data and compute resources required to pre-train them from scratch.\\nBuilding a rule-based expert system does not provide a foundation model. Training a decision tree creates a traditional ML model, not a foundation model. Writing custom inference code without any model provides no predictive capability at all."},{"id":"aif-q352","question":"A company wants to experiment with multiple foundation models before selecting one for production. Which AWS service provides access to a variety of pre-trained foundation models from different providers?","options":{"A":"Amazon Personalize","B":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","C":"Amazon Comprehend","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart provides access to a variety of pre-trained foundation models from AWS and third-party providers, allowing teams to experiment with different models and deploy them with minimal setup, which directly addresses the company\'s need.\\nAmazon Personalize builds recommendation models from user interaction data rather than providing access to general-purpose foundation models. Amazon Comprehend is a managed NLP service for text analysis. Amazon Transcribe converts speech to text. None of these provides a catalog of foundation models from multiple providers."},{"id":"aif-q354","question":"An enterprise is modernizing legacy applications and wants an AWS service that helps transform and migrate workloads using AI-assisted tooling. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"AWS Transform","B":"Amazon Textract","C":"Amazon Translate","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Transform is an AWS service that uses AI-assisted tooling to help enterprises transform and modernize their legacy applications and workloads, which directly meets the company\'s requirement.\\nAmazon Textract extracts text and data from documents. Amazon Translate converts text between languages. Amazon Transcribe converts speech to text. None of these is designed for application modernization and transformation."},{"id":"aif-q355","question":"Which description best defines agentic AI?","options":{"A":"AI that generates images from text prompts","B":"AI that autonomously plans, decides, and takes actions to achieve goals","C":"AI that converts speech to text","D":"AI that groups similar data points into clusters"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Agentic AI refers to AI systems that can autonomously plan, make decisions, and take actions to accomplish goals, often by using tools, managing memory, and orchestrating multi-step workflows without constant human direction.\\nGenerating images from text prompts describes diffusion models or image-generation AI. Converting speech to text describes speech recognition. Grouping similar data points into clusters describes unsupervised learning techniques like K-means."},{"id":"aif-q356","question":"How does agentic AI differ from traditional AI systems?","options":{"A":"Agentic AI requires more training data than traditional AI","B":"Agentic AI can only process text, while traditional AI handles all data types","C":"Agentic AI can autonomously execute multi-step tasks, while traditional AI typically responds to single requests","D":"Agentic AI is always more accurate than traditional AI"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Agentic AI systems can autonomously break down goals into steps, use tools, and execute multi-step workflows to achieve objectives, whereas traditional AI systems typically process individual requests and return single responses without autonomous planning or action-taking.\\nAgentic AI does not inherently require more training data than traditional AI. Agentic AI can work with multiple data types, not just text. Accuracy depends on the specific model and task, not on whether the system is agentic."},{"id":"aif-q357","question":"A development team wants an AI assistant that can answer questions about their codebase, explain existing code, and help troubleshoot issues directly in their IDE. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Q Developer","C":"Amazon Textract","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Q Developer is an AI-powered assistant that integrates with IDEs to answer questions about code, explain how existing code works, and help developers troubleshoot issues, which matches what the team needs.\\nAmazon Comprehend analyzes text for sentiment and entities but does not assist with code. Amazon Textract extracts text from documents and images. Amazon Transcribe converts speech to text. None of these provides code-aware assistance in an IDE."},{"id":"aif-q358","question":"A software team wants to use AI to help define requirements, generate design documents, and implement features based on specifications. Which AWS service supports this spec-driven development workflow?","options":{"A":"Amazon Personalize","B":"Amazon Rekognition","C":"Kiro","D":"Amazon Polly"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Kiro is an AI-powered development environment that supports spec-driven development, helping teams define requirements, generate design documents, and implement features based on specifications.\\nAmazon Personalize generates recommendations based on user behavior. Amazon Rekognition analyzes images and video. Amazon Polly converts text to speech. None of these supports spec-driven software development workflows."},{"id":"aif-q359","question":"A business analyst wants to create interactive dashboards with AI-generated insights and natural language querying of data. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Athena","B":"Amazon Quick","C":"Amazon Redshift","D":"AWS Glue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Quick (formerly Amazon QuickSight Q) provides AI-powered business intelligence with natural language querying, allowing business analysts to ask questions in plain language and receive visualizations and insights from their data.\\nAmazon Athena runs SQL queries on data in Amazon S3 but does not provide AI-generated insights or natural language querying. Amazon Redshift is a data warehouse for analytics workloads. AWS Glue is a data integration service for ETL jobs. None of these offers the AI-powered natural language business intelligence experience."},{"id":"aif-q360","question":"A contact center wants to build a voice-enabled virtual agent that understands customer requests and routes calls to the appropriate department. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Polly","B":"Amazon Lex","C":"Amazon Translate","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Lex is a service for building conversational interfaces using voice and text, enabling the contact center to create a virtual agent that understands spoken requests and can route calls based on customer intent.\\nAmazon Polly converts text to speech but does not understand or interpret spoken input. Amazon Translate converts text between languages. Amazon Comprehend extracts insights from text but does not build interactive conversational agents."},{"id":"aif-q361","question":"A law firm has thousands of legal documents and wants employees to find relevant case law and contract clauses using natural language questions. Which type of AI application best addresses this need?","options":{"A":"Fraud Detection","B":"Knowledge Base","C":"Time Series Forecasting","D":"Anomaly Detection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A knowledge base application uses AI to index, understand, and retrieve information from large document collections, allowing users to find relevant content by asking natural language questions, which is exactly what the law firm needs for searching legal documents.\\nFraud detection identifies suspicious transactions or activities. Time series forecasting predicts future values from historical sequential data. Anomaly detection flags unusual patterns in data. None of these enables natural language search across document collections."},{"id":"aif-q362","question":"What does the term Large Language Model (LLM) refer to?","options":{"A":"A database that stores large amounts of text","B":"A deep learning model trained on vast text data to understand and generate human language","C":"A tool for translating between programming languages","D":"A system that converts speech to text"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Large Language Model (LLM) is a deep learning model trained on massive amounts of text data that can understand context, generate coherent text, answer questions, and perform various language tasks.\\nA database stores data but does not understand or generate language. A programming language translator converts code syntax, not natural language understanding. A speech-to-text system transcribes audio but is not what the term LLM describes."},{"id":"aif-q363","question":"Which statement best describes Generative AI?","options":{"A":"AI that only classifies data into predefined categories","B":"AI that creates new content such as text, images, or audio based on learned patterns","C":"AI that exclusively detects anomalies in datasets","D":"AI that only retrieves existing information from databases"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Generative AI refers to AI systems that can create new content, including text, images, audio, video, and code, by learning patterns from training data and generating novel outputs that resemble that data.\\nClassifying data into predefined categories describes classification models, not generative models. Detecting anomalies describes anomaly detection systems. Retrieving existing information describes search or retrieval systems. None of these involves creating new content."},{"id":"aif-q364","question":"A company wants an AI system that can autonomously research competitors, compile findings into a report, and email the summary to stakeholders without human intervention at each step. Which type of AI application best fits this requirement?","options":{"A":"Speech Recognition","B":"Image Classification","C":"Agentic AI","D":"Sentiment Analysis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Agentic AI can autonomously plan and execute multi-step workflows, such as researching information, compiling reports, and sending emails, without requiring human intervention at each step, which matches the company\'s requirement.\\nSpeech recognition converts spoken audio to text. Image classification assigns labels to images. Sentiment analysis determines the emotional tone of text. None of these can autonomously orchestrate multi-step tasks across different systems."},{"id":"aif-q365","question":"Which statement best describes how Deep Learning differs from traditional Machine Learning?","options":{"A":"Deep Learning requires manually engineered features, while traditional Machine Learning does not","B":"Deep Learning uses multi-layered neural networks that automatically learn feature representations from raw data","C":"Deep Learning can only be applied to text data","D":"Deep Learning always requires less data than traditional Machine Learning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Deep Learning uses neural networks with many layers that automatically discover useful feature representations directly from raw data, removing the need for manual feature engineering that traditional Machine Learning typically requires.\\nTraditional Machine Learning is the approach that usually depends on manually engineered features, so option A reverses the relationship. Deep Learning applies to images, audio, text, and other data types, not only text. Deep Learning generally requires more data than traditional Machine Learning because its multi-layered networks have many more parameters to train."},{"id":"aif-q366","question":"A retail chain wants to automatically detect and blur customer faces in its security camera footage before storing the recordings. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Textract","B":"Amazon Rekognition","C":"Amazon Comprehend","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Rekognition is a Computer Vision service that can detect, analyze, and identify faces in images and video, so it can locate customer faces in security footage for the company to blur before storage.\\nAmazon Textract extracts text and structured data from documents and images but does not detect faces. Amazon Comprehend is a Natural Language Processing service that analyzes text for sentiment, entities, and key phrases. Amazon Transcribe converts spoken audio into text. None of these provides facial detection in video."},{"id":"aif-q367","question":"Which of the following is an example of unstructured data?","options":{"A":"A database table of customer orders with columns for date, quantity, and price","B":"A spreadsheet listing employee IDs, names, and salaries","C":"A collection of free-form customer support emails","D":"A CSV file containing sensor readings indexed by timestamp"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Free-form customer support emails are unstructured data because they have no predefined schema or fixed format: the content varies in length, style, and layout, making them unsuitable for direct storage in rows and columns without processing.\\nA database table with defined columns, a spreadsheet with named fields, and a CSV file with consistent columns are all examples of structured data because each record follows a predefined schema where every field has a known type and position."},{"id":"aif-q368","question":"In the context of MLOps, what does managing technical debt refer to?","options":{"A":"Reducing the financial cost of cloud compute instances","B":"Addressing accumulated shortcuts, outdated dependencies, and undocumented processes that make ML systems harder to maintain over time","C":"Increasing the number of model parameters to improve accuracy","D":"Deleting old datasets that are no longer needed"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Technical debt in MLOps refers to the accumulated cost of shortcuts, poorly documented pipelines, outdated dependencies, and tightly coupled components that make ML systems progressively harder to maintain, extend, and debug over time. Managing it means proactively refactoring, documenting, and modernizing these systems.\\nReducing cloud compute costs is cost optimization, not technical debt management. Increasing model parameters is a modeling decision related to capacity, not maintenance burden. Deleting old datasets is a data governance activity rather than a response to accumulated engineering shortcuts."},{"id":"aif-q369","question":"A data science team runs dozens of training jobs with different configurations each week. It needs to compare results and reproduce any previous run. Which MLOps practice meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Model deployment","B":"Experiment tracking","C":"Data labeling","D":"Feature serving"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Experiment tracking records the parameters, metrics, code versions, and artifacts for every training run, allowing the team to compare results across runs and reproduce any previous experiment exactly, which supports the MLOps principles of experimentation and repeatable processes.\\nModel deployment is the process of making a trained model available for inference and does not record or compare training configurations. Data labeling assigns ground-truth annotations to training examples, not to tracking the outcomes of training jobs. Feature serving delivers pre-computed features to models at inference time and has nothing to do with recording training experiment details."},{"id":"aif-q370","question":"What is the purpose of hyperparameter tuning in the ML development lifecycle?","options":{"A":"To collect and clean the raw training data","B":"To find the model configuration settings that produce the best performance","C":"To deploy the trained model to a production endpoint","D":"To monitor the model for data drift after deployment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hyperparameter tuning searches for the optimal configuration settings, such as learning rate, batch size, and number of layers, that are set before training begins and directly influence model performance. Finding the best combination maximizes the model\'s accuracy or other target metric.\\nCollecting and cleaning raw data is the data collection and preprocessing stage. Deploying the model to a production endpoint is the deployment stage. Monitoring for data drift is part of the post-deployment monitoring stage. Each of these is a separate step in the ML lifecycle."},{"id":"aif-q371","question":"In Machine Learning, what does the term bias most commonly refer to?","options":{"A":"The speed at which a model trains","B":"A systematic error that causes a model to consistently favor certain outcomes over others","C":"The total number of parameters in a model","D":"The process of splitting data into training and test sets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In Machine Learning, bias refers to a systematic error where the model consistently skews toward certain predictions or outcomes, which can arise from unrepresentative training data, flawed assumptions in the algorithm, or imbalanced datasets. Identifying and reducing bias is essential for building fair and accurate models.\\nTraining speed is a measure of computational performance, not bias. The total number of parameters describes model capacity or complexity. Splitting data into training and test sets is a data preparation step called train-test splitting. None of these relates to the concept of systematic prediction error."},{"id":"aif-q109","question":"A team has trained a model to predict customer churn. The model is highly accurate on the training data but predicts poorly on new, unseen customers. Which change will resolve this?","options":{"A":"Reduce the regularization strength so the model can fit more complex patterns","B":"Add further features to the input dataset","C":"Continue training the model for more epochs","D":"Increase the regularization strength so the model becomes less complex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The model is overfitting: it has effectively memorised the training data and fails to generalise to new, unseen customers. Increasing the regularization strength penalises large weights and reduces model complexity, which encourages a simpler model that captures general patterns rather than noise, closing the gap between training and real-world performance.\\nReducing the regularization strength would increase model complexity and make the overfitting worse. Adding further input features also tends to increase complexity, again worsening generalisation. Continuing training for more epochs lets the model fit the training data even more closely, which deepens the overfitting rather than resolving it."},{"id":"aif-q044","question":"A retail company built a model to forecast product prices. It scored very well on the training dataset, but once it was released to production its accuracy dropped sharply. What should the company do to address this problem?","options":{"A":"Train the model for a longer period of time","B":"Increase the amount of data used for training","C":"Reduce the amount of data used for training","D":"Add more hyperparameters to the model"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Strong results on the training set paired with a sharp drop in production is the classic signature of overfitting, where the model has memorised the training examples instead of learning general patterns. Training on a larger and more varied dataset helps the model generalise to unseen data and closes the gap between training and production performance.\\nReducing the training data leaves the model even less to learn from and usually makes overfitting and poor generalisation worse. Adding more hyperparameters increases model complexity, which tends to deepen overfitting rather than relieve it. Training for longer simply fits the existing training data more tightly and does nothing to improve performance on new data, so it does not solve the generalisation problem."},{"id":"aif-q002","question":"A publisher needs to turn long research reports into short briefs that capture their main points, using a large language model (LLM). Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Build a recommendation engine","B":"Develop a summarization chatbot","C":"Build a named entity recognition system","D":"Create a multi-language translation system"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A summarization chatbot uses an LLM to read long documents and produce a shorter version that keeps the essential points, which is exactly what condensing research reports into briefs requires.\\nA recommendation engine suggests relevant items from user behavior and does not condense documents. A named entity recognition system only pulls out entities such as names, dates, and places rather than producing a coherent summary. A multi-language translation system converts text between languages without shortening or distilling the content."},{"id":"aif-q010","question":"A smart-home device maker wants its appliances to run language-model inference on the device itself, with the lowest latency possible. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Deploy optimized large language models (LLMs) on the edge devices","B":"Deploy optimized small language models (SLMs) on the edge devices","C":"Call a centralized small language model (SLM) API asynchronously from the edge devices","D":"Call a centralized large language model (LLM) API asynchronously from the edge devices"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Deploying optimized small language models (SLMs) on the edge devices keeps inference local and avoids network round-trips, which delivers the lowest possible latency, and SLMs are compact enough to run on constrained edge hardware.\\nDeploying LLMs on edge devices is impractical because their size and compute demands exceed typical edge hardware and would raise latency. Calling a centralized SLM or LLM API requires network communication, and the asynchronous round-trips add latency rather than minimizing it."},{"id":"aif-q012","question":"A fintech engineering team plans to adopt Amazon Q Developer to ship features faster. What can Amazon Q Developer do to help the team?","options":{"A":"Run an application without provisioning or managing servers","B":"Convert audio files into text documents by using ML models","C":"Generate code snippets and provide reference tracking and open source license tracking","D":"Enable voice commands for coding and natural-language search"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Q Developer boosts developer productivity by generating code snippets and providing reference tracking and open source license tracking.\\nRunning an application without provisioning or managing servers describes serverless compute such as AWS Lambda. Converting audio files into text describes Amazon Transcribe. Enabling voice commands for coding is not a function of Amazon Q Developer."},{"id":"aif-q018","question":"An ecommerce team is building visual search that accepts both a typed phrase and a product photo as the query. Which kind of foundation model (FM) should power it?","options":{"A":"Text embedding model","B":"Multimodal embedding model","C":"Image generation model","D":"Multimodal generation model"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A multimodal embedding model encodes both text and images into a shared vector space, so queries of either type can be compared and matched, which is what a search application handling text and image queries needs.\\nA text embedding model handles only text. An image generation model creates images from prompts rather than encoding queries for search. A multimodal generation model produces new content across modalities rather than the embeddings needed for similarity search."},{"id":"aif-q022","question":"A marketing agency is exploring generative AI. Which of these tasks is a generative AI use case?","options":{"A":"Improving network security by using an intrusion detection system","B":"Forecasting stock market trends from historical financial data","C":"Generating photorealistic images from text descriptions for a marketing campaign","D":"Speeding up database queries with optimized indexing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Generative AI creates new content, and producing photorealistic images from text descriptions for a marketing campaign is content generation, a classic generative AI use case.\\nIntrusion detection is a security and classification task. Forecasting stock trends from historical data is predictive analytics. Optimizing database indexing is performance tuning. None of these create new content, so they are not generative AI use cases."},{"id":"aif-q023","question":"While picking a foundation model (FM) on Amazon Bedrock, a research team needs to know how much text it can pack into a single prompt. Which model property tells them this?","options":{"A":"Temperature","B":"Model size","C":"Context window","D":"Batch size"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The context window defines how many tokens a foundation model can take in at once, so it determines how much information fits into a single prompt.\\nTemperature controls output randomness, not input capacity. Model size relates to parameter count and overall capability, not prompt length. Batch size concerns how many inputs are processed together, not how much text one prompt can hold."},{"id":"aif-q032","question":"A small studio wants to prototype on Amazon Bedrock on a tight budget and avoid any long-term commitment. Which Amazon Bedrock pricing model fits?","options":{"A":"Provisioned Throughput","B":"Model customization","C":"On-Demand","D":"Spot Instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The On-Demand pricing model charges only for what is used with no upfront or long-term commitment, which fits a limited budget that needs flexibility.\\nProvisioned Throughput requires committing to and paying for a fixed capacity level over time. Model customization is a fine-tuning activity, not a flexible pricing option. Spot Instances are an Amazon EC2 pricing concept and are not an Amazon Bedrock pricing model."},{"id":"aif-q033","question":"A data-science team wants to stand up a foundation model (FM) quickly inside its own VPC. Which AWS service or feature lets the team do that?","options":{"A":"Amazon Personalize","B":"PartyRock, an Amazon Bedrock Playground","C":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","D":"Amazon SageMaker AI endpoints"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart offers pre-built foundation models that a team can deploy quickly inside its own VPC and begin using right away, which matches the requirement.\\nAmazon Personalize is a recommendation service, not an FM-deployment feature. PartyRock is a public playground for experimenting with apps, not for deploying FMs in a VPC. Amazon SageMaker AI endpoints host models for inference, but they do not provide a curated catalog of pre-built foundation models ready to deploy with a few clicks, which is the value JumpStart adds."},{"id":"aif-q035","question":"A retail company keeps large volumes of sales data in a database. It wants an application that lets non-technical staff type a plain-language question and have it automatically turned into a SQL query. Which type of model is best suited to power this application?","options":{"A":"Residual neural network (ResNet)","B":"Support vector machine (SVM)","C":"WaveNet","D":"Generative pre-trained transformer (GPT)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Generative pre-trained transformers are large language models trained to generate text, and they can translate a natural-language request into a structured SQL query, which lets non-technical staff retrieve data without writing SQL themselves, exactly as the requirement describes.\\nA residual neural network (ResNet) is a deep architecture built for image recognition and computer vision, not language generation. A support vector machine (SVM) is a classic supervised algorithm for classification or regression and cannot generate SQL from text. WaveNet is a generative model for producing realistic audio and speech, so it is the wrong modality for converting text into queries."},{"id":"aif-q050","question":"A company summarizes contracts with an Amazon Bedrock base model and has now trained a custom model for better quality. What must the company do to use the custom model in Amazon Bedrock?","options":{"A":"Deploy the custom model to an Amazon SageMaker AI endpoint for real-time inference","B":"Register the model in the Amazon SageMaker Model Registry","C":"Request model access for the custom model in Amazon Bedrock","D":"Purchase Provisioned Throughput for the custom model"},"correct":"D","explanation":"To run a custom (fine-tuned) model in Amazon Bedrock, the company must purchase Provisioned Throughput for that model, which allocates the dedicated capacity Bedrock requires to serve custom models.\\nDeploying to a SageMaker AI endpoint or registering in the SageMaker Model Registry are Amazon SageMaker workflows, not how Bedrock serves a custom model. Requesting model access applies to Bedrock base models, not to using your own custom model."},{"id":"aif-q055","question":"A company is building an application that must generate synthetic data based on its existing data. Which type of model can the company use to meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"XGBoost","B":"WaveNet","C":"Generative adversarial network (GAN)","D":"Residual neural network"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A generative adversarial network (GAN) is built from two competing neural networks, a generator that creates synthetic samples resembling the real data and a discriminator that tries to tell real from generated, and through this competition it learns to produce realistic synthetic data based on an existing dataset, which is exactly what the company needs.\\nXGBoost is a gradient-boosting algorithm for supervised classification and regression, not for generating new data. A residual neural network (ResNet) is a deep convolutional architecture used mainly for image recognition and classification. WaveNet is a generative model, but it is specialized for synthesising raw audio such as speech rather than producing general synthetic data from an arbitrary existing dataset."},{"id":"aif-q063","question":"In AI and Natural Language Processing (NLP), what is the term for the numerical representations of real-world objects and concepts that help models better understand text?","options":{"A":"Tokens","B":"Parameters","C":"Embeddings","D":"Models"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Embeddings are numerical vector representations of objects and concepts such as words or phrases; by placing similar items close together in vector space they capture relationships and meaning, which helps AI and NLP models understand and work with text more effectively.\\nTokens are the individual units, such as words or sub-words, that text is split into before processing, not representations of meaning. A model is the trained system that makes predictions, not a representation of a concept. Parameters are the internal weights a model learns during training and do not represent real-world objects or concepts."},{"id":"aif-q074","question":"A retail analyst wants charts of top-product sales by store over the last year generated automatically. Which AWS solution fits this need?","options":{"A":"Amazon Q Developer","B":"Amazon Q in Amazon QuickSight","C":"Amazon Q in AWS Chatbot","D":"Amazon Q in Amazon EC2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Q in Amazon QuickSight lets users ask questions in natural language and automatically builds charts and visualizations, such as total sales for top products, which meets the requirement.\\nAmazon Q Developer assists with coding tasks. Amazon Q in AWS Chatbot helps operate AWS resources from chat channels. Amazon Q in Amazon EC2 provides EC2 guidance. None of these generate business data visualizations."},{"id":"aif-q080","question":"A travel company receives a flood of booking questions every day and plans to use Agents for Amazon Bedrock to keep up. What is a key benefit of using these agents?","options":{"A":"Generating custom foundation models (FMs) to predict customer needs","B":"Choosing a foundation model (FM) based on predefined criteria and metrics","C":"Automating repetitive tasks and orchestrating complex, multi-step workflows","D":"Calling several foundation models (FMs) at once and merging their results"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Agents for Amazon Bedrock automate repetitive tasks and orchestrate complex, multi-step workflows, which lets the company handle high volumes of inquiries faster and more efficiently.\\nAgents do not generate custom foundation models, they do not choose a model by predefined criteria, and their purpose is not to call multiple models and merge results. Their value is task automation and workflow orchestration."},{"id":"aif-q083","question":"A startup is budgeting for a generative AI app on Amazon Bedrock and wants to know what drives the cost of each inference from a large language model (LLM). Which factor is it?","options":{"A":"The temperature value used for generation","B":"The amount of data used to train the LLM","C":"The number of tokens consumed","D":"The total time spent training the LLM"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inference cost is driven by the number of tokens consumed, because LLM pricing charges for the input and output tokens processed by each request.\\nThe temperature value only shapes output randomness and has no cost impact. The amount of training data and the total training time affect the one-time cost of building the model, not the cost of running inference."},{"id":"aif-q085","question":"A company customized an Amazon Bedrock foundation model (FM) and now needs to upload a dataset for Bedrock to validate the model\'s answers. Which AWS service should hold that dataset?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)","B":"AWS Snowcone","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 is the scalable object storage service that integrates with Amazon Bedrock, so it is the right place to upload and store the validation dataset for Bedrock to use.\\nAmazon EBS provides block storage for EC2 volumes, Amazon EFS provides shared file storage for instances, and AWS Snowcone is an edge data-transfer device. None of these is the storage service that Bedrock reads datasets from."},{"id":"aif-q099","question":"A logistics company\'s analysts have little coding experience but need to build data integration jobs in AWS Glue. Which AWS service can help them?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon Q Developer","D":"Amazon Personalize"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Q Developer is a generative AI coding assistant that can generate and explain code, helping users with limited programming experience build and work with AWS Glue jobs.\\nAWS Config tracks resource configuration and compliance. Amazon Personalize provides recommendations. Amazon Comprehend performs NLP tasks such as sentiment and entity analysis. None of these helps write or simplify AWS Glue code."},{"id":"aif-q105","question":"A development team wants a large language model (LLM) to write code from plain-language comments. Which LLM capability does this rely on?","options":{"A":"Text summarization","B":"Text classification","C":"Text generation","D":"Text completion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Text generation is the LLM capability that produces new text, in this case code, from a given input such as natural-language comments, which is exactly what the requirement needs.\\nText summarization condenses existing text. Text classification assigns labels to text. Text completion only continues a given snippet rather than generating full code from descriptive comments."},{"id":"aif-q112","question":"A pharmacy chain wants a short overview of each new medication based on its customer reviews. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Build a time-series forecasting model to analyze the reviews with Amazon Personalize","B":"Build a classification model that sorts medications into groups with Amazon Comprehend","C":"Generate medication review summaries with Amazon Bedrock large language models (LLMs)","D":"Generate medication review summaries with Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock provides large language models well suited to summarization, so generating concise summaries of medication reviews with Bedrock LLMs meets the requirement.\\nA time-series forecasting model with Amazon Personalize predicts future values and is not for summarizing text. A classification model with Amazon Comprehend sorts items into categories rather than producing a written overview. Amazon Rekognition analyzes images and video, not text reviews."},{"id":"aif-q129","question":"A developer wants a service that offers foundation models (FMs) for building and scaling generative AI applications. Which AWS service provides this?","options":{"A":"Amazon Q Developer","B":"Amazon Kendra","C":"Amazon Bedrock","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock gives access to foundation models from AWS and third parties through a managed service, so users can build and scale generative AI applications without managing infrastructure.\\nAmazon Q Developer is a coding assistant. Amazon Kendra is an intelligent enterprise search service. Amazon Comprehend is an NLP service. None of these provides foundation models for building generative AI apps."},{"id":"aif-q134","question":"A subscription box company runs a generative AI support chatbot and wants to measure its financial effect on operations. Which metric should the company track?","options":{"A":"Number of customer inquiries handled","B":"Cost per customer conversation","C":"Average handle time (AHT)","D":"Cost of training the AI models"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cost per customer conversation directly quantifies the operational expense of using the chatbot, so the company can measure its financial effect and compare it to alternatives such as human agents.\\nThe number of inquiries handled measures volume, not cost. Average handle time measures efficiency and speed. The cost of training the models is a one-time build cost, not the ongoing operational cost of conversations."},{"id":"aif-q137","question":"An insurer\'s support chatbot on Amazon Bedrock needs several turns to resolve a claim. How can the large language model (LLM) use content from the customer\'s earlier messages?","options":{"A":"Turn on model invocation logging to collect the messages","B":"Use Amazon Personalize to store the conversation history","C":"Include the previous messages in the model prompt","D":"Use Provisioned Throughput for the LLM"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Including the previous messages in the model prompt gives the LLM the conversation history it needs, so it can keep context across turns and respond coherently.\\nModel invocation logging records requests for monitoring but does not feed history back into the model. Amazon Personalize is a recommendation service, not a way to supply prompt context. Provisioned Throughput sets capacity and does not add conversation context."},{"id":"aif-q138","question":"A call-center team manually gives customers location-based product tips and wants foundation models (FMs) to automate it. Which AWS service meets this need?","options":{"A":"Amazon Macie","B":"Amazon Bedrock","C":"Amazon Textract","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock lets the company use foundation models to generate product descriptions and location-based recommendations without managing infrastructure, which automates the task.\\nAmazon Macie discovers and protects sensitive data. Amazon Textract extracts text from documents. Amazon Transcribe converts speech to text. None of these provides foundation models to generate recommendations."},{"id":"aif-q152","question":"An investment firm uses ML across its operations. Which of these is a generative AI use case?","options":{"A":"Classifying customers by product usage","B":"Forecasting revenue for certain products","C":"Summarizing customer complaints","D":"Segmenting customers by type of investment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Summarizing customer complaints generates new, condensed text from longer input, which is a generative AI task typically handled by large language models.\\nClassifying customers by product usage and forecasting revenue are predictive tasks, and segmenting customers by investment type is clustering. These are traditional ML tasks that do not generate new content."},{"id":"aif-q158","question":"Which statement describes a defining characteristic of transformer-based language models?","options":{"A":"They use convolutional layers to apply filters across the input and capture local patterns","B":"They use self-attention mechanisms to capture contextual relationships","C":"They can process only text data","D":"They process a sequence one element at a time in repeated cycles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Transformer-based models use self-attention mechanisms that weigh the importance of each token relative to the others, which lets them capture contextual relationships across an entire input.\\nConvolutional layers are characteristic of CNNs, not transformers. Transformers are not limited to text and can be multimodal. Processing one element at a time in cycles describes recurrent networks (RNNs), not the parallel self-attention of transformers."},{"id":"aif-q168","question":"A newsroom is piloting a generative AI writing aide. Pilot traffic is light, performance is not a concern, and future usage is unpredictable; the newsroom wants the lowest cost. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Use GPU-powered Amazon EC2 instances","B":"Use Amazon Bedrock with On-Demand pricing","C":"Use Amazon Bedrock with Provisioned Throughput","D":"Use Amazon SageMaker JumpStart"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock with On-Demand pricing charges only for the inference calls actually made, with no capacity commitment, so it keeps costs low during a small pilot and scales elastically with unpredictable future demand.\\nGPU-powered EC2 instances incur ongoing costs whether or not they are used. Provisioned Throughput requires paying for committed capacity that low, unpredictable usage would waste. Amazon SageMaker JumpStart still requires provisioning and managing hosting capacity, which is not the most cost-effective for this case."},{"id":"aif-q174","question":"A company fine-tuned a custom large language model (LLM) from Amazon Bedrock and will serve a steady, predictable number of requests per minute in production. Which option is MOST cost-effective?","options":{"A":"Deploy the model on an Amazon EC2 compute optimized instance","B":"Purchase Provisioned Throughput for the model on Amazon Bedrock","C":"Use the model with On-Demand throughput on Amazon Bedrock","D":"Store the model in Amazon S3 and host it with AWS Lambda"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For steady, predictable request volume, Provisioned Throughput on Amazon Bedrock offers a lower per-request cost by committing to a fixed throughput level, making it the most cost-effective way to serve a custom model.\\nAn EC2 compute optimized instance means self-managing infrastructure and is not how Bedrock serves custom models. On-Demand throughput costs more per request at steady high volume. Storing the model in Amazon S3 and hosting it with AWS Lambda is not a supported way to serve a Bedrock custom model."},{"id":"aif-q375","question":"A medical clinic wants a generative AI tool with speech-to-text that helps doctors dictate and create clinical notes. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Q Developer","B":"Amazon Polly","C":"AWS HealthScribe","D":"Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS HealthScribe is purpose-built for healthcare and combines speech recognition with generative AI to transcribe clinical conversations and produce structured clinical notes, which meets the clinic\'s need.\\nAmazon Q Developer is a coding assistant. Amazon Polly performs text-to-speech, the opposite direction. Amazon Rekognition analyzes images and video, not clinical speech."},{"id":"aif-q184","question":"In a large language model (LLM), what do vector embeddings make possible?","options":{"A":"Splitting text into manageable pieces of data","B":"Comparing texts mathematically","C":"Grouping a set of characters to be treated as a single unit","D":"Counting every word in the input"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vector embeddings convert text into numerical vectors that capture meaning, so texts can be compared mathematically by measuring distance or similarity, which supports tasks like retrieval and clustering.\\nSplitting text into manageable pieces describes chunking. Grouping characters into a single unit describes tokenization. Counting every word is a simple word-frequency count, not an embedding."},{"id":"aif-q197","question":"An edtech company is building an app where students type a question or snap a photo of one, and the app returns a written answer with an explanation. Which type of model should power the app?","options":{"A":"Computer Vision model","B":"Diffusion model","C":"Large multimodal language model","D":"Text-to-speech model"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A large multimodal language model can take both text and images as input and generate text output, so it can accept a typed or photographed question and return a written answer with an explanation.\\nA Computer Vision model analyzes images but does not generate explanatory text answers. A diffusion model generates images, not text answers. A text-to-speech model converts text into audio, which does not produce written answers from mixed inputs."},{"id":"aif-q199","question":"Which statement correctly describes embeddings in generative AI?","options":{"A":"Embeddings search data to find the most helpful information for answering natural-language questions","B":"Embeddings lower hardware requirements by using a less precise data type for weights and activations","C":"Embeddings represent data as high-dimensional vectors that capture semantic relationships","D":"Embeddings store and retrieve data for generative AI applications"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Embeddings map inputs such as words or documents into high-dimensional vectors where semantic similarity corresponds to closeness in the vector space, letting models reason about meaning and relationships.\\nSearching data to find helpful information describes retrieval, as in RAG. Reducing hardware needs with a less precise data type describes quantization. Storing and retrieving data describes a database or vector store, not embeddings themselves."},{"id":"aif-q201","question":"A company needs a generative AI model for an app that must answer users in real time. Which model characteristic matters most?","options":{"A":"Model complexity","B":"Inference speed","C":"Training time","D":"Innovation speed"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inference speed determines how quickly the model generates a response, so it is the key characteristic for a real-time application where users need answers with low latency.\\nModel complexity affects capability and cost but not directly the real-time requirement. Training time is a one-time build concern. Innovation speed describes how fast a model family evolves, none of which governs real-time response latency."},{"id":"aif-q202","question":"What is the term for the instructions you give a foundation model (FM) to help it return a more accurate answer?","options":{"A":"Direction","B":"Dialog","C":"Prompt","D":"Translation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A prompt is the input instruction or query provided to a foundation model that guides what it should do, helping it produce a more accurate response.\\nDirection and dialog are not the term for the model\'s input instruction. Translation is a task type, not the instruction itself."},{"id":"aif-q242","question":"A company wants an AI assistant that lets employees ask questions about internal company data. Which AWS service meets this need?","options":{"A":"Amazon Rekognition","B":"Amazon Textract","C":"Amazon Q Business","D":"Amazon Lex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Q Business builds generative AI assistants that connect to internal data sources, so employees can ask questions and get answers, which meets the requirement.\\nAmazon Rekognition analyzes images and video. Amazon Textract extracts text from documents. Amazon Lex builds conversational chatbots but is not designed as an enterprise assistant over internal company data."},{"id":"aif-q250","question":"A team wants a low-cost, hands-on way to experiment with and learn about generative AI applications. Which solution is MOST cost-effective?","options":{"A":"Amazon Q Developer","B":"Amazon Bedrock PartyRock","C":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","D":"Amazon Q Business"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock PartyRock provides a low-cost, hands-on playground for building and experimenting with generative AI applications, which fits learning in an experimental environment cost-effectively.\\nAmazon Q Developer is a coding assistant. Amazon SageMaker JumpStart requires provisioning and managing model hosting. Amazon Q Business is an enterprise assistant over company data. None of these is a low-cost experimentation playground for learning about generative AI apps."},{"id":"aif-q275","question":"A studio that produces video content wants to use generative AI to create new clips and cut production time. Which solution meets these requirements MOST operationally efficiently?","options":{"A":"Use the Amazon Titan Image Generator model on Amazon Bedrock to create intermediate images, then assemble videos with video editing software","B":"Use the Amazon Nova Reel model on Amazon Bedrock to generate videos","C":"Use the Amazon Nova Canvas model on Amazon Bedrock to create intermediate images, then assemble videos with video editing software","D":"Use the Amazon Nova Pro model on Amazon Bedrock to generate videos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Amazon Nova Reel model on Amazon Bedrock is built for generative video creation, so it lets the company generate new video content directly and reduce production time in the most operationally efficient way.\\nUsing Amazon Titan Image Generator or Amazon Nova Canvas produces only still images that then require manual editing into videos, which adds steps. Amazon Nova Pro is a general multimodal model and is not the purpose-built video-generation option."},{"id":"aif-q278","question":"A compliance team wants to pull the key insights out of long policy documents so staff can work faster. Which generative AI strategy meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Regression","B":"Summarization","C":"Clustering","D":"Classification"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Summarization condenses large documents into their key insights, so employees can quickly grasp and act on the essential information.\\nRegression predicts numeric values, clustering groups similar items, and classification assigns labels. None of these extracts and condenses the key insights from a document."},{"id":"aif-q282","question":"An online store uses a generative AI assistant on Amazon Bedrock to recommend products and wants to measure its direct impact on sales. Which metric meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"The number of customer interactions with the AI assistant","B":"Sentiment analysis scores from customer feedback after interactions","C":"The conversion rate of customers who purchase after interacting with the AI assistant","D":"Natural language understanding accuracy rates"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Conversion rate, the share of customers who buy after interacting with the assistant, ties assistant engagement directly to sales outcomes, so it measures the direct impact on sales.\\nThe number of interactions measures usage volume, not sales. Sentiment scores measure customer mood. Natural language understanding accuracy measures technical quality. None of these directly measures sales impact."},{"id":"aif-q284","question":"Which scenario is a practical use case for generative AI?","options":{"A":"Using an ML model to forecast product demand","B":"Tracking website traffic and user behavior with an analytics dashboard","C":"Using a chatbot to give human-like responses to customer queries in real time","D":"Using a rule-based recommendation engine to suggest products"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A chatbot that gives human-like responses to customer queries in real time uses a generative model to create relevant, natural-language answers, which is a practical generative AI use case.\\nForecasting product demand with an ML model is predictive analytics. An analytics dashboard tracks metrics. A rule-based recommendation engine follows fixed rules. None of these generates new content."},{"id":"aif-q293","question":"What is the difference between discriminative and generative AI models?","options":{"A":"Discriminative models are faster than generative models","B":"Discriminative models are more accurate than generative models","C":"Discriminative models classify or predict, while generative models create new content","D":"Discriminative models need more data than generative models"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Discriminative models learn to classify inputs or predict outcomes, whereas generative models learn to create new content similar to their training data; this is the core distinction between the two.\\nClaims that discriminative models are inherently faster, more accurate, or need more data are not the defining difference and are not generally true; the real distinction is what each type of model produces."},{"id":"aif-q295","question":"What is the primary purpose of Amazon Q Business?","options":{"A":"To train custom ML models","B":"To store large datasets","C":"To provide AI-powered business assistance over company data","D":"To monitor infrastructure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Q Business is a generative AI assistant for the enterprise; its primary purpose is to provide AI-powered assistance over a company\'s internal data, answering questions, generating content, and summarizing information while respecting access controls.\\nTraining custom ML models is the role of Amazon SageMaker AI. Storing large datasets is the role of services like Amazon S3. Monitoring infrastructure is the role of services like Amazon CloudWatch. None of these is the purpose of Amazon Q Business."},{"id":"aif-q298","question":"A company is comparing Amazon Nova models on Amazon Bedrock and needs a multimodal model that supports several languages. Which Nova model meets these requirements MOST cost-effectively?","options":{"A":"Nova Pro","B":"Nova Lite","C":"Nova Canvas","D":"Nova Reel"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Nova Lite is a low-cost multimodal model that supports multiple languages, so it meets the multimodal and multilingual requirements as the most cost-effective Nova option.\\nAmazon Nova Pro is also multimodal but is a higher-cost, higher-capability model. Amazon Nova Canvas generates images and Amazon Nova Reel generates video, so these single-purpose generation models are not cost-effective multilingual multimodal choices."},{"id":"aif-q303","question":"An edtech company is building a reading app that lets students add illustrations to their stories. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use Amazon Polly to create an audiobook from the story text","B":"Use Amazon Rekognition to analyze image content and detect text attributes","C":"Use Amazon Bedrock Stable Diffusion 3.5 Large to generate images from text inputs","D":"Create a standard prompt template and use Amazon Q Business to illustrate the stories"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Stable Diffusion 3.5 Large generates images from text prompts, so students can create illustrations for their stories from descriptions, which supports reading comprehension with visuals.\\nAmazon Polly converts text to speech, producing audio rather than illustrations. Amazon Rekognition analyzes existing images rather than generating new ones. Amazon Q Business is an enterprise assistant over company data and does not generate story illustrations."},{"id":"aif-q309","question":"A developer wants to quickly create a test case and documentation for some code with the LEAST effort. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Upload the code to an online coding assistant","B":"Use Amazon Q Developer in an integrated development environment (IDE)","C":"Build an application that uses foundation models (FMs)","D":"Research and write the test cases by hand, then add the documentation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Q Developer in an IDE can automatically generate test cases and documentation for code, which meets the requirement with the least effort.\\nUploading code to an online coding assistant adds manual steps and data-handling concerns. Building a custom application on foundation models is far more effort. Researching and writing everything by hand is the most effort of all."},{"id":"aif-q310","question":"An ecommerce company needs to generate thousands of unique product descriptions a day, written as paragraphs with a consistent style and tone. Which type of generative model meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"A variational autoencoder (VAE) model","B":"A diffusion model","C":"A transformer-based model","D":"A generative adversarial network (GAN) model"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Transformer-based models, such as large language models, generate coherent paragraphs of text with consistent style and tone and scale to producing thousands of unique descriptions, which fits the requirement.\\nVariational autoencoders and generative adversarial networks are used mainly to generate images or other data, not fluent long-form text. Diffusion models generate images, so none of these is suited to producing styled product-description text at scale."},{"id":"aif-q334","question":"What is a token in the context of generative AI?","options":{"A":"A security feature","B":"A type of neural network","C":"A unit of text processed by the model","D":"A model evaluation metric"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A token is the basic unit of text, such as a word or sub-word, that a model processes and generates, which is what the term refers to.\\nA token is not a security feature, not a type of neural network, and not a model evaluation metric; those describe unrelated concepts."},{"id":"aif-q336","question":"What is a primary advantage of the adaptability of generative AI?","options":{"A":"It can work only with structured data","B":"It always produces perfect results","C":"It can handle a wide range of tasks and domains","D":"It removes the need for any human oversight"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adaptability means a generative model can be applied to a wide range of tasks and domains, which is its key advantage.\\nGenerative AI is not limited to structured data, it does not always produce perfect results, and it does not remove the need for human oversight; these claims misstate its capabilities."},{"id":"aif-q338","question":"Which AWS service is designed specifically for developing generative AI applications?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon Bedrock","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon RDS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock is purpose-built for generative AI, providing access to foundation models and tools to build and scale generative AI applications.\\nAmazon EC2 provides general compute, Amazon S3 provides object storage, and Amazon RDS provides managed relational databases; none is specifically designed for developing generative AI applications."},{"id":"aif-q339","question":"What is a foundation model in generative AI?","options":{"A":"A model that can only generate text","B":"A model designed specifically for image generation","C":"A large, pre-trained model that can be adapted for many tasks","D":"A model that requires no training data"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A foundation model is a large model pre-trained on vast amounts of data that can be adapted or fine-tuned for many downstream tasks such as text generation, summarization, and question answering.\\nA foundation model is not limited to text and is not built only for image generation, and it is trained on large amounts of data, so the claim that it needs no training data is incorrect."},{"id":"aif-q341","question":"What is a primary advantage of using AWS generative AI services to build applications?","options":{"A":"They are always free","B":"They guarantee 100% accuracy","C":"They lower the barrier to entry","D":"They remove the need for any coding"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS generative AI services provide managed infrastructure, pre-trained foundation models, and APIs, which lower the barrier to entry by removing the need to build and train large models from scratch.\\nThese services are not always free, they do not guarantee perfect accuracy, and they do not remove the need for any coding; those statements overstate what the services provide."},{"id":"aif-q082","question":"In generative AI, what are tokens?","options":{"A":"The vector representations of words or concepts","B":"The basic units of input and output, such as words or subwords","C":"The instructions or prompts given to the model","D":"The hidden layers of the neural network"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tokens are the smallest units, such as words, subwords, or characters, that a generative model reads and produces, forming the basis of its input and output.\\nVector representations describe embeddings, prompts are the instructions given to the model, and hidden layers are part of the model\'s architecture; none of these defines a token."},{"id":"aif-q193","question":"Which two statements about a model\'s latent space are correct?","options":{"A":"It must be stored in a specific type of database","B":"It captures the model\'s internal understanding of relationships among concepts","C":"It applies only to image data","D":"It enables computing semantic similarity between different inputs","E":"It is a human-readable lookup table"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"A model\'s latent space is the internal representation where it organizes concepts, so it both captures the relationships the model has learned and lets it compute semantic similarity between different inputs.\\nLatent space is not limited to images, does not require a specific database type, and is not a human-readable lookup table."},{"id":"aif-q198","question":"In the generative AI model lifecycle, which stage runs tests to check the model\'s accuracy?","options":{"A":"Pre-training","B":"Fine-tuning","C":"Evaluation","D":"Deployment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The evaluation stage tests the model against validation or hold-out data using metrics such as accuracy before it moves to deployment.\\nPre-training builds the base model, fine-tuning adapts it to a task, and deployment puts the finished model into production; none of these is where accuracy testing happens."},{"id":"aif-q289","question":"What is the primary benefit of Amazon SageMaker JumpStart?","options":{"A":"It reduces inference costs","B":"It provides access to pre-trained models and solutions","C":"It automatically tunes model hyperparameters","D":"It manages data labeling with a human workforce"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart provides access to pre-trained models, built-in algorithms, and end-to-end solutions, which speeds up the start of machine learning and generative AI projects.\\nReducing inference cost, hyperparameter tuning, and managed human labeling are handled by other capabilities, such as SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning and SageMaker Ground Truth Plus."},{"id":"aif-q340","question":"Which of the following is NOT a stage of the foundation model (FM) lifecycle?","options":{"A":"Fine-tuning","B":"Marketing","C":"Pre-training","D":"Deployment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Marketing is a business activity and is not part of the technical FM lifecycle, which covers stages such as data selection, pre-training, fine-tuning, evaluation, and deployment.\\nFine-tuning, pre-training, and deployment are all genuine lifecycle stages, so none of them is the correct answer to what is NOT a stage."},{"id":"aif-q342","question":"In generative AI, what is prompt engineering?","options":{"A":"A method for compressing models to run faster","B":"The process of crafting effective input prompts to guide a model\'s outputs","C":"A technique for designing a model\'s neural network architecture","D":"The process of labeling training data for supervised learning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Prompt engineering is the practice of designing and refining input prompts, for example by adding context, constraints, or examples, to guide a generative model toward accurate, relevant outputs.\\nIt is not model compression, not the design of a neural network architecture, and not the labeling of training data."},{"id":"aif-q077","question":"An AI practitioner uses a large language model to write marketing copy. The output sounds plausible and factual but is actually incorrect. Which problem is the model exhibiting?","options":{"A":"Data leakage","B":"Hallucination","C":"Overfitting","D":"Underfitting"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hallucination is when a large language model produces content that sounds plausible and factual but is actually incorrect or fabricated, which matches the marketing copy described.\\nData leakage is the unintended exposure of information, overfitting is when a model memorizes training data and generalizes poorly, and underfitting is when a model is too simple to capture patterns. None of these describes confidently stated but false output."},{"id":"aif-q180","question":"A company has an application that ingests consumer complaints from public sources and processes them with complex hard-coded rules. The company wants to scale this logic across new markets and product lines. Which advantage of generative AI models applies to this scenario?","options":{"A":"Predictable outputs","B":"Adaptability","C":"Reduced sensitivity to input changes","D":"Explainability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Adaptability is the generative AI advantage that applies here: the models can generalize learned patterns to new markets and product lines with little manual reconfiguration, avoiding the need to rewrite complex hard-coded rules.\\nPredictable outputs and reduced sensitivity to input changes are not strengths of generative AI, which is nondeterministic and sensitive to input wording. Explainability is unrelated to scaling the complaint-processing logic."},{"id":"aif-q302","question":"An AI practitioner notices that a large language model returns different responses each time it receives the same input. Which risk of AI does this describe?","options":{"A":"Hallucinations","B":"Nondeterminism","C":"Accuracy","D":"Multimodality"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nondeterminism is the property by which a large language model can return different responses to the same input because of randomness in how it generates text, which matches the observed behavior.\\nHallucinations are fabricated but plausible outputs, accuracy concerns correctness, and multimodality is the ability to work across data types such as text and images. None of these describes varying responses to identical inputs."},{"id":"aif-q337","question":"In the context of generative AI, what is a hallucination?","options":{"A":"A visual output produced by the model","B":"A type of model architecture","C":"An incorrect or fabricated output presented as fact","D":"A method of training a model"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A hallucination is an output that is incorrect or fabricated but is presented by the model as if it were factual.\\nA hallucination is not a visual output, a type of model architecture, or a method of training a model. These options describe unrelated concepts."},{"id":"aif-q343","question":"Which of the following is a potential disadvantage of generative AI solutions?","options":{"A":"Adaptability","B":"Responsiveness","C":"Inaccuracy","D":"Simplicity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inaccuracy is a known disadvantage of generative AI, which can produce incorrect, biased, or fabricated information that requires validation and human oversight.\\nAdaptability and responsiveness are advantages of generative AI, and simplicity is not a disadvantage. None of these represents a drawback of generative AI solutions."},{"id":"aif-q353","question":"A media company wants to use an AWS-developed foundation model to generate marketing copy and images. Which AWS service provides first-party foundation models built by Amazon?","options":{"A":"Amazon Rekognition","B":"Amazon Nova","C":"Amazon Comprehend","D":"Amazon Polly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Nova is a family of foundation models developed by AWS that can generate text, images, and other content, making it the appropriate choice when a company wants to use first-party Amazon-built foundation models for content generation.\\nAmazon Rekognition analyzes images and video for object detection and facial recognition but does not generate content. Amazon Comprehend extracts insights such as sentiment and entities from text. Amazon Polly converts text to speech. None of these is a generative foundation model built by Amazon."},{"id":"aif-q376","question":"A development team is building a customer support assistant on Amazon Bedrock. The assistant must remember account details from earlier in the conversation, reference relevant help articles, and follow company guidelines. Which concept describes managing all of this information that is fed to the foundation model?","options":{"A":"Prompt engineering","B":"Context engineering","C":"Model distillation","D":"Continuous pre-training"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Context engineering is the practice of managing the full set of information provided to a foundation model at inference time, including conversation history, retrieved documents, system instructions, and any other relevant data, so the model produces accurate, grounded responses that account for all necessary context.\\nPrompt engineering focuses specifically on crafting the instruction or query text itself, whereas context engineering encompasses the broader strategy of assembling all relevant information the model needs. Model distillation compresses a larger model into a smaller one for efficiency. Continuous pre-training updates a model\'s knowledge by training on new data over time, not by managing runtime context."},{"id":"aif-q377","question":"A legal firm\'s AI assistant on Amazon Bedrock must combine the user\'s question, relevant contract clauses retrieved from a knowledge base, the firm\'s compliance rules, and the conversation history into a single model call. Which practice governs how these elements are assembled and prioritized?","options":{"A":"Fine-tuning","B":"Hyperparameter tuning","C":"Context engineering","D":"Feature engineering"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Context engineering governs how all the information elements, such as retrieved documents, system rules, user input, and conversation history, are assembled, prioritized, and structured before being passed to the model, ensuring it has the right context to answer accurately.\\nFine-tuning updates model weights with new training data and does not control what is passed at inference time. Hyperparameter tuning adjusts training configuration settings like learning rate and batch size. Feature engineering transforms raw data into model inputs during the ML training pipeline, not at inference time for a foundation model."},{"id":"aif-q378","question":"What is the role of Model Context Protocol (MCP) in agentic AI systems?","options":{"A":"It compresses models to reduce inference latency","B":"It provides a standard interface for connecting AI agents to external tools and data sources","C":"It encrypts model weights during training","D":"It measures model accuracy on benchmark datasets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Model Context Protocol (MCP) provides a standardized interface that allows AI agents to connect to external tools, APIs, and data sources in a consistent way, enabling them to retrieve information and take actions across different systems without custom integrations for each one.\\nModel compression for reduced latency describes techniques like quantization or distillation. Encrypting model weights is a security practice handled by services like AWS KMS. Measuring accuracy on benchmarks is model evaluation. None of these describes a protocol for agent-to-tool connectivity."},{"id":"aif-q379","question":"A financial services company wants multiple specialized AI agents to collaborate on complex customer requests. One agent handles account queries, another processes transactions, and a third checks compliance. Which agentic AI concept does this architecture represent?","options":{"A":"Single-agent reinforcement learning","B":"Multi-agent system pattern","C":"Supervised classification pipeline","D":"Batch inference workflow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A multi-agent system pattern uses multiple specialized AI agents that communicate and collaborate to handle complex tasks, with each agent responsible for a specific capability such as account queries, transactions, or compliance checks, which matches the architecture described.\\nSingle-agent reinforcement learning trains one agent through environmental rewards without collaboration between multiple agents. A supervised classification pipeline assigns labels to inputs and does not involve agent collaboration. A batch inference workflow processes data in bulk offline and has no concept of multiple agents working together."},{"id":"aif-q380","question":"An AI agent assists a healthcare researcher over multiple sessions spanning several days. The agent needs to recall findings from previous sessions to avoid repeating work. Which agentic AI capability enables this?","options":{"A":"Tool usage","B":"Memory management","C":"Workflow orchestration","D":"Model pre-training"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Memory management in agentic AI allows an agent to persist and retrieve information across sessions, so it can recall previous findings, avoid redundant work, and build on past interactions over time.\\nTool usage enables agents to call external APIs and services but does not handle storing and retrieving past interaction history. Workflow orchestration coordinates the sequence of steps an agent takes but does not address long-term memory. Model pre-training builds the base model\'s knowledge during initial training, not the ability to remember individual user sessions."},{"id":"aif-q381","question":"A startup wants to build custom AI agents using an open-source Python SDK that supports tool use, multi-agent orchestration, and integration with multiple model providers. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Personalize","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Strands Agents","D":"Amazon Lex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Strands Agents is an open-source Python SDK from AWS that lets developers build AI agents with tool use, multi-agent orchestration, and flexibility to work with different model providers, which matches what the startup needs.\\nAmazon Personalize generates recommendations from user behavior data. Amazon Comprehend performs Natural Language Processing tasks like sentiment analysis. Amazon Lex builds conversational chatbots and voice interfaces. None of these provides an open-source SDK for building custom AI agents with multi-agent orchestration."},{"id":"aif-q382","question":"An enterprise wants managed infrastructure to deploy, scale, and monitor its AI agents in production without handling the underlying compute and networking. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore","B":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas","C":"Amazon Kendra","D":"Amazon Polly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore provides managed infrastructure for deploying, scaling, and monitoring AI agents in production, handling the compute, networking, and operational concerns so the enterprise can focus on agent logic rather than infrastructure management.\\nAmazon SageMaker Canvas is a no-code interface for building ML models. Amazon Kendra provides intelligent enterprise search. Amazon Polly converts text to speech. None of these provides managed infrastructure specifically for running AI agents in production."},{"id":"aif-q383","question":"A travel agency builds an AI agent that can search flight availability, book tickets, and process payments by calling the airline\'s APIs. Which agentic AI capability allows the agent to interact with these external systems?","options":{"A":"Memory management","B":"Tool usage","C":"Model fine-tuning","D":"Prompt caching"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tool usage is the agentic AI capability that allows an agent to call external APIs, services, and systems to retrieve information or take actions, which is how the travel agent searches flights, books tickets, and processes payments through the airline\'s APIs.\\nMemory management stores and retrieves information across interactions but does not call external systems. Model fine-tuning updates model weights during training rather than enabling runtime API calls. Prompt caching stores frequently used prompts for efficiency but does not interact with external systems."},{"id":"aif-q437","question":"A company uses Amazon Bedrock for a customer support chatbot and notices costs increasing. The team discovers that full conversation histories are included in every prompt. Which aspect of the token-based pricing model explains this?","options":{"A":"Cost is based on the number of API calls regardless of content","B":"Cost increases with the total number of input and output tokens processed per request","C":"Cost is determined by the time the model spends processing each request","D":"Cost depends on the number of concurrent users"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Token-based pricing charges for the total number of input and output tokens processed in each request, so including long conversation histories increases the input token count on every call and directly drives up costs.\\nAmazon Bedrock does not charge a flat fee per API call regardless of content. Pricing is not based on processing time or concurrent user count; it is based on token volume."},{"id":"aif-q438","question":"How does reducing the maximum output token limit affect cost and performance when using a foundation model?","options":{"A":"It increases cost but has no effect on response time","B":"It decreases cost and can reduce response time","C":"It has no effect on cost or response time","D":"It increases response time but decreases cost"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Reducing the maximum output token limit means fewer tokens are generated per request, which directly lowers cost under token-based pricing and can also reduce response time because the model produces less output.\\nFewer output tokens means less to pay for, so cost does not increase. Output tokens directly affect both billing and generation time, so there is an effect. Generating fewer tokens is faster, not slower."},{"id":"aif-q439","question":"A startup with no ML infrastructure wants to launch a generative AI feature quickly. Which advantage of using AWS GenAI services like Amazon Bedrock is most relevant?","options":{"A":"The startup must train its own foundation model from scratch","B":"AWS GenAI services lower the barrier to entry by providing managed access to pre-trained foundation models","C":"AWS GenAI services require the startup to manage GPU clusters","D":"AWS GenAI services are only available to enterprise customers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS GenAI services like Amazon Bedrock provide managed access to pre-trained foundation models through APIs, which lowers the barrier to entry by removing the need to train models, manage infrastructure, or procure GPUs, letting the startup launch quickly.\\nThe startup does not need to train its own model because pre-trained models are available. AWS manages the infrastructure so the startup does not manage GPU clusters. These services are available to all customers, not only enterprises."},{"id":"aif-q440","question":"A healthcare company is evaluating whether to build its generative AI application on AWS. Which benefit of AWS infrastructure is most important for this regulated industry?","options":{"A":"AWS provides the lowest possible inference latency globally","B":"AWS infrastructure provides built-in security, compliance certifications, and data protection controls","C":"AWS guarantees that AI models never produce incorrect outputs","D":"AWS eliminates the need for any data governance policies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS infrastructure provides security controls, compliance certifications (such as HIPAA eligibility), and data protection features that regulated industries like healthcare require to deploy AI applications responsibly and meet legal obligations.\\nNo provider guarantees the lowest latency globally in all cases. No AI system can guarantee it never produces incorrect outputs. AWS does not eliminate the need for data governance; organizations must still implement their own policies."},{"id":"aif-q441","question":"A retail company deployed a generative AI chatbot six months ago and wants to measure its business value. Which metric best indicates the chatbot\'s impact on revenue?","options":{"A":"Model parameter count","B":"Average revenue per user","C":"Number of training epochs","D":"Inference latency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Average revenue per user measures the financial value generated from each customer, so tracking whether it increases after deploying the chatbot directly indicates the chatbot\'s revenue impact.\\nModel parameter count describes model size. Number of training epochs is a training configuration detail. Inference latency measures response speed. None of these reflects revenue impact."},{"id":"aif-q442","question":"A company uses a generative AI assistant to help its support team and wants to measure operational efficiency gains. Which metric should it track?","options":{"A":"ROUGE score","B":"Efficiency measured as reduction in average handle time per case","C":"BERTScore","D":"Model perplexity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Reduction in average handle time per case directly measures how much faster agents resolve issues with the AI assistant\'s help, which is an operational efficiency gain and a business value metric for GenAI applications.\\nROUGE and BERTScore measure generated text quality against references. Model perplexity measures language-modeling fit. None of these captures operational efficiency."},{"id":"aif-q443","question":"Which stage of the foundation model lifecycle involves selecting the right data sources and preparing them before any model training begins?","options":{"A":"Deployment","B":"Evaluation","C":"Data selection","D":"Feedback"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Data selection is the first stage of the foundation model lifecycle, where appropriate data sources are identified and curated before pre-training begins. The quality and breadth of selected data directly shapes what the model learns.\\nDeployment is a later stage where the trained model is made available for inference. Evaluation assesses model performance after training. Feedback is the final stage where user interactions inform improvements."},{"id":"aif-q450","question":"A company invested in a generative AI solution to automate report writing. Which metric best measures whether this investment delivered financial value?","options":{"A":"Return on investment (ROI)","B":"Model accuracy","C":"Number of model parameters","D":"Training data volume"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Return on investment (ROI) measures whether the financial benefits of the AI solution, such as labor savings and faster output, exceed the costs of building and running it, which directly shows whether the investment delivered financial value.\\nModel accuracy measures prediction quality but not financial return. Number of parameters and training data volume are technical details unrelated to business value."},{"id":"aif-q005","question":"A travel company uses a pre-trained large language model (LLM) to power a booking assistant. The company needs the assistant\'s replies to be brief and written in a particular language. Which approach will best align the LLM\'s output with these expectations?","options":{"A":"Choose an LLM of a different size","B":"Increase the temperature value","C":"Refine the prompt","D":"Increase the Top K value"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Refining the prompt lets the company state the required response length and target language directly, so the model returns brief replies in the chosen language.\\nChoosing a different model size changes the model\'s capacity and cost rather than enforcing length or language. Increasing the temperature adds randomness, making the output less controlled. Increasing the Top K value widens the pool of candidate tokens, which also does not control response length or language."},{"id":"aif-q008","question":"A manufacturer needs a high-accuracy labeled image dataset for a defect-detection model and wants to keep mislabeled examples to a minimum. Which approach best meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Automated annotation with Amazon Rekognition Custom Labels","B":"Human-in-the-loop validation with Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth Plus","C":"Synthetic image generation with an Amazon Bedrock foundation model","D":"Automated active-learning labeling with Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Human-in-the-loop validation with Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth Plus routes labels through expert reviewers who verify and correct them, producing the highest-accuracy annotations with the fewest mistakes.\\nAmazon Rekognition Custom Labels automates labeling but, without human review, can propagate errors; generating synthetic images with an Amazon Bedrock model creates data yet does nothing to validate the labels; SageMaker Ground Truth\'s automated active-learning labeling cuts effort but raises the chance of incorrect annotations, the opposite of the goal."},{"id":"aif-q019","question":"A company uses a foundation model (FM) from Amazon Bedrock for a search tool. The company wants to fine-tune the FM on its own data to make the tool more accurate. Which approach will correctly fine-tune the model?","options":{"A":"Purchase Provisioned Throughput for Amazon Bedrock","B":"Supply labeled examples that each contain a prompt field and a completion field","C":"Prepare the dataset as a .txt file that holds multiple lines of .csv content","D":"Train the model on journals and textbooks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fine-tuning a model on Amazon Bedrock requires a labeled dataset in which each record pairs an input prompt with the expected completion, so the model learns the desired input-to-output behavior from the company data.\\nProvisioned Throughput is a capacity and pricing option for inference, not a fine-tuning method. A .txt file containing .csv lines is not the required fine-tuning format. Training on journals and textbooks is unstructured content that lacks the prompt-and-completion pairs that fine-tuning needs."},{"id":"aif-q021","question":"An engineering team wants to use Amazon OpenSearch Service as the backbone of a vector search application. Which capability makes this possible?","options":{"A":"Full-text keyword (BM25) search and ranking","B":"Geospatial indexing and queries","C":"k-NN nearest neighbor search over high-dimensional vectors","D":"Cross-cluster replication for disaster recovery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"k-NN nearest neighbor search lets Amazon OpenSearch Service index embeddings and return the vectors most similar to a query, which is the core of a vector database application such as semantic search.\\nBM25 full-text search matches keywords rather than semantic vectors; geospatial indexing handles location data; cross-cluster replication supports availability and disaster recovery. None provides vector similarity search."},{"id":"aif-q024","question":"A company is building a self-service support chatbot on a foundation model (FM). The chatbot\'s responses must match the company\'s brand tone. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Iterate on and refine the prompt until the FM produces responses in the desired style","B":"Set a low limit on the number of tokens the FM can produce","C":"Use batch inference to generate detailed responses","D":"Set a higher temperature value"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Iteratively refining the prompt steers the foundation model toward responses that match the company brand tone, because the prompt instructions and examples shape the style of the output.\\nLimiting output tokens only shortens responses. Batch inference changes how requests are processed, not their tone. Raising the temperature increases randomness, which makes tone less consistent rather than more aligned."},{"id":"aif-q025","question":"A company wants to use a large language model (LLM) on Amazon Bedrock to label text passages as positive or negative. Which prompt engineering strategy meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Add a detailed explanation of sentiment analysis and how LLMs work to the prompt","B":"Provide only the new passage to classify, with no examples or additional context","C":"Include a few example passages, each labeled positive or negative, in the prompt","D":"Include the new passage along with a few examples of unrelated tasks such as summarization or translation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Including a few example passages, each labeled positive or negative, is few-shot prompting, which shows the model the input-to-label pattern and improves classification accuracy on a new passage.\\nAdding an explanation of how sentiment analysis or LLMs work provides theory but no examples to learn from. Providing only the passage with no examples is zero-shot and gives the model less guidance. Examples of unrelated tasks such as summarization or translation steer the model away from sentiment classification."},{"id":"aif-q029","question":"A startup is launching an LLM-powered virtual agent and wants to stop users from coaxing it into unsafe actions or leaking its hidden instructions. Which action most directly reduces this risk?","options":{"A":"Increase the model\'s temperature so responses vary more","B":"Raise the Top P value to widen token sampling","C":"Add system instructions in a prompt template that teach the model to recognize and refuse manipulation attempts","D":"Reduce the maximum output length on each request"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A prompt template with system instructions that teach the model to spot and refuse manipulation patterns is a prompt-engineering safeguard that directly lowers the risk of prompt injection and instruction leakage.\\nRaising temperature or Top P only changes how random the sampling is, and shortening the maximum output length just truncates responses; none of these counters prompt manipulation."},{"id":"aif-q038","question":"A logistics company runs an Amazon Titan model on Amazon Bedrock and wants the model\'s answers to draw on its internal shipping documents. Which solution adds this private context?","options":{"A":"Fine-tune the FM on the internal documents","B":"Create an Amazon Bedrock knowledge base over the internal documents","C":"Lower the temperature value","D":"Switch to a larger foundation model"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An Amazon Bedrock knowledge base indexes the internal documents and retrieves the relevant passages at query time (RAG), so the Titan model grounds its answers in the company\'s private data without any training.\\nFine-tuning can embed knowledge but is costlier and static, so it is not the targeted way to supplement answers with frequently used documents; lowering the temperature only reduces randomness; switching to a larger model adds capability but still gives it no access to the private documents."},{"id":"aif-q046","question":"An appliance maker wants a chat interface over its PDF product manuals using large language models (LLMs) on Amazon Bedrock, at the lowest cost. Which approach fits best?","options":{"A":"Add all the manuals to every prompt as context","B":"Upload the manuals to an Amazon Bedrock knowledge base and retrieve only the relevant passages per query","C":"Fine-tune a model on the manuals and serve the fine-tuned model","D":"Continuously pre-train the base model on the manual content"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A knowledge base retrieves just the passages relevant to each question, so the model gets the right context cheaply, without paying to send whole manuals every time or to train the model.\\nPasting all manuals into every prompt inflates token costs; fine-tuning adds training cost and must be redone when manuals change; continuous pre-training on the corpus is the most expensive option and unnecessary for a Q&A interface."},{"id":"aif-q064","question":"A biotech firm\'s chatbot answers questions from dense clinical research papers, but even after several prompt tweaks the model handles the specialized terminology poorly. What will best improve it?","options":{"A":"Use few-shot prompting with sample question-and-answer pairs","B":"Apply domain adaptation fine-tuning on the specialized corpus","C":"Add a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) step over the papers","D":"Lower the temperature to make answers more focused"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Domain adaptation fine-tuning trains the model on the specialized corpus so it actually learns the clinical terminology, which is what fixes poor comprehension of dense domain language.\\nFew-shot prompting and a lower temperature shape responses but do not teach new vocabulary; a RAG step retrieves relevant passages, yet the model still struggles to interpret the specialized terms it has not learned."},{"id":"aif-q069","question":"An online learning company runs a question-answering assistant and wants the answer style to match each learner\'s age range, which the app already passes to the model. Which approach needs the LEAST effort?","options":{"A":"Train a separate fine-tuned model for each age range","B":"Build a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline of age-specific content","C":"Post-process every response with a second model to adjust the tone","D":"Add a role and audience instruction to the prompt that states the user\'s age range"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Adding a role and audience instruction to the prompt is a simple prompt-engineering change that steers the style to the stated age range with almost no extra work.\\nFine-tuning a model per age range, building a RAG pipeline of age-specific content, and running a second model to rewrite tone all add significant data, infrastructure, or processing effort."},{"id":"aif-q076","question":"A team sends a daily few-shot prompt with 10 examples to a base model on Amazon Bedrock. The model performs well, but the team wants to cut the monthly cost. Which change helps?","options":{"A":"Purchase Provisioned Throughput for the model","B":"Reduce the number of examples and tokens in the prompt","C":"Fine-tune the model so the examples are no longer needed in the prompt","D":"Move the workload to a larger, more capable model"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because cost scales with the tokens processed, trimming the number of examples and tokens in the prompt lowers the bill while the model keeps performing well.\\nProvisioned Throughput is meant for high, steady volume and would cost more for a once-a-day job; fine-tuning removes in-prompt examples but adds training cost; moving to a larger model increases the per-call cost."},{"id":"aif-q079","question":"A travel company localizes its help articles into several languages with an LLM and scores each machine translation against a human reference. Which metric is purpose-built for machine translation?","options":{"A":"BERTScore","B":"Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation (ROUGE)","C":"Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU)","D":"Word error rate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"BLEU is designed for machine translation, measuring n-gram overlap between the candidate translation and one or more human references, which is exactly the comparison described.\\nROUGE targets summarization recall; BERTScore measures general semantic similarity rather than serving as the standard translation metric; word error rate evaluates speech transcription."},{"id":"aif-q081","question":"What is a benefit of continuously pre-training a foundation model (FM)?","options":{"A":"It adapts the model to a specific labeled task dataset","B":"It keeps improving the model\'s knowledge by training it further on new data over time","C":"It compresses the model to shrink its size and speed up inference","D":"It removes the need for an evaluation step"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Continuous pre-training keeps exposing the FM to fresh, mostly unlabeled data, so its knowledge stays current and its performance improves over time.\\nAdapting to a labeled task dataset describes fine-tuning; shrinking and speeding up the model describes distillation or quantization; and no training method removes the need to evaluate the model."},{"id":"aif-q086","question":"Which prompt-based attack is specifically aimed at revealing a model\'s hidden system instructions?","options":{"A":"Jailbreaking","B":"Prompt injection","C":"Extracting the prompt template","D":"Prompted persona switching"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Extracting (leaking) the prompt template coaxes the model into disclosing its underlying system prompt, directly exposing the hidden instructions that configure its behavior.\\nJailbreaking bypasses safety controls, prompt injection inserts adversarial instructions to hijack behavior, and persona switching pushes the model into a different role; none of these is aimed at dumping the system instructions themselves."},{"id":"aif-q088","question":"A social platform wants to compare the output toxicity of several candidate LLMs from Amazon SageMaker JumpStart with the LEAST operational effort. Which evaluation approach fits?","options":{"A":"Model evaluation with a human review workforce","B":"Automatic model evaluation using built-in toxicity metrics","C":"Crowd-sourced human rating","D":"A/B testing the models with live users"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Automatic model evaluation scores outputs with built-in toxicity metrics and needs no people to recruit or coordinate, giving the lowest operational effort.\\nHuman-workforce evaluation and crowd-sourced rating both require managing reviewers, and A/B testing with live users adds exposure risk and coordination overhead."},{"id":"aif-q095","question":"Which prompting approach is used to harden a model against prompt injection attacks?","options":{"A":"Chain-of-thought prompting","B":"Few-shot prompting","C":"Adversarial prompting","D":"Zero-shot prompting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adversarial prompting deliberately crafts and tests hostile inputs so injection attempts can be detected and neutralized, hardening the model against them.\\nChain-of-thought, few-shot, and zero-shot prompting shape how the model reasons or learns from examples but offer no defense against malicious prompts."},{"id":"aif-q096","question":"An IT support team fine-tuned an LLM to answer help desk questions and wants to confirm the fine-tuning improved its accuracy. Which metric should it use?","options":{"A":"Precision","B":"F1 score","C":"Time to first token","D":"Perplexity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The F1 score balances precision and recall in one number, so it reflects how accurately and completely the model answers, making it the right way to measure the fine-tuning gain.\\nPrecision alone ignores missed correct answers; time to first token measures latency; perplexity measures language-modeling fit rather than task accuracy."},{"id":"aif-q097","question":"A company uses Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) with Amazon Bedrock and Stable Diffusion to create product images, but the results are random and miss requested details. The team wants the images to follow the prompts more closely. Which change helps?","options":{"A":"Increase the number of generation steps","B":"Increase the classifier-free guidance (CFG) scale","C":"Set a fixed random seed","D":"Increase the negative prompt weight"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The CFG scale controls how strongly the model follows the text prompt, so raising it makes the generated image adhere more closely to the requested details.\\nMore generation steps mainly refine denoising quality; a fixed seed only makes results reproducible; increasing the negative prompt weight strengthens what to avoid rather than how closely the image matches the main prompt."},{"id":"aif-q098","question":"A telecom company wants an LLM chatbot that gives support agents real-time answers grounded in internal policy documents, at the lowest cost. Which approach fits best?","options":{"A":"Fine-tune the LLM on the policy documents","B":"Implement Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) over the policy documents","C":"Pre-train a new model from scratch on the policy corpus","D":"Paste all policy documents into every prompt as context"},"correct":"B","explanation":"RAG retrieves the relevant policy passages at query time and grounds the chatbot\'s answers in them, delivering accurate responses cheaply with no training.\\nFine-tuning adds training cost and must be repeated as policies change; pre-training from scratch is by far the most expensive option; pasting all documents into every prompt drives up token costs."},{"id":"aif-q102","question":"A company built a text summarization solution on Amazon Bedrock and will use Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation. Which metric should it use to measure summary quality?","options":{"A":"Area under the ROC curve (AUC)","B":"BERTScore","C":"Mean squared error (MSE)","D":"Perplexity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"BERTScore compares the generated summary with reference text using contextual embeddings, capturing semantic similarity, which makes it suitable for measuring summarization quality.\\nAUC measures binary classification ranking, MSE measures regression error, and perplexity measures language-modeling fit; none of these compares a summary against references for quality."},{"id":"aif-q106","question":"A language-learning app calls an LLM to rewrite text more clearly and has reference examples of the improved versions. It wants the model\'s output to resemble those examples. Which metric should it use?","options":{"A":"Perplexity of the generated text","B":"Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation (ROUGE)","C":"Semantic robustness score","D":"Average response latency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ROUGE measures the n-gram and sequence overlap between the model\'s output and the reference examples, so it directly captures how closely the output resembles those improved versions.\\nPerplexity reflects language-modeling fit, semantic robustness measures stability under input changes, and latency measures speed; none compares output against reference examples."},{"id":"aif-q107","question":"A company\'s image-generation model keeps producing pictures unrelated to the prompts, and the team wants to adjust its prompting to cut down on these. Which technique helps?","options":{"A":"Add few-shot example images","B":"Use negative prompts that state what to exclude","C":"Use zero-shot prompts","D":"Make the prompts more open-ended"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Negative prompts explicitly tell the model what to leave out, constraining generation so fewer off-topic elements appear.\\nAdding example images or using zero-shot prompts gives no instruction about what to avoid, and making prompts more open-ended increases drift rather than reducing it."},{"id":"aif-q108","question":"An electronics retailer wants an LLM to write short, feature-focused product descriptions. Which prompt-engineering approach works best?","options":{"A":"Write one generic prompt for all products and manually edit each output","B":"Create category-specific prompts that highlight key features and specify the output format and length","C":"List every product attribute in a single prompt and ask for a description","D":"Ask the model for the most creative description it can produce, then trim it"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Category-specific prompts that call out the key features and set the format and length steer the model to consistent, concise, feature-focused descriptions with little rework.\\nA single generic prompt needs heavy manual editing; listing every attribute produces long, unfocused text; asking for maximum creativity and trimming wastes effort and drifts from the required style."},{"id":"aif-q119","question":"An insurance company wants an AI application that lets employees look up open claims, retrieve the details of a specific claim, and pull the claim\'s documents. Which solution fits best?","options":{"A":"Use Agents for Amazon Bedrock with Amazon Fraud Detector","B":"Use Agents for Amazon Bedrock with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases","C":"Use an Amazon Bedrock knowledge base on its own, without an agent","D":"Train a new ML model with Amazon SageMaker AI"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Agents for Amazon Bedrock carry out the multi-step actions (looking up claims, extracting details, fetching documents) and call an Amazon Bedrock knowledge base to retrieve the claim data, which together meet all three needs.\\nPairing the agent with Amazon Fraud Detector adds fraud scoring rather than document retrieval; a knowledge base alone can retrieve content but cannot orchestrate the lookups and actions; training a new model with Amazon SageMaker AI is far more effort than needed."},{"id":"aif-q128","question":"An analyst adds an instruction to a prompt on Amazon Bedrock asking the model to show its work and explain each step while solving math problems. Which prompt-engineering technique is being used?","options":{"A":"Few-shot prompting","B":"Tree of thoughts prompting","C":"Chain-of-thought prompting","D":"Zero-shot prompting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chain-of-thought prompting asks the model to lay out its intermediate reasoning step by step, which is exactly what instructing it to show its work does, improving accuracy on math problems.\\nFew-shot prompting supplies worked examples; tree of thoughts explores several reasoning branches in parallel; zero-shot prompting gives only an instruction with no reasoning scaffold."},{"id":"aif-q131","question":"A company needs higher-quality answers from a large language model (LLM) on complex problems that require detailed, step-by-step reasoning. Which prompt-engineering technique fits?","options":{"A":"Directional stimulus prompting","B":"Prompt chaining","C":"Least-to-most prompting","D":"Chain-of-thought prompting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chain-of-thought prompting guides the model to produce its reasoning as a series of logical steps within the response, which improves answer quality on complex problems.\\nDirectional stimulus prompting steers output with hints or keywords; prompt chaining sends several separate prompts in sequence; least-to-most prompting decomposes a task into ordered subproblems but is a distinct technique from generating an in-line reasoning chain."},{"id":"aif-q143","question":"A retailer runs an AI assistant that helps agents answer frequently asked questions, but the questions keep changing. Agents type a question and need an up-to-date generated answer at the lowest cost. Which approach fits?","options":{"A":"Use Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) with prompt engineering","B":"Fine-tune the model on a recurring schedule","C":"Pre-train the model on the FAQ data and benchmark it","D":"Embed the full FAQ set into the system prompt"},"correct":"A","explanation":"RAG retrieves the most relevant, current information at query time and prompt engineering shapes the response, so the assistant stays accurate as questions change without repeated training cost.\\nScheduled fine-tuning and pre-training both require recurring, expensive training runs; embedding the full FAQ set into every prompt drives up token costs and does not scale as the FAQ grows."},{"id":"aif-q146","question":"A company wants more accurate answers from a generative AI application on Amazon Bedrock and wants the cheapest way to get them. Which approach fits?","options":{"A":"Fine-tune the FM","B":"Use prompt engineering","C":"Retrain the FM from scratch","D":"Apply model distillation to the FM"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Prompt engineering improves accuracy by refining the instructions and context with no training cost, making it the cheapest option.\\nFine-tuning adds training cost and data preparation, model distillation requires a separate training process to build a smaller model, and retraining from scratch is the most expensive approach of all."},{"id":"aif-q155","question":"An application summarizes books of varying lengths but fails on some of the longer ones. What is the most likely cause?","options":{"A":"The temperature is set too high","B":"The input exceeds the model\'s maximum context window","C":"The maximum output length is set too low","D":"The model does not support few-shot prompting"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A model can only process a limited number of input tokens, so a book whose length exceeds the maximum context window cannot be handled in one pass, which explains why only the longer books fail.\\nA high temperature changes randomness, not capacity; a low maximum output length would shorten every summary rather than fail on selected books; and few-shot support is irrelevant to summarizing one long input."},{"id":"aif-q156","question":"An airline wants a text chatbot for flight schedules, bookings, and payments, built on large language models (LLMs) plus a knowledge base, with the LEAST development effort. Which solution fits?","options":{"A":"Train models with Amazon SageMaker Autopilot","B":"Fine-tune models on Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","C":"Build a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) agent with Amazon Bedrock","D":"Build a custom orchestration service with AWS Lambda and a self-hosted model"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock lets the team combine an LLM with a knowledge base using RAG to deliver accurate, current answers with minimal development effort.\\nSageMaker Autopilot trains traditional ML models from tabular data; fine-tuning on SageMaker JumpStart needs data preparation and a training run; building a custom Lambda orchestration layer over a self-hosted model is the most work of all."},{"id":"aif-q161","question":"Which statement describes a genuine risk or limitation of prompt engineering for a generative AI model?","options":{"A":"Prompt engineering guarantees deterministic outputs, so validation is unnecessary","B":"Prompt engineering can expose the model to prompt injection attacks","C":"Prompt engineering permanently updates the model\'s weights with each prompt","D":"Prompt engineering eliminates all risk of data poisoning and model hijacking"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A real limitation of prompt engineering is that crafted inputs can still be exploited through prompt injection, where an attacker manipulates the prompt to change the model\'s behavior.\\nPrompt engineering does not make outputs deterministic or remove the need for validation, it does not update the model\'s weights (that is fine-tuning), and it cannot eliminate risks such as data poisoning or model hijacking."},{"id":"aif-q162","question":"A publisher\'s Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) application must feel near real time, while new articles are published daily. Which two pipeline steps can run as offline batch jobs?","options":{"A":"Generating embeddings for each incoming user query","B":"Generating embeddings for the published content","C":"Ranking and returning results for a user\'s query","D":"Building or updating the vector search index","E":"Detecting the language of each user query"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Generating content embeddings and building or updating the search index depend only on the documents, so they can run in periodic batches as new articles arrive, off the real-time path.\\nGenerating embeddings for a user query, ranking and returning results, and detecting a query\'s language all happen per request and must run in real time."},{"id":"aif-q163","question":"Which technique decomposes a complex task into smaller subtasks that are sent to a large language model (LLM) in sequence, each building on the last?","options":{"A":"Tree of thoughts","B":"Chain-of-thought prompting","C":"Prompt chaining","D":"Few-shot prompting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prompt chaining breaks a complex task into smaller subtasks and feeds them to the LLM one after another, with each step using the output of the previous one.\\nChain-of-thought reasons step by step within a single response; tree of thoughts explores multiple reasoning branches; few-shot prompting supplies examples rather than sequencing subtasks."},{"id":"aif-q164","question":"An AI practitioner must improve the accuracy of a text-generation model that depends on rapidly changing inventory data. Which technique helps most?","options":{"A":"Fine-tune the model on historical inventory data","B":"Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)","C":"Transfer learning","D":"Continuous pre-training on inventory snapshots"},"correct":"B","explanation":"RAG fetches the current inventory data at inference time and conditions generation on it, so the output reflects the latest information and stays accurate without retraining.\\nFine-tuning on historical data, transfer learning, and continuous pre-training on snapshots all bake data in at training time, so they go stale as the inventory keeps changing."},{"id":"aif-q167","question":"In a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) workflow, what is the main purpose of chunking documents?","options":{"A":"To reduce the cost of storing the documents","B":"To improve the relevance of passages retrieved from the vector index","C":"To speed up training of the model","D":"To avoid having to generate embeddings for the documents"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Splitting documents into smaller, semantically coherent chunks lets the retriever return only the most relevant segments for a query, improving the relevance of the context passed to the model.\\nChunking is not aimed at lowering storage cost, it does not speed up model training, and chunked text is still embedded, so it does not avoid generating embeddings."},{"id":"aif-q173","question":"Which statement describes an advantage of Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) for natural language processing (NLP) tasks?","options":{"A":"It speeds up training of the underlying language model","B":"It reduces the model\'s parameter count to save memory","C":"It draws on external knowledge sources to produce more accurate, informative responses","D":"It guarantees deterministic, repeatable outputs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"RAG retrieves relevant external documents at inference time and conditions generation on them, enriching responses with up-to-date, domain-specific knowledge without retraining the model.\\nRAG does not speed up training or reduce the model\'s parameter count, and it does not make a generative model\'s outputs deterministic."},{"id":"aif-q175","question":"Which technique trains an AI model on a labeled dataset so it adapts to a specific industry\'s terminology and requirements?","options":{"A":"Continuous pre-training","B":"In-context learning","C":"Fine-tuning","D":"Model distillation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fine-tuning continues training a pre-trained model on a labeled, domain-specific dataset so it learns the industry\'s terminology and task requirements directly from that data.\\nContinuous pre-training relies on large unlabeled data; in-context learning adds examples at inference time without training; model distillation transfers knowledge into a smaller model rather than adapting it to a labeled domain dataset."},{"id":"aif-q176","question":"A team built an agent with Amazon Bedrock Agents and wants to improve its accuracy by giving it a few specific examples. Which approach fits?","options":{"A":"Fine-tune the underlying model on the examples","B":"Add the examples to the agent\'s advanced prompts","C":"Add the examples to an Amazon Bedrock knowledge base","D":"Create a guardrail that includes the examples"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Adding the specific input-output examples to the agent\'s advanced prompts is few-shot prompting, which steers the agent toward more accurate behavior with no retraining or extra services.\\nFine-tuning the model is far heavier for a handful of examples; a knowledge base supplies retrieved context rather than behavioral examples; a guardrail enforces safety policies, not example-driven accuracy."},{"id":"aif-q178","question":"A company will use instruction-based fine-tuning to adapt a foundation model (FM) to its domain. How should it prepare the training data?","options":{"A":"Assemble a large unlabeled domain corpus and continue pre-training","B":"Create instruction-and-response (question-and-answer) pairs for the domain","C":"Build a vector index of the documents for retrieval","D":"Collect human preference rankings of model outputs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Instruction-based fine-tuning needs instruction-response examples, so curating domain-specific question-and-answer pairs teaches the model how to interpret domain queries and produce correct answers.\\nAn unlabeled corpus is for continuous pre-training; a vector index supports RAG rather than training; human preference rankings are the data used for RLHF, not instruction tuning."},{"id":"aif-q188","question":"Which metric is commonly used to evaluate how well a foundation model (FM) performs on text summarization?","options":{"A":"F1 score","B":"Mean squared error (MSE)","C":"Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation (ROUGE)","D":"Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU) score"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ROUGE measures the overlap between a generated summary and reference summaries, which makes it the standard metric for summarization quality.\\nF1 is a classification metric, MSE measures regression error, and BLEU is built for machine translation; none targets summarization."},{"id":"aif-q189","question":"What is a benefit of fine-tuning a foundation model (FM)?","options":{"A":"It lets the model pull in external data at inference time","B":"It improves the model\'s performance on a specific task by further training it on task data","C":"It compresses the model to reduce size and speed up inference","D":"It refreshes the model\'s general knowledge by pre-training on newer data"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fine-tuning updates the model\'s weights using task-relevant data, sharpening its accuracy and relevance on that specific task.\\nPulling in external data at inference time describes RAG; compressing the model describes distillation; refreshing general knowledge with newer pre-training describes continuous pre-training."},{"id":"aif-q194","question":"What are two primary use cases for storing embeddings in a vector database such as Amazon OpenSearch Service?","options":{"A":"Exact keyword string matching","B":"Semantic search over documents","C":"Scheduled batch ETL jobs","D":"Similarity-based recommendations","E":"Transactional primary-key lookups"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Semantic search and similarity-based recommendations both rely on nearest neighbor comparison of embeddings, which is exactly what a vector database provides.\\nExact keyword matching is lexical rather than semantic, and primary-key lookups and scheduled batch ETL are standard database operations that do not use vector similarity."},{"id":"aif-q196","question":"For AI applications, which two capabilities of Amazon Aurora with the pgvector extension are the most important?","options":{"A":"Standard SQL operations","B":"Vector similarity search","C":"Web hosting","D":"Combining vectors with structured data","E":"Email handling"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Vector similarity search lets the application find the most semantically related content, which is the core retrieval step in tasks such as RAG, and the ability to combine vectors with structured data means those searches can run alongside existing relational records in the same database, so together these two capabilities are what make Aurora with pgvector valuable for AI workloads.\\nStandard SQL operations are a general database capability that is not specific to AI applications. Web hosting and email handling are not functions of Amazon Aurora at all and are unrelated to vector storage or AI workloads."},{"id":"aif-q212","question":"A company wants a large language model (LLM) to write product descriptions and plans to include several example descriptions that follow a fixed format in the prompt. Which prompt-engineering technique does this use?","options":{"A":"Zero-shot prompting","B":"One-shot prompting","C":"Few-shot prompting","D":"Role prompting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Few-shot prompting places several examples in the prompt so the model learns the pattern and format and generates new descriptions in the same style, without any training.\\nZero-shot prompting supplies no examples, one-shot prompting supplies only a single example, and role prompting assigns a persona but does not provide formatted examples."},{"id":"aif-q214","question":"A grocery chain wants a chatbot that checks live inventory and tells shoppers where a product is located in the store. Which prompt-engineering technique should it use?","options":{"A":"Few-shot prompting","B":"Chain-of-thought prompting","C":"Reasoning and acting (ReAct) prompting","D":"Zero-shot prompting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ReAct prompting interleaves the model\'s reasoning with explicit actions, such as calling a live inventory API, then uses the returned data to answer, which is what is needed to check stock and report a product\'s location in real time.\\nFew-shot, chain-of-thought, and zero-shot prompting shape reasoning or supply examples but provide no mechanism to call an external system for live data."},{"id":"aif-q219","question":"A company measures its translation tool by comparing the tool\'s outputs with human translations of the same documents. Which evaluation strategy should it use?","options":{"A":"Use ROUGE to estimate the absolute translation quality of the two methods","B":"Use the Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU) score to estimate the relative translation quality of the two methods","C":"Use the Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU) score to estimate the absolute translation quality of the two methods","D":"Use ROUGE to estimate the relative translation quality of the two methods"},"correct":"B","explanation":"BLEU measures n-gram overlap against reference translations, so applying it to both the tool\'s outputs and the human outputs on the same documents gives comparable scores for judging which method is relatively better.\\nBLEU produces a relative comparison, not an absolute quality measure, so the absolute-BLEU option is wrong; ROUGE is built for summarization, making it the wrong metric for comparing translations."},{"id":"aif-q224","question":"A company\'s generative AI application uses a pre-trained foundation model (FM) on Amazon Bedrock and wants its answers to include the company\'s own information, at the lowest cost. Which solution fits?","options":{"A":"Use Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases","B":"Deploy a custom model on Amazon Bedrock","C":"Fine-tune the FM on the company information","D":"Use Amazon Bedrock Agents"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases index and retrieve the company\'s documents at inference time and inject relevant context into the prompt (RAG), adding the information cheaply with no training.\\nDeploying a custom model and fine-tuning the FM both add training cost; Amazon Bedrock Agents orchestrate tasks but do not, on their own, supply document context."},{"id":"aif-q226","question":"A company is building an HR-policy chatbot on a large language model (LLM) with a large set of digital documents and wants to optimize the quality of its answers. Which technique fits best?","options":{"A":"Set the temperature to 1","B":"Use Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)","C":"Fine-tune the model on the policy documents","D":"Decrease the token size"},"correct":"B","explanation":"RAG retrieves precise, current passages from the policy documents at query time and grounds the chatbot\'s answers in them, optimizing accuracy without overloading the context window.\\nSetting the temperature to 1 only adds randomness; fine-tuning is costly and goes stale as policies change; decreasing the token size drops the context the model needs."},{"id":"aif-q227","question":"An education company trains a custom large language model (LLM) for teenagers and wants the output to match their style, including creative spelling and shorthand. Which metric should it use to assess the output against reference examples?","options":{"A":"BERTScore","B":"Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU) score","C":"Perplexity","D":"F1 score"},"correct":"B","explanation":"BLEU measures surface-level n-gram overlap between the model\'s output and reference text, so it is suited to checking how closely the model reproduces the exact teen-style spelling and shorthand.\\nBERTScore measures semantic similarity rather than surface forms; perplexity measures language-modeling fit; F1 is a classification metric."},{"id":"aif-q234","question":"A bank is building a chatbot to answer account-opening questions using public bank documents on Amazon Bedrock with prompt engineering. Which technique fits?","options":{"A":"Directional stimulus prompting","B":"Zero-shot prompting","C":"Few-shot prompting","D":"One-shot prompting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Few-shot prompting supplies several example questions and answers drawn from the bank documents, guiding the model to produce accurate, well-formatted responses without training.\\nDirectional stimulus prompting steers output with hints, zero-shot prompting provides no examples, and one-shot prompting provides only a single example."},{"id":"aif-q237","question":"A company wants a chatbot for employee policy questions; policies change often and answers must reflect updates in near real time. Which solution fits?","options":{"A":"Fine-tune a large language model (LLM) on the policy text with Amazon SageMaker AI","B":"Create a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) workflow with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases","C":"Continuously pre-train an LLM on the policy documents","D":"Build a custom application with Amazon Q Business"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A RAG workflow with Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases retrieves the latest policy documents at query time, so the chatbot reflects updates in near real time without retraining.\\nFine-tuning and continuous pre-training must be re-run whenever policies change; a custom Amazon Q Business application is more development effort and is not the targeted way to keep the answers current here."},{"id":"aif-q238","question":"A company uses supervised learning to train a model on a small labeled dataset for a specific task. Which step of the foundation model (FM) lifecycle is this?","options":{"A":"Pre-training","B":"Continuous pre-training","C":"Fine-tuning","D":"Deployment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fine-tuning continues training a pre-trained model on a small labeled dataset for a target task, which is exactly the supervised step described.\\nPre-training and continuous pre-training use large, mostly unlabeled data; deployment puts the finished model into production. None of these is the supervised, task-specific training step."},{"id":"aif-q258","question":"A company wants to label training data with human feedback to fine-tune a foundation model (FM) but does not want to build labeling tools or manage a workforce. Which AWS service or feature fits?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","B":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","C":"Amazon SageMaker Autopilot","D":"Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth Plus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth Plus is a fully managed labeling service that uses a human workforce, so the company gets human-labeled data without building labeling tools or managing labelers.\\nSageMaker Data Wrangler prepares and transforms data, SageMaker Clarify detects bias and explains predictions, and SageMaker Autopilot automatically builds models. None of these provides managed human labeling."},{"id":"aif-q259","question":"A media streaming company wants natural-language image search with similarity and nearest neighbor queries, backed by a vector database. Which AWS service fits?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon OpenSearch Service","C":"Amazon Personalize","D":"Amazon RDS for MySQL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon OpenSearch Service supports vector storage with similarity (nearest neighbor) search, which is what natural-language image search and filtering require.\\nAmazon DynamoDB (key-value) and Amazon RDS for MySQL (relational) lack native vector similarity search, and Amazon Personalize generates recommendations rather than serving as a general vector store."},{"id":"aif-q265","question":"A company is comparing several large language models (LLMs) for text summarization and needs a metric for summary quality. Which metric fits?","options":{"A":"Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU) score","B":"Area under the ROC curve (AUC)","C":"Perplexity","D":"Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation (ROUGE)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ROUGE measures the overlap between generated summaries and reference summaries, making it the standard metric for summarization quality.\\nBLEU is built for translation, AUC is a classification metric, and perplexity measures language-modeling fit; none is designed for summary quality."},{"id":"aif-q271","question":"A company needs an AI solution that produces both images and text descriptions for a product catalog and is comparing foundation models (FMs) by the output types each supports. Which FM characteristic is it evaluating?","options":{"A":"Latency","B":"Context window length","C":"Modality","D":"Multilingual support"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Modality describes the input and output types an FM supports, such as text or images, so evaluating whether a model can output both images and text descriptions is an assessment of modality.\\nLatency is response speed, context window length is how much input and output fit, and multilingual support concerns languages; none describes the output types a model can generate."},{"id":"aif-q272","question":"An online retailer wants to evaluate a model that labels each customer review as positive, negative, or neutral. Which model evaluation task type fits this requirement?","options":{"A":"Open-ended text generation","B":"Text summarization","C":"Classification","D":"Question and answer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Classification assigns each input to one of a fixed set of predefined categories, so sorting every customer review into one of three sentiment labels (positive, negative, or neutral) is a classification task and is evaluated with classification metrics.\\nOpen-ended text generation produces free-form text from a prompt and has no fixed set of output labels. Text summarization condenses longer text into a shorter version rather than assigning it to a category. Question and answer returns a response to a posed question rather than placing the input into predefined sentiment categories."},{"id":"aif-q283","question":"Which AWS service stores embeddings in a vector database for use with foundation models (FMs) and Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"Amazon OpenSearch Service","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon Redshift"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon OpenSearch Service provides a vector database that stores and searches embeddings, which is what powers RAG workflows and integrations with foundation models.\\nAmazon S3 is object storage, Amazon DynamoDB is a key-value database, and Amazon Redshift is a data warehouse; none offers native vector search over embeddings."},{"id":"aif-q285","question":"A company is building a generative AI solution on Amazon Bedrock and needs a service that provides vector database storage and vector search. Which AWS service fits?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon ElastiCache","C":"Amazon OpenSearch Service","D":"Amazon Redshift"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon OpenSearch Service supports vector storage and similarity search over embeddings, so it can back a generative AI solution on Amazon Bedrock.\\nAmazon DynamoDB is a key-value database, Amazon ElastiCache is an in-memory cache, and Amazon Redshift is a data warehouse; none provides native vector database and search capabilities."},{"id":"aif-q288","question":"When designing prompts for a generative AI model, which two practices are important?","options":{"A":"Maximize prompt length regardless of relevance","B":"Provide clear context and instructions","C":"Use ambiguous wording to encourage creativity","D":"Include relevant examples when they help","E":"Omit instructions and rely on model defaults"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Clear context and instructions tell the model what is expected, and relevant examples (few-shot prompting) guide it toward the desired output format and style, so both improve prompt quality.\\nMaximizing length regardless of relevance, using ambiguous wording, and omitting instructions all add noise or ambiguity and degrade the model\'s responses."},{"id":"aif-q294","question":"Which two metrics are commonly used to evaluate the output quality of generative AI models?","options":{"A":"GPU memory usage","B":"Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU) score for translation quality","C":"Request throughput in requests per second","D":"Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation (ROUGE) score for summarization quality","E":"Model parameter count"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"BLEU assesses translation quality and ROUGE assesses summarization quality by comparing generated text to reference text, so both measure the quality of generative output.\\nGPU memory usage, request throughput, and model parameter count are infrastructure and model-size metrics that say nothing about output quality."},{"id":"aif-q296","question":"Which two capabilities of Amazon OpenSearch Service are most relevant for AI/ML workloads?","options":{"A":"Full-text BM25 keyword search","B":"k-NN nearest neighbor search over vectors","C":"Geospatial distance queries","D":"Storing and indexing embeddings as a vector database","E":"Automated model training"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"k-NN nearest neighbor search and storing and indexing embeddings as a vector database underpin AI/ML tasks such as semantic search and RAG.\\nBM25 keyword search is lexical rather than semantic, geospatial queries handle location data, and OpenSearch does not perform model training."},{"id":"aif-q301","question":"A company wants an AI assistant that can evaluate specific data sources, call external APIs, and then generate, compare, and prioritize response options. Which Amazon Bedrock feature fits?","options":{"A":"Prompt Management","B":"Knowledge Bases","C":"Agents","D":"Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Agents orchestrate multi-step tasks, including calling external APIs and generating, comparing, and prioritizing response options, which matches all of the stated needs.\\nPrompt Management versions prompts, Knowledge Bases supply retrieved context, and Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation scores models; none orchestrates API calls and decision-making on its own."},{"id":"aif-q344","question":"In generative AI, what distinguishes zero-shot, one-shot, and few-shot prompting?","options":{"A":"The model architecture used","B":"The number of examples provided in the prompt","C":"The number of reasoning steps the model takes","D":"The amount of data used to fine-tune the model"},"correct":"B","explanation":"These terms describe how many examples are placed in the prompt: zero-shot gives none, one-shot gives a single example, and few-shot gives a small set, all without retraining the model.\\nThey do not refer to the model architecture, the number of reasoning steps, or the amount of fine-tuning data, which are unrelated to how many in-prompt examples are supplied."},{"id":"aif-q041","question":"An ML team is training a foundation model (FM) and wants to push its accuracy up to a required threshold. What should the team do?","options":{"A":"Decrease the number of epochs","B":"Decrease the batch size","C":"Increase the number of epochs","D":"Increase the temperature parameter"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Increasing the number of epochs lets the model train over more passes of the data, which generally improves accuracy until it reaches the target level.\\nDecreasing the epochs gives the model less training and tends to lower accuracy. Decreasing the batch size mainly affects training dynamics and memory use rather than reliably raising accuracy toward a target. Temperature is an inference parameter that controls output randomness and has no effect on training accuracy."},{"id":"aif-q048","question":"A cosmetics brand wants a pre-trained generative AI model to write campaign copy that fits its brand voice and messaging. What should the team do?","options":{"A":"Add more layers to the model\'s architecture to increase its complexity","B":"Craft clear, well-structured prompts that provide instructions and context to guide the model\'s output","C":"Tune the model\'s architecture and hyperparameters to improve its overall performance","D":"Collect a large, diverse dataset and pre-train a new generative model from scratch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Crafting clear prompts with instructions and context guides the pre-trained model to generate content that matches the brand voice, without modifying the model itself.\\nAdding layers or tuning the architecture and hyperparameters means re-engineering or retraining the model, which is unnecessary and costly here. Pre-training a new model from scratch is even more resource-intensive and is not needed to align output with brand voice."},{"id":"aif-q051","question":"A consultancy must choose an Amazon Bedrock model for internal use, and its analysts care about the writing style of the answers. How should the firm choose the model?","options":{"A":"Evaluate the models with built-in prompt datasets","B":"Choose the model based on public model leaderboards","C":"Evaluate the models with a human workforce using custom prompt datasets","D":"Compare the models using the InvocationLatency runtime metric in Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Using a human workforce to evaluate the models against custom prompt datasets captures subjective style preferences that automated scores miss, so it best identifies the model whose responses match what employees prefer.\\nBuilt-in prompt datasets and public leaderboards measure general performance, not the company specific style preference. The InvocationLatency metric in Amazon CloudWatch measures response speed, not response style."},{"id":"aif-q065","question":"A news app runs sentiment analysis with a large language model (LLM) on Amazon Bedrock and wants the same input to yield steadier results. Which inference parameter should it change?","options":{"A":"Increase the temperature value","B":"Increase the maximum generation length","C":"Decrease the temperature value","D":"Reduce the maximum number of output tokens"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lowering the temperature reduces randomness in token selection, making the model outputs more deterministic and consistent for the same input.\\nRaising the temperature increases randomness and variability. Changing the maximum generation length or reducing the number of output tokens only affects response length, not how consistent the responses are."},{"id":"aif-q070","question":"A team needs to check how accurately its image-classification foundation model (FM) performs. Which strategy should the team use?","options":{"A":"Count the number of layers in the model\'s neural network","B":"Calculate the total cost of the resources the model uses","C":"Check the color accuracy of the images the model processes","D":"Measure the model\'s accuracy on a predefined benchmark dataset"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Measuring the model\'s accuracy against a predefined benchmark dataset is the standard way to assess classification accuracy, because performance is compared against known correct labels.\\nCounting neural-network layers describes architecture, not accuracy. Calculating total resource cost measures expense. Checking the color accuracy of processed images is unrelated to classification correctness."},{"id":"aif-q075","question":"A telecom company\'s chatbot uses a large language model (LLM) on Amazon Bedrock for intent detection, and the team wants to improve accuracy with few-shot learning. What data does the team need?","options":{"A":"Pairs of user messages and their correct user intents","B":"Pairs of user messages and their correct chatbot responses","C":"Pairs of chatbot responses and their correct user intents","D":"Pairs of user intents and their correct chatbot responses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Few-shot learning supplies the model with example input-output pairs for the task, and intent detection maps a user message to an intent, so the company needs pairs of user messages and their correct user intents.\\nPairs that involve chatbot responses, whether messages-to-responses, responses-to-intents, or intents-to-responses, train the wrong mapping and do not demonstrate how to detect a user intent from a message."},{"id":"aif-q089","question":"During a red-team test of a foundation model (FM), an engineer tries to get past its safety filters so it produces harmful text. What is this technique called?","options":{"A":"Fuzzing the training data to find vulnerabilities","B":"Jailbreaking","C":"Authorized penetration testing","D":"Denial-of-service (DoS) attack"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Jailbreaking is the attempt to circumvent or disable a model built-in safety controls so it produces restricted or harmful content, which is exactly what the engineer is doing.\\nFuzzing the training data probes for vulnerabilities by feeding malformed inputs during development, not by bypassing safety filters. Authorized penetration testing is a sanctioned security assessment. A denial-of-service attack targets availability rather than defeating content safeguards."},{"id":"aif-q110","question":"A team is building a conversational search feature and needs a database that can store generative AI embeddings as vectors and run similarity queries over them. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Athena","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL","D":"Amazon EMR"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL supports the pgvector extension, which stores, indexes, and runs similarity queries over vector embeddings, making it suitable for storing and querying embeddings as vectors.\\nAmazon Athena is a serverless query service for data in Amazon S3. Amazon Redshift is a data warehouse for analytics. Amazon EMR runs big-data processing frameworks. None of these is designed to store and query vector embeddings for similarity search."},{"id":"aif-q114","question":"How can a team determine whether a foundation model (FM) effectively meets its business objectives?","options":{"A":"Evaluate the model\'s performance on benchmark datasets","B":"Assess how well the model aligns with the specific use cases it must support","C":"Analyze the model\'s architecture and hyperparameters","D":"Measure the computational resources the model needs for deployment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Assessing how well the model aligns with the specific use cases it must support shows whether its outputs solve the real business problems, which is how you tell if it meets business objectives.\\nBenchmark performance measures general accuracy, not business fit. Analyzing architecture and hyperparameters is a technical detail unrelated to business value. Measuring computational resources addresses cost and feasibility, not whether the objectives are met."},{"id":"aif-q132","question":"A company wants to keep its foundation model (FM) current by regularly retraining it on the latest data. Which training strategy meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Batch learning","B":"Static training","C":"Latent training","D":"Continuous pre-training"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Continuous pre-training periodically ingests the latest data and updates the model\'s weights, so the foundation model stays current without retraining from scratch each time.\\nBatch learning processes data in fixed batches but is not a strategy for ongoing model refresh. Static training produces a one-time fixed model that does not update. Latent training is not a recognized training strategy."},{"id":"aif-q136","question":"A company\'s large language model (LLM) keeps producing hallucinations. Which change can reduce them?","options":{"A":"Decrease the temperature inference parameter for the model","B":"Use Agents for Amazon Bedrock to supervise the model\'s training","C":"Preprocess the data to remove any data that causes hallucinations","D":"Switch to a foundation model (FM) that is trained never to hallucinate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lowering the temperature parameter reduces the randomness of the model\'s token selection, so it relies on higher-probability outputs and is less likely to produce hallucinated content.\\nAgents for Amazon Bedrock orchestrate tasks and do not supervise training. You cannot reliably identify and remove specific data that causes hallucinations through preprocessing. No foundation model is guaranteed never to hallucinate, so switching models does not solve the problem."},{"id":"aif-q140","question":"How does top-p (nucleus) sampling differ from temperature-based sampling?","options":{"A":"It samples from the smallest possible set of tokens","B":"It always produces deterministic output","C":"It samples only from the set of tokens whose probabilities add up to p","D":"It works only with specific model architectures"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Top-p (nucleus) sampling dynamically restricts the candidate tokens to the smallest set whose cumulative probability reaches p and then samples from that set, which adapts the choice of tokens to the probability distribution at each step.\\nIt does not fix the token set to the smallest possible size regardless of probabilities. It does not produce deterministic output, since it still samples. It is not limited to specific model architectures."},{"id":"aif-q190","question":"A retailer has 100 high-quality agent-customer conversations and wants its chatbot\'s replies to match the company\'s tone. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use Amazon Personalize to generate the responses","B":"Create an Amazon Bedrock fine-tuning job","C":"Create an Amazon SageMaker HyperPod pre-training job","D":"Host the model on Amazon SageMaker AI and apply TensorRT to optimize LLM inference"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An Amazon Bedrock fine-tuning job adapts a foundation model on the company\'s own example conversations, teaching it the company\'s tone so the chatbot reflects it in responses.\\nAmazon Personalize is a recommendation service, not a way to style chatbot text. A SageMaker HyperPod pre-training job is for large-scale pre-training from scratch, which is overkill for 100 examples. Hosting the model and applying TensorRT optimizes inference speed, not response tone."},{"id":"aif-q195","question":"An AI practitioner is comparing top-p (nucleus) sampling with temperature-based sampling. What distinguishes top-p sampling?","options":{"A":"It works only with certain model architectures","B":"It always returns the same output for a given prompt","C":"It limits sampling to exactly one token each step","D":"It samples from the smallest set of top tokens whose probabilities sum to p"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Top-p (nucleus) sampling chooses the smallest set of highest-probability tokens whose cumulative probability reaches p and samples from that set, adapting the candidate pool to the distribution at each step rather than using a fixed cutoff.\\nIt is not restricted to particular model architectures. It still samples, so it does not return identical output every time. It does not limit selection to a single token."},{"id":"aif-q200","question":"A company is adding a large language model (LLM) to its app and needs responses that are as deterministic and stable as possible. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Add \\"make your response deterministic\\" to the end of the prompt before sending it","B":"Set the temperature parameter to 1 when submitting the prompt","C":"Set the temperature parameter to 0 when submitting the prompt","D":"Add \\"make your response deterministic\\" to the start of the prompt before sending it"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Setting the temperature to 0 forces greedy decoding, so the model selects the most probable next token every time and produces deterministic, stable outputs.\\nSetting the temperature to 1 keeps full randomness, which is the opposite of deterministic. Adding an instruction like make your response deterministic at the start or end of the prompt does not control the sampling process and will not reliably make output deterministic."},{"id":"aif-q210","question":"Which large language model (LLM) parameter sets how many candidate next tokens the model considers at each generation step?","options":{"A":"Maximum tokens","B":"Temperature","C":"Top K","D":"Batch size"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Top K parameter limits sampling at each step to the K most probable next tokens, so it directly controls how many candidate tokens the model considers when generating each token.\\nMaximum tokens caps the total length of the output, not the candidate pool at each step. Temperature adjusts the randomness of the distribution rather than the number of candidates. Batch size concerns how many inputs are processed together, not token selection."},{"id":"aif-q220","question":"An AI practitioner wants a large language model (LLM) to produce more diverse and creative output. How should the practitioner adjust an inference parameter?","options":{"A":"Decrease the Top K value","B":"Increase the response length","C":"Increase the temperature value","D":"Decrease the prompt length"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Raising the temperature flattens the model\'s probability distribution and increases the chance of selecting less-likely tokens, which produces more varied and creative responses.\\nDecreasing the Top K value narrows the candidate tokens and makes output more focused, not more diverse. Increasing the response length only makes output longer. Decreasing the prompt length reduces context and does not increase creativity."},{"id":"aif-q239","question":"A company will fine-tune a large language model (LLM) on Amazon Bedrock to refine the style of its app\'s output messages. Which type of data does the company need?","options":{"A":"Samples of only input messages","B":"Paired samples of input messages and their corresponding output messages","C":"Samples of only output messages","D":"Separate, unpaired samples of input messages and output messages"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fine-tuning to refine output style requires paired examples of input messages and their corresponding output messages, so the model learns the mapping from input to the desired styled output.\\nSamples of only input messages or only output messages lack the input-to-output relationship the model must learn. Separate, unpaired samples do not show which output goes with which input, so the model cannot learn the styling."},{"id":"aif-q246","question":"Which two parameters are commonly used to control the diversity of a generative AI model\'s output?","options":{"A":"Model size","B":"Top-k sampling","C":"Training dataset size","D":"Top-p (nucleus) sampling","E":"Hardware configuration"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Top-k sampling limits selection to the k most likely next tokens, and Top-p (nucleus) sampling selects from the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities sum to p; both adjust the candidate pool to balance diversity and quality of generated text.\\nModel size, training dataset size, and hardware configuration affect a model\'s capability, cost, or speed, but they are not runtime parameters that control output diversity."},{"id":"aif-q247","question":"A design team uses an image-generation foundation model (FM) on Amazon Bedrock and wants to control how detailed or abstract each generated image is. Which model parameter should they adjust?","options":{"A":"Model checkpoint","B":"Generation step","C":"Batch size","D":"Token length"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The generation step parameter sets how many refinement steps the model runs while creating an image; more steps yield more detailed images and fewer steps yield more abstract ones, so it controls the level of detail.\\nA model checkpoint selects a saved version of the model, not the detail of a single image. Batch size sets how many images are produced at once. Token length applies to text generation, not image detail."},{"id":"aif-q257","question":"A team generates images with an Amazon Nova Canvas model and needs to keep specific items out of the results. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use a higher temperature value","B":"Use a negative prompt","C":"Use a more detailed prompt","D":"Switch to another foundation model (FM)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A negative prompt specifies the items or characteristics the model should avoid, so it keeps the unwanted items out of the generated images.\\nA higher temperature only adds randomness. A more detailed prompt describes what to include but does not reliably exclude specific items. Switching to another foundation model does not provide a mechanism to exclude items."},{"id":"aif-q260","question":"A company is building a generative AI tool and will customize a foundation model (FM) with its internal documents. Which approach meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Classification","B":"Distillation","C":"Continued pre-training","D":"Regression"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Continued pre-training further trains a foundation model on the company\'s internal documents, so the model adapts to the company\'s domain and content.\\nClassification and regression are supervised ML tasks, not ways to customize an FM on documents. Distillation transfers knowledge from a larger model to a smaller one rather than adapting a model to internal documents."},{"id":"aif-q266","question":"A research group wants several scientists to manually compare the outputs of different generative AI models for a defined prompt. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use automatic evaluation on Amazon Personalize","B":"Use model evaluation on Amazon Bedrock","C":"Use content moderation on Amazon Rekognition","D":"Use sentiment analysis on Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock model evaluation supports human-based evaluation, so a team of scientists can review and compare the models\' outputs against the group\'s prompt and criteria.\\nAmazon Personalize provides recommendations. Amazon Rekognition does image and video content moderation. Amazon Comprehend does NLP tasks such as sentiment analysis. None of these provides human evaluation of generative model output."},{"id":"aif-q042","question":"A support center rolls out a large language model (LLM) chatbot to cut down how many steps agents take when answering customer questions. Which business metric best shows whether the chatbot is making a difference?","options":{"A":"Corporate social responsibility","B":"Regulatory compliance","C":"Average call duration","D":"Website engagement rate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Average call duration directly reflects how much the chatbot reduces the steps agents take to resolve questions, so tracking it measures the chatbot\'s business impact.\\nCorporate social responsibility and regulatory compliance are organizational concerns unrelated to how quickly agents resolve customer questions. Website engagement rate measures online visitor behavior, not call center efficiency."},{"id":"aif-q244","question":"For AI workloads, what is the main benefit of pairing Amazon Aurora with the pgvector extension?","options":{"A":"It automatically generates AI models","B":"It provides pre-trained language models","C":"It enables vector similarity search inside a relational database","D":"It provides serverless computing capabilities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Aurora with the pgvector extension enables vector similarity search inside a relational database, so embeddings can be stored alongside structured data and queried with SQL while keeping relational features.\\nAurora does not automatically generate AI models or provide pre-trained language models. Serverless capacity is a deployment option but is not the primary advantage of pgvector for AI applications."},{"id":"aif-q384","question":"A company runs several generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock and wants to version its prompts, compare different prompt variants, and roll back to a previous version if quality drops. Which Amazon Bedrock feature meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases","B":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Amazon Bedrock Prompt Management","D":"Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Prompt Management lets teams create, version, and manage prompt templates, compare variants, and roll back to earlier versions, providing the lifecycle controls the company needs to maintain prompt quality across its applications.\\nAmazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases index and retrieve documents for RAG workflows. Amazon Bedrock Guardrails filter harmful or unwanted content. Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation scores and compares model performance. None of these manages prompt versions or supports rollback."},{"id":"aif-q385","question":"A company deployed an AI assistant on Amazon Bedrock to handle customer service requests. It wants to measure how effectively the assistant resolves customer issues without escalation. Which metric best captures this?","options":{"A":"BLEU score","B":"Task completion rate","C":"Perplexity","D":"Model parameter count"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Task completion rate measures the percentage of customer requests the assistant fully resolves without needing human escalation, directly reflecting how effectively it meets the business objective of handling service requests autonomously.\\nBLEU score measures translation quality by comparing generated text to references. Perplexity measures how well a language model predicts text. Model parameter count describes model size. None of these measures whether the assistant successfully completes customer tasks."},{"id":"aif-q386","question":"Which two metrics are examples of business-alignment metrics for evaluating AI applications?","options":{"A":"User satisfaction","B":"BERTScore","C":"Cost per interaction","D":"ROUGE score","E":"Model training time"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"User satisfaction measures how well the AI application meets end-user expectations, and cost per interaction measures the operational expense of each user engagement, both of which directly align with business objectives and are defined as business-alignment metrics for AI applications.\\nBERTScore and ROUGE score are technical metrics that measure the quality of generated text against references rather than business outcomes. Model training time is an operational metric about how long training takes and says nothing about whether the application achieves its business goals."},{"id":"aif-q387","question":"A company is building a knowledge graph that also needs to store vector embeddings and run similarity searches over them alongside graph traversals. Which AWS database service supports both graph queries and vector search?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon Neptune","C":"Amazon Redshift","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Neptune is a graph database that also supports vector similarity search, allowing the company to store embeddings alongside knowledge graph relationships and run both graph traversals and nearest-neighbor queries in the same database.\\nAmazon DynamoDB is a key-value and document database without native graph or vector search capabilities. Amazon Redshift is a data warehouse for analytics. Amazon ElastiCache is an in-memory caching service. None of these combines graph queries with vector similarity search."},{"id":"aif-q388","question":"A company generates long-form marketing content with a foundation model and wants to evaluate quality at scale without relying on human reviewers. Which evaluation approach uses another large language model to score the outputs?","options":{"A":"BLEU scoring","B":"LLM-as-a-judge","C":"Benchmark dataset comparison","D":"A/B testing with live users"},"correct":"B","explanation":"LLM-as-a-judge uses a separate large language model to evaluate and score the outputs of another model against defined criteria, providing scalable quality assessment without requiring human reviewers for every output.\\nBLEU scoring compares generated text to reference translations using n-gram overlap and requires pre-written references. Benchmark dataset comparison evaluates against fixed test sets with known answers. A/B testing requires live users to compare variants. None of these uses another LLM to judge output quality."},{"id":"aif-q436","question":"A company deployed a RAG-based customer support assistant and wants to evaluate whether the retrieval component returns relevant documents for user queries. Which approach should the team use?","options":{"A":"Measure the model\'s perplexity on a held-out dataset","B":"Compare retrieved passages against known-correct source documents for a set of test queries","C":"Count the total number of API calls made to Amazon Bedrock","D":"Measure the BLEU score of the model\'s outputs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Evaluating a RAG application requires assessing whether the retrieval component returns relevant documents, which is done by comparing retrieved passages for test queries against known-correct source documents and measuring retrieval precision and recall.\\nPerplexity evaluates language model quality, not retrieval relevance. Counting API calls measures usage volume. BLEU score evaluates translation quality. None of these assesses the retrieval pipeline."},{"id":"aif-q444","question":"A company sends the same lengthy system prompt thousands of times daily with only the user query changing, and it wants to reduce latency and cost. Which FM selection criterion is most relevant?","options":{"A":"Multilingual support","B":"Prompt caching","C":"Model complexity","D":"Diffusion capability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Prompt caching stores the processed representation of a repeated system prompt so the model does not re-process it on every request, reducing both latency and cost when the same prefix is sent repeatedly with only the user query varying.\\nMultilingual support concerns language coverage. Model complexity relates to reasoning capability. Diffusion capability applies to image generation models. None of these addresses the cost of processing a repeated prompt prefix."},{"id":"aif-q445","question":"A company needs a foundation model that can accept both text and images as input and produce text output. Which FM selection criterion determines whether a model supports this?","options":{"A":"Temperature range","B":"Modality","C":"Context window size","D":"Training dataset size"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Modality describes the types of input and output a model supports, such as text, images, audio, or video. A model must support multimodal input (text and images) to accept both types and produce text output.\\nTemperature range controls output randomness. Context window size determines how much information fits in a single prompt. Training dataset size affects model knowledge but does not determine supported input types."},{"id":"aif-q446","question":"A company runs a large foundation model on Amazon Bedrock but finds the inference cost too high. It wants a smaller, cheaper model that retains most of the large model\'s quality. Which customization approach meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)","B":"Model distillation","C":"Continuous pre-training","D":"In-context learning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Model distillation trains a smaller student model to replicate the behavior of a larger teacher model, producing a compact model that retains most of the original\'s quality at lower inference cost.\\nRAG retrieves external documents at query time but does not reduce model size or cost. Continuous pre-training updates knowledge but does not shrink the model. In-context learning adds examples to the prompt, which actually increases token costs."},{"id":"aif-q447","question":"A travel company wants an AI system that can search flight availability, compare prices across airlines, book tickets, and send confirmation emails, all from a single customer request. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"A text summarization model","B":"An AI agent that orchestrates multi-step tasks using tool calls","C":"A classification model that routes requests to departments","D":"A recommendation engine that suggests popular flights"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An AI agent can break down the customer\'s request into steps (searching flights, comparing prices, booking, and sending confirmation) and execute each by calling the appropriate tools and APIs, which is the defining capability of agents for complex business workflows.\\nA summarization model condenses text but cannot take actions. A classification model routes requests but cannot execute multi-step tasks. A recommendation engine suggests items but cannot book flights or send emails."},{"id":"aif-q448","question":"Which two practices improve the quality of responses from a foundation model through prompt engineering?","options":{"A":"Be specific and concise in instructions","B":"Use vague language to allow creative freedom","C":"Experiment with different prompt structures and iterate","D":"Always use the maximum token limit for prompts","E":"Avoid providing any context to keep prompts short"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Being specific and concise tells the model exactly what is expected without ambiguity, and experimenting with different prompt structures and iterating helps discover what works best for each task, both of which are prompt engineering best practices.\\nVague language introduces ambiguity that degrades response quality. Using the maximum token limit wastes tokens on irrelevant content. Avoiding context deprives the model of information it needs to respond accurately."},{"id":"aif-q449","question":"A team manages dozens of prompts across multiple generative AI applications and wants to track changes, compare variants, and revert to previous versions if quality drops. Which Amazon Bedrock feature meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases","B":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Amazon Bedrock Prompt Management","D":"Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Prompt Management provides prompt versioning, variant comparison, and rollback capabilities, giving teams lifecycle control over their prompts across multiple applications.\\nKnowledge Bases index and retrieve documents for RAG. Guardrails filter content for safety. Model Evaluation scores model performance. None of these manages prompt versions."},{"id":"aif-q001","question":"A logistics company uses ML models to forecast demand each quarter. An analyst is preparing a report that helps stakeholders understand how the trained models reach their predictions. What should the analyst include to satisfy the transparency and explainability needs?","options":{"A":"Sample data used for training","B":"Model convergence tables","C":"Partial dependence plots (PDPs)","D":"Source code used to train the model"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Partial dependence plots (PDPs) show how one or more input features relate to a model\'s predicted output, which makes them an effective way to explain feature influence and give stakeholders transparency into how the trained models reach their forecasts.\\nSource code used to train the model and sample training data reveal implementation and inputs but do not explain how individual features drive predictions. Model convergence tables describe how training progressed toward a solution rather than how the finished model behaves, so they do not meet the explainability requirement."},{"id":"aif-q003","question":"A research lab needs to sort genetic samples into 20 categories based on their characteristics. The team requires an algorithm whose internal decision logic can be clearly documented to show how it produces each output. Which algorithm fits this need?","options":{"A":"Logistic regression","B":"Decision trees","C":"Neural networks","D":"Linear regression"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Decision trees make their internal logic explicit through a series of readable split rules, so the team can document exactly how the model moves from input characteristics to one of the 20 output categories. This interpretability is why decision trees fit the requirement.\\nNeural networks are highly opaque, so their internal mechanism is difficult to document. Linear regression predicts a continuous value rather than assigning discrete categories, which does not match a 20-class problem. Logistic regression is interpretable through its coefficients but is less natural for clearly documenting decision paths across many classes than a decision tree."},{"id":"aif-q047","question":"A media platform plans to use a large language model for content moderation and wants to check its outputs for bias and unfair treatment of particular groups. Which data source allows this assessment with the least administrative effort?","options":{"A":"Moderation logs","B":"User-generated content","C":"Benchmark datasets","D":"Internal content moderation guidelines"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Benchmark datasets are standardized, curated datasets created specifically to evaluate models for bias and fairness, so they let the platform assess the model\'s outputs consistently with very little administrative effort.\\nUser-generated content and moderation logs would need substantial manual labeling and review and do not provide a consistent fairness baseline. Internal content moderation guidelines are policy documents, not a dataset that can be used to measure bias in outputs."},{"id":"aif-q049","question":"A lender is building a generative AI solution that offers discounts to new applicants based on business rules. To use the model responsibly and limit bias that could harm some customers, which two actions should the company take?","options":{"A":"Check the data for imbalances or disparities across groups","B":"Run the model as frequently as possible","C":"Evaluate the model\'s behavior so the company can offer transparency to stakeholders","D":"Use the ROUGE metric to measure the quality of generated text","E":"Keep the model\'s inference time within accepted limits"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Checking the data for imbalances or disparities helps surface and reduce bias that could unfairly affect some applicants, and evaluating the model\'s behavior lets the company explain its decisions and offer transparency to stakeholders. Both are core responsible AI practices for limiting bias.\\nRunning the model frequently and keeping inference time within limits are operational concerns that do not address bias. The ROUGE metric measures the overlap of generated text with reference text for summarization quality and does not detect bias."},{"id":"aif-q052","question":"A student submits essays by copying text produced by a generative AI tool without attribution. Which responsible AI challenge does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"Toxicity","B":"Hallucinations","C":"Privacy","D":"Plagiarism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Reusing generative AI output as one\'s own work without attribution is plagiarism, which is a recognized responsible AI and intellectual-property concern.\\nToxicity refers to harmful or offensive content, hallucinations are fabricated but plausible-sounding outputs, and privacy concerns the handling of personal data. None of these describes copying generated text and presenting it as original work."},{"id":"aif-q057","question":"A store installed cameras and uses an ML model to review the footage for possible theft. The model flags people from one ethnic group far more often than others. Which type of bias is influencing the model\'s output?","options":{"A":"Measurement bias","B":"Sampling bias","C":"Confirmation bias","D":"Observer bias"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sampling bias arises when the training data does not represent the full population, which can cause a model to flag one ethnic group far more often than others. The skewed flagging points to training data that under-represented some groups.\\nMeasurement bias comes from systematic errors in how data is recorded. Confirmation bias and observer bias relate to how people interpret or label results, not to the representativeness of the training sample."},{"id":"aif-q067","question":"A hospital put a disease-detection model into service on Amazon Bedrock. To respect privacy rules, the model must never expose personal patient details in its replies, and staff must be notified whenever that happens. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Use Amazon Macie to scan the model\'s output for sensitive data and set up alerts for potential exposure","B":"Use Amazon Bedrock Guardrails to filter the content and configure Amazon CloudWatch alarms for notifications","C":"Configure AWS CloudTrail to monitor the model\'s responses and create alerts for any detected personal data","D":"Use Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect data drift and alert when model quality degrades"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails can filter the model\'s output so personal patient information is not returned, and Amazon CloudWatch alarms can notify staff whenever a policy violation occurs.\\nAmazon Macie discovers sensitive data in Amazon S3 but does not filter a model\'s live responses. AWS CloudTrail logs API calls rather than inspecting response content. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor detects data drift and quality issues, not disclosure of personal information."},{"id":"aif-q071","question":"An insurance firm plans to deploy a large language model to automate document processing and wants to proceed responsibly to avoid harm. Which two actions should the firm take?","options":{"A":"Include fairness metrics when evaluating the model","B":"Adjust the model\'s temperature parameter","C":"Modify the training data to reduce bias","D":"Avoid overfitting on the training data","E":"Apply prompt engineering techniques"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Including fairness metrics when evaluating the model helps confirm it treats cases equitably, and modifying the training data to reduce bias addresses bias at its source. Both directly support responsible use and reduce potential harm.\\nAdjusting the temperature parameter and applying prompt engineering techniques tune output style and quality but do not address fairness or bias. Avoiding overfitting improves generalization but is a general model-quality goal rather than a measure that limits biased or harmful outcomes."},{"id":"aif-q091","question":"A research team builds custom ML models and shares the model artifacts with other teams, while keeping the training code and data. The team wants a standardized way to document and later audit each model it publishes. Which solution should the team use?","options":{"A":"Write the details in documents and store them in Amazon S3","B":"Commit the model training scripts to a Git repository","C":"Create Amazon SageMaker Model Cards that capture intended uses and training and inference details","D":"Use AWS AI Service Cards to describe the models"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards provide a standardized place to document a model\'s intended uses along with its training and inference details, giving the team a consistent, shareable record that other teams can later audit.\\nWriting the details in documents stored in Amazon S3 is unstructured and not standardized. Committing training scripts to a Git repository versions code rather than model metadata. AWS AI Service Cards describe AWS\'s own managed AI services, not custom models the team builds."},{"id":"aif-q094","question":"A bank wants to build an ML system to help its risk team decide loan allocations across different demographic groups. What should the bank do to develop an unbiased model?","options":{"A":"Reduce the size of the training dataset","B":"Make sure the model\'s predictions match historical results","C":"Build a separate model for each demographic group","D":"Measure class imbalance in the training data and adjust the training process accordingly"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Measuring class imbalance in the training data and adapting the training process, for example through resampling or class weights, helps the model treat under-represented groups equitably and reduces biased predictions.\\nReducing the dataset size removes information and can worsen bias. Forcing predictions to match historical results can carry past discrimination into the model. Building a separate model per demographic group can entrench disparities and does not address imbalance within the data."},{"id":"aif-q100","question":"A company is building a mobile app that uses a phone camera to assess insect bites. To train its image classification model, the company gathers a wide range of bite photos spanning different genders, ethnicities, and regions. Which responsible AI principle does this reflect?","options":{"A":"Explainability","B":"Transparency","C":"Fairness","D":"Governance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gathering bite photos across different genders, ethnicities, and regions is aimed at preventing the model from working better for some groups than others, which reflects the fairness principle of responsible AI.\\nExplainability and transparency concern revealing how the model reaches its outputs, and governance concerns policies and oversight. None of these describes the act of assembling a representative, balanced dataset to avoid biased outcomes."},{"id":"aif-q101","question":"A company is developing an ML model for loan approvals. It needs to detect bias in the model and also explain the model\'s predictions. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","B":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards","D":"AWS AI Service Cards"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify both detects bias in data and model predictions and provides explainability for those predictions, which covers the two requirements for the loan-approval model.\\nAmazon SageMaker Data Wrangler prepares and transforms data. Amazon SageMaker Model Cards document model details for transparency but do not run bias analysis. AWS AI Service Cards describe AWS managed AI services rather than a customer\'s own model."},{"id":"aif-q111","question":"A bank is building a loan-decision solution on a foundation model. For audit and oversight, the bank needs the model\'s decisions to be explainable. Which factor most affects how explainable those decisions are?","options":{"A":"Training time","B":"Model complexity","C":"Number of hyperparameters","D":"Deployment time"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Model complexity most affects explainability: simpler models such as linear models or decision trees are easier to interpret, while highly complex models like deep neural networks behave as black boxes whose decisions are hard to explain for audit purposes.\\nTraining time, the number of hyperparameters, and deployment time relate to how the model is built and operated, but they do not determine how interpretable its decisions are."},{"id":"aif-q113","question":"A company wants a lead-scoring application that lets employees see and adjust the weight given to each input variable based on their domain expertise. Which type of ML model supports this requirement?","options":{"A":"K-nearest neighbors (k-NN) model","B":"Logistic regression model","C":"Deep learning model built on principal components","D":"Neural network"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A logistic regression model assigns an explicit weight (coefficient) to each input variable, so employees can see how each feature contributes and adjust those weights using their domain expertise. This interpretability matches the requirement.\\nK-nearest neighbors makes predictions from neighboring data points and has no per-feature weights to adjust. A neural network and a deep learning model built on principal components are opaque and do not expose simple, adjustable feature weights."},{"id":"aif-q122","question":"Which technique can a company use to reduce bias and toxicity in a generative AI application during the postprocessing stage of the ML lifecycle?","options":{"A":"Data augmentation","B":"Human-in-the-loop","C":"Feature engineering","D":"Adversarial training"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Human-in-the-loop adds human review during the postprocessing stage so that biased or toxic outputs can be caught and corrected before they reach users, which reduces bias and toxicity in the generative AI application.\\nData augmentation and feature engineering act on data before or during training, and adversarial training hardens the model during training. None of these operates as a postprocessing safeguard on generated outputs."},{"id":"aif-q141","question":"What is a benefit of using Amazon SageMaker Model Cards to document AI models?","options":{"A":"Reducing a model\'s overall computational requirements","B":"Standardizing information about a model\'s purpose, performance, and limitations","C":"Providing a visually appealing summary of a model\'s capabilities","D":"Physically storing models for archival purposes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards standardize the documentation of a model\'s purpose, performance, and limitations, which supports transparency, governance, and easier tracking of models over time.\\nReducing computational requirements and physically storing models for archival are not functions of Model Cards. Providing a visually appealing summary is a presentation detail rather than the core benefit of standardized documentation."},{"id":"aif-q144","question":"A company built an AI resume-screening system trained on a large dataset of resumes that did not represent all demographic groups. Which core dimension of responsible AI does this situation involve?","options":{"A":"Transparency","B":"Fairness","C":"Privacy and security","D":"Explainability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Training on a dataset that does not represent all demographic groups can lead to biased predictions, which is a fairness concern. Fairness is the responsible AI dimension focused on equitable outcomes across groups.\\nTransparency and explainability concern revealing how the model makes decisions, and privacy and security concern protecting data. None of these describes the bias introduced by an unrepresentative training dataset."},{"id":"aif-q149","question":"A company deploys AI/ML models on AWS and wants to offer transparency into how the models make decisions and provide explanations for their outputs. Which AWS service or feature meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon Lex","B":"Amazon Rekognition","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards document key details such as a model\'s purpose, training data, performance, and limitations, which gives stakeholders transparency into how the model makes decisions and helps explain its outputs.\\nAmazon Lex builds conversational interfaces, Amazon Rekognition analyzes images and video, and Amazon Comprehend performs natural language processing. None of these is designed to document model transparency and explainability."},{"id":"aif-q165","question":"A company is collaborating with several research institutes to develop an AI model. It needs standardized documentation that tracks model versions and records how the model was developed. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Track model changes by using a Git repository","B":"Track model changes by using Amazon Comprehend","C":"Track model changes by using Amazon SageMaker Model Cards","D":"Track model changes by using Amazon S3"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards provide a standardized framework for documenting model details and versions along with a record of how the model was developed, which meets the need for shared, auditable documentation across collaborators.\\nA Git repository versions code rather than standardized model documentation. Amazon Comprehend performs natural language processing, and Amazon S3 stores objects without any model-documentation structure."},{"id":"aif-q172","question":"A hospital built an AI system that gives patients personalized treatment recommendations. The system must explain the reasoning behind each recommendation and make those insights accessible to both doctors and patients. Which human-centered design principle does this reflect?","options":{"A":"Data governance","B":"Privacy and security","C":"Explainability","D":"Fairness"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Explaining the reasoning behind each treatment recommendation and making those insights accessible to doctors and patients reflects explainability, a human-centered design principle that focuses on making AI decisions understandable.\\nPrivacy and security concern protecting patient data, fairness concerns equitable outcomes across groups, and data governance concerns policies for managing data. None of these describes revealing the rationale behind the system\'s recommendations."},{"id":"aif-q187","question":"A healthcare company wants to use generative AI to answer patients\' medical questions. Which AWS service should it use to support responsible AI for the application?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Amazon Rekognition","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails let the company define and enforce safety, content, and bias controls on generative AI responses, which supports responsible AI for an application that answers patients\' medical questions.\\nAmazon Inspector scans for software vulnerabilities, Amazon Rekognition analyzes images and video, and AWS Trusted Advisor checks accounts against best practices. None of these enforces responsible AI controls on generated content."},{"id":"aif-q203","question":"A retail company wants to build a product recommendation model using responsible practices. Which practice should it apply when collecting data to reduce bias in the model?","options":{"A":"Use data only from the company\'s most active customers","B":"Collect data from a single high-value customer segment","C":"Make sure the data is representative of the company\'s entire customer base","D":"Use the smallest dataset that lets the model train quickly"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Collecting data that is representative of the company\'s entire customer base reduces skew toward any single group and helps the recommendation model treat all customers equitably, which is the responsible practice for limiting bias.\\nUsing data only from the most active customers or from a single high-value segment produces a skewed dataset that increases bias. Using the smallest dataset that trains quickly risks under-representing groups and does not address fairness."},{"id":"aif-q207","question":"A company deployed a generative AI model for customer segmentation, and it has run in production for a long time. The team now notices inconsistent responses and wants to evaluate the model for bias and drift. Which AWS service or feature meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","B":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","D":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor continuously collects data from a deployed model and detects data drift, concept drift, and shifts in bias over time, so it can surface the inconsistencies appearing in the long-running segmentation model.\\nAmazon SageMaker Clarify measures bias and explainability at a point in time rather than continuously monitoring production. Amazon SageMaker Feature Store manages features, and Amazon SageMaker Model Cards document model details; neither monitors a model for drift."},{"id":"aif-q222","question":"A company is adding AI to its recruitment and hiring process and wants to reduce bias risks and keep hiring decisions equitable. Which two core dimensions of responsible AI should the company focus on?","options":{"A":"Fairness","B":"Flexibility","C":"Tolerance","D":"Open source","E":"Transparency"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Fairness keeps hiring outcomes equitable across demographic groups, which directly mitigates bias, and transparency makes the AI\'s decisions clear so they can be explained and audited. Both are core dimensions of responsible AI.\\nFlexibility, tolerance, and open source are not core responsible AI dimensions for ensuring equitable, accountable hiring decisions."},{"id":"aif-q225","question":"A food company is collecting a dataset to predict customer food preferences and wants the preferences of all demographic groups represented in the data. Which dataset characteristic does this describe?","options":{"A":"Accuracy","B":"Diversity","C":"Recency bias","D":"Reliability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Making sure the dataset represents all demographic groups addresses its diversity, so the model can learn from a wide range of customer preferences.\\nAccuracy and reliability concern the correctness and consistency of the data rather than how broadly it represents groups. Recency bias is a flaw in which recent data is over-weighted, which is not what including all demographics describes."},{"id":"aif-q231","question":"An AI practitioner is building an ML model and wants to give stakeholders transparency and explainability into its predictions. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Present the model\'s Shapley values","B":"Provide the model\'s accuracy measure","C":"Provide the model\'s confusion matrix","D":"Provide a secure model inference endpoint"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Shapley values quantify how much each feature contributes to an individual prediction, which gives stakeholders transparency and explainability into why the model produced a given output.\\nAccuracy and the confusion matrix measure overall model performance but do not explain individual predictions. A secure inference endpoint concerns how the model is deployed and accessed, not how its predictions are explained."},{"id":"aif-q252","question":"A bank is building a generative AI application for loan approval decisions and needs the outputs to be responsible and fair. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Use a deep learning model with many hidden layers","B":"Review the training data for bias and include data from all demographic groups","C":"Keep the model\'s decision-making process secret to protect proprietary algorithms","D":"Continuously monitor the model on a single static test dataset"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Reviewing the training data for bias and including data from all demographic groups reduces biased outcomes at the source, which is essential for a responsible and fair loan-approval application.\\nUsing a deep learning model with many hidden layers can reduce explainability and does not address fairness. Keeping the decision process secret undermines transparency. Monitoring on a single static test dataset does not detect or correct bias in the data."},{"id":"aif-q255","question":"A company has an ML model and wants to understand how the model arrives at its predictions. Which term describes understanding a model\'s predictions?","options":{"A":"Model interpretability","B":"Model training","C":"Model performance","D":"Model interoperability"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Model interpretability refers to understanding how a model arrives at its predictions, which is exactly what the company wants.\\nModel training is the process of fitting the model to data, model performance measures how well it predicts, and model interoperability concerns working across systems. None of these terms describes understanding the reasoning behind predictions."},{"id":"aif-q262","question":"A bank uses a generative AI model to set credit limits for new customers and wants to make the model\'s decision-making process more transparent to those customers. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Replace the ML model with a rule-based system","B":"Apply explainable AI techniques to show which factors influenced the model\'s decision","C":"Build an interactive UI that gives customers detailed technical explanations of the system","D":"Increase the model\'s accuracy to reduce the need for transparency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Applying explainable AI techniques surfaces the specific factors that influenced each credit-limit decision, making the generative AI model\'s reasoning transparent to customers.\\nReplacing the model with a rule-based system changes the approach instead of explaining the model. An interactive UI with detailed technical explanations does not necessarily reveal which factors drove a decision. Increasing accuracy improves predictions but does not make the decision process transparent."},{"id":"aif-q267","question":"An AI practitioner wants to evaluate ML models and provide explanations of their predictions to customers and stakeholders. Which AWS service or feature meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Quick","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify provides feature-attribution explanations for model predictions, so the practitioner can generate and share interpretability reports with customers and stakeholders.\\nAmazon Quick is a business intelligence service, Amazon Comprehend performs natural language processing, and AWS Trusted Advisor checks accounts against best practices. None of these explains ML model predictions."},{"id":"aif-q279","question":"A company uses Amazon SageMaker to deploy a model that detects whether social media posts contain certain topics. The company needs to show how different input features influence the model\'s behavior. Which SageMaker feature meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"SageMaker Ground Truth","B":"SageMaker Clarify","C":"SageMaker Feature Store","D":"SageMaker Canvas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify offers model explainability, including feature attribution, so it can show how different input features influence the deployed model\'s behavior.\\nSageMaker Ground Truth labels data, SageMaker Feature Store manages features, and SageMaker Canvas builds models without code. None of these explains how input features drive model behavior."},{"id":"aif-q287","question":"A company built an ML model that approves or rejects loan applications. To meet regulatory requirements, its decision-making must be transparent and explainable, and the company must document the process for audits. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon Textract","B":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards","C":"AWS CloudFormation","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards document a model\'s intended use, performance, and decision-making details, which provides the transparency, explainability, and audit record the loan model must meet for regulatory purposes.\\nAmazon Textract extracts text from documents, AWS CloudFormation provisions infrastructure, and Amazon Comprehend performs natural language processing. None of these documents a model\'s decision-making for transparency and audit."},{"id":"aif-q292","question":"Which two Amazon Bedrock features help with content filtering and safety?","options":{"A":"Guardrails for filtering content","B":"Model customization options","C":"Prompt management capabilities","D":"Watermark detection for AI-generated content","E":"Knowledge base integration"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails filter harmful or unwanted content in both prompts and responses, and watermark detection identifies AI-generated content, which supports transparency and helps prevent misuse. Together these two features address content filtering and safety.\\nModel customization, prompt management, and knowledge base integration improve a model\'s capability, reuse, and relevance, but none of them filters content or provides a safety control."},{"id":"aif-q053","question":"A pharmaceutical company will train its own large language model (LLM) on private data and wants to limit the carbon footprint of training. Which Amazon EC2 instance type has the LEAST environmental impact for training LLMs?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2 P series","B":"Amazon EC2 Trn series","C":"Amazon EC2 G series","D":"Amazon EC2 C series"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EC2 Trn series instances are powered by AWS Trainium, which is purpose-built for energy-efficient training of large models, so it has the least environmental impact of the listed options.\\nThe C series is general-purpose and compute-optimized CPU instances that are not specialized for efficient LLM training. The G and P series are GPU instances optimized for performance rather than energy efficiency, so they have a larger environmental footprint for this workload."},{"id":"aif-q160","question":"A gaming community platform uses Amazon Bedrock Guardrails to screen harmful user inputs and model outputs. Which two content categories can the guardrails filter?","options":{"A":"Politics","B":"Hate","C":"Gambling","D":"Violence"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails include configurable content filters for harmful categories such as Hate and Violence, which can be applied to both user inputs and model outputs.\\nPolitics and Gambling are not standard Bedrock Guardrails content-filter categories, so the guardrails do not filter by them."},{"id":"aif-q192","question":"A forum operator wants to stop users from posting discriminatory content and plans to use Amazon Bedrock. How can the operator use Amazon Bedrock to meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Restrict user conversations to a set of predefined topics","B":"Block interactions that relate to predefined disallowed topics","C":"Let users interact based on their personal preferences","D":"Offer a range of responses for users to choose from"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails let the operator define disallowed topics or categories such as discriminatory or hateful content and automatically block inputs and outputs that fall under them, which prevents that content from being posted.\\nRestricting conversations to predefined allowed topics is a different control and would limit normal use rather than block specific harmful content. Letting users interact by preference and offering a range of responses do nothing to filter discriminatory content."},{"id":"aif-q037","question":"A team trains a model that generates images of people in various jobs, but imbalanced data skews certain attributes and introduces bias. Which technique addresses this?","options":{"A":"Monitor the class distribution after deployment","B":"Augment the data to balance underrepresented classes","C":"Train for more epochs on the existing imbalanced data","D":"Apply stronger output filtering to the generated images"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Augmenting the data to balance underrepresented classes reduces the skew that causes the bias, addressing the problem at its source.\\nMonitoring only detects imbalance, training more epochs on the same imbalanced data reinforces the bias, and output filtering screens results without fixing the underlying dataset."},{"id":"aif-q054","question":"A company builds an app that creates children\'s stories with Amazon Bedrock and must keep the content appropriate for young users. Which feature fits?","options":{"A":"Agents for Amazon Bedrock","B":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Amazon Bedrock Prompt Management","D":"Amazon Bedrock playgrounds"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails apply content filters and topic restrictions to model output, keeping generated stories appropriate for children.\\nAgents orchestrate tasks, Prompt Management versions prompts, and playgrounds is an experimentation console; none enforces content safety."},{"id":"aif-q123","question":"An audit found that a bank\'s fine-tuned loan-approval model favors one demographic group. What is the MOST cost-effective fix?","options":{"A":"Use Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) with the fine-tuned model","B":"Add more diverse, representative training data and fine-tune again","C":"Pre-train a brand-new model on more diverse data","D":"Apply Amazon Bedrock Guardrails to block biased outputs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Adding more diverse, representative data and fine-tuning the existing model again teaches it to treat groups fairly, which is the most cost-effective fix.\\nRAG adds external context but does not correct learned bias, pre-training a new model is far more expensive, and Guardrails filter unsafe content rather than addressing demographic bias in the decisions."},{"id":"aif-q169","question":"A Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) newsletter app on Amazon Bedrock is surfacing politically slanted content. Which Amazon Bedrock Guardrails feature can filter this?","options":{"A":"Word filters","B":"Content filters","C":"Denied topics","D":"Sensitive information filters"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Denied topics let you define subjects, such as politics, that the model must avoid, and Amazon Bedrock enforces this during generation to filter out politically slanted content.\\nWord filters block only specific terms, content filters target categories like hate or violence, and sensitive information filters redact data such as PII."},{"id":"aif-q249","question":"An online store\'s chatbot must filter harmful content from both user prompts and its responses. Which Amazon Bedrock feature fits?","options":{"A":"Amazon Bedrock Agents","B":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases","D":"Amazon Bedrock custom models"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails filter harmful or inappropriate content in both input prompts and model responses, providing the safeguards the chatbot needs.\\nAmazon Bedrock Agents orchestrate tasks, Knowledge Bases retrieve context, and custom models customize behavior; none filters harmful content."},{"id":"aif-q297","question":"A company uses large language models (LLMs) for tutoring apps and needs configurable safeguards that enforce standard safety rules with the LEAST effort. Which solution fits?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","B":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation","D":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails provide configurable safeguards that enforce safety rules on LLM inputs and outputs with little setup effort.\\nSageMaker Clarify detects bias, Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation scores models, and SageMaker JumpStart offers pre-trained models; none enforces configurable safety rules."},{"id":"aif-q311","question":"A model performs well on training data but poorly on evaluation data. What is the most likely cause?","options":{"A":"The model is underfit","B":"The model is overfit","C":"There is data leakage between the train and test sets","D":"The model needs more training epochs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Overfitting occurs when a model learns the training data too closely, including noise, so it performs well on training data but fails to generalize to unseen evaluation data.\\nAn underfit model performs poorly even on training data, data leakage tends to inflate test performance rather than depress it, and adding more epochs would worsen overfitting rather than explain it."},{"id":"aif-q039","question":"A bank is adapting a foundation model (FM) to support its lending decisions. Regulators require the model to be transparent and explainable. Which solution will meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Configure security and compliance with Amazon Inspector","B":"Secure and encrypt the training data with Amazon Macie","C":"Produce metrics, reports, and example explanations with Amazon SageMaker Clarify","D":"Collect more data and add custom labels with Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify supports transparency and explainability by producing metrics, reports, and example explanations that show how a model reaches its decisions, which helps satisfy regulatory requirements.\\nAmazon Inspector scans for software vulnerabilities and Amazon Macie protects sensitive data, neither of which explains model behavior. Collecting more data and adding labels with Amazon Rekognition addresses image labeling, not model explainability."},{"id":"aif-q060","question":"A company has built a chatbot that answers natural-language questions by returning images. It wants to make sure the chatbot never returns inappropriate or unwanted images. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Retrain the model on a large public dataset","B":"Automate the collection of user feedback","C":"Integrate content moderation APIs","D":"Carry out model validation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Content moderation APIs automatically scan the images the chatbot would return, then detect and block inappropriate, unsafe, or unwanted content before it reaches the user, so integrating them directly enforces the requirement that the chatbot never serves such images.\\nRetraining the model on a large public dataset adds no filtering and could even introduce more unwanted content. Carrying out model validation checks that the model behaves as expected during development but does not screen individual responses at runtime. Automating the collection of user feedback gathers input to improve the system over time but does nothing to block inappropriate images as they are served."},{"id":"aif-q171","question":"An electronics retailer has built an AI assistant that answers customer questions using its product manuals. Which approach is most likely to increase customers\' trust in the assistant\'s answers?","options":{"A":"Displaying the model\'s confidence score with each answer","B":"Giving the assistant a robotic on-screen avatar","C":"Linking to the product manual sections that support each answer","D":"Tuning the assistant to match the brand\'s tone of voice"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Linking to the specific product manual sections that support each answer lets customers verify the information for themselves, which builds trust by grounding the response in a citable, authoritative source rather than relying on the model\'s output alone.\\nDisplaying the model\'s confidence score exposes an internal value that customers are not equipped to interpret, and a high score on a wrong answer would mislead rather than reassure. Giving the assistant a robotic on-screen avatar is a presentation choice that signals the agent is not human but says nothing about the accuracy of its answers. Tuning the assistant to match the brand\'s tone of voice improves consistency and style but gives customers no way to confirm that the information itself is correct."},{"id":"aif-q389","question":"A publishing company uses a generative AI model to create illustrations for its books. The company discovers that some generated images closely resemble copyrighted artwork from other publishers. Which responsible AI concern does this represent?","options":{"A":"Model hallucination","B":"Intellectual property infringement","C":"Data drift","D":"Overfitting"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When a generative AI model produces outputs that closely resemble copyrighted works, it raises the risk of intellectual property infringement, which is a legal risk of working with generative AI that companies must address through proper data curation and output review.\\nModel hallucination refers to generating plausible but factually incorrect content, not reproducing existing copyrighted material. Data drift describes changes in input data patterns over time. Overfitting means a model memorizes training data too closely and generalizes poorly, which is a different technical problem from IP infringement."},{"id":"aif-q390","question":"A bank deploys a generative AI chatbot that provides incorrect financial guidance to several customers, leading to complaints and negative press coverage. Which legal risk of generative AI is the bank experiencing?","options":{"A":"Sampling bias in training data","B":"Loss of customer trust","C":"Model underfitting","D":"Increased inference latency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loss of customer trust is a recognized legal risk of deploying generative AI, where inaccurate or harmful outputs erode confidence in the organization, lead to customer complaints, and can result in reputational damage and regulatory scrutiny.\\nSampling bias is a data quality issue that can cause unfair predictions but is not the legal risk being experienced here. Model underfitting means the model is too simple to learn patterns, which is a technical training issue. Increased inference latency is a performance concern unrelated to customer trust or legal risk."},{"id":"aif-q391","question":"A healthcare organization uses an ML model to flag abnormal lab results. To ensure responsible AI practices, it wants medical professionals to review any prediction the model is uncertain about before the result is sent to patients. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","B":"Amazon Augmented AI (Amazon A2I)","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","D":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Augmented AI (Amazon A2I) enables human review workflows where predictions below a confidence threshold are routed to qualified reviewers, so medical professionals can verify uncertain results before they reach patients, supporting responsible AI through human oversight.\\nAmazon SageMaker Clarify detects bias and explains predictions but does not route uncertain predictions to human reviewers. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor detects drift in deployed models rather than enabling human review of individual predictions. Amazon Bedrock Guardrails filter content from generative AI models but do not create human review loops for traditional ML predictions."},{"id":"aif-q392","question":"Which of the following is a legal risk that organizations should consider when deploying generative AI applications to customers?","options":{"A":"Reduced model training time","B":"Increased hardware utilization","C":"Potential harm to end users from inaccurate or biased outputs","D":"Lower cloud computing costs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Potential harm to end users from inaccurate or biased outputs is a legal risk organizations must consider, as customers who receive incorrect advice, biased decisions, or harmful content may suffer real consequences, leading to liability, regulatory penalties, and litigation.\\nReduced model training time, increased hardware utilization, and lower cloud computing costs are operational benefits or metrics that do not represent legal risks to end users."},{"id":"aif-q454","question":"A company is choosing between several foundation models and wants to minimize the environmental impact of its AI workloads. Which responsible AI practice should guide this decision?","options":{"A":"Choose the largest model available to maximize accuracy","B":"Select a model sized appropriately for the task to reduce unnecessary compute and energy consumption","C":"Always use the most expensive model to ensure quality","D":"Run inference continuously to keep the endpoint warm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Selecting a model appropriately sized for the task avoids wasting compute resources and energy on unnecessary capacity, which directly reduces the environmental footprint of AI workloads and reflects responsible model selection practices focused on sustainability.\\nChoosing the largest model wastes resources when a smaller one suffices. Using the most expensive model does not inherently reduce environmental impact. Running inference continuously wastes energy when demand is intermittent."},{"id":"aif-q455","question":"A company is deciding between a deep neural network and a decision tree for a customer-facing application. The deep neural network is more accurate, but the company needs to explain every decision to regulators. Which tradeoff does this represent?","options":{"A":"Cost vs latency","B":"Interpretability vs performance","C":"Training time vs accuracy","D":"Data volume vs model size"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The interpretability-versus-performance tradeoff means more complex models (like deep neural networks) often achieve higher accuracy but are harder to explain, while simpler models (like decision trees) are easy to interpret but may sacrifice some performance, which is exactly the tension the company faces.\\nCost vs latency, training time vs accuracy, and data volume vs model size are different tradeoffs that do not describe the tension between explanation capability and prediction accuracy."},{"id":"aif-q009","question":"A startup runs a chatbot built on an Amazon Bedrock foundation model (FM). The chatbot needs to read files in an Amazon S3 bucket that is protected with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3), yet every attempt to read them fails. What will resolve the issue?","options":{"A":"Use prompt engineering to tell the model where to look for the data in Amazon S3","B":"Strip any sensitive details out of the S3 data","C":"Grant the IAM role that Amazon Bedrock assumes permission to decrypt the data with the correct encryption key","D":"Update the S3 bucket policy to allow public internet access to the data"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The read failures are a permissions problem: the IAM role that Amazon Bedrock assumes must be allowed to access and decrypt the encrypted objects, so granting it permission to decrypt the data with the correct key resolves the access failure.\\nPrompt engineering cannot grant access to encrypted data. Removing sensitive data does not address the missing decrypt permission and defeats the purpose of using the data. Opening the bucket to the public internet is an insecure change that still does not give the role decrypt permission."},{"id":"aif-q013","question":"A bank is building an AI application on Amazon Bedrock. The application sits in a VPC that, to satisfy regulators, must be cut off from all internet traffic. Which AWS service or feature meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Internet gateway","B":"Amazon Macie","C":"AWS PrivateLink","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS PrivateLink creates private connectivity between a VPC and AWS services such as Amazon Bedrock, so traffic stays on the AWS network and never reaches the public internet, which satisfies the no-internet compliance requirement.\\nAn internet gateway does the opposite of what is required by giving the VPC a path to the public internet. Amazon Macie discovers and protects sensitive data in Amazon S3 and does not provide private connectivity. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that serves content over the internet."},{"id":"aif-q026","question":"A security company invokes foundation models (FMs) on Amazon Bedrock and wants to limit use to approved users only. Before tightening its IAM roles and policies later, it first needs to spot any attempts to reach the models without authorization. Which AWS service should it use?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"Amazon Inspector","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Audit Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail records API activity across AWS services, so it captures who attempted to call Amazon Bedrock and when, letting the company identify unauthorized access attempts and refine its IAM roles and policies.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor gives best-practice recommendations on cost, performance, and security but does not log individual access attempts. Amazon Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities. AWS Audit Manager automates evidence collection for audits rather than tracking who tried to access a service."},{"id":"aif-q028","question":"A company regularly audits its operations alongside independent software vendors (ISVs) and wants an email alert each time a new ISV compliance report is published. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"AWS Data Exchange","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Artifact","D":"AWS Audit Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Artifact provides on-demand access to compliance reports and agreements, including third-party reports, and can notify the company when a new report becomes available.\\nAWS Data Exchange is a marketplace for third-party datasets, not compliance reports. AWS Trusted Advisor offers best-practice checks. AWS Audit Manager automates evidence collection for the company\'s own audits rather than distributing ISV reports."},{"id":"aif-q030","question":"An insurer is mapping its generative AI projects onto the Generative AI Security Scoping Matrix. Which scope leaves the insurer with the MOST security responsibility?","options":{"A":"Building and training a generative AI model from scratch using data the company owns","B":"Fine-tuning an existing third-party foundation model (FM) with the company\'s own data","C":"Building an application on top of an existing third-party foundation model (FM)","D":"Using a third-party enterprise application that has generative AI features built in"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Building and training a model from scratch on data the company owns gives the company full control over the data, training, deployment, and safeguards, so it carries the most security responsibility.\\nFine-tuning a third-party FM, building an application on a third-party FM, and using a vendor application with embedded generative AI each shift increasing portions of responsibility to the provider, so they grant the company less ownership."},{"id":"aif-q061","question":"An AI practitioner relies on an Amazon Bedrock base model to summarize support chats and wants to keep a record of what goes into and comes out of the model for monitoring. Which approach should the practitioner take?","options":{"A":"Set AWS CloudTrail as the model\'s log destination","B":"Configure model invocation logging in Amazon EventBridge","C":"Turn on model invocation logging in Amazon Bedrock","D":"Set AWS Audit Manager as the model\'s log destination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Turning on model invocation logging in Amazon Bedrock captures and stores the model\'s input and output data, which is exactly what the practitioner needs to monitor invocations.\\nAWS CloudTrail records management API calls but not the model\'s prompt and response payloads. Amazon EventBridge routes events between services and is not where Bedrock invocation logging is enabled. AWS Audit Manager collects audit evidence rather than invocation data."},{"id":"aif-q066","question":"A company plans to build a large language model (LLM) application with Amazon Bedrock using customer data held in Amazon S3. Its policy says each team may reach only the records of its own customers. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Create a single Amazon Bedrock service role with full Amazon S3 access, then create IAM roles per team scoped to each team\'s customer folders","B":"Create an Amazon Bedrock custom service role for each team that grants access only to that team\'s customer data","C":"Create one service role with Amazon S3 access and require teams to name the customer on each Amazon Bedrock request","D":"Redact personal data in Amazon S3 and update the bucket policy to allow team access to customer data"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Creating a separate Amazon Bedrock custom service role for each team, scoped to only that team\'s customer data, enforces the policy through least-privilege access and prevents teams from reaching other teams\' data.\\nA single role with full Amazon S3 access plus per-team IAM roles still grants the shared Bedrock role broad access, risking exposure. A single role that depends on teams naming the customer relies on cooperation rather than enforced controls. Redacting data and opening the bucket policy does not segregate access by team."},{"id":"aif-q078","question":"An AI practitioner fine-tuned a custom model on Amazon Bedrock using a dataset that contained confidential records. The practitioner must guarantee that the model\'s inference responses are never based on that confidential data. What should the practitioner do?","options":{"A":"Mask the confidential data in the inference responses with dynamic data masking","B":"Encrypt the confidential data in the inference responses with Amazon SageMaker AI","C":"Delete the custom model, remove the confidential data from the training dataset, and retrain the model","D":"Encrypt the confidential data in the custom model with AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because the confidential data was part of the training set, the only reliable fix is to delete the custom model, remove the confidential data from the dataset, and retrain so the model never learned patterns from that data.\\nDynamic data masking and encrypting responses act after the model has already learned from the confidential data, so the information can still influence outputs. Encrypting the data in the model with AWS KMS protects it at rest but does not stop the model from generating responses based on it."},{"id":"aif-q084","question":"A team builds and trains ML models in Amazon SageMaker Studio notebooks, keeping its data in an Amazon S3 bucket. It needs to control how data moves between Amazon S3 and the notebooks. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use Amazon Macie to monitor SageMaker Studio","B":"Configure SageMaker to use a VPC with an Amazon S3 endpoint","C":"Use Amazon Inspector to monitor SageMaker Studio","D":"Configure SageMaker to use S3 Glacier Deep Archive"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Configuring Amazon SageMaker to use a VPC with an Amazon S3 endpoint creates a managed, private path for data to flow between Amazon S3 and the Studio notebooks without traversing the public internet.\\nAmazon Macie and Amazon Inspector monitor for sensitive data and vulnerabilities respectively, but neither manages the data path between services. Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive is a low-cost archival storage class and does not control data flow."},{"id":"aif-q087","question":"A company is about to adopt Amazon Bedrock and needs to know which security duties stay with the company under the AWS shared responsibility model. Which duty belongs to the company?","options":{"A":"Protecting the infrastructure that hosts Amazon Bedrock","B":"Patching and updating the versions of Amazon Bedrock","C":"Provisioning Amazon Bedrock within the company network","D":"Securing the company\'s data in transit and at rest"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, AWS secures the cloud infrastructure and managed services such as Amazon Bedrock, while the customer is responsible for security in the cloud, including protecting its own data in transit and at rest.\\nPatching Amazon Bedrock versions and protecting the hosting infrastructure are AWS responsibilities for a managed service. Provisioning Amazon Bedrock within the company network is not how the managed service is accessed and is not a customer security responsibility."},{"id":"aif-q090","question":"To satisfy regulators, a company must run its Amazon SageMaker AI training and inference in a sealed environment with no internet connectivity. Which solution will meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Associate appropriate IAM roles with the SageMaker AI jobs","B":"Encrypt the data at rest using encryption for SageMaker geospatial capabilities","C":"Run the SageMaker AI training and inference jobs with network isolation","D":"Run the SageMaker AI training and inference jobs with SageMaker Experiments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Running the Amazon SageMaker AI training and inference jobs with network isolation removes internet access for those jobs, keeping them in the isolated environment that the regulations require.\\nAssociating IAM roles controls permissions but does not remove internet connectivity. Encryption for geospatial capabilities protects data at rest, and SageMaker Experiments tracks training runs; neither isolates the job from the internet."},{"id":"aif-q104","question":"A company runs custom models on Amazon Bedrock for a generative AI application and wants the model artifacts produced during customization jobs encrypted with a key the company controls. Which AWS service meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon Macie","B":"AWS Secrets Manager","C":"AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) lets the company create and control its own encryption key and use it to encrypt the model artifacts produced by Amazon Bedrock customization jobs.\\nAmazon Macie discovers sensitive data and Amazon Inspector scans for vulnerabilities, neither of which manages customer encryption keys. AWS Secrets Manager stores credentials and secrets, not the encryption keys used to protect model artifacts."},{"id":"aif-q124","question":"A company must record every call to its Amazon Bedrock API and keep those records safely for five years as cheaply as possible. Which two choices (one AWS service and one S3 storage class) should it combine to meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering","D":"AWS Audit Manager","E":"Amazon S3 Standard"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS CloudTrail is designed to log API requests made to AWS services, including Amazon Bedrock, and Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves the stored logs between access tiers to keep long-term retention costs low, so together they meet the logging and five-year, low-cost retention requirements.\\nAmazon CloudWatch focuses on metrics and operational monitoring rather than recording API calls, and AWS Audit Manager collects audit evidence rather than logging every API request. Amazon S3 Standard costs more than Intelligent-Tiering for data that is rarely accessed over a long retention period."},{"id":"aif-q126","question":"A hospital is building an AI system that helps doctors diagnose illnesses from patient files and medical images. By law, the sensitive patient data must stay within the country where it is held. Which data governance strategy ensures compliance and protects privacy?","options":{"A":"Data quality","B":"Data enrichment","C":"Data residency","D":"Data discoverability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Data residency ensures data is stored and processed only within a specific geographic location, which directly meets the requirement that the patient data never leaves the country and protects patient privacy.\\nData quality concerns the accuracy and completeness of data, data enrichment adds information to existing records, and data discoverability helps users find data; none of these controls where the data is physically stored."},{"id":"aif-q139","question":"A company will move customer service emails into Amazon S3 for an analytics application. Because some emails hold sensitive data, the company wants an alert whenever such data appears. Which solution fully automates this detection with the LEAST development effort?","options":{"A":"Build several custom regex patterns and run them in a custom application to detect sensitive data","B":"Configure Amazon Macie to detect sensitive information in the documents uploaded to Amazon S3","C":"Deploy a large language model (LLM) on Amazon SageMaker endpoints to redact sensitive data","D":"Ask customers not to share sensitive information in their emails"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Macie is a managed service that uses machine learning to automatically discover sensitive data such as PII in Amazon S3 and can raise alerts when it is found, meeting the requirement with the least development effort.\\nBuilding custom regex patterns in a custom application and deploying an LLM on Amazon SageMaker endpoints both require significant development and maintenance. Asking customers not to share sensitive information does not detect or alert on anything."},{"id":"aif-q145","question":"A global bank created an ML application that analyzes market data to surface trends. Throughout development, the bank wants ongoing checks that its policies and industry regulations are being followed. Which two AWS services help the bank assess compliance?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Audit Manager","E":"Amazon Inspector"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Audit Manager continuously collects evidence and maps it to regulatory frameworks to assess compliance, and AWS Config tracks configuration changes against rules so the bank can confirm its resources stay within policy throughout development.\\nAmazon CloudWatch monitors metrics and logs, AWS CloudTrail records API activity, and Amazon Inspector scans for software vulnerabilities; these support security and operations but are not the primary services for assessing compliance against policies and regulations."},{"id":"aif-q151","question":"Which AWS feature offers a consistent way to document the details of ML models for governance and reporting?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","B":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards","D":"Amazon SageMaker Model Registry"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards provide a standardized record of a model\'s intended use, training data, evaluation results, and compliance details, supporting governance and reporting throughout the model\'s lifecycle.\\nAmazon SageMaker Model Monitor watches deployed models for drift, Amazon SageMaker JumpStart provides pre-built models and solutions, and Amazon SageMaker Model Registry stores and versions models; none of these is the standardized documentation record used for governance and reporting."},{"id":"aif-q179","question":"When models are trained on AWS, which machine learning technique helps keep the underlying training data private and compliant?","options":{"A":"Transfer learning","B":"Unsupervised learning","C":"Federated learning","D":"Reinforcement learning"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Federated learning trains a shared model across multiple data holders without moving the raw data to a central location, so each participant keeps its private data in place, which helps preserve privacy and meet compliance requirements.\\nTransfer learning, unsupervised learning, and reinforcement learning are approaches defined by how a model learns from data, not techniques designed to keep training data private and compliant."},{"id":"aif-q185","question":"A hospital wants to fine-tune a foundation model (FM) with AWS services while keeping its data private and inside the AWS Region where it is stored. Which two steps achieve this MOST cost-effectively?","options":{"A":"Host the model on premises with AWS Outposts","B":"Use AWS PrivateLink with a VPC","C":"Host the Amazon Bedrock API on premises","D":"Use the Amazon Bedrock API"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Calling the Amazon Bedrock API keeps fine-tuning within the managed service in the same AWS Region, and routing that traffic through a VPC with AWS PrivateLink keeps it on the AWS private network, meeting the privacy requirement cost-effectively without extra infrastructure.\\nHosting the model on premises with AWS Outposts and hosting the Amazon Bedrock API on premises both add hardware and operational cost and are not how the managed Amazon Bedrock service is consumed."},{"id":"aif-q186","question":"A bank running its generative AI models on AWS must produce reports that prove it meets international rules for handling sensitive customer data. Which AWS service meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon Macie","C":"AWS Artifact","D":"AWS Secrets Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Artifact provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports and certifications such as ISO and SOC, which the bank can use to demonstrate that its workloads and data handling meet international regulatory requirements.\\nAWS Config tracks resource configuration, Amazon Macie discovers sensitive data, and AWS Secrets Manager stores credentials; none of these produces the compliance reports needed to prove regulatory adherence."},{"id":"aif-q208","question":"A company with Amazon Bedrock access wants to control which models specific employees are allowed to use. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Use AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) service roles to restrict model subscription","B":"Use AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies to restrict model access","C":"Use Amazon Inspector to monitor model access","D":"Use AWS Security Token Service (AWS STS) to generate temporary credentials for model use"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies can allow or deny Amazon Bedrock API actions scoped to specific model resources, so the company can restrict which models each employee is allowed to invoke.\\nIAM service roles grant permissions to services rather than restricting which models a user can subscribe to, AWS STS issues temporary credentials but does not by itself limit model access, and Amazon Inspector scans for vulnerabilities rather than controlling access."},{"id":"aif-q215","question":"A company analyzes confidential documents with a third-party model on Amazon Bedrock and is worried about data privacy. Which statement explains how Amazon Bedrock keeps that data private?","options":{"A":"User inputs are kept confidential, but model outputs are shared with the third-party model providers","B":"User inputs and model outputs are anonymized and then shared with the third-party model providers","C":"User inputs and model outputs are not shared with any third-party model providers","D":"User inputs and model outputs are redacted before they are shared with the third-party model providers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock processes data within the AWS environment and does not share user inputs or model outputs with the third-party model providers, so the company\'s confidential documents stay private.\\nThe options stating that outputs are shared, that inputs and outputs are anonymized and shared, or that data is redacted before sharing are all incorrect because Amazon Bedrock does not send the inputs or outputs to the model providers at all."},{"id":"aif-q229","question":"A bank needs its generative AI chatbot to stay factual for regulatory reasons. Which solution helps stop the underlying foundation model (FM) from producing hallucinated answers?","options":{"A":"Use AWS Config to query compliance metadata using natural language","B":"Use AWS Audit Manager to prepare IT audit and compliance reports","C":"Configure Amazon Bedrock Guardrails to evaluate user inputs and model responses","D":"Use Amazon Inspector to scan workloads for vulnerabilities"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails lets the bank define policies that evaluate user inputs and model responses, including contextual grounding checks, helping keep the chatbot\'s answers factual and reducing hallucinated content for compliance.\\nAWS Config tracks resource configuration, AWS Audit Manager prepares audit and compliance reports, and Amazon Inspector scans workloads for vulnerabilities; none of these constrains a model\'s generated responses."},{"id":"aif-q235","question":"A healthcare company plans to fine-tune a model on Amazon Bedrock using sensitive data held in private databases inside its VPC. The data must never leave the company\'s private network. Which solution meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Restrict access to Amazon Bedrock by using AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies","B":"Use AWS PrivateLink to connect the VPC and Amazon Bedrock","C":"Use AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) keys to encrypt the data","D":"Route traffic to Amazon Bedrock through an internet gateway in the VPC"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS PrivateLink establishes a private connection between the VPC and Amazon Bedrock, so traffic travels over the AWS private network instead of the public internet, keeping the sensitive data inside the company\'s private network during fine-tuning.\\nIAM policies control who is allowed to call Amazon Bedrock but do not keep the network traffic off the public internet. AWS KMS encrypts data at rest and does not change the network path the data takes. An internet gateway routes VPC traffic to the public internet, which is the opposite of keeping the data on a private network."},{"id":"aif-q248","question":"A company with offices around the world requires that every API call between its generative AI applications and the foundation models (FMs) avoid the public internet. Which AWS service should it use?","options":{"A":"Amazon Q","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS PrivateLink","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS PrivateLink provides private connectivity between VPCs and AWS services, so API calls between the generative AI applications and the foundation models stay on the AWS network and off the public internet.\\nAmazon Q is a generative AI assistant, AWS CloudTrail logs API activity, and Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that serves traffic over the internet; none of these keeps API traffic off the public internet."},{"id":"aif-q269","question":"A company wants to reach Amazon Bedrock APIs privately from within its AWS account while keeping its data away from the public internet. Which solution meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Use AWS Glue to set up data encryption across the company\'s data catalog","B":"Use AWS PrivateLink to configure a private connection between the company\'s VPC and Amazon Bedrock","C":"Use Amazon CloudFront to restrict access to the company\'s private content","D":"Use AWS Lake Formation to manage centralized data governance and cross-account data sharing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS PrivateLink configures a private connection between the company\'s VPC and Amazon Bedrock APIs, so calls and data transfers stay within the AWS network and are not exposed to the public internet.\\nAWS Glue handles data integration and cataloging, Amazon CloudFront restricts and delivers content over the internet, and AWS Lake Formation manages data governance and sharing; none of these creates a private network connection to Amazon Bedrock."},{"id":"aif-q305","question":"A company acquires International Organization for Standardization (ISO) accreditation to manage AI risks and to use AI responsibly. What does this accreditation reflect about the company?","options":{"A":"All members of the company are ISO certified.","B":"All AI systems that the company uses are ISO certified.","C":"All AI application team members are ISO certified.","D":"The company\'s development framework is ISO certified."},"correct":"D","explanation":"ISO accreditation certifies that the company\'s development framework, processes, and controls for managing AI meet the requirements of the relevant ISO standard, reflecting that the organization has demonstrated responsible and risk-aware AI governance practices at a structural level.\\nAll members of the company being ISO certified is incorrect because organizational accreditation does not automatically certify every individual employee; personal ISO certification is a separate process that individuals undertake independently. All AI systems being ISO certified is incorrect because the accreditation applies to the framework and processes used to develop and govern AI, not to the individual systems or models produced within it. All AI application team members being ISO certified is incorrect for the same reason, as individual employees require separate certification and are not automatically certified by the organization achieving accreditation."},{"id":"aif-q307","question":"A company keeps rules that set how long information is stored and when it should be removed. Which data governance strategy does this describe?","options":{"A":"Data quality standards","B":"Data retention","C":"Log storage","D":"Data de-identification"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Data retention is the set of policies that define how long data is stored and when it must be deleted, which is exactly what the company\'s guidelines describe.\\nData quality standards govern accuracy and completeness, log storage concerns where logs are kept, and data de-identification removes identifying details; none of these defines storage duration and deletion timing."},{"id":"aif-q040","question":"A chatbot built on a fine-tuned Amazon SageMaker JumpStart model must meet several regulatory frameworks. Which two capabilities help the company demonstrate compliance?","options":{"A":"Cost optimization","B":"Data protection","C":"Elastic auto scaling","D":"Threat detection","E":"Faster time to market"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Data protection (safeguarding sensitive data) and threat detection (identifying security risks) are security controls that regulators expect, so they directly support demonstrating compliance.\\nCost optimization, elastic auto scaling, and faster time to market are performance and business concerns that do not establish regulatory compliance."},{"id":"aif-q213","question":"A bank fine-tunes an LLM on Amazon Bedrock for loan questions and must ensure the model never reveals private customer data. Which solution fits?","options":{"A":"Increase the Top K parameter","B":"Use Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Remove personally identifiable information (PII) from the data before fine-tuning","D":"Encrypt the data in Amazon S3 before fine-tuning"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Removing PII before fine-tuning prevents the model from ever ingesting private details, so it cannot reproduce them later, which is the most reliable safeguard.\\nEncrypting the data in Amazon S3 protects it at rest but the model still learns the PII, Amazon Bedrock Guardrails filter outputs after the data has been ingested, and Top K only affects token sampling."},{"id":"aif-q218","question":"A company wants to control which publicly available foundation models (FMs) its employees can access. Which solution fits?","options":{"A":"Analyze usage in AWS Cost Explorer","B":"Configure Amazon SageMaker JumpStart to restrict which FMs are discoverable","C":"Download compliance reports from AWS Artifact","D":"Review recommendations in AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart lets administrators curate the model catalog, so configuring it to restrict which FMs are discoverable controls the models employees can access.\\nAWS Cost Explorer analyzes spending, AWS Artifact provides compliance documents, and AWS Trusted Advisor gives best-practice recommendations; none governs which models employees can access."},{"id":"aif-q299","question":"A generative AI chatbot on an Amazon Bedrock foundation model (FM) is vulnerable to prompt injection. How can the company secure it with the LEAST effort?","options":{"A":"Fine-tune the FM to avoid harmful responses","B":"Apply Amazon Bedrock Guardrails content filters and denied topics","C":"Switch to a different foundation model","D":"Add more few-shot examples to the prompt"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails content filters and denied topics can be configured without retraining to block unsafe prompts and responses, mitigating prompt injection with the least effort.\\nFine-tuning needs data and a training run, switching FMs does not inherently stop injection, and adding few-shot examples does not defend against malicious prompts."},{"id":"aif-q034","question":"How can a company use large language models securely on Amazon Bedrock?","options":{"A":"Configure IAM roles and policies based on least privilege","B":"Enable AWS Audit Manager to run automatic model evaluation jobs","C":"Enable Amazon Bedrock automatic model evaluation jobs","D":"Use Amazon CloudWatch Logs to make models explainable and monitor for bias"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Configuring IAM roles and policies based on least privilege restricts which users and applications can access the Amazon Bedrock models and actions, which is the foundational way to use large language models securely.\\nAWS Audit Manager collects evidence for compliance and does not run model evaluation jobs. Amazon Bedrock model evaluation assesses model performance rather than securing access. Amazon CloudWatch Logs records activity but does not make models explainable or detect bias."},{"id":"aif-q133","question":"Which option describes a characteristic of AI governance frameworks that help build trust and deploy human-centered AI?","options":{"A":"Expanding AI initiatives across business units to maximize long-term value","B":"Aligning AI projects with revenue goals and stakeholder expectations","C":"Driving business transformation and competitive growth through AI","D":"Developing policies and guidelines for data, transparency, responsible AI, and compliance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AI governance frameworks are characterized by developing policies and guidelines covering data, transparency, responsible AI, and compliance, which builds trust and supports human-centered AI.\\nExpanding initiatives across business units, aligning with revenue goals and stakeholder expectations, and driving business transformation are general business aims, not defining characteristics of an AI governance framework."},{"id":"aif-q159","question":"A company uses AI to generate customer credit scores during loan applications and plans to expand into a new geographic market. To operate there, which type of compliance laws should the company review?","options":{"A":"Local health data protection laws","B":"Local payment card data protection laws","C":"Local algorithm accountability laws","D":"Local education privacy laws"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because automated credit scoring makes decisions about individuals, the company must review local algorithm accountability laws, which govern the fairness, transparency, and impact of automated decision-making in the new market.\\nLocal health data, payment card, and education privacy laws apply to other types of data and use cases, not to the accountability of automated credit-scoring decisions."},{"id":"aif-q191","question":"An ecommerce company runs an AI chatbot on its website that lets customers submit orders around the clock. Which input vulnerability should the company address before launching the chatbot?","options":{"A":"Data leakage","B":"Prompt injection","C":"Large language model (LLM) hallucinations","D":"Concept drift"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Prompt injection is an input vulnerability in which a user crafts malicious input to override or manipulate the chatbot\'s instructions, so the company must sanitize and constrain inputs before launch.\\nData leakage is the exposure of sensitive information, large language model hallucinations are fabricated outputs, and concept drift is a gradual change in data patterns over time. None of these is an input-level attack on the chatbot\'s instructions."},{"id":"aif-q254","question":"A company wants to make sure its AI system is fair and explainable and decides to require training for its AI development team. Which training meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Training on advanced coding skills","B":"Training on data privacy and encryption protocols","C":"Training on bias awareness and responsible AI","D":"Training on advanced ML algorithms"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Training the AI development team on bias awareness and responsible AI gives the team the knowledge and practices needed to build systems that are fair and explainable, which meets the requirement.\\nTraining on advanced coding skills, advanced ML algorithms, or data privacy and encryption protocols builds other valuable skills but does not directly address fairness and explainability."},{"id":"aif-q393","question":"A company is deploying AI agents on Amazon Bedrock that need to access multiple backend systems on behalf of users. The company wants each agent to authenticate with its own identity rather than sharing a single set of credentials. Which AWS feature meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity","C":"Amazon Macie","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity provides identity management for AI agents, allowing each agent to authenticate with its own distinct identity when accessing backend systems, which supports the principle of least privilege and avoids sharing credentials.\\nAWS Secrets Manager stores and rotates credentials but does not provide per-agent identity management. Amazon Macie discovers sensitive data in Amazon S3. AWS CloudTrail logs API activity for auditing. None of these assigns individual identities to AI agents."},{"id":"aif-q394","question":"A compliance team at a pharmaceutical company uses a generative AI assistant to answer questions about drug interactions. To reduce the risk of the model generating incorrect medical information, the team wants every answer verified against approved reference documents. Which technique meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Increase the model\'s temperature parameter","B":"Apply RAG grounding with approved reference documents","C":"Fine-tune the model on more general medical data","D":"Use a larger foundation model"},"correct":"B","explanation":"RAG grounding retrieves relevant passages from approved reference documents at query time and uses them to verify and anchor the model\'s responses, ensuring answers are traceable to authoritative sources and reducing the risk of hallucinated medical information.\\nIncreasing the temperature adds randomness and makes hallucinations more likely, not less. Fine-tuning on more general medical data does not guarantee answers are grounded in specific approved documents. Using a larger model may improve general capability but does not verify outputs against authoritative references."},{"id":"aif-q395","question":"Which two techniques help improve the accuracy of a generative AI model\'s outputs by detecting potential hallucinations before they reach users?","options":{"A":"Output validation against source documents","B":"Increasing the model\'s training data volume","C":"Confidence scoring to flag low-certainty responses","D":"Reducing the number of model parameters","E":"Adding more GPU compute during inference"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Output validation checks model responses against source documents to confirm factual accuracy, and confidence scoring assigns a certainty measure to each response so low-confidence outputs can be flagged for review or withheld from users. Together these techniques catch potential hallucinations before they reach end users.\\nIncreasing training data volume may improve general model quality but does not detect hallucinations in real time. Reducing model parameters simplifies the model but does not add runtime verification. Adding more GPU compute speeds up inference but provides no mechanism to detect or prevent inaccurate outputs."},{"id":"aif-q396","question":"A financial institution trains ML models using data from multiple internal systems. Regulators require the institution to trace every piece of training data back to its original source and demonstrate that the data was collected and processed in compliance with policy. Which practice meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Data augmentation","B":"Data lineage tracking","C":"Data normalization","D":"Data balancing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Data lineage tracking documents the origin, movement, and transformation of data throughout its lifecycle, so the institution can trace every piece of training data back to its source and prove it was handled in compliance with policy, which satisfies the regulatory requirement.\\nData augmentation creates additional training examples to improve model performance. Data normalization rescales features to a common range. Data balancing adjusts class distributions to reduce bias. None of these provides a traceable record of data origins and transformations for regulatory purposes."},{"id":"aif-q397","question":"A company wants to document the training data sources, processing steps, and intended use of each ML model it deploys, so auditors can verify data provenance. Which AWS feature provides a standardized way to record this information?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards","B":"Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","C":"Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","D":"Amazon SageMaker Autopilot"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards provide a standardized framework for documenting model details including training data sources, processing steps, evaluation results, and intended use, giving auditors a consistent record of data provenance and model lineage.\\nAmazon SageMaker Data Wrangler prepares and transforms data but does not produce audit-ready model documentation. Amazon SageMaker Feature Store manages and serves features for ML models. Amazon SageMaker Autopilot automates model building. None of these creates the standardized documentation record needed for data provenance audits."},{"id":"aif-q451","question":"A company uses AI models trained on customer data and needs to define rules for how long training datasets are kept, when they are archived, and when they must be deleted. Which data governance strategy does this describe?","options":{"A":"Data residency","B":"Data lifecycle management","C":"Data logging","D":"Data monitoring"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Data lifecycle management defines rules for how data is created, stored, archived, and deleted over time, which is what setting retention and deletion schedules for training datasets describes.\\nData residency governs where data is stored geographically. Data logging records events for auditing. Data monitoring observes quality and usage in real time. None of these sets archival and deletion timelines."},{"id":"aif-q452","question":"A company\'s data governance team wants to continuously watch how AI training data is accessed and used across the organization to detect unauthorized usage patterns. Which data governance strategy does this describe?","options":{"A":"Data retention","B":"Data residency","C":"Data observation and monitoring","D":"Data logging"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Data observation and monitoring involves continuously watching how data is accessed and used to detect anomalies and unauthorized patterns, which is the governance strategy that addresses the team\'s need to oversee AI training data usage.\\nData retention defines how long data is stored. Data residency controls geographic location. Data logging records individual access events but does not provide the ongoing surveillance and pattern detection that monitoring does."},{"id":"aif-q453","question":"A company building AI systems on AWS wants to record every access to its training datasets for auditing purposes. Which AWS service provides this logging capability?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail logs API calls made to AWS services, including data access events for services like Amazon S3, creating an audit trail that records who accessed training data, when, and what they did.\\nAWS Config tracks resource configuration changes, not data access events. AWS Trusted Advisor provides best-practice recommendations. Amazon Inspector scans for vulnerabilities. None of these logs individual data access events for auditing."},{"id":"aif-q456","question":"A financial institution trains ML models using data from multiple systems. Regulators require the institution to trace every piece of training data back to its source. Which practice meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Data augmentation","B":"Data lineage tracking","C":"Data normalization","D":"Data balancing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Data lineage tracking documents the origin, movement, and transformation of data throughout its lifecycle, so the institution can trace every piece of training data back to its source and demonstrate compliant handling.\\nData augmentation creates additional training examples. Data normalization rescales features. Data balancing adjusts class distributions. None provides a traceable record of data origins."}],"language":"en","attribution":"Preguntas de GitHub — nastaso/cloudcertprep (https://github.com/nastaso/cloudcertprep), licencia MIT. Adaptadas al formato de este sitio.","license":"MIT"},{"id":"aws-clf-c02-open","title":"AWS Cloud Practitioner (CLF-C02) — banco abierto (EN)","questions":[{"id":"q004","question":"Which of the following options are related to the reliability of AWS?","options":{"A":"Applying the principle of least privilege to all AWS resources","B":"Automatically provisioning new resources to meet demand","C":"All AWS services are considered Global Services, and this design helps customers serve their international users","D":"Providing compensation to customers if issues occur","E":"Ability to recover quickly from failures"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Automatically provisioning new resources to meet demand and the ability to recover quickly from failures are both core reliability principles, ensuring the system continues operating correctly under varying conditions and after failure events.\\nApplying the principle of least privilege is a security practice related to access control, not a reliability principle. All AWS services being considered global services is false, as many services are regional, and global reach is a separate concept from reliability. Providing compensation to customers if issues occur describes an SLA remediation mechanism and is not a design principle that contributes to system reliability."},{"id":"q010","question":"A Japanese company hosts their applications on Amazon EC2 instances in the Tokyo Region. The company has opened new branches in the United States, and the US users are complaining of high latency. What can the company do to reduce latency for the users in the US while minimizing costs?","options":{"A":"Applying the Route 53 latency-based routing policy","B":"Registering a new US domain name to serve the users in the US","C":"Building a new data center in the US and implementing a hybrid model","D":"Deploying new Amazon EC2 instances in a Region located in the US"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Deploying EC2 instances in a US Region places compute resources physically closer to US users, directly reducing network latency at minimal additional cost.\\nApplying a Route 53 latency-based routing policy directs users to the Region with the lowest latency, but this only optimizes routing and does not itself reduce latency if all compute resources remain in Tokyo. Registering a new US domain name changes how users reach the application by name but does not move any compute resources closer to US users or reduce network latency. Building a new data center in the US and implementing a hybrid model introduces significant capital expenditure and operational complexity, contradicting the requirement to minimize costs."},{"id":"q013","question":"Adjusting compute capacity dynamically to reduce cost is an implementation of which AWS cloud best practice?","options":{"A":"Build security in every layer","B":"Parallelize tasks","C":"Implement elasticity","D":"Adopt monolithic architecture"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Elasticity is the cloud best practice of dynamically adjusting compute capacity up or down in response to demand, avoiding over-provisioning during low-traffic periods and under-provisioning during peaks, which directly reduces cost.\\nBuild security in every layer is the Defense in Depth principle from the Security pillar of the Well-Architected Framework, which addresses threat protection rather than capacity management. Parallelizing tasks is a performance design principle that distributes workloads across multiple smaller components to increase throughput, not a mechanism for dynamically adjusting capacity to control costs. Adopting monolithic architecture consolidates all application components into a single tightly coupled unit, which is the opposite of a cloud best practice and does not support dynamic scaling."},{"id":"q014","question":"What are the benefits of having infrastructure hosted in AWS?","options":{"A":"Increasing speed and agility","B":"Gaining unlimited free storage for all data","C":"Gaining complete control over the physical infrastructure","D":"Operating applications on behalf of customers","E":"All of the physical security and most of the data and network security are taken care of for you"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS increases speed and agility by allowing customers to provision resources in minutes rather than weeks, and handles physical security along with most data and network security so customers can focus on building applications rather than managing infrastructure.\\nUnlimited free storage is not an AWS benefit, as storage services such as Amazon S3 and EBS are billed based on the amount of data stored and retrieved. Complete control over physical infrastructure is not available to AWS customers, as AWS owns and manages all physical hardware, data centers, and networking equipment on the customer\'s behalf. Operating applications on behalf of customers is not an AWS benefit, as customers remain responsible for building, deploying, and managing their own applications under the shared responsibility model."},{"id":"q015","question":"What is the advantage of the AWS-recommended practice of \\"decoupling\\" applications?","options":{"A":"Allows treating an application as a single, cohesive unit","B":"Reduces inter-dependencies so that failures do not impact other components of the application","C":"Allows updates of any monolithic application quickly and easily","D":"Allows tracking of any API call made to any AWS service"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Decoupling means designing components to operate independently so that a failure in one part does not cascade into failures across the rest of the application, improving overall resilience.\\nTreating an application as a single cohesive unit describes a monolithic architecture, which is the opposite of decoupling and creates tightly coupled dependencies between components. Allowing quick updates to a monolithic application describes an advantage of a well-structured monolith, not of decoupling, and updating a monolith typically requires deploying the entire application as a unit. Tracking API calls made to AWS services describes the function of AWS CloudTrail, which is an auditing and logging service unrelated to application architecture design."},{"id":"q019","question":"One of the most important AWS best-practices to follow is the cloud architecture principle of elasticity. How does this principle improve your architecture\'s design?","options":{"A":"By automatically scaling your on-premises resources based on changes in demand","B":"By automatically scaling your AWS resources using an Elastic Load Balancer","C":"By reducing interdependencies between application components wherever possible","D":"By automatically provisioning the required AWS resources based on changes in demand"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Elasticity in AWS means the platform automatically provisions and de-provisions resources in response to real-time changes in demand, so workloads consume only what they need and scale back when demand drops.\\nAutomatically scaling on-premises resources is not what elasticity describes in the AWS context; elasticity is a cloud characteristic that removes the need to invest in on-premises hardware at all, as the cloud provider dynamically adjusts compute capacity. An Elastic Load Balancer distributes incoming traffic across existing instances to prevent any single instance from being overwhelmed, but it does not add or remove instances in response to demand; that capacity adjustment is performed by Auto Scaling. Reducing interdependencies between application components describes the design principle of loose coupling, which improves resilience and maintainability, but is a separate architectural concept from elasticity."},{"id":"q023","question":"Which of the following does NOT belong to the AWS Cloud Computing models?","options":{"A":"Platform as a Service (PaaS)","B":"Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)","C":"Software as a Service (SaaS)","D":"Networking as a Service (NaaS)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The three recognized AWS cloud computing service models are IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS. Networking as a Service is not one of them and does not exist as a defined cloud computing model in the AWS framework.\\nPlatform as a Service is one of the three recognized cloud computing models, providing customers with a managed platform for developing and deploying applications without managing the underlying infrastructure. Infrastructure as a Service is one of the three recognized cloud computing models, giving customers virtualized compute, storage, and networking resources they manage themselves. Software as a Service is one of the three recognized cloud computing models, delivering fully managed applications to end users over the internet without any infrastructure or platform management required."},{"id":"q065","question":"According to best practices, how should an application be designed to run in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Use tightly coupled components","B":"Use loosely coupled components","C":"Use monolithic components","D":"Use stateful components"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Using loosely coupled components means each part of an application can scale, fail, and be updated independently without cascading failures to other components, which is the foundational AWS best practice for building resilient and scalable architectures.\\nUsing tightly coupled components creates direct dependencies between parts of an application so that a failure or change in one component directly affects others, increasing the risk of cascading failures and making the system harder to scale and maintain independently. A monolithic design combines all components into a single unit that is difficult to scale or update independently, which is the opposite of the modular approach AWS recommends. Stateful components store session data locally on a specific instance, which prevents requests from being distributed freely across instances and undermines horizontal scaling and resilience."},{"id":"q077","question":"How does AWS shorten the time to provision IT resources?","options":{"A":"It supplies an online IT ticketing platform for resource requests","B":"It supports automatic code validation services","C":"It provides the ability to programmatically provision existing resources","D":"It automates the resource request process from a company\'s IT vendor list"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS allows resources to be provisioned programmatically through APIs, CloudFormation templates, and SDKs, drastically reducing the time to deploy infrastructure compared to traditional procurement and physical setup.\\nAWS does not operate an IT ticketing platform for resource requests. Resources are provisioned on demand through self-service APIs and consoles without requiring formal request and approval workflows. AWS does not shorten provisioning time through code validation services, which are a software quality tool unrelated to how quickly infrastructure can be deployed. AWS does not automate requests from a company\'s existing IT vendor list, as it replaces traditional vendor procurement entirely by providing infrastructure directly through its own platform."},{"id":"q084","question":"Web servers running on Amazon EC2 access a legacy application running in a corporate data center. What term would describe this model?","options":{"A":"Cloud-native","B":"Partner network","C":"Hybrid architecture","D":"Infrastructure as a service"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When applications in the AWS Cloud communicate with a legacy application in a corporate data center, this is a hybrid architecture that combines on-premises and cloud resources in a single solution.\\nCloud-native describes applications designed and built entirely to run in the cloud, with no dependency on on-premises infrastructure or legacy systems. Partner network refers to the AWS Partner Network, which is a program for technology and consulting companies that build solutions on AWS, and is not a deployment model. Infrastructure as a Service is a cloud service model that provides virtualized compute, storage, and networking resources, and does not describe the combination of cloud and on-premises components in a single architecture."},{"id":"q099","question":"Distributing workloads across multiple Availability Zones supports which cloud architecture design principle?","options":{"A":"Implement automation","B":"Design for agility","C":"Design for failure","D":"Implement elasticity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Distributing workloads across multiple Availability Zones ensures the application can continue operating if one AZ fails, embodying the design for failure principle, where the architecture anticipates and accommodates component failures.\\nImplement automation refers to reducing manual intervention through scripts and managed services, not to fault tolerance across zones. Design for agility refers to the ability to rapidly experiment and deploy, not to handling infrastructure failures. Implement elasticity refers to automatically scaling resources in response to demand, not to distributing workloads for fault tolerance."},{"id":"q128","question":"What is an advantage of deploying an application across multiple Availability Zones?","options":{"A":"There is a lower risk of service failure if a natural disaster causes a service disruption in a given AWS Region","B":"The application will have higher availability because it can withstand a service disruption in one Availability Zone","C":"There will be better coverage as Availability Zones are geographically distant and can serve a wider area","D":"There will be decreased application latency that will improve the user experience"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Deploying across multiple Availability Zones keeps an application running when one AZ experiences an outage, as traffic fails over to instances in the remaining AZs, directly increasing availability.\\nMultiple AZs within a single Region do not protect against a natural disaster affecting the entire Region, as all AZs in a Region share the same geographic area and would be exposed to the same regional disruption. Availability Zones within a Region are intentionally close together with low-latency links between them, so they do not provide wider geographic coverage or serve users in distant locations. Deploying across multiple AZs redistributes existing resources rather than reducing the distance between the application and its users, so it does not decrease latency."},{"id":"q130","question":"Which of the following is an AWS Cloud architecture design principle?","options":{"A":"Implement single points of failure","B":"Implement loose coupling","C":"Implement monolithic design","D":"Implement vertical scaling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loose coupling is a core AWS cloud architecture principle where components interact through well-defined interfaces, allowing each to scale, update, or fail independently without cascading effects on other parts of the system.\\nImplementing single points of failure is an anti-pattern that directly contradicts cloud architecture best practices, as the goal is to eliminate dependencies that could bring down an entire system. Monolithic design tightly couples all application components into a single unit, making it harder to scale, update, or recover individual parts independently. Vertical scaling increases the capacity of a single instance rather than distributing workloads, which limits scalability and creates potential single points of failure."},{"id":"q132","question":"A company will be moving from an on-premises data center to the AWS Cloud. What would be one financial difference after the move?","options":{"A":"Moving from variable operational expense (opex) to upfront capital expense (capex)","B":"Moving from upfront capital expense (capex) to variable capital expense (capex)","C":"Moving from upfront capital expense (capex) to variable operational expense (opex)","D":"Elimination of upfront capital expense (capex) and elimination of variable operational expense (opex)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"On-premises infrastructure requires large upfront capital expenditures for hardware and facilities, while AWS converts that spending to variable operational expense where you pay only for what you consume with no hardware purchases required.\\nMoving from variable operational expense (opex) to upfront capital expense (capex) this reverses the actual direction of the shift, and on-premises environments are characterized by capex, not opex, as the starting point. Moving from upfront capital expense (capex) to variable capital expense (capex) AWS usage is billed as operational expense, not capital expense, so the destination category in this option is incorrect. Elimination of upfront capital expense (capex) and elimination of variable operational expense (opex) moving to AWS eliminates capex but introduces opex rather than eliminating it, as ongoing usage costs are paid as operational expense."},{"id":"q141","question":"One of the advantages to moving infrastructure from an on-premises data center to the AWS Cloud is:","options":{"A":"It allows the business to eliminate IT bills","B":"It allows the business to put a server in each customer\'s data center","C":"It allows the business to focus on business activities","D":"It allows the business to leave servers unpatched"},"correct":"C","explanation":"By offloading infrastructure management to AWS, companies can redirect their IT staff and resources toward developing and improving their core products and services rather than maintaining data centers.\\nAWS does not eliminate IT bills, as customers continue to pay for the services they consume under the pay-as-you-go model. Placing a server in each customer\'s data center describes an on-premises or edge deployment model, which is the opposite of migrating infrastructure to the cloud. Moving to AWS does not allow servers to go unpatched, as the Shared Responsibility Model still requires customers to patch the operating systems and applications running on their own instances."},{"id":"q143","question":"How can a user protect against AWS service disruptions if a natural disaster affects an entire geographic area?","options":{"A":"Deploy applications across multiple Availability Zones within an AWS Region","B":"Use a hybrid cloud computing deployment model within the geographic area","C":"Deploy applications across multiple AWS Regions","D":"Store application artifacts using AWS Artifact and replicate them across multiple AWS Regions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A natural disaster affecting an entire geographic area could take down a whole AWS Region, so deploying across multiple Regions in different geographies ensures the application remains operational even if one Region becomes completely unavailable.\\nDeploying across multiple Availability Zones within a single Region protects against isolated data center failures but does not provide protection if the entire Region and its surrounding geographic area is affected by a natural disaster. Using a hybrid cloud deployment model within the same geographic area keeps infrastructure in the affected zone, meaning a regional natural disaster would still disrupt both the on-premises and cloud components simultaneously. AWS Artifact is a portal for accessing AWS compliance reports and security documentation, not a storage or replication service, making this option factually incorrect as a disaster recovery strategy."},{"id":"q146","question":"A customer is deploying a new application and needs to choose an AWS Region. Which of the following factors could influence the customer\'s decision?","options":{"A":"Reduced latency to users","B":"The application\'s presentation in the local language","C":"Data sovereignty compliance","D":"Cooling costs in hotter climates","E":"Proximity to the customer\'s office for on-site visits"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Proximity to end users reduces network latency for a better application experience, and data sovereignty compliance requirements mandate that data remains within certain geographic boundaries, making both primary factors in Region selection.\\nPresenting an application in the local language is an application-layer localisation decision handled through software configuration, not a factor that influences which AWS Region infrastructure is deployed in. Cooling costs in hotter climates are an operational expense absorbed entirely by AWS as part of managing its own data centers, and are not a cost or consideration that customers factor into Region selection. Proximity to the customer\'s office for on-site visits is not a valid technical or regulatory criterion for Region selection, as AWS data centers are not customer-accessible facilities."},{"id":"q148","question":"Which disaster recovery scenario offers the lowest probability of down time?","options":{"A":"Backup and restore","B":"Pilot light","C":"Warm standby","D":"Multi-site active-active"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Multi-site active-active runs fully redundant live copies of your application across multiple sites simultaneously, so if one site fails the other immediately serves all traffic with essentially zero downtime.\\nBackup and restore has the highest recovery time of all DR strategies. Data is restored from backups after a failure, meaning significant downtime is expected. Pilot light keeps a minimal version of the environment running and scales up during a disaster, resulting in a short but non-zero recovery time. Warm standby runs a scaled-down but fully functional version of the environment, which requires scaling up before handling full traffic load."},{"id":"q153","question":"What can assist in evaluating an application for migration to the cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Professional Services","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"AWS Partner Network (APN)","E":"AWS Secrets Manager"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Professional Services provides expert consulting engagements to help evaluate and plan migrations, and AWS Partner Network partners offer specialized migration expertise through certified consulting and technology firms.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor scans existing AWS environments for best practice recommendations across cost, performance, security, and fault tolerance, but does not evaluate whether an application is suitable for migration to the cloud. AWS Systems Manager is an operational management service for configuring, patching, and managing AWS resources at scale, and is not a migration advisory or evaluation service. AWS Secrets Manager is a service for storing and rotating application secrets such as database credentials and API keys, and has no role in evaluating applications for cloud migration."},{"id":"q156","question":"What is a value proposition of the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS is responsible for security in the AWS Cloud","B":"No long-term contract is required","C":"Provision new servers in days","D":"AWS manages user applications in the AWS Cloud"},"correct":"B","explanation":"No long-term contract is required because AWS uses a pay-as-you-go model, allowing customers to use services on demand and stop at any time without upfront commitments or termination penalties.\\nAWS is responsible for security in the cloud is a partial truth that misrepresents the Shared Responsibility Model. Security is shared, with AWS responsible for the infrastructure and customers responsible for their data and configurations. Provision new servers in days is inaccurate. One of AWS\'s key advantages is provisioning resources in minutes, not days, which is the reality that distinguishes cloud from traditional procurement. AWS manages user applications in the AWS Cloud is false. Application management remains the customer\'s responsibility under the Shared Responsibility Model."},{"id":"q161","question":"What are the benefits of developing and running a new application in the AWS Cloud compared to on-premises?","options":{"A":"AWS automatically distributes the data globally for higher durability","B":"AWS will take care of operating the application","C":"AWS makes it easy to architect for high availability","D":"AWS can easily accommodate application demand changes","E":"AWS takes care of application security patching"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"AWS makes it easy to architect for high availability using multi-AZ deployments, load balancers, and managed services, and the cloud\'s elasticity allows applications to scale up or down to match demand changes quickly.\\nAWS does not automatically distribute all data globally by default, as data residency and replication are configuration choices the customer makes depending on the services and settings they select. AWS does not take care of operating the application, as customers remain responsible for deploying, managing, and maintaining their own application code and runtime environments. AWS does not take care of application security patching, as patching the application layer is the customer\'s responsibility under the Shared Responsibility Model."},{"id":"q173","question":"A solution that is able to support growth in users, traffic, or data size with no drop in performance aligns with which cloud architecture principle?","options":{"A":"Think parallel","B":"Implement elasticity","C":"Decouple your components","D":"Design for failure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Elasticity is the ability to scale resources up or down automatically to handle increases in users, traffic, or data size without any degradation in performance, making it the principle that directly addresses growth support.\\nThink parallel refers to the principle of designing systems to handle tasks simultaneously across multiple resources to improve throughput and speed, not specifically to handle growth in users or data size without performance loss. Decouple your components refers to reducing dependencies between application components so failures do not cascade, which improves resilience rather than addressing performance under growth. Design for failure refers to building systems that anticipate and recover from component failures automatically, which addresses resilience and availability rather than scaling to support growth."},{"id":"q174","question":"Which AWS Cloud benefit eliminates the need for users to try estimating future infrastructure usage?","options":{"A":"Easy and fast deployment of applications in multiple Regions around the world","B":"Security of the AWS Cloud","C":"Elasticity of the AWS Cloud","D":"Lower variable costs due to massive economies of scale"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cloud elasticity allows resources to scale up or down on demand, eliminating the need to predict and provision for peak future capacity in advance.\\nEasy and fast deployment of applications in multiple Regions is a benefit of AWS\'s global infrastructure, enabling speed and reach, but does not remove the need to estimate future infrastructure usage. Security of the AWS Cloud refers to the protections AWS provides for its infrastructure under the shared responsibility model, which is unrelated to capacity planning or infrastructure estimation. Lower variable costs due to massive economies of scale means AWS can offer reduced pricing as its customer base grows, but lower costs do not eliminate the underlying need to estimate how much infrastructure will be required."},{"id":"q187","question":"How can the AWS Cloud increase user workforce productivity after migration from an on-premises data center?","options":{"A":"Users do not have to wait for infrastructure provisioning","B":"The AWS Cloud infrastructure is much faster than an on-premises data center infrastructure","C":"AWS takes over application configuration management on behalf of users","D":"Users do not need to address security and compliance issues"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With AWS, developers and engineers can self-provision infrastructure in minutes through the console or APIs, eliminating the waiting time associated with traditional hardware procurement and setup.\\nThe AWS Cloud infrastructure is much faster than an on-premises data center infrastructure is misleading. The productivity gain comes from removing provisioning delays, not from raw infrastructure speed. AWS takes over application configuration management on behalf of users is false. Application configuration remains the customer\'s responsibility under the Shared Responsibility Model. Users do not need to address security and compliance issues is false. Customers remain responsible for security in the cloud, including their applications, data, and access controls."},{"id":"q189","question":"What is an example of agility in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Access to multiple instance types","B":"Access to managed services","C":"Using Consolidated Billing to produce one bill","D":"Decreased acquisition time for new compute resources"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Agility in the AWS Cloud is best illustrated by the dramatically reduced time to acquire and provision new compute resources, from weeks on-premises to minutes in the cloud, enabling teams to experiment and iterate faster.\\nAccess to multiple instance types is a flexibility benefit that allows customers to match compute to workload requirements, but does not directly describe the speed of acquiring new resources. Access to managed services reduces operational overhead by offloading infrastructure management to AWS, but is an example of reduced complexity rather than agility. Consolidated Billing is a cost management feature that combines charges from multiple accounts into a single bill, which is a billing convenience and unrelated to the speed of resource acquisition."},{"id":"q203","question":"Which of the below is a best-practice when designing solutions on AWS?","options":{"A":"Invest heavily in architecting your environment, as it is not easy to change your design later","B":"Use AWS reservations to reduce costs when testing your production environment","C":"Automate wherever possible to make architectural experimentation easier","D":"Provision a large compute capacity to handle any spikes in load"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS best practice is to automate infrastructure and deployment wherever possible, which makes architectural experimentation faster and reduces human error.\\nInvesting heavily in upfront architectural design contradicts the cloud principle that infrastructure should be treated as flexible and changeable, with automation enabling rapid iteration rather than requiring heavy planning before deployment. Using AWS reservations to reduce costs when testing production environments misapplies Reserved Instances, which are a billing commitment for predictable steady-state workloads rather than a cost reduction tool for testing environments. Provisioning large compute capacity to handle any spikes in load describes over-provisioning, which is an on-premises anti-pattern that cloud best practices replace with elastic scaling that dynamically matches capacity to actual demand."},{"id":"q206","question":"The principle “design for failure and nothing will fail” is very important when designing your AWS Cloud architecture. Which of the following would help adhere to this principle?","options":{"A":"Multi-factor authentication","B":"Availability Zones","C":"Elastic Load Balancing","D":"Penetration testing","E":"Vertical Scaling"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Deploying across multiple Availability Zones provides physical redundancy so that if one zone experiences a failure, instances in the remaining zones continue serving traffic without interruption. Elastic Load Balancing automatically detects unhealthy targets and routes traffic only to healthy ones, preventing a single failed instance from bringing down the entire application. Both directly implement the design-for-failure principle.\\nMulti-factor authentication adds a second layer of security to account and user sign-in, and is an access security control with no bearing on fault tolerance or application resilience. Penetration testing simulates attacks against an application to identify security vulnerabilities, and is a security assessment practice rather than an architectural design approach for surviving component failures. Vertical scaling increases the capacity of a single instance by adding more CPU or memory, which creates a larger single point of failure rather than distributing risk, and is the opposite of the redundancy that design-for-failure requires."},{"id":"q215","question":"Which of the following is one of the benefits of moving infrastructure from an on-premises data center to AWS?","options":{"A":"Free support for all enterprise customers","B":"Automatic data protection","C":"Reduced Capital Expenditure (CapEx)","D":"AWS holds responsibility for managing customer applications"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Migrating from on-premises to AWS converts large upfront capital expenditures such as purchasing and maintaining physical hardware into smaller variable operational expenses, directly reducing CapEx and improving financial flexibility.\\nAWS does not provide free support to all enterprise customers, as support is available through paid tiers ranging from Developer to Enterprise, with costs varying by plan. AWS does not automatically protect data on behalf of customers, as data protection responsibilities such as encryption and backup configuration remain with the customer under the Shared Responsibility Model. AWS does not manage customer applications, as application-layer responsibilities including code, configuration, and runtime management remain with the customer regardless of which infrastructure services they use."},{"id":"q216","question":"Which of the following are important design principles you should adopt when designing systems on AWS?","options":{"A":"Always use Global Services in your architecture rather than Regional Services","B":"Always choose to pay as you go","C":"Treat servers as fixed resources","D":"Automate wherever possible","E":"Remove single points of failure"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Automating wherever possible reduces manual errors and speeds recovery, and removing single points of failure through redundancy ensures continued operation if one component fails, both being core AWS design principles.\\nAlways using Global Services rather than Regional Services is not a recognized design principle, as the choice between global and regional services depends on the specific workload requirements and is not a blanket architectural rule. Always choosing to pay as you go is a billing model characteristic rather than a system design principle, and pricing decisions are made based on cost optimization goals rather than architectural patterns. Treating servers as fixed resources contradicts the cloud design principle of elasticity, which calls for treating infrastructure as disposable and dynamically scalable rather than permanent and static."},{"id":"q225","question":"What are two advantages of using Cloud Computing over using traditional data centers?","options":{"A":"Reserved Compute capacity","B":"Eliminating Single Points of Failure (SPOFs)","C":"Distributed infrastructure","D":"Virtualized compute resources","E":"Dedicated hosting"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Cloud computing eliminates single points of failure through redundancy across multiple data centers, and its geographically distributed infrastructure means no single physical failure can take down the entire system, both of which are structural advantages over traditional data centers.\\nReserved compute capacity requires upfront commitment to fixed resources and exists in both cloud and traditional environments, so it is not a differentiating advantage of cloud. Virtualized compute resources are used in traditional data centers as well as in the cloud, making virtualization itself a shared characteristic rather than a cloud advantage. Dedicated hosting allocates physical hardware exclusively to one customer and is available in both cloud and traditional environments, so it does not represent an advantage of cloud computing over traditional infrastructure."},{"id":"q227","question":"Which statement best describes the operational excellence pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework?","options":{"A":"The ability of a system to recover gracefully from failure","B":"The efficient use of computing resources to meet requirements","C":"The ability to monitor systems and improve supporting processes and procedures","D":"The ability to manage datacenter operations more efficiently"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Operational Excellence pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework focuses on running and monitoring workloads effectively, continuously improving processes, and automating operations to deliver business value.\\nThe ability of a system to recover gracefully from failure describes the Reliability pillar, which focuses on workload availability and the ability to recover from disruptions. The efficient use of computing resources to meet requirements describes the Performance Efficiency pillar, which focuses on using the right resource types and sizes to match workload demands. Managing datacenter operations more efficiently does not correspond to any Well-Architected pillar, as AWS manages all physical datacenter infrastructure on behalf of customers and this is not a customer concern in the cloud."},{"id":"q242","question":"Jessica is managing an e-commerce web application in AWS. The application is hosted on six EC2 instances. One day, three of the instances crashed; but none of her customers were affected. What has Jessica done correctly in this scenario?","options":{"A":"She has properly built an elastic system","B":"She has properly built a fault tolerant system","C":"She has properly built an encrypted system","D":"She has properly built a scalable system"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A fault-tolerant system is designed to continue operating correctly even when some of its components fail, which is exactly what the scenario describes: three of six instances crashed but no customers were affected.\\nAn elastic system automatically scales capacity up or down in response to changes in demand, but elasticity does not address continued operation during component failure. An encrypted system protects data from unauthorised access through cryptographic controls, which is a security property unrelated to maintaining availability when instances crash. A scalable system is designed to handle growth in workload or users by adding resources, but scalability does not ensure the application keeps running when existing instances fail."},{"id":"q253","question":"Using Amazon EC2 falls under which of the following cloud computing models?","options":{"A":"IaaS & SaaS","B":"IaaS","C":"SaaS","D":"PaaS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EC2 is IaaS because AWS provides the virtualized compute infrastructure while the customer manages the operating system, middleware, and applications above it.\\nIaaS and SaaS combined is not a recognized classification for any single service, as each service falls under one model rather than spanning two. SaaS delivers fully managed software applications to end users with no infrastructure or platform management required, whereas EC2 requires customers to manage everything above the hypervisor. PaaS provides a managed platform where customers deploy application code without managing the underlying infrastructure, whereas EC2 gives customers direct control over the operating system and runtime environment."},{"id":"q254","question":"Which of the below is a best-practice when building applications on AWS?","options":{"A":"Strengthen physical security by applying the principle of least privilege","B":"Ensure that the application runs on hardware from trusted vendors","C":"Use IAM policies to maintain performance","D":"Decouple the components of the application so that they run independently"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Decoupling application components so they communicate through well-defined interfaces such as APIs or queues rather than direct dependencies means each component can scale, fail, and be updated independently without causing cascading failures throughout the application.\\nApplying the principle of least privilege is a security best practice for controlling access permissions, and while important, it relates to identity and access management rather than application architecture design. Ensuring that applications run on hardware from trusted vendors is not a customer concern on AWS, as AWS manages all underlying physical infrastructure and hardware selection on behalf of the customer. IAM policies are used to define access permissions for users and roles, and are an identity and access management tool rather than a mechanism for maintaining application performance."},{"id":"q261","question":"Your application has recently experienced significant global growth, and international users are complaining of high latency. What is the AWS characteristic that can help improve your international users\' experience?","options":{"A":"Elasticity","B":"Global reach","C":"Data durability","D":"High availability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS global reach, through its worldwide network of Regions and edge locations, enables applications to be deployed closer to international users, reducing the network distance that data must travel and lowering latency for a global audience.\\nElasticity is the ability to automatically scale resources up or down in response to changing demand, and addresses capacity flexibility rather than the geographic proximity that causes latency for international users. Data durability refers to the long-term integrity and redundancy of stored data to prevent loss, and is a storage protection characteristic with no bearing on network latency or geographic reach. High availability refers to the ability of a system to remain operational and accessible with minimal downtime, and addresses uptime and fault tolerance rather than reducing latency for geographically distributed users."},{"id":"q263","question":"A company has business critical workloads hosted on AWS and they are unwilling to accept any downtime. Which of the following is a recommended best practice to protect their workloads in the event of an unexpected natural disaster?","options":{"A":"Replicate data across multiple Edge Locations worldwide and use Amazon CloudFront to perform automatic failover in the event of an outage","B":"Deploy AWS resources across multiple Availability Zones within the same AWS Region","C":"Create point-in-time backups in another subnet and recover this data when a disaster occurs","D":"Deploy AWS resources to another AWS Region and implement an Active-Active disaster recovery strategy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Deploying AWS resources to a second AWS Region and running an Active-Active disaster recovery strategy means both Regions serve live production traffic simultaneously, so if a natural disaster eliminates one Region entirely, the other continues serving users without any downtime.\\nAmazon CloudFront distributes and caches content at edge locations to reduce latency for end users, but it is a content delivery service and does not replicate workloads or perform automatic failover of application infrastructure in the event of a regional disaster. Deploying resources across multiple Availability Zones within a single Region improves resilience against isolated failures, but a large-scale natural disaster can affect an entire Region simultaneously, making Multi-AZ alone insufficient when zero downtime is required. Creating point-in-time backups in another subnet stores recovery data within the same Region and requires manual restoration time, which directly conflicts with the requirement to accept no downtime. Subnets are also intra-Region constructs that offer no geographic separation."},{"id":"q271","question":"Which of the following should be considered when performing a TCO analysis to compare the costs of running an application on AWS instead of on-premises?","options":{"A":"Application development","B":"Market research","C":"Business analysis","D":"Physical hardware"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Physical hardware costs, including servers, networking, and storage, must be purchased and maintained on-premises but are replaced by AWS managed infrastructure in the cloud, making them the key differentiator in a TCO comparison.\\nApplication development costs remain relatively constant regardless of where an application is hosted, so they do not meaningfully affect an on-premises versus cloud comparison. Market research is not a component of TCO analysis. Business analysis costs also remain consistent regardless of hosting environment and are not a factor in infrastructure cost comparisons."},{"id":"q276","question":"Which of the following statements describes the AWS Cloud\'s agility?","options":{"A":"AWS allows you to host your applications in multiple regions around the world","B":"AWS provides customizable hardware at the lowest possible cost","C":"AWS allows you to provision resources in minutes","D":"AWS allows you to pay upfront to reduce costs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Cloud agility refers to the ability to provision infrastructure resources in minutes through the console, CLI, or API, dramatically faster than the weeks or months required for on-premises hardware procurement.\\nHosting applications in multiple Regions describes global reach, which is a separate cloud benefit from agility. AWS providing customizable hardware at the lowest possible cost misrepresents how AWS works, as customers do not configure or own the underlying hardware. Paying upfront to reduce costs describes Reserved Instance pricing, which is the opposite of the pay-as-you-go model that defines cloud economics."},{"id":"q291","question":"Which of the following is a benefit of running an application in multiple Availability Zones?","options":{"A":"Allows you to exceed AWS service limits","B":"Reduces application response time between servers and global users","C":"Increases available compute capacity","D":"Increases the availability of your application"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Deploying an application across multiple Availability Zones ensures it continues running from the remaining AZs if one experiences an outage, directly increasing availability by eliminating single points of failure within a Region.\\nAWS service limits are account and resource quotas set by AWS that can be increased through a support request, and are entirely unrelated to how many Availability Zones an application is deployed across. Reducing application response time for global users requires deploying across multiple Regions or using a content delivery network such as CloudFront, as AZs within a single Region are geographically close together and do not reduce latency for distant users. Increasing available compute capacity requires provisioning additional instances or enabling Auto Scaling, as spreading existing resources across multiple AZs redistributes capacity rather than adding to it."},{"id":"q303","question":"What are the benefits of using an AWS-managed service?","options":{"A":"Provides complete control over the virtual infrastructure","B":"Allows customers to deliver new solutions faster","C":"Lowers operational complexity","D":"Eliminates the need to encrypt data","E":"Allows developers to control all patching related activities"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS managed services handle infrastructure provisioning, patching, and maintenance, which lowers operational complexity for customers and lets them deliver new solutions faster by focusing on application logic instead of infrastructure management.\\nProviding complete control over the virtual infrastructure is a characteristic of IaaS services such as EC2, not managed services, which deliberately abstract away infrastructure management in exchange for reduced operational burden. Using a managed service does not eliminate the need to encrypt data, as data protection responsibilities including encryption at rest and in transit remain with the customer under the Shared Responsibility Model. Managed services remove patching responsibilities from the customer rather than giving developers control over them, as AWS handles all underlying patching and maintenance on the customer\'s behalf."},{"id":"q307","question":"What is the benefit of using an API to access AWS Services?","options":{"A":"It improves the performance of AWS resources","B":"It reduces the time needed to provision AWS resources","C":"It reduces the number of developers necessary","D":"It allows for programmatic management of AWS resources"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS APIs enable programmatic management of AWS resources, allowing developers to automate provisioning, configuration, and monitoring through code rather than manual console interactions.\\nUsing an API to access AWS services does not inherently improve the performance of the underlying resources, as API access is a management interface rather than a performance enhancement mechanism. While automation through APIs can speed up workflows, the primary benefit is programmatic control rather than a reduction in provisioning time specifically. APIs enable developers to interact with AWS services through code, but they do not reduce the number of developers needed to manage an environment and may in fact require additional development skills."},{"id":"q320","question":"Which design principles relate to performance efficiency in AWS?","options":{"A":"Build multi-region architectures to better serve global customers","B":"Apply security at all layers","C":"Implement strong Identity and Access controls","D":"Use serverless architectures","E":"Enable audit logging"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Multi-region architectures serve global customers with lower latency, and serverless architectures automatically scale to match demand without capacity planning, both being key design principles of the Performance Efficiency pillar.\\nApplying security at all layers is a design principle of the Security pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework, not the Performance Efficiency pillar. Implementing strong identity and access controls is also a Security pillar principle focused on protecting resources through least privilege and access management, and is unrelated to performance efficiency. Enabling audit logging is a Security and Operational Excellence pillar practice used for compliance and incident investigation, and is not a Performance Efficiency design principle."},{"id":"q322","question":"Why would an organization decide to use AWS over an on-premises data center?","options":{"A":"Free commercial software licenses","B":"Free technical support","C":"Elastic resources","D":"On-site visits for auditing","E":"Cost Savings"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"AWS provides elastic resources that scale up and down with demand, eliminating the need to over-provision capacity, and offers cost savings through pay-as-you-go pricing that avoids large upfront capital investments required by on-premises data centers.\\nAWS does not provide free commercial software licenses, as third-party software costs are separate from AWS infrastructure pricing and must be purchased independently or through AWS Marketplace. AWS does not provide free technical support, as support is available through paid tiers and basic support only covers account and billing queries without technical assistance. AWS does not offer on-site visits for auditing, as customers requiring compliance evidence use AWS audit reports, certifications, and the AWS Artifact portal rather than physical site access."},{"id":"q327","question":"What is the framework created by AWS Professional Services that helps organizations design a road map to successful cloud adoption?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"AWS WAF","C":"AWS CAF","D":"Amazon EFS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS Cloud Adoption Framework is a guidance framework created by AWS Professional Services that helps organizations plan and execute their cloud adoption journey across six perspectives: Business, People, Governance, Platform, Security, and Operations.\\nAWS Secrets Manager stores, rotates, and manages access to secrets such as database credentials and API keys, and is a security service for credential management with no connection to cloud adoption planning or road map development. AWS WAF is a web application firewall that filters malicious HTTP requests based on defined rules to protect web applications from common exploits, and is a security service with no connection to cloud adoption frameworks or strategic road maps. Amazon EFS is a managed shared network file system that provides concurrent file access from multiple compute instances, and is a storage service with no connection to cloud adoption planning or strategic frameworks."},{"id":"aif-q329","question":"Which of the following is an example of unsupervised learning?","options":{"A":"Predicting house prices","B":"Spam detection","C":"Clustering customer segments","D":"Image classification"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Clustering customer segments is an example of unsupervised learning because the algorithm groups data points based on similarity without being given any labeled examples or predefined categories to learn from. The model discovers the structure in the data on its own.\\nPredicting house prices uses labeled training data where each example has a known price, making it a supervised regression task. Spam detection trains a model on emails already labeled as spam or not spam, making it a supervised classification task. Image classification trains a model on images with known labels, such as cat or dog, also making it a supervised classification task."},{"id":"q330","question":"Why do many startup companies prefer AWS over traditional on-premises solutions?","options":{"A":"AWS allows them to pay later when their business succeed","B":"AWS can build complete data centers faster than any other Cloud provider","C":"Using AWS, they can reduce time-to-market by focusing on business activities rather than on building and managing data centers","D":"AWS removes the need to invest in operational expenditure","E":"Using AWS allows companies to replace large capital expenditure with low variable costs"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Startups prefer AWS because they can replace large upfront capital expenditure with low variable pay-as-you-go costs, and reduce time-to-market by focusing on product development rather than building and managing data centers.\\nAWS allows them to pay later when their business succeeds is false. AWS requires payment for resources as they are used, not deferred until a business becomes profitable. AWS can build complete data centers faster than any other cloud provider is false and not a stated AWS value proposition. AWS removes the need to invest in operational expenditure is false. Operational expenditure still exists on AWS; the key shift is from upfront capital expenditure to ongoing variable costs."},{"id":"q333","question":"As part of the AWS Migration Acceleration Program (MAP), what does AWS provide to accelerate Enterprise adoption of AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Partners","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Professional Services","D":"Amazon Athena","E":"Amazon Pinpoint"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"As part of the AWS Migration Acceleration Program, AWS provides access to AWS Partners including consulting and technology partners with migration expertise, and AWS Professional Services which works directly with customers on cloud adoption and migration projects. Both are core components of the MAP program.\\nAWS Artifact is a self-service portal for accessing AWS compliance reports and security agreements, and is a compliance documentation resource rather than a component of the Migration Acceleration Program. Amazon Athena is a serverless query service that analyzes data stored in Amazon S3 using SQL, and is an analytics service with no role in enterprise cloud adoption acceleration or migration support. Amazon Pinpoint is a customer engagement service for sending targeted messages via email, SMS, and push notifications, and is a marketing communications tool with no connection to enterprise cloud migration acceleration."},{"id":"q343","question":"A company is using EC2 Instances to run their e-commerce site on the AWS platform. If the site becomes unavailable, the company will lose a significant amount of money for each minute the site is unavailable. Which design principle should the company use to minimize the risk of an outage?","options":{"A":"Least Privilege","B":"Pilot Light","C":"Fault Tolerance","D":"Multi-threading"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fault tolerance is the ability of a system to continue operating without interruption when one or more components fail, achieved through redundancy across Availability Zones and automatic failover, directly minimizing the risk of costly outages.\\nLeast privilege is a security principle that restricts user and service permissions to the minimum required to perform their function, and has no bearing on system availability or outage prevention. Pilot light is a disaster recovery strategy that keeps a minimal version of the environment running in a secondary Region, which addresses recovery after an outage rather than preventing one through redundancy. Multi-threading is a software programming technique that allows concurrent execution of multiple tasks within a single application, and is unrelated to cloud architecture design for availability."},{"id":"q371","question":"Which of the following is a best practice for making your application on AWS highly available?","options":{"A":"Deploy the application to at least two Availability Zones","B":"Deploy the application across multiple AWS Regions","C":"Deploy the application code on at least two servers in the same Availability Zone","D":"Rewrite the application code to handle all incoming requests"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Deploying an application across at least two Availability Zones ensures that if one AZ experiences an outage, the application continues running in the other, providing high availability.\\nDeploying across multiple AWS Regions provides geographic redundancy for disaster recovery but introduces significant complexity and cost, and is not the standard best practice for achieving high availability within a single application deployment. Deploying on multiple servers within the same Availability Zone improves redundancy at the server level but does not protect against an AZ-wide outage, leaving the application vulnerable to a single point of failure. Rewriting application code to handle all incoming requests on a single deployment does not address infrastructure failure and cannot provide high availability if the underlying infrastructure becomes unavailable."},{"id":"q372","question":"Which of the following should be taken into account when performing a TCO analysis regarding the costs of running an application on AWS VS on-premises?","options":{"A":"Labor and IT costs","B":"Cooling and power consumption","C":"Amazon EBS computing power","D":"Software architecture","E":"Software compatibility"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"A TCO analysis compares on-premises costs including labor, IT staff, cooling, and power consumption against AWS costs, where these physical infrastructure expenses are eliminated.\\nAmazon EBS computing power is an AWS service cost rather than an on-premises infrastructure expense, so it does not belong in a TCO comparison of what customers stop paying for when they migrate. Software architecture refers to how an application is designed and structured, which is a technical consideration rather than an infrastructure cost factored into a TCO analysis. Software compatibility refers to whether applications can run in a new environment, which is a migration planning concern rather than a cost category included in a TCO comparison."},{"id":"q388","question":"A key practice when designing solutions on AWS is to minimize dependencies between components so that the failure of a single component does not impact other components. What is this practice called?","options":{"A":"Elastic coupling","B":"Loosely coupling","C":"Scalable coupling","D":"Tightly coupling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loose coupling is the design practice of minimizing dependencies between application components so that a failure in one component does not cascade to others, improving overall system resilience.\\nElastic coupling is not a recognized cloud design principle or architectural term, and may cause confusion with Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling or Elastic Load Balancing, which are services rather than coupling patterns. Scalable coupling is not a recognized architectural concept, and scalability is a separate design goal achieved through practices such as horizontal scaling rather than a type of component coupling. Tight coupling is the opposite of the correct answer, describing an architecture where components are highly dependent on each other, meaning a failure in one component is more likely to cascade and impact others."},{"id":"q397","question":"Which of the following are advantages of using AWS as a cloud computing provider?","options":{"A":"Eliminates the need to monitor servers and applications","B":"Manages all the compliance and auditing tasks","C":"Provides custom hardware to meet any specification","D":"Eliminates the need to guess on infrastructure capacity needs","E":"Enables customers to trade their capital expenses for operational expenses"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"AWS eliminates the need to guess infrastructure capacity through on-demand scaling, and enables customers to trade large upfront capital expenses for smaller variable operational expenses based on actual usage.\\nCustomers remain responsible for monitoring their own servers and applications, as AWS provides the tools but does not perform monitoring on the customer\'s behalf. Compliance and auditing responsibilities are shared, with customers accountable for their own data, access controls, and application-level compliance regardless of using AWS. AWS does not provide custom hardware to individual customer specifications, as customers select from standardized instance types and managed services within the shared infrastructure."},{"id":"q406","question":"Which of the following is a cloud computing deployment model that connects infrastructure and applications between cloud-based resources and existing resources not located in the cloud?","options":{"A":"On-premises","B":"Mixed","C":"Hybrid","D":"Cloud"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A hybrid deployment model connects cloud-based resources with existing infrastructure not located in the cloud, such as on-premises data centers, allowing organizations to extend their environment into AWS while keeping some workloads running locally.\\nOn-premises is a deployment model where all infrastructure is hosted and managed within an organization\'s own facilities with no cloud integration, which is the opposite of connecting cloud and non-cloud resources. Mixed is not a recognized cloud computing deployment model and does not appear in the AWS framework alongside on-premises, hybrid, and cloud. Cloud is a deployment model where all infrastructure and applications run entirely within the cloud provider\'s environment with no dependency on locally hosted resources, meaning there are no existing non-cloud resources to connect to."},{"id":"q420","question":"What does the term “Economies of scale” mean?","options":{"A":"It means that you save more when you consume more","B":"It means as more time passes using AWS, you pay more for its services","C":"It means that AWS will continuously lower costs as it grows","D":"It means that you have the ability to pay as you go"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Economies of scale means that as AWS grows and aggregates more customer usage, it achieves lower per-unit costs and continuously passes those savings on to customers through regular price reductions.\\nSaving more when you consume more describes volume-based discounts such as Reserved Instances or tiered pricing, which is a separate pricing concept rather than the definition of economies of scale. Paying more over time as usage increases describes the opposite of what economies of scale delivers, as AWS prices have consistently decreased over time rather than increased. The ability to pay as you go describes the pay-as-you-go billing model, which is a separate cloud adoption benefit unrelated to the cost reductions driven by aggregated purchasing power."},{"id":"q439","question":"Why are Serverless Architectures more economical than Server-based Architectures?","options":{"A":"Serverless Architectures eliminate all networking costs associated with running applications","B":"With Server-based Architectures, compute resources continue to run all the time, but with Serverless Architectures, compute resources are only used when code is being executed","C":"When you reserve serverless capacity, you will get large discounts compared to server reservation","D":"With Serverless Architectures you have the ability to scale automatically up or down as demand changes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Serverless architectures are more economical because compute resources are only allocated during actual code execution, meaning there are no charges for idle capacity, unlike server-based architectures where instances run continuously and incur costs even when no requests are being processed.\\nServerless architectures do not eliminate networking costs; data transfer and API call costs still apply and are billed separately based on usage. Reserved capacity discounts do not apply to serverless compute in the same way they apply to EC2 instances; serverless pricing is based entirely on the number of requests and duration of execution rather than reserved capacity commitments. The ability to scale automatically is also available on server-based architectures using Auto Scaling groups, so this is not a characteristic unique to serverless; the key economic advantage is the elimination of costs during idle periods."},{"id":"q459","question":"The owner of an E-Commerce application notices that the compute capacity requirements vary heavily from time to time. What makes AWS more economical than traditional data centers for this type of application?","options":{"A":"AWS allows customers to launch powerful EC2 instances to handle spikes in load","B":"AWS allows customers to pay upfront to get bigger discounts","C":"AWS allows customers to launch and terminate EC2 instances based on demand","D":"AWS allows customers to choose cheaper types of EC2 instances that best fit their needs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS allows customers to launch and terminate EC2 instances based on real-time demand, so during low-traffic periods they stop paying for unused capacity, unlike traditional data centers where hardware sits idle regardless of utilization.\\nLaunching powerful instances to handle spikes in load describes vertical scaling, which still results in over-provisioned and idle capacity during low-traffic periods and does not address the core economic inefficiency of traditional data centers. Paying upfront to get bigger discounts describes Reserved Instance pricing, which is a commitment-based model that reduces per-unit cost but does not eliminate the problem of paying for capacity during periods of low demand. Choosing cheaper instance types reduces the unit cost of compute but does not eliminate idle capacity costs, as the instances would still run and incur charges regardless of whether they are actively being used."},{"id":"q467","question":"Which of the following are AWS cloud design principles?","options":{"A":"Reserved capacity instead of on demand","B":"Loose coupling over tight coupling","C":"Servers instead of managed services","D":"Disposable resources instead of fixed servers","E":"Multi-AZ deployments instead of multi-region deployments"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Loose coupling minimizes dependencies between components so that failures do not cascade, and treating resources as disposable means designing for failure by replacing instances rather than fixing them, enabling rapid recovery.\\nReserved capacity instead of on demand is the opposite of a cloud design principle, as cloud encourages provisioning resources dynamically based on actual need. Servers instead of managed services contradicts the cloud principle of offloading operational burden to AWS wherever possible. Multi-AZ versus multi-region is not a recognized AWS design principle and presents a false trade-off, as both are valid availability strategies depending on the use case."},{"id":"q470","question":"You have developed a web application targeting a global audience. Which of the following will help you achieve the highest redundancy and fault tolerance from an infrastructure perspective?","options":{"A":"There is no need to architect for these capabilities in AWS, as AWS is redundant by default","B":"Deploy the application in a single Availability Zone","C":"Deploy the application in multiple Availability Zones in a single AWS region","D":"Deploy the application in multiple Availability Zones in multiple AWS regions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Deploying across multiple Availability Zones in multiple AWS Regions provides the highest level of redundancy and fault tolerance, protecting against both AZ-level failures and Region-wide outages simultaneously.\\nAWS is not redundant by default for customer workloads. Customers must architect their own applications for redundancy using the available infrastructure components. Deploying in a single Availability Zone provides no redundancy, as an AZ failure would take the entire application offline. Deploying across multiple Availability Zones in a single Region protects against AZ-level failures but leaves the application vulnerable to a Region-wide outage."},{"id":"q514","question":"How can you increase your application\'s fault-tolerance while it is being hosted in AWS?","options":{"A":"Deploy your application across multiple EC2 instances","B":"Deploy your application across multiple Availability Zones","C":"Host your application on one powerful EC2 instance type instead of multiple smaller instances","D":"Deploy the underlying application resources across multiple subnets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Deploying across multiple Availability Zones ensures that if one AZ experiences a failure, the application continues running in another, providing fault tolerance through physical redundancy within a Region.\\nDeploying across multiple EC2 instances improves capacity and availability within a single location but does not protect against an AZ-level failure if all instances are in the same zone. Hosting on one powerful EC2 instance consolidates the application onto a single point of failure, which reduces fault tolerance rather than increasing it. Deploying across multiple subnets improves network segmentation but does not provide fault tolerance unless those subnets span multiple Availability Zones."},{"id":"q517","question":"Which of the following is a benefit of the \\"Loose Coupling\\" architecture principle?","options":{"A":"It eliminates the need for change management","B":"It allows for Cross-Region Replication","C":"It helps AWS customers reduce Privileged Access to AWS resources","D":"It allows individual application components or services to be modified without affecting other components"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Loose coupling allows individual components to be modified, updated, or replaced independently without affecting other parts of the system, enabling faster development cycles and more resilient architectures.\\nEliminating the need for change management is incorrect because change management remains necessary in any architecture. Loose coupling makes changes safer and less disruptive, but does not remove the need to manage them. Cross-Region Replication is a data redundancy and disaster recovery strategy that is unrelated to the architectural principle of reducing dependencies between components. Reducing Privileged Access to AWS resources is a security practice related to the principle of least privilege, not a benefit of loose coupling."},{"id":"q519","question":"Which of the following Cloud Computing deployment models eliminates the need to run and maintain physical data centers?","options":{"A":"On-premises","B":"IaaS","C":"PaaS","D":"Cloud"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The cloud deployment model eliminates the need to run and maintain physical data centers entirely, as all infrastructure is hosted and managed by the cloud provider in their facilities.\\nOn-premises is a deployment model where all infrastructure is owned and operated within an organization\'s own facilities, which is the opposite of eliminating physical data center management. IaaS is a cloud service model that provides virtualized compute, storage, and networking resources, and is not a deployment model that addresses where or how infrastructure is physically managed. PaaS is a cloud service model that provides a managed platform for application development and deployment, and is similarly not a deployment model that determines physical data center responsibility."},{"id":"q530","question":"AWS provides disaster recovery capability by allowing customers to deploy infrastructure into multiple [...].","options":{"A":"Regions","B":"Transportation devices","C":"Support plans","D":"Edge locations"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Regions are geographically separate locations around the world, meaning infrastructure deployed across multiple Regions remains operational even if an entire Region is affected by an outage.\\nTransportation devices are physical data transfer hardware such as AWS Snow Family devices and have no role in disaster recovery architecture. Support plans are subscription tiers that provide access to AWS technical assistance and do not affect infrastructure availability or redundancy. Edge locations are points of presence used for content delivery and caching, not designed for deploying full infrastructure for disaster recovery purposes."},{"id":"q533","question":"Which pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework provides recommendations to help customers select the right compute resources based on workload requirements?","options":{"A":"Operational Excellence","B":"Security","C":"Performance Efficiency","D":"Reliability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Performance Efficiency pillar of the Well-Architected Framework focuses on selecting the right resource types and sizes based on workload requirements to use computing resources efficiently.\\nOperational Excellence focuses on running and monitoring systems to deliver business value and continually improving processes and procedures, and does not specifically address compute resource selection based on workload requirements. Security focuses on protecting information and systems through risk assessment, access management, and controls, and is not concerned with matching compute resources to workload performance needs. Reliability focuses on ensuring a workload can recover from failures and dynamically acquire resources to meet demand, but its emphasis is on resilience and recovery rather than on selecting the right compute resource types for efficiency."},{"id":"q541","question":"Which is a recommended pattern for designing a highly available architecture on AWS?","options":{"A":"Ensure that components have low-latency network connectivity","B":"Run enough Amazon EC2 instances to operate at peak load","C":"Ensure that the application is designed to accommodate failure of any single component","D":"Use a monolithic application that handles all operations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Designing an application to accommodate the failure of any single component, through redundancy, health checks, and automatic failover, is the core principle of designing for failure and is the recommended pattern for achieving high availability on AWS.\\nEnsuring low-latency network connectivity is a performance optimization consideration that improves user experience, but low latency alone does not protect an application from component failures or maintain availability during an outage. Running enough EC2 instances to handle peak load addresses capacity planning but does not build in redundancy or fault tolerance, meaning a single component failure could still bring down the application. A monolithic application that handles all operations consolidates functionality into a single unit, which creates a single point of failure and is the opposite of a highly available architecture that can survive individual component failures."},{"id":"q542","question":"Which AWS characteristics make AWS cost effective for a workload with dynamic user demand?","options":{"A":"High availability","B":"Shared security model","C":"Elasticity","D":"Pay-as-you-go pricing","E":"Reliability"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Elasticity lets you automatically scale resources up or down with demand, and pay-as-you-go pricing ensures you only pay for what you actually use, making both ideal for workloads with unpredictable, dynamic demand.\\nHigh availability ensures that applications remain accessible despite failures by using redundancy across multiple Availability Zones, and while it contributes to reliability it does not directly reduce costs for workloads with dynamic demand. The shared security model defines the division of security responsibilities between AWS and the customer, and is a governance principle rather than a characteristic that makes AWS cost effective for variable workloads. Reliability refers to the ability of a workload to perform its intended function correctly and consistently, and while important for system design it is not itself a pricing or cost efficiency characteristic that reduces costs for dynamic demand."},{"id":"q557","question":"When architecting cloud applications, which of the following are a key design principle?","options":{"A":"Use the largest instance possible","B":"Provision capacity for peak load","C":"Use the Scrum development process","D":"Implement elasticity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Elasticity is a key cloud design principle that means automatically scaling compute resources up or down based on actual demand, avoiding over-provisioning and reducing costs compared to traditional fixed-capacity infrastructure.\\nUsing the largest instance possible is the opposite of good cloud design, as it leads to over-provisioning and unnecessary cost rather than matching capacity to actual demand. Provisioning capacity for peak load is a traditional on-premises approach that results in idle resources during normal periods, which cloud elasticity is specifically designed to replace. Using the Scrum development process is a software delivery methodology unrelated to cloud architecture design principles."},{"id":"q561","question":"Which of the following is a benefit of using the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Permissive security removes the administrative burden","B":"Ability to focus on revenue-generating activities","C":"Control over cloud network hardware","D":"Choice of specific cloud hardware vendors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS offloads infrastructure management to its own teams, freeing customers from data center operations so they can redirect time and resources toward product development, customer engagement, and other revenue-generating activities.\\nPermissive security is not an AWS benefit and directly contradicts AWS\'s security model, which is built on the principle of least privilege and layered access controls. Control over cloud network hardware is not available to customers, as AWS manages all physical networking infrastructure on their behalf. Choice of specific cloud hardware vendors is not offered to customers, as AWS selects and manages its own hardware independently of customer preferences."},{"id":"q572","question":"Which AWS feature should a customer leverage to achieve high availability of an application?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"Availability Zones","C":"Data centers","D":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Deploying across multiple Availability Zones ensures an application remains available if one zone fails, as each AZ is a physically separate location within a Region with independent power, networking, and connectivity.\\nAWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network link between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, which improves connectivity but does not provide application redundancy or fault tolerance. Data centers are the physical facilities that make up Availability Zones and are managed entirely by AWS, so customers cannot directly leverage individual data centers as an architectural feature. Amazon VPC provides an isolated virtual network within AWS for resource deployment and access control, but does not itself provide redundancy or ensure an application stays available during a failure."},{"id":"q574","question":"Which of the following is a cloud architectural design principle?","options":{"A":"Scale up not out","B":"Loosely couple components","C":"Build monolithic systems","D":"Use commercial database software"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loosely coupling components means designing them to interact through well-defined interfaces such as queues or APIs, so that changes or failures in one component do not directly impact others.\\nScaling up not out describes vertical scaling, which is an on-premises approach that cloud best practices discourage in favor of horizontal scaling through adding more instances. Building monolithic systems consolidates all application components into a single tightly coupled unit, which is the opposite of the cloud design principle of breaking applications into loosely coupled, independently deployable components. Using commercial database software is a technology procurement decision rather than an architectural design principle, and cloud best practices favor managed and purpose-built database services over proprietary commercial software where appropriate."},{"id":"q577","question":"The financial benefits of using AWS are:","options":{"A":"Reduced Total Cost of Ownership (TCO)","B":"Increased capital expenditure (capex)","C":"Reduced operational expenditure (opex)","D":"Deferred payment plans for startups","E":"Business credit lines for startups"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS reduces Total Cost of Ownership by eliminating data center capital expenses, and reduces operational expenditure through pay-as-you-go pricing and managed services that lower staffing and maintenance costs.\\nIncreased capital expenditure describes the opposite of an AWS financial benefit. Moving to AWS replaces large upfront hardware investments with variable operational costs, reducing rather than increasing capital expenditure. Deferred payment plans for startups is not a standard AWS financial benefit. AWS charges are billed monthly based on actual usage, and there are no deferred payment arrangements offered as a general benefit to startup customers. Business credit lines for startups are not provided by AWS as a standard financial benefit. AWS does have programs such as AWS Activate that provide credits to startups, but these are promotional credits rather than credit lines or financing arrangements."},{"id":"q583","question":"What costs are included when comparing AWS Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) with on-premises TCO?","options":{"A":"Project management","B":"Antivirus software licensing","C":"Data center security","D":"Software development"},"correct":"C","explanation":"On-premises TCO includes physical data center security costs such as guards, access controls, and surveillance. These are eliminated when moving to AWS, where physical security is entirely AWS\'s responsibility.\\nProject management costs apply equally to both on-premises and cloud environments and are not a differentiating factor in a TCO comparison. Antivirus software licensing is an application-layer cost that remains the customer\'s responsibility regardless of whether they run on-premises or on AWS. Software development costs are also consistent across environments and do not change based on infrastructure hosting decisions."},{"id":"q590","question":"Which of the following is an important architectural design principle when designing cloud applications?","options":{"A":"Use multiple Availability Zones","B":"Use tightly coupled components","C":"Use open source software","D":"Provision extra capacity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Using multiple Availability Zones ensures high availability by distributing your application across physically isolated data centers, so that a failure in any single zone does not take the entire application offline.\\nUsing tightly coupled components is an anti-pattern in cloud architecture, as tight dependencies between components mean a failure in one can cascade and bring down others. Using open source software is a development and licensing choice that has no bearing on cloud architectural design principles for availability or resilience. Provisioning extra capacity contradicts the cloud principle of elasticity, which favors scaling dynamically based on actual demand rather than guessing and over-provisioning upfront."},{"id":"q600","question":"Which AWS feature will reduce the customer\'s total cost of ownership (TCO)?","options":{"A":"Shared responsibility security model","B":"Single tenancy","C":"Elastic computing","D":"Encryption"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Elastic computing lets customers match resource capacity to actual demand, scaling up during peaks and down during lulls, eliminating payment for idle infrastructure and directly reducing total cost of ownership.\\nThe Shared Responsibility Model defines the division of security duties between AWS and the customer, but does not itself reduce infrastructure costs or TCO. Single tenancy dedicates hardware to a single customer, which typically increases cost compared to the shared multi-tenant model that underpins AWS\'s economies of scale. Encryption is a security control that protects data at rest and in transit, and has no direct impact on infrastructure costs or TCO."},{"id":"q604","question":"Which feature of the AWS Cloud will support an international company\'s requirement for low latency to all of its customers?","options":{"A":"Fault tolerance","B":"Global reach","C":"Pay-as-you-go pricing","D":"High availability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS\'s global reach, with Regions and edge locations spread worldwide, allows companies to deploy resources close to their international customers, directly reducing network latency.\\nFault tolerance refers to a system\'s ability to continue operating despite component failures, not to reducing geographic latency. Pay-as-you-go pricing is a billing model and has no effect on network latency or geographic reach. High availability ensures systems remain operational with minimal downtime, but does not address the physical proximity needed to reduce latency for international customers."},{"id":"q614","question":"Which design principles for cloud architecture are recommended when re-architecting a large monolithic application?","options":{"A":"Use manual monitoring","B":"Use fixed servers","C":"Implement loose coupling","D":"Rely on individual components","E":"Design for scalability"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Loose coupling reduces inter-component dependencies so that a failure in one part of the application does not cascade to others, and designing for scalability ensures each component can independently handle varying loads, both being essential principles when breaking apart a monolithic architecture.\\nManual monitoring relies on human observation rather than automated alerting and is an anti-pattern in cloud architecture. Fixed servers contradict the cloud principle of elasticity and scalability, locking capacity to a predetermined amount regardless of demand. Relying on individual components is the defining characteristic of a monolithic architecture and is the problem being solved, not a recommended design principle."},{"id":"q621","question":"How do customers benefit from Amazon\'s massive economies of scale?","options":{"A":"Periodic price reductions as the result of Amazon\'s operational efficiencies","B":"New Amazon EC2 instance types providing the latest hardware","C":"The ability to scale up and down when needed","D":"Increased reliability in the underlying hardware of Amazon EC2 instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS passes its operational efficiencies and massive purchasing power to customers through periodic price reductions, lowering the cost of cloud services as AWS\'s scale grows.\\nNew EC2 instance types providing the latest hardware is a product development activity, not a direct financial benefit passed to customers from economies of scale. The ability to scale up and down when needed describes elasticity, which is a separate cloud benefit unrelated to economies of scale. Increased reliability in the underlying hardware describes AWS infrastructure quality, not a cost or efficiency benefit derived from operating at massive scale."},{"id":"q634","question":"Which of the following are benefits of the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Unlimited uptime","B":"Elasticity","C":"Agility","D":"Colocation","E":"Capital expenses"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Elasticity allows resources to automatically scale up and down based on demand, and agility enables rapid provisioning and experimentation without long procurement cycles. Both are core advantages of the AWS Cloud over traditional infrastructure.\\nUnlimited uptime is not a guaranteed or marketed AWS benefit, as AWS services are subject to availability SLAs but not unconditional uptime. Colocation refers to housing your own physical servers in a third-party data center, which is a traditional infrastructure model rather than a cloud benefit. Capital expenses are a cost of traditional on-premises infrastructure that AWS replaces with variable operational expenditure, making them a disadvantage of the old model, not a benefit of the cloud."},{"id":"q640","question":"When comparing AWS Cloud with on-premises Total Cost of Ownership, which expenses must be considered?","options":{"A":"Software development","B":"Project management","C":"Storage hardware","D":"Physical servers","E":"Antivirus software license"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Storage hardware and physical servers are on-premises capital expenses that must be purchased, maintained, and eventually replaced, making them key differentiators in a TCO comparison since they are eliminated when moving to AWS.\\nSoftware development costs remain consistent regardless of whether infrastructure is on-premises or in the cloud and are not a differentiating factor in a TCO analysis. Project management costs also apply equally across both environments and do not change based on infrastructure hosting decisions. Antivirus software licensing remains the customer\'s responsibility in both on-premises and cloud environments and is not eliminated by moving to AWS."},{"id":"q642","question":"Which scenarios represent the concept of elasticity on AWS?","options":{"A":"Scaling the number of Amazon EC2 instances based on traffic","B":"Resizing Amazon RDS instances as business needs change","C":"Automatically directing traffic to less-utilized Amazon EC2 instances","D":"Using AWS compliance documents to accelerate the compliance process","E":"Having the ability to create and govern environments using code"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Scaling EC2 instances horizontally based on traffic and resizing RDS instances vertically as business needs change both demonstrate elasticity, which is the ability to adapt resource capacity dynamically to match demand.\\nAutomatically directing traffic to less-utilized EC2 instances describes load balancing, which optimizes resource utilization but does not change the amount of resources provisioned. Using AWS compliance documents to accelerate the compliance process describes the concept of inherited compliance, not elasticity. Having the ability to create and govern environments using code describes infrastructure as code, which is a separate cloud principle unrelated to dynamic resource scaling."},{"id":"q644","question":"A company is considering moving its on-premises data center to AWS. What factors should be included in doing a Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) analysis?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2 instance availability","B":"Power consumption of the data center","C":"Labor costs to replace old servers","D":"Application developer time","E":"Database engine capacity"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Power consumption and labor costs for server replacement are significant on-premises expenses that disappear when migrating to AWS, making them the relevant factors in a TCO comparison.\\nAmazon EC2 instance availability is an AWS-side consideration, not an on-premises cost factor included in a TCO analysis. Application developer time remains consistent regardless of hosting environment and is not a differentiating infrastructure cost. Database engine capacity is a performance and sizing consideration, not an on-premises cost component that changes when moving to AWS."},{"id":"q651","question":"Which of the following deployment models enables customers to fully trade their capital IT expenses for operational expenses?","options":{"A":"On-premises","B":"Hybrid","C":"Cloud","D":"Platform as a service"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The cloud deployment model fully replaces upfront capital expenditure on hardware and facilities with variable operational expenses based on actual usage, eliminating the need to own or maintain physical infrastructure.\\nAn on-premises deployment requires the organization to purchase, own, and maintain all physical hardware and facilities, which preserves rather than eliminates capital expenditure and offers no conversion to operational expenses. A hybrid deployment combines on-premises infrastructure with cloud resources, meaning capital expenditure on owned hardware is retained alongside operational cloud costs rather than being fully traded for operational expenses. Platform as a service is a cloud service delivery model in which a provider manages a platform for building and running applications, and is not a deployment model describing where infrastructure is hosted."},{"id":"q662","question":"Which architectural principle is used when deploying an Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) instance in Multiple Availability Zone mode?","options":{"A":"Implement loose coupling","B":"Design for failure","C":"Automate everything that can be automated","D":"Use services, not servers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Deploying RDS in Multi-AZ mode applies the design for failure principle by maintaining a synchronous standby replica in another Availability Zone, automatically failing over if the primary instance becomes unavailable.\\nLoose coupling refers to reducing interdependencies between application components so failures do not cascade, not to database redundancy across zones. Automate everything that can be automated refers to replacing manual operational tasks with scripted or managed processes to improve consistency. Use services, not servers refers to leveraging managed AWS services instead of provisioning and maintaining your own infrastructure."},{"id":"q666","question":"A company\'s web application currently has tight dependencies on underlying components, so when one component fails the entire web application fails. Applying which AWS Cloud design principle will address the current design issue?","options":{"A":"Implementing elasticity, enabling the application to scale up or scale down as demand changes","B":"Enabling several EC2 instances to run in parallel to achieve better performance","C":"Focusing on decoupling components by isolating them and ensuring individual components can function when other components fail","D":"Doubling EC2 computing resources to increase system fault tolerance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Decoupling components means isolating them so each can function independently, communicating through asynchronous interfaces rather than direct calls, so a failure in one component does not bring down the entire application.\\nImplementing elasticity addresses scaling resources up and down in response to demand, but does not resolve the tight dependency problem that causes cascading failures. Enabling EC2 instances to run in parallel improves performance and throughput but does not isolate components from each other\'s failures. Doubling EC2 computing resources adds capacity but does not change the tightly coupled architecture, meaning a component failure would still take down the whole application."},{"id":"q671","question":"Which of the following is an AWS Well-Architected Framework design principle related to reliability?","options":{"A":"Deployment to a single Availability Zone","B":"Ability to recover from failure","C":"Design for cost optimization","D":"Perform operations as code"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ability to recover from failure is a core design principle of the Reliability pillar, which focuses on ensuring workloads perform their intended function correctly and consistently, including through automatic recovery mechanisms.\\nDeployment to a single Availability Zone is actually an anti-pattern for reliability, as it creates a single point of failure rather than providing fault tolerance. Design for cost optimization describes the goal of the Cost Optimization pillar, not the Reliability pillar. Perform operations as code is a design principle of the Operational Excellence pillar, focused on automating operational tasks rather than on failure recovery."},{"id":"q672","question":"What is an advantage of using the AWS Cloud over a traditional on-premises solution?","options":{"A":"Users do not have to guess about future capacity needs","B":"Users can utilize existing hardware contracts for purchases","C":"Users can fix costs no matter what their traffic is","D":"Users can avoid audits by using reports from AWS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS eliminates the need to guess future capacity needs by providing on-demand scaling, allowing organizations to provision exactly what they need and adjust in minutes rather than committing to fixed hardware infrastructure months or years in advance.\\nUtilizing existing hardware contracts for purchases is a characteristic of traditional on-premises procurement rather than an advantage of AWS; on the cloud, customers avoid hardware contracts entirely by paying only for what they consume. Fixing costs regardless of traffic is not an advantage of AWS; cloud costs scale with usage, and AWS offers variable pricing that adjusts to actual demand rather than fixed costs, which is one of its financial advantages over traditional fixed-capacity infrastructure. Avoiding audits using AWS reports is not a valid advantage; while AWS Artifact provides compliance reports that support customer audits, customers remain subject to their own regulatory audit requirements and cannot avoid audits by virtue of using AWS."},{"id":"q681","question":"Why should a company choose AWS instead of a traditional data center?","options":{"A":"AWS provides users with full control over the underlying resources","B":"AWS does not require long-term contracts and provides a pay-as-you-go model","C":"AWS offers edge locations in every country, supporting global reach","D":"AWS has no limits on the number of resources that can be created"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS offers a pay-as-you-go pricing model with no long-term contracts required for most services, allowing companies to avoid large upfront capital expenditure and pay only for the resources they actually consume.\\nAWS does not provide users with full control over the underlying physical resources; AWS manages the hardware, hypervisor, and physical infrastructure, while customers manage their workloads and data. AWS does not have edge locations in every country; AWS has edge locations in many countries for services like CloudFront, but coverage is not universal across every nation, and this is not a core reason to choose AWS over a traditional data center. While AWS has very high service limits, there are limits on the number of resources that can be created per account per Region; customers can request limit increases, but resources are not truly unlimited, making this statement inaccurate."},{"id":"q686","question":"How does AWS MOST effectively reduce computing costs for a growing start-up company?","options":{"A":"It provides on-demand resources for peak usage","B":"It automates the provisioning of individual developer environments","C":"It automates customer relationship management","D":"It implements a fixed monthly computing budget"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS provides on-demand resources that can be provisioned instantly for peak usage and released when not needed, so a growing startup only pays for the compute capacity it actually uses rather than purchasing hardware sized for maximum anticipated load.\\nAutomating the provisioning of individual developer environments is a benefit of infrastructure as code tools such as AWS CloudFormation, but is a developer productivity feature rather than the primary mechanism by which AWS reduces computing costs for a growing startup. Automating customer relationship management describes CRM software functionality and is entirely unrelated to how AWS reduces computing infrastructure costs for companies. Implementing a fixed monthly computing budget is a financial governance practice that sets spending limits, but a fixed budget does not itself reduce computing costs and constrains flexibility rather than enabling cost-efficient scaling with demand."},{"id":"q687","question":"A startup is working on a new application that needs to go to market quickly. The application requirements may need to be adjusted in the near future. Which of the following is a characteristic of the AWS Cloud that would meet this specific need?","options":{"A":"Elasticity","B":"Reliability","C":"Performance","D":"Agility"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Agility in the AWS Cloud means the ability to quickly provision resources, iterate on application requirements, and deploy changes rapidly, enabling startups to go to market fast and adapt as needs change.\\nElasticity refers to dynamically scaling resources up or down in response to demand, which addresses capacity management rather than the speed of building and iterating on new applications. Reliability refers to a workload\'s ability to perform its intended function correctly and recover automatically from failures, which is an architectural quality rather than a characteristic that accelerates time to market. Performance refers to using computing resources efficiently to meet workload requirements, which is unrelated to the speed of provisioning and iterating on new application requirements."},{"id":"q691","question":"When comparing AWS with on-premises Total Cost of Ownership (TCO), what costs are included?","options":{"A":"Data center security","B":"Business analysis","C":"Project management","D":"Operating system administration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Data center security costs such as physical access controls, surveillance, and guards are part of on-premises TCO that are eliminated when migrating to AWS, where physical security is included in the service.\\nBusiness analysis is a professional function that evaluates organizational needs and solutions, and is not a physical infrastructure cost included in an on-premises TCO comparison. Project management is a professional service cost associated with planning and executing initiatives, and is not a data center infrastructure expense factored into TCO. Operating system administration is a labour cost that customers retain responsibility for under the Shared Responsibility Model regardless of whether they are on-premises or on AWS, so it is not eliminated by migration and does not differentiate the two TCO models."},{"id":"q698","question":"Which of the following is an advantage of using AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS audits user data","B":"Data is automatically secure","C":"There is no guessing on capacity needs","D":"AWS manages compliance needs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS eliminates the need to guess capacity needs by providing on-demand scaling, so organizations can provision exactly what they need and adjust in minutes rather than purchasing hardware based on peak capacity forecasts.\\nAWS does not audit user data; AWS has strict policies and controls against accessing customer data without authorisation, and the customer remains responsible for the privacy and governance of data stored in their AWS environment. Data stored in AWS is not automatically secure; customers must actively configure encryption, access controls, and security settings for their own data, as the shared responsibility model requires customers to protect the data they put in the cloud. AWS does not manage the compliance needs of individual customers; while AWS provides compliance certifications for its infrastructure and tools through Artifact to support customer compliance efforts, each customer is responsible for applying those frameworks to their own workloads and meeting their specific regulatory requirements."},{"id":"q701","question":"Which principles are used to architect applications for reliability on the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Design for automated failure recovery","B":"Use multiple Availability Zones","C":"Manage changes via documented processes","D":"Test for moderate demand to ensure reliability","E":"Backup recovery to an on-premises environment"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Designing for automated failure recovery ensures systems self-heal without manual intervention, and deploying across multiple Availability Zones protects against AZ-level failures, both being core reliability principles.\\nManaging changes via documented processes is an Operational Excellence principle focused on controlled change management, not a reliability architecture principle. Testing for moderate demand does not reflect a recognized reliability principle, as the Well-Architected Framework recommends testing recovery procedures and scaling under peak and failure conditions rather than moderate load. Backing up recovery to an on-premises environment introduces a dependency on local infrastructure, which contradicts the cloud reliability principle of eliminating single points of failure and keeping recovery within the AWS environment."},{"id":"q703","question":"What is an example of high availability in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Consulting AWS technical support at any time day or night","B":"Ensuring an application remains accessible, even if a resource fails","C":"Making any AWS service available for use by paying on demand","D":"Deploying in any part of the world using AWS Regions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"High availability means an application remains accessible even when individual resources fail, achieved through redundancy across Availability Zones, auto-scaling, and automatic failover mechanisms.\\nConsulting AWS technical support at any time describes the 24/7 availability of support plans, which is a service offering rather than an architectural characteristic of the cloud. Making any AWS service available by paying on demand describes the pay-as-you-go pricing model, which is a billing concept unrelated to fault tolerance or uptime. Deploying in any part of the world using AWS Regions describes global reach, which is a separate cloud benefit and does not by itself ensure an application remains accessible if a resource fails."},{"id":"q721","question":"Which benefit of the AWS Cloud supports matching the supply of resources with changing workload demands?","options":{"A":"Security","B":"Reliability","C":"Elasticity","D":"High availability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Elasticity is the ability to automatically scale resources up or down to match changing workload demands, ensuring you have the right amount of capacity without over-provisioning or under-provisioning.\\nSecurity refers to protecting data, systems, and assets through controls and risk mitigation strategies, and is unrelated to matching resource supply with demand. Reliability refers to a workload\'s ability to perform its intended function correctly and recover automatically from failures, which is an architectural quality rather than a capacity management concept. High availability refers to keeping an application operational during failures through redundancy, which addresses uptime rather than the dynamic adjustment of resources to match workload demand."},{"id":"q723","question":"Which of the following are benefits of hosting infrastructure in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"There are no upfront commitments","B":"AWS manages all security in the cloud","C":"Users have the ability to provision resources on demand","D":"Users have access to free and unlimited storage","E":"Users have control over the physical infrastructure"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS requires no upfront hardware commitments, allowing organizations to pay only for what they consume, and resources can be provisioned on demand in minutes allowing businesses to scale quickly without waiting for hardware procurement and installation.\\nAWS does not manage all security in the cloud; under the shared responsibility model, AWS secures the underlying infrastructure while customers are responsible for securing their applications, data, and configurations that run on top of that infrastructure. Users do not have access to free and unlimited storage on AWS; storage services such as S3 and EBS are priced based on usage, and while there are free tier limits for new accounts, storage is not free or unlimited in general. Users do not have control over the physical infrastructure; one of the trade-offs of moving to the cloud is that customers give up physical access to and control of the underlying hardware, trusting AWS to manage it securely and reliably."},{"id":"q728","question":"What are the benefits of using the AWS Cloud for companies with customers in many countries around the world?","options":{"A":"Companies can deploy applications in multiple AWS Regions to reduce latency","B":"Amazon Translate automatically translates third-party website interfaces into multiple languages","C":"Amazon CloudFront has multiple edge locations around the world to reduce latency","D":"Amazon Comprehend allows users to build applications that can respond to user requests in many languages","E":"Elastic Load Balancing can distribute application web traffic to multiple AWS Regions around the world, which reduces latency"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Deploying across multiple AWS Regions places resources physically closer to international users, and CloudFront\'s global edge locations cache content near end users worldwide, both directly reducing latency for global customers.\\nAmazon Translate is a machine translation service for converting text between languages and does not automatically translate website interfaces without custom integration. Amazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service for analyzing and extracting meaning from text, not a tool for enabling multilingual application responses. Elastic Load Balancing distributes traffic across targets within a single Region and cannot route traffic between Regions to reduce geographic latency."},{"id":"q731","question":"When a company provisions web servers in multiple AWS Regions, what is being increased?","options":{"A":"Coupling","B":"Availability","C":"Security","D":"Durability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Provisioning web servers across multiple AWS Regions increases availability by ensuring that if one entire Region experiences an outage, users can be served from servers in another Region.\\nCoupling refers to the degree of dependency between application components, and is not affected by how many Regions servers are deployed in. Security is not increased simply by deploying across multiple Regions, as security depends on configuration and controls applied consistently regardless of geographic distribution. Durability refers to the long-term persistence of stored data without corruption or loss, which is a storage characteristic unrelated to where web servers are provisioned."},{"id":"q736","question":"Which of the following are pillars of the AWS Well-Architected Framework?","options":{"A":"Multiple Availability Zones","B":"Performance efficiency","C":"Security","D":"Encryption usage","E":"High availability"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"The AWS Well-Architected Framework has six pillars: Operational Excellence, Security, Reliability, Performance Efficiency, Cost Optimization, and Sustainability. Performance Efficiency and Security are both official pillars.\\nMultiple Availability Zones is an infrastructure concept and a deployment strategy, not a Well-Architected pillar. Encryption usage is a security practice that falls under the Security pillar, not a pillar in its own right. High availability is a design goal associated with the Reliability pillar, not a standalone pillar."},{"id":"q741","question":"Treating infrastructure as code in the AWS Cloud allows users to:","options":{"A":"Automate migration of on-premises hardware to AWS data centers","B":"Let a third party automate an audit of the AWS infrastructure","C":"Turn over application code to AWS so it can run on the AWS infrastructure","D":"Automate the infrastructure provisioning process"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Infrastructure as code allows users to define and automate the provisioning of AWS resources using templates, enabling repeatable, version-controlled deployments without manual configuration.\\nAutomating migration of on-premises hardware to AWS data centers is not possible through infrastructure as code, as physical hardware migration is a separate process managed outside of code-based provisioning. Letting a third party automate an audit of AWS infrastructure describes a compliance or security activity unrelated to infrastructure provisioning. Turning over application code to AWS to run on its infrastructure misrepresents how AWS works, as customers are always responsible for their own application code and deployment."},{"id":"q750","question":"What are the immediate benefits of using the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Increased IT staff","B":"Capital expenses are replaced with variable expenses","C":"User control of infrastructure","D":"Increased agility","E":"AWS holds responsibility for security in the cloud"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Moving to AWS immediately replaces large upfront capital expenses with variable pay-as-you-go expenses, and increases agility by enabling rapid provisioning of resources in minutes instead of weeks.\\nMoving to AWS typically reduces the need for undifferentiated infrastructure management work rather than increasing IT staff headcount. User control of infrastructure decreases when moving to AWS, as physical hardware management is handled entirely by AWS under the Shared Responsibility Model. AWS holds responsibility for security of the cloud, covering physical infrastructure and managed services, but security in the cloud remains the customer\'s responsibility, making this option factually incorrect."},{"id":"q752","question":"What should users do if they want to install an application in geographically isolated locations?","options":{"A":"Install the application using multiple internet gateways","B":"Deploy the application to an Amazon VPC","C":"Deploy the application to multiple AWS Regions","D":"Configure the application using multiple NAT gateways"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Deploying to multiple AWS Regions places an application in geographically isolated locations around the world, each with independent infrastructure for maximum geographic separation.\\nInstalling the application using multiple internet gateways is incorrect. Internet gateways connect a VPC to the internet and have no bearing on geographic isolation. Deploying the application to an Amazon VPC is incorrect. A VPC is a private network within a single Region and does not provide geographic isolation across locations. Configuring the application using multiple NAT gateways is incorrect. NAT gateways enable outbound internet access for private subnets and are unrelated to geographic distribution."},{"id":"q753","question":"A system in the AWS Cloud is designed to withstand the failure of one or more components. What is this an example of?","options":{"A":"Elasticity","B":"High Availability","C":"Scalability","D":"Agility"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A system designed to withstand component failures is an example of high availability, which ensures an application remains accessible through redundancy and automatic failover mechanisms.\\nElasticity refers to dynamically scaling resources up or down in response to demand, which addresses capacity management rather than fault tolerance. Scalability refers to the ability to handle increased load by adding resources, which is about growth capacity rather than continued operation during failures. Agility refers to the speed at which resources can be provisioned and iterated on, which is unrelated to a system\'s ability to withstand component failures."},{"id":"q761","question":"A company wants to build its new application workloads in the AWS Cloud instead of using on-premises resources. What expense can be reduced using the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"The cost of writing custom-built Java or Node .js code","B":"Penetration testing for security","C":"Hardware required to support new applications","D":"Writing specific test cases for third-party applications"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Moving workloads to AWS eliminates the capital expenditure required to purchase, install, rack, and cable physical servers and networking equipment for new applications, replacing it with pay-as-you-go consumption pricing.\\nThe cost of writing custom application code in languages such as Java or Node.js remains unchanged when moving to the cloud, as software development is a people and process cost that is independent of whether the infrastructure is on-premises or hosted on AWS. Penetration testing costs for security remain with the application team whether on-premises or in the cloud, as the need to test application and infrastructure security exists regardless of where the workload runs, and AWS provides guidance for testing its own components separately. Writing test cases for third-party applications is a development and quality assurance task that remains consistent regardless of infrastructure location, as it is determined by the third-party software requirements rather than by the hosting environment."},{"id":"q763","question":"What does it mean if a user deploys a hybrid cloud architecture on AWS?","options":{"A":"All resources run using on-premises infrastructure","B":"Some resources run on-premises and some run in a colocation center","C":"All resources run in the AWS Cloud","D":"Some resources run on-premises and some run in the AWS Cloud"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A hybrid cloud architecture means some resources run on-premises in existing data centers while other resources run in the AWS Cloud, connected through services such as AWS VPN or AWS Direct Connect.\\nRunning all resources using on-premises infrastructure describes a fully on-premises deployment with no cloud involvement, which is the opposite of a cloud architecture. Running some resources on-premises and some in a colocation center describes a split between owned and rented physical data center space, but does not involve any cloud provider and therefore does not constitute a hybrid cloud architecture. Running all resources in the AWS Cloud describes a fully cloud-native deployment, which is a public cloud architecture rather than a hybrid one."},{"id":"q764","question":"What are the advantages of the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Fixed rate monthly cost","B":"No need to guess capacity requirements","C":"Increased speed to market","D":"Increased upfront capital expenditure","E":"Physical access to cloud data centers"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS eliminates the need to guess capacity requirements by providing on-demand scaling, and increases speed to market by enabling rapid provisioning of resources in minutes rather than weeks.\\nFixed rate monthly cost is the opposite of how AWS pricing works. AWS uses a variable pay-as-you-go model, not fixed monthly billing. Increased upfront capital expenditure is also the opposite of an AWS advantage. One of the core benefits of cloud is replacing upfront capital expenditure with variable operational costs. Physical access to cloud data centers is not available to customers and is not an AWS advantage."},{"id":"q765","question":"When comparing the total cost of ownership (TCO) of an on-premises infrastructure to a cloud architecture, what costs should be considered?","options":{"A":"The credit card processing fees for application transactions in the cloud","B":"The cost of purchasing and installing server hardware in the on-premises data","C":"The cost of administering the infrastructure, including operating system and software installations, patches, backups, and recovering from failures","D":"The costs of third-party penetration testing","E":"The advertising costs associated with an ongoing enterprise-wide campaign"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"The cost of purchasing and installing physical server hardware on-premises, and the ongoing administrative cost of managing infrastructure including OS installations, patching, backups, and failure recovery, are both direct costs of maintaining on-premises infrastructure that disappear when migrating to AWS and should be included in a TCO comparison.\\nCredit card processing fees for application transactions are a business operational cost that exists regardless of whether the infrastructure is on-premises or in the cloud, as they are determined by the payment processor rather than the hosting environment and are therefore not relevant to a cloud versus on-premises TCO comparison. Third-party penetration testing costs may apply in both on-premises and cloud environments depending on the organization\'s security requirements, and are not a distinguishing cost factor that differentiates on-premises TCO from cloud architecture costs. Advertising costs for enterprise-wide campaigns are a marketing expense entirely unrelated to the infrastructure hosting model, and have no connection to the cost comparison between on-premises data center infrastructure and cloud architecture."},{"id":"q772","question":"What is an AWS Cloud design best practice?","options":{"A":"Tight coupling of components","B":"Single point of failure","C":"High availability","D":"Overprovisioning of resources"},"correct":"C","explanation":"High availability is an AWS Cloud design best practice that ensures applications remain accessible through redundancy, multi-AZ deployments, and automatic failover mechanisms.\\nTight coupling of components is an anti-pattern in cloud design, as it creates dependencies between services that cause cascading failures and contradicts the best practice of loose coupling. Single point of failure is an architectural weakness that cloud design best practices are specifically aimed at eliminating through redundancy and distributed deployments. Overprovisioning of resources is an on-premises habit that cloud adoption is intended to eliminate through pay-as-you-go pricing and elastic scaling rather than a best practice to follow."},{"id":"q773","question":"Why is AWS more economical than traditional data centers for applications with varying compute workloads?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) costs are billed on a monthly basis","B":"Customers retain full administrative access to their Amazon EC2 instances","C":"Amazon EC2 instances can be launched on-demand when needed","D":"Customers can permanently run enough instances to handle peak workloads"},"correct":"C","explanation":"EC2 instances can be launched on demand when needed and terminated when no longer required, so you only pay for compute during actual usage rather than maintaining hardware sized for peak capacity at all times.\\nAmazon EC2 costs are not billed on a monthly basis. Linux-based EC2 instances are billed per second with a one-minute minimum, meaning you pay only for the exact compute time consumed rather than a fixed monthly charge. Customers retaining full administrative access to their EC2 instances is a capability that gives customers control over their environment, but administrative access is not itself a reason why AWS is more economical than traditional data centers. Permanently running enough instances to handle peak workloads describes the traditional data center approach of over-provisioning for maximum anticipated load, which is exactly the model that makes traditional infrastructure less economical than the elastic, on-demand AWS model."},{"id":"q783","question":"Which of the following is an example of how moving to the AWS Cloud reduces upfront cost?","options":{"A":"By replacing large variable costs with lower capital investments","B":"By replacing large capital investments with lower variable costs","C":"By allowing the provisioning of compute and storage at a fixed level to meet peak demand","D":"By replacing the repeated scaling of virtual servers with a simpler fixed-scale model"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Moving to AWS replaces large upfront capital investments in hardware and facilities with lower, variable pay-as-you-go costs based on actual resource consumption.\\nReplacing large variable costs with lower capital investments describes the opposite of the AWS model. Traditional data centers often have both large upfront capital costs and ongoing variable operational costs, while AWS eliminates the capital component and replaces it with variable usage-based charges. Allowing the provisioning of compute and storage at a fixed level to meet peak demand describes the traditional on-premises approach of purchasing maximum anticipated capacity upfront, which is a model AWS specifically moves customers away from. Replacing the repeated scaling of virtual servers with a simpler fixed-scale model describes a static capacity model that is the opposite of the AWS approach, which enables dynamic scaling rather than replacing it with a fixed scale."},{"id":"q785","question":"Which cloud design principle aligns with AWS Cloud best practices?","options":{"A":"Create fixed dependencies among application components","B":"Aggregate services on a single instance","C":"Deploy applications in a single Availability Zone","D":"Distribute the compute load across multiple resources"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Distributing compute load across multiple resources follows the horizontal scaling best practice, improving fault tolerance and performance by avoiding single points of failure.\\nCreating fixed dependencies among application components violates the loose coupling principle, which aims to reduce interdependencies so failures do not cascade across the system. Aggregating services on a single instance creates a single point of failure and contradicts the best practice of distributing workloads across multiple resources. Deploying in a single Availability Zone exposes the application to an outage if that AZ fails, which contradicts the best practice of designing for high availability."},{"id":"q789","question":"An application designed to span multiple Availability Zones is described as:","options":{"A":"Being highly available","B":"Having global reach","C":"Using an economy of scale","D":"Having elasticity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An application spanning multiple Availability Zones remains operational if one AZ experiences an outage, which is the definition of high availability.\\nGlobal reach describes deploying across multiple Regions or using a content delivery network to serve users worldwide, which goes beyond AZ-level redundancy within a single Region. Economy of scale refers to the cost benefits AWS passes to customers through aggregated purchasing power, which is a pricing concept unrelated to application architecture. Elasticity refers to dynamically scaling resources up or down in response to demand, which is independent of how many Availability Zones an application spans."},{"id":"q790","question":"A new service using AWS must be highly available. Yet, due to regulatory requirements, all of its Amazon EC2 instances must be located in a single geographic area. According to best practices, to meet these requirements, the EC2 instances must be placed in at least two:","options":{"A":"AWS Regions","B":"Availability Zones","C":"Subnets","D":"Placement groups"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Availability Zones are isolated data center locations within a single AWS Region connected by low-latency links. Deploying EC2 instances across at least two AZs in the same Region provides high availability and meets the geographic restriction requirement.\\nAWS Regions are separate geographic areas, each containing multiple AZs. Deploying across multiple Regions would violate the requirement that all instances remain in a single geographic area, making this option non-compliant. Subnets are subdivisions of a VPC and can exist within a single AZ; spreading instances across subnets within the same AZ does not provide protection against an AZ-level failure. Placement groups control how instances are physically placed within an AZ for low latency or spread placement, but they do not provide cross-AZ fault isolation and are not a high-availability mechanism for surviving AZ outages."},{"id":"q802","question":"Which of the following are advantages of the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS manages the maintenance of the cloud infrastructure","B":"AWS manages the security of applications built on AWS","C":"AWS manages capacity planning for physical servers","D":"AWS manages the development of applications on AWS","E":"AWS manages cost planning for virtual servers"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS manages the maintenance of cloud infrastructure including hardware, networking, and facilities, and manages capacity planning for physical servers, freeing customers from these operational burdens and allowing them to focus on their applications and business objectives.\\nAWS does not manage the security of applications built on AWS, as application-level security including code vulnerabilities, access controls, and data protection remain the customer\'s responsibility under the Shared Responsibility Model. AWS does not manage the development of applications on AWS, as customers are solely responsible for designing, building, and maintaining their own applications regardless of which AWS services they run on. AWS does not manage cost planning for virtual servers, as customers are responsible for selecting the right instance types, monitoring usage, and optimizing their own spending through tools like Cost Explorer and AWS Budgets."},{"id":"q803","question":"A user deploys an Amazon RDS DB instance in multiple Availability Zones. This strategy involves which pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework?","options":{"A":"Performance efficiency","B":"Reliability","C":"Cost optimization","D":"Security"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Deploying across multiple Availability Zones ensures the database can withstand an AZ failure and recover automatically, which directly maps to the Reliability pillar\'s focus on workload recovery and availability.\\nPerformance Efficiency focuses on using compute resources efficiently to meet performance requirements, not on redundancy across failure domains. Cost Optimization focuses on eliminating unnecessary spend and choosing the right resource types, not on fault tolerance. Security focuses on protecting data and systems through access controls and detective mechanisms, not on availability architecture."},{"id":"q816","question":"A company that does business online needs to quickly deliver new functionality in an iterative manner, minimizing the time to market. Which AWS Cloud feature can provide this?","options":{"A":"Elasticity","B":"High availability","C":"Agility","D":"Reliability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Agility in the AWS Cloud enables companies to quickly provision resources, iterate on features, and deploy changes rapidly, minimizing time to market for new functionality.\\nElasticity refers to dynamically scaling resources up or down in response to demand, which addresses capacity management rather than the speed of delivering new features. High availability refers to keeping an application operational during failures through redundancy, which is an uptime characteristic unrelated to development and deployment speed. Reliability refers to a workload\'s ability to perform its intended function correctly and recover from failures, which is an architectural quality rather than a feature that accelerates time to market."},{"id":"q827","question":"According to the AWS Well-Architected Framework, what change management steps should be taken to achieve reliability in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Use AWS Config to generate an inventory of AWS resources","B":"Use service limits to prevent users from creating or making changes to AWS resources","C":"Use AWS CloudTrail to record AWS API calls into an auditable log file","D":"Use AWS Certificate Manager to whitelist approved AWS resources and services","E":"Use Amazon GuardDuty to validate configuration changes made to AWS resources"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Using AWS Config to generate an inventory of AWS resources provides visibility into what resources exist and how they are configured, supporting reliability by enabling change tracking, and using AWS CloudTrail to record AWS API calls creates an auditable log of all changes made, enabling root cause analysis when failures occur. Both are change management steps that support reliability in the AWS Cloud.\\nUsing service limits to prevent users from creating or making changes to AWS resources would block necessary scaling and operational changes, contradicting the reliability goal of being able to adapt to changing demand and recover from failures quickly. Using AWS Certificate Manager to whitelist approved resources and services is not an accurate description of what Certificate Manager does; it provisions SSL/TLS certificates and does not provide a resource whitelisting capability for change management. Using Amazon GuardDuty to validate configuration changes is not what GuardDuty does; it detects threats and malicious activity rather than validating whether resource configurations comply with desired states, which is the function of AWS Config."},{"id":"q829","question":"Which design principle should be considered when architecting in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Think of servers as non-disposable resources","B":"Use synchronous integration of services","C":"Design loosely coupled components","D":"Implement the least permissive rules for security groups"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Designing loosely coupled components means systems are built so that individual components can fail or be replaced without causing cascading failures, which is a core AWS Well-Architected design principle for building resilient, maintainable cloud architectures.\\nThinking of servers as non-disposable resources is the opposite of an AWS design principle; the cloud encourages treating infrastructure as disposable and automating replacements, allowing failed instances to be terminated and new ones launched automatically rather than being manually repaired. Using synchronous integration of services increases tight coupling and means a failure in one service blocks others waiting for a response; AWS design principles favor asynchronous and event-driven patterns where components can operate independently without waiting for each other. Implementing the least permissive rules for security groups is a security best practice for network access control but is not a cloud architecture design principle; it relates to security hardening rather than the broader system architecture pattern described by the Well-Architected Framework."},{"id":"q831","question":"A batch workload takes 5 hours to finish on an Amazon EC2 instance. The amount of data to be processed doubles monthly and the processing time is proportional. What is the best cloud architecture to address this consistently growing demand?","options":{"A":"Run the application on a bigger EC2 instance size","B":"Switch to an EC2 instance family that better matches batch requirements","C":"Distribute the application across multiple EC2 instances and run the workload in parallel","D":"Run the application on a bare metal EC2 instance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Distributing the workload across multiple EC2 instances and running in parallel applies horizontal scaling, which handles consistently growing demand by adding more machines rather than relying on a single larger instance that will eventually hit capacity limits.\\nRunning the application on a bigger EC2 instance applies vertical scaling, which has an upper size limit and creates a single point of failure, making it unsuitable for consistently doubling workloads over time. Switching to a different EC2 instance family optimizes for workload characteristics such as compute or memory intensity, but does not address the problem of consistently growing processing volume. Running on a bare metal EC2 instance provides direct access to physical hardware without a hypervisor layer, which can improve performance for certain workloads but does not scale to handle doubling data volumes."},{"id":"q833","question":"The ability to horizontally scale Amazon EC2 instances based on demand is an example of which concept in the AWS Cloud value proposition?","options":{"A":"Economy of scale","B":"Elasticity","C":"High availability","D":"Agility"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Horizontally scaling EC2 instances based on demand is elasticity, the ability to automatically add or remove compute resources to match workload requirements in real time.\\nEconomy of scale refers to the cost reductions AWS passes to customers through aggregated purchasing power across millions of users, which is a pricing benefit rather than a capacity management concept. High availability refers to keeping an application operational during failures through redundancy across multiple locations, which is unrelated to dynamically adjusting the number of instances. Agility refers to the speed at which customers can provision resources and iterate on new functionality, which addresses time to market rather than dynamic capacity adjustment."},{"id":"q840","question":"IT systems should be designed to reduce interdependencies, so that a change or failure in one component does not cascade to other components. This is an example of which principle of cloud architecture design?","options":{"A":"Scalability","B":"Loose coupling","C":"Automation","D":"Automatic scaling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loose coupling reduces interdependencies between components so that a change or failure in one does not cascade to others, improving system resilience through independent, isolated components.\\nScalability refers to the ability to handle increases in load by adding resources, not to reducing component interdependencies. Automation refers to reducing manual intervention through scripted or managed processes, not to architectural independence between components. Automatic scaling refers to dynamically adjusting resource capacity in response to demand, not to isolating components from each other."},{"id":"q844","question":"What is the benefit of elasticity in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Ensure web traffic is automatically spread across multiple AWS Regions","B":"Minimize storage costs by automatically archiving log data","C":"Enable AWS to automatically select the most cost-effective services","D":"Automatically adjust the required compute capacity to maintain consistent performance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Elasticity automatically adjusts compute capacity in response to changing demand, scaling out during traffic peaks and scaling in during quiet periods to maintain consistent performance without manual intervention.\\nSpreading web traffic across multiple Regions describes global load balancing or DNS routing, which is a separate capability unrelated to dynamically adjusting compute capacity. Automatically archiving log data to minimize storage costs describes a data lifecycle management function, not a characteristic of elasticity. Automatically selecting the most cost-effective services is not a feature AWS provides on behalf of customers, as service selection remains the customer\'s responsibility."},{"id":"q845","question":"The continual reduction of AWS Cloud pricing is due to:","options":{"A":"Pay-as-you go pricing","B":"The AWS global infrastructure","C":"Economies of scale","D":"Reserved storage pricing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As AWS grows and serves more customers, it achieves lower per-unit costs through economies of scale and passes those savings on to customers through regular price reductions.\\nPay-as-you-go pricing is a billing model that charges for actual usage rather than upfront commitments, but it does not cause prices to decrease over time. The AWS global infrastructure describes the physical footprint of Regions, AZs, and edge locations, which supports service delivery but is not the mechanism behind continual price reductions. Reserved storage pricing is a commitment-based discount option for specific services, not a driver of overall AWS pricing reductions across the platform."},{"id":"q853","question":"Which AWS Cloud best practice uses the elasticity and agility of cloud computing?","options":{"A":"Provision capacity based on past usage and theoretical peaks","B":"Dynamically and predictively scale to meet usage demands","C":"Build the application and infrastructure in a data center that grants physical access","D":"Break apart the application into loosely coupled components"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dynamically and predictively scaling to meet usage demands leverages both elasticity through automatic scaling and agility through rapid adaptation, which are core cloud computing advantages.\\nProvisioning capacity based on past usage and theoretical peaks is an on-premises approach that leads to over-provisioning and idle resources, which cloud adoption is specifically designed to eliminate. Building applications in a data center that grants physical access describes an on-premises or colocation model and contradicts the cloud principle of abstracting away physical infrastructure management. Breaking apart an application into loosely coupled components is a good architectural practice that improves resilience and maintainability, but it describes the loose coupling design principle rather than the use of elasticity and agility."},{"id":"q854","question":"Which method helps to optimize costs of users moving to the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Paying only for what is used","B":"Purchasing hardware before it is needed","C":"Manually provisioning cloud resources","D":"Purchasing for the maximum possible load"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pay-as-you-go model optimizes costs by ensuring you only pay for the resources you actually consume, eliminating waste from over-provisioning or purchasing hardware in advance of actual need.\\nPurchasing hardware before it is needed describes the traditional capital expenditure model of procuring infrastructure ahead of demand, which leads to over-provisioning and wasted spend rather than optimizing costs. Manually provisioning cloud resources introduces human effort and potential delays in responding to demand changes, and does not optimize costs compared to automated scaling that matches capacity precisely to actual usage. Purchasing for the maximum possible load means over-provisioning infrastructure to handle the worst-case demand scenario, which maximizes costs by paying for capacity that sits idle during normal usage periods rather than scaling with actual demand."},{"id":"q861","question":"What is one of the core principles to follow when designing a highly available application in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Design using a serverless architecture","B":"Assume that all components within an application can fail","C":"Design AWS Auto Scaling into every application","D":"Design all components using open-source code"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Assuming that all components can fail is a core principle of high availability design, as it drives architects to build redundancy, health checks, and automatic failover into every layer of an application rather than relying on any single component remaining available.\\nDesigning using a serverless architecture is a valid approach for some workloads but is not a universal principle for high availability, as server-based architectures can also achieve high availability through redundancy and failover. Designing Auto Scaling into every application is a good practice for scalability but is not a universal requirement for high availability, as some applications achieve availability through redundancy rather than scaling. Using open-source code is a software development choice that has no bearing on whether an application is designed to remain available when components fail."},{"id":"q867","question":"A Cloud Practitioner is developing a disaster recovery plan and intends to replicate data between multiple geographic areas. Which of the following meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"AWS Accounts","B":"AWS Regions","C":"Availability Zones","D":"Edge locations"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Regions are geographically separate areas around the world, making them the correct choice for replicating data across different physical locations to satisfy disaster recovery requirements.\\nAWS Accounts are a logical boundary for managing resources and billing, not a geographic infrastructure component. Availability Zones are isolated data center clusters within a single Region, so replicating between AZs does not achieve geographic separation across different areas of the world. Edge locations are points of presence used for content delivery and caching, not designed for data replication or disaster recovery purposes."},{"id":"q875","question":"A company wants to use Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) to deploy a global commercial application. The deployment solution should be built with the highest redundancy and fault tolerance. Based on this situation, the Amazon EC2 instances should be deployed:","options":{"A":"In a single Availability Zone in one AWS Region","B":"With multiple Elastic Network Interfaces belonging to different subnets","C":"Across multiple Availability Zones in one AWS Region","D":"Across multiple Availability Zones in two AWS Regions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Deploying across multiple Availability Zones in two AWS Regions provides protection against both AZ-level and Region-level failures, achieving the highest level of redundancy and fault tolerance available.\\nDeploying in a single Availability Zone in one Region creates a single point of failure at both the AZ and Region level, providing no redundancy against outages. Using multiple Elastic Network Interfaces in different subnets improves network configuration flexibility within an instance but does not provide redundancy against compute or infrastructure failures. Deploying across multiple AZs in a single Region protects against AZ-level failures but leaves the application vulnerable to a full Regional outage, which does not meet the requirement for the highest redundancy."},{"id":"q886","question":"What is an Availability Zone in AWS?","options":{"A":"One or more physical data centers","B":"A completely isolated geographic location","C":"One or more edge locations based around the world","D":"A data center location with a single source of power and networking"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An Availability Zone consists of one or more physical data centers, each with independent power, cooling, and networking, designed so that a failure in one AZ does not affect others in the same Region.\\nA completely isolated geographic location describes an AWS Region, not an Availability Zone; Regions are the large geographic areas, and AZs are the isolated facilities within a Region. Edge locations are Points of Presence used by services such as CloudFront to cache content close to users; they are not Availability Zones and have no hosting relationship with EC2 instances or core AWS services. A description of a single source of power and networking is the opposite of what an AZ provides; AZs are specifically designed with redundant power and networking to protect against single points of failure."},{"id":"q897","question":"A company has distributed its workload on both the AWS Cloud and some on-premises servers. What type of architecture is this?","options":{"A":"Virtual private network","B":"Virtual private cloud","C":"Hybrid cloud","D":"Private cloud"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A hybrid cloud architecture distributes workloads between on-premises servers and the AWS Cloud, combining existing data center infrastructure with cloud resources connected via VPN or Direct Connect.\\nA virtual private network is a connectivity technology that creates encrypted tunnels between networks, and is not itself an architecture type describing how workloads are distributed between cloud and on-premises environments. A virtual private cloud is a logically isolated network environment within AWS, and describes a networking construct rather than a deployment model that spans both cloud and on-premises infrastructure. A private cloud is a deployment model where all infrastructure is dedicated to a single organization and hosted either on-premises or by a third party, with no shared public cloud component."},{"id":"q913","question":"Fault tolerance refers to:","options":{"A":"The ability of an application to accommodate growth without changing design","B":"How well and how quickly an application\'s environment can have lost data restored","C":"How secure your application is","D":"The built-in redundancy of an application\'s components"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fault tolerance refers to the built-in redundancy of an application\'s components, ensuring the system continues operating correctly even when individual components fail.\\nThe ability to accommodate growth without changing design describes scalability, which is a separate cloud concept focused on handling increased demand. How well and how quickly lost data can be restored describes recoverability, which relates to backup and disaster recovery rather than continued operation during failures. How secure an application is describes the security posture of a system, which is an entirely separate concern from redundancy and fault tolerance."},{"id":"q927","question":"Which AWS cloud architecture principle states that systems should reduce interdependencies?","options":{"A":"Scalability","B":"Services, not servers","C":"Removing single points of failure","D":"Loose coupling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Loose coupling is the principle that systems should reduce interdependencies between components, so that changes or failures in one do not cascade to others.\\nScalability refers to the ability to handle increases in load by adding resources, not to reducing interdependencies. Services, not servers is the principle of using managed AWS services instead of running your own infrastructure. Removing single points of failure is a reliability principle focused on redundancy, not on reducing component interdependencies."},{"id":"q935","question":"What AWS benefit refers to a customer\'s ability to deploy applications that scale up and down to meet variable demand?","options":{"A":"Elasticity","B":"Agility","C":"Security","D":"Scalability"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Elasticity refers to the ability to automatically scale resources both up and down to match variable demand, ensuring capacity adjusts in both directions without manual intervention.\\nAgility refers to the speed at which customers can provision resources and iterate on new functionality, which addresses time to market rather than dynamic capacity adjustment. Security refers to protecting data, systems, and assets through controls and risk mitigation strategies, and is unrelated to scaling resources in response to demand. Scalability refers to a system\'s ability to handle increased load by growing capacity, but unlike elasticity it does not inherently include automatic scaling back down when demand decreases."},{"id":"q937","question":"A company wants to set up a highly available workload in AWS with a disaster recovery plan that will allow the company to recover in case of a regional service interruption. Which configuration will meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Run on two Availability Zones in one AWS Region, using the additional Availability Zones in the AWS Region for the disaster recovery site","B":"Run on two Availability Zones in one AWS Region, using another AWS Region for the disaster recovery site","C":"Run on two Availability Zones in one AWS Region, using a local AWS Region for the disaster recovery site","D":"Run across two AWS Regions, using a third AWS Region for the disaster recovery site"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Running across two AZs in one Region provides high availability within the Region, and using a separate Region as the disaster recovery site protects against a full regional service interruption through geographic redundancy.\\nUsing additional AZs within the same Region as the disaster recovery site does not protect against a regional interruption, as all AZs in a Region would be affected by the same regional outage. A local AWS Region refers to a limited-scope Region designed for data residency requirements and typically does not offer the full service availability needed to serve as a complete disaster recovery site. Running across two Regions with a third as the disaster recovery site introduces unnecessary complexity and cost, as two Regions already provide the geographic separation needed for high availability and regional failover."},{"id":"q947","question":"Performing operations as code is a design principle that supports which pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework?","options":{"A":"Performance efficiency","B":"Operational excellence","C":"Reliability","D":"Security"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Performing operations as code is an explicit design principle of the Operational Excellence pillar, which focuses on running and improving operations by automating processes and responding to events programmatically.\\nPerformance Efficiency focuses on using compute resources efficiently to meet system requirements, not on automating operational processes. Reliability focuses on ensuring a workload performs its intended function correctly and consistently, with design principles around recovery and redundancy. Security focuses on protecting data, systems, and assets through controls and detective mechanisms."},{"id":"q948","question":"Which design principle is achieved by following the reliability pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework?","options":{"A":"Vertical scaling","B":"Manual failure recovery","C":"Testing recovery procedures","D":"Changing infrastructure manually"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Testing recovery procedures is a core design principle of the Reliability pillar, ensuring workloads can recover from failures by regularly validating disaster recovery and backup processes.\\nVertical scaling is a performance and capacity concept associated with the Performance Efficiency pillar, not a design principle of the Reliability pillar. Manual failure recovery contradicts the Reliability pillar\'s principle of automating failure recovery, as manual processes introduce delays and human error during outages. Changing infrastructure manually contradicts the Reliability pillar\'s principle of managing change through automation to reduce the risk of introducing failures."},{"id":"q955","question":"A user has underutilized on-premises resources. Which AWS Cloud concept can BEST address this issue?","options":{"A":"High availability","B":"Elasticity","C":"Security","D":"Loose coupling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Elasticity allows you to scale resources up or down based on actual demand, directly addressing underutilization by ensuring you only pay for what you use instead of maintaining idle capacity.\\nHigh availability ensures an application remains operational during failures through redundancy, but does not address the problem of over-provisioned or underutilized resources. Security refers to protecting data, systems, and assets through controls and risk mitigation, which is unrelated to resource utilization. Loose coupling is an architectural principle that reduces dependencies between components to limit the blast radius of failures, and does not address idle or underutilized capacity."},{"id":"q981","question":"What are the advantages of deploying an application with Amazon EC2 instances in multiple Availability Zones?","options":{"A":"Preventing a single point of failure","B":"Reducing the operational costs of the application","C":"Allowing the application to serve cross-region users with low latency","D":"Increasing the availability of the application","E":"Increasing the load of the application"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Deploying across multiple Availability Zones eliminates single points of failure by ensuring the application continues running if one AZ goes down, directly increasing overall availability.\\nReducing operational costs is not a direct benefit of multi-AZ deployment and can in fact increase costs slightly due to running resources across more locations. Serving cross-region users with low latency requires deploying across multiple Regions or using a content delivery network, not spreading instances across AZs within a single Region. Increasing the load of the application describes adding more demand, which is not a benefit of any architectural pattern."},{"id":"q995","question":"Which AWS Cloud design principles can help increase reliability?","options":{"A":"Using monolithic architecture","B":"Measuring overall efficiency","C":"Testing recovery procedures","D":"Adopting a consumption model","E":"Automatically recovering from failure"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Testing recovery procedures validates that disaster recovery plans work correctly, and automatically recovering from failure using health checks and auto-scaling ensures systems self-heal without manual intervention, both being core Reliability pillar design principles.\\nMonolithic architecture tightly couples all application components together, meaning a failure in one part can bring down the entire system, which reduces rather than increases reliability. Measuring overall efficiency is a design principle associated with the Performance Efficiency pillar, not the Reliability pillar. Adopting a consumption model is a cost management principle associated with the Cost Optimization pillar, focused on paying only for what is used rather than on system resilience."},{"id":"q1007","question":"Which cloud computing benefit does AWS demonstrate with its ability to offer lower variable costs as a result of high purchase volumes?","options":{"A":"Pay-as-you-go pricing","B":"High availability","C":"Global reach","D":"Economies of scale"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Economies of scale allow AWS to lower variable costs for customers by aggregating demand across millions of users, giving AWS far greater purchasing power than any individual organization could achieve on its own.\\nPay-as-you-go pricing is a billing model that charges customers only for what they consume, which is a separate cloud benefit unrelated to purchase volume driving down costs. High availability refers to designing systems to remain operational during failures, which is an architectural benefit rather than a cost benefit derived from purchasing power. Global reach refers to the ability to deploy infrastructure in multiple geographic locations worldwide, which is an infrastructure benefit rather than an economic one."},{"id":"q1034","question":"Which pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework focuses on the ability of a workload to perform its intended function correctly and consistently?","options":{"A":"Security","B":"Reliability","C":"Performance Efficiency","D":"Operational Excellence"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Reliability pillar focuses on the ability of a workload to perform its intended function correctly and consistently, including the ability to recover from failures and meet availability requirements over time.\\nSecurity focuses on protecting data, systems, and assets through access controls, encryption, and detective mechanisms. Performance Efficiency focuses on using computing resources efficiently to meet workload requirements as demand changes. Operational Excellence focuses on running and monitoring systems to deliver business value and continuously improving processes and procedures."},{"id":"q1035","question":"Which pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework includes the ability to run workloads effectively, gain insight into their operations, and continuously improve supporting processes?","options":{"A":"Operational Excellence","B":"Reliability","C":"Performance Efficiency","D":"Cost Optimization"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Operational Excellence pillar covers the ability to run workloads effectively, gain insight into operations through monitoring and observation, and continuously improve supporting processes and procedures.\\nReliability focuses on ensuring a workload performs its intended function correctly and recovers automatically from failures, not on operational processes and continuous improvement. Performance Efficiency focuses on using computing resources efficiently to meet workload requirements as demand changes over time. Cost Optimization focuses on eliminating unnecessary expenditure and selecting the right resource types to reduce overall spending."},{"id":"q1036","question":"Which pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework focuses on selecting the right resource types and sizes based on workload requirements?","options":{"A":"Reliability","B":"Cost Optimization","C":"Performance Efficiency","D":"Sustainability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Performance Efficiency pillar focuses on using computing resources efficiently to meet workload requirements, including selecting the right resource types and sizes and maintaining efficiency as demand changes over time.\\nReliability focuses on ensuring a workload performs its intended function correctly and recovers from failures, not on resource selection. Cost Optimization focuses on eliminating unnecessary spend and reducing overall costs, which overlaps with right-sizing but is not the pillar that specifically defines it as a design principle. Sustainability focuses on reducing the environmental impact of cloud workloads by minimizing energy consumption and maximizing resource utilization."},{"id":"q1037","question":"Which pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework focuses on minimizing the environmental impacts of running cloud workloads?","options":{"A":"Cost Optimization","B":"Operational Excellence","C":"Performance Efficiency","D":"Sustainability"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Sustainability pillar focuses on minimizing the environmental impacts of running cloud workloads, including understanding usage impacts, maximizing utilization, and adopting managed services to reduce the resources required.\\nCost Optimization focuses on eliminating unnecessary spending and achieving financial efficiency, not on reducing environmental impact. Operational Excellence focuses on running workloads effectively, monitoring operations, and continuously improving processes and procedures. Performance Efficiency focuses on using computing resources efficiently to meet workload requirements as demand changes, which addresses technical resource use rather than environmental outcomes."},{"id":"q1038","question":"Which pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework focuses on protecting data, systems, and assets through risk assessments and mitigation strategies?","options":{"A":"Operational Excellence","B":"Reliability","C":"Security","D":"Performance Efficiency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Security pillar focuses on protecting data, systems, and assets through risk assessments, mitigation strategies, and controls covering confidentiality, integrity, and identity management.\\nOperational Excellence focuses on running workloads effectively, monitoring operations, and continuously improving processes and procedures. Reliability focuses on ensuring a workload performs its intended function correctly and recovers automatically from failures. Performance Efficiency focuses on using computing resources efficiently to meet workload requirements as demand changes over time."},{"id":"q1039","question":"Which pillar of the AWS Well-Architected Framework focuses on avoiding unnecessary costs and using the most cost-effective resources?","options":{"A":"Cost Optimization","B":"Sustainability","C":"Operational Excellence","D":"Performance Efficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Cost Optimization pillar focuses on avoiding unnecessary costs, selecting the most appropriate resource types, and scaling without overspending.\\nSustainability focuses on reducing environmental impact of cloud workloads, such as minimizing energy consumption. Operational Excellence focuses on running and monitoring systems to deliver business value and improving processes. Performance Efficiency focuses on using compute resources efficiently to meet performance requirements."},{"id":"q1060","question":"Which TWO of the following are perspectives of the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)?","options":{"A":"Governance","B":"Financial","C":"People","D":"Infrastructure","E":"Agility"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"The AWS CAF organizes guidance into six perspectives: Business, People, Governance, Platform, Security, and Operations. Governance focuses on orchestrating cloud initiatives while maximizing organizational benefits and minimizing transformation-related risks, and People focuses on developing an organization-wide change management strategy and building cloud skills across the workforce.\\nFinancial is not one of the six AWS CAF perspectives; financial and cost-related guidance falls within the Business perspective, which addresses how cloud adoption drives business value and return on investment rather than existing as its own separate financial perspective. Infrastructure is not one of the six AWS CAF perspectives; guidance on building and provisioning the technology foundation for cloud workloads falls under the Platform perspective rather than a perspective named Infrastructure. Agility is a benefit and characteristic of cloud computing but is not one of the six CAF perspectives; it does not appear in the CAF framework structure alongside Business, People, Governance, Platform, Security, and Operations."},{"id":"q1061","question":"According to the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF), which TWO of the following are benefits that organizations can achieve through successful cloud adoption?","options":{"A":"Reduced business risk","B":"Elimination of all operational expenditure","C":"Improved environmental, social, and governance (ESG) performance","D":"Immediate compliance with all regulatory requirements","E":"Guaranteed elimination of all security vulnerabilities"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"The AWS CAF identifies four key benefits of cloud adoption: reduced business risk through improved reliability, increased performance, and enhanced security posture; improved ESG performance through reduced carbon footprint and greater energy efficiency enabled by cloud infrastructure; increased revenue through the ability to develop and deliver new products faster; and increased operational efficiency through optimized cost structures and workforce productivity.\\nElimination of all operational expenditure is not an AWS CAF benefit; moving to the cloud converts large upfront capital expenditure to variable operational expenditure rather than eliminating ongoing costs, as customers continue to pay for cloud services based on actual consumption and operational costs such as staffing and tooling remain. Immediate compliance with all regulatory requirements is not a benefit the CAF promises; while AWS provides compliance certifications and tools to support customers\' compliance programs, each customer is still responsible for applying the appropriate controls to their own workloads and meeting their specific regulatory obligations. Guaranteed elimination of all security vulnerabilities is not a CAF-defined benefit; while cloud adoption can improve the security posture of an organization through shared responsibility and access to advanced security services, the CAF does not promise that cloud migration eliminates all security risk."},{"id":"q1062","question":"A company needs a disaster recovery strategy where a scaled-down but fully functional version of its environment runs continuously in a secondary AWS Region, enabling rapid failover by scaling up to full capacity when a disaster occurs. Which disaster recovery strategy does this describe?","options":{"A":"Backup and restore","B":"Pilot light","C":"Warm standby","D":"Multi-site active-active"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Warm standby is a disaster recovery strategy where a scaled-down but fully operational version of the application environment runs continuously in a secondary Region, allowing the company to fail over quickly by scaling the standby environment up to full production capacity when a disaster occurs, providing a balance between recovery speed and ongoing cost.\\nBackup and restore involves storing regular data backups and restoring them to a newly provisioned environment after a disaster, resulting in the longest recovery time of all DR strategies as infrastructure must be built from scratch during recovery rather than already being available. Pilot light keeps only the minimal core components of the environment running in the secondary Region, such as a replicated database, with other infrastructure shut down and requiring provisioning during failover, resulting in faster recovery than backup and restore but slower than warm standby since more infrastructure must be brought online. Multi-site active-active runs full production copies of the environment across multiple sites simultaneously, providing the fastest possible failover with essentially zero downtime, but at the highest ongoing cost since all sites must maintain full production capacity at all times."},{"id":"q1063","question":"Which disaster recovery strategy has the longest recovery time and the lowest cost because no standby infrastructure is kept running in the secondary location?","options":{"A":"Multi-site active-active","B":"Warm standby","C":"Pilot light","D":"Backup and restore"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Backup and restore has the longest recovery time objective of all disaster recovery strategies because after a failure the entire infrastructure must be provisioned from scratch and data must be restored from backups before the application can resume serving traffic, but it has the lowest ongoing cost because no secondary infrastructure is running during normal operations.\\nMulti-site active-active runs full production capacity at multiple sites simultaneously, providing the fastest failover with essentially zero downtime, but requires the highest ongoing investment as full infrastructure costs are incurred at every site continuously. Warm standby maintains a scaled-down but functional version of the environment running continuously, resulting in faster recovery than backup and restore but at a higher ongoing cost since resources are always running in the secondary location. Pilot light keeps only critical core components such as a replicated database running continuously with other infrastructure shut down, resulting in faster recovery than backup and restore but slower than warm standby, at an ongoing cost between the two."},{"id":"q1064","question":"A company wants to move an existing on-premises application to AWS as quickly as possible without making any changes to the application code or architecture. Which migration strategy should the company use?","options":{"A":"Replatform","B":"Refactor","C":"Rehost","D":"Repurchase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rehosting, commonly referred to as lift-and-shift, involves moving an application to the cloud without making any changes to the application code or architecture, making it the fastest migration approach that allows organizations to realize cloud benefits quickly with minimal upfront migration effort.\\nReplatforming involves making targeted optimizations during migration, such as moving from a self-managed database to a managed service, to gain cloud benefits while leaving the core application architecture unchanged; this requires some changes and therefore more effort than a pure rehost. Refactoring involves re-architecting the application to fully leverage cloud-native capabilities such as microservices and serverless computing, providing the greatest long-term benefits but requiring the most time and development investment of any migration strategy. Repurchasing involves replacing the existing application entirely with a different product, typically a SaaS solution, which requires learning a new platform and migrating data rather than simply moving the existing application to new infrastructure."},{"id":"q1065","question":"A company is migrating an application to AWS and decides to move its self-managed MySQL database running on a virtual machine to Amazon RDS, without changing the core application logic or rewriting any code. Which migration strategy does this represent?","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Repurchase","C":"Replatform","D":"Refactor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Replatforming, also known as lift-tinker-and-shift, involves making targeted optimizations during migration to take advantage of cloud-managed capabilities, such as moving from a self-managed database to Amazon RDS to gain automated backups, patching, and scaling, without re-architecting the core application or changing its code.\\nRehosting moves an application to the cloud with no changes to architecture or code, making it a pure lift-and-shift; switching from a self-managed database to Amazon RDS represents a change to managed infrastructure and therefore goes beyond a simple rehost. Repurchasing replaces an existing application with a fundamentally different product, such as moving to a third-party SaaS database; moving to Amazon RDS uses the same MySQL engine in managed form rather than replacing the application with a different product from a different vendor. Refactoring involves re-architecting the application to use cloud-native services and patterns such as serverless functions or microservices, which represents a far more significant change to the application design than simply migrating the database to a managed service."},{"id":"q1066","question":"During migration planning, a company discovers that 30% of its on-premises applications have not been used in over two years and provide no current business value. Which migration strategy should be applied to these applications?","options":{"A":"Retain","B":"Rehost","C":"Retire","D":"Replatform"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Retiring involves decommissioning applications that are no longer needed or that provide no business value, which reduces the scope and cost of migration by eliminating unnecessary workloads rather than spending effort and money moving them to the cloud where they would continue to consume resources.\\nRetaining involves keeping certain applications running in their current on-premises environment, typically because they are not yet ready to migrate due to technical or compliance constraints, but this strategy still incurs ongoing maintenance costs rather than eliminating the burden of applications with no business purpose. Rehosting moves applications to the cloud with no changes, which would still consume cloud resources and generate ongoing costs for applications that have no business value; decommissioning is the correct choice when there is no reason to preserve the application at all. Replatforming involves optimizing applications during migration by moving to managed services, which requires migration effort and ongoing cloud resource costs that would be wasted entirely on applications that serve no business purpose."},{"id":"q1092","question":"Which AWS framework provides guidance organized into six perspectives to help organizations identify and prioritize cloud transformation opportunities?","options":{"A":"AWS Well-Architected Framework","B":"AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)","C":"AWS Migration Acceleration Program","D":"AWS Shared Responsibility Model"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The AWS Cloud Adoption Framework organizes guidance into six perspectives, Business, People, Governance, Platform, Security, and Operations, to help organizations identify skill gaps, process improvements, and technical requirements for successful cloud transformation.\\nThe AWS Well-Architected Framework provides best practices for designing and operating workloads in the cloud across six pillars, but it is focused on workload architecture rather than organizational cloud adoption planning. The AWS Migration Acceleration Program is a consulting and technology engagement program that helps customers accelerate their migration to AWS, but it is not a framework of perspectives for cloud transformation planning. The AWS Shared Responsibility Model defines the division of security responsibilities between AWS and the customer, and is a security governance concept rather than a cloud adoption planning framework."},{"id":"q1093","question":"A company is planning a cloud migration and needs to assess the readiness of its workforce and identify skill gaps. Which AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) perspective addresses this need?","options":{"A":"Business","B":"People","C":"Operations","D":"Platform"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The People perspective of the AWS CAF focuses on evaluating organizational structures, roles, and skills to ensure the workforce is ready for cloud adoption, including training, change management, and career development.\\nThe Business perspective focuses on ensuring that cloud investments are aligned with business outcomes and that stakeholders can articulate the business case for cloud adoption, rather than workforce readiness. The Operations perspective focuses on defining how day-to-day cloud operations will be managed, monitored, and improved, rather than workforce skills assessment. The Platform perspective focuses on building a scalable cloud platform architecture including networking, compute, and storage design, rather than people and organizational readiness."},{"id":"q1094","question":"Which perspectives of the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) are categorized as technical capabilities? (Select TWO)","options":{"A":"Business","B":"Platform","C":"People","D":"Operations","E":"Governance"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"The AWS CAF groups its six perspectives into two categories: business capabilities (Business, People, Governance) and technical capabilities (Platform, Security, Operations). Platform and Operations both fall under technical capabilities.\\nBusiness is a business capability perspective focused on aligning cloud investments with business outcomes, not a technical capability. People is a business capability perspective focused on organizational readiness, culture, and workforce skills. Governance is a business capability perspective focused on orchestrating cloud initiatives, maximizing benefits, and minimizing transformation risks."},{"id":"q1095","question":"A company wants to ensure its cloud adoption minimizes transformation-related risks and aligns with regulatory requirements. Which AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) perspective is MOST relevant?","options":{"A":"Security","B":"Business","C":"Governance","D":"Operations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Governance perspective of the AWS CAF focuses on orchestrating cloud initiatives while maximizing organizational benefits and minimizing transformation-related risks, including aligning cloud strategy with regulatory and compliance requirements.\\nThe Security perspective focuses on achieving the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data and cloud workloads through security controls, rather than broader transformation risk management and regulatory alignment. The Business perspective focuses on ensuring cloud investments deliver measurable business outcomes, rather than risk management and regulatory compliance. The Operations perspective focuses on ensuring cloud services are delivered at a level that meets the needs of the business on a day-to-day basis, rather than strategic risk and compliance alignment."},{"id":"q1096","question":"Which AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) perspective helps ensure that cloud workloads achieve the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data?","options":{"A":"Governance","B":"Operations","C":"Platform","D":"Security"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Security perspective of the AWS CAF focuses on achieving the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data and cloud workloads, including identity management, threat detection, infrastructure protection, and incident response.\\nThe Governance perspective focuses on orchestrating cloud initiatives, aligning cloud strategy with business goals, and managing transformation risks, rather than the technical security posture of workloads. The Operations perspective focuses on delivering cloud services to meet business needs through monitoring, incident management, and continuous improvement of operational processes. The Platform perspective focuses on designing and building a scalable, enterprise-grade cloud environment including infrastructure, networking, and provisioning, rather than data protection and security controls."},{"id":"q1117","question":"A company wants to use the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) to build a business case for cloud migration. Which CAF perspective should they focus on?","options":{"A":"People","B":"Business","C":"Platform","D":"Governance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Business perspective of the AWS CAF helps stakeholders understand how cloud adoption can accelerate business outcomes, articulate a compelling business case, and ensure that cloud investments are tied to measurable business value.\\nThe People perspective focuses on organizational readiness, skills development, and change management rather than building a financial or strategic business case for migration. The Platform perspective focuses on designing the cloud infrastructure architecture including compute, networking, and storage, which is a technical concern rather than a business justification activity. The Governance perspective focuses on managing risks and aligning cloud initiatives with compliance requirements, which supports the business case but is not the perspective directly responsible for articulating it."},{"id":"q1118","question":"Which AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) perspective focuses on designing and implementing new cloud environments with enterprise-grade scalability?","options":{"A":"Operations","B":"Security","C":"Platform","D":"People"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Platform perspective focuses on building an enterprise-grade, scalable cloud platform by designing the target architecture for networking, compute, storage, and database services, and establishing patterns for provisioning and managing cloud resources.\\nThe Operations perspective focuses on delivering and managing cloud services to meet day-to-day business needs through monitoring, incident management, and process improvement, not on designing the underlying platform architecture. The Security perspective focuses on protecting data and workloads through identity management, threat detection, and security controls, rather than designing the cloud infrastructure platform. The People perspective focuses on workforce readiness, organizational change, and skills development, which is unrelated to technical platform architecture."},{"id":"q1119","question":"A company needs to define how it will manage and monitor cloud workloads on a day-to-day basis after migration. Which AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) perspective addresses this?","options":{"A":"Platform","B":"Governance","C":"Operations","D":"Business"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Operations perspective of the AWS CAF focuses on ensuring that cloud services are delivered and managed to meet ongoing business needs, covering areas such as monitoring, incident management, event management, and continuous improvement of operational processes.\\nThe Platform perspective focuses on designing and building the cloud environment architecture, which is a one-time design activity rather than ongoing day-to-day management. The Governance perspective focuses on strategic alignment, risk management, and compliance, which are higher-level concerns than daily operational monitoring and incident management. The Business perspective focuses on aligning cloud investments with business outcomes and measuring return on investment, rather than operational management of workloads."},{"id":"q1120","question":"How many perspectives does the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) define?","options":{"A":"Four","B":"Five","C":"Six","D":"Seven"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS CAF defines six perspectives: Business, People, and Governance (business capabilities), and Platform, Security, and Operations (technical capabilities), providing comprehensive guidance for cloud transformation.\\nFour perspectives would be incomplete, as the CAF explicitly defines six distinct areas of focus to cover both business and technical dimensions of cloud adoption. Five perspectives would be missing one of the six defined areas, and does not match the actual CAF structure. Seven perspectives exceeds the actual number defined by the framework, which groups its guidance into exactly six perspectives."},{"id":"q1121","question":"Which AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) perspectives are categorized as business capabilities? (Select TWO)","options":{"A":"Business","B":"Platform","C":"Operations","D":"Security","E":"Governance"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"The AWS CAF categorizes Business, People, and Governance as business capability perspectives, which focus on strategic alignment, organizational readiness, workforce skills, risk governance, and ensuring cloud investments deliver business value; of the options listed, Business and Governance are the two business capability perspectives.\\nPlatform is a technical capability perspective that focuses on designing and building the cloud environment architecture, not on business alignment or organizational readiness. Operations is a technical capability perspective that focuses on running, monitoring, and optimizing cloud workloads day to day, not on business-level planning or governance. Security is a technical capability perspective that focuses on protecting data confidentiality, integrity, and availability through technical security controls, not on business value or governance."},{"id":"q1122","question":"A company is evaluating its cloud readiness and discovers that its IT staff lacks experience with AWS services. According to the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF), which action should the company take?","options":{"A":"Delay migration until all staff are fully trained","B":"Invest in training and upskilling programs aligned with the People perspective","C":"Outsource all cloud operations to a third party permanently","D":"Proceed with migration without any training to save time"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The People perspective of the AWS CAF recommends investing in training, upskilling, and organizational change management to close skill gaps and prepare the workforce for cloud adoption, ensuring sustainable internal capability rather than dependency on external resources.\\nDelaying migration indefinitely until all staff are fully trained is impractical and unnecessary, as the CAF recommends iterative learning alongside migration rather than waiting for complete readiness. Outsourcing all operations permanently does not build internal capability and creates long-term dependency, which contradicts the People perspective\'s goal of developing organizational cloud skills. Proceeding without training risks operational failures, security incidents, and inefficient resource usage, and ignores the People perspective\'s emphasis on workforce readiness."},{"id":"q1123","question":"Which of the following is a key focus area of the Governance perspective in the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)?","options":{"A":"Designing the target cloud architecture","B":"Managing benefits, risks, and compliance during cloud transformation","C":"Monitoring application performance after deployment","D":"Configuring identity and access management controls"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Governance perspective focuses on orchestrating cloud initiatives to maximize organizational benefits while minimizing transformation risks, including budget management, portfolio management, compliance alignment, and risk mitigation strategies.\\nDesigning the target cloud architecture is a focus of the Platform perspective, which handles infrastructure, networking, and compute design decisions. Monitoring application performance after deployment is a focus of the Operations perspective, which manages day-to-day service delivery, observability, and incident management. Configuring identity and access management controls is a focus of the Security perspective, which handles identity management, permissions, and threat detection."},{"id":"q1124","question":"The AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) identifies four transformation domains. Which of the following is one of those domains?","options":{"A":"Technology","B":"Networking","C":"Database","D":"Compute"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The AWS CAF identifies four transformation domains: Technology, Process, Organization, and Product. The Technology domain covers migration and modernisation of infrastructure, applications, and data platforms to leverage cloud capabilities.\\nNetworking is a component within the Platform perspective and Technology transformation domain, but it is not itself one of the four transformation domains defined by the CAF. Database is a specific service category within cloud technology, not one of the four transformation domains that the CAF uses to structure cloud adoption activities. Compute is a specific resource type within cloud infrastructure, not one of the four high-level transformation domains defined by the CAF."},{"id":"q1125","question":"Which transformation domains are defined by the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)? (Select TWO)","options":{"A":"Product","B":"Security","C":"Process","D":"Infrastructure","E":"Compliance"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"The AWS CAF defines four transformation domains: Technology, Process, Organization, and Product. Process focuses on digitising, automating, and optimizing business operations, while Product focuses on reimagining the business model using new capabilities enabled by cloud.\\nSecurity is one of the six CAF perspectives, not one of the four transformation domains, and it provides guidance on protecting data and workloads rather than defining a transformation pathway. Infrastructure is a component within the Technology transformation domain and the Platform perspective, but it is not itself one of the four transformation domains. Compliance is an area addressed by the Governance perspective, but it is not one of the four transformation domains defined by the CAF."},{"id":"q1126","question":"A company wants to use the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) to assess which of its applications are ready to move to AWS. Which CAF activity does this describe?","options":{"A":"Envision phase","B":"Align phase","C":"Launch phase","D":"Scale phase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Align phase of the AWS CAF focuses on identifying capability gaps, evaluating cross-organizational dependencies, and assessing application readiness to create a detailed action plan for cloud adoption, making it the phase where migration readiness is determined.\\nThe Envision phase focuses on identifying how cloud can accelerate business outcomes and creating a shared vision for transformation, which happens before readiness assessment. The Launch phase focuses on delivering pilot initiatives and demonstrating incremental business value through initial cloud deployments, which occurs after readiness has been assessed. The Scale phase focuses on expanding cloud adoption across the organization and optimizing operating models for long-term scale, which comes after initial launches have been proven successful."},{"id":"q1127","question":"What is the correct order of the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) phases for cloud transformation?","options":{"A":"Align, Envision, Launch, Scale","B":"Envision, Align, Launch, Scale","C":"Launch, Envision, Scale, Align","D":"Envision, Launch, Align, Scale"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The AWS CAF defines the cloud transformation journey in four phases: Envision (identify how cloud accelerates business outcomes), Align (identify capability gaps and create an action plan), Launch (deliver pilot initiatives), and Scale (expand adoption across the organization).\\nStarting with Align before Envision skips the critical first step of establishing a shared vision for why the organization is adopting cloud, which is needed to guide the alignment activities. Starting with Launch before Align means deploying to the cloud without first assessing readiness or creating an action plan, which risks uncoordinated and unsuccessful migrations. Placing Align after Launch means assessing readiness after already deploying workloads, which reverses the logical order of planning before execution."},{"id":"q1128","question":"A company is using the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) and wants to establish a Cloud Center of Excellence (CCoE). Which phase of the CAF transformation journey typically involves this activity?","options":{"A":"Envision","B":"Align","C":"Launch","D":"Scale"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Launch phase of the AWS CAF involves delivering pilot initiatives, building a Cloud Center of Excellence to provide guidance and best practices, and demonstrating incremental business value through initial cloud deployments that establish the foundation for broader adoption.\\nThe Envision phase focuses on creating a shared transformation vision and identifying how cloud can drive business outcomes, which occurs before establishing operational structures like a CCoE. The Align phase focuses on assessing readiness, identifying gaps, and creating an action plan, which is planning work that precedes the hands-on launch activities. The Scale phase focuses on expanding adoption across the entire organization after the CCoE and initial patterns have already been established during the Launch phase."},{"id":"q1097","question":"A company wants to move its on-premises application to AWS as quickly as possible with minimal changes to the existing code. Which migration strategy does this describe?","options":{"A":"Replatform","B":"Refactor","C":"Rehost","D":"Repurchase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rehosting, also known as lift and shift, involves moving an application to the cloud without making any changes to the application code or architecture, making it the fastest migration strategy for getting workloads into AWS.\\nReplatforming involves making targeted optimizations during migration, such as moving a database to a managed service like Amazon RDS, which requires some changes beyond a simple lift and shift. Refactoring involves redesigning the application to take advantage of cloud-native features such as serverless computing or microservices, which requires significant development effort and is not a minimal-change approach. Repurchasing involves replacing the existing application with a different product, typically a SaaS solution, which is not a strategy for moving existing code to AWS."},{"id":"q1098","question":"During a cloud migration assessment, a company identifies several legacy applications that are no longer in use. Which migration strategy should they apply to these applications?","options":{"A":"Retain","B":"Retire","C":"Rehost","D":"Relocate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retiring means decommissioning applications that are no longer needed, which reduces the overall migration scope and eliminates the cost and effort of maintaining unused software.\\nRetaining means keeping applications in their current on-premises environment because they are not ready to migrate, which is appropriate for applications still in active use rather than those that are no longer needed. Rehosting means moving an application to AWS without changes, which would waste resources on an application that provides no business value. Relocating means moving infrastructure to AWS with minimal changes, such as using VMware Cloud on AWS, which is also not appropriate for applications that should be decommissioned."},{"id":"q1099","question":"A company is migrating its on-premises email system and decides to replace it with Amazon WorkMail, a cloud-based SaaS email solution. Which migration strategy does this describe?","options":{"A":"Replatform","B":"Repurchase","C":"Refactor","D":"Rehost"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Repurchasing involves moving to a different product, typically replacing an existing application with a cloud-based SaaS alternative, which is exactly what switching from an on-premises email system to Amazon WorkMail represents.\\nReplatforming involves making targeted optimizations to an existing application during migration, such as moving a self-managed database to Amazon RDS, rather than replacing the entire application with a different product. Refactoring involves redesigning the existing application architecture to leverage cloud-native features, not replacing the application with a completely different product. Rehosting involves moving the existing application as-is to the cloud, which would mean running the same on-premises email system on EC2 rather than switching to a SaaS replacement."},{"id":"q1100","question":"A company wants to migrate its on-premises MySQL database to Amazon RDS for MySQL to take advantage of managed patching and backups, without changing the application code. Which migration strategy does this describe?","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Replatform","C":"Refactor","D":"Retain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Replatforming, also known as lift, tinker, and shift, involves making targeted optimizations during migration without changing the core application architecture, such as moving a self-managed database to a managed service like Amazon RDS to benefit from automated patching and backups.\\nRehosting would mean moving the MySQL database to an EC2 instance and continuing to manage it manually, which does not take advantage of managed database features. Refactoring would involve redesigning the application to use a fundamentally different database approach, such as migrating from a relational database to a NoSQL service like DynamoDB, which goes beyond targeted optimization. Retaining means keeping the workload in its current on-premises environment, which does not involve any migration to AWS."},{"id":"q1101","question":"Which migration strategy involves redesigning an application to take full advantage of cloud-native features such as serverless computing and microservices?","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Replatform","C":"Repurchase","D":"Refactor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Refactoring, also known as re-architecting, involves fundamentally redesigning an application to leverage cloud-native capabilities such as AWS Lambda for serverless computing, Amazon SQS for decoupled messaging, and containerised microservices, typically delivering the greatest long-term cloud benefits at the highest initial effort.\\nRehosting moves an application to the cloud without any architectural changes, preserving the existing design rather than adopting cloud-native patterns. Replatforming makes targeted optimizations during migration, such as switching to managed services, but does not involve a full architectural redesign. Repurchasing replaces the existing application with a different product, typically a SaaS solution, rather than redesigning the existing application architecture."},{"id":"q1102","question":"A company has applications that cannot be migrated to the cloud due to unresolved compliance dependencies. Which migration strategy should they apply to these applications?","options":{"A":"Retire","B":"Retain","C":"Relocate","D":"Repurchase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retaining means keeping applications in their current on-premises environment, which is the appropriate strategy for workloads that are not yet ready to migrate due to compliance constraints, technical dependencies, or other unresolved requirements.\\nRetiring means decommissioning applications that are no longer needed, which is not appropriate for applications that are still in active use and have unresolved dependencies. Relocating means moving infrastructure to AWS with minimal changes, such as using VMware Cloud on AWS, which does not address the underlying compliance dependencies preventing migration. Repurchasing means replacing the application with a SaaS alternative, which also does not resolve the compliance constraints that are blocking the migration."},{"id":"q1103","question":"Which of the following are valid migration strategies defined in the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)? (Select TWO)","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Revalidate","C":"Repurchase","D":"Redistribute","E":"Reconfigure"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Rehost and Repurchase are two of the seven migration strategies (7 Rs) defined in the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework: Rehost, Replatform, Refactor, Repurchase, Retire, Retain, and Relocate.\\nRevalidate is not a recognized migration strategy in the AWS CAF or any standard AWS migration framework. Redistribute is not a recognized migration strategy in the AWS CAF and does not correspond to any defined cloud migration approach. Reconfigure is not a recognized migration strategy in the AWS CAF; the closest strategy involving changes is Replatform, which makes targeted optimizations during migration."},{"id":"q1129","question":"A company currently runs VMware-based virtual machines on-premises and wants to move them to AWS with minimal changes to the existing VMware environment. Which migration strategy does this describe?","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Replatform","C":"Relocate","D":"Refactor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Relocating involves moving infrastructure to AWS without purchasing new hardware, rewriting applications, or modifying existing operations, such as migrating VMware-based workloads to VMware Cloud on AWS where the existing VMware environment continues to function with minimal changes.\\nRehosting involves moving individual applications to AWS infrastructure such as EC2 instances, which requires converting virtual machines rather than preserving the existing VMware management layer. Replatforming involves making targeted optimizations during migration such as switching to managed services, which goes beyond simply moving the existing VMware environment as-is. Refactoring involves redesigning the application architecture to use cloud-native services, which is the opposite of preserving the existing VMware-based infrastructure."},{"id":"q1130","question":"How many migration strategies (Rs) are defined in the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)?","options":{"A":"Five","B":"Six","C":"Seven","D":"Eight"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS CAF defines seven migration strategies known as the 7 Rs: Rehost, Replatform, Refactor (Re-architect), Repurchase, Retire, Retain, and Relocate, each representing a different approach to handling workloads during a cloud migration.\\nFive Rs was an earlier version of the migration strategies that did not include Relocate and Retain, and does not reflect the current AWS CAF guidance. Six Rs is another historical version that predates the addition of the Relocate strategy, and is no longer the complete set defined by the current AWS CAF. Eight Rs exceeds the number of strategies defined by the AWS CAF, which recognizes exactly seven distinct migration approaches."},{"id":"q1131","question":"A company wants to migrate a large on-premises Oracle database to Amazon DynamoDB to improve scalability and reduce licensing costs. Which migration strategy does this describe?","options":{"A":"Replatform","B":"Rehost","C":"Refactor","D":"Repurchase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Migrating from a relational Oracle database to a NoSQL service like Amazon DynamoDB requires fundamentally redesigning the data model and application logic, which is a refactoring or re-architecting strategy that leverages cloud-native capabilities for improved scalability and cost.\\nReplatforming would involve moving Oracle to Amazon RDS for Oracle or a compatible managed relational database, making targeted optimizations without changing the fundamental database paradigm. Rehosting would mean running Oracle on an EC2 instance exactly as it ran on-premises, with no changes to the database engine or architecture. Repurchasing would mean replacing the application with a completely different commercial product or SaaS solution, not redesigning the database layer to use a different AWS-native service."},{"id":"q1132","question":"Which AWS service helps automate the lift-and-shift migration of servers from on-premises or other clouds to AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Database Migration Service","B":"AWS Application Migration Service","C":"AWS Migration Hub","D":"AWS Snow Family"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Application Migration Service (AWS MGN) automates the lift-and-shift rehosting process by continuously replicating source servers to AWS, performing automated machine conversion, and launching fully provisioned instances on AWS with minimal downtime.\\nAWS Database Migration Service migrates databases to AWS, supporting both homogeneous and heterogeneous migrations, but it handles database workloads specifically rather than full server migrations. AWS Migration Hub provides a central location to track the progress of migrations across multiple AWS tools, but it is a tracking and visibility service rather than a tool that performs the actual server migration. AWS Snow Family provides physical devices for transferring large volumes of data to AWS offline, but does not automate the conversion and launching of servers in the cloud."},{"id":"q1133","question":"Which AWS service provides a central location to track the progress of application migrations across multiple AWS migration tools?","options":{"A":"AWS Application Migration Service","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Migration Hub","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Migration Hub provides a single dashboard to discover existing servers, plan migrations, and track the status of application migrations across AWS migration tools such as Application Migration Service, Database Migration Service, and partner solutions.\\nAWS Application Migration Service performs the actual server replication and migration to AWS, but it handles the migration execution rather than providing a centralized tracking dashboard across multiple tools. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and management events for auditing purposes, and is an activity logging service rather than a migration progress tracking tool. AWS Config records and evaluates resource configurations for compliance, and monitors the state of AWS resources rather than tracking migration progress."},{"id":"q1134","question":"A company needs to migrate a 100 TB on-premises database to AWS but has limited network bandwidth. Which AWS service is MOST appropriate for this data transfer?","options":{"A":"AWS DataSync","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS Snowball Edge","D":"Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Snowball Edge is a physical data transfer device that can hold up to 80 TB per device, allowing large-scale data transfers to AWS by shipping the device rather than transferring over the network, making it ideal when network bandwidth is limited and transfer times over the internet would be impractical.\\nAWS DataSync automates data transfers between on-premises storage and AWS over the network, but with limited bandwidth a 100 TB transfer would take an unacceptably long time. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection to AWS which improves bandwidth and consistency, but provisioning a Direct Connect link takes weeks and still may not provide sufficient bandwidth for a timely 100 TB transfer. Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration speeds up uploads to S3 using CloudFront edge locations, but it still relies on the internet and would be constrained by the company\'s limited network bandwidth."},{"id":"q1135","question":"Which migration strategy provides the GREATEST long-term benefit from cloud-native features but requires the MOST development effort?","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Replatform","C":"Refactor","D":"Relocate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Refactoring or re-architecting an application to use cloud-native services such as serverless computing, managed databases, and event-driven architectures delivers the greatest long-term benefits in scalability, performance, and cost efficiency, but requires the most significant investment in development time and effort.\\nRehosting provides the fastest migration path with minimal effort but does not take advantage of cloud-native features, meaning long-term benefits are limited to infrastructure-level improvements only. Replatforming provides moderate cloud benefits with moderate effort by making targeted optimizations, but does not achieve the full potential of cloud-native architecture. Relocating preserves the existing infrastructure environment and provides the least cloud-native benefit, as workloads continue to run in their original configuration."},{"id":"q1136","question":"A company is migrating to AWS and wants to assess its on-premises infrastructure to understand server dependencies and utilization. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"AWS Migration Hub","B":"AWS Application Discovery Service","C":"AWS Application Migration Service","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Application Discovery Service collects detailed information about on-premises servers including configuration, utilization, and network dependencies, helping companies plan their migration by understanding what they have and how applications are interconnected.\\nAWS Migration Hub provides a central dashboard for tracking migration progress across multiple tools, but it relies on discovery tools like Application Discovery Service to gather the underlying infrastructure data. AWS Application Migration Service performs the actual server migration by replicating and launching instances on AWS, but it does not discover or assess on-premises infrastructure. AWS Trusted Advisor provides best-practice recommendations for existing AWS environments across security, cost, and performance, but it does not discover or assess on-premises infrastructure."},{"id":"q1137","question":"Which AWS service is designed specifically to migrate databases from on-premises to AWS, supporting both same-engine and cross-engine migrations?","options":{"A":"AWS Application Migration Service","B":"AWS Schema Conversion Tool","C":"AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","D":"AWS DataSync"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Database Migration Service supports continuous data replication from on-premises databases to AWS with minimal downtime, handling both homogeneous migrations where the source and target engines are the same and heterogeneous migrations where the engines differ.\\nAWS Application Migration Service migrates entire servers including the operating system and applications to AWS, but it is not designed for database-specific migrations that require data replication and schema handling. AWS Schema Conversion Tool converts database schemas and stored procedures from one engine to another to support heterogeneous migrations, but it handles schema conversion rather than the actual data migration itself. AWS DataSync automates data transfers between on-premises storage systems and AWS storage services such as S3 and EFS, but it is a file-based data movement service not designed for database migration."},{"id":"q1138","question":"A company is migrating a monolithic application to AWS and decides to break it into microservices using AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway. Which migration strategy does this represent?","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Replatform","C":"Repurchase","D":"Refactor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Breaking a monolithic application into microservices using cloud-native services like AWS Lambda and Amazon API Gateway is a refactoring or re-architecting strategy that fundamentally changes the application design to leverage cloud capabilities.\\nRehosting would move the monolithic application to EC2 as-is without changing its architecture, preserving the monolith rather than decomposing it into microservices. Replatforming would make targeted improvements such as moving the database to a managed service, but would not involve a fundamental architectural change like decomposing a monolith into microservices. Repurchasing would replace the application with a different commercial or SaaS product, not redesign the existing application into a microservices architecture."},{"id":"q1139","question":"A company has 500 on-premises servers to migrate. After assessment, 200 are identified as candidates for lift-and-shift, 100 need database engine changes, 50 will be replaced with SaaS, and 150 are no longer needed. Which combination of migration strategies applies? (Select TWO)","options":{"A":"Rehost for the 200 servers","B":"Replatform for the 100 database servers","C":"Retire for the 150 unused servers","D":"Retain for the 50 SaaS replacements","E":"Relocate for the 200 servers"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Rehost applies to the 200 servers being moved as-is through lift-and-shift migration, and Retire applies to the 150 servers that are no longer needed and should be decommissioned, reducing the overall migration scope and cost.\\nReplatform is incorrect for the 100 database servers because changing the database engine entirely, such as from Oracle to PostgreSQL, constitutes refactoring rather than replatforming, which would be moving Oracle to RDS for Oracle. Retain means keeping workloads on-premises, which does not describe the 50 applications being replaced with SaaS solutions, that would be Repurchase. Relocate means moving VMware-based infrastructure to VMware Cloud on AWS, which is a specific strategy for preserving VMware environments and does not describe a general lift-and-shift migration."},{"id":"q1140","question":"Which of the following BEST describes the difference between rehosting and replatforming as migration strategies?","options":{"A":"Rehosting changes the application code while replatforming does not","B":"Rehosting moves the application as-is while replatforming makes targeted optimizations","C":"Rehosting replaces the application with SaaS while replatforming keeps the original","D":"Rehosting is slower than replatforming because it requires more testing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rehosting, or lift and shift, moves the application to AWS without any changes to the code or architecture, while replatforming, or lift, tinker, and shift, makes targeted optimizations during migration such as switching to a managed database or changing the caching layer, without fundamentally redesigning the application.\\nRehosting does not change any application code, and replatforming makes only targeted changes, so the first option reverses the relationship between the two strategies. Rehosting moves the existing application to the cloud rather than replacing it with SaaS, which would be the repurchase strategy. Rehosting is generally faster than replatforming because it requires fewer changes and less testing, not slower, since the application is moved without modification."},{"id":"q003","question":"You have noticed that several critical Amazon EC2 instances have been terminated. Which of the following AWS services would help you determine who took this action?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"EC2 Instance Usage Report"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and account activity across an AWS environment, capturing who performed each action, from which IP address, and at what time, making it the definitive source for identifying who terminated EC2 instances.\\nAmazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and does not record who performed API actions or management events. AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and is an advisory tool rather than an audit log of account activity. EC2 Instance Usage Report provides billing and usage data about EC2 instances and does not contain API call logs or identity information about who terminated instances."},{"id":"q005","question":"Which statement is true regarding the AWS Shared Responsibility Model?","options":{"A":"Responsibilities vary depending on the services used","B":"Security of the IaaS services is the responsibility of AWS","C":"Patching the guest OS is always the responsibility of AWS","D":"Security of the managed services is the responsibility of the customer"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Under the Shared Responsibility Model, who is responsible for what shifts depending on the service type, with AWS taking on more responsibility for managed services while customers handle more of the stack for IaaS services.\\nSecurity of IaaS services is not solely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers using IaaS must manage the guest operating system, application stack, and data, while AWS is only responsible for the underlying infrastructure. Patching the guest OS is not always AWS\'s responsibility, as for IaaS services such as EC2 the customer is fully responsible for patching the operating system they run on their instances. Security of managed services is not solely the customer\'s responsibility, as AWS takes on significantly more of the security burden for managed services such as RDS and Lambda, covering the underlying platform and infrastructure layers."},{"id":"q011","question":"An organization has a large number of technical employees who operate their AWS Cloud infrastructure. What does AWS provide to help organize them into teams and then assign the appropriate permissions for each team?","options":{"A":"IAM roles","B":"IAM users","C":"IAM user groups","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IAM user groups let you organize multiple users together and assign shared permissions at the group level via IAM policies, making it easy to manage access for entire teams without configuring each user individually.\\nIAM roles provide temporary security credentials that can be assumed by users, services, or applications, and are not designed to organize teams or assign shared permissions to a fixed set of users in the way groups are. IAM users are individual identities each with their own credentials and permissions, and managing large numbers of users individually by attaching policies to each one is impractical compared to using groups. AWS Organizations manages multiple AWS accounts under a single structure for consolidated billing and policy enforcement, and is not the service used to organize individual IAM users into teams within a single account."},{"id":"q022","question":"What does the \\"Principle of Least Privilege\\" refer to?","options":{"A":"You should grant your users only the permissions they need when they need them and nothing more","B":"All IAM users should have at least the necessary permissions to access the core AWS services","C":"All trusted IAM users should have access to any AWS service in the respective AWS account","D":"IAM users should not be granted any permissions; to keep your account safe"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Principle of Least Privilege means granting users only the exact permissions they need to perform their job and nothing beyond that, minimizing the risk of accidental or malicious misuse of AWS resources.\\nGranting IAM users at least the necessary permissions to access core AWS services describes a minimum baseline, which is the opposite of least privilege; least privilege means restricting permissions to only what is needed for a specific task rather than ensuring access to common services. Granting all trusted users access to any AWS service in the account contradicts least privilege by providing broad unrestricted access rather than targeted permissions. Granting no permissions to any IAM user is not a practical interpretation of least privilege, which is about granting the minimum required permissions rather than granting none at all."},{"id":"q026","question":"Hundreds of thousands of DDoS attacks are recorded every month worldwide. What service does AWS provide to help protect AWS Customers from these attacks?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon Cognito","D":"AWS WAF","E":"AWS KMS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Shield provides managed DDoS protection at the network and transport layers, and AWS WAF lets you create rules that filter malicious web traffic at the application layer. Together they defend against the full spectrum of DDoS attacks.\\nAWS Config continuously monitors and records AWS resource configurations to evaluate compliance, and is a configuration management and auditing service with no capability to block or mitigate DDoS traffic. Amazon Cognito is a user identity and authentication service for web and mobile applications, providing sign-up and sign-in functionality and federated identity, and has no DDoS mitigation capabilities. AWS KMS is a key management service for creating and controlling encryption keys used to protect data, and is not involved in network traffic filtering or DDoS defense."},{"id":"q031","question":"Which of the following services allows customers to manage their agreements with AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact is a self-service portal where customers can access and manage AWS compliance reports and agreements, including NDA and Business Associate Addendum (BAA) documents that govern the relationship between customers and AWS.\\nAWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services, and is a certificate management service rather than an agreement management portal. AWS Systems Manager provides operational tools for managing AWS resources at scale including patching, parameter storage, and session management, and is not used to manage contractual agreements with AWS. AWS Organizations manages multiple AWS accounts under a master account structure for consolidated billing and service control policies, and is not a portal for managing compliance reports or agreements."},{"id":"q032","question":"Which of the following are examples of AWS-managed services, where AWS is responsible for the operational and maintenance burdens of running the service?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon EMR","D":"AWS IAM","E":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB and Amazon EMR are fully managed services where AWS handles provisioning, patching, scaling, and maintenance automatically, freeing customers from managing the underlying infrastructure.\\nAmazon VPC is a networking construct that customers configure and manage themselves, including subnets, route tables, internet gateways, and security groups, making it a customer-configured resource rather than a fully managed service. AWS IAM is a service customers use to manage their own users, roles, and permissions, and while AWS maintains the IAM infrastructure, customers are responsible for all identity and access configuration. Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud provides virtual servers where customers are responsible for the operating system, installed software, patches, and configuration, making it an infrastructure service where the customer manages the virtual machine layer rather than a fully managed one."},{"id":"q036","question":"Which of the following must an IAM user provide to interact with AWS services using the AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI)?","options":{"A":"Access keys","B":"Secret token","C":"UserID","D":"User name and password"},"correct":"A","explanation":"IAM access keys, consisting of an access key ID and secret access key, are the credentials used to authenticate API requests programmatically through the AWS CLI, allowing IAM users to interact with AWS services from the command line without the Management Console.\\nA secret token is not a standard IAM credential type for CLI access; temporary security credentials issued to IAM roles include a session token in addition to an access key ID and secret, but a standalone secret token alone is not used for CLI authentication. A UserID is an internal AWS identifier assigned to IAM users but is not a credential that can be supplied to authenticate CLI requests. A user name and password are the credentials used to log in to the AWS Management Console interactively, not to authenticate programmatic CLI requests which require access keys."},{"id":"q037","question":"You have AWS Basic support, and you have discovered that some AWS resources are being used maliciously, and those resources could potentially compromise your data. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Contact the AWS Customer Service team","B":"Contact the AWS Abuse Team","C":"Contact the AWS Concierge team","D":"Contact the AWS Security team"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The AWS Abuse Team handles reports of AWS infrastructure being used for malicious activity such as hacking, spam, or unauthorised use, and this channel is available to all customers regardless of their support tier.\\nThe AWS Customer Service team handles general account and billing enquiries, and is not the designated contact for reporting malicious use of AWS infrastructure. The AWS Concierge team is a dedicated support resource available exclusively to Enterprise support plan customers for billing and account assistance, and does not handle abuse or malicious activity reports. The AWS Security team manages AWS\'s own internal security posture and vulnerability disclosure program, and is not the correct channel for customers to report malicious use of AWS resources within their own accounts."},{"id":"q038","question":"Which of the following are examples of AWS shared controls?","options":{"A":"Patch Management","B":"IAM Management","C":"VPC Management","D":"Configuration Management","E":"Data Center operations"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Patch Management and Configuration Management are both shared controls where AWS and the customer each manage their own layer independently, with AWS responsible for the infrastructure and customers responsible for their own operating systems and applications.\\nIAM Management is a customer responsibility, as customers exclusively control their own users, roles, and permissions within their AWS account. VPC Management is a customer responsibility, as customers define and configure their own virtual network settings including subnets, route tables, and security groups. Data Center operations are exclusively AWS\'s responsibility, covering all physical infrastructure management."},{"id":"q043","question":"Under the shared responsibility model, which of the following is the responsibility of AWS?","options":{"A":"Client-side encryption","B":"Configuring infrastructure devices","C":"Server-side encryption","D":"Filtering traffic with Security Groups"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Configuring infrastructure devices such as networking hardware and physical servers is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to or control over the underlying physical infrastructure.\\nClient-side encryption is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers choose whether and how to encrypt data before sending it to AWS. Server-side encryption is also the customer\'s responsibility, as customers decide whether to enable encryption at rest for their data stored in AWS services. Filtering traffic with Security Groups is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers define and manage their own Security Group rules within their AWS environment."},{"id":"q046","question":"Your company is developing a critical web application in AWS, and the security of the application is a top priority. Which of the following AWS services will provide infrastructure security optimization recommendations?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield","B":"AWS Management Console","C":"AWS Secrets Manager","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides real-time recommendations across five categories including security, cost optimization, performance, fault tolerance, and service limits, helping you follow AWS best practices.\\nAWS Shield is a managed DDoS protection service that defends against distributed denial of service attacks, and does not provide broad infrastructure security recommendations. AWS Management Console is the web-based interface for accessing and managing AWS services, and does not analyze or make recommendations about your environment. AWS Secrets Manager is a service for storing, rotating, and managing access to secrets such as database credentials and API keys, and does not provide infrastructure optimization recommendations."},{"id":"q048","question":"In the AWS Shared responsibility Model, which of the following are the responsibility of the customer?","options":{"A":"Disk disposal","B":"Controlling physical access to compute resources","C":"Patching the Network infrastructure","D":"Setting password complexity rules","E":"Configuring network access rules"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Setting password complexity rules and configuring network access rules such as security groups and NACLs are both customer responsibilities, as customers control their own identity policies and network configuration within their AWS environment.\\nDisk disposal is AWS\'s responsibility, as AWS manages the secure decommissioning and destruction of physical storage hardware. Controlling physical access to compute resources is AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to the underlying physical infrastructure. Patching the network infrastructure is AWS\'s responsibility, covering the managed networking layer beneath the customer\'s virtual network."},{"id":"q056","question":"Which of the following is the customer\'s responsibility under the AWS shared responsibility model?","options":{"A":"Patching underlying infrastructure","B":"Physical security","C":"Patching Amazon EC2 instances","D":"Patching network infrastructure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Customers are responsible for patching the operating system and software on EC2 instances they launch, as the guest OS and everything running on it falls within the customer\'s side of the Shared Responsibility Model.\\nPatching underlying infrastructure is AWS\'s responsibility, covering the hypervisor and physical hardware beneath the customer\'s instances. Physical security is AWS\'s responsibility exclusively, as customers have no access to or control over AWS data centers. Patching network infrastructure is AWS\'s responsibility, covering the managed networking layer that underpins all customer virtual networks."},{"id":"q057","question":"According to the AWS shared responsibility model who is responsible for configuration management?","options":{"A":"It is solely the responsibility of the customer","B":"It is solely the responsibility of AWS","C":"It is shared between AWS and the customer","D":"It is not part of the AWS shared responsibility model"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Configuration management is a shared control where AWS manages the configuration of its infrastructure, while the customer is responsible for configuring the operating systems, databases, and applications they deploy.\\nConfiguration management is not solely the customer\'s responsibility, as AWS independently manages the configuration of all underlying infrastructure components including hardware, networking, and managed services. Configuration management is not solely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers retain full accountability for configuring the resources and workloads they deploy on top of the AWS infrastructure. Configuration management is explicitly recognized as a shared control in the AWS Shared Responsibility Model and is therefore fully accounted for within it."},{"id":"q058","question":"Which security service automatically recognizes and classifies sensitive data or intellectual property on AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"Amazon Macie","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Shield"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Macie uses machine learning to automatically discover, classify, and protect sensitive data such as personally identifiable information and intellectual property stored in Amazon S3, making it the correct service for automatically recognizing and classifying sensitive data.\\nAmazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors AWS accounts and workloads for malicious activity and anomalous behavior using machine learning and threat intelligence, and focuses on detecting security threats rather than classifying data. Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and does not classify or discover sensitive data stored in S3. AWS Shield provides managed DDoS protection at the network and transport layers, and is a traffic protection service with no capability to recognize or classify sensitive data."},{"id":"q060","question":"Under the shared responsibility model, which of the following tasks are the responsibility of the AWS customer?","options":{"A":"Ensuring that application data is encrypted at rest","B":"Ensuring that AWS NTP servers are set to the correct time","C":"Ensuring that users have received security training in the use of AWS services","D":"Ensuring that access to data centers is restricted","E":"Ensuring that hardware is disposed of properly"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Customers are responsible for encrypting their application data at rest and for training their users on proper security practices when using AWS services, both sitting on the customer side of the model.\\nEnsuring that AWS NTP servers are set to the correct time is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to or control over the underlying infrastructure components that provide time synchronisation. Ensuring that access to data centers is restricted is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, covering physical access controls, surveillance, and protection of the facilities that house AWS infrastructure. Ensuring that hardware is disposed of properly is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers never take possession of physical hardware and AWS manages all end-of-life processes for its own equipment."},{"id":"q064","question":"Which AWS service allows users to identify the changes made to a resource over time?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Service Catalog","D":"AWS IAM"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Config continuously tracks and records configuration changes to AWS resources over time, allowing you to review historical configurations and identify what changed and when.\\nAmazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service that scans EC2 instances for security findings, not a service for tracking configuration changes over time. AWS Service Catalog allows organizations to create and manage approved portfolios of IT services for deployment, unrelated to tracking resource changes. AWS IAM manages user identities, roles, and access permissions within an AWS account and does not record or track configuration changes to resources."},{"id":"q075","question":"Access keys in AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) are used to:","options":{"A":"Log in to the AWS Management Console","B":"Make programmatic calls to AWS from AWS APIs","C":"Log in to Amazon EC2 instances","D":"Authenticate to AWS CodeCommit repositories"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IAM access keys, consisting of an access key ID and a secret access key, are the credentials used to authenticate programmatic calls to AWS APIs via the CLI, SDKs, or direct HTTP requests, allowing applications and scripts to interact with AWS services without using the console.\\nLogging in to the AWS Management Console requires a user name and password combination, not access keys, which are reserved for programmatic and CLI access rather than browser-based console authentication. Logging in to Amazon EC2 instances uses SSH key pairs on Linux instances or a password retrieved via the console for Windows instances, and is separate from the IAM access keys used for API authentication. Authenticating to AWS CodeCommit repositories uses Git credentials or SSH keys configured separately from IAM access keys, though access keys can be used with the AWS CLI to interact with CodeCommit programmatically."},{"id":"q078","question":"Which AWS services can be used to gather information about AWS account activity?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Cloud9","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS CloudHSM","E":"Amazon CloudWatch"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"AWS CloudTrail logs all API calls and account activity across an AWS account, providing a complete audit trail of who did what and when. Amazon CloudWatch collects metrics, logs, and event data from AWS services and resources, providing operational visibility into account activity and performance.\\nAmazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and distributes content to users at edge locations worldwide, and does not gather information about account activity. AWS Cloud9 is a cloud-based integrated development environment for writing, running, and debugging code, and has no account monitoring or activity logging capability. AWS CloudHSM is a hardware security module service that manages and stores cryptographic keys in dedicated hardware, and is not a tool for monitoring or gathering information about account activity."},{"id":"q090","question":"Which AWS IAM feature allows developers to access AWS services through the AWS CLI?","options":{"A":"API keys","B":"Access keys","C":"User names/Passwords","D":"SSH keys"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IAM access keys, consisting of an access key ID and a secret access key, are the credentials used to authenticate programmatic requests to AWS services through the AWS CLI and SDKs.\\nAPI keys is an informal term not specific to AWS IAM; AWS programmatic access uses access keys consisting of an access key ID and secret access key, and this informal label does not accurately describe the credential type. User names and passwords are used to log in to the AWS Management Console interactively, and are not the credentials used for CLI or SDK programmatic access, which requires access keys. SSH keys are used to connect securely to EC2 instances via Secure Shell and are not the credentials that authenticate developers\' CLI requests to AWS services."},{"id":"q091","question":"The user is fully responsible for which action when running workloads on AWS?","options":{"A":"Patching the infrastructure components","B":"Maintaining the underlying infrastructure components","C":"Maintaining physical and environmental controls","D":"Implementing controls to route application traffic"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Customers are fully responsible for implementing application-level controls such as routing traffic within their own systems, as these decisions sit entirely within the customer\'s layer of the Shared Responsibility Model.\\nPatching the infrastructure components is AWS\'s responsibility, covering the physical servers, networking equipment, and hypervisor layer beneath the customer\'s workloads. Maintaining the underlying infrastructure components is AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to or control over the physical hardware running their workloads. Maintaining physical and environmental controls is AWS\'s responsibility exclusively, covering data center facilities, power, cooling, and physical security."},{"id":"q103","question":"Which of the following will enhance the security of access to the AWS Management Console?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS Multi-Factor Authentication (AWS MFA)","D":"Security groups","E":"Password policies"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"MFA adds a second authentication factor beyond a password to protect console logins, and password policies enforce complexity and rotation requirements. Both directly enhance the security of access to the AWS Management Console.\\nAWS Secrets Manager stores, rotates, and manages access to secrets such as database credentials and API keys, and is a credentials management service rather than a mechanism for enhancing the authentication security of the AWS Management Console itself. AWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for encrypting data in transit between applications and users, and has no role in controlling or securing console login access. Security groups act as virtual firewalls that control inbound and outbound network traffic at the EC2 instance level, and operate at the network layer rather than the console authentication layer."},{"id":"q105","question":"For which auditing process does AWS have sole responsibility?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM policies","B":"Physical security","C":"Amazon S3 bucket policies","D":"AWS CloudTrail Logs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Under the shared responsibility model, AWS has sole responsibility for physical security of its data centers, including controls such as perimeter security, surveillance, and hardware disposal, as customers never have access to AWS physical facilities.\\nAWS IAM policies define permissions for users, groups, and roles within an AWS account, and creating, managing, and auditing these policies is entirely the customer\'s responsibility as they reflect each customer\'s own access control decisions. Amazon S3 bucket policies control access to objects stored within S3 buckets, and configuring and auditing these policies is the customer\'s responsibility as they govern how the customer\'s own data is accessed and shared. AWS CloudTrail logs record API calls and account activity across an AWS environment, and while CloudTrail is an AWS-managed service, enabling it, configuring log retention, and auditing the logs it produces are all customer responsibilities."},{"id":"q107","question":"Which of the following common IT tasks can AWS cover to free up company IT resources?","options":{"A":"Patching database software","B":"Testing application releases","C":"Backing up databases","D":"Creating database schema","E":"Running penetration tests"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Patching database software and backing up databases are maintenance tasks that AWS handles automatically for managed services such as RDS, freeing up customers\' IT resources from these time-consuming operational responsibilities.\\nTesting application releases is the customer\'s responsibility as it requires knowledge of the specific application logic, acceptance criteria, and business requirements that only the customer possesses, making it a task AWS cannot cover on the customer\'s behalf. Creating database schema requires understanding of the customer\'s data model and application requirements, and is a design and development activity that the customer must perform rather than a managed service task AWS provides. Running penetration tests is a security activity that customers perform on their own applications and infrastructure, and is not an operational burden AWS covers as part of its managed service offerings."},{"id":"q114","question":"If a customer needs to audit the change management of AWS resources, which of the following AWS services should the customer use?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Config continuously records resource configurations and changes over time, providing a detailed audit trail of what changed, when it changed, and who made the change, making it the purpose-built service for auditing change management of AWS resources.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, but does not record or audit configuration changes to individual resources over time. Amazon CloudWatch collects metrics, logs, and event data from AWS services and resources to provide operational monitoring and alerting, and does not track resource configuration history or change management. Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and is a security testing tool rather than a configuration change auditing service."},{"id":"q117","question":"Which of the following Identity and Access Management (IAM) entities is associated with an access key ID and secret access key when using AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI)?","options":{"A":"IAM group","B":"IAM user","C":"IAM role","D":"IAM policy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IAM users are the entities that have access keys consisting of an access key ID and secret access key associated with them for programmatic CLI access to AWS services.\\nAn IAM group is a collection of IAM users used to manage shared permissions collectively, and is not an identity that can authenticate or be issued access keys of any kind. An IAM role uses temporary security credentials that are automatically issued and rotated when the role is assumed, and does not have permanent access keys associated with it. An IAM policy is a JSON document that defines permissions by specifying allowed or denied actions on resources, and is a permissions construct rather than an identity that can hold or be associated with access keys."},{"id":"q118","question":"Under the shared responsibility model, which of the following is the customer responsible for?","options":{"A":"Ensuring that disk drives are wiped after use","B":"Ensuring that firmware is updated on hardware devices","C":"Ensuring that data is encrypted at rest","D":"Ensuring that network cables are category six or higher"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Data encryption at rest is a customer responsibility. AWS provides the tools and services to enable encryption, but customers decide whether and how to encrypt their data.\\nEnsuring disk drives are wiped after use is AWS\'s responsibility. AWS handles the secure decommissioning and disposal of physical storage hardware. Ensuring firmware is updated on hardware devices is AWS\'s responsibility. Customers have no access to or control over physical hardware. Ensuring network cables are category six or higher is AWS\'s responsibility. Physical networking infrastructure is managed entirely by AWS."},{"id":"q120","question":"What credential components are required to gain programmatic access to an AWS account?","options":{"A":"An access key ID","B":"A primary key","C":"A secret access key","D":"A user ID","E":"A secondary key"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Programmatic AWS access requires an access key ID to identify the calling entity and a secret access key to sign requests, together authenticating API calls made via the CLI or SDKs.\\nA primary key is not a valid AWS IAM credential component; it is a database term for a unique row identifier and has no role in AWS programmatic authentication. A user ID is an internal AWS identifier assigned to IAM users for tracking purposes but cannot be supplied as a credential to authenticate programmatic API requests. A secondary key is not a recognized AWS IAM credential type, and no such concept exists in the AWS authentication model for programmatic access."},{"id":"q121","question":"Which of the following is a shared control between the customer and AWS?","options":{"A":"Providing a key for Amazon S3 client-side encryption","B":"Configuration of an Amazon EC2 instance","C":"Environmental controls of physical AWS data centers","D":"Awareness"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Awareness and training is a shared control because both AWS and the customer must independently ensure their own staff understand their respective security responsibilities, regardless of what the other party does.\\nProviding a key for Amazon S3 client-side encryption is solely the customer\'s responsibility, as AWS has no involvement in client-side key generation or management. Configuration of an Amazon EC2 instance is solely the customer\'s responsibility, as customers control the guest OS and everything installed on it. Environmental controls of physical AWS data centers are solely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to or involvement in physical facility management."},{"id":"q126","question":"Which of the following security measures protect access to an AWS account?","options":{"A":"Enable AWS CloudTrail","B":"Grant least privilege access to IAM users","C":"Create one IAM user and share with many developers and users","D":"Enable Amazon CloudFront","E":"Activate multi-factor authentication (MFA) for privileged users"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Granting least privilege access ensures IAM users only have the permissions they need, reducing the blast radius of a compromised account, and activating MFA for privileged users adds a critical second authentication factor that protects against stolen credentials. Both directly protect access to an AWS account.\\nEnabling AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity for auditing and compliance, and while it improves visibility into account actions after the fact, it does not prevent unauthorised access and is therefore not a security measure that protects access. Creating one IAM user shared across many developers is a security anti-pattern because it prevents individual accountability, makes credential rotation difficult, and means that a single compromised credential grants access to everyone sharing that account. Enabling Amazon CloudFront provides a content delivery network for distributing web content globally, and has no connection to protecting access to an AWS account."},{"id":"q144","question":"Which activity is a customer responsibility in the AWS Cloud according to the AWS shared responsibility model?","options":{"A":"Ensuring network connectivity from AWS to the internet","B":"Patching and fixing flaws within the AWS Cloud infrastructure","C":"Ensuring the physical security of cloud data centers","D":"Ensuring Amazon EBS volumes are backed up"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Under the Shared Responsibility Model, backing up EBS volumes through snapshots is the customer\'s responsibility, as AWS does not automatically back up EBS volumes on the customer\'s behalf.\\nEnsuring network connectivity from AWS to the internet is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, as AWS manages and maintains the underlying network infrastructure that connects its services to the public internet. Patching and fixing flaws within the AWS Cloud infrastructure is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to the underlying hardware, hypervisors, or physical networking components. Ensuring the physical security of cloud data centers is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, covering access controls, surveillance, and protection of the facilities that house AWS infrastructure."},{"id":"q150","question":"How can a customer increase security to AWS account logons?","options":{"A":"Configure AWS Certificate Manager","B":"Enable Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)","C":"Use Amazon Cognito to manage access","D":"Configure a strong password policy","E":"Enable AWS Organizations"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Enabling MFA requires a second factor beyond the password to complete sign-in, and configuring a strong password policy enforces complexity, length, and rotation requirements. Both directly strengthen AWS account logon security.\\nAWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for encrypting data in transit between applications and users, and has no role in controlling or securing account logon processes. Amazon Cognito manages user authentication and access for customer-facing applications through user pools and identity pools, and is designed for application-level user management rather than securing AWS account logons directly. AWS Organizations is an account management service for centrally governing and consolidating multiple AWS accounts, and does not itself provide logon security features for individual account access."},{"id":"q158","question":"Which AWS service is used to track, record, and audit configuration changes made to AWS resources?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Config continuously records configuration changes to AWS resources and maintains a history of those changes, enabling audit and compliance reviews of how resources have been configured over time.\\nAWS Shield is a managed DDoS protection service that safeguards AWS applications against distributed denial-of-service attacks, and has no capability to track or audit resource configuration changes. AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) is a service for controlling who can authenticate and what actions they are authorised to perform, and manages permissions and identities rather than recording configuration changes to AWS resources. Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and is a security testing tool rather than a configuration change tracking or auditing service."},{"id":"q162","question":"Which of the following services falls under the responsibility of the customer to maintain operating system configuration, security patching, and networking?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon EC2","C":"Amazon ElastiCache","D":"AWS Fargate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With Amazon EC2, the customer is responsible for the guest operating system including configuration, security patching, and networking setup, as AWS only manages the underlying physical infrastructure.\\nAmazon RDS is a fully managed relational database service where AWS handles operating system patching, database engine updates, and infrastructure maintenance on the customer\'s behalf. Amazon ElastiCache is a fully managed in-memory caching service where AWS manages the underlying operating system and infrastructure, removing those responsibilities from the customer entirely. AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers where AWS manages the underlying infrastructure and operating system, meaning the customer only needs to manage their container images and application code."},{"id":"q163","question":"AWS supports which of the following methods to add security to Identity and Access Management (IAM) users?","options":{"A":"Implementing Amazon Rekognition","B":"Using AWS Shield-protected resources","C":"Blocking access with Security Groups","D":"Using Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)","E":"Enforcing password strength and expiration"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"MFA adds a second authentication factor beyond a password, and enforcing password strength and expiration policies reduce the risk of credential compromise. Both are supported IAM security methods that directly protect IAM user accounts.\\nAmazon Rekognition is a machine learning service for analyzing images and videos to detect objects, faces, and text, and has no role in adding security to IAM user authentication. AWS Shield provides managed DDoS protection at the network and transport layers, and is a traffic protection service rather than a mechanism for securing IAM user accounts. Security groups act as virtual firewalls that control inbound and outbound network traffic at the EC2 instance level, and operate at the network layer rather than the IAM authentication layer."},{"id":"q169","question":"A Cloud Practitioner must determine if any security groups in an AWS account have been provisioned to allow unrestricted access for specific ports. What is the SIMPLEST way to do this?","options":{"A":"Review the inbound rules for each security group in the Amazon EC2 management console to check for port 0.0.0.0/0","B":"Run AWS Trusted Advisor and review the findings","C":"Open the AWS IAM console and check the inbound rule filters for open access","D":"In AWS Config, create a custom rule that invokes an AWS Lambda function to review firewall rules for inbound access"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor automatically scans your account and flags security groups with unrestricted access (0.0.0.0/0) to specific ports as part of its Security category checks, making it the simplest and most direct approach without manual investigation.\\nManually reviewing inbound rules for each security group in the EC2 console to look for 0.0.0.0/0 entries would work but is time-consuming and error-prone at scale, making it far less simple than using Trusted Advisor\'s automated check. The AWS IAM console manages identities, users, roles, and permissions, and does not contain security group configurations or inbound traffic rules, making it the wrong place to check for unrestricted port access. Creating a custom AWS Config rule that invokes a Lambda function to review firewall rules would provide automated ongoing compliance checks but requires significant setup effort, making it far more complex than running the existing Trusted Advisor check."},{"id":"q170","question":"Which of the following security-related services does AWS offer?","options":{"A":"Multi-factor authentication physical tokens","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor security checks","C":"Data encryption","D":"Automated penetration testing","E":"Amazon S3 copyrighted content detection"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor includes security checks that flag vulnerabilities such as open security groups, and AWS provides data encryption capabilities through services like KMS and native encryption across storage and database services. Both are genuine AWS security-related offerings.\\nMulti-factor authentication physical tokens are hardware MFA devices that customers purchase independently rather than a service provided by AWS; customers configure them with their accounts but AWS does not supply or manufacture the hardware tokens. Automated penetration testing is not an AWS service offering; AWS permits customers to conduct their own penetration testing on permitted services, but AWS does not perform automated penetration testing on customer workloads as a service. Amazon S3 copyrighted content detection is not an AWS service; Amazon Macie detects sensitive data categories such as PII in S3, but copyright detection is not within the scope of available AWS security services."},{"id":"q171","question":"Which of the following services have Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) mitigation features?","options":{"A":"AWS WAF","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon EC2","D":"Amazon CloudFront","E":"Amazon Inspector"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS WAF filters malicious HTTP and HTTPS traffic at the application layer by blocking requests that match defined rules, and Amazon CloudFront integrates with AWS Shield to absorb volumetric DDoS attacks at edge locations before they reach origin servers. Both directly provide DDoS mitigation features.\\nAmazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database service, and while AWS protects the underlying infrastructure it runs on, DynamoDB itself is not a service that provides DDoS mitigation features. Amazon EC2 provides virtual compute instances and does not include built-in DDoS mitigation; customers can configure security groups and use additional services like Shield and WAF to protect applications running on EC2. Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and does not provide DDoS mitigation capabilities."},{"id":"q175","question":"What can users access from AWS Artifact?","options":{"A":"AWS security and compliance documents","B":"A download of configuration management details for all AWS resources","C":"Training materials for AWS services","D":"A security assessment of the applications deployed in the AWS Cloud"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact is a self-service portal that provides on-demand access to AWS security and compliance documents such as SOC reports, ISO certifications, PCI attestations, and other auditor-issued compliance materials.\\nConfiguration management details are not available from Artifact; AWS Config tracks resource configuration history and CloudTrail logs API activity, but neither is reached through Artifact. Training materials come from AWS Training and Certification and AWS Skill Builder, not Artifact, which holds only compliance documents. A security assessment of deployed applications is performed by Amazon Inspector, which scans EC2 instances and container images for vulnerabilities, not by Artifact."},{"id":"q179","question":"Which of the following can limit Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) bucket access to specific users?","options":{"A":"A public and private key-pair","B":"Amazon Inspector","C":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies","D":"Security Groups"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IAM policies can be applied directly to IAM users or groups to control exactly who can access an S3 bucket and what operations they can perform, providing fine-grained access control that limits access to specific users.\\nSigning in with AWS account credentials provides root-level access to all S3 buckets by default rather than limiting access to specific users; root credentials should never be used for day-to-day access and do not restrict bucket access to particular individuals. Enabling S3 versioning preserves multiple versions of objects to protect against accidental deletion, and is a data recovery feature rather than an access restriction mechanism that limits which users can access the bucket. Setting a bucket policy to make the bucket private removes public access, but without specifying allowed principals it does not grant access to any specific users; additional policy statements identifying particular IAM principals are required to limit access to specific users."},{"id":"q181","question":"Where are AWS compliance documents, such as an SOC 1 report, located?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Artifact","D":"AWS Certificate Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Artifact is the dedicated self-service portal for accessing AWS compliance documentation, including SOC 1, SOC 2, ISO 27001, and PCI DSS reports, allowing customers to download these documents for their own audit and compliance purposes.\\nAmazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and network exposure risks, and does not provide or store AWS compliance certifications or SOC reports. AWS CloudTrail logs all API calls and management events in an AWS account for auditing purposes, and produces customer-specific activity logs rather than AWS compliance certifications or third-party audit reports. AWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services to encrypt data in transit, and is a certificate management service rather than a repository of compliance reports."},{"id":"q182","question":"Which of the following tasks is the responsibility of AWS?","options":{"A":"Encrypting client-side data","B":"Configuring AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) roles","C":"Securing the Amazon EC2 hypervisor","D":"Setting user password policies"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Under the shared responsibility model, AWS is responsible for securing the underlying hypervisor that runs EC2 virtual machines, as this forms part of the infrastructure layer that customers have no access to or control over.\\nEncrypting client-side data is the customer\'s responsibility, as AWS has no involvement in data that is encrypted before being sent to AWS services. Configuring IAM roles is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers define and manage the permissions that control what actions users and services can perform within their account. Setting user password policies is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers control the IAM password policies that govern how their users authenticate to the AWS Management Console."},{"id":"q183","question":"Under the shared responsibility model which of the following areas are the customer\'s responsibility?","options":{"A":"Firmware upgrades of network infrastructure","B":"Patching of operating systems","C":"Patching of the underlying hypervisor","D":"Physical security of data centers","E":"Configuration of the security group"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Customers are responsible for patching the operating systems running on their EC2 instances and for configuring security groups to control inbound and outbound traffic within their AWS environment, both sitting on the customer side of the model.\\nFirmware upgrades of network infrastructure are entirely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to or control over the physical networking equipment that underpins the AWS Cloud. Patching the underlying hypervisor is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, as the virtualization layer sits below the operating system and is managed exclusively by AWS. Physical security of data centers is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, covering access controls, surveillance, and protection of the facilities that house AWS infrastructure."},{"id":"q190","question":"Which AWS security service protects applications from distributed denial of service attacks with always-on detection and automatic inline mitigations?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF)","C":"Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)","D":"AWS Shield"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Shield provides always-on DDoS protection at no extra cost for all AWS customers, automatically detecting and mitigating the most common volumetric and protocol-based DDoS attacks without any customer configuration.\\nAWS WAF is a web application firewall that filters application-layer HTTP and HTTPS traffic based on configurable rules, and while it helps mitigate application-layer attacks, it is a separate service from Shield and requires customer configuration rather than providing automatic always-on protection. Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that uses machine learning to identify malicious activity and anomalous behavior in AWS accounts, and is an alerting and detection service rather than a service that actively blocks DDoS traffic. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and management events for auditing and compliance purposes, and has no capability to detect or protect against distributed denial of service attacks."},{"id":"q199","question":"A company has moved to AWS recently. Which of the following AWS Services will help ensure that they have the proper security settings?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"Amazon Inspector","C":"Amazon SNS","D":"Amazon CloudWatch","E":"Concierge Support Team"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor provides automated security checks identifying vulnerabilities such as unrestricted security group access and missing MFA, and Amazon Inspector performs automated security assessments of EC2 instances to identify vulnerabilities and unintended network access. Both help ensure proper security settings.\\nAmazon SNS is a fully managed publish/subscribe messaging service for sending notifications to subscribers, and does not evaluate or ensure AWS security settings or configurations. Amazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics and logs for observability and alerting, and while it can trigger notifications on specific metrics or log patterns, it does not actively assess or validate AWS resource security configurations. The Concierge Support Team assists Enterprise support plan customers with billing and account management, and does not perform technical security assessments or review resource security settings."},{"id":"q200","question":"What is the AWS feature that provides an additional level of security above the default authentication mechanism of usernames and passwords?","options":{"A":"Encrypted keys","B":"Email verification","C":"AWS KMS","D":"AWS MFA"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Multi-Factor Authentication adds a second layer of security by requiring a one-time code from a hardware or virtual device in addition to a username and password, providing an additional level of security above the default username and password mechanism.\\nEncrypted keys refer to cryptographic keys used for data encryption purposes such as those managed by AWS KMS, and while they protect stored data, they do not provide an additional authentication mechanism for user login security. Email verification is used to confirm ownership of an email address during account setup or password recovery flows, and is not an ongoing additional security layer applied on top of username and password for AWS console logins. AWS KMS is a key management service for creating and managing cryptographic keys used to encrypt data at rest and in transit, and does not add authentication factors to the login process."},{"id":"q204","question":"According to the AWS Acceptable Use Policy, which of the following statements is true regarding penetration testing of EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"Penetration testing is not allowed in AWS","B":"Penetration testing is performed automatically by AWS to determine vulnerabilities in your AWS infrastructure","C":"Penetration testing can be performed by the customer on their own instances without prior authorization from AWS","D":"The AWS customers are only allowed to perform penetration testing on services managed by AWS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS\'s Acceptable Use Policy allows customers to perform penetration testing on their own EC2 instances and certain other permitted services without prior authorisation from AWS, provided the testing complies with the AUP conditions.\\nPenetration testing is not prohibited in AWS; the Acceptable Use Policy explicitly permits customers to conduct penetration testing on their own resources within defined guidelines, making the claim that it is not allowed incorrect. AWS does not perform automated penetration testing on behalf of customers; Amazon Inspector performs automated security assessments for vulnerabilities and network exposure, but this is not the same as penetration testing, and AWS does not conduct adversarial testing of customer infrastructure. AWS customers are permitted to perform penetration testing on their own services and resources, not only on services managed by AWS; the restriction is that testing must target the customer\'s own resources rather than AWS shared infrastructure."},{"id":"q208","question":"According to the AWS Shared responsibility model, which of the following are the responsibility of the customer?","options":{"A":"Managing environmental events of AWS data centers","B":"Protecting the confidentiality of data in transit in Amazon S3","C":"Controlling physical access to AWS Regions","D":"Ensuring that the underlying EC2 host is configured properly","E":"Patching applications installed on Amazon EC2"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Customers are responsible for protecting the confidentiality of data in transit using encryption such as HTTPS/TLS on connections to Amazon S3, and for patching operating systems and applications installed on Amazon EC2 instances as part of managing their guest environment.\\nManaging environmental events at AWS data centers, including power, cooling, fire suppression, and natural disaster response, is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as part of securing the physical infrastructure that customers never interact with. Controlling physical access to AWS Regions and data center facilities is AWS\'s responsibility; customers have no access to physical infrastructure and rely on AWS to implement badge-based entry, biometric controls, and security staff. Ensuring that the underlying EC2 host is configured properly falls under AWS\'s responsibility; customers manage the guest operating system and above, while AWS is responsible for the hypervisor, host hardware, and underlying virtualization layer."},{"id":"q211","question":"Which of the following is equivalent to a username and password and is used to authenticate your programmatic access to AWS services and APIs?","options":{"A":"Instance Password","B":"Key pairs","C":"Access Keys","D":"MFA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Access Keys, consisting of an access key ID and a secret access key, are the credentials used to authenticate programmatic API calls, functioning like a username and password for AWS SDKs and CLI.\\nAn instance password refers to the Windows administrator password generated for EC2 Windows instances to enable RDP access, which is a server login credential rather than an API authentication mechanism equivalent to a username and password. Key pairs are RSA public/private key combinations used to authenticate SSH connections to Linux EC2 instances, and while they serve an authentication purpose, they are for instance-level SSH access rather than AWS API and service authentication. MFA is an additional authentication factor added on top of primary credentials such as a username and password, requiring a one-time code from a device, and is a supplementary mechanism rather than a standalone credential pair equivalent to a username and password."},{"id":"q213","question":"What is the AWS service that enables you to manage all of your AWS accounts from a single master account?","options":{"A":"AWS WAF","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Organizations","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Organizations enables you to create a hierarchy of AWS accounts managed from a single master account, applying policies and consolidated billing across all member accounts.\\nAWS WAF is a web application firewall that filters and blocks malicious web traffic using managed and custom rule sets, and has no role in managing multiple AWS accounts. AWS Trusted Advisor analyzes your AWS environment and provides recommendations across cost optimization, security, and performance, but does not provide centralized management of multiple accounts. AWS Config continuously monitors and records AWS resource configurations for compliance purposes, but operates within individual accounts rather than providing centralized multi-account management."},{"id":"q219","question":"Which of the following services can help protect your web applications from SQL injection and other vulnerabilities in your application code?","options":{"A":"Amazon Cognito","B":"AWS IAM","C":"Amazon Aurora","D":"AWS WAF"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS WAF is a web application firewall that lets you create rules to inspect and filter HTTP and HTTPS traffic, blocking common web exploits such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting before they reach your application.\\nAmazon Cognito is a user identity and authentication service for web and mobile applications that handles user sign-up, sign-in, and access control for application users, and does not inspect or filter HTTP traffic for SQL injection or other web exploits. AWS IAM manages user permissions and access to AWS services and resources, and is an identity and access management service rather than a traffic inspection tool that can block malicious web requests. Amazon Aurora is a relational database service compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL, and is a managed database offering rather than a security service for filtering web application traffic."},{"id":"q221","question":"Based on the AWS Shared Responsibility Model, which of the following are the sole responsibility of AWS?","options":{"A":"Monitoring network performance","B":"Installing software on EC2 instances","C":"Creating hypervisors","D":"Configuring Access Control Lists (ACLs)","E":"Hardware maintenance"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Creating and managing hypervisors and maintaining physical hardware are solely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to or control over the virtualization layer or the underlying infrastructure that powers AWS services.\\nMonitoring network performance for their own applications and workloads is the customer\'s responsibility, as AWS provides the tools but customers are accountable for observing and responding to performance within their environment. Installing software on EC2 instances is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers manage everything above the hypervisor including the operating system, applications, and any software they choose to run. Configuring Access Control Lists is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers define and manage their own network access rules within their VPC environment."},{"id":"q223","question":"What are the default security credentials that are required to access the AWS management console for an IAM user account?","options":{"A":"MFA","B":"Security tokens","C":"A user name and password","D":"Access keys"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IAM user console access is secured with a user name and password combination as the default credentials, providing the basic authentication required to access the AWS Management Console.\\nMFA is an optional additional authentication factor that can be layered on top of a username and password to require a one-time code from a device, but it is not the default credential requirement and is configured separately from the primary user name and password. Security tokens are temporary credentials consisting of an access key ID, secret access key, and session token that are issued when an IAM role is assumed, and are used for programmatic and role-based access rather than as default console credentials for IAM user accounts. Access keys consisting of an access key ID and secret access key are the credentials used for programmatic API and CLI access, not for logging into the AWS Management Console, which uses user name and password."},{"id":"q226","question":"Which of the following aspects of security are managed by AWS?","options":{"A":"Encryption of EBS volumes","B":"VPC security","C":"Access permissions","D":"Hardware patching","E":"Securing global physical infrastructure"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Hardware patching and maintenance of the physical servers that run AWS services, and securing the global physical infrastructure including data centers, power, cooling, and networking, are entirely AWS\'s responsibility under the shared responsibility model.\\nEncryption of EBS volumes is a customer responsibility that involves enabling EBS encryption at the volume or account level and selecting appropriate KMS keys to protect data at rest on block storage volumes. VPC security, including the configuration of security groups, network ACLs, and route tables, is the customer\'s responsibility as it governs how network traffic flows within and to the customer\'s own virtual private cloud. Access permissions, including creating IAM users, groups, roles, and attaching policies that control who can access which AWS services and resources, are entirely the customer\'s responsibility to define and manage."},{"id":"q229","question":"What are the change management tools that helps AWS customers audit and monitor all resource changes in their AWS environment?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"AWS Transit Gateway","D":"AWS X-Ray","E":"AWS Config"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and resource changes for auditing, and AWS Config continuously monitors and records resource configuration changes and evaluates them against compliance rules. Together they are the primary change management and auditing tools in AWS.\\nAmazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service that uses machine learning to extract insights such as entities, sentiment, and key phrases from text, and is an AI analytics service with no capability to audit or monitor resource configuration changes. AWS Transit Gateway connects VPCs and on-premises networks through a central hub, providing network routing and connectivity rather than auditing or change management functionality. AWS X-Ray is an application performance tracing service that analyzes requests as they flow through distributed applications, and is a debugging and performance tool rather than an infrastructure change auditing service."},{"id":"q231","question":"Which of the following services will help businesses ensure compliance in AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Migration Hub","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail logs all API activity and account events, creating a complete audit trail that helps organizations demonstrate compliance with regulatory requirements and investigate security incidents.\\nAmazon CloudFront is a global content delivery network that caches and distributes web content to users at low latency through edge locations, and is a performance and distribution service with no capability to record audit logs or help with regulatory compliance. AWS Migration Hub provides a central dashboard for tracking the progress of application migrations to AWS, and is a migration management tool rather than a compliance or audit service. Amazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics, logs, and events for AWS resources and applications, providing visibility into performance and operational health, but it does not create the comprehensive API audit trail needed for regulatory compliance."},{"id":"q236","question":"Which of the following AWS security features is associated with an EC2 instance and functions to filter incoming traffic requests?","options":{"A":"AWS X-Ray","B":"Network ACL","C":"Security Groups","D":"VPC Flow logs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Security groups act as a stateful virtual firewall directly associated with EC2 instances, evaluating and filtering inbound and outbound traffic based on protocol, port, and source or destination rules defined by the customer.\\nAWS X-Ray is an application tracing and debugging service that analyzes requests as they flow through distributed applications to identify performance bottlenecks and errors, and has no capability to filter or control network traffic to EC2 instances. Network ACLs are stateless firewalls that operate at the subnet level rather than the EC2 instance level, applying rules to all traffic entering or leaving a subnet without tracking connection state, and are not directly associated with individual instances. VPC Flow Logs capture metadata about the IP traffic flowing to and from network interfaces within a VPC, providing visibility for analysis and troubleshooting, but they record traffic information rather than filter or block incoming requests."},{"id":"q238","question":"Using Amazon RDS falls under the shared responsibility model. Which of the following are customer responsibilities?","options":{"A":"Building the relational database schema","B":"Performing backups","C":"Managing the database settings","D":"Patching the database software","E":"Installing the database software"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Under the Shared Responsibility Model for Amazon RDS, the customer is responsible for designing the database schema and managing database-level settings such as parameters and user permissions.\\nPerforming backups is managed by AWS for RDS, which automates backup retention and point-in-time recovery without requiring the customer to manually initiate or manage backup processes. Patching the database software is entirely AWS\'s responsibility for RDS, as AWS handles all engine-level patching and maintenance windows as part of the managed service. Installing the database software is entirely AWS\'s responsibility for RDS, as AWS provisions, installs, and maintains the underlying database engine on the customer\'s behalf."},{"id":"q252","question":"An organization runs many systems and uses many AWS products. Which of the following services enables them to control how each developer interacts with these products?","options":{"A":"AWS Identity and Access Management","B":"Amazon RDS","C":"Network Access Control Lists","D":"Amazon EMR"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Identity and Access Management allows administrators to create users, groups, and roles with fine-grained permission policies that control exactly which AWS services and actions each developer or system can access across all AWS products.\\nAmazon RDS is a fully managed relational database service for running databases such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, and Oracle in the cloud, and is a data storage service rather than a tool for controlling developer access to AWS products. Network Access Control Lists are stateless subnet-level firewalls within a VPC that control inbound and outbound network traffic based on IP address and port rules, and are a network security mechanism rather than a service for managing developer permissions across AWS products. Amazon EMR is a managed cloud big data platform for running large-scale distributed data processing jobs using frameworks such as Apache Spark and Hadoop, and is a data processing service rather than an access management solution."},{"id":"q258","question":"What is the AWS service that performs automated network assessments of Amazon EC2 instances to check for vulnerabilities?","options":{"A":"Amazon Kinesis","B":"Security groups","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Network Access Control Lists"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Inspector automatically assesses EC2 instances for unintended network access and known software vulnerabilities by scanning network configurations and installed packages against vulnerability databases, generating prioritized findings.\\nAmazon Kinesis is a real-time data streaming service for ingesting, processing, and analyzing streaming data at scale, and has no capability to perform security assessments or network vulnerability scans of EC2 instances. Security groups act as stateful virtual firewalls that control which traffic is allowed to reach EC2 instances based on defined rules, and are a traffic filtering mechanism rather than a service that performs automated vulnerability assessments. AWS Network Access Control Lists are stateless subnet-level firewalls that allow or deny traffic based on IP address and port rules, and are a network traffic control mechanism rather than an assessment service that identifies vulnerabilities."},{"id":"q259","question":"Under the Shared Responsibility Model, which of the following controls do customers fully inherit from AWS?","options":{"A":"Patch management controls","B":"Database controls","C":"Awareness & Training","D":"Environmental controls","E":"Physical controls"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Physical controls such as data center physical security and hardware protection, and environmental controls such as power, cooling, and fire suppression, are entirely managed by AWS and fully inherited by customers who have no access to or responsibility for these areas.\\nPatch management is a shared control where AWS patches the underlying infrastructure and managed services, while customers retain responsibility for patching their own operating systems and applications. Database controls are a shared control where AWS manages the database engine in fully managed services, while customers remain responsible for access management, schema design, and data handling. Awareness and training is a shared control where AWS trains its own employees, while customers are independently responsible for training their own staff on security and compliance practices."},{"id":"q269","question":"Which of the following can help protect your EC2 instances from DDoS attacks?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudHSM","B":"Security Groups","C":"AWS Batch","D":"AWS IAM","E":"Network Access Control Lists (Network ACLs)"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Security groups are stateful instance-level firewalls that can block unwanted traffic before it reaches EC2 instances, and Network ACLs are stateless subnet-level controls that can block volumetric attack traffic at the network perimeter. Both can help protect EC2 instances from DDoS traffic.\\nAWS CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules for generating and managing cryptographic keys within the AWS Cloud, and is a key management service with no capability to block or mitigate DDoS traffic targeting EC2 instances. AWS Batch is a fully managed batch computing service that dynamically provisions compute resources for batch jobs, and is a job scheduling and compute service with no network traffic filtering or DDoS protection capability. AWS IAM manages user permissions and access to AWS services and resources, and is an identity and access management service rather than a network defense tool that can block DDoS traffic."},{"id":"q280","question":"Which of the following is used to control network traffic in AWS?","options":{"A":"Network Access Control Lists (NACLs)","B":"Key Pairs","C":"Access Keys","D":"IAM Policies","E":"Security Groups"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Network ACLs are stateless subnet-level firewalls that evaluate all inbound and outbound traffic based on numbered rules, and security groups are stateful instance-level firewalls that control traffic to and from EC2 instances. Both are used to control network traffic in AWS.\\nKey pairs are RSA public/private key combinations used to authenticate SSH connections to EC2 instances, and are an instance access credential rather than a network traffic control mechanism. Access keys consisting of an access key ID and secret access key are used to authenticate programmatic API calls to AWS services, and are an API authentication credential rather than a tool for controlling network traffic within a VPC. IAM policies are JSON documents that define permissions for users, groups, and roles to access AWS services and resources, and operate at the API authorisation layer rather than controlling IP-level network traffic flow."},{"id":"q292","question":"Data security is one of the top priorities of AWS. How does AWS deal with old storage devices that have reached the end of their useful life?","options":{"A":"AWS sells the old devices to other hosting providers","B":"AWS destroys the old devices in accordance with industry-standard practices","C":"AWS sends the old devices for remanufacturing","D":"AWS stores the old devices in a secure place"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS decommissions storage devices using industry-standard techniques such as DoD 5220.22-M or NIST 800-88 to ensure that data cannot be recovered from retired hardware, fulfilling their security of the cloud obligations under the shared responsibility model.\\nAWS does not sell old storage devices to other hosting providers, as doing so would create an unacceptable risk of data exposure; decommissioned devices are physically destroyed or securely wiped following strict protocols before disposal. AWS does not use third-party audits to verify data removal on old devices; rather, AWS performs the decommissioning itself using documented internal processes and the resulting policies and certifications are available to customers through AWS Artifact. AWS does not return old storage devices to the hardware vendors for resale, as the devices contain customer data that must be securely destroyed before any transfer outside AWS\'s direct control."},{"id":"q293","question":"A developer needs to set up an SSL security certificate for a client\'s eCommerce website in order to use the HTTPS protocol. Which of the following AWS services can be used to deploy the required SSL server certificates?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS Directory Service","D":"AWS Identity and Access Management","E":"AWS Config"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Certificate Manager is the primary service for provisioning, managing, and deploying SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services such as CloudFront and Elastic Load Balancing. AWS IAM also supports uploading and storing SSL server certificates that can be deployed to services not yet supported by ACM, making both valid options.\\nAmazon Route 53 is a DNS and domain registration service that manages how traffic is routed to resources, and has no capability to provision or deploy SSL certificates. AWS Directory Service is a managed Microsoft Active Directory service used for identity management, and is unrelated to SSL certificate provisioning. AWS Config is a compliance and configuration monitoring service that tracks resource configuration changes, and does not provision or deploy SSL certificates."},{"id":"q295","question":"A company is planning to migrate an application from Amazon EC2 to AWS Lambda to use a serverless architecture. Which of the following will be the responsibility of AWS after migration?","options":{"A":"Application management","B":"Capacity management","C":"Access control","D":"Operating system maintenance","E":"Data management"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"After migrating to AWS Lambda, AWS takes full responsibility for capacity management by automatically scaling functions based on demand, and for operating system maintenance as Lambda runs entirely on AWS-managed infrastructure with no customer access to the underlying OS.\\nApplication management remains the customer\'s responsibility after migration, as customers are always accountable for the code they write, deploy, and maintain within their Lambda functions. Access control remains the customer\'s responsibility, as customers must configure IAM roles, permissions, and resource policies to control who and what can invoke their Lambda functions. Data management remains the customer\'s responsibility, as customers control what data their functions process, where it is stored, and how it is handled throughout its lifecycle."},{"id":"q300","question":"Which AWS Service is used to manage user permissions?","options":{"A":"Security Groups","B":"Amazon ECS","C":"AWS IAM","D":"AWS Support"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Identity and Access Management allows administrators to create users, groups, roles, and policies that control who can access which AWS services and resources, making it the primary service for managing user permissions in AWS.\\nAWS Key Management Service creates and manages cryptographic keys for encrypting and decrypting data, and is a data protection service rather than an access permission management tool for controlling which users can access AWS resources. AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events for auditing and compliance purposes, providing visibility into who did what and when, but it records activity rather than managing or enforcing user permissions. Amazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics and events for AWS resources and applications, providing observability into performance and operational health, but has no capability to manage user access permissions to AWS services."},{"id":"q302","question":"A company needs to track resource changes using the API call history. Which AWS service can help the company achieve this goal?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS CloudFormation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail records every API call made in your AWS account, including the identity of the caller, timestamp, and parameters, making it the primary service for tracking resource changes via API history.\\nAWS Config tracks the configuration state of AWS resources over time and evaluates them against compliance rules, but it records configuration changes rather than the underlying API calls that caused them. Amazon CloudWatch is a monitoring and observability service that collects metrics, logs, and alarms from AWS resources, and does not record API call history or track who made changes to resources. AWS CloudFormation is an infrastructure-as-code service that provisions and manages AWS resources through templates, and does not record or track API call history across an account."},{"id":"q305","question":"What is the AWS\' recommendation regarding access keys?","options":{"A":"Delete all access keys and use passwords instead","B":"Only share them with trusted people","C":"Rotate them regularly","D":"Save them within your application code"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS recommends rotating access keys regularly to limit the window of exposure if a key is ever compromised, as shorter-lived credentials reduce the risk of unauthorised access going undetected.\\nDeleting all access keys and using passwords instead is not a valid approach, as passwords are used for console access while access keys are required for programmatic access through the CLI and API, and both have legitimate roles in a well-managed AWS account. Sharing access keys only with trusted people still violates the principle of least privilege, as access keys should be scoped to individual IAM users or roles rather than shared between people. Saving access keys within application code is a significant security risk as code repositories can be exposed publicly or internally, and AWS recommends using IAM roles for applications running on AWS resources instead of embedding credentials in code."},{"id":"q306","question":"What is the AWS IAM feature that provides an additional layer of security on top of username and password authentication?","options":{"A":"Key Pair","B":"Access Keys","C":"SDK","D":"MFA"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS IAM Multi-Factor Authentication adds a second verification layer requiring a one-time code from a hardware or virtual device on top of the username and password, providing an additional authentication factor that significantly increases account security.\\nEncryption is a data protection mechanism used to secure data at rest or in transit, and while it protects the confidentiality of stored information, it does not add an authentication layer on top of username and password for account login. AWS Access Advisor shows which AWS services IAM users and roles have accessed and when, helping administrators reduce permissions to only those actually used, but it is a permissions visibility tool rather than an authentication security mechanism. Least privilege access is a permissions management principle that involves granting users only the minimum permissions needed for their tasks, reducing the blast radius of compromised credentials, but it does not add an additional authentication factor on top of the login process."},{"id":"q312","question":"For managed services like Amazon DynamoDB, which of the below is AWS responsible for?","options":{"A":"Protecting credentials","B":"Logging access activity","C":"Patching the database software","D":"Operating system maintenance","E":"Creating access policies"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"For fully managed services like Amazon DynamoDB, AWS is responsible for patching the database software and maintaining the underlying operating system, as customers have no access to the infrastructure layer beneath the service.\\nProtecting credentials is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers manage their own IAM users, access keys, and authentication mechanisms. Logging access activity is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers must enable and configure AWS CloudTrail and DynamoDB Streams if they want to capture access logs. Creating access policies is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers define and manage the IAM policies that control who can access their DynamoDB tables and what actions they can perform."},{"id":"q316","question":"A company has discovered that multiple S3 buckets were deleted, but it is unclear who performed these actions. Which AWS service can be used to identify who deleted the buckets?","options":{"A":"SNS logs","B":"SQS logs","C":"CloudWatch Logs","D":"CloudTrail logs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events in an AWS account including S3 bucket deletion events, capturing who performed the action, the source IP address, and the timestamp, making it the correct service for identifying who deleted the buckets.\\nSNS logs is not a recognized AWS service or log type; Amazon SNS is a messaging service for sending notifications and does not produce logs of S3 API activity or deletion events. SQS logs is not a recognized AWS service or log type; Amazon SQS is a managed message queue service for decoupling application components and does not produce logs of S3 management activity. CloudWatch Logs collects and stores log data from AWS services and applications for monitoring and analysis, but S3 bucket deletion events are management events captured by CloudTrail rather than CloudWatch, making CloudWatch Logs the wrong place to identify who deleted S3 buckets."},{"id":"q319","question":"What are AWS shared controls?","options":{"A":"Controls that are solely the responsibility of the customer based on the application they are deploying within AWS services","B":"Controls that a customer inherits from AWS","C":"Controls that apply to both the infrastructure layer and customer layers","D":"Controls that the customer and AWS collaborate together upon to secure the infrastructure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Shared controls are responsibilities where both AWS and the customer independently apply controls in their own respective domains, such as AWS patching its infrastructure while the customer patches their guest operating system.\\nControls solely the responsibility of the customer describes customer-specific controls, not shared controls, as these apply only to the customer\'s side of the model. Controls that a customer inherits from AWS describes inherited controls, where compliance certifications and physical security measures are passed down from AWS to the customer, which is a separate category from shared controls. Controls that apply to both the infrastructure and customer layers is a misleading description because shared controls do not mean the same single control applies at both layers, but rather that each party manages their own version of the same type of control independently."},{"id":"q321","question":"Which of the below are responsibilities of the customer when using Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"Protecting sensitive data","B":"Patching of the underlying infrastructure","C":"Setup and operation of managed databases","D":"Maintaining consistent hardware components","E":"Installing and configuring third-party software"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Customers using Amazon EC2 are responsible for managing user access to their EC2 instances, including configuring operating system users and SSH keys, and for protecting data at rest and in transit on those instances through encryption and access controls.\\nPreventing physical access to EC2 hardware is AWS\'s responsibility as customers have no access to or visibility into the physical data center facilities where EC2 instances run. Patching the network infrastructure is AWS\'s responsibility as the underlying physical and virtual networking components that connect EC2 instances are managed and maintained entirely by AWS. Managing the hypervisor is AWS\'s responsibility as the virtualization layer that abstracts physical hardware into EC2 instances is part of the AWS infrastructure that customers cannot access or modify."},{"id":"q323","question":"Which of the following AWS services can help you perform security analysis and regulatory compliance auditing?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Batch","D":"Amazon ECS","E":"AWS Config"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Amazon Inspector automatically assesses applications for vulnerabilities and deviations from best practices, and AWS Config continuously monitors resource configurations and evaluates them against compliance rules. Both are purpose-built for security analysis and regulatory compliance auditing.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, but offers high-level checks and guidance rather than the deep automated security assessments and compliance rule evaluation that Inspector and Config provide. AWS Batch is a managed service for running batch computing workloads at scale, and is a job scheduling and compute service with no security analysis or compliance auditing capability. Amazon ECS is a container orchestration service that manages the deployment and scaling of containerised applications, and is a compute service with no security analysis or compliance auditing capability."},{"id":"q332","question":"Which of the following can be used to protect data at rest on Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"Versioning","B":"Deduplication","C":"Permissions","D":"Decryption","E":"Conversion"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 versioning retains multiple versions of objects, protecting against accidental deletion and allowing restoration of previous versions, and S3 permissions through IAM policies and bucket policies control who can access and modify objects. Both help protect data at rest on Amazon S3.\\nDeduplication is a storage optimization technique that removes duplicate copies of data to save space, and is not a data protection mechanism for Amazon S3; S3 does not expose deduplication as a security feature. Decryption is the process of converting encrypted data back to its original form, and while decryption is part of an encryption workflow, enabling decryption alone does not protect data at rest and is not a data protection mechanism for S3. Conversion refers to changing data formats or types, and has no relevance to protecting data stored in Amazon S3."},{"id":"q336","question":"When running a workload in AWS, the customer is NOT responsible for:","options":{"A":"Running penetration tests","B":"Reserving capacity","C":"Data center operations","D":"Auditing and regulatory compliance","E":"Infrastructure security"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Data center operations including physical facility management, power, cooling, and hardware decommissioning, and infrastructure security including the physical and virtual networking, hypervisor, and server hardware, are entirely AWS\'s responsibility and are not tasks the customer performs.\\nRunning penetration tests is the customer\'s own responsibility; AWS allows customers to conduct penetration testing on their own resources following the Acceptable Use Policy, but the decision to run tests and the tests themselves are not something AWS performs for customers. Reserving capacity, such as purchasing Reserved Instances or Savings Plans for predictable workloads, is a customer decision and action taken to optimize costs rather than an AWS-managed task. Auditing and regulatory compliance is a shared responsibility; AWS provides compliance reports and certifications through AWS Artifact, but customers are responsible for applying those to their own applications and ensuring their workloads meet regulatory requirements."},{"id":"q341","question":"Which AWS service or feature is used to manage the keys used to encrypt customer data?","options":{"A":"AWS KMS","B":"AWS Service Control Policies (SCPs)","C":"Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)","D":"Amazon Macie"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Key Management Service lets you create, manage, and control cryptographic keys used to encrypt your data across AWS services, providing centralized key management with audit logging via CloudTrail.\\nAWS Service Control Policies are organizational policies used within AWS Organizations to restrict the maximum permissions available to accounts in an organization, and are an access governance tool rather than a key management service for encryption keys. Multi-Factor Authentication adds a second verification factor to the login process requiring a code from a hardware or virtual device, and is an authentication security mechanism rather than a service for managing encryption keys. Amazon Macie uses machine learning to automatically discover and protect sensitive data in Amazon S3, and is a data classification and protection service rather than a key management service for creating and controlling encryption keys."},{"id":"q342","question":"Which AWS Service allows customers to download AWS SOC & PCI reports?","options":{"A":"AWS Well-Architected Tool","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Glue","D":"Amazon Chime"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Artifact is a self-service portal for on-demand access to AWS compliance reports including SOC and PCI reports, and agreement documents such as Business Associate Addenda, making it the correct service for downloading AWS SOC and PCI compliance reports.\\nAWS Well-Architected Tool is a service that helps you review the state of your workloads and compare them against AWS architectural best practices across the six pillars, and is a workload review and guidance tool rather than a compliance report repository. AWS Glue is a fully managed extract, transform, and load service for preparing and transforming data for analytics, and is a data integration service with no connection to compliance documentation. Amazon Chime is a unified communications service for online meetings, video conferencing, and chat, and has no relationship to compliance reports or AWS certification documents."},{"id":"q345","question":"What features does AWS offer to help protect your data in the Cloud?","options":{"A":"Access control","B":"Physical MFA devices","C":"Data encryption","D":"Unlimited storage","E":"Load balancing"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS offers access control through IAM policies, security groups, and network ACLs to restrict who can reach your data, and data encryption at rest and in transit to protect data even if it is intercepted or accessed without authorisation. Both are purpose-built data protection features.\\nPhysical MFA devices add a second layer of authentication to protect account and user access, but they secure who can log in rather than directly protecting the data itself once access is granted. Unlimited storage is a capacity characteristic of services such as Amazon S3, and is a storage scaling feature with no data protection or security capability. Load balancing distributes incoming traffic across multiple targets to improve availability and performance, and is a resilience and throughput feature with no role in protecting data from unauthorised access or exposure."},{"id":"q348","question":"Which methods can be used by customers to interact with AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)?","options":{"A":"AWS CLI","B":"AWS Security Groups","C":"AWS SDKs","D":"AWS Network Access Control Lists","E":"AWS CodeCommit"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"The AWS CLI provides command-line access to IAM for creating users, assigning permissions, and managing credentials, and AWS SDKs allow programmatic integration with IAM in application code to automate identity and access management operations. Both are valid methods for interacting with IAM.\\nAWS Security Groups are virtual firewalls that control inbound and outbound network traffic at the EC2 instance level, and are a network security component rather than a method for interacting with IAM. AWS Network Access Control Lists are stateless firewalls that control traffic at the subnet level within a VPC, and are a network traffic filtering mechanism rather than an interface for managing IAM identities or permissions. AWS CodeCommit is a fully managed source control service for hosting Git repositories, and is a code collaboration tool with no connection to interacting with IAM beyond using IAM for access control."},{"id":"q349","question":"Which of the following are types of AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) identities?","options":{"A":"AWS Resource Groups","B":"IAM Policies","C":"IAM Roles","D":"IAM Users","E":"AWS Organizations"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"IAM Users represent individual people or applications with specific credentials and permissions, and IAM Roles provide temporary security credentials that can be assumed by users, services, or AWS resources. Both are types of IAM identities.\\nAWS Resource Groups organize AWS resources by tags or CloudFormation stacks to make it easier to manage and automate operations across groups of resources, and are not IAM identities that can be used for authentication or permission assignment. IAM Policies are JSON documents that define permissions specifying allowed or denied actions on AWS resources, and are permission documents attached to identities rather than identity types themselves. AWS Organizations is a service for managing multiple AWS accounts under a central structure for consolidated billing and governance, and is an account management service rather than an IAM identity type."},{"id":"q351","question":"How does AWS notify customers about security and privacy events pertaining to AWS services?","options":{"A":"Using the AWS ACM service","B":"Using Security Bulletins","C":"Using the AWS Management Console","D":"Using Compliance Resources"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS publishes Security Bulletins to notify customers about security and privacy events affecting AWS services, providing details on vulnerabilities and recommended actions.\\nAWS Certificate Manager is a service that provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS resources, and is not a channel through which AWS communicates security or privacy event notifications to customers. The AWS Management Console is a web-based interface for accessing and managing AWS services, and while customers can view some account-level alerts there, it is not the mechanism AWS uses to publish security and privacy event notifications. AWS Compliance Resources are documents and reports such as audit certifications and compliance guides that help customers understand AWS\'s regulatory posture, and are not used to notify customers about specific security or privacy events."},{"id":"q352","question":"Which IAM entity can best be used to grant temporary access to your AWS resources?","options":{"A":"IAM Users","B":"Key Pair","C":"IAM Roles","D":"IAM Groups"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IAM Roles provide temporary security credentials that expire automatically, making them the best choice for granting short-term or temporary access to AWS resources without sharing long-term credentials.\\nIAM Users are identities with permanent, long-term credentials such as passwords and access keys, and while permissions can be granted or revoked, users themselves represent persistent identities rather than a mechanism designed specifically for temporary access. Key pairs are RSA public/private key combinations used to authenticate SSH connections to EC2 Linux instances, and are an instance access mechanism rather than an IAM feature designed for granting temporary access to AWS services and resources. IAM Groups are collections of IAM users that share the same attached policies, and are used to manage permissions for multiple users collectively rather than for granting temporary access to any single entity."},{"id":"q355","question":"A company is seeking to better secure its AWS account from unauthorized access. Which of the below options can the customer use to achieve this goal?","options":{"A":"Restrict any API call made through SDKs or CLI","B":"Create one IAM account for each department in the company (Development, QA, Production), and share it across all staff in that department","C":"Require Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) for all IAM User access","D":"Set up two login passwords"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Requiring MFA for all IAM users adds a second verification step beyond a password, significantly reducing the risk of unauthorised access even if a user\'s password is stolen or guessed, making it an effective control for securing the AWS account.\\nRestricting all API calls through SDKs or CLI would completely prevent programmatic and automated access to AWS services, making it operationally unworkable for development and automation workflows rather than a practical security measure. Creating a single IAM account per department shared across all staff removes individual accountability, makes it impossible to trace actions to specific users, and means a single compromised password grants access to everyone in the department, making this a security anti-pattern rather than a security improvement. Setting up two login passwords is not a feature AWS supports; AWS accounts use a single password for console access, and improved security beyond the password is achieved through MFA rather than multiple passwords."},{"id":"q361","question":"Which of the following are examples of the customer\'s responsibility to implement security IN the cloud?","options":{"A":"Building a schema for an application","B":"Replacing physical hardware","C":"Creating a new hypervisor","D":"Patch management of the underlying infrastructure","E":"File system encryption"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Building an application schema is an application development task that customers perform as part of managing their applications in the cloud, and encrypting file systems on EC2 instances to protect data at rest is a customer responsibility for security in the cloud. Both fall within the customer\'s side of the shared responsibility model.\\nReplacing physical hardware is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as customers have no access to or interaction with the physical servers, storage devices, and networking equipment in AWS data centers. Creating a new hypervisor is AWS\'s responsibility as the virtualization layer that abstracts physical hardware into virtual instances is part of the AWS-managed infrastructure that customers cannot access or modify. Patch management of the underlying infrastructure, including the host operating systems, hypervisor, and physical network equipment, is AWS\'s responsibility, whereas customers are responsible for patching the guest operating system on their own EC2 instances."},{"id":"q362","question":"Which of the following is a type of MFA device that customers can use to protect their AWS resources?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudHSM","B":"U2F Security Key","C":"AWS Access Keys","D":"AWS Key Pair"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A U2F Security Key is a physical hardware device that authenticates via USB or NFC using cryptographic protocols, providing a hardware-based second factor for AWS MFA that protects accounts from phishing and credential theft.\\nAWS CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules for generating and managing cryptographic keys for data encryption, and is a key management service rather than an MFA device used to authenticate AWS account logins. AWS Access Keys consisting of an access key ID and secret access key are credentials used for programmatic API and CLI access, and are a programmatic authentication mechanism rather than an MFA device that adds a second factor to console login. AWS Key Pairs are RSA public/private key combinations used to authenticate SSH connections to Linux EC2 instances, and are an instance-level access mechanism rather than an MFA device for AWS account authentication."},{"id":"q367","question":"You have just hired a skilled sys-admin to join your team. As usual, you have created a new IAM user for him to interact with AWS services. On his first day, you ask him to create snapshots of all existing Amazon EBS volumes and save them in a new Amazon S3 bucket. However, the new member reports back that he is unable to create neither EBS snapshots nor S3 buckets. What might prevent him from doing this simple task?","options":{"A":"EBS and S3 are accessible only to the root account owner","B":"The systems administrator must contact AWS Support first to activate his new IAM account","C":"There is not enough space in S3 to store the snapshots","D":"There is a non-explicit deny to all new users"},"correct":"D","explanation":"By default, new IAM users have no permissions attached to them, meaning all actions are implicitly denied until explicit permissions are granted through policies, which is why the new administrator cannot create EBS snapshots or S3 buckets.\\nEBS and S3 are not accessible only to the root account owner; they can be accessed by any IAM user, group, or role that has been granted the appropriate permissions through IAM policies, making this statement factually incorrect. A new IAM user does not need to contact AWS Support to activate their account; IAM users are created and active immediately, and the issue is the absence of permissions rather than an account activation requirement. There is no inherent storage limit in S3 that would prevent creating a new bucket for snapshots; S3 scales automatically to accommodate any amount of data, and the inability to create the bucket is due to missing permissions rather than a storage capacity issue."},{"id":"q368","question":"An external auditor is requesting a log of all accesses to the AWS resources in the company\'s account. Which of the following services will provide the auditor with the requested information?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"Amazon CloudFront","C":"AWS CloudFormation","D":"Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events in an AWS account including who accessed which resources, from what IP address, and at what time, providing exactly the information an auditor needs to review access logs.\\nAmazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and distributes web content to users at low latency through edge locations, and is a performance optimization service with no capability to log all accesses to AWS account resources. AWS CloudFormation is an infrastructure as code service for provisioning and managing AWS resources through templates, and is a provisioning and automation tool rather than a service that records access logs to AWS resources. Amazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics, logs, and events for AWS resources and applications, and while it can collect application logs and service metrics, it does not provide the comprehensive API call audit trail that records all AWS resource accesses the way CloudTrail does."},{"id":"q369","question":"Which AWS service allows a company to connect their AWS resources with an on-premises Microsoft Active Directory, enabling users to sign in with their existing corporate credentials?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM Identity Center","B":"AWS Directory Service","C":"Amazon Cognito","D":"AWS Secrets Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Directory Service provides managed Microsoft Active Directory in the AWS Cloud, allowing organizations to connect their AWS resources with an existing on-premises Active Directory so users can access AWS applications using their established corporate credentials.\\nAWS IAM Identity Center provides single sign-on access to multiple AWS accounts and business applications, but does not provide the managed directory infrastructure needed to extend on-premises Active Directory into AWS. Amazon Cognito provides user identity and authentication for customer-facing web and mobile applications, and is not designed for corporate directory integration with AWS resources. AWS Secrets Manager stores, rotates, and retrieves credentials and secrets programmatically, but does not manage directory services or user authentication against Active Directory."},{"id":"q375","question":"Which AWS service enables you to quickly purchase and deploy SSL/TLS certificates?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"AWS ACM","C":"Amazon Detective","D":"AWS WAF"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Certificate Manager lets you provision, manage, and deploy SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services such as Elastic Load Balancers and CloudFront distributions, offering free public certificates that renew automatically.\\nAmazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors AWS accounts and workloads for malicious activity using machine learning and threat intelligence, and has no capability to provision or manage SSL/TLS certificates. Amazon Detective helps analyze and visualise security data to investigate the root cause of suspicious activity, providing forensic investigation capabilities rather than SSL/TLS certificate provisioning. AWS WAF is a web application firewall that filters HTTP and HTTPS traffic based on defined rules to block common web exploits, and is a traffic filtering service rather than a certificate provisioning service."},{"id":"q380","question":"A company wants to grant a new employee long-term access to manage Amazon DynamoDB databases. Which of the following is a recommended best-practice when granting these permissions?","options":{"A":"Create an IAM role and attach a policy with Amazon DynamoDB access permissions","B":"Create an IAM role and attach a policy with Administrator access permissions","C":"Create an IAM user and attach a policy with Amazon DynamoDB access permissions","D":"Create an IAM user and attach a policy with Administrator access permissions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For a specific employee requiring long-term access, an IAM user is the appropriate identity type, and attaching a policy scoped only to Amazon DynamoDB follows the principle of least privilege by granting only the permissions needed for that role.\\nCreating an IAM role and attaching DynamoDB access permissions would be appropriate for applications or services that need temporary access to DynamoDB, but for a specific long-term employee, an IAM user is the correct identity type rather than a role designed for temporary credential assumption. Creating an IAM role with Administrator access permissions would grant the new employee unrestricted access to all AWS services and resources, violating the principle of least privilege and posing an unnecessary security risk. Creating an IAM user with Administrator access permissions would grant unrestricted access to all AWS services rather than scoping permissions to only Amazon DynamoDB, directly violating the principle of least privilege."},{"id":"q381","question":"When granting permissions to applications running on Amazon EC2 instances, which of the following is considered best practice?","options":{"A":"Generate new IAM access keys every time you delegate permissions","B":"Store the required AWS credentials directly within the application code","C":"Use temporary security credentials (IAM roles) instead of long-term access keys","D":"Do nothing; Applications that run on Amazon EC2 instances do not need permission to interact with other AWS services or resources"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IAM Roles provide temporary security credentials that are automatically rotated when assumed, eliminating the need to embed long-term access keys in application code and reducing the risk of credential exposure if the application is compromised.\\nGenerating new IAM access keys every time permissions are delegated would result in a large number of static, long-term credentials that must be tracked and managed, increasing the risk of credential exposure rather than reducing it, and is not a security best practice for applications on EC2. Storing required AWS credentials directly in application code is a serious security anti-pattern that risks credential exposure through code repositories, log files, or application errors, making it the opposite of a best practice. Applications running on EC2 frequently need to interact with other AWS services such as S3, DynamoDB, or SQS to function correctly, making the assumption that no permissions are needed factually incorrect for most real-world applications."},{"id":"q384","question":"Which of the following can be used to enable the Virtual Multi-Factor Authentication?","options":{"A":"Amazon Connect","B":"AWS CLI","C":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","D":"Amazon SNS","E":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Virtual MFA can be enabled through AWS IAM, which provides the console interface for assigning and configuring MFA devices for users, and through the AWS CLI, which allows MFA settings to be managed programmatically.\\nAmazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service for handling customer communications, entirely unrelated to authentication or MFA configuration. Amazon SNS is a notification service for sending messages to subscribers and has no role in enabling or managing MFA. Amazon VPC is a virtual private network service for isolating and configuring cloud network infrastructure, unrelated to user authentication settings."},{"id":"q387","question":"What should you do if you see resources, which you don\'t remember creating, in the AWS Management Console?","options":{"A":"Stop all running services and open an investigation","B":"Give your root account password to AWS Support so that they can assist in troubleshooting and securing the account","C":"Check the AWS CloudTrail logs and delete all IAM users that have access to your resources","D":"Open an investigation and delete any potentially compromised IAM users","E":"Change your AWS root account password and the passwords of any IAM users"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"If unrecognized resources appear in your AWS account, this may indicate a security compromise where an attacker has gained access and is creating resources, so you should change your root account password and access keys immediately to revoke the attacker\'s access and protect the account.\\nClosing the account immediately would permanently terminate all resources and data without first investigating the extent of the compromise or recovering any workloads, making it a disproportionate response before proper incident response steps have been completed. Removing all IAM users from the account would lock out your legitimate team members and prevent any investigation or remediation work from being performed, making incident response significantly harder without actually addressing the underlying security compromise. Opening a new root account does not address the compromised account, as the original account with its resources and data would still be exposed; incident response requires securing the existing account rather than abandoning it."},{"id":"q395","question":"A company is developing a mobile application and wants to allow users to use their Amazon, Apple, Facebook, or Google identities to authenticate to the application. Which AWS service should the company use for this purpose?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"AWS Directory Service","C":"Amazon Cognito","D":"AWS IAM"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Cognito supports federated identity authentication, allowing users to sign in with their existing Amazon, Apple, Facebook, or Google accounts and exchange those tokens for AWS credentials, making it the correct service for social identity provider integration.\\nAmazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors AWS accounts and workloads for malicious activity and anomalous behavior, and is not a service for enabling user authentication with social identity providers. AWS Directory Service provides managed Microsoft Active Directory for enterprise identity management and SSO with corporate credentials, and is designed for workforce identity rather than customer-facing social identity provider authentication. AWS IAM manages user identities and permissions for accessing AWS services and resources, and while IAM can grant permissions to users authenticated through Cognito, it does not itself handle federated sign-in with Amazon, Apple, Facebook, or Google."},{"id":"q403","question":"There is a requirement to grant a DevOps team full administrative access to all resources in an AWS account. Who can grant them these permissions?","options":{"A":"AWS account owner","B":"AWS technical account manager","C":"AWS security team","D":"AWS cloud support engineers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The AWS account owner holds the root user credentials and has full administrative access to all resources in the account, making them the only entity with the authority to grant full administrative permissions to other users or teams through IAM policies.\\nAn AWS Technical Account Manager is an AWS employee assigned to help Enterprise support plan customers with architectural guidance and support, and does not have the ability to grant permissions within a customer\'s AWS account. The AWS Security team manages AWS\'s own internal security posture and is not involved in configuring IAM permissions within individual customer accounts. AWS Cloud Support Engineers provide technical support for resolving issues and answering questions about AWS services, and do not have access to configure IAM policies or grant permissions within customer accounts."},{"id":"q410","question":"Which statement is true in relation to the security of Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"You should use instance store volumes to store login data","B":"You should regularly patch the operating system and applications on your EC2 instances","C":"You should deploy critical components of your application in the Availability Zone that you trust","D":"You can track all API calls using Amazon Athena"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS shared responsibility means the customer is responsible for securing what runs on EC2, including the operating system and applications, which requires regularly applying security patches to close vulnerabilities that could be exploited by attackers.\\nInstance store volumes provide temporary, high-speed local storage that is deleted when the instance stops or is terminated, making them inappropriate for storing login data that must persist across instance restarts or replacements. Deploying critical application components in a single trusted Availability Zone reduces fault tolerance and does not improve security; best practice is to distribute across multiple Availability Zones for resilience, and no AZ is inherently more trusted than another within the same region. Amazon Athena is a serverless query service for analyzing data stored in S3 using SQL, and does not track API calls; AWS CloudTrail is the correct service for recording API activity across an AWS account."},{"id":"q414","question":"Which of the following strategies helps protect your AWS root account?","options":{"A":"Delete root user access keys if you do not need them","B":"Apply MFA for the root account and use it for all of your work","C":"Access the root account only from your personal Mobile Phone","D":"Only share your AWS account password or access keys with trusted persons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Deleting root user access keys removes a persistent attack vector, since the root account has unrestricted access to all AWS services and resources, and AWS best practice recommends using IAM users for daily tasks and eliminating root access keys entirely to protect the account.\\nApplying MFA for the root account is correct but the second part of the option is wrong; AWS recommends enabling MFA on the root account but using it as infrequently as possible and relying on IAM users for day-to-day work, not using the root account for all tasks. Accessing the root account only from a personal mobile phone does not constitute a recognized security control; MFA using an authenticator app on a mobile device is recommended, but restricting access to a specific physical device is not an AWS security best practice and introduces its own risks. Sharing your AWS account password or access keys with trusted persons violates fundamental security principles; credentials should never be shared as this removes individual accountability and increases the risk of credential exposure."},{"id":"q416","question":"You have just set up your AWS environment and have created six IAM user accounts for the DevOps team. What is the AWS recommendation when granting permissions to these IAM accounts?","options":{"A":"Attach a separate IAM policy for each individual account","B":"Apply the Principle of Least Privilege","C":"For security purposes, you should not grant any permission to the DevOps team","D":"Create six different IAM passwords"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Principle of Least Privilege means granting each IAM user only the minimum permissions necessary to perform their assigned job functions, reducing the impact of any accidental or malicious use of credentials.\\nAttaching a separate IAM policy to each individual account is a valid approach for highly differentiated permissions but is operationally complex; using IAM groups to assign shared policies to all DevOps team members with similar roles is the recommended approach for teams rather than managing policies per user individually. For security purposes, you should not withhold all permissions from the DevOps team; the team needs appropriate permissions to perform their infrastructure management tasks, and the goal is to define those permissions carefully rather than provide none at all. Creating six different IAM passwords involves setting distinct password requirements per user, but password variation does not address the core security concern of permission scope; applying least privilege principles to what each user is allowed to do is the AWS recommendation."},{"id":"q424","question":"Which of the following services provide real-time auditing for compliance and vulnerabilities?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon MQ","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor","E":"Amazon Cognito"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Config continuously monitors and records resource configurations to evaluate compliance against desired rules, and AWS Trusted Advisor provides real-time checks across security, performance, cost, and fault tolerance categories. Both provide real-time auditing capabilities.\\nAmazon Redshift is a fully managed cloud data warehouse service for running analytical queries on large datasets, and is a data analytics service rather than a real-time auditing tool for compliance or vulnerability assessment. Amazon MQ is a managed message broker service for Apache ActiveMQ and RabbitMQ that enables application messaging and decoupling, and is a messaging infrastructure service with no auditing or compliance checking capability. Amazon Cognito is a user identity and authentication service for web and mobile applications that handles sign-up, sign-in, and access control, and is an identity service rather than a compliance auditing or vulnerability assessment tool."},{"id":"q428","question":"What best describes penetration testing?","options":{"A":"Testing your application\'s response time from different locations","B":"Testing your network to find security vulnerabilities that an attacker could exploit","C":"Testing your instances to check for the unhealthy ones","D":"Testing your software for bugs and errors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Penetration testing is the practice of simulating cyberattacks against your own systems to discover exploitable security vulnerabilities before malicious attackers can find and use them, allowing you to remediate weaknesses proactively.\\nTesting application response time from different locations describes performance and latency testing, which evaluates how quickly an application responds to requests from various geographic locations rather than identifying security vulnerabilities that attackers could exploit. Testing instances to check for unhealthy ones describes health checking or monitoring, which verifies that compute instances are operational and responsive rather than assessing the security posture of the network for exploitable weaknesses. Testing software for bugs and errors describes functional or quality assurance testing, which validates that code behaves as expected rather than specifically probing for security vulnerabilities that an attacker could use to gain unauthorised access."},{"id":"q443","question":"Which of the following can help secure your sensitive data in Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"Delete the encryption keys once your data is encrypted","B":"With AWS you do not need to worry about encryption","C":"Enable S3 Encryption","D":"Encrypt the data prior to uploading it","E":"Delete all IAM users that have access to S3"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Enabling S3 encryption through server-side encryption with S3-managed keys, KMS keys, or customer-provided keys protects object confidentiality at rest, and encrypting data before uploading it provides client-side encryption that ensures data is protected before it ever leaves the client. Both are valid approaches to securing sensitive data in Amazon S3.\\nDeleting encryption keys once data is encrypted would permanently prevent decryption of the stored data, making it impossible to read or use the data again; encryption keys must be retained and managed carefully to ensure continued access to encrypted data. AWS does not automatically handle all security concerns by default; customers are responsible for configuring appropriate encryption, access controls, and bucket policies to protect their data, as the shared responsibility model requires active customer participation in data security. Deleting all IAM users with access to S3 would prevent all legitimate users from accessing the data and disrupt normal operations; proper access control involves granting least privilege permissions to the appropriate users rather than eliminating all access."},{"id":"q450","question":"Which of the following services gives you access to all AWS auditor-issued reports and certifications?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact provides on-demand, self-service access to AWS compliance reports and auditor-issued certifications such as SOC, PCI DSS, and ISO documents, directly from the AWS Management Console without needing to contact AWS support.\\nAWS Config continuously monitors and records resource configurations against compliance rules to help manage configuration drift and evaluate resource compliance, and is a resource configuration compliance tool rather than a repository of AWS auditor-issued certifications. Amazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics, logs, and events for AWS resources and applications to support observability and alerting, and has no connection to providing access to AWS compliance reports or third-party audit certifications. AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events across an AWS account for auditing and security analysis, and produces customer-specific activity logs rather than auditor-issued AWS compliance certifications."},{"id":"q455","question":"For compliance and regulatory purposes, a government agency requires that their applications must run on hardware that is dedicated to them only. How can you meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Use EC2 Dedicated Hosts","B":"Use EC2 Reserved Instances","C":"Use EC2 Spot Instances","D":"Use EC2 On-demand Instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Hosts provide physical servers fully dedicated to your use, giving you visibility and control over instance placement on hardware that is not shared with other AWS customers, satisfying strict compliance and regulatory requirements for physical isolation.\\nEC2 Reserved Instances are a pricing model that provides a significant discount in exchange for a commitment to use a specific instance type for one or three years, and they do not guarantee that the underlying hardware is dedicated to a single customer; reserved instances can run on shared multi-tenant hardware. EC2 Spot Instances allow you to use spare AWS capacity at a reduced price, with the caveat that they can be interrupted when AWS needs the capacity back, and they do not provide dedicated physical hardware or compliance guarantees about hardware isolation. EC2 On-Demand Instances allow you to pay for compute capacity by the hour or second without any upfront commitments, and they run on shared multi-tenant hardware rather than dedicated servers, making them unsuitable for compliance requirements mandating physical hardware dedication."},{"id":"q458","question":"What can you use to assign permissions directly to an IAM user?","options":{"A":"IAM Identity","B":"IAM Group","C":"IAM Role","D":"IAM Policy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An IAM Policy is a JSON document that defines permissions by specifying allowed or denied actions on AWS resources, and can be attached directly to an IAM user to grant specific access rights.\\nIAM Identity is not a standalone IAM construct used to assign permissions; AWS IAM Identity Center is a separate service for managing workforce access across multiple accounts, and does not refer to a permission assignment mechanism within IAM itself. An IAM Group is a collection of IAM users that share the same attached policies, and while attaching a policy to a group affects all its members, the group itself is not what assigns permissions directly to an individual user. An IAM Role is an identity that defines a set of permissions and is assumed temporarily by users, services, or applications, and is not attached directly to a user the way a policy is."},{"id":"q463","question":"Which of the following would you use to manage your encryption keys in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS KMS","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS CodeDeploy","D":"AWS CodeCommit","E":"CloudHSM"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS KMS provides centralized software-based key management with AWS-managed infrastructure and integrates natively with most AWS services for encryption at rest, and AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules for customers requiring hardware-based key control for regulatory compliance. Both are valid services for managing encryption keys in the AWS Cloud.\\nAWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for encrypting data in transit between applications and users, and is a certificate provisioning service rather than a key management service for creating and storing encryption keys. AWS CodeDeploy is an automated deployment service that delivers application code to EC2 instances, Lambda functions, and on-premises servers, and is a deployment automation tool with no encryption key management capability. AWS CodeCommit is a fully managed source control service for hosting Git repositories for version-controlled code storage, and is a code collaboration tool rather than an encryption key management service."},{"id":"q466","question":"A company has infrastructure hosted in an on-premises data center. They currently have an operations team that takes care of identity management. If they decide to migrate to the AWS cloud, which of the following services would help them perform the same role in AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM","B":"AWS Outposts","C":"AWS Directory Service","D":"Amazon Redshift"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Identity and Access Management is the service that provides centralized control over authentication and authorisation for AWS resources, fulfilling the same identity management role in the cloud that an on-premises operations team performs in a traditional data center.\\nAWS Outposts extends AWS infrastructure and services to on-premises locations and is a compute and storage solution rather than an identity management service. AWS Directory Service provides managed Microsoft Active Directory integration for enterprise identity, which can complement IAM but is a specialized service for organizations needing AD integration rather than the core AWS identity management service. Amazon Redshift is a fully managed cloud data warehousing service designed for analytical queries on large datasets and has no capability to manage user identities or control access to AWS services."},{"id":"q468","question":"Where can AWS account owners get a list of all users in their account, including the status of their AWS credentials?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"IAM Credential Report","C":"AWS Artifact reports","D":"AWS Cost and Usage Report"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The IAM credential report provides a comprehensive list of all IAM users in an AWS account along with the status of their credentials including passwords, access keys, MFA status, and last used dates, making it the correct tool for auditing user credentials.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor provides automated checks across security, performance, cost, and fault tolerance categories and includes some IAM-related security checks, but does not generate a downloadable report listing all users and their individual credential status details. Amazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics and logs for AWS resources and applications, and has no capability to list IAM users or report on the status of their credentials such as access keys and MFA devices. AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events for auditing purposes, and while it logs IAM-related actions, it does not provide a consolidated report of all current users and the live status of their credentials."},{"id":"q469","question":"Which of the following services enables you to easily generate and use your own encryption keys in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS CloudHSM","D":"AWS WAF"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated, single-tenant hardware security modules in the AWS Cloud, giving you full control over encryption key generation and management with FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated hardware for customers requiring hardware-based key control.\\nAWS Shield provides managed DDoS protection at the network and transport layers, and is a traffic protection service with no capability to generate or manage encryption keys. AWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services to encrypt data in transit, and is a certificate management service rather than a general-purpose encryption key generation service. AWS WAF is a web application firewall that filters HTTP and HTTPS traffic based on defined rules to block web exploits, and is a traffic filtering service with no capability to generate or manage customer encryption keys."},{"id":"q473","question":"Which of the following resources can an AWS customer use to learn more about prohibited uses of the services offered by AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Service Control Policies (SCPs)","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Acceptable Use Policy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The AWS Acceptable Use Policy outlines prohibited uses of AWS services, including activities such as sending spam, hosting illegal content, or launching attacks, and applies to all AWS customers and users.\\nAWS Service Control Policies are permission guardrails applied within AWS Organizations to restrict what actions accounts can perform, not a document describing prohibited uses of AWS services to external customers. AWS Artifact is a self-service portal for accessing AWS compliance reports and security documentation such as SOC reports and ISO certifications, not a resource for understanding usage restrictions. AWS Budgets is a cost management tool for setting spending thresholds and receiving alerts, unrelated to acceptable use or prohibited activities."},{"id":"q474","question":"Which of the following security resources are available to any user for free?","options":{"A":"AWS Bulletins","B":"AWS Technical Account Manager","C":"AWS Support API","D":"AWS Security Blog","E":"AWS Classroom Training"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS documentation provides comprehensive technical guides and API references for all AWS services at no cost and is freely accessible to any user, and AWS discussion forums allow any user to post questions and share knowledge about AWS services at no charge. Both are free security resources.\\nAWS Support Center provides technical case support that requires a paid support plan to submit cases, and while some basic content is available for free, the case submission feature for getting direct engineering assistance is not a free resource available to all users without a qualifying support plan. AWS Managed Services provides ongoing management of AWS infrastructure on behalf of enterprise customers for a fee, and is a paid service rather than a free security resource available to any user. Amazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service that requires configuration and generates findings, and while there is a free trial period, it is a paid service rather than a free resource available to all users without any cost."},{"id":"q476","question":"Which of the following is the responsibility of AWS according to the AWS Shared Responsibility Model?","options":{"A":"Securing regions and edge locations","B":"Performing auditing tasks","C":"Monitoring AWS resources usage","D":"Securing access to AWS resources"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Under the Shared Responsibility Model, AWS is responsible for securing the physical infrastructure, including Regions, Availability Zones, and edge locations that make up the AWS global network.\\nPerforming auditing tasks is the customer\'s responsibility. Customers are responsible for auditing their own resource usage, access logs, and compliance activities. Monitoring AWS resources usage is the customer\'s responsibility. Tools like CloudWatch are available but it is the customer who configures and acts on monitoring. Securing access to AWS resources is the customer\'s responsibility. Managing IAM users, roles, and permissions falls under security in the cloud, not security of the cloud."},{"id":"q479","question":"Which of the following services enables you to generate and use your own encryption keys on dedicated hardware in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS CloudHSM","D":"AWS KMS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated, single-tenant hardware security modules within the AWS Cloud, giving customers full control over key generation and management on physical HSM hardware that only they can access, satisfying strict regulatory requirements for hardware-based key custody.\\nAWS Secrets Manager stores, rotates, and manages access to secrets such as database credentials, API keys, and OAuth tokens, and is a secrets management service rather than a service for generating customer-controlled encryption keys on dedicated hardware. AWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services to encrypt data in transit, and is a certificate management service that does not provide customer-controlled encryption keys on dedicated hardware. AWS KMS is a managed service that generates and manages encryption keys using a software-based service backed by hardware that AWS manages and shares across multiple customers, and does not provide the dedicated single-tenant hardware access that CloudHSM offers."},{"id":"q480","question":"Who is responsible for scaling a DynamoDB database in the AWS Shared Responsibility Model?","options":{"A":"Your security team","B":"Your development team","C":"AWS","D":"Your internal DevOps team"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database service where AWS handles all scaling automatically, including read and write capacity adjustments, so customers do not need to manage or plan for database scaling themselves.\\nThe customer\'s security team focuses on security policies, access controls, and compliance rather than the operational scaling of a managed database service, and DynamoDB\'s autoscaling is handled by AWS infrastructure rather than any customer team. The development team builds and maintains application code, and while they configure DynamoDB table settings during development, the actual scaling of a fully managed service is performed automatically by AWS rather than manually by developers. The internal DevOps team manages infrastructure, deployments, and operational workflows, but the scaling of a fully managed service like DynamoDB is handled automatically by AWS and does not require DevOps intervention."},{"id":"q487","question":"Which feature enables users to sign into their AWS accounts with their existing corporate credentials?","options":{"A":"Federation","B":"Access keys","C":"IAM Permissions","D":"AWS WAF rules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Identity federation allows users to sign into AWS using credentials from an external identity provider such as Active Directory or SAML, eliminating the need to create separate IAM users for each corporate employee.\\nAccess keys are long-term credentials consisting of an access key ID and secret used for programmatic access to AWS via the CLI or API, and are not a mechanism for signing into AWS with corporate credentials. IAM Permissions define what actions an authenticated user or role is allowed to perform on AWS resources, and are an authorisation mechanism rather than a feature that enables corporate credential sign-in. AWS WAF rules define conditions for filtering web traffic to protect applications from common exploits, and are a web security feature with no connection to identity or authentication."},{"id":"q488","question":"According to the AWS shared responsibility model, what are the controls that customers fully inherit from AWS?","options":{"A":"Awareness and Training","B":"Communications controls","C":"Data center security controls","D":"Environmental controls","E":"Resource Configuration Management"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Data center security controls and environmental controls are fully inherited from AWS because customers have no physical access to AWS facilities and take on zero responsibility for managing them.\\nAwareness and Training this is a shared control where both AWS and the customer independently maintain their own training programs for their respective workforces, so customers do not fully inherit it from AWS. Communications controls this is also a shared control, with each party responsible for securing and managing communications within their own environment. Resource Configuration Management customers are responsible for configuring the AWS resources they deploy, such as security groups, IAM policies, and application settings, making this a customer-managed control rather than an inherited one."},{"id":"q491","question":"Which of the following are part of the seven design principles for security in the cloud?","options":{"A":"Use manual monitoring techniques to protect your AWS resources","B":"Use IAM roles to grant temporary access instead of long-term credentials","C":"Scale horizontally to protect from failures","D":"Enable real-time traceability","E":"Never store sensitive data in the cloud"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Implementing strong identity foundations through least privilege and appropriate credential management, and enabling traceability by monitoring, alerting, and auditing all actions and resource changes, are two of the seven security design principles of the Well-Architected Framework.\\nUsing the same AWS account for all workloads in a single environment contradicts the security principle of applying security at all layers and using multiple accounts to isolate workloads, and is an anti-pattern rather than a security design principle. Replacing IAM users with API keys as the primary access mechanism would use long-term credentials for all access rather than temporary credentials through roles, which is the opposite of the principle favoring short-term credentials and role-based access. Granting root user access to all authorised users contradicts the principle of granting least privilege and protecting root account credentials, as the root user should be used minimally and its credentials never shared."},{"id":"q493","question":"What is the main benefit of attaching security groups to an Amazon RDS instance?","options":{"A":"Manages user access and encryption keys","B":"Controls what IP address ranges can connect to your database instance","C":"Deploys SSL/TLS certificates for use with your database instance","D":"Distributes incoming traffic across multiple targets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Security groups attached to an RDS instance act as a virtual firewall controlling which IP address ranges and EC2 instances are allowed to establish connections to the database, restricting network access to only authorised sources.\\nManaging user access and encryption keys describes AWS IAM and AWS KMS respectively, which are separate services for identity management and key management rather than functions provided by security groups attached to an RDS instance. Deploying SSL/TLS certificates for database connections is performed by AWS Certificate Manager and the RDS parameter group configuration, not by security groups, which control network connectivity rather than certificate provisioning. Distributing incoming traffic across multiple targets describes an Elastic Load Balancer, which routes requests to multiple backend instances, and is not a function of security groups attached to a database instance."},{"id":"q496","question":"You have been tasked with auditing the security of your VPC. As part of this process, you need to start by analyzing what inbound and outbound traffic is allowed on your EC2 instances. What two parts of the VPC do you need to check to accomplish this task?","options":{"A":"Network ACLs and Traffic Manager","B":"Network ACLs and Subnets","C":"Security Groups and Internet Gateways","D":"Security Groups and Network ACLs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Security groups control inbound and outbound traffic at the EC2 instance level with stateful evaluation, and Network ACLs control traffic at the subnet level with stateless rule evaluation. Checking both gives a complete picture of what traffic is allowed to and from EC2 instances in the VPC.\\nNetwork ACLs and Traffic Manager is not a valid combination for VPC traffic analysis; AWS Traffic Manager does not exist as an AWS service name, and the correct subnet-level control is Network ACLs rather than a non-existent Traffic Manager. Network ACLs and Subnets is partially correct in that subnets are VPC components, but subnets themselves are network segments rather than traffic control mechanisms, and checking subnets does not tell you what traffic rules are applied; you need to check the ACLs attached to those subnets. Security groups and Internet Gateways is partially correct in that Internet Gateways allow internet traffic into the VPC, but they do not define granular allow/deny rules for instance-level traffic; checking security groups alone misses the subnet-level controls provided by Network ACLs."},{"id":"q499","question":"Which statement is true in relation to security in AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS manages everything related to EC2 operating systems","B":"AWS customers are responsible for patching any database software running on Amazon EC2","C":"Server side encryption is the responsibility of AWS","D":"AWS is responsible for the security of your application"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Under the shared responsibility model, customers are responsible for patching and maintaining any software they install on EC2 instances, including database software, operating systems, and applications.\\nAWS does not manage everything related to EC2 operating systems. The guest operating system, including configuration, patching, and security, is the customer\'s responsibility, while AWS manages only the underlying physical infrastructure. Server-side encryption is not automatically the responsibility of AWS. Customers choose whether to enable encryption and can manage their own keys through AWS KMS, making encryption a shared responsibility depending on how it is configured. AWS is not responsible for the security of customer applications. Application-level security, including code quality, access controls, and data handling, is entirely the customer\'s responsibility under the shared responsibility model."},{"id":"q507","question":"What are the main differences between an IAM user and an IAM role in AWS?","options":{"A":"An IAM user is uniquely associated with only one person, however a role is intended to be assumable by anyone who needs it","B":"An IAM user has permanent credentials associated with it, however a role has temporary credentials associated with it","C":"IAM users are more cost effective than IAM roles","D":"A role is uniquely associated with only one person, however an IAM user is intended to be assumable by anyone who needs it","E":"An IAM user has temporary credentials associated with it, however a role has permanent credentials associated with it"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"An IAM user is a permanent identity associated with one specific person and has long-term credentials such as a password and access keys, while an IAM role provides temporary credentials and can be assumed by multiple users, services, or applications that need it.\\nThe option stating that a role is uniquely associated with only one person and a user is assumable by anyone reverses the correct relationship; IAM users represent individual people with their own credentials, while roles are designed to be assumed by anyone or any service that requires the permissions they define. The option stating that IAM users have temporary credentials and roles have permanent credentials is the opposite of the correct relationship; IAM users have long-term permanent credentials while roles issue temporary credentials that expire automatically when the session ends. IAM users and roles are not priced differently from each other; there is no direct cost associated with creating IAM users or roles, and the claim that users are more cost effective is not a meaningful comparison."},{"id":"q509","question":"What does Amazon GuardDuty do to protect AWS accounts and workloads?","options":{"A":"Notifies AWS customers about abuse events once they are reported","B":"Continuously monitors AWS infrastructure and helps detect threats such as attacker reconnaissance or account compromise","C":"Helps AWS customers identify the root cause of potential security issues","D":"Checks security groups for rules that allow unrestricted access to AWS resources"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors AWS accounts and workloads using machine learning and threat intelligence to identify suspicious activity such as attacker reconnaissance, compromised instances, or unusual API calls.\\nNotifying customers about abuse events once they are reported is the function of the AWS Abuse Team, which handles reports of AWS resources being used for malicious activity and communicates with affected account holders, rather than a capability of GuardDuty. Helping customers identify the root cause of potential security issues describes Amazon Detective, which analyzes and visualises security data from GuardDuty and CloudTrail to trace the origin and scope of suspicious activity rather than detecting threats in real time. Checking security groups for rules that allow unrestricted access to AWS resources is a function of AWS Trusted Advisor, which performs security checks including identification of overly permissive security group configurations, not GuardDuty."},{"id":"q516","question":"Which of the following requires an access key ID and a secret access key to get long-lived programmatic access to AWS resources?","options":{"A":"IAM group","B":"IAM user","C":"IAM role","D":"AWS account root user","E":"AWS Technical Account Manager"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"IAM users and the AWS account root user are both identities that can be issued long-lived access key IDs and secret access keys for programmatic access to AWS resources via the CLI or SDKs.\\nAn IAM group is a collection of IAM users used to manage shared permissions collectively, and is not an identity that can authenticate or be issued access keys of any kind. An IAM role uses temporary security credentials that are automatically issued and rotated when the role is assumed, and does not support long-lived access keys. An AWS Technical Account Manager is an AWS support professional assigned to Enterprise support plan customers, and is a person rather than an IAM identity construct that can hold or be issued access keys."},{"id":"q523","question":"Which of the following AWS services integrates with AWS Shield and AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF) to protect against network and application layer DDoS attacks?","options":{"A":"Amazon EFS","B":"AWS Secrets Manager","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront integrates natively with AWS Shield for DDoS protection at the network layer and with AWS WAF for application-layer traffic filtering, providing a layered defense against both network and application layer DDoS attacks at the edge.\\nAmazon EFS is a fully managed elastic file system for providing shared file storage to EC2 instances and other compute services, and is a storage service with no capability to integrate with Shield or WAF for DDoS protection. AWS Secrets Manager stores, rotates, and manages access to secrets such as database credentials and API keys, and is a credentials management service rather than a service that integrates with Shield and WAF for traffic protection. AWS Systems Manager provides operational tools for managing AWS resources at scale including patching, configuration management, and session management, and is an operations service rather than a content delivery or traffic filtering service."},{"id":"q524","question":"Which of the following services is used when encrypting EBS volumes?","options":{"A":"AWS WAF","B":"AWS KMS","C":"Amazon Macie","D":"Amazon GuardDuty"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS KMS manages the encryption keys used for EBS volume encryption, handling key creation, storage, rotation, and access control, integrating transparently with EBS to encrypt data at rest.\\nAWS WAF is a web application firewall that filters HTTP and HTTPS traffic to block common web exploits such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting, and is a traffic filtering service with no role in managing encryption keys for EBS volumes. Amazon Macie uses machine learning to discover and classify sensitive data stored in Amazon S3, and is a data classification service for S3 rather than a key management service for EBS encryption. Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that monitors AWS accounts and workloads for malicious activity using machine learning and threat intelligence, and is a security monitoring service rather than a key management service for encrypting EBS volumes."},{"id":"q525","question":"The AWS account administrator of your company has been fired. With the permissions granted to him as an administrator, he was able to create multiple IAM user accounts and access keys. Additionally, you are not sure whether he has access to the AWS root account or not. What should you do immediately to protect your AWS infrastructure?","options":{"A":"Download all the attached policies in a safe place","B":"Delete all IAM accounts and recreate them","C":"Use the CloudWatch service to check all API calls that have been made in your account since the administrator was fired","D":"Rotate all access keys","E":"Change the email address and password of the root user account and enable MFA"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Rotating all access keys immediately invalidates any keys the former administrator may have copied or retained, and changing the root account email and password with MFA enabled prevents root-level access using any credentials the administrator may have had.\\nDownloading attached policies to a safe place does not address the immediate security risk; the priority is to revoke the former administrator\'s access rather than preserving policy documentation, and policies can be reviewed from the console at any time. Deleting all IAM accounts and recreating them would lock out the entire team immediately and disrupt operations without actually addressing whether the root account is compromised; the root account password change must take priority. Using CloudWatch to check all API calls made since the administrator was fired is a useful forensic step for understanding what actions were taken, but it is a post-incident investigation activity rather than an immediate protective measure that should come before securing credentials."},{"id":"q531","question":"A financial services company decides to migrate one of its applications to AWS. The application deals with sensitive data, such as credit card information, and must run on a PCI-compliant environment. Which of the following is the company\'s responsibility when building a PCI-compliant environment in AWS?","options":{"A":"Start the migration process immediately as all AWS services are PCI compliant","B":"Ensure that AWS services are configured properly to meet all PCI DSS standards","C":"Restrict any access to cardholder data and create a policy that addresses information security for all personnel","D":"Configure the underlying infrastructure of AWS services to meet all PCI DSS requirements","E":"Ensure that all PCI DSS physical security requirements are met"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Customers must properly configure AWS services to meet PCI DSS standards as AWS\'s compliance certifications cover the infrastructure layer but not the customer\'s specific service configurations, and customers must restrict cardholder data access and maintain an information security policy as these are customer responsibilities under PCI DSS.\\nNot all AWS services are PCI DSS compliant by default; only specific AWS services are in scope for PCI compliance, and customers must verify that the services they use are in scope and configured correctly to maintain a PCI-compliant environment. Configuring the underlying AWS infrastructure to meet PCI DSS requirements is not the customer\'s responsibility; AWS manages and maintains the physical infrastructure, hardware, and network to meet PCI standards, and customers are responsible only for how they configure the services built on top of that infrastructure. Ensuring that all PCI DSS physical security requirements are met is AWS\'s responsibility; the physical security of data centers, including access controls, surveillance, and hardware handling, is managed by AWS under the shared responsibility model."},{"id":"q535","question":"Which of the following steps should be taken by a customer when conducting penetration testing on AWS?","options":{"A":"Conduct penetration testing using Amazon Inspector, and then notify AWS support","B":"Request and wait for approval from the customer\'s internal security team, and then conduct testing","C":"Notify AWS support, and then conduct testing immediately","D":"Request and wait for approval from AWS support, and then conduct testing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Since 2019, AWS allows customers to perform penetration testing on permitted services including EC2, RDS, CloudFront, and API Gateway without prior AWS approval, so customers should follow their own internal security review and authorisation process before conducting tests.\\nAmazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service that scans for known vulnerabilities and network misconfigurations, but it is not a penetration testing tool and using it does not substitute for or require notification to AWS support before conducting actual penetration testing. AWS no longer requires customers to notify AWS support before conducting penetration testing on permitted services; the policy changed to allow testing without prior approval, so notifying and then conducting testing immediately is based on an outdated requirement. Requesting and waiting for approval from AWS support before conducting penetration testing is no longer required for permitted services; AWS updated its policy to allow customers to test their own resources without prior approval, removing the requirement for a formal approval process."},{"id":"q550","question":"Which task is AWS responsible for in the shared responsibility model for security and compliance?","options":{"A":"Granting access to individuals and services","B":"Encrypting data in transit","C":"Updating Amazon EC2 host firmware","D":"Updating operating systems"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Updating the host firmware on EC2 physical servers is AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to or control over the underlying hardware beneath their instances.\\nGranting access to individuals and services is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers manage their own IAM users, roles, and permissions within their AWS account. Encrypting data in transit is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers decide whether and how to implement encryption for their own data using HTTPS, TLS, or other mechanisms. Updating operating systems is the customer\'s responsibility on services like EC2, where customers control the guest OS and are accountable for keeping it patched and up to date."},{"id":"q553","question":"According to the AWS shared responsibility model what is the sole responsibility of AWS?","options":{"A":"Application security","B":"Edge location management","C":"Patch management","D":"Client-side data"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Managing edge locations, the physical infrastructure used by CloudFront and other services, is solely AWS\'s responsibility as part of its obligation to operate and maintain the global infrastructure underpinning its services.\\nApplication security is the customer\'s responsibility under the Shared Responsibility Model, as customers are accountable for securing the code, configurations, and access controls within their own applications. Patch management is a shared control where AWS patches the underlying infrastructure and managed services, while customers retain responsibility for patching their own operating systems and applications. Client-side data is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers control how their data is classified, encrypted, and managed on the client side regardless of which AWS services they use."},{"id":"q555","question":"Which AWS service identifies security groups that allow unrestricted access to a user\'s AWS resources?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"Amazon Inspector","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor checks your security groups for rules that allow unrestricted access (0.0.0.0/0) to specific ports such as SSH and RDP, flagging potential security risks in its Security category checks.\\nAmazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and while it identifies network reachability issues, the simplest tool for checking security groups for unrestricted access is Trusted Advisor. Amazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics and logs for AWS resources and applications, and is an observability service rather than a tool that audits security group configurations for overly permissive rules. AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events for auditing, capturing who created or modified security group rules and when, but it records actions after the fact rather than proactively identifying groups with unrestricted access."},{"id":"q564","question":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, which of the following activities are the customer\'s responsibility?","options":{"A":"Patching operating system components for Amazon Relational Database Server (Amazon RDS)","B":"Encrypting data on the client-side","C":"Training the data center staff","D":"Configuring Network Access Control Lists (ACL)","E":"Maintaining environmental controls within a data center"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Customers are responsible for encrypting data on the client side before sending it to AWS, and for configuring Network ACLs to control inbound and outbound traffic at the subnet level in their VPC.\\nPatching operating system components for Amazon RDS is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, as RDS is a managed service where AWS handles all underlying OS and database engine patching on the customer\'s behalf. Training data center staff is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to or involvement in the physical facilities and operations teams that run AWS infrastructure. Maintaining environmental controls within a data center such as power, cooling, and fire suppression is entirely AWS\'s responsibility and is fully inherited by customers under the model."},{"id":"q565","question":"Under the shared responsibility model, which of the following is a shared control between a customer and AWS?","options":{"A":"Physical controls","B":"Patch management","C":"Zone security","D":"Data center auditing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Patch management is a shared control. AWS patches the underlying infrastructure and managed service platforms, while customers are responsible for patching their own guest operating systems and applications.\\nPhysical controls are AWS\'s responsibility exclusively, covering physical security of data centers and hardware. Zone security is also AWS\'s responsibility, covering the physical and network isolation between Availability Zones. Data center auditing is AWS\'s responsibility, though customers can request compliance reports via AWS Artifact."},{"id":"q573","question":"Which AWS service or feature can enhance network security by blocking requests from a particular network for a web application on AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS WAF","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS Organizations","E":"Network ACLs"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS WAF creates rules to block requests from specific IP addresses, networks, or matching certain patterns, filtering malicious web traffic at the application layer, and Network ACLs are stateless subnet-level firewalls that can block traffic from specific IP ranges at the network layer. Both can enhance network security by blocking requests from particular networks.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor provides automated recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance categories, and while it identifies insecure configurations, it does not itself block network requests or actively enforce traffic rules. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection between on-premises data centers and AWS, and is a connectivity service rather than a tool for blocking or filtering web application traffic. AWS Organizations manages multiple AWS accounts under a central structure for governance and consolidated billing, and does not have the capability to block network traffic from specific IP ranges for web applications."},{"id":"q575","question":"Which service enables risk auditing by continuously monitoring and logging account activity, including user actions in the AWS Management Console and AWS SDKs?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Config","D":"AWS Health"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail continuously logs all account activity and API calls across AWS infrastructure, providing a complete audit trail that supports risk auditing, compliance monitoring, and security analysis.\\nAmazon CloudWatch collects metrics, logs, and event data from AWS services and resources to provide operational visibility into performance and health, and while it can monitor logs, it does not record API calls or user actions for risk auditing and compliance purposes. AWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration state of AWS resources to assess compliance against desired settings, and focuses on resource configuration changes rather than logging user actions and API calls for risk auditing. AWS Health provides personalized alerts and remediation guidance when AWS is experiencing events that may affect your resources, and is a service health notification tool rather than an account activity logging or risk auditing service."},{"id":"q576","question":"Where can AWS compliance and certification reports be downloaded?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Concierge","C":"AWS Certificate Manager","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact is the self-service portal where customers can download AWS compliance and certification reports including SOC, PCI, ISO, and HIPAA eligibility documentation without needing to contact AWS directly.\\nAWS Concierge is a dedicated support resource for Enterprise support plan customers that assists with billing and account management, and is not a portal for downloading compliance reports or technical certification documents. AWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for encrypting data in transit between applications and users, and is a certificate provisioning service rather than a repository of AWS compliance and audit reports. AWS Trusted Advisor provides automated recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance categories, and while it includes security checks, it does not provide downloadable AWS compliance certification reports."},{"id":"q581","question":"What is the AWS customer responsible for according to the AWS shared responsibility model?","options":{"A":"Physical access controls","B":"Data encryption","C":"Secure disposal of storage devices","D":"Environmental risk management"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Data encryption is the customer\'s responsibility under the shared responsibility model. AWS provides the tools and services to enable encryption, such as AWS KMS and native encryption options across storage services, but the customer decides whether to enable encryption and manages the keys and access policies.\\nPhysical access controls at AWS data centers are entirely AWS\'s responsibility; customers have no access to the physical facilities and rely on AWS to implement and enforce physical security measures. Secure disposal of storage devices is AWS\'s responsibility, as customers never have physical possession of AWS hardware and AWS follows industry standards for decommissioning and disposing of storage media. Environmental risk management for the AWS infrastructure, including temperature control, humidity, and fire suppression in data centers, is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as part of securing the underlying physical infrastructure."},{"id":"q587","question":"Which AWS service would you use to obtain compliance reports and certificates?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Lambda","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Certificate Manager"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports and certifications including SOC, PCI DSS, ISO, and HIPAA documents, available directly from the AWS Management Console without needing to engage AWS support.\\nAWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that runs code in response to events without requiring server provisioning or management, and is a compute service with no connection to compliance reports or certification documentation. Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and network misconfigurations, and produces customer-specific vulnerability findings rather than AWS compliance certifications. AWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services, and is a certificate management service rather than a repository of compliance reports or regulatory certifications."},{"id":"q589","question":"How would an AWS customer easily apply common access controls to a large set of users?","options":{"A":"Apply an IAM policy to an IAM group","B":"Apply an IAM policy to an IAM role","C":"Apply the same IAM policy to all IAM users with access to the same workload","D":"Apply an IAM policy to an Amazon Cognito user pool"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Attaching an IAM policy to an IAM group applies those permissions to all users in the group simultaneously, making it the most efficient way to manage common access controls for a large number of users without configuring each user individually.\\nApplying an IAM policy to an IAM role would affect entities that assume that role, such as applications or services, rather than applying consistent controls directly to a large set of named users who need the same permissions. Applying the same IAM policy to all IAM users individually would achieve the desired result but requires editing each user\'s policy separately, which is time-consuming and error-prone at scale compared to using a group. Applying an IAM policy to an Amazon Cognito user pool affects application users authenticating through Cognito rather than IAM users managing AWS resources, and is used for application-level access control rather than AWS service permission management."},{"id":"q618","question":"Which of the following is a component of the shared responsibility model managed entirely by AWS?","options":{"A":"Patching operating system software","B":"Encrypting data","C":"Enforcing multi-factor authentication","D":"Auditing physical data center assets"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Auditing physical data center assets including hardware inventory, facility inspections, and physical access logs is entirely AWS\'s responsibility under the shared responsibility model, as customers never have physical access to or visibility into AWS data center infrastructure.\\nPatching the operating system software on EC2 instances is the customer\'s responsibility under the shared responsibility model, as AWS manages the underlying hypervisor and hardware but customers are responsible for maintaining the guest operating system on their virtual machines. Encrypting data at rest and in transit is the customer\'s responsibility; AWS provides the tools and services to enable encryption such as KMS and ACM, but customers must decide what to encrypt and configure it appropriately. Enforcing multi-factor authentication for IAM users and the root account is the customer\'s responsibility, as AWS provides MFA functionality but customers must enable and configure it for their account and users."},{"id":"q628","question":"Which of the following is entirely the responsibility of AWS, according to the AWS shared responsibility model?","options":{"A":"Patching of the guest operating system","B":"Security awareness and training","C":"Physical and environmental controls","D":"Development of an IAM password policy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Physical and environmental controls including data center security, cooling systems, fire suppression, and power management are entirely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to or involvement in managing AWS physical infrastructure.\\nPatching of the guest operating system on EC2 instances is the customer\'s responsibility; AWS manages the underlying hardware and hypervisor but customers are responsible for keeping the operating system and applications on their instances up to date. Security awareness and training for employees and users of the AWS environment is the customer\'s responsibility; AWS provides security best practice guidance and training resources, but the organization\'s own training program for its staff is a customer obligation. Developing an IAM password policy is the customer\'s responsibility; AWS provides IAM with configurable password policy settings, but defining and enforcing the specific password requirements for an organization\'s IAM users is a customer configuration task."},{"id":"q633","question":"Which AWS IAM feature is used to associate a set of permissions with multiple users?","options":{"A":"Multi-factor authentication","B":"Groups","C":"Password policies","D":"Access keys"},"correct":"B","explanation":"IAM Groups allow you to attach permission policies to the group, and all users added to that group automatically inherit those permissions, making it the IAM feature used to associate a set of permissions with multiple users at once.\\nMulti-factor authentication adds a second verification layer beyond passwords to secure individual user logins, and is an authentication security mechanism rather than a feature for associating a set of permissions with multiple users simultaneously. Password policies define password complexity, rotation, and reuse rules for IAM user accounts, and are an authentication credential management feature rather than a mechanism for associating permissions with multiple users. Access keys consisting of an access key ID and secret access key are programmatic credentials for CLI and API access associated with individual IAM users, and are an authentication mechanism rather than a feature for associating permissions with groups of users."},{"id":"q635","question":"Which of the following can a customer use to enable single sign-on (SSO) to the AWS Management Console?","options":{"A":"Amazon Connect","B":"AWS Directory Service","C":"Amazon Pinpoint","D":"Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Directory Service integrates with Microsoft Active Directory, enabling single sign-on to the AWS Management Console using existing corporate usernames and passwords without creating separate IAM users for each employee.\\nAmazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service that handles customer interactions through voice and chat channels, and has no capability to enable corporate credential federation or single sign-on authentication to the AWS Management Console. Amazon Pinpoint is an outbound communications service for sending targeted messages via email, SMS, and push notifications to application users, and is a marketing and engagement service with no connection to SSO or corporate identity federation for console access. Amazon Rekognition is a machine learning service for analyzing images and videos to detect objects, faces, and scenes, and is a visual analysis service with no role in enabling corporate credential single sign-on."},{"id":"q637","question":"Which of the following benefits does the AWS Compliance program provide to AWS customers?","options":{"A":"It allows customers to inherit AWS compliance certifications and reduces the scope of their own compliance audits","B":"AWS is responsible for the maintenance of common compliance framework documentation","C":"It assures customers that AWS is maintaining physical security and data protection","D":"It ensures the use of compliance frameworks that are being used by other cloud providers","E":"It will adopt new compliance frameworks as they become relevant to customer workloads"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"The AWS Compliance program allows customers to inherit AWS\'s third-party certifications and audit results, reducing the time and cost of their own compliance efforts. It also assures customers that AWS maintains physical security and data protection standards at the infrastructure level.\\nAWS is not solely responsible for maintaining compliance framework documentation - compliance is a shared responsibility, and customers remain responsible for ensuring their own workloads meet applicable regulations. AWS does not guarantee alignment with frameworks simply because other cloud providers use them - its compliance programs are based on relevant industry standards and regulatory requirements. AWS does not make binding commitments to automatically adopt any new compliance framework that becomes relevant to customer workloads."},{"id":"q638","question":"Which of the following services provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"Amazon GuardDuty","D":"AWS KMS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Artifact provides on-demand, self-service access to AWS compliance reports and certifications including SOC, PCI DSS, ISO, and HIPAA documentation, directly from the AWS Management Console.\\nAWS IAM manages user identities and permissions for accessing AWS services and resources, and is an access management service rather than a repository of compliance reports or certifications. Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that monitors AWS accounts and workloads for malicious activity using machine learning and threat intelligence, and is a security monitoring service rather than a compliance report portal. AWS KMS is a key management service for creating and managing encryption keys used to protect data at rest and in transit, and is an encryption infrastructure service rather than a service providing access to compliance documentation."},{"id":"q639","question":"As part of the AWS shared responsibility model, which of the following operational controls do users fully inherit from AWS?","options":{"A":"Security management of data center","B":"Patch management","C":"Configuration management","D":"User and access management"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Security management of the data center is an operational control customers fully inherit from AWS, meaning AWS performs all physical security operations and customers benefit from this protection without any action required on their part.\\nPatch management is a shared control where AWS patches the infrastructure and managed services it operates, while customers are responsible for patching the operating systems and applications running on their own EC2 instances and other customer-managed resources. Configuration management is also a shared control where AWS maintains the configuration of its own infrastructure components while customers are responsible for configuring their own operating systems, applications, and AWS services to meet their security and operational requirements. User and access management is the customer\'s responsibility; customers create and manage IAM users, groups, roles, and policies, and are fully accountable for who has access to their AWS resources and what those identities are permitted to do."},{"id":"q641","question":"Under the shared responsibility model, which of the following tasks are the responsibility of the customer?","options":{"A":"Maintaining the underlying Amazon EC2 hardware","B":"Managing the VPC network access control lists","C":"Encrypting data in transit and at rest","D":"Replacing failed hard disk drives","E":"Deploying hardware in different Availability Zones"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Customers are responsible for managing VPC network access control lists to define inbound and outbound traffic rules at the subnet level, and for encrypting their own data in transit using TLS and at rest using services such as KMS and S3 server-side encryption.\\nMaintaining the underlying EC2 hardware including servers, storage, and networking infrastructure is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no physical access to the data center equipment that hosts EC2 instances. Replacing failed hard disk drives is AWS\'s responsibility as part of managing the physical infrastructure, and customers experience this transparently through AWS\'s hardware redundancy and replacement processes without any action required. Deploying hardware in different Availability Zones is an AWS infrastructure responsibility; AWS designs and maintains the physical separation of AZs, while customers choose which AZ to deploy their resources in but do not control the underlying hardware deployment."},{"id":"q646","question":"What function do security groups serve related Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) instance security?","options":{"A":"Act as a virtual firewall for the Amazon EC2 instance","B":"Secure AWS user accounts with AWS identity and Access Management (IAM) policies","C":"Provide DDoS protection with AWS Shield","D":"Use Amazon CloudFront to protect the Amazon EC2 instance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Security groups act as virtual firewalls for EC2 instances, controlling inbound and outbound traffic at the instance level by evaluating rules based on protocol, port, and source or destination, providing the primary network security mechanism for EC2 instance access.\\nSecuring AWS user accounts with IAM policies is the function of AWS Identity and Access Management, which controls which users can access which AWS services and what actions they can perform, rather than a function of security groups which operate at the network traffic level. Providing DDoS protection is the function of AWS Shield, which provides managed distributed denial of service mitigation at the network and transport layers, rather than security groups which evaluate individual connection attempts based on rules. Using Amazon CloudFront to protect EC2 instances involves routing traffic through CloudFront\'s edge network with Shield and WAF integrations, and is a separate architectural pattern rather than a function performed by security groups directly."},{"id":"q652","question":"How is asset management on AWS easier than asset management in a physical data center?","options":{"A":"AWS provides a Configuration Management Database that users can maintain","B":"AWS performs infrastructure discovery scans on the customer\'s behalf","C":"Amazon EC2 automatically generates an asset report and places it in the customer\'s specified Amazon S3 bucket","D":"Users can gather asset metadata reliably with a few API calls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"On AWS, users can gather asset metadata reliably by making a few API calls using services such as AWS Config, Systems Manager Inventory, or the AWS CLI, providing comprehensive and up-to-date information about all resources across an account.\\nAWS does not provide a pre-built Configuration Management Database that customers maintain; AWS Config tracks resource configurations automatically, but maintaining a traditional CMDB requires third-party tooling or custom development rather than being a built-in AWS service offering. AWS does not perform infrastructure discovery scans on the customer\'s behalf; services like AWS Config and Systems Manager provide discovery capabilities, but these are tools customers use themselves rather than automated scans performed by AWS on the customer\'s account. Amazon EC2 does not automatically generate asset reports and place them in S3; while instances report some metadata, comprehensive asset reporting requires active use of AWS management services like Config, Systems Manager, or the CLI rather than automatic report generation."},{"id":"q654","question":"Using AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) to grant access only to the resources needed to perform a task is a concept known as:","options":{"A":"Restricted access","B":"As-needed access","C":"Least privilege access","D":"Token access"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Least privilege access means granting IAM users only the minimum permissions they need to perform their specific assigned tasks, reducing the risk of accidental or intentional misuse of AWS resources through overly broad permission grants.\\nRestricted access is a general term that implies limiting access, but it does not specifically capture the principle of granting the minimum required permissions; least privilege is the precise IAM concept that defines permissions based on task requirements rather than broadly restricting access. As-needed access is an informal description that partially captures the idea of granting permissions when needed, but least privilege is the established AWS and security industry term for the principle of granting minimum necessary permissions. Token access refers to the use of temporary security tokens issued when IAM roles are assumed, and describes a credential mechanism rather than the principle of scoping permissions to what is required for a task."},{"id":"q656","question":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, customer responsibilities include which one of the following?","options":{"A":"Securing the hardware, software, facilities, and networks that run all products and services","B":"Providing certificates, reports, and other documentation directly to AWS customers under NDA","C":"Configuring the operating system, network, and firewall","D":"Obtaining industry certifications and independent third-party attestations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Configuring the operating system, network settings, and firewall rules on EC2 instances falls within the customer\'s responsibility under the shared responsibility model, as AWS provides the underlying infrastructure while customers are responsible for what they install and configure on top of it.\\nSecuring the hardware, software, facilities, and networks that run all AWS products and services is AWS\'s responsibility under the shared responsibility model, as customers have no access to or control over the physical and virtual infrastructure that AWS manages. Providing compliance certificates, reports, and documentation to customers under NDA is a service AWS provides through AWS Artifact, not a customer responsibility; customers access these documents rather than create or provide them. Obtaining industry certifications and third-party attestations for the AWS infrastructure is AWS\'s responsibility, as AWS undergoes independent audits for certifications such as ISO, SOC, and PCI DSS that apply to the infrastructure layer rather than being a customer obligation."},{"id":"q657","question":"Which managed AWS service provides real-time guidance on AWS security best practices?","options":{"A":"AWS X-Ray","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS Systems Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides real-time recommendations across security, cost optimization, performance, fault tolerance, and service limits, directly guiding customers toward security best practices.\\nAmazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics and logs for AWS resources and applications including CPU utilization, error rates, and custom metrics, and is an observability service rather than a service that provides security best practice guidance. AWS Config continuously monitors and records resource configurations against desired state rules, and is a compliance evaluation service rather than a service that provides proactive guidance on security best practices. AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events for auditing and compliance purposes, and is an activity logging service rather than a service that provides real-time guidance on improving the security posture of an AWS environment."},{"id":"q659","question":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, customers are responsible for which aspects of security in the cloud?","options":{"A":"Virtualization management","B":"Hardware management","C":"Encryption management","D":"Facilities management","E":"Firewall management"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Customers are responsible for managing encryption of their data at rest and in transit including configuring encryption settings and managing KMS keys, and for configuring firewall rules through security groups and Network ACLs to control traffic to their resources.\\nVirtualization management is not a standard shared responsibility category; the underlying virtualization infrastructure including the hypervisor is AWS\'s responsibility, while customers manage the virtual machines that run on top of it rather than the visualisation layer itself. Hardware management including physical servers, storage devices, and networking equipment is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as customers have no access to or interaction with the physical infrastructure in AWS data centers. Facilities management including data center physical security, power, and cooling is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as customers never have physical access to or involvement in managing the facilities that house AWS infrastructure."},{"id":"q661","question":"What is a responsibility of AWS in the shared responsibility model?","options":{"A":"Updating the network ACLs to block traffic to vulnerable ports","B":"Patching operating systems running on Amazon EC2 instances","C":"Updating the firmware on the underlying EC2 hosts","D":"Updating the security group rules to block traffic to the vulnerable ports"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Updating the firmware on EC2 host hardware is AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to the physical servers and AWS manages all maintenance of the hardware layer including firmware updates for security and performance improvements.\\nUpdating network ACLs to block traffic to vulnerable ports is the customer\'s responsibility; customers configure their VPC network ACLs to define which traffic is allowed or denied at the subnet level as part of their security configuration. Patching operating systems running on EC2 instances is the customer\'s responsibility; AWS manages the underlying hypervisor and host hardware, but the guest operating system and its patches are the customer\'s obligation. Updating security group rules to block traffic to vulnerable ports is the customer\'s responsibility; customers define and maintain security group rules to control which traffic reaches their EC2 instances as part of their network security configuration."},{"id":"q663","question":"What does it mean to grant least privilege to AWS IAM users?","options":{"A":"It is granting permissions to a single user only","B":"It is granting permissions using AWS IAM policies only","C":"It is granting AdministratorAccess policy permissions to trustworthy users","D":"It is granting only the permissions required to perform a given task"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Least privilege means granting only the minimum permissions required to perform a specific task, ensuring that IAM users cannot access resources or perform actions beyond what their job requires, minimizing the risk of misuse.\\nGranting permissions to a single user only describes individual rather than shared access, but does not capture the concept of scoping permissions to the minimum required; least privilege is about the breadth of permissions granted rather than the number of users receiving them. Granting permissions using IAM policies only is a description of the mechanism for assigning permissions in AWS, but does not define the principle of least privilege, which is about minimizing the scope of those permissions rather than the method of granting them. Granting AdministratorAccess permissions to trustworthy users is the opposite of least privilege; administrator access grants unrestricted access to all AWS services and resources, which far exceeds the minimum permissions needed for most tasks regardless of how trustworthy the user is."},{"id":"q683","question":"Which AWS service provides a self-service portal for on-demand access to AWS compliance reports?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Artifact"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Artifact is a self-service portal providing on-demand access to AWS compliance reports and certifications including SOC, PCI, and ISO documents, making it the purpose-built service for customers requiring on-demand access to compliance reports.\\nAWS Config continuously monitors and records resource configurations to evaluate compliance against desired rules, and is a resource configuration compliance tool rather than a portal for downloading AWS auditor-issued compliance reports. AWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services to protect data in transit, and is a certificate provisioning and management service rather than a compliance documentation repository. Amazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and network misconfigurations, and produces customer-specific assessment findings rather than AWS compliance reports."},{"id":"q692","question":"According to the AWS shared responsibility model, what is AWS responsible for?","options":{"A":"Configuring Amazon VPC","B":"Managing application code","C":"Maintaining application traffic","D":"Managing the network infrastructure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS is responsible for managing the physical and virtual network infrastructure that underpins its global cloud, including the routers, switches, and physical cabling that connect data centers, Availability Zones, and Regions.\\nConfiguring Amazon VPC is the customer\'s responsibility; customers define their VPC IP address ranges, subnets, route tables, internet gateways, and security settings as part of their network design within AWS. Managing application code is entirely the customer\'s responsibility; AWS provides the compute and runtime services, but the application logic, code quality, and updates are owned and maintained by the customer. Maintaining application traffic, including managing load balancers, CDN configurations, and DNS routing, is the customer\'s responsibility for their own applications, though AWS manages the underlying network infrastructure those services run on."},{"id":"q694","question":"Which AWS tool will identify security groups that grant unrestricted Internet access to a limited list of ports?","options":{"A":"AWS Organizations","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Usage Report","D":"Amazon EC2 dashboard"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor scans your security groups and flags those that grant unrestricted internet access (0.0.0.0/0) to specific ports such as SSH, RDP, and common database ports, helping identify and close potential security vulnerabilities.\\nAWS Organizations manages multiple AWS accounts under a central structure for consolidated billing and governance with service control policies, and does not have the capability to inspect individual security group rules or identify overly permissive internet access configurations. AWS Usage Reports provide data about AWS service usage and costs, and are billing and consumption tracking tools rather than security assessment tools that evaluate network access control configurations. The Amazon EC2 dashboard displays information about running instances, instance types, and their states, and while security groups are visible in the console, the dashboard does not automatically identify which groups grant unrestricted internet access the way Trusted Advisor\'s automated check does."},{"id":"q697","question":"Which of the following is the responsibility of AWS?","options":{"A":"Setting up AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) users and groups","B":"Physically destroying storage media at end of life","C":"Patching guest operating systems","D":"Configuring security settings on Amazon EC2 instances"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS is responsible for physically destroying storage media at end of life using industry-standard techniques to prevent data leakage, following secure decommissioning procedures as part of their data center operations under the shared responsibility model.\\nSetting up IAM users and groups is the customer\'s responsibility; customers define their own identity and access management structure including creating users, assigning them to groups, and attaching policies that control what each identity can access. Patching guest operating systems on EC2 instances is the customer\'s responsibility; AWS manages the underlying host hardware and hypervisor, but the operating system running inside the virtual machine must be patched and maintained by the customer. Configuring security settings on EC2 instances including firewall rules, operating system hardening, and application security configurations is the customer\'s responsibility as part of securing what they deploy and manage on AWS compute resources."},{"id":"q702","question":"What tasks should a customer perform when that customer suspects an AWS account has been compromised?","options":{"A":"Rotate passwords and access keys","B":"Remove MFA tokens","C":"Move resources to a different AWS Region","D":"Delete AWS CloudTrail Resources","E":"Contact AWS Support"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"When an account is suspected compromised, immediately rotating all passwords and access keys invalidates potentially stolen credentials, and contacting AWS Support provides assistance with the investigation and remediation of the compromise.\\nRemoving MFA tokens would reduce account security at precisely the moment it is most needed, as MFA provides an additional authentication barrier that helps prevent unauthorised access even if credentials have been stolen. Moving resources to a different AWS Region does not address the underlying compromise and has no effect on the attacker\'s ability to access the account using stolen credentials. Deleting AWS CloudTrail resources would destroy the audit trail needed to investigate how the compromise occurred and what actions the attacker took, making remediation significantly harder."},{"id":"q705","question":"What is an AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) role?","options":{"A":"A user associated with an AWS resource","B":"A group associated with an AWS resource","C":"An entity that defines a set of permissions for use with an AWS resource","D":"An authentication credential associated with a multi-factor authentication (MFA) token"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An IAM role is an entity that defines a set of permissions for making AWS service requests, and unlike an IAM user it does not have permanent credentials; instead it provides temporary security credentials to whoever or whatever assumes it.\\nA user associated with an AWS resource describes an IAM user or service account assigned to interact with specific resources, but this does not capture the defining characteristic of an IAM role, which is that it can be assumed temporarily by multiple different entities rather than being permanently associated with a single one. A group associated with an AWS resource describes an IAM group used to manage shared permissions for multiple users, not a role, which is assumed dynamically by users, services, or applications that need its permissions temporarily. An authentication credential associated with an MFA token describes a multi-factor authentication mechanism, not an IAM role, which is an identity type that provides temporary credentials and permissions rather than being a specific authentication token."},{"id":"q717","question":"Which service enables customers to audit and monitor changes in AWS resources?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"Amazon GuardDuty","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Config continuously monitors and records AWS resource configurations, tracking changes over time and evaluating configurations against desired state compliance rules, making it the correct service for auditing and monitoring resource changes.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor provides automated recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance categories by analyzing your AWS environment, and is an advisory tool rather than a service that specifically records and audits individual resource configuration changes over time. Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that uses machine learning to identify malicious activity and anomalous behavior in AWS accounts and workloads, and is a security monitoring service rather than a resource configuration change auditing tool. Amazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and network misconfigurations, and produces security assessment findings rather than tracking and auditing resource configuration changes."},{"id":"q722","question":"A user is running an application on AWS and notices that one or more AWS-owned IP addresses is involved in a distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attack. Who should the user contact FIRST about this situation?","options":{"A":"AWS Premium Support","B":"AWS Technical Account Manager","C":"AWS Solutions Architect","D":"AWS Abuse team"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The AWS Abuse Team handles reports of AWS resources being used in malicious activities including DDoS attacks, and should be the first point of contact when AWS-owned IP addresses are identified as participating in an attack, as they can investigate and stop the misuse.\\nAWS Premium Support assists paying customers with technical issues and architectural guidance related to their own AWS environment, but is not the designated channel for reporting abuse originating from AWS IP addresses belonging to other accounts. The AWS Technical Account Manager is a support resource for Enterprise plan customers providing proactive guidance and operational reviews, but is not the appropriate contact for reporting external abuse incidents involving AWS infrastructure. AWS Solutions Architects help customers design architectures and optimize workloads, and are not the contact for reporting security incidents or abuse involving AWS infrastructure and IP addresses."},{"id":"q727","question":"Which AWS service offers on-demand access to AWS security and compliance reports?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Health","D":"Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Artifact provides on-demand access to AWS security and compliance reports including SOC, PCI, ISO, and other certifications, available directly from the AWS Management Console to help customers meet their own regulatory requirements.\\nAWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events for auditing and compliance purposes, and produces customer-specific activity logs rather than AWS security certification reports issued by third-party auditors. AWS Health provides personalized alerts about AWS events that may affect a customer\'s specific resources or service availability, and is a service health and notification tool rather than a repository of compliance reports. Amazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics and logs for AWS resources and applications, and is an observability and alerting service rather than a source of AWS security and compliance documentation."},{"id":"q730","question":"Which AWS service provides inbound and outbound network ACLs to harden external connectivity to Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"Amazon VPC","D":"Amazon API Gateway"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon VPC provides Network Access Control Lists that act as stateless firewalls at the subnet level, controlling inbound and outbound traffic to harden network connectivity for EC2 instances, as well as security groups at the instance level for additional control.\\nAWS IAM manages user identities and permissions for accessing AWS services and resources, and is an access control service rather than a network security component that provides inbound and outbound traffic filtering via ACLs. Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service for handling customer interactions through voice and chat, and is a communications service with no connection to VPC networking or network ACL configuration. Amazon API Gateway is a fully managed service for creating, publishing, and managing APIs, and is an application integration layer rather than a VPC networking component that provides network ACLs to control connectivity."},{"id":"q733","question":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, AWS is responsible for which security-related task?","options":{"A":"Lifecycle management of IAM credentials","B":"Physical security of global infrastructure","C":"Encryption of Amazon EBS volumes","D":"Firewall configuration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS is responsible for the physical security of its global infrastructure, including the data centers, networking hardware, and facilities, as customers have no access to or control over any physical component of the AWS environment.\\nLifecycle management of IAM credentials including creating, rotating, and deleting access keys and passwords for IAM users is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers define and manage the identities used to access their AWS resources. Encryption of Amazon EBS volumes is the customer\'s responsibility; while AWS provides KMS for key management and EBS supports encryption, customers must choose to enable encryption and configure the appropriate keys for their volumes. Firewall configuration through security groups and network ACLs is the customer\'s responsibility as part of securing network access to their own resources within their VPC."},{"id":"q735","question":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, which of the following is an example of security in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Managing edge locations","B":"Physical security","C":"Firewall configuration","D":"Global infrastructure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Firewall configuration through security groups and network ACLs, which customers define to control inbound and outbound traffic to their resources, is the customer\'s responsibility and is an example of security performed within the cloud on the customer\'s side of the shared responsibility model.\\nManaging edge locations is AWS\'s responsibility; AWS operates the global network of edge locations for CloudFront and other services, and customers benefit from these locations without being responsible for their management or security. Physical security of AWS data centers and hardware is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as customers never have physical access to the facilities where AWS infrastructure runs. Global infrastructure, including the physical data centers, Availability Zones, and Regions, is designed, built, and operated by AWS, and maintaining its security and availability is an AWS responsibility rather than a customer one."},{"id":"q740","question":"A web application running on AWS has been spammed with malicious requests from a recurring set of IP addresses. Which AWS service can help secure the application and block the malicious traffic?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM","B":"Amazon GuardDuty","C":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","D":"AWS WAF"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS WAF lets you create rules to block requests from specific IP addresses or ranges, inspect request content, and filter traffic patterns, providing effective application-layer protection against repeated malicious requests from known IP addresses.\\nAWS IAM manages user identities and permissions for accessing AWS services and resources, and is an access control service rather than a service that can block malicious HTTP traffic targeting a web application. Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that uses machine learning to identify malicious activity and anomalous behavior in AWS accounts, and while it can detect suspicious patterns, it does not actively block or filter web application traffic in real time. Amazon SNS is a fully managed publish/subscribe messaging service for sending notifications to subscribers, and is a messaging and notification service with no capability to inspect or block malicious web traffic patterns."},{"id":"q746","question":"A user needs an automated security assessment report that will identify unintended network access to Amazon EC2 instances and vulnerabilities on those instances. Which AWS service will provide this assessment report?","options":{"A":"EC2 security groups","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon Macie","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Inspector automatically assesses EC2 instances for unintended network access by analyzing network configurations and known software vulnerabilities by scanning installed packages against vulnerability databases, generating prioritized security assessment reports.\\nEC2 security groups are virtual firewalls that control which traffic is allowed to reach EC2 instances based on defined rules, and are a preventive network control rather than an automated assessment service that identifies vulnerabilities and generates reports. AWS Config continuously monitors and records resource configurations against desired state compliance rules, and is a configuration compliance evaluation service rather than a vulnerability assessment tool that scans for network exposure or software weaknesses. Amazon Macie uses machine learning to discover and classify sensitive data stored in Amazon S3, and is a data classification and protection service for S3 rather than a service that assesses EC2 instances for network access vulnerabilities."},{"id":"q748","question":"Where can users find a catalog of AWS-recognized providers of third-party security solutions?","options":{"A":"AWS Service Catalog","B":"AWS Marketplace","C":"AWS Quick Start","D":"AWS CodeDeploy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Marketplace is a digital catalog where users can find, purchase, and deploy third-party software solutions from independent software vendors that are vetted by AWS, including security tools such as firewalls, intrusion detection, and compliance products.\\nAWS Service Catalog allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of approved IT services for internal use within their own AWS environment, and is an internal governance tool for managing approved product portfolios rather than a public catalog of third-party security vendors. AWS Quick Start provides pre-built, automated deployments of popular technologies using AWS CloudFormation templates, and is a deployment acceleration resource rather than a catalog of third-party security solution providers. AWS CodeDeploy is an automated deployment service that delivers application code to EC2 instances, Lambda functions, and on-premises servers, and is a deployment automation tool with no connection to browsing or purchasing third-party security solutions."},{"id":"q755","question":"Within the AWS shared responsibility model, who is responsible for security and compliance?","options":{"A":"The customer is responsible","B":"AWS is responsible","C":"AWS and the customer share responsibility","D":"AWS shares responsibility with the relevant governing body"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Security and compliance is a shared responsibility between AWS and the customer; AWS secures the underlying cloud infrastructure while customers are responsible for securing their workloads, data, and configurations built on top of it.\\nThe customer alone being responsible for security and compliance is incorrect; under the shared responsibility model, AWS manages security of the cloud infrastructure including physical data centers, hypervisor, and global network, which are entirely outside customer control. AWS alone being responsible for security and compliance is incorrect; customers must actively manage security in the cloud including IAM policies, encryption configuration, network security, and application security, all of which AWS cannot control or configure on the customer\'s behalf. AWS sharing responsibility with a relevant governing body is not how the shared responsibility model works; the model divides responsibility between AWS and the customer based on which layer of the stack each party controls, with no involvement from external regulatory bodies in the operational security responsibility."},{"id":"q757","question":"Which AWS service is used to provide encryption for Amazon EBS?","options":{"A":"AWS Certificate Manager","B":"AWS Systems Manager","C":"AWS KMS","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Key Management Service manages the encryption keys used for EBS volume encryption, handling key creation, rotation, and access control, integrating transparently with EBS to protect data at rest.\\nAWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for encrypting data in transit between applications and clients, and is a certificate management service rather than a key management service for EBS volume encryption at rest. AWS Systems Manager provides operational management tools for EC2 instances and other resources including patching, configuration management, and session management, and is an operations service rather than an encryption key management service for EBS volumes. AWS Config continuously monitors and records resource configurations against desired state compliance rules, and is a configuration compliance evaluation service rather than an encryption key management service."},{"id":"q760","question":"What does AWS Shield Standard provide?","options":{"A":"WAF rules","B":"DDoS protection","C":"Identity and Access Management (IAM) permissions and access to resources","D":"Data encryption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Shield Standard provides automatic DDoS protection at no extra cost for all AWS customers, automatically detecting and mitigating common network and transport layer attacks including UDP floods and SYN floods without any configuration required.\\nWAF rules are managed by AWS WAF, which is a separate service from Shield that filters application-layer HTTP and HTTPS traffic based on configurable rules, and AWS Shield Standard does not provide WAF rule functionality. IAM permissions and access to resources are managed by AWS Identity and Access Management, which controls who can access which AWS services, and have no connection to the DDoS protection that AWS Shield provides. Data encryption is provided by services such as AWS KMS, S3 server-side encryption, and EBS encryption, and has no relationship to AWS Shield, which is specifically a DDoS mitigation service."},{"id":"q766","question":"What is one of the customer\'s responsibilities according to the AWS shared responsibility model?","options":{"A":"Virtualization infrastructure","B":"Network infrastructure","C":"Application security","D":"Physical security of hardware"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Application security, including securing application code, data access controls, and authentication configurations, is the customer\'s responsibility under the shared responsibility model as it falls within the customer\'s side of the stack that AWS cannot access or control.\\nVirtualization infrastructure including the hypervisor that abstracts physical hardware into virtual machines is managed and secured entirely by AWS as part of its security of the cloud obligations, and customers have no access to or responsibility for this layer. Network infrastructure including the global network connecting AWS Regions, Availability Zones, and edge locations is owned and maintained by AWS and is not a customer responsibility, as customers configure virtual networking within their VPC but do not manage the underlying physical network. Physical security of hardware including data center access controls, server security, and decommissioning of physical equipment is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as customers have no access to or interaction with AWS physical facilities."},{"id":"q770","question":"Where should users report that AWS resources are being used for malicious purposes?","options":{"A":"AWS Abuse team","B":"AWS Shield","C":"AWS Support","D":"AWS Developer Forums"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The AWS Abuse Team should be contacted when AWS resources or IP addresses are being used for malicious purposes such as hosting spam, conducting DDoS attacks, or distributing malware, as they are the designated channel for investigating and stopping such misuse.\\nAWS Shield provides managed DDoS protection that automatically detects and mitigates DDoS attacks against your own resources, and is a protective service for your own infrastructure rather than a channel for reporting AWS resources being abused by others. AWS Support assists customers with technical issues and questions about their own AWS environment, but is not the designated channel for reporting that AWS infrastructure belonging to other accounts is being used for malicious purposes. AWS Developer Forums are community discussion spaces where developers share knowledge and ask questions about AWS services, and are not an appropriate reporting channel for security incidents involving malicious use of AWS resources."},{"id":"q771","question":"Which AWS service needs to be enabled to track all user account changes within the AWS Management Console?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","C":"VPC Flow Logs","D":"AWS CloudHSM"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and user actions in the AWS Management Console, providing a complete audit trail of account changes for security analysis and compliance.\\nAmazon Simple Notification Service is a fully managed pub/sub messaging service that delivers notifications to subscribers such as Lambda functions, HTTP endpoints, and email addresses, and has no capability to record or track user account changes within the AWS Management Console. VPC Flow Logs capture information about IP traffic flowing to and from network interfaces within a VPC, and is a network traffic logging feature that records connection-level data rather than user actions or account changes in the console. AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules for generating and managing cryptographic keys, and is a key management and encryption service with no capability to track or audit user activity within the AWS Management Console."},{"id":"q777","question":"Which of the following is AWS\'s responsibility under the AWS shared responsibility model?","options":{"A":"Configuring third-party applications","B":"Maintaining physical hardware","C":"Securing application access and data","D":"Managing custom Amazon Machine Images (AMIs)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Maintaining physical hardware including servers, storage devices, and networking equipment in data centers is exclusively AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to or interaction with the physical infrastructure that powers AWS services.\\nConfiguring third-party applications deployed on AWS resources is the customer\'s responsibility; AWS provides the compute, storage, and networking infrastructure, but customers are responsible for installing, configuring, and securing any third-party software they choose to run on that infrastructure. Securing application access and data, including setting up appropriate IAM policies, encryption configurations, and access controls for applications and data stored in AWS, is the customer\'s responsibility as part of security in the cloud. Managing custom Amazon Machine Images including creating, maintaining, and updating AMIs used to launch EC2 instances is the customer\'s responsibility as part of managing their own compute environment."},{"id":"q779","question":"How would a system administrator add an additional layer of login security to a user\'s AWS Management Console?","options":{"A":"Use AWS Cloud Directory","B":"Audit AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) roles","C":"Enable Multi-Factor Authentication","D":"Enable AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enabling Multi-Factor Authentication adds a second verification layer beyond a password, requiring a time-based one-time code from a hardware or virtual device to complete login, providing an additional layer of security for AWS Management Console access.\\nAWS Cloud Directory is a managed directory service for building cloud-native directories that organize hierarchical data such as organizational structures and device registries, and is not a mechanism for adding a login security layer to the AWS Management Console. Auditing IAM roles reviews what permissions have been granted across an account, which is a governance activity that helps identify excessive permissions but does not add an additional authentication step to the login process itself. Enabling AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity for auditing and compliance, and while it improves visibility into who did what after login, it does not add any additional layer of security to the login authentication process."},{"id":"q780","question":"Which service can identify the user that made the API call when an Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) instance is terminated?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS X-Ray","D":"AWS Identity and Access Management (AWS IAM)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail logs all API calls including EC2 instance terminations, recording which IAM user or role made the call, the source IP address, and the timestamp, making it the purpose-built service for identifying who performed a specific action in an AWS account.\\nAmazon CloudWatch collects metrics, logs, and event data from AWS services and resources to provide operational monitoring and alerting, and does not record API call identity or attribution information for specific actions such as instance terminations. AWS X-Ray is a distributed tracing service that analyzes and debugs the performance of applications by mapping requests as they flow through application components, and is a performance analysis tool with no API call logging or user attribution capability. AWS Identity and Access Management defines and manages permissions for users and roles, controlling what actions they are allowed to perform, but does not itself log or record which user made a specific API call or when."},{"id":"q782","question":"Where can a customer find information about prohibited actions on AWS infrastructure?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","C":"AWS Billing Console","D":"AWS Acceptable Use Policy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The AWS Acceptable Use Policy defines what activities are prohibited on AWS infrastructure including illegal content hosting, network attacks, and sending spam, and is the authoritative reference for customers seeking to understand what actions are not permitted.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor provides automated recommendations for improving the security, cost efficiency, performance, and fault tolerance of AWS environments, and is an advisory tool rather than a document defining prohibited uses of the AWS platform. AWS IAM manages user identities and permissions to control which AWS services and resources users can access, and is an access management service rather than a policy document listing prohibited infrastructure activities. The AWS Billing Console displays cost and usage data, invoices, and payment information for an AWS account, and is a financial management interface with no connection to defining or documenting prohibited uses of AWS infrastructure."},{"id":"q786","question":"Which of the following are recommended practices for managing IAM users?","options":{"A":"Require IAM users to change their passwords after a specified period of time","B":"Prevent IAM users from reusing previous passwords","C":"Recommend that the same password be used on AWS and other sites","D":"Require IAM users to store their passwords in raw text","E":"Disable multi-factor authentication (MFA) for IAM users"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Requiring password rotation after a specified period limits how long any compromised password remains valid, and preventing password reuse ensures users cannot immediately revert to previously exposed credentials after a required change.\\nRecommending the same password across AWS and other sites creates a significant security risk through credential reuse, as a breach of any other site would then compromise the AWS account; password uniqueness for each service is a fundamental security best practice. Requiring IAM users to store passwords in raw unencrypted text would expose those passwords to anyone who gains access to wherever they are stored, creating severe security vulnerabilities; passwords should be kept confidential and never stored in plain text. Disabling MFA for IAM users removes a critical second authentication factor, leaving accounts protected only by passwords and significantly increasing the risk of unauthorised access if credentials are compromised."},{"id":"q793","question":"Which situation should be reported to the AWS Abuse team?","options":{"A":"An Availability Zone has a service disruption","B":"An intrusion attempt is made from an AWS IP address","C":"A user has trouble accessing an Amazon S3 bucket from an AWS IP address","D":"A user needs to change payment methods due to a compromise"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intrusion attempts originating from AWS IP addresses are abuse scenarios that should be reported to the AWS Abuse Team, which investigates misuse of AWS resources for malicious activities such as hacking, phishing, and denial of service attacks.\\nAn Availability Zone service disruption is an infrastructure availability issue that should be monitored through the AWS Health Dashboard and reported to AWS Support if it is causing operational impact, rather than being an abuse scenario for the Abuse Team. A user having trouble accessing an S3 bucket from an AWS IP address is a technical access or permission issue that should be investigated by checking IAM policies, bucket policies, and VPC configurations, and addressed through AWS Support if needed rather than reported to the Abuse Team. A user needing to change payment methods due to a compromise is a billing and account security matter that should be handled through the AWS Management Console or by contacting AWS Customer Service for billing assistance."},{"id":"q796","question":"Which of the following are components of Amazon VPC?","options":{"A":"Objects","B":"Subnets","C":"Buckets","D":"Internet gateways","E":"Access key"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Subnets divide a VPC\'s IP address range into segments that organize resources and define routing boundaries within the VPC, and internet gateways enable communication between resources in a VPC and the internet. Both are fundamental VPC components.\\nObjects is an Amazon S3 term for the data items stored within S3 buckets, and is a storage abstraction concept with no connection to Amazon VPC networking components such as subnets, route tables, or gateways. Buckets are Amazon S3 containers for storing objects and are the primary storage unit in the S3 service, and are not components of Amazon VPC, which is a virtual network service rather than an object storage service. Access keys are IAM credentials consisting of an access key ID and secret access key used for programmatic API and CLI authentication, and are identity management constructs rather than VPC networking components."},{"id":"q800","question":"Which functions can users perform using AWS KMS?","options":{"A":"Create and manage AWS access keys for the AWS account root user","B":"Create and manage AWS access keys for an AWS account IAM user","C":"Create and manage keys for encryption and decryption of data","D":"Create and manage keys for multi-factor authentication"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS KMS lets users create and manage cryptographic keys for encrypting and decrypting data across AWS services, providing centralized key lifecycle management with audit logging of key usage via CloudTrail.\\nCreating and managing AWS access keys for the root user is done through the IAM section of the AWS Management Console, and access keys are authentication credentials rather than cryptographic keys managed by KMS. Creating and managing AWS access keys for IAM users is performed through the IAM console or CLI, and access keys are programmatic authentication credentials unrelated to the encryption key management function of AWS KMS. Creating and managing keys for multi-factor authentication involves configuring virtual or hardware MFA devices in the IAM console, and MFA tokens are authentication mechanisms rather than cryptographic keys for data encryption and decryption."},{"id":"q806","question":"Which Amazon VPC feature enables users to capture information about the IP traffic that reaches Amazon EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"Security groups","B":"Elastic network interfaces","C":"Network ACLs","D":"VPC Flow Logs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"VPC Flow Logs capture metadata about the IP traffic going to and from network interfaces in a VPC, providing visibility into traffic patterns for security analysis, compliance auditing, and connectivity troubleshooting.\\nSecurity groups act as stateful virtual firewalls at the EC2 instance level that control which traffic is allowed in and out based on defined rules, but they filter traffic rather than capturing or logging information about it. Elastic network interfaces are virtual network cards that attach to EC2 instances to provide network connectivity, and are a networking component rather than a feature for capturing or recording IP traffic data. Network ACLs act as stateless firewalls at the subnet level that allow or deny traffic based on IP address and port rules, but like security groups they filter traffic rather than capture or log information about it."},{"id":"q808","question":"Which AWS service will provide a way to generate encryption keys that can be used to encrypt data?","options":{"A":"Amazon Macie","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","D":"AWS Secrets Manager","E":"AWS CloudHSM"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"AWS KMS generates and manages encryption keys through a software-based, AWS-managed infrastructure, and AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules that give customers full control over key generation and management. Both services provide ways to generate encryption keys that can be used to encrypt data.\\nAmazon Macie uses machine learning to discover and classify sensitive data in Amazon S3, and is a data classification service rather than a key generation service that provides encryption keys for protecting data. AWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for encrypting data in transit, and does not generate general-purpose encryption keys for use in protecting customer data at rest. AWS Secrets Manager stores, rotates, and manages access to secrets such as database credentials and API keys, and while it uses encryption to protect stored secrets, it is not a service that generates encryption keys for customers to use in their own data encryption workflows."},{"id":"q811","question":"Which AWS service helps users meet contractual and regulatory compliance requirements for data security by using dedicated hardware appliances within the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"AWS CloudHSM","C":"AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","D":"AWS Directory Service"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated, single-tenant hardware security modules within the AWS Cloud that meet FIPS 140-2 Level 3 compliance requirements, offering complete customer control over key generation and management on dedicated hardware for organizations with strict contractual or regulatory mandates.\\nAWS Secrets Manager stores, rotates, and manages secrets such as database credentials and API keys; it handles the secret lifecycle rather than providing dedicated hardware appliances. AWS KMS uses HSMs but shares the underlying hardware across customers, so it does not offer the dedicated single-tenant hardware that some contractual or regulatory requirements mandate. AWS Directory Service provides managed Microsoft Active Directory for identity management and single sign-on, with no connection to hardware security modules or key management."},{"id":"q812","question":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, the customer manages which of the following?","options":{"A":"Decommissioning of physical storage devices","B":"Security group and ACL configuration","C":"Patch management of an Amazon RDS instance operating system","D":"Controlling physical access to data centers","E":"Patch management of an Amazon EC2 instance operating system"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Customers manage security group and ACL configurations to control inbound and outbound network access to their resources as part of securing their network environment, and are responsible for patching the guest operating system on EC2 instances including applying security updates and vulnerability patches.\\nDecommissioning physical storage devices including secure data erasure and physical destruction is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as part of managing the physical infrastructure in data centers that customers never access. Controlling physical access to AWS data centers including badge readers, biometrics, and surveillance is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as customers have no presence in or access to the facilities where AWS hardware is located. Patch management for the Amazon RDS instance operating system is AWS\'s responsibility as RDS is a managed service where AWS handles the underlying database engine patching, OS patching, and maintenance; customers only need to manage patching of databases they run on EC2 themselves."},{"id":"q815","question":"What is a user responsible for when running an application in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Managing physical hardware","B":"Updating the underlying hypervisor","C":"Providing a list of users approved for data center access","D":"Managing application software updates"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When running applications in the AWS Cloud, users are responsible for managing application software updates, including patches, version upgrades, and security fixes for their own application code.\\nManaging physical hardware is entirely AWS\'s responsibility, as customers have no access to the underlying physical servers, networking equipment, or data center infrastructure that runs AWS services. Updating the underlying hypervisor is AWS\'s responsibility, as the virtualization layer that runs EC2 instances is part of the infrastructure that AWS owns and maintains on behalf of all customers. Providing a list of users approved for data center access is AWS\'s responsibility, as AWS manages all physical access controls to its data centers and customers have no role in determining who can enter AWS facilities."},{"id":"q819","question":"Which AWS service helps identify malicious or unauthorized activities in AWS accounts and workloads?","options":{"A":"Amazon Rekognition","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"Amazon GuardDuty","D":"Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors AWS accounts, CloudTrail event logs, VPC Flow Logs, and DNS logs using machine learning and threat intelligence to identify malicious activity and unauthorised behavior.\\nAWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events in an AWS account for auditing and compliance purposes, and provides the raw activity data that GuardDuty analyzes, but CloudTrail itself does not identify or alert on malicious or unauthorised activities. AWS Trusted Advisor provides automated recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance categories including security checks for open ports and missing MFA, but does not continuously monitor for active threats or ongoing malicious behavior in accounts. Amazon Macie uses machine learning to discover and classify sensitive data stored in Amazon S3, and is a data classification and protection service rather than a real-time threat detection service monitoring account-wide activity."},{"id":"q822","question":"A company is hosting a web application in a Docker container on Amazon EC2. AWS is responsible for which of the following tasks?","options":{"A":"Scaling the web application and services developed with Docker","B":"Provisioning or scheduling containers to run on clusters and maintain their availability","C":"Performing hardware maintenance in the AWS facilities that run the AWS Cloud","D":"Managing the guest operating system, including updates and security patches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When a customer runs a Docker container on Amazon EC2, AWS is responsible for the physical infrastructure layer, which includes performing hardware maintenance in the AWS facilities that run the AWS Cloud.\\nScaling the web application and services developed with Docker is the customer\'s responsibility, as EC2-based container workloads require the customer to configure scaling through services such as EC2 Auto Scaling or Amazon ECS. Provisioning or scheduling containers to run on clusters and maintaining their availability is also the customer\'s responsibility when running Docker directly on EC2, as the customer manages the container runtime and orchestration layer rather than AWS. Managing the guest operating system including updates and security patches is the customer\'s responsibility for EC2 instances, as the Shared Responsibility Model places operating system management firmly with the customer for IaaS services."},{"id":"q824","question":"Which actions represent best practices for using AWS IAM?","options":{"A":"Configure a strong password policy","B":"Share the security credentials among users of AWS accounts who are in the same Region","C":"Use access keys to log in to the AWS Management Console","D":"Rotate access keys on a regular basis","E":"Avoid using IAM roles to delegate permissions"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Applying least privilege permissions ensures IAM users and roles have only the access they need to perform their tasks, and using IAM roles for applications running on EC2 instances avoids embedding long-term credentials in code. Both are IAM security best practices.\\nSharing credentials between users contradicts IAM best practices; each user should have their own unique credentials to maintain individual accountability and allow precise permission scoping, and sharing credentials prevents tracking who performed which actions. Storing access keys in plaintext on EC2 instances is a security anti-pattern that risks credential exposure through instance metadata, log files, or shared access to the instance; IAM roles should be used instead to provide temporary credentials automatically. Using the root user account for all administrative tasks contradicts IAM best practices; the root account should be used only for tasks that specifically require it, and IAM users with appropriate permissions should be used for all regular administrative activities."},{"id":"q825","question":"Which AWS feature or service can be used to capture information about incoming and outgoing traffic in an AWS VPC infrastructure?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"VPC Flow Logs","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"VPC Flow Logs capture metadata about IP traffic flowing to and from network interfaces in an Amazon VPC, recording information about source and destination IP addresses, ports, protocols, and whether traffic was allowed or rejected.\\nAWS CloudTrail records API calls and management events across an AWS account, capturing who made requests and what changes were made to AWS resources, but does not capture network-level packet flow data between instances the way VPC Flow Logs do. AWS Config continuously monitors and records AWS resource configurations and evaluates them against compliance rules, and is a configuration state tracking service rather than a network traffic capture and logging service. Amazon CloudWatch collects operational metrics, logs, and events from AWS resources and applications for monitoring and alerting purposes, and while it can collect application-level logs, it does not capture the IP traffic flow data between network interfaces that VPC Flow Logs provide."},{"id":"q828","question":"Which service can be used to monitor and receive alerts for AWS account root user AWS Management Console sign-in events?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS IAM"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch can monitor for root user console sign-in events through CloudTrail integration, and can be configured to send alerts via SNS when the root user logs in, enabling security teams to detect and respond to unexpected root account access.\\nAWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events including root user sign-ins, and is the source of the sign-in event data, but does not itself send alerts or notifications when specific events occur without integration with CloudWatch Events or EventBridge. AWS Config continuously monitors resource configurations and evaluates compliance, and while it tracks resource state changes, it is not used to send real-time alerts for specific account login events such as root user console sign-ins. AWS Artifact provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports and certifications, and is a documentation portal with no capability to monitor or alert on AWS account activity or login events."},{"id":"q839","question":"Which tasks are the customer\'s responsibility in the AWS shared responsibility model?","options":{"A":"Infrastructure facilities access management","B":"Cloud infrastructure hardware lifecycle management","C":"Configuration management of user\'s applications","D":"Networking infrastructure protection","E":"Security groups configuration"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Customers are responsible for configuration management of their own applications, including how those applications are set up and maintained, and for configuring security groups that control network access to their resources. Both fall on the customer side of the shared responsibility model.\\nInfrastructure facilities access management refers to physical access controls for AWS data centers, which is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as customers have no access to or control over the physical facilities hosting their workloads. Cloud infrastructure hardware lifecycle management covers the procurement, maintenance, and decommissioning of physical servers and networking equipment, which AWS manages on behalf of all customers without any customer involvement. Networking infrastructure protection refers to securing the underlying global network that connects AWS Regions, Availability Zones, and edge locations, which is AWS\'s responsibility as part of its security of the cloud obligations."},{"id":"q843","question":"A web application is hosted on AWS using an Elastic Load Balancer, multiple Amazon EC2 instances, and Amazon RDS. Which security measures fall under the responsibility of AWS?","options":{"A":"Running a virus scan on EC2 instances","B":"Protecting against IP spoofing and packet sniffing","C":"Installing the latest security patches on the RDS instance","D":"Encrypting communication between the EC2 instances and the Elastic Load Balancer","E":"Configuring a security group and a network access control list (NACL) for EC2"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS is responsible for protecting against IP spoofing and packet sniffing at the network level as part of securing the infrastructure, and for patching the underlying operating system of managed RDS instances as RDS is a fully managed service where AWS handles database engine and OS patching.\\nRunning a virus scan on EC2 instances is the customer\'s responsibility as the customer manages the guest operating system and installed software on their EC2 instances, including security tooling to detect malicious software. Encrypting communication between EC2 instances and the Elastic Load Balancer requires customer configuration of SSL/TLS settings in the application and load balancer, as the customer controls their own application architecture and must choose to enable encrypted communications. Configuring security groups and network ACLs for EC2 instances is the customer\'s responsibility for defining which network traffic is allowed to and from their own compute resources within the VPC."},{"id":"q846","question":"A company needs an Amazon S3 bucket that cannot have any public objects due to compliance requirements. How can this be accomplished?","options":{"A":"Enable S3 Block Public Access from the AWS Management Console","B":"Hold a team meeting to discuss the importance of only uploading private S3 objects","C":"Require all S3 objects to be manually approved before uploading","D":"Create a service to monitor all S3 uploads and remove any public uploads"},"correct":"A","explanation":"S3 Block Public Access is an account-level or bucket-level setting that overrides any policies or ACLs that would grant public access, ensuring no objects in the bucket can be made public regardless of how they are uploaded.\\nHolding a team meeting relies on human awareness and voluntary compliance, which does not technically enforce the restriction and cannot guarantee that public objects will never be uploaded. Requiring manual approval before uploading is not a native S3 feature and introduces an unscalable manual process that still does not technically prevent public objects from being created. Creating a custom monitoring service to detect and remove public uploads introduces unnecessary complexity, delay between upload and remediation, and relies on reactive rather than preventive controls, making it far less reliable than the built-in Block Public Access setting."},{"id":"q849","question":"Who is responsible for patching the guest operating system for Amazon RDS?","options":{"A":"The AWS Product team","B":"The customer Database Administrator","C":"Managed partners","D":"AWS Support"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Patching the guest operating system for Amazon RDS is AWS\'s responsibility, as RDS is a fully managed database service where AWS handles all underlying infrastructure maintenance including OS patches and database engine updates.\\nThe customer is not responsible for patching the guest OS in Amazon RDS because RDS abstracts the infrastructure layer completely; unlike EC2 where customers manage the operating system, RDS instances have their OS managed and patched by AWS as part of the managed service model. The AWS compliance team does not patch RDS operating systems; patching is performed by the RDS service engineering team as part of routine managed service maintenance, not by a compliance function within AWS. AWS Trusted Advisor provides recommendations and best practice checks across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, but does not perform operational tasks such as patching database instance operating systems."},{"id":"q855","question":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, which of the following is a customer responsibility?","options":{"A":"Installing security patches for the Xen and KVM hypervisors","B":"Installing operating system patches for Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Installing operating system security patches for Amazon EC2 database instances","D":"Installing operating system security patches for Amazon RDS database instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Customers are responsible for installing operating system security patches on their Amazon EC2 instances, including any database software running on those instances, as the guest operating system falls within the customer responsibility boundary under the shared responsibility model.\\nInstalling security patches for the Xen and KVM hypervisors is an AWS responsibility, as AWS owns and manages the virtualization layer that sits between the physical hardware and the customer\'s instances, and customers have no access to or visibility of the hypervisor. Installing operating system patches for Amazon DynamoDB is an AWS responsibility, as DynamoDB is a fully managed service where AWS handles all underlying infrastructure, operating system, and database engine maintenance on the customer\'s behalf. Installing operating system security patches for Amazon RDS database instances is an AWS responsibility, as RDS is a fully managed relational database service where AWS manages the operating system and database engine patching, leaving customers responsible only for their data and application-level configurations."},{"id":"q857","question":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, the security and patching of the guest operating system is the responsibility of:","options":{"A":"AWS Support","B":"The customer","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Under the Shared Responsibility Model, the customer is responsible for security and patching of the guest operating system on EC2 instances, including applying security updates and configuring the OS, as AWS only manages the infrastructure beneath the hypervisor.\\nAWS Support provides technical assistance and guidance to customers experiencing issues with their AWS environment, but is not responsible for patching or securing operating systems on customer instances. AWS Systems Manager can assist customers in automating patch management tasks on EC2 instances, but it is a tool that helps customers fulfill their own patching responsibility rather than shifting that responsibility to AWS. AWS Config records and evaluates configuration changes to AWS resources for compliance purposes, and does not perform or take responsibility for operating system patching on EC2 instances."},{"id":"q858","question":"Which AWS service makes it easy to create and manage AWS users and groups, and provide them with secure access to AWS resources at no charge?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","D":"AWS Firewall Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Identity and Access Management is the service that allows you to create and manage AWS users and groups, assign permissions through policies, and control which users have access to which AWS services and resources securely.\\nAmazon Cognito is an identity service for web and mobile application users that enables sign-up, sign-in, and federated access with external identity providers, and is designed for application-level user management rather than managing AWS service access for administrators. AWS Single Sign-On, now called AWS IAM Identity Center, provides single sign-on access to multiple AWS accounts and applications using one set of credentials, and while it builds on IAM, the foundational service for creating and managing users and groups within AWS is IAM itself. AWS Directory Service provides managed Microsoft Active Directory for enterprise identity management and integrates with IAM for AWS console access, but the primary service for creating and managing the users, groups, and access controls used within AWS itself is IAM."},{"id":"q859","question":"Which AWS service provides on-demand access to AWS security and compliance documentation?","options":{"A":"AWS Directory Service","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Artifact is the self-service portal that provides on-demand access to AWS security and compliance documentation including SOC reports, ISO certifications, PCI attestations, and other compliance materials available directly from the AWS console.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor provides automated recommendations and checks across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance categories to help improve your AWS environment, and is an advisory service rather than a documentation and compliance report portal. Amazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and produces customer-specific assessment findings rather than AWS security and compliance documentation. Amazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics, logs, and events for AWS resources and applications, and is an observability and alerting service rather than a source of AWS compliance documentation."},{"id":"q862","question":"A user needs to generate a report that outlines the status of key security checks in an AWS account. The report must include the status of Amazon S3 bucket permissions, whether multi-factor authentication is enabled for the AWS account root user, if any security groups are configured to allow unrestricted access. Where can all this information be found in one location?","options":{"A":"Amazon QuickSight dashboard","B":"AWS CloudTrail trails","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor report","D":"IAM credential report"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor provides a report-style dashboard of check results across security, cost, performance, fault tolerance, and service limits, including key security checks such as MFA on root account, exposed access keys, and open security groups.\\nAmazon QuickSight is a business intelligence and data visualisation service for creating dashboards and reports from data sources, and while it can display data, it does not provide automated AWS security check results or account-level security status summaries. AWS CloudTrail trails record API calls and account activity for auditing purposes, and while they capture security-relevant events, they produce raw logs rather than a summarized status report of key security check findings. The IAM credential report lists all IAM users and the status of their credentials including password age, access key status, and MFA enablement, and while it is a useful security tool, it covers only IAM credential status rather than the broader set of key security checks that Trusted Advisor evaluates."},{"id":"q865","question":"Using AWS Config to record, audit, and evaluate changes to AWS resources to enable traceability is an example of which AWS Well-Architected Framework pillar?","options":{"A":"Security","B":"Operational excellence","C":"Performance efficiency","D":"Cost optimization"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Using AWS Config to record, audit, and evaluate changes to resources is an example of enabling traceability, which is a core design principle of the Security pillar focused on monitoring and detective controls.\\nOperational Excellence focuses on running and improving operations through automation and responding to events, not on detective monitoring of resource changes for traceability. Performance Efficiency focuses on using computing resources efficiently to meet workload requirements as demand changes over time. Cost Optimization focuses on eliminating unnecessary spend and selecting the right resource types to reduce overall costs."},{"id":"q873","question":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, which of the following are customer responsibilities?","options":{"A":"Setting up server-side encryption on an Amazon S3 bucket","B":"Amazon RDS instance patching","C":"Network and firewall configurations","D":"Physical security of data center facilities","E":"Compute capacity availability"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Customers are responsible for setting up server-side encryption on S3 buckets to protect data at rest, and for configuring network and firewall settings such as security groups and NACLs to control traffic to their resources. Both fall within the customer\'s side of the Shared Responsibility Model.\\nAmazon RDS instance patching is AWS\'s responsibility for managed database services, as AWS handles all engine-level patching and maintenance windows without customer involvement. Physical security of data center facilities is exclusively AWS\'s responsibility, covering the buildings, hardware, and environmental controls that customers have no access to. Compute capacity availability is AWS\'s responsibility, as AWS manages the underlying infrastructure to ensure that compute resources remain available for customers to provision and use."},{"id":"q880","question":"Which of the following acts as a virtual firewall at the Amazon EC2 instance level to control traffic for one or more instances?","options":{"A":"Access keys","B":"Virtual private gateways","C":"Security groups","D":"Access Control Lists (ACL)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Security groups act as a virtual firewall at the EC2 instance level, controlling inbound and outbound traffic for one or more instances by evaluating rules based on protocol, port, and source and destination.\\nAccess keys are long-term credentials consisting of an access key ID and secret used for programmatic access to AWS via the CLI or API, and are an authentication mechanism with no connection to network traffic control. Virtual private gateways are the AWS-side endpoint of a site-to-site VPN connection that enables encrypted communication between a VPC and an on-premises network, and are a connectivity component rather than a traffic filtering mechanism at the instance level. Access Control Lists operate as stateless firewalls at the subnet level rather than the instance level, applying inbound and outbound rules to all traffic entering or leaving a subnet without tracking connection state the way security groups do."},{"id":"q883","question":"Which AWS service or feature helps restrict the AWS services, resources, and individual API actions the users and roles in each member account can access?","options":{"A":"Amazon Cognito","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"AWS Shield","D":"AWS Firewall Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Organizations Service Control Policies allow organizations to define and enforce the maximum permissions available to all accounts within an organization or specific organizational units, restricting which AWS services, resources, and API actions users in those accounts can access.\\nAWS WAF is a web application firewall that filters HTTP and HTTPS traffic based on defined rules to block common web exploits such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting, and operates at the application traffic layer rather than restricting access to AWS services and API actions for IAM users. Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that monitors AWS accounts and workloads for malicious activity and anomalous behavior, and is a detection and alerting service rather than a service that restricts which AWS services and API actions users can invoke. AWS Firewall Manager centrally manages AWS WAF rules, Shield protections, and security group policies across multiple accounts in an organization, and is a security management layer rather than a service that enforces IAM-level API action restrictions."},{"id":"q884","question":"What is the best resource for a user to find compliance-related information and reports about AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Marketplace","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Support"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact is a self-service portal that provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports, certifications, and agreements, making it the best resource for users seeking compliance-related information and documentation about AWS.\\nAWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events in an AWS account for auditing and forensic purposes, and is an activity logging service rather than a source of compliance reports or certification documentation about AWS infrastructure. The Amazon Inspector console provides vulnerability assessment findings for EC2 instances and container images, and is a security assessment tool for customer workloads rather than a repository of AWS-level compliance information and reports. AWS Support provides technical assistance and guidance for using AWS services, and while support engineers can answer compliance-related questions, the self-service repository for compliance documentation and formal reports is AWS Artifact."},{"id":"q889","question":"A user would like to encrypt data that is received, stored, and managed by AWS CloudTrail. Which AWS service will provide this capability?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"AWS Systems Manager","C":"AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","D":"AWS Certificate Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS KMS manages encryption keys and integrates natively with CloudTrail to encrypt log files stored in S3, ensuring that CloudTrail log data is protected at rest using KMS-managed keys.\\nAWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for encrypting data in transit between applications and end users, and is a certificate management service rather than a service for encrypting data received and stored by CloudTrail. Amazon Macie uses machine learning to discover and classify sensitive data stored in S3, and while it can identify sensitive content in CloudTrail logs stored in S3, it does not perform the encryption of that data. AWS Shield provides managed DDoS protection at the network and transport layers, and is a traffic protection service with no capability to encrypt data stored by CloudTrail or any other AWS service."},{"id":"q895","question":"Which AWS service provides the ability to detect inadvertent data leaks of personally identifiable information (PII) and user credential data?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"Amazon Inspector","C":"Amazon Macie","D":"AWS Shield"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Macie uses machine learning to automatically discover and classify sensitive data in Amazon S3 including personally identifiable information and user credentials, detecting potential data leaks and inadvertent exposure of sensitive data.\\nAmazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that monitors AWS accounts and workloads for malicious activity and anomalous behavior using machine learning and threat intelligence, and focuses on detecting security threats rather than identifying specific sensitive data types stored in S3. Amazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and network misconfigurations, and does not classify or detect sensitive data such as PII stored in S3. AWS Shield provides managed DDoS protection at the network and transport layers, and is a traffic protection service with no capability to discover or detect data leaks of personally identifiable information."},{"id":"q898","question":"Which of the following describes a security best practice that can be implemented using AWS IAM?","options":{"A":"Disable AWS Management Console access for all users","B":"Generate secret keys for every IAM user","C":"Grant permissions to users who are required to perform a given task only","D":"Store AWS credentials within Amazon EC2 instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Disabling and removing unnecessary IAM user credentials including inactive access keys and old passwords reduces the attack surface by eliminating credentials that could be exploited if compromised, making it an IAM security best practice.\\nAssigning the same permissions to all IAM users regardless of their roles violates the principle of least privilege and creates unnecessary risk by giving every user access to resources they do not need, which is an anti-pattern rather than a security best practice. Creating access keys for all IAM users by default creates unnecessary long-term credentials for users who may only need console access, increasing the risk of credential exposure without a corresponding operational benefit. Using a single shared IAM user for all team members removes individual accountability and prevents identifying which specific person performed an action, contradicting the IAM best practice of using individual accounts with unique credentials."},{"id":"q901","question":"A company needs protection from expanded distributed denial of service (DDoS) attacks on its website and assistance from AWS experts during such events. Which AWS managed service will meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield Advanced","B":"AWS Firewall Manager","C":"AWS WAF","D":"Amazon GuardDuty"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Shield Advanced provides expanded DDoS protection with real-time attack visibility, access to the DDoS Response Team, and cost protection for scaling costs incurred during attacks, meeting the requirements for protection against expanded DDoS attacks with dedicated response assistance.\\nAWS WAF filters HTTP and HTTPS traffic at the application layer based on configurable rules, and while it helps mitigate application-layer attacks and integrates with Shield Advanced, it does not on its own provide the expanded DDoS protection and dedicated response team access that the question requires. Amazon GuardDuty detects threats and malicious activity in AWS accounts using machine learning, and while it can identify DDoS-related anomalies, it is a detection service rather than an active DDoS mitigation service with a dedicated response team. AWS Trusted Advisor provides automated security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance recommendations, and is an advisory tool with no capability to protect against or respond to distributed denial of service attacks."},{"id":"q903","question":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, what are the customer\'s responsibilities?","options":{"A":"Physical and environmental security","B":"Physical network devices including firewalls","C":"Storage device decommissioning","D":"Security of data in transit","E":"Data integrity authentication"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Customers are responsible for securing data in transit by implementing encryption such as TLS/SSL to protect data as it moves between systems, and for data integrity authentication to verify that data has not been tampered with. Both fall within the customer\'s side of the Shared Responsibility Model.\\nPhysical and environmental security of data centers is exclusively AWS\'s responsibility, covering the buildings, power, cooling, and physical access controls that customers have no involvement in. Physical network devices including firewalls are AWS\'s responsibility, as customers manage virtual networking constructs such as security groups and NACLs but have no access to the underlying physical network hardware. Storage device decommissioning is AWS\'s responsibility, as AWS manages the secure disposal and destruction of physical storage media when it reaches end of life."},{"id":"q906","question":"Which of the following allows an application running on an Amazon EC2 instance to securely write data to an Amazon S3 bucket without using long-term credentials?","options":{"A":"Amazon Cognito","B":"AWS Shield","C":"AWS IAM role","D":"AWS IAM user access key"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An IAM role attached to an EC2 instance provides temporary security credentials to applications running on that instance, allowing them to make authenticated AWS API calls to S3 without requiring hardcoded access keys in the application code.\\nAWS CloudTrail records API calls and management events for auditing purposes, and while it logs the S3 write operations, it does not itself grant the permissions or credentials that allow an application to authenticate and write data to S3. An S3 bucket policy can grant permissions for a specific EC2 instance to write to a bucket, but the instance still needs valid AWS credentials to authenticate those requests, and an IAM role attached to the instance is the recommended way to provide those credentials securely. An IAM user with programmatic access could provide access keys for the application to use, but storing long-term access keys in application code or on the instance is a security anti-pattern; IAM roles provide temporary credentials automatically and are the recommended approach."},{"id":"q912","question":"Which of the following tasks can only be performed after signing in with AWS account root user credentials?","options":{"A":"Closing an AWS account","B":"Creating a new IAM policy","C":"Changing AWS Support plans","D":"Attaching a role to an Amazon EC2 instance","E":"Generating access keys for IAM users"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Closing an AWS account and changing the AWS Support plan are tasks that can only be performed by the root user, as these are account-level actions that AWS reserves exclusively for the account owner to prevent unauthorised changes to fundamental account settings.\\nCreating a new IAM policy can be performed by any IAM user or role that has been granted the appropriate IAM permissions, and does not require root user credentials. Attaching a role to an Amazon EC2 instance can be performed by any IAM user or role with the necessary EC2 and IAM permissions, and is a routine operational task that does not require root access. Generating access keys for IAM users can be performed by IAM administrators with the appropriate permissions, and is not restricted to the root user account."},{"id":"q917","question":"A company is launching an e-commerce site that will store and process credit card data. The company requires information about AWS compliance reports and AWS agreements. Which AWS service provides on-demand access to these items?","options":{"A":"AWS Certificate Manager","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Artifact","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Artifact provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports such as PCI DSS and SOC reports, and AWS agreements such as the Business Associate Addendum, making it the purpose-built service for companies requiring compliance documentation when handling sensitive data like credit card information.\\nAWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for encrypting data in transit between applications and users, and is a certificate management service with no capability to provide compliance reports or AWS agreements. AWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration state of AWS resources to assess compliance against desired settings, and is a resource configuration auditing tool rather than a repository of AWS compliance documentation and agreements. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity across an AWS environment for auditing and compliance purposes, and captures operational audit logs rather than providing access to AWS compliance reports or legal agreements."},{"id":"q919","question":"Which AWS service can be used to track unauthorized API calls?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and account activity including unauthorised or unusual API calls, making it the correct service for tracking API calls that may indicate unauthorised access or activity in an AWS environment.\\nAmazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics, logs, and events for AWS resources and applications, and while CloudWatch can receive CloudTrail events and trigger alarms, it does not itself track or record API call history to identify unauthorised API actions. Amazon Detective analyzes and visualises security data from multiple sources to investigate the root cause of suspicious activity, and uses CloudTrail data as one of its inputs, but is an investigation tool that builds on top of CloudTrail rather than performing the original API call recording. AWS Trusted Advisor provides automated recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance categories, and is an advisory tool rather than a service that tracks or records API activity."},{"id":"q920","question":"A user needs to regularly audit and evaluate the setup of all AWS resources, identify non-compliant accounts, and be notified when a resource changes. Which AWS service can be used to meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Resource Access Manager","D":"AWS Systems Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Config continuously monitors resource configurations, evaluates them against compliance rules, tracks changes over time, and sends notifications when resources change or become non-compliant, making it the purpose-built service for ongoing audit, compliance evaluation, and change notification requirements.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides automated recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, but delivers periodic advisory checks rather than continuously tracking individual resource configuration changes and sending notifications when specific resources become non-compliant. AWS Resource Access Manager enables sharing of AWS resources such as subnets and Transit Gateways across AWS accounts within an organization, and is a resource sharing service with no capability to audit configurations or notify on compliance violations. AWS Systems Manager provides operational tools for managing and automating tasks across AWS resources including patching, parameter storage, and run commands, and while it can automate remediation it does not continuously evaluate resource configurations against compliance rules or send notifications when resources change."},{"id":"q924","question":"A user needs to automatically discover, classify, and protect sensitive data stored in Amazon S3. Which AWS service can meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"Amazon Macie","C":"Amazon GuardDuty","D":"AWS Secrets Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Macie uses machine learning to automatically discover, classify, and protect sensitive data including PII stored in Amazon S3, providing visibility into data security risks and detecting potential data leaks.\\nAmazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and does not discover or classify sensitive data stored in Amazon S3. Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that monitors AWS accounts and workloads for malicious activity and anomalous behavior using machine learning and threat intelligence, and is a threat detection service rather than a data classification and protection service for sensitive data in S3. AWS Secrets Manager stores, rotates, and manages access to secrets such as database credentials and API keys, and is a secrets lifecycle management service rather than a service for automatically discovering and classifying sensitive data stored in S3."},{"id":"q928","question":"What is the MOST effective resource for staying up to date on AWS security announcements?","options":{"A":"AWS Health Dashboard","B":"AWS Secrets Manager","C":"AWS Security Bulletins","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Security Bulletins provide timely notifications about newly discovered security vulnerabilities and recommended remediation actions for AWS services, making them the most effective resource for staying up to date on AWS security announcements.\\nThe AWS Health Dashboard provides personalized alerts about events that may affect a customer\'s specific AWS resources and service availability, and while it includes health events for security issues, the dedicated source for proactive AWS security announcements and vulnerability disclosures is the AWS Security Bulletins page. AWS Secrets Manager stores, rotates, and manages access to secrets such as database credentials and API keys, and is a credentials management service with no connection to security announcements or vulnerability notifications. Amazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities, and generates findings about the customer\'s own resources rather than providing AWS-level security announcements."},{"id":"q931","question":"Which AWS service allows users to download security and compliance reports about the AWS infrastructure on demand?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"AWS Security Hub","C":"AWS Artifact","D":"AWS Shield"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Artifact provides on-demand access to AWS security and compliance reports including SOC, PCI DSS, ISO, and HIPAA documentation, available for download directly from the AWS Management Console without contacting AWS.\\nAmazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics and logs for AWS resources and applications, and is an observability and alerting service rather than a repository of AWS security and compliance reports. AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events across an AWS account for auditing purposes, and produces customer-specific activity logs rather than AWS security certification and compliance documentation. Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors accounts and workloads for malicious activity, and is a real-time security monitoring service rather than a source of downloadable compliance reports."},{"id":"q936","question":"During a compliance review, an auditor requests AWS compliance documentation such as SOC 2 reports. Which AWS service provides on-demand access to these documents?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Config","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports and certifications such as SOC 2 documents, allowing auditors and customers to download them directly from the console without requesting them through AWS Support.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor provides automated recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance categories for improving an AWS environment, and is an advisory tool rather than a compliance documentation repository. AWS Config continuously monitors and records resource configurations against compliance rules, and is a resource configuration compliance evaluation service rather than a portal for downloading AWS-level compliance certifications. Amazon S3 is an object storage service for storing and retrieving data, and while AWS Artifact downloads documents stored in S3 on the backend, S3 itself is not the portal customers use to find and download compliance documentation."},{"id":"q941","question":"A company wants to allow full access to an Amazon S3 bucket for a particular user. Which element in the S3 bucket policy holds the user details that describe who needs access to the S3 bucket?","options":{"A":"Principal","B":"Action","C":"Resource","D":"Statement"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Principal element in an S3 bucket policy specifies who is allowed or denied access, and holds the user, account, role, or service details that describe the identity being granted or denied permissions.\\nAction specifies which S3 operations are permitted or denied, such as s3:GetObject or s3:PutObject, and defines what the principal can do rather than identifying who the principal is. Resource specifies which S3 bucket or objects the policy applies to, identifying the target of the policy rather than the identity being granted access. Statement is the top-level container element that wraps the individual policy blocks including Principal, Action, Effect, and Resource, and is the structural wrapper rather than the element that holds user details."},{"id":"q945","question":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, which of the following is a responsibility of AWS?","options":{"A":"Enabling server-side encryption for objects stored in S3","B":"Applying AWS IAM security policies","C":"Patching the operating system on an Amazon EC2 instance","D":"Applying updates to the hypervisor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS is responsible for the security of the underlying infrastructure including the data center physical security, hardware, and the software that runs the hypervisor and global network components, as these are outside the customer\'s control.\\nManaging application access rights for users accessing customer-built applications is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers control who can access their applications through application-level authentication and authorisation mechanisms. Keeping the operating system on Amazon EC2 instances up to date by applying security patches is the customer\'s responsibility, as the guest OS running inside EC2 virtual machines must be maintained by the customer who has administrative access to it. Configuring client-side data encryption to protect data before it is sent to AWS services is the customer\'s responsibility for data that requires end-to-end protection, as the customer controls the encryption process on their own systems before data leaves their environment."},{"id":"q954","question":"Which AWS service can be used to track resource changes and establish compliance?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Config continuously monitors and records configuration changes to AWS resources and evaluates those changes against compliance rules, identifying non-compliant resources and enabling auditing of the configuration history for compliance purposes.\\nAmazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics, logs, and events for performance and observability, and while it can trigger alarms on unusual activity, it does not track resource configuration changes or evaluate compliance against desired configuration states. AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events for auditing, and while it logs who made configuration changes and when, it does not evaluate whether those configurations comply with defined rules the way Config does. Amazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service for EC2 instances and container images that identifies software vulnerabilities and unintended network access, and is not a resource configuration tracking and compliance evaluation service."},{"id":"q959","question":"A developer has been hired by a large company and needs AWS credentials. Which are security best practices that should be followed?","options":{"A":"Grant the developer access to only the AWS resources needed to perform the job","B":"Share the AWS account root user credentials with the developer","C":"Add the developer to the administrator\'s group in AWS IAM","D":"Configure a password policy that ensures the developer\'s password cannot be changed","E":"Ensure the account password policy requires a minimum length"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Granting the developer access only to the resources needed follows the principle of least privilege, and ensuring a minimum password length enforces credential security requirements. Both are IAM security best practices for onboarding new developers.\\nSharing the AWS account root user credentials with any individual is a critical security violation, as the root user has unrestricted access to all AWS services and resources and should be protected and used only for tasks that specifically require it. Adding the developer to the administrator\'s group grants unrestricted access to all AWS services and resources, which directly violates the principle of least privilege and is not a security best practice for a new developer whose access requirements are specific to their role. Configuring a password policy that prevents the developer from changing their own password is a security anti-pattern, as the ability to change passwords is necessary for credential hygiene and periodic rotation."},{"id":"q962","question":"A user is planning to migrate an application workload to the AWS Cloud. Which control becomes the responsibility of AWS once the migration is complete?","options":{"A":"Patching the guest operating system","B":"Maintaining physical and environmental controls","C":"Protecting communications and maintaining zone security","D":"Patching specific applications"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Maintaining physical and environmental controls, including data center power, cooling, fire suppression, and physical security, becomes AWS\'s responsibility once a workload is migrated to the cloud.\\nPatching the guest operating system remains the customer\'s responsibility after migration to AWS. For services such as EC2, customers retain full control over the operating system and are responsible for applying updates and patches. Protecting communications and maintaining zone security is a shared responsibility that customers retain ownership of, including configuring network security controls such as security groups, network ACLs, and encryption in transit for their applications. Patching specific applications is always the customer\'s responsibility regardless of the deployment model, as AWS has no visibility into the application software customers choose to run on their infrastructure."},{"id":"q969","question":"A company wants to ensure that AWS Management Console users are meeting password complexity requirements. How can the company configure password complexity?","options":{"A":"Using an AWS IAM user policy","B":"Using an AWS Organizations service control policy (SCP)","C":"Using an AWS IAM account password policy","D":"Using an AWS Security Hub managed insight"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IAM password policies allow administrators to enforce password complexity requirements such as minimum length, character type requirements, and expiration intervals, ensuring all IAM users must create passwords that meet the defined standards.\\nAWS Secrets Manager stores, rotates, and manages access to secrets such as database credentials and API keys, and is a secrets lifecycle management service rather than a tool for setting password complexity requirements for AWS Management Console users. Amazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and has no capability to enforce password requirements for AWS console users. AWS Directory Service provides managed Microsoft Active Directory for enterprise identity management and domain joining, and while it can enforce password policies for Active Directory users, the correct service for enforcing password requirements for IAM users accessing the AWS Management Console is the IAM password policy."},{"id":"q970","question":"Under the AWS shared responsibility model, which of the following is the customer\'s responsibility?","options":{"A":"Patching guest OS and applications","B":"Patching and fixing flaws in the infrastructure","C":"Physical and environmental controls","D":"Configuration of AWS infrastructure devices"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Managing application data encryption and configuring security for their own applications are customer responsibilities under the shared responsibility model, as customers control their own data and application layer while AWS manages the underlying infrastructure.\\nSecuring the infrastructure that runs all AWS Cloud services including physical servers, networking hardware, and data center facilities is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as part of the security of the cloud. Providing the physical security of global data centers including perimeter access controls, surveillance, and environmental protections is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as customers never have access to the physical facilities. Maintaining the hardware that provides the foundation of the global infrastructure including servers, storage devices, and networking equipment is entirely AWS\'s responsibility as part of owning and operating the physical cloud infrastructure."},{"id":"q971","question":"Which of the following tasks is required to deploy a PCI-compliant workload on AWS?","options":{"A":"Use any AWS service and implement PCI controls at the application layer","B":"Use an AWS service that is in-scope for PCI compliance and raise an AWS support ticket to enable PCI compliance at the application layer","C":"Use any AWS service and raise an AWS support ticket to enable PCI compliance on that service","D":"Use an AWS service that is in scope for PCI compliance and apply PCI controls at the application layer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Deploying applications on the correct services in compliance with PCI DSS requirements is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers must choose PCI-eligible AWS services and configure them correctly to meet the applicable PCI DSS controls on the customer\'s side of the shared responsibility model.\\nAWS does not ensure that all physical facilities and data centers meet PCI requirements on the customer\'s behalf; rather, AWS obtains and maintains PCI DSS certification for its infrastructure, and this certification is available to customers through AWS Artifact as evidence that the underlying platform is PCI-compliant. AWS does not patch all AWS systems used in the environment on the customer\'s behalf; for managed services AWS handles patching, but for services like EC2 the customer is responsible for patching the guest operating system and any installed software. Configuring network ACLs on AWS on the customer\'s behalf is not an AWS responsibility; customers configure their own VPC network ACLs to control traffic as part of their PCI-compliant network security architecture."},{"id":"q977","question":"A web developer is concerned that a DDoS attack could target an application. Which AWS services or features can help protect against such an attack?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"Amazon CloudFront","D":"AWS Support Center","E":"AWS Health Dashboard"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS Shield provides automatic DDoS mitigation at the network and transport layers, and Amazon CloudFront absorbs and distributes traffic at edge locations, reducing the impact of volumetric attacks before they reach the application origin. Both help protect against DDoS attacks.\\nAWS CloudTrail records API calls and management events for auditing purposes, and while it logs account activity, it has no capability to detect or mitigate active DDoS attacks against a web application. AWS Support Center is a portal for submitting and managing support cases, and is not a technical service that provides protection against or mitigation of DDoS attacks. The AWS Health Dashboard provides real-time and historical status information about AWS services and personalized event alerts, and is a monitoring and notification tool rather than a service that actively protects applications from DDoS attacks."},{"id":"q978","question":"Which AWS service gives users on-demand, self-service access to AWS compliance control reports?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon GuardDuty","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS Artifact"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Artifact provides on-demand, self-service access to AWS compliance control reports including SOC, PCI, ISO, and HIPAA documentation directly from the AWS Management Console without requiring a request through AWS Support.\\nAWS Config tracks the configuration state of resources and audits how those configurations change over time, and is a resource configuration auditing service rather than a portal for downloading compliance control reports. Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that uses machine learning to monitor accounts and workloads for malicious activity, and is security focused rather than a compliance documentation portal. AWS Trusted Advisor inspects an account and provides best practice recommendations across cost optimization, performance, security, fault tolerance, and service limits, and is an advisory tool rather than a source of compliance control reports."},{"id":"q979","question":"A company wants to provide one of its employees with access to Amazon RDS. The company also wants to limit the interaction to only the AWS CLI and AWS software development kits (SDKs). Which combination of actions should the company take to meet these requirements while following the principles of least privilege?","options":{"A":"Create an IAM user and provide AWS Management Console access only","B":"Create an IAM user and provide programmatic access only","C":"Create an IAM role and provide AWS Management Console access only","D":"Create an IAM policy with administrator access and attach it to the IAM user","E":"Create an IAM policy with Amazon RDS access and attach it to the IAM user"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"An IAM policy that grants Amazon RDS access can be attached directly to the specific IAM user, limiting that employee\'s AWS permissions to only the RDS actions specified in the policy and no other services.\\nCreating a separate AWS account for the user would be an excessive measure for granting one employee RDS access, as it introduces administrative overhead for account management and does not simplify access control compared to using an IAM policy within the existing account. Creating an IAM user with full administrator access would grant unrestricted access to all AWS services and resources, directly violating the principle of least privilege and creating unnecessary security risk for a user who only needs access to RDS. Placing all existing IAM users into a new IAM group with RDS access would grant RDS permissions to the entire user population rather than limiting access to the single employee who requires it, which contradicts the goal of providing targeted access."},{"id":"q980","question":"A company has a compliance requirement to record and evaluate configuration changes, as well as perform remediation actions on AWS resources. Which AWS service should the company use?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS Secrets Manager","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Config records configuration changes, evaluates them against compliance rules, and can trigger automated remediation actions when resources become non-compliant, meeting all three requirements of recording, evaluating, and remediating.\\nAWS Secrets Manager stores, rotates, and manages access to secrets such as database credentials and API keys, and is a credentials management service with no capability to record configuration changes or trigger compliance remediation. AWS CloudTrail logs all API calls and account activity for auditing purposes, and while it records who made changes and when, it does not evaluate resources against compliance rules or trigger remediation actions. AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and offers advisory guidance rather than automated compliance rule evaluation or remediation capability."},{"id":"q984","question":"Which of the following is the customer\'s responsibility when using Amazon RDS?","options":{"A":"Patching the operating system of underlying hardware","B":"Controlling traffic to and from the database through security groups","C":"Running backups that enable point-in-time recovery of a DB instance","D":"Replacing failed DB instances"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Patching the database and operating system on EC2-hosted databases is the customer\'s responsibility when using Amazon RDS is a managed service where AWS handles patching; however the question asks about RDS specifically. Under the RDS model, configuring security groups to control network access to the RDS instance is the customer\'s responsibility as customers define which EC2 instances and IP addresses can connect to their database.\\nInstalling the database software on the underlying server is not the customer\'s responsibility for Amazon RDS; AWS manages the database engine installation, configuration, and updates as part of the fully managed service offering. Patching the underlying operating system of the RDS instance is AWS\'s responsibility; RDS is a managed service where AWS performs OS and database engine patching automatically without customer involvement. Replacing failed database hardware is AWS\'s responsibility as part of managing the physical infrastructure; customers benefit from AWS\'s hardware redundancy and replacement processes transparently without any action required on their part."},{"id":"q985","question":"What is the customer\'s responsibility when using AWS Lambda?","options":{"A":"Operating system configuration","B":"Application management","C":"Platform management","D":"Code encryption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service where AWS manages the underlying infrastructure, and the customer\'s responsibility is limited to managing their application code, including writing, deploying, and maintaining the functions that run on the platform.\\nOperating system configuration is AWS\'s responsibility for Lambda, as the service abstracts away the underlying compute infrastructure entirely and customers have no access to or control over the operating system. Platform management is also AWS\'s responsibility for Lambda, as AWS handles the runtime environment, scaling, availability, and maintenance of the serverless platform on the customer\'s behalf. Code encryption is not a customer responsibility in the sense described, as AWS encrypts Lambda function code at rest by default, and while customers can optionally use their own KMS keys, encryption is handled automatically by AWS rather than being a customer-managed task."},{"id":"q992","question":"Which AWS service should a cloud practitioner use to identify security vulnerabilities of an AWS account?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"Amazon Cognito","C":"Amazon Macie","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor identifies potential security vulnerabilities by checking for common misconfigurations such as unrestricted security group access, missing MFA on the root account, and publicly accessible S3 buckets, helping customers address security weaknesses proactively.\\nAmazon Detective is an investigation service that analyzes security data from GuardDuty, CloudTrail, and VPC Flow Logs to help security teams investigate the root cause of suspicious activity, and is a forensic investigation tool rather than a service for identifying security vulnerabilities in an AWS account configuration. Amazon Macie uses machine learning to discover and classify sensitive data in Amazon S3, and is a data classification service for detecting potential data exposure rather than a tool for auditing account-wide security configurations and vulnerabilities. AWS Shield provides managed DDoS protection at the network and transport layers, and is a traffic protection service rather than a security vulnerability identification tool."},{"id":"q997","question":"Which AWS service can be used to retrieve compliance reports on demand?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Security Hub","D":"AWS Certificate Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Artifact provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports including SOC, PCI, ISO, and other certifications, available for download directly from the AWS Management Console.\\nAWS Secrets Manager stores, rotates, and manages access to secrets such as database credentials and API keys, and is a credentials management service with no capability to retrieve or provide compliance documentation. AWS Security Hub provides a centralized view of security alerts and compliance status across an AWS environment by aggregating findings from multiple security services, and is a security posture management tool rather than a repository of AWS compliance reports. AWS Certificate Manager provisions and manages SSL/TLS certificates for encrypting data in transit between applications and users, and is a certificate management service with no connection to compliance report retrieval."},{"id":"q998","question":"A company has an AWS-hosted website located behind an Application Load Balancer. The company wants to safeguard the website from SQL injection or cross-site scripting. Which AWS service should the company use?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"AWS WAF","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS WAF protects web applications behind Application Load Balancers from common exploits like SQL injection and cross-site scripting by filtering malicious HTTP requests based on custom rules.\\nAmazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors AWS accounts and workloads for malicious activity and anomalous behavior, and is a detection and alerting service rather than a tool for filtering malicious web requests at the application layer. AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and is an advisory tool with no capability to intercept or block malicious web traffic. Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and is a vulnerability scanning tool rather than a web traffic filtering service."},{"id":"q1001","question":"An external auditor has requested that a company provide a list of all its IAM users, including the status of users\' credentials and access keys. What is the SIMPLEST way to provide this information?","options":{"A":"Create an IAM user account for the auditor, granting the auditor administrator permissions","B":"Take a screenshot of each user\'s page in the AWS Management Console, then provide the screenshots to the auditor","C":"Download the IAM credential report, then provide the report to the auditor","D":"Download the AWS Trusted Advisor report, then provide the report to the auditor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The IAM credential report is a downloadable CSV file that lists all IAM users in the account along with the status of their credentials including passwords, access keys, MFA devices, and last-used timestamps, providing exactly what is needed for a user access audit.\\nAWS Config continuously monitors and records resource configuration changes and evaluates them against compliance rules, and is a resource configuration compliance tool rather than an IAM user listing service. Amazon CloudWatch monitors operational metrics and logs for AWS resources and applications, and is an observability service rather than a source of IAM user account information or credential status reports. AWS CloudTrail records all API calls and management events including IAM-related actions, and while it logs who created users and when, it does not provide a current consolidated list of all users and their credential status in the way the credential report does."},{"id":"q1004","question":"A company wants to track AWS resource configuration changes for compliance reasons. Which AWS feature can be used to meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","B":"AWS Organizations service control policies (SCPs)","C":"AWS Config rules","D":"VPC Flow Logs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Config continuously monitors and records resource configuration changes and evaluates them against compliance rules, generating a complete history of configuration changes that auditors and compliance teams can use to demonstrate adherence to standards.\\nAWS CloudTrail records all API calls and account activity for auditing and forensic purposes, and while it captures who made configuration changes and when, it does not evaluate whether those configurations comply with defined rules or provide the configuration state history that Config does. Amazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service for EC2 instances and container images that identifies software vulnerabilities and network misconfigurations, and is a security scanning tool rather than a resource configuration tracking and compliance evaluation feature. AWS Trusted Advisor provides automated recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance categories, and is an advisory tool rather than a resource configuration change tracking and compliance evaluation service."},{"id":"q1006","question":"The AWS IAM best practice for granting least privilege is to:","options":{"A":"Apply an IAM policy to an IAM group and limit the size of the group","B":"Require multi-factor authentication (MFA) for all IAM users","C":"Require each IAM user who has different permissions to have multiple passwords","D":"Apply an IAM policy only to IAM users who require it"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The principle of least privilege requires granting only the permissions necessary for a user to perform their specific job function, which means applying IAM policies only to the users who actually require those permissions rather than broadly assigning permissions across all users.\\nApplying an IAM policy to an IAM group and limiting the size of the group addresses group management rather than least privilege, as the size of a group has no bearing on whether permissions are appropriately scoped to what users actually need. Requiring MFA for all IAM users is an authentication security best practice that protects against compromised credentials, but it does not relate to scoping permissions to the minimum required level. Requiring IAM users with different permissions to have multiple passwords conflates authentication with authorisation, and having multiple passwords does not control or limit what actions a user is permitted to perform."},{"id":"q1010","question":"Which AWS service or feature can be used to prevent SQL injection attacks?","options":{"A":"Security groups","B":"Network ACLs","C":"AWS WAF","D":"IAM policy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS WAF is a web application firewall that inspects HTTP and HTTPS traffic and can be configured with rules to detect and block SQL injection attempts before they reach the application.\\nAmazon VPC provides network isolation and security controls at the infrastructure level through security groups and network ACLs, but does not inspect application-layer HTTP traffic content for SQL injection patterns the way WAF does. AWS Shield provides managed DDoS protection at the network and transport layers to defend against volumetric attacks, and is not designed to inspect or block application-layer exploits such as SQL injection. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and management events for auditing purposes, and while it logs activity in the AWS environment, it does not inspect web application traffic or block SQL injection attacks."},{"id":"q1104","question":"A company needs to continuously assess its AWS environment against compliance frameworks such as PCI DSS and GDPR and automatically collect evidence for audits. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Audit Manager","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Audit Manager continuously collects evidence from AWS services, maps it to compliance framework controls such as PCI DSS and GDPR, and organizes it into audit-ready reports, automating much of the manual effort involved in compliance assessments.\\nAWS Artifact provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports and certifications such as SOC and ISO documents, but it does not assess a customer\'s own environment or collect evidence for customer-specific compliance audits. AWS Config records and evaluates resource configurations against compliance rules, and while it contributes evidence that Audit Manager can consume, it does not itself organize findings into structured audit reports mapped to compliance frameworks. Amazon Inspector scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and is a vulnerability scanning tool rather than a compliance audit evidence collection service."},{"id":"q1105","question":"Which AWS service helps simplify audit preparation by automatically collecting and organizing evidence from AWS usage and activity into assessment reports?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"AWS Audit Manager","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS Security Hub"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Audit Manager automates evidence collection from multiple AWS sources including CloudTrail logs, Config evaluations, and Security Hub findings, and organizes this evidence into assessment reports that map directly to compliance framework requirements, significantly reducing audit preparation effort.\\nAWS CloudTrail records API calls and management events across an AWS account for auditing and forensic analysis, but it captures raw activity logs rather than organizing them into structured compliance assessment reports. AWS Trusted Advisor provides automated best-practice checks across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance categories, but delivers advisory recommendations rather than structured audit evidence mapped to compliance frameworks. AWS Security Hub aggregates and prioritizes security findings from multiple AWS services into a centralized dashboard, but it is a security posture management tool rather than a compliance audit preparation and evidence collection service."},{"id":"q001","question":"AWS allows users to manage their resources using a web based user interface. What is the name of this interface?","options":{"A":"AWS CLI","B":"AWS API","C":"AWS SDK","D":"AWS Management Console"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The AWS Management Console is the web-based graphical interface used to manage and interact with AWS resources directly from a browser.\\nThe AWS CLI is a command-line tool that allows users to interact with AWS services through typed commands in a terminal, not through a web-based graphical interface. The AWS API provides programmatic access to AWS services through HTTP requests made directly by applications or scripts, and has no graphical user interface. The AWS SDK provides libraries and tools for interacting with AWS services through application code in languages such as Python, Java, and JavaScript, and is not a user-facing interface for managing resources."},{"id":"q002","question":"Which of the following is an example of horizontal scaling in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Replacing an existing EC2 instance with a larger, more powerful one","B":"Increasing the compute capacity of a single EC2 instance to address the growing demands of an application","C":"Adding more RAM capacity to an EC2 instance","D":"Adding more EC2 instances of the same size to handle an increase in traffic"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Horizontal scaling means adding more instances of the same size to distribute load, while vertical scaling means increasing the capacity of a single instance.\\nReplacing an existing instance with a larger one is vertical scaling, not horizontal. Increasing the compute capacity of a single instance describes vertical scaling, which is the opposite of horizontal scaling. Adding more RAM to a single instance is another form of vertical scaling, as it increases the power of one instance rather than adding more instances."},{"id":"q007","question":"A company has developed an eCommerce web application on AWS. What should they do to ensure that the application has the highest level of availability?","options":{"A":"Deploy the application across multiple Availability Zones and edge locations","B":"Deploy the application across multiple Availability Zones and subnets","C":"Deploy the application across multiple Regions and Availability Zones","D":"Deploy the application across multiple VPCs and subnets"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Deploying across multiple Regions and Availability Zones provides the highest level of availability by protecting against both Availability Zone-level failures and Region-level failures simultaneously.\\nDeploying across multiple Availability Zones and edge locations adds content delivery reach through CloudFront but does not protect application infrastructure against a Regional failure. Deploying across multiple Availability Zones and subnets improves resilience against isolated failures within a Region, but a Regional outage would still affect the entire deployment. Deploying across multiple VPCs and subnets creates network segmentation within a Region but provides no geographic redundancy against Availability Zone or Regional failures."},{"id":"q008","question":"What does AWS Snowball Edge provide?","options":{"A":"Built-in computing capabilities that allow customers to process data locally","B":"A catalog of third-party software solutions that customers need to build solutions and run their businesses","C":"A hybrid cloud storage between on-premises environments and the AWS Cloud","D":"An Exabyte-scale data transfer service that allows you to move extremely large amounts of data to AWS","E":"Secure transfer of large amounts of data into and out of AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS Snowball Edge includes built-in compute capabilities that allow customers to process and analyze data locally before transferring it to AWS, and securely transfers large amounts of data into and out of AWS using a rugged physical appliance.\\nA catalog of third-party software solutions describes AWS Marketplace, which is a digital catalog for finding and deploying software from independent vendors. A hybrid cloud storage between on-premises environments and AWS describes AWS Storage Gateway, which provides ongoing hybrid connectivity between local environments and AWS cloud storage. An exabyte-scale data transfer service describes AWS Snowmobile, which is a separate Snow Family service designed for migrating extremely large datasets using a secure shipping container rather than an appliance."},{"id":"q012","question":"A company has decided to migrate its Oracle database to AWS. Which AWS service can help achieve this without negatively impacting the functionality of the source database?","options":{"A":"AWS OpsWorks","B":"AWS Database Migration Service","C":"AWS Server Migration Service","D":"AWS Application Discovery Service"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Database Migration Service is purpose-built for migrating databases to AWS with minimal downtime, keeping the source database fully operational throughout the migration process.\\nAWS OpsWorks (deprecated) is a configuration management service using Chef and Puppet for automating server configuration and has no capability for database migration. AWS Server Migration Service is designed for migrating virtual machines from on-premises environments to AWS, not for migrating databases. AWS Application Discovery Service helps plan migrations by collecting data about on-premises servers and dependencies, but does not perform the actual migration of databases or any other workloads."},{"id":"q018","question":"What should you do in order to keep the data on EBS volumes safe?","options":{"A":"Create EBS snapshots","B":"Ensure that EBS data is encrypted at rest","C":"Replicate EBS volumes across multiple Availability Zones","D":"Use AWS Backup to automate and manage backup schedules","E":"Increase the size of the EBS volume regularly"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"EBS snapshots create point-in-time backups of your volume stored durably in Amazon S3, allowing data to be restored if a volume is lost or corrupted. Enabling encryption at rest protects the data stored on EBS volumes from unauthorised access using AWS KMS managed keys.\\nReplicating EBS volumes across multiple Availability Zones improves availability but does not protect against accidental deletion or data corruption, as a replicated volume would reflect the same corrupted state. Using AWS Backup automates backup management across multiple AWS services but is not itself a direct method of keeping EBS data safe without also taking snapshots. Increasing the size of an EBS volume adds storage capacity but has no effect on data protection or security."},{"id":"q021","question":"What does Amazon CloudFront use to distribute content to global users with low latency?","options":{"A":"AWS Global Accelerator","B":"AWS Regions","C":"AWS Edge Locations","D":"AWS Availability Zones"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CloudFront delivers content from edge locations, a global network of points of presence that cache content close to end users to minimize latency.\\nAWS Global Accelerator also uses the AWS global network to improve performance for global users, but it routes traffic to optimal regional endpoints rather than caching content at edge locations, making it a different service with a different mechanism. AWS Regions are geographic areas containing multiple Availability Zones where customers deploy workloads, and CloudFront does not distribute content by routing requests to a Region in the way a standard application deployment would. AWS Availability Zones are isolated data center clusters within a Region used for redundancy and fault tolerance, and are not the mechanism CloudFront uses to cache and serve content to global users."},{"id":"q024","question":"The identification process of an online financial services company requires that new users must complete an online interview with their security team. The completed recorded interviews are only required in the event of a legal issue or a regulatory compliance breach. What is the most cost-effective service to store the recorded videos?","options":{"A":"S3 Intelligent-Tiering","B":"Amazon S3 Standard","C":"Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive","D":"Amazon EBS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive is the lowest-cost AWS storage class, designed for data that is rarely accessed and can tolerate retrieval times of hours, making it ideal for compliance archives that may never be needed.\\nS3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves data between access tiers based on usage patterns, but it is better suited for data with unpredictable access frequency rather than data that is known upfront to be rarely accessed. Amazon S3 Standard provides low-latency, high-throughput object storage for frequently accessed data, making it more expensive than necessary for videos that will rarely if ever be retrieved. Amazon EBS provides block storage volumes attached to EC2 instances for active workloads, and is not a cost-effective solution for long-term archival of infrequently accessed video files."},{"id":"q025","question":"Which service provides DNS in the AWS cloud?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon CloudFront","D":"Amazon EMR"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 is the scalable and highly available Domain Name System service provided by AWS, used to route end users to applications by translating domain names to IP addresses.\\nAWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration of AWS resources to assess compliance and detect changes, and has no DNS or traffic routing capability. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and distributes content at edge locations worldwide to reduce latency, and while it works alongside Route 53 in many architectures it is a content delivery service rather than a DNS service. Amazon EMR is a managed cluster platform for processing large volumes of data using big data frameworks such as Hadoop and Spark, and has no connection to DNS or network routing."},{"id":"q027","question":"A company is deploying a new two-tier web application in AWS. Where should the most frequently accessed data be stored so that the application\'s response time is optimal?","options":{"A":"AWS OpsWorks","B":"AWS Storage Gateway","C":"Amazon EBS volume","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon ElastiCache is an in-memory caching service that provides sub-millisecond response times for frequently accessed data, making it the purpose-built solution for optimizing application response time in a two-tier web architecture.\\nAWS OpsWorks (deprecated) is a configuration management service that automates server setup and maintenance using Chef and Puppet, and has no capability to store data or improve application response times through caching. AWS Storage Gateway connects on-premises environments to AWS cloud storage for hybrid storage integration, and is a storage bridging service rather than a low-latency caching layer for web application data. Amazon EBS provides persistent block storage volumes that attach directly to EC2 instances for low-latency disk access, and while it offers faster access than object storage, it does not provide the sub-millisecond in-memory performance that ElastiCache delivers for frequently accessed data."},{"id":"q030","question":"Which of the following can be described as a global content delivery network (CDN) service?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS Regions","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront is AWS\'s global CDN service that caches and delivers content from edge locations worldwide, reducing latency for end users regardless of their location.\\nAWS VPN creates encrypted network connections between on-premises infrastructure and AWS over the public internet, and has no content delivery or caching capability. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network link between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and is a connectivity service rather than a content delivery network. AWS Regions are geographic areas containing multiple Availability Zones where AWS infrastructure is hosted, and are a component of global infrastructure rather than a CDN service."},{"id":"q033","question":"Your company has a data store application that requires access to a NoSQL database. Which AWS database offering would meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon Aurora","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon Elastic Block Store","D":"Amazon Redshift"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL key-value and document database designed for applications that need fast, flexible, schema-less data storage.\\nAmazon Aurora is a fully managed relational database compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL that uses structured schemas and SQL, and does not support NoSQL data models. Amazon Elastic Block Store provides block storage volumes that attach to EC2 instances for use as persistent disks, and is a storage service with no database capability of any kind. Amazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse service optimized for running analytical SQL queries across large datasets, and is not a NoSQL database."},{"id":"q039","question":"In order to implement best practices when dealing with a “Single Point of Failure,” you should attempt to build as much automation as possible in both detecting and reacting to failure. Which of the following AWS services would help?","options":{"A":"ELB","B":"Auto Scaling","C":"Amazon Athena","D":"ECR","E":"Amazon EC2"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Elastic Load Balancing automatically detects unhealthy instances and stops sending traffic to them, while Auto Scaling automatically replaces failed instances, together eliminating single points of failure through automated detection and recovery.\\nAmazon Athena is a serverless query service for analyzing data stored in S3 using standard SQL and has no role in detecting or recovering from infrastructure failures. Amazon Elastic Container Registry is a managed container image registry for storing and managing Docker images, unrelated to failure detection or automated recovery. Amazon EC2 provides the virtual server instances themselves but does not natively automate the detection of or response to failures without ELB and Auto Scaling configured around it."},{"id":"q040","question":"A company is planning to host an educational website on AWS. Their video courses will be streamed all around the world. Which of the following AWS services will help achieve high transfer speeds?","options":{"A":"Amazon SNS","B":"Amazon Kinesis Video Streams","C":"AWS CloudFormation","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront is a CDN that caches video content at edge locations around the world, delivering it to viewers from the nearest location and achieving high transfer speeds globally.\\nAmazon SNS is a pub/sub messaging service for sending notifications to multiple subscribers and has no role in delivering or streaming video content globally. Amazon Kinesis Video Streams ingests and processes live video streams from devices for real-time analysis and storage, but is not a content delivery service for distributing pre-recorded video to global audiences with high transfer speeds. AWS CloudFormation is an infrastructure-as-code service for provisioning AWS resources through templates, and has no capability to deliver or accelerate content to global users."},{"id":"q041","question":"A developer is planning to build a two-tier web application that has a MySQL database layer. Which of the following AWS database services would provide automated backups for the application?","options":{"A":"A MySQL database installed on an EC2 instance","B":"Amazon Aurora","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon Neptune"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Aurora is a MySQL-compatible managed relational database that automatically handles backups, replication, and failover, unlike running MySQL on EC2 where the customer manages everything themselves.\\nA MySQL database installed on an EC2 instance is a self-managed deployment where the customer is responsible for configuring and running their own backup processes, with no automated backup capability provided by AWS. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL key-value and document database that is not MySQL-compatible and is not suited to replacing a relational MySQL database layer. Amazon Neptune is a fully managed graph database service designed for highly connected datasets such as social networks and knowledge graphs, and is not a relational database option for MySQL workloads."},{"id":"q042","question":"What is the AWS service that enables AWS architects to manage infrastructure as code?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudFormation","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon SES","D":"Amazon EMR"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS CloudFormation lets you define your entire AWS infrastructure as code using JSON or YAML templates, enabling repeatable and automated provisioning of resources without manual configuration.\\nAWS Config continuously monitors and records the configuration state of AWS resources and evaluates them against desired settings, but it does not provision or manage infrastructure. Amazon SES is a cloud-based email sending and receiving service, entirely unrelated to infrastructure management. Amazon EMR is a managed big data processing service for running frameworks such as Apache Spark and Hadoop, unrelated to infrastructure as code."},{"id":"q044","question":"What does the AWS Health Dashboard provide?","options":{"A":"Detailed troubleshooting guidance to address AWS events impacting your resources","B":"Health checks for Auto Scaling instances","C":"Recommendations for Cost Optimization","D":"A dashboard detailing vulnerabilities in your applications","E":"Personalized view of AWS service health"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard provides a personalized view of how AWS service events affect your specific resources, and detailed troubleshooting guidance to help you respond to and resolve those events.\\nHealth checks for Auto Scaling instances are a feature of Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling that monitors instance health and replaces unhealthy instances automatically, and are not a capability of the AWS Health Dashboard. Recommendations for cost optimization are provided by AWS Trusted Advisor, which inspects your environment and suggests ways to reduce spending, and are not a feature of the AWS Health Dashboard. A dashboard detailing vulnerabilities in applications describes Amazon Inspector, which scans for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and is not a function of the AWS Health Dashboard."},{"id":"q045","question":"You have deployed your application on multiple Amazon EC2 instances. Your customers complain that sometimes they can\'t reach your application. Which AWS service allows you to monitor the performance of your EC2 instances to assist in troubleshooting these issues?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch collects and monitors metrics, logs, and events from EC2 instances, enabling you to set alarms and visualise performance data to diagnose availability and performance issues affecting your application.\\nAWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that runs code in response to events and has no capability for monitoring the performance or availability of EC2 instances. AWS Config records and evaluates configuration changes to AWS resources for compliance and auditing purposes, but does not collect performance metrics or help troubleshoot availability issues. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and user activity across your AWS account for auditing and governance purposes, but does not monitor instance performance metrics or application availability."},{"id":"q047","question":"Which of the following is not a benefit of Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 provides unlimited storage for any type of data","B":"Amazon S3 can run any type of application or backend system","C":"Amazon S3 stores any number of objects, but with object size limits","D":"Amazon S3 can be scaled manually to store and retrieve any amount of data from anywhere","E":"Amazon S3 provides 99.999999999% (11 9\'s) of data durability"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 cannot run applications or backend systems, as it is an object storage service rather than a compute platform. Running applications and backend systems is the function of services such as Amazon EC2 and AWS Lambda. Amazon S3 scales automatically rather than manually, so the claim that it scales manually is factually incorrect and therefore not a genuine benefit.\\nAmazon S3 does provide virtually unlimited storage for any type of data as objects, making option A a true statement and therefore not the answer. Amazon S3 stores any number of objects but does impose a 5 TB size limit per individual object, making option C an accurate description of its behavior. Amazon S3 is designed to provide 99.999999999% durability for objects stored across its infrastructure, making option E a genuine and well-known benefit."},{"id":"q053","question":"Which of the following are features of Amazon CloudWatch Logs?","options":{"A":"Summaries by Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","B":"Free Amazon OpenSearch Service analytics","C":"Provided at no charge","D":"Real-time monitoring","E":"Adjustable retention"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch Logs supports real-time monitoring of log data and configurable retention periods, allowing you to control exactly how long log data is kept before it is automatically deleted.\\nAmazon SNS is a notification and messaging service that can be triggered by CloudWatch alarms, but summarizing log data is not a native feature of CloudWatch Logs. Amazon CloudWatch Logs is not free, as charges apply based on the volume of data ingested, stored, and analyzed. Amazon OpenSearch Service is a separate analytics service that can be integrated with CloudWatch Logs as a destination, but free OpenSearch analytics is not a native feature of CloudWatch Logs."},{"id":"q054","question":"Which of the following is an AWS-managed compute service?","options":{"A":"Amazon SWF","B":"Amazon EC2","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"Amazon Aurora"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Lambda is a fully managed serverless compute service where you upload your code and AWS handles all underlying infrastructure, patching, and scaling automatically, with no server management required from the customer.\\nAmazon Simple Workflow Service is a workflow orchestration service for coordinating tasks across distributed application components, and is not a compute service that provides or manages underlying infrastructure. Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers with full operating system access, but requires the customer to manage the guest operating system, patching, and capacity configuration, making it a customer-managed rather than a fully AWS-managed compute service. Amazon Aurora is a fully managed relational database service compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL, and is a database service rather than a compute service."},{"id":"q055","question":"A company wants to reduce the physical compute footprint that developers use to run code. Which service would meet that need by enabling serverless architectures?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)","B":"AWS Lambda","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"AWS CodeBuild"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Lambda runs code in response to events without requiring any server provisioning or management, directly eliminating the physical compute footprint that developers would otherwise need to maintain.\\nAmazon EC2 provides virtual servers where customers are responsible for provisioning and managing the underlying instances, making it a server-based rather than serverless solution. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database service and does not provide compute execution for running application code. AWS CodeBuild is a fully managed build service for compiling source code and running tests, not a serverless compute service for running application logic."},{"id":"q063","question":"Which AWS services should be used for read/write of constantly changing data?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 GLACIER","B":"Amazon RDS","C":"AWS Snowball","D":"Amazon Redshift","E":"Amazon EFS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Amazon RDS supports frequent read/write operations on relational data with full ACID transaction support, making it well suited for applications with constantly changing structured data. Amazon EFS provides a shared network file system with concurrent read/write access for multiple EC2 instances, making it appropriate for workloads that require continuous file-level changes.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier is an archival storage service designed for data that is rarely accessed and does not support the frequent read/write operations that constantly changing data requires. AWS Snowball is a physical device used to transfer large volumes of data into or out of AWS and has no capability to serve as a read/write data store for active workloads. Amazon Redshift is a data warehouse service optimized for running complex analytical queries across large datasets, not for handling frequent transactional read/write operations on constantly changing data."},{"id":"q067","question":"Which of the following is an AWS managed Domain Name System (DNS) web service?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"Amazon Neptune","C":"Amazon SageMaker","D":"Amazon Lightsail"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 is AWS\'s managed DNS web service that translates domain names into IP addresses and also provides traffic routing policies, health checks, and domain registration.\\nAmazon Neptune is a fully managed graph database service for applications that work with highly connected datasets, unrelated to DNS or traffic routing. Amazon SageMaker is a managed machine learning platform for building, training, and deploying ML models, entirely unrelated to DNS. Amazon Lightsail provides simplified virtual private servers with pre-configured compute and networking for straightforward workloads, not a DNS service."},{"id":"q070","question":"Which service is best for storing common database query results, which helps to alleviate database access load?","options":{"A":"Amazon Machine Learning","B":"Amazon SQS","C":"Amazon ElastiCache","D":"Amazon EC2 Instance Store"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon ElastiCache provides in-memory caching using Redis or Memcached, storing frequently queried results so applications can retrieve them without hitting the database repeatedly, dramatically reducing database load.\\nAmazon Machine Learning is a service for building and training machine learning models and has no role in database caching or query result storage. Amazon SQS is a fully managed message queuing service for decoupling application components, not for caching database query results. Amazon EC2 Instance Store is ephemeral block storage attached directly to an EC2 instance that does not persist beyond the instance lifecycle and is not designed for shared caching of database results."},{"id":"q073","question":"A company wants to focus on business activities instead of managing compute and capacity. Which AWS service can be used to automatically add or remove Amazon EC2 instances based on demand?","options":{"A":"Elastic Load Balancer","B":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","C":"Amazon Route 53","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling monitors demand and automatically adds or removes EC2 instances to maintain performance and minimize cost, freeing teams from manual capacity management so they can focus on their applications.\\nElastic Load Balancing distributes incoming traffic across existing EC2 instances to prevent any single instance from being overwhelmed, but does not add or remove instances in response to changes in demand. Amazon Route 53 is a DNS and domain routing service that directs users to application endpoints and has no role in provisioning or removing compute capacity. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and serves content from edge locations closer to end users, and does not manage or scale EC2 instance capacity."},{"id":"q076","question":"Which AWS service can be used to query stored datasets directly from Amazon S3 using standard SQL?","options":{"A":"AWS Glue","B":"Amazon EMR","C":"Amazon CloudSearch","D":"Amazon Athena"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Athena lets you run standard SQL queries directly against data stored in S3 without loading it into a database first, making it the purpose-built service for querying datasets directly from S3.\\nAWS Glue is a serverless ETL service for discovering, preparing, and transforming data for analytics pipelines, not for running ad hoc SQL queries directly against S3. Amazon EMR provides managed clusters for processing large datasets using frameworks like Spark and Hadoop, but requires provisioning infrastructure rather than querying S3 directly with standard SQL. Amazon CloudSearch is a managed search service for adding full-text search functionality to applications and has no capability for SQL-based data querying."},{"id":"q081","question":"Which of the following services is in the category of AWS serverless platform?","options":{"A":"Amazon EMR","B":"Elastic Load Balancing","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"Amazon EC2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that runs code in response to events without requiring you to provision or manage any underlying servers.\\nAmazon EMR is a managed cluster platform that runs big data frameworks such as Hadoop and Spark on provisioned EC2 instances, and is not a serverless service. Elastic Load Balancing distributes incoming traffic across multiple targets such as EC2 instances, and is a traffic management service rather than a compute platform of any kind. Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers in the cloud that require you to provision, configure, and manage the underlying instances, making it the opposite of a serverless compute service."},{"id":"q085","question":"What technology enables compute capacity to adjust as loads change?","options":{"A":"Load balancing","B":"Automatic failover","C":"Round robin","D":"Auto Scaling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Auto Scaling monitors load and automatically adjusts the number of compute instances to match demand, ensuring capacity scales up during peak periods and scales back down when demand drops, without any manual intervention.\\nLoad balancing distributes incoming traffic across existing compute instances to prevent any single instance from being overwhelmed, but does not add or remove capacity in response to changing load. Automatic failover redirects traffic to a healthy instance or resource when a failure is detected, which addresses availability rather than capacity adjustment. Round robin is a traffic distribution method that cycles requests evenly across a set of servers, and like load balancing, manages traffic distribution rather than adjusting the number of available compute instances."},{"id":"q086","question":"Which AWS service is a managed NoSQL database?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon Aurora","D":"Amazon RDS for MariaDB"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB is AWS\'s fully managed NoSQL database service that supports key-value and document data structures, designed for applications requiring consistent single-digit millisecond performance at any scale.\\nAmazon Redshift is a fully managed data warehouse service designed for running complex analytical queries across large volumes of structured data, not a NoSQL database for key-value or document storage. Amazon Aurora is a fully managed relational database engine compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL, and uses SQL-based structured schemas rather than the flexible NoSQL data models that DynamoDB provides. Amazon RDS for MariaDB is a managed relational database service running the MariaDB engine, which uses structured SQL tables and is not a NoSQL database service."},{"id":"q087","question":"Which of the following is a correct relationship between regions, Availability Zones, and edge locations?","options":{"A":"Data centers contain regions","B":"Regions contain Availability Zones","C":"Availability Zones contain edge locations","D":"Edge locations contain regions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Regions are geographic areas that contain multiple Availability Zones. Each AZ consists of one or more data centers, and edge locations are separate points of presence used by CloudFront, independent of AZs.\\nData centers contain regions reverses the hierarchy. Data centers sit inside AZs, which sit inside Regions. Availability Zones contain edge locations is false. Edge locations are separate infrastructure from AZs and exist independently across the globe. Edge locations contain regions is false. Edge locations are smaller points of presence and have no containing relationship with Regions."},{"id":"q088","question":"What approach to transcoding a large number of individual video files adheres to AWS architecture principles?","options":{"A":"Using many instances in parallel","B":"Using a single large instance during off-peak hours","C":"Using dedicated hardware","D":"Using a large GPU instance type"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Using many instances in parallel applies horizontal scaling, a core AWS architecture principle that distributes workloads across multiple instances simultaneously for greater speed and cost efficiency than sequential processing.\\nUsing a single large instance during off-peak hours applies vertical scaling and sequential processing, which contradicts AWS architecture principles favoring parallelism and does not reduce total processing time. Using dedicated hardware provides physical server isolation for compliance or licensing requirements but does not improve throughput for large-scale parallel workloads. Using a large GPU instance type optimizes for graphics processing performance on a single instance rather than distributing the workload across multiple parallel instances."},{"id":"q089","question":"Which AWS services can host a Microsoft SQL Server database?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS)","C":"Amazon Aurora","D":"Amazon Redshift","E":"Amazon S3"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Microsoft SQL Server can be hosted on Amazon EC2 instances where the customer installs and manages the database themselves, or via Amazon RDS for SQL Server where AWS manages the underlying infrastructure, patching, and backups as a fully managed service.\\nAmazon Aurora supports MySQL and PostgreSQL-compatible database engines only and does not support Microsoft SQL Server. Amazon Redshift is a data warehouse service optimized for analytical queries on large datasets, not a relational database engine for hosting SQL Server workloads. Amazon S3 is an object storage service and cannot host or run a relational database engine."},{"id":"q093","question":"What time-savings advantage is offered with the use of Amazon Rekognition?","options":{"A":"Amazon Rekognition provides automatic watermarking of images","B":"Amazon Rekognition provides automatic detection of objects appearing in pictures","C":"Amazon Recognition provides the ability to resize millions of images automatically","D":"Amazon Rekognition uses Amazon Mechanical Turk to allow humans to bid on object detection jobs"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Rekognition automatically analyzes images and videos to detect objects, scenes, faces, text, and activities using machine learning, eliminating the time that would otherwise be spent on manual image review or building custom ML pipelines.\\nAutomatic watermarking of images is not a feature of Amazon Rekognition, which is an analysis and detection service rather than an image editing or rights management tool. Resizing millions of images automatically is not a Rekognition capability and describes an image processing task better suited to a custom solution or a service like AWS Lambda with an image library. Amazon Rekognition does not use Amazon Mechanical Turk or involve human bidding on jobs, as its detection capabilities are fully automated through deep learning models without human intervention."},{"id":"q094","question":"Which AWS service can be used to automatically scale an application up and down without making capacity planning decisions?","options":{"A":"AWS Auto Scaling","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Lambda"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of EC2 instances based on defined policies, eliminating the need for manual capacity planning decisions and ensuring the application always has the right amount of compute capacity.\\nAmazon Redshift is a fully managed data warehouse service designed for running analytical queries across large datasets, and has no capability to automatically scale application infrastructure. AWS CloudTrail is a logging and auditing service that records API calls and account activity across an AWS environment, and does not manage or adjust application capacity. AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that runs code in response to events without provisioning servers, but it is an event-driven execution environment rather than a service that scales existing application infrastructure up and down."},{"id":"q095","question":"Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) offers which of the following benefits over traditional database management?","options":{"A":"AWS manages the data stored in Amazon RDS tables","B":"AWS manages the maintenance of the operating system","C":"AWS automatically scales up instance types on demand","D":"AWS manages the database type"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With Amazon RDS, AWS manages the underlying operating system patching, backups, and maintenance windows, freeing customers from OS-level administration while customers retain responsibility for their data, schema, and database-level configuration.\\nAWS does not manage the data stored in RDS tables, as customers retain full responsibility for the data they load, modify, and delete within their databases. RDS does not automatically scale instance types on demand, as customers must manually resize instances or enable storage autoscaling, unlike serverless services such as DynamoDB which scale automatically. AWS does not manage the database type in RDS, as customers choose and remain responsible for selecting the database engine such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, or Oracle when creating an instance."},{"id":"q098","question":"Which service stores objects, provides real-time access to those objects, and offers versioning and lifecycle capabilities?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Glacier","B":"AWS Storage Gateway","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon EBS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 stores objects with real-time access, supports versioning to track and restore previous versions, and offers lifecycle policies to manage object transitions, making it the service that matches all three described capabilities.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier is an archival storage service designed for data that is rarely accessed and can tolerate retrieval times of hours, and does not provide the real-time access or versioning capabilities the question describes. AWS Storage Gateway is a hybrid storage service that connects on-premises environments to AWS cloud storage for ongoing integration, and is not an object storage service with versioning or lifecycle management. Amazon EBS provides persistent block storage volumes that attach to EC2 instances, and does not store objects or support versioning and lifecycle policies."},{"id":"q108","question":"A company wants to expand from one AWS Region into a second AWS Region. What does the company need to do to start supporting the new Region?","options":{"A":"Contact an AWS Account Manager to sign a new contract","B":"Move an Availability Zone to the new Region","C":"Begin deploying resources in the second Region","D":"Download the AWS Management Console for the new Region"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Regions are available globally and customers can begin provisioning resources in any Region at any time through the console, CLI, or API without any additional agreements or setup required.\\nContacting an AWS Account Manager to sign a new contract is not required because AWS uses a self-service, pay-as-you-go model with no per-Region contracts. Availability Zones are fixed infrastructure components within a Region and cannot be moved or reassigned. The AWS Management Console is a web-based interface accessed through a browser and does not require a separate download for each Region."},{"id":"q109","question":"Why is it beneficial to use Elastic Load Balancers with applications?","options":{"A":"They allow for the conversion from Application Load Balancers to Classic Load Balancers","B":"They are capable of handling constant changes in network traffic patterns","C":"They automatically adjust capacity","D":"They are provided at no charge to users"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Elastic Load Balancers automatically distribute incoming traffic across multiple registered targets and adapt to constantly changing network traffic patterns without requiring manual intervention, ensuring no single target becomes a bottleneck.\\nElastic Load Balancers do not convert between Application Load Balancers and Classic Load Balancers; these are distinct load balancer types that cannot be converted to one another, and migration requires deploying a new load balancer rather than in-place conversion. ELB does not itself adjust the number of compute instances; that is the responsibility of Auto Scaling groups working in conjunction with a load balancer. Elastic Load Balancing is not provided at no charge; there are per-hour charges for each load balancer plus data processing charges, making cost a factor when designing load-balanced architectures."},{"id":"q111","question":"What is the lowest-cost, durable storage option for retaining database backups for immediate retrieval?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval","C":"Amazon EBS","D":"Amazon EC2 Instance Store"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon S3 Standard provides durable, highly available object storage with immediate millisecond retrieval, and is more cost-effective than EBS for backup storage while meeting the immediate retrieval requirement.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is designed for long-term archival storage at very low cost, and retrieval takes from minutes to hours, making it unsuitable for backups that require immediate access. Amazon EBS provides persistent block storage volumes attached directly to EC2 instances for low-latency disk access, and is significantly more expensive per gigabyte than S3 for storing backup data. Amazon EC2 Instance Store provides temporary block storage physically attached to the host machine, and is ephemeral storage that is lost when the instance stops, making it entirely unsuitable for durable backup storage."},{"id":"q116","question":"Which AWS managed service is used to host databases?","options":{"A":"AWS Batch","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Data Pipeline","D":"Amazon RDS"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon RDS is a fully managed relational database service that supports multiple database engines including MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, and SQL Server, handling provisioning, patching, backups, and high availability automatically.\\nAWS Batch is a fully managed service for running large-scale batch computing jobs across dynamically provisioned compute resources, and has no database hosting capability. AWS Artifact is a self-service portal that provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports and security documentation, and is entirely unrelated to database hosting. AWS Data Pipeline (deprecated) is an orchestration service for scheduling and automating the movement and transformation of data between AWS services and on-premises sources, and is not a service for hosting or managing databases."},{"id":"q119","question":"Which AWS service provides a simple and scalable shared file storage solution for use with Linux-based AWS and on-premises servers?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier","C":"Amazon EBS","D":"Amazon EFS"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Elastic File System (EFS) is a managed NFS file system that can be mounted simultaneously by multiple Linux EC2 instances and accessed from on-premises servers, providing simple, scalable shared file storage.\\nAmazon S3 is an object storage service designed for storing and retrieving files via HTTP APIs, and does not provide a mountable shared file system for Linux servers. Amazon S3 Glacier is an archival storage class within Amazon S3 designed for long-term data retention with infrequent access, and has no shared file system capability. Amazon Elastic Block Store (EBS) provides block-level storage volumes that attach to a single EC2 instance at a time, and cannot be shared across multiple servers simultaneously."},{"id":"q122","question":"Which type of AWS storage is ephemeral and is deleted when an instance is stopped or terminated?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon EC2 instance store","C":"Amazon EFS","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"B","explanation":"EC2 instance store provides ephemeral block storage that is physically attached to the host machine and is permanently deleted when the instance is stopped or terminated, making it suitable only for temporary data.\\nAmazon EBS volumes are persistent block storage that exist independently of the instance lifecycle, so data is retained even after the instance is stopped or terminated. Amazon EFS is a persistent shared file system that remains available across multiple instances and is not tied to the lifecycle of any single instance. Amazon S3 is persistent object storage that exists independently of any compute instance and retains data until explicitly deleted."},{"id":"q124","question":"Which services are parts of the AWS serverless platform?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2, Amazon S3, Amazon Athena","B":"Amazon Kinesis, Amazon SQS, Amazon EMR","C":"AWS Step Functions, Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon SNS","D":"Amazon Athena, Amazon Cognito, Amazon EC2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Step Functions, Amazon DynamoDB, and Amazon SNS are all fully serverless services that require no server provisioning and scale automatically, making this the only group composed entirely of serverless services.\\nAmazon EC2 requires customers to provision and manage virtual server instances, and while Amazon S3 and Athena are serverless, EC2 disqualifies this group. Amazon EMR requires provisioning and managing clusters of servers for big data processing, disqualifying this group even though Kinesis and SQS are serverless. Amazon EC2 is not a serverless service, which disqualifies this group regardless of whether Athena and Cognito qualify individually."},{"id":"q127","question":"Which AWS service provides the ability to manage infrastructure as code?","options":{"A":"AWS CodePipeline","B":"AWS CodeDeploy","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS CloudFormation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS CloudFormation allows you to define AWS infrastructure in JSON or YAML templates, enabling repeatable and automated provisioning of entire environments through infrastructure as code.\\nAWS CodePipeline automates the stages of a software release pipeline including source, build, and deploy phases, but does not provision or manage AWS infrastructure through templates. AWS CodeDeploy automates the deployment of application code to EC2 instances, Lambda functions, and on-premises servers, and does not manage infrastructure provisioning. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and is a networking service entirely unrelated to infrastructure as code."},{"id":"q129","question":"A customer needs to run a MySQL database that easily scales. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"Amazon Aurora","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Aurora is a MySQL and PostgreSQL-compatible relational database built for the cloud that automatically scales storage and can handle high throughput, making it the purpose-built choice for a MySQL workload that needs to scale easily.\\nAmazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse service optimized for running analytical SQL queries across large datasets, and is not a relational database engine compatible with MySQL workloads. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL key-value and document database, and does not support MySQL or relational data models. Amazon ElastiCache is a managed in-memory caching service designed to accelerate application performance by storing frequently accessed data in memory, and is not a relational database capable of running MySQL workloads."},{"id":"q134","question":"Which AWS service should be used for long-term, low-cost storage of data backups?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier","C":"AWS Snowball","D":"Amazon EBS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon S3 Glacier is designed for long-term archival and data backup at very low storage costs, making it the purpose-built service for retaining backups cheaply over extended periods.\\nAmazon RDS is a fully managed relational database service for active transactional workloads, and is not designed for storing infrequently accessed backup archives at low cost. AWS Snowball is a physical data transport device used for migrating large volumes of data into or out of AWS, and is a migration tool rather than a long-term storage service. Amazon EBS provides persistent block storage volumes attached to EC2 instances for low-latency disk access, and is significantly more expensive per gigabyte than S3 Glacier for storing backup data that is accessed infrequently."},{"id":"q138","question":"What can AWS edge locations be used for?","options":{"A":"Hosting applications","B":"Delivering content closer to users","C":"Running NoSQL database caching services","D":"Reducing traffic on the server by caching responses","E":"Sending notification messages to end users"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Edge locations deliver cached content geographically close to end users to reduce latency, and they absorb repeated requests by serving cached responses so that fewer requests reach the origin server.\\nHosting applications compute resources for running applications are provided by EC2 instances and other services in AWS Regions and Availability Zones, not at edge locations. Running NoSQL database caching services managed database and caching services such as DynamoDB and ElastiCache run in Regions, not at edge locations. Sending notification messages to end users message delivery to end users is handled by services like Amazon SNS, which operates from Regional endpoints, not edge locations."},{"id":"q149","question":"Which service\'s PRIMARY purpose is software version control?","options":{"A":"AWS CodeBuild","B":"AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI)","C":"Amazon Cognito","D":"AWS CodeCommit"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS CodeCommit is a fully managed Git-based source control service designed specifically for software version control, allowing teams to securely store and manage their code repositories.\\nAWS CodeBuild is a fully managed build service that compiles source code and runs tests to produce deployment-ready packages, but its primary purpose is building and testing code rather than storing and versioning it. AWS CLI is a command-line tool for interacting with and managing AWS services and resources, and is not a source control or version management service. Amazon Cognito is a user identity and authentication service for web and mobile applications, and has no connection to software version control."},{"id":"q151","question":"Which of the following components of the AWS Global Infrastructure consists of one or more discrete data centers interconnected through low latency links?","options":{"A":"Availability Zone","B":"Edge location","C":"Region","D":"Private networking"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An Availability Zone consists of one or more discrete data centers with independent power and networking, interconnected to other AZs within the same Region via low-latency links.\\nEdge locations are separate points of presence used for content delivery and caching, not data center clusters with redundant infrastructure. A Region is the broader geographic area that contains multiple Availability Zones, not the data center cluster itself. Private networking is not a component of the AWS global infrastructure hierarchy."},{"id":"q154","question":"A characteristic of edge locations is that they:","options":{"A":"Host Amazon EC2 instances closer to users","B":"Help lower latency and improve performance for users","C":"Cache frequently changing data without reaching the origin server","D":"Refresh data changes daily"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Edge locations cache content geographically close to end users, reducing round-trip distance and lowering latency to improve performance for global applications.\\nHost Amazon EC2 instances closer to users. EC2 instances run in Regions and AZs, not at edge locations. Cache frequently changing data without reaching the origin server. Edge locations cache static or infrequently changing content, not frequently changing data. Refresh data changes daily. Edge locations serve cached content based on TTL settings, not on a fixed daily refresh cycle."},{"id":"q164","question":"Which service provides a hybrid storage service that enables on-premises applications to seamlessly use cloud storage?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Glacier","B":"AWS Snowball","C":"AWS Storage Gateway","D":"Amazon Elastic Block Storage (Amazon EBS)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Storage Gateway is a hybrid storage service that integrates on-premises environments with AWS cloud storage, presenting cloud storage as local file shares, volumes, or tape to on-premises applications.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier is an archival storage class within Amazon S3 designed for long-term data retention with infrequent access, and does not provide a hybrid storage integration layer for on-premises applications. AWS Snowball is a physical data transfer device used to move large volumes of data into or out of AWS, and is a migration tool rather than an ongoing hybrid storage service. Amazon Elastic Block Store (EBS) provides block-level storage volumes that attach to EC2 instances within AWS, and cannot be accessed directly by on-premises applications as a seamless cloud storage extension."},{"id":"q166","question":"Which of the following is a component of the AWS Global Infrastructure?","options":{"A":"Amazon Alexa","B":"AWS Regions","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Regions are the physical, geographically distributed components of the AWS Global Infrastructure, each containing multiple Availability Zones that provide redundancy and fault isolation.\\nAmazon Alexa is a voice assistant service that enables conversational interactions with applications, and is not a component of the physical infrastructure that underpins AWS. Amazon Lightsail is a simplified compute service designed for developers who need an easy way to launch virtual servers and applications, and is not an infrastructure component. AWS Organizations is an account management service that enables centralized governance and billing across multiple AWS accounts, and is not part of the AWS Global Infrastructure."},{"id":"q168","question":"A company wants to migrate its applications to a VPC on AWS. These applications will need to access on-premises resources. What combination of actions will enable the company to accomplish this goals?","options":{"A":"Use the AWS Service Catalog to identify a list of on-premises resources that can be migrated","B":"Build a VPN connection between an on-premises device and a virtual private gateway in the new VPC","C":"Use Amazon Athena to query data from the on-premises database servers","D":"Connect the company\'s on-premises data center to AWS using AWS Direct Connect","E":"Leverage Amazon CloudFront to restrict access to static web content provided through the company\'s on-premises web servers"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"A site-to-site VPN through a virtual private gateway provides an encrypted tunnel over the internet between on-premises and the VPC, and AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network link, both extending the on-premises network into AWS.\\nAWS Service Catalog is a tool for managing and provisioning approved IT service portfolios within an organization, and has no capability to identify or connect on-premises resources to a VPC. Amazon Athena is a serverless query service that analyzes data stored in Amazon S3, and cannot query data directly from on-premises database servers. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and distributes content globally, and is not a mechanism for restricting or routing access between on-premises servers and a VPC."},{"id":"q172","question":"Which of the following AWS features enables a user to launch a pre-configured Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) instance?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)","B":"Amazon Machine Image","C":"Amazon EC2 Systems Manager","D":"Amazon AppStream 2.0"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An Amazon Machine Image is a pre-configured template containing the operating system, application server, and application code used to launch a new EC2 instance in a consistent, known state.\\nAmazon EBS is a block storage service that provides persistent volumes to attach to EC2 instances, not a template for launching pre-configured instances. AWS Systems Manager provides operational tools for managing, patching, and automating tasks on running EC2 instances, but does not launch pre-configured instances. Amazon AppStream 2.0 is an application streaming service that delivers desktop applications to users through a browser, entirely unrelated to EC2 instance configuration."},{"id":"q178","question":"How do Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling groups help achieve high availability for a web application?","options":{"A":"They automatically add more instances across multiple AWS Regions based on global demand of the application","B":"They automatically add or replace instances across multiple Availability Zones when the application needs it","C":"They enable the application\'s static content to reside closer to end users","D":"They are able to distribute incoming requests across a tier of web server instances"},"correct":"B","explanation":"EC2 Auto Scaling groups span multiple Availability Zones and automatically add or replace instances when existing ones fail or become unhealthy, maintaining the desired capacity and enabling high availability.\\nAuto Scaling groups operate within a single Region across multiple Availability Zones, and do not automatically add instances across multiple Regions in response to global demand. Enabling static content to reside closer to end users describes a content delivery network such as Amazon CloudFront, not a capability of EC2 Auto Scaling groups. Distributing incoming requests across a tier of web server instances describes the function of a load balancer such as an Application Load Balancer, which is a separate service that works alongside but is distinct from Auto Scaling groups."},{"id":"q184","question":"A company is looking for a scalable data warehouse solution. Which of the following AWS solutions would meet the company\'s needs?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3)","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon Kinesis","D":"Amazon Redshift"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service designed for running complex analytical queries across large datasets, making it the purpose-built solution for scalable data warehousing.\\nAmazon S3 is an object storage service for storing and retrieving any type of data, not a data warehouse for running analytical queries. Amazon DynamoDB is a NoSQL key-value and document database optimized for high-speed transactional workloads, not for complex analytical queries across large datasets. Amazon Kinesis is a service for collecting, processing, and analyzing real-time streaming data, not for warehousing and querying historical datasets."},{"id":"q185","question":"Which AWS services provide a way to extend an on-premises architecture to the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"Amazon CloudFront","D":"AWS Storage Gateway","E":"Amazon Connect"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and AWS Storage Gateway integrates on-premises storage environments with cloud storage, both directly extending an on-premises architecture into the AWS Cloud.\\nAmazon EBS is a block storage service for EC2 instances that operates entirely within AWS and has no on-premises connectivity function. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and distributes content globally but does not connect or extend on-premises networks to AWS. Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service for managing customer communications and is unrelated to hybrid network or storage connectivity."},{"id":"q192","question":"A company is considering using AWS for a self-hosted database that requires a nightly shutdown for maintenance and cost-saving purposes. Which service should the company use?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) with Amazon EC2 instance store","D":"Amazon EC2 with Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"EC2 with EBS is the right choice because EBS volumes persist independently from the instance. When the instance is stopped nightly, the data on EBS is retained.\\nAmazon Redshift is a fully managed data warehouse service optimized for analytical SQL queries across large datasets, and as a managed service it cannot be stopped on a nightly schedule the way an EC2 instance can, making it unsuitable for a workload with a nightly shutdown requirement. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database service that scales automatically and operates continuously as a managed service, and cannot be stopped on a schedule for maintenance or cost-saving purposes. Amazon EC2 with Amazon EC2 instance store provides temporary block storage that is physically attached to the host machine, and this storage is lost when the instance is stopped, making it unsuitable for a database that requires data persistence across nightly shutdowns."},{"id":"q194","question":"Which service provides object-level storage in AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon Instance Store","C":"Amazon EFS","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon S3 is AWS\'s object storage service where each item is stored as an object containing data, metadata, and a unique key within a bucket, making it suitable for storing any type of file at virtually unlimited scale.\\nAmazon EBS provides block storage volumes that are attached to EC2 instances for use as persistent disks, and operates at the block level rather than the object level. Amazon Instance Store provides temporary block storage physically attached to the host server, which is lost when the instance stops and is not object-level storage. Amazon EFS provides a managed file storage system that can be mounted across multiple EC2 instances simultaneously, and operates at the file level rather than the object level."},{"id":"q195","question":"A company is concerned that they are spending money on underutilized compute resources in AWS. Which AWS feature will help ensure that their applications are automatically adding/removing EC2 compute capacity to closely match the required demand?","options":{"A":"AWS Elastic Load Balancer","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"AWS Auto Scaling","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Auto Scaling monitors application metrics and automatically adjusts the number of EC2 instances to match demand, ensuring you are not paying for idle capacity.\\nAWS Elastic Load Balancer distributes incoming traffic across multiple targets such as EC2 instances to improve availability and fault tolerance, but does not adjust the number of instances in response to demand changes. AWS Budgets lets you set cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and while it can alert you to over-spending it cannot itself add or remove EC2 instances to match required demand. AWS Cost Explorer provides visualisation and analysis of AWS spending patterns over time, and is a cost analysis tool rather than a service that automatically adjusts compute capacity."},{"id":"q196","question":"Which S3 storage class is best for data with unpredictable access patterns?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval","C":"Amazon S3 Standard","D":"Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access"},"correct":"A","explanation":"S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves objects between access tiers based on changing access patterns, optimizing costs when access frequency is unpredictable without incurring retrieval fees.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is designed for archival data that is rarely accessed and can tolerate retrieval times ranging from minutes to hours, making it unsuitable for data with unpredictable but potentially frequent access needs. Amazon S3 Standard is optimized for frequently accessed data and charges a higher storage price, making it cost-inefficient for data with unpredictable patterns that may include long periods of inactivity. Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access is designed for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid retrieval when needed, and incurs retrieval fees that make it costly when access patterns are unpredictable."},{"id":"q197","question":"What is the AWS database service that allows you to upload data structured in key-value format?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon Aurora","C":"Amazon Redshift","D":"Amazon RDS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database that natively supports key-value and document data models, making it ideal for uploading and retrieving data structured as key-value pairs.\\nAmazon Aurora is a fully managed relational database engine compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL that uses structured SQL schemas, and does not support the flexible key-value data model that DynamoDB provides. Amazon Redshift is a fully managed data warehouse service designed for running complex analytical queries across large volumes of structured data, and is not a key-value database. Amazon RDS is a managed relational database service supporting multiple SQL engines, and uses structured table-based schemas rather than the key-value data model."},{"id":"q202","question":"You work as an on-premises MySQL DBA. The work of database configuration, backups, patching, and DR can be time-consuming and repetitive. Your company has decided to migrate to the AWS Cloud. Which of the following can help save time on database maintenance so you can focus on data architecture and performance?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon RDS is a fully managed relational database service where AWS handles configuration, backups, patching, and failover, freeing the DBA to focus on data architecture and performance.\\nAmazon Redshift is a data warehousing service designed for analytics, not transactional relational database workloads. Amazon DynamoDB is a NoSQL key-value database and would not be a like-for-like replacement for a MySQL relational workload. Amazon CloudWatch is a monitoring and observability service, not a database service."},{"id":"q205","question":"Which service is used to ensure that messages between software components are not lost if one or more components fail?","options":{"A":"Amazon SQS","B":"Amazon SES","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Amazon Connect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SQS is a fully managed message queuing service that stores messages even when the consuming component is unavailable, preventing message loss in distributed architectures.\\nAmazon SES is an email sending and receiving service designed for transactional and marketing communications, and does not queue messages between software components to prevent loss during failures. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and has no role in storing or managing messages between application components. Amazon Connect is a cloud contact center service for managing customer communications via voice and chat, and is not a message queuing or application integration service."},{"id":"q207","question":"What is the AWS service that provides a virtual network dedicated to your AWS account?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"AWS Subnets","C":"AWS Dedicated Hosts","D":"Amazon VPC"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon VPC (Virtual Private Cloud) creates a logically isolated, private virtual network dedicated to your AWS account, where you control IP addressing, subnets, routing, and gateways.\\nAWS VPN establishes an encrypted connection over the public internet between your on-premises network and an existing VPC, but is a connectivity feature rather than the virtual network itself. AWS Subnets are subdivisions of a VPC\'s IP address range used to organize resources within a VPC, not a standalone networking service. AWS Dedicated Hosts are physical servers with EC2 instance capacity allocated exclusively to your account for compute workloads, not a networking or virtual network construct."},{"id":"q209","question":"Which of the following AWS services can be used as a compute resource?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon EC2","E":"AWS Lambda"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Amazon EC2 provides resizable virtual servers that run applications on managed compute infrastructure, and AWS Lambda runs code as serverless functions in response to events, making both compute resources.\\nAmazon VPC is a networking service that provides logically isolated network environments within AWS, and does not itself execute code or provide compute capacity. Amazon CloudWatch is a monitoring and observability service that collects metrics, logs, and events from AWS resources, and has no compute execution capability. Amazon S3 is an object storage service designed for storing and retrieving data, and does not provide any compute or code execution functionality."},{"id":"q210","question":"Your company is designing a new application that will store and retrieve photos and videos. Which of the following services should you recommend as the underlying storage mechanism?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon SQS","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon EC2 Instance Store"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 is designed for storing and retrieving any amount of unstructured data, including photos and videos, with high durability and scalability, making it the purpose-built object storage solution for this use case.\\nAmazon EBS provides persistent block storage volumes that attach directly to EC2 instances, and is designed for low-latency disk access rather than storing and serving large volumes of unstructured media objects. Amazon SQS is a fully managed message queuing service for decoupling application components, and is a messaging service with no capability to store or retrieve files. Amazon EC2 Instance Store provides temporary block storage physically attached to the host machine running an EC2 instance, and is ephemeral storage that is lost when the instance stops, making it entirely unsuitable for persistent photo and video storage."},{"id":"q212","question":"What does Amazon ElastiCache provide?","options":{"A":"In-memory caching for read-heavy applications","B":"A managed relational database service","C":"An online software store that allows customers to launch pre-configured software with just a few clicks","D":"A domain name system in the cloud"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon ElastiCache provides in-memory caching using Redis or Memcached engines, storing frequently accessed data in RAM and delivering sub-millisecond read latency for read-heavy applications.\\nA managed relational database service describes Amazon RDS, which handles structured data with SQL-based querying rather than in-memory caching. An online software store that allows customers to launch pre-configured software describes AWS Marketplace, not a caching service. A domain name system in the cloud describes Amazon Route 53, which handles DNS routing and domain registration rather than in-memory data caching."},{"id":"q217","question":"Which AWS Service can be used to establish a dedicated, private network connection between AWS and your datacenter?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"Amazon CloudFront","C":"AWS Snowball","D":"Amazon Route 53"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated, private physical network connection from your on-premises data center to AWS, bypassing the public internet to deliver more consistent bandwidth, lower latency, and increased reliability for hybrid cloud workloads.\\nAmazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and distributes web content to end users from edge locations worldwide, and is a content delivery service rather than a private network connectivity solution for data centers. AWS Snowball is a physical data transfer device used to move large volumes of data into or out of AWS by shipping the device, designed for one-time or periodic bulk data transfers rather than ongoing private network connectivity. Amazon Route 53 is a scalable DNS service that translates domain names to IP addresses and supports routing policies, and is a DNS management service with no capability to establish dedicated network links between on-premises infrastructure and AWS."},{"id":"q218","question":"You are working on two projects that require completely different network configurations. Which AWS service or feature will allow you to isolate resources and network configurations?","options":{"A":"Internet gateways","B":"Virtual Private Cloud","C":"Security Groups","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon VPC lets you provision logically isolated network environments within AWS with full control over IP ranges, subnets, routing tables, and gateways, allowing you to run completely separate network configurations for different projects.\\nInternet gateways enable outbound and inbound internet access within a VPC but are a component of a VPC rather than a mechanism for isolating entire network configurations. Security groups control inbound and outbound traffic at the instance level and operate within a VPC, so they filter traffic but do not provide network-level isolation between projects. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network for caching and distributing content globally, unrelated to network isolation or configuration management."},{"id":"q220","question":"An organization needs to analyze and process a large number of data sets. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"Amazon EMR","B":"Amazon MQ","C":"Amazon SNS","D":"Amazon SQS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon EMR is a managed big data platform that runs distributed processing frameworks such as Apache Hadoop and Spark, making it the purpose-built service for analyzing and processing large volumes of data at scale.\\nAmazon MQ is a managed message broker service for applications that use open-standard messaging protocols such as AMQP and MQTT, and is a messaging integration tool with no data processing or analytics capability. Amazon SNS is a fully managed pub/sub notification service for delivering messages to subscribers such as Lambda functions, email addresses, and HTTP endpoints, and has no capability to process or analyze large datasets. Amazon SQS is a fully managed message queuing service that decouples application components by holding messages in a queue for asynchronous processing, and is an application integration tool rather than a data analytics service."},{"id":"q222","question":"What is the AWS service that provides you the highest level of control over the underlying virtual infrastructure?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon EC2","D":"Amazon RDS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon EC2 gives customers the highest level of control over virtual infrastructure, including choice of operating system, instance type, networking, storage, and the ability to install and configure any software on the underlying instance.\\nAmazon Redshift is a fully managed data warehouse service where AWS abstracts and manages the underlying infrastructure, leaving customers responsible only for their data and queries rather than the virtual infrastructure itself. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database service where AWS handles all infrastructure, patching, and scaling automatically, giving customers no visibility or control over the underlying virtual infrastructure. Amazon RDS is a fully managed relational database service where AWS manages the operating system, database engine patching, and underlying infrastructure, abstracting those layers away from the customer entirely."},{"id":"q224","question":"In your on-premises environment, you can create as many virtual servers as you need from a single template. What can you use to perform the same in AWS?","options":{"A":"IAM","B":"An internet gateway","C":"EBS Snapshot","D":"AMI"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An Amazon Machine Image is a template containing the operating system, application software, and configuration needed to launch an EC2 instance, directly equivalent to the on-premises practice of spinning up multiple virtual servers from a single template.\\nIAM is an identity and access management service that controls permissions and user access within AWS, and has no role in creating or launching virtual server instances. An internet gateway is a VPC component that enables communication between resources inside a VPC and the public internet, and is unrelated to server provisioning or templating. An EBS snapshot is a point-in-time backup of a storage volume used for data recovery purposes, not a template for launching new instances with a defined operating system and configuration."},{"id":"q228","question":"AWS has created a large number of Edge Locations as part of its Global Infrastructure. Which of the following is NOT a benefit of using Edge Locations?","options":{"A":"Edge locations are used by CloudFront to cache the most recent responses","B":"Edge locations are used by CloudFront to improve your end users\' experience when uploading files","C":"Edge locations are used by CloudFront to distribute traffic across multiple instances to reduce latency","D":"Edge locations are used by CloudFront to distribute content to global users with low latency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Edge locations cache and deliver content with low latency to global users and also improve upload performance through services like S3 Transfer Acceleration, but they do not distribute traffic across multiple compute instances. Distributing traffic across instances is the function of Elastic Load Balancing, making C the statement that does not describe an edge location benefit.\\nCaching the most recent responses is a genuine edge location benefit, as CloudFront stores copies of content closer to end users to reduce origin fetches. Improving upload performance for end users is a genuine edge location benefit, as S3 Transfer Acceleration routes uploads through the nearest edge location for faster transfer. Distributing content to global users with low latency is the primary purpose of edge locations and a core CloudFront benefit."},{"id":"q230","question":"Which of the following services allows you to run containerized applications on a cluster of EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"Amazon ECS","B":"AWS Data Pipeline","C":"AWS Cloud9","D":"AWS Health Dashboard"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon ECS is a fully managed container orchestration service that allows you to run and manage Docker containers on a cluster of EC2 instances or using AWS Fargate for serverless compute.\\nAWS Data Pipeline (deprecated) is a service for orchestrating and automating the movement and transformation of data between AWS services, not for running containerised applications. AWS Cloud9 is a cloud-based integrated development environment for writing and debugging code, unrelated to container orchestration. AWS Health Dashboard provides alerts and guidance when AWS service events may affect your resources, not a compute or container service."},{"id":"q232","question":"Which of the following procedures will help reduce your Amazon S3 costs?","options":{"A":"Use S3 Lifecycle policies to move old files automatically to Amazon S3 Glacier","B":"Use the right combination of storage classes based on different use cases","C":"Pick the right Availability Zone for your S3 bucket","D":"Move all the data stored in S3 Standard to Amazon EBS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Using the right S3 storage class for each use case, such as S3 Standard for frequently accessed data, S3 Standard-Infrequent Access for less frequent access, and S3 Glacier for archival, is the most effective way to reduce S3 storage costs.\\nUsing S3 Lifecycle policies to automatically transition old objects to Amazon S3 Glacier reduces storage costs for archived data, but applies only to objects that qualify for archival rather than optimizing the full range of S3 storage costs across all use cases. S3 does not charge differently based on which Availability Zone a bucket is associated with, and selecting a different Availability Zone has no effect on S3 pricing. Moving data from S3 Standard to Amazon EBS would increase costs significantly, as EBS is block storage attached to EC2 instances and charges per provisioned gigabyte regardless of actual usage, making it far more expensive than S3 for object storage."},{"id":"q233","question":"What are the AWS services/features that can help you maintain a highly available and fault-tolerant architecture in AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"AWS Auto Scaling","C":"Elastic Load Balancer","D":"AWS CloudFormation","E":"Network ACLs"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS Auto Scaling automatically adds or removes instances based on demand, maintaining availability when traffic spikes or instances fail. Elastic Load Balancer distributes traffic across healthy instances and integrates with Auto Scaling to replace unhealthy ones, together providing a highly available and fault-tolerant architecture.\\nAWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and is a connectivity service rather than a tool for maintaining application availability or fault tolerance. AWS CloudFormation provisions and manages infrastructure through templates, and while it can deploy highly available architectures, it does not itself maintain availability or respond to failures at runtime. Network ACLs control inbound and outbound traffic at the subnet level for security purposes, and do not contribute to availability or fault tolerance."},{"id":"q235","question":"What is the AWS service/feature that takes advantage of Amazon CloudFront\'s globally distributed edge locations to transfer files to S3 with higher upload speeds?","options":{"A":"S3 Transfer Acceleration","B":"AWS WAF","C":"AWS Snowmobile","D":"AWS Snowball"},"correct":"A","explanation":"S3 Transfer Acceleration uses CloudFront\'s globally distributed edge locations as entry points for uploads to S3, routing data over AWS\'s optimized backbone network to reach the target S3 bucket faster than a standard upload.\\nAWS WAF is a web application firewall that filters and monitors HTTP traffic to protect web applications from common exploits, and has no file transfer or upload acceleration capability. AWS Snowmobile is a physical data transport service using a shipping container-sized device designed for exabyte-scale migrations, not for accelerating online uploads to S3. AWS Snowball is a physical data transfer appliance used for large-scale offline data migrations, and does not leverage edge locations or accelerate internet-based uploads."},{"id":"q237","question":"Which AWS services can be used to improve the performance of a global application and reduce latency for its users?","options":{"A":"AWS KMS","B":"AWS Global Accelerator","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS Glue","E":"Amazon CloudFront"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator improves global application performance by routing user traffic through the AWS private backbone network to the nearest healthy endpoint, reducing internet latency, and Amazon CloudFront caches and serves content from edge locations worldwide, bringing content closer to users and reducing latency for global audiences.\\nAWS KMS is an encryption key management service for creating and controlling cryptographic keys used to protect data, and has no capability to improve application performance or reduce latency. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and is designed for hybrid connectivity rather than improving performance for global end users. AWS Glue is a managed ETL service for discovering, preparing, and transforming data for analytics workloads, and is a data integration service with no application performance or latency capability."},{"id":"q239","question":"A company has a large amount of structured data stored in their on-premises data center. They are planning to migrate all the data to AWS, what is the most appropriate AWS database option?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon SNS","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon RDS is the appropriate service for migrating structured relational data, supporting multiple SQL engines including MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server, and MariaDB in a managed environment that mirrors on-premises relational database setups.\\nAmazon DynamoDB is a NoSQL key-value and document database designed for unstructured or semi-structured data, making it unsuitable for migrating structured relational workloads. Amazon SNS is a managed messaging and notification service used for pub/sub communication between applications, and has no database capability. Amazon ElastiCache is an in-memory caching service used to accelerate application performance, not a relational database for storing and migrating structured data."},{"id":"q241","question":"What is the AWS serverless service that allows you to run your applications without any administrative burden?","options":{"A":"Amazon Lightsail","B":"AWS Lambda","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"Amazon EC2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that runs your code in response to events without requiring you to provision, manage, or scale any servers or infrastructure, eliminating all administrative burden.\\nAmazon Lightsail is a simplified compute service for deploying virtual servers, containers, and web applications with minimal configuration, but it still involves provisioning and managing server-based resources rather than being a truly serverless service. Amazon RDS is a fully managed relational database service, but managed does not mean serverless and customers still select and provision database instances with associated compute resources. Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers in the cloud that require you to provision, configure, and manage the underlying instances, making it the opposite of a serverless service with no administrative burden."},{"id":"q243","question":"Where can you store files in AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon EFS","B":"Amazon SNS","C":"Amazon EBS","D":"Amazon ECS","E":"Amazon EMR"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Amazon EFS is a fully managed, scalable NFS file system that can be mounted by multiple EC2 instances simultaneously, and Amazon EBS provides block storage volumes that attach to individual EC2 instances. Both are storage services where files can be stored in AWS.\\nAmazon SNS is a fully managed publish/subscribe messaging service used to send notifications to subscribers such as Lambda functions, HTTP endpoints, and email addresses, and is a messaging service rather than a file storage service. Amazon ECS is a fully managed container orchestration service for running containerised applications using Docker containers, and is a compute service for running application workloads rather than a storage service. Amazon EMR is a managed big data platform for processing large-scale datasets using frameworks such as Apache Spark and Hadoop, and is a data processing service rather than a storage destination for files."},{"id":"q244","question":"Which AWS service can be used to store and reliably deliver messages across distributed systems?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Queue Service","B":"AWS Storage Gateway","C":"Amazon Simple Email Service","D":"Amazon Simple Storage Service"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SQS is a fully managed message queuing service that stores messages reliably until they are processed, ensuring no message is lost even if one or more components in a distributed system temporarily fail.\\nAWS Storage Gateway connects on-premises environments to AWS cloud storage for hybrid storage use cases, and has no message queuing or delivery capability. Amazon Simple Email Service is a cloud-based email sending and receiving service designed for transactional and marketing emails, not for queuing messages between distributed application components. Amazon S3 is an object storage service for storing and retrieving files, and does not provide message queuing or guaranteed delivery between distributed systems."},{"id":"q247","question":"What is the primary storage service used by Amazon RDS database instances?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Glacier","B":"Amazon EBS","C":"Amazon EFS","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon RDS database instances use Amazon EBS volumes as their primary storage layer, providing persistent block-level storage that persists independently of the database instance lifecycle.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier is an archival storage service designed for long-term data retention at low cost and is not used as active storage for running database instances. Amazon EFS is a shared file system service mountable across multiple EC2 instances and is not the primary storage layer for RDS. Amazon S3 is an object storage service accessed via API and is not used as block-level storage for database instances."},{"id":"q248","question":"A company is developing a new application using a microservices framework. The new application is having performance and latency issues. Which AWS Service should be used to troubleshoot these issues?","options":{"A":"AWS CodePipeline","B":"AWS X-Ray","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS X-Ray provides distributed tracing for microservices applications, allowing developers to visualise the end-to-end path of requests, identify bottlenecks, and diagnose the root cause of performance and latency issues.\\nAWS CodePipeline automates the steps of a continuous delivery pipeline for releasing application code, including building, testing, and deploying, and is a CI/CD automation tool with no capability to trace requests or diagnose runtime performance issues. Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and is a security tool rather than a performance troubleshooting service. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity across an AWS environment for auditing and compliance purposes, and does not trace application requests or provide visibility into latency and performance bottlenecks within microservices."},{"id":"q249","question":"Which of the following AWS services is designed with native Multi-AZ fault tolerance in mind?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"AWS Snowball","C":"Amazon Simple Storage Service","D":"Amazon EBS","E":"Amazon DynamoDB"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 stores data redundantly across multiple facilities within a Region by default, providing native Multi-AZ durability without any customer configuration. Amazon DynamoDB automatically replicates data across multiple Availability Zones within a Region, making Multi-AZ fault tolerance a built-in characteristic of the service.\\nAmazon Redshift is a data warehouse service that deploys into a single AZ by default and requires additional configuration such as Multi-AZ deployment or snapshot restore to achieve cross-AZ resilience. AWS Snowball is a physical data transfer device used to move large amounts of data into or out of AWS and has no relevance to Multi-AZ fault tolerance as a cloud service. Amazon EBS volumes are confined to a single Availability Zone by default and must be explicitly snapshotted and restored or replicated to achieve availability across AZs."},{"id":"q250","question":"What are the Amazon RDS features that can be used to improve the availability of your database?","options":{"A":"AWS Regions","B":"Multi-AZ Deployment","C":"Automatic patching","D":"Read Replicas","E":"Edge Locations"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Multi-AZ Deployment maintains a synchronous standby replica in a separate Availability Zone with automatic failover if the primary instance fails, and Read Replicas can be promoted to a standalone database to restore availability for read-heavy workloads, both being Amazon RDS features that directly improve database availability.\\nAWS Regions are a geographic infrastructure concept and not a feature within Amazon RDS that can be enabled to improve the availability of an existing database instance. Automatic patching is a maintenance feature that keeps the database engine up to date, reducing admin burden, but it does not contribute to availability during failures or outages. Edge locations are points of presence used by CloudFront for content caching and delivery, and are not a component or feature of Amazon RDS in any way."},{"id":"q251","question":"Sarah has deployed an application in the Northern California (us-west-1) region. After examining the application\'s traffic, she notices that about 30% of the traffic is coming from Asia. What can she do to reduce latency for the users in Asia?","options":{"A":"Replicate the current resources across multiple Availability Zones within the same region","B":"Migrate the application to a hosting provider in Asia","C":"Recreate the website content","D":"Create a CDN using CloudFront, so that content is cached at Edge Locations close to and in Asia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Creating a CloudFront distribution caches content at edge locations near Asian users, dramatically reducing round-trip latency without needing to move or replicate the origin infrastructure.\\nReplicating resources across multiple Availability Zones within the same Region improves availability but does not reduce geographic latency for users in Asia. Migrating to a hosting provider in Asia would reduce latency but introduces significant operational complexity and is not an AWS solution. Recreating the website content has no effect on network latency and does not address the geographic distance between the origin and end users."},{"id":"q256","question":"Amazon S3 Glacier is an Amazon S3 storage class that is suitable for storing which types of data?","options":{"A":"Active archives","B":"Dynamic website assets","C":"Long-term analytic data","D":"Active database records","E":"Cached data"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 Glacier is designed for data that is infrequently accessed but must be retained for long periods, making active archives kept for compliance or historical reference and long-term analytic data retained for future analysis the primary use cases.\\nDynamic website assets require fast, frequent access with millisecond retrieval times, making S3 Standard the appropriate storage class rather than Glacier\'s archival retrieval model. Active databases require real-time read and write operations that are incompatible with Glacier\'s retrieval delays, making services such as RDS or DynamoDB the appropriate choice. Cached data requires sub-millisecond access speeds to serve its purpose of accelerating application performance, making a service such as ElastiCache the appropriate solution rather than an archival storage class."},{"id":"q257","question":"What does Amazon Elastic Beanstalk provide?","options":{"A":"A PaaS solution to automate application deployment","B":"A compute engine for Amazon ECS","C":"A scalable file storage solution for use with AWS and on-premises servers","D":"A NoSQL database service"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk is a Platform as a Service offering that automatically handles deployment, capacity provisioning, load balancing, auto-scaling, and application health monitoring, allowing developers to upload their application code and have the platform manage the infrastructure.\\nAmazon ECS is the container orchestration service that runs Docker containers and is the compute engine for containerised workloads, but it is not an abstraction that automatically manages deployment and infrastructure for general applications in the way Beanstalk does. A scalable file storage solution describes Amazon EFS, which provides shared NFS storage for EC2 instances; Elastic Beanstalk is a deployment and management platform, not a storage service. A NoSQL database service describes Amazon DynamoDB, which provides fully managed key-value and document storage; Elastic Beanstalk is a deployment platform rather than a database."},{"id":"q265","question":"What is the AWS tool that enables you to use scripts to manage all AWS services and resources?","options":{"A":"AWS Console","B":"AWS Service Catalog","C":"AWS OpsWorks","D":"AWS CLI"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The AWS CLI is a unified tool that enables customers to manage AWS services and automate tasks via scripts and command-line commands, providing programmatic access to the full range of AWS functionality.\\nThe AWS Console is a web-based graphical interface for managing AWS services through a browser, and does not support script-based automation or command-line interaction. AWS Service Catalog is a tool for creating and managing approved portfolios of IT products within an organization, and is not used for scripting or direct resource management. AWS OpsWorks (deprecated) is a configuration management service that uses Chef and Puppet to automate server configuration and deployment, and is not a general-purpose scripting tool for managing all AWS services."},{"id":"q266","question":"What are the connectivity options that can be used to build hybrid cloud architectures?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Cloud9","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS CloudTrail","E":"AWS VPN"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private physical connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and AWS VPN creates an encrypted tunnel over the public internet. Both are the standard connectivity options for building hybrid cloud architectures that link on-premises environments with AWS.\\nAWS Artifact is a self-service portal for accessing AWS compliance reports and security certifications, and has no role in establishing network connectivity between on-premises environments and AWS. AWS Cloud9 is a browser-based integrated development environment for writing and running code, and is not a connectivity or networking service. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity for auditing and governance purposes, and does not provide network connectivity between on-premises infrastructure and the AWS Cloud."},{"id":"q267","question":"A company has deployed a new web application on multiple Amazon EC2 instances. Which of the following should they use to ensure that the incoming HTTP traffic is distributed evenly across the instances?","options":{"A":"AWS EC2 Auto Recovery","B":"AWS Auto Scaling","C":"AWS Network Load Balancer","D":"AWS Application Load Balancer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An Application Load Balancer operates at Layer 7 and distributes incoming HTTP and HTTPS traffic across EC2 instances based on rules such as URL path or host header, making it the correct choice for evenly distributing web application traffic.\\nAWS EC2 Auto Recovery monitors individual instances and restores them if they fail, but does not distribute traffic across multiple instances. AWS Auto Scaling adjusts the number of running instances based on demand but does not handle traffic distribution across existing instances. AWS Network Load Balancer operates at Layer 4 and is optimized for TCP and UDP traffic requiring ultra-high performance, not for general HTTP web application traffic distribution."},{"id":"q268","question":"Which of the following AWS offerings is a MySQL-compatible relational database service that can scale capacity automatically based on demand?","options":{"A":"Amazon Neptune","B":"Amazon Aurora","C":"Amazon RDS for SQL Server","D":"Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Aurora is a MySQL and PostgreSQL-compatible managed relational database that automatically scales storage as needed, making it the purpose-built solution for applications requiring MySQL compatibility with automatic capacity scaling.\\nAmazon Neptune is a fully managed graph database service designed for storing and querying highly connected data such as social networks and knowledge graphs, and is not a MySQL-compatible relational database. Amazon RDS for SQL Server is a managed relational database running Microsoft\'s SQL Server engine, which is not MySQL-compatible and does not automatically scale storage capacity on demand. Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL is a managed relational database running the PostgreSQL engine which, while sharing some compatibility with Aurora, does not auto-scale storage the way Aurora does."},{"id":"q270","question":"What is the AWS data warehouse service that supports a high level of query performance on large amounts of datasets?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon Kinesis","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon RDS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse optimized for complex analytical queries on large structured datasets using columnar storage and massively parallel processing.\\nAmazon Kinesis is a service for ingesting and processing real-time streaming data, not a data warehouse for analytical queries on large stored datasets. Amazon DynamoDB is a NoSQL database optimized for high-speed key-value and document workloads, not for complex analytical queries across large datasets. Amazon RDS is a managed relational database service designed for transactional workloads, not for the petabyte-scale analytical query performance that a data warehouse provides."},{"id":"q274","question":"A customer spent a lot of time configuring a newly deployed Amazon EC2 instance. After the workload increases, the customer decides to provision another EC2 instance with an identical configuration. How can the customer achieve this?","options":{"A":"By creating an AWS Config template from the old instance and launching a new instance from it","B":"By creating an EBS Snapshot of the old instance","C":"By installing Aurora on EC2 and launching a new instance from it","D":"By creating an AMI from the old instance and launching a new instance from it"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Creating an AMI from a configured EC2 instance captures its operating system, software, and settings as a reusable template, allowing you to launch new instances with identical configurations at any time.\\nCreating an AWS Config template manages compliance rules and configuration drift detection, not instance images or replication. Creating an EBS Snapshot backs up the data on a volume but does not capture the full instance configuration including the OS and software settings needed to launch an identical instance. Installing Aurora on EC2 is not a valid approach, as Aurora is a managed database service that runs on AWS infrastructure and is not installed on customer EC2 instances."},{"id":"q277","question":"What are the benefits of using the Amazon Relational Database Service?","options":{"A":"Lower administrative burden","B":"Complete control over the underlying host","C":"Resizable compute capacity","D":"Scales automatically to larger or smaller instance types","E":"Supports the document and key-value data structure"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Amazon RDS reduces the administrative burden of database management by handling routine tasks such as backups, patching, and failover automatically, and allows you to resize compute capacity to match changing workload demands.\\nComplete control over the underlying host is a characteristic of Amazon EC2, where customers manage the operating system directly. RDS abstracts the host away entirely and does not expose it to the customer. RDS requires manual instance type changes rather than scaling automatically in response to demand, so it does not auto-scale to larger or smaller instance types. Document and key-value data structures are supported by Amazon DynamoDB, a NoSQL service, whereas RDS is relational and uses structured SQL schemas."},{"id":"q278","question":"What is the connectivity option that uses Internet Protocol Security (IPSec) to establish encrypted connectivity between an on-premises network and the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Internet Gateway","B":"AWS IQ","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS Site-to-Site VPN"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Site-to-Site VPN uses IPSec to create an encrypted tunnel between an on-premises network and an AWS VPC over the public internet, providing secure connectivity without dedicated physical infrastructure.\\nInternet Gateway connects a VPC to the public internet for inbound and outbound traffic, but does not establish encrypted tunnels to on-premises networks. AWS IQ is a marketplace for connecting customers with AWS-certified experts for professional services, unrelated to network connectivity. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private circuit between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, but does not use IPSec and does not travel over the public internet."},{"id":"q282","question":"Which AWS service provides the current status of all AWS services in all AWS Regions?","options":{"A":"AWS Service Catalog","B":"AWS Management Console","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS Health Dashboard"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard provides real-time status information about all AWS services across all AWS Regions, allowing anyone to check the current operational health of the AWS infrastructure.\\nAWS Service Catalog allows organizations to create and manage approved catalogs of IT services for internal use and self-service provisioning, and has no connection to displaying the current status of AWS services globally. AWS Management Console is the web-based interface for accessing and managing AWS services, and while it provides access to many dashboards it is not itself the service that publishes current status information about AWS infrastructure health. Amazon CloudWatch collects metrics, logs, and event data from your own AWS resources and applications to provide operational monitoring and alerting, and is a customer-facing monitoring tool rather than a service that publishes the health status of AWS infrastructure itself."},{"id":"q283","question":"Which AWS service or feature can be used to call AWS Services from different programming languages?","options":{"A":"AWS Software Development Kit","B":"AWS Command Line Interface","C":"AWS CodeDeploy","D":"AWS Management Console"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS SDKs are available for multiple programming languages including Python, Java, JavaScript, and .NET, providing libraries that allow developers to interact with and call AWS services programmatically from within their application code.\\nAWS Command Line Interface is a tool for interacting with AWS services through terminal commands and scripts, and is not designed for calling AWS services from within application code written in a programming language. AWS CodeDeploy is a deployment service that automates the release of application code to EC2 instances, Lambda functions, and on-premises servers, and has no role in enabling programmatic access to AWS services from application code. AWS Management Console is a web-based graphical interface for managing AWS services through a browser, and does not provide programmatic access from programming languages."},{"id":"q284","question":"Which AWS Service can be used to register a new domain name?","options":{"A":"Amazon Personalize","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"AWS KMS","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 provides domain name registration in addition to DNS routing, health checks, and traffic management, making it the only AWS service that can register a new domain name.\\nAmazon Personalize is a machine learning service that provides personalized product and content recommendations, and has no domain registration or DNS functionality. AWS KMS is a key management service used to create and control encryption keys for securing data, and is unrelated to domain registration. AWS Config tracks and records AWS resource configurations and evaluates them against compliance rules, and has no domain registration capability."},{"id":"q285","question":"App development companies move their business to AWS to reduce time-to-market and improve customer satisfaction, what are the AWS automation tools that help them deploy their applications faster?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudFormation","B":"AWS Migration Hub","C":"AWS IAM","D":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","E":"Amazon Macie"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS CloudFormation lets teams define infrastructure as code in templates that can be version-controlled and deployed automatically, and AWS Elastic Beanstalk abstracts infrastructure management so developers can deploy applications simply by uploading their code. Both directly accelerate application deployment.\\nAWS Migration Hub provides a central location for tracking the progress of application migrations to AWS, and is a migration monitoring tool rather than an automation tool for deploying applications faster. AWS IAM is an identity and access management service for controlling who can access AWS resources and what actions they can perform, and is a security and permissions service with no application deployment or automation capability. Amazon Macie is a data security service that uses machine learning to automatically discover and protect sensitive data stored in Amazon S3, and is entirely unrelated to application deployment or automation."},{"id":"q287","question":"A company has hundreds of VPCs in multiple AWS Regions worldwide. What service does AWS offer to simplify the connection management among the VPCs?","options":{"A":"VPC Peering","B":"AWS Transit Gateway","C":"Amazon Connect","D":"Security Groups"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Transit Gateway acts as a central network hub that connects hundreds or thousands of VPCs across multiple Regions through a single managed gateway, eliminating the complexity of managing individual point-to-point connections.\\nVPC Peering creates individual one-to-one connections between VPC pairs, which becomes unmanageable at scale with hundreds of VPCs. Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service for managing customer communications and has nothing to do with network connectivity. Security Groups are instance-level firewalls that control inbound and outbound traffic rules and do not manage connections between VPCs."},{"id":"q289","question":"Why does every AWS Region contain multiple Availability Zones?","options":{"A":"Multiple Availability Zones allows you to build resilient and highly available architectures","B":"Multiple Availability Zones results in lower total cost compared to deploying in a single Availability Zone","C":"Multiple Availability Zones allows for data replication and global reach","D":"Multiple Availability Zones within a region increases the storage capacity available in that region"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Multiple Availability Zones within a Region are geographically separated, each with independent power, cooling, and networking, so a failure in one AZ does not affect others, allowing customers to build resilient and highly available architectures.\\nDeploying across multiple Availability Zones does not lower total cost, as running resources in multiple AZs typically increases cost compared to a single AZ deployment. Multiple Availability Zones provide redundancy within a Region but do not provide global reach, which requires deploying across multiple Regions or using a content delivery network. The number of Availability Zones in a Region does not increase the storage capacity available, as storage capacity is determined by the services and configurations a customer selects rather than the AZ count."},{"id":"q294","question":"Which of the following AWS services scale automatically without your intervention?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon S3","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"Amazon EMR","E":"Amazon EBS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 automatically scales to handle any amount of data and any request rate without manual intervention, and AWS Lambda scales automatically from zero to thousands of concurrent executions in response to incoming events.\\nAmazon EC2 does not scale automatically by default and requires customers to configure Auto Scaling groups to add or remove instances based on demand. Amazon EMR requires customers to manually resize clusters or configure managed scaling policies, and does not scale automatically without intervention. Amazon EBS volumes are fixed in size when provisioned and must be manually resized, and do not scale automatically in response to usage."},{"id":"q296","question":"How do ELBs improve the reliability of your application?","options":{"A":"By distributing traffic across multiple S3 buckets","B":"By replicating data to multiple availability zones","C":"By creating database Read Replicas","D":"By ensuring that only healthy targets receive traffic"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Elastic Load Balancers continuously monitor the health of registered targets and only route traffic to instances that pass health checks, ensuring unhealthy targets do not receive requests.\\nDistributing traffic across multiple S3 buckets describes a function that ELBs do not perform, as Elastic Load Balancers route traffic to compute targets such as EC2 instances, containers, and Lambda functions rather than object storage buckets. Replicating data to multiple Availability Zones is a feature of services such as Amazon RDS Multi-AZ, which synchronously replicates database data across AZs, and is not a function of Elastic Load Balancers. Creating database Read Replicas is a feature of Amazon RDS that offloads read traffic from a primary database instance, and is entirely unrelated to load balancing or application traffic routing."},{"id":"q298","question":"A customer is planning to move billions of images and videos to be stored on Amazon S3. The customer has approximately 60 petabytes of data to move. Which of the following AWS services is the best choice to transfer the data to AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Snowball","B":"Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration","C":"AWS Snowmobile","D":"Amazon VP"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Snowmobile is an exabyte-scale data transfer service delivered as a ruggedised 45-foot shipping container, capable of transferring up to 100 petabytes per unit, making it the appropriate choice for a 60 petabyte migration.\\nAWS Snowball is a physical data transport device suited for transferring up to 80 terabytes per device, which is far below the 60 petabyte requirement and would require an impractical number of devices compared to Snowmobile. Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration speeds up uploads to S3 by routing traffic through CloudFront edge locations over the internet, but is not suitable for petabyte-scale data migrations where physical transport is required. Amazon VPC is a networking service for provisioning isolated virtual networks within AWS, and has no role in physically or programmatically transferring large datasets into AWS."},{"id":"q299","question":"A company plans to migrate a large amount of archived data to AWS. The archived data must be maintained for a period of 5 years and must be retrievable within 5 hours of a request. What is the most cost-effective AWS storage service to use?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Glacier","B":"Amazon EFS","C":"Amazon S3 Standard","D":"Amazon EBS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon S3 Glacier provides low-cost archival storage with retrieval options including Expedited (1 to 5 minutes), Standard (3 to 5 hours), and Bulk (5 to 12 hours). For a five-hour retrieval requirement, S3 Glacier Standard retrieval meets the SLA at the lowest cost.\\nAmazon EFS is a managed shared network file system that provides concurrent file access from multiple instances, and is designed for active file workloads rather than long-term archival storage where cost is the primary concern. Amazon S3 Standard provides durable, highly available object storage with immediate millisecond retrieval, and while it meets the retrieval requirement it is significantly more expensive per gigabyte than S3 Glacier for storing five years of infrequently accessed archival data. Amazon EBS provides persistent block storage volumes attached directly to EC2 instances for low-latency disk access, and is a compute-attached storage service not designed for long-term archival or for storing data independently of a running instance."},{"id":"q304","question":"Which of the following are use cases for Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"Hosting static websites","B":"Hosting websites that require sustained high CPU utilization","C":"Cost-effective database and log storage","D":"A media store for the CloudFront service","E":"Processing data streams at any scale"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 can host static websites by serving HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files directly, and it serves as an origin store for Amazon CloudFront, delivering media content globally through edge caching.\\nHosting websites that require sustained high CPU utilization describes a compute-intensive workload that requires EC2 instances or similar compute services, as S3 is an object storage service with no CPU processing capability. Cost-effective database and log storage conflates two different use cases, as S3 can store log files but is not a database service and purpose-built database services such as RDS or DynamoDB are more appropriate for structured data. Processing data streams at any scale describes the function of Amazon Kinesis, which is designed for real-time data stream ingestion and processing, not Amazon S3."},{"id":"q308","question":"A company is planning to migrate a database with high read/write activity to AWS. What is the best storage option to use?","options":{"A":"AWS Storage Gateway","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon EBS","D":"Amazon S3 Glacier"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon EBS provides high-performance block storage volumes that attach directly to EC2 instances, delivering the low-latency I/O needed for databases with high read/write activity.\\nAWS Storage Gateway is a hybrid storage service that connects on-premises environments to AWS cloud storage, and is not designed for high-performance database workloads requiring low-latency block storage. Amazon S3 is an object storage service optimized for storing and retrieving large amounts of unstructured data, and does not provide the low-latency block-level I/O that databases with high read/write activity require. Amazon S3 Glacier is an archival storage service designed for data that is rarely accessed and can tolerate retrieval times of hours, making it entirely unsuitable for active database workloads."},{"id":"q310","question":"What is the AWS service that provides five times the performance of a standard MySQL database?","options":{"A":"Amazon Aurora","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon Neptune"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Aurora is a MySQL and PostgreSQL-compatible relational database that delivers up to five times the throughput of standard MySQL, with the reliability and availability of a commercial database at a fraction of the cost.\\nAmazon Redshift is a fully managed data warehouse service optimized for running complex analytical queries across large datasets, and is not a MySQL-compatible relational database designed for high-throughput transactional workloads. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database that supports key-value and document data models, and does not offer MySQL compatibility or the relational capabilities that Aurora provides. Amazon Neptune is a fully managed graph database service designed for storing and querying highly connected data, and has no MySQL compatibility or relation to the performance characteristics described."},{"id":"q311","question":"What does AWS Service Catalog provide?","options":{"A":"It enables customers to quickly find descriptions and use cases for AWS services","B":"It enables customers to explore the different catalogs of AWS services","C":"It simplifies organizing and governing commonly deployed IT services","D":"It allows developers to deploy infrastructure on AWS using familiar programming languages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Service Catalog allows organizations to create and manage approved catalogs of IT services, enabling standardized governance and self-service deployment of commonly used resources across the organization.\\nProviding descriptions and use cases for AWS services describes the function of the AWS documentation and the AWS console service pages, not AWS Service Catalog, which is a governance and deployment tool rather than an informational reference resource. Enabling customers to explore different catalogs of AWS services describes the AWS Marketplace, where customers can find and purchase third-party software and services, rather than AWS Service Catalog, which manages internally approved IT products. Allowing developers to deploy infrastructure using familiar programming languages describes the AWS Cloud Development Kit, which lets developers define cloud infrastructure in languages such as Python, TypeScript, and Java, and is a developer tooling service rather than a catalog management and governance platform."},{"id":"q313","question":"Which of the following AWS services helps with planning application migration to the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS Snowball","B":"AWS Application Discovery Service","C":"AWS DMS","D":"AWS Migration Hub"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Application Discovery Service collects information about on-premises servers, including configuration, usage, and dependencies, to help plan and prioritize migration to the AWS Cloud.\\nAWS Snowball is a physical device used to transfer large volumes of data into AWS and is a data transfer tool rather than a migration planning service. AWS Database Migration Service migrates databases to AWS with minimal downtime, but handles the execution of database migrations rather than the discovery and planning phase. AWS Migration Hub provides a central location to track the progress of migrations across multiple AWS and partner tools, but it monitors migration status rather than collecting the server data needed to plan a migration."},{"id":"q315","question":"Which statement best describes the concept of an AWS region?","options":{"A":"An AWS Region is a geographical location with a collection of Edge locations","B":"An AWS Region is a virtual network dedicated only to a single AWS customer","C":"An AWS Region is a geographical location with a collection of Availability Zones","D":"An AWS Region represents the country where the AWS infrastructure exist"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An AWS Region is a physical geographic location that contains a collection of Availability Zones, each consisting of one or more discrete data centers with redundant power, networking, and connectivity.\\nA geographical location with a collection of edge locations confuses Regions with the CloudFront edge network, which is a separate layer of infrastructure distributed independently of Regions. A virtual network dedicated only to a single AWS customer describes an Amazon VPC, not a Region, as Regions are shared physical infrastructure used by all AWS customers. A country where AWS infrastructure exists is too broad and inaccurate, as multiple Regions can exist within the same country and a Region is a specific defined geographic area, not a national boundary."},{"id":"q317","question":"Which of the following are factors in determining the appropriate database technology to use for a specific workload?","options":{"A":"Availability Zones","B":"Data sovereignty","C":"The number of reads and writes per second","D":"The nature of the queries","E":"Software bugs"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"The number of reads and writes per second determines throughput requirements and helps identify whether a database needs to handle high-volume transactional workloads, and the nature of the queries determines whether a relational database for complex joins or a NoSQL database for simple key-value lookups is the better fit.\\nAvailability Zones are an infrastructure consideration for deploying databases with high availability and redundancy, but they do not influence which database technology or engine is appropriate for a given workload. Data sovereignty refers to legal and regulatory requirements about where data must be stored geographically, which affects Region selection but does not determine the appropriate database technology for a workload. Software bugs are a development and quality assurance concern unrelated to the criteria used to select an appropriate database technology."},{"id":"q324","question":"Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2 is considered a Serverless Web Service","B":"Amazon EC2 eliminates the need to invest in hardware upfront","C":"Amazon EC2 can launch as many or as few virtual servers as needed","D":"Amazon EC2 offers scalable computing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers that customers manage, including the operating system and software stack. It is a server-based compute service, not a serverless one. AWS Lambda is the serverless compute service where AWS manages all underlying infrastructure.\\nAmazon EC2 does eliminate the need to invest in hardware upfront, as customers pay only for the compute capacity they use rather than purchasing and maintaining physical servers. Amazon EC2 does allow customers to launch as many or as few virtual servers as needed, scaling capacity up or down in response to changing requirements. Amazon EC2 does offer scalable computing, enabling customers to increase or decrease capacity within minutes to match workload demands."},{"id":"q325","question":"What is the AWS compute service that executes code only when triggered by events?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","D":"Amazon EC2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that runs code in response to events such as HTTP requests, file uploads, or database changes, without requiring you to provision or manage servers.\\nAmazon CloudWatch is a monitoring and observability service that collects metrics and logs from AWS resources, and does not execute application code. AWS Elastic Beanstalk is a platform service that deploys and manages web applications by automatically provisioning the underlying infrastructure, but it runs continuously on provisioned servers rather than executing code only in response to events. Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers where customers are responsible for provisioning and managing the instances, making it a server-based solution rather than an event-driven serverless compute service."},{"id":"q326","question":"Both AWS and traditional IT distributors provide a wide range of virtual servers to meet their customers\' requirements. What is the name of these virtual servers in AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS Snapshots","B":"Amazon VP","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"Amazon EC2 Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon EC2 instances are virtual servers in the AWS Cloud that provide resizable compute capacity, allowing customers to run applications on AWS infrastructure just as they would on physical servers.\\nAmazon EBS Snapshots are point-in-time backups of EBS storage volumes, not virtual servers. Amazon VPC is a networking service that provides an isolated virtual network environment within AWS, not a virtual server. Amazon Lightsail also provides virtual servers but is designed for simpler workloads with a more limited and fixed configuration, whereas EC2 offers the full range of instance types and customization options comparable to traditional IT virtual server offerings."},{"id":"q331","question":"What are the benefits of using DynamoDB?","options":{"A":"Automatically scales to meet required throughput capacity","B":"Provides resizable instances to match the current demand","C":"Supports both relational and non-relational data models","D":"Offers extremely low (single-digit millisecond) latency","E":"Supports the most popular NoSQL database engines such as CouchDB and MongoDB"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB automatically scales throughput capacity to handle traffic fluctuations and delivers consistent single-digit millisecond latency at any scale, making it ideal for high-performance applications.\\nProviding resizable instances describes the EC2 model of vertical scaling, whereas DynamoDB is serverless and has no concept of instances that customers provision or resize. DynamoDB is a NoSQL database service that supports key-value and document data models only, and does not support relational data models. DynamoDB is a proprietary AWS NoSQL service and does not support or run third-party database engines such as CouchDB or MongoDB."},{"id":"q337","question":"Which AWS service can be used to send promotional text messages (SMS) to more than 200 countries worldwide?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","B":"Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3)","C":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","D":"Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Simple Notification Service supports sending SMS text messages to over 200 countries worldwide, in addition to push notifications and email, making it the purpose-built solution for promotional messaging at scale.\\nAmazon Simple Email Service is an email sending service designed for transactional and marketing email communications, and does not support SMS text messaging. Amazon Simple Storage Service is an object storage service for storing and retrieving data such as files, images, and backups, and has no messaging or SMS capability of any kind. Amazon Simple Queue Service is a fully managed message queuing service that enables decoupling of application components by passing messages between them, and is an application integration tool rather than a service for sending SMS messages to end users."},{"id":"q338","question":"Which of the following allows you to create new RDS instances?","options":{"A":"AWS CodeDeploy","B":"AWS Quick Starts","C":"AWS CloudFormation","D":"AWS DMS","E":"AWS Management Console"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"AWS CloudFormation can define and provision RDS instances through infrastructure-as-code templates, and the AWS Management Console provides a graphical interface for creating RDS instances directly.\\nAWS CodeDeploy is a deployment service that automates releasing application code to compute targets such as EC2 instances and Lambda functions, and has no capability to create or provision RDS database instances. AWS Quick Starts are pre-built deployment templates that help customers launch AWS environments following best practices, but they are a reference architecture resource rather than a service that directly creates or provisions RDS instances. AWS DMS migrates existing databases to AWS with minimal downtime, and is a migration tool rather than a service for creating new RDS instances from scratch."},{"id":"q350","question":"Which of the following Amazon RDS features facilitates offloading of database read activity?","options":{"A":"Database Snapshots","B":"Multi-AZ Deployments","C":"Automated Backups","D":"Read Replicas"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon RDS Read Replicas create read-only copies of your database that handle read queries, offloading read traffic from the primary instance and improving overall application performance.\\nDatabase Snapshots are point-in-time backups of an RDS instance stored in Amazon S3, used for data recovery and restoration rather than for distributing or offloading read activity. Multi-AZ Deployments maintain a synchronous standby replica of the database in a different Availability Zone for high availability and automatic failover, but the standby instance does not serve read traffic and exists solely for redundancy. Automated Backups enable point-in-time recovery by continuously backing up the database and transaction logs, and are a data protection feature rather than a mechanism for offloading read activity."},{"id":"q353","question":"A company has a web application that is hosted on a single EC2 instance and is approaching 100 percent CPU utilization during peak loads. Rather than scaling the server vertically, the company has decided to deploy three Amazon EC2 instances in parallel and to distribute traffic across the three servers. What AWS service should the company use to distribute the traffic evenly?","options":{"A":"AWS Global Accelerator","B":"Application Load Balancer","C":"Amazon CloudFront","D":"AWS Transit Gateway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An Application Load Balancer distributes incoming HTTP and HTTPS traffic across multiple EC2 instances, automatically routing requests to healthy targets and balancing the load evenly across the fleet.\\nAWS Global Accelerator improves the availability and performance of applications for global users by routing traffic through the AWS global network to the nearest healthy endpoint, and is a global traffic routing service rather than a tool for distributing load evenly across instances within a single deployment. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and serves content from edge locations worldwide to reduce latency for end users, and is a content distribution service rather than a load balancing solution for distributing application traffic across EC2 instances. AWS Transit Gateway acts as a central network hub connecting multiple VPCs and on-premises networks, and is a network connectivity service with no capability to distribute application traffic across compute instances."},{"id":"q354","question":"Which of the following approaches will help you eliminate human error and automate the process of creating and updating your AWS environment?","options":{"A":"Use Software test automation tools","B":"Use AWS CodeDeploy to build and automate your AWS environment","C":"Use code to provision and operate your AWS infrastructure","D":"Migrate all of your applications to a dedicated host"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Infrastructure as code uses tools such as AWS CloudFormation to define and provision AWS resources programmatically through templates, eliminating manual console steps and making environment creation repeatable, consistent, and automated.\\nSoftware test automation tools are used to automate testing of application code, such as running unit tests or integration tests, and do not address the provisioning and configuration of AWS infrastructure. AWS CodeDeploy is a deployment service that automates application deployments to EC2 instances, Lambda functions, or on-premises servers; it handles application code deployment but does not provision or configure the underlying AWS infrastructure environment. Migrating applications to a dedicated host changes the underlying physical server isolation model for compliance or licensing purposes, but does not automate or codify the process of creating and updating the AWS environment."},{"id":"q357","question":"Which of the following factors should be considered when determining the region in which AWS Resources will be deployed?","options":{"A":"The AWS Region\'s security level","B":"Data sovereignty","C":"Cost","D":"The planned number of VPCs","E":"Geographic proximity to the company\'s location"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Data sovereignty requirements dictate that data must stay in specific geographic locations for legal or regulatory reasons, and pricing varies between Regions, making both critical factors when choosing where to deploy AWS resources.\\nAll AWS Regions are built to the same security standards, so the security level of a Region is not a differentiating factor when selecting where to deploy. The planned number of VPCs is an architectural decision made after Region selection and has no bearing on which Region to choose. Geographic proximity to the company\'s location is not a primary selection factor, as latency considerations relate to where end users are located rather than where the company itself is based."},{"id":"q358","question":"You are running a financial services web application on AWS. The application uses a MySQL database to store data. Which of the following AWS services would improve the performance of your application by allowing you to retrieve information from fast in-memory caches?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic File System (EFS)","B":"Amazon Neptune","C":"Amazon ElastiCache","D":"Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon ElastiCache is a managed in-memory caching service supporting Redis and Memcached that stores frequently accessed data in memory, delivering sub-millisecond response times for read-heavy applications such as a financial services web application backed by MySQL.\\nAmazon Elastic File System is a managed shared network file system designed for concurrent access from multiple compute instances, and is a file storage service with no in-memory caching capability. Amazon Neptune is a fully managed graph database service optimized for storing and querying highly connected data such as social networks and knowledge graphs, and is a specialized database rather than an in-memory caching layer. Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) is an in-memory caching service specifically designed to accelerate Amazon DynamoDB read performance, and is tightly coupled to DynamoDB rather than being a general-purpose caching layer compatible with a MySQL database."},{"id":"q359","question":"What are the advantages of using Auto Scaling Groups for EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"Auto Scaling Groups caches the most recent responses at global edge locations to reduce latency and improve performance","B":"Auto Scaling Groups scales EC2 instances in multiple Availability Zones to increase application availability and fault tolerance","C":"Auto Scaling Groups scales EC2 instances across multiple regions to reduce latency for global users","D":"Auto Scaling Groups distributes application traffic across multiple Availability Zones to enhance performance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Auto Scaling Groups automatically adjust the number of EC2 instances across multiple Availability Zones based on demand, increasing both application availability and fault tolerance by ensuring capacity matches load.\\nCaching responses at global edge locations to reduce latency describes Amazon CloudFront, not Auto Scaling Groups, which have no caching capability. Auto Scaling Groups operate within a single Region and cannot scale EC2 instances across multiple Regions, so cross-region latency reduction is not a function they provide. Distributing application traffic across multiple Availability Zones describes Elastic Load Balancing, which works alongside Auto Scaling Groups but is a separate service responsible for traffic distribution."},{"id":"q363","question":"A company is seeking to deploy an existing .NET application onto AWS as quickly as possible. Which AWS Service should the customer use to achieve this goal?","options":{"A":"Amazon SNS","B":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk is a managed application deployment service that provisions the underlying infrastructure, handles deployment, capacity provisioning, load balancing, and auto scaling, enabling rapid deployment of existing .NET applications with minimal configuration.\\nAmazon SNS is a publish/subscribe messaging service used to fan out notifications to subscribers such as email, SMS, or other applications, and does not provide any capability to deploy or run application code. AWS Systems Manager is an operations management service used for tasks such as patching, inventory, and configuration management of existing resources, and is not a platform for deploying .NET applications. AWS Trusted Advisor is an advisory service that inspects your AWS environment and provides recommendations on cost optimization, performance, security, and fault tolerance, and cannot be used to deploy applications."},{"id":"q365","question":"Which AWS service helps enterprises extend their on-premises storage to AWS in a cost-effective manner?","options":{"A":"AWS Transfer Family","B":"AWS Storage Gateway","C":"Amazon Aurora","D":"Amazon EFS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Storage Gateway connects on-premises environments to AWS cloud storage, providing a hybrid storage solution that allows organizations to extend their existing on-premises storage infrastructure to AWS in a cost-effective way by tiering infrequently accessed data to the cloud.\\nAWS Transfer Family provides managed file transfer workflows using SFTP, FTPS, and FTP protocols to move files directly into and out of Amazon S3 or EFS, and is a data transfer service rather than a hybrid storage extension solution for on-premises environments. Amazon Aurora is a fully managed, high-performance relational database engine compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL, and is a database service rather than a storage extension solution for on-premises data. Amazon EFS is a managed, scalable NFS file system accessible by multiple EC2 instances in the cloud, and while it provides file storage, it is a cloud-native service rather than a gateway for extending on-premises storage capacity to AWS."},{"id":"q366","question":"A company is building an online cloud storage platform. They need a storage service that can scale capacity automatically, while minimizing cost. Which AWS storage service should the company use to meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Storage Service","B":"Amazon Elastic Block Store","C":"Amazon Elastic Container Service","D":"AWS Storage Gateway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon S3 provides virtually unlimited object storage that scales automatically with usage and charges only for what you store, making it ideal for cloud storage platforms that need elastic capacity at low cost.\\nAmazon Elastic Block Store provides persistent block storage volumes that attach directly to EC2 instances for low-latency disk access, and has a fixed provisioned capacity model where you pay for the volume size you provision rather than scaling automatically based on objects stored. Amazon Elastic Container Service is a container orchestration service that manages the deployment and scaling of containerised applications, and is a compute service rather than a storage service for an object storage platform. AWS Storage Gateway connects on-premises environments to AWS cloud storage for hybrid storage integration, and is designed for bridging on-premises infrastructure with AWS rather than serving as the underlying storage for a cloud-native storage platform."},{"id":"q374","question":"Which of the following AWS offerings are serverless services?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"AWS Lambda","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon EMR","E":"Amazon RDS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS Lambda runs code in response to events without requiring any server provisioning or management, and Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database that scales automatically without customers needing to manage any underlying infrastructure. Both are serverless services where AWS manages all underlying infrastructure.\\nAmazon EC2 provides virtual servers where customers are responsible for provisioning, configuring, and managing the instances, making it a server-based rather than serverless compute service. Amazon EMR runs big data processing frameworks on provisioned clusters of EC2 instances, requiring customers to manage the underlying cluster infrastructure rather than abstracting it away. Amazon RDS is a managed relational database service, but customers must select and manage the underlying instance type, size, and maintenance windows, meaning it is a managed rather than fully serverless service."},{"id":"q376","question":"Which AWS service provides the ability to automate the configuration, management, and patching of EC2 instances at scale?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS Systems Manager","C":"AWS Auto Scaling","D":"AWS CloudFormation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Systems Manager provides a suite of tools for automating operational tasks across EC2 instances at scale, including configuration management, patch management, run command execution, and inventory collection without needing to log into individual instances.\\nAWS Config records and evaluates configuration changes to AWS resources for compliance and auditing purposes, but does not automate configuration or patching of instances. AWS Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of EC2 instances based on demand but does not manage instance configuration or patching. AWS CloudFormation automates the provisioning of AWS infrastructure through templates but does not handle ongoing configuration management or patching of running instances."},{"id":"q377","question":"A customer is seeking to store objects in their AWS environment and to make those objects downloadable over the internet. Which AWS Service can be used to accomplish this?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon EFS","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon Instance Store"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 stores objects that can be made publicly accessible via unique URLs, making it the standard AWS service for hosting and serving downloadable content over the internet.\\nAmazon EBS provides block storage volumes that attach to individual EC2 instances and are not accessible directly over the internet as downloadable objects. Amazon EFS provides a shared file system mountable across EC2 instances via NFS protocol and is not designed for serving objects as publicly downloadable content over the internet. Amazon Instance Store provides temporary block storage physically attached to the host server running an EC2 instance, is ephemeral by nature, and cannot be accessed over the internet."},{"id":"q378","question":"Which of the following services can be used to monitor the HTTP and HTTPS requests that are forwarded to Amazon CloudFront?","options":{"A":"AWS WAF","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS Cloud9","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch monitors HTTP and HTTPS requests forwarded to CloudFront distributions, providing metrics such as request count, error rates, and latency that can be visualised and used to set alarms.\\nAWS WAF is a web application firewall that filters and blocks malicious HTTP and HTTPS requests based on defined rules, but it inspects and controls traffic rather than monitoring and reporting on request metrics. AWS Cloud9 is a cloud-based integrated development environment for writing, running, and debugging code, and has no monitoring or traffic analysis capability. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity across AWS services for auditing and compliance purposes, and does not monitor HTTP and HTTPS request metrics for CloudFront distributions."},{"id":"q383","question":"An organization has a legacy application designed using monolithic-based architecture. Which AWS service can be used to decouple the components of the application?","options":{"A":"Amazon SQS","B":"Amazon SNS","C":"Amazon EventBridge","D":"AWS Step Functions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SQS is a fully managed message queuing service that decouples application components by allowing each component to send and receive messages independently, enabling a monolithic application to be broken into loosely coupled parts that communicate asynchronously.\\nAmazon SNS is a publish/subscribe messaging service that broadcasts messages to multiple subscribers simultaneously, which is useful for fan-out notification patterns but is not the primary service for decoupling monolithic application components through asynchronous message queuing. Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus that routes events between AWS services, SaaS applications, and custom applications based on event patterns; while it supports decoupled architectures, it is event-driven routing rather than a message queue for decoupling component dependencies in a monolithic application. AWS Step Functions is a serverless workflow orchestration service that coordinates multiple AWS services into structured workflows, which handles workflow state management rather than providing the message queuing mechanism needed to decouple monolithic application components."},{"id":"q385","question":"According to best practices, which of the below options is best suited for processing a large number of binary files?","options":{"A":"Vertically scaling EC2 instances","B":"Running RDS instances in parallel","C":"Vertically scaling RDS instances","D":"Running EC2 instances in parallel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Running multiple EC2 instances in parallel distributes the processing of large binary files across many machines simultaneously, completing the work faster than scaling a single instance vertically.\\nVertically scaling EC2 instances increases the power of a single machine, which has practical limits and does not distribute the workload across multiple processors simultaneously, making it less efficient for large parallel processing tasks. Running RDS instances in parallel is not applicable to processing binary files, as RDS is a relational database service designed for structured data storage and querying rather than file processing workloads. Vertically scaling RDS instances increases database capacity and performance, which is also irrelevant to processing binary files as RDS serves no role in compute-based file processing tasks."},{"id":"q389","question":"Which AWS Service offers an NFS file system that can be mounted concurrently from multiple EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic File System","B":"Amazon Simple Storage Service","C":"Amazon Elastic Block Store","D":"AWS Storage Gateway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon EFS provides a fully managed NFS file system that can be mounted concurrently by thousands of EC2 instances, enabling shared file storage across multiple servers simultaneously.\\nAmazon S3 is an object storage service accessed via API or HTTP rather than mounted as a file system, and does not support NFS protocol or concurrent mounting by EC2 instances. Amazon EBS provides block storage volumes that can only be attached to a single EC2 instance at a time and does not support concurrent mounting across multiple instances. AWS Storage Gateway is a hybrid storage service that connects on-premises environments to AWS cloud storage and is not designed for concurrent NFS mounting across EC2 instances."},{"id":"q390","question":"Availability Zones within a Region are connected over low-latency links. Which of the following is a benefit of these links?","options":{"A":"Create private connection to your data center","B":"Achieve global high availability","C":"Automate the process of provisioning new compute resources","D":"Make synchronous replication of your data possible"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Low-latency links between Availability Zones within a Region enable synchronous data replication, meaning data written in one AZ is immediately confirmed as written in another before the operation completes, supporting high availability within a Region.\\nCreating a private connection to a data center describes AWS Direct Connect, which is a separate networking service and has nothing to do with the internal links between Availability Zones. Achieving global high availability requires deploying across multiple Regions rather than relying on AZ links within a single Region, which only provides availability at a regional level. Automating the provisioning of compute resources describes services like EC2 Auto Scaling and is unrelated to the low-latency connectivity that exists between Availability Zones."},{"id":"q391","question":"Which of the following are true regarding the languages that are supported on AWS Lambda?","options":{"A":"Lambda only supports Python and Node.js, but third party plugins are available to convert code in other languages to these formats","B":"Lambda natively supports a number of programming languages such as Node.js, Python, and Java","C":"Lambda is AWS\' proprietary programming language for microservices","D":"Lambda doesn\'t support programming languages; it is a serverless compute service","E":"Lambda can support any programming language using an API"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"AWS Lambda natively supports multiple programming languages including Node.js, Python, Java, Go, and .NET, and can support virtually any other language through custom runtimes via the Lambda Runtime API.\\nLambda only supporting Python and Node.js is false, as Lambda has native support for a broad range of languages without any third-party plugins. Lambda being AWS\'s proprietary programming language is false, as Lambda is a compute service that runs code written in standard programming languages, not a language itself. Lambda not supporting programming languages is false and contradicts how the service works, as running code in a supported language is its core function."},{"id":"q392","question":"What are the capabilities of AWS X-Ray?","options":{"A":"Automatically decouples application components","B":"Facilitates tracking of user requests to identify application issues","C":"Helps improve application performance","D":"Deploys applications to Amazon EC2 instances","E":"Deploys applications to on-premises servers"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS X-Ray traces user requests as they travel through your application, helping you identify performance bottlenecks and errors, and providing the visibility needed to improve overall application performance.\\nAutomatically decoupling application components describes a design architecture pattern, not a capability of any single AWS service. AWS X-Ray observes and analyzes existing application behavior but does not modify application architecture. Deploying applications to Amazon EC2 instances describes AWS CodeDeploy, which automates application deployments to compute services including EC2. Deploying applications to on-premises servers also describes AWS CodeDeploy, which supports deployment targets beyond AWS including on-premises servers, making it unrelated to application tracing or performance analysis."},{"id":"q393","question":"Which of the following is true regarding the AWS availability zones and edge locations?","options":{"A":"Edge locations are located in separate Availability Zones worldwide to serve global customers","B":"An availability zone exists within an edge location to distribute content globally with low latency","C":"An Availability Zone is a geographic location where AWS provides multiple, physically separated and isolated edge locations","D":"An AWS Availability Zone is an isolated location within an AWS Region, however edge locations are located in multiple cities worldwide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An Availability Zone is an isolated data center location within an AWS Region with independent power, cooling, and networking, while edge locations are separate infrastructure points distributed across hundreds of cities worldwide to serve content closer to end users.\\nEdge locations are located in separate Availability Zones worldwide to serve global customers edge locations are an independent layer of infrastructure and are not located within or associated with Availability Zones. An availability zone exists within an edge location to distribute content globally with low latency the relationship is inverted here, and Availability Zones are part of Regions, not contained within edge locations. An Availability Zone is a geographic location where AWS provides multiple, physically separated and isolated edge locations this confuses the definition of an Availability Zone with that of a Region, and edge locations are not nested inside Availability Zones."},{"id":"q396","question":"Which AWS Service allows customers to create a template that programmatically defines policies and configurations of all AWS resources as code and so that the same template can be reused among multiple projects?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudFormation","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Auto Scaling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS CloudFormation lets you define your entire infrastructure as reusable templates in JSON or YAML, enabling consistent and repeatable provisioning of the same resource configurations across multiple projects.\\nAWS Config continuously monitors and records the configuration state of existing AWS resources and evaluates them against desired settings, but does not provision infrastructure from templates. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and user activity across your AWS account for auditing and compliance purposes, unrelated to infrastructure provisioning. AWS Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of compute resources in response to demand but does not define or provision infrastructure configurations as reusable templates."},{"id":"q398","question":"A customer is planning to migrate their Microsoft SQL Server databases to AWS. Which AWS Services can the customer use to run their Microsoft SQL Server database on AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Fargate","B":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"AWS Database Migration service (DMS)","E":"AWS Lambda"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Microsoft SQL Server can run on Amazon EC2 as a self-managed installation with full OS-level control, or on Amazon RDS as a fully managed engine where AWS handles patching, backups, and high availability automatically.\\nAWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for running containers and does not support running a SQL Server database directly as a managed or self-managed database engine. AWS Database Migration Service is used to migrate databases from one source to a target with minimal downtime, but it is a migration tool rather than a platform for running SQL Server on an ongoing basis. AWS Lambda is a serverless function execution service that runs event-driven code and is not designed to host or run database engines such as SQL Server."},{"id":"q399","question":"Which AWS service monitors the availability of endpoints and automatically routes traffic away from unhealthy resources?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudFormation","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"Amazon Aurora"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 performs health checks on endpoints, monitoring their availability and automatically routing traffic away from unhealthy resources to maintain application uptime through DNS failover.\\nAWS CloudFormation provisions and manages AWS infrastructure using code-based templates, and does not monitor endpoint availability or route traffic. Amazon CloudWatch monitors metrics, logs, and alarms for AWS resources, but does not perform DNS-based routing or redirect traffic away from unhealthy endpoints. Amazon Aurora is a managed relational database engine and has no endpoint monitoring or traffic routing capability."},{"id":"q400","question":"A company is developing an application that will leverage facial recognition to automate photo tagging. Which AWS service should the company use for facial recognition?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Textract","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Rekognition uses deep learning to analyze images and videos, providing facial recognition, object detection, and scene analysis capabilities that can automate tasks like photo tagging.\\nAmazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service that analyzes and extracts meaning from written text, and has no capability to process images or perform facial recognition. Amazon Textract extracts printed text, handwriting, and structured data from scanned documents and images, but does not perform facial recognition or analyze visual scenes. Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service that converts written text into natural-sounding audio, and has no image analysis or facial recognition capability."},{"id":"q401","question":"Which of the following are examples of AWS-managed databases?","options":{"A":"Amazon Neptune","B":"Amazon CloudSearch","C":"Microsoft SQL Server on Amazon EC2","D":"MySQL on Amazon EC2","E":"Amazon RDS for MySQL"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Amazon Neptune is a fully managed graph database, and Amazon RDS for MySQL is a fully managed relational database, with AWS handling provisioning, patching, backups, and recovery for both.\\nAmazon CloudSearch is a managed search service for building search functionality into applications, and is not a database service. Microsoft SQL Server on Amazon EC2 is a self-managed database deployment where the customer is responsible for installation, patching, backups, and maintenance, making it the opposite of an AWS-managed database. MySQL on Amazon EC2 is also a self-managed deployment with the same customer responsibilities, and does not benefit from the managed database features that AWS provides through RDS."},{"id":"q404","question":"You need to migrate a large number of on-premises workloads to AWS. Which AWS service is the most appropriate?","options":{"A":"AWS File Transfer Acceleration","B":"AWS Application Migration Service","C":"AWS Database Migration Service","D":"AWS Application Discovery Service"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Application Migration Service automates the migration of on-premises server workloads to AWS by continuously replicating source servers and allowing cutover with minimal downtime, making it the recommended service for large-scale server migrations.\\nAWS File Transfer Acceleration is not a real AWS service and does not exist in the AWS service catalog. AWS Database Migration Service is purpose-built for migrating databases rather than entire server workloads, making it too narrow for general on-premises workload migration. AWS Application Discovery Service gathers information about on-premises environments to help plan migrations but does not perform the migration itself."},{"id":"q405","question":"What are some key benefits of using AWS CloudFormation?","options":{"A":"It helps AWS customers deploy their applications without worrying about the underlying infrastructure","B":"It applies advanced IAM security features automatically","C":"It automates the provisioning and updating of your infrastructure in a safe and controlled manner","D":"It allows you to model your entire infrastructure in just a text file","E":"It compiles and builds application code in a timely manner"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"AWS CloudFormation allows you to model your entire infrastructure in a JSON or YAML text file, and automates the provisioning and updating of resources in a safe, controlled, and repeatable manner.\\nDeploying applications without worrying about underlying infrastructure describes AWS Elastic Beanstalk, which abstracts infrastructure management for application deployment, not CloudFormation. Applying advanced IAM security features automatically is not a CloudFormation capability, as IAM policies and security configurations must still be defined and managed by the customer. Compiling and building application code is a function of build tools such as AWS CodeBuild, not CloudFormation, which deals with infrastructure provisioning rather than application code."},{"id":"q407","question":"A company is hosting business critical workloads in an AWS Region. To protect against data loss and ensure business continuity, a mirror image of the current AWS environment should be created in another AWS Region. Company policy requires that the standby environment must be available in minutes in case of an outage in the primary AWS Region. Which AWS service can be used to meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","B":"AWS Application Migration Service","C":"AWS Backup","D":"AWS Glue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery continuously replicates machines into a staging area in another AWS Region and can launch fully provisioned instances within minutes during an outage, providing fast cross-Region failover.\\nAWS Application Migration Service is a server migration tool designed to move workloads to AWS, not to maintain a live standby environment for disaster recovery purposes. AWS Backup is a centralized backup service that automates data protection across AWS services, but does not maintain a continuously replicated standby environment that can be activated within minutes. AWS Glue is a serverless data integration service used for ETL workloads and data cataloguing, and has no disaster recovery or replication capability."},{"id":"q408","question":"Which of the following S3 storage classes is most appropriate to host static assets for a popular e-commerce website with stable access patterns?","options":{"A":"S3 Standard-IA","B":"S3 Intelligent-Tiering","C":"S3 Glacier Deep Archive","D":"S3 Standard"},"correct":"D","explanation":"S3 Standard is designed for frequently accessed data with low latency and high throughput, making it the most appropriate storage class for static assets on a popular e-commerce site with consistent, stable access patterns.\\nS3 Standard-IA is designed for data that is accessed infrequently but requires rapid access when needed, and carries a per-retrieval charge that makes it more expensive than S3 Standard for assets that are accessed consistently and frequently. S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves objects between access tiers based on changing access patterns, making it better suited for data with unpredictable or unknown access frequency rather than assets with stable, predictable demand. S3 Glacier Deep Archive is the lowest-cost storage class designed for long-term archival of data that is rarely if ever accessed, and is entirely unsuitable for serving live website assets that require immediate low-latency retrieval."},{"id":"q409","question":"You want to create a backup of your data in another geographical location. Where should you create this backup?","options":{"A":"In another Edge location","B":"In another Region","C":"In another VPC","D":"In another Availability Zone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Storing backups in another AWS Region places your data in a completely separate geographic location, protecting against Region-level disasters such as natural catastrophes or widespread outages.\\nEdge locations are points of presence used by services such as Amazon CloudFront to cache and deliver content to users with low latency, and are not storage locations where backups can be created or maintained. A VPC is a logically isolated virtual network within a single AWS Region, and storing data in another VPC does not place it in a different geographical location. An Availability Zone is a physically separate data center within a single AWS Region, and while storing backups in another AZ improves resilience against localised failures, it does not satisfy the requirement for a different geographical location as multiple AZs share the same Region."},{"id":"q412","question":"Which of the following is a feature of Amazon RDS that performs automatic failover when the primary database fails to respond?","options":{"A":"RDS Single-AZ","B":"RDS Read Replicas","C":"RDS Snapshots","D":"RDS Multi-AZ"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon RDS Multi-AZ maintains a synchronous standby replica in a different Availability Zone and automatically fails over to it if the primary database becomes unresponsive, minimizing downtime.\\nRDS Single-AZ deploys the database in a single Availability Zone with no standby instance, meaning there is no automatic failover if the primary database becomes unavailable. RDS Read Replicas create read-only copies of the database to offload read traffic from the primary instance, but they are not synchronised in real time and do not provide automatic failover for high availability. RDS Snapshots are point-in-time backups used for data recovery and restoration, and cannot perform automatic failover in response to a database failure."},{"id":"q421","question":"A company experiences fluctuations in traffic patterns to their e-commerce website when running flash sales. What service can help the company dynamically match the required compute capacity to handle spikes in traffic during flash sales?","options":{"A":"AWS Auto Scaling","B":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud","C":"Amazon Elastic File System","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of EC2 instances in response to real-time demand, adding capacity during traffic spikes like flash sales and removing it when demand drops to control costs.\\nAmazon EC2 provides the virtual server instances that run the application, but on its own it does not automatically add or remove capacity in response to traffic fluctuations. Amazon Elastic File System is a managed shared file storage service for use with EC2 and other compute services, unrelated to dynamic compute scaling. Amazon ElastiCache is an in-memory caching service for improving database and application read performance, not a service for dynamically adjusting compute capacity."},{"id":"q422","question":"Which of the below options is true of Amazon VPC?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC allows customers to control user interactions with all other AWS resources","B":"AWS Customers have complete control over their Amazon VPC virtual networking environment","C":"AWS is responsible for all the management and configuration details of Amazon VPC","D":"Amazon VPC helps customers to review their AWS architecture and adopt best practices"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon VPC gives customers complete control over their virtual networking environment, including selection of IP address ranges, creation of subnets, and configuration of route tables and network gateways.\\nControlling user interactions with AWS resources is the function of AWS IAM, which manages permissions and access policies, not Amazon VPC. AWS is not responsible for all management and configuration of Amazon VPC, as customers retain full responsibility for configuring their own subnets, route tables, security groups, and network ACLs. Reviewing AWS architecture and adopting best practices describes the AWS Well-Architected Tool, not Amazon VPC, which is a networking service rather than an architectural review service."},{"id":"q425","question":"Which AWS service can be used to automate the configuration and management of EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"AWS OpsWorks","B":"AWS CloudFormation","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Systems Manager provides a suite of tools to automate the configuration, maintenance, and management of EC2 instances at scale, including applying configuration policies, running commands across fleets, and maintaining desired state without manual intervention.\\nAWS OpsWorks (deprecated) was a configuration management service using Puppet and Chef that has been discontinued by AWS as of May 2024 and is no longer available. AWS CloudFormation automates the provisioning of AWS infrastructure using templates, but operates at the infrastructure level rather than managing the ongoing configuration of running instances. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity for auditing and compliance purposes, and has no server configuration or automation capability."},{"id":"q427","question":"Select the services that are server-based:","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"AWS Fargate","E":"Amazon EMR"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Amazon RDS runs on provisioned database server instances that customers select and manage, and Amazon EMR runs on clusters of EC2 instances for big data processing, making both server-based services.\\nAmazon DynamoDB is a fully serverless NoSQL database that scales automatically without any server provisioning or management required. AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that runs code in response to events without requiring customers to provision or manage any servers. AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers that removes the need to provision or manage the underlying EC2 instances."},{"id":"q429","question":"Which of the following are use cases for Amazon EMR?","options":{"A":"Enables you to backup extremely large amounts of data at very low costs","B":"Enables you to move Exabyte-scale data from on-premises datacenters into AWS","C":"Enables you to analyze and process extremely large amounts of data in a timely manner","D":"Enables you to easily run and scale Apache Spark, Hadoop,and other Big Data frameworks","E":"Enables you to easily run and manage Docker containers"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Amazon EMR provides a managed cluster platform for running big data frameworks like Apache Spark and Hadoop, enabling both the analysis and processing of extremely large datasets at scale.\\nBacking up extremely large amounts of data at very low costs describes Amazon S3 combined with S3 Glacier storage classes, which are purpose-built for durable, low-cost data storage and archival. Moving exabyte-scale data from on-premises data centers into AWS describes AWS Snowmobile, which is a physical data transfer service for migrating extremely large datasets using a secure shipping container. Running and managing Docker containers describes Amazon ECS or AWS Fargate, which are container orchestration services unrelated to big data processing frameworks."},{"id":"q431","question":"A developer wants to quickly deploy and manage his application in the AWS Cloud, but he doesn\'t have any experience with cloud computing. Which of the following AWS services would help the developer achieve his goal?","options":{"A":"AWS Fargate","B":"AWS Batch","C":"Amazon Personalize","D":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk automatically handles the deployment details of capacity provisioning, load balancing, auto-scaling, and application health monitoring, allowing developers without cloud infrastructure experience to deploy and manage applications without needing to understand the underlying infrastructure.\\nAWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers that removes the need to manage servers for containerised workloads, but it requires knowledge of container concepts and configuration, making it more complex to adopt for developers without cloud experience compared to the abstraction Beanstalk provides. AWS Batch manages the infrastructure required to run batch computing jobs at scale, and is designed for processing large numbers of batch workloads rather than helping developers quickly deploy and manage general-purpose applications. Amazon Personalize is a fully managed machine learning service that builds real-time personalization and recommendation systems, and is an AI/ML service for a specific use case rather than a general application deployment and management platform."},{"id":"q433","question":"For Amazon RDS databases, what does AWS perform on your behalf?","options":{"A":"Database setup","B":"Network traffic protection","C":"Management of the operating system","D":"Access management","E":"Management of firewall rules"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"For Amazon RDS, AWS handles the initial database setup including provisioning and configuration, and manages the underlying operating system including patching and maintenance, removing these operational burdens from the customer.\\nNetwork traffic protection is a shared responsibility where customers configure security groups and NACLs to control traffic to their RDS instances, rather than AWS managing this on the customer\'s behalf. Access management is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers control their own IAM policies and database user credentials. Management of firewall rules is the customer\'s responsibility, as customers define the security group rules that control inbound and outbound access to their RDS instances."},{"id":"q435","question":"A media company has an application that requires the transfer of large data sets to and from AWS every day. This data is business critical and should be transferred over a consistent connection. Which AWS service should the company use?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"AWS Snowmobile","D":"AWS VPN"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection between an on-premises data center and AWS, providing consistent throughput and lower latency than internet-based connections, making it the right choice for daily large-scale business-critical data transfers.\\nAmazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service for extracting insights and relationships from text and has no role in network connectivity or data transfer. AWS Snowmobile is a physical data transfer service for one-time migration of extremely large datasets to AWS, not for recurring daily transfers requiring a consistent network connection. AWS VPN establishes an encrypted connection over the public internet, which does not provide the consistent throughput and reliability guarantees required for business-critical daily large data transfers."},{"id":"q436","question":"What is the main benefit of the AWS Storage Gateway service?","options":{"A":"It automates the process of building, maintaining, and running ETL jobs","B":"It provides physical devices to migrate data from on premises to AWS","C":"It allows integration of on-premises IT environments with Cloud Storage","D":"It provides hardware-based key storage for regulatory compliance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Storage Gateway integrates on-premises IT environments with AWS cloud storage by providing standard storage protocols such as NFS, SMB, and iSCSI that connect local applications seamlessly to S3, EBS, or Glacier.\\nAutomating the process of building, maintaining, and running ETL jobs describes AWS Glue, which is a serverless data integration service for preparing and transforming data for analytics pipelines. Providing physical devices to migrate data from on-premises to AWS describes the AWS Snow Family, specifically AWS Snowball or Snowmobile, which are physical appliances for bulk data transfer rather than ongoing hybrid storage integration. Providing hardware-based key storage for regulatory compliance describes AWS CloudHSM, which is a dedicated hardware security module for generating and managing cryptographic keys."},{"id":"q437","question":"To protect against data loss, you need to backup your database regularly. What is the most cost-effective storage option that provides immediate retrieval of your backups?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive","B":"Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access","C":"Amazon S3 Glacier","D":"Instance Store"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access offers lower storage costs than S3 Standard while still providing immediate, millisecond retrieval, making it cost-effective for backups that are accessed infrequently but need to be available instantly.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive is the lowest-cost storage class for long-term archival data that is accessed very rarely, with retrieval times of 12 hours or more, making it unsuitable for backups that require immediate retrieval. Amazon S3 Glacier provides low-cost archival storage with retrieval options ranging from minutes to hours depending on the retrieval tier selected, and while less expensive than S3 Standard it does not provide the immediate millisecond retrieval required for backup recovery. Instance Store provides temporary block storage physically attached to the host machine running an EC2 instance, and is ephemeral storage that is lost when the instance stops, making it entirely unsuitable for durable backup storage."},{"id":"q438","question":"Which service can you use to route traffic to the endpoint that provides the best application performance for your users worldwide?","options":{"A":"AWS Global Accelerator","B":"AWS Data Pipeline","C":"Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","D":"Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator uses the AWS global network to route user traffic to the optimal healthy endpoint based on performance, geography, and health checks, improving application performance and availability for global users.\\nAWS Data Pipeline (deprecated) is a service for orchestrating and automating the movement and transformation of data between AWS services, unrelated to traffic routing or application performance. Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator is an in-memory cache specifically for DynamoDB that reduces read latency for database queries, not a service for routing application traffic to optimal endpoints. Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration speeds up uploads to S3 buckets by routing traffic through CloudFront edge locations, which is specific to S3 data transfer and not a general application traffic routing solution."},{"id":"q440","question":"Which of the below options are use cases of the Amazon Route 53 service?","options":{"A":"Point-to-point connectivity between an on-premises data center and AWS","B":"Detects configuration changes in the AWS environment","C":"DNS configuration and management","D":"Manages global application traffic through a variety of routing types","E":"Provides infrastructure security optimization recommendations"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Amazon Route 53 is a scalable DNS service that translates domain names to IP addresses, and it manages global application traffic through routing policies including latency-based, geolocation, weighted, and failover routing.\\nPoint-to-point connectivity between an on-premises data center and AWS describes AWS Direct Connect, which provides a dedicated private network link between on-premises infrastructure and AWS. Detecting configuration changes in the AWS environment describes AWS Config, which continuously monitors and records resource configurations and evaluates them against desired settings. Providing infrastructure security optimization recommendations describes AWS Trusted Advisor, which analyzes your environment against best practice checks across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance."},{"id":"q441","question":"You want to transfer 200 Terabytes of data from on-premises locations to the AWS Cloud. Which of the following can do the job in a cost-effective way?","options":{"A":"AWS Snowmobile","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS DMS","D":"AWS Snowball"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Snowball is a physical data transfer device that moves up to 80 TB per device, making it cost-effective for transferring 200 TB of data without relying on slow or expensive network transfers.\\nAWS Snowmobile is designed for migrations at exabyte scale, typically 10 PB or more, making it far more than necessary and not cost-effective for a 200 TB transfer. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network link between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, but transferring 200 TB over a network connection would be slow and costly compared to physical data transport. AWS DMS is purpose-built for migrating databases rather than transferring large volumes of unstructured data between on-premises locations and AWS."},{"id":"q442","question":"You have a real-time IoT application that requires sub-millisecond latency. Which of the following services should you use?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon Athena","C":"AWS Cloud9","D":"Amazon ElastiCache for Redis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon ElastiCache for Redis is an in-memory data store that delivers sub-millisecond response times, making it the appropriate choice for a real-time IoT application requiring extremely low latency.\\nAmazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse service optimized for running analytical SQL queries across large datasets, and is designed for throughput-oriented batch analytics rather than sub-millisecond real-time data access. Amazon Athena is a serverless query service that analyzes data stored in Amazon S3 using SQL, and involves query execution times measured in seconds rather than the sub-millisecond latency that real-time IoT applications require. AWS Cloud9 is a cloud-based integrated development environment for writing, running, and debugging code, and is a developer tool with no data storage or low-latency serving capability."},{"id":"q444","question":"Which AWS service helps developers compile and test their code?","options":{"A":"AWS CodeDeploy","B":"AWS CodeCommit","C":"AWS CodePipeline","D":"AWS CodeBuild"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS CodeBuild is a fully managed continuous integration service that compiles source code, runs tests, and produces deployment-ready software packages without requiring you to manage build servers.\\nAWS CodeDeploy automates the deployment of applications to EC2 instances, Lambda functions, and on-premises servers, but does not compile or test code. AWS CodeCommit is a managed source control service for hosting Git repositories, used for storing and versioning code rather than building or testing it. AWS CodePipeline orchestrates the stages of a release pipeline including source, build, and deploy phases, but delegates the actual compilation and testing to CodeBuild rather than performing it directly."},{"id":"q446","question":"What does the Amazon CloudFront service provide?","options":{"A":"Tracks user activity and API usage","B":"Increases application availability by caching at the edge","C":"Enables faster disaster recovery","D":"Stores archived data at very low costs","E":"Delivers content to end users with low latency"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Amazon CloudFront is a CDN that caches content at edge locations worldwide, increasing application availability through redundant caching and delivering content to end users with low latency regardless of their location.\\nTracking user activity and API usage across an AWS account describes AWS CloudTrail, an audit logging service that is unrelated to content delivery or edge caching. Faster disaster recovery is a benefit associated with multi-region architecture and services like AWS Backup or Route 53 failover routing, not a capability provided by a content delivery network. Storing archived data at very low costs describes Amazon S3 Glacier, an archival storage service with no content delivery or edge caching capability."},{"id":"q447","question":"You are facing a lot of problems with your current contact center. Which service provides a cloud-based contact center that can deliver a better service for your customers?","options":{"A":"Amazon Lightsail","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service that lets you set up a customer service center in minutes with pay-per-use pricing and no infrastructure to manage, making it the purpose-built solution for replacing a traditional contact center.\\nAmazon Lightsail is a simplified cloud platform for deploying small applications and websites with predictable pricing, and has no contact center or customer communication functionality. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and is a networking service entirely unrelated to contact center operations. AWS Elastic Beanstalk is a PaaS service for deploying and managing web applications, not a contact center or customer service platform."},{"id":"q451","question":"You manage a blog on AWS that has different environments: development, testing, and production. What can you use to create a custom console for each environment to view and manage your resources easily?","options":{"A":"AWS Resource Groups","B":"AWS Placement Groups","C":"AWS Management Console","D":"AWS Tag Editor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Resource Groups let you create logical groups of resources based on tags, enabling you to view and manage resources for each environment such as development, testing, and production from a custom console view.\\nAWS Placement Groups control how EC2 instances are physically placed on underlying hardware to optimize for latency or availability, and have no role in organizing or viewing resources by environment. AWS Management Console is the web-based interface for accessing all AWS services, but it does not provide custom grouped views per environment on its own. AWS Tag Editor is a tool for finding and managing tags across resources, but it does not create custom console views for grouped resource management."},{"id":"q452","question":"Which AWS service collects metrics from running EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS CloudFormation","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch collects and monitors metrics from EC2 instances including CPU utilization, disk I/O, and network traffic, providing visibility into instance performance and health.\\nAmazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and does not collect performance metrics. AWS CloudFormation is an infrastructure-as-code service that provisions and manages AWS resources through templates, and has no monitoring or metrics collection capability. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity across AWS services for auditing and compliance purposes, and does not collect instance-level performance metrics."},{"id":"q453","question":"Your web application currently faces performance issues and suffers from long load times. Which of the following AWS services could help fix these issues and improve performance?","options":{"A":"Amazon Detective","B":"AWS X-Ray","C":"AWS Security Hub","D":"AWS Shield"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS X-Ray traces requests as they travel through your application, identifying performance bottlenecks and helping you pinpoint the root cause of long load times and latency issues.\\nAmazon Detective is a security investigation service that analyzes and visualises data from AWS logs to help identify the root cause of security findings and suspicious activity, and has no capability to diagnose application performance issues or load times. AWS Security Hub aggregates and prioritizes security findings from multiple AWS services and third-party tools into a centralized dashboard, and is a security posture management tool rather than an application performance diagnostics service. AWS Shield is a managed DDoS protection service that safeguards applications against distributed denial-of-service attacks, and does not address application performance bottlenecks or load time issues."},{"id":"q454","question":"Which of the following compute resources are serverless?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"AWS Fargate","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"Amazon ECS","E":"Amazon EMR"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS Fargate runs containers without requiring you to provision or manage the underlying servers, and AWS Lambda runs code in response to events without provisioning any infrastructure. Both are serverless compute services where AWS handles all underlying infrastructure management.\\nAmazon EC2 provides virtual servers where customers are responsible for provisioning, configuring, and managing the instances, making it a server-based rather than serverless compute service. Amazon ECS is a container orchestration service that, when used without Fargate, requires customers to manage the underlying EC2 instances in the cluster. Amazon EMR is a managed big data processing service that runs on provisioned clusters of EC2 instances, not a serverless compute resource."},{"id":"q457","question":"An organization needs to build a financial application that requires support for ACID transactions. Which AWS database service is most appropriate in this case?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon RDS","C":"AWS CloudHSM","D":"AWS DMS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon RDS is a managed relational database service that supports ACID transactions natively through engines such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, and SQL Server, making it ideal for financial applications requiring data consistency.\\nAmazon Redshift is a cloud data warehouse service optimized for analytical queries across large datasets, and while it has some transactional support it is not designed for the high-volume ACID transaction processing required by financial applications. AWS CloudHSM is a hardware security module service that provides dedicated cryptographic key storage and processing, and has no database or transaction management capability. AWS DMS is a database migration service used to move data between database platforms, and is not a database engine that can host or process application transactions."},{"id":"q460","question":"Amazon RDS supports multiple database engines to choose from. Which of the following is not one of them?","options":{"A":"PostgreSQL","B":"Oracle","C":"Microsoft SQL Server","D":"Teradata"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Teradata is not a supported engine in Amazon RDS. The supported engines are PostgreSQL, Oracle, Microsoft SQL Server, MySQL, MariaDB, and Amazon Aurora.\\nPostgreSQL is a fully supported RDS engine available for both standard and Aurora deployments. Oracle is a supported RDS engine, allowing customers to run Oracle databases on managed infrastructure. Microsoft SQL Server is a supported RDS engine, available across several editions including Express, Web, Standard, and Enterprise."},{"id":"q461","question":"Which of the following AWS services would help you migrate on-premise databases to AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS DMS","B":"Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration","C":"AWS Directory Service","D":"AWS Transit Gateway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Database Migration Service is purpose-built for migrating databases from on-premises to AWS, supporting both homogeneous migrations such as MySQL to MySQL and heterogeneous migrations between different database engines, with minimal downtime during the migration.\\nAmazon S3 Transfer Acceleration uses CloudFront edge locations to speed up uploads of data to S3 and is a file transfer acceleration feature rather than a database migration service. AWS Directory Service provides managed Microsoft Active Directory integration for enterprise identity management and has no capability to migrate or replicate relational or NoSQL database data. AWS Transit Gateway connects multiple VPCs and on-premises networks through a central hub for network routing and connectivity, and is a networking service that facilitates traffic routing rather than a database migration tool."},{"id":"q462","question":"For new AWS customers, what is the EASIEST way to launch a simple WordPress website on AWS?","options":{"A":"Run WordPress on an Amazon Lightsail instance","B":"Install WordPress on an Amazon EC2 instance","C":"Use the Amazon S3 Web hosting feature","D":"Host the website directly on AWS Cloud Development Kit (AWS CDK)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Lightsail provides pre-configured blueprints including a one-click WordPress installation, making it the easiest option for new customers who want a simple website without managing underlying infrastructure.\\nInstalling WordPress on an EC2 instance requires manually provisioning a server, configuring networking, installing a web stack, and managing ongoing maintenance, making it significantly more complex than Lightsail for a simple use case. Amazon S3 can host static websites but does not support WordPress, which requires server-side processing and a database. AWS CDK is an infrastructure as code framework for defining cloud resources programmatically and cannot directly host websites or applications."},{"id":"q464","question":"Which of the following services allows you to install and run custom relational database software?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon Cognito","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers with full operating system-level access, allowing you to install and run any custom database software of your choice on the underlying instance.\\nAmazon Cognito is a user identity and authentication service for web and mobile applications that manages user sign-up, sign-in, and access control, and has no capability to host or run database software. Amazon RDS is a managed relational database service that supports specific pre-configured database engines such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server, and does not allow you to install or run custom database software outside of those supported engines. Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans AWS workloads for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and has no role in hosting or running database software."},{"id":"q465","question":"Your application requirements for CPU and RAM are changing in an unpredictable way. Which service can be used to dynamically adjust these resources based on load?","options":{"A":"AWS Auto Scaling","B":"Elastic Load Balancing","C":"Amazon Route 53","D":"Amazon Elastic Container Service"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Auto Scaling dynamically adjusts the number of EC2 instances based on real-time demand metrics such as CPU and memory utilization, automatically adding or removing capacity as workload requirements change.\\nElastic Load Balancing distributes incoming traffic across multiple targets to improve availability and prevent any single instance from being overwhelmed, but it does not add or remove compute resources in response to changing CPU and RAM requirements. Amazon Route 53 is a DNS and traffic routing service that resolves domain names and directs users to application endpoints, and has no capability to provision or adjust compute resources. Amazon Elastic Container Service is a container orchestration service for running and managing containerised applications, and while it can scale tasks, it is not the purpose-built solution for dynamically adjusting EC2 compute resources based on unpredictable load."},{"id":"q471","question":"For some services, AWS automatically replicates data across multiple Availability Zones to provide fault tolerance in the event of a server failure or Availability Zone outage. Which services automatically replicate data across Availability Zones?","options":{"A":"Instance Store","B":"S3","C":"DynamoDB","D":"Amazon Route 53","E":"AWS VPN"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 automatically replicates objects across a minimum of three Availability Zones within a Region, and DynamoDB replicates data across multiple AZs by default, both providing built-in fault tolerance.\\nInstance Store is temporary block storage attached directly to the host EC2 instance. It is not replicated and data is lost if the instance stops or fails. Amazon Route 53 is a DNS and traffic routing service and does not replicate data across AZs in the same way. AWS VPN is a network connectivity service for securely connecting on-premises networks to AWS, unrelated to data replication."},{"id":"q475","question":"How can you protect data stored on Amazon S3 from accidental deletion?","options":{"A":"By enabling S3 Versioning","B":"By configuring S3 Bucket Policies","C":"By configuring S3 Lifecycle Policies","D":"By disabling S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"S3 Versioning keeps every version of every object in the bucket, so even if an object is accidentally deleted, the previous version is retained and can be restored to recover the data.\\nConfiguring S3 Bucket Policies controls who has permission to access, upload, or delete objects in a bucket, but does not preserve deleted objects or maintain previous versions for recovery. Configuring S3 Lifecycle Policies automates the transition of objects between storage classes or schedules their expiration after a defined period, which can actually result in objects being deleted rather than protecting them from accidental deletion. Disabling S3 Cross-Region Replication removes the ability to copy objects to a bucket in another Region for redundancy purposes, and has no effect on preventing or recovering from accidental deletion in the source bucket."},{"id":"q478","question":"Which of the following is NOT a benefit of using AWS Lambda?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda runs code without provisioning or managing servers","B":"AWS Lambda provides resizable compute capacity in the cloud","C":"There is no charge when your AWS Lambda code is not running","D":"AWS Lambda can be called directly from any mobile app"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Lambda functions cannot be invoked directly from a mobile app without using an intermediary API layer such as Amazon API Gateway or AWS AppSync; direct invocation from arbitrary external code is not supported, making this statement false rather than a genuine benefit.\\nLambda running code without provisioning or managing servers is a genuine benefit; it eliminates the need to select, patch, or maintain underlying compute infrastructure. Lambda does provide resizable compute capacity, automatically scaling from a few requests per day to thousands per second based on the incoming trigger volume. There is genuinely no charge when Lambda code is not running; billing is based on the number of requests and the duration of execution, making it cost-free during idle periods."},{"id":"q483","question":"Which AWS service allows you to build a data warehouse in the cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Redshift is a fully managed petabyte-scale cloud data warehouse that uses columnar storage and parallel query execution to deliver fast analytics on large datasets.\\nAWS Shield is a managed DDoS protection service that safeguards AWS applications against distributed denial-of-service attacks, and has no connection to data storage or analytics. Amazon RDS is a fully managed relational database service supporting transactional workloads, and is designed for operational databases with frequent read/write activity rather than the large-scale analytical query workloads that a data warehouse serves. Amazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service that analyzes written text to extract insights such as sentiment, entities, and key phrases, and is an AI service with no data warehousing capability."},{"id":"q485","question":"Which of the following services is an AWS repository management system that allows for storing, versioning, and managing your application code?","options":{"A":"AWS CodePipeline","B":"AWS CodeCommit","C":"AWS X-Ray","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CodeCommit is a fully managed source control service that hosts private Git repositories, providing secure storage, versioning, and collaboration features for application code.\\nAWS CodePipeline automates the steps of a continuous delivery pipeline for releasing application code, including building, testing, and deploying, and is a CI/CD orchestration tool rather than a repository management service for storing and versioning code. AWS X-Ray provides distributed tracing for applications to visualise request paths and diagnose latency and performance issues, and has no source control or repository management capability. Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and is a security tool entirely unrelated to code storage or versioning."},{"id":"q486","question":"Which AWS service can be used to route end users to the nearest AWS Region to reduce latency?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Global Accelerator","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Amazon Route 53"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 supports latency-based routing, which directs user requests to the AWS Region that provides the lowest latency for the end user, routing traffic to the endpoint that will respond fastest based on measured network conditions.\\nAmazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches content at edge locations to reduce latency for end users, but it caches static and dynamic content rather than routing application traffic to the nearest Region based on latency measurements. AWS Global Accelerator improves global application availability and performance by routing traffic over the AWS global network to the nearest healthy endpoint, and while it also reduces latency, the question specifically asks about routing end users to the nearest AWS Region. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection between an on-premises data center and AWS, reducing latency on hybrid workloads, but it does not route end users to the nearest AWS Region for cloud-hosted applications."},{"id":"q490","question":"Which of the following procedures can reduce latency when your end users are retrieving data?","options":{"A":"Store media assets in the region closest to your end users","B":"Store media assets on an additional EBS volume and increase the capacity of your server","C":"Replicate media assets to at least two availability zones","D":"Reduce the size of media assets using the Amazon Elastic Transcoder","E":"Store media assets in S3 and use CloudFront to distribute these assets"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Storing media assets in the Region closest to end users reduces the physical distance data must travel, and using Amazon CloudFront with S3 caches content at edge locations worldwide, both directly reducing retrieval latency for end users.\\nAdding an EBS volume and increasing server capacity addresses storage and compute performance but does not reduce the network distance between the origin and end users. Replicating assets across multiple Availability Zones improves availability and fault tolerance within a Region but does not reduce latency for geographically distant users. Amazon Elastic Transcoder converts media files into different formats for playback on various devices and has no effect on the latency of content delivery to end users."},{"id":"q494","question":"A company wants to use Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) to run its containerized applications. For compliance reasons, the company wants to retain complete visibility and control over the underlying server cluster. Which Amazon ECS launch type will satisfy these requirements?","options":{"A":"EC2 launch type","B":"Fargate launch type","C":"Lightsail launch type","D":"Lambda launch type"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The EC2 launch type runs containers on EC2 instances that you provision and manage, giving you full visibility and control over the underlying server cluster including instance types, operating system, and cluster configuration.\\nThe Fargate launch type is a serverless compute engine for containers where AWS manages the underlying infrastructure completely, abstracting away the server cluster so customers have no visibility or control over the underlying servers, which is the opposite of what the compliance requirement demands. Lightsail launch type is not a valid ECS launch type; Amazon Lightsail is a simplified compute and hosting service for straightforward workloads and is not an ECS launch option. Lambda launch type is not a valid ECS launch type; AWS Lambda runs serverless functions in response to events and is not a launch mechanism for ECS containerised applications."},{"id":"q498","question":"You have just finished writing your application code. Which service can be used to automate the deployment and scaling of your application?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Storage Service","B":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","C":"AWS CodeCommit","D":"Amazon Elastic File System"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk automatically handles deployment, capacity provisioning, load balancing, auto-scaling, and application health monitoring, letting you focus on writing code rather than managing infrastructure.\\nAmazon Simple Storage Service is an object storage service for storing and retrieving data, and has no capability to deploy or scale application code. AWS CodeCommit is a managed Git-based source control service for storing and versioning code repositories, and is a version control tool rather than a deployment or scaling service. Amazon Elastic File System is a managed shared network file system for use with AWS compute services, and is a storage service with no application deployment or scaling capability."},{"id":"q500","question":"Amazon EC2 instances are conceptually very similar to traditional servers. However, using Amazon EC2 server instances in the same manner as traditional hardware server instances is only a starting point. What are the main benefits of using the AWS EC2 instances instead of traditional servers?","options":{"A":"Improves Fault-Tolerance","B":"Provides your business with a seamless remote accessibility","C":"Prevents unauthorized users from getting into your network","D":"Provides automatic data backups","E":"Can be scaled manually in a shorter period of time"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"EC2 instances improve fault tolerance through features such as Auto Scaling, Multi-AZ deployment, and Elastic Load Balancing integration, and can be scaled up or down manually in minutes rather than the weeks required to procure and provision physical hardware.\\nSeamless remote accessibility is available with both traditional servers and EC2 instances and is not a differentiating benefit of EC2 over physical hardware. Preventing unauthorised users from accessing your network is a security responsibility that applies equally to both environments and is not an inherent EC2 advantage over traditional servers. Automatic data backups are not provided by EC2 by default, as customers must configure backup solutions such as AWS Backup or EBS snapshots separately."},{"id":"q502","question":"Which AWS service provides the EASIEST way to set up and manage a secure, well-architected, multi-account AWS environment?","options":{"A":"AWS Control Tower","B":"Amazon Macie","C":"AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager","D":"AWS Security Hub"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Control Tower automates the setup of a secure, well-architected multi-account environment based on AWS best practices, providing pre-configured guardrails and a landing zone dashboard for centralized governance.\\nAmazon Macie is a data security service that uses machine learning to discover and protect sensitive data stored in Amazon S3, and has no role in multi-account environment setup or governance. AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager automates the patching of operating systems and applications on EC2 instances and is not related to account architecture or multi-account management. AWS Security Hub aggregates and prioritizes security findings from multiple AWS services into a centralized dashboard, but does not set up or manage multi-account environments."},{"id":"q503","question":"A company is running a large web application that needs to always be available. The application tends to slow down when CPU usage is greater than 60%. How can they track when CPU usage goes above 60% for any of the EC2 instances in their account?","options":{"A":"Use Amazon CloudFront to monitor the CPU usage","B":"Set the AWS Config CPU threshold to 60% to receive a notification when EC2 usage exceeds that value","C":"Use Amazon CloudWatch Alarms to monitor the CPU and alert when the CPU usage is >= 60%","D":"Use Amazon Simple Notification Service (SNS) to monitor the utilization of the server"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch Alarms let you define thresholds on metrics such as CPU utilization and trigger notifications or automated actions when the threshold is breached, making it the purpose-built solution for alerting when CPU usage exceeds 60%.\\nAmazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and distributes content at edge locations worldwide to reduce latency for end users, and has no capability to monitor instance-level performance metrics such as CPU usage. AWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration state of AWS resources to assess compliance and detect configuration changes, and does not monitor real-time performance metrics or support CPU threshold alerting. Amazon Simple Notification Service is a messaging service that delivers notifications to subscribers, and while it can be used alongside CloudWatch to deliver alerts, it cannot itself monitor or evaluate CPU utilization metrics."},{"id":"q504","question":"What is the recommended storage option when hosting an often-changing database on an Amazon EC2 instance?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon RDS","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon DynamoDB"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon EBS provides persistent block storage volumes that attach to EC2 instances, delivering the consistent low-latency I/O performance needed for databases with frequently changing data.\\nAmazon RDS is a fully managed relational database service that runs on its own infrastructure, and is not a storage option for a database hosted directly on an EC2 instance. Amazon S3 provides object storage accessible via API calls and is designed for storing files, backups, and static assets rather than serving as the underlying block storage for a database engine running on EC2. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed serverless NoSQL database service that runs on AWS infrastructure, and is not a storage volume that can be attached to an EC2 instance."},{"id":"q505","question":"You are working as a site reliability engineer (SRE) in an AWS environment. Which of the following services helps monitor your applications?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"Amazon CloudSearch","C":"Amazon EMR","D":"Amazon CloudHSM"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch monitors applications and infrastructure by collecting metrics, logs, and events, providing dashboards and alarms that help SREs identify and respond to operational issues.\\nAmazon CloudSearch is a managed search service for building search functionality into applications, and is a search indexing tool with no application monitoring or observability capability. Amazon EMR is a managed big data platform for processing large datasets using frameworks such as Apache Hadoop and Spark, and is a data processing service with no application monitoring capability. Amazon CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules for managing cryptographic keys, and is a security and key management service with no role in monitoring application performance or health."},{"id":"q510","question":"Which database service should you use if your application and data schema require \\"joins\\" or complex transactions?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon DocumentDB","D":"Amazon DynamoDB"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon RDS is a managed relational database service that supports SQL queries including joins and complex ACID transactions, making it the right choice for applications with structured, relational data schemas.\\nAmazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse service that supports SQL and joins but is optimized for analytical queries across large datasets rather than the transactional workloads that require ACID compliance. Amazon DocumentDB is a document database compatible with MongoDB that stores flexible JSON-like data and does not support relational joins or complex multi-table transactions. Amazon DynamoDB is a NoSQL key-value and document database designed for high-throughput low-latency access patterns, and does not support relational joins or complex transactional queries."},{"id":"q512","question":"What should you consider when storing data in Amazon S3 Glacier?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Glacier only accepts data in a compressed format","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier can only be used to store frequently accessed data and data archives","C":"Amazon S3 Glacier does not provide immediate retrieval of data","D":"Attach Amazon S3 Glacier to an EC2 instance to be able to store data"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 Glacier is designed for archival storage and does not provide immediate data retrieval. Retrieval times range from minutes to hours depending on the retrieval option selected, which makes it unsuitable for workloads requiring instant access to data.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier accepts data in any format and has no requirement for data to be compressed before storage. Amazon S3 Glacier is specifically designed for infrequently accessed archival data, not frequently accessed data, making the claim that it supports frequently accessed data factually incorrect. Amazon S3 Glacier is a standalone storage service accessed directly through the AWS console, API, or S3 lifecycle policies, and does not need to be attached to an EC2 instance to store data."},{"id":"q513","question":"Engineers are wasting a lot of time and effort managing batch computing software in traditional data centers. Which of the following AWS services allows them to easily run thousands of batch computing jobs?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"AWS Batch","C":"Lambda@Edge","D":"AWS Fargate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Batch automatically provisions the optimal quantity and type of compute resources based on the volume and requirements of batch jobs, removing the need to manage batch computing infrastructure manually and allowing engineers to focus on their workloads rather than the underlying infrastructure.\\nAmazon EC2 provides virtual servers that could run batch workloads, but requires engineers to manually provision, configure, and manage the instances and batch scheduling software themselves, which is exactly the problem the scenario describes. Lambda@Edge runs AWS Lambda functions at CloudFront edge locations to process content closer to end users, and is designed for lightweight event-driven tasks rather than large-scale batch computing jobs. AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for running containers without managing servers, but is designed for containerised application workloads rather than purpose-built batch job scheduling and management at scale."},{"id":"q518","question":"A company needs to host a big data application on AWS using EC2 instances. Which of the following AWS Storage services would they choose to automatically get high throughput to multiple compute nodes?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Block Store","B":"AWS Storage Gateway","C":"Amazon Elastic File System","D":"S3"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Elastic File System (EFS) provides a shared NFS file system that automatically scales and delivers high throughput to multiple EC2 instances concurrently, making it ideal for big data workloads that require parallel access across compute nodes.\\nAmazon Elastic Block Store provides block storage volumes that attach to a single EC2 instance at a time and cannot be simultaneously shared across multiple compute nodes for parallel access. AWS Storage Gateway is a hybrid storage service that connects on-premises environments to AWS cloud storage, and is not designed for high-throughput shared access between EC2 instances. Amazon S3 provides object storage accessible via API calls rather than a mounted file system, and does not deliver the low-latency, high-throughput shared file system access that big data workloads running on EC2 require."},{"id":"q521","question":"What is the benefit of Amazon EBS volumes being automatically replicated within the same availability zone?","options":{"A":"Elasticity","B":"Durability","C":"Traceability","D":"Accessibility"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EBS volumes are automatically replicated within their Availability Zone to protect against hardware component failures, ensuring that the data remains durable even if an individual physical drive fails within the underlying infrastructure.\\nElasticity refers to the ability to dynamically scale compute resources up or down based on demand, which is a capacity management concept unrelated to the automatic replication of EBS volume data within an AZ. Traceability refers to the ability to audit and log changes to resources and actions performed in an AWS environment, which is a governance and compliance concept rather than a storage characteristic related to data replication. Accessibility refers to the ability to reach or connect to resources, which describes network and permission connectivity rather than the durability protection that replication within an AZ provides for stored data."},{"id":"q526","question":"What is the Amazon ElastiCache service used for?","options":{"A":"Provide an in-memory data storage service","B":"Reduce delivery costs using Edge Locations","C":"Improve web application performance","D":"Provide a Chef-compatible cache to speed up application response","E":"Distribute requests to multiple instances"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Amazon ElastiCache provides a fully managed in-memory data store supporting Redis and Memcached, caching frequently accessed data to dramatically reduce database query latency and improve web application performance.\\nReducing delivery costs using edge locations describes Amazon CloudFront, which caches content geographically closer to users rather than providing in-memory database caching. Providing a Chef-compatible cache describes AWS OpsWorks, which uses Chef and Puppet for configuration management and has no role in in-memory data caching. Distributing requests to multiple instances describes Elastic Load Balancing, which routes incoming traffic across targets rather than storing or caching data in memory."},{"id":"q527","question":"The elasticity of the AWS Cloud enables customers to save costs when compared to traditional hosting providers. What can AWS customers do to benefit from the elasticity of the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Deploy your resources across multiple Availability Zones","B":"Use Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","C":"Deploy your resources in another region","D":"Use Elastic Load Balancing","E":"Use Serverless Computing whenever possible"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"EC2 Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of instances based on demand, ensuring customers pay only for what they use, and serverless computing charges only for actual execution time with no idle capacity costs, both directly leveraging cloud elasticity to reduce costs.\\nDeploying across multiple Availability Zones improves availability and fault tolerance but does not dynamically adjust resource capacity in response to demand. Deploying resources in another Region improves geographic reach and disaster recovery but is not a mechanism for elastic cost savings. Elastic Load Balancing distributes incoming traffic across existing instances but does not scale the number of instances up or down based on demand."},{"id":"q529","question":"Each AWS Region is composed of multiple Availability Zones. Which of the following best describes what an Availability Zone is?","options":{"A":"It is a data center designed to be completely isolated from other data centers in the same region","B":"It is a collection of data centers distributed in multiple countries","C":"It is a logically isolated network of the AWS Cloud","D":"It is a distinct location within a region that is insulated from failures in other Availability Zones"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An Availability Zone is a distinct location within a Region consisting of one or more data centers, designed to be isolated from failures in other AZs through independent power, cooling, and networking.\\nA single data center completely isolated from other data centers in the same Region is inaccurate, as an AZ can contain multiple data centers and is isolated from failures, not from all connectivity. A collection of data centers distributed across multiple countries describes an AWS Region or the global infrastructure as a whole, not an individual AZ. A logically isolated network of the AWS Cloud describes an Amazon VPC, not an Availability Zone."},{"id":"q532","question":"What is the maximum amount of data that can be stored in S3 in a single AWS account?","options":{"A":"100 petabytes","B":"Virtually unlimited storage","C":"5 terabytes","D":"10 exabytes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon S3 is designed to provide virtually unlimited storage capacity. There is no fixed maximum on the total amount of data an account can store, and S3 automatically scales to accommodate any volume of objects.\\n100 petabytes is a fixed storage limit that does not apply to Amazon S3; individual objects have a maximum size of 5 terabytes, but this is a per-object limit rather than a total account storage cap. 5 terabytes is the maximum size of a single S3 object, not the total storage limit for an account; the total amount of data stored across all objects in an account is not capped. 10 exabytes is a fixed storage limit that does not apply to Amazon S3; the service scales automatically to any volume without a declared maximum."},{"id":"q534","question":"Which AWS service delivers data, videos, applications, and APIs to users globally with low latency and high transfer speeds?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"Amazon CloudFront","D":"Amazon EC2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront is a global content delivery network that delivers data, videos, applications, and APIs to users worldwide with low latency and high transfer speeds through a network of edge locations.\\nAmazon Route 53 is a DNS and domain routing service that directs users to application endpoints based on routing policies, but it routes traffic rather than caching and delivering content at edge locations. Amazon Connect is a cloud contact center service for managing customer communications via voice and chat, and has no role in content delivery or global data transfer. Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers for running applications and workloads, but requires manual configuration to serve content globally and does not natively deliver it with low latency to users worldwide through edge locations."},{"id":"q537","question":"Which element of the AWS global infrastructure consists of one or more discrete data centers each with redundant power networking and connectivity which are housed in separate facilities?","options":{"A":"AWS Regions","B":"Availability Zones","C":"Edge locations","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An Availability Zone consists of one or more discrete data centers, each with redundant power, networking, and connectivity, housed in separate facilities within a Region.\\nAWS Regions are geographic areas that contain multiple Availability Zones, not individual data center clusters. Edge locations are points of presence used for content delivery and caching, not data center groupings with redundant infrastructure. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network service that uses edge locations, not a global infrastructure component in its own right."},{"id":"q538","question":"How many Availability Zones should compute resources be provisioned across to achieve high availability?","options":{"A":"A minimum of one","B":"A minimum of two","C":"A minimum of three","D":"A minimum of four or more"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A minimum of two Availability Zones is required for high availability. If one AZ fails, the application continues operating from the other with minimal disruption.\\nA minimum of one provides no redundancy. A single AZ failure would take the entire application down. A minimum of three or four or more exceeds the baseline requirement. While deploying across more AZs can increase resilience further, two is the recognized minimum for high availability in AWS architecture."},{"id":"q539","question":"The AWS Cloud\'s multiple Regions are an example of:","options":{"A":"Agility","B":"Global infrastructure","C":"Elasticity","D":"Pay-as-you-go pricing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Regions spread across the globe are part of the AWS global infrastructure, which includes Regions, Availability Zones, and edge locations designed to deliver services worldwide.\\nAgility refers to the speed at which customers can provision resources and iterate on new functionality, which is a cloud adoption benefit rather than a characteristic of physical infrastructure placement. Elasticity refers to dynamically scaling resources up or down in response to demand, which is a compute and capacity concept unrelated to the geographic distribution of Regions. Pay-as-you-go pricing is a billing model where customers pay only for what they consume, which is a commercial benefit rather than an infrastructure concept."},{"id":"q540","question":"Which AWS service can be used to manually launch instances based on resource requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon EC2","D":"Amazon ECS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon EC2 lets you manually launch virtual server instances with your choice of instance type, operating system, and configuration, providing full control over compute resource provisioning.\\nAmazon EBS provides persistent block storage volumes that attach to EC2 instances as disks, and is a storage service rather than a compute service capable of launching instances. Amazon S3 is an object storage service for storing and retrieving files and data, and has no capability to launch or run compute instances. Amazon ECS is a container orchestration service that manages the deployment and scaling of containerised applications, and while it does run tasks on underlying compute, it does not manually launch instances based on resource requirements in the way EC2 does."},{"id":"q544","question":"What is one of the advantages of the Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS)?","options":{"A":"It simplifies relational database administration tasks","B":"It provides 99.99999999999% reliability and durability","C":"It automatically scales databases for loads","D":"It enables users to dynamically adjust CPU and RAM resources"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon RDS simplifies relational database administration by automating time-consuming tasks like hardware provisioning, database setup, patching, and backups, letting customers focus on application development rather than infrastructure management.\\n99.9999999999% reliability and durability is a figure associated with Amazon S3\'s object durability, not a published SLA for Amazon RDS. Automatically scaling databases for loads describes Amazon Aurora Serverless, which scales compute capacity automatically based on demand, not a default behavior of standard RDS instances. Dynamically adjusting CPU and RAM resources describes the behavior of services with automatic vertical scaling, which RDS does not provide natively as customers must manually modify instance types to change compute resources."},{"id":"q545","question":"Which of the following AWS Cloud services can be used to run a customer-managed relational database?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"Amazon ElastiCache","D":"Amazon DynamoDB"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers with full operating system-level access, allowing you to install, configure, and manage any relational database software yourself, giving you complete control over the database configuration.\\nAmazon Route 53 is a DNS and traffic routing service that manages domain name resolution and directs users to application endpoints, and has no capability to host or run database software. Amazon ElastiCache is a managed in-memory caching service designed to accelerate application performance by storing frequently accessed data in memory, and is not a relational database platform. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL key-value and document database service, and is not a relational database and does not support customer-managed database software installation or configuration."},{"id":"q546","question":"A user is planning to launch two additional Amazon EC2 instances to increase availability. Which action should the user take?","options":{"A":"Launch the instances across multiple Availability Zones in a single AWS Region","B":"Launch the instances as EC2 Reserved Instances in the same AWS Region and the same Availability Zone","C":"Launch the instances in multiple AWS Regions but in the same Availability Zone","D":"Launch the instances as EC2 Spot Instances in the same AWS Region but in different Availability Zones"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Launching instances across multiple Availability Zones within a single Region provides high availability by ensuring that if one Availability Zone experiences a failure, the instances in the remaining zones continue serving traffic without interruption.\\nLaunching Reserved Instances in the same Region and Availability Zone secures a capacity reservation and reduces cost through a billing commitment, but concentrating both instances in a single Availability Zone creates a single point of failure with no availability benefit. Launching instances in multiple Regions but in the same Availability Zone type introduces geographic separation at the Regional level but does not protect against failures within a Region since Availability Zones within each Region remain independent failure domains. Launching Spot Instances in different Availability Zones uses spare EC2 capacity at reduced cost, but Spot Instances can be interrupted by AWS at any time, making them unsuitable as a reliability mechanism for increasing availability."},{"id":"q548","question":"Which AWS services allow companies to connect an Amazon VPC to an on-premises data center?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon API Gateway","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS VPN creates an encrypted connection over the public internet to connect on-premises networks to a VPC, and AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private physical network connection from an on-premises data center to AWS. Both are services that provide hybrid connectivity between on-premises infrastructure and the AWS Cloud.\\nAmazon Redshift is a fully managed cloud data warehousing service designed for running analytical SQL queries on large datasets, and is a database service with no capability to connect on-premises networks to AWS. Amazon API Gateway is a fully managed service for creating, publishing, and managing REST, HTTP, and WebSocket APIs, and is an API management service rather than a network connectivity solution for linking on-premises data centers to VPCs."},{"id":"q549","question":"Which AWS service or feature can be used to monitor CPU usage?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"VPC Flow Logs","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch collects and monitors CPU utilization metrics from EC2 instances by default, and can trigger alarms or display dashboards when usage exceeds defined thresholds.\\nAWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity across an AWS environment for auditing and compliance purposes, and does not collect or monitor instance-level performance metrics such as CPU usage. VPC Flow Logs capture information about IP traffic flowing to and from network interfaces within a VPC, and is a network traffic logging feature with no capability to monitor compute resource metrics such as CPU utilization. AWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration state of AWS resources to assess compliance and detect configuration changes, and does not monitor real-time performance metrics."},{"id":"q552","question":"Which storage service can be used as a low-cost option for hosting static websites?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 GLACIER","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS)","D":"Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon S3 can host static websites by serving HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and media files directly via HTTP, offering a low-cost, highly durable, and scalable solution without needing a web server.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier is an archival storage service designed for long-term retention of infrequently accessed data and does not support static website hosting or direct HTTP serving of content. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database service for storing and querying structured data, and is not a storage service capable of hosting website files. Amazon EFS provides a shared network file system for use with EC2 instances and does not support static website hosting or public HTTP access to files."},{"id":"q558","question":"A company has deployed several relational databases on Amazon EC2 instances. Every month the database software vendor releases new security patches that need to be applied to the databases. What is the MOST efficient way to apply the security patches?","options":{"A":"Connect to each database instance on a monthly basis and download and apply the necessary security patches from the vendor","B":"Enable automate patching for the instances using the Amazon RDS console","C":"In AWS Config, configure a rule for the instances and the required patch level","D":"Use AWS Systems Manager to automate database patching according to a schedule"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager automates the process of patching managed EC2 instances with security updates on a defined schedule, eliminating the need for manual intervention each time a vendor releases new patches.\\nConnecting to each database instance individually on a monthly basis to download and apply patches manually is the least efficient approach, requiring significant administrative effort that scales poorly as the number of instances grows. Amazon RDS console patching applies to databases managed by the RDS service, and cannot be used to patch database software running on self-managed EC2 instances where the customer is responsible for the operating system and software. AWS Config is a configuration compliance and change tracking service that can assess whether instances meet a required patch level, but does not itself automate or apply patches to instances."},{"id":"q559","question":"Which mechanism allows developers to access AWS services from application code?","options":{"A":"AWS Software Development Kit","B":"AWS Management Console","C":"AWS CodePipeline","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The AWS Software Development Kit provides language-specific libraries and APIs in languages including Python, Java, JavaScript, and Go, allowing developers to integrate AWS service calls directly into application code through programmatic interfaces.\\nThe AWS Management Console is a web-based graphical interface for interacting with AWS services through a browser, not a mechanism that developers embed in application code for programmatic service access. AWS CodePipeline is a continuous delivery service that automates build, test, and deploy stages of a release pipeline, and is a CI/CD tool for application deployment rather than a means for application code to interact with AWS services. AWS Config continuously tracks and records resource configuration changes and evaluates them against compliance rules, and is a governance and compliance service rather than a development tool for enabling application code to call AWS services."},{"id":"q563","question":"What is Amazon CloudWatch?","options":{"A":"A service that records API calls and account activity across an AWS environment for auditing purposes","B":"A service that monitors application performance by tracing requests as they travel through application components","C":"A service that collects metrics and operational data from AWS resources with customizable alarms and notification channels","D":"A service that captures and processes real-time data streams from applications and infrastructure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch is a monitoring service that acts as a metrics repository, collecting performance and operational data from AWS resources and allowing you to set customizable notification thresholds and alarm channels.\\nAWS CloudTrail records all API calls and account activity across an AWS environment and produces audit logs for governance and compliance purposes, but does not collect resource performance metrics or send operational alarms. AWS X-Ray traces requests as they travel through distributed application components to identify performance bottlenecks and errors, but is an application debugging tool rather than an infrastructure-wide metrics and alerting service. Amazon Kinesis captures and processes real-time data streams from applications and infrastructure at scale, but is a data streaming and ingestion service rather than a monitoring and observability platform."},{"id":"q570","question":"Which of the following services will automatically scale with an expected increase in web traffic?","options":{"A":"AWS CodePipeline","B":"Elastic Load Balancing","C":"Amazon EBS","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Elastic Load Balancing automatically scales its request handling capacity in response to incoming application traffic, distributing requests across healthy targets without manual intervention.\\nAWS CodePipeline automates the stages of a software release pipeline including source, build, and deploy phases, and has no capability for handling or scaling web traffic. Amazon EBS provides block storage volumes attached to EC2 instances that must be manually resized, and does not scale in response to web traffic. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and is a networking connectivity service unrelated to web traffic scaling."},{"id":"q571","question":"Which service provides a virtually unlimited amount of online highly durable object storage?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS)","C":"Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS)","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon S3 provides virtually unlimited, highly durable object storage with 99.999999999% durability, designed for storing and retrieving any amount of data from anywhere on the web.\\nAmazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse service for running analytical queries on large datasets, not an object storage service. Amazon EFS is a managed shared file system for use with compute instances, providing file storage rather than object storage and not designed for unlimited web-accessible data storage. Amazon ECS is a container orchestration service for running and managing Docker containers, entirely unrelated to object storage."},{"id":"q578","question":"Which AWS service can serve a static website?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"Amazon QuickSight","D":"AWS X-Ray"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon S3 can serve static websites by hosting HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and image files, delivering them directly via HTTP without needing to run a web server.\\nAmazon Route 53 is a DNS and domain registration service that routes traffic to endpoints, but does not host or serve website content itself. Amazon QuickSight is a business intelligence and data visualisation service for building dashboards and reports, and has no web hosting capability. AWS X-Ray is an application tracing and debugging service that helps analyze performance of distributed applications, and is entirely unrelated to hosting or serving website content."},{"id":"q580","question":"Which of the following are main components of the AWS global infrastructure?","options":{"A":"Resource groups","B":"Availability Zones","C":"Security groups","D":"Regions","E":"Amazon Machine Images (AMIS)"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Regions and Availability Zones are the two main building blocks of AWS global infrastructure, with Regions being geographic areas and each Region containing multiple isolated Availability Zones.\\nResource groups are a management tool used to organize and tag AWS resources within an account, and are not a component of the physical global infrastructure. Security groups are virtual firewalls that control inbound and outbound traffic for AWS resources, and are a networking feature rather than an infrastructure component. Amazon Machine Images are templates used to launch EC2 instances containing the operating system and configuration, and are a compute feature rather than part of the global infrastructure hierarchy."},{"id":"q584","question":"Which services can be used across hybrid AWS Cloud architectures?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"Virtual Private Gateway","C":"Classic Load Balancer","D":"Auto Scaling","E":"Amazon CloudWatch default metrics"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Amazon Route 53 provides DNS services that work across both cloud and on-premises environments, and a Virtual Private Gateway enables encrypted VPN connections between a VPC and an on-premises data center, both making them suitable for hybrid architectures.\\nClassic Load Balancer distributes traffic across EC2 instances within AWS and operates entirely within the cloud, with no hybrid connectivity capability. Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of EC2 instances based on demand within AWS, and does not extend to or integrate with on-premises infrastructure. Amazon CloudWatch default metrics monitor AWS resource performance within the cloud, and while it can be extended to on-premises with a custom agent, it is not itself a hybrid architecture service."},{"id":"q585","question":"Which statement best describes Elastic Load Balancing?","options":{"A":"It translates a domain name into an IP address using DNS","B":"It distributes incoming application traffic across one or more Amazon EC2 instances","C":"It collects metrics on connected Amazon EC2 instances","D":"It automatically adjusts the number of Amazon EC2 instances to support incoming traffic"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Elastic Load Balancing distributes incoming application traffic across multiple EC2 instances or targets, improving availability and fault tolerance by ensuring no single instance is overwhelmed.\\nTranslating a domain name into an IP address using DNS describes the function of Amazon Route 53, which is a DNS routing service and not a traffic distribution service. Collecting metrics on connected EC2 instances describes the function of Amazon CloudWatch, which monitors resource performance and operational data rather than distributing traffic. Automatically adjusting the number of EC2 instances to support incoming traffic describes the function of Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling, which adds or removes instances based on demand rather than distributing traffic across existing instances."},{"id":"q586","question":"Which of the following is a fast and reliable NoSQL database service?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon RDS","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database that delivers single-digit millisecond performance at any scale, with built-in security, backup, and in-memory caching.\\nAmazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse service optimized for running analytical SQL queries across large datasets, and is a relational columnar store rather than a NoSQL database service. Amazon RDS is a fully managed relational database service supporting engines such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server, and uses structured schemas and SQL rather than the flexible schema-less data models that NoSQL databases provide. Amazon S3 is an object storage service for storing and retrieving files and unstructured data, and while it can store large volumes of data it is not a database service and does not support the query patterns or data models of a NoSQL database."},{"id":"q592","question":"What is the benefit of using AWS managed services, such as Amazon ElastiCache and Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS)?","options":{"A":"They require the customer to monitor and replace failing instances","B":"They have better performance than customer-managed services","C":"They simplify patching and updating underlying OSs","D":"They do not require the customer to optimize instance type or size selections"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS managed services like Amazon ElastiCache and Amazon RDS handle patching and updating the underlying operating systems and database engines, freeing customers from these time-consuming maintenance tasks.\\nRequiring the customer to monitor and replace failing instances describes the opposite of a managed service benefit, as AWS managed services handle failure detection and recovery automatically, removing that burden from customers. Having better performance than customer-managed services is not guaranteed as a universal benefit, since performance depends on instance type, configuration, and workload rather than simply on whether a service is managed. Not requiring the customer to optimize instance type or size selections is incorrect, as customers are still responsible for choosing the appropriate instance type and size to match their workload requirements even when using managed services."},{"id":"q596","question":"Which of the following are characteristics of Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"A global file system","B":"An object store","C":"A local file store","D":"A network file system","E":"A durable storage system"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 is an object store that provides highly durable storage with 99.999999999% durability, storing data as objects in buckets rather than as files in a file system or blocks on a disk.\\nA global file system describes Amazon EFS, which provides a shared network file system accessible across multiple EC2 instances, not an object storage service. A local file store describes temporary instance storage such as EC2 instance store volumes, which are physically attached to the host and lost when the instance stops. A network file system also describes Amazon EFS, which mounts as a shared file system over a network connection rather than storing data as objects."},{"id":"q609","question":"Which of the following are valid ways for a customer to interact with AWS services?","options":{"A":"Command line interface","B":"On-premises","C":"Software Development Kits","D":"Software-as-a-service","E":"Hybrid"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"The AWS Command Line Interface allows command-based interaction with AWS services from a terminal, and AWS Software Development Kits provide programmatic access to AWS services from application code written in multiple programming languages. Both are direct, supported methods for interacting with AWS services.\\nOn-premises refers to infrastructure and software hosted in a company\'s own physical data center rather than in the cloud, and is a deployment model rather than a method of interacting with AWS services. Software-as-a-service is a cloud delivery model in which software applications are provided over the internet by a vendor, and is a service consumption model rather than a way for customers to interact with AWS services directly. Hybrid refers to an architecture that combines on-premises infrastructure with cloud resources, and is a deployment strategy rather than an interaction method for AWS services."},{"id":"q611","question":"Which of the following AWS services can be used to serve large amounts of online video content with the lowest possible latency?","options":{"A":"AWS Storage Gateway","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon Elastic File System (EFS)","D":"Amazon S3 Glacier","E":"Amazon CloudFront"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 stores video content with high durability and scalability, and Amazon CloudFront caches and delivers that content from edge locations worldwide with low latency, making them the purpose-built combination for serving large-scale online video efficiently.\\nAWS Storage Gateway connects on-premises environments to AWS cloud storage for hybrid storage integration, and is not designed for serving or distributing online video content to end users. Amazon Elastic File System is a managed shared network file system for use with AWS compute services, and is not optimized for delivering large volumes of video content at low latency to a distributed audience. Amazon S3 Glacier is an archival storage service designed for data that is rarely accessed and can tolerate retrieval times of minutes to hours, making it entirely unsuitable for serving online video content that requires immediate low-latency delivery."},{"id":"q615","question":"Which AWS service provides a personalized view of the health of specific AWS services that power a customer\'s workloads running on AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS X-Ray","C":"AWS Health Dashboard","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard provides a personalized, account-specific view of AWS service events affecting your workloads through its \\"Your account health\\" section, offering proactive alerts and remediation guidance tailored to your environment.\\nAmazon CloudWatch monitors metrics, logs, and alarms for resources within a customer\'s own AWS environment but does not provide alerts about AWS service events affecting your workloads. AWS X-Ray is an application tracing service for analyzing distributed application performance, not a service health monitoring tool. AWS Trusted Advisor analyzes your environment against best practice checks across cost, performance, security, and fault tolerance, but does not provide personalized service health alerts."},{"id":"q616","question":"Which of the following can an AWS customer use to launch a new Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) cluster?","options":{"A":"AWS Concierge","B":"AWS CloudFormation","C":"Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3)","D":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","E":"AWS Management Console"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"AWS CloudFormation can provision RDS clusters through infrastructure-as-code templates, and the AWS Management Console provides a graphical interface for launching RDS clusters directly, making both valid launch methods.\\nAWS Concierge is a support service available to enterprise plan customers that assists with billing and account inquiries, and has no capability to provision or launch AWS resources such as RDS clusters. Amazon S3 is an object storage service for storing and retrieving files and data, and cannot be used to launch or provision database clusters. Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of EC2 compute instances in response to demand, and has no capability to launch or manage RDS database clusters."},{"id":"q620","question":"Which of the following features can be configured through the Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC) Dashboard?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront distributions","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"Security Groups","D":"Subnets","E":"Elastic Load Balancing"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Security Groups and Subnets are both native features of Amazon VPC, with Security Groups controlling traffic at the instance level and Subnets defining IP address ranges and network segmentation within a VPC.\\nAmazon CloudFront distributions are configured through the CloudFront console and are a separate CDN service with no configuration surface within the Amazon VPC Dashboard. Amazon Route 53 is a DNS and traffic routing service managed through its own console, and is not a feature configured within Amazon VPC. Elastic Load Balancing is a separate service that distributes traffic across targets and is configured through the EC2 or dedicated load balancer console, not through the Amazon VPC Dashboard."},{"id":"q625","question":"Which AWS services are defined as global instead of regional?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"Amazon EC2","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon CloudFront","E":"Amazon DynamoDB"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Amazon Route 53 is a global DNS service that operates without being tied to any single AWS Region, and Amazon CloudFront is a global content delivery network whose edge locations span the globe independently of regional deployments. Both are global services rather than regional.\\nAmazon EC2 is a regional service; instances are launched in specific Availability Zones within specific Regions, and each EC2 deployment is bound to its Region. Amazon S3 is generally considered a regional service because buckets are created in a specific Region and data resides there by default, even though bucket names are globally unique. Amazon DynamoDB is a regional service where tables are created and data is stored in a specific Region, though Global Tables can replicate across Regions as an additional feature."},{"id":"q636","question":"What are the multiple, isolated locations within an AWS Region that are connected by low-latency networks called?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connects","B":"Amazon VPCs","C":"Edge locations","D":"Availability Zones"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Availability Zones are multiple, isolated locations within an AWS Region, each with independent power, cooling, and networking, connected to each other by low-latency links for high availability.\\nAWS Direct Connect is a dedicated private network connection service for linking an on-premises data center to AWS, not an infrastructure component within a Region. Amazon VPCs are virtual private networks that customers configure within a Region to isolate their resources, not physical infrastructure locations. Edge locations are points of presence outside of Regions used for content delivery and caching, not isolated locations within a Region connected by low-latency links."},{"id":"q650","question":"A company is designing an application hosted in a single AWS Region serving end-users spread across the world. The company wants to provide the end-users low latency access to the application data. Which of the following services will help fulfill this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"Amazon Route 53 global DNS","D":"Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) transfer acceleration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront serves content from edge locations closest to each end user, dramatically reducing round-trip latency by caching application data at over 400 points of presence worldwide, so users receive responses from a nearby location rather than the origin in a single Region.\\nAWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection from an on-premises data center to AWS, reducing latency on hybrid workloads, but it does not serve application content to end users distributed across the world from a global edge network. Amazon Route 53 with latency-based routing directs users to the nearest AWS Region, which reduces latency compared to serving all users from a single Region, but once the connection reaches the Region it does not cache content at edge locations the way CloudFront does. Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration uses CloudFront\'s edge locations to speed up data uploads to S3, but it is an upload acceleration feature rather than a content delivery mechanism that serves application data to end users with low latency."},{"id":"q653","question":"What feature of Amazon RDS helps to create globally redundant databases?","options":{"A":"Snapshots","B":"Automatic patching and updating","C":"Cross-Region read replicas","D":"Provisioned IOPS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon RDS Cross-Region read replicas create asynchronously replicated copies of a database in a separate AWS Region, providing globally redundant data that can be promoted to a standalone primary in the event of a regional failure.\\nSnapshots are point-in-time backups stored in S3 that can be copied to another Region for disaster recovery purposes, but they require manual restoration and do not provide a continuously synchronised replica that can be quickly promoted, making them less suitable for global redundancy. Automatic patching and updating is a managed maintenance feature that keeps the database engine and operating system up to date, but it does not replicate data across Regions or contribute to global redundancy. Provisioned IOPS is a storage option that delivers predictable high I/O performance for latency-sensitive workloads, and is a performance configuration rather than a replication or redundancy feature."},{"id":"q658","question":"Which feature adds elasticity to Amazon EC2 instances to handle the changing demand for workloads?","options":{"A":"Resource groups","B":"Lifecycle policies","C":"Application Load Balancer","D":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling monitors defined metrics and automatically adjusts the number of EC2 instances up or down based on demand conditions, adding elasticity to the application by ensuring it has the right capacity to handle current workloads.\\nResource groups organize AWS resources by tags or CloudFormation stacks to make them easier to manage, automate, and monitor collectively, but they do not add compute capacity or enable dynamic scaling of EC2 instances. Lifecycle policies in the context of Amazon S3 automatically transition objects to different storage classes or expire them after a specified period, and are a storage management feature rather than a compute elasticity mechanism. Application Load Balancer distributes incoming traffic across registered EC2 instances to prevent any single instance from being overwhelmed, but it does not adjust the number of running instances; that capacity change is the role of Auto Scaling."},{"id":"q660","question":"Which AWS hybrid storage service enables on-premises applications to seamlessly use AWS Cloud storage through standard file-storage protocols?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"AWS Snowball","C":"AWS Storage Gateway","D":"AWS Snowball Edge"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Storage Gateway provides on-premises applications with seamless access to AWS cloud storage through standard protocols such as NFS, SMB, and iSCSI, bridging hybrid storage environments without requiring applications to be rewritten.\\nAWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and is a network connectivity service rather than a storage integration service that exposes cloud storage through file protocols. AWS Snowball is a physical data transport device used for migrating large volumes of data into or out of AWS, and is a one-time or periodic data transfer tool rather than a service that provides ongoing seamless access to cloud storage for on-premises applications. AWS Snowball Edge is a physical device that adds local compute and storage capability at the edge for use cases where connectivity is limited or intermittent, and is a portable edge computing device rather than a hybrid storage integration service using standard file protocols."},{"id":"q664","question":"What is a benefit of loose coupling as a principle of cloud architecture design?","options":{"A":"It facilitates low-latency request handling","B":"It allows applications to have dependent workflows","C":"It prevents cascading failures between different components","D":"It allows companies to focus on their physical data center operations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loose coupling prevents cascading failures because components interact through well-defined interfaces such as queues and APIs rather than direct dependencies, so a failure in one component does not bring down others.\\nLow-latency request handling is a performance characteristic achieved through infrastructure choices such as edge caching and regional deployment, and is not a benefit of loose coupling as an architectural pattern. Allowing applications to have dependent workflows describes tight coupling, which is the opposite of loose coupling and increases the risk that a failure in one component will impact others. Allowing companies to focus on physical data center operations describes an on-premises operational model, which is the opposite of what cloud adoption enables and is entirely unrelated to loose coupling as a design principle."},{"id":"q665","question":"A director has been tasked with investigating hybrid cloud architecture. The company currently accesses AWS over the public internet. Which service will facilitate private hybrid connectivity?","options":{"A":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC) NAT Gateway","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) Transfer Acceleration","D":"AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated, private network connection between on-premises data centers and AWS, bypassing the public internet to provide consistent performance, lower latency, and enhanced security for hybrid cloud connectivity.\\nAmazon VPC NAT Gateway allows instances in private subnets to initiate outbound connections to the internet while preventing unsolicited inbound connections, and is an outbound internet access mechanism for private resources rather than a service that facilitates private hybrid connectivity between on-premises and AWS. Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration speeds up data transfers to and from S3 buckets by routing traffic through AWS CloudFront edge locations, and is a data transfer performance optimization rather than a private hybrid connectivity solution. AWS WAF is a web application firewall that filters HTTP and HTTPS traffic based on defined rules, and is a traffic filtering security service rather than a network connectivity solution for hybrid cloud architecture."},{"id":"q674","question":"Which of the following services is a MySQL-compatible database that automatically grows storage as needed?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)","B":"Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) for MySQL","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"Amazon Aurora"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Aurora is a MySQL and PostgreSQL-compatible database that automatically grows storage in increments of 10 GB up to 128 TB, without requiring manual intervention or downtime for storage management.\\nAmazon EC2 is a virtual server service that provides compute capacity and does not include a built-in database engine or automatic storage scaling. Amazon RDS for MySQL is a managed MySQL database but requires manual storage scaling or the enabling of storage autoscaling as a separate configuration, and does not grow automatically by default in the same way Aurora does. Amazon Lightsail is a simplified cloud platform for deploying small applications and websites and does not offer the automatic storage scaling capabilities described."},{"id":"q675","question":"Which Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC) feature enables users to connect two VPCs together?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC endpoints","B":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) ClassicLink","C":"Amazon VPC peering","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon VPC peering creates a private networking connection between two VPCs, allowing resources in each VPC to communicate with each other using private IP addresses as if they were on the same network.\\nAmazon VPC endpoints enable private connectivity between a VPC and supported AWS services without requiring traffic to traverse the public internet, but do not connect two VPCs together. EC2 ClassicLink was a legacy feature that allowed EC2-Classic instances to communicate with VPC resources, not a mechanism for connecting two VPCs to each other. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private circuit between an on-premises data center and AWS, not a connection between two VPCs."},{"id":"q678","question":"Which AWS service provides a secure, fast, and cost-effective way to migrate or transport exabyte-scale datasets into AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Batch","B":"AWS Snowball","C":"AWS Migration Hub","D":"AWS Snowmobile"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Snowmobile is an exabyte-scale data transfer service using a 45-foot shipping container that can move up to 100 PB per trip, designed for the largest possible data migrations to AWS.\\nAWS Batch is a managed service for running batch computing workloads at scale across EC2 instances, and is a job scheduling and compute service with no capability to physically transport or migrate datasets into AWS. AWS Snowball is a physical data transport device suited for transferring up to 80 TB per device, which at 500 TB would require multiple devices but at exabyte scale is not designed for migrations of that magnitude, where Snowmobile is the appropriate choice. AWS Migration Hub provides a centralized location for tracking the progress of application migrations to AWS using various migration tools, and is a migration monitoring service rather than a physical data transport solution for exabyte-scale datasets."},{"id":"q680","question":"Which load balancer types are available with Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)?","options":{"A":"Public load balancers with AWS Application Auto Scaling capabilities","B":"F5 Big-IP and Citrix NetScaler load balancers","C":"Classic Load Balancers","D":"Cross-zone load balancers with public and private IPs","E":"Application Load Balancers"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Classic Load Balancers are the legacy ELB option supporting HTTP, HTTPS, and TCP traffic, and Application Load Balancers are the modern Layer 7 option for HTTP and HTTPS routing with advanced request-based routing rules, both being official ELB load balancer types.\\nPublic load balancers with AWS Application Auto Scaling capabilities conflates two separate services and does not describe a load balancer type available within ELB. F5 Big-IP and Citrix NetScaler are third-party load balancing products that run on-premises or on EC2, not native ELB load balancer types. Cross-zone load balancers with public and private IPs describes a configuration feature of ELB rather than a distinct load balancer type."},{"id":"q682","question":"Which solution provides the FASTEST application response times to frequently accessed data to users in multiple AWS Regions?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail across multiple Availability Zones","B":"Amazon CloudFront to edge locations","C":"AWS CloudFormation in multiple regions","D":"A virtual private gateway over AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront caches frequently accessed content at edge locations worldwide, serving responses from the location nearest to the requesting user, which provides the fastest response times for globally distributed users compared to fetching from a single origin Region each time.\\nAWS CloudTrail records API calls and management events for auditing and compliance across Availability Zones but is not involved in serving application content or reducing response latency for users. AWS CloudFormation provisions and manages infrastructure through code templates and can be deployed across multiple Regions for consistency, but it is an infrastructure provisioning service rather than a content acceleration solution that reduces response times for end users. A virtual private gateway over AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network link between an on-premises environment and an AWS Region, reducing latency on that specific path but not serving application content to globally distributed users at the lowest possible latency."},{"id":"q684","question":"Which of the following AWS services can be used to run a self-managed database?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"AWS X-Ray","C":"AWS Snowmobile","D":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers with full OS-level access, allowing you to install and manage any database software yourself with complete control over configuration, tuning, and maintenance.\\nAmazon Route 53 is a DNS and traffic routing service with no compute capability for hosting or running database software. AWS X-Ray is an application tracing and debugging service for analyzing distributed application performance, unrelated to database hosting. AWS Snowmobile is a physical data transfer service for migrating extremely large datasets to AWS and cannot be used to run or host any software."},{"id":"q699","question":"Which AWS service would a customer use with a static website to achieve lower latency and high transfer speeds?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda","B":"Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator","C":"Amazon Route 53","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront caches static website content at edge locations worldwide, delivering it to users from the nearest location to reduce latency and increase transfer speeds.\\nAWS Lambda is a serverless compute service for running event-driven code and has no role in caching or delivering static website content. Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator is an in-memory cache specifically for DynamoDB database queries and is unrelated to static website content delivery. Amazon Route 53 is a DNS and traffic routing service that directs users to the correct endpoint but does not cache or deliver content itself."},{"id":"q700","question":"Which services manage and automate application deployments on AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","B":"AWS CodeCommit","C":"AWS CodePipeline","D":"AWS CloudFormation","E":"AWS Config"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk automates application deployment and scaling by handling the underlying infrastructure provisioning, load balancing, and monitoring automatically. AWS CloudFormation automates infrastructure provisioning through reusable templates, allowing teams to define and deploy AWS resources consistently and repeatedly.\\nAWS CodeCommit is a managed source control service for hosting private Git repositories and has no role in deploying or managing applications. AWS CodePipeline is a continuous delivery service that orchestrates the stages of a release pipeline, but it coordinates the deployment process rather than managing or automating the deployment environment itself. AWS Config continuously monitors and records AWS resource configurations for compliance purposes and does not manage or automate application deployments."},{"id":"q704","question":"A company wants to monitor the CPU usage of its Amazon EC2 resources. Which AWS service should the company use?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS Cost and Usage report","D":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch monitors CPU utilization and other performance metrics from EC2 instances, providing dashboards, alarms, and automated actions based on resource usage thresholds.\\nAWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity for auditing and security purposes, and does not collect or display resource performance metrics. AWS Cost and Usage Report provides detailed billing and cost data for AWS services, and has no capability for monitoring compute performance or resource utilization. Amazon SNS is a messaging service that sends notifications and alerts to subscribers, and while it can be triggered by CloudWatch alarms, it does not itself monitor or collect performance metrics."},{"id":"q708","question":"Which of the following AWS services provide compute resources?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda","B":"Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS)","C":"AWS CodeDeploy","D":"Amazon S3 Glacier","E":"AWS Organizations"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"AWS Lambda provides serverless compute by running code in response to events without requiring you to provision or manage servers, and Amazon ECS runs containerised applications on managed compute infrastructure. Both are purpose-built AWS compute services.\\nAWS CodeDeploy automates the deployment of application code to compute targets such as EC2 instances and Lambda functions, and is a deployment automation tool rather than a compute service that provides resources. Amazon S3 Glacier is an archival storage service designed for rarely accessed data with retrieval times ranging from minutes to hours, and is a storage service with no compute capability. AWS Organizations is a account management service for centrally governing and consolidating multiple AWS accounts, and has no compute capability of any kind."},{"id":"q709","question":"Which AWS service enables users to deploy infrastructure as code by automating the process of provisioning resources?","options":{"A":"Amazon GameLift","B":"AWS CloudFormation","C":"AWS Data Pipeline","D":"AWS Glue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudFormation enables infrastructure as code by letting you define AWS resources in JSON or YAML templates, automating the provisioning and management of an entire infrastructure stack in a repeatable and consistent way.\\nAmazon GameLift is a managed service for deploying, operating, and scaling cloud-based game servers, entirely unrelated to infrastructure provisioning. AWS Data Pipeline (deprecated) is a service for orchestrating and automating the movement and transformation of data between AWS services, not for provisioning infrastructure resources. AWS Glue is a managed extract, transform, and load service for preparing and moving data for analytics workloads, unrelated to infrastructure as code."},{"id":"q711","question":"Which of the following allows users to provision a dedicated network connection from their internal network to AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudHSM","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS VPN","D":"Amazon Connect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection from your internal network to AWS, providing more consistent network performance than internet-based connections.\\nAWS CloudHSM is a hardware security module service that provides dedicated cryptographic key storage and processing, and has no network connectivity capability. AWS VPN creates an encrypted tunnel over the public internet between on-premises networks and AWS, but does not provide a dedicated private connection with guaranteed bandwidth and consistent performance. Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service that enables customer service operations, and is entirely unrelated to network connectivity between on-premises infrastructure and AWS."},{"id":"q712","question":"Which services use AWS edge locations?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Shield","C":"Amazon EC2","D":"Amazon RDS","E":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Amazon CloudFront uses edge locations to cache and deliver content to users from the nearest point of presence, and AWS Shield provides DDoS protection at the edge by inspecting and mitigating attack traffic before it reaches the origin infrastructure.\\nAmazon EC2 instances are deployed in Availability Zones within AWS Regions, not at edge locations; EC2 is a regional compute service whose instances run in data centers rather than in the edge Points of Presence used by CloudFront and Shield. Amazon RDS is a fully managed relational database service that runs in specific AWS Regions and Availability Zones, not at edge locations, and is a regional service without an edge presence. Amazon ElastiCache provides in-memory caching within AWS Regions to reduce database load, and while it improves latency for applications within a Region, it does not operate at edge locations and is not a globally distributed edge service."},{"id":"q713","question":"Which service would provide network connectivity in a hybrid architecture that includes the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS Directory Service","D":"Amazon API Gateway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, making it the purpose-built service for establishing network connectivity in a hybrid architecture.\\nAmazon VPC provisions an isolated virtual network within AWS for deploying and connecting cloud resources, and while it is a foundational networking component on the AWS side, it does not itself bridge on-premises infrastructure to the AWS Cloud. AWS Directory Service provides managed Microsoft Active Directory in the cloud for identity and access management, and is an authentication and directory tool with no network connectivity capability between on-premises and cloud environments. Amazon API Gateway is a fully managed service for creating, publishing, and managing APIs at scale, and is an application integration service with no capability to establish dedicated network connectivity in a hybrid architecture."},{"id":"q720","question":"Which AWS service is a content delivery network that securely delivers data, video, and applications to users globally with low latency and high speeds?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudFormation","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"Amazon CloudFront","D":"Amazon Pinpoint"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront is a global CDN that securely delivers data, video, and applications from edge locations closest to users worldwide, minimizing latency and maximizing transfer speeds.\\nAWS CloudFormation is an infrastructure-as-code service that provisions and manages AWS resources through templates, and has no content delivery or edge caching capability. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, which improves hybrid connectivity but does not deliver content to global end users. Amazon Pinpoint is a customer engagement service used for targeted marketing communications such as email, SMS, and push notifications, and is entirely unrelated to content delivery or network acceleration."},{"id":"q729","question":"Which AWS service handles the deployment details of capacity provisioning, load balancing, Auto Scaling, and application health monitoring?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","C":"Amazon Route 53","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk automatically handles capacity provisioning, load balancing, Auto Scaling, and application health monitoring, allowing developers to deploy applications without needing to manage the underlying infrastructure.\\nAWS Config records and evaluates configuration changes to AWS resources for compliance and auditing purposes and has no role in application deployment or infrastructure provisioning. Amazon Route 53 is a DNS and traffic routing service and does not handle application deployment, load balancing, or health monitoring. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network for caching and distributing content globally and is not an application deployment or infrastructure management service."},{"id":"q742","question":"A company requires a dedicated network connection between its on-premises servers and the AWS Cloud. Which AWS service should be used?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"Amazon API Gateway","D":"Amazon Connect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private physical network connection between on-premises servers and AWS, delivering consistent performance and predictable latency that is not subject to internet congestion.\\nAWS VPN creates an encrypted tunnel between on-premises infrastructure and AWS but routes traffic over the public internet, meaning performance can vary and it does not provide the dedicated physical link the question requires. Amazon API Gateway is a managed service for creating, publishing, and securing APIs that connect applications to backend services, and has no role in establishing network connectivity between on-premises infrastructure and AWS. Amazon Connect is a cloud contact center service for managing customer communications via voice and chat, and is unrelated to network connectivity or on-premises integration."},{"id":"q743","question":"AWS CloudFormation is designed to help the user:","options":{"A":"Model and provision resources","B":"Update application code","C":"Set up data lakes","D":"Create reports for billing"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS CloudFormation is designed to model and provision AWS resources using templates, enabling you to define your entire infrastructure stack as code and deploy it in an automated, repeatable way.\\nUpdating application code is handled by developer tools such as AWS CodeDeploy, which manages application deployments to instances and serverless functions. CloudFormation provisions and manages infrastructure resources, not application-level code changes. Setting up data lakes is the function of services such as AWS Lake Formation, which simplifies building, securing, and managing data lakes on AWS. CloudFormation can provision the underlying resources but is not itself a data lake service. Creating reports for billing is the function of AWS Cost Explorer and AWS Billing, which provide visibility into spending and usage across an account. CloudFormation has no reporting or billing capability."},{"id":"q744","question":"Which of the following is an AWS database service?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)","C":"Amazon S3 Glacier","D":"AWS Snowball"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Redshift is a fully managed cloud data warehouse service designed for storing and querying large volumes of structured data, making it an AWS database service.\\nAmazon Elastic Block Store provides persistent block storage volumes that attach to EC2 instances for use as raw storage, and is not a database service. Amazon S3 Glacier is an archival object storage service designed for long-term retention of infrequently accessed data, not a database for querying or managing structured records. AWS Snowball is a physical edge computing and data transfer device used to move large amounts of data into or out of AWS, and has no role in storing or querying data as a database service."},{"id":"q749","question":"A Cloud Practitioner needs to store data for 7 years to meet regulatory requirements. Which AWS service will meet this requirement at the LOWEST cost?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"AWS Snowball","C":"Amazon Redshift","D":"Amazon S3 Glacier"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon S3 Glacier provides the lowest-cost storage for long-term data archival, making it ideal for regulatory data that must be retained for years but is rarely accessed.\\nAmazon S3 Standard provides durable, highly available object storage with immediate millisecond retrieval, and while it meets long-term retention requirements it is significantly more expensive per gigabyte than S3 Glacier for data that is rarely accessed over a seven-year period. AWS Snowball is a physical data transport device used for migrating large volumes of data into or out of AWS, and is a one-time transfer tool rather than a long-term storage service for meeting regulatory retention requirements. Amazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse service optimized for running analytical SQL queries across large datasets, and is designed for active analytical workloads rather than low-cost long-term retention of infrequently accessed regulatory data."},{"id":"q751","question":"What is the purpose of AWS Storage Gateway?","options":{"A":"It ensures on-premises data storage is 99.999999999% durable","B":"It transports petabytes of data to and from AWS","C":"It connects to multiple Amazon EC2 instances","D":"It connects on-premises data storage to the AWS Cloud"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Storage Gateway is a hybrid cloud storage service that gives you on-premises access to virtually unlimited cloud storage. It connects your existing on-premises applications and workflows to AWS storage services like Amazon S3, Amazon EBS, and Amazon FSx through standard storage protocols such as NFS, SMB, and iSCSI.\\nThe 99.999999999% (11 nines) durability figure is a specific design characteristic of Amazon S3\'s object storage, not a guarantee applied to local on-premises hardware. Transporting petabytes of data physically to and from AWS is the primary function of the AWS Snow Family (such as AWS Snowball), whereas Storage Gateway is for ongoing network-based integration. Connecting to multiple Amazon EC2 instances is a feature of shared storage services like Amazon EFS or Amazon FSx, rather than a hybrid connectivity solution for on-premises data centers."},{"id":"q754","question":"A Cloud Practitioner needs a consistent and dedicated connection between AWS resources and an on-premises system. Which AWS service can fulfill this requirement?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"AWS VPN","C":"Amazon Connect","D":"AWS Data Pipeline"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect provides a consistent dedicated private network connection between on-premises systems and AWS, delivering predictable performance and lower latency than internet-based connections.\\nAWS VPN creates an encrypted tunnel between on-premises infrastructure and AWS but routes traffic over the public internet, meaning performance can vary and it does not provide the dedicated physical connection the question requires. Amazon Connect is a cloud contact center service for managing customer communications via voice and chat, and is entirely unrelated to network connectivity between on-premises systems and AWS. AWS Data Pipeline (deprecated) is a workflow orchestration service for automating the movement and transformation of data between AWS services and on-premises sources, and is a data processing tool with no network connectivity capability."},{"id":"q759","question":"Which of the following describes the relationships among AWS Regions, Availability Zones, and edge locations?","options":{"A":"There are more AWS Regions than Availability Zones","B":"There are more edge locations than AWS Regions","C":"An edge location is an Availability Zone","D":"There are more AWS Regions than edge locations","E":"There are more Availability Zones than AWS Regions"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"There are hundreds of edge locations worldwide compared to dozens of Regions, and each Region contains multiple Availability Zones, making both counts greater than the number of Regions.\\nThere are more AWS Regions than Availability Zones is false. Every Region contains at least two or three AZs, so AZs always outnumber Regions. An edge location is an Availability Zone is false. Edge locations are separate points of presence used for content delivery and are not part of the AZ infrastructure. There are more AWS Regions than edge locations is false. Edge locations number in the hundreds, far exceeding the number of Regions."},{"id":"q767","question":"What helps a company provide a lower latency experience to its users globally?","options":{"A":"Using an AWS Region that is central to all users","B":"Using a second Availability Zone in the AWS Region that is being used","C":"Enabling caching in the AWS Region that is being used","D":"Using edge locations to put content closer to all users"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Edge locations place cached content closer to users globally through Amazon CloudFront, providing the lowest possible latency regardless of where the origin server is located.\\nUsing a single central Region still requires users in distant locations to route traffic across long network distances, and no single Region can be geographically central to all users worldwide. Adding a second Availability Zone improves availability and fault tolerance within a Region but does not reduce the physical distance between the application and global users. Enabling caching within a single Region keeps cached content in one geographic location, which only benefits users close to that Region rather than reducing latency for users globally."},{"id":"q769","question":"Which Amazon RDS feature can be used to achieve high availability?","options":{"A":"Multiple Availability Zones","B":"Amazon Reserved Instances","C":"Provisioned IOPS storage","D":"Enhanced monitoring"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment maintains a synchronous standby replica in a different Availability Zone and performs automatic failover to the standby with no manual intervention if the primary instance becomes unavailable, providing high availability for database workloads.\\nAmazon Reserved Instances are a billing model that offers significant discounts in exchange for a one-year or three-year commitment, and are a cost optimization tool rather than a high-availability feature for database instances. Provisioned IOPS storage delivers consistent, low-latency I/O performance for demanding workloads by allocating dedicated I/O capacity, and is a performance optimization option rather than a mechanism for achieving high availability across Availability Zones. Enhanced Monitoring provides granular real-time metrics on the operating system of an RDS instance at intervals as short as one second, enabling detailed performance visibility, but is a monitoring feature rather than a high-availability configuration."},{"id":"q774","question":"Which AWS service would simplify migration of a database to AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Storage Gateway","B":"AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","C":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)","D":"Amazon AppStream 2.0"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Database Migration Service simplifies database migration by managing the data replication from the source to the target with minimal downtime, supporting homogeneous migrations such as Oracle to Oracle and heterogeneous migrations between different database engines.\\nAWS Storage Gateway connects on-premises environments to AWS cloud storage for hybrid storage use cases, and while it handles data movement, it is a storage integration service for files and block data rather than a service designed to migrate relational databases. Amazon EC2 can host a custom database migration process, but it requires customers to set up, configure, and manage all migration tooling manually, making it far less straightforward than using the purpose-built DMS service. Amazon AppStream 2.0 is a fully managed application streaming service that streams desktop applications from AWS to a user\'s browser, and is a virtual desktop and application delivery service with no relationship to database migration."},{"id":"q776","question":"Which AWS networking service enables a company to create a virtual network within AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC) enables companies to create isolated virtual networks within AWS, with full control over IP addressing, subnets, routing, and network gateways.\\nAWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration state of AWS resources to assess compliance and detect configuration changes, and is a governance and auditing tool with no capability to provision or define virtual networks. Amazon Route 53 is a DNS and traffic routing service that resolves domain names and directs users to application endpoints, and is a domain management service rather than a service for creating virtual networks within AWS. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and is a hybrid connectivity service for linking external networks to AWS rather than a service for creating virtual networks within AWS itself."},{"id":"q778","question":"Which component of AWS global infrastructure does Amazon CloudFront use to ensure low-latency delivery?","options":{"A":"AWS Regions","B":"AWS edge locations","C":"AWS Availability Zones","D":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront uses AWS edge locations, distributed across hundreds of cities worldwide, to cache and deliver content from the location nearest to each user for low-latency delivery.\\nAWS Regions are geographic areas containing multiple AZs, used for deploying and running workloads, not for edge caching and content delivery. AWS Availability Zones are isolated data center clusters within a Region, used for high availability and redundancy, not content delivery. Amazon VPC is a private virtual network within a Region, used for isolating and controlling network traffic, not for delivering content globally."},{"id":"q781","question":"Which service would you use to send alerts based on Amazon CloudWatch alarms?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon Route 53"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SNS delivers notifications triggered by CloudWatch alarms by sending messages to subscribed endpoints such as email addresses, SMS, Lambda functions, SQS queues, and HTTP endpoints, making it the integration point for alarm-based alerting.\\nAWS CloudTrail records API calls and management events in an AWS account for auditing and compliance, and does not send alerts based on CloudWatch alarms; it is a logging service rather than a notification delivery mechanism. AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment against best practice checks across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and while it generates recommendations, it does not integrate with CloudWatch alarms to send triggered alerts. Amazon Route 53 is a scalable DNS service supporting domain registration, routing, and health checks, and while it has its own health check alerting, it does not serve as the notification delivery service for general CloudWatch alarms."},{"id":"q784","question":"When designing a typical three-tier web application, which AWS services and/or features improve availability and reduce the impact of failures?","options":{"A":"AWS Auto Scaling for Amazon EC2 instances","B":"Amazon VPC subnet ACLs to check the health of a service","C":"Distributed resources across multiple Availability Zones","D":"Amazon CloudWatch alarms to notify when an EC2 instance enters an unhealthy state","E":"Distributed resources across multiple AWS points of presence"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS Auto Scaling automatically replaces unhealthy instances and adjusts capacity, and distributing resources across multiple Availability Zones provides redundancy, both improving availability and reducing the impact of failures.\\nVPC subnet ACLs are stateless network access control rules that filter traffic at the subnet level, and do not perform health checks or contribute to application availability. CloudWatch alarms send notifications when a metric crosses a threshold but do not automatically remediate failures or restore availability on their own. Points of presence are CloudFront edge locations used for content delivery and caching, not for hosting application tiers or providing redundancy for a three-tier web application."},{"id":"q794","question":"A company is planning to launch an ecommerce site in a single AWS Region to a worldwide user base. Which AWS services will allow the company to reach users and provide low latency and high transfer speeds?","options":{"A":"Application Load Balancer","B":"AWS Global Accelerator","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Amazon CloudFront","E":"AWS Lambda"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator routes traffic over the AWS global network to the optimal endpoint based on health, geography, and routing policies, and Amazon CloudFront caches content at edge locations worldwide. Together they provide low latency and high transfer speeds for a global user base.\\nApplication Load Balancer distributes traffic across targets within an AWS Region, improving availability and performance for applications within a Region, but does not extend reach to global users across multiple geographic locations. AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that runs code in response to events without managing servers, and is a compute service rather than a global content delivery or traffic acceleration solution. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection from an on-premises data center to AWS for hybrid workloads, and is not a solution for reducing latency for internet-based end users distributed worldwide."},{"id":"q795","question":"Which AWS service or resource is serverless?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda","B":"Amazon EC2 instances","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that runs code in response to events without requiring you to provision, manage, or scale any underlying server infrastructure.\\nAmazon EC2 instances are virtual servers that require you to select instance types, manage capacity, and maintain the underlying operating system. Amazon Lightsail provides virtual private servers with pre-configured compute, storage, and networking, making it a server-based service aimed at simpler workloads. Amazon ElastiCache is a managed in-memory caching service that runs on node-based infrastructure within a VPC, not a serverless service."},{"id":"q804","question":"Which AWS services provide a user with connectivity between the AWS Cloud and on-premises resources?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"Amazon Cognito","D":"AWS Direct Connect","E":"AWS Managed Services"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS VPN creates an encrypted tunnel over the public internet between an on-premises network and an AWS VPC, and AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private physical network connection from an on-premises data center to AWS. Both provide connectivity between on-premises resources and the AWS Cloud.\\nAmazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service that enables customer service operations through voice and chat channels, and is a business communications tool rather than a network connectivity solution for linking on-premises infrastructure to AWS. Amazon Cognito provides user identity and authentication services for web and mobile applications, managing user sign-up, sign-in, and access control, and is an identity service rather than a network connectivity solution. AWS Managed Services provides ongoing management of AWS infrastructure to reduce operational overhead, and is an operational management offering rather than a network connectivity service between on-premises environments and the AWS Cloud."},{"id":"q807","question":"A company wants to migrate a MySQL database to AWS but does not have the budget for Database Administrators to handle routine tasks including provisioning, patching, and performing backups. Which AWS service will support this use case?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon DocumentDB","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon RDS is a managed MySQL-compatible database service that handles routine tasks like provisioning, patching, backups, and recovery, eliminating the need for dedicated Database Administrators to manage these operational responsibilities.\\nAmazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL key-value and document database, and while it eliminates DBA overhead it does not support MySQL or relational data models, making it unsuitable as a direct migration target for a MySQL database. Amazon DocumentDB is a managed document database service compatible with MongoDB, and is designed for JSON document workloads rather than relational MySQL database migrations. Amazon ElastiCache is a managed in-memory caching service designed to accelerate application performance by storing frequently accessed data in memory, and is not a relational database service capable of running or replacing a MySQL database."},{"id":"q813","question":"Which AWS service is suitable for an event-driven workload?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"AWS Fargate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Lambda is designed for event-driven workloads, automatically running code in response to events from services like S3, DynamoDB, API Gateway, and CloudWatch without provisioning servers.\\nAmazon EC2 provides virtual servers that customers provision and manage continuously, and is designed for workloads that require persistent running instances rather than code that executes only in response to events. AWS Elastic Beanstalk deploys and manages applications on continuously running infrastructure, handling scaling and provisioning automatically, but is not designed for event-driven execution of individual functions. AWS Fargate runs containerised workloads without managing the underlying servers, but containers run as persistent tasks rather than executing in response to individual events the way Lambda functions do."},{"id":"q814","question":"What is a characteristic of Amazon S3 cross-region replication?","options":{"A":"Both source and destination S3 buckets must have versioning disabled","B":"The source and destination S3 buckets cannot be in different AWS Regions","C":"S3 buckets configured for cross-region replication can be owned by a single AWS account or by different accounts","D":"The source S3 bucket owner must have the source and destination AWS Regions disabled for their account"},"correct":"C","explanation":"S3 cross-region replication supports buckets owned by a single AWS account or by different accounts, enabling cross-account replication scenarios for data sharing and compliance.\\nCross-region replication requires versioning to be enabled on both the source and destination buckets, not disabled, making this option factually incorrect. Cross-region replication is specifically designed to replicate objects between buckets in different AWS Regions, so source and destination buckets must be in different Regions by definition. AWS Regions do not need to be disabled for either account involved in a cross-region replication configuration, as replication works across any enabled Regions without any such restriction."},{"id":"q818","question":"Amazon Route 53 enables users to:","options":{"A":"Encrypt data in transit","B":"Register DNS domain names","C":"Generate and manage SSL certificates","D":"Establish a dedicated network connection to AWS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 is a DNS service that enables users to register domain names, manage DNS records, and route internet traffic to AWS resources or external endpoints.\\nEncrypting data in transit is handled by AWS services such as AWS Certificate Manager and AWS KMS, not by Route 53, which manages DNS routing rather than cryptographic operations. Generating and managing SSL certificates is the function of AWS Certificate Manager, which provisions and renews certificates for use with AWS resources, not a capability of Route 53. Establishing a dedicated network connection to AWS is the function of AWS Direct Connect, which provides a private physical link between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, not a DNS or domain registration service."},{"id":"q823","question":"Users are reporting latency when connecting to a website with a global customer base. Which AWS service will improve the customer experience by reducing latency?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","D":"AWS Transit Gateway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront caches content at edge locations worldwide, delivering it from the location nearest to each user, which significantly reduces latency for a website with a global customer base.\\nAWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network link between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, which improves connectivity for enterprise networks but does not reduce latency for public internet users accessing a website. Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling adjusts the number of instances based on demand to maintain performance and availability, but does not reduce the geographic distance between the server and global users. AWS Transit Gateway connects VPCs and on-premises networks through a central hub, and is a network routing service with no content delivery or latency optimization capability for end users."},{"id":"q826","question":"A company wants to use an AWS service to monitor the health of application endpoints, with the ability to route traffic to healthy regional endpoints to improve application availability. Which service will support these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS Global Accelerator","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator continuously monitors the health of application endpoints and automatically routes incoming user traffic to the nearest healthy endpoint over the AWS global network, improving availability and enabling cross-Region failover without DNS changes.\\nAmazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and does not monitor endpoint health or route application traffic. Amazon CloudWatch collects and monitors metrics, logs, and events from AWS services and applications, and while it can detect and alert on endpoint health issues, it does not itself route application traffic to healthy endpoints. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches content at edge locations and improves performance for static and dynamic content delivery, but it does not perform application-level endpoint health monitoring and traffic routing across Regions in the way Global Accelerator does."},{"id":"q830","question":"Which AWS services can be used to move data from on-premises data centers to AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Snowball","B":"AWS Lambda","C":"AWS ElastiCache","D":"AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","E":"Amazon API Gateway"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Snowball physically transports large volumes of data to AWS using secure hardware appliances, and AWS DMS migrates databases from on-premises or other sources to AWS with minimal downtime. Both are specifically designed for moving data from on-premises environments to AWS.\\nAWS Lambda is a serverless compute service for running event-driven code and has no role in data migration or physical data transport. AWS ElastiCache is an in-memory caching service for improving application performance and is not a data migration tool. Amazon API Gateway is a service for creating and managing APIs to expose backend services over HTTP and has no capability for migrating or transporting data from on-premises to AWS."},{"id":"q834","question":"An ecommerce company anticipates a huge increase in web traffic for two very popular upcoming shopping holidays. Which AWS service or feature can be configured to dynamically adjust resources to meet this change in demand?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","C":"Amazon Forecast","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling dynamically adjusts the number of EC2 instances based on demand, automatically scaling out to handle traffic spikes and scaling in when demand drops, making it the purpose-built solution for meeting variable workload requirements.\\nAWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity across an AWS environment for auditing and compliance purposes, and has no capability to adjust or provision resources in response to changes in demand. Amazon Forecast is a machine learning service that generates time-series predictions such as demand or inventory forecasts, and while it can predict future traffic patterns it cannot itself take action to adjust infrastructure resources. AWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration of AWS resources for compliance and change management purposes, and has no capability to dynamically scale resources in response to traffic or demand changes."},{"id":"q835","question":"Which AWS service enables users to securely connect to AWS resources over the public internet?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC peering","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS VPN","D":"Amazon Pinpoint"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS VPN creates an encrypted tunnel over the public internet to securely connect on-premises networks or remote users to AWS resources, providing secure connectivity without requiring a dedicated physical line.\\nAmazon VPC peering connects two VPCs privately within the AWS network, allowing them to communicate with each other, but does not provide connectivity from outside AWS over the public internet. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network link between on-premises infrastructure and AWS that bypasses the public internet entirely, making it a private rather than internet-based connectivity option. Amazon Pinpoint is a customer engagement service used for sending targeted marketing messages and notifications, and is entirely unrelated to network connectivity."},{"id":"q837","question":"A company is running an ecommerce application hosted in Europe. To decrease latency for users who access the website from other parts of the world, the company would like to cache frequently accessed static content closer to the users. Which AWS service will support these requirements?","options":{"A":"Amazon ElastiCache","B":"Amazon CloudFront","C":"Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS)","D":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront caches frequently accessed static content at edge locations worldwide, serving it from the location nearest to each user to reduce latency for a global audience.\\nAmazon ElastiCache is an in-memory caching service that reduces database query latency within an application, but it does not cache or distribute content to geographically dispersed users at edge locations. Amazon Elastic File System provides a shared file system for EC2 instances within AWS and does not cache or distribute content to geographically dispersed users. Amazon Elastic Block Store provides block storage volumes that attach to individual EC2 instances and has no capability to cache or deliver content to users in different geographic locations."},{"id":"q838","question":"Which AWS service will help users determine if an application running on an Amazon EC2 instance has sufficient CPU capacity?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch monitors EC2 CPU utilization metrics and can display dashboards showing whether an instance has sufficient CPU capacity for its workload, triggering alarms when defined thresholds are exceeded.\\nAWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration of AWS resources to assess compliance and detect configuration changes, and does not monitor real-time performance metrics such as CPU utilization. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity across an AWS environment for auditing and compliance purposes, and has no capability to monitor instance-level performance metrics. Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and is a security tool rather than a performance monitoring service."},{"id":"q842","question":"An application runs on multiple Amazon EC2 instances that access a shared file system simultaneously. Which AWS storage service should be used?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon EFS","C":"Amazon S3","D":"AWS Artifact"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EFS provides a fully managed shared NFS file system that multiple EC2 instances can mount and access simultaneously, supporting concurrent read and write operations across instances.\\nAmazon EBS volumes can only be attached to a single EC2 instance at a time in most configurations, making them unsuitable for shared simultaneous access across multiple instances. Amazon S3 is an object storage service accessible via API calls, not a mountable file system suitable for applications requiring shared concurrent file system access. AWS Artifact is a self-service portal for accessing AWS compliance reports and agreements, entirely unrelated to storage."},{"id":"q850","question":"Which AWS services may be scaled using AWS Auto Scaling?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon Route 53","E":"Amazon Redshift"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Amazon EC2 instances can be scaled using EC2 Auto Scaling, which automatically adjusts the number of instances based on demand, and Amazon DynamoDB tables can be scaled using Application Auto Scaling to automatically adjust read and write capacity in response to traffic patterns. Both integrate directly with AWS Auto Scaling.\\nAmazon S3 is an object storage service that scales automatically to accommodate any amount of data without any configuration required, and does not use or require AWS Auto Scaling to manage its capacity. Amazon Route 53 is a DNS and traffic routing service that manages domain name resolution and traffic policies, and has no compute or database capacity that would be managed through AWS Auto Scaling. Amazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse service that has its own concurrency scaling and resizing capabilities built in, and does not integrate with AWS Auto Scaling in the way EC2 and DynamoDB do."},{"id":"q851","question":"Which of the following are benefits of AWS Global Accelerator?","options":{"A":"Reduced cost to run services on AWS","B":"Improved availability of applications deployed on AWS","C":"Higher durability of data stored on AWS","D":"Decreased latency to reach applications deployed on AWS","E":"Higher security of data stored on AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator improves application availability by routing traffic to healthy endpoints across AWS Regions, and decreases latency by directing users to the optimal endpoint over the AWS global network rather than the public internet.\\nReduced cost to run services on AWS is not a benefit of AWS Global Accelerator, which is a network performance and availability service that does not reduce the underlying cost of running compute, storage, or database services on AWS. Higher durability of data stored on AWS is a characteristic of storage services such as Amazon S3, which is designed for 99.999999999 percent durability, and is entirely unrelated to the network traffic routing capabilities of AWS Global Accelerator. Higher security of data stored on AWS is a function of encryption, access control, and compliance services rather than a network routing service, and AWS Global Accelerator does not provide data storage or data security capabilities."},{"id":"q860","question":"Which AWS service can be used to turn text into life-like speech?","options":{"A":"Amazon Polly","B":"Amazon Transcribe","C":"Amazon Rekognition","D":"Amazon Lex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service that uses advanced deep learning to convert text into natural-sounding speech in multiple languages and voices.\\nAmazon Transcribe is a speech-to-text service that converts audio recordings into written text, which is the reverse function of converting text into speech. Amazon Rekognition is a computer vision service that analyzes images and videos to detect objects, faces, and scenes, and has no capability to generate or process speech. Amazon Lex is a service for building conversational interfaces and chatbots using voice and text, but it processes and understands speech as input rather than converting written text into spoken audio output."},{"id":"q864","question":"Which of the AWS global infrastructure is used to cache copies of content for faster delivery to users across the globe?","options":{"A":"AWS Regions","B":"Availability Zones","C":"Edge locations","D":"Data centers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Edge locations are points of presence in the AWS global infrastructure used by CloudFront and other services to cache copies of content close to end users for faster delivery worldwide.\\nAWS Regions are geographic areas containing multiple Availability Zones where customers deploy their workloads, and are not designed for content caching or low-latency delivery to end users. Availability Zones are isolated data center clusters within a Region used for redundancy and high availability, not for caching content at the edge. Data centers are the physical facilities that house AWS infrastructure and are not a distinct infrastructure tier that customers interact with directly for content delivery purposes."},{"id":"q866","question":"A user needs to quickly deploy a non-relational database on AWS. The user does not want to manage the underlying hardware or the database software. Which AWS service can be used to accomplish this?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon Aurora","D":"Amazon Redshift"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed non-relational NoSQL database that requires no hardware provisioning, software installation, or database management, and scales automatically to handle any workload.\\nAmazon RDS is a fully managed relational database service supporting engines such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server, and does not support non-relational data models. Amazon Aurora is a fully managed relational database compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL, and is also a relational rather than a non-relational database service. Amazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse service optimized for running analytical SQL queries across large datasets, and is a relational service with no non-relational capability."},{"id":"q872","question":"Which AWS services can be used to provide network connectivity between an on-premises network and a VPC?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS Data Pipeline","D":"AWS VPN","E":"Amazon Connect"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private physical connection between an on-premises network and a VPC, and AWS VPN creates an encrypted tunnel over the public internet between an on-premises network and a VPC. Both are purpose-built network connectivity solutions.\\nAmazon Route 53 is a DNS and traffic routing service that translates domain names to IP addresses and manages traffic flow, and does not establish network connectivity between on-premises infrastructure and a VPC. AWS Data Pipeline (deprecated) is a workflow orchestration service for automating the movement and transformation of data between AWS services and on-premises sources, and is a data processing tool with no network connectivity capability. Amazon Connect is a cloud contact center service for managing customer communications via voice and chat, and is entirely unrelated to network connectivity."},{"id":"q876","question":"A company has an application with users in both Australia and Brazil. All the company infrastructure is currently provisioned in the Asia Pacific (Sydney) Region in Australia, and Brazilian users are experiencing high latency. What should the company do to reduce latency?","options":{"A":"Implement AWS Direct Connect for users in Brazil","B":"Provision resources in the South America (S\xe3o Paulo) Region in Brazil","C":"Use AWS Transit Gateway to quickly route users from Brazil to the application","D":"Launch additional Amazon EC2 instances in Sydney to handle the demand"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Provisioning resources in the South America (S\xe3o Paulo) Region places infrastructure geographically close to Brazilian users, directly reducing the network distance and latency compared to routing all traffic to Sydney.\\nAWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network link between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, but does not reduce latency for end users accessing a cloud-hosted application over the internet. AWS Transit Gateway connects VPCs and on-premises networks through a central hub, and is not designed to reduce geographic latency for end users accessing applications from distant locations. Launching additional EC2 instances in Sydney increases capacity in Australia but does not reduce the physical distance between the application and Brazilian users, so latency would remain unchanged."},{"id":"q879","question":"Which AWS hybrid storage service enables a user\'s on-premises applications to seamlessly use AWS Cloud storage?","options":{"A":"AWS Backup","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS Storage Gateway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Storage Gateway is a hybrid storage service that gives on-premises applications seamless access to AWS cloud storage through standard file, volume, and tape storage protocols.\\nAWS Backup is a centralized backup service for automating and managing backups of AWS resources, and does not provide on-premises applications with access to cloud storage. Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service for managing customer communications, and is entirely unrelated to storage or hybrid connectivity. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network link between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, but is a networking service rather than a storage integration service."},{"id":"q881","question":"What is the most efficient way to establish network connectivity from on-premises to multiple VPCs in different AWS Regions?","options":{"A":"Use AWS Direct Connect","B":"Use AWS VPN","C":"Use AWS Client VPN","D":"Use an AWS Transit Gateway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Transit Gateway acts as a central hub that connects multiple VPCs across different Regions and on-premises networks, providing the most efficient way to manage complex network connectivity at scale.\\nAWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private link between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, but does not itself manage connectivity between multiple VPCs across different Regions. AWS VPN creates an encrypted tunnel between on-premises networks and a single VPC, and does not efficiently scale to connect multiple VPCs across multiple Regions from a single connection. AWS Client VPN is an endpoint-based service that enables individual devices to securely access AWS resources remotely, and is not designed for site-to-site or multi-VPC connectivity."},{"id":"q885","question":"Which Amazon S3 storage class is optimized to provide access to data with lower resiliency requirements, but rapid access when needed such as duplicate backups?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Standard","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive","C":"Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access","D":"Amazon S3 Glacier"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access stores data in a single Availability Zone at lower cost with lower resiliency, but provides rapid millisecond access when needed, making it suitable for duplicate backups that can be recreated.\\nAmazon S3 Standard is designed for frequently accessed data with high durability across multiple AZs, making it more expensive and over-specified for data with lower resiliency requirements such as duplicate backups. Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive is designed for long-term archival of data that is rarely accessed, with retrieval times of up to 12 hours, making it unsuitable for workloads requiring rapid access. Amazon S3 Glacier is designed for archival storage with retrieval times ranging from minutes to hours, and does not provide the rapid millisecond access required for the use case described."},{"id":"q888","question":"Which AWS service enables users to create copies of resources across AWS Regions?","options":{"A":"Amazon ElastiCache","B":"AWS CloudFormation","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Systems Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudFormation uses template files to define infrastructure as code, and these templates can be deployed into multiple AWS Regions, creating identical copies of an entire infrastructure stack including all its resources and configurations in each target Region.\\nAmazon ElastiCache provides in-memory caching for improving application performance by reducing database load, and is a caching service rather than a service for replicating or copying infrastructure resources across Regions. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and management events for auditing and compliance purposes, and while it can be configured to deliver logs to multiple Regions, it does not enable users to create copies of AWS infrastructure resources in other Regions. AWS Systems Manager provides operational management capabilities for AWS resources including patch management, parameter storage, and automation, but does not provide a mechanism for replicating or deploying complete sets of AWS resources across multiple Regions."},{"id":"q891","question":"Which of the following are AWS compute services?","options":{"A":"Amazon Lightsail","B":"AWS Systems Manager","C":"AWS CloudFormation","D":"AWS Batch","E":"Amazon Inspector"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Amazon Lightsail provides simplified virtual private servers for deploying compute workloads, and AWS Batch manages and runs batch computing jobs across dynamically provisioned compute resources. Both are compute services.\\nAWS Systems Manager is an operations management service for automating configuration, patching, and inventory tasks on EC2 instances, not a compute service that provisions or runs workloads. AWS CloudFormation is an infrastructure as code service for provisioning and managing AWS resources through templates, not a compute service in its own right. Amazon Inspector is an automated vulnerability assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for security findings, not a compute service."},{"id":"q900","question":"Which AWS service or feature allows a user to easily scale connectivity among thousands of VPCs?","options":{"A":"VPC peering","B":"AWS Transit Gateway","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS Global Accelerator"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Transit Gateway acts as a central hub that connects thousands of VPCs and on-premises networks through a single gateway, scaling connectivity far more efficiently than managing individual connections between each network.\\nVPC peering creates a direct connection between two VPCs but does not scale to thousands of VPCs, as each pair requires its own peering connection and peering is non-transitive. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private circuit between an on-premises data center and AWS but does not connect VPCs to each other. AWS Global Accelerator improves the availability and performance of applications for global users by routing traffic through the AWS global network, but does not provide connectivity between VPCs."},{"id":"q899","question":"What can be used to automate and manage secure, well-architected, multi-account AWS environments?","options":{"A":"AWS shared responsibility model","B":"AWS Control Tower","C":"AWS Security Hub","D":"AWS Well-Architected Tool"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Control Tower automates the setup of a secure, multi-account AWS environment using landing zones and guardrails that enforce governance policies, making it the service for automating and managing secure, well-architected multi-account environments.\\nAWS Config monitors and records resource configurations against compliance rules within accounts, and while it supports governance, it is a per-account compliance evaluation service rather than a service that automates the provisioning and management of entire multi-account environments. AWS Artifact provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports and certifications, and is a documentation portal rather than a service that automates the creation or management of multi-account AWS environments. AWS CloudFormation is an infrastructure as code service for provisioning AWS resources through templates, and while it can be used to set up environments, it requires custom template development and does not provide the out-of-the-box multi-account governance and guardrails that Control Tower delivers."},{"id":"q905","question":"Which AWS container service will help a user install, operate, and scale the cluster management infrastructure?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR)","B":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","C":"Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS)","D":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon ECS is a fully managed container orchestration service that handles installing, operating, and scaling the cluster management infrastructure for running Docker containers, removing the need to manage your own orchestration software.\\nAmazon ECR is a fully managed container registry for storing, managing, and deploying container images, not a service for running or orchestrating containers. AWS Elastic Beanstalk is a platform-as-a-service for deploying web applications and services, not a dedicated container cluster management solution. Amazon EBS is a block storage service for attaching persistent storage volumes to EC2 instances, unrelated to container orchestration."},{"id":"q908","question":"What is the purpose of having an internet gateway within a VPC?","options":{"A":"To create a VPN connection to the VPC","B":"To allow communication between the VPC and the Internet","C":"To impose bandwidth constraints on internet traffic","D":"To load balance traffic from the Internet across Amazon EC2 instances"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An internet gateway enables communication between resources in a VPC and the public internet, allowing instances in public subnets to send and receive traffic from the internet.\\nCreating a VPN connection to the VPC is the function of a Virtual Private Gateway, not an internet gateway, as VPN connections are used to link a VPC to an on-premises network. Imposing bandwidth constraints on internet traffic is not a function of any VPC gateway component, as bandwidth management is handled at the application or network policy level. Load balancing traffic from the internet across EC2 instances is the function of an Elastic Load Balancer, not an internet gateway."},{"id":"q909","question":"A company must ensure that its endpoint for a database instance remains the same after a single Availability Zone service interruption. The application needs to resume database operations without the need for manual administrative intervention. How can these requirements be met?","options":{"A":"Use multiple Amazon Route 53 routes to the standby database instance endpoint hosted on AWS Storage Gateway","B":"Configure Amazon RDS Multi-Availability Zone deployments with automatic failover to the standby","C":"Add multiple Application Load Balancers and deploy the database instance with AWS Elastic Beanstalk","D":"Deploy a single Network Load Balancer to distribute incoming traffic across multiple Amazon CloudFront origins"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon RDS Multi-AZ maintains a synchronous standby replica with the same DNS endpoint in a different Availability Zone, so when a failure occurs the endpoint automatically resolves to the standby without any change to the application connection string or manual intervention.\\nUsing multiple Route 53 routes to a standby database hosted on AWS Storage Gateway is not a viable approach; Storage Gateway is a hybrid storage service rather than a database hosting solution, and this configuration would not maintain the same endpoint or provide automated failover. Adding multiple Application Load Balancers and deploying the database with Elastic Beanstalk would not address database high availability; Beanstalk manages application deployment and ALBs route web traffic, but neither provides the database-level failover with endpoint persistence that the requirement specifies. Deploying a Network Load Balancer across CloudFront origins would not meet this requirement; NLB distributes network traffic to targets, and CloudFront serves web content from edge locations, neither of which applies to maintaining a consistent database instance endpoint with automatic AZ failover."},{"id":"q910","question":"Which AWS managed service can be used to distribute traffic between one or more Amazon EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"NAT gateway","B":"Elastic Load Balancing","C":"Amazon Athena","D":"AWS PrivateLink"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Elastic Load Balancing automatically distributes incoming application traffic across multiple EC2 instances, improving availability and fault tolerance by ensuring no single instance is overwhelmed.\\nA NAT gateway allows instances in a private subnet to initiate outbound internet traffic while preventing inbound connections, and has no role in distributing traffic across instances. Amazon Athena is an interactive query service for analyzing data stored in S3 using SQL, entirely unrelated to traffic distribution. AWS PrivateLink provides private connectivity between VPCs and AWS services without exposing traffic to the public internet, but does not distribute traffic across compute instances."},{"id":"q915","question":"Which AWS service can be used in the application deployment process?","options":{"A":"AWS AppSync","B":"AWS Batch","C":"AWS CodePipeline","D":"AWS DataSync"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS CodePipeline is a continuous delivery service that automates the build, test, and deploy phases of the application deployment process whenever code changes are made.\\nAWS AppSync is a managed GraphQL service that enables applications to retrieve and manipulate data from multiple sources through a single API, and has no role in automating application deployment pipelines. AWS Batch is a fully managed service for running large-scale batch computing jobs, and is designed for data processing workloads rather than application deployment automation. AWS DataSync is a data transfer service that automates moving data between on-premises storage and AWS, and has no application deployment or continuous delivery capability."},{"id":"q918","question":"Which AWS service or feature allows the user to manage cross-region application traffic?","options":{"A":"Amazon AppStream 2.0","B":"Amazon VPC","C":"Elastic Load Balancer","D":"Amazon Route 53"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 supports multiple routing policies including latency-based routing, geolocation routing, and failover routing that direct application traffic across multiple AWS Regions, enabling global traffic management with health checks and automatic failover.\\nAmazon AppStream 2.0 is a managed application and desktop streaming service that delivers desktop applications securely through a browser, and is an end-user computing service rather than a networking or traffic management tool for cross-Region application traffic. Amazon VPC provides isolated networking within an AWS Region, including subnets, route tables, security groups, and gateways, but VPC is a regional construct and does not manage application traffic routing between multiple Regions. Elastic Load Balancer distributes incoming traffic across multiple targets within a single AWS Region, and while it supports multi-AZ distribution within a Region, it does not route traffic across multiple different AWS Regions."},{"id":"q921","question":"A company must store critical business data in Amazon S3 with a backup to another AWS Region. How can this be achieved?","options":{"A":"Use an Amazon CloudFront Content Delivery Network (CDN) to cache data globally","B":"Set up Amazon S3 cross-region replication to another AWS Region","C":"Configure the AWS Backup service to back up to the data to another AWS Region","D":"Take Amazon S3 bucket snapshots and copy that data to another AWS Region"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon S3 cross-region replication automatically copies objects uploaded to a source bucket in one Region to a destination bucket in another Region, ensuring that a backup of the critical data exists in a geographically separate location.\\nUsing Amazon CloudFront to cache data globally distributes content to edge locations for faster access by end users, but CDN caching is not a durable backup mechanism; cached content is temporary and can be evicted, and CloudFront is not designed for storing permanent backup copies of S3 data. AWS Backup can back up data from various AWS services including S3, but for continuous cross-region replication of S3 objects the native S3 cross-region replication feature is the direct, purpose-built solution. Taking Amazon S3 bucket snapshots is not a native S3 feature; S3 does not have a native snapshot capability like EBS, and the correct approach for S3 backup to another Region is cross-region replication rather than snapshots."},{"id":"q925","question":"Which components are required to build a successful site-to-site VPN connection on AWS?","options":{"A":"Internet gateway","B":"NAT gateway","C":"Customer gateway","D":"Transit gateway","E":"Virtual private gateway"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"A site-to-site VPN connection requires a customer gateway representing the on-premises VPN device and a virtual private gateway attached to the VPC on the AWS side, together forming the two endpoints of the encrypted tunnel.\\nAn internet gateway connects a VPC to the public internet for general inbound and outbound traffic, and is not a component of a site-to-site VPN connection. A NAT gateway allows resources in a private subnet to initiate outbound internet connections while remaining unreachable from the internet, and has no role in establishing a VPN tunnel between on-premises infrastructure and AWS. A transit gateway acts as a central hub connecting multiple VPCs and on-premises networks together, and while it can be used in more complex VPN architectures, it is not a required component of a basic site-to-site VPN connection."},{"id":"q929","question":"Which AWS service offers persistent storage for a file system?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"Amazon EC2 instance store","C":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon EBS provides persistent block storage volumes that retain data independently of the EC2 instance lifecycle, making it suitable for file systems, databases, and applications requiring durable storage.\\nAmazon S3 is an object storage service designed for storing and retrieving files via API, and does not provide a mountable file system for use as persistent block storage. Amazon EC2 instance store provides temporary block storage that is physically attached to the host server and is lost when the instance stops or terminates, making it non-persistent. Amazon ElastiCache is an in-memory caching service used to improve application performance, and has no file system or persistent storage capability."},{"id":"q932","question":"Which of the following AWS services are serverless?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda","B":"Amazon OpenSearch Service","C":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","D":"Amazon DynamoDB","E":"Amazon Redshift"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Lambda runs code without provisioning or managing servers, and Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database that scales automatically without any server provisioning, making both fully serverless services.\\nAmazon OpenSearch Service requires customers to provision and manage clusters of instances, making it a server-based service rather than serverless. AWS Elastic Beanstalk is a platform service that automatically handles deployment and scaling, but still provisions and manages EC2 instances underneath, making it server-based. Amazon Redshift requires customers to provision and manage clusters for data warehousing workloads, and is not a serverless service by default."},{"id":"q933","question":"Which AWS managed services can be used to extend an on-premises data center to the AWS network?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"NAT gateway","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Amazon Connect","E":"Amazon Route 53"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS VPN creates encrypted tunnels over the public internet to connect on-premises networks to the AWS network, and AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private physical network connection between an on-premises data center and AWS. Both are the standard managed services for extending on-premises infrastructure to the AWS network.\\nA NAT gateway enables instances in a private subnet to initiate outbound traffic to the internet while preventing inbound connections, and is a VPC networking component rather than a service for connecting on-premises data centers to AWS. Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service for managing customer communications, and has no role in network connectivity or extending on-premises infrastructure to AWS. Amazon Route 53 is a DNS and domain routing service that directs users to application endpoints, and does not establish network connections between on-premises environments and AWS."},{"id":"q938","question":"A company has a 500 TB image repository that needs to be transported to AWS for processing. Which AWS service can import this data MOST cost-effectively?","options":{"A":"AWS Snowball","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS VPN","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Snowball can transport up to 80 TB per device and is the most cost-effective physical transport option for 500 TB, requiring only a small number of devices rather than relying on expensive or time-consuming network transfer.\\nAWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS for ongoing hybrid connectivity, and while it offers consistent bandwidth it is designed for continuous network access rather than one-time bulk data transport of hundreds of terabytes. AWS VPN creates an encrypted tunnel between on-premises infrastructure and AWS over the public internet, and is a network connectivity service unsuitable for transferring 500 TB of data due to the time and bandwidth costs involved in moving that volume over the internet. Amazon S3 is the destination storage service for the data rather than the transport mechanism. Uploading 500 TB directly to S3 over the internet would be prohibitively slow and expensive compared to physical transport using AWS Snowball devices."},{"id":"q939","question":"Which AWS service can run a managed PostgreSQL database that provides online transaction processing (OLTP)?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon Athena","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"Amazon EMR"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon RDS supports PostgreSQL as a managed database engine, handling automated provisioning, patching, backups, and high availability for online transaction processing workloads.\\nAmazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL key-value and document database, not a relational database engine and does not support PostgreSQL or OLTP workloads in the traditional sense. Amazon Athena is an interactive query service for analyzing data stored in S3 using SQL and is not a transactional database. Amazon EMR is a managed big data processing service for running distributed frameworks such as Spark and Hadoop, unrelated to relational database management."},{"id":"q951","question":"Which are benefits of using Amazon RDS over Amazon EC2 when running relational databases on AWS?","options":{"A":"Automated backups","B":"Schema management","C":"Indexing of tables","D":"Software patching","E":"Extract, transform, and load (ETL) management"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Amazon RDS provides automated backups with point-in-time recovery and handles software patching of the database engine automatically, freeing teams from these operational tasks that would need to be managed manually on EC2.\\nSchema management is the responsibility of the customer regardless of whether the database runs on RDS or EC2, as AWS has no visibility into the application\'s data model or business logic and does not manage database schemas. Indexing of tables is also a customer responsibility on both RDS and EC2, as index design depends on the application\'s query patterns and must be defined and maintained by the database administrator. ETL management involves extracting, transforming, and loading data between systems, which is handled by dedicated services such as AWS Glue rather than being a feature provided by RDS over EC2."},{"id":"q952","question":"What does the Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class offer?","options":{"A":"Payment flexibility by reserving storage capacity","B":"Long-term retention of data by copying the data to an encrypted Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS) volume","C":"Automatic cost savings by moving objects between tiers based on access pattern changes","D":"Secure, durable, and lowest cost storage for data archival"},"correct":"C","explanation":"S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically monitors access patterns and moves objects between a frequent access tier and an infrequent access tier without performance impact or operational overhead, reducing storage costs for data with unpredictable or changing access patterns.\\nPayment flexibility by reserving storage capacity describes a commitment-based pricing model that does not exist for Amazon S3, which charges based on actual usage rather than reserved capacity. Long-term retention of data by copying it to an encrypted EBS volume is not how S3 Intelligent-Tiering works. EBS is block storage attached to EC2 instances and is entirely separate from S3 storage classes, and Intelligent-Tiering manages data within S3 itself. Secure, durable, and lowest cost storage for data archival describes Amazon S3 Glacier, which is a separate storage class designed specifically for infrequently accessed archival data with retrieval times ranging from minutes to hours, not S3 Intelligent-Tiering."},{"id":"q953","question":"A company has multiple data sources across the organization and wants to consolidate data into one data warehouse. Which AWS service can be used to meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon Athena","D":"Amazon QuickSight"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Redshift is a fully managed cloud data warehouse designed to consolidate data from multiple sources and run complex analytical queries at scale using columnar storage and massively parallel processing.\\nAmazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL key-value and document database optimized for high-speed transactional workloads, not for consolidating data from multiple sources into a warehouse for analytics. Amazon Athena is an interactive query service that analyzes data directly in S3 using SQL, but it does not consolidate or store data in a warehouse. Amazon QuickSight is a business intelligence and data visualisation service for building dashboards and reports, not a data warehouse for consolidating data."},{"id":"q960","question":"Which AWS storage service is designed to transfer petabytes of data in and out of the cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS Storage Gateway","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"AWS Snowball"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Snowball is a physical data transport device from the AWS Snow Family designed to transfer petabytes of data in and out of the AWS Cloud, bypassing slow or expensive network transfers for large-scale migrations.\\nAWS Storage Gateway is a hybrid storage service that connects on-premises environments to AWS cloud storage for ongoing integration, and is not designed for bulk petabyte-scale physical data transfer. Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive is a low-cost archival storage class for data that is rarely accessed, and has no capability to physically or programmatically transfer large volumes of data into AWS. Amazon Lightsail is a simplified compute service for deploying virtual servers, containers, and web applications with minimal configuration, and has no connection to large-scale data transfer or migration."},{"id":"q961","question":"Which service provides a user the ability to warehouse data in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Amazon EFS","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"Amazon VPC"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Redshift is a fully managed data warehouse service that enables you to run complex analytical queries against petabytes of structured data using SQL, making it the purpose-built solution for data warehousing in the AWS Cloud.\\nAmazon EFS is a managed shared file system for use with compute instances, providing file storage rather than a data warehousing or analytical query capability. Amazon RDS is a managed relational database service designed for transactional workloads, not for petabyte-scale analytical data warehousing. Amazon VPC is a virtual private network service for isolating and configuring cloud network infrastructure, entirely unrelated to data storage or warehousing."},{"id":"q964","question":"Which AWS service can be used to provide an on-demand, cloud-based contact center?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"AWS Support Center","D":"AWS Managed Services"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service that can be set up in minutes, providing on-demand scalability with pay-per-use pricing for customer service operations.\\nAWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS for hybrid connectivity, and is a networking service with no capability to provide contact center functionality for managing customer communications. AWS Support Center is the portal where AWS customers create, view, and manage their own support cases with AWS, and is an internal support management interface rather than a service for building customer-facing contact centers. AWS Managed Services provides operational management of AWS infrastructure on behalf of enterprise customers including monitoring, patching, and incident management, and is an infrastructure operations service rather than a contact center platform for managing customer interactions."},{"id":"q966","question":"Which component must be attached to a VPC to enable inbound Internet access?","options":{"A":"NAT gateway","B":"VPC endpoint","C":"VPN connection","D":"Internet gateway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An internet gateway must be attached to a VPC to enable inbound internet access, allowing resources in public subnets to receive incoming traffic from the internet.\\nA NAT gateway allows resources in private subnets to initiate outbound connections to the internet but does not enable inbound internet traffic to reach resources inside a VPC. A VPC endpoint enables private connectivity between a VPC and supported AWS services without routing traffic over the internet, and has no role in enabling inbound internet access. A VPN connection creates an encrypted tunnel between a VPC and an on-premises or remote network over the public internet, and is not the component that enables general inbound internet access to a VPC."},{"id":"q968","question":"A company has a MySQL database running on a single Amazon EC2 instance. The company now requires higher availability in the event of an outage. Which set of tasks would meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"Add an Application Load Balancer in front of the EC2 instance","B":"Configure EC2 Auto Recovery to move the instance to another Availability Zone","C":"Migrate to Amazon RDS and enable Multi-AZ","D":"Enable termination protection for the EC2 instance to avoid outages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon RDS with Multi-AZ enabled maintains a synchronous standby in a separate Availability Zone and performs automatic failover with no manual intervention, providing high availability that a single EC2-hosted MySQL instance cannot match.\\nAdding an Application Load Balancer in front of a single EC2 database instance would distribute traffic but there would still be only one database instance, meaning a failure of that instance would take down the database regardless of the load balancer; ALBs distribute traffic across multiple targets and do not themselves provide database high availability. Configuring EC2 Auto Recovery moves a failed instance to new underlying hardware within the same Availability Zone if the underlying hardware fails, but this does not protect against AZ-level failures and involves downtime during recovery rather than the near-instantaneous failover that Multi-AZ RDS provides. Enabling termination protection prevents accidental termination of an EC2 instance through the console or API, but it does not prevent the instance from failing due to software issues, storage problems, or AZ-level events, and does not provide redundancy or automatic recovery."},{"id":"q972","question":"A company is building an application that requires the ability to send, store, and receive messages between application components. The company has another requirement to process messages in first-in, first-out (FIFO) order. Which AWS service should the company use?","options":{"A":"AWS Step Functions","B":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","C":"Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","D":"Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon SQS supports FIFO queues that guarantee messages are processed exactly once in the exact order they are received, making it the correct service for applications that require strict first-in, first-out message ordering with deduplication.\\nAWS Step Functions is a workflow orchestration service that coordinates distributed application components through visual workflows and state machines, and while it sequences steps in a defined order, it is not a message queuing service for decoupling application components that need to exchange messages in FIFO order. Amazon SNS is a fully managed pub/sub notification service that broadcasts messages to multiple subscribed endpoints simultaneously, and it does not support FIFO guarantees for ordered message delivery between specific senders and receivers. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams is a real-time data streaming service designed for processing large volumes of streaming data from multiple producers, and while it preserves order within a shard, it is designed for high-throughput analytics pipelines rather than application-to-application message queuing with FIFO guarantees."},{"id":"q975","question":"A user has limited knowledge of AWS services, but wants to quickly deploy a scalable Node.js application in the AWS Cloud. Which service should be used to deploy the application?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudFormation","B":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","C":"Amazon EC2","D":"AWS OpsWorks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk automatically handles deployment, scaling, load balancing, and monitoring for supported platforms including Node.js, allowing users with limited AWS knowledge to get a scalable application running quickly without configuring the underlying infrastructure.\\nAWS CloudFormation is an infrastructure as code service for provisioning AWS resources using templates, which requires significant AWS knowledge to use effectively and is not designed for quick application deployment. Amazon EC2 provides raw virtual servers that require manual configuration of the operating system, runtime, networking, and scaling, making it unsuitable for users with limited AWS experience who need a quick deployment. AWS OpsWorks (deprecated) is a configuration management service using Chef and Puppet that requires expertise in those tools to manage application deployments, adding complexity rather than simplifying it."},{"id":"q989","question":"Which AWS service or feature requires an internet service provider (ISP) and a colocation facility to be implemented?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Internet gateway"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect requires an ISP and a colocation facility at an AWS Direct Connect location to establish a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, making it the only option here with those physical prerequisites.\\nAWS VPN establishes an encrypted connection over the public internet and requires no ISP arrangement or colocation facility beyond a standard internet connection. Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service for managing customer communications and is entirely unrelated to network connectivity or physical infrastructure requirements. An internet gateway is a VPC component that enables communication between resources in a VPC and the public internet and requires no ISP or colocation arrangement to implement."},{"id":"q990","question":"Which AWS services offer compute capabilities?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)","D":"Amazon Cognito","E":"AWS Lambda"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Amazon EC2 provides virtual servers for running compute workloads with full control over the operating system and configuration, and AWS Lambda runs code in response to events without provisioning or managing any servers. Both are compute services that execute workloads.\\nAmazon S3 is an object storage service for storing and retrieving data via API and has no compute execution capability. Amazon Elastic Block Store is a block storage service that provides persistent volumes attached to EC2 instances, not a compute service in its own right. Amazon Cognito is an identity service for managing user authentication and access control for web and mobile applications, unrelated to compute capabilities."},{"id":"q991","question":"Which AWS service can be used to privately store and manage versions of source code?","options":{"A":"AWS CodeBuild","B":"AWS CodeCommit","C":"AWS CodePipeline","D":"AWS X-Ray"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CodeCommit is a fully managed source control service that hosts private Git repositories, providing secure storage and version management for source code.\\nAWS CodeBuild is a fully managed build service that compiles source code, runs tests, and produces deployment-ready packages, but does not store or manage source code repositories. AWS CodePipeline is a continuous delivery service that automates the stages of a release pipeline, orchestrating build, test, and deploy steps rather than storing or versioning source code. AWS X-Ray is a unified interface for managing software development projects that brings together multiple developer tools, but is a project management layer rather than a dedicated source code repository service."},{"id":"q993","question":"A company wants to ensure its infrastructure is designed for fault tolerance and business continuity in the event of an environmental disruption. Which AWS infrastructure component should the company replicate across?","options":{"A":"Edge locations","B":"Availability Zones","C":"Regions","D":"Amazon Route 53"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Replicating infrastructure across multiple Availability Zones within a Region provides fault tolerance and business continuity, ensuring operations continue even if one AZ is disrupted by an environmental event.\\nEdge locations are points of presence used by CloudFront to cache content close to end users, and are not infrastructure components that customers replicate their workloads across for fault tolerance. Regions are the broadest geographic groupings in AWS infrastructure, and while deploying across multiple Regions provides disaster recovery, replicating across Availability Zones within a Region is the standard approach for fault tolerance against environmental disruptions. Amazon Route 53 is a DNS service that routes traffic between endpoints, and is not an infrastructure component that workloads are replicated across."},{"id":"q994","question":"Which AWS service or feature is used to send both text and email messages from distributed applications?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","B":"Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","C":"Amazon CloudWatch alerts","D":"Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SNS is a pub/sub messaging service that sends both text (SMS) and email messages from distributed applications to subscribers through topics, making it suitable for sending notifications across multiple channels simultaneously.\\nAmazon Simple Email Service is a cloud-based email sending service designed for sending marketing, transactional, and bulk email, but it only supports email and cannot send SMS text messages. Amazon CloudWatch alerts trigger notifications when metric thresholds are breached, but CloudWatch itself does not send text or email messages directly to end users from distributed applications. Amazon SQS is a message queuing service that buffers messages between application components to enable asynchronous communication, but it does not send text or email messages to subscribers."},{"id":"q996","question":"A company wants to connect to AWS over a private, low-latency connection from its remote office. What is the recommended method to meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Create a VPN tunnel","B":"Connect across the public internet","C":"Use VPC peering to create a connection","D":"Use AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private physical connection between a remote office and AWS, bypassing the public internet entirely to deliver consistent low-latency performance with predictable throughput.\\nA VPN tunnel encrypts traffic between a remote office and AWS but routes it over the public internet, meaning latency and performance can vary with internet conditions rather than being guaranteed by a dedicated link. Connecting across the public internet provides no dedicated bandwidth, no privacy guarantees, and no consistent latency, making it unsuitable for workloads requiring reliable, low-latency connectivity to AWS. VPC peering creates a private network connection between two VPCs within AWS to allow direct traffic routing between them, and cannot be used to connect an external remote office to AWS infrastructure."},{"id":"q999","question":"How should a web application be deployed to ensure high availability in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Deploy multiple instances of the application in multiple Availability Zones","B":"Deploy multiple instances of the application in a single Availability Zone","C":"Deploy the application to a compute-optimized Amazon EC2 instance in a single Availability Zone","D":"Deploy the application in one Amazon EC2 instance in an Auto Scaling group"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Deploying multiple instances across multiple Availability Zones ensures high availability by providing redundancy, so if one AZ fails, instances in other AZs continue serving traffic without interruption.\\nDeploying multiple instances in a single Availability Zone improves capacity and performance but provides no protection against an AZ-level failure, leaving the application vulnerable to a single point of failure. Deploying to a compute-optimized EC2 instance in a single Availability Zone addresses performance for compute-intensive workloads but offers no redundancy or fault tolerance. Deploying one EC2 instance in an Auto Scaling group allows the group to replace a failed instance but still leaves a gap in availability while the replacement is provisioned, and does not protect against an AZ failure."},{"id":"q1005","question":"A company is building an application that needs to deliver images and videos globally with minimal latency. Which approach can the company use to accomplish this in a cost effective manner?","options":{"A":"Deliver the content through Amazon CloudFront","B":"Store the content on Amazon S3 and enable S3 cross-region replication","C":"Implement a VPN across multiple AWS Regions","D":"Deliver the content through AWS PrivateLink"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront caches images and videos at hundreds of edge locations worldwide, serving content to users from the nearest edge location rather than the origin, which minimizes latency and maximizes transfer speeds in a cost-effective manner for globally distributed audiences.\\nStoring content on Amazon S3 and enabling cross-region replication copies objects to S3 buckets in other Regions, which improves data availability and durability, but users still retrieve content directly from S3 in a specific Region rather than from a globally distributed edge network, resulting in higher latency than CloudFront provides. Implementing a VPN across multiple AWS Regions creates encrypted network connectivity between Regions or between on-premises environments and AWS, but a VPN is not a mechanism for delivering content to end users with low latency and does not provide edge caching capabilities. Delivering content through AWS PrivateLink provides private connectivity between VPCs and AWS services without traffic traversing the public internet, and is designed for secure service-to-service communication within AWS rather than for delivering content to external users worldwide with low latency."},{"id":"q1008","question":"A pharmaceutical company operates its infrastructure in a single AWS Region. The company has thousands of VPCs in a various AWS accounts that it wants to interconnect. Which AWS service or feature should the company use to help simplify management and reduce operational costs?","options":{"A":"VPC endpoint","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS Transit Gateway","D":"VPC peering"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Transit Gateway acts as a central network hub that connects thousands of VPCs across multiple AWS accounts through a single managed gateway, eliminating the need to manage individual point-to-point connections and significantly reducing operational complexity and cost at scale.\\nVPC endpoints provide private connectivity between a VPC and supported AWS services without traversing the public internet, but do not connect VPCs to each other. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network link between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, making it irrelevant when the goal is interconnecting VPCs that are already within AWS. VPC peering creates individual one-to-one connections between VPC pairs, which becomes unmanageable and costly at scale when thousands of VPCs need to communicate."},{"id":"q1011","question":"A company has users across multiple geographic regions experiencing high latency when accessing its application hosted in a single AWS Region. Which AWS service can improve global application availability and performance by directing traffic through the AWS global network?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Global Accelerator","C":"Amazon Route 53","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator improves availability and performance for global users by directing traffic through the AWS global network infrastructure using Anycast IP addresses to route requests to the optimal endpoint, reducing latency regardless of where users are located.\\nAmazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and serves static and dynamic content from edge locations close to users, but it is designed for content caching rather than routing live application traffic through the AWS global network to a single hosted endpoint. Amazon Route 53 is a DNS service that resolves domain names to IP addresses and can route traffic using latency-based or geolocation policies, but it operates at the DNS layer rather than directing traffic through the AWS global network infrastructure. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and is not a service for improving performance for geographically distributed internet users accessing a cloud-hosted application."},{"id":"q1012","question":"Which AWS service provides static Anycast IP addresses that act as a fixed entry point to application endpoints in one or more AWS Regions?","options":{"A":"Elastic Load Balancing","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"AWS Global Accelerator","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator provides two static Anycast IP addresses that serve as a fixed entry point for applications, routing traffic through the AWS global network to endpoints in the nearest Region for improved performance and availability.\\nElastic Load Balancing distributes incoming traffic across multiple targets within a single Region and does not provide static Anycast IP addresses or route traffic across multiple Regions through the AWS global network. Amazon Route 53 is a DNS-based routing service that resolves domain names to IP addresses and can route traffic using policies such as latency-based or geolocation routing, but it operates at the DNS layer rather than providing static Anycast IP addresses as fixed entry points. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and serves content from edge locations close to users, and does not provide static Anycast IP addresses or act as a fixed network entry point for application endpoints."},{"id":"q1013","question":"A company needs to run AWS services on-premises due to data residency requirements. Which AWS service allows the company to run AWS infrastructure and services in its own data center?","options":{"A":"AWS Wavelength","B":"AWS Local Zones","C":"AWS Outposts","D":"AWS Snow Family"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Outposts is a fully managed service that delivers AWS infrastructure, services, APIs, and tools to a customer\'s own data center, allowing workloads with strict data residency requirements to remain on-premises while still using AWS managed services.\\nAWS Wavelength extends AWS compute and storage to the edge of 5G networks for ultra-low latency applications, not for running AWS services inside a customer\'s own data center. AWS Local Zones place AWS compute, storage, and database services closer to large population centers for latency-sensitive workloads, but the infrastructure remains AWS-owned and operated. AWS Snow Family provides physical devices for edge computing and offline data transfer to and from AWS, not for running a permanent AWS infrastructure deployment on-premises."},{"id":"q1014","question":"Which AWS hybrid cloud solution brings native AWS services, infrastructure, and operating models to virtually any data center, co-location space, or on-premises facility?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"AWS VPN","C":"AWS Storage Gateway","D":"AWS Outposts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Outposts extends native AWS infrastructure, services, and operating models to on-premises locations, data centers, and co-location spaces, providing a truly consistent hybrid experience with the same AWS APIs and tools used in the cloud.\\nAWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network link between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, but does not bring AWS infrastructure physically on-premises. AWS VPN creates encrypted network connections between on-premises environments and AWS over the public internet, and is a connectivity service rather than an infrastructure extension. AWS Storage Gateway connects on-premises storage environments to AWS cloud storage, but only bridges storage and does not extend the full range of native AWS services and infrastructure to on-premises locations."},{"id":"q1016","question":"A media company needs to run latency-sensitive applications closer to end users in a specific metropolitan area that does not have a nearby AWS Region. Which AWS infrastructure option should they consider?","options":{"A":"AWS Outposts","B":"AWS Local Zones","C":"AWS Wavelength","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Local Zones place AWS compute, storage, database, and other select services closer to large population centers and IT hubs where no nearby AWS Region exists, providing single-digit millisecond latency to end users for latency-sensitive applications.\\nAWS Outposts brings native AWS infrastructure and services into a customer\'s own on-premises data center, and is designed for workloads that must remain on-premises rather than for extending AWS presence to new metropolitan areas. AWS Wavelength embeds AWS compute and storage within telecommunications providers\' 5G networks to deliver ultra-low latency for mobile and connected device use cases, and is specific to 5G edge deployments rather than general metropolitan area coverage. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and distributes content at edge locations worldwide to reduce latency for end users, but it is a content caching service rather than an infrastructure option for running latency-sensitive compute workloads closer to users."},{"id":"q1017","question":"A company wants to build an event-driven architecture where multiple AWS services and SaaS applications can react to events in real time. Which AWS service provides a serverless event bus for this purpose?","options":{"A":"Amazon SNS","B":"Amazon SQS","C":"Amazon EventBridge","D":"AWS Step Functions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus that ingests events from your own applications, SaaS providers, and AWS services, and routes them to targets using filtering and routing rules, making it the purpose-built solution for event-driven architectures.\\nAmazon SNS is a fully managed pub/sub messaging service that pushes notifications to subscribers such as Lambda functions, HTTP endpoints, and email addresses, and is a notification delivery service rather than an event bus capable of routing structured events from SaaS applications with filtering rules. Amazon SQS is a fully managed message queuing service that decouples application components by holding messages in a queue for processing, and is a point-to-point queuing tool rather than a serverless event bus for real-time event routing across multiple services and SaaS integrations. AWS Step Functions is a serverless workflow orchestration service that coordinates the steps of distributed applications using visual state machines, and is a workflow execution tool rather than an event bus."},{"id":"q1018","question":"Which AWS service enables applications to respond to state changes in AWS resources by routing events to targets such as Lambda functions, SQS queues, or SNS topics?","options":{"A":"Amazon Kinesis","B":"Amazon EventBridge","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"Amazon MQ"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus that detects and routes events from AWS services, custom applications, and SaaS partners to targets such as Lambda, SQS, and SNS, enabling event-driven architectures.\\nAmazon Kinesis is a service for ingesting and processing high-volume real-time streaming data, not for routing discrete events between AWS services. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity for auditing purposes, and does not route events to application targets. Amazon MQ is a managed message broker for applications that use standard messaging protocols, and is not an event routing service for AWS resource state changes."},{"id":"q1023","question":"A company wants to set up a cloud-based contact center to handle customer service calls. Which AWS service provides this capability?","options":{"A":"Amazon Chime","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"AWS Support Center","D":"Amazon WorkSpaces"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service that makes it easy to set up and manage a customer service center with pay-per-use pricing and no infrastructure to manage.\\nAmazon Chime was a communications service for video conferencing, messaging, and online meetings that was retired in February 2024, and was designed for internal business collaboration rather than customer-facing contact center operations. AWS Support Center is the portal for managing AWS support cases and accessing technical assistance from AWS, not a service for building customer contact centers. Amazon WorkSpaces is a managed virtual desktop service for delivering cloud-based desktops to end users, unrelated to contact center or customer service capabilities."},{"id":"q1024","question":"A company wants to build, train, and deploy machine learning models at scale. Which AWS service provides a fully managed environment for this purpose?","options":{"A":"Amazon Rekognition","B":"Amazon SageMaker","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker is a fully managed machine learning service that provides tools to build, train, and deploy ML models at scale, covering the entire machine learning workflow in a single managed environment.\\nAmazon Rekognition is a computer vision service that analyzes images and videos to detect faces, objects, and scenes, and does not provide a general-purpose environment for building and training custom ML models. AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that runs code in response to events, and while it can run inference on pre-built models, it is not designed for building and training machine learning models at scale. Amazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service that extracts meaning and insights from text, and is a pre-built ML service rather than a platform for building and training custom models."},{"id":"q1025","question":"Which AWS service can automatically detect objects, people, text, and activities in images and videos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Transcribe","C":"Amazon Rekognition","D":"Amazon Textract"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Rekognition is a deep learning-based image and video analysis service that can automatically detect objects, people, text, scenes, and activities in visual content.\\nAmazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service that analyzes and extracts meaning from written text, and has no capability to process images or videos. Amazon Transcribe converts spoken audio into written text, and is a speech-to-text service rather than an image or video analysis service. Amazon Textract extracts printed text, handwriting, and structured data from scanned documents and images, but does not detect objects, people, or activities in images and videos the way Rekognition does."},{"id":"q1026","question":"A company needs to analyze customer reviews to determine sentiment and extract key phrases. Which AWS service uses natural language processing (NLP) for this purpose?","options":{"A":"Amazon Lex","B":"Amazon Polly","C":"Amazon Comprehend","D":"Amazon Translate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Comprehend uses natural language processing to analyze text and extract insights such as sentiment, entities, key phrases, and language, making it the purpose-built NLP service for analyzing customer reviews.\\nAmazon Lex is a service for building conversational interfaces such as chatbots using voice and text, and is a dialogue management tool rather than a text analysis or sentiment extraction service. Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service that converts written text into lifelike spoken audio, and has no capability to analyze text for sentiment or extract key phrases. Amazon Translate is a neural machine translation service that converts text from one language to another, and is a language conversion tool rather than a service for performing sentiment analysis or key phrase extraction."},{"id":"q1027","question":"Which AWS service enables developers to build conversational interfaces (chatbots) using voice and text?","options":{"A":"Amazon Polly","B":"Amazon Lex","C":"Amazon Transcribe","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Lex provides deep learning functionality for building conversational interfaces and chatbots using voice and text, and is the same technology that powers Amazon Alexa.\\nAmazon Polly converts written text into natural-sounding spoken audio and is a text-to-speech service, not a tool for building conversational interfaces that understand and respond to user input. Amazon Transcribe converts spoken audio into written text and is a speech-to-text service, focused on transcription rather than building interactive conversational experiences. Amazon Comprehend analyzes written text using natural language processing to extract meaning, sentiment, and entities, and does not process voice input or enable conversational interactions."},{"id":"q1028","question":"Which AWS service converts text into lifelike speech, allowing developers to create applications that talk?","options":{"A":"Amazon Transcribe","B":"Amazon Translate","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Lex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Polly converts text into lifelike speech using deep learning, supporting dozens of languages and voices to enable developers to build applications that talk.\\nAmazon Transcribe does the opposite of text-to-speech, converting audio speech into written text, making it unrelated to generating spoken output from written content. Amazon Translate is a neural machine translation service that converts text from one language to another, and has no text-to-speech capability. Amazon Lex is a service for building conversational chatbot interfaces using voice and text, and does not itself convert written text into lifelike speech output."},{"id":"q1029","question":"A company needs to automatically convert audio recordings from customer calls into text. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"Amazon Polly","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon Transcribe","D":"Amazon Lex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Transcribe is an automatic speech recognition service that converts spoken audio into written text, making it the right choice for transcribing customer call recordings.\\nAmazon Polly performs the reverse function, converting written text into natural-sounding spoken audio, and cannot process audio recordings into text. Amazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service that analyzes and extracts meaning from written text, and requires text as input rather than audio recordings. Amazon Lex is a service for building conversational chatbot interfaces using voice and text, focused on understanding and responding to user input rather than transcribing recorded audio into text."},{"id":"q1030","question":"Which AWS service provides neural machine translation to convert text between different languages?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Transcribe","C":"Amazon Lex","D":"Amazon Translate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Translate uses neural machine translation to deliver fast, high-quality translation of text between different languages.\\nAmazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service that analyzes written text to extract insights such as sentiment, entities, and key phrases, and does not perform language translation. Amazon Transcribe converts spoken audio into written text, and is a speech-to-text service rather than a translation service. Amazon Lex is a service for building conversational interfaces and chatbots using voice and text, and has no language translation capability."},{"id":"q1031","question":"Which AWS service allows customers to run Docker containers without managing the underlying infrastructure?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"AWS Fargate","C":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","D":"Amazon EKS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers that removes the need to provision or manage the underlying server infrastructure, allowing customers to run Docker containers by specifying only CPU and memory requirements.\\nAmazon EC2 provides virtual server instances that customers must provision, configure, and manage themselves, making it a server-based option that requires infrastructure management. AWS Elastic Beanstalk is a platform service for deploying web applications that abstracts infrastructure management, but is not designed specifically for running Docker containers without infrastructure oversight. Amazon EKS is a managed Kubernetes service for orchestrating containerised workloads, but still requires customers to provision and manage the underlying EC2 node capacity unless explicitly paired with Fargate."},{"id":"q1032","question":"A company wants to use Kubernetes to orchestrate containerized applications on AWS. Which AWS service provides a managed Kubernetes experience?","options":{"A":"Amazon ECS","B":"Amazon EKS","C":"AWS Fargate","D":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EKS is a fully managed Kubernetes service that makes it easy to run Kubernetes on AWS without needing to install, operate, or maintain your own Kubernetes control plane.\\nAmazon ECS is AWS\'s proprietary container orchestration service that uses its own scheduling and management model rather than Kubernetes, and is not a managed Kubernetes experience. AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers that removes the need to manage underlying server infrastructure, but is a compute layer rather than a Kubernetes orchestration service. AWS Elastic Beanstalk is a platform service for deploying web applications that abstracts infrastructure management, and is not a container orchestration or Kubernetes service."},{"id":"q1033","question":"Which AWS service is a fully managed container orchestration service that supports Docker containers and allows scheduling containers on a managed cluster of Amazon EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"Amazon EKS","B":"AWS Lambda","C":"Amazon ECS","D":"AWS Batch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon ECS is a fully managed container orchestration service that supports Docker containers and allows scheduling and running containers on a managed cluster of EC2 instances, integrating deeply with the AWS ecosystem.\\nAmazon EKS is a managed Kubernetes service for orchestrating containerised workloads using Kubernetes rather than ECS\'s native scheduling. AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that runs code in response to events without provisioning or managing servers, not a container orchestration platform. AWS Batch is a managed service for running large-scale batch computing jobs and is not designed for general container orchestration or scheduling."},{"id":"q1042","question":"A company needs to automate the transfer of data between its on-premises storage systems and Amazon S3. Which AWS service simplifies and accelerates this data transfer?","options":{"A":"AWS Storage Gateway","B":"AWS DataSync","C":"AWS Snowball","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS DataSync is a data transfer service that simplifies, automates, and accelerates moving data between on-premises storage systems and AWS storage services including Amazon S3, Amazon EFS, and Amazon FSx.\\nAWS Storage Gateway is a hybrid storage service that presents cloud storage as local file shares, volumes, or tape to on-premises applications for ongoing access, rather than automating one-time or scheduled data transfer to S3. AWS Snowball is a physical data transfer device designed for offline bulk data migration where transferring over the network is impractical due to data volume or bandwidth constraints, and does not automate ongoing transfers between on-premises systems and S3. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, which improves network throughput and consistency but does not itself automate or manage the transfer of data between storage systems."},{"id":"q1043","question":"Which AWS service can automatically move data between on-premises storage and Amazon EFS or Amazon S3 up to 10 times faster than open-source tools?","options":{"A":"AWS Transfer Family","B":"AWS DataSync","C":"AWS Migration Hub","D":"Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS DataSync automatically transfers data between on-premises storage and Amazon EFS or Amazon S3 up to 10 times faster than open-source tools, with built-in encryption, data validation, and network optimization.\\nAWS Transfer Family provides fully managed file transfer workflows into and out of Amazon S3 and Amazon EFS using protocols such as SFTP, FTPS, and FTP, and is a protocol-based file transfer service rather than a high-speed automated data movement service between on-premises storage and AWS. AWS Migration Hub provides a central location for tracking the progress of application migrations across multiple AWS migration tools, and is a migration monitoring and coordination service rather than a data transfer service. Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is a fully managed service for loading real-time streaming data into destinations such as Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, and Amazon OpenSearch Service, and is a streaming data ingestion service rather than a tool for transferring files from on-premises storage."},{"id":"q1044","question":"A company needs a cloud-native business intelligence (BI) service to create interactive dashboards and visualizations from multiple data sources. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"Amazon Athena","B":"Amazon QuickSight","C":"Amazon Redshift","D":"AWS Glue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon QuickSight is a cloud-native serverless business intelligence service for creating interactive dashboards and visualisations that can connect to a wide range of data sources including S3, RDS, Redshift, and Athena.\\nAmazon Athena is an interactive query service for analyzing data stored in S3 using SQL and is a data source that QuickSight can connect to, not a visualisation tool. Amazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse for storing and querying large volumes of structured data, not a business intelligence or dashboard service. AWS Glue is a managed extract, transform, and load service for preparing and moving data between sources, not for creating dashboards or visualisations."},{"id":"q1045","question":"Which AWS service provides serverless business intelligence with pay-per-session pricing and machine learning-powered insights?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon EMR","C":"Amazon QuickSight","D":"Amazon Athena"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon QuickSight is a serverless business intelligence service that offers pay-per-session pricing and machine learning-powered insights through natural language queries, making it the purpose-built BI tool in the AWS analytics portfolio.\\nAmazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse service optimized for running analytical SQL queries across large datasets, and is a data storage and querying service rather than a business intelligence or visualisation tool. Amazon EMR is a managed cluster platform for processing large volumes of data using big data frameworks such as Hadoop and Spark, and is a data processing service with no BI or visualisation capability. Amazon Athena is a serverless query service that analyzes data stored in Amazon S3 using SQL, and is a querying tool rather than a business intelligence service with dashboards, visualisations, or ML-powered insights."},{"id":"q1046","question":"A company wants to centrally manage and automate backups across multiple AWS services including Amazon EBS, Amazon RDS, and Amazon DynamoDB. Which AWS service provides this capability?","options":{"A":"AWS Storage Gateway","B":"Amazon S3 Lifecycle","C":"AWS Backup","D":"AWS CloudFormation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Backup is a fully managed service that centralizes and automates data protection across multiple AWS services including Amazon EBS, Amazon RDS, and Amazon DynamoDB, providing a single place to manage backup policies and monitor activity.\\nAWS Storage Gateway is a hybrid storage service that connects on-premises environments to AWS cloud storage, and is not designed to centrally manage and automate backups across AWS services. Amazon S3 Lifecycle is a configuration feature that automatically transitions objects between S3 storage classes or expires them after a set period, and does not manage backups across other AWS services. AWS CloudFormation is an infrastructure-as-code service that provisions and manages AWS resources through templates, and has no capability to automate or centralize data backup across services."},{"id":"q1047","question":"Which AWS service allows you to define backup policies, schedule backups, and monitor backup activity across your AWS resources from a single console?","options":{"A":"AWS Systems Manager","B":"AWS Backup","C":"Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Backup is a fully managed backup service that makes it easy to centralize and automate the back up of data across AWS services in the cloud and on-premises. It provides a single console to create backup plans (policies) that automate backup scheduling and retention management for multiple services like Amazon EBS, RDS, DynamoDB, EFS, and more.\\nAWS Systems Manager is a management service used to view and control your AWS infrastructure, focusing on operational tasks like patching and configuration management rather than centralized data backups. Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager (DLM) is designed specifically to automate the creation, retention, and deletion of Amazon EBS snapshots and EBS-backed AMIs; it does not provide a centralized solution for other AWS services. AWS Config is a service that enables you to assess, audit, and evaluate the configurations of your AWS resources, providing a history of resource changes rather than managing backup schedules or policies."},{"id":"q1048","question":"A company needs a fully managed extract, transform, and load (ETL) service to prepare and transform data for analytics. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"Amazon Kinesis","B":"AWS Glue","C":"Amazon EMR","D":"AWS Data Pipeline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Glue is a fully managed serverless ETL service that crawls data sources, creates a metadata catalog, and prepares and transforms data for analytics workloads.\\nAmazon Kinesis is a real-time data streaming service designed for ingesting and processing continuous data streams, and is not an ETL service for preparing and transforming data for analytics. Amazon EMR is a big data processing service that runs open-source frameworks such as Apache Hadoop and Spark, and while it can perform transformations it requires significant configuration and cluster management rather than being a fully managed ETL service. AWS Data Pipeline (deprecated) is an orchestration service that automates the movement and transformation of data between AWS services and on-premises sources on a scheduled basis, but it is a workflow orchestration tool rather than a fully managed ETL service purpose-built for analytics preparation."},{"id":"q1049","question":"Which AWS service automatically discovers and catalogs metadata from various data sources and can generate ETL scripts for data transformation?","options":{"A":"Amazon Athena","B":"AWS Glue","C":"Amazon Redshift Spectrum","D":"AWS Lake Formation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Glue automatically discovers and catalogs metadata from data sources using crawlers that populate the Glue Data Catalog, and can generate and run ETL scripts to transform data for analytics workloads.\\nAmazon Athena is a serverless query service that analyzes data stored in Amazon S3 using SQL, and relies on the Glue Data Catalog as its metadata store rather than performing discovery or ETL itself. Amazon Redshift Spectrum is a feature of Amazon Redshift that enables querying data stored in S3 directly from Redshift without loading it, and is a query extension rather than a metadata discovery or ETL service. AWS Lake Formation is a service for building, securing, and managing data lakes on AWS, and while it uses the Glue Data Catalog, it is focused on data lake governance rather than automated metadata discovery or ETL script generation."},{"id":"q1050","question":"A company needs to collect, process, and analyze real-time streaming data from IoT devices. Which AWS service is designed for real-time data streaming?","options":{"A":"Amazon SQS","B":"Amazon Kinesis","C":"Amazon SNS","D":"AWS Batch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Kinesis is designed to collect, process, and analyze real-time streaming data at scale, making it the right choice for continuously ingesting and processing data from IoT devices as it is generated.\\nAmazon SQS is a message queuing service that decouples application components by buffering messages between them, but it is designed for asynchronous message delivery between services rather than continuous real-time data stream processing. Amazon SNS is a pub/sub notification service that pushes messages to multiple subscribers simultaneously, and is designed for event notifications and alerts rather than ingesting and processing high-volume continuous data streams. AWS Batch is a managed service for running large-scale batch computing jobs on dynamically provisioned resources, and processes data in scheduled or on-demand batches rather than in real time."},{"id":"q1051","question":"Which AWS service can ingest and process hundreds of terabytes of data per hour from sources like website clickstreams, financial transactions, and social media feeds?","options":{"A":"Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","B":"Amazon SQS","C":"Amazon Redshift","D":"AWS Data Pipeline"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Kinesis Data Streams continuously captures and processes large volumes of streaming data in real time from sources such as website clickstreams, financial transactions, and social media feeds.\\nAmazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) is a managed message queuing service used to decouple application components by passing messages between them, and is not designed for high-throughput real-time data stream ingestion at scale. Amazon Redshift is a cloud data warehouse service optimized for running analytical queries across large datasets, and does not ingest or process continuous real-time data streams. AWS Data Pipeline (deprecated) is an orchestration service for scheduling and automating the movement and transformation of data between AWS services and on-premises sources on a batch basis, and is not suited for real-time streaming ingestion."},{"id":"q1052","question":"A company wants to migrate its on-premises Oracle database to Amazon Aurora with minimal downtime. Which AWS service facilitates this database migration?","options":{"A":"AWS DataSync","B":"AWS Database Migration Service (DMS)","C":"AWS Application Migration Service","D":"AWS Transfer Family"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Database Migration Service helps migrate databases to AWS with minimal downtime by keeping the source database fully operational during the migration process, and supports both homogeneous migrations such as Oracle to Oracle and heterogeneous migrations such as Oracle to Amazon Aurora.\\nAWS DataSync is a data transfer service that automates moving files and objects between on-premises storage and AWS storage services such as S3 and EFS, and is designed for file and object storage transfer rather than database migration. AWS Application Migration Service automates the migration of on-premises servers and applications to AWS by replicating them at the server level, and is a server migration service rather than a database migration service. AWS Transfer Family provides managed file transfer workflows into and out of Amazon S3 and Amazon EFS using protocols such as SFTP and FTP, and is a file transfer service with no database migration capability."},{"id":"q1053","question":"Which AWS service supports both homogeneous (e.g., Oracle to Oracle) and heterogeneous (e.g., Oracle to Aurora) database migrations?","options":{"A":"AWS Schema Conversion Tool","B":"AWS Database Migration Service (DMS)","C":"AWS Migration Hub","D":"AWS Snowball Edge"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Database Migration Service supports both homogeneous migrations, such as Oracle to Oracle, and heterogeneous migrations, such as Oracle to Aurora, keeping the source database fully operational during the migration to minimize downtime.\\nAWS Schema Conversion Tool is used alongside AWS DMS for heterogeneous migrations to convert the source database schema to be compatible with the target database engine, but it does not perform the actual data migration itself. AWS Migration Hub provides a centralized dashboard for tracking the progress of application migrations across multiple AWS migration tools, and is not a database migration service. AWS Snowball Edge is a physical edge computing and data transfer device used to move large volumes of data into AWS when network transfer is impractical, and is not a database migration service."},{"id":"q1054","question":"A company wants to continuously replicate data from an on-premises database to an AWS database for disaster recovery purposes. Which AWS service supports continuous data replication?","options":{"A":"AWS Backup","B":"AWS Database Migration Service (DMS)","C":"Amazon RDS Multi-AZ","D":"AWS CloudFormation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS DMS supports continuous data replication using Change Data Capture, streaming ongoing changes from an on-premises database to an AWS database in near real-time, making it suitable for disaster recovery and data synchronisation scenarios.\\nAWS Backup creates point-in-time snapshots of AWS resources on a scheduled basis, which provides recovery points but does not continuously replicate live database changes. Amazon RDS Multi-AZ maintains a synchronous standby replica within AWS for high availability and automatic failover, but does not replicate data from on-premises sources. AWS CloudFormation provisions and manages AWS infrastructure using code-based templates, and has no database replication capability."},{"id":"q1067","question":"A company wants to enable employees to search across thousands of internal documents, SharePoint sites, and wikis using plain English questions and receive precise answers rather than a list of links. Which AWS service provides this intelligent enterprise search capability?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon OpenSearch Service","C":"Amazon Kendra","D":"Amazon Lex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Kendra is an intelligent enterprise search service powered by machine learning that allows users to ask questions in natural language and receive precise answers drawn from across multiple data sources such as documents, SharePoint sites, and databases, rather than returning a simple ranked list of matching links.\\nAmazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service that extracts insights such as sentiment, entities, and key phrases from already-digitised text, but it does not provide a search interface or retrieve answers from a document corpus in response to natural language user queries. Amazon OpenSearch Service is a managed search and analytics engine suited for log analytics, full-text search, and operational monitoring, and returns ranked document matches based on keyword relevance rather than providing the intelligent question-answering and precise answer extraction that Kendra offers through machine learning. Amazon Lex is a service for building conversational chatbots and voice interfaces using automatic speech recognition and natural language understanding, and is designed for managing multi-turn dialogues rather than indexing and searching across enterprise document repositories."},{"id":"q1068","question":"Which AWS service uses machine learning to index content from enterprise data sources and enable users to ask natural language questions to find specific answers within that content?","options":{"A":"Amazon Transcribe","B":"Amazon Kendra","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Translate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Kendra uses machine learning to index content from a wide range of enterprise data sources and enables users to search that content by asking natural language questions, with the service intelligently identifying and surfacing the most relevant answers rather than returning a list of documents.\\nAmazon Transcribe is a speech-to-text service that converts audio recordings and spoken language into written text transcripts, and is an audio processing service with no capability to index enterprise documents or respond to natural language search queries. Amazon Polly is a text-to-speech service that converts written text into lifelike spoken audio output, and is an audio synthesis service rather than a search or document information retrieval service. Amazon Translate is a neural machine translation service for converting text between different languages, and is a language conversion service with no capability to index documents or respond to natural language search queries."},{"id":"q1070","question":"A company is building a recommendation engine that needs to store and query complex relationships between users, products, categories, and purchase history to surface personalized suggestions. Which AWS database service is purpose-built for storing and traversing highly connected data?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon Neptune"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Neptune is a fully managed graph database service designed specifically for storing and navigating highly connected datasets where the relationships between data points are as important as the data itself, making it the purpose-built choice for use cases such as recommendation engines, social networks, fraud detection, and knowledge graphs.\\nAmazon RDS is a managed relational database service that uses tables with rows and columns connected through SQL joins to represent relationships, and while it can model connected data, it does not provide the native graph traversal efficiency and flexibility of a dedicated graph database for complex multi-hop relationship queries. Amazon Redshift is a fully managed cloud data warehouse optimized for running analytical queries on large historical datasets for business intelligence and reporting, and is not designed for real-time traversal of highly connected relationship graphs. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL key-value and document database optimized for high-throughput access to items by primary key, and is not purpose-built for efficiently storing or querying the dense relationship structures characteristic of graph database workloads."},{"id":"q1071","question":"A developer needs to build a serverless order processing workflow that involves calling multiple AWS Lambda functions in sequence, handling errors at each step, automatically retrying failed steps, and branching based on order type. Which AWS service is designed for this purpose?","options":{"A":"Amazon SQS","B":"Amazon EventBridge","C":"AWS Step Functions","D":"Amazon SNS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Step Functions is a serverless orchestration service that coordinates multiple AWS services into visual workflows, supporting sequential and parallel execution, error handling, automatic retries, and conditional branching, making it the purpose-built service for building complex multi-step workflows such as order processing pipelines.\\nAmazon SQS is a managed message queuing service that decouples application components by buffering messages between them, but does not provide workflow orchestration, branching logic, built-in retry handling, or the ability to coordinate a sequence of steps across multiple services in a stateful workflow. Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus that routes events between AWS services and applications based on rules, and is designed for event-driven integration and routing rather than for orchestrating complex stateful multi-step workflows with conditional branching and error handling. Amazon SNS is a managed publish/subscribe messaging service that delivers notifications simultaneously to multiple subscribers, and is an event fan-out service rather than an orchestration service capable of managing state, sequencing steps, and handling errors across a multi-step workflow."},{"id":"q1072","question":"A company wants to securely connect millions of industrial sensors to AWS so that device data can be routed to Amazon Kinesis for real-time processing and to Amazon S3 for storage. Which AWS service manages the device connectivity, authentication, and message routing?","options":{"A":"AWS IoT Greengrass","B":"Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","C":"AWS IoT Core","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS IoT Core is a managed cloud service that allows connected devices to interact securely with cloud applications and other devices at scale, handling device authentication, connection management, and message routing to AWS services such as Kinesis and S3 through configurable rules, making it the foundation for connecting IoT devices to the AWS Cloud.\\nAWS IoT Greengrass is the edge component of the AWS IoT platform that extends cloud capabilities to devices for local processing and offline operation, and works alongside IoT Core rather than replacing it as the primary cloud-side service for managing device connectivity and message routing to AWS. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams is a real-time data streaming service for ingesting and processing high-throughput data, and while it can receive data routed from IoT Core, it is a data processing service rather than a device connectivity and authentication platform. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection between on-premises data centers and AWS, and is a network connectivity service for enterprise infrastructure rather than a platform for managing IoT device connections, authentication, and message routing at scale."},{"id":"q1074","question":"A software company wants to deliver its desktop application to customers through a web browser without requiring customers to download or install anything locally. The application should run on AWS infrastructure and stream its interface to the browser. Which AWS service should the company use?","options":{"A":"Amazon WorkSpaces","B":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web","C":"Amazon AppStream 2.0","D":"AWS Amplify"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon AppStream 2.0 is a fully managed application streaming service that runs desktop applications on AWS infrastructure and streams the application interface to users\' web browsers, allowing customers to use the software without downloading, installing, or managing it on their local devices.\\nAmazon WorkSpaces is a fully managed virtual desktop service that provides each user with a complete persistent cloud desktop environment, which is more infrastructure than required to deliver a single application through a browser and involves per-user desktop provisioning rather than application-level streaming. Amazon WorkSpaces Web is a low-cost managed service for providing browser-based access to internal websites and SaaS applications from unmanaged devices, and is designed for web content access rather than for streaming full desktop applications running on AWS compute. AWS Amplify is a development framework and hosting service for building and deploying full-stack web and mobile applications, and is a developer toolset rather than an application streaming or virtual desktop delivery service."},{"id":"q1075","question":"A company wants to replace its physical desktop computers with cloud-hosted virtual desktops that employees can access from any device anywhere, with each employee having their own persistent personalized desktop environment. Which AWS service provides this capability?","options":{"A":"Amazon AppStream 2.0","B":"Amazon WorkSpaces","C":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web","D":"Amazon EC2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon WorkSpaces is a fully managed, secure virtual desktop infrastructure service that provides each user with a persistent cloud desktop accessible from any supported device, allowing companies to replace physical desktop hardware while giving employees a consistent personalized computing environment that follows them wherever they work.\\nAmazon AppStream 2.0 streams specific desktop applications to a browser without providing a persistent full desktop environment; users cannot install their own applications or maintain personal desktop settings across sessions, making it suited for delivering specific software rather than replacing complete personal computers. Amazon WorkSpaces Web provides browser-based access to internal websites and SaaS applications from unmanaged devices, and is a restricted access solution for web content rather than a complete persistent virtual desktop that replaces a personal computer. Amazon EC2 can run Windows or Linux virtual machines that technically function as desktops, but customers must provision, patch, and manage the instances themselves and build their own desktop management infrastructure rather than using a fully managed desktop service."},{"id":"q1076","question":"A company wants to allow employees to use personal unmanaged devices to access internal web applications and approved SaaS tools securely, while ensuring that no corporate data can be downloaded or copied to those personal devices. Which AWS service is designed for this use case?","options":{"A":"Amazon WorkSpaces","B":"AWS Client VPN","C":"Amazon AppStream 2.0","D":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web is a low-cost, fully managed service that provides secure browser-based access to internal websites and approved SaaS applications from unmanaged personal devices, with built-in controls that prevent users from downloading, printing, or copying corporate data to their local devices.\\nAmazon WorkSpaces provides full persistent virtual desktops, which is significantly more capability and cost than required for the sole purpose of providing managed web access, and does not specifically target the use case of restricting data exfiltration from unmanaged personal devices accessing web applications. AWS Client VPN extends full encrypted network access to AWS resources and on-premises systems from user devices, giving the device direct network connectivity rather than providing a controlled browser environment that prevents data from reaching the local device. Amazon AppStream 2.0 streams full desktop applications to a browser and is suited for delivering installed software rather than providing managed, restricted access to internal websites and SaaS applications from unmanaged devices."},{"id":"q1077","question":"A frontend developer wants to build and deploy a full-stack web application on AWS that includes user authentication, a data API, and static hosting, without configuring or managing any underlying cloud infrastructure. Which AWS service provides an integrated development and deployment platform for this use case?","options":{"A":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","B":"AWS Amplify","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"AWS AppSync"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Amplify provides a complete set of tools and services for frontend web and mobile developers to build full-stack applications on AWS, offering client libraries for authentication, data APIs, and storage along with a managed continuous deployment hosting service, without requiring developers to configure or manage the underlying cloud infrastructure.\\nAWS Elastic Beanstalk is a platform as a service that handles deployment and capacity provisioning for backend web applications, but is focused on backend server hosting rather than providing the frontend development libraries, mobile SDKs, and integrated CI/CD hosting pipeline that Amplify delivers for building modern full-stack applications. Amazon Lightsail is a simplified cloud platform for deploying virtual servers, containers, and databases for straightforward workloads, and while it simplifies infrastructure management it does not provide the integrated development framework, authentication library, or automated deployment workflows that Amplify offers. AWS AppSync is a managed GraphQL API service that can serve as the data layer for applications, and while it integrates with Amplify, it is a standalone API service rather than the full-stack development and hosting platform that Amplify provides."},{"id":"q1082","question":"A company\'s e-commerce application needs to automatically send order confirmation emails, shipping notification emails, and password reset emails to customers at high volume directly from application code. Which AWS service is designed for this use case?","options":{"A":"Amazon SNS","B":"Amazon SES","C":"Amazon Connect","D":"Amazon Pinpoint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES) is a cloud-based email sending service designed for high-volume transactional and application-triggered email delivery, providing the infrastructure, deliverability management, and sending metrics needed to reliably deliver order confirmations, shipping notifications, and password reset messages to customers at scale.\\nAmazon SNS is a fully managed publish/subscribe messaging service primarily used for sending notifications to application endpoints, Lambda functions, SQS queues, and mobile devices, and while it can deliver email to subscribed addresses, it is not optimized for high-volume transactional email delivery with the deliverability management, bounce handling, and per-message statistics that SES provides. Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service for handling customer service interactions through voice and chat channels, and is a real-time agent communication platform rather than an automated email delivery service for application-triggered messages. Amazon Pinpoint is a customer engagement service for sending targeted messages across multiple channels including email, SMS, and push notifications, and while it supports email, it is oriented toward segmented marketing campaigns rather than high-volume transactional email delivery triggered by individual application events."},{"id":"q1083","question":"Which AWS service allows a company to send both application-triggered transactional emails such as order receipts and bulk marketing emails such as promotional newsletters through a single high-deliverability email platform?","options":{"A":"Amazon SNS","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"Amazon SES","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES) is a scalable, cost-effective email platform that supports both transactional email sending for automated application-triggered messages and bulk email sending for marketing campaigns and newsletters, providing deliverability tools, sending statistics, and reputation management through a unified service.\\nAmazon SNS is a publish/subscribe messaging service used to deliver notifications to multiple endpoints such as Lambda, SQS, and HTTP, and while it can send email to subscribed addresses as part of a notification workflow, it is not a purpose-built email platform with the bulk sending capabilities, unsubscribe management, and marketing email features that SES provides. Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service for managing voice and chat customer interactions, and is a customer service communication platform rather than an email sending service for either transactional or marketing purposes. Amazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service that extracts insights such as sentiment, entities, and key phrases from text data, and is an analytics service with no capability to send or deliver email messages."},{"id":"q1084","question":"A company receives thousands of scanned paper forms each day and needs to automatically extract text, tables, and specific field values from these documents without writing custom code or training machine learning models. Which AWS service should the company use?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Rekognition","C":"Amazon Transcribe","D":"Amazon Textract"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Textract is a machine learning service that automatically extracts printed text, handwriting, structured tables, and form field data from scanned documents and images, going beyond simple character recognition to understand document structure and the relationships between fields, without requiring custom code or model training.\\nAmazon Comprehend is a natural language processing service that analyzes already-digitised text to extract entities, sentiment, and key phrases, but does not extract text or structured data from scanned images or PDF documents; it processes text that has already been extracted rather than performing the extraction itself. Amazon Rekognition is a machine learning service for analyzing images and videos to detect objects, scenes, activities, and faces, and while it can detect text in images as a capability, it does not extract structured tables or form fields from scanned business documents the way Textract does. Amazon Transcribe is a speech-to-text service that converts audio and video recordings into written text transcripts, and is designed for processing spoken language in audio files rather than for extracting text and structured data from scanned document images."},{"id":"q1085","question":"A development team is building a collaborative mobile application where multiple users need to see real-time updates to shared data. The team wants to use GraphQL and have the backend automatically push data changes to all connected clients. Which AWS service provides managed GraphQL APIs with built-in real-time data synchronisation?","options":{"A":"Amazon API Gateway","B":"Amazon Kinesis","C":"AWS AppSync","D":"AWS Amplify"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS AppSync is a fully managed GraphQL service that simplifies building APIs for web and mobile applications, with built-in support for real-time data updates through GraphQL subscriptions that automatically push changes to all connected clients whenever underlying data is modified, making it ideal for collaborative and live-updating applications.\\nAmazon API Gateway is a fully managed service for creating RESTful, HTTP, and WebSocket APIs, and while WebSocket APIs support two-way communication, API Gateway does not natively provide GraphQL schema management, resolver configuration, or the subscription-based real-time data push model that AppSync delivers for building collaborative applications. Amazon Kinesis is a real-time data streaming service for ingesting and processing high-throughput data streams, and is designed for building data pipelines and analytics rather than for providing a GraphQL API layer with subscription-based real-time updates to application clients. AWS Amplify is a full-stack development framework that provides client-side libraries for connecting applications to AppSync APIs, but Amplify is the frontend tooling layer rather than the managed GraphQL service that hosts the API, manages data sources, and delivers real-time subscriptions to clients."},{"id":"q1106","question":"A company needs a fully managed file system that is compatible with the Windows Server Message Block (SMB) protocol for migrating Windows-based workloads to AWS. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"Amazon EFS","B":"Amazon FSx for Windows File Server","C":"Amazon S3","D":"AWS Storage Gateway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon FSx for Windows File Server provides a fully managed Windows-native file system built on Windows Server with full SMB protocol support, Active Directory integration, and NTFS, making it the purpose-built service for migrating Windows-based file workloads to AWS.\\nAmazon EFS is a fully managed NFS file system designed for Linux-based workloads and does not support the SMB protocol required by Windows applications. Amazon S3 is an object storage service accessed via HTTP APIs and is not a file system that supports SMB protocol access for Windows workloads. AWS Storage Gateway provides hybrid cloud storage that connects on-premises environments to AWS storage services, but it is a gateway appliance rather than a fully managed cloud-native file system."},{"id":"q1107","question":"Which AWS service provides fully managed third-party file systems optimized for workloads such as high-performance computing, machine learning, and Windows-based applications?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon FSx","D":"Amazon EFS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon FSx provides fully managed file systems built on popular third-party and open-source technologies including Lustre for high-performance computing, NetApp ONTAP for enterprise workloads, OpenZFS, and Windows File Server, each optimized for specific workload requirements.\\nAmazon EBS provides block-level storage volumes attached to individual EC2 instances and is a block storage service rather than a managed file system supporting multiple file system technologies. Amazon S3 is an object storage service designed for storing and retrieving any amount of data via HTTP APIs, and does not provide file system access through protocols like NFS, SMB, or Lustre. Amazon EFS is a managed NFS file system for Linux workloads and supports only the NFS protocol, unlike FSx which offers multiple file system types for different workload requirements."},{"id":"q1108","question":"A company wants to allow employees to securely access internal web applications from personal unmanaged devices without installing any software on those devices. Which AWS service meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"Amazon WorkSpaces","B":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web","C":"Amazon AppStream 2.0","D":"AWS Client VPN"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web provides a fully managed, browser-based service that allows users to securely access internal web applications and SaaS applications from any device with a web browser, without requiring software installation or a full virtual desktop environment.\\nAmazon WorkSpaces provides fully managed persistent virtual desktops in the cloud, which is a heavier solution than needed for simple web application access and requires a client application to be installed on the device. Amazon AppStream 2.0 streams desktop applications to users through a web browser, but it is designed for streaming full desktop applications rather than providing lightweight secure browser access to web applications. AWS Client VPN provides secure remote access to an AWS network from individual devices, but it requires VPN client software to be installed and configured on each device."},{"id":"q1109","question":"Which AWS service provides a fully managed browser-based solution for accessing web applications without provisioning a full virtual desktop?","options":{"A":"Amazon AppStream 2.0","B":"Amazon WorkSpaces","C":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web is a low-cost, fully managed browser-based service specifically designed for securely accessing web applications and websites without the overhead of provisioning and managing full virtual desktop infrastructure.\\nAmazon AppStream 2.0 is an application streaming service that delivers desktop applications to users through a web browser, and is designed for streaming resource-intensive applications rather than providing lightweight web-only browser access. Amazon WorkSpaces provisions full persistent virtual desktops with a complete operating system environment, which is more than what is needed for web-only application access and carries higher cost and management overhead. AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and is a networking service with no capability to provide browser-based application access."},{"id":"q1110","question":"A company needs to track and manage its Microsoft and Oracle software licenses as it migrates workloads to AWS. Which AWS service helps manage software license compliance?","options":{"A":"AWS Systems Manager","B":"AWS License Manager","C":"AWS Config","D":"AWS Service Catalog"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS License Manager helps customers manage software license entitlements from vendors such as Microsoft, Oracle, and SAP across AWS and on-premises environments, providing centralized tracking and enforcement of licensing rules to prevent compliance violations.\\nAWS Systems Manager provides operational tools for managing EC2 instances and on-premises servers including patching, automation, and parameter storage, but does not track or enforce software licensing rules. AWS Config records and evaluates the configuration of AWS resources against compliance rules, but it monitors resource configurations rather than tracking software license entitlements and usage. AWS Service Catalog allows organizations to create and manage approved catalogs of IT services for deployment on AWS, but it governs which products can be deployed rather than tracking software license compliance."},{"id":"q1111","question":"Which AWS service provides a centralized way to manage software license entitlements and set licensing rules to prevent exceeding license limits?","options":{"A":"AWS Marketplace","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"AWS License Manager","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS License Manager enables customers to define licensing rules based on their agreements with software vendors, track license usage across AWS and on-premises environments, and enforce limits to prevent exceeding entitlements, reducing the risk of non-compliance and unexpected licensing costs.\\nAWS Marketplace is a digital catalog for finding, buying, and deploying third-party software that runs on AWS, and while it handles procurement of new licenses, it does not manage existing license entitlements or enforce usage limits across environments. AWS Organizations is an account management service for centrally governing multiple AWS accounts through policies and consolidated billing, and does not provide software license tracking or enforcement. AWS Trusted Advisor provides automated recommendations for improving security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance of an AWS environment, but does not track or manage software licensing compliance."},{"id":"q1112","question":"A company runs containerised applications on Amazon ECS and needs a fully managed registry to store, manage, and deploy Docker container images. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"AWS CodeCommit","C":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR)","D":"AWS CodeArtifact"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry is a fully managed Docker container registry that integrates natively with Amazon ECS and Amazon EKS, providing secure, scalable storage for container images with built-in vulnerability scanning and lifecycle policies.\\nAmazon S3 is an object storage service that can store any type of file, but it is not a container registry and does not provide the Docker registry API, image layer management, or native container service integration that ECR offers. AWS CodeCommit is a managed Git repository service for storing and versioning source code, not container images, and does not provide Docker registry functionality. AWS CodeArtifact is a managed artifact repository for storing and sharing software packages such as npm, Maven, and Python packages, but it does not support Docker container images."},{"id":"q1113","question":"A company wants to set up a low-cost disaster recovery solution that can automatically recover on-premises servers on AWS in the event of a site failure. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"AWS Backup","B":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","C":"Amazon S3 Cross-Region Replication","D":"AWS DataSync"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery continuously replicates source servers to AWS using lightweight agents, maintaining a low-cost staging area, and can quickly launch fully provisioned recovery instances in minutes when a disaster occurs, providing an automated and cost-effective disaster recovery solution.\\nAWS Backup is a centralized backup service that automates and manages backups across AWS services, but it creates point-in-time backups for data recovery rather than providing continuous replication and rapid server recovery for disaster recovery scenarios. Amazon S3 Cross-Region Replication automatically copies S3 objects to a bucket in a different Region for data durability, but it only replicates S3 data and cannot recover full server environments including operating systems and applications. AWS DataSync automates data transfer between on-premises storage and AWS storage services, but it is a data movement tool rather than a disaster recovery service that can launch recovery instances."},{"id":"q1114","question":"A company with multiple AWS accounts within an organization wants to share VPC subnets and AWS Transit Gateways across accounts without duplicating resources. Which AWS service enables this?","options":{"A":"AWS Organizations","B":"VPC Peering","C":"AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)","D":"AWS Control Tower"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Resource Access Manager enables customers to securely share AWS resources such as VPC subnets, Transit Gateways, and Route 53 Resolver rules across AWS accounts within an organization, eliminating the need to create duplicate resources in each account.\\nAWS Organizations provides centralized management and governance of multiple AWS accounts through service control policies and consolidated billing, but it does not itself provide the mechanism to share specific resources like subnets or Transit Gateways between accounts. VPC Peering creates a networking connection between two VPCs that allows traffic to flow between them, but it is a point-to-point connection and does not share resources like subnets or Transit Gateways across accounts. AWS Control Tower automates the setup and governance of a secure multi-account AWS environment based on best practices, but it is an account governance service that does not provide resource sharing capabilities."},{"id":"q1141","question":"A company wants to provide employees with a generative AI-powered assistant that can answer questions about company data, generate content, and automate tasks using enterprise knowledge sources. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker AI","B":"Amazon Q","C":"Amazon Lex","D":"Amazon Kendra"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Q is a generative AI-powered assistant designed for businesses that can be customized with enterprise data to answer questions, generate content, take actions, and automate workflows, providing a ready-to-use AI assistant without requiring machine learning expertise.\\nAmazon SageMaker AI is a fully managed platform for building, training, and deploying custom machine learning models, which requires data science expertise and is designed for custom ML development rather than a ready-to-use conversational AI assistant. Amazon Lex is a service for building conversational chatbots using voice and text interfaces, but it requires developers to define intents and conversation flows rather than providing a pre-built generative AI assistant that works with enterprise knowledge. Amazon Kendra is an intelligent enterprise search service that indexes documents and returns relevant answers to search queries, but it is a search engine rather than a generative AI assistant that can create content and automate tasks."},{"id":"q006","question":"You have set up consolidated billing for several AWS accounts. One of the accounts has purchased a number of reserved instances for 3 years. Which of the following is true regarding this scenario?","options":{"A":"The Reserved Instance discounts can only be shared with the master account","B":"All accounts can receive the hourly cost benefit of the Reserved Instances","C":"The purchased instances will have better performance than On-demand instances","D":"There are no cost benefits from using consolidated billing; It is for informational purposes only"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With consolidated billing, Reserved Instance discounts are automatically shared across all accounts in the organization, so any account can benefit from the hourly cost savings.\\nThe Reserved Instance discounts applying only to the master account is incorrect because consolidated billing specifically shares RI benefits across all member accounts in the organization, not just the account that purchased them. The claim that reserved instances have better performance than On-Demand instances is false because both run on the same underlying AWS infrastructure, and the Reserved Instance pricing model is a billing commitment rather than a different hardware tier. Consolidated billing does provide real cost benefits beyond purely organizational purposes, combining usage for volume discounts and sharing Reserved Instance savings across accounts, so the claim that it is for informational purposes only is incorrect."},{"id":"q009","question":"A company has an AWS Enterprise Support plan. They want quick and efficient guidance with their billing and account inquiries. Which of the following should the company use?","options":{"A":"AWS Health Dashboard","B":"AWS Support Concierge","C":"AWS Customer Service","D":"AWS Business Support"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The AWS Support Concierge, available exclusively on the Enterprise Support plan, is a dedicated team for billing and account inquiries, providing fast and personalized assistance.\\nAWS Health Dashboard provides real-time and personalized alerts about AWS service events that may affect your resources, and is a service health monitoring tool rather than a billing or account support contact. AWS Customer Service handles general account and billing enquiries for all customers but does not provide the dedicated, personalized support that the Concierge team offers to Enterprise plan holders. AWS Business Support provides 24/7 access to Cloud Support Engineers for technical issues and includes Infrastructure Event Management for an additional fee, but does not include a dedicated Concierge team for billing and account guidance."},{"id":"q016","question":"Which of the following helps a customer view the Amazon EC2 billing activity for the past month?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"AWS Cost & Usage Reports"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Cost and Usage Reports provide the most detailed and comprehensive breakdown of actual AWS spending, including EC2 billing activity, by time period and resource.\\nAWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost governance and alerting tool rather than a service for reviewing historical billing activity. The AWS Pricing Calculator estimates future costs for AWS services before provisioning them, and does not provide reports on historical billing activity. AWS Systems Manager provides operational tools for managing and automating tasks across AWS infrastructure, and has no connection to billing reporting or cost analysis."},{"id":"q017","question":"What do you gain from setting up consolidated billing for five different AWS accounts under another master account?","options":{"A":"AWS services\' costs will be reduced to half the original price","B":"The consolidated billing feature is just for organizational purpose","C":"Each AWS account gets volume discounts","D":"Each AWS account gets five times the free-tier services capacity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Consolidated billing aggregates usage across all accounts, which can push the combined usage into higher volume tiers and unlock volume discounts that individual accounts would not reach on their own.\\nAWS services costs are not reduced to half the original price under consolidated billing. Volume discounts apply incrementally based on combined usage tiers and vary by service rather than applying a fixed 50% reduction. Consolidated billing provides genuine cost benefits beyond purely organizational purposes through volume discounts and Reserved Instance sharing, so the claim that it is purely organizational is incorrect. Each AWS account does not receive five times the free-tier services capacity under consolidated billing. The free tier applies per organization rather than per account, and consolidated billing does not multiply free-tier allowances."},{"id":"q020","question":"A startup company is operating on limited funds and is extremely concerned about cost overruns. Which of the below options can be used to notify the company when their monthly AWS bill exceeds $2000?","options":{"A":"Set up a CloudWatch billing alarm that triggers an SNS notification when the threshold is exceeded","B":"Configure Amazon Simple Email Service to send billing alerts to their email address on a daily basis","C":"Configure AWS Budgets to alert the company when the threshold is exceeded","D":"Configure AWS CloudTrail to automatically delete all AWS resources when the threshold is exceeded","E":"Configure Amazon Connect to alert the company when the threshold is exceeded"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"A CloudWatch billing alarm triggers an SNS notification when costs exceed a set threshold, and AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost thresholds with automatic alerts. Both are native AWS tools for cost monitoring.\\nAmazon Simple Email Service is an email sending service for transactional and marketing communications, and while it can send emails it does not natively monitor AWS billing or trigger alerts based on cost thresholds. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity for auditing purposes, and has no capability to monitor billing thresholds or delete resources in response to cost events. Amazon Connect is a cloud contact center service for managing customer communications via voice and chat, and has no connection to AWS billing monitoring or cost threshold alerting."},{"id":"q028","question":"You want to run a questionnaire application for only one day (without interruption), which Amazon EC2 purchase option should you use?","options":{"A":"Reserved instances","B":"Spot instances","C":"Dedicated instances","D":"On-demand instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"On-Demand instances let you run workloads for any duration without long-term commitments or interruptions, making them ideal for short, one-time tasks where continuous availability is required.\\nReserved Instances require a minimum one-year commitment and are cost-effective for steady-state workloads running continuously over that period, making them a poor fit for a single-day task where the commitment term would far exceed the actual usage. Spot Instances use spare AWS capacity at a significant discount but can be interrupted by AWS at any time, making them unsuitable for a workload that must run for a full day without interruption. Dedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single customer for compliance or licensing purposes, and while they provide isolation they are more expensive than standard On-Demand Instances and do not offer any advantage for a simple one-day task."},{"id":"q029","question":"You are working on a project that involves creating thumbnails of millions of images. Consistent uptime is not an issue, and continuous processing is not required. Which EC2 buying option would be the most cost-effective?","options":{"A":"Reserved Instances","B":"On-demand Instances","C":"Dedicated Instances","D":"Spot Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spot Instances use spare AWS capacity at steep discounts and are perfect for fault-tolerant batch jobs like image processing, where interruptions are acceptable and uptime is not critical.\\nReserved Instances provide a significant discount over On-Demand pricing for steady-state workloads through a one or three-year commitment, but are not cost-effective for batch workloads that run only periodically and can tolerate interruptions. On-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no discount, making them significantly more expensive than Spot Instances for a batch image processing workload where the timing is flexible. Dedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single customer and are designed for compliance and licensing isolation requirements, not for cost optimization of batch processing workloads."},{"id":"q034","question":"As part of the Enterprise support plan, who is the primary point of contact for ongoing support needs?","options":{"A":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) user","B":"Infrastructure Event Management (IEM) engineer","C":"AWS Consulting Partners","D":"AWS Technical Account Manager (TAM)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The AWS Technical Account Manager is included in the Enterprise Support plan as the primary dedicated contact who provides proactive guidance and coordinates AWS support across your account.\\nAn AWS IAM user is an identity within an AWS account used to authenticate and authorise access to AWS services, and is not a support contact or a role within the AWS Support organization. An Infrastructure Event Management engineer provides architectural guidance and real-time support during specific planned events under the Enterprise Support plan, but is engaged for specific events rather than serving as the ongoing primary support contact. AWS Consulting Partners are professional services firms in the AWS Partner Network that help customers design and build on AWS, and are external organizations rather than AWS Support personnel assigned to an account."},{"id":"q035","question":"How can you view the distribution of AWS spending in one of your AWS accounts?","options":{"A":"By using Amazon VPC console","B":"By contacting the AWS Support team","C":"By using AWS Cost Explorer","D":"By contacting the AWS Finance team"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Cost Explorer provides interactive charts and reports that visualise your AWS spending patterns over time, broken down by service, account, or tag.\\nThe Amazon VPC console is a networking tool for managing virtual private clouds, subnets, gateways, and network configuration, and has no connection to billing information or spending distribution. Contacting the AWS Support team provides technical guidance and issue resolution, and is not a self-service mechanism for viewing the distribution of AWS spending across your account. Contacting the AWS Finance team is not a supported customer-facing channel for accessing billing and spending information, which is available directly through the AWS Management Console via Cost Explorer and billing dashboards."},{"id":"q049","question":"What does AWS provide to deploy popular technologies such as IBM MQ on AWS with the least amount of effort and time?","options":{"A":"Amazon Aurora","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS Quick Start reference deployments","D":"AWS OpsWorks"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Quick Start reference deployments provide automated gold-standard templates that deploy popular IT solutions on AWS in minutes, giving administrators a fast path to production-ready environments built on AWS best practices.\\nAmazon Aurora is a fully managed relational database compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL, and is a database service with no capability to deploy or provision third-party technologies or solutions. Amazon CloudWatch collects metrics, logs, and event data from AWS services and resources to provide operational monitoring and alerting, and is a monitoring service with no deployment or provisioning capability. AWS OpsWorks (deprecated) was a configuration management service that used Chef and Puppet to automate server configuration, and while it could automate some infrastructure tasks it is not a pre-built deployment resource for rapidly deploying popular third-party technologies with minimal effort. Note: AWS OpsWorks was deprecated in 2024."},{"id":"q050","question":"An organization has decided to purchase an Amazon EC2 Reserved Instance (RI) for three years in order to reduce costs. It is possible that the application workloads could change during the reservation period. What is the EC2 Reserved Instance (RI) type that will allow the company to exchange the purchased reserved instance for another reserved instance with higher computing power if they need to?","options":{"A":"Scheduled Reserved Instance","B":"Standard Reserved Instance","C":"On-Demand Instance","D":"Convertible Reserved Instance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Convertible Reserved Instances allow you to exchange your reservation for another Convertible RI with different attributes such as instance type, operating system, or tenancy during the term, providing flexibility when workload requirements change.\\nScheduled Reserved Instances were a former RI type that allowed you to reserve capacity for specific recurring time windows, and did not support attribute exchanges during the term. Note: Scheduled Reserved Instances have been deprecated by AWS. Standard Reserved Instances offer the highest discount but are fixed to a specific instance family and cannot be exchanged for a different configuration during the reservation period. On-Demand Instances are a separate EC2 purchasing model that charges for compute on an hourly or per-second basis with no commitment, and are not a Reserved Instance type at all."},{"id":"q052","question":"Which of the following Reserved Instance (RI) pricing models provides the highest average savings compared to On-Demand pricing?","options":{"A":"One-year, No Upfront, Standard RI pricing","B":"One-year, All Upfront, Convertible RI pricing","C":"Three-year, All Upfront, Standard RI pricing","D":"Three-year, No Upfront, Convertible RI pricing"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Three-year All Upfront Standard RIs combine the longest commitment term with full prepayment and the Standard type, which together yield the deepest discount compared to On-Demand pricing.\\nA one-year No Upfront Standard RI offers the lowest upfront cost but provides the smallest discount among the options listed, as the short term and absence of prepayment both reduce the overall savings. A one-year All Upfront Convertible RI benefits from full prepayment but is limited by the shorter term and the Convertible type, which offers less discount than Standard RIs in exchange for flexibility to exchange attributes. A three-year No Upfront Convertible RI benefits from the long commitment term but sacrifices discount depth through both the absence of prepayment and the Convertible type designation, making it less cost-effective than the All Upfront Standard equivalent."},{"id":"q059","question":"Which of the following BEST describe the AWS pricing model?","options":{"A":"Fixed-term","B":"Pay-as-you-go","C":"Colocation","D":"Planned","E":"Variable cost"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"AWS pricing is pay-as-you-go and variable cost. There are no fixed terms required, no colocation fees, and no mandatory upfront plans.\\nFixed-term pricing requires a commitment to a set contract duration, which is the opposite of the AWS pay-as-you-go model where usage can scale up or down at any time without contractual obligations. Colocation refers to housing customer-owned servers in a third-party data center, which is an on-premises model rather than a cloud pricing principle. Planned pricing implies predetermined usage schedules and costs, which contradicts the AWS model where customers pay based on actual consumption without mandatory advance planning."},{"id":"q061","question":"A customer is using multiple AWS accounts with separate billing. How can the customer take advantage of volume discounts with minimal impact to the AWS resources?","options":{"A":"Create one global AWS account and move all AWS resources to that account","B":"Sign up for three years of Reserved Instance pricing up front","C":"Use the consolidated billing feature from AWS Organizations","D":"Sign up for the AWS Enterprise support plan to get volume discounts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Organizations consolidated billing aggregates usage across all member accounts, qualifying the organization for volume pricing tiers without requiring resource consolidation or upfront RI commitments.\\nCreating one global account and moving all resources is a high-impact change that would eliminate account-level isolation, security boundaries, and governance, and it is unnecessary because consolidated billing earns the same volume discounts without migration. Reserved Instance pricing locks compute into a specific instance type and term, which lowers cost for predictable workloads but does not provide cross-account volume discounts. The Enterprise Support plan adds Technical Account Managers and premium support, but support tiers do not grant volume discounts on service usage."},{"id":"q062","question":"Which Amazon EC2 pricing model offers the MOST significant discount when compared to On-Demand instances?","options":{"A":"Partial Upfront Reserved Instances for a 1-year term","B":"All Upfront Reserved Instances for a 1-year term","C":"All Upfront Reserved Instances for a 3-year term","D":"No Upfront Reserved Instances for a 3-year term"},"correct":"C","explanation":"All Upfront Reserved Instances for a 3-year term provide the maximum possible EC2 discount because you combine the longest commitment term with full prepayment, which together yield the deepest savings compared to On-Demand pricing.\\nPartial Upfront Reserved Instances for a 1-year term offer a moderate discount, but the shorter term and partial prepayment both reduce the total savings compared to the 3-year All Upfront option. All Upfront Reserved Instances for a 1-year term offer a good discount through full prepayment but are limited by the shorter commitment term, which yields less total savings than a 3-year commitment. No Upfront Reserved Instances for a 3-year term benefit from the long commitment term but forgo the additional discount that comes with upfront payment, making them less cost-effective than All Upfront options."},{"id":"q066","question":"Which benefits are included with the AWS Business Support plan?","options":{"A":"24/7 assistance by way of live chat or a telephone call","B":"Support from a dedicated AWS Technical Account Manager","C":"An unlimited number of cases and contacts","D":"15-minute response time for production system interruption cases","E":"Annual operational reviews with AWS Solutions Architects"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS Business Support includes 24/7 access to Cloud Support Engineers via phone, email, and chat, plus an unlimited number of support cases and contacts.\\nSupport from a dedicated Technical Account Manager who provides proactive guidance and acts as a primary contact is exclusively available with the AWS Enterprise Support plan and is not included in Business Support. A 15-minute response time for production system interruption cases is an Enterprise Support feature. Business Support provides a one-hour response SLA for production system-down cases, not 15 minutes. Annual operational reviews with AWS Solutions Architects are an Enterprise Support benefit and are not included in the Business Support plan."},{"id":"q068","question":"A user must meet compliance and software licensing requirements that state a workload must be hosted on a physical server. Which Amazon EC2 instance pricing option will meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Dedicated Hosts","B":"Dedicated Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Hosts provide a physical server dedicated entirely to your use with full visibility into sockets, cores, and host ID, which is required for server-bound software licensing such as BYOL and for certain compliance frameworks that mandate physical isolation.\\nDedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single customer but do not provide visibility into the specific physical host, which is required for most per-socket or per-core licensing models and does not satisfy all compliance requirements for physical server isolation. Spot Instances use spare AWS capacity at a significant discount but can be interrupted by AWS at any time, making them unsuitable for workloads with compliance requirements that mandate consistent physical server hosting. Reserved Instances are a pricing commitment for EC2 capacity that can reduce costs over a 1 or 3-year term, but they run on shared infrastructure and do not provide physical server isolation or host-level visibility required for BYOL licensing."},{"id":"q069","question":"Which of the Reserved Instance (RI) pricing models can change the attributes of the RI as long as the exchange results in the creation of RIs of equal or greater value?","options":{"A":"Dedicated RIs","B":"Scheduled RIs","C":"Convertible RIs","D":"Standard RIs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Convertible RIs allow you to exchange them for other Convertible RIs of equal or greater value, letting you change instance family, OS, tenancy, or payment option over the term. Standard RIs cannot be exchanged.\\nDedicated RIs are a tenancy attribute rather than a separate RI type, allowing the instance to run on hardware dedicated to a single customer, and this designation does not affect whether the RI can be exchanged. Scheduled RIs were a former RI type that allowed reservation of capacity for specific recurring time windows, and did not support attribute exchanges during the term. Note: Scheduled RIs have been deprecated by AWS. Standard RIs provide the highest discount among RI types but are locked to a specific instance family, size, operating system, and tenancy, and cannot be exchanged for a different configuration once purchased."},{"id":"q071","question":"When should a company consider using Amazon EC2 Spot Instances?","options":{"A":"For non-production applications","B":"For stateful workloads","C":"For applications that cannot have interruptions","D":"For fault-tolerant flexible applications","E":"For sensitive database applications"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Spot Instances are best for non-production applications that can tolerate interruptions and for fault-tolerant, flexible workloads that can checkpoint and resume. Stateful, high-uptime, or sensitive database workloads should not use Spot.\\nStateful workloads that maintain persistent connections or application state are not suitable for Spot Instances because an interruption would disrupt that state, requiring complex recovery mechanisms. Applications that cannot have interruptions require guaranteed availability that Spot Instances cannot provide, as AWS may reclaim Spot capacity at any time with a two-minute warning. Sensitive database applications require consistent availability, data integrity, and predictable performance, making them poorly suited to the interruption risk inherent in Spot Instance pricing."},{"id":"q074","question":"Which is the minimum AWS Support plan that includes Infrastructure Event Management without additional costs?","options":{"A":"Enterprise","B":"Business","C":"Developer","D":"Basic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Infrastructure Event Management is included at no extra cost only with the AWS Enterprise Support plan, providing architectural guidance and real-time support during planned events such as product launches and traffic spikes.\\nBusiness Support customers can access Infrastructure Event Management but must pay an additional fee for each event engagement, making it not included as a standard benefit at no extra cost. Developer Support provides business-hours email access to Cloud Support Engineers for general technical questions and does not include Infrastructure Event Management at any price point. Basic Support provides access to documentation, whitepapers, and the seven core Trusted Advisor checks, and does not include any form of event management support."},{"id":"q080","question":"A user wants guidance on possible savings when migrating from on-premises to AWS. Which tool is suitable for this scenario?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Pricing Calculator","D":"AWS Well-Architected Tool"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator allows users to model AWS service configurations and estimate costs, enabling comparison between on-premises infrastructure costs and projected AWS spending to understand potential savings from migration.\\nAWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost monitoring tool for active AWS workloads rather than a pre-migration savings estimation tool. AWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of existing AWS spending and usage patterns, and requires active AWS usage to generate data rather than providing pre-migration cost comparisons. The AWS Well-Architected Tool helps review workloads against the six pillars of the Well-Architected Framework to identify architectural risks and improvements, and is not a cost comparison or savings estimation tool."},{"id":"q082","question":"The use of what AWS feature or service allows companies to track and categorize spending on a detailed level?","options":{"A":"Cost allocation tags","B":"Consolidated billing","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Marketplace"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cost allocation tags are key-value labels attached to AWS resources that enable you to organize and track spending at a granular level by project, team, or environment in Cost Explorer and billing reports.\\nConsolidated billing combines usage from all member accounts in an AWS Organization to produce a single invoice and qualify for volume discounts, but does not itself provide the granular resource-level categorization that tags enable for detailed cost tracking. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when exceeded, and is a cost governance and alerting tool rather than a mechanism for categorizing and tracking spending at a detailed resource level. AWS Marketplace is a digital catalog for discovering and purchasing third-party software solutions that run on AWS, and has no connection to cost categorization or spending analysis."},{"id":"q083","question":"Which of the following inspects AWS environments to find opportunities that can save money for users and also improve system performance?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"Consolidated billing","D":"Detailed billing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor scans your AWS environment and provides recommendations across cost optimization, performance, security, fault tolerance, and service limits, helping save money and improve architecture.\\nAWS Cost Explorer is a tool for visualising and analyzing historical and forecasted AWS spending patterns, but it does not inspect environments for performance improvement opportunities or architectural recommendations. Consolidated billing is a feature of AWS Organizations that combines charges from multiple accounts into a single bill for simplified payment, and has no environment inspection or recommendation capability. Detailed billing provides granular line-item reports of AWS usage and charges for cost analysis purposes, and does not identify opportunities to save money or improve system performance."},{"id":"q092","question":"Which AWS support plan includes a dedicated Technical Account Manager?","options":{"A":"Developer","B":"Enterprise","C":"Business","D":"Basic"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A dedicated Technical Account Manager who provides proactive guidance and acts as a primary contact is exclusively available with the AWS Enterprise Support plan.\\nDeveloper Support provides business-hours email access to Cloud Support Engineers for general technical questions, and does not include a dedicated Technical Account Manager or any dedicated account management resource. Business Support provides 24/7 access to Cloud Support Engineers via phone, email, and chat plus full Trusted Advisor checks, and while it offers substantial support resources it does not include a dedicated Technical Account Manager. Basic Support provides access to documentation, whitepapers, and the seven core Trusted Advisor checks, and includes no dedicated support personnel or account management."},{"id":"q097","question":"A customer would like to design and build a new workload on AWS Cloud but does not have the AWS-related software technical expertise in-house. Which of the following AWS programs can a customer take advantage of to achieve that outcome?","options":{"A":"AWS Partner Network Technology Partners","B":"AWS Marketplace","C":"AWS Partner Network Consulting Partners","D":"AWS Service Catalog"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Partner Network Consulting Partners are professional services firms that help customers design, build, migrate, and manage AWS workloads. Technology Partners provide software and tools; the Marketplace is for purchasing software.\\nAWS Partner Network Technology Partners develop software, tools, and services that complement AWS, and are product companies rather than professional services firms that help customers directly design and build workloads from scratch. AWS Marketplace is a digital catalog where customers can discover and purchase third-party software solutions, and is a procurement platform rather than a professional services resource for technical expertise and hands-on workload development. AWS Service Catalog allows organizations to create and manage approved catalogs of IT services for internal use and self-service provisioning, and is an internal governance tool rather than a professional services program."},{"id":"q100","question":"Which service should a customer use to consolidate and centrally manage multiple AWS accounts?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"AWS Schema Conversion Tool","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Organizations allows you to create a hierarchy of AWS accounts, consolidate billing across accounts, and apply governance policies through Service Control Policies centrally from a management account.\\nAWS IAM manages users, roles, and permissions within a single account and cannot manage or consolidate multiple accounts. AWS Schema Conversion Tool is a database migration tool for converting database schemas between different engines, unrelated to account management. AWS Config tracks and records configuration changes to resources within an account, but does not provide multi-account consolidation or governance."},{"id":"q101","question":"How can a company reduce its Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) using AWS?","options":{"A":"By minimizing large capital expenditures","B":"By having no responsibility for third-party license costs","C":"By having no operational expenditures","D":"By having AWS manage applications"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS shifts infrastructure spending from large upfront capital expenditures to smaller, ongoing operational expenses, directly reducing TCO by eliminating the need to over-provision for peak demand.\\nHaving no responsibility for third-party license costs is not a general AWS benefit, as customers still manage and pay for their own software licenses when running applications on AWS, unless they use AWS-provided licenses through services such as Amazon RDS. AWS does not eliminate operational expenditures. Customers continue to pay ongoing usage-based costs for the AWS services they consume, which are operational expenses rather than capital expenses. AWS does not manage customer applications by default. Application-level management including code, configuration, and updates remains the customer\'s responsibility under the shared responsibility model."},{"id":"q102","question":"Which options does AWS make available for customers who want to learn about security in the cloud in an instructor-led setting?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Online Tech Talks","C":"AWS Blog","D":"AWS Forums","E":"AWS Classroom Training"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"AWS Online Tech Talks are live and on-demand webinars that include interactive Q&A sessions, and AWS Classroom Training offers instructor-led courses delivered by certified trainers, both providing structured learning in an instructor-led or live setting.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor is an automated tool that provides real-time recommendations across cost, security, performance, and fault tolerance, not a learning or training resource. AWS Blog is a self-service publishing channel for news and technical content that customers read independently, not an instructor-led format. AWS Forums is a community-driven discussion platform where customers help each other with questions, not a structured instructor-led learning environment."},{"id":"q106","question":"Which of the following are advantages of AWS consolidated billing?","options":{"A":"The ability to receive one bill for multiple accounts","B":"Service limits increasing by default in all accounts","C":"A fixed discount on the monthly bill","D":"Potential volume discounts, as usage in all accounts is combined","E":"The automatic extension of the master account\'s AWS support plan to all accounts"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Consolidated billing produces a single combined invoice for all accounts in an AWS Organization, and it pools usage across all accounts to qualify for volume pricing tiers.\\nService limits do not automatically increase for all accounts under consolidated billing. Service limit increases must be requested individually through AWS Support on a per-service and per-region basis. Consolidated billing does not provide a fixed discount on the monthly bill. Volume discounts are applied progressively based on combined usage tiers and vary by service rather than applying a uniform percentage reduction. The master account\'s AWS Support plan is not automatically extended to all member accounts. Each account within an AWS Organization must maintain its own support plan, and support plan costs and benefits remain account-specific."},{"id":"q110","question":"Which is the MINIMUM AWS Support plan that allows for one-hour target response time for support cases?","options":{"A":"Enterprise","B":"Business","C":"Developer","D":"Basic"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Business Support guarantees a one-hour response time for production system-down cases and provides 24/7 phone, email, and chat access. Developer Support offers business-hours email only; Basic has no case support; Enterprise offers faster response but at higher cost.\\nEnterprise Support provides faster response times including a 15-minute SLA for business-critical system failures, and also meets the one-hour production system-down SLA, but it is a higher-cost plan than Business Support and therefore not the minimum required. Developer Support provides business-hours email access to Cloud Support Engineers with response times measured in hours rather than one hour, and does not include 24/7 access or a one-hour production system-down SLA. Basic Support provides access to documentation and the seven core Trusted Advisor checks but includes no technical case support and no defined response time SLAs."},{"id":"q112","question":"What AWS team assists customers with accelerating cloud adoption through paid engagements in any of several specialty practice areas?","options":{"A":"AWS Enterprise Support","B":"AWS Solutions Architects","C":"AWS Professional Services","D":"AWS Account Managers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Professional Services is a global team of experts that helps customers achieve their desired business outcomes on AWS through paid engagements across specialty practice areas including migration, security, and data analytics.\\nAWS Enterprise Support is a paid support plan that provides access to a Technical Account Manager and 24/7 access to senior cloud support engineers, but it is a support tier rather than a consulting engagement team that works on specialty practice projects. AWS Solutions Architects advise customers on designing well-architected solutions and are available through various AWS programs, but they provide architectural guidance rather than leading paid consulting engagements across specialty practice areas. AWS Account Managers manage the overall commercial relationship between AWS and a customer, but they handle account and sales activities rather than delivering hands-on technical consulting engagements."},{"id":"q113","question":"A company needs 24/7 phone, email, and chat access with a response time of less than 1 hour if a production system has a service interruption. Which AWS Support plan meets these requirements at the LOWEST cost?","options":{"A":"Basic","B":"Developer","C":"Business","D":"Enterprise"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Business Support is the lowest-cost plan that provides 24/7 phone, email, and chat access to Cloud Support Engineers with a one-hour response SLA for production system interruptions.\\nBasic Support provides access to documentation, whitepapers, and the seven core Trusted Advisor checks, but includes no technical case support or access to Cloud Support Engineers. Developer Support provides email access to Cloud Support Engineers during business hours only, and does not include 24/7 phone or chat access or a one-hour production system response SLA. Enterprise Support also provides 24/7 access with faster response times including a 15-minute SLA for business-critical system failures, but is a higher-cost plan than Business Support and therefore not the minimum required to meet these specific requirements."},{"id":"q115","question":"How does AWS Trusted Advisor provide guidance to users of the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"It identifies software vulnerabilities in applications running on AWS","B":"It provides a list of cost optimization recommendations based on current AWS usage","C":"It detects potential security vulnerabilities caused by permissions settings on account resources","D":"It automatically corrects potential security issues caused by permissions settings on account resources","E":"It provides proactive alerting whenever an Amazon EC2 instance has been compromised"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Trusted Advisor analyzes your AWS environment and provides cost optimization recommendations based on current usage patterns, and it flags potential security issues caused by overly permissive IAM or resource settings. It does not automatically remediate issues or alert on compromised instances.\\nIdentifying software vulnerabilities in applications running on AWS is the function of Amazon Inspector, which scans EC2 instances and container images for CVEs and unintended network exposure, rather than Trusted Advisor which checks against AWS best practices. Automatically correcting potential security issues is not a function of Trusted Advisor, which provides advisory recommendations and flags concerns but does not take automated remediation actions on your resources. Providing proactive alerting when an EC2 instance has been compromised is the function of Amazon GuardDuty, which uses machine learning and threat intelligence to detect suspicious behavior, rather than Trusted Advisor which performs periodic checks against predefined best practice criteria."},{"id":"q123","question":"Which of the following is an advantage of consolidated billing on AWS?","options":{"A":"Volume pricing qualification","B":"Shared access permissions","C":"Multiple bills per account","D":"Eliminates the need for tagging"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Consolidated billing combines usage from all member accounts in an AWS Organization, qualifying the organization for volume pricing tiers that individual accounts might not reach on their own.\\nShared access permissions are not a benefit of consolidated billing. Access control is managed through IAM policies, Service Control Policies, and resource-based policies, none of which are affected by enabling consolidated billing. Consolidated billing produces a single invoice for all accounts rather than multiple bills per account, so the option describing multiple bills per account is the opposite of what consolidated billing provides. Consolidated billing does not eliminate the need for tagging. Cost allocation tags remain essential for attributing costs to specific projects, teams, or environments even when usage is aggregated under a single consolidated bill."},{"id":"q125","question":"Which of the following Amazon EC2 pricing models allow customers to use existing server-bound software licenses?","options":{"A":"Spot Instances","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"Dedicated Hosts","D":"On-Demand Instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Hosts provide a physical server dedicated to your use, which is required to use existing per-socket, per-core, or per-VM software licenses. Spot, Reserved, and On-Demand all run on shared or unspecified hardware and do not support license binding to specific physical hosts.\\nSpot Instances use spare AWS capacity and run on shared infrastructure without any visibility into the underlying physical host, making them entirely unsuitable for licensing models that require binding to a specific physical server. Reserved Instances are a pricing commitment for EC2 capacity that reduces costs over a 1 or 3-year term, but they run on standard shared infrastructure and do not provide the host-level visibility required for most BYOL licensing scenarios. On-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no long-term commitment, and like Reserved Instances they run on shared infrastructure without the physical server visibility and dedication required for server-bound software licenses."},{"id":"q131","question":"AWS Enterprise Support users have access to which service or feature that is not available to users with other AWS Support plans?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Support case","C":"AWS Support Concierge","D":"Amazon Connect"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS Support Concierge team, dedicated billing and account experts who provide personalized guidance, is available exclusively to Enterprise Support customers.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor is available in a limited form with Basic and Developer Support providing the seven core checks, and in full with Business and Enterprise Support, so it is not exclusive to Enterprise users. AWS Support cases can be opened by customers on Developer, Business, and Enterprise Support plans, and are not exclusive to Enterprise users. Amazon Connect is a cloud contact center service for managing customer communications via voice and chat, and is an AWS product that customers deploy rather than a support program or resource exclusive to any support tier."},{"id":"q133","question":"When performing a cost analysis that supports physical isolation of a customer workload, which compute hosting model should be accounted for in the Total Cost of Ownership (TCO)?","options":{"A":"Dedicated Hosts","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"On-Demand Instances","D":"No Upfront Reserved Instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Hosts provide physical isolation of the workload on a server dedicated entirely to one customer, which is required for certain compliance mandates or licensing models. This hosting model has a higher cost that must be factored into TCO calculations.\\nReserved Instances are a pricing commitment that reduces EC2 costs over a 1 or 3-year term, and while they lower ongoing costs they run on standard shared infrastructure and do not provide the physical isolation that a TCO analysis for compliance-mandated workloads must account for. On-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate on shared infrastructure with no long-term commitment, and while they are useful for variable workloads they do not provide physical isolation and have higher per-unit costs that would not reflect a compliance-focused TCO calculation. No Upfront Reserved Instances provide a discount over On-Demand through a long-term commitment without any initial payment, but like standard Reserved Instances they run on shared infrastructure and do not account for the physical isolation costs required by compliance mandates."},{"id":"q135","question":"Which is the MINIMUM AWS Support plan that provides technical support through phone calls?","options":{"A":"Enterprise","B":"Business","C":"Developer","D":"Basic"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Business Support is the minimum plan that includes 24/7 phone support with a dedicated team of Cloud Support Engineers. Developer Support only provides email during business hours; Basic has no technical phone or case support.\\nEnterprise Support provides 24/7 phone access and faster response times including a 15-minute SLA for business-critical system failures, but is a higher-cost plan than Business Support and therefore not the minimum required to access phone support. Developer Support provides business-hours email access to Cloud Support Engineers but does not include any phone support at any time. Basic Support provides access to documentation, whitepapers, and the seven core Trusted Advisor checks, and includes no phone support or technical case assistance."},{"id":"q136","question":"Which Amazon EC2 instance pricing model can provide discounts of up to 90%?","options":{"A":"Reserved Instances","B":"On-Demand","C":"Dedicated Hosts","D":"Spot Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spot Instances use spare AWS capacity and can offer discounts of up to 90% compared to On-Demand prices. Reserved Instances offer up to approximately 72% discount; On-Demand is the baseline price; Dedicated Hosts typically cost more than standard options.\\nReserved Instances offer savings of up to approximately 72% compared to On-Demand pricing in exchange for a 1 or 3-year commitment, which is a significant discount but substantially less than the up to 90% available through Spot Instances. On-Demand Instances are charged at the standard AWS rate with no discount, as they are the baseline pricing model against which all other purchasing options are measured. Dedicated Hosts are priced at a premium over standard EC2 pricing to account for the dedicated physical infrastructure, making them more expensive rather than cheaper than standard purchasing options."},{"id":"q139","question":"A company is planning to migrate from on-premises to the AWS Cloud. Which AWS tool or service provides detailed reports on estimated cost savings after migration?","options":{"A":"AWS Pricing Calculator","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Migration Hub"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator allows customers to model AWS service configurations and estimate costs, enabling comparison with on-premises infrastructure spending to produce detailed reports on projected savings from migration.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of existing AWS spending and usage patterns with forecasting capability, and requires active AWS usage data to generate analysis rather than providing pre-migration cost estimates. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost monitoring and governance tool rather than a migration savings estimation service. AWS Migration Hub provides a centralized location for tracking the progress of application migrations, and is a migration tracking service rather than a cost estimation tool."},{"id":"q142","question":"How can an AWS user with an AWS Basic Support plan obtain technical assistance from AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Senior Support Engineers","B":"AWS Technical Account Managers","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS Discussion Forums"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Basic Support includes access to AWS documentation, whitepapers, and community Discussion Forums where other users and AWS staff may respond to questions. TAMs are Enterprise-only; Senior Support Engineers are Business and Enterprise-only; Trusted Advisor is limited to basic checks on the free plan.\\nAWS Senior Support Engineers are Cloud Support Engineers who provide technical assistance and are accessible to Business and Enterprise Support customers, and are not available to Basic Support plan holders. AWS Technical Account Managers are dedicated support professionals exclusive to Enterprise Support customers, and are not included in any lower-tier support plan. AWS Trusted Advisor is available to Basic Support customers but only for the seven core checks, rather than the full suite of checks that requires Business or Enterprise Support."},{"id":"q145","question":"In which scenario should Amazon EC2 Spot Instances be used?","options":{"A":"A company wants to move its main website to AWS from an on-premises web server","B":"A company has a number of application services whose Service Level Agreement (SLA) requires 99.999% uptime","C":"A company\'s heavily used legacy database is currently running on-premises","D":"A company has a number of infrequent, interruptible jobs that are currently using On-Demand Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spot Instances are ideal for flexible, interruptible workloads that can tolerate being interrupted when capacity is reclaimed. High-availability services, production websites, and legacy databases all require consistent availability that Spot Instances cannot guarantee.\\nMoving a main production website to AWS requires consistent availability and uptime, as any interruption from a Spot Instance reclamation would result in downtime for end users. An application with a 99.999% uptime SLA requires that instances are always available, which Spot Instances cannot guarantee given that AWS may interrupt them at any time with a two-minute warning. A heavily used legacy database running on-premises requires consistent performance and data availability that Spot Instance interruptions would compromise, making this workload unsuitable for the Spot purchasing model."},{"id":"q152","question":"One benefit of On-Demand Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) pricing is:","options":{"A":"The ability to bid for a lower hourly cost","B":"Paying a daily rate regardless of time used","C":"Paying only for time used","D":"Pre-paying for instances and paying a lower hourly rate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"On-Demand pricing means you pay only for the compute time you actually use, with no upfront costs or long-term commitments. Bidding is a Spot Instance feature; daily rate billing and pre-payment are characteristics of other pricing models.\\nThe ability to bid for a lower hourly cost is a characteristic of Spot Instances, which are priced based on spare EC2 capacity, not a feature of On-Demand pricing which has a fixed rate set by AWS. Paying a daily rate regardless of time used describes a fixed daily pricing model, which is not how On-Demand EC2 works. On-Demand pricing charges per second with a one-minute minimum. Pre-paying for instances to receive a lower hourly rate describes Reserved Instance pricing, where customers commit to one or three years in exchange for discounted rates, not the On-Demand model."},{"id":"q157","question":"A company is migrating an application that is running non-interruptible workloads for a three-year time frame. Which pricing construct would provide the MOST cost-effective solution?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2 Spot Instances","B":"Amazon EC2 Dedicated Instances","C":"Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances","D":"Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For steady-state, non-interruptible workloads running over a three-year period, Reserved Instances offer the largest discount compared to On-Demand. Spot Instances can be interrupted; On-Demand has no discount; Dedicated Instances are for isolation, not cost savings.\\nAmazon EC2 Spot Instances can be interrupted by AWS with a two-minute warning when spare capacity is reclaimed, making them unsuitable for non-interruptible workloads that must remain available at all times. Amazon EC2 Dedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single customer for compliance and licensing isolation purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options rather than providing cost savings. Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no discount, making them the most expensive option for a steady-state workload running continuously over three years."},{"id":"q160","question":"How can one AWS account use Reserved Instances from another AWS account?","options":{"A":"By using Amazon EC2 Dedicated Instances","B":"By using AWS Organizations consolidated billing","C":"By using the AWS Cost Explorer tool","D":"By using AWS Budgets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Organizations consolidated billing pools Reserved Instance usage across all member accounts, so unused RIs in one account can automatically apply to matching usage in another, reducing costs across the organization.\\nAmazon EC2 Dedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single customer for isolation and licensing purposes, and have no mechanism for sharing Reserved Instance discounts between accounts. The AWS Cost Explorer tool provides visualisation and analysis of AWS spending and usage patterns, and while it can show RI utilization data it cannot itself enable or facilitate RI sharing between accounts. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts, and is a monitoring and governance tool with no capability to share Reserved Instance benefits across accounts."},{"id":"q165","question":"Where should a company go to search software listings from independent software vendors to find, test, buy, and deploy software that runs on AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Marketplace","B":"AWS Service Catalog","C":"AWS Artifact","D":"Amazon CloudSearch"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Marketplace is the curated digital catalog where independent software vendors list their products, allowing customers to find, test, buy, and deploy software that runs on AWS.\\nAWS Service Catalog allows organizations to create and manage approved portfolios of IT products for internal deployment, but does not host third-party software listings from independent vendors. AWS Artifact is a self-service portal for accessing AWS compliance reports and security certifications, not a software discovery or procurement platform. Amazon CloudSearch is a managed search service for adding search functionality to applications and has no role in software vendor listings or procurement."},{"id":"q167","question":"Which Amazon EC2 pricing model adjusts based on supply and demand of EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Convertible Reserved Instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Spot Instance pricing fluctuates in real time based on the available supply of spare EC2 capacity and customer demand, which can result in prices up to 90% lower than On-Demand.\\nOn-Demand Instances are charged at a fixed hourly rate set by AWS based on instance type and region, and the price does not vary based on supply and demand or change in real time. Reserved Instances allow customers to commit to a specific instance type for one or three years in exchange for a fixed discounted rate, and the reserved price is locked at purchase and does not fluctuate based on supply and demand. Convertible Reserved Instances are a type of Reserved Instance that can be exchanged for other Convertible RIs with different attributes, and like Standard RIs they are priced at a fixed discounted rate that does not adjust based on supply and demand."},{"id":"q176","question":"Compared with costs in traditional and virtualized data centers, AWS has:","options":{"A":"Greater variable costs and greater upfront costs","B":"Fixed usage costs and lower upfront costs","C":"Lower variable costs and greater upfront costs","D":"Lower variable costs and lower upfront costs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS leverages economies of scale and a shared infrastructure model to deliver lower variable costs per unit than traditional or virtualized data centers, and its pay-as-you-go model eliminates large upfront capital costs, resulting in both lower variable costs and lower upfront costs.\\nGreater variable costs and greater upfront costs describes the opposite of the AWS model, which specifically reduces both variable costs through economies of scale and eliminates upfront capital expenditure through pay-as-you-go pricing. Fixed usage costs and lower upfront costs partially describes AWS in that upfront costs are lower, but AWS costs are variable rather than fixed since they scale directly with usage and resource consumption. Lower variable costs and greater upfront costs partially describes the traditional data center model where purchasing hardware requires large upfront investment, but on AWS there are no significant upfront costs as infrastructure is consumed as a service."},{"id":"q180","question":"How should a customer forecast the future costs for running a new web application?","options":{"A":"Amazon Aurora Backtrack","B":"Amazon CloudWatch Billing Alarms","C":"AWS Pricing Calculator","D":"AWS Cost and Usage Report"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator allows customers to model AWS service configurations and estimate future costs before deploying them, making it the purpose-built tool for forecasting the cost of a new application.\\nAmazon Aurora Backtrack is a feature that allows an Aurora database to be rewound to a previous point in time without restoring from a backup, and is a database recovery feature with no connection to cost forecasting. Amazon CloudWatch Billing Alarms trigger notifications when actual AWS spending exceeds a defined threshold, and are a cost alerting tool for active workloads rather than a forecasting tool for new applications. AWS Cost and Usage Reports provide the most detailed breakdown of actual AWS charges already incurred, and are a historical cost analysis tool rather than a future cost estimation service."},{"id":"q193","question":"A global company with a large number of AWS accounts is seeking a way in which they can centrally manage billing and security policies across all accounts. Which AWS Service will assist them in meeting these goals?","options":{"A":"AWS Organizations","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"IAM User Groups","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Organizations provides centralized management of multiple AWS accounts, including consolidated billing and the ability to apply Service Control Policies as security guardrails across all accounts.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, but operates at the individual account level and does not provide centralized management of billing or security policies across multiple accounts. IAM User Groups are a collection of IAM users that share the same attached policies within a single AWS account, and operate at the account level rather than providing cross-account billing or policy management. AWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration of AWS resources to assess compliance and detect changes, but operates at the account level and does not provide cross-account billing management or security policy enforcement across an organization."},{"id":"q198","question":"Which of the following is NOT correct regarding Amazon EC2 On-demand instances?","options":{"A":"You have to pay a start-up fee when launching a new instance for the first time","B":"The on-demand instances follow the AWS pay-as-you-go pricing model","C":"With on-demand instances, no longer-term commitments or upfront payments are needed","D":"When using on-demand Linux instances, you are charged per second based on an hourly rate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"On-Demand EC2 instances have no start-up fee. You simply pay for the compute time you use with no upfront charges. On-Demand instances follow pay-as-you-go pricing, require no commitments, and Linux instances are billed per second.\\nThere is no start-up fee when launching an On-Demand EC2 instance for the first time. AWS charges only for the compute time the instance runs, with no additional fees for initial provisioning or launch. The AWS pay-as-you-go pricing model for On-Demand instances means you are charged only for the time the instance runs, with no additional start-up fees, fixed monthly charges, or minimum usage requirements. With On-Demand instances there are no longer-term commitments or upfront payments required, allowing you to start and stop instances at any time based on your needs. Linux-based EC2 instances are indeed billed per second based on an hourly rate, with a minimum charge of one full minute, which is an accurate statement and not the incorrect one the question asks you to identify."},{"id":"q201","question":"A company is introducing a new product to their customers, and is expecting a surge in traffic to their web application. As part of their Enterprise Support plan, which of the following provides the company with architectural and scaling guidance?","options":{"A":"AWS Knowledge Center","B":"AWS Health Dashboard","C":"Infrastructure Event Management","D":"AWS Support Concierge Service"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Infrastructure Event Management is an Enterprise Support feature that provides architectural guidance and real-time support during planned events such as product launches and traffic spikes.\\nAWS Knowledge Center is a publicly accessible library of frequently asked questions and solutions for common AWS issues and use cases, and is a self-service resource rather than a dedicated program that provides personalized architectural guidance during high-traffic events. AWS Health Dashboard sends proactive personalized alerts about AWS events that may affect your resources and provides remediation guidance, but is a monitoring and alerting tool rather than a program for receiving architectural guidance during a planned product launch. AWS Support Concierge Service is available to Enterprise Support customers as a dedicated resource for billing and account inquiries, and does not provide architectural guidance or scaling support for planned traffic events."},{"id":"q214","question":"Which of the following EC2 instance purchasing options supports the Bring Your Own License (BYOL) model for almost every BYOL scenario?","options":{"A":"Dedicated Instances","B":"Dedicated Hosts","C":"On-demand Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"B","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Hosts provide a physical server dedicated entirely to your use with visibility into sockets and cores, which is required to bring existing per-socket or per-core software licenses under the BYOL model.\\nDedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single customer but do not provide visibility into the specific physical host, including socket and core counts, which is required for most per-socket or per-core licensing models. On-Demand Instances are charged at the standard hourly rate on shared infrastructure with full hardware multi-tenancy, and do not provide the physical server dedication or host-level visibility required for any meaningful BYOL scenario. Reserved Instances are a pricing commitment that reduces costs over a one or three-year term, but like On-Demand Instances they run on standard shared infrastructure and do not provide the host-level visibility needed for server-bound software licensing."},{"id":"q234","question":"Which of the following activities may help reduce your AWS monthly costs?","options":{"A":"Enabling Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling for all of your workloads","B":"Using the AWS Network Load Balancer (NLB) to load balance the incoming HTTP requests","C":"Removing all of your Cost Allocation Tags","D":"Deploying your AWS resources across multiple Availability Zones"},"correct":"A","explanation":"EC2 Auto Scaling ensures you run only the capacity you need, scaling in during low demand and out during high demand, which directly reduces costs by eliminating over-provisioning.\\nUsing the AWS Network Load Balancer to load balance incoming HTTP requests distributes traffic across instances to improve availability and performance, but does not change the number of running instances or address the underlying cost of over-provisioned capacity. Removing all Cost Allocation Tags reduces billing visibility and the ability to attribute costs to specific projects or teams, but has no effect on the actual AWS charges being incurred. Deploying AWS resources across multiple Availability Zones improves resilience by distributing workloads across separate failure domains, but typically increases costs by running additional redundant resources rather than reducing monthly charges."},{"id":"q240","question":"A company has created a solution that helps AWS customers improve their architectures on AWS. Which AWS program may support this company?","options":{"A":"APN Consulting Partners","B":"AWS Technical Account Manager","C":"APN Technology Partners","D":"AWS Professional Services"},"correct":"A","explanation":"APN Consulting Partners are professional services firms certified by AWS to design, build, and manage customer workloads on AWS, including solutions that help other customers improve their architectures.\\nAWS TAM is a Technical Account Manager, which is a direct AWS resource assigned to enterprise support customers rather than an independent partner program a company can join to offer consulting services. APN Technology Partners are companies that provide software products and solutions that run on or integrate with AWS, rather than consulting or professional services that help customers improve their architectures. AWS Professional Services is a global team of AWS experts that works directly with customers on their own projects, and is not a program that external companies can join to deliver services to other customers."},{"id":"q245","question":"Which of the following describes the payment model that AWS makes available for customers that can commit to using Amazon EC2 over a one or 3-year term to reduce their total computing costs?","options":{"A":"Pay less as AWS grows","B":"Pay as you go","C":"Pay less by using more","D":"Save when you reserve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The AWS pricing construct for committing to EC2 usage over one or three years in exchange for reduced rates aligns with the principle of \'Save when you reserve.\'\\n\'Pay less as AWS grows\' refers to the long-term pricing principle where AWS passes economies of scale savings to customers over time as AWS infrastructure expands, rather than describing a specific payment model tied to individual usage commitments. \'Pay as you go\' describes the On-Demand pricing model where customers pay only for the resources they consume with no upfront commitment, rather than a model involving a one or three-year term commitment. \'Pay less by using more\' describes volume discounts where per-unit costs decrease as total usage increases, such as the tiered pricing on Amazon S3, rather than savings derived from making a time-based usage commitment."},{"id":"q246","question":"A company is migrating its on-premises database to Amazon RDS. What should the company do to ensure Amazon RDS costs are kept to a minimum?","options":{"A":"Right-size before and after migration","B":"Use a Multi-Region Active-Passive architecture","C":"Combine On-demand Capacity Reservations with Saving Plans","D":"Use a Multi-Region Active-Active architecture"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Right-sizing, choosing the most appropriate instance type and size for your actual workload, both before migration and after based on real usage, is the most effective way to minimize RDS costs without sacrificing performance.\\nUsing a Multi-Region Active-Passive architecture distributes a workload across two Regions where one serves as a standby for disaster recovery, which increases infrastructure costs by running resources in a second Region rather than minimizing them. Combining On-Demand Capacity Reservations with Savings Plans can reduce EC2 costs for specific use cases but adds billing complexity and is not the primary lever for minimizing RDS costs. Using a Multi-Region Active-Active architecture runs the full workload simultaneously across multiple Regions to serve users with low latency globally, which significantly increases costs rather than minimizing them."},{"id":"q260","question":"A company needs to host a database in Amazon RDS for at least three years. Which of the following options would be the most cost-effective solution?","options":{"A":"Reserved Instances - No Upfront","B":"Reserved Instances - Partial Upfront","C":"On-Demand Instances","D":"Spot Instances"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For a three-year database commitment, Reserved Instances with Partial Upfront payment offers the best balance of discount depth and manageable cash flow, providing a deeper discount than No Upfront while avoiding the full capital outlay of All Upfront payment.\\nReserved Instances with No Upfront payment still provide a discount over On-Demand for a three-year commitment, but the discount is shallower than Partial or All Upfront options because AWS does not receive any payment at the start of the term. On-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no discount, making them the most expensive option for a steady-state workload running for three years. Spot Instances are not available for Amazon RDS, as RDS is a fully managed database service that does not support the Spot purchasing model."},{"id":"q262","question":"Savings Plans are available for which of the following AWS compute services?","options":{"A":"AWS Batch","B":"AWS Outposts","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"Amazon EC2","E":"AWS Lambda"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"AWS Savings Plans are flexible pricing models available for Amazon EC2 and AWS Lambda, offering savings over On-Demand in exchange for a one or three-year usage commitment.\\nAWS Batch is a managed service for running batch computing workloads at scale, and while it can use EC2 instances that may be covered by Savings Plans, AWS Batch itself as a service is not directly covered by a Savings Plan commitment. AWS Outposts extends AWS infrastructure to customer on-premises environments, and workloads running on Outposts are not covered by Compute Savings Plans in the same way as standard AWS Region deployments. Amazon Lightsail is a simplified compute service with straightforward bundled pricing for virtual servers and web applications, and is not covered by AWS Savings Plans."},{"id":"q264","question":"Which statement is correct with regards to AWS service limits?","options":{"A":"You can contact AWS support to increase the service limits","B":"Each IAM user has the same service limit","C":"There are no service limits on AWS","D":"You can use the AWS Trusted Advisor to monitor your service limits","E":"The Amazon Simple Email Service is responsible for sending email notifications when usage approaches a service limit"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS service limits (quotas) apply at the account level and can be increased by submitting a support request to AWS. AWS Trusted Advisor monitors current usage against service limits and alerts you when you approach them.\\nService limits apply to the entire AWS account rather than to individual IAM users, so each IAM user does not have their own separate service limit. AWS does impose service limits on all accounts by default, so the claim that there are no service limits is factually incorrect. Amazon Simple Email Service is an email sending service for transactional and marketing communications, and is not responsible for monitoring or sending notifications about service limit usage."},{"id":"q272","question":"How are AWS customers billed for Linux-based Amazon EC2 usage?","options":{"A":"EC2 instances will be billed on one second increments, with a minimum of one minute","B":"EC2 instances will be billed on one hour increments, with a minimum of one day","C":"EC2 instances will be billed on one minute increments, with a minimum of one hour","D":"EC2 instances will be billed on one day increments, with a minimum of one month"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Linux-based EC2 instances are billed per second of actual usage, with a minimum charge of one full minute. This granular billing model means short-lived workloads are not over-charged.\\nBilling on one-hour increments with a daily minimum was the original EC2 billing model before per-second billing was introduced, and significantly over-charges short-lived workloads compared to the current per-second model. Billing on one-minute increments with an hourly minimum would over-charge instances running for less than an hour and is not the current billing model for Linux EC2 instances. Billing on a daily increment with a monthly minimum describes a model that does not exist for EC2 instances and would make short-duration workloads prohibitively expensive compared to the actual per-second billing that applies."},{"id":"q273","question":"Which of the following will impact the price paid for an EC2 instance?","options":{"A":"Instance type","B":"The Availability Zone where the instance is provisioned","C":"Load balancing","D":"Number of buckets","E":"Number of private IPs"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"The EC2 instance type determines the base rate, and the Availability Zone where the instance is provisioned can affect pricing because Spot Instance prices vary by AZ and some On-Demand regional prices differ.\\nLoad balancing distributes incoming traffic across multiple EC2 instances to improve availability, and while Elastic Load Balancers incur their own separate charges, the use of load balancing does not affect the price of the EC2 instances themselves. The number of S3 buckets you have does not affect EC2 instance pricing in any way, as S3 storage costs are calculated separately based on stored data volume and storage class. The number of private IP addresses assigned to an EC2 instance does not affect its pricing, as private IP addresses within a VPC are provided at no additional cost."},{"id":"q275","question":"A company uses AWS Organizations to manage all of its AWS accounts. Which of the following allows the company to restrict what services and actions are allowed in each individual account?","options":{"A":"IAM Principals","B":"AWS Service Control Policies (SCPs)","C":"IAM policies","D":"AWS Fargate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Service Control Policies in AWS Organizations are permission guardrails applied to accounts or organizational units that restrict which AWS services and actions are available within those accounts, even for root users.\\nIAM Principals are the entities such as users, roles, and groups that IAM policies are attached to, and are components of the identity and access management system within a single account rather than a mechanism for restricting services and actions across multiple accounts. IAM policies define permissions for users, groups, and roles within an individual AWS account, and while they control access within an account they cannot enforce restrictions that apply across multiple accounts in an AWS Organization the way SCPs can. AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for running containers that abstracts the underlying infrastructure management, and is a compute service with no connection to account-level governance or cross-account service restriction."},{"id":"q279","question":"What is the minimum level of AWS support that provides 24/7 access to technical support engineers via phone and chat?","options":{"A":"Enterprise Support","B":"Developer Support","C":"Basic Support","D":"Business Support"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Business Support is the minimum plan that includes 24/7 phone and chat access to Cloud Support Engineers, with a one-hour response SLA for production system-down cases.\\nEnterprise Support also provides 24/7 phone and chat access to Cloud Support Engineers but is a higher-tier plan than Business Support, making it not the minimum level that satisfies this requirement. Developer Support provides email access to Cloud Support Engineers during business hours only, and does not include 24/7 phone or chat access. Basic Support provides access to documentation, whitepapers, and AWS Trusted Advisor core checks but includes no technical case support or access to Cloud Support Engineers."},{"id":"q281","question":"A company has developed a media transcoding application in AWS. The application is designed to recover quickly from hardware failures. Which one of the following types of instance would be the most cost-effective choice to use?","options":{"A":"Reserved instances","B":"Spot Instances","C":"On-Demand instances","D":"Dedicated instances"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Spot Instances are ideal for workloads that are fault-tolerant and can handle interruptions, such as media transcoding, which can checkpoint and resume. Since the application is designed to recover quickly from hardware failures, it tolerates interruptions, making Spot Instances the most cost-effective choice.\\nReserved Instances provide a significant discount over On-Demand pricing for steady-state workloads through a one or three-year commitment, but are not the most cost-effective option for a fault-tolerant application that qualifies for the deeper discounts available through Spot pricing. On-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no discount, making them significantly more expensive than Spot Instances for a batch workload where cost minimization is the primary goal and interruptions are acceptable. Dedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single customer for compliance and licensing isolation purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options rather than providing cost savings for a fault-tolerant processing workload."},{"id":"q286","question":"Which AWS service provides cost-optimization recommendations?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"Amazon QuickSight","D":"AWS X-Ray"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor continuously analyzes your AWS environment and provides specific cost-optimization recommendations such as identifying underutilized EC2 instances, idle load balancers, and unattached EBS volumes.\\nThe AWS Pricing Calculator estimates future costs for new AWS service configurations before provisioning them, and is a planning tool for forecasting spend rather than a service that analyzes existing infrastructure to identify cost-saving opportunities. Amazon QuickSight is a serverless business intelligence service for creating interactive dashboards and visualisations from business data, and has no capability to analyze AWS infrastructure usage or generate cost-optimization recommendations. AWS X-Ray is a distributed tracing service that analyzes and debugs the performance of applications by mapping requests as they flow through application components, and is a performance analysis tool with no cost-optimization or infrastructure analysis capability."},{"id":"q288","question":"What is one benefit and one drawback of buying a reserved EC2 instance?","options":{"A":"Instances can be shut down by AWS at any time with no notification","B":"Reserved instances require at least a one-year pricing commitment","C":"There is no additional charge for using dedicated instances","D":"Reserved instances provide a significant discount compared to on-demand instances","E":"Reserved instances are best suited for periodic workloads"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Reserved Instances require a minimum one-year commitment, which is the primary drawback for teams that need flexibility. The key benefit is a significant discount compared to On-Demand pricing.\\nInstances being shut down by AWS at any time without notification is a characteristic of Spot Instances, which can be interrupted when AWS reclaims spare capacity, rather than Reserved Instances which continue running based on the customer\'s control. There is an additional charge for using Dedicated Instances, which run on hardware dedicated to a single customer and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options, so the claim that there is no additional charge is incorrect. Reserved Instances are best suited for steady-state workloads that run continuously or predictably rather than periodic workloads, because the commitment is for consistent capacity and the discount does not scale down when the instance is not running."},{"id":"q290","question":"What is the most cost-effective purchasing option for running a set of EC2 instances that must always be available for a period of two months?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Spot Instances","C":"Reserved Instances - All Upfront","D":"Reserved Instances - No Upfront"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For a workload that must always be available but only for two months, On-Demand Instances are the best choice as you pay only for the compute time you use with no long-term commitment, and the short duration does not justify the minimum one-year Reserved Instance term.\\nSpot Instances can be interrupted by AWS when spare capacity is reclaimed, directly violating the requirement that instances must always be available. Reserved Instances with All Upfront payment require a minimum one-year commitment, which would cost significantly more over just two months than paying On-Demand for that period. Reserved Instances with No Upfront payment also require a minimum one-year commitment, making the total cost over the commitment term far exceed the two-month requirement."},{"id":"q297","question":"A company needs to migrate their website from on-premises to AWS. Security is a major concern for them, so they need to host their website on hardware that is NOT shared with other AWS customers. Which of the following EC2 instance options meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"On-demand instances","B":"Spot instances","C":"Dedicated instances","D":"Reserved instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Instances run on hardware that is physically isolated at the host level and is not shared with any other AWS customer account, meeting the physical isolation requirement.\\nOn-demand instances run on shared hardware and are billed by the second with no upfront commitment, but do not provide any physical isolation from other AWS customers. Spot instances run on shared spare AWS capacity at discounted rates and offer no hardware isolation or tenancy guarantees. Reserved Instances are a billing and pricing model that offers discounted rates in exchange for a usage commitment, not a tenancy type, and do not guarantee dedicated hardware."},{"id":"q301","question":"Which support plan includes AWS Support Concierge Service?","options":{"A":"Business Support","B":"Developer Support","C":"Enterprise Support","D":"Basic Support"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS Support Concierge is a dedicated billing and account expert team available exclusively to Enterprise Support customers, providing personalized guidance on billing inquiries and account best practices.\\nBusiness Support provides 24/7 access to Cloud Support Engineers for technical issues and includes Infrastructure Event Management for an additional fee, but does not include the dedicated Concierge team for billing and account guidance that is exclusive to Enterprise Support. Developer Support provides business-hours email access to Cloud Support Engineers for general technical questions, and does not include billing specialists or dedicated account management resources. Basic Support provides access to documentation, whitepapers, and the seven core Trusted Advisor checks, and includes no dedicated support personnel or specialized billing assistance."},{"id":"q309","question":"How can AWS customers track and avoid over-spending on underutilized reserved instances?","options":{"A":"Customers can add all AWS accounts to an AWS Organization, enable Consolidated Billing, and turn off Reserved Instance sharing","B":"Customers can use Amazon Neptune to track and analyze their usage patterns, detect underutilized reserved instances, and then sell them on the Amazon EC2 Reserved Instance Marketplace","C":"Customers can use the AWS Budgets service to track the reserved instances usage and set up alert notifications when their utilization drops below the threshold that they define","D":"Customers can use Amazon CloudTrail to automatically check for unused reservations and get recommendations to reduce their bill"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Budgets lets you set custom utilization thresholds for Reserved Instances and sends alert notifications when utilization drops below your defined level, helping you identify and address underutilized reservations.\\nAdding all AWS accounts to an AWS Organization and enabling consolidated billing allows Reserved Instance benefits to be shared across accounts, but turning off Reserved Instance sharing would prevent that benefit rather than helping track underutilized reservations. Amazon Neptune is a fully managed graph database service for applications that work with highly connected datasets, and has no capability to track Reserved Instance usage, detect underutilization, or facilitate resale on the RI Marketplace. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity across an AWS environment for auditing and compliance purposes, and does not analyze Reserved Instance utilization or generate billing reduction recommendations."},{"id":"q314","question":"A company is trying to analyze the costs applied to their AWS account recently. Which of the following provides them the most granular data about their AWS costs and usage?","options":{"A":"Amazon Machine Image","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Cost & Usage Report","D":"Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Cost and Usage Reports provide the most granular data available about AWS costs, breaking down charges by hour, service, resource, and tags for detailed analysis and auditing.\\nAn Amazon Machine Image is a template containing the software configuration required to launch an EC2 instance, and has no connection to cost analysis or billing data. AWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of AWS spending patterns over time and supports filtering by service, account, tag, and time period, but provides less granular data than the Cost and Usage Report which includes hourly resource-level detail. Amazon CloudWatch collects metrics, logs, and event data from AWS services and resources to provide operational monitoring and alerting, and while it can monitor some billing metrics it does not provide the detailed cost and usage breakdown that the Cost and Usage Report delivers."},{"id":"q318","question":"What are the benefits of implementing a tagging strategy for AWS resources?","options":{"A":"Quickly identify resources that belong to a specific project","B":"Quickly identify software solutions on AWS","C":"Track API calls in your AWS account","D":"Quickly identify deleted resources and their metadata","E":"Track AWS spending across multiple resources"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Tags are key-value metadata pairs that let you quickly identify which resources belong to specific projects and track AWS spending across those resources for cost allocation and organizational clarity.\\nQuickly identifying software solutions on AWS describes browsing AWS Marketplace, which is unrelated to tagging resources within an account. Tracking API calls in an AWS account describes the function of AWS CloudTrail, which logs account activity rather than organizing resources by metadata. Quickly identifying deleted resources and their metadata is not a function of tagging, as tags are attached to existing resources and are removed when resources are deleted."},{"id":"q328","question":"TYMO Cloud Corp is looking forward to migrating their entire on-premises data center to AWS. What tool can they use to perform a cost-benefit analysis of moving to the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Migration Evaluator","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Pricing Calculator"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Migration Evaluator helps organizations compare the cost of running workloads on-premises versus on AWS, factoring in infrastructure, labor, and operational expenses to support a migration business case.\\nAWS Cost Explorer is used to visualise and analyze spending on existing AWS usage, not to compare on-premises costs against AWS before migration. AWS Budgets is used to set spending thresholds and receive alerts on existing AWS accounts, not to evaluate migration cost savings. The AWS Pricing Calculator estimates monthly costs for a planned AWS architecture but does not compare those costs against on-premises infrastructure to produce a cost-benefit analysis."},{"id":"q334","question":"AWS recommends some practices to help organizations avoid unexpected charges on their bill. Which of the following is NOT one of these practices?","options":{"A":"Deleting unused EBS volumes after terminating an EC2 instance","B":"Deleting unused Auto Scaling launch configurations","C":"Deleting unused Elastic Load Balancers","D":"Releasing unused Elastic IPs after terminating an EC2 instance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Deleting unused Auto Scaling launch configurations does not generate AWS charges, so it is not a cost-saving practice. In contrast, unused EBS volumes, Elastic Load Balancers, and Elastic IPs all incur ongoing charges that should be cleaned up.\\nDeleting unused EBS volumes after terminating an EC2 instance is a recommended cost-saving practice because EBS volumes continue to incur storage charges even when no instance is attached to them. Deleting unused Elastic Load Balancers is a recommended cost-saving practice because load balancers incur an hourly charge regardless of whether they are serving traffic. Releasing unused Elastic IP addresses after terminating an EC2 instance is a recommended cost-saving practice because AWS charges for Elastic IPs that are allocated but not associated with a running instance."},{"id":"q335","question":"What is the AWS tool that can help a company visualize their AWS spending in the last few months?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of AWS spending over time, allowing you to filter by service, account, tag, or time period to identify spending trends and anomalies across recent months.\\nThe AWS Pricing Calculator estimates future costs for AWS services before you use them, and does not provide visualisation of historical spending. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a proactive cost governance tool rather than a historical spending visualisation service. AWS Organizations provides centralized management of multiple AWS accounts including consolidated billing and the ability to apply Service Control Policies, but does not itself provide visualisation of spending patterns."},{"id":"q339","question":"One of the major advantages of using AWS is cost savings. What does AWS provide to reduce the cost of running Amazon EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"Low monthly instance maintenance costs","B":"Low-cost instance tagging","C":"Per-second instance billing","D":"Low instance start-up fees"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon EC2 offers per-second billing with a one-minute minimum, so customers pay only for the exact compute time they use rather than rounding up to the nearest hour, directly reducing costs.\\nLow monthly instance maintenance costs is not an AWS cost reduction benefit, as maintenance of the underlying physical infrastructure is AWS\'s responsibility under the shared responsibility model and is included in the standard pricing. Low-cost instance tagging refers to the use of resource tags for cost allocation, and while tags are free to apply they are a cost visibility mechanism rather than a mechanism that reduces the price of running EC2 instances. Low instance start-up fees is a fabricated pricing concept. Amazon EC2 On-Demand instances have no start-up fee and charge only for the time the instance runs."},{"id":"q340","question":"Which AWS Group assists customers in achieving their desired business outcomes?","options":{"A":"AWS Security Team","B":"AWS Professional Services","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS Concierge Support Team"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Professional Services is a team of experts that works directly with customers to help them achieve their desired business outcomes through cloud strategy, architecture guidance, and migration support.\\nThe AWS Security Team manages AWS\'s own internal security posture and the responsible disclosure program for AWS infrastructure, and is not a customer-facing team that works with customers to achieve business outcomes on AWS. AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides automated recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and is a self-service advisory tool rather than a professional services team that works directly with customers. The AWS Concierge Support Team is available to Enterprise Support customers for billing and account inquiries, providing dedicated account guidance rather than strategic business outcome or cloud migration support."},{"id":"q344","question":"You decide to buy a reserved instance for a term of one year. Which option provides the largest total discount?","options":{"A":"All up-front reservation","B":"All reserved instance payment options provide the same discount level","C":"Partial up-front reservation","D":"No up-front reservation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The All Upfront payment option for Reserved Instances provides the largest discount because you pay the entire term cost at the beginning, earning you the maximum savings.\\nAll Reserved Instance payment options do not provide the same discount level. The three options, All Upfront, Partial Upfront, and No Upfront, each provide progressively lower discounts as the amount paid upfront decreases. Partial Upfront reservation splits the payment between an initial upfront amount and lower monthly fees, providing a moderate discount that is greater than No Upfront but less than All Upfront. No Upfront reservation provides the lowest discount among the three RI payment options because no initial payment is made, meaning AWS offers less incentive than with Partial or All Upfront options."},{"id":"q346","question":"An AWS customer has used one Amazon Linux instance for 2 hours, 5 minutes and 9 seconds, and one CentOS instance for 4 hours, 23 minutes and 7 seconds. How much time will the customer be billed for?","options":{"A":"3 hours for the Linux instance and 5 hours for the CentOS instance","B":"2 hours, 5 minutes and 9 seconds for the Linux instance and 4 hours, 23 minutes and 7 seconds for the CentOS instance","C":"2 hours, 5 minutes and 9 seconds for the Linux instance and 5 hours for the CentOS instance","D":"3 hours for the Linux instance and 4 hours, 23 minutes and 7 seconds for the CentOS instance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Linux instances are billed per second with a one-minute minimum, so 2 hours, 5 minutes, and 9 seconds is the exact billable time. CentOS instances on the AWS Marketplace are billed per hour rounded up, resulting in 5 hours.\\nBilling both instances at rounded-up hour increments would apply the old hourly billing model to the Linux instance, which would result in 3 hours rather than the exact per-second duration. Billing both instances at exact per-second precision would incorrectly apply the Linux per-second billing model to the CentOS instance, which is billed per hour rounded up. Billing the Linux instance at rounded-up hours and the CentOS at exact seconds reverses the correct billing models for each instance type."},{"id":"q347","question":"What is the AWS Support feature that allows customers to manage support cases programmatically?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Operations Support","C":"AWS Support API","D":"AWS Health Dashboard"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS Support API allows customers to create, update, and resolve support cases programmatically, enabling integration with internal ticketing systems and automated incident workflows.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides automated recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and is an advisory tool rather than a feature for managing support cases programmatically. AWS Operations Support is not a recognized AWS service or support feature name. AWS Health Dashboard provides a personalized view of AWS service events affecting your resources along with remediation guidance, and is an observability tool rather than a programmatic interface for managing support cases."},{"id":"q356","question":"Which AWS Service offers volume discounts based on usage?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon S3 uses a tiered pricing model where the per-GB storage cost decreases as your total usage increases, automatically providing volume discounts to customers who store more data.\\nAmazon VPC is a networking service that lets you provision isolated virtual networks within AWS, and while VPC itself is free the resources deployed within it such as NAT Gateways are charged individually without volume-based discounts. Amazon Lightsail is a simplified compute service with straightforward bundled pricing for virtual servers and web applications, and uses fixed pricing tiers rather than volume discounts based on usage. AWS Cost Explorer provides visualisation and analysis of AWS spending patterns over time, and is a cost management tool rather than an AWS service that applies volume-based pricing discounts."},{"id":"q360","question":"The TCO gap between AWS infrastructure and traditional infrastructure has widened over the recent years. Which of the following could be the reason for that?","options":{"A":"AWS helps customers invest more in capital expenditures","B":"AWS automates all infrastructure operations, so customers save more on human resources costs","C":"AWS continues to lower the cost of cloud computing for its customers","D":"AWS secures AWS resources at no additional charge"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS continuously reduces pricing as it achieves greater economies of scale, passing savings to customers, which widens the total cost of ownership gap compared to traditional on-premises infrastructure.\\nAWS does not help customers invest more in capital expenditures. On the contrary, AWS shifts infrastructure spending from capital expenditure to operational expenditure, reducing the need for large upfront hardware investments. AWS automates many infrastructure tasks through managed services and orchestration tools, but customers still incur human resources costs for cloud engineers and architects, and AWS does not eliminate all infrastructure operations requiring staff. AWS does secure its own infrastructure at no additional charge as part of its responsibilities under the shared responsibility model, but this has always been the case and is not the primary reason the TCO gap has widened over time."},{"id":"q364","question":"Which of the following is NOT a factor when estimating the costs of Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"The amount of time the instances will be running","B":"Number of security groups","C":"Allocated Elastic IP Addresses","D":"Number of Hosted Zones","E":"Number of instances"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Security groups and Hosted Zones do not factor into EC2 pricing. EC2 costs are determined by instance type, running time, number of instances, storage, and data transfer.\\nThe amount of time instances will be running directly affects EC2 costs, as Linux instances are billed per second and all instances stop accruing compute charges only when stopped or terminated. Allocated Elastic IP addresses incur charges when they are allocated but not associated with a running instance, and are a cost associated with EC2 deployments that must be considered when estimating total costs. The number of EC2 instances directly determines the scale of compute costs, as each running instance accrues charges based on its type and running time."},{"id":"q370","question":"A user has opened a \\"Production System Down\\" support case to get help from AWS Support after a production system disruption. What is the expected response time for this type of support case?","options":{"A":"12 hours","B":"15 minutes","C":"24 hours","D":"One hour"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For a Production System Down case on the Business or Enterprise Support plan, the expected response time is one hour, ensuring critical production outages receive rapid attention from AWS Support.\\nA 12-hour response time corresponds to the general guidance response target for Developer Support customers on a system-impaired case, not the production system-down response time for Business or Enterprise Support. A 15-minute response time is the SLA for business-critical system failures under the Enterprise Support plan, which is a faster tier than the one-hour production system-down SLA available starting at Business Support. A 24-hour response time is the general guidance response target for Developer Support customers on general guidance cases, and does not apply to production system outages at the Business or Enterprise Support level."},{"id":"q373","question":"Your company requires a response time of less than 15 minutes from support interactions about their business-critical systems that are hosted on AWS if those systems go down. Which AWS Support Plan should this company use?","options":{"A":"AWS Basic Support","B":"AWS Developer Support","C":"AWS Business Support","D":"AWS Enterprise Support"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Enterprise Support provides a 15-minute response time for business-critical system-down cases, which is the fastest response SLA available across all AWS Support plans.\\nAWS Basic Support provides access to documentation, whitepapers, and the seven core Trusted Advisor checks, and includes no technical case support or any defined response time SLA for system failures. AWS Developer Support provides email access to Cloud Support Engineers during business hours with response times measured in hours rather than minutes, and does not include 24/7 access or a 15-minute response SLA. AWS Business Support provides 24/7 access to Cloud Support Engineers with a one-hour response SLA for production system-down cases, which does not meet the requirement for a response within 15 minutes."},{"id":"q379","question":"A company is migrating a web application to AWS. The application\'s compute capacity is continually utilized throughout the year. Which of the below options offers the company the most cost-effective solution?","options":{"A":"On-demand Instances","B":"Dedicated Hosts","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For steady-state workloads with continual utilization throughout the year, Reserved Instances provide significant discounts over On-Demand pricing in exchange for a 1 or 3-year commitment, making them the most cost-effective option.\\nOn-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no discount, making them the most expensive option for a web application that uses compute capacity continuously throughout the year. Dedicated Hosts provide a physical server dedicated entirely to a single customer for compliance and licensing purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options rather than providing cost savings for a standard continuously running web application. Spot Instances can be interrupted by AWS when spare capacity is reclaimed, making them unsuitable for a production web application that requires continual availability throughout the year."},{"id":"q382","question":"Which of the following will help AWS customers save on costs when migrating their workloads to AWS?","options":{"A":"Use servers instead of managed services","B":"Use existing third-party software licenses on AWS","C":"Migrate production workloads to AWS edge locations instead of AWS Regions","D":"Use AWS Outposts to run all workloads in a cost-optimized environment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS supports Bring Your Own License, allowing customers to reuse existing third-party software licenses on AWS, which avoids paying for new licenses and reduces migration costs.\\nUsing servers instead of managed services typically increases operational costs by requiring customers to manage and maintain the underlying operating systems, databases, and software, rather than reducing them through the efficiency of AWS managed services. Migrating production workloads to AWS edge locations instead of AWS Regions is not a valid migration strategy, as edge locations are designed for content caching and delivery rather than hosting production compute workloads. Using AWS Outposts to run all workloads does not save migration costs, as Outposts are designed for workloads that must remain on-premises due to latency or data residency requirements, and involve additional costs for dedicated AWS-managed hardware in the customer\'s facility."},{"id":"q386","question":"A company is planning to use Amazon S3 and Amazon CloudFront to distribute its video courses globally. What tool can the company use to estimate the costs of these services?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Cost & Usage Report"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator lets you estimate costs for AWS services before you use them, allowing you to model configurations for S3 storage and CloudFront distribution to forecast monthly expenses.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides visualisation and analysis of existing AWS spending patterns over time and requires active usage data to generate analysis rather than providing pre-deployment cost estimates for services not yet in use. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost governance and alerting tool rather than a service for estimating future costs before deploying new services. The AWS Cost and Usage Report provides the most granular breakdown of actual AWS charges already incurred, and is a historical billing analysis tool rather than a cost estimation service for planned deployments."},{"id":"q394","question":"Which features are included in the AWS Business Support Plan?","options":{"A":"24/7 access to customer service","B":"Access to Cloud Support Engineers via email only during business hours","C":"Access to the Infrastructure Event Management (IEM) feature for additional fee","D":"24/7 access to the Technical Account Manager feature","E":"Partial access to the core Trusted Advisor checks"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"The Business Support plan includes 24/7 access to customer service via phone, chat, and email, and access to Infrastructure Event Management for an additional fee to support planned events like launches.\\nAccess to Cloud Support Engineers via email only during business hours is a feature of the Developer Support plan, not Business Support, which provides 24/7 access via phone, email, and chat. 24/7 access to a dedicated Technical Account Manager is an Enterprise Support feature and is not included in the Business Support plan. Partial access to the core Trusted Advisor checks is incorrect because Business Support customers have access to the full suite of Trusted Advisor checks, not just the seven core checks that Basic and Developer Support customers receive."},{"id":"q402","question":"A company\'s AWS workflow requires that it periodically perform large-scale image and video processing jobs. The customer is seeking to minimize cost and has stated that the amount of time it takes to process these jobs is not critical, but that cost minimization is the most important factor in designing the solution. Which EC2 instance class is best suited for this processing?","options":{"A":"EC2 On-Demand Instances","B":"EC2 Reserved Instances - No Upfront","C":"EC2 Spot Instances","D":"EC2 Reserved Instances - All Upfront"},"correct":"C","explanation":"EC2 Spot Instances offer up to 90% discounts by using spare AWS capacity, making them the most cost-effective option for workloads that are flexible on timing and can tolerate interruptions, like batch image and video processing.\\nEC2 On-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no discount, making them the most expensive option for this type of flexible, non-time-critical batch workload. EC2 Reserved Instances with No Upfront payment provide a discount for steady-state workloads over a 1 or 3-year commitment, but are not cost-effective for workloads that run only periodically and do not require continuous capacity. EC2 Reserved Instances with All Upfront payment provide the deepest Reserved Instance discount through full prepayment, but require a long-term commitment and are not the most cost-effective option for periodic workloads that qualify for Spot pricing."},{"id":"q411","question":"What does AWS Cost Explorer provide to help manage your AWS spend?","options":{"A":"Cost comparisons between AWS Cloud environments and on-premises environments","B":"Accurate estimates of AWS service costs based on your expected usage","C":"Consolidated billing","D":"Highly accurate cost forecasts for up to 12 months ahead"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Cost Explorer uses historical spending data to generate highly accurate cost forecasts for up to 12 months ahead, helping organizations plan budgets and anticipate future AWS spending.\\nCost comparisons between AWS Cloud environments and on-premises environments are produced by the AWS Pricing Calculator, which allows customers to model AWS configurations and compare projected costs against on-premises infrastructure spending. Accurate estimates of AWS service costs based on expected usage are produced by the AWS Pricing Calculator, which is designed for pre-deployment cost modeling rather than analyzing historical usage. Consolidated billing is a feature of AWS Organizations that combines usage and invoices from multiple accounts into a single bill, and is not a capability of AWS Cost Explorer."},{"id":"q413","question":"You are using several on-demand EC2 Instances to run your development environment. What is the best way to reduce your charges when these instances are not in use?","options":{"A":"Deleting all EBS volumes attached to the instances","B":"You cannot minimize charges for on-demand instances","C":"Terminating the instances","D":"Stopping the instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stopping On-Demand EC2 instances halts compute charges while preserving the instance and its EBS volumes, so you can restart later without losing your configuration or data.\\nDeleting all EBS volumes attached to the instances would destroy all data stored on those volumes, which is an irreversible action that eliminates your development environment rather than simply reducing charges while it is not in use. On-Demand instances can be stopped to reduce charges, so the claim that charges cannot be minimized is incorrect. Stopping an instance is the recommended approach for development environments that are used intermittently. Terminating the instances permanently destroys them along with any instance store data, and while it does stop all charges it is an irreversible action that would require rebuilding the development environment from scratch."},{"id":"q415","question":"Which of the following factors should be considered for Amazon EBS pricing?","options":{"A":"The size of volumes provisioned per month","B":"The compute capacity you consume","C":"The amount of data you have stored in snapshots","D":"The compute time you consume","E":"The number of Snowball storage devices you request"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Amazon EBS pricing is based on the size of provisioned volumes per gigabyte per month and the amount of data stored in EBS snapshots, which are incremental backups stored in Amazon S3 and billed separately.\\nThe compute capacity you consume is an EC2 pricing factor based on instance type and size, not an EBS pricing factor. Compute time is billed through EC2 instance pricing rather than EBS volume pricing, which charges for provisioned storage regardless of whether the instance is running. The number of AWS Snowball devices requested affects Snow Family pricing and is entirely unrelated to Amazon EBS volume or snapshot costs."},{"id":"q417","question":"Which of the following has the greatest impact on cost?","options":{"A":"Compute charges","B":"The number of services used","C":"Data Transfer In charges","D":"Data Transfer Out charges","E":"The number of IAM roles provisioned"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Compute charges and Data Transfer Out charges are typically the two largest cost drivers in AWS. Data Transfer In is free, and IAM roles have no associated cost.\\nThe number of AWS services used does not directly determine cost impact. A single heavily used service can cost far more than dozens of lightly used services, so cost impact is driven by usage and pricing rates rather than the count of services consumed. Data Transfer In charges to AWS are free for all data ingested into the AWS network from the internet or from on-premises environments, making inbound data transfer one of the few cost components that does not contribute to the bill. The number of IAM roles provisioned incurs no charges. IAM roles are free to create and there is no per-role cost, so provisioning additional roles has no impact on your AWS bill."},{"id":"q418","question":"Who from the following will get the largest discount?","options":{"A":"A user who chooses to buy On-demand, Convertible, Partial upfront instances","B":"A user who chooses to buy Reserved, Convertible, All upfront instances","C":"A user who chooses to buy Reserved, Standard, No upfront instances","D":"A user who chooses to buy Reserved, Standard, All upfront instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Reserved, Standard, All Upfront instances provide the deepest discount because Standard RIs offer larger savings than Convertible RIs, and paying everything upfront gives the maximum reduction over partial or no upfront options.\\nOn-Demand, Convertible, Partial Upfront is not a valid purchasing combination as On-Demand instances do not have Convertible or upfront payment options. On-Demand pricing simply charges for compute time used with no commitment. Reserved, Convertible, All Upfront provides a meaningful discount through a long-term commitment with full prepayment, but Convertible RIs offer less discount than Standard RIs in exchange for the flexibility to exchange attributes. Reserved, Standard, No Upfront provides a discount through the long-term commitment of Standard RIs but without any upfront payment, which yields a smaller total discount than options that include upfront payment."},{"id":"q419","question":"Which of the following is an available option when purchasing Amazon EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"The ability to bid to get the lowest possible prices","B":"The ability to register EC2 instances to get volume discounts on every hour the instances are running","C":"The ability to buy Dedicated Instances for up to 90% discount","D":"The ability to pay upfront to get lower hourly costs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Reserved Instances allow you to pay upfront in exchange for lower hourly rates over a 1 or 3-year term, providing predictable savings for steady-state workloads.\\nThe ability to bid for a lower hourly cost describes Spot Instances, which are priced based on spare EC2 capacity through a market-based mechanism rather than a commitment-based model. The ability to register EC2 instances to get volume discounts on every hour they are running is not a real EC2 purchasing option. Volume discounts in AWS apply through mechanisms such as consolidated billing or S3 storage tiers rather than through instance registration. The ability to buy Dedicated Instances for up to 90% discount is incorrect on two counts: Dedicated Instances are priced at a premium rather than a discount, and the 90% discount figure applies to Spot Instances rather than Dedicated Instances."},{"id":"q423","question":"Which tool can a non-AWS customer use to compare the cost of on-premises environment resources to AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Migration Evaluator"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Migration Evaluator is designed specifically for organizations comparing on-premises costs to AWS, providing a detailed cost-benefit analysis even before they have an AWS account.\\nAWS Cost Explorer is used to visualise and analyze spending patterns on existing AWS accounts, and requires an active AWS account to use. AWS Pricing Calculator estimates monthly costs for a planned AWS architecture but does not compare those costs against on-premises infrastructure. AWS Budgets is used to set spending thresholds and receive alerts on an existing AWS account, and has no capability to evaluate migration cost savings."},{"id":"q430","question":"Your CTO has asked you to contact AWS support using the chat feature to ask for guidance related to EBS. However, when you open the AWS support center you can\'t see a way to contact support via Chat. What should you do?","options":{"A":"There is no chat feature in AWS support","B":"The chat feature is available for all plans for an additional fee, but you have to request it first","C":"At a minimum, upgrade to Business support plan","D":"Upgrade from the Basic Support plan to Developer Support"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chat support is available starting from the Business Support plan, so upgrading to at least Business tier is the minimum requirement to access the chat feature in the AWS Support Center.\\nAWS support does have a chat feature, but it is only available on Business and Enterprise plans, making this option factually incorrect. The chat feature is not available as an add-on for an additional fee on lower-tier plans, as it is an included feature of the Business and Enterprise support tiers only. The Developer Support plan provides access to AWS Support engineers via email only, and upgrading from Basic to Developer does not unlock the chat feature."},{"id":"q432","question":"Which statement best describes the AWS Pay-As-You-Go pricing model?","options":{"A":"With AWS, you replace low upfront expenses with large variable payments","B":"With AWS, you replace low upfront expenses with large fixed payments","C":"With AWS, you replace large upfront expenses with low fixed payments","D":"With AWS, you replace large capital expenses with low variable payments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Pay-As-You-Go model replaces large upfront capital expenses with low variable payments based on actual consumption, so you only pay for the resources you use.\\nReplacing low upfront expenses with large variable payments inverts the AWS pricing model, which specifically eliminates large upfront capital costs rather than introducing new large variable costs. Replacing low upfront expenses with large fixed payments describes neither the on-premises nor the AWS model accurately. On-premises typically has large upfront expenses, and AWS replaces those with low variable costs rather than fixed ones. Replacing large upfront expenses with low fixed payments partially captures the idea of reducing upfront costs, but AWS pricing is variable rather than fixed, scaling directly with actual resource consumption."},{"id":"q434","question":"Which of the following strategies help analyze costs in AWS?","options":{"A":"Using tags to group resources","B":"Using AWS CloudFormation to automate the deployment of resources","C":"Deploying resources of the same type in different regions","D":"Configuring Amazon Inspector to automatically analyze costs and email reports"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Using tags to group and label AWS resources by project, department, or environment enables detailed cost analysis and allocation through AWS Cost Explorer and Cost and Usage Reports.\\nUsing AWS CloudFormation to automate the deployment of resources improves operational efficiency and repeatability, but automating deployments does not itself provide visibility into how costs are distributed across different parts of the organization. Deploying resources of the same type in different regions may be necessary for latency or compliance reasons, but distributing resources geographically does not help analyze which projects or teams are driving specific costs without tags to identify resource ownership. Configuring Amazon Inspector to automatically analyze costs is incorrect because Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and has no capability to analyze AWS costs or generate billing reports."},{"id":"q445","question":"Which of the following will affect how much you are charged for storing objects in S3?","options":{"A":"Using default encryption for any number of S3 buckets","B":"The number of EBS volumes attached to your instances","C":"The storage class used for the objects stored","D":"Creating and deleting S3 buckets","E":"The total size in gigabytes of all objects stored"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"S3 pricing depends on the storage class used and the total size of all objects stored. Creating buckets, using encryption, and EBS volumes do not affect S3 storage charges.\\nUsing default encryption for S3 buckets does not incur additional S3 storage charges. Server-side encryption with S3-managed keys is provided at no extra cost, and encrypting objects does not increase the storage charge beyond the standard price for the storage class used. The number of EBS volumes attached to your EC2 instances is a separate billing item for block storage and has no effect on S3 object storage charges. Creating and deleting S3 buckets does not incur any charges. AWS does not charge for the creation, management, or deletion of S3 buckets themselves, only for the data stored within them and requests made against them."},{"id":"q448","question":"You have migrated your application to AWS recently. How can you view the AWS costs applied to your account?","options":{"A":"Using the AWS Cost and Usage Report","B":"Using AWS Cost Explorer","C":"Using the AWS Pricing Calculator","D":"Using the Amazon VPC dashboard"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The AWS Cost and Usage Report provides the most comprehensive view of your actual AWS costs, breaking down charges by service, resource, and time period so you can see exactly what you are being charged for.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations and analysis of AWS spending patterns over time and is well-suited for exploring costs, but the Cost and Usage Report provides the most granular and complete raw data for detailed cost review. The AWS Pricing Calculator is used to estimate future costs for AWS services before provisioning them, and does not display actual charges already applied to your account. The Amazon VPC dashboard is a networking console for managing virtual private clouds, subnets, and network configuration, and has no connection to cost viewing or billing information."},{"id":"q449","question":"Which of the following are valid Amazon EC2 Reserved Instance types?","options":{"A":"Convertible","B":"Expedited","C":"Bulk","D":"Spot","E":"Standard"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Standard Reserved Instances provide the highest discount but are locked to a specific instance family, while Convertible Reserved Instances offer flexibility to change instance types at a slightly lower discount. Both are valid RI types.\\nExpedited is a retrieval speed option for Amazon S3 Glacier that provides faster access to archived data, and is not a Reserved Instance type. Bulk is also a retrieval tier for Amazon S3 Glacier that provides the lowest-cost but slowest data retrieval, and is not a Reserved Instance type. Spot is a separate EC2 purchasing model that uses spare AWS capacity at significant discounts and can be interrupted by AWS at any time, and is not a type of Reserved Instance."},{"id":"q456","question":"Which AWS Cost Governance best practice recommends refining workloads regularly to make the most of existing AWS resources and reduce costs?","options":{"A":"Tagging Enforcement","B":"Architecture Optimization","C":"Budgeting Processes","D":"Resource Controls"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Architecture Optimization involves regularly reviewing and refining workloads to ensure they use the most efficient AWS resource types, sizes, and configurations, directly reducing costs while maintaining performance.\\nTagging Enforcement is a cost governance practice that requires resources to be labeled with specific tags before they can be created or used, which improves cost visibility and attribution but is a compliance and visibility mechanism rather than a practice focused on refining workloads to better utilize existing resources. Budgeting Processes involve setting cost and usage thresholds and tracking actual spending against those budgets, which helps manage financial expectations but does not itself involve reviewing and optimizing underlying resource configurations. Resource Controls restrict which resources users can provision or how they can be configured, and while they prevent unnecessary resource creation they are a governance guardrail rather than a practice of regularly reviewing existing workloads."},{"id":"q472","question":"Which of the following factors affect Amazon CloudFront cost?","options":{"A":"Number of Requests","B":"Traffic Distribution","C":"Number of Volumes","D":"Instance type","E":"Storage Class"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Amazon CloudFront pricing is based on the number of requests served and the geographic distribution of traffic, as different edge location regions have different pricing tiers.\\nThe number of EBS volumes attached to EC2 instances is a separate billing item for block storage and has no effect on CloudFront pricing, which is based entirely on content delivery and request activity. The EC2 instance type running your origin servers may affect compute costs, but it does not factor into CloudFront\'s content delivery pricing, which charges for data transfer out and requests at the edge. The S3 storage class used for objects at the origin affects S3 storage costs but does not directly factor into CloudFront pricing, which is based on the delivery of content to end users."},{"id":"q477","question":"Which of the following AWS support plans provides access to only the seven core AWS Trusted Advisor checks?","options":{"A":"Business & Enterprise Support","B":"Basic & Developer Support","C":"Developer & Enterprise Support","D":"Developer & Business Support"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Basic and Developer Support plans provide access to only the seven core Trusted Advisor checks. The full set of Trusted Advisor checks requires a Business or Enterprise Support plan.\\nBusiness and Enterprise Support plans both provide access to the full suite of Trusted Advisor checks rather than only the seven core checks, so this option describes the plans with the most Trusted Advisor access rather than those limited to seven checks. Developer and Enterprise Support is an incorrect pairing because Developer Support is limited to the seven core checks while Enterprise Support has access to the full suite, meaning they are at opposite ends of the Trusted Advisor access spectrum. Developer and Business Support is an incorrect pairing because Business Support provides the full suite of Trusted Advisor checks while Developer Support is limited to the seven core checks, so only Developer Support belongs in this limited-access category."},{"id":"q482","question":"What are the benefits of the AWS Organizations service?","options":{"A":"Control access to AWS services","B":"Help organizations design and maintain an accelerated path to successful cloud adoption","C":"Manage your organization\'s payment methods","D":"Help organization achieve their desired business outcomes with AWS","E":"Consolidate billing across multiple AWS accounts"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS Organizations enables centralized control over AWS service access using Service Control Policies, and consolidates billing from multiple accounts into a single payment.\\nHelping organizations design and maintain an accelerated path to successful cloud adoption describes the AWS Cloud Adoption Framework, which is a guidance framework created by AWS Professional Services rather than a capability of AWS Organizations. Managing your organization\'s payment methods is handled through the AWS Billing and Cost Management console at the individual account level, and is not a specific feature provided by AWS Organizations. Helping organizations achieve their desired business outcomes with AWS describes AWS Professional Services, which works directly with customers on cloud strategy and implementation rather than being a function of AWS Organizations."},{"id":"q484","question":"What AWS service allows you to buy third-party software solutions and services that run on AWS resources?","options":{"A":"AWS Application Discovery Service","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Marketplace","D":"AWS Resource Groups"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Marketplace is a digital catalog where customers can find, buy, and deploy third-party software solutions that run on AWS, including AMIs, SaaS applications, and data products.\\nAWS Application Discovery Service collects information about on-premises servers and workloads to help plan cloud migrations, and is a migration planning tool with no capability to purchase or deploy third-party software. AWS Cost Explorer provides visualisation and analysis of AWS spending and usage patterns over time, and is a cost management tool rather than a software procurement platform. AWS Resource Groups organizes AWS resources into logical groups based on tags or CloudFormation stacks for easier management, and has no role in discovering or purchasing third-party software solutions."},{"id":"q489","question":"What can you access by visiting the URL: http://status.aws.amazon.com?","options":{"A":"AWS Billing Dashboard","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Health Dashboard","D":"AWS Security Hub"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS Health Dashboard at status.aws.amazon.com provides real-time and historical information about the availability and operational health of all AWS services across all Regions.\\nThe AWS Billing Dashboard is accessed through the AWS Management Console and displays cost and usage summaries for your account, and is not accessible at the status.aws.amazon.com URL. AWS Cost Explorer is a cost management tool for visualising and analyzing AWS spending patterns over time, and is accessed through the billing section of the AWS Management Console rather than the public status URL. AWS Security Hub provides a centralized view of security alerts and compliance status across an AWS environment, and is a security posture management tool accessed through the AWS Management Console rather than the public status URL."},{"id":"q492","question":"A company is migrating production workloads to AWS, and they are concerned about cost management across different departments. Which option should the company implement to categorize and track AWS spending?","options":{"A":"Use the AWS Pricing Calculator service to monitor the costs incurred by each department","B":"Use Amazon Aurora to forecast AWS spending based on usage","C":"Apply cost allocation tags to segment AWS costs by different e projects and departments","D":"Configure AWS Price List API to receive billing updates for each department automatically"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cost allocation tags are key-value metadata that you apply to AWS resources, enabling you to segment and categorize costs by project, department, or environment in Cost Explorer and billing reports.\\nThe AWS Pricing Calculator is used to estimate future costs for new AWS service configurations before deploying them, and does not monitor or categorize actual spending incurred by active workloads. Amazon Aurora is a fully managed relational database service compatible with MySQL and PostgreSQL, and is a database engine with no capability to forecast AWS spending or categorize costs by department. The AWS Price List API provides programmatic access to AWS service pricing data for building custom pricing tools, and delivers pricing information rather than automatically updating billing categories based on department usage."},{"id":"q495","question":"You have multiple standalone AWS accounts and you want to decrease your AWS monthly charges. What should you do?","options":{"A":"Try to remove unnecessary AWS accounts","B":"Add the accounts to an AWS Organization and use Consolidated Billing","C":"Track the AWS charges that are incurred by the member accounts","D":"Enable AWS tiered-pricing before provisioning resources"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Adding standalone accounts to an AWS Organization with Consolidated Billing aggregates usage across all accounts, enabling volume discounts through combined usage tiers and simplifying payment through a single bill.\\nRemoving unnecessary AWS accounts may reduce spend but does not unlock volume discounts or address the structural billing inefficiency of running separate accounts. Tracking AWS charges incurred by member accounts is a cost visibility activity and does not reduce charges on its own. AWS tiered pricing is applied automatically based on usage volume and cannot be manually enabled before provisioning resources."},{"id":"q497","question":"What does the AWS \\"Business\\" support plan provide?","options":{"A":"Access to the full set of Trusted Advisor checks","B":"Support Concierge Service","C":"Less than 15 minutes response-time support if your business critical system goes down","D":"AWS Support API","E":"Proactive Technical Account Management"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"The Business Support plan provides access to the full set of Trusted Advisor checks across all five categories, and the AWS Support API for programmatic management of support cases.\\nThe Support Concierge Service is available exclusively to Enterprise Support customers for dedicated billing and account guidance, and is not included in the Business Support plan. A response time of less than 15 minutes for business-critical system failures is an Enterprise Support feature. Business Support provides a one-hour response SLA for production system-down cases. Proactive Technical Account Management is an Enterprise Support feature. Business Support does not include a designated Technical Account Manager who provides ongoing proactive guidance."},{"id":"q501","question":"Which statement is true regarding AWS pricing?","options":{"A":"With the AWS pay-as-you-go pricing model, you don\'t have to pay any upfront fee","B":"You have no responsibility for third-party software license costs","C":"You only pay for the individual services that you need with no long-term contracts","D":"For some services, you have to pay a startup fee in order to get the service running","E":"There are no reservations on AWS, you only pay for what you use"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS pay-as-you-go pricing requires no upfront fees or long-term contracts. You pay only for the individual services you use with no minimum commitments.\\nCustomers do retain responsibility for third-party software license costs when running applications on AWS, unless they use AWS-provided licenses through services such as Amazon RDS. AWS does not take on software licensing obligations on the customer\'s behalf. For some services AWS does offer a startup fee, and Reserved Instances require upfront payment. However, the pay-as-you-go model for On-Demand services requires no startup fee. AWS does offer reservations such as Reserved Instances and Savings Plans that provide discounts in exchange for usage commitments, so the claim that there are no reservations on AWS is incorrect."},{"id":"q506","question":"What factors determine how you are charged when using AWS Lambda?","options":{"A":"Storage consumed","B":"Number of requests to your functions","C":"Number of volumes","D":"Placement groups","E":"Compute time consumed"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"AWS Lambda pricing is based on the number of requests to your functions and the compute time consumed, measured in GB-seconds of execution duration, with a generous free tier for both dimensions.\\nStorage consumed is not a Lambda pricing factor in the same way. While Lambda functions have temporary storage available in the execution environment, Lambda pricing is based on requests and compute duration rather than storage consumption. The number of volumes is an Amazon EBS pricing concept for block storage attached to EC2 instances, and has no bearing on how Lambda functions are charged. Placement groups are a feature for controlling how EC2 instances are placed on underlying hardware to optimize network performance or fault tolerance, and are an EC2 concept with no connection to Lambda pricing."},{"id":"q508","question":"Which of the following actions may reduce Amazon EBS costs?","options":{"A":"Deleting unused buckets","B":"Using reservations","C":"Deleting unnecessary snapshots","D":"Changing the type of the volume","E":"Distributing requests to multiple volumes"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Deleting unnecessary EBS snapshots removes the storage costs for backups you no longer need, and changing to a more cost-effective volume type such as moving from gp2 to gp3 reduces the per-gigabyte cost for the same capacity.\\nDeleting unused buckets is an Amazon S3 action that reduces S3 storage costs, but S3 buckets are entirely separate from EBS volumes and deleting them has no effect on EBS pricing. Using reservations is not a pricing mechanism available for EBS volumes. EBS pricing is based on provisioned storage and snapshot data rather than commitment-based reservations. Distributing requests to multiple volumes may improve performance by reducing I/O contention, but spreading data across more volumes increases total provisioned storage and therefore increases rather than reduces EBS costs."},{"id":"q511","question":"Which of the following makes it easier for you to categorize, manage and filter your resources?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS Service Catalog","C":"AWS Directory Service","D":"AWS Tagging"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS tags are key-value metadata pairs attached to resources, making it easy to categorize, filter, search, and manage resources by project, environment, owner, or any other custom criteria.\\nAmazon CloudWatch is a monitoring and observability service for collecting metrics, logs, and alarms on AWS resources, not a resource categorization or filtering tool. AWS Service Catalog allows organizations to create and manage approved catalogs of IT services for internal deployment, not for tagging or filtering existing resources. AWS Directory Service provides managed Microsoft Active Directory for user authentication and access management, unrelated to resource categorization or metadata management."},{"id":"q515","question":"Which of the following AWS Support Plans gives you 24/7 access to Cloud Support Engineers via email & phone?","options":{"A":"Developer","B":"Premium","C":"Enterprise","D":"Standard","E":"Business"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"The Business and Enterprise Support plans both provide 24/7 access to Cloud Support Engineers via phone, email, and chat, making them the two plans that meet this requirement.\\nDeveloper Support provides email access to Cloud Support Engineers during business hours only, and does not include 24/7 phone or chat access. Premium Support is not a real AWS support plan name. The actual AWS support tiers are Basic, Developer, Business, Enterprise On-Ramp, and Enterprise. Standard Support is not a real AWS support plan name. It does not correspond to any of the five AWS Support plan tiers."},{"id":"q520","question":"What are the benefits of the AWS Marketplace service?","options":{"A":"Protects customers by performing periodic security checks on listed products","B":"Per-second billing","C":"Provides cheaper options for purchasing Amazon EC2 on-demand instances","D":"Provides flexible pricing options that suit most customer needs","E":"Provides software solutions that run on AWS or any other Cloud vendor"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"AWS Marketplace offers flexible pricing options including hourly, monthly, annual, and bring-your-own-license models, and provides software solutions that can run on AWS or other cloud platforms, giving customers broad choice and cost flexibility.\\nAWS Marketplace does not perform periodic security checks on listed products. Responsibility for product security remains with the independent software vendors who list their products. Per-second billing is an EC2 pricing feature, not a benefit of AWS Marketplace. Providing cheaper options for purchasing EC2 on-demand instances is not a function of AWS Marketplace, which is a catalog for third-party software, not a mechanism for discounting AWS compute pricing."},{"id":"q522","question":"You are planning to launch an advertising campaign over the coming weekend to promote a new digital product. It is expected that there will be heavy spikes in load during the campaign period, and you can\'t afford any downtime. You need additional compute resources to handle the additional load. What is the most cost-effective EC2 instance purchasing option for this job?","options":{"A":"Savings Plans","B":"Spot Instances","C":"Reserved Instances","D":"On-Demand Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"On-Demand Instances are the best choice for short-term, unpredictable workloads where downtime is unacceptable, providing guaranteed availability for the campaign period without any risk of interruption.\\nSavings Plans provide discounts in exchange for a commitment to a consistent usage level over one or three years, and are designed for steady-state long-term workloads rather than a short-term weekend advertising campaign. Spot Instances use spare AWS capacity at significant discounts but can be interrupted by AWS at any time, making them unsuitable for a campaign where downtime cannot be afforded. Reserved Instances require a minimum one-year commitment, which would cost significantly more over a single weekend than paying On-Demand for that short period."},{"id":"q528","question":"What are some of the benefits of using On-Demand EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"They provide free capacity when testing your new applications","B":"They are cheaper than all other EC2 options","C":"They remove the need to buy “safety net” capacity to handle periodic traffic spikes","D":"They only require 1-2 days for setup and configuration","E":"You can increase or decrease your compute capacity depending on the demands of your application"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"On-Demand Instances eliminate the need to pre-purchase safety net capacity for traffic spikes, and let you increase or decrease compute capacity at any time to match application demand.\\nOn-Demand Instances do not provide free capacity for testing. All compute time is charged at the standard On-Demand rate, and there is no free usage tier specifically for testing with On-Demand instances beyond the general AWS free tier. On-Demand Instances are not cheaper than all other EC2 options. Spot Instances offer discounts of up to 90% and Reserved Instances offer up to 72% savings, both significantly cheaper than On-Demand for suitable workloads. On-Demand Instances do not require 1 to 2 days for setup and configuration. They can be launched within minutes through the AWS Management Console, CLI, or API with no lengthy provisioning process."},{"id":"q536","question":"Which AWS Cost Management tool allows you to view the most granular data about your AWS bill?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"AWS Cost and Usage report","D":"AWS Billing dashboard"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS Cost and Usage Report provides the most granular billing data available, breaking down costs by hour, service, resource, and tags for detailed analysis and auditing.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of AWS spending patterns over time with filtering and grouping capabilities, and while it is excellent for analysis and forecasting it provides less granular raw data than the Cost and Usage Report. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost governance and alerting tool rather than a granular billing data reporting service. The AWS Billing dashboard provides a high-level summary of your current month\'s charges and recent billing history, and offers a much less granular view of costs than the detailed hourly and resource-level data in the Cost and Usage Report."},{"id":"q543","question":"An administrator needs to rapidly deploy a popular IT solution and start using it immediately. Where can the administrator find assistance?","options":{"A":"AWS Well-Architected Framework documentation","B":"Amazon CloudFront","C":"AWS CodeCommit","D":"AWS Quick Start reference deployments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Quick Start reference deployments provide automated gold-standard templates that deploy popular IT solutions on AWS in minutes, giving administrators a fast path to production-ready environments built on AWS best practices.\\nAWS Well-Architected Framework documentation provides architectural guidance and best practices for designing cloud workloads across six pillars, but is a reference document rather than a deployment tool that can provision a solution immediately. Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches and distributes content to users at edge locations worldwide, and has no capability to deploy or provision IT solutions. AWS CodeCommit is a managed Git-based source control service for storing and versioning code repositories, and is a developer tool with no rapid deployment or solution provisioning capability."},{"id":"q551","question":"Which of the following security-related actions are available at no cost?","options":{"A":"Calling AWS Support","B":"Contacting AWS Professional Services to request a workshop","C":"Accessing forums, blogs, and whitepapers","D":"Attending AWS classes at a local university"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS forums, blogs, and whitepapers are publicly available security resources that anyone can access at no cost, providing guidance on security best practices, architecture patterns, and compliance considerations.\\nCalling AWS Support for security-related technical assistance requires at minimum a Developer Support plan, which has an associated monthly fee, making phone and chat support with engineers a paid service rather than a free action. Contacting AWS Professional Services to request a workshop is a paid engagement. AWS Professional Services charges for consulting time and workshop delivery rather than providing these services at no cost. Attending AWS classes at a local university is not an AWS-provided service and incurs academic fees unrelated to AWS pricing. AWS does offer its own training through AWS Training and Certification, but instructor-led courses carry a cost."},{"id":"q566","question":"Which AWS service is used to pay AWS bills, and monitor usage and budget costs?","options":{"A":"AWS Billing and Cost Management","B":"Consolidated billing","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"Amazon QuickSight"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Billing and Cost Management is the central service for paying AWS bills, monitoring usage, and managing budgets, providing tools including Cost Explorer, Budgets, and billing alerts in one place.\\nConsolidated billing is a feature of AWS Organizations that combines usage and invoices from multiple accounts into a single bill, and is a billing aggregation feature rather than a standalone service used to pay bills and monitor usage. Amazon CloudWatch collects metrics, logs, and event data from AWS services and resources to provide operational monitoring and alerting, and while it can monitor billing metrics it is a monitoring service rather than the primary service for paying bills and managing budgets. Amazon QuickSight is a serverless business intelligence service for creating interactive dashboards and visualisations from business data, and is not used to pay AWS bills or manage AWS account budgets."},{"id":"q568","question":"Which AWS feature allows a company to take advantage of usage tiers for services across multiple member accounts?","options":{"A":"Service control policies (SCPs)","B":"Consolidated billing","C":"All Upfront Reserved Instances","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Consolidated billing through AWS Organizations combines usage across all member accounts, allowing the organization to reach higher volume tiers and qualify for usage-based discounts that individual accounts would not achieve independently.\\nService Control Policies are permission guardrails applied through AWS Organizations that restrict which AWS services and actions are available within member accounts, and are a governance mechanism rather than a feature that enables usage tier benefits. All Upfront Reserved Instances are a purchasing commitment where you pay the full cost of the reservation at the start of the term in exchange for the maximum discount, and apply to a single account rather than aggregating usage across multiple accounts. AWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations and analysis of AWS spending patterns over time, and is a cost analysis tool rather than a feature that aggregates usage across accounts to qualify for volume pricing tiers."},{"id":"q582","question":"If each department within a company has its own AWS account, what is one way to enable consolidated billing?","options":{"A":"Use AWS Budgets on each account to pay only to budget","B":"Contact AWS Support for a monthly bill","C":"Create an AWS Organization from the payer account and invite the other accounts to join","D":"Put all invoices into one Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) bucket, load data into Amazon Redshift, and then run a billing report"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Creating an AWS Organization from the payer account and inviting other accounts to join enables consolidated billing, centralizing payment under a single invoice while maintaining each department\'s account independence.\\nUsing AWS Budgets on each account to pay only to budget is a cost governance practice for individual accounts, and does not consolidate the separate payment methods across accounts into a unified billing structure. Contacting AWS Support for a monthly bill is not a self-service mechanism for enabling consolidated billing, which is set up directly through AWS Organizations rather than through a support request. Putting all invoices into an S3 bucket and loading data into Redshift to run a billing report creates a custom reporting solution that provides visibility into costs but does not consolidate the actual billing and payment methods into a single invoice."},{"id":"q591","question":"Which service allows a company with multiple AWS accounts to combine its usage to obtain volume discounts?","options":{"A":"AWS Server Migration Service","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Organizations with consolidated billing combines usage across all member accounts, allowing the organization to qualify for volume pricing tiers and Reserved Instance sharing that individual accounts cannot achieve alone.\\nAWS Server Migration Service is a tool for migrating on-premises servers to AWS, and has no connection to combining usage across accounts or obtaining volume discounts. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost governance and alerting tool rather than a service that aggregates usage across accounts for volume discounts. AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and is an advisory tool rather than a service that combines usage across multiple accounts."},{"id":"q629","question":"Which AWS service provides personalized alerts when an AWS event may impact a company\'s AWS resources?","options":{"A":"AWS Health Dashboard","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS Infrastructure Event Management"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard sends proactive personalized alerts when AWS events may impact your specific resources, providing remediation guidance tailored to your account rather than showing general service status.\\nAmazon CloudWatch collects metrics, logs, and event data from AWS services and resources to provide operational monitoring and alerting for performance and health of your own workloads, but does not send personalized alerts about AWS service-level events that may affect your resources. AWS Trusted Advisor analyzes your AWS environment against best practice checks across cost, performance, security, and fault tolerance, but does not send alerts about AWS service events or infrastructure disruptions. AWS Infrastructure Event Management is a paid support program for planning large-scale events such as product launches, and is not an alerting service for AWS health events affecting your resources."},{"id":"q630","question":"Which of the following are categories of AWS Trusted Advisor?","options":{"A":"Fault Tolerance","B":"Instance Usage","C":"Infrastructure","D":"Performance","E":"Storage Capacity"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor provides recommendations across five categories: Cost Optimization, Performance, Security, Fault Tolerance, and Service Limits. Fault Tolerance and Performance are both official categories.\\nInstance Usage is not a Trusted Advisor category. Usage-related recommendations fall under Cost Optimization rather than a dedicated instance usage category. Infrastructure is not a Trusted Advisor category and does not correspond to any of the five defined recommendation areas. Storage Capacity is not a Trusted Advisor category. Storage-related checks appear within Cost Optimization or Fault Tolerance depending on the nature of the recommendation."},{"id":"q643","question":"When is it beneficial for a company to use a Spot Instance?","options":{"A":"When there is flexibility in when an application needs to run","B":"When there are mission-critical workloads","C":"When dedicated capacity is needed","D":"When an instance should not be stopped"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Spot Instances are ideal when there is flexibility in when an application needs to run, because they use spare EC2 capacity at discounts of up to 90% and can be interrupted when that capacity is needed elsewhere.\\nMission-critical workloads require guaranteed availability and cannot tolerate the interruptions that Spot Instances may experience, making On-Demand or Reserved Instances more appropriate for those use cases. When dedicated capacity is needed, either Dedicated Hosts or Dedicated Instances are appropriate depending on the licensing and compliance requirements, as they provide physical isolation that Spot Instances running on shared infrastructure cannot guarantee. When an instance should not be stopped, Spot Instances are unsuitable because AWS may reclaim Spot capacity at any time with a two-minute warning, making On-Demand or Reserved Instances the correct choice for workloads that must remain continuously active."},{"id":"q645","question":"How does AWS charge for AWS Lambda?","options":{"A":"Users bid on the maximum price they are willing to pay per hour","B":"Users choose a 1-, 3- or 5-year upfront payment term","C":"Users pay for the required permanent storage on a file system or in a database","D":"Users pay based on the number of requests and consumed compute resources"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Lambda charges based on the number of requests and the compute resources consumed, measured in GB-seconds of execution duration, making it a true pay-per-use serverless pricing model.\\nUsers bidding on the maximum price they are willing to pay per hour describes the Spot Instance purchasing model for EC2, not Lambda, which has fixed pay-per-use pricing rather than a bidding mechanism. Choosing a 1, 3, or 5-year upfront payment term describes the Reserved Instance pricing model for EC2 and RDS, not Lambda, which has no commitment-based pricing option. Paying for required permanent storage on a file system or in a database describes storage service pricing, not Lambda pricing, which is based on function execution rather than persistent storage consumption."},{"id":"q647","question":"What will help a company perform a cost benefit analysis of migrating to the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator allows companies to model on-premises or current cloud configurations alongside projected AWS configurations to produce a detailed cost comparison, making it the purpose-built tool for performing a cost benefit analysis of migrating to AWS.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of existing AWS spending and usage patterns with forecasting capability, and requires active AWS usage data to generate analysis rather than providing pre-migration cost benefit comparisons. The AWS Cost and Usage Report provides the most granular breakdown of actual AWS charges already incurred, and is a historical billing analysis tool rather than a cost benefit comparison tool for evaluating a potential migration. AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your existing AWS environment and provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and is an advisory tool for optimizing an existing deployment rather than a cost benefit analysis tool for a migration decision."},{"id":"q648","question":"Which of the following provides the ability to share the cost benefits of Reserved Instances across AWS accounts?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer between AWS accounts","B":"Linked accounts and consolidated billing","C":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) Reserved Instance Utilization Report","D":"Amazon EC2 Instance Usage Report between AWS accounts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Linked accounts under consolidated billing in AWS Organizations automatically share Reserved Instance cost benefits across all member accounts, so unused RIs in one account apply to matching usage in another.\\nAWS Cost Explorer between AWS accounts provides visualisation and analysis of spending across linked accounts but does not itself enable the sharing of Reserved Instance benefits, which is a function of consolidated billing within AWS Organizations. The Amazon EC2 Reserved Instance Utilization Report shows how effectively your Reserved Instances are being used, providing visibility into RI utilization rather than being the mechanism that enables RI benefit sharing across accounts. Amazon EC2 Instance Usage Reports show historical usage patterns for EC2 instances within an account, and are a reporting tool rather than the mechanism that enables Reserved Instance benefit sharing between accounts."},{"id":"q649","question":"A company has multiple AWS accounts and wants to simplify and consolidate its billing process. Which AWS service will achieve this?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost and Usage Reports","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"AWS Cost Explorer","D":"AWS Budgets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Organizations provides consolidated billing, combining multiple AWS accounts under a single payer account to simplify the billing process with a single invoice covering all member accounts.\\nAWS Cost and Usage Reports provide the most granular breakdown of actual AWS charges by service, resource, and time period, and are a billing analysis tool rather than a service that consolidates payment across multiple accounts. AWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of AWS spending patterns and supports filtering and forecasting, and is a cost analysis tool rather than a service that simplifies billing by consolidating multiple accounts. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost governance and alerting tool rather than a billing consolidation service."},{"id":"q655","question":"Which methods can be used to identify AWS costs by departments?","options":{"A":"Enable multi-factor authentication for the AWS account root user","B":"Create separate accounts for each department","C":"Use Reserved Instances whenever possible","D":"Use tags to associate each instance with a particular department","E":"Pay bills using purchase orders"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Creating separate AWS accounts per department isolates costs naturally as all charges within each account belong to that department, and applying tags to resources allows cost allocation by department within shared accounts through Cost Explorer reports.\\nEnabling multi-factor authentication for the AWS account root user is a security best practice for protecting account access, and has no connection to identifying or attributing costs by department. Using Reserved Instances reduces compute costs through long-term commitments, and while this benefits the overall bill it does not provide a mechanism for identifying or separating costs by department. Paying bills using purchase orders is a payment method for settling AWS invoices, and has no connection to identifying which department incurred which costs."},{"id":"q668","question":"What AWS service would be used to centrally manage AWS access across multiple accounts?","options":{"A":"AWS Service Catalog","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Organizations provides centralized management of multiple AWS accounts, including Service Control Policies that restrict which services and actions are available within member accounts, enabling governance of access across the entire organization.\\nAWS Service Catalog allows organizations to create and manage approved catalogs of IT services for internal use and self-service provisioning, and is a product and portfolio management tool rather than a service for centrally governing access across multiple accounts. AWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration of AWS resources to assess compliance and detect changes, and is a configuration auditing tool rather than a service for managing access permissions across multiple accounts. AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and is an advisory tool that operates within a single account rather than providing centralized access management across multiple accounts."},{"id":"q669","question":"Which AWS service can a customer use to set up an alert notification when the account is approaching a particular dollar amount?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost and Usage reports","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"AWS Cost Explorer","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Budgets lets you set custom spending thresholds and sends alert notifications via email or SNS when your actual or forecasted costs approach or exceed those thresholds, making it the purpose-built alerting tool for cost management.\\nAWS Cost and Usage Reports provide the most granular breakdown of actual AWS charges by service, resource, and time period, and are a billing data and analysis tool rather than a threshold-based alerting service. AWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of AWS spending patterns and supports cost forecasting, and is an analysis tool for understanding spending trends rather than a service that sends alerts when spending approaches a defined limit. AWS Trusted Advisor provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance including checks for underutilized resources, but does not send custom threshold-based alerts when account spending approaches a dollar amount you define."},{"id":"q670","question":"Which is the MINIMUM AWS Support plan that provides designated Technical Account Managers?","options":{"A":"Enterprise","B":"Business","C":"Developer","D":"Basic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Enterprise Support plan is the minimum tier that includes a designated Technical Account Manager who provides proactive guidance, acts as a primary support contact, and coordinates AWS resources on behalf of the customer.\\nBusiness Support provides 24/7 access to Cloud Support Engineers via phone, email, and chat along with a one-hour response SLA for production system-down cases, but does not include a designated Technical Account Manager. Developer Support provides business-hours email access to Cloud Support Engineers for general technical questions, and does not include a Technical Account Manager or 24/7 dedicated support resources. Basic Support provides access to documentation, whitepapers, and the seven core Trusted Advisor checks, and includes no dedicated support personnel, technical case support, or account management."},{"id":"q673","question":"Which Amazon EC2 pricing model is the MOST cost efficient for an uninterruptible workload that runs once a year for 24 hours?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Dedicated Instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"On-Demand Instances are most cost-efficient for a short, uninterruptible workload that runs once a year, since there is no long-term commitment and you pay only for the 24 hours of actual usage.\\nReserved Instances require a minimum one-year commitment, so purchasing a reservation for a workload that only runs for 24 hours once a year would result in paying for an entire year of capacity while using only a single day of it. Spot Instances use spare AWS capacity at significant discounts but can be interrupted by AWS at any time, making them unsuitable for a workload that must remain active and uninterrupted for its entire 24-hour duration. Dedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single customer for compliance and licensing isolation purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options, making them more expensive than On-Demand Instances for this simple use case."},{"id":"q677","question":"A company is considering migrating its applications to AWS. The company wants to compare the cost of running the workload on-premises to running the equivalent workload on the AWS platform. Which tool can be used to perform this comparison?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Billing and Cost Management console","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator allows companies to model the configuration of equivalent workloads on AWS and compare the projected costs against their current on-premises spending, making it the purpose-built tool for this type of cost comparison.\\nThe AWS Cost and Usage Report provides the most granular breakdown of actual AWS charges already incurred, and is a historical billing analysis tool rather than a tool for comparing projected AWS costs against on-premises spending. The AWS Billing and Cost Management console provides access to billing information, invoices, and cost management tools for active AWS accounts, and does not provide a structured way to compare on-premises infrastructure costs against projected AWS spending. AWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of existing AWS spending patterns and supports forecasting for active workloads, and requires active AWS usage data rather than providing a comparison against on-premises costs for a workload not yet on AWS."},{"id":"q685","question":"What exclusive benefit is provided to users with Enterprise Support?","options":{"A":"Access to a Technical Project Manager","B":"Access to a Technical Account Manager","C":"Access to a Cloud Support Engineer","D":"Access to a Solutions Architect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Technical Account Manager is exclusively available with the Enterprise Support plan, providing dedicated proactive guidance, coordinating AWS support resources, and acting as a primary ongoing contact for the account.\\nA Technical Project Manager is not an AWS Support resource. AWS does not offer a Technical Project Manager role through any support plan, making this an invalid option that does not correspond to a real AWS support benefit. Access to Cloud Support Engineers via phone, email, and chat is available with Business and Enterprise Support plans, and is not an exclusive benefit of Enterprise Support. Access to a Solutions Architect for architectural guidance is available to Enterprise Support customers through the TAM and architectural reviews, but Solutions Architects also engage with customers across other programs and are not exclusively an Enterprise Support benefit."},{"id":"q688","question":"Which AWS Support plan provides a full set of AWS Trusted Advisor checks?","options":{"A":"Business and Developer Support","B":"Business and Basic Support","C":"Enterprise and Developer Support","D":"Enterprise and Business Support"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The full set of AWS Trusted Advisor checks across all five categories is available with Enterprise and Business Support plans. Developer and Basic Support plans provide access to only the seven core checks.\\nBusiness and Developer Support is an incorrect pairing because Developer Support provides only the seven core checks while Business Support provides the full suite, placing them at opposite levels of Trusted Advisor access. Business and Basic Support is an incorrect pairing because Basic Support provides only the seven core checks while Business Support provides the full suite, so they are not equivalent in Trusted Advisor access. Enterprise and Developer Support is an incorrect pairing because Developer Support is limited to the seven core checks while Enterprise Support provides the full suite, so they do not share the same level of Trusted Advisor access."},{"id":"q693","question":"Which service should be used to estimate the costs of running a new project on AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Cost Explorer API","D":"AWS Budgets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator allows you to model AWS service configurations and estimate the monthly costs of running a new project before any resources are deployed, making it the purpose-built tool for pre-deployment cost estimation.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of existing AWS spending and usage patterns, and requires active AWS usage data to generate analysis rather than providing cost estimates for new projects not yet deployed. The AWS Cost Explorer API provides programmatic access to the same historical cost and usage data that the Cost Explorer console displays, and like the console it requires existing usage data rather than supporting pre-deployment project cost estimation. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost governance and alerting tool for active workloads rather than a pre-deployment cost estimation service."},{"id":"q695","question":"Which AWS service can be used to generate alerts based on an estimated monthly bill?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS X-Ray","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch can monitor estimated AWS charges and trigger billing alarms when costs exceed thresholds you define, sending notifications through Amazon SNS to alert you before your bill reaches unexpected levels.\\nAWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration state of AWS resources to assess compliance and detect configuration changes, and has no capability to generate alerts based on estimated monthly billing amounts. AWS X-Ray is a distributed tracing service that analyzes and debugs application performance by mapping requests as they flow through application components, and is a performance tool with no connection to billing monitoring or cost alerts. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity for auditing and compliance purposes, and while it logs billing-related API actions it does not generate alerts based on estimated monthly billing thresholds."},{"id":"q706","question":"What are the advantages of Reserved Instances?","options":{"A":"They provide a discount over on-demand pricing","B":"They provide access to additional instance types","C":"They provide additional networking capability","D":"Customers can upgrade instances as new types become available","E":"Customers can reserve capacity in an Availability Zone"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Reserved Instances provide significant discounts over On-Demand pricing of up to 72%, and they allow you to reserve capacity in a specific Availability Zone, ensuring that capacity is available when you need it.\\nReserved Instances do not provide access to additional instance types beyond what is available on On-Demand. The instance types available to you are the same regardless of how you purchase them. Reserved Instances do not provide additional networking capability. Network performance is determined by the instance type and configuration rather than the purchasing model. Customers cannot upgrade Reserved Instance types as new types become available unless they hold Convertible RIs, which allow exchanges. Standard RIs are locked to the specific instance type purchased."},{"id":"q707","question":"A customer runs an On-Demand Amazon Linux EC2 instance for 3 hours, 5 minutes, and 6 seconds. For how much time will the customer be billed?","options":{"A":"3 hours, 5 minutes","B":"3 hours, 5 minutes, and 6 seconds","C":"3 hours, 6 minutes","D":"4 hours"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Linux EC2 instances are billed per second with a one-minute minimum, so the customer is billed for the exact duration of 3 hours, 5 minutes, and 6 seconds.\\nBilling for 3 hours and 5 minutes would omit the 6 seconds of actual usage, which would be incorrect under per-second billing where the exact duration including seconds is charged. Billing for 3 hours and 6 minutes would round the 5 minutes and 6 seconds up to the next full minute, which was the old per-hour billing approach and does not apply to Linux instances under current per-second billing. Billing for 4 hours would apply full-hour rounding to the entire session, which is the old billing model that predates per-second billing and would significantly overcharge compared to the actual usage time."},{"id":"q714","question":"Which tool can be used to compare the costs of running a web application in a traditional hosting environment to running it on AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","D":"AWS Pricing Calculator"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator allows you to model the configuration of a web application on AWS and compare the projected costs against the equivalent traditional hosting environment costs, making it the purpose-built tool for this type of comparison.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of existing AWS spending and usage patterns, and requires active AWS usage data to generate analysis rather than providing comparisons against traditional hosting costs for workloads not yet on AWS. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost governance and alerting tool for active workloads rather than a comparison tool for evaluating migration economics. The AWS Cost and Usage Report provides the most granular breakdown of actual AWS charges already incurred, and is a historical billing analysis tool rather than a tool for comparing projected AWS costs against traditional hosting expenses."},{"id":"q715","question":"What is the value of using third-party software from AWS Marketplace instead of installing third-party software on Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"Users pay for software by the hour or month depending on licensing","B":"AWS Marketplace enables the user to launch applications with 1-Click","C":"AWS Marketplace data encryption is managed by a third-party vendor","D":"AWS Marketplace eliminates the need to upgrade to newer software versions","E":"Users can deploy third-party software without testing"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"AWS Marketplace offers flexible hourly or monthly licensing for third-party software, and enables one-click deployment that automates the launch of pre-configured software stacks without manual installation.\\nAWS Marketplace data encryption is not managed by a third-party vendor on behalf of the customer. Data encryption remains the customer\'s responsibility under the shared responsibility model regardless of whether software is sourced from Marketplace. AWS Marketplace does not eliminate the need to upgrade to newer software versions. Vendors release updated versions in Marketplace and customers must choose to subscribe to or upgrade to newer listings. Users cannot deploy third-party Marketplace software without testing. Thorough testing remains an important step in any software deployment to ensure compatibility and security, regardless of whether the software comes from Marketplace."},{"id":"q725","question":"What is AWS Trusted Advisor?","options":{"A":"It is an AWS staff member who provides recommendations and best practices on how to use AWS","B":"It is a network of AWS partners who provide recommendations and best practices on how to use AWS","C":"It is an online tool with a set of automated checks that provides recommendations on cost optimization, performance, and security","D":"It is another name for AWS Technical Account Managers who provide recommendations on cost optimization, performance, and security"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor is an online tool that runs automated checks against your AWS environment and provides actionable recommendations across cost optimization, performance, security, fault tolerance, and service limits.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor is an automated online tool rather than an AWS staff member. It does not involve human advisors providing personalized recommendations, and operates through automated checks rather than individual consultations. Trusted Advisor is an AWS-owned automated service rather than a network of AWS partners. The AWS Partner Network is a separate program of external consulting and technology firms, not a component of the Trusted Advisor service. Trusted Advisor is a self-service automated tool accessible through the AWS Management Console, and is entirely distinct from Technical Account Managers, who are dedicated human support professionals available only on Enterprise Support plans."},{"id":"q726","question":"Which AWS service or feature allows a company to visualize, understand, and manage AWS costs and usage over time?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Organizations","D":"Consolidated billing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations that let you understand, analyze, and manage your AWS costs and usage over time, with filtering, grouping, and forecasting capabilities.\\nAWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a proactive cost governance and alerting tool rather than a service for visualising and analyzing historical spending patterns. AWS Organizations provides centralized management of multiple AWS accounts including consolidated billing and Service Control Policies, and is an account governance service rather than a cost visualisation tool. Consolidated billing is a feature of AWS Organizations that combines usage and invoices from multiple accounts into a single bill, and is a billing aggregation feature rather than a tool for visualising and managing costs over time."},{"id":"q732","question":"The pay-as-you-go pricing model for AWS services:","options":{"A":"Reduces capital expenditures","B":"Requires payment up front for AWS services","C":"Is relevant only for Amazon EC2, Amazon S3, and Amazon RDS","D":"Reduces operational expenditures"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pay-as-you-go pricing reduces capital expenditures by eliminating the need to purchase hardware upfront. Instead of large investments before deployment, costs are variable and based on actual consumption.\\nThe pay-as-you-go model does not require upfront payment for AWS services. On-Demand pricing charges only after resources are consumed, which is the opposite of requiring payment before use. Pay-as-you-go pricing applies broadly across AWS services including compute, storage, databases, networking, and many others, and is not limited to EC2, S3, and RDS. Pay-as-you-go pricing reduces capital expenditures rather than operational expenditures. AWS usage fees are themselves operational expenses, so while the model shifts spending from capital to operational it does not reduce operational expenditures overall."},{"id":"q734","question":"Which AWS service enables users to consolidate billing across multiple accounts?","options":{"A":"Amazon QuickSight","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"Amazon Forecast"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Organizations enables consolidated billing across multiple accounts, providing a single payment method and combined usage that can qualify for volume discounts.\\nAmazon QuickSight is a serverless business intelligence service for creating dashboards and visualisations from business data, and has no capability to consolidate billing across multiple AWS accounts. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost governance tool rather than a service that consolidates billing across accounts. Amazon Forecast is a machine learning service that generates time-series predictions such as demand or inventory forecasts, and is an AI analytics service with no connection to billing consolidation across AWS accounts."},{"id":"q737","question":"After selecting an Amazon EC2 Dedicated Host reservation, which pricing option would provide the largest discount?","options":{"A":"No upfront payment","B":"Hourly on-demand payment","C":"Partial upfront payment","D":"All upfront payment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"All upfront payment for a Dedicated Host reservation provides the largest discount because paying the full cost at the start of the term reduces AWS\'s billing risk and earns the maximum savings over the reservation period.\\nNo upfront payment provides the lowest discount among the three payment options for Dedicated Host reservations. Without any initial payment, AWS offers less incentive compared to partial or full upfront options. Hourly on-demand payment is the most expensive option for Dedicated Hosts as it has no commitment and no discount, equivalent to standard On-Demand pricing rather than the reserved pricing tiers. Partial upfront payment splits the cost between an initial payment and ongoing monthly charges, providing a moderate discount that is greater than no upfront but less than the maximum available through all upfront payment."},{"id":"q738","question":"A Cloud Practitioner is asked how to estimate the cost of using a new application on AWS. What is the MOST appropriate response?","options":{"A":"Inform the user that AWS pricing allows for on-demand pricing","B":"Direct the user to the AWS Pricing Calculator for an estimate","C":"Use Amazon QuickSight to analyze current spending on-premises","D":"Use Amazon AppStream 2.0 for real-time pricing analytics"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator allows users to model AWS service configurations and generate detailed cost estimates before any resources are deployed, making it the most appropriate tool to direct a user to when they need to estimate the cost of a new application.\\nInforming the user that AWS uses on-demand pricing is accurate but does not help them estimate actual costs for a specific application, as it provides no tool or mechanism for calculating projected spend. Amazon QuickSight is a serverless business intelligence service for creating dashboards and visualisations from business data, and is not a tool for analyzing on-premises spending or estimating AWS costs. Amazon AppStream 2.0 is a fully managed application streaming service that delivers desktop applications to users through a browser, and has no capability for real-time pricing analytics or cost estimation."},{"id":"q747","question":"How can a company isolate the costs of production and non-production workloads on AWS?","options":{"A":"Create Identity and Access Management (IAM) roles for production and non-production workloads","B":"Use different accounts for production and non-production expenses","C":"Use Amazon EC2 for non-production workloads and other services for production workloads","D":"Use Amazon CloudWatch to monitor the use of services"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Using separate AWS accounts for production and non-production workloads isolates costs at the account level, allowing billing to naturally reflect each environment\'s consumption and making it straightforward to attribute and review costs independently.\\nCreating IAM roles for production and non-production workloads controls who can access each environment\'s resources but does not isolate or separately track the costs incurred by each environment, as IAM roles are access control constructs rather than billing boundaries. Using Amazon EC2 for non-production and other services for production workloads segments the infrastructure by service type rather than by environment boundary, which does not provide clean cost isolation and makes cost tracking more complex rather than simpler. Using Amazon CloudWatch to monitor service usage provides operational visibility into resource consumption and can trigger cost-related alerts, but monitoring usage is not the same as isolating costs between environments; CloudWatch does not create billing boundaries between production and non-production workloads."},{"id":"q758","question":"How does AWS charge for AWS Lambda usage once the free tier has been exceeded?","options":{"A":"By the time it takes for the Lambda function to execute","B":"By the number of versions of a specific Lambda function","C":"By the number of requests made for a given Lambda function","D":"By the programming language that is used for the Lambda function","E":"By the total number of Lambda functions in an AWS account"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS Lambda charges are based on the execution time of the function measured in GB-seconds and the number of requests made to trigger the function, with both dimensions incurring charges once the free tier is exceeded.\\nThe number of versions of a specific Lambda function does not affect pricing. Lambda versions are used for deployment management and rollback purposes, and maintaining multiple versions does not increase charges. The programming language used for a Lambda function does not affect its cost. Lambda charges the same rate per GB-second of execution regardless of whether the function is written in Python, Node.js, Java, or any other supported runtime. The total number of Lambda functions in an AWS account does not affect pricing. Lambda charges based on actual invocations and execution duration rather than the number of functions defined in the account."},{"id":"q762","question":"What does AWS Marketplace allow users to do?","options":{"A":"Sell unused Amazon EC2 Spot Instances","B":"Sell solutions to other AWS users","C":"Buy third-party software that runs on AWS","D":"Purchase AWS security and compliance documents","E":"Order AWS Snowball"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS Marketplace allows vendors to sell their software solutions to other AWS users, and allows buyers to purchase and deploy third-party software that runs on AWS infrastructure. Both are core functions of the platform.\\nSelling unused Amazon EC2 Spot Instances is not a function of AWS Marketplace. Spot Instance capacity is made available by AWS itself through the EC2 console based on unused compute capacity, and individual customers cannot list or sell their own Spot Instances. Purchasing AWS security and compliance documents describes AWS Artifact, which provides on-demand access to AWS compliance reports and security agreements, and is not a function of AWS Marketplace. Ordering AWS Snowball is done directly through the AWS console as part of the Snow Family service request process, and is not a procurement action available through AWS Marketplace."},{"id":"q768","question":"Which AWS service provides a quick and automated way to create and manage AWS accounts?","options":{"A":"AWS QuickSight","B":"Amazon Lightsail","C":"AWS Organizations","D":"Amazon Connect"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Organizations provides automated account creation and centralized management of multiple AWS accounts, including consolidated billing, Service Control Policies, and organizational units.\\nAWS QuickSight is a serverless business intelligence service for creating dashboards and visualisations from business data, and has no capability to create or manage AWS accounts. Amazon Lightsail is a simplified compute service for deploying virtual servers and web applications with straightforward pricing, and is a compute service rather than an account management tool. Amazon Connect is a cloud contact center service for managing customer communications via voice and chat, and is a customer engagement service with no connection to AWS account creation or management."},{"id":"q775","question":"Which AWS offering enables customers to find, buy, and immediately start using software solutions in their AWS environment?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS Service Catalog","C":"AWS Partner Network (APN)","D":"AWS Marketplace"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Marketplace is a digital catalog of thousands of software products from independent software vendors that customers can find, subscribe to, and immediately deploy into their AWS environment, often with flexible licensing models including pay-as-you-go and BYOL.\\nAWS Config is a service that continuously monitors and records resource configurations and evaluates them against compliance rules, providing governance and change management capabilities rather than a software procurement catalog. AWS Service Catalog allows organizations to create and manage approved portfolios of IT services for internal use, enabling governance and self-service deployment of pre-approved resources, but it is an internal governance tool rather than an external marketplace where customers can find and purchase third-party software solutions. AWS Partner Network is the global partner program for companies that build with or sell AWS, providing training, certification, and co-selling benefits, and is a partner ecosystem program rather than a place for customers to purchase and deploy software solutions."},{"id":"q787","question":"A company is migrating from on-premises data centers to the AWS Cloud and is looking for hands-on help with the project. How can the company get this support?","options":{"A":"Ask for a quote from the AWS Marketplace team to perform a migration into the company\'s AWS account","B":"Contact AWS Support and open a case for assistance","C":"Use AWS Professional Services to provide guidance and to set up an AWS Landing Zone in the company\'s AWS account","D":"Select a partner from the AWS Partner Network (APN) to assist with the migration","E":"Use Amazon Connect to create a new request for proposal (RFP) for expert assistance in migrating to the AWS Cloud"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"AWS Professional Services provides hands-on migration guidance and Landing Zone setup, and AWS Partner Network partners offer specialized migration consulting and implementation support, both providing the direct hands-on assistance the company needs.\\nThe AWS Marketplace team provides a platform for discovering and purchasing third-party software and services, and does not perform migrations directly into customer accounts. AWS Support cases are used to resolve technical issues and service-related problems, not to engage hands-on project delivery teams for migration work. Amazon Connect is a cloud-based contact center service for managing customer communications, and has no function related to submitting proposals or engaging migration expertise."},{"id":"q788","question":"How does the AWS Enterprise Support Concierge team help users?","options":{"A":"Supporting application development","B":"Providing architecture guidance","C":"Answering billing and account inquires","D":"Answering questions regarding technical support cases"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS Enterprise Support Concierge team specializes in answering billing and account inquiries, helping Enterprise customers navigate complex billing questions and manage account-level issues with dedicated expertise.\\nSupporting application development is the domain of AWS Developer Support, Solutions Architects, and Professional Services rather than the Concierge team, which focuses specifically on billing and account matters. Providing architecture guidance is the function of AWS Solutions Architects and the Technical Account Manager included in the Enterprise Support plan, not the Concierge team which handles billing and account inquiries. Answering questions regarding technical support cases is the role of Cloud Support Engineers, who handle technical issues via phone, email, and chat, rather than the Concierge team which is dedicated to billing and account topics."},{"id":"q791","question":"Which AWS tool is used to compare the cost of running an application on-premises to running the application in the AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","C":"AWS Pricing Calculator","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator allows you to model the configuration of an application on AWS and compare the projected costs against the equivalent on-premises running costs, making it the purpose-built tool for this comparison.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor inspects your existing AWS environment and provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and is an advisory tool for optimizing an active deployment rather than a cost comparison tool. The AWS Cost and Usage Report provides the most granular breakdown of actual AWS charges already incurred, and is a historical billing analysis tool rather than a tool for comparing projected AWS costs against on-premises expenses. AWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of existing AWS spending and usage patterns with forecasting capability, and requires active AWS usage data to generate analysis rather than providing comparisons against on-premises costs."},{"id":"q792","question":"A company has multiple AWS accounts within AWS Organizations and wants to apply the Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances benefit to a single account only. Which action should be taken?","options":{"A":"Purchase the Reserved Instances from master payer account and turn off Reserved Instance sharing","B":"Enable billing alerts in the AWS Billing and Cost Management console","C":"Purchase the Reserved Instances in individual linked accounts and turn off Reserved Instance sharing from the payer level","D":"Enable Reserved Instance sharing in the AWS Billing and Cost Management console"},"correct":"A","explanation":"To restrict Reserved Instance benefits to a single account, purchase the RIs from the master payer account and turn off Reserved Instance sharing in the billing settings, preventing the discounts from applying to other member accounts.\\nEnabling billing alerts in the AWS Billing and Cost Management console creates notifications when spending exceeds thresholds, and has no effect on how Reserved Instance benefits are shared or restricted across accounts in an organization. Purchasing the Reserved Instances in individual linked accounts and turning off RI sharing from the payer level would apply RI restrictions organization-wide rather than limiting benefits to a single specific account. Enabling Reserved Instance sharing in the AWS Billing and Cost Management console would expand RI benefit sharing across all member accounts, which is the opposite of restricting the benefit to a single account."},{"id":"q797","question":"AWS Budgets can be used to:","options":{"A":"Prevent a given user from creating a resource","B":"Send an alert when the utilization of Reserved Instances drops below a certain percentage","C":"Set resource limits in AWS accounts to prevent overspending","D":"Split an AWS bill across multiple forms of payment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Budgets supports Reserved Instance utilization budgets that send alerts when the utilization of Reserved Instances drops below a defined percentage threshold, helping customers ensure they are getting value from their RI commitments.\\nPreventing a given user from creating a resource is an access control function achieved through IAM policies or Service Control Policies in AWS Organizations, not through AWS Budgets. AWS Budgets is an alerting and monitoring tool and cannot enforce hard limits or prevent resource creation, making setting resource limits to prevent overspending an incorrect description of its capabilities. Splitting an AWS bill across multiple forms of payment is a billing and payment method configuration handled through the AWS Billing Console, and is not a feature of AWS Budgets."},{"id":"q799","question":"The AWS Trusted Advisor checks include recommendations regarding which of the following?","options":{"A":"Information on Amazon S3 bucket permissions","B":"AWS service outages","C":"Multi-factor authentication enabled on the AWS account root user","D":"Available software patches","E":"Number of users in the account"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor checks S3 bucket permissions to identify those with overly permissive public access that could expose data, and verifies that MFA is enabled on the root user account, both falling under its Security checks category.\\nAWS service outages are tracked by the AWS Health Dashboard, which provides real-time and historical status information about AWS services across all Regions, and are not a category of checks performed by Trusted Advisor. Available software patches for EC2 instance operating systems are identified by Amazon Inspector and AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager, and are not a category of recommendations provided by Trusted Advisor. The number of users in the account is visible through the IAM console and IAM credential reports, and is not a recommendation category in Trusted Advisor, which focuses on security, cost, performance, fault tolerance, and service limits."},{"id":"q817","question":"Which features or services can be used to monitor costs and expenses for an AWS account?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","B":"AWS product pages","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Billing alerts and Amazon CloudWatch alarms","E":"AWS Price List API"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"The AWS Cost and Usage Report provides the most comprehensive view of actual AWS costs broken down by service, resource, and time period, and billing alerts combined with Amazon CloudWatch alarms proactively notify you when actual or forecasted spend exceeds defined thresholds. Both are purpose-built tools for monitoring AWS costs and expenses.\\nAWS product pages describe the features, use cases, and pricing of individual AWS services, and are a reference resource rather than a tool for actively monitoring costs and expenses in your account. AWS Trusted Advisor provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance including checks for underutilized resources, but is an advisory tool rather than a direct cost and expense monitoring service. The AWS Price List API provides programmatic access to AWS service pricing data for building custom pricing tools, and is a pricing data source rather than a tool for monitoring actual costs and expenses in your account."},{"id":"q820","question":"A company wants to try a third-party ecommerce solution before deciding to use it long term. Which AWS service or tool will support this effort?","options":{"A":"AWS Marketplace","B":"AWS Partner Network (APN)","C":"AWS Managed Services","D":"AWS Service Catalog"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Marketplace offers free trials and flexible pay-as-you-go pricing for third-party software solutions, allowing companies to evaluate an ecommerce platform before committing to long-term use.\\nAWS Partner Network is a program for consulting and technology partners who build and sell AWS-based solutions, not a platform for trialling third-party software. AWS Managed Services provides operational management of AWS infrastructure on behalf of customers and does not offer third-party software trials. AWS Service Catalog allows organizations to create and manage approved catalogs of IT services for internal use, not for discovering or trialling third-party commercial software."},{"id":"q821","question":"Which AWS service should be used to create a billing alarm?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"Amazon QuickSight"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch can create billing alarms that monitor your estimated AWS charges and send notifications through Amazon SNS when costs exceed thresholds you define, making it the service used to set up billing alerts.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and while it includes checks for underutilized resources it does not create billing alarms that alert on spending thresholds in real time. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity across your AWS environment for auditing and compliance purposes, and does not create billing alarms or monitor estimated charges. Amazon QuickSight is a serverless business intelligence service for creating dashboards and visualisations from business data, and has no capability to create or manage billing alarms based on AWS spending thresholds."},{"id":"q832","question":"Each department within a company has its own independent AWS account and its own payment method. New company leadership wants to centralize departmental governance and consolidate payments. How can this be achieved using AWS services or features?","options":{"A":"Forward monthly invoices for each account. Then create IAM roles to allow cross-account access","B":"Create a new AWS account. Then configure AWS Organizations and invite all existing accounts to join","C":"Configure AWS Organizations in each of the existing accounts. Then link all accounts together","D":"Use Cost Explorer to combine costs from all accounts. Then replicate IAM policies across accounts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Creating a new AWS account and configuring AWS Organizations allows you to invite existing accounts to join, enabling centralized governance through Service Control Policies and consolidated billing under a single payer account.\\nForwarding monthly invoices and creating IAM roles for cross-account access addresses reporting and permissions separately, but does not centralize governance or consolidate payments into a single billing structure under a payer account. AWS Organizations can only have one management account within an organization, and configuring it in multiple existing accounts simultaneously is not possible. The correct approach is to configure it in a single management account and then invite others to join. Using Cost Explorer to combine cost views and replicating IAM policies across accounts provides some visibility and consistency, but does not centralize governance through SCPs or consolidate payments into a single invoice the way AWS Organizations does."},{"id":"q836","question":"Which tool is used to forecast AWS spending?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"Cost Explorer","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Cost Explorer provides cost forecasting capabilities that use historical spending data to project future AWS expenses, helping organizations anticipate and plan for upcoming costs.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor analyzes your environment and provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and is an advisory tool for optimizing existing deployments rather than a forecasting service for future spending. AWS Organizations provides centralized account management with consolidated billing and governance controls, and is an account structure and governance service rather than a tool for forecasting future AWS spending. Amazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and is a security tool with no cost forecasting capability."},{"id":"q847","question":"A Cloud Practitioner identifies a billing issue after examining the AWS Cost and Usage report in the AWS Management Console. Which action can be taken to resolve this?","options":{"A":"Open a detailed case related to billing and submit it to AWS Support for help","B":"Upload data describing the issue to a new object in a private Amazon S3 bucket","C":"Create a pricing application and deploy it to a right-sized Amazon EC2 instance for more information","D":"Proceed with creating a new dashboard in Amazon QuickSight"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Opening a detailed billing support case with AWS Support is the appropriate action to resolve billing issues, as AWS Support can investigate the specific charges, clarify billing discrepancies, and apply corrections where appropriate.\\nUploading data describing the issue to a private Amazon S3 bucket stores the information but does not submit it to anyone who can investigate or resolve the billing issue, as S3 is a storage service rather than a support channel. Creating a pricing application and deploying it to an EC2 instance for more information introduces unnecessary complexity and cost rather than addressing the billing issue through the appropriate AWS Support channel. Proceeding to create a new dashboard in Amazon QuickSight would visualise billing data but does not resolve the underlying billing issue, as QuickSight is an analytics tool rather than a support or dispute resolution mechanism."},{"id":"q848","question":"What does the AWS Pricing Calculator do?","options":{"A":"Compares on-premises costs to colocation environments","B":"Estimates monthly billing based on projected AWS usage","C":"Estimates power consumption at existing data centers","D":"Estimates CPU utilization of running instances"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator allows users to model AWS service configurations and estimate monthly costs based on projected usage, helping customers forecast AWS spending before deploying resources.\\nComparing on-premises costs to colocation environments is a function outside the scope of the AWS Pricing Calculator, which focuses on estimating the cost of AWS services rather than comparing third-party hosting options. Estimating power consumption at existing data centers is not a function of the AWS Pricing Calculator. Electricity and physical infrastructure costs are an on-premises concern that AWS abstracts away entirely for customers. Estimating CPU utilization of running instances is a function of Amazon CloudWatch, which collects and monitors performance metrics from active AWS resources, rather than the Pricing Calculator which estimates costs before deployment."},{"id":"q852","question":"A user who wants to get help with billing and reactivate a suspended account should submit an account and billing request to:","options":{"A":"The AWS Support forum","B":"AWS Abuse","C":"An AWS Solutions Architect","D":"AWS Support"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Support handles billing inquiries and account reactivation requests. Users should submit an account and billing support case through the AWS Support Center, which is accessible even without an active support plan.\\nThe AWS Support forum is a community-driven resource where customers and AWS staff exchange questions and answers, and is not the appropriate channel for resolving account suspension or submitting official billing requests. AWS Abuse handles reports of AWS resources being used for malicious activity such as spam, hacking, or unauthorised use, and is not the correct contact for billing inquiries or account reactivation. An AWS Solutions Architect provides architectural guidance and design support for building on AWS, and does not handle billing inquiries or account reactivation requests."},{"id":"q856","question":"The AWS Cost Management tools give users the ability to do which of the following?","options":{"A":"Terminate all AWS resources automatically if budget thresholds are exceeded","B":"Break down AWS costs by day, service, and linked AWS account","C":"Create budgets and receive notifications if current of forecasted usage exceeds the budgets","D":"Switch automatically to Reserved Instances or Spot Instances, whichever is most cost-effective","E":"Move data stored in Amazon S3 to a more cost-effective storage class"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS Cost Management tools break down costs by day, service, and linked account for detailed analysis, and allow you to create budgets and receive notifications when actual or forecasted usage exceeds defined thresholds.\\nTerminating all AWS resources automatically if budget thresholds are exceeded is not a standard function of AWS Cost Management tools. AWS Budgets can trigger automated actions such as applying IAM policies to restrict further provisioning, but does not automatically terminate existing resources. Switching automatically to Reserved Instances or Spot Instances based on which is more cost-effective is not a function of AWS Cost Management tools. Instance purchasing decisions require deliberate configuration rather than automatic switching based on price comparisons. Moving data stored in S3 to a more cost-effective storage class is managed through S3 Lifecycle policies configured on individual buckets, and is a storage management feature rather than a function of the AWS Cost Management toolset."},{"id":"q863","question":"Which Amazon EC2 pricing model should be used to comply with per-core software license requirements?","options":{"A":"Dedicated Hosts","B":"On-Demand Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Hosts provide visibility into the physical server\'s sockets and cores, allowing you to use existing per-core or per-socket software licenses under the Bring Your Own License model.\\nOn-Demand Instances run on shared infrastructure where the underlying physical server details are abstracted away, providing no visibility into socket or core topology required for per-core software license compliance. Spot Instances use spare AWS capacity on shared infrastructure and can be interrupted at any time, providing neither the physical server visibility required for per-core licensing nor the consistent availability needed for production database workloads. Reserved Instances are a pricing commitment model that reduces costs over a one or three-year term, but they run on standard shared infrastructure without host-level visibility and do not satisfy per-core or per-socket software license requirements."},{"id":"q868","question":"Which features and benefits does the AWS Organizations service provide?","options":{"A":"Establishing real-time communications between members of an internal team","B":"Facilitating the use of NoSQL databases","C":"Providing automated security checks","D":"Implementing consolidated billing","E":"Enforcing the governance of AWS accounts"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"AWS Organizations implements consolidated billing to combine multiple accounts under one payment method, and enforces governance through Service Control Policies that restrict which services and actions are available within member accounts.\\nEstablishing real-time communications between members of an internal team is the function of collaboration tools such as Amazon Chime or AWS Wickr, not AWS Organizations, which manages account structure and billing. Facilitating the use of NoSQL databases is the function of services such as Amazon DynamoDB, which provides managed NoSQL database capabilities, rather than AWS Organizations which is an account management service. Providing automated security checks is the function of services such as Amazon Inspector, AWS Security Hub, and Amazon GuardDuty, rather than AWS Organizations which focuses on account governance and billing consolidation."},{"id":"q870","question":"Which tool is best suited for combining the billing of AWS accounts that were previously independent from one another?","options":{"A":"Detailed billing report","B":"Consolidated billing","C":"AWS Cost and Usage report","D":"Cost allocation report"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Consolidated billing through AWS Organizations combines previously independent accounts under a single payer, simplifying payment with a single invoice and enabling volume discounts through combined usage.\\nDetailed billing reports provide a historical breakdown of charges within an individual account, and are a reporting tool rather than a mechanism for combining the billing of previously separate accounts. The AWS Cost and Usage Report provides the most granular breakdown of actual charges by service, resource, and time period, and is a billing analysis tool rather than a feature for combining billing across independent accounts. A cost allocation report breaks down charges by tagged resources to support chargeback and attribution, and is a cost categorization tool rather than a mechanism for consolidating billing from independent accounts."},{"id":"q871","question":"The AWS Pricing Calculator is used to:","options":{"A":"Receive reports that break down AWS Cloud compute costs by duration, resource, or tags","B":"Estimate savings when comparing the AWS Cloud to an on-premises environment","C":"Monitor actual AWS costs compared to estimated costs and send alerts","D":"Enable billing alerts when spending exceeds a defined threshold"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator is used to model AWS service configurations and estimate costs, enabling customers to compare projected AWS spending against their current on-premises infrastructure costs to understand potential savings.\\nReceiving reports that break down AWS Cloud compute costs by duration, resource, or tags is the function of the AWS Cost and Usage Report, which provides granular historical billing data rather than pre-deployment cost estimates. Monitoring actual AWS costs compared to estimated costs describes a combination of AWS Budgets and AWS Cost Explorer, which track real spending against targets rather than providing pre-deployment estimates. Enabling billing alerts when spending exceeds a defined threshold is the function of AWS Budgets combined with Amazon CloudWatch billing alarms, not the AWS Pricing Calculator which is used before resources are deployed."},{"id":"q874","question":"What is the MINIMUM AWS Support plan level that will provide users with access to the AWS Support API?","options":{"A":"Developer","B":"Enterprise","C":"Business","D":"Basic"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Business Support plan is the minimum tier that provides access to the AWS Support API, enabling programmatic management of support cases and integration with existing service management systems.\\nDeveloper Support provides business-hours email access to Cloud Support Engineers but does not include the AWS Support API, limiting case management to the console and email rather than programmatic access. Enterprise Support also provides access to the AWS Support API along with a dedicated TAM and faster response times, but it is a higher-cost plan than Business Support and therefore not the minimum required. Basic Support provides access to documentation, whitepapers, and the seven core Trusted Advisor checks, and does not include the AWS Support API or the ability to open technical support cases."},{"id":"q877","question":"An Amazon EC2 instance runs only when needed yet must remain active for the duration of the process. What is the most appropriate purchasing option?","options":{"A":"Dedicated Instances","B":"Spot Instances","C":"On-Demand Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"On-Demand Instances are ideal for workloads that run only when needed but must not be interrupted, since they have no long-term commitment and provide guaranteed availability for the duration of the process.\\nDedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single customer for compliance and licensing isolation purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options, making them more expensive than On-Demand for a simple use case without isolation requirements. Spot Instances can be interrupted by AWS when spare capacity is reclaimed, directly violating the requirement that the instance must remain active for the full duration of the process. Reserved Instances require a minimum one-year commitment, which is not appropriate for a workload that only runs occasionally when needed rather than continuously over a long term."},{"id":"q878","question":"Which AWS dashboard displays relevant and timely information to help users manage events in progress, and provides proactive notifications to help plan for scheduled activities?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch dashboard","B":"AWS Health Dashboard","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor dashboard","D":"AWS Systems Manager dashboard"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard provides personalized and timely information about AWS events that may affect your specific resources, proactive notifications for scheduled maintenance activities, and guidance to help you manage events in progress.\\nAmazon CloudWatch dashboard displays metrics and alarms for your own AWS resources and applications, and is an operational monitoring tool for your workloads rather than a source of personalized notifications about AWS service events and scheduled infrastructure changes. AWS Trusted Advisor analyzes your environment against best practice checks across cost, performance, security, and fault tolerance, and provides advisory recommendations rather than real-time event notifications or proactive alerts about scheduled AWS infrastructure activities. AWS Systems Manager dashboard provides operational visibility and management tools for your AWS infrastructure including patch compliance, inventory, and automation status, and is a management console for your resources rather than a notification service for AWS service events."},{"id":"q882","question":"Which AWS Support plan provides access to architectural and operational reviews, as well as 24/7 access to Senior Cloud Support Engineers through email, online chat, and phone?","options":{"A":"Basic","B":"Business","C":"Developer","D":"Enterprise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Enterprise Support plan provides access to architectural and operational reviews including Well-Architected reviews, and 24/7 access to Senior Cloud Support Engineers via email, chat, and phone.\\nBasic Support provides access to documentation, whitepapers, and the seven core Trusted Advisor checks, and does not include architectural reviews or 24/7 access to Senior Cloud Support Engineers. Business Support provides 24/7 access to Cloud Support Engineers and a one-hour response SLA for production system-down cases, but does not include architectural and operational reviews which are exclusive to Enterprise Support. Developer Support provides business-hours email access to Cloud Support Engineers for general technical questions, and does not include 24/7 access, phone support, or architectural and operational reviews."},{"id":"q892","question":"How can a company separate costs for network traffic, Amazon EC2, Amazon S3, and other AWS services by department?","options":{"A":"Add department-specific tags to each resource","B":"Create a separate VPC for each department","C":"Create a separate AWS account for each department","D":"Use AWS Organizations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Creating a separate AWS account for each department provides the cleanest cost separation, as all charges within each account belong exclusively to that department and appear as a distinct line item on a consolidated bill.\\nAdding department-specific tags to each resource enables cost attribution within shared accounts through Cost Explorer, but tagging relies on consistent human application and does not provide the hard billing boundaries that separate accounts create. Creating a separate VPC for each department isolates network resources and can improve security, but VPCs do not create separate invoices or cost boundaries as all VPC resources still appear on the same AWS account bill. Using AWS Organizations provides account governance and consolidated billing but does not itself separate costs by department. It enables visibility across accounts but the cost separation comes from having distinct accounts rather than from Organizations itself."},{"id":"q893","question":"What is a benefit of consolidated billing for AWS accounts?","options":{"A":"Access to AWS Health Dashboard","B":"Combined usage volume discounts","C":"Improved account security","D":"Centralized AWS IAM"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Consolidated billing combines usage across all member accounts in an AWS Organization, qualifying the organization for volume pricing tiers that individual accounts might not reach on their own.\\nAccess to AWS Health Dashboard is available to all AWS customers regardless of whether they use consolidated billing, and is not a benefit that consolidated billing specifically enables. Improved account security is not a direct benefit of consolidated billing. Security posture is governed through IAM, Service Control Policies, and security configurations rather than through billing structure. Centralized AWS IAM is not a benefit of consolidated billing. IAM operates at the individual account level, and centralized access management across accounts is handled through AWS Organizations policies and IAM roles rather than through consolidated billing."},{"id":"q894","question":"Which AWS service will allow a user to set custom cost and usage limits, and will alert when the thresholds are exceeded?","options":{"A":"AWS Organizations","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"Cost Explorer","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds with configurable alerts that notify you via email or SNS when actual or forecasted spending exceeds your defined limits.\\nAWS Organizations provides centralized account management with consolidated billing and governance controls, and is an account structure service rather than a service for setting custom cost thresholds and sending alerts when those limits are breached. AWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of AWS spending patterns and supports cost forecasting, and is an analysis and planning tool rather than a threshold-based alerting service. AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your environment and provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and is an advisory tool rather than a service for setting and monitoring custom spending limits."},{"id":"q896","question":"Which tool can be used to monitor AWS service limits?","options":{"A":"AWS Pricing Calculator","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Health Dashboard","D":"AWS Cost and Usage Report"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor includes checks that monitor your usage against AWS service limits and alerts you when you are approaching or have reached a limit, helping you avoid service disruptions caused by limit exhaustion.\\nThe AWS Pricing Calculator is used to estimate future costs for AWS services before provisioning them, and does not monitor current service usage against limits. AWS Health Dashboard provides personalized alerts about AWS events and scheduled maintenance that may affect your resources, and while it can notify you about limit-related events it is not the primary tool for actively monitoring service limit usage. AWS Cost and Usage Reports provide granular data about actual AWS spending broken down by service, resource, and time period, and are a billing analysis tool rather than a service limit monitoring tool."},{"id":"q902","question":"A company\'s application has flexible start and end times. Which Amazon EC2 pricing model will be the MOST cost-effective?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Spot Instances","C":"Reserved Instances","D":"Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Spot Instances offer up to 90% savings over On-Demand pricing and are ideal for applications with flexible start and end times, since they can be interrupted and restarted to take advantage of spare capacity pricing.\\nOn-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate and while they provide guaranteed availability, they do not take advantage of the cost savings available for workloads that can flex their timing around spare capacity availability. Reserved Instances require a one or three-year commitment and are designed for steady-state workloads with predictable usage, making them poorly suited for workloads with flexible schedules that do not require continuous capacity. Dedicated Hosts provide a physical server dedicated entirely to a single customer for compliance and licensing purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options rather than providing cost savings for flexible workloads."},{"id":"q904","question":"A cloud practitioner has a data analysis workload that is infrequently executed and can be interrupted without harm. To optimize for cost, which Amazon EC2 purchasing option should be used?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Spot Instances provide the deepest discounts for workloads that are infrequently executed and can be interrupted, making them the most cost-effective option when timing flexibility and interruption tolerance are present.\\nOn-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no discount, making them significantly more expensive than Spot Instances for a workload that qualifies for Spot pricing due to its interruptible and flexible nature. Reserved Instances provide discounts through long-term commitments of one or three years, but are designed for steady-state workloads running continuously rather than infrequently executed workloads where the commitment would result in paying for unused capacity. Dedicated Hosts provide a physical server dedicated to a single customer for compliance and licensing isolation purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options rather than providing cost savings for infrequent, interruptible workloads."},{"id":"q907","question":"A company with a Developer-level AWS Support plan provisioned an Amazon RDS database and cannot connect to it. Who should the developer contact for this level of support?","options":{"A":"AWS Support using a support case","B":"AWS Professional Services","C":"AWS technical account manager","D":"AWS consulting partners"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With a Developer Support plan, the developer should contact AWS Support by opening a support case through the AWS Management Console, which provides email-based assistance from Cloud Support Engineers during business hours.\\nAWS Professional Services is a team of experts that works with customers on cloud strategy, architecture, and migration engagements, and is not the appropriate channel for resolving a technical connectivity issue with an existing AWS service. A Technical Account Manager provides proactive guidance and is a dedicated contact for Enterprise Support customers only, and is not available to customers on the Developer Support plan. AWS consulting partners are external professional services firms in the AWS Partner Network, and are not the appropriate contact for technical support issues with AWS services under an active AWS Support plan."},{"id":"q911","question":"AWS Trusted Advisor provides recommendations on which of the following?","options":{"A":"Cost optimization","B":"Auditing","C":"Serverless architecture","D":"Performance","E":"Scalability"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor provides recommendations across five categories: Cost Optimization, Performance, Security, Fault Tolerance, and Service Limits. Cost Optimization and Performance are both official Trusted Advisor categories.\\nAuditing is not a Trusted Advisor category. Audit trail and compliance monitoring is handled by services such as AWS CloudTrail and AWS Config rather than being a category within the Trusted Advisor recommendation framework. Serverless architecture is not a Trusted Advisor category. Serverless design guidance is available through AWS documentation and the Well-Architected Framework rather than through automated Trusted Advisor checks. Scalability is not a Trusted Advisor category. While some Trusted Advisor checks address resource limits that could constrain scaling, scalability as a concept is addressed through the AWS Well-Architected Framework\'s Performance Efficiency pillar rather than as a dedicated Trusted Advisor category."},{"id":"q914","question":"A company operating in the AWS Cloud requires separate invoices for specific environments, such as development, testing, and production. How can this be achieved?","options":{"A":"Use multiple AWS accounts","B":"Use resource tagging","C":"Use multiple VPCs","D":"Use Cost Explorer"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Using multiple AWS accounts, one per environment, provides completely separate invoices for development, testing, and production since all charges within each account are billed independently under consolidated billing.\\nUsing resource tagging allows cost allocation by environment within a shared account through Cost Explorer, but tags rely on consistent application and do not create separate invoices. All tagged resources still appear on the same account\'s bill. Using multiple VPCs isolates network resources for each environment within the same account, but VPCs do not create separate invoices as all resources across all VPCs in an account are billed together on a single invoice. AWS Cost Explorer provides visualisation and analysis of spending patterns and supports filtering by tag or service, but is an analysis tool that reads from existing billing data rather than a mechanism for generating separate invoices per environment."},{"id":"q916","question":"What can be used to reduce the cost of running Amazon EC2 instances?","options":{"A":"Spot Instances for stateless and flexible workloads","B":"Memory optimized instances for high-compute workloads","C":"On-Demand Instances for high-cost and sustained workloads","D":"Reserved Instances for sustained workloads","E":"Spend limits set using AWS Budgets"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Spot Instances provide deep discounts for flexible, stateless workloads that can tolerate interruptions, and Reserved Instances offer significant savings for sustained workloads with predictable usage through long-term commitments. Both directly reduce the cost of running EC2 instances.\\nMemory optimized instances are designed for workloads that process large datasets in memory such as in-memory databases and real-time processing, and selecting the right instance type improves performance efficiency but does not itself reduce cost unless it right-sizes an over-provisioned instance. On-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no discount, making them the most expensive EC2 purchasing option and not a mechanism for reducing costs compared to reserved or spot pricing. Spend limits set using AWS Budgets alert you when spending exceeds a threshold but do not themselves reduce the cost of running EC2 instances. Budgets are a governance and visibility tool rather than a pricing mechanism."},{"id":"q922","question":"Which AWS Cloud service can send alerts to customers if custom spending thresholds are exceeded?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Cost Allocation Tags","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Budgets sends alert notifications when actual or forecasted spending exceeds custom thresholds you define, enabling proactive cost management before bills grow unexpectedly.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations of historical AWS spending and usage patterns with forecasting capability, and is an analysis and planning tool rather than a service that sends automated threshold-based alerts. AWS Cost Allocation Tags are key-value labels applied to AWS resources that enable cost attribution by project, team, or environment in billing reports, and are a cost categorization mechanism rather than an alerting service. AWS Organizations provides centralized account management with consolidated billing and governance controls, and is an account structure and governance service rather than a service that sends spending threshold alerts."},{"id":"q926","question":"Which Amazon EC2 pricing option is best suited for applications with short-term, spiky, or unpredictable workloads that cannot be interrupted?","options":{"A":"Spot Instances","B":"Dedicated Hosts","C":"On-Demand Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"On-Demand Instances are best for short-term, spiky, or unpredictable workloads that cannot be interrupted, since they have no long-term commitment, no upfront cost, and no risk of capacity interruption.\\nSpot Instances use spare AWS capacity at significant discounts but can be interrupted by AWS at any time, making them unsuitable for workloads that cannot be interrupted during execution. Dedicated Hosts provide a physical server dedicated to a single customer for compliance and licensing purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options, making them more expensive and unnecessary for workloads without isolation requirements. Reserved Instances require a minimum one-year commitment and are designed for predictable, steady-state workloads. Committing to a reservation for a short-term, spiky, or unpredictable workload would result in paying for capacity beyond actual usage."},{"id":"q930","question":"Which of the following allows AWS users to manage cost allocations for billing?","options":{"A":"Tagging resources","B":"Limiting who can create resources","C":"Adding a secondary payment method","D":"Running all operations on a single AWS account"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tagging resources with key-value pairs such as department names enables cost allocation tracking through AWS Cost Explorer and billing reports, allowing each resource\'s costs to be attributed to the appropriate business unit.\\nLimiting who can create resources is an IAM and Service Control Policy governance practice that controls access permissions, and while it prevents unauthorised resource creation it does not itself allocate or attribute costs to specific business units. Adding a secondary payment method changes how AWS bills are paid rather than how costs are categorized or attributed across the organization, and has no effect on cost allocation reporting. Running all operations on a single AWS account consolidates billing into one place but does not provide cost allocation by department without tags or other attribution mechanisms to distinguish which resources belong to which business unit."},{"id":"q934","question":"Which requirement must be met for a member account to be unlinked from an AWS Organizations account?","options":{"A":"The linked account must be actively compliant with AWS System and Organization Controls (SOC)","B":"The payer and the linked account must both create AWS Support cases to request that the member account be unlinked from the organization","C":"The member account must meet the requirements of a standalone account","D":"The payer account must be used to remove the linked account from the organization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"To unlink a member account from AWS Organizations, the account must meet the requirements of a standalone account, including having valid payment information, a support plan, and contact information configured independently.\\nActive compliance with AWS System and Organization Controls is not a requirement for unlinking a member account from AWS Organizations. SOC compliance is an AWS certification program rather than a precondition for account separation. Both the payer and the linked account are not required to create AWS Support cases to request unlinking. The process of removing a member account is performed directly through the AWS Organizations console by the management account or by the member account itself. The payer account can remove a linked account from the organization through the management console, but the member account itself can also leave the organization directly, so it is not strictly required that the payer account initiates the removal."},{"id":"q940","question":"Which of the following assist in identifying costs by department?","options":{"A":"Using tags on resources","B":"Using multiple AWS accounts","C":"Using an account manager","D":"Using AWS Trusted Advisor","E":"Using Consolidated Billing"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Using tags on resources enables cost allocation by department through Cost Explorer reports, and using multiple AWS accounts per department creates hard billing boundaries with separate invoices that cleanly separate costs.\\nUsing an account manager refers to a support relationship rather than a technical mechanism for identifying costs by department, and does not provide a method for attributing or separating AWS resource costs across business units. Using AWS Trusted Advisor inspects your environment for best practice recommendations across cost, performance, security, and fault tolerance, but does not provide a mechanism for identifying or attributing costs to specific departments. Using consolidated billing combines usage from multiple accounts into a single invoice, which is the billing aggregation mechanism that enables the separation achieved by multiple accounts to be viewed together, rather than being an identification mechanism on its own."},{"id":"q942","question":"Which AWS service allows for effective cost management of multiple AWS accounts?","options":{"A":"AWS Organizations","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Amazon Connect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Organizations provides consolidated billing and Service Control Policies for effective cost management across multiple AWS accounts, enabling volume discounts through combined usage and governance controls to prevent overspending.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor inspects your existing AWS environment and provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, but operates primarily within individual accounts rather than providing a centralized cost management structure across multiple accounts. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private network connection between on-premises infrastructure and AWS, and is a networking service with no capability for managing costs across multiple AWS accounts. Amazon Connect is a cloud contact center service for managing customer communications via voice and chat, and is a customer engagement service entirely unrelated to AWS account cost management."},{"id":"q943","question":"A company is piloting a new customer-facing application on Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) for one month. What pricing model is appropriate?","options":{"A":"Reserved Instances","B":"Spot Instances","C":"On-Demand Instances","D":"Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"On-Demand Instances are appropriate for a one-month pilot because they require no long-term commitment, the customer-facing application cannot risk interruption from Spot reclamation, and the short duration does not justify a Reserved Instance term.\\nReserved Instances require a minimum one-year commitment, making them entirely unsuitable for a one-month pilot where the reservation term would far exceed the intended usage period. Spot Instances can be interrupted by AWS when spare capacity is reclaimed, which is unacceptable for a customer-facing application that must remain consistently available throughout the pilot period. Dedicated Hosts provide physical server isolation for compliance and licensing purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options, making them unnecessarily expensive for a standard one-month pilot application."},{"id":"q944","question":"Which AWS tools automatically forecast future AWS costs?","options":{"A":"AWS Support Center","B":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","C":"AWS Pricing Calculator","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Cost Explorer uses historical spending data and machine learning to automatically generate cost forecasts for up to 12 months ahead, making it the AWS tool that automatically forecasts future costs.\\nAWS Support Center is the portal for creating, viewing, and managing AWS support cases, and has no connection to cost forecasting or financial analysis. The AWS Cost and Usage Report provides the most granular breakdown of actual AWS charges already incurred, and is a historical billing analysis tool rather than a forecasting service. The AWS Pricing Calculator allows users to model AWS service configurations and estimate costs for new deployments, but requires manual input and does not automatically generate forecasts based on existing usage patterns the way Cost Explorer does."},{"id":"q946","question":"A user is able to set up a master payer account to view consolidated billing reports through:","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"Amazon Macie","C":"Amazon QuickSight","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Organizations enables a master payer account to view consolidated billing reports across all member accounts, providing a unified view of spending and usage with the ability to apply governance policies.\\nAWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost governance and alerting tool rather than the service that enables consolidated billing report access through a master payer account. Amazon Macie is a data security service that uses machine learning to discover and protect sensitive data stored in Amazon S3, and is a security service with no connection to consolidated billing or master payer account reporting. Amazon QuickSight is a serverless business intelligence service for creating dashboards and visualisations from business data, and while it can visualise billing data it is not itself the service that enables consolidated billing through a master payer account structure."},{"id":"q949","question":"What is a characteristic of Convertible Reserved Instances (RIs)?","options":{"A":"Users can exchange Convertible RIs for other Convertible RIs from a different instance family with an equal or higher value than the Convertible Reserved Instances that you are exchanging","B":"Users can exchange Convertible RIs for other Convertible RIs in different AWS Regions","C":"Users can sell and buy Convertible RIs on the AWS Marketplace","D":"Users can shorten the term of their Convertible RIs by merging them with other Convertible RIs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Convertible Reserved Instances can be exchanged for other Convertible RIs from a different instance family, as long as the new RI has an equal or higher value, providing flexibility to adapt to changing needs.\\nConvertible RIs are Region-specific and cannot be exchanged for RIs in a different AWS Region. Only Standard Reserved Instances can be listed on the AWS Reserved Instance Marketplace, not Convertible RIs. Merging Convertible RIs to shorten their term is not a supported feature, as RI terms are fixed at one or three years and cannot be modified by combining reservations."},{"id":"q950","question":"An architecture design includes Amazon EC2, an Elastic Load Balancer, and Amazon RDS. What is the BEST way to get a monthly cost estimation for this architecture?","options":{"A":"Open an AWS Support case, provide the architecture proposal, and ask for a monthly cost estimation","B":"Collect the published prices of the AWS services and calculate the monthly estimate","C":"Use the AWS Pricing Calculator to estimate the monthly cost","D":"Use the AWS Cost and Usage Report to estimate the monthly cost"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator allows you to model the specific configuration of your EC2 instances, Elastic Load Balancer, and RDS database to generate a detailed and accurate monthly cost estimate before deployment.\\nOpening an AWS Support case to request a cost estimation is unnecessarily complex and time-consuming when a self-service tool like the AWS Pricing Calculator provides instant, detailed estimates without requiring support engagement. Collecting published prices and calculating manually is possible but error-prone and inefficient compared to the AWS Pricing Calculator, which automatically accounts for all pricing dimensions including data transfer, storage, and regional pricing variations. The AWS Cost and Usage Report provides granular data about actual AWS charges already incurred on active resources, and cannot be used to estimate the monthly cost of an architecture that has not yet been deployed."},{"id":"q956","question":"A user has a stateful workload that will run on Amazon EC2 for the next 3 years. What is the MOST cost-effective pricing model for this workload?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"Dedicated Instances","D":"Spot Instances"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Reserved Instances provide the most cost-effective pricing for a 3-year stateful workload with predictable, steady-state usage, offering up to 72% discount over On-Demand through a long-term commitment.\\nOn-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no discount, making them the most expensive option for a workload running continuously for three years where the cost reduction from Reserved Instances would be substantial. Dedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single customer for compliance and licensing purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options rather than providing cost savings for a standard stateful workload. Spot Instances can be interrupted by AWS when spare capacity is reclaimed, making them unsuitable for a stateful workload that must remain continuously active and retain its state throughout the three-year period."},{"id":"q957","question":"A cloud practitioner needs an Amazon EC2 instance to launch and run for 7 hours without interruptions. What is the most suitable and cost-effective option for this task?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instance","B":"Reserved Instance","C":"Dedicated Host","D":"Spot Instance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"On-Demand Instances are most suitable for a short 7-hour task that cannot be interrupted, providing guaranteed availability with no long-term commitment and no risk of interruption during execution.\\nReserved Instances require a minimum one-year commitment, making them entirely unsuitable for a single 7-hour task where the commitment period would vastly exceed the actual usage duration. Dedicated Hosts provide physical server isolation for compliance and licensing purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options, making them unnecessarily expensive for a straightforward 7-hour task without isolation requirements. Spot Instances can be interrupted by AWS when spare capacity is reclaimed, directly violating the requirement that the instance must run for 7 hours without interruptions."},{"id":"q958","question":"Which of the following are benefits of using AWS Trusted Advisor?","options":{"A":"Providing high-performance container orchestration","B":"Creating and rotating encryption keys","C":"Detecting underutilized resources to save costs","D":"Improving security by proactively monitoring the AWS environment","E":"Implementing enforced tagging across AWS resources"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor detects underutilized resources such as idle EC2 instances and unused load balancers to help reduce costs, and proactively monitors the AWS environment for security vulnerabilities and misconfigurations, making both cost optimization and security improvement core benefits of the service.\\nProviding high-performance container orchestration describes Amazon ECS or Amazon EKS, which are compute services for running containerised workloads, and is entirely unrelated to Trusted Advisor. Creating and rotating encryption keys describes AWS Key Management Service, which manages cryptographic keys for data protection, and is not a function of Trusted Advisor. Implementing enforced tagging across AWS resources describes a governance capability achieved through AWS Organizations Service Control Policies or AWS Config rules, and is not something Trusted Advisor can enforce."},{"id":"q965","question":"What tool enables customers without an AWS account to estimate costs for almost all AWS services?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Pricing Calculator"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The AWS Pricing Calculator is publicly accessible without an AWS account, allowing anyone to model AWS service configurations and generate cost estimates for almost all AWS services before committing to any deployment.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides visualisation and analysis of actual AWS spending patterns and requires an active AWS account with usage history to generate meaningful data. The AWS Cost and Usage Report provides granular billing data for active AWS accounts, and requires an AWS account to be configured and generating charges before it can provide any data. AWS Budgets requires an active AWS account to set up cost and usage thresholds and send alerts, and cannot be used by someone without an account to estimate costs for AWS services."},{"id":"q967","question":"Which pricing model would result in maximum Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) savings for a database server that must be online for one year?","options":{"A":"Spot Instance","B":"On-Demand Instance","C":"Partial Upfront Reserved Instance","D":"No Upfront Reserved Instance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A Partial Upfront Reserved Instance for one year provides meaningful savings over On-Demand while requiring less cash upfront than All Upfront, making it a cost-effective option for a database that must remain online continuously for a year.\\nSpot Instances can be interrupted by AWS when spare capacity is reclaimed, making them entirely unsuitable for a database server that must remain online continuously for a full year. On-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no discount, making them significantly more expensive than Reserved Instances for a workload running continuously for a year with a predictable steady-state usage pattern. No Upfront Reserved Instances also provide a discount over On-Demand for a one-year commitment, but the discount is shallower than Partial Upfront because AWS does not receive any payment at the start of the term and therefore offers less incentive."},{"id":"q973","question":"AnyCompany recently purchased Example Corp. Both companies use AWS resources, and AnyCompany wants a single aggregated bill. Which option allows AnyCompany to receive a single bill?","options":{"A":"Example Corp. must submit a request to its AWS Solutions Architect or AWS Technical Account Manager to link the accounts and consolidate billing","B":"AnyCompany must create a new support case in the AWS Support Center requesting that both bills be combined","C":"Send an invitation to join the organization from AnyCompany\'s AWS Organizations master account to Example Corp","D":"Migrate the Example Corp. VPCs, Amazon EC2 instances, and other resources into the AnyCompany AWS account"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sending an invitation from AnyCompany\'s AWS Organizations master account to Example Corp adds them to the organization, enabling consolidated billing under a single invoice and combined usage for volume discounts.\\nRequesting that an AWS solutions architect or TAM link accounts is not the self-service process for joining AWS Organizations. Account consolidation is configured directly through the AWS Organizations console without requiring AWS staff intervention. Creating a new support case requesting that both bills be combined is not the process for enabling consolidated billing, which is configured through AWS Organizations rather than through support requests. Migrating Example Corp\'s VPCs, EC2 instances, and other resources into AnyCompany\'s account would merge the infrastructure into a single account, which is a complex and disruptive migration entirely unnecessary when AWS Organizations can combine billing while keeping accounts separate."},{"id":"q974","question":"Which tool can be used to create alerts when the actual or forecasted cost of AWS services exceeds a certain threshold?","options":{"A":"Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Budgets creates alerts when actual or forecasted costs exceed defined thresholds, sending notifications via email or SNS to help you manage spending proactively before bills reach unexpected levels.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations and analysis of historical AWS spending patterns with forecasting capability, and is an analysis tool rather than a threshold-based alerting service that sends notifications when costs exceed limits. The AWS Cost and Usage Report provides the most granular breakdown of actual charges by service, resource, and time period, and is a historical billing data tool rather than a service that creates and sends cost threshold alerts. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity across your AWS environment for auditing and compliance purposes, and does not monitor billing or send alerts when spending exceeds defined cost thresholds."},{"id":"q976","question":"Which AWS Trusted Advisor check is available to all AWS users?","options":{"A":"Core checks","B":"All checks","C":"Cost optimization checks","D":"Fault tolerance checks"},"correct":"A","explanation":"All AWS users, including those on the Basic free support plan, have access to the seven core Trusted Advisor checks covering security and service limits, providing a baseline level of advisory guidance regardless of support tier.\\nAccess to all Trusted Advisor checks requires a Business or Enterprise Support plan. Basic and Developer Support users are limited to the seven core checks and cannot access the full suite of recommendations across all five categories. Cost optimization checks are part of the full Trusted Advisor suite that requires Business or Enterprise Support, and are not available to all AWS users on the Basic plan. Fault tolerance checks are part of the full Trusted Advisor suite that requires Business or Enterprise Support, and are not available to all AWS users. Only the seven core checks spanning basic security and service limits are universally available."},{"id":"q982","question":"A workload on AWS will run for the foreseeable future by using a consistent number of Amazon EC2 instances. What pricing model will minimize cost while ensuring that compute resources remain available?","options":{"A":"Dedicated Hosts","B":"On-Demand Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Reserved Instances are the most cost-effective option for a consistent, long-running workload, providing significant discounts of up to 72% over On-Demand pricing in exchange for a one or three-year commitment.\\nDedicated Hosts provide physical server isolation for compliance and licensing purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options, making them more expensive rather than minimizing cost for a standard consistent workload. On-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no discount, making them the most expensive option for a workload running continuously at a consistent level where Reserved Instance savings would be substantial over time. Spot Instances can be interrupted by AWS when spare capacity is reclaimed, and the question specifies that compute resources must remain available, making Spot Instances unsuitable despite their lower cost."},{"id":"q983","question":"Which tool can be used to identify scheduled changes to the AWS infrastructure?","options":{"A":"AWS Health Dashboard","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"Billing Dashboard","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard provides personalized proactive notifications about scheduled maintenance, infrastructure changes, and lifecycle events that may affect your specific AWS resources, making it the purpose-built tool for identifying planned changes to AWS infrastructure.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor analyzes your AWS environment against best practice checks across cost, performance, security, and fault tolerance, and provides advisory recommendations rather than notifications about scheduled AWS infrastructure changes. The Billing Dashboard displays cost and usage summaries for your AWS account, and is a financial reporting tool with no connection to identifying scheduled infrastructure changes. AWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration state of your AWS resources to assess compliance and detect changes you make, and does not provide information about scheduled changes to the AWS infrastructure itself."},{"id":"q986","question":"A company wants to be notified when its AWS Cloud costs or usage exceed defined thresholds. Which AWS service will support these requirements?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"Cost Explorer","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"Amazon Macie"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Budgets sends notifications when AWS costs or usage exceed custom-defined thresholds, enabling proactive spending management with alerts delivered via email or Amazon SNS.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides visualisation and analysis of historical AWS spending and usage patterns with forecasting capability, and is an analysis tool rather than a threshold-based notification service that alerts when spending exceeds defined limits. AWS CloudTrail records API calls and account activity for auditing and compliance purposes, and does not monitor billing thresholds or send notifications when spending exceeds defined cost or usage limits. Amazon Macie is a data security service that uses machine learning to discover and protect sensitive data stored in Amazon S3, and is a security service entirely unrelated to cost monitoring or spending threshold notifications."},{"id":"q988","question":"Which AWS service allows customers to purchase unused Amazon EC2 capacity at an often discounted rate?","options":{"A":"Reserved Instances","B":"On-Demand Instances","C":"Dedicated Instances","D":"Spot Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spot Instances allow customers to purchase unused EC2 capacity at discounts of up to 90% compared to On-Demand pricing, with the trade-off that AWS may reclaim that capacity when needed.\\nReserved Instances provide discounts through long-term commitments of one or three years and guarantee that capacity is available for the duration of the reservation, making them designed for steady predictable workloads rather than for purchasing unused spare capacity. On-Demand Instances are charged at the standard AWS rate with no discount, as they are the baseline pricing model that customers pay for guaranteed access to compute capacity on demand. Dedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single customer for compliance and licensing isolation purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options rather than providing access to unused capacity at a discount."},{"id":"q1000","question":"A company is running a self-managed Oracle database directly on Amazon EC2 for its steady-state database. The company wants to reduce compute costs. Which option should the company use to maximize savings over a 3-year term?","options":{"A":"EC2 Dedicated Instances","B":"EC2 Spot Instances","C":"EC2 Reserved Instances","D":"EC2 On-Demand Instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"EC2 Reserved Instances provide the maximum savings for a steady-state 3-year workload, offering up to 72% discount over On-Demand pricing through a long-term commitment that matches the predictable usage pattern of a self-managed Oracle database.\\nEC2 Dedicated Instances run on hardware dedicated to a single customer for compliance and licensing purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options rather than maximizing savings for a long-running database workload. EC2 Spot Instances can be interrupted by AWS when spare capacity is reclaimed, making them entirely unsuitable for a production Oracle database that must remain continuously available for steady-state operations. EC2 On-Demand Instances charge the full hourly rate with no discount, making them the most expensive option for a workload running continuously for three years where the substantial Reserved Instance savings would be foregone."},{"id":"q1002","question":"What are the benefits of consolidated billing for AWS Cloud services?","options":{"A":"Volume discounts","B":"A minimal additional fee for use","C":"One bill for multiple accounts","D":"Installment payment options","E":"Custom cost and usage budget creation"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Consolidated billing provides volume discounts by aggregating usage across all accounts to qualify for tiered pricing, and delivers a single combined invoice covering all member accounts in the organization.\\nA minimal additional fee for use is incorrect because consolidated billing itself is provided at no additional charge by AWS Organizations. The billing consolidation feature does not carry a separate per-account or per-usage fee. Instalment payment options are not a feature of consolidated billing. AWS charges are billed monthly based on actual usage, and there are no instalment or deferred payment arrangements associated with consolidated billing. Custom cost and usage budget creation is a feature of AWS Budgets, which is a separate cost management service that allows you to set spending thresholds and receive alerts, not a benefit of consolidated billing itself."},{"id":"q1003","question":"A company is expecting a short-term spike in internet traffic for its application. During the traffic increase, the application cannot be interrupted. The company also needs to minimize cost and maximize flexibility. Which Amazon EC2 instance type should the company use to meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Spot Instances","C":"Reserved Instances","D":"Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"On-Demand Instances are best for short-term traffic spikes that cannot be interrupted, providing maximum flexibility with no commitment and no risk of capacity interruption during the surge period.\\nSpot Instances use spare AWS capacity at significant discounts but can be interrupted at any time, directly violating the requirement that the application cannot be interrupted during the traffic spike. Reserved Instances require a one or three-year commitment and are designed for steady-state predictable workloads, making them a poor fit for handling an unexpected short-term traffic spike where the commitment would far exceed the actual usage period. Dedicated Hosts provide physical server isolation for compliance and licensing purposes at a premium price, and are unnecessary for handling a temporary traffic spike that has no isolation or licensing requirements."},{"id":"q1009","question":"How can AWS enable a company to control expenses as an application\'s usage changes unpredictably?","options":{"A":"AWS will refund the cost difference if a customer moves to larger servers","B":"The application can be built to scale up or down automatically as resources are needed","C":"Spot instances will automatically be used if the price is lower than on-demand instances","D":"Amazon CloudWatch will automatically predict what resources are needed"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS enables expense control through auto-scaling, which automatically adjusts resources up or down based on actual demand, ensuring that you only pay for what is actually needed as usage changes unpredictably.\\nAWS does not refund the cost difference if a customer moves to larger servers. Instance resizing is a configuration change that affects future charges, and there is no refund mechanism for moving between instance sizes. Spot Instances are not automatically used when their price is lower than On-Demand. Using Spot Instances requires deliberate configuration through launch templates, Auto Scaling groups, or Spot Fleet requests rather than automatic switching. Amazon CloudWatch monitors metrics and can trigger scaling actions through Auto Scaling policies, but does not itself automatically predict or provision the specific resources needed. Prediction requires configuring predictive scaling or target tracking policies rather than being a default CloudWatch behavior."},{"id":"q1019","question":"A company wants to use machine learning to analyze the configuration and utilization of their AWS resources and receive recommendations to right-size their workloads. Which AWS service provides this capability?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Compute Optimizer","D":"AWS Budgets"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Compute Optimizer uses machine learning to analyze historical utilization metrics and provide recommendations for optimal EC2 instance types, right-sizing suggestions, and configuration improvements to reduce costs while maintaining performance.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor provides recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance based on best practice checks, and while it can flag underutilized instances it does not use machine learning to analyze detailed utilization patterns for granular right-sizing recommendations. AWS Cost Explorer provides visualisation and analysis of spending and usage patterns with forecasting capability, and while it includes a right-sizing recommendation feature it is primarily a cost analysis tool rather than a machine learning-based resource optimization service. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost governance and alerting tool rather than a machine learning-based service for analyzing resource configuration and utilization."},{"id":"q1020","question":"Which AWS service analyzes the configuration and utilization metrics of Amazon EC2 instances and provides recommendations for optimal instance types?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"AWS Compute Optimizer","C":"AWS Config","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Compute Optimizer recommends optimal AWS resource configurations by analyzing utilization data using machine learning models, providing specific instance type recommendations based on actual CPU, memory, and network usage patterns.\\nAmazon Inspector is an automated security assessment service that scans EC2 instances and container images for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure, and is a security tool with no capability to analyze utilization metrics or recommend optimal instance types. AWS Config continuously tracks and records the configuration state of AWS resources to assess compliance against desired settings, and focuses on configuration compliance rather than analyzing performance metrics to recommend right-sized instance types. AWS Cost Explorer provides visualisation and analysis of spending and usage patterns and includes a basic right-sizing recommendation feature, but is primarily a cost management tool rather than a dedicated machine learning-based service for EC2 instance optimization."},{"id":"q1021","question":"A company wants to automatically detect unusual spending patterns in their AWS account and receive alerts when anomalies are found. Which AWS service uses machine learning to monitor costs and detect anomalies?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"AWS Cost Anomaly Detection","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon GuardDuty"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Cost Anomaly Detection uses machine learning to continuously monitor cost and usage patterns and detect unusual spending that deviates from expected norms, sending alerts when anomalies are identified without requiring manual threshold configuration.\\nAWS Budgets allows you to set custom spending and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, but requires you to manually define the expected spending levels rather than automatically detecting anomalies using machine learning. AWS Trusted Advisor provides best practice recommendations across security, cost, performance, and fault tolerance, and while it flags some cost inefficiencies it does not continuously monitor spending patterns to automatically detect unusual cost anomalies. Amazon GuardDuty is a threat detection service that continuously monitors AWS accounts and workloads for malicious activity and anomalous behavior using machine learning, and is a security threat detection service rather than a cost anomaly monitoring service."},{"id":"q1022","question":"Which AWS service leverages machine learning models to identify unexpected changes in AWS spending without requiring users to define spending thresholds?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"AWS Cost Anomaly Detection","D":"AWS Pricing Calculator"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Cost Anomaly Detection automatically learns your spending patterns and uses machine learning to identify unexpected cost increases without requiring users to define specific thresholds, sending alerts when anomalies are detected.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations and analysis of historical spending with forecasting capability, and while it shows spending trends it does not automatically detect anomalies or send alerts without user-defined thresholds. AWS Budgets requires users to define specific cost and usage thresholds before it can send alerts, meaning it cannot identify unexpected changes without prior manual configuration of expected spending levels. The AWS Pricing Calculator is used to estimate future costs for planned AWS deployments before resources are provisioned, and has no capability to monitor active spending patterns or detect unexpected cost changes."},{"id":"q1040","question":"Which AWS pricing model provides flexible pricing with savings of up to 72% on compute usage, regardless of instance family, size, OS, or tenancy?","options":{"A":"Reserved Instances","B":"Spot Instances","C":"Savings Plans","D":"Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Savings Plans offer flexible pricing with up to 72% savings on compute usage in exchange for a one or three-year commitment to a consistent spend level, applying automatically across EC2 instance families, sizes, operating systems, and tenancy.\\nReserved Instances provide significant discounts but are locked to a specific instance family, size, operating system, and region unless they are Convertible RIs, making them less flexible than Savings Plans which apply automatically across these dimensions. Spot Instances offer discounts of up to 90% by using spare AWS capacity, but can be interrupted by AWS at any time and do not provide the consistent availability or flexibility across instance families that Savings Plans offer. Dedicated Hosts provide physical server isolation for compliance and licensing purposes, and are priced at a premium over standard EC2 options rather than providing the flexible compute savings that Savings Plans are designed to deliver."},{"id":"q1041","question":"What is a key difference between AWS Savings Plans and Reserved Instances?","options":{"A":"Savings Plans require a 5-year commitment while Reserved Instances require 1 or 3 years","B":"Savings Plans apply across instance families and Regions while Standard Reserved Instances are specific to an instance type and Region","C":"Reserved Instances offer greater discounts than Savings Plans","D":"Savings Plans can only be used with Amazon EC2 while Reserved Instances work with all compute services"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Compute Savings Plans provide flexibility across instance families, sizes, operating systems, tenancy, and regions, while Standard Reserved Instances are locked to a specific instance type, operating system, and region.\\nSavings Plans are available with one or three-year commitments, not a five-year commitment. Both Savings Plans and Reserved Instances are available with the same one and three-year term options. Reserved Instances do not offer greater discounts than Savings Plans as a general rule. Both can offer similar discount levels, and Compute Savings Plans can match or exceed the discounts of many Reserved Instance configurations while offering greater flexibility. Savings Plans apply to EC2 instances, AWS Fargate, and AWS Lambda compute usage, not exclusively to EC2. Reserved Instances are also available for services beyond EC2 including RDS and ElastiCache, so neither option is exclusively limited to EC2."},{"id":"q1055","question":"A company with multiple business units wants to map AWS costs to each department for internal chargeback. Which AWS feature allows them to create custom cost categories?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Cost Categories","C":"AWS Cost Allocation Tags","D":"AWS Budgets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Cost Categories allows you to create custom groupings of your cost and usage data by mapping costs into meaningful categories aligned with your business structure, enabling chargeback and showback reporting for internal departments.\\nAWS Cost Explorer provides interactive visualisations and analysis of spending patterns with filtering capabilities, and while it can display costs by tag it does not provide a dedicated mechanism for creating custom named cost categories for chargeback mapping. AWS Cost Allocation Tags enable you to label individual resources with department metadata so their costs appear separately in billing reports, but tags operate at the resource level and require consistent application rather than providing a higher-level category mapping framework. AWS Budgets lets you set custom cost and usage thresholds and receive alerts when those thresholds are exceeded, and is a cost governance and alerting tool rather than a service for creating custom cost category structures for chargeback purposes."},{"id":"q1089","question":"A company has the AWS Business Support plan and opens a support case because its production workload is experiencing degraded performance but has not completely stopped. What is the target initial response time for this case?","options":{"A":"Less than 24 hours","B":"Less than 12 hours","C":"Less than 4 hours","D":"Less than 1 hour"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Under the AWS Business Support plan, a production system impaired case, where the workload is experiencing degraded performance but has not completely stopped, has a target initial response time of less than 4 hours, reflecting the elevated urgency of an affected production environment that has not yet reached the highest severity level.\\nLess than 24 hours is the target response time for general guidance cases under the Business Support plan, which apply to questions and requests that do not involve any impact to a running production system and represent the lowest priority level. Less than 12 hours is the target response time for a system impaired case under the AWS Developer Support plan, and is not the correct SLA for production system impaired cases under the Business Support plan, which provides faster response times than Developer Support for production-affecting issues. Less than 1 hour is the target response time for a production system down case under the Business Support plan, which applies when a production workload has completely stopped functioning and represents a higher severity level than production system impaired."},{"id":"q1090","question":"Which of the following is a key difference between the AWS Developer Support plan and the AWS Business Support plan?","options":{"A":"Developer Support includes a Technical Account Manager; Business Support does not","B":"Business Support provides 24/7 access to Cloud Support Engineers via phone, email, and chat; Developer Support does not","C":"Developer Support provides access to the full set of AWS Trusted Advisor checks; Business Support provides access to only the seven core checks","D":"Business Support is only available to members of the AWS Partner Network"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Business Support provides 24/7 access to Cloud Support Engineers through phone, email, and chat channels, while AWS Developer Support provides access to Cloud Support Associates only during business hours and only via email, making round-the-clock multi-channel technical support a key differentiator of the Business plan.\\nA Technical Account Manager (TAM) is exclusively available to customers on the AWS Enterprise Support and Enterprise On-Ramp plans and is not included in either Developer or Business Support, so this does not describe a difference between these two plans. Access to the full set of AWS Trusted Advisor checks is a feature of Business, Enterprise On-Ramp, and Enterprise Support plans; Developer Support provides access to only the seven core Trusted Advisor checks, meaning Business Support offers broader Trusted Advisor coverage than Developer Support, not narrower. AWS Business Support is available to any AWS customer who chooses to purchase it regardless of AWS Partner status, and is not restricted to members of the AWS Partner Network."},{"id":"q1091","question":"A company runs a business-critical application on AWS and requires a support plan that guarantees a response within 15 minutes when the application is completely unavailable. Which AWS Support plan meets this requirement?","options":{"A":"AWS Developer Support","B":"AWS Business Support","C":"AWS Enterprise On-Ramp Support","D":"AWS Enterprise Support"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Enterprise Support is the only plan that provides a target response time of less than 15 minutes for business-critical system down cases, where a production system supporting critical business operations has become completely unavailable, reflecting the highest urgency level in the AWS support tier structure.\\nAWS Developer Support targets a response time of less than 12 hours for system impaired cases and does not offer a production or business-critical system down severity category, making it insufficient for any workload requiring guaranteed sub-hour response times. AWS Business Support targets a response time of less than 1 hour for production system down cases, which is faster than Developer Support but does not meet the sub-15-minute guarantee that only the most premium support tiers provide. AWS Enterprise On-Ramp Support targets a response time of less than 30 minutes for business-critical system down cases, which is faster than Business Support but still does not achieve the sub-15-minute response guarantee that only the full AWS Enterprise Support plan provides."},{"id":"q1115","question":"Which AWS service provides a central location to view and manage the default limits on AWS resources for an account and request increases when needed?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"Service Quotas","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Service Quotas is a centralized service that allows customers to view their current service limits across AWS services, track utilization against those limits, and request quota increases directly from the console, providing a single place to manage all resource limits.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor includes a service limits check that flags when usage approaches certain default limits, but it only monitors a subset of limits and does not provide the ability to view all quotas or submit increase requests directly. AWS Organizations is an account management service for governing multiple AWS accounts through policies and consolidated billing, and does not provide visibility into or management of individual service resource limits. AWS Config records and evaluates the configuration of AWS resources against compliance rules, and monitors resource settings rather than tracking or managing service-level resource limits."},{"id":"q1116","question":"A company is approaching the maximum number of Amazon VPCs allowed in a Region and needs to request an increase. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"AWS Support","B":"Service Quotas","C":"AWS Config","D":"AWS Budgets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Service Quotas allows customers to view their current quota values for AWS services, monitor usage against those quotas, and submit requests to increase specific limits such as the maximum number of VPCs per Region, all from a single console.\\nAWS Support can be used to submit a support case to request limit increases, but Service Quotas provides a more direct and self-service approach specifically designed for viewing and managing resource limits without needing to create a support case. AWS Config tracks the configuration state of AWS resources and evaluates compliance against rules, but it does not monitor or manage service quota limits or provide the ability to request increases. AWS Budgets is a cost management tool for setting spending and usage thresholds and receiving alerts when those thresholds are approached, and has no capability to view or manage service resource limits."}],"language":"en","attribution":"Preguntas de GitHub — nastaso/cloudcertprep (https://github.com/nastaso/cloudcertprep), licencia MIT. Adaptadas al formato de este sitio.","license":"MIT"},{"id":"aws-saa-c03-open","title":"AWS Solutions Architect Associate (SAA-C03) — banco abierto (EN)","questions":[{"id":"saa-d1-001","question":"A company wants to ensure that data stored in Amazon S3 is encrypted at rest using keys fully managed by AWS, with no customer-managed key configuration, no per-key access policies, and no AWS KMS audit trail. Which encryption option should they use?","options":{"A":"SSE-S3","B":"SSE-KMS","C":"SSE-C","D":"Client-side encryption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"SSE-S3 uses Amazon S3 managed keys (AES-256) with no customer involvement, no per-key policies, and no KMS audit trail.\\nSSE-KMS uses AWS KMS keys (AWS-managed or customer-managed) and adds KMS access policies and CloudTrail integration, which is more than the requirement asks for. SSE-C requires the customer to provide and manage their own keys on every request. Client-side encryption happens before upload and is fully customer-managed."},{"id":"saa-d1-002","question":"Which AWS service provides a managed hardware security module (HSM) for cryptographic key storage?","options":{"A":"AWS KMS","B":"AWS CloudHSM","C":"AWS Secrets Manager","D":"AWS Certificate Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated HSM instances. KMS is a managed key service but uses shared infrastructure. Secrets Manager stores secrets, not HSM keys. ACM manages SSL/TLS certificates."},{"id":"saa-d1-003","question":"A solutions architect needs to restrict access to an S3 bucket so that only a specific IAM role can access it. What should they configure?","options":{"A":"S3 ACL","B":"S3 bucket policy with a Condition element","C":"S3 Object Lock","D":"S3 Versioning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A bucket policy with a Condition element can restrict access to a specific IAM role ARN. ACLs are legacy and less precise. Object Lock prevents deletion. Versioning preserves object versions."},{"id":"saa-d1-004","question":"Which feature of Amazon VPC allows you to monitor network traffic flowing to and from network interfaces?","options":{"A":"VPC Peering","B":"VPC Flow Logs","C":"NAT Gateway","D":"Internet Gateway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"VPC Flow Logs capture information about IP traffic going to and from network interfaces in a VPC. VPC Peering connects VPCs. NAT Gateway enables outbound internet for private subnets. Internet Gateway connects a VPC to the internet."},{"id":"saa-d1-005","question":"A company requires all API calls to AWS services to be logged for compliance. Which service should they enable?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Config","D":"Amazon GuardDuty"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail records API calls made to AWS services. CloudWatch monitors metrics and logs. AWS Config tracks resource configurations. GuardDuty is a threat detection service."},{"id":"saa-d2-001","question":"A web application must remain available even if an entire Availability Zone fails. Which architecture pattern achieves this?","options":{"A":"Deploy to a single AZ with Auto Scaling","B":"Deploy across multiple AZs with an Application Load Balancer","C":"Deploy to a single AZ with a larger instance type","D":"Deploy to a single Region with CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Multi-AZ deployment with an ALB distributes traffic across AZs, providing resilience against a single AZ failure. A single AZ deployment, regardless of scaling or instance size, cannot survive an AZ outage."},{"id":"saa-d2-002","question":"Which Amazon RDS feature provides automatic failover to a standby replica in a different Availability Zone?","options":{"A":"Read Replicas","B":"Multi-AZ deployment","C":"Aurora Serverless","D":"RDS Proxy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Multi-AZ deployment maintains a synchronous standby replica in a different AZ with automatic failover. Read Replicas are for read scaling, not automatic failover. Aurora Serverless auto-scales capacity. RDS Proxy manages database connections."},{"id":"saa-d2-003","question":"A company needs to decouple their application components to handle variable processing loads. Which service should they use?","options":{"A":"Amazon SQS","B":"Amazon RDS","C":"Amazon EC2","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SQS is a fully managed message queuing service that decouples application components. RDS is a database service. EC2 is a compute service. S3 is object storage."},{"id":"saa-d2-004","question":"Which S3 storage class should be used for data that is accessed less than once per quarter and can tolerate retrieval times of 12 hours?","options":{"A":"S3 Standard","B":"S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval","C":"S3 Glacier Deep Archive","D":"S3 One Zone-IA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Glacier Deep Archive is the lowest-cost storage class, suitable for data accessed rarely with retrieval times of 12-48 hours. Glacier Instant Retrieval provides millisecond access. One Zone-IA stores data in one AZ."},{"id":"saa-d2-005","question":"An application uses Amazon DynamoDB and must be able to recover from accidental table deletion. Which feature should be enabled?","options":{"A":"DynamoDB Streams","B":"Point-in-time recovery (PITR)","C":"DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","D":"Global tables"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Point-in-time recovery allows continuous backups of DynamoDB tables and restoration to any point in the last 35 days. Streams captures table changes. DAX is an in-memory cache. Global tables provide multi-region replication."},{"id":"saa-d3-001","question":"A company needs to serve static content to users worldwide with low latency. Which AWS service should they use?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon CloudFront","C":"AWS Global Accelerator","D":"Amazon Route 53"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CloudFront is a CDN that caches content at edge locations worldwide for low-latency delivery. Global Accelerator improves availability and performance for dynamic content. Route 53 is a DNS service. EC2 is a compute service."},{"id":"saa-d3-002","question":"Which service should be used to run containers without managing servers or clusters?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon ECS on EC2","C":"AWS Fargate","D":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers that removes the need to manage servers. ECS on EC2 requires managing EC2 instances. Elastic Beanstalk is a PaaS for web applications."},{"id":"saa-d3-003","question":"An application requires sub-millisecond read latency for a DynamoDB table. Which feature should a solutions architect recommend?","options":{"A":"DynamoDB Streams","B":"DynamoDB global tables","C":"DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","D":"DynamoDB on-demand capacity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DAX is an in-memory cache for DynamoDB that provides microsecond read latency. Streams captures changes. Global tables provide multi-region replication. On-demand capacity auto-scales throughput."},{"id":"saa-d3-004","question":"A company needs to process real-time streaming data from IoT sensors. Which service should they use?","options":{"A":"Amazon SQS","B":"Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","C":"Amazon SNS","D":"Amazon MQ"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Kinesis Data Streams is designed for real-time streaming data ingestion and processing. SQS is for message queuing. SNS is for pub/sub notifications. Amazon MQ is a managed message broker."},{"id":"saa-d3-005","question":"Which AWS service provides a fully managed, highly available, and scalable file storage for use with EC2 instances across multiple Availability Zones?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon EFS","C":"Amazon S3","D":"AWS Storage Gateway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EFS provides scalable, elastic file storage accessible from multiple EC2 instances across AZs. EBS is block storage attached to a single instance. S3 is object storage. Storage Gateway connects on-premises to AWS storage."},{"id":"saa-d4-001","question":"A company wants to reduce EC2 costs for workloads that can tolerate interruptions. Which purchasing option should they use?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Spot Instances offer up to 90% discount but can be interrupted. On-Demand has no discount. Reserved Instances require commitment. Dedicated Hosts provide physical server isolation."},{"id":"saa-d4-002","question":"Which AWS service helps identify underutilized resources and provides cost optimization recommendations?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Cost Explorer","D":"AWS Pricing Calculator"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trusted Advisor inspects your AWS environment and provides recommendations for cost optimization, security, performance, and fault tolerance. Cost Explorer visualizes costs. Budgets sets alerts. Pricing Calculator estimates costs."},{"id":"saa-d4-003","question":"A company runs a development environment that is only used during business hours. Which approach minimizes costs?","options":{"A":"Use Reserved Instances","B":"Use On-Demand Instances and stop them after hours","C":"Use Spot Instances","D":"Use Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Stopping On-Demand instances when not in use eliminates compute charges (only storage costs remain). Reserved Instances are for steady-state workloads. Spot Instances can be interrupted. Dedicated Hosts are expensive."},{"id":"saa-d4-004","question":"Which S3 feature can automatically move objects to cheaper storage classes based on access patterns?","options":{"A":"S3 Versioning","B":"S3 Lifecycle policies","C":"S3 Replication","D":"S3 Object Lock"},"correct":"B","explanation":"S3 Lifecycle policies automatically transition objects between storage classes or expire them based on rules. Versioning preserves object versions. Replication copies objects across buckets. Object Lock prevents deletion."},{"id":"saa-d4-005","question":"A solutions architect needs to choose between Amazon RDS and Amazon DynamoDB. Which factor most favours DynamoDB?","options":{"A":"Complex SQL queries with joins","B":"Consistent single-digit millisecond latency at any scale","C":"ACID transactions across multiple tables","D":"Schema enforcement and referential integrity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"DynamoDB provides consistent single-digit millisecond performance at any scale. RDS is better for complex SQL queries, multi-table ACID transactions, and schema enforcement."}],"language":"en","attribution":"Preguntas de GitHub — nastaso/cloudcertprep (https://github.com/nastaso/cloudcertprep), licencia MIT. Adaptadas al formato de este sitio.","license":"MIT"},{"id":"aws-aif-c01-open-es","title":"AWS AI Practitioner (AIF-C01) — banco abierto (ES)","language":"es","attribution":"Preguntas de GitHub — nastaso/cloudcertprep (https://github.com/nastaso/cloudcertprep), licencia MIT. Adaptadas al formato de este sitio. Traduccion automatica; revisar antes de publicar.","license":"MIT","questions":[{"id":"aif-q004","question":"Un fabricante cre\xf3 un modelo que clasifica fotos de piezas como defectuosas o no. El equipo quiere saber qu\xe9 proporci\xf3n de las im\xe1genes de piezas clasific\xf3 correctamente el modelo. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica de evaluaci\xf3n deber\xedan usar?","options":{"A":"Error absoluto medio (MAE)","B":"N\xfamero de \xe9pocas de entrenamiento","C":"Ra\xedz del error cuadr\xe1tico medio (RMSE)","D":"Exactitud"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La exactitud es la relaci\xf3n entre las predicciones correctas y el n\xfamero total de predicciones, por lo que mide directamente cu\xe1ntas im\xe1genes de piezas clasific\xf3 correctamente el modelo, que es exactamente lo que el equipo quiere para esta tarea de clasificaci\xf3n.\\nLa ra\xedz del error cuadr\xe1tico medio (RMSE) y el error absoluto medio (MAE) son m\xe9tricas de regresi\xf3n que cuantifican la diferencia entre valores continuos predichos y reales, por lo que ninguna es adecuada para medir la correcci\xf3n de una clasificaci\xf3n. El n\xfamero de \xe9pocas de entrenamiento es una configuraci\xf3n de entrenamiento que controla cu\xe1ntas pasadas hace el modelo sobre los datos; no es una m\xe9trica de evaluaci\xf3n en absoluto."},{"id":"aif-q006","question":"Una empresa ejecuta un pipeline de ML en Amazon SageMaker AI en producci\xf3n. Sus solicitudes transportan payloads grandes de hasta 1 GB y pueden tardar hasta una hora en procesarse, y la empresa quiere una latencia casi en tiempo real. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n de inferencia de SageMaker AI cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Inferencia en tiempo real","B":"Batch Transform","C":"Inferencia sin servidor","D":"Inferencia as\xedncrona"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La inferencia as\xedncrona est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para payloads grandes de hasta 1 GB y tiempos de procesamiento largos de hasta una hora; pone en cola las solicitudes entrantes y las procesa en segundo plano, devolviendo los resultados cuando est\xe1n listos sin requerir que el solicitante espere de forma s\xedncrona, lo que se adapta a cargas de trabajo que necesitan manejar solicitudes exigentes casi en tiempo real.\\nLa inferencia en tiempo real est\xe1 orientada a payloads peque\xf1os y respuestas s\xedncronas r\xe1pidas, y sus l\xedmites de payload y tiempo de espera se superar\xedan con entradas de 1 GB y procesamiento de una hora. La inferencia sin servidor se adapta a tr\xe1fico intermitente con payloads m\xe1s peque\xf1os y, asimismo, no puede manejar entradas tan grandes ni duraciones tan largas. Batch Transform procesa grandes conjuntos de datos sin conexi\xf3n en lote y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para ofrecer latencia casi en tiempo real."},{"id":"aif-q007","question":"Una empresa necesita crear modelos para varias tareas nuevas pero relacionadas. En lugar de entrenar modelos desde cero, quiere adaptar modelos preentrenados existentes. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de ML cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Aumentar el n\xfamero de \xe9pocas","B":"Usar aprendizaje por transferencia","C":"Disminuir el n\xfamero de \xe9pocas","D":"Usar aprendizaje no supervisado"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El aprendizaje por transferencia permite a una empresa tomar un modelo ya entrenado en una tarea y adaptarlo a tareas nuevas y relacionadas, lo que reutiliza el conocimiento capturado en el modelo preentrenado y ahorra tiempo y datos significativos en comparaci\xf3n con entrenar desde cero.\\nAumentar el n\xfamero de \xe9pocas simplemente entrena un modelo durante m\xe1s pasadas sobre los datos y no proporciona una forma de reutilizar un modelo preentrenado existente. Disminuir el n\xfamero de \xe9pocas reduce las pasadas de entrenamiento y, asimismo, no tiene nada que ver con adaptar modelos preentrenados para tareas nuevas. El aprendizaje no supervisado encuentra patrones en datos no etiquetados, pero es un enfoque de aprendizaje, no una estrategia para reutilizar y adaptar un modelo ya entrenado."},{"id":"aif-q014","question":"Una startup est\xe1 creando una aplicaci\xf3n de cuestionarios educativos que hace a los jugadores preguntas como: \\"Una bolsa contiene seis canicas rojas, cuatro verdes y tres amarillas. \xbfCu\xe1l es la probabilidad de sacar una canica verde?\\" \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque satisface este requisito con la MENOR sobrecarga operativa?","options":{"A":"Entrenar un modelo de regresi\xf3n de aprendizaje supervisado para predecir la probabilidad","B":"Crear un modelo de aprendizaje no supervisado para estimar la densidad de probabilidad","C":"Aplicar aprendizaje por refuerzo para entrenar un modelo que devuelva la probabilidad","D":"Escribir c\xf3digo simple que calcule la probabilidad usando reglas aritm\xe9ticas b\xe1sicas"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La probabilidad aqu\xed es un c\xe1lculo determinista de resultados favorables dividido por resultados totales, por lo que unas pocas l\xedneas de c\xf3digo que apliquen aritm\xe9tica b\xe1sica producen una respuesta exacta sin datos que recopilar, sin modelo que entrenar y sin endpoint que alojar, lo que da la menor sobrecarga operativa.\\nLas tres opciones de machine learning levantan un pipeline innecesario de recopilaci\xf3n de datos, entrenamiento, ajuste y alojamiento para un problema que ya tiene una respuesta de forma cerrada conocida. M\xe1s all\xe1 de ese desperdicio compartido, la regresi\xf3n supervisada solo aproximar\xeda un valor que se puede calcular exactamente; el aprendizaje no supervisado est\xe1 pensado para descubrir estructura en datos no etiquetados, lo cual es irrelevante cuando la f\xf3rmula se conoce; y el aprendizaje por refuerzo est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la toma de decisiones secuencial guiada por recompensas en un entorno, no para responder una pregunta aritm\xe9tica est\xe1tica."},{"id":"aif-q015","question":"Una empresa ha implementado un modelo en producci\xf3n y quiere una m\xe9trica que refleje con qu\xe9 eficiencia opera el modelo en tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"Latencia promedio de inferencia","B":"Puntuaci\xf3n de satisfacci\xf3n del cliente (CSAT)","C":"N\xfamero de ejemplos de entrenamiento","D":"Tiempo de entrenamiento por \xe9poca"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La latencia promedio de inferencia captura cu\xe1nto tarda el modelo en devolver una predicci\xf3n una vez que est\xe1 en ejecuci\xf3n, por lo que refleja directamente la eficiencia en tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n de operar el modelo en producci\xf3n, donde una latencia menor significa respuestas m\xe1s r\xe1pidas y eficientes.\\nLa puntuaci\xf3n de satisfacci\xf3n del cliente (CSAT) mide cu\xe1n satisfechos est\xe1n los usuarios con un producto o servicio, lo cual es un resultado de negocio en lugar de una medida de eficiencia operativa. El tiempo de entrenamiento por \xe9poca y el n\xfamero de ejemplos de entrenamiento se relacionan con la fase de entrenamiento, es decir, cu\xe1nto duran las ejecuciones de entrenamiento y cu\xe1ntos datos usan, y no dicen nada sobre la eficiencia con la que opera el modelo implementado en el momento de la inferencia."},{"id":"aif-q017","question":"Un minorista ha acumulado petabytes de registros de clientes que no tienen etiquetas ni categor\xedas predefinidas. El negocio quiere agrupar a estos clientes en segmentos distintos para dirigirse a ellos con promociones personalizadas. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque de machine learning se ajusta mejor a este requisito?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje supervisado","B":"Aprendizaje no supervisado","C":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo","D":"Aprendizaje semisupervisado"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El aprendizaje no supervisado descubre patrones y agrupaciones naturales dentro de datos no etiquetados, lo que lo convierte en el enfoque adecuado para segmentar clientes cuando no existen etiquetas ni categor\xedas predefinidas, como es el caso de los registros no etiquetados del minorista.\\nEl aprendizaje supervisado requiere datos de entrenamiento etiquetados donde cada registro tiene una salida objetivo conocida, lo cual el minorista no tiene. El aprendizaje por refuerzo entrena a un agente mediante recompensas y penalizaciones mientras interact\xfaa con un entorno, lo que no aplica a la segmentaci\xf3n de un conjunto de datos est\xe1tico de clientes. El aprendizaje semisupervisado se basa en una peque\xf1a cantidad de datos etiquetados combinada con un grupo m\xe1s grande de datos no etiquetados, pero aqu\xed no hay etiquetas en absoluto, por lo que un enfoque puramente no supervisado es el ajuste correcto."},{"id":"aif-q020","question":"Una empresa quiere usar IA para proteger su aplicaci\xf3n de amenazas comprobando si una direcci\xf3n IP entrante proviene de una fuente sospechosa. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Crear un sistema de reconocimiento de voz","B":"Desarrollar un sistema de detecci\xf3n de anomal\xedas","C":"Crear un sistema de pron\xf3stico de fraude","D":"Configurar un sistema de reconocimiento de entidades nombradas usando Procesamiento de Lenguaje Natural (NLP)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un sistema de detecci\xf3n de anomal\xedas aprende c\xf3mo son el tr\xe1fico y el comportamiento normales y marca la actividad que se desv\xeda de esos patrones, por lo que puede identificar direcciones IP que se comportan de forma sospechosa o difieren de fuentes leg\xedtimas, cumpliendo directamente el objetivo de proteger la aplicaci\xf3n de amenazas.\\nUn sistema de reconocimiento de voz convierte audio hablado en texto y no tiene nada que ver con analizar direcciones IP. Un sistema de reconocimiento de entidades nombradas basado en NLP extrae entidades como nombres y lugares de texto, no se\xf1ales de amenazas de red. Un sistema de pron\xf3stico de fraude predice vol\xfamenes o tendencias futuras de fraude en lugar de marcar una IP sospechosa individual a medida que llega."},{"id":"aif-q027","question":"Una empresa ha creado un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes y quiere que una aplicaci\xf3n web lo llame para obtener predicciones en tiempo real. La empresa necesita una forma totalmente administrada de alojar el modelo y servir esas predicciones que escale autom\xe1ticamente con la demanda. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Usar Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference para implementar el modelo","B":"Usar Amazon CloudFront para implementar el modelo","C":"Usar Amazon API Gateway para alojar el modelo y servir predicciones","D":"Usar AWS Batch para alojar el modelo y servir predicciones"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference es una opci\xf3n totalmente administrada que aloja un modelo y sirve predicciones bajo demanda mientras aprovisiona y escala autom\xe1ticamente la computaci\xf3n subyacente, de modo que una aplicaci\xf3n web puede llamar al modelo para obtener predicciones en tiempo real sin que la empresa administre capacidad.\\nAmazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y distribuye contenido para reducir la latencia, y no puede alojar un modelo de ML ni generar predicciones. Amazon API Gateway crea y administra la capa de API que est\xe1 delante de un backend, pero no aloja por s\xed mismo un modelo ni ejecuta inferencia y a\xfan necesitar\xeda un servicio de computaci\xf3n detr\xe1s. AWS Batch ejecuta trabajos de computaci\xf3n por lotes en computaci\xf3n administrada y est\xe1 orientado al procesamiento masivo sin conexi\xf3n en lugar de servir predicciones bajo demanda y de baja latencia a una aplicaci\xf3n web."},{"id":"aif-q031","question":"Un investigador de vida silvestre tiene una gran colecci\xf3n de fotos de animales y quiere identificar y categorizar autom\xe1ticamente los animales en cada imagen sin esfuerzo manual. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Reconocimiento de entidades nombradas","B":"Inpainting","C":"Detecci\xf3n de objetos","D":"Detecci\xf3n de anomal\xedas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La detecci\xf3n de objetos es una t\xe9cnica de visi\xf3n artificial que encuentra y clasifica autom\xe1ticamente los objetos dentro de una imagen, por lo que puede localizar cada animal en una foto y asignarlo a una categor\xeda sin esfuerzo manual, que es exactamente lo que necesita el investigador.\\nLa detecci\xf3n de anomal\xedas marca puntos de datos que se desv\xedan de los patrones normales, no el contenido de una imagen. El reconocimiento de entidades nombradas es una t\xe9cnica de procesamiento de lenguaje natural (NLP) que extrae entidades como nombres y lugares del texto en lugar de fotos. El inpainting es una t\xe9cnica generativa de visi\xf3n artificial que rellena regiones faltantes o eliminadas de una imagen, en lugar de identificar y categorizar lo que contiene."},{"id":"aif-q045","question":"Una cadena de hoteles quiere leer el sentimiento de rese\xf1as escritas por hu\xe9spedes. \xbfQu\xe9 dos servicios de AWS pueden cumplir este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Polly","C":"Amazon Bedrock","D":"Amazon Lex","E":"Amazon Rekognition"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Amazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural (NLP) con an\xe1lisis de sentimiento integrado que puede determinar si el texto es positivo, negativo, neutral o mixto, lo que lo hace adecuado para analizar rese\xf1as escritas. Amazon Bedrock proporciona acceso a modelos fundacionales que tambi\xe9n pueden clasificar el sentimiento del texto mediante prompting.\\nAmazon Polly es un servicio de texto a voz que convierte texto escrito en audio hablado realista, que es lo opuesto a analizar el contenido del texto. Amazon Lex es un servicio para crear chatbots conversacionales e interfaces de voz, no para analizar el sentimiento de texto existente. Amazon Rekognition es un servicio de visi\xf3n artificial que analiza im\xe1genes y video para detectar objetos, rostros y escenas, y no puede procesar texto escrito para sentimiento."},{"id":"aif-q056","question":"Una empresa de electr\xf3nica quiere pronosticar la demanda de clientes para sus productos de memoria. Su equipo no tiene habilidades de programaci\xf3n ni conocimiento de algoritmos de ML, pero necesita construir un modelo predictivo basado en datos que utilice datos internos y externos. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Importar los datos a Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler y construir los modelos de pron\xf3stico usando algoritmos integrados de Amazon SageMaker AI","B":"Importar los datos a Amazon SageMaker Canvas y construir los modelos de pron\xf3stico en Canvas","C":"Almacenar los datos en Amazon S3 y construir los modelos de pron\xf3stico usando algoritmos integrados de Amazon SageMaker AI","D":"Importar los datos a Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler y construir los modelos de pron\xf3stico escribiendo c\xf3digo de entrenamiento personalizado"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas es un servicio sin c\xf3digo que permite a usuarios de negocio importar datos y construir modelos de ML y generar predicciones mediante una interfaz visual de apuntar y hacer clic, sin escribir c\xf3digo ni comprender algoritmos de ML, que es exactamente lo que necesita un equipo sin experiencia en programaci\xf3n ni en ML para pronosticar la demanda.\\nLas otras opciones requieren habilidades t\xe9cnicas que el equipo no tiene. Usar algoritmos integrados de Amazon SageMaker AI, ya sea que los datos se almacenen en Amazon S3 o se preparen en Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler, todav\xeda implica programar para construir y entrenar los modelos, y escribir c\xf3digo de entrenamiento personalizado claramente tambi\xe9n. Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler tambi\xe9n se enfoca en la preparaci\xf3n de datos en lugar de la construcci\xf3n de modelos sin c\xf3digo, por lo que ninguna de estas cumple el requisito de no requerir c\xf3digo."},{"id":"aif-q058","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 construyendo un chatbot de atenci\xf3n al cliente que deber\xeda mejorar continuamente sus respuestas aprendiendo de sus conversaciones previas. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de aprendizaje de IA proporciona esta capacidad de automejora?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje supervisado usando un conjunto etiquetado manualmente de respuestas buenas y malas de ejemplo","B":"Aprendizaje no supervisado para agrupar preguntas similares de clientes en cl\xfasteres","C":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo que recompensa las respuestas que obtienen comentarios positivos de los clientes","D":"Aprendizaje supervisado impulsado por una base de conocimiento de preguntas frecuentes actualizada regularmente"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El aprendizaje por refuerzo entrena un modelo recompensando resultados deseables y penalizando los indeseables, lo que permite al chatbot mejorar continuamente sus respuestas bas\xe1ndose en se\xf1ales reales, como comentarios positivos de los clientes, sin requerir un conjunto de datos etiquetado preparado manualmente para cada actualizaci\xf3n.\\nEl aprendizaje supervisado con un conjunto de datos curado manualmente de respuestas buenas y malas requiere esfuerzo humano continuo para etiquetar ejemplos de entrenamiento y no permite la automejora aut\xf3noma a partir de interacciones en vivo. El aprendizaje no supervisado identifica patrones y cl\xfasteres en datos no etiquetados, lo que podr\xeda ayudar a agrupar preguntas similares de clientes, pero no proporciona ning\xfan mecanismo para mejorar la calidad de las respuestas bas\xe1ndose en se\xf1ales de retroalimentaci\xf3n. El aprendizaje supervisado con una base de datos de preguntas frecuentes actualizada continuamente tambi\xe9n depende del mantenimiento manual del conjunto de datos subyacente en lugar del aprendizaje aut\xf3nomo a partir de las interacciones con los clientes."},{"id":"aif-q059","question":"Un practicante de IA ha entrenado un modelo de aprendizaje profundo que clasifica im\xe1genes en diferentes razas de perros. Ahora el practicante quiere medir qu\xe9 tan bien se desempe\xf1a el modelo. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica ayudar\xe1 a evaluar el rendimiento del modelo?","options":{"A":"Matriz de confusi\xf3n","B":"Matriz de correlaci\xf3n","C":"Puntuaci\xf3n R2","D":"Error cuadr\xe1tico medio (MSE)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Una matriz de confusi\xf3n es la m\xe9trica adecuada para evaluar un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n porque desglosa las predicciones en verdaderos positivos, falsos positivos, verdaderos negativos y falsos negativos, brindando una vista detallada de d\xf3nde el modelo es correcto y d\xf3nde clasifica err\xf3neamente cada raza.\\nUna matriz de correlaci\xf3n mide las relaciones estad\xedsticas entre variables de un conjunto de datos y es una herramienta de exploraci\xf3n de datos usada durante EDA, no una m\xe9trica de rendimiento del modelo. La puntuaci\xf3n R2, o coeficiente de determinaci\xf3n, mide qu\xe9 tan bien un modelo de regresi\xf3n explica la varianza en valores de salida continuos y no es aplicable a tareas de clasificaci\xf3n. El error cuadr\xe1tico medio mide la diferencia cuadr\xe1tica promedio entre valores continuos predichos y reales, lo que lo convierte en una m\xe9trica de regresi\xf3n que tambi\xe9n es inadecuada para evaluar un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q062","question":"Una compa\xf1\xeda de seguros necesita ejecutar su modelo de ML sobre varios a\xf1os de registros de siniestros archivados para generar predicciones, con cada conjunto de datos de varios gigabytes. Los resultados no se requieren de inmediato. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n de inferencia de Amazon SageMaker AI se adapta mejor a esta necesidad?","options":{"A":"Inferencia sin servidor","B":"Transformaci\xf3n por lotes","C":"Inferencia en tiempo real","D":"Inferencia as\xedncrona"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La transformaci\xf3n por lotes ejecuta la inferencia como un trabajo sin conexi\xf3n sobre un conjunto de datos completo sin un endpoint persistente, por lo que puede procesar los datos archivados de varios gigabytes de manera eficiente cuando las predicciones no se necesitan de inmediato.\\nLa inferencia en tiempo real y la inferencia sin servidor devuelven predicciones de forma s\xedncrona desde un endpoint activo para solicitudes bajo demanda con baja latencia, por lo que ninguna es adecuada para el procesamiento masivo sin conexi\xf3n de grandes conjuntos de datos. La inferencia as\xedncrona pone en cola las solicitudes entrantes y est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para cargas \xfatiles individuales grandes que necesitan manejo casi en tiempo real, en lugar de ejecutar un solo trabajo sobre un conjunto de datos archivado completo."},{"id":"aif-q068","question":"Una agencia de reclutamiento recibe un volumen creciente de curr\xedculums en archivos PDF que ya no puede revisar manualmente. Necesita una forma automatizada de convertir estos curr\xedculums PDF en texto plano para su posterior procesamiento. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Lex","B":"Amazon Textract","C":"Amazon Transcribe","D":"Amazon Personalize"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Textract extrae autom\xe1ticamente texto y datos de documentos escaneados y PDF, por lo que puede convertir los curr\xedculums en texto plano para el procesamiento posterior.\\nAmazon Lex crea chatbots conversacionales e interfaces de voz. Amazon Transcribe convierte audio de voz en texto en lugar de leer documentos. Amazon Personalize genera recomendaciones en tiempo real. Ninguno de estos extrae texto de archivos PDF."},{"id":"aif-q072","question":"Un equipo de ciencia de datos acaba de reunir un nuevo conjunto de datos y lo est\xe1 examinando calculando estad\xedsticas resumidas, trazando gr\xe1ficos y construyendo una matriz de correlaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 etapa del pipeline de ML describe esto?","options":{"A":"Entrenamiento del modelo","B":"An\xe1lisis exploratorio de datos","C":"Recopilaci\xf3n de datos","D":"Ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El an\xe1lisis exploratorio de datos es la etapa en la que un equipo resume un conjunto de datos mediante estad\xedsticas, visualizaciones y matrices de correlaci\xf3n para comprender su estructura, distribuci\xf3n y relaciones antes de que comience cualquier modelado, que es exactamente lo que est\xe1 haciendo el equipo.\\nEl entrenamiento del modelo es una etapa posterior en la que un algoritmo aprende patrones de datos preparados, no donde se examina el conjunto de datos sin procesar. La recopilaci\xf3n de datos es la etapa anterior de reunir los datos sin procesar; aunque los datos acaban de reunirse, las actividades descritas se refieren a analizarlos en lugar de recopilarlos. La ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas crea o selecciona variables de entrada para mejorar el rendimiento del modelo, lo que ocurre despu\xe9s de que se comprenden los datos."},{"id":"aif-q073","question":"Una empresa recuper\xf3 un conjunto de documentos de texto despu\xe9s de un error en la base de datos, pero faltan algunas palabras. Quiere un modelo de ML que pueda predecir la palabra m\xe1s probable para cada espacio en blanco. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de modelo es adecuado?","options":{"A":"Modelado de temas","B":"Modelos de clustering","C":"Modelos basados en BERT","D":"Modelos de anal\xedtica prescriptiva"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los modelos basados en BERT son modelos de lenguaje transformer entrenados con modelado de lenguaje enmascarado, por lo que predicen un token faltante aprovechando el contexto que lo rodea por ambos lados, que es exactamente lo que se requiere para sugerir palabras que completen los espacios.\\nEl modelado de temas descubre los temas presentes en una colecci\xf3n de documentos, pero no predice palabras faltantes individuales. Los modelos de clustering agrupan elementos similares de forma no supervisada y no producen predicciones a nivel de palabra. Los modelos de anal\xedtica prescriptiva recomiendan qu\xe9 acci\xf3n tomar dado un resultado previsto y no tienen nada que ver con la completaci\xf3n de texto."},{"id":"aif-q092","question":"Una empresa de software quiere usar AI para aumentar la productividad de sus desarrolladores al escribir software. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Usar un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n binaria para generar revisiones de c\xf3digo","B":"Usar un algoritmo de clustering para agrupar fragmentos de c\xf3digo similares","C":"Usar una herramienta de Natural Language Processing (NLP) para generar c\xf3digo","D":"Usar un modelo de pron\xf3stico para predecir n\xfameros futuros de defectos de c\xf3digo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las herramientas de Natural Language Processing (NLP), que sustentan a los asistentes de programaci\xf3n de IA generativa, pueden convertir descripciones en lenguaje natural en c\xf3digo funcional, autocompletar fragmentos y escribir bloques completos de c\xf3digo, automatizando trabajo repetitivo y acelerando el desarrollo, por lo que aumentan directamente la productividad de los desarrolladores.\\nUn modelo de clasificaci\xf3n binaria solo asigna entradas a una de dos categor\xedas, por lo que no puede generar c\xf3digo ni revisiones. Un algoritmo de clustering agrupa elementos similares, pero no produce c\xf3digo nuevo. Un modelo de pron\xf3stico predice valores num\xe9ricos futuros, como recuentos de defectos a lo largo del tiempo, y de igual manera no ayuda a los desarrolladores a escribir c\xf3digo m\xe1s r\xe1pido."},{"id":"aif-q093","question":"Una tienda minorista planea pronosticar la demanda de un producto durante las pr\xf3ximas semanas usando el algoritmo Amazon SageMaker AI DeepAR. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de datos se requieren para cumplir este requisito?","options":{"A":"Datos de texto","B":"Datos de series temporales","C":"Datos de audio","D":"Datos de imagen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"DeepAR es un algoritmo de pron\xf3stico que predice valores futuros a partir de observaciones hist\xf3ricas registradas en secuencia a lo largo del tiempo, por lo que necesita datos de series temporales, como la demanda pasada indexada por fecha, para aprender patrones temporales y proyectarlos hacia el futuro.\\nLos datos de texto son adecuados para tareas de Natural Language Processing, los datos de imagen para tareas de Computer Vision y los datos de audio para procesamiento de voz o sonido; ninguno de estos captura las mediciones cronol\xf3gicas indexadas por tiempo en las que se basa un modelo de pron\xf3stico de demanda."},{"id":"aif-q103","question":"Un practicante de AI necesita predecir a qu\xe9 especie pertenece cada flor, usando su longitud de p\xe9talo, ancho de p\xe9talo, longitud de s\xe9palo y ancho de s\xe9palo. \xbfQu\xe9 algoritmo cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Regresi\xf3n lineal","B":"K-nearest neighbors (k-NN)","C":"K-means","D":"Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"K-nearest neighbors (k-NN) es un algoritmo de clasificaci\xf3n supervisada que etiqueta un nuevo punto de datos seg\xfan la clase mayoritaria de sus vecinos m\xe1s cercanos en el espacio de caracter\xedsticas, por lo que se ajusta a asignar una flor a una especie a partir de sus medidas de p\xe9talos y s\xe9palos.\\nK-means es un algoritmo de clustering no supervisado que agrupa datos sin etiquetar en lugar de asignar categor\xedas conocidas. Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA) es un m\xe9todo de pron\xf3stico de series temporales para datos secuenciales ordenados en el tiempo. La regresi\xf3n lineal predice un valor num\xe9rico continuo, no una categor\xeda."},{"id":"aif-q115","question":"Un equipo de investigaci\xf3n de vida silvestre quiere entrenar un modelo que identifique la especie animal que aparece en fotos de c\xe1maras trampa. Tiene un gran conjunto de im\xe1genes que ya est\xe1n etiquetadas con la especie correcta y no etiquetar\xe1 m\xe1s. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de aprendizaje deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo","B":"Aprendizaje supervisado","C":"Aprendizaje activo","D":"Aprendizaje no supervisado"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El aprendizaje supervisado es la elecci\xf3n correcta porque el equipo ya tiene un gran conjunto de datos en el que cada imagen est\xe1 emparejada con una etiqueta conocida que identifica la especie. El modelo aprende a mapear entradas a las etiquetas de salida correctas a partir de estos datos de entrenamiento etiquetados, que es la caracter\xedstica definitoria del aprendizaje supervisado.\\nEl aprendizaje no supervisado se usa cuando no hay datos etiquetados disponibles y el modelo debe descubrir patrones o agrupaciones por s\xed mismo, lo cual no aplica aqu\xed porque ya existen datos etiquetados. El aprendizaje por refuerzo entrena a un agente para tomar acciones en un entorno con el fin de maximizar una recompensa acumulada mediante prueba y error, y no es adecuado para clasificar im\xe1genes a partir de un conjunto de datos etiquetado est\xe1tico. El aprendizaje activo es una estrategia que selecciona iterativamente los ejemplos m\xe1s inciertos y solicita a etiquetadores humanos que los anoten, reduciendo el esfuerzo total de etiquetado necesario; sin embargo, el equipo declar\xf3 expl\xedcitamente que no etiquetar\xe1 m\xe1s datos, por lo que el mecanismo central del aprendizaje activo no se puede usar y no ofrece ning\xfan beneficio en este escenario."},{"id":"aif-q117","question":"Una cadena de restaurantes quiere construir un modelo de ML para reducir el desperdicio diario de alimentos y aumentar los ingresos, y necesita que la precisi\xf3n del modelo siga mejorando con el tiempo. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Usar Amazon Personalize y refinarlo solo con datos hist\xf3ricos","B":"Usar Amazon Rekognition para mejorar el modelo","C":"Usar Amazon SageMaker AI y reentrenarlo con datos m\xe1s recientes","D":"Usar Amazon CloudWatch para examinar los pedidos de los clientes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker AI es una plataforma de ML totalmente administrada que admite el ciclo de vida completo de desarrollo del modelo, incluida la creaci\xf3n, el entrenamiento y el despliegue de modelos. El reentrenamiento con datos m\xe1s recientes permite que el modelo aprenda continuamente de patrones recientes en la demanda y el desperdicio de alimentos, mejorando directamente la precisi\xf3n con el tiempo a medida que cambian las condiciones.\\nAmazon Personalize ofrece recomendaciones personalizadas de productos y contenido y no es una plataforma de prop\xf3sito general para entrenar modelos personalizados de regresi\xf3n o pron\xf3stico; confiar solo en datos hist\xf3ricos tambi\xe9n impedir\xeda que el modelo se adapte a cambios recientes. Amazon CloudWatch es un servicio de monitoreo y observabilidad para m\xe9tricas, registros y alarmas en recursos de AWS, por lo que no puede examinar pedidos de clientes ni entrenar modelos de ML. Amazon Rekognition es un servicio de Computer Vision para an\xe1lisis de im\xe1genes y videos, como detecci\xf3n de objetos y reconocimiento facial, que no aplica a un modelo de planificaci\xf3n de demanda para reducir el desperdicio de alimentos."},{"id":"aif-q118","question":"Una empresa inmobiliaria ha creado un modelo de ML que predice precios de venta de viviendas y quiere alojarlo para obtener predicciones sin tener que administrar servidores ni infraestructura. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Desplegar el modelo en una instancia de Amazon EC2","B":"Desplegar el modelo usando Amazon CloudFront con una integraci\xf3n de Amazon S3","C":"Desplegar el modelo en un endpoint de Amazon SageMaker AI","D":"Desplegar el modelo en un cl\xfaster de Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Un endpoint de Amazon SageMaker AI es una opci\xf3n de despliegue totalmente administrada que gestiona autom\xe1ticamente los servidores subyacentes, el escalado y el mantenimiento, por lo que la empresa puede servir predicciones desde su modelo sin aprovisionar ni administrar infraestructura propia.\\nDesplegar el modelo en una instancia de Amazon EC2 o en un cl\xfaster de Amazon EKS requerir\xeda que la empresa aprovisione, escale, aplique parches y administre los servidores o contenedores por s\xed misma, que es lo opuesto a lo que quiere. Amazon CloudFront con Amazon S3 es una combinaci\xf3n de entrega de contenido y almacenamiento est\xe1tico para distribuir archivos y contenido web, no un servicio para alojar y ejecutar inferencia de modelos de ML."},{"id":"aif-q120","question":"Una empresa manufacturera usa AI para inspeccionar productos y encontrar da\xf1os o defectos. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de aplicaci\xf3n de AI est\xe1 usando la empresa?","options":{"A":"Sistema de recomendaci\xf3n","B":"Procesamiento de lenguaje natural (NLP)","C":"Visi\xf3n por computadora","D":"Procesamiento de im\xe1genes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Computer Vision es el campo de la AI que permite a las m\xe1quinas interpretar y analizar informaci\xf3n visual de im\xe1genes y videos, lo que lo convierte en la tecnolog\xeda detr\xe1s de los sistemas de inspecci\xf3n automatizados que detectan da\xf1os o defectos en productos en una l\xednea de producci\xf3n.\\nUn sistema de recomendaci\xf3n analiza el comportamiento y las preferencias del usuario para sugerir productos o contenido relevante, lo cual no est\xe1 relacionado con la detecci\xf3n visual de defectos. Natural Language Processing (NLP) se centra en comprender y generar lenguaje humano en texto o voz, no en analizar im\xe1genes. El procesamiento de im\xe1genes se refiere a la manipulaci\xf3n de bajo nivel de im\xe1genes, como redimensionarlas, filtrarlas o mejorarlas; si bien puede formar parte de un flujo de visi\xf3n, no implica la interpretaci\xf3n y clasificaci\xf3n impulsadas por AI que se requieren para la detecci\xf3n de defectos."},{"id":"aif-q121","question":"Una empresa quiere crear un modelo de ML para predecir la satisfacci\xf3n del cliente y necesita que el ajuste del modelo sea totalmente automatizado. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Athena","B":"Amazon Personalize","C":"Amazon Comprehend","D":"Amazon SageMaker AI"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker AI es una plataforma de ML totalmente administrada cuya caracter\xedstica Automatic Model Tuning (optimizaci\xf3n de hiperpar\xe1metros) busca autom\xe1ticamente los mejores hiperpar\xe1metros y ajusta el modelo sin esfuerzo manual, que es exactamente el ajuste totalmente automatizado que la empresa necesita para su modelo de satisfacci\xf3n del cliente.\\nAmazon Personalize es un servicio administrado para generar recomendaciones personalizadas, no para entrenar y ajustar un modelo de predicci\xf3n personalizado. Amazon Athena es un servicio de consultas interactivas para analizar datos en Amazon S3 con SQL y no tiene capacidad de ajuste de modelos. Amazon Comprehend es un servicio de Procesamiento de Lenguaje Natural (NLP) para extraer informaci\xf3n de texto y no entrena ni ajusta modelos de ML personalizados."},{"id":"aif-q127","question":"Una empresa necesita un servicio de AWS altamente escalable para monitorear el rendimiento de sus sistemas de ML en producci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch es un servicio de monitoreo y observabilidad altamente escalable que recopila m\xe9tricas en tiempo real, como uso de recursos, latencia de inferencia, recuentos de solicitudes y errores, y admite alarmas, paneles y acciones automatizadas, lo que lo hace adecuado para el seguimiento del rendimiento de los sistemas de ML.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra la actividad de la API para auditor\xeda y gobernanza, no para m\xe9tricas de rendimiento. AWS Config realiza un seguimiento de los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos y del cumplimiento. AWS Trusted Advisor ofrece recomendaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas sobre costo, seguridad y tolerancia a fallos. Ninguno de estos est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para el monitoreo de rendimiento en tiempo real de sistemas de ML."},{"id":"aif-q130","question":"Una organizaci\xf3n sin fines de lucro est\xe1 creando una aplicaci\xf3n telef\xf3nica para personas con baja visi\xf3n. La aplicaci\xf3n debe entender preguntas habladas y responder con salida de voz. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Crear modelos de ML para encontrar patrones en datos num\xe9ricos","B":"Usar resumen con IA generativa para producir texto similar al humano","C":"Crear modelos personalizados para clasificaci\xf3n y reconocimiento de im\xe1genes","D":"Usar una red neuronal de aprendizaje profundo para realizar reconocimiento de voz"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Una red neuronal de aprendizaje profundo para reconocimiento de voz permite que la aplicaci\xf3n convierta las palabras habladas de los usuarios en texto sobre el que pueda actuar y, combinada con texto a voz, puede responder con salida de voz, lo que se ajusta a una aplicaci\xf3n para usuarios con baja visi\xf3n.\\nLos modelos de ML para encontrar patrones num\xe9ricos abordan datos tabulares, no voz. El resumen con IA generativa condensa texto, pero no captura ni reproduce audio. Los modelos de clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes analizan im\xe1genes, lo cual no est\xe1 relacionado con o\xedr y hablar."},{"id":"aif-q135","question":"Una empresa inmobiliaria quiere estimar el precio de venta de viviendas a partir de caracter\xedsticas como superficie, ubicaci\xf3n y n\xfamero de habitaciones. \xbfQu\xe9 algoritmo deber\xeda usar para cumplir este requisito?","options":{"A":"K-means","B":"Regresi\xf3n log\xedstica","C":"Regresi\xf3n lineal","D":"K-vecinos m\xe1s cercanos (k-NN)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La regresi\xf3n lineal es un algoritmo supervisado que modela la relaci\xf3n entre las caracter\xedsticas de entrada y un objetivo num\xe9rico continuo, lo que la convierte en la opci\xf3n est\xe1ndar para estimar el precio de venta de una vivienda a partir de atributos como superficie, ubicaci\xf3n y n\xfamero de habitaciones.\\nK-means es un algoritmo de agrupamiento no supervisado que agrupa registros similares y no predice un valor num\xe9rico. La regresi\xf3n log\xedstica predice la probabilidad de un resultado categ\xf3rico en lugar de un precio continuo, a pesar de su nombre. K-vecinos m\xe1s cercanos (k-NN) puede realizar regresi\xf3n, pero la regresi\xf3n lineal es el algoritmo can\xf3nico y m\xe1s interpretable para predecir valores continuos como los precios de las viviendas a partir de caracter\xedsticas num\xe9ricas."},{"id":"aif-q142","question":"Al evaluar un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n, \xbfqu\xe9 mide una puntuaci\xf3n F1?","options":{"A":"La velocidad del modelo para generar respuestas","B":"El costo financiero de operar el modelo","C":"La precisi\xf3n y la exhaustividad (recall) del modelo","D":"La eficiencia energ\xe9tica de los c\xe1lculos del modelo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La puntuaci\xf3n F1 es la media arm\xf3nica de la precisi\xf3n y la exhaustividad (recall), por lo que mide el equilibrio entre ambas y es especialmente \xfatil cuando las clases est\xe1n desequilibradas.\\nLa velocidad de respuesta es una medida de latencia. El costo operativo es una medida financiera. La eficiencia energ\xe9tica es una medida de sostenibilidad. Ninguna de estas es lo que captura una puntuaci\xf3n F1."},{"id":"aif-q170","question":"Un minorista en l\xednea ha creado un sistema que marca pedidos como potencialmente fraudulentos, y un equipo revisa manualmente cada pedido marcado. La empresa quiere reducir el tiempo que el equipo desperdicia revisando pedidos marcados que resultan ser leg\xedtimos. \xbfPara qu\xe9 m\xe9trica de evaluaci\xf3n deber\xeda optimizar?","options":{"A":"Exhaustividad (Recall)","B":"Precisi\xf3n","C":"Puntuaci\xf3n F1","D":"Exactitud"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La precisi\xf3n es la proporci\xf3n de casos marcados que son genuinamente fraudulentos, calculada como TP / (TP + FP). Optimizar la precisi\xf3n minimiza los falsos positivos, que son exactamente los pedidos leg\xedtimos marcados err\xf3neamente que desperdician el tiempo de revisi\xf3n del equipo, por lo que apunta directamente al objetivo de la empresa.\\nLa exhaustividad (recall) mide qu\xe9 parte del fraude real detecta el sistema, TP / (TP + FN); maximizarla reduce el fraude no detectado, pero no hace nada para limitar los falsos positivos e incluso puede aumentarlos. La exactitud es la proporci\xf3n de todas las predicciones que son correctas, lo que resulta enga\xf1oso en datos de fraude desequilibrados donde la mayor\xeda de las transacciones son leg\xedtimas. La puntuaci\xf3n F1 es la media arm\xf3nica de la precisi\xf3n y la exhaustividad (recall) y equilibra ambas, en lugar de reducir espec\xedficamente los falsos positivos que generan la carga de revisi\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q177","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n es un beneficio de usar Infraestructura como C\xf3digo (IaC) en Machine Learning Operations (MLOps)?","options":{"A":"IaC elimina la necesidad de ajustar hiperpar\xe1metros","B":"IaC siempre aprovisiona instancias de c\xf3mputo potentes, lo que contribuye al entrenamiento de modelos m\xe1s precisos","C":"IaC agiliza el despliegue de cargas de trabajo de ML escalables y consistentes en entornos de nube","D":"IaC minimiza los gastos generales al desplegar solo instancias de bajo costo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IaC permite definir, versionar y automatizar la infraestructura en c\xf3digo, lo que habilita un aprovisionamiento repetible y consistente de entornos de ML a trav\xe9s de las etapas de desarrollo, prueba y producci\xf3n, y respalda directamente los principios de MLOps de sistemas escalables y procesos repetibles.\\nEl ajuste de hiperpar\xe1metros es una actividad de optimizaci\xf3n del modelo que ajusta par\xe1metros como la tasa de aprendizaje y el tama\xf1o del lote durante el entrenamiento para mejorar el rendimiento del modelo; est\xe1 completamente separado del aprovisionamiento de infraestructura, por lo que IaC no puede eliminarlo. IaC no aprovisiona inherentemente instancias potentes o de bajo costo, ya que simplemente aprovisiona los recursos que se definen en el c\xf3digo de configuraci\xf3n; la elecci\xf3n del tipo de instancia y del perfil de costo la determina el ingeniero que escribe la plantilla, no IaC en s\xed."},{"id":"aif-q181","question":"Un banco quiere etiquetar cada transacci\xf3n con tarjeta de cr\xe9dito como fraudulenta o leg\xedtima seg\xfan sus datos de transacci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de modelo de ML cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Regresi\xf3n","B":"Clasificaci\xf3n binaria","C":"Clasificaci\xf3n multiclase","D":"Difusi\xf3n"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La clasificaci\xf3n binaria predice a cu\xe1l de exactamente dos categor\xedas pertenece una entrada, por lo que es el tipo de modelo adecuado para etiquetar cada transacci\xf3n como uno de dos resultados, fraudulenta o leg\xedtima, seg\xfan sus caracter\xedsticas.\\nLa clasificaci\xf3n multiclase asigna las entradas a una de tres o m\xe1s categor\xedas, lo que no es necesario cuando solo hay dos resultados posibles. La regresi\xf3n predice valores num\xe9ricos continuos en lugar de categor\xedas discretas. Los modelos de difusi\xf3n son modelos generativos que crean contenido nuevo, como im\xe1genes, no modelos que clasifican transacciones."},{"id":"aif-q182","question":"Una cl\xednica m\xe9dica quiere que los m\xe9dicos dicten sus notas cl\xednicas y que el audio se convierta autom\xe1ticamente en texto. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Transcribe","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Transcribe es el servicio de IA administrado que convierte voz en texto, por lo que puede transformar las notas cl\xednicas dictadas por los m\xe9dicos en texto escrito, lo cual cumple el requisito.\\nAmazon Polly hace lo contrario: convierte texto en voz. Amazon Rekognition analiza im\xe1genes y video. Amazon Comprehend extrae informaci\xf3n como entidades y sentimiento de texto que ya existe, pero no convierte voz en texto."},{"id":"aif-q183","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 tipo de modelo de IA est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para predecir valores num\xe9ricos continuos?","options":{"A":"Transformer","B":"Multimodal","C":"Difusi\xf3n","D":"Regresi\xf3n"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La regresi\xf3n es una t\xe9cnica de aprendizaje supervisado dise\xf1ada para predecir valores num\xe9ricos continuos mediante el aprendizaje de la relaci\xf3n entre las caracter\xedsticas de entrada y una salida num\xe9rica, lo que la convierte en el tipo de modelo adecuado para tareas como pronosticar cifras de ventas, estimar precios de viviendas o predecir temperaturas.\\nLos modelos de difusi\xf3n son modelos generativos que aprenden a crear contenido nuevo, como im\xe1genes o audio, mediante la eliminaci\xf3n gradual del ruido aleatorio, y no se utilizan para tareas de predicci\xf3n num\xe9rica. Los Transformer son una arquitectura de red neuronal que procesa datos secuenciales mediante mecanismos de atenci\xf3n y constituyen la base de los modelos de lenguaje grandes, pero la arquitectura en s\xed no es un tipo de modelo asociado con la predicci\xf3n num\xe9rica. Los modelos multimodales est\xe1n dise\xf1ados para procesar y generar varios tipos de datos, como texto, im\xe1genes y audio, de forma conjunta, lo que describe las modalidades de entrada y salida de un modelo en lugar de su funci\xf3n predictiva."},{"id":"aif-q204","question":"Un servicio de streaming por suscripci\xf3n est\xe1 creando un modelo de ML para predecir qu\xe9 clientes probablemente cancelar\xe1n su plan. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica es apropiada para evaluar este modelo de clasificaci\xf3n binaria?","options":{"A":"R cuadrado","B":"Error cuadr\xe1tico medio (MSE)","C":"Puntuaci\xf3n F1","D":"Tiempo de entrenamiento del modelo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La puntuaci\xf3n F1 combina la precisi\xf3n y la exhaustividad (recall) en un solo valor, lo que la hace adecuada para una tarea de clasificaci\xf3n binaria como la predicci\xf3n de abandono (churn), donde identificar correctamente a los clientes que cancelar\xe1n es importante y es necesario un equilibrio entre falsos positivos y falsos negativos.\\nR cuadrado y el error cuadr\xe1tico medio (MSE) son m\xe9tricas de regresi\xf3n que miden el error en predicciones de valores continuos, por lo que no se aplican a una tarea de clasificaci\xf3n. El tiempo de entrenamiento del modelo solo refleja cu\xe1nto tarda el modelo en entrenarse, una cifra operativa que no dice nada sobre la precisi\xf3n con la que el modelo predice el abandono."},{"id":"aif-q205","question":"Mientras eval\xfaa un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n en Amazon SageMaker AI, un practicante de IA necesita una m\xe9trica que proporcione la proporci\xf3n de elementos clasificados correctamente respecto del n\xfamero total de elementos clasificados, ya sea correcta o incorrectamente. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Exhaustividad (recall)","B":"Precisi\xf3n","C":"Exactitud","D":"Puntuaci\xf3n F1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La exactitud es el n\xfamero de predicciones correctas (verdaderos positivos m\xe1s verdaderos negativos) dividido por el n\xfamero total de predicciones (todos los verdaderos y falsos positivos y negativos), que es exactamente la proporci\xf3n de elementos clasificados correctamente respecto de cada elemento clasificado que describe el requisito.\\nLa precisi\xf3n mide la proporci\xf3n de predicciones positivas que son realmente correctas (verdaderos positivos sobre verdaderos positivos m\xe1s falsos positivos), no todas las clasificaciones. La exhaustividad (recall) mide la proporci\xf3n de positivos reales que el modelo identifica correctamente (verdaderos positivos sobre verdaderos positivos m\xe1s falsos negativos). La puntuaci\xf3n F1 es la media arm\xf3nica de la precisi\xf3n y la exhaustividad (recall), y equilibra esos dos valores en lugar de informar las clasificaciones correctas generales."},{"id":"aif-q206","question":"Una empresa de log\xedstica ingiere varios gigabytes de registros de env\xedos cada d\xeda y los utiliza para entrenar un modelo de ML que predice los vol\xfamenes de entrega del d\xeda siguiente. La empresa solo necesita predicciones generadas una vez al d\xeda sobre el conjunto completo de datos acumulados. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de inferencia cumple mejor este requisito?","options":{"A":"Inferencia por lotes","B":"Inferencia as\xedncrona","C":"Inferencia en tiempo real","D":"Inferencia sin servidor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La inferencia por lotes est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para predicciones de gran volumen sobre grandes conjuntos de datos procesados juntos seg\xfan un cronograma, por lo que la empresa puede ejecutar un \xfanico trabajo diario sobre todos los registros de env\xedos acumulados para generar sus pron\xf3sticos sin mantener un endpoint persistente.\\nLa inferencia as\xedncrona es adecuada para manejar solicitudes individuales con cargas \xfatiles grandes o tiempos de procesamiento largos mediante su puesta en cola, en lugar de puntuar un conjunto de datos diario completo en una \xfanica ejecuci\xf3n programada. La inferencia en tiempo real proporciona predicciones inmediatas a trav\xe9s de un endpoint que se ejecuta continuamente para casos de uso de baja latencia, lo cual es innecesario y m\xe1s costoso cuando los resultados solo se necesitan una vez al d\xeda. La inferencia sin servidor aprovisiona autom\xe1ticamente capacidad para solicitudes intermitentes y bajo demanda, lo que se adapta al tr\xe1fico impredecible en lugar de a un trabajo por lotes predecible procesado seg\xfan un cronograma diario fijo."},{"id":"aif-q209","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica de ML utiliza datos de entrenamiento etiquetados con los valores de salida correctos?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje supervisado","B":"Aprendizaje no supervisado","C":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo","D":"Aprendizaje por transferencia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El aprendizaje supervisado entrena modelos con conjuntos de datos en los que cada ejemplo de entrada se empareja con una etiqueta de salida correcta conocida, lo que permite al modelo aprender la correspondencia entre las entradas y los objetivos y hacer predicciones sobre nuevos datos no vistos.\\nEl aprendizaje no supervisado trabaja con datos completamente sin etiquetar y descubre patrones o agrupaciones ocultos sin ning\xfan valor de salida conocido del cual aprender. El aprendizaje por refuerzo no utiliza datos de entrenamiento etiquetados en absoluto; en su lugar, entrena a un agente para tomar acciones en un entorno recompensando los resultados deseables y penalizando los indeseables. El aprendizaje por transferencia es una t\xe9cnica en la que un modelo preentrenado en una tarea se adapta para una tarea diferente, lo que se trata de reutilizar el conocimiento existente del modelo en lugar de describir c\xf3mo est\xe1n estructurados los datos de entrenamiento."},{"id":"aif-q216","question":"Un estudio que produce pel\xedculas animadas quiere generar autom\xe1ticamente subt\xedtulos para el di\xe1logo de sus videos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Transcribe","B":"Amazon Translate","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Transcribe utiliza reconocimiento autom\xe1tico de voz para convertir audio hablado en texto con marcas de tiempo y puede generar archivos de subt\xedtulos, por lo que puede generar los subt\xedtulos para el di\xe1logo de los videos animados del estudio.\\nAmazon Translate convierte texto de un idioma a otro, pero no convierte audio en texto, por lo que no puede producir subt\xedtulos por s\xed solo. Amazon Polly realiza la tarea opuesta: sintetiza voz a partir de texto escrito en lugar de transcribir voz. Amazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que extrae informaci\xf3n como sentimiento y entidades de texto existente y no crea transcripciones a partir de audio."},{"id":"aif-q217","question":"Un minorista en l\xednea quiere dividir a sus clientes en segmentos seg\xfan su comportamiento de compra y sus preferencias para poder adaptar la experiencia de la aplicaci\xf3n a cada segmento. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica de ML deber\xeda utilizar?","options":{"A":"Clasificaci\xf3n","B":"Regresi\xf3n","C":"Agrupamiento (clustering)","D":"Generaci\xf3n de contenido"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El agrupamiento (clustering) es una t\xe9cnica no supervisada que agrupa autom\xe1ticamente a clientes con historiales de compra y preferencias similares en segmentos sin etiquetas predefinidas, que es exactamente lo que el minorista necesita para personalizar la experiencia de cada segmento.\\nLa clasificaci\xf3n es una t\xe9cnica supervisada que asigna elementos a categor\xedas predefinidas, lo que requerir\xeda que los segmentos estuvieran etiquetados de antemano, pero no lo est\xe1n. La regresi\xf3n es una t\xe9cnica supervisada que predice un valor num\xe9rico continuo, como el gasto esperado, no grupos de clientes similares. La generaci\xf3n de contenido produce contenido nuevo, como texto o im\xe1genes, y no segmenta clientes en absoluto."},{"id":"aif-q228","question":"Una empresa quiere leer autom\xe1ticamente los correos electr\xf3nicos de soporte entrantes y dirigir cada uno a un tema como facturaci\xf3n, problemas t\xe9cnicos o cambios de cuenta. \xbfQu\xe9 concepto de IA representa este escenario?","options":{"A":"Reconocimiento de voz","B":"Procesamiento del Lenguaje Natural (NLP)","C":"Visi\xf3n por Computadora","D":"Pron\xf3stico"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Leer correos electr\xf3nicos de soporte y dirigir cada uno a un tema es una tarea de Procesamiento del Lenguaje Natural, espec\xedficamente clasificaci\xf3n de texto, porque requiere interpretar lenguaje humano en texto y asignarlo a una categor\xeda predefinida.\\nEl Reconocimiento de voz convierte audio hablado en texto y se ocupa de la palabra hablada, en lugar de analizar y categorizar texto escrito existente. La Visi\xf3n por Computadora interpreta im\xe1genes y video en lugar de lenguaje, por lo que no se aplica al contenido de correos electr\xf3nicos. El Pron\xf3stico predice valores num\xe9ricos futuros a partir de datos hist\xf3ricos, lo cual no est\xe1 relacionado con ordenar mensajes escritos por tema."},{"id":"aif-q232","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando una aplicaci\xf3n que debe organizar autom\xe1ticamente clientes y productos en grupos de elementos similares seg\xfan sus atributos. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de ML debe usar la empresa para cumplir este requisito?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje supervisado","B":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo","C":"Aprendizaje no supervisado","D":"Aprendizaje semisupervisado"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El aprendizaje no supervisado descubre autom\xe1ticamente patrones y agrupaciones naturales dentro de los datos, lo que lo convierte en la estrategia correcta para agrupar clientes y productos similares por sus atributos cuando no se proporcionan grupos predefinidos.\\nEl aprendizaje supervisado requiere datos de entrenamiento etiquetados con salidas objetivo conocidas, por lo que no puede formar grupos cuando no se suministran etiquetas ni categor\xedas. El aprendizaje por refuerzo entrena a un agente mediante recompensas y penalizaciones a medida que interact\xfaa con un entorno, lo cual no se aplica a agrupar elementos en un conjunto de datos est\xe1tico. El aprendizaje semisupervisado combina una peque\xf1a cantidad de datos etiquetados con un grupo mayor de datos no etiquetados, pero aqu\xed no hay etiquetas en absoluto, por lo que un enfoque totalmente no supervisado es la opci\xf3n correcta."},{"id":"aif-q233","question":"Una plataforma de aprendizaje en l\xednea publica sus materiales de curso en ingl\xe9s y quiere poner el mismo contenido a disposici\xf3n de estudiantes en varios otros idiomas. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Agregar Amazon Transcribe a la plataforma","B":"Usar la funci\xf3n de traducci\xf3n en tiempo real de Amazon Translate","C":"Agregar Amazon Personalize a la plataforma","D":"Usar la funci\xf3n de procesamiento de documentos en tiempo real de Amazon Textract"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Translate proporciona traducci\xf3n de idiomas r\xe1pida y de alta calidad de texto, lo que lo convierte en la opci\xf3n correcta para convertir autom\xe1ticamente materiales de cursos del ingl\xe9s a varios otros idiomas.\\nAmazon Transcribe es un servicio de reconocimiento autom\xe1tico de voz que convierte audio hablado en texto, el cual no traduce contenido entre idiomas. Amazon Personalize genera recomendaciones personalizadas basadas en el comportamiento del usuario, lo cual no est\xe1 relacionado con traducir contenido. Amazon Textract extrae texto y datos estructurados de documentos escaneados e im\xe1genes, lo que aborda la extracci\xf3n de datos de documentos en lugar de la traducci\xf3n de idiomas."},{"id":"aif-q236","question":"Un cineasta documental quiere llegar a una audiencia m\xe1s amplia agregando autom\xe1ticamente subt\xedtulos y voces en off en varios idiomas a sus pel\xedculas. \xbfQu\xe9 dos pasos en conjunto cumplen este requisito?","options":{"A":"Usar Amazon Polly para generar voces en off en varios idiomas","B":"Usar Amazon Transcribe y Amazon Translate para producir subt\xedtulos en varios idiomas","C":"Usar Amazon Translate para generar voces en off en varios idiomas","D":"Usar Amazon Textract y Amazon Translate para producir subt\xedtulos en varios idiomas"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Amazon Transcribe convierte el di\xe1logo hablado de la pel\xedcula en texto mediante reconocimiento autom\xe1tico de voz, y Amazon Translate luego convierte ese texto a otros idiomas para crear subt\xedtulos multiling\xfces. Amazon Polly es un servicio de conversi\xf3n de texto a voz que convierte el texto traducido en audio hablado realista, lo que permite las voces en off multiling\xfces que necesita el cineasta.\\nLa opci\xf3n que combina Amazon Textract con Amazon Translate es incorrecta porque Textract extrae texto de documentos escaneados e im\xe1genes mediante reconocimiento \xf3ptico de caracteres, no de audio hablado en archivos de video. La opci\xf3n que usa solo Amazon Translate para voces en off es incorrecta porque Translate convierte texto entre idiomas, pero genera texto, no audio; generar voces en off habladas requiere Amazon Polly para sintetizar voz a partir del texto traducido."},{"id":"aif-q240","question":"Una empresa de atenci\xf3n m\xe9dica ha entrenado un modelo con datos hist\xf3ricos de pacientes, incluidos antecedentes m\xe9dicos, datos demogr\xe1ficos y tratamientos, para predecir en tiempo real si un paciente ser\xe1 reingresado dentro de los 30 d\xedas posteriores al alta. \xbfQu\xe9 tarea de este escenario representa la inferencia del modelo de IA?","options":{"A":"Aplicar el modelo entrenado para predecir si un paciente ser\xe1 reingresado","B":"Recopilar registros hist\xf3ricos de reingresos de pacientes","C":"Medir el rendimiento del modelo usando m\xe9tricas de evaluaci\xf3n adecuadas","D":"Analizar los datos para descubrir patrones y correlaciones entre pacientes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La inferencia del modelo es la etapa de aplicar un modelo ya entrenado a datos nuevos para generar predicciones, por lo que usar el modelo entrenado para predecir si un paciente ser\xe1 reingresado es la tarea que representa la inferencia en este escenario.\\nRecopilar registros hist\xf3ricos de reingresos es la etapa de recopilaci\xf3n de datos que ocurre antes del entrenamiento. Medir el rendimiento del modelo con m\xe9tricas de evaluaci\xf3n es la etapa de evaluaci\xf3n, que eval\xfaa un modelo en lugar de servir predicciones. Analizar los datos para descubrir patrones y correlaciones es un an\xe1lisis exploratorio realizado antes o durante la construcci\xf3n del modelo, no el acto de generar predicciones a partir de un modelo terminado."},{"id":"aif-q241","question":"Un banco quiere configurar flujos de trabajo en los que el personal revise las predicciones producidas por sus modelos de ML. Necesita definir umbrales de confianza que determinen cu\xe1ndo se env\xeda una predicci\xf3n para revisi\xf3n humana y ajustar esos umbrales a medida que cambian sus necesidades. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","B":"Amazon Inspector","C":"Amazon Augmented AI (Amazon A2I)","D":"AWS Audit Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Augmented AI (Amazon A2I) est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para agregar revisi\xf3n humana de predicciones de ML, lo que permite al banco establecer umbrales de confianza que deciden cu\xe1ndo se env\xeda una predicci\xf3n a un revisor humano y ajustar esos umbrales con el tiempo.\\nAmazon SageMaker Ground Truth tambi\xe9n se basa en trabajadores humanos, pero es para etiquetar conjuntos de datos de entrenamiento en lugar de revisar predicciones de modelos en vivo contra umbrales de confianza. Amazon Inspector es un servicio de seguridad automatizado que escanea cargas de trabajo en busca de vulnerabilidades, y AWS Audit Manager automatiza la recopilaci\xf3n de evidencia para auditor\xedas de cumplimiento; ninguno tiene relaci\xf3n con la revisi\xf3n humana de predicciones de ML."},{"id":"aif-q243","question":"Una empresa minorista quiere crear e implementar modelos de ML en AWS sin escribir c\xf3digo. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Rekognition","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas","D":"AWS DeepRacer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas ofrece una interfaz visual sin c\xf3digo que permite a los usuarios crear, entrenar e implementar modelos de ML personalizados sin escribir c\xf3digo, que es exactamente lo que la empresa necesita.\\nAmazon Rekognition (Visi\xf3n por Computadora) y Amazon Comprehend (Procesamiento del Lenguaje Natural) son servicios de IA preconstruidos que devuelven predicciones de sus propios modelos en lugar de permitirle crear e implementar los suyos. AWS DeepRacer es una herramienta educativa para aprender Aprendizaje por refuerzo mediante un autom\xf3vil de carreras simulado, no un servicio para crear modelos de ML empresariales."},{"id":"aif-q251","question":"Una empresa necesita recopilar un conjunto de datos grande para entrenar a un asistente de IA en un \xe1rea de contenido espec\xedfica. \xbfQu\xe9 conjunto de datos cumplir\xe1 este requisito?","options":{"A":"Conversaciones diversas que usan terminolog\xeda relevante","B":"Datos de series temporales de ventas hist\xf3ricas de prop\xf3sito general","C":"An\xe1lisis de sentimiento de art\xedculos de noticias","D":"IDs de producto \xfanicos e IDs de usuario correspondientes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Conversaciones diversas que usan terminolog\xeda relevante proporcionan el lenguaje contextual y espec\xedfico del dominio que un asistente de IA necesita para aprender a comprender y responder con precisi\xf3n dentro del \xe1rea de contenido objetivo, lo que convierte a este en el conjunto de datos de entrenamiento adecuado.\\nLos datos de series temporales de ventas hist\xf3ricas de prop\xf3sito general son datos num\xe9ricos estructurados adecuados para pronosticar tendencias a lo largo del tiempo, no para entrenar a un asistente conversacional en una materia especializada. El an\xe1lisis de sentimiento de art\xedculos de noticias produce clasificaciones de tono positivo o negativo en lugar de los ejemplos conversacionales necesarios para ense\xf1ar a un asistente c\xf3mo interactuar. Los IDs de producto \xfanicos y los IDs de usuario correspondientes son identificadores usados para tareas como sistemas de recomendaci\xf3n, y no contienen contenido ling\xfc\xedstico ni contextual del que un asistente pueda aprender a conversar."},{"id":"aif-q256","question":"Un minorista en l\xednea quiere dividir a sus clientes en grupos seg\xfan sus datos demogr\xe1ficos y su comportamiento de compra. \xbfQu\xe9 algoritmo deber\xeda usar para cumplir este requisito?","options":{"A":"\xc1rbol de decisi\xf3n","B":"M\xe1quina de vectores de soporte","C":"K-vecinos m\xe1s cercanos (k-NN)","D":"K-means"},"correct":"D","explanation":"K-means es un algoritmo de agrupamiento no supervisado que divide los puntos de datos en un n\xfamero elegido de grupos seg\xfan su similitud, por lo que segmenta naturalmente a los clientes por datos demogr\xe1ficos y patrones de compra compartidos sin necesidad de ejemplos etiquetados.\\nK-vecinos m\xe1s cercanos (k-NN), \xc1rbol de decisi\xf3n y M\xe1quina de vectores de soporte son todos algoritmos supervisados que requieren datos de entrenamiento etiquetados para predecir un objetivo o clase conocidos, por lo que sirven para clasificaci\xf3n o regresi\xf3n en lugar de descubrir agrupaciones naturales en datos de clientes no etiquetados."},{"id":"aif-q264","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes escenarios es un ejemplo de aprendizaje no supervisado?","options":{"A":"Un modelo que organiza miles de art\xedculos de noticias en temas relacionados sin categor\xedas predefinidas","B":"Un modelo que marca una solicitud de pr\xe9stamo como de alto riesgo o de bajo riesgo","C":"Un modelo que pronostica la demanda de electricidad de una ciudad para el d\xeda siguiente","D":"Un modelo que ense\xf1a a un robot a caminar recompensando movimientos exitosos"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Organizar art\xedculos de noticias en temas relacionados sin categor\xedas predefinidas es un ejemplo de aprendizaje no supervisado, donde el modelo encuentra patrones y agrupaciones naturales en los datos sin depender de salidas etiquetadas.\\nMarcar una solicitud de pr\xe9stamo como de alto riesgo o de bajo riesgo es aprendizaje supervisado, porque el modelo se entrena con ejemplos etiquetados donde el resultado correcto ya se conoce. Pronosticar la demanda de electricidad de una ciudad para el d\xeda siguiente tambi\xe9n es aprendizaje supervisado, espec\xedficamente una tarea de regresi\xf3n que aprende de valores hist\xf3ricos etiquetados. Ense\xf1ar a un robot a caminar recompensando movimientos exitosos es aprendizaje por refuerzo, donde un agente mejora al recibir recompensas y penalizaciones por sus acciones en lugar de descubrir agrupaciones en datos no etiquetados."},{"id":"aif-q268","question":"Un equipo de soporte usa an\xe1lisis de sentimiento para determinar el estado de \xe1nimo expresado en los comentarios de los clientes. El an\xe1lisis de sentimiento es un subconjunto de \xbfqu\xe9 campo m\xe1s amplio de la IA?","options":{"A":"Procesamiento de Lenguaje Natural (NLP)","B":"Visi\xf3n por Computadora","C":"Pron\xf3stico de series temporales","D":"Reconocimiento de voz"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El an\xe1lisis de sentimiento es una tarea de Procesamiento de Lenguaje Natural (NLP). NLP es el campo de la IA enfocado en permitir que las m\xe1quinas comprendan, interpreten y extraigan significado del lenguaje humano, y detectar el tono emocional del texto es una de sus aplicaciones principales.\\nVisi\xf3n por Computadora se ocupa de interpretar im\xe1genes y video en lugar de lenguaje. El Reconocimiento de voz convierte audio hablado en texto y no analiza por s\xed mismo opiniones o sentimiento. El Pron\xf3stico de series temporales predice valores num\xe9ricos futuros a partir de datos hist\xf3ricos secuenciales y no est\xe1 relacionado con comprender el lenguaje."},{"id":"aif-q270","question":"Una empresa de SaaS recopila grandes vol\xfamenes de comentarios de clientes de formato libre y quiere analizar su sentimiento. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Usar un algoritmo de regresi\xf3n para clasificar los comentarios en categor\xedas predefinidas","B":"Usar un motor de recomendaciones para detectar el sentimiento del usuario","C":"Usar un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) para realizar Procesamiento de Lenguaje Natural (NLP) para el an\xe1lisis de sentimiento","D":"Usar un algoritmo de series temporales para predecir el sentimiento del usuario a partir de comentarios pasados"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Un modelo de lenguaje grande puede aplicar Procesamiento de Lenguaje Natural (NLP) para leer texto no estructurado y determinar si el sentimiento es positivo, negativo o neutro, lo que se adapta a grandes vol\xfamenes de comentarios de formato libre.\\nUn algoritmo de regresi\xf3n predice valores num\xe9ricos, no el sentimiento a partir de texto. Un motor de recomendaciones sugiere elementos seg\xfan el comportamiento. Un algoritmo de series temporales pronostica valores a lo largo del tiempo. Ninguno de estos analiza el sentimiento de texto no estructurado."},{"id":"aif-q273","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un ejemplo de Aprendizaje No Supervisado?","options":{"A":"Entrenar un modelo para identificar animales en fotograf\xedas","B":"Producir texto similar al humano en respuesta a un mensaje escrito","C":"Agrupar puntos de datos en grupos seg\xfan qu\xe9 tan similares son","D":"Predecir el precio de venta de una casa a partir de caracter\xedsticas como su tama\xf1o y ubicaci\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Agrupar puntos de datos en grupos por similitud es un ejemplo cl\xe1sico de Aprendizaje No Supervisado, donde el algoritmo encuentra estructura y agrupaciones naturales en los datos por s\xed solo, sin ejemplos etiquetados que lo gu\xeden.\\nEntrenar un modelo para identificar animales en fotograf\xedas es Aprendizaje Supervisado, ya que aprende de im\xe1genes ya etiquetadas con el animal correcto. Predecir el precio de venta de una casa a partir de sus caracter\xedsticas tambi\xe9n es Aprendizaje Supervisado, espec\xedficamente regresi\xf3n, porque se entrena con ejemplos de precios conocidos. Producir texto similar al humano a partir de un mensaje es IA Generativa, que crea contenido nuevo en lugar de descubrir agrupaciones en datos no etiquetados."},{"id":"aif-q276","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 entrenando modelos de ML con conjuntos de datos. Los conjuntos de datos contienen algunas clases que tienen m\xe1s ejemplos que otras clases. La empresa quiere medir qu\xe9 tan bien equilibra el modelo la detecci\xf3n y el etiquetado de las clases. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica deber\xeda usar la empresa?","options":{"A":"Exactitud","B":"Recall","C":"Precisi\xf3n","D":"Puntuaci\xf3n F1"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La puntuaci\xf3n F1 es la media arm\xf3nica de precisi\xf3n y recall, lo que la convierte en la m\xe9trica m\xe1s apropiada para conjuntos de datos desequilibrados porque penaliza a los modelos que tienen buen rendimiento en una medida pero malo en la otra, asegurando que el modelo realmente equilibre tanto la detecci\xf3n de todas las instancias de una clase como su etiquetado correcto en todas las clases.\\nLa exactitud mide el porcentaje general de predicciones correctas, pero es enga\xf1osa en conjuntos de datos desequilibrados porque un modelo que siempre predice la clase mayoritaria puede lograr una alta exactitud mientras falla por completo en detectar las clases minoritarias. Recall mide qu\xe9 tan bien detecta el modelo todas las instancias reales de cada clase, capturando la capacidad de encontrar verdaderos positivos, pero ignora la tasa de falsos positivos y, por lo tanto, no refleja qu\xe9 tan bien equilibra el modelo la detecci\xf3n y el etiquetado correcto simult\xe1neamente. La precisi\xf3n mide con qu\xe9 frecuencia una predicci\xf3n positiva es realmente correcta, pero ignora los falsos negativos, lo que significa que un modelo que hace muy pocas predicciones puede lograr una alta precisi\xf3n mientras pierde la mayor\xeda de las instancias reales en clases subrepresentadas."},{"id":"aif-q277","question":"Un banco revisa registros de transacciones financieras y asigna cada uno a una categor\xeda personal o empresarial, registrando esa categor\xeda en el registro. \xbfQu\xe9 paso de preparaci\xf3n de datos describe esto?","options":{"A":"Normalizaci\xf3n de datos","B":"Codificaci\xf3n de datos","C":"Balanceo de datos","D":"Etiquetado de datos"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El etiquetado de datos es el paso de adjuntar una categor\xeda o etiqueta significativa a cada registro para que pueda servir como objetivo para el Aprendizaje Supervisado, que es exactamente lo que logra asignar a cada transacci\xf3n una categor\xeda personal o empresarial y almacenarla en el registro.\\nLa codificaci\xf3n de datos convierte valores categ\xf3ricos en una forma num\xe9rica que los algoritmos pueden procesar, en lugar de crear las categor\xedas en primer lugar. La normalizaci\xf3n de datos reescala las caracter\xedsticas num\xe9ricas a un rango com\xfan para que ninguna caracter\xedstica individual domine. El balanceo de datos ajusta la proporci\xf3n de clases en un conjunto de datos, por ejemplo mediante sobremuestreo o submuestreo, para que est\xe9n representadas de manera m\xe1s uniforme; ninguno de estos produce las etiquetas de categor\xeda en s\xed."},{"id":"aif-q280","question":"Un proveedor de energ\xeda quiere monitorear los niveles de voltaje en una red el\xe9ctrica en regiones rurales. Planea registrar lecturas de voltaje continuamente y almacenarlas en Amazon RDS, luego analizar c\xf3mo fluct\xfaan las lecturas a lo largo de cada d\xeda para construir un modelo de IA que prediga posibles cortes de energ\xeda. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de datos deber\xeda recopilar el proveedor para esta tarea?","options":{"A":"Datos tabulares","B":"Datos de texto","C":"Datos de series temporales","D":"Datos de audio"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los datos de series temporales consisten en valores registrados secuencialmente a lo largo del tiempo, como lecturas de voltaje tomadas a intervalos regulares, que es exactamente lo que se necesita para analizar las fluctuaciones a lo largo del d\xeda y construir un modelo que prediga cortes de energ\xeda.\\nLos datos tabulares organizan la informaci\xf3n en filas y columnas de caracter\xedsticas, pero no capturan inherentemente la secuencia ordenada en el tiempo requerida para modelar cambios a lo largo del d\xeda. Los datos de texto representan lenguaje escrito no estructurado, como documentos o mensajes, que no es relevante para mediciones num\xe9ricas de voltaje. Los datos de audio capturan se\xf1ales de sonido y no tienen aplicaci\xf3n para monitorear lecturas el\xe9ctricas a lo largo del tiempo."},{"id":"aif-q281","question":"Un fabricante quiere entrenar un modelo de ML que marque lecturas inusuales en los datos de sensores de sus equipos, pero no tiene ejemplos etiquetados de los cuales aprender. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9todo de ML cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"\xc1rbol de decisi\xf3n","B":"Autoencoders","C":"Regresi\xf3n lineal","D":"Regresi\xf3n log\xedstica"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Autoencoders es un m\xe9todo no supervisado que aprende a reconstruir patrones normales en los datos; las lecturas que producen un error de reconstrucci\xf3n grande se destacan como anomal\xedas, por lo que la t\xe9cnica puede detectar comportamiento an\xf3malo de los sensores sin ning\xfan dato de entrenamiento etiquetado.\\n\xc1rbol de decisi\xf3n, regresi\xf3n lineal y regresi\xf3n log\xedstica fallan por la misma raz\xf3n: son m\xe9todos supervisados que requieren ejemplos etiquetados de los cuales aprender, que la empresa no tiene. La regresi\xf3n lineal predice valores continuos y las otras dos realizan clasificaci\xf3n, pero ninguna puede entrenarse para detecci\xf3n de anomal\xedas con datos no etiquetados."},{"id":"aif-q286","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n de transmisi\xf3n de m\xfasica quiere sugerir canciones a cada oyente seg\xfan su historial de escucha. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Personalize","B":"Amazon Transcribe","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Personalize es un servicio de ML totalmente gestionado que ofrece recomendaciones personalizadas en tiempo real analizando el historial, las interacciones y el comportamiento de cada usuario, lo que lo hace id\xf3neo para casos de uso como sugerir canciones en una aplicaci\xf3n de transmisi\xf3n de m\xfasica.\\nAmazon Transcribe es un servicio de reconocimiento autom\xe1tico del habla que convierte audio y voz en transcripciones de texto y no desempe\xf1a ning\xfan papel en el an\xe1lisis del historial de escucha ni en la generaci\xf3n de recomendaciones. Amazon Polly es un servicio de texto a voz que sintetiza texto escrito en habla realista y no puede generar recomendaciones. Amazon Comprehend es un servicio de Procesamiento de Lenguaje Natural (NLP) que extrae informaci\xf3n como sentimiento, entidades y frases clave del texto y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para producir recomendaciones personalizadas."},{"id":"aif-q290","question":"Un equipo est\xe1 adoptando MLOps para gestionar sus flujos de trabajo de machine learning de forma m\xe1s fiable. \xbfQu\xe9 dos pr\xe1cticas debe priorizar el equipo?","options":{"A":"Versionado de modelos para la reproducibilidad","B":"Pruebas y validaci\xf3n automatizadas de modelos","C":"Procesos de despliegue solo manuales","D":"Documentaci\xf3n m\xednima","E":"Sin monitoreo en producci\xf3n"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"El versionado de modelos para la reproducibilidad permite a los equipos rastrear cambios, reproducir resultados y revertir a versiones anteriores cuando sea necesario, lo que respalda directamente el objetivo de MLOps de procesos repetibles. Las pruebas y validaci\xf3n automatizadas de modelos verifican que un modelo se desempe\xf1e correctamente antes del despliegue, lo que respalda el objetivo de MLOps de lograr preparaci\xf3n para producci\xf3n. Juntas, estas pr\xe1cticas ayudan a los equipos a construir sistemas de ML escalables y fiables seg\xfan se define en el objetivo 1.3.5.\\nLos procesos de despliegue solo manuales contradicen el objetivo de MLOps de automatizar flujos de trabajo repetibles para reducir errores y aumentar la consistencia. La documentaci\xf3n m\xednima socava la colaboraci\xf3n, la auditabilidad y el mantenimiento, todos importantes para operaciones de ML sostenibles y la gesti\xf3n de la deuda t\xe9cnica. Sin monitoreo en producci\xf3n es lo contrario de una buena pr\xe1ctica, ya que el monitoreo continuo es esencial para detectar la degradaci\xf3n del rendimiento y la deriva de datos con el tiempo, lo cual se menciona expl\xedcitamente en los objetivos de MLOps."},{"id":"aif-q300","question":"En el contexto de la IA, \xbfa qu\xe9 se refiere el t\xe9rmino inferencia?","options":{"A":"Recopilar los datos de entrenamiento usados para construir sistemas de IA","B":"Dise\xf1ar algoritmos de IA completamente nuevos desde cero","C":"Fusionar varios modelos de IA en un \xfanico modelo","D":"Aplicar un modelo ya entrenado a datos nuevos y no vistos para producir predicciones o decisiones"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La inferencia es la etapa en la que un modelo ya entrenado se aplica a datos nuevos y no vistos para generar predicciones o decisiones, aprovechando los patrones que aprendi\xf3 durante el entrenamiento. Es distinta del entrenamiento, cuando el modelo todav\xeda est\xe1 aprendiendo de los datos.\\nDise\xf1ar nuevos algoritmos de IA es trabajo de investigaci\xf3n y desarrollo, no el acto de ejecutar un modelo terminado. Fusionar varios modelos en uno describe el ensamblado o la consolidaci\xf3n de modelos, en lugar de producir predicciones. Recopilar datos de entrenamiento es el paso de recolecci\xf3n de datos que ocurre antes del entrenamiento, mucho antes de la inferencia."},{"id":"aif-q304","question":"Una agencia de salud p\xfablica ha recopilado un a\xf1o de registros de pacientes y quiere producir un informe mensual de tendencias para los funcionarios. Cada mes necesita ejecutar un modelo sobre un gran conjunto de registros hist\xf3ricos en un momento programado, sin requisito de resultados inmediatos. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9todo de inferencia satisface esta necesidad de la forma m\xe1s rentable?","options":{"A":"Inferencia en tiempo real","B":"Inferencia as\xedncrona","C":"Batch transform","D":"Inferencia sin servidor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Batch transform est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para ejecutar inferencia sobre un gran conjunto de datos de una vez como un trabajo programado y sin conexi\xf3n, por lo que la agencia puede procesar un mes completo de registros para generar el informe sin pagar por un endpoint en ejecuci\xf3n continua, lo que lo convierte en la opci\xf3n m\xe1s rentable para esta carga de trabajo masiva peri\xf3dica.\\nLa inferencia en tiempo real mantiene un endpoint permanentemente disponible para predicciones individuales de baja latencia, lo cual es innecesario y m\xe1s costoso para un trabajo masivo programado. La inferencia sin servidor es adecuada para tr\xe1fico de solicitudes intermitente e impredecible que a\xfan necesita una respuesta r\xe1pida por solicitud, no para el procesamiento masivo programado por lotes. La inferencia as\xedncrona pone en cola solicitudes individuales con cargas \xfatiles grandes o tiempos de procesamiento largos para un manejo casi en tiempo real, pero a\xfan ejecuta un endpoint alojado y no est\xe1 pensada para procesar un conjunto de datos completo en una sola pasada."},{"id":"aif-q306","question":"Un banco est\xe1 construyendo un modelo de ML para predecir la probabilidad de que un solicitante de pr\xe9stamo incurra en incumplimiento. El modelo usa datos del solicitante, como ingresos, puntaje crediticio, antig\xfcedad laboral y deuda existente. El conjunto de datos hist\xf3rico incluye una etiqueta que indica si cada solicitante anterior finalmente incurri\xf3 en incumplimiento. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica de ML cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje no supervisado","B":"Aprendizaje supervisado","C":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo","D":"Aprendizaje semisupervisado"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El aprendizaje supervisado es la t\xe9cnica correcta cuando el conjunto de datos contiene tanto caracter\xedsticas de entrada, como ingresos, puntaje crediticio y deuda existente, como una etiqueta conocida que indica el resultado, porque el modelo aprende a predecir ese objetivo etiquetado a partir de los datos de ejemplo.\\nEl aprendizaje no supervisado trabaja con datos no etiquetados para descubrir patrones o grupos, lo que no se ajusta a un problema en el que ya se proporciona un resultado de incumplimiento conocido para cada registro. El aprendizaje por refuerzo entrena a un agente mediante recompensas y penalizaciones a medida que interact\xfaa con un entorno, lo que no aplica a predecir un resultado a partir de un conjunto de datos etiquetado fijo. El aprendizaje semisupervisado se usa cuando solo una peque\xf1a parte de los datos est\xe1 etiquetada y el resto no, mientras que aqu\xed cada registro hist\xf3rico ya tiene un resultado conocido, lo que hace que el aprendizaje totalmente supervisado sea la opci\xf3n adecuada."},{"id":"aif-q308","question":"Un servicio para compartir fotos necesita transponer y rotar grandes lotes de im\xe1genes usando transformaciones num\xe9ricas simples. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n es M\xc1S eficiente operativamente?","options":{"A":"Construir una red neuronal profunda que tome las im\xe1genes como entrada","B":"Usar un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) de Amazon Bedrock con una temperatura alta","C":"Crear una funci\xf3n de AWS Lambda para realizar las transformaciones","D":"Usar AWS Glue Data Quality para corregir cada imagen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Una funci\xf3n de AWS Lambda puede ejecutar operaciones num\xe9ricas sencillas sobre im\xe1genes, como transponer y rotar, a escala sin entrenar ni alojar ning\xfan modelo, lo que es el enfoque m\xe1s eficiente operativamente.\\nConstruir una red neuronal profunda es un esfuerzo innecesario para transformaciones deterministas. Un LLM de Amazon Bedrock genera texto y no puede transformar im\xe1genes. AWS Glue Data Quality valida y limpia datos, no rota im\xe1genes."},{"id":"aif-q312","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n describe mejor c\xf3mo se relacionan la Inteligencia Artificial (IA) y el Machine Learning (ML)?","options":{"A":"La IA es una rama del ML","B":"IA y ML significan lo mismo","C":"El ML es una rama de la IA","D":"IA y ML son campos no relacionados"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Machine Learning es una rama de la Inteligencia Artificial. La IA es el campo amplio de construir sistemas que imitan la inteligencia humana, y el ML es un enfoque dentro de ella que permite a los sistemas aprender patrones a partir de datos y mejorar sin ser programados expl\xedcitamente para cada regla.\\nAfirmar que la IA es una rama del ML invierte la relaci\xf3n y es incorrecto. Decir que los dos no est\xe1n relacionados ignora que el ML est\xe1 dentro del campo m\xe1s amplio de la IA. Afirmar que significan lo mismo equipara err\xf3neamente el campo amplio con solo una de sus t\xe9cnicas, pasando por alto otros enfoques de IA, como los sistemas basados en reglas o expertos."},{"id":"aif-q313","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes NO es un tipo de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje supervisado","B":"Aprendizaje no supervisado","C":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo","D":"Aprendizaje diagn\xf3stico"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El aprendizaje diagn\xf3stico no es un tipo reconocido de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico y no aparece en la literatura de AI/ML ni en la gu\xeda del examen de AWS, por lo que es la opci\xf3n que no pertenece entre los paradigmas de aprendizaje de ML establecidos.\\nEl aprendizaje supervisado, el aprendizaje no supervisado y el aprendizaje por refuerzo son los tres tipos principales de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico. El aprendizaje supervisado entrena modelos con conjuntos de datos etiquetados donde cada entrada est\xe1 emparejada con una salida correcta conocida. El aprendizaje no supervisado descubre patrones ocultos en datos no etiquetados sin valores de salida predefinidos de los que aprender. El aprendizaje por refuerzo entrena a un agente para tomar decisiones secuenciales recompensando los comportamientos deseables y penalizando los indeseables."},{"id":"aif-q314","question":"Un equipo est\xe1 creando un modelo de Computer Vision. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de datos es el m\xe1s adecuado para entrenarlo?","options":{"A":"Datos de texto","B":"Datos de im\xe1genes","C":"Datos tabulares","D":"Datos de series temporales"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los datos de im\xe1genes son los m\xe1s adecuados porque los modelos de Computer Vision aprenden de las caracter\xedsticas visuales y los patrones de p\xedxeles en las im\xe1genes (y fotogramas de video) para realizar tareas como detecci\xf3n de objetos y clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes.\\nLos datos tabulares contienen filas y columnas estructuradas y se adaptan a problemas cl\xe1sicos de ML como la regresi\xf3n. Los datos de series temporales capturan valores ordenados en el tiempo, como lecturas de sensores o de ventas. Los datos de texto son la entrada para tareas de Natural Language Processing (NLP). Ninguno de estos contiene la informaci\xf3n visual que necesita un modelo de Computer Vision."},{"id":"aif-q315","question":"Una empresa quiere analizar autom\xe1ticamente comentarios de clientes escritos como texto. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS es el m\xe1s adecuado para esta tarea de Natural Language Processing (NLP)?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker AI","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Comprehend es un servicio gestionado de Natural Language Processing que analiza texto para extraer sentimiento, frases clave, entidades e idioma, lo que lo convierte en la opci\xf3n espec\xedficamente dise\xf1ada para analizar comentarios de clientes.\\nAmazon SageMaker AI es una plataforma para crear, entrenar e implementar modelos de ML personalizados, que podr\xeda realizar NLP pero requiere un esfuerzo de desarrollo significativo en lugar de proporcionar capacidades de NLP listas para usar. Amazon Polly es un servicio de texto a voz que convierte texto escrito en habla realista, que es lo opuesto a analizar texto. Amazon Transcribe es un servicio de reconocimiento autom\xe1tico de voz que convierte audio hablado en texto y no analiza el significado ni el sentimiento de ese texto."},{"id":"aif-q316","question":"Durante el ciclo de vida de desarrollo de ML, \xbfpor qu\xe9 realiza un cient\xedfico de datos un an\xe1lisis exploratorio de datos (EDA) antes de crear un modelo?","options":{"A":"Para entrenar el modelo","B":"Para implementar el modelo","C":"Para descubrir la estructura, la calidad y las relaciones dentro de los datos","D":"Para monitorear el modelo en producci\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El an\xe1lisis exploratorio de datos se lleva a cabo al principio del ciclo de vida de desarrollo de ML para descubrir la estructura, la distribuci\xf3n, la calidad y las relaciones dentro de un conjunto de datos, lo que orienta decisiones posteriores sobre limpieza de datos, ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas y selecci\xf3n de algoritmos antes de que comience cualquier entrenamiento.\\nEl entrenamiento del modelo es una etapa posterior que solo ocurre despu\xe9s de que se completan el EDA y la preparaci\xf3n de datos. La implementaci\xf3n del modelo ocurre despu\xe9s del entrenamiento y la evaluaci\xf3n y se ocupa de servir predicciones, no de comprender los datos. El monitoreo del modelo en producci\xf3n forma parte de la etapa final del ciclo de vida, que se centra en detectar degradaci\xf3n del rendimiento y deriva de datos una vez que el modelo ya est\xe1 implementado."},{"id":"aif-q317","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de estas NO ser\xeda normalmente una etapa en un pipeline de ML?","options":{"A":"Entrenamiento del modelo","B":"Publicidad de marca","C":"Recolecci\xf3n de datos","D":"Ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La publicidad de marca es una actividad de marketing dirigida a promocionar un producto y aumentar su reconocimiento, por lo que no forma parte de un pipeline de ML, que se compone de etapas t\xe9cnicas para crear, validar e implementar modelos.\\nLa recolecci\xf3n de datos, la ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas y el entrenamiento del modelo son etapas genuinas de un pipeline de ML t\xedpico. La recolecci\xf3n de datos re\xfane los datos brutos relevantes de las fuentes disponibles. La ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas transforma esos datos brutos en variables de entrada significativas para mejorar el rendimiento del modelo. El entrenamiento del modelo ajusta un modelo a los datos preparados ajustando sus par\xe1metros para minimizar el error de predicci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q318","question":"En el contexto de las m\xe9tricas de rendimiento de modelos, \xbfqu\xe9 significa la sigla AUC?","options":{"A":"Area Under the Curve","B":"Average Usage Cost","C":"Augmented Use Case","D":"Automated Utility Calculation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AUC significa Area Under the Curve, espec\xedficamente el \xe1rea bajo la curva ROC (Receiver Operating Characteristic). Mide qu\xe9 tan bien un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n separa las clases y var\xeda de 0 a 1, donde 1.0 indica una separaci\xf3n perfecta y 0.5 significa que el modelo no es mejor que una conjetura aleatoria.\\nLas otras opciones son expansiones inventadas que combinan una palabra con A, U y C, pero ninguna de ellas es el significado reconocido de AUC como m\xe9trica de rendimiento de modelos."},{"id":"aif-q319","question":"Tienes un conjunto de datos grande en el que cada ejemplo ya est\xe1 etiquetado. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico es el m\xe1s adecuado?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje no supervisado","B":"Aprendizaje supervisado","C":"Aprendizaje semisupervisado","D":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El aprendizaje supervisado se entrena con ejemplos etiquetados, donde cada entrada est\xe1 emparejada con su salida correcta, de modo que el modelo aprende la asignaci\xf3n de entradas a objetivos conocidos. Eso lo convierte en la opci\xf3n correcta cuando se tiene un conjunto de datos grande y totalmente etiquetado.\\nEl aprendizaje no supervisado trabaja con datos no etiquetados para descubrir estructura o agrupaciones ocultas, por lo que no aprovecha las etiquetas disponibles aqu\xed. El aprendizaje semisupervisado est\xe1 pensado para casos con una peque\xf1a cantidad de datos etiquetados junto con una gran cantidad de datos no etiquetados, no para un conjunto totalmente etiquetado. El aprendizaje por refuerzo entrena a un agente mediante recompensas de prueba y error de un entorno y no depende en absoluto de un conjunto de datos etiquetado."},{"id":"aif-q320","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una ventaja clave de usar modelos preentrenados?","options":{"A":"Siempre superan a los modelos personalizados","B":"Est\xe1n garantizados como m\xe1s precisos para cada tarea","C":"Se pueden usar de inmediato sin proporcionar tus propios datos de entrenamiento","D":"Eliminan la necesidad de ajustar para tareas especializadas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Un modelo preentrenado ya ha sido entrenado con un conjunto de datos grande, por lo que se puede aplicar de inmediato para inferencia sin que el usuario proporcione sus propios datos de entrenamiento, lo que ahorra el tiempo, los datos y el esfuerzo de crear un modelo desde cero y es su principal ventaja.\\nAfirmar que un modelo preentrenado siempre supera a los modelos personalizados o que est\xe1 garantizado como m\xe1s preciso para cada tarea son exageraciones, porque un modelo creado o ajustado para una tarea espec\xedfica puede superar f\xe1cilmente a un modelo preentrenado general. Decir que elimina la necesidad de ajustar para tareas especializadas tambi\xe9n es falso, ya que los modelos preentrenados con frecuencia deben ajustarse para funcionar bien en trabajos espec\xedficos de un dominio."},{"id":"aif-q321","question":"Un cient\xedfico de datos quiere encontrar autom\xe1ticamente los hiperpar\xe1metros \xf3ptimos para un modelo en lugar de ajustarlos manualmente. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS es el m\xe1s adecuado para esta tarea?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Autopilot","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Autopilot crea, entrena y ajusta autom\xe1ticamente modelos de machine learning, incluida la exploraci\xf3n y selecci\xf3n de configuraciones de hiperpar\xe1metros en nombre del cient\xedfico de datos, lo que elimina la necesidad de experimentar manualmente con hiperpar\xe1metros.\\nAmazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que extrae informaci\xf3n como sentimiento y entidades de texto, y no tiene ning\xfan papel en el entrenamiento de modelos ni en el ajuste de hiperpar\xe1metros. Amazon Polly es un servicio de texto a voz que convierte texto escrito en voz realista, lo que no est\xe1 relacionado con la creaci\xf3n o el ajuste de modelos de ML. Amazon Transcribe es un servicio de reconocimiento autom\xe1tico de voz que convierte audio en texto y, del mismo modo, no participa en la optimizaci\xf3n de hiperpar\xe1metros."},{"id":"aif-q322","question":"En un contexto de machine learning, \xbfde qu\xe9 es abreviatura el t\xe9rmino MLOps?","options":{"A":"Machine Learning Optimization","B":"Managed Learning Outputs","C":"Machine Learning Operations","D":"Model Lifecycle Operations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MLOps significa Machine Learning Operations, el conjunto de pr\xe1cticas que unen el machine learning con los principios de DevOps para automatizar y gestionar el despliegue, el monitoreo y el mantenimiento continuo de los modelos en producci\xf3n.\\nLas opciones restantes son expansiones inventadas que combinan palabras plausibles relacionadas con machine learning, pero ninguna de ellas es el significado reconocido de la sigla, por lo que todas son incorrectas."},{"id":"aif-q323","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes NO es una m\xe9trica de negocio t\xedpica utilizada para evaluar modelos de Machine Learning?","options":{"A":"Retorno de la inversi\xf3n (ROI)","B":"Puntuaci\xf3n F1","C":"Satisfacci\xf3n del cliente","D":"Tiempo de comercializaci\xf3n"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La puntuaci\xf3n F1 es una m\xe9trica de rendimiento del modelo, la media arm\xf3nica de precisi\xf3n y recall, por lo que mide la calidad de las predicciones en lugar del valor de negocio, lo que la convierte en la opci\xf3n que no es una m\xe9trica de negocio.\\nEl retorno de la inversi\xf3n (ROI), la satisfacci\xf3n del cliente y el tiempo de comercializaci\xf3n son m\xe9tricas de negocio que capturan el impacto real, el valor y los resultados operativos de desplegar un modelo, en lugar de su exactitud estad\xedstica."},{"id":"aif-q324","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes se consideran m\xe9tricas de negocio para la evaluaci\xf3n de modelos de IA?","options":{"A":"Puntuaci\xf3n de precisi\xf3n","B":"Puntuaci\xf3n de satisfacci\xf3n del cliente","C":"Precisi\xf3n media promedio","D":"Tiempo hasta el valor","E":"Error cuadr\xe1tico medio"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"La puntuaci\xf3n de satisfacci\xf3n del cliente mide qu\xe9 tan bien una soluci\xf3n de IA satisface las necesidades del usuario final y refleja directamente el valor entregado al negocio, mientras que el tiempo hasta el valor mide con qu\xe9 rapidez la soluci\xf3n de IA comienza a entregar resultados de negocio medibles, por lo que ambas son ejemplos de m\xe9tricas de negocio utilizadas para evaluar si un modelo de IA est\xe1 cumpliendo los objetivos organizacionales.\\nLa puntuaci\xf3n de precisi\xf3n es una m\xe9trica t\xe9cnica de rendimiento del modelo que mide la proporci\xf3n de predicciones positivas que son realmente correctas (verdaderos positivos divididos entre todas las predicciones positivas), no una medida de resultado de negocio. La precisi\xf3n media promedio es una m\xe9trica t\xe9cnica de evaluaci\xf3n utilizada en tareas de recuperaci\xf3n de informaci\xf3n y detecci\xf3n de objetos para cuantificar qu\xe9 tan bien un modelo clasifica resultados relevantes, midiendo la exactitud en salidas clasificadas en lugar del valor de negocio. El error cuadr\xe1tico medio es una m\xe9trica de regresi\xf3n que mide la magnitud promedio de los errores de predicci\xf3n al tomar la ra\xedz cuadrada de la media de las diferencias al cuadrado entre los valores predichos y los reales; de nuevo, una medida t\xe9cnica de rendimiento en lugar de un indicador de impacto de negocio."},{"id":"aif-q325","question":"Una empresa ha implementado una soluci\xf3n de IA y quiere evaluar su \xe9xito. \xbfQu\xe9 dos tipos de m\xe9tricas deben incluirse en la evaluaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Mejoras en las operaciones de negocio","B":"Exactitud de predicci\xf3n del modelo","C":"Retorno de la inversi\xf3n (ROI)","D":"Solo m\xe9tricas de rendimiento t\xe9cnico","E":"Solo satisfacci\xf3n del usuario"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Las mejoras en las operaciones de negocio y el retorno de la inversi\xf3n (ROI) miden el valor real que entrega una soluci\xf3n de IA y, juntas, complementan la evaluaci\xf3n t\xe9cnica para dar una visi\xf3n completa de si la soluci\xf3n ha tenido \xe9xito.\\nSolo m\xe9tricas de rendimiento t\xe9cnico y solo satisfacci\xf3n del usuario fallan por la misma raz\xf3n: juzgar el \xe9xito en una \xfanica dimensi\xf3n ignora el valor de negocio m\xe1s amplio que la soluci\xf3n pretende crear. La exactitud de predicci\xf3n del modelo es solo una medida t\xe9cnica \xfatil para monitorear la calidad del modelo, pero por s\xed sola no dice nada sobre si la soluci\xf3n cumple sus objetivos de negocio."},{"id":"aif-q326","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 tipo de machine learning es m\xe1s apropiado para entrenar a un agente para que tome decisiones interactuando con su entorno?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje supervisado","B":"Aprendizaje no supervisado","C":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo","D":"Aprendizaje por transferencia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El aprendizaje por refuerzo entrena a un agente para que tome decisiones interactuando con un entorno, donde recibe recompensas por acciones deseables y penalizaciones por acciones indeseables y aprende gradualmente una estrategia que maximiza la recompensa acumulada.\\nEl aprendizaje supervisado entrena un modelo con ejemplos etiquetados con salidas correctas conocidas, en lugar de que un agente aprenda de las consecuencias de sus propias acciones. El aprendizaje no supervisado encuentra patrones o grupos en datos sin etiquetar y no implica un agente, un entorno ni una se\xf1al de recompensa. El aprendizaje por transferencia reutiliza el conocimiento de un modelo entrenado en una tarea para mejorar el rendimiento en una tarea relacionada, lo que describe una forma de reutilizar el conocimiento del modelo en lugar de aprender mediante la interacci\xf3n con el entorno."},{"id":"aif-q327","question":"Una empresa quiere generar una narraci\xf3n de audio hablado a partir de sus art\xedculos escritos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS es la mejor opci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Amazon Transcribe","B":"Amazon Polly","C":"Amazon Translate","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Polly es un servicio de texto a voz que convierte texto escrito en audio hablado realista, que es exactamente lo que requiere generar una narraci\xf3n a partir de art\xedculos.\\nAmazon Transcribe hace lo contrario: convierte audio de voz en texto. Amazon Translate convierte texto de un idioma a otro. Amazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural (NLP) que extrae informaci\xf3n como sentimiento y entidades del texto, pero no produce audio."},{"id":"aif-q328","question":"Dentro del ciclo de vida de desarrollo de ML, \xbfcu\xe1l es el objetivo principal de la ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas?","options":{"A":"Crear nuevas variables de entrada o transformar las existentes para que el modelo tenga un mejor rendimiento","B":"Recopilar datos sin procesar adicionales para el proyecto","C":"Medir qu\xe9 tan bien rinde el modelo entrenado","D":"Lanzar el modelo a un entorno de producci\xf3n"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas es la etapa en la que los datos sin procesar se convierten en entradas \xfatiles para el modelo mediante la creaci\xf3n de nuevas caracter\xedsticas o la transformaci\xf3n de las existentes, lo que mejora la exactitud y el rendimiento general del modelo.\\nRecopilar datos sin procesar adicionales describe la etapa de recopilaci\xf3n de datos, medir qu\xe9 tan bien rinde el modelo describe la evaluaci\xf3n del modelo y lanzar el modelo a producci\xf3n describe el despliegue; cada una de estas es una etapa separada del ciclo de vida de ML en lugar del prop\xf3sito de la ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas."},{"id":"aif-q330","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la diferencia principal entre la inferencia por lotes y la inferencia en tiempo real?","options":{"A":"La inferencia por lotes siempre es m\xe1s precisa","B":"La inferencia en tiempo real solo se puede realizar con conjuntos de datos peque\xf1os","C":"La inferencia por lotes procesa m\xfaltiples entradas a la vez, mientras que la inferencia en tiempo real procesa entradas individuales a medida que llegan","D":"La inferencia en tiempo real siempre es m\xe1s r\xe1pida que la inferencia por lotes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La inferencia por lotes recopila m\xfaltiples entradas y las procesa juntas en un \xfanico trabajo, lo que la hace adecuada para cargas de trabajo no sensibles al tiempo donde la prioridad son el rendimiento y la eficiencia. La inferencia en tiempo real procesa cada entrada individualmente a medida que llega y devuelve una predicci\xf3n de inmediato, lo que la hace adecuada para aplicaciones que requieren respuestas instant\xe1neas, como detecci\xf3n de fraude o moderaci\xf3n de contenido.\\nLa inferencia por lotes no siempre es m\xe1s precisa que la inferencia en tiempo real porque la precisi\xf3n es una propiedad del modelo y de sus datos de entrenamiento, no del m\xe9todo de inferencia utilizado para servir predicciones. La inferencia en tiempo real no est\xe1 limitada a conjuntos de datos peque\xf1os, ya que procesa entradas individuales bajo demanda independientemente del tama\xf1o general de los datos que se sirven. La inferencia en tiempo real no siempre es m\xe1s r\xe1pida que la inferencia por lotes porque, si bien ofrece una latencia menor por solicitud individual, la inferencia por lotes puede lograr un mayor rendimiento general al procesar muchas entradas simult\xe1neamente."},{"id":"aif-q331","question":"Un equipo quiere usar un \xfanico servicio de AWS para construir, entrenar, implementar y monitorear modelos de machine learning de principio a fin. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio es el m\xe1s adecuado para esto?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon SageMaker AI","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Translate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker AI es una plataforma totalmente administrada que admite todo el ciclo de vida de machine learning, incluidos la preparaci\xf3n de datos, el entrenamiento de modelos, el ajuste de hiperpar\xe1metros, la implementaci\xf3n y el monitoreo continuo, lo que la convierte en la opci\xf3n correcta para administrar modelos de ML de principio a fin.\\nAmazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural para extraer informaci\xf3n como sentimiento y entidades de texto, no una plataforma para administrar todo el ciclo de vida de ML. Amazon Polly es un servicio de texto a voz que convierte texto en habla realista, lo que aborda una \xfanica capacidad de IA en lugar del desarrollo de modelos de principio a fin. Amazon Translate proporciona traducci\xf3n de idioma entre idiomas y, de igual manera, cumple una tarea espec\xedfica en lugar de admitir el ciclo de vida completo de ML."},{"id":"aif-q333","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de estos NO es un caso de uso t\xedpico de IA o ML?","options":{"A":"Detecci\xf3n de fraude","B":"Mantenimiento manual de registros","C":"Reconocimiento de voz","D":"Sistemas de recomendaci\xf3n"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El mantenimiento manual de registros no es un caso de uso t\xedpico de IA/ML porque es una tarea administrativa rutinaria y determinista que no implica aprender de datos ni hacer predicciones.\\nLa detecci\xf3n de fraude, los sistemas de recomendaci\xf3n y el reconocimiento de voz son aplicaciones establecidas de IA/ML que se basan en modelos de machine learning para identificar patrones, hacer predicciones o interpretar datos no estructurados, como transacciones, comportamiento del usuario o audio."},{"id":"aif-q345","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS se pueden usar para an\xe1lisis de documentos y extracci\xf3n de informaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Amazon Textract","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Translate","E":"Amazon Transcribe"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Amazon Textract extrae texto y datos estructurados, como formularios y tablas, directamente de documentos e im\xe1genes mediante reconocimiento \xf3ptico de caracteres, lo que lo convierte en el servicio principal para la capa de an\xe1lisis de documentos. Luego, Amazon Comprehend aplica procesamiento de lenguaje natural al texto extra\xeddo para identificar entidades, frases clave, sentimiento y otras perspectivas, lo que permite la extracci\xf3n de informaci\xf3n del contenido de los documentos.\\nAmazon Polly convierte texto en voz sintetizada, Amazon Translate realiza traducci\xf3n de idioma entre idiomas y Amazon Transcribe convierte audio hablado en transcripciones de texto. Ninguno de estos servicios est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para an\xe1lisis de documentos o extracci\xf3n de informaci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q016","question":"Un equipo de soporte de telecomunicaciones quiere estudiar de qu\xe9 hablan los clientes durante las llamadas telef\xf3nicas grabadas y extraer los puntos importantes. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Crear etiquetas de clasificaci\xf3n con Amazon Comprehend","B":"Construir un chatbot conversacional con Amazon Lex","C":"Convertir las grabaciones de llamadas a texto con Amazon Transcribe","D":"Extraer informaci\xf3n de las grabaciones con Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Transcribe convierte el habla de las grabaciones de llamadas en texto, que el equipo luego puede analizar para extraer la informaci\xf3n clave tratada.\\nAmazon Comprehend analiza texto para obtener informaci\xf3n como clasificaci\xf3n, pero necesita texto en lugar de audio sin procesar como entrada. Amazon Lex construye chatbots conversacionales y no transcribe grabaciones existentes. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor vigila modelos implementados para detectar deriva y no tiene nada que ver con la transcripci\xf3n de audio."},{"id":"aif-q116","question":"\xbfEn qu\xe9 etapa del ciclo de vida de ML se establecen principalmente las obligaciones regulatorias y de cumplimiento?","options":{"A":"Entrenamiento del modelo","B":"Recopilaci\xf3n de datos","C":"Identificaci\xf3n del objetivo de negocio","D":"Ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las obligaciones regulatorias y de cumplimiento se establecen principalmente durante la identificaci\xf3n del objetivo de negocio, porque definir lo que se quiere construir determina qu\xe9 leyes y regulaciones se aplican al proyecto antes de que se recopilen o procesen datos.\\nLa recopilaci\xf3n de datos es donde esas obligaciones se aplican en la pr\xe1ctica, como obtener consentimiento y verificar las fuentes de datos, pero las obligaciones en s\xed deben entenderse antes de que comience la recopilaci\xf3n. El entrenamiento del modelo y la ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas son etapas t\xe9cnicas posteriores centradas en construir y optimizar el modelo, no en identificar el marco legal y regulatorio que rige el proyecto."},{"id":"aif-q148","question":"Un servicio de streaming estudia los h\xe1bitos y datos demogr\xe1ficos de los suscriptores para sugerir programas personalizados. Despu\xe9s de poner su modelo personalizado en producci\xf3n, el equipo necesita vigilar si la calidad del modelo disminuye con el tiempo. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","D":"Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor monitorea continuamente un modelo implementado en producci\xf3n y detecta deriva y degradaci\xf3n de la calidad con el tiempo, que es lo que el equipo necesita vigilar a medida que cambia el comportamiento de los suscriptores.\\nAmazon SageMaker Clarify se centra en sesgo y explicabilidad, mientras que Amazon Comprehend y Amazon Rekognition son servicios administrados para an\xe1lisis de texto e im\xe1genes, ninguno de los cuales monitorea un modelo implementado para detectar deriva de calidad."},{"id":"aif-q166","question":"Una organizaci\xf3n ejecuta muchos modelos de ML en producci\xf3n y necesita saber cu\xe1ndo alguno de ellos comienza a comportarse de manera diferente a su l\xednea base para que el equipo pueda intervenir. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","B":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","C":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","D":"Amazon SageMaker HyperPod"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor mide continuamente la calidad de los datos y de las predicciones de los modelos implementados y alerta cuando se desv\xedan de una l\xednea base, lo que permite a la organizaci\xf3n detectar y resolver cambios de calidad r\xe1pidamente.\\nAmazon SageMaker Data Wrangler prepara datos, Amazon SageMaker JumpStart proporciona modelos preconstruidos y Amazon SageMaker HyperPod proporciona infraestructura de entrenamiento distribuido; ninguno de estos monitorea modelos implementados para detectar cambios de calidad."},{"id":"aif-q230","question":"Despu\xe9s de producir varios modelos de ML, un equipo necesita un \xfanico lugar para almacenarlos, gestionarlos y rastrear cada versi\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","B":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Registry","D":"AWS Audit Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Registry es un repositorio central para almacenar, gestionar y versionar modelos de ML, y para rastrear su estado de despliegue a lo largo del ciclo de vida, lo que satisface las necesidades del equipo.\\nAmazon SageMaker Model Monitor detecta drift en modelos desplegados, Amazon SageMaker Canvas crea modelos con una interfaz sin c\xf3digo, y AWS Audit Manager recopila evidencia de auditor\xeda; ninguno de estos es el servicio para el versionado y la gesti\xf3n de modelos."},{"id":"aif-q261","question":"Mientras monitorean un modelo predictivo con Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor, un equipo observa que el data drift supera el umbral que hab\xedan establecido. El equipo quiere limitar cualquier da\xf1o que esto pueda causar al modelo. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Ajustar la sensibilidad del monitoreo","B":"Reiniciar el endpoint de SageMaker AI","C":"Reentrenar el modelo con datos nuevos","D":"Configurar el seguimiento de experimentos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cuando el data drift supera el umbral definido, los datos ya no coinciden con aquellos con los que se entren\xf3 el modelo, por lo que reentrenar el modelo con datos nuevos restaura su precisi\xf3n y limita el impacto negativo.\\nAjustar la sensibilidad del monitoreo solo cambia cu\xe1ndo se activan las alertas, reiniciar el endpoint no aborda los datos modificados, y configurar el seguimiento de experimentos registra ejecuciones sin corregir el drift."},{"id":"aif-q332","question":"\xbfPor qu\xe9 los equipos monitorean los modelos una vez que est\xe1n en producci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Para recopilar m\xe1s datos","B":"Para detectar problemas como model drift o data drift","C":"Para realizar ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas","D":"Para entrenar nuevos modelos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El prop\xf3sito principal del monitoreo de modelos en producci\xf3n es detectar problemas como model drift o data drift para que los equipos puedan actuar antes de que el rendimiento se degrade.\\nRecopilar m\xe1s datos, realizar ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas y entrenar nuevos modelos son actividades separadas del ciclo de vida, no el prop\xf3sito de monitorear un modelo desplegado."},{"id":"aif-q346","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 tarea est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para realizar Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor?","options":{"A":"Servir modelos para inferencia","B":"Detectar data drift y degradaci\xf3n del rendimiento del modelo","C":"Crear conjuntos de datos de entrenamiento","D":"Entrenar modelos m\xe1s r\xe1pido"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor vigila continuamente los modelos desplegados y detecta data drift y degradaci\xf3n del rendimiento del modelo, alertando a los equipos cuando se necesita investigaci\xf3n o reentrenamiento.\\nServir modelos para inferencia, crear conjuntos de datos de entrenamiento y entrenar modelos m\xe1s r\xe1pido son manejados por otras capacidades y no son el prop\xf3sito de Model Monitor."},{"id":"aif-q125","question":"Una tienda en l\xednea quiere adaptar sus recomendaciones a cada comprador individual. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS encaja?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Personalize","C":"Amazon Kendra","D":"Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Personalize construye modelos de machine learning a partir del comportamiento del usuario para entregar recomendaciones individualizadas, que es exactamente lo que requiere adaptar los resultados por comprador.\\nAmazon Comprehend analiza texto, Amazon Kendra proporciona b\xfasqueda empresarial, y Amazon Rekognition analiza im\xe1genes y video; ninguno personaliza recomendaciones por usuario."},{"id":"aif-q150","question":"Una empresa necesita producir autom\xe1ticamente las descripciones de sus productos en varios idiomas. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS hace esto?","options":{"A":"Amazon Polly","B":"Amazon Translate","C":"Amazon Comprehend","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Translate utiliza traducci\xf3n autom\xe1tica neuronal para convertir texto entre idiomas autom\xe1ticamente, que es lo que requiere generar descripciones de productos en m\xfaltiples idiomas.\\nAmazon Polly convierte texto a voz, Amazon Comprehend extrae insights del texto, y Amazon Transcribe convierte voz a texto; ninguno traduce texto."},{"id":"aif-q153","question":"Un hospital quiere una aplicaci\xf3n de IA que lea registros de pacientes estructurados, extraiga los detalles cl\xednicos relevantes y produzca res\xfamenes concisos. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n encaja?","options":{"A":"Usar Amazon Personalize para modelar el engagement del paciente y pasar la salida a un modelo general","B":"Usar Amazon Comprehend Medical para extraer entidades y relaciones m\xe9dicas, y luego aplicar l\xf3gica basada en reglas para formatear los res\xfamenes","C":"Usar Amazon Textract para digitalizar documentos escaneados, y luego ejecutar extracci\xf3n de palabras clave","D":"Usar Amazon Kendra para indexar los registros para b\xfasqueda, y luego aplicar una plantilla"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Comprehend Medical est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para extraer entidades y relaciones m\xe9dicas a partir de texto cl\xednico, y aplicar l\xf3gica basada en reglas despu\xe9s formatea la informaci\xf3n en res\xfamenes concisos, cumpliendo ambas necesidades.\\nAmazon Personalize modela el engagement para recomendaciones, Amazon Textract digitaliza documentos sin interpretar el significado cl\xednico, y Amazon Kendra indexa contenido para b\xfasqueda en lugar de extraer entidades cl\xednicas estructuradas."},{"id":"aif-q221","question":"Una empresa ha creado modelos personalizados de Computer Vision y necesita una interfaz f\xe1cil de usar para etiquetar datos y reducir errores en nuevas im\xe1genes del mundo real. \xbfQu\xe9 herramienta de AWS encaja?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas","B":"Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","C":"Amazon Rekognition Custom Labels","D":"Amazon SageMaker Autopilot"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth proporciona una interfaz de etiquetado integrada con flujos de trabajo y controles de calidad para tareas como Computer Vision, produciendo datos etiquetados de alta calidad que reducen los errores del modelo.\\nSageMaker Canvas es una interfaz sin c\xf3digo para crear modelos, Rekognition Custom Labels entrena un modelo en lugar de ofrecer una interfaz de etiquetado general, y SageMaker Autopilot crea modelos autom\xe1ticamente a partir de datos tabulares."},{"id":"aif-q223","question":"Un banco implement\xf3 un modelo de ML para predecir la rotaci\xf3n de clientes y quiere medir con qu\xe9 precisi\xf3n predice la rotaci\xf3n en comparaci\xf3n con el comportamiento real. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica es adecuada?","options":{"A":"Error cuadr\xe1tico medio (RMSE)","B":"Puntuaci\xf3n F1","C":"Retorno de la inversi\xf3n (ROI)","D":"Perplejidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La puntuaci\xf3n F1 equilibra la precisi\xf3n y la exhaustividad (recall), lo que la hace adecuada para medir la precisi\xf3n en una tarea de clasificaci\xf3n binaria, como predecir si un cliente abandonar\xe1.\\nRMSE mide el error de las predicciones de regresi\xf3n, ROI es una m\xe9trica de valor empresarial y la perplejidad mide el ajuste de modelado de lenguaje; ninguna se ajusta a la clasificaci\xf3n binaria de abandono."},{"id":"aif-q245","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 dos capacidades hacen que Amazon Kendra sea valioso para aplicaciones de b\xfasqueda empresarial?","options":{"A":"Alojamiento gestionado de bases de datos relacionales","B":"Comprensi\xf3n del lenguaje natural","C":"Transcodificaci\xf3n de video","D":"B\xfasqueda sem\xe1ntica","E":"Monitoreo de infraestructura"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Amazon Kendra proporciona comprensi\xf3n del lenguaje natural que interpreta la intenci\xf3n del usuario y b\xfasqueda sem\xe1ntica que devuelve resultados por significado y contexto, lo que en conjunto lo hace eficaz para la b\xfasqueda empresarial.\\nEl alojamiento gestionado de bases de datos relacionales, la transcodificaci\xf3n de video y el monitoreo de infraestructura son tareas no relacionadas que Kendra no realiza."},{"id":"aif-q263","question":"La calidad de inferencia de un modelo disminuy\xf3 despu\xe9s de cuatro meses en producci\xf3n. El equipo quiere alertas si la calidad vuelve a degradarse y prevenir que se repita. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n es adecuada?","options":{"A":"Crear un nuevo modelo y monitorear la deriva con Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","B":"Reentrenar el modelo y monitorear la deriva con Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","C":"Reentrenar el modelo y monitorear la deriva con Amazon SageMaker Clarify","D":"Crear un nuevo modelo y observarlo solo con alarmas de Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor detecta continuamente la deriva de datos y del modelo en producci\xf3n y puede enviar alertas, de modo que el equipo recibe notificaci\xf3n y puede reentrenar antes de que la calidad vuelva a degradarse.\\nSageMaker Clarify se centra en el sesgo y la explicabilidad, SageMaker Feature Store almacena caracter\xedsticas y las alarmas de CloudWatch rastrean m\xe9tricas de infraestructura, pero no detectan por s\xed solas la deriva del modelo."},{"id":"aif-q274","question":"Una empresa de aprendizaje en l\xednea tiene grandes vol\xfamenes de material educativo y quiere b\xfasqueda empresarial en todo ese contenido. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS es adecuado?","options":{"A":"Amazon Polly","B":"Amazon Kendra","C":"Amazon Textract","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Kendra es un servicio inteligente de b\xfasqueda empresarial que busca en grandes vol\xfamenes de contenido no estructurado, como materiales educativos, lo que se ajusta a esta necesidad.\\nAmazon Polly convierte texto a voz, Amazon Textract extrae texto de documentos y Amazon Comprehend analiza texto para obtener informaci\xf3n; ninguno proporciona b\xfasqueda empresarial."},{"id":"aif-q011","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando un modelo de ML con Amazon SageMaker y necesita compartir y gestionar variables utilizadas para el desarrollo del modelo entre varios equipos. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de SageMaker cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","B":"Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","C":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","D":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Feature Store es un repositorio centralizado para almacenar, compartir y reutilizar caracter\xedsticas, lo que permite a varios equipos gestionar las mismas variables para el desarrollo del modelo.\\nAmazon SageMaker Data Wrangler prepara y transforma datos, Amazon SageMaker Clarify detecta sesgo y admite explicabilidad, y Amazon SageMaker Model Cards documenta los detalles del modelo. Ninguna de estas caracter\xedsticas est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para compartir y gestionar caracter\xedsticas entre equipos."},{"id":"aif-q036","question":"Una empresa implement\xf3 un modelo de aprendizaje profundo para la detecci\xf3n de objetos en producci\xf3n. Cuando el modelo examina una nueva imagen para identificar los objetos que contiene, \xbfqu\xe9 proceso de IA est\xe1 ocurriendo?","options":{"A":"Entrenamiento","B":"Inferencia","C":"Correcci\xf3n de sesgo","D":"Implementaci\xf3n del modelo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La inferencia es el proceso en el que un modelo implementado analiza datos nuevos, como una nueva imagen, para producir una predicci\xf3n, por lo que identificar objetos en una imagen entrante es inferencia.\\nEl entrenamiento es el proceso anterior de ajustar el modelo a los datos, la implementaci\xf3n del modelo es hacer que el modelo entrenado est\xe9 disponible para atender solicitudes y la correcci\xf3n de sesgo ajusta los resultados injustos. Ninguno de estos describe analizar una nueva imagen para hacer una predicci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q147","question":"Una empresa quiere encontrar lenguaje da\xf1ino en los comentarios de las publicaciones en redes sociales y no planea usar datos etiquetados para entrenar un modelo. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque deber\xeda usar la empresa?","options":{"A":"Usar la moderaci\xf3n de Amazon Rekognition","B":"Usar la detecci\xf3n de toxicidad de Amazon Comprehend","C":"Usar algoritmos integrados de Amazon SageMaker para entrenar un modelo","D":"Usar Amazon Polly para monitorear los comentarios"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Comprehend proporciona procesamiento de lenguaje natural preentrenado, incluida la detecci\xf3n de toxicidad, por lo que puede identificar lenguaje da\xf1ino en los comentarios sin que la empresa tenga que etiquetar datos ni entrenar un modelo.\\nAmazon Rekognition modera im\xe1genes y video, no texto. Los algoritmos integrados de Amazon SageMaker requerir\xedan datos etiquetados para entrenar un modelo. Amazon Polly convierte texto a voz y no analiza texto para detectar contenido da\xf1ino."},{"id":"aif-q253","question":"Un profesional con experiencia limitada en ML quiere predecir la rotaci\xf3n de empleados sin escribir ning\xfan c\xf3digo. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de Amazon SageMaker cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"SageMaker Canvas","B":"SageMaker Clarify","C":"SageMaker Model Monitor","D":"SageMaker Data Wrangler"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas ofrece una interfaz visual sin c\xf3digo que permite a un usuario con experiencia limitada en ML crear, entrenar y generar predicciones, como la rotaci\xf3n de empleados, sin escribir c\xf3digo.\\nSageMaker Clarify detecta sesgo y explica las predicciones, SageMaker Model Monitor rastrea los modelos implementados para detectar deriva y SageMaker Data Wrangler prepara datos. Ninguna de estas proporciona una forma sin c\xf3digo de crear un modelo predictivo."},{"id":"aif-q347","question":"Un banco debe proporcionar a los reguladores una explicaci\xf3n clara de cada factor que influy\xf3 en cada decisi\xf3n de pr\xe9stamo. El equipo est\xe1 eligiendo entre un modelo de gradient boosting y un modelo de lenguaje grande. \xbfQu\xe9 factor favorece m\xe1s fuertemente el uso del modelo ML tradicional?","options":{"A":"Requisitos de explicabilidad","B":"Soporte multiling\xfce","C":"Capacidad de generaci\xf3n de contenido","D":"Necesidades de interfaz conversacional"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Los modelos ML tradicionales, como gradient boosting, producen resultados que se pueden rastrear hasta caracter\xedsticas de entrada espec\xedficas y sus pesos, lo que los hace adecuados para entornos regulados donde cada decisi\xf3n debe ser completamente explicable para auditores y reguladores.\\nEl soporte multiling\xfce y las necesidades de interfaz conversacional son fortalezas de los modelos fundacionales, no razones para elegir ML tradicional. La capacidad de generaci\xf3n de contenido es una caracter\xedstica definitoria de la IA generativa y es irrelevante para un modelo de decisi\xf3n de pr\xe9stamos que clasifica solicitudes en lugar de generar texto."},{"id":"aif-q348","question":"Una startup de atenci\xf3n m\xe9dica desea implementar una herramienta de apoyo diagn\xf3stico que debe ejecutarse en dispositivos con memoria limitada y sin conexi\xf3n a internet. \xbfQu\xe9 restricci\xf3n favorece m\xe1s fuertemente un modelo ML tradicional sobre un modelo fundacional?","options":{"A":"La necesidad de generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes en tiempo real","B":"Restricciones operativas de memoria y conectividad","C":"El requisito de soporte de chat multiling\xfce","D":"El deseo de resumen de texto abierto"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los modelos ML tradicionales suelen ser mucho m\xe1s peque\xf1os y pueden ejecutarse de manera eficiente en dispositivos de borde con recursos limitados sin requerir conectividad en la nube, lo que los convierte en la opci\xf3n adecuada cuando se aplican restricciones operativas como memoria limitada y funcionamiento sin conexi\xf3n.\\nLa generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes en tiempo real, el soporte de chat multiling\xfce y el resumen de texto abierto son capacidades que favorecen a los modelos fundacionales, no restricciones que llevar\xedan a un equipo a elegir ML tradicional."},{"id":"aif-q349","question":"Una empresa de log\xedstica procesa miles de solicitudes de rutas de entrega cada d\xeda. \xbfQu\xe9 beneficio proporciona AI/ML que un sistema puramente basado en reglas no puede lograr f\xe1cilmente?","options":{"A":"Resultados deterministas para cada entrada","B":"Capacidad de escalar y adaptarse a patrones cambiantes en los datos","C":"Correcci\xf3n garantizada sin validaci\xf3n","D":"Eliminaci\xf3n de todos los costos computacionales"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los sistemas AI/ML aprenden patrones a partir de datos y pueden adaptarse a medida que esos patrones cambian con el tiempo, y escalan para manejar grandes vol\xfamenes de solicitudes al generalizar a partir de ejemplos en lugar de requerir reglas codificadas manualmente para cada escenario, lo cual es una ventaja clave sobre los sistemas basados en reglas.\\nLos resultados deterministas son en realidad una caracter\xedstica de los sistemas basados en reglas, no un beneficio \xfanico de AI/ML. Ning\xfan sistema puede garantizar correcci\xf3n sin validaci\xf3n. AI/ML no elimina los costos computacionales; de hecho, el entrenamiento y la inferencia a menudo requieren recursos de c\xf3mputo significativos."},{"id":"aif-q350","question":"Un minorista desea personalizar recomendaciones de productos para millones de clientes. \xbfPor qu\xe9 AI/ML es m\xe1s adecuado que un enfoque manual para esta tarea?","options":{"A":"AI/ML elimina la necesidad de cualquier dato","B":"AI/ML puede procesar grandes vol\xfamenes de datos e identificar patrones que los humanos no pueden detectar f\xe1cilmente","C":"AI/ML garantiza 100 % de precisi\xf3n en cada predicci\xf3n","D":"AI/ML elimina la necesidad de infraestructura de c\xf3mputo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AI/ML destaca en procesar grandes vol\xfamenes de datos de clientes, identificar patrones y preferencias sutiles, y generar recomendaciones personalizadas a una escala que ser\xeda poco pr\xe1ctica de lograr manualmente para los humanos, por lo que aporta valor para este caso de uso.\\nAI/ML requiere datos para aprender, por lo que no elimina la necesidad de ning\xfan dato. Ning\xfan sistema garantiza 100 % de precisi\xf3n. AI/ML requiere infraestructura de c\xf3mputo para entrenamiento e inferencia, por lo que no elimina las necesidades de infraestructura."},{"id":"aif-q351","question":"Una startup desea usar un modelo fundacional para su aplicaci\xf3n, pero carece de los datos y recursos para entrenar uno desde cero. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque permite al equipo obtener un modelo fundacional sin preentrenarlo por su cuenta?","options":{"A":"Usar un modelo preentrenado de c\xf3digo abierto de un repositorio de modelos","B":"Construir en su lugar un sistema experto basado en reglas","C":"Recopilar m\xe1s datos etiquetados y entrenar un \xe1rbol de decisi\xf3n","D":"Escribir c\xf3digo de inferencia personalizado sin ning\xfan modelo"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Los modelos preentrenados de c\xf3digo abierto est\xe1n disponibles en repositorios de modelos y se pueden usar directamente o ajustar para tareas espec\xedficas, lo que permite a los equipos aprovechar modelos fundacionales sin los enormes datos y recursos de c\xf3mputo necesarios para preentrenarlos desde cero.\\nConstruir un sistema experto basado en reglas no proporciona un modelo fundacional. Entrenar un \xe1rbol de decisi\xf3n crea un modelo ML tradicional, no un modelo fundacional. Escribir c\xf3digo de inferencia personalizado sin ning\xfan modelo no proporciona ninguna capacidad predictiva."},{"id":"aif-q352","question":"Una empresa desea experimentar con m\xfaltiples modelos fundacionales antes de seleccionar uno para producci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona acceso a una variedad de modelos fundacionales preentrenados de diferentes proveedores?","options":{"A":"Amazon Personalize","B":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","C":"Amazon Comprehend","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart proporciona acceso a una variedad de modelos fundacionales preentrenados de AWS y proveedores externos, lo que permite a los equipos experimentar con diferentes modelos e implementarlos con una configuraci\xf3n m\xednima, lo que aborda directamente la necesidad de la empresa.\\nAmazon Personalize crea modelos de recomendaci\xf3n a partir de datos de interacci\xf3n de usuarios en lugar de proporcionar acceso a modelos fundacionales de prop\xf3sito general. Amazon Comprehend es un servicio NLP administrado para an\xe1lisis de texto. Amazon Transcribe convierte voz a texto. Ninguno de estos proporciona un cat\xe1logo de modelos fundacionales de m\xfaltiples proveedores."},{"id":"aif-q354","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 modernizando aplicaciones heredadas y desea un servicio de AWS que ayude a transformar y migrar cargas de trabajo mediante herramientas asistidas por AI. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"AWS Transform","B":"Amazon Textract","C":"Amazon Translate","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Transform es un servicio de AWS que utiliza herramientas asistidas por AI para ayudar a las empresas a transformar y modernizar sus aplicaciones heredadas y cargas de trabajo, lo que cumple directamente el requisito de la empresa.\\nAmazon Textract extrae texto y datos de documentos. Amazon Translate convierte texto entre idiomas. Amazon Transcribe convierte voz a texto. Ninguno de estos est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la modernizaci\xf3n y transformaci\xf3n de aplicaciones."},{"id":"aif-q355","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 descripci\xf3n define mejor la IA ag\xe9ntica?","options":{"A":"IA que genera im\xe1genes a partir de indicaciones de texto","B":"IA que planifica, decide y toma acciones de forma aut\xf3noma para lograr objetivos","C":"IA que convierte voz a texto","D":"IA que agrupa puntos de datos similares en cl\xfasteres"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La IA ag\xe9ntica se refiere a sistemas de IA que pueden planificar, tomar decisiones y ejecutar acciones de forma aut\xf3noma para cumplir objetivos, a menudo mediante el uso de herramientas, la gesti\xf3n de memoria y la orquestaci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo de varios pasos sin direcci\xf3n humana constante.\\nGenerar im\xe1genes a partir de indicaciones de texto describe modelos de difusi\xf3n o IA de generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes. Convertir voz a texto describe reconocimiento de voz. Agrupar puntos de datos similares en cl\xfasteres describe t\xe9cnicas de aprendizaje no supervisado como K-means."},{"id":"aif-q356","question":"\xbfEn qu\xe9 se diferencia la AI ag\xe9ntica de los sistemas de AI tradicionales?","options":{"A":"La AI ag\xe9ntica requiere m\xe1s datos de entrenamiento que la AI tradicional","B":"La AI ag\xe9ntica solo puede procesar texto, mientras que la AI tradicional maneja todos los tipos de datos","C":"La AI ag\xe9ntica puede ejecutar tareas de varios pasos de forma aut\xf3noma, mientras que la AI tradicional suele responder a solicitudes individuales","D":"La AI ag\xe9ntica siempre es m\xe1s precisa que la AI tradicional"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los sistemas de AI ag\xe9ntica pueden descomponer objetivos en pasos de forma aut\xf3noma, usar herramientas y ejecutar flujos de trabajo de varios pasos para lograr objetivos, mientras que los sistemas de AI tradicionales suelen procesar solicitudes individuales y devolver respuestas \xfanicas sin planificaci\xf3n ni ejecuci\xf3n de acciones aut\xf3nomas.\\nLa AI ag\xe9ntica no requiere inherentemente m\xe1s datos de entrenamiento que la AI tradicional. La AI ag\xe9ntica puede trabajar con m\xfaltiples tipos de datos, no solo texto. La precisi\xf3n depende del modelo y la tarea espec\xedficos, no de si el sistema es ag\xe9ntico."},{"id":"aif-q357","question":"Un equipo de desarrollo quiere un asistente de AI que pueda responder preguntas sobre su base de c\xf3digo, explicar c\xf3digo existente y ayudar a solucionar problemas directamente en su IDE. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Q Developer","C":"Amazon Textract","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Q Developer es un asistente impulsado por AI que se integra con IDE para responder preguntas sobre c\xf3digo, explicar c\xf3mo funciona el c\xf3digo existente y ayudar a los desarrolladores a solucionar problemas, lo que coincide con lo que necesita el equipo.\\nAmazon Comprehend analiza texto para determinar sentimiento y entidades, pero no ayuda con c\xf3digo. Amazon Textract extrae texto de documentos e im\xe1genes. Amazon Transcribe convierte voz a texto. Ninguno de estos proporciona asistencia consciente del c\xf3digo en un IDE."},{"id":"aif-q358","question":"Un equipo de software quiere usar AI para ayudar a definir requisitos, generar documentos de dise\xf1o e implementar funcionalidades basadas en especificaciones. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS admite este flujo de trabajo de desarrollo basado en especificaciones?","options":{"A":"Amazon Personalize","B":"Amazon Rekognition","C":"Kiro","D":"Amazon Polly"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Kiro es un entorno de desarrollo impulsado por AI que admite desarrollo basado en especificaciones, ayudando a los equipos a definir requisitos, generar documentos de dise\xf1o e implementar funcionalidades basadas en especificaciones.\\nAmazon Personalize genera recomendaciones basadas en el comportamiento del usuario. Amazon Rekognition analiza im\xe1genes y video. Amazon Polly convierte texto a voz. Ninguno de estos admite flujos de trabajo de desarrollo de software basados en especificaciones."},{"id":"aif-q359","question":"Un analista de negocio quiere crear paneles interactivos con informaci\xf3n generada por AI y consultas de datos en lenguaje natural. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Athena","B":"Amazon Quick","C":"Amazon Redshift","D":"AWS Glue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Quick (anteriormente Amazon QuickSight Q) proporciona inteligencia de negocio impulsada por AI con consultas en lenguaje natural, lo que permite a los analistas de negocio hacer preguntas en lenguaje sencillo y recibir visualizaciones e informaci\xf3n de sus datos.\\nAmazon Athena ejecuta consultas SQL sobre datos en Amazon S3, pero no proporciona informaci\xf3n generada por AI ni consultas en lenguaje natural. Amazon Redshift es un almac\xe9n de datos para cargas de trabajo anal\xedticas. AWS Glue es un servicio de integraci\xf3n de datos para trabajos de ETL. Ninguno de estos ofrece la experiencia de inteligencia de negocio en lenguaje natural impulsada por AI."},{"id":"aif-q360","question":"Un centro de contacto quiere crear un agente virtual habilitado por voz que comprenda las solicitudes de los clientes y enrute las llamadas al departamento adecuado. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Polly","B":"Amazon Lex","C":"Amazon Translate","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Lex es un servicio para crear interfaces conversacionales mediante voz y texto, lo que permite al centro de contacto crear un agente virtual que comprenda solicitudes habladas y pueda enrutar llamadas seg\xfan la intenci\xf3n del cliente.\\nAmazon Polly convierte texto a voz, pero no comprende ni interpreta la entrada hablada. Amazon Translate convierte texto entre idiomas. Amazon Comprehend extrae informaci\xf3n de texto, pero no crea agentes conversacionales interactivos."},{"id":"aif-q361","question":"Un bufete de abogados tiene miles de documentos legales y quiere que los empleados encuentren jurisprudencia y cl\xe1usulas contractuales relevantes usando preguntas en lenguaje natural. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de aplicaci\xf3n de AI aborda mejor esta necesidad?","options":{"A":"Detecci\xf3n de fraude","B":"Base de conocimiento","C":"Pron\xf3stico de series temporales","D":"Detecci\xf3n de anomal\xedas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Una aplicaci\xf3n de base de conocimiento usa AI para indexar, comprender y recuperar informaci\xf3n de grandes colecciones de documentos, lo que permite a los usuarios encontrar contenido relevante haciendo preguntas en lenguaje natural, que es exactamente lo que el bufete de abogados necesita para buscar documentos legales.\\nLa detecci\xf3n de fraude identifica transacciones o actividades sospechosas. El pron\xf3stico de series temporales predice valores futuros a partir de datos secuenciales hist\xf3ricos. La detecci\xf3n de anomal\xedas se\xf1ala patrones inusuales en los datos. Ninguno de estos permite la b\xfasqueda en lenguaje natural en colecciones de documentos."},{"id":"aif-q362","question":"\xbfA qu\xe9 se refiere el t\xe9rmino modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM)?","options":{"A":"Una base de datos que almacena grandes cantidades de texto","B":"Un modelo de aprendizaje profundo entrenado con vastos datos de texto para comprender y generar lenguaje humano","C":"Una herramienta para traducir entre lenguajes de programaci\xf3n","D":"Un sistema que convierte voz a texto"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) es un modelo de aprendizaje profundo entrenado con cantidades masivas de datos de texto que puede comprender el contexto, generar texto coherente, responder preguntas y realizar diversas tareas de lenguaje.\\nUna base de datos almacena datos, pero no comprende ni genera lenguaje. Un traductor de lenguajes de programaci\xf3n convierte la sintaxis del c\xf3digo, no la comprensi\xf3n del lenguaje natural. Un sistema de voz a texto transcribe audio, pero no es lo que describe el t\xe9rmino LLM."},{"id":"aif-q363","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n describe mejor la AI generativa?","options":{"A":"AI que solo clasifica datos en categor\xedas predefinidas","B":"AI que crea contenido nuevo, como texto, im\xe1genes o audio, a partir de patrones aprendidos","C":"AI que detecta exclusivamente anomal\xedas en conjuntos de datos","D":"AI que solo recupera informaci\xf3n existente de bases de datos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La AI generativa se refiere a sistemas de AI que pueden crear contenido nuevo, incluidos texto, im\xe1genes, audio, video y c\xf3digo, al aprender patrones de los datos de entrenamiento y generar resultados novedosos que se asemejan a esos datos.\\nClasificar datos en categor\xedas predefinidas describe modelos de clasificaci\xf3n, no modelos generativos. Detectar anomal\xedas describe sistemas de detecci\xf3n de anomal\xedas. Recuperar informaci\xf3n existente describe sistemas de b\xfasqueda o recuperaci\xf3n. Ninguno de estos implica crear contenido nuevo."},{"id":"aif-q364","question":"Una empresa desea un sistema de IA que pueda investigar competidores de forma aut\xf3noma, recopilar hallazgos en un informe y enviar el resumen por correo electr\xf3nico a las partes interesadas sin intervenci\xf3n humana en cada paso. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de aplicaci\xf3n de IA se ajusta mejor a este requisito?","options":{"A":"Reconocimiento de voz","B":"Clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes","C":"IA ag\xe9ntica","D":"An\xe1lisis de sentimientos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La IA ag\xe9ntica puede planificar y ejecutar de forma aut\xf3noma flujos de trabajo de varios pasos, como investigar informaci\xf3n, recopilar informes y enviar correos electr\xf3nicos, sin requerir intervenci\xf3n humana en cada paso, lo que coincide con el requisito de la empresa.\\nEl reconocimiento de voz convierte audio hablado en texto. La clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes asigna etiquetas a im\xe1genes. El an\xe1lisis de sentimientos determina el tono emocional del texto. Ninguno de estos puede orquestar de forma aut\xf3noma tareas de varios pasos en diferentes sistemas."},{"id":"aif-q365","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n describe mejor en qu\xe9 se diferencia el aprendizaje profundo del aprendizaje autom\xe1tico tradicional?","options":{"A":"El aprendizaje profundo requiere caracter\xedsticas dise\xf1adas manualmente, mientras que el aprendizaje autom\xe1tico tradicional no","B":"El aprendizaje profundo utiliza redes neuronales de m\xfaltiples capas que aprenden autom\xe1ticamente representaciones de caracter\xedsticas a partir de datos sin procesar","C":"El aprendizaje profundo solo se puede aplicar a datos de texto","D":"El aprendizaje profundo siempre requiere menos datos que el aprendizaje autom\xe1tico tradicional"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El aprendizaje profundo utiliza redes neuronales con muchas capas que descubren autom\xe1ticamente representaciones de caracter\xedsticas \xfatiles directamente a partir de datos sin procesar, eliminando la necesidad de la ingenier\xeda manual de caracter\xedsticas que el aprendizaje autom\xe1tico tradicional suele requerir.\\nEl aprendizaje autom\xe1tico tradicional es el enfoque que normalmente depende de caracter\xedsticas dise\xf1adas manualmente, por lo que la opci\xf3n A invierte la relaci\xf3n. El aprendizaje profundo se aplica a im\xe1genes, audio, texto y otros tipos de datos, no solo a texto. El aprendizaje profundo generalmente requiere m\xe1s datos que el aprendizaje autom\xe1tico tradicional porque sus redes de m\xfaltiples capas tienen muchos m\xe1s par\xe1metros que entrenar."},{"id":"aif-q366","question":"Una cadena minorista desea detectar y desenfocar autom\xe1ticamente los rostros de los clientes en las grabaciones de sus c\xe1maras de seguridad antes de almacenar las grabaciones. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Textract","B":"Amazon Rekognition","C":"Amazon Comprehend","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Rekognition es un servicio de visi\xf3n por computadora que puede detectar, analizar e identificar rostros en im\xe1genes y video, por lo que puede localizar los rostros de los clientes en las grabaciones de seguridad para que la empresa los desenfoque antes del almacenamiento.\\nAmazon Textract extrae texto y datos estructurados de documentos e im\xe1genes, pero no detecta rostros. Amazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que analiza texto para determinar sentimiento, entidades y frases clave. Amazon Transcribe convierte audio hablado en texto. Ninguno de estos proporciona detecci\xf3n facial en video."},{"id":"aif-q367","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un ejemplo de datos no estructurados?","options":{"A":"Una tabla de base de datos de pedidos de clientes con columnas para fecha, cantidad y precio","B":"Una hoja de c\xe1lculo que enumera identificadores de empleados, nombres y salarios","C":"Una colecci\xf3n de correos electr\xf3nicos de soporte al cliente de formato libre","D":"Un archivo CSV que contiene lecturas de sensores indexadas por marca de tiempo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los correos electr\xf3nicos de soporte al cliente de formato libre son datos no estructurados porque no tienen un esquema predefinido ni un formato fijo: el contenido var\xeda en longitud, estilo y dise\xf1o, lo que los hace inadecuados para el almacenamiento directo en filas y columnas sin procesamiento.\\nUna tabla de base de datos con columnas definidas, una hoja de c\xe1lculo con campos nombrados y un archivo CSV con columnas coherentes son ejemplos de datos estructurados porque cada registro sigue un esquema predefinido donde cada campo tiene un tipo y una posici\xf3n conocidos."},{"id":"aif-q368","question":"En el contexto de MLOps, \xbfa qu\xe9 se refiere la gesti\xf3n de la deuda t\xe9cnica?","options":{"A":"Reducir el costo financiero de las instancias de c\xf3mputo en la nube","B":"Abordar atajos acumulados, dependencias desactualizadas y procesos no documentados que hacen que los sistemas de ML sean m\xe1s dif\xedciles de mantener con el tiempo","C":"Aumentar el n\xfamero de par\xe1metros del modelo para mejorar la precisi\xf3n","D":"Eliminar conjuntos de datos antiguos que ya no son necesarios"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La deuda t\xe9cnica en MLOps se refiere al costo acumulado de atajos, canalizaciones mal documentadas, dependencias desactualizadas y componentes estrechamente acoplados que hacen que los sistemas de ML sean progresivamente m\xe1s dif\xedciles de mantener, ampliar y depurar con el tiempo. Gestionarla significa refactorizar, documentar y modernizar estos sistemas de forma proactiva.\\nReducir los costos de c\xf3mputo en la nube es optimizaci\xf3n de costos, no gesti\xf3n de deuda t\xe9cnica. Aumentar los par\xe1metros del modelo es una decisi\xf3n de modelado relacionada con la capacidad, no con la carga de mantenimiento. Eliminar conjuntos de datos antiguos es una actividad de gobernanza de datos en lugar de una respuesta a atajos de ingenier\xeda acumulados."},{"id":"aif-q369","question":"Un equipo de ciencia de datos ejecuta decenas de trabajos de entrenamiento con diferentes configuraciones cada semana. Necesita comparar resultados y reproducir cualquier ejecuci\xf3n anterior. \xbfQu\xe9 pr\xe1ctica de MLOps cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Despliegue de modelos","B":"Seguimiento de experimentos","C":"Etiquetado de datos","D":"Servicio de caracter\xedsticas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El seguimiento de experimentos registra los par\xe1metros, las m\xe9tricas, las versiones de c\xf3digo y los artefactos de cada ejecuci\xf3n de entrenamiento, lo que permite al equipo comparar resultados entre ejecuciones y reproducir exactamente cualquier experimento anterior, lo que respalda los principios de MLOps de experimentaci\xf3n y procesos repetibles.\\nEl despliegue de modelos es el proceso de poner un modelo entrenado a disposici\xf3n para inferencia y no registra ni compara configuraciones de entrenamiento. El etiquetado de datos asigna anotaciones de referencia a ejemplos de entrenamiento, no al seguimiento de los resultados de los trabajos de entrenamiento. El servicio de caracter\xedsticas entrega caracter\xedsticas precalculadas a los modelos en el momento de la inferencia y no tiene nada que ver con registrar detalles de experimentos de entrenamiento."},{"id":"aif-q370","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el prop\xf3sito del ajuste de hiperpar\xe1metros en el ciclo de vida de desarrollo de ML?","options":{"A":"Recopilar y limpiar los datos de entrenamiento sin procesar","B":"Encontrar los valores de configuraci\xf3n del modelo que producen el mejor rendimiento","C":"Desplegar el modelo entrenado en un endpoint de producci\xf3n","D":"Monitorear el modelo en busca de deriva de datos despu\xe9s del despliegue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El ajuste de hiperpar\xe1metros busca los valores de configuraci\xf3n \xf3ptimos, como la tasa de aprendizaje, el tama\xf1o de lote y el n\xfamero de capas, que se establecen antes de que comience el entrenamiento y que influyen directamente en el rendimiento del modelo. Encontrar la mejor combinaci\xf3n maximiza la precisi\xf3n del modelo u otra m\xe9trica objetivo.\\nRecopilar y limpiar datos sin procesar es la etapa de recopilaci\xf3n y preprocesamiento de datos. Desplegar el modelo en un endpoint de producci\xf3n es la etapa de despliegue. Monitorear la deriva de datos forma parte de la etapa de monitoreo posterior al despliegue. Cada una de estas es un paso separado en el ciclo de vida de ML."},{"id":"aif-q371","question":"En el aprendizaje autom\xe1tico, \xbfa qu\xe9 se refiere m\xe1s com\xfanmente el t\xe9rmino sesgo?","options":{"A":"La velocidad a la que se entrena un modelo","B":"Un error sistem\xe1tico que hace que un modelo favorezca consistentemente ciertos resultados sobre otros","C":"El n\xfamero total de par\xe1metros de un modelo","D":"El proceso de dividir los datos en conjuntos de entrenamiento y prueba"},"correct":"B","explanation":"En el aprendizaje autom\xe1tico, el sesgo se refiere a un error sistem\xe1tico en el que el modelo se inclina consistentemente hacia ciertas predicciones o resultados, lo que puede surgir de datos de entrenamiento no representativos, supuestos defectuosos en el algoritmo o conjuntos de datos desequilibrados. Identificar y reducir el sesgo es esencial para construir modelos justos y precisos.\\nLa velocidad de entrenamiento es una medida de rendimiento computacional, no de sesgo. El n\xfamero total de par\xe1metros describe la capacidad o complejidad del modelo. Dividir los datos en conjuntos de entrenamiento y prueba es un paso de preparaci\xf3n de datos llamado divisi\xf3n entrenamiento-prueba. Ninguno de estos se relaciona con el concepto de error de predicci\xf3n sistem\xe1tico."},{"id":"aif-q109","question":"Un equipo ha entrenado un modelo para predecir el abandono de clientes. El modelo es muy preciso con los datos de entrenamiento, pero predice mal a clientes nuevos no vistos. \xbfQu\xe9 cambio resolver\xe1 esto?","options":{"A":"Reducir la fuerza de regularizaci\xf3n para que el modelo pueda ajustarse a patrones m\xe1s complejos","B":"Agregar m\xe1s caracter\xedsticas al conjunto de datos de entrada","C":"Continuar entrenando el modelo durante m\xe1s \xe9pocas","D":"Aumentar la fuerza de regularizaci\xf3n para que el modelo sea menos complejo"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El modelo est\xe1 sobreajustado: ha memorizado efectivamente los datos de entrenamiento y no logra generalizar a clientes nuevos no vistos. Aumentar la fuerza de regularizaci\xf3n penaliza los pesos grandes y reduce la complejidad del modelo, lo que favorece un modelo m\xe1s simple que captura patrones generales en lugar de ruido, cerrando la brecha entre el entrenamiento y el rendimiento en el mundo real.\\nReducir la fuerza de regularizaci\xf3n aumentar\xeda la complejidad del modelo y empeorar\xeda el sobreajuste. Agregar m\xe1s caracter\xedsticas de entrada tambi\xe9n tiende a aumentar la complejidad, lo que nuevamente empeora la generalizaci\xf3n. Continuar entrenando durante m\xe1s \xe9pocas permite que el modelo se ajuste a\xfan m\xe1s a los datos de entrenamiento, lo que profundiza el sobreajuste en lugar de resolverlo."},{"id":"aif-q044","question":"Una empresa minorista cre\xf3 un modelo para pronosticar los precios de los productos. Obtuvo muy buenos resultados en el conjunto de datos de entrenamiento, pero cuando se lanz\xf3 a producci\xf3n su precisi\xf3n cay\xf3 dr\xe1sticamente. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer la empresa para abordar este problema?","options":{"A":"Entrenar el modelo durante un per\xedodo de tiempo m\xe1s largo","B":"Aumentar la cantidad de datos utilizados para el entrenamiento","C":"Reducir la cantidad de datos utilizados para el entrenamiento","D":"Agregar m\xe1s hiperpar\xe1metros al modelo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los buenos resultados en el conjunto de entrenamiento combinados con una ca\xedda dr\xe1stica en producci\xf3n son la se\xf1al cl\xe1sica de sobreajuste, en el que el modelo ha memorizado los ejemplos de entrenamiento en lugar de aprender patrones generales. Entrenar con un conjunto de datos m\xe1s grande y variado ayuda al modelo a generalizar a datos no vistos y cierra la brecha entre el rendimiento en entrenamiento y en producci\xf3n.\\nReducir los datos de entrenamiento deja al modelo a\xfan menos de lo que aprender y normalmente empeora el sobreajuste y la mala generalizaci\xf3n. Agregar m\xe1s hiperpar\xe1metros aumenta la complejidad del modelo, lo que tiende a profundizar el sobreajuste en lugar de aliviarlo. Entrenar durante m\xe1s tiempo simplemente ajusta m\xe1s estrechamente los datos de entrenamiento existentes y no hace nada para mejorar el rendimiento en datos nuevos, por lo que no resuelve el problema de generalizaci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q002","question":"Una editorial necesita convertir informes de investigaci\xf3n largos en res\xfamenes breves que capturen sus puntos principales, utilizando un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM). \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Crear un motor de recomendaciones","B":"Desarrollar un chatbot de resumen","C":"Crear un sistema de reconocimiento de entidades nombradas","D":"Crear un sistema de traducci\xf3n multiling\xfce"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un chatbot de resumen utiliza un LLM para leer documentos largos y producir una versi\xf3n m\xe1s corta que conserva los puntos esenciales, que es exactamente lo que requiere condensar informes de investigaci\xf3n en res\xfamenes breves.\\nUn motor de recomendaciones sugiere elementos relevantes a partir del comportamiento del usuario y no condensa documentos. Un sistema de reconocimiento de entidades nombradas solo extrae entidades como nombres, fechas y lugares, en lugar de producir un resumen coherente. Un sistema de traducci\xf3n multiling\xfce convierte texto entre idiomas sin acortar ni destilar el contenido."},{"id":"aif-q010","question":"Un fabricante de dispositivos para el hogar inteligente quiere que sus electrodom\xe9sticos ejecuten inferencia de modelos de lenguaje en el propio dispositivo, con la menor latencia posible. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Implementar modelos de lenguaje grandes (LLM) optimizados en los dispositivos de borde","B":"Implementar modelos de lenguaje peque\xf1os (SLM) optimizados en los dispositivos de borde","C":"Llamar de forma as\xedncrona a una API de modelo de lenguaje peque\xf1o (SLM) centralizado desde los dispositivos de borde","D":"Llamar de forma as\xedncrona a una API de modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) centralizado desde los dispositivos de borde"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Implementar modelos de lenguaje peque\xf1os (SLM) optimizados en los dispositivos de borde mantiene la inferencia local y evita los viajes de ida y vuelta por la red, lo que ofrece la menor latencia posible, y los SLM son lo suficientemente compactos para ejecutarse en hardware de borde con recursos limitados.\\nImplementar LLM en dispositivos de borde no es pr\xe1ctico porque su tama\xf1o y demandas de c\xf3mputo superan el hardware de borde t\xedpico y aumentar\xedan la latencia. Llamar a una API de SLM o LLM centralizado requiere comunicaci\xf3n de red, y los viajes de ida y vuelta as\xedncronos agregan latencia en lugar de minimizarla."},{"id":"aif-q012","question":"Un equipo de ingenier\xeda de una fintech planea adoptar Amazon Q Developer para lanzar funcionalidades m\xe1s r\xe1pido. \xbfQu\xe9 puede hacer Amazon Q Developer para ayudar al equipo?","options":{"A":"Ejecutar una aplicaci\xf3n sin aprovisionar ni administrar servidores","B":"Convertir archivos de audio en documentos de texto mediante el uso de modelos de ML","C":"Generar fragmentos de c\xf3digo y proporcionar seguimiento de referencias y seguimiento de licencias de c\xf3digo abierto","D":"Habilitar comandos de voz para programar y b\xfasqueda en lenguaje natural"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Q Developer aumenta la productividad de los desarrolladores al generar fragmentos de c\xf3digo y proporcionar seguimiento de referencias y seguimiento de licencias de c\xf3digo abierto.\\nEjecutar una aplicaci\xf3n sin aprovisionar ni administrar servidores describe la computaci\xf3n sin servidor, como AWS Lambda. Convertir archivos de audio en texto describe Amazon Transcribe. Habilitar comandos de voz para programar no es una funci\xf3n de Amazon Q Developer."},{"id":"aif-q018","question":"Un equipo de comercio electr\xf3nico est\xe1 creando una b\xfasqueda visual que acepta tanto una frase escrita como una foto de producto como consulta. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de modelo fundacional (FM) deber\xeda impulsarla?","options":{"A":"Modelo de embedding de texto","B":"Modelo de embedding multimodal","C":"Modelo de generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes","D":"Modelo de generaci\xf3n multimodal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un modelo de embedding multimodal codifica tanto texto como im\xe1genes en un espacio vectorial compartido, de modo que las consultas de cualquiera de los dos tipos se pueden comparar y emparejar, que es lo que necesita una aplicaci\xf3n de b\xfasqueda que maneja consultas de texto e im\xe1genes.\\nUn modelo de embedding de texto solo maneja texto. Un modelo de generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes crea im\xe1genes a partir de prompts en lugar de codificar consultas para la b\xfasqueda. Un modelo de generaci\xf3n multimodal produce contenido nuevo en distintas modalidades en lugar de los embeddings necesarios para la b\xfasqueda por similitud."},{"id":"aif-q022","question":"Una agencia de marketing est\xe1 explorando la IA generativa. \xbfCu\xe1l de estas tareas es un caso de uso de IA generativa?","options":{"A":"Mejorar la seguridad de la red mediante el uso de un sistema de detecci\xf3n de intrusiones","B":"Pronosticar tendencias del mercado de valores a partir de datos financieros hist\xf3ricos","C":"Generar im\xe1genes fotorrealistas a partir de descripciones de texto para una campa\xf1a de marketing","D":"Acelerar las consultas de bases de datos con indexaci\xf3n optimizada"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La IA generativa crea contenido nuevo, y producir im\xe1genes fotorrealistas a partir de descripciones de texto para una campa\xf1a de marketing es generaci\xf3n de contenido, un caso de uso cl\xe1sico de la IA generativa.\\nLa detecci\xf3n de intrusiones es una tarea de seguridad y clasificaci\xf3n. Pronosticar tendencias del mercado de valores a partir de datos hist\xf3ricos es an\xe1lisis predictivo. Optimizar la indexaci\xf3n de bases de datos es ajuste de rendimiento. Ninguna de estas opciones crea contenido nuevo, por lo que no son casos de uso de IA generativa."},{"id":"aif-q023","question":"Al elegir un modelo fundacional (FM) en Amazon Bedrock, un equipo de investigaci\xf3n necesita saber cu\xe1nto texto puede incluir en un solo prompt. \xbfQu\xe9 propiedad del modelo les indica esto?","options":{"A":"Temperatura","B":"Tama\xf1o del modelo","C":"Ventana de contexto","D":"Tama\xf1o de lote"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La ventana de contexto define cu\xe1ntos tokens puede recibir un modelo fundacional a la vez, por lo que determina cu\xe1nta informaci\xf3n cabe en un solo prompt.\\nLa temperatura controla la aleatoriedad de la salida, no la capacidad de entrada. El tama\xf1o del modelo se relaciona con el n\xfamero de par\xe1metros y la capacidad general, no con la longitud del prompt. El tama\xf1o de lote se refiere a cu\xe1ntas entradas se procesan juntas, no a cu\xe1nto texto puede contener un prompt."},{"id":"aif-q032","question":"Un peque\xf1o estudio quiere crear un prototipo en Amazon Bedrock con un presupuesto ajustado y evitar cualquier compromiso a largo plazo. \xbfQu\xe9 modelo de precios de Amazon Bedrock se ajusta?","options":{"A":"Provisioned Throughput","B":"Personalizaci\xf3n de modelos","C":"On-Demand","D":"Spot Instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El modelo de precios On-Demand cobra solo por lo que se usa, sin compromiso inicial ni a largo plazo, lo que se ajusta a un presupuesto limitado que necesita flexibilidad.\\nProvisioned Throughput requiere comprometerse y pagar por un nivel de capacidad fijo a lo largo del tiempo. La personalizaci\xf3n de modelos es una actividad de fine-tuning, no una opci\xf3n de precios flexible. Spot Instances es un concepto de precios de Amazon EC2 y no es un modelo de precios de Amazon Bedrock."},{"id":"aif-q033","question":"Un equipo de ciencia de datos quiere implementar r\xe1pidamente un modelo fundacional (FM) dentro de su propia VPC. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS permite al equipo hacerlo?","options":{"A":"Amazon Personalize","B":"PartyRock, an Amazon Bedrock Playground","C":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","D":"Amazon SageMaker AI endpoints"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart ofrece modelos fundacionales preconstruidos que un equipo puede implementar r\xe1pidamente dentro de su propia VPC y comenzar a usar de inmediato, lo que cumple con el requisito.\\nAmazon Personalize es un servicio de recomendaciones, no una caracter\xedstica de implementaci\xf3n de FM. PartyRock es un playground p\xfablico para experimentar con aplicaciones, no para implementar FM en una VPC. Los endpoints de Amazon SageMaker AI alojan modelos para inferencia, pero no proporcionan un cat\xe1logo curado de modelos fundacionales preconstruidos listos para implementar con unos pocos clics, que es el valor que aporta JumpStart."},{"id":"aif-q035","question":"Una empresa minorista mantiene grandes vol\xfamenes de datos de ventas en una base de datos. Quiere una aplicaci\xf3n que permita al personal no t\xe9cnico escribir una pregunta en lenguaje natural y que esta se convierta autom\xe1ticamente en una consulta SQL. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de modelo es el m\xe1s adecuado para impulsar esta aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Red neuronal residual (ResNet)","B":"M\xe1quina de vectores de soporte (SVM)","C":"WaveNet","D":"Transformer generativo preentrenado (GPT)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Los transformers generativos preentrenados son modelos de lenguaje grandes entrenados para generar texto, y pueden traducir una solicitud en lenguaje natural a una consulta SQL estructurada, lo que permite al personal no t\xe9cnico recuperar datos sin escribir SQL por s\xed mismo, exactamente como describe el requisito.\\nUna red neuronal residual (ResNet) es una arquitectura profunda creada para reconocimiento de im\xe1genes y visi\xf3n por computadora, no para generaci\xf3n de lenguaje. Una m\xe1quina de vectores de soporte (SVM) es un algoritmo supervisado cl\xe1sico para clasificaci\xf3n o regresi\xf3n y no puede generar SQL a partir de texto. WaveNet es un modelo generativo para producir audio y voz realistas, por lo que es la modalidad incorrecta para convertir texto en consultas."},{"id":"aif-q050","question":"Una empresa resume contratos con un modelo base de Amazon Bedrock y ahora ha entrenado un modelo personalizado para obtener mejor calidad. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer la empresa para usar el modelo personalizado en Amazon Bedrock?","options":{"A":"Implementar el modelo personalizado en un endpoint de Amazon SageMaker AI para inferencia en tiempo real","B":"Registrar el modelo en Amazon SageMaker Model Registry","C":"Solicitar acceso al modelo para el modelo personalizado en Amazon Bedrock","D":"Comprar Provisioned Throughput para el modelo personalizado"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Para ejecutar un modelo personalizado (fine-tuned) en Amazon Bedrock, la empresa debe comprar Provisioned Throughput para ese modelo, lo que asigna la capacidad dedicada que Bedrock requiere para servir modelos personalizados.\\nImplementar en un endpoint de SageMaker AI o registrar en SageMaker Model Registry son flujos de trabajo de Amazon SageMaker, no la forma en que Bedrock sirve un modelo personalizado. Solicitar acceso al modelo se aplica a los modelos base de Bedrock, no al uso de tu propio modelo personalizado."},{"id":"aif-q055","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando una aplicaci\xf3n que debe generar datos sint\xe9ticos basados en sus datos existentes. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de modelo puede usar la empresa para cumplir con este requisito?","options":{"A":"XGBoost","B":"WaveNet","C":"Red generativa adversarial (GAN)","D":"Red neuronal residual"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Una red generativa adversarial (GAN) se construye a partir de dos redes neuronales que compiten entre s\xed: un generador que crea muestras sint\xe9ticas similares a los datos reales y un discriminador que intenta distinguir lo real de lo generado; mediante esta competencia, aprende a producir datos sint\xe9ticos realistas basados en un conjunto de datos existente, que es exactamente lo que necesita la empresa.\\nXGBoost es un algoritmo de gradient boosting para clasificaci\xf3n y regresi\xf3n supervisadas, no para generar nuevos datos. Una red neuronal residual (ResNet) es una arquitectura convolucional profunda usada principalmente para reconocimiento y clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes. WaveNet es un modelo generativo, pero est\xe1 especializado en sintetizar audio sin procesar, como voz, en lugar de producir datos sint\xe9ticos generales a partir de un conjunto de datos existente arbitrario."},{"id":"aif-q063","question":"En IA y procesamiento de lenguaje natural (NLP), \xbfcu\xe1l es el t\xe9rmino para las representaciones num\xe9ricas de objetos y conceptos del mundo real que ayudan a los modelos a comprender mejor el texto?","options":{"A":"Tokens","B":"Par\xe1metros","C":"Embeddings","D":"Modelos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los embeddings son representaciones vectoriales num\xe9ricas de objetos y conceptos, como palabras o frases; al colocar elementos similares cerca unos de otros en el espacio vectorial, capturan relaciones y significado, lo que ayuda a los modelos de IA y NLP a comprender y trabajar con texto de manera m\xe1s eficaz.\\nLos tokens son las unidades individuales, como palabras o subpalabras, en las que se divide el texto antes del procesamiento, no representaciones de significado. Un modelo es el sistema entrenado que hace predicciones, no una representaci\xf3n de un concepto. Los par\xe1metros son los pesos internos que un modelo aprende durante el entrenamiento y no representan objetos o conceptos del mundo real."},{"id":"aif-q074","question":"Un analista de retail quiere gr\xe1ficos de ventas de los productos principales por tienda durante el \xfaltimo a\xf1o generados autom\xe1ticamente. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n de AWS se ajusta a esta necesidad?","options":{"A":"Amazon Q Developer","B":"Amazon Q en Amazon QuickSight","C":"Amazon Q en AWS Chatbot","D":"Amazon Q en Amazon EC2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Q en Amazon QuickSight permite a los usuarios hacer preguntas en lenguaje natural y crea autom\xe1ticamente gr\xe1ficos y visualizaciones, como las ventas totales de los productos principales, lo que cumple con el requisito.\\nAmazon Q Developer ayuda con tareas de codificaci\xf3n. Amazon Q en AWS Chatbot ayuda a operar recursos de AWS desde canales de chat. Amazon Q en Amazon EC2 proporciona orientaci\xf3n sobre EC2. Ninguno de estos genera visualizaciones de datos de negocio."},{"id":"aif-q080","question":"Una empresa de viajes recibe una avalancha de preguntas de reservas cada d\xeda y planea usar Agents for Amazon Bedrock para mantenerse al d\xeda. \xbfCu\xe1l es un beneficio clave de usar estos agentes?","options":{"A":"Generar modelos fundacionales (FM) personalizados para predecir las necesidades de los clientes","B":"Elegir un modelo fundacional (FM) seg\xfan criterios y m\xe9tricas predefinidos","C":"Automatizar tareas repetitivas y orquestar flujos de trabajo complejos de varios pasos","D":"Llamar a varios modelos fundacionales (FM) a la vez y fusionar sus resultados"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Agents for Amazon Bedrock automatiza tareas repetitivas y orquesta flujos de trabajo complejos de varios pasos, lo que permite a la empresa gestionar altos vol\xfamenes de consultas de forma m\xe1s r\xe1pida y eficiente.\\nLos agentes no generan modelos fundacionales personalizados, no eligen un modelo seg\xfan criterios predefinidos y su prop\xf3sito no es llamar a varios modelos y fusionar resultados. Su valor es la automatizaci\xf3n de tareas y la orquestaci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo."},{"id":"aif-q083","question":"Una startup est\xe1 presupuestando una aplicaci\xf3n de IA generativa en Amazon Bedrock y quiere saber qu\xe9 impulsa el costo de cada inferencia de un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM). \xbfQu\xe9 factor es?","options":{"A":"El valor de temperatura utilizado para la generaci\xf3n","B":"La cantidad de datos utilizados para entrenar el LLM","C":"La cantidad de tokens consumidos","D":"El tiempo total dedicado al entrenamiento del LLM"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El costo de inferencia se rige por la cantidad de tokens consumidos, porque el precio de los LLM cobra por los tokens de entrada y salida procesados en cada solicitud.\\nEl valor de temperatura solo determina la aleatoriedad de la salida y no tiene impacto en el costo. La cantidad de datos de entrenamiento y el tiempo total de entrenamiento afectan el costo \xfanico de construir el modelo, no el costo de ejecutar la inferencia."},{"id":"aif-q085","question":"Una empresa personaliz\xf3 un modelo base (FM) de Amazon Bedrock y ahora necesita cargar un conjunto de datos para que Bedrock valide las respuestas del modelo. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS debe alojar ese conjunto de datos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)","B":"AWS Snowcone","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 es el servicio de almacenamiento de objetos escalable que se integra con Amazon Bedrock, por lo que es el lugar adecuado para cargar y almacenar el conjunto de datos de validaci\xf3n que Bedrock utilizar\xe1.\\nAmazon EBS proporciona almacenamiento en bloques para vol\xfamenes de EC2, Amazon EFS proporciona almacenamiento de archivos compartido para instancias y AWS Snowcone es un dispositivo de transferencia de datos de borde. Ninguno de estos es el servicio de almacenamiento desde el que Bedrock lee conjuntos de datos."},{"id":"aif-q099","question":"Los analistas de una empresa de log\xedstica tienen poca experiencia en programaci\xf3n, pero necesitan crear trabajos de integraci\xf3n de datos en AWS Glue. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede ayudarlos?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon Q Developer","D":"Amazon Personalize"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Q Developer es un asistente de programaci\xf3n de IA generativa que puede generar y explicar c\xf3digo, lo que ayuda a usuarios con experiencia limitada en programaci\xf3n a crear y trabajar con trabajos de AWS Glue.\\nAWS Config realiza un seguimiento de la configuraci\xf3n y el cumplimiento de los recursos. Amazon Personalize proporciona recomendaciones. Amazon Comprehend realiza tareas de NLP, como an\xe1lisis de sentimiento y de entidades. Ninguno de estos ayuda a escribir o simplificar c\xf3digo de AWS Glue."},{"id":"aif-q105","question":"Un equipo de desarrollo quiere que un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) escriba c\xf3digo a partir de comentarios en lenguaje natural. \xbfEn qu\xe9 capacidad del LLM se basa esto?","options":{"A":"Resumen de texto","B":"Clasificaci\xf3n de texto","C":"Generaci\xf3n de texto","D":"Completado de texto"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La generaci\xf3n de texto es la capacidad del LLM que produce texto nuevo, en este caso c\xf3digo, a partir de una entrada dada, como comentarios en lenguaje natural, que es exactamente lo que necesita el requisito.\\nEl resumen de texto condensa texto existente. La clasificaci\xf3n de texto asigna etiquetas al texto. El completado de texto solo contin\xfaa un fragmento dado en lugar de generar c\xf3digo completo a partir de comentarios descriptivos."},{"id":"aif-q112","question":"Una cadena de farmacias quiere un resumen breve de cada medicamento nuevo basado en las rese\xf1as de sus clientes. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Crear un modelo de previsi\xf3n de series temporales para analizar las rese\xf1as con Amazon Personalize","B":"Crear un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n que agrupe los medicamentos en grupos con Amazon Comprehend","C":"Generar res\xfamenes de rese\xf1as de medicamentos con modelos de lenguaje grandes (LLM) de Amazon Bedrock","D":"Generar res\xfamenes de rese\xf1as de medicamentos con Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock ofrece modelos de lenguaje grandes adecuados para el resumen, por lo que generar res\xfamenes concisos de rese\xf1as de medicamentos con LLM de Bedrock cumple el requisito.\\nUn modelo de previsi\xf3n de series temporales con Amazon Personalize predice valores futuros y no sirve para resumir texto. Un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n con Amazon Comprehend clasifica elementos en categor\xedas en lugar de producir un resumen escrito. Amazon Rekognition analiza im\xe1genes y video, no rese\xf1as de texto."},{"id":"aif-q129","question":"Un desarrollador quiere un servicio que ofrezca modelos base (FM) para crear y escalar aplicaciones de IA generativa. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona esto?","options":{"A":"Amazon Q Developer","B":"Amazon Kendra","C":"Amazon Bedrock","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock da acceso a modelos base de AWS y de terceros a trav\xe9s de un servicio administrado, de modo que los usuarios pueden crear y escalar aplicaciones de IA generativa sin administrar infraestructura.\\nAmazon Q Developer es un asistente de programaci\xf3n. Amazon Kendra es un servicio de b\xfasqueda empresarial inteligente. Amazon Comprehend es un servicio de NLP. Ninguno de estos proporciona modelos base para crear aplicaciones de IA generativa."},{"id":"aif-q134","question":"Una empresa de cajas de suscripci\xf3n opera un chatbot de soporte de IA generativa y quiere medir su efecto financiero en las operaciones. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica debe monitorear la empresa?","options":{"A":"N\xfamero de consultas de clientes atendidas","B":"Costo por conversaci\xf3n con el cliente","C":"Tiempo medio de atenci\xf3n (AHT)","D":"Costo de entrenar los modelos de IA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El costo por conversaci\xf3n con el cliente cuantifica directamente el gasto operativo de usar el chatbot, por lo que la empresa puede medir su efecto financiero y compararlo con alternativas como agentes humanos.\\nEl n\xfamero de consultas atendidas mide el volumen, no el costo. El tiempo medio de atenci\xf3n mide la eficiencia y la velocidad. El costo de entrenar los modelos es un costo \xfanico de construcci\xf3n, no el costo operativo continuo de las conversaciones."},{"id":"aif-q137","question":"El chatbot de soporte de una aseguradora en Amazon Bedrock necesita varios turnos para resolver un siniestro. \xbfC\xf3mo puede el modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) usar el contenido de los mensajes anteriores del cliente?","options":{"A":"Activar el registro de invocaciones del modelo para recopilar los mensajes","B":"Usar Amazon Personalize para almacenar el historial de la conversaci\xf3n","C":"Incluir los mensajes anteriores en el prompt del modelo","D":"Usar Provisioned Throughput para el LLM"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Incluir los mensajes anteriores en el prompt del modelo proporciona al LLM el historial de la conversaci\xf3n que necesita, de modo que puede mantener el contexto entre turnos y responder de forma coherente.\\nEl registro de invocaciones del modelo registra las solicitudes para monitoreo, pero no devuelve el historial al modelo. Amazon Personalize es un servicio de recomendaciones, no una forma de proporcionar contexto al prompt. Provisioned Throughput establece la capacidad y no a\xf1ade contexto de conversaci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q138","question":"Un equipo de centro de llamadas brinda manualmente a los clientes consejos de productos basados en la ubicaci\xf3n y quiere que los modelos fundacionales (FMs) automaticen esta tarea. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS satisface esta necesidad?","options":{"A":"Amazon Macie","B":"Amazon Bedrock","C":"Amazon Textract","D":"Amazon Transcribe"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock permite a la empresa usar modelos fundacionales para generar descripciones de productos y recomendaciones basadas en la ubicaci\xf3n sin administrar infraestructura, lo que automatiza la tarea.\\nAmazon Macie descubre y protege datos sensibles. Amazon Textract extrae texto de documentos. Amazon Transcribe convierte voz a texto. Ninguno de estos proporciona modelos fundacionales para generar recomendaciones."},{"id":"aif-q152","question":"Una empresa de inversiones usa ML en todas sus operaciones. \xbfCu\xe1l de estos es un caso de uso de IA generativa?","options":{"A":"Clasificar clientes por uso de productos","B":"Pronosticar ingresos de ciertos productos","C":"Resumir quejas de clientes","D":"Segmentar clientes por tipo de inversi\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Resumir las quejas de clientes genera texto nuevo y condensado a partir de una entrada m\xe1s extensa, lo cual es una tarea de IA generativa que normalmente manejan los modelos de lenguaje grandes.\\nClasificar clientes por uso de productos y pronosticar ingresos son tareas predictivas, y segmentar clientes por tipo de inversi\xf3n es agrupamiento (clustering). Estas son tareas tradicionales de ML que no generan contenido nuevo."},{"id":"aif-q158","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n describe una caracter\xedstica definitoria de los modelos de lenguaje basados en transformers?","options":{"A":"Usan capas convolucionales para aplicar filtros a trav\xe9s de la entrada y capturar patrones locales","B":"Usan mecanismos de autoatenci\xf3n para capturar relaciones contextuales","C":"Solo pueden procesar datos de texto","D":"Procesan una secuencia un elemento a la vez en ciclos repetidos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los modelos basados en transformers usan mecanismos de autoatenci\xf3n que ponderan la importancia de cada token en relaci\xf3n con los dem\xe1s, lo que les permite capturar relaciones contextuales en toda una entrada.\\nLas capas convolucionales son caracter\xedsticas de las CNN, no de los transformers. Los transformers no se limitan al texto y pueden ser multimodales. Procesar un elemento a la vez en ciclos describe a las redes recurrentes (RNN), no la autoatenci\xf3n paralela de los transformers."},{"id":"aif-q168","question":"Una sala de redacci\xf3n est\xe1 probando un asistente de escritura de IA generativa. El tr\xe1fico de la prueba es bajo, el rendimiento no es una preocupaci\xf3n y el uso futuro es impredecible; la sala de redacci\xf3n quiere el costo m\xe1s bajo. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Usar instancias Amazon EC2 con GPU","B":"Usar Amazon Bedrock con precios On-Demand","C":"Usar Amazon Bedrock con Provisioned Throughput","D":"Usar Amazon SageMaker JumpStart"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock con precios On-Demand cobra solo por las llamadas de inferencia que realmente se realizan, sin compromiso de capacidad, por lo que mantiene los costos bajos durante una prueba peque\xf1a y escala de forma el\xe1stica con la demanda futura impredecible.\\nLas instancias EC2 con GPU generan costos continuos se usen o no. Provisioned Throughput requiere pagar por capacidad comprometida que el uso bajo e impredecible desperdiciar\xeda. Amazon SageMaker JumpStart todav\xeda requiere aprovisionar y administrar capacidad de alojamiento, lo que no es lo m\xe1s rentable para este caso."},{"id":"aif-q174","question":"Una empresa ajust\xf3 (fine-tuned) un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) personalizado desde Amazon Bedrock y atender\xe1 una cantidad constante y predecible de solicitudes por minuto en producci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n es M\xc1S rentable?","options":{"A":"Implementar el modelo en una instancia Amazon EC2 optimizada para computaci\xf3n","B":"Comprar Provisioned Throughput para el modelo en Amazon Bedrock","C":"Usar el modelo con rendimiento On-Demand en Amazon Bedrock","D":"Almacenar el modelo en Amazon S3 y alojarlo con AWS Lambda"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Para un volumen de solicitudes constante y predecible, Provisioned Throughput en Amazon Bedrock ofrece un costo por solicitud m\xe1s bajo al comprometerse con un nivel de rendimiento fijo, lo que lo convierte en la forma m\xe1s rentable de atender un modelo personalizado.\\nUna instancia EC2 optimizada para computaci\xf3n implica autoadministrar la infraestructura y no es la forma en que Bedrock atiende modelos personalizados. El rendimiento On-Demand cuesta m\xe1s por solicitud con un volumen alto constante. Almacenar el modelo en Amazon S3 y alojarlo con AWS Lambda no es una forma compatible de atender un modelo personalizado de Bedrock."},{"id":"aif-q375","question":"Una cl\xednica m\xe9dica quiere una herramienta de IA generativa con conversi\xf3n de voz a texto que ayude a los m\xe9dicos a dictar y crear notas cl\xednicas. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Q Developer","B":"Amazon Polly","C":"AWS HealthScribe","D":"Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS HealthScribe est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para el sector salud y combina reconocimiento de voz con IA generativa para transcribir conversaciones cl\xednicas y producir notas cl\xednicas estructuradas, lo que satisface la necesidad de la cl\xednica.\\nAmazon Q Developer es un asistente de programaci\xf3n. Amazon Polly realiza conversi\xf3n de texto a voz, la direcci\xf3n opuesta. Amazon Rekognition analiza im\xe1genes y video, no voz cl\xednica."},{"id":"aif-q184","question":"En un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM), \xbfqu\xe9 hacen posible los embeddings vectoriales?","options":{"A":"Dividir el texto en piezas manejables de datos","B":"Comparar textos matem\xe1ticamente","C":"Agrupar un conjunto de caracteres para tratarlos como una sola unidad","D":"Contar cada palabra en la entrada"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los embeddings vectoriales convierten el texto en vectores num\xe9ricos que capturan el significado, por lo que los textos se pueden comparar matem\xe1ticamente midiendo la distancia o similitud, lo que respalda tareas como recuperaci\xf3n y agrupamiento.\\nDividir el texto en piezas manejables describe el chunking. Agrupar caracteres en una sola unidad describe la tokenizaci\xf3n. Contar cada palabra es un simple recuento de frecuencia de palabras, no un embedding."},{"id":"aif-q197","question":"Una empresa de tecnolog\xeda educativa est\xe1 creando una app en la que los estudiantes escriben una pregunta o le toman una foto, y la app devuelve una respuesta escrita con una explicaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de modelo deber\xeda impulsar la app?","options":{"A":"Modelo de visi\xf3n por computadora","B":"Modelo de difusi\xf3n","C":"Modelo de lenguaje multimodal grande","D":"Modelo de texto a voz"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Un modelo de lenguaje multimodal grande puede recibir tanto texto como im\xe1genes como entrada y generar salida de texto, por lo que puede aceptar una pregunta escrita o fotografiada y devolver una respuesta escrita con una explicaci\xf3n.\\nUn modelo de visi\xf3n por computadora analiza im\xe1genes, pero no genera respuestas de texto explicativas. Un modelo de difusi\xf3n genera im\xe1genes, no respuestas de texto. Un modelo de texto a voz convierte texto en audio, lo que no produce respuestas escritas a partir de entradas mixtas."},{"id":"aif-q199","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n describe correctamente los embeddings en la IA generativa?","options":{"A":"Los embeddings buscan en los datos para encontrar la informaci\xf3n m\xe1s \xfatil para responder preguntas en lenguaje natural","B":"Los embeddings reducen los requisitos de hardware al usar un tipo de dato menos preciso para los pesos y las activaciones","C":"Los embeddings representan los datos como vectores de alta dimensionalidad que capturan relaciones sem\xe1nticas","D":"Los embeddings almacenan y recuperan datos para aplicaciones de IA generativa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los embeddings asignan entradas como palabras o documentos a vectores de alta dimensionalidad donde la similitud sem\xe1ntica corresponde a la cercan\xeda en el espacio vectorial, lo que permite a los modelos razonar sobre el significado y las relaciones.\\nBuscar en los datos para encontrar informaci\xf3n \xfatil describe la recuperaci\xf3n, como en RAG. Reducir las necesidades de hardware con un tipo de dato menos preciso describe la cuantizaci\xf3n. Almacenar y recuperar datos describe una base de datos o un almac\xe9n de vectores, no los embeddings en s\xed."},{"id":"aif-q201","question":"Una empresa necesita un modelo de IA generativa para una aplicaci\xf3n que debe responder a los usuarios en tiempo real. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica del modelo es m\xe1s importante?","options":{"A":"Complejidad del modelo","B":"Velocidad de inferencia","C":"Tiempo de entrenamiento","D":"Velocidad de innovaci\xf3n"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La velocidad de inferencia determina con qu\xe9 rapidez el modelo genera una respuesta, por lo que es la caracter\xedstica clave para una aplicaci\xf3n en tiempo real en la que los usuarios necesitan respuestas con baja latencia.\\nLa complejidad del modelo afecta la capacidad y el costo, pero no directamente el requisito de tiempo real. El tiempo de entrenamiento es una consideraci\xf3n \xfanica de construcci\xf3n. La velocidad de innovaci\xf3n describe con qu\xe9 rapidez evoluciona una familia de modelos; ninguna de estas gobierna la latencia de respuesta en tiempo real."},{"id":"aif-q202","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el t\xe9rmino para las instrucciones que se dan a un modelo fundacional (FM) para ayudarlo a devolver una respuesta m\xe1s precisa?","options":{"A":"Direcci\xf3n","B":"Di\xe1logo","C":"Prompt","D":"Traducci\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Un prompt es la instrucci\xf3n o consulta de entrada que se proporciona a un modelo fundacional y que gu\xeda lo que debe hacer, lo que lo ayuda a producir una respuesta m\xe1s precisa.\\nDirecci\xf3n y di\xe1logo no son el t\xe9rmino para la instrucci\xf3n de entrada del modelo. Traducci\xf3n es un tipo de tarea, no la instrucci\xf3n en s\xed."},{"id":"aif-q242","question":"Una empresa quiere un asistente de IA que permita a los empleados hacer preguntas sobre datos internos de la empresa. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS satisface esta necesidad?","options":{"A":"Amazon Rekognition","B":"Amazon Textract","C":"Amazon Q Business","D":"Amazon Lex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Q Business crea asistentes de IA generativa que se conectan a fuentes de datos internas, de modo que los empleados pueden hacer preguntas y obtener respuestas, lo que satisface el requisito.\\nAmazon Rekognition analiza im\xe1genes y video. Amazon Textract extrae texto de documentos. Amazon Lex crea chatbots conversacionales, pero no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado como un asistente empresarial sobre datos internos de la empresa."},{"id":"aif-q250","question":"Un equipo quiere una forma pr\xe1ctica y de bajo costo para experimentar con aplicaciones de IA generativa y aprender sobre ellas. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n es M\xc1S rentable?","options":{"A":"Amazon Q Developer","B":"Amazon Bedrock PartyRock","C":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","D":"Amazon Q Business"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock PartyRock proporciona un entorno pr\xe1ctico de bajo costo para crear y experimentar con aplicaciones de IA generativa, lo que se ajusta al aprendizaje en un entorno experimental de manera rentable.\\nAmazon Q Developer es un asistente de programaci\xf3n. Amazon SageMaker JumpStart requiere aprovisionar y administrar el alojamiento de modelos. Amazon Q Business es un asistente empresarial sobre datos de la empresa. Ninguno de estos es un entorno de experimentaci\xf3n de bajo costo para aprender sobre aplicaciones de IA generativa."},{"id":"aif-q275","question":"Un estudio que produce contenido de video quiere usar IA generativa para crear nuevos clips y reducir el tiempo de producci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos de la forma M\xc1S eficiente desde el punto de vista operativo?","options":{"A":"Usar el modelo Amazon Titan Image Generator en Amazon Bedrock para crear im\xe1genes intermedias y luego ensamblar videos con software de edici\xf3n de video","B":"Usar el modelo Amazon Nova Reel en Amazon Bedrock para generar videos","C":"Usar el modelo Amazon Nova Canvas en Amazon Bedrock para crear im\xe1genes intermedias y luego ensamblar videos con software de edici\xf3n de video","D":"Usar el modelo Amazon Nova Pro en Amazon Bedrock para generar videos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El modelo Amazon Nova Reel en Amazon Bedrock est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la creaci\xf3n de video generativo, por lo que permite a la empresa generar nuevo contenido de video directamente y reducir el tiempo de producci\xf3n de la forma m\xe1s eficiente desde el punto de vista operativo.\\nUsar Amazon Titan Image Generator o Amazon Nova Canvas produce solo im\xe1genes fijas que luego requieren edici\xf3n manual para convertirlas en videos, lo que agrega pasos. Amazon Nova Pro es un modelo multimodal general y no es la opci\xf3n espec\xedficamente dise\xf1ada para la generaci\xf3n de video."},{"id":"aif-q278","question":"Un equipo de cumplimiento quiere extraer las ideas clave de documentos de pol\xedticas extensos para que el personal pueda trabajar m\xe1s r\xe1pido. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de IA generativa cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Regresi\xf3n","B":"Resumen","C":"Agrupamiento en cl\xfasteres","D":"Clasificaci\xf3n"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El resumen condensa documentos extensos en sus ideas clave, de modo que los empleados pueden comprender y actuar r\xe1pidamente sobre la informaci\xf3n esencial.\\nLa regresi\xf3n predice valores num\xe9ricos, el agrupamiento en cl\xfasteres agrupa elementos similares y la clasificaci\xf3n asigna etiquetas. Ninguna de estas extrae y condensa las ideas clave de un documento."},{"id":"aif-q282","question":"Una tienda en l\xednea usa un asistente de IA generativa en Amazon Bedrock para recomendar productos y quiere medir su impacto directo en las ventas. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"El n\xfamero de interacciones de los clientes con el asistente de IA","B":"Las puntuaciones del an\xe1lisis de sentimiento de los comentarios de los clientes despu\xe9s de las interacciones","C":"La tasa de conversi\xf3n de los clientes que compran despu\xe9s de interactuar con el asistente de IA","D":"Las tasas de precisi\xf3n de comprensi\xf3n del lenguaje natural"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La tasa de conversi\xf3n, la proporci\xf3n de clientes que compran despu\xe9s de interactuar con el asistente, vincula directamente la interacci\xf3n con el asistente con los resultados de ventas, por lo que mide el impacto directo en las ventas.\\nEl n\xfamero de interacciones mide el volumen de uso, no las ventas. Las puntuaciones de sentimiento miden el estado de \xe1nimo del cliente. La precisi\xf3n de comprensi\xf3n del lenguaje natural mide la calidad t\xe9cnica. Ninguna de estas mide directamente el impacto en las ventas."},{"id":"aif-q284","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 escenario es un caso de uso pr\xe1ctico de IA generativa?","options":{"A":"Usar un modelo de ML para pronosticar la demanda de productos","B":"Rastrear el tr\xe1fico del sitio web y el comportamiento de los usuarios con un panel de an\xe1lisis","C":"Usar un chatbot para dar respuestas similares a las humanas a las consultas de los clientes en tiempo real","D":"Usar un motor de recomendaciones basado en reglas para sugerir productos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Un chatbot que da respuestas similares a las humanas a las consultas de los clientes en tiempo real usa un modelo generativo para crear respuestas relevantes en lenguaje natural, lo cual es un caso de uso pr\xe1ctico de IA generativa.\\nPronosticar la demanda de productos con un modelo de ML es anal\xedtica predictiva. Un panel de an\xe1lisis rastrea m\xe9tricas. Un motor de recomendaciones basado en reglas sigue reglas fijas. Ninguno de estos genera contenido nuevo."},{"id":"aif-q293","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la diferencia entre los modelos de IA discriminativos y generativos?","options":{"A":"Los modelos discriminativos son m\xe1s r\xe1pidos que los modelos generativos","B":"Los modelos discriminativos son m\xe1s precisos que los modelos generativos","C":"Los modelos discriminativos clasifican o predicen, mientras que los modelos generativos crean contenido nuevo","D":"Los modelos discriminativos necesitan m\xe1s datos que los modelos generativos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los modelos discriminativos aprenden a clasificar entradas o predecir resultados, mientras que los modelos generativos aprenden a crear contenido nuevo similar a sus datos de entrenamiento; esta es la distinci\xf3n central entre ambos.\\nLas afirmaciones de que los modelos discriminativos son inherentemente m\xe1s r\xe1pidos, m\xe1s precisos o necesitan m\xe1s datos no son la diferencia definitoria y no son generalmente ciertas; la verdadera distinci\xf3n es lo que produce cada tipo de modelo."},{"id":"aif-q295","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el prop\xf3sito principal de Amazon Q Business?","options":{"A":"Entrenar modelos de ML personalizados","B":"Almacenar grandes conjuntos de datos","C":"Proporcionar asistencia empresarial impulsada por IA sobre los datos de la empresa","D":"Supervisar la infraestructura"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Q Business es un asistente de IA generativa para la empresa; su prop\xf3sito principal es proporcionar asistencia impulsada por IA sobre los datos internos de una empresa, responder preguntas, generar contenido y resumir informaci\xf3n respetando los controles de acceso.\\nEntrenar modelos de ML personalizados es la funci\xf3n de Amazon SageMaker AI. Almacenar grandes conjuntos de datos es la funci\xf3n de servicios como Amazon S3. Supervisar la infraestructura es la funci\xf3n de servicios como Amazon CloudWatch. Ninguno de estos es el prop\xf3sito de Amazon Q Business."},{"id":"aif-q298","question":"Una empresa compara modelos Amazon Nova en Amazon Bedrock y necesita un modelo multimodal que admita varios idiomas. \xbfQu\xe9 modelo Nova cumple estos requisitos de la forma M\xc1S rentable?","options":{"A":"Nova Pro","B":"Nova Lite","C":"Nova Canvas","D":"Nova Reel"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Nova Lite es un modelo multimodal de bajo costo que admite varios idiomas, por lo que cumple los requisitos multimodales y multiling\xfces como la opci\xf3n Nova m\xe1s rentable.\\nAmazon Nova Pro tambi\xe9n es multimodal, pero es un modelo de mayor costo y mayores capacidades. Amazon Nova Canvas genera im\xe1genes y Amazon Nova Reel genera video, por lo que estos modelos de generaci\xf3n de prop\xf3sito \xfanico no son opciones multimodales multiling\xfces rentables."},{"id":"aif-q303","question":"Una empresa de tecnolog\xeda educativa est\xe1 creando una aplicaci\xf3n de lectura que permite a los estudiantes agregar ilustraciones a sus historias. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Usar Amazon Polly para crear un audiolibro a partir del texto de la historia","B":"Usar Amazon Rekognition para analizar el contenido de im\xe1genes y detectar atributos de texto","C":"Usar Amazon Bedrock Stable Diffusion 3.5 Large para generar im\xe1genes a partir de entradas de texto","D":"Crear una plantilla de prompt est\xe1ndar y usar Amazon Q Business para ilustrar las historias"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Stable Diffusion 3.5 Large genera im\xe1genes a partir de prompts de texto, por lo que los estudiantes pueden crear ilustraciones para sus historias a partir de descripciones, lo que apoya la comprensi\xf3n lectora con elementos visuales.\\nAmazon Polly convierte texto a voz, produciendo audio en lugar de ilustraciones. Amazon Rekognition analiza im\xe1genes existentes en lugar de generar nuevas. Amazon Q Business es un asistente empresarial sobre datos de la empresa y no genera ilustraciones para historias."},{"id":"aif-q309","question":"Un desarrollador quiere crear r\xe1pidamente un caso de prueba y documentaci\xf3n para alg\xfan c\xf3digo con el MENOR esfuerzo. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Cargar el c\xf3digo en un asistente de programaci\xf3n en l\xednea","B":"Usar Amazon Q Developer en un entorno de desarrollo integrado (IDE)","C":"Crear una aplicaci\xf3n que use modelos fundacionales (FMs)","D":"Investigar y escribir los casos de prueba a mano, luego agregar la documentaci\xf3n"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Q Developer en un IDE puede generar autom\xe1ticamente casos de prueba y documentaci\xf3n para el c\xf3digo, lo que cumple el requisito con el menor esfuerzo.\\nCargar el c\xf3digo en un asistente de programaci\xf3n en l\xednea agrega pasos manuales y preocupaciones sobre el manejo de datos. Crear una aplicaci\xf3n personalizada sobre modelos fundacionales requiere mucho m\xe1s esfuerzo. Investigar y escribir todo a mano es el mayor esfuerzo de todos."},{"id":"aif-q310","question":"Una empresa de comercio electr\xf3nico necesita generar miles de descripciones de productos \xfanicas por d\xeda, escritas como p\xe1rrafos con un estilo y tono consistentes. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de modelo generativo cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Un modelo de autoencoder variacional (VAE)","B":"Un modelo de difusi\xf3n","C":"Un modelo basado en transformadores","D":"Un modelo de red generativa adversarial (GAN)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los modelos basados en transformadores, como los modelos de lenguaje grandes, generan p\xe1rrafos de texto coherentes con un estilo y tono consistentes y escalan para producir miles de descripciones \xfanicas, lo que se ajusta al requisito.\\nLos autoencoders variacionales y las redes generativas adversarias se usan principalmente para generar im\xe1genes u otros datos, no texto fluido de formato largo. Los modelos de difusi\xf3n generan im\xe1genes, por lo que ninguno de estos es adecuado para producir texto de descripciones de productos con estilo a escala."},{"id":"aif-q334","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 es un token en el contexto de la IA generativa?","options":{"A":"Una caracter\xedstica de seguridad","B":"Un tipo de red neuronal","C":"Una unidad de texto procesada por el modelo","D":"Una m\xe9trica de evaluaci\xf3n del modelo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Un token es la unidad b\xe1sica de texto, como una palabra o subpalabra, que un modelo procesa y genera, que es a lo que se refiere el t\xe9rmino.\\nUn token no es una caracter\xedstica de seguridad, no es un tipo de red neuronal y no es una m\xe9trica de evaluaci\xf3n del modelo; esos describen conceptos no relacionados."},{"id":"aif-q336","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una ventaja principal de la adaptabilidad de la IA generativa?","options":{"A":"Solo puede trabajar con datos estructurados","B":"Siempre produce resultados perfectos","C":"Puede manejar una amplia gama de tareas y dominios","D":"Elimina la necesidad de cualquier supervisi\xf3n humana"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La adaptabilidad significa que un modelo generativo se puede aplicar a una amplia gama de tareas y dominios, lo cual es su ventaja clave.\\nLa IA generativa no se limita a datos estructurados, no siempre produce resultados perfectos y no elimina la necesidad de supervisi\xf3n humana; estas afirmaciones describen incorrectamente sus capacidades."},{"id":"aif-q338","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para desarrollar aplicaciones de IA generativa?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon Bedrock","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon RDS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para IA generativa, ya que proporciona acceso a modelos fundacionales y herramientas para crear y escalar aplicaciones de IA generativa.\\nAmazon EC2 proporciona capacidad de c\xf3mputo general, Amazon S3 proporciona almacenamiento de objetos y Amazon RDS proporciona bases de datos relacionales administradas; ninguno est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para desarrollar aplicaciones de IA generativa."},{"id":"aif-q339","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 es un modelo fundacional en la IA generativa?","options":{"A":"Un modelo que solo puede generar texto","B":"Un modelo dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para la generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes","C":"Un modelo grande y preentrenado que se puede adaptar para muchas tareas","D":"Un modelo que no requiere datos de entrenamiento"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Un modelo fundacional es un modelo grande preentrenado con grandes cantidades de datos que se puede adaptar o ajustar para muchas tareas posteriores, como generaci\xf3n de texto, resumen y respuesta a preguntas.\\nUn modelo fundacional no se limita al texto ni est\xe1 dise\xf1ado solo para la generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes, y se entrena con grandes cantidades de datos, por lo que la afirmaci\xf3n de que no necesita datos de entrenamiento es incorrecta."},{"id":"aif-q341","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una ventaja principal de usar servicios de IA generativa de AWS para crear aplicaciones?","options":{"A":"Siempre son gratuitos","B":"Garantizan una precisi\xf3n del 100 %","C":"Reducen la barrera de entrada","D":"Eliminan la necesidad de cualquier codificaci\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los servicios de IA generativa de AWS proporcionan infraestructura administrada, modelos fundacionales preentrenados y API, lo que reduce la barrera de entrada al eliminar la necesidad de crear y entrenar modelos grandes desde cero.\\nEstos servicios no siempre son gratuitos, no garantizan una precisi\xf3n perfecta y no eliminan la necesidad de cualquier codificaci\xf3n; esas afirmaciones exageran lo que proporcionan los servicios."},{"id":"aif-q082","question":"En la IA generativa, \xbfqu\xe9 son los tokens?","options":{"A":"Las representaciones vectoriales de palabras o conceptos","B":"Las unidades b\xe1sicas de entrada y salida, como palabras o subpalabras","C":"Las instrucciones o prompts que se dan al modelo","D":"Las capas ocultas de la red neuronal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los tokens son las unidades m\xe1s peque\xf1as, como palabras, subpalabras o caracteres, que un modelo generativo lee y produce, y forman la base de su entrada y salida.\\nLas representaciones vectoriales describen embeddings, los prompts son las instrucciones que se dan al modelo y las capas ocultas forman parte de la arquitectura del modelo; ninguna de estas define un token."},{"id":"aif-q193","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 dos afirmaciones sobre el espacio latente de un modelo son correctas?","options":{"A":"Debe almacenarse en un tipo espec\xedfico de base de datos","B":"Captura la comprensi\xf3n interna del modelo sobre las relaciones entre conceptos","C":"Se aplica solo a datos de im\xe1genes","D":"Permite calcular la similitud sem\xe1ntica entre diferentes entradas","E":"Es una tabla de b\xfasqueda legible por humanos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"El espacio latente de un modelo es la representaci\xf3n interna donde organiza conceptos, por lo que tanto captura las relaciones que el modelo ha aprendido como le permite calcular la similitud sem\xe1ntica entre diferentes entradas.\\nEl espacio latente no se limita a im\xe1genes, no requiere un tipo espec\xedfico de base de datos y no es una tabla de b\xfasqueda legible por humanos."},{"id":"aif-q198","question":"En el ciclo de vida de un modelo de IA generativa, \xbfqu\xe9 etapa ejecuta pruebas para comprobar la precisi\xf3n del modelo?","options":{"A":"Preentrenamiento","B":"Ajuste fino","C":"Evaluaci\xf3n","D":"Despliegue"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La etapa de evaluaci\xf3n prueba el modelo con datos de validaci\xf3n o de reserva (hold-out) usando m\xe9tricas como la precisi\xf3n antes de pasar al despliegue.\\nEl preentrenamiento construye el modelo base, el ajuste fino lo adapta a una tarea y el despliegue pone el modelo terminado en producci\xf3n; ninguna de estas es donde se realiza la prueba de precisi\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q289","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el beneficio principal de Amazon SageMaker JumpStart?","options":{"A":"Reduce los costos de inferencia","B":"Proporciona acceso a modelos y soluciones preentrenados","C":"Ajusta autom\xe1ticamente los hiperpar\xe1metros del modelo","D":"Gestiona el etiquetado de datos con una fuerza laboral humana"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart proporciona acceso a modelos preentrenados, algoritmos integrados y soluciones de extremo a extremo, lo que acelera el inicio de proyectos de machine learning e IA generativa.\\nLa reducci\xf3n del costo de inferencia, el ajuste de hiperpar\xe1metros y el etiquetado humano administrado se gestionan mediante otras capacidades, como SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning y SageMaker Ground Truth Plus."},{"id":"aif-q340","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes NO es una etapa del ciclo de vida de los modelos fundacionales (FM)?","options":{"A":"Ajuste fino","B":"Marketing","C":"Preentrenamiento","D":"Despliegue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Marketing es una actividad empresarial y no forma parte del ciclo de vida t\xe9cnico de los FM, que abarca etapas como selecci\xf3n de datos, preentrenamiento, ajuste fino, evaluaci\xf3n y despliegue.\\nEl ajuste fino, el preentrenamiento y el despliegue son etapas genuinas del ciclo de vida, por lo que ninguna de ellas es la respuesta correcta a qu\xe9 NO es una etapa."},{"id":"aif-q342","question":"En IA generativa, \xbfqu\xe9 es la ingenier\xeda de prompts?","options":{"A":"Un m\xe9todo para comprimir modelos para que se ejecuten m\xe1s r\xe1pido","B":"El proceso de elaborar prompts de entrada eficaces para guiar las salidas de un modelo","C":"Una t\xe9cnica para dise\xf1ar la arquitectura de la red neuronal de un modelo","D":"El proceso de etiquetar datos de entrenamiento para el aprendizaje supervisado"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La ingenier\xeda de prompts es la pr\xe1ctica de dise\xf1ar y refinar prompts de entrada, por ejemplo a\xf1adiendo contexto, restricciones o ejemplos, para guiar a un modelo generativo hacia salidas precisas y relevantes.\\nNo es compresi\xf3n de modelos, ni el dise\xf1o de una arquitectura de red neuronal, ni el etiquetado de datos de entrenamiento."},{"id":"aif-q077","question":"Un profesional de IA usa un modelo de lenguaje grande para escribir textos de marketing. La salida suena plausible y factual, pero en realidad es incorrecta. \xbfQu\xe9 problema est\xe1 exhibiendo el modelo?","options":{"A":"Fuga de datos","B":"Alucinaci\xf3n","C":"Sobreajuste","D":"Subajuste"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La alucinaci\xf3n ocurre cuando un modelo de lenguaje grande produce contenido que suena plausible y factual, pero que en realidad es incorrecto o fabricado, lo cual coincide con el texto de marketing descrito.\\nLa fuga de datos es la exposici\xf3n no intencionada de informaci\xf3n, el sobreajuste es cuando un modelo memoriza los datos de entrenamiento y generaliza mal, y el subajuste es cuando un modelo es demasiado simple para capturar patrones. Ninguna de estas describe una salida expresada con seguridad pero falsa."},{"id":"aif-q180","question":"Una empresa tiene una aplicaci\xf3n que ingiere quejas de consumidores de fuentes p\xfablicas y las procesa con reglas complejas codificadas de forma r\xedgida. La empresa quiere escalar esta l\xf3gica a nuevos mercados y l\xedneas de productos. \xbfQu\xe9 ventaja de los modelos de IA generativa se aplica a este escenario?","options":{"A":"Salidas predecibles","B":"Adaptabilidad","C":"Menor sensibilidad a los cambios en la entrada","D":"Explicabilidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La adaptabilidad es la ventaja de la IA generativa que se aplica aqu\xed: los modelos pueden generalizar patrones aprendidos a nuevos mercados y l\xedneas de productos con poca reconfiguraci\xf3n manual, evitando la necesidad de reescribir reglas complejas codificadas de forma r\xedgida.\\nLas salidas predecibles y la menor sensibilidad a los cambios en la entrada no son fortalezas de la IA generativa, que es no determinista y sensible a la redacci\xf3n de la entrada. La explicabilidad no est\xe1 relacionada con escalar la l\xf3gica de procesamiento de quejas."},{"id":"aif-q302","question":"Un profesional de IA observa que un modelo de lenguaje grande devuelve respuestas diferentes cada vez que recibe la misma entrada. \xbfQu\xe9 riesgo de la IA describe esto?","options":{"A":"Alucinaciones","B":"No determinismo","C":"Exactitud","D":"Multimodalidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El no determinismo es la propiedad por la cual un modelo de lenguaje grande puede devolver respuestas diferentes ante la misma entrada debido a la aleatoriedad en c\xf3mo genera el texto, lo cual coincide con el comportamiento observado.\\nLas alucinaciones son salidas fabricadas pero plausibles, la exactitud se refiere a la correcci\xf3n y la multimodalidad es la capacidad de trabajar con distintos tipos de datos, como texto e im\xe1genes. Ninguna de estas describe respuestas variables ante entradas id\xe9nticas."},{"id":"aif-q337","question":"En el contexto de la IA generativa, \xbfqu\xe9 es una alucinaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Una salida visual producida por el modelo","B":"Un tipo de arquitectura de modelo","C":"Una salida incorrecta o fabricada presentada como un hecho","D":"Un m\xe9todo de entrenamiento de un modelo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Una alucinaci\xf3n es una salida que es incorrecta o fabricada, pero que el modelo presenta como si fuera factual.\\nUna alucinaci\xf3n no es una salida visual, un tipo de arquitectura de modelo ni un m\xe9todo de entrenamiento de un modelo. Estas opciones describen conceptos no relacionados."},{"id":"aif-q343","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una posible desventaja de las soluciones de IA generativa?","options":{"A":"Adaptabilidad","B":"Capacidad de respuesta","C":"Inexactitud","D":"Simplicidad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La inexactitud es una desventaja conocida de la IA generativa, que puede producir informaci\xf3n incorrecta, sesgada o fabricada que requiere validaci\xf3n y supervisi\xf3n humana.\\nLa adaptabilidad y la capacidad de respuesta son ventajas de la IA generativa, y la simplicidad no es una desventaja. Ninguna de estas representa un inconveniente de las soluciones de IA generativa."},{"id":"aif-q353","question":"Una empresa de medios quiere usar un modelo fundacional desarrollado por AWS para generar textos de marketing e im\xe1genes. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona modelos fundacionales propios creados por Amazon?","options":{"A":"Amazon Rekognition","B":"Amazon Nova","C":"Amazon Comprehend","D":"Amazon Polly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Nova es una familia de modelos fundacionales desarrollados por AWS que pueden generar texto, im\xe1genes y otro contenido, lo que la convierte en la opci\xf3n adecuada cuando una empresa quiere usar modelos fundacionales propios creados por Amazon para la generaci\xf3n de contenido.\\nAmazon Rekognition analiza im\xe1genes y video para la detecci\xf3n de objetos y el reconocimiento facial, pero no genera contenido. Amazon Comprehend extrae informaci\xf3n como sentimiento y entidades del texto. Amazon Polly convierte texto a voz. Ninguno de estos es un modelo fundacional generativo creado por Amazon."},{"id":"aif-q376","question":"Un equipo de desarrollo est\xe1 creando un asistente de atenci\xf3n al cliente en Amazon Bedrock. El asistente debe recordar detalles de la cuenta de partes anteriores de la conversaci\xf3n, hacer referencia a art\xedculos de ayuda relevantes y seguir las directrices de la empresa. \xbfQu\xe9 concepto describe la gesti\xf3n de toda esta informaci\xf3n que se proporciona al modelo fundacional?","options":{"A":"Ingenier\xeda de prompts","B":"Ingenier\xeda de contexto","C":"Destilaci\xf3n de modelos","D":"Preentrenamiento continuo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La ingenier\xeda de contexto es la pr\xe1ctica de gestionar el conjunto completo de informaci\xf3n que se proporciona a un modelo fundacional en el momento de la inferencia, incluido el historial de conversaci\xf3n, los documentos recuperados, las instrucciones del sistema y cualquier otro dato relevante, para que el modelo produzca respuestas precisas y fundamentadas que tengan en cuenta todo el contexto necesario.\\nLa ingenier\xeda de prompts se centra espec\xedficamente en elaborar el texto de la instrucci\xf3n o consulta en s\xed, mientras que la ingenier\xeda de contexto abarca la estrategia m\xe1s amplia de ensamblar toda la informaci\xf3n relevante que el modelo necesita. La destilaci\xf3n de modelos comprime un modelo m\xe1s grande en uno m\xe1s peque\xf1o para mayor eficiencia. El preentrenamiento continuo actualiza el conocimiento de un modelo entrenando con nuevos datos a lo largo del tiempo, no gestionando el contexto en tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q377","question":"El asistente de IA de un bufete de abogados en Amazon Bedrock debe combinar la pregunta del usuario, las cl\xe1usulas contractuales relevantes recuperadas de una base de conocimiento, las reglas de cumplimiento del bufete y el historial de conversaci\xf3n en una \xfanica llamada al modelo. \xbfQu\xe9 pr\xe1ctica rige c\xf3mo se ensamblan y priorizan estos elementos?","options":{"A":"Ajuste fino","B":"Ajuste de hiperpar\xe1metros","C":"Ingenier\xeda de contexto","D":"Ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La ingenier\xeda de contexto rige c\xf3mo todos los elementos de informaci\xf3n, como los documentos recuperados, las reglas del sistema, la entrada del usuario y el historial de conversaci\xf3n, se ensamblan, priorizan y estructuran antes de pasarse al modelo, lo que garantiza que tenga el contexto adecuado para responder con precisi\xf3n.\\nEl ajuste fino actualiza los pesos del modelo con nuevos datos de entrenamiento y no controla lo que se pasa en el momento de la inferencia. El ajuste de hiperpar\xe1metros ajusta configuraciones de entrenamiento como la tasa de aprendizaje y el tama\xf1o de lote. La ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas transforma datos brutos en entradas del modelo durante el flujo de entrenamiento de ML, no en el momento de la inferencia para un modelo fundacional."},{"id":"aif-q378","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el papel de Model Context Protocol (MCP) en los sistemas de IA ag\xe9ntica?","options":{"A":"Comprime modelos para reducir la latencia de inferencia","B":"Proporciona una interfaz est\xe1ndar para conectar agentes de IA con herramientas y fuentes de datos externas","C":"Cifra los pesos del modelo durante el entrenamiento","D":"Mide la precisi\xf3n del modelo en conjuntos de datos de referencia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Model Context Protocol (MCP) proporciona una interfaz estandarizada que permite a los agentes de IA conectarse a herramientas, API y fuentes de datos externas de manera consistente, lo que les permite recuperar informaci\xf3n y realizar acciones en distintos sistemas sin integraciones personalizadas para cada uno.\\nLa compresi\xf3n de modelos para reducir la latencia describe t\xe9cnicas como la cuantizaci\xf3n o la destilaci\xf3n. El cifrado de pesos del modelo es una pr\xe1ctica de seguridad gestionada por servicios como AWS KMS. La medici\xf3n de la precisi\xf3n en benchmarks es evaluaci\xf3n de modelos. Ninguna de estas describe un protocolo para la conectividad entre agentes y herramientas."},{"id":"aif-q379","question":"Una empresa de servicios financieros quiere que varios agentes de IA especializados colaboren en solicitudes complejas de clientes. Un agente gestiona consultas de cuentas, otro procesa transacciones y un tercero verifica el cumplimiento. \xbfQu\xe9 concepto de IA ag\xe9ntica representa esta arquitectura?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo de un solo agente","B":"Patr\xf3n de sistema multiagente","C":"Flujo de clasificaci\xf3n supervisada","D":"Flujo de trabajo de inferencia por lotes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un patr\xf3n de sistema multiagente utiliza varios agentes de IA especializados que se comunican y colaboran para gestionar tareas complejas, y cada agente es responsable de una capacidad espec\xedfica, como consultas de cuentas, transacciones o verificaciones de cumplimiento, lo que coincide con la arquitectura descrita.\\nEl aprendizaje por refuerzo de un solo agente entrena a un agente mediante recompensas del entorno sin colaboraci\xf3n entre varios agentes. Un flujo de clasificaci\xf3n supervisada asigna etiquetas a las entradas y no implica colaboraci\xf3n entre agentes. Un flujo de trabajo de inferencia por lotes procesa datos en masa sin conexi\xf3n y no tiene el concepto de varios agentes trabajando juntos."},{"id":"aif-q380","question":"Un agente de IA ayuda a un investigador de atenci\xf3n sanitaria a lo largo de varias sesiones que abarcan varios d\xedas. El agente necesita recordar hallazgos de sesiones anteriores para evitar repetir trabajo. \xbfQu\xe9 capacidad de IA ag\xe9ntica permite esto?","options":{"A":"Uso de herramientas","B":"Gesti\xf3n de memoria","C":"Orquestaci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo","D":"Preentrenamiento de modelos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La gesti\xf3n de memoria en la IA ag\xe9ntica permite que un agente persista y recupere informaci\xf3n entre sesiones, de modo que puede recordar hallazgos anteriores, evitar trabajo redundante y aprovechar interacciones pasadas a lo largo del tiempo.\\nEl uso de herramientas permite que los agentes llamen a API y servicios externos, pero no gestiona el almacenamiento y la recuperaci\xf3n del historial de interacciones pasadas. La orquestaci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo coordina la secuencia de pasos que sigue un agente, pero no aborda la memoria a largo plazo. El preentrenamiento de modelos construye el conocimiento del modelo base durante el entrenamiento inicial, no la capacidad de recordar sesiones individuales de usuarios."},{"id":"aif-q381","question":"Una startup quiere crear agentes de IA personalizados usando un SDK de Python de c\xf3digo abierto que admita uso de herramientas, orquestaci\xf3n multiagente e integraci\xf3n con varios proveedores de modelos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Personalize","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Strands Agents","D":"Amazon Lex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Strands Agents es un SDK de Python de c\xf3digo abierto de AWS que permite a los desarrolladores crear agentes de IA con uso de herramientas, orquestaci\xf3n multiagente y flexibilidad para trabajar con distintos proveedores de modelos, lo que coincide con lo que necesita la startup.\\nAmazon Personalize genera recomendaciones a partir de datos de comportamiento de usuarios. Amazon Comprehend realiza tareas de procesamiento de lenguaje natural, como an\xe1lisis de sentimiento. Amazon Lex crea chatbots conversacionales e interfaces de voz. Ninguno de estos proporciona un SDK de c\xf3digo abierto para crear agentes de IA personalizados con orquestaci\xf3n multiagente."},{"id":"aif-q382","question":"Una empresa quiere infraestructura gestionada para implementar, escalar y monitorear sus agentes de IA en producci\xf3n sin encargarse de la computaci\xf3n y las redes subyacentes. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore","B":"Amazon SageMaker Canvas","C":"Amazon Kendra","D":"Amazon Polly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore proporciona infraestructura gestionada para implementar, escalar y monitorear agentes de IA en producci\xf3n, y se encarga de la computaci\xf3n, las redes y los aspectos operativos para que la empresa pueda centrarse en la l\xf3gica de los agentes en lugar de la gesti\xf3n de infraestructura.\\nAmazon SageMaker Canvas es una interfaz sin c\xf3digo para crear modelos de ML. Amazon Kendra proporciona b\xfasqueda empresarial inteligente. Amazon Polly convierte texto en voz. Ninguno de estos proporciona infraestructura gestionada espec\xedficamente para ejecutar agentes de IA en producci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q383","question":"Una agencia de viajes crea un agente de IA que puede buscar disponibilidad de vuelos, reservar billetes y procesar pagos llamando a las API de la aerol\xednea. \xbfQu\xe9 capacidad de IA ag\xe9ntica permite que el agente interact\xfae con estos sistemas externos?","options":{"A":"Gesti\xf3n de memoria","B":"Uso de herramientas","C":"Ajuste fino de modelos","D":"Cach\xe9 de prompts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El uso de herramientas es la capacidad de IA ag\xe9ntica que permite que un agente llame a API, servicios y sistemas externos para recuperar informaci\xf3n o realizar acciones, que es como el agente de viajes busca vuelos, reserva billetes y procesa pagos a trav\xe9s de las API de la aerol\xednea.\\nLa gesti\xf3n de memoria almacena y recupera informaci\xf3n entre interacciones, pero no llama a sistemas externos. El ajuste fino de modelos actualiza los pesos del modelo durante el entrenamiento en lugar de permitir llamadas a API en tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n. El almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de prompts guarda prompts de uso frecuente para mayor eficiencia, pero no interact\xfaa con sistemas externos."},{"id":"aif-q437","question":"Una empresa usa Amazon Bedrock para un chatbot de atenci\xf3n al cliente y observa que los costos aumentan. El equipo descubre que los historiales completos de conversaci\xf3n se incluyen en cada prompt. \xbfQu\xe9 aspecto del modelo de precios basado en tokens explica esto?","options":{"A":"El costo se basa en el n\xfamero de llamadas a la API independientemente del contenido","B":"El costo aumenta con el n\xfamero total de tokens de entrada y salida procesados por solicitud","C":"El costo se determina por el tiempo que el modelo tarda en procesar cada solicitud","D":"El costo depende del n\xfamero de usuarios concurrentes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El precio basado en tokens cobra por el n\xfamero total de tokens de entrada y salida procesados en cada solicitud, por lo que incluir historiales largos de conversaci\xf3n aumenta el recuento de tokens de entrada en cada llamada y eleva directamente los costos.\\nAmazon Bedrock no cobra una tarifa fija por llamada a la API independientemente del contenido. El precio no se basa en el tiempo de procesamiento ni en el n\xfamero de usuarios concurrentes; se basa en el volumen de tokens."},{"id":"aif-q438","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo afecta reducir el l\xedmite m\xe1ximo de tokens de salida al costo y al rendimiento cuando se usa un modelo fundacional?","options":{"A":"Aumenta el costo pero no tiene efecto en el tiempo de respuesta","B":"Disminuye el costo y puede reducir el tiempo de respuesta","C":"No tiene efecto en el costo ni en el tiempo de respuesta","D":"Aumenta el tiempo de respuesta pero disminuye el costo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Reducir el l\xedmite m\xe1ximo de tokens de salida significa que se generan menos tokens por solicitud, lo que reduce directamente el costo con el precio basado en tokens y tambi\xe9n puede reducir el tiempo de respuesta porque el modelo produce menos salida.\\nMenos tokens de salida significa menos que pagar, por lo que el costo no aumenta. Los tokens de salida afectan directamente tanto la facturaci\xf3n como el tiempo de generaci\xf3n, por lo que s\xed hay un efecto. Generar menos tokens es m\xe1s r\xe1pido, no m\xe1s lento."},{"id":"aif-q439","question":"Una startup sin infraestructura de ML quiere lanzar r\xe1pidamente una funci\xf3n de IA generativa. \xbfQu\xe9 ventaja de usar servicios de GenAI de AWS como Amazon Bedrock es m\xe1s relevante?","options":{"A":"La startup debe entrenar su propio modelo fundacional desde cero","B":"Los servicios de GenAI de AWS reducen la barrera de entrada al proporcionar acceso administrado a modelos fundacionales preentrenados","C":"Los servicios de GenAI de AWS requieren que la startup gestione cl\xfasteres de GPU","D":"Los servicios de GenAI de AWS solo est\xe1n disponibles para clientes empresariales"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los servicios de GenAI de AWS como Amazon Bedrock proporcionan acceso administrado a modelos fundacionales preentrenados mediante API, lo que reduce la barrera de entrada al eliminar la necesidad de entrenar modelos, gestionar infraestructura o adquirir GPU, y permite que la startup lance r\xe1pidamente.\\nLa startup no necesita entrenar su propio modelo porque hay modelos preentrenados disponibles. AWS gestiona la infraestructura, por lo que la startup no gestiona cl\xfasteres de GPU. Estos servicios est\xe1n disponibles para todos los clientes, no solo para empresas."},{"id":"aif-q440","question":"Una empresa de atenci\xf3n m\xe9dica eval\xfaa si construir su aplicaci\xf3n de IA generativa en AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 beneficio de la infraestructura de AWS es m\xe1s importante para esta industria regulada?","options":{"A":"AWS proporciona la menor latencia de inferencia posible a nivel global","B":"La infraestructura de AWS proporciona seguridad integrada, certificaciones de cumplimiento y controles de protecci\xf3n de datos","C":"AWS garantiza que los modelos de IA nunca produzcan resultados incorrectos","D":"AWS elimina la necesidad de cualquier pol\xedtica de gobernanza de datos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La infraestructura de AWS proporciona controles de seguridad, certificaciones de cumplimiento (como la elegibilidad para HIPAA) y funciones de protecci\xf3n de datos que las industrias reguladas como la atenci\xf3n m\xe9dica requieren para implementar aplicaciones de IA de forma responsable y cumplir con las obligaciones legales.\\nNing\xfan proveedor garantiza la latencia m\xe1s baja a nivel global en todos los casos. Ning\xfan sistema de IA puede garantizar que nunca produzca resultados incorrectos. AWS no elimina la necesidad de gobernanza de datos; las organizaciones a\xfan deben implementar sus propias pol\xedticas."},{"id":"aif-q441","question":"Una empresa minorista implement\xf3 un chatbot de IA generativa hace seis meses y quiere medir su valor empresarial. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica indica mejor el impacto del chatbot en los ingresos?","options":{"A":"Cantidad de par\xe1metros del modelo","B":"Ingreso promedio por usuario","C":"N\xfamero de \xe9pocas de entrenamiento","D":"Latencia de inferencia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El ingreso promedio por usuario mide el valor financiero generado por cada cliente, por lo que rastrear si aumenta despu\xe9s de implementar el chatbot indica directamente el impacto del chatbot en los ingresos.\\nLa cantidad de par\xe1metros del modelo describe el tama\xf1o del modelo. El n\xfamero de \xe9pocas de entrenamiento es un detalle de configuraci\xf3n del entrenamiento. La latencia de inferencia mide la velocidad de respuesta. Ninguna de estas refleja el impacto en los ingresos."},{"id":"aif-q442","question":"Una empresa usa un asistente de IA generativa para ayudar a su equipo de soporte y quiere medir las ganancias de eficiencia operativa. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica deber\xeda seguir?","options":{"A":"Puntuaci\xf3n ROUGE","B":"Eficiencia medida como reducci\xf3n del tiempo medio de atenci\xf3n por caso","C":"BERTScore","D":"Perplejidad del modelo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La reducci\xf3n del tiempo medio de atenci\xf3n por caso mide directamente cu\xe1nto m\xe1s r\xe1pido resuelven los agentes los problemas con la ayuda del asistente de IA, lo que constituye una ganancia de eficiencia operativa y una m\xe9trica de valor empresarial para aplicaciones de GenAI.\\nROUGE y BERTScore miden la calidad del texto generado frente a referencias. La perplejidad del modelo mide el ajuste del modelado de lenguaje. Ninguna de estas captura la eficiencia operativa."},{"id":"aif-q443","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 etapa del ciclo de vida del modelo fundacional implica seleccionar las fuentes de datos correctas y prepararlas antes de que comience cualquier entrenamiento del modelo?","options":{"A":"Despliegue","B":"Evaluaci\xf3n","C":"Selecci\xf3n de datos","D":"Retroalimentaci\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La selecci\xf3n de datos es la primera etapa del ciclo de vida del modelo fundacional, donde se identifican y curan las fuentes de datos adecuadas antes de que comience el preentrenamiento. La calidad y amplitud de los datos seleccionados determinan directamente lo que aprende el modelo.\\nEl despliegue es una etapa posterior en la que el modelo entrenado se pone a disposici\xf3n para inferencia. La evaluaci\xf3n eval\xfaa el rendimiento del modelo despu\xe9s del entrenamiento. La retroalimentaci\xf3n es la etapa final en la que las interacciones de los usuarios informan mejoras."},{"id":"aif-q450","question":"Una empresa invirti\xf3 en una soluci\xf3n de IA generativa para automatizar la redacci\xf3n de informes. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica mide mejor si esta inversi\xf3n aport\xf3 valor financiero?","options":{"A":"Retorno de la inversi\xf3n (ROI)","B":"Exactitud del modelo","C":"N\xfamero de par\xe1metros del modelo","D":"Volumen de datos de entrenamiento"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El retorno de la inversi\xf3n (ROI) mide si los beneficios financieros de la soluci\xf3n de IA, como el ahorro de mano de obra y la salida m\xe1s r\xe1pida, superan los costos de crearla y operarla, lo que muestra directamente si la inversi\xf3n aport\xf3 valor financiero.\\nLa exactitud del modelo mide la calidad de las predicciones pero no el retorno financiero. El n\xfamero de par\xe1metros y el volumen de datos de entrenamiento son detalles t\xe9cnicos no relacionados con el valor empresarial."},{"id":"aif-q005","question":"Una empresa de viajes usa un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) preentrenado para impulsar un asistente de reservas. La empresa necesita que las respuestas del asistente sean breves y est\xe9n escritas en un idioma en particular. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque alinear\xe1 mejor la salida del LLM con estas expectativas?","options":{"A":"Elegir un LLM de un tama\xf1o diferente","B":"Aumentar el valor de temperature","C":"Refinar el prompt","D":"Aumentar el valor de Top K"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Refinar el prompt permite a la empresa indicar directamente la longitud de respuesta requerida y el idioma objetivo, de modo que el modelo devuelva respuestas breves en el idioma elegido.\\nElegir un tama\xf1o de modelo diferente cambia la capacidad y el costo del modelo en lugar de imponer la longitud o el idioma. Aumentar la temperature agrega aleatoriedad, lo que hace que la salida est\xe9 menos controlada. Aumentar el valor de Top K ampl\xeda el conjunto de tokens candidatos, lo que tampoco controla la longitud de la respuesta ni el idioma."},{"id":"aif-q008","question":"Un fabricante necesita un conjunto de datos de im\xe1genes etiquetadas de alta precisi\xf3n para un modelo de detecci\xf3n de defectos y quiere mantener al m\xednimo los ejemplos mal etiquetados. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque cumple mejor estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Anotaci\xf3n automatizada con Amazon Rekognition Custom Labels","B":"Validaci\xf3n con intervenci\xf3n humana mediante Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth Plus","C":"Generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes sint\xe9ticas con un modelo fundacional de Amazon Bedrock","D":"Etiquetado automatizado con aprendizaje activo mediante Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La validaci\xf3n con intervenci\xf3n humana mediante Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth Plus env\xeda las etiquetas a revisores expertos que las verifican y corrigen, lo que produce las anotaciones de mayor precisi\xf3n con la menor cantidad de errores.\\nAmazon Rekognition Custom Labels automatiza el etiquetado, pero, sin revisi\xf3n humana, puede propagar errores; generar im\xe1genes sint\xe9ticas con un modelo de Amazon Bedrock crea datos, pero no hace nada para validar las etiquetas; el etiquetado automatizado con aprendizaje activo de SageMaker Ground Truth reduce el esfuerzo, pero aumenta la probabilidad de anotaciones incorrectas, lo contrario del objetivo."},{"id":"aif-q019","question":"Una empresa usa un modelo fundacional (FM) de Amazon Bedrock para una herramienta de b\xfasqueda. La empresa quiere ajustar el FM con sus propios datos para hacer la herramienta m\xe1s precisa. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque ajustar\xe1 correctamente el modelo?","options":{"A":"Comprar Provisioned Throughput para Amazon Bedrock","B":"Proporcionar ejemplos etiquetados que contengan cada uno un campo prompt y un campo completion","C":"Preparar el conjunto de datos como un archivo .txt que contenga varias l\xedneas de contenido .csv","D":"Entrenar el modelo con revistas y libros de texto"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El ajuste fino de un modelo en Amazon Bedrock requiere un conjunto de datos etiquetado en el que cada registro empareje un prompt de entrada con el completion esperado, para que el modelo aprenda de los datos de la empresa el comportamiento deseado de entrada a salida.\\nProvisioned Throughput es una opci\xf3n de capacidad y precios para la inferencia, no un m\xe9todo de ajuste fino. Un archivo .txt que contiene l\xedneas .csv no es el formato requerido para el ajuste fino. Entrenar con revistas y libros de texto es contenido no estructurado que carece de los pares de prompt y completion que necesita el ajuste fino."},{"id":"aif-q021","question":"Un equipo de ingenier\xeda quiere usar Amazon OpenSearch Service como base de una aplicaci\xf3n de b\xfasqueda vectorial. \xbfQu\xe9 capacidad hace esto posible?","options":{"A":"B\xfasqueda y clasificaci\xf3n de texto completo por palabras clave (BM25)","B":"Indexaci\xf3n y consultas geoespaciales","C":"B\xfasqueda de vecinos m\xe1s cercanos k-NN sobre vectores de alta dimensi\xf3n","D":"Replicaci\xf3n entre cl\xfasteres para recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La b\xfasqueda de vecinos m\xe1s cercanos k-NN permite a Amazon OpenSearch Service indexar embeddings y devolver los vectores m\xe1s similares a una consulta, lo cual es el n\xfacleo de una aplicaci\xf3n de base de datos vectorial como la b\xfasqueda sem\xe1ntica.\\nLa b\xfasqueda de texto completo BM25 coincide con palabras clave en lugar de vectores sem\xe1nticos; la indexaci\xf3n geoespacial maneja datos de ubicaci\xf3n; la replicaci\xf3n entre cl\xfasteres admite disponibilidad y recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres. Ninguna proporciona b\xfasqueda por similitud vectorial."},{"id":"aif-q024","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando un chatbot de soporte de autoservicio sobre un modelo fundacional (FM). Las respuestas del chatbot deben coincidir con el tono de marca de la empresa. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Iterar y refinar el prompt hasta que el FM produzca respuestas en el estilo deseado","B":"Establecer un l\xedmite bajo en la cantidad de tokens que el FM puede producir","C":"Usar inferencia por lotes para generar respuestas detalladas","D":"Establecer un valor de temperature m\xe1s alto"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Refinar el prompt de forma iterativa orienta el modelo fundacional hacia respuestas que coinciden con el tono de marca de la empresa, porque las instrucciones y los ejemplos del prompt dan forma al estilo de la salida.\\nLimitar los tokens de salida solo acorta las respuestas. La inferencia por lotes cambia c\xf3mo se procesan las solicitudes, no su tono. Aumentar la temperature incrementa la aleatoriedad, lo que hace que el tono sea menos consistente en lugar de estar m\xe1s alineado."},{"id":"aif-q025","question":"Una empresa quiere usar un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) en Amazon Bedrock para etiquetar pasajes de texto como positivos o negativos. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de ingenier\xeda de prompts cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Agregar al prompt una explicaci\xf3n detallada del an\xe1lisis de sentimiento y de c\xf3mo funcionan los LLM","B":"Proporcionar solo el nuevo pasaje a clasificar, sin ejemplos ni contexto adicional","C":"Incluir en el prompt algunos pasajes de ejemplo, cada uno etiquetado como positivo o negativo","D":"Incluir el nuevo pasaje junto con algunos ejemplos de tareas no relacionadas, como resumen o traducci\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Incluir algunos pasajes de ejemplo, cada uno etiquetado como positivo o negativo, es few-shot prompting, lo que muestra al modelo el patr\xf3n de entrada a etiqueta y mejora la precisi\xf3n de la clasificaci\xf3n en un nuevo pasaje.\\nAgregar una explicaci\xf3n de c\xf3mo funcionan el an\xe1lisis de sentimiento o los LLM proporciona teor\xeda, pero no ejemplos de los que aprender. Proporcionar solo el pasaje sin ejemplos es zero-shot y da al modelo menos orientaci\xf3n. Los ejemplos de tareas no relacionadas, como resumen o traducci\xf3n, alejan al modelo de la clasificaci\xf3n de sentimiento."},{"id":"aif-q029","question":"Una startup est\xe1 lanzando un agente virtual impulsado por un LLM y quiere evitar que los usuarios lo induzcan a realizar acciones inseguras o a filtrar sus instrucciones ocultas. \xbfQu\xe9 acci\xf3n reduce m\xe1s directamente este riesgo?","options":{"A":"Aumentar la temperature del modelo para que las respuestas var\xeden m\xe1s","B":"Aumentar el valor de Top P para ampliar el muestreo de tokens","C":"Agregar instrucciones de sistema en una plantilla de prompt que ense\xf1en al modelo a reconocer y rechazar intentos de manipulaci\xf3n","D":"Reducir la longitud m\xe1xima de salida en cada solicitud"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Una plantilla de prompt con instrucciones de sistema que ense\xf1en al modelo a detectar y rechazar patrones de manipulaci\xf3n es una salvaguarda de ingenier\xeda de prompts que reduce directamente el riesgo de prompt injection y de filtraci\xf3n de instrucciones.\\nAumentar la temperature o Top P solo cambia cu\xe1n aleatorio es el muestreo, y acortar la longitud m\xe1xima de salida solo trunca las respuestas; ninguna de estas opciones contrarresta la manipulaci\xf3n de prompts."},{"id":"aif-q038","question":"Una empresa de log\xedstica ejecuta un modelo Amazon Titan en Amazon Bedrock y quiere que las respuestas del modelo se basen en sus documentos internos de env\xedo. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n agrega este contexto privado?","options":{"A":"Ajustar finamente el FM con los documentos internos","B":"Crear una base de conocimiento de Amazon Bedrock sobre los documentos internos","C":"Reducir el valor de temperature","D":"Cambiar a un modelo fundacional m\xe1s grande"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Una base de conocimiento de Amazon Bedrock indexa los documentos internos y recupera los pasajes relevantes en el momento de la consulta (RAG), de modo que el modelo Titan fundamenta sus respuestas en los datos privados de la empresa sin ning\xfan entrenamiento.\\nEl ajuste fino puede incorporar conocimiento, pero es m\xe1s costoso y est\xe1tico, por lo que no es la forma espec\xedfica de complementar las respuestas con documentos de uso frecuente; reducir la temperature solo disminuye la aleatoriedad; cambiar a un modelo m\xe1s grande agrega capacidad, pero sigue sin darle acceso a los documentos privados."},{"id":"aif-q046","question":"Un fabricante de electrodom\xe9sticos quiere una interfaz de chat sobre sus manuales de productos en PDF usando modelos de lenguaje grandes (LLM) en Amazon Bedrock, al menor costo. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque se ajusta mejor?","options":{"A":"Agregar todos los manuales a cada prompt como contexto","B":"Cargar los manuales en una base de conocimiento de Amazon Bedrock y recuperar solo los pasajes relevantes por consulta","C":"Realizar un ajuste fino (fine-tuning) de un modelo con los manuales y servir el modelo ajustado","D":"Preentrenar continuamente el modelo base con el contenido de los manuales"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Una base de conocimiento recupera solo los pasajes relevantes para cada pregunta, por lo que el modelo obtiene el contexto correcto de forma econ\xf3mica, sin pagar por enviar manuales completos cada vez ni por entrenar el modelo.\\nPegar todos los manuales en cada prompt infla los costos de tokens; el ajuste fino agrega costo de entrenamiento y debe rehacerse cuando cambian los manuales; el preentrenamiento continuo sobre el corpus es la opci\xf3n m\xe1s costosa e innecesaria para una interfaz de preguntas y respuestas."},{"id":"aif-q064","question":"El chatbot de una empresa biotecnol\xf3gica responde preguntas a partir de densos art\xedculos de investigaci\xf3n cl\xednica, pero incluso despu\xe9s de varios ajustes al prompt el modelo maneja mal la terminolog\xeda especializada. \xbfQu\xe9 lo mejorar\xe1 m\xe1s?","options":{"A":"Usar few-shot prompting con pares de ejemplo de preguntas y respuestas","B":"Aplicar fine-tuning de adaptaci\xf3n al dominio sobre el corpus especializado","C":"Agregar un paso de Generaci\xf3n Aumentada por Recuperaci\xf3n (RAG) sobre los art\xedculos","D":"Bajar la temperatura para que las respuestas sean m\xe1s enfocadas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El fine-tuning de adaptaci\xf3n al dominio entrena el modelo con el corpus especializado para que realmente aprenda la terminolog\xeda cl\xednica, que es lo que corrige la mala comprensi\xf3n del lenguaje denso del dominio.\\nEl few-shot prompting y una temperatura m\xe1s baja moldean las respuestas pero no ense\xf1an vocabulario nuevo; un paso de RAG recupera pasajes relevantes, pero el modelo sigue teniendo dificultades para interpretar los t\xe9rminos especializados que no ha aprendido."},{"id":"aif-q069","question":"Una empresa de aprendizaje en l\xednea ejecuta un asistente de preguntas y respuestas y quiere que el estilo de las respuestas coincida con el rango de edad de cada estudiante, que la aplicaci\xf3n ya pasa al modelo. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque requiere el MENOR esfuerzo?","options":{"A":"Entrenar un modelo ajustado por separado para cada rango de edad","B":"Construir un pipeline de Generaci\xf3n Aumentada por Recuperaci\xf3n (RAG) de contenido espec\xedfico por edad","C":"Posprocesar cada respuesta con un segundo modelo para ajustar el tono","D":"Agregar al prompt una instrucci\xf3n de rol y audiencia que indique el rango de edad del usuario"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Agregar al prompt una instrucci\xf3n de rol y audiencia es un cambio simple de ingenier\xeda de prompts que orienta el estilo hacia el rango de edad indicado con casi ning\xfan trabajo adicional.\\nRealizar fine-tuning de un modelo por rango de edad, construir un pipeline de RAG de contenido espec\xedfico por edad y ejecutar un segundo modelo para reescribir el tono agregan esfuerzo significativo de datos, infraestructura o procesamiento."},{"id":"aif-q076","question":"Un equipo env\xeda a diario un prompt few-shot con 10 ejemplos a un modelo base en Amazon Bedrock. El modelo funciona bien, pero el equipo quiere reducir el costo mensual. \xbfQu\xe9 cambio ayuda?","options":{"A":"Comprar Provisioned Throughput para el modelo","B":"Reducir la cantidad de ejemplos y tokens en el prompt","C":"Realizar fine-tuning del modelo para que los ejemplos ya no sean necesarios en el prompt","D":"Mover la carga de trabajo a un modelo m\xe1s grande y capaz"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Como el costo escala con los tokens procesados, reducir la cantidad de ejemplos y tokens en el prompt baja la factura mientras el modelo sigue funcionando bien.\\nProvisioned Throughput est\xe1 pensado para vol\xfamenes altos y constantes y costar\xeda m\xe1s para un trabajo una vez al d\xeda; el fine-tuning elimina los ejemplos del prompt pero agrega costo de entrenamiento; pasar a un modelo m\xe1s grande aumenta el costo por llamada."},{"id":"aif-q079","question":"Una empresa de viajes localiza sus art\xedculos de ayuda a varios idiomas con un LLM y punt\xfaa cada traducci\xf3n autom\xe1tica contra una referencia humana. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica est\xe1 dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para la traducci\xf3n autom\xe1tica?","options":{"A":"BERTScore","B":"Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation (ROUGE)","C":"Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU)","D":"Tasa de error de palabras"},"correct":"C","explanation":"BLEU est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para la traducci\xf3n autom\xe1tica y mide la superposici\xf3n de n-gramas entre la traducci\xf3n candidata y una o m\xe1s referencias humanas, que es exactamente la comparaci\xf3n descrita.\\nROUGE se orienta al recall en resumen; BERTScore mide similitud sem\xe1ntica general en lugar de servir como la m\xe9trica est\xe1ndar de traducci\xf3n; la tasa de error de palabras eval\xfaa la transcripci\xf3n de voz."},{"id":"aif-q081","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es un beneficio del preentrenamiento continuo de un modelo fundacional (FM)?","options":{"A":"Adapta el modelo a un conjunto de datos de tarea etiquetado espec\xedfico","B":"Sigue mejorando el conocimiento del modelo al entrenarlo m\xe1s con datos nuevos a lo largo del tiempo","C":"Comprime el modelo para reducir su tama\xf1o y acelerar la inferencia","D":"Elimina la necesidad de un paso de evaluaci\xf3n"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El preentrenamiento continuo mantiene expuesto al FM a datos nuevos, en su mayor\xeda no etiquetados, de modo que su conocimiento se mantiene actualizado y su rendimiento mejora con el tiempo.\\nAdaptar el modelo a un conjunto de datos de tarea etiquetado describe el fine-tuning; reducir y acelerar el modelo describe la destilaci\xf3n o cuantizaci\xf3n; y ning\xfan m\xe9todo de entrenamiento elimina la necesidad de evaluar el modelo."},{"id":"aif-q086","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 ataque basado en prompts tiene como objetivo espec\xedfico revelar las instrucciones de sistema ocultas de un modelo?","options":{"A":"Jailbreaking","B":"Inyecci\xf3n de prompts","C":"Extracci\xf3n de la plantilla del prompt","D":"Cambio de persona inducido por prompt"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La extracci\xf3n (filtraci\xf3n) de la plantilla del prompt induce al modelo a divulgar su prompt de sistema subyacente, exponiendo directamente las instrucciones ocultas que configuran su comportamiento.\\nEl jailbreaking omite los controles de seguridad, la inyecci\xf3n de prompts inserta instrucciones adversarias para secuestrar el comportamiento y el cambio de persona empuja al modelo a un rol diferente; ninguno de estos tiene como objetivo volcar las instrucciones de sistema en s\xed."},{"id":"aif-q088","question":"Una plataforma social quiere comparar la toxicidad de salida de varios LLM candidatos de Amazon SageMaker JumpStart con el MENOR esfuerzo operativo. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque de evaluaci\xf3n se ajusta?","options":{"A":"Evaluaci\xf3n de modelos con personal humano de revisi\xf3n","B":"Evaluaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de modelos usando m\xe9tricas de toxicidad integradas","C":"Calificaci\xf3n humana mediante crowdsourcing","D":"Pruebas A/B de los modelos con usuarios reales"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La evaluaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de modelos punt\xfaa las salidas con m\xe9tricas de toxicidad integradas y no necesita personas a quienes reclutar o coordinar, lo que ofrece el menor esfuerzo operativo.\\nLa evaluaci\xf3n con personal humano y la calificaci\xf3n mediante crowdsourcing requieren gestionar revisores, y las pruebas A/B con usuarios reales agregan riesgo de exposici\xf3n y sobrecarga de coordinaci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q095","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 enfoque de prompting se utiliza para reforzar un modelo contra ataques de inyecci\xf3n de prompts?","options":{"A":"Prompting de cadena de pensamiento","B":"Prompting few-shot","C":"Prompting adversarial","D":"Prompting zero-shot"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El prompting adversarial dise\xf1a y prueba deliberadamente entradas hostiles para que los intentos de inyecci\xf3n se puedan detectar y neutralizar, reforzando el modelo contra ellos.\\nEl prompting de cadena de pensamiento, few-shot y zero-shot moldean c\xf3mo razona el modelo o aprende de ejemplos, pero no ofrecen defensa contra prompts maliciosos."},{"id":"aif-q096","question":"Un equipo de soporte de TI realiz\xf3 un ajuste fino de un LLM para responder preguntas de help desk y quiere confirmar que el ajuste fino mejor\xf3 su exactitud. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"Precisi\xf3n","B":"Puntuaci\xf3n F1","C":"Tiempo hasta el primer token","D":"Perplejidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La puntuaci\xf3n F1 equilibra precisi\xf3n y recall en un solo n\xfamero, por lo que refleja con qu\xe9 exactitud y completitud responde el modelo, lo que la convierte en la forma correcta de medir la mejora del ajuste fino.\\nLa precisi\xf3n por s\xed sola ignora las respuestas correctas omitidas; el tiempo hasta el primer token mide la latencia; la perplejidad mide el ajuste del modelado de lenguaje, no la exactitud en la tarea."},{"id":"aif-q097","question":"Una empresa usa Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) con Amazon Bedrock y Stable Diffusion para crear im\xe1genes de productos, pero los resultados son aleatorios y no incluyen los detalles solicitados. El equipo quiere que las im\xe1genes sigan m\xe1s fielmente los prompts. \xbfQu\xe9 cambio ayuda?","options":{"A":"Aumentar el n\xfamero de pasos de generaci\xf3n","B":"Aumentar la escala de gu\xeda sin clasificador (CFG)","C":"Establecer una semilla aleatoria fija","D":"Aumentar el peso del prompt negativo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La escala CFG controla con qu\xe9 fuerza sigue el modelo el prompt de texto, por lo que aumentarla hace que la imagen generada se adhiera m\xe1s estrechamente a los detalles solicitados.\\nM\xe1s pasos de generaci\xf3n principalmente refinan la calidad de la eliminaci\xf3n de ruido; una semilla fija solo hace que los resultados sean reproducibles; aumentar el peso del prompt negativo refuerza lo que se debe evitar, no cu\xe1n estrechamente coincide la imagen con el prompt principal."},{"id":"aif-q098","question":"Una empresa de telecomunicaciones quiere un chatbot con LLM que d\xe9 a los agentes de soporte respuestas en tiempo real fundamentadas en documentos de pol\xedticas internas, al menor costo. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque encaja mejor?","options":{"A":"Realizar un ajuste fino del LLM con los documentos de pol\xedticas","B":"Implementar Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) sobre los documentos de pol\xedticas","C":"Preentrenar un modelo nuevo desde cero con el corpus de pol\xedticas","D":"Pegar todos los documentos de pol\xedticas en cada prompt como contexto"},"correct":"B","explanation":"RAG recupera los pasajes relevantes de las pol\xedticas en el momento de la consulta y fundamenta las respuestas del chatbot en ellos, lo que proporciona respuestas precisas a bajo costo sin entrenamiento.\\nEl ajuste fino a\xf1ade costo de entrenamiento y debe repetirse a medida que cambian las pol\xedticas; el preentrenamiento desde cero es, con diferencia, la opci\xf3n m\xe1s costosa; pegar todos los documentos en cada prompt aumenta los costos de tokens."},{"id":"aif-q102","question":"Una empresa cre\xf3 una soluci\xf3n de resumen de texto en Amazon Bedrock y usar\xe1 Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica deber\xeda usar para medir la calidad del resumen?","options":{"A":"\xc1rea bajo la curva ROC (AUC)","B":"BERTScore","C":"Error cuadr\xe1tico medio (MSE)","D":"Perplejidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"BERTScore compara el resumen generado con el texto de referencia mediante embeddings contextuales, capturando la similitud sem\xe1ntica, lo que lo hace adecuado para medir la calidad del resumen.\\nAUC mide el ranking de clasificaci\xf3n binaria, MSE mide el error de regresi\xf3n y la perplejidad mide el ajuste del modelado de lenguaje; ninguno compara un resumen con referencias para evaluar calidad."},{"id":"aif-q106","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n de aprendizaje de idiomas llama a un LLM para reescribir texto con mayor claridad y tiene ejemplos de referencia de las versiones mejoradas. Quiere que la salida del modelo se parezca a esos ejemplos. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"Perplejidad del texto generado","B":"Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation (ROUGE)","C":"Puntuaci\xf3n de robustez sem\xe1ntica","D":"Latencia promedio de respuesta"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ROUGE mide la superposici\xf3n de n-gramas y secuencias entre la salida del modelo y los ejemplos de referencia, por lo que captura directamente cu\xe1n estrechamente se parece la salida a esas versiones mejoradas.\\nLa perplejidad refleja el ajuste del modelado de lenguaje, la robustez sem\xe1ntica mide la estabilidad ante cambios en la entrada y la latencia mide la velocidad; ninguno compara la salida con ejemplos de referencia."},{"id":"aif-q107","question":"El modelo de generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes de una empresa sigue produciendo im\xe1genes no relacionadas con los prompts, y el equipo quiere ajustar su prompting para reducirlas. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica ayuda?","options":{"A":"A\xf1adir im\xe1genes de ejemplo few-shot","B":"Usar prompts negativos que indiquen qu\xe9 excluir","C":"Usar prompts zero-shot","D":"Hacer los prompts m\xe1s abiertos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los prompts negativos indican expl\xedcitamente al modelo qu\xe9 dejar fuera, restringiendo la generaci\xf3n para que aparezcan menos elementos fuera de tema.\\nA\xf1adir im\xe1genes de ejemplo o usar prompts zero-shot no da ninguna instrucci\xf3n sobre qu\xe9 evitar, y hacer los prompts m\xe1s abiertos aumenta la deriva en lugar de reducirla."},{"id":"aif-q108","question":"Un minorista de electr\xf3nica quiere que un LLM escriba descripciones de productos breves y centradas en caracter\xedsticas. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque de ingenier\xeda de prompts funciona mejor?","options":{"A":"Escribir un prompt gen\xe9rico para todos los productos y editar manualmente cada salida","B":"Crear prompts espec\xedficos por categor\xeda que destaquen caracter\xedsticas clave y especifiquen el formato y la longitud de salida","C":"Enumerar todos los atributos del producto en un solo prompt y pedir una descripci\xf3n","D":"Pedir al modelo la descripci\xf3n m\xe1s creativa que pueda producir y luego recortarla"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los prompts espec\xedficos por categor\xeda que se\xf1alan las caracter\xedsticas clave y establecen el formato y la longitud gu\xedan al modelo hacia descripciones coherentes, concisas y centradas en caracter\xedsticas con poco retrabajo.\\nUn \xfanico prompt gen\xe9rico requiere mucha edici\xf3n manual; enumerar todos los atributos produce texto largo y poco enfocado; pedir la m\xe1xima creatividad y recortar desperdicia esfuerzo y se desv\xeda del estilo requerido."},{"id":"aif-q119","question":"Una compa\xf1\xeda de seguros quiere una aplicaci\xf3n de IA que permita a los empleados consultar siniestros abiertos, recuperar los detalles de un siniestro espec\xedfico y obtener los documentos del siniestro. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n se ajusta mejor?","options":{"A":"Usar Agents for Amazon Bedrock con Amazon Fraud Detector","B":"Usar Agents for Amazon Bedrock con Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases","C":"Usar una base de conocimiento de Amazon Bedrock por s\xed sola, sin un agente","D":"Entrenar un nuevo modelo de ML con Amazon SageMaker AI"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Agents for Amazon Bedrock realiza las acciones de varios pasos (consultar siniestros, extraer detalles y obtener documentos) y llama a una base de conocimiento de Amazon Bedrock para recuperar los datos del siniestro, lo que en conjunto satisface las tres necesidades.\\nCombinar el agente con Amazon Fraud Detector agrega puntuaci\xf3n de fraude en lugar de recuperaci\xf3n de documentos; una base de conocimiento por s\xed sola puede recuperar contenido, pero no puede orquestar las consultas y acciones; entrenar un nuevo modelo con Amazon SageMaker AI requiere mucho m\xe1s esfuerzo del necesario."},{"id":"aif-q128","question":"Un analista agrega una instrucci\xf3n a un prompt en Amazon Bedrock pidiendo al modelo que muestre su trabajo y explique cada paso mientras resuelve problemas matem\xe1ticos. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica de ingenier\xeda de prompts se est\xe1 utilizando?","options":{"A":"Few-shot prompting","B":"Tree of thoughts prompting","C":"Chain-of-thought prompting","D":"Zero-shot prompting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chain-of-thought prompting pide al modelo que exponga su razonamiento intermedio paso a paso, que es exactamente lo que hace la instrucci\xf3n de mostrar su trabajo, mejorando la precisi\xf3n en problemas matem\xe1ticos.\\nFew-shot prompting proporciona ejemplos resueltos; tree of thoughts explora varias ramas de razonamiento en paralelo; zero-shot prompting proporciona solo una instrucci\xf3n sin andamiaje de razonamiento."},{"id":"aif-q131","question":"Una empresa necesita respuestas de mayor calidad de un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) en problemas complejos que requieren un razonamiento detallado y paso a paso. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica de ingenier\xeda de prompts se ajusta?","options":{"A":"Directional stimulus prompting","B":"Prompt chaining","C":"Least-to-most prompting","D":"Chain-of-thought prompting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chain-of-thought prompting gu\xeda al modelo para que produzca su razonamiento como una serie de pasos l\xf3gicos dentro de la respuesta, lo que mejora la calidad de las respuestas en problemas complejos.\\nDirectional stimulus prompting orienta la salida con pistas o palabras clave; prompt chaining env\xeda varios prompts separados en secuencia; least-to-most prompting descompone una tarea en subproblemas ordenados, pero es una t\xe9cnica distinta de generar una cadena de razonamiento en l\xednea."},{"id":"aif-q143","question":"Un minorista opera un asistente de IA que ayuda a los agentes a responder preguntas frecuentes, pero las preguntas cambian continuamente. Los agentes escriben una pregunta y necesitan una respuesta generada actualizada al menor costo. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque se ajusta?","options":{"A":"Usar Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) con ingenier\xeda de prompts","B":"Ajustar el modelo mediante fine-tuning de forma recurrente","C":"Preentrenar el modelo con los datos de preguntas frecuentes y evaluarlo con benchmarks","D":"Insertar el conjunto completo de preguntas frecuentes en el prompt del sistema"},"correct":"A","explanation":"RAG recupera la informaci\xf3n m\xe1s relevante y actual en el momento de la consulta y la ingenier\xeda de prompts da forma a la respuesta, por lo que el asistente se mantiene preciso a medida que cambian las preguntas sin costos recurrentes de entrenamiento.\\nEl fine-tuning programado y el preentrenamiento requieren ambos ejecuciones de entrenamiento recurrentes y costosas; insertar el conjunto completo de preguntas frecuentes en cada prompt aumenta los costos de tokens y no escala a medida que crece el conjunto de preguntas frecuentes."},{"id":"aif-q146","question":"Una empresa quiere respuestas m\xe1s precisas de una aplicaci\xf3n de IA generativa en Amazon Bedrock y desea la forma m\xe1s econ\xf3mica de obtenerlas. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque se ajusta?","options":{"A":"Hacer fine-tuning del FM","B":"Usar ingenier\xeda de prompts","C":"Reentrenar el FM desde cero","D":"Aplicar destilaci\xf3n de modelos al FM"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La ingenier\xeda de prompts mejora la precisi\xf3n al refinar las instrucciones y el contexto sin costo de entrenamiento, lo que la convierte en la opci\xf3n m\xe1s econ\xf3mica.\\nEl fine-tuning agrega costo de entrenamiento y preparaci\xf3n de datos, la destilaci\xf3n de modelos requiere un proceso de entrenamiento separado para construir un modelo m\xe1s peque\xf1o, y reentrenar desde cero es el enfoque m\xe1s costoso de todos."},{"id":"aif-q155","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n resume libros de distintas longitudes, pero falla en algunos de los m\xe1s largos. \xbfCu\xe1l es la causa m\xe1s probable?","options":{"A":"La temperatura est\xe1 configurada demasiado alta","B":"La entrada supera la ventana de contexto m\xe1xima del modelo","C":"La longitud m\xe1xima de salida est\xe1 configurada demasiado baja","D":"El modelo no admite few-shot prompting"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un modelo solo puede procesar un n\xfamero limitado de tokens de entrada, por lo que un libro cuya longitud supera la ventana de contexto m\xe1xima no se puede manejar en una sola pasada, lo que explica por qu\xe9 solo fallan los libros m\xe1s largos.\\nUna temperatura alta cambia la aleatoriedad, no la capacidad; una longitud m\xe1xima de salida baja acortar\xeda todos los res\xfamenes en lugar de fallar en determinados libros; y la compatibilidad con few-shot es irrelevante para resumir una entrada larga."},{"id":"aif-q156","question":"Una aerol\xednea quiere un chatbot de texto para horarios de vuelos, reservas y pagos, construido sobre modelos de lenguaje grandes (LLM) m\xe1s una base de conocimiento, con el MENOR esfuerzo de desarrollo. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n se ajusta?","options":{"A":"Entrenar modelos con Amazon SageMaker Autopilot","B":"Hacer fine-tuning de modelos en Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","C":"Construir un agente de Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) con Amazon Bedrock","D":"Construir un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n personalizado con AWS Lambda y un modelo autoalojado"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock permite al equipo combinar un LLM con una base de conocimiento usando RAG para ofrecer respuestas precisas y actuales con un esfuerzo m\xednimo de desarrollo.\\nSageMaker Autopilot entrena modelos de ML tradicionales a partir de datos tabulares; el fine-tuning en SageMaker JumpStart requiere preparaci\xf3n de datos y una ejecuci\xf3n de entrenamiento; construir una capa de orquestaci\xf3n personalizada con Lambda sobre un modelo autoalojado es lo que m\xe1s trabajo requiere de todas las opciones."},{"id":"aif-q161","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n describe un riesgo o limitaci\xf3n genuino de la ingenier\xeda de prompts para un modelo de IA generativa?","options":{"A":"La ingenier\xeda de prompts garantiza salidas deterministas, por lo que la validaci\xf3n es innecesaria","B":"La ingenier\xeda de prompts puede exponer el modelo a ataques de inyecci\xf3n de prompts","C":"La ingenier\xeda de prompts actualiza permanentemente los pesos del modelo con cada prompt","D":"La ingenier\xeda de prompts elimina todo riesgo de envenenamiento de datos y secuestro de modelos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Una limitaci\xf3n real de la ingenier\xeda de prompts es que las entradas manipuladas a\xfan se pueden explotar mediante inyecci\xf3n de prompts, donde un atacante manipula el prompt para cambiar el comportamiento del modelo.\\nLa ingenier\xeda de prompts no hace que las salidas sean deterministas ni elimina la necesidad de validaci\xf3n, no actualiza los pesos del modelo (eso es fine-tuning) y no puede eliminar riesgos como el envenenamiento de datos o el secuestro de modelos."},{"id":"aif-q162","question":"La aplicaci\xf3n de Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) de un editor debe percibirse como de tiempo casi real, mientras se publican nuevos art\xedculos a diario. \xbfQu\xe9 dos pasos del pipeline pueden ejecutarse como trabajos por lotes fuera de l\xednea?","options":{"A":"Generar embeddings para cada consulta de usuario entrante","B":"Generar embeddings para el contenido publicado","C":"Clasificar y devolver resultados para la consulta de un usuario","D":"Construir o actualizar el \xedndice de b\xfasqueda vectorial","E":"Detectar el idioma de cada consulta de usuario"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Generar los embeddings del contenido y construir o actualizar el \xedndice de b\xfasqueda dependen \xfanicamente de los documentos, por lo que pueden ejecutarse en lotes peri\xf3dicos a medida que llegan nuevos art\xedculos, fuera de la ruta de tiempo real.\\nGenerar embeddings para la consulta de un usuario, clasificar y devolver resultados, y detectar el idioma de una consulta ocurren por solicitud y deben ejecutarse en tiempo real."},{"id":"aif-q163","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica descompone una tarea compleja en subtareas m\xe1s peque\xf1as que se env\xedan a un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) en secuencia, cada una construida sobre la anterior?","options":{"A":"Tree of thoughts","B":"Chain-of-thought prompting","C":"Prompt chaining","D":"Few-shot prompting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prompt chaining divide una tarea compleja en subtareas m\xe1s peque\xf1as y las alimenta al LLM una tras otra, y cada paso utiliza la salida del anterior.\\nChain-of-thought razona paso a paso dentro de una \xfanica respuesta; tree of thoughts explora m\xfaltiples ramas de razonamiento; few-shot prompting proporciona ejemplos en lugar de secuenciar subtareas."},{"id":"aif-q164","question":"Un practicante de IA debe mejorar la exactitud de un modelo de generaci\xf3n de texto que depende de datos de inventario que cambian r\xe1pidamente. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica ayuda m\xe1s?","options":{"A":"Ajustar el modelo con fine-tuning sobre datos hist\xf3ricos de inventario","B":"Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)","C":"Transfer learning","D":"Preentrenamiento continuo sobre instant\xe1neas de inventario"},"correct":"B","explanation":"RAG obtiene los datos de inventario actuales en el momento de la inferencia y condiciona la generaci\xf3n a ellos, de modo que la salida refleja la informaci\xf3n m\xe1s reciente y se mantiene exacta sin reentrenar.\\nEl fine-tuning sobre datos hist\xf3ricos, el transfer learning y el preentrenamiento continuo sobre instant\xe1neas incorporan los datos en el momento del entrenamiento, por lo que quedan obsoletos a medida que el inventario sigue cambiando."},{"id":"aif-q167","question":"En un flujo de trabajo de Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG), \xbfcu\xe1l es el prop\xf3sito principal del chunking de documentos?","options":{"A":"Reducir el costo de almacenar los documentos","B":"Mejorar la relevancia de los pasajes recuperados del \xedndice vectorial","C":"Acelerar el entrenamiento del modelo","D":"Evitar tener que generar embeddings para los documentos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dividir los documentos en fragmentos m\xe1s peque\xf1os y sem\xe1nticamente coherentes permite que el recuperador devuelva solo los segmentos m\xe1s relevantes para una consulta, lo que mejora la relevancia del contexto que se pasa al modelo.\\nEl chunking no tiene como objetivo reducir el costo de almacenamiento, no acelera el entrenamiento del modelo y el texto dividido en fragmentos igualmente se somete a embedding, por lo que no evita generar embeddings."},{"id":"aif-q173","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n describe una ventaja de Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) para tareas de procesamiento de lenguaje natural (NLP)?","options":{"A":"Acelera el entrenamiento del modelo de lenguaje subyacente","B":"Reduce la cantidad de par\xe1metros del modelo para ahorrar memoria","C":"Se apoya en fuentes de conocimiento externas para producir respuestas m\xe1s exactas e informativas","D":"Garantiza salidas deterministas y repetibles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"RAG recupera documentos externos relevantes en el momento de la inferencia y condiciona la generaci\xf3n a ellos, lo que enriquece las respuestas con conocimiento actualizado y espec\xedfico del dominio sin reentrenar el modelo.\\nRAG no acelera el entrenamiento ni reduce la cantidad de par\xe1metros del modelo, y no hace que las salidas de un modelo generativo sean deterministas."},{"id":"aif-q175","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica entrena un modelo de IA con un conjunto de datos etiquetado para que se adapte a la terminolog\xeda y los requisitos de una industria espec\xedfica?","options":{"A":"Preentrenamiento continuo","B":"In-context learning","C":"Fine-tuning","D":"Destilaci\xf3n de modelos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El fine-tuning contin\xfaa el entrenamiento de un modelo preentrenado con un conjunto de datos etiquetado y espec\xedfico del dominio, de modo que aprenda la terminolog\xeda y los requisitos de la tarea de la industria directamente a partir de esos datos.\\nEl preentrenamiento continuo se basa en grandes vol\xfamenes de datos sin etiquetar; el in-context learning a\xf1ade ejemplos en el momento de la inferencia sin entrenamiento; la destilaci\xf3n de modelos transfiere conocimiento a un modelo m\xe1s peque\xf1o en lugar de adaptarlo a un conjunto de datos de dominio etiquetado."},{"id":"aif-q176","question":"Un equipo cre\xf3 un agente con Amazon Bedrock Agents y quiere mejorar su exactitud proporcion\xe1ndole algunos ejemplos espec\xedficos. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque es adecuado?","options":{"A":"Aplicar fine-tuning al modelo subyacente con los ejemplos","B":"A\xf1adir los ejemplos a los prompts avanzados del agente","C":"A\xf1adir los ejemplos a una base de conocimiento de Amazon Bedrock","D":"Crear un guardrail que incluya los ejemplos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A\xf1adir los ejemplos espec\xedficos de entrada y salida a los prompts avanzados del agente es few-shot prompting, lo que orienta al agente hacia un comportamiento m\xe1s exacto sin reentrenamiento ni servicios adicionales.\\nAplicar fine-tuning al modelo es mucho m\xe1s costoso para un pu\xf1ado de ejemplos; una base de conocimiento proporciona contexto recuperado en lugar de ejemplos de comportamiento; un guardrail aplica pol\xedticas de seguridad, no una exactitud basada en ejemplos."},{"id":"aif-q178","question":"Una empresa utilizar\xe1 fine-tuning basado en instrucciones para adaptar un modelo fundacional (FM) a su dominio. \xbfC\xf3mo deber\xeda preparar los datos de entrenamiento?","options":{"A":"Reunir un gran corpus de dominio sin etiquetar y continuar el preentrenamiento","B":"Crear pares de instrucci\xf3n y respuesta (pregunta y respuesta) para el dominio","C":"Construir un \xedndice vectorial de los documentos para la recuperaci\xf3n","D":"Recopilar clasificaciones de preferencias humanas de las salidas del modelo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El fine-tuning basado en instrucciones necesita ejemplos de instrucci\xf3n y respuesta, por lo que curar pares de pregunta y respuesta espec\xedficos del dominio ense\xf1a al modelo c\xf3mo interpretar consultas del dominio y producir respuestas correctas.\\nUn corpus sin etiquetar sirve para el preentrenamiento continuo; un \xedndice vectorial admite RAG en lugar del entrenamiento; las clasificaciones de preferencias humanas son los datos que se usan para RLHF, no para el ajuste por instrucciones."},{"id":"aif-q188","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica se usa com\xfanmente para evaluar qu\xe9 tan bien se desempe\xf1a un modelo fundacional (FM) en el resumen de texto?","options":{"A":"Puntuaci\xf3n F1","B":"Error cuadr\xe1tico medio (MSE)","C":"Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation (ROUGE)","D":"Puntuaci\xf3n Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ROUGE mide la superposici\xf3n entre un resumen generado y los res\xfamenes de referencia, lo que la convierte en la m\xe9trica est\xe1ndar para la calidad del resumen.\\nF1 es una m\xe9trica de clasificaci\xf3n, MSE mide el error de regresi\xf3n y BLEU est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para la traducci\xf3n autom\xe1tica; ninguna se orienta al resumen."},{"id":"aif-q189","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es un beneficio del ajuste fino de un modelo fundacional (FM)?","options":{"A":"Permite que el modelo incorpore datos externos en el momento de la inferencia","B":"Mejora el rendimiento del modelo en una tarea espec\xedfica mediante un entrenamiento adicional con datos de la tarea","C":"Comprime el modelo para reducir su tama\xf1o y acelerar la inferencia","D":"Actualiza el conocimiento general del modelo mediante el preentrenamiento con datos m\xe1s recientes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El ajuste fino actualiza los pesos del modelo utilizando datos relevantes para la tarea, lo que mejora su precisi\xf3n y relevancia en esa tarea espec\xedfica.\\nIncorporar datos externos en el momento de la inferencia describe RAG; comprimir el modelo describe la destilaci\xf3n; actualizar el conocimiento general con un preentrenamiento m\xe1s reciente describe el preentrenamiento continuo."},{"id":"aif-q194","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son dos casos de uso principales para almacenar embeddings en una base de datos vectorial como Amazon OpenSearch Service?","options":{"A":"Coincidencia exacta de cadenas de palabras clave","B":"B\xfasqueda sem\xe1ntica sobre documentos","C":"Trabajos ETL por lotes programados","D":"Recomendaciones basadas en similitud","E":"Consultas transaccionales por clave primaria"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"La b\xfasqueda sem\xe1ntica y las recomendaciones basadas en similitud dependen de la comparaci\xf3n de vecinos m\xe1s cercanos de los embeddings, que es exactamente lo que proporciona una base de datos vectorial.\\nLa coincidencia exacta de palabras clave es l\xe9xica en lugar de sem\xe1ntica, y las consultas por clave primaria y los trabajos ETL por lotes programados son operaciones de base de datos est\xe1ndar que no utilizan similitud vectorial."},{"id":"aif-q196","question":"Para aplicaciones de IA, \xbfcu\xe1les dos capacidades de Amazon Aurora con la extensi\xf3n pgvector son las m\xe1s importantes?","options":{"A":"Operaciones SQL est\xe1ndar","B":"B\xfasqueda por similitud vectorial","C":"Alojamiento web","D":"Combinaci\xf3n de vectores con datos estructurados","E":"Gesti\xf3n de correo electr\xf3nico"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"La b\xfasqueda por similitud vectorial permite que la aplicaci\xf3n encuentre el contenido m\xe1s relacionado sem\xe1nticamente, que es el paso central de recuperaci\xf3n en tareas como RAG, y la capacidad de combinar vectores con datos estructurados significa que esas b\xfasquedas pueden ejecutarse junto con registros relacionales existentes en la misma base de datos, por lo que, en conjunto, estas dos capacidades son las que hacen que Aurora con pgvector sea valiosa para las cargas de trabajo de IA.\\nLas operaciones SQL est\xe1ndar son una capacidad general de base de datos que no es espec\xedfica de las aplicaciones de IA. El alojamiento web y la gesti\xf3n de correo electr\xf3nico no son funciones de Amazon Aurora en absoluto y no est\xe1n relacionados con el almacenamiento vectorial ni con las cargas de trabajo de IA."},{"id":"aif-q212","question":"Una empresa quiere que un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) escriba descripciones de productos y planea incluir en el prompt varias descripciones de ejemplo que siguen un formato fijo. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica de ingenier\xeda de prompts utiliza esto?","options":{"A":"Prompting sin ejemplos (zero-shot)","B":"Prompting con un ejemplo (one-shot)","C":"Prompting con pocos ejemplos (few-shot)","D":"Prompting de rol"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El prompting con pocos ejemplos (few-shot) coloca varios ejemplos en el prompt para que el modelo aprenda el patr\xf3n y el formato y genere nuevas descripciones con el mismo estilo, sin ning\xfan entrenamiento.\\nEl prompting sin ejemplos (zero-shot) no proporciona ejemplos, el prompting con un ejemplo (one-shot) proporciona solo un ejemplo y el prompting de rol asigna una persona, pero no proporciona ejemplos con formato."},{"id":"aif-q214","question":"Una cadena de supermercados quiere un chatbot que consulte el inventario en vivo e indique a los compradores d\xf3nde se encuentra un producto en la tienda. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica de ingenier\xeda de prompts deber\xeda utilizar?","options":{"A":"Prompting con pocos ejemplos (few-shot)","B":"Prompting de cadena de pensamiento (chain-of-thought)","C":"Prompting de razonamiento y actuaci\xf3n (ReAct)","D":"Prompting sin ejemplos (zero-shot)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El prompting ReAct intercala el razonamiento del modelo con acciones expl\xedcitas, como llamar a una API de inventario en vivo, y luego utiliza los datos devueltos para responder, que es lo que se necesita para consultar el inventario e informar la ubicaci\xf3n de un producto en tiempo real.\\nEl prompting con pocos ejemplos, el prompting de cadena de pensamiento y el prompting sin ejemplos moldean el razonamiento o proporcionan ejemplos, pero no ofrecen ning\xfan mecanismo para llamar a un sistema externo y obtener datos en vivo."},{"id":"aif-q219","question":"Una empresa eval\xfaa su herramienta de traducci\xf3n comparando las salidas de la herramienta con traducciones humanas de los mismos documentos. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de evaluaci\xf3n deber\xeda utilizar?","options":{"A":"Usar ROUGE para estimar la calidad absoluta de traducci\xf3n de los dos m\xe9todos","B":"Usar la puntuaci\xf3n Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU) para estimar la calidad relativa de traducci\xf3n de los dos m\xe9todos","C":"Usar la puntuaci\xf3n Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU) para estimar la calidad absoluta de traducci\xf3n de los dos m\xe9todos","D":"Usar ROUGE para estimar la calidad relativa de traducci\xf3n de los dos m\xe9todos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"BLEU mide la superposici\xf3n de n-gramas con las traducciones de referencia, por lo que aplicarla tanto a las salidas de la herramienta como a las salidas humanas en los mismos documentos proporciona puntuaciones comparables para juzgar qu\xe9 m\xe9todo es relativamente mejor.\\nBLEU produce una comparaci\xf3n relativa, no una medida absoluta de calidad, por lo que la opci\xf3n de BLEU absoluto es incorrecta; ROUGE est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para el resumen, lo que la convierte en la m\xe9trica incorrecta para comparar traducciones."},{"id":"aif-q224","question":"La aplicaci\xf3n de IA generativa de una empresa utiliza un modelo fundacional (FM) preentrenado en Amazon Bedrock y quiere que sus respuestas incluyan la informaci\xf3n propia de la empresa, al menor costo. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n se ajusta?","options":{"A":"Usar Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases","B":"Implementar un modelo personalizado en Amazon Bedrock","C":"Realizar un ajuste fino del FM con la informaci\xf3n de la empresa","D":"Usar Amazon Bedrock Agents"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases indexa y recupera los documentos de la empresa en el momento de la inferencia e inyecta contexto relevante en el prompt (RAG), agregando la informaci\xf3n de forma econ\xf3mica y sin entrenamiento.\\nImplementar un modelo personalizado y realizar un ajuste fino del FM a\xf1aden costo de entrenamiento; Amazon Bedrock Agents orquesta tareas, pero no proporciona por s\xed mismo contexto de documentos."},{"id":"aif-q226","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando un chatbot de pol\xedticas de RR. HH. sobre un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) con un amplio conjunto de documentos digitales y quiere optimizar la calidad de sus respuestas. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica encaja mejor?","options":{"A":"Establecer la temperatura en 1","B":"Usar Generaci\xf3n Aumentada por Recuperaci\xf3n (RAG)","C":"Ajustar el modelo con los documentos de pol\xedticas","D":"Disminuir el tama\xf1o de los tokens"},"correct":"B","explanation":"RAG recupera pasajes precisos y actuales de los documentos de pol\xedticas en el momento de la consulta y fundamenta las respuestas del chatbot en ellos, optimizando la precisi\xf3n sin sobrecargar la ventana de contexto.\\nEstablecer la temperatura en 1 solo a\xf1ade aleatoriedad; el ajuste fino es costoso y queda obsoleto a medida que cambian las pol\xedticas; disminuir el tama\xf1o de los tokens elimina el contexto que el modelo necesita."},{"id":"aif-q227","question":"Una empresa educativa entrena un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) personalizado para adolescentes y quiere que la salida coincida con su estilo, incluida la ortograf\xeda creativa y las abreviaturas. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica deber\xeda usar para evaluar la salida frente a ejemplos de referencia?","options":{"A":"BERTScore","B":"Puntuaci\xf3n Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU)","C":"Perplejidad","D":"Puntuaci\xf3n F1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"BLEU mide la superposici\xf3n superficial de n-gramas entre la salida del modelo y el texto de referencia, por lo que es adecuada para comprobar con qu\xe9 fidelidad el modelo reproduce la ortograf\xeda y las abreviaturas exactas del estilo adolescente.\\nBERTScore mide la similitud sem\xe1ntica en lugar de las formas superficiales; la perplejidad mide el ajuste del modelado del lenguaje; F1 es una m\xe9trica de clasificaci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q234","question":"Un banco est\xe1 creando un chatbot para responder preguntas sobre la apertura de cuentas usando documentos p\xfablicos del banco en Amazon Bedrock con ingenier\xeda de prompts. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica encaja?","options":{"A":"Prompting de est\xedmulo direccional","B":"Prompting zero-shot","C":"Prompting few-shot","D":"Prompting one-shot"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El prompting few-shot proporciona varias preguntas y respuestas de ejemplo extra\xeddas de los documentos del banco, guiando al modelo a producir respuestas precisas y bien formateadas sin entrenamiento.\\nEl prompting de est\xedmulo direccional orienta la salida con indicios, el prompting zero-shot no proporciona ejemplos y el prompting one-shot proporciona solo un \xfanico ejemplo."},{"id":"aif-q237","question":"Una empresa quiere un chatbot para preguntas sobre pol\xedticas de empleados; las pol\xedticas cambian con frecuencia y las respuestas deben reflejar las actualizaciones casi en tiempo real. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n encaja?","options":{"A":"Ajustar un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) con el texto de las pol\xedticas con Amazon SageMaker AI","B":"Crear un flujo de trabajo de Generaci\xf3n Aumentada por Recuperaci\xf3n (RAG) con Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases","C":"Preentrenar continuamente un LLM con los documentos de pol\xedticas","D":"Crear una aplicaci\xf3n personalizada con Amazon Q Business"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un flujo de trabajo RAG con Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases recupera los documentos de pol\xedticas m\xe1s recientes en el momento de la consulta, por lo que el chatbot refleja las actualizaciones casi en tiempo real sin reentrenamiento.\\nEl ajuste fino y el preentrenamiento continuo deben volver a ejecutarse cada vez que cambian las pol\xedticas; una aplicaci\xf3n personalizada de Amazon Q Business requiere m\xe1s esfuerzo de desarrollo y no es la forma espec\xedfica de mantener las respuestas actualizadas en este caso."},{"id":"aif-q238","question":"Una empresa usa aprendizaje supervisado para entrenar un modelo con un peque\xf1o conjunto de datos etiquetados para una tarea espec\xedfica. \xbfQu\xe9 paso del ciclo de vida del modelo fundacional (FM) es este?","options":{"A":"Preentrenamiento","B":"Preentrenamiento continuo","C":"Ajuste fino","D":"Despliegue"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El ajuste fino contin\xfaa el entrenamiento de un modelo preentrenado con un peque\xf1o conjunto de datos etiquetados para una tarea objetivo, que es exactamente el paso supervisado descrito.\\nEl preentrenamiento y el preentrenamiento continuo usan grandes datos mayormente no etiquetados; el despliegue pone el modelo terminado en producci\xf3n. Ninguno de estos es el paso de entrenamiento supervisado y espec\xedfico de la tarea."},{"id":"aif-q258","question":"Una empresa quiere etiquetar datos de entrenamiento con retroalimentaci\xf3n humana para ajustar un modelo fundacional (FM), pero no quiere crear herramientas de etiquetado ni gestionar una fuerza laboral. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS encaja?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","B":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","C":"Amazon SageMaker Autopilot","D":"Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth Plus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth Plus es un servicio de etiquetado totalmente gestionado que utiliza una fuerza laboral humana, por lo que la empresa obtiene datos etiquetados por humanos sin crear herramientas de etiquetado ni gestionar etiquetadores.\\nSageMaker Data Wrangler prepara y transforma datos, SageMaker Clarify detecta sesgo y explica predicciones, y SageMaker Autopilot crea modelos autom\xe1ticamente. Ninguno de estos proporciona etiquetado humano gestionado."},{"id":"aif-q259","question":"Una empresa de streaming de medios quiere b\xfasqueda de im\xe1genes en lenguaje natural con consultas de similitud y vecinos m\xe1s cercanos, respaldada por una base de datos vectorial. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS encaja?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon OpenSearch Service","C":"Amazon Personalize","D":"Amazon RDS for MySQL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon OpenSearch Service admite almacenamiento vectorial con b\xfasqueda por similitud (vecinos m\xe1s cercanos), que es lo que requieren la b\xfasqueda de im\xe1genes en lenguaje natural y el filtrado.\\nAmazon DynamoDB (clave-valor) y Amazon RDS for MySQL (relacional) carecen de b\xfasqueda nativa por similitud vectorial, y Amazon Personalize genera recomendaciones en lugar de servir como almac\xe9n vectorial general."},{"id":"aif-q265","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 comparando varios modelos de lenguaje grandes (LLM) para el resumen de texto y necesita una m\xe9trica para la calidad del resumen. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica encaja?","options":{"A":"Puntuaci\xf3n Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU)","B":"\xc1rea bajo la curva ROC (AUC)","C":"Perplejidad","D":"Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation (ROUGE)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ROUGE mide la superposici\xf3n entre los res\xfamenes generados y los res\xfamenes de referencia, lo que la convierte en la m\xe9trica est\xe1ndar para la calidad del resumen.\\nBLEU est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para la traducci\xf3n, AUC es una m\xe9trica de clasificaci\xf3n y la perplejidad mide el ajuste del modelado del lenguaje; ninguna est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para la calidad del resumen."},{"id":"aif-q271","question":"Una empresa necesita una soluci\xf3n de IA que produzca tanto im\xe1genes como descripciones de texto para un cat\xe1logo de productos y est\xe1 comparando modelos fundacionales (FM) por los tipos de salida que admite cada uno. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de los FM est\xe1 evaluando?","options":{"A":"Latencia","B":"Longitud de la ventana de contexto","C":"Modalidad","D":"Compatibilidad multiling\xfce"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La modalidad describe los tipos de entrada y salida que admite un FM, como texto o im\xe1genes, por lo que evaluar si un modelo puede generar tanto im\xe1genes como descripciones de texto es una evaluaci\xf3n de la modalidad.\\nLa latencia es la velocidad de respuesta, la longitud de la ventana de contexto es cu\xe1nto de entrada y salida cabe, y la compatibilidad multiling\xfce se refiere a los idiomas; ninguna describe los tipos de salida que un modelo puede generar."},{"id":"aif-q272","question":"Un minorista en l\xednea quiere evaluar un modelo que etiqueta cada rese\xf1a de cliente como positiva, negativa o neutral. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de tarea de evaluaci\xf3n de modelos se ajusta a este requisito?","options":{"A":"Generaci\xf3n de texto abierta","B":"Resumen de texto","C":"Clasificaci\xf3n","D":"Preguntas y respuestas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La clasificaci\xf3n asigna cada entrada a una de un conjunto fijo de categor\xedas predefinidas, por lo que clasificar cada rese\xf1a de cliente en una de tres etiquetas de sentimiento (positiva, negativa o neutral) es una tarea de clasificaci\xf3n y se eval\xfaa con m\xe9tricas de clasificaci\xf3n.\\nLa generaci\xf3n de texto abierta produce texto de forma libre a partir de un prompt y no tiene un conjunto fijo de etiquetas de salida. El resumen de texto condensa texto m\xe1s largo en una versi\xf3n m\xe1s corta en lugar de asignarlo a una categor\xeda. Preguntas y respuestas devuelve una respuesta a una pregunta planteada en lugar de colocar la entrada en categor\xedas de sentimiento predefinidas."},{"id":"aif-q283","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS almacena embeddings en una base de datos vectorial para usarlos con modelos fundacionales (FM) y Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"Amazon OpenSearch Service","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon Redshift"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon OpenSearch Service proporciona una base de datos vectorial que almacena y busca embeddings, que es lo que impulsa los flujos de trabajo de RAG y las integraciones con modelos fundacionales.\\nAmazon S3 es almacenamiento de objetos, Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos de clave-valor y Amazon Redshift es un almac\xe9n de datos; ninguno ofrece b\xfasqueda vectorial nativa sobre embeddings."},{"id":"aif-q285","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando una soluci\xf3n de IA generativa en Amazon Bedrock y necesita un servicio que proporcione almacenamiento en base de datos vectorial y b\xfasqueda vectorial. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se ajusta?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon ElastiCache","C":"Amazon OpenSearch Service","D":"Amazon Redshift"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon OpenSearch Service admite almacenamiento vectorial y b\xfasqueda por similitud sobre embeddings, por lo que puede respaldar una soluci\xf3n de IA generativa en Amazon Bedrock.\\nAmazon DynamoDB es una base de datos de clave-valor, Amazon ElastiCache es una cach\xe9 en memoria y Amazon Redshift es un almac\xe9n de datos; ninguno proporciona capacidades nativas de base de datos vectorial y b\xfasqueda."},{"id":"aif-q288","question":"Al dise\xf1ar prompts para un modelo de IA generativa, \xbfqu\xe9 dos pr\xe1cticas son importantes?","options":{"A":"Maximizar la longitud del prompt independientemente de la relevancia","B":"Proporcionar contexto e instrucciones claros","C":"Usar redacci\xf3n ambigua para fomentar la creatividad","D":"Incluir ejemplos relevantes cuando ayuden","E":"Omitir las instrucciones y confiar en los valores predeterminados del modelo"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Un contexto y unas instrucciones claros le indican al modelo qu\xe9 se espera, y los ejemplos relevantes (few-shot prompting) lo gu\xedan hacia el formato y el estilo de salida deseados, por lo que ambos mejoran la calidad del prompt.\\nMaximizar la longitud independientemente de la relevancia, usar una redacci\xf3n ambigua y omitir las instrucciones a\xf1aden ruido o ambig\xfcedad y degradan las respuestas del modelo."},{"id":"aif-q294","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 dos m\xe9tricas se usan com\xfanmente para evaluar la calidad de salida de los modelos de IA generativa?","options":{"A":"Uso de memoria de GPU","B":"Puntuaci\xf3n Bilingual Evaluation Understudy (BLEU) para la calidad de traducci\xf3n","C":"Rendimiento de solicitudes en solicitudes por segundo","D":"Puntuaci\xf3n Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation (ROUGE) para la calidad de resumen","E":"Recuento de par\xe1metros del modelo"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"BLEU eval\xfaa la calidad de traducci\xf3n y ROUGE eval\xfaa la calidad de resumen comparando el texto generado con el texto de referencia, por lo que ambas miden la calidad de la salida generativa.\\nEl uso de memoria de GPU, el rendimiento de solicitudes y el recuento de par\xe1metros del modelo son m\xe9tricas de infraestructura y de tama\xf1o del modelo que no dicen nada sobre la calidad de la salida."},{"id":"aif-q296","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 dos capacidades de Amazon OpenSearch Service son m\xe1s relevantes para las cargas de trabajo de IA/ML?","options":{"A":"B\xfasqueda por palabra clave BM25 de texto completo","B":"B\xfasqueda de vecinos m\xe1s cercanos k-NN sobre vectores","C":"Consultas de distancia geoespacial","D":"Almacenar e indexar embeddings como una base de datos vectorial","E":"Entrenamiento automatizado de modelos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"La b\xfasqueda de vecinos m\xe1s cercanos k-NN y el almacenamiento e indexaci\xf3n de embeddings como una base de datos vectorial sustentan tareas de IA/ML como la b\xfasqueda sem\xe1ntica y RAG.\\nLa b\xfasqueda por palabra clave BM25 es l\xe9xica en lugar de sem\xe1ntica, las consultas geoespaciales manejan datos de ubicaci\xf3n y OpenSearch no realiza entrenamiento de modelos."},{"id":"aif-q301","question":"Una empresa quiere un asistente de IA que pueda evaluar fuentes de datos espec\xedficas, llamar a APIs externas y luego generar, comparar y priorizar opciones de respuesta. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de Amazon Bedrock se ajusta?","options":{"A":"Gesti\xf3n de prompts","B":"Bases de conocimiento","C":"Agentes","D":"Evaluaci\xf3n de modelos de Amazon Bedrock"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Agents orquesta tareas de varios pasos, incluida la llamada a APIs externas y la generaci\xf3n, comparaci\xf3n y priorizaci\xf3n de opciones de respuesta, lo que coincide con todas las necesidades indicadas.\\nPrompt Management versiona prompts, Knowledge Bases proporciona contexto recuperado y Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation punt\xfaa modelos; ninguno orquesta llamadas a API y toma de decisiones por s\xed solo."},{"id":"aif-q344","question":"En la IA generativa, \xbfqu\xe9 distingue el prompting zero-shot, one-shot y few-shot?","options":{"A":"La arquitectura del modelo utilizada","B":"El n\xfamero de ejemplos proporcionados en el prompt","C":"El n\xfamero de pasos de razonamiento que realiza el modelo","D":"La cantidad de datos utilizados para ajustar el modelo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Estos t\xe9rminos describen cu\xe1ntos ejemplos se colocan en el prompt: zero-shot no proporciona ninguno, one-shot proporciona un \xfanico ejemplo y few-shot proporciona un conjunto peque\xf1o, todo sin reentrenar el modelo.\\nNo se refieren a la arquitectura del modelo, al n\xfamero de pasos de razonamiento ni a la cantidad de datos de ajuste fino, que no est\xe1n relacionados con cu\xe1ntos ejemplos dentro del prompt se proporcionan."},{"id":"aif-q041","question":"Un equipo de ML est\xe1 entrenando un modelo fundacional (FM) y quiere elevar su precisi\xf3n hasta un umbral requerido. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer el equipo?","options":{"A":"Disminuir el n\xfamero de \xe9pocas","B":"Disminuir el tama\xf1o de lote","C":"Aumentar el n\xfamero de \xe9pocas","D":"Aumentar el par\xe1metro de temperatura"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aumentar el n\xfamero de \xe9pocas permite que el modelo entrene durante m\xe1s pasadas sobre los datos, lo que generalmente mejora la precisi\xf3n hasta alcanzar el nivel objetivo.\\nDisminuir las \xe9pocas proporciona menos entrenamiento al modelo y tiende a reducir la precisi\xf3n. Disminuir el tama\xf1o de lote afecta principalmente la din\xe1mica de entrenamiento y el uso de memoria, en lugar de elevar de forma fiable la precisi\xf3n hacia un objetivo. La temperatura es un par\xe1metro de inferencia que controla la aleatoriedad de la salida y no tiene efecto sobre la precisi\xf3n de entrenamiento."},{"id":"aif-q048","question":"Una marca de cosm\xe9ticos quiere que un modelo de IA generativa preentrenado escriba textos de campa\xf1a que se ajusten a la voz y los mensajes de su marca. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer el equipo?","options":{"A":"A\xf1adir m\xe1s capas a la arquitectura del modelo para aumentar su complejidad","B":"Crear prompts claros y bien estructurados que proporcionen instrucciones y contexto para guiar la salida del modelo","C":"Ajustar la arquitectura y los hiperpar\xe1metros del modelo para mejorar su rendimiento general","D":"Recopilar un conjunto de datos amplio y diverso y preentrenar un nuevo modelo generativo desde cero"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Crear prompts claros con instrucciones y contexto gu\xeda al modelo preentrenado a generar contenido que coincida con la voz de la marca, sin modificar el modelo en s\xed.\\nA\xf1adir capas o ajustar la arquitectura y los hiperpar\xe1metros implica redise\xf1ar o reentrenar el modelo, lo cual es innecesario y costoso en este caso. Preentrenar un nuevo modelo desde cero consume a\xfan m\xe1s recursos y no es necesario para alinear la salida con la voz de la marca."},{"id":"aif-q051","question":"Una consultora debe elegir un modelo de Amazon Bedrock para uso interno, y a sus analistas les importa el estilo de redacci\xf3n de las respuestas. \xbfC\xf3mo deber\xeda la empresa elegir el modelo?","options":{"A":"Evaluar los modelos con conjuntos de datos de prompts integrados","B":"Elegir el modelo bas\xe1ndose en clasificaciones p\xfablicas de modelos","C":"Evaluar los modelos con un equipo humano utilizando conjuntos de datos de prompts personalizados","D":"Comparar los modelos utilizando la m\xe9trica de tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n InvocationLatency en Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Utilizar un equipo humano para evaluar los modelos con conjuntos de datos de prompts personalizados captura preferencias de estilo subjetivas que las puntuaciones automatizadas pasan por alto, por lo que identifica mejor el modelo cuyas respuestas coinciden con lo que prefieren los empleados.\\nLos conjuntos de datos de prompts integrados y las clasificaciones p\xfablicas de modelos miden el rendimiento general, no la preferencia de estilo espec\xedfica de la empresa. La m\xe9trica InvocationLatency en Amazon CloudWatch mide la velocidad de respuesta, no el estilo de respuesta."},{"id":"aif-q065","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n de noticias ejecuta an\xe1lisis de sentimiento con un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) en Amazon Bedrock y quiere que la misma entrada produzca resultados m\xe1s estables. \xbfQu\xe9 par\xe1metro de inferencia deber\xeda cambiar?","options":{"A":"Aumentar el valor de temperatura","B":"Aumentar la longitud m\xe1xima de generaci\xf3n","C":"Disminuir el valor de temperatura","D":"Reducir el n\xfamero m\xe1ximo de tokens de salida"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Reducir la temperatura disminuye la aleatoriedad en la selecci\xf3n de tokens, lo que hace que las salidas del modelo sean m\xe1s deterministas y consistentes para la misma entrada.\\nAumentar la temperatura incrementa la aleatoriedad y la variabilidad. Cambiar la longitud m\xe1xima de generaci\xf3n o reducir el n\xfamero de tokens de salida solo afecta la longitud de la respuesta, no la consistencia de las respuestas."},{"id":"aif-q070","question":"Un equipo necesita comprobar con qu\xe9 precisi\xf3n funciona su modelo fundacional (FM) de clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia deber\xeda utilizar el equipo?","options":{"A":"Contar el n\xfamero de capas en la red neuronal del modelo","B":"Calcular el coste total de los recursos que utiliza el modelo","C":"Comprobar la precisi\xf3n del color de las im\xe1genes que procesa el modelo","D":"Medir la precisi\xf3n del modelo en un conjunto de datos de referencia predefinido"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Medir la precisi\xf3n del modelo frente a un conjunto de datos de referencia predefinido es la forma est\xe1ndar de evaluar la precisi\xf3n de clasificaci\xf3n, porque el rendimiento se compara con etiquetas correctas conocidas.\\nContar las capas de la red neuronal describe la arquitectura, no la precisi\xf3n. Calcular el coste total de los recursos mide el gasto. Comprobar la precisi\xf3n del color de las im\xe1genes procesadas no est\xe1 relacionado con la correctitud de la clasificaci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q075","question":"El chatbot de una empresa de telecomunicaciones utiliza un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) en Amazon Bedrock para la detecci\xf3n de intenciones, y el equipo quiere mejorar la precisi\xf3n con aprendizaje few-shot. \xbfQu\xe9 datos necesita el equipo?","options":{"A":"Pares de mensajes de usuario y sus intenciones de usuario correctas","B":"Pares de mensajes de usuario y sus respuestas correctas del chatbot","C":"Pares de respuestas del chatbot y sus intenciones de usuario correctas","D":"Pares de intenciones de usuario y sus respuestas correctas del chatbot"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El aprendizaje few-shot proporciona al modelo pares de ejemplo entrada-salida para la tarea, y la detecci\xf3n de intenciones asigna un mensaje de usuario a una intenci\xf3n, por lo que la empresa necesita pares de mensajes de usuario y sus intenciones de usuario correctas.\\nLos pares que involucran respuestas del chatbot, ya sean mensajes-a-respuestas, respuestas-a-intenciones o intenciones-a-respuestas, entrenan la asignaci\xf3n incorrecta y no demuestran c\xf3mo detectar la intenci\xf3n de un usuario a partir de un mensaje."},{"id":"aif-q089","question":"Durante una prueba de red team de un modelo fundacional (FM), un ingeniero intenta eludir sus filtros de seguridad para que produzca texto da\xf1ino. \xbfC\xf3mo se llama esta t\xe9cnica?","options":{"A":"Fuzzing de los datos de entrenamiento para encontrar vulnerabilidades","B":"Jailbreaking","C":"Pruebas de penetraci\xf3n autorizadas","D":"Ataque de denegaci\xf3n de servicio (DoS)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El jailbreaking es el intento de eludir o desactivar los controles de seguridad integrados de un modelo para que produzca contenido restringido o da\xf1ino, que es exactamente lo que est\xe1 haciendo el ingeniero.\\nEl fuzzing de los datos de entrenamiento busca vulnerabilidades alimentando entradas malformadas durante el desarrollo, no eludiendo los filtros de seguridad. Las pruebas de penetraci\xf3n autorizadas son una evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad sancionada. Un ataque de denegaci\xf3n de servicio se dirige a la disponibilidad en lugar de derrotar las salvaguardas de contenido."},{"id":"aif-q110","question":"Un equipo est\xe1 creando una funci\xf3n de b\xfasqueda conversacional y necesita una base de datos que pueda almacenar embeddings de IA generativa como vectores y ejecutar consultas de similitud sobre ellos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Athena","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL","D":"Amazon EMR"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL admite la extensi\xf3n pgvector, que almacena, indexa y ejecuta consultas de similitud sobre embeddings vectoriales, lo que lo hace adecuado para almacenar y consultar embeddings como vectores.\\nAmazon Athena es un servicio de consultas sin servidor para datos en Amazon S3. Amazon Redshift es un almac\xe9n de datos para anal\xedtica. Amazon EMR ejecuta marcos de procesamiento de big data. Ninguno de estos est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para almacenar y consultar embeddings vectoriales para b\xfasqueda por similitud."},{"id":"aif-q114","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puede un equipo determinar si un modelo fundacional (FM) cumple eficazmente sus objetivos de negocio?","options":{"A":"Evaluar el rendimiento del modelo en conjuntos de datos de referencia","B":"Evaluar qu\xe9 tan bien se alinea el modelo con los casos de uso espec\xedficos que debe admitir","C":"Analizar la arquitectura y los hiperpar\xe1metros del modelo","D":"Medir los recursos computacionales que el modelo necesita para su despliegue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Evaluar qu\xe9 tan bien se alinea el modelo con los casos de uso espec\xedficos que debe admitir muestra si sus resultados resuelven los problemas reales del negocio, que es c\xf3mo se determina si cumple los objetivos de negocio.\\nEl rendimiento en benchmarks mide la precisi\xf3n general, no la adecuaci\xf3n al negocio. Analizar la arquitectura y los hiperpar\xe1metros es un detalle t\xe9cnico no relacionado con el valor del negocio. Medir los recursos computacionales aborda el costo y la viabilidad, no si se cumplen los objetivos."},{"id":"aif-q132","question":"Una empresa quiere mantener actualizado su modelo fundacional (FM) reentren\xe1ndolo regularmente con los datos m\xe1s recientes. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de entrenamiento cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje por lotes","B":"Entrenamiento est\xe1tico","C":"Entrenamiento latente","D":"Preentrenamiento continuo"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El preentrenamiento continuo ingiere peri\xf3dicamente los datos m\xe1s recientes y actualiza los pesos del modelo, de modo que el modelo fundacional se mantiene actualizado sin reentrenarlo desde cero cada vez.\\nEl aprendizaje por lotes procesa datos en lotes fijos, pero no es una estrategia para la actualizaci\xf3n continua del modelo. El entrenamiento est\xe1tico produce un modelo fijo de una sola vez que no se actualiza. El entrenamiento latente no es una estrategia de entrenamiento reconocida."},{"id":"aif-q136","question":"El modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) de una empresa sigue produciendo alucinaciones. \xbfQu\xe9 cambio puede reducirlas?","options":{"A":"Disminuir el par\xe1metro de inferencia de temperatura del modelo","B":"Usar Agents for Amazon Bedrock para supervisar el entrenamiento del modelo","C":"Preprocesar los datos para eliminar cualquier dato que cause alucinaciones","D":"Cambiar a un modelo fundacional (FM) que est\xe9 entrenado para nunca alucinar"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reducir el par\xe1metro de temperatura disminuye la aleatoriedad en la selecci\xf3n de tokens del modelo, por lo que se apoya en salidas de mayor probabilidad y es menos probable que produzca contenido alucinado.\\nAgents for Amazon Bedrock orquesta tareas y no supervisa el entrenamiento. No se pueden identificar y eliminar de forma confiable datos espec\xedficos que causan alucinaciones mediante el preprocesamiento. Ning\xfan modelo fundacional tiene garant\xeda de nunca alucinar, por lo que cambiar de modelo no resuelve el problema."},{"id":"aif-q140","question":"\xbfEn qu\xe9 se diferencia el muestreo top-p (n\xfacleo) del muestreo basado en temperatura?","options":{"A":"Muestrea del conjunto de tokens m\xe1s peque\xf1o posible","B":"Siempre produce una salida determinista","C":"Muestrea solo del conjunto de tokens cuyas probabilidades suman p","D":"Funciona solo con arquitecturas de modelo espec\xedficas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El muestreo top-p (n\xfacleo) restringe din\xe1micamente los tokens candidatos al conjunto m\xe1s peque\xf1o cuya probabilidad acumulada alcanza p y luego muestrea de ese conjunto, lo que adapta la elecci\xf3n de tokens a la distribuci\xf3n de probabilidad en cada paso.\\nNo fija el conjunto de tokens al tama\xf1o m\xe1s peque\xf1o posible independientemente de las probabilidades. No produce una salida determinista, ya que sigue muestreando. No est\xe1 limitado a arquitecturas de modelo espec\xedficas."},{"id":"aif-q190","question":"Un minorista tiene 100 conversaciones de alta calidad entre agentes y clientes y quiere que las respuestas de su chatbot coincidan con el tono de la empresa. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Usar Amazon Personalize para generar las respuestas","B":"Crear un trabajo de ajuste fino de Amazon Bedrock","C":"Crear un trabajo de preentrenamiento de Amazon SageMaker HyperPod","D":"Alojar el modelo en Amazon SageMaker AI y aplicar TensorRT para optimizar la inferencia del LLM"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un trabajo de ajuste fino de Amazon Bedrock adapta un modelo fundacional con las conversaciones de ejemplo propias de la empresa, ense\xf1\xe1ndole el tono de la empresa para que el chatbot lo refleje en las respuestas.\\nAmazon Personalize es un servicio de recomendaciones, no una forma de dar estilo al texto del chatbot. Un trabajo de preentrenamiento de SageMaker HyperPod es para preentrenamiento a gran escala desde cero, lo cual es excesivo para 100 ejemplos. Alojar el modelo y aplicar TensorRT optimiza la velocidad de inferencia, no el tono de las respuestas."},{"id":"aif-q195","question":"Un profesional de IA est\xe1 comparando el muestreo top-p (n\xfacleo) con el muestreo basado en temperatura. \xbfQu\xe9 distingue al muestreo top-p?","options":{"A":"Funciona solo con ciertas arquitecturas de modelo","B":"Siempre devuelve la misma salida para un prompt dado","C":"Limita el muestreo a exactamente un token en cada paso","D":"Muestrea del conjunto m\xe1s peque\xf1o de tokens principales cuyas probabilidades suman p"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El muestreo top-p (n\xfacleo) elige el conjunto m\xe1s peque\xf1o de tokens de mayor probabilidad cuya probabilidad acumulada alcanza p y muestrea de ese conjunto, adaptando el conjunto de candidatos a la distribuci\xf3n en cada paso en lugar de usar un corte fijo.\\nNo est\xe1 restringido a arquitecturas de modelo particulares. Sigue muestreando, por lo que no devuelve una salida id\xe9ntica cada vez. No limita la selecci\xf3n a un solo token."},{"id":"aif-q200","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 agregando un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) a su aplicaci\xf3n y necesita respuestas que sean lo m\xe1s deterministas y estables posible. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Agregar \\"make your response deterministic\\" al final del prompt antes de enviarlo","B":"Establecer el par\xe1metro de temperatura en 1 al enviar el prompt","C":"Establecer el par\xe1metro de temperatura en 0 al enviar el prompt","D":"Agregar \\"make your response deterministic\\" al inicio del prompt antes de enviarlo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Establecer la temperatura en 0 fuerza la decodificaci\xf3n codiciosa, por lo que el modelo selecciona el token siguiente m\xe1s probable cada vez y produce salidas deterministas y estables.\\nEstablecer la temperatura en 1 mantiene la aleatoriedad total, que es lo opuesto a determinista. Agregar una instrucci\xf3n como \\"make your response deterministic\\" al inicio o al final del prompt no controla el proceso de muestreo y no har\xe1 que la salida sea determinista de forma confiable."},{"id":"aif-q210","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 par\xe1metro de un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) establece cu\xe1ntos tokens siguientes candidatos considera el modelo en cada paso de generaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"M\xe1ximo de tokens","B":"Temperatura","C":"Top K","D":"Tama\xf1o de lote"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El par\xe1metro Top K limita el muestreo en cada paso a los K tokens siguientes m\xe1s probables, por lo que controla directamente cu\xe1ntos tokens candidatos considera el modelo al generar cada token.\\nEl m\xe1ximo de tokens limita la longitud total de la salida, no el conjunto de candidatos en cada paso. La temperatura ajusta la aleatoriedad de la distribuci\xf3n, no la cantidad de candidatos. El tama\xf1o de lote se refiere a cu\xe1ntas entradas se procesan juntas, no a la selecci\xf3n de tokens."},{"id":"aif-q220","question":"Un profesional de IA quiere que un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) produzca una salida m\xe1s diversa y creativa. \xbfC\xf3mo deber\xeda ajustar un par\xe1metro de inferencia?","options":{"A":"Disminuir el valor de Top K","B":"Aumentar la longitud de la respuesta","C":"Aumentar el valor de la temperatura","D":"Disminuir la longitud del prompt"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aumentar la temperatura aplana la distribuci\xf3n de probabilidad del modelo y aumenta la probabilidad de seleccionar tokens menos probables, lo que produce respuestas m\xe1s variadas y creativas.\\nDisminuir el valor de Top K reduce los tokens candidatos y hace que la salida sea m\xe1s enfocada, no m\xe1s diversa. Aumentar la longitud de la respuesta solo hace que la salida sea m\xe1s larga. Disminuir la longitud del prompt reduce el contexto y no aumenta la creatividad."},{"id":"aif-q239","question":"Una empresa ajustar\xe1 finamente un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) en Amazon Bedrock para refinar el estilo de los mensajes de salida de su aplicaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de datos necesita la empresa?","options":{"A":"Muestras solo de mensajes de entrada","B":"Muestras emparejadas de mensajes de entrada y sus correspondientes mensajes de salida","C":"Muestras solo de mensajes de salida","D":"Muestras separadas y no emparejadas de mensajes de entrada y mensajes de salida"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El ajuste fino para refinar el estilo de salida requiere ejemplos emparejados de mensajes de entrada y sus correspondientes mensajes de salida, de modo que el modelo aprenda la correspondencia entre la entrada y la salida con el estilo deseado.\\nLas muestras solo de mensajes de entrada o solo de mensajes de salida carecen de la relaci\xf3n entrada-salida que el modelo debe aprender. Las muestras separadas y no emparejadas no muestran qu\xe9 salida corresponde a qu\xe9 entrada, por lo que el modelo no puede aprender el estilo."},{"id":"aif-q246","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 dos par\xe1metros se usan com\xfanmente para controlar la diversidad de la salida de un modelo de IA generativa?","options":{"A":"Tama\xf1o del modelo","B":"Muestreo top-k","C":"Tama\xf1o del conjunto de datos de entrenamiento","D":"Muestreo top-p (n\xfacleo)","E":"Configuraci\xf3n de hardware"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"El muestreo top-k limita la selecci\xf3n a los k tokens siguientes m\xe1s probables, y el muestreo top-p (n\xfacleo) selecciona del conjunto m\xe1s peque\xf1o de tokens cuyas probabilidades suman p; ambos ajustan el conjunto de candidatos para equilibrar la diversidad y la calidad del texto generado.\\nEl tama\xf1o del modelo, el tama\xf1o del conjunto de datos de entrenamiento y la configuraci\xf3n de hardware afectan la capacidad, el costo o la velocidad del modelo, pero no son par\xe1metros en tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n que controlen la diversidad de la salida."},{"id":"aif-q247","question":"Un equipo de dise\xf1o usa un modelo base (FM) de generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes en Amazon Bedrock y quiere controlar qu\xe9 tan detallada o abstracta es cada imagen generada. \xbfQu\xe9 par\xe1metro del modelo deber\xeda ajustar?","options":{"A":"Punto de control del modelo","B":"Paso de generaci\xf3n","C":"Tama\xf1o de lote","D":"Longitud de tokens"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El par\xe1metro de paso de generaci\xf3n establece cu\xe1ntos pasos de refinamiento ejecuta el modelo mientras crea una imagen; m\xe1s pasos producen im\xe1genes m\xe1s detalladas y menos pasos producen im\xe1genes m\xe1s abstractas, por lo que controla el nivel de detalle.\\nUn punto de control del modelo selecciona una versi\xf3n guardada del modelo, no el detalle de una imagen individual. El tama\xf1o de lote establece cu\xe1ntas im\xe1genes se producen a la vez. La longitud de tokens se aplica a la generaci\xf3n de texto, no al detalle de la imagen."},{"id":"aif-q257","question":"Un equipo genera im\xe1genes con un modelo Amazon Nova Canvas y necesita mantener elementos espec\xedficos fuera de los resultados. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Usar un valor de temperatura m\xe1s alto","B":"Usar un prompt negativo","C":"Usar un prompt m\xe1s detallado","D":"Cambiar a otro modelo base (FM)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un prompt negativo especifica los elementos o caracter\xedsticas que el modelo debe evitar, por lo que mantiene los elementos no deseados fuera de las im\xe1genes generadas.\\nUna temperatura m\xe1s alta solo agrega aleatoriedad. Un prompt m\xe1s detallado describe qu\xe9 incluir, pero no excluye de forma confiable elementos espec\xedficos. Cambiar a otro modelo base no proporciona un mecanismo para excluir elementos."},{"id":"aif-q260","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando una herramienta de IA generativa y personalizar\xe1 un modelo base (FM) con sus documentos internos. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Clasificaci\xf3n","B":"Destilaci\xf3n","C":"Preentrenamiento continuado","D":"Regresi\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El preentrenamiento continuado entrena m\xe1s un modelo base con los documentos internos de la empresa, de modo que el modelo se adapta al dominio y al contenido de la empresa.\\nLa clasificaci\xf3n y la regresi\xf3n son tareas de ML supervisado, no formas de personalizar un FM con documentos. La destilaci\xf3n transfiere conocimiento de un modelo m\xe1s grande a uno m\xe1s peque\xf1o, en lugar de adaptar un modelo a documentos internos."},{"id":"aif-q266","question":"Un grupo de investigaci\xf3n quiere que varios cient\xedficos comparen manualmente las salidas de diferentes modelos de IA generativa para un prompt definido. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Usar evaluaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica en Amazon Personalize","B":"Usar evaluaci\xf3n de modelos en Amazon Bedrock","C":"Usar moderaci\xf3n de contenido en Amazon Rekognition","D":"Usar an\xe1lisis de sentimiento en Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La evaluaci\xf3n de modelos de Amazon Bedrock admite evaluaci\xf3n humana, por lo que un equipo de cient\xedficos puede revisar y comparar las salidas de los modelos con el prompt y los criterios del grupo.\\nAmazon Personalize proporciona recomendaciones. Amazon Rekognition realiza moderaci\xf3n de contenido de im\xe1genes y videos. Amazon Comprehend realiza tareas de NLP como el an\xe1lisis de sentimiento. Ninguno de estos proporciona evaluaci\xf3n humana de la salida de modelos generativos."},{"id":"aif-q042","question":"Un centro de soporte implementa un chatbot de modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) para reducir la cantidad de pasos que realizan los agentes al responder las preguntas de los clientes. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica de negocio muestra mejor si el chatbot est\xe1 marcando una diferencia?","options":{"A":"Responsabilidad social corporativa","B":"Cumplimiento normativo","C":"Duraci\xf3n promedio de la llamada","D":"Tasa de interacci\xf3n del sitio web"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La duraci\xf3n promedio de la llamada refleja directamente en qu\xe9 medida el chatbot reduce los pasos que realizan los agentes para resolver las preguntas, por lo que medirla permite evaluar el impacto del chatbot en el negocio.\\nLa responsabilidad social corporativa y el cumplimiento normativo son preocupaciones organizacionales no relacionadas con la rapidez con la que los agentes resuelven las preguntas de los clientes. La tasa de interacci\xf3n del sitio web mide el comportamiento de los visitantes en l\xednea, no la eficiencia del centro de llamadas."},{"id":"aif-q244","question":"Para cargas de trabajo de IA, \xbfcu\xe1l es el principal beneficio de combinar Amazon Aurora con la extensi\xf3n pgvector?","options":{"A":"Genera modelos de IA autom\xe1ticamente","B":"Proporciona modelos de lenguaje preentrenados","C":"Permite la b\xfasqueda por similitud vectorial dentro de una base de datos relacional","D":"Proporciona capacidades de computaci\xf3n sin servidor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Aurora con la extensi\xf3n pgvector permite la b\xfasqueda por similitud vectorial dentro de una base de datos relacional, de modo que los embeddings se pueden almacenar junto con los datos estructurados y consultarse con SQL mientras se conservan las caracter\xedsticas relacionales.\\nAurora no genera modelos de IA autom\xe1ticamente ni proporciona modelos de lenguaje preentrenados. La capacidad sin servidor es una opci\xf3n de implementaci\xf3n, pero no es la principal ventaja de pgvector para aplicaciones de IA."},{"id":"aif-q384","question":"Una empresa ejecuta varias aplicaciones de IA generativa en Amazon Bedrock y desea versionar sus prompts, comparar diferentes variantes de prompts y revertir a una versi\xf3n anterior si la calidad disminuye. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de Amazon Bedrock cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases","B":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Amazon Bedrock Prompt Management","D":"Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Prompt Management permite a los equipos crear, versionar y administrar plantillas de prompts, comparar variantes y revertir a versiones anteriores, lo que proporciona los controles de ciclo de vida que la empresa necesita para mantener la calidad de los prompts en sus aplicaciones.\\nAmazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases indexa y recupera documentos para flujos de trabajo RAG. Amazon Bedrock Guardrails filtra contenido da\xf1ino o no deseado. Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation punt\xfaa y compara el rendimiento de los modelos. Ninguna de estas caracter\xedsticas administra versiones de prompts ni admite reversi\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q385","question":"Una empresa implement\xf3 un asistente de IA en Amazon Bedrock para gestionar solicitudes de servicio al cliente. Desea medir con qu\xe9 eficacia el asistente resuelve los problemas de los clientes sin escalamiento. \xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9trica captura mejor esto?","options":{"A":"Puntuaci\xf3n BLEU","B":"Tasa de finalizaci\xf3n de tareas","C":"Perplejidad","D":"Recuento de par\xe1metros del modelo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La tasa de finalizaci\xf3n de tareas mide el porcentaje de solicitudes de clientes que el asistente resuelve por completo sin necesidad de escalamiento humano, lo que refleja directamente con qu\xe9 eficacia cumple el objetivo de negocio de gestionar solicitudes de servicio de forma aut\xf3noma.\\nLa puntuaci\xf3n BLEU mide la calidad de la traducci\xf3n al comparar el texto generado con referencias. La perplejidad mide qu\xe9 tan bien un modelo de lenguaje predice texto. El recuento de par\xe1metros del modelo describe el tama\xf1o del modelo. Ninguna de estas m\xe9tricas mide si el asistente completa con \xe9xito las tareas de los clientes."},{"id":"aif-q386","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les dos m\xe9tricas son ejemplos de m\xe9tricas de alineaci\xf3n con el negocio para evaluar aplicaciones de IA?","options":{"A":"Satisfacci\xf3n del usuario","B":"BERTScore","C":"Costo por interacci\xf3n","D":"Puntuaci\xf3n ROUGE","E":"Tiempo de entrenamiento del modelo"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"La satisfacci\xf3n del usuario mide qu\xe9 tan bien la aplicaci\xf3n de IA cumple las expectativas del usuario final, y el costo por interacci\xf3n mide el gasto operativo de cada interacci\xf3n del usuario; ambas se alinean directamente con los objetivos de negocio y se definen como m\xe9tricas de alineaci\xf3n con el negocio para aplicaciones de IA.\\nBERTScore y la puntuaci\xf3n ROUGE son m\xe9tricas t\xe9cnicas que miden la calidad del texto generado frente a referencias, en lugar de resultados de negocio. El tiempo de entrenamiento del modelo es una m\xe9trica operativa sobre cu\xe1nto tarda el entrenamiento y no dice nada sobre si la aplicaci\xf3n alcanza sus objetivos de negocio."},{"id":"aif-q387","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando un grafo de conocimiento que tambi\xe9n necesita almacenar embeddings vectoriales y ejecutar b\xfasquedas por similitud sobre ellos junto con recorridos de grafo. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de base de datos de AWS admite tanto consultas de grafo como b\xfasqueda vectorial?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon Neptune","C":"Amazon Redshift","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Neptune es una base de datos de grafos que tambi\xe9n admite b\xfasqueda por similitud vectorial, lo que permite a la empresa almacenar embeddings junto con las relaciones del grafo de conocimiento y ejecutar tanto recorridos de grafo como consultas de vecinos m\xe1s cercanos en la misma base de datos.\\nAmazon DynamoDB es una base de datos de clave-valor y de documentos sin capacidades nativas de b\xfasqueda de grafos o vectorial. Amazon Redshift es un almac\xe9n de datos para anal\xedtica. Amazon ElastiCache es un servicio de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria. Ninguno de estos combina consultas de grafo con b\xfasqueda por similitud vectorial."},{"id":"aif-q388","question":"Una empresa genera contenido de marketing de formato largo con un modelo fundacional y desea evaluar la calidad a escala sin depender de revisores humanos. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque de evaluaci\xf3n utiliza otro modelo de lenguaje grande para puntuar las salidas?","options":{"A":"Puntuaci\xf3n BLEU","B":"LLM como juez","C":"Comparaci\xf3n con un conjunto de datos de referencia","D":"Pruebas A/B con usuarios reales"},"correct":"B","explanation":"LLM como juez utiliza un modelo de lenguaje grande separado para evaluar y puntuar las salidas de otro modelo seg\xfan criterios definidos, lo que proporciona una evaluaci\xf3n de calidad escalable sin requerir revisores humanos para cada salida.\\nLa puntuaci\xf3n BLEU compara el texto generado con traducciones de referencia mediante superposici\xf3n de n-gramas y requiere referencias escritas previamente. La comparaci\xf3n con un conjunto de datos de referencia eval\xfaa contra conjuntos de prueba fijos con respuestas conocidas. Las pruebas A/B requieren usuarios reales para comparar variantes. Ninguno de estos enfoques utiliza otro LLM para juzgar la calidad de las salidas."},{"id":"aif-q436","question":"Una empresa implement\xf3 un asistente de soporte al cliente basado en RAG y desea evaluar si el componente de recuperaci\xf3n devuelve documentos relevantes para las consultas de los usuarios. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque debe usar el equipo?","options":{"A":"Medir la perplejidad del modelo en un conjunto de datos reservado","B":"Comparar los pasajes recuperados con documentos de origen correctos conocidos para un conjunto de consultas de prueba","C":"Contar el n\xfamero total de llamadas a la API realizadas a Amazon Bedrock","D":"Medir la puntuaci\xf3n BLEU de las salidas del modelo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Evaluar una aplicaci\xf3n RAG requiere determinar si el componente de recuperaci\xf3n devuelve documentos relevantes, lo que se logra comparando los pasajes recuperados para consultas de prueba con documentos de origen correctos conocidos y midiendo la precisi\xf3n y la exhaustividad de la recuperaci\xf3n.\\nLa perplejidad eval\xfaa la calidad del modelo de lenguaje, no la relevancia de la recuperaci\xf3n. Contar llamadas a la API mide el volumen de uso. La puntuaci\xf3n BLEU eval\xfaa la calidad de la traducci\xf3n. Ninguno de estos enfoques eval\xfaa el pipeline de recuperaci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q444","question":"Una empresa env\xeda el mismo prompt de sistema extenso miles de veces al d\xeda y solo cambia la consulta del usuario, y quiere reducir la latencia y el costo. \xbfQu\xe9 criterio de selecci\xf3n de FM es m\xe1s relevante?","options":{"A":"Soporte multiling\xfce","B":"Almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de prompts","C":"Complejidad del modelo","D":"Capacidad de difusi\xf3n"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de prompts guarda la representaci\xf3n procesada de un prompt de sistema repetido para que el modelo no lo vuelva a procesar en cada solicitud, lo que reduce tanto la latencia como el costo cuando se env\xeda repetidamente el mismo prefijo y solo var\xeda la consulta del usuario.\\nEl soporte multiling\xfce se refiere a la cobertura de idiomas. La complejidad del modelo se relaciona con la capacidad de razonamiento. La capacidad de difusi\xf3n se aplica a modelos de generaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes. Ninguno de estos aborda el costo de procesar un prefijo de prompt repetido."},{"id":"aif-q445","question":"Una empresa necesita un modelo fundacional que pueda aceptar tanto texto como im\xe1genes como entrada y producir texto como salida. \xbfQu\xe9 criterio de selecci\xf3n de FM determina si un modelo admite esto?","options":{"A":"Rango de temperatura","B":"Modalidad","C":"Tama\xf1o de la ventana de contexto","D":"Tama\xf1o del conjunto de datos de entrenamiento"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La modalidad describe los tipos de entrada y salida que admite un modelo, como texto, im\xe1genes, audio o video. Un modelo debe admitir entrada multimodal (texto e im\xe1genes) para aceptar ambos tipos y producir texto como salida.\\nEl rango de temperatura controla la aleatoriedad de la salida. El tama\xf1o de la ventana de contexto determina cu\xe1nta informaci\xf3n cabe en un solo prompt. El tama\xf1o del conjunto de datos de entrenamiento afecta el conocimiento del modelo, pero no determina los tipos de entrada admitidos."},{"id":"aif-q446","question":"Una empresa ejecuta un modelo fundacional grande en Amazon Bedrock, pero considera que el costo de inferencia es demasiado alto. Quiere un modelo m\xe1s peque\xf1o y econ\xf3mico que conserve la mayor parte de la calidad del modelo grande. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque de personalizaci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Generaci\xf3n aumentada por recuperaci\xf3n (RAG)","B":"Destilaci\xf3n de modelos","C":"Preentrenamiento continuo","D":"Aprendizaje en contexto"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La destilaci\xf3n de modelos entrena un modelo estudiante m\xe1s peque\xf1o para replicar el comportamiento de un modelo maestro m\xe1s grande, produciendo un modelo compacto que conserva la mayor parte de la calidad del original con un costo de inferencia menor.\\nRAG recupera documentos externos en el momento de la consulta, pero no reduce el tama\xf1o ni el costo del modelo. El preentrenamiento continuo actualiza el conocimiento, pero no reduce el modelo. El aprendizaje en contexto agrega ejemplos al prompt, lo que en realidad aumenta los costos de tokens."},{"id":"aif-q447","question":"Una empresa de viajes quiere un sistema de IA que pueda buscar disponibilidad de vuelos, comparar precios entre aerol\xedneas, reservar boletos y enviar correos electr\xf3nicos de confirmaci\xf3n, todo a partir de una \xfanica solicitud del cliente. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Un modelo de resumen de texto","B":"Un agente de IA que orquesta tareas de varios pasos mediante llamadas a herramientas","C":"Un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n que enruta solicitudes a departamentos","D":"Un motor de recomendaciones que sugiere vuelos populares"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un agente de IA puede desglosar la solicitud del cliente en pasos (buscar vuelos, comparar precios, reservar y enviar la confirmaci\xf3n) y ejecutar cada uno llamando a las herramientas y API adecuadas, que es la capacidad definitoria de los agentes para flujos de trabajo empresariales complejos.\\nUn modelo de resumen condensa texto, pero no puede realizar acciones. Un modelo de clasificaci\xf3n enruta solicitudes, pero no puede ejecutar tareas de varios pasos. Un motor de recomendaciones sugiere elementos, pero no puede reservar vuelos ni enviar correos electr\xf3nicos."},{"id":"aif-q448","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 dos pr\xe1cticas mejoran la calidad de las respuestas de un modelo fundacional mediante la ingenier\xeda de prompts?","options":{"A":"Ser espec\xedfico y conciso en las instrucciones","B":"Usar lenguaje vago para permitir libertad creativa","C":"Experimentar con diferentes estructuras de prompt e iterar","D":"Usar siempre el l\xedmite m\xe1ximo de tokens para los prompts","E":"Evitar proporcionar cualquier contexto para mantener los prompts cortos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Ser espec\xedfico y conciso le dice al modelo exactamente qu\xe9 se espera sin ambig\xfcedad, y experimentar con diferentes estructuras de prompt e iterar ayuda a descubrir qu\xe9 funciona mejor para cada tarea, y ambas son pr\xe1cticas recomendadas de ingenier\xeda de prompts.\\nEl lenguaje vago introduce ambig\xfcedad que degrada la calidad de las respuestas. Usar el l\xedmite m\xe1ximo de tokens desperdicia tokens en contenido irrelevante. Evitar el contexto priva al modelo de la informaci\xf3n que necesita para responder con precisi\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q449","question":"Un equipo administra docenas de prompts en m\xfaltiples aplicaciones de IA generativa y quiere realizar un seguimiento de los cambios, comparar variantes y revertir a versiones anteriores si la calidad disminuye. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de Amazon Bedrock cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases","B":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Amazon Bedrock Prompt Management","D":"Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Prompt Management proporciona versionado de prompts, comparaci\xf3n de variantes y capacidades de reversi\xf3n, lo que brinda a los equipos control del ciclo de vida de sus prompts en m\xfaltiples aplicaciones.\\nKnowledge Bases indexa y recupera documentos para RAG. Guardrails filtra contenido por seguridad. Model Evaluation punt\xfaa el rendimiento del modelo. Ninguna de estas administra versiones de prompts."},{"id":"aif-q001","question":"Una empresa de log\xedstica usa modelos de ML para pronosticar la demanda cada trimestre. Un analista est\xe1 preparando un informe que ayuda a las partes interesadas a comprender c\xf3mo los modelos entrenados llegan a sus predicciones. \xbfQu\xe9 debe incluir el analista para satisfacer las necesidades de transparencia y explicabilidad?","options":{"A":"Datos de muestra usados para el entrenamiento","B":"Tablas de convergencia del modelo","C":"Gr\xe1ficos de dependencia parcial (PDP)","D":"C\xf3digo fuente usado para entrenar el modelo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los gr\xe1ficos de dependencia parcial (PDP) muestran c\xf3mo una o m\xe1s caracter\xedsticas de entrada se relacionan con la salida predicha por un modelo, lo que los convierte en una forma eficaz de explicar la influencia de las caracter\xedsticas y brindar a las partes interesadas transparencia sobre c\xf3mo los modelos entrenados llegan a sus pron\xf3sticos.\\nEl c\xf3digo fuente usado para entrenar el modelo y los datos de entrenamiento de muestra revelan la implementaci\xf3n y las entradas, pero no explican c\xf3mo las caracter\xedsticas individuales impulsan las predicciones. Las tablas de convergencia del modelo describen c\xf3mo avanz\xf3 el entrenamiento hacia una soluci\xf3n en lugar de c\xf3mo se comporta el modelo terminado, por lo que no cumplen el requisito de explicabilidad."},{"id":"aif-q003","question":"Un laboratorio de investigaci\xf3n necesita clasificar muestras gen\xe9ticas en 20 categor\xedas seg\xfan sus caracter\xedsticas. El equipo requiere un algoritmo cuya l\xf3gica de decisi\xf3n interna pueda documentarse claramente para mostrar c\xf3mo produce cada salida. \xbfQu\xe9 algoritmo se ajusta a esta necesidad?","options":{"A":"Regresi\xf3n log\xedstica","B":"\xc1rboles de decisi\xf3n","C":"Redes neuronales","D":"Regresi\xf3n lineal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los \xe1rboles de decisi\xf3n hacen expl\xedcita su l\xf3gica interna mediante una serie de reglas de divisi\xf3n legibles, por lo que el equipo puede documentar exactamente c\xf3mo el modelo pasa de las caracter\xedsticas de entrada a una de las 20 categor\xedas de salida. Esta interpretabilidad es la raz\xf3n por la que los \xe1rboles de decisi\xf3n se ajustan al requisito.\\nLas redes neuronales son altamente opacas, por lo que su mecanismo interno es dif\xedcil de documentar. La regresi\xf3n lineal predice un valor continuo en lugar de asignar categor\xedas discretas, lo que no coincide con un problema de 20 clases. La regresi\xf3n log\xedstica es interpretable mediante sus coeficientes, pero es menos natural para documentar claramente rutas de decisi\xf3n en muchas clases que un \xe1rbol de decisi\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q047","question":"Una plataforma de medios planea usar un modelo de lenguaje grande para la moderaci\xf3n de contenido y quiere verificar sus salidas en busca de sesgo y trato injusto hacia grupos particulares. \xbfQu\xe9 fuente de datos permite esta evaluaci\xf3n con el menor esfuerzo administrativo?","options":{"A":"Registros de moderaci\xf3n","B":"Contenido generado por usuarios","C":"Conjuntos de datos de referencia","D":"Directrices internas de moderaci\xf3n de contenido"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los conjuntos de datos de referencia son conjuntos de datos estandarizados y curados creados espec\xedficamente para evaluar modelos en cuanto a sesgo y equidad, por lo que permiten a la plataforma evaluar las salidas del modelo de forma consistente con muy poco esfuerzo administrativo.\\nEl contenido generado por usuarios y los registros de moderaci\xf3n requerir\xedan un etiquetado y una revisi\xf3n manuales sustanciales y no proporcionan una l\xednea base de equidad consistente. Las directrices internas de moderaci\xf3n de contenido son documentos de pol\xedticas, no un conjunto de datos que pueda usarse para medir el sesgo en las salidas."},{"id":"aif-q049","question":"Un prestamista est\xe1 creando una soluci\xf3n de IA generativa que ofrece descuentos a nuevos solicitantes con base en reglas de negocio. Para usar el modelo de forma responsable y limitar el sesgo que podr\xeda perjudicar a algunos clientes, \xbfqu\xe9 dos acciones debe tomar la empresa?","options":{"A":"Verificar los datos en busca de desequilibrios o disparidades entre grupos","B":"Ejecutar el modelo con la mayor frecuencia posible","C":"Evaluar el comportamiento del modelo para que la empresa pueda ofrecer transparencia a las partes interesadas","D":"Usar la m\xe9trica ROUGE para medir la calidad del texto generado","E":"Mantener el tiempo de inferencia del modelo dentro de l\xedmites aceptados"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Verificar los datos en busca de desequilibrios o disparidades ayuda a detectar y reducir el sesgo que podr\xeda afectar injustamente a algunos solicitantes, y evaluar el comportamiento del modelo permite a la empresa explicar sus decisiones y ofrecer transparencia a las partes interesadas. Ambas son pr\xe1cticas centrales de IA responsable para limitar el sesgo.\\nEjecutar el modelo con frecuencia y mantener el tiempo de inferencia dentro de l\xedmites son cuestiones operativas que no abordan el sesgo. La m\xe9trica ROUGE mide la superposici\xf3n del texto generado con el texto de referencia para la calidad de resumen y no detecta sesgo."},{"id":"aif-q052","question":"Un estudiante entrega ensayos copiando texto producido por una herramienta de IA generativa sin atribuci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 desaf\xedo de IA responsable ilustra esto?","options":{"A":"Toxicidad","B":"Alucinaciones","C":"Privacidad","D":"Plagio"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Reutilizar la salida de IA generativa como trabajo propio sin atribuci\xf3n es plagio, lo cual es una preocupaci\xf3n reconocida de IA responsable y de propiedad intelectual.\\nLa toxicidad se refiere a contenido da\xf1ino u ofensivo, las alucinaciones son salidas fabricadas pero que suenan plausibles y la privacidad se refiere al manejo de datos personales. Ninguna de estas describe copiar texto generado y presentarlo como trabajo original."},{"id":"aif-q057","question":"Una tienda instal\xf3 c\xe1maras y usa un modelo de ML para revisar las grabaciones en busca de posibles robos. El modelo se\xf1ala a personas de un grupo \xe9tnico con mucha m\xe1s frecuencia que a otros. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de sesgo est\xe1 influyendo en la salida del modelo?","options":{"A":"Sesgo de medici\xf3n","B":"Sesgo de muestreo","C":"Sesgo de confirmaci\xf3n","D":"Sesgo del observador"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El sesgo de muestreo surge cuando los datos de entrenamiento no representan a la poblaci\xf3n completa, lo que puede hacer que un modelo se\xf1ale a un grupo \xe9tnico con mucha m\xe1s frecuencia que a otros. El marcado sesgado apunta a datos de entrenamiento que subrepresentaron a algunos grupos.\\nEl sesgo de medici\xf3n proviene de errores sistem\xe1ticos en c\xf3mo se registran los datos. El sesgo de confirmaci\xf3n y el sesgo del observador se relacionan con c\xf3mo las personas interpretan o etiquetan los resultados, no con la representatividad de la muestra de entrenamiento."},{"id":"aif-q067","question":"Un hospital puso en servicio un modelo de detecci\xf3n de enfermedades en Amazon Bedrock. Para respetar las reglas de privacidad, el modelo nunca debe exponer detalles personales de pacientes en sus respuestas, y el personal debe recibir una notificaci\xf3n siempre que eso ocurra. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Usar Amazon Macie para escanear la salida del modelo en busca de datos sensibles y configurar alertas para posibles exposiciones","B":"Usar Amazon Bedrock Guardrails para filtrar el contenido y configurar alarmas de Amazon CloudWatch para notificaciones","C":"Configurar AWS CloudTrail para monitorear las respuestas del modelo y crear alertas para cualquier dato personal detectado","D":"Usar Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor para detectar desviaci\xf3n de datos y alertar cuando la calidad del modelo se degrade"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails puede filtrar la salida del modelo para que no se devuelva informaci\xf3n personal de pacientes, y las alarmas de Amazon CloudWatch pueden notificar al personal siempre que ocurra una violaci\xf3n de pol\xedticas.\\nAmazon Macie descubre datos sensibles en Amazon S3 pero no filtra las respuestas en vivo de un modelo. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a API en lugar de inspeccionar el contenido de las respuestas. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor detecta desviaci\xf3n de datos y problemas de calidad, no la divulgaci\xf3n de informaci\xf3n personal."},{"id":"aif-q071","question":"Una aseguradora planea implementar un modelo de lenguaje grande para automatizar el procesamiento de documentos y quiere proceder de forma responsable para evitar da\xf1os. \xbfQu\xe9 dos acciones debe tomar la empresa?","options":{"A":"Incluir m\xe9tricas de equidad al evaluar el modelo","B":"Ajustar el par\xe1metro de temperatura del modelo","C":"Modificar los datos de entrenamiento para reducir el sesgo","D":"Evitar el sobreajuste en los datos de entrenamiento","E":"Aplicar t\xe9cnicas de ingenier\xeda de prompts"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Incluir m\xe9tricas de equidad al evaluar el modelo ayuda a confirmar que trata los casos de manera equitativa, y modificar los datos de entrenamiento para reducir el sesgo aborda el sesgo en su origen. Ambas respaldan directamente el uso responsable y reducen el posible da\xf1o.\\nAjustar el par\xe1metro de temperatura y aplicar t\xe9cnicas de ingenier\xeda de prompts ajustan el estilo y la calidad de la salida, pero no abordan la equidad ni el sesgo. Evitar el sobreajuste mejora la generalizaci\xf3n, pero es un objetivo general de calidad del modelo en lugar de una medida que limite resultados sesgados o da\xf1inos."},{"id":"aif-q091","question":"Un equipo de investigaci\xf3n crea modelos de ML personalizados y comparte los artefactos del modelo con otros equipos, mientras conserva el c\xf3digo de entrenamiento y los datos. El equipo quiere una forma estandarizada de documentar y auditar posteriormente cada modelo que publica. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n debe usar el equipo?","options":{"A":"Escribir los detalles en documentos y almacenarlos en Amazon S3","B":"Hacer commit de los scripts de entrenamiento del modelo en un repositorio Git","C":"Crear Amazon SageMaker Model Cards que capturen los usos previstos y los detalles de entrenamiento e inferencia","D":"Usar AWS AI Service Cards para describir los modelos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards proporciona un lugar estandarizado para documentar los usos previstos de un modelo junto con sus detalles de entrenamiento e inferencia, lo que brinda al equipo un registro consistente y compartible que otros equipos pueden auditar posteriormente.\\nEscribir los detalles en documentos almacenados en Amazon S3 no es estructurado ni estandarizado. Hacer commit de los scripts de entrenamiento en un repositorio Git versiona el c\xf3digo en lugar de los metadatos del modelo. AWS AI Service Cards describen los servicios de IA administrados propios de AWS, no los modelos personalizados que crea el equipo."},{"id":"aif-q094","question":"Un banco quiere crear un sistema de ML para ayudar a su equipo de riesgos a decidir la asignaci\xf3n de pr\xe9stamos entre diferentes grupos demogr\xe1ficos. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer el banco para desarrollar un modelo sin sesgo?","options":{"A":"Reducir el tama\xf1o del conjunto de datos de entrenamiento","B":"Asegurarse de que las predicciones del modelo coincidan con los resultados hist\xf3ricos","C":"Crear un modelo separado para cada grupo demogr\xe1fico","D":"Medir el desequilibrio de clases en los datos de entrenamiento y ajustar el proceso de entrenamiento en consecuencia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Medir el desequilibrio de clases en los datos de entrenamiento y adaptar el proceso de entrenamiento, por ejemplo mediante remuestreo o pesos de clase, ayuda al modelo a tratar de forma equitativa a los grupos subrepresentados y reduce las predicciones sesgadas.\\nReducir el tama\xf1o del conjunto de datos elimina informaci\xf3n y puede empeorar el sesgo. Forzar que las predicciones coincidan con los resultados hist\xf3ricos puede trasladar la discriminaci\xf3n pasada al modelo. Crear un modelo separado por grupo demogr\xe1fico puede afianzar las disparidades y no aborda el desequilibrio dentro de los datos."},{"id":"aif-q100","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando una aplicaci\xf3n m\xf3vil que utiliza la c\xe1mara del tel\xe9fono para evaluar picaduras de insectos. Para entrenar su modelo de clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes, la empresa re\xfane una amplia variedad de fotos de picaduras que abarcan diferentes g\xe9neros, etnias y regiones. \xbfQu\xe9 principio de IA responsable refleja esto?","options":{"A":"Explicabilidad","B":"Transparencia","C":"Equidad","D":"Gobernanza"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Reunir fotos de picaduras de diferentes g\xe9neros, etnias y regiones tiene como objetivo evitar que el modelo funcione mejor para algunos grupos que para otros, lo que refleja el principio de equidad de la IA responsable.\\nLa explicabilidad y la transparencia se refieren a revelar c\xf3mo el modelo llega a sus resultados, y la gobernanza se refiere a pol\xedticas y supervisi\xf3n. Ninguno de estos describe el acto de reunir un conjunto de datos representativo y equilibrado para evitar resultados sesgados."},{"id":"aif-q101","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 desarrollando un modelo de ML para aprobaciones de pr\xe9stamos. Necesita detectar sesgo en el modelo y tambi\xe9n explicar las predicciones del modelo. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","B":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards","D":"AWS AI Service Cards"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify detecta sesgo tanto en los datos como en las predicciones del modelo y proporciona explicabilidad para esas predicciones, lo que cubre los dos requisitos para el modelo de aprobaci\xf3n de pr\xe9stamos.\\nAmazon SageMaker Data Wrangler prepara y transforma datos. Amazon SageMaker Model Cards documenta los detalles del modelo para transparencia, pero no ejecuta an\xe1lisis de sesgo. AWS AI Service Cards describen los servicios de IA administrados por AWS en lugar del modelo propio de un cliente."},{"id":"aif-q111","question":"Un banco est\xe1 creando una soluci\xf3n de decisi\xf3n de pr\xe9stamos sobre un modelo fundacional. Para auditor\xeda y supervisi\xf3n, el banco necesita que las decisiones del modelo sean explicables. \xbfQu\xe9 factor afecta m\xe1s a cu\xe1n explicables son esas decisiones?","options":{"A":"Tiempo de entrenamiento","B":"Complejidad del modelo","C":"N\xfamero de hiperpar\xe1metros","D":"Tiempo de despliegue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La complejidad del modelo es lo que m\xe1s afecta a la explicabilidad: los modelos m\xe1s simples, como los modelos lineales o los \xe1rboles de decisi\xf3n, son m\xe1s f\xe1ciles de interpretar, mientras que los modelos altamente complejos, como las redes neuronales profundas, se comportan como cajas negras cuyas decisiones son dif\xedciles de explicar con fines de auditor\xeda.\\nEl tiempo de entrenamiento, el n\xfamero de hiperpar\xe1metros y el tiempo de despliegue se relacionan con c\xf3mo se construye y opera el modelo, pero no determinan cu\xe1n interpretables son sus decisiones."},{"id":"aif-q113","question":"Una empresa quiere una aplicaci\xf3n de puntuaci\xf3n de clientes potenciales (lead scoring) que permita a los empleados ver y ajustar el peso asignado a cada variable de entrada seg\xfan su experiencia en el dominio. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de modelo de ML admite este requisito?","options":{"A":"Modelo de K vecinos m\xe1s cercanos (k-NN)","B":"Modelo de regresi\xf3n log\xedstica","C":"Modelo de aprendizaje profundo construido sobre componentes principales","D":"Red neuronal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un modelo de regresi\xf3n log\xedstica asigna un peso expl\xedcito (coeficiente) a cada variable de entrada, de modo que los empleados pueden ver c\xf3mo contribuye cada caracter\xedstica y ajustar esos pesos usando su experiencia en el dominio. Esta interpretabilidad coincide con el requisito.\\nK vecinos m\xe1s cercanos hace predicciones a partir de puntos de datos vecinos y no tiene pesos por caracter\xedstica que ajustar. Una red neuronal y un modelo de aprendizaje profundo construido sobre componentes principales son opacos y no exponen pesos de caracter\xedsticas simples y ajustables."},{"id":"aif-q122","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica puede usar una empresa para reducir el sesgo y la toxicidad en una aplicaci\xf3n de IA generativa durante la etapa de posprocesamiento del ciclo de vida de ML?","options":{"A":"Aumento de datos","B":"Humano en el bucle (human-in-the-loop)","C":"Ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas","D":"Entrenamiento adversario"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Human-in-the-loop (humano en el bucle) a\xf1ade revisi\xf3n humana durante la etapa de posprocesamiento para que los resultados sesgados o t\xf3xicos se puedan detectar y corregir antes de que lleguen a los usuarios, lo que reduce el sesgo y la toxicidad en la aplicaci\xf3n de IA generativa.\\nEl aumento de datos y la ingenier\xeda de caracter\xedsticas act\xfaan sobre los datos antes o durante el entrenamiento, y el entrenamiento adversario refuerza el modelo durante el entrenamiento. Ninguno de estos funciona como una salvaguarda de posprocesamiento sobre los resultados generados."},{"id":"aif-q141","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es un beneficio de usar Amazon SageMaker Model Cards para documentar modelos de IA?","options":{"A":"Reducir los requisitos computacionales generales de un modelo","B":"Estandarizar la informaci\xf3n sobre el prop\xf3sito, el rendimiento y las limitaciones de un modelo","C":"Proporcionar un resumen visualmente atractivo de las capacidades de un modelo","D":"Almacenar f\xedsicamente modelos con fines de archivo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards estandariza la documentaci\xf3n del prop\xf3sito, el rendimiento y las limitaciones de un modelo, lo que respalda la transparencia, la gobernanza y un seguimiento m\xe1s sencillo de los modelos a lo largo del tiempo.\\nReducir los requisitos computacionales y almacenar f\xedsicamente modelos para archivo no son funciones de Model Cards. Proporcionar un resumen visualmente atractivo es un detalle de presentaci\xf3n en lugar del beneficio principal de la documentaci\xf3n estandarizada."},{"id":"aif-q144","question":"Una empresa cre\xf3 un sistema de IA para la selecci\xf3n de curr\xedculos entrenado con un gran conjunto de datos de curr\xedculos que no representaba a todos los grupos demogr\xe1ficos. \xbfQu\xe9 dimensi\xf3n central de la IA responsable involucra esta situaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Transparencia","B":"Equidad","C":"Privacidad y seguridad","D":"Explicabilidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Entrenar con un conjunto de datos que no representa a todos los grupos demogr\xe1ficos puede generar predicciones sesgadas, lo cual es una preocupaci\xf3n de equidad. La equidad es la dimensi\xf3n de la IA responsable centrada en resultados equitativos entre grupos.\\nLa transparencia y la explicabilidad se refieren a revelar c\xf3mo toma decisiones el modelo, y la privacidad y la seguridad se refieren a proteger los datos. Ninguna de estas describe el sesgo introducido por un conjunto de datos de entrenamiento no representativo."},{"id":"aif-q149","question":"Una empresa despliega modelos de IA/ML en AWS y quiere ofrecer transparencia sobre c\xf3mo toman decisiones los modelos y proporcionar explicaciones de sus resultados. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Lex","B":"Amazon Rekognition","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards documenta detalles clave como el prop\xf3sito de un modelo, los datos de entrenamiento, el rendimiento y las limitaciones, lo que brinda a las partes interesadas transparencia sobre c\xf3mo toma decisiones el modelo y ayuda a explicar sus resultados.\\nAmazon Lex crea interfaces conversacionales, Amazon Rekognition analiza im\xe1genes y video, y Amazon Comprehend realiza procesamiento de lenguaje natural. Ninguno de estos est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para documentar la transparencia y explicabilidad de un modelo."},{"id":"aif-q165","question":"Una empresa colabora con varios institutos de investigaci\xf3n para desarrollar un modelo de IA. Necesita documentaci\xf3n estandarizada que rastree las versiones del modelo y registre c\xf3mo se desarroll\xf3 el modelo. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple con estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Rastrear los cambios del modelo mediante un repositorio de Git","B":"Rastrear los cambios del modelo mediante Amazon Comprehend","C":"Rastrear los cambios del modelo mediante Amazon SageMaker Model Cards","D":"Rastrear los cambios del modelo mediante Amazon S3"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards proporciona un marco estandarizado para documentar los detalles y las versiones del modelo, junto con un registro de c\xf3mo se desarroll\xf3 el modelo, lo que satisface la necesidad de documentaci\xf3n compartida y auditable entre colaboradores.\\nUn repositorio de Git versiona c\xf3digo en lugar de documentaci\xf3n estandarizada del modelo. Amazon Comprehend realiza procesamiento de lenguaje natural y Amazon S3 almacena objetos sin ninguna estructura de documentaci\xf3n del modelo."},{"id":"aif-q172","question":"Un hospital construy\xf3 un sistema de IA que brinda a los pacientes recomendaciones de tratamiento personalizadas. El sistema debe explicar el razonamiento detr\xe1s de cada recomendaci\xf3n y hacer que esa informaci\xf3n sea accesible tanto para m\xe9dicos como para pacientes. \xbfQu\xe9 principio de dise\xf1o centrado en el humano refleja esto?","options":{"A":"Gobernanza de datos","B":"Privacidad y seguridad","C":"Explicabilidad","D":"Equidad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Explicar el razonamiento detr\xe1s de cada recomendaci\xf3n de tratamiento y hacer que esa informaci\xf3n sea accesible para m\xe9dicos y pacientes refleja la explicabilidad, un principio de dise\xf1o centrado en el humano que se enfoca en hacer comprensibles las decisiones de IA.\\nLa privacidad y la seguridad se refieren a proteger los datos de los pacientes, la equidad se refiere a resultados equitativos entre grupos y la gobernanza de datos se refiere a pol\xedticas para gestionar datos. Ninguno de estos describe revelar la raz\xf3n detr\xe1s de las recomendaciones del sistema."},{"id":"aif-q187","question":"Una empresa de atenci\xf3n m\xe9dica quiere usar IA generativa para responder preguntas m\xe9dicas de los pacientes. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS debe usar para respaldar la IA responsable para la aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Amazon Rekognition","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails permite a la empresa definir e imponer controles de seguridad, contenido y sesgo sobre las respuestas de IA generativa, lo que respalda la IA responsable para una aplicaci\xf3n que responde preguntas m\xe9dicas de pacientes.\\nAmazon Inspector escanea en busca de vulnerabilidades de software, Amazon Rekognition analiza im\xe1genes y video, y AWS Trusted Advisor verifica cuentas con respecto a las mejores pr\xe1cticas. Ninguno de estos impone controles de IA responsable sobre el contenido generado."},{"id":"aif-q203","question":"Una empresa minorista quiere construir un modelo de recomendaci\xf3n de productos usando pr\xe1cticas responsables. \xbfQu\xe9 pr\xe1ctica debe aplicar al recopilar datos para reducir el sesgo en el modelo?","options":{"A":"Usar datos solo de los clientes m\xe1s activos de la empresa","B":"Recopilar datos de un \xfanico segmento de clientes de alto valor","C":"Asegurarse de que los datos sean representativos de toda la base de clientes de la empresa","D":"Usar el conjunto de datos m\xe1s peque\xf1o que permita entrenar el modelo r\xe1pidamente"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Recopilar datos que sean representativos de toda la base de clientes de la empresa reduce el sesgo hacia cualquier grupo \xfanico y ayuda al modelo de recomendaci\xf3n a tratar a todos los clientes de manera equitativa, que es la pr\xe1ctica responsable para limitar el sesgo.\\nUsar datos solo de los clientes m\xe1s activos o de un \xfanico segmento de alto valor produce un conjunto de datos sesgado que aumenta el sesgo. Usar el conjunto de datos m\xe1s peque\xf1o que permita entrenar r\xe1pidamente corre el riesgo de subrepresentar grupos y no aborda la equidad."},{"id":"aif-q207","question":"Una empresa implement\xf3 un modelo de IA generativa para segmentaci\xf3n de clientes, y ha estado en producci\xf3n durante mucho tiempo. El equipo ahora nota respuestas inconsistentes y quiere evaluar el modelo para detectar sesgo y deriva. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS cumple con este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","B":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","D":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor recopila datos de forma continua de un modelo implementado y detecta deriva de datos, deriva de concepto y cambios en el sesgo a lo largo del tiempo, por lo que puede poner de manifiesto las inconsistencias que aparecen en el modelo de segmentaci\xf3n de larga duraci\xf3n.\\nAmazon SageMaker Clarify mide el sesgo y la explicabilidad en un momento dado en lugar de monitorear producci\xf3n de forma continua. Amazon SageMaker Feature Store gestiona caracter\xedsticas y Amazon SageMaker Model Cards documentan los detalles del modelo; ninguno monitorea un modelo en busca de deriva."},{"id":"aif-q222","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 agregando IA a su proceso de reclutamiento y contrataci\xf3n y quiere reducir los riesgos de sesgo y mantener equitativas las decisiones de contrataci\xf3n. \xbfEn qu\xe9 dos dimensiones centrales de la IA responsable debe enfocarse la empresa?","options":{"A":"Equidad","B":"Flexibilidad","C":"Tolerancia","D":"C\xf3digo abierto","E":"Transparencia"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"La equidad mantiene los resultados de contrataci\xf3n equitativos entre grupos demogr\xe1ficos, lo que mitiga directamente el sesgo, y la transparencia hace claras las decisiones de la IA para que puedan explicarse y auditarse. Ambas son dimensiones centrales de la IA responsable.\\nLa flexibilidad, la tolerancia y el c\xf3digo abierto no son dimensiones centrales de la IA responsable para garantizar decisiones de contrataci\xf3n equitativas y responsables."},{"id":"aif-q225","question":"Una empresa de alimentos est\xe1 recopilando un conjunto de datos para predecir las preferencias alimentarias de los clientes y quiere que las preferencias de todos los grupos demogr\xe1ficos est\xe9n representadas en los datos. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica del conjunto de datos describe esto?","options":{"A":"Exactitud","B":"Diversidad","C":"Sesgo de recencia","D":"Fiabilidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Asegurarse de que el conjunto de datos represente a todos los grupos demogr\xe1ficos aborda su diversidad, de modo que el modelo pueda aprender de una amplia variedad de preferencias de los clientes.\\nLa exactitud y la fiabilidad se refieren a la correcci\xf3n y la consistencia de los datos en lugar de qu\xe9 tan ampliamente representan a los grupos. El sesgo de recencia es una falla en la que los datos recientes tienen un peso excesivo, lo cual no es lo que describe incluir todos los datos demogr\xe1ficos."},{"id":"aif-q231","question":"Un practicante de IA est\xe1 construyendo un modelo de ML y quiere brindar a las partes interesadas transparencia y explicabilidad sobre sus predicciones. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple con este requisito?","options":{"A":"Presentar los valores Shapley del modelo","B":"Proporcionar la medida de exactitud del modelo","C":"Proporcionar la matriz de confusi\xf3n del modelo","D":"Proporcionar un endpoint de inferencia seguro para el modelo"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Los valores Shapley cuantifican cu\xe1nto contribuye cada caracter\xedstica a una predicci\xf3n individual, lo que brinda a las partes interesadas transparencia y explicabilidad sobre por qu\xe9 el modelo produjo un resultado determinado.\\nLa exactitud y la matriz de confusi\xf3n miden el rendimiento general del modelo, pero no explican predicciones individuales. Un endpoint de inferencia seguro se refiere a c\xf3mo se implementa y se accede al modelo, no a c\xf3mo se explican sus predicciones."},{"id":"aif-q252","question":"Un banco est\xe1 creando una aplicaci\xf3n de IA generativa para decisiones de aprobaci\xf3n de pr\xe9stamos y necesita que los resultados sean responsables y justos. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Usar un modelo de aprendizaje profundo con muchas capas ocultas","B":"Revisar los datos de entrenamiento en busca de sesgos e incluir datos de todos los grupos demogr\xe1ficos","C":"Mantener en secreto el proceso de toma de decisiones del modelo para proteger los algoritmos propietarios","D":"Monitorear continuamente el modelo con un \xfanico conjunto de datos de prueba est\xe1tico"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Revisar los datos de entrenamiento en busca de sesgos e incluir datos de todos los grupos demogr\xe1ficos reduce los resultados sesgados en la fuente, lo cual es esencial para una aplicaci\xf3n de aprobaci\xf3n de pr\xe9stamos responsable y justa.\\nUsar un modelo de aprendizaje profundo con muchas capas ocultas puede reducir la explicabilidad y no aborda la equidad. Mantener en secreto el proceso de decisi\xf3n socava la transparencia. Monitorear con un \xfanico conjunto de datos de prueba est\xe1tico no detecta ni corrige el sesgo en los datos."},{"id":"aif-q255","question":"Una empresa tiene un modelo de ML y quiere entender c\xf3mo llega el modelo a sus predicciones. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9rmino describe la comprensi\xf3n de las predicciones de un modelo?","options":{"A":"Interpretabilidad del modelo","B":"Entrenamiento del modelo","C":"Rendimiento del modelo","D":"Interoperabilidad del modelo"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La interpretabilidad del modelo se refiere a entender c\xf3mo llega un modelo a sus predicciones, que es exactamente lo que la empresa quiere.\\nEl entrenamiento del modelo es el proceso de ajustar el modelo a los datos, el rendimiento del modelo mide qu\xe9 tan bien predice y la interoperabilidad del modelo se refiere a trabajar entre sistemas. Ninguno de estos t\xe9rminos describe la comprensi\xf3n del razonamiento detr\xe1s de las predicciones."},{"id":"aif-q262","question":"Un banco usa un modelo de IA generativa para establecer l\xedmites de cr\xe9dito para nuevos clientes y quiere hacer m\xe1s transparente el proceso de toma de decisiones del modelo para esos clientes. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Reemplazar el modelo de ML con un sistema basado en reglas","B":"Aplicar t\xe9cnicas de IA explicable para mostrar qu\xe9 factores influyeron en la decisi\xf3n del modelo","C":"Crear una interfaz de usuario interactiva que brinde a los clientes explicaciones t\xe9cnicas detalladas del sistema","D":"Aumentar la precisi\xf3n del modelo para reducir la necesidad de transparencia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aplicar t\xe9cnicas de IA explicable expone los factores espec\xedficos que influyeron en cada decisi\xf3n de l\xedmite de cr\xe9dito, lo que hace transparente el razonamiento del modelo de IA generativa para los clientes.\\nReemplazar el modelo con un sistema basado en reglas cambia el enfoque en lugar de explicar el modelo. Una interfaz de usuario interactiva con explicaciones t\xe9cnicas detalladas no necesariamente revela qu\xe9 factores impulsaron una decisi\xf3n. Aumentar la precisi\xf3n mejora las predicciones, pero no hace transparente el proceso de decisi\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q267","question":"Un profesional de IA quiere evaluar modelos de ML y proporcionar explicaciones de sus predicciones a clientes y partes interesadas. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Quick","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify proporciona explicaciones de atribuci\xf3n de caracter\xedsticas para las predicciones de modelos, por lo que el profesional puede generar y compartir informes de interpretabilidad con clientes y partes interesadas.\\nAmazon Quick es un servicio de inteligencia empresarial, Amazon Comprehend realiza procesamiento de lenguaje natural y AWS Trusted Advisor verifica las cuentas seg\xfan las mejores pr\xe1cticas. Ninguno de estos explica las predicciones de modelos de ML."},{"id":"aif-q279","question":"Una empresa usa Amazon SageMaker para implementar un modelo que detecta si las publicaciones de redes sociales contienen ciertos temas. La empresa necesita mostrar c\xf3mo diferentes caracter\xedsticas de entrada influyen en el comportamiento del modelo. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de SageMaker cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"SageMaker Ground Truth","B":"SageMaker Clarify","C":"SageMaker Feature Store","D":"SageMaker Canvas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify ofrece explicabilidad de modelos, incluida la atribuci\xf3n de caracter\xedsticas, por lo que puede mostrar c\xf3mo diferentes caracter\xedsticas de entrada influyen en el comportamiento del modelo implementado.\\nSageMaker Ground Truth etiqueta datos, SageMaker Feature Store administra caracter\xedsticas y SageMaker Canvas crea modelos sin c\xf3digo. Ninguno de estos explica c\xf3mo las caracter\xedsticas de entrada impulsan el comportamiento del modelo."},{"id":"aif-q287","question":"Una empresa cre\xf3 un modelo de ML que aprueba o rechaza solicitudes de pr\xe9stamos. Para cumplir con los requisitos regulatorios, su toma de decisiones debe ser transparente y explicable, y la empresa debe documentar el proceso para auditor\xedas. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Textract","B":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards","C":"AWS CloudFormation","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards documenta el uso previsto de un modelo, su rendimiento y los detalles de la toma de decisiones, lo que proporciona la transparencia, la explicabilidad y el registro de auditor\xeda que el modelo de pr\xe9stamos debe cumplir para fines regulatorios.\\nAmazon Textract extrae texto de documentos, AWS CloudFormation aprovisiona infraestructura y Amazon Comprehend realiza procesamiento de lenguaje natural. Ninguno de estos documenta la toma de decisiones de un modelo para transparencia y auditor\xeda."},{"id":"aif-q292","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 dos caracter\xedsticas de Amazon Bedrock ayudan con el filtrado de contenido y la seguridad?","options":{"A":"Guardrails para filtrar contenido","B":"Opciones de personalizaci\xf3n de modelos","C":"Capacidades de administraci\xf3n de prompts","D":"Detecci\xf3n de marcas de agua para contenido generado por IA","E":"Integraci\xf3n con bases de conocimiento"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails filtra contenido da\xf1ino o no deseado tanto en los prompts como en las respuestas, y la detecci\xf3n de marcas de agua identifica contenido generado por IA, lo que respalda la transparencia y ayuda a prevenir el uso indebido. Juntas, estas dos caracter\xedsticas abordan el filtrado de contenido y la seguridad.\\nLa personalizaci\xf3n de modelos, la administraci\xf3n de prompts y la integraci\xf3n con bases de conocimiento mejoran la capacidad, la reutilizaci\xf3n y la relevancia de un modelo, pero ninguna de ellas filtra contenido ni proporciona un control de seguridad."},{"id":"aif-q053","question":"Una empresa farmac\xe9utica entrenar\xe1 su propio modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) con datos privados y quiere limitar la huella de carbono del entrenamiento. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de instancia de Amazon EC2 tiene el MENOR impacto ambiental para entrenar LLM?","options":{"A":"Serie P de Amazon EC2","B":"Serie Trn de Amazon EC2","C":"Serie G de Amazon EC2","D":"Serie C de Amazon EC2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las instancias de la serie Trn de Amazon EC2 funcionan con AWS Trainium, que est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para el entrenamiento energ\xe9ticamente eficiente de modelos grandes, por lo que tiene el menor impacto ambiental de las opciones enumeradas.\\nLa serie C son instancias de CPU de uso general y optimizadas para computaci\xf3n que no est\xe1n especializadas en el entrenamiento eficiente de LLM. Las series G y P son instancias de GPU optimizadas para el rendimiento en lugar de la eficiencia energ\xe9tica, por lo que tienen una mayor huella ambiental para esta carga de trabajo."},{"id":"aif-q160","question":"Una plataforma de comunidad de videojuegos usa Amazon Bedrock Guardrails para filtrar entradas de usuario y salidas del modelo da\xf1inas. \xbfQu\xe9 dos categor\xedas de contenido pueden filtrar los guardrails?","options":{"A":"Pol\xedtica","B":"Odio","C":"Juegos de azar","D":"Violencia"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails incluye filtros de contenido configurables para categor\xedas da\xf1inas como Odio y Violencia, que se pueden aplicar tanto a las entradas de usuario como a las salidas del modelo.\\nPol\xedtica y Juegos de azar no son categor\xedas est\xe1ndar de filtros de contenido de Bedrock Guardrails, por lo que los guardrails no filtran por ellas."},{"id":"aif-q192","question":"Un operador de foro quiere evitar que los usuarios publiquen contenido discriminatorio y planea usar Amazon Bedrock. \xbfC\xf3mo puede el operador usar Amazon Bedrock para cumplir este requisito?","options":{"A":"Restringir las conversaciones de los usuarios a un conjunto de temas predefinidos","B":"Bloquear las interacciones que se relacionen con temas predefinidos no permitidos","C":"Permitir que los usuarios interact\xfaen seg\xfan sus preferencias personales","D":"Ofrecer una variedad de respuestas para que los usuarios elijan"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails permite al operador definir temas o categor\xedas no permitidos, como contenido discriminatorio u odioso, y bloquear autom\xe1ticamente las entradas y salidas que entren en ellos, lo que evita que se publique ese contenido.\\nRestringir las conversaciones a temas permitidos predefinidos es un control diferente y limitar\xeda el uso normal en lugar de bloquear contenido da\xf1ino espec\xedfico. Permitir que los usuarios interact\xfaen seg\xfan sus preferencias y ofrecer una variedad de respuestas no hace nada para filtrar contenido discriminatorio."},{"id":"aif-q037","question":"Un equipo entrena un modelo que genera im\xe1genes de personas en diversos trabajos, pero los datos desequilibrados desv\xedan ciertos atributos e introducen sesgo. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica aborda esto?","options":{"A":"Monitorear la distribuci\xf3n de clases despu\xe9s del despliegue","B":"Aumentar los datos para equilibrar las clases subrepresentadas","C":"Entrenar durante m\xe1s \xe9pocas con los datos desequilibrados existentes","D":"Aplicar un filtrado de salida m\xe1s estricto a las im\xe1genes generadas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aumentar los datos para equilibrar las clases subrepresentadas reduce la desviaci\xf3n que causa el sesgo, abordando el problema en su origen.\\nMonitorear solo detecta el desequilibrio, entrenar durante m\xe1s \xe9pocas con los mismos datos desequilibrados refuerza el sesgo y el filtrado de salida examina los resultados sin corregir el conjunto de datos subyacente."},{"id":"aif-q054","question":"Una empresa crea una aplicaci\xf3n que genera cuentos infantiles con Amazon Bedrock y debe mantener el contenido apropiado para usuarios j\xf3venes. \xbfQu\xe9 funci\xf3n es adecuada?","options":{"A":"Agents for Amazon Bedrock","B":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Amazon Bedrock Prompt Management","D":"Amazon Bedrock playgrounds"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails aplica filtros de contenido y restricciones de temas a la salida del modelo, manteniendo los cuentos generados apropiados para ni\xf1os.\\nAgents orquesta tareas, Prompt Management versiona prompts y playgrounds es una consola de experimentaci\xf3n; ninguno aplica seguridad de contenido."},{"id":"aif-q123","question":"Una auditor\xeda encontr\xf3 que el modelo de aprobaci\xf3n de pr\xe9stamos ajustado de un banco favorece a un grupo demogr\xe1fico. \xbfCu\xe1l es la soluci\xf3n M\xc1S rentable?","options":{"A":"Usar Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) con el modelo ajustado","B":"Agregar datos de entrenamiento m\xe1s diversos y representativos y volver a ajustar","C":"Preentrenar un modelo completamente nuevo con datos m\xe1s diversos","D":"Aplicar Amazon Bedrock Guardrails para bloquear salidas sesgadas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Agregar datos m\xe1s diversos y representativos y volver a ajustar el modelo existente le ense\xf1a a tratar a los grupos de manera justa, lo que constituye la soluci\xf3n m\xe1s rentable.\\nRAG agrega contexto externo, pero no corrige el sesgo aprendido; preentrenar un modelo nuevo es mucho m\xe1s costoso, y Guardrails filtra contenido no seguro en lugar de abordar el sesgo demogr\xe1fico en las decisiones."},{"id":"aif-q169","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n de bolet\xedn informativo con Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) en Amazon Bedrock est\xe1 mostrando contenido con inclinaci\xf3n pol\xedtica. \xbfQu\xe9 funci\xf3n de Amazon Bedrock Guardrails puede filtrar esto?","options":{"A":"Filtros de palabras","B":"Filtros de contenido","C":"Temas denegados","D":"Filtros de informaci\xf3n sensible"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los temas denegados permiten definir asuntos, como pol\xedtica, que el modelo debe evitar, y Amazon Bedrock aplica esto durante la generaci\xf3n para filtrar contenido con inclinaci\xf3n pol\xedtica.\\nLos filtros de palabras bloquean solo t\xe9rminos espec\xedficos, los filtros de contenido se dirigen a categor\xedas como odio o violencia, y los filtros de informaci\xf3n sensible redactan datos como PII."},{"id":"aif-q249","question":"El chatbot de una tienda en l\xednea debe filtrar contenido da\xf1ino tanto de los prompts de usuario como de sus respuestas. \xbfQu\xe9 funci\xf3n de Amazon Bedrock es adecuada?","options":{"A":"Amazon Bedrock Agents","B":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Amazon Bedrock Knowledge Bases","D":"Modelos personalizados de Amazon Bedrock"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails filtra contenido da\xf1ino o inapropiado tanto en los prompts de entrada como en las respuestas del modelo, proporcionando las protecciones que necesita el chatbot.\\nAmazon Bedrock Agents orquesta tareas, Knowledge Bases recupera contexto y los modelos personalizados adaptan el comportamiento; ninguno filtra contenido da\xf1ino."},{"id":"aif-q297","question":"Una empresa usa modelos de lenguaje grandes (LLMs) para aplicaciones de tutor\xeda y necesita protecciones configurables que apliquen reglas de seguridad est\xe1ndar con el MENOR esfuerzo. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n es adecuada?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","B":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation","D":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails proporciona protecciones configurables que aplican reglas de seguridad a las entradas y salidas de los LLM con poco esfuerzo de configuraci\xf3n.\\nSageMaker Clarify detecta sesgo, Amazon Bedrock Model Evaluation eval\xfaa modelos y SageMaker JumpStart ofrece modelos preentrenados; ninguno aplica reglas de seguridad configurables."},{"id":"aif-q311","question":"Un modelo tiene un buen rendimiento con los datos de entrenamiento pero un rendimiento deficiente con los datos de evaluaci\xf3n. \xbfCu\xe1l es la causa m\xe1s probable?","options":{"A":"El modelo est\xe1 subajustado","B":"El modelo est\xe1 sobreajustado","C":"Hay fuga de datos entre los conjuntos de entrenamiento y prueba","D":"El modelo necesita m\xe1s \xe9pocas de entrenamiento"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El sobreajuste ocurre cuando un modelo aprende los datos de entrenamiento con demasiada exactitud, incluido el ruido, por lo que tiene un buen rendimiento con los datos de entrenamiento pero no logra generalizar a datos de evaluaci\xf3n no vistos.\\nUn modelo subajustado tiene un rendimiento deficiente incluso con los datos de entrenamiento, la fuga de datos tiende a inflar el rendimiento de prueba en lugar de reducirlo, y a\xf1adir m\xe1s \xe9pocas empeorar\xeda el sobreajuste en lugar de explicarlo."},{"id":"aif-q039","question":"Un banco est\xe1 adaptando un modelo fundacional (FM) para respaldar sus decisiones de pr\xe9stamo. Los reguladores exigen que el modelo sea transparente y explicable. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Configurar la seguridad y el cumplimiento con Amazon Inspector","B":"Proteger y cifrar los datos de entrenamiento con Amazon Macie","C":"Generar m\xe9tricas, informes y explicaciones de ejemplo con Amazon SageMaker Clarify","D":"Recopilar m\xe1s datos y a\xf1adir etiquetas personalizadas con Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify admite la transparencia y la explicabilidad al generar m\xe9tricas, informes y explicaciones de ejemplo que muestran c\xf3mo un modelo llega a sus decisiones, lo que ayuda a cumplir los requisitos regulatorios.\\nAmazon Inspector analiza vulnerabilidades de software y Amazon Macie protege datos sensibles, ninguna de las cuales explica el comportamiento del modelo. Recopilar m\xe1s datos y a\xf1adir etiquetas con Amazon Rekognition aborda el etiquetado de im\xe1genes, no la explicabilidad del modelo."},{"id":"aif-q060","question":"Una empresa ha creado un chatbot que responde preguntas en lenguaje natural devolviendo im\xe1genes. Quiere asegurarse de que el chatbot nunca devuelva im\xe1genes inapropiadas o no deseadas. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Reentrenar el modelo con un gran conjunto de datos p\xfablico","B":"Automatizar la recopilaci\xf3n de comentarios de los usuarios","C":"Integrar API de moderaci\xf3n de contenido","D":"Llevar a cabo la validaci\xf3n del modelo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las API de moderaci\xf3n de contenido examinan autom\xe1ticamente las im\xe1genes que devolver\xeda el chatbot y, luego, detectan y bloquean contenido inapropiado, inseguro o no deseado antes de que llegue al usuario, por lo que integrarlas aplica directamente el requisito de que el chatbot nunca sirva esas im\xe1genes.\\nReentrenar el modelo con un gran conjunto de datos p\xfablico no a\xf1ade ning\xfan filtrado y podr\xeda incluso introducir m\xe1s contenido no deseado. Llevar a cabo la validaci\xf3n del modelo comprueba que el modelo se comporta como se espera durante el desarrollo, pero no filtra respuestas individuales en tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n. Automatizar la recopilaci\xf3n de comentarios de los usuarios re\xfane informaci\xf3n para mejorar el sistema con el tiempo, pero no hace nada para bloquear im\xe1genes inapropiadas a medida que se sirven."},{"id":"aif-q171","question":"Un minorista de electr\xf3nica ha creado un asistente de IA que responde preguntas de los clientes usando sus manuales de productos. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque es m\xe1s probable que aumente la confianza de los clientes en las respuestas del asistente?","options":{"A":"Mostrar la puntuaci\xf3n de confianza del modelo con cada respuesta","B":"Darle al asistente un avatar rob\xf3tico en pantalla","C":"Enlazar a las secciones del manual del producto que respaldan cada respuesta","D":"Ajustar el asistente para que coincida con el tono de voz de la marca"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enlazar a las secciones espec\xedficas del manual del producto que respaldan cada respuesta permite a los clientes verificar la informaci\xf3n por s\xed mismos, lo que genera confianza al fundamentar la respuesta en una fuente autorizada y citable en lugar de depender \xfanicamente de la salida del modelo.\\nMostrar la puntuaci\xf3n de confianza del modelo expone un valor interno que los clientes no est\xe1n capacitados para interpretar, y una puntuaci\xf3n alta en una respuesta incorrecta los inducir\xeda a error en lugar de tranquilizarlos. Darle al asistente un avatar rob\xf3tico en pantalla es una elecci\xf3n de presentaci\xf3n que indica que el agente no es humano, pero no dice nada sobre la exactitud de sus respuestas. Ajustar el asistente para que coincida con el tono de voz de la marca mejora la coherencia y el estilo, pero no ofrece a los clientes ninguna forma de confirmar que la informaci\xf3n en s\xed sea correcta."},{"id":"aif-q389","question":"Una editorial utiliza un modelo de IA generativa para crear ilustraciones para sus libros. La empresa descubre que algunas im\xe1genes generadas se parecen mucho a obras protegidas por derechos de autor de otras editoriales. \xbfQu\xe9 preocupaci\xf3n de IA responsable representa esto?","options":{"A":"Alucinaci\xf3n del modelo","B":"Infracci\xf3n de propiedad intelectual","C":"Deriva de datos","D":"Sobreajuste"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cuando un modelo de IA generativa produce resultados que se parecen mucho a obras protegidas por derechos de autor, plantea el riesgo de infracci\xf3n de propiedad intelectual, que es un riesgo legal de trabajar con IA generativa que las empresas deben abordar mediante una curaci\xf3n de datos y una revisi\xf3n de resultados adecuadas.\\nLa alucinaci\xf3n del modelo se refiere a generar contenido plausible pero f\xe1cticamente incorrecto, no a reproducir material existente protegido por derechos de autor. La deriva de datos describe cambios en los patrones de los datos de entrada a lo largo del tiempo. El sobreajuste significa que un modelo memoriza los datos de entrenamiento con demasiada exactitud y generaliza mal, lo cual es un problema t\xe9cnico diferente de la infracci\xf3n de propiedad intelectual."},{"id":"aif-q390","question":"Un banco despliega un chatbot de IA generativa que proporciona orientaci\xf3n financiera incorrecta a varios clientes, lo que genera quejas y cobertura de prensa negativa. \xbfQu\xe9 riesgo legal de la IA generativa est\xe1 experimentando el banco?","options":{"A":"Sesgo de muestreo en los datos de entrenamiento","B":"P\xe9rdida de confianza de los clientes","C":"Subajuste del modelo","D":"Mayor latencia de inferencia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La p\xe9rdida de confianza de los clientes es un riesgo legal reconocido del despliegue de IA generativa, en el que los resultados inexactos o da\xf1inos erosionan la confianza en la organizaci\xf3n, dan lugar a quejas de los clientes y pueden provocar da\xf1os reputacionales y escrutinio regulatorio.\\nEl sesgo de muestreo es un problema de calidad de los datos que puede causar predicciones injustas, pero no es el riesgo legal que se experimenta aqu\xed. El subajuste del modelo significa que el modelo es demasiado simple para aprender patrones, lo cual es un problema t\xe9cnico de entrenamiento. El aumento de la latencia de inferencia es una preocupaci\xf3n de rendimiento no relacionada con la confianza de los clientes ni con el riesgo legal."},{"id":"aif-q391","question":"Una organizaci\xf3n de atenci\xf3n m\xe9dica utiliza un modelo de ML para se\xf1alar resultados de laboratorio anormales. Para garantizar pr\xe1cticas de IA responsable, quiere que profesionales m\xe9dicos revisen cualquier predicci\xf3n sobre la que el modelo tenga incertidumbre antes de que el resultado se env\xede a los pacientes. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Clarify","B":"Amazon Augmented AI (Amazon A2I)","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","D":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Augmented AI (Amazon A2I) permite flujos de trabajo de revisi\xf3n humana en los que las predicciones por debajo de un umbral de confianza se enrutan a revisores calificados, de modo que los profesionales m\xe9dicos pueden verificar resultados inciertos antes de que lleguen a los pacientes, lo que respalda la IA responsable mediante la supervisi\xf3n humana.\\nAmazon SageMaker Clarify detecta sesgos y explica las predicciones, pero no enruta predicciones inciertas a revisores humanos. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor detecta deriva en modelos implementados en lugar de permitir la revisi\xf3n humana de predicciones individuales. Amazon Bedrock Guardrails filtra contenido de modelos de IA generativa, pero no crea bucles de revisi\xf3n humana para predicciones de ML tradicionales."},{"id":"aif-q392","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un riesgo legal que las organizaciones deben considerar al implementar aplicaciones de IA generativa para los clientes?","options":{"A":"Tiempo reducido de entrenamiento del modelo","B":"Mayor utilizaci\xf3n de hardware","C":"Posible da\xf1o a los usuarios finales por resultados inexactos o sesgados","D":"Menores costos de computaci\xf3n en la nube"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El posible da\xf1o a los usuarios finales por resultados inexactos o sesgados es un riesgo legal que las organizaciones deben considerar, ya que los clientes que reciben asesoramiento incorrecto, decisiones sesgadas o contenido da\xf1ino pueden sufrir consecuencias reales, lo que genera responsabilidad legal, sanciones regulatorias y litigios.\\nEl tiempo reducido de entrenamiento del modelo, la mayor utilizaci\xf3n de hardware y los menores costos de computaci\xf3n en la nube son beneficios operativos o m\xe9tricas que no representan riesgos legales para los usuarios finales."},{"id":"aif-q454","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 eligiendo entre varios modelos fundacionales y quiere minimizar el impacto ambiental de sus cargas de trabajo de IA. \xbfQu\xe9 pr\xe1ctica de IA responsable deber\xeda guiar esta decisi\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Elegir el modelo m\xe1s grande disponible para maximizar la precisi\xf3n","B":"Seleccionar un modelo dimensionado adecuadamente para la tarea para reducir el c\xf3mputo y el consumo de energ\xeda innecesarios","C":"Usar siempre el modelo m\xe1s costoso para garantizar la calidad","D":"Ejecutar la inferencia de forma continua para mantener el endpoint caliente"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Seleccionar un modelo dimensionado adecuadamente para la tarea evita desperdiciar recursos de c\xf3mputo y energ\xeda en capacidad innecesaria, lo que reduce directamente la huella ambiental de las cargas de trabajo de IA y refleja pr\xe1cticas responsables de selecci\xf3n de modelos centradas en la sostenibilidad.\\nElegir el modelo m\xe1s grande desperdicia recursos cuando uno m\xe1s peque\xf1o es suficiente. Usar el modelo m\xe1s costoso no reduce inherentemente el impacto ambiental. Ejecutar la inferencia de forma continua desperdicia energ\xeda cuando la demanda es intermitente."},{"id":"aif-q455","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 decidiendo entre una red neuronal profunda y un \xe1rbol de decisi\xf3n para una aplicaci\xf3n de cara al cliente. La red neuronal profunda es m\xe1s precisa, pero la empresa necesita explicar cada decisi\xf3n a los reguladores. \xbfQu\xe9 compensaci\xf3n representa esto?","options":{"A":"Costo vs latencia","B":"Interpretabilidad vs rendimiento","C":"Tiempo de entrenamiento vs precisi\xf3n","D":"Volumen de datos vs tama\xf1o del modelo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La compensaci\xf3n entre interpretabilidad y rendimiento significa que los modelos m\xe1s complejos (como las redes neuronales profundas) a menudo logran una mayor precisi\xf3n pero son m\xe1s dif\xedciles de explicar, mientras que los modelos m\xe1s simples (como los \xe1rboles de decisi\xf3n) son f\xe1ciles de interpretar pero pueden sacrificar algo de rendimiento, que es exactamente la tensi\xf3n que enfrenta la empresa.\\nCosto vs latencia, tiempo de entrenamiento vs precisi\xf3n, y volumen de datos vs tama\xf1o del modelo son compensaciones diferentes que no describen la tensi\xf3n entre la capacidad de explicaci\xf3n y la precisi\xf3n de predicci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q009","question":"Una startup ejecuta un chatbot construido sobre un modelo fundacional (FM) de Amazon Bedrock. El chatbot necesita leer archivos en un bucket de Amazon S3 que est\xe1 protegido con claves administradas por Amazon S3 (SSE-S3), pero todos los intentos de leerlos fallan. \xbfQu\xe9 resolver\xe1 el problema?","options":{"A":"Usar ingenier\xeda de prompts para indicarle al modelo d\xf3nde buscar los datos en Amazon S3","B":"Eliminar cualquier detalle sensible de los datos de S3","C":"Otorgar al rol de IAM que asume Amazon Bedrock permiso para descifrar los datos con la clave de cifrado correcta","D":"Actualizar la pol\xedtica del bucket de S3 para permitir el acceso p\xfablico a los datos desde internet"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los fallos de lectura son un problema de permisos: se debe permitir que el rol de IAM que asume Amazon Bedrock acceda y descifre los objetos cifrados, por lo que otorgarle permiso para descifrar los datos con la clave correcta resuelve el fallo de acceso.\\nLa ingenier\xeda de prompts no puede otorgar acceso a datos cifrados. Eliminar los datos sensibles no aborda el permiso de descifrado faltante y frustra el prop\xf3sito de usar los datos. Abrir el bucket a internet p\xfablico es un cambio inseguro que a\xfan no otorga permiso de descifrado al rol."},{"id":"aif-q013","question":"Un banco est\xe1 construyendo una aplicaci\xf3n de IA en Amazon Bedrock. La aplicaci\xf3n se encuentra en una VPC que, para satisfacer a los reguladores, debe estar aislada de todo el tr\xe1fico de internet. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Internet gateway","B":"Amazon Macie","C":"AWS PrivateLink","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS PrivateLink crea conectividad privada entre una VPC y servicios de AWS como Amazon Bedrock, por lo que el tr\xe1fico permanece en la red de AWS y nunca llega a internet p\xfablico, lo que satisface el requisito de cumplimiento de no usar internet.\\nUn Internet gateway hace lo contrario de lo requerido al darle a la VPC una ruta hacia internet p\xfablico. Amazon Macie descubre y protege datos sensibles en Amazon S3 y no proporciona conectividad privada. Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que sirve contenido a trav\xe9s de internet."},{"id":"aif-q026","question":"Una empresa de seguridad invoca modelos fundacionales (FM) en Amazon Bedrock y quiere limitar el uso solo a usuarios aprobados. Antes de endurecer sus roles y pol\xedticas de IAM m\xe1s adelante, primero necesita detectar cualquier intento de acceder a los modelos sin autorizaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"Amazon Inspector","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Audit Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra la actividad de la API en los servicios de AWS, por lo que captura qui\xe9n intent\xf3 llamar a Amazon Bedrock y cu\xe1ndo, lo que permite a la empresa identificar intentos de acceso no autorizados y refinar sus roles y pol\xedticas de IAM.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor brinda recomendaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas sobre costo, rendimiento y seguridad, pero no registra intentos de acceso individuales. Amazon Inspector escanea cargas de trabajo en busca de vulnerabilidades de software. AWS Audit Manager automatiza la recopilaci\xf3n de evidencia para auditor\xedas en lugar de rastrear qui\xe9n intent\xf3 acceder a un servicio."},{"id":"aif-q028","question":"Una empresa audita regularmente sus operaciones junto con proveedores de software independientes (ISV) y quiere una alerta por correo electr\xf3nico cada vez que se publique un nuevo informe de cumplimiento de un ISV. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"AWS Data Exchange","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Artifact","D":"AWS Audit Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes de cumplimiento y acuerdos, incluidos informes de terceros, y puede notificar a la empresa cuando un nuevo informe est\xe9 disponible.\\nAWS Data Exchange es un marketplace de conjuntos de datos de terceros, no de informes de cumplimiento. AWS Trusted Advisor ofrece verificaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas. AWS Audit Manager automatiza la recopilaci\xf3n de evidencia para las propias auditor\xedas de la empresa en lugar de distribuir informes de ISV."},{"id":"aif-q030","question":"Una aseguradora est\xe1 asignando sus proyectos de IA generativa a la Generative AI Security Scoping Matrix. \xbfQu\xe9 alcance deja a la aseguradora con la MAYOR responsabilidad de seguridad?","options":{"A":"Construir y entrenar un modelo de IA generativa desde cero usando datos que posee la empresa","B":"Ajustar un modelo fundacional (FM) de terceros existente con los datos propios de la empresa","C":"Construir una aplicaci\xf3n sobre un modelo fundacional (FM) de terceros existente","D":"Usar una aplicaci\xf3n empresarial de terceros que tiene funciones de IA generativa integradas"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Construir y entrenar un modelo desde cero con datos que posee la empresa le da a la empresa control total sobre los datos, el entrenamiento, el despliegue y las protecciones, por lo que conlleva la mayor responsabilidad de seguridad.\\nAjustar un FM de terceros, construir una aplicaci\xf3n sobre un FM de terceros y usar una aplicaci\xf3n de proveedor con IA generativa integrada trasladan cada vez m\xe1s partes de la responsabilidad al proveedor, por lo que otorgan a la empresa menos propiedad."},{"id":"aif-q061","question":"Un profesional de IA se basa en un modelo base de Amazon Bedrock para resumir chats de soporte y quiere mantener un registro de lo que entra y sale del modelo para monitoreo. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque deber\xeda tomar el profesional?","options":{"A":"Establecer AWS CloudTrail como destino de registro del modelo","B":"Configurar el registro de invocaciones del modelo en Amazon EventBridge","C":"Activar el registro de invocaciones del modelo en Amazon Bedrock","D":"Establecer AWS Audit Manager como destino de registro del modelo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Activar el registro de invocaciones del modelo en Amazon Bedrock captura y almacena los datos de entrada y salida del modelo, que es exactamente lo que el profesional necesita para monitorear las invocaciones.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API de administraci\xf3n, pero no los payloads de prompt y respuesta del modelo. Amazon EventBridge enruta eventos entre servicios y no es donde se habilita el registro de invocaciones de Bedrock. AWS Audit Manager recopila evidencia de auditor\xeda en lugar de datos de invocaci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q066","question":"Una empresa planea crear una aplicaci\xf3n de modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) con Amazon Bedrock usando datos de clientes almacenados en Amazon S3. Su pol\xedtica indica que cada equipo solo puede acceder a los registros de sus propios clientes. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Crear un \xfanico rol de servicio de Amazon Bedrock con acceso completo a Amazon S3 y, luego, crear roles de IAM por equipo con alcance a las carpetas de clientes de cada equipo","B":"Crear un rol de servicio personalizado de Amazon Bedrock para cada equipo que otorgue acceso solo a los datos de clientes de ese equipo","C":"Crear un \xfanico rol de servicio con acceso a Amazon S3 y exigir que los equipos indiquen el cliente en cada solicitud de Amazon Bedrock","D":"Ocultar los datos personales en Amazon S3 y actualizar la pol\xedtica del bucket para permitir que los equipos accedan a los datos de clientes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Crear un rol de servicio personalizado de Amazon Bedrock separado para cada equipo, con alcance solo a los datos de clientes de ese equipo, aplica la pol\xedtica mediante acceso de m\xednimo privilegio y evita que los equipos accedan a los datos de otros equipos.\\nUn \xfanico rol con acceso completo a Amazon S3 m\xe1s roles de IAM por equipo sigue otorgando al rol compartido de Bedrock un acceso amplio, lo que conlleva riesgo de exposici\xf3n. Un \xfanico rol que depende de que los equipos indiquen el cliente se basa en la cooperaci\xf3n en lugar de controles aplicados. Ocultar los datos y abrir la pol\xedtica del bucket no segrega el acceso por equipo."},{"id":"aif-q078","question":"Un profesional de IA realiz\xf3 un ajuste fino de un modelo personalizado en Amazon Bedrock usando un conjunto de datos que conten\xeda registros confidenciales. El profesional debe garantizar que las respuestas de inferencia del modelo nunca se basen en esos datos confidenciales. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer el profesional?","options":{"A":"Enmascarar los datos confidenciales en las respuestas de inferencia con enmascaramiento din\xe1mico de datos","B":"Cifrar los datos confidenciales en las respuestas de inferencia con Amazon SageMaker AI","C":"Eliminar el modelo personalizado, quitar los datos confidenciales del conjunto de datos de entrenamiento y volver a entrenar el modelo","D":"Cifrar los datos confidenciales en el modelo personalizado con AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Como los datos confidenciales formaban parte del conjunto de entrenamiento, la \xfanica soluci\xf3n fiable es eliminar el modelo personalizado, quitar los datos confidenciales del conjunto de datos y volver a entrenar para que el modelo nunca haya aprendido patrones de esos datos.\\nEl enmascaramiento din\xe1mico de datos y el cifrado de las respuestas act\xfaan despu\xe9s de que el modelo ya haya aprendido de los datos confidenciales, por lo que la informaci\xf3n a\xfan puede influir en las salidas. Cifrar los datos en el modelo con AWS KMS los protege en reposo, pero no impide que el modelo genere respuestas basadas en ellos."},{"id":"aif-q084","question":"Un equipo crea y entrena modelos de ML en cuadernos de Amazon SageMaker Studio y mantiene sus datos en un bucket de Amazon S3. Necesita controlar c\xf3mo se mueven los datos entre Amazon S3 y los cuadernos. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Usar Amazon Macie para monitorear SageMaker Studio","B":"Configurar SageMaker para usar una VPC con un endpoint de Amazon S3","C":"Usar Amazon Inspector para monitorear SageMaker Studio","D":"Configurar SageMaker para usar S3 Glacier Deep Archive"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Configurar Amazon SageMaker para usar una VPC con un endpoint de Amazon S3 crea una ruta privada y administrada para que los datos fluyan entre Amazon S3 y los cuadernos de Studio sin atravesar la Internet p\xfablica.\\nAmazon Macie y Amazon Inspector monitorean datos confidenciales y vulnerabilidades, respectivamente, pero ninguno administra la ruta de datos entre los servicios. Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive es una clase de almacenamiento de archivo de bajo costo y no controla el flujo de datos."},{"id":"aif-q087","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 a punto de adoptar Amazon Bedrock y necesita saber qu\xe9 responsabilidades de seguridad siguen siendo de la empresa seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 responsabilidad corresponde a la empresa?","options":{"A":"Proteger la infraestructura que aloja Amazon Bedrock","B":"Aplicar parches y actualizar las versiones de Amazon Bedrock","C":"Aprovisionar Amazon Bedrock dentro de la red de la empresa","D":"Proteger los datos de la empresa en tr\xe1nsito y en reposo"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, AWS protege la infraestructura de la nube y los servicios administrados, como Amazon Bedrock, mientras que el cliente es responsable de la seguridad en la nube, incluida la protecci\xf3n de sus propios datos en tr\xe1nsito y en reposo.\\nAplicar parches a las versiones de Amazon Bedrock y proteger la infraestructura de alojamiento son responsabilidades de AWS para un servicio administrado. Aprovisionar Amazon Bedrock dentro de la red de la empresa no es la forma en que se accede al servicio administrado y no es una responsabilidad de seguridad del cliente."},{"id":"aif-q090","question":"Para satisfacer a los reguladores, una empresa debe ejecutar el entrenamiento e inferencia de Amazon SageMaker AI en un entorno sellado sin conectividad a Internet. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumplir\xe1 estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Asociar roles de IAM apropiados con los trabajos de SageMaker AI","B":"Cifrar los datos en reposo usando el cifrado para las capacidades geoespaciales de SageMaker","C":"Ejecutar los trabajos de entrenamiento e inferencia de SageMaker AI con aislamiento de red","D":"Ejecutar los trabajos de entrenamiento e inferencia de SageMaker AI con SageMaker Experiments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ejecutar los trabajos de entrenamiento e inferencia de Amazon SageMaker AI con aislamiento de red elimina el acceso a Internet para esos trabajos, manteni\xe9ndolos en el entorno aislado que exigen las regulaciones.\\nAsociar roles de IAM controla los permisos, pero no elimina la conectividad a Internet. El cifrado para las capacidades geoespaciales protege los datos en reposo y SageMaker Experiments realiza un seguimiento de las ejecuciones de entrenamiento; ninguna de estas opciones a\xedsla el trabajo de Internet."},{"id":"aif-q104","question":"Una empresa ejecuta modelos personalizados en Amazon Bedrock para una aplicaci\xf3n de IA generativa y quiere que los artefactos de modelo producidos durante los trabajos de personalizaci\xf3n se cifren con una clave que la empresa controle. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Macie","B":"AWS Secrets Manager","C":"AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) permite a la empresa crear y controlar su propia clave de cifrado y usarla para cifrar los artefactos de modelo producidos por los trabajos de personalizaci\xf3n de Amazon Bedrock.\\nAmazon Macie descubre datos confidenciales y Amazon Inspector escanea en busca de vulnerabilidades; ninguno de los dos administra claves de cifrado de clientes. AWS Secrets Manager almacena credenciales y secretos, no las claves de cifrado que se usan para proteger los artefactos de modelo."},{"id":"aif-q124","question":"Una empresa debe registrar cada llamada a su API de Amazon Bedrock y conservar esos registros de forma segura durante cinco a\xf1os al menor costo posible. \xbfQu\xe9 dos opciones (un servicio de AWS y una clase de almacenamiento de S3) debe combinar para cumplir estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering","D":"AWS Audit Manager","E":"Amazon S3 Standard"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS CloudTrail est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para registrar las solicitudes de API realizadas a los servicios de AWS, incluido Amazon Bedrock, y Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering mueve autom\xe1ticamente los registros almacenados entre niveles de acceso para mantener bajos los costos de retenci\xf3n a largo plazo, por lo que, en conjunto, cumplen los requisitos de registro y de retenci\xf3n durante cinco a\xf1os a bajo costo.\\nAmazon CloudWatch se centra en m\xe9tricas y monitoreo operativo en lugar de registrar llamadas a la API, y AWS Audit Manager recopila evidencia de auditor\xeda en lugar de registrar cada solicitud de API. Amazon S3 Standard cuesta m\xe1s que Intelligent-Tiering para datos a los que se accede con poca frecuencia durante un per\xedodo de retenci\xf3n prolongado."},{"id":"aif-q126","question":"Un hospital est\xe1 creando un sistema de IA que ayuda a los m\xe9dicos a diagnosticar enfermedades a partir de archivos de pacientes e im\xe1genes m\xe9dicas. Por ley, los datos confidenciales de los pacientes deben permanecer dentro del pa\xeds donde se almacenan. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de gobernanza de datos garantiza el cumplimiento y protege la privacidad?","options":{"A":"Calidad de los datos","B":"Enriquecimiento de datos","C":"Residencia de datos","D":"Capacidad de descubrimiento de datos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La residencia de datos garantiza que los datos se almacenen y procesen solo dentro de una ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica espec\xedfica, lo que cumple directamente el requisito de que los datos de los pacientes nunca salgan del pa\xeds y protege la privacidad de los pacientes.\\nLa calidad de los datos se refiere a la exactitud y la integridad de los datos, el enriquecimiento de datos agrega informaci\xf3n a los registros existentes y la capacidad de descubrimiento de datos ayuda a los usuarios a encontrar datos; ninguna de estas opciones controla d\xf3nde se almacenan f\xedsicamente los datos."},{"id":"aif-q139","question":"Una empresa mover\xe1 correos electr\xf3nicos de atenci\xf3n al cliente a Amazon S3 para una aplicaci\xf3n de anal\xedtica. Como algunos correos contienen datos sensibles, la empresa quiere una alerta siempre que aparezcan dichos datos. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n automatiza por completo esta detecci\xf3n con el MENOR esfuerzo de desarrollo?","options":{"A":"Crear varios patrones regex personalizados y ejecutarlos en una aplicaci\xf3n personalizada para detectar datos sensibles","B":"Configurar Amazon Macie para detectar informaci\xf3n sensible en los documentos cargados en Amazon S3","C":"Implementar un modelo de lenguaje grande (LLM) en endpoints de Amazon SageMaker para redactar datos sensibles","D":"Pedir a los clientes que no compartan informaci\xf3n sensible en sus correos electr\xf3nicos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Macie es un servicio administrado que utiliza machine learning para descubrir autom\xe1ticamente datos sensibles, como PII, en Amazon S3 y puede generar alertas cuando los encuentra, lo que cumple el requisito con el menor esfuerzo de desarrollo.\\nCrear patrones regex personalizados en una aplicaci\xf3n personalizada e implementar un LLM en endpoints de Amazon SageMaker requieren ambos un desarrollo y mantenimiento significativos. Pedir a los clientes que no compartan informaci\xf3n sensible no detecta ni alerta sobre nada."},{"id":"aif-q145","question":"Un banco global cre\xf3 una aplicaci\xf3n de ML que analiza datos de mercado para descubrir tendencias. A lo largo del desarrollo, el banco quiere comprobaciones continuas de que se cumplen sus pol\xedticas y las regulaciones del sector. \xbfQu\xe9 dos servicios de AWS ayudan al banco a evaluar el cumplimiento?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Audit Manager","E":"Amazon Inspector"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Audit Manager recopila evidencia de forma continua y la asigna a marcos regulatorios para evaluar el cumplimiento, y AWS Config rastrea cambios de configuraci\xf3n con respecto a reglas para que el banco pueda confirmar que sus recursos se mantienen dentro de la pol\xedtica durante todo el desarrollo.\\nAmazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas y registros, AWS CloudTrail registra la actividad de API y Amazon Inspector escanea en busca de vulnerabilidades de software; estos respaldan la seguridad y las operaciones, pero no son los servicios principales para evaluar el cumplimiento con respecto a pol\xedticas y regulaciones."},{"id":"aif-q151","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de AWS ofrece una forma coherente de documentar los detalles de los modelos de ML para gobernanza y generaci\xf3n de informes?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","B":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","C":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards","D":"Amazon SageMaker Model Registry"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards proporciona un registro estandarizado del uso previsto de un modelo, los datos de entrenamiento, los resultados de evaluaci\xf3n y los detalles de cumplimiento, lo que respalda la gobernanza y la generaci\xf3n de informes durante todo el ciclo de vida del modelo.\\nAmazon SageMaker Model Monitor vigila los modelos implementados en busca de deriva, Amazon SageMaker JumpStart proporciona modelos y soluciones precompilados, y Amazon SageMaker Model Registry almacena y versiona modelos; ninguno de estos es el registro de documentaci\xf3n estandarizado utilizado para gobernanza y generaci\xf3n de informes."},{"id":"aif-q179","question":"Cuando los modelos se entrenan en AWS, \xbfqu\xe9 t\xe9cnica de machine learning ayuda a mantener los datos de entrenamiento subyacentes privados y conformes con la normativa?","options":{"A":"Aprendizaje por transferencia","B":"Aprendizaje no supervisado","C":"Aprendizaje federado","D":"Aprendizaje por refuerzo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El aprendizaje federado entrena un modelo compartido entre m\xfaltiples poseedores de datos sin mover los datos sin procesar a una ubicaci\xf3n central, por lo que cada participante mantiene sus datos privados en su lugar, lo que ayuda a preservar la privacidad y cumplir los requisitos de cumplimiento.\\nEl aprendizaje por transferencia, el aprendizaje no supervisado y el aprendizaje por refuerzo son enfoques definidos por c\xf3mo un modelo aprende de los datos, no t\xe9cnicas dise\xf1adas para mantener los datos de entrenamiento privados y en cumplimiento normativo."},{"id":"aif-q185","question":"Un hospital quiere ajustar un modelo fundacional (FM) con servicios de AWS mientras mantiene sus datos privados y dentro de la Regi\xf3n de AWS donde est\xe1n almacenados. \xbfQu\xe9 dos pasos logran esto de la forma M\xc1S rentable?","options":{"A":"Alojar el modelo en las instalaciones con AWS Outposts","B":"Usar AWS PrivateLink con una VPC","C":"Alojar la API de Amazon Bedrock en las instalaciones","D":"Usar la API de Amazon Bedrock"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Llamar a la API de Amazon Bedrock mantiene el ajuste fino dentro del servicio administrado en la misma Regi\xf3n de AWS, y enrutar ese tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de una VPC con AWS PrivateLink lo mantiene en la red privada de AWS, lo que cumple el requisito de privacidad de forma rentable sin infraestructura adicional.\\nAlojar el modelo en las instalaciones con AWS Outposts y alojar la API de Amazon Bedrock en las instalaciones a\xf1aden costo de hardware y operativo y no es la forma en que se consume el servicio administrado Amazon Bedrock."},{"id":"aif-q186","question":"Un banco que ejecuta sus modelos de IA generativa en AWS debe producir informes que demuestren que cumple las reglas internacionales para el manejo de datos confidenciales de clientes. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon Macie","C":"AWS Artifact","D":"AWS Secrets Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes y certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS, como ISO y SOC, que el banco puede usar para demostrar que sus cargas de trabajo y el manejo de datos cumplen los requisitos regulatorios internacionales.\\nAWS Config rastrea la configuraci\xf3n de recursos, Amazon Macie descubre datos sensibles y AWS Secrets Manager almacena credenciales; ninguno de estos produce los informes de cumplimiento necesarios para demostrar la adherencia regulatoria."},{"id":"aif-q208","question":"Una empresa con acceso a Amazon Bedrock quiere controlar qu\xe9 modelos pueden usar empleados espec\xedficos. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Usar roles de servicio de AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) para restringir la suscripci\xf3n a modelos","B":"Usar pol\xedticas de AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) para restringir el acceso a modelos","C":"Usar Amazon Inspector para monitorear el acceso a modelos","D":"Usar AWS Security Token Service (AWS STS) para generar credenciales temporales para el uso de modelos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las pol\xedticas de AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) pueden permitir o denegar acciones de API de Amazon Bedrock con alcance a recursos de modelos espec\xedficos, por lo que la empresa puede restringir qu\xe9 modelos puede invocar cada empleado.\\nLos roles de servicio de IAM otorgan permisos a servicios en lugar de restringir a qu\xe9 modelos puede suscribirse un usuario, AWS STS emite credenciales temporales pero no limita por s\xed mismo el acceso a modelos, y Amazon Inspector escanea en busca de vulnerabilidades en lugar de controlar el acceso."},{"id":"aif-q215","question":"Una empresa analiza documentos confidenciales con un modelo de terceros en Amazon Bedrock y le preocupa la privacidad de los datos. \xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n explica c\xf3mo Amazon Bedrock mantiene privados esos datos?","options":{"A":"Las entradas del usuario se mantienen confidenciales, pero las salidas del modelo se comparten con los proveedores de modelos de terceros","B":"Las entradas del usuario y las salidas del modelo se anonimizan y luego se comparten con los proveedores de modelos de terceros","C":"Las entradas del usuario y las salidas del modelo no se comparten con ning\xfan proveedor de modelos de terceros","D":"Las entradas del usuario y las salidas del modelo se redactan antes de compartirse con los proveedores de modelos de terceros"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock procesa los datos dentro del entorno de AWS y no comparte las entradas del usuario ni las salidas del modelo con los proveedores de modelos de terceros, por lo que los documentos confidenciales de la empresa permanecen privados.\\nLas opciones que indican que las salidas se comparten, que las entradas y salidas se anonimizan y se comparten, o que los datos se redactan antes de compartirse son todas incorrectas porque Amazon Bedrock no env\xeda las entradas ni las salidas a los proveedores de modelos en absoluto."},{"id":"aif-q229","question":"Un banco necesita que su chatbot de IA generativa se mantenga fiel a los hechos por motivos regulatorios. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n ayuda a evitar que el modelo fundacional (FM) subyacente produzca respuestas alucinadas?","options":{"A":"Usar AWS Config para consultar metadatos de cumplimiento mediante lenguaje natural","B":"Usar AWS Audit Manager para preparar informes de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento de TI","C":"Configurar Amazon Bedrock Guardrails para evaluar las entradas del usuario y las respuestas del modelo","D":"Usar Amazon Inspector para escanear cargas de trabajo en busca de vulnerabilidades"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock Guardrails permite al banco definir pol\xedticas que eval\xfaan las entradas del usuario y las respuestas del modelo, incluidos controles de fundamentaci\xf3n contextual, lo que ayuda a mantener las respuestas del chatbot fieles a los hechos y a reducir el contenido alucinado para el cumplimiento.\\nAWS Config rastrea la configuraci\xf3n de los recursos, AWS Audit Manager prepara informes de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y Amazon Inspector escanea cargas de trabajo en busca de vulnerabilidades; ninguna de estas limita las respuestas generadas por un modelo."},{"id":"aif-q235","question":"Una empresa de atenci\xf3n m\xe9dica planea ajustar un modelo en Amazon Bedrock usando datos sensibles almacenados en bases de datos privadas dentro de su VPC. Los datos nunca deben salir de la red privada de la empresa. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Restringir el acceso a Amazon Bedrock mediante pol\xedticas de AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","B":"Usar AWS PrivateLink para conectar la VPC y Amazon Bedrock","C":"Usar claves de AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) para cifrar los datos","D":"Enrutar el tr\xe1fico a Amazon Bedrock a trav\xe9s de una puerta de enlace de internet en la VPC"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS PrivateLink establece una conexi\xf3n privada entre la VPC y Amazon Bedrock, por lo que el tr\xe1fico viaja por la red privada de AWS en lugar de la internet p\xfablica, manteniendo los datos sensibles dentro de la red privada de la empresa durante el ajuste.\\nLas pol\xedticas de IAM controlan qui\xe9n puede llamar a Amazon Bedrock, pero no mantienen el tr\xe1fico de red fuera de la internet p\xfablica. AWS KMS cifra los datos en reposo y no cambia la ruta de red que toman los datos. Una puerta de enlace de internet enruta el tr\xe1fico de la VPC hacia la internet p\xfablica, lo que es lo opuesto a mantener los datos en una red privada."},{"id":"aif-q248","question":"Una empresa con oficinas en todo el mundo requiere que cada llamada a la API entre sus aplicaciones de IA generativa y los modelos fundacionales (FM) evite la internet p\xfablica. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon Q","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS PrivateLink","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS PrivateLink proporciona conectividad privada entre las VPC y los servicios de AWS, por lo que las llamadas a la API entre las aplicaciones de IA generativa y los modelos fundacionales permanecen en la red de AWS y fuera de la internet p\xfablica.\\nAmazon Q es un asistente de IA generativa, AWS CloudTrail registra la actividad de la API y Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que sirve tr\xe1fico por internet; ninguno de estos mantiene el tr\xe1fico de la API fuera de la internet p\xfablica."},{"id":"aif-q269","question":"Una empresa quiere acceder a las API de Amazon Bedrock de forma privada desde dentro de su cuenta de AWS mientras mantiene sus datos fuera de la internet p\xfablica. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Usar AWS Glue para configurar el cifrado de datos en el cat\xe1logo de datos de la empresa","B":"Usar AWS PrivateLink para configurar una conexi\xf3n privada entre la VPC de la empresa y Amazon Bedrock","C":"Usar Amazon CloudFront para restringir el acceso al contenido privado de la empresa","D":"Usar AWS Lake Formation para gestionar la gobernanza centralizada de datos y el uso compartido de datos entre cuentas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS PrivateLink configura una conexi\xf3n privada entre la VPC de la empresa y las API de Amazon Bedrock, por lo que las llamadas y transferencias de datos permanecen dentro de la red de AWS y no se exponen a la internet p\xfablica.\\nAWS Glue se encarga de la integraci\xf3n y catalogaci\xf3n de datos, Amazon CloudFront restringe y entrega contenido por internet, y AWS Lake Formation gestiona la gobernanza y el uso compartido de datos; ninguna de estas crea una conexi\xf3n de red privada a Amazon Bedrock."},{"id":"aif-q305","question":"Una empresa obtiene la acreditaci\xf3n de la Organizaci\xf3n Internacional de Normalizaci\xf3n (ISO) para gestionar riesgos de IA y usar la IA de forma responsable. \xbfQu\xe9 refleja esta acreditaci\xf3n sobre la empresa?","options":{"A":"Todos los miembros de la empresa est\xe1n certificados por ISO.","B":"Todos los sistemas de IA que usa la empresa est\xe1n certificados por ISO.","C":"Todos los miembros del equipo de aplicaciones de IA est\xe1n certificados por ISO.","D":"El framework de desarrollo de la empresa est\xe1 certificado por ISO."},"correct":"D","explanation":"La acreditaci\xf3n ISO certifica que el framework, los procesos y los controles de desarrollo de la empresa para gestionar la IA cumplen los requisitos de la norma ISO pertinente, lo que refleja que la organizaci\xf3n ha demostrado pr\xe1cticas de gobernanza de IA responsables y conscientes de los riesgos a nivel estructural.\\nQue todos los miembros de la empresa est\xe9n certificados por ISO es incorrecto porque la acreditaci\xf3n organizacional no certifica autom\xe1ticamente a cada empleado; la certificaci\xf3n personal ISO es un proceso aparte que las personas realizan de forma independiente. Que todos los sistemas de IA est\xe9n certificados por ISO es incorrecto porque la acreditaci\xf3n se aplica al framework y a los procesos usados para desarrollar y gobernar la IA, no a los sistemas o modelos individuales producidos dentro de \xe9l. Que todos los miembros del equipo de aplicaciones de IA est\xe9n certificados por ISO es incorrecto por la misma raz\xf3n, ya que los empleados individuales requieren certificaci\xf3n por separado y no quedan certificados autom\xe1ticamente por la acreditaci\xf3n obtenida por la organizaci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q307","question":"Una empresa mantiene reglas que establecen cu\xe1nto tiempo se almacena la informaci\xf3n y cu\xe1ndo debe eliminarse. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de gobernanza de datos describe esto?","options":{"A":"Est\xe1ndares de calidad de datos","B":"Retenci\xf3n de datos","C":"Almacenamiento de registros","D":"Desidentificaci\xf3n de datos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La retenci\xf3n de datos es el conjunto de pol\xedticas que definen cu\xe1nto tiempo se almacenan los datos y cu\xe1ndo deben eliminarse, que es exactamente lo que describen las directrices de la empresa.\\nLos est\xe1ndares de calidad de datos rigen la exactitud y la integridad, el almacenamiento de registros se refiere a d\xf3nde se guardan los registros y la desidentificaci\xf3n de datos elimina los detalles identificativos; ninguno de estos define la duraci\xf3n del almacenamiento ni el momento de eliminaci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q040","question":"Un chatbot construido sobre un modelo ajustado de Amazon SageMaker JumpStart debe cumplir varios marcos regulatorios. \xbfQu\xe9 dos capacidades ayudan a la empresa a demostrar el cumplimiento?","options":{"A":"Optimizaci\xf3n de costos","B":"Protecci\xf3n de datos","C":"Auto scaling el\xe1stico","D":"Detecci\xf3n de amenazas","E":"Mayor velocidad de comercializaci\xf3n"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"La protecci\xf3n de datos (salvaguardar datos sensibles) y la detecci\xf3n de amenazas (identificar riesgos de seguridad) son controles de seguridad que los reguladores esperan, por lo que respaldan directamente la demostraci\xf3n del cumplimiento.\\nLa optimizaci\xf3n de costos, el auto scaling el\xe1stico y la mayor velocidad de comercializaci\xf3n son cuestiones de rendimiento y de negocio que no establecen cumplimiento regulatorio."},{"id":"aif-q213","question":"Un banco ajusta un LLM en Amazon Bedrock para preguntas sobre pr\xe9stamos y debe asegurarse de que el modelo nunca revele datos privados de clientes. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n es adecuada?","options":{"A":"Aumentar el par\xe1metro Top K","B":"Usar Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Eliminar la informaci\xf3n de identificaci\xf3n personal (PII) de los datos antes del ajuste","D":"Cifrar los datos en Amazon S3 antes del ajuste"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Eliminar la PII antes del ajuste evita que el modelo llegue a ingerir detalles privados, por lo que no puede reproducirlos despu\xe9s, lo cual es la salvaguarda m\xe1s fiable.\\nCifrar los datos en Amazon S3 los protege en reposo, pero el modelo sigue aprendiendo la PII; Amazon Bedrock Guardrails filtra las salidas despu\xe9s de que se han ingerido los datos, y Top K solo afecta el muestreo de tokens."},{"id":"aif-q218","question":"Una empresa quiere controlar a qu\xe9 modelos fundacionales (FM) disponibles p\xfablicamente pueden acceder sus empleados. \xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n es la adecuada?","options":{"A":"Analizar el uso en AWS Cost Explorer","B":"Configurar Amazon SageMaker JumpStart para restringir qu\xe9 FM se pueden descubrir","C":"Descargar informes de cumplimiento de AWS Artifact","D":"Revisar las recomendaciones de AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker JumpStart permite a los administradores curar el cat\xe1logo de modelos, por lo que configurarlo para restringir qu\xe9 FM se pueden descubrir controla los modelos a los que pueden acceder los empleados.\\nAWS Cost Explorer analiza el gasto, AWS Artifact proporciona documentos de cumplimiento y AWS Trusted Advisor ofrece recomendaciones de pr\xe1cticas recomendadas; ninguno gobierna a qu\xe9 modelos pueden acceder los empleados."},{"id":"aif-q299","question":"Un chatbot de IA generativa basado en un modelo fundacional (FM) de Amazon Bedrock es vulnerable a la inyecci\xf3n de prompts. \xbfC\xf3mo puede la empresa protegerlo con el MENOR esfuerzo?","options":{"A":"Realizar un ajuste fino del FM para evitar respuestas da\xf1inas","B":"Aplicar los filtros de contenido y los temas denegados de Amazon Bedrock Guardrails","C":"Cambiar a un modelo fundacional diferente","D":"Agregar m\xe1s ejemplos few-shot al prompt"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los filtros de contenido y los temas denegados de Amazon Bedrock Guardrails se pueden configurar sin reentrenamiento para bloquear prompts y respuestas no seguras, lo que mitiga la inyecci\xf3n de prompts con el menor esfuerzo.\\nEl ajuste fino requiere datos y una ejecuci\xf3n de entrenamiento, cambiar de FM no detiene inherentemente la inyecci\xf3n y agregar ejemplos few-shot no defiende contra prompts maliciosos."},{"id":"aif-q034","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puede una empresa usar modelos de lenguaje grandes de forma segura en Amazon Bedrock?","options":{"A":"Configurar roles y pol\xedticas de IAM basados en el principio de m\xednimo privilegio","B":"Habilitar AWS Audit Manager para ejecutar trabajos de evaluaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de modelos","C":"Habilitar los trabajos de evaluaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de modelos de Amazon Bedrock","D":"Usar Amazon CloudWatch Logs para hacer que los modelos sean explicables y monitorear sesgos"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Configurar roles y pol\xedticas de IAM basados en el principio de m\xednimo privilegio restringe qu\xe9 usuarios y aplicaciones pueden acceder a los modelos y acciones de Amazon Bedrock, lo cual es la forma fundamental de usar modelos de lenguaje grandes de forma segura.\\nAWS Audit Manager recopila evidencia para cumplimiento y no ejecuta trabajos de evaluaci\xf3n de modelos. La evaluaci\xf3n de modelos de Amazon Bedrock eval\xfaa el rendimiento de los modelos en lugar de proteger el acceso. Amazon CloudWatch Logs registra actividad, pero no hace que los modelos sean explicables ni detecta sesgos."},{"id":"aif-q133","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n describe una caracter\xedstica de los marcos de gobernanza de IA que ayudan a generar confianza y desplegar IA centrada en las personas?","options":{"A":"Expandir las iniciativas de IA en todas las unidades de negocio para maximizar el valor a largo plazo","B":"Alinear los proyectos de IA con los objetivos de ingresos y las expectativas de las partes interesadas","C":"Impulsar la transformaci\xf3n empresarial y el crecimiento competitivo mediante la IA","D":"Desarrollar pol\xedticas y directrices para datos, transparencia, IA responsable y cumplimiento"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Los marcos de gobernanza de IA se caracterizan por desarrollar pol\xedticas y directrices que cubren datos, transparencia, IA responsable y cumplimiento, lo que genera confianza y respalda la IA centrada en las personas.\\nExpandir iniciativas en todas las unidades de negocio, alinear con objetivos de ingresos y expectativas de las partes interesadas, e impulsar la transformaci\xf3n empresarial son objetivos empresariales generales, no caracter\xedsticas definitorias de un marco de gobernanza de IA."},{"id":"aif-q159","question":"Una empresa usa IA para generar puntajes de cr\xe9dito de clientes durante solicitudes de pr\xe9stamo y planea expandirse a un nuevo mercado geogr\xe1fico. Para operar all\xed, \xbfqu\xe9 tipo de leyes de cumplimiento deber\xeda revisar la empresa?","options":{"A":"Leyes locales de protecci\xf3n de datos de salud","B":"Leyes locales de protecci\xf3n de datos de tarjetas de pago","C":"Leyes locales de responsabilidad algor\xedtmica","D":"Leyes locales de privacidad educativa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Debido a que la puntuaci\xf3n crediticia automatizada toma decisiones sobre personas, la empresa debe revisar las leyes locales de responsabilidad algor\xedtmica, que rigen la equidad, la transparencia y el impacto de la toma de decisiones automatizada en el nuevo mercado.\\nLas leyes locales de datos de salud, tarjetas de pago y privacidad educativa se aplican a otros tipos de datos y casos de uso, no a la responsabilidad de las decisiones automatizadas de puntuaci\xf3n crediticia."},{"id":"aif-q191","question":"Una empresa de comercio electr\xf3nico ejecuta un chatbot de IA en su sitio web que permite a los clientes enviar pedidos las 24 horas. \xbfQu\xe9 vulnerabilidad de entrada deber\xeda abordar la empresa antes de lanzar el chatbot?","options":{"A":"Fuga de datos","B":"Inyecci\xf3n de prompts","C":"Alucinaciones de modelos de lenguaje grandes (LLM)","D":"Deriva conceptual"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La inyecci\xf3n de prompts es una vulnerabilidad de entrada en la que un usuario elabora una entrada maliciosa para anular o manipular las instrucciones del chatbot, por lo que la empresa debe sanitizar y restringir las entradas antes del lanzamiento.\\nLa fuga de datos es la exposici\xf3n de informaci\xf3n sensible, las alucinaciones de modelos de lenguaje grandes son salidas fabricadas y la deriva conceptual es un cambio gradual en los patrones de datos a lo largo del tiempo. Ninguna de estas es un ataque a nivel de entrada contra las instrucciones del chatbot."},{"id":"aif-q254","question":"Una empresa quiere asegurarse de que su sistema de IA sea justo y explicable y decide exigir capacitaci\xf3n para su equipo de desarrollo de IA. \xbfQu\xe9 capacitaci\xf3n cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Capacitaci\xf3n en habilidades avanzadas de programaci\xf3n","B":"Capacitaci\xf3n en privacidad de datos y protocolos de cifrado","C":"Capacitaci\xf3n en concientizaci\xf3n sobre sesgos e IA responsable","D":"Capacitaci\xf3n en algoritmos avanzados de ML"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Capacitar al equipo de desarrollo de IA en concientizaci\xf3n sobre sesgos e IA responsable le proporciona los conocimientos y las pr\xe1cticas necesarios para construir sistemas justos y explicables, lo que cumple el requisito.\\nLa capacitaci\xf3n en habilidades avanzadas de programaci\xf3n, algoritmos avanzados de ML o privacidad de datos y protocolos de cifrado desarrolla otras habilidades valiosas, pero no aborda directamente la equidad y la explicabilidad."},{"id":"aif-q393","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 desplegando agentes de IA en Amazon Bedrock que necesitan acceder a m\xfaltiples sistemas backend en nombre de los usuarios. La empresa quiere que cada agente se autentique con su propia identidad en lugar de compartir un \xfanico conjunto de credenciales. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity","C":"Amazon Macie","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Identity proporciona administraci\xf3n de identidades para agentes de IA, lo que permite que cada agente se autentique con su propia identidad distinta al acceder a sistemas backend, lo que respalda el principio de m\xednimo privilegio y evita compartir credenciales.\\nAWS Secrets Manager almacena y rota credenciales, pero no proporciona administraci\xf3n de identidad por agente. Amazon Macie descubre datos sensibles en Amazon S3. AWS CloudTrail registra la actividad de API para auditor\xeda. Ninguna de estas asigna identidades individuales a los agentes de IA."},{"id":"aif-q394","question":"Un equipo de cumplimiento de una empresa farmac\xe9utica utiliza un asistente de IA generativa para responder preguntas sobre interacciones medicamentosas. Para reducir el riesgo de que el modelo genere informaci\xf3n m\xe9dica incorrecta, el equipo desea que cada respuesta se verifique con documentos de referencia aprobados. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9cnica cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Aumentar el par\xe1metro temperature del modelo","B":"Aplicar fundamentaci\xf3n con RAG usando documentos de referencia aprobados","C":"Ajustar el modelo mediante fine-tuning con m\xe1s datos m\xe9dicos generales","D":"Usar un modelo fundacional m\xe1s grande"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La fundamentaci\xf3n con RAG recupera pasajes relevantes de documentos de referencia aprobados en el momento de la consulta y los usa para verificar y anclar las respuestas del modelo, lo que garantiza que las respuestas sean trazables a fuentes autorizadas y reduce el riesgo de informaci\xf3n m\xe9dica alucinada.\\nAumentar la temperatura agrega aleatoriedad y hace que las alucinaciones sean m\xe1s probables, no menos. El fine-tuning con m\xe1s datos m\xe9dicos generales no garantiza que las respuestas est\xe9n fundamentadas en documentos aprobados espec\xedficos. Usar un modelo m\xe1s grande puede mejorar la capacidad general, pero no verifica las salidas contra referencias autorizadas."},{"id":"aif-q395","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 dos t\xe9cnicas ayudan a mejorar la exactitud de las salidas de un modelo de IA generativa al detectar posibles alucinaciones antes de que lleguen a los usuarios?","options":{"A":"Validaci\xf3n de salidas contra documentos de origen","B":"Aumentar el volumen de datos de entrenamiento del modelo","C":"Puntuaci\xf3n de confianza para marcar respuestas de baja certeza","D":"Reducir el n\xfamero de par\xe1metros del modelo","E":"Agregar m\xe1s capacidad de c\xf3mputo de GPU durante la inferencia"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"La validaci\xf3n de salidas comprueba las respuestas del modelo contra documentos de origen para confirmar la exactitud factual, y la puntuaci\xf3n de confianza asigna una medida de certeza a cada respuesta para que las salidas de baja confianza se puedan marcar para revisi\xf3n o retener a los usuarios. Juntas, estas t\xe9cnicas detectan posibles alucinaciones antes de que lleguen a los usuarios finales.\\nAumentar el volumen de datos de entrenamiento puede mejorar la calidad general del modelo, pero no detecta alucinaciones en tiempo real. Reducir los par\xe1metros del modelo simplifica el modelo, pero no agrega verificaci\xf3n en tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n. Agregar m\xe1s capacidad de c\xf3mputo de GPU acelera la inferencia, pero no proporciona ning\xfan mecanismo para detectar o prevenir salidas inexactas."},{"id":"aif-q396","question":"Una instituci\xf3n financiera entrena modelos de ML usando datos de m\xfaltiples sistemas internos. Los reguladores exigen que la instituci\xf3n rastree cada pieza de datos de entrenamiento hasta su origen original y demuestre que los datos se recopilaron y procesaron en cumplimiento de la pol\xedtica. \xbfQu\xe9 pr\xe1ctica cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Aumento de datos","B":"Seguimiento de linaje de datos","C":"Normalizaci\xf3n de datos","D":"Balanceo de datos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El seguimiento de linaje de datos documenta el origen, el movimiento y la transformaci\xf3n de los datos a lo largo de su ciclo de vida, de modo que la instituci\xf3n puede rastrear cada pieza de datos de entrenamiento hasta su origen y demostrar que se manej\xf3 en cumplimiento de la pol\xedtica, lo que satisface el requisito regulatorio.\\nEl aumento de datos crea ejemplos de entrenamiento adicionales para mejorar el rendimiento del modelo. La normalizaci\xf3n de datos reescala las caracter\xedsticas a un rango com\xfan. El balanceo de datos ajusta las distribuciones de clases para reducir el sesgo. Ninguna de estas pr\xe1cticas proporciona un registro trazable de los or\xedgenes y transformaciones de datos con fines regulatorios."},{"id":"aif-q397","question":"Una empresa desea documentar las fuentes de datos de entrenamiento, los pasos de procesamiento y el uso previsto de cada modelo de ML que implementa, para que los auditores puedan verificar la procedencia de los datos. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de AWS proporciona una forma estandarizada de registrar esta informaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards","B":"Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","C":"Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","D":"Amazon SageMaker Autopilot"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker Model Cards proporcionan un marco estandarizado para documentar detalles del modelo, incluidas las fuentes de datos de entrenamiento, los pasos de procesamiento, los resultados de evaluaci\xf3n y el uso previsto, lo que brinda a los auditores un registro consistente de la procedencia de los datos y el linaje del modelo.\\nAmazon SageMaker Data Wrangler prepara y transforma datos, pero no produce documentaci\xf3n del modelo lista para auditor\xeda. Amazon SageMaker Feature Store administra y sirve caracter\xedsticas para modelos de ML. Amazon SageMaker Autopilot automatiza la creaci\xf3n de modelos. Ninguna de estas crea el registro de documentaci\xf3n estandarizado necesario para las auditor\xedas de procedencia de datos."},{"id":"aif-q451","question":"Una empresa utiliza modelos de IA entrenados con datos de clientes y necesita definir reglas sobre cu\xe1nto tiempo se conservan los conjuntos de datos de entrenamiento, cu\xe1ndo se archivan y cu\xe1ndo deben eliminarse. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de gobernanza de datos describe esto?","options":{"A":"Residencia de datos","B":"Gesti\xf3n del ciclo de vida de los datos","C":"Registro de datos","D":"Monitoreo de datos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La gesti\xf3n del ciclo de vida de los datos define reglas sobre c\xf3mo se crean, almacenan, archivan y eliminan los datos a lo largo del tiempo, que es lo que describe el establecimiento de programaciones de retenci\xf3n y eliminaci\xf3n para los conjuntos de datos de entrenamiento.\\nLa residencia de datos rige d\xf3nde se almacenan los datos geogr\xe1ficamente. El registro de datos registra eventos para auditor\xeda. El monitoreo de datos observa la calidad y el uso en tiempo real. Ninguna de estas establece cronogramas de archivo y eliminaci\xf3n."},{"id":"aif-q452","question":"El equipo de gobernanza de datos de una empresa desea vigilar continuamente c\xf3mo se accede y se utilizan los datos de entrenamiento de IA en toda la organizaci\xf3n para detectar patrones de uso no autorizados. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de gobernanza de datos describe esto?","options":{"A":"Retenci\xf3n de datos","B":"Residencia de datos","C":"Observaci\xf3n y monitoreo de datos","D":"Registro de datos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La observaci\xf3n y el monitoreo de datos implican vigilar continuamente c\xf3mo se accede y se utilizan los datos para detectar anomal\xedas y patrones no autorizados, que es la estrategia de gobernanza que aborda la necesidad del equipo de supervisar el uso de los datos de entrenamiento de IA.\\nLa retenci\xf3n de datos define cu\xe1nto tiempo se almacenan los datos. La residencia de datos controla la ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica. El registro de datos registra eventos de acceso individuales, pero no proporciona la vigilancia continua ni la detecci\xf3n de patrones que ofrece el monitoreo."},{"id":"aif-q453","question":"Una empresa que construye sistemas de IA en AWS desea registrar cada acceso a sus conjuntos de datos de entrenamiento con fines de auditor\xeda. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona esta capacidad de registro?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra las llamadas a la API realizadas a los servicios de AWS, incluidos los eventos de acceso a datos para servicios como Amazon S3, lo que crea un rastro de auditor\xeda que registra qui\xe9n accedi\xf3 a los datos de entrenamiento, cu\xe1ndo y qu\xe9 hizo.\\nAWS Config rastrea cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos, no eventos de acceso a datos. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas. Amazon Inspector escanea en busca de vulnerabilidades. Ninguno de estos registra eventos individuales de acceso a datos para auditor\xeda."},{"id":"aif-q456","question":"Una instituci\xf3n financiera entrena modelos de ML usando datos de m\xfaltiples sistemas. Los reguladores exigen que la instituci\xf3n rastree cada pieza de datos de entrenamiento hasta su origen. \xbfQu\xe9 pr\xe1ctica cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Aumento de datos","B":"Seguimiento de linaje de datos","C":"Normalizaci\xf3n de datos","D":"Balanceo de datos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El seguimiento de linaje de datos documenta el origen, el movimiento y la transformaci\xf3n de los datos a lo largo de su ciclo de vida, de modo que la instituci\xf3n puede rastrear cada pieza de datos de entrenamiento hasta su origen y demostrar un manejo conforme a las pol\xedticas.\\nEl aumento de datos crea ejemplos de entrenamiento adicionales. La normalizaci\xf3n de datos reescala las caracter\xedsticas. El balanceo de datos ajusta las distribuciones de clases. Ninguna proporciona un registro trazable de los or\xedgenes de los datos."}]},{"id":"aws-clf-c02-open-es","title":"AWS Cloud Practitioner (CLF-C02) — banco abierto (ES)","language":"es","attribution":"Preguntas de GitHub — nastaso/cloudcertprep (https://github.com/nastaso/cloudcertprep), licencia MIT. Adaptadas al formato de este sitio. Traduccion automatica; revisar antes de publicar.","license":"MIT","questions":[{"id":"q004","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes opciones est\xe1n relacionadas con la confiabilidad de AWS?","options":{"A":"Aplicar el principio de m\xednimo privilegio a todos los recursos de AWS","B":"Aprovisionar autom\xe1ticamente nuevos recursos para satisfacer la demanda","C":"Todos los servicios de AWS se consideran servicios globales, y este dise\xf1o ayuda a los clientes a atender a sus usuarios internacionales","D":"Proporcionar compensaci\xf3n a los clientes si ocurren problemas","E":"Capacidad de recuperarse r\xe1pidamente de fallas"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Aprovisionar autom\xe1ticamente nuevos recursos para satisfacer la demanda y la capacidad de recuperarse r\xe1pidamente de fallas son ambos principios fundamentales de confiabilidad, lo que garantiza que el sistema siga funcionando correctamente en condiciones variables y despu\xe9s de eventos de falla.\\nAplicar el principio de m\xednimo privilegio es una pr\xe1ctica de seguridad relacionada con el control de acceso, no un principio de confiabilidad. Que todos los servicios de AWS se consideren servicios globales es falso, ya que muchos servicios son regionales, y el alcance global es un concepto separado de la confiabilidad. Proporcionar compensaci\xf3n a los clientes si ocurren problemas describe un mecanismo de remediaci\xf3n de SLA y no es un principio de dise\xf1o que contribuya a la confiabilidad del sistema."},{"id":"q010","question":"Una empresa japonesa aloja sus aplicaciones en instancias de Amazon EC2 en la Regi\xf3n de Tokio. La empresa ha abierto nuevas sucursales en Estados Unidos y los usuarios de EE. UU. se quejan de una latencia alta. \xbfQu\xe9 puede hacer la empresa para reducir la latencia de los usuarios en EE. UU. mientras minimiza los costos?","options":{"A":"Aplicar la pol\xedtica de enrutamiento basada en latencia de Route 53","B":"Registrar un nuevo nombre de dominio de EE. UU. para atender a los usuarios en EE. UU.","C":"Construir un nuevo centro de datos en EE. UU. e implementar un modelo h\xedbrido","D":"Implementar nuevas instancias de Amazon EC2 en una Regi\xf3n ubicada en EE. UU."},"correct":"D","explanation":"Implementar instancias de EC2 en una Regi\xf3n de EE. UU. coloca los recursos de c\xf3mputo f\xedsicamente m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios de EE. UU., lo que reduce directamente la latencia de red con un costo adicional m\xednimo.\\nAplicar una pol\xedtica de enrutamiento basada en latencia de Route 53 dirige a los usuarios a la Regi\xf3n con la latencia m\xe1s baja, pero esto solo optimiza el enrutamiento y no reduce por s\xed mismo la latencia si todos los recursos de c\xf3mputo permanecen en Tokio. Registrar un nuevo nombre de dominio de EE. UU. cambia la forma en que los usuarios llegan a la aplicaci\xf3n por nombre, pero no mueve ning\xfan recurso de c\xf3mputo m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios de EE. UU. ni reduce la latencia de red. Construir un nuevo centro de datos en EE. UU. e implementar un modelo h\xedbrido introduce un gasto de capital significativo y complejidad operativa, lo que contradice el requisito de minimizar los costos."},{"id":"q013","question":"Ajustar din\xe1micamente la capacidad de c\xf3mputo para reducir costos es una implementaci\xf3n de \xbfcu\xe1l buena pr\xe1ctica de la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Incorporar seguridad en cada capa","B":"Paralelizar tareas","C":"Implementar elasticidad","D":"Adoptar una arquitectura monol\xedtica"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La elasticidad es la buena pr\xe1ctica de la nube de ajustar din\xe1micamente la capacidad de c\xf3mputo hacia arriba o hacia abajo en respuesta a la demanda, evitando el sobreaprovisionamiento durante per\xedodos de bajo tr\xe1fico y el subaprovisionamiento durante los picos, lo que reduce directamente el costo.\\nIncorporar seguridad en cada capa es el principio de Defensa en Profundidad del pilar de Seguridad del Well-Architected Framework, que aborda la protecci\xf3n contra amenazas en lugar de la gesti\xf3n de capacidad. Paralelizar tareas es un principio de dise\xf1o de rendimiento que distribuye las cargas de trabajo entre m\xfaltiples componentes m\xe1s peque\xf1os para aumentar el rendimiento, no un mecanismo para ajustar din\xe1micamente la capacidad y controlar los costos. Adoptar una arquitectura monol\xedtica consolida todos los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n en una \xfanica unidad fuertemente acoplada, que es lo opuesto a una buena pr\xe1ctica de la nube y no admite el escalado din\xe1mico."},{"id":"q014","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son los beneficios de tener la infraestructura alojada en AWS?","options":{"A":"Aumentar la velocidad y la agilidad","B":"Obtener almacenamiento gratuito ilimitado para todos los datos","C":"Obtener control completo sobre la infraestructura f\xedsica","D":"Operar aplicaciones en nombre de los clientes","E":"Toda la seguridad f\xedsica y la mayor parte de la seguridad de los datos y de la red se gestionan para usted"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS aumenta la velocidad y la agilidad al permitir que los clientes aprovisionen recursos en minutos en lugar de semanas, y gestiona la seguridad f\xedsica junto con la mayor parte de la seguridad de datos y red para que los clientes puedan centrarse en crear aplicaciones en lugar de gestionar infraestructura.\\nEl almacenamiento gratuito ilimitado no es un beneficio de AWS, ya que los servicios de almacenamiento como Amazon S3 y EBS se facturan seg\xfan la cantidad de datos almacenados y recuperados. El control completo sobre la infraestructura f\xedsica no est\xe1 disponible para los clientes de AWS, ya que AWS posee y gestiona todo el hardware f\xedsico, los centros de datos y el equipo de red en nombre del cliente. Operar aplicaciones en nombre de los clientes no es un beneficio de AWS, ya que los clientes siguen siendo responsables de crear, implementar y gestionar sus propias aplicaciones bajo el modelo de responsabilidad compartida."},{"id":"q015","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la ventaja de la pr\xe1ctica recomendada por AWS de \\"desacoplar\\" aplicaciones?","options":{"A":"Permite tratar una aplicaci\xf3n como una \xfanica unidad cohesiva","B":"Reduce las interdependencias para que las fallas no impacten otros componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n","C":"Permite actualizar cualquier aplicaci\xf3n monol\xedtica de forma r\xe1pida y sencilla","D":"Permite rastrear cualquier llamada a la API realizada a cualquier servicio de AWS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El desacoplamiento significa dise\xf1ar componentes para que funcionen de forma independiente, de modo que una falla en una parte no se propague en fallas por el resto de la aplicaci\xf3n, mejorando la resiliencia general.\\nTratar una aplicaci\xf3n como una \xfanica unidad cohesiva describe una arquitectura monol\xedtica, que es lo opuesto al desacoplamiento y crea dependencias fuertemente acopladas entre componentes. Permitir actualizaciones r\xe1pidas de una aplicaci\xf3n monol\xedtica describe una ventaja de un monolito bien estructurado, no del desacoplamiento, y actualizar un monolito normalmente requiere implementar toda la aplicaci\xf3n como una unidad. Rastrear llamadas a la API realizadas a servicios de AWS describe la funci\xf3n de AWS CloudTrail, que es un servicio de auditor\xeda y registro no relacionado con el dise\xf1o de la arquitectura de aplicaciones."},{"id":"q019","question":"Una de las buenas pr\xe1cticas de AWS m\xe1s importantes que se deben seguir es el principio de elasticidad de la arquitectura de la nube. \xbfC\xf3mo mejora este principio el dise\xf1o de su arquitectura?","options":{"A":"Al escalar autom\xe1ticamente sus recursos locales seg\xfan los cambios en la demanda","B":"Al escalar autom\xe1ticamente sus recursos de AWS usando un Elastic Load Balancer","C":"Al reducir las interdependencias entre los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n siempre que sea posible","D":"Al aprovisionar autom\xe1ticamente los recursos de AWS requeridos seg\xfan los cambios en la demanda"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La elasticidad en AWS significa que la plataforma aprovisiona y desaprovisiona recursos autom\xe1ticamente en respuesta a cambios en la demanda en tiempo real, de modo que las cargas de trabajo consumen solo lo que necesitan y se reducen cuando la demanda disminuye.\\nEscalar autom\xe1ticamente recursos locales no es lo que describe la elasticidad en el contexto de AWS; la elasticidad es una caracter\xedstica de la nube que elimina la necesidad de invertir en hardware local, ya que el proveedor de la nube ajusta din\xe1micamente la capacidad de c\xf3mputo. Un Elastic Load Balancer distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre las instancias existentes para evitar que una sola instancia se sobrecargue, pero no agrega ni elimina instancias en respuesta a la demanda; ese ajuste de capacidad lo realiza Auto Scaling. Reducir las interdependencias entre los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n describe el principio de dise\xf1o de acoplamiento d\xe9bil, que mejora la resiliencia y el mantenimiento, pero es un concepto arquitect\xf3nico separado de la elasticidad."},{"id":"q023","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes NO pertenece a los modelos de computaci\xf3n en la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Plataforma como servicio (PaaS)","B":"Infraestructura como servicio (IaaS)","C":"Software como servicio (SaaS)","D":"Redes como servicio (NaaS)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Los tres modelos de servicio de computaci\xf3n en la nube reconocidos por AWS son IaaS, PaaS y SaaS. Redes como servicio no es uno de ellos y no existe como un modelo de computaci\xf3n en la nube definido en el marco de AWS.\\nPlataforma como servicio es uno de los tres modelos de computaci\xf3n en la nube reconocidos, que proporciona a los clientes una plataforma gestionada para desarrollar e implementar aplicaciones sin gestionar la infraestructura subyacente. Infraestructura como servicio es uno de los tres modelos de computaci\xf3n en la nube reconocidos, que brinda a los clientes recursos virtualizados de c\xf3mputo, almacenamiento y red que ellos mismos gestionan. Software como servicio es uno de los tres modelos de computaci\xf3n en la nube reconocidos, que entrega aplicaciones totalmente gestionadas a los usuarios finales por internet sin requerir ninguna gesti\xf3n de infraestructura ni plataforma."},{"id":"q065","question":"Seg\xfan las buenas pr\xe1cticas, \xbfc\xf3mo se debe dise\xf1ar una aplicaci\xf3n para ejecutarse en la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Usar componentes fuertemente acoplados","B":"Usar componentes d\xe9bilmente acoplados","C":"Usar componentes monol\xedticos","D":"Usar componentes con estado"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Usar componentes d\xe9bilmente acoplados significa que cada parte de una aplicaci\xf3n puede escalar, fallar y actualizarse de forma independiente sin propagar fallas a otros componentes, lo cual es la buena pr\xe1ctica fundamental de AWS para construir arquitecturas resilientes y escalables.\\nUsar componentes fuertemente acoplados crea dependencias directas entre las partes de una aplicaci\xf3n, de modo que una falla o un cambio en un componente afecta directamente a los dem\xe1s, lo que aumenta el riesgo de fallas en cascada y hace que el sistema sea m\xe1s dif\xedcil de escalar y mantener de forma independiente. Un dise\xf1o monol\xedtico combina todos los componentes en una sola unidad que es dif\xedcil de escalar o actualizar de forma independiente, lo que es lo opuesto al enfoque modular que recomienda AWS. Los componentes con estado almacenan datos de sesi\xf3n localmente en una instancia espec\xedfica, lo que impide que las solicitudes se distribuyan libremente entre las instancias y socava el escalado horizontal y la resiliencia."},{"id":"q077","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo reduce AWS el tiempo para aprovisionar recursos de TI?","options":{"A":"Proporciona una plataforma en l\xednea de tickets de TI para solicitudes de recursos","B":"Admite servicios de validaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de c\xf3digo","C":"Brinda la capacidad de aprovisionar recursos existentes mediante programaci\xf3n","D":"Automatiza el proceso de solicitud de recursos a partir de la lista de proveedores de TI de una empresa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS permite aprovisionar recursos mediante programaci\xf3n a trav\xe9s de API, plantillas de CloudFormation y SDK, lo que reduce dr\xe1sticamente el tiempo para implementar infraestructura en comparaci\xf3n con la adquisici\xf3n tradicional y la configuraci\xf3n f\xedsica.\\nAWS no opera una plataforma de tickets de TI para solicitudes de recursos. Los recursos se aprovisionan bajo demanda mediante API y consolas de autoservicio sin requerir flujos formales de solicitud y aprobaci\xf3n. AWS no reduce el tiempo de aprovisionamiento mediante servicios de validaci\xf3n de c\xf3digo, que son una herramienta de calidad de software no relacionada con la rapidez con la que se puede implementar infraestructura. AWS no automatiza las solicitudes a partir de la lista existente de proveedores de TI de una empresa, ya que reemplaza por completo la adquisici\xf3n tradicional de proveedores al proporcionar infraestructura directamente a trav\xe9s de su propia plataforma."},{"id":"q084","question":"Los servidores web que se ejecutan en Amazon EC2 acceden a una aplicaci\xf3n heredada que se ejecuta en un centro de datos corporativo. \xbfQu\xe9 t\xe9rmino describir\xeda este modelo?","options":{"A":"Nativo de la nube","B":"Red de socios","C":"Arquitectura h\xedbrida","D":"Infraestructura como servicio"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cuando las aplicaciones en la nube de AWS se comunican con una aplicaci\xf3n heredada en un centro de datos corporativo, se trata de una arquitectura h\xedbrida que combina recursos locales y de la nube en una \xfanica soluci\xf3n.\\nNativo de la nube describe aplicaciones dise\xf1adas y construidas completamente para ejecutarse en la nube, sin dependencia de infraestructura local ni sistemas heredados. Red de socios se refiere a AWS Partner Network, que es un programa para empresas de tecnolog\xeda y consultor\xeda que crean soluciones en AWS, y no es un modelo de implementaci\xf3n. Infraestructura como servicio es un modelo de servicio en la nube que proporciona recursos virtualizados de c\xf3mputo, almacenamiento y redes, y no describe la combinaci\xf3n de componentes de la nube y locales en una \xfanica arquitectura."},{"id":"q099","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 principio de dise\xf1o de arquitectura en la nube respalda la distribuci\xf3n de cargas de trabajo entre varias Availability Zones?","options":{"A":"Implementar automatizaci\xf3n","B":"Dise\xf1ar para la agilidad","C":"Dise\xf1ar para el fallo","D":"Implementar elasticidad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Distribuir cargas de trabajo entre varias Availability Zones garantiza que la aplicaci\xf3n pueda seguir funcionando si una AZ falla, lo que encarna el principio de dise\xf1ar para el fallo, donde la arquitectura anticipa y acomoda fallos de componentes.\\nImplementar automatizaci\xf3n se refiere a reducir la intervenci\xf3n manual mediante scripts y servicios administrados, no a la tolerancia a fallos entre zonas. Dise\xf1ar para la agilidad se refiere a la capacidad de experimentar e implementar r\xe1pidamente, no a manejar fallos de infraestructura. Implementar elasticidad se refiere a escalar recursos autom\xe1ticamente en respuesta a la demanda, no a distribuir cargas de trabajo para tolerancia a fallos."},{"id":"q128","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una ventaja de implementar una aplicaci\xf3n en varias Availability Zones?","options":{"A":"Existe un menor riesgo de fallo del servicio si un desastre natural causa una interrupci\xf3n del servicio en una regi\xf3n de AWS determinada","B":"La aplicaci\xf3n tendr\xe1 mayor disponibilidad porque puede soportar una interrupci\xf3n del servicio en una Availability Zone","C":"Habr\xe1 una mejor cobertura, ya que las Availability Zones est\xe1n geogr\xe1ficamente distantes y pueden dar servicio a un \xe1rea m\xe1s amplia","D":"Habr\xe1 una latencia de aplicaci\xf3n reducida que mejorar\xe1 la experiencia del usuario"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Implementar en varias Availability Zones mantiene una aplicaci\xf3n en funcionamiento cuando una AZ experimenta una interrupci\xf3n, ya que el tr\xe1fico conmuta a las instancias de las AZ restantes, lo que aumenta directamente la disponibilidad.\\nVarias AZ dentro de una sola regi\xf3n no protegen contra un desastre natural que afecte a toda la regi\xf3n, ya que todas las AZ de una regi\xf3n comparten la misma \xe1rea geogr\xe1fica y estar\xedan expuestas a la misma interrupci\xf3n regional. Las Availability Zones dentro de una regi\xf3n est\xe1n intencionalmente cerca unas de otras con enlaces de baja latencia entre ellas, por lo que no proporcionan una cobertura geogr\xe1fica m\xe1s amplia ni dan servicio a usuarios en ubicaciones distantes. Implementar en varias AZ redistribuye los recursos existentes en lugar de reducir la distancia entre la aplicaci\xf3n y sus usuarios, por lo que no disminuye la latencia."},{"id":"q130","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un principio de dise\xf1o de arquitectura en la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Implementar puntos \xfanicos de fallo","B":"Implementar acoplamiento d\xe9bil","C":"Implementar dise\xf1o monol\xedtico","D":"Implementar escalado vertical"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El acoplamiento d\xe9bil es un principio central de la arquitectura en la nube de AWS en el que los componentes interact\xfaan a trav\xe9s de interfaces bien definidas, lo que permite que cada uno escale, se actualice o falle de forma independiente sin efectos en cascada sobre otras partes del sistema.\\nImplementar puntos \xfanicos de fallo es un antipatr\xf3n que contradice directamente las mejores pr\xe1cticas de arquitectura en la nube, ya que el objetivo es eliminar dependencias que podr\xedan derribar un sistema completo. El dise\xf1o monol\xedtico acopla estrechamente todos los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n en una sola unidad, lo que dificulta escalar, actualizar o recuperar partes individuales de forma independiente. El escalado vertical aumenta la capacidad de una sola instancia en lugar de distribuir cargas de trabajo, lo que limita la escalabilidad y crea posibles puntos \xfanicos de fallo."},{"id":"q132","question":"Una empresa se trasladar\xe1 de un centro de datos local a la nube de AWS. \xbfCu\xe1l ser\xeda una diferencia financiera despu\xe9s del traslado?","options":{"A":"Pasar de gastos operativos variables (opex) a gastos de capital iniciales (capex)","B":"Pasar de gastos de capital iniciales (capex) a gastos de capital variables (capex)","C":"Pasar de gastos de capital iniciales (capex) a gastos operativos variables (opex)","D":"Eliminaci\xf3n de los gastos de capital iniciales (capex) y eliminaci\xf3n de los gastos operativos variables (opex)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La infraestructura local requiere grandes gastos de capital iniciales para hardware e instalaciones, mientras que AWS convierte ese gasto en gastos operativos variables, en los que solo se paga por lo que se consume sin necesidad de comprar hardware.\\nPasar de gastos operativos variables (opex) a gastos de capital iniciales (capex): esto invierte la direcci\xf3n real del cambio, y los entornos locales se caracterizan por capex, no por opex, como punto de partida. Pasar de gastos de capital iniciales (capex) a gastos de capital variables (capex): el uso de AWS se factura como gasto operativo, no como gasto de capital, por lo que la categor\xeda de destino en esta opci\xf3n es incorrecta. Eliminaci\xf3n de los gastos de capital iniciales (capex) y eliminaci\xf3n de los gastos operativos variables (opex): trasladarse a AWS elimina capex pero introduce opex en lugar de eliminarlo, ya que los costos de uso continuo se pagan como gasto operativo."},{"id":"q141","question":"Una de las ventajas de trasladar la infraestructura de un centro de datos local a la nube de AWS es:","options":{"A":"Permite a la empresa eliminar las facturas de TI","B":"Permite a la empresa colocar un servidor en el centro de datos de cada cliente","C":"Permite a la empresa centrarse en las actividades del negocio","D":"Permite a la empresa dejar los servidores sin parches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Al delegar la administraci\xf3n de la infraestructura a AWS, las empresas pueden redirigir su personal y recursos de TI hacia el desarrollo y la mejora de sus productos y servicios principales, en lugar de mantener centros de datos.\\nAWS no elimina las facturas de TI, ya que los clientes siguen pagando por los servicios que consumen bajo el modelo de pago por uso. Colocar un servidor en el centro de datos de cada cliente describe un modelo de implementaci\xf3n local o en el borde, que es lo opuesto a migrar la infraestructura a la nube. Trasladarse a AWS no permite que los servidores queden sin parches, ya que el Modelo de Responsabilidad Compartida todav\xeda requiere que los clientes apliquen parches a los sistemas operativos y las aplicaciones que se ejecutan en sus propias instancias."},{"id":"q143","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puede un usuario protegerse contra interrupciones del servicio de AWS si un desastre natural afecta a toda un \xe1rea geogr\xe1fica?","options":{"A":"Implementar aplicaciones en varias Availability Zones dentro de una regi\xf3n de AWS","B":"Usar un modelo de implementaci\xf3n de computaci\xf3n en la nube h\xedbrida dentro del \xe1rea geogr\xe1fica","C":"Implementar aplicaciones en varias regiones de AWS","D":"Almacenar artefactos de aplicaciones usando AWS Artifact y replicarlos en varias regiones de AWS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Un desastre natural que afecte a toda un \xe1rea geogr\xe1fica podr\xeda dejar inoperativa una regi\xf3n completa de AWS, por lo que implementar en varias regiones en distintas geograf\xedas garantiza que la aplicaci\xf3n siga operativa incluso si una regi\xf3n queda completamente no disponible.\\nImplementar en varias Availability Zones dentro de una sola regi\xf3n protege contra fallos aislados de centros de datos, pero no proporciona protecci\xf3n si toda la regi\xf3n y su \xe1rea geogr\xe1fica circundante se ven afectadas por un desastre natural. Usar un modelo de implementaci\xf3n de nube h\xedbrida dentro de la misma \xe1rea geogr\xe1fica mantiene la infraestructura en la zona afectada, lo que significa que un desastre natural regional seguir\xeda interrumpiendo simult\xe1neamente tanto los componentes locales como los de la nube. AWS Artifact es un portal para acceder a informes de cumplimiento y documentaci\xf3n de seguridad de AWS, no un servicio de almacenamiento o replicaci\xf3n, lo que hace que esta opci\xf3n sea incorrecta en cuanto a los hechos como estrategia de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres."},{"id":"q146","question":"Un cliente est\xe1 implementando una nueva aplicaci\xf3n y necesita elegir una Regi\xf3n de AWS. \xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes factores podr\xedan influir en la decisi\xf3n del cliente?","options":{"A":"Menor latencia para los usuarios","B":"La presentaci\xf3n de la aplicaci\xf3n en el idioma local","C":"Cumplimiento de soberan\xeda de datos","D":"Costos de refrigeraci\xf3n en climas m\xe1s c\xe1lidos","E":"Proximidad a la oficina del cliente para visitas presenciales"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"La proximidad a los usuarios finales reduce la latencia de red para una mejor experiencia de la aplicaci\xf3n, y los requisitos de cumplimiento de soberan\xeda de datos obligan a que los datos permanezcan dentro de ciertos l\xedmites geogr\xe1ficos, por lo que ambos son factores principales en la selecci\xf3n de Regi\xf3n.\\nPresentar una aplicaci\xf3n en el idioma local es una decisi\xf3n de localizaci\xf3n de la capa de aplicaci\xf3n que se gestiona mediante configuraci\xf3n de software, no un factor que influya en en qu\xe9 Regi\xf3n de AWS se implementa la infraestructura. Los costos de refrigeraci\xf3n en climas m\xe1s c\xe1lidos son un gasto operativo que AWS absorbe por completo como parte de la gesti\xf3n de sus propios centros de datos, y no son un costo ni una consideraci\xf3n que los clientes incluyan en la selecci\xf3n de Regi\xf3n. La proximidad a la oficina del cliente para visitas presenciales no es un criterio t\xe9cnico ni regulatorio v\xe1lido para la selecci\xf3n de Regi\xf3n, ya que los centros de datos de AWS no son instalaciones accesibles para los clientes."},{"id":"q148","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l escenario de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres ofrece la menor probabilidad de tiempo de inactividad?","options":{"A":"Copia de seguridad y restauraci\xf3n","B":"Pilot light","C":"Warm standby","D":"Activo-activo multisitio"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Multi-site active-active ejecuta copias activas completamente redundantes de la aplicaci\xf3n en varios sitios simult\xe1neamente, de modo que si un sitio falla, el otro atiende inmediatamente todo el tr\xe1fico con pr\xe1cticamente cero tiempo de inactividad.\\nBackup and restore tiene el mayor tiempo de recuperaci\xf3n de todas las estrategias de DR. Los datos se restauran desde las copias de seguridad despu\xe9s de una falla, lo que implica un tiempo de inactividad significativo. Pilot light mantiene en ejecuci\xf3n una versi\xf3n m\xednima del entorno y escala durante un desastre, lo que da como resultado un tiempo de recuperaci\xf3n corto pero distinto de cero. Warm standby ejecuta una versi\xf3n reducida pero totalmente funcional del entorno, que requiere escalar antes de manejar la carga completa de tr\xe1fico."},{"id":"q153","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 puede ayudar a evaluar una aplicaci\xf3n para migrarla a la nube?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Professional Services","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"AWS Partner Network (APN)","E":"AWS Secrets Manager"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Professional Services ofrece servicios de consultor\xeda especializados para ayudar a evaluar y planificar migraciones, y los socios de AWS Partner Network ofrecen experiencia especializada en migraciones mediante empresas consultoras y de tecnolog\xeda certificadas.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor analiza los entornos de AWS existentes para obtener recomendaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas en costo, rendimiento, seguridad y tolerancia a fallos, pero no eval\xfaa si una aplicaci\xf3n es apta para migrarla a la nube. AWS Systems Manager es un servicio de gesti\xf3n operativa para configurar, aplicar parches y gestionar recursos de AWS a escala, y no es un servicio de asesor\xeda o evaluaci\xf3n de migraciones. AWS Secrets Manager es un servicio para almacenar y rotar secretos de aplicaciones, como credenciales de bases de datos y claves de API, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en la evaluaci\xf3n de aplicaciones para la migraci\xf3n a la nube."},{"id":"q156","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una propuesta de valor de AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS es responsable de la seguridad en AWS Cloud","B":"No se requiere un contrato a largo plazo","C":"Aprovisionar nuevos servidores en d\xedas","D":"AWS gestiona las aplicaciones de usuario en AWS Cloud"},"correct":"B","explanation":"No se requiere un contrato a largo plazo porque AWS utiliza un modelo de pago por uso, lo que permite a los clientes usar servicios bajo demanda y detenerlos en cualquier momento sin compromisos iniciales ni penalizaciones por cancelaci\xf3n.\\nQue AWS es responsable de la seguridad en la nube es una verdad parcial que tergiversa el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida. La seguridad es compartida: AWS es responsable de la infraestructura y los clientes son responsables de sus datos y configuraciones. Aprovisionar nuevos servidores en d\xedas es inexacto. Una de las ventajas clave de AWS es aprovisionar recursos en minutos, no en d\xedas, que es la realidad que distingue a la nube de las adquisiciones tradicionales. Que AWS gestiona las aplicaciones de usuario en AWS Cloud es falso. La gesti\xf3n de aplicaciones sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida."},{"id":"q161","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son los beneficios de desarrollar y ejecutar una nueva aplicaci\xf3n en AWS Cloud en comparaci\xf3n con on-premises?","options":{"A":"AWS distribuye autom\xe1ticamente los datos a nivel global para una mayor durabilidad","B":"AWS se encargar\xe1 de operar la aplicaci\xf3n","C":"AWS facilita el dise\xf1o de arquitecturas para alta disponibilidad","D":"AWS puede adaptarse f\xe1cilmente a los cambios en la demanda de la aplicaci\xf3n","E":"AWS se encarga de aplicar parches de seguridad a la aplicaci\xf3n"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"AWS facilita el dise\xf1o para alta disponibilidad mediante implementaciones multi-AZ, balanceadores de carga y servicios administrados, y la elasticidad de la nube permite que las aplicaciones escalen hacia arriba o hacia abajo para adaptarse r\xe1pidamente a los cambios en la demanda.\\nAWS no distribuye autom\xe1ticamente todos los datos a nivel global de forma predeterminada, ya que la residencia y la replicaci\xf3n de datos son decisiones de configuraci\xf3n que el cliente toma seg\xfan los servicios y las opciones de configuraci\xf3n que seleccione. AWS no se encarga de operar la aplicaci\xf3n, ya que los clientes siguen siendo responsables de implementar, administrar y mantener su propio c\xf3digo de aplicaci\xf3n y entornos de ejecuci\xf3n. AWS no se encarga de aplicar parches de seguridad a la aplicaci\xf3n, ya que aplicar parches a la capa de aplicaci\xf3n es responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida."},{"id":"q173","question":"Una soluci\xf3n que puede soportar el crecimiento en usuarios, tr\xe1fico o tama\xf1o de los datos sin disminuci\xf3n del rendimiento, \xbfcon qu\xe9 principio de arquitectura en la nube se alinea?","options":{"A":"Pensar en paralelo","B":"Implementar elasticidad","C":"Desacoplar los componentes","D":"Dise\xf1ar para fallos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La elasticidad es la capacidad de escalar recursos hacia arriba o hacia abajo autom\xe1ticamente para manejar aumentos en usuarios, tr\xe1fico o tama\xf1o de los datos sin ninguna degradaci\xf3n del rendimiento, lo que la convierte en el principio que aborda directamente el soporte al crecimiento.\\nPensar en paralelo se refiere al principio de dise\xf1ar sistemas para manejar tareas simult\xe1neamente en m\xfaltiples recursos para mejorar la capacidad de procesamiento y la velocidad, no espec\xedficamente para manejar el crecimiento en usuarios o tama\xf1o de los datos sin p\xe9rdida de rendimiento. Desacoplar los componentes se refiere a reducir las dependencias entre los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n para que los fallos no se propaguen en cascada, lo que mejora la resiliencia en lugar de abordar el rendimiento bajo crecimiento. Dise\xf1ar para fallos se refiere a construir sistemas que anticipen y se recuperen autom\xe1ticamente de fallos de componentes, lo que aborda la resiliencia y la disponibilidad en lugar del escalado para soportar el crecimiento."},{"id":"q174","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 beneficio de AWS Cloud elimina la necesidad de que los usuarios intenten estimar el uso futuro de la infraestructura?","options":{"A":"Implementaci\xf3n f\xe1cil y r\xe1pida de aplicaciones en m\xfaltiples Regiones de todo el mundo","B":"Seguridad de AWS Cloud","C":"Elasticidad de AWS Cloud","D":"Menores costos variables debido a econom\xedas de escala masivas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La elasticidad de la nube permite que los recursos escalen hacia arriba o hacia abajo seg\xfan la demanda, lo que elimina la necesidad de predecir y aprovisionar de antemano la capacidad m\xe1xima futura.\\nLa implementaci\xf3n f\xe1cil y r\xe1pida de aplicaciones en m\xfaltiples Regiones es un beneficio de la infraestructura global de AWS, que permite velocidad y alcance, pero no elimina la necesidad de estimar el uso futuro de la infraestructura. La seguridad de AWS Cloud se refiere a las protecciones que AWS proporciona para su infraestructura seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, lo cual no est\xe1 relacionado con la planificaci\xf3n de capacidad ni con la estimaci\xf3n de infraestructura. Los menores costos variables debido a econom\xedas de escala masivas significan que AWS puede ofrecer precios reducidos a medida que crece su base de clientes, pero los menores costos no eliminan la necesidad subyacente de estimar cu\xe1nta infraestructura se requerir\xe1."},{"id":"q187","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puede AWS Cloud aumentar la productividad de la fuerza laboral de los usuarios despu\xe9s de la migraci\xf3n desde un centro de datos local?","options":{"A":"Los usuarios no tienen que esperar el aprovisionamiento de infraestructura","B":"La infraestructura de AWS Cloud es mucho m\xe1s r\xe1pida que la infraestructura de un centro de datos local","C":"AWS se hace cargo de la administraci\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n de la aplicaci\xf3n en nombre de los usuarios","D":"Los usuarios no necesitan abordar cuestiones de seguridad y cumplimiento"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Con AWS, los desarrolladores e ingenieros pueden autoprovisionar infraestructura en minutos a trav\xe9s de la consola o las API, lo que elimina el tiempo de espera asociado con la adquisici\xf3n y configuraci\xf3n tradicional de hardware.\\nLa afirmaci\xf3n de que la infraestructura de AWS Cloud es mucho m\xe1s r\xe1pida que la infraestructura de un centro de datos local es enga\xf1osa. La ganancia de productividad proviene de eliminar las demoras de aprovisionamiento, no de la velocidad bruta de la infraestructura. La afirmaci\xf3n de que AWS se hace cargo de la administraci\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n de la aplicaci\xf3n en nombre de los usuarios es falsa. La configuraci\xf3n de la aplicaci\xf3n sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida. La afirmaci\xf3n de que los usuarios no necesitan abordar cuestiones de seguridad y cumplimiento es falsa. Los clientes siguen siendo responsables de la seguridad en la nube, incluidos sus aplicaciones, datos y controles de acceso."},{"id":"q189","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es un ejemplo de agilidad en la AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Acceso a varios tipos de instancia","B":"Acceso a servicios administrados","C":"Uso de Consolidated Billing para generar una sola factura","D":"Menor tiempo de adquisici\xf3n de nuevos recursos de c\xf3mputo"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La agilidad en la AWS Cloud se ilustra mejor con la dr\xe1stica reducci\xf3n del tiempo para adquirir y aprovisionar nuevos recursos de c\xf3mputo, que pasa de semanas en las instalaciones a minutos en la nube, lo que permite a los equipos experimentar e iterar m\xe1s r\xe1pido.\\nEl acceso a varios tipos de instancia es un beneficio de flexibilidad que permite a los clientes ajustar el c\xf3mputo a los requisitos de la carga de trabajo, pero no describe directamente la velocidad de adquisici\xf3n de nuevos recursos. El acceso a servicios administrados reduce la sobrecarga operativa al transferir a AWS la gesti\xf3n de la infraestructura, pero es un ejemplo de menor complejidad en lugar de agilidad. Consolidated Billing es una funci\xf3n de gesti\xf3n de costos que combina los cargos de varias cuentas en una sola factura, lo que constituye una comodidad de facturaci\xf3n y no est\xe1 relacionado con la velocidad de adquisici\xf3n de recursos."},{"id":"q203","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada al dise\xf1ar soluciones en AWS?","options":{"A":"Invertir mucho en dise\xf1ar la arquitectura de tu entorno, ya que no es f\xe1cil cambiar el dise\xf1o m\xe1s adelante","B":"Usar reservas de AWS para reducir costos cuando pruebas tu entorno de producci\xf3n","C":"Automatizar siempre que sea posible para facilitar la experimentaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica","D":"Aprovisionar una gran capacidad de c\xf3mputo para manejar cualquier pico de carga"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La pr\xe1ctica recomendada de AWS es automatizar la infraestructura y el despliegue siempre que sea posible, lo que hace m\xe1s r\xe1pida la experimentaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica y reduce el error humano.\\nInvertir mucho en el dise\xf1o arquitect\xf3nico inicial contradice el principio de la nube de que la infraestructura debe tratarse como flexible y modificable, con automatizaci\xf3n que permita iterar r\xe1pidamente en lugar de exigir una planificaci\xf3n intensa antes del despliegue. Usar reservas de AWS para reducir costos al probar entornos de producci\xf3n aplica mal las Reserved Instances, que son un compromiso de facturaci\xf3n para cargas de trabajo predecibles en estado estable y no una herramienta de reducci\xf3n de costos para entornos de prueba. Aprovisionar una gran capacidad de c\xf3mputo para manejar cualquier pico de carga describe el sobreaprovisionamiento, que es un antipatr\xf3n en las instalaciones que las pr\xe1cticas recomendadas de la nube reemplazan con escalado el\xe1stico que ajusta din\xe1micamente la capacidad a la demanda real."},{"id":"q206","question":"El principio “dise\xf1ar para el fallo y nada fallar\xe1” es muy importante al dise\xf1ar tu arquitectura de AWS Cloud. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes ayudar\xeda a cumplir este principio?","options":{"A":"Autenticaci\xf3n multifactor","B":"Availability Zones","C":"Elastic Load Balancing","D":"Pruebas de penetraci\xf3n","E":"Escalado vertical"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Desplegar en varias Availability Zones proporciona redundancia f\xedsica para que, si una zona experimenta un fallo, las instancias de las zonas restantes contin\xfaen atendiendo el tr\xe1fico sin interrupci\xf3n. Elastic Load Balancing detecta autom\xe1ticamente los destinos en mal estado y enruta el tr\xe1fico solo a los saludables, lo que evita que una sola instancia fallida derribe toda la aplicaci\xf3n. Ambos implementan directamente el principio de dise\xf1o para el fallo.\\nLa autenticaci\xf3n multifactor agrega una segunda capa de seguridad al inicio de sesi\xf3n de la cuenta y los usuarios, y es un control de seguridad de acceso sin relaci\xf3n con la tolerancia a fallos ni la resiliencia de la aplicaci\xf3n. Las pruebas de penetraci\xf3n simulan ataques contra una aplicaci\xf3n para identificar vulnerabilidades de seguridad, y son una pr\xe1ctica de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad en lugar de un enfoque de dise\xf1o arquitect\xf3nico para sobrevivir a fallos de componentes. El escalado vertical aumenta la capacidad de una sola instancia al agregar m\xe1s CPU o memoria, lo que crea un \xfanico punto de fallo m\xe1s grande en lugar de distribuir el riesgo, y es lo opuesto a la redundancia que requiere el dise\xf1o para el fallo."},{"id":"q215","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es uno de los beneficios de mover la infraestructura de un centro de datos local a AWS?","options":{"A":"Soporte gratuito para todos los clientes empresariales","B":"Protecci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de datos","C":"Menor gasto de capital (CapEx)","D":"AWS se encarga de gestionar las aplicaciones de los clientes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Migrar de un entorno local a AWS convierte grandes gastos de capital iniciales, como la compra y el mantenimiento de hardware f\xedsico, en gastos operativos variables m\xe1s peque\xf1os, lo que reduce directamente el CapEx y mejora la flexibilidad financiera.\\nAWS no proporciona soporte gratuito a todos los clientes empresariales, ya que el soporte est\xe1 disponible mediante niveles de pago que van de Developer a Enterprise, con costos que var\xedan seg\xfan el plan. AWS no protege autom\xe1ticamente los datos en nombre de los clientes, ya que las responsabilidades de protecci\xf3n de datos, como el cifrado y la configuraci\xf3n de copias de seguridad, siguen siendo del cliente seg\xfan el Modelo de Responsabilidad Compartida. AWS no gestiona las aplicaciones de los clientes, ya que las responsabilidades de la capa de aplicaci\xf3n, incluidos el c\xf3digo, la configuraci\xf3n y la gesti\xf3n del runtime, siguen siendo del cliente independientemente de los servicios de infraestructura que utilice."},{"id":"q216","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son principios de dise\xf1o importantes que deber\xedas adoptar al dise\xf1ar sistemas en AWS?","options":{"A":"Usar siempre Global Services en tu arquitectura en lugar de Regional Services","B":"Elegir siempre pago por uso","C":"Tratar los servidores como recursos fijos","D":"Automatizar siempre que sea posible","E":"Eliminar los puntos \xfanicos de fallo"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Automatizar siempre que sea posible reduce los errores manuales y acelera la recuperaci\xf3n, y eliminar los puntos \xfanicos de fallo mediante redundancia garantiza la operaci\xf3n continua si un componente falla; ambos son principios de dise\xf1o fundamentales de AWS.\\nUsar siempre Global Services en lugar de Regional Services no es un principio de dise\xf1o reconocido, ya que la elecci\xf3n entre servicios globales y regionales depende de los requisitos espec\xedficos de la carga de trabajo y no es una regla arquitect\xf3nica general. Elegir siempre pagar por uso es una caracter\xedstica del modelo de facturaci\xf3n en lugar de un principio de dise\xf1o de sistemas, y las decisiones de precios se toman con base en objetivos de optimizaci\xf3n de costos, no en patrones arquitect\xf3nicos. Tratar los servidores como recursos fijos contradice el principio de dise\xf1o de la nube de elasticidad, que exige tratar la infraestructura como desechable y escalable din\xe1micamente en lugar de permanente y est\xe1tica."},{"id":"q225","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son dos ventajas de usar la computaci\xf3n en la nube en lugar de centros de datos tradicionales?","options":{"A":"Capacidad de c\xf3mputo reservada","B":"Eliminaci\xf3n de puntos \xfanicos de fallo (SPOFs)","C":"Infraestructura distribuida","D":"Recursos de c\xf3mputo virtualizados","E":"Hosting dedicado"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"La computaci\xf3n en la nube elimina los puntos \xfanicos de fallo mediante redundancia en varios centros de datos, y su infraestructura distribuida geogr\xe1ficamente significa que ning\xfan fallo f\xedsico \xfanico puede derribar todo el sistema, ambas ventajas estructurales sobre los centros de datos tradicionales.\\nLa capacidad de c\xf3mputo reservada requiere un compromiso inicial con recursos fijos y existe tanto en entornos de nube como tradicionales, por lo que no es una ventaja diferenciadora de la nube. Los recursos de c\xf3mputo virtualizados se utilizan tanto en centros de datos tradicionales como en la nube, lo que hace que la virtualizaci\xf3n en s\xed sea una caracter\xedstica compartida en lugar de una ventaja de la nube. El hosting dedicado asigna hardware f\xedsico exclusivamente a un cliente y est\xe1 disponible tanto en entornos de nube como tradicionales, por lo que no representa una ventaja de la computaci\xf3n en la nube sobre la infraestructura tradicional."},{"id":"q227","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 declaraci\xf3n describe mejor el pilar de excelencia operativa del AWS Well-Architected Framework?","options":{"A":"La capacidad de un sistema de recuperarse correctamente de un fallo","B":"El uso eficiente de los recursos de c\xf3mputo para cumplir los requisitos","C":"La capacidad de monitorear sistemas y mejorar los procesos y procedimientos de soporte","D":"La capacidad de gestionar las operaciones del centro de datos de forma m\xe1s eficiente"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El pilar de Excelencia Operativa del AWS Well-Architected Framework se centra en ejecutar y monitorear las cargas de trabajo de forma eficaz, mejorar continuamente los procesos y automatizar las operaciones para aportar valor empresarial.\\nLa capacidad de un sistema de recuperarse correctamente de un fallo describe el pilar de Confiabilidad, que se centra en la disponibilidad de la carga de trabajo y la capacidad de recuperarse de interrupciones. El uso eficiente de los recursos de c\xf3mputo para cumplir los requisitos describe el pilar de Eficiencia del Rendimiento, que se centra en usar los tipos y tama\xf1os de recursos adecuados para satisfacer las demandas de la carga de trabajo. Gestionar las operaciones del centro de datos de forma m\xe1s eficiente no corresponde a ning\xfan pilar del Well-Architected, ya que AWS gestiona toda la infraestructura f\xedsica del centro de datos en nombre de los clientes y esto no es una preocupaci\xf3n del cliente en la nube."},{"id":"q242","question":"Jessica gestiona una aplicaci\xf3n web de comercio electr\xf3nico en AWS. La aplicaci\xf3n est\xe1 alojada en seis instancias EC2. Un d\xeda, tres de las instancias fallaron; pero ninguno de sus clientes se vio afectado. \xbfQu\xe9 ha hecho Jessica correctamente en este escenario?","options":{"A":"Ha construido correctamente un sistema el\xe1stico","B":"Ha construido correctamente un sistema tolerante a fallos","C":"Ha construido correctamente un sistema cifrado","D":"Ha construido correctamente un sistema escalable"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un sistema tolerante a fallos est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para continuar operando correctamente incluso cuando algunos de sus componentes fallan, que es exactamente lo que describe el escenario: tres de seis instancias fallaron pero ning\xfan cliente se vio afectado.\\nUn sistema el\xe1stico escala autom\xe1ticamente la capacidad hacia arriba o hacia abajo en respuesta a cambios en la demanda, pero la elasticidad no aborda la operaci\xf3n continua durante un fallo de componentes. Un sistema cifrado protege los datos contra accesos no autorizados mediante controles criptogr\xe1ficos, lo cual es una propiedad de seguridad no relacionada con mantener la disponibilidad cuando las instancias fallan. Un sistema escalable est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para manejar el crecimiento de la carga de trabajo o de los usuarios agregando recursos, pero la escalabilidad no garantiza que la aplicaci\xf3n siga funcionando cuando fallan las instancias existentes."},{"id":"q253","question":"\xbfEl uso de Amazon EC2 corresponde a cu\xe1l de los siguientes modelos de computaci\xf3n en la nube?","options":{"A":"IaaS y SaaS","B":"IaaS","C":"SaaS","D":"PaaS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EC2 es IaaS porque AWS proporciona la infraestructura de computaci\xf3n virtualizada mientras el cliente administra el sistema operativo, el middleware y las aplicaciones por encima de ella.\\nLa combinaci\xf3n de IaaS y SaaS no es una clasificaci\xf3n reconocida para ning\xfan servicio individual, ya que cada servicio se incluye en un modelo en lugar de abarcar dos. SaaS entrega aplicaciones de software totalmente administradas a los usuarios finales sin que se requiera administraci\xf3n de infraestructura ni plataforma, mientras que EC2 requiere que los clientes administren todo lo que est\xe1 por encima del hipervisor. PaaS proporciona una plataforma administrada donde los clientes implementan c\xf3digo de aplicaci\xf3n sin administrar la infraestructura subyacente, mientras que EC2 brinda a los clientes control directo sobre el sistema operativo y el entorno de ejecuci\xf3n."},{"id":"q254","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada al crear aplicaciones en AWS?","options":{"A":"Reforzar la seguridad f\xedsica aplicando el principio de m\xednimo privilegio","B":"Asegurar que la aplicaci\xf3n se ejecute en hardware de proveedores confiables","C":"Usar pol\xedticas de IAM para mantener el rendimiento","D":"Desacoplar los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n para que se ejecuten de forma independiente"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Desacoplar los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n para que se comuniquen mediante interfaces bien definidas, como API o colas, en lugar de dependencias directas, significa que cada componente puede escalar, fallar y actualizarse de forma independiente sin causar fallos en cascada en toda la aplicaci\xf3n.\\nAplicar el principio de m\xednimo privilegio es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de seguridad para controlar los permisos de acceso y, aunque es importante, se relaciona con la gesti\xf3n de identidades y accesos en lugar del dise\xf1o de la arquitectura de aplicaciones. Asegurar que las aplicaciones se ejecuten en hardware de proveedores confiables no es una preocupaci\xf3n del cliente en AWS, ya que AWS administra toda la infraestructura f\xedsica subyacente y la selecci\xf3n de hardware en nombre del cliente. Las pol\xedticas de IAM se usan para definir permisos de acceso para usuarios y roles, y son una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de identidades y accesos en lugar de un mecanismo para mantener el rendimiento de la aplicaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q261","question":"Tu aplicaci\xf3n ha experimentado recientemente un crecimiento global significativo y los usuarios internacionales se quejan de alta latencia. \xbfCu\xe1l es la caracter\xedstica de AWS que puede ayudar a mejorar la experiencia de tus usuarios internacionales?","options":{"A":"Elasticidad","B":"Alcance global","C":"Durabilidad de los datos","D":"Alta disponibilidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El alcance global de AWS, a trav\xe9s de su red mundial de Regiones y ubicaciones de borde, permite implementar aplicaciones m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios internacionales, lo que reduce la distancia de red que deben recorrer los datos y disminuye la latencia para una audiencia global.\\nLa elasticidad es la capacidad de escalar recursos autom\xe1ticamente hacia arriba o hacia abajo en respuesta a la demanda cambiante, y aborda la flexibilidad de capacidad en lugar de la proximidad geogr\xe1fica que causa latencia para los usuarios internacionales. La durabilidad de los datos se refiere a la integridad y redundancia a largo plazo de los datos almacenados para evitar p\xe9rdidas, y es una caracter\xedstica de protecci\xf3n del almacenamiento que no influye en la latencia de red ni en el alcance geogr\xe1fico. La alta disponibilidad se refiere a la capacidad de un sistema de permanecer operativo y accesible con un tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo, y aborda el tiempo de actividad y la tolerancia a fallos en lugar de reducir la latencia para usuarios distribuidos geogr\xe1ficamente."},{"id":"q263","question":"Una empresa tiene cargas de trabajo cr\xedticas para el negocio alojadas en AWS y no est\xe1 dispuesta a aceptar ning\xfan tiempo de inactividad. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada para proteger sus cargas de trabajo en caso de un desastre natural inesperado?","options":{"A":"Replicar datos en m\xfaltiples ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo y usar Amazon CloudFront para realizar conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica en caso de una interrupci\xf3n","B":"Implementar recursos de AWS en m\xfaltiples Zonas de disponibilidad dentro de la misma Regi\xf3n de AWS","C":"Crear copias de seguridad en un punto en el tiempo en otra subred y recuperar estos datos cuando ocurra un desastre","D":"Implementar recursos de AWS en otra Regi\xf3n de AWS e implementar una estrategia de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres Activo-Activo"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Implementar recursos de AWS en una segunda Regi\xf3n de AWS y ejecutar una estrategia de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres Activo-Activo significa que ambas Regiones atienden tr\xe1fico de producci\xf3n en vivo simult\xe1neamente, por lo que si un desastre natural elimina una Regi\xf3n por completo, la otra contin\xfaa atendiendo a los usuarios sin ning\xfan tiempo de inactividad.\\nAmazon CloudFront distribuye y almacena en cach\xe9 contenido en ubicaciones de borde para reducir la latencia de los usuarios finales, pero es un servicio de entrega de contenido y no replica cargas de trabajo ni realiza conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica de la infraestructura de aplicaciones en caso de un desastre regional. Implementar recursos en m\xfaltiples Zonas de disponibilidad dentro de una sola Regi\xf3n mejora la resistencia frente a fallos aislados, pero un desastre natural a gran escala puede afectar a una Regi\xf3n completa simult\xe1neamente, lo que hace que solo Multi-AZ sea insuficiente cuando se requiere cero tiempo de inactividad. Crear copias de seguridad en un punto en el tiempo en otra subred almacena datos de recuperaci\xf3n dentro de la misma Regi\xf3n y requiere tiempo de restauraci\xf3n manual, lo que entra en conflicto directo con el requisito de no aceptar ning\xfan tiempo de inactividad. Las subredes tambi\xe9n son construcciones intra-Regi\xf3n que no ofrecen separaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica."},{"id":"q271","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes aspectos debe considerarse al realizar un an\xe1lisis de TCO para comparar los costos de ejecutar una aplicaci\xf3n en AWS en lugar de on-premises?","options":{"A":"Desarrollo de aplicaciones","B":"Investigaci\xf3n de mercado","C":"An\xe1lisis de negocio","D":"Hardware f\xedsico"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Los costos de hardware f\xedsico, incluidos servidores, redes y almacenamiento, deben comprarse y mantenerse on-premises, pero son reemplazados por la infraestructura administrada de AWS en la nube, lo que los convierte en el diferenciador clave en una comparaci\xf3n de TCO.\\nLos costos de desarrollo de aplicaciones permanecen relativamente constantes independientemente de d\xf3nde se aloje una aplicaci\xf3n, por lo que no afectan de manera significativa la comparaci\xf3n entre on-premises y la nube. La investigaci\xf3n de mercado no es un componente del an\xe1lisis de TCO. Los costos de an\xe1lisis de negocio tambi\xe9n permanecen consistentes independientemente del entorno de alojamiento y no son un factor en las comparaciones de costos de infraestructura."},{"id":"q276","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes afirmaciones describe la agilidad de la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS te permite alojar tus aplicaciones en m\xfaltiples regiones de todo el mundo","B":"AWS proporciona hardware personalizable al menor costo posible","C":"AWS te permite aprovisionar recursos en minutos","D":"AWS te permite pagar por adelantado para reducir costos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La agilidad de la nube de AWS se refiere a la capacidad de aprovisionar recursos de infraestructura en minutos a trav\xe9s de la consola, la CLI o la API, dr\xe1sticamente m\xe1s r\xe1pido que las semanas o meses requeridos para la adquisici\xf3n de hardware on-premises.\\nAlojar aplicaciones en m\xfaltiples Regiones describe el alcance global, que es un beneficio de la nube separado de la agilidad. Que AWS proporcione hardware personalizable al menor costo posible representa incorrectamente c\xf3mo funciona AWS, ya que los clientes no configuran ni poseen el hardware subyacente. Pagar por adelantado para reducir costos describe el precio de Reserved Instance, que es lo opuesto al modelo de pago por uso que define la econom\xeda de la nube."},{"id":"q291","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un beneficio de ejecutar una aplicaci\xf3n en m\xfaltiples Zonas de disponibilidad?","options":{"A":"Te permite exceder los l\xedmites de servicio de AWS","B":"Reduce el tiempo de respuesta de la aplicaci\xf3n entre servidores y usuarios globales","C":"Aumenta la capacidad de computaci\xf3n disponible","D":"Aumenta la disponibilidad de tu aplicaci\xf3n"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Implementar una aplicaci\xf3n en m\xfaltiples Zonas de disponibilidad garantiza que siga ejecut\xe1ndose desde las AZ restantes si una experimenta una interrupci\xf3n, lo que aumenta directamente la disponibilidad al eliminar puntos \xfanicos de fallo dentro de una Regi\xf3n.\\nLos l\xedmites de servicio de AWS son cuotas de cuenta y recursos establecidas por AWS que se pueden aumentar mediante una solicitud de soporte, y no tienen relaci\xf3n alguna con el n\xfamero de Zonas de disponibilidad en las que se implementa una aplicaci\xf3n. Reducir el tiempo de respuesta de la aplicaci\xf3n para usuarios globales requiere implementar en m\xfaltiples Regiones o usar una red de entrega de contenido como CloudFront, ya que las AZ dentro de una sola Regi\xf3n est\xe1n geogr\xe1ficamente cerca y no reducen la latencia para usuarios distantes. Aumentar la capacidad de computaci\xf3n disponible requiere aprovisionar instancias adicionales o habilitar Auto Scaling, ya que distribuir los recursos existentes en m\xfaltiples AZ redistribuye la capacidad en lugar de aumentarla."},{"id":"q303","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son los beneficios de usar un servicio administrado por AWS?","options":{"A":"Proporciona control completo sobre la infraestructura virtual","B":"Permite a los clientes entregar nuevas soluciones m\xe1s r\xe1pido","C":"Reduce la complejidad operativa","D":"Elimina la necesidad de cifrar los datos","E":"Permite a los desarrolladores controlar todas las actividades relacionadas con la aplicaci\xf3n de parches"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Los servicios administrados de AWS se encargan del aprovisionamiento, la aplicaci\xf3n de parches y el mantenimiento de la infraestructura, lo que reduce la complejidad operativa para los clientes y les permite entregar nuevas soluciones m\xe1s r\xe1pido al centrarse en la l\xf3gica de la aplicaci\xf3n en lugar de la administraci\xf3n de la infraestructura.\\nProporcionar control completo sobre la infraestructura virtual es una caracter\xedstica de los servicios IaaS como EC2, no de los servicios administrados, que deliberadamente abstraen la administraci\xf3n de la infraestructura a cambio de una menor carga operativa. Usar un servicio administrado no elimina la necesidad de cifrar los datos, ya que las responsabilidades de protecci\xf3n de datos, incluido el cifrado en reposo y en tr\xe1nsito, siguen siendo del cliente seg\xfan el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida. Los servicios administrados eliminan las responsabilidades de aplicaci\xf3n de parches del cliente en lugar de dar a los desarrolladores control sobre ellas, ya que AWS realiza toda la aplicaci\xf3n de parches y el mantenimiento subyacentes en nombre del cliente."},{"id":"q307","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el beneficio de usar una API para acceder a los servicios de AWS?","options":{"A":"Mejora el rendimiento de los recursos de AWS","B":"Reduce el tiempo necesario para aprovisionar recursos de AWS","C":"Reduce el n\xfamero de desarrolladores necesarios","D":"Permite la gesti\xf3n program\xe1tica de los recursos de AWS"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las API de AWS permiten la gesti\xf3n program\xe1tica de los recursos de AWS, lo que permite a los desarrolladores automatizar el aprovisionamiento, la configuraci\xf3n y la monitorizaci\xf3n mediante c\xf3digo en lugar de interacciones manuales con la consola.\\nUsar una API para acceder a los servicios de AWS no mejora inherentemente el rendimiento de los recursos subyacentes, ya que el acceso por API es una interfaz de gesti\xf3n y no un mecanismo de mejora del rendimiento. Aunque la automatizaci\xf3n mediante API puede acelerar los flujos de trabajo, el beneficio principal es el control program\xe1tico y no espec\xedficamente la reducci\xf3n del tiempo de aprovisionamiento. Las API permiten a los desarrolladores interactuar con los servicios de AWS mediante c\xf3digo, pero no reducen el n\xfamero de desarrolladores necesarios para gestionar un entorno y, de hecho, pueden requerir habilidades de desarrollo adicionales."},{"id":"q320","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 principios de dise\xf1o se relacionan con la eficiencia del rendimiento en AWS?","options":{"A":"Crear arquitecturas multirregi\xf3n para atender mejor a clientes globales","B":"Aplicar seguridad en todas las capas","C":"Implementar controles s\xf3lidos de identidad y acceso","D":"Usar arquitecturas sin servidor","E":"Habilitar el registro de auditor\xeda"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Las arquitecturas multirregi\xf3n atienden a clientes globales con menor latencia, y las arquitecturas sin servidor escalan autom\xe1ticamente para ajustarse a la demanda sin planificaci\xf3n de capacidad; ambos son principios de dise\xf1o clave del pilar de Eficiencia del Rendimiento.\\nAplicar seguridad en todas las capas es un principio de dise\xf1o del pilar de Seguridad del AWS Well-Architected Framework, no del pilar de Eficiencia del Rendimiento. Implementar controles s\xf3lidos de identidad y acceso tambi\xe9n es un principio del pilar de Seguridad centrado en proteger los recursos mediante el privilegio m\xednimo y la gesti\xf3n de acceso, y no est\xe1 relacionado con la eficiencia del rendimiento. Habilitar el registro de auditor\xeda es una pr\xe1ctica de los pilares de Seguridad y Excelencia Operacional que se usa para el cumplimiento y la investigaci\xf3n de incidentes, y no es un principio de dise\xf1o de Eficiencia del Rendimiento."},{"id":"q322","question":"\xbfPor qu\xe9 una organizaci\xf3n decidir\xeda usar AWS en lugar de un centro de datos on-premises?","options":{"A":"Licencias de software comercial gratuitas","B":"Soporte t\xe9cnico gratuito","C":"Recursos el\xe1sticos","D":"Visitas presenciales para auditor\xeda","E":"Ahorro de costos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"AWS proporciona recursos el\xe1sticos que escalan hacia arriba y hacia abajo seg\xfan la demanda, lo que elimina la necesidad de aprovisionar capacidad en exceso, y ofrece ahorro de costos mediante precios de pago por uso que evitan las grandes inversiones de capital inicial requeridas por los centros de datos on-premises.\\nAWS no proporciona licencias de software comercial gratuitas, ya que los costos de software de terceros son independientes de los precios de la infraestructura de AWS y deben comprarse por separado o a trav\xe9s de AWS Marketplace. AWS no proporciona soporte t\xe9cnico gratuito, ya que el soporte est\xe1 disponible mediante niveles de pago y el soporte b\xe1sico solo cubre consultas de cuenta y facturaci\xf3n sin asistencia t\xe9cnica. AWS no ofrece visitas presenciales para auditor\xeda, ya que los clientes que requieren evidencia de cumplimiento usan informes de auditor\xeda, certificaciones y el portal AWS Artifact de AWS en lugar de acceso f\xedsico al sitio."},{"id":"q327","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el framework creado por AWS Professional Services que ayuda a las organizaciones a dise\xf1ar una hoja de ruta para una adopci\xf3n exitosa de la nube?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"AWS WAF","C":"AWS CAF","D":"Amazon EFS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El AWS Cloud Adoption Framework es un framework de orientaci\xf3n creado por AWS Professional Services que ayuda a las organizaciones a planificar y ejecutar su adopci\xf3n de la nube en seis perspectivas: Negocio, Personas, Gobernanza, Plataforma, Seguridad y Operaciones.\\nAWS Secrets Manager almacena, rota y gestiona el acceso a secretos, como credenciales de bases de datos y claves de API, y es un servicio de seguridad para la gesti\xf3n de credenciales sin conexi\xf3n con la planificaci\xf3n de adopci\xf3n de la nube ni el desarrollo de hojas de ruta. AWS WAF es un firewall de aplicaciones web que filtra solicitudes HTTP maliciosas seg\xfan reglas definidas para proteger aplicaciones web de exploits comunes, y es un servicio de seguridad sin conexi\xf3n con frameworks de adopci\xf3n de la nube ni hojas de ruta estrat\xe9gicas. Amazon EFS es un sistema de archivos de red compartido administrado que proporciona acceso concurrente a archivos desde m\xfaltiples instancias de computaci\xf3n, y es un servicio de almacenamiento sin conexi\xf3n con la planificaci\xf3n de adopci\xf3n de la nube ni frameworks estrat\xe9gicos."},{"id":"aif-q329","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un ejemplo de aprendizaje no supervisado?","options":{"A":"Predecir precios de viviendas","B":"Detecci\xf3n de spam","C":"Agrupaci\xf3n en cl\xfasteres de segmentos de clientes","D":"Clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La agrupaci\xf3n en cl\xfasteres de segmentos de clientes es un ejemplo de aprendizaje no supervisado porque el algoritmo agrupa puntos de datos seg\xfan su similitud sin que se le proporcionen ejemplos etiquetados ni categor\xedas predefinidas de las que aprender. El modelo descubre la estructura de los datos por s\xed solo.\\nPredecir precios de viviendas usa datos de entrenamiento etiquetados donde cada ejemplo tiene un precio conocido, lo que lo convierte en una tarea de regresi\xf3n supervisada. La detecci\xf3n de spam entrena un modelo con correos electr\xf3nicos ya etiquetados como spam o no spam, lo que la convierte en una tarea de clasificaci\xf3n supervisada. La clasificaci\xf3n de im\xe1genes entrena un modelo con im\xe1genes de etiquetas conocidas, como gato o perro, lo que tambi\xe9n la convierte en una tarea de clasificaci\xf3n supervisada."},{"id":"q330","question":"\xbfPor qu\xe9 muchas empresas emergentes prefieren AWS a las soluciones on-premises tradicionales?","options":{"A":"AWS les permite pagar m\xe1s tarde cuando su negocio tenga \xe9xito","B":"AWS puede construir centros de datos completos m\xe1s r\xe1pido que cualquier otro proveedor de nube","C":"Usando AWS, pueden reducir el tiempo de comercializaci\xf3n al centrarse en las actividades del negocio en lugar de construir y gestionar centros de datos","D":"AWS elimina la necesidad de invertir en gastos operativos","E":"Usar AWS permite a las empresas reemplazar grandes gastos de capital por costos variables bajos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Las empresas emergentes prefieren AWS porque pueden reemplazar grandes gastos de capital iniciales por costos variables bajos de pago por uso, y reducir el tiempo de comercializaci\xf3n al centrarse en el desarrollo de productos en lugar de construir y gestionar centros de datos.\\nLa afirmaci\xf3n de que AWS les permite pagar m\xe1s tarde cuando su negocio tenga \xe9xito es falsa. AWS requiere el pago de los recursos a medida que se usan, no de forma diferida hasta que una empresa sea rentable. La afirmaci\xf3n de que AWS puede construir centros de datos completos m\xe1s r\xe1pido que cualquier otro proveedor de nube es falsa y no es una propuesta de valor declarada de AWS. La afirmaci\xf3n de que AWS elimina la necesidad de invertir en gastos operativos es falsa. Los gastos operativos siguen existiendo en AWS; el cambio clave es de un gasto de capital inicial a costos variables continuos."},{"id":"q333","question":"Como parte del AWS Migration Acceleration Program (MAP), \xbfqu\xe9 proporciona AWS para acelerar la adopci\xf3n de AWS por parte de las empresas?","options":{"A":"AWS Partners","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Professional Services","D":"Amazon Athena","E":"Amazon Pinpoint"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Como parte del AWS Migration Acceleration Program, AWS proporciona acceso a AWS Partners, incluidos socios de consultor\xeda y tecnolog\xeda con experiencia en migraci\xf3n, y a AWS Professional Services, que trabaja directamente con los clientes en proyectos de adopci\xf3n de la nube y migraci\xf3n. Ambos son componentes centrales del programa MAP.\\nAWS Artifact es un portal de autoservicio para acceder a informes de cumplimiento y acuerdos de seguridad de AWS, y es un recurso de documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento en lugar de un componente del Migration Acceleration Program. Amazon Athena es un servicio de consultas sin servidor que analiza datos almacenados en Amazon S3 usando SQL, y es un servicio de anal\xedtica sin funci\xf3n en la aceleraci\xf3n de la adopci\xf3n de la nube empresarial ni en el soporte de migraci\xf3n. Amazon Pinpoint es un servicio de participaci\xf3n de clientes para enviar mensajes dirigidos por correo electr\xf3nico, SMS y notificaciones push, y es una herramienta de comunicaciones de marketing sin conexi\xf3n con la aceleraci\xf3n de la migraci\xf3n de la nube empresarial."},{"id":"q343","question":"Una empresa usa instancias EC2 para ejecutar su sitio de comercio electr\xf3nico en la plataforma de AWS. Si el sitio deja de estar disponible, la empresa perder\xe1 una cantidad significativa de dinero por cada minuto que el sitio est\xe9 no disponible. \xbfQu\xe9 principio de dise\xf1o deber\xeda usar la empresa para minimizar el riesgo de una interrupci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Privilegio m\xednimo","B":"Pilot Light","C":"Tolerancia a fallos","D":"Multihilo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La tolerancia a fallos es la capacidad de un sistema de seguir funcionando sin interrupci\xf3n cuando uno o m\xe1s componentes fallan, lograda mediante redundancia en Zonas de Disponibilidad y conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica, lo que minimiza directamente el riesgo de interrupciones costosas.\\nEl privilegio m\xednimo es un principio de seguridad que restringe los permisos de usuarios y servicios al m\xednimo necesario para realizar su funci\xf3n, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con la disponibilidad del sistema ni con la prevenci\xf3n de interrupciones. Pilot Light es una estrategia de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres que mantiene una versi\xf3n m\xednima del entorno en ejecuci\xf3n en una regi\xf3n secundaria, lo que aborda la recuperaci\xf3n despu\xe9s de una interrupci\xf3n en lugar de prevenirla mediante redundancia. El multihilo es una t\xe9cnica de programaci\xf3n de software que permite la ejecuci\xf3n concurrente de m\xfaltiples tareas dentro de una sola aplicaci\xf3n, y no est\xe1 relacionada con el dise\xf1o de arquitecturas en la nube para la disponibilidad."},{"id":"q371","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada para hacer que tu aplicaci\xf3n en AWS tenga alta disponibilidad?","options":{"A":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en al menos dos Zonas de Disponibilidad","B":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS","C":"Implementar el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n en al menos dos servidores en la misma Zona de Disponibilidad","D":"Reescribir el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n para gestionar todas las solicitudes entrantes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Implementar una aplicaci\xf3n en al menos dos Zonas de Disponibilidad garantiza que, si una AZ experimenta una interrupci\xf3n, la aplicaci\xf3n siga ejecut\xe1ndose en la otra, lo que proporciona alta disponibilidad.\\nImplementar en m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS proporciona redundancia geogr\xe1fica para recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres, pero introduce complejidad y costo significativos, y no es la pr\xe1ctica recomendada est\xe1ndar para lograr alta disponibilidad dentro de una \xfanica implementaci\xf3n de aplicaci\xf3n. Implementar en m\xfaltiples servidores dentro de la misma Zona de Disponibilidad mejora la redundancia a nivel de servidor, pero no protege contra una interrupci\xf3n de toda la AZ, lo que deja la aplicaci\xf3n vulnerable a un punto \xfanico de fallo. Reescribir el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n para gestionar todas las solicitudes entrantes en una \xfanica implementaci\xf3n no aborda fallos de infraestructura y no puede proporcionar alta disponibilidad si la infraestructura subyacente deja de estar disponible."},{"id":"q372","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes debe tenerse en cuenta al realizar un an\xe1lisis de TCO en relaci\xf3n con los costos de ejecutar una aplicaci\xf3n en AWS frente a on-premises?","options":{"A":"Costos de mano de obra y de TI","B":"Refrigeraci\xf3n y consumo de energ\xeda","C":"Capacidad de c\xf3mputo de Amazon EBS","D":"Arquitectura de software","E":"Compatibilidad de software"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Un an\xe1lisis de TCO compara los costos on-premises, incluidos mano de obra, personal de TI, refrigeraci\xf3n y consumo de energ\xeda, con los costos de AWS, donde estos gastos de infraestructura f\xedsica se eliminan.\\nLa capacidad de c\xf3mputo de Amazon EBS es un costo de servicio de AWS en lugar de un gasto de infraestructura on-premises, por lo que no corresponde a una comparaci\xf3n de TCO sobre lo que los clientes dejan de pagar cuando migran. La arquitectura de software se refiere a c\xf3mo se dise\xf1a y estructura una aplicaci\xf3n, lo cual es una consideraci\xf3n t\xe9cnica en lugar de un costo de infraestructura considerado en un an\xe1lisis de TCO. La compatibilidad de software se refiere a si las aplicaciones pueden ejecutarse en un nuevo entorno, lo cual es una preocupaci\xf3n de planificaci\xf3n de migraci\xf3n en lugar de una categor\xeda de costo incluida en una comparaci\xf3n de TCO."},{"id":"q388","question":"Una pr\xe1ctica clave al dise\xf1ar soluciones en AWS es minimizar las dependencias entre componentes para que el fallo de un solo componente no afecte a otros componentes. \xbfC\xf3mo se llama esta pr\xe1ctica?","options":{"A":"Acoplamiento el\xe1stico","B":"Acoplamiento flexible","C":"Acoplamiento escalable","D":"Acoplamiento fuerte"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El acoplamiento flexible es la pr\xe1ctica de dise\xf1o de minimizar las dependencias entre los componentes de una aplicaci\xf3n para que un fallo en un componente no se propague en cascada a otros, mejorando la resiliencia general del sistema.\\nEl acoplamiento el\xe1stico no es un principio de dise\xf1o en la nube ni un t\xe9rmino arquitect\xf3nico reconocido, y puede causar confusi\xf3n con Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling o Elastic Load Balancing, que son servicios en lugar de patrones de acoplamiento. El acoplamiento escalable no es un concepto arquitect\xf3nico reconocido, y la escalabilidad es un objetivo de dise\xf1o separado que se logra mediante pr\xe1cticas como el escalado horizontal en lugar de un tipo de acoplamiento de componentes. El acoplamiento fuerte es lo contrario de la respuesta correcta, ya que describe una arquitectura en la que los componentes dependen en gran medida unos de otros, lo que significa que es m\xe1s probable que un fallo en un componente se propague en cascada y afecte a otros."},{"id":"q397","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes son ventajas de usar AWS como proveedor de servicios de computaci\xf3n en la nube?","options":{"A":"Elimina la necesidad de monitorear servidores y aplicaciones","B":"Gestiona todas las tareas de cumplimiento y auditor\xeda","C":"Proporciona hardware personalizado para cumplir cualquier especificaci\xf3n","D":"Elimina la necesidad de adivinar las necesidades de capacidad de la infraestructura","E":"Permite a los clientes cambiar sus gastos de capital por gastos operativos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"AWS elimina la necesidad de adivinar la capacidad de la infraestructura mediante el escalado bajo demanda, y permite a los clientes cambiar grandes gastos de capital iniciales por gastos operativos variables m\xe1s peque\xf1os basados en el uso real.\\nLos clientes siguen siendo responsables de monitorear sus propios servidores y aplicaciones, ya que AWS proporciona las herramientas pero no realiza el monitoreo en nombre del cliente. Las responsabilidades de cumplimiento y auditor\xeda son compartidas, y los clientes son responsables de sus propios datos, controles de acceso y cumplimiento a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n independientemente de que usen AWS. AWS no proporciona hardware personalizado seg\xfan las especificaciones de cada cliente, ya que los clientes eligen entre tipos de instancias estandarizados y servicios administrados dentro de la infraestructura compartida."},{"id":"q406","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un modelo de implementaci\xf3n de computaci\xf3n en la nube que conecta la infraestructura y las aplicaciones entre recursos basados en la nube y recursos existentes que no est\xe1n ubicados en la nube?","options":{"A":"On-premises","B":"Mixto","C":"H\xedbrido","D":"Nube"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Un modelo de implementaci\xf3n h\xedbrido conecta recursos basados en la nube con infraestructura existente que no est\xe1 ubicada en la nube, como centros de datos on-premises, lo que permite a las organizaciones extender su entorno a AWS mientras mantienen algunas cargas de trabajo ejecut\xe1ndose localmente.\\nOn-premises es un modelo de implementaci\xf3n en el que toda la infraestructura se aloja y gestiona dentro de las instalaciones de una organizaci\xf3n sin integraci\xf3n con la nube, lo cual es lo contrario de conectar recursos en la nube y recursos que no est\xe1n en la nube. Mixto no es un modelo de implementaci\xf3n de computaci\xf3n en la nube reconocido y no aparece en el marco de AWS junto con on-premises, h\xedbrido y nube. Nube es un modelo de implementaci\xf3n en el que toda la infraestructura y las aplicaciones se ejecutan completamente dentro del entorno del proveedor de la nube sin dependencia de recursos alojados localmente, lo que significa que no hay recursos existentes que no est\xe9n en la nube a los que conectarse."},{"id":"q420","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 significa el t\xe9rmino “Econom\xedas de escala”?","options":{"A":"Significa que ahorras m\xe1s cuando consumes m\xe1s","B":"Significa que a medida que pasa m\xe1s tiempo usando AWS, pagas m\xe1s por sus servicios","C":"Significa que AWS reducir\xe1 continuamente los costos a medida que crece","D":"Significa que tienes la capacidad de pagar seg\xfan el uso"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Econom\xedas de escala significa que, a medida que AWS crece y agrega m\xe1s uso de los clientes, logra costos unitarios m\xe1s bajos y transfiere continuamente esos ahorros a los clientes mediante reducciones de precios regulares.\\nAhorrar m\xe1s cuando consumes m\xe1s describe descuentos basados en volumen, como Reserved Instances o precios por niveles, lo cual es un concepto de precios separado en lugar de la definici\xf3n de econom\xedas de escala. Pagar m\xe1s con el tiempo a medida que aumenta el uso describe lo contrario de lo que ofrecen las econom\xedas de escala, ya que los precios de AWS han disminuido consistentemente con el tiempo en lugar de aumentar. La capacidad de pagar seg\xfan el uso describe el modelo de facturaci\xf3n pay-as-you-go, que es un beneficio separado de la adopci\xf3n de la nube no relacionado con las reducciones de costos impulsadas por el poder de compra agregado."},{"id":"q439","question":"\xbfPor qu\xe9 las arquitecturas sin servidor son m\xe1s econ\xf3micas que las arquitecturas basadas en servidores?","options":{"A":"Las arquitecturas sin servidor eliminan todos los costos de red asociados con la ejecuci\xf3n de aplicaciones","B":"Con las arquitecturas basadas en servidores, los recursos de c\xf3mputo contin\xfaan ejecut\xe1ndose todo el tiempo, pero con las arquitecturas sin servidor, los recursos de c\xf3mputo solo se usan cuando se est\xe1 ejecutando el c\xf3digo","C":"Cuando reservas capacidad sin servidor, obtienes grandes descuentos en comparaci\xf3n con la reserva de servidores","D":"Con las arquitecturas sin servidor, tienes la capacidad de escalar autom\xe1ticamente hacia arriba o hacia abajo a medida que cambia la demanda"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las arquitecturas sin servidor son m\xe1s econ\xf3micas porque los recursos de c\xf3mputo solo se asignan durante la ejecuci\xf3n real del c\xf3digo, lo que significa que no hay cargos por capacidad inactiva, a diferencia de las arquitecturas basadas en servidores, donde las instancias se ejecutan de forma continua y generan costos incluso cuando no se est\xe1n procesando solicitudes.\\nLas arquitecturas sin servidor no eliminan los costos de red; los costos de transferencia de datos y de llamadas a API siguen aplic\xe1ndose y se facturan por separado seg\xfan el uso. Los descuentos por capacidad reservada no se aplican al c\xf3mputo sin servidor de la misma manera que se aplican a las instancias EC2; el precio sin servidor se basa completamente en la cantidad de solicitudes y la duraci\xf3n de la ejecuci\xf3n en lugar de compromisos de capacidad reservada. La capacidad de escalar autom\xe1ticamente tambi\xe9n est\xe1 disponible en arquitecturas basadas en servidores mediante grupos de Auto Scaling, por lo que no es una caracter\xedstica exclusiva de sin servidor; la ventaja econ\xf3mica clave es la eliminaci\xf3n de costos durante los per\xedodos de inactividad."},{"id":"q459","question":"El propietario de una aplicaci\xf3n de comercio electr\xf3nico observa que los requisitos de capacidad de c\xf3mputo var\xedan considerablemente con el tiempo. \xbfQu\xe9 hace que AWS sea m\xe1s econ\xf3mico que los centros de datos tradicionales para este tipo de aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"AWS permite a los clientes lanzar instancias EC2 potentes para manejar picos de carga","B":"AWS permite a los clientes pagar por adelantado para obtener mayores descuentos","C":"AWS permite a los clientes lanzar y terminar instancias EC2 seg\xfan la demanda","D":"AWS permite a los clientes elegir tipos de instancias EC2 m\xe1s econ\xf3micos que se adapten mejor a sus necesidades"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS permite a los clientes lanzar y terminar instancias EC2 seg\xfan la demanda en tiempo real, por lo que durante los per\xedodos de bajo tr\xe1fico dejan de pagar por capacidad no utilizada, a diferencia de los centros de datos tradicionales, donde el hardware permanece inactivo independientemente de la utilizaci\xf3n.\\nLanzar instancias potentes para manejar picos de carga describe el escalado vertical, que a\xfan da como resultado capacidad sobreaprovisionada e inactiva durante los per\xedodos de bajo tr\xe1fico y no aborda la ineficiencia econ\xf3mica central de los centros de datos tradicionales. Pagar por adelantado para obtener mayores descuentos describe el precio de Reserved Instances, que es un modelo basado en compromiso que reduce el costo por unidad pero no elimina el problema de pagar por capacidad durante los per\xedodos de baja demanda. Elegir tipos de instancias m\xe1s econ\xf3micos reduce el costo unitario de c\xf3mputo pero no elimina los costos de capacidad inactiva, ya que las instancias seguir\xedan ejecut\xe1ndose y generando cargos independientemente de si se est\xe1n usando activamente."},{"id":"q467","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son principios de dise\xf1o de la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Capacidad reservada en lugar de bajo demanda","B":"Acoplamiento d\xe9bil en lugar de acoplamiento fuerte","C":"Servidores en lugar de servicios administrados","D":"Recursos desechables en lugar de servidores fijos","E":"Implementaciones Multi-AZ en lugar de implementaciones multirregi\xf3n"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"El acoplamiento d\xe9bil minimiza las dependencias entre componentes para que las fallas no se propaguen en cascada, y tratar los recursos como desechables significa dise\xf1ar para la falla reemplazando instancias en lugar de repararlas, lo que permite una recuperaci\xf3n r\xe1pida.\\nLa capacidad reservada en lugar de bajo demanda es lo opuesto a un principio de dise\xf1o de la nube, ya que la nube fomenta el aprovisionamiento din\xe1mico de recursos seg\xfan la necesidad real. Los servidores en lugar de servicios administrados contradice el principio de la nube de delegar la carga operativa a AWS siempre que sea posible. Multi-AZ frente a multirregi\xf3n no es un principio de dise\xf1o reconocido de AWS y presenta una disyuntiva falsa, ya que ambas son estrategias de disponibilidad v\xe1lidas seg\xfan el caso de uso."},{"id":"q470","question":"Has desarrollado una aplicaci\xf3n web dirigida a una audiencia global. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones te ayudar\xe1 a lograr la mayor redundancia y tolerancia a fallos desde una perspectiva de infraestructura?","options":{"A":"No es necesario dise\xf1ar para estas capacidades en AWS, ya que AWS es redundante de forma predeterminada","B":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en una sola zona de disponibilidad","C":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en varias zonas de disponibilidad en una sola regi\xf3n de AWS","D":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en varias zonas de disponibilidad en varias regiones de AWS"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Implementar en varias zonas de disponibilidad en varias regiones de AWS proporciona el mayor nivel de redundancia y tolerancia a fallos, lo que protege simult\xe1neamente contra fallas a nivel de AZ y interrupciones en toda la regi\xf3n.\\nAWS no es redundante de forma predeterminada para las cargas de trabajo de los clientes. Los clientes deben dise\xf1ar sus propias aplicaciones para la redundancia utilizando los componentes de infraestructura disponibles. Implementar en una sola zona de disponibilidad no proporciona redundancia, ya que una falla de AZ dejar\xeda toda la aplicaci\xf3n fuera de l\xednea. Implementar en varias zonas de disponibilidad en una sola regi\xf3n protege contra fallas a nivel de AZ, pero deja la aplicaci\xf3n vulnerable a una interrupci\xf3n en toda la regi\xf3n."},{"id":"q514","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puedes aumentar la tolerancia a fallos de tu aplicaci\xf3n mientras est\xe1 alojada en AWS?","options":{"A":"Implementar tu aplicaci\xf3n en varias instancias EC2","B":"Implementar tu aplicaci\xf3n en varias zonas de disponibilidad","C":"Alojar tu aplicaci\xf3n en un solo tipo de instancia EC2 potente en lugar de varias instancias m\xe1s peque\xf1as","D":"Implementar los recursos subyacentes de la aplicaci\xf3n en varias subredes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Implementar en varias zonas de disponibilidad garantiza que, si una AZ experimenta una falla, la aplicaci\xf3n siga ejecut\xe1ndose en otra, lo que proporciona tolerancia a fallos mediante redundancia f\xedsica dentro de una regi\xf3n.\\nImplementar en varias instancias EC2 mejora la capacidad y la disponibilidad dentro de una sola ubicaci\xf3n, pero no protege contra una falla a nivel de AZ si todas las instancias est\xe1n en la misma zona. Alojar en una sola instancia EC2 potente consolida la aplicaci\xf3n en un \xfanico punto de falla, lo que reduce la tolerancia a fallos en lugar de aumentarla. Implementar en varias subredes mejora la segmentaci\xf3n de red, pero no proporciona tolerancia a fallos a menos que esas subredes abarquen varias zonas de disponibilidad."},{"id":"q517","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es un beneficio del principio de arquitectura de \\"acoplamiento d\xe9bil\\"?","options":{"A":"Elimina la necesidad de la gesti\xf3n de cambios","B":"Permite Cross-Region Replication","C":"Ayuda a los clientes de AWS a reducir el acceso privilegiado a los recursos de AWS","D":"Permite modificar componentes o servicios individuales de la aplicaci\xf3n sin afectar a otros componentes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El acoplamiento d\xe9bil permite modificar, actualizar o reemplazar componentes individuales de forma independiente sin afectar otras partes del sistema, lo que habilita ciclos de desarrollo m\xe1s r\xe1pidos y arquitecturas m\xe1s resilientes.\\nEliminar la necesidad de la gesti\xf3n de cambios es incorrecto porque la gesti\xf3n de cambios sigue siendo necesaria en cualquier arquitectura. El acoplamiento d\xe9bil hace que los cambios sean m\xe1s seguros y menos disruptivos, pero no elimina la necesidad de gestionarlos. Cross-Region Replication es una estrategia de redundancia de datos y recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres que no est\xe1 relacionada con el principio arquitect\xf3nico de reducir dependencias entre componentes. Reducir el acceso privilegiado a los recursos de AWS es una pr\xe1ctica de seguridad relacionada con el principio de m\xednimo privilegio, no un beneficio del acoplamiento d\xe9bil."},{"id":"q519","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes modelos de implementaci\xf3n de computaci\xf3n en la nube elimina la necesidad de ejecutar y mantener centros de datos f\xedsicos?","options":{"A":"On-premises","B":"IaaS","C":"PaaS","D":"Nube"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El modelo de implementaci\xf3n en la nube elimina por completo la necesidad de ejecutar y mantener centros de datos f\xedsicos, ya que el proveedor de la nube aloja y administra toda la infraestructura en sus instalaciones.\\nOn-premises es un modelo de implementaci\xf3n en el que toda la infraestructura es propiedad de una organizaci\xf3n y se opera dentro de sus propias instalaciones, lo que es lo opuesto a eliminar la gesti\xf3n de centros de datos f\xedsicos. IaaS es un modelo de servicio en la nube que proporciona recursos virtualizados de c\xf3mputo, almacenamiento y redes, y no es un modelo de implementaci\xf3n que aborde d\xf3nde o c\xf3mo se administra f\xedsicamente la infraestructura. PaaS es un modelo de servicio en la nube que proporciona una plataforma administrada para el desarrollo y la implementaci\xf3n de aplicaciones y, de manera similar, no es un modelo de implementaci\xf3n que determine la responsabilidad f\xedsica del centro de datos."},{"id":"q530","question":"AWS proporciona capacidad de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres al permitir que los clientes implementen infraestructura en m\xfaltiples [...].","options":{"A":"Regiones","B":"Dispositivos de transporte","C":"Planes de soporte","D":"Ubicaciones de borde"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Las regiones de AWS son ubicaciones geogr\xe1ficamente separadas en todo el mundo, lo que significa que la infraestructura implementada en varias regiones sigue operativa incluso si una regi\xf3n completa se ve afectada por una interrupci\xf3n.\\nLos dispositivos de transporte son hardware f\xedsico de transferencia de datos, como los dispositivos de AWS Snow Family, y no tienen ning\xfan rol en la arquitectura de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres. Los planes de soporte son niveles de suscripci\xf3n que proporcionan acceso a la asistencia t\xe9cnica de AWS y no afectan la disponibilidad ni la redundancia de la infraestructura. Las ubicaciones de borde son puntos de presencia utilizados para la entrega y el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de contenido, y no est\xe1n dise\xf1adas para implementar infraestructura completa con fines de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres."},{"id":"q533","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 pilar del AWS Well-Architected Framework proporciona recomendaciones para ayudar a los clientes a seleccionar los recursos de c\xf3mputo adecuados seg\xfan los requisitos de la carga de trabajo?","options":{"A":"Excelencia operativa","B":"Seguridad","C":"Eficiencia del rendimiento","D":"Confiabilidad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El pilar de Eficiencia del rendimiento del Well-Architected Framework se centra en seleccionar los tipos y tama\xf1os de recursos adecuados seg\xfan los requisitos de la carga de trabajo para utilizar los recursos de c\xf3mputo de manera eficiente.\\nLa Excelencia operativa se centra en ejecutar y monitorear sistemas para entregar valor empresarial y mejorar continuamente los procesos y procedimientos, y no aborda espec\xedficamente la selecci\xf3n de recursos de c\xf3mputo seg\xfan los requisitos de la carga de trabajo. La Seguridad se centra en proteger la informaci\xf3n y los sistemas mediante la evaluaci\xf3n de riesgos, la gesti\xf3n de accesos y los controles, y no se ocupa de hacer coincidir los recursos de c\xf3mputo con las necesidades de rendimiento de la carga de trabajo. La Confiabilidad se centra en garantizar que una carga de trabajo pueda recuperarse de fallas y adquirir recursos din\xe1micamente para satisfacer la demanda, pero su \xe9nfasis est\xe1 en la resiliencia y la recuperaci\xf3n en lugar de seleccionar los tipos de recursos de c\xf3mputo adecuados para la eficiencia."},{"id":"q541","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es un patr\xf3n recomendado para dise\xf1ar una arquitectura de alta disponibilidad en AWS?","options":{"A":"Asegurar que los componentes tengan conectividad de red de baja latencia","B":"Ejecutar suficientes instancias de Amazon EC2 para operar a carga m\xe1xima","C":"Asegurar que la aplicaci\xf3n est\xe9 dise\xf1ada para admitir la falla de cualquier componente individual","D":"Usar una aplicaci\xf3n monol\xedtica que maneje todas las operaciones"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dise\xf1ar una aplicaci\xf3n para admitir la falla de cualquier componente individual, mediante redundancia, verificaciones de estado y conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica, es el principio central del dise\xf1o para fallas y es el patr\xf3n recomendado para lograr alta disponibilidad en AWS.\\nAsegurar la conectividad de red de baja latencia es una consideraci\xf3n de optimizaci\xf3n del rendimiento que mejora la experiencia del usuario, pero la baja latencia por s\xed sola no protege una aplicaci\xf3n de fallas de componentes ni mantiene la disponibilidad durante una interrupci\xf3n. Ejecutar suficientes instancias EC2 para manejar la carga m\xe1xima aborda la planificaci\xf3n de capacidad, pero no incorpora redundancia ni tolerancia a fallos, lo que significa que una falla de un solo componente a\xfan podr\xeda dejar inactiva la aplicaci\xf3n. Una aplicaci\xf3n monol\xedtica que maneja todas las operaciones consolida la funcionalidad en una sola unidad, lo que crea un \xfanico punto de falla y es lo opuesto a una arquitectura de alta disponibilidad que puede sobrevivir a fallas de componentes individuales."},{"id":"q542","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedsticas de AWS hacen que AWS sea rentable para una carga de trabajo con demanda din\xe1mica de usuarios?","options":{"A":"Alta disponibilidad","B":"Modelo de seguridad compartida","C":"Elasticidad","D":"Precios de pago por uso","E":"Confiabilidad"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"La elasticidad permite escalar recursos autom\xe1ticamente hacia arriba o hacia abajo seg\xfan la demanda, y los precios de pago por uso garantizan que solo pagues por lo que realmente usas, lo que hace que ambos sean ideales para cargas de trabajo con demanda din\xe1mica e impredecible.\\nLa alta disponibilidad garantiza que las aplicaciones sigan siendo accesibles a pesar de las fallas mediante el uso de redundancia en m\xfaltiples Availability Zones, y aunque contribuye a la confiabilidad, no reduce directamente los costos para cargas de trabajo con demanda din\xe1mica. El modelo de seguridad compartida define la divisi\xf3n de responsabilidades de seguridad entre AWS y el cliente, y es un principio de gobernanza m\xe1s que una caracter\xedstica que haga que AWS sea rentable para cargas de trabajo variables. La confiabilidad se refiere a la capacidad de una carga de trabajo de realizar su funci\xf3n prevista de manera correcta y consistente, y aunque es importante para el dise\xf1o del sistema, no es en s\xed misma una caracter\xedstica de precios o eficiencia de costos que reduzca los costos para la demanda din\xe1mica."},{"id":"q557","question":"Al dise\xf1ar la arquitectura de aplicaciones en la nube, \xbfcu\xe1l de los siguientes es un principio clave de dise\xf1o?","options":{"A":"Usar la instancia m\xe1s grande posible","B":"Aprovisionar capacidad para el pico de carga","C":"Usar el proceso de desarrollo Scrum","D":"Implementar elasticidad"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La elasticidad es un principio clave de dise\xf1o en la nube que implica escalar autom\xe1ticamente los recursos de c\xf3mputo hacia arriba o hacia abajo seg\xfan la demanda real, evitando el sobreaprovisionamiento y reduciendo costos en comparaci\xf3n con la infraestructura tradicional de capacidad fija.\\nUsar la instancia m\xe1s grande posible es lo opuesto a un buen dise\xf1o en la nube, ya que conduce al sobreaprovisionamiento y a costos innecesarios en lugar de ajustar la capacidad a la demanda real. Aprovisionar capacidad para el pico de carga es un enfoque tradicional on-premises que da como resultado recursos inactivos durante los per\xedodos normales, que la elasticidad de la nube est\xe1 dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para reemplazar. Usar el proceso de desarrollo Scrum es una metodolog\xeda de entrega de software no relacionada con los principios de dise\xf1o de arquitectura en la nube."},{"id":"q561","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un beneficio de usar AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"La seguridad permisiva elimina la carga administrativa","B":"Capacidad de enfocarse en actividades que generan ingresos","C":"Control sobre el hardware de red de la nube","D":"Elecci\xf3n de proveedores espec\xedficos de hardware de nube"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS delega la gesti\xf3n de la infraestructura a sus propios equipos, liberando a los clientes de las operaciones del centro de datos para que puedan redirigir tiempo y recursos hacia el desarrollo de productos, la interacci\xf3n con el cliente y otras actividades que generan ingresos.\\nLa seguridad permisiva no es un beneficio de AWS y contradice directamente el modelo de seguridad de AWS, que se basa en el principio de menor privilegio y en controles de acceso por capas. El control sobre el hardware de red de la nube no est\xe1 disponible para los clientes, ya que AWS gestiona toda la infraestructura de red f\xedsica en su nombre. La elecci\xf3n de proveedores espec\xedficos de hardware de nube no se ofrece a los clientes, ya que AWS selecciona y gestiona su propio hardware independientemente de las preferencias del cliente."},{"id":"q572","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de AWS deber\xeda aprovechar un cliente para lograr alta disponibilidad de una aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"Availability Zones","C":"Centros de datos","D":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Implementar en m\xfaltiples Availability Zones garantiza que una aplicaci\xf3n siga disponible si una zona falla, ya que cada AZ es una ubicaci\xf3n f\xedsicamente separada dentro de una Regi\xf3n con energ\xeda, red y conectividad independientes.\\nAWS Direct Connect proporciona un enlace de red privado dedicado entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS, lo que mejora la conectividad pero no proporciona redundancia ni tolerancia a fallos para la aplicaci\xf3n. Los centros de datos son las instalaciones f\xedsicas que componen las Availability Zones y son gestionados completamente por AWS, por lo que los clientes no pueden aprovechar directamente centros de datos individuales como una caracter\xedstica arquitect\xf3nica. Amazon VPC proporciona una red virtual aislada dentro de AWS para el despliegue de recursos y el control de acceso, pero no proporciona por s\xed misma redundancia ni garantiza que una aplicaci\xf3n permanezca disponible durante una falla."},{"id":"q574","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un principio de dise\xf1o de arquitectura en la nube?","options":{"A":"Escalar hacia arriba, no hacia afuera","B":"Acoplar d\xe9bilmente los componentes","C":"Construir sistemas monol\xedticos","D":"Usar software de base de datos comercial"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acoplar d\xe9bilmente los componentes significa dise\xf1arlos para que interact\xfaen a trav\xe9s de interfaces bien definidas, como colas o API, de modo que los cambios o fallas en un componente no impacten directamente a los dem\xe1s.\\nEscalar hacia arriba, no hacia afuera describe el escalado vertical, que es un enfoque on-premises que las mejores pr\xe1cticas de la nube desaconsejan en favor del escalado horizontal mediante la adici\xf3n de m\xe1s instancias. Construir sistemas monol\xedticos consolida todos los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n en una \xfanica unidad fuertemente acoplada, que es lo opuesto al principio de dise\xf1o en la nube de dividir las aplicaciones en componentes d\xe9bilmente acoplados y desplegables de forma independiente. Usar software de base de datos comercial es una decisi\xf3n de adquisici\xf3n de tecnolog\xeda en lugar de un principio de dise\xf1o arquitect\xf3nico, y las mejores pr\xe1cticas de la nube favorecen los servicios de base de datos administrados y creados para un prop\xf3sito espec\xedfico sobre el software comercial propietario cuando corresponda."},{"id":"q577","question":"Los beneficios financieros de usar AWS son:","options":{"A":"Reducci\xf3n del Costo Total de Propiedad (TCO)","B":"Aumento del gasto de capital (capex)","C":"Reducci\xf3n del gasto operativo (opex)","D":"Planes de pago diferido para startups","E":"L\xedneas de cr\xe9dito empresariales para startups"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS reduce el Costo Total de Propiedad al eliminar los gastos de capital del centro de datos, y reduce el gasto operativo mediante precios de pago por uso y servicios administrados que disminuyen los costos de personal y mantenimiento.\\nEl aumento del gasto de capital describe lo opuesto a un beneficio financiero de AWS. Migrar a AWS reemplaza las grandes inversiones iniciales en hardware por costos operativos variables, lo que reduce en lugar de aumentar el gasto de capital. Los planes de pago diferido para startups no son un beneficio financiero est\xe1ndar de AWS. Los cargos de AWS se facturan mensualmente seg\xfan el uso real, y no existen acuerdos de pago diferido ofrecidos como un beneficio general para clientes startup. AWS no proporciona l\xedneas de cr\xe9dito empresariales para startups como un beneficio financiero est\xe1ndar. AWS s\xed tiene programas como AWS Activate que proporcionan cr\xe9ditos a startups, pero estos son cr\xe9ditos promocionales en lugar de l\xedneas de cr\xe9dito o acuerdos de financiamiento."},{"id":"q583","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 costos se incluyen al comparar el Costo Total de Propiedad (TCO) de AWS con el TCO on-premises?","options":{"A":"Gesti\xf3n de proyectos","B":"Licenciamiento de software antivirus","C":"Seguridad del centro de datos","D":"Desarrollo de software"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El TCO on-premises incluye costos de seguridad f\xedsica del centro de datos, como guardias, controles de acceso y vigilancia. Estos se eliminan al migrar a AWS, donde la seguridad f\xedsica es responsabilidad totalmente de AWS.\\nLos costos de gesti\xf3n de proyectos se aplican por igual tanto a entornos on-premises como en la nube y no son un factor diferenciador en una comparaci\xf3n de TCO. El licenciamiento de software antivirus es un costo de la capa de aplicaci\xf3n que sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente independientemente de si se ejecuta on-premises o en AWS. Los costos de desarrollo de software tambi\xe9n son consistentes entre entornos y no cambian seg\xfan las decisiones de alojamiento de infraestructura."},{"id":"q590","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un principio importante de dise\xf1o arquitect\xf3nico al dise\xf1ar aplicaciones en la nube?","options":{"A":"Usar m\xfaltiples Availability Zones","B":"Usar componentes fuertemente acoplados","C":"Usar software de c\xf3digo abierto","D":"Aprovisionar capacidad adicional"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Usar m\xfaltiples Availability Zones garantiza alta disponibilidad al distribuir la aplicaci\xf3n en centros de datos f\xedsicamente aislados, de modo que una falla en cualquier zona individual no deje toda la aplicaci\xf3n fuera de l\xednea.\\nUsar componentes fuertemente acoplados es un antipatr\xf3n en la arquitectura en la nube, ya que las dependencias estrechas entre componentes hacen que una falla en uno pueda propagarse y derribar a otros. Usar software de c\xf3digo abierto es una elecci\xf3n de desarrollo y licenciamiento que no tiene relaci\xf3n con los principios de dise\xf1o arquitect\xf3nico en la nube para la disponibilidad o la resiliencia. Aprovisionar capacidad adicional contradice el principio de elasticidad de la nube, que favorece el escalado din\xe1mico basado en la demanda real en lugar de adivinar y sobreaprovisionar de antemano."},{"id":"q600","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de AWS reducir\xe1 el costo total de propiedad (TCO) del cliente?","options":{"A":"Modelo de seguridad de responsabilidad compartida","B":"Inquilino \xfanico","C":"Computaci\xf3n el\xe1stica","D":"Cifrado"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La computaci\xf3n el\xe1stica permite a los clientes ajustar la capacidad de los recursos a la demanda real, escalando durante los picos y reduciendo durante las temporadas bajas, lo que elimina el pago por infraestructura inactiva y reduce directamente el costo total de propiedad.\\nEl Modelo de Responsabilidad Compartida define la divisi\xf3n de las responsabilidades de seguridad entre AWS y el cliente, pero no reduce por s\xed mismo los costos de infraestructura ni el TCO. El inquilino \xfanico dedica hardware a un solo cliente, lo que suele aumentar el costo en comparaci\xf3n con el modelo multiinquilino compartido que sustenta las econom\xedas de escala de AWS. El cifrado es un control de seguridad que protege los datos en reposo y en tr\xe1nsito, y no tiene impacto directo en los costos de infraestructura ni en el TCO."},{"id":"q604","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de la nube de AWS admitir\xe1 el requisito de una empresa internacional de baja latencia para todos sus clientes?","options":{"A":"Tolerancia a fallos","B":"Alcance global","C":"Precios de pago por uso","D":"Alta disponibilidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El alcance global de AWS, con Regiones y ubicaciones de borde distribuidas en todo el mundo, permite a las empresas implementar recursos cerca de sus clientes internacionales, lo que reduce directamente la latencia de red.\\nLa tolerancia a fallos se refiere a la capacidad de un sistema para seguir funcionando a pesar de las fallas de componentes, no a reducir la latencia geogr\xe1fica. Los precios de pago por uso son un modelo de facturaci\xf3n y no tienen efecto en la latencia de red ni en el alcance geogr\xe1fico. La alta disponibilidad garantiza que los sistemas permanezcan operativos con un tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo, pero no aborda la proximidad f\xedsica necesaria para reducir la latencia de los clientes internacionales."},{"id":"q614","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 principios de dise\xf1o para la arquitectura en la nube se recomiendan al redise\xf1ar una aplicaci\xf3n monol\xedtica grande?","options":{"A":"Usar monitoreo manual","B":"Usar servidores fijos","C":"Implementar acoplamiento d\xe9bil","D":"Depender de componentes individuales","E":"Dise\xf1ar para la escalabilidad"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"El acoplamiento d\xe9bil reduce las dependencias entre componentes para que una falla en una parte de la aplicaci\xf3n no se propague a las dem\xe1s, y dise\xf1ar para la escalabilidad garantiza que cada componente pueda manejar cargas variables de forma independiente; ambos son principios esenciales al descomponer una arquitectura monol\xedtica.\\nEl monitoreo manual depende de la observaci\xf3n humana en lugar de alertas automatizadas y es un antipatr\xf3n en la arquitectura en la nube. Los servidores fijos contradicen el principio de elasticidad y escalabilidad de la nube, ya que fijan la capacidad a una cantidad predeterminada independientemente de la demanda. Depender de componentes individuales es la caracter\xedstica definitoria de una arquitectura monol\xedtica y es el problema que se est\xe1 resolviendo, no un principio de dise\xf1o recomendado."},{"id":"q621","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo se benefician los clientes de las enormes econom\xedas de escala de Amazon?","options":{"A":"Reducciones de precios peri\xf3dicas como resultado de las eficiencias operativas de Amazon","B":"Nuevos tipos de instancias de Amazon EC2 que proporcionan el hardware m\xe1s reciente","C":"La capacidad de escalar hacia arriba y hacia abajo cuando sea necesario","D":"Mayor confiabilidad en el hardware subyacente de las instancias de Amazon EC2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS transfiere sus eficiencias operativas y su enorme poder de compra a los clientes mediante reducciones de precios peri\xf3dicas, lo que reduce el costo de los servicios en la nube a medida que crece la escala de AWS.\\nLos nuevos tipos de instancias de EC2 que proporcionan el hardware m\xe1s reciente son una actividad de desarrollo de productos, no un beneficio financiero directo que se transfiera a los clientes a partir de las econom\xedas de escala. La capacidad de escalar hacia arriba y hacia abajo cuando sea necesario describe la elasticidad, que es un beneficio de la nube separado y no relacionado con las econom\xedas de escala. La mayor confiabilidad en el hardware subyacente describe la calidad de la infraestructura de AWS, no un beneficio de costo o eficiencia derivado de operar a una escala masiva."},{"id":"q634","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son beneficios de la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Tiempo de actividad ilimitado","B":"Elasticidad","C":"Agilidad","D":"Colocaci\xf3n","E":"Gastos de capital"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"La elasticidad permite que los recursos escalen autom\xe1ticamente hacia arriba y hacia abajo seg\xfan la demanda, y la agilidad permite el aprovisionamiento r\xe1pido y la experimentaci\xf3n sin ciclos de adquisici\xf3n prolongados. Ambas son ventajas principales de la nube de AWS sobre la infraestructura tradicional.\\nEl tiempo de actividad ilimitado no es un beneficio garantizado ni promocionado por AWS, ya que los servicios de AWS est\xe1n sujetos a SLA de disponibilidad pero no a un tiempo de actividad incondicional. La colocaci\xf3n se refiere a alojar sus propios servidores f\xedsicos en un centro de datos de terceros, lo que es un modelo de infraestructura tradicional en lugar de un beneficio de la nube. Los gastos de capital son un costo de la infraestructura local tradicional que AWS reemplaza con gasto operativo variable, lo que los convierte en una desventaja del modelo antiguo, no en un beneficio de la nube."},{"id":"q640","question":"Al comparar la nube de AWS con el costo total de propiedad local, \xbfqu\xe9 gastos deben considerarse?","options":{"A":"Desarrollo de software","B":"Gesti\xf3n de proyectos","C":"Hardware de almacenamiento","D":"Servidores f\xedsicos","E":"Licencia de software antivirus"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"El hardware de almacenamiento y los servidores f\xedsicos son gastos de capital locales que deben comprarse, mantenerse y eventualmente reemplazarse, lo que los convierte en diferenciadores clave en una comparaci\xf3n de TCO, ya que se eliminan al migrar a AWS.\\nLos costos de desarrollo de software se mantienen constantes independientemente de si la infraestructura es local o en la nube y no son un factor diferenciador en un an\xe1lisis de TCO. Los costos de gesti\xf3n de proyectos tambi\xe9n se aplican por igual en ambos entornos y no cambian seg\xfan las decisiones de alojamiento de infraestructura. El licenciamiento de software antivirus sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente tanto en entornos locales como en la nube y no se elimina al migrar a AWS."},{"id":"q642","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 escenarios representan el concepto de elasticidad en AWS?","options":{"A":"Escalar el n\xfamero de instancias de Amazon EC2 seg\xfan el tr\xe1fico","B":"Redimensionar instancias de Amazon RDS a medida que cambian las necesidades del negocio","C":"Dirigir autom\xe1ticamente el tr\xe1fico a instancias de Amazon EC2 menos utilizadas","D":"Usar documentos de cumplimiento de AWS para acelerar el proceso de cumplimiento","E":"Tener la capacidad de crear y gobernar entornos usando c\xf3digo"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Escalar instancias de EC2 horizontalmente seg\xfan el tr\xe1fico y redimensionar instancias de RDS verticalmente a medida que cambian las necesidades del negocio demuestran elasticidad, que es la capacidad de adaptar din\xe1micamente la capacidad de los recursos para satisfacer la demanda.\\nDirigir autom\xe1ticamente el tr\xe1fico a instancias de EC2 menos utilizadas describe el balanceo de carga, que optimiza la utilizaci\xf3n de recursos pero no cambia la cantidad de recursos aprovisionados. Usar documentos de cumplimiento de AWS para acelerar el proceso de cumplimiento describe el concepto de cumplimiento heredado, no la elasticidad. Tener la capacidad de crear y gobernar entornos usando c\xf3digo describe la infraestructura como c\xf3digo, que es un principio de nube separado y no relacionado con el escalado din\xe1mico de recursos."},{"id":"q644","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 considerando mover su centro de datos local a AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 factores deben incluirse al realizar un an\xe1lisis de costo total de propiedad (TCO)?","options":{"A":"Disponibilidad de instancias de Amazon EC2","B":"Consumo de energ\xeda del centro de datos","C":"Costos de mano de obra para reemplazar servidores antiguos","D":"Tiempo de los desarrolladores de aplicaciones","E":"Capacidad del motor de base de datos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"El consumo de energ\xeda y los costos de mano de obra para el reemplazo de servidores son gastos locales significativos que desaparecen al migrar a AWS, lo que los convierte en los factores relevantes en una comparaci\xf3n de TCO.\\nLa disponibilidad de instancias de Amazon EC2 es una consideraci\xf3n del lado de AWS, no un factor de costo local incluido en un an\xe1lisis de TCO. El tiempo de los desarrolladores de aplicaciones se mantiene constante independientemente del entorno de alojamiento y no es un costo de infraestructura diferenciador. La capacidad del motor de base de datos es una consideraci\xf3n de rendimiento y dimensionamiento, no un componente de costo local que cambie al migrar a AWS."},{"id":"q651","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes modelos de implementaci\xf3n permite a los clientes cambiar por completo sus gastos de capital de TI por gastos operativos?","options":{"A":"On-premises","B":"H\xedbrido","C":"Cloud","D":"Platform as a service"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El modelo de implementaci\xf3n cloud reemplaza por completo el gasto de capital inicial en hardware e instalaciones por gastos operativos variables basados en el uso real, eliminando la necesidad de poseer o mantener infraestructura f\xedsica.\\nUna implementaci\xf3n on-premises requiere que la organizaci\xf3n compre, posea y mantenga todo el hardware e instalaciones f\xedsicas, lo que conserva en lugar de eliminar el gasto de capital y no ofrece conversi\xf3n a gastos operativos. Una implementaci\xf3n h\xedbrida combina infraestructura on-premises con recursos cloud, lo que significa que el gasto de capital en hardware propio se mantiene junto con los costos operativos de cloud en lugar de cambiarse por completo por gastos operativos. Platform as a service es un modelo de entrega de servicios cloud en el que un proveedor gestiona una plataforma para crear y ejecutar aplicaciones, y no es un modelo de implementaci\xf3n que describa d\xf3nde se aloja la infraestructura."},{"id":"q662","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 principio arquitect\xf3nico se utiliza al implementar una instancia de Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) en modo de m\xfaltiples Availability Zones?","options":{"A":"Implementar un acoplamiento d\xe9bil","B":"Dise\xf1ar para el fallo","C":"Automatizar todo lo que se pueda automatizar","D":"Usar servicios, no servidores"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Implementar RDS en modo Multi-AZ aplica el principio de dise\xf1o para el fallo al mantener una r\xe9plica en espera s\xedncrona en otra Availability Zone, con conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica si la instancia principal deja de estar disponible.\\nEl acoplamiento d\xe9bil se refiere a reducir las interdependencias entre los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n para que los fallos no se propaguen en cascada, no a la redundancia de bases de datos entre zonas. Automatizar todo lo que se pueda automatizar se refiere a reemplazar tareas operativas manuales por procesos scriptados o gestionados para mejorar la consistencia. Usar servicios, no servidores se refiere a aprovechar los servicios gestionados de AWS en lugar de aprovisionar y mantener su propia infraestructura."},{"id":"q666","question":"La aplicaci\xf3n web de una empresa tiene actualmente dependencias estrechas con los componentes subyacentes, por lo que cuando un componente falla, toda la aplicaci\xf3n web falla. \xbfQu\xe9 principio de dise\xf1o de AWS Cloud aplicado abordar\xe1 el problema de dise\xf1o actual?","options":{"A":"Implementar elasticidad, lo que permite a la aplicaci\xf3n escalar hacia arriba o hacia abajo seg\xfan cambie la demanda","B":"Habilitar varias instancias de EC2 para que se ejecuten en paralelo y lograr un mejor rendimiento","C":"Enfocarse en desacoplar los componentes aisl\xe1ndolos y asegurando que los componentes individuales puedan funcionar cuando otros componentes fallen","D":"Duplicar los recursos de c\xf3mputo de EC2 para aumentar la tolerancia a fallos del sistema"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Desacoplar los componentes significa aislarlos para que cada uno pueda funcionar de forma independiente, comunic\xe1ndose mediante interfaces as\xedncronas en lugar de llamadas directas, de modo que el fallo de un componente no derribe toda la aplicaci\xf3n.\\nImplementar elasticidad aborda el escalado de recursos hacia arriba y hacia abajo en respuesta a la demanda, pero no resuelve el problema de dependencia estrecha que causa fallos en cascada. Habilitar instancias de EC2 para que se ejecuten en paralelo mejora el rendimiento y la capacidad de procesamiento, pero no a\xedsla los componentes de los fallos de los dem\xe1s. Duplicar los recursos de c\xf3mputo de EC2 agrega capacidad, pero no cambia la arquitectura estrechamente acoplada, lo que significa que el fallo de un componente seguir\xeda derribando toda la aplicaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q671","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un principio de dise\xf1o del AWS Well-Architected Framework relacionado con la confiabilidad?","options":{"A":"Implementaci\xf3n en una sola Availability Zone","B":"Capacidad de recuperarse de un fallo","C":"Dise\xf1ar para la optimizaci\xf3n de costos","D":"Realizar operaciones como c\xf3digo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La capacidad de recuperarse de un fallo es un principio de dise\xf1o central del pilar de Reliability, que se enfoca en garantizar que las cargas de trabajo realicen su funci\xf3n prevista de manera correcta y consistente, incluso mediante mecanismos de recuperaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica.\\nLa implementaci\xf3n en una sola Availability Zone es en realidad un antipatr\xf3n para la confiabilidad, ya que crea un \xfanico punto de fallo en lugar de proporcionar tolerancia a fallos. Dise\xf1ar para la optimizaci\xf3n de costos describe el objetivo del pilar de Cost Optimization, no del pilar de Reliability. Realizar operaciones como c\xf3digo es un principio de dise\xf1o del pilar de Operational Excellence, enfocado en automatizar tareas operativas en lugar de en la recuperaci\xf3n de fallos."},{"id":"q672","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una ventaja de usar AWS Cloud frente a una soluci\xf3n on-premises tradicional?","options":{"A":"Los usuarios no tienen que adivinar las necesidades futuras de capacidad","B":"Los usuarios pueden utilizar contratos de hardware existentes para compras","C":"Los usuarios pueden fijar los costos sin importar su tr\xe1fico","D":"Los usuarios pueden evitar auditor\xedas usando informes de AWS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS elimina la necesidad de adivinar las necesidades futuras de capacidad al proporcionar escalado bajo demanda, lo que permite a las organizaciones aprovisionar exactamente lo que necesitan y ajustarse en minutos en lugar de comprometerse con infraestructura de hardware fija con meses o a\xf1os de anticipaci\xf3n.\\nUtilizar contratos de hardware existentes para compras es una caracter\xedstica de la adquisici\xf3n on-premises tradicional en lugar de una ventaja de AWS; en la nube, los clientes evitan por completo los contratos de hardware al pagar solo por lo que consumen. Fijar los costos independientemente del tr\xe1fico no es una ventaja de AWS; los costos de cloud escalan con el uso, y AWS ofrece precios variables que se ajustan a la demanda real en lugar de costos fijos, lo cual es una de sus ventajas financieras sobre la infraestructura tradicional de capacidad fija. Evitar auditor\xedas usando informes de AWS no es una ventaja v\xe1lida; si bien AWS Artifact proporciona informes de cumplimiento que respaldan las auditor\xedas de los clientes, los clientes siguen sujetos a sus propios requisitos de auditor\xeda regulatoria y no pueden evitar las auditor\xedas por el hecho de usar AWS."},{"id":"q681","question":"\xbfPor qu\xe9 deber\xeda una empresa elegir AWS en lugar de un centro de datos tradicional?","options":{"A":"AWS proporciona a los usuarios control total sobre los recursos subyacentes","B":"AWS no requiere contratos a largo plazo y proporciona un modelo de pago por uso","C":"AWS ofrece ubicaciones de borde en todos los pa\xedses, lo que respalda el alcance global","D":"AWS no tiene l\xedmites en la cantidad de recursos que se pueden crear"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS ofrece un modelo de precios de pago por uso sin contratos a largo plazo requeridos para la mayor\xeda de los servicios, lo que permite a las empresas evitar grandes gastos de capital iniciales y pagar solo por los recursos que realmente consumen.\\nAWS no proporciona a los usuarios control total sobre los recursos f\xedsicos subyacentes; AWS gestiona el hardware, el hipervisor y la infraestructura f\xedsica, mientras que los clientes gestionan sus cargas de trabajo y datos. AWS no tiene ubicaciones de borde en todos los pa\xedses; AWS tiene ubicaciones de borde en muchos pa\xedses para servicios como CloudFront, pero la cobertura no es universal en todas las naciones, y esta no es una raz\xf3n principal para elegir AWS en lugar de un centro de datos tradicional. Si bien AWS tiene l\xedmites de servicio muy altos, existen l\xedmites en la cantidad de recursos que se pueden crear por cuenta por Region; los clientes pueden solicitar aumentos de l\xedmites, pero los recursos no son realmente ilimitados, lo que hace que esta afirmaci\xf3n sea inexacta."},{"id":"q686","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo reduce AWS M\xc1S eficazmente los costos de c\xf3mputo para una empresa emergente en crecimiento?","options":{"A":"Proporciona recursos bajo demanda para los picos de uso","B":"Automatiza el aprovisionamiento de entornos individuales de desarrolladores","C":"Automatiza la gesti\xf3n de relaciones con los clientes","D":"Implementa un presupuesto mensual fijo de c\xf3mputo"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS proporciona recursos bajo demanda que se pueden aprovisionar al instante para los picos de uso y liberar cuando no se necesitan, por lo que una empresa emergente en crecimiento solo paga por la capacidad de c\xf3mputo que realmente usa en lugar de comprar hardware dimensionado para la carga m\xe1xima prevista.\\nAutomatizar el aprovisionamiento de entornos individuales de desarrolladores es un beneficio de las herramientas de infraestructura como c\xf3digo, como AWS CloudFormation, pero es una caracter\xedstica de productividad para desarrolladores en lugar del mecanismo principal por el cual AWS reduce los costos de c\xf3mputo para una empresa emergente en crecimiento. Automatizar la gesti\xf3n de relaciones con los clientes describe la funcionalidad del software de CRM y no tiene relaci\xf3n alguna con c\xf3mo AWS reduce los costos de infraestructura de c\xf3mputo para las empresas. Implementar un presupuesto mensual fijo de c\xf3mputo es una pr\xe1ctica de gobernanza financiera que establece l\xedmites de gasto, pero un presupuesto fijo no reduce por s\xed mismo los costos de c\xf3mputo y restringe la flexibilidad en lugar de permitir un escalado rentable seg\xfan la demanda."},{"id":"q687","question":"Una startup est\xe1 trabajando en una nueva aplicaci\xf3n que necesita llegar al mercado r\xe1pidamente. Es posible que los requisitos de la aplicaci\xf3n deban ajustarse en el futuro cercano. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una caracter\xedstica de AWS Cloud que satisfar\xeda esta necesidad espec\xedfica?","options":{"A":"Elasticidad","B":"Confiabilidad","C":"Rendimiento","D":"Agilidad"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La agilidad en AWS Cloud significa la capacidad de aprovisionar recursos r\xe1pidamente, iterar sobre los requisitos de la aplicaci\xf3n e implementar cambios con rapidez, lo que permite a las startups llegar al mercado r\xe1pido y adaptarse a medida que cambian las necesidades.\\nLa elasticidad se refiere a escalar din\xe1micamente los recursos hacia arriba o hacia abajo en respuesta a la demanda, lo que aborda la gesti\xf3n de capacidad en lugar de la velocidad de crear e iterar sobre nuevas aplicaciones. La confiabilidad se refiere a la capacidad de una carga de trabajo de realizar su funci\xf3n prevista correctamente y recuperarse autom\xe1ticamente de fallos, lo cual es una cualidad arquitect\xf3nica en lugar de una caracter\xedstica que acelera el tiempo de comercializaci\xf3n. El rendimiento se refiere a usar los recursos de c\xf3mputo de manera eficiente para cumplir con los requisitos de la carga de trabajo, lo cual no est\xe1 relacionado con la velocidad de aprovisionamiento e iteraci\xf3n sobre nuevos requisitos de aplicaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q691","question":"Al comparar AWS con el costo total de propiedad (TCO) en las instalaciones, \xbfqu\xe9 costos se incluyen?","options":{"A":"Seguridad del centro de datos","B":"An\xe1lisis de negocio","C":"Gesti\xf3n de proyectos","D":"Administraci\xf3n de sistemas operativos"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Los costos de seguridad del centro de datos, como los controles de acceso f\xedsico, la vigilancia y los guardias, forman parte del TCO on-premises que se eliminan al migrar a AWS, donde la seguridad f\xedsica est\xe1 incluida en el servicio.\\nEl an\xe1lisis de negocio es una funci\xf3n profesional que eval\xfaa necesidades y soluciones organizacionales, y no es un costo de infraestructura f\xedsica incluido en una comparaci\xf3n de TCO on-premises. La gesti\xf3n de proyectos es un costo de servicio profesional asociado con la planificaci\xf3n y ejecuci\xf3n de iniciativas, y no es un gasto de infraestructura de centro de datos que se incluya como factor en el TCO. La administraci\xf3n de sistemas operativos es un costo de mano de obra del que los clientes siguen siendo responsables seg\xfan el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida, independientemente de si est\xe1n on-premises o en AWS, por lo que no se elimina con la migraci\xf3n y no diferencia los dos modelos de TCO."},{"id":"q698","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una ventaja de usar AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS audita los datos del usuario","B":"Los datos son autom\xe1ticamente seguros","C":"No hay que adivinar las necesidades de capacidad","D":"AWS gestiona las necesidades de cumplimiento"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS elimina la necesidad de adivinar las necesidades de capacidad al proporcionar escalado bajo demanda, de modo que las organizaciones pueden aprovisionar exactamente lo que necesitan y ajustarlo en minutos en lugar de comprar hardware bas\xe1ndose en previsiones de capacidad m\xe1xima.\\nAWS no audita los datos del usuario; AWS tiene pol\xedticas y controles estrictos contra el acceso a datos de clientes sin autorizaci\xf3n, y el cliente sigue siendo responsable de la privacidad y gobernanza de los datos almacenados en su entorno de AWS. Los datos almacenados en AWS no son autom\xe1ticamente seguros; los clientes deben configurar activamente el cifrado, los controles de acceso y la configuraci\xf3n de seguridad para sus propios datos, ya que el modelo de responsabilidad compartida requiere que los clientes protejan los datos que colocan en la nube. AWS no gestiona las necesidades de cumplimiento de clientes individuales; aunque AWS proporciona certificaciones de cumplimiento para su infraestructura y herramientas a trav\xe9s de Artifact para respaldar los esfuerzos de cumplimiento de los clientes, cada cliente es responsable de aplicar esos marcos a sus propias cargas de trabajo y de cumplir con sus requisitos regulatorios espec\xedficos."},{"id":"q701","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 principios se utilizan para dise\xf1ar aplicaciones para la confiabilidad en la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Dise\xf1ar para la recuperaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica ante fallas","B":"Usar m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad","C":"Gestionar los cambios mediante procesos documentados","D":"Probar con demanda moderada para garantizar la confiabilidad","E":"Respaldar la recuperaci\xf3n en un entorno on-premises"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Dise\xf1ar para la recuperaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica ante fallas garantiza que los sistemas se autorreparen sin intervenci\xf3n manual, y desplegar en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad protege contra fallas a nivel de AZ, siendo ambos principios fundamentales de confiabilidad.\\nGestionar los cambios mediante procesos documentados es un principio de Excelencia Operativa centrado en la gesti\xf3n controlada de cambios, no un principio de arquitectura de confiabilidad. Probar con demanda moderada no refleja un principio de confiabilidad reconocido, ya que el Well-Architected Framework recomienda probar los procedimientos de recuperaci\xf3n y el escalado en condiciones de carga m\xe1xima y de falla, en lugar de carga moderada. Respaldar la recuperaci\xf3n en un entorno on-premises introduce una dependencia de la infraestructura local, lo que contradice el principio de confiabilidad en la nube de eliminar los puntos \xfanicos de falla y mantener la recuperaci\xf3n dentro del entorno de AWS."},{"id":"q703","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es un ejemplo de alta disponibilidad en la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Consultar el soporte t\xe9cnico de AWS en cualquier momento, de d\xeda o de noche","B":"Garantizar que una aplicaci\xf3n siga siendo accesible, incluso si un recurso falla","C":"Hacer que cualquier servicio de AWS est\xe9 disponible para su uso pagando bajo demanda","D":"Implementar en cualquier parte del mundo usando las Regiones de AWS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La alta disponibilidad significa que una aplicaci\xf3n sigue siendo accesible incluso cuando fallan recursos individuales, lo que se logra mediante redundancia en las Zonas de Disponibilidad, escalado autom\xe1tico y mecanismos de conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica.\\nConsultar el soporte t\xe9cnico de AWS en cualquier momento describe la disponibilidad 24/7 de los planes de soporte, que es una oferta de servicio y no una caracter\xedstica arquitect\xf3nica de la nube. Hacer que cualquier servicio de AWS est\xe9 disponible pagando bajo demanda describe el modelo de precios de pago por uso, que es un concepto de facturaci\xf3n no relacionado con la tolerancia a fallos ni el tiempo de actividad. Implementar en cualquier parte del mundo usando las Regiones de AWS describe el alcance global, que es un beneficio de la nube separado y por s\xed mismo no garantiza que una aplicaci\xf3n siga siendo accesible si falla un recurso."},{"id":"q721","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 beneficio de la nube de AWS respalda la adaptaci\xf3n del suministro de recursos a las demandas cambiantes de las cargas de trabajo?","options":{"A":"Seguridad","B":"Confiabilidad","C":"Elasticidad","D":"Alta disponibilidad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La elasticidad es la capacidad de escalar recursos autom\xe1ticamente hacia arriba o hacia abajo para adaptarse a demandas cambiantes de las cargas de trabajo, lo que garantiza que se tenga la cantidad correcta de capacidad sin aprovisionar en exceso ni en defecto.\\nSeguridad se refiere a proteger datos, sistemas y activos mediante controles y estrategias de mitigaci\xf3n de riesgos, y no est\xe1 relacionada con adaptar el suministro de recursos a la demanda. Confiabilidad se refiere a la capacidad de una carga de trabajo de realizar su funci\xf3n prevista correctamente y recuperarse autom\xe1ticamente de fallas, lo cual es una cualidad arquitect\xf3nica y no un concepto de gesti\xf3n de capacidad. Alta disponibilidad se refiere a mantener una aplicaci\xf3n operativa durante fallas mediante redundancia, lo que aborda el tiempo de actividad en lugar del ajuste din\xe1mico de recursos para adaptarse a la demanda de la carga de trabajo."},{"id":"q723","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son beneficios de alojar infraestructura en la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"No hay compromisos iniciales","B":"AWS gestiona toda la seguridad en la nube","C":"Los usuarios tienen la capacidad de aprovisionar recursos bajo demanda","D":"Los usuarios tienen acceso a almacenamiento gratuito e ilimitado","E":"Los usuarios tienen control sobre la infraestructura f\xedsica"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS no requiere compromisos iniciales de hardware, lo que permite a las organizaciones pagar solo por lo que consumen, y los recursos se pueden aprovisionar bajo demanda en minutos, lo que permite a las empresas escalar r\xe1pidamente sin esperar la adquisici\xf3n e instalaci\xf3n de hardware.\\nAWS no gestiona toda la seguridad en la nube; seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, AWS protege la infraestructura subyacente mientras los clientes son responsables de proteger sus aplicaciones, datos y configuraciones que se ejecutan sobre esa infraestructura. Los usuarios no tienen acceso a almacenamiento gratuito e ilimitado en AWS; los servicios de almacenamiento como S3 y EBS tienen precios basados en el uso, y aunque existen l\xedmites del nivel gratuito para cuentas nuevas, el almacenamiento no es gratuito ni ilimitado en general. Los usuarios no tienen control sobre la infraestructura f\xedsica; una de las compensaciones de pasar a la nube es que los clientes renuncian al acceso f\xedsico y al control del hardware subyacente, confiando en que AWS lo gestione de forma segura y confiable."},{"id":"q728","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son los beneficios de usar la nube de AWS para empresas con clientes en muchos pa\xedses del mundo?","options":{"A":"Las empresas pueden implementar aplicaciones en m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS para reducir la latencia","B":"Amazon Translate traduce autom\xe1ticamente las interfaces de sitios web de terceros a varios idiomas","C":"Amazon CloudFront tiene m\xfaltiples ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo para reducir la latencia","D":"Amazon Comprehend permite a los usuarios crear aplicaciones que pueden responder a solicitudes de usuarios en muchos idiomas","E":"Elastic Load Balancing puede distribuir el tr\xe1fico web de aplicaciones a m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS en todo el mundo, lo que reduce la latencia"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Implementar en m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS coloca los recursos f\xedsicamente m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios internacionales, y las ubicaciones de borde globales de CloudFront almacenan en cach\xe9 contenido cerca de los usuarios finales en todo el mundo, lo que reduce directamente la latencia para clientes globales.\\nAmazon Translate es un servicio de traducci\xf3n autom\xe1tica para convertir texto entre idiomas y no traduce autom\xe1ticamente las interfaces de sitios web sin una integraci\xf3n personalizada. Amazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural para analizar y extraer significado del texto, no una herramienta para habilitar respuestas de aplicaciones multiling\xfces. Elastic Load Balancing distribuye tr\xe1fico entre destinos dentro de una sola Regi\xf3n y no puede enrutar tr\xe1fico entre Regiones para reducir la latencia geogr\xe1fica."},{"id":"q731","question":"Cuando una empresa aprovisiona servidores web en m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS, \xbfqu\xe9 se est\xe1 incrementando?","options":{"A":"Acoplamiento","B":"Disponibilidad","C":"Seguridad","D":"Durabilidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aprovisionar servidores web en m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS aumenta la disponibilidad al garantizar que, si una Regi\xf3n completa experimenta una interrupci\xf3n, los usuarios puedan ser atendidos desde servidores en otra Regi\xf3n.\\nEl acoplamiento se refiere al grado de dependencia entre componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n y no se ve afectado por la cantidad de Regiones en las que se implementan los servidores. La seguridad no aumenta simplemente por implementar en m\xfaltiples Regiones, ya que depende de la configuraci\xf3n y los controles aplicados de forma coherente independientemente de la distribuci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica. La durabilidad se refiere a la persistencia a largo plazo de los datos almacenados sin corrupci\xf3n ni p\xe9rdida, lo cual es una caracter\xedstica de almacenamiento no relacionada con d\xf3nde se aprovisionan los servidores web."},{"id":"q736","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son pilares del AWS Well-Architected Framework?","options":{"A":"M\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad","B":"Eficiencia en el rendimiento","C":"Seguridad","D":"Uso de cifrado","E":"Alta disponibilidad"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"El AWS Well-Architected Framework tiene seis pilares: Excelencia operativa, Seguridad, Confiabilidad, Eficiencia en el rendimiento, Optimizaci\xf3n de costos y Sostenibilidad. Performance Efficiency y Security son ambos pilares oficiales.\\nM\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad es un concepto de infraestructura y una estrategia de despliegue, no un pilar del Well-Architected. El uso de cifrado es una pr\xe1ctica de seguridad que se incluye en el pilar de Seguridad, no un pilar por s\xed mismo. La alta disponibilidad es un objetivo de dise\xf1o asociado con el pilar de Confiabilidad, no un pilar independiente."},{"id":"q741","question":"Tratar la infraestructura como c\xf3digo en la AWS Cloud permite a los usuarios:","options":{"A":"Automatizar la migraci\xf3n de hardware local a los centros de datos de AWS","B":"Permitir que un tercero automatice una auditor\xeda de la infraestructura de AWS","C":"Entregar el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n a AWS para que se ejecute en la infraestructura de AWS","D":"Automatizar el proceso de aprovisionamiento de infraestructura"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La infraestructura como c\xf3digo permite a los usuarios definir y automatizar el aprovisionamiento de recursos de AWS mediante plantillas, lo que permite implementaciones repetibles y controladas por versiones sin configuraci\xf3n manual.\\nAutomatizar la migraci\xf3n de hardware local a los centros de datos de AWS no es posible mediante infraestructura como c\xf3digo, ya que la migraci\xf3n de hardware f\xedsico es un proceso separado gestionado fuera del aprovisionamiento basado en c\xf3digo. Permitir que un tercero automatice una auditor\xeda de la infraestructura de AWS describe una actividad de cumplimiento o seguridad no relacionada con el aprovisionamiento de infraestructura. Entregar el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n a AWS para que se ejecute en su infraestructura representa err\xf3neamente c\xf3mo funciona AWS, ya que los clientes siempre son responsables de su propio c\xf3digo de aplicaci\xf3n y despliegue."},{"id":"q750","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son los beneficios inmediatos de usar la AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Mayor personal de TI","B":"Los gastos de capital se reemplazan por gastos variables","C":"Control del usuario sobre la infraestructura","D":"Mayor agilidad","E":"AWS asume la responsabilidad de la seguridad en la nube"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Pasar a AWS reemplaza de inmediato grandes gastos de capital iniciales con gastos variables de pago por uso, y aumenta la agilidad al permitir el aprovisionamiento r\xe1pido de recursos en minutos en lugar de semanas.\\nPasar a AWS normalmente reduce la necesidad de trabajo de gesti\xf3n de infraestructura no diferenciada en lugar de aumentar la plantilla de personal de TI. El control del usuario sobre la infraestructura disminuye al pasar a AWS, ya que la gesti\xf3n del hardware f\xedsico la realiza completamente AWS seg\xfan el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida. AWS tiene la responsabilidad de la seguridad de la nube, que abarca la infraestructura f\xedsica y los servicios gestionados, pero la seguridad en la nube sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente, lo que hace que esta opci\xf3n sea incorrecta desde el punto de vista f\xe1ctico."},{"id":"q752","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 deben hacer los usuarios si quieren instalar una aplicaci\xf3n en ubicaciones geogr\xe1ficamente aisladas?","options":{"A":"Instalar la aplicaci\xf3n usando m\xfaltiples internet gateways","B":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en una Amazon VPC","C":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS","D":"Configurar la aplicaci\xf3n usando m\xfaltiples NAT gateways"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Implementar en m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS ubica una aplicaci\xf3n en ubicaciones geogr\xe1ficamente aisladas alrededor del mundo, cada una con infraestructura independiente para m\xe1xima separaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica.\\nInstalar la aplicaci\xf3n usando m\xfaltiples internet gateways es incorrecto. Los internet gateways conectan una VPC a internet y no tienen relaci\xf3n con el aislamiento geogr\xe1fico. Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en una Amazon VPC es incorrecto. Una VPC es una red privada dentro de una sola Regi\xf3n y no proporciona aislamiento geogr\xe1fico entre ubicaciones. Configurar la aplicaci\xf3n usando m\xfaltiples NAT gateways es incorrecto. Los NAT gateways permiten el acceso saliente a internet para subredes privadas y no est\xe1n relacionados con la distribuci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica."},{"id":"q753","question":"Un sistema en la AWS Cloud est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para soportar el fallo de uno o m\xe1s componentes. \xbfDe qu\xe9 es esto un ejemplo?","options":{"A":"Elasticidad","B":"Alta disponibilidad","C":"Escalabilidad","D":"Agilidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un sistema dise\xf1ado para soportar fallos de componentes es un ejemplo de alta disponibilidad, que garantiza que una aplicaci\xf3n siga siendo accesible mediante redundancia y mecanismos de conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1ticos.\\nLa elasticidad se refiere al escalado din\xe1mico de recursos hacia arriba o hacia abajo en respuesta a la demanda, lo que aborda la gesti\xf3n de capacidad en lugar de la tolerancia a fallos. La escalabilidad se refiere a la capacidad de manejar una mayor carga a\xf1adiendo recursos, lo que tiene que ver con la capacidad de crecimiento en lugar de la operaci\xf3n continua durante fallos. La agilidad se refiere a la velocidad con la que se pueden aprovisionar recursos e iterar sobre ellos, lo que no est\xe1 relacionado con la capacidad de un sistema para soportar fallos de componentes."},{"id":"q761","question":"Una empresa quiere construir sus nuevas cargas de trabajo de aplicaciones en la AWS Cloud en lugar de usar recursos locales. \xbfQu\xe9 gasto se puede reducir usando la AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"El costo de escribir c\xf3digo Java o Node.js personalizado","B":"Pruebas de penetraci\xf3n para seguridad","C":"Hardware necesario para soportar nuevas aplicaciones","D":"Escribir casos de prueba espec\xedficos para aplicaciones de terceros"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mover cargas de trabajo a AWS elimina el gasto de capital requerido para comprar, instalar, montar en rack y cablear servidores f\xedsicos y equipos de red para nuevas aplicaciones, reemplaz\xe1ndolo con precios de consumo de pago por uso.\\nEl costo de escribir c\xf3digo de aplicaci\xf3n personalizado en lenguajes como Java o Node.js permanece sin cambios al pasar a la nube, ya que el desarrollo de software es un costo de personas y procesos que es independiente de si la infraestructura es local o est\xe1 alojada en AWS. Los costos de pruebas de penetraci\xf3n para seguridad permanecen con el equipo de la aplicaci\xf3n tanto si es local como si est\xe1 en la nube, ya que la necesidad de probar la seguridad de la aplicaci\xf3n y de la infraestructura existe independientemente de d\xf3nde se ejecute la carga de trabajo, y AWS proporciona orientaci\xf3n para probar sus propios componentes por separado. Escribir casos de prueba para aplicaciones de terceros es una tarea de desarrollo y aseguramiento de calidad que se mantiene consistente independientemente de la ubicaci\xf3n de la infraestructura, ya que est\xe1 determinada por los requisitos del software de terceros en lugar del entorno de alojamiento."},{"id":"q763","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 significa si un usuario implementa una arquitectura de nube h\xedbrida en AWS?","options":{"A":"Todos los recursos se ejecutan usando infraestructura local","B":"Algunos recursos se ejecutan localmente y algunos se ejecutan en un centro de coubicaci\xf3n","C":"Todos los recursos se ejecutan en la AWS Cloud","D":"Algunos recursos se ejecutan localmente y algunos se ejecutan en la AWS Cloud"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Una arquitectura de nube h\xedbrida significa que algunos recursos se ejecutan localmente en centros de datos existentes mientras que otros recursos se ejecutan en la AWS Cloud, conectados mediante servicios como AWS VPN o AWS Direct Connect.\\nEjecutar todos los recursos usando infraestructura local describe una implementaci\xf3n totalmente local sin participaci\xf3n de la nube, que es lo opuesto a una arquitectura de nube. Ejecutar algunos recursos localmente y algunos en un centro de coubicaci\xf3n describe una divisi\xf3n entre espacio f\xedsico de centro de datos propio y alquilado, pero no implica a ning\xfan proveedor de nube y, por lo tanto, no constituye una arquitectura de nube h\xedbrida. Ejecutar todos los recursos en la AWS Cloud describe una implementaci\xf3n totalmente nativa de la nube, que es una arquitectura de nube p\xfablica en lugar de h\xedbrida."},{"id":"q764","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son las ventajas de la AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Costo mensual de tarifa fija","B":"No hay necesidad de adivinar los requisitos de capacidad","C":"Mayor velocidad para llegar al mercado","D":"Mayor gasto de capital inicial","E":"Acceso f\xedsico a los centros de datos en la nube"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS elimina la necesidad de adivinar los requisitos de capacidad al proporcionar escalado bajo demanda, y aumenta la velocidad para llegar al mercado al permitir el aprovisionamiento r\xe1pido de recursos en minutos en lugar de semanas.\\nEl costo mensual de tarifa fija es lo opuesto a c\xf3mo funciona el precio de AWS. AWS utiliza un modelo variable de pago por uso, no facturaci\xf3n mensual fija. El mayor gasto de capital inicial tambi\xe9n es lo opuesto a una ventaja de AWS. Uno de los beneficios principales de la nube es reemplazar el gasto de capital inicial con costos operativos variables. El acceso f\xedsico a los centros de datos en la nube no est\xe1 disponible para los clientes y no es una ventaja de AWS."},{"id":"q765","question":"Al comparar el costo total de propiedad (TCO) de una infraestructura local con una arquitectura en la nube, \xbfqu\xe9 costos se deben considerar?","options":{"A":"Las comisiones por procesamiento de tarjetas de cr\xe9dito para transacciones de la aplicaci\xf3n en la nube","B":"El costo de comprar e instalar hardware de servidor en el centro de datos local","C":"El costo de administrar la infraestructura, incluidas las instalaciones de sistemas operativos y software, parches, copias de seguridad y la recuperaci\xf3n ante fallos","D":"Los costos de las pruebas de penetraci\xf3n de terceros","E":"Los costos de publicidad asociados con una campa\xf1a continua en toda la empresa"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"El costo de comprar e instalar hardware de servidor f\xedsico en las instalaciones locales, y el costo administrativo continuo de gestionar la infraestructura, incluidas las instalaciones de SO, la aplicaci\xf3n de parches, las copias de seguridad y la recuperaci\xf3n ante fallos, son ambos costos directos del mantenimiento de la infraestructura local que desaparecen al migrar a AWS y deben incluirse en una comparaci\xf3n de TCO.\\nLas comisiones por procesamiento de tarjetas de cr\xe9dito para transacciones de la aplicaci\xf3n son un costo operativo del negocio que existe independientemente de si la infraestructura es local o est\xe1 en la nube, ya que las determina el procesador de pagos y no el entorno de alojamiento; por lo tanto, no son relevantes para una comparaci\xf3n de TCO entre la nube y las instalaciones locales. Los costos de pruebas de penetraci\xf3n de terceros pueden aplicarse tanto en entornos locales como en la nube, seg\xfan los requisitos de seguridad de la organizaci\xf3n, y no son un factor de costo distintivo que diferencie el TCO local de los costos de la arquitectura en la nube. Los costos de publicidad para campa\xf1as en toda la empresa son un gasto de marketing totalmente ajeno al modelo de alojamiento de la infraestructura y no tienen relaci\xf3n con la comparaci\xf3n de costos entre la infraestructura del centro de datos local y la arquitectura en la nube."},{"id":"q772","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de dise\xf1o de AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Acoplamiento fuerte de componentes","B":"Punto \xfanico de fallo","C":"Alta disponibilidad","D":"Sobreaprovisionamiento de recursos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La alta disponibilidad es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de dise\xf1o de AWS Cloud que garantiza que las aplicaciones sigan siendo accesibles mediante redundancia, implementaciones en m\xfaltiples AZ y mecanismos de conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1ticos.\\nEl acoplamiento fuerte de componentes es un antipatr\xf3n en el dise\xf1o en la nube, ya que crea dependencias entre servicios que provocan fallos en cascada y contradice la pr\xe1ctica recomendada del acoplamiento d\xe9bil. El punto \xfanico de fallo es una debilidad arquitect\xf3nica que las pr\xe1cticas recomendadas de dise\xf1o en la nube tienen como objetivo espec\xedfico eliminar mediante redundancia e implementaciones distribuidas. El sobreaprovisionamiento de recursos es un h\xe1bito local que la adopci\xf3n de la nube pretende eliminar mediante precios de pago por uso y escalado el\xe1stico, en lugar de una pr\xe1ctica recomendada que se deba seguir."},{"id":"q773","question":"\xbfPor qu\xe9 AWS es m\xe1s econ\xf3mico que los centros de datos tradicionales para aplicaciones con cargas de trabajo de c\xf3mputo variables?","options":{"A":"Los costos de Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) se facturan mensualmente","B":"Los clientes conservan acceso administrativo completo a sus instancias de Amazon EC2","C":"Las instancias de Amazon EC2 se pueden lanzar bajo demanda cuando sea necesario","D":"Los clientes pueden ejecutar permanentemente suficientes instancias para manejar cargas de trabajo m\xe1ximas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las instancias de EC2 se pueden lanzar bajo demanda cuando sea necesario y terminarse cuando ya no se requieran, por lo que solo se paga por el c\xf3mputo durante el uso real en lugar de mantener hardware dimensionado para la capacidad m\xe1xima en todo momento.\\nLos costos de Amazon EC2 no se facturan mensualmente. Las instancias de EC2 basadas en Linux se facturan por segundo con un m\xednimo de un minuto, lo que significa que se paga solo por el tiempo exacto de c\xf3mputo consumido en lugar de un cargo mensual fijo. Que los clientes conserven acceso administrativo completo a sus instancias de EC2 es una capacidad que les da control sobre su entorno, pero el acceso administrativo no es en s\xed mismo una raz\xf3n por la que AWS sea m\xe1s econ\xf3mico que los centros de datos tradicionales. Ejecutar permanentemente suficientes instancias para manejar cargas de trabajo m\xe1ximas describe el enfoque tradicional de los centros de datos de sobreaprovisionar para la carga m\xe1xima prevista, que es exactamente el modelo que hace que la infraestructura tradicional sea menos econ\xf3mica que el modelo el\xe1stico y bajo demanda de AWS."},{"id":"q783","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un ejemplo de c\xf3mo migrar a AWS Cloud reduce el costo inicial?","options":{"A":"Al reemplazar grandes costos variables con inversiones de capital menores","B":"Al reemplazar grandes inversiones de capital con costos variables menores","C":"Al permitir el aprovisionamiento de c\xf3mputo y almacenamiento a un nivel fijo para satisfacer la demanda m\xe1xima","D":"Al reemplazar el escalado repetido de servidores virtuales con un modelo de escala fija m\xe1s simple"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Migrar a AWS reemplaza grandes inversiones de capital iniciales en hardware e instalaciones con costos variables menores de pago por uso basados en el consumo real de recursos.\\nReemplazar grandes costos variables con inversiones de capital menores describe lo contrario del modelo de AWS. Los centros de datos tradicionales suelen tener tanto grandes costos de capital iniciales como costos operativos variables continuos, mientras que AWS elimina el componente de capital y lo reemplaza con cargos variables basados en el uso. Permitir el aprovisionamiento de c\xf3mputo y almacenamiento a un nivel fijo para satisfacer la demanda m\xe1xima describe el enfoque local tradicional de comprar la capacidad m\xe1xima prevista por adelantado, que es un modelo del que AWS espec\xedficamente aleja a los clientes. Reemplazar el escalado repetido de servidores virtuales con un modelo de escala fija m\xe1s simple describe un modelo de capacidad est\xe1tica que es lo opuesto al enfoque de AWS, que permite el escalado din\xe1mico en lugar de reemplazarlo por una escala fija."},{"id":"q785","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 principio de dise\xf1o en la nube se alinea con las pr\xe1cticas recomendadas de AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Crear dependencias fijas entre los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n","B":"Agregar servicios en una \xfanica instancia","C":"Implementar aplicaciones en una \xfanica Zona de Disponibilidad","D":"Distribuir la carga de c\xf3mputo entre m\xfaltiples recursos"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Distribuir la carga de c\xf3mputo entre m\xfaltiples recursos sigue la pr\xe1ctica recomendada de escalado horizontal, lo que mejora la tolerancia a fallos y el rendimiento al evitar puntos \xfanicos de fallo.\\nCrear dependencias fijas entre los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n viola el principio de acoplamiento d\xe9bil, que tiene como objetivo reducir las interdependencias para que los fallos no se propaguen en cascada por todo el sistema. Agregar servicios en una \xfanica instancia crea un punto \xfanico de fallo y contradice la pr\xe1ctica recomendada de distribuir las cargas de trabajo entre m\xfaltiples recursos. Implementar en una \xfanica Zona de Disponibilidad expone la aplicaci\xf3n a una interrupci\xf3n si esa AZ falla, lo que contradice la pr\xe1ctica recomendada de dise\xf1ar para alta disponibilidad."},{"id":"q789","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n dise\xf1ada para abarcar m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad se describe como:","options":{"A":"Ser altamente disponible","B":"Tener alcance global","C":"Usar una econom\xeda de escala","D":"Tener elasticidad"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Una aplicaci\xf3n que abarca m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad sigue operativa si una AZ experimenta una interrupci\xf3n, que es la definici\xf3n de alta disponibilidad.\\nEl alcance global describe implementar en m\xfaltiples Regiones o usar una red de entrega de contenido para atender a usuarios de todo el mundo, lo que va m\xe1s all\xe1 de la redundancia a nivel de AZ dentro de una \xfanica Regi\xf3n. La econom\xeda de escala se refiere a los beneficios de costo que AWS traslada a los clientes mediante el poder de compra agregado, que es un concepto de precios no relacionado con la arquitectura de la aplicaci\xf3n. La elasticidad se refiere a escalar recursos din\xe1micamente hacia arriba o hacia abajo en respuesta a la demanda, lo cual es independiente de cu\xe1ntas Zonas de Disponibilidad abarque una aplicaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q790","question":"Un nuevo servicio que utiliza AWS debe tener alta disponibilidad. Sin embargo, debido a requisitos regulatorios, todas sus instancias de Amazon EC2 deben ubicarse en una \xfanica \xe1rea geogr\xe1fica. De acuerdo con las pr\xe1cticas recomendadas, para cumplir estos requisitos, las instancias de EC2 deben colocarse en al menos dos:","options":{"A":"Regiones de AWS","B":"Zonas de Disponibilidad","C":"Subredes","D":"Grupos de ubicaci\xf3n"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las Zonas de Disponibilidad son ubicaciones de centros de datos aisladas dentro de una \xfanica Regi\xf3n de AWS, conectadas mediante enlaces de baja latencia. Implementar instancias de EC2 en al menos dos Zonas de Disponibilidad de la misma Regi\xf3n proporciona alta disponibilidad y cumple con el requisito de restricci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica.\\nLas Regiones de AWS son \xe1reas geogr\xe1ficas separadas, cada una con m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad. Implementar en m\xfaltiples Regiones violar\xeda el requisito de que todas las instancias permanezcan en una \xfanica \xe1rea geogr\xe1fica, por lo que esta opci\xf3n no es conforme. Las subredes son subdivisiones de una VPC y pueden existir dentro de una \xfanica AZ; distribuir instancias entre subredes dentro de la misma AZ no proporciona protecci\xf3n contra un fallo a nivel de AZ. Los grupos de ubicaci\xf3n controlan c\xf3mo se colocan f\xedsicamente las instancias dentro de una AZ para lograr baja latencia o colocaci\xf3n distribuida, pero no proporcionan aislamiento de fallos entre AZ y no son un mecanismo de alta disponibilidad para sobrevivir a interrupciones de AZ."},{"id":"q802","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes son ventajas de AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS gestiona el mantenimiento de la infraestructura en la nube","B":"AWS gestiona la seguridad de las aplicaciones creadas en AWS","C":"AWS gestiona la planificaci\xf3n de capacidad para servidores f\xedsicos","D":"AWS gestiona el desarrollo de aplicaciones en AWS","E":"AWS gestiona la planificaci\xf3n de costos para servidores virtuales"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS gestiona el mantenimiento de la infraestructura en la nube, incluidos el hardware, las redes y las instalaciones, y gestiona la planificaci\xf3n de capacidad para servidores f\xedsicos, lo que libera a los clientes de estas cargas operativas y les permite centrarse en sus aplicaciones y objetivos de negocio.\\nAWS no gestiona la seguridad de las aplicaciones creadas en AWS, ya que la seguridad a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n, incluidas las vulnerabilidades del c\xf3digo, los controles de acceso y la protecci\xf3n de datos, sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida. AWS no gestiona el desarrollo de aplicaciones en AWS, ya que los clientes son los \xfanicos responsables de dise\xf1ar, crear y mantener sus propias aplicaciones independientemente de los servicios de AWS que ejecuten. AWS no gestiona la planificaci\xf3n de costos para servidores virtuales, ya que los clientes son responsables de seleccionar los tipos de instancia correctos, monitorear el uso y optimizar su propio gasto mediante herramientas como Cost Explorer y AWS Budgets."},{"id":"q803","question":"Un usuario implementa una instancia de base de datos de Amazon RDS en varias zonas de disponibilidad. \xbfA qu\xe9 pilar de AWS Well-Architected Framework corresponde esta estrategia?","options":{"A":"Eficiencia del rendimiento","B":"Confiabilidad","C":"Optimizaci\xf3n de costos","D":"Seguridad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Implementar en varias zonas de disponibilidad garantiza que la base de datos pueda resistir la falla de una zona de disponibilidad y recuperarse autom\xe1ticamente, lo que se relaciona directamente con el enfoque del pilar de Confiabilidad en la recuperaci\xf3n y la disponibilidad de las cargas de trabajo.\\nLa Eficiencia del rendimiento se centra en usar los recursos de c\xf3mputo de manera eficiente para cumplir los requisitos de rendimiento, no en la redundancia entre dominios de falla. La Optimizaci\xf3n de costos se centra en eliminar gastos innecesarios y elegir los tipos de recursos adecuados, no en la tolerancia a fallas. La Seguridad se centra en proteger los datos y los sistemas mediante controles de acceso y mecanismos de detecci\xf3n, no en la arquitectura de disponibilidad."},{"id":"q816","question":"Una empresa que hace negocios en l\xednea necesita entregar nueva funcionalidad r\xe1pidamente de forma iterativa, minimizando el tiempo de lanzamiento al mercado. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de AWS Cloud puede proporcionar esto?","options":{"A":"Elasticidad","B":"Alta disponibilidad","C":"Agilidad","D":"Confiabilidad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La agilidad en AWS Cloud permite a las empresas aprovisionar recursos r\xe1pidamente, iterar sobre funcionalidades e implementar cambios con rapidez, minimizando el tiempo de lanzamiento al mercado de nueva funcionalidad.\\nLa elasticidad se refiere a escalar recursos din\xe1micamente hacia arriba o hacia abajo en respuesta a la demanda, lo que aborda la gesti\xf3n de capacidad en lugar de la velocidad de entrega de nueva funcionalidad. La alta disponibilidad se refiere a mantener una aplicaci\xf3n operativa durante fallas mediante redundancia, lo cual es una caracter\xedstica de disponibilidad no relacionada con la velocidad de desarrollo e implementaci\xf3n. La confiabilidad se refiere a la capacidad de una carga de trabajo de realizar su funci\xf3n prevista correctamente y recuperarse de fallas, lo cual es una cualidad arquitect\xf3nica en lugar de una caracter\xedstica que acelera el tiempo de lanzamiento al mercado."},{"id":"q827","question":"Seg\xfan AWS Well-Architected Framework, \xbfqu\xe9 pasos de gesti\xf3n de cambios se deben tomar para lograr confiabilidad en AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Usar AWS Config para generar un inventario de recursos de AWS","B":"Usar l\xedmites de servicio para evitar que los usuarios creen o modifiquen recursos de AWS","C":"Usar AWS CloudTrail para registrar las llamadas a la API de AWS en un archivo de registro auditable","D":"Usar AWS Certificate Manager para incluir en la lista blanca recursos y servicios aprobados de AWS","E":"Usar Amazon GuardDuty para validar los cambios de configuraci\xf3n realizados en los recursos de AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Usar AWS Config para generar un inventario de recursos de AWS proporciona visibilidad sobre qu\xe9 recursos existen y c\xf3mo est\xe1n configurados, lo que respalda la confiabilidad al permitir el seguimiento de cambios, y usar AWS CloudTrail para registrar las llamadas a la API de AWS crea un registro auditable de todos los cambios realizados, lo que permite el an\xe1lisis de causa ra\xedz cuando ocurren fallas. Ambos son pasos de gesti\xf3n de cambios que respaldan la confiabilidad en AWS Cloud.\\nUsar l\xedmites de servicio para evitar que los usuarios creen o modifiquen recursos de AWS bloquear\xeda el escalado necesario y los cambios operativos, contradiciendo el objetivo de confiabilidad de poder adaptarse a la demanda cambiante y recuperarse de fallas r\xe1pidamente. Usar AWS Certificate Manager para incluir en la lista blanca recursos y servicios aprobados no es una descripci\xf3n precisa de lo que hace Certificate Manager; aprovisiona certificados SSL/TLS y no proporciona una capacidad de lista blanca de recursos para la gesti\xf3n de cambios. Usar Amazon GuardDuty para validar cambios de configuraci\xf3n no es lo que hace GuardDuty; detecta amenazas y actividad maliciosa en lugar de validar si las configuraciones de recursos cumplen con los estados deseados, que es la funci\xf3n de AWS Config."},{"id":"q829","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 principio de dise\xf1o se debe considerar al dise\xf1ar arquitecturas en AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Considerar los servidores como recursos no desechables","B":"Usar integraci\xf3n s\xedncrona de servicios","C":"Dise\xf1ar componentes d\xe9bilmente acoplados","D":"Implementar las reglas menos permisivas para los grupos de seguridad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dise\xf1ar componentes d\xe9bilmente acoplados significa que los sistemas se construyen de modo que los componentes individuales puedan fallar o reemplazarse sin causar fallas en cascada, lo cual es un principio de dise\xf1o central de AWS Well-Architected para construir arquitecturas cloud resilientes y mantenibles.\\nConsiderar los servidores como recursos no desechables es lo opuesto a un principio de dise\xf1o de AWS; la nube fomenta tratar la infraestructura como desechable y automatizar los reemplazos, permitiendo que las instancias fallidas se terminen y se lancen nuevas autom\xe1ticamente en lugar de repararlas manualmente. Usar integraci\xf3n s\xedncrona de servicios aumenta el acoplamiento fuerte y significa que una falla en un servicio bloquea a otros que esperan una respuesta; los principios de dise\xf1o de AWS favorecen patrones as\xedncronos y basados en eventos donde los componentes pueden operar de forma independiente sin esperarse entre s\xed. Implementar las reglas menos permisivas para los grupos de seguridad es una buena pr\xe1ctica de seguridad para el control de acceso a la red, pero no es un principio de dise\xf1o de arquitectura cloud; se relaciona con el endurecimiento de la seguridad en lugar del patr\xf3n de arquitectura de sistema m\xe1s amplio descrito por AWS Well-Architected Framework."},{"id":"q831","question":"Una carga de trabajo por lotes tarda 5 horas en finalizar en una instancia de Amazon EC2. La cantidad de datos a procesar se duplica mensualmente y el tiempo de procesamiento es proporcional. \xbfCu\xe1l es la mejor arquitectura cloud para abordar esta demanda en crecimiento constante?","options":{"A":"Ejecutar la aplicaci\xf3n en un tama\xf1o de instancia EC2 m\xe1s grande","B":"Cambiar a una familia de instancias EC2 que se ajuste mejor a los requisitos de procesamiento por lotes","C":"Distribuir la aplicaci\xf3n entre varias instancias EC2 y ejecutar la carga de trabajo en paralelo","D":"Ejecutar la aplicaci\xf3n en una instancia EC2 bare metal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Distribuir la carga de trabajo entre varias instancias EC2 y ejecutarla en paralelo aplica escalado horizontal, que maneja la demanda en crecimiento constante agregando m\xe1s m\xe1quinas en lugar de depender de una \xfanica instancia m\xe1s grande que eventualmente alcanzar\xe1 l\xedmites de capacidad.\\nEjecutar la aplicaci\xf3n en una instancia EC2 m\xe1s grande aplica escalado vertical, que tiene un l\xedmite superior de tama\xf1o y crea un punto \xfanico de falla, lo que lo hace inadecuado para cargas de trabajo que se duplican constantemente con el tiempo. Cambiar a una familia de instancias EC2 diferente optimiza seg\xfan las caracter\xedsticas de la carga de trabajo, como la intensidad de c\xf3mputo o memoria, pero no aborda el problema del volumen de procesamiento en crecimiento constante. Ejecutar en una instancia EC2 bare metal proporciona acceso directo al hardware f\xedsico sin una capa de hipervisor, lo que puede mejorar el rendimiento para ciertas cargas de trabajo, pero no escala para manejar vol\xfamenes de datos que se duplican."},{"id":"q833","question":"La capacidad de escalar horizontalmente instancias de Amazon EC2 seg\xfan la demanda es un ejemplo de \xbfqu\xe9 concepto en la propuesta de valor de AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Econom\xeda de escala","B":"Elasticidad","C":"Alta disponibilidad","D":"Agilidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Escalar horizontalmente instancias EC2 seg\xfan la demanda es elasticidad, la capacidad de agregar o quitar recursos de c\xf3mputo autom\xe1ticamente para coincidir con los requisitos de la carga de trabajo en tiempo real.\\nLa econom\xeda de escala se refiere a las reducciones de costos que AWS traslada a los clientes mediante el poder de compra agregado de millones de usuarios, lo cual es un beneficio de precios en lugar de un concepto de gesti\xf3n de capacidad. La alta disponibilidad se refiere a mantener una aplicaci\xf3n operativa durante fallas mediante redundancia en varias ubicaciones, lo cual no est\xe1 relacionado con ajustar din\xe1micamente el n\xfamero de instancias. La agilidad se refiere a la velocidad con la que los clientes pueden aprovisionar recursos e iterar sobre nueva funcionalidad, lo que aborda el tiempo de lanzamiento al mercado en lugar del ajuste din\xe1mico de capacidad."},{"id":"q840","question":"Los sistemas de TI deben dise\xf1arse para reducir las interdependencias, de modo que un cambio o falla en un componente no se propague en cascada a otros componentes. \xbfEsto es un ejemplo de qu\xe9 principio de dise\xf1o de arquitectura cloud?","options":{"A":"Escalabilidad","B":"Acoplamiento d\xe9bil","C":"Automatizaci\xf3n","D":"Escalado autom\xe1tico"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El acoplamiento d\xe9bil reduce las interdependencias entre componentes para que un cambio o falla en uno no se propague en cascada a otros, mejorando la resiliencia del sistema mediante componentes independientes y aislados.\\nLa escalabilidad se refiere a la capacidad de manejar aumentos de carga agregando recursos, no a reducir las interdependencias entre componentes. La automatizaci\xf3n se refiere a reducir la intervenci\xf3n manual mediante procesos automatizados o gestionados, no a la independencia arquitect\xf3nica entre componentes. El escalado autom\xe1tico se refiere a ajustar din\xe1micamente la capacidad de recursos en respuesta a la demanda, no a aislar componentes entre s\xed."},{"id":"q844","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el beneficio de la elasticidad en AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Garantizar que el tr\xe1fico web se distribuya autom\xe1ticamente entre varias regiones de AWS","B":"Minimizar los costos de almacenamiento archivando autom\xe1ticamente los datos de registro","C":"Permitir que AWS seleccione autom\xe1ticamente los servicios m\xe1s rentables","D":"Ajustar autom\xe1ticamente la capacidad de c\xf3mputo requerida para mantener un rendimiento constante"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La elasticidad ajusta autom\xe1ticamente la capacidad de c\xf3mputo en respuesta a la demanda cambiante, escalando hacia afuera durante los picos de tr\xe1fico y escalando hacia adentro durante los per\xedodos de baja actividad para mantener un rendimiento constante sin intervenci\xf3n manual.\\nDistribuir el tr\xe1fico web entre varias regiones describe el balanceo de carga global o el enrutamiento DNS, que es una capacidad separada no relacionada con el ajuste din\xe1mico de la capacidad de c\xf3mputo. Archivar autom\xe1ticamente los datos de registro para minimizar los costos de almacenamiento describe una funci\xf3n de gesti\xf3n del ciclo de vida de los datos, no una caracter\xedstica de la elasticidad. Seleccionar autom\xe1ticamente los servicios m\xe1s rentables no es una caracter\xedstica que AWS proporcione en nombre de los clientes, ya que la selecci\xf3n de servicios sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente."},{"id":"q845","question":"La reducci\xf3n continua de los precios de AWS Cloud se debe a:","options":{"A":"Precios de pago por uso","B":"La infraestructura global de AWS","C":"Econom\xedas de escala","D":"Precios de almacenamiento reservado"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A medida que AWS crece y atiende a m\xe1s clientes, logra costos unitarios m\xe1s bajos mediante econom\xedas de escala y traslada esos ahorros a los clientes a trav\xe9s de reducciones de precios peri\xf3dicas.\\nEl pago por uso es un modelo de facturaci\xf3n que cobra por el uso real en lugar de compromisos por adelantado, pero no hace que los precios disminuyan con el tiempo. La infraestructura global de AWS describe la huella f\xedsica de Regiones, AZs y ubicaciones de borde, que respalda la entrega de servicios pero no es el mecanismo detr\xe1s de las reducciones continuas de precios. Los precios de almacenamiento reservado son una opci\xf3n de descuento basada en compromiso para servicios espec\xedficos, no un impulsor de las reducciones generales de precios de AWS en toda la plataforma."},{"id":"q853","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 buena pr\xe1ctica de AWS Cloud utiliza la elasticidad y la agilidad de la computaci\xf3n en la nube?","options":{"A":"Aprovisionar capacidad seg\xfan el uso pasado y los picos te\xf3ricos","B":"Escalar de forma din\xe1mica y predictiva para satisfacer las demandas de uso","C":"Construir la aplicaci\xf3n y la infraestructura en un centro de datos que otorgue acceso f\xedsico","D":"Dividir la aplicaci\xf3n en componentes d\xe9bilmente acoplados"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Escalar de forma din\xe1mica y predictiva para satisfacer las demandas de uso aprovecha tanto la elasticidad mediante el escalado autom\xe1tico como la agilidad mediante la adaptaci\xf3n r\xe1pida, que son ventajas fundamentales de la computaci\xf3n en la nube.\\nAprovisionar capacidad seg\xfan el uso pasado y los picos te\xf3ricos es un enfoque local que genera sobreaprovisionamiento y recursos inactivos, que la adopci\xf3n de la nube est\xe1 dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para eliminar. Construir aplicaciones en un centro de datos que otorgue acceso f\xedsico describe un modelo local o de colocation y contradice el principio de la nube de abstraer la gesti\xf3n de la infraestructura f\xedsica. Dividir una aplicaci\xf3n en componentes d\xe9bilmente acoplados es una buena pr\xe1ctica de arquitectura que mejora la resiliencia y el mantenimiento, pero describe el principio de dise\xf1o de acoplamiento d\xe9bil en lugar del uso de la elasticidad y la agilidad."},{"id":"q854","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9todo ayuda a optimizar los costos de los usuarios que migran a AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Pagar solo por lo que se usa","B":"Comprar hardware antes de que sea necesario","C":"Aprovisionar manualmente los recursos en la nube","D":"Comprar para la carga m\xe1xima posible"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El modelo de pago por uso optimiza los costos al garantizar que solo se pague por los recursos que realmente se consumen, eliminando el desperdicio por sobreaprovisionamiento o por comprar hardware antes de la necesidad real.\\nComprar hardware antes de que sea necesario describe el modelo tradicional de gasto de capital de adquirir infraestructura antes de la demanda, lo que genera sobreaprovisionamiento y gasto desperdiciado en lugar de optimizar costos. Aprovisionar manualmente los recursos en la nube introduce esfuerzo humano y posibles demoras en la respuesta a los cambios de demanda, y no optimiza los costos en comparaci\xf3n con el escalado automatizado que ajusta la capacidad con precisi\xf3n al uso real. Comprar para la carga m\xe1xima posible significa sobreaprovisionar infraestructura para manejar el escenario de demanda m\xe1s desfavorable, lo que maximiza los costos al pagar por capacidad que permanece inactiva durante los per\xedodos de uso normal en lugar de escalar seg\xfan la demanda real."},{"id":"q861","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es uno de los principios fundamentales que se deben seguir al dise\xf1ar una aplicaci\xf3n de alta disponibilidad en AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Dise\xf1ar usando una arquitectura sin servidor","B":"Suponer que todos los componentes dentro de una aplicaci\xf3n pueden fallar","C":"Dise\xf1ar AWS Auto Scaling en cada aplicaci\xf3n","D":"Dise\xf1ar todos los componentes usando c\xf3digo de c\xf3digo abierto"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Suponer que todos los componentes pueden fallar es un principio fundamental del dise\xf1o de alta disponibilidad, ya que impulsa a los arquitectos a incorporar redundancia, verificaciones de estado y conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica en cada capa de una aplicaci\xf3n en lugar de depender de que alg\xfan componente individual permanezca disponible.\\nDise\xf1ar usando una arquitectura sin servidor es un enfoque v\xe1lido para algunas cargas de trabajo, pero no es un principio universal para la alta disponibilidad, ya que las arquitecturas basadas en servidores tambi\xe9n pueden lograr alta disponibilidad mediante redundancia y conmutaci\xf3n por error. Dise\xf1ar Auto Scaling en cada aplicaci\xf3n es una buena pr\xe1ctica para la escalabilidad, pero no es un requisito universal para la alta disponibilidad, ya que algunas aplicaciones logran disponibilidad mediante redundancia en lugar de escalado. Usar c\xf3digo de c\xf3digo abierto es una elecci\xf3n de desarrollo de software que no influye en si una aplicaci\xf3n est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para permanecer disponible cuando los componentes fallan."},{"id":"q867","question":"Un Cloud Practitioner est\xe1 desarrollando un plan de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres y tiene la intenci\xf3n de replicar datos entre m\xfaltiples \xe1reas geogr\xe1ficas. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes cumple estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"AWS Accounts","B":"AWS Regions","C":"Availability Zones","D":"Ubicaciones de borde"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Regions son \xe1reas geogr\xe1ficamente separadas en todo el mundo, lo que las convierte en la opci\xf3n correcta para replicar datos entre diferentes ubicaciones f\xedsicas para satisfacer los requisitos de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres.\\nAWS Accounts son un l\xedmite l\xf3gico para gestionar recursos y facturaci\xf3n, no un componente de infraestructura geogr\xe1fica. Availability Zones son cl\xfasteres de centros de datos aislados dentro de una sola Region, por lo que la replicaci\xf3n entre AZs no logra una separaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica entre diferentes \xe1reas del mundo. Las ubicaciones de borde son puntos de presencia utilizados para la entrega y el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de contenido, no est\xe1n dise\xf1adas para la replicaci\xf3n de datos ni para fines de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres."},{"id":"q875","question":"Una empresa quiere usar Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) para implementar una aplicaci\xf3n comercial global. La soluci\xf3n de implementaci\xf3n debe construirse con la m\xe1xima redundancia y tolerancia a fallos. Seg\xfan esta situaci\xf3n, las instancias de Amazon EC2 deben implementarse:","options":{"A":"En una sola Availability Zone en una AWS Region","B":"Con m\xfaltiples Elastic Network Interfaces pertenecientes a subredes diferentes","C":"En m\xfaltiples Availability Zones en una AWS Region","D":"En m\xfaltiples Availability Zones en dos AWS Regions"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Implementar en m\xfaltiples Availability Zones en dos AWS Regions proporciona protecci\xf3n contra fallas tanto a nivel de AZ como a nivel de Region, logrando el mayor nivel de redundancia y tolerancia a fallos disponible.\\nImplementar en una sola Availability Zone en una Region crea un \xfanico punto de falla tanto a nivel de AZ como de Region, sin proporcionar redundancia contra interrupciones. Usar m\xfaltiples Elastic Network Interfaces en subredes diferentes mejora la flexibilidad de configuraci\xf3n de red dentro de una instancia, pero no proporciona redundancia contra fallas de c\xf3mputo o de infraestructura. Implementar en m\xfaltiples AZs en una sola Region protege contra fallas a nivel de AZ, pero deja la aplicaci\xf3n vulnerable a una interrupci\xf3n completa de la Region, lo que no cumple con el requisito de m\xe1xima redundancia."},{"id":"q886","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 es una Availability Zone en AWS?","options":{"A":"Uno o m\xe1s centros de datos f\xedsicos","B":"Una ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica completamente aislada","C":"Una o m\xe1s ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo","D":"Una ubicaci\xf3n de centro de datos con una \xfanica fuente de energ\xeda y red"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Una Availability Zone consta de uno o m\xe1s centros de datos f\xedsicos, cada uno con energ\xeda, refrigeraci\xf3n y red independientes, dise\xf1ados para que una falla en una AZ no afecte a las dem\xe1s en la misma Region.\\nUna ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica completamente aislada describe una AWS Region, no una Availability Zone; las Regions son las grandes \xe1reas geogr\xe1ficas y las AZs son las instalaciones aisladas dentro de una Region. Las ubicaciones de borde son Puntos de Presencia utilizados por servicios como CloudFront para almacenar contenido en cach\xe9 cerca de los usuarios; no son Availability Zones y no tienen relaci\xf3n de alojamiento con instancias de EC2 ni con los servicios principales de AWS. Una descripci\xf3n de una \xfanica fuente de energ\xeda y red es lo opuesto a lo que proporciona una AZ; las AZs est\xe1n dise\xf1adas espec\xedficamente con energ\xeda y red redundantes para protegerse contra puntos \xfanicos de falla."},{"id":"q897","question":"Una empresa ha distribuido su carga de trabajo tanto en AWS Cloud como en algunos servidores locales. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de arquitectura es esta?","options":{"A":"Red privada virtual","B":"Nube privada virtual","C":"Nube h\xedbrida","D":"Nube privada"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Una arquitectura de nube h\xedbrida distribuye cargas de trabajo entre servidores locales y AWS Cloud, combinando la infraestructura de centro de datos existente con recursos en la nube conectados mediante VPN o Direct Connect.\\nUna red privada virtual es una tecnolog\xeda de conectividad que crea t\xfaneles cifrados entre redes y no es en s\xed misma un tipo de arquitectura que describa c\xf3mo se distribuyen las cargas de trabajo entre entornos en la nube y locales. Una nube privada virtual es un entorno de red l\xf3gicamente aislado dentro de AWS y describe una construcci\xf3n de red en lugar de un modelo de implementaci\xf3n que abarca tanto la infraestructura en la nube como la local. Una nube privada es un modelo de implementaci\xf3n en el que toda la infraestructura est\xe1 dedicada a una sola organizaci\xf3n y se aloja localmente o por un tercero, sin ning\xfan componente de nube p\xfablica compartida."},{"id":"q913","question":"La tolerancia a fallos se refiere a:","options":{"A":"La capacidad de una aplicaci\xf3n de acomodar el crecimiento sin cambiar el dise\xf1o","B":"Qu\xe9 tan bien y qu\xe9 tan r\xe1pido se pueden restaurar los datos perdidos en el entorno de una aplicaci\xf3n","C":"Qu\xe9 tan segura es tu aplicaci\xf3n","D":"La redundancia integrada de los componentes de una aplicaci\xf3n"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La tolerancia a fallos se refiere a la redundancia integrada de los componentes de una aplicaci\xf3n, lo que garantiza que el sistema siga funcionando correctamente incluso cuando fallan componentes individuales.\\nLa capacidad de acomodar el crecimiento sin cambiar el dise\xf1o describe la escalabilidad, que es un concepto de nube separado centrado en manejar una mayor demanda. Qu\xe9 tan bien y qu\xe9 tan r\xe1pido se pueden restaurar los datos perdidos describe la recuperabilidad, que se relaciona con copias de seguridad y recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres, no con la operaci\xf3n continua durante fallos. Qu\xe9 tan segura es una aplicaci\xf3n describe la postura de seguridad de un sistema, que es un asunto completamente separado de la redundancia y la tolerancia a fallos."},{"id":"q927","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l principio de arquitectura de nube de AWS establece que los sistemas deben reducir las interdependencias?","options":{"A":"Escalabilidad","B":"Servicios, no servidores","C":"Eliminaci\xf3n de puntos \xfanicos de fallo","D":"Acoplamiento d\xe9bil"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El acoplamiento d\xe9bil es el principio de que los sistemas deben reducir las interdependencias entre componentes, de modo que los cambios o fallos en uno no se propaguen en cascada a los dem\xe1s.\\nLa escalabilidad se refiere a la capacidad de manejar aumentos de carga agregando recursos, no a reducir interdependencias. Servicios, no servidores es el principio de usar servicios administrados de AWS en lugar de ejecutar tu propia infraestructura. La eliminaci\xf3n de puntos \xfanicos de fallo es un principio de confiabilidad centrado en la redundancia, no en reducir las interdependencias entre componentes."},{"id":"q935","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 beneficio de AWS se refiere a la capacidad de un cliente de implementar aplicaciones que escalan hacia arriba y hacia abajo para satisfacer una demanda variable?","options":{"A":"Elasticidad","B":"Agilidad","C":"Seguridad","D":"Escalabilidad"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La elasticidad se refiere a la capacidad de escalar recursos autom\xe1ticamente tanto hacia arriba como hacia abajo para coincidir con una demanda variable, lo que garantiza que la capacidad se ajuste en ambas direcciones sin intervenci\xf3n manual.\\nLa agilidad se refiere a la velocidad con la que los clientes pueden aprovisionar recursos e iterar sobre nueva funcionalidad, lo que aborda el tiempo de comercializaci\xf3n en lugar del ajuste din\xe1mico de capacidad. La seguridad se refiere a proteger datos, sistemas y activos mediante controles y estrategias de mitigaci\xf3n de riesgos, y no est\xe1 relacionada con escalar recursos en respuesta a la demanda. La escalabilidad se refiere a la capacidad de un sistema de manejar una mayor carga aumentando la capacidad, pero, a diferencia de la elasticidad, no incluye inherentemente el escalado autom\xe1tico hacia abajo cuando disminuye la demanda."},{"id":"q937","question":"Una empresa quiere configurar una carga de trabajo de alta disponibilidad en AWS con un plan de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres que le permita recuperarse en caso de una interrupci\xf3n regional del servicio. \xbfQu\xe9 configuraci\xf3n cumplir\xe1 estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Ejecutar en dos Zonas de disponibilidad en una Regi\xf3n de AWS, usando las Zonas de disponibilidad adicionales en la Regi\xf3n de AWS para el sitio de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres","B":"Ejecutar en dos Zonas de disponibilidad en una Regi\xf3n de AWS, usando otra Regi\xf3n de AWS para el sitio de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres","C":"Ejecutar en dos Zonas de disponibilidad en una Regi\xf3n de AWS, usando una Regi\xf3n de AWS local para el sitio de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres","D":"Ejecutar en dos Regiones de AWS, usando una tercera Regi\xf3n de AWS para el sitio de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ejecutar en dos AZ en una Regi\xf3n proporciona alta disponibilidad dentro de la Regi\xf3n, y usar una Regi\xf3n separada como sitio de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres protege contra una interrupci\xf3n regional completa del servicio mediante redundancia geogr\xe1fica.\\nUsar AZ adicionales dentro de la misma Regi\xf3n como sitio de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres no protege contra una interrupci\xf3n regional, ya que todas las AZ de una Regi\xf3n se ver\xedan afectadas por la misma interrupci\xf3n regional. Una Regi\xf3n de AWS local se refiere a una Regi\xf3n de alcance limitado dise\xf1ada para requisitos de residencia de datos y normalmente no ofrece la disponibilidad de servicios completa necesaria para servir como un sitio de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres completo. Ejecutar en dos Regiones con una tercera como sitio de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres introduce complejidad y costo innecesarios, ya que dos Regiones ya proporcionan la separaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica necesaria para alta disponibilidad y conmutaci\xf3n por error regional."},{"id":"q947","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l pilar del AWS Well-Architected Framework respalda el principio de dise\xf1o de realizar operaciones como c\xf3digo?","options":{"A":"Eficiencia de rendimiento","B":"Excelencia operativa","C":"Confiabilidad","D":"Seguridad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Realizar operaciones como c\xf3digo es un principio de dise\xf1o expl\xedcito del pilar de Excelencia operativa, que se centra en ejecutar y mejorar las operaciones automatizando procesos y respondiendo a eventos mediante programaci\xf3n.\\nLa eficiencia de rendimiento se centra en usar los recursos de c\xf3mputo de manera eficiente para cumplir los requisitos del sistema, no en automatizar procesos operativos. La confiabilidad se centra en garantizar que una carga de trabajo realice su funci\xf3n prevista de manera correcta y consistente, con principios de dise\xf1o en torno a la recuperaci\xf3n y la redundancia. La seguridad se centra en proteger datos, sistemas y activos mediante controles y mecanismos de detecci\xf3n."},{"id":"q948","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 principio de dise\xf1o se logra al seguir el pilar de confiabilidad del AWS Well-Architected Framework?","options":{"A":"Escalado vertical","B":"Recuperaci\xf3n manual ante fallos","C":"Probar procedimientos de recuperaci\xf3n","D":"Cambiar la infraestructura manualmente"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Probar los procedimientos de recuperaci\xf3n es un principio de dise\xf1o central del pilar de Confiabilidad, que garantiza que las cargas de trabajo puedan recuperarse de fallos validando regularmente los procesos de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres y de copias de seguridad.\\nEl escalado vertical es un concepto de rendimiento y capacidad asociado con el pilar de Eficiencia de rendimiento, no un principio de dise\xf1o del pilar de Confiabilidad. La recuperaci\xf3n manual ante fallos contradice el principio del pilar de Confiabilidad de automatizar la recuperaci\xf3n ante fallos, ya que los procesos manuales introducen retrasos y error humano durante las interrupciones. Cambiar la infraestructura manualmente contradice el principio del pilar de Confiabilidad de gestionar el cambio mediante automatizaci\xf3n para reducir el riesgo de introducir fallos."},{"id":"q955","question":"Un usuario tiene recursos locales infrautilizados. \xbfQu\xe9 concepto de AWS Cloud puede abordar MEJOR este problema?","options":{"A":"Alta disponibilidad","B":"Elasticidad","C":"Seguridad","D":"Acoplamiento d\xe9bil"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La elasticidad te permite escalar recursos hacia arriba o hacia abajo seg\xfan la demanda real, abordando directamente la infrautilizaci\xf3n al garantizar que solo pagues por lo que usas en lugar de mantener capacidad inactiva.\\nLa alta disponibilidad garantiza que una aplicaci\xf3n siga operativa durante fallos mediante redundancia, pero no aborda el problema de recursos aprovisionados en exceso o infrautilizados. La seguridad se refiere a proteger datos, sistemas y activos mediante controles y mitigaci\xf3n de riesgos, lo cual no est\xe1 relacionado con la utilizaci\xf3n de recursos. El acoplamiento d\xe9bil es un principio arquitect\xf3nico que reduce las dependencias entre componentes para limitar el radio de impacto de los fallos, y no aborda la capacidad inactiva o infrautilizada."},{"id":"q981","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son las ventajas de implementar una aplicaci\xf3n con instancias de Amazon EC2 en varias Zonas de disponibilidad?","options":{"A":"Prevenir un punto \xfanico de fallo","B":"Reducir los costos operativos de la aplicaci\xf3n","C":"Permitir que la aplicaci\xf3n atienda a usuarios entre regiones con baja latencia","D":"Aumentar la disponibilidad de la aplicaci\xf3n","E":"Aumentar la carga de la aplicaci\xf3n"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Implementar en varias Zonas de disponibilidad elimina puntos \xfanicos de fallo al garantizar que la aplicaci\xf3n siga ejecut\xe1ndose si una AZ deja de funcionar, lo que aumenta directamente la disponibilidad general.\\nReducir los costos operativos no es un beneficio directo del despliegue en varias AZ y, de hecho, puede aumentar ligeramente los costos debido a la ejecuci\xf3n de recursos en m\xe1s ubicaciones. Atender a usuarios entre regiones con baja latencia requiere implementar en varias Regiones o usar una red de entrega de contenido, no distribuir instancias entre AZ dentro de una sola Regi\xf3n. Aumentar la carga de la aplicaci\xf3n describe agregar m\xe1s demanda, lo cual no es un beneficio de ning\xfan patr\xf3n arquitect\xf3nico."},{"id":"q995","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 principios de dise\xf1o de AWS Cloud pueden ayudar a aumentar la confiabilidad?","options":{"A":"Usar una arquitectura monol\xedtica","B":"Medir la eficiencia general","C":"Probar los procedimientos de recuperaci\xf3n","D":"Adoptar un modelo de consumo","E":"Recuperarse autom\xe1ticamente de fallos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Probar los procedimientos de recuperaci\xf3n valida que los planes de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres funcionen correctamente, y recuperarse autom\xe1ticamente de fallos mediante comprobaciones de estado y escalado autom\xe1tico garantiza que los sistemas se autorreparen sin intervenci\xf3n manual; ambos son principios de dise\xf1o fundamentales del pilar de Confiabilidad. La arquitectura monol\xedtica acopla estrechamente todos los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n, lo que significa que un fallo en una parte puede derribar todo el sistema, lo que reduce en lugar de aumentar la confiabilidad. Medir la eficiencia general es un principio de dise\xf1o asociado con el pilar de Eficiencia del rendimiento, no con el pilar de Confiabilidad. Adoptar un modelo de consumo es un principio de gesti\xf3n de costos asociado con el pilar de Optimizaci\xf3n de costos, centrado en pagar solo por lo que se usa en lugar de en la resiliencia del sistema."},{"id":"q1007","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 beneficio de la computaci\xf3n en la nube demuestra AWS con su capacidad de ofrecer costos variables m\xe1s bajos como resultado de altos vol\xfamenes de compra?","options":{"A":"Precios de pago por uso","B":"Alta disponibilidad","C":"Alcance global","D":"Econom\xedas de escala"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las econom\xedas de escala permiten a AWS reducir los costos variables para los clientes al agregar la demanda de millones de usuarios, lo que le da a AWS un poder de compra mucho mayor que el que cualquier organizaci\xf3n individual podr\xeda lograr por s\xed sola. El modelo de precios de pago por uso es un modelo de facturaci\xf3n que cobra a los clientes solo por lo que consumen, lo cual es un beneficio de la nube separado y no relacionado con el volumen de compra que reduce los costos. La alta disponibilidad se refiere a dise\xf1ar sistemas para que permanezcan operativos durante fallos, lo cual es un beneficio arquitect\xf3nico en lugar de un beneficio de costos derivado del poder de compra. El alcance global se refiere a la capacidad de implementar infraestructura en m\xfaltiples ubicaciones geogr\xe1ficas en todo el mundo, lo cual es un beneficio de infraestructura en lugar de uno econ\xf3mico."},{"id":"q1034","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 pilar del AWS Well-Architected Framework se centra en la capacidad de una carga de trabajo para realizar su funci\xf3n prevista de forma correcta y consistente?","options":{"A":"Seguridad","B":"Confiabilidad","C":"Eficiencia del rendimiento","D":"Excelencia operativa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El pilar de Confiabilidad se centra en la capacidad de una carga de trabajo para realizar su funci\xf3n prevista de forma correcta y consistente, incluida la capacidad de recuperarse de fallos y cumplir los requisitos de disponibilidad a lo largo del tiempo. Seguridad se centra en proteger datos, sistemas y activos mediante controles de acceso, cifrado y mecanismos de detecci\xf3n. Eficiencia del rendimiento se centra en usar los recursos de computaci\xf3n de forma eficiente para satisfacer los requisitos de la carga de trabajo a medida que cambia la demanda. Excelencia operativa se centra en ejecutar y monitorear sistemas para entregar valor empresarial y mejorar continuamente los procesos y procedimientos."},{"id":"q1035","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 pilar del AWS Well-Architected Framework incluye la capacidad de ejecutar cargas de trabajo de forma eficaz, obtener informaci\xf3n sobre sus operaciones y mejorar continuamente los procesos de soporte?","options":{"A":"Excelencia operativa","B":"Confiabilidad","C":"Eficiencia del rendimiento","D":"Optimizaci\xf3n de costos"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El pilar de Excelencia operativa abarca la capacidad de ejecutar cargas de trabajo de forma eficaz, obtener informaci\xf3n sobre las operaciones mediante monitoreo y observaci\xf3n, y mejorar continuamente los procesos y procedimientos de soporte. Confiabilidad se centra en garantizar que una carga de trabajo realice su funci\xf3n prevista correctamente y se recupere autom\xe1ticamente de los fallos, no en los procesos operativos y la mejora continua. Eficiencia del rendimiento se centra en usar los recursos de computaci\xf3n de forma eficiente para satisfacer los requisitos de la carga de trabajo a medida que cambia la demanda a lo largo del tiempo. Optimizaci\xf3n de costos se centra en eliminar gastos innecesarios y seleccionar los tipos de recursos adecuados para reducir el gasto general."},{"id":"q1036","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 pilar del AWS Well-Architected Framework se centra en seleccionar los tipos y tama\xf1os de recursos adecuados seg\xfan los requisitos de la carga de trabajo?","options":{"A":"Confiabilidad","B":"Optimizaci\xf3n de costos","C":"Eficiencia del rendimiento","D":"Sostenibilidad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El pilar de Eficiencia del rendimiento se centra en usar los recursos de computaci\xf3n de forma eficiente para satisfacer los requisitos de la carga de trabajo, incluida la selecci\xf3n de los tipos y tama\xf1os de recursos adecuados y el mantenimiento de la eficiencia a medida que cambia la demanda a lo largo del tiempo. Confiabilidad se centra en garantizar que una carga de trabajo realice su funci\xf3n prevista correctamente y se recupere de los fallos, no en la selecci\xf3n de recursos. Optimizaci\xf3n de costos se centra en eliminar el gasto innecesario y reducir los costos generales, lo cual se superpone con el ajuste del tama\xf1o correcto (right-sizing), pero no es el pilar que lo define espec\xedficamente como principio de dise\xf1o. Sostenibilidad se centra en reducir el impacto ambiental de las cargas de trabajo en la nube minimizando el consumo de energ\xeda y maximizando la utilizaci\xf3n de los recursos."},{"id":"q1037","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 pilar del AWS Well-Architected Framework se centra en minimizar los impactos ambientales de ejecutar cargas de trabajo en la nube?","options":{"A":"Optimizaci\xf3n de costos","B":"Excelencia operativa","C":"Eficiencia del rendimiento","D":"Sostenibilidad"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El pilar de Sostenibilidad se centra en minimizar los impactos ambientales de ejecutar cargas de trabajo en la nube, incluido comprender los impactos del uso, maximizar la utilizaci\xf3n y adoptar servicios administrados para reducir los recursos necesarios. Optimizaci\xf3n de costos se centra en eliminar el gasto innecesario y lograr eficiencia financiera, no en reducir el impacto ambiental. Excelencia operativa se centra en ejecutar cargas de trabajo de forma eficaz, monitorear las operaciones y mejorar continuamente los procesos y procedimientos. Eficiencia del rendimiento se centra en usar los recursos de computaci\xf3n de forma eficiente para satisfacer los requisitos de la carga de trabajo a medida que cambia la demanda, lo que aborda el uso t\xe9cnico de recursos en lugar de los resultados ambientales."},{"id":"q1038","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 pilar del AWS Well-Architected Framework se centra en proteger datos, sistemas y activos mediante evaluaciones de riesgos y estrategias de mitigaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Excelencia operativa","B":"Confiabilidad","C":"Seguridad","D":"Eficiencia del rendimiento"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El pilar de Seguridad se centra en proteger datos, sistemas y activos mediante evaluaciones de riesgos, estrategias de mitigaci\xf3n y controles que abarcan la confidencialidad, la integridad y la gesti\xf3n de identidades. Excelencia operativa se centra en ejecutar cargas de trabajo de forma eficaz, monitorear las operaciones y mejorar continuamente los procesos y procedimientos. Confiabilidad se centra en garantizar que una carga de trabajo realice su funci\xf3n prevista correctamente y se recupere autom\xe1ticamente de los fallos. Eficiencia del rendimiento se centra en usar los recursos de computaci\xf3n de forma eficiente para satisfacer los requisitos de la carga de trabajo a medida que cambia la demanda a lo largo del tiempo."},{"id":"q1039","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 pilar del AWS Well-Architected Framework se centra en evitar costos innecesarios y usar los recursos m\xe1s rentables?","options":{"A":"Optimizaci\xf3n de costos","B":"Sostenibilidad","C":"Excelencia operativa","D":"Eficiencia del rendimiento"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El pilar de Optimizaci\xf3n de costos se centra en evitar costos innecesarios, seleccionar los tipos de recursos m\xe1s adecuados y escalar sin gastar de m\xe1s. Sostenibilidad se centra en reducir el impacto ambiental de las cargas de trabajo en la nube, como minimizar el consumo de energ\xeda. Excelencia operativa se centra en ejecutar y monitorear sistemas para entregar valor empresarial y mejorar los procesos. Eficiencia del rendimiento se centra en usar los recursos de computaci\xf3n de forma eficiente para cumplir los requisitos de rendimiento."},{"id":"q1060","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les DOS de las siguientes son perspectivas del AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)?","options":{"A":"Gobernanza","B":"Financiera","C":"Personas","D":"Infraestructura","E":"Agilidad"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"El AWS CAF organiza la gu\xeda en seis perspectivas: Negocio, Personas, Gobernanza, Plataforma, Seguridad y Operaciones. Gobernanza se centra en orquestar las iniciativas de nube mientras maximiza los beneficios organizacionales y minimiza los riesgos relacionados con la transformaci\xf3n, y Personas se centra en desarrollar una estrategia de gesti\xf3n del cambio para toda la organizaci\xf3n y en desarrollar habilidades de nube en toda la fuerza laboral.\\nFinanciera no es una de las seis perspectivas del AWS CAF; la gu\xeda financiera y relacionada con costos se incluye dentro de la perspectiva de Negocio, que aborda c\xf3mo la adopci\xf3n de la nube impulsa el valor empresarial y el retorno de la inversi\xf3n en lugar de existir como su propia perspectiva financiera separada. Infraestructura no es una de las seis perspectivas del AWS CAF; la gu\xeda sobre c\xf3mo construir y aprovisionar la base tecnol\xf3gica para cargas de trabajo en la nube se incluye en la perspectiva de Plataforma en lugar de una perspectiva llamada Infraestructura. Agilidad es un beneficio y una caracter\xedstica de la computaci\xf3n en la nube, pero no es una de las seis perspectivas del CAF; no aparece en la estructura del marco CAF junto con Negocio, Personas, Gobernanza, Plataforma, Seguridad y Operaciones."},{"id":"q1061","question":"Seg\xfan el AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF), \xbfcu\xe1les DOS de los siguientes son beneficios que las organizaciones pueden lograr mediante una adopci\xf3n exitosa de la nube?","options":{"A":"Reducci\xf3n del riesgo empresarial","B":"Eliminaci\xf3n de todos los gastos operativos","C":"Mejora del desempe\xf1o ambiental, social y de gobernanza (ESG)","D":"Cumplimiento inmediato de todos los requisitos regulatorios","E":"Eliminaci\xf3n garantizada de todas las vulnerabilidades de seguridad"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"El AWS CAF identifica cuatro beneficios clave de la adopci\xf3n de la nube: reducci\xf3n del riesgo empresarial mediante una mejor confiabilidad, mayor rendimiento y una postura de seguridad mejorada; mejora del desempe\xf1o ESG mediante una menor huella de carbono y una mayor eficiencia energ\xe9tica habilitadas por la infraestructura de nube; aumento de los ingresos mediante la capacidad de desarrollar y entregar nuevos productos m\xe1s r\xe1pido; y aumento de la eficiencia operativa mediante estructuras de costos optimizadas y productividad de la fuerza laboral.\\nLa eliminaci\xf3n de todos los gastos operativos no es un beneficio del AWS CAF; migrar a la nube convierte un gran gasto de capital inicial en gasto operativo variable en lugar de eliminar los costos continuos, ya que los clientes siguen pagando por los servicios de nube seg\xfan el consumo real y los costos operativos, como personal y herramientas, permanecen. El cumplimiento inmediato de todos los requisitos regulatorios no es un beneficio que prometa el CAF; si bien AWS proporciona certificaciones y herramientas de cumplimiento para respaldar los programas de cumplimiento de los clientes, cada cliente sigue siendo responsable de aplicar los controles adecuados a sus propias cargas de trabajo y de cumplir sus obligaciones regulatorias espec\xedficas. La eliminaci\xf3n garantizada de todas las vulnerabilidades de seguridad no es un beneficio definido por el CAF; si bien la adopci\xf3n de la nube puede mejorar la postura de seguridad de una organizaci\xf3n mediante la responsabilidad compartida y el acceso a servicios de seguridad avanzados, el CAF no promete que la migraci\xf3n a la nube elimine todo riesgo de seguridad."},{"id":"q1062","question":"Una empresa necesita una estrategia de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres en la que una versi\xf3n reducida pero totalmente funcional de su entorno se ejecute continuamente en una regi\xf3n secundaria de AWS, lo que permita una conmutaci\xf3n por error r\xe1pida al escalar a la capacidad total cuando ocurra un desastre. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres describe esto?","options":{"A":"Copia de seguridad y restauraci\xf3n","B":"Luz piloto","C":"Standby en caliente","D":"Activo-activo multisitio"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El standby en caliente es una estrategia de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres en la que una versi\xf3n reducida pero totalmente operativa del entorno de aplicaciones se ejecuta continuamente en una regi\xf3n secundaria, lo que permite a la empresa realizar una conmutaci\xf3n por error r\xe1pida al escalar el entorno de standby a la capacidad de producci\xf3n total cuando ocurre un desastre, lo que proporciona un equilibrio entre la velocidad de recuperaci\xf3n y el costo continuo.\\nLa copia de seguridad y restauraci\xf3n implica almacenar copias de seguridad peri\xf3dicas de los datos y restaurarlas en un entorno reci\xe9n aprovisionado despu\xe9s de un desastre, lo que da como resultado el tiempo de recuperaci\xf3n m\xe1s largo de todas las estrategias de DR, ya que la infraestructura debe construirse desde cero durante la recuperaci\xf3n en lugar de estar ya disponible. La luz piloto mantiene solo los componentes centrales m\xednimos del entorno en ejecuci\xf3n en la regi\xf3n secundaria, como una base de datos replicada, con otra infraestructura apagada y que requiere aprovisionamiento durante la conmutaci\xf3n por error, lo que da como resultado una recuperaci\xf3n m\xe1s r\xe1pida que la copia de seguridad y restauraci\xf3n pero m\xe1s lenta que el standby en caliente, ya que se debe poner en l\xednea m\xe1s infraestructura. El activo-activo multisitio ejecuta copias completas de producci\xf3n del entorno en varios sitios simult\xe1neamente, lo que proporciona la conmutaci\xf3n por error m\xe1s r\xe1pida posible con esencialmente cero tiempo de inactividad, pero con el costo continuo m\xe1s alto, ya que todos los sitios deben mantener la capacidad de producci\xf3n total en todo momento."},{"id":"q1063","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres tiene el tiempo de recuperaci\xf3n m\xe1s largo y el costo m\xe1s bajo porque no se mantiene infraestructura de standby en ejecuci\xf3n en la ubicaci\xf3n secundaria?","options":{"A":"Activo-activo multisitio","B":"Standby en caliente","C":"Luz piloto","D":"Copia de seguridad y restauraci\xf3n"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La copia de seguridad y restauraci\xf3n tiene el objetivo de tiempo de recuperaci\xf3n m\xe1s largo de todas las estrategias de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres, porque despu\xe9s de una falla se debe aprovisionar toda la infraestructura desde cero y se deben restaurar los datos desde las copias de seguridad antes de que la aplicaci\xf3n pueda reanudar el servicio de tr\xe1fico, pero tiene el costo continuo m\xe1s bajo porque no hay infraestructura secundaria en ejecuci\xf3n durante las operaciones normales.\\nEl activo-activo multisitio ejecuta la capacidad de producci\xf3n total en varios sitios simult\xe1neamente, lo que proporciona la conmutaci\xf3n por error m\xe1s r\xe1pida con esencialmente cero tiempo de inactividad, pero requiere la inversi\xf3n continua m\xe1s alta, ya que se incurre en costos de infraestructura total en cada sitio de manera continua. El standby en caliente mantiene una versi\xf3n reducida pero funcional del entorno en ejecuci\xf3n continua, lo que da como resultado una recuperaci\xf3n m\xe1s r\xe1pida que la copia de seguridad y restauraci\xf3n pero con un costo continuo m\xe1s alto, ya que los recursos siempre est\xe1n en ejecuci\xf3n en la ubicaci\xf3n secundaria. La luz piloto mantiene solo componentes centrales cr\xedticos, como una base de datos replicada, en ejecuci\xf3n continua, con otra infraestructura apagada, lo que da como resultado una recuperaci\xf3n m\xe1s r\xe1pida que la copia de seguridad y restauraci\xf3n pero m\xe1s lenta que el standby en caliente, con un costo continuo entre ambos."},{"id":"q1064","question":"Una empresa quiere mover una aplicaci\xf3n local existente a AWS lo m\xe1s r\xe1pido posible sin realizar ning\xfan cambio en el c\xf3digo o la arquitectura de la aplicaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n debe usar la empresa?","options":{"A":"Replataformar (Replatform)","B":"Refactorizar (Refactor)","C":"Rehost (reubicar)","D":"Readquirir (Repurchase)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El rehosting, com\xfanmente conocido como lift-and-shift, implica mover una aplicaci\xf3n a la nube sin realizar ning\xfan cambio en el c\xf3digo o la arquitectura de la aplicaci\xf3n, lo que lo convierte en el enfoque de migraci\xf3n m\xe1s r\xe1pido y permite a las organizaciones obtener beneficios de la nube r\xe1pidamente con un esfuerzo de migraci\xf3n inicial m\xednimo.\\nEl replatforming implica realizar optimizaciones espec\xedficas durante la migraci\xf3n, como pasar de una base de datos autogestionada a un servicio administrado, para obtener beneficios de la nube sin cambiar la arquitectura central de la aplicaci\xf3n; esto requiere algunos cambios y, por lo tanto, m\xe1s esfuerzo que un rehost puro. El refactoring implica redise\xf1ar la arquitectura de la aplicaci\xf3n para aprovechar plenamente las capacidades nativas de la nube, como microservicios y computaci\xf3n sin servidor, lo que proporciona los mayores beneficios a largo plazo pero requiere la mayor inversi\xf3n de tiempo y desarrollo de todas las estrategias de migraci\xf3n. El repurchasing implica reemplazar la aplicaci\xf3n existente por completo con un producto diferente, normalmente una soluci\xf3n SaaS, lo que requiere aprender una nueva plataforma y migrar datos en lugar de simplemente mover la aplicaci\xf3n existente a una nueva infraestructura."},{"id":"q1065","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 migrando una aplicaci\xf3n a AWS y decide mover su base de datos MySQL autogestionada que se ejecuta en una m\xe1quina virtual a Amazon RDS, sin cambiar la l\xf3gica central de la aplicaci\xf3n ni reescribir c\xf3digo. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n representa esto?","options":{"A":"Rehost (reubicar)","B":"Readquirir (Repurchase)","C":"Replataformar (Replatform)","D":"Refactorizar (Refactor)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El replatforming, tambi\xe9n conocido como lift-tinker-and-shift, implica realizar optimizaciones espec\xedficas durante la migraci\xf3n para aprovechar las capacidades administradas de la nube, como pasar de una base de datos autogestionada a Amazon RDS para obtener copias de seguridad automatizadas, parches y escalado, sin redise\xf1ar la arquitectura de la aplicaci\xf3n central ni cambiar su c\xf3digo.\\nEl rehosting mueve una aplicaci\xf3n a la nube sin cambios en la arquitectura ni en el c\xf3digo, lo que lo convierte en un lift-and-shift puro; cambiar de una base de datos autogestionada a Amazon RDS representa un cambio a infraestructura administrada y, por lo tanto, va m\xe1s all\xe1 de un simple rehost. El repurchasing reemplaza una aplicaci\xf3n existente por un producto fundamentalmente diferente, como pasar a una base de datos SaaS de terceros; moverse a Amazon RDS usa el mismo motor MySQL en forma administrada en lugar de reemplazar la aplicaci\xf3n por un producto diferente de un proveedor diferente. El refactoring implica redise\xf1ar la arquitectura de la aplicaci\xf3n para usar servicios y patrones nativos de la nube, como funciones sin servidor o microservicios, lo que representa un cambio mucho m\xe1s significativo en el dise\xf1o de la aplicaci\xf3n que simplemente migrar la base de datos a un servicio administrado."},{"id":"q1066","question":"Durante la planificaci\xf3n de la migraci\xf3n, una empresa descubre que el 30 % de sus aplicaciones locales no se han utilizado en m\xe1s de dos a\xf1os y no aportan valor empresarial actual. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n se debe aplicar a estas aplicaciones?","options":{"A":"Retener","B":"Rehost (reubicar)","C":"Retirar","D":"Replataformar (Replatform)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Retirar implica dar de baja aplicaciones que ya no son necesarias o que no aportan valor empresarial, lo que reduce el alcance y el costo de la migraci\xf3n al eliminar cargas de trabajo innecesarias en lugar de gastar esfuerzo y dinero en moverlas a la nube, donde seguir\xedan consumiendo recursos.\\nRetener implica mantener ciertas aplicaciones en ejecuci\xf3n en su entorno local actual, normalmente porque a\xfan no est\xe1n listas para migrar debido a restricciones t\xe9cnicas o de cumplimiento, pero esta estrategia todav\xeda incurre en costos de mantenimiento continuos en lugar de eliminar la carga de aplicaciones sin prop\xf3sito empresarial. Rehost (reubicar) mueve aplicaciones a la nube sin cambios, lo que todav\xeda consumir\xeda recursos de nube y generar\xeda costos continuos para aplicaciones que no tienen valor empresarial; dar de baja es la opci\xf3n correcta cuando no hay ninguna raz\xf3n para conservar la aplicaci\xf3n en absoluto. Replataformar implica optimizar aplicaciones durante la migraci\xf3n al pasar a servicios administrados, lo que requiere esfuerzo de migraci\xf3n y costos continuos de recursos de nube que se desperdiciar\xedan por completo en aplicaciones que no cumplen ning\xfan prop\xf3sito empresarial."},{"id":"q1092","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 marco de AWS proporciona gu\xeda organizada en seis perspectivas para ayudar a las organizaciones a identificar y priorizar oportunidades de transformaci\xf3n a la nube?","options":{"A":"AWS Well-Architected Framework","B":"AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)","C":"AWS Migration Acceleration Program","D":"AWS Shared Responsibility Model"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El AWS Cloud Adoption Framework organiza la gu\xeda en seis perspectivas: Negocio, Personas, Gobernanza, Plataforma, Seguridad y Operaciones, para ayudar a las organizaciones a identificar brechas de habilidades, mejoras de procesos y requisitos t\xe9cnicos para una transformaci\xf3n exitosa a la nube.\\nEl AWS Well-Architected Framework proporciona mejores pr\xe1cticas para dise\xf1ar y operar cargas de trabajo en la nube a trav\xe9s de seis pilares, pero se centra en la arquitectura de cargas de trabajo en lugar de en la planificaci\xf3n de la adopci\xf3n de la nube a nivel organizacional. El AWS Migration Acceleration Program es un programa de consultor\xeda y tecnolog\xeda que ayuda a los clientes a acelerar su migraci\xf3n a AWS, pero no es un marco de perspectivas para la planificaci\xf3n de la transformaci\xf3n a la nube. El AWS Shared Responsibility Model define la divisi\xf3n de responsabilidades de seguridad entre AWS y el cliente, y es un concepto de gobernanza de seguridad en lugar de un marco de planificaci\xf3n de adopci\xf3n de la nube."},{"id":"q1093","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 planificando una migraci\xf3n a la nube y necesita evaluar la preparaci\xf3n de su fuerza laboral e identificar brechas de habilidades. \xbfQu\xe9 perspectiva del AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) aborda esta necesidad?","options":{"A":"Negocio","B":"Personas","C":"Operaciones","D":"Plataforma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La perspectiva Personas del AWS CAF se centra en evaluar las estructuras organizativas, los roles y las habilidades para garantizar que la fuerza laboral est\xe9 preparada para la adopci\xf3n de la nube, incluidos la capacitaci\xf3n, la gesti\xf3n del cambio y el desarrollo profesional.\\nLa perspectiva Negocio se centra en garantizar que las inversiones en la nube est\xe9n alineadas con los resultados del negocio y que las partes interesadas puedan articular el caso de negocio para la adopci\xf3n de la nube, en lugar de la preparaci\xf3n de la fuerza laboral. La perspectiva Operaciones se centra en definir c\xf3mo se gestionar\xe1n, monitorear\xe1n y mejorar\xe1n las operaciones diarias en la nube, en lugar de la evaluaci\xf3n de habilidades de la fuerza laboral. La perspectiva Plataforma se centra en construir una arquitectura de plataforma en la nube escalable que incluya dise\xf1o de redes, c\xf3mputo y almacenamiento, en lugar de la preparaci\xf3n de las personas y la organizaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q1094","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 perspectivas del AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) se categorizan como capacidades t\xe9cnicas? (Seleccione DOS)","options":{"A":"Negocio","B":"Plataforma","C":"Personas","D":"Operaciones","E":"Gobernanza"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"El AWS CAF agrupa sus seis perspectivas en dos categor\xedas: capacidades de negocio (Negocio, Personas, Gobernanza) y capacidades t\xe9cnicas (Plataforma, Seguridad, Operaciones). Tanto Plataforma como Operaciones se incluyen en las capacidades t\xe9cnicas.\\nNegocio es una perspectiva de capacidad de negocio centrada en alinear las inversiones en la nube con los resultados del negocio, no una capacidad t\xe9cnica. Personas es una perspectiva de capacidad de negocio centrada en la preparaci\xf3n organizativa, la cultura y las habilidades de la fuerza laboral. Gobernanza es una perspectiva de capacidad de negocio centrada en orquestar las iniciativas en la nube, maximizar los beneficios y minimizar los riesgos de la transformaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q1095","question":"Una empresa quiere garantizar que su adopci\xf3n de la nube minimice los riesgos relacionados con la transformaci\xf3n y se alinee con los requisitos regulatorios. \xbfQu\xe9 perspectiva del AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) es M\xc1S relevante?","options":{"A":"Seguridad","B":"Negocio","C":"Gobernanza","D":"Operaciones"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La perspectiva Gobernanza del AWS CAF se centra en orquestar las iniciativas en la nube mientras maximiza los beneficios organizativos y minimiza los riesgos relacionados con la transformaci\xf3n, incluida la alineaci\xf3n de la estrategia de nube con los requisitos regulatorios y de cumplimiento.\\nLa perspectiva Seguridad se centra en lograr la confidencialidad, integridad y disponibilidad de los datos y las cargas de trabajo en la nube mediante controles de seguridad, en lugar de la gesti\xf3n m\xe1s amplia de riesgos de transformaci\xf3n y la alineaci\xf3n regulatoria. La perspectiva Negocio se centra en garantizar que las inversiones en la nube entreguen resultados de negocio medibles, en lugar de la gesti\xf3n de riesgos y el cumplimiento regulatorio. La perspectiva Operaciones se centra en garantizar que los servicios en la nube se entreguen a un nivel que satisfaga las necesidades del negocio d\xeda a d\xeda, en lugar de la alineaci\xf3n estrat\xe9gica de riesgos y cumplimiento."},{"id":"q1096","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 perspectiva del AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) ayuda a garantizar que las cargas de trabajo en la nube logren la confidencialidad, integridad y disponibilidad de los datos?","options":{"A":"Gobernanza","B":"Operaciones","C":"Plataforma","D":"Seguridad"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La perspectiva Seguridad del AWS CAF se centra en lograr la confidencialidad, integridad y disponibilidad de los datos y las cargas de trabajo en la nube, incluidos la gesti\xf3n de identidades, la detecci\xf3n de amenazas, la protecci\xf3n de la infraestructura y la respuesta a incidentes.\\nLa perspectiva Gobernanza se centra en orquestar las iniciativas en la nube, alinear la estrategia de nube con los objetivos del negocio y gestionar los riesgos de la transformaci\xf3n, en lugar de la postura t\xe9cnica de seguridad de las cargas de trabajo. La perspectiva Operaciones se centra en entregar servicios en la nube para satisfacer las necesidades del negocio mediante monitoreo, gesti\xf3n de incidentes y mejora continua de los procesos operativos. La perspectiva Plataforma se centra en dise\xf1ar y construir un entorno de nube escalable de nivel empresarial que incluya infraestructura, redes y aprovisionamiento, en lugar de la protecci\xf3n de datos y los controles de seguridad."},{"id":"q1117","question":"Una empresa quiere usar el AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) para construir un caso de negocio para la migraci\xf3n a la nube. \xbfEn qu\xe9 perspectiva del CAF deber\xeda centrarse?","options":{"A":"Personas","B":"Negocio","C":"Plataforma","D":"Gobernanza"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La perspectiva Negocio del AWS CAF ayuda a las partes interesadas a comprender c\xf3mo la adopci\xf3n de la nube puede acelerar los resultados del negocio, articular un caso de negocio convincente y garantizar que las inversiones en la nube est\xe9n vinculadas a un valor de negocio medible.\\nLa perspectiva Personas se centra en la preparaci\xf3n organizativa, el desarrollo de habilidades y la gesti\xf3n del cambio, en lugar de construir un caso de negocio financiero o estrat\xe9gico para la migraci\xf3n. La perspectiva Plataforma se centra en dise\xf1ar la arquitectura de infraestructura en la nube, incluidos c\xf3mputo, redes y almacenamiento, lo cual es una preocupaci\xf3n t\xe9cnica en lugar de una actividad de justificaci\xf3n del negocio. La perspectiva Gobernanza se centra en gestionar riesgos y alinear las iniciativas en la nube con los requisitos de cumplimiento, lo que respalda el caso de negocio, pero no es la perspectiva directamente responsable de articularlo."},{"id":"q1118","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 perspectiva del AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) se centra en dise\xf1ar e implementar nuevos entornos de nube con escalabilidad de nivel empresarial?","options":{"A":"Operaciones","B":"Seguridad","C":"Plataforma","D":"Personas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La perspectiva Plataforma se centra en construir una plataforma en la nube escalable de nivel empresarial mediante el dise\xf1o de la arquitectura objetivo para servicios de redes, c\xf3mputo, almacenamiento y bases de datos, y el establecimiento de patrones para aprovisionar y gestionar recursos en la nube.\\nLa perspectiva Operaciones se centra en entregar y gestionar servicios en la nube para satisfacer las necesidades diarias del negocio mediante monitoreo, gesti\xf3n de incidentes y mejora de procesos, no en dise\xf1ar la arquitectura de la plataforma subyacente. La perspectiva Seguridad se centra en proteger los datos y las cargas de trabajo mediante gesti\xf3n de identidades, detecci\xf3n de amenazas y controles de seguridad, en lugar de dise\xf1ar la plataforma de infraestructura en la nube. La perspectiva Personas se centra en la preparaci\xf3n de la fuerza laboral, el cambio organizativo y el desarrollo de habilidades, lo cual no est\xe1 relacionado con la arquitectura t\xe9cnica de la plataforma."},{"id":"q1119","question":"Una empresa necesita definir c\xf3mo gestionar\xe1 y monitorear\xe1 las cargas de trabajo en la nube d\xeda a d\xeda despu\xe9s de la migraci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 perspectiva del AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) aborda esto?","options":{"A":"Plataforma","B":"Gobernanza","C":"Operaciones","D":"Negocio"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La perspectiva Operaciones del AWS CAF se centra en garantizar que los servicios en la nube se entreguen y gestionen para satisfacer las necesidades continuas del negocio, cubriendo \xe1reas como monitoreo, gesti\xf3n de incidentes, gesti\xf3n de eventos y mejora continua de los procesos operativos.\\nLa perspectiva Plataforma se centra en dise\xf1ar y construir la arquitectura del entorno de nube, lo cual es una actividad de dise\xf1o \xfanica en lugar de gesti\xf3n diaria continua. La perspectiva Gobernanza se centra en la alineaci\xf3n estrat\xe9gica, la gesti\xf3n de riesgos y el cumplimiento, que son preocupaciones de mayor nivel que el monitoreo operativo diario y la gesti\xf3n de incidentes. La perspectiva Negocio se centra en alinear las inversiones en la nube con los resultados del negocio y medir el retorno de la inversi\xf3n, en lugar de la gesti\xf3n operativa de las cargas de trabajo."},{"id":"q1120","question":"\xbfCu\xe1ntas perspectivas define el AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)?","options":{"A":"Cuatro","B":"Cinco","C":"Seis","D":"Siete"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El AWS CAF define seis perspectivas: Negocio, Personas y Gobernanza (capacidades de negocio), y Plataforma, Seguridad y Operaciones (capacidades t\xe9cnicas), lo que proporciona una gu\xeda integral para la transformaci\xf3n a la nube.\\nCuatro perspectivas ser\xedan incompletas, ya que el CAF define expl\xedcitamente seis \xe1reas de enfoque distintas para cubrir tanto las dimensiones de negocio como las t\xe9cnicas de la adopci\xf3n de la nube. Cinco perspectivas omitir\xedan una de las seis \xe1reas definidas y no coinciden con la estructura real del CAF. Siete perspectivas superan el n\xfamero real definido por el marco, que agrupa su gu\xeda en exactamente seis perspectivas."},{"id":"q1121","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 perspectivas del AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) se clasifican como capacidades de negocio? (Seleccione DOS)","options":{"A":"Business","B":"Platform","C":"Operations","D":"Security","E":"Governance"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"El AWS CAF clasifica Business, People y Governance como perspectivas de capacidad de negocio, que se centran en la alineaci\xf3n estrat\xe9gica, la preparaci\xf3n organizacional, las habilidades de la fuerza laboral, la gobernanza de riesgos y en garantizar que las inversiones en la nube aporten valor de negocio; de las opciones enumeradas, Business y Governance son las dos perspectivas de capacidad de negocio.\\nPlatform es una perspectiva de capacidad t\xe9cnica que se centra en dise\xf1ar y construir la arquitectura del entorno de nube, no en la alineaci\xf3n de negocio ni en la preparaci\xf3n organizacional. Operations es una perspectiva de capacidad t\xe9cnica que se centra en ejecutar, monitorear y optimizar las cargas de trabajo en la nube d\xeda a d\xeda, no en la planificaci\xf3n ni la gobernanza a nivel de negocio. Security es una perspectiva de capacidad t\xe9cnica que se centra en proteger la confidencialidad, integridad y disponibilidad de los datos mediante controles t\xe9cnicos de seguridad, no en el valor de negocio ni la gobernanza."},{"id":"q1122","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 evaluando su preparaci\xf3n para la nube y descubre que su personal de TI carece de experiencia con los servicios de AWS. Seg\xfan el AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF), \xbfqu\xe9 acci\xf3n deber\xeda tomar la empresa?","options":{"A":"Retrasar la migraci\xf3n hasta que todo el personal est\xe9 completamente capacitado","B":"Invertir en programas de capacitaci\xf3n y mejora de habilidades alineados con la perspectiva People","C":"Externalizar permanentemente todas las operaciones en la nube a un tercero","D":"Proceder con la migraci\xf3n sin ninguna capacitaci\xf3n para ahorrar tiempo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La perspectiva People del AWS CAF recomienda invertir en capacitaci\xf3n, mejora de habilidades y gesti\xf3n del cambio organizacional para cerrar brechas de habilidades y preparar a la fuerza laboral para la adopci\xf3n de la nube, garantizando una capacidad interna sostenible en lugar de dependencia de recursos externos.\\nRetrasar la migraci\xf3n indefinidamente hasta que todo el personal est\xe9 completamente capacitado es poco pr\xe1ctico e innecesario, ya que el CAF recomienda el aprendizaje iterativo junto con la migraci\xf3n en lugar de esperar a una preparaci\xf3n completa. Externalizar permanentemente todas las operaciones no desarrolla capacidad interna y crea dependencia a largo plazo, lo que contradice el objetivo de la perspectiva People de desarrollar habilidades en la nube dentro de la organizaci\xf3n. Proceder sin capacitaci\xf3n conlleva riesgos de fallas operativas, incidentes de seguridad y uso ineficiente de recursos, e ignora el \xe9nfasis de la perspectiva People en la preparaci\xf3n de la fuerza laboral."},{"id":"q1123","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un \xe1rea de enfoque clave de la perspectiva Governance en el AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)?","options":{"A":"Dise\xf1ar la arquitectura de nube objetivo","B":"Gestionar beneficios, riesgos y cumplimiento durante la transformaci\xf3n a la nube","C":"Monitorear el rendimiento de las aplicaciones despu\xe9s del despliegue","D":"Configurar controles de gesti\xf3n de identidades y accesos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La perspectiva Governance se centra en orquestar las iniciativas de nube para maximizar los beneficios organizacionales mientras se minimizan los riesgos de la transformaci\xf3n, incluida la gesti\xf3n presupuestaria, la gesti\xf3n de portafolios, la alineaci\xf3n con el cumplimiento y las estrategias de mitigaci\xf3n de riesgos.\\nDise\xf1ar la arquitectura de nube objetivo es un enfoque de la perspectiva Platform, que se ocupa de las decisiones de dise\xf1o de infraestructura, redes y c\xf3mputo. Monitorear el rendimiento de las aplicaciones despu\xe9s del despliegue es un enfoque de la perspectiva Operations, que gestiona la entrega diaria de servicios, la observabilidad y la gesti\xf3n de incidentes. Configurar controles de gesti\xf3n de identidades y accesos es un enfoque de la perspectiva Security, que se ocupa de la gesti\xf3n de identidades, los permisos y la detecci\xf3n de amenazas."},{"id":"q1124","question":"El AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) identifica cuatro dominios de transformaci\xf3n. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es uno de esos dominios?","options":{"A":"Technology","B":"Networking","C":"Database","D":"Compute"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El AWS CAF identifica cuatro dominios de transformaci\xf3n: Technology, Process, Organization y Product. El dominio Technology abarca la migraci\xf3n y modernizaci\xf3n de infraestructura, aplicaciones y plataformas de datos para aprovechar las capacidades de la nube.\\nNetworking es un componente dentro de la perspectiva Platform y del dominio de transformaci\xf3n Technology, pero no es en s\xed mismo uno de los cuatro dominios de transformaci\xf3n definidos por el CAF. Database es una categor\xeda de servicio espec\xedfica dentro de la tecnolog\xeda de nube, no uno de los cuatro dominios de transformaci\xf3n que el CAF utiliza para estructurar las actividades de adopci\xf3n de la nube. Compute es un tipo de recurso espec\xedfico dentro de la infraestructura de nube, no uno de los cuatro dominios de transformaci\xf3n de alto nivel definidos por el CAF."},{"id":"q1125","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 dominios de transformaci\xf3n define el AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)? (Seleccione DOS)","options":{"A":"Product","B":"Security","C":"Process","D":"Infrastructure","E":"Compliance"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"El AWS CAF define cuatro dominios de transformaci\xf3n: Technology, Process, Organization y Product. Process se centra en digitalizar, automatizar y optimizar las operaciones de negocio, mientras que Product se centra en reimaginar el modelo de negocio utilizando nuevas capacidades habilitadas por la nube.\\nSecurity es una de las seis perspectivas del CAF, no uno de los cuatro dominios de transformaci\xf3n, y proporciona orientaci\xf3n sobre la protecci\xf3n de datos y cargas de trabajo en lugar de definir una v\xeda de transformaci\xf3n. Infrastructure es un componente dentro del dominio de transformaci\xf3n Technology y de la perspectiva Platform, pero no es en s\xed mismo uno de los cuatro dominios de transformaci\xf3n. Compliance es un \xe1rea abordada por la perspectiva Governance, pero no es uno de los cuatro dominios de transformaci\xf3n definidos por el CAF."},{"id":"q1126","question":"Una empresa quiere utilizar el AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) para evaluar cu\xe1les de sus aplicaciones est\xe1n listas para migrar a AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 actividad del CAF describe esto?","options":{"A":"Fase Envision","B":"Fase Align","C":"Fase Launch","D":"Fase Scale"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La fase Align del AWS CAF se centra en identificar brechas de capacidades, evaluar dependencias entre organizaciones y evaluar la preparaci\xf3n de las aplicaciones para crear un plan de acci\xf3n detallado para la adopci\xf3n de la nube, lo que la convierte en la fase en la que se determina la preparaci\xf3n para la migraci\xf3n.\\nLa fase Envision se centra en identificar c\xf3mo la nube puede acelerar los resultados de negocio y en crear una visi\xf3n compartida para la transformaci\xf3n, lo que ocurre antes de la evaluaci\xf3n de preparaci\xf3n. La fase Launch se centra en entregar iniciativas piloto y demostrar valor de negocio incremental mediante despliegues iniciales en la nube, lo que ocurre despu\xe9s de que se ha evaluado la preparaci\xf3n. La fase Scale se centra en ampliar la adopci\xf3n de la nube en toda la organizaci\xf3n y optimizar los modelos operativos para el escalado a largo plazo, lo que viene despu\xe9s de que los lanzamientos iniciales se han demostrado exitosos."},{"id":"q1127","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el orden correcto de las fases del AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) para la transformaci\xf3n a la nube?","options":{"A":"Align, Envision, Launch, Scale","B":"Envision, Align, Launch, Scale","C":"Launch, Envision, Scale, Align","D":"Envision, Launch, Align, Scale"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El AWS CAF define el viaje de transformaci\xf3n a la nube en cuatro fases: Envision (identificar c\xf3mo la nube acelera los resultados de negocio), Align (identificar brechas de capacidades y crear un plan de acci\xf3n), Launch (entregar iniciativas piloto) y Scale (ampliar la adopci\xf3n en toda la organizaci\xf3n).\\nComenzar con Align antes de Envision omite el primer paso cr\xedtico de establecer una visi\xf3n compartida de por qu\xe9 la organizaci\xf3n est\xe1 adoptando la nube, que se necesita para guiar las actividades de alineaci\xf3n. Comenzar con Launch antes de Align significa desplegar en la nube sin evaluar primero la preparaci\xf3n ni crear un plan de acci\xf3n, lo que conlleva el riesgo de migraciones descoordinadas y fallidas. Colocar Align despu\xe9s de Launch significa evaluar la preparaci\xf3n despu\xe9s de ya haber desplegado cargas de trabajo, lo que invierte el orden l\xf3gico de planificar antes de ejecutar."},{"id":"q1128","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 utilizando el AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF) y quiere establecer un Cloud Center of Excellence (CCoE). \xbfQu\xe9 fase del viaje de transformaci\xf3n del CAF normalmente involucra esta actividad?","options":{"A":"Envision","B":"Align","C":"Launch","D":"Scale"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La fase Launch del AWS CAF implica entregar iniciativas piloto, construir un Cloud Center of Excellence para proporcionar orientaci\xf3n y mejores pr\xe1cticas, y demostrar valor de negocio incremental mediante despliegues iniciales en la nube que establecen la base para una adopci\xf3n m\xe1s amplia.\\nLa fase Envision se centra en crear una visi\xf3n compartida de transformaci\xf3n e identificar c\xf3mo la nube puede impulsar los resultados de negocio, lo que ocurre antes de establecer estructuras operativas como un CCoE. La fase Align se centra en evaluar la preparaci\xf3n, identificar brechas y crear un plan de acci\xf3n, que es trabajo de planificaci\xf3n que precede a las actividades pr\xe1cticas de lanzamiento. La fase Scale se centra en ampliar la adopci\xf3n en toda la organizaci\xf3n despu\xe9s de que el CCoE y los patrones iniciales ya se han establecido durante la fase Launch."},{"id":"q1097","question":"Una empresa quiere mover su aplicaci\xf3n local a AWS lo m\xe1s r\xe1pido posible con cambios m\xednimos en el c\xf3digo existente. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n describe esto?","options":{"A":"Replatform","B":"Refactor","C":"Rehost","D":"Repurchase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rehosting, tambi\xe9n conocido como lift and shift, implica mover una aplicaci\xf3n a la nube sin realizar cambios en el c\xf3digo ni en la arquitectura de la aplicaci\xf3n, lo que lo convierte en la estrategia de migraci\xf3n m\xe1s r\xe1pida para llevar cargas de trabajo a AWS.\\nReplatforming implica realizar optimizaciones espec\xedficas durante la migraci\xf3n, como mover una base de datos a un servicio administrado como Amazon RDS, lo que requiere algunos cambios m\xe1s all\xe1 de un simple lift and shift. Refactoring implica redise\xf1ar la aplicaci\xf3n para aprovechar caracter\xedsticas nativas de la nube, como la computaci\xf3n sin servidor o los microservicios, lo que requiere un esfuerzo de desarrollo significativo y no es un enfoque de cambios m\xednimos. Repurchasing implica reemplazar la aplicaci\xf3n existente por un producto diferente, normalmente una soluci\xf3n SaaS, que no es una estrategia para mover el c\xf3digo existente a AWS."},{"id":"q1098","question":"Durante una evaluaci\xf3n de migraci\xf3n a la nube, una empresa identifica varias aplicaciones heredadas que ya no est\xe1n en uso. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n deber\xedan aplicar a estas aplicaciones?","options":{"A":"Retain","B":"Retire","C":"Rehost","D":"Relocate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retiring significa retirar aplicaciones que ya no son necesarias, lo que reduce el alcance general de la migraci\xf3n y elimina el costo y el esfuerzo de mantener software sin uso.\\nRetaining significa mantener las aplicaciones en su entorno local actual porque no est\xe1n listas para migrar, lo cual es adecuado para aplicaciones que a\xfan est\xe1n en uso activo en lugar de aquellas que ya no son necesarias. Rehosting significa mover una aplicaci\xf3n a AWS sin cambios, lo que desperdiciar\xeda recursos en una aplicaci\xf3n que no aporta valor empresarial. Relocating significa mover infraestructura a AWS con cambios m\xednimos, como usar VMware Cloud on AWS, lo que tampoco es adecuado para aplicaciones que deber\xedan retirarse."},{"id":"q1099","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 migrando su sistema de correo local y decide reemplazarlo con Amazon WorkMail, una soluci\xf3n de correo SaaS basada en la nube. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n describe esto?","options":{"A":"Replatform","B":"Repurchase","C":"Refactor","D":"Rehost"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Repurchasing implica pasar a un producto diferente, normalmente reemplazando una aplicaci\xf3n existente por una alternativa SaaS basada en la nube, que es exactamente lo que representa cambiar de un sistema de correo local a Amazon WorkMail.\\nReplatforming implica realizar optimizaciones espec\xedficas en una aplicaci\xf3n existente durante la migraci\xf3n, como mover una base de datos autoadministrada a Amazon RDS, en lugar de reemplazar toda la aplicaci\xf3n por un producto diferente. Refactoring implica redise\xf1ar la arquitectura de la aplicaci\xf3n existente para aprovechar caracter\xedsticas nativas de la nube, no reemplazar la aplicaci\xf3n por un producto completamente diferente. Rehosting implica mover la aplicaci\xf3n existente tal cual a la nube, lo que significar\xeda ejecutar el mismo sistema de correo local en EC2 en lugar de cambiar a un reemplazo SaaS."},{"id":"q1100","question":"Una empresa quiere migrar su base de datos MySQL local a Amazon RDS para MySQL para aprovechar la aplicaci\xf3n de parches y las copias de seguridad administradas, sin cambiar el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n describe esto?","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Replatform","C":"Refactor","D":"Retain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Replatforming, tambi\xe9n conocido como lift, tinker, and shift, implica realizar optimizaciones espec\xedficas durante la migraci\xf3n sin cambiar la arquitectura central de la aplicaci\xf3n, como mover una base de datos autoadministrada a un servicio administrado como Amazon RDS para beneficiarse de la aplicaci\xf3n de parches y las copias de seguridad automatizadas.\\nRehosting implicar\xeda mover la base de datos MySQL a una instancia EC2 y seguir administr\xe1ndola manualmente, lo que no aprovecha las caracter\xedsticas de las bases de datos administradas. Refactoring implicar\xeda redise\xf1ar la aplicaci\xf3n para usar un enfoque de base de datos fundamentalmente diferente, como migrar de una base de datos relacional a un servicio NoSQL como DynamoDB, lo que va m\xe1s all\xe1 de una optimizaci\xf3n espec\xedfica. Retaining implica mantener la carga de trabajo en su entorno local actual, lo que no implica ninguna migraci\xf3n a AWS."},{"id":"q1101","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n implica redise\xf1ar una aplicaci\xf3n para aprovechar al m\xe1ximo caracter\xedsticas nativas de la nube, como la computaci\xf3n sin servidor y los microservicios?","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Replatform","C":"Repurchase","D":"Refactor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Refactoring, tambi\xe9n conocido como re-architecting, implica redise\xf1ar fundamentalmente una aplicaci\xf3n para aprovechar capacidades nativas de la nube, como AWS Lambda para computaci\xf3n sin servidor, Amazon SQS para mensajer\xeda desacoplada y microservicios en contenedores, lo que normalmente ofrece los mayores beneficios de la nube a largo plazo con el mayor esfuerzo inicial.\\nRehosting mueve una aplicaci\xf3n a la nube sin ning\xfan cambio arquitect\xf3nico, preservando el dise\xf1o existente en lugar de adoptar patrones nativos de la nube. Replatforming realiza optimizaciones espec\xedficas durante la migraci\xf3n, como cambiar a servicios administrados, pero no implica un redise\xf1o arquitect\xf3nico completo. Repurchasing reemplaza la aplicaci\xf3n existente por un producto diferente, normalmente una soluci\xf3n SaaS, en lugar de redise\xf1ar la arquitectura de la aplicaci\xf3n existente."},{"id":"q1102","question":"Una empresa tiene aplicaciones que no se pueden migrar a la nube debido a dependencias de cumplimiento normativo no resueltas. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n deber\xedan aplicar a estas aplicaciones?","options":{"A":"Retire","B":"Retain","C":"Relocate","D":"Repurchase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Retaining significa mantener las aplicaciones en su entorno local actual, que es la estrategia adecuada para cargas de trabajo que a\xfan no est\xe1n listas para migrar debido a restricciones de cumplimiento normativo, dependencias t\xe9cnicas u otros requisitos no resueltos.\\nRetiring significa retirar aplicaciones que ya no son necesarias, lo que no es adecuado para aplicaciones que siguen en uso activo y tienen dependencias no resueltas. Relocating significa mover infraestructura a AWS con cambios m\xednimos, como usar VMware Cloud on AWS, lo que no aborda las dependencias de cumplimiento subyacentes que impiden la migraci\xf3n. Repurchasing significa reemplazar la aplicaci\xf3n por una alternativa SaaS, lo que tampoco resuelve las restricciones de cumplimiento que bloquean la migraci\xf3n."},{"id":"q1103","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes son estrategias de migraci\xf3n v\xe1lidas definidas en el AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)? (Selecciona DOS)","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Revalidate","C":"Repurchase","D":"Redistribute","E":"Reconfigure"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Rehost y Repurchase son dos de las siete estrategias de migraci\xf3n (7 Rs) definidas en el AWS Cloud Adoption Framework: Rehost, Replatform, Refactor, Repurchase, Retire, Retain y Relocate.\\nRevalidate no es una estrategia de migraci\xf3n reconocida en el AWS CAF ni en ning\xfan marco de migraci\xf3n est\xe1ndar de AWS. Redistribute no es una estrategia de migraci\xf3n reconocida en el AWS CAF y no corresponde a ning\xfan enfoque de migraci\xf3n a la nube definido. Reconfigure no es una estrategia de migraci\xf3n reconocida en el AWS CAF; la estrategia m\xe1s cercana que implica cambios es Replatform, que realiza optimizaciones espec\xedficas durante la migraci\xf3n."},{"id":"q1129","question":"Una empresa actualmente ejecuta m\xe1quinas virtuales basadas en VMware en sus instalaciones y quiere moverlas a AWS con cambios m\xednimos en el entorno VMware existente. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n describe esto?","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Replatform","C":"Relocate","D":"Refactor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Relocating implica mover infraestructura a AWS sin comprar hardware nuevo, reescribir aplicaciones ni modificar las operaciones existentes, como migrar cargas de trabajo basadas en VMware a VMware Cloud on AWS, donde el entorno VMware existente sigue funcionando con cambios m\xednimos.\\nRehosting implica mover aplicaciones individuales a la infraestructura de AWS, como instancias EC2, lo que requiere convertir m\xe1quinas virtuales en lugar de preservar la capa de administraci\xf3n de VMware existente. Replatforming implica realizar optimizaciones espec\xedficas durante la migraci\xf3n, como cambiar a servicios administrados, lo que va m\xe1s all\xe1 de simplemente mover el entorno VMware existente tal cual. Refactoring implica redise\xf1ar la arquitectura de la aplicaci\xf3n para usar servicios nativos de la nube, que es lo opuesto a preservar la infraestructura existente basada en VMware."},{"id":"q1130","question":"\xbfCu\xe1ntas estrategias de migraci\xf3n (Rs) se definen en AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)?","options":{"A":"Cinco","B":"Seis","C":"Siete","D":"Ocho"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS CAF define siete estrategias de migraci\xf3n conocidas como las 7 Rs: Rehost, Replatform, Refactor (Re-architect), Repurchase, Retire, Retain y Relocate, cada una representa un enfoque diferente para manejar cargas de trabajo durante una migraci\xf3n a la nube.\\nFive Rs era una versi\xf3n anterior de las estrategias de migraci\xf3n que no inclu\xeda Relocate y Retain, y no refleja la gu\xeda actual de AWS CAF. Six Rs es otra versi\xf3n hist\xf3rica que es anterior a la adici\xf3n de la estrategia Relocate, y ya no es el conjunto completo definido por AWS CAF actual. Eight Rs excede el n\xfamero de estrategias definidas por AWS CAF, que reconoce exactamente siete enfoques de migraci\xf3n distintos."},{"id":"q1131","question":"Una empresa quiere migrar una base de datos Oracle on-premises grande a Amazon DynamoDB para mejorar la escalabilidad y reducir los costos de licencias. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n describe esto?","options":{"A":"Replatform","B":"Rehost","C":"Refactor","D":"Repurchase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Migrar de una base de datos relacional Oracle a un servicio NoSQL como Amazon DynamoDB requiere redise\xf1ar fundamentalmente el modelo de datos y la l\xf3gica de la aplicaci\xf3n, lo que es una estrategia de refactorizaci\xf3n o re-arquitectura que aprovecha las capacidades nativas de la nube para mejorar la escalabilidad y el costo.\\nReplatforming implicar\xeda mover Oracle a Amazon RDS for Oracle o a una base de datos relacional administrada compatible, realizando optimizaciones espec\xedficas sin cambiar el paradigma fundamental de la base de datos. Rehosting significar\xeda ejecutar Oracle en una instancia EC2 exactamente como se ejecutaba on-premises, sin cambios en el motor de base de datos ni en la arquitectura. Repurchasing significar\xeda reemplazar la aplicaci\xf3n con un producto comercial o una soluci\xf3n SaaS completamente diferente, no redise\xf1ar la capa de base de datos para usar un servicio nativo de AWS diferente."},{"id":"q1132","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ayuda a automatizar la migraci\xf3n lift-and-shift de servidores desde on-premises u otras nubes a AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Database Migration Service","B":"AWS Application Migration Service","C":"AWS Migration Hub","D":"AWS Snow Family"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Application Migration Service (AWS MGN) automatiza el proceso de rehosting lift-and-shift replicando continuamente servidores de origen a AWS, realizando conversi\xf3n automatizada de m\xe1quinas y lanzando instancias completamente aprovisionadas en AWS con tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo.\\nAWS Database Migration Service migra bases de datos a AWS, admite migraciones homog\xe9neas y heterog\xe9neas, pero maneja cargas de trabajo de bases de datos espec\xedficamente en lugar de migraciones completas de servidores. AWS Migration Hub proporciona una ubicaci\xf3n central para rastrear el progreso de las migraciones a trav\xe9s de m\xfaltiples herramientas de AWS, pero es un servicio de seguimiento y visibilidad en lugar de una herramienta que realiza la migraci\xf3n real del servidor. AWS Snow Family proporciona dispositivos f\xedsicos para transferir grandes vol\xfamenes de datos a AWS sin conexi\xf3n, pero no automatiza la conversi\xf3n y el lanzamiento de servidores en la nube."},{"id":"q1133","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona una ubicaci\xf3n central para realizar un seguimiento del progreso de las migraciones de aplicaciones en m\xfaltiples herramientas de migraci\xf3n de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Application Migration Service","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Migration Hub","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Migration Hub proporciona un \xfanico panel para descubrir servidores existentes, planificar migraciones y realizar un seguimiento del estado de las migraciones de aplicaciones en herramientas de migraci\xf3n de AWS como Application Migration Service, Database Migration Service y soluciones de socios.\\nAWS Application Migration Service realiza la replicaci\xf3n y migraci\xf3n real de servidores a AWS, pero maneja la ejecuci\xf3n de la migraci\xf3n en lugar de proporcionar un panel de seguimiento centralizado en m\xfaltiples herramientas. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n con fines de auditor\xeda, y es un servicio de registro de actividad en lugar de una herramienta de seguimiento del progreso de migraci\xf3n. AWS Config registra y eval\xfaa configuraciones de recursos para cumplimiento, y monitorea el estado de recursos de AWS en lugar de realizar un seguimiento del progreso de migraci\xf3n."},{"id":"q1134","question":"Una empresa necesita migrar una base de datos on-premises de 100 TB a AWS pero tiene ancho de banda de red limitado. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS es M\xc1S apropiado para esta transferencia de datos?","options":{"A":"AWS DataSync","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS Snowball Edge","D":"Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Snowball Edge es un dispositivo f\xedsico de transferencia de datos que puede contener hasta 80 TB por dispositivo, lo que permite transferencias de datos a gran escala a AWS enviando el dispositivo en lugar de transferir por la red, lo que lo hace ideal cuando el ancho de banda de red es limitado y los tiempos de transferencia por Internet ser\xedan poco pr\xe1cticos.\\nAWS DataSync automatiza transferencias de datos entre almacenamiento on-premises y AWS por la red, pero con ancho de banda limitado una transferencia de 100 TB tomar\xeda un tiempo inaceptablemente largo. AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada a AWS que mejora el ancho de banda y la consistencia, pero aprovisionar un enlace Direct Connect toma semanas y a\xfan podr\xeda no proporcionar ancho de banda suficiente para una transferencia oportuna de 100 TB. Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration acelera las cargas a S3 usando ubicaciones de borde de CloudFront, pero a\xfan depende de Internet y estar\xeda limitado por el ancho de banda de red limitado de la empresa."},{"id":"q1135","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n proporciona el MAYOR beneficio a largo plazo de las caracter\xedsticas nativas de la nube pero requiere el MAYOR esfuerzo de desarrollo?","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Replatform","C":"Refactor","D":"Relocate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Refactorizar o re-arquitecturar una aplicaci\xf3n para usar servicios nativos de la nube como computaci\xf3n sin servidor, bases de datos administradas y arquitecturas orientadas a eventos brinda los mayores beneficios a largo plazo en escalabilidad, rendimiento y eficiencia de costos, pero requiere la inversi\xf3n m\xe1s significativa en tiempo y esfuerzo de desarrollo.\\nRehosting proporciona la ruta de migraci\xf3n m\xe1s r\xe1pida con esfuerzo m\xednimo pero no aprovecha las caracter\xedsticas nativas de la nube, lo que significa que los beneficios a largo plazo se limitan solo a mejoras a nivel de infraestructura. Replatforming proporciona beneficios de nube moderados con esfuerzo moderado al realizar optimizaciones espec\xedficas, pero no logra todo el potencial de la arquitectura nativa de la nube. Relocating preserva el entorno de infraestructura existente y proporciona el menor beneficio nativo de la nube, ya que las cargas de trabajo contin\xfaan ejecut\xe1ndose en su configuraci\xf3n original."},{"id":"q1136","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 migrando a AWS y quiere evaluar su infraestructura on-premises para comprender las dependencias y la utilizaci\xf3n de los servidores. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"AWS Migration Hub","B":"AWS Application Discovery Service","C":"AWS Application Migration Service","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Application Discovery Service recopila informaci\xf3n detallada sobre servidores on-premises, incluida la configuraci\xf3n, la utilizaci\xf3n y las dependencias de red, lo que ayuda a las empresas a planificar su migraci\xf3n al comprender qu\xe9 tienen y c\xf3mo est\xe1n interconectadas las aplicaciones.\\nAWS Migration Hub proporciona un panel central para rastrear el progreso de la migraci\xf3n en m\xfaltiples herramientas, pero depende de herramientas de descubrimiento como Application Discovery Service para recopilar los datos de infraestructura subyacentes. AWS Application Migration Service realiza la migraci\xf3n real del servidor replicando y lanzando instancias en AWS, pero no descubre ni eval\xfaa infraestructura on-premises. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas para entornos de AWS existentes en seguridad, costo y rendimiento, pero no descubre ni eval\xfaa infraestructura on-premises."},{"id":"q1137","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para migrar bases de datos desde on-premises a AWS, admitiendo migraciones del mismo motor y entre motores?","options":{"A":"AWS Application Migration Service","B":"AWS Schema Conversion Tool","C":"AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","D":"AWS DataSync"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Database Migration Service admite replicaci\xf3n continua de datos desde bases de datos on-premises a AWS con tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo, manejando tanto migraciones homog\xe9neas donde los motores de origen y destino son iguales como migraciones heterog\xe9neas donde los motores difieren.\\nAWS Application Migration Service migra servidores completos, incluidos el sistema operativo y las aplicaciones, a AWS, pero no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para migraciones espec\xedficas de bases de datos que requieren replicaci\xf3n de datos y manejo de esquemas. AWS Schema Conversion Tool convierte esquemas de bases de datos y procedimientos almacenados de un motor a otro para admitir migraciones heterog\xe9neas, pero maneja la conversi\xf3n de esquemas en lugar de la migraci\xf3n de datos real en s\xed. AWS DataSync automatiza transferencias de datos entre sistemas de almacenamiento on-premises y servicios de almacenamiento de AWS como S3 y EFS, pero es un servicio de movimiento de datos basado en archivos no dise\xf1ado para migraci\xf3n de bases de datos."},{"id":"q1138","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 migrando una aplicaci\xf3n monol\xedtica a AWS y decide dividirla en microservicios usando AWS Lambda y Amazon API Gateway. \xbfQu\xe9 estrategia de migraci\xf3n representa esto?","options":{"A":"Rehost","B":"Replatform","C":"Repurchase","D":"Refactor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dividir una aplicaci\xf3n monol\xedtica en microservicios usando servicios nativos de la nube como AWS Lambda y Amazon API Gateway es una estrategia de refactorizaci\xf3n o rearquitectura que cambia fundamentalmente el dise\xf1o de la aplicaci\xf3n para aprovechar las capacidades de la nube.\\nRehosting mover\xeda la aplicaci\xf3n monol\xedtica a EC2 tal cual, sin cambiar su arquitectura, preservando el monolito en lugar de descomponerlo en microservicios. Replatforming har\xeda mejoras puntuales, como mover la base de datos a un servicio administrado, pero no implicar\xeda un cambio arquitect\xf3nico fundamental como descomponer un monolito en microservicios. Repurchasing reemplazar\xeda la aplicaci\xf3n por un producto comercial o SaaS diferente, no redise\xf1ar\xeda la aplicaci\xf3n existente en una arquitectura de microservicios."},{"id":"q1139","question":"Una empresa tiene 500 servidores on-premises para migrar. Despu\xe9s de la evaluaci\xf3n, 200 se identifican como candidatos para lift-and-shift, 100 necesitan cambios de motor de base de datos, 50 se reemplazar\xe1n con SaaS y 150 ya no son necesarios. \xbfQu\xe9 combinaci\xf3n de estrategias de migraci\xf3n aplica? (Seleccione DOS)","options":{"A":"Rehost para los 200 servidores","B":"Replatform para los 100 servidores de base de datos","C":"Retire para los 150 servidores no utilizados","D":"Retain para los 50 reemplazos con SaaS","E":"Relocate para los 200 servidores"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Rehost aplica a los 200 servidores que se mueven tal cual mediante una migraci\xf3n lift-and-shift, y Retire aplica a los 150 servidores que ya no son necesarios y deben darse de baja, lo que reduce el alcance y el costo general de la migraci\xf3n.\\nReplatform es incorrecto para los 100 servidores de base de datos porque cambiar completamente el motor de base de datos, por ejemplo de Oracle a PostgreSQL, constituye refactorizaci\xf3n en lugar de replatforming, que ser\xeda mover Oracle a RDS for Oracle. Retain significa mantener las cargas de trabajo on-premises, lo que no describe las 50 aplicaciones que se reemplazan con soluciones SaaS; eso ser\xeda Repurchase. Relocate significa mover infraestructura basada en VMware a VMware Cloud on AWS, que es una estrategia espec\xedfica para preservar entornos VMware y no describe una migraci\xf3n lift-and-shift general."},{"id":"q1140","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes describe MEJOR la diferencia entre rehosting y replatforming como estrategias de migraci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Rehosting cambia el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n mientras que replatforming no","B":"Rehosting mueve la aplicaci\xf3n tal cual mientras que replatforming realiza optimizaciones puntuales","C":"Rehosting reemplaza la aplicaci\xf3n con SaaS mientras que replatforming mantiene la original","D":"Rehosting es m\xe1s lento que replatforming porque requiere m\xe1s pruebas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rehosting, o lift and shift, mueve la aplicaci\xf3n a AWS sin ning\xfan cambio en el c\xf3digo ni en la arquitectura, mientras que replatforming, o lift, tinker, and shift, realiza optimizaciones puntuales durante la migraci\xf3n, como cambiar a una base de datos administrada o modificar la capa de cach\xe9, sin redise\xf1ar fundamentalmente la aplicaci\xf3n.\\nRehosting no cambia ning\xfan c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n, y replatforming solo realiza cambios puntuales, por lo que la primera opci\xf3n invierte la relaci\xf3n entre ambas estrategias. Rehosting mueve la aplicaci\xf3n existente a la nube en lugar de reemplazarla con SaaS, lo cual ser\xeda la estrategia repurchase. Rehosting generalmente es m\xe1s r\xe1pido que replatforming porque requiere menos cambios y menos pruebas, no m\xe1s lento, ya que la aplicaci\xf3n se mueve sin modificaciones."},{"id":"q003","question":"Ha notado que se han terminado varias instancias cr\xedticas de Amazon EC2. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS le ayudar\xeda a determinar qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 esta acci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"EC2 Instance Usage Report"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y la actividad de la cuenta en un entorno de AWS, capturando qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 cada acci\xf3n, desde qu\xe9 direcci\xf3n IP y a qu\xe9 hora, lo que lo convierte en la fuente definitiva para identificar qui\xe9n termin\xf3 las instancias EC2.\\nAmazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y no registra qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 acciones de API ni eventos de administraci\xf3n. AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones sobre seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento en lugar de un registro de auditor\xeda de la actividad de la cuenta. EC2 Instance Usage Report proporciona datos de facturaci\xf3n y uso sobre instancias EC2 y no contiene registros de llamadas a la API ni informaci\xf3n de identidad sobre qui\xe9n termin\xf3 las instancias."},{"id":"q005","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n es verdadera respecto al Modelo de Responsabilidad Compartida de AWS?","options":{"A":"Las responsabilidades var\xedan seg\xfan los servicios utilizados","B":"La seguridad de los servicios IaaS es responsabilidad de AWS","C":"Aplicar parches al sistema operativo invitado es siempre responsabilidad de AWS","D":"La seguridad de los servicios administrados es responsabilidad del cliente"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Seg\xfan el Modelo de Responsabilidad Compartida, qui\xe9n es responsable de qu\xe9 cambia seg\xfan el tipo de servicio, y AWS asume m\xe1s responsabilidad en los servicios administrados mientras los clientes gestionan m\xe1s partes de la pila en los servicios IaaS.\\nLa seguridad de los servicios IaaS no es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes que usan IaaS deben gestionar el sistema operativo invitado, la pila de aplicaciones y los datos, mientras que AWS solo es responsable de la infraestructura subyacente. Aplicar parches al sistema operativo invitado no es siempre responsabilidad de AWS, ya que en servicios IaaS como EC2 el cliente es totalmente responsable de aplicar parches al sistema operativo que ejecuta en sus instancias. La seguridad de los servicios administrados no es responsabilidad exclusiva del cliente, ya que AWS asume significativamente m\xe1s carga de seguridad en servicios administrados como RDS y Lambda, cubriendo las capas de plataforma e infraestructura subyacentes."},{"id":"q011","question":"Una organizaci\xf3n tiene una gran cantidad de empleados t\xe9cnicos que operan su infraestructura de AWS Cloud. \xbfQu\xe9 proporciona AWS para ayudar a organizarlos en equipos y luego asignar los permisos adecuados para cada equipo?","options":{"A":"Roles de IAM","B":"Usuarios de IAM","C":"Grupos de usuarios de IAM","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los grupos de usuarios de IAM permiten organizar varios usuarios juntos y asignar permisos compartidos a nivel de grupo mediante pol\xedticas de IAM, lo que facilita la gesti\xf3n del acceso para equipos completos sin configurar cada usuario individualmente.\\nLos roles de IAM proporcionan credenciales de seguridad temporales que pueden asumir usuarios, servicios o aplicaciones, y no est\xe1n dise\xf1ados para organizar equipos ni asignar permisos compartidos a un conjunto fijo de usuarios de la forma en que lo hacen los grupos. Los usuarios de IAM son identidades individuales, cada una con sus propias credenciales y permisos, y gestionar grandes cantidades de usuarios individualmente adjuntando pol\xedticas a cada uno es poco pr\xe1ctico en comparaci\xf3n con usar grupos. AWS Organizations gestiona m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS bajo una \xfanica estructura para facturaci\xf3n consolidada y aplicaci\xf3n de pol\xedticas, y no es el servicio que se utiliza para organizar usuarios individuales de IAM en equipos dentro de una sola cuenta."},{"id":"q022","question":"\xbfA qu\xe9 se refiere el \\"Principio de menor privilegio\\"?","options":{"A":"Debe conceder a sus usuarios solo los permisos que necesitan cuando los necesitan y nada m\xe1s","B":"Todos los usuarios de IAM deben tener al menos los permisos necesarios para acceder a los servicios principales de AWS","C":"Todos los usuarios de IAM de confianza deben tener acceso a cualquier servicio de AWS en la cuenta de AWS correspondiente","D":"No se debe conceder ning\xfan permiso a los usuarios de IAM; para mantener su cuenta segura"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El Principio de menor privilegio significa conceder a los usuarios \xfanicamente los permisos exactos que necesitan para realizar su trabajo y nada m\xe1s all\xe1 de eso, minimizando el riesgo de uso indebido accidental o malicioso de los recursos de AWS.\\nConceder a los usuarios de IAM al menos los permisos necesarios para acceder a los servicios principales de AWS describe una l\xednea base m\xednima, que es lo opuesto al menor privilegio; el menor privilegio significa restringir los permisos solo a lo que se necesita para una tarea espec\xedfica en lugar de garantizar el acceso a servicios comunes. Conceder a todos los usuarios de confianza acceso a cualquier servicio de AWS en la cuenta contradice el menor privilegio al proporcionar acceso amplio sin restricciones en lugar de permisos espec\xedficos. No conceder permisos a ning\xfan usuario de IAM no es una interpretaci\xf3n pr\xe1ctica del menor privilegio, que se trata de conceder los permisos m\xednimos requeridos en lugar de no conceder ninguno."},{"id":"q026","question":"Se registran cientos de miles de ataques DDoS cada mes en todo el mundo. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio proporciona AWS para ayudar a proteger a los clientes de AWS de estos ataques?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon Cognito","D":"AWS WAF","E":"AWS KMS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Shield proporciona protecci\xf3n administrada contra DDoS en las capas de red y transporte, y AWS WAF le permite crear reglas que filtran el tr\xe1fico web malicioso en la capa de aplicaci\xf3n. Juntos defienden contra todo el espectro de ataques DDoS.\\nAWS Config monitorea y registra continuamente las configuraciones de los recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n y auditor\xeda sin capacidad para bloquear o mitigar tr\xe1fico DDoS. Amazon Cognito es un servicio de identidad y autenticaci\xf3n de usuarios para aplicaciones web y m\xf3viles, que proporciona funcionalidad de registro e inicio de sesi\xf3n e identidad federada, y no tiene capacidades de mitigaci\xf3n de DDoS. AWS KMS es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de claves para crear y controlar claves de cifrado utilizadas para proteger datos, y no participa en el filtrado de tr\xe1fico de red ni en la defensa contra DDoS."},{"id":"q031","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios permite a los clientes gestionar sus acuerdos con AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact es un portal de autoservicio donde los clientes pueden acceder y gestionar los informes de cumplimiento y acuerdos de AWS, incluidos los documentos NDA y Business Associate Addendum (BAA) que rigen la relaci\xf3n entre los clientes y AWS.\\nAWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y gestiona certificados SSL/TLS para su uso con servicios de AWS, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de certificados en lugar de un portal de gesti\xf3n de acuerdos. AWS Systems Manager proporciona herramientas operativas para gestionar recursos de AWS a escala, incluidos parches, almacenamiento de par\xe1metros y gesti\xf3n de sesiones, y no se utiliza para gestionar acuerdos contractuales con AWS. AWS Organizations gestiona varias cuentas de AWS bajo una estructura de cuenta maestra para facturaci\xf3n consolidada y pol\xedticas de control de servicios, y no es un portal para gestionar informes de cumplimiento o acuerdos."},{"id":"q032","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son ejemplos de servicios administrados por AWS, donde AWS es responsable de las cargas operativas y de mantenimiento de ejecutar el servicio?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon EMR","D":"AWS IAM","E":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB y Amazon EMR son servicios totalmente administrados en los que AWS gestiona el aprovisionamiento, la aplicaci\xf3n de parches, el escalado y el mantenimiento de forma autom\xe1tica, liberando a los clientes de la gesti\xf3n de la infraestructura subyacente.\\nAmazon VPC es una construcci\xf3n de red que los clientes configuran y gestionan ellos mismos, incluidas subredes, tablas de rutas, puertas de enlace de internet y grupos de seguridad, lo que lo convierte en un recurso configurado por el cliente en lugar de un servicio totalmente administrado. AWS IAM es un servicio que los clientes utilizan para gestionar sus propios usuarios, roles y permisos, y aunque AWS mantiene la infraestructura de IAM, los clientes son responsables de toda la configuraci\xf3n de identidad y acceso. Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud proporciona servidores virtuales en los que los clientes son responsables del sistema operativo, el software instalado, los parches y la configuraci\xf3n, lo que lo convierte en un servicio de infraestructura en el que el cliente gestiona la capa de m\xe1quina virtual en lugar de uno totalmente administrado."},{"id":"q036","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes debe proporcionar un usuario de IAM para interactuar con los servicios de AWS mediante la AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI)?","options":{"A":"Claves de acceso","B":"Token secreto","C":"UserID","D":"Nombre de usuario y contrase\xf1a"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Las claves de acceso de IAM, compuestas por un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta, son las credenciales que se utilizan para autenticar solicitudes de API mediante programaci\xf3n a trav\xe9s de la AWS CLI, lo que permite a los usuarios de IAM interactuar con los servicios de AWS desde la l\xednea de comandos sin la AWS Management Console.\\nUn token secreto no es un tipo de credencial est\xe1ndar de IAM para el acceso a la CLI; las credenciales de seguridad temporales emitidas para roles de IAM incluyen un token de sesi\xf3n adem\xe1s de un ID de clave de acceso y un secreto, pero un token secreto independiente por s\xed solo no se utiliza para la autenticaci\xf3n de la CLI. Un UserID es un identificador interno de AWS asignado a los usuarios de IAM, pero no es una credencial que se pueda proporcionar para autenticar solicitudes de la CLI. Un nombre de usuario y una contrase\xf1a son las credenciales que se utilizan para iniciar sesi\xf3n en la AWS Management Console de forma interactiva, no para autenticar solicitudes program\xe1ticas de la CLI, que requieren claves de acceso."},{"id":"q037","question":"Tiene soporte AWS Basic y ha descubierto que algunos recursos de AWS se est\xe1n utilizando de forma maliciosa, y esos recursos podr\xedan potencialmente comprometer sus datos. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer?","options":{"A":"Contactar con el equipo de AWS Customer Service","B":"Contactar con el AWS Abuse Team","C":"Contactar con el equipo de AWS Concierge","D":"Contactar con el equipo de AWS Security"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El AWS Abuse Team gestiona los informes de infraestructura de AWS utilizada para actividades maliciosas, como hacking, spam o uso no autorizado, y este canal est\xe1 disponible para todos los clientes independientemente de su nivel de soporte.\\nEl equipo de AWS Customer Service gestiona consultas generales de cuenta y facturaci\xf3n, y no es el contacto designado para informar sobre el uso malicioso de la infraestructura de AWS. El equipo de AWS Concierge es un recurso de soporte dedicado disponible exclusivamente para clientes del plan de soporte Enterprise para asistencia de facturaci\xf3n y cuenta, y no gestiona informes de abuso o actividad maliciosa. El equipo de AWS Security gestiona la postura de seguridad interna de AWS y el programa de divulgaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades, y no es el canal correcto para que los clientes informen sobre el uso malicioso de recursos de AWS dentro de sus propias cuentas."},{"id":"q038","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son ejemplos de controles compartidos de AWS?","options":{"A":"Gesti\xf3n de parches","B":"Gesti\xf3n de IAM","C":"Gesti\xf3n de VPC","D":"Gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n","E":"Operaciones del centro de datos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"La gesti\xf3n de parches y la gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n son controles compartidos en los que AWS y el cliente gestionan cada uno su propia capa de forma independiente, con AWS responsable de la infraestructura y los clientes responsables de sus propios sistemas operativos y aplicaciones.\\nLa gesti\xf3n de IAM es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes controlan exclusivamente sus propios usuarios, roles y permisos dentro de su cuenta de AWS. La gesti\xf3n de VPC es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes definen y configuran su propia configuraci\xf3n de red virtual, incluidas subredes, tablas de rutas y grupos de seguridad. Las operaciones del centro de datos son responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS y abarcan toda la gesti\xf3n de la infraestructura f\xedsica."},{"id":"q043","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, \xbfcu\xe1l de los siguientes es responsabilidad de AWS?","options":{"A":"Cifrado del lado del cliente","B":"Configurar dispositivos de infraestructura","C":"Cifrado del lado del servidor","D":"Filtrar tr\xe1fico con Security Groups"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Configurar dispositivos de infraestructura, como hardware de red y servidores f\xedsicos, es responsabilidad enteramente de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni control sobre la infraestructura f\xedsica subyacente.\\nEl cifrado del lado del cliente es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes eligen si cifrar los datos y c\xf3mo hacerlo antes de enviarlos a AWS. El cifrado del lado del servidor tambi\xe9n es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes deciden si habilitar el cifrado en reposo para sus datos almacenados en servicios de AWS. Filtrar tr\xe1fico con Security Groups es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes definen y gestionan sus propias reglas de Security Group dentro de su entorno de AWS."},{"id":"q046","question":"Su empresa est\xe1 desarrollando una aplicaci\xf3n web cr\xedtica en AWS, y la seguridad de la aplicaci\xf3n es una prioridad m\xe1xima. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS proporcionar\xe1 recomendaciones de optimizaci\xf3n de seguridad de la infraestructura?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield","B":"AWS Management Console","C":"AWS Secrets Manager","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones en tiempo real en cinco categor\xedas, incluidas seguridad, optimizaci\xf3n de costos, rendimiento, tolerancia a fallas y l\xedmites de servicio, lo que le ayuda a seguir las mejores pr\xe1cticas de AWS.\\nAWS Shield es un servicio administrado de protecci\xf3n DDoS que defiende contra ataques de denegaci\xf3n de servicio distribuido, y no proporciona recomendaciones amplias de seguridad de la infraestructura. AWS Management Console es la interfaz basada en web para acceder y gestionar servicios de AWS, y no analiza ni hace recomendaciones sobre su entorno. AWS Secrets Manager es un servicio para almacenar, rotar y gestionar el acceso a secretos, como credenciales de bases de datos y claves de API, y no proporciona recomendaciones de optimizaci\xf3n de infraestructura."},{"id":"q048","question":"En el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1les de los siguientes son responsabilidad del cliente?","options":{"A":"Desecho de discos","B":"Controlar el acceso f\xedsico a los recursos de c\xf3mputo","C":"Aplicar parches a la infraestructura de red","D":"Establecer reglas de complejidad de contrase\xf1as","E":"Configurar reglas de acceso a la red"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Establecer reglas de complejidad de contrase\xf1as y configurar reglas de acceso a la red, como grupos de seguridad y NACLs, son responsabilidades del cliente, ya que los clientes controlan sus propias pol\xedticas de identidad y configuraci\xf3n de red dentro de su entorno de AWS.\\nEl desecho de discos es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que AWS gestiona la retirada y destrucci\xf3n seguras del hardware de almacenamiento f\xedsico. Controlar el acceso f\xedsico a los recursos de c\xf3mputo es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso a la infraestructura f\xedsica subyacente. Aplicar parches a la infraestructura de red es responsabilidad de AWS y abarca la capa de red administrada que se encuentra debajo de la red virtual del cliente."},{"id":"q056","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS?","options":{"A":"Aplicar parches a la infraestructura subyacente","B":"Seguridad f\xedsica","C":"Aplicar parches a las instancias de Amazon EC2","D":"Aplicar parches a la infraestructura de red"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los clientes son responsables de aplicar parches al sistema operativo y al software de las instancias de EC2 que lanzan, ya que el sistema operativo invitado y todo lo que se ejecuta en \xe9l forman parte del lado del cliente en el Modelo de Responsabilidad Compartida.\\nAplicar parches a la infraestructura subyacente es responsabilidad de AWS, e incluye el hipervisor y el hardware f\xedsico que est\xe1n debajo de las instancias del cliente. La seguridad f\xedsica es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni control sobre los centros de datos de AWS. Aplicar parches a la infraestructura de red es responsabilidad de AWS, e incluye la capa de red administrada que sustenta todas las redes virtuales de los clientes."},{"id":"q057","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfqui\xe9n es responsable de la gesti\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Es responsabilidad \xfanicamente del cliente","B":"Es responsabilidad \xfanicamente de AWS","C":"Es compartida entre AWS y el cliente","D":"No forma parte del modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La gesti\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n es un control compartido en el que AWS gestiona la configuraci\xf3n de su infraestructura, mientras que el cliente es responsable de configurar los sistemas operativos, las bases de datos y las aplicaciones que implementa.\\nLa gesti\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n no es responsabilidad \xfanicamente del cliente, ya que AWS gestiona de forma independiente la configuraci\xf3n de todos los componentes de infraestructura subyacentes, incluidos hardware, redes y servicios administrados. La gesti\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n no es responsabilidad \xfanicamente de AWS, ya que los clientes conservan total responsabilidad de configurar los recursos y las cargas de trabajo que implementan sobre la infraestructura de AWS. La gesti\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n se reconoce expl\xedcitamente como un control compartido en el Modelo de Responsabilidad Compartida de AWS y, por lo tanto, queda plenamente contemplada dentro de \xe9l."},{"id":"q058","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de seguridad reconoce y clasifica autom\xe1ticamente datos sensibles o propiedad intelectual en AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"Amazon Macie","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Shield"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Macie utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para descubrir, clasificar y proteger autom\xe1ticamente datos sensibles, como informaci\xf3n de identificaci\xf3n personal y propiedad intelectual almacenada en Amazon S3, por lo que es el servicio correcto para reconocer y clasificar autom\xe1ticamente datos sensibles.\\nAmazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que monitorea continuamente cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS en busca de actividad maliciosa y comportamiento an\xf3malo mediante aprendizaje autom\xe1tico e inteligencia sobre amenazas, y se centra en detectar amenazas de seguridad en lugar de clasificar datos. Amazon Inspector es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad automatizado que escanea instancias de EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y no clasifica ni descubre datos sensibles almacenados en S3. AWS Shield proporciona protecci\xf3n administrada contra DDoS en las capas de red y transporte, y es un servicio de protecci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico sin capacidad para reconocer o clasificar datos sensibles."},{"id":"q060","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, \xbfcu\xe1les de las siguientes tareas son responsabilidad del cliente de AWS?","options":{"A":"Garantizar que los datos de las aplicaciones est\xe9n cifrados en reposo","B":"Garantizar que los servidores NTP de AWS est\xe9n configurados con la hora correcta","C":"Garantizar que los usuarios hayan recibido capacitaci\xf3n en seguridad sobre el uso de los servicios de AWS","D":"Garantizar que el acceso a los centros de datos est\xe9 restringido","E":"Garantizar que el hardware se deseche correctamente"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Los clientes son responsables de cifrar los datos de sus aplicaciones en reposo y de capacitar a sus usuarios sobre las pr\xe1cticas de seguridad adecuadas al usar los servicios de AWS, y ambas responsabilidades corresponden al lado del cliente en el modelo.\\nGarantizar que los servidores NTP de AWS est\xe9n configurados con la hora correcta es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni control sobre los componentes de infraestructura subyacentes que proporcionan la sincronizaci\xf3n horaria. Garantizar que el acceso a los centros de datos est\xe9 restringido es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, e incluye controles de acceso f\xedsico, vigilancia y protecci\xf3n de las instalaciones que albergan la infraestructura de AWS. Garantizar que el hardware se deseche correctamente es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes nunca toman posesi\xf3n del hardware f\xedsico y AWS gestiona todos los procesos de fin de vida \xfatil de su propio equipo."},{"id":"q064","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a los usuarios identificar los cambios realizados en un recurso a lo largo del tiempo?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Service Catalog","D":"AWS IAM"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Config rastrea y registra continuamente los cambios de configuraci\xf3n en los recursos de AWS a lo largo del tiempo, lo que permite revisar configuraciones hist\xf3ricas e identificar qu\xe9 cambi\xf3 y cu\xe1ndo.\\nAmazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades que escanea instancias de EC2 en busca de hallazgos de seguridad, no un servicio para rastrear cambios de configuraci\xf3n a lo largo del tiempo. AWS Service Catalog permite a las organizaciones crear y gestionar portafolios aprobados de servicios de TI para su implementaci\xf3n, sin relaci\xf3n con el seguimiento de cambios en los recursos. AWS IAM gestiona identidades de usuario, roles y permisos de acceso dentro de una cuenta de AWS y no registra ni rastrea cambios de configuraci\xf3n en los recursos."},{"id":"q075","question":"Las claves de acceso en AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) se utilizan para:","options":{"A":"Iniciar sesi\xf3n en la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS","B":"Realizar llamadas program\xe1ticas a AWS desde las API de AWS","C":"Iniciar sesi\xf3n en instancias de Amazon EC2","D":"Autenticarse en repositorios de AWS CodeCommit"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las claves de acceso de IAM, compuestas por un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta, son las credenciales que se utilizan para autenticar llamadas program\xe1ticas a las API de AWS mediante la CLI, los SDK o solicitudes HTTP directas, lo que permite que las aplicaciones y los scripts interact\xfaen con los servicios de AWS sin usar la consola.\\nIniciar sesi\xf3n en la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS requiere una combinaci\xf3n de nombre de usuario y contrase\xf1a, no claves de acceso, que est\xe1n reservadas para el acceso program\xe1tico y por CLI en lugar de la autenticaci\xf3n en la consola basada en navegador. Iniciar sesi\xf3n en instancias de Amazon EC2 utiliza pares de claves SSH en instancias de Linux o una contrase\xf1a recuperada mediante la consola para instancias de Windows, y es independiente de las claves de acceso de IAM que se usan para la autenticaci\xf3n de API. La autenticaci\xf3n en repositorios de AWS CodeCommit utiliza credenciales de Git o claves SSH configuradas de forma independiente de las claves de acceso de IAM, aunque las claves de acceso se pueden usar con la AWS CLI para interactuar con CodeCommit de forma program\xe1tica."},{"id":"q078","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS se pueden utilizar para recopilar informaci\xf3n sobre la actividad de la cuenta de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Cloud9","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS CloudHSM","E":"Amazon CloudWatch"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y la actividad de la cuenta en una cuenta de AWS, lo que proporciona un registro de auditor\xeda completo de qui\xe9n hizo qu\xe9 y cu\xe1ndo. Amazon CloudWatch recopila m\xe9tricas, registros y datos de eventos de los servicios y recursos de AWS, lo que proporciona visibilidad operativa sobre la actividad y el rendimiento de la cuenta.\\nAmazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y distribuye contenido a los usuarios en ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo, y no recopila informaci\xf3n sobre la actividad de la cuenta. AWS Cloud9 es un entorno de desarrollo integrado basado en la nube para escribir, ejecutar y depurar c\xf3digo, y no tiene capacidad de monitoreo de cuentas ni de registro de actividad. AWS CloudHSM es un servicio de m\xf3dulo de seguridad de hardware que gestiona y almacena claves criptogr\xe1ficas en hardware dedicado, y no es una herramienta para monitorear o recopilar informaci\xf3n sobre la actividad de la cuenta."},{"id":"q090","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de AWS IAM permite a los desarrolladores acceder a los servicios de AWS a trav\xe9s de la AWS CLI?","options":{"A":"Claves de API","B":"Claves de acceso","C":"Nombres de usuario/Contrase\xf1as","D":"Claves SSH"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las claves de acceso de IAM, compuestas por un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta, son las credenciales que se utilizan para autenticar solicitudes program\xe1ticas a los servicios de AWS mediante la AWS CLI y los SDK.\\nClaves de API es un t\xe9rmino informal que no es espec\xedfico de AWS IAM; el acceso program\xe1tico a AWS utiliza claves de acceso compuestas por un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta, y esta etiqueta informal no describe con precisi\xf3n el tipo de credencial. Los nombres de usuario y las contrase\xf1as se utilizan para iniciar sesi\xf3n de forma interactiva en la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS, y no son las credenciales que se usan para el acceso program\xe1tico por CLI o SDK, que requiere claves de acceso. Las claves SSH se utilizan para conectarse de forma segura a instancias de EC2 mediante Secure Shell y no son las credenciales que autentican las solicitudes de la CLI de los desarrolladores a los servicios de AWS."},{"id":"q091","question":"\xbfDe cu\xe1l de las siguientes acciones es totalmente responsable el usuario cuando ejecuta cargas de trabajo en AWS?","options":{"A":"Aplicar parches a los componentes de infraestructura","B":"Mantener los componentes de infraestructura subyacentes","C":"Mantener los controles f\xedsicos y ambientales","D":"Implementar controles para enrutar el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Los clientes son totalmente responsables de implementar controles a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n, como el enrutamiento del tr\xe1fico dentro de sus propios sistemas, ya que estas decisiones se ubican por completo en la capa del cliente del Modelo de responsabilidad compartida.\\nAplicar parches a los componentes de infraestructura es responsabilidad de AWS, y abarca los servidores f\xedsicos, el equipamiento de red y la capa de hipervisor debajo de las cargas de trabajo del cliente. Mantener los componentes de infraestructura subyacentes es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni control sobre el hardware f\xedsico que ejecuta sus cargas de trabajo. Mantener los controles f\xedsicos y ambientales es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, y abarca las instalaciones del centro de datos, la energ\xeda, la refrigeraci\xf3n y la seguridad f\xedsica."},{"id":"q103","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones mejora la seguridad del acceso a la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS Multi-Factor Authentication (AWS MFA)","D":"Security groups","E":"Pol\xedticas de contrase\xf1as"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"MFA agrega un segundo factor de autenticaci\xf3n adem\xe1s de la contrase\xf1a para proteger los inicios de sesi\xf3n en la consola, y las pol\xedticas de contrase\xf1as imponen requisitos de complejidad y rotaci\xf3n. Ambas mejoran directamente la seguridad del acceso a la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS.\\nAWS Secrets Manager almacena, rota y gestiona el acceso a secretos como credenciales de bases de datos y claves de API, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de credenciales en lugar de un mecanismo para mejorar la seguridad de autenticaci\xf3n de la propia consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS. AWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y gestiona certificados SSL/TLS para cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito entre aplicaciones y usuarios, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en el control o la protecci\xf3n del acceso de inicio de sesi\xf3n a la consola. Los security groups act\xfaan como firewalls virtuales que controlan el tr\xe1fico de red entrante y saliente a nivel de instancia de EC2, y operan en la capa de red en lugar de la capa de autenticaci\xf3n de la consola."},{"id":"q105","question":"\xbfPor cu\xe1l proceso de auditor\xeda tiene AWS responsabilidad exclusiva?","options":{"A":"Pol\xedticas de AWS IAM","B":"Seguridad f\xedsica","C":"Pol\xedticas de bucket de Amazon S3","D":"Registros de AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, AWS tiene responsabilidad exclusiva sobre la seguridad f\xedsica de sus centros de datos, incluidos controles como la seguridad perimetral, la vigilancia y la eliminaci\xf3n del hardware, ya que los clientes nunca tienen acceso a las instalaciones f\xedsicas de AWS.\\nLas pol\xedticas de AWS IAM definen permisos para usuarios, grupos y roles dentro de una cuenta de AWS, y crear, gestionar y auditar estas pol\xedticas es responsabilidad totalmente del cliente, ya que reflejan sus propias decisiones de control de acceso. Las pol\xedticas de bucket de Amazon S3 controlan el acceso a los objetos almacenados en los buckets de S3, y configurar y auditar estas pol\xedticas es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que rigen c\xf3mo se accede a los datos propios del cliente y c\xf3mo se comparten. Los registros de AWS CloudTrail registran llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta en un entorno de AWS y, aunque CloudTrail es un servicio gestionado por AWS, habilitarlo, configurar la retenci\xf3n de registros y auditar los registros que produce son responsabilidades del cliente."},{"id":"q107","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes tareas comunes de TI puede cubrir AWS para liberar recursos de TI de la empresa?","options":{"A":"Aplicar parches al software de base de datos","B":"Probar lanzamientos de aplicaciones","C":"Hacer copias de seguridad de bases de datos","D":"Crear el esquema de base de datos","E":"Ejecutar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Aplicar parches al software de base de datos y hacer copias de seguridad de bases de datos son tareas de mantenimiento que AWS gestiona autom\xe1ticamente para servicios gestionados como RDS, lo que libera los recursos de TI de los clientes de estas responsabilidades operativas que consumen tiempo.\\nProbar lanzamientos de aplicaciones es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que requiere conocimiento de la l\xf3gica espec\xedfica de la aplicaci\xf3n, los criterios de aceptaci\xf3n y los requisitos del negocio que solo el cliente posee, lo que la convierte en una tarea que AWS no puede cubrir en nombre del cliente. Crear el esquema de base de datos requiere comprender el modelo de datos del cliente y los requisitos de la aplicaci\xf3n, y es una actividad de dise\xf1o y desarrollo que el cliente debe realizar en lugar de una tarea de servicio gestionado que AWS proporcione. Ejecutar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n es una actividad de seguridad que los clientes realizan en sus propias aplicaciones e infraestructura, y no es una carga operativa que AWS cubra como parte de sus ofertas de servicios gestionados."},{"id":"q114","question":"Si un cliente necesita auditar la gesti\xf3n de cambios de los recursos de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS debe usar?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Config registra continuamente las configuraciones de los recursos y los cambios a lo largo del tiempo, lo que proporciona un registro de auditor\xeda detallado de qu\xe9 cambi\xf3, cu\xe1ndo cambi\xf3 y qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 el cambio, lo que lo convierte en el servicio dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para auditar la gesti\xf3n de cambios de los recursos de AWS.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona tu entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones en materia de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, pero no registra ni audita los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos individuales a lo largo del tiempo. Amazon CloudWatch recopila m\xe9tricas, registros y datos de eventos de servicios y recursos de AWS para proporcionar monitoreo operativo y alertas, y no realiza un seguimiento del historial de configuraci\xf3n de recursos ni de la gesti\xf3n de cambios. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad que busca vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y es una herramienta de pruebas de seguridad en lugar de un servicio de auditor\xeda de cambios de configuraci\xf3n."},{"id":"q117","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes entidades de Identity and Access Management (IAM) est\xe1 asociada con un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta cuando se usa AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI)?","options":{"A":"Grupo de IAM","B":"Usuario de IAM","C":"Rol de IAM","D":"Pol\xedtica de IAM"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los usuarios de IAM son las entidades que tienen claves de acceso, compuestas por un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta, asociadas para el acceso program\xe1tico mediante CLI a los servicios de AWS.\\nUn grupo de IAM es una colecci\xf3n de usuarios de IAM que se utiliza para gestionar permisos compartidos de forma colectiva, y no es una identidad que pueda autenticarse ni a la que se le puedan emitir claves de acceso de ning\xfan tipo. Un rol de IAM utiliza credenciales de seguridad temporales que se emiten y rotan autom\xe1ticamente cuando se asume el rol, y no tiene claves de acceso permanentes asociadas. Una pol\xedtica de IAM es un documento JSON que define permisos al especificar acciones permitidas o denegadas sobre recursos, y es un constructo de permisos en lugar de una identidad que pueda contener claves de acceso o estar asociada a ellas."},{"id":"q118","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, \xbfde cu\xe1l de las siguientes es responsable el cliente?","options":{"A":"Asegurar que las unidades de disco se borren despu\xe9s de su uso","B":"Asegurar que el firmware se actualice en los dispositivos de hardware","C":"Asegurar que los datos est\xe9n cifrados en reposo","D":"Asegurar que los cables de red sean categor\xeda seis o superior"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El cifrado de datos en reposo es responsabilidad del cliente. AWS proporciona las herramientas y los servicios para habilitar el cifrado, pero los clientes deciden si cifrar sus datos y c\xf3mo hacerlo.\\nAsegurar que las unidades de disco se borren despu\xe9s de su uso es responsabilidad de AWS. AWS se encarga de la baja segura y la eliminaci\xf3n del hardware de almacenamiento f\xedsico. Asegurar que el firmware se actualice en los dispositivos de hardware es responsabilidad de AWS. Los clientes no tienen acceso ni control sobre el hardware f\xedsico. Asegurar que los cables de red sean categor\xeda seis o superior es responsabilidad de AWS. La infraestructura de red f\xedsica es gestionada \xedntegramente por AWS."},{"id":"q120","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 componentes de credenciales se requieren para obtener acceso program\xe1tico a una cuenta de AWS?","options":{"A":"Un ID de clave de acceso","B":"Una clave primaria","C":"Una clave de acceso secreta","D":"Un ID de usuario","E":"Una clave secundaria"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"El acceso program\xe1tico a AWS requiere un ID de clave de acceso para identificar a la entidad que realiza la llamada y una clave de acceso secreta para firmar las solicitudes, y en conjunto autentican las llamadas a la API realizadas mediante la CLI o los SDK.\\nUna clave primaria no es un componente v\xe1lido de credenciales de AWS IAM; es un t\xe9rmino de bases de datos para un identificador \xfanico de fila y no tiene ning\xfan rol en la autenticaci\xf3n program\xe1tica de AWS. Un ID de usuario es un identificador interno de AWS asignado a los usuarios de IAM con fines de seguimiento, pero no puede proporcionarse como credencial para autenticar solicitudes program\xe1ticas a la API. Una clave secundaria no es un tipo de credencial reconocido de AWS IAM, y no existe tal concepto en el modelo de autenticaci\xf3n de AWS para acceso program\xe1tico."},{"id":"q121","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un control compartido entre el cliente y AWS?","options":{"A":"Proporcionar una clave para el cifrado del lado del cliente de Amazon S3","B":"Configuraci\xf3n de una instancia de Amazon EC2","C":"Controles ambientales de los centros de datos f\xedsicos de AWS","D":"Concientizaci\xf3n"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La concientizaci\xf3n y la capacitaci\xf3n son un control compartido porque tanto AWS como el cliente deben garantizar de forma independiente que su propio personal comprenda sus respectivas responsabilidades de seguridad, independientemente de lo que haga la otra parte.\\nProporcionar una clave para el cifrado del lado del cliente de Amazon S3 es responsabilidad exclusiva del cliente, ya que AWS no participa en la generaci\xf3n ni la gesti\xf3n de claves del lado del cliente. La configuraci\xf3n de una instancia de Amazon EC2 es responsabilidad exclusiva del cliente, ya que los clientes controlan el sistema operativo invitado y todo lo instalado en \xe9l. Los controles ambientales de los centros de datos f\xedsicos de AWS son responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni participan en la gesti\xf3n de las instalaciones f\xedsicas."},{"id":"q126","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes medidas de seguridad protegen el acceso a una cuenta de AWS?","options":{"A":"Habilitar AWS CloudTrail","B":"Otorgar acceso con privilegios m\xednimos a los usuarios de IAM","C":"Crear un solo usuario de IAM y compartirlo con muchos desarrolladores y usuarios","D":"Habilitar Amazon CloudFront","E":"Activar la autenticaci\xf3n multifactor (MFA) para usuarios privilegiados"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Otorgar acceso con privilegios m\xednimos garantiza que los usuarios de IAM solo tengan los permisos que necesitan, lo que reduce el alcance del impacto de una cuenta comprometida, y activar MFA para usuarios privilegiados a\xf1ade un segundo factor de autenticaci\xf3n cr\xedtico que protege contra el robo de credenciales. Ambos protegen directamente el acceso a una cuenta de AWS.\\nHabilitar AWS CloudTrail registra las llamadas a la API y la actividad de la cuenta para auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y aunque mejora la visibilidad de las acciones de la cuenta a posteriori, no impide el acceso no autorizado y, por lo tanto, no es una medida de seguridad que proteja el acceso. Crear un solo usuario de IAM compartido entre muchos desarrolladores es un antipatr\xf3n de seguridad porque impide la responsabilidad individual, dificulta la rotaci\xf3n de credenciales y significa que una sola credencial comprometida otorga acceso a todos los que comparten esa cuenta. Habilitar Amazon CloudFront proporciona una red de entrega de contenido para distribuir contenido web a nivel global y no tiene relaci\xf3n con la protecci\xf3n del acceso a una cuenta de AWS."},{"id":"q144","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 actividad es responsabilidad del cliente en la nube de AWS seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS?","options":{"A":"Garantizar la conectividad de red desde AWS hacia internet","B":"Aplicar parches y corregir fallos dentro de la infraestructura de la nube de AWS","C":"Garantizar la seguridad f\xedsica de los centros de datos en la nube","D":"Garantizar que los vol\xfamenes de Amazon EBS est\xe9n respaldados"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, respaldar vol\xfamenes de EBS mediante instant\xe1neas es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que AWS no respalda autom\xe1ticamente los vol\xfamenes de EBS en nombre del cliente.\\nGarantizar la conectividad de red desde AWS hacia internet es responsabilidad enteramente de AWS, ya que AWS gestiona y mantiene la infraestructura de red subyacente que conecta sus servicios a la internet p\xfablica. Aplicar parches y corregir fallos dentro de la infraestructura de la nube de AWS es responsabilidad enteramente de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso al hardware subyacente, a los hipervisores ni a los componentes de red f\xedsica. Garantizar la seguridad f\xedsica de los centros de datos en la nube es responsabilidad enteramente de AWS, e incluye controles de acceso, vigilancia y protecci\xf3n de las instalaciones que albergan la infraestructura de AWS."},{"id":"q150","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puede un cliente aumentar la seguridad de los inicios de sesi\xf3n en la cuenta de AWS?","options":{"A":"Configurar AWS Certificate Manager","B":"Habilitar la autenticaci\xf3n multifactor (MFA)","C":"Usar Amazon Cognito para gestionar el acceso","D":"Configurar una pol\xedtica de contrase\xf1as seguras","E":"Habilitar AWS Organizations"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Habilitar MFA requiere un segundo factor adem\xe1s de la contrase\xf1a para completar el inicio de sesi\xf3n, y configurar una pol\xedtica de contrase\xf1as seguras impone requisitos de complejidad, longitud y rotaci\xf3n. Ambos fortalecen directamente la seguridad de los inicios de sesi\xf3n en la cuenta de AWS.\\nAWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y gestiona certificados SSL/TLS para cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito entre aplicaciones y usuarios, y no tiene ninguna funci\xf3n en el control o la protecci\xf3n de los procesos de inicio de sesi\xf3n de cuentas. Amazon Cognito gestiona la autenticaci\xf3n y el acceso de usuarios para aplicaciones orientadas al cliente mediante grupos de usuarios y grupos de identidades, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la gesti\xf3n de usuarios a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n en lugar de proteger directamente los inicios de sesi\xf3n en cuentas de AWS. AWS Organizations es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de cuentas para gobernar y consolidar de forma centralizada m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS, y no proporciona por s\xed mismo funciones de seguridad de inicio de sesi\xf3n para el acceso a cuentas individuales."},{"id":"q158","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se utiliza para rastrear, registrar y auditar los cambios de configuraci\xf3n realizados en los recursos de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Config registra continuamente los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS y mantiene un historial de esos cambios, lo que permite revisiones de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento sobre c\xf3mo se han configurado los recursos a lo largo del tiempo.\\nAWS Shield es un servicio gestionado de protecci\xf3n contra DDoS que salvaguarda las aplicaciones de AWS frente a ataques de denegaci\xf3n de servicio distribuido y no tiene capacidad para rastrear o auditar cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos. AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) es un servicio para controlar qui\xe9n puede autenticarse y qu\xe9 acciones est\xe1 autorizado a realizar, y gestiona permisos e identidades en lugar de registrar cambios de configuraci\xf3n en los recursos de AWS. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad que analiza instancias de EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y es una herramienta de pruebas de seguridad en lugar de un servicio de seguimiento o auditor\xeda de cambios de configuraci\xf3n."},{"id":"q162","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios cae bajo la responsabilidad del cliente de mantener la configuraci\xf3n del sistema operativo, la aplicaci\xf3n de parches de seguridad y las tareas de red?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon EC2","C":"Amazon ElastiCache","D":"AWS Fargate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Con Amazon EC2, el cliente es responsable del sistema operativo invitado, incluidos la configuraci\xf3n, la aplicaci\xf3n de parches de seguridad y la configuraci\xf3n de red, ya que AWS solo gestiona la infraestructura f\xedsica subyacente.\\nAmazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional totalmente gestionado en el que AWS se encarga de la aplicaci\xf3n de parches del sistema operativo, las actualizaciones del motor de base de datos y el mantenimiento de la infraestructura en nombre del cliente. Amazon ElastiCache es un servicio de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria totalmente gestionado en el que AWS gestiona el sistema operativo y la infraestructura subyacentes, lo que elimina por completo esas responsabilidades del cliente. AWS Fargate es un motor de c\xf3mputo sin servidor para contenedores en el que AWS gestiona la infraestructura y el sistema operativo subyacentes, lo que significa que el cliente solo necesita gestionar sus im\xe1genes de contenedor y el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q163","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes m\xe9todos admite AWS para a\xf1adir seguridad a los usuarios de Identity and Access Management (IAM)?","options":{"A":"Implementar Amazon Rekognition","B":"Usar recursos protegidos por AWS Shield","C":"Bloquear el acceso con Security Groups","D":"Usar la autenticaci\xf3n multifactor (MFA)","E":"Aplicar la fortaleza y la expiraci\xf3n de contrase\xf1as"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"MFA a\xf1ade un segundo factor de autenticaci\xf3n adem\xe1s de una contrase\xf1a, y aplicar pol\xedticas de fortaleza y expiraci\xf3n de contrase\xf1as reduce el riesgo de compromiso de credenciales. Ambos son m\xe9todos de seguridad de IAM admitidos que protegen directamente las cuentas de usuario de IAM.\\nAmazon Rekognition es un servicio de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para analizar im\xe1genes y videos y detectar objetos, rostros y texto, y no tiene ninguna funci\xf3n en a\xf1adir seguridad a la autenticaci\xf3n de usuarios de IAM. AWS Shield proporciona protecci\xf3n gestionada contra DDoS en las capas de red y transporte, y es un servicio de protecci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico en lugar de un mecanismo para proteger cuentas de usuario de IAM. Los grupos de seguridad act\xfaan como firewalls virtuales que controlan el tr\xe1fico de red entrante y saliente a nivel de instancia de EC2, y operan en la capa de red en lugar de la capa de autenticaci\xf3n de IAM."},{"id":"q169","question":"Un Cloud Practitioner debe determinar si se han aprovisionado grupos de seguridad en una cuenta de AWS para permitir acceso sin restricciones a puertos espec\xedficos. \xbfCu\xe1l es la forma M\xc1S SENCILLA de hacerlo?","options":{"A":"Revisar las reglas de entrada de cada grupo de seguridad en la consola de administraci\xf3n de Amazon EC2 para comprobar si hay 0.0.0.0/0 en el puerto","B":"Ejecutar AWS Trusted Advisor y revisar los hallazgos","C":"Abrir la consola de AWS IAM y comprobar los filtros de reglas de entrada para acceso abierto","D":"En AWS Config, crear una regla personalizada que invoque una funci\xf3n de AWS Lambda para revisar las reglas de firewall para acceso entrante"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor escanea autom\xe1ticamente su cuenta y marca los grupos de seguridad con acceso sin restricciones (0.0.0.0/0) a puertos espec\xedficos como parte de sus comprobaciones de la categor\xeda de seguridad, lo que lo convierte en el enfoque m\xe1s sencillo y directo sin investigaci\xf3n manual.\\nRevisar manualmente las reglas de entrada de cada grupo de seguridad en la consola de EC2 para buscar entradas 0.0.0.0/0 funcionar\xeda, pero consume tiempo y es propenso a errores a escala, lo que lo hace mucho menos sencillo que usar la comprobaci\xf3n automatizada de Trusted Advisor. La consola de AWS IAM gestiona identidades, usuarios, roles y permisos, y no contiene configuraciones de grupos de seguridad ni reglas de tr\xe1fico entrante, por lo que es el lugar equivocado para comprobar el acceso sin restricciones a puertos. Crear una regla personalizada de AWS Config que invoque una funci\xf3n de Lambda para revisar las reglas de firewall proporcionar\xeda comprobaciones de cumplimiento continuas automatizadas, pero requiere un esfuerzo de configuraci\xf3n significativo, lo que la hace mucho m\xe1s compleja que ejecutar la comprobaci\xf3n existente de Trusted Advisor."},{"id":"q170","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios relacionados con la seguridad ofrece AWS?","options":{"A":"Tokens f\xedsicos de autenticaci\xf3n multifactor","B":"Comprobaciones de seguridad de AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"Cifrado de datos","D":"Pruebas de penetraci\xf3n automatizadas","E":"Detecci\xf3n de contenido protegido por derechos de autor en Amazon S3"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor incluye comprobaciones de seguridad que se\xf1alan vulnerabilidades como grupos de seguridad abiertos, y AWS proporciona capacidades de cifrado de datos mediante servicios como KMS y cifrado nativo en servicios de almacenamiento y bases de datos. Ambas son ofertas genuinas de AWS relacionadas con la seguridad.\\nLos tokens f\xedsicos de autenticaci\xf3n multifactor son dispositivos MFA de hardware que los clientes compran de forma independiente, no un servicio proporcionado por AWS; los clientes los configuran con sus cuentas, pero AWS no suministra ni fabrica los tokens de hardware. Las pruebas de penetraci\xf3n automatizadas no son una oferta de servicio de AWS; AWS permite a los clientes realizar sus propias pruebas de penetraci\xf3n en servicios permitidos, pero AWS no realiza pruebas de penetraci\xf3n automatizadas en las cargas de trabajo de los clientes como servicio. La detecci\xf3n de contenido protegido por derechos de autor en Amazon S3 no es un servicio de AWS; Amazon Macie detecta categor\xedas de datos sensibles, como PII, en S3, pero la detecci\xf3n de derechos de autor no est\xe1 dentro del alcance de los servicios de seguridad disponibles de AWS."},{"id":"q171","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes servicios tienen caracter\xedsticas de mitigaci\xf3n de ataques de denegaci\xf3n de servicio distribuida (DDoS)?","options":{"A":"AWS WAF","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon EC2","D":"Amazon CloudFront","E":"Amazon Inspector"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS WAF filtra tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS malicioso en la capa de aplicaci\xf3n al bloquear solicitudes que coinciden con reglas definidas, y Amazon CloudFront se integra con AWS Shield para absorber ataques DDoS volum\xe9tricos en ubicaciones de borde antes de que lleguen a los servidores de origen. Ambos proporcionan directamente caracter\xedsticas de mitigaci\xf3n de DDoS.\\nAmazon DynamoDB es un servicio de base de datos NoSQL totalmente administrado y, aunque AWS protege la infraestructura subyacente en la que se ejecuta, DynamoDB en s\xed no es un servicio que proporcione caracter\xedsticas de mitigaci\xf3n de DDoS. Amazon EC2 proporciona instancias de computaci\xf3n virtuales y no incluye mitigaci\xf3n de DDoS integrada; los clientes pueden configurar grupos de seguridad y usar servicios adicionales como Shield y WAF para proteger las aplicaciones que se ejecutan en EC2. Amazon Inspector es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad automatizado que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y no proporciona capacidades de mitigaci\xf3n de DDoS."},{"id":"q175","question":"\xbfA qu\xe9 pueden acceder los usuarios desde AWS Artifact?","options":{"A":"Documentos de seguridad y cumplimiento de AWS","B":"Una descarga de los detalles de gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n de todos los recursos de AWS","C":"Materiales de capacitaci\xf3n para servicios de AWS","D":"Una evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad de las aplicaciones implementadas en AWS Cloud"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact es un portal de autoservicio que proporciona acceso bajo demanda a documentos de seguridad y cumplimiento de AWS, como informes SOC, certificaciones ISO, atestaciones PCI y otros materiales de cumplimiento emitidos por auditores.\\nLos detalles de gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n no est\xe1n disponibles en Artifact; AWS Config rastrea el historial de configuraci\xf3n de recursos y CloudTrail registra la actividad de API, pero no se accede a ninguno de los dos a trav\xe9s de Artifact. Los materiales de capacitaci\xf3n provienen de AWS Training and Certification y AWS Skill Builder, no de Artifact, que solo contiene documentos de cumplimiento. Amazon Inspector realiza una evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad de las aplicaciones implementadas, que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades, no Artifact."},{"id":"q179","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes puede limitar el acceso al bucket de Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) a usuarios espec\xedficos?","options":{"A":"Un par de claves p\xfablica y privada","B":"Amazon Inspector","C":"Pol\xedticas de AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","D":"Grupos de seguridad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las pol\xedticas de IAM se pueden aplicar directamente a usuarios o grupos de IAM para controlar exactamente qui\xe9n puede acceder a un bucket de S3 y qu\xe9 operaciones puede realizar, lo que proporciona un control de acceso detallado que limita el acceso a usuarios espec\xedficos.\\nIniciar sesi\xf3n con credenciales de la cuenta de AWS proporciona acceso de nivel ra\xedz a todos los buckets de S3 de forma predeterminada en lugar de limitar el acceso a usuarios espec\xedficos; las credenciales ra\xedz nunca deben usarse para el acceso diario y no restringen el acceso al bucket a personas concretas. Habilitar el versionado de S3 conserva varias versiones de objetos para proteger contra la eliminaci\xf3n accidental, y es una caracter\xedstica de recuperaci\xf3n de datos en lugar de un mecanismo de restricci\xf3n de acceso que limite qu\xe9 usuarios pueden acceder al bucket. Establecer una pol\xedtica de bucket para hacer privado el bucket elimina el acceso p\xfablico, pero sin especificar principales permitidos no otorga acceso a ning\xfan usuario espec\xedfico; se requieren declaraciones de pol\xedtica adicionales que identifiquen principales de IAM concretos para limitar el acceso a usuarios espec\xedficos."},{"id":"q181","question":"\xbfD\xf3nde se encuentran los documentos de cumplimiento de AWS, como un informe SOC 1?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Artifact","D":"AWS Certificate Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Artifact es el portal de autoservicio dedicado para acceder a la documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento de AWS, incluidos informes SOC 1, SOC 2, ISO 27001 y PCI DSS, lo que permite a los clientes descargar estos documentos para sus propios fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento.\\nAmazon Inspector es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad automatizado que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y riesgos de exposici\xf3n de red, y no proporciona ni almacena certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS ni informes SOC. AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y los eventos de administraci\xf3n en una cuenta de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda, y produce registros de actividad espec\xedficos del cliente en lugar de certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS o informes de auditor\xeda de terceros. AWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y administra certificados SSL/TLS para su uso con servicios de AWS a fin de cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de certificados en lugar de un repositorio de informes de cumplimiento."},{"id":"q182","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes tareas es responsabilidad de AWS?","options":{"A":"Cifrar datos del lado del cliente","B":"Configurar roles de AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","C":"Proteger el hipervisor de Amazon EC2","D":"Establecer pol\xedticas de contrase\xf1as de usuarios"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, AWS es responsable de proteger el hipervisor subyacente que ejecuta las m\xe1quinas virtuales de EC2, ya que forma parte de la capa de infraestructura a la que los clientes no tienen acceso ni control.\\nCifrar datos del lado del cliente es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que AWS no participa en los datos que se cifran antes de enviarse a los servicios de AWS. Configurar roles de IAM es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes definen y administran los permisos que controlan qu\xe9 acciones pueden realizar los usuarios y servicios dentro de su cuenta. Establecer pol\xedticas de contrase\xf1as de usuarios es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes controlan las pol\xedticas de contrase\xf1as de IAM que rigen c\xf3mo se autentican sus usuarios en AWS Management Console."},{"id":"q183","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, \xbfcu\xe1les de las siguientes \xe1reas son responsabilidad del cliente?","options":{"A":"Actualizaciones de firmware de la infraestructura de red","B":"Aplicaci\xf3n de parches a los sistemas operativos","C":"Aplicaci\xf3n de parches al hipervisor subyacente","D":"Seguridad f\xedsica de los centros de datos","E":"Configuraci\xf3n del grupo de seguridad"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Los clientes son responsables de aplicar parches a los sistemas operativos que se ejecutan en sus instancias EC2 y de configurar grupos de seguridad para controlar el tr\xe1fico entrante y saliente dentro de su entorno de AWS, y ambas responsabilidades recaen en el lado del cliente del modelo.\\nLas actualizaciones de firmware de la infraestructura de red son responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni control sobre el equipo de red f\xedsico que sustenta AWS Cloud. La aplicaci\xf3n de parches al hipervisor subyacente es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que la capa de virtualizaci\xf3n se encuentra debajo del sistema operativo y la administra exclusivamente AWS. La seguridad f\xedsica de los centros de datos es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, e incluye controles de acceso, vigilancia y protecci\xf3n de las instalaciones que albergan la infraestructura de AWS."},{"id":"q190","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de seguridad de AWS protege las aplicaciones contra ataques de denegaci\xf3n de servicio distribuida con detecci\xf3n siempre activa y mitigaciones autom\xe1ticas en l\xednea?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF)","C":"Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)","D":"AWS Shield"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Shield proporciona protecci\xf3n DDoS siempre activa sin costo adicional para todos los clientes de AWS, y detecta y mitiga autom\xe1ticamente los ataques DDoS volum\xe9tricos y basados en protocolos m\xe1s comunes sin necesidad de configuraci\xf3n por parte del cliente.\\nAWS WAF es un firewall de aplicaciones web que filtra tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS en la capa de aplicaci\xf3n seg\xfan reglas configurables y, aunque ayuda a mitigar ataques en la capa de aplicaci\xf3n, es un servicio separado de Shield y requiere configuraci\xf3n del cliente en lugar de proporcionar protecci\xf3n autom\xe1tica siempre activa. Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para identificar actividad maliciosa y comportamiento an\xf3malo en cuentas de AWS, y es un servicio de alerta y detecci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio que bloquee activamente tr\xe1fico DDoS. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no tiene capacidad para detectar ni proteger contra ataques de denegaci\xf3n de servicio distribuida."},{"id":"q199","question":"Una empresa se ha mudado a AWS recientemente. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS ayudar\xe1 a garantizar que tengan la configuraci\xf3n de seguridad adecuada?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"Amazon Inspector","C":"Amazon SNS","D":"Amazon CloudWatch","E":"Concierge Support Team"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona comprobaciones de seguridad automatizadas que identifican vulnerabilidades como el acceso no restringido a grupos de seguridad y la falta de MFA, y Amazon Inspector realiza evaluaciones de seguridad automatizadas de instancias EC2 para identificar vulnerabilidades y acceso de red no intencionado. Ambos ayudan a garantizar la configuraci\xf3n de seguridad adecuada.\\nAmazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda de publicaci\xf3n/suscripci\xf3n totalmente administrado para enviar notificaciones a los suscriptores, y no eval\xfaa ni garantiza la configuraci\xf3n o los ajustes de seguridad de AWS. Amazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas y registros operativos para observabilidad y alertas, y aunque puede activar notificaciones sobre m\xe9tricas espec\xedficas o patrones de registro, no eval\xfaa ni valida activamente las configuraciones de seguridad de los recursos de AWS. El Concierge Support Team ayuda a los clientes del plan de soporte Enterprise con la facturaci\xf3n y la gesti\xf3n de cuentas, y no realiza evaluaciones t\xe9cnicas de seguridad ni revisa la configuraci\xf3n de seguridad de los recursos."},{"id":"q200","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la caracter\xedstica de AWS que proporciona un nivel adicional de seguridad por encima del mecanismo de autenticaci\xf3n predeterminado de nombres de usuario y contrase\xf1as?","options":{"A":"Claves cifradas","B":"Verificaci\xf3n por correo electr\xf3nico","C":"AWS KMS","D":"AWS MFA"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Multi-Factor Authentication agrega una segunda capa de seguridad al requerir un c\xf3digo de un solo uso de un dispositivo de hardware o virtual, adem\xe1s de un nombre de usuario y una contrase\xf1a, lo que proporciona un nivel adicional de seguridad por encima del mecanismo predeterminado de nombre de usuario y contrase\xf1a.\\nLas claves cifradas se refieren a claves criptogr\xe1ficas utilizadas con fines de cifrado de datos, como las administradas por AWS KMS, y aunque protegen los datos almacenados, no proporcionan un mecanismo de autenticaci\xf3n adicional para la seguridad del inicio de sesi\xf3n del usuario. La verificaci\xf3n por correo electr\xf3nico se utiliza para confirmar la propiedad de una direcci\xf3n de correo electr\xf3nico durante el flujo de configuraci\xf3n de la cuenta o de recuperaci\xf3n de contrase\xf1a, y no es una capa de seguridad adicional continua aplicada sobre el nombre de usuario y la contrase\xf1a para los inicios de sesi\xf3n en la consola de AWS. AWS KMS es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de claves para crear y administrar claves criptogr\xe1ficas utilizadas para cifrar datos en reposo y en tr\xe1nsito, y no agrega factores de autenticaci\xf3n al proceso de inicio de sesi\xf3n."},{"id":"q204","question":"Seg\xfan la AWS Acceptable Use Policy, \xbfcu\xe1l de las siguientes afirmaciones es verdadera con respecto a las pruebas de penetraci\xf3n de instancias EC2?","options":{"A":"Las pruebas de penetraci\xf3n no est\xe1n permitidas en AWS","B":"AWS realiza autom\xe1ticamente pruebas de penetraci\xf3n para determinar vulnerabilidades en su infraestructura de AWS","C":"El cliente puede realizar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n en sus propias instancias sin autorizaci\xf3n previa de AWS","D":"Los clientes de AWS solo pueden realizar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n en servicios administrados por AWS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La AWS Acceptable Use Policy permite a los clientes realizar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n en sus propias instancias EC2 y en ciertos otros servicios permitidos sin autorizaci\xf3n previa de AWS, siempre que las pruebas cumplan con las condiciones de la AUP.\\nLas pruebas de penetraci\xf3n no est\xe1n prohibidas en AWS; la Acceptable Use Policy permite expl\xedcitamente a los clientes realizar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n en sus propios recursos dentro de las directrices definidas, por lo que la afirmaci\xf3n de que no est\xe1n permitidas es incorrecta. AWS no realiza pruebas de penetraci\xf3n automatizadas en nombre de los clientes; Amazon Inspector realiza evaluaciones de seguridad automatizadas para vulnerabilidades y exposici\xf3n de red, pero esto no es lo mismo que las pruebas de penetraci\xf3n, y AWS no realiza pruebas adversarias de la infraestructura de los clientes. Se permite a los clientes de AWS realizar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n en sus propios servicios y recursos, no solo en servicios administrados por AWS; la restricci\xf3n es que las pruebas deben dirigirse a los recursos propios del cliente en lugar de a la infraestructura compartida de AWS."},{"id":"q208","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1les de las siguientes son responsabilidad del cliente?","options":{"A":"Gestionar los eventos ambientales de los centros de datos de AWS","B":"Proteger la confidencialidad de los datos en tr\xe1nsito en Amazon S3","C":"Controlar el acceso f\xedsico a las Regiones de AWS","D":"Garantizar que el host EC2 subyacente est\xe9 configurado correctamente","E":"Aplicar parches a las aplicaciones instaladas en Amazon EC2"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Los clientes son responsables de proteger la confidencialidad de los datos en tr\xe1nsito mediante el uso de cifrado, como HTTPS/TLS, en las conexiones a Amazon S3, y de aplicar parches a los sistemas operativos y las aplicaciones instaladas en las instancias Amazon EC2 como parte de la gesti\xf3n de su entorno invitado.\\nGestionar los eventos ambientales en los centros de datos de AWS, incluidos la energ\xeda, la refrigeraci\xf3n, la supresi\xf3n de incendios y la respuesta ante desastres naturales, es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS como parte de la protecci\xf3n de la infraestructura f\xedsica con la que los clientes nunca interact\xfaan. Controlar el acceso f\xedsico a las Regiones de AWS y a las instalaciones de los centros de datos es responsabilidad de AWS; los clientes no tienen acceso a la infraestructura f\xedsica y dependen de AWS para implementar la entrada mediante tarjetas de identificaci\xf3n, los controles biom\xe9tricos y el personal de seguridad. Garantizar que el host EC2 subyacente est\xe9 configurado correctamente corresponde a la responsabilidad de AWS; los clientes administran el sistema operativo invitado y las capas superiores, mientras que AWS es responsable del hipervisor, el hardware del host y la capa de virtualizaci\xf3n subyacente."},{"id":"q211","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es equivalente a un nombre de usuario y contrase\xf1a y se utiliza para autenticar su acceso program\xe1tico a los servicios y las API de AWS?","options":{"A":"Contrase\xf1a de instancia","B":"Pares de claves","C":"Claves de acceso","D":"MFA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las claves de acceso de AWS, compuestas por un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta, son las credenciales utilizadas para autenticar llamadas program\xe1ticas a la API, y funcionan como un nombre de usuario y una contrase\xf1a para los SDK de AWS y la CLI.\\nUna contrase\xf1a de instancia se refiere a la contrase\xf1a de administrador de Windows generada para las instancias EC2 Windows para habilitar el acceso RDP, la cual es una credencial de inicio de sesi\xf3n del servidor en lugar de un mecanismo de autenticaci\xf3n de API equivalente a un nombre de usuario y contrase\xf1a. Los pares de claves son combinaciones de clave p\xfablica/privada RSA utilizadas para autenticar conexiones SSH a instancias Linux EC2, y aunque cumplen una funci\xf3n de autenticaci\xf3n, son para acceso SSH a nivel de instancia en lugar de autenticaci\xf3n de API y servicios de AWS. MFA es un factor de autenticaci\xf3n adicional agregado sobre las credenciales principales, como un nombre de usuario y una contrase\xf1a, que requiere un c\xf3digo de un solo uso de un dispositivo, y es un mecanismo complementario en lugar de un par de credenciales independiente equivalente a un nombre de usuario y contrase\xf1a."},{"id":"q213","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio de AWS que le permite administrar todas sus cuentas de AWS desde una \xfanica cuenta maestra?","options":{"A":"AWS WAF","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Organizations","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Organizations le permite crear una jerarqu\xeda de cuentas de AWS administradas desde una \xfanica cuenta maestra, aplicando pol\xedticas y facturaci\xf3n consolidada en todas las cuentas miembro.\\nAWS WAF es un firewall de aplicaciones web que filtra y bloquea el tr\xe1fico web malicioso mediante conjuntos de reglas administrados y personalizados, y no tiene ninguna funci\xf3n en la administraci\xf3n de m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS. AWS Trusted Advisor analiza su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones en materia de optimizaci\xf3n de costos, seguridad y rendimiento, pero no proporciona administraci\xf3n centralizada de m\xfaltiples cuentas. AWS Config supervisa y registra continuamente las configuraciones de los recursos de AWS con fines de cumplimiento, pero opera dentro de cuentas individuales en lugar de proporcionar administraci\xf3n centralizada de m\xfaltiples cuentas."},{"id":"q219","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios puede ayudar a proteger sus aplicaciones web contra la inyecci\xf3n SQL y otras vulnerabilidades en el c\xf3digo de su aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Amazon Cognito","B":"AWS IAM","C":"Amazon Aurora","D":"AWS WAF"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS WAF es un firewall de aplicaciones web que le permite crear reglas para inspeccionar y filtrar el tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS, bloqueando exploits web comunes, como la inyecci\xf3n SQL y el cross-site scripting, antes de que lleguen a su aplicaci\xf3n.\\nAmazon Cognito es un servicio de identidad y autenticaci\xf3n de usuarios para aplicaciones web y m\xf3viles que gestiona el registro, el inicio de sesi\xf3n y el control de acceso de los usuarios de la aplicaci\xf3n, y no inspecciona ni filtra el tr\xe1fico HTTP en busca de inyecci\xf3n SQL u otros exploits web. AWS IAM administra los permisos de los usuarios y el acceso a los servicios y recursos de AWS, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de identidades y accesos en lugar de una herramienta de inspecci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico que pueda bloquear solicitudes web maliciosas. Amazon Aurora es un servicio de base de datos relacional compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL, y es una oferta de base de datos administrada en lugar de un servicio de seguridad para filtrar el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones web."},{"id":"q221","question":"Seg\xfan el AWS Shared Responsibility Model, \xbfcu\xe1les de las siguientes son responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS?","options":{"A":"Monitorear el rendimiento de la red","B":"Instalar software en las instancias EC2","C":"Crear hipervisores","D":"Configurar listas de control de acceso (ACLs)","E":"Mantenimiento del hardware"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Crear y administrar hipervisores y mantener el hardware f\xedsico es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni control sobre la capa de virtualizaci\xf3n ni sobre la infraestructura subyacente que impulsa los servicios de AWS.\\nMonitorear el rendimiento de la red para sus propias aplicaciones y cargas de trabajo es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que AWS proporciona las herramientas, pero los clientes son responsables de observar y responder al rendimiento dentro de su entorno. Instalar software en instancias EC2 es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes administran todo lo que est\xe1 por encima del hipervisor, incluidos el sistema operativo, las aplicaciones y cualquier software que elijan ejecutar. Configurar listas de control de acceso es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes definen y administran sus propias reglas de acceso a la red dentro de su entorno de VPC."},{"id":"q223","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son las credenciales de seguridad predeterminadas que se requieren para acceder a la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS para una cuenta de usuario de IAM?","options":{"A":"MFA","B":"Tokens de seguridad","C":"Un nombre de usuario y una contrase\xf1a","D":"Claves de acceso"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El acceso a la consola de los usuarios de IAM se protege con una combinaci\xf3n de nombre de usuario y contrase\xf1a como credenciales predeterminadas, lo que proporciona la autenticaci\xf3n b\xe1sica necesaria para acceder a la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS.\\nMFA es un factor de autenticaci\xf3n adicional opcional que se puede superponer a un nombre de usuario y contrase\xf1a para exigir un c\xf3digo de un solo uso de un dispositivo, pero no es el requisito de credencial predeterminado y se configura por separado del nombre de usuario y la contrase\xf1a principales. Los tokens de seguridad son credenciales temporales que consisten en un ID de clave de acceso, una clave de acceso secreta y un token de sesi\xf3n que se emiten cuando se asume un rol de IAM, y se utilizan para acceso program\xe1tico y basado en roles en lugar de como credenciales de consola predeterminadas para cuentas de usuario de IAM. Las claves de acceso, que consisten en un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta, son las credenciales utilizadas para el acceso program\xe1tico a la API y la CLI, no para iniciar sesi\xf3n en la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS, que utiliza nombre de usuario y contrase\xf1a."},{"id":"q226","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes aspectos de seguridad son gestionados por AWS?","options":{"A":"Cifrado de vol\xfamenes de EBS","B":"Seguridad de VPC","C":"Permisos de acceso","D":"Aplicaci\xf3n de parches de hardware","E":"Protecci\xf3n de la infraestructura f\xedsica global"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"La aplicaci\xf3n de parches de hardware y el mantenimiento de los servidores f\xedsicos que ejecutan los servicios de AWS, y la protecci\xf3n de la infraestructura f\xedsica global, incluidos centros de datos, energ\xeda, refrigeraci\xf3n y redes, son responsabilidad completamente de AWS seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida.\\nEl cifrado de vol\xfamenes de EBS es una responsabilidad del cliente que implica habilitar el cifrado de EBS a nivel de volumen o de cuenta y seleccionar las claves de KMS adecuadas para proteger los datos en reposo en vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque. La seguridad de VPC, incluida la configuraci\xf3n de grupos de seguridad, ACL de red y tablas de rutas, es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que gobierna c\xf3mo fluye el tr\xe1fico de red dentro y hacia la nube privada virtual del propio cliente. Los permisos de acceso, incluida la creaci\xf3n de usuarios, grupos y roles de IAM y la vinculaci\xf3n de pol\xedticas que controlan qui\xe9n puede acceder a qu\xe9 servicios y recursos de AWS, son responsabilidad completamente del cliente definirlos y gestionarlos."},{"id":"q229","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son las herramientas de gesti\xf3n de cambios que ayudan a los clientes de AWS a auditar y monitorear todos los cambios de recursos en su entorno de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"AWS Transit Gateway","D":"AWS X-Ray","E":"AWS Config"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y los cambios de recursos para auditor\xeda, y AWS Config monitorea y registra continuamente los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos y los eval\xfaa con respecto a reglas de cumplimiento. Juntas son las principales herramientas de gesti\xf3n de cambios y auditor\xeda en AWS.\\nAmazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para extraer informaci\xf3n como entidades, sentimiento y frases clave de texto, y es un servicio de anal\xedtica de IA sin capacidad para auditar o monitorear cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos. AWS Transit Gateway conecta VPC y redes locales a trav\xe9s de un concentrador central, proporcionando enrutamiento y conectividad de red en lugar de funcionalidad de auditor\xeda o gesti\xf3n de cambios. AWS X-Ray es un servicio de seguimiento del rendimiento de aplicaciones que analiza las solicitudes a medida que fluyen por aplicaciones distribuidas, y es una herramienta de depuraci\xf3n y rendimiento en lugar de un servicio de auditor\xeda de cambios de infraestructura."},{"id":"q231","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios ayudar\xe1 a las empresas a garantizar el cumplimiento en AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Migration Hub","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra toda la actividad de la API y los eventos de la cuenta, creando un rastro de auditor\xeda completo que ayuda a las organizaciones a demostrar el cumplimiento de los requisitos regulatorios e investigar incidentes de seguridad.\\nAmazon CloudFront es una red global de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y distribuye contenido web a los usuarios con baja latencia a trav\xe9s de ubicaciones de borde, y es un servicio de rendimiento y distribuci\xf3n sin capacidad para registrar rastros de auditor\xeda o ayudar con el cumplimiento regulatorio. AWS Migration Hub proporciona un panel central para realizar el seguimiento del progreso de las migraciones de aplicaciones a AWS, y es una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de migraciones en lugar de un servicio de cumplimiento o auditor\xeda. Amazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas, registros y eventos operativos de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS, proporcionando visibilidad del rendimiento y la salud operativa, pero no crea el rastro de auditor\xeda completo de la API necesario para el cumplimiento regulatorio."},{"id":"q236","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes caracter\xedsticas de seguridad de AWS est\xe1 asociada con una instancia de EC2 y funciona para filtrar solicitudes de tr\xe1fico entrante?","options":{"A":"AWS X-Ray","B":"Network ACL","C":"Security Groups","D":"VPC Flow logs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los Security Groups act\xfaan como un firewall virtual con estado asociado directamente a las instancias de EC2, evaluando y filtrando el tr\xe1fico de entrada y salida seg\xfan reglas de protocolo, puerto y origen o destino definidas por el cliente.\\nAWS X-Ray es un servicio de seguimiento y depuraci\xf3n de aplicaciones que analiza las solicitudes a medida que fluyen a trav\xe9s de aplicaciones distribuidas para identificar cuellos de botella de rendimiento y errores, y no tiene capacidad para filtrar o controlar el tr\xe1fico de red hacia las instancias de EC2. Las Network ACLs son firewalls sin estado que operan a nivel de subred en lugar del nivel de instancia de EC2, aplicando reglas a todo el tr\xe1fico que entra o sale de una subred sin realizar seguimiento del estado de la conexi\xf3n, y no est\xe1n asociadas directamente con instancias individuales. Los VPC Flow Logs capturan metadatos sobre el tr\xe1fico IP que fluye hacia y desde las interfaces de red dentro de una VPC, proporcionando visibilidad para an\xe1lisis y soluci\xf3n de problemas, pero registran informaci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico en lugar de filtrar o bloquear solicitudes entrantes."},{"id":"q238","question":"El uso de Amazon RDS cae bajo el modelo de responsabilidad compartida. \xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes son responsabilidades del cliente?","options":{"A":"Construir el esquema de la base de datos relacional","B":"Realizar copias de seguridad","C":"Gestionar la configuraci\xf3n de la base de datos","D":"Aplicar parches al software de la base de datos","E":"Instalar el software de la base de datos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida para Amazon RDS, el cliente es responsable de dise\xf1ar el esquema de la base de datos y gestionar la configuraci\xf3n a nivel de base de datos, como par\xe1metros y permisos de usuario.\\nLa realizaci\xf3n de copias de seguridad la gestiona AWS para RDS, que automatiza la retenci\xf3n de copias de seguridad y la recuperaci\xf3n a un punto en el tiempo sin requerir que el cliente inicie o gestione manualmente los procesos de copia de seguridad. La aplicaci\xf3n de parches al software de la base de datos es responsabilidad completamente de AWS para RDS, ya que AWS gestiona todos los parches a nivel de motor y las ventanas de mantenimiento como parte del servicio administrado. La instalaci\xf3n del software de la base de datos es responsabilidad completamente de AWS para RDS, ya que AWS aprovisiona, instala y mantiene el motor de base de datos subyacente en nombre del cliente."},{"id":"q252","question":"Una organizaci\xf3n ejecuta muchos sistemas y utiliza muchos productos de AWS. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios les permite controlar c\xf3mo interact\xfaa cada desarrollador con estos productos?","options":{"A":"AWS Identity and Access Management","B":"Amazon RDS","C":"Network Access Control Lists","D":"Amazon EMR"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Identity and Access Management permite a los administradores crear usuarios, grupos y roles con pol\xedticas de permisos detalladas que controlan exactamente a qu\xe9 servicios y acciones de AWS puede acceder cada desarrollador o sistema en todos los productos de AWS.\\nAmazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado para ejecutar bases de datos como MySQL, PostgreSQL y Oracle en la nube, y es un servicio de almacenamiento de datos en lugar de una herramienta para controlar el acceso de los desarrolladores a los productos de AWS. Las Network Access Control Lists son firewalls sin estado a nivel de subred dentro de una VPC que controlan el tr\xe1fico de red entrante y saliente seg\xfan reglas de direcci\xf3n IP y puerto, y son un mecanismo de seguridad de red en lugar de un servicio para gestionar permisos de desarrolladores en los productos de AWS. Amazon EMR es una plataforma de macrodatos en la nube administrada para ejecutar trabajos de procesamiento de datos distribuidos a gran escala usando frameworks como Apache Spark y Hadoop, y es un servicio de procesamiento de datos en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de gesti\xf3n de acceso."},{"id":"q258","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio de AWS que realiza evaluaciones de red automatizadas de instancias de Amazon EC2 para buscar vulnerabilidades?","options":{"A":"Amazon Kinesis","B":"Security groups","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Network Access Control Lists"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Inspector eval\xfaa autom\xe1ticamente las instancias de EC2 en busca de acceso de red no intencionado y vulnerabilidades de software conocidas escaneando configuraciones de red y paquetes instalados contra bases de datos de vulnerabilidades, generando hallazgos priorizados.\\nAmazon Kinesis es un servicio de transmisi\xf3n de datos en tiempo real para ingerir, procesar y analizar datos de transmisi\xf3n a escala, y no tiene capacidad para realizar evaluaciones de seguridad ni escaneos de vulnerabilidades de red de instancias de EC2. Los Security Groups act\xfaan como firewalls virtuales con estado que controlan qu\xe9 tr\xe1fico puede llegar a las instancias de EC2 seg\xfan reglas definidas, y son un mecanismo de filtrado de tr\xe1fico en lugar de un servicio que realiza evaluaciones automatizadas de vulnerabilidades. Las AWS Network Access Control Lists son firewalls sin estado a nivel de subred que permiten o deniegan tr\xe1fico seg\xfan reglas de direcci\xf3n IP y puerto, y son un mecanismo de control de tr\xe1fico de red en lugar de un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n que identifica vulnerabilidades."},{"id":"q259","question":"En el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, \xbfcu\xe1les de los siguientes controles heredan completamente los clientes de AWS?","options":{"A":"Controles de gesti\xf3n de parches","B":"Controles de base de datos","C":"Concientizaci\xf3n y capacitaci\xf3n","D":"Controles ambientales","E":"Controles f\xedsicos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Los controles f\xedsicos, como la seguridad f\xedsica del centro de datos y la protecci\xf3n del hardware, y los controles ambientales, como la energ\xeda, la refrigeraci\xf3n y la supresi\xf3n de incendios, son administrados \xedntegramente por AWS y los heredan completamente los clientes que no tienen acceso ni responsabilidad sobre estas \xe1reas.\\n\\nLa gesti\xf3n de parches es un control compartido en el que AWS aplica parches a la infraestructura subyacente y a los servicios administrados, mientras que los clientes conservan la responsabilidad de aplicar parches a sus propios sistemas operativos y aplicaciones. Los controles de base de datos son un control compartido en el que AWS administra el motor de base de datos en los servicios totalmente administrados, mientras que los clientes siguen siendo responsables de la gesti\xf3n de accesos, el dise\xf1o del esquema y el manejo de datos. La concientizaci\xf3n y capacitaci\xf3n es un control compartido en el que AWS capacita a sus propios empleados, mientras que los clientes son responsables de forma independiente de capacitar a su propio personal en pr\xe1cticas de seguridad y cumplimiento."},{"id":"q269","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes puede ayudar a proteger sus instancias EC2 de ataques DDoS?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudHSM","B":"Grupos de seguridad","C":"AWS Batch","D":"AWS IAM","E":"Listas de control de acceso de red (Network ACLs)"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Los grupos de seguridad son firewalls con estado a nivel de instancia que pueden bloquear el tr\xe1fico no deseado antes de que llegue a las instancias EC2, y las Network ACLs son controles sin estado a nivel de subred que pueden bloquear tr\xe1fico de ataques volum\xe9tricos en el per\xedmetro de la red. Ambos pueden ayudar a proteger las instancias EC2 del tr\xe1fico DDoS.\\n\\nAWS CloudHSM proporciona m\xf3dulos de seguridad de hardware dedicados para generar y gestionar claves criptogr\xe1ficas dentro de AWS Cloud, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de claves sin capacidad para bloquear o mitigar tr\xe1fico DDoS dirigido a instancias EC2. AWS Batch es un servicio de computaci\xf3n por lotes totalmente administrado que aprovisiona din\xe1micamente recursos de computaci\xf3n para trabajos por lotes, y es un servicio de programaci\xf3n de trabajos y computaci\xf3n sin capacidad de filtrado de tr\xe1fico de red ni de protecci\xf3n DDoS. AWS IAM gestiona los permisos de usuario y el acceso a los servicios y recursos de AWS, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de identidades y accesos en lugar de una herramienta de defensa de red que pueda bloquear tr\xe1fico DDoS."},{"id":"q280","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes se usa para controlar el tr\xe1fico de red en AWS?","options":{"A":"Listas de control de acceso de red (NACLs)","B":"Pares de claves","C":"Claves de acceso","D":"Pol\xedticas de IAM","E":"Grupos de seguridad"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Las Network ACLs son firewalls sin estado a nivel de subred que eval\xfaan todo el tr\xe1fico entrante y saliente seg\xfan reglas numeradas, y los grupos de seguridad son firewalls con estado a nivel de instancia que controlan el tr\xe1fico hacia y desde las instancias EC2. Ambos se usan para controlar el tr\xe1fico de red en AWS.\\n\\nLos pares de claves son combinaciones de claves p\xfablica/privada RSA que se usan para autenticar conexiones SSH a instancias EC2, y son una credencial de acceso a la instancia en lugar de un mecanismo de control del tr\xe1fico de red. Las claves de acceso, compuestas por un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta, se usan para autenticar llamadas program\xe1ticas a la API de los servicios de AWS, y son una credencial de autenticaci\xf3n de API en lugar de una herramienta para controlar el tr\xe1fico de red dentro de una VPC. Las pol\xedticas de IAM son documentos JSON que definen permisos para que usuarios, grupos y roles accedan a servicios y recursos de AWS, y operan en la capa de autorizaci\xf3n de la API en lugar de controlar el flujo de tr\xe1fico de red a nivel de IP."},{"id":"q292","question":"La seguridad de los datos es una de las principales prioridades de AWS. \xbfC\xf3mo maneja AWS los dispositivos de almacenamiento antiguos que han alcanzado el final de su vida \xfatil?","options":{"A":"AWS vende los dispositivos antiguos a otros proveedores de alojamiento","B":"AWS destruye los dispositivos antiguos de acuerdo con pr\xe1cticas est\xe1ndar de la industria","C":"AWS env\xeda los dispositivos antiguos para su refabricaci\xf3n","D":"AWS almacena los dispositivos antiguos en un lugar seguro"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS da de baja los dispositivos de almacenamiento usando t\xe9cnicas est\xe1ndar de la industria, como DoD 5220.22-M o NIST 800-88, para garantizar que los datos no se puedan recuperar del hardware retirado, cumpliendo con sus obligaciones de seguridad de la nube en el marco del modelo de responsabilidad compartida.\\n\\nAWS no vende dispositivos de almacenamiento antiguos a otros proveedores de alojamiento, ya que hacerlo crear\xeda un riesgo inaceptable de exposici\xf3n de datos; los dispositivos dados de baja se destruyen f\xedsicamente o se borran de forma segura siguiendo protocolos estrictos antes de su eliminaci\xf3n. AWS no utiliza auditor\xedas de terceros para verificar la eliminaci\xf3n de datos en dispositivos antiguos; m\xe1s bien, AWS realiza la baja por s\xed misma usando procesos internos documentados, y las pol\xedticas y certificaciones resultantes est\xe1n disponibles para los clientes a trav\xe9s de AWS Artifact. AWS no devuelve los dispositivos de almacenamiento antiguos a los proveedores de hardware para su reventa, ya que los dispositivos contienen datos de clientes que deben destruirse de forma segura antes de cualquier transferencia fuera del control directo de AWS."},{"id":"q293","question":"Un desarrollador necesita configurar un certificado de seguridad SSL para el sitio web de comercio electr\xf3nico de un cliente a fin de usar el protocolo HTTPS. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS se puede usar para implementar los certificados de servidor SSL requeridos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS Directory Service","D":"AWS Identity and Access Management","E":"AWS Config"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Certificate Manager es el servicio principal para aprovisionar, gestionar e implementar certificados SSL/TLS para su uso con servicios de AWS como CloudFront y Elastic Load Balancing. AWS IAM tambi\xe9n admite la carga y el almacenamiento de certificados de servidor SSL que se pueden implementar en servicios que ACM a\xfan no admite, por lo que ambas son opciones v\xe1lidas.\\n\\nAmazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y registro de dominios que gestiona c\xf3mo se enruta el tr\xe1fico hacia los recursos, y no tiene la capacidad de aprovisionar ni implementar certificados SSL. AWS Directory Service es un servicio administrado de Microsoft Active Directory que se usa para la gesti\xf3n de identidades y no est\xe1 relacionado con el aprovisionamiento de certificados SSL. AWS Config es un servicio de monitoreo de cumplimiento y configuraci\xf3n que rastrea los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos y no aprovisiona ni implementa certificados SSL."},{"id":"q295","question":"Una empresa planea migrar una aplicaci\xf3n de Amazon EC2 a AWS Lambda para usar una arquitectura sin servidor. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes ser\xe1 responsabilidad de AWS despu\xe9s de la migraci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Gesti\xf3n de la aplicaci\xf3n","B":"Gesti\xf3n de la capacidad","C":"Control de acceso","D":"Mantenimiento del sistema operativo","E":"Gesti\xf3n de datos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Despu\xe9s de migrar a AWS Lambda, AWS asume toda la responsabilidad de la gesti\xf3n de la capacidad al escalar autom\xe1ticamente las funciones seg\xfan la demanda, y del mantenimiento del sistema operativo, ya que Lambda se ejecuta por completo en infraestructura administrada por AWS sin que el cliente tenga acceso al sistema operativo subyacente.\\n\\nLa gesti\xf3n de la aplicaci\xf3n sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente despu\xe9s de la migraci\xf3n, ya que los clientes siempre son responsables del c\xf3digo que escriben, implementan y mantienen dentro de sus funciones Lambda. El control de acceso sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes deben configurar roles, permisos y pol\xedticas de recursos de IAM para controlar qui\xe9n y qu\xe9 puede invocar sus funciones Lambda. La gesti\xf3n de datos sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes controlan qu\xe9 datos procesan sus funciones, d\xf3nde se almacenan y c\xf3mo se manejan a lo largo de su ciclo de vida."},{"id":"q300","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se usa para gestionar los permisos de usuario?","options":{"A":"Grupos de seguridad","B":"Amazon ECS","C":"AWS IAM","D":"AWS Support"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Identity and Access Management permite a los administradores crear usuarios, grupos, roles y pol\xedticas que controlan qui\xe9n puede acceder a qu\xe9 servicios y recursos de AWS, lo que lo convierte en el servicio principal para gestionar los permisos de usuario en AWS.\\n\\nAWS Key Management Service crea y gestiona claves criptogr\xe1ficas para cifrar y descifrar datos, y es un servicio de protecci\xf3n de datos en lugar de una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de permisos de acceso para controlar qu\xe9 usuarios pueden acceder a los recursos de AWS. AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y eventos de gesti\xf3n con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, lo que proporciona visibilidad sobre qui\xe9n hizo qu\xe9 y cu\xe1ndo, pero registra la actividad en lugar de gestionar o aplicar los permisos de usuario. Amazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas y eventos operativos de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS, lo que proporciona observabilidad sobre el rendimiento y la salud operativa, pero no tiene la capacidad de gestionar permisos de acceso de usuarios a los servicios de AWS."},{"id":"q302","question":"Una empresa necesita rastrear los cambios de recursos usando el historial de llamadas a la API. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede ayudar a la empresa a lograr este objetivo?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS CloudFormation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra cada llamada a la API realizada en su cuenta de AWS, incluida la identidad del autor de la llamada, la marca de tiempo y los par\xe1metros, lo que lo convierte en el servicio principal para rastrear cambios de recursos mediante el historial de API.\\n\\nAWS Config rastrea el estado de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS a lo largo del tiempo y los eval\xfaa con respecto a reglas de cumplimiento, pero registra cambios de configuraci\xf3n en lugar de las llamadas a la API subyacentes que los causaron. Amazon CloudWatch es un servicio de monitoreo y observabilidad que recopila m\xe9tricas, registros y alarmas de los recursos de AWS, y no registra el historial de llamadas a la API ni rastrea qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 cambios en los recursos. AWS CloudFormation es un servicio de infraestructura como c\xf3digo que aprovisiona y gestiona recursos de AWS mediante plantillas, y no registra ni rastrea el historial de llamadas a la API en una cuenta."},{"id":"q305","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la recomendaci\xf3n de AWS con respecto a las claves de acceso?","options":{"A":"Eliminar todas las claves de acceso y usar contrase\xf1as en su lugar","B":"Compartirlas solo con personas de confianza","C":"Rotarlas peri\xf3dicamente","D":"Guardarlas dentro del c\xf3digo de tu aplicaci\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS recomienda rotar las claves de acceso peri\xf3dicamente para limitar la ventana de exposici\xf3n si alguna vez se compromete una clave, ya que las credenciales de vida \xfatil m\xe1s corta reducen el riesgo de que el acceso no autorizado pase desapercibido.\\nEliminar todas las claves de acceso y usar contrase\xf1as en su lugar no es un enfoque v\xe1lido, ya que las contrase\xf1as se usan para el acceso a la consola mientras que las claves de acceso son necesarias para el acceso program\xe1tico a trav\xe9s de la CLI y la API, y ambas tienen roles leg\xedtimos en una cuenta de AWS bien administrada. Compartir las claves de acceso solo con personas de confianza sigue violando el principio de privilegios m\xednimos, ya que las claves de acceso deben estar limitadas a usuarios o roles de IAM individuales en lugar de compartirse entre personas. Guardar las claves de acceso dentro del c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n es un riesgo de seguridad significativo, ya que los repositorios de c\xf3digo pueden quedar expuestos p\xfablica o internamente, y AWS recomienda usar roles de IAM para aplicaciones que se ejecutan en recursos de AWS en lugar de incrustar credenciales en el c\xf3digo."},{"id":"q306","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la caracter\xedstica de AWS IAM que proporciona una capa adicional de seguridad adem\xe1s de la autenticaci\xf3n con nombre de usuario y contrase\xf1a?","options":{"A":"Par de claves","B":"Claves de acceso","C":"SDK","D":"MFA"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La autenticaci\xf3n multifactor de AWS IAM agrega una segunda capa de verificaci\xf3n que requiere un c\xf3digo de un solo uso de un dispositivo de hardware o virtual adem\xe1s del nombre de usuario y la contrase\xf1a, proporcionando un factor de autenticaci\xf3n adicional que aumenta significativamente la seguridad de la cuenta.\\nEl cifrado es un mecanismo de protecci\xf3n de datos utilizado para proteger datos en reposo o en tr\xe1nsito, y aunque protege la confidencialidad de la informaci\xf3n almacenada, no agrega una capa de autenticaci\xf3n adem\xe1s del nombre de usuario y la contrase\xf1a para el inicio de sesi\xf3n de la cuenta. AWS Access Advisor muestra a qu\xe9 servicios de AWS han accedido los usuarios y roles de IAM y cu\xe1ndo, lo que ayuda a los administradores a reducir los permisos solo a aquellos que realmente se usan, pero es una herramienta de visibilidad de permisos en lugar de un mecanismo de seguridad de autenticaci\xf3n. El acceso con privilegios m\xednimos es un principio de gesti\xf3n de permisos que consiste en otorgar a los usuarios solo los permisos m\xednimos necesarios para sus tareas, reduciendo el radio de impacto de credenciales comprometidas, pero no agrega un factor de autenticaci\xf3n adicional al proceso de inicio de sesi\xf3n."},{"id":"q312","question":"Para servicios administrados como Amazon DynamoDB, \xbfde cu\xe1l de los siguientes es responsable AWS?","options":{"A":"Proteger las credenciales","B":"Registrar la actividad de acceso","C":"Aplicar parches al software de base de datos","D":"Mantenimiento del sistema operativo","E":"Crear pol\xedticas de acceso"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Para servicios totalmente administrados como Amazon DynamoDB, AWS es responsable de aplicar parches al software de base de datos y de mantener el sistema operativo subyacente, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso a la capa de infraestructura debajo del servicio.\\nLa protecci\xf3n de credenciales es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes gestionan sus propios usuarios de IAM, claves de acceso y mecanismos de autenticaci\xf3n. El registro de la actividad de acceso es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes deben habilitar y configurar AWS CloudTrail y DynamoDB Streams si desean capturar registros de acceso. La creaci\xf3n de pol\xedticas de acceso es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes definen y gestionan las pol\xedticas de IAM que controlan qui\xe9n puede acceder a sus tablas de DynamoDB y qu\xe9 acciones puede realizar."},{"id":"q316","question":"Una empresa ha descubierto que se eliminaron varios buckets de S3, pero no est\xe1 claro qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 estas acciones. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para identificar qui\xe9n elimin\xf3 los buckets?","options":{"A":"Registros de SNS","B":"Registros de SQS","C":"CloudWatch Logs","D":"Registros de CloudTrail"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n en una cuenta de AWS, incluidos los eventos de eliminaci\xf3n de buckets de S3, capturando qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 la acci\xf3n, la direcci\xf3n IP de origen y la marca de tiempo, lo que lo convierte en el servicio correcto para identificar qui\xe9n elimin\xf3 los buckets.\\nLos registros de SNS no son un servicio de AWS ni un tipo de registro reconocido; Amazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda para enviar notificaciones y no produce registros de actividad de la API de S3 ni de eventos de eliminaci\xf3n. Los registros de SQS no son un servicio de AWS ni un tipo de registro reconocido; Amazon SQS es un servicio de cola de mensajes administrado para desacoplar componentes de aplicaciones y no produce registros de actividad de administraci\xf3n de S3. CloudWatch Logs recopila y almacena datos de registro de servicios y aplicaciones de AWS para monitoreo y an\xe1lisis, pero los eventos de eliminaci\xf3n de buckets de S3 son eventos de administraci\xf3n capturados por CloudTrail en lugar de CloudWatch, por lo que CloudWatch Logs es el lugar incorrecto para identificar qui\xe9n elimin\xf3 los buckets de S3."},{"id":"q319","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 son los controles compartidos de AWS?","options":{"A":"Controles que son responsabilidad exclusiva del cliente seg\xfan la aplicaci\xf3n que implementa dentro de los servicios de AWS","B":"Controles que un cliente hereda de AWS","C":"Controles que se aplican tanto a la capa de infraestructura como a las capas del cliente","D":"Controles sobre los que el cliente y AWS colaboran conjuntamente para proteger la infraestructura"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Los controles compartidos son responsabilidades en las que tanto AWS como el cliente aplican controles de forma independiente en sus respectivos dominios, como AWS aplicando parches a su infraestructura mientras el cliente aplica parches a su sistema operativo invitado.\\nLos controles que son responsabilidad exclusiva del cliente describen controles espec\xedficos del cliente, no controles compartidos, ya que estos se aplican solo al lado del cliente del modelo. Los controles que un cliente hereda de AWS describen controles heredados, donde las certificaciones de cumplimiento y las medidas de seguridad f\xedsica se transmiten de AWS al cliente, lo cual es una categor\xeda separada de los controles compartidos. La descripci\xf3n de controles que se aplican tanto a la capa de infraestructura como a las capas del cliente es enga\xf1osa porque los controles compartidos no significan que el mismo control \xfanico se aplique en ambas capas, sino que cada parte gestiona su propia versi\xf3n del mismo tipo de control de forma independiente."},{"id":"q321","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son responsabilidades del cliente al usar Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"Proteger datos confidenciales","B":"Aplicar parches a la infraestructura subyacente","C":"Configuraci\xf3n y operaci\xf3n de bases de datos administradas","D":"Mantener componentes de hardware consistentes","E":"Instalar y configurar software de terceros"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Los clientes que usan Amazon EC2 son responsables de gestionar el acceso de usuarios a sus instancias EC2, incluida la configuraci\xf3n de usuarios del sistema operativo y claves SSH, y de proteger los datos en reposo y en tr\xe1nsito en esas instancias mediante cifrado y controles de acceso.\\nImpedir el acceso f\xedsico al hardware de EC2 es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni visibilidad a las instalaciones f\xedsicas del centro de datos donde se ejecutan las instancias EC2. La aplicaci\xf3n de parches a la infraestructura de red es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que los componentes de red f\xedsicos y virtuales subyacentes que conectan las instancias EC2 son gestionados y mantenidos en su totalidad por AWS. La gesti\xf3n del hipervisor es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que la capa de virtualizaci\xf3n que abstrae el hardware f\xedsico en instancias EC2 forma parte de la infraestructura de AWS a la que los clientes no pueden acceder ni modificar."},{"id":"q323","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS puede ayudarte a realizar an\xe1lisis de seguridad y auditor\xedas de cumplimiento normativo?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Batch","D":"Amazon ECS","E":"AWS Config"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Amazon Inspector eval\xfaa autom\xe1ticamente las aplicaciones en busca de vulnerabilidades y desviaciones de las mejores pr\xe1cticas, y AWS Config monitorea continuamente las configuraciones de los recursos y las eval\xfaa contra reglas de cumplimiento. Ambos est\xe1n dise\xf1ados espec\xedficamente para el an\xe1lisis de seguridad y la auditor\xeda de cumplimiento normativo.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones en seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, pero ofrece verificaciones y orientaci\xf3n de alto nivel en lugar de las evaluaciones de seguridad automatizadas y profundas y la evaluaci\xf3n de reglas de cumplimiento que ofrecen Inspector y Config. AWS Batch es un servicio administrado para ejecutar cargas de trabajo de computaci\xf3n por lotes a escala, y es un servicio de programaci\xf3n de trabajos y computaci\xf3n sin capacidad de an\xe1lisis de seguridad ni auditor\xeda de cumplimiento. Amazon ECS es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores que gestiona el despliegue y el escalado de aplicaciones contenedorizadas, y es un servicio de computaci\xf3n sin capacidad de an\xe1lisis de seguridad ni auditor\xeda de cumplimiento."},{"id":"q332","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes se puede utilizar para proteger datos en reposo en Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"Control de versiones","B":"Deduplicaci\xf3n","C":"Permisos","D":"Descifrado","E":"Conversi\xf3n"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"El control de versiones de Amazon S3 conserva m\xfaltiples versiones de los objetos, lo que protege contra la eliminaci\xf3n accidental y permite restaurar versiones anteriores, y los permisos de S3 mediante pol\xedticas de IAM y pol\xedticas de bucket controlan qui\xe9n puede acceder a los objetos y modificarlos. Ambos ayudan a proteger los datos en reposo en Amazon S3.\\nLa deduplicaci\xf3n es una t\xe9cnica de optimizaci\xf3n de almacenamiento que elimina copias duplicadas de datos para ahorrar espacio, y no es un mecanismo de protecci\xf3n de datos para Amazon S3; S3 no expone la deduplicaci\xf3n como una caracter\xedstica de seguridad. El descifrado es el proceso de convertir datos cifrados de nuevo a su forma original, y aunque el descifrado forma parte de un flujo de trabajo de cifrado, habilitar solo el descifrado no protege los datos en reposo y no es un mecanismo de protecci\xf3n de datos para S3. La conversi\xf3n se refiere a cambiar formatos o tipos de datos y no tiene relevancia para proteger los datos almacenados en Amazon S3."},{"id":"q336","question":"Al ejecutar una carga de trabajo en AWS, el cliente NO es responsable de:","options":{"A":"Ejecutar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n","B":"Reservar capacidad","C":"Operaciones del centro de datos","D":"Auditor\xeda y cumplimiento normativo","E":"Seguridad de la infraestructura"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Las operaciones del centro de datos, incluida la gesti\xf3n de las instalaciones f\xedsicas, la energ\xeda, la refrigeraci\xf3n y la retirada de hardware, y la seguridad de la infraestructura, incluida la red f\xedsica y virtual, el hipervisor y el hardware del servidor, son responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS y no son tareas que realice el cliente.\\nEjecutar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n es responsabilidad del propio cliente; AWS permite a los clientes realizar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n en sus propios recursos siguiendo la Pol\xedtica de uso aceptable, pero la decisi\xf3n de ejecutar las pruebas y las pruebas en s\xed no son algo que AWS realice para los clientes. Reservar capacidad, como comprar Reserved Instances o Savings Plans para cargas de trabajo predecibles, es una decisi\xf3n y acci\xf3n del cliente tomada para optimizar costos en lugar de una tarea gestionada por AWS. La auditor\xeda y el cumplimiento normativo son una responsabilidad compartida; AWS proporciona informes y certificaciones de cumplimiento a trav\xe9s de AWS Artifact, pero los clientes son responsables de aplicarlos a sus propias aplicaciones y de garantizar que sus cargas de trabajo cumplan los requisitos normativos."},{"id":"q341","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS se utiliza para gestionar las claves usadas para cifrar los datos de los clientes?","options":{"A":"AWS KMS","B":"AWS Service Control Policies (SCPs)","C":"Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)","D":"Amazon Macie"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Key Management Service permite crear, gestionar y controlar claves criptogr\xe1ficas usadas para cifrar los datos en los servicios de AWS, lo que proporciona una gesti\xf3n centralizada de claves con registro de auditor\xeda mediante CloudTrail.\\nAWS Service Control Policies son pol\xedticas organizativas usadas dentro de AWS Organizations para restringir los permisos m\xe1ximos disponibles para las cuentas de una organizaci\xf3n, y son una herramienta de gobernanza de acceso en lugar de un servicio de gesti\xf3n de claves para claves de cifrado. Multi-Factor Authentication a\xf1ade un segundo factor de verificaci\xf3n al proceso de inicio de sesi\xf3n que requiere un c\xf3digo de un dispositivo de hardware o virtual, y es un mecanismo de seguridad de autenticaci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio para gestionar claves de cifrado. Amazon Macie usa aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para descubrir y proteger autom\xe1ticamente datos sensibles en Amazon S3, y es un servicio de clasificaci\xf3n y protecci\xf3n de datos en lugar de un servicio de gesti\xf3n de claves para crear y controlar claves de cifrado."},{"id":"q342","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a los clientes descargar informes SOC y PCI de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Well-Architected Tool","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Glue","D":"Amazon Chime"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Artifact es un portal de autoservicio para acceder bajo demanda a informes de cumplimiento de AWS, incluidos informes SOC y PCI, y a documentos de acuerdo como Business Associate Addenda, lo que lo convierte en el servicio correcto para descargar informes de cumplimiento SOC y PCI de AWS.\\nAWS Well-Architected Tool es un servicio que ayuda a revisar el estado de las cargas de trabajo y compararlas con las pr\xe1cticas recomendadas de arquitectura de AWS en los seis pilares, y es una herramienta de revisi\xf3n y orientaci\xf3n de cargas de trabajo en lugar de un repositorio de informes de cumplimiento. AWS Glue es un servicio totalmente gestionado de extracci\xf3n, transformaci\xf3n y carga para preparar y transformar datos para an\xe1lisis, y es un servicio de integraci\xf3n de datos sin conexi\xf3n con la documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento. Amazon Chime es un servicio de comunicaciones unificadas para reuniones en l\xednea, videoconferencias y chat, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con informes de cumplimiento ni con documentos de certificaci\xf3n de AWS."},{"id":"q345","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedsticas ofrece AWS para ayudar a proteger sus datos en la nube?","options":{"A":"Control de acceso","B":"Dispositivos MFA f\xedsicos","C":"Cifrado de datos","D":"Almacenamiento ilimitado","E":"Balanceo de carga"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS ofrece control de acceso mediante pol\xedticas de IAM, grupos de seguridad y ACL de red para restringir qui\xe9n puede llegar a sus datos, y cifrado de datos en reposo y en tr\xe1nsito para proteger los datos incluso si se interceptan o se accede a ellos sin autorizaci\xf3n. Ambas son caracter\xedsticas de protecci\xf3n de datos dise\xf1adas espec\xedficamente.\\nLos dispositivos MFA f\xedsicos a\xf1aden una segunda capa de autenticaci\xf3n para proteger el acceso a la cuenta y a los usuarios, pero protegen qui\xe9n puede iniciar sesi\xf3n en lugar de proteger directamente los datos en s\xed una vez concedido el acceso. El almacenamiento ilimitado es una caracter\xedstica de capacidad de servicios como Amazon S3, y es una caracter\xedstica de escalado de almacenamiento sin capacidad de protecci\xf3n ni seguridad de datos. El balanceo de carga distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre varios destinos para mejorar la disponibilidad y el rendimiento, y es una caracter\xedstica de resiliencia y rendimiento sin ninguna funci\xf3n en la protecci\xf3n de datos frente a accesos o exposiciones no autorizados."},{"id":"q348","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9todos pueden usar los clientes para interactuar con AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)?","options":{"A":"AWS CLI","B":"AWS Security Groups","C":"AWS SDKs","D":"AWS Network Access Control Lists","E":"AWS CodeCommit"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS CLI proporciona acceso de l\xednea de comandos a IAM para crear usuarios, asignar permisos y gestionar credenciales, y AWS SDKs permiten la integraci\xf3n program\xe1tica con IAM en el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n para automatizar operaciones de gesti\xf3n de identidades y accesos. Ambos son m\xe9todos v\xe1lidos para interactuar con IAM.\\nAWS Security Groups son firewalls virtuales que controlan el tr\xe1fico de red entrante y saliente a nivel de instancia de EC2, y son un componente de seguridad de red en lugar de un m\xe9todo para interactuar con IAM. AWS Network Access Control Lists son firewalls sin estado que controlan el tr\xe1fico a nivel de subred dentro de una VPC, y son un mecanismo de filtrado de tr\xe1fico de red en lugar de una interfaz para gestionar identidades o permisos de IAM. AWS CodeCommit es un servicio de control de c\xf3digo fuente totalmente gestionado para alojar repositorios Git, y es una herramienta de colaboraci\xf3n de c\xf3digo sin conexi\xf3n con la interacci\xf3n con IAM m\xe1s all\xe1 de usar IAM para el control de acceso."},{"id":"q349","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son tipos de identidades de AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)?","options":{"A":"AWS Resource Groups","B":"IAM Policies","C":"IAM Roles","D":"IAM Users","E":"AWS Organizations"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"IAM Users representan personas o aplicaciones individuales con credenciales y permisos espec\xedficos, e IAM Roles proporcionan credenciales de seguridad temporales que pueden asumir usuarios, servicios o recursos de AWS. Ambos son tipos de identidades de IAM.\\nAWS Resource Groups organizan recursos de AWS por etiquetas o pilas de CloudFormation para facilitar la gesti\xf3n y automatizaci\xf3n de operaciones en grupos de recursos, y no son identidades de IAM que puedan usarse para autenticaci\xf3n o asignaci\xf3n de permisos. IAM Policies son documentos JSON que definen permisos especificando acciones permitidas o denegadas sobre recursos de AWS, y son documentos de permisos adjuntos a identidades en lugar de tipos de identidad en s\xed. AWS Organizations es un servicio para gestionar varias cuentas de AWS bajo una estructura central para facturaci\xf3n consolidada y gobernanza, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de cuentas en lugar de un tipo de identidad de IAM."},{"id":"q351","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo notifica AWS a los clientes sobre eventos de seguridad y privacidad relacionados con los servicios de AWS?","options":{"A":"Usando el servicio AWS ACM","B":"Usando Security Bulletins","C":"Usando AWS Management Console","D":"Usando recursos de cumplimiento"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS publica Security Bulletins para notificar a los clientes sobre eventos de seguridad y privacidad que afectan a los servicios de AWS, proporcionando detalles sobre vulnerabilidades y acciones recomendadas.\\nAWS Certificate Manager es un servicio que aprovisiona y gestiona certificados SSL/TLS para usarlos con recursos de AWS, y no es un canal a trav\xe9s del cual AWS comunique a los clientes notificaciones de eventos de seguridad o privacidad. AWS Management Console es una interfaz basada en web para acceder y gestionar servicios de AWS, y aunque los clientes pueden ver algunas alertas a nivel de cuenta all\xed, no es el mecanismo que AWS usa para publicar notificaciones de eventos de seguridad y privacidad. AWS Compliance Resources son documentos e informes, como certificaciones de auditor\xeda y gu\xedas de cumplimiento, que ayudan a los clientes a comprender la postura normativa de AWS, y no se usan para notificar a los clientes sobre eventos espec\xedficos de seguridad o privacidad."},{"id":"q352","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 entidad de IAM se puede usar mejor para conceder acceso temporal a sus recursos de AWS?","options":{"A":"IAM Users","B":"Key Pair","C":"IAM Roles","D":"IAM Groups"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IAM Roles proporcionan credenciales de seguridad temporales que expiran autom\xe1ticamente, lo que las convierte en la mejor opci\xf3n para conceder acceso a corto plazo o temporal a recursos de AWS sin compartir credenciales a largo plazo.\\nIAM Users son identidades con credenciales permanentes a largo plazo, como contrase\xf1as y claves de acceso, y aunque se pueden conceder o revocar permisos, los usuarios en s\xed representan identidades persistentes en lugar de un mecanismo dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para acceso temporal. Key pairs son combinaciones de claves p\xfablicas/privadas RSA usadas para autenticar conexiones SSH a instancias Linux de EC2, y son un mecanismo de acceso a instancias en lugar de una caracter\xedstica de IAM dise\xf1ada para conceder acceso temporal a servicios y recursos de AWS. IAM Groups son colecciones de usuarios de IAM que comparten las mismas pol\xedticas adjuntas, y se usan para gestionar permisos para varios usuarios de forma colectiva en lugar de para conceder acceso temporal a una entidad individual."},{"id":"q355","question":"Una empresa busca proteger mejor su cuenta de AWS contra accesos no autorizados. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones puede usar el cliente para lograr este objetivo?","options":{"A":"Restringir cualquier llamada a la API realizada a trav\xe9s de SDKs o CLI","B":"Crear una cuenta de IAM para cada departamento de la empresa (Desarrollo, QA, Producci\xf3n) y compartirla entre todo el personal de ese departamento","C":"Requerir autenticaci\xf3n multifactor (MFA) para todo el acceso de usuarios de IAM","D":"Configurar dos contrase\xf1as de inicio de sesi\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Requerir MFA para todos los usuarios de IAM agrega un segundo paso de verificaci\xf3n adem\xe1s de una contrase\xf1a, lo que reduce significativamente el riesgo de acceso no autorizado incluso si la contrase\xf1a de un usuario es robada o adivinada, lo que lo convierte en un control eficaz para proteger la cuenta de AWS.\\nRestringir todas las llamadas a la API a trav\xe9s de SDKs o CLI impedir\xeda por completo el acceso program\xe1tico y automatizado a los servicios de AWS, lo que lo har\xeda operativamente inviable para los flujos de trabajo de desarrollo y automatizaci\xf3n en lugar de una medida de seguridad pr\xe1ctica. Crear una \xfanica cuenta de IAM por departamento compartida entre todo el personal elimina la responsabilidad individual, hace imposible rastrear las acciones hasta usuarios espec\xedficos y significa que una \xfanica contrase\xf1a comprometida otorga acceso a todos en el departamento, lo que convierte esto en un antipatr\xf3n de seguridad en lugar de una mejora de seguridad. Configurar dos contrase\xf1as de inicio de sesi\xf3n no es una funci\xf3n que AWS admita; las cuentas de AWS usan una \xfanica contrase\xf1a para el acceso a la consola, y la mejora de seguridad m\xe1s all\xe1 de la contrase\xf1a se logra mediante MFA en lugar de m\xfaltiples contrase\xf1as."},{"id":"q361","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son ejemplos de la responsabilidad del cliente de implementar seguridad EN la nube?","options":{"A":"Crear un esquema para una aplicaci\xf3n","B":"Reemplazar hardware f\xedsico","C":"Crear un nuevo hypervisor","D":"Gesti\xf3n de parches de la infraestructura subyacente","E":"Cifrado del sistema de archivos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Crear un esquema de aplicaci\xf3n es una tarea de desarrollo de aplicaciones que los clientes realizan como parte de la gesti\xf3n de sus aplicaciones en la nube, y cifrar los sistemas de archivos en las instancias EC2 para proteger los datos en reposo es una responsabilidad del cliente para la seguridad en la nube. Ambos se encuentran dentro del lado del cliente del modelo de responsabilidad compartida.\\nReemplazar el hardware f\xedsico es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni interacci\xf3n con los servidores f\xedsicos, los dispositivos de almacenamiento y el equipamiento de red de los centros de datos de AWS. Crear un nuevo hypervisor es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que la capa de virtualizaci\xf3n que abstrae el hardware f\xedsico en instancias virtuales forma parte de la infraestructura administrada por AWS a la que los clientes no pueden acceder ni modificar. La gesti\xf3n de parches de la infraestructura subyacente, incluidos los sistemas operativos host, el hypervisor y el equipamiento de red f\xedsico, es responsabilidad de AWS, mientras que los clientes son responsables de aplicar parches al sistema operativo invitado en sus propias instancias EC2."},{"id":"q362","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un tipo de dispositivo MFA que los clientes pueden usar para proteger sus recursos de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudHSM","B":"Llave de seguridad U2F","C":"Claves de acceso de AWS","D":"Par de claves de AWS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Una llave de seguridad U2F es un dispositivo de hardware f\xedsico que se autentica mediante USB o NFC usando protocolos criptogr\xe1ficos, lo que proporciona un segundo factor basado en hardware para MFA de AWS que protege las cuentas contra phishing y robo de credenciales.\\nAWS CloudHSM proporciona m\xf3dulos de seguridad de hardware dedicados para generar y administrar claves criptogr\xe1ficas para el cifrado de datos, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de claves en lugar de un dispositivo MFA usado para autenticar inicios de sesi\xf3n de cuentas de AWS. Las claves de acceso de AWS, que consisten en un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta, son credenciales usadas para el acceso program\xe1tico a la API y la CLI, y son un mecanismo de autenticaci\xf3n program\xe1tico en lugar de un dispositivo MFA que agrega un segundo factor al inicio de sesi\xf3n en la consola. Los pares de claves de AWS son combinaciones de claves p\xfablica/privada RSA usadas para autenticar conexiones SSH a instancias EC2 de Linux, y son un mecanismo de acceso a nivel de instancia en lugar de un dispositivo MFA para la autenticaci\xf3n de cuentas de AWS."},{"id":"q367","question":"Acaba de contratar a un administrador de sistemas experto para unirse a su equipo. Como de costumbre, ha creado un nuevo usuario de IAM para que interact\xfae con los servicios de AWS. En su primer d\xeda, le pide que cree instant\xe1neas de todos los vol\xfamenes de Amazon EBS existentes y las guarde en un nuevo bucket de Amazon S3. Sin embargo, el nuevo miembro informa que no puede crear ni instant\xe1neas de EBS ni buckets de S3. \xbfQu\xe9 podr\xeda impedirle realizar esta sencilla tarea?","options":{"A":"EBS y S3 solo son accesibles para el propietario de la cuenta ra\xedz","B":"El administrador de sistemas debe contactar primero a AWS Support para activar su nueva cuenta de IAM","C":"No hay suficiente espacio en S3 para almacenar las instant\xe1neas","D":"Existe una denegaci\xf3n no expl\xedcita para todos los usuarios nuevos"},"correct":"D","explanation":"De forma predeterminada, los nuevos usuarios de IAM no tienen permisos adjuntos, lo que significa que todas las acciones se deniegan impl\xedcitamente hasta que se conceden permisos expl\xedcitos mediante pol\xedticas, y por eso el nuevo administrador no puede crear instant\xe1neas de EBS ni buckets de S3.\\nEBS y S3 no solo son accesibles para el propietario de la cuenta ra\xedz; cualquier usuario, grupo o rol de IAM al que se le hayan concedido los permisos adecuados mediante pol\xedticas de IAM puede acceder a ellos, lo que hace que esta afirmaci\xf3n sea f\xe1cticamente incorrecta. Un nuevo usuario de IAM no necesita contactar a AWS Support para activar su cuenta; los usuarios de IAM se crean y quedan activos de inmediato, y el problema es la ausencia de permisos en lugar de un requisito de activaci\xf3n de cuenta. No existe un l\xedmite de almacenamiento inherente en S3 que impida crear un nuevo bucket para instant\xe1neas; S3 escala autom\xe1ticamente para adaptarse a cualquier cantidad de datos, y la incapacidad de crear el bucket se debe a permisos faltantes en lugar de un problema de capacidad de almacenamiento."},{"id":"q368","question":"Un auditor externo solicita un registro de todos los accesos a los recursos de AWS en la cuenta de la empresa. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios proporcionar\xe1 al auditor la informaci\xf3n solicitada?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"Amazon CloudFront","C":"AWS CloudFormation","D":"Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y los eventos de administraci\xf3n en una cuenta de AWS, incluidos qui\xe9n accedi\xf3 a qu\xe9 recursos, desde qu\xe9 direcci\xf3n IP y a qu\xe9 hora, lo que proporciona exactamente la informaci\xf3n que un auditor necesita para revisar los registros de acceso.\\nAmazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y distribuye contenido web a los usuarios con baja latencia a trav\xe9s de ubicaciones de borde, y es un servicio de optimizaci\xf3n del rendimiento sin capacidad para registrar todos los accesos a los recursos de la cuenta de AWS. AWS CloudFormation es un servicio de infraestructura como c\xf3digo para aprovisionar y administrar recursos de AWS mediante plantillas, y es una herramienta de aprovisionamiento y automatizaci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio que registra registros de acceso a recursos de AWS. Amazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas operativas, registros y eventos de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS, y aunque puede recopilar registros de aplicaciones y m\xe9tricas de servicios, no proporciona el registro de auditor\xeda completo de llamadas a la API que registra todos los accesos a recursos de AWS como lo hace CloudTrail."},{"id":"q369","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a una empresa conectar sus recursos de AWS con un Microsoft Active Directory local, lo que permite a los usuarios iniciar sesi\xf3n con sus credenciales corporativas existentes?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM Identity Center","B":"AWS Directory Service","C":"Amazon Cognito","D":"AWS Secrets Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Directory Service proporciona Microsoft Active Directory administrado en la nube de AWS, lo que permite a las organizaciones conectar sus recursos de AWS con un Active Directory local existente para que los usuarios puedan acceder a las aplicaciones de AWS usando sus credenciales corporativas establecidas.\\nAWS IAM Identity Center proporciona acceso de inicio de sesi\xf3n \xfanico a m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS y aplicaciones empresariales, pero no proporciona la infraestructura de directorio administrada necesaria para extender un Active Directory local a AWS. Amazon Cognito proporciona identidad de usuario y autenticaci\xf3n para aplicaciones web y m\xf3viles orientadas al cliente, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la integraci\xf3n de directorios corporativos con recursos de AWS. AWS Secrets Manager almacena, rota y recupera credenciales y secretos de forma program\xe1tica, pero no administra servicios de directorio ni autenticaci\xf3n de usuarios contra Active Directory."},{"id":"q375","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS le permite comprar e implementar r\xe1pidamente certificados SSL/TLS?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"AWS ACM","C":"Amazon Detective","D":"AWS WAF"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Certificate Manager le permite aprovisionar, administrar e implementar certificados SSL/TLS para usarlos con servicios de AWS como Elastic Load Balancers y distribuciones de CloudFront, y ofrece certificados p\xfablicos gratuitos que se renuevan autom\xe1ticamente.\\nAmazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que monitorea continuamente cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS en busca de actividad maliciosa usando aprendizaje autom\xe1tico e inteligencia de amenazas, y no tiene capacidad para aprovisionar ni administrar certificados SSL/TLS. Amazon Detective ayuda a analizar y visualizar datos de seguridad para investigar la causa ra\xedz de actividades sospechosas, y proporciona capacidades de investigaci\xf3n forense en lugar de aprovisionamiento de certificados SSL/TLS. AWS WAF es un firewall de aplicaciones web que filtra el tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS seg\xfan reglas definidas para bloquear exploits web comunes, y es un servicio de filtrado de tr\xe1fico en lugar de un servicio de aprovisionamiento de certificados."},{"id":"q380","question":"Una empresa quiere otorgar a un nuevo empleado acceso a largo plazo para administrar bases de datos Amazon DynamoDB. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada al otorgar estos permisos?","options":{"A":"Crear un rol de IAM y adjuntar una pol\xedtica con permisos de acceso a Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Crear un rol de IAM y adjuntar una pol\xedtica con permisos de acceso de Administrator","C":"Crear un usuario de IAM y adjuntar una pol\xedtica con permisos de acceso a Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Crear un usuario de IAM y adjuntar una pol\xedtica con permisos de acceso de Administrator"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Para un empleado espec\xedfico que requiere acceso a largo plazo, un usuario de IAM es el tipo de identidad adecuado, y adjuntar una pol\xedtica limitada solo a Amazon DynamoDB sigue el principio de privilegio m\xednimo al otorgar \xfanicamente los permisos necesarios para ese rol.\\nCrear un rol de IAM y adjuntar permisos de acceso a DynamoDB ser\xeda apropiado para aplicaciones o servicios que necesitan acceso temporal a DynamoDB, pero para un empleado espec\xedfico a largo plazo, un usuario de IAM es el tipo de identidad correcto en lugar de un rol dise\xf1ado para la asunci\xf3n de credenciales temporales. Crear un rol de IAM con permisos de acceso de Administrator otorgar\xeda al nuevo empleado acceso sin restricciones a todos los servicios y recursos de AWS, lo que violar\xeda el principio de privilegio m\xednimo y plantear\xeda un riesgo de seguridad innecesario. Crear un usuario de IAM con permisos de acceso de Administrator otorgar\xeda acceso sin restricciones a todos los servicios de AWS en lugar de limitar los permisos solo a Amazon DynamoDB, lo que viola directamente el principio de privilegio m\xednimo."},{"id":"q381","question":"Al conceder permisos a aplicaciones que se ejecutan en instancias de Amazon EC2, \xbfcu\xe1l de las siguientes se considera una pr\xe1ctica recomendada?","options":{"A":"Generar nuevas claves de acceso de IAM cada vez que se deleguen permisos","B":"Almacenar las credenciales de AWS requeridas directamente dentro del c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n","C":"Usar credenciales de seguridad temporales (roles de IAM) en lugar de claves de acceso de largo plazo","D":"No hacer nada; las aplicaciones que se ejecutan en instancias de Amazon EC2 no necesitan permisos para interactuar con otros servicios o recursos de AWS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los roles de IAM proporcionan credenciales de seguridad temporales que se rotan autom\xe1ticamente cuando se asumen, lo que elimina la necesidad de incrustar claves de acceso de largo plazo en el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n y reduce el riesgo de exposici\xf3n de credenciales si la aplicaci\xf3n se ve comprometida. Generar nuevas claves de acceso de IAM cada vez que se delegan permisos dar\xeda como resultado una gran cantidad de credenciales est\xe1ticas de largo plazo que deben rastrearse y administrarse, lo que aumenta el riesgo de exposici\xf3n de credenciales en lugar de reducirlo, y no es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de seguridad para aplicaciones en EC2. Almacenar las credenciales de AWS requeridas directamente en el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n es un antipatr\xf3n de seguridad grave que implica el riesgo de exposici\xf3n de credenciales a trav\xe9s de repositorios de c\xf3digo, archivos de registro o errores de la aplicaci\xf3n, lo que lo convierte en lo opuesto a una pr\xe1ctica recomendada. Las aplicaciones que se ejecutan en EC2 con frecuencia necesitan interactuar con otros servicios de AWS, como S3, DynamoDB o SQS, para funcionar correctamente, por lo que suponer que no se necesitan permisos es f\xe1cticamente incorrecto para la mayor\xeda de las aplicaciones del mundo real."},{"id":"q384","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes se puede utilizar para habilitar la autenticaci\xf3n multifactor virtual?","options":{"A":"Amazon Connect","B":"AWS CLI","C":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","D":"Amazon SNS","E":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"La MFA virtual se puede habilitar a trav\xe9s de AWS IAM, que proporciona la interfaz de la consola para asignar y configurar dispositivos MFA para usuarios, y a trav\xe9s de AWS CLI, que permite administrar la configuraci\xf3n de MFA mediante programaci\xf3n. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube para gestionar las comunicaciones con los clientes, completamente ajeno a la autenticaci\xf3n o a la configuraci\xf3n de MFA. Amazon SNS es un servicio de notificaciones para enviar mensajes a suscriptores y no tiene ninguna funci\xf3n en la habilitaci\xf3n o administraci\xf3n de MFA. Amazon VPC es un servicio de red privada virtual para aislar y configurar la infraestructura de red en la nube, no est\xe1 relacionado con la configuraci\xf3n de autenticaci\xf3n de usuarios."},{"id":"q387","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer si ve recursos que no recuerda haber creado en la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS?","options":{"A":"Detener todos los servicios en ejecuci\xf3n y abrir una investigaci\xf3n","B":"Dar la contrase\xf1a de su cuenta ra\xedz a AWS Support para que puedan ayudar a solucionar problemas y proteger la cuenta","C":"Revisar los registros de AWS CloudTrail y eliminar todos los usuarios de IAM que tengan acceso a sus recursos","D":"Abrir una investigaci\xf3n y eliminar cualquier usuario de IAM que pueda estar comprometido","E":"Cambiar la contrase\xf1a de su cuenta ra\xedz de AWS y las contrase\xf1as de cualquier usuario de IAM"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Si aparecen recursos no reconocidos en su cuenta de AWS, esto puede indicar un compromiso de seguridad en el que un atacante ha obtenido acceso y est\xe1 creando recursos, por lo que debe cambiar la contrase\xf1a de su cuenta ra\xedz y las claves de acceso de inmediato para revocar el acceso del atacante y proteger la cuenta. Cerrar la cuenta de inmediato terminar\xeda permanentemente con todos los recursos y datos sin investigar primero el alcance del compromiso ni recuperar cargas de trabajo, lo que constituye una respuesta desproporcionada antes de completar los pasos adecuados de respuesta a incidentes. Eliminar todos los usuarios de IAM de la cuenta bloquear\xeda a los miembros leg\xedtimos de su equipo y evitar\xeda que se realice cualquier trabajo de investigaci\xf3n o remediaci\xf3n, lo que dificulta significativamente la respuesta a incidentes sin abordar realmente el compromiso de seguridad subyacente. Abrir una nueva cuenta ra\xedz no soluciona la cuenta comprometida, ya que la cuenta original con sus recursos y datos seguir\xeda expuesta; la respuesta a incidentes requiere proteger la cuenta existente en lugar de abandonarla."},{"id":"q395","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 desarrollando una aplicaci\xf3n m\xf3vil y desea permitir que los usuarios usen sus identidades de Amazon, Apple, Facebook o Google para autenticarse en la aplicaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar la empresa para este prop\xf3sito?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"AWS Directory Service","C":"Amazon Cognito","D":"AWS IAM"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Cognito admite la autenticaci\xf3n de identidad federada, lo que permite a los usuarios iniciar sesi\xf3n con sus cuentas existentes de Amazon, Apple, Facebook o Google e intercambiar esos tokens por credenciales de AWS, lo que lo convierte en el servicio correcto para la integraci\xf3n con proveedores de identidad social. Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que monitorea continuamente cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS en busca de actividad maliciosa y comportamiento an\xf3malo, y no es un servicio para habilitar la autenticaci\xf3n de usuarios con proveedores de identidad social. AWS Directory Service proporciona Microsoft Active Directory administrado para la gesti\xf3n de identidades empresariales y SSO con credenciales corporativas, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para identidades de la fuerza laboral en lugar de autenticaci\xf3n con proveedores de identidad social orientada al cliente. AWS IAM administra identidades de usuario y permisos para acceder a servicios y recursos de AWS, y aunque IAM puede otorgar permisos a usuarios autenticados mediante Cognito, no maneja por s\xed mismo el inicio de sesi\xf3n federado con Amazon, Apple, Facebook o Google."},{"id":"q403","question":"Existe el requisito de otorgar a un equipo de DevOps acceso administrativo completo a todos los recursos de una cuenta de AWS. \xbfQui\xe9n puede otorgarles estos permisos?","options":{"A":"Propietario de la cuenta de AWS","B":"Gerente de cuenta t\xe9cnico de AWS","C":"Equipo de seguridad de AWS","D":"Ingenieros de soporte en la nube de AWS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El propietario de la cuenta de AWS posee las credenciales del usuario ra\xedz y tiene acceso administrativo completo a todos los recursos de la cuenta, lo que lo convierte en la \xfanica entidad con la autoridad para otorgar permisos administrativos completos a otros usuarios o equipos mediante pol\xedticas de IAM. Un Gerente de cuenta t\xe9cnico de AWS es un empleado de AWS asignado para ayudar a los clientes del plan de soporte Enterprise con orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica y soporte, y no tiene la capacidad de otorgar permisos dentro de la cuenta de AWS de un cliente. El equipo de seguridad de AWS gestiona la postura de seguridad interna de AWS y no participa en la configuraci\xf3n de permisos de IAM dentro de cuentas de clientes individuales. Los ingenieros de soporte en la nube de AWS brindan soporte t\xe9cnico para resolver problemas y responder preguntas sobre los servicios de AWS, y no tienen acceso para configurar pol\xedticas de IAM ni otorgar permisos dentro de las cuentas de clientes."},{"id":"q410","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n es verdadera en relaci\xf3n con la seguridad de Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"Debe usar vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento de instancia para almacenar datos de inicio de sesi\xf3n","B":"Debe aplicar regularmente parches al sistema operativo y a las aplicaciones en sus instancias de EC2","C":"Debe implementar los componentes cr\xedticos de su aplicaci\xf3n en la zona de disponibilidad en la que conf\xeda","D":"Puede rastrear todas las llamadas a la API usando Amazon Athena"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La responsabilidad compartida de AWS significa que el cliente es responsable de proteger lo que se ejecuta en EC2, incluidos el sistema operativo y las aplicaciones, lo que requiere aplicar regularmente parches de seguridad para cerrar vulnerabilidades que podr\xedan ser explotadas por atacantes. Los vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento de instancia proporcionan almacenamiento local temporal y de alta velocidad que se elimina cuando la instancia se detiene o se termina, lo que los hace inadecuados para almacenar datos de inicio de sesi\xf3n que deben persistir entre reinicios o reemplazos de instancias. Implementar componentes cr\xedticos de la aplicaci\xf3n en una sola zona de disponibilidad de confianza reduce la tolerancia a fallos y no mejora la seguridad; la pr\xe1ctica recomendada es distribuir entre varias zonas de disponibilidad para lograr resiliencia, y ninguna zona de disponibilidad es inherentemente m\xe1s confiable que otra dentro de la misma regi\xf3n. Amazon Athena es un servicio de consultas sin servidor para analizar datos almacenados en S3 mediante SQL, y no rastrea llamadas a la API; AWS CloudTrail es el servicio correcto para registrar la actividad de la API en una cuenta de AWS."},{"id":"q414","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes estrategias ayuda a proteger su cuenta ra\xedz de AWS?","options":{"A":"Eliminar las claves de acceso del usuario ra\xedz si no las necesita","B":"Aplicar MFA para la cuenta ra\xedz y usarla para todo su trabajo","C":"Acceder a la cuenta ra\xedz solo desde su tel\xe9fono m\xf3vil personal","D":"Compartir solo la contrase\xf1a de su cuenta de AWS o las claves de acceso con personas de confianza"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Eliminar las claves de acceso del usuario ra\xedz elimina un vector de ataque persistente, ya que la cuenta ra\xedz tiene acceso sin restricciones a todos los servicios y recursos de AWS, y la pr\xe1ctica recomendada de AWS es usar usuarios de IAM para las tareas diarias y eliminar por completo las claves de acceso de la cuenta ra\xedz para proteger la cuenta. Aplicar MFA para la cuenta ra\xedz es correcto, pero la segunda parte de la opci\xf3n es incorrecta; AWS recomienda habilitar MFA en la cuenta ra\xedz pero usarla con la menor frecuencia posible y depender de usuarios de IAM para el trabajo diario, no usar la cuenta ra\xedz para todas las tareas. Acceder a la cuenta ra\xedz solo desde un tel\xe9fono m\xf3vil personal no constituye un control de seguridad reconocido; se recomienda MFA mediante una aplicaci\xf3n autenticadora en un dispositivo m\xf3vil, pero restringir el acceso a un dispositivo f\xedsico espec\xedfico no es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de seguridad de AWS e introduce sus propios riesgos. Compartir la contrase\xf1a de su cuenta de AWS o las claves de acceso con personas de confianza viola los principios fundamentales de seguridad; las credenciales nunca deben compartirse, ya que esto elimina la responsabilidad individual y aumenta el riesgo de exposici\xf3n de credenciales."},{"id":"q416","question":"Acaba de configurar su entorno de AWS y ha creado seis cuentas de usuario de IAM para el equipo de DevOps. \xbfCu\xe1l es la recomendaci\xf3n de AWS al otorgar permisos a estas cuentas de IAM?","options":{"A":"Adjuntar una pol\xedtica de IAM separada para cada cuenta individual","B":"Aplicar el principio de menor privilegio","C":"Por motivos de seguridad, no debe otorgar ning\xfan permiso al equipo de DevOps","D":"Crear seis contrase\xf1as de IAM diferentes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El principio de menor privilegio significa otorgar a cada usuario de IAM solo los permisos m\xednimos necesarios para realizar las funciones de su puesto asignado, lo que reduce el impacto de cualquier uso accidental o malicioso de las credenciales. Adjuntar una pol\xedtica de IAM separada a cada cuenta individual es un enfoque v\xe1lido para permisos altamente diferenciados, pero es operativamente complejo; usar grupos de IAM para asignar pol\xedticas compartidas a todos los miembros del equipo de DevOps con roles similares es el enfoque recomendado para equipos en lugar de administrar pol\xedticas por usuario individualmente. Por motivos de seguridad, no debe retener todos los permisos del equipo de DevOps; el equipo necesita permisos adecuados para realizar sus tareas de administraci\xf3n de infraestructura, y el objetivo es definir esos permisos cuidadosamente en lugar de no proporcionar ninguno. Crear seis contrase\xf1as de IAM diferentes implica establecer requisitos de contrase\xf1a distintos por usuario, pero la variaci\xf3n de contrase\xf1as no aborda la preocupaci\xf3n de seguridad central del alcance de los permisos; aplicar principios de menor privilegio a lo que cada usuario puede hacer es la recomendaci\xf3n de AWS."},{"id":"q424","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios proporciona auditor\xeda en tiempo real para cumplimiento y vulnerabilidades?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon MQ","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor","E":"Amazon Cognito"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Config monitorea y registra continuamente las configuraciones de recursos para evaluar el cumplimiento con las reglas deseadas, y AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona comprobaciones en tiempo real en las categor\xedas de seguridad, rendimiento, costo y tolerancia a fallos. Ambos proporcionan capacidades de auditor\xeda en tiempo real.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos en la nube totalmente administrado para ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas en grandes conjuntos de datos, y es un servicio de anal\xedtica de datos en lugar de una herramienta de auditor\xeda en tiempo real para evaluaci\xf3n de cumplimiento o vulnerabilidades. Amazon MQ es un servicio administrado de broker de mensajes para Apache ActiveMQ y RabbitMQ que permite la mensajer\xeda y el desacoplamiento de aplicaciones, y es un servicio de infraestructura de mensajer\xeda sin capacidad de auditor\xeda ni de verificaci\xf3n de cumplimiento. Amazon Cognito es un servicio de identidad de usuario y autenticaci\xf3n para aplicaciones web y m\xf3viles que gestiona el registro, el inicio de sesi\xf3n y el control de acceso, y es un servicio de identidad en lugar de una herramienta de auditor\xeda de cumplimiento o evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades."},{"id":"q428","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 describe mejor las pruebas de penetraci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Probar el tiempo de respuesta de tu aplicaci\xf3n desde diferentes ubicaciones","B":"Probar tu red para encontrar vulnerabilidades de seguridad que un atacante podr\xeda explotar","C":"Probar tus instancias para detectar las que no est\xe1n saludables","D":"Probar tu software en busca de errores y fallos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las pruebas de penetraci\xf3n son la pr\xe1ctica de simular ciberataques contra tus propios sistemas para descubrir vulnerabilidades de seguridad explotables antes de que atacantes maliciosos puedan encontrarlas y usarlas, lo que te permite remediar debilidades de forma proactiva.\\nProbar el tiempo de respuesta de la aplicaci\xf3n desde diferentes ubicaciones describe pruebas de rendimiento y latencia, que eval\xfaan la rapidez con la que una aplicaci\xf3n responde a solicitudes desde diversas ubicaciones geogr\xe1ficas en lugar de identificar vulnerabilidades de seguridad que los atacantes podr\xedan explotar. Probar instancias para detectar las que no est\xe1n saludables describe la verificaci\xf3n de estado o monitoreo, que verifica que las instancias de c\xf3mputo est\xe9n operativas y respondan en lugar de evaluar la postura de seguridad de la red en busca de debilidades explotables. Probar software en busca de errores y fallos describe pruebas funcionales o de aseguramiento de calidad, que validan que el c\xf3digo se comporta como se espera en lugar de sondear espec\xedficamente vulnerabilidades de seguridad que un atacante podr\xeda usar para obtener acceso no autorizado."},{"id":"q443","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones puede ayudar a proteger tus datos sensibles en Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"Eliminar las claves de cifrado una vez que tus datos est\xe9n cifrados","B":"Con AWS no necesitas preocuparte por el cifrado","C":"Habilitar el cifrado de S3","D":"Cifrar los datos antes de cargarlos","E":"Eliminar todos los usuarios de IAM que tengan acceso a S3"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Habilitar el cifrado de S3 mediante cifrado del lado del servidor con claves administradas por S3, claves de KMS o claves proporcionadas por el cliente protege la confidencialidad de los objetos en reposo, y cifrar los datos antes de cargarlos proporciona cifrado del lado del cliente que garantiza que los datos est\xe9n protegidos antes de que salgan del cliente. Ambos son enfoques v\xe1lidos para proteger datos sensibles en Amazon S3.\\nEliminar las claves de cifrado una vez que los datos est\xe1n cifrados impedir\xeda permanentemente el descifrado de los datos almacenados, haciendo imposible volver a leer o usar los datos; las claves de cifrado deben conservarse y administrarse cuidadosamente para garantizar el acceso continuo a los datos cifrados. AWS no maneja autom\xe1ticamente todas las preocupaciones de seguridad de forma predeterminada; los clientes son responsables de configurar el cifrado, los controles de acceso y las pol\xedticas de bucket adecuados para proteger sus datos, ya que el modelo de responsabilidad compartida requiere la participaci\xf3n activa del cliente en la seguridad de los datos. Eliminar todos los usuarios de IAM con acceso a S3 impedir\xeda que todos los usuarios leg\xedtimos accedan a los datos e interrumpir\xeda las operaciones normales; el control de acceso adecuado implica otorgar permisos de m\xednimo privilegio a los usuarios apropiados en lugar de eliminar todo el acceso."},{"id":"q450","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios te da acceso a todos los informes y certificaciones emitidos por auditores de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda y de autoservicio a los informes de cumplimiento de AWS y a las certificaciones emitidas por auditores, como documentos SOC, PCI DSS e ISO, directamente desde la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS sin necesidad de contactar al soporte de AWS.\\nAWS Config monitorea y registra continuamente las configuraciones de recursos frente a reglas de cumplimiento para ayudar a gestionar la desviaci\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n y evaluar el cumplimiento de los recursos, y es una herramienta de cumplimiento de configuraci\xf3n de recursos en lugar de un repositorio de certificaciones emitidas por auditores de AWS. Amazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas operativas, registros y eventos de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS para respaldar la observabilidad y las alertas, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con proporcionar acceso a informes de cumplimiento de AWS ni a certificaciones de auditor\xeda de terceros. AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n en una cuenta de AWS para auditor\xeda y an\xe1lisis de seguridad, y produce registros de actividad espec\xedficos del cliente en lugar de certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS emitidas por auditores."},{"id":"q455","question":"Por motivos de cumplimiento y regulatorios, una agencia gubernamental requiere que sus aplicaciones se ejecuten en hardware dedicado exclusivamente a ellos. \xbfC\xf3mo puedes cumplir este requisito?","options":{"A":"Usar EC2 Dedicated Hosts","B":"Usar EC2 Reserved Instances","C":"Usar EC2 Spot Instances","D":"Usar EC2 On-demand Instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Hosts proporcionan servidores f\xedsicos totalmente dedicados a tu uso, lo que te da visibilidad y control sobre la ubicaci\xf3n de las instancias en hardware que no se comparte con otros clientes de AWS, satisfaciendo estrictos requisitos de cumplimiento y regulatorios de aislamiento f\xedsico.\\nEC2 Reserved Instances son un modelo de precios que proporciona un descuento significativo a cambio de un compromiso de usar un tipo de instancia espec\xedfico durante uno o tres a\xf1os, y no garantizan que el hardware subyacente est\xe9 dedicado a un solo cliente; las instancias reservadas pueden ejecutarse en hardware compartido multiinquilino. EC2 Spot Instances te permiten usar capacidad sobrante de AWS a un precio reducido, con la salvedad de que pueden interrumpirse cuando AWS necesita recuperar la capacidad, y no proporcionan hardware f\xedsico dedicado ni garant\xedas de cumplimiento sobre aislamiento de hardware. EC2 On-Demand Instances te permiten pagar por capacidad de c\xf3mputo por hora o por segundo sin compromisos iniciales, y se ejecutan en hardware compartido multiinquilino en lugar de servidores dedicados, lo que las hace inadecuadas para requisitos de cumplimiento que exigen dedicaci\xf3n de hardware f\xedsico."},{"id":"q458","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 puedes usar para asignar permisos directamente a un usuario de IAM?","options":{"A":"IAM Identity","B":"IAM Group","C":"IAM Role","D":"IAM Policy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Una IAM Policy es un documento JSON que define permisos especificando acciones permitidas o denegadas sobre recursos de AWS, y se puede adjuntar directamente a un usuario de IAM para otorgar derechos de acceso espec\xedficos.\\nIAM Identity no es un componente de IAM independiente que se use para asignar permisos; AWS IAM Identity Center es un servicio separado para administrar el acceso de la fuerza laboral en varias cuentas, y no se refiere a un mecanismo de asignaci\xf3n de permisos dentro de IAM en s\xed. Un IAM Group es una colecci\xf3n de usuarios de IAM que comparten las mismas pol\xedticas adjuntas, y aunque adjuntar una pol\xedtica a un grupo afecta a todos sus miembros, el grupo en s\xed no es lo que asigna permisos directamente a un usuario individual. Un IAM Role es una identidad que define un conjunto de permisos y que usuarios, servicios o aplicaciones asumen temporalmente, y no se adjunta directamente a un usuario de la forma en que lo hace una pol\xedtica."},{"id":"q463","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes usar\xedas para administrar tus claves de cifrado en AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS KMS","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS CodeDeploy","D":"AWS CodeCommit","E":"CloudHSM"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS KMS proporciona administraci\xf3n centralizada de claves basada en software con infraestructura administrada por AWS y se integra de forma nativa con la mayor\xeda de los servicios de AWS para el cifrado en reposo, y AWS CloudHSM proporciona m\xf3dulos de seguridad de hardware dedicados para clientes que requieren control de claves basado en hardware para cumplimiento normativo. Ambos son servicios v\xe1lidos para administrar claves de cifrado en AWS Cloud.\\nAWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y administra certificados SSL/TLS para cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito entre aplicaciones y usuarios, y es un servicio de aprovisionamiento de certificados en lugar de un servicio de administraci\xf3n de claves para crear y almacenar claves de cifrado. AWS CodeDeploy es un servicio de despliegue automatizado que entrega c\xf3digo de aplicaci\xf3n a instancias de EC2, funciones de Lambda y servidores locales, y es una herramienta de automatizaci\xf3n de despliegues sin capacidad de administraci\xf3n de claves de cifrado. AWS CodeCommit es un servicio de control de c\xf3digo fuente totalmente administrado para alojar repositorios de Git para almacenamiento de c\xf3digo con control de versiones, y es una herramienta de colaboraci\xf3n de c\xf3digo en lugar de un servicio de administraci\xf3n de claves de cifrado."},{"id":"q466","question":"Una empresa tiene infraestructura alojada en un centro de datos local. Actualmente tienen un equipo de operaciones que se encarga de la administraci\xf3n de identidades. Si deciden migrar a AWS Cloud, \xbfcu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios les ayudar\xeda a desempe\xf1ar la misma funci\xf3n en AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM","B":"AWS Outposts","C":"AWS Directory Service","D":"Amazon Redshift"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Identity and Access Management es el servicio que proporciona control centralizado sobre la autenticaci\xf3n y autorizaci\xf3n para recursos de AWS, cumpliendo la misma funci\xf3n de administraci\xf3n de identidades en la nube que un equipo de operaciones local desempe\xf1a en un centro de datos tradicional.\\nAWS Outposts extiende la infraestructura y los servicios de AWS a ubicaciones locales y es una soluci\xf3n de c\xf3mputo y almacenamiento en lugar de un servicio de administraci\xf3n de identidades. AWS Directory Service proporciona integraci\xf3n administrada con Microsoft Active Directory para identidad empresarial, lo que puede complementar a IAM, pero es un servicio especializado para organizaciones que necesitan integraci\xf3n con AD en lugar del servicio principal de administraci\xf3n de identidades de AWS. Amazon Redshift es un servicio de almacenamiento de datos en la nube totalmente administrado dise\xf1ado para consultas anal\xedticas en grandes conjuntos de datos y no tiene capacidad para administrar identidades de usuario ni controlar el acceso a servicios de AWS."},{"id":"q468","question":"\xbfD\xf3nde pueden los propietarios de cuentas de AWS obtener una lista de todos los usuarios de su cuenta, incluido el estado de sus credenciales de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"IAM Credential Report","C":"AWS Artifact reports","D":"AWS Cost and Usage Report"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El IAM Credential Report proporciona una lista completa de todos los usuarios de IAM en una cuenta de AWS junto con el estado de sus credenciales, incluidas contrase\xf1as, claves de acceso, estado de MFA y fechas de \xfaltimo uso, lo que lo convierte en la herramienta correcta para auditar las credenciales de los usuarios.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor proporciona comprobaciones automatizadas en las categor\xedas de seguridad, rendimiento, costo y tolerancia a fallos, e incluye algunas comprobaciones de seguridad relacionadas con IAM, pero no genera un informe descargable que enumere a todos los usuarios y los detalles del estado de sus credenciales individuales. Amazon CloudWatch supervisa m\xe9tricas operativas y registros de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS, y no tiene la capacidad de enumerar usuarios de IAM ni de informar sobre el estado de sus credenciales, como claves de acceso y dispositivos MFA. AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y los eventos de administraci\xf3n con fines de auditor\xeda y, aunque registra acciones relacionadas con IAM, no proporciona un informe consolidado de todos los usuarios actuales y el estado en vivo de sus credenciales."},{"id":"q469","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios permite generar y usar f\xe1cilmente sus propias claves de cifrado en la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS CloudHSM","D":"AWS WAF"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS CloudHSM proporciona m\xf3dulos de seguridad de hardware dedicados y de un solo inquilino en la nube de AWS, lo que proporciona control total sobre la generaci\xf3n y gesti\xf3n de claves de cifrado con hardware validado FIPS 140-2 Nivel 3 para clientes que requieren control de claves basado en hardware.\\nAWS Shield proporciona protecci\xf3n DDoS administrada en las capas de red y transporte, y es un servicio de protecci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico sin capacidad para generar o administrar claves de cifrado. AWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y administra certificados SSL/TLS para su uso con servicios de AWS con el fin de cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de certificados en lugar de un servicio de generaci\xf3n de claves de cifrado de uso general. AWS WAF es un firewall de aplicaciones web que filtra el tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS seg\xfan reglas definidas para bloquear exploits web, y es un servicio de filtrado de tr\xe1fico sin capacidad para generar o administrar claves de cifrado de clientes."},{"id":"q473","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes recursos puede usar un cliente de AWS para obtener m\xe1s informaci\xf3n sobre los usos prohibidos de los servicios que ofrece AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Service Control Policies (SCPs)","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Acceptable Use Policy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La AWS Acceptable Use Policy describe los usos prohibidos de los servicios de AWS, incluidas actividades como enviar spam, alojar contenido ilegal o lanzar ataques, y se aplica a todos los clientes y usuarios de AWS.\\nLas AWS Service Control Policies son barreras de permisos aplicadas dentro de AWS Organizations para restringir qu\xe9 acciones pueden realizar las cuentas, no un documento que describa los usos prohibidos de los servicios de AWS para clientes externos. AWS Artifact es un portal de autoservicio para acceder a informes de cumplimiento y documentaci\xf3n de seguridad de AWS, como informes SOC y certificaciones ISO, no un recurso para entender las restricciones de uso. AWS Budgets es una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de costos para establecer umbrales de gasto y recibir alertas, no est\xe1 relacionada con el uso aceptable ni con las actividades prohibidas."},{"id":"q474","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes recursos de seguridad est\xe1n disponibles para cualquier usuario de forma gratuita?","options":{"A":"AWS Bulletins","B":"AWS Technical Account Manager","C":"AWS Support API","D":"AWS Security Blog","E":"AWS Classroom Training"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"La documentaci\xf3n de AWS proporciona gu\xedas t\xe9cnicas completas y referencias de API para todos los servicios de AWS sin costo y es accesible gratuitamente para cualquier usuario, y los foros de discusi\xf3n de AWS permiten a cualquier usuario publicar preguntas y compartir conocimientos sobre los servicios de AWS sin cargo. Ambos son recursos de seguridad gratuitos.\\nAWS Support Center proporciona soporte t\xe9cnico de casos que requiere un plan de soporte de pago para enviar casos y, aunque parte del contenido b\xe1sico est\xe1 disponible de forma gratuita, la funci\xf3n de env\xedo de casos para obtener asistencia directa de ingenier\xeda no es un recurso gratuito disponible para todos los usuarios sin un plan de soporte que cumpla los requisitos. AWS Managed Services proporciona administraci\xf3n continua de la infraestructura de AWS en nombre de clientes empresariales por una tarifa, y es un servicio de pago en lugar de un recurso de seguridad gratuito disponible para cualquier usuario. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades que requiere configuraci\xf3n y genera hallazgos y, aunque hay un per\xedodo de prueba gratuito, es un servicio de pago en lugar de un recurso gratuito disponible para todos los usuarios sin ning\xfan costo."},{"id":"q476","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es responsabilidad de AWS seg\xfan el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS?","options":{"A":"Proteger las regiones y las ubicaciones de borde","B":"Realizar tareas de auditor\xeda","C":"Supervisar el uso de los recursos de AWS","D":"Proteger el acceso a los recursos de AWS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"En el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida, AWS es responsable de proteger la infraestructura f\xedsica, incluidas las regiones, las zonas de disponibilidad y las ubicaciones de borde que conforman la red global de AWS.\\nRealizar tareas de auditor\xeda es responsabilidad del cliente. Los clientes son responsables de auditar su propio uso de recursos, registros de acceso y actividades de cumplimiento. Supervisar el uso de los recursos de AWS es responsabilidad del cliente. Herramientas como CloudWatch est\xe1n disponibles, pero es el cliente quien configura y act\xfaa sobre la supervisi\xf3n. Proteger el acceso a los recursos de AWS es responsabilidad del cliente. La gesti\xf3n de usuarios, roles y permisos de IAM queda dentro de la seguridad en la nube, no de la seguridad de la nube."},{"id":"q479","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios permite generar y usar sus propias claves de cifrado en hardware dedicado en la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS CloudHSM","D":"AWS KMS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS CloudHSM proporciona m\xf3dulos de seguridad de hardware dedicados y de un solo inquilino dentro de la nube de AWS, lo que proporciona a los clientes control total sobre la generaci\xf3n y gesti\xf3n de claves en hardware HSM f\xedsico al que solo ellos pueden acceder, satisfaciendo requisitos regulatorios estrictos para la custodia de claves basada en hardware.\\nAWS Secrets Manager almacena, rota y administra el acceso a secretos como credenciales de bases de datos, claves de API y tokens OAuth, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de secretos en lugar de un servicio para generar claves de cifrado controladas por el cliente en hardware dedicado. AWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y administra certificados SSL/TLS para su uso con servicios de AWS con el fin de cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de certificados que no proporciona claves de cifrado controladas por el cliente en hardware dedicado. AWS KMS es un servicio administrado que genera y gestiona claves de cifrado mediante un servicio basado en software respaldado por hardware que AWS administra y comparte entre varios clientes, y no proporciona el acceso a hardware dedicado de un solo inquilino que ofrece CloudHSM."},{"id":"q480","question":"\xbfQui\xe9n es responsable de escalar una base de datos DynamoDB en el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS?","options":{"A":"Su equipo de seguridad","B":"Su equipo de desarrollo","C":"AWS","D":"Su equipo interno de DevOps"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB es un servicio de base de datos NoSQL totalmente administrado en el que AWS gestiona todo el escalado autom\xe1ticamente, incluidos los ajustes de capacidad de lectura y escritura, por lo que los clientes no necesitan administrar ni planificar el escalado de la base de datos por s\xed mismos.\\nEl equipo de seguridad del cliente se centra en pol\xedticas de seguridad, controles de acceso y cumplimiento en lugar del escalado operativo de un servicio de base de datos administrado, y el escalado autom\xe1tico de DynamoDB lo gestiona la infraestructura de AWS en lugar de cualquier equipo del cliente. El equipo de desarrollo crea y mantiene el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n y, aunque configura los ajustes de la tabla de DynamoDB durante el desarrollo, el escalado real de un servicio totalmente administrado lo realiza AWS autom\xe1ticamente en lugar de los desarrolladores manualmente. El equipo interno de DevOps gestiona la infraestructura, las implementaciones y los flujos de trabajo operativos, pero el escalado de un servicio totalmente administrado como DynamoDB lo gestiona AWS autom\xe1ticamente y no requiere intervenci\xf3n de DevOps."},{"id":"q487","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 funci\xf3n permite a los usuarios iniciar sesi\xf3n en sus cuentas de AWS con sus credenciales corporativas existentes?","options":{"A":"Federaci\xf3n","B":"Claves de acceso","C":"Permisos de IAM","D":"Reglas de AWS WAF"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La federaci\xf3n de identidades permite a los usuarios iniciar sesi\xf3n en AWS usando credenciales de un proveedor de identidades externo, como Active Directory o SAML, lo que elimina la necesidad de crear usuarios de IAM separados para cada empleado corporativo.\\nLas claves de acceso son credenciales de larga duraci\xf3n que consisten en un ID de clave de acceso y un secreto utilizados para el acceso program\xe1tico a AWS mediante la CLI o la API, y no son un mecanismo para iniciar sesi\xf3n en AWS con credenciales corporativas. Los permisos de IAM definen qu\xe9 acciones puede realizar un usuario o rol autenticado en los recursos de AWS, y son un mecanismo de autorizaci\xf3n en lugar de una funci\xf3n que permita el inicio de sesi\xf3n con credenciales corporativas. Las reglas de AWS WAF definen condiciones para filtrar el tr\xe1fico web y proteger las aplicaciones de exploits comunes, y son una funci\xf3n de seguridad web sin conexi\xf3n con la identidad o la autenticaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q488","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1les son los controles que los clientes heredan completamente de AWS?","options":{"A":"Concientizaci\xf3n y capacitaci\xf3n","B":"Controles de comunicaciones","C":"Controles de seguridad del centro de datos","D":"Controles ambientales","E":"Gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n de recursos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Los controles de seguridad del centro de datos y los controles ambientales se heredan completamente de AWS porque los clientes no tienen acceso f\xedsico a las instalaciones de AWS y no asumen ninguna responsabilidad por gestionarlos.\\nConcientizaci\xf3n y capacitaci\xf3n: este es un control compartido en el que tanto AWS como el cliente mantienen de forma independiente sus propios programas de capacitaci\xf3n para sus respectivas plantillas de personal, por lo que los clientes no lo heredan completamente de AWS. Controles de comunicaciones: este tambi\xe9n es un control compartido, y cada parte es responsable de proteger y gestionar las comunicaciones dentro de su propio entorno. Gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n de recursos: los clientes son responsables de configurar los recursos de AWS que implementan, como grupos de seguridad, pol\xedticas de IAM y configuraciones de aplicaciones, lo que hace que este sea un control gestionado por el cliente en lugar de uno heredado."},{"id":"q491","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes forman parte de los siete principios de dise\xf1o para la seguridad en la nube?","options":{"A":"Usar t\xe9cnicas de monitoreo manual para proteger sus recursos de AWS","B":"Usar roles de IAM para otorgar acceso temporal en lugar de credenciales a largo plazo","C":"Escalar horizontalmente para protegerse de las fallas","D":"Habilitar la trazabilidad en tiempo real","E":"Nunca almacenar datos confidenciales en la nube"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Implementar bases s\xf3lidas de identidad mediante el privilegio m\xednimo y la gesti\xf3n adecuada de credenciales, y habilitar la trazabilidad mediante el monitoreo, las alertas y la auditor\xeda de todas las acciones y cambios de recursos, son dos de los siete principios de dise\xf1o de seguridad del Well-Architected Framework.\\nUsar la misma cuenta de AWS para todas las cargas de trabajo en un \xfanico entorno contradice el principio de seguridad de aplicar seguridad en todas las capas y usar varias cuentas para aislar cargas de trabajo, y es un antipatr\xf3n en lugar de un principio de dise\xf1o de seguridad. Reemplazar usuarios de IAM por claves de API como mecanismo de acceso principal usar\xeda credenciales a largo plazo para todo el acceso en lugar de credenciales temporales mediante roles, lo cual es lo opuesto al principio que favorece credenciales a corto plazo y acceso basado en roles. Otorgar acceso de usuario root a todos los usuarios autorizados contradice el principio de otorgar privilegio m\xednimo y proteger las credenciales de la cuenta root, ya que el usuario root debe usarse m\xednimamente y sus credenciales nunca deben compartirse."},{"id":"q493","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el beneficio principal de adjuntar grupos de seguridad a una instancia de Amazon RDS?","options":{"A":"Gestiona el acceso de usuarios y las claves de cifrado","B":"Controla qu\xe9 rangos de direcciones IP pueden conectarse a su instancia de base de datos","C":"Despliega certificados SSL/TLS para usarlos con su instancia de base de datos","D":"Distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre varios destinos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los grupos de seguridad adjuntos a una instancia de RDS act\xfaan como un firewall virtual que controla qu\xe9 rangos de direcciones IP y qu\xe9 instancias de EC2 pueden establecer conexiones a la base de datos, restringiendo el acceso de red solo a fuentes autorizadas.\\nGestionar el acceso de usuarios y las claves de cifrado describe AWS IAM y AWS KMS, respectivamente, que son servicios separados para la gesti\xf3n de identidades y la gesti\xf3n de claves, en lugar de funciones proporcionadas por los grupos de seguridad adjuntos a una instancia de RDS. El despliegue de certificados SSL/TLS para conexiones de base de datos lo realiza AWS Certificate Manager y la configuraci\xf3n del grupo de par\xe1metros de RDS, no los grupos de seguridad, que controlan la conectividad de red en lugar del aprovisionamiento de certificados. Distribuir el tr\xe1fico entrante entre varios destinos describe un Elastic Load Balancer, que enruta solicitudes a varias instancias de backend, y no es una funci\xf3n de los grupos de seguridad adjuntos a una instancia de base de datos."},{"id":"q496","question":"Se le ha encargado auditar la seguridad de su VPC. Como parte de este proceso, debe comenzar analizando qu\xe9 tr\xe1fico entrante y saliente est\xe1 permitido en sus instancias de EC2. \xbfQu\xe9 dos partes de la VPC debe revisar para realizar esta tarea?","options":{"A":"Network ACLs y Traffic Manager","B":"Network ACLs y subredes","C":"Grupos de seguridad e Internet Gateways","D":"Grupos de seguridad y Network ACLs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Los grupos de seguridad controlan el tr\xe1fico entrante y saliente a nivel de instancia de EC2 con evaluaci\xf3n con estado, y las Network ACLs controlan el tr\xe1fico a nivel de subred con evaluaci\xf3n de reglas sin estado. Revisar ambos proporciona una imagen completa del tr\xe1fico permitido hacia y desde las instancias de EC2 en la VPC.\\nNetwork ACLs y Traffic Manager no es una combinaci\xf3n v\xe1lida para el an\xe1lisis de tr\xe1fico de VPC; AWS Traffic Manager no existe como nombre de servicio de AWS, y el control correcto a nivel de subred son las Network ACLs, no un Traffic Manager inexistente. Network ACLs y subredes es parcialmente correcto en el sentido de que las subredes son componentes de VPC, pero las subredes en s\xed son segmentos de red en lugar de mecanismos de control de tr\xe1fico, y revisar las subredes no indica qu\xe9 reglas de tr\xe1fico se aplican; debe revisar las ACL asociadas a esas subredes. Grupos de seguridad e Internet Gateways es parcialmente correcto en el sentido de que Internet Gateways permiten el tr\xe1fico de internet hacia la VPC, pero no definen reglas granulares de permitir/denegar para el tr\xe1fico a nivel de instancia; revisar solo los grupos de seguridad omite los controles a nivel de subred proporcionados por las Network ACLs."},{"id":"q499","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n es verdadera en relaci\xf3n con la seguridad en AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS gestiona todo lo relacionado con los sistemas operativos de EC2","B":"Los clientes de AWS son responsables de aplicar parches a cualquier software de base de datos que se ejecute en Amazon EC2","C":"El cifrado del lado del servidor es responsabilidad de AWS","D":"AWS es responsable de la seguridad de su aplicaci\xf3n"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, los clientes son responsables de aplicar parches y mantener cualquier software que instalen en instancias de EC2, incluidos el software de base de datos, los sistemas operativos y las aplicaciones.\\nAWS no gestiona todo lo relacionado con los sistemas operativos de EC2. El sistema operativo invitado, incluida su configuraci\xf3n, aplicaci\xf3n de parches y seguridad, es responsabilidad del cliente, mientras que AWS gestiona solo la infraestructura f\xedsica subyacente. El cifrado del lado del servidor no es autom\xe1ticamente responsabilidad de AWS. Los clientes eligen si habilitar el cifrado y pueden gestionar sus propias claves mediante AWS KMS, lo que hace que el cifrado sea una responsabilidad compartida seg\xfan c\xf3mo se configure. AWS no es responsable de la seguridad de las aplicaciones de los clientes. La seguridad a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n, incluida la calidad del c\xf3digo, los controles de acceso y el manejo de datos, es totalmente responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida."},{"id":"q507","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son las principales diferencias entre un usuario de IAM y un rol de IAM en AWS?","options":{"A":"Un usuario de IAM est\xe1 asociado de forma \xfanica con una sola persona; sin embargo, un rol est\xe1 pensado para que lo asuma cualquiera que lo necesite","B":"Un usuario de IAM tiene credenciales permanentes asociadas; sin embargo, un rol tiene credenciales temporales asociadas","C":"Los usuarios de IAM son m\xe1s rentables que los roles de IAM","D":"Un rol est\xe1 asociado de forma \xfanica con una sola persona; sin embargo, un usuario de IAM est\xe1 pensado para que lo asuma cualquiera que lo necesite","E":"Un usuario de IAM tiene credenciales temporales asociadas; sin embargo, un rol tiene credenciales permanentes asociadas"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Un usuario de IAM es una identidad permanente asociada con una persona espec\xedfica y tiene credenciales a largo plazo, como una contrase\xf1a y claves de acceso, mientras que un rol de IAM proporciona credenciales temporales y pueden asumirlo varios usuarios, servicios o aplicaciones que lo necesiten.\\nLa opci\xf3n que afirma que un rol est\xe1 asociado de forma \xfanica con una sola persona y que cualquier persona puede asumir un usuario invierte la relaci\xf3n correcta; los usuarios de IAM representan personas individuales con sus propias credenciales, mientras que los roles est\xe1n dise\xf1ados para que los asuma cualquier persona o servicio que requiera los permisos que definen. La opci\xf3n que afirma que los usuarios de IAM tienen credenciales temporales y que los roles tienen credenciales permanentes es lo opuesto a la relaci\xf3n correcta; los usuarios de IAM tienen credenciales permanentes a largo plazo, mientras que los roles emiten credenciales temporales que expiran autom\xe1ticamente cuando finaliza la sesi\xf3n. Los usuarios y roles de IAM no tienen precios diferentes entre s\xed; no hay un costo directo asociado con la creaci\xf3n de usuarios o roles de IAM, y la afirmaci\xf3n de que los usuarios son m\xe1s rentables no es una comparaci\xf3n significativa."},{"id":"q509","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 hace Amazon GuardDuty para proteger las cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS?","options":{"A":"Notifica a los clientes de AWS sobre eventos de abuso una vez que se reportan","B":"Monitorea continuamente la infraestructura de AWS y ayuda a detectar amenazas como el reconocimiento por parte de atacantes o el compromiso de cuentas","C":"Ayuda a los clientes de AWS a identificar la causa ra\xedz de posibles problemas de seguridad","D":"Revisa los grupos de seguridad en busca de reglas que permitan acceso sin restricciones a los recursos de AWS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que monitorea continuamente las cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS mediante aprendizaje autom\xe1tico e inteligencia sobre amenazas para identificar actividad sospechosa, como el reconocimiento por parte de atacantes, instancias comprometidas o llamadas inusuales a la API.\\nNotificar a los clientes sobre eventos de abuso una vez que se reportan es funci\xf3n del AWS Abuse Team, que gestiona los reportes de recursos de AWS utilizados para actividad maliciosa y se comunica con los titulares de cuentas afectados, en lugar de ser una capacidad de GuardDuty. Ayudar a los clientes a identificar la causa ra\xedz de posibles problemas de seguridad describe Amazon Detective, que analiza y visualiza datos de seguridad de GuardDuty y CloudTrail para rastrear el origen y el alcance de la actividad sospechosa en lugar de detectar amenazas en tiempo real. Revisar los grupos de seguridad en busca de reglas que permitan acceso sin restricciones a los recursos de AWS es una funci\xf3n de AWS Trusted Advisor, que realiza comprobaciones de seguridad, incluida la identificaci\xf3n de configuraciones de grupos de seguridad demasiado permisivas, no de GuardDuty."},{"id":"q516","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes requiere un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta para obtener acceso program\xe1tico de larga duraci\xf3n a los recursos de AWS?","options":{"A":"Grupo de IAM","B":"Usuario de IAM","C":"Rol de IAM","D":"Usuario root de la cuenta de AWS","E":"AWS Technical Account Manager"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Los usuarios de IAM y el usuario root de la cuenta de AWS son ambas identidades a las que se pueden emitir IDs de clave de acceso y claves de acceso secretas de larga duraci\xf3n para el acceso program\xe1tico a los recursos de AWS mediante la CLI o los SDK.\\nUn grupo de IAM es una colecci\xf3n de usuarios de IAM que se utiliza para gestionar permisos compartidos de forma colectiva, y no es una identidad que pueda autenticarse ni recibir claves de acceso de ning\xfan tipo. Un rol de IAM utiliza credenciales de seguridad temporales que se emiten y rotan autom\xe1ticamente cuando se asume el rol, y no admite claves de acceso de larga duraci\xf3n. Un AWS Technical Account Manager es un profesional de soporte de AWS asignado a clientes del plan de soporte Enterprise, y es una persona en lugar de una construcci\xf3n de identidad de IAM que pueda tener o recibir claves de acceso."},{"id":"q523","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS se integra con AWS Shield y AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF) para protegerse contra ataques DDoS en la capa de red y de aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Amazon EFS","B":"AWS Secrets Manager","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront se integra de forma nativa con AWS Shield para la protecci\xf3n contra DDoS en la capa de red y con AWS WAF para el filtrado de tr\xe1fico en la capa de aplicaci\xf3n, lo que proporciona una defensa por capas contra ataques DDoS tanto en la capa de red como en la de aplicaci\xf3n en el borde.\\nAmazon EFS es un sistema de archivos el\xe1stico totalmente administrado para proporcionar almacenamiento de archivos compartido a instancias EC2 y otros servicios de c\xf3mputo, y es un servicio de almacenamiento sin capacidad de integrarse con Shield o WAF para la protecci\xf3n contra DDoS. AWS Secrets Manager almacena, rota y administra el acceso a secretos como credenciales de bases de datos y claves de API, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de credenciales en lugar de un servicio que se integre con Shield y WAF para la protecci\xf3n del tr\xe1fico. AWS Systems Manager proporciona herramientas operativas para administrar recursos de AWS a escala, incluidos parcheo, administraci\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de sesiones, y es un servicio de operaciones en lugar de un servicio de entrega de contenido o filtrado de tr\xe1fico."},{"id":"q524","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios se utiliza al cifrar vol\xfamenes de EBS?","options":{"A":"AWS WAF","B":"AWS KMS","C":"Amazon Macie","D":"Amazon GuardDuty"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS KMS administra las claves de cifrado utilizadas para el cifrado de vol\xfamenes de EBS, encarg\xe1ndose de la creaci\xf3n, el almacenamiento, la rotaci\xf3n y el control de acceso de las claves, y se integra de forma transparente con EBS para cifrar datos en reposo.\\nAWS WAF es un firewall de aplicaciones web que filtra tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS para bloquear exploits web comunes, como inyecci\xf3n SQL y cross-site scripting, y es un servicio de filtrado de tr\xe1fico sin ninguna funci\xf3n en la administraci\xf3n de claves de cifrado para vol\xfamenes de EBS. Amazon Macie usa machine learning para descubrir y clasificar datos sensibles almacenados en Amazon S3, y es un servicio de clasificaci\xf3n de datos para S3 en lugar de un servicio de administraci\xf3n de claves para el cifrado de EBS. Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que monitorea cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS en busca de actividad maliciosa mediante machine learning y threat intelligence, y es un servicio de monitoreo de seguridad en lugar de un servicio de administraci\xf3n de claves para cifrar vol\xfamenes de EBS."},{"id":"q525","question":"El administrador de la cuenta de AWS de su empresa ha sido despedido. Con los permisos que se le otorgaron como administrador, pudo crear m\xfaltiples cuentas de usuario de IAM y claves de acceso. Adem\xe1s, no est\xe1 seguro de si tiene acceso a la cuenta ra\xedz (root) de AWS o no. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer de inmediato para proteger su infraestructura de AWS?","options":{"A":"Descargar todas las pol\xedticas adjuntas en un lugar seguro","B":"Eliminar todas las cuentas de IAM y volver a crearlas","C":"Usar el servicio CloudWatch para verificar todas las llamadas a la API que se han realizado en su cuenta desde que el administrador fue despedido","D":"Rotar todas las claves de acceso","E":"Cambiar la direcci\xf3n de correo electr\xf3nico y la contrase\xf1a de la cuenta de usuario ra\xedz y habilitar MFA"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Rotar todas las claves de acceso de inmediato invalida cualquier clave que el exadministrador haya podido copiar o conservar, y cambiar el correo electr\xf3nico y la contrase\xf1a de la cuenta ra\xedz con MFA habilitado impide el acceso a nivel ra\xedz mediante cualquier credencial que el administrador haya podido tener.\\nDescargar las pol\xedticas adjuntas a un lugar seguro no aborda el riesgo de seguridad inmediato; la prioridad es revocar el acceso del exadministrador en lugar de preservar documentaci\xf3n de pol\xedticas, y las pol\xedticas se pueden revisar desde la consola en cualquier momento. Eliminar todas las cuentas de IAM y volver a crearlas bloquear\xeda inmediatamente a todo el equipo e interrumpir\xeda las operaciones sin abordar realmente si la cuenta ra\xedz est\xe1 comprometida; el cambio de contrase\xf1a de la cuenta ra\xedz debe tener prioridad. Usar CloudWatch para verificar todas las llamadas a la API realizadas desde que el administrador fue despedido es un paso forense \xfatil para comprender qu\xe9 acciones se tomaron, pero es una actividad de investigaci\xf3n posterior al incidente en lugar de una medida de protecci\xf3n inmediata que deber\xeda hacerse antes de asegurar las credenciales."},{"id":"q531","question":"Una empresa de servicios financieros decide migrar una de sus aplicaciones a AWS. La aplicaci\xf3n maneja datos sensibles, como informaci\xf3n de tarjetas de cr\xe9dito, y debe ejecutarse en un entorno compatible con PCI. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es responsabilidad de la empresa al construir un entorno compatible con PCI en AWS?","options":{"A":"Comenzar el proceso de migraci\xf3n de inmediato, ya que todos los servicios de AWS cumplen con PCI","B":"Asegurarse de que los servicios de AWS est\xe9n configurados correctamente para cumplir con todos los est\xe1ndares PCI DSS","C":"Restringir cualquier acceso a los datos de los titulares de tarjetas y crear una pol\xedtica que aborde la seguridad de la informaci\xf3n para todo el personal","D":"Configurar la infraestructura subyacente de los servicios de AWS para cumplir con todos los requisitos de PCI DSS","E":"Asegurarse de que se cumplan todos los requisitos de seguridad f\xedsica de PCI DSS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Los clientes deben configurar correctamente los servicios de AWS para cumplir con los est\xe1ndares PCI DSS, ya que las certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS cubren la capa de infraestructura pero no las configuraciones espec\xedficas de los servicios del cliente, y los clientes deben restringir el acceso a los datos de los titulares de tarjetas y mantener una pol\xedtica de seguridad de la informaci\xf3n, ya que estas son responsabilidades del cliente seg\xfan PCI DSS.\\nNo todos los servicios de AWS son compatibles con PCI DSS de forma predeterminada; solo servicios espec\xedficos de AWS est\xe1n dentro del alcance del cumplimiento de PCI, y los clientes deben verificar que los servicios que utilizan est\xe9n dentro del alcance y configurados correctamente para mantener un entorno compatible con PCI. Configurar la infraestructura subyacente de AWS para cumplir con los requisitos de PCI DSS no es responsabilidad del cliente; AWS administra y mantiene la infraestructura f\xedsica, el hardware y la red para cumplir con los est\xe1ndares PCI, y los clientes solo son responsables de c\xf3mo configuran los servicios construidos sobre esa infraestructura. Asegurarse de que se cumplan todos los requisitos de seguridad f\xedsica de PCI DSS es responsabilidad de AWS; la seguridad f\xedsica de los centros de datos, incluidos los controles de acceso, la vigilancia y el manejo del hardware, es administrada por AWS bajo el modelo de responsabilidad compartida."},{"id":"q535","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes pasos debe tomar un cliente al realizar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n en AWS?","options":{"A":"Realizar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n usando Amazon Inspector y luego notificar al soporte de AWS","B":"Solicitar y esperar la aprobaci\xf3n del equipo de seguridad interno del cliente, y luego realizar las pruebas","C":"Notificar al soporte de AWS y luego realizar las pruebas de inmediato","D":"Solicitar y esperar la aprobaci\xf3n del soporte de AWS, y luego realizar las pruebas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Desde 2019, AWS permite a los clientes realizar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n en servicios permitidos, incluidos EC2, RDS, CloudFront y API Gateway, sin aprobaci\xf3n previa de AWS, por lo que los clientes deben seguir su propio proceso interno de revisi\xf3n y autorizaci\xf3n de seguridad antes de realizar las pruebas.\\nAmazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades que escanea en busca de vulnerabilidades conocidas y configuraciones de red incorrectas, pero no es una herramienta de pruebas de penetraci\xf3n y usarlo no sustituye ni requiere notificar al soporte de AWS antes de realizar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n reales. AWS ya no requiere que los clientes notifiquen al soporte de AWS antes de realizar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n en servicios permitidos; la pol\xedtica cambi\xf3 para permitir pruebas sin aprobaci\xf3n previa, por lo que notificar y luego realizar las pruebas de inmediato se basa en un requisito obsoleto. Solicitar y esperar la aprobaci\xf3n del soporte de AWS antes de realizar pruebas de penetraci\xf3n ya no es necesario para los servicios permitidos; AWS actualiz\xf3 su pol\xedtica para permitir que los clientes prueben sus propios recursos sin aprobaci\xf3n previa, eliminando el requisito de un proceso de aprobaci\xf3n formal."},{"id":"q550","question":"\xbfDe qu\xe9 tarea es responsable AWS en el modelo de responsabilidad compartida para seguridad y cumplimiento?","options":{"A":"Otorgar acceso a personas y servicios","B":"Cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito","C":"Actualizar el firmware del host de Amazon EC2","D":"Actualizar sistemas operativos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Actualizar el firmware del host en los servidores f\xedsicos de EC2 es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni control sobre el hardware subyacente debajo de sus instancias.\\nOtorgar acceso a personas y servicios es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes administran sus propios usuarios, roles y permisos de IAM dentro de su cuenta de AWS. Cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes deciden si implementar cifrado para sus propios datos y c\xf3mo hacerlo usando HTTPS, TLS u otros mecanismos. Actualizar sistemas operativos es responsabilidad del cliente en servicios como EC2, donde los clientes controlan el sistema operativo invitado y son responsables de mantenerlo parcheado y actualizado."},{"id":"q553","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1l es la responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS?","options":{"A":"Seguridad de las aplicaciones","B":"Gesti\xf3n de edge locations","C":"Gesti\xf3n de parches","D":"Datos del lado del cliente"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Administrar las edge locations, la infraestructura f\xedsica utilizada por CloudFront y otros servicios, es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS como parte de su obligaci\xf3n de operar y mantener la infraestructura global que sustenta sus servicios.\\nLa seguridad de las aplicaciones es responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida, ya que los clientes son responsables de proteger el c\xf3digo, las configuraciones y los controles de acceso dentro de sus propias aplicaciones. La gesti\xf3n de parches es un control compartido donde AWS parchea la infraestructura subyacente y los servicios administrados, mientras que los clientes mantienen la responsabilidad de parchear sus propios sistemas operativos y aplicaciones. Los datos del lado del cliente son responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes controlan c\xf3mo se clasifican, cifran y administran sus datos del lado del cliente independientemente de los servicios de AWS que utilicen."},{"id":"q555","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS identifica los grupos de seguridad que permiten acceso sin restricciones a los recursos de AWS de un usuario?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"Amazon Inspector","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor verifica los grupos de seguridad en busca de reglas que permitan acceso sin restricciones (0.0.0.0/0) a puertos espec\xedficos, como SSH y RDP, y se\xf1ala posibles riesgos de seguridad en sus verificaciones de la categor\xeda Security.\\nAmazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencional, y aunque identifica problemas de alcanzabilidad de red, la herramienta m\xe1s simple para verificar los grupos de seguridad en busca de acceso sin restricciones es Trusted Advisor. Amazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas operativas y registros de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS, y es un servicio de observabilidad en lugar de una herramienta que audita configuraciones de grupos de seguridad en busca de reglas demasiado permisivas. AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n para auditor\xeda, capturando qui\xe9n cre\xf3 o modific\xf3 reglas de grupos de seguridad y cu\xe1ndo, pero registra acciones despu\xe9s de que ocurren en lugar de identificar de forma proactiva grupos con acceso sin restricciones."},{"id":"q564","question":"En el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1les de las siguientes actividades son responsabilidad del cliente?","options":{"A":"Aplicar parches a los componentes del sistema operativo para Amazon Relational Database Server (Amazon RDS)","B":"Cifrar datos en el lado del cliente","C":"Capacitar al personal del centro de datos","D":"Configurar listas de control de acceso de red (Network Access Control Lists, ACL)","E":"Mantener los controles ambientales dentro de un centro de datos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Los clientes son responsables de cifrar los datos en el lado del cliente antes de enviarlos a AWS, y de configurar las listas de control de acceso de red (ACL) para controlar el tr\xe1fico de entrada y salida a nivel de subred en su VPC.\\nAplicar parches a los componentes del sistema operativo para Amazon RDS es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que RDS es un servicio administrado en el que AWS gestiona la aplicaci\xf3n de parches de todo el sistema operativo subyacente y del motor de base de datos en nombre del cliente. Capacitar al personal del centro de datos es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni participaci\xf3n en las instalaciones f\xedsicas ni en los equipos de operaciones que ejecutan la infraestructura de AWS. Mantener los controles ambientales dentro de un centro de datos, como energ\xeda, refrigeraci\xf3n y supresi\xf3n de incendios, es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS y los clientes la heredan por completo en el modelo."},{"id":"q565","question":"En el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, \xbfcu\xe1l de los siguientes es un control compartido entre un cliente y AWS?","options":{"A":"Controles f\xedsicos","B":"Gesti\xf3n de parches","C":"Seguridad de zona","D":"Auditor\xeda del centro de datos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La gesti\xf3n de parches es un control compartido. AWS aplica parches a la infraestructura subyacente y a las plataformas de servicios administrados, mientras que los clientes son responsables de aplicar parches a sus propios sistemas operativos invitados y aplicaciones.\\nLos controles f\xedsicos son responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS y abarcan la seguridad f\xedsica de los centros de datos y del hardware. La seguridad de zona tambi\xe9n es responsabilidad de AWS y abarca el aislamiento f\xedsico y de red entre las zonas de disponibilidad. La auditor\xeda del centro de datos es responsabilidad de AWS, aunque los clientes pueden solicitar informes de cumplimiento a trav\xe9s de AWS Artifact."},{"id":"q573","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS puede mejorar la seguridad de red al bloquear solicitudes de una red en particular para una aplicaci\xf3n web en AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS WAF","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS Organizations","E":"Listas de control de acceso de red (Network ACLs)"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS WAF crea reglas para bloquear solicitudes de direcciones IP espec\xedficas, redes o que coincidan con ciertos patrones, filtrando tr\xe1fico web malicioso en la capa de aplicaci\xf3n, y Network ACLs son firewalls sin estado a nivel de subred que pueden bloquear tr\xe1fico de rangos de IP espec\xedficos en la capa de red. Ambos pueden mejorar la seguridad de red al bloquear solicitudes de redes en particular.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas en las categor\xedas de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos y, aunque identifica configuraciones inseguras, no bloquea por s\xed mismo solicitudes de red ni aplica activamente reglas de tr\xe1fico. AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre centros de datos locales y AWS, y es un servicio de conectividad, no una herramienta para bloquear o filtrar tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones web. AWS Organizations administra varias cuentas de AWS bajo una estructura central para la gobernanza y la facturaci\xf3n consolidada, y no tiene la capacidad de bloquear tr\xe1fico de red de rangos de IP espec\xedficos para aplicaciones web."},{"id":"q575","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio permite la auditor\xeda de riesgos al monitorear y registrar continuamente la actividad de la cuenta, incluidas las acciones del usuario en la AWS Management Console y en los AWS SDKs?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Config","D":"AWS Health"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra continuamente toda la actividad de la cuenta y las llamadas a la API en la infraestructura de AWS, lo que proporciona un registro de auditor\xeda completo que respalda la auditor\xeda de riesgos, el monitoreo de cumplimiento y el an\xe1lisis de seguridad.\\nAmazon CloudWatch recopila m\xe9tricas, registros y datos de eventos de servicios y recursos de AWS para proporcionar visibilidad operativa sobre el rendimiento y el estado y, aunque puede monitorear registros, no registra llamadas a la API ni acciones del usuario para fines de auditor\xeda de riesgos y cumplimiento. AWS Config rastrea y registra continuamente el estado de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento con la configuraci\xf3n deseada, y se enfoca en cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos en lugar de registrar acciones del usuario y llamadas a la API para auditor\xeda de riesgos. AWS Health proporciona alertas personalizadas y orientaci\xf3n de remediaci\xf3n cuando AWS experimenta eventos que pueden afectar sus recursos, y es una herramienta de notificaci\xf3n del estado del servicio, no un servicio de registro de actividad de la cuenta ni de auditor\xeda de riesgos."},{"id":"q576","question":"\xbfD\xf3nde se pueden descargar los informes de cumplimiento y certificaci\xf3n de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Concierge","C":"AWS Certificate Manager","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact es el portal de autoservicio donde los clientes pueden descargar informes de cumplimiento y certificaci\xf3n de AWS, incluida la documentaci\xf3n de elegibilidad para SOC, PCI, ISO y HIPAA, sin necesidad de contactar directamente a AWS.\\nAWS Concierge es un recurso de soporte dedicado para clientes del plan de soporte Enterprise que ayuda con la facturaci\xf3n y la administraci\xf3n de cuentas, y no es un portal para descargar informes de cumplimiento ni documentos de certificaci\xf3n t\xe9cnica. AWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y administra certificados SSL/TLS para cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito entre aplicaciones y usuarios, y es un servicio de aprovisionamiento de certificados, no un repositorio de informes de cumplimiento y auditor\xeda de AWS. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas en las categor\xedas de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos y, aunque incluye verificaciones de seguridad, no proporciona informes descargables de certificaci\xf3n de cumplimiento de AWS."},{"id":"q581","question":"\xbfDe qu\xe9 es responsable el cliente de AWS seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS?","options":{"A":"Controles de acceso f\xedsico","B":"Cifrado de datos","C":"Eliminaci\xf3n segura de dispositivos de almacenamiento","D":"Gesti\xf3n de riesgos ambientales"},"correct":"B","explanation":"El cifrado de datos es responsabilidad del cliente en el modelo de responsabilidad compartida. AWS proporciona las herramientas y servicios para habilitar el cifrado, como AWS KMS y opciones de cifrado nativas en los servicios de almacenamiento, pero el cliente decide si habilitar el cifrado y administra las claves y las pol\xedticas de acceso.\\nLos controles de acceso f\xedsico en los centros de datos de AWS son responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS; los clientes no tienen acceso a las instalaciones f\xedsicas y dependen de AWS para implementar y aplicar las medidas de seguridad f\xedsica. La eliminaci\xf3n segura de dispositivos de almacenamiento es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que los clientes nunca tienen posesi\xf3n f\xedsica del hardware de AWS y AWS sigue est\xe1ndares de la industria para la desmantelaci\xf3n y eliminaci\xf3n de medios de almacenamiento. La gesti\xf3n de riesgos ambientales para la infraestructura de AWS, incluido el control de temperatura, la humedad y la supresi\xf3n de incendios en los centros de datos, es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS como parte de la protecci\xf3n de la infraestructura f\xedsica subyacente."},{"id":"q587","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS usar\xeda para obtener informes de cumplimiento y certificados?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Lambda","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Certificate Manager"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes de cumplimiento y certificaciones de AWS, incluidos documentos de SOC, PCI DSS, ISO y HIPAA, disponibles directamente desde la AWS Management Console sin necesidad de contactar al soporte de AWS.\\nAWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor que ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin requerir aprovisionamiento ni administraci\xf3n de servidores, y es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin conexi\xf3n con informes de cumplimiento ni documentaci\xf3n de certificaci\xf3n. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y configuraciones de red incorrectas, y produce hallazgos de vulnerabilidades espec\xedficos del cliente en lugar de certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS. AWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y administra certificados SSL/TLS para su uso con servicios de AWS, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de certificados, no un repositorio de informes de cumplimiento ni certificaciones regulatorias."},{"id":"q589","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo aplicar\xeda f\xe1cilmente un cliente de AWS controles de acceso comunes a un gran conjunto de usuarios?","options":{"A":"Aplicar una pol\xedtica de IAM a un grupo de IAM","B":"Aplicar una pol\xedtica de IAM a un rol de IAM","C":"Aplicar la misma pol\xedtica de IAM a todos los usuarios de IAM con acceso a la misma carga de trabajo","D":"Aplicar una pol\xedtica de IAM a un user pool de Amazon Cognito"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Adjuntar una pol\xedtica de IAM a un grupo de IAM aplica esos permisos a todos los usuarios del grupo simult\xe1neamente, lo que lo convierte en la forma m\xe1s eficiente de administrar controles de acceso comunes para un gran n\xfamero de usuarios sin configurar cada usuario individualmente.\\nAplicar una pol\xedtica de IAM a un rol de IAM afectar\xeda a las entidades que asumen ese rol, como aplicaciones o servicios, en lugar de aplicar controles consistentes directamente a un gran conjunto de usuarios nombrados que necesitan los mismos permisos. Aplicar la misma pol\xedtica de IAM a todos los usuarios de IAM individualmente lograr\xeda el resultado deseado, pero requiere editar la pol\xedtica de cada usuario por separado, lo cual consume mucho tiempo y es propenso a errores a escala en comparaci\xf3n con usar un grupo. Aplicar una pol\xedtica de IAM a un user pool de Amazon Cognito afecta a los usuarios de aplicaciones que se autentican mediante Cognito, no a los usuarios de IAM que administran recursos de AWS, y se usa para el control de acceso a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n en lugar de la administraci\xf3n de permisos de servicios de AWS."},{"id":"q618","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un componente del modelo de responsabilidad compartida gestionado completamente por AWS?","options":{"A":"Aplicaci\xf3n de parches al software del sistema operativo","B":"Cifrado de datos","C":"Aplicaci\xf3n de autenticaci\xf3n multifactor","D":"Auditor\xeda de los activos f\xedsicos del centro de datos"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La auditor\xeda de los activos f\xedsicos del centro de datos, incluidos el inventario de hardware, las inspecciones de las instalaciones y los registros de acceso f\xedsico, es responsabilidad completamente de AWS seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, ya que los clientes nunca tienen acceso f\xedsico ni visibilidad sobre la infraestructura de los centros de datos de AWS. La aplicaci\xf3n de parches al software del sistema operativo en instancias EC2 es responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, ya que AWS gestiona el hipervisor y el hardware subyacentes, pero los clientes son responsables de mantener el sistema operativo invitado en sus m\xe1quinas virtuales. El cifrado de datos en reposo y en tr\xe1nsito es responsabilidad del cliente; AWS proporciona las herramientas y los servicios para habilitar el cifrado, como KMS y ACM, pero los clientes deben decidir qu\xe9 cifrar y configurarlo adecuadamente. La aplicaci\xf3n de autenticaci\xf3n multifactor para usuarios de IAM y la cuenta ra\xedz es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que AWS proporciona la funcionalidad de MFA, pero los clientes deben habilitarla y configurarla para su cuenta y sus usuarios."},{"id":"q628","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es responsabilidad completamente de AWS, seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS?","options":{"A":"Aplicaci\xf3n de parches al sistema operativo invitado","B":"Concientizaci\xf3n y capacitaci\xf3n en seguridad","C":"Controles f\xedsicos y ambientales","D":"Desarrollo de una pol\xedtica de contrase\xf1as de IAM"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los controles f\xedsicos y ambientales, incluidos la seguridad del centro de datos, los sistemas de refrigeraci\xf3n, la supresi\xf3n de incendios y la gesti\xf3n de energ\xeda, son responsabilidad completamente de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni participaci\xf3n en la gesti\xf3n de la infraestructura f\xedsica de AWS. La aplicaci\xf3n de parches al sistema operativo invitado en instancias EC2 es responsabilidad del cliente; AWS gestiona el hardware y el hipervisor subyacentes, pero los clientes son responsables de mantener actualizados el sistema operativo y las aplicaciones en sus instancias. La concientizaci\xf3n y capacitaci\xf3n en seguridad para los empleados y usuarios del entorno de AWS es responsabilidad del cliente; AWS proporciona orientaci\xf3n sobre mejores pr\xe1cticas de seguridad y recursos de capacitaci\xf3n, pero el programa de capacitaci\xf3n de la propia organizaci\xf3n para su personal es una obligaci\xf3n del cliente. Desarrollar una pol\xedtica de contrase\xf1as de IAM es responsabilidad del cliente; AWS proporciona IAM con configuraciones de pol\xedtica de contrase\xf1as configurables, pero definir y aplicar los requisitos espec\xedficos de contrase\xf1as para los usuarios de IAM de una organizaci\xf3n es una tarea de configuraci\xf3n del cliente."},{"id":"q633","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de AWS IAM se utiliza para asociar un conjunto de permisos con varios usuarios?","options":{"A":"Autenticaci\xf3n multifactor","B":"Grupos","C":"Pol\xedticas de contrase\xf1as","D":"Claves de acceso"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los Grupos de IAM permiten adjuntar pol\xedticas de permisos al grupo, y todos los usuarios agregados a ese grupo heredan autom\xe1ticamente esos permisos, lo que lo convierte en la caracter\xedstica de IAM utilizada para asociar un conjunto de permisos con varios usuarios a la vez. La autenticaci\xf3n multifactor agrega una segunda capa de verificaci\xf3n m\xe1s all\xe1 de las contrase\xf1as para proteger los inicios de sesi\xf3n individuales de los usuarios, y es un mecanismo de seguridad de autenticaci\xf3n en lugar de una caracter\xedstica para asociar un conjunto de permisos con varios usuarios simult\xe1neamente. Las pol\xedticas de contrase\xf1as definen reglas de complejidad, rotaci\xf3n y reutilizaci\xf3n de contrase\xf1as para las cuentas de usuario de IAM, y son una caracter\xedstica de gesti\xf3n de credenciales de autenticaci\xf3n en lugar de un mecanismo para asociar permisos con varios usuarios. Las claves de acceso, compuestas por un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta, son credenciales program\xe1ticas para el acceso a CLI y API asociadas con usuarios individuales de IAM, y son un mecanismo de autenticaci\xf3n en lugar de una caracter\xedstica para asociar permisos con grupos de usuarios."},{"id":"q635","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes puede usar un cliente para habilitar el inicio de sesi\xf3n \xfanico (SSO) en la Consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon Connect","B":"AWS Directory Service","C":"Amazon Pinpoint","D":"Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Directory Service se integra con Microsoft Active Directory, lo que permite el inicio de sesi\xf3n \xfanico en la Consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS utilizando nombres de usuario y contrase\xf1as corporativos existentes sin crear usuarios de IAM separados para cada empleado. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube que gestiona las interacciones con los clientes a trav\xe9s de canales de voz y chat, y no tiene capacidad para habilitar la federaci\xf3n de credenciales corporativas ni la autenticaci\xf3n de inicio de sesi\xf3n \xfanico en la Consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS. Amazon Pinpoint es un servicio de comunicaciones salientes para enviar mensajes dirigidos por correo electr\xf3nico, SMS y notificaciones push a los usuarios de aplicaciones, y es un servicio de marketing y participaci\xf3n sin conexi\xf3n con SSO ni con la federaci\xf3n de identidades corporativas para el acceso a la consola. Amazon Rekognition es un servicio de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para analizar im\xe1genes y videos y detectar objetos, rostros y escenas, y es un servicio de an\xe1lisis visual sin funci\xf3n alguna para habilitar el inicio de sesi\xf3n \xfanico con credenciales corporativas."},{"id":"q637","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes beneficios proporciona el programa de cumplimiento de AWS a los clientes de AWS?","options":{"A":"Permite a los clientes heredar las certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS y reduce el alcance de sus propias auditor\xedas de cumplimiento","B":"AWS es responsable del mantenimiento de la documentaci\xf3n de los marcos de cumplimiento comunes","C":"Garantiza a los clientes que AWS mantiene la seguridad f\xedsica y la protecci\xf3n de datos","D":"Asegura el uso de marcos de cumplimiento que est\xe1n siendo utilizados por otros proveedores de nube","E":"Adoptar\xe1 nuevos marcos de cumplimiento a medida que se vuelvan relevantes para las cargas de trabajo de los clientes"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"El programa de cumplimiento de AWS permite a los clientes heredar las certificaciones y los resultados de auditor\xeda de terceros de AWS, lo que reduce el tiempo y el costo de sus propios esfuerzos de cumplimiento. Tambi\xe9n garantiza a los clientes que AWS mantiene est\xe1ndares de seguridad f\xedsica y protecci\xf3n de datos a nivel de infraestructura. AWS no es responsable \xfanicamente del mantenimiento de la documentaci\xf3n de los marcos de cumplimiento; el cumplimiento es una responsabilidad compartida y los clientes siguen siendo responsables de garantizar que sus propias cargas de trabajo cumplan con las regulaciones aplicables. AWS no garantiza la alineaci\xf3n con marcos simplemente porque otros proveedores de nube los utilicen; sus programas de cumplimiento se basan en est\xe1ndares de la industria y requisitos regulatorios relevantes. AWS no asume compromisos vinculantes de adoptar autom\xe1ticamente cualquier nuevo marco de cumplimiento que se vuelva relevante para las cargas de trabajo de los clientes."},{"id":"q638","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios proporciona acceso bajo demanda a los informes de cumplimiento de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"Amazon GuardDuty","D":"AWS KMS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda y de autoservicio a los informes y certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS, incluida la documentaci\xf3n de SOC, PCI DSS, ISO y HIPAA, directamente desde la Consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS. AWS IAM gestiona las identidades y los permisos de los usuarios para acceder a los servicios y recursos de AWS, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de acceso en lugar de un repositorio de informes o certificaciones de cumplimiento. Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que monitorea las cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS en busca de actividad maliciosa mediante aprendizaje autom\xe1tico e inteligencia de amenazas, y es un servicio de monitoreo de seguridad en lugar de un portal de informes de cumplimiento. AWS KMS es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de claves para crear y gestionar claves de cifrado utilizadas para proteger datos en reposo y en tr\xe1nsito, y es un servicio de infraestructura de cifrado en lugar de un servicio que proporciona acceso a documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento."},{"id":"q639","question":"Como parte del modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1l de los siguientes controles operativos heredan completamente los usuarios de AWS?","options":{"A":"Gesti\xf3n de seguridad del centro de datos","B":"Gesti\xf3n de parches","C":"Gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n","D":"Gesti\xf3n de usuarios y accesos"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La gesti\xf3n de seguridad del centro de datos es un control operativo que los clientes heredan completamente de AWS, lo que significa que AWS realiza todas las operaciones de seguridad f\xedsica y los clientes se benefician de esta protecci\xf3n sin necesidad de realizar ninguna acci\xf3n por su parte. La gesti\xf3n de parches es un control compartido en el que AWS aplica parches a la infraestructura y a los servicios gestionados que opera, mientras que los clientes son responsables de aplicar parches a los sistemas operativos y las aplicaciones que se ejecutan en sus propias instancias EC2 y otros recursos gestionados por el cliente. La gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n tambi\xe9n es un control compartido en el que AWS mantiene la configuraci\xf3n de sus propios componentes de infraestructura, mientras que los clientes son responsables de configurar sus propios sistemas operativos, aplicaciones y servicios de AWS para cumplir con sus requisitos de seguridad y operativos. La gesti\xf3n de usuarios y accesos es responsabilidad del cliente; los clientes crean y gestionan usuarios, grupos, roles y pol\xedticas de IAM, y son completamente responsables de qui\xe9n tiene acceso a sus recursos de AWS y de qu\xe9 pueden hacer esas identidades."},{"id":"q641","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, \xbfcu\xe1les de las siguientes tareas son responsabilidad del cliente?","options":{"A":"Mantener el hardware subyacente de Amazon EC2","B":"Gestionar las listas de control de acceso de red de VPC","C":"Cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito y en reposo","D":"Reemplazar las unidades de disco duro fallidas","E":"Implementar hardware en diferentes Zonas de disponibilidad"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Los clientes son responsables de gestionar las listas de control de acceso de red de VPC para definir reglas de tr\xe1fico entrante y saliente a nivel de subred, y de cifrar sus propios datos en tr\xe1nsito mediante TLS y en reposo mediante servicios como KMS y el cifrado del lado del servidor de S3. Mantener el hardware subyacente de EC2, incluidos servidores, almacenamiento e infraestructura de red, es responsabilidad completamente de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso f\xedsico al equipo del centro de datos que aloja las instancias EC2. Reemplazar las unidades de disco duro fallidas es responsabilidad de AWS como parte de la gesti\xf3n de la infraestructura f\xedsica, y los clientes lo experimentan de forma transparente mediante los procesos de redundancia y reemplazo de hardware de AWS sin necesidad de realizar ninguna acci\xf3n. Implementar hardware en diferentes Zonas de disponibilidad es una responsabilidad de la infraestructura de AWS; AWS dise\xf1a y mantiene la separaci\xf3n f\xedsica de las AZ, mientras que los clientes eligen en qu\xe9 AZ implementar sus recursos, pero no controlan la implementaci\xf3n del hardware subyacente."},{"id":"q646","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 funci\xf3n cumplen los grupos de seguridad relacionados con la seguridad de las instancias de Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)?","options":{"A":"Actuar como un firewall virtual para la instancia de Amazon EC2","B":"Proteger las cuentas de usuario de AWS con pol\xedticas de AWS identity and Access Management (IAM)","C":"Proporcionar protecci\xf3n DDoS con AWS Shield","D":"Usar Amazon CloudFront para proteger la instancia de Amazon EC2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Los grupos de seguridad act\xfaan como firewalls virtuales para las instancias de EC2, controlando el tr\xe1fico entrante y saliente a nivel de instancia mediante la evaluaci\xf3n de reglas basadas en el protocolo, el puerto y el origen o destino, lo que proporciona el mecanismo principal de seguridad de red para el acceso a las instancias de EC2.\\nProteger las cuentas de usuario de AWS con pol\xedticas de AWS Identity and Access Management es la funci\xf3n de AWS Identity and Access Management, que controla qu\xe9 usuarios pueden acceder a qu\xe9 servicios de AWS y qu\xe9 acciones pueden realizar, en lugar de una funci\xf3n de los grupos de seguridad que operan a nivel de tr\xe1fico de red. Proporcionar protecci\xf3n DDoS es la funci\xf3n de AWS Shield, que proporciona mitigaci\xf3n administrada de denegaci\xf3n de servicio distribuido en las capas de red y transporte, en lugar de los grupos de seguridad que eval\xfaan intentos de conexi\xf3n individuales basados en reglas. Usar Amazon CloudFront para proteger instancias de EC2 implica enrutar el tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de la red de borde de CloudFront con integraciones de Shield y WAF, y es un patr\xf3n arquitect\xf3nico separado en lugar de una funci\xf3n realizada directamente por los grupos de seguridad."},{"id":"q652","question":"\xbfPor qu\xe9 la gesti\xf3n de activos en AWS es m\xe1s sencilla que la gesti\xf3n de activos en un centro de datos f\xedsico?","options":{"A":"AWS proporciona una base de datos de gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n (CMDB) que los usuarios pueden mantener","B":"AWS realiza escaneos de descubrimiento de infraestructura en nombre del cliente","C":"Amazon EC2 genera autom\xe1ticamente un informe de activos y lo coloca en el bucket de Amazon S3 especificado por el cliente","D":"Los usuarios pueden recopilar metadatos de activos de manera confiable con unas pocas llamadas a la API"},"correct":"D","explanation":"En AWS, los usuarios pueden recopilar metadatos de activos de manera confiable realizando unas pocas llamadas a la API mediante servicios como AWS Config, Systems Manager Inventory o la AWS CLI, lo que proporciona informaci\xf3n completa y actualizada sobre todos los recursos de una cuenta.\\nAWS no proporciona una base de datos de gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n (CMDB) preconstruida que los clientes mantengan; AWS Config rastrea las configuraciones de recursos autom\xe1ticamente, pero mantener una CMDB tradicional requiere herramientas de terceros o desarrollo personalizado en lugar de ser una oferta de servicio integrada de AWS. AWS no realiza escaneos de descubrimiento de infraestructura en nombre del cliente; servicios como AWS Config y Systems Manager proporcionan capacidades de descubrimiento, pero estas son herramientas que los clientes usan ellos mismos en lugar de escaneos automatizados realizados por AWS en la cuenta del cliente. Amazon EC2 no genera autom\xe1ticamente informes de activos ni los coloca en S3; si bien las instancias informan algunos metadatos, la generaci\xf3n de informes de activos completa requiere el uso activo de servicios de gesti\xf3n de AWS como Config, Systems Manager o la CLI en lugar de la generaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de informes."},{"id":"q654","question":"Usar AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) para otorgar acceso solo a los recursos necesarios para realizar una tarea es un concepto conocido como:","options":{"A":"Acceso restringido","B":"Acceso seg\xfan necesidad","C":"Acceso de privilegio m\xednimo","D":"Acceso por token"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El acceso de privilegio m\xednimo significa otorgar a los usuarios de IAM solo los permisos m\xednimos que necesitan para realizar sus tareas espec\xedficas asignadas, lo que reduce el riesgo de uso indebido accidental o intencional de los recursos de AWS mediante concesiones de permisos demasiado amplias.\\nEl acceso restringido es un t\xe9rmino general que implica limitar el acceso, pero no captura espec\xedficamente el principio de otorgar los permisos m\xednimos requeridos; el privilegio m\xednimo es el concepto preciso de IAM que define permisos basados en los requisitos de la tarea en lugar de restringir ampliamente el acceso. El acceso seg\xfan necesidad es una descripci\xf3n informal que captura parcialmente la idea de otorgar permisos cuando se necesitan, pero el privilegio m\xednimo es el t\xe9rmino establecido de AWS y de la industria de la seguridad para el principio de otorgar los permisos m\xednimos necesarios. El acceso por token se refiere al uso de tokens de seguridad temporales emitidos cuando se asumen roles de IAM, y describe un mecanismo de credenciales en lugar del principio de delimitar los permisos a lo que se requiere para una tarea."},{"id":"q656","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1l de las siguientes es una responsabilidad del cliente?","options":{"A":"Proteger el hardware, el software, las instalaciones y las redes que ejecutan todos los productos y servicios","B":"Proporcionar certificados, informes y otra documentaci\xf3n directamente a los clientes de AWS bajo NDA","C":"Configurar el sistema operativo, la red y el firewall","D":"Obtener certificaciones de la industria y atestaciones independientes de terceros"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Configurar el sistema operativo, la configuraci\xf3n de red y las reglas de firewall en las instancias de EC2 queda dentro de la responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, ya que AWS proporciona la infraestructura subyacente mientras que los clientes son responsables de lo que instalan y configuran sobre ella.\\nProteger el hardware, el software, las instalaciones y las redes que ejecutan todos los productos y servicios de AWS es responsabilidad de AWS seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni control sobre la infraestructura f\xedsica y virtual que administra AWS. Proporcionar certificados de cumplimiento, informes y documentaci\xf3n a los clientes bajo NDA es un servicio que AWS proporciona a trav\xe9s de AWS Artifact, no una responsabilidad del cliente; los clientes acceden a estos documentos en lugar de crearlos o proporcionarlos. Obtener certificaciones de la industria y atestaciones de terceros para la infraestructura de AWS es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que AWS se somete a auditor\xedas independientes para certificaciones como ISO, SOC y PCI DSS que se aplican a la capa de infraestructura en lugar de ser una obligaci\xf3n del cliente."},{"id":"q657","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio administrado de AWS proporciona orientaci\xf3n en tiempo real sobre las mejores pr\xe1cticas de seguridad de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS X-Ray","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS Systems Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones en tiempo real sobre seguridad, optimizaci\xf3n de costos, rendimiento, tolerancia a fallos y l\xedmites de servicio, guiando directamente a los clientes hacia las mejores pr\xe1cticas de seguridad.\\nAmazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas operativas y registros de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS, incluidos la utilizaci\xf3n de CPU, las tasas de error y las m\xe9tricas personalizadas, y es un servicio de observabilidad en lugar de un servicio que proporciona orientaci\xf3n sobre mejores pr\xe1cticas de seguridad. AWS Config monitorea y registra continuamente las configuraciones de recursos frente a reglas de estado deseado, y es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de cumplimiento en lugar de un servicio que proporciona orientaci\xf3n proactiva sobre mejores pr\xe1cticas de seguridad. AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y eventos de gesti\xf3n con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y es un servicio de registro de actividad en lugar de un servicio que proporciona orientaci\xf3n en tiempo real sobre c\xf3mo mejorar la postura de seguridad de un entorno de AWS."},{"id":"q659","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfde qu\xe9 aspectos de la seguridad en la nube son responsables los clientes?","options":{"A":"Gesti\xf3n de la virtualizaci\xf3n","B":"Gesti\xf3n del hardware","C":"Gesti\xf3n del cifrado","D":"Gesti\xf3n de las instalaciones","E":"Gesti\xf3n del firewall"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Los clientes son responsables de gestionar el cifrado de sus datos en reposo y en tr\xe1nsito, incluida la configuraci\xf3n de los ajustes de cifrado y la gesti\xf3n de las claves de KMS, y de configurar reglas de firewall mediante grupos de seguridad y ACL de red para controlar el tr\xe1fico hacia sus recursos.\\nLa gesti\xf3n de la virtualizaci\xf3n no es una categor\xeda est\xe1ndar de responsabilidad compartida; la infraestructura de virtualizaci\xf3n subyacente, incluido el hipervisor, es responsabilidad de AWS, mientras que los clientes gestionan las m\xe1quinas virtuales que se ejecutan sobre ella en lugar de la capa de virtualizaci\xf3n en s\xed. La gesti\xf3n del hardware, incluidos los servidores f\xedsicos, los dispositivos de almacenamiento y el equipo de red, es responsabilidad completamente de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni interacci\xf3n con la infraestructura f\xedsica en los centros de datos de AWS. La gesti\xf3n de las instalaciones, incluida la seguridad f\xedsica del centro de datos, la energ\xeda y la refrigeraci\xf3n, es responsabilidad completamente de AWS, ya que los clientes nunca tienen acceso f\xedsico ni participaci\xf3n en la gesti\xf3n de las instalaciones que albergan la infraestructura de AWS."},{"id":"q661","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una responsabilidad de AWS en el modelo de responsabilidad compartida?","options":{"A":"Actualizar las ACL de red para bloquear el tr\xe1fico hacia puertos vulnerables","B":"Aplicar parches a los sistemas operativos que se ejecutan en instancias de Amazon EC2","C":"Actualizar el firmware de los hosts de EC2 subyacentes","D":"Actualizar las reglas de los grupos de seguridad para bloquear el tr\xe1fico hacia los puertos vulnerables"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Actualizar el firmware del hardware de los hosts de EC2 es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso a los servidores f\xedsicos y AWS gestiona todo el mantenimiento de la capa de hardware, incluidas las actualizaciones de firmware para mejoras de seguridad y rendimiento.\\nActualizar las ACL de red para bloquear el tr\xe1fico hacia puertos vulnerables es responsabilidad del cliente; los clientes configuran las ACL de red de su VPC para definir qu\xe9 tr\xe1fico se permite o se deniega a nivel de subred como parte de su configuraci\xf3n de seguridad. Aplicar parches a los sistemas operativos que se ejecutan en instancias de EC2 es responsabilidad del cliente; AWS gestiona el hipervisor subyacente y el hardware del host, pero el sistema operativo invitado y sus parches son obligaci\xf3n del cliente. Actualizar las reglas de los grupos de seguridad para bloquear el tr\xe1fico hacia puertos vulnerables es responsabilidad del cliente; los clientes definen y mantienen las reglas de los grupos de seguridad para controlar qu\xe9 tr\xe1fico llega a sus instancias de EC2 como parte de su configuraci\xf3n de seguridad de red."},{"id":"q663","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 significa otorgar el privilegio m\xednimo a los usuarios de AWS IAM?","options":{"A":"Es otorgar permisos a un solo usuario \xfanicamente","B":"Es otorgar permisos usando solo pol\xedticas de AWS IAM","C":"Es otorgar permisos de la pol\xedtica AdministratorAccess a usuarios de confianza","D":"Es otorgar solo los permisos necesarios para realizar una tarea determinada"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El privilegio m\xednimo significa otorgar solo los permisos m\xednimos necesarios para realizar una tarea espec\xedfica, lo que garantiza que los usuarios de IAM no puedan acceder a recursos ni realizar acciones m\xe1s all\xe1 de lo que requiere su trabajo, minimizando el riesgo de uso indebido.\\nOtorgar permisos a un solo usuario \xfanicamente describe acceso individual en lugar de compartido, pero no captura el concepto de limitar los permisos al m\xednimo requerido; el privilegio m\xednimo se refiere a la amplitud de los permisos otorgados en lugar del n\xfamero de usuarios que los reciben. Otorgar permisos usando solo pol\xedticas de IAM es una descripci\xf3n del mecanismo para asignar permisos en AWS, pero no define el principio de privilegio m\xednimo, que se refiere a minimizar el alcance de esos permisos en lugar del m\xe9todo para otorgarlos. Otorgar permisos AdministratorAccess a usuarios de confianza es lo opuesto al privilegio m\xednimo; el acceso de administrador otorga acceso sin restricciones a todos los servicios y recursos de AWS, lo que supera con creces los permisos m\xednimos necesarios para la mayor\xeda de las tareas, independientemente de cu\xe1n confiable sea el usuario."},{"id":"q683","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona un portal de autoservicio para el acceso bajo demanda a los informes de cumplimiento de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Artifact"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Artifact es un portal de autoservicio que proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes y certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS, incluidos documentos SOC, PCI e ISO, lo que lo convierte en el servicio dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para clientes que requieren acceso bajo demanda a informes de cumplimiento.\\nAWS Config supervisa y registra de forma continua las configuraciones de los recursos para evaluar el cumplimiento con las reglas deseadas, y es una herramienta de cumplimiento de configuraci\xf3n de recursos en lugar de un portal para descargar informes de cumplimiento emitidos por auditores de AWS. AWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y administra certificados SSL/TLS para su uso con servicios de AWS con el fin de proteger los datos en tr\xe1nsito, y es un servicio de aprovisionamiento y administraci\xf3n de certificados en lugar de un repositorio de documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades que escanea instancias de EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y configuraciones de red incorrectas, y produce hallazgos de evaluaci\xf3n espec\xedficos del cliente en lugar de informes de cumplimiento de AWS."},{"id":"q692","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfde qu\xe9 es responsable AWS?","options":{"A":"Configurar Amazon VPC","B":"Administrar el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n","C":"Mantener el tr\xe1fico de la aplicaci\xf3n","D":"Administrar la infraestructura de red"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS es responsable de administrar la infraestructura de red f\xedsica y virtual que sustenta su nube global, incluidos los routers, switches y el cableado f\xedsico que conectan los centros de datos, las zonas de disponibilidad y las regiones.\\nConfigurar Amazon VPC es responsabilidad del cliente; los clientes definen los rangos de direcciones IP de su VPC, subredes, tablas de rutas, puertas de enlace de internet y configuraciones de seguridad como parte de su dise\xf1o de red dentro de AWS. Administrar el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n es enteramente responsabilidad del cliente; AWS proporciona los servicios de c\xf3mputo y de tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n, pero la l\xf3gica de la aplicaci\xf3n, la calidad del c\xf3digo y las actualizaciones son propiedad y responsabilidad del cliente. Mantener el tr\xe1fico de la aplicaci\xf3n, incluida la administraci\xf3n de balanceadores de carga, configuraciones de CDN y enrutamiento DNS, es responsabilidad del cliente para sus propias aplicaciones, aunque AWS administra la infraestructura de red subyacente sobre la que se ejecutan esos servicios."},{"id":"q694","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 herramienta de AWS identificar\xe1 los grupos de seguridad que otorgan acceso a Internet sin restricciones a una lista limitada de puertos?","options":{"A":"AWS Organizations","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Usage Report","D":"Panel de Amazon EC2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor escanea sus grupos de seguridad y se\xf1ala aquellos que otorgan acceso a Internet sin restricciones (0.0.0.0/0) a puertos espec\xedficos como SSH, RDP y puertos de bases de datos comunes, lo que ayuda a identificar y cerrar posibles vulnerabilidades de seguridad.\\nAWS Organizations administra m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS bajo una estructura central para la facturaci\xf3n consolidada y el gobierno con pol\xedticas de control de servicios, y no tiene la capacidad de inspeccionar reglas individuales de grupos de seguridad ni de identificar configuraciones de acceso a Internet excesivamente permisivas. AWS Usage Reports proporciona datos sobre el uso y los costos de los servicios de AWS, y son herramientas de seguimiento de facturaci\xf3n y consumo en lugar de herramientas de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad que eval\xfaan configuraciones de control de acceso a la red. El panel de Amazon EC2 muestra informaci\xf3n sobre las instancias en ejecuci\xf3n, los tipos de instancia y sus estados, y aunque los grupos de seguridad son visibles en la consola, el panel no identifica autom\xe1ticamente qu\xe9 grupos otorgan acceso a Internet sin restricciones de la forma en que lo hace la comprobaci\xf3n automatizada de Trusted Advisor."},{"id":"q697","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es responsabilidad de AWS?","options":{"A":"Configurar usuarios y grupos de AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","B":"Destruir f\xedsicamente los medios de almacenamiento al final de su vida \xfatil","C":"Aplicar parches a los sistemas operativos invitados","D":"Configurar los ajustes de seguridad en las instancias de Amazon EC2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS es responsable de destruir f\xedsicamente los medios de almacenamiento al final de su vida \xfatil utilizando t\xe9cnicas est\xe1ndar de la industria para evitar filtraciones de datos, siguiendo procedimientos seguros de desmantelamiento como parte de sus operaciones de centros de datos bajo el modelo de responsabilidad compartida.\\nConfigurar usuarios y grupos de IAM es responsabilidad del cliente; los clientes definen su propia estructura de administraci\xf3n de identidades y accesos, incluida la creaci\xf3n de usuarios, la asignaci\xf3n de estos a grupos y la adjunci\xf3n de pol\xedticas que controlan a qu\xe9 puede acceder cada identidad. Aplicar parches a los sistemas operativos invitados en las instancias de EC2 es responsabilidad del cliente; AWS administra el hardware del host subyacente y el hipervisor, pero el sistema operativo que se ejecuta dentro de la m\xe1quina virtual debe ser parcheado y mantenido por el cliente. Configurar los ajustes de seguridad en las instancias de EC2, incluidas las reglas de firewall, el endurecimiento del sistema operativo y las configuraciones de seguridad de las aplicaciones, es responsabilidad del cliente como parte de la protecci\xf3n de lo que implementa y administra en los recursos de c\xf3mputo de AWS."},{"id":"q702","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 tareas debe realizar un cliente cuando sospecha que una cuenta de AWS ha sido comprometida?","options":{"A":"Rotar contrase\xf1as y claves de acceso","B":"Eliminar los tokens de MFA","C":"Mover los recursos a una regi\xf3n de AWS diferente","D":"Eliminar los recursos de AWS CloudTrail","E":"Contactar con AWS Support"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Cuando se sospecha que una cuenta ha sido comprometida, rotar inmediatamente todas las contrase\xf1as y claves de acceso invalida las credenciales potencialmente robadas, y contactar con AWS Support proporciona asistencia con la investigaci\xf3n y la remediaci\xf3n del compromiso.\\nEliminar los tokens de MFA reducir\xeda la seguridad de la cuenta precisamente en el momento en que m\xe1s se necesita, ya que MFA proporciona una barrera de autenticaci\xf3n adicional que ayuda a prevenir el acceso no autorizado incluso si las credenciales han sido robadas. Mover los recursos a una regi\xf3n de AWS diferente no aborda el compromiso subyacente y no tiene ning\xfan efecto sobre la capacidad del atacante de acceder a la cuenta utilizando credenciales robadas. Eliminar los recursos de AWS CloudTrail destruir\xeda el registro de auditor\xeda necesario para investigar c\xf3mo se produjo el compromiso y qu\xe9 acciones tom\xf3 el atacante, lo que dificultar\xeda significativamente la remediaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q705","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 es un rol de AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)?","options":{"A":"Un usuario asociado con un recurso de AWS","B":"Un grupo asociado con un recurso de AWS","C":"Una entidad que define un conjunto de permisos para su uso con un recurso de AWS","D":"Una credencial de autenticaci\xf3n asociada con un token de autenticaci\xf3n multifactor (MFA)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Un rol de IAM es una entidad que define un conjunto de permisos para realizar solicitudes a los servicios de AWS y, a diferencia de un usuario de IAM, no tiene credenciales permanentes; en su lugar, proporciona credenciales de seguridad temporales a quien o a lo que lo asuma.\\nUn usuario asociado con un recurso de AWS describe un usuario de IAM o una cuenta de servicio asignada para interactuar con recursos espec\xedficos, pero esto no captura la caracter\xedstica definitoria de un rol de IAM, que es que puede ser asumido temporalmente por m\xfaltiples entidades diferentes en lugar de estar asociado permanentemente con una sola. Un grupo asociado con un recurso de AWS describe un grupo de IAM utilizado para administrar permisos compartidos para m\xfaltiples usuarios, no un rol, el cual es asumido din\xe1micamente por usuarios, servicios o aplicaciones que necesitan sus permisos temporalmente. Una credencial de autenticaci\xf3n asociada con un token de MFA describe un mecanismo de autenticaci\xf3n multifactor, no un rol de IAM, que es un tipo de identidad que proporciona credenciales y permisos temporales en lugar de ser un token de autenticaci\xf3n espec\xedfico."},{"id":"q717","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio permite a los clientes auditar y supervisar los cambios en los recursos de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"Amazon GuardDuty","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Config supervisa y registra de forma continua las configuraciones de los recursos de AWS, haciendo un seguimiento de los cambios a lo largo del tiempo y evaluando las configuraciones con respecto a reglas de cumplimiento del estado deseado, lo que lo convierte en el servicio correcto para auditar y supervisar los cambios en los recursos.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas en las categor\xedas de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos mediante el an\xe1lisis de su entorno de AWS, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento en lugar de un servicio que registra y audita espec\xedficamente los cambios individuales en la configuraci\xf3n de los recursos a lo largo del tiempo. Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para identificar actividad maliciosa y comportamientos an\xf3malos en cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS, y es un servicio de supervisi\xf3n de seguridad en lugar de una herramienta de auditor\xeda de cambios en la configuraci\xf3n de los recursos. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades que escanea instancias de EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y configuraciones de red incorrectas, y produce hallazgos de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad en lugar de rastrear y auditar los cambios en la configuraci\xf3n de los recursos."},{"id":"q722","question":"Un usuario est\xe1 ejecutando una aplicaci\xf3n en AWS y nota que una o m\xe1s direcciones IP propiedad de AWS est\xe1n involucradas en un ataque de denegaci\xf3n de servicio distribuido (DDoS). \xbfCon qui\xe9n debe contactar el usuario PRIMERO sobre esta situaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"AWS Premium Support","B":"AWS Technical Account Manager","C":"AWS Solutions Architect","D":"Equipo de abuso de AWS"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El equipo de abuso de AWS (AWS Abuse Team) gestiona los informes de recursos de AWS utilizados en actividades maliciosas, incluidos los ataques DDoS, y debe ser el primer punto de contacto cuando se identifica que direcciones IP propiedad de AWS participan en un ataque, ya que puede investigar y detener el uso indebido.\\nAWS Premium Support ayuda a los clientes de pago con problemas t\xe9cnicos y orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica relacionada con su propio entorno de AWS, pero no es el canal designado para informar sobre abusos originados en direcciones IP de AWS pertenecientes a otras cuentas. El AWS Technical Account Manager es un recurso de soporte para clientes del plan Enterprise que proporciona orientaci\xf3n proactiva y revisiones operativas, pero no es el contacto apropiado para informar sobre incidentes de abuso externos que involucran la infraestructura de AWS. Los AWS Solutions Architects ayudan a los clientes a dise\xf1ar arquitecturas y optimizar cargas de trabajo, y no son el contacto para informar sobre incidentes de seguridad o abusos que involucran la infraestructura y las direcciones IP de AWS."},{"id":"q727","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ofrece acceso bajo demanda a los informes de seguridad y cumplimiento de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Health","D":"Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda a los informes de seguridad y cumplimiento de AWS, incluidos SOC, PCI, ISO y otras certificaciones, disponibles directamente desde AWS Management Console para ayudar a los clientes a cumplir sus propios requisitos regulatorios.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y los eventos de administraci\xf3n con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y produce registros de actividad espec\xedficos del cliente en lugar de informes de certificaci\xf3n de seguridad de AWS emitidos por auditores externos. AWS Health proporciona alertas personalizadas sobre eventos de AWS que pueden afectar los recursos espec\xedficos de un cliente o la disponibilidad del servicio, y es una herramienta de estado y notificaci\xf3n de servicios en lugar de un repositorio de informes de cumplimiento. Amazon CloudWatch supervisa m\xe9tricas operativas y registros de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS, y es un servicio de observabilidad y alertas en lugar de una fuente de documentaci\xf3n de seguridad y cumplimiento de AWS."},{"id":"q730","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona ACL de red de entrada y salida para reforzar la conectividad externa a Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"Amazon VPC","D":"Amazon API Gateway"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon VPC proporciona Network Access Control Lists (listas de control de acceso a la red) que act\xfaan como firewalls sin estado a nivel de subred, controlando el tr\xe1fico de entrada y salida para reforzar la conectividad de red de las instancias EC2, as\xed como grupos de seguridad a nivel de instancia para un control adicional.\\nAWS IAM gestiona identidades y permisos de usuario para acceder a servicios y recursos de AWS, y es un servicio de control de acceso en lugar de un componente de seguridad de red que proporcione filtrado de tr\xe1fico de entrada y salida mediante ACL. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube para gestionar interacciones con clientes mediante voz y chat, y es un servicio de comunicaciones sin conexi\xf3n con la red de VPC ni con la configuraci\xf3n de ACL de red. Amazon API Gateway es un servicio totalmente administrado para crear, publicar y gestionar API, y es una capa de integraci\xf3n de aplicaciones en lugar de un componente de red de VPC que proporcione ACL de red para controlar la conectividad."},{"id":"q733","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfde qu\xe9 tarea relacionada con la seguridad es responsable AWS?","options":{"A":"Gesti\xf3n del ciclo de vida de las credenciales de IAM","B":"Seguridad f\xedsica de la infraestructura global","C":"Cifrado de vol\xfamenes de Amazon EBS","D":"Configuraci\xf3n de firewall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS es responsable de la seguridad f\xedsica de su infraestructura global, incluidos los centros de datos, el hardware de red y las instalaciones, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni control sobre ning\xfan componente f\xedsico del entorno de AWS.\\nLa gesti\xf3n del ciclo de vida de las credenciales de IAM, incluida la creaci\xf3n, rotaci\xf3n y eliminaci\xf3n de claves de acceso y contrase\xf1as para usuarios de IAM, es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes definen y gestionan las identidades utilizadas para acceder a sus recursos de AWS. El cifrado de vol\xfamenes de Amazon EBS es responsabilidad del cliente; aunque AWS proporciona KMS para la gesti\xf3n de claves y EBS admite cifrado, los clientes deben optar por habilitar el cifrado y configurar las claves adecuadas para sus vol\xfamenes. La configuraci\xf3n de firewall mediante grupos de seguridad y ACL de red es responsabilidad del cliente como parte de la protecci\xf3n del acceso de red a sus propios recursos dentro de su VPC."},{"id":"q735","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1l de las siguientes es un ejemplo de seguridad en la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Gesti\xf3n de ubicaciones de borde","B":"Seguridad f\xedsica","C":"Configuraci\xf3n de firewall","D":"Infraestructura global"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La configuraci\xf3n de firewall mediante grupos de seguridad y ACL de red, que los clientes definen para controlar el tr\xe1fico de entrada y salida hacia sus recursos, es responsabilidad del cliente y es un ejemplo de seguridad realizada dentro de la nube en el lado del cliente del modelo de responsabilidad compartida.\\nLa gesti\xf3n de ubicaciones de borde es responsabilidad de AWS; AWS opera la red global de ubicaciones de borde para CloudFront y otros servicios, y los clientes se benefician de estas ubicaciones sin ser responsables de su gesti\xf3n ni seguridad. La seguridad f\xedsica de los centros de datos y el hardware de AWS es completamente responsabilidad de AWS, ya que los clientes nunca tienen acceso f\xedsico a las instalaciones donde se ejecuta la infraestructura de AWS. La infraestructura global, incluidos los centros de datos f\xedsicos, las Zonas de disponibilidad y las Regiones, est\xe1 dise\xf1ada, construida y operada por AWS, y mantener su seguridad y disponibilidad es responsabilidad de AWS en lugar del cliente."},{"id":"q740","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n web que se ejecuta en AWS ha recibido spam de solicitudes maliciosas desde un conjunto recurrente de direcciones IP. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede ayudar a proteger la aplicaci\xf3n y bloquear el tr\xe1fico malicioso?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM","B":"Amazon GuardDuty","C":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","D":"AWS WAF"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS WAF permite crear reglas para bloquear solicitudes de direcciones o rangos de IP espec\xedficos, inspeccionar el contenido de las solicitudes y filtrar patrones de tr\xe1fico, lo que proporciona una protecci\xf3n eficaz a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n contra solicitudes maliciosas repetidas desde direcciones IP conocidas.\\nAWS IAM gestiona identidades y permisos de usuario para acceder a servicios y recursos de AWS, y es un servicio de control de acceso en lugar de un servicio que pueda bloquear tr\xe1fico HTTP malicioso dirigido a una aplicaci\xf3n web. Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para identificar actividad maliciosa y comportamiento an\xf3malo en cuentas de AWS, y aunque puede detectar patrones sospechosos, no bloquea ni filtra activamente el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones web en tiempo real. Amazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda de publicaci\xf3n/suscripci\xf3n totalmente administrado para enviar notificaciones a suscriptores, y es un servicio de mensajer\xeda y notificaci\xf3n sin capacidad para inspeccionar o bloquear patrones de tr\xe1fico web malicioso."},{"id":"q746","question":"Un usuario necesita un informe automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad que identifique el acceso de red no intencionado a instancias de Amazon EC2 y las vulnerabilidades en esas instancias. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporcionar\xe1 este informe de evaluaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Grupos de seguridad de EC2","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon Macie","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Inspector eval\xfaa autom\xe1ticamente las instancias EC2 en busca de acceso de red no intencionado mediante el an\xe1lisis de configuraciones de red y vulnerabilidades de software conocidas mediante el escaneo de paquetes instalados contra bases de datos de vulnerabilidades, generando informes de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad priorizados.\\nLos grupos de seguridad de EC2 son firewalls virtuales que controlan qu\xe9 tr\xe1fico puede llegar a las instancias EC2 seg\xfan reglas definidas, y son un control de red preventivo en lugar de un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n automatizada que identifique vulnerabilidades y genere informes. AWS Config supervisa y registra continuamente las configuraciones de recursos frente a reglas de cumplimiento del estado deseado, y es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de cumplimiento de configuraci\xf3n en lugar de una herramienta de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades que escanee en busca de exposici\xf3n de red o debilidades de software. Amazon Macie utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para descubrir y clasificar datos confidenciales almacenados en Amazon S3, y es un servicio de clasificaci\xf3n y protecci\xf3n de datos para S3 en lugar de un servicio que eval\xfae instancias EC2 en busca de vulnerabilidades de acceso de red."},{"id":"q748","question":"\xbfD\xf3nde pueden encontrar los usuarios un cat\xe1logo de proveedores de soluciones de seguridad de terceros reconocidos por AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Service Catalog","B":"AWS Marketplace","C":"AWS Quick Start","D":"AWS CodeDeploy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Marketplace es un cat\xe1logo digital donde los usuarios pueden encontrar, comprar e implementar soluciones de software de terceros de proveedores de software independientes que son evaluados por AWS, incluidos herramientas de seguridad como firewalls, detecci\xf3n de intrusiones y productos de cumplimiento.\\nAWS Service Catalog permite a las organizaciones crear y gestionar cat\xe1logos de servicios de TI aprobados para uso interno dentro de su propio entorno de AWS, y es una herramienta de gobernanza interna para gestionar portafolios de productos aprobados en lugar de un cat\xe1logo p\xfablico de proveedores de seguridad de terceros. AWS Quick Start proporciona implementaciones automatizadas preconstruidas de tecnolog\xedas populares mediante plantillas de AWS CloudFormation, y es un recurso para acelerar implementaciones en lugar de un cat\xe1logo de proveedores de soluciones de seguridad de terceros. AWS CodeDeploy es un servicio de implementaci\xf3n automatizada que entrega c\xf3digo de aplicaci\xf3n a instancias EC2, funciones Lambda y servidores locales, y es una herramienta de automatizaci\xf3n de implementaciones sin conexi\xf3n con la navegaci\xf3n o compra de soluciones de seguridad de terceros."},{"id":"q755","question":"Dentro del modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfqui\xe9n es responsable de la seguridad y el cumplimiento?","options":{"A":"El cliente es responsable","B":"AWS es responsable","C":"AWS y el cliente comparten la responsabilidad","D":"AWS comparte la responsabilidad con el organismo regulador correspondiente"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La seguridad y el cumplimiento son una responsabilidad compartida entre AWS y el cliente; AWS protege la infraestructura subyacente de la nube, mientras que los clientes son responsables de proteger sus cargas de trabajo, datos y configuraciones construidas sobre ella.\\nQue el cliente sea el \xfanico responsable de la seguridad y el cumplimiento es incorrecto; seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, AWS gestiona la seguridad de la infraestructura de la nube, incluidos los centros de datos f\xedsicos, el hipervisor y la red global, que est\xe1n completamente fuera del control del cliente. Que AWS sea el \xfanico responsable de la seguridad y el cumplimiento es incorrecto; los clientes deben gestionar activamente la seguridad en la nube, incluidas las pol\xedticas de IAM, la configuraci\xf3n de cifrado, la seguridad de red y la seguridad de aplicaciones, todo lo cual AWS no puede controlar ni configurar en nombre del cliente. Que AWS comparta la responsabilidad con un organismo regulador correspondiente no es c\xf3mo funciona el modelo de responsabilidad compartida; el modelo divide la responsabilidad entre AWS y el cliente seg\xfan qu\xe9 capa de la pila controla cada parte, sin participaci\xf3n de organismos reguladores externos en la responsabilidad operativa de seguridad."},{"id":"q757","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se utiliza para proporcionar cifrado para Amazon EBS?","options":{"A":"AWS Certificate Manager","B":"AWS Systems Manager","C":"AWS KMS","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Key Management Service gestiona las claves de cifrado utilizadas para el cifrado de vol\xfamenes de EBS, encarg\xe1ndose de la creaci\xf3n, rotaci\xf3n y control de acceso de las claves, e integr\xe1ndose de forma transparente con EBS para proteger los datos en reposo.\\nAWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y gestiona certificados SSL/TLS para cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito entre aplicaciones y clientes, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de certificados en lugar de un servicio de gesti\xf3n de claves para el cifrado en reposo de vol\xfamenes de EBS. AWS Systems Manager proporciona herramientas de gesti\xf3n operativa para instancias de EC2 y otros recursos, incluidos parches, gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n y gesti\xf3n de sesiones, y es un servicio de operaciones en lugar de un servicio de gesti\xf3n de claves de cifrado para vol\xfamenes de EBS. AWS Config supervisa y registra continuamente las configuraciones de recursos frente a reglas de cumplimiento del estado deseado, y es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de cumplimiento de configuraci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio de gesti\xf3n de claves de cifrado."},{"id":"q760","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 proporciona AWS Shield Standard?","options":{"A":"Reglas de WAF","B":"Protecci\xf3n contra DDoS","C":"Permisos y acceso a recursos de Identity and Access Management (IAM)","D":"Cifrado de datos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Shield Standard proporciona protecci\xf3n autom\xe1tica contra DDoS sin costo adicional para todos los clientes de AWS, detectando y mitigando autom\xe1ticamente ataques comunes de capa de red y de transporte, incluidos ataques de inundaci\xf3n UDP y SYN, sin necesidad de configuraci\xf3n.\\nLas reglas de WAF son gestionadas por AWS WAF, que es un servicio separado de Shield que filtra tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS de capa de aplicaci\xf3n seg\xfan reglas configurables, y AWS Shield Standard no proporciona funcionalidad de reglas de WAF. Los permisos y el acceso a recursos de IAM son gestionados por AWS Identity and Access Management, que controla qui\xe9n puede acceder a qu\xe9 servicios de AWS, y no tienen relaci\xf3n con la protecci\xf3n contra DDoS que proporciona AWS Shield. El cifrado de datos es proporcionado por servicios como AWS KMS, el cifrado del lado del servidor de S3 y el cifrado de EBS, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con AWS Shield, que es espec\xedficamente un servicio de mitigaci\xf3n de DDoS."},{"id":"q766","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una de las responsabilidades del cliente seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS?","options":{"A":"Infraestructura de virtualizaci\xf3n","B":"Infraestructura de red","C":"Seguridad de las aplicaciones","D":"Seguridad f\xedsica del hardware"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La seguridad de las aplicaciones, incluida la protecci\xf3n del c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n, los controles de acceso a datos y las configuraciones de autenticaci\xf3n, es responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, ya que forma parte del lado del cliente en la pila a la que AWS no puede acceder ni controlar.\\nLa infraestructura de virtualizaci\xf3n, incluido el hipervisor que abstrae el hardware f\xedsico en m\xe1quinas virtuales, es gestionada y protegida en su totalidad por AWS como parte de sus obligaciones de seguridad de la nube, y los clientes no tienen acceso ni responsabilidad sobre esta capa. La infraestructura de red, incluida la red global que conecta las Regiones de AWS, las Zonas de Disponibilidad y las ubicaciones de borde, es propiedad de AWS y la mantiene AWS, y no es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes configuran redes virtuales dentro de su VPC pero no gestionan la red f\xedsica subyacente. La seguridad f\xedsica del hardware, incluidos los controles de acceso a los centros de datos, la seguridad de los servidores y la retirada de equipos f\xedsicos, es responsabilidad enteramente de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni interacci\xf3n con las instalaciones f\xedsicas de AWS."},{"id":"q770","question":"\xbfD\xf3nde deben informar los usuarios que se est\xe1n utilizando recursos de AWS para fines maliciosos?","options":{"A":"Equipo de abuso de AWS","B":"AWS Shield","C":"AWS Support","D":"Foros para desarrolladores de AWS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Se debe contactar al equipo de abuso de AWS cuando se est\xe1n utilizando recursos o direcciones IP de AWS para fines maliciosos, como alojar spam, realizar ataques DDoS o distribuir malware, ya que es el canal designado para investigar y detener dicho uso indebido.\\nAWS Shield proporciona protecci\xf3n administrada contra DDoS que detecta y mitiga autom\xe1ticamente ataques DDoS contra sus propios recursos, y es un servicio de protecci\xf3n para su propia infraestructura en lugar de un canal para informar recursos de AWS que est\xe1n siendo abusados por otros. AWS Support ayuda a los clientes con problemas t\xe9cnicos y preguntas sobre su propio entorno de AWS, pero no es el canal designado para informar que la infraestructura de AWS perteneciente a otras cuentas se est\xe1 utilizando para fines maliciosos. Los foros para desarrolladores de AWS son espacios de discusi\xf3n de la comunidad donde los desarrolladores comparten conocimientos y hacen preguntas sobre los servicios de AWS, y no son un canal adecuado para informar incidentes de seguridad relacionados con el uso malicioso de recursos de AWS."},{"id":"q771","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS debe habilitarse para realizar un seguimiento de todos los cambios en las cuentas de usuario dentro de AWS Management Console?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","C":"VPC Flow Logs","D":"AWS CloudHSM"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y las acciones de los usuarios en AWS Management Console, proporcionando un rastro de auditor\xeda completo de los cambios en la cuenta para an\xe1lisis de seguridad y cumplimiento.\\nAmazon Simple Notification Service es un servicio de mensajer\xeda pub/sub totalmente administrado que entrega notificaciones a suscriptores como funciones Lambda, endpoints HTTP y direcciones de correo electr\xf3nico, y no tiene capacidad para registrar o rastrear cambios en las cuentas de usuario dentro de AWS Management Console. VPC Flow Logs captura informaci\xf3n sobre el tr\xe1fico IP que fluye hacia y desde las interfaces de red dentro de una VPC, y es una funci\xf3n de registro de tr\xe1fico de red que registra datos a nivel de conexi\xf3n en lugar de acciones de usuario o cambios de cuenta en la consola. AWS CloudHSM proporciona m\xf3dulos de seguridad de hardware dedicados para generar y gestionar claves criptogr\xe1ficas, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de claves y cifrado sin capacidad para rastrear o auditar la actividad del usuario dentro de AWS Management Console."},{"id":"q777","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es responsabilidad de AWS seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS?","options":{"A":"Configurar aplicaciones de terceros","B":"Mantener el hardware f\xedsico","C":"Proteger el acceso a las aplicaciones y los datos","D":"Gestionar Amazon Machine Images (AMIs) personalizadas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mantener el hardware f\xedsico, incluidos servidores, dispositivos de almacenamiento y equipos de red en los centros de datos, es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni interacci\xf3n con la infraestructura f\xedsica que impulsa los servicios de AWS.\\nConfigurar aplicaciones de terceros implementadas en recursos de AWS es responsabilidad del cliente; AWS proporciona la infraestructura de c\xf3mputo, almacenamiento y red, pero los clientes son responsables de instalar, configurar y proteger cualquier software de terceros que elijan ejecutar en esa infraestructura. Proteger el acceso a las aplicaciones y los datos, incluida la configuraci\xf3n de pol\xedticas de IAM adecuadas, configuraciones de cifrado y controles de acceso para aplicaciones y datos almacenados en AWS, es responsabilidad del cliente como parte de la seguridad en la nube. Gestionar Amazon Machine Images personalizadas, incluida la creaci\xf3n, el mantenimiento y la actualizaci\xf3n de AMIs utilizadas para lanzar instancias de EC2, es responsabilidad del cliente como parte de la gesti\xf3n de su propio entorno de c\xf3mputo."},{"id":"q779","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo agregar\xeda un administrador de sistemas una capa adicional de seguridad de inicio de sesi\xf3n a AWS Management Console de un usuario?","options":{"A":"Usar AWS Cloud Directory","B":"Auditar los roles de AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","C":"Habilitar la autenticaci\xf3n multifactor","D":"Habilitar AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Habilitar la autenticaci\xf3n multifactor agrega una segunda capa de verificaci\xf3n m\xe1s all\xe1 de una contrase\xf1a, lo que requiere un c\xf3digo de un solo uso basado en tiempo de un dispositivo de hardware o virtual para completar el inicio de sesi\xf3n, proporcionando una capa adicional de seguridad para el acceso a AWS Management Console.\\nAWS Cloud Directory es un servicio de directorio administrado para crear directorios nativos de la nube que organizan datos jer\xe1rquicos, como estructuras organizativas y registros de dispositivos, y no es un mecanismo para agregar una capa de seguridad de inicio de sesi\xf3n a AWS Management Console. Auditar los roles de IAM revisa qu\xe9 permisos se han concedido en una cuenta, lo cual es una actividad de gobernanza que ayuda a identificar permisos excesivos pero no agrega un paso de autenticaci\xf3n adicional al proceso de inicio de sesi\xf3n en s\xed. Habilitar AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta para auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y aunque mejora la visibilidad sobre qui\xe9n hizo qu\xe9 despu\xe9s del inicio de sesi\xf3n, no agrega ninguna capa adicional de seguridad al proceso de autenticaci\xf3n de inicio de sesi\xf3n."},{"id":"q780","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio puede identificar al usuario que realiz\xf3 la llamada a la API cuando se termina una instancia de Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS X-Ray","D":"AWS Identity and Access Management (AWS IAM)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API, incluidas las terminaciones de instancias de EC2, registrando qu\xe9 usuario o rol de IAM realiz\xf3 la llamada, la direcci\xf3n IP de origen y la marca de tiempo, lo que lo convierte en el servicio dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para identificar qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 una acci\xf3n espec\xedfica en una cuenta de AWS.\\nAmazon CloudWatch recopila m\xe9tricas, registros y datos de eventos de servicios y recursos de AWS para proporcionar supervisi\xf3n operativa y alertas, y no registra informaci\xf3n de identidad ni atribuci\xf3n de llamadas a la API para acciones espec\xedficas como las terminaciones de instancias. AWS X-Ray es un servicio de seguimiento distribuido que analiza y depura el rendimiento de las aplicaciones mediante el mapeo de solicitudes a medida que fluyen a trav\xe9s de los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de rendimiento sin capacidad de registro de llamadas a la API ni de atribuci\xf3n de usuarios. AWS Identity and Access Management define y gestiona permisos para usuarios y roles, controlando qu\xe9 acciones se les permite realizar, pero no registra ni documenta por s\xed mismo qu\xe9 usuario realiz\xf3 una llamada a la API espec\xedfica ni cu\xe1ndo."},{"id":"q782","question":"\xbfD\xf3nde puede un cliente encontrar informaci\xf3n sobre acciones prohibidas en la infraestructura de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","C":"AWS Billing Console","D":"AWS Acceptable Use Policy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"La AWS Acceptable Use Policy define qu\xe9 actividades est\xe1n prohibidas en la infraestructura de AWS, incluido el alojamiento de contenido ilegal, los ataques de red y el env\xedo de spam, y es la referencia autorizada para los clientes que buscan comprender qu\xe9 acciones no est\xe1n permitidas.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas para mejorar la seguridad, la eficiencia de costos, el rendimiento y la tolerancia a fallas de los entornos de AWS, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento en lugar de un documento que define usos prohibidos de la plataforma de AWS. AWS IAM administra identidades y permisos de usuarios para controlar a qu\xe9 servicios y recursos de AWS pueden acceder los usuarios, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de acceso en lugar de un documento de pol\xedticas que enumera actividades de infraestructura prohibidas. AWS Billing Console muestra datos de costos y uso, facturas e informaci\xf3n de pago de una cuenta de AWS, y es una interfaz de administraci\xf3n financiera sin conexi\xf3n con la definici\xf3n o documentaci\xf3n de usos prohibidos de la infraestructura de AWS."},{"id":"q786","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes son pr\xe1cticas recomendadas para administrar usuarios de IAM?","options":{"A":"Requerir que los usuarios de IAM cambien sus contrase\xf1as despu\xe9s de un per\xedodo de tiempo especificado","B":"Impedir que los usuarios de IAM reutilicen contrase\xf1as anteriores","C":"Recomendar que se use la misma contrase\xf1a en AWS y en otros sitios","D":"Requerir que los usuarios de IAM almacenen sus contrase\xf1as en texto sin formato","E":"Deshabilitar la autenticaci\xf3n multifactor (MFA) para los usuarios de IAM"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Requerir la rotaci\xf3n de contrase\xf1as despu\xe9s de un per\xedodo especificado limita cu\xe1nto tiempo permanece v\xe1lida una contrase\xf1a comprometida, y evitar la reutilizaci\xf3n de contrase\xf1as asegura que los usuarios no puedan volver inmediatamente a credenciales expuestas previamente despu\xe9s de un cambio obligatorio.\\nRecomendar la misma contrase\xf1a en AWS y en otros sitios crea un riesgo de seguridad significativo mediante la reutilizaci\xf3n de credenciales, ya que una brecha en cualquier otro sitio comprometer\xeda entonces la cuenta de AWS; la unicidad de la contrase\xf1a para cada servicio es una pr\xe1ctica de seguridad fundamental. Requerir que los usuarios de IAM almacenen sus contrase\xf1as en texto sin formato sin cifrar expondr\xeda esas contrase\xf1as a cualquiera que obtenga acceso a donde se almacenen, creando vulnerabilidades de seguridad graves; las contrase\xf1as deben mantenerse confidenciales y nunca almacenarse en texto sin formato. Deshabilitar MFA para los usuarios de IAM elimina un segundo factor de autenticaci\xf3n cr\xedtico, dejando las cuentas protegidas solo por contrase\xf1as y aumentando significativamente el riesgo de acceso no autorizado si las credenciales se comprometen."},{"id":"q793","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 situaci\xf3n debe reportarse al equipo de AWS Abuse?","options":{"A":"Una Zona de Disponibilidad tiene una interrupci\xf3n del servicio","B":"Se realiza un intento de intrusi\xf3n desde una direcci\xf3n IP de AWS","C":"Un usuario tiene problemas para acceder a un bucket de Amazon S3 desde una direcci\xf3n IP de AWS","D":"Un usuario necesita cambiar los m\xe9todos de pago debido a un compromiso"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los intentos de intrusi\xf3n que se originan en direcciones IP de AWS son escenarios de abuso que deben reportarse al equipo de AWS Abuse, que investiga el uso indebido de recursos de AWS para actividades maliciosas como hacking, phishing y ataques de denegaci\xf3n de servicio.\\nUna interrupci\xf3n del servicio en una Zona de Disponibilidad es un problema de disponibilidad de la infraestructura que debe monitorearse a trav\xe9s del AWS Health Dashboard y reportarse a AWS Support si est\xe1 causando impacto operativo, en lugar de ser un escenario de abuso para el equipo de Abuse. Que un usuario tenga problemas para acceder a un bucket de S3 desde una direcci\xf3n IP de AWS es un problema t\xe9cnico de acceso o permisos que debe investigarse verificando pol\xedticas de IAM, pol\xedticas de bucket y configuraciones de VPC, y abordarse a trav\xe9s de AWS Support si es necesario, en lugar de reportarse al equipo de Abuse. Que un usuario necesite cambiar los m\xe9todos de pago debido a un compromiso es un asunto de facturaci\xf3n y seguridad de la cuenta que debe manejarse a trav\xe9s de la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS o contactando al servicio al cliente de AWS para obtener asistencia de facturaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q796","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son componentes de Amazon VPC?","options":{"A":"Objects","B":"Subredes","C":"Buckets","D":"Puertas de enlace de Internet","E":"Clave de acceso"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Las subredes dividen el rango de direcciones IP de una VPC en segmentos que organizan recursos y definen l\xedmites de enrutamiento dentro de la VPC, y las puertas de enlace de Internet permiten la comunicaci\xf3n entre los recursos de una VPC e Internet. Ambos son componentes fundamentales de VPC.\\nObjects es un t\xe9rmino de Amazon S3 para los elementos de datos almacenados dentro de los buckets de S3, y es un concepto de abstracci\xf3n de almacenamiento sin conexi\xf3n con componentes de redes de Amazon VPC, como subredes, tablas de rutas o puertas de enlace. Los buckets son contenedores de Amazon S3 para almacenar objetos y son la unidad de almacenamiento principal del servicio S3, y no son componentes de Amazon VPC, que es un servicio de red virtual en lugar de un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos. Las claves de acceso son credenciales de IAM que consisten en un ID de clave de acceso y una clave de acceso secreta utilizadas para la autenticaci\xf3n program\xe1tica de API y CLI, y son construcciones de administraci\xf3n de identidades en lugar de componentes de redes de VPC."},{"id":"q800","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 funciones pueden realizar los usuarios con AWS KMS?","options":{"A":"Crear y administrar claves de acceso de AWS para el usuario ra\xedz de la cuenta de AWS","B":"Crear y administrar claves de acceso de AWS para un usuario de IAM de una cuenta de AWS","C":"Crear y administrar claves para el cifrado y descifrado de datos","D":"Crear y administrar claves para la autenticaci\xf3n multifactor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS KMS permite a los usuarios crear y administrar claves criptogr\xe1ficas para cifrar y descifrar datos en los servicios de AWS, proporcionando administraci\xf3n centralizada del ciclo de vida de las claves con registro de auditor\xeda del uso de claves mediante CloudTrail.\\nLa creaci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de claves de acceso de AWS para el usuario ra\xedz se realiza a trav\xe9s de la secci\xf3n IAM de la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS, y las claves de acceso son credenciales de autenticaci\xf3n en lugar de claves criptogr\xe1ficas administradas por KMS. La creaci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de claves de acceso de AWS para usuarios de IAM se realiza mediante la consola o la CLI de IAM, y las claves de acceso son credenciales de autenticaci\xf3n program\xe1tica no relacionadas con la funci\xf3n de administraci\xf3n de claves de cifrado de AWS KMS. La creaci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de claves para la autenticaci\xf3n multifactor implica configurar dispositivos MFA virtuales o de hardware en la consola de IAM, y los tokens MFA son mecanismos de autenticaci\xf3n en lugar de claves criptogr\xe1ficas para el cifrado y descifrado de datos."},{"id":"q806","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de Amazon VPC permite a los usuarios capturar informaci\xf3n sobre el tr\xe1fico IP que llega a las instancias de Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"Grupos de seguridad","B":"Interfaces de red el\xe1sticas","C":"Listas de control de acceso de red (ACL de red)","D":"VPC Flow Logs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"VPC Flow Logs captura metadatos sobre el tr\xe1fico IP que va hacia y desde las interfaces de red en una VPC, proporcionando visibilidad de los patrones de tr\xe1fico para an\xe1lisis de seguridad, auditor\xeda de cumplimiento y soluci\xf3n de problemas de conectividad.\\nLos grupos de seguridad act\xfaan como firewalls virtuales con estado a nivel de instancia de EC2 que controlan qu\xe9 tr\xe1fico se permite de entrada y salida seg\xfan reglas definidas, pero filtran el tr\xe1fico en lugar de capturar o registrar informaci\xf3n sobre \xe9l. Las interfaces de red el\xe1sticas son tarjetas de red virtuales que se conectan a instancias de EC2 para proporcionar conectividad de red, y son un componente de red en lugar de una funci\xf3n para capturar o registrar datos de tr\xe1fico IP. Las listas de control de acceso de red (ACL de red) act\xfaan como firewalls sin estado a nivel de subred que permiten o deniegan tr\xe1fico seg\xfan reglas de direcci\xf3n IP y puerto, pero al igual que los grupos de seguridad, filtran el tr\xe1fico en lugar de capturar o registrar informaci\xf3n sobre \xe9l."},{"id":"q808","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona una forma de generar claves de cifrado que pueden usarse para cifrar datos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Macie","B":"AWS Certificate Manager","C":"AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","D":"AWS Secrets Manager","E":"AWS CloudHSM"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"AWS KMS genera y administra claves de cifrado mediante una infraestructura basada en software administrada por AWS, y AWS CloudHSM proporciona m\xf3dulos de seguridad de hardware dedicados que brindan a los clientes control total sobre la generaci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de claves. Ambos servicios proporcionan formas de generar claves de cifrado que pueden usarse para cifrar datos.\\nAmazon Macie utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para descubrir y clasificar datos confidenciales en Amazon S3, y es un servicio de clasificaci\xf3n de datos en lugar de un servicio de generaci\xf3n de claves que proporcione claves de cifrado para proteger datos. AWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y administra certificados SSL/TLS para cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito, y no genera claves de cifrado de uso general para proteger datos en reposo de clientes. AWS Secrets Manager almacena, rota y administra el acceso a secretos, como credenciales de bases de datos y claves de API, y aunque utiliza cifrado para proteger los secretos almacenados, no es un servicio que genere claves de cifrado para que los clientes las usen en sus propios flujos de trabajo de cifrado de datos."},{"id":"q811","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ayuda a los usuarios a cumplir requisitos de cumplimiento contractuales y regulatorios para la seguridad de los datos mediante el uso de dispositivos de hardware dedicados dentro de AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"AWS CloudHSM","C":"AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","D":"AWS Directory Service"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudHSM proporciona m\xf3dulos de seguridad de hardware dedicados de inquilino \xfanico dentro de AWS Cloud que cumplen los requisitos de cumplimiento FIPS 140-2 Nivel 3, ofreciendo control completo del cliente sobre la generaci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de claves en hardware dedicado para organizaciones con mandatos contractuales o regulatorios estrictos.\\nAWS Secrets Manager almacena, rota y administra secretos, como credenciales de bases de datos y claves de API; maneja el ciclo de vida de los secretos en lugar de proporcionar dispositivos de hardware dedicados. AWS KMS utiliza HSMs pero comparte el hardware subyacente entre clientes, por lo que no ofrece el hardware dedicado de inquilino \xfanico que algunos requisitos contractuales o regulatorios exigen. AWS Directory Service proporciona Microsoft Active Directory administrado para administraci\xf3n de identidades e inicio de sesi\xf3n \xfanico, sin conexi\xf3n con m\xf3dulos de seguridad de hardware o administraci\xf3n de claves."},{"id":"q812","question":"En el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1l de los siguientes elementos gestiona el cliente?","options":{"A":"La retirada de servicio de dispositivos de almacenamiento f\xedsicos","B":"La configuraci\xf3n de grupos de seguridad y ACL","C":"La gesti\xf3n de parches del sistema operativo de una instancia de Amazon RDS","D":"El control del acceso f\xedsico a los centros de datos","E":"La gesti\xf3n de parches del sistema operativo de una instancia de Amazon EC2"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Los clientes gestionan las configuraciones de grupos de seguridad y ACL para controlar el acceso de red entrante y saliente a sus recursos como parte de la protecci\xf3n de su entorno de red, y son responsables de aplicar parches al sistema operativo invitado en instancias EC2, incluida la aplicaci\xf3n de actualizaciones de seguridad y parches de vulnerabilidad.\\nLa retirada de servicio de dispositivos de almacenamiento f\xedsicos, incluido el borrado seguro de datos y la destrucci\xf3n f\xedsica, es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS como parte de la gesti\xf3n de la infraestructura f\xedsica en centros de datos a los que los clientes nunca acceden. El control del acceso f\xedsico a los centros de datos de AWS, incluidos lectores de tarjetas, biometr\xeda y vigilancia, es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen presencia ni acceso a las instalaciones donde se ubica el hardware de AWS. La gesti\xf3n de parches del sistema operativo de la instancia de Amazon RDS es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que RDS es un servicio gestionado en el que AWS se encarga del parcheo del motor de base de datos subyacente, del parcheo del sistema operativo y del mantenimiento; los clientes solo necesitan gestionar el parcheo de las bases de datos que ejecutan ellos mismos en EC2."},{"id":"q815","question":"\xbfDe qu\xe9 es responsable un usuario cuando ejecuta una aplicaci\xf3n en la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Gestionar el hardware f\xedsico","B":"Actualizar el hipervisor subyacente","C":"Proporcionar una lista de usuarios aprobados para el acceso al centro de datos","D":"Gestionar las actualizaciones del software de la aplicaci\xf3n"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cuando se ejecutan aplicaciones en la nube de AWS, los usuarios son responsables de gestionar las actualizaciones del software de la aplicaci\xf3n, incluidos parches, actualizaciones de versi\xf3n y correcciones de seguridad para su propio c\xf3digo de aplicaci\xf3n.\\nGestionar el hardware f\xedsico es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso a los servidores f\xedsicos subyacentes, al equipamiento de red ni a la infraestructura de centros de datos que ejecuta los servicios de AWS. Actualizar el hipervisor subyacente es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que la capa de virtualizaci\xf3n que ejecuta las instancias EC2 forma parte de la infraestructura que AWS posee y mantiene en nombre de todos los clientes. Proporcionar una lista de usuarios aprobados para el acceso al centro de datos es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que AWS gestiona todos los controles de acceso f\xedsico a sus centros de datos y los clientes no tienen ning\xfan rol en determinar qui\xe9n puede entrar en las instalaciones de AWS."},{"id":"q819","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ayuda a identificar actividades maliciosas o no autorizadas en cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon Rekognition","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"Amazon GuardDuty","D":"Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que monitorea continuamente cuentas de AWS, registros de eventos de CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs y registros DNS usando aprendizaje autom\xe1tico e inteligencia sobre amenazas para identificar actividad maliciosa y comportamiento no autorizado.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y eventos de gesti\xf3n en una cuenta de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y proporciona los datos de actividad sin procesar que GuardDuty analiza, pero CloudTrail por s\xed mismo no identifica ni alerta sobre actividades maliciosas o no autorizadas. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas en las categor\xedas de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, incluidos controles de seguridad para puertos abiertos y MFA ausente, pero no monitorea continuamente amenazas activas ni comportamiento malicioso en curso en las cuentas. Amazon Macie usa aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para descubrir y clasificar datos sensibles almacenados en Amazon S3, y es un servicio de clasificaci\xf3n y protecci\xf3n de datos en lugar de un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas en tiempo real que monitorea la actividad de toda la cuenta."},{"id":"q822","question":"Una empresa aloja una aplicaci\xf3n web en un contenedor Docker en Amazon EC2. \xbfDe cu\xe1l de las siguientes tareas es responsable AWS?","options":{"A":"Escalar la aplicaci\xf3n web y los servicios desarrollados con Docker","B":"Aprovisionar o programar contenedores para que se ejecuten en cl\xfasteres y mantener su disponibilidad","C":"Realizar el mantenimiento del hardware en las instalaciones de AWS que ejecutan la nube de AWS","D":"Gestionar el sistema operativo invitado, incluidas las actualizaciones y los parches de seguridad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cuando un cliente ejecuta un contenedor Docker en Amazon EC2, AWS es responsable de la capa de infraestructura f\xedsica, lo que incluye realizar el mantenimiento del hardware en las instalaciones de AWS que ejecutan la nube de AWS.\\nEscalar la aplicaci\xf3n web y los servicios desarrollados con Docker es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que las cargas de trabajo de contenedores basadas en EC2 requieren que el cliente configure el escalado mediante servicios como EC2 Auto Scaling o Amazon ECS. Aprovisionar o programar contenedores para que se ejecuten en cl\xfasteres y mantener su disponibilidad tambi\xe9n es responsabilidad del cliente cuando ejecuta Docker directamente en EC2, ya que el cliente gestiona el entorno de ejecuci\xf3n de contenedores y la capa de orquestaci\xf3n en lugar de AWS. Gestionar el sistema operativo invitado, incluidas las actualizaciones y los parches de seguridad, es responsabilidad del cliente para las instancias EC2, ya que el modelo de responsabilidad compartida deja firmemente la gesti\xf3n del sistema operativo en manos del cliente para los servicios IaaS."},{"id":"q824","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 acciones representan pr\xe1cticas recomendadas para usar AWS IAM?","options":{"A":"Configurar una pol\xedtica de contrase\xf1as segura","B":"Compartir las credenciales de seguridad entre los usuarios de cuentas de AWS que est\xe1n en la misma Regi\xf3n","C":"Usar claves de acceso para iniciar sesi\xf3n en la Consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS","D":"Rotar las claves de acceso de forma regular","E":"Evitar usar roles de IAM para delegar permisos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Aplicar permisos de m\xednimo privilegio garantiza que los usuarios y roles de IAM tengan solo el acceso que necesitan para realizar sus tareas, y usar roles de IAM para aplicaciones que se ejecutan en instancias EC2 evita incrustar credenciales de larga duraci\xf3n en el c\xf3digo. Ambas son pr\xe1cticas recomendadas de seguridad de IAM.\\nCompartir credenciales entre usuarios contradice las pr\xe1cticas recomendadas de IAM; cada usuario debe tener sus propias credenciales \xfanicas para mantener la responsabilidad individual y permitir un alcance preciso de los permisos, y compartir credenciales impide rastrear qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 qu\xe9 acciones. Almacenar claves de acceso en texto plano en instancias EC2 es un antipatr\xf3n de seguridad que conlleva el riesgo de exposici\xf3n de credenciales a trav\xe9s de los metadatos de la instancia, archivos de registro o acceso compartido a la instancia; en su lugar, se deben usar roles de IAM para proporcionar credenciales temporales autom\xe1ticamente. Usar la cuenta de usuario ra\xedz para todas las tareas administrativas contradice las pr\xe1cticas recomendadas de IAM; la cuenta ra\xedz solo debe usarse para tareas que la requieran espec\xedficamente, y se deben usar usuarios de IAM con permisos adecuados para todas las actividades administrativas habituales."},{"id":"q825","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica o servicio de AWS se puede usar para capturar informaci\xf3n sobre el tr\xe1fico entrante y saliente en una infraestructura de AWS VPC?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"VPC Flow Logs","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"VPC Flow Logs captura metadatos sobre el tr\xe1fico IP que fluye hacia y desde las interfaces de red en una Amazon VPC, registrando informaci\xf3n sobre las direcciones IP de origen y destino, los puertos, los protocolos y si el tr\xe1fico fue permitido o rechazado.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y eventos de gesti\xf3n en una cuenta de AWS, capturando qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 solicitudes y qu\xe9 cambios se hicieron en los recursos de AWS, pero no captura datos de flujo de paquetes a nivel de red entre instancias como lo hacen VPC Flow Logs. AWS Config monitorea y registra continuamente las configuraciones de los recursos de AWS y las eval\xfaa seg\xfan reglas de cumplimiento, y es un servicio de seguimiento del estado de la configuraci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio de captura y registro de tr\xe1fico de red. Amazon CloudWatch recopila m\xe9tricas operativas, registros y eventos de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS con fines de monitoreo y alertas, y aunque puede recopilar registros a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n, no captura los datos de flujo de tr\xe1fico IP entre interfaces de red que proporciona VPC Flow Logs."},{"id":"q828","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio se puede usar para monitorear y recibir alertas sobre eventos de inicio de sesi\xf3n en la Consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS del usuario ra\xedz de la cuenta de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS IAM"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch puede monitorear eventos de inicio de sesi\xf3n en la consola del usuario ra\xedz mediante la integraci\xf3n con CloudTrail, y se puede configurar para enviar alertas a trav\xe9s de SNS cuando el usuario ra\xedz inicia sesi\xf3n, lo que permite a los equipos de seguridad detectar y responder a accesos inesperados a la cuenta ra\xedz.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y eventos de gesti\xf3n, incluidos los inicios de sesi\xf3n del usuario ra\xedz, y es la fuente de los datos del evento de inicio de sesi\xf3n, pero por s\xed mismo no env\xeda alertas ni notificaciones cuando ocurren eventos espec\xedficos sin integraci\xf3n con CloudWatch Events o EventBridge. AWS Config monitorea continuamente las configuraciones de los recursos y eval\xfaa el cumplimiento, y aunque rastrea cambios en el estado de los recursos, no se usa para enviar alertas en tiempo real para eventos de inicio de sesi\xf3n espec\xedficos de la cuenta, como los inicios de sesi\xf3n en la consola del usuario ra\xedz. AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes y certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS, y es un portal de documentaci\xf3n sin capacidad para monitorear ni alertar sobre la actividad de la cuenta de AWS o eventos de inicio de sesi\xf3n."},{"id":"q839","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 tareas son responsabilidad del cliente en el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS?","options":{"A":"Gesti\xf3n del acceso a las instalaciones de infraestructura","B":"Gesti\xf3n del ciclo de vida del hardware de la infraestructura en la nube","C":"Gesti\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n de las aplicaciones del usuario","D":"Protecci\xf3n de la infraestructura de red","E":"Configuraci\xf3n de grupos de seguridad"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Los clientes son responsables de la gesti\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n de sus propias aplicaciones, incluida la forma en que esas aplicaciones se configuran y mantienen, y de configurar los grupos de seguridad que controlan el acceso de red a sus recursos. Ambos aspectos recaen en el lado del cliente del modelo de responsabilidad compartida.\\nLa gesti\xf3n del acceso a las instalaciones de infraestructura se refiere a los controles de acceso f\xedsico para los centros de datos de AWS, lo cual es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes no tienen acceso ni control sobre las instalaciones f\xedsicas que alojan sus cargas de trabajo. La gesti\xf3n del ciclo de vida del hardware de la infraestructura en la nube abarca la adquisici\xf3n, el mantenimiento y la retirada de servicio de servidores f\xedsicos y equipos de red, que AWS gestiona en nombre de todos los clientes sin ninguna participaci\xf3n del cliente. La protecci\xf3n de la infraestructura de red se refiere a proteger la red global subyacente que conecta las Regiones de AWS, las Zonas de Disponibilidad y las ubicaciones de borde, lo cual es responsabilidad de AWS como parte de sus obligaciones de seguridad de la nube."},{"id":"q843","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n web est\xe1 alojada en AWS usando un Elastic Load Balancer, varias instancias de Amazon EC2 y Amazon RDS. \xbfQu\xe9 medidas de seguridad son responsabilidad de AWS?","options":{"A":"Ejecutar un an\xe1lisis de virus en las instancias de EC2","B":"Protegerse contra la suplantaci\xf3n de IP y la captura de paquetes","C":"Instalar los parches de seguridad m\xe1s recientes en la instancia de RDS","D":"Cifrar la comunicaci\xf3n entre las instancias de EC2 y el Elastic Load Balancer","E":"Configurar un grupo de seguridad y una lista de control de acceso a la red (NACL) para EC2"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS es responsable de proteger contra la suplantaci\xf3n de IP y la captura de paquetes a nivel de red como parte de la seguridad de la infraestructura, y de aplicar parches al sistema operativo subyacente de las instancias administradas de RDS, ya que RDS es un servicio completamente administrado en el que AWS se encarga de la aplicaci\xf3n de parches al motor de base de datos y al sistema operativo.\\nEjecutar un an\xe1lisis de virus en las instancias de EC2 es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que el cliente administra el sistema operativo invitado y el software instalado en sus instancias de EC2, incluido el conjunto de herramientas de seguridad para detectar software malicioso. Cifrar la comunicaci\xf3n entre las instancias de EC2 y el Elastic Load Balancer requiere que el cliente configure los ajustes de SSL/TLS en la aplicaci\xf3n y el balanceador de carga, ya que el cliente controla su propia arquitectura de aplicaci\xf3n y debe elegir habilitar las comunicaciones cifradas. Configurar grupos de seguridad y ACL de red para las instancias de EC2 es responsabilidad del cliente para definir qu\xe9 tr\xe1fico de red se permite hacia y desde sus propios recursos de c\xf3mputo dentro de la VPC."},{"id":"q846","question":"Una empresa necesita un bucket de Amazon S3 que no pueda tener ning\xfan objeto p\xfablico debido a requisitos de cumplimiento. \xbfC\xf3mo se puede lograr esto?","options":{"A":"Habilitar S3 Block Public Access desde la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS","B":"Realizar una reuni\xf3n de equipo para hablar sobre la importancia de subir solo objetos privados de S3","C":"Requerir que todos los objetos de S3 se aprueben manualmente antes de subirlos","D":"Crear un servicio para monitorear todas las cargas a S3 y eliminar cualquier carga p\xfablica"},"correct":"A","explanation":"S3 Block Public Access es una configuraci\xf3n a nivel de cuenta o de bucket que anula cualquier pol\xedtica o ACL que otorgue acceso p\xfablico, lo que garantiza que ning\xfan objeto del bucket pueda hacerse p\xfablico independientemente de c\xf3mo se cargue.\\nRealizar una reuni\xf3n de equipo depende de la concientizaci\xf3n humana y del cumplimiento voluntario, lo que no aplica t\xe9cnicamente la restricci\xf3n y no puede garantizar que nunca se carguen objetos p\xfablicos. Requerir aprobaci\xf3n manual antes de la carga no es una funci\xf3n nativa de S3 e introduce un proceso manual que no escala y que aun as\xed no impide t\xe9cnicamente que se creen objetos p\xfablicos. Crear un servicio de monitoreo personalizado para detectar y eliminar cargas p\xfablicas introduce complejidad innecesaria, retraso entre la carga y la remediaci\xf3n, y depende de controles reactivos en lugar de preventivos, lo que lo hace mucho menos confiable que la configuraci\xf3n integrada Block Public Access."},{"id":"q849","question":"\xbfQui\xe9n es responsable de aplicar parches al sistema operativo invitado de Amazon RDS?","options":{"A":"El equipo de producto de AWS","B":"El administrador de bases de datos del cliente","C":"Socios administrados","D":"AWS Support"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aplicar parches al sistema operativo invitado de Amazon RDS es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que RDS es un servicio de base de datos completamente administrado en el que AWS se encarga de todo el mantenimiento de la infraestructura subyacente, incluidos los parches del sistema operativo y las actualizaciones del motor de base de datos.\\nEl cliente no es responsable de aplicar parches al sistema operativo invitado en Amazon RDS porque RDS abstrae completamente la capa de infraestructura; a diferencia de EC2, donde los clientes administran el sistema operativo, las instancias de RDS tienen su sistema operativo administrado y parcheado por AWS como parte del modelo de servicio administrado. El equipo de cumplimiento de AWS no aplica parches a los sistemas operativos de RDS; la aplicaci\xf3n de parches la realiza el equipo de ingenier\xeda del servicio RDS como parte del mantenimiento rutinario del servicio administrado, no una funci\xf3n de cumplimiento dentro de AWS. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones y comprobaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas en seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, pero no realiza tareas operativas como aplicar parches a los sistemas operativos de las instancias de base de datos."},{"id":"q855","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1l de las siguientes es responsabilidad del cliente?","options":{"A":"Instalar parches de seguridad para los hipervisores Xen y KVM","B":"Instalar parches del sistema operativo para Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Instalar parches de seguridad del sistema operativo para las instancias de base de datos de Amazon EC2","D":"Instalar parches de seguridad del sistema operativo para las instancias de base de datos de Amazon RDS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los clientes son responsables de instalar parches de seguridad del sistema operativo en sus instancias de Amazon EC2, incluido cualquier software de base de datos que se ejecute en esas instancias, ya que el sistema operativo invitado queda dentro del l\xedmite de responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida.\\nInstalar parches de seguridad para los hipervisores Xen y KVM es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que AWS es propietaria y administra la capa de virtualizaci\xf3n que se encuentra entre el hardware f\xedsico y las instancias del cliente, y los clientes no tienen acceso ni visibilidad del hipervisor. Instalar parches del sistema operativo para Amazon DynamoDB es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que DynamoDB es un servicio completamente administrado en el que AWS se encarga de todo el mantenimiento de la infraestructura subyacente, del sistema operativo y del motor de base de datos en nombre del cliente. Instalar parches de seguridad del sistema operativo para las instancias de base de datos de Amazon RDS es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional completamente administrado en el que AWS administra la aplicaci\xf3n de parches al sistema operativo y al motor de base de datos, dejando a los clientes responsables solo de sus datos y de las configuraciones a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q857","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, la seguridad y la aplicaci\xf3n de parches al sistema operativo invitado son responsabilidad de:","options":{"A":"AWS Support","B":"El cliente","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, el cliente es responsable de la seguridad y la aplicaci\xf3n de parches al sistema operativo invitado en las instancias de EC2, incluida la aplicaci\xf3n de actualizaciones de seguridad y la configuraci\xf3n del sistema operativo, ya que AWS solo administra la infraestructura que est\xe1 por debajo del hipervisor.\\nAWS Support proporciona asistencia t\xe9cnica y orientaci\xf3n a los clientes que tienen problemas con su entorno de AWS, pero no es responsable de aplicar parches ni de proteger los sistemas operativos en las instancias del cliente. AWS Systems Manager puede ayudar a los clientes a automatizar tareas de administraci\xf3n de parches en instancias de EC2, pero es una herramienta que ayuda a los clientes a cumplir con su propia responsabilidad de aplicaci\xf3n de parches en lugar de transferir esa responsabilidad a AWS. AWS Config registra y eval\xfaa los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS con fines de cumplimiento, y no realiza ni asume la responsabilidad de la aplicaci\xf3n de parches al sistema operativo en las instancias de EC2."},{"id":"q858","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS facilita la creaci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de usuarios y grupos de AWS, y les proporciona acceso seguro a los recursos de AWS sin costo?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","D":"AWS Firewall Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Identity and Access Management es el servicio que permite crear y administrar usuarios y grupos de AWS, asignar permisos mediante pol\xedticas y controlar de forma segura qu\xe9 usuarios tienen acceso a qu\xe9 servicios y recursos de AWS.\\nAmazon Cognito es un servicio de identidad para usuarios de aplicaciones web y m\xf3viles que permite el registro, el inicio de sesi\xf3n y el acceso federado con proveedores de identidad externos, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la administraci\xf3n de usuarios a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n en lugar de administrar el acceso a los servicios de AWS para administradores. AWS Single Sign-On, ahora llamado AWS IAM Identity Center, proporciona acceso de inicio de sesi\xf3n \xfanico a varias cuentas y aplicaciones de AWS usando un \xfanico conjunto de credenciales y, aunque se basa en IAM, el servicio fundamental para crear y administrar usuarios y grupos dentro de AWS es IAM en s\xed. AWS Directory Service proporciona Microsoft Active Directory administrado para la administraci\xf3n de identidades empresariales y se integra con IAM para el acceso a la consola de AWS, pero el servicio principal para crear y administrar los usuarios, grupos y controles de acceso usados dentro de AWS es IAM."},{"id":"q859","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona acceso bajo demanda a la documentaci\xf3n de seguridad y cumplimiento de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Directory Service","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Artifact es el portal de autoservicio que proporciona acceso bajo demanda a la documentaci\xf3n de seguridad y cumplimiento de AWS, incluidos informes SOC, certificaciones ISO, atestaciones PCI y otros materiales de cumplimiento disponibles directamente desde la consola de AWS.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones y comprobaciones automatizadas en las categor\xedas de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos para ayudar a mejorar su entorno de AWS, y es un servicio de asesoramiento en lugar de un portal de documentaci\xf3n e informes de cumplimiento. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades que analiza instancias de EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y produce hallazgos de evaluaci\xf3n espec\xedficos del cliente en lugar de documentaci\xf3n de seguridad y cumplimiento de AWS. Amazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas operativas, registros y eventos de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS, y es un servicio de observabilidad y alertas en lugar de una fuente de documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento de AWS."},{"id":"q862","question":"Un usuario necesita generar un informe que describa el estado de las comprobaciones de seguridad clave en una cuenta de AWS. El informe debe incluir el estado de los permisos de los buckets de Amazon S3, si la autenticaci\xf3n multifactor est\xe1 habilitada para el usuario ra\xedz de la cuenta de AWS y si alg\xfan grupo de seguridad est\xe1 configurado para permitir acceso sin restricciones. \xbfD\xf3nde se puede encontrar toda esta informaci\xf3n en un solo lugar?","options":{"A":"Panel de Amazon QuickSight","B":"Rastros de AWS CloudTrail","C":"Informe de AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Informe de credenciales de IAM"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona un panel con formato de informe de los resultados de las comprobaciones en seguridad, costo, rendimiento, tolerancia a fallos y l\xedmites de servicio, incluidas comprobaciones de seguridad clave como MFA en la cuenta ra\xedz, claves de acceso expuestas y grupos de seguridad abiertos.\\nAmazon QuickSight es un servicio de inteligencia empresarial y visualizaci\xf3n de datos para crear paneles e informes a partir de fuentes de datos y, aunque puede mostrar datos, no proporciona resultados automatizados de comprobaciones de seguridad de AWS ni res\xfamenes del estado de seguridad a nivel de cuenta. Los rastros de AWS CloudTrail registran llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta con fines de auditor\xeda y, aunque capturan eventos relevantes para la seguridad, producen registros sin procesar en lugar de un informe resumido del estado de los hallazgos de las comprobaciones de seguridad clave. El informe de credenciales de IAM enumera todos los usuarios de IAM y el estado de sus credenciales, incluida la antig\xfcedad de la contrase\xf1a, el estado de la clave de acceso y la habilitaci\xf3n de MFA y, aunque es una herramienta de seguridad \xfatil, solo cubre el estado de las credenciales de IAM en lugar del conjunto m\xe1s amplio de comprobaciones de seguridad clave que eval\xfaa Trusted Advisor."},{"id":"q865","question":"Usar AWS Config para registrar, auditar y evaluar cambios en los recursos de AWS para permitir la trazabilidad es un ejemplo de \xbfqu\xe9 pilar del AWS Well-Architected Framework?","options":{"A":"Seguridad","B":"Excelencia operativa","C":"Eficiencia de rendimiento","D":"Optimizaci\xf3n de costos"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Usar AWS Config para registrar, auditar y evaluar cambios en los recursos es un ejemplo de habilitar la trazabilidad, que es un principio de dise\xf1o central del pilar de Seguridad enfocado en el monitoreo y los controles de detecci\xf3n.\\nLa Excelencia operativa se enfoca en ejecutar y mejorar las operaciones mediante automatizaci\xf3n y responder a eventos, no en el monitoreo de detecci\xf3n de cambios en los recursos para trazabilidad. La Eficiencia de rendimiento se enfoca en usar los recursos de c\xf3mputo de manera eficiente para cumplir los requisitos de la carga de trabajo a medida que la demanda cambia con el tiempo. La Optimizaci\xf3n de costos se enfoca en eliminar gastos innecesarios y seleccionar los tipos de recursos adecuados para reducir los costos generales."},{"id":"q873","question":"En el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1les de las siguientes son responsabilidades del cliente?","options":{"A":"Configurar el cifrado del lado del servidor en un bucket de Amazon S3","B":"Aplicaci\xf3n de parches a la instancia de Amazon RDS","C":"Configuraciones de red y firewall","D":"Seguridad f\xedsica de las instalaciones del centro de datos","E":"Disponibilidad de capacidad de c\xf3mputo"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Los clientes son responsables de configurar el cifrado del lado del servidor en buckets de S3 para proteger los datos en reposo, y de configurar ajustes de red y firewall, como grupos de seguridad y NACL, para controlar el tr\xe1fico hacia sus recursos. Ambos aspectos corresponden al lado del cliente en el Modelo de responsabilidad compartida.\\nLa aplicaci\xf3n de parches a la instancia de Amazon RDS es responsabilidad de AWS para los servicios de base de datos administrados, ya que AWS gestiona toda la aplicaci\xf3n de parches a nivel de motor y las ventanas de mantenimiento sin participaci\xf3n del cliente. La seguridad f\xedsica de las instalaciones del centro de datos es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, y abarca los edificios, el hardware y los controles ambientales a los que los clientes no tienen acceso. La disponibilidad de capacidad de c\xf3mputo es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que AWS administra la infraestructura subyacente para garantizar que los recursos de c\xf3mputo permanezcan disponibles para que los clientes los aprovisionen y usen."},{"id":"q880","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes act\xfaa como un firewall virtual a nivel de instancia de Amazon EC2 para controlar el tr\xe1fico de una o m\xe1s instancias?","options":{"A":"Claves de acceso","B":"Puertas de enlace privadas virtuales","C":"Grupos de seguridad","D":"Listas de control de acceso (ACL)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los grupos de seguridad act\xfaan como un firewall virtual a nivel de instancia de EC2, controlando el tr\xe1fico de entrada y salida de una o m\xe1s instancias al evaluar reglas basadas en protocolo, puerto y origen y destino.\\nLas claves de acceso son credenciales de largo plazo que consisten en un ID de clave de acceso y un secreto usados para el acceso program\xe1tico a AWS mediante la CLI o la API, y son un mecanismo de autenticaci\xf3n sin conexi\xf3n con el control del tr\xe1fico de red. Las puertas de enlace privadas virtuales son el endpoint del lado de AWS de una conexi\xf3n VPN de sitio a sitio que permite la comunicaci\xf3n cifrada entre una VPC y una red local, y son un componente de conectividad en lugar de un mecanismo de filtrado de tr\xe1fico a nivel de instancia. Las listas de control de acceso operan como firewalls sin estado a nivel de subred en lugar de a nivel de instancia, aplicando reglas de entrada y salida a todo el tr\xe1fico que entra o sale de una subred sin rastrear el estado de la conexi\xf3n como lo hacen los grupos de seguridad."},{"id":"q883","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS ayuda a restringir los servicios, recursos y acciones de API individuales de AWS a los que pueden acceder los usuarios y roles de cada cuenta miembro?","options":{"A":"Amazon Cognito","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"AWS Shield","D":"AWS Firewall Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las pol\xedticas de control de servicios de AWS Organizations permiten a las organizaciones definir y aplicar los permisos m\xe1ximos disponibles para todas las cuentas dentro de una organizaci\xf3n o unidades organizativas espec\xedficas, restringiendo a qu\xe9 servicios, recursos y acciones de API de AWS pueden acceder los usuarios de esas cuentas.\\nAWS WAF es un firewall de aplicaciones web que filtra tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS seg\xfan reglas definidas para bloquear exploits web comunes como inyecci\xf3n SQL y cross-site scripting, y opera en la capa de tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones en lugar de restringir el acceso a servicios de AWS y acciones de API para usuarios de IAM. Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que monitorea cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS en busca de actividad maliciosa y comportamiento an\xf3malo, y es un servicio de detecci\xf3n y alerta en lugar de un servicio que restrinja qu\xe9 servicios de AWS y acciones de API pueden invocar los usuarios. AWS Firewall Manager administra de forma centralizada reglas de AWS WAF, protecciones de Shield y pol\xedticas de grupos de seguridad en varias cuentas de una organizaci\xf3n, y es una capa de administraci\xf3n de seguridad en lugar de un servicio que aplique restricciones a nivel de IAM sobre acciones de API."},{"id":"q884","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el mejor recurso para que un usuario encuentre informaci\xf3n y reportes relacionados con el cumplimiento sobre AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Marketplace","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS Support"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact es un portal de autoservicio que proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes de cumplimiento, certificaciones y acuerdos de AWS, lo que lo convierte en el mejor recurso para los usuarios que buscan informaci\xf3n y documentaci\xf3n relacionadas con el cumplimiento sobre AWS.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n en una cuenta de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y forenses, y es un servicio de registro de actividad en lugar de una fuente de informes de cumplimiento o documentaci\xf3n de certificaci\xf3n sobre la infraestructura de AWS. La consola de Amazon Inspector proporciona hallazgos de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades para instancias de EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores, y es una herramienta de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad para cargas de trabajo de clientes en lugar de un repositorio de informaci\xf3n e informes de cumplimiento a nivel de AWS. AWS Support brinda asistencia t\xe9cnica y orientaci\xf3n para usar los servicios de AWS, y aunque los ingenieros de soporte pueden responder preguntas relacionadas con el cumplimiento, el repositorio de autoservicio para documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento e informes formales es AWS Artifact."},{"id":"q889","question":"Un usuario desea cifrar los datos que recibe, almacena y administra AWS CloudTrail. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporcionar\xe1 esta capacidad?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"AWS Systems Manager","C":"AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","D":"AWS Certificate Manager"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS KMS administra claves de cifrado y se integra de forma nativa con CloudTrail para cifrar los archivos de registro almacenados en S3, lo que garantiza que los datos de registro de CloudTrail est\xe9n protegidos en reposo mediante claves administradas por KMS.\\nAWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y administra certificados SSL/TLS para cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito entre aplicaciones y usuarios finales, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de certificados en lugar de un servicio para cifrar datos recibidos y almacenados por CloudTrail. Amazon Macie usa aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para descubrir y clasificar datos sensibles almacenados en S3, y aunque puede identificar contenido sensible en los registros de CloudTrail almacenados en S3, no realiza el cifrado de esos datos. AWS Shield proporciona protecci\xf3n administrada contra DDoS en las capas de red y transporte, y es un servicio de protecci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico sin capacidad para cifrar datos almacenados por CloudTrail o cualquier otro servicio de AWS."},{"id":"q895","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona la capacidad de detectar filtraciones inadvertidas de datos de informaci\xf3n de identificaci\xf3n personal (PII) y datos de credenciales de usuario?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"Amazon Inspector","C":"Amazon Macie","D":"AWS Shield"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Macie usa aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para descubrir y clasificar autom\xe1ticamente datos sensibles en Amazon S3, incluida informaci\xf3n de identificaci\xf3n personal y credenciales de usuario, detectando posibles filtraciones de datos y exposici\xf3n inadvertida de datos sensibles.\\nAmazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que monitorea cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS en busca de actividad maliciosa y comportamiento an\xf3malo mediante aprendizaje autom\xe1tico e inteligencia de amenazas, y se enfoca en detectar amenazas de seguridad en lugar de identificar tipos espec\xedficos de datos sensibles almacenados en S3. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades que escanea instancias de EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y configuraciones incorrectas de red, y no clasifica ni detecta datos sensibles como PII almacenada en S3. AWS Shield proporciona protecci\xf3n administrada contra DDoS en las capas de red y transporte, y es un servicio de protecci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico sin capacidad para descubrir o detectar filtraciones de datos de informaci\xf3n de identificaci\xf3n personal."},{"id":"q898","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes describe una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de seguridad que se puede implementar usando AWS IAM?","options":{"A":"Deshabilitar el acceso a la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS para todos los usuarios","B":"Generar claves secretas para cada usuario de IAM","C":"Otorgar permisos solo a los usuarios que deban realizar una tarea determinada","D":"Almacenar credenciales de AWS dentro de instancias de Amazon EC2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Deshabilitar y eliminar credenciales de usuario de IAM innecesarias, incluidas claves de acceso inactivas y contrase\xf1as antiguas, reduce la superficie de ataque al eliminar credenciales que podr\xedan aprovecharse si se comprometen, lo que la convierte en una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de seguridad de IAM.\\nAsignar los mismos permisos a todos los usuarios de IAM independientemente de sus roles viola el principio de menor privilegio y crea riesgos innecesarios al dar a cada usuario acceso a recursos que no necesita, lo cual es un antipatr\xf3n en lugar de una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de seguridad. Crear claves de acceso para todos los usuarios de IAM de forma predeterminada genera credenciales de largo plazo innecesarias para usuarios que quiz\xe1s solo necesiten acceso a la consola, lo que aumenta el riesgo de exposici\xf3n de credenciales sin un beneficio operativo correspondiente. Usar un \xfanico usuario de IAM compartido para todos los miembros del equipo elimina la responsabilidad individual e impide identificar qu\xe9 persona espec\xedfica realiz\xf3 una acci\xf3n, lo que contradice la pr\xe1ctica recomendada de IAM de usar cuentas individuales con credenciales \xfanicas."},{"id":"q901","question":"Una empresa necesita protecci\xf3n contra ataques distribuidos de denegaci\xf3n de servicio (DDoS) ampliados en su sitio web y asistencia de expertos de AWS durante dichos eventos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio administrado de AWS cumplir\xe1 estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield Advanced","B":"AWS Firewall Manager","C":"AWS WAF","D":"Amazon GuardDuty"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Shield Advanced proporciona protecci\xf3n DDoS ampliada con visibilidad de ataques en tiempo real, acceso al Equipo de Respuesta a DDoS y protecci\xf3n de costos para los costos de escalado incurridos durante los ataques, lo que cumple los requisitos de protecci\xf3n contra ataques DDoS ampliados con asistencia de respuesta dedicada.\\nAWS WAF filtra tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS en la capa de aplicaci\xf3n seg\xfan reglas configurables y, aunque ayuda a mitigar ataques en la capa de aplicaci\xf3n y se integra con Shield Advanced, por s\xed solo no proporciona la protecci\xf3n DDoS ampliada ni el acceso al equipo de respuesta dedicado que requiere la pregunta. Amazon GuardDuty detecta amenazas y actividad maliciosa en cuentas de AWS mediante machine learning y, aunque puede identificar anomal\xedas relacionadas con DDoS, es un servicio de detecci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio activo de mitigaci\xf3n de DDoS con un equipo de respuesta dedicado. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento sin capacidad para proteger contra ataques distribuidos de denegaci\xf3n de servicio ni responder a ellos."},{"id":"q903","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1les son las responsabilidades del cliente?","options":{"A":"Seguridad f\xedsica y del entorno","B":"Dispositivos de red f\xedsicos, incluidos los firewalls","C":"Desmantelamiento de dispositivos de almacenamiento","D":"Seguridad de los datos en tr\xe1nsito","E":"Autenticaci\xf3n de integridad de los datos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Los clientes son responsables de proteger los datos en tr\xe1nsito implementando cifrado como TLS/SSL para proteger los datos a medida que se mueven entre sistemas, y de la autenticaci\xf3n de integridad de los datos para verificar que los datos no hayan sido alterados. Ambos recaen en el lado del cliente del Modelo de Responsabilidad Compartida.\\nLa seguridad f\xedsica y del entorno de los centros de datos es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, y abarca los edificios, la energ\xeda, la refrigeraci\xf3n y los controles de acceso f\xedsico en los que los clientes no participan. Los dispositivos de red f\xedsicos, incluidos los firewalls, son responsabilidad de AWS, ya que los clientes administran constructos de red virtuales como grupos de seguridad y NACL, pero no tienen acceso al hardware de red f\xedsico subyacente. El desmantelamiento de dispositivos de almacenamiento es responsabilidad de AWS, ya que AWS gestiona la eliminaci\xf3n y destrucci\xf3n seguras de los medios de almacenamiento f\xedsico cuando llegan al final de su vida \xfatil."},{"id":"q906","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones permite que una aplicaci\xf3n que se ejecuta en una instancia de Amazon EC2 escriba datos de forma segura en un bucket de Amazon S3 sin usar credenciales a largo plazo?","options":{"A":"Amazon Cognito","B":"AWS Shield","C":"Rol de IAM de AWS","D":"Clave de acceso de usuario de IAM de AWS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Un rol de IAM adjunto a una instancia de EC2 proporciona credenciales de seguridad temporales a las aplicaciones que se ejecutan en esa instancia, lo que les permite realizar llamadas autenticadas a la API de AWS a S3 sin requerir claves de acceso codificadas de forma r\xedgida en el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n con fines de auditor\xeda y, aunque registra las operaciones de escritura en S3, no otorga por s\xed mismo los permisos ni las credenciales que permiten a una aplicaci\xf3n autenticarse y escribir datos en S3. Una pol\xedtica de bucket de S3 puede otorgar permisos para que una instancia de EC2 espec\xedfica escriba en un bucket, pero la instancia a\xfan necesita credenciales de AWS v\xe1lidas para autenticar esas solicitudes, y un rol de IAM adjunto a la instancia es la forma recomendada de proporcionar esas credenciales de forma segura. Un usuario de IAM con acceso program\xe1tico podr\xeda proporcionar claves de acceso para que las use la aplicaci\xf3n, pero almacenar claves de acceso a largo plazo en el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n o en la instancia es un antipatr\xf3n de seguridad; los roles de IAM proporcionan credenciales temporales autom\xe1ticamente y son el enfoque recomendado."},{"id":"q912","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes tareas solo se puede realizar despu\xe9s de iniciar sesi\xf3n con las credenciales del usuario ra\xedz de la cuenta de AWS?","options":{"A":"Cerrar una cuenta de AWS","B":"Crear una nueva pol\xedtica de IAM","C":"Cambiar los planes de AWS Support","D":"Adjuntar un rol a una instancia de Amazon EC2","E":"Generar claves de acceso para usuarios de IAM"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Cerrar una cuenta de AWS y cambiar el plan de AWS Support son tareas que solo puede realizar el usuario ra\xedz, ya que son acciones a nivel de cuenta que AWS reserva exclusivamente al propietario de la cuenta para evitar cambios no autorizados en la configuraci\xf3n fundamental de la cuenta.\\nCrear una nueva pol\xedtica de IAM puede realizarla cualquier usuario o rol de IAM al que se le hayan otorgado los permisos de IAM adecuados, y no requiere credenciales de usuario ra\xedz. Adjuntar un rol a una instancia de Amazon EC2 puede realizarlo cualquier usuario o rol de IAM con los permisos necesarios de EC2 e IAM, y es una tarea operativa rutinaria que no requiere acceso ra\xedz. Generar claves de acceso para usuarios de IAM pueden realizarlo administradores de IAM con los permisos adecuados, y no est\xe1 restringido a la cuenta de usuario ra\xedz."},{"id":"q917","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 lanzando un sitio de comercio electr\xf3nico que almacenar\xe1 y procesar\xe1 datos de tarjetas de cr\xe9dito. La empresa requiere informaci\xf3n sobre los informes de cumplimiento de AWS y los acuerdos de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona acceso bajo demanda a estos elementos?","options":{"A":"AWS Certificate Manager","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Artifact","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes de cumplimiento de AWS, como informes PCI DSS y SOC, y a acuerdos de AWS, como el Addendum de Asociado Comercial (Business Associate Addendum), lo que lo convierte en el servicio dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para empresas que requieren documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento al manejar datos sensibles como la informaci\xf3n de tarjetas de cr\xe9dito.\\nAWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y administra certificados SSL/TLS para cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito entre aplicaciones y usuarios, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de certificados sin capacidad para proporcionar informes de cumplimiento ni acuerdos de AWS. AWS Config rastrea y registra continuamente el estado de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento respecto de la configuraci\xf3n deseada, y es una herramienta de auditor\xeda de configuraci\xf3n de recursos en lugar de un repositorio de documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento y acuerdos de AWS. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta en un entorno de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y captura registros de auditor\xeda operativos en lugar de proporcionar acceso a informes de cumplimiento de AWS o acuerdos legales."},{"id":"q919","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede usar para rastrear llamadas a la API no autorizadas?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y la actividad de la cuenta, incluidas las llamadas a la API no autorizadas o inusuales, lo que lo convierte en el servicio correcto para rastrear llamadas a la API que puedan indicar acceso o actividad no autorizados en un entorno de AWS.\\nAmazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas operativas, registros y eventos de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS y, aunque CloudWatch puede recibir eventos de CloudTrail y activar alarmas, no rastrea ni registra por s\xed mismo el historial de llamadas a la API para identificar acciones de API no autorizadas. Amazon Detective analiza y visualiza datos de seguridad de m\xfaltiples fuentes para investigar la causa ra\xedz de actividad sospechosa, y usa datos de CloudTrail como una de sus entradas, pero es una herramienta de investigaci\xf3n que se basa en CloudTrail en lugar de realizar el registro original de llamadas a la API. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas en las categor\xedas de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento en lugar de un servicio que rastrea o registra actividad de API."},{"id":"q920","question":"Un usuario necesita auditar y evaluar regularmente la configuraci\xf3n de todos los recursos de AWS, identificar cuentas que no cumplen y recibir notificaciones cuando un recurso cambie. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede usar para cumplir estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Resource Access Manager","D":"AWS Systems Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Config monitorea continuamente las configuraciones de los recursos, las eval\xfaa frente a reglas de cumplimiento, rastrea los cambios a lo largo del tiempo y env\xeda notificaciones cuando los recursos cambian o dejan de cumplir, lo que lo convierte en el servicio dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para requisitos de auditor\xeda continua, evaluaci\xf3n de cumplimiento y notificaci\xf3n de cambios.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas en seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, pero entrega comprobaciones de asesoramiento peri\xf3dicas en lugar de rastrear continuamente cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos individuales y enviar notificaciones cuando recursos espec\xedficos dejan de cumplir. AWS Resource Access Manager permite compartir recursos de AWS, como subredes y Transit Gateways, entre cuentas de AWS dentro de una organizaci\xf3n, y es un servicio de uso compartido de recursos sin capacidad para auditar configuraciones ni notificar sobre infracciones de cumplimiento. AWS Systems Manager proporciona herramientas operativas para administrar y automatizar tareas en recursos de AWS, incluidas la aplicaci\xf3n de parches, el almacenamiento de par\xe1metros y comandos de ejecuci\xf3n (run commands), y aunque puede automatizar la remediaci\xf3n, no eval\xfaa continuamente las configuraciones de los recursos frente a reglas de cumplimiento ni env\xeda notificaciones cuando los recursos cambian."},{"id":"q924","question":"Un usuario necesita descubrir, clasificar y proteger autom\xe1ticamente datos sensibles almacenados en Amazon S3. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede cumplir estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"Amazon Macie","C":"Amazon GuardDuty","D":"AWS Secrets Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Macie usa machine learning para descubrir, clasificar y proteger autom\xe1ticamente datos sensibles, incluida la PII, almacenados en Amazon S3, lo que proporciona visibilidad sobre los riesgos de seguridad de los datos y detecta posibles fugas de datos.\\nAmazon Inspector es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad automatizada que analiza instancias de EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y no descubre ni clasifica datos sensibles almacenados en Amazon S3. Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que monitorea cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS en busca de actividad maliciosa y comportamiento an\xf3malo mediante machine learning e inteligencia de amenazas, y es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas en lugar de un servicio de clasificaci\xf3n y protecci\xf3n de datos sensibles en S3. AWS Secrets Manager almacena, rota y administra el acceso a secretos, como credenciales de bases de datos y claves de API, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n del ciclo de vida de secretos en lugar de un servicio para descubrir y clasificar autom\xe1ticamente datos sensibles almacenados en S3."},{"id":"q928","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el recurso M\xc1S eficaz para mantenerse actualizado sobre los anuncios de seguridad de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Health Dashboard","B":"AWS Secrets Manager","C":"AWS Security Bulletins","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los AWS Security Bulletins proporcionan notificaciones oportunas sobre vulnerabilidades de seguridad reci\xe9n descubiertas y acciones de remediaci\xf3n recomendadas para los servicios de AWS, lo que los convierte en el recurso m\xe1s eficaz para mantenerse actualizado sobre los anuncios de seguridad de AWS.\\nEl AWS Health Dashboard proporciona alertas personalizadas sobre eventos que pueden afectar los recursos espec\xedficos de AWS de un cliente y la disponibilidad del servicio, y aunque incluye eventos de estado para problemas de seguridad, la fuente dedicada para anuncios proactivos de seguridad de AWS y divulgaciones de vulnerabilidades es la p\xe1gina de AWS Security Bulletins. AWS Secrets Manager almacena, rota y gestiona el acceso a secretos como credenciales de bases de datos y claves de API, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de credenciales sin conexi\xf3n con anuncios de seguridad o notificaciones de vulnerabilidades. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software, y genera hallazgos sobre los propios recursos del cliente en lugar de proporcionar anuncios de seguridad a nivel de AWS."},{"id":"q931","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a los usuarios descargar informes de seguridad y cumplimiento sobre la infraestructura de AWS bajo demanda?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"AWS Security Hub","C":"AWS Artifact","D":"AWS Shield"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes de seguridad y cumplimiento de AWS, incluida documentaci\xf3n de SOC, PCI DSS, ISO y HIPAA, disponible para descargar directamente desde la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS sin contactar con AWS.\\nAmazon CloudWatch supervisa m\xe9tricas operativas y registros de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS, y es un servicio de observabilidad y alertas en lugar de un repositorio de informes de seguridad y cumplimiento de AWS. AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n en una cuenta de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda, y produce registros de actividad espec\xedficos del cliente en lugar de documentaci\xf3n de certificaci\xf3n y cumplimiento de seguridad de AWS. Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que supervisa continuamente cuentas y cargas de trabajo en busca de actividad maliciosa, y es un servicio de supervisi\xf3n de seguridad en tiempo real en lugar de una fuente de informes de cumplimiento descargables."},{"id":"q936","question":"Durante una revisi\xf3n de cumplimiento, un auditor solicita documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento de AWS, como informes SOC 2. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona acceso bajo demanda a estos documentos?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Config","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes y certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS, como documentos SOC 2, lo que permite a auditores y clientes descargarlos directamente desde la consola sin solicitarlos a trav\xe9s de AWS Support.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas en las categor\xedas de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos para mejorar un entorno de AWS, y es una herramienta de asesor\xeda en lugar de un repositorio de documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento. AWS Config supervisa y registra continuamente las configuraciones de recursos frente a reglas de cumplimiento, y es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n del cumplimiento de la configuraci\xf3n de recursos en lugar de un portal para descargar certificaciones de cumplimiento a nivel de AWS. Amazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos para almacenar y recuperar datos, y aunque AWS Artifact descarga documentos almacenados en S3 en el backend, S3 en s\xed no es el portal que los clientes usan para encontrar y descargar documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento."},{"id":"q941","question":"Una empresa quiere permitir acceso completo a un bucket de Amazon S3 para un usuario en particular. \xbfQu\xe9 elemento de la pol\xedtica del bucket de S3 contiene los detalles del usuario que describen qui\xe9n necesita acceso al bucket de S3?","options":{"A":"Principal","B":"Action","C":"Resource","D":"Statement"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El elemento Principal de una pol\xedtica de bucket de S3 especifica a qui\xe9n se le permite o deniega el acceso, y contiene los detalles del usuario, cuenta, rol o servicio que describen la identidad a la que se conceden o deniegan permisos.\\nAction especifica qu\xe9 operaciones de S3 est\xe1n permitidas o denegadas, como s3:GetObject o s3:PutObject, y define qu\xe9 puede hacer el principal en lugar de identificar qui\xe9n es el principal. Resource especifica a qu\xe9 bucket u objetos de S3 se aplica la pol\xedtica, identificando el destino de la pol\xedtica en lugar de la identidad a la que se concede acceso. Statement es el elemento contenedor de nivel superior que engloba los bloques individuales de la pol\xedtica, incluidos Principal, Action, Effect y Resource, y es el contenedor estructural en lugar del elemento que contiene los detalles del usuario."},{"id":"q945","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1l de las siguientes es una responsabilidad de AWS?","options":{"A":"Habilitar el cifrado del lado del servidor para objetos almacenados en S3","B":"Aplicar pol\xedticas de seguridad de AWS IAM","C":"Aplicar parches al sistema operativo en una instancia de Amazon EC2","D":"Aplicar actualizaciones al hipervisor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS es responsable de la seguridad de la infraestructura subyacente, incluida la seguridad f\xedsica del centro de datos, el hardware y el software que ejecuta el hipervisor y los componentes de la red global, ya que est\xe1n fuera del control del cliente.\\nGestionar los derechos de acceso de las aplicaciones para los usuarios que acceden a aplicaciones creadas por el cliente es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes controlan qui\xe9n puede acceder a sus aplicaciones mediante mecanismos de autenticaci\xf3n y autorizaci\xf3n a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n. Mantener actualizado el sistema operativo en las instancias de Amazon EC2 aplicando parches de seguridad es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que el sistema operativo invitado que se ejecuta dentro de las m\xe1quinas virtuales de EC2 debe ser mantenido por el cliente que tiene acceso administrativo a \xe9l. Configurar el cifrado de datos del lado del cliente para proteger los datos antes de que se env\xeden a los servicios de AWS es responsabilidad del cliente para los datos que requieren protecci\xf3n de extremo a extremo, ya que el cliente controla el proceso de cifrado en sus propios sistemas antes de que los datos salgan de su entorno."},{"id":"q954","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para rastrear cambios en los recursos y establecer el cumplimiento?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Config supervisa y registra continuamente los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS y eval\xfaa esos cambios frente a reglas de cumplimiento, identificando recursos no conformes y permitiendo auditar el historial de configuraci\xf3n con fines de cumplimiento.\\nAmazon CloudWatch supervisa m\xe9tricas operativas, registros y eventos para rendimiento y observabilidad, y aunque puede activar alarmas ante actividad inusual, no rastrea cambios en la configuraci\xf3n de recursos ni eval\xfaa el cumplimiento frente a estados de configuraci\xf3n deseados. AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n para auditor\xeda, y aunque registra qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 cambios de configuraci\xf3n y cu\xe1ndo, no eval\xfaa si esas configuraciones cumplen con reglas definidas como lo hace Config. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades para instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores que identifica vulnerabilidades de software y acceso de red no intencionado, y no es un servicio de seguimiento de la configuraci\xf3n de recursos y evaluaci\xf3n de cumplimiento."},{"id":"q959","question":"Un desarrollador ha sido contratado por una empresa grande y necesita credenciales de AWS. \xbfCu\xe1les son las mejores pr\xe1cticas de seguridad que se deben seguir?","options":{"A":"Conceder al desarrollador acceso solo a los recursos de AWS necesarios para realizar el trabajo","B":"Compartir las credenciales del usuario ra\xedz de la cuenta de AWS con el desarrollador","C":"Agregar al desarrollador al grupo de administradores en AWS IAM","D":"Configurar una pol\xedtica de contrase\xf1as que asegure que la contrase\xf1a del desarrollador no se pueda cambiar","E":"Asegurar que la pol\xedtica de contrase\xf1as de la cuenta requiera una longitud m\xednima"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Conceder al desarrollador acceso solo a los recursos necesarios sigue el principio de privilegio m\xednimo, y asegurar una longitud m\xednima de contrase\xf1a aplica requisitos de seguridad de credenciales. Ambas son mejores pr\xe1cticas de seguridad de IAM para la incorporaci\xf3n de nuevos desarrolladores.\\nCompartir las credenciales del usuario ra\xedz de la cuenta de AWS con cualquier persona es una violaci\xf3n cr\xedtica de seguridad, ya que el usuario ra\xedz tiene acceso sin restricciones a todos los servicios y recursos de AWS y debe protegerse y usarse solo para tareas que lo requieran espec\xedficamente. Agregar al desarrollador al grupo de administradores otorga acceso sin restricciones a todos los servicios y recursos de AWS, lo que viola directamente el principio de privilegio m\xednimo y no es una mejor pr\xe1ctica de seguridad para un nuevo desarrollador cuyos requisitos de acceso son espec\xedficos para su rol. Configurar una pol\xedtica de contrase\xf1as que impida al desarrollador cambiar su propia contrase\xf1a es un antipatr\xf3n de seguridad, ya que la capacidad de cambiar contrase\xf1as es necesaria para la higiene de credenciales y la rotaci\xf3n peri\xf3dica."},{"id":"q962","question":"Un usuario planea migrar una carga de trabajo de aplicaciones a la nube de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 control pasa a ser responsabilidad de AWS una vez completada la migraci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Aplicar parches al sistema operativo invitado","B":"Mantener controles f\xedsicos y ambientales","C":"Proteger las comunicaciones y mantener la seguridad de las zonas","D":"Aplicar parches a aplicaciones espec\xedficas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mantener controles f\xedsicos y ambientales, incluidos la energ\xeda, la refrigeraci\xf3n, la supresi\xf3n de incendios y la seguridad f\xedsica del centro de datos, pasa a ser responsabilidad de AWS una vez que una carga de trabajo se migra a la nube.\\nAplicar parches al sistema operativo invitado sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente despu\xe9s de la migraci\xf3n a AWS. Para servicios como EC2, los clientes conservan el control total sobre el sistema operativo y son responsables de aplicar actualizaciones y parches. Proteger las comunicaciones y mantener la seguridad de las zonas es una responsabilidad compartida de la que los clientes conservan la titularidad, incluida la configuraci\xf3n de controles de seguridad de red como grupos de seguridad, ACL de red y cifrado en tr\xe1nsito para sus aplicaciones. Aplicar parches a aplicaciones espec\xedficas es siempre responsabilidad del cliente independientemente del modelo de despliegue, ya que AWS no tiene visibilidad sobre el software de aplicaci\xf3n que los clientes eligen ejecutar en su infraestructura."},{"id":"q969","question":"Una empresa quiere asegurarse de que los usuarios de AWS Management Console cumplan con los requisitos de complejidad de contrase\xf1as. \xbfC\xf3mo puede la empresa configurar la complejidad de contrase\xf1as?","options":{"A":"Usando una pol\xedtica de usuario de AWS IAM","B":"Usando una pol\xedtica de control de servicios (SCP) de AWS Organizations","C":"Usando una pol\xedtica de contrase\xf1as de cuenta de AWS IAM","D":"Usando un insight administrado de AWS Security Hub"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las pol\xedticas de contrase\xf1as de IAM permiten a los administradores aplicar requisitos de complejidad de contrase\xf1as, como longitud m\xednima, requisitos de tipo de caracteres e intervalos de expiraci\xf3n, lo que garantiza que todos los usuarios de IAM deban crear contrase\xf1as que cumplan con los est\xe1ndares definidos.\\nAWS Secrets Manager almacena, rota y administra el acceso a secretos, como credenciales de bases de datos y claves de API, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n del ciclo de vida de secretos, no una herramienta para establecer requisitos de complejidad de contrase\xf1as para los usuarios de AWS Management Console. Amazon Inspector es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n automatizada de vulnerabilidades que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y no tiene capacidad para aplicar requisitos de contrase\xf1as para los usuarios de AWS Management Console. AWS Directory Service proporciona Microsoft Active Directory administrado para la gesti\xf3n de identidades empresariales y la uni\xf3n a dominios y, aunque puede aplicar pol\xedticas de contrase\xf1as para usuarios de Active Directory, el servicio correcto para aplicar requisitos de contrase\xf1as para los usuarios de IAM que acceden a AWS Management Console es la pol\xedtica de contrase\xf1as de IAM."},{"id":"q970","question":"Seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1l de las siguientes es responsabilidad del cliente?","options":{"A":"Aplicar parches al sistema operativo invitado y a las aplicaciones","B":"Aplicar parches y corregir fallos en la infraestructura","C":"Controles f\xedsicos y ambientales","D":"Configuraci\xf3n de dispositivos de infraestructura de AWS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Administrar el cifrado de datos de aplicaciones y configurar la seguridad de sus propias aplicaciones son responsabilidades del cliente seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, ya que los clientes controlan sus propios datos y la capa de aplicaci\xf3n, mientras que AWS administra la infraestructura subyacente.\\nProteger la infraestructura que ejecuta todos los servicios de AWS Cloud, incluidos servidores f\xedsicos, hardware de red e instalaciones de centros de datos, es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS como parte de la seguridad de la nube. Proporcionar la seguridad f\xedsica de los centros de datos globales, incluidos controles de acceso perimetral, vigilancia y protecciones ambientales, es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS, ya que los clientes nunca tienen acceso a las instalaciones f\xedsicas. Mantener el hardware que proporciona la base de la infraestructura global, incluidos servidores, dispositivos de almacenamiento y equipos de red, es responsabilidad exclusiva de AWS como parte de ser propietario y operar la infraestructura f\xedsica de la nube."},{"id":"q971","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes tareas se requiere para implementar una carga de trabajo compatible con PCI en AWS?","options":{"A":"Usar cualquier servicio de AWS e implementar controles PCI en la capa de aplicaci\xf3n","B":"Usar un servicio de AWS que est\xe9 dentro del alcance del cumplimiento PCI y abrir un ticket de soporte de AWS para habilitar el cumplimiento PCI en la capa de aplicaci\xf3n","C":"Usar cualquier servicio de AWS y abrir un ticket de soporte de AWS para habilitar el cumplimiento PCI en ese servicio","D":"Usar un servicio de AWS que est\xe9 dentro del alcance del cumplimiento PCI y aplicar controles PCI en la capa de aplicaci\xf3n"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Implementar aplicaciones en los servicios correctos de conformidad con los requisitos de PCI DSS es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes deben elegir servicios de AWS elegibles para PCI y configurarlos correctamente para cumplir con los controles de PCI DSS aplicables en el lado del cliente del modelo de responsabilidad compartida.\\nAWS no garantiza que todas las instalaciones f\xedsicas y centros de datos cumplan con los requisitos PCI en nombre del cliente; m\xe1s bien, AWS obtiene y mantiene la certificaci\xf3n PCI DSS para su infraestructura, y esta certificaci\xf3n est\xe1 disponible para los clientes a trav\xe9s de AWS Artifact como evidencia de que la plataforma subyacente cumple con PCI. AWS no aplica parches a todos los sistemas de AWS utilizados en el entorno en nombre del cliente; para servicios administrados, AWS se encarga de la aplicaci\xf3n de parches, pero para servicios como EC2, el cliente es responsable de aplicar parches al sistema operativo invitado y a cualquier software instalado. Configurar listas de control de acceso de red (ACL de red) en AWS en nombre del cliente no es responsabilidad de AWS; los clientes configuran sus propias ACL de red de VPC para controlar el tr\xe1fico como parte de su arquitectura de seguridad de red compatible con PCI."},{"id":"q977","question":"Un desarrollador web est\xe1 preocupado de que un ataque DDoS pueda tener como objetivo una aplicaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 servicios o caracter\xedsticas de AWS pueden ayudar a proteger contra dicho ataque?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"Amazon CloudFront","D":"AWS Support Center","E":"AWS Health Dashboard"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS Shield proporciona mitigaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de DDoS en las capas de red y transporte, y Amazon CloudFront absorbe y distribuye el tr\xe1fico en ubicaciones de borde, lo que reduce el impacto de los ataques volum\xe9tricos antes de que lleguen al origen de la aplicaci\xf3n. Ambos ayudan a proteger contra ataques DDoS.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n con fines de auditor\xeda y, aunque registra la actividad de la cuenta, no tiene capacidad para detectar o mitigar ataques DDoS activos contra una aplicaci\xf3n web. AWS Support Center es un portal para enviar y administrar casos de soporte, y no es un servicio t\xe9cnico que proporcione protecci\xf3n o mitigaci\xf3n contra ataques DDoS. AWS Health Dashboard proporciona informaci\xf3n de estado en tiempo real e hist\xf3rica sobre los servicios de AWS y alertas de eventos personalizadas, y es una herramienta de monitoreo y notificaci\xf3n, no un servicio que proteja activamente las aplicaciones contra ataques DDoS."},{"id":"q978","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS brinda a los usuarios acceso bajo demanda y de autoservicio a los informes de controles de cumplimiento de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon GuardDuty","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS Artifact"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda y de autoservicio a los informes de controles de cumplimiento de AWS, incluida la documentaci\xf3n de SOC, PCI, ISO y HIPAA, directamente desde AWS Management Console sin requerir una solicitud a trav\xe9s de AWS Support.\\nAWS Config rastrea el estado de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos y audita c\xf3mo cambian esas configuraciones con el tiempo, y es un servicio de auditor\xeda de configuraci\xf3n de recursos, no un portal para descargar informes de controles de cumplimiento. Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para monitorear cuentas y cargas de trabajo en busca de actividad maliciosa, y est\xe1 enfocado en seguridad, no es un portal de documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento. AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona una cuenta y proporciona recomendaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas en optimizaci\xf3n de costos, rendimiento, seguridad, tolerancia a fallas y l\xedmites de servicio, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento, no una fuente de informes de controles de cumplimiento."},{"id":"q979","question":"Una empresa quiere proporcionar a uno de sus empleados acceso a Amazon RDS. La empresa tambi\xe9n quiere limitar la interacci\xf3n \xfanicamente a AWS CLI y a los kits de desarrollo de software (SDK) de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 combinaci\xf3n de acciones debe tomar la empresa para cumplir estos requisitos mientras sigue los principios de privilegio m\xednimo?","options":{"A":"Crear un usuario de IAM y proporcionar acceso \xfanicamente a AWS Management Console","B":"Crear un usuario de IAM y proporcionar acceso program\xe1tico \xfanicamente","C":"Crear un rol de IAM y proporcionar acceso \xfanicamente a AWS Management Console","D":"Crear una pol\xedtica de IAM con acceso de administrador y adjuntarla al usuario de IAM","E":"Crear una pol\xedtica de IAM con acceso a Amazon RDS y adjuntarla al usuario de IAM"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Una pol\xedtica de IAM que otorga acceso a Amazon RDS se puede adjuntar directamente al usuario de IAM espec\xedfico, lo que limita los permisos de AWS de ese empleado \xfanicamente a las acciones de RDS especificadas en la pol\xedtica y a ning\xfan otro servicio.\\nCrear una cuenta de AWS separada para el usuario ser\xeda una medida excesiva para otorgar acceso a RDS a un solo empleado, ya que introduce sobrecarga administrativa para la administraci\xf3n de cuentas y no simplifica el control de acceso en comparaci\xf3n con el uso de una pol\xedtica de IAM dentro de la cuenta existente. Crear un usuario de IAM con acceso de administrador completo otorgar\xeda acceso sin restricciones a todos los servicios y recursos de AWS, lo que violar\xeda directamente el principio de privilegio m\xednimo y crear\xeda un riesgo de seguridad innecesario para un usuario que solo necesita acceso a RDS. Colocar a todos los usuarios de IAM existentes en un nuevo grupo de IAM con acceso a RDS otorgar\xeda permisos de RDS a toda la poblaci\xf3n de usuarios en lugar de limitar el acceso al \xfanico empleado que lo requiere, lo que contradice el objetivo de proporcionar acceso espec\xedfico."},{"id":"q980","question":"Una empresa tiene un requisito de cumplimiento de registrar y evaluar cambios de configuraci\xf3n, as\xed como realizar acciones de remediaci\xf3n en recursos de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS debe usar la empresa?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS Secrets Manager","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Config registra los cambios de configuraci\xf3n, los eval\xfaa con respecto a reglas de cumplimiento y puede activar acciones de remediaci\xf3n automatizadas cuando los recursos dejan de cumplir, lo que cumple los tres requisitos de registrar, evaluar y remediar.\\nAWS Secrets Manager almacena, rota y administra el acceso a secretos, como credenciales de bases de datos y claves de API, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de credenciales sin capacidad para registrar cambios de configuraci\xf3n ni activar la remediaci\xf3n de cumplimiento. AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y la actividad de la cuenta con fines de auditor\xeda y, aunque registra qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 cambios y cu\xe1ndo, no eval\xfaa los recursos con respecto a reglas de cumplimiento ni activa acciones de remediaci\xf3n. AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones en materia de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallas, y ofrece orientaci\xf3n de asesoramiento en lugar de evaluaci\xf3n automatizada de reglas de cumplimiento o capacidad de remediaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q984","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es responsabilidad del cliente cuando se usa Amazon RDS?","options":{"A":"Aplicar parches al sistema operativo del hardware subyacente","B":"Controlar el tr\xe1fico hacia y desde la base de datos mediante grupos de seguridad","C":"Ejecutar copias de seguridad que permitan la recuperaci\xf3n a un punto en el tiempo de una instancia de base de datos","D":"Reemplazar instancias de base de datos fallidas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aplicar parches a la base de datos y al sistema operativo en bases de datos alojadas en EC2 es responsabilidad del cliente cuando se usa Amazon RDS, que es un servicio administrado en el que AWS se encarga de la aplicaci\xf3n de parches; sin embargo, la pregunta se refiere espec\xedficamente a RDS. Seg\xfan el modelo de RDS, configurar grupos de seguridad para controlar el acceso de red a la instancia de RDS es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes definen qu\xe9 instancias de EC2 y direcciones IP pueden conectarse a su base de datos.\\nInstalar el software de base de datos en el servidor subyacente no es responsabilidad del cliente para Amazon RDS; AWS administra la instalaci\xf3n, configuraci\xf3n y actualizaciones del motor de base de datos como parte de la oferta de servicio completamente administrado. Aplicar parches al sistema operativo subyacente de la instancia de RDS es responsabilidad de AWS; RDS es un servicio administrado en el que AWS realiza la aplicaci\xf3n de parches del sistema operativo y del motor de base de datos autom\xe1ticamente sin intervenci\xf3n del cliente. Reemplazar el hardware de base de datos fallido es responsabilidad de AWS como parte de la administraci\xf3n de la infraestructura f\xedsica; los clientes se benefician de los procesos de redundancia y reemplazo de hardware de AWS de forma transparente sin necesidad de realizar ninguna acci\xf3n de su parte."},{"id":"q985","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la responsabilidad del cliente cuando utiliza AWS Lambda?","options":{"A":"Configuraci\xf3n del sistema operativo","B":"Gesti\xf3n de la aplicaci\xf3n","C":"Gesti\xf3n de la plataforma","D":"Cifrado del c\xf3digo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor en el que AWS gestiona la infraestructura subyacente y la responsabilidad del cliente se limita a gestionar el c\xf3digo de su aplicaci\xf3n, incluida la escritura, el despliegue y el mantenimiento de las funciones que se ejecutan en la plataforma.\\nLa configuraci\xf3n del sistema operativo es responsabilidad de AWS para Lambda, ya que el servicio abstrae por completo la infraestructura de c\xf3mputo subyacente y los clientes no tienen acceso ni control sobre el sistema operativo. La gesti\xf3n de la plataforma tambi\xe9n es responsabilidad de AWS para Lambda, ya que AWS se encarga del entorno de ejecuci\xf3n, el escalado, la disponibilidad y el mantenimiento de la plataforma sin servidor en nombre del cliente. El cifrado del c\xf3digo no es una responsabilidad del cliente en el sentido descrito, ya que AWS cifra el c\xf3digo de las funciones de Lambda en reposo de forma predeterminada y, aunque los clientes pueden usar opcionalmente sus propias claves KMS, AWS gestiona el cifrado autom\xe1ticamente en lugar de ser una tarea administrada por el cliente."},{"id":"q992","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS debe utilizar un cloud practitioner para identificar vulnerabilidades de seguridad de una cuenta de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"Amazon Cognito","C":"Amazon Macie","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor identifica posibles vulnerabilidades de seguridad al comprobar configuraciones incorrectas comunes, como acceso sin restricciones en grupos de seguridad, falta de MFA en la cuenta ra\xedz y buckets de S3 accesibles p\xfablicamente, lo que ayuda a los clientes a abordar debilidades de seguridad de forma proactiva.\\nAmazon Detective es un servicio de investigaci\xf3n que analiza datos de seguridad de GuardDuty, CloudTrail y VPC Flow Logs para ayudar a los equipos de seguridad a investigar la causa ra\xedz de actividad sospechosa, y es una herramienta de investigaci\xf3n forense en lugar de un servicio para identificar vulnerabilidades de seguridad en la configuraci\xf3n de una cuenta de AWS. Amazon Macie utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para descubrir y clasificar datos confidenciales en Amazon S3, y es un servicio de clasificaci\xf3n de datos para detectar posible exposici\xf3n de datos en lugar de una herramienta para auditar configuraciones de seguridad y vulnerabilidades en toda la cuenta. AWS Shield proporciona protecci\xf3n administrada contra DDoS en las capas de red y transporte, y es un servicio de protecci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico en lugar de una herramienta de identificaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades de seguridad."},{"id":"q997","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para recuperar informes de cumplimiento bajo demanda?","options":{"A":"AWS Secrets Manager","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Security Hub","D":"AWS Certificate Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes de cumplimiento de AWS, incluidos SOC, PCI, ISO y otras certificaciones, disponibles para descargar directamente desde la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS.\\nAWS Secrets Manager almacena, rota y gestiona el acceso a secretos como credenciales de bases de datos y claves de API, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de credenciales sin capacidad para recuperar o proporcionar documentaci\xf3n de cumplimiento. AWS Security Hub proporciona una vista centralizada de alertas de seguridad y estado de cumplimiento en un entorno de AWS al agregar hallazgos de m\xfaltiples servicios de seguridad, y es una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de la postura de seguridad en lugar de un repositorio de informes de cumplimiento de AWS. AWS Certificate Manager aprovisiona y gestiona certificados SSL/TLS para cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito entre aplicaciones y usuarios, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de certificados sin conexi\xf3n con la recuperaci\xf3n de informes de cumplimiento."},{"id":"q998","question":"Una empresa tiene un sitio web alojado en AWS detr\xe1s de un Application Load Balancer. La empresa desea proteger el sitio web de inyecci\xf3n SQL o cross-site scripting. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS debe utilizar la empresa?","options":{"A":"Amazon GuardDuty","B":"AWS WAF","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS WAF protege las aplicaciones web detr\xe1s de Application Load Balancers de exploits comunes como inyecci\xf3n SQL y cross-site scripting al filtrar solicitudes HTTP maliciosas seg\xfan reglas personalizadas.\\nAmazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que monitorea continuamente cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS en busca de actividad maliciosa y comportamiento an\xf3malo, y es un servicio de detecci\xf3n y alerta en lugar de una herramienta para filtrar solicitudes web maliciosas en la capa de aplicaci\xf3n. AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones en seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento sin capacidad para interceptar o bloquear tr\xe1fico web malicioso. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y es una herramienta de escaneo de vulnerabilidades en lugar de un servicio de filtrado de tr\xe1fico web."},{"id":"q1001","question":"Un auditor externo ha solicitado que una empresa proporcione una lista de todos sus usuarios de IAM, incluido el estado de las credenciales y claves de acceso de los usuarios. \xbfCu\xe1l es la forma M\xc1S SENCILLA de proporcionar esta informaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Crear una cuenta de usuario de IAM para el auditor, otorgando al auditor permisos de administrador","B":"Tomar una captura de pantalla de la p\xe1gina de cada usuario en la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS, luego proporcionar las capturas de pantalla al auditor","C":"Descargar el informe de credenciales de IAM, luego proporcionar el informe al auditor","D":"Descargar el informe de AWS Trusted Advisor, luego proporcionar el informe al auditor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El informe de credenciales de IAM es un archivo CSV descargable que enumera todos los usuarios de IAM de la cuenta junto con el estado de sus credenciales, incluidas contrase\xf1as, claves de acceso, dispositivos MFA y marcas de tiempo de \xfaltimo uso, lo que proporciona exactamente lo que se necesita para una auditor\xeda de acceso de usuarios.\\nAWS Config monitorea y registra continuamente los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos y los eval\xfaa seg\xfan reglas de cumplimiento, y es una herramienta de cumplimiento de configuraci\xf3n de recursos en lugar de un servicio de listado de usuarios de IAM. Amazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas operativas y logs de recursos y aplicaciones de AWS, y es un servicio de observabilidad en lugar de una fuente de informaci\xf3n de cuentas de usuarios de IAM o informes de estado de credenciales. AWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n, incluidas acciones relacionadas con IAM, y aunque registra qui\xe9n cre\xf3 usuarios y cu\xe1ndo, no proporciona una lista consolidada actual de todos los usuarios y su estado de credenciales de la forma en que lo hace el informe de credenciales."},{"id":"q1004","question":"Una empresa desea realizar un seguimiento de los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos de AWS por motivos de cumplimiento. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de AWS se puede utilizar para cumplir este requisito?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","B":"Pol\xedticas de control de servicios (SCPs) de AWS Organizations","C":"Reglas de AWS Config","D":"VPC Flow Logs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Config monitorea y registra continuamente los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos y los eval\xfaa seg\xfan reglas de cumplimiento, generando un historial completo de cambios de configuraci\xf3n que auditores y equipos de cumplimiento pueden utilizar para demostrar el cumplimiento de est\xe1ndares.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a la API y la actividad de la cuenta con fines de auditor\xeda y forenses, y aunque captura qui\xe9n realiz\xf3 cambios de configuraci\xf3n y cu\xe1ndo, no eval\xfaa si esas configuraciones cumplen con reglas definidas ni proporciona el historial de estado de configuraci\xf3n que ofrece Config. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades para instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores que identifica vulnerabilidades de software y configuraciones incorrectas de red, y es una herramienta de escaneo de seguridad en lugar de una caracter\xedstica de seguimiento de cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos y evaluaci\xf3n de cumplimiento. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas en las categor\xedas de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento en lugar de un servicio de seguimiento de cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos y evaluaci\xf3n de cumplimiento."},{"id":"q1006","question":"La pr\xe1ctica recomendada de AWS IAM para otorgar el m\xednimo privilegio es:","options":{"A":"Aplicar una pol\xedtica de IAM a un grupo de IAM y limitar el tama\xf1o del grupo","B":"Requerir autenticaci\xf3n multifactor (MFA) para todos los usuarios de IAM","C":"Requerir que cada usuario de IAM que tenga permisos diferentes tenga m\xfaltiples contrase\xf1as","D":"Aplicar una pol\xedtica de IAM solo a los usuarios de IAM que la requieran"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El principio de m\xednimo privilegio requiere otorgar solo los permisos necesarios para que un usuario realice su funci\xf3n laboral espec\xedfica, lo que significa aplicar pol\xedticas de IAM solo a los usuarios que realmente requieren esos permisos en lugar de asignar permisos de forma amplia a todos los usuarios.\\nAplicar una pol\xedtica de IAM a un grupo de IAM y limitar el tama\xf1o del grupo aborda la gesti\xf3n de grupos en lugar del m\xednimo privilegio, ya que el tama\xf1o de un grupo no influye en si los permisos est\xe1n adecuadamente delimitados a lo que los usuarios realmente necesitan. Requerir MFA para todos los usuarios de IAM es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de seguridad de autenticaci\xf3n que protege contra credenciales comprometidas, pero no se relaciona con delimitar los permisos al nivel m\xednimo requerido. Requerir que los usuarios de IAM con permisos diferentes tengan m\xfaltiples contrase\xf1as confunde autenticaci\xf3n con autorizaci\xf3n, y tener m\xfaltiples contrase\xf1as no controla ni limita las acciones que un usuario tiene permitido realizar."},{"id":"q1010","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS se puede utilizar para prevenir ataques de inyecci\xf3n SQL?","options":{"A":"Grupos de seguridad","B":"ACL de red","C":"AWS WAF","D":"Pol\xedtica de IAM"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS WAF es un firewall de aplicaciones web que inspecciona el tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS y se puede configurar con reglas para detectar y bloquear intentos de inyecci\xf3n SQL antes de que lleguen a la aplicaci\xf3n.\\nAmazon VPC proporciona aislamiento de red y controles de seguridad a nivel de infraestructura mediante grupos de seguridad y ACL de red, pero no inspecciona el contenido del tr\xe1fico HTTP de la capa de aplicaci\xf3n en busca de patrones de inyecci\xf3n SQL como lo hace WAF. AWS Shield proporciona protecci\xf3n administrada contra DDoS en las capas de red y transporte para defenderse de ataques volum\xe9tricos, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para inspeccionar o bloquear exploits de la capa de aplicaci\xf3n, como la inyecci\xf3n SQL. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n con fines de auditor\xeda, y aunque registra la actividad en el entorno de AWS, no inspecciona el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones web ni bloquea ataques de inyecci\xf3n SQL."},{"id":"q1104","question":"Una empresa necesita evaluar continuamente su entorno de AWS frente a marcos de cumplimiento como PCI DSS y GDPR y recopilar autom\xe1ticamente evidencia para auditor\xedas. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xedan usar?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Audit Manager","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Audit Manager recopila continuamente evidencia de servicios de AWS, la asigna a controles de marcos de cumplimiento como PCI DSS y GDPR, y la organiza en informes listos para auditor\xeda, automatizando gran parte del esfuerzo manual que implican las evaluaciones de cumplimiento.\\nAWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes y certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS, como documentos SOC e ISO, pero no eval\xfaa el entorno propio del cliente ni recopila evidencia para auditor\xedas de cumplimiento espec\xedficas del cliente. AWS Config registra y eval\xfaa configuraciones de recursos frente a reglas de cumplimiento y, aunque aporta evidencia que Audit Manager puede consumir, no organiza por s\xed mismo los hallazgos en informes de auditor\xeda estructurados y asignados a marcos de cumplimiento. Amazon Inspector escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y es una herramienta de escaneo de vulnerabilidades, no un servicio de recopilaci\xf3n de evidencia para auditor\xedas de cumplimiento."},{"id":"q1105","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ayuda a simplificar la preparaci\xf3n de auditor\xedas al recopilar y organizar autom\xe1ticamente evidencia del uso y la actividad de AWS en informes de evaluaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"AWS Audit Manager","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS Security Hub"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Audit Manager automatiza la recopilaci\xf3n de evidencia de m\xfaltiples fuentes de AWS, incluidos registros de CloudTrail, evaluaciones de Config y hallazgos de Security Hub, y organiza esta evidencia en informes de evaluaci\xf3n que se asignan directamente a los requisitos de marcos de cumplimiento, lo que reduce significativamente el esfuerzo de preparaci\xf3n de auditor\xedas.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n en una cuenta de AWS para auditor\xeda y an\xe1lisis forense, pero captura registros de actividad sin procesar en lugar de organizarlos en informes estructurados de evaluaci\xf3n de cumplimiento. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona comprobaciones automatizadas de mejores pr\xe1cticas en categor\xedas de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, pero entrega recomendaciones de asesoramiento en lugar de evidencia de auditor\xeda estructurada asignada a marcos de cumplimiento. AWS Security Hub agrega y prioriza hallazgos de seguridad de m\xfaltiples servicios de AWS en un panel centralizado, pero es una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de la postura de seguridad, no un servicio de preparaci\xf3n de auditor\xedas de cumplimiento y recopilaci\xf3n de evidencia."},{"id":"q001","question":"AWS permite a los usuarios administrar sus recursos mediante una interfaz de usuario basada en web. \xbfC\xf3mo se llama esta interfaz?","options":{"A":"AWS CLI","B":"AWS API","C":"AWS SDK","D":"AWS Management Console"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Management Console es la interfaz gr\xe1fica basada en web que se usa para administrar e interactuar con los recursos de AWS directamente desde un navegador.\\nAWS CLI es una herramienta de l\xednea de comandos que permite a los usuarios interactuar con los servicios de AWS mediante comandos escritos en una terminal, no mediante una interfaz gr\xe1fica basada en web. AWS API proporciona acceso program\xe1tico a los servicios de AWS mediante solicitudes HTTP realizadas directamente por aplicaciones o scripts, y no tiene interfaz gr\xe1fica de usuario. AWS SDK proporciona bibliotecas y herramientas para interactuar con los servicios de AWS mediante c\xf3digo de aplicaci\xf3n en lenguajes como Python, Java y JavaScript, y no es una interfaz orientada al usuario para administrar recursos."},{"id":"q002","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un ejemplo de escalado horizontal en AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Reemplazar una instancia EC2 existente por una m\xe1s grande y potente","B":"Aumentar la capacidad de c\xf3mputo de una \xfanica instancia EC2 para abordar las crecientes demandas de una aplicaci\xf3n","C":"Agregar m\xe1s capacidad de RAM a una instancia EC2","D":"Agregar m\xe1s instancias EC2 del mismo tama\xf1o para manejar un aumento del tr\xe1fico"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El escalado horizontal significa agregar m\xe1s instancias del mismo tama\xf1o para distribuir la carga, mientras que el escalado vertical significa aumentar la capacidad de una \xfanica instancia.\\nReemplazar una instancia existente por una m\xe1s grande es escalado vertical, no horizontal. Aumentar la capacidad de c\xf3mputo de una \xfanica instancia describe el escalado vertical, que es lo opuesto al escalado horizontal. Agregar m\xe1s RAM a una \xfanica instancia es otra forma de escalado vertical, ya que aumenta la potencia de una instancia en lugar de agregar m\xe1s instancias."},{"id":"q007","question":"Una empresa ha desarrollado una aplicaci\xf3n web de comercio electr\xf3nico en AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedan hacer para garantizar que la aplicaci\xf3n tenga el mayor nivel de disponibilidad?","options":{"A":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en varias Zonas de disponibilidad y ubicaciones de borde","B":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en varias Zonas de disponibilidad y subredes","C":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en varias Regiones y Zonas de disponibilidad","D":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en varias VPC y subredes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Implementar en varias Regiones y Zonas de disponibilidad proporciona el mayor nivel de disponibilidad al proteger simult\xe1neamente contra fallos a nivel de Zona de disponibilidad y fallos a nivel de Regi\xf3n.\\nImplementar en varias Zonas de disponibilidad y ubicaciones de borde agrega alcance de entrega de contenido mediante CloudFront, pero no protege la infraestructura de la aplicaci\xf3n contra un fallo regional. Implementar en varias Zonas de disponibilidad y subredes mejora la resiliencia contra fallos aislados dentro de una Regi\xf3n, pero una interrupci\xf3n regional seguir\xeda afectando toda la implementaci\xf3n. Implementar en varias VPC y subredes crea segmentaci\xf3n de red dentro de una Regi\xf3n, pero no proporciona redundancia geogr\xe1fica contra fallos de Zona de disponibilidad o regionales."},{"id":"q008","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 proporciona AWS Snowball Edge?","options":{"A":"Capacidades de c\xf3mputo integradas que permiten a los clientes procesar datos localmente","B":"Un cat\xe1logo de soluciones de software de terceros que los clientes necesitan para crear soluciones y operar sus negocios","C":"Un almacenamiento en la nube h\xedbrido entre entornos locales y AWS Cloud","D":"Un servicio de transferencia de datos a escala de exabytes que permite mover cantidades extremadamente grandes de datos a AWS","E":"Transferencia segura de grandes cantidades de datos hacia y desde AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS Snowball Edge incluye capacidades de c\xf3mputo integradas que permiten a los clientes procesar y analizar datos localmente antes de transferirlos a AWS, y transfiere de forma segura grandes cantidades de datos hacia y desde AWS mediante un dispositivo f\xedsico resistente.\\nUn cat\xe1logo de soluciones de software de terceros describe AWS Marketplace, que es un cat\xe1logo digital para encontrar e implementar software de proveedores independientes. Un almacenamiento en la nube h\xedbrido entre entornos locales y AWS describe AWS Storage Gateway, que proporciona conectividad h\xedbrida continua entre entornos locales y el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS. Un servicio de transferencia de datos a escala de exabytes describe AWS Snowmobile, que es un servicio separado de Snow Family dise\xf1ado para migrar conjuntos de datos extremadamente grandes mediante un contenedor de env\xedo seguro en lugar de un dispositivo."},{"id":"q012","question":"Una empresa ha decidido migrar su base de datos Oracle a AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede ayudar a lograrlo sin afectar negativamente la funcionalidad de la base de datos de origen?","options":{"A":"AWS OpsWorks","B":"AWS Database Migration Service","C":"AWS Server Migration Service","D":"AWS Application Discovery Service"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Database Migration Service est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para migrar bases de datos a AWS con un tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo, manteniendo la base de datos de origen totalmente operativa durante todo el proceso de migraci\xf3n.\\nAWS OpsWorks (obsoleto) es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n que utiliza Chef y Puppet para automatizar la configuraci\xf3n de servidores y no tiene capacidad para la migraci\xf3n de bases de datos. AWS Server Migration Service est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para migrar m\xe1quinas virtuales desde entornos locales a AWS, no para migrar bases de datos. AWS Application Discovery Service ayuda a planificar migraciones al recopilar datos sobre servidores locales y dependencias, pero no realiza la migraci\xf3n real de bases de datos ni de otras cargas de trabajo."},{"id":"q018","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas hacer para mantener seguros los datos en vol\xfamenes EBS?","options":{"A":"Crear instant\xe1neas de EBS","B":"Asegurar que los datos de EBS est\xe9n cifrados en reposo","C":"Replicar vol\xfamenes EBS en varias Zonas de disponibilidad","D":"Usar AWS Backup para automatizar y gestionar los programas de copia de seguridad","E":"Aumentar el tama\xf1o del volumen EBS con regularidad"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Las instant\xe1neas de EBS crean copias de seguridad en un punto en el tiempo de tu volumen almacenadas de forma duradera en Amazon S3, lo que permite restaurar los datos si un volumen se pierde o se corrompe. Habilitar el cifrado en reposo protege los datos almacenados en vol\xfamenes EBS del acceso no autorizado mediante claves administradas por AWS KMS.\\nReplicar vol\xfamenes EBS en varias Zonas de disponibilidad mejora la disponibilidad, pero no protege contra la eliminaci\xf3n accidental o la corrupci\xf3n de datos, ya que un volumen replicado reflejar\xeda el mismo estado corrupto. Usar AWS Backup automatiza la gesti\xf3n de copias de seguridad en m\xfaltiples servicios de AWS, pero no es en s\xed mismo un m\xe9todo directo para mantener seguros los datos de EBS sin tomar tambi\xe9n instant\xe1neas. Aumentar el tama\xf1o de un volumen EBS agrega capacidad de almacenamiento, pero no tiene efecto en la protecci\xf3n ni en la seguridad de los datos."},{"id":"q021","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 utiliza Amazon CloudFront para distribuir contenido a usuarios globales con baja latencia?","options":{"A":"AWS Global Accelerator","B":"AWS Regions","C":"AWS Edge Locations","D":"AWS Availability Zones"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CloudFront entrega contenido desde ubicaciones de borde, una red global de puntos de presencia que almacenan en cach\xe9 el contenido cerca de los usuarios finales para minimizar la latencia.\\nAWS Global Accelerator tambi\xe9n utiliza la red global de AWS para mejorar el rendimiento de los usuarios globales, pero enruta el tr\xe1fico a endpoints regionales \xf3ptimos en lugar de almacenar contenido en cach\xe9 en ubicaciones de borde, por lo que es un servicio diferente con un mecanismo diferente. AWS Regions son \xe1reas geogr\xe1ficas que contienen m\xfaltiples Availability Zones donde los clientes implementan cargas de trabajo, y CloudFront no distribuye contenido enrutando solicitudes a una Regi\xf3n como lo har\xeda una implementaci\xf3n de aplicaci\xf3n est\xe1ndar. AWS Availability Zones son cl\xfasteres de centros de datos aislados dentro de una Regi\xf3n que se utilizan para redundancia y tolerancia a fallos, y no son el mecanismo que CloudFront utiliza para almacenar en cach\xe9 y servir contenido a usuarios globales."},{"id":"q024","question":"El proceso de identificaci\xf3n de una empresa de servicios financieros en l\xednea requiere que los nuevos usuarios completen una entrevista en l\xednea con su equipo de seguridad. Las entrevistas grabadas completadas solo se requieren en caso de un problema legal o una violaci\xf3n de cumplimiento normativo. \xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio m\xe1s rentable para almacenar los videos grabados?","options":{"A":"S3 Intelligent-Tiering","B":"Amazon S3 Standard","C":"Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive","D":"Amazon EBS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive es la clase de almacenamiento de AWS de menor costo, dise\xf1ada para datos a los que se accede con poca frecuencia y que pueden tolerar tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n de horas, lo que la hace ideal para archivos de cumplimiento que tal vez nunca se necesiten.\\nS3 Intelligent-Tiering mueve autom\xe1ticamente los datos entre niveles de acceso seg\xfan los patrones de uso, pero es m\xe1s adecuado para datos con frecuencia de acceso impredecible que para datos que se sabe de antemano que se acceden con poca frecuencia. Amazon S3 Standard proporciona almacenamiento de objetos de baja latencia y alto rendimiento para datos de acceso frecuente, lo que lo hace m\xe1s caro de lo necesario para videos que rara vez o nunca se recuperar\xe1n. Amazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque conectados a instancias EC2 para cargas de trabajo activas, y no es una soluci\xf3n rentable para el archivo a largo plazo de archivos de video a los que se accede con poca frecuencia."},{"id":"q025","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio proporciona DNS en la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon CloudFront","D":"Amazon EMR"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 es el servicio de Sistema de Nombres de Dominio escalable y de alta disponibilidad proporcionado por AWS, que se utiliza para enrutar a los usuarios finales a las aplicaciones mediante la traducci\xf3n de nombres de dominio a direcciones IP.\\nAWS Config realiza un seguimiento y registra continuamente la configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento y detectar cambios, y no tiene capacidad de DNS ni de enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico. Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y distribuye contenido en ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo para reducir la latencia, y aunque se usa junto con Route 53 en muchas arquitecturas, es un servicio de entrega de contenido en lugar de un servicio de DNS. Amazon EMR es una plataforma de cl\xfaster administrada para procesar grandes vol\xfamenes de datos usando frameworks de big data como Hadoop y Spark, y no tiene conexi\xf3n con DNS ni con el enrutamiento de red."},{"id":"q027","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 implementando una nueva aplicaci\xf3n web de dos niveles en AWS. \xbfD\xf3nde se deben almacenar los datos a los que se accede con m\xe1s frecuencia para que el tiempo de respuesta de la aplicaci\xf3n sea \xf3ptimo?","options":{"A":"AWS OpsWorks","B":"AWS Storage Gateway","C":"Amazon EBS volume","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon ElastiCache es un servicio de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria que proporciona tiempos de respuesta de submilisegundos para datos a los que se accede con frecuencia, lo que lo convierte en la soluci\xf3n dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para optimizar el tiempo de respuesta de la aplicaci\xf3n en una arquitectura web de dos niveles.\\nAWS OpsWorks (obsoleto) es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n que automatiza la configuraci\xf3n y el mantenimiento de servidores usando Chef y Puppet, y no tiene capacidad para almacenar datos ni mejorar los tiempos de respuesta de la aplicaci\xf3n mediante almacenamiento en cach\xe9. AWS Storage Gateway conecta entornos locales con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS para la integraci\xf3n de almacenamiento h\xedbrido, y es un servicio de puente de almacenamiento en lugar de una capa de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de baja latencia para datos de aplicaciones web. Amazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque persistentes que se conectan directamente a instancias EC2 para acceso a disco de baja latencia, y aunque ofrece un acceso m\xe1s r\xe1pido que el almacenamiento de objetos, no proporciona el rendimiento en memoria de submilisegundos que ElastiCache entrega para datos a los que se accede con frecuencia."},{"id":"q030","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes puede describirse como un servicio global de red de entrega de contenido (CDN)?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS Regions","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront es el servicio global de CDN de AWS que almacena en cach\xe9 y entrega contenido desde ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo, lo que reduce la latencia para los usuarios finales independientemente de su ubicaci\xf3n.\\nAWS VPN crea conexiones de red cifradas entre la infraestructura local y AWS a trav\xe9s de Internet p\xfablico, y no tiene capacidad de entrega de contenido ni de almacenamiento en cach\xe9. AWS Direct Connect proporciona un enlace de red privado dedicado entre la infraestructura local y AWS, y es un servicio de conectividad en lugar de una red de entrega de contenido. AWS Regions son \xe1reas geogr\xe1ficas que contienen m\xfaltiples Availability Zones donde se aloja la infraestructura de AWS, y son un componente de la infraestructura global en lugar de un servicio de CDN."},{"id":"q033","question":"Su empresa tiene una aplicaci\xf3n de almac\xe9n de datos que requiere acceso a una base de datos NoSQL. \xbfQu\xe9 oferta de base de datos de AWS cumplir\xeda este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Aurora","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon Elastic Block Store","D":"Amazon Redshift"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL de clave-valor y documentos totalmente administrada, dise\xf1ada para aplicaciones que necesitan almacenamiento de datos r\xe1pido, flexible y sin esquema.\\nAmazon Aurora es una base de datos relacional totalmente administrada compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL que utiliza esquemas estructurados y SQL, y no admite modelos de datos NoSQL. Amazon Elastic Block Store proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque que se conectan a instancias EC2 para usarse como discos persistentes, y es un servicio de almacenamiento sin ninguna capacidad de base de datos de ning\xfan tipo. Amazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos administrado optimizado para ejecutar consultas SQL anal\xedticas en grandes conjuntos de datos, y no es una base de datos NoSQL."},{"id":"q039","question":"Para implementar las mejores pr\xe1cticas al tratar con un “Single Point of Failure” (punto \xfanico de fallo), se debe intentar construir la mayor automatizaci\xf3n posible tanto en la detecci\xf3n como en la reacci\xf3n ante fallos. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS ayudar\xeda?","options":{"A":"ELB","B":"Auto Scaling","C":"Amazon Athena","D":"ECR","E":"Amazon EC2"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Elastic Load Balancing detecta autom\xe1ticamente las instancias en mal estado y deja de enviarles tr\xe1fico, mientras que Auto Scaling reemplaza autom\xe1ticamente las instancias fallidas, eliminando juntos los puntos \xfanicos de fallo mediante detecci\xf3n y recuperaci\xf3n automatizadas.\\nAmazon Athena es un servicio de consultas sin servidor para analizar datos almacenados en S3 usando SQL est\xe1ndar y no tiene ning\xfan rol en la detecci\xf3n o recuperaci\xf3n de fallos de infraestructura. Amazon Elastic Container Registry es un registro administrado de im\xe1genes de contenedores para almacenar y administrar im\xe1genes Docker, no relacionado con la detecci\xf3n de fallos o la recuperaci\xf3n automatizada. Amazon EC2 proporciona las propias instancias de servidor virtual, pero no automatiza de forma nativa la detecci\xf3n de fallos ni la respuesta ante ellos sin ELB y Auto Scaling configurados a su alrededor."},{"id":"q040","question":"Una empresa planea alojar un sitio web educativo en AWS. Sus cursos en video se transmitir\xe1n en todo el mundo. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS ayudar\xe1 a lograr altas velocidades de transferencia?","options":{"A":"Amazon SNS","B":"Amazon Kinesis Video Streams","C":"AWS CloudFormation","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront es una CDN que almacena en cach\xe9 contenido de video en ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo, entreg\xe1ndolo a los espectadores desde la ubicaci\xf3n m\xe1s cercana y logrando altas velocidades de transferencia a nivel global.\\nAmazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda de publicaci\xf3n/suscripci\xf3n para enviar notificaciones a m\xfaltiples suscriptores y no tiene ning\xfan rol en la entrega o transmisi\xf3n de contenido de video a nivel global. Amazon Kinesis Video Streams ingiere y procesa flujos de video en vivo desde dispositivos para an\xe1lisis y almacenamiento en tiempo real, pero no es un servicio de entrega de contenido para distribuir video pregrabado a audiencias globales con altas velocidades de transferencia. AWS CloudFormation es un servicio de infraestructura como c\xf3digo para aprovisionar recursos de AWS mediante plantillas, y no tiene capacidad para entregar o acelerar contenido a usuarios globales."},{"id":"q041","question":"Un desarrollador planea crear una aplicaci\xf3n web de dos niveles que tiene una capa de base de datos MySQL. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de bases de datos de AWS proporcionar\xeda copias de seguridad automatizadas para la aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Una base de datos MySQL instalada en una instancia EC2","B":"Amazon Aurora","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon Neptune"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Aurora es una base de datos relacional administrada compatible con MySQL que gestiona autom\xe1ticamente las copias de seguridad, la replicaci\xf3n y la conmutaci\xf3n por error, a diferencia de ejecutar MySQL en EC2, donde el cliente gestiona todo por s\xed mismo.\\nUna base de datos MySQL instalada en una instancia EC2 es un despliegue autoadministrado en el que el cliente es responsable de configurar y ejecutar sus propios procesos de copia de seguridad, sin capacidad de copia de seguridad automatizada proporcionada por AWS. Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL de clave-valor y de documentos totalmente administrada que no es compatible con MySQL y no es adecuada para sustituir una capa de base de datos relacional MySQL. Amazon Neptune es un servicio de base de datos de grafos totalmente administrado dise\xf1ado para conjuntos de datos altamente conectados, como redes sociales y grafos de conocimiento, y no es una opci\xf3n de base de datos relacional para cargas de trabajo MySQL."},{"id":"q042","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio de AWS que permite a los arquitectos de AWS administrar la infraestructura como c\xf3digo?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudFormation","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon SES","D":"Amazon EMR"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS CloudFormation permite definir toda la infraestructura de AWS como c\xf3digo mediante plantillas JSON o YAML, lo que habilita el aprovisionamiento repetible y automatizado de recursos sin configuraci\xf3n manual.\\nAWS Config supervisa y registra continuamente el estado de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS y los eval\xfaa frente a la configuraci\xf3n deseada, pero no aprovisiona ni administra la infraestructura. Amazon SES es un servicio basado en la nube para enviar y recibir correo electr\xf3nico, totalmente ajeno a la administraci\xf3n de infraestructura. Amazon EMR es un servicio administrado de procesamiento de big data para ejecutar frameworks como Apache Spark y Hadoop, ajeno a la infraestructura como c\xf3digo."},{"id":"q044","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 proporciona el AWS Health Dashboard?","options":{"A":"Gu\xeda detallada para la resoluci\xf3n de problemas a fin de abordar eventos de AWS que afectan sus recursos","B":"Comprobaciones de estado para instancias de Auto Scaling","C":"Recomendaciones para la optimizaci\xf3n de costos","D":"Un panel que detalla las vulnerabilidades en sus aplicaciones","E":"Vista personalizada del estado de los servicios de AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard proporciona una vista personalizada de c\xf3mo los eventos de los servicios de AWS afectan sus recursos espec\xedficos, y una gu\xeda detallada para la resoluci\xf3n de problemas que le ayuda a responder a esos eventos y resolverlos.\\nLas comprobaciones de estado para instancias de Auto Scaling son una caracter\xedstica de Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling que supervisa el estado de las instancias y reemplaza autom\xe1ticamente las instancias en mal estado, y no son una capacidad del AWS Health Dashboard. Las recomendaciones para la optimizaci\xf3n de costos las proporciona AWS Trusted Advisor, que inspecciona su entorno y sugiere formas de reducir el gasto, y no son una caracter\xedstica del AWS Health Dashboard. Un panel que detalla las vulnerabilidades en las aplicaciones describe Amazon Inspector, que escanea en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y no es una funci\xf3n del AWS Health Dashboard."},{"id":"q045","question":"Ha implementado su aplicaci\xf3n en varias instancias de Amazon EC2. Sus clientes se quejan de que a veces no pueden acceder a su aplicaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS le permite supervisar el rendimiento de sus instancias EC2 para ayudar a solucionar estos problemas?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda","B":"AWS Config","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch recopila y supervisa m\xe9tricas, registros y eventos de las instancias EC2, lo que le permite configurar alarmas y visualizar datos de rendimiento para diagnosticar problemas de disponibilidad y rendimiento que afectan su aplicaci\xf3n.\\nAWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor que ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos y no tiene capacidad para supervisar el rendimiento ni la disponibilidad de las instancias EC2. AWS Config registra y eval\xfaa los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS con fines de cumplimiento y auditor\xeda, pero no recopila m\xe9tricas de rendimiento ni ayuda a solucionar problemas de disponibilidad. AWS CloudTrail registra las llamadas a la API y la actividad de los usuarios en su cuenta de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y gobernanza, pero no supervisa las m\xe9tricas de rendimiento de las instancias ni la disponibilidad de las aplicaciones."},{"id":"q047","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes NO es un beneficio de Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 proporciona almacenamiento ilimitado para cualquier tipo de datos","B":"Amazon S3 puede ejecutar cualquier tipo de aplicaci\xf3n o sistema de backend","C":"Amazon S3 almacena cualquier cantidad de objetos, pero con l\xedmites de tama\xf1o de objeto","D":"Amazon S3 se puede escalar manualmente para almacenar y recuperar cualquier cantidad de datos desde cualquier lugar","E":"Amazon S3 proporciona 99.999999999% (11 nueves) de durabilidad de los datos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 no puede ejecutar aplicaciones ni sistemas de backend, ya que es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos en lugar de una plataforma de c\xf3mputo. Ejecutar aplicaciones y sistemas de backend es funci\xf3n de servicios como Amazon EC2 y AWS Lambda. Amazon S3 se escala autom\xe1ticamente, no manualmente, por lo que la afirmaci\xf3n de que se escala manualmente es incorrecta desde el punto de vista de los hechos y, por lo tanto, no es un beneficio genuino.\\nAmazon S3 s\xed proporciona almacenamiento pr\xe1cticamente ilimitado para cualquier tipo de datos como objetos, lo que hace que la opci\xf3n A sea una afirmaci\xf3n verdadera y, por lo tanto, no sea la respuesta. Amazon S3 almacena cualquier cantidad de objetos, pero impone un l\xedmite de tama\xf1o de 5 TB por objeto individual, lo que hace que la opci\xf3n C sea una descripci\xf3n precisa de su comportamiento. Amazon S3 est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para proporcionar una durabilidad del 99.999999999% para los objetos almacenados en su infraestructura, lo que hace que la opci\xf3n E sea un beneficio genuino y bien conocido."},{"id":"q053","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes son caracter\xedsticas de Amazon CloudWatch Logs?","options":{"A":"Res\xfamenes mediante Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","B":"An\xe1lisis gratuito de Amazon OpenSearch Service","C":"Proporcionado sin cargo","D":"Supervisi\xf3n en tiempo real","E":"Retenci\xf3n ajustable"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch Logs admite la supervisi\xf3n en tiempo real de los datos de registro y per\xedodos de retenci\xf3n configurables, lo que le permite controlar exactamente cu\xe1nto tiempo se conservan los datos de registro antes de que se eliminen autom\xe1ticamente.\\nAmazon SNS es un servicio de notificaciones y mensajer\xeda que puede activarse mediante alarmas de CloudWatch, pero resumir datos de registro no es una caracter\xedstica nativa de CloudWatch Logs. Amazon CloudWatch Logs no es gratuito, ya que se aplican cargos seg\xfan el volumen de datos ingeridos, almacenados y analizados. Amazon OpenSearch Service es un servicio de an\xe1lisis independiente que se puede integrar con CloudWatch Logs como destino, pero el an\xe1lisis gratuito de OpenSearch no es una caracter\xedstica nativa de CloudWatch Logs."},{"id":"q054","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un servicio de c\xf3mputo administrado por AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon SWF","B":"Amazon EC2","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"Amazon Aurora"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor totalmente administrado en el que usted carga su c\xf3digo y AWS gestiona autom\xe1ticamente toda la infraestructura subyacente, la aplicaci\xf3n de parches y el escalado, sin que el cliente tenga que administrar servidores.\\nAmazon Simple Workflow Service es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo para coordinar tareas entre componentes de aplicaciones distribuidas, y no es un servicio de c\xf3mputo que proporcione o administre infraestructura subyacente. Amazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales con acceso completo al sistema operativo, pero requiere que el cliente administre el sistema operativo invitado, la aplicaci\xf3n de parches y la configuraci\xf3n de capacidad, lo que lo convierte en un servicio de c\xf3mputo administrado por el cliente en lugar de un servicio de c\xf3mputo totalmente administrado por AWS. Amazon Aurora es un servicio de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL, y es un servicio de base de datos en lugar de un servicio de c\xf3mputo."},{"id":"q055","question":"Una empresa quiere reducir la huella f\xedsica de c\xf3mputo que los desarrolladores utilizan para ejecutar c\xf3digo. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio satisfar\xeda esa necesidad al habilitar arquitecturas sin servidor?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)","B":"AWS Lambda","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"AWS CodeBuild"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Lambda ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin requerir aprovisionamiento ni administraci\xf3n de servidores, lo que elimina directamente la huella f\xedsica de c\xf3mputo que, de otro modo, los desarrolladores tendr\xedan que mantener.\\nAmazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales en los que los clientes son responsables de aprovisionar y administrar las instancias subyacentes, lo que lo convierte en una soluci\xf3n basada en servidores en lugar de sin servidor. Amazon DynamoDB es un servicio de base de datos NoSQL totalmente administrado y no proporciona ejecuci\xf3n de c\xf3mputo para ejecutar c\xf3digo de aplicaciones. AWS CodeBuild es un servicio de compilaci\xf3n totalmente administrado para compilar c\xf3digo fuente y ejecutar pruebas, no un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor para ejecutar la l\xf3gica de las aplicaciones."},{"id":"q063","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS se deben usar para lectura/escritura de datos que cambian constantemente?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 GLACIER","B":"Amazon RDS","C":"AWS Snowball","D":"Amazon Redshift","E":"Amazon EFS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Amazon RDS admite operaciones frecuentes de lectura/escritura en datos relacionales con compatibilidad total de transacciones ACID, lo que lo hace muy adecuado para aplicaciones con datos estructurados que cambian constantemente. Amazon EFS proporciona un sistema de archivos de red compartido con acceso concurrente de lectura/escritura para varias instancias de EC2, lo que lo hace apropiado para cargas de trabajo que requieren cambios continuos a nivel de archivo.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier es un servicio de almacenamiento de archivo dise\xf1ado para datos a los que se accede con poca frecuencia y no admite las operaciones frecuentes de lectura/escritura que requieren los datos que cambian constantemente. AWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico que se utiliza para transferir grandes vol\xfamenes de datos hacia o desde AWS y no tiene capacidad para funcionar como un almac\xe9n de datos de lectura/escritura para cargas de trabajo activas. Amazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos optimizado para ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas complejas sobre grandes conjuntos de datos, no para manejar operaciones transaccionales frecuentes de lectura/escritura sobre datos que cambian constantemente."},{"id":"q067","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un servicio web de Sistema de Nombres de Dominio (DNS) administrado por AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"Amazon Neptune","C":"Amazon SageMaker","D":"Amazon Lightsail"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 es el servicio web DNS administrado de AWS que traduce nombres de dominio en direcciones IP y tambi\xe9n proporciona pol\xedticas de enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico, comprobaciones de estado y registro de dominios.\\nAmazon Neptune es un servicio de base de datos de grafos totalmente administrado para aplicaciones que trabajan con conjuntos de datos altamente conectados, no relacionado con DNS ni con el enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico. Amazon SageMaker es una plataforma de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico administrada para crear, entrenar e implementar modelos de ML, totalmente ajena a DNS. Amazon Lightsail proporciona servidores privados virtuales simplificados con capacidad de c\xf3mputo y redes preconfiguradas para cargas de trabajo sencillas, no es un servicio DNS."},{"id":"q070","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio es mejor para almacenar resultados comunes de consultas a bases de datos, lo que ayuda a aliviar la carga de acceso a la base de datos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Machine Learning","B":"Amazon SQS","C":"Amazon ElastiCache","D":"Amazon EC2 Instance Store"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon ElastiCache proporciona almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria mediante Redis o Memcached, almacenando los resultados consultados con frecuencia para que las aplicaciones puedan recuperarlos sin consultar la base de datos repetidamente, lo que reduce dr\xe1sticamente la carga de la base de datos.\\nAmazon Machine Learning es un servicio para crear y entrenar modelos de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico y no tiene ning\xfan rol en el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de bases de datos ni en el almacenamiento de resultados de consultas. Amazon SQS es un servicio de colas de mensajes totalmente administrado para desacoplar componentes de aplicaciones, no para almacenar en cach\xe9 resultados de consultas a bases de datos. Amazon EC2 Instance Store es almacenamiento de bloques ef\xedmero conectado directamente a una instancia de EC2 que no persiste m\xe1s all\xe1 del ciclo de vida de la instancia y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 compartido de resultados de bases de datos."},{"id":"q073","question":"Una empresa quiere centrarse en las actividades comerciales en lugar de administrar c\xf3mputo y capacidad. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede usar para agregar o quitar autom\xe1ticamente instancias de Amazon EC2 seg\xfan la demanda?","options":{"A":"Elastic Load Balancer","B":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","C":"Amazon Route 53","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling monitorea la demanda y agrega o quita autom\xe1ticamente instancias de EC2 para mantener el rendimiento y minimizar el costo, liberando a los equipos de la administraci\xf3n manual de la capacidad para que puedan centrarse en sus aplicaciones.\\nElastic Load Balancing distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre las instancias de EC2 existentes para evitar que una sola instancia se sature, pero no agrega ni quita instancias en respuesta a cambios en la demanda. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de dominios que dirige a los usuarios a los puntos de conexi\xf3n de las aplicaciones y no tiene ning\xfan rol en el aprovisionamiento ni en la eliminaci\xf3n de capacidad de c\xf3mputo. Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y sirve contenido desde ubicaciones de borde m\xe1s cercanas a los usuarios finales, y no administra ni escala la capacidad de las instancias de EC2."},{"id":"q076","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede usar para consultar conjuntos de datos almacenados directamente en Amazon S3 mediante SQL est\xe1ndar?","options":{"A":"AWS Glue","B":"Amazon EMR","C":"Amazon CloudSearch","D":"Amazon Athena"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Athena permite ejecutar consultas SQL est\xe1ndar directamente contra datos almacenados en S3 sin cargarlos primero en una base de datos, lo que lo convierte en el servicio dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para consultar conjuntos de datos directamente desde S3.\\nAWS Glue es un servicio ETL sin servidor para descubrir, preparar y transformar datos para canalizaciones anal\xedticas, no para ejecutar consultas SQL ad hoc directamente contra S3. Amazon EMR proporciona cl\xfasteres administrados para procesar grandes conjuntos de datos usando marcos como Spark y Hadoop, pero requiere aprovisionar infraestructura en lugar de consultar S3 directamente con SQL est\xe1ndar. Amazon CloudSearch es un servicio de b\xfasqueda administrado para agregar funcionalidad de b\xfasqueda de texto completo a las aplicaciones y no tiene capacidad para consultar datos basados en SQL."},{"id":"q081","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios pertenece a la categor\xeda de plataforma sin servidor de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon EMR","B":"Elastic Load Balancing","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"Amazon EC2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor que ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin requerir que se aprovisione o administre ning\xfan servidor subyacente.\\nAmazon EMR es una plataforma de cl\xfasteres administrada que ejecuta marcos de macrodatos como Hadoop y Spark en instancias de EC2 aprovisionadas, y no es un servicio sin servidor. Elastic Load Balancing distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre varios destinos, como instancias de EC2, y es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico en lugar de una plataforma de c\xf3mputo de cualquier tipo. Amazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales en la nube que requieren aprovisionar, configurar y administrar las instancias subyacentes, lo que lo convierte en lo opuesto a un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor."},{"id":"q085","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 tecnolog\xeda permite que la capacidad de c\xf3mputo se ajuste a medida que cambian las cargas?","options":{"A":"Equilibrio de carga","B":"Conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica","C":"Round robin","D":"Auto Scaling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Auto Scaling monitorea la carga y ajusta autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias de c\xf3mputo para satisfacer la demanda, lo que garantiza que la capacidad se escale durante los per\xedodos de m\xe1xima actividad y se reduzca cuando la demanda disminuya, sin ninguna intervenci\xf3n manual.\\nEl equilibrio de carga distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre las instancias de c\xf3mputo existentes para evitar que una sola instancia se sature, pero no agrega ni quita capacidad en respuesta a una carga cambiante. La conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica redirige el tr\xe1fico a una instancia o recurso saludable cuando se detecta una falla, lo que aborda la disponibilidad en lugar del ajuste de capacidad. Round robin es un m\xe9todo de distribuci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico que rota las solicitudes de manera uniforme entre un conjunto de servidores y, al igual que el equilibrio de carga, administra la distribuci\xf3n del tr\xe1fico en lugar de ajustar el n\xfamero de instancias de c\xf3mputo disponibles."},{"id":"q086","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS es una base de datos NoSQL administrada?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon Aurora","D":"Amazon RDS for MariaDB"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB es el servicio de base de datos NoSQL totalmente administrado de AWS que admite estructuras de datos de clave-valor y de documentos, dise\xf1ado para aplicaciones que requieren un rendimiento constante de milisegundos de un solo d\xedgito a cualquier escala.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos totalmente administrado dise\xf1ado para ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas complejas sobre grandes vol\xfamenes de datos estructurados, no es una base de datos NoSQL para almacenamiento de clave-valor o de documentos. Amazon Aurora es un motor de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL, y utiliza esquemas estructurados basados en SQL en lugar de los modelos de datos NoSQL flexibles que ofrece DynamoDB. Amazon RDS for MariaDB es un servicio de base de datos relacional administrado que ejecuta el motor MariaDB, el cual utiliza tablas SQL estructuradas y no es un servicio de base de datos NoSQL."},{"id":"q087","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una relaci\xf3n correcta entre las Regiones, las Zonas de Disponibilidad y las ubicaciones de borde?","options":{"A":"Los centros de datos contienen Regiones","B":"Las Regiones contienen Zonas de Disponibilidad","C":"Las Zonas de Disponibilidad contienen ubicaciones de borde","D":"Las ubicaciones de borde contienen Regiones"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las Regiones son \xe1reas geogr\xe1ficas que contienen m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad. Cada AZ consta de uno o m\xe1s centros de datos, y las ubicaciones de borde son puntos de presencia separados usados por CloudFront, independientes de las AZ.\\nLos centros de datos contienen Regiones invierte la jerarqu\xeda. Los centros de datos est\xe1n dentro de las AZ, que est\xe1n dentro de las Regiones. Las Zonas de Disponibilidad contienen ubicaciones de borde es falso. Las ubicaciones de borde son infraestructura separada de las AZ y existen de forma independiente en todo el mundo. Las ubicaciones de borde contienen Regiones es falso. Las ubicaciones de borde son puntos de presencia m\xe1s peque\xf1os y no tienen una relaci\xf3n de contenci\xf3n con las Regiones."},{"id":"q088","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 enfoque para transcodificar una gran cantidad de archivos de video individuales cumple con los principios de arquitectura de AWS?","options":{"A":"Usar muchas instancias en paralelo","B":"Usar una \xfanica instancia grande durante las horas de menor actividad","C":"Usar hardware dedicado","D":"Usar un tipo de instancia GPU grande"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Usar muchas instancias en paralelo aplica el escalado horizontal, un principio central de la arquitectura de AWS que distribuye las cargas de trabajo entre varias instancias simult\xe1neamente para lograr mayor velocidad y eficiencia de costos que el procesamiento secuencial.\\nUsar una \xfanica instancia grande durante las horas de menor actividad aplica escalado vertical y procesamiento secuencial, lo que contradice los principios de arquitectura de AWS que favorecen el paralelismo y no reduce el tiempo total de procesamiento. Usar hardware dedicado proporciona aislamiento de servidores f\xedsicos para requisitos de cumplimiento o licenciamiento, pero no mejora el rendimiento para cargas de trabajo paralelas a gran escala. Usar un tipo de instancia GPU grande optimiza el rendimiento de procesamiento gr\xe1fico en una sola instancia en lugar de distribuir la carga de trabajo entre varias instancias paralelas."},{"id":"q089","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS pueden alojar una base de datos Microsoft SQL Server?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS)","C":"Amazon Aurora","D":"Amazon Redshift","E":"Amazon S3"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Microsoft SQL Server se puede alojar en instancias de Amazon EC2, donde el cliente instala y administra la base de datos por s\xed mismo, o mediante Amazon RDS para SQL Server, donde AWS administra la infraestructura subyacente, el parcheo y las copias de seguridad como un servicio completamente administrado.\\nAmazon Aurora solo admite motores de base de datos compatibles con MySQL y PostgreSQL y no admite Microsoft SQL Server. Amazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos optimizado para consultas anal\xedticas sobre grandes conjuntos de datos, no un motor de base de datos relacional para alojar cargas de trabajo de SQL Server. Amazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos y no puede alojar ni ejecutar un motor de base de datos relacional."},{"id":"q093","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 ventaja de ahorro de tiempo ofrece el uso de Amazon Rekognition?","options":{"A":"Amazon Rekognition proporciona marca de agua autom\xe1tica en las im\xe1genes","B":"Amazon Rekognition proporciona detecci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de objetos que aparecen en las im\xe1genes","C":"Amazon Recognition proporciona la capacidad de redimensionar millones de im\xe1genes autom\xe1ticamente","D":"Amazon Rekognition usa Amazon Mechanical Turk para permitir que los humanos pujen por trabajos de detecci\xf3n de objetos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Rekognition analiza autom\xe1ticamente im\xe1genes y videos para detectar objetos, escenas, rostros, texto y actividades mediante aprendizaje autom\xe1tico, lo que elimina el tiempo que de otro modo se dedicar\xeda a la revisi\xf3n manual de im\xe1genes o a la creaci\xf3n de canalizaciones de ML personalizadas.\\nLa marca de agua autom\xe1tica de im\xe1genes no es una caracter\xedstica de Amazon Rekognition, que es un servicio de an\xe1lisis y detecci\xf3n en lugar de una herramienta de edici\xf3n de im\xe1genes o gesti\xf3n de derechos. Redimensionar millones de im\xe1genes autom\xe1ticamente no es una capacidad de Rekognition y describe una tarea de procesamiento de im\xe1genes m\xe1s adecuada para una soluci\xf3n personalizada o un servicio como AWS Lambda con una biblioteca de im\xe1genes. Amazon Rekognition no usa Amazon Mechanical Turk ni implica que humanos pujen por trabajos, ya que sus capacidades de detecci\xf3n son totalmente automatizadas mediante modelos de aprendizaje profundo sin intervenci\xf3n humana."},{"id":"q094","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede usar para escalar autom\xe1ticamente una aplicaci\xf3n hacia arriba y hacia abajo sin tomar decisiones de planificaci\xf3n de capacidad?","options":{"A":"AWS Auto Scaling","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Lambda"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Auto Scaling ajusta autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias de EC2 seg\xfan pol\xedticas definidas, lo que elimina la necesidad de tomar decisiones manuales de planificaci\xf3n de capacidad y garantiza que la aplicaci\xf3n siempre tenga la cantidad correcta de capacidad de c\xf3mputo.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos completamente administrado dise\xf1ado para ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas sobre grandes conjuntos de datos, y no tiene capacidad para escalar autom\xe1ticamente la infraestructura de la aplicaci\xf3n. AWS CloudTrail es un servicio de registro y auditor\xeda que registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta en un entorno de AWS, y no administra ni ajusta la capacidad de la aplicaci\xf3n. AWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor que ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin aprovisionar servidores, pero es un entorno de ejecuci\xf3n basado en eventos en lugar de un servicio que escala la infraestructura de una aplicaci\xf3n existente hacia arriba y hacia abajo."},{"id":"q095","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes beneficios ofrece Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) sobre la administraci\xf3n tradicional de bases de datos?","options":{"A":"AWS administra los datos almacenados en las tablas de Amazon RDS","B":"AWS administra el mantenimiento del sistema operativo","C":"AWS escala autom\xe1ticamente los tipos de instancia a demanda","D":"AWS administra el tipo de base de datos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Con Amazon RDS, AWS administra el parcheo del sistema operativo subyacente, las copias de seguridad y las ventanas de mantenimiento, lo que libera a los clientes de la administraci\xf3n a nivel de sistema operativo mientras estos conservan la responsabilidad sobre sus datos, el esquema y la configuraci\xf3n a nivel de base de datos.\\nAWS no administra los datos almacenados en las tablas de RDS, ya que los clientes conservan toda la responsabilidad sobre los datos que cargan, modifican y eliminan dentro de sus bases de datos. RDS no escala autom\xe1ticamente los tipos de instancia a demanda, ya que los clientes deben redimensionar las instancias manualmente o habilitar el escalado autom\xe1tico del almacenamiento, a diferencia de servicios sin servidor como DynamoDB, que escalan autom\xe1ticamente. AWS no administra el tipo de base de datos en RDS, ya que los clientes eligen y siguen siendo responsables de seleccionar el motor de base de datos, como MySQL, PostgreSQL u Oracle, al crear una instancia."},{"id":"q098","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio almacena objetos, proporciona acceso en tiempo real a esos objetos y ofrece capacidades de versionado y ciclo de vida?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Glacier","B":"AWS Storage Gateway","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon EBS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 almacena objetos con acceso en tiempo real, admite versionado para rastrear y restaurar versiones anteriores, y ofrece pol\xedticas de ciclo de vida para administrar las transiciones de objetos, lo que lo convierte en el servicio que coincide con las tres capacidades descritas.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier es un servicio de almacenamiento de archivo dise\xf1ado para datos a los que se accede con poca frecuencia y que pueden tolerar tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n de horas, y no proporciona el acceso en tiempo real ni las capacidades de versionado que describe la pregunta. AWS Storage Gateway es un servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido que conecta entornos locales con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS para la integraci\xf3n continua, y no es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos con versionado o administraci\xf3n del ciclo de vida. Amazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque persistentes que se adjuntan a instancias de EC2, y no almacena objetos ni admite pol\xedticas de versionado y ciclo de vida."},{"id":"q108","question":"Una empresa quiere expandirse de una Regi\xf3n de AWS a una segunda Regi\xf3n de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 necesita hacer la empresa para comenzar a admitir la nueva Regi\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Contactar a un AWS Account Manager para firmar un nuevo contrato","B":"Mover una Zona de Disponibilidad a la nueva Regi\xf3n","C":"Comenzar a implementar recursos en la segunda Regi\xf3n","D":"Descargar la AWS Management Console para la nueva Regi\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las Regiones de AWS est\xe1n disponibles globalmente y los clientes pueden comenzar a aprovisionar recursos en cualquier Regi\xf3n en cualquier momento a trav\xe9s de la consola, la CLI o la API sin necesidad de acuerdos ni configuraci\xf3n adicionales.\\nContactar a un AWS Account Manager para firmar un nuevo contrato no es necesario porque AWS utiliza un modelo de autoservicio y pago por uso sin contratos por Regi\xf3n. Las Zonas de Disponibilidad son componentes de infraestructura fijos dentro de una Regi\xf3n y no se pueden mover ni reasignar. La AWS Management Console es una interfaz basada en web a la que se accede mediante un navegador y no requiere una descarga separada para cada Regi\xf3n."},{"id":"q109","question":"\xbfPor qu\xe9 es beneficioso usar Elastic Load Balancers con aplicaciones?","options":{"A":"Permiten la conversi\xf3n de Application Load Balancers a Classic Load Balancers","B":"Son capaces de manejar cambios constantes en los patrones de tr\xe1fico de red","C":"Ajustan la capacidad autom\xe1ticamente","D":"Se proporcionan sin cargo a los usuarios"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Elastic Load Balancers distribuyen autom\xe1ticamente el tr\xe1fico entrante entre m\xfaltiples destinos registrados y se adaptan a patrones de tr\xe1fico de red en constante cambio sin requerir intervenci\xf3n manual, lo que garantiza que ning\xfan destino individual se convierta en un cuello de botella.\\nElastic Load Balancers no convierten entre Application Load Balancers y Classic Load Balancers; estos son tipos de balanceadores de carga distintos que no se pueden convertir entre s\xed, y la migraci\xf3n requiere implementar un nuevo balanceador de carga en lugar de una conversi\xf3n in situ. ELB no ajusta por s\xed mismo el n\xfamero de instancias de c\xf3mputo; esa es la responsabilidad de los grupos de Auto Scaling que trabajan junto con un balanceador de carga. Elastic Load Balancing no se proporciona sin cargo; hay cargos por hora para cada balanceador de carga m\xe1s cargos por procesamiento de datos, lo que hace que el costo sea un factor al dise\xf1ar arquitecturas con balanceo de carga."},{"id":"q111","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la opci\xf3n de almacenamiento duradero de menor costo para retener copias de seguridad de bases de datos con recuperaci\xf3n inmediata?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval","C":"Amazon EBS","D":"Amazon EC2 Instance Store"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon S3 Standard proporciona almacenamiento de objetos duradero y de alta disponibilidad con recuperaci\xf3n inmediata en milisegundos, y es m\xe1s rentable que EBS para almacenamiento de copias de seguridad al tiempo que cumple el requisito de recuperaci\xf3n inmediata.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para almacenamiento de archivo a largo plazo a muy bajo costo, y la recuperaci\xf3n tarda desde minutos hasta horas, lo que lo hace inadecuado para copias de seguridad que requieren acceso inmediato. Amazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloques persistentes conectados directamente a instancias EC2 para acceso a disco de baja latencia, y es significativamente m\xe1s costoso por gigabyte que S3 para almacenar datos de copias de seguridad. Amazon EC2 Instance Store proporciona almacenamiento en bloques temporal conectado f\xedsicamente a la m\xe1quina host, y es almacenamiento ef\xedmero que se pierde cuando la instancia se detiene, lo que lo hace totalmente inadecuado para almacenamiento duradero de copias de seguridad."},{"id":"q116","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio administrado de AWS se utiliza para alojar bases de datos?","options":{"A":"AWS Batch","B":"AWS Artifact","C":"AWS Data Pipeline","D":"Amazon RDS"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado que admite varios motores de bases de datos, incluidos MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle y SQL Server, y gestiona autom\xe1ticamente el aprovisionamiento, la aplicaci\xf3n de parches, las copias de seguridad y la alta disponibilidad.\\nAWS Batch es un servicio totalmente administrado para ejecutar trabajos de computaci\xf3n por lotes a gran escala en recursos de c\xf3mputo aprovisionados din\xe1micamente, y no tiene capacidad para alojar bases de datos. AWS Artifact es un portal de autoservicio que proporciona acceso bajo demanda a los informes de cumplimiento y la documentaci\xf3n de seguridad de AWS, y no tiene relaci\xf3n alguna con el alojamiento de bases de datos. AWS Data Pipeline (obsoleto) es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n para programar y automatizar el movimiento y la transformaci\xf3n de datos entre servicios de AWS y fuentes locales, y no es un servicio para alojar o administrar bases de datos."},{"id":"q119","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona una soluci\xf3n de almacenamiento de archivos compartidos simple y escalable para usar con servidores de AWS basados en Linux y servidores locales?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier","C":"Amazon EBS","D":"Amazon EFS"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Elastic File System (EFS) es un sistema de archivos NFS administrado que pueden montar simult\xe1neamente varias instancias EC2 de Linux y al que se puede acceder desde servidores locales, lo que proporciona almacenamiento de archivos compartidos simple y escalable.\\nAmazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos dise\xf1ado para almacenar y recuperar archivos mediante API HTTP, y no proporciona un sistema de archivos compartidos montable para servidores Linux. Amazon S3 Glacier es una clase de almacenamiento de archivo dentro de Amazon S3 dise\xf1ada para la retenci\xf3n de datos a largo plazo con acceso poco frecuente, y no tiene capacidad de sistema de archivos compartidos. Amazon Elastic Block Store (EBS) proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento a nivel de bloque que se conectan a una sola instancia EC2 a la vez, y no se pueden compartir entre varios servidores simult\xe1neamente."},{"id":"q122","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 tipo de almacenamiento de AWS es ef\xedmero y se elimina cuando una instancia se detiene o se termina?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon EC2 instance store","C":"Amazon EFS","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"B","explanation":"EC2 instance store proporciona almacenamiento en bloques ef\xedmero que est\xe1 conectado f\xedsicamente a la m\xe1quina host y se elimina de forma permanente cuando la instancia se detiene o se termina, por lo que solo es adecuado para datos temporales.\\nLos vol\xfamenes de Amazon EBS son almacenamiento en bloques persistente que existe independientemente del ciclo de vida de la instancia, por lo que los datos se conservan incluso despu\xe9s de que la instancia se detiene o se termina. Amazon EFS es un sistema de archivos compartido persistente que permanece disponible en varias instancias y no est\xe1 vinculado al ciclo de vida de ninguna instancia individual. Amazon S3 es almacenamiento de objetos persistente que existe independientemente de cualquier instancia de c\xf3mputo y conserva los datos hasta que se eliminan expl\xedcitamente."},{"id":"q124","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios forman parte de la plataforma sin servidor de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2, Amazon S3, Amazon Athena","B":"Amazon Kinesis, Amazon SQS, Amazon EMR","C":"AWS Step Functions, Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon SNS","D":"Amazon Athena, Amazon Cognito, Amazon EC2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Step Functions, Amazon DynamoDB y Amazon SNS son servicios totalmente sin servidor que no requieren aprovisionamiento de servidores y escalan autom\xe1ticamente, lo que convierte a este en el \xfanico grupo compuesto completamente por servicios sin servidor.\\nAmazon EC2 requiere que los clientes aprovisionen y administren instancias de servidores virtuales, y aunque Amazon S3 y Athena son sin servidor, EC2 descalifica a este grupo. Amazon EMR requiere aprovisionar y administrar cl\xfasteres de servidores para el procesamiento de macrodatos, lo que descalifica a este grupo aunque Kinesis y SQS sean sin servidor. Amazon EC2 no es un servicio sin servidor, lo que descalifica a este grupo independientemente de si Athena y Cognito califican individualmente."},{"id":"q127","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona la capacidad de administrar infraestructura como c\xf3digo?","options":{"A":"AWS CodePipeline","B":"AWS CodeDeploy","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS CloudFormation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS CloudFormation permite definir la infraestructura de AWS en plantillas JSON o YAML, lo que habilita el aprovisionamiento repetible y automatizado de entornos completos mediante infraestructura como c\xf3digo.\\nAWS CodePipeline automatiza las etapas de una canalizaci\xf3n de lanzamiento de software, incluidas las fases de origen, compilaci\xf3n e implementaci\xf3n, pero no aprovisiona ni administra la infraestructura de AWS mediante plantillas. AWS CodeDeploy automatiza la implementaci\xf3n del c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n en instancias EC2, funciones Lambda y servidores locales, y no administra el aprovisionamiento de infraestructura. AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, y es un servicio de redes totalmente ajeno a la infraestructura como c\xf3digo."},{"id":"q129","question":"Un cliente necesita ejecutar una base de datos MySQL que escale f\xe1cilmente. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon Aurora","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Aurora es una base de datos relacional compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL creada para la nube que escala autom\xe1ticamente el almacenamiento y puede manejar un alto rendimiento, lo que la convierte en la opci\xf3n espec\xedficamente dise\xf1ada para una carga de trabajo MySQL que necesita escalar f\xe1cilmente.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos administrado optimizado para ejecutar consultas SQL anal\xedticas en grandes conjuntos de datos, y no es un motor de base de datos relacional compatible con cargas de trabajo MySQL. Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL de clave-valor y de documentos totalmente administrada, y no admite MySQL ni modelos de datos relacionales. Amazon ElastiCache es un servicio de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria administrado dise\xf1ado para acelerar el rendimiento de las aplicaciones mediante el almacenamiento de datos de acceso frecuente en memoria, y no es una base de datos relacional capaz de ejecutar cargas de trabajo MySQL."},{"id":"q134","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se debe utilizar para el almacenamiento a largo plazo y de bajo costo de copias de seguridad de datos?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier","C":"AWS Snowball","D":"Amazon EBS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon S3 Glacier est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para el archivado y la copia de seguridad de datos a largo plazo con costos de almacenamiento muy bajos, lo que lo convierte en el servicio creado espec\xedficamente para conservar copias de seguridad de forma econ\xf3mica durante per\xedodos prolongados.\\nAmazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado para cargas de trabajo transaccionales activas, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para almacenar archivos de copias de seguridad de acceso poco frecuente a bajo costo. AWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico de transporte de datos que se utiliza para migrar grandes vol\xfamenes de datos hacia o desde AWS, y es una herramienta de migraci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio de almacenamiento a largo plazo. Amazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque persistentes conectados a instancias EC2 para acceso a disco de baja latencia, y es significativamente m\xe1s costoso por gigabyte que S3 Glacier para almacenar datos de copia de seguridad a los que se accede con poca frecuencia."},{"id":"q138","question":"\xbfPara qu\xe9 se pueden utilizar las ubicaciones de borde de AWS?","options":{"A":"Alojamiento de aplicaciones","B":"Entrega de contenido m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios","C":"Ejecuci\xf3n de servicios de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de bases de datos NoSQL","D":"Reducci\xf3n del tr\xe1fico en el servidor mediante el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de respuestas","E":"Env\xedo de mensajes de notificaci\xf3n a los usuarios finales"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Las ubicaciones de borde entregan contenido almacenado en cach\xe9 geogr\xe1ficamente cerca de los usuarios finales para reducir la latencia, y absorben solicitudes repetidas al servir respuestas almacenadas en cach\xe9 para que lleguen menos solicitudes al servidor de origen.\\nEl alojamiento de aplicaciones: los recursos de c\xf3mputo para ejecutar aplicaciones son proporcionados por instancias EC2 y otros servicios en Regiones de AWS y Zonas de disponibilidad, no en ubicaciones de borde. La ejecuci\xf3n de servicios de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de bases de datos NoSQL: los servicios administrados de base de datos y almacenamiento en cach\xe9, como DynamoDB y ElastiCache, se ejecutan en Regiones, no en ubicaciones de borde. El env\xedo de mensajes de notificaci\xf3n a usuarios finales: la entrega de mensajes a usuarios finales la manejan servicios como Amazon SNS, que opera desde endpoints regionales, no desde ubicaciones de borde."},{"id":"q149","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio cuyo prop\xf3sito PRINCIPAL es el control de versiones de software?","options":{"A":"AWS CodeBuild","B":"AWS Command Line Interface (AWS CLI)","C":"Amazon Cognito","D":"AWS CodeCommit"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS CodeCommit es un servicio de control de c\xf3digo fuente basado en Git totalmente administrado dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para el control de versiones de software, lo que permite a los equipos almacenar y administrar de forma segura sus repositorios de c\xf3digo.\\nAWS CodeBuild es un servicio de compilaci\xf3n totalmente administrado que compila c\xf3digo fuente y ejecuta pruebas para producir paquetes listos para implementar, pero su prop\xf3sito principal es compilar y probar c\xf3digo, no almacenarlo ni versionarlo. AWS CLI es una herramienta de l\xednea de comandos para interactuar con los servicios y recursos de AWS y administrarlos, y no es un servicio de control de c\xf3digo fuente ni de gesti\xf3n de versiones. Amazon Cognito es un servicio de identidad y autenticaci\xf3n de usuarios para aplicaciones web y m\xf3viles, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con el control de versiones de software."},{"id":"q151","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes componentes de la infraestructura global de AWS consiste en uno o m\xe1s centros de datos discretos interconectados mediante enlaces de baja latencia?","options":{"A":"Zona de disponibilidad","B":"Ubicaci\xf3n de borde","C":"Regi\xf3n","D":"Redes privadas"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Una Zona de disponibilidad consta de uno o m\xe1s centros de datos discretos con energ\xeda y red independientes, interconectados con otras AZ dentro de la misma Regi\xf3n mediante enlaces de baja latencia.\\nLas ubicaciones de borde son puntos de presencia separados que se utilizan para la entrega y el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de contenido, no cl\xfasteres de centros de datos con infraestructura redundante. Una Regi\xf3n es el \xe1rea geogr\xe1fica m\xe1s amplia que contiene varias Zonas de disponibilidad, no el cl\xfaster de centros de datos en s\xed. Las redes privadas no son un componente de la jerarqu\xeda de infraestructura global de AWS."},{"id":"q154","question":"Una caracter\xedstica de las ubicaciones de borde es que:","options":{"A":"Alojan instancias de Amazon EC2 m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios","B":"Ayudan a reducir la latencia y mejorar el rendimiento para los usuarios","C":"Almacenan en cach\xe9 datos que cambian con frecuencia sin llegar al servidor de origen","D":"Actualizan los cambios de datos diariamente"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las ubicaciones de borde almacenan en cach\xe9 contenido geogr\xe1ficamente cerca de los usuarios finales, lo que reduce la distancia de ida y vuelta y disminuye la latencia para mejorar el rendimiento de las aplicaciones globales.\\nAlojan instancias de Amazon EC2 m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios. Las instancias EC2 se ejecutan en Regiones y AZ, no en ubicaciones de borde. Almacenan en cach\xe9 datos que cambian con frecuencia sin llegar al servidor de origen. Las ubicaciones de borde almacenan en cach\xe9 contenido est\xe1tico o que cambia con poca frecuencia, no datos que cambian con frecuencia. Actualizan los cambios de datos diariamente. Las ubicaciones de borde sirven contenido almacenado en cach\xe9 seg\xfan la configuraci\xf3n de TTL, no seg\xfan un ciclo fijo de actualizaci\xf3n diaria."},{"id":"q164","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio proporciona un servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido que permite a las aplicaciones locales usar sin problemas el almacenamiento en la nube?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Glacier","B":"AWS Snowball","C":"AWS Storage Gateway","D":"Amazon Elastic Block Storage (Amazon EBS)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Storage Gateway es un servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido que integra entornos locales con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS, presentando el almacenamiento en la nube como recursos compartidos de archivos, vol\xfamenes o cintas locales a las aplicaciones locales.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier es una clase de almacenamiento de archivo dentro de Amazon S3 dise\xf1ada para la retenci\xf3n de datos a largo plazo con acceso poco frecuente, y no proporciona una capa de integraci\xf3n de almacenamiento h\xedbrido para aplicaciones locales. AWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico de transferencia de datos que se utiliza para mover grandes vol\xfamenes de datos hacia o desde AWS, y es una herramienta de migraci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido continuo. Amazon Elastic Block Store (EBS) proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento a nivel de bloque que se conectan a instancias EC2 dentro de AWS, y las aplicaciones locales no pueden acceder directamente a ellos como una extensi\xf3n perfecta del almacenamiento en la nube."},{"id":"q166","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un componente de la infraestructura global de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon Alexa","B":"Regiones de AWS","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las Regiones de AWS son los componentes f\xedsicos y geogr\xe1ficamente distribuidos de la infraestructura global de AWS, y cada una contiene varias Zonas de disponibilidad que proporcionan redundancia y aislamiento de fallas.\\nAmazon Alexa es un servicio de asistente de voz que permite interacciones conversacionales con aplicaciones, y no es un componente de la infraestructura f\xedsica que sustenta AWS. Amazon Lightsail es un servicio de c\xf3mputo simplificado dise\xf1ado para desarrolladores que necesitan una forma f\xe1cil de lanzar servidores virtuales y aplicaciones, y no es un componente de infraestructura. AWS Organizations es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de cuentas que permite la gobernanza y la facturaci\xf3n centralizadas en varias cuentas de AWS, y no forma parte de la infraestructura global de AWS."},{"id":"q168","question":"Una empresa desea migrar sus aplicaciones a una VPC en AWS. Estas aplicaciones necesitar\xe1n acceder a recursos locales. \xbfQu\xe9 combinaci\xf3n de acciones permitir\xe1 a la empresa lograr este objetivo?","options":{"A":"Usar AWS Service Catalog para identificar una lista de recursos locales que se pueden migrar","B":"Construir una conexi\xf3n VPN entre un dispositivo local y una puerta de enlace privada virtual en la nueva VPC","C":"Usar Amazon Athena para consultar datos de los servidores de bases de datos locales","D":"Conectar el centro de datos local de la empresa a AWS mediante AWS Direct Connect","E":"Aprovechar Amazon CloudFront para restringir el acceso al contenido web est\xe1tico proporcionado a trav\xe9s de los servidores web locales de la empresa"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Una VPN de sitio a sitio a trav\xe9s de una puerta de enlace privada virtual proporciona un t\xfanel cifrado por Internet entre el entorno local y la VPC, y AWS Direct Connect proporciona un enlace de red privado dedicado; ambos extienden la red local hacia AWS.\\nAWS Service Catalog es una herramienta para administrar y aprovisionar portafolios aprobados de servicios de TI dentro de una organizaci\xf3n, y no tiene la capacidad de identificar ni conectar recursos locales a una VPC. Amazon Athena es un servicio de consultas sin servidor que analiza datos almacenados en Amazon S3, y no puede consultar datos directamente desde servidores de bases de datos locales. Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y distribuye contenido a nivel global, y no es un mecanismo para restringir ni enrutar el acceso entre servidores locales y una VPC."},{"id":"q172","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes caracter\xedsticas de AWS permite a un usuario lanzar una instancia de Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) preconfigurada?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)","B":"Amazon Machine Image","C":"Amazon EC2 Systems Manager","D":"Amazon AppStream 2.0"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Una Amazon Machine Image es una plantilla preconfigurada que contiene el sistema operativo, el servidor de aplicaciones y el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n que se utiliza para lanzar una nueva instancia de EC2 en un estado coherente y conocido.\\nAmazon EBS es un servicio de almacenamiento de bloques que proporciona vol\xfamenes persistentes para adjuntar a las instancias de EC2, no una plantilla para lanzar instancias preconfiguradas. AWS Systems Manager proporciona herramientas operativas para gestionar, aplicar parches y automatizar tareas en instancias de EC2 en ejecuci\xf3n, pero no lanza instancias preconfiguradas. Amazon AppStream 2.0 es un servicio de transmisi\xf3n de aplicaciones que entrega aplicaciones de escritorio a los usuarios a trav\xe9s de un navegador, completamente ajeno a la configuraci\xf3n de instancias de EC2."},{"id":"q178","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo ayudan los grupos de Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling a lograr alta disponibilidad para una aplicaci\xf3n web?","options":{"A":"Agregan autom\xe1ticamente m\xe1s instancias en varias Regiones de AWS seg\xfan la demanda global de la aplicaci\xf3n","B":"Agregan o reemplazan autom\xe1ticamente instancias en varias Zonas de Disponibilidad cuando la aplicaci\xf3n lo necesita","C":"Permiten que el contenido est\xe1tico de la aplicaci\xf3n resida m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios finales","D":"Pueden distribuir las solicitudes entrantes entre un nivel de instancias de servidor web"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los grupos de EC2 Auto Scaling abarcan varias Zonas de Disponibilidad y agregan o reemplazan autom\xe1ticamente instancias cuando las existentes fallan o dejan de estar saludables, manteniendo la capacidad deseada y permitiendo alta disponibilidad.\\nLos grupos de Auto Scaling operan dentro de una sola Regi\xf3n en varias Zonas de Disponibilidad y no agregan autom\xe1ticamente instancias en varias Regiones en respuesta a la demanda global. Permitir que el contenido est\xe1tico resida m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios finales describe una red de entrega de contenido como Amazon CloudFront, no una capacidad de los grupos de EC2 Auto Scaling. Distribuir las solicitudes entrantes entre un nivel de instancias de servidor web describe la funci\xf3n de un balanceador de carga como Application Load Balancer, que es un servicio separado que funciona junto con los grupos de Auto Scaling pero es distinto de ellos."},{"id":"q184","question":"Una empresa busca una soluci\xf3n de almac\xe9n de datos escalable. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes soluciones de AWS satisfar\xeda las necesidades de la empresa?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3)","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon Kinesis","D":"Amazon Redshift"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos totalmente administrado y a escala de petabytes dise\xf1ado para ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas complejas en grandes conjuntos de datos, lo que lo convierte en la soluci\xf3n espec\xedficamente dise\xf1ada para el almacenamiento de datos escalable.\\nAmazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos para almacenar y recuperar cualquier tipo de datos, no un almac\xe9n de datos para ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas. Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL de clave-valor y de documentos optimizada para cargas de trabajo transaccionales de alta velocidad, no para consultas anal\xedticas complejas en grandes conjuntos de datos. Amazon Kinesis es un servicio para recopilar, procesar y analizar datos de transmisi\xf3n en tiempo real, no para almacenar y consultar conjuntos de datos hist\xf3ricos."},{"id":"q185","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS proporcionan una forma de extender una arquitectura local a la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"Amazon CloudFront","D":"AWS Storage Gateway","E":"Amazon Connect"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, y AWS Storage Gateway integra entornos de almacenamiento local con almacenamiento en la nube, y ambos extienden directamente una arquitectura local a la nube de AWS.\\nAmazon EBS es un servicio de almacenamiento de bloques para instancias de EC2 que opera completamente dentro de AWS y no tiene funci\xf3n de conectividad local. Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y distribuye contenido a nivel global, pero no conecta ni extiende redes locales a AWS. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube para gestionar las comunicaciones con los clientes y no est\xe1 relacionado con la conectividad de red h\xedbrida o de almacenamiento."},{"id":"q192","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 considerando usar AWS para una base de datos autohospedada que requiere un apagado nocturno para fines de mantenimiento y ahorro de costos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio deber\xeda usar la empresa?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) with Amazon EC2 instance store","D":"Amazon EC2 with Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"EC2 con EBS es la opci\xf3n correcta porque los vol\xfamenes de EBS persisten de forma independiente de la instancia. Cuando la instancia se detiene cada noche, los datos en EBS se conservan.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos totalmente administrado optimizado para consultas SQL anal\xedticas en grandes conjuntos de datos y, como servicio administrado, no se puede detener con una programaci\xf3n nocturna de la forma en que se puede hacer con una instancia de EC2, por lo que no es adecuado para una carga de trabajo con requisito de apagado nocturno. Amazon DynamoDB es un servicio de base de datos NoSQL totalmente administrado que escala autom\xe1ticamente y opera de forma continua como servicio administrado, y no se puede detener seg\xfan una programaci\xf3n para fines de mantenimiento o ahorro de costos. Amazon EC2 con Amazon EC2 instance store proporciona almacenamiento de bloques temporal que est\xe1 f\xedsicamente conectado a la m\xe1quina host, y este almacenamiento se pierde cuando la instancia se detiene, lo que lo hace inadecuado para una base de datos que requiere persistencia de datos entre apagados nocturnos."},{"id":"q194","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio proporciona almacenamiento a nivel de objeto en AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon Instance Store","C":"Amazon EFS","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon S3 es el servicio de almacenamiento de objetos de AWS, donde cada elemento se almacena como un objeto que contiene datos, metadatos y una clave \xfanica dentro de un bucket, lo que lo hace adecuado para almacenar cualquier tipo de archivo a una escala pr\xe1cticamente ilimitada.\\nAmazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento de bloques que se adjuntan a las instancias de EC2 para usarse como discos persistentes, y opera a nivel de bloque en lugar de a nivel de objeto. Amazon Instance Store proporciona almacenamiento de bloques temporal conectado f\xedsicamente al servidor host, que se pierde cuando la instancia se detiene y no es almacenamiento a nivel de objeto. Amazon EFS proporciona un sistema de almacenamiento de archivos administrado que se puede montar en varias instancias de EC2 simult\xe1neamente, y opera a nivel de archivo en lugar de a nivel de objeto."},{"id":"q195","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 preocupada porque est\xe1 gastando dinero en recursos de c\xf3mputo subutilizados en AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de AWS ayudar\xe1 a garantizar que sus aplicaciones agreguen o quiten autom\xe1ticamente capacidad de c\xf3mputo de EC2 para ajustarse estrechamente a la demanda requerida?","options":{"A":"AWS Elastic Load Balancer","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"AWS Auto Scaling","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Auto Scaling monitorea las m\xe9tricas de la aplicaci\xf3n y ajusta autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias de EC2 para satisfacer la demanda, lo que garantiza que no est\xe9 pagando por capacidad inactiva.\\nAWS Elastic Load Balancer distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre varios destinos, como instancias de EC2, para mejorar la disponibilidad y la tolerancia a fallos, pero no ajusta el n\xfamero de instancias en respuesta a cambios en la demanda. AWS Budgets le permite establecer umbrales de costo y uso y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, y aunque puede alertarle sobre el gasto excesivo, no puede por s\xed mismo agregar o quitar instancias de EC2 para satisfacer la demanda requerida. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaci\xf3n y an\xe1lisis de los patrones de gasto de AWS a lo largo del tiempo, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de costos en lugar de un servicio que ajusta autom\xe1ticamente la capacidad de c\xf3mputo."},{"id":"q196","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 clase de almacenamiento de S3 es mejor para datos con patrones de acceso impredecibles?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval","C":"Amazon S3 Standard","D":"Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access"},"correct":"A","explanation":"S3 Intelligent-Tiering mueve autom\xe1ticamente los objetos entre niveles de acceso seg\xfan patrones de acceso cambiantes, lo que optimiza los costos cuando la frecuencia de acceso es impredecible sin incurrir en cargos por recuperaci\xf3n.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para datos de archivo a los que se accede con poca frecuencia y que pueden tolerar tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n que van de minutos a horas, lo que lo hace inadecuado para datos con necesidades de acceso impredecibles pero potencialmente frecuentes. Amazon S3 Standard est\xe1 optimizado para datos a los que se accede con frecuencia y cobra un precio de almacenamiento m\xe1s alto, lo que lo hace ineficiente en costos para datos con patrones impredecibles que pueden incluir largos per\xedodos de inactividad. Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para datos a los que se accede con menos frecuencia pero que requieren una recuperaci\xf3n r\xe1pida cuando es necesario, y genera cargos por recuperaci\xf3n que lo hacen costoso cuando los patrones de acceso son impredecibles."},{"id":"q197","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio de base de datos de AWS que permite cargar datos estructurados en formato clave-valor?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon Aurora","C":"Amazon Redshift","D":"Amazon RDS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL totalmente administrada que admite de forma nativa los modelos de datos clave-valor y de documentos, lo que la hace ideal para cargar y recuperar datos estructurados como pares clave-valor.\\nAmazon Aurora es un motor de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL que utiliza esquemas SQL estructurados y no admite el modelo de datos flexible clave-valor que ofrece DynamoDB. Amazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos totalmente administrado dise\xf1ado para ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas complejas sobre grandes vol\xfamenes de datos estructurados, y no es una base de datos clave-valor. Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional administrado que admite m\xfaltiples motores SQL y utiliza esquemas estructurados basados en tablas en lugar del modelo de datos clave-valor."},{"id":"q202","question":"Trabajas como DBA de MySQL on-premises. El trabajo de configuraci\xf3n de bases de datos, copias de seguridad, aplicaci\xf3n de parches y DR puede consumir mucho tiempo y ser repetitivo. Tu empresa ha decidido migrar a la nube de AWS. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones puede ayudar a ahorrar tiempo en el mantenimiento de la base de datos para que puedas centrarte en la arquitectura de datos y el rendimiento?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado en el que AWS se encarga de la configuraci\xf3n, las copias de seguridad, la aplicaci\xf3n de parches y el failover, lo que libera al DBA para centrarse en la arquitectura de datos y el rendimiento.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos dise\xf1ado para an\xe1lisis, no para cargas de trabajo de bases de datos relacionales transaccionales. Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL clave-valor y no ser\xeda un reemplazo equivalente para una carga de trabajo relacional MySQL. Amazon CloudWatch es un servicio de monitoreo y observabilidad, no un servicio de base de datos."},{"id":"q205","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio se utiliza para garantizar que los mensajes entre componentes de software no se pierdan si uno o m\xe1s componentes fallan?","options":{"A":"Amazon SQS","B":"Amazon SES","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Amazon Connect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SQS es un servicio de colas de mensajes totalmente administrado que almacena mensajes incluso cuando el componente consumidor no est\xe1 disponible, lo que evita la p\xe9rdida de mensajes en arquitecturas distribuidas.\\nAmazon SES es un servicio de env\xedo y recepci\xf3n de correo electr\xf3nico dise\xf1ado para comunicaciones transaccionales y de marketing, y no encola mensajes entre componentes de software para evitar p\xe9rdidas durante fallos. AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en el almacenamiento o la gesti\xf3n de mensajes entre componentes de aplicaciones. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto en la nube para gestionar comunicaciones con clientes mediante voz y chat, y no es un servicio de colas de mensajes ni de integraci\xf3n de aplicaciones."},{"id":"q207","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio de AWS que proporciona una red virtual dedicada a tu cuenta de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"AWS Subnets","C":"AWS Dedicated Hosts","D":"Amazon VPC"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon VPC (Virtual Private Cloud) crea una red virtual privada y l\xf3gicamente aislada dedicada a tu cuenta de AWS, donde controlas el direccionamiento IP, las subredes, el enrutamiento y las puertas de enlace.\\nAWS VPN establece una conexi\xf3n cifrada a trav\xe9s de Internet p\xfablica entre tu red on-premises y una VPC existente, pero es una funci\xf3n de conectividad, no la red virtual en s\xed. AWS Subnets son subdivisiones del rango de direcciones IP de una VPC que se utilizan para organizar recursos dentro de una VPC, no un servicio de red independiente. AWS Dedicated Hosts son servidores f\xedsicos con capacidad de instancias EC2 asignada exclusivamente a tu cuenta para cargas de trabajo de computaci\xf3n, no un componente de red ni de red virtual."},{"id":"q209","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS se puede utilizar como recurso de computaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon EC2","E":"AWS Lambda"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"Amazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales redimensionables que ejecutan aplicaciones en infraestructura de computaci\xf3n administrada, y AWS Lambda ejecuta c\xf3digo como funciones serverless en respuesta a eventos, por lo que ambos son recursos de computaci\xf3n.\\nAmazon VPC es un servicio de red que proporciona entornos de red l\xf3gicamente aislados dentro de AWS y no ejecuta c\xf3digo ni proporciona capacidad de computaci\xf3n por s\xed mismo. Amazon CloudWatch es un servicio de monitoreo y observabilidad que recopila m\xe9tricas, logs y eventos de los recursos de AWS, y no tiene capacidad de ejecuci\xf3n de computaci\xf3n. Amazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos dise\xf1ado para almacenar y recuperar datos, y no proporciona ninguna funcionalidad de computaci\xf3n ni de ejecuci\xf3n de c\xf3digo."},{"id":"q210","question":"Tu empresa est\xe1 dise\xf1ando una nueva aplicaci\xf3n que almacenar\xe1 y recuperar\xe1 fotos y videos. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios deber\xedas recomendar como mecanismo de almacenamiento subyacente?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon SQS","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon EC2 Instance Store"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para almacenar y recuperar cualquier cantidad de datos no estructurados, incluidas fotos y videos, con alta durabilidad y escalabilidad, lo que lo convierte en la soluci\xf3n de almacenamiento de objetos espec\xedficamente dise\xf1ada para este caso de uso.\\nAmazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloques persistentes que se adjuntan directamente a las instancias EC2, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para acceso a disco de baja latencia en lugar de almacenar y servir grandes vol\xfamenes de objetos multimedia no estructurados. Amazon SQS es un servicio de colas de mensajes totalmente administrado para desacoplar componentes de aplicaciones, y es un servicio de mensajer\xeda sin capacidad para almacenar o recuperar archivos. Amazon EC2 Instance Store proporciona almacenamiento en bloques temporal conectado f\xedsicamente a la m\xe1quina host que ejecuta una instancia EC2, y es almacenamiento ef\xedmero que se pierde cuando la instancia se detiene, lo que lo hace totalmente inadecuado para el almacenamiento persistente de fotos y videos."},{"id":"q212","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 proporciona Amazon ElastiCache?","options":{"A":"Cach\xe9 en memoria para aplicaciones intensivas en lectura","B":"Un servicio de base de datos relacional administrado","C":"Una tienda de software en l\xednea que permite a los clientes lanzar software preconfigurado con solo unos clics","D":"Un sistema de nombres de dominio en la nube"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon ElastiCache proporciona cach\xe9 en memoria mediante los motores Redis o Memcached, almacenando datos de acceso frecuente en RAM y ofreciendo latencia de lectura inferior al milisegundo para aplicaciones intensivas en lectura.\\nUn servicio de base de datos relacional administrado describe Amazon RDS, que gestiona datos estructurados con consultas basadas en SQL en lugar de cach\xe9 en memoria. Una tienda de software en l\xednea que permite a los clientes lanzar software preconfigurado describe AWS Marketplace, no un servicio de cach\xe9. Un sistema de nombres de dominio en la nube describe Amazon Route 53, que gestiona el enrutamiento DNS y el registro de dominios en lugar de cach\xe9 de datos en memoria."},{"id":"q217","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para establecer una conexi\xf3n de red privada y dedicada entre AWS y tu centro de datos?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"Amazon CloudFront","C":"AWS Snowball","D":"Amazon Route 53"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red f\xedsica privada y dedicada desde tu centro de datos on-premises hacia AWS, evitando Internet p\xfablica para ofrecer un ancho de banda m\xe1s constante, menor latencia y mayor confiabilidad para cargas de trabajo de nube h\xedbrida.\\nAmazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y distribuye contenido web a los usuarios finales desde ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo, y es un servicio de entrega de contenido en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de conectividad de red privada para centros de datos. AWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico de transferencia de datos que se utiliza para mover grandes vol\xfamenes de datos hacia o desde AWS mediante el env\xedo del dispositivo, dise\xf1ado para transferencias masivas de datos \xfanicas o peri\xf3dicas en lugar de conectividad de red privada continua. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio DNS escalable que traduce nombres de dominio a direcciones IP y admite pol\xedticas de enrutamiento, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de DNS sin capacidad para establecer enlaces de red dedicados entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS."},{"id":"q218","question":"Est\xe1s trabajando en dos proyectos que requieren configuraciones de red completamente diferentes. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS te permitir\xe1 aislar recursos y configuraciones de red?","options":{"A":"Puertas de enlace de Internet","B":"Virtual Private Cloud","C":"Security Groups","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon VPC te permite aprovisionar entornos de red l\xf3gicamente aislados dentro de AWS con control total sobre rangos de IP, subredes, tablas de enrutamiento y puertas de enlace, lo que te permite ejecutar configuraciones de red completamente separadas para diferentes proyectos.\\nLas puertas de enlace de Internet permiten el acceso a Internet de entrada y salida dentro de una VPC, pero son un componente de una VPC en lugar de un mecanismo para aislar configuraciones de red completas. Los grupos de seguridad controlan el tr\xe1fico de entrada y salida a nivel de instancia y operan dentro de una VPC, por lo que filtran el tr\xe1fico, pero no proporcionan aislamiento a nivel de red entre proyectos. Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido para almacenar en cach\xe9 y distribuir contenido globalmente, sin relaci\xf3n con el aislamiento de red o la gesti\xf3n de configuraciones."},{"id":"q220","question":"Una organizaci\xf3n necesita analizar y procesar una gran cantidad de conjuntos de datos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda utilizar?","options":{"A":"Amazon EMR","B":"Amazon MQ","C":"Amazon SNS","D":"Amazon SQS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon EMR es una plataforma de big data administrada que ejecuta frameworks de procesamiento distribuido como Apache Hadoop y Spark, lo que la convierte en el servicio dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para analizar y procesar grandes vol\xfamenes de datos a escala.\\nAmazon MQ es un servicio de broker de mensajes administrado para aplicaciones que utilizan protocolos de mensajer\xeda de est\xe1ndar abierto como AMQP y MQTT, y es una herramienta de integraci\xf3n de mensajer\xeda sin capacidad de procesamiento o an\xe1lisis de datos. Amazon SNS es un servicio de notificaciones pub/sub totalmente administrado para entregar mensajes a suscriptores como funciones Lambda, direcciones de correo electr\xf3nico y endpoints HTTP, y no tiene capacidad para procesar o analizar grandes conjuntos de datos. Amazon SQS es un servicio de colas de mensajes totalmente administrado que desacopla los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n al mantener mensajes en una cola para su procesamiento as\xedncrono, y es una herramienta de integraci\xf3n de aplicaciones en lugar de un servicio de an\xe1lisis de datos."},{"id":"q222","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio de AWS que te proporciona el mayor nivel de control sobre la infraestructura virtual subyacente?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon EC2","D":"Amazon RDS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon EC2 brinda a los clientes el mayor nivel de control sobre la infraestructura virtual, incluida la elecci\xf3n del sistema operativo, el tipo de instancia, las redes, el almacenamiento y la capacidad de instalar y configurar cualquier software en la instancia subyacente.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos totalmente administrado en el que AWS abstrae y administra la infraestructura subyacente, lo que deja a los clientes responsables solo de sus datos y consultas en lugar de la propia infraestructura virtual. Amazon DynamoDB es un servicio de base de datos NoSQL totalmente administrado en el que AWS gestiona autom\xe1ticamente toda la infraestructura, las actualizaciones y el escalado, sin dar a los clientes visibilidad ni control sobre la infraestructura virtual subyacente. Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado en el que AWS administra el sistema operativo, las actualizaciones del motor de base de datos y la infraestructura subyacente, abstrayendo esas capas por completo del cliente."},{"id":"q224","question":"En tu entorno on-premises, puedes crear tantos servidores virtuales como necesites a partir de una \xfanica plantilla. \xbfQu\xe9 puedes usar para realizar lo mismo en AWS?","options":{"A":"IAM","B":"Una puerta de enlace de Internet","C":"EBS Snapshot","D":"AMI"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Una Amazon Machine Image es una plantilla que contiene el sistema operativo, el software de aplicaci\xf3n y la configuraci\xf3n necesarios para lanzar una instancia de EC2, directamente equivalente a la pr\xe1ctica on-premises de crear m\xfaltiples servidores virtuales a partir de una \xfanica plantilla.\\nIAM es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de identidades y accesos que controla los permisos y el acceso de los usuarios dentro de AWS, y no tiene ninguna funci\xf3n en la creaci\xf3n o el lanzamiento de instancias de servidores virtuales. Una puerta de enlace de Internet es un componente de VPC que permite la comunicaci\xf3n entre los recursos dentro de una VPC e Internet p\xfablica, y no est\xe1 relacionada con el aprovisionamiento de servidores ni con plantillas. Una instant\xe1nea de EBS es una copia de seguridad en un punto en el tiempo de un volumen de almacenamiento que se utiliza con fines de recuperaci\xf3n de datos, no una plantilla para lanzar nuevas instancias con un sistema operativo y una configuraci\xf3n definidos."},{"id":"q228","question":"AWS ha creado una gran cantidad de Edge Locations como parte de su infraestructura global. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes NO es un beneficio de usar Edge Locations?","options":{"A":"CloudFront utiliza las Edge Locations para almacenar en cach\xe9 las respuestas m\xe1s recientes","B":"CloudFront utiliza las Edge Locations para mejorar la experiencia de tus usuarios finales al cargar archivos","C":"CloudFront utiliza las Edge Locations para distribuir el tr\xe1fico entre varias instancias a fin de reducir la latencia","D":"CloudFront utiliza las Edge Locations para distribuir contenido a usuarios globales con baja latencia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las Edge Locations almacenan en cach\xe9 y entregan contenido con baja latencia a usuarios globales y tambi\xe9n mejoran el rendimiento de carga a trav\xe9s de servicios como S3 Transfer Acceleration, pero no distribuyen el tr\xe1fico entre varias instancias de c\xf3mputo. Distribuir el tr\xe1fico entre instancias es la funci\xf3n de Elastic Load Balancing, lo que hace que C sea la afirmaci\xf3n que no describe un beneficio de las Edge Locations.\\nAlmacenar en cach\xe9 las respuestas m\xe1s recientes es un beneficio real de las Edge Locations, ya que CloudFront almacena copias del contenido m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios finales para reducir las solicitudes al origen. Mejorar el rendimiento de carga para los usuarios finales es un beneficio real de las Edge Locations, ya que S3 Transfer Acceleration enruta las cargas a trav\xe9s de la Edge Location m\xe1s cercana para una transferencia m\xe1s r\xe1pida. Distribuir contenido a usuarios globales con baja latencia es el prop\xf3sito principal de las Edge Locations y un beneficio central de CloudFront."},{"id":"q230","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios te permite ejecutar aplicaciones en contenedores en un cl\xfaster de instancias EC2?","options":{"A":"Amazon ECS","B":"AWS Data Pipeline","C":"AWS Cloud9","D":"AWS Health Dashboard"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon ECS es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores totalmente administrado que te permite ejecutar y gestionar contenedores Docker en un cl\xfaster de instancias EC2 o utilizando AWS Fargate para c\xf3mputo sin servidor.\\nAWS Data Pipeline (obsoleto) es un servicio para orquestar y automatizar el movimiento y la transformaci\xf3n de datos entre servicios de AWS, no para ejecutar aplicaciones en contenedores. AWS Cloud9 es un entorno de desarrollo integrado basado en la nube para escribir y depurar c\xf3digo, sin relaci\xf3n con la orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores. AWS Health Dashboard proporciona alertas y orientaci\xf3n cuando los eventos de los servicios de AWS pueden afectar tus recursos, no es un servicio de c\xf3mputo o de contenedores."},{"id":"q232","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes procedimientos te ayudar\xe1 a reducir tus costos de Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"Usar pol\xedticas de ciclo de vida de S3 para mover archivos antiguos autom\xe1ticamente a Amazon S3 Glacier","B":"Usar la combinaci\xf3n correcta de clases de almacenamiento seg\xfan los diferentes casos de uso","C":"Elegir la zona de disponibilidad correcta para tu bucket de S3","D":"Mover todos los datos almacenados en S3 Standard a Amazon EBS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Usar la clase de almacenamiento de S3 correcta para cada caso de uso, como S3 Standard para datos de acceso frecuente, S3 Standard-Infrequent Access para acceso menos frecuente y S3 Glacier para archivo, es la forma m\xe1s eficaz de reducir los costos de almacenamiento de S3.\\nUsar pol\xedticas de ciclo de vida de S3 para transferir autom\xe1ticamente objetos antiguos a Amazon S3 Glacier reduce los costos de almacenamiento para datos archivados, pero se aplica solo a los objetos que califican para archivado en lugar de optimizar toda la gama de costos de almacenamiento de S3 en todos los casos de uso. S3 no cobra de manera diferente seg\xfan la zona de disponibilidad con la que est\xe9 asociado un bucket, y seleccionar una zona de disponibilidad diferente no tiene efecto en el precio de S3. Mover datos de S3 Standard a Amazon EBS aumentar\xeda los costos significativamente, ya que EBS es almacenamiento en bloques adjunto a instancias EC2 y cobra por gigabyte aprovisionado independientemente del uso real, lo que lo hace mucho m\xe1s costoso que S3 para el almacenamiento de objetos."},{"id":"q233","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son los servicios/caracter\xedsticas de AWS que pueden ayudarte a mantener una arquitectura altamente disponible y tolerante a fallos en AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"AWS Auto Scaling","C":"Elastic Load Balancer","D":"AWS CloudFormation","E":"Network ACLs"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS Auto Scaling agrega o elimina instancias autom\xe1ticamente seg\xfan la demanda, manteniendo la disponibilidad cuando hay picos de tr\xe1fico o fallan instancias. Elastic Load Balancer distribuye el tr\xe1fico entre instancias saludables y se integra con Auto Scaling para reemplazar las instancias no saludables, y juntos proporcionan una arquitectura altamente disponible y tolerante a fallos.\\nAWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS, y es un servicio de conectividad en lugar de una herramienta para mantener la disponibilidad de las aplicaciones o la tolerancia a fallos. AWS CloudFormation aprovisiona y gestiona la infraestructura mediante plantillas y, si bien puede implementar arquitecturas altamente disponibles, no mantiene por s\xed mismo la disponibilidad ni responde a fallos en tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n. Las Network ACLs controlan el tr\xe1fico de entrada y salida a nivel de subred con fines de seguridad, y no contribuyen a la disponibilidad ni a la tolerancia a fallos."},{"id":"q235","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio/caracter\xedstica de AWS que aprovecha las ubicaciones de borde distribuidas globalmente de Amazon CloudFront para transferir archivos a S3 con mayores velocidades de carga?","options":{"A":"S3 Transfer Acceleration","B":"AWS WAF","C":"AWS Snowmobile","D":"AWS Snowball"},"correct":"A","explanation":"S3 Transfer Acceleration utiliza las ubicaciones de borde distribuidas globalmente de CloudFront como puntos de entrada para las cargas a S3, enrutando los datos a trav\xe9s de la red backbone optimizada de AWS para llegar al bucket de S3 de destino m\xe1s r\xe1pido que una carga est\xe1ndar.\\nAWS WAF es un firewall de aplicaciones web que filtra y monitorea el tr\xe1fico HTTP para proteger aplicaciones web de exploits comunes, y no tiene capacidad de transferencia de archivos ni de aceleraci\xf3n de cargas. AWS Snowmobile es un servicio de transporte f\xedsico de datos que utiliza un dispositivo del tama\xf1o de un contenedor de env\xedo dise\xf1ado para migraciones a escala de exabytes, no para acelerar cargas en l\xednea a S3. AWS Snowball es un dispositivo de transferencia f\xedsica de datos usado para migraciones de datos offline a gran escala, y no aprovecha ubicaciones de borde ni acelera cargas basadas en internet."},{"id":"q237","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS se pueden usar para mejorar el rendimiento de una aplicaci\xf3n global y reducir la latencia para sus usuarios?","options":{"A":"AWS KMS","B":"AWS Global Accelerator","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS Glue","E":"Amazon CloudFront"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator mejora el rendimiento de aplicaciones globales al enrutar el tr\xe1fico de usuarios a trav\xe9s de la red backbone privada de AWS hacia el endpoint saludable m\xe1s cercano, reduciendo la latencia de internet, y Amazon CloudFront almacena en cach\xe9 y sirve contenido desde ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo, acercando el contenido a los usuarios y reduciendo la latencia para audiencias globales.\\nAWS KMS es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de claves de cifrado para crear y controlar claves criptogr\xe1ficas usadas para proteger datos, y no tiene capacidad para mejorar el rendimiento de aplicaciones ni reducir la latencia. AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para conectividad h\xedbrida en lugar de mejorar el rendimiento para usuarios finales globales. AWS Glue es un servicio ETL administrado para descubrir, preparar y transformar datos para cargas de trabajo anal\xedticas, y es un servicio de integraci\xf3n de datos sin capacidad de rendimiento de aplicaciones ni latencia."},{"id":"q239","question":"Una empresa tiene una gran cantidad de datos estructurados almacenados en su centro de datos local. Planean migrar todos los datos a AWS, \xbfcu\xe1l es la opci\xf3n de base de datos de AWS m\xe1s apropiada?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon SNS","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon RDS es el servicio apropiado para migrar datos relacionales estructurados, ya que admite m\xfaltiples motores SQL, incluidos MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server y MariaDB, en un entorno administrado que replica configuraciones de bases de datos relacionales locales.\\nAmazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL de clave-valor y de documentos dise\xf1ada para datos no estructurados o semiestructurados, lo que la hace inadecuada para migrar cargas de trabajo relacionales estructuradas. Amazon SNS es un servicio administrado de mensajer\xeda y notificaciones usado para comunicaci\xf3n pub/sub entre aplicaciones, y no tiene capacidad de base de datos. Amazon ElastiCache es un servicio de cach\xe9 en memoria usado para acelerar el rendimiento de aplicaciones, no una base de datos relacional para almacenar y migrar datos estructurados."},{"id":"q241","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio serverless de AWS que permite ejecutar tus aplicaciones sin ninguna carga administrativa?","options":{"A":"Amazon Lightsail","B":"AWS Lambda","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"Amazon EC2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo serverless que ejecuta tu c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin requerir que aprovisiones, administres o escales servidores o infraestructura, eliminando toda la carga administrativa.\\nAmazon Lightsail es un servicio de c\xf3mputo simplificado para implementar servidores virtuales, contenedores y aplicaciones web con configuraci\xf3n m\xednima, pero a\xfan implica aprovisionar y administrar recursos basados en servidores en lugar de ser un servicio verdaderamente serverless. Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado, pero administrado no significa serverless y los clientes a\xfan seleccionan y aprovisionan instancias de base de datos con recursos de c\xf3mputo asociados. Amazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales en la nube que requieren que aprovisiones, configures y administres las instancias subyacentes, lo que lo convierte en lo opuesto a un servicio serverless sin carga administrativa."},{"id":"q243","question":"\xbfD\xf3nde puedes almacenar archivos en AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon EFS","B":"Amazon SNS","C":"Amazon EBS","D":"Amazon ECS","E":"Amazon EMR"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Amazon EFS es un sistema de archivos NFS escalable y totalmente administrado que puede ser montado por m\xfaltiples instancias EC2 simult\xe1neamente, y Amazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque que se adjuntan a instancias EC2 individuales. Ambos son servicios de almacenamiento donde se pueden almacenar archivos en AWS.\\nAmazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda de publicaci\xf3n/suscripci\xf3n totalmente administrado usado para enviar notificaciones a suscriptores como funciones Lambda, endpoints HTTP y direcciones de correo electr\xf3nico, y es un servicio de mensajer\xeda en lugar de un servicio de almacenamiento de archivos. Amazon ECS es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores totalmente administrado para ejecutar aplicaciones en contenedores usando contenedores Docker, y es un servicio de c\xf3mputo para ejecutar cargas de trabajo de aplicaciones en lugar de un servicio de almacenamiento. Amazon EMR es una plataforma de big data administrada para procesar conjuntos de datos a gran escala usando frameworks como Apache Spark y Hadoop, y es un servicio de procesamiento de datos en lugar de un destino de almacenamiento para archivos."},{"id":"q244","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede usar para almacenar y entregar mensajes de forma confiable entre sistemas distribuidos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Queue Service","B":"AWS Storage Gateway","C":"Amazon Simple Email Service","D":"Amazon Simple Storage Service"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SQS es un servicio de colas de mensajes totalmente administrado que almacena mensajes de forma confiable hasta que se procesan, asegurando que no se pierda ning\xfan mensaje incluso si uno o m\xe1s componentes de un sistema distribuido fallan temporalmente.\\nAWS Storage Gateway conecta entornos locales con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS para casos de uso de almacenamiento h\xedbrido, y no tiene capacidad de colas ni de entrega de mensajes. Amazon Simple Email Service es un servicio en la nube de env\xedo y recepci\xf3n de correo electr\xf3nico dise\xf1ado para correos transaccionales y de marketing, no para poner en cola mensajes entre componentes de aplicaciones distribuidas. Amazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos para almacenar y recuperar archivos, y no proporciona colas de mensajes ni entrega garantizada entre sistemas distribuidos."},{"id":"q247","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio de almacenamiento principal usado por las instancias de base de datos de Amazon RDS?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Glacier","B":"Amazon EBS","C":"Amazon EFS","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las instancias de base de datos de Amazon RDS usan vol\xfamenes de Amazon EBS como su capa de almacenamiento principal, proporcionando almacenamiento de bloques persistente que persiste independientemente del ciclo de vida de la instancia de base de datos.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier es un servicio de almacenamiento de archivo dise\xf1ado para retenci\xf3n de datos a largo plazo a bajo costo y no se usa como almacenamiento activo para instancias de base de datos en ejecuci\xf3n. Amazon EFS es un servicio de sistema de archivos compartido que se puede montar en m\xfaltiples instancias EC2 y no es la capa de almacenamiento principal para RDS. Amazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos al que se accede mediante API y no se usa como almacenamiento a nivel de bloque para instancias de base de datos."},{"id":"q248","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 desarrollando una nueva aplicaci\xf3n usando un framework de microservicios. La nueva aplicaci\xf3n tiene problemas de rendimiento y latencia. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se debe usar para solucionar estos problemas?","options":{"A":"AWS CodePipeline","B":"AWS X-Ray","C":"Amazon Inspector","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS X-Ray proporciona trazabilidad distribuida para aplicaciones de microservicios, permitiendo a los desarrolladores visualizar la ruta de extremo a extremo de las solicitudes, identificar cuellos de botella y diagnosticar la causa ra\xedz de problemas de rendimiento y latencia.\\nAWS CodePipeline automatiza los pasos de una canalizaci\xf3n de entrega continua para liberar c\xf3digo de aplicaciones, incluidos la compilaci\xf3n, las pruebas y el despliegue, y es una herramienta de automatizaci\xf3n de CI/CD sin capacidad para rastrear solicitudes o diagnosticar problemas de rendimiento en tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n. Amazon Inspector es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad automatizado que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y es una herramienta de seguridad en lugar de un servicio de soluci\xf3n de problemas de rendimiento. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta en un entorno de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no rastrea solicitudes de aplicaciones ni proporciona visibilidad de cuellos de botella de latencia y rendimiento dentro de microservicios."},{"id":"q249","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS est\xe1 dise\xf1ado pensando en la tolerancia a fallos nativa Multi-AZ?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"AWS Snowball","C":"Amazon Simple Storage Service","D":"Amazon EBS","E":"Amazon DynamoDB"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 almacena datos de forma redundante en m\xfaltiples instalaciones dentro de una Regi\xf3n por defecto, proporcionando durabilidad Multi-AZ nativa sin ninguna configuraci\xf3n del cliente. Amazon DynamoDB replica autom\xe1ticamente los datos en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad dentro de una Regi\xf3n, haciendo que la tolerancia a fallos Multi-AZ sea una caracter\xedstica integrada del servicio.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de data warehouse que se implementa en una sola AZ por defecto y requiere configuraci\xf3n adicional, como implementaci\xf3n Multi-AZ o restauraci\xf3n desde snapshot, para lograr resiliencia entre AZ. AWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico de transferencia de datos usado para mover grandes cantidades de datos hacia o desde AWS y no tiene relevancia para la tolerancia a fallos Multi-AZ como servicio en la nube. Los vol\xfamenes de Amazon EBS est\xe1n confinados a una sola Zona de Disponibilidad por defecto y deben someterse a snapshot y restaurarse o replicarse expl\xedcitamente para lograr disponibilidad entre AZ."},{"id":"q250","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son las caracter\xedsticas de Amazon RDS que se pueden usar para mejorar la disponibilidad de su base de datos?","options":{"A":"AWS Regions","B":"Multi-AZ Deployment","C":"Aplicaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de parches","D":"Read Replicas","E":"Edge Locations"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Multi-AZ Deployment mantiene una r\xe9plica standby s\xedncrona en una Zona de Disponibilidad separada con conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica si la instancia primaria falla, y Read Replicas se pueden promover a una base de datos independiente para restaurar la disponibilidad en cargas de trabajo con mucha lectura; ambas son caracter\xedsticas de Amazon RDS que mejoran directamente la disponibilidad de la base de datos.\\nAWS Regions es un concepto de infraestructura geogr\xe1fica y no una caracter\xedstica dentro de Amazon RDS que se pueda habilitar para mejorar la disponibilidad de una instancia de base de datos existente. La aplicaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de parches es una caracter\xedstica de mantenimiento que mantiene actualizado el motor de la base de datos, reduciendo la carga administrativa, pero no contribuye a la disponibilidad durante fallos o interrupciones. Las ubicaciones de borde son puntos de presencia usados por CloudFront para el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 y la entrega de contenido, y no son un componente ni una caracter\xedstica de Amazon RDS de ninguna manera."},{"id":"q251","question":"Sarah ha implementado una aplicaci\xf3n en la regi\xf3n Northern California (us-west-1). Despu\xe9s de examinar el tr\xe1fico de la aplicaci\xf3n, nota que aproximadamente el 30% del tr\xe1fico proviene de Asia. \xbfQu\xe9 puede hacer para reducir la latencia para los usuarios en Asia?","options":{"A":"Replicar los recursos actuales en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad dentro de la misma regi\xf3n","B":"Migrar la aplicaci\xf3n a un proveedor de hosting en Asia","C":"Recrear el contenido del sitio web","D":"Crear una CDN usando CloudFront, para que el contenido se almacene en cach\xe9 en Edge Locations cercanas a Asia y en Asia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crear una distribuci\xf3n de CloudFront almacena en cach\xe9 el contenido en ubicaciones de borde cercanas a los usuarios asi\xe1ticos, reduciendo dr\xe1sticamente la latencia de ida y vuelta sin necesidad de mover o replicar la infraestructura de origen.\\nReplicar recursos en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad dentro de la misma Regi\xf3n mejora la disponibilidad, pero no reduce la latencia geogr\xe1fica para los usuarios en Asia. Migrar a un proveedor de hosting en Asia reducir\xeda la latencia, pero introduce una complejidad operativa significativa y no es una soluci\xf3n de AWS. Recrear el contenido del sitio web no tiene efecto en la latencia de red y no aborda la distancia geogr\xe1fica entre el origen y los usuarios finales."},{"id":"q256","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 tipos de datos son adecuados para almacenar en Amazon S3 Glacier, una clase de almacenamiento de Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"Archivos archivados activos","B":"Activos din\xe1micos de sitios web","C":"Datos anal\xedticos a largo plazo","D":"Registros activos de bases de datos","E":"Datos en cach\xe9"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 Glacier est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para datos a los que se accede con poca frecuencia pero que deben conservarse durante periodos largos, lo que hace que los archivos archivados activos conservados por cumplimiento normativo o referencia hist\xf3rica y los datos anal\xedticos a largo plazo retenidos para an\xe1lisis futuros sean los casos de uso principales.\\nLos activos din\xe1micos de sitios web requieren acceso r\xe1pido y frecuente con tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n de milisegundos, por lo que S3 Standard es la clase de almacenamiento adecuada en lugar del modelo de recuperaci\xf3n de archivado de Glacier. Las bases de datos activas requieren operaciones de lectura y escritura en tiempo real que son incompatibles con las demoras de recuperaci\xf3n de Glacier, por lo que servicios como RDS o DynamoDB son la opci\xf3n adecuada. Los datos en cach\xe9 requieren velocidades de acceso de submilisegundos para cumplir su prop\xf3sito de acelerar el rendimiento de las aplicaciones, por lo que un servicio como ElastiCache es la soluci\xf3n adecuada en lugar de una clase de almacenamiento de archivado."},{"id":"q257","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 proporciona Amazon Elastic Beanstalk?","options":{"A":"Una soluci\xf3n PaaS para automatizar el despliegue de aplicaciones","B":"Un motor de c\xf3mputo para Amazon ECS","C":"Una soluci\xf3n de almacenamiento de archivos escalable para usar con servidores de AWS y on-premises","D":"Un servicio de base de datos NoSQL"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk es una oferta de Plataforma como Servicio que gestiona autom\xe1ticamente el despliegue, el aprovisionamiento de capacidad, el balanceo de carga, el auto scaling y la supervisi\xf3n del estado de las aplicaciones, lo que permite a los desarrolladores cargar el c\xf3digo de su aplicaci\xf3n y hacer que la plataforma gestione la infraestructura.\\nAmazon ECS es el servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores que ejecuta contenedores Docker y es el motor de c\xf3mputo para cargas de trabajo en contenedores, pero no es una abstracci\xf3n que gestione autom\xe1ticamente el despliegue y la infraestructura para aplicaciones generales de la forma en que lo hace Beanstalk. Una soluci\xf3n de almacenamiento de archivos escalable describe Amazon EFS, que proporciona almacenamiento NFS compartido para instancias EC2; Elastic Beanstalk es una plataforma de despliegue y gesti\xf3n, no un servicio de almacenamiento. Un servicio de base de datos NoSQL describe Amazon DynamoDB, que proporciona almacenamiento clave-valor y de documentos totalmente gestionado; Elastic Beanstalk es una plataforma de despliegue en lugar de una base de datos."},{"id":"q265","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la herramienta de AWS que le permite usar scripts para gestionar todos los servicios y recursos de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Console","B":"AWS Service Catalog","C":"AWS OpsWorks","D":"AWS CLI"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS CLI es una herramienta unificada que permite a los clientes gestionar servicios de AWS y automatizar tareas mediante scripts y comandos de l\xednea de comandos, proporcionando acceso program\xe1tico a toda la gama de funcionalidades de AWS.\\nAWS Console es una interfaz gr\xe1fica basada en web para gestionar servicios de AWS a trav\xe9s de un navegador, y no admite automatizaci\xf3n basada en scripts ni interacci\xf3n por l\xednea de comandos. AWS Service Catalog es una herramienta para crear y gestionar portafolios aprobados de productos de TI dentro de una organizaci\xf3n, y no se utiliza para scripting ni para la gesti\xf3n directa de recursos. AWS OpsWorks (obsoleto) es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n que usa Chef y Puppet para automatizar la configuraci\xf3n y el despliegue de servidores, y no es una herramienta de scripting de prop\xf3sito general para gestionar todos los servicios de AWS."},{"id":"q266","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son las opciones de conectividad que se pueden usar para construir arquitecturas de nube h\xedbrida?","options":{"A":"AWS Artifact","B":"AWS Cloud9","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS CloudTrail","E":"AWS VPN"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n f\xedsica privada dedicada entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS, y AWS VPN crea un t\xfanel cifrado sobre la internet p\xfablica. Ambas son las opciones de conectividad est\xe1ndar para construir arquitecturas de nube h\xedbrida que enlazan entornos on-premises con AWS.\\nAWS Artifact es un portal de autoservicio para acceder a informes de cumplimiento y certificaciones de seguridad de AWS, y no tiene ning\xfan papel en el establecimiento de conectividad de red entre entornos on-premises y AWS. AWS Cloud9 es un entorno de desarrollo integrado basado en navegador para escribir y ejecutar c\xf3digo, y no es un servicio de conectividad ni de red. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta con fines de auditor\xeda y gobernanza, y no proporciona conectividad de red entre la infraestructura on-premises y la nube de AWS."},{"id":"q267","question":"Una empresa ha implementado una nueva aplicaci\xf3n web en m\xfaltiples instancias de Amazon EC2. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes deber\xedan usar para asegurar que el tr\xe1fico HTTP entrante se distribuya uniformemente entre las instancias?","options":{"A":"AWS EC2 Auto Recovery","B":"AWS Auto Scaling","C":"AWS Network Load Balancer","D":"AWS Application Load Balancer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Un Application Load Balancer opera en la capa 7 y distribuye el tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS entrante entre instancias EC2 seg\xfan reglas como la ruta de la URL o el encabezado del host, lo que lo convierte en la opci\xf3n correcta para distribuir uniformemente el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones web.\\nAWS EC2 Auto Recovery supervisa instancias individuales y las restaura si fallan, pero no distribuye tr\xe1fico entre m\xfaltiples instancias. AWS Auto Scaling ajusta el n\xfamero de instancias en ejecuci\xf3n seg\xfan la demanda, pero no gestiona la distribuci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico entre las instancias existentes. AWS Network Load Balancer opera en la capa 4 y est\xe1 optimizado para tr\xe1fico TCP y UDP que requiere un rendimiento ultraalto, no para la distribuci\xf3n general de tr\xe1fico HTTP de aplicaciones web."},{"id":"q268","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes ofertas de AWS es un servicio de base de datos relacional compatible con MySQL que puede escalar la capacidad autom\xe1ticamente seg\xfan la demanda?","options":{"A":"Amazon Neptune","B":"Amazon Aurora","C":"Amazon RDS for SQL Server","D":"Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Aurora es una base de datos relacional administrada compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL que escala autom\xe1ticamente el almacenamiento seg\xfan sea necesario, lo que la convierte en la soluci\xf3n dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para aplicaciones que requieren compatibilidad con MySQL y escalado autom\xe1tico de capacidad.\\nAmazon Neptune es un servicio de base de datos de grafos totalmente administrado dise\xf1ado para almacenar y consultar datos altamente conectados, como redes sociales y grafos de conocimiento, y no es una base de datos relacional compatible con MySQL. Amazon RDS for SQL Server es una base de datos relacional administrada que ejecuta el motor SQL Server de Microsoft, que no es compatible con MySQL y no escala autom\xe1ticamente la capacidad de almacenamiento seg\xfan la demanda. Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL es una base de datos relacional administrada que ejecuta el motor PostgreSQL que, aunque comparte cierta compatibilidad con Aurora, no escala autom\xe1ticamente el almacenamiento de la misma forma que Aurora."},{"id":"q270","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio de almac\xe9n de datos de AWS que admite un alto nivel de rendimiento de consultas en grandes cantidades de conjuntos de datos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon Kinesis","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon RDS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Redshift es un almac\xe9n de datos totalmente administrado a escala de petabytes optimizado para consultas anal\xedticas complejas en grandes conjuntos de datos estructurados mediante almacenamiento columnar y procesamiento masivamente paralelo.\\nAmazon Kinesis es un servicio para ingerir y procesar datos de transmisi\xf3n en tiempo real, no un almac\xe9n de datos para consultas anal\xedticas sobre grandes conjuntos de datos almacenados. Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL optimizada para cargas de trabajo de clave-valor y de documentos de alta velocidad, no para consultas anal\xedticas complejas en grandes conjuntos de datos. Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional administrado dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo transaccionales, no para el rendimiento de consultas anal\xedticas a escala de petabytes que proporciona un almac\xe9n de datos."},{"id":"q274","question":"Un cliente dedic\xf3 mucho tiempo a configurar una instancia de Amazon EC2 reci\xe9n implementada. Despu\xe9s de que aumenta la carga de trabajo, el cliente decide aprovisionar otra instancia de EC2 con una configuraci\xf3n id\xe9ntica. \xbfC\xf3mo puede lograrlo el cliente?","options":{"A":"Creando una plantilla de AWS Config a partir de la instancia anterior y lanzando una nueva instancia desde ella","B":"Creando una instant\xe1nea de EBS de la instancia anterior","C":"Instalando Aurora en EC2 y lanzando una nueva instancia desde ella","D":"Creando una AMI a partir de la instancia anterior y lanzando una nueva instancia desde ella"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crear una AMI a partir de una instancia de EC2 configurada captura su sistema operativo, software y configuraci\xf3n como una plantilla reutilizable, lo que permite lanzar nuevas instancias con configuraciones id\xe9nticas en cualquier momento.\\nCrear una plantilla de AWS Config gestiona reglas de cumplimiento y detecci\xf3n de desviaciones de configuraci\xf3n, no im\xe1genes de instancias ni replicaci\xf3n. Crear una instant\xe1nea de EBS respalda los datos de un volumen, pero no captura la configuraci\xf3n completa de la instancia, incluidos el sistema operativo y la configuraci\xf3n de software necesarios para lanzar una instancia id\xe9ntica. Instalar Aurora en EC2 no es un enfoque v\xe1lido, ya que Aurora es un servicio de base de datos administrado que se ejecuta en la infraestructura de AWS y no se instala en instancias de EC2 de clientes."},{"id":"q277","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son los beneficios de usar Amazon Relational Database Service?","options":{"A":"Menor carga administrativa","B":"Control completo sobre el host subyacente","C":"Capacidad de c\xf3mputo redimensionable","D":"Escala autom\xe1ticamente a tipos de instancia m\xe1s grandes o m\xe1s peque\xf1os","E":"Admite la estructura de datos de documentos y de clave-valor"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Amazon RDS reduce la carga administrativa de la gesti\xf3n de bases de datos al encargarse autom\xe1ticamente de tareas rutinarias como copias de seguridad, aplicaci\xf3n de parches y conmutaci\xf3n por error, y permite redimensionar la capacidad de c\xf3mputo para adaptarla a las demandas cambiantes de la carga de trabajo.\\nEl control completo sobre el host subyacente es una caracter\xedstica de Amazon EC2, donde los clientes gestionan directamente el sistema operativo. RDS abstrae completamente el host y no lo expone al cliente. RDS requiere cambios manuales del tipo de instancia en lugar de escalar autom\xe1ticamente en respuesta a la demanda, por lo que no escala autom\xe1ticamente a tipos de instancia m\xe1s grandes o m\xe1s peque\xf1os. Las estructuras de datos de documentos y de clave-valor son compatibles con Amazon DynamoDB, un servicio NoSQL, mientras que RDS es relacional y utiliza esquemas SQL estructurados."},{"id":"q278","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la opci\xf3n de conectividad que utiliza Internet Protocol Security (IPSec) para establecer conectividad cifrada entre una red local y la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Internet Gateway","B":"AWS IQ","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS Site-to-Site VPN"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Site-to-Site VPN utiliza IPSec para crear un t\xfanel cifrado entre una red local y una VPC de AWS a trav\xe9s de internet p\xfablica, lo que proporciona conectividad segura sin infraestructura f\xedsica dedicada.\\nInternet Gateway conecta una VPC a internet p\xfablica para el tr\xe1fico entrante y saliente, pero no establece t\xfaneles cifrados hacia redes locales. AWS IQ es un marketplace para conectar a clientes con expertos certificados por AWS para servicios profesionales, sin relaci\xf3n con la conectividad de red. AWS Direct Connect proporciona un circuito privado dedicado entre la infraestructura local y AWS, pero no utiliza IPSec y no viaja a trav\xe9s de internet p\xfablica."},{"id":"q282","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona el estado actual de todos los servicios de AWS en todas las regiones de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Service Catalog","B":"AWS Management Console","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS Health Dashboard"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard proporciona informaci\xf3n de estado en tiempo real sobre todos los servicios de AWS en todas las regiones de AWS, lo que permite a cualquiera consultar el estado operativo actual de la infraestructura de AWS.\\nAWS Service Catalog permite a las organizaciones crear y gestionar cat\xe1logos aprobados de servicios de TI para uso interno y aprovisionamiento de autoservicio, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con mostrar el estado actual de los servicios de AWS a nivel global. AWS Management Console es la interfaz web para acceder a los servicios de AWS y gestionarlos y, aunque proporciona acceso a muchos paneles, no es en s\xed misma el servicio que publica informaci\xf3n de estado actual sobre el estado de la infraestructura de AWS. Amazon CloudWatch recopila m\xe9tricas, registros y datos de eventos de sus propios recursos y aplicaciones de AWS para proporcionar monitoreo operativo y alertas, y es una herramienta de monitoreo orientada al cliente en lugar de un servicio que publica el estado de la infraestructura de AWS en s\xed."},{"id":"q283","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS se puede utilizar para llamar a los servicios de AWS desde diferentes lenguajes de programaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"AWS Software Development Kit","B":"AWS Command Line Interface","C":"AWS CodeDeploy","D":"AWS Management Console"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Los SDK de AWS est\xe1n disponibles para m\xfaltiples lenguajes de programaci\xf3n, incluidos Python, Java, JavaScript y .NET, y proporcionan bibliotecas que permiten a los desarrolladores interactuar con los servicios de AWS y llamarlos mediante programaci\xf3n desde el c\xf3digo de su aplicaci\xf3n.\\nAWS Command Line Interface es una herramienta para interactuar con los servicios de AWS mediante comandos y scripts de terminal, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para llamar a los servicios de AWS desde el c\xf3digo de una aplicaci\xf3n escrito en un lenguaje de programaci\xf3n. AWS CodeDeploy es un servicio de implementaci\xf3n que automatiza la publicaci\xf3n de c\xf3digo de aplicaci\xf3n en instancias de EC2, funciones de Lambda y servidores locales, y no tiene ninguna funci\xf3n en permitir el acceso program\xe1tico a los servicios de AWS desde el c\xf3digo de aplicaci\xf3n. AWS Management Console es una interfaz gr\xe1fica basada en web para gestionar los servicios de AWS a trav\xe9s de un navegador, y no proporciona acceso program\xe1tico desde lenguajes de programaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q284","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para registrar un nuevo nombre de dominio?","options":{"A":"Amazon Personalize","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"AWS KMS","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 proporciona registro de nombres de dominio adem\xe1s de enrutamiento DNS, comprobaciones de estado y gesti\xf3n del tr\xe1fico, lo que lo convierte en el \xfanico servicio de AWS que puede registrar un nuevo nombre de dominio.\\nAmazon Personalize es un servicio de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico que proporciona recomendaciones personalizadas de productos y contenido, y no tiene funcionalidad de registro de dominios ni DNS. AWS KMS es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de claves que se utiliza para crear y controlar claves de cifrado para proteger datos, y no est\xe1 relacionado con el registro de dominios. AWS Config rastrea y registra las configuraciones de los recursos de AWS y las eval\xfaa con respecto a reglas de cumplimiento, y no tiene capacidad de registro de dominios."},{"id":"q285","question":"Las empresas de desarrollo de aplicaciones trasladan su negocio a AWS para reducir el tiempo de salida al mercado y mejorar la satisfacci\xf3n del cliente; \xbfcu\xe1les son las herramientas de automatizaci\xf3n de AWS que les ayudan a implementar sus aplicaciones m\xe1s r\xe1pido?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudFormation","B":"AWS Migration Hub","C":"AWS IAM","D":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","E":"Amazon Macie"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS CloudFormation permite a los equipos definir la infraestructura como c\xf3digo en plantillas que se pueden controlar por versiones e implementar autom\xe1ticamente, y AWS Elastic Beanstalk abstrae la administraci\xf3n de la infraestructura para que los desarrolladores puedan implementar aplicaciones simplemente cargando su c\xf3digo. Ambos aceleran directamente la implementaci\xf3n de aplicaciones. AWS Migration Hub proporciona una ubicaci\xf3n central para realizar seguimiento del progreso de las migraciones de aplicaciones a AWS, y es una herramienta de monitoreo de migraciones en lugar de una herramienta de automatizaci\xf3n para implementar aplicaciones m\xe1s r\xe1pido. AWS IAM es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de identidades y accesos para controlar qui\xe9n puede acceder a los recursos de AWS y qu\xe9 acciones puede realizar, y es un servicio de seguridad y permisos sin capacidad de implementaci\xf3n de aplicaciones ni automatizaci\xf3n. Amazon Macie es un servicio de seguridad de datos que utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para descubrir y proteger autom\xe1ticamente datos confidenciales almacenados en Amazon S3, y no tiene relaci\xf3n alguna con la implementaci\xf3n de aplicaciones ni con la automatizaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q287","question":"Una empresa tiene cientos de VPC en m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS en todo el mundo. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio ofrece AWS para simplificar la administraci\xf3n de conexiones entre las VPC?","options":{"A":"VPC Peering","B":"AWS Transit Gateway","C":"Amazon Connect","D":"Security Groups"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Transit Gateway act\xfaa como un concentrador de red central que conecta cientos o miles de VPC en m\xfaltiples Regiones a trav\xe9s de una \xfanica puerta de enlace administrada, lo que elimina la complejidad de administrar conexiones punto a punto individuales. VPC Peering crea conexiones individuales uno a uno entre pares de VPC, lo que se vuelve inmanejable a escala con cientos de VPC. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube para administrar las comunicaciones con clientes y no tiene nada que ver con la conectividad de red. Los Security Groups son firewalls a nivel de instancia que controlan las reglas de tr\xe1fico entrante y saliente y no administran conexiones entre VPC."},{"id":"q289","question":"\xbfPor qu\xe9 cada Regi\xf3n de AWS contiene m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad?","options":{"A":"M\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad le permiten crear arquitecturas resilientes y de alta disponibilidad","B":"M\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad dan como resultado un costo total menor en comparaci\xf3n con la implementaci\xf3n en una sola Zona de Disponibilidad","C":"M\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad permiten la replicaci\xf3n de datos y el alcance global","D":"M\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad dentro de una regi\xf3n aumentan la capacidad de almacenamiento disponible en esa regi\xf3n"},"correct":"A","explanation":"M\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad dentro de una Regi\xf3n est\xe1n separadas geogr\xe1ficamente, cada una con energ\xeda, refrigeraci\xf3n y red independientes, por lo que una falla en una AZ no afecta a las dem\xe1s, lo que permite a los clientes crear arquitecturas resilientes y de alta disponibilidad. Implementar en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad no reduce el costo total, ya que ejecutar recursos en m\xfaltiples AZ normalmente aumenta el costo en comparaci\xf3n con una implementaci\xf3n en una sola AZ. M\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad proporcionan redundancia dentro de una Regi\xf3n, pero no proporcionan alcance global, lo cual requiere implementar en m\xfaltiples Regiones o usar una red de entrega de contenido. La cantidad de Zonas de Disponibilidad en una Regi\xf3n no aumenta la capacidad de almacenamiento disponible, ya que la capacidad de almacenamiento est\xe1 determinada por los servicios y las configuraciones que selecciona un cliente, no por la cantidad de AZ."},{"id":"q294","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes servicios de AWS escalan autom\xe1ticamente sin su intervenci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon S3","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"Amazon EMR","E":"Amazon EBS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 escala autom\xe1ticamente para manejar cualquier cantidad de datos y cualquier tasa de solicitudes sin intervenci\xf3n manual, y AWS Lambda escala autom\xe1ticamente desde cero hasta miles de ejecuciones simult\xe1neas en respuesta a eventos entrantes. Amazon EC2 no escala autom\xe1ticamente de forma predeterminada y requiere que los clientes configuren grupos de Auto Scaling para agregar o quitar instancias seg\xfan la demanda. Amazon EMR requiere que los clientes cambien manualmente el tama\xf1o de los cl\xfasteres o configuren pol\xedticas de escalado administrado, y no escala autom\xe1ticamente sin intervenci\xf3n. Los vol\xfamenes de Amazon EBS tienen un tama\xf1o fijo cuando se aprovisionan y se deben cambiar de tama\xf1o manualmente, y no escalan autom\xe1ticamente en respuesta al uso."},{"id":"q296","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo mejoran los ELB la confiabilidad de su aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Al distribuir el tr\xe1fico entre m\xfaltiples buckets de S3","B":"Al replicar datos en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad","C":"Al crear r\xe9plicas de lectura de base de datos","D":"Al garantizar que solo los destinos en buen estado reciban tr\xe1fico"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Los Elastic Load Balancers monitorean continuamente el estado de los destinos registrados y solo enrutan el tr\xe1fico a las instancias que pasan las verificaciones de estado, lo que garantiza que los destinos en mal estado no reciban solicitudes. Distribuir el tr\xe1fico entre m\xfaltiples buckets de S3 describe una funci\xf3n que los ELB no realizan, ya que los Elastic Load Balancers enrutan el tr\xe1fico a destinos de c\xf3mputo como instancias EC2, contenedores y funciones Lambda, en lugar de a buckets de almacenamiento de objetos. Replicar datos en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad es una caracter\xedstica de servicios como Amazon RDS Multi-AZ, que replica sincr\xf3nicamente los datos de la base de datos entre AZ, y no es una funci\xf3n de los Elastic Load Balancers. Crear r\xe9plicas de lectura de base de datos es una caracter\xedstica de Amazon RDS que descarga el tr\xe1fico de lectura de una instancia de base de datos principal, y no tiene relaci\xf3n alguna con el balanceo de carga ni con el enrutamiento del tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones."},{"id":"q298","question":"Un cliente planea mover miles de millones de im\xe1genes y videos para almacenarlos en Amazon S3. El cliente tiene aproximadamente 60 petabytes de datos para mover. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS es la mejor opci\xf3n para transferir los datos a AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Snowball","B":"Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration","C":"AWS Snowmobile","D":"Amazon VP"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Snowmobile es un servicio de transferencia de datos a escala de exabytes que se entrega como un contenedor de transporte resistente de 45 pies, capaz de transferir hasta 100 petabytes por unidad, lo que lo convierte en la opci\xf3n adecuada para una migraci\xf3n de 60 petabytes. AWS Snowball es un dispositivo de transporte f\xedsico de datos adecuado para transferir hasta 80 terabytes por dispositivo, lo que est\xe1 muy por debajo del requisito de 60 petabytes y requerir\xeda una cantidad impracticable de dispositivos en comparaci\xf3n con Snowmobile. Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration acelera las cargas a S3 enrutando el tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de ubicaciones de borde de CloudFront por internet, pero no es adecuado para migraciones de datos a escala de petabytes en las que se requiere transporte f\xedsico. Amazon VPC es un servicio de red para aprovisionar redes virtuales aisladas dentro de AWS, y no tiene ninguna funci\xf3n en la transferencia f\xedsica o program\xe1tica de grandes conjuntos de datos a AWS."},{"id":"q299","question":"Una empresa planea migrar una gran cantidad de datos archivados a AWS. Los datos archivados deben mantenerse durante un per\xedodo de 5 a\xf1os y deben poder recuperarse dentro de las 5 horas posteriores a una solicitud. \xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio de almacenamiento de AWS m\xe1s rentable para usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Glacier","B":"Amazon EFS","C":"Amazon S3 Standard","D":"Amazon EBS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon S3 Glacier proporciona almacenamiento de archivo de bajo costo con opciones de recuperaci\xf3n que incluyen Expedited (1 a 5 minutos), Standard (3 a 5 horas) y Bulk (5 a 12 horas). Para un requisito de recuperaci\xf3n de cinco horas, la recuperaci\xf3n Standard de S3 Glacier cumple con el SLA al costo m\xe1s bajo. Amazon EFS es un sistema de archivos de red compartido administrado que proporciona acceso concurrente a archivos desde m\xfaltiples instancias, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo de archivos activos en lugar de almacenamiento de archivo a largo plazo, donde el costo es la principal preocupaci\xf3n. Amazon S3 Standard proporciona almacenamiento de objetos duradero y de alta disponibilidad con recuperaci\xf3n inmediata en milisegundos, y aunque cumple con el requisito de recuperaci\xf3n, es significativamente m\xe1s costoso por gigabyte que S3 Glacier para almacenar cinco a\xf1os de datos de archivo a los que se accede con poca frecuencia. Amazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque persistentes conectados directamente a instancias EC2 para acceso a disco de baja latencia, y es un servicio de almacenamiento conectado a c\xf3mputo que no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para archivo a largo plazo ni para almacenar datos independientemente de una instancia en ejecuci\xf3n."},{"id":"q304","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son casos de uso de Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"Alojamiento de sitios web est\xe1ticos","B":"Alojamiento de sitios web que requieren un uso sostenido alto de CPU","C":"Almacenamiento rentable de bases de datos y registros","D":"Un almac\xe9n de medios para el servicio CloudFront","E":"Procesamiento de flujos de datos a cualquier escala"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 puede alojar sitios web est\xe1ticos sirviendo archivos HTML, CSS y JavaScript directamente, y sirve como almac\xe9n de origen para Amazon CloudFront, entregando contenido multimedia a nivel global mediante almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de borde. Alojar sitios web que requieren un uso sostenido alto de CPU describe una carga de trabajo intensiva en c\xf3mputo que requiere instancias EC2 o servicios de c\xf3mputo similares, ya que S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos sin capacidad de procesamiento de CPU. El almacenamiento rentable de bases de datos y registros combina dos casos de uso diferentes, ya que S3 puede almacenar archivos de registro pero no es un servicio de base de datos, y los servicios de base de datos dise\xf1ados para fines espec\xedficos, como RDS o DynamoDB, son m\xe1s apropiados para datos estructurados. El procesamiento de flujos de datos a cualquier escala describe la funci\xf3n de Amazon Kinesis, que est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la ingesta y el procesamiento de flujos de datos en tiempo real, no de Amazon S3."},{"id":"q308","question":"Una empresa planea migrar una base de datos con alta actividad de lectura/escritura a AWS. \xbfCu\xe1l es la mejor opci\xf3n de almacenamiento para usar?","options":{"A":"AWS Storage Gateway","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon EBS","D":"Amazon S3 Glacier"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque de alto rendimiento que se adjuntan directamente a las instancias EC2, entregando la E/S de baja latencia necesaria para bases de datos con alta actividad de lectura/escritura.\\nAWS Storage Gateway es un servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido que conecta entornos locales con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo de bases de datos de alto rendimiento que requieren almacenamiento en bloque de baja latencia. Amazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos optimizado para almacenar y recuperar grandes cantidades de datos no estructurados, y no proporciona la E/S a nivel de bloque de baja latencia que requieren las bases de datos con alta actividad de lectura/escritura. Amazon S3 Glacier es un servicio de almacenamiento de archivo dise\xf1ado para datos a los que se accede con poca frecuencia y que pueden tolerar tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n de horas, lo que lo hace completamente inadecuado para cargas de trabajo de bases de datos activas."},{"id":"q310","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio de AWS que proporciona cinco veces el rendimiento de una base de datos MySQL est\xe1ndar?","options":{"A":"Amazon Aurora","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon Neptune"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Aurora es una base de datos relacional compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL que ofrece hasta cinco veces el rendimiento de MySQL est\xe1ndar, con la confiabilidad y disponibilidad de una base de datos comercial a una fracci\xf3n del costo.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos totalmente administrado optimizado para ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas complejas en grandes conjuntos de datos, y no es una base de datos relacional compatible con MySQL dise\xf1ada para cargas de trabajo transaccionales de alto rendimiento. Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL totalmente administrada que admite modelos de datos de clave-valor y de documentos, y no ofrece compatibilidad con MySQL ni las capacidades relacionales que proporciona Aurora. Amazon Neptune es un servicio de base de datos de grafos totalmente administrado dise\xf1ado para almacenar y consultar datos altamente conectados, y no tiene compatibilidad con MySQL ni relaci\xf3n con las caracter\xedsticas de rendimiento descritas."},{"id":"q311","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 proporciona AWS Service Catalog?","options":{"A":"Permite a los clientes encontrar r\xe1pidamente descripciones y casos de uso de los servicios de AWS","B":"Permite a los clientes explorar los diferentes cat\xe1logos de servicios de AWS","C":"Simplifica la organizaci\xf3n y la gobernanza de servicios de TI com\xfanmente implementados","D":"Permite a los desarrolladores implementar infraestructura en AWS usando lenguajes de programaci\xf3n conocidos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Service Catalog permite a las organizaciones crear y administrar cat\xe1logos aprobados de servicios de TI, lo que habilita la gobernanza estandarizada y el despliegue de autoservicio de recursos de uso com\xfan en toda la organizaci\xf3n.\\nProporcionar descripciones y casos de uso para los servicios de AWS describe la funci\xf3n de la documentaci\xf3n de AWS y las p\xe1ginas de servicios de la consola de AWS, no AWS Service Catalog, que es una herramienta de gobernanza y despliegue en lugar de un recurso de referencia informativo. Permitir a los clientes explorar diferentes cat\xe1logos de servicios de AWS describe AWS Marketplace, donde los clientes pueden encontrar y comprar software y servicios de terceros, en lugar de AWS Service Catalog, que administra productos de TI aprobados internamente. Permitir a los desarrolladores implementar infraestructura usando lenguajes de programaci\xf3n conocidos describe AWS Cloud Development Kit, que permite a los desarrolladores definir infraestructura en la nube en lenguajes como Python, TypeScript y Java, y es un servicio de herramientas para desarrolladores en lugar de una plataforma de administraci\xf3n y gobernanza de cat\xe1logos."},{"id":"q313","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS ayuda con la planificaci\xf3n de la migraci\xf3n de aplicaciones a la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Snowball","B":"AWS Application Discovery Service","C":"AWS DMS","D":"AWS Migration Hub"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Application Discovery Service recopila informaci\xf3n sobre servidores locales, incluida la configuraci\xf3n, el uso y las dependencias, para ayudar a planificar y priorizar la migraci\xf3n a la nube de AWS.\\nAWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico que se usa para transferir grandes vol\xfamenes de datos a AWS y es una herramienta de transferencia de datos en lugar de un servicio de planificaci\xf3n de migraci\xf3n. AWS Database Migration Service migra bases de datos a AWS con un tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo, pero se encarga de la ejecuci\xf3n de las migraciones de bases de datos en lugar de la fase de descubrimiento y planificaci\xf3n. AWS Migration Hub proporciona una ubicaci\xf3n central para rastrear el progreso de las migraciones a trav\xe9s de m\xfaltiples herramientas de AWS y de socios, pero monitorea el estado de la migraci\xf3n en lugar de recopilar los datos del servidor necesarios para planificar una migraci\xf3n."},{"id":"q315","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n describe mejor el concepto de una regi\xf3n de AWS?","options":{"A":"Una regi\xf3n de AWS es una ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica con una colecci\xf3n de ubicaciones de borde","B":"Una regi\xf3n de AWS es una red virtual dedicada solo a un \xfanico cliente de AWS","C":"Una regi\xf3n de AWS es una ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica con una colecci\xf3n de zonas de disponibilidad","D":"Una regi\xf3n de AWS representa el pa\xeds donde existe la infraestructura de AWS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Una regi\xf3n de AWS es una ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica f\xedsica que contiene una colecci\xf3n de zonas de disponibilidad, cada una compuesta por uno o m\xe1s centros de datos discretos con energ\xeda, red y conectividad redundantes.\\nUna ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica con una colecci\xf3n de ubicaciones de borde confunde las regiones con la red de borde de CloudFront, que es una capa separada de infraestructura distribuida de forma independiente de las regiones. Una red virtual dedicada solo a un \xfanico cliente de AWS describe un Amazon VPC, no una regi\xf3n, ya que las regiones son infraestructura f\xedsica compartida utilizada por todos los clientes de AWS. Un pa\xeds donde existe infraestructura de AWS es demasiado amplio e inexacto, ya que pueden existir m\xfaltiples regiones dentro del mismo pa\xeds y una regi\xf3n es un \xe1rea geogr\xe1fica espec\xedfica definida, no una frontera nacional."},{"id":"q317","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son factores para determinar la tecnolog\xeda de base de datos adecuada para una carga de trabajo espec\xedfica?","options":{"A":"Zonas de disponibilidad","B":"Soberan\xeda de datos","C":"El n\xfamero de lecturas y escrituras por segundo","D":"La naturaleza de las consultas","E":"Errores de software"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"El n\xfamero de lecturas y escrituras por segundo determina los requisitos de rendimiento y ayuda a identificar si una base de datos necesita manejar cargas de trabajo transaccionales de gran volumen, y la naturaleza de las consultas determina si una base de datos relacional para joins complejos o una base de datos NoSQL para b\xfasquedas simples de clave-valor es la opci\xf3n m\xe1s adecuada.\\nLas zonas de disponibilidad son una consideraci\xf3n de infraestructura para implementar bases de datos con alta disponibilidad y redundancia, pero no influyen en qu\xe9 tecnolog\xeda o motor de base de datos es adecuado para una carga de trabajo determinada. La soberan\xeda de datos se refiere a requisitos legales y regulatorios sobre d\xf3nde deben almacenarse geogr\xe1ficamente los datos, lo que afecta la selecci\xf3n de la regi\xf3n pero no determina la tecnolog\xeda de base de datos adecuada para una carga de trabajo. Los errores de software son una preocupaci\xf3n de desarrollo y aseguramiento de la calidad no relacionada con los criterios utilizados para seleccionar una tecnolog\xeda de base de datos adecuada."},{"id":"q324","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes NO es una caracter\xedstica de Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2 se considera un servicio web sin servidor","B":"Amazon EC2 elimina la necesidad de invertir en hardware por adelantado","C":"Amazon EC2 puede lanzar tantos o tan pocos servidores virtuales como sea necesario","D":"Amazon EC2 ofrece computaci\xf3n escalable"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales que los clientes administran, incluido el sistema operativo y la pila de software. Es un servicio de computaci\xf3n basado en servidores, no uno sin servidor. AWS Lambda es el servicio de computaci\xf3n sin servidor donde AWS administra toda la infraestructura subyacente.\\nAmazon EC2 s\xed elimina la necesidad de invertir en hardware por adelantado, ya que los clientes pagan solo por la capacidad de computaci\xf3n que utilizan en lugar de comprar y mantener servidores f\xedsicos. Amazon EC2 s\xed permite a los clientes lanzar tantos o tan pocos servidores virtuales como sea necesario, escalando la capacidad hacia arriba o hacia abajo en respuesta a requisitos cambiantes. Amazon EC2 s\xed ofrece computaci\xf3n escalable, permitiendo a los clientes aumentar o disminuir la capacidad en minutos para coincidir con las demandas de la carga de trabajo."},{"id":"q325","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el servicio de computaci\xf3n de AWS que ejecuta c\xf3digo solo cuando lo activan eventos?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","D":"Amazon EC2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Lambda es un servicio de computaci\xf3n sin servidor que ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos como solicitudes HTTP, cargas de archivos o cambios en bases de datos, sin requerir que aprovisione o administre servidores.\\nAmazon CloudWatch es un servicio de monitoreo y observabilidad que recopila m\xe9tricas y registros de los recursos de AWS, y no ejecuta c\xf3digo de aplicaciones. AWS Elastic Beanstalk es un servicio de plataforma que implementa y administra aplicaciones web aprovisionando autom\xe1ticamente la infraestructura subyacente, pero se ejecuta continuamente en servidores aprovisionados en lugar de ejecutar c\xf3digo solo en respuesta a eventos. Amazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales donde los clientes son responsables de aprovisionar y administrar las instancias, lo que lo convierte en una soluci\xf3n basada en servidores en lugar de un servicio de computaci\xf3n sin servidor impulsado por eventos."},{"id":"q326","question":"Tanto AWS como los distribuidores tradicionales de TI ofrecen una amplia gama de servidores virtuales para satisfacer los requisitos de sus clientes. \xbfC\xf3mo se llaman estos servidores virtuales en AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS Snapshots","B":"Amazon VP","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"Amazon EC2 Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las instancias de Amazon EC2 son servidores virtuales en la nube de AWS que proporcionan capacidad de c\xf3mputo redimensionable, lo que permite a los clientes ejecutar aplicaciones en la infraestructura de AWS tal como lo har\xedan en servidores f\xedsicos.\\nAmazon EBS Snapshots son copias de seguridad en un punto en el tiempo de vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento EBS, no servidores virtuales. Amazon VPC es un servicio de redes que proporciona un entorno de red virtual aislado dentro de AWS, no un servidor virtual. Amazon Lightsail tambi\xe9n proporciona servidores virtuales, pero est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo m\xe1s simples con una configuraci\xf3n m\xe1s limitada y fija, mientras que EC2 ofrece la gama completa de tipos de instancia y opciones de personalizaci\xf3n comparables a las ofertas tradicionales de servidores virtuales de TI."},{"id":"q331","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son los beneficios de usar DynamoDB?","options":{"A":"Escala autom\xe1ticamente para satisfacer la capacidad de rendimiento requerida","B":"Proporciona instancias redimensionables para adaptarse a la demanda actual","C":"Admite modelos de datos relacionales y no relacionales","D":"Ofrece una latencia extremadamente baja (de un solo d\xedgito en milisegundos)","E":"Admite los motores de bases de datos NoSQL m\xe1s populares, como CouchDB y MongoDB"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB escala autom\xe1ticamente la capacidad de rendimiento para manejar las fluctuaciones del tr\xe1fico y ofrece una latencia consistente de un solo d\xedgito en milisegundos a cualquier escala, lo que lo hace ideal para aplicaciones de alto rendimiento.\\nProporcionar instancias redimensionables describe el modelo de escalado vertical de EC2, mientras que DynamoDB es sin servidor y no tiene el concepto de instancias que los clientes aprovisionen o redimensionen. DynamoDB es un servicio de base de datos NoSQL que solo admite modelos de datos de clave-valor y de documentos, y no admite modelos de datos relacionales. DynamoDB es un servicio NoSQL propietario de AWS y no admite ni ejecuta motores de bases de datos de terceros, como CouchDB o MongoDB."},{"id":"q337","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para enviar mensajes de texto promocionales (SMS) a m\xe1s de 200 pa\xedses en todo el mundo?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","B":"Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3)","C":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","D":"Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Simple Notification Service admite el env\xedo de mensajes de texto SMS a m\xe1s de 200 pa\xedses en todo el mundo, adem\xe1s de notificaciones push y correo electr\xf3nico, lo que lo convierte en la soluci\xf3n creada espec\xedficamente para la mensajer\xeda promocional a escala.\\nAmazon Simple Email Service es un servicio de env\xedo de correo electr\xf3nico dise\xf1ado para comunicaciones transaccionales y de marketing por correo electr\xf3nico, y no admite mensajes de texto SMS. Amazon Simple Storage Service es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos para almacenar y recuperar datos como archivos, im\xe1genes y copias de seguridad, y no tiene ninguna capacidad de mensajer\xeda ni SMS de ning\xfan tipo. Amazon Simple Queue Service es un servicio de colas de mensajes totalmente administrado que permite desacoplar componentes de aplicaciones al pasar mensajes entre ellos, y es una herramienta de integraci\xf3n de aplicaciones en lugar de un servicio para enviar mensajes SMS a los usuarios finales."},{"id":"q338","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones permite crear nuevas instancias de RDS?","options":{"A":"AWS CodeDeploy","B":"AWS Quick Starts","C":"AWS CloudFormation","D":"AWS DMS","E":"AWS Management Console"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"AWS CloudFormation puede definir y aprovisionar instancias de RDS mediante plantillas de infraestructura como c\xf3digo, y AWS Management Console proporciona una interfaz gr\xe1fica para crear instancias de RDS directamente.\\nAWS CodeDeploy es un servicio de despliegue que automatiza la publicaci\xf3n de c\xf3digo de aplicaciones en destinos de c\xf3mputo, como instancias de EC2 y funciones de Lambda, y no tiene la capacidad de crear o aprovisionar instancias de bases de datos RDS. AWS Quick Starts son plantillas de despliegue preconstruidas que ayudan a los clientes a lanzar entornos de AWS siguiendo las mejores pr\xe1cticas, pero son un recurso de arquitectura de referencia en lugar de un servicio que cree o aprovisione directamente instancias de RDS. AWS DMS migra bases de datos existentes a AWS con un tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo, y es una herramienta de migraci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio para crear nuevas instancias de RDS desde cero."},{"id":"q350","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes caracter\xedsticas de Amazon RDS facilita la descarga de la actividad de lectura de la base de datos?","options":{"A":"Instant\xe1neas de base de datos","B":"Implementaciones Multi-AZ","C":"Copias de seguridad automatizadas","D":"R\xe9plicas de lectura"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las r\xe9plicas de lectura de Amazon RDS crean copias de solo lectura de la base de datos que manejan consultas de lectura, descargando el tr\xe1fico de lectura de la instancia principal y mejorando el rendimiento general de la aplicaci\xf3n.\\nLas instant\xe1neas de base de datos son copias de seguridad en un punto en el tiempo de una instancia de RDS almacenadas en Amazon S3, que se utilizan para la recuperaci\xf3n y restauraci\xf3n de datos en lugar de para distribuir o descargar la actividad de lectura. Las implementaciones Multi-AZ mantienen una r\xe9plica en espera s\xedncrona de la base de datos en una zona de disponibilidad diferente para alta disponibilidad y conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica, pero la instancia en espera no atiende tr\xe1fico de lectura y existe \xfanicamente para redundancia. Las copias de seguridad automatizadas permiten la recuperaci\xf3n en un punto en el tiempo al realizar copias de seguridad continuas de la base de datos y los registros de transacciones, y son una caracter\xedstica de protecci\xf3n de datos en lugar de un mecanismo para descargar la actividad de lectura."},{"id":"q353","question":"Una empresa tiene una aplicaci\xf3n web alojada en una \xfanica instancia de EC2 y se est\xe1 acercando al 100 por ciento de utilizaci\xf3n de CPU durante las cargas m\xe1ximas. En lugar de escalar el servidor verticalmente, la empresa ha decidido implementar tres instancias de Amazon EC2 en paralelo y distribuir el tr\xe1fico entre los tres servidores. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar la empresa para distribuir el tr\xe1fico de manera uniforme?","options":{"A":"AWS Global Accelerator","B":"Application Load Balancer","C":"Amazon CloudFront","D":"AWS Transit Gateway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un Application Load Balancer distribuye el tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS entrante entre varias instancias de EC2, enrutando autom\xe1ticamente las solicitudes a destinos en buen estado y equilibrando la carga de manera uniforme en todo el conjunto.\\nAWS Global Accelerator mejora la disponibilidad y el rendimiento de las aplicaciones para usuarios globales al enrutar el tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de la red global de AWS hasta el endpoint en buen estado m\xe1s cercano, y es un servicio de enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico global en lugar de una herramienta para distribuir la carga de manera uniforme entre instancias dentro de una \xfanica implementaci\xf3n. Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y entrega contenido desde ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo para reducir la latencia de los usuarios finales, y es un servicio de distribuci\xf3n de contenido en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de balanceo de carga para distribuir el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones entre instancias de EC2. AWS Transit Gateway act\xfaa como un concentrador de red central que conecta m\xfaltiples VPC y redes locales, y es un servicio de conectividad de red sin capacidad para distribuir tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones entre instancias de c\xf3mputo."},{"id":"q354","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes enfoques le ayudar\xe1 a eliminar el error humano y automatizar el proceso de creaci\xf3n y actualizaci\xf3n de su entorno de AWS?","options":{"A":"Usar herramientas de automatizaci\xf3n de pruebas de software","B":"Usar AWS CodeDeploy para construir y automatizar su entorno de AWS","C":"Usar c\xf3digo para aprovisionar y operar su infraestructura de AWS","D":"Migrar todas sus aplicaciones a un host dedicado"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La infraestructura como c\xf3digo utiliza herramientas como AWS CloudFormation para definir y aprovisionar recursos de AWS de forma program\xe1tica mediante plantillas, lo que elimina los pasos manuales en la consola y hace que la creaci\xf3n de entornos sea repetible, consistente y automatizada.\\nLas herramientas de automatizaci\xf3n de pruebas de software se utilizan para automatizar las pruebas de c\xf3digo de aplicaciones, como la ejecuci\xf3n de pruebas unitarias o pruebas de integraci\xf3n, y no abordan el aprovisionamiento y la configuraci\xf3n de la infraestructura de AWS. AWS CodeDeploy es un servicio de despliegue que automatiza los despliegues de aplicaciones en instancias de EC2, funciones de Lambda o servidores locales; maneja el despliegue del c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n, pero no aprovisiona ni configura el entorno de infraestructura subyacente de AWS. Migrar aplicaciones a un host dedicado cambia el modelo subyacente de aislamiento del servidor f\xedsico por motivos de cumplimiento o licenciamiento, pero no automatiza ni codifica el proceso de creaci\xf3n y actualizaci\xf3n del entorno de AWS."},{"id":"q357","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes factores se debe considerar al determinar la regi\xf3n en la que se implementar\xe1n los recursos de AWS?","options":{"A":"El nivel de seguridad de la regi\xf3n de AWS","B":"Soberan\xeda de los datos","C":"Costo","D":"La cantidad planificada de VPC","E":"La proximidad geogr\xe1fica a la ubicaci\xf3n de la empresa"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Los requisitos de soberan\xeda de los datos dictan que los datos deben permanecer en ubicaciones geogr\xe1ficas espec\xedficas por motivos legales o regulatorios, y los precios var\xedan entre regiones, lo que hace que ambos sean factores cr\xedticos al elegir d\xf3nde implementar los recursos de AWS.\\nTodas las regiones de AWS se construyen con los mismos est\xe1ndares de seguridad, por lo que el nivel de seguridad de una regi\xf3n no es un factor diferenciador al seleccionar d\xf3nde implementar. La cantidad planificada de VPC es una decisi\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica que se toma despu\xe9s de la selecci\xf3n de la regi\xf3n y no influye en qu\xe9 regi\xf3n elegir. La proximidad geogr\xe1fica a la ubicaci\xf3n de la empresa no es un factor de selecci\xf3n principal, ya que las consideraciones de latencia se relacionan con d\xf3nde se encuentran los usuarios finales, no con d\xf3nde est\xe1 ubicada la empresa en s\xed."},{"id":"q358","question":"Est\xe1 ejecutando una aplicaci\xf3n web de servicios financieros en AWS. La aplicaci\xf3n utiliza una base de datos MySQL para almacenar datos. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS mejorar\xeda el rendimiento de su aplicaci\xf3n al permitirle recuperar informaci\xf3n de cach\xe9s en memoria r\xe1pidas?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic File System (EFS)","B":"Amazon Neptune","C":"Amazon ElastiCache","D":"Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon ElastiCache es un servicio administrado de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria que admite Redis y Memcached y que almacena datos a los que se accede con frecuencia en memoria, lo que ofrece tiempos de respuesta por debajo del milisegundo para aplicaciones con muchas lecturas, como una aplicaci\xf3n web de servicios financieros respaldada por MySQL.\\nAmazon Elastic File System es un sistema de archivos de red compartido administrado dise\xf1ado para el acceso concurrente desde m\xfaltiples instancias de c\xf3mputo, y es un servicio de almacenamiento de archivos sin capacidad de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria. Amazon Neptune es un servicio de base de datos de grafos totalmente administrado optimizado para almacenar y consultar datos altamente conectados, como redes sociales y grafos de conocimiento, y es una base de datos especializada en lugar de una capa de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria. Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) es un servicio de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para acelerar el rendimiento de lectura de Amazon DynamoDB, y est\xe1 estrechamente vinculado a DynamoDB en lugar de ser una capa de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de prop\xf3sito general compatible con una base de datos MySQL."},{"id":"q359","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son las ventajas de usar Auto Scaling Groups para instancias EC2?","options":{"A":"Auto Scaling Groups almacena en cach\xe9 las respuestas m\xe1s recientes en ubicaciones de borde globales para reducir la latencia y mejorar el rendimiento","B":"Auto Scaling Groups escala instancias EC2 en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad para aumentar la disponibilidad de la aplicaci\xf3n y la tolerancia a fallos","C":"Auto Scaling Groups escala instancias EC2 en m\xfaltiples regiones para reducir la latencia de los usuarios globales","D":"Auto Scaling Groups distribuye el tr\xe1fico de la aplicaci\xf3n entre m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad para mejorar el rendimiento"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Auto Scaling Groups ajusta autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias EC2 en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad seg\xfan la demanda, lo que aumenta tanto la disponibilidad de la aplicaci\xf3n como la tolerancia a fallos al garantizar que la capacidad coincida con la carga.\\nEl almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de respuestas en ubicaciones de borde globales para reducir la latencia describe a Amazon CloudFront, no a Auto Scaling Groups, que no tienen capacidad de almacenamiento en cach\xe9. Auto Scaling Groups opera dentro de una sola Regi\xf3n y no puede escalar instancias EC2 en m\xfaltiples Regiones, por lo que la reducci\xf3n de latencia entre regiones no es una funci\xf3n que proporcionen. La distribuci\xf3n del tr\xe1fico de la aplicaci\xf3n entre m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad describe Elastic Load Balancing, que funciona junto con Auto Scaling Groups pero es un servicio separado responsable de la distribuci\xf3n del tr\xe1fico."},{"id":"q363","question":"Una empresa busca implementar una aplicaci\xf3n .NET existente en AWS lo m\xe1s r\xe1pido posible. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar el cliente para lograr este objetivo?","options":{"A":"Amazon SNS","B":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk es un servicio administrado de implementaci\xf3n de aplicaciones que aprovisiona la infraestructura subyacente, gestiona la implementaci\xf3n, el aprovisionamiento de capacidad, el balanceo de carga y el escalado autom\xe1tico, lo que permite implementar r\xe1pidamente aplicaciones .NET existentes con una configuraci\xf3n m\xednima.\\nAmazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda de publicaci\xf3n/suscripci\xf3n que se utiliza para distribuir notificaciones a suscriptores como correo electr\xf3nico, SMS u otras aplicaciones, y no proporciona ninguna capacidad para implementar o ejecutar c\xf3digo de aplicaci\xf3n. AWS Systems Manager es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de operaciones que se utiliza para tareas como la aplicaci\xf3n de parches, el inventario y la gesti\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n de recursos existentes, y no es una plataforma para implementar aplicaciones .NET. AWS Trusted Advisor es un servicio de asesoramiento que inspecciona su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones sobre optimizaci\xf3n de costos, rendimiento, seguridad y tolerancia a fallos, y no se puede utilizar para implementar aplicaciones."},{"id":"q365","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ayuda a las empresas a extender su almacenamiento local a AWS de manera rentable?","options":{"A":"AWS Transfer Family","B":"AWS Storage Gateway","C":"Amazon Aurora","D":"Amazon EFS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Storage Gateway conecta entornos locales con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS, y proporciona una soluci\xf3n de almacenamiento h\xedbrido que permite a las organizaciones extender su infraestructura de almacenamiento local existente a AWS de manera rentable al mover datos de acceso poco frecuente a la nube por niveles.\\nAWS Transfer Family proporciona flujos de trabajo administrados de transferencia de archivos mediante los protocolos SFTP, FTPS y FTP para mover archivos directamente hacia y desde Amazon S3 o EFS, y es un servicio de transferencia de datos en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de extensi\xf3n de almacenamiento h\xedbrido para entornos locales. Amazon Aurora es un motor de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado y de alto rendimiento compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL, y es un servicio de base de datos en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de extensi\xf3n de almacenamiento para datos locales. Amazon EFS es un sistema de archivos NFS administrado y escalable al que pueden acceder varias instancias EC2 en la nube, y aunque proporciona almacenamiento de archivos, es un servicio nativo de la nube en lugar de una puerta de enlace para extender la capacidad de almacenamiento local a AWS."},{"id":"q366","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando una plataforma de almacenamiento en la nube en l\xednea. Necesitan un servicio de almacenamiento que pueda escalar la capacidad autom\xe1ticamente y minimizar el costo. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de almacenamiento de AWS deber\xeda usar la empresa para cumplir estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Storage Service","B":"Amazon Elastic Block Store","C":"Amazon Elastic Container Service","D":"AWS Storage Gateway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon S3 proporciona almacenamiento de objetos pr\xe1cticamente ilimitado que se escala autom\xe1ticamente con el uso y solo cobra por lo que almacena, lo que lo hace ideal para plataformas de almacenamiento en la nube que necesitan capacidad el\xe1stica a bajo costo.\\nAmazon Elastic Block Store proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque persistentes que se conectan directamente a instancias EC2 para acceso a disco de baja latencia, y tiene un modelo de capacidad aprovisionada fija en el que se paga por el tama\xf1o del volumen que se aprovisiona en lugar de escalar autom\xe1ticamente seg\xfan los objetos almacenados. Amazon Elastic Container Service es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores que gestiona la implementaci\xf3n y el escalado de aplicaciones en contenedores, y es un servicio de c\xf3mputo en lugar de un servicio de almacenamiento para una plataforma de almacenamiento de objetos. AWS Storage Gateway conecta entornos locales con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS para la integraci\xf3n de almacenamiento h\xedbrido, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para conectar la infraestructura local con AWS en lugar de servir como el almacenamiento subyacente para una plataforma de almacenamiento nativa de la nube."},{"id":"q374","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes ofertas de AWS son servicios sin servidor?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"AWS Lambda","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon EMR","E":"Amazon RDS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS Lambda ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin requerir aprovisionamiento ni gesti\xf3n de servidores, y Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL totalmente administrada que se escala autom\xe1ticamente sin que los clientes necesiten gestionar ninguna infraestructura subyacente. Ambos son servicios sin servidor en los que AWS gestiona toda la infraestructura subyacente.\\nAmazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales en los que los clientes son responsables de aprovisionar, configurar y gestionar las instancias, lo que lo convierte en un servicio de c\xf3mputo basado en servidores en lugar de sin servidor. Amazon EMR ejecuta marcos de procesamiento de big data en cl\xfasteres aprovisionados de instancias EC2, lo que requiere que los clientes gestionen la infraestructura del cl\xfaster subyacente en lugar de abstraerla. Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional administrado, pero los clientes deben seleccionar y gestionar el tipo de instancia, el tama\xf1o y las ventanas de mantenimiento subyacentes, lo que significa que es un servicio administrado en lugar de un servicio totalmente sin servidor."},{"id":"q376","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona la capacidad de automatizar la configuraci\xf3n, la gesti\xf3n y la aplicaci\xf3n de parches de instancias EC2 a escala?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS Systems Manager","C":"AWS Auto Scaling","D":"AWS CloudFormation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Systems Manager proporciona un conjunto de herramientas para automatizar tareas operativas en instancias EC2 a escala, incluida la gesti\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n, la gesti\xf3n de parches, la ejecuci\xf3n de comandos y la recopilaci\xf3n de inventario sin necesidad de iniciar sesi\xf3n en instancias individuales.\\nAWS Config registra y eval\xfaa los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS con fines de cumplimiento y auditor\xeda, pero no automatiza la configuraci\xf3n ni la aplicaci\xf3n de parches de las instancias. AWS Auto Scaling ajusta autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias EC2 seg\xfan la demanda, pero no gestiona la configuraci\xf3n ni la aplicaci\xf3n de parches de las instancias. AWS CloudFormation automatiza el aprovisionamiento de la infraestructura de AWS mediante plantillas, pero no se encarga de la gesti\xf3n continua de la configuraci\xf3n ni de la aplicaci\xf3n de parches de las instancias en ejecuci\xf3n."},{"id":"q377","question":"Un cliente busca almacenar objetos en su entorno de AWS y hacer que esos objetos se puedan descargar por Internet. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede usar para lograr esto?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon EFS","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon Instance Store"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 almacena objetos que se pueden hacer p\xfablicamente accesibles mediante URL \xfanicas, lo que lo convierte en el servicio est\xe1ndar de AWS para alojar y servir contenido descargable por Internet.\\nAmazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque que se conectan a instancias EC2 individuales y no son accesibles directamente por Internet como objetos descargables. Amazon EFS proporciona un sistema de archivos compartido que se puede montar en instancias EC2 mediante el protocolo NFS y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para servir objetos como contenido descargable p\xfablicamente por Internet. Amazon Instance Store proporciona almacenamiento en bloque temporal conectado f\xedsicamente al servidor host que ejecuta una instancia EC2, es ef\xedmero por naturaleza y no se puede acceder a \xe9l por Internet."},{"id":"q378","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios se puede utilizar para monitorear las solicitudes HTTP y HTTPS que se reenv\xedan a Amazon CloudFront?","options":{"A":"AWS WAF","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS Cloud9","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch monitorea las solicitudes HTTP y HTTPS reenviadas a las distribuciones de CloudFront, proporcionando m\xe9tricas como el recuento de solicitudes, las tasas de error y la latencia que se pueden visualizar y utilizar para configurar alarmas.\\nAWS WAF es un firewall de aplicaciones web que filtra y bloquea solicitudes HTTP y HTTPS maliciosas seg\xfan reglas definidas, pero inspecciona y controla el tr\xe1fico en lugar de monitorear e informar sobre las m\xe9tricas de las solicitudes. AWS Cloud9 es un entorno de desarrollo integrado basado en la nube para escribir, ejecutar y depurar c\xf3digo, y no tiene capacidad de monitoreo ni de an\xe1lisis de tr\xe1fico. AWS CloudTrail registra las llamadas a la API y la actividad de la cuenta en los servicios de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no monitorea las m\xe9tricas de solicitudes HTTP y HTTPS de las distribuciones de CloudFront."},{"id":"q383","question":"Una organizaci\xf3n tiene una aplicaci\xf3n heredada dise\xf1ada con una arquitectura monol\xedtica. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para desacoplar los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Amazon SQS","B":"Amazon SNS","C":"Amazon EventBridge","D":"AWS Step Functions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SQS es un servicio de colas de mensajes completamente administrado que desacopla los componentes de una aplicaci\xf3n al permitir que cada componente env\xede y reciba mensajes de forma independiente, lo que permite dividir una aplicaci\xf3n monol\xedtica en partes d\xe9bilmente acopladas que se comunican de forma as\xedncrona.\\nAmazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda de publicaci\xf3n/suscripci\xf3n que difunde mensajes a m\xfaltiples suscriptores simult\xe1neamente, lo cual es \xfatil para patrones de notificaci\xf3n de tipo fan-out, pero no es el servicio principal para desacoplar componentes de aplicaciones monol\xedticas mediante colas de mensajes as\xedncronas. Amazon EventBridge es un bus de eventos sin servidor que enruta eventos entre servicios de AWS, aplicaciones SaaS y aplicaciones personalizadas seg\xfan patrones de eventos; aunque admite arquitecturas desacopladas, se trata de enrutamiento basado en eventos en lugar de una cola de mensajes para desacoplar las dependencias entre componentes de una aplicaci\xf3n monol\xedtica. AWS Step Functions es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo sin servidor que coordina m\xfaltiples servicios de AWS en flujos de trabajo estructurados, lo cual se ocupa de la gesti\xf3n del estado del flujo de trabajo en lugar de proporcionar el mecanismo de colas de mensajes necesario para desacoplar los componentes de una aplicaci\xf3n monol\xedtica."},{"id":"q385","question":"Seg\xfan las buenas pr\xe1cticas, \xbfcu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones es la m\xe1s adecuada para procesar una gran cantidad de archivos binarios?","options":{"A":"Escalar verticalmente las instancias EC2","B":"Ejecutar instancias RDS en paralelo","C":"Escalar verticalmente las instancias RDS","D":"Ejecutar instancias EC2 en paralelo"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ejecutar m\xfaltiples instancias EC2 en paralelo distribuye el procesamiento de grandes archivos binarios entre muchas m\xe1quinas simult\xe1neamente, completando el trabajo m\xe1s r\xe1pido que escalar verticalmente una sola instancia.\\nEscalar verticalmente las instancias EC2 aumenta la potencia de una sola m\xe1quina, lo que tiene l\xedmites pr\xe1cticos y no distribuye la carga de trabajo entre m\xfaltiples procesadores simult\xe1neamente, por lo que resulta menos eficiente para tareas de procesamiento paralelo a gran escala. Ejecutar instancias RDS en paralelo no es aplicable al procesamiento de archivos binarios, ya que RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional dise\xf1ado para el almacenamiento y la consulta de datos estructurados, en lugar de cargas de trabajo de procesamiento de archivos. Escalar verticalmente las instancias RDS aumenta la capacidad y el rendimiento de la base de datos, lo cual tambi\xe9n es irrelevante para el procesamiento de archivos binarios, ya que RDS no tiene ning\xfan rol en las tareas de procesamiento de archivos basadas en c\xf3mputo."},{"id":"q389","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ofrece un sistema de archivos NFS que se puede montar de forma concurrente desde m\xfaltiples instancias EC2?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic File System","B":"Amazon Simple Storage Service","C":"Amazon Elastic Block Store","D":"AWS Storage Gateway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon EFS proporciona un sistema de archivos NFS completamente administrado que puede ser montado de forma concurrente por miles de instancias EC2, lo que permite el almacenamiento de archivos compartido entre m\xfaltiples servidores simult\xe1neamente.\\nAmazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos al que se accede mediante API o HTTP en lugar de montarse como un sistema de archivos, y no admite el protocolo NFS ni el montaje concurrente por parte de instancias EC2. Amazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento de bloques que solo se pueden adjuntar a una \xfanica instancia EC2 a la vez y no admite el montaje concurrente en m\xfaltiples instancias. AWS Storage Gateway es un servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido que conecta entornos locales con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para el montaje NFS concurrente en instancias EC2."},{"id":"q390","question":"Las Zonas de Disponibilidad dentro de una Regi\xf3n est\xe1n conectadas mediante enlaces de baja latencia. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es un beneficio de estos enlaces?","options":{"A":"Crear una conexi\xf3n privada a su centro de datos","B":"Lograr alta disponibilidad global","C":"Automatizar el proceso de aprovisionamiento de nuevos recursos de c\xf3mputo","D":"Hacer posible la replicaci\xf3n s\xedncrona de sus datos"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Los enlaces de baja latencia entre las Zonas de Disponibilidad dentro de una Regi\xf3n permiten la replicaci\xf3n s\xedncrona de datos, lo que significa que los datos escritos en una AZ se confirman inmediatamente como escritos en otra antes de que finalice la operaci\xf3n, lo que respalda la alta disponibilidad dentro de una Regi\xf3n.\\nCrear una conexi\xf3n privada a un centro de datos describe AWS Direct Connect, que es un servicio de red independiente y no tiene nada que ver con los enlaces internos entre Zonas de Disponibilidad. Lograr alta disponibilidad global requiere implementar en m\xfaltiples Regiones en lugar de depender de los enlaces de AZ dentro de una sola Regi\xf3n, lo que solo proporciona disponibilidad a nivel regional. Automatizar el aprovisionamiento de recursos de c\xf3mputo describe servicios como EC2 Auto Scaling y no est\xe1 relacionado con la conectividad de baja latencia que existe entre las Zonas de Disponibilidad."},{"id":"q391","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes afirmaciones son verdaderas con respecto a los lenguajes que son compatibles en AWS Lambda?","options":{"A":"Lambda solo admite Python y Node.js, pero hay plugins de terceros disponibles para convertir c\xf3digo en otros lenguajes a estos formatos","B":"Lambda admite de forma nativa varios lenguajes de programaci\xf3n como Node.js, Python y Java","C":"Lambda es el lenguaje de programaci\xf3n propietario de AWS para microservicios","D":"Lambda no admite lenguajes de programaci\xf3n; es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor","E":"Lambda puede admitir cualquier lenguaje de programaci\xf3n mediante una API"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"AWS Lambda admite de forma nativa varios lenguajes de programaci\xf3n, incluidos Node.js, Python, Java, Go y .NET, y puede admitir pr\xe1cticamente cualquier otro lenguaje mediante runtimes personalizados a trav\xe9s de la Lambda Runtime API.\\nQue Lambda solo admita Python y Node.js es falso, ya que Lambda tiene soporte nativo para una amplia gama de lenguajes sin necesidad de plugins de terceros. Que Lambda sea el lenguaje de programaci\xf3n propietario de AWS es falso, ya que Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo que ejecuta c\xf3digo escrito en lenguajes de programaci\xf3n est\xe1ndar, no un lenguaje en s\xed mismo. Que Lambda no admita lenguajes de programaci\xf3n es falso y contradice c\xf3mo funciona el servicio, ya que ejecutar c\xf3digo en un lenguaje compatible es su funci\xf3n principal."},{"id":"q392","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son las capacidades de AWS X-Ray?","options":{"A":"Desacopla autom\xe1ticamente los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n","B":"Facilita el seguimiento de las solicitudes de los usuarios para identificar problemas en la aplicaci\xf3n","C":"Ayuda a mejorar el rendimiento de la aplicaci\xf3n","D":"Implementa aplicaciones en instancias de Amazon EC2","E":"Implementa aplicaciones en servidores locales"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS X-Ray rastrea las solicitudes de los usuarios a medida que atraviesan su aplicaci\xf3n, lo que le ayuda a identificar cuellos de botella de rendimiento y errores, y proporciona la visibilidad necesaria para mejorar el rendimiento general de la aplicaci\xf3n.\\nDesacoplar autom\xe1ticamente los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n describe un patr\xf3n de arquitectura de dise\xf1o, no una capacidad de ning\xfan servicio de AWS en particular. AWS X-Ray observa y analiza el comportamiento existente de la aplicaci\xf3n, pero no modifica la arquitectura de la aplicaci\xf3n. Implementar aplicaciones en instancias de Amazon EC2 describe AWS CodeDeploy, que automatiza las implementaciones de aplicaciones en servicios de c\xf3mputo, incluido EC2. Implementar aplicaciones en servidores locales tambi\xe9n describe AWS CodeDeploy, que admite destinos de implementaci\xf3n m\xe1s all\xe1 de AWS, incluidos los servidores locales, lo que lo hace no relacionado con el seguimiento de aplicaciones ni el an\xe1lisis de rendimiento."},{"id":"q393","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes afirmaciones es verdadera con respecto a las zonas de disponibilidad y las ubicaciones de borde de AWS?","options":{"A":"Las ubicaciones de borde se encuentran en Zonas de Disponibilidad separadas en todo el mundo para atender a clientes globales","B":"Existe una zona de disponibilidad dentro de una ubicaci\xf3n de borde para distribuir contenido globalmente con baja latencia","C":"Una Zona de Disponibilidad es una ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica donde AWS proporciona m\xfaltiples ubicaciones de borde f\xedsicamente separadas y aisladas","D":"Una Zona de Disponibilidad de AWS es una ubicaci\xf3n aislada dentro de una Regi\xf3n de AWS, sin embargo, las ubicaciones de borde se encuentran en m\xfaltiples ciudades de todo el mundo"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Una Zona de Disponibilidad es una ubicaci\xf3n de centro de datos aislada dentro de una Regi\xf3n de AWS con energ\xeda, refrigeraci\xf3n y red independientes, mientras que las ubicaciones de borde son puntos de infraestructura separados distribuidos en cientos de ciudades de todo el mundo para servir contenido m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios finales.\\nQue las ubicaciones de borde se encuentren en Zonas de Disponibilidad separadas en todo el mundo para atender a clientes globales: las ubicaciones de borde son una capa de infraestructura independiente y no se encuentran dentro de las Zonas de Disponibilidad ni est\xe1n asociadas a ellas. Que exista una zona de disponibilidad dentro de una ubicaci\xf3n de borde para distribuir contenido globalmente con baja latencia: aqu\xed la relaci\xf3n est\xe1 invertida, y las Zonas de Disponibilidad forman parte de las Regiones, no est\xe1n contenidas dentro de las ubicaciones de borde. Que una Zona de Disponibilidad sea una ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica donde AWS proporciona m\xfaltiples ubicaciones de borde f\xedsicamente separadas y aisladas: esto confunde la definici\xf3n de una Zona de Disponibilidad con la de una Regi\xf3n, y las ubicaciones de borde no est\xe1n anidadas dentro de las Zonas de Disponibilidad."},{"id":"q396","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a los clientes crear una plantilla que defina mediante programaci\xf3n las pol\xedticas y configuraciones de todos los recursos de AWS como c\xf3digo, de modo que la misma plantilla se pueda reutilizar entre varios proyectos?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudFormation","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Auto Scaling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS CloudFormation permite definir toda la infraestructura como plantillas reutilizables en JSON o YAML, lo que habilita el aprovisionamiento consistente y repetible de las mismas configuraciones de recursos en varios proyectos.\\nAWS Config monitorea y registra continuamente el estado de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS existentes y los eval\xfaa frente a la configuraci\xf3n deseada, pero no aprovisiona infraestructura a partir de plantillas. AWS CloudTrail registra las llamadas a la API y la actividad de los usuarios en su cuenta de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, sin relaci\xf3n con el aprovisionamiento de infraestructura. AWS Auto Scaling ajusta autom\xe1ticamente la cantidad de recursos de c\xf3mputo en respuesta a la demanda, pero no define ni aprovisiona configuraciones de infraestructura como plantillas reutilizables."},{"id":"q398","question":"Un cliente planea migrar sus bases de datos de Microsoft SQL Server a AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS puede usar el cliente para ejecutar su base de datos de Microsoft SQL Server en AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Fargate","B":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"AWS Database Migration service (DMS)","E":"AWS Lambda"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Microsoft SQL Server puede ejecutarse en Amazon EC2 como una instalaci\xf3n autogestionada con control total a nivel de sistema operativo, o en Amazon RDS como un motor totalmente administrado donde AWS se encarga autom\xe1ticamente de los parches, las copias de seguridad y la alta disponibilidad.\\nAWS Fargate es un motor de c\xf3mputo sin servidor para ejecutar contenedores y no admite ejecutar una base de datos SQL Server directamente como un motor de base de datos administrado o autogestionado. AWS Database Migration Service se usa para migrar bases de datos desde un origen a un destino con un tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo, pero es una herramienta de migraci\xf3n en lugar de una plataforma para ejecutar SQL Server de forma continua. AWS Lambda es un servicio de ejecuci\xf3n de funciones sin servidor que ejecuta c\xf3digo basado en eventos y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para alojar ni ejecutar motores de bases de datos como SQL Server."},{"id":"q399","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS monitorea la disponibilidad de los endpoints y enruta autom\xe1ticamente el tr\xe1fico fuera de los recursos en mal estado?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudFormation","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"Amazon Aurora"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 realiza comprobaciones de estado en los endpoints, monitorea su disponibilidad y enruta autom\xe1ticamente el tr\xe1fico fuera de los recursos en mal estado para mantener la disponibilidad de la aplicaci\xf3n mediante la conmutaci\xf3n por error de DNS.\\nAWS CloudFormation aprovisiona y administra la infraestructura de AWS usando plantillas basadas en c\xf3digo, y no monitorea la disponibilidad de los endpoints ni enruta tr\xe1fico. Amazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas, registros y alarmas de los recursos de AWS, pero no realiza enrutamiento basado en DNS ni redirige el tr\xe1fico fuera de endpoints en mal estado. Amazon Aurora es un motor de base de datos relacional administrado y no tiene capacidad de monitoreo de endpoints ni de enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico."},{"id":"q400","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 desarrollando una aplicaci\xf3n que aprovechar\xe1 el reconocimiento facial para automatizar el etiquetado de fotos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar la empresa para el reconocimiento facial?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Textract","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Rekognition"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Rekognition usa aprendizaje profundo para analizar im\xe1genes y videos, y proporciona capacidades de reconocimiento facial, detecci\xf3n de objetos y an\xe1lisis de escenas que pueden automatizar tareas como el etiquetado de fotos.\\nAmazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que analiza y extrae significado del texto escrito, y no tiene capacidad para procesar im\xe1genes ni realizar reconocimiento facial. Amazon Textract extrae texto impreso, escritura a mano y datos estructurados de documentos escaneados e im\xe1genes, pero no realiza reconocimiento facial ni analiza escenas visuales. Amazon Polly es un servicio de texto a voz que convierte texto escrito en audio de sonido natural, y no tiene capacidad de an\xe1lisis de im\xe1genes ni de reconocimiento facial."},{"id":"q401","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son ejemplos de bases de datos administradas por AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon Neptune","B":"Amazon CloudSearch","C":"Microsoft SQL Server en Amazon EC2","D":"MySQL en Amazon EC2","E":"Amazon RDS for MySQL"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Amazon Neptune es una base de datos de grafos totalmente administrada, y Amazon RDS for MySQL es una base de datos relacional totalmente administrada, con AWS encarg\xe1ndose del aprovisionamiento, los parches, las copias de seguridad y la recuperaci\xf3n para ambas.\\nAmazon CloudSearch es un servicio de b\xfasqueda administrado para incorporar funcionalidad de b\xfasqueda en aplicaciones, y no es un servicio de base de datos. Microsoft SQL Server en Amazon EC2 es una implementaci\xf3n de base de datos autogestionada donde el cliente es responsable de la instalaci\xf3n, los parches, las copias de seguridad y el mantenimiento, lo que lo convierte en lo opuesto a una base de datos administrada por AWS. MySQL en Amazon EC2 tambi\xe9n es una implementaci\xf3n autogestionada con las mismas responsabilidades del cliente, y no se beneficia de las funciones de base de datos administrada que AWS ofrece mediante RDS."},{"id":"q404","question":"Necesita migrar una gran cantidad de cargas de trabajo locales a AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS es el m\xe1s adecuado?","options":{"A":"AWS File Transfer Acceleration","B":"AWS Application Migration Service","C":"AWS Database Migration Service","D":"AWS Application Discovery Service"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Application Migration Service automatiza la migraci\xf3n de cargas de trabajo de servidores locales a AWS mediante la replicaci\xf3n continua de los servidores de origen y permite la conmutaci\xf3n con un tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo, lo que lo convierte en el servicio recomendado para migraciones de servidores a gran escala.\\nAWS File Transfer Acceleration no es un servicio real de AWS y no existe en el cat\xe1logo de servicios de AWS. AWS Database Migration Service est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para migrar bases de datos en lugar de cargas de trabajo de servidores completas, lo que lo hace demasiado limitado para la migraci\xf3n general de cargas de trabajo locales. AWS Application Discovery Service recopila informaci\xf3n sobre entornos locales para ayudar a planificar migraciones, pero no realiza la migraci\xf3n en s\xed."},{"id":"q405","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son algunos beneficios clave de usar AWS CloudFormation?","options":{"A":"Ayuda a los clientes de AWS a implementar sus aplicaciones sin preocuparse por la infraestructura subyacente","B":"Aplica autom\xe1ticamente funciones avanzadas de seguridad de IAM","C":"Automatiza el aprovisionamiento y la actualizaci\xf3n de su infraestructura de forma segura y controlada","D":"Le permite modelar toda su infraestructura en solo un archivo de texto","E":"Compila y construye el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n de manera oportuna"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"AWS CloudFormation permite modelar toda su infraestructura en un archivo de texto JSON o YAML, y automatiza el aprovisionamiento y la actualizaci\xf3n de recursos de forma segura, controlada y repetible.\\nImplementar aplicaciones sin preocuparse por la infraestructura subyacente describe AWS Elastic Beanstalk, que abstrae la administraci\xf3n de la infraestructura para la implementaci\xf3n de aplicaciones, no CloudFormation. Aplicar autom\xe1ticamente funciones avanzadas de seguridad de IAM no es una capacidad de CloudFormation, ya que las pol\xedticas de IAM y las configuraciones de seguridad a\xfan deben ser definidas y administradas por el cliente. Compilar y construir el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n es una funci\xf3n de herramientas de compilaci\xf3n como AWS CodeBuild, no de CloudFormation, que se ocupa del aprovisionamiento de infraestructura en lugar del c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q407","question":"Una empresa aloja cargas de trabajo cr\xedticas para el negocio en una regi\xf3n de AWS. Para protegerse contra la p\xe9rdida de datos y garantizar la continuidad del negocio, se debe crear una imagen espejo del entorno actual de AWS en otra regi\xf3n de AWS. La pol\xedtica de la empresa exige que el entorno de espera est\xe9 disponible en minutos en caso de una interrupci\xf3n en la regi\xf3n primaria de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede usar para cumplir estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","B":"AWS Application Migration Service","C":"AWS Backup","D":"AWS Glue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery replica continuamente las m\xe1quinas en un \xe1rea de preparaci\xf3n en otra regi\xf3n de AWS y puede lanzar instancias totalmente aprovisionadas en minutos durante una interrupci\xf3n, lo que proporciona una conmutaci\xf3n por error r\xe1pida entre regiones.\\nAWS Application Migration Service es una herramienta de migraci\xf3n de servidores dise\xf1ada para mover cargas de trabajo a AWS, no para mantener un entorno de espera activo con fines de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres. AWS Backup es un servicio de copia de seguridad centralizado que automatiza la protecci\xf3n de datos en los servicios de AWS, pero no mantiene un entorno de espera replicado continuamente que pueda activarse en minutos. AWS Glue es un servicio de integraci\xf3n de datos sin servidor utilizado para cargas de trabajo ETL y catalogaci\xf3n de datos, y no tiene capacidad de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres ni de replicaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q408","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes clases de almacenamiento de S3 es m\xe1s apropiada para alojar recursos est\xe1ticos para un sitio web de comercio electr\xf3nico popular con patrones de acceso estables?","options":{"A":"S3 Standard-IA","B":"S3 Intelligent-Tiering","C":"S3 Glacier Deep Archive","D":"S3 Standard"},"correct":"D","explanation":"S3 Standard est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para datos de acceso frecuente con baja latencia y alto rendimiento, lo que lo convierte en la clase de almacenamiento m\xe1s apropiada para recursos est\xe1ticos en un sitio de comercio electr\xf3nico popular con patrones de acceso consistentes y estables.\\nS3 Standard-IA est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para datos a los que se accede con poca frecuencia pero que requieren acceso r\xe1pido cuando es necesario, y conlleva un cargo por recuperaci\xf3n que lo hace m\xe1s costoso que S3 Standard para recursos a los que se accede de forma consistente y frecuente. S3 Intelligent-Tiering mueve autom\xe1ticamente los objetos entre niveles de acceso seg\xfan patrones de acceso cambiantes, lo que lo hace m\xe1s adecuado para datos con frecuencia de acceso impredecible o desconocida que para recursos con demanda estable y predecible. S3 Glacier Deep Archive es la clase de almacenamiento de menor costo dise\xf1ada para el archivado a largo plazo de datos a los que rara vez se accede, si es que se accede, y es totalmente inadecuada para servir recursos de sitios web activos que requieren recuperaci\xf3n inmediata de baja latencia."},{"id":"q409","question":"Deseas crear una copia de seguridad de tus datos en otra ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica. \xbfD\xf3nde deber\xedas crear esta copia de seguridad?","options":{"A":"En otra ubicaci\xf3n de borde","B":"En otra Regi\xf3n","C":"En otra VPC","D":"En otra Zona de Disponibilidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Almacenar las copias de seguridad en otra Regi\xf3n de AWS coloca tus datos en una ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica completamente separada, lo que protege contra desastres a nivel de Regi\xf3n, como cat\xe1strofes naturales o interrupciones generalizadas.\\nLas ubicaciones de borde son puntos de presencia utilizados por servicios como Amazon CloudFront para almacenar en cach\xe9 y entregar contenido a los usuarios con baja latencia, y no son ubicaciones de almacenamiento donde se puedan crear o mantener copias de seguridad. Una VPC es una red virtual aislada l\xf3gicamente dentro de una \xfanica Regi\xf3n de AWS, y almacenar datos en otra VPC no los coloca en una ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica diferente. Una Zona de Disponibilidad es un centro de datos f\xedsicamente separado dentro de una \xfanica Regi\xf3n de AWS y, si bien almacenar copias de seguridad en otra AZ mejora la resiliencia frente a fallos localizados, no satisface el requisito de una ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica diferente, ya que varias AZ comparten la misma Regi\xf3n."},{"id":"q412","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una caracter\xedstica de Amazon RDS que realiza conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica cuando la base de datos principal deja de responder?","options":{"A":"RDS Single-AZ","B":"RDS Read Replicas","C":"RDS Snapshots","D":"RDS Multi-AZ"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon RDS Multi-AZ mantiene una r\xe9plica de reserva s\xedncrona en una Zona de Disponibilidad diferente y realiza autom\xe1ticamente la conmutaci\xf3n por error a ella si la base de datos principal deja de responder, minimizando el tiempo de inactividad.\\nRDS Single-AZ implementa la base de datos en una \xfanica Zona de Disponibilidad sin instancia de reserva, lo que significa que no hay conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica si la base de datos principal deja de estar disponible. RDS Read Replicas crea copias de solo lectura de la base de datos para descargar el tr\xe1fico de lectura de la instancia principal, pero no se sincronizan en tiempo real y no proporcionan conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica para alta disponibilidad. RDS Snapshots son copias de seguridad en un punto en el tiempo utilizadas para la recuperaci\xf3n y restauraci\xf3n de datos, y no pueden realizar conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica en respuesta a un fallo de la base de datos."},{"id":"q421","question":"Una empresa experimenta fluctuaciones en los patrones de tr\xe1fico hacia su sitio web de comercio electr\xf3nico cuando realiza ventas flash. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio puede ayudar a la empresa a igualar din\xe1micamente la capacidad de c\xf3mputo requerida para manejar picos de tr\xe1fico durante las ventas flash?","options":{"A":"AWS Auto Scaling","B":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud","C":"Amazon Elastic File System","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Auto Scaling ajusta autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias EC2 en respuesta a la demanda en tiempo real, agregando capacidad durante picos de tr\xe1fico como ventas flash y retir\xe1ndola cuando la demanda disminuye para controlar los costos.\\nAmazon EC2 proporciona las instancias de servidor virtual que ejecutan la aplicaci\xf3n, pero por s\xed solo no agrega ni quita capacidad autom\xe1ticamente en respuesta a las fluctuaciones de tr\xe1fico. Amazon Elastic File System es un servicio de almacenamiento de archivos compartido administrado para usar con EC2 y otros servicios de c\xf3mputo, no relacionado con el escalado din\xe1mico de c\xf3mputo. Amazon ElastiCache es un servicio de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria para mejorar el rendimiento de lectura de bases de datos y aplicaciones, no un servicio para ajustar din\xe1micamente la capacidad de c\xf3mputo."},{"id":"q422","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones es verdadera sobre Amazon VPC?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC permite a los clientes controlar las interacciones de los usuarios con todos los dem\xe1s recursos de AWS","B":"Los clientes de AWS tienen control completo sobre su entorno de redes virtuales de Amazon VPC","C":"AWS es responsable de todos los detalles de administraci\xf3n y configuraci\xf3n de Amazon VPC","D":"Amazon VPC ayuda a los clientes a revisar su arquitectura de AWS y adoptar las mejores pr\xe1cticas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon VPC brinda a los clientes control completo sobre su entorno de redes virtuales, incluida la selecci\xf3n de rangos de direcciones IP, la creaci\xf3n de subredes y la configuraci\xf3n de tablas de rutas y puertas de enlace de red.\\nControlar las interacciones de los usuarios con los recursos de AWS es funci\xf3n de AWS IAM, que administra permisos y pol\xedticas de acceso, no de Amazon VPC. AWS no es responsable de toda la administraci\xf3n y configuraci\xf3n de Amazon VPC, ya que los clientes conservan la responsabilidad total de configurar sus propias subredes, tablas de rutas, grupos de seguridad y ACL de red. Revisar la arquitectura de AWS y adoptar las mejores pr\xe1cticas describe AWS Well-Architected Tool, no Amazon VPC, que es un servicio de redes en lugar de un servicio de revisi\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica."},{"id":"q425","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para automatizar la configuraci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de instancias EC2?","options":{"A":"AWS OpsWorks","B":"AWS CloudFormation","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Systems Manager proporciona un conjunto de herramientas para automatizar la configuraci\xf3n, el mantenimiento y la administraci\xf3n de instancias EC2 a escala, incluyendo la aplicaci\xf3n de pol\xedticas de configuraci\xf3n, la ejecuci\xf3n de comandos en flotas y el mantenimiento del estado deseado sin intervenci\xf3n manual.\\nAWS OpsWorks (obsoleto) era un servicio de gesti\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n que utilizaba Puppet y Chef, y AWS lo discontinu\xf3 en mayo de 2024, por lo que ya no est\xe1 disponible. AWS CloudFormation automatiza el aprovisionamiento de la infraestructura de AWS mediante plantillas, pero opera a nivel de infraestructura en lugar de administrar la configuraci\xf3n continua de instancias en ejecuci\xf3n. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no tiene capacidad de configuraci\xf3n ni automatizaci\xf3n de servidores."},{"id":"q427","question":"Selecciona los servicios que est\xe1n basados en servidores:","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"AWS Fargate","E":"Amazon EMR"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Amazon RDS se ejecuta en instancias de servidor de bases de datos aprovisionadas que los clientes seleccionan y administran, y Amazon EMR se ejecuta en cl\xfasteres de instancias EC2 para el procesamiento de big data, por lo que ambos son servicios basados en servidores.\\nAmazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL totalmente serverless que escala autom\xe1ticamente sin requerir aprovisionamiento ni administraci\xf3n de servidores. AWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo serverless que ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin requerir que los clientes aprovisionen o administren servidores. AWS Fargate es un motor de c\xf3mputo serverless para contenedores que elimina la necesidad de aprovisionar o administrar las instancias EC2 subyacentes."},{"id":"q429","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son casos de uso de Amazon EMR?","options":{"A":"Te permite respaldar cantidades extremadamente grandes de datos a costos muy bajos","B":"Te permite mover datos a escala de exabytes desde centros de datos locales hacia AWS","C":"Te permite analizar y procesar cantidades extremadamente grandes de datos de manera oportuna","D":"Te permite ejecutar y escalar f\xe1cilmente Apache Spark, Hadoop y otros frameworks de Big Data","E":"Te permite ejecutar y administrar f\xe1cilmente contenedores Docker"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Amazon EMR proporciona una plataforma de cl\xfaster administrada para ejecutar frameworks de big data como Apache Spark y Hadoop, lo que permite tanto el an\xe1lisis como el procesamiento de conjuntos de datos extremadamente grandes a escala.\\nRespaldar cantidades extremadamente grandes de datos a costos muy bajos describe Amazon S3 combinado con las clases de almacenamiento S3 Glacier, que est\xe1n dise\xf1adas espec\xedficamente para el almacenamiento y archivado de datos duraderos y de bajo costo. Mover datos a escala de exabytes desde centros de datos locales hacia AWS describe AWS Snowmobile, que es un servicio de transferencia f\xedsica de datos para migrar conjuntos de datos extremadamente grandes mediante un contenedor de env\xedo seguro. Ejecutar y administrar contenedores Docker describe Amazon ECS o AWS Fargate, que son servicios de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores no relacionados con frameworks de procesamiento de big data."},{"id":"q431","question":"Un desarrollador quiere implementar y gestionar r\xe1pidamente su aplicaci\xf3n en la nube de AWS, pero no tiene ninguna experiencia con computaci\xf3n en la nube. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS ayudar\xeda al desarrollador a lograr su objetivo?","options":{"A":"AWS Fargate","B":"AWS Batch","C":"Amazon Personalize","D":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk gestiona autom\xe1ticamente los detalles de implementaci\xf3n del aprovisionamiento de capacidad, el balanceo de carga, el escalado autom\xe1tico y la supervisi\xf3n del estado de la aplicaci\xf3n, lo que permite a los desarrolladores sin experiencia en infraestructura en la nube implementar y gestionar aplicaciones sin necesidad de comprender la infraestructura subyacente.\\nAWS Fargate es un motor de computaci\xf3n sin servidor para contenedores que elimina la necesidad de gestionar servidores para cargas de trabajo en contenedores, pero requiere conocimiento de conceptos y configuraci\xf3n de contenedores, lo que lo hace m\xe1s complejo de adoptar para desarrolladores sin experiencia en la nube en comparaci\xf3n con la abstracci\xf3n que proporciona Beanstalk. AWS Batch gestiona la infraestructura necesaria para ejecutar trabajos de computaci\xf3n por lotes a escala, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para procesar grandes cantidades de cargas de trabajo por lotes en lugar de ayudar a los desarrolladores a implementar y gestionar r\xe1pidamente aplicaciones de prop\xf3sito general. Amazon Personalize es un servicio de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico totalmente gestionado que crea sistemas de personalizaci\xf3n y recomendaci\xf3n en tiempo real, y es un servicio de IA/ML para un caso de uso espec\xedfico en lugar de una plataforma general de implementaci\xf3n y gesti\xf3n de aplicaciones."},{"id":"q433","question":"Para las bases de datos Amazon RDS, \xbfqu\xe9 realiza AWS en su nombre?","options":{"A":"Configuraci\xf3n de la base de datos","B":"Protecci\xf3n del tr\xe1fico de red","C":"Gesti\xf3n del sistema operativo","D":"Gesti\xf3n de acceso","E":"Gesti\xf3n de reglas de firewall"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Para Amazon RDS, AWS se encarga de la configuraci\xf3n inicial de la base de datos, incluido el aprovisionamiento y la configuraci\xf3n, y gestiona el sistema operativo subyacente, incluidos la aplicaci\xf3n de parches y el mantenimiento, lo que elimina estas cargas operativas del cliente.\\nLa protecci\xf3n del tr\xe1fico de red es una responsabilidad compartida en la que los clientes configuran grupos de seguridad y NACL para controlar el tr\xe1fico hacia sus instancias RDS, en lugar de que AWS lo gestione en nombre del cliente. La gesti\xf3n de acceso es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes controlan sus propias pol\xedticas de IAM y las credenciales de usuario de la base de datos. La gesti\xf3n de reglas de firewall es responsabilidad del cliente, ya que los clientes definen las reglas de los grupos de seguridad que controlan el acceso de entrada y salida a sus instancias RDS."},{"id":"q435","question":"Una empresa de medios tiene una aplicaci\xf3n que requiere la transferencia de grandes conjuntos de datos hacia y desde AWS todos los d\xedas. Estos datos son cr\xedticos para el negocio y deben transferirse mediante una conexi\xf3n constante. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda utilizar la empresa?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"AWS Snowmobile","D":"AWS VPN"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre un centro de datos local y AWS, lo que proporciona un rendimiento constante y una latencia menor que las conexiones basadas en Internet, lo que lo convierte en la opci\xf3n correcta para transferencias diarias de datos a gran escala cr\xedticos para el negocio.\\nAmazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural para extraer informaci\xf3n y relaciones del texto y no tiene ning\xfan rol en la conectividad de red ni en la transferencia de datos. AWS Snowmobile es un servicio de transferencia f\xedsica de datos para la migraci\xf3n \xfanica de conjuntos de datos extremadamente grandes a AWS, no para transferencias diarias recurrentes que requieran una conexi\xf3n de red constante. AWS VPN establece una conexi\xf3n cifrada a trav\xe9s de Internet p\xfablica, que no proporciona las garant\xedas de rendimiento constante y confiabilidad requeridas para transferencias diarias de grandes datos cr\xedticos para el negocio."},{"id":"q436","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el principal beneficio del servicio AWS Storage Gateway?","options":{"A":"Automatiza el proceso de creaci\xf3n, mantenimiento y ejecuci\xf3n de trabajos ETL","B":"Proporciona dispositivos f\xedsicos para migrar datos desde instalaciones locales a AWS","C":"Permite la integraci\xf3n de entornos de TI locales con el almacenamiento en la nube","D":"Proporciona almacenamiento de claves basado en hardware para el cumplimiento normativo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Storage Gateway integra entornos de TI locales con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS al proporcionar protocolos de almacenamiento est\xe1ndar como NFS, SMB e iSCSI que conectan aplicaciones locales de forma transparente con S3, EBS o Glacier.\\nAutomatizar el proceso de creaci\xf3n, mantenimiento y ejecuci\xf3n de trabajos ETL describe AWS Glue, que es un servicio de integraci\xf3n de datos sin servidor para preparar y transformar datos para canalizaciones de anal\xedtica. Proporcionar dispositivos f\xedsicos para migrar datos desde instalaciones locales a AWS describe la familia AWS Snow, espec\xedficamente AWS Snowball o Snowmobile, que son dispositivos f\xedsicos para la transferencia masiva de datos en lugar de la integraci\xf3n de almacenamiento h\xedbrido continua. Proporcionar almacenamiento de claves basado en hardware para el cumplimiento normativo describe AWS CloudHSM, que es un m\xf3dulo de seguridad de hardware dedicado para generar y gestionar claves criptogr\xe1ficas."},{"id":"q437","question":"Para protegerte contra la p\xe9rdida de datos, necesitas hacer copias de seguridad de tu base de datos con regularidad. \xbfCu\xe1l es la opci\xf3n de almacenamiento m\xe1s rentable que proporciona la recuperaci\xf3n inmediata de tus copias de seguridad?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive","B":"Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access","C":"Amazon S3 Glacier","D":"Instance Store"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access ofrece costos de almacenamiento m\xe1s bajos que S3 Standard y aun as\xed proporciona recuperaci\xf3n inmediata en milisegundos, lo que lo hace rentable para copias de seguridad a las que se accede con poca frecuencia pero que deben estar disponibles al instante.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive es la clase de almacenamiento de menor costo para datos de archivo a largo plazo a los que se accede muy rara vez, con tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n de 12 horas o m\xe1s, lo que lo hace inadecuado para copias de seguridad que requieren recuperaci\xf3n inmediata. Amazon S3 Glacier proporciona almacenamiento de archivo de bajo costo con opciones de recuperaci\xf3n que van desde minutos hasta horas seg\xfan el nivel de recuperaci\xf3n seleccionado, y aunque es menos costoso que S3 Standard, no proporciona la recuperaci\xf3n inmediata en milisegundos requerida para la recuperaci\xf3n de copias de seguridad. Instance Store proporciona almacenamiento de bloques temporal conectado f\xedsicamente a la m\xe1quina host que ejecuta una instancia EC2, y es almacenamiento ef\xedmero que se pierde cuando la instancia se detiene, lo que lo hace totalmente inadecuado para el almacenamiento duradero de copias de seguridad."},{"id":"q438","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio puedes usar para enrutar el tr\xe1fico al endpoint que proporciona el mejor rendimiento de la aplicaci\xf3n para tus usuarios en todo el mundo?","options":{"A":"AWS Global Accelerator","B":"AWS Data Pipeline","C":"Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","D":"Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator utiliza la red global de AWS para enrutar el tr\xe1fico de los usuarios al endpoint \xf3ptimo y saludable seg\xfan el rendimiento, la geograf\xeda y las comprobaciones de estado, lo que mejora el rendimiento y la disponibilidad de las aplicaciones para usuarios globales.\\nAWS Data Pipeline (obsoleto) es un servicio para orquestar y automatizar el movimiento y la transformaci\xf3n de datos entre servicios de AWS, no est\xe1 relacionado con el enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico ni con el rendimiento de las aplicaciones. Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator es una cach\xe9 en memoria espec\xedficamente para DynamoDB que reduce la latencia de lectura de las consultas a la base de datos, no es un servicio para enrutar el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones a endpoints \xf3ptimos. Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration acelera las cargas a buckets de S3 al enrutar el tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de ubicaciones de borde de CloudFront, lo cual es espec\xedfico para la transferencia de datos de S3 y no una soluci\xf3n general de enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones."},{"id":"q440","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes opciones son casos de uso del servicio Amazon Route 53?","options":{"A":"Conectividad punto a punto entre un centro de datos local y AWS","B":"Detecta cambios de configuraci\xf3n en el entorno de AWS","C":"Configuraci\xf3n y gesti\xf3n de DNS","D":"Gestiona el tr\xe1fico global de aplicaciones mediante una variedad de tipos de enrutamiento","E":"Proporciona recomendaciones de optimizaci\xf3n de seguridad de la infraestructura"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS escalable que traduce nombres de dominio a direcciones IP, y gestiona el tr\xe1fico global de aplicaciones mediante pol\xedticas de enrutamiento que incluyen enrutamiento basado en latencia, geolocalizaci\xf3n, ponderado y por conmutaci\xf3n por error.\\nLa conectividad punto a punto entre un centro de datos local y AWS describe AWS Direct Connect, que proporciona un enlace de red privada dedicado entre la infraestructura local y AWS. La detecci\xf3n de cambios de configuraci\xf3n en el entorno de AWS describe AWS Config, que supervisa y registra continuamente las configuraciones de los recursos y las eval\xfaa con respecto a la configuraci\xf3n deseada. Proporcionar recomendaciones de optimizaci\xf3n de seguridad de la infraestructura describe AWS Trusted Advisor, que analiza tu entorno seg\xfan comprobaciones de pr\xe1cticas recomendadas en seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos."},{"id":"q441","question":"Quieres transferir 200 terabytes de datos desde ubicaciones locales a la nube de AWS. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones puede realizar el trabajo de forma rentable?","options":{"A":"AWS Snowmobile","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS DMS","D":"AWS Snowball"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico de transferencia de datos que mueve hasta 80 TB por dispositivo, lo que lo hace rentable para transferir 200 TB de datos sin depender de transferencias de red lentas o costosas.\\nAWS Snowmobile est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para migraciones a escala de exabytes, normalmente 10 PB o m\xe1s, lo que lo hace mucho m\xe1s de lo necesario y no rentable para una transferencia de 200 TB. AWS Direct Connect proporciona un enlace de red privada dedicado entre la infraestructura local y AWS, pero transferir 200 TB a trav\xe9s de una conexi\xf3n de red ser\xeda lento y costoso en comparaci\xf3n con el transporte f\xedsico de datos. AWS DMS est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para migrar bases de datos en lugar de transferir grandes vol\xfamenes de datos no estructurados entre ubicaciones locales y AWS."},{"id":"q442","question":"Tiene una aplicaci\xf3n de IoT en tiempo real que requiere una latencia de submilisegundos. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon Athena","C":"AWS Cloud9","D":"Amazon ElastiCache for Redis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon ElastiCache for Redis es un almac\xe9n de datos en memoria que ofrece tiempos de respuesta de submilisegundos, lo que lo convierte en la opci\xf3n adecuada para una aplicaci\xf3n de IoT en tiempo real que requiere una latencia extremadamente baja.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos administrado optimizado para ejecutar consultas SQL anal\xedticas sobre grandes conjuntos de datos, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para an\xe1lisis por lotes orientados al rendimiento en lugar de acceso a datos en tiempo real de submilisegundos. Amazon Athena es un servicio de consultas sin servidor que analiza datos almacenados en Amazon S3 mediante SQL, e implica tiempos de ejecuci\xf3n de consultas medidos en segundos en lugar de la latencia de submilisegundos que requieren las aplicaciones de IoT en tiempo real. AWS Cloud9 es un entorno de desarrollo integrado basado en la nube para escribir, ejecutar y depurar c\xf3digo, y es una herramienta para desarrolladores sin capacidad de almacenamiento de datos ni de servicio de baja latencia."},{"id":"q444","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ayuda a los desarrolladores a compilar y probar su c\xf3digo?","options":{"A":"AWS CodeDeploy","B":"AWS CodeCommit","C":"AWS CodePipeline","D":"AWS CodeBuild"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS CodeBuild es un servicio de integraci\xf3n continua totalmente administrado que compila c\xf3digo fuente, ejecuta pruebas y produce paquetes de software listos para implementar sin requerir que administre servidores de compilaci\xf3n.\\nAWS CodeDeploy automatiza la implementaci\xf3n de aplicaciones en instancias EC2, funciones Lambda y servidores locales, pero no compila ni prueba c\xf3digo. AWS CodeCommit es un servicio de control de c\xf3digo fuente administrado para alojar repositorios Git, utilizado para almacenar y versionar c\xf3digo en lugar de compilarlo o probarlo. AWS CodePipeline orquesta las etapas de una canalizaci\xf3n de lanzamiento, incluidas las fases de origen, compilaci\xf3n e implementaci\xf3n, pero delega la compilaci\xf3n y las pruebas reales a CodeBuild en lugar de realizarlas directamente."},{"id":"q446","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 proporciona el servicio Amazon CloudFront?","options":{"A":"Rastrea la actividad de los usuarios y el uso de API","B":"Aumenta la disponibilidad de la aplicaci\xf3n mediante el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en el edge","C":"Permite una recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres m\xe1s r\xe1pida","D":"Almacena datos archivados a costos muy bajos","E":"Entrega contenido a los usuarios finales con baja latencia"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Amazon CloudFront es una CDN que almacena contenido en cach\xe9 en ubicaciones de edge en todo el mundo, lo que aumenta la disponibilidad de la aplicaci\xf3n mediante almacenamiento en cach\xe9 redundante y entrega contenido a los usuarios finales con baja latencia independientemente de su ubicaci\xf3n.\\nEl seguimiento de la actividad de los usuarios y del uso de API en una cuenta de AWS describe AWS CloudTrail, un servicio de registro de auditor\xeda que no est\xe1 relacionado con la entrega de contenido ni con el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en el edge. La recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres m\xe1s r\xe1pida es un beneficio asociado con la arquitectura multirregi\xf3n y servicios como AWS Backup o el enrutamiento de conmutaci\xf3n por error de Route 53, no una capacidad proporcionada por una red de entrega de contenido. El almacenamiento de datos archivados a costos muy bajos describe Amazon S3 Glacier, un servicio de almacenamiento de archivos sin capacidad de entrega de contenido ni de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en el edge."},{"id":"q447","question":"Est\xe1 enfrentando muchos problemas con su centro de contacto actual. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio proporciona un centro de contacto basado en la nube que puede ofrecer un mejor servicio a sus clientes?","options":{"A":"Amazon Lightsail","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube que le permite configurar un centro de servicio al cliente en minutos con precios de pago por uso y sin infraestructura que administrar, lo que lo convierte en la soluci\xf3n creada espec\xedficamente para reemplazar un centro de contacto tradicional.\\nAmazon Lightsail es una plataforma en la nube simplificada para implementar aplicaciones peque\xf1as y sitios web con precios predecibles, y no tiene funcionalidad de centro de contacto ni de comunicaci\xf3n con clientes. AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, y es un servicio de redes completamente no relacionado con las operaciones de un centro de contacto. AWS Elastic Beanstalk es un servicio PaaS para implementar y administrar aplicaciones web, no una plataforma de centro de contacto ni de servicio al cliente."},{"id":"q451","question":"Administra un blog en AWS que tiene diferentes entornos: desarrollo, pruebas y producci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 puede usar para crear una consola personalizada para cada entorno y ver y administrar sus recursos f\xe1cilmente?","options":{"A":"AWS Resource Groups","B":"AWS Placement Groups","C":"AWS Management Console","D":"AWS Tag Editor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Resource Groups le permite crear grupos l\xf3gicos de recursos basados en etiquetas, lo que le permite ver y administrar los recursos de cada entorno, como desarrollo, pruebas y producci\xf3n, desde una vista de consola personalizada.\\nAWS Placement Groups controla c\xf3mo se colocan f\xedsicamente las instancias EC2 en el hardware subyacente para optimizar la latencia o la disponibilidad, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en la organizaci\xf3n o visualizaci\xf3n de recursos por entorno. AWS Management Console es la interfaz basada en web para acceder a todos los servicios de AWS, pero no proporciona vistas agrupadas personalizadas por entorno por s\xed sola. AWS Tag Editor es una herramienta para encontrar y administrar etiquetas en los recursos, pero no crea vistas de consola personalizadas para la administraci\xf3n de recursos agrupados."},{"id":"q452","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS recopila m\xe9tricas de las instancias EC2 en ejecuci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS CloudFormation","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch recopila y monitorea m\xe9tricas de las instancias EC2, incluida la utilizaci\xf3n de CPU, E/S de disco y tr\xe1fico de red, lo que proporciona visibilidad del rendimiento y el estado de las instancias.\\nAmazon Inspector es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad automatizado que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y no recopila m\xe9tricas de rendimiento. AWS CloudFormation es un servicio de infraestructura como c\xf3digo que aprovisiona y administra recursos de AWS mediante plantillas, y no tiene capacidad de monitoreo ni de recopilaci\xf3n de m\xe9tricas. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta en los servicios de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no recopila m\xe9tricas de rendimiento a nivel de instancia."},{"id":"q453","question":"Su aplicaci\xf3n web actualmente enfrenta problemas de rendimiento y sufre tiempos de carga prolongados. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS podr\xeda ayudar a solucionar estos problemas y mejorar el rendimiento?","options":{"A":"Amazon Detective","B":"AWS X-Ray","C":"AWS Security Hub","D":"AWS Shield"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS X-Ray rastrea las solicitudes a medida que viajan por su aplicaci\xf3n, identificando cuellos de botella de rendimiento y ayud\xe1ndole a determinar la causa ra\xedz de los tiempos de carga prolongados y los problemas de latencia.\\nAmazon Detective es un servicio de investigaci\xf3n de seguridad que analiza y visualiza datos de los registros de AWS para ayudar a identificar la causa ra\xedz de hallazgos de seguridad y actividad sospechosa, y no tiene capacidad para diagnosticar problemas de rendimiento de aplicaciones ni tiempos de carga. AWS Security Hub agrega y prioriza hallazgos de seguridad de m\xfaltiples servicios de AWS y herramientas de terceros en un panel centralizado, y es una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de la postura de seguridad en lugar de un servicio de diagn\xf3stico de rendimiento de aplicaciones. AWS Shield es un servicio administrado de protecci\xf3n contra DDoS que protege las aplicaciones contra ataques de denegaci\xf3n de servicio distribuido, y no aborda cuellos de botella de rendimiento de aplicaciones ni problemas de tiempo de carga."},{"id":"q454","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes recursos de c\xf3mputo son sin servidor?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"AWS Fargate","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"Amazon ECS","E":"Amazon EMR"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS Fargate ejecuta contenedores sin requerir que aprovisione o gestione los servidores subyacentes, y AWS Lambda ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin aprovisionar infraestructura. Ambos son servicios de c\xf3mputo sin servidor donde AWS gestiona toda la infraestructura subyacente.\\nAmazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales donde los clientes son responsables de aprovisionar, configurar y administrar las instancias, lo que lo convierte en un servicio de c\xf3mputo basado en servidores en lugar de sin servidor. Amazon ECS es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores que, cuando se usa sin Fargate, requiere que los clientes administren las instancias EC2 subyacentes en el cl\xfaster. Amazon EMR es un servicio administrado de procesamiento de macrodatos que se ejecuta en cl\xfasteres aprovisionados de instancias EC2, no un recurso de c\xf3mputo sin servidor."},{"id":"q457","question":"Una organizaci\xf3n necesita crear una aplicaci\xf3n financiera que requiera soporte para transacciones ACID. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de base de datos de AWS es m\xe1s apropiado en este caso?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon RDS","C":"AWS CloudHSM","D":"AWS DMS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional administrado que admite transacciones ACID de forma nativa a trav\xe9s de motores como MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle y SQL Server, lo que lo hace ideal para aplicaciones financieras que requieren consistencia de datos.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos en la nube optimizado para consultas anal\xedticas sobre grandes conjuntos de datos y, aunque tiene cierto soporte transaccional, no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para el procesamiento de transacciones ACID de alto volumen que requieren las aplicaciones financieras. AWS CloudHSM es un servicio de m\xf3dulo de seguridad de hardware que proporciona almacenamiento y procesamiento dedicados de claves criptogr\xe1ficas, y no tiene capacidad de base de datos ni de gesti\xf3n de transacciones. AWS DMS es un servicio de migraci\xf3n de bases de datos que se utiliza para mover datos entre plataformas de bases de datos, y no es un motor de base de datos que pueda alojar o procesar transacciones de aplicaciones."},{"id":"q460","question":"Amazon RDS admite varios motores de base de datos para elegir. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes no es uno de ellos?","options":{"A":"PostgreSQL","B":"Oracle","C":"Microsoft SQL Server","D":"Teradata"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Teradata no es un motor compatible en Amazon RDS. Los motores compatibles son PostgreSQL, Oracle, Microsoft SQL Server, MySQL, MariaDB y Amazon Aurora.\\nPostgreSQL es un motor de RDS totalmente compatible disponible tanto para implementaciones est\xe1ndar como Aurora. Oracle es un motor de RDS compatible, que permite a los clientes ejecutar bases de datos Oracle en infraestructura administrada. Microsoft SQL Server es un motor de RDS compatible, disponible en varias ediciones, incluidas Express, Web, Standard y Enterprise."},{"id":"q461","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS te ayudar\xeda a migrar bases de datos locales a AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS DMS","B":"Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration","C":"AWS Directory Service","D":"AWS Transit Gateway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Database Migration Service est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para migrar bases de datos desde entornos locales a AWS, y admite tanto migraciones homog\xe9neas, como de MySQL a MySQL, como migraciones heterog\xe9neas entre diferentes motores de bases de datos, con un tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo durante la migraci\xf3n.\\nAmazon S3 Transfer Acceleration utiliza ubicaciones de borde de CloudFront para acelerar las cargas de datos a S3 y es una funci\xf3n de aceleraci\xf3n de transferencia de archivos, no un servicio de migraci\xf3n de bases de datos. AWS Directory Service proporciona integraci\xf3n administrada con Microsoft Active Directory para la gesti\xf3n de identidades empresariales y no tiene capacidad para migrar o replicar datos de bases de datos relacionales o NoSQL. AWS Transit Gateway conecta m\xfaltiples VPC y redes locales a trav\xe9s de un concentrador central para el enrutamiento y la conectividad de red, y es un servicio de red que facilita el enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico, no una herramienta de migraci\xf3n de bases de datos."},{"id":"q462","question":"Para los nuevos clientes de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1l es la forma M\xc1S F\xc1CIL de lanzar un sitio web simple de WordPress en AWS?","options":{"A":"Ejecutar WordPress en una instancia de Amazon Lightsail","B":"Instalar WordPress en una instancia de Amazon EC2","C":"Usar la funci\xf3n de alojamiento web de Amazon S3","D":"Alojar el sitio web directamente en AWS Cloud Development Kit (AWS CDK)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Lightsail proporciona planos preconfigurados, incluida una instalaci\xf3n de WordPress con un solo clic, lo que la convierte en la opci\xf3n m\xe1s sencilla para clientes nuevos que desean un sitio web simple sin administrar la infraestructura subyacente.\\nInstalar WordPress en una instancia de EC2 requiere aprovisionar manualmente un servidor, configurar redes, instalar una pila web y gestionar el mantenimiento continuo, lo que lo hace significativamente m\xe1s complejo que Lightsail para un caso de uso simple. Amazon S3 puede alojar sitios web est\xe1ticos, pero no admite WordPress, que requiere procesamiento del lado del servidor y una base de datos. AWS CDK es un marco de infraestructura como c\xf3digo para definir recursos en la nube mediante programaci\xf3n y no puede alojar sitios web o aplicaciones directamente."},{"id":"q464","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios te permite instalar y ejecutar software de base de datos relacional personalizado?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon Cognito","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales con acceso completo a nivel de sistema operativo, lo que le permite instalar y ejecutar cualquier software de base de datos personalizado que elija en la instancia subyacente.\\nAmazon Cognito es un servicio de identidad de usuario y autenticaci\xf3n para aplicaciones web y m\xf3viles que gestiona el registro, el inicio de sesi\xf3n y el control de acceso de los usuarios, y no tiene capacidad para alojar o ejecutar software de base de datos. Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional administrado que admite motores de base de datos preconfigurados espec\xedficos, como MySQL, PostgreSQL y SQL Server, y no permite instalar o ejecutar software de base de datos personalizado fuera de esos motores compatibles. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad que analiza las cargas de trabajo de AWS en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y no tiene ning\xfan papel en el alojamiento o la ejecuci\xf3n de software de base de datos."},{"id":"q465","question":"Los requisitos de CPU y RAM de tu aplicaci\xf3n cambian de forma impredecible. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio se puede utilizar para ajustar din\xe1micamente estos recursos seg\xfan la carga?","options":{"A":"AWS Auto Scaling","B":"Elastic Load Balancing","C":"Amazon Route 53","D":"Amazon Elastic Container Service"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Auto Scaling ajusta din\xe1micamente el n\xfamero de instancias EC2 seg\xfan m\xe9tricas de demanda en tiempo real, como la utilizaci\xf3n de CPU y memoria, y agrega o elimina capacidad autom\xe1ticamente a medida que cambian los requisitos de carga de trabajo.\\nElastic Load Balancing distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre m\xfaltiples destinos para mejorar la disponibilidad y evitar que una sola instancia se sobrecargue, pero no agrega ni elimina recursos de c\xf3mputo en respuesta a requisitos cambiantes de CPU y RAM. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico que resuelve nombres de dominio y dirige a los usuarios a los endpoints de las aplicaciones, y no tiene capacidad para aprovisionar o ajustar recursos de c\xf3mputo. Amazon Elastic Container Service es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores para ejecutar y administrar aplicaciones en contenedores y, aunque puede escalar tareas, no es la soluci\xf3n dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para ajustar din\xe1micamente recursos de c\xf3mputo de EC2 seg\xfan cargas impredecibles."},{"id":"q471","question":"Para algunos servicios, AWS replica autom\xe1ticamente los datos entre m\xfaltiples zonas de disponibilidad para proporcionar tolerancia a fallos en caso de falla de un servidor o interrupci\xf3n de una zona de disponibilidad. \xbfQu\xe9 servicios replican autom\xe1ticamente los datos entre zonas de disponibilidad?","options":{"A":"Instance Store","B":"S3","C":"DynamoDB","D":"Amazon Route 53","E":"AWS VPN"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 replica autom\xe1ticamente los objetos en un m\xednimo de tres zonas de disponibilidad dentro de una regi\xf3n, y DynamoDB replica los datos en m\xfaltiples AZ de forma predeterminada; ambos proporcionan tolerancia a fallos integrada.\\nInstance Store es almacenamiento de bloques temporal conectado directamente a la instancia EC2 host. No se replica y los datos se pierden si la instancia se detiene o falla. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico y no replica datos entre AZ de la misma manera. AWS VPN es un servicio de conectividad de red para conectar de forma segura redes locales a AWS, no relacionado con la replicaci\xf3n de datos."},{"id":"q475","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puedes proteger los datos almacenados en Amazon S3 contra la eliminaci\xf3n accidental?","options":{"A":"Habilitando S3 Versioning","B":"Configurando S3 Bucket Policies","C":"Configurando S3 Lifecycle Policies","D":"Deshabilitando S3 Cross-Region Replication (CRR)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"S3 Versioning conserva cada versi\xf3n de cada objeto en el bucket, por lo que incluso si un objeto se elimina accidentalmente, la versi\xf3n anterior se conserva y se puede restaurar para recuperar los datos.\\nConfigurar S3 Bucket Policies controla qui\xe9n tiene permiso para acceder, cargar o eliminar objetos en un bucket, pero no conserva los objetos eliminados ni mantiene versiones anteriores para su recuperaci\xf3n. Configurar S3 Lifecycle Policies automatiza la transici\xf3n de objetos entre clases de almacenamiento o programa su vencimiento despu\xe9s de un per\xedodo definido, lo que en realidad puede provocar que los objetos se eliminen en lugar de protegerlos contra la eliminaci\xf3n accidental. Deshabilitar S3 Cross-Region Replication elimina la capacidad de copiar objetos a un bucket en otra regi\xf3n con fines de redundancia, y no tiene efecto en la prevenci\xf3n o recuperaci\xf3n de la eliminaci\xf3n accidental en el bucket de origen."},{"id":"q478","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes NO es un beneficio de usar AWS Lambda?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda ejecuta c\xf3digo sin aprovisionar ni administrar servidores","B":"AWS Lambda proporciona capacidad de c\xf3mputo redimensionable en la nube","C":"No hay cargo cuando tu c\xf3digo de AWS Lambda no se est\xe1 ejecutando","D":"AWS Lambda se puede llamar directamente desde cualquier app m\xf3vil"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las funciones de AWS Lambda no se pueden invocar directamente desde una app m\xf3vil sin usar una capa de API intermedia, como Amazon API Gateway o AWS AppSync; no se admite la invocaci\xf3n directa desde c\xf3digo externo arbitrario, lo que hace que esta afirmaci\xf3n sea falsa en lugar de un beneficio genuino.\\nQue Lambda ejecute c\xf3digo sin aprovisionar ni administrar servidores es un beneficio genuino; elimina la necesidad de seleccionar, aplicar parches o mantener la infraestructura de c\xf3mputo subyacente. Lambda s\xed proporciona capacidad de c\xf3mputo redimensionable, escalando autom\xe1ticamente desde unas pocas solicitudes por d\xeda hasta miles por segundo seg\xfan el volumen de desencadenadores entrante. Realmente no hay cargo cuando el c\xf3digo de Lambda no se est\xe1 ejecutando; la facturaci\xf3n se basa en la cantidad de solicitudes y la duraci\xf3n de la ejecuci\xf3n, lo que lo hace sin costo durante los periodos de inactividad."},{"id":"q483","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS te permite construir un almac\xe9n de datos en la nube?","options":{"A":"AWS Shield","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Redshift es un almac\xe9n de datos en la nube completamente administrado a escala de petabytes que usa almacenamiento columnar y ejecuci\xf3n de consultas en paralelo para ofrecer anal\xedtica r\xe1pida sobre grandes conjuntos de datos.\\nAWS Shield es un servicio administrado de protecci\xf3n contra DDoS que salvaguarda las aplicaciones de AWS frente a ataques de denegaci\xf3n de servicio distribuido y no tiene relaci\xf3n con el almacenamiento de datos ni con la anal\xedtica. Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional completamente administrado que admite cargas de trabajo transaccionales y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para bases de datos operativas con actividad frecuente de lectura/escritura, en lugar de las cargas de trabajo de consultas anal\xedticas a gran escala que atiende un almac\xe9n de datos. Amazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que analiza texto escrito para extraer informaci\xf3n como sentimiento, entidades y frases clave, y es un servicio de IA sin capacidad de almacenamiento de datos."},{"id":"q485","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios es un sistema de administraci\xf3n de repositorios de AWS que permite almacenar, versionar y administrar el c\xf3digo de tu aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"AWS CodePipeline","B":"AWS CodeCommit","C":"AWS X-Ray","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CodeCommit es un servicio de control de c\xf3digo fuente completamente administrado que aloja repositorios Git privados y proporciona almacenamiento seguro, versionado y funciones de colaboraci\xf3n para el c\xf3digo de aplicaciones.\\nAWS CodePipeline automatiza los pasos de una canalizaci\xf3n de entrega continua para publicar c\xf3digo de aplicaciones, incluidos la compilaci\xf3n, las pruebas y el despliegue, y es una herramienta de orquestaci\xf3n de CI/CD en lugar de un servicio de administraci\xf3n de repositorios para almacenar y versionar c\xf3digo. AWS X-Ray proporciona seguimiento distribuido para aplicaciones con el fin de visualizar las rutas de solicitudes y diagnosticar problemas de latencia y rendimiento, y no tiene capacidad de control de c\xf3digo fuente ni de administraci\xf3n de repositorios. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad que busca vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y es una herramienta de seguridad completamente ajena al almacenamiento o versionado de c\xf3digo."},{"id":"q486","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede usar para enrutar a los usuarios finales a la Regi\xf3n de AWS m\xe1s cercana y reducir la latencia?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Global Accelerator","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Amazon Route 53"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 admite enrutamiento basado en latencia, que dirige las solicitudes de los usuarios a la Regi\xf3n de AWS que proporciona la menor latencia para el usuario final, enrutando el tr\xe1fico al endpoint que responder\xe1 m\xe1s r\xe1pido seg\xfan las condiciones de red medidas.\\nAmazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena contenido en cach\xe9 en ubicaciones de borde para reducir la latencia de los usuarios finales, pero almacena en cach\xe9 contenido est\xe1tico y din\xe1mico en lugar de enrutar el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones a la Regi\xf3n m\xe1s cercana seg\xfan mediciones de latencia. AWS Global Accelerator mejora la disponibilidad y el rendimiento global de las aplicaciones al enrutar el tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de la red global de AWS hacia el endpoint en buen estado m\xe1s cercano y, aunque tambi\xe9n reduce la latencia, la pregunta solicita espec\xedficamente enrutar a los usuarios finales a la Regi\xf3n de AWS m\xe1s cercana. AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre un centro de datos local y AWS, lo que reduce la latencia en cargas de trabajo h\xedbridas, pero no enruta a los usuarios finales a la Regi\xf3n de AWS m\xe1s cercana para aplicaciones alojadas en la nube."},{"id":"q490","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes procedimientos puede reducir la latencia cuando tus usuarios finales recuperan datos?","options":{"A":"Almacenar los recursos multimedia en la regi\xf3n m\xe1s cercana a tus usuarios finales","B":"Almacenar los recursos multimedia en un volumen EBS adicional y aumentar la capacidad de tu servidor","C":"Replicar los recursos multimedia en al menos dos zonas de disponibilidad","D":"Reducir el tama\xf1o de los recursos multimedia usando Amazon Elastic Transcoder","E":"Almacenar los recursos multimedia en S3 y usar CloudFront para distribuir estos recursos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Almacenar los recursos multimedia en la Regi\xf3n m\xe1s cercana a los usuarios finales reduce la distancia f\xedsica que deben recorrer los datos, y usar Amazon CloudFront con S3 almacena contenido en cach\xe9 en ubicaciones de borde de todo el mundo, lo que reduce directamente la latencia de recuperaci\xf3n para los usuarios finales.\\nAgregar un volumen EBS y aumentar la capacidad del servidor aborda el rendimiento de almacenamiento y c\xf3mputo, pero no reduce la distancia de red entre el origen y los usuarios finales. Replicar los recursos en varias Zonas de Disponibilidad mejora la disponibilidad y la tolerancia a fallos dentro de una Regi\xf3n, pero no reduce la latencia para usuarios geogr\xe1ficamente distantes. Amazon Elastic Transcoder convierte archivos multimedia en diferentes formatos para su reproducci\xf3n en diversos dispositivos y no tiene efecto en la latencia de entrega de contenido a los usuarios finales."},{"id":"q494","question":"Una empresa quiere usar Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) para ejecutar sus aplicaciones en contenedores. Por motivos de cumplimiento, la empresa quiere conservar visibilidad y control completos sobre el cl\xfaster de servidores subyacente. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de lanzamiento de Amazon ECS satisfar\xe1 estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Tipo de lanzamiento EC2","B":"Tipo de lanzamiento Fargate","C":"Tipo de lanzamiento Lightsail","D":"Tipo de lanzamiento Lambda"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El tipo de lanzamiento EC2 ejecuta contenedores en instancias EC2 que t\xfa aprovisionas y administras, lo que te brinda visibilidad y control completos sobre el cl\xfaster de servidores subyacente, incluidos los tipos de instancia, el sistema operativo y la configuraci\xf3n del cl\xfaster.\\nEl tipo de lanzamiento Fargate es un motor de c\xf3mputo sin servidor para contenedores en el que AWS administra la infraestructura subyacente por completo, abstrayendo el cl\xfaster de servidores para que los clientes no tengan visibilidad ni control sobre los servidores subyacentes, lo cual es lo opuesto a lo que exige el requisito de cumplimiento. El tipo de lanzamiento Lightsail no es un tipo de lanzamiento v\xe1lido de ECS; Amazon Lightsail es un servicio simplificado de c\xf3mputo y alojamiento para cargas de trabajo sencillas y no es una opci\xf3n de lanzamiento de ECS. El tipo de lanzamiento Lambda no es un tipo de lanzamiento v\xe1lido de ECS; AWS Lambda ejecuta funciones sin servidor en respuesta a eventos y no es un mecanismo de lanzamiento para aplicaciones en contenedores de ECS."},{"id":"q498","question":"Acabas de terminar de escribir el c\xf3digo de tu aplicaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio se puede usar para automatizar el despliegue y el escalado de tu aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Storage Service","B":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","C":"AWS CodeCommit","D":"Amazon Elastic File System"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk gestiona autom\xe1ticamente el despliegue, el aprovisionamiento de capacidad, el balanceo de carga, el escalado autom\xe1tico y el monitoreo del estado de las aplicaciones, lo que te permite concentrarte en escribir c\xf3digo en lugar de administrar infraestructura.\\nAmazon Simple Storage Service es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos para almacenar y recuperar datos, y no tiene capacidad para desplegar ni escalar c\xf3digo de aplicaciones. AWS CodeCommit es un servicio administrado de control de c\xf3digo fuente basado en Git para almacenar y versionar repositorios de c\xf3digo, y es una herramienta de control de versiones en lugar de un servicio de despliegue o escalado. Amazon Elastic File System es un sistema de archivos de red compartido administrado para usarse con los servicios de c\xf3mputo de AWS, y es un servicio de almacenamiento sin capacidad de despliegue ni escalado de aplicaciones."},{"id":"q500","question":"Las instancias de Amazon EC2 son conceptualmente muy similares a los servidores tradicionales. Sin embargo, usar instancias de servidor de Amazon EC2 de la misma manera que las instancias de servidor de hardware tradicionales es solo un punto de partida. \xbfCu\xe1les son los principales beneficios de usar las instancias de AWS EC2 en lugar de servidores tradicionales?","options":{"A":"Mejora la tolerancia a fallos","B":"Proporciona a tu empresa accesibilidad remota perfecta","C":"Evita que usuarios no autorizados accedan a tu red","D":"Proporciona copias de seguridad autom\xe1ticas de los datos","E":"Se puede escalar manualmente en un periodo de tiempo m\xe1s corto"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Las instancias EC2 mejoran la tolerancia a fallos mediante funciones como Auto Scaling, el despliegue Multi-AZ y la integraci\xf3n con Elastic Load Balancing, y se pueden escalar hacia arriba o hacia abajo manualmente en minutos en lugar de las semanas necesarias para adquirir y aprovisionar hardware f\xedsico.\\nLa accesibilidad remota perfecta est\xe1 disponible tanto con servidores tradicionales como con instancias EC2 y no es un beneficio diferenciador de EC2 frente al hardware f\xedsico. Evitar que usuarios no autorizados accedan a tu red es una responsabilidad de seguridad que se aplica por igual a ambos entornos y no es una ventaja inherente de EC2 sobre los servidores tradicionales. EC2 no proporciona copias de seguridad autom\xe1ticas de los datos de forma predeterminada, ya que los clientes deben configurar por separado soluciones de copia de seguridad como AWS Backup o instant\xe1neas de EBS."},{"id":"q502","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona la forma m\xe1s sencilla de configurar y administrar un entorno de AWS seguro, bien arquitecturado y con m\xfaltiples cuentas?","options":{"A":"AWS Control Tower","B":"Amazon Macie","C":"AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager","D":"AWS Security Hub"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Control Tower automatiza la configuraci\xf3n de un entorno con m\xfaltiples cuentas seguro y bien arquitecturado basado en las mejores pr\xe1cticas de AWS, proporcionando guardrails preconfigurados y un panel de landing zone para la gobernanza centralizada.\\nAmazon Macie es un servicio de seguridad de datos que utiliza machine learning para descubrir y proteger datos sensibles almacenados en Amazon S3, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en la configuraci\xf3n o gobernanza de entornos con m\xfaltiples cuentas. AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager automatiza el parcheo de sistemas operativos y aplicaciones en instancias EC2 y no est\xe1 relacionado con la arquitectura de cuentas ni la administraci\xf3n de m\xfaltiples cuentas. AWS Security Hub agrega y prioriza hallazgos de seguridad de m\xfaltiples servicios de AWS en un panel centralizado, pero no configura ni administra entornos con m\xfaltiples cuentas."},{"id":"q503","question":"Una empresa ejecuta una aplicaci\xf3n web grande que necesita estar siempre disponible. La aplicaci\xf3n tiende a ralentizarse cuando el uso de CPU es mayor al 60%. \xbfC\xf3mo pueden rastrear cu\xe1ndo el uso de CPU supera el 60% en cualquiera de las instancias EC2 de su cuenta?","options":{"A":"Usar Amazon CloudFront para monitorear el uso de CPU","B":"Establecer el umbral de CPU de AWS Config en 60% para recibir una notificaci\xf3n cuando el uso de EC2 supere ese valor","C":"Usar Amazon CloudWatch Alarms para monitorear la CPU y alertar cuando el uso de CPU sea >= 60%","D":"Usar Amazon Simple Notification Service (SNS) para monitorear la utilizaci\xf3n del servidor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch Alarms permite definir umbrales sobre m\xe9tricas como la utilizaci\xf3n de CPU y activar notificaciones o acciones automatizadas cuando se supera el umbral, lo que lo convierte en la soluci\xf3n dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para alertar cuando el uso de CPU supera el 60%.\\nAmazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y distribuye contenido en ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo para reducir la latencia de los usuarios finales, y no tiene capacidad para monitorear m\xe9tricas de rendimiento a nivel de instancia, como el uso de CPU. AWS Config rastrea y registra continuamente el estado de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento y detectar cambios de configuraci\xf3n, y no monitorea m\xe9tricas de rendimiento en tiempo real ni admite alertas de umbrales de CPU. Amazon Simple Notification Service es un servicio de mensajer\xeda que entrega notificaciones a los suscriptores y, aunque se puede usar junto con CloudWatch para entregar alertas, no puede monitorear ni evaluar por s\xed mismo m\xe9tricas de utilizaci\xf3n de CPU."},{"id":"q504","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la opci\xf3n de almacenamiento recomendada al alojar una base de datos que cambia con frecuencia en una instancia de Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon RDS","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon DynamoDB"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque persistentes que se conectan a instancias EC2, ofreciendo el rendimiento de E/S de baja latencia y consistente que necesitan las bases de datos con datos que cambian con frecuencia.\\nAmazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional completamente administrado que se ejecuta en su propia infraestructura, y no es una opci\xf3n de almacenamiento para una base de datos alojada directamente en una instancia EC2. Amazon S3 proporciona almacenamiento de objetos accesible mediante llamadas a la API y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para almacenar archivos, copias de seguridad y activos est\xe1ticos, en lugar de servir como almacenamiento en bloque subyacente para un motor de base de datos que se ejecuta en EC2. Amazon DynamoDB es un servicio de base de datos NoSQL sin servidor completamente administrado que se ejecuta en la infraestructura de AWS, y no es un volumen de almacenamiento que se pueda conectar a una instancia EC2."},{"id":"q505","question":"Trabajas como site reliability engineer (SRE) en un entorno de AWS. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios te ayuda a monitorear tus aplicaciones?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"Amazon CloudSearch","C":"Amazon EMR","D":"Amazon CloudHSM"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch monitorea aplicaciones e infraestructura mediante la recopilaci\xf3n de m\xe9tricas, logs y eventos, proporcionando paneles y alarmas que ayudan a los SRE a identificar y responder a problemas operativos.\\nAmazon CloudSearch es un servicio de b\xfasqueda administrado para incorporar funcionalidad de b\xfasqueda en aplicaciones, y es una herramienta de indexaci\xf3n de b\xfasqueda sin capacidad de monitoreo u observabilidad de aplicaciones. Amazon EMR es una plataforma administrada de big data para procesar grandes conjuntos de datos usando frameworks como Apache Hadoop y Spark, y es un servicio de procesamiento de datos sin capacidad de monitoreo de aplicaciones. Amazon CloudHSM proporciona m\xf3dulos de seguridad de hardware dedicados para administrar claves criptogr\xe1ficas, y es un servicio de seguridad y administraci\xf3n de claves sin ning\xfan rol en el monitoreo del rendimiento o la salud de las aplicaciones."},{"id":"q510","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de base de datos deber\xedas usar si tu aplicaci\xf3n y esquema de datos requieren \\"joins\\" o transacciones complejas?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon DocumentDB","D":"Amazon DynamoDB"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional administrado que admite consultas SQL, incluidos joins y transacciones ACID complejas, lo que lo convierte en la opci\xf3n correcta para aplicaciones con esquemas de datos relacionales y estructurados.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de data warehouse administrado que admite SQL y joins, pero est\xe1 optimizado para consultas anal\xedticas sobre grandes conjuntos de datos en lugar de cargas de trabajo transaccionales que requieren cumplimiento ACID. Amazon DocumentDB es una base de datos de documentos compatible con MongoDB que almacena datos flexibles similares a JSON y no admite joins relacionales ni transacciones complejas entre m\xfaltiples tablas. Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL de clave-valor y de documentos dise\xf1ada para patrones de acceso de alto throughput y baja latencia, y no admite joins relacionales ni consultas transaccionales complejas."},{"id":"q512","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 deber\xedas considerar al almacenar datos en Amazon S3 Glacier?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Glacier solo acepta datos en formato comprimido","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier solo se puede usar para almacenar datos de acceso frecuente y archivos de datos","C":"Amazon S3 Glacier no proporciona recuperaci\xf3n inmediata de datos","D":"Adjuntar Amazon S3 Glacier a una instancia de EC2 para poder almacenar datos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 Glacier est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para almacenamiento de archivo y no proporciona recuperaci\xf3n inmediata de datos. Los tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n var\xedan desde minutos hasta horas seg\xfan la opci\xf3n de recuperaci\xf3n seleccionada, lo que lo hace inadecuado para cargas de trabajo que requieren acceso instant\xe1neo a los datos.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier acepta datos en cualquier formato y no requiere que los datos se compriman antes del almacenamiento. Amazon S3 Glacier est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para datos de archivo de acceso poco frecuente, no para datos de acceso frecuente, por lo que la afirmaci\xf3n de que admite datos de acceso frecuente es f\xe1cticamente incorrecta. Amazon S3 Glacier es un servicio de almacenamiento independiente al que se accede directamente mediante la consola de AWS, la API o las pol\xedticas de ciclo de vida de S3, y no necesita conectarse a una instancia EC2 para almacenar datos."},{"id":"q513","question":"Los ingenieros est\xe1n desperdiciando mucho tiempo y esfuerzo administrando software de computaci\xf3n por lotes en centros de datos tradicionales. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS les permite ejecutar f\xe1cilmente miles de trabajos de computaci\xf3n por lotes?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"AWS Batch","C":"Lambda@Edge","D":"AWS Fargate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Batch aprovisiona autom\xe1ticamente la cantidad y el tipo \xf3ptimos de recursos de c\xf3mputo seg\xfan el volumen y los requisitos de los trabajos por lotes, eliminando la necesidad de administrar manualmente la infraestructura de computaci\xf3n por lotes y permitiendo a los ingenieros centrarse en sus cargas de trabajo en lugar de en la infraestructura subyacente.\\nAmazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales que podr\xedan ejecutar cargas de trabajo por lotes, pero requiere que los ingenieros aprovisionen, configuren y administren manualmente las instancias y el software de programaci\xf3n de lotes, que es exactamente el problema que describe el escenario. Lambda@Edge ejecuta funciones de AWS Lambda en ubicaciones de borde de CloudFront para procesar contenido m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios finales, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para tareas ligeras orientadas a eventos en lugar de trabajos de computaci\xf3n por lotes a gran escala. AWS Fargate es un motor de c\xf3mputo sin servidor para ejecutar contenedores sin administrar servidores, pero est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo de aplicaciones en contenedores en lugar de la programaci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de trabajos por lotes a escala dise\xf1adas espec\xedficamente."},{"id":"q518","question":"Una empresa necesita alojar una aplicaci\xf3n de big data en AWS usando instancias de EC2. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de almacenamiento de AWS elegir\xedan para obtener autom\xe1ticamente alto throughput hacia m\xfaltiples nodos de c\xf3mputo?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Block Store","B":"AWS Storage Gateway","C":"Amazon Elastic File System","D":"S3"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Elastic File System (EFS) proporciona un sistema de archivos NFS compartido que escala autom\xe1ticamente y entrega alto throughput a m\xfaltiples instancias EC2 de forma simult\xe1nea, lo que lo hace ideal para cargas de trabajo de big data que requieren acceso paralelo entre nodos de c\xf3mputo.\\nAmazon Elastic Block Store proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque que se conectan a una sola instancia EC2 a la vez y no se pueden compartir simult\xe1neamente entre m\xfaltiples nodos de c\xf3mputo para acceso paralelo. AWS Storage Gateway es un servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido que conecta entornos on-premises con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para acceso compartido de alto throughput entre instancias EC2. Amazon S3 proporciona almacenamiento de objetos accesible mediante llamadas a la API en lugar de un sistema de archivos montado, y no entrega el acceso a sistema de archivos compartido de baja latencia y alto throughput que requieren las cargas de trabajo de big data que se ejecutan en EC2."},{"id":"q521","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el beneficio de que los vol\xfamenes de Amazon EBS se repliquen autom\xe1ticamente dentro de la misma Zona de Disponibilidad?","options":{"A":"Elasticidad","B":"Durabilidad","C":"Trazabilidad","D":"Accesibilidad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los vol\xfamenes de Amazon EBS se replican autom\xe1ticamente dentro de su Zona de Disponibilidad para protegerlos contra fallos de componentes de hardware, lo que garantiza que los datos sigan siendo duraderos incluso si una unidad f\xedsica individual falla dentro de la infraestructura subyacente. La elasticidad se refiere a la capacidad de escalar din\xe1micamente los recursos de c\xf3mputo hacia arriba o hacia abajo seg\xfan la demanda, lo cual es un concepto de gesti\xf3n de capacidad no relacionado con la replicaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de los datos del volumen de EBS dentro de una AZ. La trazabilidad se refiere a la capacidad de auditar y registrar cambios en los recursos y acciones realizadas en un entorno de AWS, lo cual es un concepto de gobernanza y cumplimiento en lugar de una caracter\xedstica de almacenamiento relacionada con la replicaci\xf3n de datos. La accesibilidad se refiere a la capacidad de alcanzar o conectarse a los recursos, lo que describe la conectividad de red y permisos en lugar de la protecci\xf3n de durabilidad que la replicaci\xf3n dentro de una AZ proporciona a los datos almacenados."},{"id":"q526","question":"\xbfPara qu\xe9 se utiliza el servicio Amazon ElastiCache?","options":{"A":"Proporcionar un servicio de almacenamiento de datos en memoria","B":"Reducir los costos de entrega utilizando Edge Locations","C":"Mejorar el rendimiento de las aplicaciones web","D":"Proporcionar una cach\xe9 compatible con Chef para acelerar la respuesta de las aplicaciones","E":"Distribuir solicitudes entre varias instancias"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Amazon ElastiCache proporciona un almac\xe9n de datos en memoria completamente gestionado que admite Redis y Memcached, y almacena en cach\xe9 los datos a los que se accede con frecuencia para reducir dr\xe1sticamente la latencia de las consultas a la base de datos y mejorar el rendimiento de las aplicaciones web. Reducir los costos de entrega utilizando Edge Locations describe Amazon CloudFront, que almacena en cach\xe9 contenido geogr\xe1ficamente m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios en lugar de proporcionar almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de bases de datos en memoria. Proporcionar una cach\xe9 compatible con Chef describe AWS OpsWorks, que utiliza Chef y Puppet para la gesti\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n y no tiene ninguna funci\xf3n en el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de datos en memoria. Distribuir solicitudes entre varias instancias describe Elastic Load Balancing, que enruta el tr\xe1fico entrante entre los destinos en lugar de almacenar o cachear datos en memoria."},{"id":"q527","question":"La elasticidad de la nube de AWS permite a los clientes ahorrar costos en comparaci\xf3n con los proveedores de alojamiento tradicionales. \xbfQu\xe9 pueden hacer los clientes de AWS para beneficiarse de la elasticidad de la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Implementar sus recursos en varias Zonas de Disponibilidad","B":"Utilizar Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","C":"Implementar sus recursos en otra regi\xf3n","D":"Utilizar Elastic Load Balancing","E":"Utilizar computaci\xf3n sin servidor siempre que sea posible"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"EC2 Auto Scaling ajusta autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias seg\xfan la demanda, lo que garantiza que los clientes paguen solo por lo que usan, y la computaci\xf3n sin servidor cobra solo por el tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n real, sin costos por capacidad inactiva; ambos aprovechan directamente la elasticidad de la nube para reducir costos. Implementar en varias Zonas de Disponibilidad mejora la disponibilidad y la tolerancia a fallos, pero no ajusta din\xe1micamente la capacidad de los recursos en respuesta a la demanda. Implementar recursos en otra Regi\xf3n mejora el alcance geogr\xe1fico y la recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres, pero no es un mecanismo para lograr ahorros de costos el\xe1sticos. Elastic Load Balancing distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre las instancias existentes, pero no escala el n\xfamero de instancias hacia arriba o hacia abajo seg\xfan la demanda."},{"id":"q529","question":"Cada Regi\xf3n de AWS est\xe1 compuesta por varias Zonas de Disponibilidad. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones describe mejor qu\xe9 es una Zona de Disponibilidad?","options":{"A":"Es un centro de datos dise\xf1ado para estar completamente aislado de otros centros de datos en la misma regi\xf3n","B":"Es una colecci\xf3n de centros de datos distribuidos en varios pa\xedses","C":"Es una red l\xf3gicamente aislada de la nube de AWS","D":"Es una ubicaci\xf3n distinta dentro de una regi\xf3n que est\xe1 aislada de fallos en otras Zonas de Disponibilidad"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Una Zona de Disponibilidad es una ubicaci\xf3n distinta dentro de una Regi\xf3n que consta de uno o m\xe1s centros de datos, dise\xf1ada para estar aislada de fallos en otras AZ mediante suministro el\xe9ctrico, refrigeraci\xf3n y redes independientes. Un \xfanico centro de datos completamente aislado de otros centros de datos en la misma Regi\xf3n es inexacto, ya que una AZ puede contener varios centros de datos y est\xe1 aislada de fallos, no de toda la conectividad. Una colecci\xf3n de centros de datos distribuidos en varios pa\xedses describe una Regi\xf3n de AWS o la infraestructura global en su conjunto, no una AZ individual. Una red l\xf3gicamente aislada de la nube de AWS describe una Amazon VPC, no una Zona de Disponibilidad."},{"id":"q532","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la cantidad m\xe1xima de datos que se puede almacenar en S3 en una sola cuenta de AWS?","options":{"A":"100 petabytes","B":"Almacenamiento pr\xe1cticamente ilimitado","C":"5 terabytes","D":"10 exabytes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon S3 est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para proporcionar una capacidad de almacenamiento pr\xe1cticamente ilimitada. No existe un m\xe1ximo fijo en la cantidad total de datos que una cuenta puede almacenar, y S3 escala autom\xe1ticamente para adaptarse a cualquier volumen de objetos. 100 petabytes es un l\xedmite de almacenamiento fijo que no se aplica a Amazon S3; los objetos individuales tienen un tama\xf1o m\xe1ximo de 5 terabytes, pero este es un l\xedmite por objeto en lugar de un l\xedmite total de almacenamiento de la cuenta. 5 terabytes es el tama\xf1o m\xe1ximo de un solo objeto de S3, no el l\xedmite total de almacenamiento de una cuenta; la cantidad total de datos almacenados en todos los objetos de una cuenta no tiene un l\xedmite establecido. 10 exabytes es un l\xedmite de almacenamiento fijo que no se aplica a Amazon S3; el servicio escala autom\xe1ticamente a cualquier volumen sin un m\xe1ximo declarado."},{"id":"q534","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS entrega datos, videos, aplicaciones y API a usuarios de todo el mundo con baja latencia y altas velocidades de transferencia?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"Amazon CloudFront","D":"Amazon EC2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront es una red global de entrega de contenido que entrega datos, videos, aplicaciones y API a usuarios de todo el mundo con baja latencia y altas velocidades de transferencia a trav\xe9s de una red de Edge Locations. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de dominios que dirige a los usuarios a los endpoints de las aplicaciones seg\xfan las pol\xedticas de enrutamiento, pero enruta el tr\xe1fico en lugar de almacenar en cach\xe9 y entregar contenido en Edge Locations. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto en la nube para gestionar las comunicaciones con los clientes por voz y chat, y no tiene ninguna funci\xf3n en la entrega de contenido ni en la transferencia global de datos. Amazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales para ejecutar aplicaciones y cargas de trabajo, pero requiere configuraci\xf3n manual para servir contenido a nivel global y no lo entrega de forma nativa con baja latencia a usuarios de todo el mundo a trav\xe9s de Edge Locations."},{"id":"q537","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 elemento de la infraestructura global de AWS consta de uno o m\xe1s centros de datos discretos, cada uno con energ\xeda, redes y conectividad redundantes, que se alojan en instalaciones separadas?","options":{"A":"Regiones de AWS","B":"Zonas de Disponibilidad","C":"Edge Locations","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Una Zona de Disponibilidad consta de uno o m\xe1s centros de datos discretos, cada uno con energ\xeda, redes y conectividad redundantes, alojados en instalaciones separadas dentro de una Regi\xf3n. Las Regiones de AWS son \xe1reas geogr\xe1ficas que contienen varias Zonas de Disponibilidad, no cl\xfasteres individuales de centros de datos. Las Edge Locations son puntos de presencia utilizados para la entrega y el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de contenido, no agrupaciones de centros de datos con infraestructura redundante. Amazon CloudFront es un servicio de red de entrega de contenido que utiliza Edge Locations, no un componente de infraestructura global por derecho propio."},{"id":"q538","question":"\xbfEn cu\xe1ntas Zonas de Disponibilidad se deben aprovisionar los recursos de c\xf3mputo para lograr alta disponibilidad?","options":{"A":"Un m\xednimo de una","B":"Un m\xednimo de dos","C":"Un m\xednimo de tres","D":"Un m\xednimo de cuatro o m\xe1s"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Se requiere un m\xednimo de dos Zonas de Disponibilidad para lograr alta disponibilidad. Si una AZ falla, la aplicaci\xf3n sigue funcionando desde la otra con una interrupci\xf3n m\xednima. Un m\xednimo de una no proporciona redundancia. El fallo de una sola AZ dejar\xeda toda la aplicaci\xf3n fuera de servicio. Un m\xednimo de tres o cuatro o m\xe1s supera el requisito b\xe1sico. Aunque implementar en m\xe1s AZ puede aumentar a\xfan m\xe1s la resiliencia, dos es el m\xednimo reconocido para alta disponibilidad en la arquitectura de AWS."},{"id":"q539","question":"Las m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS Cloud son un ejemplo de:","options":{"A":"Agilidad","B":"Infraestructura global","C":"Elasticidad","D":"Precios de pago por uso"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las Regiones de AWS distribuidas por todo el mundo forman parte de la infraestructura global de AWS, que incluye Regiones, Zonas de disponibilidad y ubicaciones de borde dise\xf1adas para ofrecer servicios en todo el mundo.\\nLa agilidad se refiere a la velocidad con la que los clientes pueden aprovisionar recursos e iterar sobre nuevas funcionalidades, lo cual es un beneficio de la adopci\xf3n de la nube m\xe1s que una caracter\xedstica de la ubicaci\xf3n f\xedsica de la infraestructura. La elasticidad se refiere a escalar recursos din\xe1micamente hacia arriba o hacia abajo en respuesta a la demanda, lo cual es un concepto de c\xf3mputo y capacidad no relacionado con la distribuci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica de las Regiones. El precio de pago por uso es un modelo de facturaci\xf3n en el que los clientes pagan solo por lo que consumen, lo cual es un beneficio comercial m\xe1s que un concepto de infraestructura."},{"id":"q540","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para lanzar instancias manualmente seg\xfan los requisitos de recursos?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon EC2","D":"Amazon ECS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon EC2 le permite lanzar manualmente instancias de servidor virtuales con la elecci\xf3n de tipo de instancia, sistema operativo y configuraci\xf3n, proporcionando control total sobre el aprovisionamiento de recursos de c\xf3mputo.\\nAmazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque persistentes que se adjuntan a las instancias de EC2 como discos, y es un servicio de almacenamiento, no un servicio de c\xf3mputo capaz de lanzar instancias. Amazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos para almacenar y recuperar archivos y datos, y no tiene capacidad para lanzar o ejecutar instancias de c\xf3mputo. Amazon ECS es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores que gestiona el despliegue y el escalado de aplicaciones en contenedores, y aunque ejecuta tareas en c\xf3mputo subyacente, no lanza instancias manualmente seg\xfan los requisitos de recursos de la forma en que lo hace EC2."},{"id":"q544","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una de las ventajas de Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS)?","options":{"A":"Simplifica las tareas de administraci\xf3n de bases de datos relacionales","B":"Proporciona una confiabilidad y durabilidad del 99.99999999999%","C":"Escala autom\xe1ticamente las bases de datos para las cargas","D":"Permite a los usuarios ajustar din\xe1micamente los recursos de CPU y RAM"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon RDS simplifica la administraci\xf3n de bases de datos relacionales al automatizar tareas que consumen mucho tiempo, como el aprovisionamiento de hardware, la configuraci\xf3n de la base de datos, la aplicaci\xf3n de parches y las copias de seguridad, lo que permite a los clientes centrarse en el desarrollo de aplicaciones en lugar de la gesti\xf3n de infraestructura.\\n99.9999999999% de confiabilidad y durabilidad es una cifra asociada con la durabilidad de objetos de Amazon S3, no un SLA publicado para Amazon RDS. Escalar autom\xe1ticamente las bases de datos para las cargas describe Amazon Aurora Serverless, que escala la capacidad de c\xf3mputo autom\xe1ticamente seg\xfan la demanda, no un comportamiento predeterminado de las instancias est\xe1ndar de RDS. Ajustar din\xe1micamente los recursos de CPU y RAM describe el comportamiento de servicios con escalado vertical autom\xe1tico, que RDS no proporciona de forma nativa, ya que los clientes deben modificar manualmente los tipos de instancia para cambiar los recursos de c\xf3mputo."},{"id":"q545","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS Cloud se puede utilizar para ejecutar una base de datos relacional administrada por el cliente?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"Amazon ElastiCache","D":"Amazon DynamoDB"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales con acceso completo a nivel de sistema operativo, lo que le permite instalar, configurar y administrar usted mismo cualquier software de base de datos relacional, d\xe1ndole control completo sobre la configuraci\xf3n de la base de datos.\\nAmazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico que gestiona la resoluci\xf3n de nombres de dominio y dirige a los usuarios a los endpoints de las aplicaciones, y no tiene capacidad para alojar o ejecutar software de base de datos. Amazon ElastiCache es un servicio de cach\xe9 en memoria administrado dise\xf1ado para acelerar el rendimiento de las aplicaciones almacenando en memoria datos de acceso frecuente, y no es una plataforma de base de datos relacional. Amazon DynamoDB es un servicio de base de datos NoSQL de clave-valor y de documentos totalmente administrado, y no es una base de datos relacional ni admite la instalaci\xf3n o configuraci\xf3n de software de base de datos administrado por el cliente."},{"id":"q546","question":"Un usuario planea lanzar dos instancias de Amazon EC2 adicionales para aumentar la disponibilidad. \xbfQu\xe9 acci\xf3n debe tomar el usuario?","options":{"A":"Lanzar las instancias en m\xfaltiples Zonas de disponibilidad en una sola Regi\xf3n de AWS","B":"Lanzar las instancias como EC2 Reserved Instances en la misma Regi\xf3n de AWS y la misma Zona de disponibilidad","C":"Lanzar las instancias en m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS pero en la misma Zona de disponibilidad","D":"Lanzar las instancias como EC2 Spot Instances en la misma Regi\xf3n de AWS pero en diferentes Zonas de disponibilidad"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lanzar instancias en m\xfaltiples Zonas de disponibilidad dentro de una sola Regi\xf3n proporciona alta disponibilidad al garantizar que, si una Zona de disponibilidad experimenta una falla, las instancias en las zonas restantes contin\xfaen sirviendo tr\xe1fico sin interrupci\xf3n.\\nLanzar Reserved Instances en la misma Regi\xf3n y Zona de disponibilidad asegura una reserva de capacidad y reduce el costo mediante un compromiso de facturaci\xf3n, pero concentrar ambas instancias en una sola Zona de disponibilidad crea un \xfanico punto de falla sin beneficio de disponibilidad. Lanzar instancias en m\xfaltiples Regiones pero en el mismo tipo de Zona de disponibilidad introduce separaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica a nivel de Regi\xf3n, pero no protege contra fallas dentro de una Regi\xf3n, ya que las Zonas de disponibilidad dentro de cada Regi\xf3n siguen siendo dominios de falla independientes. Lanzar Spot Instances en diferentes Zonas de disponibilidad utiliza capacidad de EC2 sobrante a un costo reducido, pero AWS puede interrumpir las Spot Instances en cualquier momento, lo que las hace inadecuadas como mecanismo de confiabilidad para aumentar la disponibilidad."},{"id":"q548","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS permiten a las empresas conectar una Amazon VPC a un centro de datos local?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon API Gateway","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS VPN crea una conexi\xf3n cifrada a trav\xe9s de internet p\xfablica para conectar redes locales a una VPC, y AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red f\xedsica privada dedicada desde un centro de datos local hacia AWS. Ambos son servicios que proporcionan conectividad h\xedbrida entre la infraestructura local y AWS Cloud.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almacenamiento de datos en la nube totalmente administrado dise\xf1ado para ejecutar consultas SQL anal\xedticas en grandes conjuntos de datos, y es un servicio de base de datos sin capacidad para conectar redes locales a AWS. Amazon API Gateway es un servicio totalmente administrado para crear, publicar y gestionar API REST, HTTP y WebSocket, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de API en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de conectividad de red para enlazar centros de datos locales a VPC."},{"id":"q549","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS se puede utilizar para monitorear el uso de CPU?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"VPC Flow Logs","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch recopila y monitorea m\xe9tricas de utilizaci\xf3n de CPU de las instancias de EC2 de forma predeterminada, y puede activar alarmas o mostrar paneles cuando el uso supera los umbrales definidos.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a API y actividad de la cuenta en un entorno de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no recopila ni monitorea m\xe9tricas de rendimiento a nivel de instancia, como el uso de CPU. VPC Flow Logs captura informaci\xf3n sobre el tr\xe1fico IP que fluye hacia y desde las interfaces de red dentro de una VPC, y es una caracter\xedstica de registro de tr\xe1fico de red sin capacidad para monitorear m\xe9tricas de recursos de c\xf3mputo, como la utilizaci\xf3n de CPU. AWS Config rastrea y registra continuamente el estado de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento y detectar cambios de configuraci\xf3n, y no monitorea m\xe9tricas de rendimiento en tiempo real."},{"id":"q552","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de almacenamiento se puede utilizar como opci\xf3n de bajo costo para alojar sitios web est\xe1ticos?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 GLACIER","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS)","D":"Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon S3 puede alojar sitios web est\xe1ticos sirviendo archivos HTML, CSS, JavaScript y multimedia directamente por HTTP, ofreciendo una soluci\xf3n de bajo costo, altamente durable y escalable sin necesidad de un servidor web.\\nAmazon S3 Glacier es un servicio de almacenamiento de archivo dise\xf1ado para la retenci\xf3n a largo plazo de datos de acceso poco frecuente y no admite el alojamiento de sitios web est\xe1ticos ni el servicio directo de contenido por HTTP. Amazon DynamoDB es un servicio de base de datos NoSQL totalmente administrado para almacenar y consultar datos estructurados, y no es un servicio de almacenamiento capaz de alojar archivos de sitios web. Amazon EFS proporciona un sistema de archivos de red compartido para usarse con instancias de EC2 y no admite el alojamiento de sitios web est\xe1ticos ni el acceso HTTP p\xfablico a los archivos."},{"id":"q558","question":"Una empresa ha implementado varias bases de datos relacionales en instancias de Amazon EC2. Cada mes, el proveedor del software de base de datos publica nuevos parches de seguridad que deben aplicarse a las bases de datos. \xbfCu\xe1l es la forma M\xc1S eficiente de aplicar los parches de seguridad?","options":{"A":"Conectarse a cada instancia de base de datos mensualmente y descargar y aplicar los parches de seguridad necesarios del proveedor","B":"Habilitar el parcheo autom\xe1tico de las instancias mediante la consola de Amazon RDS","C":"En AWS Config, configurar una regla para las instancias y el nivel de parche requerido","D":"Usar AWS Systems Manager para automatizar el parcheo de bases de datos seg\xfan una programaci\xf3n"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager automatiza el proceso de aplicaci\xf3n de parches a las instancias EC2 administradas con actualizaciones de seguridad seg\xfan una programaci\xf3n definida, lo que elimina la necesidad de intervenci\xf3n manual cada vez que un proveedor publica nuevos parches.\\nConectarse a cada instancia de base de datos individualmente de forma mensual para descargar y aplicar parches manualmente es el enfoque menos eficiente, ya que requiere un esfuerzo administrativo significativo que escala mal a medida que aumenta el n\xfamero de instancias. La aplicaci\xf3n de parches desde la consola de Amazon RDS se aplica a las bases de datos administradas por el servicio RDS y no se puede usar para aplicar parches al software de base de datos que se ejecuta en instancias EC2 autoadministradas, donde el cliente es responsable del sistema operativo y del software. AWS Config es un servicio de cumplimiento de configuraci\xf3n y seguimiento de cambios que puede evaluar si las instancias cumplen con un nivel de parche requerido, pero no automatiza ni aplica parches a las instancias por s\xed mismo."},{"id":"q559","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 mecanismo permite a los desarrolladores acceder a los servicios de AWS desde el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"AWS Software Development Kit","B":"AWS Management Console","C":"AWS CodePipeline","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El AWS Software Development Kit proporciona bibliotecas y API espec\xedficas de cada lenguaje en lenguajes como Python, Java, JavaScript y Go, lo que permite a los desarrolladores integrar llamadas a servicios de AWS directamente en el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n mediante interfaces program\xe1ticas.\\nLa AWS Management Console es una interfaz gr\xe1fica basada en web para interactuar con los servicios de AWS a trav\xe9s de un navegador, no un mecanismo que los desarrolladores incorporen en el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n para el acceso program\xe1tico a los servicios. AWS CodePipeline es un servicio de entrega continua que automatiza las etapas de compilaci\xf3n, prueba y despliegue de una canalizaci\xf3n de versiones, y es una herramienta de CI/CD para el despliegue de aplicaciones en lugar de un medio para que el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n interact\xfae con los servicios de AWS. AWS Config realiza un seguimiento y registro continuo de los cambios de configuraci\xf3n de recursos y los eval\xfaa frente a reglas de cumplimiento, y es un servicio de gobernanza y cumplimiento en lugar de una herramienta de desarrollo para permitir que el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n llame a los servicios de AWS."},{"id":"q563","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 es Amazon CloudWatch?","options":{"A":"Un servicio que registra llamadas a API y actividad de la cuenta en un entorno de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda","B":"Un servicio que monitorea el rendimiento de las aplicaciones mediante el seguimiento de solicitudes a medida que viajan por los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n","C":"Un servicio que recopila m\xe9tricas y datos operativos de recursos de AWS con alarmas y canales de notificaci\xf3n personalizables","D":"Un servicio que captura y procesa flujos de datos en tiempo real de aplicaciones e infraestructura"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch es un servicio de monitoreo que act\xfaa como un repositorio de m\xe9tricas, recopilando datos de rendimiento y operativos de recursos de AWS y permiti\xe9ndole establecer umbrales de notificaci\xf3n y canales de alarma personalizables.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra todas las llamadas a API y la actividad de la cuenta en un entorno de AWS y genera registros de auditor\xeda con fines de gobernanza y cumplimiento, pero no recopila m\xe9tricas de rendimiento de recursos ni env\xeda alarmas operativas. AWS X-Ray rastrea las solicitudes a medida que viajan por componentes de aplicaciones distribuidas para identificar cuellos de botella de rendimiento y errores, pero es una herramienta de depuraci\xf3n de aplicaciones en lugar de un servicio de m\xe9tricas y alertas para toda la infraestructura. Amazon Kinesis captura y procesa flujos de datos en tiempo real de aplicaciones e infraestructura a escala, pero es un servicio de transmisi\xf3n e ingesta de datos en lugar de una plataforma de monitoreo y observabilidad."},{"id":"q570","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios se escalar\xe1 autom\xe1ticamente con un aumento esperado en el tr\xe1fico web?","options":{"A":"AWS CodePipeline","B":"Elastic Load Balancing","C":"Amazon EBS","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Elastic Load Balancing escala autom\xe1ticamente su capacidad de manejo de solicitudes en respuesta al tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones entrante, distribuyendo solicitudes entre destinos saludables sin intervenci\xf3n manual.\\nAWS CodePipeline automatiza las etapas de una canalizaci\xf3n de versiones de software, incluidas las fases de origen, compilaci\xf3n y despliegue, y no tiene capacidad para manejar o escalar tr\xe1fico web. Amazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque adjuntos a instancias EC2 que deben redimensionarse manualmente, y no escala en respuesta al tr\xe1fico web. AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, y es un servicio de conectividad de red no relacionado con el escalado del tr\xe1fico web."},{"id":"q571","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio proporciona una cantidad pr\xe1cticamente ilimitada de almacenamiento de objetos en l\xednea altamente duradero?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS)","C":"Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS)","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon S3 proporciona almacenamiento de objetos pr\xe1cticamente ilimitado y altamente duradero con una durabilidad del 99.999999999%, dise\xf1ado para almacenar y recuperar cualquier cantidad de datos desde cualquier lugar de la web.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos administrado para ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas en grandes conjuntos de datos, no un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos. Amazon EFS es un sistema de archivos compartido administrado para usar con instancias de c\xf3mputo, que proporciona almacenamiento de archivos en lugar de almacenamiento de objetos y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para almacenamiento de datos ilimitado accesible desde la web. Amazon ECS es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores para ejecutar y administrar contenedores Docker, totalmente no relacionado con el almacenamiento de objetos."},{"id":"q578","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede servir un sitio web est\xe1tico?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"Amazon QuickSight","D":"AWS X-Ray"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon S3 puede servir sitios web est\xe1ticos al alojar archivos HTML, CSS, JavaScript e im\xe1genes, entreg\xe1ndolos directamente mediante HTTP sin necesidad de ejecutar un servidor web.\\nAmazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y registro de dominios que enruta el tr\xe1fico a los puntos de enlace, pero no aloja ni sirve el contenido del sitio web por s\xed mismo. Amazon QuickSight es un servicio de inteligencia empresarial y visualizaci\xf3n de datos para crear paneles y informes, y no tiene capacidad de alojamiento web. AWS X-Ray es un servicio de rastreo y depuraci\xf3n de aplicaciones que ayuda a analizar el rendimiento de aplicaciones distribuidas, y no est\xe1 relacionado en absoluto con el alojamiento ni la entrega de contenido de sitios web."},{"id":"q580","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son componentes principales de la infraestructura global de AWS?","options":{"A":"Grupos de recursos","B":"Zonas de disponibilidad","C":"Grupos de seguridad","D":"Regiones","E":"Amazon Machine Images (AMIS)"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Las Regiones y las Zonas de disponibilidad son los dos componentes principales de la infraestructura global de AWS, donde las Regiones son \xe1reas geogr\xe1ficas y cada Regi\xf3n contiene m\xfaltiples Zonas de disponibilidad aisladas.\\nLos grupos de recursos son una herramienta de administraci\xf3n que se utiliza para organizar y etiquetar recursos de AWS dentro de una cuenta, y no son un componente de la infraestructura global f\xedsica. Los grupos de seguridad son firewalls virtuales que controlan el tr\xe1fico de entrada y salida de los recursos de AWS, y son una caracter\xedstica de red en lugar de un componente de infraestructura. Amazon Machine Images son plantillas que se utilizan para lanzar instancias EC2 y que contienen el sistema operativo y la configuraci\xf3n, y son una caracter\xedstica de c\xf3mputo en lugar de formar parte de la jerarqu\xeda de la infraestructura global."},{"id":"q584","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios se pueden usar en arquitecturas h\xedbridas de AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"Virtual Private Gateway","C":"Classic Load Balancer","D":"Auto Scaling","E":"M\xe9tricas predeterminadas de Amazon CloudWatch"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Amazon Route 53 proporciona servicios de DNS que funcionan tanto en entornos de nube como locales, y un Virtual Private Gateway permite conexiones VPN cifradas entre una VPC y un centro de datos local, lo que hace que ambos sean adecuados para arquitecturas h\xedbridas.\\nClassic Load Balancer distribuye el tr\xe1fico entre instancias EC2 dentro de AWS y opera completamente dentro de la nube, sin capacidad de conectividad h\xedbrida. Auto Scaling ajusta autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias EC2 seg\xfan la demanda dentro de AWS, y no se extiende ni se integra con la infraestructura local. Las m\xe9tricas predeterminadas de Amazon CloudWatch monitorean el rendimiento de los recursos de AWS dentro de la nube y, aunque se pueden extender a entornos locales con un agente personalizado, no son en s\xed mismas un servicio de arquitectura h\xedbrida."},{"id":"q585","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n describe mejor Elastic Load Balancing?","options":{"A":"Traduce un nombre de dominio a una direcci\xf3n IP mediante DNS","B":"Distribuye el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones entrante entre una o m\xe1s instancias de Amazon EC2","C":"Recopila m\xe9tricas de las instancias de Amazon EC2 conectadas","D":"Ajusta autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias de Amazon EC2 para admitir el tr\xe1fico entrante"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Elastic Load Balancing distribuye el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones entrante entre varias instancias de EC2 o destinos, lo que mejora la disponibilidad y la tolerancia a fallos al garantizar que ninguna instancia individual se vea sobrecargada.\\nTraducir un nombre de dominio a una direcci\xf3n IP mediante DNS describe la funci\xf3n de Amazon Route 53, que es un servicio de enrutamiento DNS y no un servicio de distribuci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico. Recopilar m\xe9tricas de las instancias de EC2 conectadas describe la funci\xf3n de Amazon CloudWatch, que supervisa el rendimiento de los recursos y los datos operativos en lugar de distribuir tr\xe1fico. Ajustar autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias de EC2 para admitir el tr\xe1fico entrante describe la funci\xf3n de Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling, que agrega o quita instancias seg\xfan la demanda en lugar de distribuir tr\xe1fico entre las instancias existentes."},{"id":"q586","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un servicio de base de datos NoSQL r\xe1pido y confiable?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon RDS","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL completamente administrada que ofrece rendimiento de milisegundos de un solo d\xedgito a cualquier escala, con seguridad, copia de seguridad y almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria integrados.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos administrado optimizado para ejecutar consultas SQL anal\xedticas en conjuntos de datos grandes, y es un almac\xe9n relacional columnar en lugar de un servicio de base de datos NoSQL. Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional completamente administrado que admite motores como MySQL, PostgreSQL y SQL Server, y utiliza esquemas estructurados y SQL en lugar de los modelos de datos flexibles sin esquema que proporcionan las bases de datos NoSQL. Amazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos para almacenar y recuperar archivos y datos no estructurados, y aunque puede almacenar grandes vol\xfamenes de datos, no es un servicio de base de datos y no admite los patrones de consulta ni los modelos de datos de una base de datos NoSQL."},{"id":"q592","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el beneficio de usar servicios administrados de AWS, como Amazon ElastiCache y Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS)?","options":{"A":"Requieren que el cliente supervise y reemplace las instancias que fallan","B":"Tienen mejor rendimiento que los servicios administrados por el cliente","C":"Simplifican la aplicaci\xf3n de parches y la actualizaci\xf3n de los sistemas operativos subyacentes","D":"No requieren que el cliente optimice la selecci\xf3n del tipo o tama\xf1o de instancia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los servicios administrados de AWS, como Amazon ElastiCache y Amazon RDS, se encargan de la aplicaci\xf3n de parches y la actualizaci\xf3n de los sistemas operativos y motores de base de datos subyacentes, lo que libera a los clientes de estas tareas de mantenimiento que consumen mucho tiempo.\\nRequerir que el cliente supervise y reemplace las instancias que fallan describe lo contrario de un beneficio de los servicios administrados, ya que los servicios administrados de AWS gestionan la detecci\xf3n y recuperaci\xf3n de fallos autom\xe1ticamente, eliminando esa carga para los clientes. Tener mejor rendimiento que los servicios administrados por el cliente no est\xe1 garantizado como un beneficio universal, ya que el rendimiento depende del tipo de instancia, la configuraci\xf3n y la carga de trabajo, en lugar de simplemente de si un servicio es administrado. No requerir que el cliente optimice la selecci\xf3n del tipo o tama\xf1o de instancia es incorrecto, ya que los clientes siguen siendo responsables de elegir el tipo y tama\xf1o de instancia adecuados para cumplir con los requisitos de su carga de trabajo incluso cuando usan servicios administrados."},{"id":"q596","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes son caracter\xedsticas de Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"Un sistema de archivos global","B":"Un almac\xe9n de objetos","C":"Un almac\xe9n de archivos local","D":"Un sistema de archivos de red","E":"Un sistema de almacenamiento duradero"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 es un almac\xe9n de objetos que proporciona almacenamiento altamente duradero con una durabilidad del 99.999999999%, y almacena los datos como objetos en buckets en lugar de como archivos en un sistema de archivos o bloques en un disco.\\nUn sistema de archivos global describe Amazon EFS, que proporciona un sistema de archivos de red compartido accesible desde varias instancias de EC2, no un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos. Un almac\xe9n de archivos local describe el almacenamiento temporal de instancias, como los vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento de instancias de EC2, que est\xe1n conectados f\xedsicamente al host y se pierden cuando la instancia se detiene. Un sistema de archivos de red tambi\xe9n describe Amazon EFS, que se monta como un sistema de archivos compartido a trav\xe9s de una conexi\xf3n de red en lugar de almacenar datos como objetos."},{"id":"q609","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes son formas v\xe1lidas en que un cliente puede interactuar con los servicios de AWS?","options":{"A":"Interfaz de l\xednea de comandos","B":"On-premises","C":"Kits de desarrollo de software","D":"Software como servicio","E":"H\xedbrido"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS Command Line Interface permite la interacci\xf3n basada en comandos con los servicios de AWS desde una terminal, y AWS Software Development Kits proporcionan acceso program\xe1tico a los servicios de AWS desde c\xf3digo de aplicaci\xf3n escrito en m\xfaltiples lenguajes de programaci\xf3n. Ambos son m\xe9todos directos y admitidos para interactuar con los servicios de AWS.\\nOn-premises se refiere a la infraestructura y el software alojados en el centro de datos f\xedsico propio de una empresa en lugar de en la nube, y es un modelo de implementaci\xf3n en lugar de un m\xe9todo para interactuar con los servicios de AWS. Software como servicio es un modelo de entrega en la nube en el que un proveedor ofrece aplicaciones de software a trav\xe9s de internet, y es un modelo de consumo de servicios en lugar de una forma en que los clientes interact\xfaen directamente con los servicios de AWS. H\xedbrido se refiere a una arquitectura que combina infraestructura on-premises con recursos en la nube, y es una estrategia de implementaci\xf3n en lugar de un m\xe9todo de interacci\xf3n para los servicios de AWS."},{"id":"q611","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS se puede usar para servir grandes cantidades de contenido de video en l\xednea con la menor latencia posible?","options":{"A":"AWS Storage Gateway","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon Elastic File System (EFS)","D":"Amazon S3 Glacier","E":"Amazon CloudFront"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Amazon S3 almacena contenido de video con alta durabilidad y escalabilidad, y Amazon CloudFront almacena en cach\xe9 y entrega ese contenido desde ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo con baja latencia, lo que los convierte en la combinaci\xf3n dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para servir video en l\xednea a gran escala de manera eficiente.\\nAWS Storage Gateway conecta entornos on-premises con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS para la integraci\xf3n de almacenamiento h\xedbrido, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para servir o distribuir contenido de video en l\xednea a los usuarios finales. Amazon Elastic File System es un sistema de archivos de red compartido administrado para usar con servicios de c\xf3mputo de AWS, y no est\xe1 optimizado para entregar grandes vol\xfamenes de contenido de video con baja latencia a una audiencia distribuida. Amazon S3 Glacier es un servicio de almacenamiento de archivos dise\xf1ado para datos a los que se accede con poca frecuencia y que pueden tolerar tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n de minutos a horas, lo que lo hace completamente inadecuado para servir contenido de video en l\xednea que requiere una entrega inmediata con baja latencia."},{"id":"q615","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona una vista personalizada del estado de servicios espec\xedficos de AWS que sustentan las cargas de trabajo de un cliente que se ejecutan en AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS X-Ray","C":"AWS Health Dashboard","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard proporciona una vista personalizada y espec\xedfica de la cuenta de los eventos de servicios de AWS que afectan tus cargas de trabajo a trav\xe9s de su secci\xf3n \\"Your account health\\", y ofrece alertas proactivas y gu\xeda de remediaci\xf3n adaptada a tu entorno.\\nAmazon CloudWatch supervisa m\xe9tricas, registros y alarmas de los recursos dentro del entorno de AWS propio de un cliente, pero no proporciona alertas sobre eventos de servicios de AWS que afectan tus cargas de trabajo. AWS X-Ray es un servicio de seguimiento de aplicaciones para analizar el rendimiento de aplicaciones distribuidas, no una herramienta de supervisi\xf3n del estado de los servicios. AWS Trusted Advisor analiza tu entorno con respecto a comprobaciones de pr\xe1cticas recomendadas en cuanto a costo, rendimiento, seguridad y tolerancia a fallos, pero no proporciona alertas personalizadas sobre el estado de los servicios."},{"id":"q616","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes puede usar un cliente de AWS para lanzar un nuevo cl\xfaster de Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS)?","options":{"A":"AWS Concierge","B":"AWS CloudFormation","C":"Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3)","D":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","E":"AWS Management Console"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"AWS CloudFormation puede aprovisionar cl\xfasteres de RDS mediante plantillas de infraestructura como c\xf3digo, y la AWS Management Console proporciona una interfaz gr\xe1fica para lanzar cl\xfasteres de RDS directamente, lo que hace que ambos sean m\xe9todos de lanzamiento v\xe1lidos.\\nAWS Concierge es un servicio de soporte disponible para clientes del plan empresarial que ayuda con consultas de facturaci\xf3n y de cuenta, y no tiene capacidad para aprovisionar o lanzar recursos de AWS, como cl\xfasteres de RDS. Amazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos para almacenar y recuperar archivos y datos, y no se puede usar para lanzar o aprovisionar cl\xfasteres de bases de datos. Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling ajusta autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias de c\xf3mputo de EC2 en respuesta a la demanda, y no tiene capacidad para lanzar o administrar cl\xfasteres de bases de datos RDS."},{"id":"q620","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes caracter\xedsticas se puede configurar a trav\xe9s del panel de Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC)?","options":{"A":"Distribuciones de Amazon CloudFront","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"Security Groups","D":"Subnets","E":"Elastic Load Balancing"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Security Groups y Subnets son caracter\xedsticas nativas de Amazon VPC; Security Groups controlan el tr\xe1fico a nivel de instancia y Subnets definen rangos de direcciones IP y la segmentaci\xf3n de red dentro de una VPC.\\nLas distribuciones de Amazon CloudFront se configuran a trav\xe9s de la consola de CloudFront y son un servicio CDN separado que no tiene superficie de configuraci\xf3n dentro del panel de Amazon VPC. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico que se administra a trav\xe9s de su propia consola, y no es una caracter\xedstica que se configure dentro de Amazon VPC. Elastic Load Balancing es un servicio separado que distribuye tr\xe1fico entre destinos y se configura a trav\xe9s de la consola de EC2 o de la consola dedicada de balanceadores de carga, no mediante el panel de Amazon VPC."},{"id":"q625","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS se definen como globales en lugar de regionales?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"Amazon EC2","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon CloudFront","E":"Amazon DynamoDB"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS global que opera sin estar vinculado a ninguna Regi\xf3n de AWS en particular, y Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido global cuyas ubicaciones de borde se distribuyen por todo el mundo independientemente de los despliegues regionales. Ambos son servicios globales en lugar de regionales.\\nAmazon EC2 es un servicio regional; las instancias se lanzan en Availability Zones espec\xedficas dentro de Regiones espec\xedficas, y cada despliegue de EC2 est\xe1 vinculado a su Regi\xf3n. Amazon S3 se considera generalmente un servicio regional porque los buckets se crean en una Regi\xf3n espec\xedfica y los datos residen all\xed de forma predeterminada, aunque los nombres de bucket son \xfanicos a nivel global. Amazon DynamoDB es un servicio regional donde las tablas se crean y los datos se almacenan en una Regi\xf3n espec\xedfica, aunque Global Tables puede replicar entre Regiones como una caracter\xedstica adicional."},{"id":"q636","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo se denominan las m\xfaltiples ubicaciones aisladas dentro de una Regi\xf3n de AWS que est\xe1n conectadas mediante redes de baja latencia?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connects","B":"Amazon VPCs","C":"Edge locations","D":"Availability Zones"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las Availability Zones son m\xfaltiples ubicaciones aisladas dentro de una Regi\xf3n de AWS, cada una con energ\xeda, refrigeraci\xf3n y red independientes, conectadas entre s\xed mediante enlaces de baja latencia para lograr alta disponibilidad.\\nAWS Direct Connect es un servicio de conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada para enlazar un centro de datos local con AWS, no un componente de infraestructura dentro de una Regi\xf3n. Amazon VPCs son redes privadas virtuales que los clientes configuran dentro de una Regi\xf3n para aislar sus recursos, no ubicaciones de infraestructura f\xedsica. Las Edge locations son puntos de presencia fuera de las Regiones que se utilizan para la entrega y el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de contenido, no ubicaciones aisladas dentro de una Regi\xf3n conectadas mediante enlaces de baja latencia."},{"id":"q650","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 dise\xf1ando una aplicaci\xf3n alojada en una \xfanica Regi\xf3n de AWS que atiende a usuarios finales distribuidos por todo el mundo. La empresa desea proporcionar a los usuarios finales acceso de baja latencia a los datos de la aplicaci\xf3n. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios ayudar\xe1 a cumplir este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"DNS global de Amazon Route 53","D":"Aceleraci\xf3n de transferencia de Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront entrega contenido desde las ubicaciones de borde m\xe1s cercanas a cada usuario final, lo que reduce dr\xe1sticamente la latencia de ida y vuelta al almacenar en cach\xe9 los datos de la aplicaci\xf3n en m\xe1s de 400 puntos de presencia en todo el mundo, de modo que los usuarios reciben respuestas desde una ubicaci\xf3n cercana en lugar del origen en una \xfanica Regi\xf3n.\\nAWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada desde un centro de datos local a AWS, lo que reduce la latencia en cargas de trabajo h\xedbridas, pero no entrega contenido de aplicaciones a usuarios finales distribuidos por todo el mundo desde una red de borde global. Amazon Route 53 con enrutamiento basado en latencia dirige a los usuarios a la Regi\xf3n de AWS m\xe1s cercana, lo que reduce la latencia en comparaci\xf3n con atender a todos los usuarios desde una \xfanica Regi\xf3n, pero una vez que la conexi\xf3n llega a la Regi\xf3n, no almacena contenido en cach\xe9 en las ubicaciones de borde como lo hace CloudFront. Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration utiliza las ubicaciones de borde de CloudFront para acelerar las cargas de datos a S3, pero es una caracter\xedstica de aceleraci\xf3n de cargas en lugar de un mecanismo de entrega de contenido que sirva datos de aplicaciones a usuarios finales con baja latencia."},{"id":"q653","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de Amazon RDS ayuda a crear bases de datos con redundancia global?","options":{"A":"Snapshots","B":"Aplicaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de parches y actualizaciones","C":"R\xe9plicas de lectura entre Regiones","D":"Provisioned IOPS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las r\xe9plicas de lectura entre Regiones de Amazon RDS crean copias de una base de datos replicadas de forma as\xedncrona en una Regi\xf3n de AWS separada, lo que proporciona datos con redundancia global que se pueden promover a una instancia primaria independiente en caso de fallo regional.\\nLos Snapshots son copias de seguridad en un punto en el tiempo almacenadas en S3 que se pueden copiar a otra Regi\xf3n con fines de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres, pero requieren restauraci\xf3n manual y no proporcionan una r\xe9plica sincronizada continuamente que se pueda promover r\xe1pidamente, por lo que son menos adecuados para la redundancia global. La aplicaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica de parches y actualizaciones es una caracter\xedstica de mantenimiento administrado que mantiene actualizados el motor de base de datos y el sistema operativo, pero no replica datos entre Regiones ni contribuye a la redundancia global. Provisioned IOPS es una opci\xf3n de almacenamiento que ofrece un alto rendimiento de E/S predecible para cargas de trabajo sensibles a la latencia, y es una configuraci\xf3n de rendimiento en lugar de una caracter\xedstica de replicaci\xf3n o redundancia."},{"id":"q658","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica agrega elasticidad a las instancias de Amazon EC2 para manejar la demanda cambiante de las cargas de trabajo?","options":{"A":"Grupos de recursos","B":"Pol\xedticas de ciclo de vida","C":"Application Load Balancer","D":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling monitorea m\xe9tricas definidas y ajusta autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias EC2 hacia arriba o hacia abajo seg\xfan las condiciones de la demanda, agregando elasticidad a la aplicaci\xf3n al garantizar que tenga la capacidad adecuada para manejar las cargas de trabajo actuales.\\nLos grupos de recursos organizan recursos de AWS por etiquetas o pilas de CloudFormation para que sea m\xe1s f\xe1cil administrarlos, automatizarlos y monitorearlos de forma colectiva, pero no agregan capacidad de c\xf3mputo ni permiten el escalado din\xe1mico de instancias EC2. Las pol\xedticas de ciclo de vida en el contexto de Amazon S3 transfieren autom\xe1ticamente objetos a diferentes clases de almacenamiento o los hacen expirar despu\xe9s de un per\xedodo especificado, y son una caracter\xedstica de administraci\xf3n de almacenamiento en lugar de un mecanismo de elasticidad de c\xf3mputo. Application Load Balancer distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre las instancias EC2 registradas para evitar que una sola instancia se sobrecargue, pero no ajusta el n\xfamero de instancias en ejecuci\xf3n; ese cambio de capacidad es funci\xf3n de Auto Scaling."},{"id":"q660","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido de AWS permite que las aplicaciones locales usen sin problemas el almacenamiento de AWS Cloud mediante protocolos est\xe1ndar de almacenamiento de archivos?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"AWS Snowball","C":"AWS Storage Gateway","D":"AWS Snowball Edge"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Storage Gateway proporciona a las aplicaciones locales acceso sin problemas al almacenamiento en la nube de AWS mediante protocolos est\xe1ndar como NFS, SMB e iSCSI, conectando entornos de almacenamiento h\xedbridos sin requerir que las aplicaciones se reescriban.\\nAWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, y es un servicio de conectividad de red en lugar de un servicio de integraci\xf3n de almacenamiento que exponga el almacenamiento en la nube a trav\xe9s de protocolos de archivos. AWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico de transporte de datos que se utiliza para migrar grandes vol\xfamenes de datos hacia o desde AWS, y es una herramienta de transferencia de datos \xfanica o peri\xf3dica en lugar de un servicio que proporcione acceso continuo y sin problemas al almacenamiento en la nube para aplicaciones locales. AWS Snowball Edge es un dispositivo f\xedsico que agrega capacidad de c\xf3mputo y almacenamiento local en el borde para casos de uso en los que la conectividad es limitada o intermitente, y es un dispositivo port\xe1til de computaci\xf3n en el borde en lugar de un servicio de integraci\xf3n de almacenamiento h\xedbrido que utilice protocolos de archivos est\xe1ndar."},{"id":"q664","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es un beneficio del acoplamiento d\xe9bil como principio del dise\xf1o de arquitectura en la nube?","options":{"A":"Facilita el procesamiento de solicitudes con baja latencia","B":"Permite que las aplicaciones tengan flujos de trabajo dependientes","C":"Previene fallos en cascada entre diferentes componentes","D":"Permite que las empresas se concentren en las operaciones de su centro de datos f\xedsico"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El acoplamiento d\xe9bil previene fallos en cascada porque los componentes interact\xfaan a trav\xe9s de interfaces bien definidas, como colas y API, en lugar de dependencias directas, de modo que un fallo en un componente no derriba a otros.\\nEl procesamiento de solicitudes con baja latencia es una caracter\xedstica de rendimiento que se logra mediante elecciones de infraestructura, como el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en el borde y el despliegue regional, y no es un beneficio del acoplamiento d\xe9bil como patr\xf3n arquitect\xf3nico. Permitir que las aplicaciones tengan flujos de trabajo dependientes describe el acoplamiento fuerte, que es lo opuesto al acoplamiento d\xe9bil y aumenta el riesgo de que un fallo en un componente afecte a otros. Permitir que las empresas se concentren en las operaciones de su centro de datos f\xedsico describe un modelo operativo local, que es lo opuesto a lo que habilita la adopci\xf3n de la nube y no est\xe1 relacionado en absoluto con el acoplamiento d\xe9bil como principio de dise\xf1o."},{"id":"q665","question":"Un director ha recibido la tarea de investigar la arquitectura de nube h\xedbrida. La empresa actualmente accede a AWS a trav\xe9s de la internet p\xfablica. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio facilitar\xe1 la conectividad h\xedbrida privada?","options":{"A":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC) NAT Gateway","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) Transfer Acceleration","D":"AWS Web Application Firewall (AWS WAF)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada y dedicada entre centros de datos locales y AWS, evitando la internet p\xfablica para proporcionar rendimiento constante, menor latencia y mayor seguridad para la conectividad de nube h\xedbrida.\\nAmazon VPC NAT Gateway permite que las instancias en subredes privadas inicien conexiones salientes a internet mientras evita conexiones entrantes no solicitadas, y es un mecanismo de acceso saliente a internet para recursos privados en lugar de un servicio que facilita la conectividad h\xedbrida privada entre entornos locales y AWS. Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration acelera las transferencias de datos hacia y desde buckets de S3 mediante el enrutamiento del tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de ubicaciones de borde de AWS CloudFront, y es una optimizaci\xf3n del rendimiento de transferencia de datos en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de conectividad h\xedbrida privada. AWS WAF es un firewall de aplicaciones web que filtra el tr\xe1fico HTTP y HTTPS seg\xfan reglas definidas, y es un servicio de seguridad de filtrado de tr\xe1fico en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de conectividad de red para la arquitectura de nube h\xedbrida."},{"id":"q674","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios es una base de datos compatible con MySQL que aumenta autom\xe1ticamente el almacenamiento seg\xfan sea necesario?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)","B":"Amazon Relational Database Service (Amazon RDS) for MySQL","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"Amazon Aurora"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Aurora es una base de datos compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL que aumenta autom\xe1ticamente el almacenamiento en incrementos de 10 GB hasta 128 TB, sin requerir intervenci\xf3n manual ni tiempo de inactividad para la gesti\xf3n del almacenamiento.\\nAmazon EC2 es un servicio de servidores virtuales que proporciona capacidad de c\xf3mputo y no incluye un motor de base de datos integrado ni escalado autom\xe1tico de almacenamiento. Amazon RDS for MySQL es una base de datos MySQL administrada, pero requiere escalado manual de almacenamiento o la habilitaci\xf3n del escalado autom\xe1tico de almacenamiento como una configuraci\xf3n separada, y no crece autom\xe1ticamente de forma predeterminada de la misma manera que Aurora. Amazon Lightsail es una plataforma en la nube simplificada para implementar aplicaciones peque\xf1as y sitios web y no ofrece las capacidades de escalado autom\xe1tico de almacenamiento descritas."},{"id":"q675","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC) permite a los usuarios conectar dos VPC entre s\xed?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC endpoints","B":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) ClassicLink","C":"Amazon VPC peering","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon VPC peering crea una conexi\xf3n de red privada entre dos VPC, lo que permite que los recursos de cada VPC se comuniquen entre s\xed mediante direcciones IP privadas como si estuvieran en la misma red.\\nAmazon VPC endpoints permiten conectividad privada entre una VPC y los servicios de AWS compatibles sin requerir que el tr\xe1fico atraviese la internet p\xfablica, pero no conectan dos VPC entre s\xed. EC2 ClassicLink era una caracter\xedstica heredada que permit\xeda a las instancias EC2-Classic comunicarse con recursos de VPC, no un mecanismo para conectar dos VPC entre s\xed. AWS Direct Connect proporciona un circuito privado dedicado entre un centro de datos local y AWS, no una conexi\xf3n entre dos VPC."},{"id":"q678","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona una forma segura, r\xe1pida y rentable de migrar o transportar conjuntos de datos a escala de exabytes a AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Batch","B":"AWS Snowball","C":"AWS Migration Hub","D":"AWS Snowmobile"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Snowmobile es un servicio de transferencia de datos a escala de exabytes que utiliza un contenedor de transporte de 45 pies que puede mover hasta 100 PB por viaje, dise\xf1ado para las migraciones de datos m\xe1s grandes posibles a AWS.\\nAWS Batch es un servicio administrado para ejecutar cargas de trabajo de c\xf3mputo por lotes a escala en instancias EC2, y es un servicio de programaci\xf3n de trabajos y c\xf3mputo sin capacidad de transportar f\xedsicamente ni migrar conjuntos de datos a AWS. AWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico de transporte de datos adecuado para transferir hasta 80 TB por dispositivo, lo que a 500 TB requerir\xeda varios dispositivos, pero a escala de exabytes no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para migraciones de esa magnitud, donde Snowmobile es la opci\xf3n adecuada. AWS Migration Hub proporciona una ubicaci\xf3n centralizada para realizar un seguimiento del progreso de las migraciones de aplicaciones a AWS mediante diversas herramientas de migraci\xf3n, y es un servicio de monitoreo de migraciones en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de transporte f\xedsico de datos para conjuntos de datos a escala de exabytes."},{"id":"q680","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 tipos de balanceadores de carga est\xe1n disponibles con Elastic Load Balancing (ELB)?","options":{"A":"Balanceadores de carga p\xfablicos con capacidades de AWS Application Auto Scaling","B":"Balanceadores de carga F5 Big-IP y Citrix NetScaler","C":"Classic Load Balancers","D":"Balanceadores de carga entre zonas con IP p\xfablicas y privadas","E":"Application Load Balancers"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Classic Load Balancers son la opci\xf3n heredada de ELB que admite tr\xe1fico HTTP, HTTPS y TCP, y Application Load Balancers son la opci\xf3n moderna de capa 7 para el enrutamiento HTTP y HTTPS con reglas avanzadas de enrutamiento basadas en solicitudes; ambos son tipos oficiales de balanceadores de carga de ELB.\\nLos balanceadores de carga p\xfablicos con capacidades de AWS Application Auto Scaling combinan dos servicios separados y no describen un tipo de balanceador de carga disponible dentro de ELB. F5 Big-IP y Citrix NetScaler son productos de balanceo de carga de terceros que se ejecutan en entornos locales o en EC2, no tipos nativos de balanceadores de carga de ELB. Los balanceadores de carga entre zonas con IP p\xfablicas y privadas describen una caracter\xedstica de configuraci\xf3n de ELB en lugar de un tipo distinto de balanceador de carga."},{"id":"q682","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n proporciona los tiempos de respuesta de aplicaci\xf3n M\xc1S R\xc1PIDOS para datos a los que se accede con frecuencia a usuarios en m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad","B":"Amazon CloudFront a ubicaciones de borde","C":"AWS CloudFormation en m\xfaltiples regiones","D":"Una puerta de enlace privada virtual sobre AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront almacena en cach\xe9 contenido al que se accede con frecuencia en ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo, y entrega respuestas desde la ubicaci\xf3n m\xe1s cercana al usuario que realiza la solicitud, lo que proporciona los tiempos de respuesta m\xe1s r\xe1pidos para usuarios distribuidos globalmente en comparaci\xf3n con obtenerlos de una \xfanica Regi\xf3n de origen cada vez.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n para auditor\xeda y cumplimiento en las Zonas de Disponibilidad, pero no participa en la entrega de contenido de aplicaciones ni en la reducci\xf3n de la latencia de respuesta para los usuarios. AWS CloudFormation aprovisiona y administra la infraestructura mediante plantillas de c\xf3digo y se puede implementar en m\xfaltiples Regiones para mantener la coherencia, pero es un servicio de aprovisionamiento de infraestructura en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de aceleraci\xf3n de contenido que reduzca los tiempos de respuesta para los usuarios finales. Una puerta de enlace privada virtual sobre AWS Direct Connect proporciona un enlace de red privado dedicado entre un entorno local y una Regi\xf3n de AWS, lo que reduce la latencia en esa ruta espec\xedfica, pero no entrega contenido de aplicaciones a usuarios distribuidos globalmente con la menor latencia posible."},{"id":"q684","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes servicios de AWS se puede utilizar para ejecutar una base de datos autogestionada?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"AWS X-Ray","C":"AWS Snowmobile","D":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales con acceso completo a nivel de sistema operativo, lo que permite instalar y administrar cualquier software de base de datos por cuenta propia con control total sobre la configuraci\xf3n, el ajuste y el mantenimiento.\\nAmazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico sin capacidad de c\xf3mputo para alojar o ejecutar software de base de datos. AWS X-Ray es un servicio de seguimiento y depuraci\xf3n de aplicaciones para analizar el rendimiento de aplicaciones distribuidas, no relacionado con el alojamiento de bases de datos. AWS Snowmobile es un servicio de transferencia f\xedsica de datos para migrar conjuntos de datos extremadamente grandes a AWS y no se puede utilizar para ejecutar ni alojar ning\xfan software."},{"id":"q699","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS usar\xeda un cliente con un sitio web est\xe1tico para lograr menor latencia y altas velocidades de transferencia?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda","B":"Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator","C":"Amazon Route 53","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront almacena en cach\xe9 el contenido de sitios web est\xe1ticos en ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo y lo entrega a los usuarios desde la ubicaci\xf3n m\xe1s cercana para reducir la latencia y aumentar las velocidades de transferencia.\\nAWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor para ejecutar c\xf3digo basado en eventos y no tiene ninguna funci\xf3n en el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 ni en la entrega de contenido de sitios web est\xe1ticos. Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator es una cach\xe9 en memoria espec\xedficamente para consultas de base de datos de DynamoDB y no est\xe1 relacionado con la entrega de contenido de sitios web est\xe1ticos. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico que dirige a los usuarios al endpoint correcto, pero no almacena en cach\xe9 ni entrega contenido por s\xed mismo."},{"id":"q700","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios gestionan y automatizan las implementaciones de aplicaciones en AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","B":"AWS CodeCommit","C":"AWS CodePipeline","D":"AWS CloudFormation","E":"AWS Config"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk automatiza la implementaci\xf3n y el escalado de aplicaciones al encargarse autom\xe1ticamente del aprovisionamiento de la infraestructura subyacente, el balanceo de carga y la supervisi\xf3n. AWS CloudFormation automatiza el aprovisionamiento de infraestructura mediante plantillas reutilizables, lo que permite a los equipos definir e implementar recursos de AWS de forma coherente y repetible.\\nAWS CodeCommit es un servicio de control de c\xf3digo fuente administrado para alojar repositorios privados de Git y no tiene funci\xf3n alguna en la implementaci\xf3n o gesti\xf3n de aplicaciones. AWS CodePipeline es un servicio de entrega continua que orquesta las etapas de una canalizaci\xf3n de lanzamiento, pero coordina el proceso de implementaci\xf3n en lugar de gestionar o automatizar el entorno de implementaci\xf3n en s\xed. AWS Config supervisa y registra continuamente las configuraciones de los recursos de AWS con fines de cumplimiento y no gestiona ni automatiza las implementaciones de aplicaciones."},{"id":"q704","question":"Una empresa quiere supervisar el uso de CPU de sus recursos de Amazon EC2. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar la empresa?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS Cost and Usage report","D":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch supervisa la utilizaci\xf3n de CPU y otras m\xe9tricas de rendimiento de las instancias de EC2, y proporciona paneles, alarmas y acciones automatizadas basadas en umbrales de uso de recursos.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra las llamadas a la API y la actividad de la cuenta con fines de auditor\xeda y seguridad, y no recopila ni muestra m\xe9tricas de rendimiento de recursos. AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona datos detallados de facturaci\xf3n y costos de los servicios de AWS, y no tiene capacidad para supervisar el rendimiento de c\xf3mputo ni la utilizaci\xf3n de recursos. Amazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda que env\xeda notificaciones y alertas a los suscriptores y, aunque puede activarse mediante alarmas de CloudWatch, no supervisa ni recopila m\xe9tricas de rendimiento por s\xed mismo."},{"id":"q708","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes servicios de AWS proporcionan recursos de c\xf3mputo?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda","B":"Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS)","C":"AWS CodeDeploy","D":"Amazon S3 Glacier","E":"AWS Organizations"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"AWS Lambda proporciona c\xf3mputo sin servidor al ejecutar c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin requerir que aprovisione o gestione servidores, y Amazon ECS ejecuta aplicaciones en contenedores sobre infraestructura de c\xf3mputo administrada. Ambos son servicios de c\xf3mputo de AWS dise\xf1ados para ese prop\xf3sito.\\nAWS CodeDeploy automatiza la implementaci\xf3n de c\xf3digo de aplicaciones en destinos de c\xf3mputo como instancias de EC2 y funciones de Lambda, y es una herramienta de automatizaci\xf3n de implementaciones en lugar de un servicio de c\xf3mputo que proporciona recursos. Amazon S3 Glacier es un servicio de almacenamiento de archivo dise\xf1ado para datos de acceso poco frecuente con tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n que van de minutos a horas, y es un servicio de almacenamiento sin capacidad de c\xf3mputo. AWS Organizations es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de cuentas para gobernar y consolidar centralmente m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS, y no tiene capacidad de c\xf3mputo de ning\xfan tipo."},{"id":"q709","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a los usuarios implementar infraestructura como c\xf3digo al automatizar el proceso de aprovisionamiento de recursos?","options":{"A":"Amazon GameLift","B":"AWS CloudFormation","C":"AWS Data Pipeline","D":"AWS Glue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudFormation permite la infraestructura como c\xf3digo al permitirle definir recursos de AWS en plantillas JSON o YAML, automatizando el aprovisionamiento y la gesti\xf3n de una pila de infraestructura completa de forma repetible y coherente.\\nAmazon GameLift es un servicio administrado para implementar, operar y escalar servidores de juegos basados en la nube, totalmente ajeno al aprovisionamiento de infraestructura. AWS Data Pipeline (en desuso) es un servicio para orquestar y automatizar el movimiento y la transformaci\xf3n de datos entre servicios de AWS, no para aprovisionar recursos de infraestructura. AWS Glue es un servicio administrado de extracci\xf3n, transformaci\xf3n y carga para preparar y mover datos para cargas de trabajo anal\xedticas, sin relaci\xf3n con la infraestructura como c\xf3digo."},{"id":"q711","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes permite a los usuarios aprovisionar una conexi\xf3n de red dedicada desde su red interna hacia AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudHSM","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS VPN","D":"Amazon Connect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada desde su red interna hacia AWS, lo que proporciona un rendimiento de red m\xe1s constante que las conexiones basadas en internet.\\nAWS CloudHSM es un servicio de m\xf3dulo de seguridad de hardware que proporciona almacenamiento y procesamiento dedicados de claves criptogr\xe1ficas, y no tiene capacidad de conectividad de red. AWS VPN crea un t\xfanel cifrado sobre el internet p\xfablico entre redes locales y AWS, pero no proporciona una conexi\xf3n privada dedicada con ancho de banda garantizado y rendimiento constante. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube que permite operaciones de atenci\xf3n al cliente, y no tiene relaci\xf3n alguna con la conectividad de red entre la infraestructura local y AWS."},{"id":"q712","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios utilizan ubicaciones de borde de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Shield","C":"Amazon EC2","D":"Amazon RDS","E":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Amazon CloudFront utiliza ubicaciones de borde para almacenar en cach\xe9 y entregar contenido a los usuarios desde el punto de presencia m\xe1s cercano, y AWS Shield proporciona protecci\xf3n contra DDoS en el borde al inspeccionar y mitigar el tr\xe1fico de ataque antes de que llegue a la infraestructura de origen.\\nLas instancias de Amazon EC2 se implementan en zonas de disponibilidad dentro de las regiones de AWS, no en ubicaciones de borde; EC2 es un servicio de c\xf3mputo regional cuyas instancias se ejecutan en centros de datos en lugar de en los puntos de presencia de borde utilizados por CloudFront y Shield. Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado que se ejecuta en regiones de AWS y zonas de disponibilidad espec\xedficas, no en ubicaciones de borde, y es un servicio regional sin presencia en el borde. Amazon ElastiCache proporciona almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria dentro de las regiones de AWS para reducir la carga de la base de datos y, aunque mejora la latencia de las aplicaciones dentro de una regi\xf3n, no opera en ubicaciones de borde y no es un servicio de borde distribuido globalmente."},{"id":"q713","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio proporcionar\xeda conectividad de red en una arquitectura h\xedbrida que incluye la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS Directory Service","D":"Amazon API Gateway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, lo que lo convierte en el servicio dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para establecer conectividad de red en una arquitectura h\xedbrida.\\nAmazon VPC aprovisiona una red virtual aislada dentro de AWS para implementar y conectar recursos en la nube y, aunque es un componente de red fundamental en el lado de AWS, no conecta por s\xed mismo la infraestructura local con la nube de AWS. AWS Directory Service proporciona Microsoft Active Directory administrado en la nube para la gesti\xf3n de identidades y accesos, y es una herramienta de autenticaci\xf3n y directorio sin capacidad de conectividad de red entre entornos locales y en la nube. Amazon API Gateway es un servicio totalmente administrado para crear, publicar y gestionar API a escala, y es un servicio de integraci\xf3n de aplicaciones sin capacidad para establecer conectividad de red dedicada en una arquitectura h\xedbrida."},{"id":"q720","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS es una red de entrega de contenido que entrega de forma segura datos, video y aplicaciones a usuarios de todo el mundo con baja latencia y altas velocidades?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudFormation","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"Amazon CloudFront","D":"Amazon Pinpoint"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront es una CDN global que entrega de forma segura datos, video y aplicaciones desde ubicaciones de borde cercanas a los usuarios de todo el mundo, minimizando la latencia y maximizando las velocidades de transferencia.\\nAWS CloudFormation es un servicio de infraestructura como c\xf3digo que aprovisiona y gestiona recursos de AWS mediante plantillas, y no tiene capacidad de entrega de contenido ni de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en el borde. AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, lo que mejora la conectividad h\xedbrida, pero no entrega contenido a usuarios finales globales. Amazon Pinpoint es un servicio de interacci\xf3n con el cliente que se utiliza para comunicaciones de marketing dirigidas, como correo electr\xf3nico, SMS y notificaciones push, y no tiene relaci\xf3n alguna con la entrega de contenido ni la aceleraci\xf3n de red."},{"id":"q729","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se encarga de los detalles de implementaci\xf3n del aprovisionamiento de capacidad, balanceo de carga, Auto Scaling y monitoreo del estado de las aplicaciones?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","C":"Amazon Route 53","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk se encarga autom\xe1ticamente del aprovisionamiento de capacidad, el balanceo de carga, Auto Scaling y el monitoreo del estado de las aplicaciones, lo que permite a los desarrolladores implementar aplicaciones sin necesidad de administrar la infraestructura subyacente.\\nAWS Config registra y eval\xfaa cambios de configuraci\xf3n en recursos de AWS con fines de cumplimiento y auditor\xeda, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en la implementaci\xf3n de aplicaciones ni en el aprovisionamiento de infraestructura. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico y no se encarga de la implementaci\xf3n de aplicaciones, el balanceo de carga ni el monitoreo de estado. Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido para almacenar en cach\xe9 y distribuir contenido globalmente y no es un servicio de implementaci\xf3n de aplicaciones ni de administraci\xf3n de infraestructura."},{"id":"q742","question":"Una empresa requiere una conexi\xf3n de red dedicada entre sus servidores on-premises y la nube de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se debe usar?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"Amazon API Gateway","D":"Amazon Connect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red f\xedsica privada dedicada entre servidores on-premises y AWS, lo que ofrece un rendimiento constante y una latencia predecible que no est\xe1 sujeta a la congesti\xf3n de Internet.\\nAWS VPN crea un t\xfanel cifrado entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS, pero enruta el tr\xe1fico por la Internet p\xfablica, lo que significa que el rendimiento puede variar y no proporciona el enlace f\xedsico dedicado que requiere la pregunta. Amazon API Gateway es un servicio administrado para crear, publicar y proteger API que conectan aplicaciones con servicios de backend, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en el establecimiento de conectividad de red entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto en la nube para administrar comunicaciones con clientes mediante voz y chat, y no est\xe1 relacionado con la conectividad de red ni con la integraci\xf3n on-premises."},{"id":"q743","question":"AWS CloudFormation est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para ayudar al usuario a:","options":{"A":"Modelar y aprovisionar recursos","B":"Actualizar el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n","C":"Configurar lagos de datos","D":"Crear informes de facturaci\xf3n"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS CloudFormation est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para modelar y aprovisionar recursos de AWS mediante plantillas, lo que permite definir toda la pila de infraestructura como c\xf3digo e implementarla de forma automatizada y repetible.\\nLa actualizaci\xf3n del c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n la manejan herramientas para desarrolladores como AWS CodeDeploy, que administra implementaciones de aplicaciones en instancias y funciones sin servidor. CloudFormation aprovisiona y administra recursos de infraestructura, no cambios de c\xf3digo a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n. La configuraci\xf3n de lagos de datos es funci\xf3n de servicios como AWS Lake Formation, que simplifica la creaci\xf3n, protecci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de lagos de datos en AWS. CloudFormation puede aprovisionar los recursos subyacentes, pero no es en s\xed mismo un servicio de lago de datos. La creaci\xf3n de informes de facturaci\xf3n es funci\xf3n de AWS Cost Explorer y AWS Billing, que brindan visibilidad sobre el gasto y el uso en una cuenta. CloudFormation no tiene capacidad de informes ni de facturaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q744","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes es un servicio de base de datos de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)","C":"Amazon S3 Glacier","D":"AWS Snowball"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos en la nube totalmente administrado dise\xf1ado para almacenar y consultar grandes vol\xfamenes de datos estructurados, lo que lo convierte en un servicio de base de datos de AWS.\\nAmazon Elastic Block Store proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque persistentes que se adjuntan a instancias EC2 para usarse como almacenamiento sin procesar, y no es un servicio de base de datos. Amazon S3 Glacier es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos de archivo dise\xf1ado para la retenci\xf3n a largo plazo de datos de acceso poco frecuente, no una base de datos para consultar o administrar registros estructurados. AWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico de computaci\xf3n de borde y transferencia de datos que se usa para mover grandes cantidades de datos hacia o desde AWS, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en el almacenamiento o la consulta de datos como servicio de base de datos."},{"id":"q749","question":"Un Cloud Practitioner necesita almacenar datos durante 7 a\xf1os para cumplir requisitos regulatorios. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumplir\xe1 este requisito al COSTO m\xe1s BAJO?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"AWS Snowball","C":"Amazon Redshift","D":"Amazon S3 Glacier"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon S3 Glacier proporciona el almacenamiento de menor costo para el archivo de datos a largo plazo, lo que lo hace ideal para datos regulatorios que deben conservarse durante a\xf1os pero a los que se accede con poca frecuencia.\\nAmazon S3 Standard proporciona almacenamiento de objetos duradero y de alta disponibilidad con recuperaci\xf3n inmediata en milisegundos, y aunque cumple con los requisitos de retenci\xf3n a largo plazo, es significativamente m\xe1s costoso por gigabyte que S3 Glacier para datos a los que se accede con poca frecuencia durante un per\xedodo de siete a\xf1os. AWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico de transporte de datos que se usa para migrar grandes vol\xfamenes de datos hacia o desde AWS, y es una herramienta de transferencia \xfanica en lugar de un servicio de almacenamiento a largo plazo para cumplir requisitos de retenci\xf3n regulatorios. Amazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos administrado optimizado para ejecutar consultas SQL anal\xedticas sobre grandes conjuntos de datos, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo anal\xedticas activas en lugar de la retenci\xf3n a largo plazo de bajo costo de datos regulatorios de acceso poco frecuente."},{"id":"q751","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el prop\xf3sito de AWS Storage Gateway?","options":{"A":"Garantiza que el almacenamiento de datos on-premises tenga una durabilidad del 99.999999999 %","B":"Transporta petabytes de datos hacia y desde AWS","C":"Se conecta a m\xfaltiples instancias de Amazon EC2","D":"Conecta el almacenamiento de datos on-premises con la nube de AWS"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Storage Gateway es un servicio de almacenamiento en la nube h\xedbrida que le brinda acceso on-premises a almacenamiento en la nube pr\xe1cticamente ilimitado. Conecta sus aplicaciones y flujos de trabajo on-premises existentes con servicios de almacenamiento de AWS como Amazon S3, Amazon EBS y Amazon FSx mediante protocolos de almacenamiento est\xe1ndar como NFS, SMB e iSCSI.\\nLa cifra de durabilidad del 99.999999999 % (11 nueves) es una caracter\xedstica de dise\xf1o espec\xedfica del almacenamiento de objetos de Amazon S3, no una garant\xeda aplicada al hardware local on-premises. El transporte f\xedsico de petabytes de datos hacia y desde AWS es la funci\xf3n principal de AWS Snow Family (como AWS Snowball), mientras que Storage Gateway es para la integraci\xf3n continua basada en red. La conexi\xf3n a m\xfaltiples instancias de Amazon EC2 es una caracter\xedstica de servicios de almacenamiento compartido como Amazon EFS o Amazon FSx, en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de conectividad h\xedbrida para centros de datos on-premises."},{"id":"q754","question":"Un Cloud Practitioner necesita una conexi\xf3n dedicada y consistente entre recursos de AWS y un sistema on-premises. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede cumplir este requisito?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"AWS VPN","C":"Amazon Connect","D":"AWS Data Pipeline"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada y consistente entre sistemas on-premises y AWS, lo que ofrece un rendimiento predecible y una latencia menor que las conexiones basadas en Internet.\\nAWS VPN crea un t\xfanel cifrado entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS, pero enruta el tr\xe1fico por la Internet p\xfablica, lo que significa que el rendimiento puede variar y no proporciona la conexi\xf3n f\xedsica dedicada que requiere la pregunta. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto en la nube para administrar comunicaciones con clientes mediante voz y chat, y no est\xe1 relacionado en absoluto con la conectividad de red entre sistemas on-premises y AWS. AWS Data Pipeline (obsoleto) es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo para automatizar el movimiento y la transformaci\xf3n de datos entre servicios de AWS y or\xedgenes on-premises, y es una herramienta de procesamiento de datos sin capacidad de conectividad de red."},{"id":"q759","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones describe las relaciones entre las Regiones de AWS, las Zonas de Disponibilidad y las ubicaciones de borde?","options":{"A":"Hay m\xe1s Regiones de AWS que Zonas de Disponibilidad","B":"Hay m\xe1s ubicaciones de borde que Regiones de AWS","C":"Una ubicaci\xf3n de borde es una Zona de Disponibilidad","D":"Hay m\xe1s Regiones de AWS que ubicaciones de borde","E":"Hay m\xe1s Zonas de Disponibilidad que Regiones de AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"Hay cientos de ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo en comparaci\xf3n con docenas de Regiones, y cada Regi\xf3n contiene varias Zonas de Disponibilidad, por lo que ambos recuentos son mayores que el n\xfamero de Regiones.\\nLa afirmaci\xf3n de que hay m\xe1s Regiones de AWS que Zonas de Disponibilidad es falsa. Cada Regi\xf3n contiene al menos dos o tres AZ, por lo que las AZ siempre superan en n\xfamero a las Regiones. La afirmaci\xf3n de que una ubicaci\xf3n de borde es una Zona de Disponibilidad es falsa. Las ubicaciones de borde son puntos de presencia separados que se usan para la entrega de contenido y no forman parte de la infraestructura de AZ. La afirmaci\xf3n de que hay m\xe1s Regiones de AWS que ubicaciones de borde es falsa. Las ubicaciones de borde suman cientos, muy por encima del n\xfamero de Regiones."},{"id":"q767","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 ayuda a una empresa a proporcionar una experiencia de menor latencia a sus usuarios a nivel global?","options":{"A":"Usar una Regi\xf3n de AWS que sea central para todos los usuarios","B":"Usar una segunda Zona de Disponibilidad en la Regi\xf3n de AWS que se est\xe1 usando","C":"Habilitar el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en la Regi\xf3n de AWS que se est\xe1 usando","D":"Usar ubicaciones de borde para acercar el contenido a todos los usuarios"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las ubicaciones de borde colocan contenido en cach\xe9 m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios a nivel global mediante Amazon CloudFront, lo que proporciona la menor latencia posible independientemente de d\xf3nde se encuentre el servidor de origen.\\nUsar una \xfanica Regi\xf3n central todav\xeda requiere que los usuarios en ubicaciones distantes enruten el tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de largas distancias de red, y ninguna Regi\xf3n puede ser geogr\xe1ficamente central para todos los usuarios del mundo. Agregar una segunda Zona de Disponibilidad mejora la disponibilidad y la tolerancia a fallos dentro de una Regi\xf3n, pero no reduce la distancia f\xedsica entre la aplicaci\xf3n y los usuarios globales. Habilitar el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 dentro de una sola Regi\xf3n mantiene el contenido en cach\xe9 en una ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica, lo que solo beneficia a los usuarios cercanos a esa Regi\xf3n en lugar de reducir la latencia para los usuarios a nivel global."},{"id":"q769","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de Amazon RDS se puede usar para lograr alta disponibilidad?","options":{"A":"M\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad","B":"Amazon Reserved Instances","C":"Almacenamiento Provisioned IOPS","D":"Monitoreo mejorado"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La implementaci\xf3n Multi-AZ de Amazon RDS mantiene una r\xe9plica en espera s\xedncrona en una Zona de Disponibilidad diferente y realiza un failover autom\xe1tico a la r\xe9plica en espera sin intervenci\xf3n manual si la instancia principal deja de estar disponible, lo que proporciona alta disponibilidad para cargas de trabajo de bases de datos.\\nAmazon Reserved Instances es un modelo de facturaci\xf3n que ofrece descuentos significativos a cambio de un compromiso de uno o tres a\xf1os, y es una herramienta de optimizaci\xf3n de costos en lugar de una caracter\xedstica de alta disponibilidad para instancias de bases de datos. El almacenamiento Provisioned IOPS ofrece un rendimiento de E/S constante y de baja latencia para cargas de trabajo exigentes al asignar capacidad de E/S dedicada, y es una opci\xf3n de optimizaci\xf3n de rendimiento en lugar de un mecanismo para lograr alta disponibilidad entre Zonas de Disponibilidad. Enhanced Monitoring proporciona m\xe9tricas granulares en tiempo real sobre el sistema operativo de una instancia de RDS en intervalos de hasta un segundo, lo que permite visibilidad detallada del rendimiento, pero es una caracter\xedstica de monitoreo en lugar de una configuraci\xf3n de alta disponibilidad."},{"id":"q774","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS simplificar\xeda la migraci\xf3n de una base de datos a AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Storage Gateway","B":"AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","C":"Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)","D":"Amazon AppStream 2.0"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Database Migration Service simplifica la migraci\xf3n de bases de datos al gestionar la replicaci\xf3n de datos desde el origen hasta el destino con un tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo, y admite migraciones homog\xe9neas, como de Oracle a Oracle, y migraciones heterog\xe9neas entre diferentes motores de bases de datos.\\nAWS Storage Gateway conecta entornos locales con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS para casos de uso de almacenamiento h\xedbrido, y aunque maneja el movimiento de datos, es un servicio de integraci\xf3n de almacenamiento para archivos y datos de bloques en lugar de un servicio dise\xf1ado para migrar bases de datos relacionales. Amazon EC2 puede alojar un proceso de migraci\xf3n de bases de datos personalizado, pero requiere que los clientes configuren y gestionen manualmente todas las herramientas de migraci\xf3n, lo que lo hace mucho menos directo que usar el servicio DMS dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente. Amazon AppStream 2.0 es un servicio completamente gestionado de transmisi\xf3n de aplicaciones que transmite aplicaciones de escritorio desde AWS al navegador de un usuario, y es un servicio de entrega de aplicaciones y escritorios virtuales sin relaci\xf3n con la migraci\xf3n de bases de datos."},{"id":"q776","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de redes de AWS permite a una empresa crear una red virtual dentro de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC) permite a las empresas crear redes virtuales aisladas dentro de AWS, con control total sobre el direccionamiento IP, las subredes, el enrutamiento y las puertas de enlace de red.\\nAWS Config rastrea y registra continuamente el estado de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento y detectar cambios de configuraci\xf3n, y es una herramienta de gobernanza y auditor\xeda sin capacidad para aprovisionar o definir redes virtuales. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico que resuelve nombres de dominio y dirige a los usuarios a los endpoints de las aplicaciones, y es un servicio de gesti\xf3n de dominios en lugar de un servicio para crear redes virtuales dentro de AWS. AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, y es un servicio de conectividad h\xedbrida para enlazar redes externas con AWS en lugar de un servicio para crear redes virtuales dentro de AWS."},{"id":"q778","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 componente de la infraestructura global de AWS usa Amazon CloudFront para garantizar una entrega de baja latencia?","options":{"A":"Regiones de AWS","B":"Ubicaciones de borde de AWS","C":"Zonas de Disponibilidad de AWS","D":"Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (Amazon VPC)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront usa ubicaciones de borde de AWS, distribuidas en cientos de ciudades de todo el mundo, para almacenar en cach\xe9 y entregar contenido desde la ubicaci\xf3n m\xe1s cercana a cada usuario con el fin de lograr una entrega de baja latencia.\\nLas Regiones de AWS son \xe1reas geogr\xe1ficas que contienen varias Zonas de Disponibilidad, se usan para implementar y ejecutar cargas de trabajo, no para almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en el borde y entrega de contenido. Las Zonas de Disponibilidad de AWS son cl\xfasteres de centros de datos aislados dentro de una Regi\xf3n, se usan para alta disponibilidad y redundancia, no para entrega de contenido. Amazon VPC es una red virtual privada dentro de una Regi\xf3n, se usa para aislar y controlar el tr\xe1fico de red, no para entregar contenido a nivel global."},{"id":"q781","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio usar\xeda para enviar alertas basadas en alarmas de Amazon CloudWatch?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon Route 53"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SNS entrega notificaciones activadas por alarmas de CloudWatch al enviar mensajes a endpoints suscritos, como direcciones de correo electr\xf3nico, SMS, funciones Lambda, colas SQS y endpoints HTTP, lo que lo convierte en el punto de integraci\xf3n para alertas basadas en alarmas.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n en una cuenta de AWS para auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no env\xeda alertas basadas en alarmas de CloudWatch; es un servicio de registro en lugar de un mecanismo de entrega de notificaciones. AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS seg\xfan comprobaciones de pr\xe1cticas recomendadas en seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y aunque genera recomendaciones, no se integra con las alarmas de CloudWatch para enviar alertas activadas. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS escalable que admite registro de dominios, enrutamiento y comprobaciones de estado, y aunque tiene sus propias alertas de comprobaci\xf3n de estado, no sirve como servicio de entrega de notificaciones para alarmas generales de CloudWatch."},{"id":"q784","question":"Al dise\xf1ar una aplicaci\xf3n web t\xedpica de tres niveles, \xbfqu\xe9 servicios y/o caracter\xedsticas de AWS mejoran la disponibilidad y reducen el impacto de las fallas?","options":{"A":"AWS Auto Scaling para instancias de Amazon EC2","B":"ACL de subred de Amazon VPC para comprobar el estado de un servicio","C":"Recursos distribuidos en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad","D":"Alarmas de Amazon CloudWatch para notificar cuando una instancia de EC2 entra en un estado no saludable","E":"Recursos distribuidos en m\xfaltiples puntos de presencia de AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS Auto Scaling reemplaza autom\xe1ticamente las instancias no saludables y ajusta la capacidad, y distribuir recursos en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad proporciona redundancia; ambas cosas mejoran la disponibilidad y reducen el impacto de las fallas.\\nLas ACL de subred de VPC son reglas de control de acceso a la red sin estado que filtran el tr\xe1fico a nivel de subred y no realizan comprobaciones de estado ni contribuyen a la disponibilidad de la aplicaci\xf3n. Las alarmas de CloudWatch env\xedan notificaciones cuando una m\xe9trica cruza un umbral, pero no remedian autom\xe1ticamente las fallas ni restauran la disponibilidad por s\xed solas. Los puntos de presencia son ubicaciones de borde de CloudFront que se usan para la entrega y el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de contenido, no para alojar niveles de la aplicaci\xf3n ni proporcionar redundancia para una aplicaci\xf3n web de tres niveles."},{"id":"q794","question":"Una empresa planea lanzar un sitio de comercio electr\xf3nico en una sola Regi\xf3n de AWS para una base de usuarios mundial. \xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS permitir\xe1n a la empresa llegar a los usuarios y proporcionar baja latencia y altas velocidades de transferencia?","options":{"A":"Application Load Balancer","B":"AWS Global Accelerator","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Amazon CloudFront","E":"AWS Lambda"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator enruta el tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de la red global de AWS hacia el endpoint \xf3ptimo seg\xfan el estado, la geograf\xeda y las pol\xedticas de enrutamiento, y Amazon CloudFront almacena contenido en cach\xe9 en ubicaciones de borde de todo el mundo. Juntos proporcionan baja latencia y altas velocidades de transferencia para una base de usuarios global.\\nApplication Load Balancer distribuye el tr\xe1fico entre los destinos dentro de una Regi\xf3n de AWS, lo que mejora la disponibilidad y el rendimiento de las aplicaciones dentro de una Regi\xf3n, pero no ampl\xeda el alcance a usuarios globales en m\xfaltiples ubicaciones geogr\xe1ficas. AWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor que ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin administrar servidores, y es un servicio de c\xf3mputo en lugar de una soluci\xf3n global de entrega de contenido o aceleraci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico. AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada desde un centro de datos local hacia AWS para cargas de trabajo h\xedbridas, y no es una soluci\xf3n para reducir la latencia de usuarios finales basados en internet distribuidos en todo el mundo."},{"id":"q795","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio o recurso de AWS es sin servidor?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda","B":"Instancias de Amazon EC2","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor que ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin requerir que se aprovisione, administre ni escale ninguna infraestructura de servidores subyacente.\\nLas instancias de Amazon EC2 son servidores virtuales que requieren seleccionar tipos de instancia, administrar la capacidad y mantener el sistema operativo subyacente. Amazon Lightsail proporciona servidores privados virtuales con c\xf3mputo, almacenamiento y redes preconfigurados, lo que lo convierte en un servicio basado en servidores orientado a cargas de trabajo m\xe1s simples. Amazon ElastiCache es un servicio de cach\xe9 en memoria administrado que se ejecuta en infraestructura basada en nodos dentro de una VPC, no es un servicio sin servidor."},{"id":"q804","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS proporcionan al usuario conectividad entre la nube de AWS y los recursos locales?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"Amazon Cognito","D":"AWS Direct Connect","E":"AWS Managed Services"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS VPN crea un t\xfanel cifrado sobre la internet p\xfablica entre una red local y una VPC de AWS, y AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red f\xedsica privada dedicada desde un centro de datos local hacia AWS. Ambos proporcionan conectividad entre recursos locales y la nube de AWS.\\nAmazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube que habilita operaciones de servicio al cliente mediante canales de voz y chat, y es una herramienta de comunicaciones empresariales en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de conectividad de red para vincular infraestructura local con AWS. Amazon Cognito proporciona servicios de identidad y autenticaci\xf3n de usuarios para aplicaciones web y m\xf3viles, administrando el registro, el inicio de sesi\xf3n y el control de acceso de los usuarios, y es un servicio de identidad en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de conectividad de red. AWS Managed Services proporciona administraci\xf3n continua de la infraestructura de AWS para reducir la sobrecarga operativa, y es una oferta de administraci\xf3n operativa en lugar de un servicio de conectividad de red entre entornos locales y la nube de AWS."},{"id":"q807","question":"Una empresa quiere migrar una base de datos MySQL a AWS, pero no tiene presupuesto para administradores de bases de datos que se encarguen de tareas rutinarias como aprovisionamiento, aplicaci\xf3n de parches y realizaci\xf3n de copias de seguridad. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS admitir\xe1 este caso de uso?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon DocumentDB","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos administrado compatible con MySQL que se encarga de tareas rutinarias como aprovisionamiento, aplicaci\xf3n de parches, copias de seguridad y recuperaci\xf3n, lo que elimina la necesidad de administradores de bases de datos dedicados para gestionar estas responsabilidades operativas.\\nAmazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL de clave-valor y documentos totalmente administrada, y aunque elimina la sobrecarga de DBA, no admite MySQL ni modelos de datos relacionales, por lo que no es adecuada como destino de migraci\xf3n directo para una base de datos MySQL. Amazon DocumentDB es un servicio de base de datos de documentos administrado compatible con MongoDB, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo de documentos JSON en lugar de migraciones de bases de datos relacionales MySQL. Amazon ElastiCache es un servicio de cach\xe9 en memoria administrado dise\xf1ado para acelerar el rendimiento de las aplicaciones almacenando datos de acceso frecuente en memoria, y no es un servicio de base de datos relacional capaz de ejecutar o reemplazar una base de datos MySQL."},{"id":"q813","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS es adecuado para una carga de trabajo basada en eventos?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"AWS Fargate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Lambda est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo basadas en eventos, ejecutando c\xf3digo autom\xe1ticamente en respuesta a eventos de servicios como S3, DynamoDB, API Gateway y CloudWatch sin aprovisionar servidores.\\nAmazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales que los clientes aprovisionan y administran de forma continua, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo que requieren instancias en ejecuci\xf3n persistente en lugar de c\xf3digo que se ejecuta solo en respuesta a eventos. AWS Elastic Beanstalk implementa y administra aplicaciones en infraestructura en ejecuci\xf3n continua, gestionando el escalado y el aprovisionamiento autom\xe1ticamente, pero no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la ejecuci\xf3n basada en eventos de funciones individuales. AWS Fargate ejecuta cargas de trabajo en contenedores sin administrar los servidores subyacentes, pero los contenedores se ejecutan como tareas persistentes en lugar de ejecutarse en respuesta a eventos individuales como lo hacen las funciones de Lambda."},{"id":"q814","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una caracter\xedstica de la replicaci\xf3n entre regiones de Amazon S3?","options":{"A":"El versionado debe estar deshabilitado tanto en los buckets de S3 de origen como de destino","B":"Los buckets de S3 de origen y destino no pueden estar en diferentes Regiones de AWS","C":"Los buckets de S3 configurados para replicaci\xf3n entre regiones pueden ser propiedad de una sola cuenta de AWS o de cuentas diferentes","D":"El propietario del bucket de S3 de origen debe tener las Regiones de AWS de origen y destino deshabilitadas para su cuenta"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La replicaci\xf3n entre regiones de S3 admite buckets que son propiedad de una sola cuenta de AWS o de cuentas diferentes, lo que habilita escenarios de replicaci\xf3n entre cuentas para compartir datos y cumplimiento.\\nLa replicaci\xf3n entre regiones requiere que el versionado est\xe9 habilitado tanto en los buckets de origen como de destino, no deshabilitado, lo que hace que esta opci\xf3n sea incorrecta desde el punto de vista factual. La replicaci\xf3n entre regiones est\xe1 dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para replicar objetos entre buckets en diferentes Regiones de AWS, por lo que los buckets de origen y destino deben estar en Regiones diferentes por definici\xf3n. No es necesario deshabilitar las Regiones de AWS para ninguna de las cuentas involucradas en una configuraci\xf3n de replicaci\xf3n entre regiones, ya que la replicaci\xf3n funciona entre cualquier Regi\xf3n habilitada sin ninguna restricci\xf3n de ese tipo."},{"id":"q818","question":"Amazon Route 53 permite a los usuarios:","options":{"A":"Cifrar datos en tr\xe1nsito","B":"Registrar nombres de dominio DNS","C":"Generar y administrar certificados SSL","D":"Establecer una conexi\xf3n de red dedicada a AWS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS que permite a los usuarios registrar nombres de dominio, administrar registros DNS y enrutar el tr\xe1fico de internet a recursos de AWS o endpoints externos.\\nEl cifrado de datos en tr\xe1nsito lo manejan servicios de AWS como AWS Certificate Manager y AWS KMS, no Route 53, que administra el enrutamiento DNS en lugar de operaciones criptogr\xe1ficas. La generaci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de certificados SSL es funci\xf3n de AWS Certificate Manager, que aprovisiona y renueva certificados para su uso con recursos de AWS, no una capacidad de Route 53. El establecimiento de una conexi\xf3n de red dedicada a AWS es funci\xf3n de AWS Direct Connect, que proporciona un enlace f\xedsico privado entre la infraestructura local y AWS, no un servicio de DNS ni de registro de dominios."},{"id":"q823","question":"Los usuarios informan latencia al conectarse a un sitio web con una base de clientes global. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS mejorar\xe1 la experiencia del cliente al reducir la latencia?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","D":"AWS Transit Gateway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront almacena en cach\xe9 contenido en ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo, entreg\xe1ndolo desde la ubicaci\xf3n m\xe1s cercana a cada usuario, lo que reduce significativamente la latencia para un sitio web con una base de clientes global.\\nAWS Direct Connect proporciona un enlace de red privado dedicado entre la infraestructura local y AWS, lo que mejora la conectividad para redes empresariales, pero no reduce la latencia para usuarios de internet p\xfablicos que acceden a un sitio web. Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling ajusta la cantidad de instancias seg\xfan la demanda para mantener el rendimiento y la disponibilidad, pero no reduce la distancia geogr\xe1fica entre el servidor y los usuarios globales. AWS Transit Gateway conecta VPC y redes locales a trav\xe9s de un concentrador central, y es un servicio de enrutamiento de red sin capacidad de entrega de contenido ni de optimizaci\xf3n de latencia para usuarios finales."},{"id":"q826","question":"Una empresa quiere usar un servicio de AWS para monitorear el estado de los endpoints de la aplicaci\xf3n, con la capacidad de enrutar el tr\xe1fico a endpoints regionales en buen estado para mejorar la disponibilidad de la aplicaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio admitir\xe1 estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS Global Accelerator","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator monitorea continuamente el estado de los endpoints de la aplicaci\xf3n y enruta autom\xe1ticamente el tr\xe1fico entrante de los usuarios al endpoint en buen estado m\xe1s cercano a trav\xe9s de la red global de AWS, lo que mejora la disponibilidad y permite la conmutaci\xf3n por error entre Regiones sin cambios de DNS.\\nAmazon Inspector es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad automatizada que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencional, y no monitorea el estado de los endpoints ni enruta el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones. Amazon CloudWatch recopila y monitorea m\xe9tricas, registros y eventos de servicios y aplicaciones de AWS, y aunque puede detectar y alertar sobre problemas de estado de los endpoints, no enruta por s\xed mismo el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones a endpoints en buen estado. Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 contenido en ubicaciones de borde y mejora el rendimiento para la entrega de contenido est\xe1tico y din\xe1mico, pero no realiza monitoreo de estado de endpoints a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n ni enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico entre Regiones de la manera en que lo hace Global Accelerator."},{"id":"q830","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS se pueden utilizar para mover datos desde centros de datos locales hacia AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Snowball","B":"AWS Lambda","C":"AWS ElastiCache","D":"AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS)","E":"Amazon API Gateway"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Snowball transporta f\xedsicamente grandes vol\xfamenes de datos a AWS mediante dispositivos de hardware seguros, y AWS DMS migra bases de datos desde entornos locales u otras fuentes a AWS con un tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo. Ambos est\xe1n dise\xf1ados espec\xedficamente para mover datos desde entornos locales a AWS.\\nAWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidores para ejecutar c\xf3digo basado en eventos y no tiene ninguna funci\xf3n en la migraci\xf3n de datos ni en el transporte f\xedsico de datos. AWS ElastiCache es un servicio de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria para mejorar el rendimiento de las aplicaciones y no es una herramienta de migraci\xf3n de datos. Amazon API Gateway es un servicio para crear y administrar API que exponen servicios de backend a trav\xe9s de HTTP y no tiene capacidad para migrar ni transportar datos desde entornos locales a AWS."},{"id":"q834","question":"Una empresa de comercio electr\xf3nico prev\xe9 un gran aumento del tr\xe1fico web para dos festividades de compras muy populares que se acercan. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS se puede configurar para ajustar recursos de forma din\xe1mica y satisfacer este cambio en la demanda?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudTrail","B":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","C":"Amazon Forecast","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling ajusta din\xe1micamente el n\xfamero de instancias de EC2 seg\xfan la demanda, escalando horizontalmente de forma autom\xe1tica para manejar picos de tr\xe1fico y reduciendo la escala cuando la demanda disminuye, lo que lo convierte en la soluci\xf3n dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para satisfacer requisitos variables de carga de trabajo.\\nAWS CloudTrail registra las llamadas a la API y la actividad de la cuenta en un entorno de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no tiene capacidad para ajustar ni aprovisionar recursos en respuesta a cambios en la demanda. Amazon Forecast es un servicio de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico que genera predicciones de series temporales, como pron\xf3sticos de demanda o inventario, y aunque puede predecir patrones de tr\xe1fico futuros, no puede por s\xed mismo tomar medidas para ajustar recursos de infraestructura. AWS Config realiza un seguimiento y registra continuamente la configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS con fines de cumplimiento y gesti\xf3n de cambios, y no tiene capacidad para escalar recursos din\xe1micamente en respuesta a cambios de tr\xe1fico o demanda."},{"id":"q835","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a los usuarios conectarse de forma segura a los recursos de AWS a trav\xe9s de la internet p\xfablica?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC peering","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS VPN","D":"Amazon Pinpoint"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS VPN crea un t\xfanel cifrado a trav\xe9s de la internet p\xfablica para conectar de forma segura redes locales o usuarios remotos con los recursos de AWS, lo que proporciona conectividad segura sin necesidad de una l\xednea f\xedsica dedicada.\\nAmazon VPC peering conecta dos VPC de forma privada dentro de la red de AWS, lo que les permite comunicarse entre s\xed, pero no proporciona conectividad desde fuera de AWS a trav\xe9s de la internet p\xfablica. AWS Direct Connect proporciona un enlace de red privado dedicado entre la infraestructura local y AWS que evita por completo la internet p\xfablica, lo que lo convierte en una opci\xf3n de conectividad privada en lugar de basada en internet. Amazon Pinpoint es un servicio de interacci\xf3n con el cliente que se utiliza para enviar mensajes de marketing y notificaciones segmentados, y no tiene relaci\xf3n alguna con la conectividad de red."},{"id":"q837","question":"Una empresa ejecuta una aplicaci\xf3n de comercio electr\xf3nico alojada en Europa. Para disminuir la latencia de los usuarios que acceden al sitio web desde otras partes del mundo, la empresa desea almacenar en cach\xe9 contenido est\xe1tico de acceso frecuente m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS admitir\xe1 estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Amazon ElastiCache","B":"Amazon CloudFront","C":"Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS)","D":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront almacena en cach\xe9 contenido est\xe1tico de acceso frecuente en ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo, y lo sirve desde la ubicaci\xf3n m\xe1s cercana a cada usuario para reducir la latencia de una audiencia global.\\nAmazon ElastiCache es un servicio de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria que reduce la latencia de las consultas a la base de datos dentro de una aplicaci\xf3n, pero no almacena en cach\xe9 ni distribuye contenido a usuarios dispersos geogr\xe1ficamente en ubicaciones de borde. Amazon Elastic File System proporciona un sistema de archivos compartido para instancias de EC2 dentro de AWS y no almacena en cach\xe9 ni distribuye contenido a usuarios dispersos geogr\xe1ficamente. Amazon Elastic Block Store proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque que se adjuntan a instancias de EC2 individuales y no tiene capacidad para almacenar en cach\xe9 ni entregar contenido a usuarios en diferentes ubicaciones geogr\xe1ficas."},{"id":"q838","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ayudar\xe1 a los usuarios a determinar si una aplicaci\xf3n que se ejecuta en una instancia de Amazon EC2 tiene capacidad de CPU suficiente?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch monitorea las m\xe9tricas de utilizaci\xf3n de CPU de EC2 y puede mostrar paneles que indican si una instancia tiene capacidad de CPU suficiente para su carga de trabajo, y activa alarmas cuando se superan los umbrales definidos.\\nAWS Config realiza un seguimiento y registra continuamente la configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento y detectar cambios de configuraci\xf3n, y no monitorea m\xe9tricas de rendimiento en tiempo real, como la utilizaci\xf3n de CPU. AWS CloudTrail registra las llamadas a la API y la actividad de la cuenta en un entorno de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no tiene capacidad para monitorear m\xe9tricas de rendimiento a nivel de instancia. Amazon Inspector es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad automatizado que escanea instancias de EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y es una herramienta de seguridad en lugar de un servicio de monitoreo de rendimiento."},{"id":"q842","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n se ejecuta en varias instancias de Amazon EC2 que acceden simult\xe1neamente a un sistema de archivos compartido. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de almacenamiento de AWS se debe utilizar?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon EFS","C":"Amazon S3","D":"AWS Artifact"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EFS proporciona un sistema de archivos NFS compartido totalmente administrado que varias instancias de EC2 pueden montar y al que pueden acceder simult\xe1neamente, admitiendo operaciones de lectura y escritura simult\xe1neas entre instancias.\\nLos vol\xfamenes de Amazon EBS solo se pueden adjuntar a una \xfanica instancia de EC2 a la vez en la mayor\xeda de las configuraciones, lo que los hace no aptos para acceso compartido simult\xe1neo entre varias instancias. Amazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos accesible mediante llamadas a la API, no un sistema de archivos montable adecuado para aplicaciones que requieren acceso compartido y simult\xe1neo al sistema de archivos. AWS Artifact es un portal de autoservicio para acceder a informes y acuerdos de cumplimiento de AWS, sin relaci\xf3n alguna con el almacenamiento."},{"id":"q850","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS se pueden escalar mediante AWS Auto Scaling?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon S3","D":"Amazon Route 53","E":"Amazon Redshift"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Las instancias de Amazon EC2 se pueden escalar mediante EC2 Auto Scaling, que ajusta autom\xe1ticamente el n\xfamero de instancias seg\xfan la demanda, y las tablas de Amazon DynamoDB se pueden escalar mediante Application Auto Scaling para ajustar autom\xe1ticamente la capacidad de lectura y escritura en respuesta a los patrones de tr\xe1fico. Ambos se integran directamente con AWS Auto Scaling.\\nAmazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos que escala autom\xe1ticamente para acomodar cualquier cantidad de datos sin necesidad de configuraci\xf3n, y no usa ni requiere AWS Auto Scaling para administrar su capacidad. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico que administra la resoluci\xf3n de nombres de dominio y las pol\xedticas de tr\xe1fico, y no tiene capacidad de c\xf3mputo ni de base de datos que se administre mediante AWS Auto Scaling. Amazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos administrado que tiene capacidades integradas propias de escalado de concurrencia y redimensionamiento, y no se integra con AWS Auto Scaling de la forma en que lo hacen EC2 y DynamoDB."},{"id":"q851","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son beneficios de AWS Global Accelerator?","options":{"A":"Menor costo para ejecutar servicios en AWS","B":"Mejor disponibilidad de las aplicaciones implementadas en AWS","C":"Mayor durabilidad de los datos almacenados en AWS","D":"Menor latencia para llegar a las aplicaciones implementadas en AWS","E":"Mayor seguridad de los datos almacenados en AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator mejora la disponibilidad de las aplicaciones al enrutar el tr\xe1fico a puntos de conexi\xf3n saludables en las regiones de AWS, y disminuye la latencia al dirigir a los usuarios al punto de conexi\xf3n \xf3ptimo a trav\xe9s de la red global de AWS en lugar de la internet p\xfablica.\\nLa reducci\xf3n del costo para ejecutar servicios en AWS no es un beneficio de AWS Global Accelerator, que es un servicio de rendimiento y disponibilidad de red que no reduce el costo subyacente de ejecutar servicios de c\xf3mputo, almacenamiento o bases de datos en AWS. La mayor durabilidad de los datos almacenados en AWS es una caracter\xedstica de servicios de almacenamiento como Amazon S3, que est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para una durabilidad del 99,999999999 por ciento, y no tiene relaci\xf3n alguna con las capacidades de enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico de red de AWS Global Accelerator. La mayor seguridad de los datos almacenados en AWS es funci\xf3n de servicios de cifrado, control de acceso y cumplimiento, en lugar de un servicio de enrutamiento de red, y AWS Global Accelerator no proporciona capacidades de almacenamiento de datos ni de seguridad de datos."},{"id":"q860","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para convertir texto en voz realista?","options":{"A":"Amazon Polly","B":"Amazon Transcribe","C":"Amazon Rekognition","D":"Amazon Lex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Polly es un servicio de texto a voz que utiliza aprendizaje profundo avanzado para convertir texto en voz de sonido natural en m\xfaltiples idiomas y voces.\\nAmazon Transcribe es un servicio de voz a texto que convierte grabaciones de audio en texto escrito, lo cual es la funci\xf3n inversa de convertir texto en voz. Amazon Rekognition es un servicio de visi\xf3n por computadora que analiza im\xe1genes y videos para detectar objetos, rostros y escenas, y no tiene capacidad para generar ni procesar voz. Amazon Lex es un servicio para crear interfaces conversacionales y chatbots usando voz y texto, pero procesa y comprende la voz como entrada en lugar de convertir texto escrito en salida de audio hablado."},{"id":"q864","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los elementos de la infraestructura global de AWS se utiliza para almacenar en cach\xe9 copias de contenido para una entrega m\xe1s r\xe1pida a los usuarios en todo el mundo?","options":{"A":"AWS Regions","B":"Availability Zones","C":"Edge locations","D":"Data centers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las edge locations son puntos de presencia en la infraestructura global de AWS utilizados por CloudFront y otros servicios para almacenar en cach\xe9 copias de contenido cerca de los usuarios finales para una entrega m\xe1s r\xe1pida en todo el mundo.\\nAWS Regions son \xe1reas geogr\xe1ficas que contienen m\xfaltiples Availability Zones donde los clientes implementan sus cargas de trabajo, y no est\xe1n dise\xf1adas para el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de contenido ni para la entrega de baja latencia a los usuarios finales. Las Availability Zones son cl\xfasteres de centros de datos aislados dentro de una Region utilizados para redundancia y alta disponibilidad, no para almacenar contenido en cach\xe9 en el edge. Los data centers son las instalaciones f\xedsicas que albergan la infraestructura de AWS y no son un nivel de infraestructura distinto con el que los clientes interact\xfaen directamente para fines de entrega de contenido."},{"id":"q866","question":"Un usuario necesita implementar r\xe1pidamente una base de datos no relacional en AWS. El usuario no desea administrar el hardware subyacente ni el software de la base de datos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para lograr esto?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon DynamoDB","C":"Amazon Aurora","D":"Amazon Redshift"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL no relacional totalmente administrada que no requiere aprovisionamiento de hardware, instalaci\xf3n de software ni administraci\xf3n de bases de datos, y se escala autom\xe1ticamente para manejar cualquier carga de trabajo.\\nAmazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado que admite motores como MySQL, PostgreSQL y SQL Server, y no admite modelos de datos no relacionales. Amazon Aurora es una base de datos relacional totalmente administrada compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL, y tambi\xe9n es un servicio de base de datos relacional en lugar de no relacional. Amazon Redshift es un servicio de data warehouse administrado optimizado para ejecutar consultas SQL anal\xedticas sobre grandes conjuntos de datos, y es un servicio relacional sin capacidad no relacional."},{"id":"q872","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS se pueden utilizar para proporcionar conectividad de red entre una red on-premises y una VPC?","options":{"A":"Amazon Route 53","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS Data Pipeline","D":"AWS VPN","E":"Amazon Connect"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n f\xedsica privada dedicada entre una red on-premises y una VPC, y AWS VPN crea un t\xfanel cifrado sobre el internet p\xfablico entre una red on-premises y una VPC. Ambos son soluciones de conectividad de red dise\xf1adas espec\xedficamente para ese fin.\\nAmazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de tr\xe1fico que traduce nombres de dominio a direcciones IP y gestiona el flujo de tr\xe1fico, y no establece conectividad de red entre la infraestructura on-premises y una VPC. AWS Data Pipeline (obsoleto) es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo para automatizar el movimiento y la transformaci\xf3n de datos entre servicios de AWS y fuentes on-premises, y es una herramienta de procesamiento de datos sin capacidad de conectividad de red. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto en la nube para gestionar las comunicaciones con los clientes mediante voz y chat, y no est\xe1 relacionado en absoluto con la conectividad de red."},{"id":"q876","question":"Una empresa tiene una aplicaci\xf3n con usuarios tanto en Australia como en Brasil. Toda la infraestructura de la empresa est\xe1 actualmente aprovisionada en la Region Asia Pacific (Sydney) en Australia, y los usuarios brasile\xf1os experimentan una latencia alta. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer la empresa para reducir la latencia?","options":{"A":"Implementar AWS Direct Connect para los usuarios en Brasil","B":"Aprovisionar recursos en la Region South America (S\xe3o Paulo) en Brasil","C":"Usar AWS Transit Gateway para enrutar r\xe1pidamente a los usuarios de Brasil hacia la aplicaci\xf3n","D":"Lanzar instancias Amazon EC2 adicionales en Sydney para manejar la demanda"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aprovisionar recursos en la Region South America (S\xe3o Paulo) coloca la infraestructura geogr\xe1ficamente cerca de los usuarios brasile\xf1os, lo que reduce directamente la distancia de red y la latencia en comparaci\xf3n con enrutar todo el tr\xe1fico a Sydney.\\nAWS Direct Connect proporciona un enlace de red privado dedicado entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS, pero no reduce la latencia para los usuarios finales que acceden a una aplicaci\xf3n alojada en la nube a trav\xe9s de internet. AWS Transit Gateway conecta VPC y redes on-premises a trav\xe9s de un concentrador central, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para reducir la latencia geogr\xe1fica de los usuarios finales que acceden a aplicaciones desde ubicaciones distantes. Lanzar instancias EC2 adicionales en Sydney aumenta la capacidad en Australia, pero no reduce la distancia f\xedsica entre la aplicaci\xf3n y los usuarios brasile\xf1os, por lo que la latencia seguir\xeda sin cambios."},{"id":"q879","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido de AWS permite que las aplicaciones on-premises de un usuario utilicen sin problemas el almacenamiento de AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"AWS Backup","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS Storage Gateway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Storage Gateway es un servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido que brinda a las aplicaciones on-premises acceso sin problemas al almacenamiento en la nube de AWS mediante protocolos est\xe1ndar de almacenamiento de archivos, vol\xfamenes y cintas.\\nAWS Backup es un servicio de copia de seguridad centralizado para automatizar y administrar copias de seguridad de recursos de AWS, y no proporciona a las aplicaciones on-premises acceso al almacenamiento en la nube. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube para gestionar las comunicaciones con los clientes, y no est\xe1 relacionado en absoluto con el almacenamiento ni la conectividad h\xedbrida. AWS Direct Connect proporciona un enlace de red privado dedicado entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS, pero es un servicio de red en lugar de un servicio de integraci\xf3n de almacenamiento."},{"id":"q881","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la forma m\xe1s eficiente de establecer conectividad de red desde on-premises hacia m\xfaltiples VPC en diferentes Regions de AWS?","options":{"A":"Usar AWS Direct Connect","B":"Usar AWS VPN","C":"Usar AWS Client VPN","D":"Usar un AWS Transit Gateway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Transit Gateway act\xfaa como un concentrador central que conecta m\xfaltiples VPC en diferentes Regions y redes on-premises, proporcionando la forma m\xe1s eficiente de administrar conectividad de red compleja a escala.\\nAWS Direct Connect establece un enlace privado dedicado entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS, pero no administra por s\xed mismo la conectividad entre m\xfaltiples VPC en diferentes Regions. AWS VPN crea un t\xfanel cifrado entre redes on-premises y una \xfanica VPC, y no escala de manera eficiente para conectar m\xfaltiples VPC en m\xfaltiples Regions desde una sola conexi\xf3n. AWS Client VPN es un servicio basado en endpoints que permite a dispositivos individuales acceder de forma segura a los recursos de AWS de forma remota, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para conectividad de sitio a sitio ni para m\xfaltiples VPC."},{"id":"q885","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 clase de almacenamiento de Amazon S3 est\xe1 optimizada para proporcionar acceso a datos con requisitos de resiliencia menores, pero acceso r\xe1pido cuando se necesita, como en el caso de copias de seguridad duplicadas?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3 Standard","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive","C":"Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access","D":"Amazon S3 Glacier"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access almacena datos en una \xfanica Availability Zone a un costo menor y con menor resiliencia, pero proporciona acceso r\xe1pido en milisegundos cuando se necesita, lo que la hace adecuada para copias de seguridad duplicadas que se pueden recrear.\\nAmazon S3 Standard est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para datos de acceso frecuente con alta durabilidad en m\xfaltiples AZ, lo que la hace m\xe1s costosa y sobredimensionada para datos con requisitos de resiliencia menores, como las copias de seguridad duplicadas. Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para el archivo a largo plazo de datos a los que se accede con poca frecuencia, con tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n de hasta 12 horas, lo que la hace inadecuada para cargas de trabajo que requieren acceso r\xe1pido. Amazon S3 Glacier est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para almacenamiento de archivo con tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n que van de minutos a horas, y no proporciona el acceso r\xe1pido en milisegundos requerido para el caso de uso descrito."},{"id":"q888","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a los usuarios crear copias de recursos entre Regiones de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon ElastiCache","B":"AWS CloudFormation","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"AWS Systems Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudFormation utiliza archivos de plantilla para definir infraestructura como c\xf3digo, y estas plantillas se pueden desplegar en m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS, creando copias id\xe9nticas de toda una pila de infraestructura, incluidos todos sus recursos y configuraciones, en cada Regi\xf3n de destino.\\nAmazon ElastiCache proporciona almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria para mejorar el rendimiento de las aplicaciones al reducir la carga de la base de datos, y es un servicio de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en lugar de un servicio para replicar o copiar recursos de infraestructura entre Regiones. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y eventos de administraci\xf3n con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y aunque se puede configurar para entregar registros a m\xfaltiples Regiones, no permite a los usuarios crear copias de recursos de infraestructura de AWS en otras Regiones. AWS Systems Manager proporciona capacidades de administraci\xf3n operativa para recursos de AWS, incluida la administraci\xf3n de parches, el almacenamiento de par\xe1metros y la automatizaci\xf3n, pero no proporciona un mecanismo para replicar o desplegar conjuntos completos de recursos de AWS en m\xfaltiples Regiones."},{"id":"q891","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son servicios de c\xf3mputo de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon Lightsail","B":"AWS Systems Manager","C":"AWS CloudFormation","D":"AWS Batch","E":"Amazon Inspector"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Amazon Lightsail proporciona servidores privados virtuales simplificados para desplegar cargas de trabajo de c\xf3mputo, y AWS Batch administra y ejecuta trabajos de computaci\xf3n por lotes en recursos de c\xf3mputo aprovisionados din\xe1micamente. Ambos son servicios de c\xf3mputo.\\nAWS Systems Manager es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de operaciones para automatizar tareas de configuraci\xf3n, aplicaci\xf3n de parches e inventario en instancias EC2, no un servicio de c\xf3mputo que aprovisiona o ejecuta cargas de trabajo. AWS CloudFormation es un servicio de infraestructura como c\xf3digo para aprovisionar y administrar recursos de AWS mediante plantillas, no un servicio de c\xf3mputo en s\xed mismo. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de vulnerabilidades que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de hallazgos de seguridad, no un servicio de c\xf3mputo."},{"id":"q900","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS permite a un usuario escalar f\xe1cilmente la conectividad entre miles de VPC?","options":{"A":"VPC peering","B":"AWS Transit Gateway","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"AWS Global Accelerator"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Transit Gateway act\xfaa como un concentrador central que conecta miles de VPC y redes locales a trav\xe9s de una \xfanica puerta de enlace, escalando la conectividad de manera mucho m\xe1s eficiente que administrar conexiones individuales entre cada red.\\nVPC peering crea una conexi\xf3n directa entre dos VPC, pero no escala a miles de VPC, ya que cada par requiere su propia conexi\xf3n de peering y el peering no es transitivo. AWS Direct Connect proporciona un circuito privado dedicado entre un centro de datos local y AWS, pero no conecta VPC entre s\xed. AWS Global Accelerator mejora la disponibilidad y el rendimiento de las aplicaciones para usuarios globales al enrutar el tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de la red global de AWS, pero no proporciona conectividad entre VPC."},{"id":"q899","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 se puede utilizar para automatizar y gestionar entornos de AWS multi-cuenta seguros y bien arquitecturados?","options":{"A":"Modelo de responsabilidad compartida de AWS","B":"AWS Control Tower","C":"AWS Security Hub","D":"AWS Well-Architected Tool"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Control Tower automatiza la configuraci\xf3n de un entorno de AWS multi-cuenta seguro mediante landing zones y guardrails que aplican pol\xedticas de gobernanza, lo que lo convierte en el servicio para automatizar y gestionar entornos multi-cuenta seguros y bien arquitecturados.\\nAWS Config monitorea y registra las configuraciones de los recursos seg\xfan las reglas de cumplimiento dentro de las cuentas y, aunque admite la gobernanza, es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de cumplimiento por cuenta en lugar de un servicio que automatiza el aprovisionamiento y la gesti\xf3n de entornos multi-cuenta completos. AWS Artifact proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes y certificaciones de cumplimiento de AWS, y es un portal de documentaci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio que automatiza la creaci\xf3n o gesti\xf3n de entornos de AWS multi-cuenta. AWS CloudFormation es un servicio de infraestructura como c\xf3digo para aprovisionar recursos de AWS mediante plantillas y, aunque se puede utilizar para configurar entornos, requiere el desarrollo de plantillas personalizadas y no proporciona la gobernanza multi-cuenta y los guardrails listos para usar que ofrece Control Tower."},{"id":"q905","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de contenedores de AWS ayudar\xe1 a un usuario a instalar, operar y escalar la infraestructura de administraci\xf3n de cl\xfasteres?","options":{"A":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR)","B":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","C":"Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS)","D":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon ECS es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores totalmente administrado que se encarga de instalar, operar y escalar la infraestructura de administraci\xf3n de cl\xfasteres para ejecutar contenedores Docker, eliminando la necesidad de administrar su propio software de orquestaci\xf3n.\\nAmazon ECR es un registro de contenedores totalmente administrado para almacenar, administrar y desplegar im\xe1genes de contenedores, no un servicio para ejecutar u orquestar contenedores. AWS Elastic Beanstalk es una plataforma como servicio para desplegar aplicaciones y servicios web, no una soluci\xf3n dedicada de administraci\xf3n de cl\xfasteres de contenedores. Amazon EBS es un servicio de almacenamiento de bloques para adjuntar vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento persistente a instancias EC2, no est\xe1 relacionado con la orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores."},{"id":"q908","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el prop\xf3sito de tener un internet gateway dentro de una VPC?","options":{"A":"Para crear una conexi\xf3n VPN a la VPC","B":"Para permitir la comunicaci\xf3n entre la VPC e Internet","C":"Para imponer restricciones de ancho de banda al tr\xe1fico de Internet","D":"Para balancear la carga del tr\xe1fico de Internet entre instancias Amazon EC2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un internet gateway permite la comunicaci\xf3n entre los recursos de una VPC e Internet p\xfablico, lo que permite que las instancias en subredes p\xfablicas env\xeden y reciban tr\xe1fico de Internet.\\nCrear una conexi\xf3n VPN a la VPC es funci\xf3n de un Virtual Private Gateway, no de un internet gateway, ya que las conexiones VPN se utilizan para enlazar una VPC con una red local. Imponer restricciones de ancho de banda al tr\xe1fico de Internet no es funci\xf3n de ning\xfan componente de puerta de enlace de VPC, ya que la administraci\xf3n del ancho de banda se maneja a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n o de pol\xedtica de red. Balancear la carga del tr\xe1fico de Internet entre instancias EC2 es funci\xf3n de un Elastic Load Balancer, no de un internet gateway."},{"id":"q909","question":"Una empresa debe asegurarse de que su endpoint para una instancia de base de datos siga siendo el mismo despu\xe9s de una interrupci\xf3n del servicio en una sola Zona de Disponibilidad. La aplicaci\xf3n necesita reanudar las operaciones de base de datos sin necesidad de intervenci\xf3n administrativa manual. \xbfC\xf3mo se pueden cumplir estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Usar m\xfaltiples rutas de Amazon Route 53 hacia el endpoint de la instancia de base de datos en espera alojado en AWS Storage Gateway","B":"Configurar despliegues de Amazon RDS Multi-Availability Zone con failover autom\xe1tico hacia la r\xe9plica en espera","C":"Agregar m\xfaltiples Application Load Balancers y desplegar la instancia de base de datos con AWS Elastic Beanstalk","D":"Desplegar un \xfanico Network Load Balancer para distribuir el tr\xe1fico entrante entre m\xfaltiples or\xedgenes de Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon RDS Multi-AZ mantiene una r\xe9plica en espera s\xedncrona con el mismo endpoint DNS en una Zona de Disponibilidad diferente, por lo que cuando ocurre una falla, el endpoint se resuelve autom\xe1ticamente hacia la r\xe9plica en espera sin ning\xfan cambio en la cadena de conexi\xf3n de la aplicaci\xf3n ni intervenci\xf3n manual.\\nUsar m\xfaltiples rutas de Route 53 hacia una base de datos en espera alojada en AWS Storage Gateway no es un enfoque viable; Storage Gateway es un servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido en lugar de una soluci\xf3n de alojamiento de bases de datos, y esta configuraci\xf3n no mantendr\xeda el mismo endpoint ni proporcionar\xeda failover automatizado. Agregar m\xfaltiples Application Load Balancers y desplegar la base de datos con Elastic Beanstalk no abordar\xeda la alta disponibilidad de la base de datos; Beanstalk administra el despliegue de aplicaciones y los ALB enrutan el tr\xe1fico web, pero ninguno proporciona el failover a nivel de base de datos con persistencia del endpoint que especifica el requisito. Desplegar un Network Load Balancer entre or\xedgenes de CloudFront no cumplir\xeda este requisito; NLB distribuye tr\xe1fico de red a los destinos y CloudFront sirve contenido web desde ubicaciones de borde, ninguno de los cuales aplica para mantener un endpoint de instancia de base de datos consistente con failover autom\xe1tico entre AZ."},{"id":"q910","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio administrado de AWS se puede utilizar para distribuir tr\xe1fico entre una o m\xe1s instancias Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"NAT gateway","B":"Elastic Load Balancing","C":"Amazon Athena","D":"AWS PrivateLink"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Elastic Load Balancing distribuye autom\xe1ticamente el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones entrante entre m\xfaltiples instancias EC2, mejorando la disponibilidad y la tolerancia a fallos al garantizar que ninguna instancia individual se vea sobrecargada.\\nUn NAT gateway permite que las instancias en una subred privada inicien tr\xe1fico de Internet saliente mientras se impiden las conexiones entrantes, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en la distribuci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico entre instancias. Amazon Athena es un servicio de consultas interactivas para analizar datos almacenados en S3 mediante SQL, completamente no relacionado con la distribuci\xf3n de tr\xe1fico. AWS PrivateLink proporciona conectividad privada entre VPC y servicios de AWS sin exponer el tr\xe1fico a Internet p\xfablico, pero no distribuye tr\xe1fico entre instancias de c\xf3mputo."},{"id":"q915","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar en el proceso de despliegue de aplicaciones?","options":{"A":"AWS AppSync","B":"AWS Batch","C":"AWS CodePipeline","D":"AWS DataSync"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS CodePipeline es un servicio de entrega continua que automatiza las fases de compilaci\xf3n, prueba y despliegue del proceso de despliegue de aplicaciones siempre que se realizan cambios en el c\xf3digo.\\nAWS AppSync es un servicio GraphQL gestionado que permite a las aplicaciones recuperar y manipular datos de m\xfaltiples fuentes a trav\xe9s de una \xfanica API, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en la automatizaci\xf3n de pipelines de despliegue de aplicaciones. AWS Batch es un servicio totalmente gestionado para ejecutar trabajos de computaci\xf3n por lotes a gran escala, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo de procesamiento de datos en lugar de la automatizaci\xf3n del despliegue de aplicaciones. AWS DataSync es un servicio de transferencia de datos que automatiza el movimiento de datos entre el almacenamiento on-premises y AWS, y no tiene capacidad de despliegue de aplicaciones ni de entrega continua."},{"id":"q918","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS permite al usuario gestionar el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones entre regiones?","options":{"A":"Amazon AppStream 2.0","B":"Amazon VPC","C":"Elastic Load Balancer","D":"Amazon Route 53"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Route 53 admite m\xfaltiples pol\xedticas de enrutamiento, incluidas el enrutamiento basado en latencia, el enrutamiento por geolocalizaci\xf3n y el enrutamiento por conmutaci\xf3n por error, que dirigen el tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones a trav\xe9s de m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS, lo que permite la gesti\xf3n global del tr\xe1fico con comprobaciones de estado y conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica.\\nAmazon AppStream 2.0 es un servicio gestionado de streaming de aplicaciones y escritorios que entrega aplicaciones de escritorio de forma segura a trav\xe9s de un navegador, y es un servicio de computaci\xf3n de usuario final en lugar de una herramienta de red o gesti\xf3n de tr\xe1fico para tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones entre Regiones. Amazon VPC proporciona redes aisladas dentro de una Regi\xf3n de AWS, incluidos subredes, tablas de rutas, grupos de seguridad y gateways, pero VPC es una construcci\xf3n regional y no gestiona el enrutamiento del tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones entre m\xfaltiples Regiones. Elastic Load Balancer distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre m\xfaltiples destinos dentro de una \xfanica Regi\xf3n de AWS, y aunque admite distribuci\xf3n multi-AZ dentro de una Regi\xf3n, no enruta tr\xe1fico entre m\xfaltiples Regiones de AWS diferentes."},{"id":"q921","question":"Una empresa debe almacenar datos cr\xedticos de negocio en Amazon S3 con una copia de seguridad en otra Regi\xf3n de AWS. \xbfC\xf3mo se puede lograr esto?","options":{"A":"Usar una red de entrega de contenido (CDN) de Amazon CloudFront para almacenar datos en cach\xe9 de forma global","B":"Configurar la replicaci\xf3n entre regiones de Amazon S3 a otra Regi\xf3n de AWS","C":"Configurar el servicio AWS Backup para respaldar los datos en otra Regi\xf3n de AWS","D":"Tomar instant\xe1neas de buckets de Amazon S3 y copiar esos datos a otra Regi\xf3n de AWS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La replicaci\xf3n entre regiones de Amazon S3 copia autom\xe1ticamente los objetos cargados en un bucket de origen en una Regi\xf3n a un bucket de destino en otra Regi\xf3n, lo que garantiza que exista una copia de seguridad de los datos cr\xedticos en una ubicaci\xf3n geogr\xe1ficamente separada.\\nUsar Amazon CloudFront para almacenar datos en cach\xe9 de forma global distribuye contenido a ubicaciones de borde para un acceso m\xe1s r\xe1pido por parte de los usuarios finales, pero el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de CDN no es un mecanismo de copia de seguridad duradero; el contenido en cach\xe9 es temporal y puede ser desalojado, y CloudFront no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para almacenar copias de seguridad permanentes de datos de S3. AWS Backup puede respaldar datos de varios servicios de AWS, incluido S3, pero para la replicaci\xf3n continua entre regiones de objetos de S3, la caracter\xedstica nativa de replicaci\xf3n entre regiones de S3 es la soluci\xf3n directa y espec\xedfica. Tomar instant\xe1neas de buckets de Amazon S3 no es una caracter\xedstica nativa de S3; S3 no tiene una capacidad de instant\xe1neas nativa como EBS, y el enfoque correcto para la copia de seguridad de S3 en otra Regi\xf3n es la replicaci\xf3n entre regiones en lugar de instant\xe1neas."},{"id":"q925","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 componentes se requieren para construir una conexi\xf3n VPN de sitio a sitio exitosa en AWS?","options":{"A":"Puerta de enlace de internet","B":"Puerta de enlace NAT","C":"Puerta de enlace de cliente","D":"Puerta de enlace de tr\xe1nsito","E":"Puerta de enlace privada virtual"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Una conexi\xf3n VPN de sitio a sitio requiere una puerta de enlace de cliente que represente el dispositivo VPN on-premises y una puerta de enlace privada virtual conectada a la VPC en el lado de AWS, que juntas forman los dos extremos del t\xfanel cifrado.\\nUna puerta de enlace de internet conecta una VPC a la internet p\xfablica para tr\xe1fico entrante y saliente general, y no es un componente de una conexi\xf3n VPN de sitio a sitio. Una puerta de enlace NAT permite a los recursos de una subred privada iniciar conexiones salientes a internet mientras permanecen inaccesibles desde internet, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en el establecimiento de un t\xfanel VPN entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS. Una puerta de enlace de tr\xe1nsito act\xfaa como un concentrador central que conecta m\xfaltiples VPC y redes on-premises entre s\xed, y aunque se puede usar en arquitecturas VPN m\xe1s complejas, no es un componente requerido de una conexi\xf3n VPN de sitio a sitio b\xe1sica."},{"id":"q929","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ofrece almacenamiento persistente para un sistema de archivos?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"Amazon EC2 instance store","C":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)","D":"Amazon ElastiCache"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento en bloque persistentes que retienen datos independientemente del ciclo de vida de la instancia EC2, lo que lo hace adecuado para sistemas de archivos, bases de datos y aplicaciones que requieren almacenamiento duradero.\\nAmazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos dise\xf1ado para almacenar y recuperar archivos mediante API, y no proporciona un sistema de archivos montable para usarse como almacenamiento en bloque persistente. Amazon EC2 instance store proporciona almacenamiento en bloque temporal que est\xe1 f\xedsicamente conectado al servidor host y se pierde cuando la instancia se detiene o termina, por lo que no es persistente. Amazon ElastiCache es un servicio de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en memoria que se usa para mejorar el rendimiento de las aplicaciones, y no tiene capacidad de sistema de archivos ni de almacenamiento persistente."},{"id":"q932","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes servicios de AWS son serverless?","options":{"A":"AWS Lambda","B":"Amazon OpenSearch Service","C":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","D":"Amazon DynamoDB","E":"Amazon Redshift"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Lambda ejecuta c\xf3digo sin aprovisionar ni administrar servidores, y Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL totalmente gestionada que escala autom\xe1ticamente sin ning\xfan aprovisionamiento de servidores, lo que hace que ambos sean servicios totalmente serverless.\\nAmazon OpenSearch Service requiere que los clientes aprovisionen y administren cl\xfasteres de instancias, lo que lo convierte en un servicio basado en servidores en lugar de serverless. AWS Elastic Beanstalk es un servicio de plataforma que maneja autom\xe1ticamente el despliegue y el escalado, pero a\xfan aprovisiona y administra instancias EC2 por debajo, lo que lo convierte en basado en servidores. Amazon Redshift requiere que los clientes aprovisionen y administren cl\xfasteres para cargas de trabajo de almacenamiento de datos, y no es un servicio serverless de forma predeterminada."},{"id":"q933","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios gestionados de AWS se pueden usar para extender un centro de datos on-premises a la red de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"Puerta de enlace NAT","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Amazon Connect","E":"Amazon Route 53"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS VPN crea t\xfaneles cifrados sobre la internet p\xfablica para conectar redes on-premises a la red de AWS, y AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red f\xedsica privada dedicada entre un centro de datos on-premises y AWS. Ambos son los servicios gestionados est\xe1ndar para extender la infraestructura on-premises a la red de AWS.\\nUna puerta de enlace NAT permite a las instancias de una subred privada iniciar tr\xe1fico saliente hacia internet mientras evita las conexiones entrantes, y es un componente de red de VPC en lugar de un servicio para conectar centros de datos on-premises a AWS. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube para gestionar las comunicaciones con los clientes, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en la conectividad de red ni en extender la infraestructura on-premises a AWS. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS y enrutamiento de dominios que dirige a los usuarios a los endpoints de las aplicaciones, y no establece conexiones de red entre entornos on-premises y AWS."},{"id":"q938","question":"Una empresa tiene un repositorio de im\xe1genes de 500 TB que debe transportarse a AWS para su procesamiento. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede importar estos datos de la forma M\xc1S rentable?","options":{"A":"AWS Snowball","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS VPN","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Snowball puede transportar hasta 80 TB por dispositivo y es la opci\xf3n de transporte f\xedsico m\xe1s rentable para 500 TB, ya que requiere solo una peque\xf1a cantidad de dispositivos en lugar de depender de una transferencia de red costosa o que consume mucho tiempo.\\nAWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS para conectividad h\xedbrida continua, y aunque ofrece ancho de banda constante, est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para acceso de red continuo en lugar de transporte masivo de datos \xfanico de cientos de terabytes. AWS VPN crea un t\xfanel cifrado entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS a trav\xe9s de la internet p\xfablica, y es un servicio de conectividad de red no adecuado para transferir 500 TB de datos debido a los costos de tiempo y ancho de banda involucrados en mover ese volumen por internet. Amazon S3 es el servicio de almacenamiento de destino para los datos en lugar del mecanismo de transporte. Cargar 500 TB directamente a S3 por internet ser\xeda prohibitivamente lento y costoso en comparaci\xf3n con el transporte f\xedsico mediante dispositivos AWS Snowball."},{"id":"q939","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede ejecutar una base de datos PostgreSQL administrada que proporciona procesamiento de transacciones en l\xednea (OLTP)?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon Athena","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"Amazon EMR"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon RDS admite PostgreSQL como motor de base de datos administrado, encarg\xe1ndose del aprovisionamiento automatizado, la aplicaci\xf3n de parches, las copias de seguridad y la alta disponibilidad para cargas de trabajo de procesamiento de transacciones en l\xednea.\\nAmazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL de clave-valor y de documentos totalmente administrada, no es un motor de base de datos relacional y no admite PostgreSQL ni cargas de trabajo OLTP en el sentido tradicional. Amazon Athena es un servicio de consultas interactivas para analizar datos almacenados en S3 mediante SQL y no es una base de datos transaccional. Amazon EMR es un servicio administrado de procesamiento de big data para ejecutar frameworks distribuidos como Spark y Hadoop, no relacionado con la administraci\xf3n de bases de datos relacionales."},{"id":"q951","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son beneficios de usar Amazon RDS en lugar de Amazon EC2 al ejecutar bases de datos relacionales en AWS?","options":{"A":"Copias de seguridad automatizadas","B":"Administraci\xf3n del esquema","C":"Indexaci\xf3n de tablas","D":"Aplicaci\xf3n de parches de software","E":"Administraci\xf3n de extracci\xf3n, transformaci\xf3n y carga (ETL)"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Amazon RDS proporciona copias de seguridad automatizadas con recuperaci\xf3n a un punto en el tiempo y gestiona autom\xe1ticamente la aplicaci\xf3n de parches de software del motor de base de datos, liberando a los equipos de estas tareas operativas que tendr\xedan que administrarse manualmente en EC2.\\nLa administraci\xf3n del esquema es responsabilidad del cliente independientemente de si la base de datos se ejecuta en RDS o en EC2, ya que AWS no tiene visibilidad del modelo de datos ni de la l\xf3gica de negocio de la aplicaci\xf3n y no administra esquemas de bases de datos. La indexaci\xf3n de tablas tambi\xe9n es responsabilidad del cliente tanto en RDS como en EC2, ya que el dise\xf1o de \xedndices depende de los patrones de consulta de la aplicaci\xf3n y debe definirlo y mantenerlo el administrador de la base de datos. La administraci\xf3n de ETL implica extraer, transformar y cargar datos entre sistemas, lo cual se maneja mediante servicios dedicados como AWS Glue en lugar de ser una caracter\xedstica que RDS proporcione sobre EC2."},{"id":"q952","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 ofrece la clase de almacenamiento Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering?","options":{"A":"Flexibilidad de pago mediante la reserva de capacidad de almacenamiento","B":"Retenci\xf3n a largo plazo de datos mediante la copia de los datos a un volumen cifrado de Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)","C":"Ahorro autom\xe1tico en costos al mover objetos entre niveles seg\xfan los cambios en los patrones de acceso","D":"Almacenamiento seguro, duradero y de menor costo para el archivado de datos"},"correct":"C","explanation":"S3 Intelligent-Tiering supervisa autom\xe1ticamente los patrones de acceso y mueve objetos entre un nivel de acceso frecuente y un nivel de acceso poco frecuente sin impacto en el rendimiento ni sobrecarga operativa, lo que reduce los costos de almacenamiento para datos con patrones de acceso impredecibles o cambiantes.\\nLa flexibilidad de pago mediante la reserva de capacidad de almacenamiento describe un modelo de precios basado en compromiso que no existe para Amazon S3, el cual cobra seg\xfan el uso real en lugar de la capacidad reservada. La retenci\xf3n a largo plazo de datos mediante la copia a un volumen cifrado de EBS no es como funciona S3 Intelligent-Tiering. EBS es almacenamiento en bloque adjunto a instancias de EC2 y est\xe1 completamente separado de las clases de almacenamiento de S3, e Intelligent-Tiering administra datos dentro de S3 mismo. El almacenamiento seguro, duradero y de menor costo para el archivado de datos describe Amazon S3 Glacier, que es una clase de almacenamiento separada dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para datos de archivo de acceso poco frecuente con tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n que van de minutos a horas, no S3 Intelligent-Tiering."},{"id":"q953","question":"Una empresa tiene m\xfaltiples fuentes de datos en toda la organizaci\xf3n y desea consolidar los datos en un \xfanico almac\xe9n de datos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para cumplir este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon DynamoDB","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon Athena","D":"Amazon QuickSight"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Redshift es un almac\xe9n de datos en la nube totalmente administrado dise\xf1ado para consolidar datos de m\xfaltiples fuentes y ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas complejas a escala mediante almacenamiento columnar y procesamiento masivamente paralelo.\\nAmazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL de clave-valor y de documentos totalmente administrada optimizada para cargas de trabajo transaccionales de alta velocidad, no para consolidar datos de m\xfaltiples fuentes en un almac\xe9n de datos para an\xe1lisis. Amazon Athena es un servicio de consultas interactivas que analiza datos directamente en S3 mediante SQL, pero no consolida ni almacena datos en un almac\xe9n de datos. Amazon QuickSight es un servicio de inteligencia de negocios y visualizaci\xf3n de datos para crear paneles e informes, no un almac\xe9n de datos para consolidar datos."},{"id":"q960","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de almacenamiento de AWS est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para transferir petabytes de datos hacia y desde la nube?","options":{"A":"AWS Storage Gateway","B":"Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"AWS Snowball"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico de transporte de datos de AWS Snow Family dise\xf1ado para transferir petabytes de datos hacia y desde AWS Cloud, evitando transferencias de red lentas o costosas para migraciones a gran escala.\\nAWS Storage Gateway es un servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido que conecta entornos locales con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS para la integraci\xf3n continua, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la transferencia f\xedsica masiva de datos a escala de petabytes. Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive es una clase de almacenamiento de archivo de bajo costo para datos a los que se accede con poca frecuencia, y no tiene capacidad para transferir grandes vol\xfamenes de datos a AWS de forma f\xedsica o program\xe1tica. Amazon Lightsail es un servicio de c\xf3mputo simplificado para implementar servidores virtuales, contenedores y aplicaciones web con una configuraci\xf3n m\xednima, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con la transferencia o migraci\xf3n de datos a gran escala."},{"id":"q961","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio proporciona al usuario la capacidad de almacenar datos en un almac\xe9n de datos en AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Amazon EFS","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon RDS","D":"Amazon VPC"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos totalmente administrado que permite ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas complejas sobre petabytes de datos estructurados mediante SQL, lo que lo convierte en la soluci\xf3n dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para el almacenamiento de datos en un almac\xe9n de datos en AWS Cloud.\\nAmazon EFS es un sistema de archivos compartido administrado para usar con instancias de c\xf3mputo, que proporciona almacenamiento de archivos en lugar de una capacidad de almac\xe9n de datos o de consultas anal\xedticas. Amazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional administrado dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo transaccionales, no para almacenamiento de datos anal\xedtico a escala de petabytes. Amazon VPC es un servicio de red privada virtual para aislar y configurar la infraestructura de red en la nube, completamente no relacionado con el almacenamiento de datos o los almacenes de datos."},{"id":"q964","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede utilizar para proporcionar un centro de contacto basado en la nube y bajo demanda?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"AWS Support Center","D":"AWS Managed Services"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube que se puede configurar en minutos, proporcionando escalabilidad bajo demanda con precios de pago por uso para operaciones de atenci\xf3n al cliente.\\nAWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS para conectividad h\xedbrida, y es un servicio de red sin capacidad para proporcionar funcionalidad de centro de contacto para gestionar comunicaciones con clientes. AWS Support Center es el portal donde los clientes de AWS crean, ven y gestionan sus propios casos de soporte con AWS, y es una interfaz de gesti\xf3n de soporte interna en lugar de un servicio para crear centros de contacto orientados al cliente. AWS Managed Services proporciona gesti\xf3n operativa de la infraestructura de AWS en nombre de clientes empresariales, incluidos monitoreo, aplicaci\xf3n de parches y gesti\xf3n de incidentes, y es un servicio de operaciones de infraestructura en lugar de una plataforma de centro de contacto para gestionar interacciones con clientes."},{"id":"q966","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 componente debe asociarse a una VPC para habilitar el acceso entrante a Internet?","options":{"A":"Puerta de enlace NAT","B":"Endpoint de VPC","C":"Conexi\xf3n VPN","D":"Puerta de enlace de Internet"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Se debe asociar una puerta de enlace de Internet a una VPC para habilitar el acceso entrante a Internet, lo que permite que los recursos de subredes p\xfablicas reciban tr\xe1fico entrante desde Internet.\\nUna puerta de enlace NAT permite que los recursos de subredes privadas inicien conexiones salientes a Internet, pero no habilita que el tr\xe1fico entrante de Internet llegue a los recursos dentro de una VPC. Un endpoint de VPC permite conectividad privada entre una VPC y servicios de AWS compatibles sin enrutar tr\xe1fico por Internet, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en habilitar el acceso entrante a Internet. Una conexi\xf3n VPN crea un t\xfanel cifrado entre una VPC y una red local o remota a trav\xe9s de Internet p\xfablica, y no es el componente que habilita el acceso entrante general a Internet hacia una VPC."},{"id":"q968","question":"Una empresa tiene una base de datos MySQL que se ejecuta en una \xfanica instancia de Amazon EC2. Ahora la empresa requiere una mayor disponibilidad en caso de una interrupci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 conjunto de tareas cumplir\xeda este requisito?","options":{"A":"Agregar un Application Load Balancer delante de la instancia de EC2","B":"Configurar EC2 Auto Recovery para mover la instancia a otra Zona de disponibilidad","C":"Migrar a Amazon RDS y habilitar Multi-AZ","D":"Habilitar la protecci\xf3n contra terminaci\xf3n para la instancia de EC2 para evitar interrupciones"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon RDS con Multi-AZ habilitado mantiene una r\xe9plica en espera s\xedncrona en una Zona de disponibilidad separada y realiza una conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica sin intervenci\xf3n manual, lo que proporciona una alta disponibilidad que una \xfanica instancia MySQL alojada en EC2 no puede igualar.\\nAgregar un Application Load Balancer delante de una \xfanica instancia de base de datos en EC2 distribuir\xeda el tr\xe1fico, pero seguir\xeda habiendo solo una instancia de base de datos, lo que significa que una falla de esa instancia dejar\xeda fuera de servicio la base de datos independientemente del load balancer; los ALB distribuyen el tr\xe1fico entre varios destinos y no proporcionan por s\xed mismos alta disponibilidad de la base de datos. Configurar EC2 Auto Recovery mueve una instancia fallida a un nuevo hardware subyacente dentro de la misma Zona de disponibilidad si el hardware subyacente falla, pero esto no protege contra fallas a nivel de AZ e implica tiempo de inactividad durante la recuperaci\xf3n en lugar de la conmutaci\xf3n por error casi instant\xe1nea que proporciona RDS Multi-AZ. Habilitar la protecci\xf3n contra terminaci\xf3n evita la terminaci\xf3n accidental de una instancia de EC2 a trav\xe9s de la consola o la API, pero no evita que la instancia falle debido a problemas de software, problemas de almacenamiento o eventos a nivel de AZ, y no proporciona redundancia ni recuperaci\xf3n autom\xe1tica."},{"id":"q972","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando una aplicaci\xf3n que requiere la capacidad de enviar, almacenar y recibir mensajes entre los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n. La empresa tiene otro requisito: procesar los mensajes en orden primero en entrar, primero en salir (FIFO). \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar la empresa?","options":{"A":"AWS Step Functions","B":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","C":"Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","D":"Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon SQS admite colas FIFO que garantizan que los mensajes se procesen exactamente una vez en el orden exacto en que se reciben, lo que lo convierte en el servicio correcto para aplicaciones que requieren un orden estricto de mensajes primero en entrar, primero en salir con deduplicaci\xf3n.\\nAWS Step Functions es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo que coordina componentes distribuidos de aplicaciones mediante flujos de trabajo visuales y m\xe1quinas de estado, y aunque secuencia los pasos en un orden definido, no es un servicio de colas de mensajes para desacoplar componentes de aplicaciones que necesitan intercambiar mensajes en orden FIFO. Amazon SNS es un servicio de notificaciones pub/sub totalmente administrado que difunde mensajes a varios endpoints suscritos simult\xe1neamente, y no admite garant\xedas FIFO para la entrega ordenada de mensajes entre remitentes y receptores espec\xedficos. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams es un servicio de transmisi\xf3n de datos en tiempo real dise\xf1ado para procesar grandes vol\xfamenes de datos de transmisi\xf3n de varios productores, y aunque conserva el orden dentro de un shard, est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para canalizaciones de anal\xedtica de alto rendimiento en lugar de colas de mensajes de aplicaci\xf3n a aplicaci\xf3n con garant\xedas FIFO."},{"id":"q975","question":"Un usuario tiene conocimientos limitados de los servicios de AWS, pero quiere implementar r\xe1pidamente una aplicaci\xf3n Node.js escalable en la nube de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio se deber\xeda usar para implementar la aplicaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"AWS CloudFormation","B":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","C":"Amazon EC2","D":"AWS OpsWorks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk maneja autom\xe1ticamente la implementaci\xf3n, el escalado, el balanceo de carga y la supervisi\xf3n para las plataformas compatibles, incluida Node.js, lo que permite a los usuarios con conocimientos limitados de AWS poner en funcionamiento r\xe1pidamente una aplicaci\xf3n escalable sin configurar la infraestructura subyacente.\\nAWS CloudFormation es un servicio de infraestructura como c\xf3digo para aprovisionar recursos de AWS mediante plantillas, lo que requiere conocimientos significativos de AWS para usarse de manera efectiva y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para una implementaci\xf3n r\xe1pida de aplicaciones. Amazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales en bruto que requieren configuraci\xf3n manual del sistema operativo, el entorno de ejecuci\xf3n, las redes y el escalado, lo que lo hace inadecuado para usuarios con experiencia limitada en AWS que necesitan una implementaci\xf3n r\xe1pida. AWS OpsWorks (obsoleto) es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n que usa Chef y Puppet y que requiere experiencia en esas herramientas para administrar implementaciones de aplicaciones, lo que a\xf1ade complejidad en lugar de simplificarla."},{"id":"q989","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS requiere un proveedor de servicios de Internet (ISP) y una instalaci\xf3n de coubicaci\xf3n para implementarse?","options":{"A":"AWS VPN","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Internet gateway"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect requiere un ISP y una instalaci\xf3n de coubicaci\xf3n en una ubicaci\xf3n de AWS Direct Connect para establecer una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, lo que lo convierte en la \xfanica opci\xf3n aqu\xed con esos requisitos f\xedsicos previos.\\nAWS VPN establece una conexi\xf3n cifrada a trav\xe9s de Internet p\xfablica y no requiere ning\xfan acuerdo con un ISP ni una instalaci\xf3n de coubicaci\xf3n m\xe1s all\xe1 de una conexi\xf3n a Internet est\xe1ndar. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube para administrar las comunicaciones con los clientes y no tiene ninguna relaci\xf3n con la conectividad de red ni con los requisitos de infraestructura f\xedsica. Un Internet gateway es un componente de VPC que permite la comunicaci\xf3n entre los recursos de una VPC e Internet p\xfablica y no requiere ning\xfan acuerdo con un ISP ni de coubicaci\xf3n para implementarse."},{"id":"q990","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicios de AWS ofrecen capacidades de c\xf3mputo?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)","D":"Amazon Cognito","E":"AWS Lambda"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Amazon EC2 proporciona servidores virtuales para ejecutar cargas de trabajo de c\xf3mputo con control total sobre el sistema operativo y la configuraci\xf3n, y AWS Lambda ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin aprovisionar ni administrar ning\xfan servidor. Ambos son servicios de c\xf3mputo que ejecutan cargas de trabajo.\\nAmazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos para almacenar y recuperar datos mediante API y no tiene capacidad de ejecuci\xf3n de c\xf3mputo. Amazon Elastic Block Store es un servicio de almacenamiento en bloques que proporciona vol\xfamenes persistentes conectados a instancias de EC2, no un servicio de c\xf3mputo por derecho propio. Amazon Cognito es un servicio de identidad para administrar la autenticaci\xf3n de usuarios y el control de acceso para aplicaciones web y m\xf3viles, y no est\xe1 relacionado con las capacidades de c\xf3mputo."},{"id":"q991","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede usar para almacenar de forma privada y administrar versiones de c\xf3digo fuente?","options":{"A":"AWS CodeBuild","B":"AWS CodeCommit","C":"AWS CodePipeline","D":"AWS X-Ray"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CodeCommit es un servicio de control de c\xf3digo fuente totalmente administrado que aloja repositorios Git privados, lo que proporciona almacenamiento seguro y administraci\xf3n de versiones para el c\xf3digo fuente.\\nAWS CodeBuild es un servicio de compilaci\xf3n totalmente administrado que compila c\xf3digo fuente, ejecuta pruebas y produce paquetes listos para implementar, pero no almacena ni administra repositorios de c\xf3digo fuente. AWS CodePipeline es un servicio de entrega continua que automatiza las etapas de una canalizaci\xf3n de lanzamiento, orquestando los pasos de compilaci\xf3n, prueba e implementaci\xf3n en lugar de almacenar o versionar c\xf3digo fuente. AWS X-Ray es una interfaz unificada para administrar proyectos de desarrollo de software que re\xfane varias herramientas para desarrolladores, pero es una capa de administraci\xf3n de proyectos en lugar de un servicio dedicado de repositorio de c\xf3digo fuente."},{"id":"q993","question":"Una empresa quiere asegurarse de que su infraestructura est\xe9 dise\xf1ada para la tolerancia a fallos y la continuidad del negocio en caso de una interrupci\xf3n ambiental. \xbfEn qu\xe9 componente de infraestructura de AWS deber\xeda replicar la empresa?","options":{"A":"Edge locations","B":"Zonas de disponibilidad","C":"Regiones","D":"Amazon Route 53"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Replicar la infraestructura en varias Zonas de disponibilidad dentro de una Regi\xf3n proporciona tolerancia a fallos y continuidad del negocio, lo que garantiza que las operaciones contin\xfaen incluso si un evento ambiental interrumpe una AZ.\\nLas Edge locations son puntos de presencia que CloudFront usa para almacenar en cach\xe9 contenido cerca de los usuarios finales, y no son componentes de infraestructura en los que los clientes repliquen sus cargas de trabajo para lograr tolerancia a fallos. Las Regiones son las agrupaciones geogr\xe1ficas m\xe1s amplias de la infraestructura de AWS, y aunque implementar en varias Regiones proporciona recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres, replicar en Zonas de disponibilidad dentro de una Regi\xf3n es el enfoque est\xe1ndar para la tolerancia a fallos frente a interrupciones ambientales. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS que enruta el tr\xe1fico entre endpoints, y no es un componente de infraestructura en el que se repliquen las cargas de trabajo."},{"id":"q994","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS se usa para enviar mensajes de texto y correo electr\xf3nico desde aplicaciones distribuidas?","options":{"A":"Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS)","B":"Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES)","C":"alertas de Amazon CloudWatch","D":"Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda pub/sub que env\xeda mensajes de texto (SMS) y de correo electr\xf3nico desde aplicaciones distribuidas a los suscriptores mediante temas, lo que lo hace adecuado para enviar notificaciones a trav\xe9s de varios canales simult\xe1neamente.\\nAmazon Simple Email Service es un servicio de env\xedo de correo electr\xf3nico basado en la nube dise\xf1ado para enviar correos electr\xf3nicos de marketing, transaccionales y masivos, pero solo admite correo electr\xf3nico y no puede enviar mensajes de texto SMS. Las alertas de Amazon CloudWatch activan notificaciones cuando se superan los umbrales de las m\xe9tricas, pero CloudWatch en s\xed no env\xeda mensajes de texto ni de correo electr\xf3nico directamente a los usuarios finales desde aplicaciones distribuidas. Amazon SQS es un servicio de colas de mensajes que almacena en b\xfafer mensajes entre componentes de aplicaciones para permitir la comunicaci\xf3n as\xedncrona, pero no env\xeda mensajes de texto ni de correo electr\xf3nico a los suscriptores."},{"id":"q996","question":"Una empresa quiere conectarse a AWS mediante una conexi\xf3n privada de baja latencia desde su oficina remota. \xbfCu\xe1l es el m\xe9todo recomendado para cumplir estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Crear un t\xfanel VPN","B":"Conectarse a trav\xe9s de internet p\xfablica","C":"Usar VPC peering para crear una conexi\xf3n","D":"Usar AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n f\xedsica privada dedicada entre una oficina remota y AWS, evitando por completo la internet p\xfablica para ofrecer un rendimiento constante de baja latencia con throughput predecible.\\nUn t\xfanel VPN cifra el tr\xe1fico entre una oficina remota y AWS, pero lo enruta a trav\xe9s de internet p\xfablica, lo que significa que la latencia y el rendimiento pueden variar seg\xfan las condiciones de internet en lugar de estar garantizados por un enlace dedicado. Conectarse a trav\xe9s de internet p\xfablica no proporciona ancho de banda dedicado, ni garant\xedas de privacidad, ni latencia constante, por lo que no es adecuado para cargas de trabajo que requieren conectividad confiable y de baja latencia a AWS. VPC peering crea una conexi\xf3n de red privada entre dos VPC dentro de AWS para permitir el enrutamiento directo de tr\xe1fico entre ellas, y no se puede usar para conectar una oficina remota externa a la infraestructura de AWS."},{"id":"q999","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo se debe implementar una aplicaci\xf3n web para garantizar alta disponibilidad en AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Implementar m\xfaltiples instancias de la aplicaci\xf3n en m\xfaltiples Availability Zones","B":"Implementar m\xfaltiples instancias de la aplicaci\xf3n en una sola Availability Zone","C":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en una instancia Amazon EC2 optimizada para computaci\xf3n en una sola Availability Zone","D":"Implementar la aplicaci\xf3n en una instancia Amazon EC2 en un Auto Scaling group"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Implementar m\xfaltiples instancias en m\xfaltiples Availability Zones garantiza alta disponibilidad al proporcionar redundancia, de modo que si una AZ falla, las instancias en otras AZ contin\xfaan atendiendo tr\xe1fico sin interrupci\xf3n.\\nImplementar m\xfaltiples instancias en una sola Availability Zone mejora la capacidad y el rendimiento, pero no proporciona protecci\xf3n contra una falla a nivel de AZ, lo que deja la aplicaci\xf3n vulnerable a un punto \xfanico de falla. Implementar en una instancia EC2 optimizada para computaci\xf3n en una sola Availability Zone aborda el rendimiento para cargas de trabajo intensivas en computaci\xf3n, pero no ofrece redundancia ni tolerancia a fallas. Implementar una instancia EC2 en un Auto Scaling group permite que el grupo reemplace una instancia fallida, pero a\xfan deja una brecha en la disponibilidad mientras se aprovisiona el reemplazo, y no protege contra una falla de AZ."},{"id":"q1005","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando una aplicaci\xf3n que necesita entregar im\xe1genes y videos a nivel global con latencia m\xednima. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque puede usar la empresa para lograrlo de manera rentable?","options":{"A":"Entregar el contenido a trav\xe9s de Amazon CloudFront","B":"Almacenar el contenido en Amazon S3 y habilitar la replicaci\xf3n entre regiones de S3","C":"Implementar una VPN en m\xfaltiples AWS Regions","D":"Entregar el contenido a trav\xe9s de AWS PrivateLink"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon CloudFront almacena en cach\xe9 im\xe1genes y videos en cientos de ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo, sirviendo contenido a los usuarios desde la ubicaci\xf3n de borde m\xe1s cercana en lugar del origen, lo que minimiza la latencia y maximiza las velocidades de transferencia de manera rentable para audiencias distribuidas globalmente.\\nAlmacenar contenido en Amazon S3 y habilitar la replicaci\xf3n entre regiones copia objetos a buckets de S3 en otras Regions, lo que mejora la disponibilidad y durabilidad de los datos, pero los usuarios a\xfan recuperan contenido directamente desde S3 en una Region espec\xedfica en lugar de desde una red de borde distribuida globalmente, lo que resulta en una latencia mayor que la que proporciona CloudFront. Implementar una VPN en m\xfaltiples AWS Regions crea conectividad de red cifrada entre Regions o entre entornos on-premises y AWS, pero una VPN no es un mecanismo para entregar contenido a usuarios finales con baja latencia y no proporciona capacidades de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 en el borde. Entregar contenido a trav\xe9s de AWS PrivateLink proporciona conectividad privada entre VPC y servicios de AWS sin que el tr\xe1fico atraviese la internet p\xfablica, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para comunicaci\xf3n segura servicio a servicio dentro de AWS en lugar de para entregar contenido a usuarios externos en todo el mundo con baja latencia."},{"id":"q1008","question":"Una empresa farmac\xe9utica opera su infraestructura en una sola AWS Region. La empresa tiene miles de VPC en varias cuentas de AWS que quiere interconectar. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS debe usar la empresa para ayudar a simplificar la administraci\xf3n y reducir los costos operativos?","options":{"A":"VPC endpoint","B":"AWS Direct Connect","C":"AWS Transit Gateway","D":"VPC peering"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Transit Gateway act\xfaa como un concentrador de red central que conecta miles de VPC en m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS a trav\xe9s de una \xfanica puerta de enlace administrada, eliminando la necesidad de administrar conexiones punto a punto individuales y reduciendo significativamente la complejidad operativa y el costo a escala.\\nLos VPC endpoints proporcionan conectividad privada entre una VPC y los servicios de AWS compatibles sin atravesar la internet p\xfablica, pero no conectan VPC entre s\xed. AWS Direct Connect establece un enlace de red privado dedicado entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS, lo que lo hace irrelevante cuando el objetivo es interconectar VPC que ya est\xe1n dentro de AWS. VPC peering crea conexiones individuales uno a uno entre pares de VPC, lo que se vuelve inmanejable y costoso a escala cuando miles de VPC necesitan comunicarse."},{"id":"q1011","question":"Una empresa tiene usuarios en m\xfaltiples regiones geogr\xe1ficas que experimentan alta latencia al acceder a su aplicaci\xf3n alojada en una sola AWS Region. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede mejorar la disponibilidad y el rendimiento global de la aplicaci\xf3n al dirigir el tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de la red global de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudFront","B":"AWS Global Accelerator","C":"Amazon Route 53","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator mejora la disponibilidad y el rendimiento para usuarios globales al dirigir el tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de la infraestructura de red global de AWS usando direcciones IP Anycast para enrutar las solicitudes al endpoint \xf3ptimo, reduciendo la latencia independientemente de d\xf3nde se encuentren los usuarios.\\nAmazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y sirve contenido est\xe1tico y din\xe1mico desde ubicaciones de borde cercanas a los usuarios, pero est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de contenido en lugar de enrutar tr\xe1fico de aplicaciones en vivo a trav\xe9s de la red global de AWS hacia un endpoint alojado \xfanico. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de DNS que resuelve nombres de dominio a direcciones IP y puede enrutar tr\xe1fico usando pol\xedticas basadas en latencia o geolocalizaci\xf3n, pero opera en la capa DNS en lugar de dirigir tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de la infraestructura de red global de AWS. AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS, y no es un servicio para mejorar el rendimiento de usuarios de internet geogr\xe1ficamente distribuidos que acceden a una aplicaci\xf3n alojada en la nube."},{"id":"q1012","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona direcciones IP Anycast est\xe1ticas que act\xfaan como un punto de entrada fijo a endpoints de aplicaciones en una o m\xe1s AWS Regions?","options":{"A":"Elastic Load Balancing","B":"Amazon Route 53","C":"AWS Global Accelerator","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Global Accelerator proporciona dos direcciones IP Anycast est\xe1ticas que sirven como punto de entrada fijo para las aplicaciones, enrutando el tr\xe1fico a trav\xe9s de la red global de AWS hacia endpoints en la Region m\xe1s cercana para mejorar el rendimiento y la disponibilidad.\\nElastic Load Balancing distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre m\xfaltiples destinos dentro de una sola Region y no proporciona direcciones IP Anycast est\xe1ticas ni enruta tr\xe1fico entre m\xfaltiples Regions a trav\xe9s de la red global de AWS. Amazon Route 53 es un servicio de enrutamiento basado en DNS que resuelve nombres de dominio a direcciones IP y puede enrutar tr\xe1fico usando pol\xedticas como enrutamiento basado en latencia o geolocalizaci\xf3n, pero opera en la capa DNS en lugar de proporcionar direcciones IP Anycast est\xe1ticas como puntos de entrada fijos. Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y sirve contenido desde ubicaciones de borde cercanas a los usuarios, y no proporciona direcciones IP Anycast est\xe1ticas ni act\xfaa como un punto de entrada de red fijo para endpoints de aplicaciones."},{"id":"q1013","question":"Una empresa necesita ejecutar servicios de AWS on-premises debido a requisitos de residencia de datos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a la empresa ejecutar infraestructura y servicios de AWS en su propio centro de datos?","options":{"A":"AWS Wavelength","B":"AWS Local Zones","C":"AWS Outposts","D":"AWS Snow Family"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Outposts es un servicio completamente administrado que entrega infraestructura, servicios, API y herramientas de AWS al centro de datos propio del cliente, permitiendo que las cargas de trabajo con estrictos requisitos de residencia de datos permanezcan on-premises mientras siguen usando servicios administrados de AWS.\\nAWS Wavelength extiende el c\xf3mputo y almacenamiento de AWS al borde de las redes 5G para aplicaciones de latencia ultra baja, no para ejecutar servicios de AWS dentro del centro de datos propio del cliente. AWS Local Zones ubica servicios de c\xf3mputo, almacenamiento y bases de datos de AWS m\xe1s cerca de grandes centros de poblaci\xf3n para cargas de trabajo sensibles a la latencia, pero la infraestructura sigue siendo propiedad de AWS y operada por AWS. AWS Snow Family proporciona dispositivos f\xedsicos para computaci\xf3n en el borde y transferencia de datos sin conexi\xf3n hacia y desde AWS, no para ejecutar una implementaci\xf3n permanente de infraestructura de AWS on-premises."},{"id":"q1014","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 soluci\xf3n de nube h\xedbrida de AWS lleva servicios, infraestructura y modelos operativos nativos de AWS a pr\xe1cticamente cualquier centro de datos, espacio de co-location o instalaci\xf3n on-premises?","options":{"A":"AWS Direct Connect","B":"AWS VPN","C":"AWS Storage Gateway","D":"AWS Outposts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Outposts extiende la infraestructura, los servicios y los modelos operativos nativos de AWS a ubicaciones on-premises, centros de datos y espacios de co-location, proporcionando una experiencia h\xedbrida verdaderamente consistente con las mismas API y herramientas de AWS utilizadas en la nube.\\nAWS Direct Connect proporciona un enlace de red privado dedicado entre la infraestructura on-premises y AWS, pero no lleva la infraestructura de AWS f\xedsicamente on-premises. AWS VPN crea conexiones de red cifradas entre entornos on-premises y AWS a trav\xe9s de la internet p\xfablica, y es un servicio de conectividad en lugar de una extensi\xf3n de infraestructura. AWS Storage Gateway conecta entornos de almacenamiento on-premises con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS, pero solo conecta almacenamiento y no extiende la gama completa de servicios e infraestructura nativos de AWS a ubicaciones on-premises."},{"id":"q1016","question":"Una empresa de medios necesita ejecutar aplicaciones sensibles a la latencia m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios finales en un \xe1rea metropolitana espec\xedfica que no tiene una Regi\xf3n de AWS cercana. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n de infraestructura de AWS deber\xedan considerar?","options":{"A":"AWS Outposts","B":"AWS Local Zones","C":"AWS Wavelength","D":"Amazon CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Local Zones coloca c\xf3mputo, almacenamiento, bases de datos y otros servicios seleccionados de AWS m\xe1s cerca de grandes centros de poblaci\xf3n y centros de TI donde no existe una Regi\xf3n de AWS cercana, lo que proporciona latencia de milisegundos de un d\xedgito a los usuarios finales para aplicaciones sensibles a la latencia.\\nAWS Outposts lleva la infraestructura y los servicios nativos de AWS al centro de datos local del cliente, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo que deben permanecer en las instalaciones en lugar de extender la presencia de AWS a nuevas \xe1reas metropolitanas. AWS Wavelength incorpora c\xf3mputo y almacenamiento de AWS dentro de las redes 5G de proveedores de telecomunicaciones para ofrecer latencia ultrabaja para casos de uso m\xf3viles y de dispositivos conectados, y es espec\xedfico para implementaciones en el borde 5G en lugar de cobertura general de \xe1reas metropolitanas. Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y distribuye contenido en ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo para reducir la latencia de los usuarios finales, pero es un servicio de almacenamiento en cach\xe9 de contenido en lugar de una opci\xf3n de infraestructura para ejecutar cargas de trabajo de c\xf3mputo sensibles a la latencia m\xe1s cerca de los usuarios."},{"id":"q1017","question":"Una empresa quiere construir una arquitectura orientada a eventos donde m\xfaltiples servicios de AWS y aplicaciones SaaS puedan reaccionar a eventos en tiempo real. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona un bus de eventos sin servidor para este prop\xf3sito?","options":{"A":"Amazon SNS","B":"Amazon SQS","C":"Amazon EventBridge","D":"AWS Step Functions"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon EventBridge es un bus de eventos sin servidor que ingiere eventos de sus propias aplicaciones, proveedores SaaS y servicios de AWS, y los enruta a destinos mediante reglas de filtrado y enrutamiento, lo que lo convierte en la soluci\xf3n creada espec\xedficamente para arquitecturas orientadas a eventos.\\nAmazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda pub/sub totalmente administrado que env\xeda notificaciones a suscriptores como funciones Lambda, endpoints HTTP y direcciones de correo electr\xf3nico, y es un servicio de entrega de notificaciones en lugar de un bus de eventos capaz de enrutar eventos estructurados desde aplicaciones SaaS con reglas de filtrado. Amazon SQS es un servicio de colas de mensajes totalmente administrado que desacopla componentes de aplicaciones al mantener mensajes en una cola para su procesamiento, y es una herramienta de colas punto a punto en lugar de un bus de eventos sin servidor para enrutamiento de eventos en tiempo real entre m\xfaltiples servicios e integraciones SaaS. AWS Step Functions es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo sin servidor que coordina los pasos de aplicaciones distribuidas mediante m\xe1quinas de estado visuales, y es una herramienta de ejecuci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo en lugar de un bus de eventos."},{"id":"q1018","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite que las aplicaciones respondan a cambios de estado en recursos de AWS enrutando eventos a destinos como funciones Lambda, colas SQS o temas SNS?","options":{"A":"Amazon Kinesis","B":"Amazon EventBridge","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"Amazon MQ"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EventBridge es un bus de eventos sin servidor que detecta y enruta eventos de servicios de AWS, aplicaciones personalizadas y socios SaaS a destinos como Lambda, SQS y SNS, lo que permite arquitecturas orientadas a eventos.\\nAmazon Kinesis es un servicio para ingerir y procesar datos de streaming en tiempo real de gran volumen, no para enrutar eventos discretos entre servicios de AWS. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta con fines de auditor\xeda, y no enruta eventos a destinos de aplicaciones. Amazon MQ es un broker de mensajes administrado para aplicaciones que usan protocolos de mensajer\xeda est\xe1ndar, y no es un servicio de enrutamiento de eventos para cambios de estado de recursos de AWS."},{"id":"q1023","question":"Una empresa quiere configurar un centro de contacto basado en la nube para manejar llamadas de servicio al cliente. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona esta capacidad?","options":{"A":"Amazon Chime","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"AWS Support Center","D":"Amazon WorkSpaces"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube que facilita la configuraci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de un centro de servicio al cliente con precios de pago por uso y sin infraestructura que administrar.\\nAmazon Chime era un servicio de comunicaciones para videoconferencias, mensajer\xeda y reuniones en l\xednea que se retir\xf3 en febrero de 2024, y estaba dise\xf1ado para colaboraci\xf3n empresarial interna en lugar de operaciones de centro de contacto orientadas al cliente. AWS Support Center es el portal para administrar casos de soporte de AWS y acceder a asistencia t\xe9cnica de AWS, no un servicio para construir centros de contacto de clientes. Amazon WorkSpaces es un servicio de escritorio virtual administrado para entregar escritorios basados en la nube a usuarios finales, no relacionado con capacidades de centro de contacto o servicio al cliente."},{"id":"q1024","question":"Una empresa quiere construir, entrenar e implementar modelos de machine learning a escala. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona un entorno totalmente administrado para este prop\xf3sito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Rekognition","B":"Amazon SageMaker","C":"AWS Lambda","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon SageMaker es un servicio de machine learning totalmente administrado que proporciona herramientas para construir, entrenar e implementar modelos de ML a escala, cubriendo todo el flujo de trabajo de machine learning en un \xfanico entorno administrado.\\nAmazon Rekognition es un servicio de visi\xf3n por computadora que analiza im\xe1genes y videos para detectar rostros, objetos y escenas, y no proporciona un entorno de prop\xf3sito general para construir y entrenar modelos de ML personalizados. AWS Lambda es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin servidor que ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos y, aunque puede ejecutar inferencia sobre modelos preconstruidos, no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para construir y entrenar modelos de machine learning a escala. Amazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que extrae significado e informaci\xf3n de texto, y es un servicio de ML preconstruido en lugar de una plataforma para construir y entrenar modelos personalizados."},{"id":"q1025","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede detectar autom\xe1ticamente objetos, personas, texto y actividades en im\xe1genes y videos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Transcribe","C":"Amazon Rekognition","D":"Amazon Textract"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Rekognition es un servicio de an\xe1lisis de im\xe1genes y videos basado en aprendizaje profundo que puede detectar autom\xe1ticamente objetos, personas, texto, escenas y actividades en contenido visual.\\nAmazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que analiza y extrae significado de texto escrito, y no tiene capacidad para procesar im\xe1genes o videos. Amazon Transcribe convierte audio hablado en texto escrito, y es un servicio de voz a texto en lugar de un servicio de an\xe1lisis de im\xe1genes o videos. Amazon Textract extrae texto impreso, escritura a mano y datos estructurados de documentos escaneados e im\xe1genes, pero no detecta objetos, personas o actividades en im\xe1genes y videos de la forma en que lo hace Rekognition."},{"id":"q1026","question":"Una empresa necesita analizar rese\xf1as de clientes para determinar sentimiento y extraer frases clave. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS usa procesamiento de lenguaje natural (NLP) para este prop\xf3sito?","options":{"A":"Amazon Lex","B":"Amazon Polly","C":"Amazon Comprehend","D":"Amazon Translate"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Comprehend usa procesamiento de lenguaje natural para analizar texto y extraer informaci\xf3n como sentimiento, entidades, frases clave e idioma, lo que lo convierte en el servicio NLP creado espec\xedficamente para analizar rese\xf1as de clientes.\\nAmazon Lex es un servicio para construir interfaces conversacionales como chatbots usando voz y texto, y es una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de di\xe1logo en lugar de un servicio de an\xe1lisis de texto o extracci\xf3n de sentimiento. Amazon Polly es un servicio de texto a voz que convierte texto escrito en audio hablado realista, y no tiene capacidad para analizar texto en busca de sentimiento ni extraer frases clave. Amazon Translate es un servicio de traducci\xf3n autom\xe1tica neuronal que convierte texto de un idioma a otro, y es una herramienta de conversi\xf3n de idioma en lugar de un servicio para realizar an\xe1lisis de sentimiento o extracci\xf3n de frases clave."},{"id":"q1027","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a los desarrolladores construir interfaces conversacionales (chatbots) usando voz y texto?","options":{"A":"Amazon Polly","B":"Amazon Lex","C":"Amazon Transcribe","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Lex proporciona funcionalidad de aprendizaje profundo para construir interfaces conversacionales y chatbots usando voz y texto, y es la misma tecnolog\xeda que impulsa Amazon Alexa.\\nAmazon Polly convierte texto escrito en audio hablado de sonido natural y es un servicio de texto a voz, no una herramienta para construir interfaces conversacionales que entiendan y respondan a la entrada del usuario. Amazon Transcribe convierte audio hablado en texto escrito y es un servicio de voz a texto, centrado en la transcripci\xf3n en lugar de construir experiencias conversacionales interactivas. Amazon Comprehend analiza texto escrito usando procesamiento de lenguaje natural para extraer significado, sentimiento y entidades, y no procesa entrada de voz ni permite interacciones conversacionales."},{"id":"q1028","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS convierte texto en voz realista, lo que permite a los desarrolladores crear aplicaciones que hablan?","options":{"A":"Amazon Transcribe","B":"Amazon Translate","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Lex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Polly convierte texto en voz realista mediante aprendizaje profundo, y admite decenas de idiomas y voces para permitir a los desarrolladores crear aplicaciones que hablan.\\nAmazon Transcribe hace lo contrario de texto a voz: convierte audio hablado en texto escrito, por lo que no est\xe1 relacionado con generar salida hablada a partir de contenido escrito. Amazon Translate es un servicio de traducci\xf3n autom\xe1tica neuronal que convierte texto de un idioma a otro y no tiene capacidad de texto a voz. Amazon Lex es un servicio para crear interfaces conversacionales de chatbot mediante voz y texto, y no convierte por s\xed mismo texto escrito en salida de voz realista."},{"id":"q1029","question":"Una empresa necesita convertir autom\xe1ticamente grabaciones de audio de llamadas de clientes en texto. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon Polly","B":"Amazon Comprehend","C":"Amazon Transcribe","D":"Amazon Lex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Transcribe es un servicio de reconocimiento autom\xe1tico del habla que convierte audio hablado en texto escrito, lo que lo convierte en la opci\xf3n correcta para transcribir grabaciones de llamadas de clientes.\\nAmazon Polly realiza la funci\xf3n inversa: convierte texto escrito en audio hablado de sonido natural y no puede procesar grabaciones de audio en texto. Amazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que analiza y extrae significado de texto escrito, y requiere texto como entrada en lugar de grabaciones de audio. Amazon Lex es un servicio para crear interfaces conversacionales de chatbot mediante voz y texto, centrado en comprender y responder a la entrada del usuario en lugar de transcribir audio grabado en texto."},{"id":"q1030","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona traducci\xf3n autom\xe1tica neuronal para convertir texto entre diferentes idiomas?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Transcribe","C":"Amazon Lex","D":"Amazon Translate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Translate utiliza traducci\xf3n autom\xe1tica neuronal para ofrecer una traducci\xf3n r\xe1pida y de alta calidad de texto entre diferentes idiomas.\\nAmazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que analiza texto escrito para extraer informaci\xf3n como sentimiento, entidades y frases clave, y no realiza traducci\xf3n de idiomas. Amazon Transcribe convierte audio hablado en texto escrito, y es un servicio de voz a texto en lugar de un servicio de traducci\xf3n. Amazon Lex es un servicio para crear interfaces conversacionales y chatbots mediante voz y texto, y no tiene capacidad de traducci\xf3n de idiomas."},{"id":"q1031","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a los clientes ejecutar contenedores Docker sin administrar la infraestructura subyacente?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"AWS Fargate","C":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","D":"Amazon EKS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Fargate es un motor de computaci\xf3n sin servidor para contenedores que elimina la necesidad de aprovisionar o administrar la infraestructura de servidores subyacente, lo que permite a los clientes ejecutar contenedores Docker especificando solo los requisitos de CPU y memoria.\\nAmazon EC2 proporciona instancias de servidor virtual que los clientes deben aprovisionar, configurar y administrar ellos mismos, lo que lo convierte en una opci\xf3n basada en servidores que requiere administraci\xf3n de infraestructura. AWS Elastic Beanstalk es un servicio de plataforma para implementar aplicaciones web que abstrae la administraci\xf3n de la infraestructura, pero no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para ejecutar contenedores Docker sin supervisi\xf3n de la infraestructura. Amazon EKS es un servicio de Kubernetes administrado para orquestar cargas de trabajo en contenedores, pero a\xfan requiere que los clientes aprovisionen y administren la capacidad subyacente de nodos EC2 a menos que se combine expl\xedcitamente con Fargate."},{"id":"q1032","question":"Una empresa quiere usar Kubernetes para orquestar aplicaciones en contenedores en AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona una experiencia de Kubernetes administrada?","options":{"A":"Amazon ECS","B":"Amazon EKS","C":"AWS Fargate","D":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EKS es un servicio de Kubernetes totalmente administrado que facilita la ejecuci\xf3n de Kubernetes en AWS sin necesidad de instalar, operar o mantener su propio plano de control de Kubernetes.\\nAmazon ECS es el servicio propietario de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores de AWS que utiliza su propio modelo de programaci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n en lugar de Kubernetes, y no es una experiencia de Kubernetes administrada. AWS Fargate es un motor de computaci\xf3n sin servidor para contenedores que elimina la necesidad de administrar la infraestructura de servidores subyacente, pero es una capa de computaci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de Kubernetes. AWS Elastic Beanstalk es un servicio de plataforma para implementar aplicaciones web que abstrae la administraci\xf3n de la infraestructura, y no es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores ni de Kubernetes."},{"id":"q1033","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores totalmente administrado que admite contenedores Docker y permite programar contenedores en un cl\xfaster administrado de instancias de Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"Amazon EKS","B":"AWS Lambda","C":"Amazon ECS","D":"AWS Batch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon ECS es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores totalmente administrado que admite contenedores Docker y permite programar y ejecutar contenedores en un cl\xfaster administrado de instancias EC2, integr\xe1ndose profundamente con el ecosistema de AWS.\\nAmazon EKS es un servicio de Kubernetes administrado para orquestar cargas de trabajo en contenedores usando Kubernetes en lugar de la programaci\xf3n nativa de ECS. AWS Lambda es un servicio de computaci\xf3n sin servidor que ejecuta c\xf3digo en respuesta a eventos sin aprovisionar ni administrar servidores, no una plataforma de orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores. AWS Batch es un servicio administrado para ejecutar trabajos de computaci\xf3n por lotes a gran escala y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para orquestaci\xf3n o programaci\xf3n general de contenedores."},{"id":"q1042","question":"Una empresa necesita automatizar la transferencia de datos entre sus sistemas de almacenamiento locales y Amazon S3. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS simplifica y acelera esta transferencia de datos?","options":{"A":"AWS Storage Gateway","B":"AWS DataSync","C":"AWS Snowball","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS DataSync es un servicio de transferencia de datos que simplifica, automatiza y acelera el movimiento de datos entre sistemas de almacenamiento locales y servicios de almacenamiento de AWS, incluidos Amazon S3, Amazon EFS y Amazon FSx.\\nAWS Storage Gateway es un servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido que presenta el almacenamiento en la nube como recursos compartidos de archivos, vol\xfamenes o cintas locales para aplicaciones locales para acceso continuo, en lugar de automatizar la transferencia de datos \xfanica o programada a S3. AWS Snowball es un dispositivo f\xedsico de transferencia de datos dise\xf1ado para migraci\xf3n masiva de datos sin conexi\xf3n cuando la transferencia por red no es pr\xe1ctica debido al volumen de datos o a las limitaciones de ancho de banda, y no automatiza transferencias continuas entre sistemas locales y S3. AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, lo que mejora el rendimiento y la consistencia de la red, pero no automatiza ni administra por s\xed mismo la transferencia de datos entre sistemas de almacenamiento."},{"id":"q1043","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede mover autom\xe1ticamente datos entre el almacenamiento local y Amazon EFS o Amazon S3 hasta 10 veces m\xe1s r\xe1pido que las herramientas de c\xf3digo abierto?","options":{"A":"AWS Transfer Family","B":"AWS DataSync","C":"AWS Migration Hub","D":"Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS DataSync transfiere autom\xe1ticamente datos entre el almacenamiento local y Amazon EFS o Amazon S3 hasta 10 veces m\xe1s r\xe1pido que las herramientas de c\xf3digo abierto, con cifrado integrado, validaci\xf3n de datos y optimizaci\xf3n de red.\\nAWS Transfer Family proporciona flujos de trabajo de transferencia de archivos totalmente administrados hacia y desde Amazon S3 y Amazon EFS usando protocolos como SFTP, FTPS y FTP, y es un servicio de transferencia de archivos basado en protocolos en lugar de un servicio de movimiento automatizado de datos de alta velocidad entre el almacenamiento local y AWS. AWS Migration Hub proporciona una ubicaci\xf3n central para realizar un seguimiento del progreso de las migraciones de aplicaciones en m\xfaltiples herramientas de migraci\xf3n de AWS, y es un servicio de supervisi\xf3n y coordinaci\xf3n de migraciones en lugar de un servicio de transferencia de datos. Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose es un servicio totalmente administrado para cargar datos de transmisi\xf3n en tiempo real en destinos como Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift y Amazon OpenSearch Service, y es un servicio de ingesta de datos de transmisi\xf3n en lugar de una herramienta para transferir archivos desde el almacenamiento local."},{"id":"q1044","question":"Una empresa necesita un servicio de inteligencia empresarial (BI) nativo de la nube para crear paneles interactivos y visualizaciones a partir de m\xfaltiples or\xedgenes de datos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xedan usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon Athena","B":"Amazon QuickSight","C":"Amazon Redshift","D":"AWS Glue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon QuickSight es un servicio de inteligencia empresarial sin servidor nativo de la nube para crear paneles interactivos y visualizaciones que pueden conectarse a una amplia gama de or\xedgenes de datos, incluidos S3, RDS, Redshift y Athena.\\nAmazon Athena es un servicio de consultas interactivas para analizar datos almacenados en S3 mediante SQL y es un origen de datos al que QuickSight puede conectarse, no una herramienta de visualizaci\xf3n. Amazon Redshift es un almac\xe9n de datos administrado para almacenar y consultar grandes vol\xfamenes de datos estructurados, no un servicio de inteligencia empresarial ni de paneles. AWS Glue es un servicio administrado de extracci\xf3n, transformaci\xf3n y carga para preparar y mover datos entre or\xedgenes, no para crear paneles ni visualizaciones."},{"id":"q1045","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona inteligencia empresarial sin servidor con precios de pago por sesi\xf3n e informaci\xf3n impulsada por aprendizaje autom\xe1tico?","options":{"A":"Amazon Redshift","B":"Amazon EMR","C":"Amazon QuickSight","D":"Amazon Athena"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon QuickSight es un servicio de inteligencia empresarial sin servidor que ofrece precios de pago por sesi\xf3n e informaci\xf3n impulsada por aprendizaje autom\xe1tico mediante consultas en lenguaje natural, lo que lo convierte en la herramienta de BI creada espec\xedficamente para ello en el portafolio de anal\xedtica de AWS.\\nAmazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos administrado optimizado para ejecutar consultas SQL anal\xedticas en grandes conjuntos de datos, y es un servicio de almacenamiento y consulta de datos en lugar de una herramienta de inteligencia empresarial o visualizaci\xf3n. Amazon EMR es una plataforma de cl\xfasteres administrada para procesar grandes vol\xfamenes de datos mediante frameworks de big data como Hadoop y Spark, y es un servicio de procesamiento de datos sin capacidad de BI ni de visualizaci\xf3n. Amazon Athena es un servicio de consultas sin servidor que analiza datos almacenados en Amazon S3 mediante SQL, y es una herramienta de consulta en lugar de un servicio de inteligencia empresarial con paneles, visualizaciones o informaci\xf3n impulsada por aprendizaje autom\xe1tico."},{"id":"q1046","question":"Una empresa quiere administrar y automatizar centralmente las copias de seguridad en m\xfaltiples servicios de AWS, incluidos Amazon EBS, Amazon RDS y Amazon DynamoDB. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona esta capacidad?","options":{"A":"AWS Storage Gateway","B":"Amazon S3 Lifecycle","C":"AWS Backup","D":"AWS CloudFormation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Backup es un servicio totalmente administrado que centraliza y automatiza la protecci\xf3n de datos en m\xfaltiples servicios de AWS, incluidos Amazon EBS, Amazon RDS y Amazon DynamoDB, y proporciona un \xfanico lugar para administrar pol\xedticas de copia de seguridad y monitorear la actividad.\\nAWS Storage Gateway es un servicio de almacenamiento h\xedbrido que conecta entornos on-premises con el almacenamiento en la nube de AWS, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para administrar y automatizar centralmente copias de seguridad en los servicios de AWS. Amazon S3 Lifecycle es una funci\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n que transfiere autom\xe1ticamente objetos entre clases de almacenamiento de S3 o los hace caducar despu\xe9s de un per\xedodo establecido, y no administra copias de seguridad en otros servicios de AWS. AWS CloudFormation es un servicio de infraestructura como c\xf3digo que aprovisiona y administra recursos de AWS mediante plantillas, y no tiene capacidad para automatizar ni centralizar la copia de seguridad de datos entre servicios."},{"id":"q1047","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS te permite definir pol\xedticas de copia de seguridad, programar copias de seguridad y monitorear la actividad de copia de seguridad en tus recursos de AWS desde una \xfanica consola?","options":{"A":"AWS Systems Manager","B":"AWS Backup","C":"Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Backup es un servicio de copia de seguridad totalmente administrado que facilita centralizar y automatizar la copia de seguridad de datos en los servicios de AWS en la nube y on-premises. Proporciona una \xfanica consola para crear planes de copia de seguridad (pol\xedticas) que automatizan la programaci\xf3n de copias de seguridad y la administraci\xf3n de la retenci\xf3n para m\xfaltiples servicios como Amazon EBS, RDS, DynamoDB, EFS y m\xe1s.\\nAWS Systems Manager es un servicio de administraci\xf3n que se usa para ver y controlar tu infraestructura de AWS, y se centra en tareas operativas como la aplicaci\xf3n de parches y la administraci\xf3n de la configuraci\xf3n en lugar de las copias de seguridad de datos centralizadas. Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager (DLM) est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para automatizar la creaci\xf3n, retenci\xf3n y eliminaci\xf3n de instant\xe1neas de Amazon EBS y AMI respaldadas por EBS; no proporciona una soluci\xf3n centralizada para otros servicios de AWS. AWS Config es un servicio que te permite evaluar, auditar y evaluar las configuraciones de tus recursos de AWS, y proporciona un historial de cambios de recursos en lugar de administrar programaciones o pol\xedticas de copia de seguridad."},{"id":"q1048","question":"Una empresa necesita un servicio de extracci\xf3n, transformaci\xf3n y carga (ETL) totalmente administrado para preparar y transformar datos para anal\xedtica. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xedan usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon Kinesis","B":"AWS Glue","C":"Amazon EMR","D":"AWS Data Pipeline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Glue es un servicio ETL sin servidor totalmente administrado que rastrea or\xedgenes de datos, crea un cat\xe1logo de metadatos y prepara y transforma datos para cargas de trabajo anal\xedticas.\\nAmazon Kinesis es un servicio de streaming de datos en tiempo real dise\xf1ado para ingerir y procesar flujos de datos continuos, y no es un servicio ETL para preparar y transformar datos para anal\xedtica. Amazon EMR es un servicio de procesamiento de big data que ejecuta frameworks de c\xf3digo abierto como Apache Hadoop y Spark, y aunque puede realizar transformaciones, requiere una configuraci\xf3n y administraci\xf3n de cl\xfasteres significativas en lugar de ser un servicio ETL totalmente administrado. AWS Data Pipeline (obsoleto) es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n que automatiza el movimiento y la transformaci\xf3n de datos entre servicios de AWS y or\xedgenes on-premises de forma programada, pero es una herramienta de orquestaci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo en lugar de un servicio ETL totalmente administrado creado espec\xedficamente para la preparaci\xf3n de anal\xedtica."},{"id":"q1049","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS descubre y cataloga autom\xe1ticamente metadatos de varios or\xedgenes de datos y puede generar scripts ETL para la transformaci\xf3n de datos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Athena","B":"AWS Glue","C":"Amazon Redshift Spectrum","D":"AWS Lake Formation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Glue descubre y cataloga autom\xe1ticamente metadatos de or\xedgenes de datos mediante rastreadores que completan el Glue Data Catalog, y puede generar y ejecutar scripts ETL para transformar datos para cargas de trabajo anal\xedticas.\\nAmazon Athena es un servicio de consultas sin servidor que analiza datos almacenados en Amazon S3 mediante SQL, y se basa en el Glue Data Catalog como su almac\xe9n de metadatos en lugar de realizar el descubrimiento o ETL por s\xed mismo. Amazon Redshift Spectrum es una caracter\xedstica de Amazon Redshift que permite consultar datos almacenados en S3 directamente desde Redshift sin cargarlos, y es una extensi\xf3n de consulta en lugar de un servicio de descubrimiento de metadatos o ETL. AWS Lake Formation es un servicio para crear, proteger y administrar lagos de datos en AWS, y aunque usa el Glue Data Catalog, se centra en la gobernanza de lagos de datos en lugar del descubrimiento automatizado de metadatos o la generaci\xf3n de scripts ETL."},{"id":"q1050","question":"Una empresa necesita recopilar, procesar y analizar datos de streaming en tiempo real de dispositivos IoT. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para streaming de datos en tiempo real?","options":{"A":"Amazon SQS","B":"Amazon Kinesis","C":"Amazon SNS","D":"AWS Batch"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Kinesis est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para recopilar, procesar y analizar datos de streaming en tiempo real a escala, lo que lo convierte en la opci\xf3n correcta para ingerir y procesar continuamente datos de dispositivos IoT a medida que se generan.\\nAmazon SQS es un servicio de colas de mensajes que desacopla los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n almacenando mensajes entre ellos, pero est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la entrega as\xedncrona de mensajes entre servicios en lugar del procesamiento continuo de flujos de datos en tiempo real. Amazon SNS es un servicio de notificaciones pub/sub que env\xeda mensajes a m\xfaltiples suscriptores simult\xe1neamente, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para notificaciones de eventos y alertas en lugar de ingerir y procesar flujos de datos continuos de alto volumen. AWS Batch es un servicio administrado para ejecutar trabajos de computaci\xf3n por lotes a gran escala en recursos aprovisionados din\xe1micamente, y procesa datos en lotes programados o bajo demanda en lugar de en tiempo real."},{"id":"q1051","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede ingerir y procesar cientos de terabytes de datos por hora desde or\xedgenes como clickstreams de sitios web, transacciones financieras y feeds de redes sociales?","options":{"A":"Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","B":"Amazon SQS","C":"Amazon Redshift","D":"AWS Data Pipeline"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon Kinesis Data Streams captura y procesa continuamente grandes vol\xfamenes de datos de streaming en tiempo real desde or\xedgenes como clickstreams de sitios web, transacciones financieras y feeds de redes sociales.\\nAmazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) es un servicio de colas de mensajes administrado que se usa para desacoplar los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n pasando mensajes entre ellos, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la ingesta de flujos de datos en tiempo real de alto rendimiento a escala. Amazon Redshift es un servicio de almac\xe9n de datos en la nube optimizado para ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas en grandes conjuntos de datos, y no ingiere ni procesa flujos de datos continuos en tiempo real. AWS Data Pipeline (obsoleto) es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n para programar y automatizar el movimiento y la transformaci\xf3n de datos entre servicios de AWS y or\xedgenes on-premises de forma por lotes, y no es adecuado para la ingesta de streaming en tiempo real."},{"id":"q1052","question":"Una empresa quiere migrar su base de datos Oracle local a Amazon Aurora con un tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS facilita esta migraci\xf3n de base de datos?","options":{"A":"AWS DataSync","B":"AWS Database Migration Service (DMS)","C":"AWS Application Migration Service","D":"AWS Transfer Family"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Database Migration Service ayuda a migrar bases de datos a AWS con un tiempo de inactividad m\xednimo al mantener la base de datos de origen completamente operativa durante el proceso de migraci\xf3n, y admite tanto migraciones homog\xe9neas, como Oracle a Oracle, como migraciones heterog\xe9neas, como Oracle a Amazon Aurora.\\nAWS DataSync es un servicio de transferencia de datos que automatiza el traslado de archivos y objetos entre el almacenamiento local y los servicios de almacenamiento de AWS, como S3 y EFS, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la transferencia de almacenamiento de archivos y objetos en lugar de la migraci\xf3n de bases de datos. AWS Application Migration Service automatiza la migraci\xf3n de servidores y aplicaciones locales a AWS replic\xe1ndolos a nivel de servidor, y es un servicio de migraci\xf3n de servidores en lugar de un servicio de migraci\xf3n de bases de datos. AWS Transfer Family proporciona flujos de trabajo de transferencia de archivos administrados hacia y desde Amazon S3 y Amazon EFS mediante protocolos como SFTP y FTP, y es un servicio de transferencia de archivos sin capacidad de migraci\xf3n de bases de datos."},{"id":"q1053","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS admite tanto migraciones de bases de datos homog\xe9neas (por ejemplo, Oracle a Oracle) como heterog\xe9neas (por ejemplo, Oracle a Aurora)?","options":{"A":"AWS Schema Conversion Tool","B":"AWS Database Migration Service (DMS)","C":"AWS Migration Hub","D":"AWS Snowball Edge"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Database Migration Service admite tanto migraciones homog\xe9neas, como Oracle a Oracle, como migraciones heterog\xe9neas, como Oracle a Aurora, manteniendo la base de datos de origen completamente operativa durante la migraci\xf3n para minimizar el tiempo de inactividad.\\nAWS Schema Conversion Tool se utiliza junto con AWS DMS para migraciones heterog\xe9neas con el fin de convertir el esquema de la base de datos de origen para que sea compatible con el motor de base de datos de destino, pero no realiza la migraci\xf3n de datos real por s\xed misma. AWS Migration Hub proporciona un panel centralizado para realizar el seguimiento del progreso de las migraciones de aplicaciones en m\xfaltiples herramientas de migraci\xf3n de AWS, y no es un servicio de migraci\xf3n de bases de datos. AWS Snowball Edge es un dispositivo f\xedsico de computaci\xf3n de borde y transferencia de datos que se utiliza para mover grandes vol\xfamenes de datos a AWS cuando la transferencia por red no es pr\xe1ctica, y no es un servicio de migraci\xf3n de bases de datos."},{"id":"q1054","question":"Una empresa quiere replicar continuamente datos desde una base de datos local a una base de datos de AWS con fines de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS admite la replicaci\xf3n continua de datos?","options":{"A":"AWS Backup","B":"AWS Database Migration Service (DMS)","C":"Amazon RDS Multi-AZ","D":"AWS CloudFormation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS DMS admite la replicaci\xf3n continua de datos mediante Change Data Capture, transmitiendo los cambios continuos desde una base de datos local a una base de datos de AWS casi en tiempo real, lo que lo hace adecuado para escenarios de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres y sincronizaci\xf3n de datos.\\nAWS Backup crea instant\xe1neas puntuales de los recursos de AWS de forma programada, lo que proporciona puntos de recuperaci\xf3n pero no replica continuamente los cambios en vivo de la base de datos. Amazon RDS Multi-AZ mantiene una r\xe9plica en espera s\xedncrona dentro de AWS para alta disponibilidad y conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica, pero no replica datos desde fuentes locales. AWS CloudFormation aprovisiona y administra la infraestructura de AWS mediante plantillas basadas en c\xf3digo, y no tiene capacidad de replicaci\xf3n de bases de datos."},{"id":"q1067","question":"Una empresa quiere permitir que los empleados busquen entre miles de documentos internos, sitios de SharePoint y wikis utilizando preguntas en lenguaje natural y reciban respuestas precisas en lugar de una lista de enlaces. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona esta capacidad de b\xfasqueda empresarial inteligente?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon OpenSearch Service","C":"Amazon Kendra","D":"Amazon Lex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Kendra es un servicio de b\xfasqueda empresarial inteligente impulsado por machine learning que permite a los usuarios formular preguntas en lenguaje natural y recibir respuestas precisas extra\xeddas de m\xfaltiples fuentes de datos, como documentos, sitios de SharePoint y bases de datos, en lugar de devolver una simple lista clasificada de enlaces coincidentes.\\nAmazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que extrae informaci\xf3n como sentimiento, entidades y frases clave de texto ya digitalizado, pero no proporciona una interfaz de b\xfasqueda ni recupera respuestas de un corpus de documentos en respuesta a consultas de usuarios en lenguaje natural. Amazon OpenSearch Service es un motor de b\xfasqueda y an\xe1lisis administrado adecuado para an\xe1lisis de registros, b\xfasqueda de texto completo y monitoreo operativo, y devuelve coincidencias de documentos clasificadas seg\xfan la relevancia de las palabras clave en lugar de proporcionar la respuesta inteligente a preguntas y la extracci\xf3n precisa de respuestas que ofrece Kendra mediante machine learning. Amazon Lex es un servicio para crear chatbots conversacionales e interfaces de voz mediante reconocimiento autom\xe1tico del habla y comprensi\xf3n del lenguaje natural, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para gestionar di\xe1logos de varios turnos en lugar de indexar y buscar en repositorios de documentos empresariales."},{"id":"q1068","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS utiliza machine learning para indexar contenido de fuentes de datos empresariales y permitir que los usuarios formulen preguntas en lenguaje natural para encontrar respuestas espec\xedficas dentro de ese contenido?","options":{"A":"Amazon Transcribe","B":"Amazon Kendra","C":"Amazon Polly","D":"Amazon Translate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Kendra utiliza machine learning para indexar contenido de una amplia variedad de fuentes de datos empresariales y permite a los usuarios buscar ese contenido formulando preguntas en lenguaje natural, y el servicio identifica y muestra de forma inteligente las respuestas m\xe1s relevantes en lugar de devolver una lista de documentos.\\nAmazon Transcribe es un servicio de conversi\xf3n de voz a texto que convierte grabaciones de audio y lenguaje hablado en transcripciones de texto escrito, y es un servicio de procesamiento de audio sin capacidad de indexar documentos empresariales ni responder a consultas de b\xfasqueda en lenguaje natural. Amazon Polly es un servicio de conversi\xf3n de texto a voz que convierte texto escrito en salida de audio hablado realista, y es un servicio de s\xedntesis de audio en lugar de un servicio de b\xfasqueda o recuperaci\xf3n de informaci\xf3n de documentos. Amazon Translate es un servicio de traducci\xf3n autom\xe1tica neuronal para convertir texto entre diferentes idiomas, y es un servicio de conversi\xf3n de idiomas sin capacidad de indexar documentos ni responder a consultas de b\xfasqueda en lenguaje natural."},{"id":"q1070","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 creando un motor de recomendaciones que necesita almacenar y consultar relaciones complejas entre usuarios, productos, categor\xedas e historial de compras para mostrar sugerencias personalizadas. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de base de datos de AWS est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para almacenar y recorrer datos altamente conectados?","options":{"A":"Amazon RDS","B":"Amazon Redshift","C":"Amazon DynamoDB","D":"Amazon Neptune"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Neptune es un servicio de base de datos de grafos completamente administrado dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para almacenar y navegar por conjuntos de datos altamente conectados, donde las relaciones entre los puntos de datos son tan importantes como los datos en s\xed, lo que lo convierte en la opci\xf3n dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para casos de uso como motores de recomendaciones, redes sociales, detecci\xf3n de fraude y grafos de conocimiento.\\nAmazon RDS es un servicio de base de datos relacional administrado que utiliza tablas con filas y columnas conectadas mediante joins SQL para representar relaciones, y aunque puede modelar datos conectados, no proporciona la eficiencia y flexibilidad nativas de recorrido de grafos de una base de datos de grafos dedicada para consultas complejas de relaciones de varios saltos. Amazon Redshift es un almac\xe9n de datos en la nube completamente administrado optimizado para ejecutar consultas anal\xedticas en grandes conjuntos de datos hist\xf3ricos para inteligencia empresarial y generaci\xf3n de informes, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para el recorrido en tiempo real de grafos de relaciones altamente conectados. Amazon DynamoDB es una base de datos NoSQL de clave-valor y documentos completamente administrada optimizada para el acceso de alto rendimiento a elementos por clave principal, y no est\xe1 dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para almacenar o consultar de forma eficiente las estructuras de relaciones densas caracter\xedsticas de las cargas de trabajo de bases de datos de grafos."},{"id":"q1071","question":"Un desarrollador necesita crear un flujo de trabajo de procesamiento de pedidos sin servidor que implique llamar a m\xfaltiples funciones AWS Lambda en secuencia, gestionar errores en cada paso, reintentar autom\xe1ticamente los pasos fallidos y ramificar seg\xfan el tipo de pedido. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para este prop\xf3sito?","options":{"A":"Amazon SQS","B":"Amazon EventBridge","C":"AWS Step Functions","D":"Amazon SNS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Step Functions es un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n sin servidor que coordina m\xfaltiples servicios de AWS en flujos de trabajo visuales, admitiendo ejecuci\xf3n secuencial y paralela, gesti\xf3n de errores, reintentos autom\xe1ticos y ramificaci\xf3n condicional, lo que lo convierte en el servicio dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para crear flujos de trabajo complejos de varios pasos, como canalizaciones de procesamiento de pedidos.\\nAmazon SQS es un servicio de colas de mensajes administrado que desacopla los componentes de una aplicaci\xf3n almacenando mensajes en b\xfafer entre ellos, pero no proporciona orquestaci\xf3n de flujos de trabajo, l\xf3gica de ramificaci\xf3n, gesti\xf3n de reintentos integrada ni la capacidad de coordinar una secuencia de pasos entre m\xfaltiples servicios en un flujo de trabajo con estado. Amazon EventBridge es un bus de eventos sin servidor que enruta eventos entre servicios de AWS y aplicaciones seg\xfan reglas, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la integraci\xf3n y el enrutamiento basados en eventos en lugar de para orquestar flujos de trabajo complejos con estado de varios pasos con ramificaci\xf3n condicional y gesti\xf3n de errores. Amazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda de publicaci\xf3n/suscripci\xf3n administrado que entrega notificaciones simult\xe1neamente a m\xfaltiples suscriptores, y es un servicio de fan-out de eventos en lugar de un servicio de orquestaci\xf3n capaz de gestionar estado, secuenciar pasos y manejar errores en un flujo de trabajo de varios pasos."},{"id":"q1072","question":"Una empresa quiere conectar de forma segura millones de sensores industriales a AWS para que los datos de los dispositivos puedan enrutarse a Amazon Kinesis para procesamiento en tiempo real y a Amazon S3 para almacenamiento. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS gestiona la conectividad de los dispositivos, la autenticaci\xf3n y el enrutamiento de mensajes?","options":{"A":"AWS IoT Greengrass","B":"Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","C":"AWS IoT Core","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS IoT Core es un servicio en la nube administrado que permite a los dispositivos conectados interactuar de forma segura con aplicaciones en la nube y otros dispositivos a escala, gestionando la autenticaci\xf3n de dispositivos, la administraci\xf3n de conexiones y el enrutamiento de mensajes a servicios de AWS como Kinesis y S3 mediante reglas configurables, lo que lo convierte en la base para conectar dispositivos IoT a la nube de AWS.\\nAWS IoT Greengrass es el componente de borde de la plataforma AWS IoT que extiende las capacidades de la nube a los dispositivos para procesamiento local y operaci\xf3n sin conexi\xf3n, y funciona junto con IoT Core en lugar de reemplazarlo como el servicio principal del lado de la nube para gestionar la conectividad de dispositivos y el enrutamiento de mensajes a AWS. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams es un servicio de transmisi\xf3n de datos en tiempo real para ingerir y procesar datos de alto rendimiento, y aunque puede recibir datos enrutados desde IoT Core, es un servicio de procesamiento de datos en lugar de una plataforma de conectividad y autenticaci\xf3n de dispositivos. AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre centros de datos locales y AWS, y es un servicio de conectividad de red para infraestructura empresarial en lugar de una plataforma para gestionar conexiones de dispositivos IoT, autenticaci\xf3n y enrutamiento de mensajes a escala."},{"id":"q1074","question":"Una empresa de software quiere entregar su aplicaci\xf3n de escritorio a los clientes a trav\xe9s de un navegador web sin requerir que los clientes descarguen o instalen nada localmente. La aplicaci\xf3n debe ejecutarse en la infraestructura de AWS y transmitir su interfaz al navegador. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS debe usar la empresa?","options":{"A":"Amazon WorkSpaces","B":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web","C":"Amazon AppStream 2.0","D":"AWS Amplify"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon AppStream 2.0 es un servicio de streaming de aplicaciones totalmente administrado que ejecuta aplicaciones de escritorio en la infraestructura de AWS y transmite la interfaz de la aplicaci\xf3n a los navegadores web de los usuarios, lo que permite a los clientes usar el software sin descargarlo, instalarlo ni administrarlo en sus dispositivos locales.\\nAmazon WorkSpaces es un servicio de escritorio virtual totalmente administrado que proporciona a cada usuario un entorno de escritorio en la nube persistente y completo, lo cual es m\xe1s infraestructura de la necesaria para entregar una sola aplicaci\xf3n a trav\xe9s de un navegador e implica aprovisionamiento de escritorios por usuario en lugar de streaming a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n. Amazon WorkSpaces Web es un servicio administrado de bajo costo para proporcionar acceso basado en navegador a sitios web internos y aplicaciones SaaS desde dispositivos no administrados, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para el acceso a contenido web en lugar de para transmitir aplicaciones de escritorio completas que se ejecutan en c\xf3mputo de AWS. AWS Amplify es un framework de desarrollo y servicio de hosting para crear y desplegar aplicaciones web y m\xf3viles full-stack, y es un conjunto de herramientas para desarrolladores en lugar de un servicio de streaming de aplicaciones o de entrega de escritorios virtuales."},{"id":"q1075","question":"Una empresa quiere reemplazar sus computadoras de escritorio f\xedsicas por escritorios virtuales alojados en la nube a los que los empleados puedan acceder desde cualquier dispositivo y en cualquier lugar, y que cada empleado tenga su propio entorno de escritorio persistente y personalizado. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona esta capacidad?","options":{"A":"Amazon AppStream 2.0","B":"Amazon WorkSpaces","C":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web","D":"Amazon EC2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon WorkSpaces es un servicio de infraestructura de escritorio virtual seguro y totalmente administrado que proporciona a cada usuario un escritorio en la nube persistente accesible desde cualquier dispositivo compatible, lo que permite a las empresas reemplazar el hardware de escritorio f\xedsico mientras brindan a los empleados un entorno inform\xe1tico personalizado y consistente que los acompa\xf1a dondequiera que trabajen.\\nAmazon AppStream 2.0 transmite aplicaciones de escritorio espec\xedficas a un navegador sin proporcionar un entorno de escritorio completo persistente; los usuarios no pueden instalar sus propias aplicaciones ni mantener configuraciones personales de escritorio entre sesiones, lo que lo hace adecuado para entregar software espec\xedfico en lugar de reemplazar computadoras personales completas. Amazon WorkSpaces Web proporciona acceso basado en navegador a sitios web internos y aplicaciones SaaS desde dispositivos no administrados, y es una soluci\xf3n de acceso restringido para contenido web en lugar de un escritorio virtual persistente completo que reemplace una computadora personal. Amazon EC2 puede ejecutar m\xe1quinas virtuales Windows o Linux que t\xe9cnicamente funcionan como escritorios, pero los clientes deben aprovisionar, aplicar parches y administrar las instancias ellos mismos y construir su propia infraestructura de administraci\xf3n de escritorios en lugar de usar un servicio de escritorio totalmente administrado."},{"id":"q1076","question":"Una empresa quiere permitir que los empleados usen dispositivos personales no administrados para acceder de forma segura a aplicaciones web internas y herramientas SaaS aprobadas, al mismo tiempo que garantiza que no se puedan descargar ni copiar datos corporativos en esos dispositivos personales. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para este caso de uso?","options":{"A":"Amazon WorkSpaces","B":"AWS Client VPN","C":"Amazon AppStream 2.0","D":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web es un servicio de bajo costo y totalmente administrado que proporciona acceso seguro basado en navegador a sitios web internos y aplicaciones SaaS aprobadas desde dispositivos personales no administrados, con controles integrados que impiden que los usuarios descarguen, impriman o copien datos corporativos en sus dispositivos locales.\\nAmazon WorkSpaces proporciona escritorios virtuales persistentes completos, lo cual es significativamente m\xe1s capacidad y costo de lo requerido para el \xfanico prop\xf3sito de proporcionar acceso web administrado, y no est\xe1 espec\xedficamente orientado al caso de uso de restringir la exfiltraci\xf3n de datos desde dispositivos personales no administrados que acceden a aplicaciones web. AWS Client VPN extiende acceso de red cifrado completo a recursos de AWS y sistemas locales desde los dispositivos de los usuarios, brindando al dispositivo conectividad de red directa en lugar de proporcionar un entorno de navegador controlado que impida que los datos lleguen al dispositivo local. Amazon AppStream 2.0 transmite aplicaciones de escritorio completas a un navegador y es adecuado para entregar software instalado en lugar de proporcionar acceso administrado y restringido a sitios web internos y aplicaciones SaaS desde dispositivos no administrados."},{"id":"q1077","question":"Un desarrollador frontend quiere crear y desplegar una aplicaci\xf3n web full-stack en AWS que incluya autenticaci\xf3n de usuarios, una API de datos y hosting est\xe1tico, sin configurar ni administrar ninguna infraestructura de nube subyacente. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona una plataforma integrada de desarrollo y despliegue para este caso de uso?","options":{"A":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk","B":"AWS Amplify","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"AWS AppSync"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Amplify proporciona un conjunto completo de herramientas y servicios para que los desarrolladores frontend web y m\xf3viles creen aplicaciones full-stack en AWS, ofreciendo bibliotecas de cliente para autenticaci\xf3n, API de datos y almacenamiento, junto con un servicio de hosting con despliegue continuo administrado, sin requerir que los desarrolladores configuren o administren la infraestructura de nube subyacente.\\nAWS Elastic Beanstalk es una plataforma como servicio que gestiona el despliegue y el aprovisionamiento de capacidad para aplicaciones web backend, pero se enfoca en el hosting de servidores backend en lugar de proporcionar las bibliotecas de desarrollo frontend, los SDK m\xf3viles y el pipeline integrado de CI/CD y hosting que Amplify ofrece para crear aplicaciones full-stack modernas. Amazon Lightsail es una plataforma de nube simplificada para desplegar servidores virtuales, contenedores y bases de datos para cargas de trabajo sencillas, y aunque simplifica la administraci\xf3n de infraestructura, no proporciona el framework de desarrollo integrado, la biblioteca de autenticaci\xf3n ni los flujos de despliegue automatizados que ofrece Amplify. AWS AppSync es un servicio administrado de API GraphQL que puede servir como la capa de datos para aplicaciones, y aunque se integra con Amplify, es un servicio de API independiente en lugar de la plataforma de desarrollo y hosting full-stack que proporciona Amplify."},{"id":"q1082","question":"La aplicaci\xf3n de comercio electr\xf3nico de una empresa necesita enviar autom\xe1ticamente correos electr\xf3nicos de confirmaci\xf3n de pedidos, correos de notificaci\xf3n de env\xedo y correos de restablecimiento de contrase\xf1a a los clientes a gran volumen directamente desde el c\xf3digo de la aplicaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para este caso de uso?","options":{"A":"Amazon SNS","B":"Amazon SES","C":"Amazon Connect","D":"Amazon Pinpoint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES) es un servicio de env\xedo de correo electr\xf3nico basado en la nube dise\xf1ado para la entrega de correo electr\xf3nico transaccional y activado por aplicaciones a gran volumen, que proporciona la infraestructura, la gesti\xf3n de entregabilidad y las m\xe9tricas de env\xedo necesarias para entregar de forma confiable confirmaciones de pedidos, notificaciones de env\xedo y mensajes de restablecimiento de contrase\xf1a a los clientes a escala.\\nAmazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda de publicaci\xf3n/suscripci\xf3n totalmente administrado que se usa principalmente para enviar notificaciones a endpoints de aplicaciones, funciones Lambda, colas SQS y dispositivos m\xf3viles, y aunque puede entregar correo electr\xf3nico a direcciones suscritas, no est\xe1 optimizado para la entrega de correo electr\xf3nico transaccional a gran volumen con la gesti\xf3n de entregabilidad, el manejo de rebotes y las estad\xedsticas por mensaje que proporciona SES. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube para gestionar interacciones de servicio al cliente mediante canales de voz y chat, y es una plataforma de comunicaci\xf3n con agentes en tiempo real en lugar de un servicio de entrega autom\xe1tica de correo electr\xf3nico para mensajes activados por aplicaciones. Amazon Pinpoint es un servicio de participaci\xf3n del cliente para enviar mensajes dirigidos a trav\xe9s de m\xfaltiples canales, incluidos correo electr\xf3nico, SMS y notificaciones push, y aunque admite correo electr\xf3nico, est\xe1 orientado a campa\xf1as de marketing segmentadas en lugar de a la entrega de correo electr\xf3nico transaccional a gran volumen activada por eventos individuales de la aplicaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q1083","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a una empresa enviar tanto correos electr\xf3nicos transaccionales activados por aplicaciones, como recibos de pedidos, como correos electr\xf3nicos de marketing masivo, como boletines promocionales, a trav\xe9s de una \xfanica plataforma de correo electr\xf3nico con alta entregabilidad?","options":{"A":"Amazon SNS","B":"Amazon Connect","C":"Amazon SES","D":"Amazon Comprehend"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES) es una plataforma de correo electr\xf3nico escalable y rentable que admite tanto el env\xedo de correo electr\xf3nico transaccional para mensajes automatizados activados por aplicaciones como el env\xedo de correo electr\xf3nico masivo para campa\xf1as de marketing y boletines, proporcionando herramientas de entregabilidad, estad\xedsticas de env\xedo y gesti\xf3n de reputaci\xf3n a trav\xe9s de un servicio unificado.\\nAmazon SNS es un servicio de mensajer\xeda de publicaci\xf3n/suscripci\xf3n que se usa para entregar notificaciones a m\xfaltiples endpoints, como Lambda, SQS y HTTP, y aunque puede enviar correo electr\xf3nico a direcciones suscritas como parte de un flujo de notificaciones, no es una plataforma de correo electr\xf3nico creada espec\xedficamente con las capacidades de env\xedo masivo, gesti\xf3n de bajas y funciones de correo electr\xf3nico de marketing que proporciona SES. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube para gestionar interacciones de clientes por voz y chat, y es una plataforma de comunicaci\xf3n de servicio al cliente en lugar de un servicio de env\xedo de correo electr\xf3nico con fines transaccionales o de marketing. Amazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que extrae informaci\xf3n como sentimiento, entidades y frases clave de datos de texto, y es un servicio de an\xe1lisis sin capacidad para enviar o entregar mensajes de correo electr\xf3nico."},{"id":"q1084","question":"Una empresa recibe miles de formularios en papel escaneados cada d\xeda y necesita extraer autom\xe1ticamente texto, tablas y valores de campos espec\xedficos de estos documentos sin escribir c\xf3digo personalizado ni entrenar modelos de machine learning. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS debe usar la empresa?","options":{"A":"Amazon Comprehend","B":"Amazon Rekognition","C":"Amazon Transcribe","D":"Amazon Textract"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Amazon Textract es un servicio de machine learning que extrae autom\xe1ticamente texto impreso, escritura manuscrita, tablas estructuradas y datos de campos de formulario de documentos e im\xe1genes escaneados, yendo m\xe1s all\xe1 del simple reconocimiento de caracteres para comprender la estructura del documento y las relaciones entre campos, sin requerir c\xf3digo personalizado ni entrenamiento de modelos.\\nAmazon Comprehend es un servicio de procesamiento de lenguaje natural que analiza texto ya digitalizado para extraer entidades, sentimiento y frases clave, pero no extrae texto ni datos estructurados de im\xe1genes escaneadas o documentos PDF; procesa texto que ya se ha extra\xeddo en lugar de realizar la extracci\xf3n en s\xed. Amazon Rekognition es un servicio de machine learning para analizar im\xe1genes y videos y detectar objetos, escenas, actividades y rostros, y aunque puede detectar texto en im\xe1genes como una capacidad, no extrae tablas estructuradas ni campos de formulario de documentos comerciales escaneados de la forma en que lo hace Textract. Amazon Transcribe es un servicio de voz a texto que convierte grabaciones de audio y video en transcripciones de texto escrito, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para procesar lenguaje hablado en archivos de audio en lugar de extraer texto y datos estructurados de im\xe1genes de documentos escaneados."},{"id":"q1085","question":"Un equipo de desarrollo est\xe1 creando una aplicaci\xf3n m\xf3vil colaborativa donde varios usuarios necesitan ver actualizaciones en tiempo real de datos compartidos. El equipo quiere usar GraphQL y que el backend env\xede autom\xe1ticamente los cambios de datos a todos los clientes conectados. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona API GraphQL administradas con sincronizaci\xf3n de datos en tiempo real integrada?","options":{"A":"Amazon API Gateway","B":"Amazon Kinesis","C":"AWS AppSync","D":"AWS Amplify"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS AppSync es un servicio GraphQL totalmente administrado que simplifica la creaci\xf3n de API para aplicaciones web y m\xf3viles, con soporte integrado para actualizaciones de datos en tiempo real a trav\xe9s de suscripciones GraphQL que env\xedan autom\xe1ticamente los cambios a todos los clientes conectados cada vez que se modifican los datos subyacentes, lo que lo hace ideal para aplicaciones colaborativas y con actualizaci\xf3n en vivo.\\nAmazon API Gateway es un servicio totalmente administrado para crear API RESTful, HTTP y WebSocket, y aunque las API WebSocket admiten comunicaci\xf3n bidireccional, API Gateway no proporciona de forma nativa la gesti\xf3n de esquemas GraphQL, la configuraci\xf3n de resolutores ni el modelo de env\xedo de datos en tiempo real basado en suscripciones que ofrece AppSync para crear aplicaciones colaborativas. Amazon Kinesis es un servicio de streaming de datos en tiempo real para ingerir y procesar flujos de datos de alto rendimiento, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para crear pipelines de datos y an\xe1lisis en lugar de proporcionar una capa de API GraphQL con actualizaciones en tiempo real basadas en suscripciones para clientes de aplicaciones. AWS Amplify es un framework de desarrollo full-stack que proporciona bibliotecas del lado del cliente para conectar aplicaciones a las API de AppSync, pero Amplify es la capa de herramientas frontend en lugar del servicio GraphQL administrado que aloja la API, administra fuentes de datos y entrega suscripciones en tiempo real a los clientes."},{"id":"q1106","question":"Una empresa necesita un sistema de archivos totalmente administrado compatible con el protocolo Windows Server Message Block (SMB) para migrar cargas de trabajo basadas en Windows a AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon EFS","B":"Amazon FSx for Windows File Server","C":"Amazon S3","D":"AWS Storage Gateway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon FSx for Windows File Server proporciona un sistema de archivos nativo de Windows totalmente administrado, construido sobre Windows Server con soporte completo del protocolo SMB, integraci\xf3n con Active Directory y NTFS, lo que lo convierte en el servicio dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para migrar cargas de trabajo de archivos basadas en Windows a AWS.\\nAmazon EFS es un sistema de archivos NFS totalmente administrado dise\xf1ado para cargas de trabajo basadas en Linux y no admite el protocolo SMB que requieren las aplicaciones Windows. Amazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos al que se accede mediante API HTTP y no es un sistema de archivos que admita acceso mediante protocolo SMB para cargas de trabajo Windows. AWS Storage Gateway proporciona almacenamiento en la nube h\xedbrida que conecta entornos locales con los servicios de almacenamiento de AWS, pero es un dispositivo de puerta de enlace en lugar de un sistema de archivos nativo de la nube totalmente administrado."},{"id":"q1107","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona sistemas de archivos de terceros totalmente administrados y optimizados para cargas de trabajo como computaci\xf3n de alto rendimiento, machine learning y aplicaciones basadas en Windows?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon FSx","D":"Amazon EFS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon FSx proporciona sistemas de archivos totalmente administrados construidos sobre tecnolog\xedas populares de terceros y de c\xf3digo abierto, incluidas Lustre para computaci\xf3n de alto rendimiento, NetApp ONTAP para cargas de trabajo empresariales, OpenZFS y Windows File Server, cada uno optimizado para requisitos de carga de trabajo espec\xedficos.\\nAmazon EBS proporciona vol\xfamenes de almacenamiento a nivel de bloque asociados a instancias EC2 individuales y es un servicio de almacenamiento de bloques en lugar de un sistema de archivos administrado que admita m\xfaltiples tecnolog\xedas de sistemas de archivos. Amazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos dise\xf1ado para almacenar y recuperar cualquier cantidad de datos mediante API HTTP, y no proporciona acceso a sistemas de archivos a trav\xe9s de protocolos como NFS, SMB o Lustre. Amazon EFS es un sistema de archivos NFS administrado para cargas de trabajo Linux y solo admite el protocolo NFS, a diferencia de FSx, que ofrece m\xfaltiples tipos de sistemas de archivos para diferentes requisitos de carga de trabajo."},{"id":"q1108","question":"Una empresa quiere permitir que los empleados accedan de forma segura a aplicaciones web internas desde dispositivos personales no administrados sin instalar ning\xfan software en esos dispositivos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Amazon WorkSpaces","B":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web","C":"Amazon AppStream 2.0","D":"AWS Client VPN"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web proporciona un servicio totalmente administrado basado en navegador que permite a los usuarios acceder de forma segura a aplicaciones web internas y aplicaciones SaaS desde cualquier dispositivo con un navegador web, sin requerir la instalaci\xf3n de software ni un entorno de escritorio virtual completo.\\nAmazon WorkSpaces proporciona escritorios virtuales persistentes totalmente administrados en la nube, lo que es una soluci\xf3n m\xe1s pesada de lo necesario para el simple acceso a aplicaciones web y requiere que se instale una aplicaci\xf3n cliente en el dispositivo. Amazon AppStream 2.0 transmite aplicaciones de escritorio a los usuarios a trav\xe9s de un navegador web, pero est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para transmitir aplicaciones de escritorio completas en lugar de proporcionar acceso web ligero y seguro a aplicaciones web. AWS Client VPN proporciona acceso remoto seguro a una red de AWS desde dispositivos individuales, pero requiere que se instale y configure software cliente VPN en cada dispositivo."},{"id":"q1109","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona una soluci\xf3n totalmente administrada basada en navegador para acceder a aplicaciones web sin aprovisionar un escritorio virtual completo?","options":{"A":"Amazon AppStream 2.0","B":"Amazon WorkSpaces","C":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web","D":"AWS Direct Connect"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon WorkSpaces Web es un servicio basado en navegador, totalmente administrado y de bajo costo, dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para acceder de forma segura a aplicaciones web y sitios web sin la sobrecarga de aprovisionar y administrar infraestructura completa de escritorios virtuales.\\nAmazon AppStream 2.0 es un servicio de transmisi\xf3n de aplicaciones que entrega aplicaciones de escritorio a los usuarios a trav\xe9s de un navegador web, y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para transmitir aplicaciones que consumen muchos recursos en lugar de proporcionar acceso ligero solo por navegador a la web. Amazon WorkSpaces aprovisiona escritorios virtuales persistentes completos con un entorno de sistema operativo completo, lo que es m\xe1s de lo necesario para el acceso a aplicaciones solo por web y conlleva mayores costos y sobrecarga de administraci\xf3n. AWS Direct Connect establece una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, y es un servicio de red sin capacidad para proporcionar acceso a aplicaciones basado en navegador."},{"id":"q1110","question":"Una empresa necesita rastrear y administrar sus licencias de software de Microsoft y Oracle a medida que migra cargas de trabajo a AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ayuda a administrar el cumplimiento de licencias de software?","options":{"A":"AWS Systems Manager","B":"AWS License Manager","C":"AWS Config","D":"AWS Service Catalog"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS License Manager ayuda a los clientes a administrar los derechos de licencia de software de proveedores como Microsoft, Oracle y SAP en entornos de AWS y locales, proporcionando seguimiento y aplicaci\xf3n centralizados de las reglas de licenciamiento para evitar infracciones de cumplimiento.\\nAWS Systems Manager proporciona herramientas operativas para administrar instancias EC2 y servidores locales, incluidos parches, automatizaci\xf3n y almacenamiento de par\xe1metros, pero no rastrea ni aplica reglas de licenciamiento de software. AWS Config registra y eval\xfaa la configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS frente a reglas de cumplimiento, pero monitorea configuraciones de recursos en lugar de rastrear derechos y uso de licencias de software. AWS Service Catalog permite a las organizaciones crear y administrar cat\xe1logos aprobados de servicios de TI para su implementaci\xf3n en AWS, pero gobierna qu\xe9 productos se pueden implementar en lugar de rastrear el cumplimiento de licencias de software."},{"id":"q1111","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona una forma centralizada de administrar los derechos de licencia de software y establecer reglas de licenciamiento para evitar exceder los l\xedmites de licencia?","options":{"A":"AWS Marketplace","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"AWS License Manager","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS License Manager permite a los clientes definir reglas de licenciamiento basadas en sus acuerdos con proveedores de software, rastrear el uso de licencias en entornos de AWS y locales, y aplicar l\xedmites para evitar exceder los derechos, lo que reduce el riesgo de incumplimiento y de costos de licencias inesperados.\\nAWS Marketplace es un cat\xe1logo digital para encontrar, comprar e implementar software de terceros que se ejecuta en AWS y, aunque gestiona la adquisici\xf3n de nuevas licencias, no administra los derechos de licencia existentes ni aplica l\xedmites de uso en los entornos. AWS Organizations es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de cuentas para gobernar de forma centralizada varias cuentas de AWS mediante pol\xedticas y facturaci\xf3n consolidada, y no proporciona seguimiento ni aplicaci\xf3n de licencias de software. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas para mejorar la seguridad, el costo, el rendimiento y la tolerancia a fallos de un entorno de AWS, pero no rastrea ni administra el cumplimiento de licencias de software."},{"id":"q1112","question":"Una empresa ejecuta aplicaciones en contenedores en Amazon ECS y necesita un registro totalmente administrado para almacenar, administrar e implementar im\xe1genes de contenedores Docker. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon S3","B":"AWS CodeCommit","C":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR)","D":"AWS CodeArtifact"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry es un registro de contenedores Docker totalmente administrado que se integra de forma nativa con Amazon ECS y Amazon EKS, y proporciona almacenamiento seguro y escalable para im\xe1genes de contenedores con an\xe1lisis de vulnerabilidades y pol\xedticas de ciclo de vida integrados.\\nAmazon S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento de objetos que puede almacenar cualquier tipo de archivo, pero no es un registro de contenedores y no proporciona la API de registro de Docker, la administraci\xf3n de capas de im\xe1genes ni la integraci\xf3n nativa con servicios de contenedores que ofrece ECR. AWS CodeCommit es un servicio de repositorio Git administrado para almacenar y versionar c\xf3digo fuente, no im\xe1genes de contenedores, y no proporciona funcionalidad de registro de Docker. AWS CodeArtifact es un repositorio de artefactos administrado para almacenar y compartir paquetes de software como paquetes npm, Maven y Python, pero no admite im\xe1genes de contenedores Docker."},{"id":"q1113","question":"Una empresa quiere configurar una soluci\xf3n de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres de bajo costo que pueda recuperar autom\xe1ticamente servidores locales en AWS en caso de falla del sitio. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"AWS Backup","B":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery","C":"Amazon S3 Cross-Region Replication","D":"AWS DataSync"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Elastic Disaster Recovery replica continuamente servidores de origen en AWS mediante agentes ligeros, mantiene un \xe1rea de preparaci\xf3n de bajo costo y puede lanzar r\xe1pidamente instancias de recuperaci\xf3n totalmente aprovisionadas en minutos cuando ocurre un desastre, lo que proporciona una soluci\xf3n de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres automatizada y rentable.\\nAWS Backup es un servicio de backup centralizado que automatiza y administra backups en los servicios de AWS, pero crea backups en un punto en el tiempo para la recuperaci\xf3n de datos en lugar de proporcionar replicaci\xf3n continua y recuperaci\xf3n r\xe1pida de servidores para escenarios de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres. Amazon S3 Cross-Region Replication copia autom\xe1ticamente objetos de S3 a un bucket en una Regi\xf3n diferente para la durabilidad de los datos, pero solo replica datos de S3 y no puede recuperar entornos de servidores completos, incluidos sistemas operativos y aplicaciones. AWS DataSync automatiza la transferencia de datos entre el almacenamiento local y los servicios de almacenamiento de AWS, pero es una herramienta de movimiento de datos en lugar de un servicio de recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres que pueda lanzar instancias de recuperaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q1114","question":"Una empresa con m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS dentro de una organizaci\xf3n desea compartir subredes de VPC y AWS Transit Gateways entre cuentas sin duplicar recursos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS lo permite?","options":{"A":"AWS Organizations","B":"VPC Peering","C":"AWS Resource Access Manager (AWS RAM)","D":"AWS Control Tower"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Resource Access Manager permite a los clientes compartir de forma segura recursos de AWS como subredes de VPC, Transit Gateways y reglas de Route 53 Resolver entre cuentas de AWS dentro de una organizaci\xf3n, eliminando la necesidad de crear recursos duplicados en cada cuenta.\\nAWS Organizations proporciona administraci\xf3n centralizada y gobernanza de m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS mediante pol\xedticas de control de servicios y facturaci\xf3n consolidada, pero no proporciona por s\xed mismo el mecanismo para compartir recursos espec\xedficos como subredes o Transit Gateways entre cuentas. VPC Peering crea una conexi\xf3n de red entre dos VPC que permite que el tr\xe1fico fluya entre ellas, pero es una conexi\xf3n punto a punto y no comparte recursos como subredes o Transit Gateways entre cuentas. AWS Control Tower automatiza la configuraci\xf3n y la gobernanza de un entorno de AWS multi-cuenta seguro basado en las mejores pr\xe1cticas, pero es un servicio de gobernanza de cuentas que no proporciona capacidades de uso compartido de recursos."},{"id":"q1141","question":"Una empresa desea proporcionar a los empleados un asistente impulsado por IA generativa que pueda responder preguntas sobre los datos de la empresa, generar contenido y automatizar tareas utilizando fuentes de conocimiento empresarial. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xedan usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon SageMaker AI","B":"Amazon Q","C":"Amazon Lex","D":"Amazon Kendra"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon Q es un asistente impulsado por IA generativa dise\xf1ado para empresas que se puede personalizar con datos empresariales para responder preguntas, generar contenido, realizar acciones y automatizar flujos de trabajo, proporcionando un asistente de IA listo para usar sin requerir experiencia en machine learning.\\nAmazon SageMaker AI es una plataforma totalmente administrada para construir, entrenar e implementar modelos de machine learning personalizados, lo que requiere experiencia en ciencia de datos y est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para el desarrollo personalizado de ML en lugar de un asistente conversacional de IA listo para usar. Amazon Lex es un servicio para construir chatbots conversacionales usando interfaces de voz y texto, pero requiere que los desarrolladores definan intenciones y flujos de conversaci\xf3n en lugar de proporcionar un asistente de IA generativa preconstruido que funcione con conocimiento empresarial. Amazon Kendra es un servicio de b\xfasqueda empresarial inteligente que indexa documentos y devuelve respuestas relevantes a consultas de b\xfasqueda, pero es un motor de b\xfasqueda en lugar de un asistente de IA generativa que pueda crear contenido y automatizar tareas."},{"id":"q006","question":"Ha configurado la facturaci\xf3n consolidada para varias cuentas de AWS. Una de las cuentas ha comprado varias reserved instances por 3 a\xf1os. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes afirmaciones es verdadera respecto a este escenario?","options":{"A":"Los descuentos de Reserved Instance solo se pueden compartir con la cuenta maestra","B":"Todas las cuentas pueden recibir el beneficio de costo por hora de las Reserved Instances","C":"Las instancias compradas tendr\xe1n un mejor rendimiento que las instancias On-Demand","D":"No hay beneficios de costo por usar facturaci\xf3n consolidada; es solo con fines informativos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Con la facturaci\xf3n consolidada, los descuentos de Reserved Instance se comparten autom\xe1ticamente entre todas las cuentas de la organizaci\xf3n, por lo que cualquier cuenta puede beneficiarse del ahorro de costo por hora.\\nQue los descuentos de Reserved Instance se apliquen solo a la cuenta maestra es incorrecto porque la facturaci\xf3n consolidada espec\xedficamente comparte los beneficios de RI entre todas las cuentas miembro de la organizaci\xf3n, no solo la cuenta que los compr\xf3. La afirmaci\xf3n de que las reserved instances tienen mejor rendimiento que las instancias On-Demand es falsa porque ambas se ejecutan sobre la misma infraestructura subyacente de AWS, y el modelo de precios de Reserved Instance es un compromiso de facturaci\xf3n en lugar de un nivel de hardware diferente. La facturaci\xf3n consolidada s\xed proporciona beneficios de costo reales m\xe1s all\xe1 de fines puramente organizativos, combinando el uso para descuentos por volumen y compartiendo los ahorros de Reserved Instance entre cuentas, por lo que la afirmaci\xf3n de que es solo con fines informativos es incorrecta."},{"id":"q009","question":"Una empresa tiene un plan AWS Enterprise Support. Desea orientaci\xf3n r\xe1pida y eficiente con sus consultas de facturaci\xf3n y de cuenta. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes deber\xeda usar la empresa?","options":{"A":"AWS Health Dashboard","B":"AWS Support Concierge","C":"AWS Customer Service","D":"AWS Business Support"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Support Concierge, disponible exclusivamente en el plan Enterprise Support, es un equipo dedicado para consultas de facturaci\xf3n y de cuenta, que proporciona asistencia r\xe1pida y personalizada.\\nAWS Health Dashboard proporciona alertas en tiempo real y personalizadas sobre eventos de servicios de AWS que pueden afectar sus recursos, y es una herramienta de monitoreo de estado de servicios en lugar de un contacto de soporte de facturaci\xf3n o de cuenta. AWS Customer Service maneja consultas generales de cuenta y facturaci\xf3n para todos los clientes, pero no proporciona el soporte dedicado y personalizado que el equipo Concierge ofrece a los titulares del plan Enterprise. AWS Business Support proporciona acceso 24/7 a Cloud Support Engineers para problemas t\xe9cnicos e incluye Infrastructure Event Management por una tarifa adicional, pero no incluye un equipo Concierge dedicado para orientaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n y de cuenta."},{"id":"q016","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes ayuda a un cliente a ver la actividad de facturaci\xf3n de Amazon EC2 del mes pasado?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Systems Manager","D":"AWS Cost & Usage Reports"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Cost and Usage Reports proporcionan el desglose m\xe1s detallado y completo del gasto real de AWS, incluida la actividad de facturaci\xf3n de EC2, por per\xedodo de tiempo y recurso.\\nAWS Budgets le permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costo y uso y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, y es una herramienta de gobernanza de costos y alertas en lugar de un servicio para revisar la actividad de facturaci\xf3n hist\xf3rica. AWS Pricing Calculator estima costos futuros de los servicios de AWS antes de aprovisionarlos, y no proporciona informes sobre la actividad de facturaci\xf3n hist\xf3rica. AWS Systems Manager proporciona herramientas operativas para administrar y automatizar tareas en la infraestructura de AWS, y no tiene conexi\xf3n con informes de facturaci\xf3n ni an\xe1lisis de costos."},{"id":"q017","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 obtiene al configurar la facturaci\xf3n consolidada para cinco cuentas de AWS diferentes bajo otra cuenta maestra?","options":{"A":"Los costos de los servicios de AWS se reducir\xe1n a la mitad del precio original","B":"La funci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n consolidada es solo con fines organizativos","C":"Cada cuenta de AWS obtiene descuentos por volumen","D":"Cada cuenta de AWS obtiene cinco veces la capacidad de servicios del nivel gratuito"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La facturaci\xf3n consolidada agrega el uso de todas las cuentas, lo que puede llevar el uso combinado a niveles de volumen superiores y desbloquear descuentos por volumen que las cuentas individuales no alcanzar\xedan por s\xed solas.\\nLos costos de los servicios de AWS no se reducen a la mitad del precio original con la facturaci\xf3n consolidada. Los descuentos por volumen se aplican de forma incremental seg\xfan los niveles de uso combinado y var\xedan seg\xfan el servicio, en lugar de aplicar una reducci\xf3n fija del 50 %. La facturaci\xf3n consolidada proporciona beneficios de costo reales m\xe1s all\xe1 de fines puramente organizativos mediante descuentos por volumen y uso compartido de Reserved Instance, por lo que la afirmaci\xf3n de que es puramente organizativa es incorrecta. Cada cuenta de AWS no recibe cinco veces la capacidad de servicios del nivel gratuito con la facturaci\xf3n consolidada. El nivel gratuito se aplica por organizaci\xf3n en lugar de por cuenta, y la facturaci\xf3n consolidada no multiplica las asignaciones del nivel gratuito."},{"id":"q020","question":"Una empresa emergente opera con fondos limitados y est\xe1 extremadamente preocupada por los sobrecostos. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones se puede usar para notificar a la empresa cuando su factura mensual de AWS supere los $2000?","options":{"A":"Configurar una alarma de facturaci\xf3n de CloudWatch que active una notificaci\xf3n de SNS cuando se supere el umbral","B":"Configurar Amazon Simple Email Service para enviar alertas de facturaci\xf3n a su direcci\xf3n de correo electr\xf3nico a diario","C":"Configurar AWS Budgets para alertar a la empresa cuando se supere el umbral","D":"Configurar AWS CloudTrail para eliminar autom\xe1ticamente todos los recursos de AWS cuando se supere el umbral","E":"Configurar Amazon Connect para alertar a la empresa cuando se supere el umbral"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Una alarma de facturaci\xf3n de CloudWatch activa una notificaci\xf3n de SNS cuando los costos superan un umbral establecido, y AWS Budgets le permite establecer umbrales de costo personalizados con alertas autom\xe1ticas. Ambas son herramientas nativas de AWS para el monitoreo de costos.\\nAmazon Simple Email Service es un servicio de env\xedo de correo electr\xf3nico para comunicaciones transaccionales y de marketing, y aunque puede enviar correos electr\xf3nicos, no monitorea de forma nativa la facturaci\xf3n de AWS ni activa alertas basadas en umbrales de costo. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta con fines de auditor\xeda, y no tiene capacidad para monitorear umbrales de facturaci\xf3n ni eliminar recursos en respuesta a eventos de costo. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto en la nube para administrar comunicaciones con clientes mediante voz y chat, y no tiene conexi\xf3n con el monitoreo de facturaci\xf3n de AWS ni con alertas de umbrales de costo."},{"id":"q028","question":"Desea ejecutar una aplicaci\xf3n de cuestionario por solo un d\xeda (sin interrupciones), \xbfqu\xe9 opci\xf3n de compra de Amazon EC2 deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"Reserved instances","B":"Spot instances","C":"Dedicated instances","D":"On-demand instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las instancias On-Demand le permiten ejecutar cargas de trabajo durante cualquier duraci\xf3n sin compromisos a largo plazo ni interrupciones, lo que las hace ideales para tareas cortas y \xfanicas en las que se requiere disponibilidad continua.\\nLas Reserved Instances requieren un compromiso m\xednimo de un a\xf1o y son rentables para cargas de trabajo de estado estable que se ejecutan continuamente durante ese per\xedodo, lo que las hace poco adecuadas para una tarea de un solo d\xeda en la que el plazo del compromiso superar\xeda con creces el uso real. Las Spot Instances usan capacidad sobrante de AWS con un descuento significativo, pero AWS puede interrumpirlas en cualquier momento, lo que las hace inadecuadas para una carga de trabajo que debe ejecutarse durante un d\xeda completo sin interrupciones. Las Dedicated Instances se ejecutan en hardware dedicado a un solo cliente por motivos de cumplimiento o licenciamiento, y aunque proporcionan aislamiento, son m\xe1s costosas que las instancias On-Demand est\xe1ndar y no ofrecen ninguna ventaja para una tarea simple de un d\xeda."},{"id":"q029","question":"Trabajas en un proyecto que implica crear miniaturas de millones de im\xe1genes. El tiempo de actividad constante no es un problema y no se requiere procesamiento continuo. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n de compra de EC2 ser\xeda la m\xe1s rentable?","options":{"A":"Reserved Instances","B":"On-demand Instances","C":"Dedicated Instances","D":"Spot Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spot Instances utiliza capacidad de AWS no utilizada con descuentos muy altos y es perfecta para trabajos por lotes tolerantes a fallos, como el procesamiento de im\xe1genes, donde las interrupciones son aceptables y el tiempo de actividad no es cr\xedtico.\\nReserved Instances ofrece un descuento significativo frente al precio de On-Demand para cargas de trabajo estables mediante un compromiso de uno o tres a\xf1os, pero no es rentable para cargas de trabajo por lotes que se ejecutan solo peri\xf3dicamente y pueden tolerar interrupciones. On-Demand Instances cobra la tarifa horaria completa sin descuento, lo que las hace significativamente m\xe1s costosas que Spot Instances para una carga de trabajo de procesamiento de im\xe1genes por lotes donde el momento es flexible. Dedicated Instances se ejecuta en hardware dedicado a un \xfanico cliente y est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para requisitos de cumplimiento y aislamiento de licencias, no para la optimizaci\xf3n de costos de cargas de trabajo de procesamiento por lotes."},{"id":"q034","question":"Como parte del plan de soporte Enterprise, \xbfqui\xe9n es el punto de contacto principal para las necesidades de soporte continuo?","options":{"A":"Usuario de AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","B":"Ingeniero de Infrastructure Event Management (IEM)","C":"AWS Consulting Partners","D":"AWS Technical Account Manager (TAM)"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El AWS Technical Account Manager est\xe1 incluido en el plan de soporte Enterprise como el contacto dedicado principal que proporciona orientaci\xf3n proactiva y coordina el soporte de AWS en toda tu cuenta.\\nUn usuario de AWS IAM es una identidad dentro de una cuenta de AWS que se utiliza para autenticar y autorizar el acceso a los servicios de AWS, y no es un contacto de soporte ni un rol dentro de la organizaci\xf3n de AWS Support. Un ingeniero de Infrastructure Event Management proporciona orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica y soporte en tiempo real durante eventos planificados espec\xedficos bajo el plan de soporte Enterprise, pero se involucra para eventos espec\xedficos en lugar de desempe\xf1arse como el contacto de soporte principal continuo. AWS Consulting Partners son empresas de servicios profesionales en AWS Partner Network que ayudan a los clientes a dise\xf1ar y construir en AWS, y son organizaciones externas en lugar de personal de AWS Support asignado a una cuenta."},{"id":"q035","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puedes ver la distribuci\xf3n del gasto de AWS en una de tus cuentas de AWS?","options":{"A":"Usando la consola de Amazon VPC","B":"Contactando al equipo de AWS Support","C":"Usando AWS Cost Explorer","D":"Contactando al equipo de AWS Finance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Cost Explorer proporciona gr\xe1ficos e informes interactivos que visualizan tus patrones de gasto de AWS a lo largo del tiempo, desglosados por servicio, cuenta o etiqueta.\\nLa consola de Amazon VPC es una herramienta de red para administrar nubes privadas virtuales, subredes, puertas de enlace y configuraci\xf3n de red, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con la informaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n ni con la distribuci\xf3n del gasto. Contactar al equipo de AWS Support proporciona orientaci\xf3n t\xe9cnica y resoluci\xf3n de problemas, y no es un mecanismo de autoservicio para ver la distribuci\xf3n del gasto de AWS en tu cuenta. Contactar al equipo de AWS Finance no es un canal compatible orientado al cliente para acceder a informaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n y gasto, la cual est\xe1 disponible directamente a trav\xe9s de la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS mediante Cost Explorer y los paneles de facturaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q049","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 proporciona AWS para desplegar tecnolog\xedas populares como IBM MQ en AWS con la menor cantidad de esfuerzo y tiempo?","options":{"A":"Amazon Aurora","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS Quick Start reference deployments","D":"AWS OpsWorks"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Quick Start reference deployments proporciona plantillas automatizadas de referencia que despliegan soluciones de TI populares en AWS en minutos, lo que brinda a los administradores una v\xeda r\xe1pida hacia entornos listos para producci\xf3n construidos sobre las mejores pr\xe1cticas de AWS.\\nAmazon Aurora es una base de datos relacional totalmente administrada compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL, y es un servicio de base de datos sin capacidad para desplegar o aprovisionar tecnolog\xedas o soluciones de terceros. Amazon CloudWatch recopila m\xe9tricas, registros y datos de eventos de servicios y recursos de AWS para proporcionar monitoreo operativo y alertas, y es un servicio de monitoreo sin capacidad de despliegue ni aprovisionamiento. AWS OpsWorks (obsoleto) era un servicio de administraci\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n que utilizaba Chef y Puppet para automatizar la configuraci\xf3n de servidores, y aunque pod\xeda automatizar algunas tareas de infraestructura, no es un recurso de despliegue preconstruido para desplegar r\xe1pidamente tecnolog\xedas populares de terceros con un esfuerzo m\xednimo. Nota: AWS OpsWorks qued\xf3 obsoleto en 2024."},{"id":"q050","question":"Una organizaci\xf3n ha decidido comprar una Amazon EC2 Reserved Instance (RI) por tres a\xf1os para reducir costos. Es posible que las cargas de trabajo de la aplicaci\xf3n cambien durante el per\xedodo de reserva. \xbfCu\xe1l es el tipo de EC2 Reserved Instance (RI) que permitir\xe1 a la empresa intercambiar la instancia reservada comprada por otra instancia reservada con mayor capacidad de c\xf3mputo si lo necesita?","options":{"A":"Scheduled Reserved Instance","B":"Standard Reserved Instance","C":"On-Demand Instance","D":"Convertible Reserved Instance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Convertible Reserved Instances permite intercambiar tu reserva por otra Convertible RI con atributos diferentes, como el tipo de instancia, el sistema operativo o la tenencia, durante el plazo, lo que proporciona flexibilidad cuando cambian los requisitos de las cargas de trabajo.\\nScheduled Reserved Instances era un tipo anterior de RI que permit\xeda reservar capacidad para ventanas de tiempo recurrentes espec\xedficas y no admit\xeda intercambios de atributos durante el plazo. Nota: AWS ha dejado obsoletas las Scheduled Reserved Instances. Standard Reserved Instances ofrece el descuento m\xe1s alto, pero est\xe1 fijada a una familia de instancias espec\xedfica y no se puede intercambiar por una configuraci\xf3n diferente durante el per\xedodo de reserva. On-Demand Instances es un modelo de compra de EC2 independiente que cobra por capacidad de c\xf3mputo por hora o por segundo sin compromiso, y no es un tipo de Reserved Instance en absoluto."},{"id":"q052","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes modelos de precios de Reserved Instance (RI) proporciona el mayor ahorro promedio en comparaci\xf3n con el precio de On-Demand?","options":{"A":"Precio de Standard RI a un a\xf1o, sin pago inicial","B":"Precio de Convertible RI a un a\xf1o, todo por adelantado","C":"Precio de Standard RI a tres a\xf1os, todo por adelantado","D":"Precio de Convertible RI a tres a\xf1os, sin pago inicial"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las Standard RI a tres a\xf1os con todo por adelantado combinan el plazo de compromiso m\xe1s largo con el pago total anticipado y el tipo Standard, lo que en conjunto produce el descuento m\xe1s profundo en comparaci\xf3n con el precio de On-Demand.\\nUna Standard RI a un a\xf1o sin pago inicial ofrece el costo inicial m\xe1s bajo, pero proporciona el menor descuento entre las opciones enumeradas, ya que tanto el plazo corto como la ausencia de pago inicial reducen el ahorro general. Una Convertible RI a un a\xf1o con todo por adelantado se beneficia del pago total anticipado, pero est\xe1 limitada por el plazo m\xe1s corto y el tipo Convertible, que ofrece menos descuento que las Standard RI a cambio de flexibilidad para intercambiar atributos. Una Convertible RI a tres a\xf1os sin pago inicial se beneficia del plazo de compromiso largo, pero sacrifica profundidad de descuento tanto por la ausencia de pago inicial como por la designaci\xf3n de tipo Convertible, lo que la hace menos rentable que la equivalente Standard RI con todo por adelantado."},{"id":"q059","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones describe MEJOR el modelo de precios de AWS?","options":{"A":"Plazo fijo","B":"Pago por uso","C":"Colocation","D":"Planificado","E":"Costo variable"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"El precio de AWS es pago por uso y costo variable. No se requieren plazos fijos, no hay tarifas de colocation y no hay planes obligatorios por adelantado.\\nEl precio de plazo fijo requiere un compromiso con una duraci\xf3n de contrato establecida, lo que es lo opuesto al modelo de pago por uso de AWS, donde el uso puede escalar hacia arriba o hacia abajo en cualquier momento sin obligaciones contractuales. Colocation se refiere al alojamiento de servidores propiedad del cliente en un centro de datos de terceros, lo cual es un modelo on-premises en lugar de un principio de precios de la nube. El precio planificado implica calendarios de uso y costos predeterminados, lo que contradice el modelo de AWS donde los clientes pagan seg\xfan el consumo real sin planificaci\xf3n anticipada obligatoria."},{"id":"q061","question":"Un cliente utiliza varias cuentas de AWS con facturaci\xf3n separada. \xbfC\xf3mo puede el cliente aprovechar los descuentos por volumen con un impacto m\xednimo en los recursos de AWS?","options":{"A":"Crear una cuenta global de AWS y mover todos los recursos de AWS a esa cuenta","B":"Suscribirse a tres a\xf1os de precios de Reserved Instance por adelantado","C":"Usar la funci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n consolidada de AWS Organizations","D":"Suscribirse al plan de soporte AWS Enterprise para obtener descuentos por volumen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La facturaci\xf3n consolidada de AWS Organizations agrega el uso de todas las cuentas miembro, lo que califica a la organizaci\xf3n para niveles de precios por volumen sin requerir la consolidaci\xf3n de recursos ni compromisos de RI por adelantado.\\nCrear una cuenta global y mover todos los recursos es un cambio de alto impacto que eliminar\xeda el aislamiento a nivel de cuenta, los l\xedmites de seguridad y la gobernanza, y no es necesario porque la facturaci\xf3n consolidada obtiene los mismos descuentos por volumen sin migraci\xf3n. El precio de Reserved Instance bloquea la capacidad de c\xf3mputo en un tipo de instancia y plazo espec\xedficos, lo que reduce el costo para cargas de trabajo predecibles, pero no proporciona descuentos por volumen entre cuentas. El plan Enterprise Support agrega Technical Account Managers y soporte premium, pero los niveles de soporte no otorgan descuentos por volumen en el uso de servicios."},{"id":"q062","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 modelo de precios de Amazon EC2 ofrece el descuento M\xc1S significativo en comparaci\xf3n con las instancias On-Demand?","options":{"A":"Partial Upfront Reserved Instances para un plazo de 1 a\xf1o","B":"All Upfront Reserved Instances para un plazo de 1 a\xf1o","C":"All Upfront Reserved Instances para un plazo de 3 a\xf1os","D":"No Upfront Reserved Instances para un plazo de 3 a\xf1os"},"correct":"C","explanation":"All Upfront Reserved Instances para un plazo de 3 a\xf1os proporcionan el m\xe1ximo descuento posible de EC2 porque combinan el plazo de compromiso m\xe1s largo con el pago completo por adelantado, lo que en conjunto genera los mayores ahorros en comparaci\xf3n con el precio On-Demand.\\nPartial Upfront Reserved Instances para un plazo de 1 a\xf1o ofrecen un descuento moderado, pero el plazo m\xe1s corto y el pago parcial por adelantado reducen los ahorros totales en comparaci\xf3n con la opci\xf3n All Upfront de 3 a\xf1os. All Upfront Reserved Instances para un plazo de 1 a\xf1o ofrecen un buen descuento mediante el pago completo por adelantado, pero est\xe1n limitadas por el plazo de compromiso m\xe1s corto, lo que genera menos ahorros totales que un compromiso de 3 a\xf1os. No Upfront Reserved Instances para un plazo de 3 a\xf1os se benefician del plazo de compromiso largo, pero renuncian al descuento adicional que conlleva el pago por adelantado, lo que las hace menos rentables que las opciones All Upfront."},{"id":"q066","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 beneficios se incluyen con el plan AWS Business Support?","options":{"A":"Asistencia 24/7 mediante chat en vivo o llamada telef\xf3nica","B":"Soporte de un AWS Technical Account Manager dedicado","C":"Un n\xfamero ilimitado de casos y contactos","D":"Tiempo de respuesta de 15 minutos para casos de interrupci\xf3n de sistemas de producci\xf3n","E":"Revisiones operativas anuales con AWS Solutions Architects"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS Business Support incluye acceso 24/7 a Cloud Support Engineers por tel\xe9fono, correo electr\xf3nico y chat, adem\xe1s de un n\xfamero ilimitado de casos y contactos de soporte.\\nEl soporte de un Technical Account Manager dedicado que brinda orientaci\xf3n proactiva y act\xfaa como contacto principal est\xe1 disponible exclusivamente con el plan AWS Enterprise Support y no se incluye en Business Support. Un tiempo de respuesta de 15 minutos para casos de interrupci\xf3n de sistemas de producci\xf3n es una caracter\xedstica de Enterprise Support. Business Support proporciona un SLA de respuesta de una hora para casos de ca\xedda de sistemas de producci\xf3n, no de 15 minutos. Las revisiones operativas anuales con AWS Solutions Architects son un beneficio de Enterprise Support y no se incluyen en el plan Business Support."},{"id":"q068","question":"Un usuario debe cumplir requisitos de cumplimiento y licenciamiento de software que establecen que una carga de trabajo debe alojarse en un servidor f\xedsico. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n de precios de instancias de Amazon EC2 cumplir\xe1 estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"Dedicated Hosts","B":"Dedicated Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Hosts proporcionan un servidor f\xedsico dedicado por completo a tu uso con visibilidad total de sockets, n\xfacleos e ID de host, lo cual es necesario para el licenciamiento de software vinculado al servidor, como BYOL, y para ciertos marcos de cumplimiento que exigen aislamiento f\xedsico.\\nDedicated Instances se ejecutan en hardware dedicado a un \xfanico cliente, pero no proporcionan visibilidad del host f\xedsico espec\xedfico, lo cual es necesario para la mayor\xeda de los modelos de licenciamiento por socket o por n\xfacleo y no satisface todos los requisitos de cumplimiento de aislamiento de servidor f\xedsico. Spot Instances usan capacidad sobrante de AWS con un descuento significativo, pero AWS puede interrumpirlas en cualquier momento, lo que las hace inadecuadas para cargas de trabajo con requisitos de cumplimiento que exigen alojamiento en servidores f\xedsicos consistente. Reserved Instances son un compromiso de precios para capacidad de EC2 que puede reducir costos durante un plazo de 1 o 3 a\xf1os, pero se ejecutan en infraestructura compartida y no proporcionan aislamiento de servidor f\xedsico ni visibilidad a nivel de host requeridos para el licenciamiento BYOL."},{"id":"q069","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los modelos de precios de Reserved Instance (RI) puede cambiar los atributos de la RI siempre que el intercambio d\xe9 como resultado la creaci\xf3n de RI de igual o mayor valor?","options":{"A":"Dedicated RIs","B":"Scheduled RIs","C":"Convertible RIs","D":"Standard RIs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las Convertible RIs permiten intercambiarlas por otras Convertible RIs de igual o mayor valor, lo que te permite cambiar la familia de instancias, el sistema operativo, la tenencia o la opci\xf3n de pago durante el plazo. Las Standard RIs no se pueden intercambiar.\\nLas Dedicated RIs son un atributo de tenencia m\xe1s que un tipo de RI separado, que permite que la instancia se ejecute en hardware dedicado a un \xfanico cliente, y esta designaci\xf3n no afecta si la RI se puede intercambiar. Las Scheduled RIs fueron un tipo de RI anterior que permit\xeda reservar capacidad para ventanas de tiempo recurrentes espec\xedficas y no admit\xeda intercambios de atributos durante el plazo. Nota: AWS ha dejado obsoletas las Scheduled RIs. Las Standard RIs ofrecen el mayor descuento entre los tipos de RI, pero est\xe1n bloqueadas a una familia de instancias, tama\xf1o, sistema operativo y tenencia espec\xedficos, y no se pueden intercambiar por una configuraci\xf3n diferente una vez compradas."},{"id":"q071","question":"\xbfCu\xe1ndo deber\xeda una empresa considerar usar Amazon EC2 Spot Instances?","options":{"A":"Para aplicaciones que no son de producci\xf3n","B":"Para cargas de trabajo con estado","C":"Para aplicaciones que no pueden tener interrupciones","D":"Para aplicaciones flexibles y tolerantes a fallos","E":"Para aplicaciones de bases de datos sensibles"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Las Spot Instances son mejores para aplicaciones que no son de producci\xf3n que pueden tolerar interrupciones y para cargas de trabajo flexibles y tolerantes a fallos que pueden guardar puntos de control y reanudarse. Las cargas de trabajo con estado, con alto tiempo de actividad o de bases de datos sensibles no deber\xedan usar Spot.\\nLas cargas de trabajo con estado que mantienen conexiones persistentes o estado de la aplicaci\xf3n no son adecuadas para Spot Instances porque una interrupci\xf3n interrumpir\xeda ese estado, lo que requerir\xeda mecanismos de recuperaci\xf3n complejos. Las aplicaciones que no pueden tener interrupciones requieren disponibilidad garantizada que las Spot Instances no pueden proporcionar, ya que AWS puede reclamar la capacidad Spot en cualquier momento con un aviso de dos minutos. Las aplicaciones de bases de datos sensibles requieren disponibilidad constante, integridad de datos y rendimiento predecible, lo que las hace poco adecuadas para el riesgo de interrupci\xf3n inherente al precio de Spot Instance."},{"id":"q074","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el plan de AWS Support m\xednimo que incluye Infrastructure Event Management sin costos adicionales?","options":{"A":"Enterprise","B":"Business","C":"Developer","D":"Basic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Infrastructure Event Management se incluye sin costo adicional solo con el plan AWS Enterprise Support, proporcionando orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica y soporte en tiempo real durante eventos planificados, como lanzamientos de productos y picos de tr\xe1fico.\\nLos clientes de Business Support pueden acceder a Infrastructure Event Management, pero deben pagar una tarifa adicional por cada participaci\xf3n en un evento, por lo que no se incluye como beneficio est\xe1ndar sin costo adicional. Developer Support proporciona acceso por correo electr\xf3nico en horario laboral a Cloud Support Engineers para preguntas t\xe9cnicas generales y no incluye Infrastructure Event Management a ning\xfan precio. Basic Support proporciona acceso a documentaci\xf3n, libros blancos y las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor, y no incluye ninguna forma de soporte de gesti\xf3n de eventos."},{"id":"q080","question":"Un usuario quiere orientaci\xf3n sobre posibles ahorros al migrar de on-premises a AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 herramienta es adecuada para este escenario?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Pricing Calculator","D":"AWS Well-Architected Tool"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator permite a los usuarios modelar configuraciones de servicios de AWS y estimar costos, lo que permite comparar los costos de la infraestructura on-premises con el gasto proyectado en AWS para comprender los posibles ahorros de la migraci\xf3n.\\nAWS Budgets te permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costos y uso y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, y es una herramienta de monitoreo de costos para cargas de trabajo activas en AWS en lugar de una herramienta de estimaci\xf3n de ahorros previa a la migraci\xf3n. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas del gasto y los patrones de uso existentes en AWS, y requiere uso activo de AWS para generar datos en lugar de proporcionar comparaciones de costos previas a la migraci\xf3n. AWS Well-Architected Tool ayuda a revisar cargas de trabajo frente a los seis pilares del Well-Architected Framework para identificar riesgos arquitect\xf3nicos y mejoras, y no es una herramienta de comparaci\xf3n de costos ni de estimaci\xf3n de ahorros."},{"id":"q082","question":"El uso de \xbfqu\xe9 caracter\xedstica o servicio de AWS permite a las empresas realizar seguimiento y categorizar el gasto a un nivel detallado?","options":{"A":"Cost allocation tags","B":"Consolidated billing","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Marketplace"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cost allocation tags son etiquetas de clave-valor adjuntas a los recursos de AWS que te permiten organizar y realizar seguimiento del gasto a un nivel granular por proyecto, equipo o entorno en Cost Explorer y en los informes de facturaci\xf3n.\\nConsolidated billing combina el uso de todas las cuentas miembro de una AWS Organization para producir una \xfanica factura y calificar para descuentos por volumen, pero no proporciona por s\xed mismo la categorizaci\xf3n granular a nivel de recurso que las etiquetas permiten para el seguimiento detallado de costos. AWS Budgets te permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costos y uso y recibir alertas cuando se superan, y es una herramienta de gobernanza y alertas de costos en lugar de un mecanismo para categorizar y realizar seguimiento del gasto a un nivel detallado de recursos. AWS Marketplace es un cat\xe1logo digital para descubrir y comprar soluciones de software de terceros que se ejecutan en AWS, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con la categorizaci\xf3n de costos ni con el an\xe1lisis de gastos."},{"id":"q083","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes inspecciona entornos de AWS para encontrar oportunidades que permitan ahorrar dinero a los usuarios y tambi\xe9n mejorar el rendimiento del sistema?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"Facturaci\xf3n consolidada","D":"Facturaci\xf3n detallada"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor escanea tu entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones en optimizaci\xf3n de costos, rendimiento, seguridad, tolerancia a fallos y l\xedmites de servicio, lo que ayuda a ahorrar dinero y mejorar la arquitectura.\\nAWS Cost Explorer es una herramienta para visualizar y analizar patrones de gasto de AWS hist\xf3ricos y previstos, pero no inspecciona entornos para identificar oportunidades de mejora de rendimiento ni recomendaciones arquitect\xf3nicas. La facturaci\xf3n consolidada es una caracter\xedstica de AWS Organizations que combina los cargos de varias cuentas en una sola factura para simplificar el pago, y no tiene capacidad de inspecci\xf3n de entornos ni de recomendaciones. La facturaci\xf3n detallada proporciona informes granulares de partidas de uso y cargos de AWS con fines de an\xe1lisis de costos, y no identifica oportunidades para ahorrar dinero ni mejorar el rendimiento del sistema."},{"id":"q092","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 plan de soporte de AWS incluye un Technical Account Manager dedicado?","options":{"A":"Developer","B":"Enterprise","C":"Business","D":"Basic"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un Technical Account Manager dedicado que proporciona orientaci\xf3n proactiva y act\xfaa como contacto principal est\xe1 disponible exclusivamente con el plan AWS Enterprise Support.\\nDeveloper Support proporciona acceso por correo electr\xf3nico en horario comercial a Cloud Support Engineers para preguntas t\xe9cnicas generales, y no incluye un Technical Account Manager dedicado ni ning\xfan recurso de gesti\xf3n de cuentas dedicado. Business Support proporciona acceso 24/7 a Cloud Support Engineers por tel\xe9fono, correo electr\xf3nico y chat, adem\xe1s de comprobaciones completas de Trusted Advisor, y aunque ofrece recursos de soporte sustanciales, no incluye un Technical Account Manager dedicado. Basic Support proporciona acceso a documentaci\xf3n, libros blancos y las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor, e incluye personal de soporte ni gesti\xf3n de cuentas dedicados."},{"id":"q097","question":"Un cliente desea dise\xf1ar y construir una nueva carga de trabajo en AWS Cloud, pero no cuenta con experiencia t\xe9cnica interna en software relacionado con AWS. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes programas de AWS puede aprovechar un cliente para lograr ese resultado?","options":{"A":"AWS Partner Network Technology Partners","B":"AWS Marketplace","C":"AWS Partner Network Consulting Partners","D":"AWS Service Catalog"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los AWS Partner Network Consulting Partners son empresas de servicios profesionales que ayudan a los clientes a dise\xf1ar, construir, migrar y administrar cargas de trabajo en AWS. Los Technology Partners proporcionan software y herramientas; el Marketplace es para comprar software.\\nLos AWS Partner Network Technology Partners desarrollan software, herramientas y servicios que complementan AWS, y son empresas de productos en lugar de empresas de servicios profesionales que ayudan a los clientes directamente a dise\xf1ar y construir cargas de trabajo desde cero. AWS Marketplace es un cat\xe1logo digital donde los clientes pueden descubrir y comprar soluciones de software de terceros, y es una plataforma de adquisici\xf3n en lugar de un recurso de servicios profesionales para experiencia t\xe9cnica y desarrollo pr\xe1ctico de cargas de trabajo. AWS Service Catalog permite a las organizaciones crear y administrar cat\xe1logos aprobados de servicios de TI para uso interno y aprovisionamiento de autoservicio, y es una herramienta de gobernanza interna en lugar de un programa de servicios profesionales."},{"id":"q100","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio debe usar un cliente para consolidar y administrar de forma centralizada varias cuentas de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS IAM","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"AWS Schema Conversion Tool","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Organizations permite crear una jerarqu\xeda de cuentas de AWS, consolidar la facturaci\xf3n entre cuentas y aplicar pol\xedticas de gobernanza mediante Service Control Policies de forma centralizada desde una cuenta de administraci\xf3n.\\nAWS IAM administra usuarios, roles y permisos dentro de una sola cuenta y no puede administrar ni consolidar varias cuentas. AWS Schema Conversion Tool es una herramienta de migraci\xf3n de bases de datos para convertir esquemas de bases de datos entre diferentes motores, no est\xe1 relacionada con la administraci\xf3n de cuentas. AWS Config rastrea y registra cambios de configuraci\xf3n en los recursos dentro de una cuenta, pero no proporciona consolidaci\xf3n ni gobernanza de varias cuentas."},{"id":"q101","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puede una empresa reducir su costo total de propiedad (TCO) usando AWS?","options":{"A":"Minimizando grandes gastos de capital","B":"Al no tener responsabilidad por los costos de licencias de terceros","C":"Al no tener gastos operativos","D":"Al hacer que AWS administre las aplicaciones"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS traslada el gasto en infraestructura de grandes gastos de capital iniciales a gastos operativos continuos m\xe1s peque\xf1os, reduciendo directamente el TCO al eliminar la necesidad de aprovisionar en exceso para la demanda m\xe1xima.\\nNo tener responsabilidad por los costos de licencias de terceros no es un beneficio general de AWS, ya que los clientes siguen administrando y pagando sus propias licencias de software cuando ejecutan aplicaciones en AWS, a menos que usen licencias proporcionadas por AWS mediante servicios como Amazon RDS. AWS no elimina los gastos operativos. Los clientes contin\xfaan pagando costos continuos basados en el uso por los servicios de AWS que consumen, los cuales son gastos operativos en lugar de gastos de capital. AWS no administra las aplicaciones de los clientes por defecto. La administraci\xf3n a nivel de aplicaci\xf3n, incluidos el c\xf3digo, la configuraci\xf3n y las actualizaciones, sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida."},{"id":"q102","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 opciones ofrece AWS a los clientes que quieren aprender sobre seguridad en la nube en un entorno con instructor?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Online Tech Talks","C":"AWS Blog","D":"AWS Forums","E":"AWS Classroom Training"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"AWS Online Tech Talks son seminarios web en vivo y bajo demanda que incluyen sesiones interactivas de preguntas y respuestas, y AWS Classroom Training ofrece cursos dirigidos por instructores certificados, ambos proporcionan aprendizaje estructurado en un entorno dirigido por instructor o en vivo.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor es una herramienta automatizada que proporciona recomendaciones en tiempo real sobre costos, seguridad, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, no es un recurso de aprendizaje o capacitaci\xf3n. AWS Blog es un canal de publicaci\xf3n de autoservicio para noticias y contenido t\xe9cnico que los clientes leen de forma independiente, no un formato dirigido por instructor. AWS Forums es una plataforma de debate impulsada por la comunidad donde los clientes se ayudan entre s\xed con preguntas, no un entorno de aprendizaje estructurado dirigido por instructor."},{"id":"q106","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes son ventajas de la facturaci\xf3n consolidada de AWS?","options":{"A":"La capacidad de recibir una sola factura para varias cuentas","B":"El aumento predeterminado de los l\xedmites de servicio en todas las cuentas","C":"Un descuento fijo en la factura mensual","D":"Posibles descuentos por volumen, ya que el uso de todas las cuentas se combina","E":"La extensi\xf3n autom\xe1tica del plan de soporte de AWS de la cuenta principal a todas las cuentas"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"La facturaci\xf3n consolidada produce una \xfanica factura combinada para todas las cuentas en una AWS Organization, y agrupa el uso de todas las cuentas para calificar para niveles de precios por volumen.\\nLos l\xedmites de servicio no aumentan autom\xe1ticamente para todas las cuentas con la facturaci\xf3n consolidada. Los aumentos de l\xedmites de servicio deben solicitarse individualmente a trav\xe9s de AWS Support por servicio y por regi\xf3n. La facturaci\xf3n consolidada no proporciona un descuento fijo en la factura mensual. Los descuentos por volumen se aplican de forma progresiva seg\xfan los niveles de uso combinado y var\xedan seg\xfan el servicio en lugar de aplicar una reducci\xf3n porcentual uniforme. El plan de soporte de AWS de la cuenta principal no se extiende autom\xe1ticamente a todas las cuentas miembro. Cada cuenta dentro de una AWS Organization debe mantener su propio plan de soporte, y los costos y beneficios del plan de soporte siguen siendo espec\xedficos de cada cuenta."},{"id":"q110","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el plan de soporte de AWS M\xcdNIMO que permite un tiempo de respuesta objetivo de una hora para los casos de soporte?","options":{"A":"Enterprise","B":"Business","C":"Developer","D":"Basic"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Business Support garantiza un tiempo de respuesta de una hora para casos de ca\xedda de sistemas en producci\xf3n y proporciona acceso 24/7 por tel\xe9fono, correo electr\xf3nico y chat. Developer Support ofrece solo correo electr\xf3nico en horario comercial; Basic no tiene soporte para casos; Enterprise ofrece una respuesta m\xe1s r\xe1pida, pero a un costo mayor.\\nEnterprise Support proporciona tiempos de respuesta m\xe1s r\xe1pidos, incluido un SLA de 15 minutos para fallas cr\xedticas del sistema para el negocio, y tambi\xe9n cumple con el SLA de una hora para ca\xedda de sistemas en producci\xf3n, pero es un plan de mayor costo que Business Support y, por lo tanto, no es el m\xednimo requerido. Developer Support proporciona acceso por correo electr\xf3nico en horario comercial a Cloud Support Engineers con tiempos de respuesta medidos en horas en lugar de una hora, y no incluye acceso 24/7 ni un SLA de una hora para ca\xedda de sistemas en producci\xf3n. Basic Support proporciona acceso a documentaci\xf3n y a las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor, pero no incluye soporte t\xe9cnico para casos ni SLA de tiempo de respuesta definidos."},{"id":"q112","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 equipo de AWS ayuda a los clientes a acelerar la adopci\xf3n de la nube mediante compromisos pagados en cualquiera de varias \xe1reas de pr\xe1ctica especializadas?","options":{"A":"AWS Enterprise Support","B":"AWS Solutions Architects","C":"AWS Professional Services","D":"AWS Account Managers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Professional Services es un equipo global de expertos que ayuda a los clientes a alcanzar los resultados empresariales deseados en AWS mediante compromisos pagados en \xe1reas de pr\xe1ctica especializadas que incluyen migraci\xf3n, seguridad y an\xe1lisis de datos.\\nAWS Enterprise Support es un plan de soporte pagado que proporciona acceso a un Technical Account Manager y acceso 24/7 a ingenieros s\xe9nior de soporte en la nube, pero es un nivel de soporte en lugar de un equipo de consultor\xeda que trabaja en proyectos de \xe1reas de pr\xe1ctica especializadas. AWS Solutions Architects asesora a los clientes sobre el dise\xf1o de soluciones bien arquitectadas y est\xe1 disponible a trav\xe9s de varios programas de AWS, pero proporciona orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica en lugar de liderar compromisos de consultor\xeda pagados en \xe1reas de pr\xe1ctica especializadas. AWS Account Managers gestiona la relaci\xf3n comercial general entre AWS y un cliente, pero se encarga de actividades de cuenta y ventas en lugar de entregar compromisos de consultor\xeda t\xe9cnica pr\xe1ctica."},{"id":"q113","question":"Una empresa necesita acceso telef\xf3nico, por correo electr\xf3nico y por chat 24/7 con un tiempo de respuesta inferior a 1 hora si un sistema de producci\xf3n tiene una interrupci\xf3n del servicio. \xbfQu\xe9 plan de AWS Support cumple estos requisitos al MENOR costo?","options":{"A":"Basic","B":"Developer","C":"Business","D":"Enterprise"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Business Support es el plan de menor costo que proporciona acceso telef\xf3nico, por correo electr\xf3nico y por chat 24/7 a Cloud Support Engineers con un SLA de respuesta de una hora para interrupciones de sistemas de producci\xf3n.\\nBasic Support proporciona acceso a documentaci\xf3n, documentos t\xe9cnicos y las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor, pero no incluye soporte t\xe9cnico por casos ni acceso a Cloud Support Engineers. Developer Support proporciona acceso por correo electr\xf3nico a Cloud Support Engineers solo durante el horario comercial y no incluye acceso telef\xf3nico ni por chat 24/7 ni un SLA de respuesta de una hora para sistemas de producci\xf3n. Enterprise Support tambi\xe9n proporciona acceso 24/7 con tiempos de respuesta m\xe1s r\xe1pidos, incluido un SLA de 15 minutos para fallas de sistemas cr\xedticos para el negocio, pero es un plan de mayor costo que Business Support y, por lo tanto, no es el m\xednimo requerido para cumplir estos requisitos espec\xedficos."},{"id":"q115","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo proporciona AWS Trusted Advisor orientaci\xf3n a los usuarios de AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Identifica vulnerabilidades de software en aplicaciones que se ejecutan en AWS","B":"Proporciona una lista de recomendaciones de optimizaci\xf3n de costos basadas en el uso actual de AWS","C":"Detecta posibles vulnerabilidades de seguridad causadas por la configuraci\xf3n de permisos en los recursos de la cuenta","D":"Corrige autom\xe1ticamente posibles problemas de seguridad causados por la configuraci\xf3n de permisos en los recursos de la cuenta","E":"Proporciona alertas proactivas cada vez que una instancia de Amazon EC2 ha sido comprometida"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Trusted Advisor analiza tu entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones de optimizaci\xf3n de costos basadas en los patrones de uso actuales, y se\xf1ala posibles problemas de seguridad causados por configuraciones de IAM o de recursos demasiado permisivas. No corrige autom\xe1ticamente los problemas ni alerta sobre instancias comprometidas.\\nIdentificar vulnerabilidades de software en aplicaciones que se ejecutan en AWS es funci\xf3n de Amazon Inspector, que analiza instancias de EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de CVE y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, en lugar de Trusted Advisor, que verifica contra las mejores pr\xe1cticas de AWS. Corregir autom\xe1ticamente posibles problemas de seguridad no es una funci\xf3n de Trusted Advisor, que proporciona recomendaciones de asesoramiento y se\xf1ala inquietudes, pero no realiza acciones de correcci\xf3n automatizadas en tus recursos. Proporcionar alertas proactivas cuando una instancia de EC2 ha sido comprometida es funci\xf3n de Amazon GuardDuty, que utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico e inteligencia de amenazas para detectar comportamiento sospechoso, en lugar de Trusted Advisor, que realiza comprobaciones peri\xf3dicas contra criterios predefinidos de mejores pr\xe1cticas."},{"id":"q123","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una ventaja de la facturaci\xf3n consolidada en AWS?","options":{"A":"Calificaci\xf3n para precios por volumen","B":"Permisos de acceso compartidos","C":"Varias facturas por cuenta","D":"Elimina la necesidad de etiquetado"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La facturaci\xf3n consolidada combina el uso de todas las cuentas miembro de una AWS Organization, lo que califica a la organizaci\xf3n para niveles de precios por volumen que las cuentas individuales podr\xedan no alcanzar por s\xed solas.\\nLos permisos de acceso compartidos no son un beneficio de la facturaci\xf3n consolidada. El control de acceso se gestiona mediante pol\xedticas de IAM, Service Control Policies y pol\xedticas basadas en recursos, ninguna de las cuales se ve afectada por habilitar la facturaci\xf3n consolidada. La facturaci\xf3n consolidada genera una sola factura para todas las cuentas en lugar de varias facturas por cuenta, por lo que la opci\xf3n que describe varias facturas por cuenta es lo opuesto a lo que proporciona la facturaci\xf3n consolidada. La facturaci\xf3n consolidada no elimina la necesidad de etiquetado. Las etiquetas de asignaci\xf3n de costos siguen siendo esenciales para atribuir costos a proyectos, equipos o entornos espec\xedficos incluso cuando el uso se agrega en una sola factura consolidada."},{"id":"q125","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes modelos de precios de Amazon EC2 permite a los clientes usar licencias de software existentes vinculadas al servidor?","options":{"A":"Spot Instances","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"Dedicated Hosts","D":"On-Demand Instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Hosts proporcionan un servidor f\xedsico dedicado a tu uso, lo cual es necesario para usar licencias de software existentes por socket, por n\xfacleo o por VM. Spot, Reserved y On-Demand se ejecutan en hardware compartido o no especificado y no admiten la vinculaci\xf3n de licencias a hosts f\xedsicos espec\xedficos.\\nSpot Instances utilizan capacidad disponible de AWS y se ejecutan en infraestructura compartida sin visibilidad del host f\xedsico subyacente, lo que las hace totalmente inadecuadas para modelos de licencias que requieren vinculaci\xf3n a un servidor f\xedsico espec\xedfico. Reserved Instances son un compromiso de precios para capacidad de EC2 que reduce los costos durante un plazo de 1 o 3 a\xf1os, pero se ejecutan en infraestructura compartida est\xe1ndar y no proporcionan la visibilidad a nivel de host requerida para la mayor\xeda de los escenarios de licencias BYOL. On-Demand Instances cobran la tarifa horaria completa sin compromiso a largo plazo y, al igual que Reserved Instances, se ejecutan en infraestructura compartida sin la visibilidad y dedicaci\xf3n del servidor f\xedsico requeridas para licencias de software vinculadas al servidor."},{"id":"q131","question":"Los usuarios de AWS Enterprise Support tienen acceso a \xbfqu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica que no est\xe1 disponible para los usuarios con otros planes de AWS Support?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"Caso de AWS Support","C":"AWS Support Concierge","D":"Amazon Connect"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El equipo AWS Support Concierge, expertos dedicados en facturaci\xf3n y cuentas que proporcionan orientaci\xf3n personalizada, est\xe1 disponible exclusivamente para clientes de Enterprise Support.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor est\xe1 disponible en forma limitada con Basic y Developer Support, que proporcionan las siete comprobaciones principales, y de forma completa con Business y Enterprise Support, por lo que no es exclusivo de los usuarios de Enterprise. Los casos de AWS Support pueden ser abiertos por clientes con planes Developer, Business y Enterprise Support, y no son exclusivos de los usuarios de Enterprise. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto en la nube para gestionar comunicaciones con clientes mediante voz y chat, y es un producto de AWS que los clientes implementan en lugar de un programa de soporte o recurso exclusivo de cualquier nivel de soporte."},{"id":"q133","question":"Al realizar un an\xe1lisis de costos que respalde el aislamiento f\xedsico de una carga de trabajo de un cliente, \xbfqu\xe9 modelo de alojamiento de c\xf3mputo debe tenerse en cuenta en el Total Cost of Ownership (TCO)?","options":{"A":"Dedicated Hosts","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"On-Demand Instances","D":"No Upfront Reserved Instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Hosts proporcionan aislamiento f\xedsico de la carga de trabajo en un servidor dedicado por completo a un cliente, lo cual es necesario para ciertos mandatos de cumplimiento o modelos de licencias. Este modelo de alojamiento tiene un costo mayor que debe tenerse en cuenta en los c\xe1lculos de TCO.\\nReserved Instances son un compromiso de precios que reduce los costos de EC2 durante un plazo de 1 o 3 a\xf1os y, aunque reducen los costos continuos, se ejecutan en infraestructura compartida est\xe1ndar y no proporcionan el aislamiento f\xedsico que debe considerar un an\xe1lisis de TCO para cargas de trabajo exigidas por cumplimiento. On-Demand Instances cobran la tarifa horaria completa en infraestructura compartida sin compromiso a largo plazo y, aunque son \xfatiles para cargas de trabajo variables, no proporcionan aislamiento f\xedsico y tienen costos por unidad m\xe1s altos que no reflejar\xedan un c\xe1lculo de TCO centrado en el cumplimiento. No Upfront Reserved Instances proporcionan un descuento sobre On-Demand mediante un compromiso a largo plazo sin pago inicial, pero al igual que las Reserved Instances est\xe1ndar se ejecutan en infraestructura compartida y no tienen en cuenta los costos de aislamiento f\xedsico requeridos por los mandatos de cumplimiento."},{"id":"q135","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el plan de AWS Support M\xcdNIMO que proporciona soporte t\xe9cnico mediante llamadas telef\xf3nicas?","options":{"A":"Enterprise","B":"Business","C":"Developer","D":"Basic"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Business Support es el plan m\xednimo que incluye soporte telef\xf3nico 24/7 con un equipo dedicado de Cloud Support Engineers. Developer Support solo proporciona correo electr\xf3nico durante el horario comercial; Basic no tiene soporte t\xe9cnico telef\xf3nico ni por casos.\\nEnterprise Support proporciona acceso telef\xf3nico 24/7 y tiempos de respuesta m\xe1s r\xe1pidos, incluido un SLA de 15 minutos para fallas de sistemas cr\xedticos para el negocio, pero es un plan de mayor costo que Business Support y, por lo tanto, no es el m\xednimo requerido para acceder al soporte telef\xf3nico. Developer Support proporciona acceso por correo electr\xf3nico a Cloud Support Engineers durante el horario comercial, pero no incluye ning\xfan soporte telef\xf3nico en ning\xfan momento. Basic Support proporciona acceso a documentaci\xf3n, documentos t\xe9cnicos y las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor, y no incluye soporte telef\xf3nico ni asistencia t\xe9cnica por casos."},{"id":"q136","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 modelo de precios de instancias de Amazon EC2 puede ofrecer descuentos de hasta el 90 %?","options":{"A":"Instancias reservadas","B":"Bajo demanda","C":"Hosts dedicados","D":"Instancias Spot"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las Instancias Spot utilizan capacidad no utilizada de AWS y pueden ofrecer descuentos de hasta el 90 % en comparaci\xf3n con los precios bajo demanda. Las Instancias reservadas ofrecen hasta aproximadamente un 72 % de descuento; bajo demanda es el precio de referencia; los Hosts dedicados suelen costar m\xe1s que las opciones est\xe1ndar.\\nLas Instancias reservadas ofrecen ahorros de hasta aproximadamente el 72 % en comparaci\xf3n con los precios bajo demanda a cambio de un compromiso de 1 o 3 a\xf1os, lo cual es un descuento significativo pero sustancialmente menor que el hasta 90 % disponible mediante Instancias Spot. Las Instancias bajo demanda se cobran a la tarifa est\xe1ndar de AWS sin descuento, ya que son el modelo de precios de referencia con el que se miden todas las dem\xe1s opciones de compra. Los Hosts dedicados tienen un precio superior al de los precios est\xe1ndar de EC2 para tener en cuenta la infraestructura f\xedsica dedicada, lo que los hace m\xe1s caros en lugar de m\xe1s econ\xf3micos que las opciones de compra est\xe1ndar."},{"id":"q139","question":"Una empresa planea migrar de las instalaciones locales a la nube de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 herramienta o servicio de AWS proporciona informes detallados sobre el ahorro de costos estimado despu\xe9s de la migraci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"AWS Pricing Calculator","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Migration Hub"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator permite a los clientes modelar configuraciones de servicios de AWS y estimar costos, lo que permite compararlos con el gasto en infraestructura local para producir informes detallados sobre los ahorros proyectados de la migraci\xf3n.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas del gasto y los patrones de uso existentes en AWS con capacidad de previsi\xf3n, y requiere datos de uso activos de AWS para generar an\xe1lisis en lugar de proporcionar estimaciones de costos previas a la migraci\xf3n. AWS Budgets permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costos y uso, y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, y es una herramienta de monitoreo y gobierno de costos en lugar de un servicio de estimaci\xf3n de ahorros de migraci\xf3n. AWS Migration Hub proporciona una ubicaci\xf3n centralizada para realizar un seguimiento del progreso de las migraciones de aplicaciones, y es un servicio de seguimiento de migraciones en lugar de una herramienta de estimaci\xf3n de costos."},{"id":"q142","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puede un usuario de AWS con un plan de AWS Basic Support obtener asistencia t\xe9cnica de AWS?","options":{"A":"Ingenieros s\xe9nior de soporte de AWS","B":"Administradores t\xe9cnicos de cuentas de AWS","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Foros de debate de AWS"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Basic Support incluye acceso a la documentaci\xf3n de AWS, documentos t\xe9cnicos y Foros de debate de la comunidad, donde otros usuarios y el personal de AWS pueden responder preguntas. Los TAM son exclusivos de Enterprise; los Ingenieros s\xe9nior de soporte son exclusivos de Business y Enterprise; Trusted Advisor se limita a comprobaciones b\xe1sicas en el plan gratuito.\\nLos Ingenieros s\xe9nior de soporte de AWS son Cloud Support Engineers que proporcionan asistencia t\xe9cnica y est\xe1n disponibles para los clientes de soporte Business y Enterprise, y no est\xe1n disponibles para los titulares del plan Basic Support. Los Administradores t\xe9cnicos de cuentas de AWS son profesionales de soporte dedicados exclusivos de los clientes de Enterprise Support, y no est\xe1n incluidos en ning\xfan plan de soporte de nivel inferior. AWS Trusted Advisor est\xe1 disponible para los clientes de Basic Support, pero solo para las siete comprobaciones principales, en lugar del conjunto completo de comprobaciones que requiere Business o Enterprise Support."},{"id":"q145","question":"\xbfEn qu\xe9 escenario se deben utilizar las Instancias Spot de Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"Una empresa quiere trasladar su sitio web principal a AWS desde un servidor web local","B":"Una empresa tiene varios servicios de aplicaciones cuyo Acuerdo de nivel de servicio (SLA) requiere un tiempo de actividad del 99,999 %","C":"La base de datos heredada muy utilizada de una empresa se ejecuta actualmente en las instalaciones locales","D":"Una empresa tiene varios trabajos poco frecuentes e interrumpibles que actualmente utilizan Instancias bajo demanda"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las Instancias Spot son ideales para cargas de trabajo flexibles e interrumpibles que pueden tolerar ser interrumpidas cuando se recupera la capacidad. Los servicios de alta disponibilidad, los sitios web de producci\xf3n y las bases de datos heredadas requieren una disponibilidad constante que las Instancias Spot no pueden garantizar.\\nTrasladar un sitio web de producci\xf3n principal a AWS requiere disponibilidad y tiempo de actividad constantes, ya que cualquier interrupci\xf3n por una recuperaci\xf3n de Instancia Spot resultar\xeda en tiempo de inactividad para los usuarios finales. Una aplicaci\xf3n con un SLA de tiempo de actividad del 99,999 % requiere que las instancias est\xe9n siempre disponibles, lo que las Instancias Spot no pueden garantizar dado que AWS puede interrumpirlas en cualquier momento con un aviso de dos minutos. Una base de datos heredada muy utilizada que se ejecuta en las instalaciones locales requiere un rendimiento constante y disponibilidad de datos que las interrupciones de Instancias Spot comprometer\xedan, lo que hace que esta carga de trabajo no sea adecuada para el modelo de compra Spot."},{"id":"q152","question":"Un beneficio del precio bajo demanda de Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) es:","options":{"A":"La capacidad de pujar por un costo por hora m\xe1s bajo","B":"Pagar una tarifa diaria independientemente del tiempo utilizado","C":"Pagar solo por el tiempo utilizado","D":"Pagar por adelantado las instancias y pagar una tarifa por hora m\xe1s baja"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El precio bajo demanda significa que solo paga por el tiempo de c\xf3mputo que realmente utiliza, sin costos iniciales ni compromisos a largo plazo. La puja es una caracter\xedstica de las Instancias Spot; la facturaci\xf3n por tarifa diaria y el pago por adelantado son caracter\xedsticas de otros modelos de precios.\\nLa capacidad de pujar por un costo por hora m\xe1s bajo es una caracter\xedstica de las Instancias Spot, que se fijan en funci\xf3n de la capacidad EC2 no utilizada, no una caracter\xedstica del precio bajo demanda que tiene una tarifa fija establecida por AWS. Pagar una tarifa diaria independientemente del tiempo utilizado describe un modelo de precios diarios fijos, que no es c\xf3mo funciona EC2 bajo demanda. El precio bajo demanda se cobra por segundo con un m\xednimo de un minuto. Pagar por adelantado las instancias para recibir una tarifa por hora m\xe1s baja describe el precio de Instancias reservadas, donde los clientes se comprometen a uno o tres a\xf1os a cambio de tarifas con descuento, no el modelo bajo demanda."},{"id":"q157","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 migrando una aplicaci\xf3n que ejecuta cargas de trabajo no interrumpibles durante un per\xedodo de tres a\xf1os. \xbfQu\xe9 construcci\xf3n de precios proporcionar\xeda la soluci\xf3n M\xc1S rentable?","options":{"A":"Instancias Spot de Amazon EC2","B":"Instancias dedicadas de Amazon EC2","C":"Instancias bajo demanda de Amazon EC2","D":"Instancias reservadas de Amazon EC2"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Para cargas de trabajo en estado estable y no interrumpibles que se ejecutan durante un per\xedodo de tres a\xf1os, las Instancias reservadas ofrecen el mayor descuento en comparaci\xf3n con bajo demanda. Las Instancias Spot pueden interrumpirse; bajo demanda no tiene descuento; las Instancias dedicadas son para aislamiento, no para ahorro de costos.\\nAWS puede interrumpir las Instancias Spot de Amazon EC2 con un aviso de dos minutos cuando se recupera la capacidad no utilizada, lo que las hace inadecuadas para cargas de trabajo no interrumpibles que deben permanecer disponibles en todo momento. Las Instancias dedicadas de Amazon EC2 se ejecutan en hardware dedicado a un solo cliente por motivos de cumplimiento y aislamiento de licencias, y tienen un precio superior al de las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2 en lugar de proporcionar ahorros de costos. Las Instancias bajo demanda de Amazon EC2 cobran la tarifa por hora completa sin descuento, lo que las convierte en la opci\xf3n m\xe1s costosa para una carga de trabajo en estado estable que se ejecuta continuamente durante tres a\xf1os."},{"id":"q160","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puede una cuenta de AWS utilizar Instancias reservadas de otra cuenta de AWS?","options":{"A":"Mediante el uso de Instancias dedicadas de Amazon EC2","B":"Mediante el uso de la facturaci\xf3n consolidada de AWS Organizations","C":"Mediante el uso de la herramienta AWS Cost Explorer","D":"Mediante el uso de AWS Budgets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La facturaci\xf3n consolidada de AWS Organizations agrupa el uso de Instancias reservadas entre todas las cuentas miembro, de modo que las RI no utilizadas de una cuenta pueden aplicarse autom\xe1ticamente al uso coincidente de otra, lo que reduce los costos en toda la organizaci\xf3n.\\nLas Instancias dedicadas de Amazon EC2 se ejecutan en hardware dedicado a un solo cliente por motivos de aislamiento y licencias, y no tienen ning\xfan mecanismo para compartir descuentos de Instancias reservadas entre cuentas. La herramienta AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaci\xf3n y an\xe1lisis del gasto y los patrones de uso de AWS, y aunque puede mostrar datos de utilizaci\xf3n de RI, no puede por s\xed misma habilitar ni facilitar el uso compartido de RI entre cuentas. AWS Budgets permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costos y uso, y recibir alertas, y es una herramienta de monitoreo y gobierno sin capacidad para compartir beneficios de Instancias reservadas entre cuentas."},{"id":"q165","question":"\xbfA d\xf3nde debe acudir una empresa para buscar listados de software de proveedores de software independientes para encontrar, probar, comprar e implementar software que se ejecuta en AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Marketplace","B":"AWS Service Catalog","C":"AWS Artifact","D":"Amazon CloudSearch"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Marketplace es el cat\xe1logo digital curado donde los proveedores de software independientes publican sus productos, lo que permite a los clientes encontrar, probar, comprar e implementar software que se ejecuta en AWS.\\nAWS Service Catalog permite a las organizaciones crear y gestionar portafolios aprobados de productos de TI para su implementaci\xf3n interna, pero no aloja listados de software de terceros de proveedores independientes. AWS Artifact es un portal de autoservicio para acceder a informes de cumplimiento y certificaciones de seguridad de AWS, no una plataforma de descubrimiento o adquisici\xf3n de software. Amazon CloudSearch es un servicio de b\xfasqueda administrado para agregar funcionalidad de b\xfasqueda a las aplicaciones y no tiene ning\xfan rol en los listados de proveedores de software ni en la adquisici\xf3n."},{"id":"q167","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 modelo de precios de Amazon EC2 se ajusta seg\xfan la oferta y la demanda de instancias EC2?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Convertible Reserved Instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El precio de Spot Instance fluct\xfaa en tiempo real seg\xfan la oferta disponible de capacidad EC2 sobrante y la demanda de los clientes, lo que puede dar lugar a precios hasta un 90 % inferiores a On-Demand.\\nLas On-Demand Instances se cobran a una tarifa horaria fija establecida por AWS seg\xfan el tipo de instancia y la regi\xf3n, y el precio no var\xeda seg\xfan la oferta y la demanda ni cambia en tiempo real. Las Reserved Instances permiten a los clientes comprometerse con un tipo de instancia espec\xedfico durante uno o tres a\xf1os a cambio de una tarifa con descuento fija, y el precio reservado se fija en el momento de la compra y no fluct\xfaa seg\xfan la oferta y la demanda. Las Convertible Reserved Instances son un tipo de Reserved Instance que se puede intercambiar por otras Convertible RIs con atributos diferentes y, al igual que las Standard RIs, tienen un precio con descuento fijo que no se ajusta seg\xfan la oferta y la demanda."},{"id":"q176","question":"En comparaci\xf3n con los costos en centros de datos tradicionales y virtualizados, AWS tiene:","options":{"A":"Mayores costos variables y mayores costos iniciales","B":"Costos de uso fijos y menores costos iniciales","C":"Menores costos variables y mayores costos iniciales","D":"Menores costos variables y menores costos iniciales"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS aprovecha las econom\xedas de escala y un modelo de infraestructura compartida para ofrecer costos variables por unidad m\xe1s bajos que los centros de datos tradicionales o virtualizados, y su modelo de pago por uso elimina los grandes costos de capital iniciales, lo que da como resultado tanto menores costos variables como menores costos iniciales.\\nMayores costos variables y mayores costos iniciales describe lo opuesto al modelo de AWS, que espec\xedficamente reduce ambos costos variables mediante econom\xedas de escala y elimina el gasto de capital inicial mediante el pago por uso. Costos de uso fijos y menores costos iniciales describe parcialmente a AWS en el sentido de que los costos iniciales son menores, pero los costos de AWS son variables en lugar de fijos, ya que escalan directamente con el uso y el consumo de recursos. Menores costos variables y mayores costos iniciales describe parcialmente el modelo de centro de datos tradicional, donde la compra de hardware requiere una gran inversi\xf3n inicial, pero en AWS no hay costos iniciales significativos, ya que la infraestructura se consume como servicio."},{"id":"q180","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo deber\xeda un cliente pronosticar los costos futuros de ejecutar una nueva aplicaci\xf3n web?","options":{"A":"Amazon Aurora Backtrack","B":"Amazon CloudWatch Billing Alarms","C":"AWS Pricing Calculator","D":"AWS Cost and Usage Report"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator permite a los clientes modelar configuraciones de servicios de AWS y estimar costos futuros antes de implementarlas, lo que la convierte en la herramienta dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para pronosticar el costo de una nueva aplicaci\xf3n.\\nAmazon Aurora Backtrack es una caracter\xedstica que permite rebobinar una base de datos Aurora a un punto anterior en el tiempo sin restaurar desde una copia de seguridad, y es una funci\xf3n de recuperaci\xf3n de bases de datos sin relaci\xf3n con el pron\xf3stico de costos. Amazon CloudWatch Billing Alarms activa notificaciones cuando el gasto real de AWS supera un umbral definido, y es una herramienta de alerta de costos para cargas de trabajo activas, no una herramienta de pron\xf3stico para nuevas aplicaciones. AWS Cost and Usage Reports proporciona el desglose m\xe1s detallado de los cargos reales de AWS ya incurridos, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de costos hist\xf3ricos, no un servicio de estimaci\xf3n de costos futuros."},{"id":"q193","question":"Una empresa global con una gran cantidad de cuentas de AWS busca una forma de gestionar de manera centralizada la facturaci\xf3n y las pol\xedticas de seguridad en todas las cuentas. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS les ayudar\xe1 a cumplir estos objetivos?","options":{"A":"AWS Organizations","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"IAM User Groups","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Organizations proporciona gesti\xf3n centralizada de m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS, incluida la facturaci\xf3n consolidada y la capacidad de aplicar Service Control Policies como barreras de seguridad en todas las cuentas.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones en materia de seguridad, costos, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, pero opera a nivel de cuenta individual y no proporciona gesti\xf3n centralizada de facturaci\xf3n ni pol\xedticas de seguridad en varias cuentas. IAM User Groups es una colecci\xf3n de usuarios de IAM que comparten las mismas pol\xedticas adjuntas dentro de una \xfanica cuenta de AWS, y opera a nivel de cuenta en lugar de proporcionar gesti\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n o pol\xedticas entre cuentas. AWS Config rastrea y registra continuamente la configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento y detectar cambios, pero opera a nivel de cuenta y no proporciona gesti\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n entre cuentas ni aplicaci\xf3n de pol\xedticas de seguridad en toda una organizaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q198","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes afirmaciones NO es correcta con respecto a las instancias Amazon EC2 On-Demand?","options":{"A":"Debe pagar una tarifa de inicio al lanzar una nueva instancia por primera vez","B":"Las instancias On-Demand siguen el modelo de precios de pago por uso de AWS","C":"Con las instancias On-Demand, no se necesitan compromisos a largo plazo ni pagos por adelantado","D":"Cuando se usan instancias Linux On-Demand, se le cobra por segundo seg\xfan una tarifa horaria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Las instancias EC2 On-Demand no tienen tarifa de inicio. Simplemente paga por el tiempo de c\xf3mputo que utiliza, sin cargos por adelantado. Las instancias On-Demand siguen el precio de pago por uso, no requieren compromisos y las instancias Linux se facturan por segundo.\\nNo hay una tarifa de inicio al lanzar una instancia EC2 On-Demand por primera vez. AWS cobra solo por el tiempo de c\xf3mputo que se ejecuta la instancia, sin cargos adicionales por aprovisionamiento o lanzamiento inicial. El modelo de precios de pago por uso de AWS para las instancias On-Demand significa que solo se le cobra por el tiempo que se ejecuta la instancia, sin tarifas de inicio adicionales, cargos mensuales fijos ni requisitos m\xednimos de uso. Con las instancias On-Demand no se requieren compromisos a largo plazo ni pagos por adelantado, lo que le permite iniciar y detener instancias en cualquier momento seg\xfan sus necesidades. Las instancias EC2 basadas en Linux s\xed se facturan por segundo seg\xfan una tarifa horaria, con un cargo m\xednimo de un minuto completo, lo cual es una afirmaci\xf3n correcta y no la incorrecta que la pregunta le pide identificar."},{"id":"q201","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 introduciendo un nuevo producto a sus clientes y espera un aumento repentino del tr\xe1fico a su aplicaci\xf3n web. Como parte de su plan Enterprise Support, \xbfcu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones proporciona a la empresa orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica y de escalado?","options":{"A":"AWS Knowledge Center","B":"AWS Health Dashboard","C":"Infrastructure Event Management","D":"AWS Support Concierge Service"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Infrastructure Event Management es una caracter\xedstica de Enterprise Support que proporciona orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica y soporte en tiempo real durante eventos planificados, como lanzamientos de productos y picos de tr\xe1fico.\\nAWS Knowledge Center es una biblioteca de acceso p\xfablico de preguntas frecuentes y soluciones para problemas y casos de uso comunes de AWS, y es un recurso de autoservicio en lugar de un programa dedicado que proporcione orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica personalizada durante eventos de alto tr\xe1fico. AWS Health Dashboard env\xeda alertas personalizadas proactivas sobre eventos de AWS que pueden afectar sus recursos y proporciona orientaci\xf3n de remediaci\xf3n, pero es una herramienta de monitoreo y alerta en lugar de un programa para recibir orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica durante un lanzamiento de producto planificado. AWS Support Concierge Service est\xe1 disponible para clientes de Enterprise Support como un recurso dedicado para consultas de facturaci\xf3n y cuenta, y no proporciona orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica ni soporte de escalado para eventos de tr\xe1fico planificados."},{"id":"q214","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones de compra de instancias EC2 admite el modelo Bring Your Own License (BYOL) para casi todos los escenarios BYOL?","options":{"A":"Dedicated Instances","B":"Dedicated Hosts","C":"On-demand Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"B","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Hosts proporciona un servidor f\xedsico dedicado completamente a su uso con visibilidad de sockets y n\xfacleos, lo cual es necesario para llevar licencias de software existentes por socket o por n\xfacleo al modelo BYOL.\\nDedicated Instances se ejecutan en hardware dedicado a un solo cliente, pero no proporcionan visibilidad del host f\xedsico espec\xedfico, incluidos los recuentos de sockets y n\xfacleos, lo cual es necesario para la mayor\xeda de los modelos de licencias por socket o por n\xfacleo. On-Demand Instances se cobran a la tarifa horaria est\xe1ndar en infraestructura compartida con multitenencia completa de hardware, y no proporcionan la dedicaci\xf3n de servidor f\xedsico ni la visibilidad a nivel de host necesarias para cualquier escenario BYOL significativo. Reserved Instances son un compromiso de precios que reduce los costos durante un plazo de uno o tres a\xf1os, pero al igual que On-Demand Instances se ejecutan en infraestructura compartida est\xe1ndar y no proporcionan la visibilidad a nivel de host necesaria para licencias de software vinculadas al servidor."},{"id":"q234","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes actividades puede ayudar a reducir sus costos mensuales de AWS?","options":{"A":"Habilitar Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling para todas sus cargas de trabajo","B":"Usar AWS Network Load Balancer (NLB) para equilibrar las solicitudes HTTP entrantes","C":"Eliminar todas sus Cost Allocation Tags","D":"Implementar sus recursos de AWS en varias Availability Zones"},"correct":"A","explanation":"EC2 Auto Scaling garantiza que solo ejecute la capacidad que necesita, reduciendo durante la baja demanda y aumentando durante la alta demanda, lo que reduce directamente los costos al eliminar el sobredimensionamiento.\\nUsar AWS Network Load Balancer para equilibrar las solicitudes HTTP entrantes distribuye el tr\xe1fico entre instancias para mejorar la disponibilidad y el rendimiento, pero no cambia el n\xfamero de instancias en ejecuci\xf3n ni aborda el costo subyacente de la capacidad sobredimensionada. Eliminar todas las Cost Allocation Tags reduce la visibilidad de la facturaci\xf3n y la capacidad de atribuir costos a proyectos o equipos espec\xedficos, pero no tiene efecto en los cargos reales de AWS en los que se incurre. Implementar recursos de AWS en varias Availability Zones mejora la resiliencia al distribuir cargas de trabajo entre dominios de fallo separados, pero normalmente aumenta los costos al ejecutar recursos redundantes adicionales en lugar de reducir los cargos mensuales."},{"id":"q240","question":"Una empresa ha creado una soluci\xf3n que ayuda a los clientes de AWS a mejorar sus arquitecturas en AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 programa de AWS puede apoyar a esta empresa?","options":{"A":"APN Consulting Partners","B":"AWS Technical Account Manager","C":"APN Technology Partners","D":"AWS Professional Services"},"correct":"A","explanation":"APN Consulting Partners son empresas de servicios profesionales certificadas por AWS para dise\xf1ar, construir y gestionar cargas de trabajo de clientes en AWS, incluidas soluciones que ayudan a otros clientes a mejorar sus arquitecturas.\\nAWS TAM es un Technical Account Manager, que es un recurso directo de AWS asignado a clientes de soporte empresarial en lugar de un programa de socios independiente al que una empresa pueda unirse para ofrecer servicios de consultor\xeda. Los APN Technology Partners son empresas que proporcionan productos de software y soluciones que se ejecutan en AWS o se integran con AWS, en lugar de consultor\xeda o servicios profesionales que ayudan a los clientes a mejorar sus arquitecturas. AWS Professional Services es un equipo global de expertos de AWS que trabaja directamente con los clientes en sus propios proyectos, y no es un programa al que las empresas externas puedan unirse para prestar servicios a otros clientes."},{"id":"q245","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes describe el modelo de pago que AWS pone a disposici\xf3n de los clientes que pueden comprometerse a usar Amazon EC2 durante un plazo de uno o tres a\xf1os para reducir sus costos totales de computaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Pague menos a medida que AWS crece","B":"Pago por uso","C":"Pague menos al usar m\xe1s","D":"Ahorre cuando reserve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El modelo de precios de AWS para comprometerse a usar EC2 durante uno o tres a\xf1os a cambio de tarifas reducidas se alinea con el principio \\"Ahorre cuando reserve\\".\\n\\"Pague menos a medida que AWS crece\\" se refiere al principio de precios a largo plazo seg\xfan el cual AWS traslada a los clientes los ahorros de econom\xedas de escala con el tiempo a medida que se expande la infraestructura de AWS, en lugar de describir un modelo de pago espec\xedfico vinculado a compromisos de uso individuales. \\"Pago por uso\\" describe el modelo de precios On-Demand en el que los clientes pagan solo por los recursos que consumen sin compromiso inicial, en lugar de un modelo que implica un compromiso de plazo de uno o tres a\xf1os. \\"Pague menos al usar m\xe1s\\" describe descuentos por volumen en los que los costos por unidad disminuyen a medida que aumenta el uso total, como los precios por niveles en Amazon S3, en lugar de ahorros derivados de un compromiso de uso basado en el tiempo."},{"id":"q246","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 migrando su base de datos local a Amazon RDS. \xbfQu\xe9 debe hacer la empresa para garantizar que los costos de Amazon RDS se mantengan al m\xednimo?","options":{"A":"Dimensionar correctamente antes y despu\xe9s de la migraci\xf3n","B":"Usar una arquitectura activa-pasiva multirregi\xf3n","C":"Combinar On-Demand Capacity Reservations con Saving Plans","D":"Usar una arquitectura activa-activa multirregi\xf3n"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dimensionar correctamente, elegir el tipo y tama\xf1o de instancia m\xe1s adecuados para su carga de trabajo real, tanto antes de la migraci\xf3n como despu\xe9s en funci\xf3n del uso real, es la forma m\xe1s eficaz de minimizar los costos de RDS sin sacrificar el rendimiento.\\nUsar una arquitectura activa-pasiva multirregi\xf3n distribuye una carga de trabajo entre dos Regiones donde una sirve como respaldo para recuperaci\xf3n ante desastres, lo que aumenta los costos de infraestructura al ejecutar recursos en una segunda Regi\xf3n en lugar de minimizarlos. Combinar On-Demand Capacity Reservations con Savings Plans puede reducir los costos de EC2 para casos de uso espec\xedficos, pero agrega complejidad de facturaci\xf3n y no es la palanca principal para minimizar los costos de RDS. Usar una arquitectura activa-activa multirregi\xf3n ejecuta la carga de trabajo completa simult\xe1neamente en varias Regiones para atender a los usuarios con baja latencia a nivel global, lo que aumenta significativamente los costos en lugar de minimizarlos."},{"id":"q260","question":"Una empresa necesita alojar una base de datos en Amazon RDS durante al menos tres a\xf1os. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones ser\xeda la soluci\xf3n m\xe1s rentable?","options":{"A":"Reserved Instances - Sin pago inicial","B":"Reserved Instances - Pago inicial parcial","C":"Instancias On-Demand","D":"Instancias Spot"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Para un compromiso de base de datos de tres a\xf1os, Reserved Instances con pago inicial parcial ofrece el mejor equilibrio entre profundidad de descuento y flujo de caja manejable, ya que proporciona un descuento mayor que Sin pago inicial y evita el desembolso total de capital del pago All Upfront.\\nReserved Instances con pago Sin pago inicial todav\xeda proporcionan un descuento sobre On-Demand para un compromiso de tres a\xf1os, pero el descuento es menor que el de las opciones Pago inicial parcial o All Upfront porque AWS no recibe ning\xfan pago al inicio del plazo. Las Instancias On-Demand cobran la tarifa horaria completa sin descuento, lo que las convierte en la opci\xf3n m\xe1s costosa para una carga de trabajo en estado estable que se ejecuta durante tres a\xf1os. Las Instancias Spot no est\xe1n disponibles para Amazon RDS, ya que RDS es un servicio de base de datos totalmente administrado que no admite el modelo de compra Spot."},{"id":"q262","question":"\xbfPara cu\xe1les de los siguientes servicios de computaci\xf3n de AWS est\xe1n disponibles los Savings Plans?","options":{"A":"AWS Batch","B":"AWS Outposts","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"Amazon EC2","E":"AWS Lambda"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"AWS Savings Plans son modelos de precios flexibles disponibles para Amazon EC2 y AWS Lambda, que ofrecen ahorros sobre On-Demand a cambio de un compromiso de uso de uno o tres a\xf1os.\\nAWS Batch es un servicio administrado para ejecutar cargas de trabajo de computaci\xf3n por lotes a escala y, aunque puede usar instancias EC2 que podr\xedan estar cubiertas por Savings Plans, AWS Batch en s\xed como servicio no est\xe1 cubierto directamente por un compromiso de Savings Plan. AWS Outposts extiende la infraestructura de AWS a entornos locales de clientes, y las cargas de trabajo que se ejecutan en Outposts no est\xe1n cubiertas por Compute Savings Plans de la misma manera que las implementaciones est\xe1ndar en Regiones de AWS. Amazon Lightsail es un servicio de computaci\xf3n simplificado con precios agrupados sencillos para servidores virtuales y aplicaciones web, y no est\xe1 cubierto por AWS Savings Plans."},{"id":"q264","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n es correcta con respecto a los l\xedmites de servicio de AWS?","options":{"A":"Puede contactar al soporte de AWS para aumentar los l\xedmites de servicio","B":"Cada usuario de IAM tiene el mismo l\xedmite de servicio","C":"No hay l\xedmites de servicio en AWS","D":"Puede usar AWS Trusted Advisor para monitorear sus l\xedmites de servicio","E":"Amazon Simple Email Service es responsable de enviar notificaciones por correo electr\xf3nico cuando el uso se acerca a un l\xedmite de servicio"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Los l\xedmites de servicio de AWS (cuotas) se aplican a nivel de cuenta y se pueden aumentar enviando una solicitud de soporte a AWS. AWS Trusted Advisor monitorea el uso actual frente a los l\xedmites de servicio y le alerta cuando se acerca a ellos.\\nLos l\xedmites de servicio se aplican a toda la cuenta de AWS en lugar de a usuarios de IAM individuales, por lo que cada usuario de IAM no tiene su propio l\xedmite de servicio separado. AWS impone l\xedmites de servicio a todas las cuentas de forma predeterminada, por lo que la afirmaci\xf3n de que no hay l\xedmites de servicio es factualmente incorrecta. Amazon Simple Email Service es un servicio de env\xedo de correo electr\xf3nico para comunicaciones transaccionales y de marketing, y no es responsable de monitorear ni enviar notificaciones sobre el uso de los l\xedmites de servicio."},{"id":"q272","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo se factura a los clientes de AWS por el uso de Amazon EC2 basado en Linux?","options":{"A":"Las instancias EC2 se facturar\xe1n en incrementos de un segundo, con un m\xednimo de un minuto","B":"Las instancias EC2 se facturar\xe1n en incrementos de una hora, con un m\xednimo de un d\xeda","C":"Las instancias EC2 se facturar\xe1n en incrementos de un minuto, con un m\xednimo de una hora","D":"Las instancias EC2 se facturar\xe1n en incrementos de un d\xeda, con un m\xednimo de un mes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Las instancias EC2 basadas en Linux se facturan por segundo de uso real, con un cargo m\xednimo de un minuto completo. Este modelo de facturaci\xf3n granular significa que las cargas de trabajo de corta duraci\xf3n no se cobran en exceso.\\nLa facturaci\xf3n en incrementos de una hora con un m\xednimo diario era el modelo de facturaci\xf3n original de EC2 antes de que se introdujera la facturaci\xf3n por segundo, y cobra significativamente de m\xe1s por las cargas de trabajo de corta duraci\xf3n en comparaci\xf3n con el modelo actual por segundo. La facturaci\xf3n en incrementos de un minuto con un m\xednimo por hora cobrar\xeda de m\xe1s a las instancias que se ejecutan durante menos de una hora y no es el modelo de facturaci\xf3n actual para las instancias EC2 de Linux. La facturaci\xf3n en un incremento diario con un m\xednimo mensual describe un modelo que no existe para las instancias EC2 y har\xeda que las cargas de trabajo de corta duraci\xf3n fueran prohibitivamente caras en comparaci\xf3n con la facturaci\xf3n real por segundo que se aplica."},{"id":"q273","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes factores afectar\xe1 el precio pagado por una instancia EC2?","options":{"A":"Tipo de instancia","B":"La Zona de Disponibilidad donde se aprovisiona la instancia","C":"Balanceo de carga","D":"N\xfamero de buckets","E":"N\xfamero de IP privadas"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"El tipo de instancia EC2 determina la tarifa base, y la Zona de Disponibilidad donde se aprovisiona la instancia puede afectar el precio porque los precios de las Instancias Spot var\xedan seg\xfan la AZ y algunos precios regionales On-Demand difieren.\\nEl balanceo de carga distribuye el tr\xe1fico entrante entre varias instancias EC2 para mejorar la disponibilidad y, aunque los Elastic Load Balancers generan sus propios cargos separados, el uso del balanceo de carga no afecta el precio de las instancias EC2 en s\xed. La cantidad de buckets de S3 que tenga no afecta el precio de las instancias EC2 de ninguna manera, ya que los costos de almacenamiento de S3 se calculan por separado seg\xfan el volumen de datos almacenados y la clase de almacenamiento. La cantidad de direcciones IP privadas asignadas a una instancia EC2 no afecta su precio, ya que las direcciones IP privadas dentro de una VPC se proporcionan sin costo adicional."},{"id":"q275","question":"Una empresa utiliza AWS Organizations para gestionar todas sus cuentas de AWS. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones permite a la empresa restringir qu\xe9 servicios y acciones est\xe1n permitidos en cada cuenta individual?","options":{"A":"IAM Principals","B":"AWS Service Control Policies (SCPs)","C":"Pol\xedticas de IAM","D":"AWS Fargate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las Service Control Policies en AWS Organizations son barreras de permisos aplicadas a cuentas o unidades organizativas que restringen qu\xe9 servicios y acciones de AWS est\xe1n disponibles dentro de esas cuentas, incluso para usuarios root. IAM Principals son las entidades, como usuarios, roles y grupos, a las que se adjuntan las pol\xedticas de IAM, y son componentes del sistema de gesti\xf3n de identidades y accesos dentro de una sola cuenta, en lugar de un mecanismo para restringir servicios y acciones en varias cuentas. Las pol\xedticas de IAM definen permisos para usuarios, grupos y roles dentro de una cuenta de AWS individual, y aunque controlan el acceso dentro de una cuenta, no pueden imponer restricciones que se apliquen en varias cuentas de una AWS Organization de la manera en que s\xed pueden hacerlo las SCPs. AWS Fargate es un motor de c\xf3mputo sin servidor para ejecutar contenedores que abstrae la gesti\xf3n de la infraestructura subyacente, y es un servicio de c\xf3mputo sin conexi\xf3n con la gobernanza a nivel de cuenta ni con la restricci\xf3n de servicios entre cuentas."},{"id":"q279","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el nivel m\xednimo de soporte de AWS que proporciona acceso 24/7 a ingenieros de soporte t\xe9cnico por tel\xe9fono y chat?","options":{"A":"Enterprise Support","B":"Developer Support","C":"Basic Support","D":"Business Support"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Business Support es el plan m\xednimo que incluye acceso telef\xf3nico y por chat 24/7 a Cloud Support Engineers, con un SLA de respuesta de una hora para casos de producci\xf3n con sistemas ca\xeddos. Enterprise Support tambi\xe9n proporciona acceso telef\xf3nico y por chat 24/7 a Cloud Support Engineers, pero es un plan de nivel superior a Business Support, por lo que no es el nivel m\xednimo que satisface este requisito. Developer Support proporciona acceso por correo electr\xf3nico a Cloud Support Engineers solo durante el horario laboral, y no incluye acceso telef\xf3nico ni por chat 24/7. Basic Support proporciona acceso a documentaci\xf3n, whitepapers y comprobaciones principales de AWS Trusted Advisor, pero no incluye soporte t\xe9cnico para casos ni acceso a Cloud Support Engineers."},{"id":"q281","question":"Una empresa ha desarrollado una aplicaci\xf3n de transcodificaci\xf3n de medios en AWS. La aplicaci\xf3n est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para recuperarse r\xe1pidamente de fallos de hardware. \xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes tipos de instancia ser\xeda la opci\xf3n m\xe1s rentable de usar?","options":{"A":"Instancias reservadas","B":"Instancias Spot","C":"Instancias On-Demand","D":"Instancias dedicadas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las Spot Instances son ideales para cargas de trabajo tolerantes a fallos que pueden manejar interrupciones, como la transcodificaci\xf3n de medios, que puede guardar puntos de control y reanudarse. Dado que la aplicaci\xf3n est\xe1 dise\xf1ada para recuperarse r\xe1pidamente de fallos de hardware, tolera interrupciones, lo que hace que las Spot Instances sean la opci\xf3n m\xe1s rentable. Las Reserved Instances proporcionan un descuento significativo sobre el precio On-Demand para cargas de trabajo en estado estable mediante un compromiso de uno o tres a\xf1os, pero no son la opci\xf3n m\xe1s rentable para una aplicaci\xf3n tolerante a fallos que califica para los descuentos m\xe1s profundos disponibles mediante el precio Spot. Las On-Demand Instances cobran la tarifa horaria completa sin descuento, lo que las hace significativamente m\xe1s caras que las Spot Instances para una carga de trabajo por lotes donde la minimizaci\xf3n de costos es el objetivo principal y las interrupciones son aceptables. Las Dedicated Instances se ejecutan en hardware dedicado a un \xfanico cliente por motivos de cumplimiento y aislamiento de licencias, y tienen un precio superior al de las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2 en lugar de proporcionar ahorros de costos para una carga de trabajo de procesamiento tolerante a fallos."},{"id":"q286","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona recomendaciones de optimizaci\xf3n de costos?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"Amazon QuickSight","D":"AWS X-Ray"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor analiza continuamente tu entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones espec\xedficas de optimizaci\xf3n de costos, como identificar instancias EC2 infrautilizadas, balanceadores de carga inactivos y vol\xfamenes EBS no adjuntos. AWS Pricing Calculator estima costos futuros para nuevas configuraciones de servicios de AWS antes de aprovisionarlas, y es una herramienta de planificaci\xf3n para pronosticar el gasto en lugar de un servicio que analiza la infraestructura existente para identificar oportunidades de ahorro. Amazon QuickSight es un servicio de inteligencia empresarial sin servidor para crear paneles interactivos y visualizaciones a partir de datos empresariales, y no tiene capacidad para analizar el uso de la infraestructura de AWS ni generar recomendaciones de optimizaci\xf3n de costos. AWS X-Ray es un servicio de trazado distribuido que analiza y depura el rendimiento de las aplicaciones mediante el mapeo de solicitudes a medida que fluyen por los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de rendimiento sin capacidad de optimizaci\xf3n de costos ni de an\xe1lisis de infraestructura."},{"id":"q288","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es un beneficio y una desventaja de comprar una instancia EC2 reservada?","options":{"A":"Las instancias pueden ser apagadas por AWS en cualquier momento sin notificaci\xf3n","B":"Las instancias reservadas requieren un compromiso de precios de al menos un a\xf1o","C":"No hay cargo adicional por usar instancias dedicadas","D":"Las instancias reservadas ofrecen un descuento significativo en comparaci\xf3n con las instancias bajo demanda","E":"Las instancias reservadas son m\xe1s adecuadas para cargas de trabajo peri\xf3dicas"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Las Reserved Instances requieren un compromiso m\xednimo de un a\xf1o, que es la principal desventaja para los equipos que necesitan flexibilidad. El beneficio clave es un descuento significativo en comparaci\xf3n con el precio On-Demand. Que AWS apague las instancias en cualquier momento sin notificaci\xf3n es una caracter\xedstica de las Spot Instances, que pueden interrumpirse cuando AWS recupera capacidad disponible, en lugar de las Reserved Instances, que contin\xfaan ejecut\xe1ndose seg\xfan el control del cliente. Existe un cargo adicional por usar Dedicated Instances, que se ejecutan en hardware dedicado a un \xfanico cliente y tienen un precio superior al de las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2, por lo que la afirmaci\xf3n de que no hay cargo adicional es incorrecta. Las Reserved Instances son m\xe1s adecuadas para cargas de trabajo en estado estable que se ejecutan de forma continua o predecible, en lugar de cargas de trabajo peri\xf3dicas, porque el compromiso es por capacidad constante y el descuento no se reduce cuando la instancia no est\xe1 en ejecuci\xf3n."},{"id":"q290","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la opci\xf3n de compra m\xe1s rentable para ejecutar un conjunto de instancias EC2 que deben estar siempre disponibles durante un per\xedodo de dos meses?","options":{"A":"Instancias On-Demand","B":"Instancias Spot","C":"Instancias reservadas - pago total por adelantado","D":"Instancias reservadas - sin pago inicial"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Para una carga de trabajo que debe estar siempre disponible pero solo durante dos meses, las On-Demand Instances son la mejor opci\xf3n, ya que solo pagas por el tiempo de c\xf3mputo que utilizas sin compromiso a largo plazo, y la duraci\xf3n corta no justifica el plazo m\xednimo de un a\xf1o de las Reserved Instances. Las Spot Instances pueden ser interrumpidas por AWS cuando se recupera capacidad disponible, lo que infringe directamente el requisito de que las instancias deben estar siempre disponibles. Las Reserved Instances con pago total por adelantado requieren un compromiso m\xednimo de un a\xf1o, lo que costar\xeda significativamente m\xe1s durante solo dos meses que pagar On-Demand por ese per\xedodo. Las Reserved Instances sin pago inicial tambi\xe9n requieren un compromiso m\xednimo de un a\xf1o, lo que hace que el costo total durante el plazo del compromiso supere con creces el requisito de dos meses."},{"id":"q297","question":"Una empresa necesita migrar su sitio web desde las instalaciones locales a AWS. La seguridad es una preocupaci\xf3n importante para ellos, por lo que necesitan alojar su sitio web en hardware que NO se comparta con otros clientes de AWS. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones de instancia EC2 cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"Instancias bajo demanda","B":"Instancias Spot","C":"Instancias dedicadas","D":"Instancias reservadas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las EC2 Dedicated Instances se ejecutan en hardware que est\xe1 aislado f\xedsicamente a nivel de host y no se comparte con ninguna otra cuenta de cliente de AWS, lo que cumple el requisito de aislamiento f\xedsico. Las On-Demand Instances se ejecutan en hardware compartido y se facturan por segundo sin compromiso inicial, pero no proporcionan ning\xfan aislamiento f\xedsico de otros clientes de AWS. Las Spot Instances se ejecutan en capacidad disponible compartida de AWS a tarifas con descuento y no ofrecen garant\xedas de aislamiento de hardware ni de tenencia. Las Reserved Instances son un modelo de facturaci\xf3n y precios que ofrece tarifas con descuento a cambio de un compromiso de uso, no un tipo de tenencia, y no garantizan hardware dedicado."},{"id":"q301","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 plan de soporte incluye AWS Support Concierge Service?","options":{"A":"Business Support","B":"Developer Support","C":"Enterprise Support","D":"Basic Support"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El AWS Support Concierge es un equipo dedicado de expertos en facturaci\xf3n y cuentas disponible exclusivamente para los clientes de Enterprise Support, que proporciona orientaci\xf3n personalizada sobre consultas de facturaci\xf3n y mejores pr\xe1cticas de cuentas. Business Support proporciona acceso 24/7 a Cloud Support Engineers para problemas t\xe9cnicos e incluye Infrastructure Event Management por una tarifa adicional, pero no incluye el equipo dedicado de Concierge para orientaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n y cuentas que es exclusivo de Enterprise Support. Developer Support proporciona acceso por correo electr\xf3nico en horario laboral a Cloud Support Engineers para preguntas t\xe9cnicas generales, y no incluye especialistas en facturaci\xf3n ni recursos dedicados de gesti\xf3n de cuentas. Basic Support proporciona acceso a documentaci\xf3n, whitepapers y las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor, y no incluye personal de soporte dedicado ni asistencia especializada en facturaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q309","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo pueden los clientes de AWS realizar un seguimiento y evitar el gasto excesivo en instancias reservadas infrautilizadas?","options":{"A":"Los clientes pueden agregar todas las cuentas de AWS a una AWS Organization, habilitar Consolidated Billing y desactivar el uso compartido de Reserved Instances","B":"Los clientes pueden usar Amazon Neptune para realizar un seguimiento y analizar sus patrones de uso, detectar instancias reservadas infrautilizadas y luego venderlas en Amazon EC2 Reserved Instance Marketplace","C":"Los clientes pueden usar el servicio AWS Budgets para realizar un seguimiento del uso de las instancias reservadas y configurar notificaciones de alerta cuando su utilizaci\xf3n caiga por debajo del umbral que definan","D":"Los clientes pueden usar Amazon CloudTrail para comprobar autom\xe1ticamente las reservas no utilizadas y obtener recomendaciones para reducir su factura"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Budgets permite establecer umbrales de utilizaci\xf3n personalizados para las instancias reservadas y env\xeda notificaciones de alerta cuando la utilizaci\xf3n cae por debajo del nivel definido, lo que ayuda a identificar y abordar reservas infrautilizadas.\\nAgregar todas las cuentas de AWS a una AWS Organization y habilitar la facturaci\xf3n consolidada permite que los beneficios de las instancias reservadas se compartan entre cuentas, pero desactivar el uso compartido de instancias reservadas impedir\xeda ese beneficio en lugar de ayudar a realizar un seguimiento de las reservas infrautilizadas. Amazon Neptune es un servicio de base de datos de grafos totalmente administrado para aplicaciones que trabajan con conjuntos de datos altamente conectados, y no tiene capacidad para realizar un seguimiento del uso de instancias reservadas, detectar infrautilizaci\xf3n ni facilitar la reventa en el RI Marketplace. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta en un entorno de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no analiza la utilizaci\xf3n de instancias reservadas ni genera recomendaciones para reducir la facturaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q314","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 intentando analizar los costos aplicados a su cuenta de AWS recientemente. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones les proporciona los datos m\xe1s granulares sobre sus costos y uso de AWS?","options":{"A":"Amazon Machine Image","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Cost & Usage Report","D":"Amazon CloudWatch"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Cost and Usage Reports proporciona los datos m\xe1s granulares disponibles sobre los costos de AWS, desglosando los cargos por hora, servicio, recurso y etiquetas para an\xe1lisis y auditor\xeda detallados.\\nUna Amazon Machine Image es una plantilla que contiene la configuraci\xf3n de software necesaria para lanzar una instancia EC2, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con el an\xe1lisis de costos ni con los datos de facturaci\xf3n. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas de los patrones de gasto de AWS a lo largo del tiempo y admite el filtrado por servicio, cuenta, etiqueta y per\xedodo de tiempo, pero proporciona datos menos granulares que el Cost and Usage Report, que incluye detalle por hora a nivel de recurso. Amazon CloudWatch recopila m\xe9tricas, registros y datos de eventos de servicios y recursos de AWS para proporcionar monitoreo operativo y alertas, y aunque puede monitorear algunas m\xe9tricas de facturaci\xf3n, no proporciona el desglose detallado de costos y uso que ofrece el Cost and Usage Report."},{"id":"q318","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son los beneficios de implementar una estrategia de etiquetado para los recursos de AWS?","options":{"A":"Identificar r\xe1pidamente los recursos que pertenecen a un proyecto espec\xedfico","B":"Identificar r\xe1pidamente soluciones de software en AWS","C":"Realizar un seguimiento de las llamadas a la API en su cuenta de AWS","D":"Identificar r\xe1pidamente los recursos eliminados y sus metadatos","E":"Realizar un seguimiento del gasto de AWS en m\xfaltiples recursos"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Las etiquetas son pares de metadatos clave-valor que le permiten identificar r\xe1pidamente qu\xe9 recursos pertenecen a proyectos espec\xedficos y realizar un seguimiento del gasto de AWS en esos recursos para la asignaci\xf3n de costos y la claridad organizacional.\\nIdentificar r\xe1pidamente soluciones de software en AWS describe la exploraci\xf3n de AWS Marketplace, lo cual no est\xe1 relacionado con el etiquetado de recursos dentro de una cuenta. Realizar un seguimiento de las llamadas a la API en una cuenta de AWS describe la funci\xf3n de AWS CloudTrail, que registra la actividad de la cuenta en lugar de organizar recursos por metadatos. Identificar r\xe1pidamente los recursos eliminados y sus metadatos no es una funci\xf3n del etiquetado, ya que las etiquetas se adjuntan a recursos existentes y se eliminan cuando se eliminan los recursos."},{"id":"q328","question":"TYMO Cloud Corp est\xe1 considerando migrar todo su centro de datos local a AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 herramienta pueden usar para realizar un an\xe1lisis de costo-beneficio de migrar a la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Migration Evaluator","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Pricing Calculator"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Migration Evaluator ayuda a las organizaciones a comparar el costo de ejecutar cargas de trabajo en las instalaciones frente a AWS, teniendo en cuenta la infraestructura, la mano de obra y los gastos operativos para respaldar un caso de negocio de migraci\xf3n.\\nAWS Cost Explorer se utiliza para visualizar y analizar el gasto del uso existente de AWS, no para comparar los costos en las instalaciones con AWS antes de la migraci\xf3n. AWS Budgets se utiliza para establecer umbrales de gasto y recibir alertas en cuentas de AWS existentes, no para evaluar los ahorros de costos de la migraci\xf3n. AWS Pricing Calculator estima los costos mensuales de una arquitectura de AWS planificada, pero no compara esos costos con la infraestructura en las instalaciones para producir un an\xe1lisis de costo-beneficio."},{"id":"q334","question":"AWS recomienda algunas pr\xe1cticas para ayudar a las organizaciones a evitar cargos inesperados en su factura. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes NO es una de estas pr\xe1cticas?","options":{"A":"Eliminar vol\xfamenes EBS no utilizados despu\xe9s de terminar una instancia EC2","B":"Eliminar configuraciones de lanzamiento de Auto Scaling no utilizadas","C":"Eliminar Elastic Load Balancers no utilizados","D":"Liberar Elastic IPs no utilizadas despu\xe9s de terminar una instancia EC2"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eliminar configuraciones de lanzamiento de Auto Scaling no utilizadas no genera cargos de AWS, por lo que no es una pr\xe1ctica de ahorro de costos. En cambio, los vol\xfamenes EBS, los Elastic Load Balancers y las Elastic IPs no utilizados s\xed generan cargos continuos que deben limpiarse.\\nEliminar vol\xfamenes EBS no utilizados despu\xe9s de terminar una instancia EC2 es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de ahorro de costos porque los vol\xfamenes EBS siguen generando cargos de almacenamiento incluso cuando no hay ninguna instancia conectada a ellos. Eliminar Elastic Load Balancers no utilizados es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de ahorro de costos porque los balanceadores de carga generan un cargo por hora independientemente de si est\xe1n sirviendo tr\xe1fico. Liberar direcciones Elastic IP no utilizadas despu\xe9s de terminar una instancia EC2 es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de ahorro de costos porque AWS cobra por las Elastic IPs asignadas pero no asociadas a una instancia en ejecuci\xf3n."},{"id":"q335","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la herramienta de AWS que puede ayudar a una empresa a visualizar su gasto de AWS en los \xfaltimos meses?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas del gasto de AWS a lo largo del tiempo, lo que le permite filtrar por servicio, cuenta, etiqueta o per\xedodo de tiempo para identificar tendencias de gasto y anomal\xedas durante los \xfaltimos meses.\\nAWS Pricing Calculator estima costos futuros de servicios de AWS antes de usarlos, y no proporciona visualizaci\xf3n del gasto hist\xf3rico. AWS Budgets le permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costo y uso, y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales; es una herramienta proactiva de gobernanza de costos en lugar de un servicio de visualizaci\xf3n del gasto hist\xf3rico. AWS Organizations proporciona administraci\xf3n centralizada de m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS, incluida la facturaci\xf3n consolidada y la capacidad de aplicar Service Control Policies, pero no proporciona por s\xed mismo visualizaci\xf3n de patrones de gasto."},{"id":"q339","question":"Una de las principales ventajas de usar AWS es el ahorro de costos. \xbfQu\xe9 proporciona AWS para reducir el costo de ejecutar instancias Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"Bajos costos mensuales de mantenimiento de instancias","B":"Etiquetado de instancias de bajo costo","C":"Facturaci\xf3n por segundo de las instancias","D":"Bajas tarifas de inicio de instancias"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon EC2 ofrece facturaci\xf3n por segundo con un m\xednimo de un minuto, por lo que los clientes pagan solo por el tiempo de c\xf3mputo exacto que utilizan en lugar de redondear a la hora m\xe1s cercana, lo que reduce directamente los costos.\\nLos bajos costos mensuales de mantenimiento de instancias no son un beneficio de reducci\xf3n de costos de AWS, ya que el mantenimiento de la infraestructura f\xedsica subyacente es responsabilidad de AWS seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida y est\xe1 incluido en el precio est\xe1ndar. El etiquetado de instancias de bajo costo se refiere al uso de etiquetas de recursos para la asignaci\xf3n de costos, y aunque las etiquetas son gratuitas de aplicar, son un mecanismo de visibilidad de costos en lugar de un mecanismo que reduce el precio de ejecutar instancias EC2. Las bajas tarifas de inicio de instancias son un concepto de precios inventado. Las instancias Amazon EC2 On-Demand no tienen tarifa de inicio y cobran solo por el tiempo que la instancia est\xe1 en ejecuci\xf3n."},{"id":"q340","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 grupo de AWS ayuda a los clientes a lograr los resultados de negocio deseados?","options":{"A":"AWS Security Team","B":"AWS Professional Services","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS Concierge Support Team"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Professional Services es un equipo de expertos que trabaja directamente con los clientes para ayudarlos a lograr los resultados de negocio deseados mediante estrategia de nube, orientaci\xf3n de arquitectura y soporte de migraci\xf3n.\\nEl AWS Security Team gestiona la propia postura de seguridad interna de AWS y el programa de divulgaci\xf3n responsable para la infraestructura de AWS, y no es un equipo orientado al cliente que trabaja con clientes para lograr resultados de negocio en AWS. AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas en seguridad, costos, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento de autoservicio en lugar de un equipo de servicios profesionales que trabaja directamente con los clientes. El AWS Concierge Support Team est\xe1 disponible para clientes de Enterprise Support para consultas de facturaci\xf3n y cuenta, y proporciona orientaci\xf3n dedicada sobre la cuenta en lugar de soporte estrat\xe9gico para resultados de negocio o migraci\xf3n a la nube."},{"id":"q344","question":"Decide comprar una instancia reservada por un plazo de un a\xf1o. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n proporciona el mayor descuento total?","options":{"A":"Reserva con pago total por adelantado (All up-front)","B":"Todas las opciones de pago de instancias reservadas proporcionan el mismo nivel de descuento","C":"Reserva con pago parcial por adelantado (Partial up-front)","D":"Reserva sin pago por adelantado (No up-front)"},"correct":"A","explanation":"La opci\xf3n de pago All Upfront para las instancias reservadas proporciona el mayor descuento porque se paga el costo total del plazo al inicio, lo que le otorga el m\xe1ximo ahorro.\\nTodas las opciones de pago de instancias reservadas no proporcionan el mismo nivel de descuento. Las tres opciones, All Upfront, Partial Upfront y No Upfront, proporcionan descuentos progresivamente menores a medida que disminuye el monto pagado por adelantado. La reserva Partial Upfront divide el pago entre un monto inicial por adelantado y tarifas mensuales m\xe1s bajas, proporcionando un descuento moderado que es mayor que el de No Upfront pero menor que el de All Upfront. La reserva No Upfront proporciona el descuento m\xe1s bajo entre las tres opciones de pago de RI porque no se realiza ning\xfan pago inicial, lo que significa que AWS ofrece menos incentivo que con las opciones Partial o All Upfront."},{"id":"q346","question":"Un cliente de AWS ha utilizado una instancia Amazon Linux durante 2 horas, 5 minutos y 9 segundos, y una instancia CentOS durante 4 horas, 23 minutos y 7 segundos. \xbfPor cu\xe1nto tiempo se le facturar\xe1 al cliente?","options":{"A":"3 horas para la instancia Linux y 5 horas para la instancia CentOS","B":"2 horas, 5 minutos y 9 segundos para la instancia Linux y 4 horas, 23 minutos y 7 segundos para la instancia CentOS","C":"2 horas, 5 minutos y 9 segundos para la instancia Linux y 5 horas para la instancia CentOS","D":"3 horas para la instancia Linux y 4 horas, 23 minutos y 7 segundos para la instancia CentOS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las instancias Amazon Linux se facturan por segundo con un m\xednimo de un minuto, por lo que 2 horas, 5 minutos y 9 segundos es el tiempo facturable exacto. Las instancias CentOS en AWS Marketplace se facturan por hora redondeando hacia arriba, lo que da como resultado 5 horas.\\nFacturar ambas instancias en incrementos de hora redondeados hacia arriba aplicar\xeda el modelo de facturaci\xf3n por hora antiguo a la instancia Linux, lo que dar\xeda como resultado 3 horas en lugar de la duraci\xf3n exacta por segundo. Facturar ambas instancias con precisi\xf3n exacta por segundo aplicar\xeda incorrectamente el modelo de facturaci\xf3n por segundo de Linux a la instancia CentOS, que se factura por hora redondeada hacia arriba. Facturar la instancia Linux por horas redondeadas hacia arriba y la CentOS por segundos exactos invierte los modelos de facturaci\xf3n correctos para cada tipo de instancia."},{"id":"q347","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es la caracter\xedstica de AWS Support que permite a los clientes gestionar casos de soporte de forma program\xe1tica?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Operations Support","C":"AWS Support API","D":"AWS Health Dashboard"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La AWS Support API permite a los clientes crear, actualizar y resolver casos de soporte de forma program\xe1tica, lo que posibilita la integraci\xf3n con sistemas internos de tickets y flujos de trabajo automatizados de incidentes.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones automatizadas en materia de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento en lugar de una caracter\xedstica para gestionar casos de soporte de forma program\xe1tica. AWS Operations Support no es un nombre reconocido de servicio o caracter\xedstica de soporte de AWS. AWS Health Dashboard proporciona una vista personalizada de los eventos de servicio de AWS que afectan a sus recursos, junto con orientaci\xf3n para la remediaci\xf3n, y es una herramienta de observabilidad en lugar de una interfaz program\xe1tica para gestionar casos de soporte."},{"id":"q356","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ofrece descuentos por volumen basados en el uso?","options":{"A":"Amazon VPC","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon Lightsail","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon S3 utiliza un modelo de precios por niveles en el que el costo de almacenamiento por GB disminuye a medida que aumenta su uso total, proporcionando autom\xe1ticamente descuentos por volumen a los clientes que almacenan m\xe1s datos.\\nAmazon VPC es un servicio de redes que le permite aprovisionar redes virtuales aisladas dentro de AWS, y aunque VPC en s\xed es gratuito, los recursos implementados dentro de \xe9l, como NAT Gateways, se cobran individualmente sin descuentos basados en volumen. Amazon Lightsail es un servicio de computaci\xf3n simplificado con precios combinados directos para servidores virtuales y aplicaciones web, y utiliza niveles de precios fijos en lugar de descuentos por volumen basados en el uso. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaci\xf3n y an\xe1lisis de los patrones de gasto de AWS a lo largo del tiempo, y es una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de costos en lugar de un servicio de AWS que aplique descuentos de precios basados en volumen."},{"id":"q360","question":"La brecha de TCO entre la infraestructura de AWS y la infraestructura tradicional se ha ampliado en los \xfaltimos a\xf1os. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes podr\xeda ser la raz\xf3n de ello?","options":{"A":"AWS ayuda a los clientes a invertir m\xe1s en gastos de capital","B":"AWS automatiza todas las operaciones de infraestructura, por lo que los clientes ahorran m\xe1s en costos de recursos humanos","C":"AWS contin\xfaa reduciendo el costo de la computaci\xf3n en la nube para sus clientes","D":"AWS protege los recursos de AWS sin cargo adicional"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS reduce continuamente los precios a medida que logra mayores econom\xedas de escala, transfiriendo los ahorros a los clientes, lo que ampl\xeda la brecha del costo total de propiedad en comparaci\xf3n con la infraestructura tradicional local.\\nAWS no ayuda a los clientes a invertir m\xe1s en gastos de capital. Por el contrario, AWS traslada el gasto en infraestructura de gastos de capital a gastos operativos, reduciendo la necesidad de grandes inversiones iniciales en hardware. AWS automatiza muchas tareas de infraestructura mediante servicios gestionados y herramientas de orquestaci\xf3n, pero los clientes siguen incurriendo en costos de recursos humanos para ingenieros y arquitectos de la nube, y AWS no elimina todas las operaciones de infraestructura que requieren personal. AWS protege su propia infraestructura sin cargo adicional como parte de sus responsabilidades bajo el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, pero esto siempre ha sido as\xed y no es la raz\xf3n principal por la que la brecha del TCO se ha ampliado con el tiempo."},{"id":"q364","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes NO es un factor al estimar los costos de Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"La cantidad de tiempo que las instancias estar\xe1n en ejecuci\xf3n","B":"N\xfamero de security groups","C":"Direcciones Elastic IP asignadas","D":"N\xfamero de Hosted Zones","E":"N\xfamero de instancias"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Los security groups y las Hosted Zones no influyen en el precio de EC2. Los costos de EC2 se determinan por el tipo de instancia, el tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n, el n\xfamero de instancias, el almacenamiento y la transferencia de datos.\\nLa cantidad de tiempo que las instancias estar\xe1n en ejecuci\xf3n afecta directamente los costos de EC2, ya que las instancias Linux se facturan por segundo y todas las instancias dejan de acumular cargos de computaci\xf3n solo cuando se detienen o se terminan. Las direcciones Elastic IP asignadas generan cargos cuando se asignan pero no se asocian a una instancia en ejecuci\xf3n, y son un costo asociado con las implementaciones de EC2 que debe considerarse al estimar los costos totales. El n\xfamero de instancias EC2 determina directamente la escala de los costos de computaci\xf3n, ya que cada instancia en ejecuci\xf3n acumula cargos seg\xfan su tipo y tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n."},{"id":"q370","question":"Un usuario ha abierto un caso de soporte \\"Production System Down\\" para obtener ayuda de AWS Support despu\xe9s de una interrupci\xf3n del sistema de producci\xf3n. \xbfCu\xe1l es el tiempo de respuesta esperado para este tipo de caso de soporte?","options":{"A":"12 horas","B":"15 minutos","C":"24 horas","D":"Una hora"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Para un caso Production System Down en el plan de soporte Business o Enterprise, el tiempo de respuesta esperado es de una hora, lo que garantiza que las interrupciones cr\xedticas de producci\xf3n reciban atenci\xf3n r\xe1pida de AWS Support.\\nUn tiempo de respuesta de 12 horas corresponde al objetivo de respuesta de orientaci\xf3n general para clientes de Developer Support en un caso de sistema deteriorado, no al tiempo de respuesta de sistema de producci\xf3n ca\xeddo para Business o Enterprise Support. Un tiempo de respuesta de 15 minutos es el SLA para fallas de sistemas cr\xedticos para el negocio bajo el plan Enterprise Support, que es un nivel m\xe1s r\xe1pido que el SLA de una hora para sistema de producci\xf3n ca\xeddo disponible a partir de Business Support. Un tiempo de respuesta de 24 horas es el objetivo de respuesta de orientaci\xf3n general para clientes de Developer Support en casos de orientaci\xf3n general, y no aplica a interrupciones de sistemas de producci\xf3n en el nivel Business o Enterprise Support."},{"id":"q373","question":"Su empresa requiere un tiempo de respuesta inferior a 15 minutos en las interacciones de soporte sobre sus sistemas cr\xedticos para el negocio alojados en AWS si esos sistemas se caen. \xbfQu\xe9 plan de AWS Support deber\xeda utilizar esta empresa?","options":{"A":"AWS Basic Support","B":"AWS Developer Support","C":"AWS Business Support","D":"AWS Enterprise Support"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Enterprise Support proporciona un tiempo de respuesta de 15 minutos para casos de sistemas cr\xedticos para el negocio ca\xeddos, que es el SLA de respuesta m\xe1s r\xe1pido disponible entre todos los planes de AWS Support.\\nAWS Basic Support proporciona acceso a documentaci\xf3n, libros blancos y las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor, y no incluye soporte t\xe9cnico de casos ni ning\xfan SLA de tiempo de respuesta definido para fallas de sistemas. AWS Developer Support proporciona acceso por correo electr\xf3nico a los Cloud Support Engineers durante el horario comercial con tiempos de respuesta medidos en horas en lugar de minutos, y no incluye acceso 24/7 ni un SLA de respuesta de 15 minutos. AWS Business Support proporciona acceso 24/7 a los Cloud Support Engineers con un SLA de respuesta de una hora para casos de sistema de producci\xf3n ca\xeddo, lo que no cumple con el requisito de respuesta dentro de 15 minutos."},{"id":"q379","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 migrando una aplicaci\xf3n web a AWS. La capacidad de c\xf3mputo de la aplicaci\xf3n se utiliza continuamente durante todo el a\xf1o. \xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones ofrece a la empresa la soluci\xf3n m\xe1s rentable?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Dedicated Hosts","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Para cargas de trabajo en estado estable con utilizaci\xf3n continua durante todo el a\xf1o, Reserved Instances ofrecen descuentos significativos sobre los precios On-Demand a cambio de un compromiso de 1 o 3 a\xf1os, lo que las convierte en la opci\xf3n m\xe1s rentable.\\nOn-Demand Instances cobran la tarifa horaria completa sin descuento, lo que las convierte en la opci\xf3n m\xe1s costosa para una aplicaci\xf3n web que utiliza capacidad de c\xf3mputo de forma continua durante todo el a\xf1o. Dedicated Hosts proporcionan un servidor f\xedsico dedicado por completo a un \xfanico cliente para fines de cumplimiento y licenciamiento, y tienen un precio superior al de las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2 en lugar de ofrecer ahorros para una aplicaci\xf3n web est\xe1ndar que se ejecuta continuamente. Spot Instances pueden ser interrumpidas por AWS cuando se recupera la capacidad disponible, lo que las hace inadecuadas para una aplicaci\xf3n web de producci\xf3n que requiere disponibilidad continua durante todo el a\xf1o."},{"id":"q382","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes ayudar\xe1 a los clientes de AWS a ahorrar en costos al migrar sus cargas de trabajo a AWS?","options":{"A":"Usar servidores en lugar de servicios administrados","B":"Usar licencias de software de terceros existentes en AWS","C":"Migrar cargas de trabajo de producci\xf3n a AWS edge locations en lugar de AWS Regions","D":"Usar AWS Outposts para ejecutar todas las cargas de trabajo en un entorno optimizado en costos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS admite Bring Your Own License, lo que permite a los clientes reutilizar licencias de software de terceros existentes en AWS, lo que evita pagar por nuevas licencias y reduce los costos de migraci\xf3n.\\nUsar servidores en lugar de servicios administrados normalmente aumenta los costos operativos al requerir que los clientes administren y mantengan los sistemas operativos, bases de datos y software subyacentes, en lugar de reducirlos mediante la eficiencia de los servicios administrados de AWS. Migrar cargas de trabajo de producci\xf3n a AWS edge locations en lugar de AWS Regions no es una estrategia de migraci\xf3n v\xe1lida, ya que las edge locations est\xe1n dise\xf1adas para el almacenamiento en cach\xe9 y la entrega de contenido en lugar de alojar cargas de trabajo de c\xf3mputo de producci\xf3n. Usar AWS Outposts para ejecutar todas las cargas de trabajo no ahorra costos de migraci\xf3n, ya que Outposts est\xe1n dise\xf1ados para cargas de trabajo que deben permanecer on-premises debido a requisitos de latencia o residencia de datos, e implican costos adicionales por hardware dedicado administrado por AWS en las instalaciones del cliente."},{"id":"q386","question":"Una empresa planea usar Amazon S3 y Amazon CloudFront para distribuir sus cursos de video globalmente. \xbfQu\xe9 herramienta puede usar la empresa para estimar los costos de estos servicios?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Cost & Usage Report"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator le permite estimar los costos de los servicios de AWS antes de usarlos, lo que le permite modelar configuraciones para el almacenamiento de S3 y la distribuci\xf3n de CloudFront para pronosticar los gastos mensuales.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaci\xf3n y an\xe1lisis de los patrones de gasto existentes de AWS a lo largo del tiempo y requiere datos de uso activo para generar an\xe1lisis, en lugar de proporcionar estimaciones de costos previas a la implementaci\xf3n para servicios que a\xfan no se utilizan. AWS Budgets le permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costos y uso y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, y es una herramienta de gobernanza de costos y alertas en lugar de un servicio para estimar costos futuros antes de implementar nuevos servicios. El AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona el desglose m\xe1s granular de los cargos reales de AWS ya incurridos, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis hist\xf3rico de facturaci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio de estimaci\xf3n de costos para implementaciones planificadas."},{"id":"q394","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedsticas se incluyen en el AWS Business Support Plan?","options":{"A":"Acceso 24/7 al servicio al cliente","B":"Acceso a Cloud Support Engineers solo por correo electr\xf3nico durante el horario laboral","C":"Acceso a la caracter\xedstica Infrastructure Event Management (IEM) por una tarifa adicional","D":"Acceso 24/7 a la caracter\xedstica Technical Account Manager","E":"Acceso parcial a las comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"El plan Business Support incluye acceso 24/7 al servicio al cliente por tel\xe9fono, chat y correo electr\xf3nico, y acceso a Infrastructure Event Management por una tarifa adicional para respaldar eventos planificados como lanzamientos.\\nEl acceso a Cloud Support Engineers solo por correo electr\xf3nico durante el horario laboral es una caracter\xedstica del plan Developer Support, no de Business Support, que proporciona acceso 24/7 por tel\xe9fono, correo electr\xf3nico y chat. El acceso 24/7 a un Technical Account Manager dedicado es una caracter\xedstica de Enterprise Support y no est\xe1 incluido en el plan Business Support. El acceso parcial a las comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor es incorrecto porque los clientes de Business Support tienen acceso al conjunto completo de comprobaciones de Trusted Advisor, no solo a las siete comprobaciones principales que reciben los clientes de Basic y Developer Support."},{"id":"q402","question":"El flujo de trabajo de AWS de una empresa requiere que realice peri\xf3dicamente trabajos de procesamiento de im\xe1genes y videos a gran escala. El cliente busca minimizar el costo y ha declarado que la cantidad de tiempo que se tarda en procesar estos trabajos no es cr\xedtica, pero que la minimizaci\xf3n de costos es el factor m\xe1s importante al dise\xf1ar la soluci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 clase de instancia EC2 es la m\xe1s adecuada para este procesamiento?","options":{"A":"EC2 On-Demand Instances","B":"EC2 Reserved Instances - No Upfront","C":"EC2 Spot Instances","D":"EC2 Reserved Instances - All Upfront"},"correct":"C","explanation":"EC2 Spot Instances ofrecen descuentos de hasta el 90 % al utilizar capacidad disponible de AWS, lo que las convierte en la opci\xf3n m\xe1s rentable para cargas de trabajo que son flexibles en cuanto al tiempo y pueden tolerar interrupciones, como el procesamiento por lotes de im\xe1genes y videos.\\nEC2 On-Demand Instances cobran la tarifa horaria completa sin descuento, lo que las convierte en la opci\xf3n m\xe1s costosa para este tipo de carga de trabajo por lotes flexible y no cr\xedtica en cuanto al tiempo. EC2 Reserved Instances con pago No Upfront proporcionan un descuento para cargas de trabajo en estado estable durante un compromiso de 1 o 3 a\xf1os, pero no son rentables para cargas de trabajo que se ejecutan solo peri\xf3dicamente y no requieren capacidad continua. EC2 Reserved Instances con pago All Upfront proporcionan el mayor descuento de Reserved Instance mediante el pago completo por adelantado, pero requieren un compromiso a largo plazo y no son la opci\xf3n m\xe1s rentable para cargas de trabajo peri\xf3dicas que califican para el precio de Spot."},{"id":"q411","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 proporciona AWS Cost Explorer para ayudar a administrar tu gasto en AWS?","options":{"A":"Comparaciones de costos entre entornos de AWS Cloud y entornos on-premises","B":"Estimaciones precisas de los costos de los servicios de AWS seg\xfan tu uso esperado","C":"Facturaci\xf3n consolidada","D":"Pron\xf3sticos de costos altamente precisos para hasta 12 meses en el futuro"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Cost Explorer utiliza datos de gasto hist\xf3rico para generar pron\xf3sticos de costos altamente precisos para hasta 12 meses en el futuro, lo que ayuda a las organizaciones a planificar presupuestos y anticipar el gasto futuro en AWS.\\nLas comparaciones de costos entre entornos de AWS Cloud y entornos on-premises las produce AWS Pricing Calculator, que permite a los clientes modelar configuraciones de AWS y comparar los costos proyectados con el gasto en infraestructura on-premises. Las estimaciones precisas de los costos de los servicios de AWS seg\xfan el uso esperado las produce AWS Pricing Calculator, que est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para el modelado de costos previo a la implementaci\xf3n en lugar de analizar el uso hist\xf3rico. La facturaci\xf3n consolidada es una caracter\xedstica de AWS Organizations que combina el uso y las facturas de varias cuentas en una sola factura, y no es una capacidad de AWS Cost Explorer."},{"id":"q413","question":"Est\xe1s usando varias EC2 Instances On-Demand para ejecutar tu entorno de desarrollo. \xbfCu\xe1l es la mejor forma de reducir tus cargos cuando estas instancias no est\xe1n en uso?","options":{"A":"Eliminar todos los vol\xfamenes de EBS adjuntos a las instancias","B":"No se pueden minimizar los cargos por las instancias On-Demand","C":"Terminar las instancias","D":"Detener las instancias"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Detener las instancias EC2 On-Demand detiene los cargos de c\xf3mputo mientras conserva la instancia y sus vol\xfamenes de EBS, por lo que puedes reiniciarla m\xe1s tarde sin perder tu configuraci\xf3n ni tus datos.\\nEliminar todos los vol\xfamenes de EBS adjuntos a las instancias destruir\xeda todos los datos almacenados en esos vol\xfamenes, lo cual es una acci\xf3n irreversible que elimina tu entorno de desarrollo en lugar de simplemente reducir los cargos mientras no est\xe1 en uso. Las instancias On-Demand se pueden detener para reducir los cargos, por lo que la afirmaci\xf3n de que los cargos no se pueden minimizar es incorrecta. Detener una instancia es el enfoque recomendado para entornos de desarrollo que se utilizan de forma intermitente. Terminar las instancias las destruye de forma permanente junto con cualquier dato del instance store y, aunque detiene todos los cargos, es una acci\xf3n irreversible que requerir\xeda reconstruir el entorno de desarrollo desde cero."},{"id":"q415","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes factores deben considerarse para el precio de Amazon EBS?","options":{"A":"El tama\xf1o de los vol\xfamenes aprovisionados por mes","B":"La capacidad de c\xf3mputo que consumes","C":"La cantidad de datos que tienes almacenados en snapshots","D":"El tiempo de c\xf3mputo que consumes","E":"La cantidad de dispositivos de almacenamiento Snowball que solicitas"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"El precio de Amazon EBS se basa en el tama\xf1o de los vol\xfamenes aprovisionados por gigabyte por mes y en la cantidad de datos almacenados en snapshots de EBS, que son copias de seguridad incrementales almacenadas en Amazon S3 y facturadas por separado.\\nLa capacidad de c\xf3mputo que consumes es un factor de precio de EC2 basado en el tipo y tama\xf1o de la instancia, no un factor de precio de EBS. El tiempo de c\xf3mputo se factura mediante el precio de las instancias EC2 en lugar del precio de los vol\xfamenes de EBS, que cobra por el almacenamiento aprovisionado independientemente de si la instancia est\xe1 en ejecuci\xf3n. La cantidad de dispositivos AWS Snowball solicitados afecta el precio de Snow Family y no tiene relaci\xf3n alguna con los costos de vol\xfamenes o snapshots de Amazon EBS."},{"id":"q417","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes tiene el mayor impacto en el costo?","options":{"A":"Cargos de c\xf3mputo","B":"La cantidad de servicios utilizados","C":"Cargos de Data Transfer In","D":"Cargos de Data Transfer Out","E":"La cantidad de roles de IAM aprovisionados"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Los cargos de c\xf3mputo y los cargos de Data Transfer Out son t\xedpicamente los dos mayores impulsores de costos en AWS. Data Transfer In es gratuito, y los roles de IAM no tienen costo asociado.\\nLa cantidad de servicios de AWS utilizados no determina directamente el impacto en el costo. Un solo servicio muy utilizado puede costar mucho m\xe1s que docenas de servicios poco utilizados, por lo que el impacto en el costo se rige por el uso y las tarifas de precios en lugar de la cantidad de servicios consumidos. Los cargos de Data Transfer In hacia AWS son gratuitos para todos los datos ingresados a la red de AWS desde internet o desde entornos on-premises, lo que hace que la transferencia de datos entrante sea uno de los pocos componentes de costo que no contribuye a la factura. La cantidad de roles de IAM aprovisionados no genera cargos. Los roles de IAM son gratuitos de crear y no hay costo por rol, por lo que aprovisionar roles adicionales no tiene impacto en su factura de AWS."},{"id":"q418","question":"\xbfQui\xe9n de los siguientes obtendr\xe1 el mayor descuento?","options":{"A":"Un usuario que elige comprar instancias On-Demand, Convertible, Partial Upfront","B":"Un usuario que elige comprar instancias Reserved, Convertible, All Upfront","C":"Un usuario que elige comprar instancias Reserved, Standard, No Upfront","D":"Un usuario que elige comprar instancias Reserved, Standard, All Upfront"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las instancias Reserved, Standard, All Upfront proporcionan el descuento m\xe1s profundo porque las RI Standard ofrecen mayores ahorros que las RI Convertible, y pagar todo por adelantado brinda la m\xe1xima reducci\xf3n frente a las opciones Partial Upfront o No Upfront.\\nOn-Demand, Convertible, Partial Upfront no es una combinaci\xf3n de compra v\xe1lida, ya que las instancias On-Demand no tienen opciones Convertible ni de pago por adelantado. El precio On-Demand simplemente cobra por el tiempo de c\xf3mputo usado sin compromiso. Reserved, Convertible, All Upfront proporciona un descuento significativo mediante un compromiso a largo plazo con pago total por adelantado, pero las RI Convertible ofrecen menos descuento que las RI Standard a cambio de la flexibilidad de intercambiar atributos. Reserved, Standard, No Upfront proporciona un descuento mediante el compromiso a largo plazo de las RI Standard pero sin ning\xfan pago por adelantado, lo que genera un descuento total menor que las opciones que incluyen pago por adelantado."},{"id":"q419","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una opci\xf3n disponible al comprar instancias de Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"La capacidad de pujar para obtener los precios m\xe1s bajos posibles","B":"La capacidad de registrar instancias EC2 para obtener descuentos por volumen en cada hora que las instancias est\xe1n en ejecuci\xf3n","C":"La capacidad de comprar Dedicated Instances con hasta 90% de descuento","D":"La capacidad de pagar por adelantado para obtener costos por hora m\xe1s bajos"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las Reserved Instances permiten pagar por adelantado a cambio de tarifas por hora m\xe1s bajas durante un plazo de 1 o 3 a\xf1os, lo que proporciona ahorros predecibles para cargas de trabajo en estado estacionario.\\nLa capacidad de pujar por un costo por hora menor describe las Spot Instances, que se cotizan en funci\xf3n de la capacidad EC2 disponible mediante un mecanismo basado en el mercado en lugar de un modelo basado en compromiso. La capacidad de registrar instancias EC2 para obtener descuentos por volumen en cada hora que est\xe1n en ejecuci\xf3n no es una opci\xf3n real de compra de EC2. Los descuentos por volumen en AWS se aplican mediante mecanismos como facturaci\xf3n consolidada o niveles de almacenamiento de S3, no mediante el registro de instancias. La capacidad de comprar Dedicated Instances con hasta 90% de descuento es incorrecta por dos motivos: las Dedicated Instances tienen un precio superior en lugar de un descuento, y la cifra de 90% de descuento se aplica a las Spot Instances, no a las Dedicated Instances."},{"id":"q423","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 herramienta puede usar un cliente que no es de AWS para comparar el costo de los recursos de un entorno on-premises con AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Migration Evaluator"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Migration Evaluator est\xe1 dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para organizaciones que comparan costos on-premises con AWS, y proporciona un an\xe1lisis detallado de costo-beneficio incluso antes de que tengan una cuenta de AWS.\\nAWS Cost Explorer se usa para visualizar y analizar patrones de gasto en cuentas de AWS existentes, y requiere una cuenta de AWS activa para usarse. AWS Pricing Calculator estima los costos mensuales de una arquitectura de AWS planificada, pero no compara esos costos con la infraestructura on-premises. AWS Budgets se usa para establecer umbrales de gasto y recibir alertas en una cuenta de AWS existente, y no tiene capacidad para evaluar ahorros de costos de migraci\xf3n."},{"id":"q430","question":"Su CTO le ha pedido que se comunique con el soporte de AWS usando la funci\xf3n de chat para pedir orientaci\xf3n relacionada con EBS. Sin embargo, cuando abre el AWS Support Center no puede ver una forma de comunicarse con el soporte por chat. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xeda hacer?","options":{"A":"No existe una funci\xf3n de chat en el soporte de AWS","B":"La funci\xf3n de chat est\xe1 disponible para todos los planes por una tarifa adicional, pero primero debe solicitarla","C":"Como m\xednimo, actualizar al plan Business Support","D":"Actualizar del plan Basic Support al Developer Support"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El soporte por chat est\xe1 disponible a partir del plan Business Support, por lo que actualizar al menos al nivel Business es el requisito m\xednimo para acceder a la funci\xf3n de chat en el AWS Support Center.\\nEl soporte de AWS s\xed tiene una funci\xf3n de chat, pero solo est\xe1 disponible en los planes Business y Enterprise, lo que hace que esta opci\xf3n sea factualmente incorrecta. La funci\xf3n de chat no est\xe1 disponible como complemento por una tarifa adicional en los planes de nivel inferior, ya que es una funci\xf3n incluida solo en los niveles de soporte Business y Enterprise. El plan Developer Support proporciona acceso a los ingenieros de AWS Support solo por correo electr\xf3nico, y actualizar de Basic a Developer no desbloquea la funci\xf3n de chat."},{"id":"q432","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n describe mejor el modelo de precios Pay-As-You-Go de AWS?","options":{"A":"Con AWS, reemplaza gastos iniciales bajos por pagos variables altos","B":"Con AWS, reemplaza gastos iniciales bajos por pagos fijos altos","C":"Con AWS, reemplaza gastos iniciales altos por pagos fijos bajos","D":"Con AWS, reemplaza gastos de capital altos por pagos variables bajos"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El modelo Pay-As-You-Go reemplaza grandes gastos de capital iniciales por pagos variables bajos basados en el consumo real, por lo que solo paga por los recursos que usa.\\nReemplazar gastos iniciales bajos por pagos variables altos invierte el modelo de precios de AWS, que espec\xedficamente elimina grandes costos de capital iniciales en lugar de introducir nuevos costos variables altos. Reemplazar gastos iniciales bajos por pagos fijos altos no describe con precisi\xf3n ni el modelo on-premises ni el de AWS. On-premises t\xedpicamente tiene grandes gastos iniciales, y AWS los reemplaza por costos variables bajos en lugar de fijos. Reemplazar gastos iniciales altos por pagos fijos bajos captura parcialmente la idea de reducir costos iniciales, pero el precio de AWS es variable en lugar de fijo, y escala directamente con el consumo real de recursos."},{"id":"q434","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes estrategias ayuda a analizar costos en AWS?","options":{"A":"Usar etiquetas (tags) para agrupar recursos","B":"Usar AWS CloudFormation para automatizar el despliegue de recursos","C":"Desplegar recursos del mismo tipo en diferentes regiones","D":"Configurar Amazon Inspector para analizar costos autom\xe1ticamente y enviar informes por correo electr\xf3nico"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Usar etiquetas (tags) para agrupar y etiquetar recursos de AWS por proyecto, departamento o entorno permite un an\xe1lisis y una asignaci\xf3n detallados de costos mediante AWS Cost Explorer y Cost and Usage Reports.\\nUsar AWS CloudFormation para automatizar el despliegue de recursos mejora la eficiencia operativa y la repetibilidad, pero automatizar despliegues no proporciona por s\xed mismo visibilidad sobre c\xf3mo se distribuyen los costos entre las diferentes partes de la organizaci\xf3n. Desplegar recursos del mismo tipo en diferentes regiones puede ser necesario por motivos de latencia o cumplimiento, pero distribuir recursos geogr\xe1ficamente no ayuda a analizar qu\xe9 proyectos o equipos est\xe1n generando costos espec\xedficos sin etiquetas para identificar la propiedad de los recursos. Configurar Amazon Inspector para analizar costos y enviar informes por correo autom\xe1ticamente es incorrecto porque Amazon Inspector es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad automatizada que escanea en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y no tiene capacidad para analizar costos de AWS ni generar informes de facturaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q445","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes afectar\xe1 cu\xe1nto se le cobra por almacenar objetos en S3?","options":{"A":"Usar cifrado predeterminado para cualquier cantidad de buckets de S3","B":"La cantidad de vol\xfamenes EBS adjuntos a sus instancias","C":"La clase de almacenamiento usada para los objetos almacenados","D":"Crear y eliminar buckets de S3","E":"El tama\xf1o total en gigabytes de todos los objetos almacenados"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"El precio de S3 depende de la clase de almacenamiento usada y del tama\xf1o total de todos los objetos almacenados. Crear buckets, usar cifrado y los vol\xfamenes EBS no afectan los cargos de almacenamiento de S3.\\nUsar cifrado predeterminado para buckets de S3 no genera cargos de almacenamiento de S3 adicionales. El cifrado del lado del servidor con claves administradas por S3 se proporciona sin costo adicional, y cifrar objetos no aumenta el cargo de almacenamiento m\xe1s all\xe1 del precio est\xe1ndar para la clase de almacenamiento usada. La cantidad de vol\xfamenes EBS adjuntos a sus instancias EC2 es un elemento de facturaci\xf3n separado para almacenamiento en bloque y no tiene efecto en los cargos de almacenamiento de objetos de S3. Crear y eliminar buckets de S3 no genera ning\xfan cargo. AWS no cobra por la creaci\xf3n, gesti\xf3n ni eliminaci\xf3n de los buckets de S3 en s\xed, solo por los datos almacenados en ellos y las solicitudes realizadas contra ellos."},{"id":"q448","question":"Ha migrado recientemente su aplicaci\xf3n a AWS. \xbfC\xf3mo puede ver los costos de AWS aplicados a su cuenta?","options":{"A":"Usando el AWS Cost and Usage Report","B":"Usando AWS Cost Explorer","C":"Usando la AWS Pricing Calculator","D":"Usando el panel de Amazon VPC"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona la vista m\xe1s completa de sus costos reales de AWS, desglosando los cargos por servicio, recurso y per\xedodo de tiempo para que pueda ver exactamente por qu\xe9 se le est\xe1 cobrando.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas y an\xe1lisis de los patrones de gasto de AWS a lo largo del tiempo y es adecuado para explorar costos, pero Cost and Usage Report proporciona los datos sin procesar m\xe1s granulares y completos para una revisi\xf3n detallada de costos. AWS Pricing Calculator se utiliza para estimar costos futuros de los servicios de AWS antes de aprovisionarlos, y no muestra los cargos reales ya aplicados a su cuenta. El panel de Amazon VPC es una consola de redes para administrar nubes privadas virtuales, subredes y configuraci\xf3n de red, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con la visualizaci\xf3n de costos ni con la informaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q449","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son tipos v\xe1lidos de Amazon EC2 Reserved Instance?","options":{"A":"Convertible","B":"Expedited","C":"Bulk","D":"Spot","E":"Standard"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Standard Reserved Instances proporcionan el mayor descuento pero est\xe1n bloqueadas a una familia de instancias espec\xedfica, mientras que Convertible Reserved Instances ofrecen flexibilidad para cambiar los tipos de instancias con un descuento ligeramente menor. Ambos son tipos v\xe1lidos de RI.\\nExpedited es una opci\xf3n de velocidad de recuperaci\xf3n para Amazon S3 Glacier que proporciona acceso m\xe1s r\xe1pido a los datos archivados, y no es un tipo de Reserved Instance. Bulk tambi\xe9n es un nivel de recuperaci\xf3n para Amazon S3 Glacier que proporciona la recuperaci\xf3n de datos de menor costo pero m\xe1s lenta, y no es un tipo de Reserved Instance. Spot es un modelo de compra de EC2 independiente que utiliza capacidad excedente de AWS con descuentos significativos y puede ser interrumpido por AWS en cualquier momento, y no es un tipo de Reserved Instance."},{"id":"q456","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 pr\xe1ctica recomendada de AWS Cost Governance recomienda refinar las cargas de trabajo peri\xf3dicamente para aprovechar al m\xe1ximo los recursos de AWS existentes y reducir costos?","options":{"A":"Tagging Enforcement","B":"Architecture Optimization","C":"Budgeting Processes","D":"Resource Controls"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Architecture Optimization implica revisar y refinar peri\xf3dicamente las cargas de trabajo para garantizar que utilicen los tipos, tama\xf1os y configuraciones de recursos de AWS m\xe1s eficientes, reduciendo directamente los costos mientras se mantiene el rendimiento.\\nTagging Enforcement es una pr\xe1ctica de gobernanza de costos que exige que los recursos se etiqueten con etiquetas espec\xedficas antes de que se puedan crear o utilizar, lo que mejora la visibilidad y la atribuci\xf3n de costos, pero es un mecanismo de cumplimiento y visibilidad en lugar de una pr\xe1ctica centrada en refinar las cargas de trabajo para utilizar mejor los recursos existentes. Budgeting Processes implica establecer umbrales de costos y uso y realizar un seguimiento del gasto real frente a esos presupuestos, lo que ayuda a gestionar las expectativas financieras, pero no implica en s\xed mismo revisar y optimizar las configuraciones de recursos subyacentes. Resource Controls restringen qu\xe9 recursos pueden aprovisionar los usuarios o c\xf3mo se pueden configurar, y aunque evitan la creaci\xf3n de recursos innecesarios, son una barrera de gobernanza en lugar de una pr\xe1ctica de revisar peri\xf3dicamente las cargas de trabajo existentes."},{"id":"q472","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes factores afectan el costo de Amazon CloudFront?","options":{"A":"Number of Requests","B":"Traffic Distribution","C":"Number of Volumes","D":"Instance type","E":"Storage Class"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"El precio de Amazon CloudFront se basa en la cantidad de solicitudes atendidas y la distribuci\xf3n geogr\xe1fica del tr\xe1fico, ya que las diferentes regiones de ubicaciones de borde tienen diferentes niveles de precios.\\nLa cantidad de vol\xfamenes EBS adjuntos a las instancias EC2 es un elemento de facturaci\xf3n independiente para el almacenamiento en bloque y no tiene efecto en el precio de CloudFront, que se basa completamente en la entrega de contenido y la actividad de solicitudes. El tipo de instancia EC2 que ejecuta sus servidores de origen puede afectar los costos de c\xf3mputo, pero no influye en el precio de entrega de contenido de CloudFront, que cobra por la transferencia de datos de salida y las solicitudes en el borde. La clase de almacenamiento de S3 utilizada para los objetos en el origen afecta los costos de almacenamiento de S3, pero no influye directamente en el precio de CloudFront, que se basa en la entrega de contenido a los usuarios finales."},{"id":"q477","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes planes de soporte de AWS proporciona acceso \xfanicamente a las siete comprobaciones principales de AWS Trusted Advisor?","options":{"A":"Business & Enterprise Support","B":"Basic & Developer Support","C":"Developer & Enterprise Support","D":"Developer & Business Support"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Los planes Basic y Developer Support proporcionan acceso \xfanicamente a las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor. El conjunto completo de comprobaciones de Trusted Advisor requiere un plan Business o Enterprise Support.\\nLos planes Business y Enterprise Support proporcionan ambos acceso al conjunto completo de comprobaciones de Trusted Advisor en lugar de solo a las siete comprobaciones principales, por lo que esta opci\xf3n describe los planes con el mayor acceso a Trusted Advisor en lugar de aquellos limitados a siete comprobaciones. Developer y Enterprise Support es una combinaci\xf3n incorrecta porque Developer Support est\xe1 limitado a las siete comprobaciones principales mientras que Enterprise Support tiene acceso al conjunto completo, lo que significa que est\xe1n en extremos opuestos del espectro de acceso a Trusted Advisor. Developer y Business Support es una combinaci\xf3n incorrecta porque Business Support proporciona el conjunto completo de comprobaciones de Trusted Advisor mientras que Developer Support est\xe1 limitado a las siete comprobaciones principales, por lo que solo Developer Support pertenece a esta categor\xeda de acceso limitado."},{"id":"q482","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son los beneficios del servicio AWS Organizations?","options":{"A":"Controlar el acceso a los servicios de AWS","B":"Ayudar a las organizaciones a dise\xf1ar y mantener una ruta acelerada hacia una adopci\xf3n exitosa de la nube","C":"Administrar los m\xe9todos de pago de su organizaci\xf3n","D":"Ayudar a las organizaciones a lograr los resultados comerciales deseados con AWS","E":"Consolidar la facturaci\xf3n en varias cuentas de AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"AWS Organizations permite el control centralizado sobre el acceso a los servicios de AWS mediante Service Control Policies y consolida la facturaci\xf3n de varias cuentas en un \xfanico pago.\\nAyudar a las organizaciones a dise\xf1ar y mantener una ruta acelerada hacia una adopci\xf3n exitosa de la nube describe el AWS Cloud Adoption Framework, que es un marco de orientaci\xf3n creado por AWS Professional Services en lugar de una capacidad de AWS Organizations. La administraci\xf3n de los m\xe9todos de pago de su organizaci\xf3n se realiza a trav\xe9s de la consola de AWS Billing and Cost Management a nivel de cuenta individual, y no es una caracter\xedstica espec\xedfica proporcionada por AWS Organizations. Ayudar a las organizaciones a lograr los resultados comerciales deseados con AWS describe AWS Professional Services, que trabaja directamente con los clientes en la estrategia e implementaci\xf3n en la nube en lugar de ser una funci\xf3n de AWS Organizations."},{"id":"q484","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS le permite comprar soluciones de software y servicios de terceros que se ejecutan en recursos de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Application Discovery Service","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Marketplace","D":"AWS Resource Groups"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Marketplace es un cat\xe1logo digital donde los clientes pueden encontrar, comprar e implementar soluciones de software de terceros que se ejecutan en AWS, incluidas AMIs, aplicaciones SaaS y productos de datos.\\nAWS Application Discovery Service recopila informaci\xf3n sobre servidores y cargas de trabajo locales para ayudar a planificar migraciones a la nube, y es una herramienta de planificaci\xf3n de migraciones sin capacidad para comprar o implementar software de terceros. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaci\xf3n y an\xe1lisis de los patrones de gasto y uso de AWS a lo largo del tiempo, y es una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de costos en lugar de una plataforma de adquisici\xf3n de software. AWS Resource Groups organiza los recursos de AWS en grupos l\xf3gicos basados en etiquetas o pilas de CloudFormation para facilitar la administraci\xf3n, y no tiene ning\xfan rol en el descubrimiento ni la compra de soluciones de software de terceros."},{"id":"q489","question":"\xbfA qu\xe9 puede acceder visitando la URL: http://status.aws.amazon.com?","options":{"A":"AWS Billing Dashboard","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Health Dashboard","D":"AWS Security Hub"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard en status.aws.amazon.com proporciona informaci\xf3n en tiempo real e hist\xf3rica sobre la disponibilidad y el estado operativo de todos los servicios de AWS en todas las regiones.\\nSe accede a AWS Billing Dashboard a trav\xe9s de la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS y muestra res\xfamenes de costos y uso de su cuenta, y no es accesible en la URL status.aws.amazon.com. AWS Cost Explorer es una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de costos para visualizar y analizar los patrones de gasto de AWS a lo largo del tiempo, y se accede a ella a trav\xe9s de la secci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n de la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS en lugar de la URL de estado p\xfablica. AWS Security Hub proporciona una vista centralizada de las alertas de seguridad y el estado de cumplimiento en un entorno de AWS, y es una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de la postura de seguridad a la que se accede a trav\xe9s de la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS en lugar de la URL de estado p\xfablica."},{"id":"q492","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 migrando cargas de trabajo de producci\xf3n a AWS y le preocupa la gesti\xf3n de costos entre diferentes departamentos. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n deber\xeda implementar la empresa para categorizar y rastrear el gasto de AWS?","options":{"A":"Usar el servicio AWS Pricing Calculator para monitorear los costos incurridos por cada departamento","B":"Usar Amazon Aurora para pronosticar el gasto de AWS seg\xfan el uso","C":"Aplicar etiquetas de asignaci\xf3n de costos para segmentar los costos de AWS por diferentes proyectos y departamentos","D":"Configurar AWS Price List API para recibir actualizaciones de facturaci\xf3n para cada departamento autom\xe1ticamente"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las etiquetas de asignaci\xf3n de costos son metadatos de clave-valor que se aplican a los recursos de AWS, lo que permite segmentar y categorizar los costos por proyecto, departamento o entorno en Cost Explorer y en los informes de facturaci\xf3n.\\nAWS Pricing Calculator se utiliza para estimar costos futuros de nuevas configuraciones de servicios de AWS antes de implementarlas, y no monitorea ni categoriza el gasto real incurrido por cargas de trabajo activas. Amazon Aurora es un servicio de base de datos relacional totalmente administrado compatible con MySQL y PostgreSQL, y es un motor de base de datos sin capacidad para pronosticar el gasto de AWS ni categorizar costos por departamento. AWS Price List API proporciona acceso program\xe1tico a los datos de precios de los servicios de AWS para crear herramientas de precios personalizadas, y entrega informaci\xf3n de precios en lugar de actualizar autom\xe1ticamente las categor\xedas de facturaci\xf3n seg\xfan el uso por departamento."},{"id":"q495","question":"Tiene varias cuentas de AWS independientes y desea disminuir sus cargos mensuales de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xeda hacer?","options":{"A":"Intentar eliminar las cuentas de AWS innecesarias","B":"Agregar las cuentas a una AWS Organization y usar Consolidated Billing","C":"Rastrear los cargos de AWS incurridos por las cuentas miembro","D":"Habilitar el precio por niveles de AWS antes de aprovisionar recursos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Agregar cuentas independientes a una AWS Organization con Consolidated Billing consolida el uso de todas las cuentas, lo que permite descuentos por volumen mediante niveles de uso combinados y simplifica el pago mediante una sola factura.\\nEliminar cuentas de AWS innecesarias puede reducir el gasto, pero no desbloquea descuentos por volumen ni aborda la ineficiencia estructural de facturaci\xf3n de ejecutar cuentas separadas. Rastrear los cargos de AWS incurridos por las cuentas miembro es una actividad de visibilidad de costos y no reduce los cargos por s\xed sola. El precio por niveles de AWS se aplica autom\xe1ticamente seg\xfan el volumen de uso y no se puede habilitar manualmente antes de aprovisionar recursos."},{"id":"q497","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 proporciona el plan de soporte \\"Business\\" de AWS?","options":{"A":"Acceso al conjunto completo de comprobaciones de Trusted Advisor","B":"Support Concierge Service","C":"Soporte con tiempo de respuesta inferior a 15 minutos si su sistema cr\xedtico para el negocio se cae","D":"AWS Support API","E":"Gesti\xf3n proactiva de cuentas t\xe9cnicas"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"El plan Business Support proporciona acceso al conjunto completo de comprobaciones de Trusted Advisor en las cinco categor\xedas, y a AWS Support API para la gesti\xf3n program\xe1tica de casos de soporte.\\nSupport Concierge Service est\xe1 disponible exclusivamente para clientes de Enterprise Support para orientaci\xf3n dedicada sobre facturaci\xf3n y cuentas, y no est\xe1 incluido en el plan Business Support. Un tiempo de respuesta menor a 15 minutos para fallas de sistemas cr\xedticos para el negocio es una caracter\xedstica de Enterprise Support. Business Support proporciona un SLA de respuesta de una hora para casos de ca\xedda de sistemas de producci\xf3n. La gesti\xf3n proactiva de cuentas t\xe9cnicas es una caracter\xedstica de Enterprise Support. Business Support no incluye un Technical Account Manager designado que proporcione orientaci\xf3n proactiva continua."},{"id":"q501","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 afirmaci\xf3n es verdadera con respecto a los precios de AWS?","options":{"A":"Con el modelo de precios de pago por uso de AWS, no tiene que pagar ninguna tarifa por adelantado","B":"No tiene responsabilidad por los costos de licencias de software de terceros","C":"Solo paga por los servicios individuales que necesita, sin contratos a largo plazo","D":"Para algunos servicios, debe pagar una tarifa de inicio para poner en marcha el servicio","E":"No hay reservas en AWS, solo paga por lo que usa"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"El modelo de precios de pago por uso de AWS no requiere tarifas por adelantado ni contratos a largo plazo. Solo paga por los servicios individuales que utiliza, sin compromisos m\xednimos.\\nLos clientes s\xed conservan la responsabilidad por los costos de licencias de software de terceros cuando ejecutan aplicaciones en AWS, a menos que utilicen licencias proporcionadas por AWS mediante servicios como Amazon RDS. AWS no asume obligaciones de licenciamiento de software en nombre del cliente. Para algunos servicios, AWS s\xed ofrece una tarifa de inicio, y las Reserved Instances requieren pago por adelantado. Sin embargo, el modelo de pago por uso para servicios On-Demand no requiere tarifa de inicio. AWS s\xed ofrece reservas, como Reserved Instances y Savings Plans, que proporcionan descuentos a cambio de compromisos de uso, por lo que la afirmaci\xf3n de que no hay reservas en AWS es incorrecta."},{"id":"q506","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 factores determinan c\xf3mo se le cobra cuando usa AWS Lambda?","options":{"A":"Almacenamiento consumido","B":"N\xfamero de solicitudes a sus funciones","C":"N\xfamero de vol\xfamenes","D":"Grupos de colocaci\xf3n","E":"Tiempo de c\xf3mputo consumido"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","E"],"explanation":"El precio de AWS Lambda se basa en el n\xfamero de solicitudes a sus funciones y en el tiempo de c\xf3mputo consumido, medido en GB-segundos de duraci\xf3n de ejecuci\xf3n, con un generoso nivel gratuito para ambas dimensiones.\\nEl almacenamiento consumido no es un factor de precio de Lambda de la misma manera. Si bien las funciones de Lambda tienen almacenamiento temporal disponible en el entorno de ejecuci\xf3n, el precio de Lambda se basa en solicitudes y duraci\xf3n de c\xf3mputo en lugar de consumo de almacenamiento. El n\xfamero de vol\xfamenes es un concepto de precio de Amazon EBS para almacenamiento en bloques adjunto a instancias EC2, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con c\xf3mo se cobran las funciones de Lambda. Los grupos de colocaci\xf3n son una caracter\xedstica para controlar c\xf3mo se colocan las instancias EC2 en el hardware subyacente para optimizar el rendimiento de red o la tolerancia a fallos, y son un concepto de EC2 sin conexi\xf3n con el precio de Lambda."},{"id":"q508","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes acciones puede reducir los costos de Amazon EBS?","options":{"A":"Eliminar los buckets no utilizados","B":"Usar reservas","C":"Eliminar las instant\xe1neas innecesarias","D":"Cambiar el tipo de volumen","E":"Distribuir las solicitudes entre varios vol\xfamenes"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"Eliminar las instant\xe1neas de EBS innecesarias elimina los costos de almacenamiento de las copias de seguridad que ya no necesita, y cambiar a un tipo de volumen m\xe1s rentable, como pasar de gp2 a gp3, reduce el costo por gigabyte para la misma capacidad.\\nEliminar los buckets no utilizados es una acci\xf3n de Amazon S3 que reduce los costos de almacenamiento de S3, pero los buckets de S3 son completamente independientes de los vol\xfamenes de EBS y eliminarlos no tiene efecto en el precio de EBS. Usar reservas no es un mecanismo de precio disponible para los vol\xfamenes de EBS. El precio de EBS se basa en el almacenamiento aprovisionado y los datos de instant\xe1neas, en lugar de reservas basadas en compromisos. Distribuir las solicitudes entre varios vol\xfamenes puede mejorar el rendimiento al reducir la contenci\xf3n de E/S, pero distribuir los datos entre m\xe1s vol\xfamenes aumenta el almacenamiento aprovisionado total y, por lo tanto, aumenta en lugar de reducir los costos de EBS."},{"id":"q511","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes opciones le facilita categorizar, administrar y filtrar sus recursos?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS Service Catalog","C":"AWS Directory Service","D":"AWS Tagging"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las etiquetas de AWS son pares de metadatos de clave-valor adjuntos a los recursos, lo que facilita categorizar, filtrar, buscar y administrar recursos por proyecto, entorno, propietario o cualquier otro criterio personalizado.\\nAmazon CloudWatch es un servicio de monitoreo y observabilidad para recopilar m\xe9tricas, registros y alarmas de los recursos de AWS, no una herramienta de categorizaci\xf3n o filtrado de recursos. AWS Service Catalog permite a las organizaciones crear y administrar cat\xe1logos aprobados de servicios de TI para su implementaci\xf3n interna, no para etiquetar o filtrar recursos existentes. AWS Directory Service proporciona Microsoft Active Directory administrado para la autenticaci\xf3n de usuarios y la administraci\xf3n de acceso, sin relaci\xf3n con la categorizaci\xf3n de recursos ni la administraci\xf3n de metadatos."},{"id":"q515","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes planes de AWS Support le brinda acceso 24/7 a Cloud Support Engineers por correo electr\xf3nico y tel\xe9fono?","options":{"A":"Developer","B":"Premium","C":"Enterprise","D":"Standard","E":"Business"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Los planes Business Support y Enterprise Support proporcionan acceso 24/7 a Cloud Support Engineers por tel\xe9fono, correo electr\xf3nico y chat, lo que los convierte en los dos planes que cumplen este requisito.\\nDeveloper Support proporciona acceso por correo electr\xf3nico a Cloud Support Engineers solo durante el horario comercial y no incluye acceso telef\xf3nico o por chat 24/7. Premium Support no es un nombre real de plan de soporte de AWS. Los niveles reales de soporte de AWS son Basic, Developer, Business, Enterprise On-Ramp y Enterprise. Standard Support no es un nombre real de plan de soporte de AWS. No corresponde a ninguno de los cinco niveles de planes de AWS Support."},{"id":"q520","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son los beneficios del servicio AWS Marketplace?","options":{"A":"Protege a los clientes realizando comprobaciones de seguridad peri\xf3dicas en los productos listados","B":"Facturaci\xf3n por segundo","C":"Proporciona opciones m\xe1s econ\xf3micas para comprar instancias Amazon EC2 bajo demanda","D":"Proporciona opciones de precios flexibles que se adaptan a la mayor\xeda de las necesidades de los clientes","E":"Proporciona soluciones de software que se ejecutan en AWS o en cualquier otro proveedor de nube"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"AWS Marketplace ofrece opciones de precios flexibles que incluyen modelos por hora, mensuales, anuales y de traiga su propia licencia, y proporciona soluciones de software que pueden ejecutarse en AWS u otras plataformas en la nube, lo que brinda a los clientes una amplia variedad de opciones y flexibilidad de costos.\\nAWS Marketplace no realiza comprobaciones de seguridad peri\xf3dicas en los productos listados. La responsabilidad de la seguridad del producto sigue siendo de los proveedores de software independientes que listan sus productos. La facturaci\xf3n por segundo es una caracter\xedstica de precios de EC2, no un beneficio de AWS Marketplace. Proporcionar opciones m\xe1s econ\xf3micas para comprar instancias EC2 bajo demanda no es una funci\xf3n de AWS Marketplace, que es un cat\xe1logo para software de terceros, no un mecanismo para descontar los precios de c\xf3mputo de AWS."},{"id":"q522","question":"Est\xe1 planificando lanzar una campa\xf1a publicitaria durante el pr\xf3ximo fin de semana para promocionar un nuevo producto digital. Se espera que haya picos fuertes de carga durante el per\xedodo de la campa\xf1a y no puede permitirse ning\xfan tiempo de inactividad. Necesita recursos de c\xf3mputo adicionales para manejar la carga adicional. \xbfCu\xe1l es la opci\xf3n de compra de instancias EC2 m\xe1s rentable para este trabajo?","options":{"A":"Savings Plans","B":"Instancias Spot","C":"Instancias reservadas","D":"Instancias bajo demanda"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las instancias bajo demanda son la mejor opci\xf3n para cargas de trabajo impredecibles y de corto plazo en las que el tiempo de inactividad es inaceptable, ya que proporcionan disponibilidad garantizada durante el per\xedodo de la campa\xf1a sin ning\xfan riesgo de interrupci\xf3n.\\nLos Savings Plans ofrecen descuentos a cambio de un compromiso de un nivel de uso constante durante uno o tres a\xf1os, y est\xe1n dise\xf1ados para cargas de trabajo de estado estable a largo plazo, no para una campa\xf1a publicitaria de fin de semana a corto plazo. Las instancias Spot utilizan capacidad ociosa de AWS con descuentos significativos, pero AWS puede interrumpirlas en cualquier momento, lo que las hace inadecuadas para una campa\xf1a en la que no se puede permitir tiempo de inactividad. Las instancias reservadas requieren un compromiso m\xednimo de un a\xf1o, lo que costar\xeda significativamente m\xe1s durante un solo fin de semana que pagar bajo demanda por ese per\xedodo corto."},{"id":"q528","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son algunos de los beneficios de usar instancias EC2 bajo demanda?","options":{"A":"Proporcionan capacidad gratuita al probar sus nuevas aplicaciones","B":"Son m\xe1s econ\xf3micas que todas las dem\xe1s opciones de EC2","C":"Eliminan la necesidad de comprar capacidad de “red de seguridad” para manejar picos de tr\xe1fico peri\xf3dicos","D":"Solo requieren de 1 a 2 d\xedas para la instalaci\xf3n y configuraci\xf3n","E":"Puede aumentar o disminuir su capacidad de c\xf3mputo seg\xfan las demandas de su aplicaci\xf3n"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","E"],"explanation":"Las instancias bajo demanda eliminan la necesidad de comprar de antemano capacidad de red de seguridad para picos de tr\xe1fico, y permiten aumentar o disminuir la capacidad de c\xf3mputo en cualquier momento para ajustarla a la demanda de la aplicaci\xf3n.\\nLas instancias bajo demanda no proporcionan capacidad gratuita para pruebas. Todo el tiempo de c\xf3mputo se cobra a la tarifa est\xe1ndar de bajo demanda, y no existe un nivel de uso gratuito espec\xedficamente para pruebas con instancias bajo demanda m\xe1s all\xe1 del nivel gratuito general de AWS. Las instancias bajo demanda no son m\xe1s econ\xf3micas que todas las dem\xe1s opciones de EC2. Las instancias Spot ofrecen descuentos de hasta el 90 % y las instancias reservadas ofrecen ahorros de hasta el 72 %, ambas significativamente m\xe1s econ\xf3micas que bajo demanda para cargas de trabajo adecuadas. Las instancias bajo demanda no requieren de 1 a 2 d\xedas para la instalaci\xf3n y configuraci\xf3n. Se pueden lanzar en minutos a trav\xe9s de la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS, la CLI o la API sin un proceso de aprovisionamiento prolongado."},{"id":"q536","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 herramienta de AWS Cost Management le permite ver los datos m\xe1s granulares sobre su factura de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","D":"Panel de facturaci\xf3n de AWS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona los datos de facturaci\xf3n m\xe1s granulares disponibles, desglosando los costos por hora, servicio, recurso y etiquetas para an\xe1lisis y auditor\xeda detallados.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas de los patrones de gasto de AWS a lo largo del tiempo con capacidades de filtrado y agrupaci\xf3n, y aunque es excelente para el an\xe1lisis y la previsi\xf3n, proporciona datos sin procesar menos granulares que el Cost and Usage Report. AWS Budgets permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costos y uso y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, y es una herramienta de gobernanza de costos y alertas en lugar de un servicio de informes de datos de facturaci\xf3n granulares. El panel de facturaci\xf3n de AWS proporciona un resumen de alto nivel de los cargos de su mes actual y del historial de facturaci\xf3n reciente, y ofrece una vista de costos mucho menos granular que los datos detallados por hora y a nivel de recurso del Cost and Usage Report."},{"id":"q543","question":"Un administrador necesita implementar r\xe1pidamente una soluci\xf3n de TI popular y comenzar a usarla de inmediato. \xbfD\xf3nde puede encontrar ayuda el administrador?","options":{"A":"Documentaci\xf3n de AWS Well-Architected Framework","B":"Amazon CloudFront","C":"AWS CodeCommit","D":"Implementaciones de referencia de AWS Quick Start"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las implementaciones de referencia de AWS Quick Start proporcionan plantillas automatizadas de referencia (gold standard) que implementan soluciones de TI populares en AWS en minutos, lo que brinda a los administradores una v\xeda r\xe1pida hacia entornos listos para producci\xf3n creados sobre las mejores pr\xe1cticas de AWS.\\nLa documentaci\xf3n de AWS Well-Architected Framework proporciona orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica y mejores pr\xe1cticas para dise\xf1ar cargas de trabajo en la nube en los seis pilares, pero es un documento de referencia en lugar de una herramienta de implementaci\xf3n que pueda aprovisionar una soluci\xf3n de inmediato. Amazon CloudFront es una red de entrega de contenido que almacena en cach\xe9 y distribuye contenido a los usuarios en ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo, y no tiene capacidad para implementar o aprovisionar soluciones de TI. AWS CodeCommit es un servicio administrado de control de c\xf3digo fuente basado en Git para almacenar y versionar repositorios de c\xf3digo, y es una herramienta para desarrolladores sin capacidad de implementaci\xf3n r\xe1pida ni aprovisionamiento de soluciones."},{"id":"q551","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes acciones relacionadas con la seguridad est\xe1n disponibles sin costo?","options":{"A":"Llamar a AWS Support","B":"Contactar a AWS Professional Services para solicitar un taller","C":"Acceder a foros, blogs y documentos t\xe9cnicos","D":"Asistir a clases de AWS en una universidad local"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Los foros, blogs y documentos t\xe9cnicos de AWS son recursos de seguridad disponibles p\xfablicamente a los que cualquiera puede acceder sin costo, y proporcionan orientaci\xf3n sobre mejores pr\xe1cticas de seguridad, patrones de arquitectura y consideraciones de cumplimiento.\\nLlamar a AWS Support para obtener asistencia t\xe9cnica relacionada con la seguridad requiere como m\xednimo un plan Developer Support, que tiene una tarifa mensual asociada, lo que convierte el soporte telef\xf3nico y por chat con ingenieros en un servicio pago en lugar de una acci\xf3n gratuita. Contactar a AWS Professional Services para solicitar un taller es un servicio pago. AWS Professional Services cobra por el tiempo de consultor\xeda y la impartici\xf3n del taller en lugar de proporcionar estos servicios sin costo. Asistir a clases de AWS en una universidad local no es un servicio proporcionado por AWS y genera tarifas acad\xe9micas no relacionadas con los precios de AWS. AWS s\xed ofrece su propia capacitaci\xf3n a trav\xe9s de AWS Training and Certification, pero los cursos impartidos por un instructor tienen un costo."},{"id":"q566","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se utiliza para pagar las facturas de AWS y monitorear el uso y los costos del presupuesto?","options":{"A":"AWS Billing and Cost Management","B":"Facturaci\xf3n consolidada","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"Amazon QuickSight"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Billing and Cost Management es el servicio central para pagar las facturas de AWS, monitorear el uso y administrar presupuestos, y proporciona herramientas como Cost Explorer, Budgets y alertas de facturaci\xf3n en un solo lugar.\\nLa facturaci\xf3n consolidada es una caracter\xedstica de AWS Organizations que combina el uso y las facturas de varias cuentas en una sola factura, y es una caracter\xedstica de agregaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio independiente que se utilice para pagar facturas y monitorear el uso. Amazon CloudWatch recopila m\xe9tricas, registros y datos de eventos de los servicios y recursos de AWS para proporcionar monitoreo operativo y alertas, y aunque puede monitorear m\xe9tricas de facturaci\xf3n, es un servicio de monitoreo en lugar del servicio principal para pagar facturas y administrar presupuestos. Amazon QuickSight es un servicio de inteligencia empresarial sin servidor para crear paneles interactivos y visualizaciones a partir de datos empresariales, y no se utiliza para pagar facturas de AWS ni administrar presupuestos de cuentas de AWS."},{"id":"q568","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de AWS permite a una empresa aprovechar los niveles de uso de los servicios en varias cuentas de miembro?","options":{"A":"Pol\xedticas de control de servicio (SCP)","B":"Facturaci\xf3n consolidada","C":"Instancias reservadas con pago total por adelantado","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La facturaci\xf3n consolidada a trav\xe9s de AWS Organizations combina el uso de todas las cuentas de miembro, lo que permite a la organizaci\xf3n alcanzar niveles de volumen superiores y calificar para descuentos basados en el uso que las cuentas individuales no lograr\xedan de forma independiente.\\nLas pol\xedticas de control de servicio son barreras de permisos aplicadas a trav\xe9s de AWS Organizations que restringen qu\xe9 servicios y acciones de AWS est\xe1n disponibles dentro de las cuentas de miembro, y son un mecanismo de gobernanza en lugar de una caracter\xedstica que habilite beneficios por niveles de uso. Las instancias reservadas con pago total por adelantado son un compromiso de compra en el que se paga el costo total de la reserva al inicio del plazo a cambio del descuento m\xe1ximo, y se aplican a una sola cuenta en lugar de agregar el uso en varias cuentas. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas y an\xe1lisis de los patrones de gasto de AWS a lo largo del tiempo, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de costos en lugar de una caracter\xedstica que agregue el uso entre cuentas para calificar para niveles de precios por volumen."},{"id":"q582","question":"Si cada departamento dentro de una empresa tiene su propia cuenta de AWS, \xbfcu\xe1l es una forma de habilitar la facturaci\xf3n consolidada?","options":{"A":"Usar AWS Budgets en cada cuenta para pagar solo hasta el presupuesto","B":"Contactar a AWS Support para recibir una factura mensual","C":"Crear una AWS Organization desde la cuenta pagadora e invitar a las otras cuentas a unirse","D":"Colocar todas las facturas en un bucket de Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3), cargar los datos en Amazon Redshift y luego ejecutar un informe de facturaci\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Crear una AWS Organization desde la cuenta pagadora e invitar a las otras cuentas a unirse habilita la facturaci\xf3n consolidada, centralizando el pago en una sola factura mientras se mantiene la independencia de la cuenta de cada departamento.\\nUsar AWS Budgets en cada cuenta para pagar solo hasta el presupuesto es una pr\xe1ctica de gobernanza de costos para cuentas individuales y no consolida los m\xe9todos de pago separados entre cuentas en una estructura de facturaci\xf3n unificada. Contactar a AWS Support para recibir una factura mensual no es un mecanismo de autoservicio para habilitar la facturaci\xf3n consolidada, la cual se configura directamente a trav\xe9s de AWS Organizations en lugar de mediante una solicitud de soporte. Colocar todas las facturas en un bucket de S3 y cargar los datos en Redshift para ejecutar un informe de facturaci\xf3n crea una soluci\xf3n de informes personalizada que proporciona visibilidad de los costos, pero no consolida la facturaci\xf3n real y los m\xe9todos de pago en una sola factura."},{"id":"q591","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio permite a una empresa con m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS combinar su uso para obtener descuentos por volumen?","options":{"A":"AWS Server Migration Service","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Organizations con facturaci\xf3n consolidada combina el uso de todas las cuentas miembro, lo que permite a la organizaci\xf3n calificar para niveles de precios por volumen y para el uso compartido de Reserved Instances que las cuentas individuales no pueden lograr por s\xed solas.\\nAWS Server Migration Service es una herramienta para migrar servidores on-premises a AWS y no tiene relaci\xf3n con combinar el uso entre cuentas ni con obtener descuentos por volumen. AWS Budgets permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costos y uso, y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales; es una herramienta de gobernanza de costos y alertas, no un servicio que agrega el uso entre cuentas para obtener descuentos por volumen. AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones sobre seguridad, costos, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos; es una herramienta de asesoramiento, no un servicio que combina el uso entre m\xfaltiples cuentas."},{"id":"q629","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona alertas personalizadas cuando un evento de AWS puede afectar los recursos de AWS de una empresa?","options":{"A":"AWS Health Dashboard","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS Infrastructure Event Management"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard env\xeda alertas personalizadas proactivas cuando los eventos de AWS pueden afectar sus recursos espec\xedficos, y proporciona una gu\xeda de remediaci\xf3n adaptada a su cuenta en lugar de mostrar el estado general del servicio.\\nAmazon CloudWatch recopila m\xe9tricas, registros y datos de eventos de los servicios y recursos de AWS para proporcionar monitoreo operativo y alertas sobre el rendimiento y la salud de sus propias cargas de trabajo, pero no env\xeda alertas personalizadas sobre eventos a nivel de servicio de AWS que puedan afectar sus recursos. AWS Trusted Advisor analiza su entorno de AWS seg\xfan las comprobaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas en materia de costos, rendimiento, seguridad y tolerancia a fallos, pero no env\xeda alertas sobre eventos de servicio de AWS ni interrupciones de infraestructura. AWS Infrastructure Event Management es un programa de soporte de pago para planificar eventos a gran escala, como lanzamientos de productos, y no es un servicio de alertas para eventos de estado de AWS que afecten sus recursos."},{"id":"q630","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de las siguientes son categor\xedas de AWS Trusted Advisor?","options":{"A":"Tolerancia a fallos","B":"Uso de instancias","C":"Infraestructura","D":"Rendimiento","E":"Capacidad de almacenamiento"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones en cinco categor\xedas: Optimizaci\xf3n de costos, Rendimiento, Seguridad, Tolerancia a fallos y L\xedmites de servicio. Tolerancia a fallos y Rendimiento son ambas categor\xedas oficiales.\\nUso de instancias no es una categor\xeda de Trusted Advisor. Las recomendaciones relacionadas con el uso se incluyen en Optimizaci\xf3n de costos, en lugar de una categor\xeda dedicada al uso de instancias. Infraestructura no es una categor\xeda de Trusted Advisor y no corresponde a ninguna de las cinco \xe1reas de recomendaci\xf3n definidas. Capacidad de almacenamiento no es una categor\xeda de Trusted Advisor. Las comprobaciones relacionadas con el almacenamiento aparecen dentro de Optimizaci\xf3n de costos o Tolerancia a fallos, seg\xfan la naturaleza de la recomendaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q643","question":"\xbfCu\xe1ndo es beneficioso para una empresa usar una Spot Instance?","options":{"A":"Cuando hay flexibilidad respecto a cu\xe1ndo debe ejecutarse una aplicaci\xf3n","B":"Cuando hay cargas de trabajo cr\xedticas para la misi\xf3n","C":"Cuando se necesita capacidad dedicada","D":"Cuando una instancia no debe detenerse"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Las Spot Instances son ideales cuando hay flexibilidad respecto a cu\xe1ndo debe ejecutarse una aplicaci\xf3n, porque usan capacidad de EC2 sobrante con descuentos de hasta el 90 % y pueden interrumpirse cuando esa capacidad se necesita en otro lugar.\\nLas cargas de trabajo cr\xedticas para la misi\xf3n requieren disponibilidad garantizada y no pueden tolerar las interrupciones que pueden experimentar las Spot Instances, por lo que On-Demand o Reserved Instances son m\xe1s adecuadas para esos casos de uso. Cuando se necesita capacidad dedicada, son adecuadas las Dedicated Hosts o las Dedicated Instances, seg\xfan los requisitos de licenciamiento y cumplimiento, ya que proporcionan aislamiento f\xedsico que las Spot Instances que se ejecutan en infraestructura compartida no pueden garantizar. Cuando una instancia no debe detenerse, las Spot Instances no son adecuadas porque AWS puede reclamar la capacidad de Spot en cualquier momento con un aviso de dos minutos, por lo que On-Demand o Reserved Instances son la opci\xf3n correcta para cargas de trabajo que deben permanecer activas de forma continua."},{"id":"q645","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo cobra AWS por AWS Lambda?","options":{"A":"Los usuarios pujan el precio m\xe1ximo que est\xe1n dispuestos a pagar por hora","B":"Los usuarios eligen un plazo de pago por adelantado de 1, 3 o 5 a\xf1os","C":"Los usuarios pagan por el almacenamiento permanente requerido en un sistema de archivos o en una base de datos","D":"Los usuarios pagan seg\xfan la cantidad de solicitudes y los recursos de c\xf3mputo consumidos"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Lambda cobra seg\xfan la cantidad de solicitudes y los recursos de c\xf3mputo consumidos, medidos en GB-segundos de duraci\xf3n de ejecuci\xf3n, lo que lo convierte en un verdadero modelo de precios sin servidor y de pago por uso.\\nQue los usuarios pujen el precio m\xe1ximo que est\xe1n dispuestos a pagar por hora describe el modelo de compra de Spot Instances para EC2, no Lambda, que tiene precios fijos de pago por uso en lugar de un mecanismo de puja. Elegir un plazo de pago por adelantado de 1, 3 o 5 a\xf1os describe el modelo de precios de Reserved Instances para EC2 y RDS, no Lambda, que no tiene una opci\xf3n de precios basada en compromiso. Pagar por el almacenamiento permanente requerido en un sistema de archivos o en una base de datos describe los precios de los servicios de almacenamiento, no los precios de Lambda, que se basan en la ejecuci\xf3n de funciones en lugar del consumo de almacenamiento persistente."},{"id":"q647","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 ayudar\xe1 a una empresa a realizar un an\xe1lisis de costo-beneficio de la migraci\xf3n a la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator permite a las empresas modelar configuraciones on-premises o configuraciones actuales en la nube junto con configuraciones proyectadas de AWS para producir una comparaci\xf3n de costos detallada, lo que la convierte en la herramienta dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para realizar un an\xe1lisis de costo-beneficio de la migraci\xf3n a AWS.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas del gasto y los patrones de uso existentes en AWS con capacidad de previsi\xf3n, y requiere datos de uso activos de AWS para generar an\xe1lisis, en lugar de proporcionar comparaciones de costo-beneficio previas a la migraci\xf3n. AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona el desglose m\xe1s granular de los cargos reales de AWS ya incurridos, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de facturaci\xf3n hist\xf3rica en lugar de una herramienta de comparaci\xf3n de costo-beneficio para evaluar una posible migraci\xf3n. AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS existente y proporciona recomendaciones sobre seguridad, costos, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento para optimizar una implementaci\xf3n existente en lugar de una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de costo-beneficio para una decisi\xf3n de migraci\xf3n."},{"id":"q648","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes proporciona la capacidad de compartir los beneficios de costo de las Reserved Instances entre cuentas de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer entre cuentas de AWS","B":"Cuentas vinculadas y facturaci\xf3n consolidada","C":"Informe de utilizaci\xf3n de Reserved Instances de Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2)","D":"Informe de uso de instancias de Amazon EC2 entre cuentas de AWS"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las cuentas vinculadas bajo la facturaci\xf3n consolidada en AWS Organizations comparten autom\xe1ticamente los beneficios de costo de las Reserved Instances entre todas las cuentas miembro, por lo que las RI no utilizadas en una cuenta se aplican al uso correspondiente en otra.\\nAWS Cost Explorer entre cuentas de AWS proporciona visualizaci\xf3n y an\xe1lisis del gasto entre cuentas vinculadas, pero no habilita por s\xed mismo el uso compartido de los beneficios de Reserved Instances, lo cual es una funci\xf3n de la facturaci\xf3n consolidada dentro de AWS Organizations. El Informe de utilizaci\xf3n de Reserved Instances de Amazon EC2 muestra con qu\xe9 eficacia se est\xe1n utilizando sus Reserved Instances, y proporciona visibilidad sobre la utilizaci\xf3n de RI en lugar de ser el mecanismo que habilita el uso compartido de los beneficios de RI entre cuentas. Los Informes de uso de instancias de Amazon EC2 muestran patrones de uso hist\xf3ricos de las instancias EC2 dentro de una cuenta, y son una herramienta de informes en lugar del mecanismo que habilita el uso compartido de los beneficios de Reserved Instances entre cuentas."},{"id":"q649","question":"Una empresa tiene varias cuentas de AWS y desea simplificar y consolidar su proceso de facturaci\xf3n. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS lograr\xe1 esto?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost and Usage Reports","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"AWS Cost Explorer","D":"AWS Budgets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Organizations ofrece facturaci\xf3n consolidada, combinando varias cuentas de AWS bajo una \xfanica cuenta de pagador para simplificar el proceso de facturaci\xf3n con una sola factura que cubre todas las cuentas miembro.\\nAWS Cost and Usage Reports proporciona el desglose m\xe1s granular de los cargos reales de AWS por servicio, recurso y per\xedodo de tiempo, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de facturaci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio que consolida el pago entre varias cuentas. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas de los patrones de gasto de AWS y admite filtrado y previsi\xf3n, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de costos en lugar de un servicio que simplifica la facturaci\xf3n al consolidar varias cuentas. AWS Budgets permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costo y uso y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, y es una herramienta de gobernanza de costos y alertas en lugar de un servicio de consolidaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q655","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 m\xe9todos se pueden utilizar para identificar los costos de AWS por departamentos?","options":{"A":"Habilitar la autenticaci\xf3n multifactor para el usuario ra\xedz de la cuenta de AWS","B":"Crear cuentas separadas para cada departamento","C":"Usar Reserved Instances siempre que sea posible","D":"Usar etiquetas para asociar cada instancia con un departamento en particular","E":"Pagar las facturas mediante \xf3rdenes de compra"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","D"],"explanation":"Crear cuentas de AWS separadas por departamento a\xedsla los costos de forma natural, ya que todos los cargos dentro de cada cuenta pertenecen a ese departamento, y aplicar etiquetas a los recursos permite la asignaci\xf3n de costos por departamento dentro de cuentas compartidas mediante informes de Cost Explorer.\\nHabilitar la autenticaci\xf3n multifactor para el usuario ra\xedz de la cuenta de AWS es una pr\xe1ctica recomendada de seguridad para proteger el acceso a la cuenta y no tiene relaci\xf3n con identificar o atribuir costos por departamento. Usar Reserved Instances reduce los costos de c\xf3mputo mediante compromisos a largo plazo y, aunque esto beneficia la factura general, no proporciona un mecanismo para identificar o separar costos por departamento. Pagar las facturas mediante \xf3rdenes de compra es un m\xe9todo de pago para liquidar facturas de AWS y no tiene relaci\xf3n con identificar qu\xe9 departamento incurri\xf3 en qu\xe9 costos."},{"id":"q668","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se usar\xeda para administrar centralmente el acceso de AWS en varias cuentas?","options":{"A":"AWS Service Catalog","B":"AWS Config","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Organizations proporciona administraci\xf3n centralizada de varias cuentas de AWS, incluidas las Service Control Policies que restringen qu\xe9 servicios y acciones est\xe1n disponibles dentro de las cuentas miembro, lo que permite la gobernanza del acceso en toda la organizaci\xf3n.\\nAWS Service Catalog permite a las organizaciones crear y administrar cat\xe1logos aprobados de servicios de TI para uso interno y aprovisionamiento de autoservicio, y es una herramienta de administraci\xf3n de productos y portafolios en lugar de un servicio para gobernar centralmente el acceso en varias cuentas. AWS Config rastrea y registra continuamente la configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento y detectar cambios, y es una herramienta de auditor\xeda de configuraci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio para administrar permisos de acceso en varias cuentas. AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones en seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallas, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento que opera dentro de una sola cuenta en lugar de proporcionar administraci\xf3n centralizada de acceso en varias cuentas."},{"id":"q669","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS puede usar un cliente para configurar una notificaci\xf3n de alerta cuando la cuenta se est\xe9 acercando a un monto en d\xf3lares en particular?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost and Usage reports","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"AWS Cost Explorer","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Budgets permite establecer umbrales de gasto personalizados y env\xeda notificaciones de alerta por correo electr\xf3nico o SNS cuando los costos reales o previstos se acercan o superan esos umbrales, lo que lo convierte en la herramienta de alertas dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para la administraci\xf3n de costos.\\nAWS Cost and Usage Reports proporciona el desglose m\xe1s granular de los cargos reales de AWS por servicio, recurso y per\xedodo de tiempo, y es una herramienta de datos y an\xe1lisis de facturaci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio de alertas basado en umbrales. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas de los patrones de gasto de AWS y admite la previsi\xf3n de costos, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis para comprender las tendencias de gasto en lugar de un servicio que env\xeda alertas cuando el gasto se acerca a un l\xedmite definido. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones en seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallas, incluidos controles de recursos subutilizados, pero no env\xeda alertas personalizadas basadas en umbrales cuando el gasto de la cuenta se acerca a un monto en d\xf3lares que usted defina."},{"id":"q670","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el plan de AWS Support M\xcdNIMO que proporciona Technical Account Managers designados?","options":{"A":"Enterprise","B":"Business","C":"Developer","D":"Basic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El plan Enterprise Support es el nivel m\xednimo que incluye un Technical Account Manager designado que proporciona orientaci\xf3n proactiva, act\xfaa como contacto principal de soporte y coordina recursos de AWS en nombre del cliente.\\nBusiness Support proporciona acceso 24/7 a Cloud Support Engineers por tel\xe9fono, correo electr\xf3nico y chat, junto con un SLA de respuesta de una hora para casos de ca\xedda de sistemas en producci\xf3n, pero no incluye un Technical Account Manager designado. Developer Support proporciona acceso por correo electr\xf3nico a Cloud Support Engineers en horario laboral para preguntas t\xe9cnicas generales, y no incluye un Technical Account Manager ni recursos de soporte dedicados 24/7. Basic Support proporciona acceso a documentaci\xf3n, whitepapers y las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor, y no incluye personal de soporte dedicado, soporte t\xe9cnico de casos ni administraci\xf3n de cuentas."},{"id":"q673","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 modelo de precios de Amazon EC2 es M\xc1S rentable para una carga de trabajo que no se puede interrumpir y que se ejecuta una vez al a\xf1o durante 24 horas?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Dedicated Instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"On-Demand Instances son m\xe1s rentables para una carga de trabajo breve y que no se puede interrumpir que se ejecuta una vez al a\xf1o, ya que no hay compromiso a largo plazo y solo se paga por las 24 horas de uso real.\\nReserved Instances requieren un compromiso m\xednimo de un a\xf1o, por lo que comprar una reserva para una carga de trabajo que solo se ejecuta durante 24 horas una vez al a\xf1o resultar\xeda en pagar por un a\xf1o completo de capacidad mientras se usa solo un d\xeda de ella. Spot Instances utilizan capacidad de AWS no utilizada con descuentos significativos, pero AWS puede interrumpirlas en cualquier momento, lo que las hace inadecuadas para una carga de trabajo que debe permanecer activa e ininterrumpida durante toda su duraci\xf3n de 24 horas. Dedicated Instances se ejecutan en hardware dedicado a un \xfanico cliente para fines de cumplimiento y aislamiento de licencias, y tienen un precio superior al de las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2, lo que las hace m\xe1s costosas que On-Demand Instances para este caso de uso simple."},{"id":"q677","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 considerando migrar sus aplicaciones a AWS. La empresa desea comparar el costo de ejecutar la carga de trabajo en las instalaciones con ejecutar la carga de trabajo equivalente en la plataforma de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 herramienta se puede utilizar para realizar esta comparaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Billing and Cost Management console","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator permite a las empresas modelar la configuraci\xf3n de cargas de trabajo equivalentes en AWS y comparar los costos proyectados con su gasto actual en las instalaciones, lo que la convierte en la herramienta dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para este tipo de comparaci\xf3n de costos.\\nAWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona el desglose m\xe1s granular de los cargos reales de AWS ya incurridos, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis hist\xf3rico de facturaci\xf3n en lugar de una herramienta para comparar costos proyectados de AWS con el gasto en las instalaciones. La consola AWS Billing and Cost Management proporciona acceso a informaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n, facturas y herramientas de administraci\xf3n de costos para cuentas de AWS activas, y no proporciona una forma estructurada de comparar los costos de infraestructura en las instalaciones con el gasto proyectado de AWS. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas de los patrones de gasto existentes de AWS y admite previsiones para cargas de trabajo activas, y requiere datos de uso activos de AWS en lugar de proporcionar una comparaci\xf3n con los costos en las instalaciones para una carga de trabajo que a\xfan no est\xe1 en AWS."},{"id":"q685","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 beneficio exclusivo se proporciona a los usuarios con Enterprise Support?","options":{"A":"Acceso a un Technical Project Manager","B":"Acceso a un Technical Account Manager","C":"Acceso a un Cloud Support Engineer","D":"Acceso a un Solutions Architect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Un Technical Account Manager est\xe1 disponible exclusivamente con el plan Enterprise Support, y proporciona orientaci\xf3n proactiva dedicada, coordina los recursos de soporte de AWS y act\xfaa como contacto principal continuo para la cuenta.\\nUn Technical Project Manager no es un recurso de AWS Support. AWS no ofrece un rol de Technical Project Manager a trav\xe9s de ning\xfan plan de soporte, lo que convierte esta en una opci\xf3n no v\xe1lida que no corresponde a un beneficio real de soporte de AWS. El acceso a Cloud Support Engineers por tel\xe9fono, correo electr\xf3nico y chat est\xe1 disponible con los planes Business y Enterprise Support, y no es un beneficio exclusivo de Enterprise Support. El acceso a un Solutions Architect para orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica est\xe1 disponible para los clientes de Enterprise Support a trav\xe9s del TAM y de las revisiones arquitect\xf3nicas, pero los Solutions Architects tambi\xe9n interact\xfaan con clientes en otros programas y no son un beneficio exclusivo de Enterprise Support."},{"id":"q688","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 plan de AWS Support proporciona un conjunto completo de comprobaciones de AWS Trusted Advisor?","options":{"A":"Soporte Business y Developer","B":"Soporte Business y Basic","C":"Soporte Enterprise y Developer","D":"Soporte Enterprise y Business"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El conjunto completo de comprobaciones de AWS Trusted Advisor en las cinco categor\xedas est\xe1 disponible con los planes Enterprise y Business Support. Los planes Developer y Basic Support proporcionan acceso solo a las siete comprobaciones principales.\\nBusiness y Developer Support es una combinaci\xf3n incorrecta porque Developer Support proporciona solo las siete comprobaciones principales, mientras que Business Support proporciona el conjunto completo, lo que los sit\xfaa en niveles opuestos de acceso a Trusted Advisor. Business y Basic Support es una combinaci\xf3n incorrecta porque Basic Support proporciona solo las siete comprobaciones principales, mientras que Business Support proporciona el conjunto completo, por lo que no son equivalentes en el acceso a Trusted Advisor. Enterprise y Developer Support es una combinaci\xf3n incorrecta porque Developer Support est\xe1 limitado a las siete comprobaciones principales, mientras que Enterprise Support proporciona el conjunto completo, por lo que no comparten el mismo nivel de acceso a Trusted Advisor."},{"id":"q693","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio se debe usar para estimar los costos de ejecutar un nuevo proyecto en AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Pricing Calculator","C":"AWS Cost Explorer API","D":"AWS Budgets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator le permite modelar configuraciones de servicios de AWS y estimar los costos mensuales de ejecutar un nuevo proyecto antes de que se despliegue cualquier recurso, lo que la convierte en la herramienta dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para la estimaci\xf3n de costos antes del despliegue.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas de los patrones existentes de gasto y uso de AWS, y requiere datos de uso activos de AWS para generar an\xe1lisis en lugar de proporcionar estimaciones de costos para nuevos proyectos a\xfan no desplegados. La API de AWS Cost Explorer proporciona acceso program\xe1tico a los mismos datos hist\xf3ricos de costos y uso que muestra la consola de Cost Explorer, y al igual que la consola, requiere datos de uso existentes en lugar de admitir la estimaci\xf3n de costos de proyectos antes del despliegue. AWS Budgets le permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costos y uso y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, y es una herramienta de gobernanza de costos y alertas para cargas de trabajo activas en lugar de un servicio de estimaci\xf3n de costos antes del despliegue."},{"id":"q695","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se puede usar para generar alertas basadas en una factura mensual estimada?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"Amazon CloudWatch","C":"AWS X-Ray","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch puede monitorear los cargos estimados de AWS y activar alarmas de facturaci\xf3n cuando los costos superan los umbrales que usted define, enviando notificaciones a trav\xe9s de Amazon SNS para alertarlo antes de que su factura alcance niveles inesperados.\\nAWS Config rastrea y registra continuamente el estado de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento y detectar cambios de configuraci\xf3n, y no tiene capacidad para generar alertas basadas en montos de facturaci\xf3n mensual estimados. AWS X-Ray es un servicio de trazado distribuido que analiza y depura el rendimiento de las aplicaciones mediante el mapeo de solicitudes a medida que fluyen por los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n, y es una herramienta de rendimiento sin conexi\xf3n con el monitoreo de facturaci\xf3n ni las alertas de costos. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y aunque registra acciones de API relacionadas con la facturaci\xf3n, no genera alertas basadas en umbrales de facturaci\xf3n mensual estimados."},{"id":"q706","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son las ventajas de las Reserved Instances?","options":{"A":"Proporcionan un descuento sobre el precio On-Demand","B":"Proporcionan acceso a tipos de instancia adicionales","C":"Proporcionan capacidad de red adicional","D":"Los clientes pueden actualizar las instancias a medida que haya nuevos tipos disponibles","E":"Los clientes pueden reservar capacidad en una Zona de disponibilidad"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","E"],"explanation":"Las Reserved Instances proporcionan descuentos significativos de hasta el 72 % sobre el precio On-Demand, y permiten reservar capacidad en una Zona de disponibilidad espec\xedfica, lo que garantiza que la capacidad est\xe9 disponible cuando la necesite.\\nLas Reserved Instances no proporcionan acceso a tipos de instancia adicionales m\xe1s all\xe1 de los disponibles en On-Demand. Los tipos de instancia disponibles son los mismos independientemente de c\xf3mo se compren. Las Reserved Instances no proporcionan capacidad de red adicional. El rendimiento de red est\xe1 determinado por el tipo de instancia y la configuraci\xf3n, no por el modelo de compra. Los clientes no pueden actualizar los tipos de Reserved Instance a medida que haya nuevos tipos disponibles, a menos que tengan RI convertibles, que permiten intercambios. Las RI est\xe1ndar est\xe1n bloqueadas al tipo de instancia espec\xedfico comprado."},{"id":"q707","question":"Un cliente ejecuta una instancia EC2 de Amazon Linux On-Demand durante 3 horas, 5 minutos y 6 segundos. \xbfPor cu\xe1nto tiempo se le facturar\xe1 al cliente?","options":{"A":"3 horas, 5 minutos","B":"3 horas, 5 minutos y 6 segundos","C":"3 horas, 6 minutos","D":"4 horas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las instancias EC2 de Amazon Linux se facturan por segundo con un m\xednimo de un minuto, por lo que al cliente se le factura la duraci\xf3n exacta de 3 horas, 5 minutos y 6 segundos.\\nFacturar 3 horas y 5 minutos omitir\xeda los 6 segundos de uso real, lo cual ser\xeda incorrecto con la facturaci\xf3n por segundo, en la que se cobra la duraci\xf3n exacta incluidos los segundos. Facturar 3 horas y 6 minutos redondear\xeda los 5 minutos y 6 segundos al siguiente minuto completo, que era el enfoque anterior de facturaci\xf3n por hora y no se aplica a las instancias Linux con la facturaci\xf3n por segundo actual. Facturar 4 horas aplicar\xeda un redondeo a la hora completa a toda la sesi\xf3n, que es el modelo de facturaci\xf3n antiguo anterior a la facturaci\xf3n por segundo y cobrar\xeda significativamente de m\xe1s en comparaci\xf3n con el tiempo de uso real."},{"id":"q714","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 herramienta se puede usar para comparar los costos de ejecutar una aplicaci\xf3n web en un entorno de hosting tradicional con ejecutarla en AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","D":"AWS Pricing Calculator"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator le permite modelar la configuraci\xf3n de una aplicaci\xf3n web en AWS y comparar los costos proyectados con los costos equivalentes de un entorno de hosting tradicional, lo que la convierte en la herramienta dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para este tipo de comparaci\xf3n.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas de los patrones existentes de gasto y uso de AWS, y requiere datos de uso activos de AWS para generar an\xe1lisis en lugar de proporcionar comparaciones con los costos de hosting tradicional para cargas de trabajo que a\xfan no est\xe1n en AWS. AWS Budgets le permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costos y uso y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, y es una herramienta de gobernanza de costos y alertas para cargas de trabajo activas en lugar de una herramienta de comparaci\xf3n para evaluar la econom\xeda de la migraci\xf3n. AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona el desglose m\xe1s granular de los cargos reales de AWS ya incurridos, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis hist\xf3rico de facturaci\xf3n en lugar de una herramienta para comparar costos proyectados de AWS con gastos de hosting tradicionales."},{"id":"q715","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el valor de usar software de terceros desde AWS Marketplace en lugar de instalar software de terceros en Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"Los usuarios pagan por el software por hora o por mes seg\xfan la licencia","B":"AWS Marketplace permite al usuario lanzar aplicaciones con 1-Click","C":"El cifrado de datos de AWS Marketplace lo gestiona un proveedor externo","D":"AWS Marketplace elimina la necesidad de actualizar a versiones m\xe1s recientes del software","E":"Los usuarios pueden desplegar software de terceros sin probarlo"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"AWS Marketplace ofrece licencias flexibles por hora o por mes para software de terceros, y permite el despliegue con un solo clic que automatiza el lanzamiento de pilas de software preconfiguradas sin instalaci\xf3n manual.\\nEl cifrado de datos de AWS Marketplace no lo gestiona un proveedor externo en nombre del cliente. El cifrado de datos sigue siendo responsabilidad del cliente seg\xfan el modelo de responsabilidad compartida, independientemente de si el software proviene de Marketplace. AWS Marketplace no elimina la necesidad de actualizar a versiones m\xe1s recientes del software. Los proveedores publican versiones actualizadas en Marketplace y los clientes deben optar por suscribirse o actualizarse a listados m\xe1s recientes. Los usuarios no pueden desplegar software de terceros de Marketplace sin probarlo. Las pruebas exhaustivas siguen siendo un paso importante en cualquier despliegue de software para garantizar la compatibilidad y la seguridad, independientemente de si el software proviene de Marketplace."},{"id":"q725","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 es AWS Trusted Advisor?","options":{"A":"Es un miembro del personal de AWS que proporciona recomendaciones y pr\xe1cticas recomendadas sobre c\xf3mo usar AWS","B":"Es una red de socios de AWS que proporcionan recomendaciones y pr\xe1cticas recomendadas sobre c\xf3mo usar AWS","C":"Es una herramienta en l\xednea con un conjunto de comprobaciones automatizadas que proporciona recomendaciones sobre optimizaci\xf3n de costos, rendimiento y seguridad","D":"Es otro nombre para los AWS Technical Account Managers que proporcionan recomendaciones sobre optimizaci\xf3n de costos, rendimiento y seguridad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor es una herramienta en l\xednea que ejecuta comprobaciones automatizadas en su entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones pr\xe1cticas sobre optimizaci\xf3n de costos, rendimiento, seguridad, tolerancia a fallos y l\xedmites de servicio.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor es una herramienta en l\xednea automatizada, no un miembro del personal de AWS. No involucra asesores humanos que proporcionen recomendaciones personalizadas, y opera mediante comprobaciones automatizadas en lugar de consultas individuales. Trusted Advisor es un servicio automatizado propiedad de AWS, no una red de socios de AWS. AWS Partner Network es un programa separado de empresas externas de consultor\xeda y tecnolog\xeda, no un componente del servicio Trusted Advisor. Trusted Advisor es una herramienta automatizada de autoservicio accesible a trav\xe9s de la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS (AWS Management Console), y es completamente distinta de los Technical Account Managers, que son profesionales de soporte humano dedicados disponibles solo en los planes Enterprise Support."},{"id":"q726","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio o caracter\xedstica de AWS permite a una empresa visualizar, comprender y gestionar los costos y el uso de AWS a lo largo del tiempo?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Organizations","D":"Facturaci\xf3n consolidada"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas que le permiten comprender, analizar y gestionar sus costos y uso de AWS a lo largo del tiempo, con capacidades de filtrado, agrupaci\xf3n y previsi\xf3n.\\nAWS Budgets le permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costos y uso, y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales; es una herramienta proactiva de gobernanza de costos y alertas, en lugar de un servicio para visualizar y analizar patrones hist\xf3ricos de gasto. AWS Organizations proporciona administraci\xf3n centralizada de m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS, incluida la facturaci\xf3n consolidada y las Service Control Policies, y es un servicio de gobernanza de cuentas en lugar de una herramienta de visualizaci\xf3n de costos. La facturaci\xf3n consolidada es una caracter\xedstica de AWS Organizations que combina el uso y las facturas de m\xfaltiples cuentas en una sola factura, y es una caracter\xedstica de agregaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n en lugar de una herramienta para visualizar y gestionar costos a lo largo del tiempo."},{"id":"q732","question":"El modelo de precios de pago por uso para los servicios de AWS:","options":{"A":"Reduce los gastos de capital","B":"Requiere pago por adelantado por los servicios de AWS","C":"Solo es relevante para Amazon EC2, Amazon S3 y Amazon RDS","D":"Reduce los gastos operativos"},"correct":"A","explanation":"El precio de pago por uso reduce los gastos de capital al eliminar la necesidad de comprar hardware por adelantado. En lugar de grandes inversiones antes del despliegue, los costos son variables y se basan en el consumo real.\\nEl modelo de pago por uso no requiere pago por adelantado por los servicios de AWS. El precio On-Demand solo cobra despu\xe9s de que se consumen los recursos, lo cual es lo opuesto a requerir pago antes del uso. El precio de pago por uso se aplica ampliamente a los servicios de AWS, incluidos c\xf3mputo, almacenamiento, bases de datos, redes y muchos otros, y no se limita a EC2, S3 y RDS. El precio de pago por uso reduce los gastos de capital en lugar de los gastos operativos. Las tarifas de uso de AWS son en s\xed mismas gastos operativos, por lo que, si bien el modelo traslada el gasto de capital a operativo, no reduce los gastos operativos en general."},{"id":"q734","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a los usuarios consolidar la facturaci\xf3n entre m\xfaltiples cuentas?","options":{"A":"Amazon QuickSight","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"Amazon Forecast"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Organizations permite la facturaci\xf3n consolidada entre m\xfaltiples cuentas, lo que proporciona un \xfanico m\xe9todo de pago y un uso combinado que puede calificar para descuentos por volumen.\\nAmazon QuickSight es un servicio de inteligencia empresarial sin servidor para crear paneles y visualizaciones a partir de datos empresariales, y no tiene capacidad para consolidar la facturaci\xf3n entre m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS. AWS Budgets le permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costos y uso, y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales; es una herramienta de gobernanza de costos en lugar de un servicio que consolida la facturaci\xf3n entre cuentas. Amazon Forecast es un servicio de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico que genera predicciones de series temporales, como pron\xf3sticos de demanda o inventario, y es un servicio de anal\xedtica de IA sin conexi\xf3n con la consolidaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n entre cuentas de AWS."},{"id":"q737","question":"Despu\xe9s de seleccionar una reserva de Amazon EC2 Dedicated Host, \xbfqu\xe9 opci\xf3n de precios proporcionar\xeda el mayor descuento?","options":{"A":"Sin pago por adelantado","B":"Pago por hora On-Demand","C":"Pago parcial por adelantado","D":"Pago total por adelantado"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El pago total por adelantado de una reserva de Dedicated Host proporciona el mayor descuento, porque pagar el costo completo al inicio del plazo reduce el riesgo de facturaci\xf3n de AWS y genera el m\xe1ximo ahorro durante el per\xedodo de reserva.\\nLa opci\xf3n sin pago por adelantado proporciona el menor descuento entre las tres opciones de pago para las reservas de Dedicated Host. Sin ning\xfan pago inicial, AWS ofrece menos incentivo en comparaci\xf3n con las opciones de pago parcial o total por adelantado. El pago por hora On-Demand es la opci\xf3n m\xe1s costosa para Dedicated Hosts, ya que no tiene compromiso ni descuento, equivalente al precio est\xe1ndar On-Demand en lugar de los niveles de precios reservados. El pago parcial por adelantado divide el costo entre un pago inicial y cargos mensuales continuos, lo que proporciona un descuento moderado que es mayor que el de sin pago por adelantado, pero menor que el m\xe1ximo disponible mediante el pago total por adelantado."},{"id":"q738","question":"Se le pregunta a un Cloud Practitioner c\xf3mo estimar el costo de usar una nueva aplicaci\xf3n en AWS. \xbfCu\xe1l es la respuesta M\xc1S apropiada?","options":{"A":"Informar al usuario que el modelo de precios de AWS permite precios On-Demand","B":"Dirigir al usuario a AWS Pricing Calculator para obtener una estimaci\xf3n","C":"Usar Amazon QuickSight para analizar el gasto actual on-premises","D":"Usar Amazon AppStream 2.0 para anal\xedtica de precios en tiempo real"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator permite a los usuarios modelar configuraciones de servicios de AWS y generar estimaciones detalladas de costos antes de que se implementen recursos, lo que la convierte en la herramienta m\xe1s apropiada a la que dirigir a un usuario cuando necesita estimar el costo de una nueva aplicaci\xf3n.\\nInformar al usuario que AWS utiliza precios On-Demand es correcto, pero no le ayuda a estimar los costos reales de una aplicaci\xf3n espec\xedfica, ya que no proporciona ninguna herramienta ni mecanismo para calcular el gasto proyectado. Amazon QuickSight es un servicio de inteligencia empresarial sin servidor para crear paneles y visualizaciones a partir de datos empresariales, y no es una herramienta para analizar el gasto on-premises ni para estimar costos de AWS. Amazon AppStream 2.0 es un servicio de streaming de aplicaciones totalmente administrado que entrega aplicaciones de escritorio a los usuarios a trav\xe9s de un navegador, y no tiene capacidad para anal\xedtica de precios en tiempo real ni estimaci\xf3n de costos."},{"id":"q747","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puede una empresa aislar los costos de las cargas de trabajo de producci\xf3n y de no producci\xf3n en AWS?","options":{"A":"Crear roles de Identity and Access Management (IAM) para cargas de trabajo de producci\xf3n y de no producci\xf3n","B":"Usar cuentas diferentes para los gastos de producci\xf3n y de no producci\xf3n","C":"Usar Amazon EC2 para cargas de trabajo de no producci\xf3n y otros servicios para cargas de trabajo de producci\xf3n","D":"Usar Amazon CloudWatch para monitorear el uso de los servicios"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Usar cuentas de AWS separadas para cargas de trabajo de producci\xf3n y de no producci\xf3n a\xedsla los costos a nivel de cuenta, lo que permite que la facturaci\xf3n refleje naturalmente el consumo de cada entorno y hace que sea sencillo atribuir y revisar los costos de forma independiente.\\nCrear roles de IAM para cargas de trabajo de producci\xf3n y de no producci\xf3n controla qui\xe9n puede acceder a los recursos de cada entorno, pero no a\xedsla ni rastrea por separado los costos incurridos por cada entorno, ya que los roles de IAM son constructos de control de acceso en lugar de l\xedmites de facturaci\xf3n. Usar Amazon EC2 para cargas de trabajo de no producci\xf3n y otros servicios para cargas de trabajo de producci\xf3n segmenta la infraestructura por tipo de servicio en lugar de por l\xedmite de entorno, lo que no proporciona un aislamiento limpio de costos y hace que el seguimiento de costos sea m\xe1s complejo en lugar de m\xe1s sencillo. Usar Amazon CloudWatch para monitorear el uso de servicios proporciona visibilidad operativa del consumo de recursos y puede activar alertas relacionadas con costos, pero monitorear el uso no es lo mismo que aislar costos entre entornos; CloudWatch no crea l\xedmites de facturaci\xf3n entre cargas de trabajo de producci\xf3n y de no producci\xf3n."},{"id":"q758","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo cobra AWS por el uso de AWS Lambda una vez que se ha superado el nivel gratuito?","options":{"A":"Por el tiempo que tarda en ejecutarse la funci\xf3n Lambda","B":"Por el n\xfamero de versiones de una funci\xf3n Lambda espec\xedfica","C":"Por el n\xfamero de solicitudes realizadas para una funci\xf3n Lambda determinada","D":"Por el lenguaje de programaci\xf3n que se utiliza para la funci\xf3n Lambda","E":"Por el n\xfamero total de funciones Lambda en una cuenta de AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"Los cargos de AWS Lambda se basan en el tiempo de ejecuci\xf3n de la funci\xf3n medido en GB-segundos y en el n\xfamero de solicitudes realizadas para activar la funci\xf3n; ambas dimensiones generan cargos una vez que se supera el nivel gratuito.\\nEl n\xfamero de versiones de una funci\xf3n Lambda espec\xedfica no afecta los precios. Las versiones de Lambda se utilizan para la gesti\xf3n de implementaciones y la reversi\xf3n, y mantener m\xfaltiples versiones no aumenta los cargos. El lenguaje de programaci\xf3n utilizado para una funci\xf3n Lambda no afecta su costo. Lambda cobra la misma tarifa por GB-segundo de ejecuci\xf3n independientemente de si la funci\xf3n est\xe1 escrita en Python, Node.js, Java o cualquier otro entorno de ejecuci\xf3n compatible. El n\xfamero total de funciones Lambda en una cuenta de AWS no afecta los precios. Lambda cobra seg\xfan las invocaciones reales y la duraci\xf3n de ejecuci\xf3n, en lugar del n\xfamero de funciones definidas en la cuenta."},{"id":"q762","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 permite hacer AWS Marketplace a los usuarios?","options":{"A":"Vender instancias Amazon EC2 Spot no utilizadas","B":"Vender soluciones a otros usuarios de AWS","C":"Comprar software de terceros que se ejecuta en AWS","D":"Comprar documentos de seguridad y cumplimiento de AWS","E":"Solicitar AWS Snowball"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"AWS Marketplace permite a los proveedores vender sus soluciones de software a otros usuarios de AWS, y permite a los compradores adquirir e implementar software de terceros que se ejecuta en la infraestructura de AWS. Ambas son funciones principales de la plataforma.\\nVender instancias Amazon EC2 Spot no utilizadas no es una funci\xf3n de AWS Marketplace. La capacidad de Spot Instance la pone a disposici\xf3n AWS misma a trav\xe9s de la consola de EC2 en funci\xf3n de la capacidad de c\xf3mputo no utilizada, y los clientes individuales no pueden listar ni vender sus propias Spot Instances. Comprar documentos de seguridad y cumplimiento de AWS describe AWS Artifact, que proporciona acceso bajo demanda a informes de cumplimiento y acuerdos de seguridad de AWS, y no es una funci\xf3n de AWS Marketplace. Solicitar AWS Snowball se realiza directamente a trav\xe9s de la consola de AWS como parte del proceso de solicitud de servicio de Snow Family, y no es una acci\xf3n de adquisici\xf3n disponible a trav\xe9s de AWS Marketplace."},{"id":"q768","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona una forma r\xe1pida y automatizada de crear y administrar cuentas de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS QuickSight","B":"Amazon Lightsail","C":"AWS Organizations","D":"Amazon Connect"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Organizations proporciona creaci\xf3n automatizada de cuentas y administraci\xf3n centralizada de m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS, incluidos facturaci\xf3n consolidada, Service Control Policies y unidades organizativas.\\nAWS QuickSight es un servicio de inteligencia empresarial sin servidor para crear paneles y visualizaciones a partir de datos empresariales, y no tiene capacidad para crear o administrar cuentas de AWS. Amazon Lightsail es un servicio de c\xf3mputo simplificado para implementar servidores virtuales y aplicaciones web con precios sencillos, y es un servicio de c\xf3mputo en lugar de una herramienta de administraci\xf3n de cuentas. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto en la nube para administrar comunicaciones con clientes mediante voz y chat, y es un servicio de interacci\xf3n con el cliente sin conexi\xf3n con la creaci\xf3n o administraci\xf3n de cuentas de AWS."},{"id":"q775","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 oferta de AWS permite a los clientes encontrar, comprar y comenzar a usar inmediatamente soluciones de software en su entorno de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Config","B":"AWS Service Catalog","C":"AWS Partner Network (APN)","D":"AWS Marketplace"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Marketplace es un cat\xe1logo digital de miles de productos de software de proveedores de software independientes que los clientes pueden encontrar, suscribirse e implementar de inmediato en su entorno de AWS, a menudo con modelos de licencia flexibles que incluyen pago por uso y BYOL.\\nAWS Config es un servicio que monitorea y registra continuamente las configuraciones de recursos y las eval\xfaa frente a reglas de cumplimiento, proporcionando capacidades de gobernanza y gesti\xf3n de cambios en lugar de un cat\xe1logo de adquisici\xf3n de software. AWS Service Catalog permite a las organizaciones crear y administrar portafolios aprobados de servicios de TI para uso interno, lo que permite gobernanza e implementaci\xf3n de autoservicio de recursos preaprobados, pero es una herramienta de gobernanza interna en lugar de un marketplace externo donde los clientes pueden encontrar y comprar soluciones de software de terceros. AWS Partner Network es el programa global de socios para empresas que construyen con AWS o venden AWS, y proporciona beneficios de capacitaci\xf3n, certificaci\xf3n y venta conjunta, y es un programa de ecosistema de socios en lugar de un lugar para que los clientes compren e implementen soluciones de software."},{"id":"q787","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 migrando de centros de datos locales a la nube de AWS y busca ayuda pr\xe1ctica con el proyecto. \xbfC\xf3mo puede la empresa obtener este soporte?","options":{"A":"Solicitar una cotizaci\xf3n al equipo de AWS Marketplace para realizar una migraci\xf3n a la cuenta de AWS de la empresa","B":"Contactar a AWS Support y abrir un caso para obtener asistencia","C":"Usar AWS Professional Services para proporcionar orientaci\xf3n y configurar una AWS Landing Zone en la cuenta de AWS de la empresa","D":"Seleccionar un socio de AWS Partner Network (APN) para ayudar con la migraci\xf3n","E":"Usar Amazon Connect para crear una nueva solicitud de propuesta (RFP) para asistencia experta en la migraci\xf3n a la nube de AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"AWS Professional Services proporciona orientaci\xf3n pr\xe1ctica para la migraci\xf3n y configuraci\xf3n de Landing Zone, y los socios de AWS Partner Network ofrecen consultor\xeda especializada en migraci\xf3n y soporte de implementaci\xf3n, ambos proporcionando la asistencia pr\xe1ctica directa que necesita la empresa.\\nEl equipo de AWS Marketplace proporciona una plataforma para descubrir y comprar software y servicios de terceros, y no realiza migraciones directamente a las cuentas de los clientes. Los casos de AWS Support se utilizan para resolver problemas t\xe9cnicos y problemas relacionados con el servicio, no para involucrar equipos de entrega de proyectos pr\xe1cticos para trabajos de migraci\xf3n. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto basado en la nube para administrar comunicaciones con clientes, y no tiene funci\xf3n relacionada con el env\xedo de propuestas o la contrataci\xf3n de experiencia en migraci\xf3n."},{"id":"q788","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo ayuda el equipo Concierge de AWS Enterprise Support a los usuarios?","options":{"A":"Apoyar el desarrollo de aplicaciones","B":"Proporcionar orientaci\xf3n de arquitectura","C":"Responder consultas de facturaci\xf3n y de cuenta","D":"Responder preguntas relacionadas con casos de soporte t\xe9cnico"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El equipo Concierge de AWS Enterprise Support se especializa en responder consultas de facturaci\xf3n y de cuenta, ayudando a los clientes Enterprise a navegar preguntas complejas de facturaci\xf3n y administrar problemas a nivel de cuenta con experiencia dedicada.\\nApoyar el desarrollo de aplicaciones es el dominio de AWS Developer Support, Solutions Architects y Professional Services en lugar del equipo Concierge, que se enfoca espec\xedficamente en asuntos de facturaci\xf3n y cuenta. Proporcionar orientaci\xf3n de arquitectura es la funci\xf3n de AWS Solutions Architects y el Technical Account Manager incluido en el plan Enterprise Support, no del equipo Concierge que maneja consultas de facturaci\xf3n y cuenta. Responder preguntas relacionadas con casos de soporte t\xe9cnico es el rol de los Cloud Support Engineers, que manejan problemas t\xe9cnicos por tel\xe9fono, correo electr\xf3nico y chat, en lugar del equipo Concierge que se dedica a temas de facturaci\xf3n y cuenta."},{"id":"q791","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 herramienta de AWS se utiliza para comparar el costo de ejecutar una aplicaci\xf3n en local con ejecutar la aplicaci\xf3n en la nube de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","C":"AWS Pricing Calculator","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"La AWS Pricing Calculator permite modelar la configuraci\xf3n de una aplicaci\xf3n en AWS y comparar los costos proyectados con los costos equivalentes de ejecuci\xf3n en local, lo que la convierte en la herramienta dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para esta comparaci\xf3n.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS existente y proporciona recomendaciones en seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento para optimizar una implementaci\xf3n activa en lugar de una herramienta de comparaci\xf3n de costos. El AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona el desglose m\xe1s granular de los cargos reales de AWS ya incurridos, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de facturaci\xf3n hist\xf3rica en lugar de una herramienta para comparar costos proyectados de AWS con gastos en local. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas de los patrones de gasto y uso existentes de AWS con capacidad de previsi\xf3n, y requiere datos de uso activo de AWS para generar an\xe1lisis en lugar de proporcionar comparaciones con costos en local."},{"id":"q792","question":"Una empresa tiene m\xfaltiples cuentas de AWS dentro de AWS Organizations y desea aplicar el beneficio de Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances a una sola cuenta \xfanicamente. \xbfQu\xe9 acci\xf3n se debe tomar?","options":{"A":"Comprar las Reserved Instances desde la cuenta de pagador maestro y desactivar el uso compartido de Reserved Instances","B":"Habilitar alertas de facturaci\xf3n en la consola de AWS Billing and Cost Management","C":"Comprar las Reserved Instances en cuentas vinculadas individuales y desactivar el uso compartido de Reserved Instances desde el nivel de pagador","D":"Habilitar el uso compartido de Reserved Instances en la consola de AWS Billing and Cost Management"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Para restringir los beneficios de Reserved Instance a una sola cuenta, compre las RI desde la cuenta de pagador maestro y desactive el uso compartido de Reserved Instance en la configuraci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n, lo que evita que los descuentos se apliquen a otras cuentas miembro.\\nHabilitar alertas de facturaci\xf3n en la consola de AWS Billing and Cost Management crea notificaciones cuando el gasto supera los umbrales, y no tiene efecto sobre c\xf3mo se comparten o restringen los beneficios de Reserved Instance entre cuentas de una organizaci\xf3n. Comprar las Reserved Instances en cuentas vinculadas individuales y desactivar el uso compartido de RI desde el nivel de pagador aplicar\xeda restricciones de RI en toda la organizaci\xf3n en lugar de limitar los beneficios a una sola cuenta espec\xedfica. Habilitar el uso compartido de Reserved Instance en la consola de AWS Billing and Cost Management ampliar\xeda el uso compartido de beneficios de RI entre todas las cuentas miembro, lo que es lo opuesto a restringir el beneficio a una sola cuenta."},{"id":"q797","question":"AWS Budgets se puede utilizar para:","options":{"A":"Impedir que un usuario determinado cree un recurso","B":"Enviar una alerta cuando la utilizaci\xf3n de Reserved Instances caiga por debajo de cierto porcentaje","C":"Establecer l\xedmites de recursos en cuentas de AWS para evitar el sobregasto","D":"Dividir una factura de AWS entre m\xfaltiples formas de pago"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Budgets admite presupuestos de utilizaci\xf3n de Reserved Instance que env\xedan alertas cuando la utilizaci\xf3n de Reserved Instances cae por debajo de un umbral porcentual definido, lo que ayuda a los clientes a asegurar que obtienen valor de sus compromisos de RI.\\nImpedir que un usuario determinado cree un recurso es una funci\xf3n de control de acceso lograda mediante pol\xedticas de IAM o Service Control Policies en AWS Organizations, no mediante AWS Budgets. AWS Budgets es una herramienta de alerta y monitoreo y no puede imponer l\xedmites estrictos ni impedir la creaci\xf3n de recursos, por lo que establecer l\xedmites de recursos para evitar el sobregasto es una descripci\xf3n incorrecta de sus capacidades. Dividir una factura de AWS entre m\xfaltiples formas de pago es una configuraci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n y m\xe9todo de pago gestionada a trav\xe9s de la AWS Billing Console, y no es una caracter\xedstica de AWS Budgets."},{"id":"q799","question":"Las verificaciones de AWS Trusted Advisor incluyen recomendaciones sobre cu\xe1l de los siguientes?","options":{"A":"Informaci\xf3n sobre permisos de bucket de Amazon S3","B":"Interrupciones de servicios de AWS","C":"Autenticaci\xf3n multifactor habilitada en el usuario ra\xedz de la cuenta de AWS","D":"Parches de software disponibles","E":"N\xfamero de usuarios en la cuenta"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor verifica los permisos de bucket de S3 para identificar aquellos con acceso p\xfablico demasiado permisivo que podr\xeda exponer datos, y verifica que MFA est\xe9 habilitada en la cuenta de usuario ra\xedz, ambas dentro de su categor\xeda de verificaciones de Seguridad.\\nLas interrupciones de servicios de AWS son monitoreadas por AWS Health Dashboard, que proporciona informaci\xf3n de estado en tiempo real e hist\xf3rica sobre los servicios de AWS en todas las Regiones, y no son una categor\xeda de verificaciones realizadas por Trusted Advisor. Los parches de software disponibles para los sistemas operativos de instancias de EC2 son identificados por Amazon Inspector y AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager, y no son una categor\xeda de recomendaciones proporcionadas por Trusted Advisor. El n\xfamero de usuarios en la cuenta es visible a trav\xe9s de la consola de IAM y los informes de credenciales de IAM, y no es una categor\xeda de recomendaci\xf3n en Trusted Advisor, que se enfoca en seguridad, costo, rendimiento, tolerancia a fallos y l\xedmites de servicio."},{"id":"q817","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedsticas o servicios se pueden usar para monitorear los costos y gastos de una cuenta de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","B":"P\xe1ginas de productos de AWS","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Alertas de facturaci\xf3n y alarmas de Amazon CloudWatch","E":"AWS Price List API"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona la vista m\xe1s completa de los costos reales de AWS desglosados por servicio, recurso y per\xedodo de tiempo, y las alertas de facturaci\xf3n combinadas con las alarmas de Amazon CloudWatch le notifican de forma proactiva cuando el gasto real o previsto supera los umbrales definidos. Ambas son herramientas dise\xf1adas espec\xedficamente para monitorear los costos y gastos de AWS.\\nLas p\xe1ginas de productos de AWS describen las caracter\xedsticas, los casos de uso y los precios de servicios individuales de AWS, y son un recurso de referencia en lugar de una herramienta para monitorear activamente los costos y gastos de su cuenta. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones en seguridad, costos, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, incluidas comprobaciones de recursos subutilizados, pero es una herramienta de asesoramiento en lugar de un servicio directo de monitoreo de costos y gastos. La AWS Price List API proporciona acceso program\xe1tico a los datos de precios de los servicios de AWS para crear herramientas de precios personalizadas, y es una fuente de datos de precios en lugar de una herramienta para monitorear los costos y gastos reales de su cuenta."},{"id":"q820","question":"Una empresa quiere probar una soluci\xf3n de comercio electr\xf3nico de terceros antes de decidir usarla a largo plazo. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio o herramienta de AWS apoyar\xe1 este esfuerzo?","options":{"A":"AWS Marketplace","B":"AWS Partner Network (APN)","C":"AWS Managed Services","D":"AWS Service Catalog"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Marketplace ofrece pruebas gratuitas y precios flexibles de pago por uso para soluciones de software de terceros, lo que permite a las empresas evaluar una plataforma de comercio electr\xf3nico antes de comprometerse a usarla a largo plazo.\\nAWS Partner Network es un programa para socios de consultor\xeda y tecnolog\xeda que crean y venden soluciones basadas en AWS, no una plataforma para probar software de terceros. AWS Managed Services proporciona gesti\xf3n operativa de la infraestructura de AWS en nombre de los clientes y no ofrece pruebas de software de terceros. AWS Service Catalog permite a las organizaciones crear y gestionar cat\xe1logos aprobados de servicios de TI para uso interno, no para descubrir o probar software comercial de terceros."},{"id":"q821","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS se debe usar para crear una alarma de facturaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"Amazon CloudWatch","D":"Amazon QuickSight"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Amazon CloudWatch puede crear alarmas de facturaci\xf3n que monitorean sus cargos estimados de AWS y env\xedan notificaciones a trav\xe9s de Amazon SNS cuando los costos superan los umbrales que usted define, por lo que es el servicio que se usa para configurar alertas de facturaci\xf3n.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones en seguridad, costos, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y aunque incluye comprobaciones de recursos subutilizados, no crea alarmas de facturaci\xf3n que alerten en tiempo real sobre umbrales de gasto. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta en su entorno de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no crea alarmas de facturaci\xf3n ni monitorea cargos estimados. Amazon QuickSight es un servicio de inteligencia empresarial sin servidor para crear paneles y visualizaciones a partir de datos empresariales, y no tiene capacidad para crear o gestionar alarmas de facturaci\xf3n basadas en umbrales de gasto de AWS."},{"id":"q832","question":"Cada departamento dentro de una empresa tiene su propia cuenta de AWS independiente y su propio m\xe9todo de pago. La nueva direcci\xf3n de la empresa quiere centralizar la gobernanza departamental y consolidar los pagos. \xbfC\xf3mo se puede lograr esto usando servicios o caracter\xedsticas de AWS?","options":{"A":"Reenviar facturas mensuales para cada cuenta. Luego, crear roles de IAM para permitir el acceso entre cuentas","B":"Crear una nueva cuenta de AWS. Luego, configurar AWS Organizations e invitar a todas las cuentas existentes a unirse","C":"Configurar AWS Organizations en cada una de las cuentas existentes. Luego, vincular todas las cuentas entre s\xed","D":"Usar Cost Explorer para combinar los costos de todas las cuentas. Luego, replicar las pol\xedticas de IAM entre cuentas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Crear una nueva cuenta de AWS y configurar AWS Organizations le permite invitar a cuentas existentes a unirse, lo que habilita la gobernanza centralizada mediante pol\xedticas de control de servicios y la facturaci\xf3n consolidada bajo una \xfanica cuenta de pagador.\\nReenviar facturas mensuales y crear roles de IAM para el acceso entre cuentas aborda los informes y los permisos por separado, pero no centraliza la gobernanza ni consolida los pagos en una \xfanica estructura de facturaci\xf3n bajo una cuenta de pagador. AWS Organizations solo puede tener una cuenta de administraci\xf3n dentro de una organizaci\xf3n, y no es posible configurarlo en varias cuentas existentes simult\xe1neamente. El enfoque correcto es configurarlo en una \xfanica cuenta de administraci\xf3n e invitar luego a las dem\xe1s a unirse. Usar Cost Explorer para combinar vistas de costos y replicar pol\xedticas de IAM entre cuentas proporciona cierta visibilidad y coherencia, pero no centraliza la gobernanza mediante SCP ni consolida los pagos en una \xfanica factura como lo hace AWS Organizations."},{"id":"q836","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 herramienta se usa para pronosticar el gasto de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"Cost Explorer","D":"Amazon Inspector"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Cost Explorer proporciona capacidades de pron\xf3stico de costos que usan datos hist\xf3ricos de gasto para proyectar los gastos futuros de AWS, lo que ayuda a las organizaciones a anticipar y planificar los pr\xf3ximos costos.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor analiza su entorno y proporciona recomendaciones en seguridad, costos, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento para optimizar implementaciones existentes en lugar de un servicio de pron\xf3stico del gasto futuro. AWS Organizations proporciona gesti\xf3n centralizada de cuentas con facturaci\xf3n consolidada y controles de gobernanza, y es un servicio de estructura de cuentas y gobernanza en lugar de una herramienta para pronosticar el gasto futuro de AWS. Amazon Inspector es un servicio automatizado de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencional, y es una herramienta de seguridad sin capacidad de pron\xf3stico de costos."},{"id":"q847","question":"Un Cloud Practitioner identifica un problema de facturaci\xf3n despu\xe9s de examinar el AWS Cost and Usage Report en la consola de administraci\xf3n de AWS. \xbfQu\xe9 acci\xf3n se puede tomar para resolverlo?","options":{"A":"Abrir un caso detallado relacionado con la facturaci\xf3n y enviarlo a AWS Support para obtener ayuda","B":"Cargar datos que describan el problema en un nuevo objeto en un bucket privado de Amazon S3","C":"Crear una aplicaci\xf3n de precios e implementarla en una instancia Amazon EC2 del tama\xf1o adecuado para obtener m\xe1s informaci\xf3n","D":"Continuar con la creaci\xf3n de un nuevo panel en Amazon QuickSight"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Abrir un caso de soporte de facturaci\xf3n detallado con AWS Support es la acci\xf3n adecuada para resolver problemas de facturaci\xf3n, ya que AWS Support puede investigar los cargos espec\xedficos, aclarar discrepancias de facturaci\xf3n y aplicar correcciones cuando corresponda.\\nCargar datos que describan el problema en un bucket privado de Amazon S3 almacena la informaci\xf3n, pero no la env\xeda a nadie que pueda investigar o resolver el problema de facturaci\xf3n, ya que S3 es un servicio de almacenamiento en lugar de un canal de soporte. Crear una aplicaci\xf3n de precios e implementarla en una instancia EC2 para obtener m\xe1s informaci\xf3n introduce complejidad y costo innecesarios en lugar de abordar el problema de facturaci\xf3n a trav\xe9s del canal adecuado de AWS Support. Continuar con la creaci\xf3n de un nuevo panel en Amazon QuickSight visualizar\xeda los datos de facturaci\xf3n, pero no resuelve el problema de facturaci\xf3n subyacente, ya que QuickSight es una herramienta de anal\xedtica en lugar de un mecanismo de soporte o resoluci\xf3n de disputas."},{"id":"q848","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 hace AWS Pricing Calculator?","options":{"A":"Compara los costos en las instalaciones con entornos de coubicaci\xf3n","B":"Estima la facturaci\xf3n mensual seg\xfan el uso proyectado de AWS","C":"Estima el consumo de energ\xeda en los centros de datos existentes","D":"Estima la utilizaci\xf3n de CPU de las instancias en ejecuci\xf3n"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator permite a los usuarios modelar configuraciones de servicios de AWS y estimar los costos mensuales seg\xfan el uso proyectado, lo que ayuda a los clientes a pronosticar el gasto de AWS antes de implementar recursos.\\nComparar los costos en las instalaciones con entornos de coubicaci\xf3n es una funci\xf3n fuera del alcance de AWS Pricing Calculator, que se centra en estimar el costo de los servicios de AWS en lugar de comparar opciones de alojamiento de terceros. Estimar el consumo de energ\xeda en los centros de datos existentes no es una funci\xf3n de AWS Pricing Calculator. Los costos de electricidad e infraestructura f\xedsica son una preocupaci\xf3n local que AWS abstrae por completo para los clientes. Estimar la utilizaci\xf3n de CPU de las instancias en ejecuci\xf3n es una funci\xf3n de Amazon CloudWatch, que recopila y monitorea m\xe9tricas de rendimiento de recursos de AWS activos, en lugar de Pricing Calculator, que estima costos antes de la implementaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q852","question":"Un usuario que desea obtener ayuda con la facturaci\xf3n y reactivar una cuenta suspendida debe enviar una solicitud de cuenta y facturaci\xf3n a:","options":{"A":"El foro de AWS Support","B":"AWS Abuse","C":"Un AWS Solutions Architect","D":"AWS Support"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Support gestiona las consultas de facturaci\xf3n y las solicitudes de reactivaci\xf3n de cuentas. Los usuarios deben enviar un caso de soporte de cuenta y facturaci\xf3n a trav\xe9s del Centro de soporte de AWS, al que se puede acceder incluso sin un plan de soporte activo.\\nEl foro de AWS Support es un recurso impulsado por la comunidad donde los clientes y el personal de AWS intercambian preguntas y respuestas, y no es el canal adecuado para resolver la suspensi\xf3n de una cuenta ni para enviar solicitudes oficiales de facturaci\xf3n. AWS Abuse gestiona los informes de recursos de AWS que se utilizan para actividades maliciosas, como spam, pirater\xeda inform\xe1tica o uso no autorizado, y no es el contacto correcto para consultas de facturaci\xf3n o reactivaci\xf3n de cuentas. Un AWS Solutions Architect proporciona orientaci\xf3n arquitect\xf3nica y soporte de dise\xf1o para construir en AWS, y no gestiona consultas de facturaci\xf3n ni solicitudes de reactivaci\xf3n de cuentas."},{"id":"q856","question":"Las herramientas de AWS Cost Management brindan a los usuarios la capacidad de realizar cu\xe1l de las siguientes acciones?","options":{"A":"Terminar autom\xe1ticamente todos los recursos de AWS si se superan los umbrales de presupuesto","B":"Desglosar los costos de AWS por d\xeda, servicio y cuenta de AWS vinculada","C":"Crear presupuestos y recibir notificaciones si el uso actual o previsto supera los presupuestos","D":"Cambiar autom\xe1ticamente a Reserved Instances o Spot Instances, la que sea m\xe1s rentable","E":"Mover datos almacenados en Amazon S3 a una clase de almacenamiento m\xe1s rentable"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["B","C"],"explanation":"Las herramientas de AWS Cost Management desglosan los costos por d\xeda, servicio y cuenta vinculada para un an\xe1lisis detallado, y permiten crear presupuestos y recibir notificaciones cuando el uso real o previsto supera los umbrales definidos.\\nTerminar autom\xe1ticamente todos los recursos de AWS si se superan los umbrales de presupuesto no es una funci\xf3n est\xe1ndar de las herramientas de AWS Cost Management. AWS Budgets puede activar acciones automatizadas, como aplicar pol\xedticas de IAM para restringir el aprovisionamiento adicional, pero no termina autom\xe1ticamente los recursos existentes. Cambiar autom\xe1ticamente a Reserved Instances o Spot Instances seg\xfan cu\xe1l sea m\xe1s rentable no es una funci\xf3n de las herramientas de AWS Cost Management. Las decisiones de compra de instancias requieren una configuraci\xf3n deliberada en lugar de un cambio autom\xe1tico basado en comparaciones de precios. Mover datos almacenados en S3 a una clase de almacenamiento m\xe1s rentable se gestiona mediante pol\xedticas de S3 Lifecycle configuradas en buckets individuales, y es una caracter\xedstica de gesti\xf3n de almacenamiento en lugar de una funci\xf3n del conjunto de herramientas de AWS Cost Management."},{"id":"q863","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 modelo de precios de Amazon EC2 se debe usar para cumplir con los requisitos de licencias de software por n\xfacleo?","options":{"A":"Dedicated Hosts","B":"On-Demand Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"A","explanation":"EC2 Dedicated Hosts brindan visibilidad de los sockets y n\xfacleos del servidor f\xedsico, lo que permite usar licencias de software existentes por n\xfacleo o por socket bajo el modelo Bring Your Own License.\\nOn-Demand Instances se ejecutan en infraestructura compartida donde los detalles del servidor f\xedsico subyacente se abstraen, sin proporcionar visibilidad de la topolog\xeda de sockets o n\xfacleos necesaria para el cumplimiento de licencias de software por n\xfacleo. Spot Instances usan capacidad de AWS disponible en infraestructura compartida y pueden interrumpirse en cualquier momento, sin proporcionar ni la visibilidad del servidor f\xedsico requerida para licencias por n\xfacleo ni la disponibilidad constante necesaria para cargas de trabajo de bases de datos de producci\xf3n. Reserved Instances son un modelo de compromiso de precios que reduce los costos durante un plazo de uno o tres a\xf1os, pero se ejecutan en infraestructura compartida est\xe1ndar sin visibilidad a nivel de host y no cumplen con los requisitos de licencias de software por n\xfacleo o por socket."},{"id":"q868","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedsticas y beneficios proporciona el servicio AWS Organizations?","options":{"A":"Establecer comunicaciones en tiempo real entre miembros de un equipo interno","B":"Facilitar el uso de bases de datos NoSQL","C":"Proporcionar comprobaciones de seguridad automatizadas","D":"Implementar facturaci\xf3n consolidada","E":"Aplicar la gobernanza de las cuentas de AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["D","E"],"explanation":"AWS Organizations implementa la facturaci\xf3n consolidada para combinar varias cuentas bajo un \xfanico m\xe9todo de pago, y aplica la gobernanza mediante Service Control Policies que restringen qu\xe9 servicios y acciones est\xe1n disponibles dentro de las cuentas miembro.\\nEstablecer comunicaciones en tiempo real entre miembros de un equipo interno es funci\xf3n de herramientas de colaboraci\xf3n como Amazon Chime o AWS Wickr, no de AWS Organizations, que gestiona la estructura de cuentas y la facturaci\xf3n. Facilitar el uso de bases de datos NoSQL es funci\xf3n de servicios como Amazon DynamoDB, que proporciona capacidades de base de datos NoSQL gestionada, en lugar de AWS Organizations, que es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de cuentas. Proporcionar comprobaciones de seguridad automatizadas es funci\xf3n de servicios como Amazon Inspector, AWS Security Hub y Amazon GuardDuty, en lugar de AWS Organizations, que se centra en la gobernanza de cuentas y la consolidaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q870","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 herramienta es la m\xe1s adecuada para combinar la facturaci\xf3n de cuentas de AWS que antes eran independientes entre s\xed?","options":{"A":"Informe detallado de facturaci\xf3n","B":"Facturaci\xf3n consolidada","C":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","D":"Informe de asignaci\xf3n de costos"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La facturaci\xf3n consolidada a trav\xe9s de AWS Organizations combina cuentas que antes eran independientes bajo un \xfanico pagador, lo que simplifica el pago con una sola factura y permite descuentos por volumen mediante el uso combinado.\\nLos informes detallados de facturaci\xf3n proporcionan un desglose hist\xf3rico de los cargos dentro de una cuenta individual, y son una herramienta de informes en lugar de un mecanismo para combinar la facturaci\xf3n de cuentas anteriormente separadas. El AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona el desglose m\xe1s granular de los cargos reales por servicio, recurso y per\xedodo de tiempo, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de facturaci\xf3n en lugar de una caracter\xedstica para combinar la facturaci\xf3n entre cuentas independientes. Un informe de asignaci\xf3n de costos desglosa los cargos por recursos etiquetados para respaldar el chargeback y la atribuci\xf3n, y es una herramienta de categorizaci\xf3n de costos en lugar de un mecanismo para consolidar la facturaci\xf3n de cuentas independientes."},{"id":"q871","question":"La AWS Pricing Calculator se utiliza para:","options":{"A":"Recibir informes que desglosan los costos de c\xf3mputo de AWS Cloud por duraci\xf3n, recurso o etiquetas","B":"Estimar ahorros al comparar AWS Cloud con un entorno on-premises","C":"Monitorear los costos reales de AWS en comparaci\xf3n con los costos estimados y enviar alertas","D":"Habilitar alertas de facturaci\xf3n cuando el gasto supera un umbral definido"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La AWS Pricing Calculator se utiliza para modelar configuraciones de servicios de AWS y estimar costos, lo que permite a los clientes comparar el gasto proyectado en AWS con sus costos actuales de infraestructura on-premises para comprender los ahorros potenciales.\\nRecibir informes que desglosan los costos de c\xf3mputo de AWS Cloud por duraci\xf3n, recurso o etiquetas es funci\xf3n del AWS Cost and Usage Report, que proporciona datos de facturaci\xf3n hist\xf3ricos granulares en lugar de estimaciones de costos previas al despliegue. Monitorear los costos reales de AWS en comparaci\xf3n con los costos estimados describe una combinaci\xf3n de AWS Budgets y AWS Cost Explorer, que realizan un seguimiento del gasto real frente a los objetivos en lugar de proporcionar estimaciones previas al despliegue. Habilitar alertas de facturaci\xf3n cuando el gasto supera un umbral definido es funci\xf3n de AWS Budgets combinado con alarmas de facturaci\xf3n de Amazon CloudWatch, no de la AWS Pricing Calculator, que se utiliza antes de que se desplieguen los recursos."},{"id":"q874","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es el nivel M\xcdNIMO del plan de AWS Support que proporcionar\xe1 a los usuarios acceso a la AWS Support API?","options":{"A":"Developer","B":"Enterprise","C":"Business","D":"Basic"},"correct":"C","explanation":"El plan Business Support es el nivel m\xednimo que proporciona acceso a la AWS Support API, lo que permite la gesti\xf3n program\xe1tica de casos de soporte y la integraci\xf3n con los sistemas de gesti\xf3n de servicios existentes.\\nDeveloper Support proporciona acceso por correo electr\xf3nico en horario laboral a los Cloud Support Engineers, pero no incluye la AWS Support API, lo que limita la gesti\xf3n de casos a la consola y el correo electr\xf3nico en lugar del acceso program\xe1tico. Enterprise Support tambi\xe9n proporciona acceso a la AWS Support API junto con un TAM dedicado y tiempos de respuesta m\xe1s r\xe1pidos, pero es un plan de mayor costo que Business Support y, por lo tanto, no es el m\xednimo requerido. Basic Support proporciona acceso a documentaci\xf3n, documentos t\xe9cnicos y las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor, y no incluye la AWS Support API ni la capacidad de abrir casos de soporte t\xe9cnico."},{"id":"q877","question":"Una instancia de Amazon EC2 se ejecuta solo cuando es necesario, pero debe permanecer activa durante la duraci\xf3n del proceso. \xbfCu\xe1l es la opci\xf3n de compra m\xe1s adecuada?","options":{"A":"Dedicated Instances","B":"Spot Instances","C":"On-Demand Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"On-Demand Instances son ideales para cargas de trabajo que se ejecutan solo cuando es necesario pero no deben interrumpirse, ya que no tienen compromiso a largo plazo y proporcionan disponibilidad garantizada durante la duraci\xf3n del proceso.\\nDedicated Instances se ejecutan en hardware dedicado a un \xfanico cliente por motivos de cumplimiento y aislamiento de licencias, y tienen un precio superior al de las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2, lo que las hace m\xe1s costosas que On-Demand para un caso de uso simple sin requisitos de aislamiento. Spot Instances pueden ser interrumpidas por AWS cuando se recupera capacidad disponible, lo que infringe directamente el requisito de que la instancia debe permanecer activa durante toda la duraci\xf3n del proceso. Reserved Instances requieren un compromiso m\xednimo de un a\xf1o, lo que no es apropiado para una carga de trabajo que solo se ejecuta ocasionalmente cuando es necesario en lugar de continuamente a largo plazo."},{"id":"q878","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 panel de AWS muestra informaci\xf3n relevante y oportuna para ayudar a los usuarios a gestionar eventos en curso y proporciona notificaciones proactivas para ayudar a planificar actividades programadas?","options":{"A":"Panel de Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS Health Dashboard","C":"Panel de AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Panel de AWS Systems Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard proporciona informaci\xf3n personalizada y oportuna sobre eventos de AWS que pueden afectar sus recursos espec\xedficos, notificaciones proactivas para actividades de mantenimiento programadas y orientaci\xf3n para ayudar a gestionar eventos en curso.\\nEl panel de Amazon CloudWatch muestra m\xe9tricas y alarmas para sus propios recursos y aplicaciones de AWS, y es una herramienta de monitoreo operativo para sus cargas de trabajo en lugar de una fuente de notificaciones personalizadas sobre eventos de servicios de AWS y cambios programados en la infraestructura. AWS Trusted Advisor analiza su entorno frente a comprobaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas en costo, rendimiento, seguridad y tolerancia a fallos, y proporciona recomendaciones de asesoramiento en lugar de notificaciones de eventos en tiempo real o alertas proactivas sobre actividades programadas de la infraestructura de AWS. El panel de AWS Systems Manager proporciona visibilidad operativa y herramientas de gesti\xf3n para su infraestructura de AWS, incluido el cumplimiento de parches, el inventario y el estado de automatizaci\xf3n, y es una consola de gesti\xf3n para sus recursos en lugar de un servicio de notificaciones para eventos de servicios de AWS."},{"id":"q882","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 plan de AWS Support proporciona acceso a revisiones arquitect\xf3nicas y operativas, as\xed como acceso 24/7 a ingenieros s\xe9nior de Cloud Support por correo electr\xf3nico, chat en l\xednea y tel\xe9fono?","options":{"A":"Basic","B":"Business","C":"Developer","D":"Enterprise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"El plan Enterprise Support proporciona acceso a revisiones arquitect\xf3nicas y operativas, incluidas las revisiones de Well-Architected, y acceso 24/7 a ingenieros s\xe9nior de Cloud Support por correo electr\xf3nico, chat y tel\xe9fono.\\nBasic Support proporciona acceso a documentaci\xf3n, documentos t\xe9cnicos (whitepapers) y las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor, y no incluye revisiones arquitect\xf3nicas ni acceso 24/7 a ingenieros s\xe9nior de Cloud Support. Business Support proporciona acceso 24/7 a ingenieros de Cloud Support y un SLA de respuesta de una hora para casos de ca\xedda del sistema en producci\xf3n, pero no incluye revisiones arquitect\xf3nicas y operativas, que son exclusivas de Enterprise Support. Developer Support proporciona acceso por correo electr\xf3nico en horario laboral a ingenieros de Cloud Support para preguntas t\xe9cnicas generales, y no incluye acceso 24/7, soporte telef\xf3nico ni revisiones arquitect\xf3nicas y operativas."},{"id":"q892","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puede una empresa separar los costos de tr\xe1fico de red, Amazon EC2, Amazon S3 y otros servicios de AWS por departamento?","options":{"A":"Agregar etiquetas espec\xedficas del departamento a cada recurso","B":"Crear una VPC separada para cada departamento","C":"Crear una cuenta de AWS separada para cada departamento","D":"Usar AWS Organizations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Crear una cuenta de AWS separada para cada departamento proporciona la separaci\xf3n de costos m\xe1s limpia, ya que todos los cargos dentro de cada cuenta pertenecen exclusivamente a ese departamento y aparecen como una l\xednea de detalle distinta en una factura consolidada.\\nAgregar etiquetas espec\xedficas del departamento a cada recurso permite la atribuci\xf3n de costos dentro de cuentas compartidas mediante Cost Explorer, pero el etiquetado depende de una aplicaci\xf3n humana constante y no proporciona los l\xedmites de facturaci\xf3n estrictos que crean las cuentas separadas. Crear una VPC separada para cada departamento a\xedsla los recursos de red y puede mejorar la seguridad, pero las VPC no crean facturas ni l\xedmites de costos separados, ya que todos los recursos de VPC siguen apareciendo en la factura de la misma cuenta de AWS. Usar AWS Organizations proporciona gobernanza de cuentas y facturaci\xf3n consolidada, pero no separa por s\xed mismo los costos por departamento. Permite visibilidad entre cuentas, pero la separaci\xf3n de costos proviene de tener cuentas distintas, no de Organizations en s\xed."},{"id":"q893","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es un beneficio de la facturaci\xf3n consolidada para las cuentas de AWS?","options":{"A":"Acceso a AWS Health Dashboard","B":"Descuentos por volumen de uso combinado","C":"Mejor seguridad de la cuenta","D":"AWS IAM centralizado"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La facturaci\xf3n consolidada combina el uso de todas las cuentas miembro en AWS Organizations, lo que permite que la organizaci\xf3n califique para niveles de precios por volumen que las cuentas individuales podr\xedan no alcanzar por s\xed solas.\\nEl acceso a AWS Health Dashboard est\xe1 disponible para todos los clientes de AWS independientemente de si usan la facturaci\xf3n consolidada, y no es un beneficio que la facturaci\xf3n consolidada habilite espec\xedficamente. Una mejor seguridad de la cuenta no es un beneficio directo de la facturaci\xf3n consolidada. La postura de seguridad se rige mediante IAM, pol\xedticas de control de servicios y configuraciones de seguridad, no mediante la estructura de facturaci\xf3n. AWS IAM centralizado no es un beneficio de la facturaci\xf3n consolidada. IAM opera a nivel de cuenta individual, y la gesti\xf3n de acceso centralizada entre cuentas se maneja mediante pol\xedticas de AWS Organizations y roles de IAM, no mediante la facturaci\xf3n consolidada."},{"id":"q894","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a un usuario establecer l\xedmites personalizados de costo y uso, y alertar cuando se superan los umbrales?","options":{"A":"AWS Organizations","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"Cost Explorer","D":"AWS Trusted Advisor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Budgets le permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costo y uso con alertas configurables que le notifican por correo electr\xf3nico o SNS cuando el gasto real o previsto supera los l\xedmites definidos.\\nAWS Organizations proporciona gesti\xf3n centralizada de cuentas con facturaci\xf3n consolidada y controles de gobernanza, y es un servicio de estructura de cuentas en lugar de un servicio para establecer umbrales de costos personalizados y enviar alertas cuando se superan esos l\xedmites. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas de los patrones de gasto de AWS y admite la previsi\xf3n de costos, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis y planificaci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio de alertas basado en umbrales. AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno y proporciona recomendaciones en materia de seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y es una herramienta de asesoramiento en lugar de un servicio para establecer y supervisar l\xedmites de gasto personalizados."},{"id":"q896","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 herramienta se puede usar para supervisar los l\xedmites de servicio de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Pricing Calculator","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Health Dashboard","D":"AWS Cost and Usage Report"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor incluye comprobaciones que monitorean su uso frente a los l\xedmites de servicio de AWS y le alertan cuando se est\xe1 acercando a un l\xedmite o lo ha alcanzado, lo que ayuda a evitar interrupciones del servicio causadas por el agotamiento de l\xedmites.\\nAWS Pricing Calculator se utiliza para estimar costos futuros de los servicios de AWS antes de aprovisionarlos, y no monitorea el uso actual del servicio frente a los l\xedmites. AWS Health Dashboard proporciona alertas personalizadas sobre eventos de AWS y mantenimiento programado que pueden afectar sus recursos, y aunque puede notificarle sobre eventos relacionados con l\xedmites, no es la herramienta principal para monitorear activamente el uso de l\xedmites de servicio. AWS Cost and Usage Reports proporciona datos granulares sobre el gasto real de AWS desglosados por servicio, recurso y per\xedodo de tiempo, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de facturaci\xf3n en lugar de una herramienta de monitoreo de l\xedmites de servicio."},{"id":"q902","question":"La aplicaci\xf3n de una empresa tiene horas de inicio y finalizaci\xf3n flexibles. \xbfQu\xe9 modelo de precios de Amazon EC2 ser\xe1 el M\xc1S rentable?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Spot Instances","C":"Reserved Instances","D":"Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las Spot Instances ofrecen ahorros de hasta el 90 % frente a los precios de On-Demand y son ideales para aplicaciones con horas de inicio y finalizaci\xf3n flexibles, ya que se pueden interrumpir y reiniciar para aprovechar los precios de capacidad disponible.\\nLas On-Demand Instances cobran la tarifa horaria completa y, aunque proporcionan disponibilidad garantizada, no aprovechan los ahorros de costos disponibles para cargas de trabajo que pueden ajustar su programaci\xf3n seg\xfan la disponibilidad de capacidad disponible. Las Reserved Instances requieren un compromiso de uno o tres a\xf1os y est\xe1n dise\xf1adas para cargas de trabajo en estado estable con uso predecible, lo que las hace poco adecuadas para cargas de trabajo con horarios flexibles que no requieren capacidad continua. Los Dedicated Hosts proporcionan un servidor f\xedsico dedicado por completo a un \xfanico cliente para fines de cumplimiento y licenciamiento, y tienen un precio superior a las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2 en lugar de proporcionar ahorros de costos para cargas de trabajo flexibles."},{"id":"q904","question":"Un profesional de la nube tiene una carga de trabajo de an\xe1lisis de datos que se ejecuta con poca frecuencia y se puede interrumpir sin perjuicio. Para optimizar el costo, \xbfqu\xe9 opci\xf3n de compra de Amazon EC2 se debe usar?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las Spot Instances proporcionan los mayores descuentos para cargas de trabajo que se ejecutan con poca frecuencia y se pueden interrumpir, lo que las convierte en la opci\xf3n m\xe1s rentable cuando existen flexibilidad de programaci\xf3n y tolerancia a interrupciones.\\nLas On-Demand Instances cobran la tarifa horaria completa sin descuento, lo que las hace significativamente m\xe1s costosas que las Spot Instances para una carga de trabajo que califica para precios de Spot debido a su naturaleza interrumpible y flexible. Las Reserved Instances proporcionan descuentos mediante compromisos a largo plazo de uno o tres a\xf1os, pero est\xe1n dise\xf1adas para cargas de trabajo en estado estable que se ejecutan continuamente en lugar de cargas de trabajo que se ejecutan con poca frecuencia, donde el compromiso dar\xeda como resultado pagar por capacidad no utilizada. Los Dedicated Hosts proporcionan un servidor f\xedsico dedicado a un \xfanico cliente para fines de aislamiento de cumplimiento y licenciamiento, y tienen un precio superior a las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2 en lugar de proporcionar ahorros de costos para cargas de trabajo poco frecuentes e interrumpibles."},{"id":"q907","question":"Una empresa con un plan de AWS Support de nivel Developer aprovision\xf3 una base de datos Amazon RDS y no puede conectarse a ella. \xbfA qui\xe9n debe contactar el desarrollador para este nivel de soporte?","options":{"A":"AWS Support mediante un caso de soporte","B":"AWS Professional Services","C":"gerente de cuenta t\xe9cnica de AWS","D":"socios de consultor\xeda de AWS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Con un plan Developer Support, el desarrollador debe contactar a AWS Support abriendo un caso de soporte a trav\xe9s de la AWS Management Console, que proporciona asistencia por correo electr\xf3nico de ingenieros de Cloud Support durante el horario laboral.\\nAWS Professional Services es un equipo de expertos que trabaja con clientes en estrategia, arquitectura y migraci\xf3n a la nube, y no es el canal adecuado para resolver un problema t\xe9cnico de conectividad con un servicio de AWS existente. Un Technical Account Manager proporciona orientaci\xf3n proactiva y es un contacto dedicado solo para clientes de Enterprise Support, y no est\xe1 disponible para clientes del plan Developer Support. Los socios de consultor\xeda de AWS son empresas externas de servicios profesionales de AWS Partner Network, y no son el contacto adecuado para problemas de soporte t\xe9cnico con servicios de AWS bajo un plan de AWS Support activo."},{"id":"q911","question":"\xbfSobre cu\xe1l de los siguientes aspectos proporciona recomendaciones AWS Trusted Advisor?","options":{"A":"Optimizaci\xf3n de costos","B":"Auditor\xeda","C":"Arquitectura sin servidor","D":"Rendimiento","E":"Escalabilidad"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones en cinco categor\xedas: Optimizaci\xf3n de costos, Rendimiento, Seguridad, Tolerancia a fallos y L\xedmites de servicio. Optimizaci\xf3n de costos y Rendimiento son ambas categor\xedas oficiales de Trusted Advisor.\\nAuditor\xeda no es una categor\xeda de Trusted Advisor. El seguimiento de auditor\xeda y la supervisi\xf3n de cumplimiento se gestionan mediante servicios como AWS CloudTrail y AWS Config, en lugar de ser una categor\xeda dentro del marco de recomendaciones de Trusted Advisor. Arquitectura sin servidor no es una categor\xeda de Trusted Advisor. La gu\xeda de dise\xf1o sin servidor est\xe1 disponible a trav\xe9s de la documentaci\xf3n de AWS y el Well-Architected Framework, en lugar de mediante comprobaciones automatizadas de Trusted Advisor. Escalabilidad no es una categor\xeda de Trusted Advisor. Si bien algunas comprobaciones de Trusted Advisor abordan l\xedmites de recursos que podr\xedan restringir el escalado, la escalabilidad como concepto se aborda a trav\xe9s del pilar de Eficiencia del Rendimiento del AWS Well-Architected Framework, en lugar de como una categor\xeda dedicada de Trusted Advisor."},{"id":"q914","question":"Una empresa que opera en la nube de AWS requiere facturas separadas para entornos espec\xedficos, como desarrollo, pruebas y producci\xf3n. \xbfC\xf3mo se puede lograr esto?","options":{"A":"Usar varias cuentas de AWS","B":"Usar etiquetado de recursos","C":"Usar varias VPC","D":"Usar Cost Explorer"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Usar varias cuentas de AWS, una por entorno, proporciona facturas completamente separadas para desarrollo, pruebas y producci\xf3n, ya que todos los cargos dentro de cada cuenta se facturan de forma independiente bajo Consolidated Billing.\\nEl etiquetado de recursos permite la asignaci\xf3n de costos por entorno dentro de una cuenta compartida a trav\xe9s de Cost Explorer, pero las etiquetas dependen de una aplicaci\xf3n coherente y no crean facturas separadas. Todos los recursos etiquetados siguen apareciendo en la factura de la misma cuenta. Usar varias VPC a\xedsla los recursos de red para cada entorno dentro de la misma cuenta, pero las VPC no crean facturas separadas, ya que todos los recursos de todas las VPC de una cuenta se facturan juntos en una sola factura. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaci\xf3n y an\xe1lisis de patrones de gasto y admite el filtrado por etiqueta o servicio, pero es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis que lee datos de facturaci\xf3n existentes en lugar de un mecanismo para generar facturas separadas por entorno."},{"id":"q916","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 se puede utilizar para reducir el costo de ejecutar instancias de Amazon EC2?","options":{"A":"Spot Instances para cargas de trabajo sin estado y flexibles","B":"Instancias optimizadas para memoria para cargas de trabajo de alto c\xf3mputo","C":"On-Demand Instances para cargas de trabajo sostenidas y de alto costo","D":"Reserved Instances para cargas de trabajo sostenidas","E":"L\xedmites de gasto establecidos mediante AWS Budgets"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","D"],"explanation":"Spot Instances proporcionan descuentos profundos para cargas de trabajo flexibles y sin estado que pueden tolerar interrupciones, y Reserved Instances ofrecen ahorros significativos para cargas de trabajo sostenidas con uso predecible mediante compromisos a largo plazo. Ambas reducen directamente el costo de ejecutar instancias de EC2.\\nLas instancias optimizadas para memoria est\xe1n dise\xf1adas para cargas de trabajo que procesan grandes conjuntos de datos en memoria, como bases de datos en memoria y procesamiento en tiempo real, y seleccionar el tipo de instancia correcto mejora la eficiencia del rendimiento, pero no reduce el costo por s\xed mismo a menos que ajuste el tama\xf1o de una instancia sobreaprovisionada. On-Demand Instances cobran la tarifa horaria completa sin descuento, lo que las convierte en la opci\xf3n de compra de EC2 m\xe1s costosa y no en un mecanismo para reducir costos en comparaci\xf3n con los precios reservados o spot. Los l\xedmites de gasto establecidos mediante AWS Budgets le alertan cuando el gasto supera un umbral, pero no reducen por s\xed mismos el costo de ejecutar instancias de EC2. Budgets es una herramienta de gobernanza y visibilidad, no un mecanismo de precios."},{"id":"q922","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de la nube de AWS puede enviar alertas a los clientes si se superan umbrales de gasto personalizados?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Cost Allocation Tags","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Budgets env\xeda notificaciones de alerta cuando el gasto real o previsto supera los umbrales personalizados que usted define, lo que permite una gesti\xf3n proactiva de costos antes de que las facturas crezcan inesperadamente.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas de patrones hist\xf3ricos de gasto y uso de AWS con capacidad de previsi\xf3n, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis y planificaci\xf3n en lugar de un servicio que env\xeda alertas automatizadas basadas en umbrales. AWS Cost Allocation Tags son etiquetas de clave-valor aplicadas a los recursos de AWS que permiten la atribuci\xf3n de costos por proyecto, equipo o entorno en los informes de facturaci\xf3n, y son un mecanismo de categorizaci\xf3n de costos en lugar de un servicio de alertas. AWS Organizations proporciona gesti\xf3n centralizada de cuentas con Consolidated Billing y controles de gobernanza, y es un servicio de estructura y gobernanza de cuentas en lugar de un servicio que env\xeda alertas de umbrales de gasto."},{"id":"q926","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n de precios de Amazon EC2 es la m\xe1s adecuada para aplicaciones con cargas de trabajo a corto plazo, con picos o impredecibles que no pueden interrumpirse?","options":{"A":"Spot Instances","B":"Dedicated Hosts","C":"On-Demand Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"On-Demand Instances son mejores para cargas de trabajo a corto plazo, con picos o impredecibles que no pueden interrumpirse, ya que no tienen compromiso a largo plazo, ni costo inicial, ni riesgo de interrupci\xf3n de capacidad.\\nSpot Instances utilizan capacidad de AWS no utilizada con descuentos significativos, pero AWS puede interrumpirlas en cualquier momento, lo que las hace inadecuadas para cargas de trabajo que no pueden interrumpirse durante la ejecuci\xf3n. Dedicated Hosts proporcionan un servidor f\xedsico dedicado a un solo cliente por motivos de cumplimiento y licenciamiento, y tienen un precio superior al de las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2, lo que las hace m\xe1s costosas e innecesarias para cargas de trabajo sin requisitos de aislamiento. Reserved Instances requieren un compromiso m\xednimo de un a\xf1o y est\xe1n dise\xf1adas para cargas de trabajo predecibles y en estado estable. Comprometerse con una reserva para una carga de trabajo a corto plazo, con picos o impredecible dar\xeda como resultado pagar por capacidad m\xe1s all\xe1 del uso real."},{"id":"q930","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes permite a los usuarios de AWS gestionar asignaciones de costos para la facturaci\xf3n?","options":{"A":"Etiquetar recursos","B":"Limitar qui\xe9n puede crear recursos","C":"Agregar un m\xe9todo de pago secundario","D":"Ejecutar todas las operaciones en una sola cuenta de AWS"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Etiquetar recursos con pares clave-valor, como nombres de departamento, permite el seguimiento de la asignaci\xf3n de costos a trav\xe9s de AWS Cost Explorer y los informes de facturaci\xf3n, lo que permite atribuir los costos de cada recurso a la unidad de negocio correspondiente.\\nLimitar qui\xe9n puede crear recursos es una pr\xe1ctica de gobernanza de IAM y Service Control Policy que controla los permisos de acceso y, si bien evita la creaci\xf3n no autorizada de recursos, no asigna ni atribuye costos a unidades de negocio espec\xedficas por s\xed misma. Agregar un m\xe9todo de pago secundario cambia c\xf3mo se pagan las facturas de AWS en lugar de c\xf3mo se categorizan o atribuyen los costos en toda la organizaci\xf3n, y no tiene efecto en los informes de asignaci\xf3n de costos. Ejecutar todas las operaciones en una sola cuenta de AWS consolida la facturaci\xf3n en un solo lugar, pero no proporciona asignaci\xf3n de costos por departamento sin etiquetas u otros mecanismos de atribuci\xf3n para distinguir qu\xe9 recursos pertenecen a qu\xe9 unidad de negocio."},{"id":"q934","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 requisito debe cumplirse para que una cuenta de miembro se desvincule de una cuenta de AWS Organizations?","options":{"A":"La cuenta vinculada debe cumplir activamente con AWS System and Organization Controls (SOC)","B":"La cuenta pagadora y la cuenta vinculada deben crear ambas casos de AWS Support para solicitar que la cuenta de miembro se desvincule de la organizaci\xf3n","C":"La cuenta de miembro debe cumplir los requisitos de una cuenta independiente","D":"La cuenta pagadora debe utilizarse para eliminar la cuenta vinculada de la organizaci\xf3n"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Para desvincular una cuenta de miembro de AWS Organizations, la cuenta debe cumplir los requisitos de una cuenta independiente, incluidos tener informaci\xf3n de pago v\xe1lida, un plan de soporte e informaci\xf3n de contacto configurados de forma independiente.\\nEl cumplimiento activo con AWS System and Organization Controls no es un requisito para desvincular una cuenta de miembro de AWS Organizations. El cumplimiento de SOC es un programa de certificaci\xf3n de AWS en lugar de una condici\xf3n previa para la separaci\xf3n de cuentas. No se requiere que tanto la cuenta pagadora como la cuenta vinculada creen casos de AWS Support para solicitar la desvinculaci\xf3n. El proceso de eliminaci\xf3n de una cuenta de miembro se realiza directamente a trav\xe9s de la consola de AWS Organizations por la cuenta de administraci\xf3n o por la propia cuenta de miembro. La cuenta pagadora puede eliminar una cuenta vinculada de la organizaci\xf3n a trav\xe9s de la consola de administraci\xf3n, pero la propia cuenta de miembro tambi\xe9n puede abandonar la organizaci\xf3n directamente, por lo que no se requiere estrictamente que la cuenta pagadora inicie la eliminaci\xf3n."},{"id":"q940","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de los siguientes ayuda a identificar costos por departamento?","options":{"A":"Usar etiquetas en los recursos","B":"Usar varias cuentas de AWS","C":"Usar un gestor de cuenta","D":"Usar AWS Trusted Advisor","E":"Usar Consolidated Billing"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","B"],"explanation":"Usar etiquetas en los recursos permite la asignaci\xf3n de costos por departamento a trav\xe9s de los informes de Cost Explorer, y usar varias cuentas de AWS por departamento crea l\xedmites de facturaci\xf3n estrictos con facturas separadas que separan claramente los costos.\\nUsar un gestor de cuenta se refiere a una relaci\xf3n de soporte en lugar de un mecanismo t\xe9cnico para identificar costos por departamento, y no proporciona un m\xe9todo para atribuir o separar los costos de los recursos de AWS entre unidades de negocio. Usar AWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno para obtener recomendaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas en materia de costos, rendimiento, seguridad y tolerancia a fallos, pero no proporciona un mecanismo para identificar o atribuir costos a departamentos espec\xedficos. Usar Consolidated Billing combina el uso de varias cuentas en una sola factura, que es el mecanismo de agregaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n que permite ver juntos la separaci\xf3n lograda por varias cuentas, en lugar de ser un mecanismo de identificaci\xf3n por s\xed mismo."},{"id":"q942","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite una gesti\xf3n eficaz de costos de varias cuentas de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Organizations","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Direct Connect","D":"Amazon Connect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Organizations proporciona facturaci\xf3n consolidada y pol\xedticas de control de servicios para una gesti\xf3n eficaz de costos en varias cuentas de AWS, lo que permite descuentos por volumen mediante el uso combinado y controles de gobernanza para evitar gastos excesivos.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor inspecciona su entorno de AWS existente y proporciona recomendaciones en materia de seguridad, costos, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, pero opera principalmente dentro de cuentas individuales en lugar de proporcionar una estructura centralizada de gesti\xf3n de costos en varias cuentas. AWS Direct Connect proporciona una conexi\xf3n de red privada dedicada entre la infraestructura local y AWS, y es un servicio de red sin capacidad para gestionar costos en varias cuentas de AWS. Amazon Connect es un servicio de centro de contacto en la nube para gestionar comunicaciones con clientes mediante voz y chat, y es un servicio de interacci\xf3n con clientes completamente no relacionado con la gesti\xf3n de costos de cuentas de AWS."},{"id":"q943","question":"Una empresa est\xe1 probando una nueva aplicaci\xf3n orientada al cliente en Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) durante un mes. \xbfQu\xe9 modelo de precios es apropiado?","options":{"A":"Instancias reservadas","B":"Instancias Spot","C":"Instancias bajo demanda","D":"Hosts dedicados"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las Instancias bajo demanda son apropiadas para una prueba piloto de un mes porque no requieren compromiso a largo plazo, la aplicaci\xf3n orientada al cliente no puede arriesgarse a interrupciones por recuperaci\xf3n de Spot, y la duraci\xf3n corta no justifica un plazo de Instancia reservada.\\nLas Instancias reservadas requieren un compromiso m\xednimo de un a\xf1o, lo que las hace totalmente inadecuadas para una prueba piloto de un mes donde el plazo de reserva superar\xeda ampliamente el per\xedodo de uso previsto. Las Instancias Spot pueden ser interrumpidas por AWS cuando se recupera capacidad no utilizada, lo cual es inaceptable para una aplicaci\xf3n orientada al cliente que debe permanecer disponible de forma consistente durante todo el per\xedodo de prueba piloto. Los Hosts dedicados proporcionan aislamiento de servidor f\xedsico por motivos de cumplimiento y licenciamiento, y tienen un precio superior a las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2, lo que los hace innecesariamente costosos para una aplicaci\xf3n est\xe1ndar de prueba piloto de un mes."},{"id":"q944","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 herramientas de AWS pronostican autom\xe1ticamente los costos futuros de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Support Center","B":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","C":"AWS Pricing Calculator","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Cost Explorer utiliza datos hist\xf3ricos de gasto y aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para generar autom\xe1ticamente pron\xf3sticos de costos de hasta 12 meses hacia el futuro, lo que lo convierte en la herramienta de AWS que pronostica autom\xe1ticamente los costos futuros.\\nAWS Support Center es el portal para crear, ver y gestionar casos de soporte de AWS, y no tiene relaci\xf3n con el pron\xf3stico de costos ni con el an\xe1lisis financiero. AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona el desglose m\xe1s granular de los cargos reales de AWS ya incurridos, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de facturaci\xf3n hist\xf3rica en lugar de un servicio de pron\xf3stico. AWS Pricing Calculator permite a los usuarios modelar configuraciones de servicios de AWS y estimar costos para nuevas implementaciones, pero requiere entrada manual y no genera autom\xe1ticamente pron\xf3sticos basados en patrones de uso existentes como lo hace Cost Explorer."},{"id":"q946","question":"Un usuario puede configurar una cuenta de pagador principal para ver informes de facturaci\xf3n consolidada mediante:","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"Amazon Macie","C":"Amazon QuickSight","D":"AWS Organizations"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Organizations permite que una cuenta de pagador principal vea informes de facturaci\xf3n consolidada de todas las cuentas miembro, proporcionando una vista unificada del gasto y del uso con la capacidad de aplicar pol\xedticas de gobernanza.\\nAWS Budgets le permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costos y uso, y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, y es una herramienta de gobernanza y alerta de costos en lugar del servicio que permite el acceso a informes de facturaci\xf3n consolidada mediante una cuenta de pagador principal. Amazon Macie es un servicio de seguridad de datos que utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para descubrir y proteger datos confidenciales almacenados en Amazon S3, y es un servicio de seguridad sin relaci\xf3n con la facturaci\xf3n consolidada ni con los informes de la cuenta de pagador principal. Amazon QuickSight es un servicio de inteligencia empresarial sin servidor para crear paneles y visualizaciones a partir de datos empresariales, y aunque puede visualizar datos de facturaci\xf3n, no es en s\xed mismo el servicio que permite la facturaci\xf3n consolidada mediante una estructura de cuenta de pagador principal."},{"id":"q949","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una caracter\xedstica de las Instancias reservadas convertibles (RI)?","options":{"A":"Los usuarios pueden intercambiar RI convertibles por otras RI convertibles de una familia de instancias diferente con un valor igual o superior al de las RI convertibles que est\xe1n intercambiando","B":"Los usuarios pueden intercambiar RI convertibles por otras RI convertibles en diferentes regiones de AWS","C":"Los usuarios pueden vender y comprar RI convertibles en AWS Marketplace","D":"Los usuarios pueden acortar el plazo de sus RI convertibles fusion\xe1ndolas con otras RI convertibles"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Las Instancias reservadas convertibles se pueden intercambiar por otras RI convertibles de una familia de instancias diferente, siempre que la nueva RI tenga un valor igual o superior, lo que proporciona flexibilidad para adaptarse a necesidades cambiantes.\\nLas RI convertibles son espec\xedficas de una regi\xf3n y no se pueden intercambiar por RI en una regi\xf3n de AWS diferente. Solo las Instancias reservadas est\xe1ndar se pueden publicar en AWS Reserved Instance Marketplace, no las RI convertibles. Fusionar RI convertibles para acortar su plazo no es una funci\xf3n compatible, ya que los plazos de las RI son fijos de uno o tres a\xf1os y no se pueden modificar combinando reservas."},{"id":"q950","question":"Un dise\xf1o de arquitectura incluye Amazon EC2, un Elastic Load Balancer y Amazon RDS. \xbfCu\xe1l es la MEJOR forma de obtener una estimaci\xf3n de costos mensual para esta arquitectura?","options":{"A":"Abrir un caso de AWS Support, proporcionar la propuesta de arquitectura y solicitar una estimaci\xf3n de costos mensual","B":"Recopilar los precios publicados de los servicios de AWS y calcular la estimaci\xf3n mensual","C":"Usar AWS Pricing Calculator para estimar el costo mensual","D":"Usar AWS Cost and Usage Report para estimar el costo mensual"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator permite modelar la configuraci\xf3n espec\xedfica de sus instancias EC2, Elastic Load Balancer y base de datos RDS para generar una estimaci\xf3n de costos mensual detallada y precisa antes del despliegue.\\nAbrir un caso de AWS Support para solicitar una estimaci\xf3n de costos es innecesariamente complejo y lento cuando una herramienta de autoservicio como AWS Pricing Calculator proporciona estimaciones instant\xe1neas y detalladas sin requerir la intervenci\xf3n del soporte. Recopilar los precios publicados y calcular manualmente es posible, pero propenso a errores e ineficiente en comparaci\xf3n con AWS Pricing Calculator, que tiene en cuenta autom\xe1ticamente todas las dimensiones de precios, incluidos la transferencia de datos, el almacenamiento y las variaciones de precios regionales. AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona datos granulares sobre los cargos reales de AWS ya incurridos en recursos activos, y no se puede usar para estimar el costo mensual de una arquitectura que a\xfan no se ha desplegado."},{"id":"q956","question":"Un usuario tiene una carga de trabajo con estado que se ejecutar\xe1 en Amazon EC2 durante los pr\xf3ximos 3 a\xf1os. \xbfCu\xe1l es el modelo de precios M\xc1S rentable para esta carga de trabajo?","options":{"A":"Instancias bajo demanda","B":"Instancias reservadas","C":"Instancias dedicadas","D":"Instancias Spot"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las Instancias reservadas proporcionan el precio m\xe1s rentable para una carga de trabajo con estado de 3 a\xf1os con uso predecible y constante, y ofrecen hasta un 72 % de descuento sobre las Instancias bajo demanda mediante un compromiso a largo plazo.\\nLas Instancias bajo demanda cobran la tarifa horaria completa sin descuento, lo que las convierte en la opci\xf3n m\xe1s costosa para una carga de trabajo que se ejecuta continuamente durante tres a\xf1os, donde la reducci\xf3n de costos de las Instancias reservadas ser\xeda sustancial. Las Instancias dedicadas se ejecutan en hardware dedicado a un \xfanico cliente por motivos de cumplimiento y licenciamiento, y tienen un precio superior a las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2 en lugar de proporcionar ahorros para una carga de trabajo est\xe1ndar con estado. Las Instancias Spot pueden ser interrumpidas por AWS cuando se recupera capacidad no utilizada, lo que las hace inadecuadas para una carga de trabajo con estado que debe permanecer activa continuamente y conservar su estado durante el per\xedodo de tres a\xf1os."},{"id":"q957","question":"Un profesional de la nube necesita una instancia de Amazon EC2 que se lance y se ejecute durante 7 horas sin interrupciones. \xbfCu\xe1l es la opci\xf3n m\xe1s adecuada y rentable para esta tarea?","options":{"A":"Instancia bajo demanda","B":"Instancia reservada","C":"Host dedicado","D":"Instancia Spot"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Las Instancias bajo demanda son m\xe1s adecuadas para una tarea corta de 7 horas que no se puede interrumpir, ya que proporcionan disponibilidad garantizada sin compromiso a largo plazo y sin riesgo de interrupci\xf3n durante la ejecuci\xf3n.\\nLas Instancias reservadas requieren un compromiso m\xednimo de un a\xf1o, lo que las hace totalmente inadecuadas para una \xfanica tarea de 7 horas en la que el per\xedodo de compromiso superar\xeda enormemente la duraci\xf3n real de uso. Los Hosts dedicados proporcionan aislamiento de servidor f\xedsico por motivos de cumplimiento y licenciamiento, y tienen un precio superior a las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2, lo que los hace innecesariamente costosos para una tarea sencilla de 7 horas sin requisitos de aislamiento. Las Instancias Spot pueden ser interrumpidas por AWS cuando se recupera capacidad no utilizada, lo que infringe directamente el requisito de que la instancia debe ejecutarse durante 7 horas sin interrupciones."},{"id":"q958","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les de los siguientes son beneficios de usar AWS Trusted Advisor?","options":{"A":"Proporcionar orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores de alto rendimiento","B":"Crear y rotar claves de cifrado","C":"Detectar recursos infrautilizados para ahorrar costos","D":"Mejorar la seguridad mediante la supervisi\xf3n proactiva del entorno de AWS","E":"Implementar etiquetado obligatorio en los recursos de AWS"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["C","D"],"explanation":"AWS Trusted Advisor detecta recursos infrautilizados, como instancias EC2 inactivas y balanceadores de carga no utilizados, para ayudar a reducir costos, y supervisa de forma proactiva el entorno de AWS en busca de vulnerabilidades de seguridad y configuraciones incorrectas, lo que hace que tanto la optimizaci\xf3n de costos como la mejora de la seguridad sean beneficios principales del servicio.\\nProporcionar orquestaci\xf3n de contenedores de alto rendimiento describe Amazon ECS o Amazon EKS, que son servicios de c\xf3mputo para ejecutar cargas de trabajo en contenedores, y no tiene relaci\xf3n alguna con Trusted Advisor. Crear y rotar claves de cifrado describe AWS Key Management Service, que administra claves criptogr\xe1ficas para la protecci\xf3n de datos, y no es una funci\xf3n de Trusted Advisor. Implementar etiquetado obligatorio en los recursos de AWS describe una capacidad de gobernanza lograda mediante pol\xedticas de control de servicio de AWS Organizations o reglas de AWS Config, y no es algo que Trusted Advisor pueda imponer."},{"id":"q965","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 herramienta permite a los clientes sin una cuenta de AWS estimar costos para casi todos los servicios de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","C":"AWS Budgets","D":"AWS Pricing Calculator"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Pricing Calculator es de acceso p\xfablico sin una cuenta de AWS, lo que permite a cualquiera modelar configuraciones de servicios de AWS y generar estimaciones de costos para casi todos los servicios de AWS antes de comprometerse con cualquier implementaci\xf3n.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaci\xf3n y an\xe1lisis de patrones reales de gasto de AWS y requiere una cuenta de AWS activa con historial de uso para generar datos significativos. AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona datos de facturaci\xf3n granulares para cuentas de AWS activas, y requiere que se configure una cuenta de AWS y que se generen cargos antes de poder proporcionar datos. AWS Budgets requiere una cuenta de AWS activa para configurar umbrales de costos y uso y enviar alertas, y no puede ser utilizado por alguien sin una cuenta para estimar costos de servicios de AWS."},{"id":"q967","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 modelo de precios generar\xeda el m\xe1ximo ahorro en Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) para un servidor de base de datos que debe estar en l\xednea durante un a\xf1o?","options":{"A":"Spot Instance","B":"On-Demand Instance","C":"Partial Upfront Reserved Instance","D":"No Upfront Reserved Instance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Una Partial Upfront Reserved Instance por un a\xf1o proporciona ahorros significativos frente a On-Demand y requiere menos pago inicial que All Upfront, lo que la convierte en una opci\xf3n rentable para una base de datos que debe permanecer en l\xednea continuamente durante un a\xf1o.\\nLas Spot Instances pueden ser interrumpidas por AWS cuando se recupera la capacidad excedente, lo que las hace totalmente inadecuadas para un servidor de base de datos que debe permanecer en l\xednea continuamente durante un a\xf1o completo. Las On-Demand Instances cobran la tarifa horaria completa sin descuento, lo que las hace significativamente m\xe1s costosas que las Reserved Instances para una carga de trabajo que se ejecuta continuamente durante un a\xf1o con un patr\xf3n de uso constante y predecible. Las No Upfront Reserved Instances tambi\xe9n ofrecen un descuento frente a On-Demand por un compromiso de un a\xf1o, pero el descuento es menor que el de Partial Upfront porque AWS no recibe ning\xfan pago al inicio del plazo y, por lo tanto, ofrece menos incentivo."},{"id":"q973","question":"AnyCompany adquiri\xf3 recientemente Example Corp. Ambas empresas usan recursos de AWS, y AnyCompany quiere una \xfanica factura agregada. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n permite a AnyCompany recibir una \xfanica factura?","options":{"A":"Example Corp. debe enviar una solicitud a su AWS Solutions Architect o AWS Technical Account Manager para vincular las cuentas y consolidar la facturaci\xf3n","B":"AnyCompany debe crear un nuevo caso de soporte en el AWS Support Center solicitando que se combinen ambas facturas","C":"Enviar una invitaci\xf3n para unirse a la organizaci\xf3n desde la cuenta maestra de AWS Organizations de AnyCompany a Example Corp","D":"Migrar las VPC, las instancias Amazon EC2 y otros recursos de Example Corp. a la cuenta de AWS de AnyCompany"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enviar una invitaci\xf3n desde la cuenta maestra de AWS Organizations de AnyCompany a Example Corp los agrega a la organizaci\xf3n, lo que permite la facturaci\xf3n consolidada en una sola factura y el uso combinado para descuentos por volumen.\\nSolicitar que un AWS solutions architect o TAM vincule cuentas no es el proceso de autoservicio para unirse a AWS Organizations. La consolidaci\xf3n de cuentas se configura directamente a trav\xe9s de la consola de AWS Organizations sin requerir la intervenci\xf3n del personal de AWS. Crear un nuevo caso de soporte solicitando que se combinen ambas facturas no es el proceso para habilitar la facturaci\xf3n consolidada, que se configura a trav\xe9s de AWS Organizations en lugar de mediante solicitudes de soporte. Migrar las VPC, las instancias EC2 y otros recursos de Example Corp. a la cuenta de AnyCompany fusionar\xeda la infraestructura en una sola cuenta, lo cual es una migraci\xf3n compleja y disruptiva totalmente innecesaria cuando AWS Organizations puede combinar la facturaci\xf3n mientras mantiene las cuentas separadas."},{"id":"q974","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 herramienta se puede usar para crear alertas cuando el costo real o previsto de los servicios de AWS supera un umbral determinado?","options":{"A":"Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"AWS Cost and Usage Report","D":"AWS CloudTrail"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Budgets crea alertas cuando los costos reales o previstos superan los umbrales definidos, y env\xeda notificaciones por correo electr\xf3nico o SNS para ayudarte a gestionar el gasto de forma proactiva antes de que las facturas alcancen niveles inesperados.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas y an\xe1lisis de patrones hist\xf3ricos de gasto de AWS con capacidad de previsi\xf3n, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis en lugar de un servicio de alertas basado en umbrales que env\xeda notificaciones cuando los costos superan l\xedmites. AWS Cost and Usage Report proporciona el desglose m\xe1s granular de los cargos reales por servicio, recurso y per\xedodo de tiempo, y es una herramienta de datos de facturaci\xf3n hist\xf3rica en lugar de un servicio que crea y env\xeda alertas de umbrales de costos. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta en tu entorno de AWS con fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no monitorea la facturaci\xf3n ni env\xeda alertas cuando el gasto supera los umbrales de costos definidos."},{"id":"q976","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 comprobaci\xf3n de AWS Trusted Advisor est\xe1 disponible para todos los usuarios de AWS?","options":{"A":"Comprobaciones principales","B":"Todas las comprobaciones","C":"Comprobaciones de optimizaci\xf3n de costos","D":"Comprobaciones de tolerancia a fallos"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Todos los usuarios de AWS, incluidos los del plan de soporte gratuito Basic, tienen acceso a las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor que cubren seguridad y l\xedmites de servicio, lo que proporciona un nivel b\xe1sico de orientaci\xf3n de asesoramiento independientemente del nivel de soporte.\\nEl acceso a todas las comprobaciones de Trusted Advisor requiere un plan de soporte Business o Enterprise. Los usuarios de Basic y Developer Support est\xe1n limitados a las siete comprobaciones principales y no pueden acceder al conjunto completo de recomendaciones en las cinco categor\xedas. Las comprobaciones de optimizaci\xf3n de costos forman parte del conjunto completo de Trusted Advisor que requiere soporte Business o Enterprise, y no est\xe1n disponibles para todos los usuarios de AWS en el plan Basic. Las comprobaciones de tolerancia a fallos forman parte del conjunto completo de Trusted Advisor que requiere soporte Business o Enterprise, y no est\xe1n disponibles para todos los usuarios de AWS. Solo las siete comprobaciones principales que abarcan seguridad b\xe1sica y l\xedmites de servicio est\xe1n disponibles universalmente."},{"id":"q982","question":"Una carga de trabajo en AWS se ejecutar\xe1 durante el futuro previsible usando una cantidad constante de instancias Amazon EC2. \xbfQu\xe9 modelo de precios minimizar\xe1 el costo y garantizar\xe1 que los recursos de c\xf3mputo permanezcan disponibles?","options":{"A":"Dedicated Hosts","B":"On-Demand Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Reserved Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las Reserved Instances son la opci\xf3n m\xe1s rentable para una carga de trabajo constante y de larga duraci\xf3n, y ofrecen descuentos significativos de hasta el 72 % frente a los precios On-Demand a cambio de un compromiso de uno o tres a\xf1os.\\nLos Dedicated Hosts proporcionan aislamiento de servidor f\xedsico para fines de cumplimiento y licenciamiento, y tienen un precio superior al de las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2, lo que los hace m\xe1s costosos en lugar de minimizar el costo para una carga de trabajo est\xe1ndar y constante. Las On-Demand Instances cobran la tarifa horaria completa sin descuento, lo que las hace la opci\xf3n m\xe1s costosa para una carga de trabajo que se ejecuta continuamente a un nivel constante en el que los ahorros de Reserved Instance ser\xedan sustanciales con el tiempo. Las Spot Instances pueden ser interrumpidas por AWS cuando se recupera la capacidad excedente, y la pregunta especifica que los recursos de c\xf3mputo deben permanecer disponibles, lo que hace que las Spot Instances no sean adecuadas a pesar de su menor costo."},{"id":"q983","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 herramienta se puede usar para identificar cambios programados en la infraestructura de AWS?","options":{"A":"AWS Health Dashboard","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"Billing Dashboard","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Health Dashboard proporciona notificaciones proactivas personalizadas sobre mantenimiento programado, cambios de infraestructura y eventos del ciclo de vida que pueden afectar tus recursos espec\xedficos de AWS, lo que la convierte en la herramienta dise\xf1ada espec\xedficamente para identificar cambios planificados en la infraestructura de AWS.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor analiza tu entorno de AWS seg\xfan comprobaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas en costo, rendimiento, seguridad y tolerancia a fallos, y proporciona recomendaciones de asesoramiento en lugar de notificaciones sobre cambios programados en la infraestructura de AWS. El Billing Dashboard muestra res\xfamenes de costos y uso de tu cuenta de AWS, y es una herramienta de informes financieros sin conexi\xf3n con la identificaci\xf3n de cambios programados en la infraestructura. AWS Config rastrea y registra continuamente el estado de configuraci\xf3n de tus recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento y detectar los cambios que realizas, y no proporciona informaci\xf3n sobre cambios programados en la propia infraestructura de AWS."},{"id":"q986","question":"Una empresa desea recibir notificaciones cuando sus costos o uso de AWS Cloud superen umbrales definidos. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS admitir\xe1 estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"Cost Explorer","C":"AWS CloudTrail","D":"Amazon Macie"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AWS Budgets env\xeda notificaciones cuando los costos o el uso de AWS superan umbrales personalizados definidos, lo que permite una gesti\xf3n proactiva del gasto con alertas entregadas por correo electr\xf3nico o Amazon SNS.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaci\xf3n y an\xe1lisis de patrones hist\xf3ricos de gasto y uso de AWS con capacidad de pron\xf3stico, y es una herramienta de an\xe1lisis en lugar de un servicio de notificaci\xf3n basado en umbrales que alerte cuando el gasto supere l\xedmites definidos. AWS CloudTrail registra llamadas a la API y actividad de la cuenta para fines de auditor\xeda y cumplimiento, y no monitorea umbrales de facturaci\xf3n ni env\xeda notificaciones cuando el gasto supera l\xedmites definidos de costo o uso. Amazon Macie es un servicio de seguridad de datos que utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para descubrir y proteger datos confidenciales almacenados en Amazon S3, y es un servicio de seguridad completamente no relacionado con el monitoreo de costos ni con notificaciones de umbrales de gasto."},{"id":"q988","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS permite a los clientes comprar capacidad de Amazon EC2 no utilizada a una tarifa a menudo con descuento?","options":{"A":"Reserved Instances","B":"On-Demand Instances","C":"Dedicated Instances","D":"Spot Instances"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Las Spot Instances permiten a los clientes comprar capacidad de EC2 no utilizada con descuentos de hasta el 90 % en comparaci\xf3n con los precios On-Demand, con la contrapartida de que AWS puede recuperar esa capacidad cuando sea necesario.\\nLas Reserved Instances ofrecen descuentos mediante compromisos a largo plazo de uno o tres a\xf1os y garantizan que la capacidad est\xe9 disponible durante la duraci\xf3n de la reserva, por lo que est\xe1n dise\xf1adas para cargas de trabajo estables y predecibles en lugar de para comprar capacidad de reserva no utilizada. Las On-Demand Instances se cobran a la tarifa est\xe1ndar de AWS sin descuento, ya que es el modelo de precios base que los clientes pagan por acceso garantizado a capacidad de c\xf3mputo bajo demanda. Las Dedicated Instances se ejecutan en hardware dedicado a un \xfanico cliente con fines de cumplimiento y licenciamiento, y tienen un precio superior a las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2 en lugar de proporcionar acceso a capacidad no utilizada con descuento."},{"id":"q1000","question":"Una empresa ejecuta una base de datos Oracle autoadministrada directamente en Amazon EC2 para su base de datos en estado estable. La empresa desea reducir los costos de c\xf3mputo. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n debe usar la empresa para maximizar los ahorros durante un plazo de 3 a\xf1os?","options":{"A":"EC2 Dedicated Instances","B":"EC2 Spot Instances","C":"EC2 Reserved Instances","D":"EC2 On-Demand Instances"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Las EC2 Reserved Instances proporcionan el m\xe1ximo ahorro para una carga de trabajo en estado estable de 3 a\xf1os, ya que ofrecen hasta un 72 % de descuento sobre los precios On-Demand mediante un compromiso a largo plazo que coincide con el patr\xf3n de uso predecible de una base de datos Oracle autoadministrada.\\nLas EC2 Dedicated Instances se ejecutan en hardware dedicado a un \xfanico cliente con fines de cumplimiento y licenciamiento, y tienen un precio superior a las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2 en lugar de maximizar los ahorros para una carga de trabajo de base de datos de larga duraci\xf3n. Las EC2 Spot Instances pueden ser interrumpidas por AWS cuando se recupera capacidad de reserva, lo que las hace completamente inadecuadas para una base de datos Oracle de producci\xf3n que debe permanecer disponible continuamente para operaciones en estado estable. Las EC2 On-Demand Instances cobran la tarifa completa por hora sin descuento, lo que las convierte en la opci\xf3n m\xe1s costosa para una carga de trabajo que se ejecuta continuamente durante tres a\xf1os, donde se renunciar\xeda a los sustanciales ahorros de Reserved Instance."},{"id":"q1002","question":"\xbfCu\xe1les son los beneficios de la facturaci\xf3n consolidada para los servicios de AWS Cloud?","options":{"A":"Descuentos por volumen","B":"Una tarifa adicional m\xednima por uso","C":"Una sola factura para varias cuentas","D":"Opciones de pago a plazos","E":"Creaci\xf3n de presupuestos personalizados de costo y uso"},"multiSelect":true,"correctAnswers":["A","C"],"explanation":"La facturaci\xf3n consolidada proporciona descuentos por volumen al agregar el uso de todas las cuentas para calificar para precios por niveles, y entrega una \xfanica factura combinada que cubre todas las cuentas miembro de la organizaci\xf3n.\\nUna tarifa adicional m\xednima por uso es incorrecta porque AWS Organizations proporciona la facturaci\xf3n consolidada sin cargo adicional. La funci\xf3n de consolidaci\xf3n de facturaci\xf3n no conlleva una tarifa separada por cuenta ni por uso. Las opciones de pago a plazos no son una funci\xf3n de la facturaci\xf3n consolidada. Los cargos de AWS se facturan mensualmente seg\xfan el uso real, y no existen acuerdos de pago a plazos o diferido asociados con la facturaci\xf3n consolidada. La creaci\xf3n de presupuestos personalizados de costo y uso es una funci\xf3n de AWS Budgets, que es un servicio separado de gesti\xf3n de costos que permite establecer umbrales de gasto y recibir alertas, no un beneficio de la facturaci\xf3n consolidada en s\xed."},{"id":"q1003","question":"Una empresa espera un aumento repentino de tr\xe1fico de internet a corto plazo para su aplicaci\xf3n. Durante el aumento de tr\xe1fico, la aplicaci\xf3n no puede interrumpirse. La empresa tambi\xe9n necesita minimizar el costo y maximizar la flexibilidad. \xbfQu\xe9 tipo de instancia de Amazon EC2 debe usar la empresa para cumplir estos requisitos?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Spot Instances","C":"Reserved Instances","D":"Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Las On-Demand Instances son mejores para aumentos de tr\xe1fico a corto plazo que no pueden interrumpirse, ya que proporcionan m\xe1xima flexibilidad sin compromiso y sin riesgo de interrupci\xf3n de capacidad durante el per\xedodo de aumento.\\nLas Spot Instances usan capacidad de reserva de AWS con descuentos significativos, pero pueden interrumpirse en cualquier momento, lo que infringe directamente el requisito de que la aplicaci\xf3n no puede interrumpirse durante el aumento de tr\xe1fico. Las Reserved Instances requieren un compromiso de uno o tres a\xf1os y est\xe1n dise\xf1adas para cargas de trabajo predecibles en estado estable, por lo que se ajustan mal al manejo de un aumento de tr\xe1fico inesperado a corto plazo, donde el compromiso superar\xeda con creces el per\xedodo de uso real. Los Dedicated Hosts proporcionan aislamiento de servidor f\xedsico con fines de cumplimiento y licenciamiento a un precio superior, y son innecesarios para manejar un aumento de tr\xe1fico temporal que no tiene requisitos de aislamiento ni licenciamiento."},{"id":"q1009","question":"\xbfC\xf3mo puede AWS permitir que una empresa controle los gastos a medida que el uso de una aplicaci\xf3n cambia de forma impredecible?","options":{"A":"AWS reembolsar\xe1 la diferencia de costo si un cliente cambia a servidores m\xe1s grandes","B":"La aplicaci\xf3n se puede construir para escalar hacia arriba o hacia abajo autom\xe1ticamente a medida que se necesitan recursos","C":"Las Spot Instances se usar\xe1n autom\xe1ticamente si el precio es menor que el de las On-Demand Instances","D":"Amazon CloudWatch predecir\xe1 autom\xe1ticamente qu\xe9 recursos se necesitan"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS permite controlar los gastos mediante el auto scaling, que ajusta autom\xe1ticamente los recursos hacia arriba o hacia abajo seg\xfan la demanda real, lo que garantiza que solo pague por lo que realmente se necesita a medida que el uso cambia de forma impredecible.\\nAWS no reembolsa la diferencia de costo si un cliente cambia a servidores m\xe1s grandes. El cambio de tama\xf1o de una instancia es un cambio de configuraci\xf3n que afecta los cargos futuros, y no existe un mecanismo de reembolso por moverse entre tama\xf1os de instancia. Las Spot Instances no se usan autom\xe1ticamente cuando su precio es menor que el de On-Demand. El uso de Spot Instances requiere una configuraci\xf3n deliberada mediante plantillas de lanzamiento, grupos de Auto Scaling o solicitudes de Spot Fleet en lugar de un cambio autom\xe1tico. Amazon CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas y puede activar acciones de escalado mediante pol\xedticas de Auto Scaling, pero no predice ni aprovisiona autom\xe1ticamente por s\xed mismo los recursos espec\xedficos necesarios. La predicci\xf3n requiere configurar escalado predictivo o pol\xedticas de seguimiento de objetivo en lugar de ser un comportamiento predeterminado de CloudWatch."},{"id":"q1019","question":"Una empresa desea usar aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para analizar la configuraci\xf3n y la utilizaci\xf3n de sus recursos de AWS y recibir recomendaciones para ajustar el tama\xf1o de sus cargas de trabajo. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona esta capacidad?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Cost Explorer","C":"AWS Compute Optimizer","D":"AWS Budgets"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Compute Optimizer utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para analizar m\xe9tricas hist\xf3ricas de utilizaci\xf3n y proporcionar recomendaciones para tipos de instancia EC2 \xf3ptimos, sugerencias de ajuste de tama\xf1o y mejoras de configuraci\xf3n para reducir costos mientras se mantiene el rendimiento.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones en seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos basadas en verificaciones de buenas pr\xe1cticas y, aunque puede se\xf1alar instancias subutilizadas, no utiliza aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para analizar patrones detallados de utilizaci\xf3n con el fin de ofrecer recomendaciones granulares de ajuste de tama\xf1o. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaci\xf3n y an\xe1lisis de patrones de gasto y uso con capacidad de pron\xf3stico y, aunque incluye una funci\xf3n de recomendaci\xf3n de ajuste de tama\xf1o, es principalmente una herramienta de an\xe1lisis de costos en lugar de un servicio de optimizaci\xf3n de recursos basado en aprendizaje autom\xe1tico. AWS Budgets permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costo y uso y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, y es una herramienta de gobernanza de costos y alertas en lugar de un servicio basado en aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para analizar la configuraci\xf3n y utilizaci\xf3n de recursos."},{"id":"q1020","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS analiza la configuraci\xf3n y las m\xe9tricas de utilizaci\xf3n de las instancias de Amazon EC2 y proporciona recomendaciones para tipos de instancia \xf3ptimos?","options":{"A":"Amazon Inspector","B":"AWS Compute Optimizer","C":"AWS Config","D":"AWS Cost Explorer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Compute Optimizer recomienda configuraciones \xf3ptimas de recursos de AWS mediante el an\xe1lisis de datos de utilizaci\xf3n con modelos de aprendizaje autom\xe1tico, y proporciona recomendaciones espec\xedficas de tipos de instancia basadas en patrones reales de uso de CPU, memoria y red.\\nAmazon Inspector es un servicio de evaluaci\xf3n de seguridad automatizado que escanea instancias EC2 e im\xe1genes de contenedores en busca de vulnerabilidades de software y exposici\xf3n de red no intencionada, y es una herramienta de seguridad sin capacidad para analizar m\xe9tricas de utilizaci\xf3n ni recomendar tipos de instancia \xf3ptimos. AWS Config rastrea y registra continuamente el estado de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS para evaluar el cumplimiento con la configuraci\xf3n deseada, y se enfoca en el cumplimiento de configuraci\xf3n en lugar de analizar m\xe9tricas de rendimiento para recomendar tipos de instancia con el tama\xf1o adecuado. AWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaci\xf3n y an\xe1lisis de patrones de gasto y uso e incluye una funci\xf3n b\xe1sica de recomendaci\xf3n de ajuste de tama\xf1o, pero es principalmente una herramienta de gesti\xf3n de costos en lugar de un servicio dedicado basado en aprendizaje autom\xe1tico para la optimizaci\xf3n de instancias EC2."},{"id":"q1021","question":"Una empresa desea detectar autom\xe1ticamente patrones de gasto inusuales en su cuenta de AWS y recibir alertas cuando se encuentren anomal\xedas. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS utiliza machine learning para monitorear costos y detectar anomal\xedas?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"AWS Cost Anomaly Detection","C":"AWS Trusted Advisor","D":"Amazon GuardDuty"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Cost Anomaly Detection utiliza machine learning para monitorear continuamente los patrones de costo y uso y detectar gastos inusuales que se desv\xedan de las normas esperadas, enviando alertas cuando se identifican anomal\xedas sin requerir la configuraci\xf3n manual de umbrales.\\nAWS Budgets permite establecer umbrales personalizados de gasto y uso y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, pero requiere que usted defina manualmente los niveles de gasto esperados en lugar de detectar anomal\xedas autom\xe1ticamente mediante machine learning. AWS Trusted Advisor proporciona recomendaciones de mejores pr\xe1cticas en seguridad, costo, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos, y aunque se\xf1ala algunas ineficiencias de costo, no monitorea continuamente los patrones de gasto para detectar autom\xe1ticamente anomal\xedas de costo inusuales. Amazon GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas que monitorea continuamente cuentas y cargas de trabajo de AWS en busca de actividad maliciosa y comportamiento an\xf3malo mediante machine learning, y es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas de seguridad en lugar de un servicio de monitoreo de anomal\xedas de costo."},{"id":"q1022","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS aprovecha modelos de machine learning para identificar cambios inesperados en el gasto de AWS sin requerir que los usuarios definan umbrales de gasto?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Budgets","C":"AWS Cost Anomaly Detection","D":"AWS Pricing Calculator"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Cost Anomaly Detection aprende autom\xe1ticamente sus patrones de gasto y utiliza machine learning para identificar aumentos de costo inesperados sin requerir que los usuarios definan umbrales espec\xedficos, enviando alertas cuando se detectan anomal\xedas.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas y an\xe1lisis del gasto hist\xf3rico con capacidad de pron\xf3stico, y aunque muestra tendencias de gasto, no detecta anomal\xedas autom\xe1ticamente ni env\xeda alertas sin umbrales definidos por el usuario. AWS Budgets requiere que los usuarios definan umbrales espec\xedficos de costo y uso antes de poder enviar alertas, lo que significa que no puede identificar cambios inesperados sin una configuraci\xf3n manual previa de los niveles de gasto esperados. AWS Pricing Calculator se utiliza para estimar costos futuros de despliegues planificados en AWS antes de aprovisionar los recursos, y no tiene capacidad para monitorear patrones de gasto activos ni detectar cambios de costo inesperados."},{"id":"q1040","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 modelo de precios de AWS proporciona precios flexibles con ahorros de hasta el 72 % en el uso de c\xf3mputo, independientemente de la familia de instancias, el tama\xf1o, el sistema operativo o la tenencia?","options":{"A":"Reserved Instances","B":"Spot Instances","C":"Savings Plans","D":"Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Savings Plans ofrecen precios flexibles con ahorros de hasta el 72 % en el uso de c\xf3mputo a cambio de un compromiso de uno o tres a\xf1os con un nivel de gasto constante, aplic\xe1ndose autom\xe1ticamente en familias de instancias, tama\xf1os, sistemas operativos y tenencia de EC2.\\nReserved Instances proporcionan descuentos significativos, pero est\xe1n bloqueadas a una familia de instancias, tama\xf1o, sistema operativo y regi\xf3n espec\xedficos, a menos que sean Convertible RIs, lo que las hace menos flexibles que Savings Plans, que se aplican autom\xe1ticamente en estas dimensiones. Spot Instances ofrecen descuentos de hasta el 90 % al utilizar capacidad sobrante de AWS, pero AWS puede interrumpirlas en cualquier momento y no proporcionan la disponibilidad constante ni la flexibilidad entre familias de instancias que ofrecen Savings Plans. Dedicated Hosts proporcionan aislamiento de servidor f\xedsico para fines de cumplimiento y licenciamiento, y tienen un precio superior al de las opciones est\xe1ndar de EC2 en lugar de proporcionar los ahorros flexibles de c\xf3mputo que Savings Plans est\xe1n dise\xf1ados para ofrecer."},{"id":"q1041","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l es una diferencia clave entre AWS Savings Plans y Reserved Instances?","options":{"A":"Savings Plans requieren un compromiso de 5 a\xf1os, mientras que Reserved Instances requieren 1 o 3 a\xf1os","B":"Savings Plans se aplican entre familias de instancias y Regiones, mientras que Standard Reserved Instances son espec\xedficas para un tipo de instancia y una Regi\xf3n","C":"Reserved Instances ofrecen mayores descuentos que Savings Plans","D":"Savings Plans solo se pueden usar con Amazon EC2, mientras que Reserved Instances funcionan con todos los servicios de c\xf3mputo"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Compute Savings Plans proporcionan flexibilidad entre familias de instancias, tama\xf1os, sistemas operativos, tenencia y regiones, mientras que Standard Reserved Instances est\xe1n bloqueadas a un tipo de instancia, sistema operativo y regi\xf3n espec\xedficos.\\nSavings Plans est\xe1n disponibles con compromisos de uno o tres a\xf1os, no con un compromiso de cinco a\xf1os. Tanto Savings Plans como Reserved Instances est\xe1n disponibles con las mismas opciones de plazo de uno y tres a\xf1os. Reserved Instances no ofrecen mayores descuentos que Savings Plans como regla general. Ambos pueden ofrecer niveles de descuento similares, y Compute Savings Plans pueden igualar o superar los descuentos de muchas configuraciones de Reserved Instance mientras ofrecen mayor flexibilidad. Savings Plans se aplican al uso de c\xf3mputo de instancias EC2, AWS Fargate y AWS Lambda, no exclusivamente a EC2. Reserved Instances tambi\xe9n est\xe1n disponibles para servicios m\xe1s all\xe1 de EC2, incluidos RDS y ElastiCache, por lo que ninguna opci\xf3n est\xe1 limitada exclusivamente a EC2."},{"id":"q1055","question":"Una empresa con varias unidades de negocio desea asignar los costos de AWS a cada departamento para el chargeback interno. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de AWS les permite crear categor\xedas de costo personalizadas?","options":{"A":"AWS Cost Explorer","B":"AWS Cost Categories","C":"AWS Cost Allocation Tags","D":"AWS Budgets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Cost Categories permite crear agrupaciones personalizadas de sus datos de costo y uso al mapear los costos en categor\xedas significativas alineadas con la estructura de su negocio, lo que permite generar informes de chargeback y showback para departamentos internos.\\nAWS Cost Explorer proporciona visualizaciones interactivas y an\xe1lisis de patrones de gasto con capacidades de filtrado, y aunque puede mostrar costos por etiqueta, no proporciona un mecanismo dedicado para crear categor\xedas de costo personalizadas con nombre para el mapeo de chargeback. AWS Cost Allocation Tags le permite etiquetar recursos individuales con metadatos de departamento para que sus costos aparezcan por separado en los informes de facturaci\xf3n, pero las etiquetas operan a nivel de recurso y requieren una aplicaci\xf3n consistente en lugar de proporcionar un marco de mapeo de categor\xedas de nivel superior. AWS Budgets le permite establecer umbrales personalizados de costo y uso y recibir alertas cuando se superan esos umbrales, y es una herramienta de gobernanza de costos y alertas en lugar de un servicio para crear estructuras de categor\xedas de costo personalizadas con fines de chargeback."},{"id":"q1089","question":"Una empresa tiene el plan AWS Business Support y abre un caso de soporte porque su carga de trabajo de producci\xf3n est\xe1 experimentando un rendimiento degradado, pero no se ha detenido por completo. \xbfCu\xe1l es el tiempo de respuesta inicial objetivo para este caso?","options":{"A":"Menos de 24 horas","B":"Menos de 12 horas","C":"Menos de 4 horas","D":"Menos de 1 hora"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Con el plan AWS Business Support, un caso de sistema de producci\xf3n con rendimiento degradado, en el que la carga de trabajo experimenta un rendimiento degradado pero no se ha detenido por completo, tiene un tiempo de respuesta inicial objetivo de menos de 4 horas, lo que refleja la mayor urgencia de un entorno de producci\xf3n afectado que a\xfan no ha alcanzado el nivel de severidad m\xe1s alto.\\nMenos de 24 horas es el tiempo de respuesta objetivo para casos de orientaci\xf3n general con el plan Business Support, que se aplican a preguntas y solicitudes que no implican ning\xfan impacto en un sistema de producci\xf3n en ejecuci\xf3n y representan el nivel de prioridad m\xe1s bajo. Menos de 12 horas es el tiempo de respuesta objetivo para un caso de sistema con rendimiento degradado con el plan AWS Developer Support, y no es el SLA correcto para casos de sistema de producci\xf3n con rendimiento degradado con el plan Business Support, que proporciona tiempos de respuesta m\xe1s r\xe1pidos que Developer Support para problemas que afectan a producci\xf3n. Menos de 1 hora es el tiempo de respuesta objetivo para un caso de sistema de producci\xf3n ca\xeddo con el plan Business Support, que se aplica cuando una carga de trabajo de producci\xf3n ha dejado de funcionar por completo y representa un nivel de severidad m\xe1s alto que sistema de producci\xf3n con rendimiento degradado."},{"id":"q1090","question":"\xbfCu\xe1l de las siguientes es una diferencia clave entre el plan AWS Developer Support y el plan AWS Business Support?","options":{"A":"Developer Support incluye un Technical Account Manager; Business Support no","B":"Business Support proporciona acceso 24/7 a Cloud Support Engineers por tel\xe9fono, correo electr\xf3nico y chat; Developer Support no","C":"Developer Support proporciona acceso al conjunto completo de comprobaciones de AWS Trusted Advisor; Business Support proporciona acceso solo a las siete comprobaciones principales","D":"Business Support solo est\xe1 disponible para miembros de AWS Partner Network"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS Business Support proporciona acceso 24/7 a Cloud Support Engineers a trav\xe9s de canales de tel\xe9fono, correo electr\xf3nico y chat, mientras que AWS Developer Support proporciona acceso a Cloud Support Associates solo durante el horario laboral y solo por correo electr\xf3nico, lo que hace que el soporte t\xe9cnico multicanal las 24 horas sea un diferenciador clave del plan Business.\\nUn Technical Account Manager (TAM) est\xe1 disponible exclusivamente para clientes con los planes AWS Enterprise Support y Enterprise On-Ramp y no est\xe1 incluido en Developer Support ni en Business Support, por lo que esto no describe una diferencia entre estos dos planes. El acceso al conjunto completo de comprobaciones de AWS Trusted Advisor es una caracter\xedstica de los planes Business, Enterprise On-Ramp y Enterprise Support; Developer Support proporciona acceso solo a las siete comprobaciones principales de Trusted Advisor, lo que significa que Business Support ofrece una cobertura de Trusted Advisor m\xe1s amplia que Developer Support, no m\xe1s limitada. AWS Business Support est\xe1 disponible para cualquier cliente de AWS que elija comprarlo, independientemente de su estado como socio de AWS, y no est\xe1 restringido a miembros de AWS Partner Network."},{"id":"q1091","question":"Una empresa ejecuta una aplicaci\xf3n cr\xedtica para el negocio en AWS y requiere un plan de soporte que garantice una respuesta en un plazo de 15 minutos cuando la aplicaci\xf3n no est\xe9 disponible por completo. \xbfQu\xe9 plan de AWS Support cumple este requisito?","options":{"A":"AWS Developer Support","B":"AWS Business Support","C":"AWS Enterprise On-Ramp Support","D":"AWS Enterprise Support"},"correct":"D","explanation":"AWS Enterprise Support es el \xfanico plan que proporciona un tiempo de respuesta objetivo de menos de 15 minutos para casos de sistema cr\xedtico para el negocio ca\xeddo, en los que un sistema de producci\xf3n que respalda operaciones comerciales cr\xedticas se ha vuelto completamente no disponible, lo que refleja el nivel de urgencia m\xe1s alto en la estructura de niveles de soporte de AWS.\\nAWS Developer Support tiene como objetivo un tiempo de respuesta de menos de 12 horas para casos de sistema con rendimiento degradado y no ofrece una categor\xeda de severidad de sistema de producci\xf3n o cr\xedtico para el negocio ca\xeddo, lo que lo hace insuficiente para cualquier carga de trabajo que requiera tiempos de respuesta garantizados inferiores a una hora. AWS Business Support tiene como objetivo un tiempo de respuesta de menos de 1 hora para casos de sistema de producci\xf3n ca\xeddo, que es m\xe1s r\xe1pido que Developer Support pero no cumple con la garant\xeda de menos de 15 minutos que solo proporcionan los niveles de soporte m\xe1s premium. AWS Enterprise On-Ramp Support tiene como objetivo un tiempo de respuesta de menos de 30 minutos para casos de sistema cr\xedtico para el negocio ca\xeddo, que es m\xe1s r\xe1pido que Business Support pero a\xfan no logra la garant\xeda de respuesta de menos de 15 minutos que solo proporciona el plan completo AWS Enterprise Support."},{"id":"q1115","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona una ubicaci\xf3n central para ver y administrar los l\xedmites predeterminados de los recursos de AWS para una cuenta y solicitar aumentos cuando sea necesario?","options":{"A":"AWS Trusted Advisor","B":"AWS Organizations","C":"Service Quotas","D":"AWS Config"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Service Quotas es un servicio centralizado que permite a los clientes ver sus l\xedmites de servicio actuales en todos los servicios de AWS, realizar un seguimiento de la utilizaci\xf3n con respecto a esos l\xedmites y solicitar aumentos de cuota directamente desde la consola, lo que proporciona un \xfanico lugar para administrar todos los l\xedmites de recursos.\\nAWS Trusted Advisor incluye una comprobaci\xf3n de l\xedmites de servicio que se\xf1ala cu\xe1ndo el uso se acerca a ciertos l\xedmites predeterminados, pero solo monitorea un subconjunto de l\xedmites y no proporciona la capacidad de ver todas las cuotas ni enviar solicitudes de aumento directamente. AWS Organizations es un servicio de administraci\xf3n de cuentas para gobernar varias cuentas de AWS mediante pol\xedticas y facturaci\xf3n consolidada, y no proporciona visibilidad ni administraci\xf3n de los l\xedmites de recursos de servicios individuales. AWS Config registra y eval\xfaa la configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS con respecto a las reglas de cumplimiento, y monitorea la configuraci\xf3n de los recursos en lugar de realizar un seguimiento o administrar los l\xedmites de recursos a nivel de servicio."},{"id":"q1116","question":"Una empresa se est\xe1 acercando al n\xfamero m\xe1ximo de Amazon VPC permitidas en una Regi\xf3n y necesita solicitar un aumento. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xeda usar?","options":{"A":"AWS Support","B":"Service Quotas","C":"AWS Config","D":"AWS Budgets"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Service Quotas permite a los clientes ver sus valores de cuota actuales para los servicios de AWS, monitorear el uso con respecto a esas cuotas y enviar solicitudes para aumentar l\xedmites espec\xedficos, como el n\xfamero m\xe1ximo de VPC por Regi\xf3n, todo desde una \xfanica consola.\\nAWS Support se puede usar para enviar un caso de soporte para solicitar aumentos de l\xedmites, pero Service Quotas proporciona un enfoque m\xe1s directo y de autoservicio dise\xf1ado espec\xedficamente para ver y administrar l\xedmites de recursos sin necesidad de crear un caso de soporte. AWS Config realiza un seguimiento del estado de configuraci\xf3n de los recursos de AWS y eval\xfaa el cumplimiento con las reglas, pero no monitorea ni administra los l\xedmites de cuota de servicio ni proporciona la capacidad de solicitar aumentos. AWS Budgets es una herramienta de administraci\xf3n de costos para establecer umbrales de gasto y uso y recibir alertas cuando se acercan esos umbrales, y no tiene capacidad para ver ni administrar los l\xedmites de recursos de servicio."}]},{"id":"aws-saa-c03-open-es","title":"AWS Solutions Architect Associate (SAA-C03) — banco abierto (ES)","language":"es","attribution":"Preguntas de GitHub — nastaso/cloudcertprep (https://github.com/nastaso/cloudcertprep), licencia MIT. Adaptadas al formato de este sitio. Traduccion automatica; revisar antes de publicar.","license":"MIT","questions":[{"id":"saa-d1-001","question":"Una empresa quiere garantizar que los datos almacenados en Amazon S3 est\xe9n cifrados en reposo utilizando claves administradas completamente por AWS, sin configuraci\xf3n de claves administradas por el cliente, sin pol\xedticas de acceso por clave y sin registro de auditor\xeda de AWS KMS. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n de cifrado deber\xedan usar?","options":{"A":"SSE-S3","B":"SSE-KMS","C":"SSE-C","D":"Cifrado del lado del cliente"},"correct":"A","explanation":"SSE-S3 utiliza claves administradas por Amazon S3 (AES-256) sin participaci\xf3n del cliente, sin pol\xedticas por clave y sin registro de auditor\xeda de KMS.\\nSSE-KMS utiliza claves de AWS KMS (administradas por AWS o administradas por el cliente) y agrega pol\xedticas de acceso de KMS e integraci\xf3n con CloudTrail, lo cual es m\xe1s de lo que solicita el requisito. SSE-C requiere que el cliente proporcione y administre sus propias claves en cada solicitud. El cifrado del lado del cliente ocurre antes de la carga y es completamente administrado por el cliente."},{"id":"saa-d1-002","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona un m\xf3dulo de seguridad de hardware (HSM) administrado para el almacenamiento de claves criptogr\xe1ficas?","options":{"A":"AWS KMS","B":"AWS CloudHSM","C":"AWS Secrets Manager","D":"AWS Certificate Manager"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudHSM proporciona instancias de HSM dedicadas. KMS es un servicio de claves administrado pero utiliza infraestructura compartida. Secrets Manager almacena secretos, no claves de HSM. ACM administra certificados SSL/TLS."},{"id":"saa-d1-003","question":"Un arquitecto de soluciones necesita restringir el acceso a un bucket de S3 para que solo un rol de IAM espec\xedfico pueda acceder a \xe9l. \xbfQu\xe9 deber\xeda configurar?","options":{"A":"S3 ACL","B":"Pol\xedtica de bucket de S3 con un elemento Condition","C":"S3 Object Lock","D":"S3 Versioning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Una pol\xedtica de bucket con un elemento Condition puede restringir el acceso a un ARN de rol de IAM espec\xedfico. Las ACL son heredadas y menos precisas. Object Lock evita la eliminaci\xf3n. Versioning conserva las versiones de los objetos."},{"id":"saa-d1-004","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de Amazon VPC le permite monitorear el tr\xe1fico de red que fluye hacia y desde las interfaces de red?","options":{"A":"VPC Peering","B":"VPC Flow Logs","C":"NAT Gateway","D":"Internet Gateway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"VPC Flow Logs captura informaci\xf3n sobre el tr\xe1fico IP que va hacia y desde las interfaces de red en una VPC. VPC Peering conecta VPC. NAT Gateway permite la salida a internet para subredes privadas. Internet Gateway conecta una VPC a internet."},{"id":"saa-d1-005","question":"Una empresa requiere que todas las llamadas a la API de los servicios de AWS se registren para cumplimiento. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio deber\xedan habilitar?","options":{"A":"Amazon CloudWatch","B":"AWS CloudTrail","C":"AWS Config","D":"Amazon GuardDuty"},"correct":"B","explanation":"AWS CloudTrail registra las llamadas a la API realizadas a los servicios de AWS. CloudWatch monitorea m\xe9tricas y registros. AWS Config realiza un seguimiento de las configuraciones de recursos. GuardDuty es un servicio de detecci\xf3n de amenazas."},{"id":"saa-d2-001","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n web debe permanecer disponible incluso si una Zona de disponibilidad completa falla. \xbfQu\xe9 patr\xf3n de arquitectura logra esto?","options":{"A":"Implementar en una sola AZ con Auto Scaling","B":"Implementar en varias AZ con un Application Load Balancer","C":"Implementar en una sola AZ con un tipo de instancia m\xe1s grande","D":"Implementar en una sola Regi\xf3n con CloudFront"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La implementaci\xf3n en varias AZ con un ALB distribuye el tr\xe1fico entre las AZ, lo que proporciona resiliencia frente a la falla de una sola AZ. Una implementaci\xf3n en una sola AZ, independientemente del escalado o del tama\xf1o de la instancia, no puede sobrevivir a una interrupci\xf3n de AZ."},{"id":"saa-d2-002","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de Amazon RDS proporciona conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica a una r\xe9plica en espera en una Zona de Disponibilidad diferente?","options":{"A":"R\xe9plicas de lectura","B":"Implementaci\xf3n Multi-AZ","C":"Aurora Serverless","D":"RDS Proxy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La implementaci\xf3n Multi-AZ mantiene una r\xe9plica en espera sincr\xf3nica en una AZ diferente con conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica. Las r\xe9plicas de lectura son para escalado de lectura, no para conmutaci\xf3n por error autom\xe1tica. Aurora Serverless escala la capacidad autom\xe1ticamente. RDS Proxy gestiona las conexiones a la base de datos."},{"id":"saa-d2-003","question":"Una empresa necesita desacoplar los componentes de su aplicaci\xf3n para manejar cargas de procesamiento variables. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio deber\xedan usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon SQS","B":"Amazon RDS","C":"Amazon EC2","D":"Amazon S3"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amazon SQS es un servicio de colas de mensajes totalmente administrado que desacopla los componentes de la aplicaci\xf3n. RDS es un servicio de base de datos. EC2 es un servicio de c\xf3mputo. S3 es almacenamiento de objetos."},{"id":"saa-d2-004","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 clase de almacenamiento de S3 se debe usar para datos a los que se accede menos de una vez por trimestre y que pueden tolerar tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n de 12 horas?","options":{"A":"S3 Standard","B":"S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval","C":"S3 Glacier Deep Archive","D":"S3 One Zone-IA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Glacier Deep Archive es la clase de almacenamiento de menor costo, adecuada para datos a los que se accede con poca frecuencia y con tiempos de recuperaci\xf3n de 12 a 48 horas. Glacier Instant Retrieval proporciona acceso en milisegundos. One Zone-IA almacena datos en una sola AZ."},{"id":"saa-d2-005","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n usa Amazon DynamoDB y debe poder recuperarse de la eliminaci\xf3n accidental de una tabla. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica se debe habilitar?","options":{"A":"DynamoDB Streams","B":"Recuperaci\xf3n en un punto en el tiempo (PITR)","C":"DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","D":"Tablas globales"},"correct":"B","explanation":"La recuperaci\xf3n en un punto en el tiempo permite copias de seguridad continuas de las tablas de DynamoDB y la restauraci\xf3n a cualquier punto de los \xfaltimos 35 d\xedas. Streams captura los cambios de las tablas. DAX es una cach\xe9 en memoria. Las tablas globales proporcionan replicaci\xf3n en varias regiones."},{"id":"saa-d3-001","question":"Una empresa necesita servir contenido est\xe1tico a usuarios de todo el mundo con baja latencia. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS deber\xedan usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon CloudFront","C":"AWS Global Accelerator","D":"Amazon Route 53"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CloudFront es una CDN que almacena en cach\xe9 el contenido en ubicaciones de borde en todo el mundo para una entrega de baja latencia. Global Accelerator mejora la disponibilidad y el rendimiento para contenido din\xe1mico. Route 53 es un servicio de DNS. EC2 es un servicio de c\xf3mputo."},{"id":"saa-d3-002","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio se debe usar para ejecutar contenedores sin administrar servidores ni cl\xfasteres?","options":{"A":"Amazon EC2","B":"Amazon ECS en EC2","C":"AWS Fargate","D":"AWS Elastic Beanstalk"},"correct":"C","explanation":"AWS Fargate es un motor de c\xf3mputo sin servidor para contenedores que elimina la necesidad de administrar servidores. ECS en EC2 requiere administrar instancias EC2. Elastic Beanstalk es un PaaS para aplicaciones web."},{"id":"saa-d3-003","question":"Una aplicaci\xf3n requiere una latencia de lectura inferior a un milisegundo para una tabla de DynamoDB. \xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica deber\xeda recomendar un arquitecto de soluciones?","options":{"A":"DynamoDB Streams","B":"Tablas globales de DynamoDB","C":"DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","D":"Capacidad bajo demanda de DynamoDB"},"correct":"C","explanation":"DAX es una cach\xe9 en memoria para DynamoDB que proporciona latencia de lectura de microsegundos. Streams captura cambios. Las tablas globales proporcionan replicaci\xf3n en varias regiones. La capacidad bajo demanda escala el rendimiento autom\xe1ticamente."},{"id":"saa-d3-004","question":"Una empresa necesita procesar datos de transmisi\xf3n en tiempo real de sensores IoT. \xbfQu\xe9 servicio deber\xedan usar?","options":{"A":"Amazon SQS","B":"Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","C":"Amazon SNS","D":"Amazon MQ"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Kinesis Data Streams est\xe1 dise\xf1ado para la ingesta y el procesamiento de datos de transmisi\xf3n en tiempo real. SQS es para colas de mensajes. SNS es para notificaciones de publicaci\xf3n/suscripci\xf3n. Amazon MQ es un agente de mensajes administrado."},{"id":"saa-d3-005","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS proporciona almacenamiento de archivos completamente administrado, altamente disponible y escalable para usar con instancias EC2 en m\xfaltiples Zonas de Disponibilidad?","options":{"A":"Amazon EBS","B":"Amazon EFS","C":"Amazon S3","D":"AWS Storage Gateway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amazon EFS proporciona almacenamiento de archivos escalable y el\xe1stico accesible desde m\xfaltiples instancias EC2 en distintas AZs. EBS es almacenamiento de bloques asociado a una \xfanica instancia. S3 es almacenamiento de objetos. Storage Gateway conecta entornos on-premises con el almacenamiento de AWS."},{"id":"saa-d4-001","question":"Una empresa quiere reducir los costos de EC2 para cargas de trabajo que pueden tolerar interrupciones. \xbfQu\xe9 opci\xf3n de compra deber\xedan usar?","options":{"A":"On-Demand Instances","B":"Reserved Instances","C":"Spot Instances","D":"Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Spot Instances ofrecen hasta un 90% de descuento, pero pueden interrumpirse. On-Demand no tiene descuento. Reserved Instances requieren compromiso. Dedicated Hosts proporcionan aislamiento de servidor f\xedsico."},{"id":"saa-d4-002","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 servicio de AWS ayuda a identificar recursos subutilizados y proporciona recomendaciones de optimizaci\xf3n de costos?","options":{"A":"AWS Budgets","B":"AWS Trusted Advisor","C":"AWS Cost Explorer","D":"AWS Pricing Calculator"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trusted Advisor inspecciona tu entorno de AWS y proporciona recomendaciones para optimizaci\xf3n de costos, seguridad, rendimiento y tolerancia a fallos. Cost Explorer visualiza costos. Budgets establece alertas. Pricing Calculator estima costos."},{"id":"saa-d4-003","question":"Una empresa ejecuta un entorno de desarrollo que solo se usa durante el horario laboral. \xbfQu\xe9 enfoque minimiza los costos?","options":{"A":"Use Reserved Instances","B":"Use On-Demand Instances and stop them after hours","C":"Use Spot Instances","D":"Use Dedicated Hosts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Detener las instancias On-Demand cuando no se usan elimina los cargos de c\xf3mputo (solo quedan los costos de almacenamiento). Reserved Instances son para cargas de trabajo en estado estable. Spot Instances pueden interrumpirse. Dedicated Hosts son costosos."},{"id":"saa-d4-004","question":"\xbfQu\xe9 caracter\xedstica de S3 puede mover autom\xe1ticamente objetos a clases de almacenamiento m\xe1s econ\xf3micas seg\xfan los patrones de acceso?","options":{"A":"S3 Versioning","B":"S3 Lifecycle policies","C":"S3 Replication","D":"S3 Object Lock"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Las pol\xedticas de S3 Lifecycle transfieren autom\xe1ticamente objetos entre clases de almacenamiento o los hacen expirar seg\xfan reglas. Versioning conserva versiones de objetos. Replication copia objetos entre buckets. Object Lock impide la eliminaci\xf3n."},{"id":"saa-d4-005","question":"Un arquitecto de soluciones necesita elegir entre Amazon RDS y Amazon DynamoDB. \xbfQu\xe9 factor favorece m\xe1s a DynamoDB?","options":{"A":"Consultas SQL complejas con joins","B":"Latencia constante de milisegundos de un solo d\xedgito a cualquier escala","C":"Transacciones ACID entre m\xfaltiples tablas","D":"Aplicaci\xf3n de esquema e integridad referencial"},"correct":"B","explanation":"DynamoDB proporciona rendimiento constante de milisegundos de un solo d\xedgito a cualquier escala. RDS es mejor para consultas SQL complejas, transacciones ACID de m\xfaltiples tablas y aplicaci\xf3n de esquema."}]}]')}}]);